if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-4276"]="# CHAPTER I\n\n“HASTE TO THE WEDDING.”\n\n“Wooed and married and a’.”\n\n“Edith!” said Margaret, gently, “Edith!”\n\nBut as Margaret half suspected, Edith had fallen asleep. She lay curled up on the sofa in the back drawing-room in Harley Street looking very lovely in her white muslin and blue ribbons. If Titania had ever been dressed in white muslin and blue ribbons, and had fallen asleep on a crimson damask sofa in a back drawing-room, Edith might have been taken for her. Margaret was struck afresh by her cousin’s beauty. They had grown up together from childhood, and all along Edith had been remarked upon by every one, except Margaret, for her prettiness; but Margaret had never thought about it until the last few days, when the prospect of soon losing her companion seemed to give force to every sweet quality and charm which Edith possessed. They had been talking about wedding dresses and wedding ceremonies; and Captain Lennox, and what he had told Edith about her future life at Corfu, where his regiment was stationed; and the difficulty of keeping a piano in good tune (a difficulty which Edith seemed to consider as one of the most formidable that could befall her in her married life), and what gowns she should want in the visits to Scotland, which would immediately succeed her marriage; but the whispered tone had latterly become more drowsy; and Margaret, after a pause of a few minutes, found, as she fancied, that in spite of the buzz in the next room, Edith had rolled herself up into a soft ball of muslin and ribbon, and silken curls, and gone off into a peaceful little after-dinner nap.\n\nMargaret had been on the point of telling her cousin of some of the plans and visions which she entertained as to her future life in the country parsonage, where her father and mother lived; and where her bright holidays had always been passed, though for the last ten years her aunt Shaw’s house had been considered as her home. But in default of a listener, she had to brood over the change in her life silently as heretofore. It was a happy brooding, although tinged with regret at being separated for an indefinite time from her gentle aunt and dear cousin. As she thought of the delight of filling the important post of only daughter in Helstone parsonage, pieces of the conversation out of the next room came upon her ears. Her aunt Shaw was talking to the five or six ladies who had been dining there, and whose husbands were still in the dining-room. They were the familiar acquaintances of the house; neighbours whom Mrs. Shaw called friends, because she happened to dine with them more frequently than with any other people, and because if she or Edith wanted anything from them, or they from her, they did not scruple to make a call at each other’s houses before luncheon. These ladies and their husbands were invited in their capacity of friends, to eat a farewell dinner in honour of Edith’s approaching marriage. Edith had rather objected to this arrangement, for Captain Lennox was expected to arrive by a late train this very evening; but, although she was a spoiled child, she was too careless and idle to have a very strong will of her own, and gave way when she found that her mother had absolutely ordered those extra delicacies of the season which are always supposed to be efficacious against immoderate grief at farewell dinners. She contented herself by leaning back in her chair, merely playing with the food on her plate, and looking grave and absent; while all around her were enjoying the mots of Mr. Grey, the gentleman who always took the bottom of the table at Mrs. Shaw’s dinner parties, and asked Edith to give them some music in the drawing-room. Mr. Grey was particularly agreeable over this farewell dinner, and the gentlemen staid downstairs longer than usual. It was very well they did—to judge from the fragments of conversation which Margaret overheard.\n\n“I suffered too much myself; not that I was not extremely happy with the poor dear General, but still disparity of age is a drawback; one that I was resolved Edith should not have to encounter. Of course, without any maternal partiality, I foresaw that the dear child was likely to marry early; indeed, I had often said that I was sure she would be married before she was nineteen. I had quite prophetic feeling when Captain Lennox”—and here the voice dropped into a whisper, but Margaret could easily supply the blank. The course of true love in Edith’s case had run remarkably smooth. Mrs. Shaw had given way to the presentiment, as she expressed it; and had rather urged on the marriage, although it was below the expectations which many of Edith’s acquaintances had formed for her, a young and pretty heiress. But Mrs. Shaw said that her only child should marry for love,—and sighed emphatically, as if love had not been her motive for marrying the General. Mrs. Shaw enjoyed the romance of the present engagement rather more than her daughter. Not but that Edith was very thoroughly and properly in love; still she would certainly have preferred a good house in Belgravia, to all the picturesqueness of the life which Captain Lennox described at Corfu. The very parts which made Margaret glow as she listened, Edith pretended to shiver and shudder at; partly for the pleasure she had in being coaxed out of her dislike by her fond lover, and partly because anything of a gipsy or make-shift life was really distasteful to her. Yet had any one come with a fine house, and a fine estate, and a fine title to boot, Edith would still have clung to Captain Lennox while the temptation lasted; when it was over, it is possible she might have had little qualms of ill-concealed regret that Captain Lennox could not have united in his person everything that was desirable. In this she was but her mother’s child; who, after deliberately marrying General Shaw with no warmer feeling than respect for his character and establishment, was constantly, though quietly, bemoaning her hard lot in being united to one whom she could not love.\n\n“I have spared no expense in her trousseau,” were the next words Margaret heard. “She has all the beautiful Indian shawls and scarfs the General gave to me, but which I shall never wear again.”\n\n“She is a lucky girl,” replied another voice, which Margaret knew to be that of Mrs. Gibson, a lady who was taking a double interest in the conversation, from the fact of one of her daughters having been married within the last few weeks. “Helen had set her heart upon an Indian shawl, but really when I found what an extravagant price was asked, I was obliged to refuse her. She will be quite envious when she hears of Edith having Indian shawls. What kind are they? Delhi? with the lovely little borders?”\n\nMargaret heard her aunt’s voice again, but this time it was as if she had raised herself up from her half-recumbent position, and were looking into the more dimly lighted back drawing-room. “Edith! Edith!” cried she; and then she sank as if wearied by the exertion. Margaret stepped forward.\n\n“Edith is asleep, Aunt Shaw. Is it anything I can do?”\n\nAll the ladies said “poor child!” on receiving this distressing intelligence about Edith; and the minute lap-dog in Mrs. Shaw’s arms began to bark, as if excited by the burst of pity.\n\n“Hush, Tiny! you naughty little girl! you will waken your mistress. It was only to ask Edith if she would tell Newton to bring down her shawls; perhaps you would go, Margaret dear?”\n\nMargaret went up into the old nursery at the very top of the house, where Newton was busy getting up some laces which were required for the wedding. While Newton went (not without a muttered grumbling) to undo the shawls, which had already been exhibited four or five times that day, Margaret looked round upon the nursery; the first room in that house with which she had become familiar nine years ago, when she was brought, all untamed from the forest, to share the home, the play, and the lessons of her cousin Edith. She remembered the dark, dim look of the London nursery, presided over by an austere and ceremonious nurse, who was terribly particular about clean hands and torn frocks. She recollected the first tea up there—separate from her father and aunt, who were dining somewhere down below, an infinite depth of stairs; for unless she were up in the sky (the child thought), they must be deep down in the bowels of the earth. At home—before she came to live in Harley Street—her mother’s dressing-room had been her nursery; and as they kept early hours in the country parsonage, Margaret had always had her meals with her father and mother. Oh! well did the tall stately girl of eighteen remember the tears shed with such wild passion of grief by the little girl of nine, as she hid her face under the bed-clothes in that first night; and how she was bidden not to cry by the nurse, because it would disturb Miss Edith; and how she had cried as bitterly, but more quietly, till her newly-seen, grand, pretty aunt had come softly upstairs with Mr. Hale to show him his little sleeping daughter. Then the little Margaret had hushed her sobs, and tried to lie quiet as if asleep, for fear of making her father unhappy by her grief, which she dared not express before her aunt, and which she rather thought it was wrong to feel at all after the long hoping, and planning, and contriving they had gone through at home, before her wardrobe could be arranged to suit her grander circumstances, and before papa could leave his parish to come up to London, even for a few days.\n\nNow she had got to love the old nursery, though it was but a dismantled place; and she looked all round with a kind of cat-like regret, at the idea of leaving it for ever in three days.\n\n“Ah Newton!” said she, “I think we shall all be sorry to leave this dear old room.”\n\n“Indeed, miss, I shan’t for one. My eyes are not so good as they were, and the light here is so bad that I can’t see to mend laces except just at the window, where there’s always a shocking draught—enough to give one one’s death of cold.”\n\n“Well, I dare say you will have both good light and plenty of warmth at Naples. You must keep as much of your darning as you can till then. Thank you, Newton, I can take them down—you’re busy.”\n\nSo Margaret went down laden with shawls, and snuffing up their spicy Eastern smell. Her aunt asked her to stand as a sort of lay figure on which to display them, as Edith was still asleep. No one thought about it; but Margaret’s tall, finely made figure, in the black silk dress which she was wearing as mourning for some distant relative of her father’s, set off the long beautiful folds of the gorgeous shawls that would have half-smothered Edith. Margaret stood right under the chandelier, quite silent and passive, while her aunt adjusted the draperies. Occasionally, as she was turned round, she caught a glimpse of herself in the mirror over the chimney-piece, and smiled at her own appearance there—the familiar features in the usual garb of a princess. She touched the shawls gently as they hung around her, and took a pleasure in their soft feel and their brilliant colours, and rather liked to be dressed in such splendour—enjoying it much as a child would do, with a quiet pleased smile on her lips. Just then the door opened, and Mr. Henry Lennox was suddenly announced. Some of the ladies started back, as if half-ashamed of their feminine interest in dress. Mrs. Shaw held out her hand to the new-comer; Margaret stood perfectly still, thinking she might be yet wanted as a sort of block for the shawls; but looking at Mr. Lennox with a bright, amused face, as if sure of his sympathy in her sense of the ludicrousness at being thus surprised.\n\nHer aunt was so much absorbed in asking Mr. Henry Lennox—who had not been able to come to dinner—all sorts of questions about his brother the bridegroom, his sister the bridesmaid (coming with the Captain from Scotland for the occasion), and various other members of the Lennox family, that Margaret saw she was no more wanted as shawl-bearer, and devoted herself to the amusement of the other visitors, whom her aunt had for the moment forgotten. Almost immediately, Edith came in from the back drawing-room, winking and blinking her eyes at the stronger light, shaking back her slightly-ruffled curls, and altogether looking like the Sleeping Beauty just startled from her dreams. Even in her slumber she had instinctively felt that a Lennox was worth rousing herself for; and she had a multitude of questions to ask about dear Janet, the future, unseen sister-in-law, for whom she professed so much affection, that if Margaret had not been very proud she might have almost felt jealous of the mushroom rival. As Margaret sank rather more into the background on her aunt’s joining the conversation, she saw Henry Lennox directing his look towards a vacant seat near her; and she knew perfectly well that as soon as Edith released him from her questioning, he would take possession of that chair. She had not been quite sure, from her aunt’s rather confused account of his engagements, whether he would come that night; it was almost a surprise to see him; and now she was sure of a pleasant evening. He liked and disliked pretty nearly the same things that she did. Margaret’s face was lightened up into an honest, open brightness. By-and-by he came. She received him with a smile which had not a tinge of shyness or self-consciousness in it.\n\n“Well, I suppose you are all in the depth of business—ladies’ business, I mean. Very different to my business, which is the real true law business. Playing with shawls is very different work to drawing up settlements.”\n\n“Ah, I knew how you would be amused to find us all so occupied in admiring finery. But really Indian shawls are very perfect things of their kind.”\n\n“I have no doubt they are. Their prices are very perfect, too. Nothing wanting.”\n\nThe gentlemen came dropping in one by one, and the buzz and noise deepened in tone.\n\n“This is your last dinner-party, is it not? There are no more before Thursday?”\n\n“No. I think after this evening we shall feel at rest, which I am sure I have not done for many weeks; at least, that kind of rest when the hands have nothing more to do, and all the arrangements are complete for an event which must occupy one’s head and heart. I shall be glad to have time to think, and I am sure Edith will.”\n\n“I am not so sure about her, but I can fancy that you will. Whenever I have seen you lately, you have been carried away by a whirlwind of some other person’s making.”\n\n“Yes,” said Margaret, rather sadly, remembering the never-ending commotion about trifles that had been going on for more than a month past: “I wonder if a marriage must always be preceded by what you call a whirlwind, or whether in some cases there might not rather be a calm and peaceful time just before it.”\n\n“Cinderella’s godmother ordering the trousseau, the wedding-breakfast, writing the notes of invitation, for instance,” said Mr. Lennox, laughing.\n\n“But are all these quite necessary troubles?” asked Margaret, looking up straight at him for an answer. A sense of indescribable weariness of all the arrangements for a pretty effect, in which Edith had been busied as supreme authority for the last six weeks, oppressed her just now; and she really wanted some one to help her to a few pleasant, quiet ideas connected with a marriage.\n\n“Oh, of course,” he replied with a change to gravity in his tone. “There are forms and ceremonies to be gone through, not so much to satisfy oneself, as to stop the world’s mouth, without which stoppage there would be very little satisfaction in life. But how would you have a wedding arranged?”\n\n“Oh, I have never thought much about it; only I should like it to be a very fine summer morning; and I should like to walk to church through the shade of trees; and not to have so many bridesmaids, and to have no wedding-breakfast. I dare say I am resolving against the very things that have given me the most trouble just now.”\n\n“No, I don’t think you are. The idea of stately simplicity accords well with your character.”\n\nMargaret did not quite like this speech; she winced away from it more, from remembering former occasions on which he had tried to lead her into a discussion (in which he took the complimentary part) about her own character and ways of going on. She cut his speech rather short by saying:\n\n“It is natural for me to think of Helstone church, and the walk to it, rather than of driving up to a London church in the middle of a paved street.”\n\n“Tell me about Helstone. You have never described it to me. I should like to have some idea of the place you will be living in, when ninety-six Harley Street will be looking dingy and dirty, and dull, and shut up. Is Helstone a village, or a town, in the first place?”\n\n“Oh, only a hamlet; I don’t think I could call it a village at all. There is the church and a few houses near it on the green—cottages, rather—with roses growing all over them.”\n\n“And flowering all the year round, especially at Christmas—make your picture complete,” said he.\n\n“No,” replied Margaret, somewhat annoyed, “I am not making a picture. I am trying to describe Helstone as it really is. You should not have said that.”\n\n“I am penitent,” he answered. “Only it really sounded like a village in a tale rather than in real life.”\n\n“And so it is,” replied Margaret, eagerly. “All the other places in England that I have seen seem so hard and prosaic-looking, after the New Forest. Helstone is like a village in a poem—in one of Tennyson’s poems. But I won’t try and describe it any more. You would only laugh at me if I told you what I think of it—what it really is.”\n\n“Indeed, I would not. But I see you are going to be very resolved. Well, then, tell me that which I should like still better to know: what the parsonage is like.”\n\n“Oh, I can’t describe my home. It is home, and I can’t put its charm into words.”\n\n“I submit. You are rather severe to-night, Margaret.”\n\n“How?” said she, turning her large soft eyes round full upon him. “I did not know I was.”\n\n“Why, because I made an unlucky remark, you will neither tell me what Helstone is like, nor will you say anything about your home, though I have told you how much I want to hear about both, the latter especially.”\n\n“But indeed I cannot tell you about my own home. I don’t quite think it is a thing to be talked about, unless you knew it.”\n\n“Well, then”—pausing for a moment—“tell me what you do there. Here you read, or have lessons, or otherwise improve your mind, till the middle of the day; take a walk before lunch, go a drive with your aunt after, and have some kind of engagement in the evening. There, now fill up your day at Helstone. Shall you ride, drive, or walk?”\n\n“Walk, decidedly. We have no horse, not even for papa. He walks to the very extremity of his parish. The walks are so beautiful, it would be a shame to drive—almost a shame to ride.”\n\n“Shall you garden much? That, I believe, is a proper employment for young ladies in the country.”\n\n“I don’t know. I am afraid I shan’t like such hard work.”\n\n“Archery parties—picnics—race balls—hunt balls?”\n\n“Oh no!” said she, laughing. “Papa’s living is very small; and even if we were near such things, I doubt if I should go to them.”\n\n“I see, you won’t tell me anything. You will only tell me that you are not going to do this and that. Before the vacation ends, I think I shall pay you a call, and see what you really do employ yourself in.”\n\n“I hope you will. Then you will see for yourself how beautiful Helstone is. Now I must go. Edith is sitting down to play, and I just know enough of music to turn over the leaves for her; and, besides, Aunt Shaw won’t like us to talk.”\n\nEdith played brilliantly. In the middle of the piece the door half-opened, and Edith saw Captain Lennox hesitating whether to come in. She threw down her music, and rushed out of the room, leaving Margaret standing confused and blushing to explain to the astonished guests what vision had shown itself to cause Edith’s sudden flight. Captain Lennox had come earlier than was expected; or was it really so late? They looked at their watches, were duly shocked, and took their leave.\n\nThen Edith came back, glowing with pleasure, half-shyly, half-proudly leading in her tall handsome Captain. His brother shook hands with him, and Mrs. Shaw welcomed him in her gentle kindly way, which had always something plaintive in it, arising from the long habit of considering herself a victim to an uncongenial marriage. Now that, the General being gone, she had every good of life, with as few drawbacks as possible, she had been rather perplexed to find an anxiety, if not a sorrow. She had, however, of late settled upon her own health as a source of apprehension; she had a nervous little cough whenever she thought about it; and some complaisant doctor ordered her just what she desired—a winter in Italy. Mrs. Shaw had as strong wishes as most people, but she never liked to do anything from the open and acknowledged motive of her own good will and pleasure; she preferred being compelled to gratify herself by some other person’s command or desire. She really did persuade herself that she was submitting to some hard external necessity; and thus she was able to moan and complain in her soft manner, all the time she was in reality doing just what she liked.\n\nIt was in this way she began to speak of her own journey to Captain Lennox, who assented, as in duty bound, to all his future mother-in-law said, while his eyes sought Edith, who was busying herself in re-arranging the tea-table, and ordering up all sorts of good things in spite of his assurances that he had dined within the last two hours.\n\nMr. Henry Lennox stood leaning against the chimney-piece, amused with the family scene. He was close by his handsome brother; he was the plain one in a singularly good-looking family; but his face was intelligent, keen, and mobile; and now and then Margaret wondered what it was that he could be thinking about, while he kept silence, but was evidently observing, with an interest that was slightly sarcastic, all that Edith and she were doing. The sarcastic feeling was called out by Mrs. Shaw’s conversation with his brother; it was separate from the interest which was excited by what he saw. He thought it a pretty sight to see the two cousins so busy in their little arrangements about the table. Edith chose to do most herself. She was in a humour to enjoy showing her lover how well she could behave as a soldier’s wife. She found out that the water in the urn was cold, and ordered up the great kitchen tea-kettle; the only consequence of which was that when she met it at the door, and tried to carry it in, it was too heavy for her, and she came in pouting, with a black mark on her muslin gown, and a little round white hand indented by the handle, which she took to show to Captain Lennox, just like a hurt child, and, of course, the remedy was the same in both cases. Margaret’s quickly-adjusted spirit-lamp was the most efficacious contrivance, though not so like the gypsy-encampment which Edith, in some of her moods, chose to consider the nearest resemblance to a barrack-life.\n\nAfter this evening all was bustle till the wedding was over.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“By the soft green light in the woody glade, On the banks of moss where thy childhood played By the household tree, thro’ which thine eye First looked in love to the summer sky.” MRS. HEMANS.\n\nMargaret was once more in her morning dress, travelling quietly home with her father, who had come up to assist at the wedding. Her mother had been detained at home by a multitude of half-reasons, none of which anybody fully understood, except Mr. Hale, who was perfectly aware that all his arguments in favour of a grey satin gown, which was midway between oldness and newness, had proved unavailing; and that, as he had not the money to equip his wife afresh, from top to toe, she would not show herself at her only sister’s only child’s wedding. If Mrs. Shaw had guessed at the real reason why Mrs. Hale did not accompany her husband, she would have showered down gowns upon her; but it was nearly twenty years since Mrs. Shaw had been the poor pretty Miss Beresford, and she had really forgotten all grievances except that of the unhappiness arising from disparity of age in married life, on which she could descant by the half-hour. Dearest Maria had married the man of her heart, only eight years older than herself, with the sweetest temper, and that blue black hair one so seldom sees. Mr. Hale was one of the most delightful preachers she had ever heard, and a perfect model of a parish priest. Perhaps it was not quite a logical deduction from all these premises, but it was still Mrs. Shaw’s characteristic conclusion, as she thought over her sister’s lot: “Married for love, what can dearest Maria have to wish for in this world?” Mrs. Hale, if she spoke truth, might have answered with a ready-made list, “a silver-grey glacé silk, a white chip bonnet, oh! dozens of things for the wedding, and hundreds of things for the house.”\n\nMargaret only knew that her mother had not found it convenient to come, and she was not sorry to think that their meeting and greeting would take place at Helstone parsonage, rather than, during the confusion of the last two or three days, in the house in Harley Street, where she herself had had to play the part of Figaro, and was wanted everywhere at one and the same time. Her mind and body ached now with the recollection of all she had done and said within the last forty-eight hours. The farewells so hurriedly taken, amongst all the other good-byes, of those she had lived with so long, oppressed her now with a sad regret for the times that were no more; it did not signify what those times had been, they were gone never to return. Margaret’s heart felt more heavy than she could ever have thought it possible in going to her own dear home, the place and the life she had longed for for years—at that time of all times for yearning and longing, just before the sharp senses lose their outlines in sleep. She took her mind away with a wrench from the recollection of the past to the bright serene contemplation of the hopeful future. Her eyes began to see, not visions of what had been, but the sight actually before her; her dear father leaning back asleep in the railway carriage. His blue-black hair was grey now, and lay thinly over his brows. The bones of his face were plainly to be seen—too plainly for beauty, if his features had been less finely cut; as it was, they had a grace if not a comeliness of their own. The face was in repose; but it was rather rest after weariness than the serene calm of the countenance of one who led a placid, contented life. Margaret was painfully struck by the worn, anxious expression; and she went back over the open and avowed circumstances of her father’s life, to find the cause for the lines that spoke so plainly of habitual distress and depression.\n\n“Poor Frederick!” thought she, sighing. “Oh! if Frederick had but been a clergyman, instead of going into the navy, and being lost to us all! I wish I knew all about it. I never understood it from Aunt Shaw; I only knew he could not come back to England because of that terrible affair. Poor dear papa! how sad he looks! I am so glad I am going home, to be at hand to comfort him and mamma.”\n\nShe was ready with a bright smile, in which there was not a trace of fatigue, to greet her father when he awakened. He smiled back again, but faintly, as if it were an unusual exertion. His face returned into its lines of habitual anxiety. He had a trick of half-opening his mouth as if to speak, which constantly unsettled the form of the lips, and gave the face an undecided expression. But he had the same large, soft eyes as his daughter,—eyes which moved slowly and almost grandly round in their orbits, and were well veiled by their transparent white eyelids. Margaret was more like him than like her mother. Sometimes people wondered that parents so handsome should have a daughter who was so far regularly beautiful; not beautiful at all, was occasionally said. Her mouth was wide; no rosebud that could only open just enough to let out a “yes” and “no,” and “an’t please you, sir.” But the wide mouth was one soft curve of rich red lips; and the skin, if not white and fair, was of an ivory smoothness and delicacy. If the look on her face was, in general, too dignified and reserved for one so young, now, talking to her father, it was bright as the morning,—full of dimples, and glances that spoke of childish gladness, and boundless hope in the future.\n\nIt was the latter part of July when Margaret returned home. The forest trees were all one dark, full, dusky green; the fern below them caught all the slanting sunbeams; the weather was sultry and broodingly still. Margaret used to tramp along by her father’s side, crushing down the fern with a cruel glee, as she felt it yield under her light foot, and send up the fragrance peculiar to it—out on the broad commons into the warm scented light, seeing multitudes of wild, free, living creatures, revelling in the sunshine, and the herbs and flowers it called forth. This life—at least these walks—realised all Margaret’s anticipations. She took a pride in her forest. Its people were her people. She made hearty friends with them; learned and delighted in using their peculiar words; took up her freedom amongst them; nursed their babies; talked or read with slow distinctness to their old people; carried dainty messes to their sick; resolved before long to teach at the school where her father went regularly every day as to an appointed task, but she was continually tempted off to go and see some individual friend—man, woman, or child—in some cottage in the green shade of the forest. Her out-of-doors life was perfect. Her in-doors life had its drawbacks. With the healthy shame of a child, she blamed herself for her keenness of sight in perceiving that all was not as it should be there. Her mother—her mother always so kind and tender towards her—seemed now and then so much discontented with their situation; thought that the bishop strangely neglected his episcopal duties, in not giving Mr. Hale a better living; and almost reproached her husband because he could not bring himself to say that he wished to leave the parish, and undertake the charge of a larger. He would sigh aloud as he answered, that if he could do what he ought in little Helstone, he should be thankful; but every day he was more overpowered; the world became more bewildering. At each repeated urgency of his wife, that he would put himself in the way of seeking some preferment, Margaret saw that her father shrank more and more; and she strove at such times to reconcile her mother to Helstone. Mrs. Hale said that the near neighbourhood of so many trees affected her health; and Margaret would try to tempt her forth on to the beautiful, broad, upland, sun-streaked, cloud-shadowed common; for she was sure that her mother had accustomed herself too much to an in-doors life, seldom extending her walks beyond the church, the school, and the neighbouring cottages. This did good for a time; but when the autumn drew on, and the weather became more changeable, her mother’s idea of the unhealthiness of the place increased; and she repined even more frequently that her husband, who was more learned than Mr. Hume, a better parish priest than Mr. Houldsworth, should not have met with the preferment that these two former neighbours of theirs had done.\n\nThis marring of the peace of home, by long hours of discontent, was what Margaret was unprepared for. She knew, and had rather revelled in the idea, that she should have to give up many luxuries, which had only been troubles and trammels to her freedom in Harley Street. Her keen enjoyment of every sensuous pleasure was balanced finely, if not overbalanced, by her conscious pride in being able to do without them all, if need were. But the cloud never comes in that quarter of the horizon from which we watch for it. There had been slight complaints and passing regrets on her mother’s part, over some trifle connected with Helstone, and her father’s position there, when Margaret had been spending her holidays at home before; but in the general happiness of the recollection of those times, she had forgotten the small details which were not so pleasant.\n\nIn the latter half of September, the autumnal rains and storms came on, and Margaret was obliged to remain more in the house than she had hitherto done. Helstone was at some distance from any neighbours of their own standard of cultivation.\n\n“It is undoubtedly one of the most out-of-the-way places in England,” said Mrs. Hale, in one of her plaintive moods. “I can’t help regretting constantly that papa has really no one to associate with here; he is so thrown away; seeing no one but farmers and labourers from week’s end to week’s end. If we only lived at the other side of the parish, it would be something; there we should be almost within walking distance of the Stansfields; certainly the Gormons would be within a walk.”\n\n“Gormons,” said Margaret, “Are those the Gormons who made their fortunes in trade at Southampton? Oh! I’m glad we don’t visit them. I don’t like shoppy people. I think we are far better off, knowing only cottagers and labourers, and people without pretence.”\n\n“You must not be so fastidious, Margaret, dear!” said her mother, secretly thinking of a young and handsome Mr. Gormon whom she had once met at Mr. Hume’s.\n\n“No! I call mine a very comprehensive taste; I like all people whose occupations have to do with land; I like soldiers and sailors, and the three learned professions, as they call them. I’m sure you don’t want me to admire butchers and bakers, and candlestick-makers, do you, mamma?”\n\n“But the Gormons were neither butchers nor bakers, but very respectable coach-builders.”\n\n“Very well. Coach-building is a trade all the same, and I think a much more useless one than that of butchers or bakers. Oh! how tired I used to be of the drives every day in Aunt Shaw’s carriage, and how I longed to walk!”\n\nAnd walk Margaret did, in spite of the weather. She was so happy out of doors, at her father’s side, that she almost danced; and with the soft violence of the west wind behind her, as she crossed some heath, she seemed to be borne onwards, as lightly and easily as the fallen leaf that was wafted along by the autumnal breeze. But the evenings were rather difficult to fill up agreeably. Immediately after tea her father withdrew into his small library, and she and her mother were left alone. Mrs. Hale had never cared much for books, and had discouraged her husband, very early in their married life, in his desire of reading aloud to her, while she worked. At one time they had tried backgammon as a resource; but as Mr. Hale grew to take an increasing interest in his school and his parishioners, he found that the interruptions which arose out of these duties were regarded as hardships by his wife, not to be accepted as the natural conditions of his profession, but to be regretted and struggled against by her as they severally arose. So he withdrew, while the children were yet young, into his library, to spend his evenings (if he were at home), in reading the speculative and metaphysical books which were his delight.\n\nWhen Margaret had been here before, she had brought down with her a great box of books, recommended by masters or governess, and had found the summer’s day all too short to get through the reading she had to do before her return to town. Now there were only the well-bound, little-read English Classics, which were weeded out of her father’s library to fill up the small book-shelves in the drawing-room. Thomson’s Season’s, Hayley’s Cowper, Middleton’s Cicero, were by far the lightest, newest, and most amusing. The book-shelves did not afford much resource. Margaret told her mother every particular of her London life, to all of which Mrs. Hale listened with interest, sometimes amused and questioning, at others a little inclined to compare her sister’s circumstances of ease and comfort with the narrower means at Helstone Vicarage. On such evenings Margaret was apt to stop talking rather abruptly, and listen to the drip-drip of the rain upon the leads of the little bow window. Once or twice Margaret found herself mechanically counting the repetition of the monotonous sound, while she wondered if she might venture to put a question on a subject very near to her heart, and ask where Frederick was now; what he was doing; how long it was since they had heard from him. But a consciousness that her mother’s delicate health and positive dislike to Helstone, all dated from the time of the mutiny in which Frederick had been engaged—the full account of which Margaret had never heard, and which now seemed doomed to be buried in sad oblivion,—made her pause and turn away from the subject each time she approached it. When she was with her mother, her father seemed the best person to apply to for information; and when with him, she thought that she could speak more easily to her mother. Probably there was nothing much to be heard that was new. In one of the letters she had received before leaving Harley Street, her father had told her that they had heard from Frederick; he was still at Rio, and very well in health, and sent his best love to her; which was dry bones, but not the living intelligence she longed for. Frederick was always spoken of, in the rare times when his name was mentioned, as “Poor Frederick.” His room was kept exactly as he had left it; and was regularly dusted, and put into order by Dixon, Mrs. Hale’s maid, who touched no other part of the household work, but always remembered the day when she had been engaged by Lady Beresford as ladies’ maid to Sir John’s wards, the pretty Miss Beresfords, the belles of Rutlandshire. Dixon had always considered Mr. Hale as the blight which had fallen upon her young lady’s prospects in life. If Miss Beresford had not been in such a hurry to marry a poor country clergyman there was no knowing what she might have become. But Dixon was too loyal to desert her in her affliction and downfall (alas, her married life). She remained with her, and was devoted to her interests; always considering herself as the good and protecting fairy, whose duty it was to baffle the malignant giant, Mr. Hale. Master Frederick had been her favourite and pride; and it was with a little softening of her dignified look and manner, that she went in weekly to arrange the chamber as carefully as if he might be coming home that very evening.\n\nMargaret could not help believing that there had been some late intelligence of Frederick, unknown to her mother, which was making her father anxious and uneasy. Mrs. Hale did not seem to perceive any alteration in her husband’s looks or ways. His spirits were tender and gentle, readily affected by any small piece of intelligence concerning the welfare of others. He would be depressed for many days after witnessing a death-bed, or hearing of any crime. But now Margaret noticed an absence of mind, as if his thoughts were pre-occupied by some subject, the oppression of which could not be relieved by any daily action, such as comforting the survivors, or teaching at the school in hope of lessening the evils in the generation to come. Mr. Hale did not go out among his parishioners as much as usual; he was more shut up in his study; was anxious for the village postman, whose summons to the household was a rap on the back-kitchen window shutter—a signal which at one time had often to be repeated before any one was sufficiently alive to the hour of the day to understand what it was, and attend to him. Now Mr. Hale loitered about the garden if the morning was fine, and if not, stood dreamily by the study window until the postman had called, or gone down the lane, giving a half-respectful, half-confidential shake of the head to the parson, who watched him away from the sweet-briar hedge, and past the great arbutus, before he turned into the room to begin his day’s work, with all the signs of a heavy heart and an occupied mind.\n\nBut Margaret was at an age when any apprehension, not absolutely based on a knowledge of facts, is easily banished for a time by a bright sunny day, or some happy outward circumstance. And when the brilliant fourteen fine days of October came on, her cares were all blown away as lightly as thistledown, and she thought of nothing but the glories of the forest. The fern-harvest was over; and now that the rain was gone, many a deep glade was accessible, into which Margaret had only peeped in July and August weather. She had learnt drawing with Edith; and she had sufficiently regretted, during the gloom of the bad weather, her idle revelling in the beauty of the woodlands, while it had yet been fine, to make her determined to sketch what she could before winter fairly set in. Accordingly, she was busy preparing her board one morning, when Sarah, the housemaid, threw wide open the drawing-room door, and announced, “Mr. Henry Lennox.”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“THE MORE HASTE THE WORSE SPEED.”\n\n“Learn to win a lady’s faith Nobly, as the thing is high; Bravely, as for life and death— With a loyal gravity.\n\nLead her from the festive boards, Point her to the starry skies, Guard her, by your truthful words, Pure from courtship’s flatteries.” MRS. BROWNING.\n\n“Mr. Henry Lennox.” Margaret had been thinking of him only a moment before, and remembering his inquiry into her probable occupations at home. It was “parler du soleil et l’on en voit les rayons”; and the brightness of the sun came over Margaret’s face as she put down her board, and went forward to shake hands with him. “Tell mamma, Sarah,” said she. “Mamma and I want to ask you so many questions about Edith; I am so much obliged to you for coming.”\n\n“Did not I say that I should?” asked he, in a lower tone than that in which he had spoken.\n\n“But I heard of you so far away in the Highlands that I never thought Hampshire could come in.”\n\n“Oh!” said he, more lightly, “our young couple were playing such foolish pranks, running all sorts of risks, climbing this mountain, sailing on that lake, that I really thought they needed a Mentor to take care of them. And indeed they did: they were quite beyond my uncle’s management, and kept the old gentleman in a panic for sixteen hours out of the twenty-four. Indeed, when I once saw how unfit they were to be trusted alone, I thought it my duty not to leave them till I had seen them safely embarked at Plymouth.”\n\n“Have you been at Plymouth? Oh! Edith never named that. To be sure, she has written in such a hurry lately. Did they really sail on Tuesday?”\n\n“Really sailed, and relieved me from many responsibilities. Edith gave me all sorts of messages for you. I believe I have a little diminutive note somewhere; yes, here it is.”\n\n“Oh! thank you,” exclaimed Margaret; and then, half wishing to read it alone and unwatched, she made the excuse of going to tell her mother again (Sarah surely had made some mistake) that Mr. Lennox was there.\n\nWhen she had left the room, he began in his scrutinising way to look about him. The little drawing-room was looking its best in the streaming light of the morning sun. The middle window in the bow was opened, and clustering roses and the scarlet honeysuckle came peeping round the corner; the small lawn was gorgeous with verbenas and geraniums of all bright colours. But the very brightness outside made the colours within seem poor and faded. The carpet was far from new; the chintz had been often washed; the whole apartment was smaller and shabbier than he had expected, as back-ground and frame-work for Margaret, herself so queenly. He took up one of the books lying on the table; it was the Paradise of Dante, in the proper old Italian binding of white vellum and gold; by it lay a dictionary, and some words copied out in Margaret’s handwriting. They were a dull list of words, but somehow he liked looking at them. He put them down with a sigh.\n\n“The living is evidently as small as she said. It seems strange, for the Beresfords belong to a good family.”\n\nMargaret meanwhile had found her mother. It was one of Mrs. Hale’s fitful days, when everything was a difficulty and a hardship: and Mr. Lennox’s appearance took this shape, although secretly she felt complimented by his thinking it worth while to call.\n\n“It is most unfortunate! We are dining early to-day, and having nothing but cold meat, in order that the servants may get on with their ironing; and yet, of course, we must ask him to dinner—Edith’s brother-in-law and all. And your papa is in such low spirits this morning about something—I don’t know what. I went into the study just now, and he had his face on the table, covering it with his hands. I told him I was sure Helstone air did not agree with him any more than with me, and he suddenly lifted up his head, and begged me not to speak a word more against Helstone, he could not bear it; if there was one place he loved on earth it was Helstone. But I am sure, for all that, it is the damp and relaxing air.”\n\nMargaret felt as if a thin cold cloud had come between her and the sun. She had listened patiently, in hopes that it might be some relief to her mother to unburden herself; but now it was time to draw her back to Mr. Lennox.\n\n“Papa likes Mr. Lennox; they got on together famously at the wedding breakfast. I daresay his coming will do papa good. And never mind the dinner, dear mamma. Cold meat will do capitally for a lunch, which is the light in which Mr. Lennox will most likely look upon a two o’clock dinner.”\n\n“But what are we to do with him till then? It is only half-past ten now.”\n\n“I’ll ask him to go out sketching with me. I know he draws, and that will take him out of your way, mamma. Only do come in now; he will think it so strange if you don’t.”\n\nMrs. Hale took off her black silk apron, and smoothed her face. She looked a very pretty lady-like woman, as she greeted Mr. Lennox with the cordiality due to one who was almost a relation. He evidently expected to be asked to spend the day, and accepted the invitation with a glad readiness that made Mrs. Hale wish she could add something to the cold beef. He was pleased with everything; delighted with Margaret’s idea of going out sketching together; would not have Mr. Hale disturbed for the world, with the prospect of so soon meeting him at dinner. Margaret brought out her drawing materials for him to choose from; and after the paper and brushes had been duly selected, the two set out in the merriest spirits in the world.\n\n“Now, please, just stop here for a minute or two,” said Margaret. “These are the cottages that haunted me so during the rainy fortnight, reproaching me for not having sketched them.”\n\n“Before they tumbled down and were no more seen. Truly, if they are to be sketched—and they are very picturesque—we had better not put it off till next year. But where shall we sit?”\n\n“Oh! You might have come straight from chambers in the Temple, instead of having been two months in the Highlands! Look at this beautiful trunk of a tree, which the woodcutters have left just in the right place for the light. I will put my plaid over it, and it will be a regular forest throne.”\n\n“With your feet in that puddle for a regal footstool! Stay, I will move, and then you can come nearer this way. Who lives in these cottages?”\n\n“They were built by squatters fifty or sixty years ago. One is uninhabited; the foresters are going to take it down, as soon as the other is dead, poor old fellow! Look—there he is—I must go and speak to him. He is so deaf you will hear all our secrets.”\n\nThe old man stood bareheaded in the sun, leaning on his stick at the front of his cottage. His stiff features relaxed into a slow smile as Margaret went up and spoke to him. Mr. Lennox hastily introduced the two figures into his sketch, and finished up the landscape with a subordinate reference to them—as Margaret perceived, when the time came for getting up, putting away water, and scraps of paper, and exhibiting to each other their sketches. She laughed and blushed: Mr. Lennox watched her countenance.\n\n“Now, I call that treacherous,” said she. “I little thought that you were making old Isaac and me into subjects, when you told me to ask him the history of these cottages.”\n\n“It was irresistible. You can’t know how strong a temptation it was. I hardly dare tell you how much I shall like this sketch.”\n\nHe was not quite sure whether she heard this latter sentence before she went to the brook to wash her palette. She came back rather flushed, but looking perfectly innocent and unconscious. He was glad of it, for the speech had slipped from him unawares—a rare thing in the case of a man who premeditated his actions so much as Henry Lennox.\n\nThe aspect of home was all right and bright when they reached it. The clouds on her mother’s brow had cleared off under the propitious influence of a brace of carp, most opportunely presented by a neighbour. Mr. Hale had returned from his morning’s round, and was awaiting his visitor just outside the wicket gate that led into the garden. He looked a complete gentleman in his rather threadbare coat and well-worn hat. Margaret was proud of her father; she had always a fresh and tender pride in seeing how favourably he impressed every stranger; still her quick eye sought over his face and found there traces of some unusual disturbance, which was only put aside, not cleared away.\n\nMr. Hale asked to look at their sketches.\n\n“I think you have made the tints on the thatch too dark, have you not?” as he returned Margaret’s to her, and held out his hand for Mr. Lennox’s, which was withheld from him one moment, no more.\n\n“No, papa! I don’t think I have. The house-leek and stone-crop have grown so much darker in the rain. Is it not like, papa?” said she, peeping over his shoulder, as he looked at the figures in Mr. Lennox’s drawing.\n\n“Yes, very like. Your figure and way of holding yourself is capital. And it is just poor old Isaac’s stiff way of stooping his long rheumatic back. What is this hanging from the branch of the tree? Not a bird’s nest, surely.”\n\n“Oh no! that is my bonnet. I never can draw with my bonnet on; it makes my head so hot. I wonder if I could manage figures. There are so many people about here whom I should like to sketch.”\n\n“I should say that a likeness you very much wish to take you would always succeed in,” said Mr. Lennox. “I have great faith in the power of will. I think myself I have succeeded pretty well in yours.” Mr. Hale had preceded them into the house, while Margaret was lingering to pluck some roses, with which to adorn her morning gown for dinner.\n\n“A regular London girl would understand the implied meaning of that speech,” thought Mr. Lennox. “She would be up to looking through every speech that a young man made her for the arrière-pensée of a compliment. But I don’t believe Margaret,—Stay!” exclaimed he, “Let me help you”; and he gathered for her some velvety cramoisy roses that were above her reach, and then dividing the spoil he placed two in his button-hole, and sent her in, pleased and happy, to arrange her flowers.\n\nThe conversation at dinner flowed on quietly and agreeably. There were plenty of questions to be asked on both sides—the latest intelligence which each could give of Mrs. Shaw’s movements in Italy to be exchanged; and in the interest of what was said, the unpretending simplicity of the parsonage-ways—above all in the neighbourhood of Margaret, Mr. Lennox forgot the little feeling of disappointment with which he had at first perceived that she had spoken but the simple truth when she had described her father’s living as very small.\n\n“Margaret, my child, you might have gathered us some pears for our dessert,” said Mr. Hale, as the hospitable luxury of a freshly-decanted bottle of wine was placed on the table.\n\nMrs. Hale was hurried. It seemed as if desserts were impromptu and unusual things at the parsonage; whereas, if Mr. Hale would only have looked behind him, he would have seen biscuits and marmalade, and what not, all arranged in formal order on the side-board. But the idea of pears had taken possession of Mr. Hale’s mind, and was not to be got rid of.\n\n“There are a few brown beurrés against the south wall which are worth all foreign fruits and preserves. Run, Margaret, and gather some.”\n\n“I propose that we adjourn into the garden, and eat them there,” said Mr. Lennox. “Nothing is so delicious as to set one’s teeth into the crisp, juicy fruit, warm and scented by the sun. The worst is, the wasps are impudent enough to dispute it with one, even at the very crisis and summit of enjoyment.”\n\nHe rose, as if to follow Margaret, who had disappeared through the window: he only awaited Mrs. Hale’s permission. She would rather have wound up the dinner in the proper way, and with all the ceremonies which had gone on so smoothly hitherto, especially as she and Dixon had got out the finger-glasses from the store-room on purpose to be as correct as became General Shaw’s widow’s sister; but as Mr. Hale got up directly, and prepared to accompany his guest, she could only submit.\n\n“I shall arm myself with a knife,” said Mr. Hale: “the days of eating fruit so primitively as you describe are over with me. I must pare it and quarter it before I can enjoy it.”\n\nMargaret made a plate for the pears out of a beet-root leaf, which threw up their brown gold colour admirably. Mr. Lennox looked more at her than at the pears; but her father, inclined to cull fastidiously the very zest and perfection of the hour he had stolen from his anxiety, chose daintily the ripest fruit, and sat down on the garden bench to enjoy it at his leisure. Margaret and Mr. Lennox strolled along the little terrace-walk under the south wall, where the bees still hummed and worked busily in their hives.\n\n“What a perfect life you seem to live here! I have always felt rather contemptuously towards the poets before, with their wishes, ‘Mine be a cot beside a hill,’ and that sort of thing; but now I am afraid that the truth is, I have been nothing better than a Cockney. Just now I feel as if twenty years’ hard study of law would be amply rewarded by one year of such an exquisite serene life as this—such skies!” looking up—“such crimson and amber foliage, so perfectly motionless as that!” pointing to some of the great forest trees which shut in the garden as if it were a nest.\n\n“You must please to remember that our skies are not always as deep a blue as they are now. We have rain, and our leaves do fall, and get sodden: though I think Helstone is about as perfect a place as any in the world. Recollect how you rather scorned my description of it one evening in Harley Street: ‘a village in a tale.’”\n\n“Scorned, Margaret! That is rather a hard word.”\n\n“Perhaps it is. Only I know I should have liked to have talked to you of what I was very full at the time, and you—what must I call it then?—spoke disrespectfully of Helstone as a mere village in a tale.”\n\n“I will never do so again,” said he, warmly. They turned the corner of the walk.\n\n“I could almost wish, Margaret——” he stopped and hesitated. It was so unusual for the fluent lawyer to hesitate that Margaret looked up at him, in a little state of questioning wonder; but in an instant—from what about him she could not tell—she wished herself back with her mother—her father—anywhere away from him, for she was sure he was going to say something to which she should not know what to reply. In another moment the strong pride that was in her came to conquer her sudden agitation, which she hoped he had not perceived. Of course she could answer, and answer the right thing; and it was poor and despicable of her to shrink from hearing any speech, as if she had not power to put an end to it with her high maidenly dignity.\n\n“Margaret,” said he, taking her by surprise, and getting sudden possession of her hand, so that she was forced to stand still and listen, despising herself for the fluttering at her heart all the time; “Margaret, I wish you did not like Helstone so much—did not seem so perfectly calm and happy here. I have been hoping for these three months past to find you regretting London—and London friends, a little—enough to make you listen more kindly” (for she was quietly, but firmly, striving to extricate her hand from his grasp) “to one who has not much to offer, it is true—nothing but prospects in the future—but who does love you, Margaret, almost in spite of himself. Margaret, have I startled you too much? Speak!” For he saw her lips quivering almost as if she were going to cry. She made a strong effort to be calm; she would not speak till she had succeeded in mastering her voice, and then she said:\n\n“I was startled. I did not know that you cared for me in that way. I have always thought of you as a friend; and, please, I would rather go on thinking of you so. I don’t like to be spoken to as you have been doing. I cannot answer you as you want me to do, and yet I should feel so sorry if I vexed you.”\n\n“Margaret,” said he, looking into her eyes, which met his with their open, straight look, expressive of the utmost good faith and reluctance to give pain, “Do you”—he was going to say—“love any one else?” But it seemed as if this question would be an insult to the pure serenity of those eyes. “Forgive me! I have been too abrupt. I am punished. Only let me hope. Give me the poor comfort of telling me you have never seen any one whom you could——” Again a pause. He could not end his sentence. Margaret reproached herself acutely as the cause of his distress.\n\n“Ah! if you had but never got this fancy into your head! It was such a pleasure to think of you as a friend.”\n\n“But I may hope, may I not, Margaret, that some time you will think of me as a lover? Not yet, I see—there is no hurry—but some time——”\n\nShe was silent for a minute or two, trying to discover the truth as it was in her own heart, before replying, then she said:\n\n“I have never thought of—you, but as a friend. I like to think of you so; but I am sure I could never think of you as anything else. Pray let us both forget that all this” (“disagreeable,” she was going to say, but stopped short) “conversation has taken place.”\n\nHe paused before he replied. Then, in his habitual coldness of tone, he answered:\n\n“Of course, as your feelings are so decided, and as this conversation has been so evidently unpleasant to you, it had better not be remembered. That is all very fine in theory, that plan of forgetting whatever is painful, but it will be somewhat difficult for me, at least, to carry it into execution.”\n\n“You are vexed,” said she, sadly; “yet how can I help it?”\n\nShe looked so truly grieved as she said this, that he struggled for a moment with his real disappointment, and then answered more cheerfully, but still with a little hardness in his tone:\n\n“You should make allowances for the mortification, not only of a lover, Margaret, but of a man not given to romance in general—prudent, worldly, as some people call me—who has been carried out of his usual habits by the force of a passion—well, we will say no more of that; but in the one outlet which he has formed for the deeper and better feelings of his nature, he meets with rejection and repulse. I shall have to console myself with scorning my own folly. A struggling barrister to think of matrimony!”\n\nMargaret could not answer this. The whole tone of it annoyed her. It seemed to touch on and call out all the points of difference which had often repelled her in him; while yet he was the pleasantest man, the most sympathising friend, the person of all others who understood her best in Harley Street. She felt a tinge of contempt mingle itself with her pain at having refused him. Her beautiful lip curled in a slight disdain. It was well that, having made the round of the garden, they came suddenly upon Mr. Hale, whose whereabouts had been quite forgotten by them. He had not yet finished the pear, which he had delicately peeled in one long strip of silver-paper thinness, and which he was enjoying in a deliberate manner. It was like the story of the eastern king, who dipped his head into a basin of water, at the magician’s command, and ere he instantly took it out went through the experience of a lifetime. Margaret felt stunned, and unable to recover her self-possession enough to join in the trivial conversation that ensued between her father and Mr. Lennox. She was grave, and little disposed to speak; full of wonder when Mr. Lennox would go, and allow her to relax into thought on the events of the last quarter of an hour. He was almost as anxious to take his departure as she was for him to leave; but a few minutes light and careless talking, carried on at whatever effort, was a sacrifice which he owed to his mortified vanity, or his self-respect. He glanced from time to time at her sad and pensive face.\n\n“I am not so indifferent to her as she believes,” thought he to himself. “I do not give up hope.”\n\nBefore a quarter of an hour was over, he had fallen into a way of conversing with quiet sarcasm; speaking of life in London and life in the country, as if he were conscious of his second mocking self, and afraid of his own satire. Mr. Hale was puzzled. His visitor was a different man to what he had seen him before at the wedding-breakfast, and at dinner to-day; a lighter, cleverer, more worldly man, and, as such, dissonant to Mr. Hale. It was a relief to all three when Mr. Lennox said that he must go directly if he meant to catch the five o’clock train. They proceeded to the house to find Mrs. Hale, and wish her good-bye. At the last moment, Henry Lennox’s real self broke through the crust.\n\n“Margaret, don’t despise me; I have a heart, notwithstanding all this good-for-nothing way of talking. As a proof of it, I believe I love you more than ever—if I do not hate you—for the disdain with which you have listened to me during this last half-hour. Good-bye, Margaret—Margaret!”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n“Cast me upon some naked shore, Where I may tracke Only the print of some sad wracke, If thou be there though the seas roare, I shall no gentler calm implore.” HABINGTON.\n\nHe was gone. The house was shut up for the evening. No more deep blue skies or crimson and amber tints. Margaret went up to dress for the early tea, finding Dixon in a pretty temper from the interruption which a visitor had naturally occasioned on a busy day. She showed it by brushing away viciously at Margaret’s hair, under pretence of being in a great hurry to go to Mrs. Hale. Yet, after all, Margaret had to wait a long time in the drawing-room before her mother came down. She sat by herself at the fire, with unlighted candles on the table behind her, thinking over the day, the happy walk, happy sketching, cheerful pleasant dinner, and the uncomfortable, miserable walk in the garden.\n\nHow different men were to women! Here was she disturbed and unhappy, because her instinct had made anything but a refusal impossible; while he, not many minutes after he had met with a rejection of what ought to have been the deepest, holiest proposal of his life, could speak as if briefs, success, and all its superficial consequences of a good house, clever and agreeable society, were the sole avowed objects of his desires. Oh dear! how she could have loved him if he had but been different, with a difference which she felt, on reflection, to be one that went low—deep down. Then she took it into her head that, after all, his lightness might be but assumed, to cover a bitterness of disappointment which would have been stamped on her own heart if she had loved and been rejected.\n\nHer mother came into the room before this whirl of thoughts was adjusted into anything like order. Margaret had to shake off the recollections of what had been done and said through the day, and turn a sympathising listener to the account of how Dixon had complained that the ironing-blanket had been burnt again; and how Susan Lightfoot had been seen with artificial flowers in her bonnet, thereby giving evidence of a vain and giddy character. Mr. Hale sipped his tea in abstracted silence; Margaret had the responses all to herself. She wondered how her father and mother could be so forgetful, so regardless of their companion through the day, as never to mention his name. She forgot that he had not made them an offer.\n\nAfter tea Mr. Hale got up, and stood with his elbow on the chimney-piece, leaning his head on his hand, musing over something, and from time to time sighing deeply. Mrs. Hale went out to consult with Dixon about some winter clothing for the poor. Margaret was preparing her mother’s worsted work, and rather shrinking from the thought of the long evening, and wishing bed-time were come that she might go over the events of the day again.\n\n“Margaret!” said Mr. Hale, at last, in a sort of sudden desperate way, that made her start. “Is that tapestry thing of immediate consequence? I mean, can you leave it and come into my study? I want to speak to you about something very serious to us all.”\n\n“Very serious to us all.” Mr. Lennox had never had the opportunity of having any private conversation with her father after her refusal, or else that would indeed be a very serious affair. In the first place, Margaret felt guilty and ashamed of having grown so much into a woman as to be thought of in marriage; and secondly, she did not know if her father might not be displeased that she had taken upon herself to decline Mr. Lennox’s proposal. But she soon felt it was not about anything, which having only lately and suddenly occurred, could have given rise to any complicated thoughts, that her father wished to speak to her. He made her take a chair by him; he stirred the fire, snuffed the candles, and sighed once or twice before he could make up his mind to say—and it came out with a jerk after all—“Margaret! I am going to leave Helstone.”\n\n“Leave Helstone, papa! But why?”\n\nMr. Hale did not answer for a minute or two. He played with some papers on the table in a nervous and confused manner, opening his lips to speak several times, but closing them again without having the courage to utter a word. Margaret could not bear the sight of the suspense, which was even more distressing to her father than to herself.\n\n“But why, dear papa? Do tell me!”\n\nHe looked up at her suddenly, and then said with a slow and enforced calmness,\n\n“Because I must no longer be a minister in the Church of England.”\n\nMargaret had imagined nothing less than that some of the preferments which her mother had so much desired had befallen her father at last—something that would force him to leave beautiful, beloved Helstone, and perhaps compel him to go and live in some of the stately and silent Closes which Margaret had seen from time to time in Cathedral towns. They were grand and imposing places, but if, to go there, it was necessary to leave Helstone as a home for ever, that would have been a sad, long, lingering pain. But nothing to the shock she received from Mr. Hale’s last speech. What could he mean? It was all the worse for being so mysterious. The aspect of piteous distress on his face, almost imploring a merciful and kind judgment from his child, gave her a sudden sickening. Could he have become implicated in anything Frederick had done? Frederick was an outlaw. Had her father, out of a natural love for his son, connived at any—\n\n“Oh! what is it? do speak, papa! tell me all! Why can you no longer be a clergyman? Surely, if the bishop were told all we know about Frederick, and the hard unjust—”\n\n“It is nothing about Frederick; the bishop would have nothing to do with that. It is all myself. Margaret, I will tell you about it. I will answer any questions this once, but after to-night let us never speak of it again. I can meet the consequences of my painful, miserable doubts; but it is an effort beyond me to speak of what has caused me so much suffering.”\n\n“Doubts, papa! Doubts as to religion?” asked Margaret, more shocked than ever.\n\n“No! not doubts as to religion; not the slightest injury to that.”\n\nHe paused. Margaret sighed, as if standing on the verge of some new horror. He began again, speaking rapidly, as if to get over a set task:\n\n“You could not understand it all, if I told you—my anxiety, for years past, to know whether I had any right to hold my living—my efforts to quench my smouldering doubts by the authority of the Church. Oh! Margaret, how I love the holy Church from which I am to be shut out!” He could not go on for a moment or two. Margaret could not tell what to say; it seemed to her as terribly mysterious as if her father were about to turn Mahometan.\n\n“I have been reading to-day of the two thousand who were ejected from their churches,”—continued Mr. Hale, smiling faintly,—“trying to steal some of their bravery; but it is of no use—no use—I cannot help feeling it acutely.”\n\n“But, papa, have you well considered? Oh! it seems so terrible, so shocking,” said Margaret, suddenly bursting into tears. The one staid foundation of her home, of her idea of her beloved father, seemed reeling and rocking. What could she say? What was to be done? The sight of her distress made Mr. Hale nerve himself, in order to try and comfort her. He swallowed down the dry choking sobs which had been heaving up from his heart hitherto, and going to his bookcase he took down a volume, which he had often been reading lately, and from which he thought he had derived strength to enter upon the course in which he was now embarked.\n\n“Listen, dear Margaret,” said he, putting one arm round her waist. She took his hand in hers and grasped it tight, but she could not lift up her head; nor indeed could she attend to what he read, so great was her internal agitation.\n\n“This is the soliloquy of one who was once a clergyman in a country parish, like me; it was written by Mr. Oldfield, minister of Carsington, in Derbyshire, a hundred and sixty years ago, or more. His trials are over. He fought the good fight.” These last two sentences he spoke low, as if to himself. Then he read aloud,—\n\n“When thou canst no longer continue in thy work without dishonour to God, discredit to religion, foregoing thy integrity, wounding conscience, spoiling thy peace, and hazarding the loss of thy salvation; in a word, when the conditions upon which thou must continue (if thou wilt continue) in thy employments are sinful, and unwarranted by the word of God, thou mayest, yea, thou must believe that God will turn thy very silence, suspension, deprivation, and laying aside, to His glory, and the advancement of the Gospel’s interest. When God will not use thee in one kind, yet He will in another. A soul that desires to serve and honour Him shall never want opportunity to do it; nor must thou so limit the Holy One of Israel, as to think He hath but one way in which He can glorify Himself by thee. He can do it by thy silence as well as by thy preaching; the laying aside as well as thy continuance in thy work. It is not pretence of doing God the greatest service, or performing the weightiest duty, that will excuse the least sin, though that sin capacitated or gave us the opportunity for doing that duty. Thou wilt have little thanks, O my soul! if, when thou art charged with corrupting God’s worship, falsifying thy vows, thou pretendest a necessity for it in order to a continuance in the ministry.”\n\nAs he read this, and glanced at much more which he did not read, he gained resolution for himself, and felt as if he too could be brave and firm in doing what he believed to be right; but as he ceased he heard Margaret’s low convulsive sob; and his courage sank down under the keen sense of suffering.\n\n“Margaret, dear!” said he, drawing her closer, “think of the early martyrs; think of the thousands who have suffered.”\n\n“But, father,” said she, suddenly lifting up her flushed, tear-wet face, “the early martyrs suffered for the truth, while you—oh! dear, dear papa!”\n\n“I suffer for conscience’ sake, my child,” said he, with a dignity that was only tremulous from the acute sensitiveness of his character; “I must do what my conscience bids. I have borne long with self-reproach that would have roused any mind less torpid and cowardly than mine.” He shook his head as he went on. “Your poor mother’s fond wish, gratified at last in the mocking way in which over-fond wishes are too often fulfilled—Sodom apples as they are—has brought on this crisis, for which I ought to be, and I hope I am thankful. It is not a month since the bishop offered me another living; if I had accepted it, I should have had to make a fresh declaration of conformity to the Liturgy at my institution. Margaret, I tried to do it; I tried to content myself with simply refusing the additional preferment, and stopping quietly here,—strangling my conscience now, as I had strained it before. God forgive me!”\n\nHe rose and walked up and down the room, speaking low words of self-reproach and humiliation, of which Margaret was thankful to hear but few. At last he said,\n\n“Margaret, I return to the old sad burden: we must leave Helstone.”\n\n“Yes! I see. But when?”\n\n“I have written to the bishop—I dare say I have told you so, but I forget things just now,” said Mr. Hale, collapsing into his depressed manner as soon as he came to talk of hard matter-of-fact details, “informing him of my intention to resign this vicarage. He has been most kind; he has used arguments and expostulations, all in vain—in vain. They are but what I have tried upon myself, without avail. I shall have to take my deed of resignation, and wait upon the bishop myself, to bid him farewell. That will be a trial. But worse, far worse, will be the parting from my dear people. There is a curate appointed to read prayers—a Mr. Brown. He will come to stay with us to-morrow. Next Sunday I preach my farewell sermon.”\n\nWas it to be so sudden then? thought Margaret; and yet perhaps it was as well. Lingering would only add stings to the pain; it was better to be stunned into numbness by hearing of all these arrangements, which seemed to be nearly completed before she had been told. “What does mamma say?” asked she, with a deep sigh.\n\nTo her surprise, her father began to walk about again before he answered. At length he stopped and replied:\n\n“Margaret, I am a poor coward after all. I cannot bear to give pain. I know so well your mother’s married life has not been all she hoped—all she had a right to expect—and this will be such a blow to her, that I have never had the heart, the power to tell her. She must be told though, now,” said he, looking wistfully at his daughter. Margaret was almost overpowered with the idea that her mother knew nothing of it all, and yet the affair was so far advanced!\n\n“Yes, indeed she must,” said Margaret. “Perhaps, after all, she may not—Oh yes! she will, she must be shocked”—as the force of the blow returned upon herself in trying to realise how another would take it. “Where are we to go to?” said she at last, struck with a fresh wonder as to their future plans, if plans indeed her father had.\n\n“To Milton-Northern,” he answered, with a dull indifference, for he had perceived that, although his daughter’s love had made her cling to him, and for a moment strive to soothe him with her love, yet the keenness of the pain was as fresh as ever in her mind.\n\n“Milton-Northern! The manufacturing town in Darkshire?”\n\n“Yes,” said he, in the same despondent, indifferent way.\n\n“Why there, papa?” asked she.\n\n“Because there I can earn bread for my family. Because I know no one there, and no one knows Helstone, or can ever talk to me about it.”\n\n“Bread for your family! I thought you and mamma had”—and then she stopped, checking her natural interest regarding their future life, as she saw the gathering gloom on her father’s brow. But he, with his quick intuitive sympathy, read in her face, as in a mirror, the reflections of his own moody depression, and turned it off with an effort.\n\n“You shall be told all, Margaret. Only help me to tell your mother. I think I could do anything but that: the idea of her distress turns me sick with dread. If I tell you all, perhaps you could break it to her to-morrow. I am going out for the day, to bid farmer Dobson and the poor people on Bracy Common good-bye. Would you dislike breaking it to her very much, Margaret?”\n\nMargaret did dislike it, did shrink from it more than from anything she had ever had to do in her life before. She could not speak, all at once. Her father said, “You dislike it very much don’t you, Margaret?” Then she conquered herself, and said, with a bright strong look on her face:\n\n“It is a painful thing, but it must be done, and I will do it as well as ever I can. You must have many painful things to do.”\n\nMr. Hale shook his head despondingly: he pressed her hand in token of gratitude. Margaret was nearly upset again into a burst of crying. To turn her thoughts, she said: “Now tell me, papa, what our plans are. You and mamma have some money, independent of the income from the living, have you not? Aunt Shaw has, I know.”\n\n“Yes. I suppose we have about a hundred and twenty pounds a year of our own. Seventy of that has always gone to Frederick, since he has been abroad. I don’t know if he wants it all,” he continued in a hesitating manner. “He must have some pay for serving with the Spanish army.”\n\n“Frederick must not suffer,” said Margaret, decidedly; “in a foreign country; so unjustly treated by his own. A hundred is left. Could not you, and I, and mamma live on a hundred a year in some very cheap—very quiet part of England? Oh! I think we could.”\n\n“No!” said Mr. Hale. “That would not answer. I must do something! I must make myself busy, to keep off morbid thoughts. Besides, in a country parish I should be so painfully reminded of Helstone, and my duties here. I could not bear it, Margaret. And a hundred a year would go a very little way, after the necessary wants of housekeeping are met, towards providing your mother with all the comforts she has been accustomed to, and ought to have. No: we must go to Milton. That is settled. I can always decide better by myself, and not influenced by those whom I love,” said he, as a half apology for having arranged so much before he had told any one of his family of his intentions. “I cannot stand objections. They make me so undecided.”\n\nMargaret resolved to keep silence. After all, what did it signify where they went, compared to the one terrible change?\n\nMr. Hale continued: “A few months ago, when my misery of doubt became more than I could bear without speaking, I wrote to Mr. Bell—you remember Mr. Bell, Margaret?”\n\n“No; I never saw him, I think. But I know who he is. Frederick’s godfather—your old tutor at Oxford, don’t you mean?”\n\n“Yes. He is a Fellow of Plymouth College there. He is a native of Milton-Northern, I believe. At any rate he has property there, which has very much increased in value since Milton has become such a large manufacturing town. Well; I had reason to suspect—to imagine—I had better say nothing about it, however. But I felt sure of sympathy from Mr. Bell. I don’t know that he gave me much strength. He has lived an easy life in his college all his days. But he has been as kind as can be. And it is owing to him we are going to Milton.”\n\n“How?” said Margaret.\n\n“Why he has tenants, and houses, and mills there; so, though he dislikes the place—too bustling for one of his habits—he is obliged to keep up some sort of connection; and he tells me that he hears there is a good opening for a private tutor there.”\n\n“A private tutor!” said Margaret, looking scornful: “What in the world do manufacturers want with the classics, or literature, or the accomplishments of a gentleman?”\n\n“Oh,” said her father, “some of them really seem to be fine fellows, conscious of their own deficiencies, which is more than many a man at Oxford is. Some want resolutely to learn, though they have come to man’s estate. Some want their children to be better instructed than they themselves have been. At any rate, there is an opening, as I have said, for a private tutor. Mr. Bell has recommended me to a Mr. Thornton, a tenant of his, and a very intelligent man, as far as I can judge from his letters. And in Milton, Margaret, I shall find a busy life, if not a happy one, and people and scenes so different that I shall never be reminded of Helstone.”\n\nThere was the secret motive, as Margaret knew from her own feelings. It would be different. Discordant as it was—with almost a detestation for all she had ever heard of the North of England, the manufacturers, the people, the wild and bleak country—there was this one recommendation—it would be different to Helstone, and could never remind them of that beloved place.\n\n“When do we go?” asked Margaret, after a short silence.\n\n“I do not know exactly. I wanted to talk it over with you. You see, your mother knows nothing about it yet: but I think, in a fortnight;—after my deed of resignation is sent in, I shall have no right to remain.”\n\nMargaret was almost stunned.\n\n“In a fortnight!”\n\n“No—no, not exactly to a day. Nothing is fixed,” said her father, with anxious hesitation, as he noticed the filmy sorrow that came over her eyes, and the sudden change in her complexion. But she recovered herself immediately.\n\n“Yes, papa, it had better be fixed soon and decidedly, as you say. Only mamma to know nothing about it! It is that that is the great perplexity.”\n\n“Poor Maria!” replied Mr. Hale tenderly. “Poor, poor Maria! Oh, if I were not married—if I were but myself in the world, how easy it would be! As it is—Margaret, I dare not tell her!”\n\n“No,” said Margaret, sadly, “I will do it. Give me till to-morrow evening to choose my time. Oh, papa,” cried she with sudden passionate entreaty, “say—tell me it is a nightmare—a horrid dream—not the real waking truth! You cannot mean that you are really going to leave the Church—to give up Helstone—to be for ever separate from me, from mamma—led away by some delusion—some temptation! You do not really mean it!”\n\nMr. Hale sat in rigid stillness while she spoke.\n\nThen he looked her in the face, and said in a slow, hoarse, measured way—“I do mean it, Margaret. You must not deceive yourself in doubting the reality of my words—my fixed intention and resolve.” He looked at her in the same steady, stony manner, for some moments after he had done speaking. She, too, gazed back with pleading eyes before she would believe it was irrevocable. Then she arose and went, without another word or look, towards the door. As her fingers were on the handle he called her back. He was standing by the fireplace, shrunk and stooping; but as she came near he drew himself up to his full height, and, placing his hands on her head, he said, solemnly:\n\n“The blessing of God be upon thee, my child!”\n\n“And may He restore you to His Church,” responded she, out of the fulness of her heart. The next moment she feared lest this answer to his blessing might be irreverent, wrong—might hurt him as coming from his daughter, and she threw her arms round his neck. He held her to him for a minute or two. She heard him murmur to himself, “The martyrs and confessors had even more pain to bear—I will not shrink.”\n\nThey were startled by hearing Mrs. Hale inquiring for her daughter. They started asunder in the full consciousness of all that was before them. Mr. Hale hurriedly said, “Go, Margaret, go. I shall be out all to-morrow. Before night you will have told your mother.”\n\n“Yes,” she replied, and she returned to the drawing-room in a stunned and dizzy state.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n“I ask Thee for a thoughtful love, Through constant watching wise, To meet the glad with joyful smiles, And to wipe the weeping eyes; And a heart at leisure from itself To soothe and sympathise.” ANON.\n\nMargaret made a good listener to all her mother’s little plans for adding some small comforts to the lot of the poorest parishioners. She could not help listening, though each new project was a stab to her heart. By the time the frost had set in, they should be far away from Helstone. Old Simon’s rheumatism might be bad and his eyesight worse; there would be no one to go and read to him, and comfort him with little porringers of broth and good red flannel; or if there was, it would be a stranger, and the old man would watch in vain for her. Mary Domville’s little crippled boy would crawl in vain to the door and look for her coming through the forest. These poor friends would never understand why she had forsaken them; and there were many others besides. “Papa has always spent the income he derived from his living in the parish. I am, perhaps, encroaching upon the next dues, but the winter is likely to be severe, and our poor people must be helped.”\n\n“Oh, mamma, let us do all we can,” said Margaret eagerly, not seeing the prudential side of the question, only grasping at the idea that they were rendering such help for the last time; “we may not be here long.”\n\n“Do you feel ill, my darling?” asks Mrs. Hale, anxiously, misunderstanding Margaret’s hint of the uncertainty of their stay at Helstone. “You look pale and tired. It is this soft, damp, unhealthy air.”\n\n“No—no, mamma, it is not that: it is delicious air. It smells of the freshest, purest fragrance, after the smokiness of Harley Street. But I am tired: it surely must be near bedtime.”\n\n“Not far off—it is half-past nine. You had better go to bed at once, dear. Ask Dixon for some gruel. I will come and see you as soon as you are in bed. I am afraid you have taken cold; or the bad air from some of the stagnant ponds—”\n\n“Oh, mamma,” said Margaret, faintly smiling as she kissed her mother, “I am quite well—don’t alarm yourself about me; I am only tired.”\n\nMargaret went up stairs. To soothe her mother’s anxiety she submitted to a basin of gruel. She was lying languidly in bed when Mrs. Hale came up to make some last inquiries and kiss her before going to her own room for the night. But the instant she heard her mother’s door locked, she sprang out of bed, and throwing her dressing-gown on, she began to pace up and down the room, until the creaking of one of the boards reminded her that she must make no noise. She went and curled herself upon the window-seat in the small, deeply-recessed window. That morning when she had looked out, her heart had danced at seeing the bright clear lights on the church tower, which foretold a fine and sunny day. This evening—sixteen hours at most had past by—she sat down, too full of sorrow to cry, but with a cold dull pain, which seemed to have pressed the youth and buoyancy out of her heart, never to return. Mr. Henry Lennox’s visit—his offer—was like a dream, a thing beside her actual life. The hard reality was, that her father had so admitted tempting doubts into his mind as to become a schismatic—an outcast; all the changes consequent upon this grouped themselves around that one great blighting fact.\n\nShe looked out upon the dark-grey lines of the church towers, square and straight in the centre of the view, cutting against the deep blue transparent depths beyond, into which she gazed, and felt that she might gaze for ever, seeing at every moment some farther distance, and yet no sign of God! It seemed to her at the moment, as if the earth was more utterly desolate than if girt in by an iron dome, behind which there might be the ineffaceable peace and glory of the Almighty: those never-ending depths of space, in their still serenity, were more mocking to her than any material bounds could be—shutting in the cries of earth’s sufferers, which now might ascend into that infinite splendour of vastness and be lost—lost for ever, before they reached His throne. In this mood her father came in unheard. The moonlight was strong enough to let him see his daughter in her unusual place and attitude. He came to her and touched her shoulder before she was aware that he was there.\n\n“Margaret, I heard you were up. I could not help coming in to ask you to pray with me—to say the Lord’s Prayer; that will do good to both of us.”\n\nMr. Hale and Margaret knelt by the window-seat—he looking up, she bowed down in humble shame. God was there, close around them, hearing her father’s whispered words. Her father might be a heretic; but had not she, in her despairing doubts not five minutes before, shown herself a far more utter sceptic? She spoke not a word, but stole to bed after her father had left her, like a child ashamed of its fault. If the world was full of perplexing problems she would trust, and only ask to see the one step needful for the hour. Mr. Lennox—his visit, his proposal—the remembrance of which had been so rudely pushed aside by the subsequent events of the day—haunted her dreams that night. He was climbing up some tree of fabulous height to reach the branch whereon was slung her bonnet: he was falling and she was struggling to save him, but held back by some invisible powerful hand. He was dead. And yet, with a shifting of the scene, she was once more in the Harley Street drawing-room, talking to him as of old, and still with a consciousness all the time that she had seen him killed by that terrible fall.\n\nMiserable, unresting night! Ill preparation for the coming day! She awoke with a start, unrefreshed, and conscious of some reality worse even than her feverish dreams. It all came back upon her; not merely the sorrow, but the terrible discord in the sorrow. Where, to what distance apart, had her father wandered, led by doubts which were to her temptations of the Evil One? She longed to ask, and yet would not have heard for all the world.\n\nThe fine crisp morning made her mother feel particularly well and happy at breakfast-time. She talked on, planning village kindness, unheeding the silence of her husband and the monosyllabic answers of Margaret. Before the things were cleared away, Mr. Hale got up; he leaned one hand on the table, as if to support himself:\n\n“I shall not be home till evening. I am going to Bracy Common, and will ask Farmer Dobson to give me something for dinner. I shall be back to tea at seven.”\n\nHe did not look at either of them, but Margaret knew what he meant. By seven the announcement must be made to her mother. Mr. Hale would have delayed making it, but Margaret was of different stuff. She could not bear the impending weight on her mind all day long: better get the worst over; the day would be too short to comfort her mother. But while she stood by the window, thinking how to begin, and waiting for the servant to have left the room, her mother had gone up stairs to put on her things to go to the school. She came down ready equipped, in a brisker mood than usual.\n\n“Mother, come round the garden with me this morning; just one turn,” said Margaret, putting her arm round Mrs. Hale’s waist.\n\nThey passed through the open window. Mrs. Hale spoke—said something—Margaret could not tell what. Her eye caught on a bee entering a deep-belled flower: when that bee flew forth with his spoil she would begin—that should be the sign. Out he came.\n\n“Mamma! Papa is going to leave Helstone!” she blurted forth. “He’s going to leave the Church, and live in Milton-Northern.” There were the three hard facts hardly spoken.\n\n“What makes you say so?” asked Mrs. Hale, in a surprised, incredulous voice. “Who has been telling you such nonsense?”\n\n“Papa himself,” said Margaret, longing to say something gentle and consoling, but literally not knowing how. They were close to a garden-bench. Mrs. Hale sat down and began to cry.\n\n“I don’t understand you,” she said. “Either you have made some great mistake, or I don’t quite understand you.”\n\n“No, mother, I have made no mistake. Papa has written to the bishop, saying that he has such doubts that he cannot conscientiously remain a priest of the Church of England, and that he must give up Helstone. He has also consulted Mr. Bell—Frederick’s godfather, you know, mamma; and it is arranged that we go to live at Milton-Northern.” Mrs. Hale looked up in Margaret’s face all the time she was speaking these words: the shadow on her countenance told that she, at least, believed in the truth of what she said.\n\n“I don’t think it can be true,” said Mrs. Hale, at length. “He would surely have told me before it came to this.”\n\nIt came strongly upon Margaret’s mind that her mother ought to have been told: that whatever her faults of discontent and repining might have been, it was an error in her father to have left her to learn his change of opinion, and his approaching change of life, from her better-informed child. Margaret sat down by her mother, and took her unresisting head on her breast, bending her own soft cheeks down caressingly to touch her face.\n\n“Dear, darling mamma! we were so afraid of giving you pain. Papa felt so acutely—you know you are not strong, and there must have been such terrible suspense to go through.”\n\n“When did he tell you, Margaret?”\n\n“Yesterday, only yesterday,” replied Margaret, detecting the jealousy which prompted the inquiry. “Poor papa!”—trying to divert her mother’s thoughts into compassionate sympathy for all her father had gone through. Mrs. Hale raised her head.\n\n“What does he mean by having doubts?” she asked. “Surely, he does not mean that he thinks differently—that he knows better than the Church.”\n\nMargaret shook her head, and the tears came into her eyes, as her mother touched the bare nerve of her own regret.\n\n“Can’t the bishop set him right?” asked Mrs. Hale half impatiently.\n\n“I’m afraid not,” said Margaret. “But I did not ask. I could not bear to hear what he might answer. It is all settled at any rate. He is going to leave Helstone in a fortnight. I am not sure if he did not say he had sent in his deed of resignation.”\n\n“In a fortnight!” exclaimed Mrs. Hale, “I do think this is very strange—not at all right. I call it very unfeeling,” said she, beginning to take relief in tears. “He has doubts you say, and gives up his living, and all without consulting me. I dare say, if he had told me his doubts at the first I could have nipped them in the bud.”\n\nMistaken as Margaret felt her father’s conduct to have been, she could not bear to hear it blamed by her mother. She knew that his very reserve had originated in a tenderness for her, which might be cowardly, but was not unfeeling.\n\n“I almost hoped you might be glad to leave Helstone, mamma,” said she, after a pause. “You have never been well in this air, you know.”\n\n“You can’t think the smoky air of a manufacturing town, all chimneys and dirt like Milton-Northern, would be better than this air, which is pure and sweet, if it is too soft and relaxing. Fancy living in the middle of factories, and factory people! Though, of course, if your father leaves the Church, we shall not be admitted into society anywhere. It will be such a disgrace to us! Poor dear Sir John! It is well he is not alive to see what your father has come to! Every day after dinner, when I was a girl, living with your Aunt Shaw, at Beresford Court, Sir John used to give for the first toast—‘Church and King, and down with the Rump.’”\n\nMargaret was glad that her mother’s thoughts were turned away from the fact of her husband’s silence to her on the point which must have been so near his heart. Next to the serious vital anxiety as to the nature of her father’s doubts, this was the one circumstance of the case that gave Margaret the most pain.\n\n“You know, we have very little society here, mamma. The Gormans, who are our nearest neighbours (to call society—and we hardly ever see them), have been in trade just as much as these Milton-Northern people.”\n\n“Yes,” says Mrs. Hale, almost indignantly, “but at any rate, the Gormans made carriages for half the gentlemen of the county, and were brought into some kind of intercourse with them; but these factory people, who on earth wears cotton that can afford linen?”\n\n“Well, mamma, I give up the cotton-spinners; I am not standing up for them, any more than for any other trades-people. Only we shall have little enough to do with them.”\n\n“Why on earth has your father fixed on Milton-Northern to live in?”\n\n“Partly,” said Margaret, sighing, “because it is so very different from Helstone—partly because Mr. Bell says there is an opening there for a private tutor.”\n\n“Private tutor in Milton! Why can’t he go to Oxford, and be a tutor to gentlemen?”\n\n“You forget, mamma! He is leaving the Church on account of his opinions—his doubts would do him no good at Oxford.”\n\nMrs. Hale was silent for some time, quietly crying. At last she said:—\n\n“And the furniture—How in the world are we to manage the removal? I never removed in my life, and only a fortnight to think about it!”\n\nMargaret was inexpressibly relieved to find that her mother’s anxiety and distress was lowered to this point, so insignificant to herself, and on which she could do so much to help. She planned and promised, and led her mother on to arrange fully as much as could be fixed before they knew somewhat more definitely what Mr. Hale intended to do. Throughout the day Margaret never left her mother; bending her whole soul to sympathise in all the various turns her feelings took; towards evening especially, as she became more and more anxious that her father should find a soothing welcome home awaiting him, after his return from his day of fatigue and distress. She dwelt upon what he must have borne in secret for long; her mother only replied coldly that he ought to have told her, and that then at any rate he would have had an adviser to give him counsel; and Margaret turned faint at heart when she heard her father’s step in the hall. She dared not go to meet him, and tell him what she had done all day, for fear of her mother’s jealous annoyance. She heard him linger, as if awaiting her, or some sign of her; and she dared not stir; she saw by her mother’s twitching lips, and changing colour, that she too was aware that her husband had returned. Presently he opened the room-door, and stood there uncertain whether to come in. His face was gray and pale; he had a timid fearful look in his eyes; something almost pitiful to see in a man’s face; but that look of despondent uncertainty, of mental and bodily languor, touched his wife’s heart. She went to him, and threw herself on his breast, crying out:—\n\n“Oh! Richard, Richard, you should have told me sooner!”\n\nAnd then, in tears, Margaret left her, as she rushed up stairs to throw herself on her bed, and hide her face in the pillows to stifle the hysteric sobs that would force their way out at last, after the rigid self-control of the whole day.\n\nHow long she lay thus she could not tell. She heard no noise, though the housemaid came in to arrange the room. The affrighted girl stole out again on tip-toe, and went and told Mrs. Dixon that Miss Hale was crying as if her heart would break: she was sure she would make herself deadly ill if she went on at that rate. In consequence of this, Margaret felt herself touched, and started up into a sitting posture; she saw the accustomed room, the figure of Dixon in shadow, as the latter stood holding a candle a little behind her, for fear of the effect on Miss Hale’s startled eyes, swollen and blinded as they were.\n\n“Oh, Dixon! I did not hear you come into the room!” said Margaret, resuming her trembling self-restraint. “Is it very late?” continued she, lifting herself languidly off the bed, yet letting her feet touch the ground without fairly standing down, as she shaded her wet ruffled hair off her face, and tried to look as though nothing were the matter; as if she had been asleep.\n\n“I can hardly tell what time it is,” replied Dixon in an aggrieved tone of voice. “Since your mamma told me this terrible news, when I dressed her for tea, I’ve lost all count of time. I’m sure I don’t know what is to become of us all. When Charlotte told me just now you were sobbing, Miss Hale, I thought, no wonder, poor thing! And master thinking of turning Dissenter at his time of life, when, if it is not to be said he’s done well in the Church, he’s not done badly after all. I had a cousin, miss, who turned Methodist preacher after he was fifty years of age, and a tailor all his life; but then he had never been able to make a pair of trousers to fit, for as long as he had been in the trade, so it was no wonder; but for master! as I said to missus, ‘What would poor Sir John have said? he never liked your marrying Mr. Hale, but if he could have known it would have come to this, he would have sworn worse oaths than ever, if that was possible!’”\n\nDixon had been so much accustomed to comment upon Mr. Hale’s proceedings to her mistress (who listened to her, or not, as she was in the humour), that she never noticed Margaret’s flashing eye and dilating nostril. To hear her father talked of in this way by a servant to her face!\n\n“Dixon,” she said, in the low tone she always used when much excited, which had a sound in it as of some distant turmoil, or threatening storm breaking far away. “Dixon! you forget to whom you are speaking.” She stood upright and firm on her feet now, confronting the waiting-maid, and fixing her with her steady discerning eye. “I am Mr. Hale’s daughter. Go! You have made a strange mistake, and one that I am sure your own good feeling will make you sorry for when you think about it.”\n\nDixon hung irresolutely about the room for a minute or two. Margaret repeated, “You may leave me, Dixon. I wish you to go.” Dixon did not know whether to resent these decided words or to cry; either course would have suited her mistress: but as she said to herself, “Miss Margaret has a touch of the old gentleman about her, as well as poor Master Frederick; I wonder where they get it from?” and she, who would have resented such words from any one less haughty and determined in manner, was subdued enough to say, in a half humble, half injured tone:\n\n“Mayn’t I unfasten your gown, miss, and do your hair?”\n\n“No! not to-night, thank you.” And Margaret gravely lighted her out of the room, and bolted the door. From henceforth Dixon obeyed and admired Margaret. She said it was because she was so like poor Master Frederick; but the truth was, that Dixon, as do many others, liked to feel herself ruled by a powerful and decided nature.\n\nMargaret needed all Dixon’s help in action, and silence in words; for, for some time, the latter thought it her duty to show her sense of affront by saying as little as possible to her young lady; so the energy came out in doing rather than in speaking. A fortnight was a very short time to make arrangements for so serious a removal; as Dixon said, “Anyone but a gentleman—indeed almost any other gentleman—” but catching a look at Margaret’s straight, stern brow just here, she coughed the remainder of the sentence away, and meekly took the horehound drop that Margaret offered her, to stop the “little tickling at my chest, miss.” But almost any one but Mr. Hale would have had practical knowledge enough to see, that in so short a time it would be difficult to fix on any house in Milton-Northern, or indeed elsewhere, to which they could remove the furniture that had of necessity been taken out of Helstone Vicarage.\n\nMrs. Hale, overpowered by all the troubles and necessities for immediate household decisions that seemed to come upon her at once, became really ill, and Margaret almost felt it as a relief when her mother fairly took to her bed, and left the management of affairs to her. Dixon, true to her post of body-guard, attended most faithfully to her mistress, and only emerged from Mrs. Hale’s bedroom to shake her head, and murmur to herself in a manner which Margaret did not choose to hear. For, the one thing clear and straight before her, was the necessity for leaving Helstone. Mr. Hale’s successor in the living was appointed; and, at any rate, after her father’s decision, there must be no lingering now, for his sake, as well as from every other consideration. For he came home every evening more and more depressed, after the necessary leave-taking which he had resolved to have with every individual parishioner. Margaret, inexperienced as she was in all the necessary matter-of-fact business to be got through, did not know to whom to apply for advice. The cook and Charlotte worked away with willing arms and stout hearts at all the moving and packing; and as far as that went, Margaret’s admirable sense enabled her to see what was best, and to direct how it should be done. But where were they to go to? In a week they must be gone. Straight to Milton, or where? So many arrangements depended on this precision that Margaret resolved to ask her father one evening, in spite of his evident fatigue and low spirits. He answered:\n\n“My dear! I have really had too much to think about to settle this. What does your mother say? What does she wish? Poor Maria!”\n\nHe met with an echo even louder than his sigh. Dixon had just come into the room for another cup of tea for Mrs. Hale, and catching Mr. Hale’s last words, and protected by his presence from Margaret’s upbraiding eyes, made bold to say, “My poor mistress!”\n\n“You don’t think her worse to-day,” said Mr. Hale, turning hastily.\n\n“I am sure I can’t say, sir. It’s not for me to judge. The illness seems so much more on the mind than on the body.”\n\nMr. Hale looked infinitely distressed.\n\n“You had better take mamma her tea while it is hot, Dixon,” said Margaret, in a tone of quiet authority.\n\n“Oh! I beg your pardon, Miss! My thoughts was otherwise occupied in thinking of my poor—— of Mrs. Hale.”\n\n“Papa!” said Margaret, “it is this suspense that is bad for you both. Of course, mamma must feel your change of opinions: we can’t help that,” she continued softly; “but now the course is clear, at least to a certain point. And I think, papa, that I could get mamma to help me in planning, if you could tell me what to plan for. She has never expressed any wish in any way, and only thinks of what can’t be helped. Are we to go straight to Milton? Have you taken a house there?”\n\n“No,” he replied, “I suppose we must go into lodgings, and look about for a house.”\n\n“And pack up the furniture so that it can be left at the railway station, till we have met with one?”\n\n“I suppose so. Do what you think best. Only remember, we shall have much less money to spend.”\n\nThey had never had much superfluity, as Margaret knew. She felt that it was a great weight suddenly thrown upon her shoulders. Four months ago, all the decisions she needed to make were what dress she would wear for dinner, and to help Edith to draw out the lists of who should take down whom in the dinner parties at home. Nor was the household in which she lived one that called for much decision. Except in the one grand case of Captain Lennox’s offer, everything went on with the regularity of clockwork. Once a year, there was a long discussion between her aunt and Edith as to whether they should go to the Isle of Wight, abroad, or to Scotland; but at such times Margaret herself was secure of drifting, without any exertion of her own, into the quiet harbour of home. Now, since that day when Mr. Lennox came, and startled her into a decision, every day brought some question, momentous to her, and to those whom she loved, to be settled.\n\nHer father went up after tea to sit with his wife. Margaret remained alone in the drawing-room. Suddenly she took a candle and went into her father’s study for a great atlas, and lugging it back into the drawing-room, she began to pore over the map of England. She was ready to look up brightly when her father came down stairs.\n\n“I have hit upon such a beautiful plan. Look here—in Darkshire, hardly the breadth of my finger from Milton, is Heston, which I have often heard of from people living in the north as such a pleasant little bathing-place. Now, don’t you think we could get mamma there with Dixon, while you and I go and look at houses, and get one all ready for her in Milton? She would get a breath of sea air to set her up for the winter, and be spared all the fatigue, and Dixon would enjoy taking care of her.”\n\n“Is Dixon to go with us?” asked Mr. Hale, in a kind of helpless dismay.\n\n“Oh, yes!” said Margaret. “Dixon quite intends it, and I don’t know what mamma would do without her.”\n\n“But we shall have to put up with a very different way of living, I am afraid. Everything is so much dearer in a town. I doubt if Dixon can make herself comfortable. To tell you the truth, Margaret, I sometimes feel as if that woman gave herself airs.”\n\n“To be sure she does, papa,” replied Margaret; “and if she has to put up with a different style of living, we shall have to put up with her airs, which will be worse. But she really loves us all, and would be miserable to leave us, I am sure—especially in this change; so, for mamma’s sake, and for the sake of her faithfulness, I do think she must go.”\n\n“Very well, my dear. Go on. I am resigned. How far is Heston from Milton? The breadth of one of your fingers does not give me a very clear idea of distance.”\n\n“Well, then, I suppose it is thirty miles; that is not much!”\n\n“Not in distance, but in—. Never mind! If you really think it will do your mother good, let it be fixed so.”\n\nThis was a great step. Now Margaret could work, and act, and plan in good earnest. And now Mrs. Hale could rouse herself from her languor, and forget her real suffering in thinking of the pleasure and the delight of going to the sea-side. Her only regret was that Mr. Hale could not be with her all the fortnight she was to be there, as he had been for a whole fortnight once, when they were engaged, and she was staying with Sir John and Lady Beresford at Torquay.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n“Unwatch’d the garden bough shall sway, The tender blossom flutter down, Unloved that beech will gather brown, The maple burn itself away;\n\nUnloved, the sun-flower, shining fair, Ray round with flames her disk of seed, And many a rose-carnation feed With summer spice the humming air;\n\nTill from the garden and the wild A fresh association blow And year by year the landscape grow Familiar to the stranger’s child;\n\nAs year by year the labourer tills His wonted glebe, or lops the glades; And year by year our memory fades From all the circle of the hills.” TENNYSON.\n\nThe last day came; the house was full of packing-cases, which were being carted off at the front door, to the nearest railway station. Even the pretty lawn at the side of the house, was made unsightly and untidy by the straw that had been wafted upon it through the open door and windows. The rooms had a strange echoing sound in them,—and the light came harshly and strongly in through the uncurtained windows,—seeming already unfamiliar and strange. Mrs. Hale’s dressing-room was left untouched to the last; and there she and Dixon were packing up clothes, and interrupting each other every now and then to exclaim at, and turn over with fond regard, some forgotten treasure, in the shape of some relic of the children while they were yet little. They did not make much progress with their work. Down-stairs, Margaret stood calm and collected, ready to counsel or advise the men who had been called in to help the cook and Charlotte. These two last, crying between whiles, wondered how the young lady could keep up so this last day, and settled it between them that she was not likely to care much for Helstone, having been so long in London. There she stood, very pale and quiet, with her large grave eyes observing everything—up to every present circumstance however small. They could not understand how her heart was aching all the time, with a heavy pressure that no sighs could lift off or relieve, and how constant exertion for her perceptive faculties was the only way to keep herself from crying out with pain. Moreover, if she gave way, who was to act? Her father was examining papers, books, registers, what not, in the vestry with the clerk; and when he came in, there were his own books to pack up, which no one but himself could do to his satisfaction. Besides, was Margaret one to give way before strange men, or even household friends like the cook and Charlotte? Not she! But at last the four packers went into the kitchen to their tea; and Margaret moved stiffly and slowly away from the place in the hall where she had been standing so long, out through the bare echoing drawing-room, into the twilight of an early November evening. There was a filmy veil of soft dull mist obscuring, but not hiding, all objects, giving them a lilac hue, for the sun had not yet fully set; a robin was singing,—perhaps, Margaret thought, the very robin that her father had so often talked of as his winter pet, and for which he had made, with his own hands, a kind of robin-house by his study-window. The leaves were more gorgeous than ever; the first touch of frost would lay them all low on the ground. Already one or two kept constantly floating down, amber and golden in the low slanting sun-rays.\n\nMargaret went along the walk under the pear-tree wall. She had never been along it since she paced it at Henry Lennox’s side. Here, at this bed of thyme, he began to speak of what she must not think of now. Her eyes were on that late-blowing rose as she was trying to answer; and she had caught the idea of the vivid beauty of the feathery leaves of the carrots in the very middle of his last sentence. Only a fortnight ago! And all so changed! Where was he now? In London,—going through the old round; dining with the old Harley Street set, or with gayer young friends of his own. Even now, while she walked sadly through that damp and drear garden in the dusk, with everything falling and fading, and turning to decay around her, he might be gladly putting away his law-books after a day of satisfactory toil, and freshening himself up, as he had told her he often did, by a run in the Temple Gardens, taking in the while the grand inarticulate mighty roar of tens of thousands of busy men, nigh at hand, but not seen, and catching ever, at his quick turns, glimpses of the lights of the city coming up out of the depths of the river. He had often spoken to Margaret of these hasty walks, snatched in the intervals between study and dinner. At his best times and in his best moods had he spoken of them; and the thought of them had struck upon her fancy. Here there was no sound. The robin had gone away into the vast stillness of night. Now and then, a cottage door in the distance was opened and shut, as if to admit the tired labourer to his home; but that sounded very far away. A stealthy, creeping, cranching sound among the crisp fallen leaves of the forest, beyond the garden, seemed almost close at hand. Margaret knew it was some poacher. Sitting up in her bedroom this past autumn, with the light of her candle extinguished, and purely revelling in the solemn beauty of the heavens and the earth, she had many a time seen the light noiseless leap of the poachers over the garden-fence, their quick tramp across the dewy moonlit lawn, their disappearance in the black still shadow beyond. The wild adventurous freedom of their life had taken her fancy; she felt inclined to wish them success; she had no fear of them. But to-night she was afraid, she knew not why. She heard Charlotte shutting the windows, and fastening up for the night, unconscious that any one had gone out into the garden. A small branch—it might be of rotten wood, or it might be broken by force—came heavily down in the nearest part of the forest; Margaret ran, swift as Camilla, down to the window, and rapped at it with a hurried tremulousness which startled Charlotte within.\n\n“Let me in! Let me in! It is only me, Charlotte!” Her heart did not still its fluttering till she was safe in the drawing-room, with the windows fastened and bolted, and the familiar walls hemming her round, and shutting her in. She had sate down upon a packing-case; cheerless, chill was the dreary and dismantled room—no fire, nor other light, but Charlotte’s long unsnuffed candle. Charlotte looked at Margaret with surprise; and Margaret, feeling it rather than seeing it, rose up.\n\n“I was afraid you were shutting me out altogether, Charlotte,” said she, half-smiling. “And then you would never have heard me in the kitchen, and the doors into the lane and churchyard are locked long ago.”\n\n“Oh, miss, I should have been sure to have missed you soon. The men would have wanted you to tell them how to go on. And I have put tea in master’s study, as being the most comfortable room, so to speak.”\n\n“Thank you, Charlotte. You are a kind girl. I shall be sorry to leave you. You must try and write to me, if I can ever give you any little help or good advice. I shall always be glad to get a letter from Helstone, you know. I shall be sure and send you my address when I know it.”\n\nThe study was all ready for tea. There was a good blazing fire, and unlighted candles on the table. Margaret sat down on the rug, partly to warm herself, for the dampness of the evening hung about her dress, and over-fatigue had made her chilly. She kept herself balanced by clasping her hands together round her knees; her head dropped a little towards her chest; the attitude was one of despondency, whatever her frame of mind might be. But when she heard her father’s step on the gravel outside, she started up, and hastily shaking her heavy black hair back, and wiping a few tears away that had come on her cheeks she knew not how, she went out to open the door for him. He showed far more depression than she did. She could hardly get him to talk, although she tried to speak on subjects that would interest him, at the cost of an effort every time which she thought would be her last.\n\n“Have you been a very long walk to-day?” asked she, on seeing his refusal to touch food of any kind.\n\n“As far as Fordham Beeches. I went to see Widow Maltby; she is sadly grieved at not having wished you good-bye. She says little Susan has kept watch down the lane for days past.—Nay, Margaret, what is the matter, dear?” The thought of the little child watching for her, and continually disappointed—from no forgetfulness on her part, but from sheer inability to leave home—was the last drop in poor Margaret’s cup, and she was sobbing away as if her heart would break. Mr. Hale was distressingly perplexed. He rose, and walked nervously up and down the room. Margaret tried to check herself, but would not speak until she could do so with firmness. She heard him talking, as if to himself.\n\n“I cannot bear it. I cannot bear to see the sufferings of others. I think I could go through my own with patience. Oh, is there no going back?”\n\n“No, father,” said Margaret, looking straight at him, and speaking low and steadily. “It is bad to believe you in error. It would be infinitely worse to have known you a hypocrite.” She dropped her voice at the last few words, as if entertaining the idea of hypocrisy for a moment in connection with her father savoured of irreverence.\n\n“Besides,” she went on, “it is only that I am tired to-night; don’t think that I am suffering from what you have done, dear papa. We can’t either of us talk about it to-night, I believe,” said she, finding that tears and sobs would come in spite of herself. “I had better go and take mamma up this cup of tea. She had hers very early, when I was too busy to go to her, and I am sure she will be glad of another now.”\n\nRailroad time inexorably wrenched them away from lovely, beloved Helstone, the next morning. They were gone; they had seen the last of the long low parsonage home, half-covered with China-roses and pyracanthus—more homelike than ever in the morning sun that glittered on its windows, each belonging to some well-loved room. Almost before they had settled themselves into the car, sent from Southampton to fetch them to the station, they were gone away to return no more. A sting at Margaret’s heart made her strive to look out to catch the last glimpse of the old church tower at the turn where she knew it might be seen above a wave of the forest trees; but her father remembered this too, and she silently acknowledged his greater right to the one window from which it could be seen. She leant back and shut her eyes, and the tears welled forth, and hung glittering for an instant on the shadowing eyelashes before rolling slowly down her cheeks, and dropping, unheeded, on her dress.\n\nThey were to stop in London all night at some quiet hotel. Poor Mrs. Hale had cried in her way nearly all day long; and Dixon showed her sorrow by extreme crossness, and a continual irritable attempt to keep her petticoats from even touching the unconscious Mr. Hale, whom she regarded as the origin of all this suffering.\n\nThey went through the well-known streets, past houses which they had often visited, past shops in which she had lounged, impatient, by her aunt’s side, while that lady was making some important and interminable decision—nay, absolutely passed acquaintances in the streets; for though the morning had been of an incalculable length to them, and they felt as if it ought long ago to have closed in for the repose of darkness, it was the very busiest time of a London afternoon in November when they arrived there. It was long since Mrs. Hale had been in London; and she roused up, almost like a child, to look about her at the different streets, and to gaze after and exclaim at the shops and carriages,—\n\n“Oh, there’s Harrison’s, where I bought so many of my wedding things. Dear! how altered! They’ve got immense plate-glass windows, larger than Crawford’s in Southampton. Oh, and there, I declare—no, it is not—yes, it is—Margaret, we have just passed Mr. Henry Lennox. Where can he be going, among all these shops?”\n\nMargaret started forwards, and as quickly fell back, half-smiling at herself for the sudden motion. They were a hundred yards away by this time; but he seemed like a relic of Helstone—he was associated with a bright morning, an eventful day, and she should have liked to have seen him, without his seeing her,—without the chance of their speaking.\n\nThe evening, without employment, passed in a room high up in an hotel, was long and heavy. Mr. Hale went out to his bookseller’s, and to call on a friend or two. Every one they saw, either in the house or out in the streets, appeared hurrying to some appointment, expected by, or expecting somebody. They alone seemed strange and friendless, and desolate. Yet within a mile, Margaret knew of house after house, where she for her own sake, and her mother for her aunt Shaw’s, would be welcomed, if they came in gladness, or even in peace of mind. If they came sorrowing, and wanting sympathy in a complicated trouble like the present, then they would be felt as a shadow in all these houses of intimate acquaintances, not friends. London life is too whirling and full to admit of even an hour of that deep silence of feeling which the friends of Job showed, when “they sat with him on the ground seven days and seven nights, and none spake a word unto him; for they saw that his grief was very great.”\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n“Mist clogs the sunshine, Smoky dwarf houses Hem me round everywhere.” MATTHEW ARNOLD.\n\nThe next afternoon, about twenty miles from Milton-Northern, they entered on the little branch railway that led to Heston. Heston itself was one long straggling street, running parallel to the seashore. It had a character of its own, as different from the little bathing-places in the south of England as they again from those of the continent. To use a Scotch word, everything looked more “purpose-like.” The country carts had more iron, and less wood and leather about the horse-gear; the people in the streets, although on pleasure bent, had yet a busy mind. The colours looked grayer—more enduring, not so gay and pretty. There were no smock-frocks, even among the country-folk; they retarded motion, and were apt to catch on machinery, and so the habit of wearing them had died out. In such towns in the south of England, Margaret had seen the shopmen, when not employed in their business, lounging a little at their doors, enjoying the fresh air, and the look up and down the street. Here, if they had any leisure from customers, they made themselves business in the shop—even, Margaret fancied, to the unnecessary unrolling and re-rolling of ribbons. All these differences struck upon her mind, as she and her mother went out next morning to look for lodgings.\n\nTheir two nights at hotels had cost more than Mr. Hale had anticipated, and they were glad to take the first clean, cheerful rooms they met with that were at liberty to receive them. There, for the first time for many days, did Margaret feel at rest. There was a dreaminess in the rest, too, which made it still more perfect and luxurious to repose in. The distant sea, lapped the sandy shore with measured sound; the nearer cries of the donkey-boys; the unusual scenes moving before her like pictures, which she cared not in her laziness to have fully explained before they passed away; the stroll down to the beach to breathe the sea-air, soft and warm on that sandy shore even to the end of November; the great long misty sea-line touching the tender-coloured sky; the white sail of a distant boat turning silver in some pale sunbeam:—it seemed as if she could dream her life away in such luxury of pensiveness, in which she made her present all in all, from not daring to think of the past, or wishing to contemplate the future.\n\nBut the future must be met, however stern and iron it be. One evening it was arranged that Margaret and her father should go the next day to Milton-Northern, and look out for a house. Mr. Hale had received several letters from Mr. Bell, and one or two from Mr. Thornton, and he was anxious to ascertain at once a good many particulars respecting his position and chances of success there, which he could only do by an interview with the latter gentleman. Margaret knew that they ought to be removing; but she had a repugnance to the idea of a manufacturing town, and believed that her mother was receiving benefit from Heston air, so she would willingly have deferred the expedition to Milton.\n\nFor several miles before they reached Milton, they saw a deep lead-coloured cloud hanging over the horizon in the direction in which it lay. It was all the darker from contrast with the pale gray-blue of the wintry sky; for in Heston there had been the earliest signs of frost. Nearer to the town, the air had a faint taste and smell of smoke; perhaps, after all, more a loss of the fragrance of grass and herbage than any positive taste or smell. Quick they were whirled over long, straight, hopeless streets of regularly-built houses, all small and of brick. Here and there a great oblong many-windowed factory stood up, like a hen among her chickens, puffing out black “unparliamentary” smoke, and sufficiently accounting for the cloud which Margaret had taken to foretell rain. As they drove through the larger and wider streets, from the station to the hotel, they had to stop constantly; great loaded lurries blocked up the not over-wide thoroughfares. Margaret had now and then been into the city in her drives with her aunt. But there the heavy lumbering vehicles seemed various in their purposes and intent; here every van, every waggon and truck, bore cotton, either in the raw shape in bags, or the woven shape in bales of calico. People thronged the footpaths, most of them well-dressed as regarded the material, but with a slovenly looseness which struck Margaret as different from the shabby, threadbare smartness of a similar class in London.\n\n“New Street,” said Mr. Hale. “This, I believe, is the principal street in Milton. Bell has often spoken to me about it. It was the opening of this street from a lane into a great thoroughfare, thirty years ago, which has caused his property to rise so much in value. Mr. Thornton’s mill must be somewhere not very far off, for he is Mr. Bell’s tenant. But I fancy he dates from his warehouse.”\n\n“Where is our hotel, papa?”\n\n“Close to the end of this street, I believe. Shall we have lunch before or after we have looked at the houses we marked in the Milton Times?”\n\n“Oh, let us get our work done first.”\n\n“Very well. Then I will only see if there is any note or letter for me from Mr. Thornton, who said he would let me know anything he might hear about these houses, and then we will set off. We will keep the cab; it will be safer than losing ourselves, and being too late for the train this afternoon.”\n\nThere were no letters awaiting him. They set out on their house-hunting. Thirty pounds a-year was all they could afford to give, but in Hampshire they could have met with a roomy house and pleasant garden for the money. Here, even the necessary accommodation of two sitting-rooms and four bed-rooms seemed unattainable. They went through their list, rejecting each as they visited it. They then looked at each other in dismay.\n\n“We must go back to the second, I think. That one,—in Crampton, don’t they call the suburb? There were three sitting-rooms; don’t you remember how we laughed at the number compared with the three bed-rooms? But I have planned it all. The front room down-stairs is to be your study and our dining-room (poor papa!), for you know, we settled mamma is to have as cheerful a sitting-room as we can get; and that front room up-stairs, with the atrocious blue and pink paper and heavy cornice, had really a pretty view over the plain, with a great bend of river, or canal, or whatever it is, down below. Then I could have the little bed-room behind, in that projection at the head of the first flight of stairs over the kitchen, you know—and you and mamma the room behind the drawing-room, and that closet in the roof will make you a splendid dressing-room.”\n\n“But Dixon, and the girl we are to have to help?”\n\n“Oh, wait a minute. I am overpowered by the discovery of my own genius for management. Dixon is to have—let me see, I had it once—the back sitting-room. I think she will like that. She grumbles so much about the stairs at Heston; and the girl is to have that sloping attic over your room and mamma’s. Won’t that do?”\n\n“I dare say it will. But the papers. What taste! And the over-loading such a house with colour and such heavy cornices!”\n\n“Never mind, papa! Surely you can charm the landlord into re-papering one or two of the rooms—the drawing-room and your bed-room—for mamma will come most in contact with them; and your book-shelves will hide a great deal of that gaudy pattern in the dining-room.”\n\n“Then you think it the best? If so, I had better go at once and call on this Mr. Donkin, to whom the advertisement refers me. I will take you back to the hotel, where you can order lunch, and rest, and by the time it is ready, I shall be with you. I hope I shall be able to get new papers.”\n\nMargaret hoped so too, though she said nothing. She had never come fairly in contact with the taste that loves ornament, however bad, more than the plainness and simplicity which are of themselves the framework of elegance.\n\nHer father took her through the entrance of the hotel, and leaving her at the foot of the staircase, went to the address of the landlord of the house they had fixed upon. Just as Margaret had her hand on the door of their sitting-room, she was followed by a quick-stepping waiter:\n\n“I beg your pardon, ma’am. The gentleman was gone so quickly, I had no time to tell him. Mr. Thornton called almost directly after you left; and as I understood, from what the gentleman said, you would be back in an hour, I told him so, and he came again about five minutes ago, and said he would wait for Mr. Hale. He is in your room now, ma’am.”\n\n“Thank you. My father will return soon, and then you can tell him.”\n\nMargaret opened the door and went in with the straight, fearless, dignified presence habitual to her. She felt no awkwardness; she had too much the habits of society for that. Here was a person come on business to her father; and, as he was one who had shown himself obliging, she was disposed to treat him with a full measure of civility. Mr. Thornton was a good deal more surprised and discomfited than she. Instead of a quiet, middle-aged clergyman, a young lady came forward with frank dignity,—a young lady of a different type to most of those he was in the habit of seeing. Her dress was very plain: a close straw bonnet of the best material and shape, trimmed with white ribbon; a large Indian shawl, which hung about her in long heavy folds, and which she wore as an empress wears her drapery. He did not understand who she was, as he caught the simple, straight, unabashed look, which showed that his being there was of no concern to the beautiful countenance, and called up no flush of surprise to the pale ivory of the complexion. He had heard that Mr. Hale had a daughter, but he had imagined that she was a little girl.\n\n“Mr. Thornton, I believe!” said Margaret, after a half-instant’s pause, during which his unready words would not come. “Will you sit down. My father brought me to the door not a minute ago, but unfortunately he was not told that you were here, and he has gone away on some business. But he will come back almost directly. I am sorry you have had the trouble of calling twice.”\n\nMr. Thornton was in habits of authority himself, but she seemed to assume some kind of rule over him at once. He had been getting impatient at the loss of his time on a market-day, the moment before she appeared, yet now he calmly took a seat at her bidding.\n\n“Do you know where it is that Mr. Hale has gone to? Perhaps I might be able to find him.”\n\n“He has gone to a Mr. Donkin in Canute Street. He is the landlord of the house my father wishes to take in Crampton.”\n\nMr. Thornton knew the house. He had seen the advertisement, and been to look at it, in compliance with a request of Mr. Bell’s that he would assist Mr. Hale to the best of his power: and also instigated by his own interest in the case of a clergyman who had given up his living under circumstances such as those of Mr. Hale. Mr. Thornton had thought that the house in Crampton was really just the thing; but now that he saw Margaret, with her superb ways of moving and looking, he began to feel ashamed of having imagined that it would do very well for the Hales, in spite of a certain vulgarity in it which had struck him at the time of his looking it over.\n\nMargaret could not help her looks; but the short curled upper lip, the round, massive up-turned chin, the manner of carrying her head, her movements, full of a soft feminine defiance, always gave strangers the impression of haughtiness. She was tired now, and would much rather have remained silent, and taken the rest her father had planned for her; but, of course, she owed to herself to be a gentlewoman, and to speak courteously from time to time to this stranger; not over-brushed, nor over-polished, it must be confessed, after his rough encounter with Milton streets and crowds. She wished that he would go, as he had once spoken of doing, instead of sitting there, answering with curt sentences all the remarks she made. She had taken off her shawl, and hung it over the back of her chair. She sat facing him and facing the light; her full beauty met his eye; her round white flexile throat rising out of the full, yet lithe figure; her lips moving so slightly as she spoke, not breaking the cold serene look of her face with any variation from the one lovely haughty curve; her eyes, with their soft gloom, meeting his with quiet maiden freedom. He almost said to himself that he did not like her, before their conversation ended; he tried so to compensate himself for the mortified feeling, that while he looked upon her with an admiration he could not repress, she looked at him with proud indifference, taking him, he thought, for what, in his irritation, he told himself he was—a great rough fellow, with not a grace or a refinement about him. Her quiet coldness of demeanour he interpreted into contemptuousness, and resented it in his heart to the pitch of almost inclining him to get up and go away, and have nothing more to do with these Hales, and their superciliousness.\n\nJust as Margaret had exhausted her last subject of conversation—and yet conversation that could hardly be called which consisted of so few and such short speeches—her father came in, and with his pleasant gentlemanly courteousness of apology, reinstated his name and family in Mr. Thornton’s good opinion.\n\nMr. Hale and his visitor had a good deal to say respecting their mutual friend, Mr. Bell; and Margaret, glad that her part of entertaining the visitor was over, went to the window to try and make herself more familiar with the strange aspect of the street. She got so much absorbed in watching what was going on outside that she hardly heard her father when he spoke to her, and he had to repeat what he said:\n\n“Margaret! the landlord will persist in admiring that hideous paper, and I am afraid we must let it remain.”\n\n“Oh dear! I am sorry!” she replied, and began to turn over in her mind the possibility of hiding part of it, at least, by some of her sketches, but gave up the idea at last, as likely only to make bad worse. Her father, meanwhile, with his kindly country hospitality, was pressing Mr. Thornton to stay to luncheon with them. It would have been very inconvenient to him to do so, yet he felt that he should have yielded, if Margaret by word or look had seconded her father’s invitation; he was glad she did not, and yet he was irritated at her for not doing so. She gave him a low, grave bow when he left, and he felt more awkward and self-conscious in every limb than he had ever done in his life before.\n\n“Well, Margaret, now to luncheon as fast as we can. Have you ordered it?”\n\n“No, papa; that man was here when I came home, and I have never had an opportunity.”\n\n“Then we must take anything we can get. He must have been waiting a long time I’m afraid.”\n\n“It seemed exceedingly long to me. I was just at the last gasp when you came in. He never went on with any subject, but gave little, short, abrupt answers.”\n\n“Very much to the point though, I should think. He is a clear-headed fellow. He said (did you hear?) that Crampton is on gravelly soil, and by far the most healthy suburb in the neighbourhood of Milton.”\n\nWhen they returned to Heston, there was the day’s account to be given to Mrs. Hale, who was full of questions, which they answered in the intervals of tea-drinking.\n\n“And what is your correspondent, Mr. Thornton, like?”\n\n“Ask Margaret,” said her husband. “She and he had a long attempt at conversation, while I was away speaking to the landlord.”\n\n“Oh! I hardly know what he is like,” said Margaret, lazily; too tired to tax her powers of description much. And then rousing herself, she said, “He is a tall, broad-shouldered man, about—how old, papa?”\n\n“I should guess about thirty.”\n\n“About thirty—with a face that is neither exactly plain, nor yet handsome, nothing remarkable—not quite a gentleman; but that was hardly to be expected.”\n\n“Not vulgar or common though,” put in her father, rather jealous of any disparagement of the sole friend he had in Milton.\n\n“Oh, no!” said Margaret. “With such an expression of resolution and power, no face, however plain in feature, could be either vulgar or common. I should not like to have to bargain with him; he looks very inflexible. Altogether a man who seems made for his niche, mamma; sagacious, and strong, as becomes a great tradesman.”\n\n“Don’t call the Milton manufacturers tradesmen, Margaret,” said her father. “They are very different.”\n\n“Are they? I apply the word to all who have something tangible to sell; but if you think the term is not correct, papa, I won’t use it. But, oh mamma! speaking of vulgarity and commonness, you must prepare yourself for our drawing-room paper. Pink and blue roses, with yellow leaves! And such a heavy cornice round the room!”\n\nBut when they removed to their new house in Milton, the obnoxious papers were gone. The landlord received their thanks very composedly; and let them think, if they liked, that he had relented from his expressed determination not to repaper. There was no particular need to tell them, that what he did not care to do for a Reverend Mr. Hale, unknown in Milton, he was only too glad to do at the one short sharp remonstrance of Mr. Thornton, the wealthy manufacturer.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n“And it’s hame, hame, hame, Hame fain wad I be.”\n\nIt needed the pretty light papering of the rooms to reconcile them to Milton. It needed more—more that could not be had. The thick yellow November fogs had come on; and the view of the plain in the valley, made by the sweeping bend of the river, was all shut out when Mrs. Hale arrived at her new home.\n\nMargaret and Dixon had been at work two days, unpacking and arranging, but everything inside the house still looked in disorder; and outside a thick fog crept up to the very windows, and was driven in to every open door in choking white wreaths of unwholsome mist.\n\n“Oh, Margaret! are we to live here?” asked Mrs. Hale in blank dismay.\n\nMargaret’s heart echoed the dreariness of the tone in which this question was put. She could scarcely command herself enough to say, “Oh, the fogs in London are sometimes far worse!”\n\n“But then you knew that London itself, and friends lay behind it. Here—well! we are desolate. Oh Dixon, what a place this is!”\n\n“Indeed, ma’am, I’m sure it will be your death before long, and then I know who’ll—stay! Miss Hale, that’s far too heavy for you to lift.”\n\n“Not at all, thank you, Dixon,” replied Margaret coldly. “The best thing we can do for mamma is to get her room quite ready for her to go to bed, while I go and bring her a cup of coffee.”\n\nMr. Hale was equally out of spirits, and equally came upon Margaret for sympathy.\n\n“Margaret, I do believe this is an unhealthy place. Only suppose that your mother’s health or yours should suffer. I wish I had gone into some country place in Wales; this is really terrible,” said he, going up to the window.\n\nThere was no comfort to be given. They were settled in Milton, and must endure smoke and fogs for a season; indeed, all other life seemed shut out from them by as thick a fog of circumstance. Only the day before, Mr. Hale had been reckoning up with dismay how much their removal and fortnight at Heston had cost, and he found it had absorbed nearly all his little stock of ready money. No! here they were, and here they must remain.\n\nAt night when Margaret realised this, she felt inclined to sit down in a stupor of despair. The heavy smoky air hung about her bedroom, which occupied the long narrow projection at the back of the house. The window, placed at the side of the oblong, looked at the blank wall of a similar projection, not above ten feet distant. It loomed through the fog like a great barrier to hope. Inside the room everything was in confusion. All their efforts had been directed to make her mother’s room comfortable. Margaret sat down on a box, the direction card upon which struck her as having been written at Helstone—beautiful, beloved Helstone! She lost herself in dismal thought: but at last she determined to take her mind away from the present; and suddenly remembered that she had a letter from Edith which she had only half read in the bustle of the morning. It was to tell of their arrival at Corfu; their voyage along the Mediterranean—their music, and dancing on board ship; the gay new life opening upon her; her house with its trellised balcony, and its views over white cliffs and deep blue sea.\n\nEdith wrote fluently and well, if not graphically. She could not only seize the salient and characteristic points of a scene, but she could enumerate enough of indiscriminate particular for Margaret to make it out for herself. Captain Lennox and another lately married officer shared a villa, high up on the beautiful precipitous rocks overhanging the sea. Their days, late as it was in the year, seemed spent in boating or land-picnics; all out-of-doors, pleasure-seeking and glad, Edith’s life seemed like the deep vault of blue sky above her, free—utterly free from fleck or cloud. Her husband had to attend drill, and she, the most musical officer’s wife there, had to copy the new and popular tunes out of the most recent English music, for the benefit of the bandmaster; those seemed their most severe and arduous duties. She expressed an affectionate hope that, if the regiment stopped another year at Corfu, Margaret might come out and pay her a long visit. She asked Margaret if she remembered the day twelve-month on which she, Edith, wrote—how it rained all day long in Harley Street; and how she would not put on her new gown to go to a stupid dinner, and get it all wet and splashed in going to the carriage; and how at that very dinner they had first met Captain Lennox.\n\nYes! Margaret remembered it well. Edith and Mrs. Shaw had gone to dinner. Margaret had joined the party in the evening. The recollection of the plentiful luxury of all arrangements, the stately handsomeness of the furniture, the size of the house, the peaceful, untroubled ease of the visitors—all came vividly before her, in strange contrast to the present time. The smooth sea of that old life closed up, without a mark left to tell where they had all been. The habitual dinners, the calls, the dancing evenings, were all going on, going on for ever, though her Aunt Shaw and Edith were no longer there; and she, of course, was even less missed. She doubted if any one of that old set ever thought of her, except Henry Lennox. He too, she knew, would strive to forget her, because of the pain she had caused him. She had heard him often boast of his power of putting any disagreeable thought far away from him. Then she penetrated farther into what might have been. If she had cared for him as a lover, and had accepted him, and this change in her father’s opinions and consequent station had taken place, she could not doubt but that it would have been impatiently received by Mr. Lennox. It was a bitter mortification to her in one sense; but she could bear it patiently, because she knew her father’s purity of purpose, and that strengthened her to endure his errors, grave and serious though in her estimation they were. But the fact of the world esteeming her father degraded, in its rough wholesale judgment, would have oppressed and irritated Mr. Lennox. As she realised what might have been, she grew to be thankful for what was. They were at the lowest now; they could not be worse. Edith’s astonishment and her Aunt Shaw’s dismay would have to be met bravely, when their letters came. So Margaret rose up and began slowly to undress herself, feeling the full luxury of acting leisurely, late as it was, after all the past hurry of the day. She fell asleep, hoping for some brightness, either internal or external. But if she had known how long it would be before the brightness came, her heart would have sunk low down. The time of the year was most unpropitious to health as well as to spirits. Her mother caught a severe cold, and Dixon herself was evidently not well, although Margaret could not insult her more than by trying to save her, or by taking any care of her. They could hear of no girl to assist her; all were at work in the factories; at least, those who applied were well scolded by Dixon, for thinking that such as they could ever be trusted to work in a gentleman’s house. So they had to keep a charwoman in almost constant employ. Margaret longed to send for Charlotte; but besides the objection of her being a better servant than they now could afford to keep, the distance was too great.\n\nMr. Hale met with several pupils, recommended to him by Mr. Bell, or by the more immediate influence of Mr. Thornton. They were mostly of the age when many boys would be still at school, but, according to the prevalent, and, apparently well-founded notions of Milton, to make a lad into a good tradesman he must be caught young, and acclimated to the life of the mill, or office, or warehouse. If he were sent to even the Scotch Universities, he came back unsettled for commercial pursuits: how much more so if he went to Oxford or Cambridge, where he could not be entered till he was eighteen? So most of the manufacturers placed their sons in situations at from fourteen or fifteen years of age, unsparingly cutting away all off-shoots in the direction of literature or high mental cultivation, in hopes of throwing the whole strength and vigour of the plant into commerce. Still there were some wiser parents; and some who had sense enough to perceive their own deficiencies, and strive to remedy them. Nay, there were a few no longer youths, but men in the prime of life, who had the stern wisdom to acknowledge their own ignorance, and to learn late what they should have learnt early. Mr. Thornton was perhaps the oldest of Mr. Hale’s pupils. He was certainly the favourite. Mr. Hale got into the habit of quoting his opinions so frequently, and with such regard, that it became a little domestic joke to wonder what time, during the hour appointed for instruction, could be given to absolute learning, so much of it appeared to have been spent in conversation.\n\nMargaret rather encouraged this light, merry way of viewing her father’s acquaintance with Mr. Thornton, because she felt that her mother was inclined to look upon this new friendship of her husband’s with jealous eyes. As long as his time had been solely occupied with his books and his parishioners, as at Helstone, she had appeared to care little whether she saw much of him or not; but now that he looked eagerly forward to each renewal of his intercourse with Mr. Thornton, she seemed hurt and annoyed, as if he were slighting her companionship for the first time. Mr. Hale’s over-praise had the usual effect of over-praise upon his auditors; they were a little inclined to rebel against Aristides being always called the Just.\n\nAfter a quiet life in a country parsonage for more than twenty years, there was something dazzling to Mr. Hale in the energy which conquered immense difficulties with ease; the power of the machinery of Milton, the power of the men of Milton, impressed him with a sense of grandeur, which he yielded to without caring to inquire into the details of its exercise. But Margaret went less abroad, among machinery and men; saw less of power in its public effect, and, as it happened, she was thrown with one or two of those who, in all measures affecting masses of people, must be acute sufferers for the good of many. The question always is, has everything been done to make the sufferings of these exceptions as small as possible? or, in the triumph of the crowded procession, have the helpless been trampled on, instead of being gently lifted aside out of the roadway of the conqueror, whom they have no power to accompany on his march?\n\nIt fell to Margaret’s share to have to look out for a servant to assist Dixon, who had at first undertaken to find just the person she wanted to do all the rough work of the house. But Dixon’s ideas of helpful girls were founded on the recollection of tidy elder scholars at Helstone school, who were only too proud to be allowed to come to the parsonage on a busy day, and treated Mrs. Dixon with all the respect, and a good deal more of fright, which they paid to Mr. and Mrs. Hale. Dixon was not unconscious of this awed reverence which was given to her; nor did she dislike it: it flattered her much as Louis the Fourteenth was flattered by his courtiers shading their eyes from the dazzling light of his presence. But nothing short of her faithful love for Mrs. Hale could have made her endure the rough independent way in which all the Milton girls, who made application for the servant’s place, replied to her inquiries respecting their qualifications. They even went the length of questioning her back again; having doubts and fears of their own, as to the solvency of a family who lived in a house of thirty pounds a-year, and yet gave themselves airs, and kept two servants, one of them so very high and mighty. Mr. Hale was no longer looked upon as Vicar of Helstone, but as a man who only spent at a certain rate. Margaret was weary and impatient of the accounts which Dixon perpetually brought to Mrs. Hale of the behaviour of these would-be servants. Not but what Margaret was repelled by the rough uncourteous manners of these people; not but what she shrunk with fastidious pride from their hail-fellow accost, and severely resented their unconcealed curiosity as to the means and position of any family who lived in Milton, and yet were not engaged in trade of some kind. But the more Margaret felt impertinence, the more likely she was to be silent on the subject; and, at any rate, if she took upon herself to make inquiry for a servant, she could spare her mother the recital of all her disappointments and fancied or real insults.\n\nMargaret accordingly went up and down to butchers and grocers, seeking for a nonpareil of a girl; and lowering her hopes and expectations every week, as she found the difficulty of meeting with any one in a manufacturing town who did not prefer the better wages and greater independence of working in a mill. It was something of a trial to Margaret to go out by herself in this busy bustling place. Mrs. Shaw’s ideas of propriety and her own helpless dependence on others, had always made her insist that a footman should accompany Edith and Margaret, if they went beyond Harley Street or the immediate neighbourhood. The limits by which this rule of her aunt’s had circumscribed Margaret’s independence had been silently rebelled against at the time: and she had doubly enjoyed the free walks and rambles of her forest life from the contrast which they presented. She went along there with a boundless fearless step, that occasionally broke out into a run, if she were in a hurry, and occasionally was stilled into perfect repose, as she stood listening to, or watching any of the wild creatures who sang in the leafy courts, or glanced out with their keen bright eyes from the low brushwood or tangled furze. It was a trial to come down from such motion or such stillness, only guided by her own sweet will, to the even and decorous pace necessary in streets. But she could have laughed at herself for minding this change, if it had not been accompanied by what was a more serious annoyance.\n\nThe side of the town on which Crampton lay was especially a thoroughfare for the factory people. In the back streets around them there were many mills, out of which poured streams of men and women two or three times a day. Until Margaret had learnt the times of their ingress and egress, she was very unfortunate in constantly falling in with them. They came rushing along, with bold, fearless faces, and loud laughs and jests, particularly aimed at all those who appeared to be above them in rank or station. The tones of their unrestrained voices, and their carelessness of all common rules of street politeness, frightened Margaret a little at first. The girls, with their rough, but not unfriendly freedom, would comment on her dress, even touch her shawl or gown to ascertain the exact material; nay, once or twice she was asked questions relative to some article which they particularly admired. There was such a simple reliance on her womanly sympathy with their love of dress, and on her kindliness, that she gladly replied to these inquiries, as soon as she understood them; and half smiled back at their remarks. She did not mind meeting any number of girls, loud spoken and boisterous though they might be. But she alternately dreaded and fired up against the workmen, who commented not on her dress, but on her looks, in the same open, fearless manner. She, who had hitherto felt that even the most refined remark on her personal appearance was an impertinence, had to endure undisguised admiration from these outspoken men. But the very outspokenness marked their innocence of any intention to hurt her delicacy, as she would have perceived if she had been less frightened by the disorderly tumult. Out of her fright came a flash of indignation which made her face scarlet, and her dark eyes gather flame, as she heard some of their speeches. Yet there were other sayings of theirs, which, when she reached the quiet safety of home, amused her even while they irritated her.\n\nFor instance, one day, after she had passed a number of men, several of whom had paid her the not unusual compliment of wishing she was their sweetheart, one of the lingerers added, “Your bonny face, my lass, makes the day look brighter.” And another day, as she was unconsciously smiling at some passing thought, she was addressed by a poorly-dressed, middle-aged workman, with, “You may well smile, my lass; many a one would smile to have such a bonny face.” This man looked so careworn that Margaret could not help giving him an answering smile, glad to think that her looks, such as they were, should have had the power to call up a pleasant thought. He seemed to understand her acknowledging glance, and a silent recognition was established between them whenever the chances of the day brought them across each other’s paths. They had never exchanged a word; nothing had been said but that first compliment; yet somehow Margaret looked upon this man with more interest than upon any one else in Milton. Once or twice, on Sundays, she saw him walking with a girl, evidently his daughter, and, if possible, still more unhealthy than he was himself.\n\nOne day Margaret and her father had been as far as the fields that lay around the town; it was early spring, and she had gathered some of the hedge and ditch flowers, dog-violets, lesser celandines, and the like, with an unspoken lament in her heart for the sweet profusion of the South. Her father had left her to go into Milton upon some business; and on the road home she met her humble friends. The girl looked wistfully at the flowers, and, acting on a sudden impulse, Margaret offered them to her. Her pale blue eyes lightened up as she took them, and her father spoke for her.\n\n“Thank yo, Miss. Bessy’ll think a deal o’ them flowers; that hoo will; and I shall think a deal o’ yor kindness. Yo’re not of this country, I reckon?”\n\n“No!” said Margaret, half sighing. “I come from the South—from Hampshire,” she continued, a little afraid of wounding his consciousness of ignorance, if she used a name which he did not understand.\n\n“That’s beyond London, I reckon? And I come fro’ Burnleyways, and forty miles to th’ North. And yet, yo see, North and South has both met and made kind o’ friends in this big smoky place.”\n\nMargaret had slackened her pace to a walk alongside of the man and his daughter, whose steps were regulated by the feebleness of the latter. She now spoke to the girl, and there was a sound of tender pity in the tone of her voice as she did so that went right to the heart of the father.\n\n“I’m afraid you are not very strong.”\n\n“No,” said the girl, “nor never will be.”\n\n“Spring is coming,” said Margaret, as if to suggest pleasant, hopeful thoughts.\n\n“Spring nor summer will do me good,” said the girl quietly.\n\nMargaret looked up at the man, almost expecting some contradiction from him, or at least some remark that would modify his daughter’s utter hopelessness. But instead, he added—\n\n“I’m afeared hoo speaks the truth. I’m afeared hoo’s too far gone in a waste.”\n\n“I shall have a spring where I am boun’ to, and flowers, and amaranths, and shining robes besides.”\n\n“Poor lass, poor lass!” said her father in a low tone. “I’m none so sure o’ that; but it’s a comfort to thee, poor lass, poor lass. Poor father! it’ll be soon.”\n\nMargaret was shocked by his words—shocked but not repelled; rather attracted and interested.\n\n“Where do you live? I think we must be neighbours, we meet so often on this road.”\n\n“We put up at nine Frances Street, second turn to th’ left at after yo’ve past th’ Goulden Dragon.”\n\n“And your name? I must not forget that.”\n\n“I’m none ashamed o’ my name. It’s Nicholas Higgins. Hoo’s called Bessy Higgins. Whatten yo’ asking for?”\n\nMargaret was surprised at this last question, for at Helstone it would have been an understood thing, after the enquiries she had made, that she intended to come and call upon any poor neighbour whose name and habitation she had asked for.\n\n“I thought—I meant to come and see you.” She suddenly felt rather shy of offering the visit, without having any reason to give for her wish to make it, beyond a kindly interest in a stranger. It seemed all at once to take the shape of an impertinence on her part; she read this meaning too in the man’s eyes.\n\n“I’m none so fond of having strange folk in my house.” But then relenting, as he saw her heightened colour, he added, “Yo’re a foreigner, as one may say, and maybe don’t know many folk here, and yo’ve given my wench here flowers out of yo’r own hand;—yo may come if yo like.”\n\nMargaret was half amused, half-nettled at this answer. She was not sure if she would go where permission was given so like a favour conferred. But when they came to the turn into Frances Street, the girl stopped a minute, and said,\n\n“Yo’ll not forget yo’re to come and see us.”\n\n“Aye, aye,” said the father, impatiently, “hoo’ll come. Hoo’s a bit set up now, because hoo thinks I might ha’ spoken more civilly; but hoo’ll think better on it, and come. I can read her proud bonny face like a book. Come along, Bess; there’s the mill bell ringing.”\n\nMargaret went home, wondering at her new friends, and smiling at the man’s insight into what had been passing in her mind. From that day Milton became a brighter place to her. It was not the long, bleak sunny days of spring, nor yet was it that time was reconciling her to the town of her habitation. It was that in it she had found a human interest.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n“Let China’s earth, enriched with coloured stains, Pencil’d with gold, and streaked with azure veins, The grateful flavour of the Indian leaf, Or Mocha’s sunburnt berry glad receive.” MRS. BARBOULD.\n\nThe day after this meeting with Higgins and his daughter Mr. Hale came upstairs into the little drawing-room at an unusual hour. He went up to different objects in the room, as if examining them, but Margaret saw that it was merely a nervous trick—a way of putting off something he wished, yet feared to say. Out it came at last—\n\n“My dear! I’ve asked Mr. Thornton to come to tea to-night.”\n\nMrs. Hale was leaning back in her easy chair, with her eyes shut, and an expression of pain on her face which had become habitual to her of late. But she roused up into querulousness at this speech of her husband’s.\n\n“Mr. Thornton!—and to-night! What in the world does the man want to come here for? And Dixon is washing my muslins and laces, and there is no soft water with these horrid east winds, which I suppose we shall have all the year round in Milton.”\n\n“The wind is veering round, my dear,” said Mr. Hale, looking out at the smoke, which drifted right from the east, only he did not yet understand the points of the compass, and rather arranged them ad libitum according to circumstances.\n\n“Don’t tell me!” said Mrs. Hale, shuddering up, and wrapping her shawl about her still more closely. “But, east or west wind, I suppose this man comes.”\n\n“Oh mamma, that shows you never saw Mr. Thornton. He looks like a person who would enjoy battling with every adverse thing he could meet with—enemies, winds, or circumstances. The more it rains and blows, the more certain we are to have him. But I’ll go and help Dixon. I’m getting to be a famous clear-starcher. And he won’t want any amusement beyond talking to Papa. Papa, I am really longing to see the Pythias to your Damon. You know I never saw him but once, and then we were so puzzled to know what to say to each other that we did not get on particularly well.”\n\n“I don’t know that you would ever like him, or think him agreeable, Margaret. He is not a lady’s man.”\n\nMargaret wreathed her throat in a scornful curve.\n\n“I don’t particularly admire ladies’ men, papa. But Mr. Thornton comes here as your friend—as one who has appreciated you”—\n\n“The only person in Milton,” said Mrs. Hale.\n\n“So we will give him a welcome, and some cocoa-nut cakes. Dixon will be flattered if we ask her to make some; and I will undertake to iron your caps, mamma.”\n\nMany a time that morning did Margaret wish Mr. Thornton far enough away. She had planned other employments for herself: a letter to Edith, a good piece of Dante, a visit to the Higginses. But, instead, she ironed away, listening to Dixon’s complaints, and only hoping that by an excess of sympathy she might prevent her from carrying the recital of her sorrows to Mrs. Hale. Every now and then, Margaret had to remind herself of her father’s regard for Mr. Thornton, to subdue the irritation of weariness that was stealing over her, and bringing on one of the bad headaches to which she had lately become liable. She could hardly speak when she sat down at last, and told her mother that she was no longer Peggy the laundry-maid, but Margaret Hale the lady. She meant this speech for a little joke, and was vexed enough with her busy tongue when she found her mother taking it seriously.\n\n“Yes! if any one had told me, when I was Miss Beresford, and one of the belles of the country, that a child of mine would have to stand half a day, in a little poky kitchen, working away like any servant, that we might prepare properly for the reception of a tradesman, and that this tradesman should be the only”—\n\n“Oh, mamma!” said Margaret, lifting herself up, “don’t punish me for so careless a speech. I don’t mind ironing, or any kind of work for you and papa. I am myself a born and bred lady through it all, even though it comes to scouring a floor, or washing dishes. I am tired now, just for a little while; but in half an hour I shall be ready to do the same over again. And as to Mr. Thornton’s being in trade, why he can’t help that now, poor fellow. I don’t suppose his education would fit him for much else.” Margaret lifted herself slowly up, and went to her own room; for just now she could not bear much more.\n\nIn Mr. Thornton’s house, at this very same time, a similar, yet different scene was going on. A large-boned lady, long past middle age, sat at work in a grim handsomely-furnished dining-room. Her features, like her frame, were strong and massive, rather than heavy. Her face moved slowly from one decided expression to another equally decided. There was no great variety in her countenance; but those who looked at it once, generally looked at it again; even the passers by in the street, half-turned their heads to gaze an instant longer at the firm, severe, dignified woman, who never gave way in street-courtesy, or paused in her straight-onward course to the clearly defined end which she proposed to herself.\n\nShe was handsomely dressed in stout black silk, of which not a thread was worn or discoloured. She was mending a large, long table-cloth of the finest texture, holding it up against the light occasionally to discover thin places, which required her delicate care. There was not a book about in the room, with the exception of Matthew Henry’s Bible commentaries, six volumes of which lay in the centre of the massive side-board, flanked by a tea-urn on one side, and a lamp on the other. In some remote apartment, there was exercise upon the piano going on. Someone was practising up a morceau de salon, playing it very rapidly, every third note, on an average, being either indistinct, or wholly missed out, and the loud chords at the end being half of them false, but not the less satisfactory to the performer. Mrs. Thornton heard a step, like her own in its decisive character, pass the dining-room door.\n\n“John! Is that you?”\n\nHer son opened the door, and showed himself.\n\n“What has brought you home so early? I thought you were going to tea with that friend of Mr. Bell’s; that Mr. Hale?”\n\n“So I am, mother. I am come home to dress!”\n\n“Dress! humph! When I was a girl, young men were satisfied with dressing once in a day. Why should you dress to go and take a cup of tea with an old parson?”\n\n“Mr. Hale is a gentleman, and his wife and daughter are ladies.”\n\n“Wife and daughter! Do they teach too? What do they do? You have never mentioned them.”\n\n“No! mother, because I have never seen Mrs. Hale; I have only seen Miss Hale for half an hour.”\n\n“Take care you don’t get caught by a penniless girl, John.”\n\n“I am not easily caught, mother, as I think you know. But I must not have Miss Hale spoken of in that way, which, you know, is offensive to me. I never was aware of any young lady trying to catch me yet, nor do I believe that any one has ever given themselves that useless trouble.”\n\nMrs. Thornton did not choose to yield the point to her son; or else she had, in general, pride enough for her sex.\n\n“Well! I only say, take care. Perhaps our Milton girls have too much spirit and good feeling to go angling after husbands; but this Miss Hale comes out of the aristocratic counties, where, if all tales be true, rich husbands are reckoned prizes.”\n\nMr. Thornton’s brow contracted, and he came a step forward into the room.\n\n“Mother” (with a short scornful laugh), “you will make me confess. The only time I saw Miss Hale, she treated me with a haughty civility which had a strong flavour of contempt in it. She held herself aloof from me as if she had been a queen, and I her humble, unwashed vassal. Be easy, mother.”\n\n“No! I am not easy nor content either. What business had she, a renegade clergyman’s daughter, to turn up her nose at you! I would dress for none of them—a saucy set! if I were you.” As he was leaving the room he said:—\n\n“Mr. Hale is good, and gentle, and learned. He is not saucy. As for Mrs. Hale, I will tell you what she is like to-night, if you care to hear.” He shut the door, and was gone.\n\n“Despise my son! treat him as her vassal, indeed! Humph! I should like to know where she could find such another! Boy and man, he’s the noblest, stoutest heart I ever knew. I don’t care if I am his mother; I can see what’s what, and not be blind. I know what Fanny is; and I know what John is. Despise him! I hate her!”\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n“We are the trees whom shaking fastens more.” GEORGE HERBERT.\n\nMr. Thornton left the house without coming into the dining-room again. He was rather late, and walked rapidly out to Crampton. He was anxious not to slight his new friend by any disrespectful unpunctuality. The church-clock struck half-past seven as he stood at the door awaiting Dixon’s slow movements; always doubly tardy when she had to degrade herself by answering the door-bell. He was ushered into the little drawing-room, and kindly greeted by Mr. Hale, who led him up to his wife, whose pale face, and shawl-draped figure made a silent excuse for the cold languor of her greeting. Margaret was lighting the lamp when he entered, for the darkness was coming on. The lamp threw a pretty light into the centre of the dusky room, from which, with country habits, they did not exclude the night-skies, and the outer darkness of air. Somehow, that room contrasted itself with the one he had lately left; handsome, ponderous, with no sign of female habitation, except in the one spot where his mother sate, and no convenience for any other employment than eating and drinking. To be sure, it was a dining-room; his mother preferred to sit in it; and her will was a household law. But the drawing-room was not like this. It was twice—twenty times as fine; not one quarter as comfortable. Here were no mirrors, not even a scrap of glass to reflect the light, and answer the same purpose as water in a landscape; no gilding; a warm, sober breadth of colouring, well relieved by the dear old Helstone chintz-curtains and chair covers. An open davenport stood in the window opposite the door; in the other there was a stand, with a tall white china vase, from which drooped wreaths of English ivy, pale green birch, and copper-coloured beech-leaves. Pretty baskets of work stood about in different places: and books, not cared for on account of their binding solely, lay on one table, as if recently put down. Behind the door was another table decked out for tea, with a white table-cloth, on which flourished the cocoa-nut cakes, and a basket piled with oranges and ruddy American apples, heaped on leaves.\n\nIt appeared to Mr. Thornton that all these graceful cares were habitual to the family; and especially of a piece with Margaret. She stood by the tea-table in a light-coloured muslin gown, which had a good deal of pink about it. She looked as if she was not attending to the conversation, but solely busy with the tea-cups, among which her round ivory hands moved with pretty, noiseless, daintiness. She had a bracelet on one taper arm, which would fall down over her round wrist. Mr. Thornton watched the replacing of this troublesome ornament with far more attention than he listened to her father. It seemed as if it fascinated him to see her push it up impatiently until it tightened her soft flesh; and then to mark the loosening—the fall. He could almost have exclaimed—“There it goes again!” There was so little left to be done after he arrived at the preparation for tea, that he was almost sorry the obligation of eating and drinking came so soon to prevent him watching Margaret. She handed him his cup of tea with the proud air of an unwilling slave; but her eye caught the moment when he was ready for another cup; and he almost longed to ask her to do for him what he saw her compelled to do for her father, who took her little finger and thumb in his masculine hand, and made them serve as sugar-tongues. Mr. Thornton saw her beautiful eyes lifted to her father, full of light, half-laughter, and half-love, as this bit of pantomime went on between the two, unobserved as they fancied, by any. Margaret’s head still ached, as the paleness of her complexion, and her silence might have testified; but she was resolved to throw herself into the breach, if there was any long untoward pause, rather than that her father’s friend, pupil, and guest should have cause to think himself in any way neglected. But the conversation went on; and Margaret drew into a corner, near her mother, with her work, after the tea-things were taken away; and felt that she might let her thoughts roam, without fear of being suddenly wanted to fill up a gap.\n\nMr. Thornton and Mr. Hale were both absorbed in the continuation of some object which had been started at their last meeting. Margaret was recalled to a sense of the present by some trivial, low-spoken remark of her mother’s; and on suddenly looking up from her work, her eye was caught by the difference of outward appearance between her father and Mr. Thornton, as betokening such distinctly opposite natures. Her father was of slight figure, which made him appear taller than he really was, when not contrasted, as at this time, with the tall, massive frame of another. The lines in her father’s face were soft and waving, with a frequent undulating kind of trembling movement passing over them, showing every fluctuating emotion; the eyelids were large and arched, giving to the eyes a peculiar languid beauty which was almost feminine. The brows were finely arched, but were by the very size of the dreamy lids, raised to a considerable distance from the eyes. Now, in Mr. Thornton’s face the straight brows fell low over the clear, deep-set, earnest eyes, which without being unpleasantly sharp, seemed intent enough to penetrate into the very heart and core of what he was looking at. The lines in the face were few but firm, as if they were carved in marble, and lay principally about the lips, which were slightly compressed, over a set of teeth so faultless and beautiful as to give the effect of sudden sunlight when the rare bright smile, coming in an instant and shining out of the eyes, changed the whole look from the severe and resolved expression of a man ready to do and dare everything, to the keen, honest, enjoyment of the moment, which is seldom shown so fearlessly and instantaneously except by children. Margaret liked this smile; it was the first thing she had admired in this new friend of her father’s; and the opposition of character, shown in all these details of appearance she had just been noticing, seemed to explain the attraction they evidently felt towards each other.\n\nShe rearranged her mother’s worsted-work, and fell back into her own thoughts—as completely forgotten by Mr. Thornton as if she had not been in the room, so thoroughly was he occupied in explaining to Mr. Hale the magnificent power, yet delicate adjustment of the might of the steam-hammer, which was recalling to Mr. Hale some of the wonderful stories of subservient genii in the Arabian Nights—one moment stretching from earth to sky and filling all the width of the horizon, at the next obediently compressed into a vase small enough to be borne in the hand of a child.\n\n“And this imagination of power, this practical realisation of a gigantic thought, came out of one man’s brain in our good town. That very man has it within him to mount, step by step, on each wonder he achieves to higher marvels still. And I’ll be bound to say, we have many among us who, if he were gone, could spring into the breach and carry on the war which compels, and shall compel, all material power to yield to science.”\n\n“Your boast reminds me of the old lines—\n\n‘I’ve a hundred captains in England,’ he said, ‘As good as ever was he.’”\n\nAt her father’s quotation Margaret looked suddenly up, with inquiring wonder in her eyes. How in the world had they got from the cog-wheels to Chevy Chace?\n\n“It is no boast of mine,” replied Mr. Thornton; “it is plain matter-of-fact. I won’t deny that I am proud of belonging to a town—or perhaps I should rather say a district—the necessities of which give birth to such grandeur of conception. I would rather be a man toiling, suffering—nay, failing and successless—here, than lead a dull prosperous life in the old worn grooves of what you call more aristocratic society down in the South, with their slow days of careless ease. One may be clogged with honey and unable to rise and fly.”\n\n“You are mistaken,” said Margaret, roused by the aspersion on her beloved South to a fond vehemence of defence, that brought the colour into her cheeks and the angry tears into her eyes. “You do not know anything about the South. If there is less adventure or less progress—I suppose I must not say less excitement—from the gambling spirit of trade, which seems requisite to force out these wonderful inventions, there is less suffering also. I see men here going about in the streets who look ground down by some pinching sorrow or care—who are not only sufferers but haters. Now, in the South we have our poor, but there is not that terrible expression in their countenances of a sullen sense of injustice which I see here. You do not know the South, Mr. Thornton,” she concluded, collapsing into a determined silence, and angry with herself for having said so much.\n\n“And may I say you do not know the North?” asked he, with an inexpressible gentleness in his tone, as he saw that he had really hurt her. She continued resolutely silent; yearning after the lovely haunts she had left far away in Hampshire, with a passionate longing that made her feel her voice would be unsteady and trembling if she spoke.\n\n“At any rate, Mr. Thornton,” said Mrs. Hale, “you will allow that Milton is a much more smoky, dirty town than you will ever meet with in the South.”\n\n“I’m afraid I must give up its cleanliness,” said Mr. Thornton, with the quick gleaming smile. “But we are bidden by parliament to burn our own smoke; so I suppose, like good little children, we shall do as we are bid—some time.”\n\n“But I think you told me you had altered your chimneys so as to consume the smoke, did you not?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\n“Mine were altered by my own will, before parliament meddled with the affair. It was an immediate outlay, but it repays me in the saving of coal. I’m not sure whether I should have done it, if I had waited until the act was passed. At any rate, I should have waited to be informed against and fined, and given all the trouble in yielding that I legally could. But all laws which depend for their enforcement upon informers and fines, become inert from the odiousness of the machinery. I doubt if there has been a chimney in Milton informed against for five years past, although some are constantly sending out one-third of their coal in what is called here unparliamentary smoke.”\n\n“I only know it is impossible to keep the muslin blinds clean here above a week together; and at Helstone we have had them up for a month or more, and they have not looked dirty at the end of that time. And as for hands—Margaret, how many times did you say you had washed your hands this morning before twelve o’clock? Three times, was it not?”\n\n“Yes, mamma.”\n\n“You seem to have a strong objection to acts of parliament and all legislation affecting your mode of management down here at Milton,” said Mr. Hale.\n\n“Yes, I have; and many others have as well. And with justice, I think. The whole machinery—I don’t mean the wood and iron machinery now—of the cotton trade is so new that it is no wonder if it does not work well in every part all at once. Seventy years ago what was it? And now what it is not? Raw, crude materials came together; men of the same level, as regarded education and station, took suddenly the different position of masters and men, owing to the motherwit, as regarded opportunities and probabilities, which distinguished some, and made them far-seeing as to what great future lay concealed in that rude model of Sir Richard Arkwright’s. The rapid development of what might be called a new trade, gave those early masters enormous power of wealth and command. I don’t mean merely over the workmen; I mean over purchasers—over the whole world’s market. Why, I may give you, as an instance, an advertisement, inserted not fifty years ago in a Milton paper, that so-and-so (one of the half-dozen calico-printers of the time) would close his warehouse at noon each day; therefore, that all purchasers must come before that hour. Fancy a man dictating in this manner the time when he would sell and when he would not sell. Now, I believe, if a good customer chose to come at midnight, I should get up, and stand hat in hand to receive his orders.”\n\nMargaret’s lip curled, but somehow she was compelled to listen; she could no longer abstract herself in her own thoughts.\n\n“I only name such things to show what almost unlimited power the manufacturers had about the beginning of this century. The men were rendered dizzy by it. Because a man was successful in his ventures, there was no reason that in all other things his mind should be well-balanced. On the contrary, his sense of justice, and his simplicity, were often utterly smothered under the glut of wealth that came down upon him; and they tell strange tales of the wild extravagance of living indulged in on gala-days by those early cotton-lords. There can be no doubt, too, of the tyranny they exercised over their work-people. You know the proverb, Mr. Hale, ‘Set a beggar on horseback, and he’ll ride to the devil,’—well, some of these early manufacturers did ride to the devil in a magnificent style—crushing human bone and flesh under their horses’ hoofs without remorse. But by-and-by came a reaction; there were more factories, more masters; more men were wanted. The power of masters and men became more evenly balanced; and now the battle is pretty fairly waged between us. We will hardly submit to the decision of an umpire, much less to the interference of a meddler with only a smattering of the knowledge of the real facts of the case, even though that meddler be called the High Court of Parliament.”\n\n“Is there necessity for calling it a battle between the two classes?” asked Mr. Hale. “I know, from your using the term, it is one which gives a true idea of the real state of things to your mind.”\n\n“It is true; and I believe it to be as much a necessity as that prudent wisdom and good conduct are always opposed to, and doing battle with ignorance and improvidence. It is one of the great beauties of our system, that a working-man may raise himself into the power and position of a master by his own exertions and behaviour; that, in fact, every one who rules himself to decency and sobriety of conduct, and attention to his duties, comes over to our ranks; it may not be always as a master, but as an overlooker, a cashier, a book-keeper, a clerk, one on the side of authority and order.”\n\n“You consider all who are unsuccessful in raising themselves in the world, from whatever cause, as your enemies, then, if I understand you rightly,” said Margaret, in a clear, cold voice.\n\n“As their own enemies, certainly,” said he, quickly, not a little piqued by the haughty disapproval her form of expression and tone of speaking implied. But, in a moment, his straightforward honesty made him feel that his words were but a poor and quibbling answer to what she had said, and, be she as scornful as she liked, it was a duty he owed to himself to explain, as truly as he could, what he did mean. Yet it was very difficult to separate her interpretation, and keep it distinct from his meaning. He could best have illustrated what he wanted to say by telling them something of his own life; but was it not too personal a subject to speak about to strangers? Still, it was the simple straightforward way of explaining his meaning; so, putting aside the touch of shyness that brought a momentary flush of colour into his dark cheek, he said:\n\n“I am not speaking without book. Sixteen years ago, my father died under very miserable circumstances. I was taken from school, and had to become a man (as well as I could) in a few days. I had such a mother as few are blest with; a woman of strong power, and firm resolve. We went into a small country town, where living was cheaper than in Milton, and where I got employment in a draper’s shop (a capital place, by the way, for obtaining a knowledge of goods). Week by week, our income came to fifteen shillings, out of which three people had to be kept. My mother managed so that I put by three out of these fifteen shillings regularly. This made the beginning; this taught me self-denial. Now that I am able to afford my mother such comforts as her age, rather than her own wish, requires, I thank her silently on each occasion for the early training she gave me. Now when I feel that in my own case it is no good luck, nor merit, nor talent,—but simply the habits of life which taught me to despise indulgences not thoroughly earned,—indeed, never to think twice about them,—I believe that this suffering, which Miss Hale says is impressed on the countenances of the people of Milton, is but the natural punishment of dishonestly-enjoyed pleasure, at some former period of their lives. I do not look on self-indulgent, sensual people as worthy of my hatred; I simply look upon them with contempt for their poorness of character.”\n\n“But you have the rudiments of a good education,” remarked Mr. Hale. “The quick zest with which you are now reading Homer, shows me that you do not come to it as an unknown book: you have read it before, and are only recalling your old knowledge.”\n\n“That is true,—I had blundered along it at school; I dare say, I was even considered a pretty fair classic in those days, though my Latin and Greek have slipt away from me since. But I ask you, what preparation were they for such a life as I had to lead? None at all. Utterly none at all. On the point of education, any man who can read and write starts fair with me in the amount of really useful knowledge that I had at that time.”\n\n“Well! I don’t agree with you. But there I am perhaps somewhat of a pedant. Did not the recollection of the heroic simplicity of the Homeric life nerve you up?”\n\n“Not one bit!” exclaimed Mr. Thornton, laughing. “I was too busy to think about any dead people, with the living pressing alongside of me, neck to neck, in the struggle for bread. Now that I have my mother safe in the quiet peace that becomes her age, and duly rewards her former exertions, I can turn to all that old narration and thoroughly enjoy it.”\n\n“I dare say, my remark came from the professional feeling of there being nothing like leather,” replied Mr. Hale.\n\nWhen Mr. Thornton rose up to go away, after shaking hands with Mr. and Mrs. Hale, he made an advance to Margaret to wish her good-bye in a similar manner. It was the frank familiar custom of the place; but Margaret was not prepared for it. She simply bowed her farewell; although the instant she saw the hand, half put out, quickly drawn back, she was sorry she had not been aware of the intention. Mr. Thornton, however, knew nothing of her sorrow, and, drawing himself up to his full height, walked off, muttering as he left the house—\n\n“A more proud, disagreeable girl I never saw. Even her great beauty is blotted out of one’s memory by her scornful ways.”\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\n“There’s iron, they say, in all our blood, And a grain or two perhaps is good; But his, he makes me harshly feel, Has got a little too much of steel.” ANON.\n\n“Margaret!” said Mr. Hale as he returned from showing his guest downstairs; “I could not help watching your face with some anxiety, when Mr. Thornton made his confession of having been a shop-boy. I knew it all along from Mr. Bell; so I was aware of what was coming; but I half expected to see you get up and leave the room.”\n\n“Oh, papa! you don’t mean that you thought me so silly? I really liked that account of himself better than anything else he said. Everything else revolted me, from its hardness; but he spoke about himself so simply—with so little of the pretence that makes the vulgarity of shop-people, and with such tender respect for his mother, that I was less likely to leave the room then than when he was boasting about Milton, as if there was not such another place in the world; or quietly professing to despise people for careless, wasteful improvidence, without ever seeming to think it his duty to try to make them different,—to give them anything of the training which his mother gave him, and to which he evidently owes his position, whatever that may be. No! his statement of having been a shop-boy was the thing I liked best of all.”\n\n“I am surprised at you, Margaret,” said her mother. “You who were always accusing people of being shoppy at Helstone! I don’t think, Mr. Hale, you have done quite right in introducing such a person to us without telling us what he had been. I really was very much afraid of showing him how much shocked I was at some parts of what he said. His father ‘dying in miserable circumstances.’ Why it might have been in the workhouse.”\n\n“I am not sure it was not worse than being in the workhouse,” replied her husband. “I heard a good deal of his previous life from Mr. Bell before we came here; and as he has told you a part, I will fill up what he left out. His father speculated wildly, failed, and then killed himself, because he could not bear the disgrace. All his former friends shrunk from the disclosures that had to be made of his dishonest gambling—wild, hopeless struggles, made with other people’s money, to regain his own moderate portion of wealth. No one came forward to help the mother and this boy. There was another child, I believe, a girl; too young to earn money, but of course she had to be kept. At least, no friend came forward immediately, and Mrs. Thornton is not one, I fancy, to wait till tardy kindness comes to find her out. So they left Milton. I knew he had gone into a shop, and that his earnings, with some fragment of property secured to his mother, had been made to keep them for a long time. Mr. Bell said they absolutely lived upon water-porridge for years—how, he did not know; but long after the creditors had given up hope of any payment of old Mr. Thornton’s debts (if, indeed, they ever had hoped at all about it, after his suicide), this young man returned to Milton, and went quietly round to each creditor, paying him the first instalment of the money owing to him. No noise—no gathering together of creditors—it was done very silently and quietly, but all was paid at last; helped on materially by the circumstance of one of the creditors, a crabbed old fellow (Mr. Bell says), taking in Mr. Thornton as a kind of partner.”\n\n“That really is fine,” said Margaret. “What a pity such a nature should be tainted by his position as a Milton manufacturer.”\n\n“How tainted?” asked her father.\n\n“Oh, papa, by that testing everything by the standard of wealth. When he spoke of the mechanical powers, he evidently looked upon them only as new ways of extending trade and making money. And the poor men around him—they were poor because they were vicious—out of the pale of his sympathies because they had not his iron nature, and the capabilities that it gives him for being rich.”\n\n“Not vicious; he never said that. Improvident and self-indulgent were his words.”\n\nMargaret was collecting her mother’s working materials, and preparing to go to bed. Just as she was leaving the room, she hesitated—she was inclined to make an acknowledgment which she thought would please her father, but which to be full and true must include a little annoyance. However, out it came.\n\n“Papa, I do think Mr. Thornton a very remarkable man; but personally I don’t like him at all.”\n\n“And I do!” said her father laughing. “Personally, as you call it, and all. I don’t set him up for a hero, or anything of that kind. But good-night, child. Your mother looks sadly tired to-night, Margaret.”\n\nMargaret had noticed her mother’s jaded appearance with anxiety for some time past, and this remark of her father’s sent her up to bed with a dim fear lying like a weight on her heart. The life in Milton was so different from what Mrs. Hale had been accustomed to live in Helstone, in and out perpetually into the fresh and open air; the air itself was so different, deprived of all revivifying principle as it seemed to be here; the domestic worries pressed so very closely, and in so new and sordid a form, upon all the women in the family, that there was good reason to fear that her mother’s health might be becoming seriously affected. There were several other signs of something wrong about Mrs. Hale. She and Dixon held mysterious consultations in her bedroom, from which Dixon would come out crying and cross, as was her custom when any distress of her mistress called upon her sympathy. Once Margaret had gone into the chamber soon after Dixon left it, and found her mother on her knees, and as Margaret stole out she caught a few words which were evidently a prayer for strength and patience to endure severe bodily suffering. Margaret yearned to re-unite the bond of intimate confidence which had been broken by her long residence at her aunt Shaw’s, and strove by gentle caresses and softened words to creep into the warmest place in her mother’s heart. But though she received caresses and fond words back again, in such profusion as would have gladdened her formerly, yet she felt that there was a secret withheld from her, and she believed it bore serious reference to her mother’s health. She lay awake very long this night, planning how to lessen the evil influence of their Milton life on her mother. A servant to give Dixon permanent assistance should be got, if she gave up her whole time to the search; and then, at any rate, her mother might have all the personal attention she required, and had been accustomed to her whole life.\n\nVisiting register offices, seeing all manner of unlikely people, and very few in the least likely, absorbed Margaret’s time and thoughts for several days. One afternoon she met Bessy Higgins in the street, and stopped to speak to her.\n\n“Well, Bessy, how are you? Better, I hope, now the wind has changed.”\n\n“Better and not better, if yo’ know what that means.”\n\n“Not exactly,” replied Margaret, smiling.\n\n“I’m better in not being torn to pieces by coughing o’ nights, but I’m weary and tired o’ Milton, and longing to get away to the land o’ Beulah? and when I think I’m farther and farther off, my heart sinks, and I’m no better; I’m worse.”\n\nMargaret turned round to walk alongside of the girl in her feeble progress homeward. But for a minute or two she did not speak. At last she said in a low voice,\n\n“Bessy, do you wish to die?” For she shrank from death herself, with all the clinging to life so natural to the young and healthy.\n\nBessy was silent in her turn for a minute or two. Then she replied,\n\n“If yo’d led the life I have, and getten as weary of it as I have, and thought at times, ‘maybe it’ll last for fifty or sixty years—it does wi’ some,’—and got dizzy and dazed, and sick, as each of them sixty years seemed to spin about me, and mock me with its length of hours and minutes, and endless bits o’ time—oh, wench! I tell thee thou’d been glad enough when th’ doctor said he feared thou’d never see another winter.”\n\n“Why, Bessy, what kind of a life has yours been?”\n\n“Nought worse than many others’, I reckon. Only I fretted against it, and they didn’t.”\n\n“But what was it? You know, I’m a stranger here, so perhaps I’m not so quick at understanding what you mean as if I’d lived all my life at Milton.”\n\n“If yo’d ha’ come to our house when yo’ said yo’ would, I could maybe ha’ told you. But father says yo’re just like th’ rest on ’em; its out o’ sight out o’ mind wi’ you.”\n\n“I don’t know who the rest are; and I’ve been very busy; and, to tell the truth, I had forgotten my promise—”\n\n“Yo’ offered it; we asked none of it.”\n\n“I had forgotten what I said for the time,” continued Margaret quietly. “I should have thought of it again when I was less busy. May I go with you now?”\n\nBessy gave a quick glance at Margaret’s face, to see if the wish expressed was really felt. The sharpness in her eye turned to a wistful longing as she met Margaret’s soft and friendly gaze.\n\n“I ha’ none so many to care for me; if yo’ care yo’ may come.”\n\nSo they walked on together in silence. As they turned up into a small court, out of a squalid street, Bessy said,\n\n“Yo’ll not be daunted if father’s at home, and speaks a bit gruffish at first. He took a mind to ye, yo’ see, and he thought a deal o’ your coming to see us; and just because he liked yo’ he was vexed and put about.”\n\n“Don’t fear, Bessy.”\n\nBut Nicholas was not at home when they entered. A great slatternly girl, not so old as Bessy, but taller and stronger, was busy at the wash-tub, knocking about the furniture in a rough capable way, but altogether making so much noise that Margaret shrunk, out of sympathy with poor Bessy, who had sat down on the first chair, as if completely tired out with her walk. Margaret asked the sister for a cup of water, and while she ran to fetch it (knocking down the fire-irons, and tumbling over a chair in her way), she unloosed Bessy’s bonnet strings, to relieve her catching breath.\n\n“Do you think such life as this is worth caring for?” gasped Bessy, at last. Margaret did not speak, but held the water to her lips. Bessy took a long and feverish draught, and then fell back and shut her eyes. Margaret heard her murmur to herself: “They shall hunger no more, neither thirst any more: neither shall the sun light on them, nor any heat.”\n\nMargaret bent over and said, “Bessy don’t be impatient with your life, whatever it is—or may have been. Remember who gave it you, and made it what it is!”\n\nShe was startled by hearing Nicholas speak behind her; he had come in without her noticing him.\n\n“Now, I’ll not have my wench preached to. She’s bad enough, as it is, with her dreams and her methodee fancies, and her visions of cities with goulden gates and precious stones. But if it amuses her I let it abe, but I’m none going to have more stuff poured into her.”\n\n“But surely,” said Margaret, facing round, “you believe in what I said, that God gave her life, and ordered what kind of life it was to be?”\n\n“I believe what I see, and no more. That’s what I believe, young woman. I don’t believe all I hear—no! not by a big deal. I did hear a young lass make an ado about knowing where we lived, and coming to see us. And my wench here thought a deal about it, and flushed up many a time, when hoo little knew as I was looking at her, at the sound of a strange step. But hoo’s come at last,—and hoo’s welcome, as long as hoo’ll keep from preaching on what hoo knows nought about.”\n\nBessy had been watching Margaret’s face; she half sate up to speak now, laying her hand on Margaret’s arm with a gesture of entreaty. “Don’t be vexed wi’ him—there’s many a one thinks like him: many and many a one here. If yo’ could hear them speak, yo’d not be shocked at him; he’s a rare good man, is father—but oh!” said she, falling back in despair, “what he says at times makes me long to die more than ever, for I want to know so many things, and am so tossed about wi’ wonder.”\n\n“Poor wench—poor old wench—I’m loth to vex thee, I am; but a man mun speak out for the truth, and when I see the world going all wrong at this time o’ day, bothering itself wi’ things it knows nought about, and leaving undone all the things that lie in disorder close at its hand—why, I say, leave a’ this talk about religion alone, and set to work on what yo’ see and know. That’s my creed. It’s simple, and not far to fetch, nor hard to work.”\n\nBut the girl only pleaded the more with Margaret.\n\n“Don’t think hardly on him—he’s a good man, he is. I sometimes think I shall be moped wi’ sorrow even in the City of God, if father is not there.” The feverish colour came into her cheek, and the feverish flame into her eye. “But you will be there, father! you shall! Oh! my heart!” She put her hand to it, and became ghastly pale.\n\nMargaret held her in her arms, and put the weary head to rest upon her bosom. She lifted the thin soft hair from off the temples, and bathed them with water. Nicholas understood all her signs for different articles with the quickness of love, and even the round-eyed sister moved with laborious gentleness at Margaret’s “hush!” Presently the spasm that foreshadowed death had passed away, and Bessy roused herself and said,—\n\n“I’ll go to bed,—it’s best place; but,” catching at Margaret’s gown, “yo’ll come again,—I know yo’ will—but just say it!”\n\n“I will come again to-morrow,” said Margaret.\n\nBessy leant back against her father, who prepared to carry her upstairs; but as Margaret rose to go, he struggled to say something; “I could wish there were a God, if it were only to ask Him to bless thee.”\n\nMargaret went away very sad and thoughtful.\n\nShe was late for tea at home. At Helstone unpunctuality at meal-times was a great fault in her mother’s eyes; but now this, as well as many other little irregularities, seemed to have lost their power of irritation, and Margaret almost longed for the old complainings.\n\n“Have you met with a servant, dear?”\n\n“No, mamma; that Anne Buckley would never have done.”\n\n“Suppose I try,” said Mr. Hale. “Everybody else has had their turn at this great difficulty. Now let me try. I may be the Cinderella to put on the slipper after all.”\n\nMargaret could hardly smile at this little joke, so oppressed was she by her visit to the Higginses.\n\n“What would you do, papa? How would you set about it?”\n\n“Why, I should apply to some good house-mother to recommend me one known to herself or her servants.”\n\n“Very good. But we must first catch our house-mother.”\n\n“You have caught her. Or rather she is coming into the snare, and you will catch her to-morrow, if you’re skilful.”\n\n“What do you mean, Mr. Hale?” asked his wife, her curiosity aroused.\n\n“Why, my paragon pupil (as Margaret calls him) has told me that his mother intends to call on Mrs. and Miss Hale to-morrow.”\n\n“Mrs. Thornton!” exclaimed Mrs. Hale.\n\n“The mother of whom he spoke to us?” said Margaret.\n\n“Mrs. Thornton; the only mother he has, I believe,” said Mr. Hale, quietly.\n\n“I shall like to see her. She must be an uncommon person,” her mother added. “Perhaps she may have a relation who might suit us, and be glad of our place. She sounded to be such a careful economical person, that I should like any one out of the same family.”\n\n“My dear,” said Mr. Hale alarmed. “Pray don’t go off on that idea. I fancy Mrs. Thornton is as haughty and proud in her way as our little Margaret here is in hers, and that she completely ignores that old time of trial, and poverty, and economy, of which he speaks so openly. I am sure, at any rate, she would not like strangers to know anything about it.”\n\n“Take notice that is not my kind of haughtiness, papa, if I have any at all; which I don’t agree to, though you’re always accusing me of it.”\n\n“I don’t know positively that it is hers either; but from little things I have gathered from him, I fancy so.”\n\nThey cared too little to ask in what manner her son had spoken about her. Margaret only wanted to know if she must stay in to receive this call, as it would prevent her going to see how Bessy was, until late in the day, since the early morning was always occupied in household affairs; and then she recollected that her mother must not be left to have the whole weight of entertaining her visitor.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\n“Well—I suppose we must.” FRIENDS IN COUNCIL.\n\nMr. Thornton had had some difficulty in working up his mother to the desired point of civility. She did not often make calls; and when she did, it was in heavy state that she went through her duties. Her son had given her a carriage; but she refused to let him keep horses for it; they were hired for the solemn occasions, when she paid morning or evening visits. She had had horses for three days, not a fortnight before, and had comfortably “killed off” all her acquaintances, who might now put themselves to trouble and expense in their turn. Yet Crampton was too far off for her to walk; and she had repeatedly questioned her son as to whether his wish that she should call on the Hales was strong enough to bear the expense of cab-hire. She would have been thankful if it had not; for, as she said, “she saw no use in making up friendships and intimacies with all the teachers and masters in Milton; why, he would be wanting her to call on Fanny’s dancing-master’s wife, the next thing!”\n\n“And so I would, mother, if Mr. Mason and his wife were friendless in a strange place, like the Hales.”\n\n“Oh! you need not speak so hastily. I am going to-morrow. I only wanted you exactly to understand about it.”\n\n“If you are going to-morrow, I shall order horses.”\n\n“Nonsense, John. One would think you were made of money.”\n\n“Not quite, yet. But about the horses I’m determined. The last time you were out in a cab, you came home with a headache from the jolting.”\n\n“I never complained of it, I’m sure.”\n\n“No! my mother is not given to complaints,” said he, a little proudly. “But so much the more I have to watch over you. Now, as for Fanny there, a little hardship would do her good.”\n\n“She is not made of the same stuff as you are, John. She could not bear it.”\n\nMrs. Thornton was silent after this; for her last words bore relation to a subject which mortified her. She had an unconscious contempt for a weak character; and Fanny was weak in the very points in which her mother and brother were strong. Mrs. Thornton was not a woman much given to reasoning; her quick judgment and firm resolution served her in good stead of any long arguments and discussions with herself; she felt instinctively that nothing could strengthen Fanny to endure hardships patiently, or face difficulties bravely; and though she winced as she made this acknowledgment to herself about her daughter, it only gave her a kind of pitying tenderness of manner towards her; much of the same description of demeanour with which mothers are wont to treat their weak and sickly children. A stranger, a careless observer might have considered that Mrs. Thornton’s manner to her children betokened far more love to Fanny than to John. But such a one would have been deeply mistaken. The very daringness with which mother and son spoke out unpalatable truths, the one to the other, showed a reliance on the firm centre of each other’s souls, which the uneasy tenderness of Mrs. Thornton’s manner to her daughter, the shame with which she thought to hide the poverty of her child in all the grand qualities which she herself possessed unconsciously, and which she set so high a value upon in others—this shame, I say, betrayed the want of a secure resting-place for her affection. She never called her son by any name but John; “love,” and “dear,” and such like terms, were reserved for Fanny. But her heart gave thanks for him day and night; and she walked proudly among women for his sake.\n\n“Fanny dear! I shall have horses to the carriage to-day, to go and call on these Hales. Should not you go and see nurse? It’s in the same direction, and she’s always so glad to see you. You could go on there while I am at Mrs. Hale’s.”\n\n“Oh! mamma, it’s such a long way, and I am so tired.”\n\n“With what?” asked Mrs. Thornton, her brow slightly contracting.\n\n“I don’t know—the weather, I think. It is so relaxing. Couldn’t you bring nurse here, mamma? The carriage could fetch her, and she could spend the rest of the day here, which I know she would like.”\n\nMrs. Thornton did not speak; but she laid her work on the table, and seemed to think.\n\n“It will be a long way for her to walk back at night!” she remarked, at last.\n\n“Oh, but I will send her home in a cab. I never thought of her walking.”\n\nAt this point, Mr. Thornton came in, just before going to the mill.\n\n“Mother! I need hardly say, that if there is any little thing that could serve Mrs. Hale as an invalid, you will offer it, I’m sure.”\n\n“If I can find it out, I will. But I have never been ill myself, so I am not much up to invalids’ fancies.”\n\n“Well! here is Fanny then, who is seldom without an ailment. She will be able to suggest something, perhaps—won’t you, Fan?”\n\n“I have not always an ailment,” said Fanny, pettishly; “and I am not going with mamma. I have a headache to-day, and I shan’t go out.”\n\nMr. Thornton looked annoyed. His mother’s eyes were bent on her work, at which she was now stitching away busily.\n\n“Fanny! I wish you to go,” said he, authoritatively. “It will do you good, instead of harm. You will oblige me by going, without my saying anything more about it.”\n\nHe went abruptly out of the room after saying this.\n\nIf he had stayed a minute longer, Fanny would have cried at his tone of command, even when he used the words, “You will oblige me.” As it was, she grumbled.\n\n“John always speaks as if I fancied I was ill, and I am sure I never do fancy any such thing. Who are these Hales that he makes such a fuss about?”\n\n“Fanny, don’t speak so of your brother. He has good reasons of some kind or other, or he would not wish us to go. Make haste and put your things on.”\n\nBut the little altercation between her son and her daughter did not incline Mrs. Thornton more favourably towards “these Hales.” Her jealous heart repeated her daughter’s question, “Who are they, that he is so anxious we should pay them all this attention?” It came up like a burden to a song, long after Fanny had forgotten all about it in the pleasant excitement of seeing the effect of a new bonnet in the looking-glass.\n\nMrs. Thornton was shy. It was only of late years that she had had leisure enough in her life to go into society; and as society she did not enjoy it. As dinner-giving, and as criticising other people’s dinners, she took satisfaction in it. But this going to make acquaintance with strangers was a very different thing. She was ill at ease, and looked more than usually stern and forbidding as she entered the Hales’ little drawing-room.\n\nMargaret was busy embroidering a small piece of cambric for some little article of dress for Edith’s expected baby—“Flimsy, useless work,” as Mrs. Thornton observed to herself. She liked Mrs. Hale’s double knitting far better; that was sensible of its kind. The room altogether was full of knick-knacks, which must take a long time to dust; and time to people of limited income was money.\n\nShe made all these reflections as she was talking in her stately way to Mrs. Hale, and uttering all the stereotyped commonplaces that most people can find to say with their senses blindfolded. Mrs. Hale was making rather more exertion in her answers, captivated by some real old lace which Mrs. Thornton wore; “lace,” as she afterwards observed to Dixon, “of that old English point which has not been made for this seventy years, and which cannot be bought. It must have been an heir-loom, and shows that she had ancestors.” So the owner of the ancestral lace became worthy of something more than the languid exertion to be agreeable to a visitor, by which Mrs. Hale’s efforts at conversation would have been otherwise bounded. And presently, Margaret, racking her brain to talk to Fanny, heard her mother and Mrs. Thornton plunge into the interminable subject of servants.\n\n“I suppose you are not musical,” said Fanny, “as I see no piano.”\n\n“I am fond of hearing good music; I cannot play well myself; and papa and mamma don’t care much about it; so we sold our old piano when we came here.”\n\n“I wonder how you can exist without one. It almost seems to me a necessary of life.”\n\n“Fifteen shillings a week, and three saved out of them!” thought Margaret to herself. “But she must have been very young. She probably has forgotten her own personal experience. But she must know of those days.” Margaret’s manner had an extra tinge of coldness in it when she next spoke.\n\n“You have good concerts here, I believe.”\n\n“Oh, yes! Delicious! Too crowded, that is the worst. The directors admit so indiscriminately. But one is sure to hear the newest music there. I always have a large order to give to Johnson’s, the day after a concert.”\n\n“Do you like new music simply for its newness, then?”\n\n“Oh; one knows it is the fashion in London, or else the singers would not bring it down here. You have been in London, of course.”\n\n“Yes,” said Margaret, “I have lived there for several years.”\n\n“Oh! London and the Alhambra are the two places I long to see!”\n\n“London and the Alhambra!”\n\n“Yes! ever since I read the Tales of the Alhambra. Don’t you know them?”\n\n“I don’t think I do. But surely, it is a very easy journey to London.”\n\n“Yes; but somehow,” said Fanny, lowering her voice, “mamma has never been to London herself, and can’t understand my longing. She is very proud of Milton; dirty, smoky place as I feel it to be. I believe she admires it the more for those very qualities.”\n\n“If it has been Mrs. Thornton’s home for some years, I can well understand her loving it,” said Margaret, in her clear, bell-like voice.\n\n“What are you saying about me, Miss Hale? May I inquire?”\n\nMargaret had not the words ready for an answer to this question, which took her a little by surprise, so Miss Thornton replied:\n\n“Oh, mamma! we are only trying to account for your being so fond of Milton.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Mrs. Thornton. “I do not feel that my very natural liking for the place where I was born and brought up,—and which has since been my residence for some years, requires any accounting for.”\n\nMargaret was vexed. As Fanny had put it, it did seem as if they had been impertinently discussing Mrs. Thornton’s feelings; but she also rose up against that lady’s manner of showing that she was offended.\n\nMrs. Thornton went on after a moment’s pause:\n\n“Do you know anything of Milton, Miss Hale? Have you seen any of our factories? our magnificent warehouses?”\n\n“No!” said Margaret. “I have not seen anything of that description as yet.”\n\nThen she felt that, by concealing her utter indifference to all such places, she was hardly speaking with truth; so she went on:\n\n“I dare say, papa would have taken me before now if I had cared. But I really do not find much pleasure in going over manufactories.”\n\n“They are very curious places,” said Mrs. Hale, “but there is so much noise and dirt always. I remember once going in a lilac silk to see candles made, and my gown was utterly ruined.”\n\n“Very probably,” said Mrs. Thornton, in a short displeased manner. “I merely thought, that as strangers newly come to reside in a town which has risen to eminence in the country, from the character and progress of its peculiar business, you might have cared to visit some of the places where it is carried on; places unique in the kingdom, I am informed. If Miss Hale changes her mind and condescends to be curious as to the manufactures of Milton, I can only say I shall be glad to procure her admission to print-works, or reed-making, or the more simple operations of spinning carried on in my son’s mill. Every improvement of machinery is, I believe, to be seen there, in its highest perfection.”\n\n“I am so glad you don’t like mills and manufactories, and all those kind of things,” said Fanny, in a half-whisper, as she rose to accompany her mother, who was taking leave of Mrs. Hale with rustling dignity.\n\n“I think I should like to know all about them, if I were you,” replied Margaret quietly.\n\n“Fanny!” said her mother, as they drove away, “we will be civil to these Hales: but don’t form one of your hasty friendships with the daughter. She will do you no good, I see. The mother looks very ill, and seems a nice, quiet kind of person.”\n\n“I don’t want to form any friendship with Miss Hale, mamma,” said Fanny, pouting. “I thought I was doing my duty by talking to her, and trying to amuse her.”\n\n“Well! at any rate John must be satisfied now.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\n“That doubt and trouble, fear and pain, And anguish, all, are shadows vain, That death itself shall not remain;\n\nThat weary deserts we may tread, A dreary labyrinth may thread. Thro’ dark ways underground be led;\n\nYet, if we will one Guide obey, The dreariest path, the darkest way Shall issue out in heavenly day;\n\nAnd we, on divers shores now cast, Shall meet, our perilous voyage past, All in our Father’s house at last!” R. C. TRENCH.\n\nMargaret flew up stairs as soon as their visitors were gone, and put on her bonnet and shawl, to run and inquire how Betsy Higgins was, and sit with her as long as she could before dinner. As she went along the crowded narrow streets, she felt how much of interest they had gained by the simple fact of her having learnt to care for a dweller in them.\n\nMary Higgins, the slatternly younger sister, had endeavoured as well as she could to tidy up the house for the expected visit. There had been rough-stoning done in the middle of the floor, while the flags under the chairs and table and round the walls retained their dark unwashed appearance. Although the day was hot, there burnt a large fire in the grate, making the whole place feel like an oven; Margaret did not understand that the lavishness of coals was a sign of hospitable welcome to her on Mary’s part, and thought that perhaps the oppressive heat was necessary for Bessy. Bessy herself lay on a squab, or short sofa, placed under the window. She was very much more feeble than on the previous day, and tired with raising herself at every step to look out and see if it was Margaret coming. And now that Margaret was there, and had taken a chair by her, Bessy lay back silent, and content to look at Margaret’s face, and touch her articles of dress, with a childish admiration of their fineness of texture.\n\n“I never knew why folk in the Bible cared for soft raiment afore. But it must be nice to go dressed as yo’ do. It’s different fro’ common. Most fine folk tire my eyes out wi’ their colours; but some how yours rest me. Where did ye get this frock?”\n\n“In London,” said Margaret, much amused.\n\n“London! Have yo’ been in London?”\n\n“Yes! I lived there for some years. But my home was in a forest; in the country.”\n\n“Tell me about it,” said Bessy. “I like to hear speak of the country, and trees, and such like things.” She leant back, and shut her eyes, and crossed her hands over her breast, lying at perfect rest, as if to receive all the ideas Margaret could suggest.\n\nMargaret had never spoken of Helstone since she left it, except just naming the place incidentally. She saw it in dreams more vivid than life, and as she fell away to slumber at nights her memory wandered in all its pleasant places. But her heart was opened to this girl: “Oh, Bessy, I loved the home we have left so dearly! I wish you could see it. I cannot tell you half its beauty. There are great trees standing all about it, with their branches stretching long and level, and making a deep shade of rest even at noonday. And yet, though every leaf may seem still, there is a continual rushing sound of movement all around—not close at hand. Then sometimes the turf is as soft and fine as velvet; and sometimes quite lush with the perpetual moisture of a little, hidden, tinkling brook near at hand. And then in other parts there are billowy ferns—whole stretches of fern; some in the green shadow; some with long streaks of golden sunlight lying on them—just like the sea.”\n\n“I have never seen the sea,” murmured Bessy. “But go on.”\n\n“Then here and there, there are wide commons, high up as if above the very tops of the trees—”\n\n“I am glad of that. I felt smothered like down below. When I have gone for an out, I’ve always wanted to get high up and see far away, and take a deep breath o’ fulness in that air. I get smothered enough in Milton, and I think the sound yo’ speak of among the trees, going on for ever and ever, would send me dazed; it’s that made my head ache so in the mill. Now on these commons, I reckon, there is but little noise?”\n\n“No,” said Margaret; “nothing but here and there a lark high in the air. Sometimes I used to hear a farmer speaking sharp and loud to his servants; but it was so far away that it only reminded me pleasantly that other people were hard at work in some distant place, while I just sat on the heather and did nothing.”\n\n“I used to think once that if I could have a day of doing nothing, to rest me—a day in some quiet place like that yo’ speak on—it would maybe set me up. But now I’ve had many days o’ idleness, and I’m just as weary ’o them as I was o’ my work. Sometimes I’m so tired out I think I cannot enjoy heaven without a piece of rest first. I’m rather afeard o’ going straight there without getting a good sleep in the grave to set me up.”\n\n“Don’t be afraid, Bessy,” said Margaret, laying her hand on the girl’s hand; “God can give you more perfect rest than even idleness on earth, or the dead sleep of the grave can do.”\n\nBessy moved uneasily; then she said:\n\n“I wish father would not speak to me as he does. He means well, as I telled yo’ yesterday, and tell yo’ again and again. But yo’ see, though I don’t believe him a bit by day, yet by night—when I’m in a fever, half asleep and half awake—it comes back upon me—oh! so bad! And I think, if this should be th’ end of all, and if all I’ve been born for is just to work my heart and my life away, and to sicken i’ this dree place, wi’ them mill-noises in my ears for ever, until I could scream out for them to stop, and let me have a little piece o’ quiet—and wi’ the fluff filling my lungs, until I thirst to death for one long deep breath o’ the clear air yo’ speak on—and my mother gone, and I never able to tell her again how I loved her, and o’ all my troubles—I think if this life is th’ end, and there’s no God to wipe away all tears from all eyes—yo’ wench, yo’!” said she, sitting up, and clutching violently, almost fiercely, at Margaret’s hand, “I could go mad, and kill yo, I could.” She fell back completely worn out with her passion. Margaret knelt down by her.\n\n“Bessy—we have a Father in Heaven.”\n\n“I know it! I know it,” moaned she, turning her head uneasily from side to side. “I’m very wicked. I’ve spoken very wickedly. Oh! don’t be frightened by me and never come again. I would not harm a hair of your head. And,” opening her eyes, and looking earnestly at Margaret, “I believe, perhaps, more than yo’ do o’ what’s to come. I read the book o’ Revelations until I know it off by heart, and I never doubt when I’m waking, and in my senses, of all the glory I’m to come to.”\n\n“Don’t let us talk of what fancies come into your head when you are feverish. I would rather hear something about what you used to do when you were well.”\n\n“I think I was well when mother died, but I have never been rightly strong sin’ somewhere about that time. I began to work in a carding-room soon after, and the fluff got into my lungs, and poisoned me.”\n\n“Fluff?” said Margaret, inquiringly.\n\n“Fluff,” repeated Bessy. “Little bits, as fly off fro’ the cotton, when they’re carding it, and fill the air till it looks all fine white dust. They say it winds rounds the lungs, and tightens them up. Anyhow, there’s many a one as works in a carding-room, that falls into a waste, coughing and spitting blood, because they’re just poisoned by the fluff.”\n\n“But can’t it be helped?” asked Margaret.\n\n“I dunno. Some folk have a great wheel at one end o’ their carding-rooms to make a draught, and carry off th’ dust; but that wheel costs a deal of money—five or six hundred pounds, maybe, and brings in no profit; so it’s but a few of th’ masters as will put ’em up; and I’ve heard tell o’ men who didn’t like working in places where there was a wheel, because they said as how it made ’em hungry, at after they’d been long used to swallowing fluff, to go without it, and that their wages ought to be raised if they were to work in such places. So between masters and men th’ wheels fall through. I know I wish there’d been a wheel in our place, though.”\n\n“Did not your father know about it?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Yes! And he was sorry. But our factory were a good one on the whole; and a steady likely set o’ people; and father was afeard of letting me go to a strange place, for though yo’ would na think it now, many a one then used to call me a gradely lass enough. And I did na like to be reckoned nesh and soft, and Mary’s schooling were to be kept up, mother said, and father he were always liking to buy books, and go to lectures o’ one kind and another—all which took money—so I just worked on till I shall ne’er get the whirr out o’ my ears, or the fluff out o’ my throat i’ this world. That’s all.”\n\n“How old are you?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Nineteen, come July.”\n\n“And I too am nineteen.” She thought more sorrowfully than Bessy did, of the contrast between them. She could not speak for a moment or two for the emotion she was trying to keep down.\n\n“About Mary,” said Bessy. “I wanted to ask yo’ to be a friend to her. She’s seventeen, but she’s th’ last on us. And I don’t want her to go to th’ mill, and yet I dunno what she’s fit for.”\n\n“She could not do”—Margaret glanced unconsciously at the uncleaned corners of the room—“She could hardly undertake a servant’s place, could she? We have an old faithful servant, almost a friend, who wants help, but who is very particular; and it would not be right to plague her with giving her any assistance that would really be an annoyance and an irritation.”\n\n“No, I see. I reckon yo’re right. Our Mary’s a good wench; but who has she had to teach her what to do about a house? No mother, and me at the mill till I was good for nothing but scolding her for doing badly what I didn’t know how to do a bit. But I wish she could ha’ lived wi’ yo’, for all that.”\n\n“But even though she may not be exactly fitted to come and live with us as a servant—and I don’t know about that—I will always try to be a friend to her for your sake, Bessy. And now I must go. I will come again as soon as I can; but if it should not be to-morrow, or the next day, or even a week or a fortnight hence, don’t think I’ve forgotten you. I may be busy.”\n\n“I’ll know yo’ won’t forget me again. I’ll not mistrust yo’ no more. But remember, in a week or a fortnight I may be dead and buried!”\n\n“I’ll come again as soon as I can, Bessy,” said Margaret, squeezing her hand tight. “But you’ll let me know if you get worse.”\n\n“Ay, that will I,” said Bessy, returning the pressure.\n\nFrom that day forwards Mrs. Hale became more and more of a suffering invalid. It was now drawing near to the anniversary of Edith’s marriage, and looking back upon the year’s accumulated heap of troubles, Margaret wondered how they had been borne. If she could have anticipated them, how she would have shrunk away and hid herself from the coming time! And yet day by day had, of itself, and by itself, been very endurable—small, keen, bright little spots of positive enjoyment having come sparkling into the very middle of sorrows. A year ago, or when she first went to Helstone, and first became silently conscious of the querulousness in her mother’s temper, she would have groaned bitterly over the idea of a long illness to be borne in a strange, desolate, noisy, busy place, with diminished comforts on every side of the home life. But with the increase of serious and just ground of complaint, a new kind of patience had sprung up in her mother’s mind. She was gentle and quiet in intense bodily suffering, almost in proportion as she had been restless and depressed when there had been no real cause for grief. Mr. Hale was in exactly that stage of apprehension which, in men of his stamp, takes the shape of wilful blindness. He was more irritated than Margaret had ever known him at his daughter’s expressed anxiety.\n\n“Indeed, Margaret, you are growing fanciful! God knows I should be the first to take the alarm if your mother were really ill; we always saw when she had her headaches at Helstone, even without her telling us. She looks quite pale and white when she is ill; and now she has a bright healthy colour in her cheeks, just as she used to have when I first knew her.”\n\n“But papa,” said Margaret, with hesitation, “do you know I think that is the flush of pain.”\n\n“Nonsense, Margaret. I tell you, you are too fanciful. You are the person not well, I think. Send for the doctor to-morrow for yourself; and then, if it will make your mind easier, he can see your mother.”\n\n“Thank you, dear papa. It will make me happier, indeed.” And she went up to him to kiss him. But he pushed her away—gently enough, but still as if she had suggested unpleasant ideas, which he should be glad to get rid of as readily as he could of her presence. He walked uneasily up and down the room.\n\n“Poor Maria!” said he, half soliloquising, “I wish one could do right without sacrificing others. I shall hate this town, and myself too, if she——. Pray, Margaret, does your mother often talk to you of the old places: of Helstone, I mean.”\n\n“No, papa,” said Margaret, sadly.\n\n“Then, you see, she can’t be fretting after them, eh? It has always been a comfort to me to think that your mother was so simple and open that I knew every little grievance she had. She never would conceal anything seriously affecting her health from me: would she, eh, Margaret? I am quite sure she would not. So don’t let me hear of these foolish morbid ideas. Come, give me a kiss, and run off to bed.”\n\nBut she heard him pacing about (racooning, as she and Edith used to call it) long after her slow and languid undressing was finished—long after as she began to listen as she lay in bed.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\n“I was used To sleep at nights as sweetly as a child,— Now if the wind blew rough, it made me start, And think of my poor boy tossing about Upon the roaring seas. And then I seemed To feel that it was hard to take him from me For such a little fault.” SOUTHEY.\n\nIt was a comfort to Margaret about this time, to find that her mother drew more tenderly and intimately towards her than she had ever done since the days of her childhood. She took her to her heart as a confidential friend—the post Margaret had always longed to fill, and had envied Dixon for being preferred to. Margaret took pains to respond to every call made upon her for sympathy—and they were many—even when they bore relation to trifles which she would no more have noticed or regarded herself than the elephant would perceive the little pin at his feet, which yet he lifts carefully up at the bidding of his keeper. All unconsciously Margaret drew near to a reward.\n\nOne evening, Mr. Hale being absent, her mother began to talk to her about her brother Frederick, the very subject on which Margaret had longed to ask questions, and almost the only one on which her timidity overcame her natural openness. The more she wanted to hear about him, the less likely she was to speak.\n\n“Oh, Margaret, it was so windy last night! It came howling down the chimney in our room! I could not sleep. I never can when there is such a terrible wind. I got into a wakeful habit when poor Frederick was at sea; and now, even if I don’t waken all at once, I dream of him in some stormy sea, with great, clear, glass-green walls of waves on either side his ship, but far higher than her very masts, curling over her with that cruel, terrible white foam, like some gigantic crested serpent. It is an old dream, but it always comes back on windy nights, till I am thankful to waken, sitting straight and stiff up in bed with my terror. Poor Frederick! He is on land now, so wind can do him no harm. Though I did think it might shake down some of those tall chimneys.”\n\n“Where is Frederick now, mamma? Our letters are directed to the care of Messrs. Barbour, at Cadiz. I know: but where is he himself?”\n\n“I can’t remember the name of the place, but he is not called Hale; you must remember that, Margaret. Notice the F. D. in every corner of the letters. He has taken the name of Dickenson. I wanted him to have been called Beresford, to which he had a kind of right, but your father thought he had better not. He might be recognised, you know, if he were called by my name.”\n\n“Mamma,” said Margaret, “I was at Aunt Shaw’s when it all happened; and I suppose I was not old enough to be told plainly about it. But I should like to know now, if I may—if it does not give you too much pain to speak about it.”\n\n“Pain! No,” replied Mrs. Hale, her cheek flushing. “Yet it is pain to think that perhaps I may never see my darling boy again. Or else he did right, Margaret. They may say what they like, but I have his own letters to show, and I’ll believe him, though he is my son, sooner than any court-martial on earth. Go to my little japan cabinet, dear, and in the second left-hand drawer you will find a packet of letters.”\n\nMargaret went. There were the yellow, sea-stained letters, with the peculiar fragrance which ocean letters have. Margaret carried them back to her mother, who untied the silken string with trembling fingers, and, examining their dates, she gave them to Margaret to read, making her hurried, anxious remarks on their contents, almost before her daughter could have understood what they were.\n\n“You see, Margaret, how from the very first he disliked Captain Reid. He was second lieutenant in the ship—the Orion—in which Frederick sailed the very first time. Poor little fellow, how well he looked in his midshipman’s dress, with his dirk in his hand, cutting open all the newspapers with it as if it were a paper-knife! But this Mr. Reid, as he was then, seemed to take a dislike to Frederick from the very beginning. And then—stay! these are the letters he wrote on board the Russell. When he was appointed to her, and found his old enemy Captain Reid in command, he did mean to bear all his tyranny patiently. Look! this is the letter. Just read it, Margaret. Where is it he says—Stop—‘my father may rely upon me, that I will bear with all proper patience everything that one officer and gentleman can take from another. But from my former knowledge of my present captain, I confess I look forward with apprehension to a long course of tyranny on board the Russell.’ You see, he promises to bear patiently, and I am sure he did, for he was the sweetest-tempered boy, when he was not vexed, that could possibly be. Is that the letter in which he speaks of Captain Reid’s impatience with the men, for not going through the ship’s manœuvres as quickly as the Avenger? You see, he says that they had many new hands on board the Russell, while the Avenger had been nearly three years on the station, with nothing to do but to keep slavers off, and work her men, till they ran up and down the rigging like rats or monkeys.”\n\nMargaret slowly read the letter, half illegible through the fading of the ink. It might be—it probably was—a statement of Captain Reid’s imperiousness in trifles, very much exaggerated by the narrator, who had written it while fresh and warm from the scene of altercation. Some sailors being aloft in the main-topsail rigging, the captain had ordered them to race down, threatening the hindmost with the cat-of-nine tails. He who was the farthest on the spar, feeling the impossibility of passing his companions, and yet passionately dreading the disgrace of the flogging, threw himself desperately down to catch a rope considerably lower, failed, and fell senseless on the deck. He only survived for a few hours afterwards, and the indignation of the ship’s crew was at boiling point when young Hale wrote.\n\n“But we did not receive this letter till long, long after we heard of the mutiny. Poor Fred! I dare say it was a comfort to him to write it, even though he could not have known how to send it, poor fellow! And then we saw a report in the papers—that’s to say, long before Fred’s letter reached us—of an atrocious mutiny having broken out on board the Russell, and that the mutineers had remained in possession of the ship, which had gone off, it was supposed, to be a pirate; and that Captain Reid was sent adrift in a boat with some men—officers or something—whose names were all given, for they were picked up by a West Indian steamer. Oh, Margaret! how your father and I turned sick over that list, when there was no name of Frederick Hale. We thought it must be some mistake; for poor Fred was such a fine fellow, only perhaps rather too passionate; and we hoped that the name of Carr, which was in the list, was a misprint for that of Hale—newspapers are so careless. And towards post-time the next day, papa set off to walk to Southampton to get the papers; and I could not stop at home, so I went to meet him. He was very late—much later than I thought he would have been; and I sat down under the hedge to wait for him. He came at last, his arms hanging loose down, his head sunk, and walking heavily along, as if every step was a labour and a trouble. Margaret, I see him now.”\n\n“Don’t go on, mamma. I can understand it all,” said Margaret, leaning up caressingly against her mother’s side, and kissing her hand.\n\n“No, you can’t, Margaret. No one can who did not see him then. I could hardly lift myself up to go and meet him—everything seemed so to reel around me all at once. And when I got to him, he did not speak, or seem surprised to see me there, more than three miles from home, beside the Oldham beech-tree; but he put my arm in his, and kept stroking my hand, as if he wanted to soothe me to be very quiet under some great heavy blow; and when I trembled so all over that I could not speak, he took me in his arms, and stooped down his head on mine, and began to shake and to cry in a strange muffled, groaning voice, till I, for very fright, stood quite still, and only begging him to tell me what he had heard. And then, with his hand jerking, as if some one else moved it against his will, he gave me a wicked newspaper to read, calling our Frederick a ‘traitor of the blackest dye,’ ‘a base, ungrateful disgrace to his profession.’ Oh! I cannot tell what bad words they did not use. I took the paper in my hands as soon as I had read it—I tore it up to little bits—I tore it—oh! I believe, Margaret, I tore it with my teeth. I did not cry. I could not. My cheeks were as hot as fire, and my very eyes burnt in my head. I saw your father looking grave at me. I said it was a lie, and so it was. Months after, this letter came, and you see what provocation Frederick had. It was not for himself, or his own injuries, he rebelled; but he would speak his mind to Captain Reid, and so it went on from bad to worse; and you see, most of the sailors stuck by Frederick.\n\n“I think, Margaret,” she continued, after a pause, in a weak, trembling, exhausted voice, “I am glad of it—I am prouder of Frederick standing up against injustice, than if he had been simply a good officer.”\n\n“I am sure I am,” said Margaret, in a firm, decided tone. “Loyalty and obedience to wisdom and justice are fine; but it is still finer to defy arbitrary power, unjustly and cruelly used—not on behalf of ourselves, but on behalf of others more helpless.”\n\n“For all that, I wish I could see Frederick once more—just once. He was my first baby, Margaret.” Mrs. Hale spoke wistfully, and almost as if apologising for the yearning, craving wish, as though it were a depreciation of her remaining child. But such an idea never crossed Margaret’s mind. She was thinking how her mother’s desire could be fulfilled.\n\n“It is six or seven years ago—would they still prosecute him, mother? If he came and stood his trial, what would be the punishment? Surely, he might bring evidence of his great provocation.”\n\n“It would do no good,” replied Mrs. Hale. “Some of the sailors who accompanied Frederick were taken, and there was a court-martial held on them on board the Amicia; I believed all they said in their defence, poor fellows, because it just agreed with Frederick’s story—but it was of no use,—” and for the first time during the conversation Mrs. Hale began to cry; yet something possessed Margaret to force the information she foresaw, yet dreaded, from her mother.\n\n“What happened to them, mamma?” asked she.\n\n“They were hung at the yard-arm,” said Mrs. Hale, solemnly. “And the worst was that the court, in condemning them to death, said they had suffered themselves to be led astray from their duty by their superior officers.”\n\nThey were silent for a long time.\n\n“And Frederick was in South America for several years, was he not?”\n\n“Yes. And now he is in Spain. At Cadiz, or somewhere near it. If he comes to England he will be hung. I shall never see his face again—for if he comes to England he will be hung.”\n\nThere was no comfort to be given. Mrs. Hale turned her face to the wall, and lay perfectly still in her mother’s despair. Nothing could be said to console her. She took her hand out of Margaret’s with a little impatient movement, as if she would fain be left alone with the recollection of her son. When Mr. Hale came in, Margaret went out, oppressed with gloom, and seeing no promise of brightness on any side of the horizon.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\n“Thought fights with thought; out springs a spark of truth From the collision of the sword and shield.” W. S. LANDOR.\n\n“Margaret,” said her father, the next day, “we must return Mrs. Thornton’s call. Your mother is not very well, and thinks she cannot walk so far; but you and I will go this afternoon.”\n\nAs they went, Mr. Hale began about his wife’s health, with a kind of veiled anxiety, which Margaret was glad to see awakened at last.\n\n“Did you consult the doctor, Margaret? Did you send for him?”\n\n“No, papa, you spoke of his coming to see me. Now I was well. But if I only knew of some good doctor, I would go this afternoon, and ask him to come, for I am sure mamma is seriously indisposed.”\n\nShe put the truth thus plainly and strongly because her father had so completely shut his mind against the idea, when she had last named her fears. But now the case was changed. He answered in a despondent tone:\n\n“Do you think she has any hidden complaint? Do you think she is really very ill? Has Dixon said anything? Oh, Margaret! I am haunted by the fear that our coming to Milton has killed her. My poor Maria!”\n\n“Oh, papa! don’t imagine such things,” said Margaret, shocked. “She is not well, that is all. Many a one is not well for a time; and with good advice gets better and stronger than ever.”\n\n“But has Dixon said anything about her?”\n\n“No! You know Dixon enjoys making a mystery out of trifles; and she has been a little mysterious about mamma’s health, which has alarmed me rather, that is all. Without any reason I dare say. You know, papa, you said the other day I was getting fanciful.”\n\n“I hope and trust you are. But don’t think of what I said then. I like you to be fanciful about your mother’s health! Don’t be afraid of telling me your fancies. I like to hear them, though I dare say, I spoke as if I was annoyed. But we will ask Mrs. Thornton if she can tell us of a good doctor. We won’t throw away our money on any but some one first-rate. Stay, we turn up this street.”\n\nThe street did not look as if it could contain any house large enough for Mrs. Thornton’s habitation. Her son’s presence never gave any impression as to the kind of house he lived in; but, unconsciously, Margaret had imagined that tall, massive, handsomely dressed Mrs. Thornton must live in a house of the same character as herself. Now Marlborough Street consisted of long rows of small houses, with a blank wall here and there; at least that was all they could see from the point at which they entered it.\n\n“He told me he lived in Marlborough Street, I’m sure,” said Mr. Hale, with a much perplexed air.\n\n“Perhaps it is one of the economies he still practises, to live in a very small house. But here are plenty of people about; let me ask.”\n\nShe accordingly inquired of a passer-by, and was informed that Mr. Thornton lived close to the mill, and had the factory lodge-door pointed out to her, at the end of the long dead wall they had noticed.\n\nThe lodge-door was like a common garden-door; on one side of it were great closed gates for the ingress and egress of lorries and wagons. The lodge-keeper admitted them into a great oblong yard, on one side of which were offices for the transaction of business; on the opposite, an immense many-windowed mill, whence proceeded the continual clank of machinery and the long groaning roar of the steam-engine, enough to deafen those who lived within the enclosure. Opposite to the wall, along which the street ran, on one of the narrow sides of the oblong, was a handsome stone-coped house,—blackened, to be sure, by the smoke, but with paint, windows, and steps kept scrupulously clean. It was evidently a house which had been built some fifty or sixty years. The stone facings—the long, narrow windows, and the number of them—the flights of steps up to the front door, ascending from either side, and guarded by railing—all witnessed to its age. Margaret only wondered why people who could afford to live in so good a house, and keep it in such perfect order, did not prefer a much smaller dwelling in the country, or even some suburb; not in the continual whirl and din of the factory. Her unaccustomed ears could hardly catch her father’s voice, as they stood on the steps awaiting the opening of the door. The yard, too, with the great doors in the dead wall as a boundary, was but a dismal look-out for the sitting-rooms of the house—as Margaret found when they had mounted the old-fashioned stairs, and been ushered into the drawing-room, the three windows of which went over the front door and the room on the right-hand side of the entrance. There was no one in the drawing-room. It seemed as though no one had been in it since the day when the furniture was bagged up with as much care as if the house was to be overwhelmed with lava, and discovered a thousand years hence. The walls were pink and gold; the pattern on the carpet represented bunches of flowers on a light ground, but it was carefully covered up in the centre by a linen drugget, glazed and colourless. The window-curtains were lace; each chair and sofa had its own particular veil of netting or knitting. Great alabaster groups occupied every flat surface, safe from dust under their glass shades. In the middle of the room, right under the bagged-up chandelier, was a large circular table, with smartly-bound books arranged at regular intervals round the circumference of its polished surface, like gaily coloured spokes of a wheel. Everything reflected light, nothing absorbed it. The whole room had a painfully spotted, spangled, speckled look about it, which impressed Margaret so unpleasantly that she was hardly conscious of the peculiar cleanliness required to keep everything so white and pure in such an atmosphere, or of the trouble that must be willingly expended to secure that effect of icy, snowy discomfort. Wherever she looked there was evidence of care and labour, but not care and labour to procure ease, to help on habits of tranquil home employment; solely to ornament and then to preserve ornament from dirt or destruction.\n\nThey had leisure to observe, and to speak to each other in low voices, before Mrs. Thornton appeared. They were talking of what all the world might hear; but it is a common effect of such a room as this to make people speak low, as if unwilling to awaken the unused echoes.\n\nAt last Mrs. Thornton came in, rustling in handsome black silk, as was her wont; her muslins and laces rivalling, not excelling, the pure whiteness of the muslins and netting of the room. Margaret explained how it was that her mother could not accompany them to return Mrs. Thornton’s call; but in her anxiety not to bring back her father’s fears too vividly, she gave but a bungling account, and left the impression on Mrs. Thornton’s mind that Mrs. Hale’s was some temporary or fanciful fine-ladyish indisposition, which might have been put aside had there been a strong enough motive; or that if it was too severe to allow her to come out that day, the call might have been deferred. Remembering, too, the horses to her carriage, hired for her own visit to the Hales, and how Fanny had been ordered to go by Mr. Thornton, in order to pay every respect to them, Mrs. Thornton drew up slightly offended, and gave Margaret no sympathy—indeed, hardly any credit for the statement of her mother’s indisposition.\n\n“How is Mr. Thornton?” asked Mr. Hale. “I was afraid he was not well, from his hurried note yesterday.”\n\n“My son is rarely ill; and when he is, he never speaks about it, or makes it an excuse for not doing anything. He told me he could not get leisure to read with you last night, sir. He regretted it, I am sure; he values the hours spent with you.”\n\n“I am sure they are equally agreeable to me,” said Mr. Hale. “It makes me feel young again to see his enjoyment and appreciation of all that is fine in classical literature.”\n\n“I have no doubt that classics are very desirable for people who have leisure. But I confess, it was against my judgment that my son renewed his study of them. The time and place in which he lives, seem to me to require all his energy and attention. Classics may do very well for men who loiter away their lives in the country or in colleges; but Milton men ought to have their thoughts and powers absorbed in the work of to-day. At least, that is my opinion.” This last clause she gave out with “the pride that apes humility.”\n\n“But, surely, if the mind is too long directed to one object only, it will get stiff and rigid, and unable to take in many interests,” said Margaret.\n\n“I do not quite understand what you mean by a mind getting stiff and rigid. Nor do I admire those whirligig characters that are full of this thing to-day, to be utterly forgetful of it in their new interest to-morrow. Having many interests does not suit the life of a Milton manufacturer. It is or ought to be enough for him to have one great desire, and to bring all the purposes of his life to bear on the fulfilment of that.”\n\n“And that is—?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\nHer sallow cheek flushed, and her eye lightened, as she answered:\n\n“To hold and maintain a high, honourable place among the merchants of his country—the men of his town. Such a place my son has earned for himself. Go where you will—I don’t say in England only, but in Europe—the name of John Thornton of Milton is known and respected amongst all men of business. Of course, it is unknown in the fashionable circles,” she continued scornfully. “Idle gentlemen and ladies are not likely to know much of a Milton manufacturer, unless he gets into parliament, or marries a lord’s daughter.”\n\nBoth Mr. Hale and Margaret had an uneasy, ludicrous consciousness that they had never heard of this great name, until Mr. Bell had written them word that Mr. Thornton would be a good friend to have in Milton. The proud mother’s world was not their world of Harley street gentilities on the one hand, or country clergymen and Hampshire squires on the other. Margaret’s face, in spite of all her endeavours to keep it simply listening in its expression, told the sensitive Mrs. Thornton this feeling of hers.\n\n“You think you never heard of this wonderful son of mine, Miss Hale. You think I’m an old woman whose ideas are hounded by Milton, and whose own crow is the whitest ever seen.”\n\n“No,” said Margaret, with some spirit. “It may be true that I was thinking I had hardly heard Mr. Thornton’s name before I came to Milton. But since I have come here, I have heard enough to make me respect and admire him, and to feel how much justice and truth there is in what you have said of him.”\n\n“Who spoke to you of him?” asked Mrs. Thornton, a little mollified, yet jealous lest any one else’s words should not have done him full justice.\n\nMargaret hesitated before she replied. She did not like this authoritative questioning. Mr. Hale came in, as he thought, to the rescue.\n\n“It was what Mr. Thornton said himself, that made us know the kind of man he was. Was it not, Margaret?”\n\nMrs. Thornton drew herself up, and said—\n\n“My son is not the one to tell of his own doings. May I again ask you, Miss Hale, from whose account you formed your favourable opinion of him? A mother is curious and greedy of commendation of her children, you know.”\n\nMargaret replied, “It was as much from what Mr. Thornton withheld of that which we had been told of his previous life by Mr. Bell,—it was more that than what he said, that made us all feel what reason you have to be proud of him.”\n\n“Mr. Bell! What can he know of John? He, living a lazy life in a drowsy college. But I’m obliged to you, Miss Hale. Many a missy young lady would have shrunk from giving an old woman the pleasure of hearing that her son was well spoken of.”\n\n“Why?” asked Margaret, looking straight at Mrs. Thornton, in bewilderment.\n\n“Why! because I suppose they might have consciences that told them how surely they were making the old mother into an advocate for them, in case they had any plans on the son’s heart.”\n\nShe smiled a grim smile, for she had been pleased by Margaret’s frankness; and perhaps she felt that she had been asking questions too much as if she had a right to catechise. Margaret laughed outright at the notion presented to her; laughed so merrily that it grated on Mrs. Thornton’s ear, as if the words that called forth that laugh, must have been utterly and entirely ludicrous.\n\nMargaret stopped her merriment as soon as she saw Mrs. Thornton’s annoyed look.\n\n“I beg your pardon, madam. But I really am very much obliged to you for exonerating me from making any plans on Mr. Thornton’s heart.”\n\n“Young ladies have, before now,” said Mrs. Thornton, stiffly.\n\n“I hope Miss Thornton is well,” put in Mr. Hale, desirous of changing the current of the conversation.\n\n“She is as well as she ever is. She is not strong,” replied Mrs. Thornton, shortly.\n\n“And Mr. Thornton? I suppose I may hope to see him on Thursday?”\n\n“I cannot answer for my son’s engagements. There is some uncomfortable work going on in the town; a threatening of a strike. If so, his experience and judgment will make him much consulted by his friends. But I should think he could come on Thursday. At any rate, I am sure he will let you know if he cannot.”\n\n“A strike!” asked Margaret. “What for? What are they going to strike for?”\n\n“For the mastership and ownership of other people’s property,” said Mrs. Thornton, with a fierce snort. “That is what they always strike for. If my son’s work-people strike, I will only say they are a pack of ungrateful hounds. But I have no doubt they will.”\n\n“They are wanting higher wages, I suppose?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\n“That is the face of the thing. But the truth is, they want to be masters, and make the masters into slaves on their own ground. They are always trying at it; they always have it in their minds; and every five or six years, there comes a struggle between masters and men. They’ll find themselves mistaken this time, I fancy,—a little out of their reckoning. If they turn out, they mayn’t find it so easy to go in again. I believe, the masters have a thing or two in their heads which will teach the men not to strike again in a hurry, if they try it this time.”\n\n“Does it make the town very rough?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Of course it does. But surely you are not a coward, are you? Milton is not the place for cowards. I have known the time when I have had to thread my way through a crowd of white, angry men, all swearing they would have Makinson’s blood as soon as he ventured to show his nose out of his factory; and he, knowing nothing of it, some one had to go and tell him, or he was a dead man; and it needed to be a woman,—so I went. And when I got in, I could not get out. It was as much as my life was worth. So I went up to the roof, where there were stones piled ready to drop on the heads of the crowd, if they tried to force the factory doors. And I would have lifted those heavy stones, and dropped them with as good an aim as the best man there, but that I fainted with the heat I had gone through. If you live in Milton, you must learn to have a brave heart, Miss Hale.”\n\n“I would do my best,” said Margaret rather pale. “I do not know whether I am brave or not till I am tried; but I am afraid I should be a coward.”\n\n“South country people are often frightened by what our Darkshire men and women only call living and struggling. But when you’ve been ten years among a people who are always owing their betters a grudge, and only waiting for an opportunity to pay it off, you’ll know whether you are a coward or not, take my word for it.”\n\nMr. Thornton came that evening to Mr. Hale’s. He was shown up into the drawing-room, where Mr. Hale was reading aloud to his wife and daughter.\n\n“I am come partly to bring you a note from my mother, and partly to apologise for not keeping to my time yesterday. The note contains the address you asked for; Dr. Donaldson.”\n\n“Thank you!” said Margaret, hastily, holding out her hand to take the note, for she did not wish her mother to hear that they had been making any inquiry about a doctor. She was pleased that Mr. Thornton seemed immediately to understand her feeling; he gave her the note without another word of explanation.\n\nMr. Hale began to talk about the strike. Mr. Thornton’s face assumed a likeness to his mother’s worst expression, which immediately repelled the watching Margaret.\n\n“Yes; the fools will have a strike. Let them. It suits us well enough. But we gave them a chance. They think trade is flourishing as it was last year. We see the storm on the horizon and draw in our sails. But because we don’t explain our reasons, they won’t believe we’re acting reasonably. We must give them line and letter for the way we choose to spend or save our money. Henderson tried a dodge with his men, out at Ashley, and failed. He rather wanted a strike; it would have suited his book well enough. So, when the men came to ask for the five per cent. they are claiming, he told ’em he’d think about it, and give them his answer on the pay day; knowing all the while what his answer would be, of course, but thinking he’d strengthen their conceit of their own way. However, they were too deep for him, and heard something about the bad prospects of trade. So in they came on the Friday, and drew back their claim, and now he’s obliged to go on working. But we Milton masters have to-day sent in our decision. We won’t advance a penny. We tell them we may have to lower wages; but can’t afford to raise. So here we stand, waiting for their next attack.”\n\n“And what will that be?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\n“I conjecture a simultaneous strike. You will see Milton without smoke in a few days, I imagine, Miss Hale.”\n\n“But why,” asked she, “could you not explain what good reason you had for expecting a bad trade? I don’t know whether I use the right words, but you will understand what I mean.”\n\n“Do you give your servants reasons for your expenditure, or your economy in the use of your own money? We, the owners of capital, have a right to choose what we will do with it.”\n\n“A human right,” said Margaret, very low.\n\n“I beg your pardon, I did not hear what you said.”\n\n“I would rather not repeat it,” said she; “it related to a feeling which I do not think you would share.”\n\n“Won’t you try me?” pleaded he; his thoughts suddenly bent upon learning what she had said. She was displeased with his pertinacity, but did not choose to affix too much importance to her words.\n\n“I said you had a human right. I meant that there seemed no reason but religious ones, why you should not do what you like with your own.”\n\n“I know we differ in our religious opinions; but don’t you give me credit for having some, though not the same as yours?”\n\nHe was speaking in a subdued voice, as if to her alone. She did not wish to be so exclusively addressed. She replied out in her usual tone:\n\n“I do not think that I have any occasion to consider your special religious opinions in the affair. All I meant to say is, that there is no human law to prevent the employers from utterly wasting or throwing away all their money, if they choose; but that there are passages in the Bible which would rather imply—to me at least—that they neglected their duties as stewards if they did so. However, I know so little about strikes and rate of wages, and capital, and labour, that I had better not talk to a political economist like you.”\n\n“Nay, the more reason,” said he eagerly. “I shall only be too glad to explain to you all that may seem anomalous or mysterious to a stranger; especially at a time like this, when our doings are sure to be canvassed by every scribbler who can hold a pen.”\n\n“Thank you,” she answered, coldly. “Of course, I shall apply to my father in the first instance for any information he can give me, if I get puzzled with living here amongst this strange society.”\n\n“You think it strange. Why?”\n\n“I don’t know—I suppose because, on the very face of it, I see two classes dependent on each other in every possible way, yet each evidently regarding the interests of the other as opposed to their own: I never lived in a place before where there were two sets of people always running each other down.”\n\n“Who have you heard running the masters down? I don’t ask who you have heard abusing the men; for I see you persist in misunderstanding what I said the other day. But who have you heard abusing the masters?”\n\nMargaret reddened; then smiled as she said,\n\n“I am not fond of being catechised. I refuse to answer your question. Besides, it has nothing to do with the fact. You must take my word for it, that I have heard some people, or, it may be, only some one of the workpeople speak as though it were the interest of the employers to keep them from acquiring money—that it would make them too independent if they had a sum in the savings’ bank.”\n\n“I dare say it was that man Higgins who told you all this,” said Mrs. Hale. Mr. Thornton did not appear to hear what Margaret evidently did not wish him to know. But he caught it nevertheless.\n\n“I heard, moreover, that it was considered to the advantage of the masters to have ignorant workmen—not hedge-lawyers, as Captain Lennox used to call those men in his company who questioned and would know the reason for every order.”\n\nThis latter part of her sentence she addressed rather to her father than to Mr. Thornton. Who is Captain Lennox? asked Mr. Thornton of himself, with a strange kind of displeasure, that prevented him for the moment from replying to her! Her father took up the conversation.\n\n“You never were fond of schools, Margaret, or you would have seen and known before this how much is being done for education in Milton.”\n\n“No!” she said, with sudden meekness. “I know I do not care enough about schools. But the knowledge and the ignorance of which I was speaking did not relate to reading and writing,—the teaching or information one can give to a child. I am sure, that what was meant was ignorance of the wisdom that shall guide men and women. I hardly know what that is. But he—that is my informant—spoke as if the masters would like their hands to be merely tall, large children—living in the present moment—with a blind unreasoning kind of obedience.”\n\n“In short, Miss Hale, it is very evident that your informant found a pretty ready listener to all the slander he chose to utter against the masters,” said Mr. Thornton, in an offended tone.\n\nMargaret did not reply. She was displeased at the personal character Mr. Thornton affixed to what she had said.\n\nMr. Hale spoke next:\n\n“I must confess that, although I have not become so intimately acquainted with any workmen as Margaret has, I am very much struck by the antagonism between the employer and the employed, on the very surface of things. I even gather this impression from what you yourself have from time to time said.”\n\nMr. Thornton paused awhile before he spoke. Margaret had just left the room, and he was vexed at the state of feeling between himself and her. However, the little annoyance, by making him cooler and more thoughtful, gave a greater dignity to what he said:\n\n“My theory is, that my interests are identical with those of my workpeople, and vice-versa. Miss Hale, I know, does not like to hear men called ‘hands,’ so I won’t use that word, though it comes most readily to my lips as the technical term, whose origin, whatever it was, dates before my time. On some future day—in some millennium—in Utopia, this unity may be brought into practice—just as I can fancy a republic the most perfect form of government.”\n\n“We will read Plato’s Republic as soon as we have finished Homer.”\n\n“Well, in the Platonic year, it may fall out that we are all—men, women, and children—fit for a republic; but give me a constitutional monarchy in our present state of morals and intelligence. In our infancy we require a wise despotism to govern us. Indeed, long past infancy, children and young people are the happiest under the unfailing laws of a discreet firm authority. I agree with Miss Hale so far as to consider our people in the condition of children, while I deny that we, the masters, have anything to do with the making or keeping them so. I maintain that despotism is the best kind of government for them; so that in the hours in which I come in contact with them I must necessarily be an autocrat. I will use my best discretion—from no humbug or philanthropic feeling, of which we have had rather too much in the North—to make wise laws and come to just decisions in the conduct of my business—laws and decisions which work for my own good in the first instance—for theirs in the second; but I will neither be forced to give my reasons, nor flinch from what I have once declared to be my resolution. Let them turn out! I shall suffer as well as they: but in the end they will find I have not bated nor altered one jot.”\n\nMargaret had re-entered the room and was sitting at her work; but she did not speak. Mr. Hale answered—\n\n“I dare say I am talking in great ignorance; but from the little I know, I should say that the masses were already passing rapidly into the troublesome stage which intervenes between childhood and manhood, in the life of the multitude as well as that of the individual. Now, the error which many parents commit in the treatment of the individual at this time is, insisting on the same unreasoning obedience as when all he had to do in the way of duty was, to obey the simple laws of ‘Come when you’re called,’ and ‘Do as you’re bid!’ But a wise parent humours the desire for independent action, so as to become the friend and adviser when his absolute rule shall cease. If I get wrong in my reasoning, recollect, it is you who adopted the analogy.”\n\n“Very lately,” said Margaret, “I heard a story of what happened in Nuremberg only three or four years ago. A rich man there lived alone in one of the immense mansions which were formerly both dwellings and warehouses. It was reported that he had a child, but no one knew of it for certain. For forty years this rumour kept rising and falling—never utterly dying away. After his death it was found to be true. He had a son—an overgrown man, with the unexercised intellect of a child, whom he had kept up in that strange way, in order to save him from temptation and error. But, of course, when this great old child was turned loose in the world, every bad counsellor had power over him. He did not know good from evil. His father had made the blunder of bringing him up in ignorance and taking it for innocence; and after fourteen months of riotous living, the city authorities had to take charge of him, in order to save him from starvation. He could not even use words effectively enough to be a successful beggar.”\n\n“I used the comparison (suggested by Miss Hale) of the position of the master to that of a parent, so I ought not to complain of your turning the simile into a weapon against me. But, Mr. Hale, when you were setting up a wise parent as a model for us, you said he humoured his children in their desire for independent action. Now certainly, the time is not come for the hands to have any independent action during business hours; I hardly know what you would mean by it then. And I say, that the masters would be trenching on the independence of their hands, in a way that I, for one, should not feel justified in doing, if we interfered too much with the life they lead out of the mills. Because they labour ten hours a-day for us, I do not see that we have any right to impose leading-strings upon them for the rest of their time. I value my own independence so highly that I can fancy no degradation greater than that of having another man perpetually directing and advising and lecturing me, or even planning too closely in any way about my actions. He might be the wisest of men or the most powerful—I should equally rebel and resent his interference. I imagine this is a stronger feeling in the North of England than in the South.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, but is not that because there has been none of the equality of friendship between the adviser and advised classes? Because every man has had to stand in an unchristian and isolated position, apart from and jealous of his brother-man: constantly afraid of his rights being trenched upon?”\n\n“I only state the fact. I am sorry to say, I have an appointment at eight o’clock, and I must just take facts as I find them to-night, without trying to account for them; which, indeed, would make no difference in determining how to act as things stand—the facts must be granted.”\n\n“But,” said Margaret in a low voice, “it seems to me that it makes all the difference in the world—.” Her father made a sign to her to be silent, and allow Mr. Thornton to finish what he had to say. He was already standing up and preparing to go.\n\n“You must grant me this one point. Given a strong feeling of independence in every Darkshire man, have I any right to obtrude my views, of the manner in which he shall act, upon another (hating it as I should do most vehemently myself), merely because he has labour to sell and I capital to buy?”\n\n“Not in the least,” said Margaret, determined to say just this one thing; “not in the least because of your labour and capital positions, whatever they are, but because you are a man, dealing with a set of men over whom you have, whether you reject the use of it or not, immense power, just because your lives and your welfare are so constantly and intimately interwoven. God has made us so that we must be mutually dependent. We may ignore our own dependence, or refuse to acknowledge that others depend upon us in more respects than the payment of weekly wages; but the thing must be, nevertheless. Neither you nor any other master can help yourselves. The most proudly independent man depends on those around him for their insensible influence on his character—his life. And the most isolated of all your Darkshire Egos has dependants clinging to him on all sides; he cannot shake them off, any more than the great rock he resembles can shake off—”\n\n“Pray don’t go into similes, Margaret; you have led us off once already,” said her father, smiling, yet uneasy at the thought that they were detaining Mr. Thornton against his will, which was a mistake; for he rather liked it, as long as Margaret would talk, although what she said only irritated him.\n\n“Just tell me, Miss Hale, are you yourself ever influenced—no, that is not a fair way of putting it;—but if you are ever conscious of being influenced by others, and not by circumstances, have those others been working directly or indirectly? Have they been labouring to exhort, to enjoin, to act rightly for the sake of example, or have they been simple, true men, taking up their duty, and doing it unflinchingly, without a thought of how their actions were to make this man industrious, that man saving? Why, if I were a workman, I should be twenty times more impressed by the knowledge that my master was honest, punctual, quick, resolute in all his doings (and hands are keener spies even than valets), than by any amount of interference, however kindly meant, with my ways of going on out of work-hours. I do not choose to think too closely on what I am myself; but, I believe, I rely on the straightforward honesty of my hands, and the open nature of their opposition, in contra-distinction to the way in which the turn-out will be managed in some mills, just because they know I scorn to take a single dishonourable advantage, or do an underhand thing myself. It goes farther than a whole course of lectures on ‘Honesty is the Best Policy’—life diluted into words. No, no! What the master is, that will the men be, without over-much taking thought on his part.”\n\n“That is a great admission,” said Margaret, laughing. “When I see men violent and obstinate, in pursuit of their rights, I may safely infer that the master is the same; that he is a little ignorant of that spirit which suffereth long, and is kind, and seeketh not her own.”\n\n“You are just like all strangers who don’t understand the working of our system, Miss Hale,” said he, hastily. “You suppose that our men are puppets of dough, ready to be moulded into any amiable form we please. You forget we have only to do with them for less than a third of their lives; and you seem not to perceive that the duties of a manufacturer are far larger and wider than those merely of an employer of labour: we have a wide commercial character to maintain, which makes us into the great pioneers of civilisation.”\n\n“It strikes me,” said Mr. Hale, smiling, “that you might pioneer a little at home. They are a rough, heathenish set of fellows, these Milton men of yours.”\n\n“They are that,” replied Mr. Thornton. “Rose-water surgery won’t do for them. Cromwell would have made a capital mill-owner, Miss Hale. I wish we had him to put down this strike for us.”\n\n“Cromwell is no hero of mine,” she said, coldly. “But I am trying to reconcile your admiration of despotism with your respect for other men’s independence of character.”\n\nHe reddened at her tone. “I choose to be the unquestioned and irresponsible master of my hands, during the hours they labour for me. But those hours past, our relation ceases; and then comes in the same respect for their independence that I myself exact.”\n\nHe did not speak again for a minute, he was too much vexed. But he shook it off, and bade Mr. and Mrs. Hale good night. Then, drawing near to Margaret, he said in a lower voice—\n\n“I spoke hastily to you once this evening, and I am afraid, rather rudely. But you know I am but an uncouth Milton manufacturer; will you forgive me?”\n\n“Certainly,” said she, smiling up in his face, the expression of which was somewhat anxious and oppressed, and hardly cleared away as he met her sweet sunny countenance, out of which all the north-wind effect of their discussion had entirely vanished. But she did not put out her hand to him, and again he felt the omission, and set it down to pride.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\n“Trust in that veiled hand, which leads None by the path that he would go; And always be for change prepared, For the world’s law is ebb and flow.” FROM THE ARABIC.\n\nThe next afternoon Dr. Donaldson came to pay his first visit to Mrs. Hale. The mystery that Margaret hoped their late habits of intimacy had broken through was resumed. She was excluded from the room, while Dixon was admitted. Margaret was not a ready lover, but where she loved she loved passionately, and with no small degree of jealousy.\n\nShe went into her mother’s bed-room, just behind the drawing-room, and paced it up and down, while awaiting the doctor’s coming out. Every now and then she stopped to listen; she fancied she heard a moan. She clenched her hands tight, and held her breath. She was sure she heard a moan. Then all was still for a few minutes more; and then there was the moving of chairs, the raised voices, all the little disturbances of leave-taking.\n\nWhen she heard the door open, she went quickly out of the bedroom.\n\n“My father is from home, Dr. Donaldson; he has to attend a pupil at this hour. May I trouble you to come into his room downstairs?”\n\nShe saw, and triumphed over all the obstacles which Dixon threw in her way; assuming her rightful position as daughter of the house in something of the spirit of the Elder Brother, which quelled the old servant’s officiousness very effectually. Margaret’s conscious assumption of this unusual dignity of demeanour towards Dixon, gave her an instant’s amusement in the midst of her anxiety. She knew, from the surprised expression on Dixon’s face, how ridiculously grand she herself must be looking; and the idea carried her downstairs into the room; it gave her that length of oblivion from the keen sharpness of the recollection of the actual business in hand. Now, that came back, and seemed to take away her breath. It was a moment or two before she could utter a word.\n\nBut she spoke with an air of command, as she asked—\n\n“What is the matter with mamma? You will oblige me by telling the simple truth.” Then, seeing a slight hesitation on the doctor’s part, she added—\n\n“I am the only child she has—here, I mean. My father is not sufficiently alarmed, I fear: and, therefore, if there is any serious apprehension, it must be broken to him gently. I can do this. I can nurse my mother. Pray, speak, sir; to see your face, and not be able to read it, gives me a worse dread than I trust any words of yours will justify.”\n\n“My dear young lady, your mother seems to have a most attentive and efficient servant, who is more like her friend——”\n\n“I am her daughter, sir.”\n\n“But when I tell you she expressly desired that you might not be told——”\n\n“I am not good or patient enough to submit to the prohibition. Besides, I am sure, you are too wise—too experienced to have promised to keep the secret.”\n\n“Well,” said he, half-smiling, though sadly enough, “there you are right. I did not promise. In fact, I fear, the secret will be known soon enough without my revealing it.”\n\nHe paused. Margaret then went very white, and compressed her lips a little more. Otherwise not a feature moved. With the quick insight into character, without which no medical man can rise to the eminence of Dr. Donaldson, he saw that she would exact the full truth; that she would know if one iota was withheld; and that the withholding would be torture more acute than the knowledge of it. He spoke two short sentences in a low voice, watching her all the time; for the pupils of her eyes dilated into a black horror, and the whiteness of her complexion became livid. He ceased speaking. He waited for that look to go off,—for her gasping breath to come. Then she said:—\n\n“I thank you most truly, sir, for your confidence. That dread has haunted me for many weeks. It is a true, real agony. My poor, poor mother!” Her lips now began to quiver, and he let her have the relief of tears, sure of her power of self-control to check them.\n\nA few tears—those were all she shed, before she recollected the many questions she longed to ask.\n\n“Will there be much suffering?”\n\nHe shook his head. “That we cannot tell. It depends on constitution; on a thousand things. But the late discoveries of medical science have given us large power of alleviation.”\n\n“My father!” said Margaret, trembling all over.\n\n“I do not know Mr. Hale. I mean, it is difficult to give advice. But I should say, bear on, with the knowledge you have forced me to give you so abruptly, till the fact which I could not withhold has become in some degree familiar to you, so that you may without too great an effort, be able to give what comfort you can to your father. Before then,—my visits, which of course, I shall repeat from time to time, although I fear I can do nothing but alleviate,—a thousand little circumstances will have occurred to awaken his alarm, to deepen it—so that he will be all the better prepared.—Nay, my dear young lady—nay, my dear—I saw Mr. Thornton, and I honour your father for the sacrifice he has made, however mistaken I may believe him to be.—Well, this once, if it will please you, my dear. Only remember, when I come again, I come as a friend. And you must learn to look upon me as such, because seeing each other—getting to know each other at such times as these, is worth years of morning calls.”\n\nMargaret could not speak for crying; but she wrang his hand at parting.\n\n“That’s what I call a fine girl!” thought Dr. Donaldson, when he was seated in his carriage, and had time to examine his ringed hand, which had slightly suffered from her pressure. “Who would have thought that little hand could have given such a squeeze? But the bones were well put together, and that gives immense power. What a queen she is! With her head thrown back at first, to force me into speaking the truth; and then bent so eagerly forward to listen. Poor thing! I must see she does not overstrain herself. Though it’s astonishing how much these thorough-bred creatures can do and suffer. That girl’s game to the back-bone. Another, who had gone that deadly colour, could never have come round without either fainting or hysterics. But she wouldn’t do either—not she! And the very force of her will brought her round. Such a girl as that would win my heart, if I were thirty years younger. It’s too late now. Ah; here we are at the Archers’.” So out he jumped, with thought, wisdom, experience, sympathy, and ready to attend to any of the calls made upon them by this family, just as if there were none other in the world.\n\nMeanwhile, Margaret had returned into her father’s study for a moment, to recover strength before going upstairs into her mother’s presence.\n\n“Oh, my God, my God! but this is terrible. How shall I bear it? Such a deadly disease! no hope! Oh, mamma, mamma, I wish I had never gone to aunt Shaw’s, and been all those precious years away from you! Poor mamma! how much she must have borne! Oh, pray thee, my God, that her sufferings may not be too acute, too dreadful. How shall I bear to see them? How can I bear papa’s agony? He must not be told yet; not all at once. It would kill him. But I won’t lose another moment of my own dear, precious mother.”\n\nShe ran upstairs. Dixon was not in the room. Mrs. Hale lay back in an easy chair, with a soft white shawl wrapped around her, and a becoming cap put on, in expectation of the doctor’s visit. Her face had a little faint colour in it, and the very exhaustion after the examination gave it a peaceful look. Margaret was surprised to see her look so calm.\n\n“Why, Margaret, how strange you look! What is the matter?” And then, as the idea stole into her mind of what was indeed the real state of the case, she added, as if a little displeased: “you have not been seeing Dr. Donaldson, and asking him any questions—have you child?” Margaret did not reply—only looked wistfully towards her. Mrs. Hale became more displeased. “He would not surely break his word to me, and”—\n\n“Oh yes, mamma, he did. I made him. It was I—blame me.” She knelt down by her mother’s side, and caught her hand—she would not let it go, though Mrs. Hale tried to pull it away. She kept kissing it, and the hot tears she shed bathed it.\n\n“Margaret, it was very wrong of you. You knew I did not wish you to know.” But, as if tired with the contest, she left her hand in Margaret’s clasp, and by-and-by she returned the pressure faintly. That encouraged Margaret to speak.\n\n“Oh, mamma! let me be your nurse. I will learn anything Dixon can teach me. But you know I am your child, and I do think I have a right to do everything for you.”\n\n“You don’t know what you are asking,” said Mrs. Hale, with a shudder.\n\n“Yes, I do. I know a great deal more than you are aware of. Let me be your nurse. Let me try, at any rate. No one has ever, shall ever try so hard as I will do. It will be such a comfort, mamma.”\n\n“My poor child! Well, you shall try. Do you know, Margaret, Dixon and I thought you would quite shrink from me if you knew—”\n\n“Dixon thought!” said Margaret, her lip curling. “Dixon could not give me credit for enough true love—for as much as herself! She thought, I suppose, that I was one of those poor sickly women who like to lie on rose leaves, and be fanned all day. Don’t let Dixon’s fancies come any more between you and me, mamma. Don’t please!” implored she.\n\n“Don’t be angry with Dixon,” said Mrs. Hale, anxiously. Margaret recovered herself.\n\n“No! I won’t. I will try and be humble, and learn her ways, if you will only let me do all I can for you. Let me be in the first place, mother—I am greedy of that. I used to fancy you would forget me while I was away at aunt Shaw’s, and cry myself to sleep at nights with that notion in my head.”\n\n“And I used to think, how will Margaret bear our make-shift poverty after the thorough comfort and luxury in Harley Street, till I have many a time been more ashamed of your seeing our contrivances at Helstone than of any stranger finding them out.”\n\n“Oh, mamma! and I did so enjoy them. They were so much more amusing than all the jog-trot Harley Street ways. The wardrobe shelf with handles that served as a supper-tray on grand occasions! And the old tea-chests stuffed and covered for ottomans! I think what you call the make-shift contrivances at dear Helstone were a charming part of the life there.”\n\n“I shall never see Helstone again, Margaret,” said Mrs. Hale, the tears welling up into her eyes. Margaret could not reply. Mrs. Hale went on. “While I was there, I was for ever wanting to leave it. Every place seemed pleasanter. And now I shall die far away from it. I am rightly punished.”\n\n“You must not talk so,” said Margaret impatiently. “He said you might live for years. Oh, mother! we will have you back at Helstone yet.”\n\n“No, never! That I must take as a just penance. But, Margaret—Frederick!”\n\nAt the mention of that one word, she suddenly cried out loud, as in some sharp agony. It seemed as if the thought of him upset all her composure, destroyed the calm, overcame the exhaustion. Wild passionate cry succeeded to cry—“Frederick! Frederick! Come to me. I am dying. Little first-born child, come to me once again!”\n\nShe was in violent hysterics. Margaret went and called Dixon in terror. Dixon came in a huff, and accused Margaret of having over-excited her mother. Margaret bore all meekly, only trusting that her father might not return. In spite of her alarm, which was even greater than the occasion warranted, she obeyed all Dixon’s directions promptly and well without a word of self-justification. By so doing she mollified her accuser. They put her mother to bed, and Margaret sate by her till she fell asleep, and afterwards till Dixon beckoned her out of the room, and, with a sour face, as if doing something against the grain, she bade her drink a cup of coffee which she had prepared for her in the drawing-room, and stood over her in a commanding attitude as she did so.\n\n“You shouldn’t have been so curious, Miss, and then you wouldn’t have needed to fret before your time. It would have come soon enough. And now, I suppose, you’ll tell master, and a pretty household I shall have of you!”\n\n“No, Dixon,” said Margaret, sorrowfully, “I will not tell papa. He could not bear it as I can.” And by way of proving how well she bore it, she burst into tears.\n\n“Ay! I knew how it would be. Now you’ll waken your mamma, just after she’s gone to sleep so quietly. Miss Margaret my dear, I’ve had to keep it down this many a week: and though I don’t pretend I can love her as you do, yet I loved her better than any other man, woman, or child—no one but Master Frederick ever came near her in my mind. Ever since Lady Beresford’s maid first took me in to see her dressed out in white crape, and corn-ears, and scarlet poppies, and I ran a needle down into my finger, and broke it in, and she tore up her worked pocket-handkerchief, after they’d cut it out, and came in to wet the bandages again with lotion when she returned from the ball—where she’d been the prettiest young lady of all—I’ve never loved any one like her. I little thought then that I should live to see her brought so low. I don’t mean no reproach to nobody. Many a one calls you pretty and handsome, and what not. Even in this smoky place, enough to blind one’s eyes, the owls can see that. But you’ll never be like your mother for beauty—never; not if you live to be a hundred.”\n\n“Mamma is very pretty still. Poor mamma!”\n\n“Now don’t ye set off again, or I shall give way at last” (whimpering). “You’ll never stand master’s coming home, and questioning, at this rate. Go out and take a walk, and come in something like. Many’s the time I’ve longed to walk it off—the thought of what was the matter with her, and how it must all end.”\n\n“Oh, Dixon!” said Margaret, “how often I’ve been cross with you, not knowing what a terrible secret you had to bear!”\n\n“Bless you, child! I like to see you showing a bit of a spirit. It’s the good old Beresford blood. Why, the last Sir John but two shot his steward down, there where he stood, for just telling him that he racked the tenants, and he’d racked the tenants till he could get no more money off them than he could get skin off a flint.”\n\n“Well, Dixon, I won’t shoot you, and I’ll try not to be cross again.”\n\n“You never have. If I’ve said it at times, it has always been to myself, just in private, by way of making a little agreeable conversation, for there’s no one here fit to talk to. And when you fire up you’re the very image of Master Frederick. I could find in my heart to put you in a passion any day just to see his stormy look coming like a great cloud over your face. But now you go out, Miss. I’ll watch over Missus; and as for master, his books are company enough for him, if he should come in.”\n\n“I will go,” said Margaret. She hung about Dixon for a minute or so, as if afraid and irresolute; then, suddenly kissing her, she went quickly out of the room.\n\n“Bless her!” said Dixon. “She’s as sweet as a nut. There are three people I love; its Missus, Master Frederick, and her. Just them three. That’s all. The rest be hanged, for I don’t know what they’re in the world for. Master was born, I suppose, for to marry missus. If I thought he loved her properly, I might get to love him in time. But he should ha’ made a deal more on her, and not been always reading, reading, thinking, thinking. See what it has brought him to. Many a one who never reads or thinks either, gets to be Rector, and Dean, and what not; and I daresay master might, if he’d just minded missus, and let the weary reading and thinking alone.—There she goes” (looking out of the window as she heard the front door shut). “Poor young lady! her clothes look shabby to what they did when she came to Helstone a year ago. Then she hadn’t so much as a darned stocking or a cleaned pair of gloves in all her wardrobe. And now—!”\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\n“There are briars besetting every path, Which call for patient care; There is a cross in every lot, And an earnest need for prayer.” ANON.\n\nMargaret went out heavily and unwillingly enough. But the length of a street—yes, the air of a Milton Street—cheered her young blood before she reached her first turning. Her step grew lighter, her lip redder. She began to take notice, instead of having her thoughts turned so exclusively inward. She saw unusual loiterers in the streets: men with their hands in their pockets sauntering along; loud-laughing and loud-spoken girls clustered together, apparently excited to high spirits, and a boisterous independence of temper and behaviour. The more ill-looking of the men—the discreditable minority—hung about on the steps of the beer-houses and gin-shops, smoking, and commenting pretty freely on every passer-by. Margaret disliked the prospect of the long walk through these streets, before she came to the fields which she had planned to reach. Instead, she would go and see Bessy Higgins. It would not be so refreshing as a quiet country walk, but still it would perhaps be doing the kinder thing.\n\nNicholas Higgins was sitting by the fire smoking, as she went in. Bessy was rocking herself on the other side.\n\nNicholas took the pipe out of his mouth, and standing up, pushed his chair towards Margaret; he leant against the chimney-piece in a lounging attitude, while she asked Bessy how she was.\n\n“Hoo’s rather down i’ th’ mouth in regard to spirits, but hoo’s better in health. Hoo doesn’t like this strike. Hoo’s a deal too much set on piece and quietness at any price.”\n\n“This is th’ third strike I’ve seen,” said she, sighing, as if that was answer and explanation enough.\n\n“Well, third time pays for all. See if we don’t dang th’ masters this time. See if they don’t come and beg us to come back at our own price. That’s all. We’ve missed it afore time, I grant yo’; but this time we’n laid our plans desperate deep.”\n\n“Why do you strike?” asked Margaret. “Striking is leaving off work till you get your own rate of wages, is it not? You must not wonder at my ignorance; where I come from I never heard of a strike.”\n\n“I wish I were there,” said Bessie, wearily. “But it’s not for me to get sick and tired o’ strikes. This is the last I’ll see. Before it’s ended I shall be in the Great City—the Holy Jerusalem.”\n\n“Hoo’s so full of th’ life to come, hoo cannot think of th’ present. Now I, yo see, am bound to do the best I can here. I think a bird i’ th’ hand is worth two i’ th’ bush. So them’s the different views we take on th’ strike question.”\n\n“But,” said Margaret, “if the people struck, as you call it, where I come from, as they are mostly all field labourers, the seed would not be sown, the hay got in, the corn reaped.”\n\n“Well?” said he. He had resumed his pipe, and put his “well” in the form of an interrogation.\n\n“Why,” she went on, “what would become of the farmers?”\n\nHe puffed away. “I reckon, they’d have either to give up their farms, or to give fair rate of wage.”\n\n“Suppose they could not, or would not do the last; they could not give up their farms all in a minute, however much they might wish to do so; but they would have no hay, nor corn to sell that year; and where would the money come from to pay the labourers’ wages the next?”\n\nStill puffing away. At last he said:\n\n“I know nought of your ways down South. I have heerd they’re a pack of spiritless, down-trodden men; welly clemmed to death; too dazed wi’ clemming to know when they’re put upon. Now, it’s not so here. We known when we’re put upon; and we’en too much blood in us to stand it. We just take our hands fro’ our looms, and say, ‘Yo’ may clem us, but yo’ll not put upon us, my masters!’ And be danged to ’em, they shan’t this time!”\n\n“I wished I lived down South,” said Bessy.\n\n“There’s a deal to bear there,” said Margaret. “There are sorrows to bear everywhere. There is very hard bodily labour to be gone through, with very little food to give strength.”\n\n“But it’s out of doors,” said Bessy. “And away from the endless, endless noise, and sickening heat.”\n\n“It’s sometimes in heavy rain, and sometimes in bitter cold. A young person can stand it; but an old man gets racked with rheumatism, and bent and withered before his time; yet he must just work on the same, or else go to the workhouse.”\n\n“I thought yo were so taken wi’ the ways of the South country.”\n\n“So I am,” said Margaret, smiling a little, as she found herself thus caught. “I only mean, Bessy, there’s good and bad in everything in this world; and as you felt the bad up here, I thought it was but fair you should know the bad down there.”\n\n“And yo say they never strike down there?” asked Nicholas abruptly.\n\n“No!” said Margaret; “I think they have too much sense.”\n\n“An’ I think,” replied he, dashing the ashes out of his pipe with so much vehemence that it broke, “it’s not that they’ve too much sense, but that they’ve too little spirit.”\n\n“Oh, father!” said Bessy, “what have ye gained by striking? Just think of that first strike when mother died—how we all had to clem—you the worst of all; and yet many a one went in every week at the same wage, till all were gone in that there was work for; and some went beggars all their lives at after.”\n\n“Ay,” said he. “That there strike was badly managed. Folk got into th’ management of it, as were either fools or not true men. Yo’ll see, it’ll be different this time.”\n\n“But all this time you’ve not told me what you’re striking for,” said Margaret, again.\n\n“Why, yo see, there’s five or six masters who have set themselves again paying the wages they’ve been paying these two years past, and flourishing upon, and getting richer upon. And now they come to us, and say we’re to take less. And we won’t. We’ll just clem them to death first; and see who’ll work for ’em then. They’ll have killed the goose that laid ’em the golden eggs, I reckon.”\n\n“And so you plan dying, in order to be revenged upon them!”\n\n“No,” said he, “I dunnot. I just look forward to the chance of dying at my post sooner than yield. That’s what folk call fine and honourable in a soldier, and why not in a poor weaver-chap?”\n\n“But,” said Margaret, “a soldier dies in the cause of the Nation—in the cause of others.”\n\nHe laughed grimly. “My lass,” said he, “yo’re but a young wench, but don’t yo think I can keep three people—that’s Bessy, and Mary, and me—on sixteen shillings a week? Dun yo think it’s for mysel’ I’m striking work at this time? It is just as much in the cause of others as yon soldier—only m’appen, the cause he dies for is just that of somebody he never clapt eyes on, nor heerd on all his born days, while I take up John Boucher’s cause, as lives next door but one, wi’ a sickly wife, and eight childer, none on ’em factory age; and I don’t take up his cause only, though he’s a poor good-for-nought, as can only manage two looms at a time, but I take up th’ cause o’ justice. Why are we to have less wage now, I ask, than two year ago?”\n\n“Don’t ask me,” said Margaret; “I am very ignorant. Ask some of your masters. Surely they will give you a reason for it. It is not merely an arbitrary decision of theirs, come to without reason.”\n\n“Yo’re just a foreigner, and nothing more,” said he, contemptuously. “Much yo know about it. Ask th’ masters! They’d tell us to mind our own business, and they’d mind theirs. Our business being, yo understand, to take the bated wage, and be thankful; and their business to bate us down to clemming point, to swell their profits. That’s what it is.”\n\n“But,” said Margaret, determined not to give way, although she saw she was irritating him, “the state of trade may be such as not to enable them to give you the same remuneration.”\n\n“State o’ trade! That’s just a piece o’ masters’ humbug. It’s rate o’ wages I was talking of. Th’ masters keep th’ state o’ trade in their own hands; and just walk it forward like a black bug-a-boo, to frighten naughty children with into being good. I’ll tell you it’s their part,—their cue, as some folks call it,—to beat us down, to swell their fortunes; and it’s ours to stand up and fight hard,—not for ourselves alone, but for them round about us—for justice and fair play. We help to make their profits, and we ought to help spend ’em. It’s not that we want their brass so much this time, as we’ve done many a time afore. We’n getten money laid by; and we’re resolved to stand and fall together; not a man on us will go in for less wage than th’ Union says is our due. So I say, ‘hooray for the strike,’ and let Thornton, and Slickson, and Hamper, and their set look to it!”\n\n“Thornton!” said Margaret. “Mr. Thornton of Marlborough Street?”\n\n“Aye! Thornton o’ Marlborough Mill, as we call him.”\n\n“He is one of the masters you are striving with, is he not? What sort of a master is he?”\n\n“Did yo’ ever see a bulldog? Set a bulldog on hind legs, and dress him up in coat and breeches, and yo’n just getten John Thornton.”\n\n“Nay,” said Margaret, laughing, “I deny that. Mr. Thornton is plain enough, but he’s not like a bulldog, with its short broad nose, and snarling upper lip.”\n\n“No! not in look, I grant yo. But let John Thornton get hold on a notion, and he’ll stick to it like a bulldog; yo might pull him away wi’ a pitchfork ere he’d leave go. He’s worth fighting wi’, is John Thornton. As for Slickson, I take it, some o’ these days he’ll wheedle his men back wi’ fair promises; that they’ll just get cheated out of as soon as they’re in his power again. He’ll work his fines well out on ’em, I’ll warrant. He’s as slippery as an eel, he is. He’s like a cat,—as sleek, and cunning, and fierce. It’ll never be an honest up and down fight wi’ him, as it will be wi’ Thornton. Thornton’s as dour as a door-nail; an obstinate chap, every inch on him,—th’ oud bulldog!”\n\n“Poor Bessy!” said Margaret, turning round to her. “You sigh over it all. You don’t like struggling and fighting as your father does, do you?”\n\n“No!” said she, heavily. “I’m sick on it. I could have wished to have had other talk about me in my latter days, than just the clashing and clanging and clattering that has ever wearied a’ my life long, about work and wages, and masters, and hands, and knobsticks.”\n\n“Poor wench! latter days be farred! Thou’rt looking a sight better already for a little stir and change. Beside, I shall be a deal here to make it more lively for thee.”\n\n“Tobacco-smoke chokes me!” said she, querulously.\n\n“Then I’ll never smoke no more i’ the house!” he replied, tenderly. “But why didst thou not tell me afore, thou foolish wench?”\n\nShe did not speak for a while, and then so low that only Margaret heard her:\n\n“I reckon, he’ll want a’ the comfort he can get out o’ either pipe or drink afore he’s done.”\n\nHer father went out of doors, evidently to finish his pipe.\n\nBessy said passionately,\n\n“Now am not I a fool,—am I not, Miss?—there, I knew I ought for to keep father at home, and away fro’ the folk that are always ready for to tempt a man, in time o’ strike, to go drink,—and there my tongue must needs quarrel with this pipe o’ his’n—and he’ll go off, I know he will,—as often as he wants to smoke—and nobody knows where it’ll end. I wish I’d letten myself be choked first.”\n\n“But does your father drink?” said Margaret.\n\n“No—not to say drink,” replied she, still in the same wild excited tone. “But what win ye have? There are days wi’ you as wi’ other folk, I suppose, when yo’ get up and go through th’ hours, just longing for a bit of a change—a bit of a fillip, as it were. I know I ha’ gone and bought a four-pounder out o’ another baker’s shop to common on such days, just because I sickened at the thought of going on for ever wi’ the same sight in my eyes, and the same sound in my ears, and the same taste i’ my mouth, and the same thought (or no thought, for that matter) in my head, day after day, for ever. I’ve longed for to be a man to go spreeing, even if it were only a tramp to some new place in search o’ work. And father—all men—have it stronger in ’em than me to get tired o’ sameness and work for ever. And what is ’em to do? It’s little blame to them if they do go into th’ gin-shop for to make their blood flow quicker, and more lively, and see things they never see at no other time—pictures, and looking-glass, and such like. But father never was a drunkard, though maybe, he’s got worse for drink, now and then. Only yo’ see,” and now her voice took a mournful, pleading tone, “at times o’ strike there’s much to knock a man down, for all they start so hopefully; and where’s the comfort to come fro’? He’ll get angry and mad—they all do—and then they get tired out wi’ being angry and mad, and maybe ha’ done things in their passion they’d be glad to forget. Bless yo’r sweet pitiful face! but yo’ dunnot know what a strike is yet.”\n\n“Come, Bessy,” said Margaret, “I won’t say you’re exaggerating, because I don’t know enough about it: but, perhaps, as you’re not well, you’re only looking on one side, and there is another and a brighter to be looked to.”\n\n“It’s all well enough for yo’ to say so, who have lived in pleasant green places all your life long, and never known want or care, or wickedness either, for that matter.”\n\n“Take care,” said Margaret, her cheek flushing, and her eye lightening, “how you judge, Bessy. I shall go home to my mother, who is so ill—so ill, Bessy, that there’s no outlet but death for her out of prison of her great suffering; and yet I must speak cheerfully to my father, who has no notion of her real state, and to whom the knowledge must come gradually. The only person—the only one who could sympathise with me and help me—whose presence could comfort my mother more than any other earthly thing—is falsely accused—would run the risk of death if he came to see his dying mother. This I tell you—only you, Bessy. You must not mention it. No other person in Milton—hardly any other person in England knows. Have I not care? Do I not know anxiety, though I go about well-dressed, and have food enough? Oh, Bessy, God is just, and our lots are well portioned out by Him, although none but He knows the bitterness of our souls.”\n\n“I ask your pardon,” replied Bessy, humbly. “Sometimes, when I’ve thought o’ my life, and the little pleasure I’ve had in it, I’ve believed that, maybe, I was one of those doomed to die by the falling of a star from heaven; ‘And the name of the star is called Wormwood; and the third part of the waters became wormwood; and men died of the waters, because they were made bitter.’ One can bear pain and sorrow better if one thinks it has been prophesied long before for one: somehow, then it seems as if my pain was needed for the fulfilment; otherways it seems all sent for nothing.”\n\n“Nay, Bessy—think!” said Margaret. “God does not willingly inflict. Don’t dwell so much on the prophecies, but read the clearer parts of the Bible.”\n\n“I dare say it would be wiser; but where would I hear such grand words of promise—hear tell o’ anything so far different fro’ this dreary world, and this town above a’ as in Revelations? Many’s the time I’ve repeated the verses in the seventh chapter to myself, just for the sound. It’s as good as an organ, and as different from every day, too. No, I cannot give up Revelations. It gives me more comfort than any other book i’ the Bible.”\n\n“Let me come and read you some of my favourite chapters.”\n\n“Ay,” said she greedily, “come. Father will maybe hear yo.’ He’s deaved wi’ my talking; he says it’s all nought to do with the things o’ to-day, and that’s his business.”\n\n“Where is your sister?”\n\n“Gone fustian cutting. I were loth to let her go; but somehow we must live; and th’ Union can’t afford us much.”\n\n“Now I must go. You have done me good, Bessy.”\n\n“I done you good!”\n\n“Yes, I came here very sad, and rather too apt to think my own cause for grief was the only one in the world. And now I hear how you have had to bear for years, and that makes me stronger.”\n\n“Bless yo’! I thought a’ the good-doing was on the side of gentlefolk. I shall get proud if I think I can do good to yo’.”\n\n“You won’t do it if you think about it. But you’ll only puzzle yourself if you do, that’s one comfort.”\n\n“You’re not like no one I ever seed. I dunno what to make of yo’.”\n\n“Nor I of myself. Good-bye!”\n\nBessy stilled her rocking to gaze after her.\n\n“I wonder if there are many folk like her down South. She’s like a breath of country air, somehow. She freshens me up above a bit. Who’d a thought that face—as bright and as strong as the angel I dream of—could have known the sorrow she speaks on? I wonder how she’ll sin. All on us must sin. I think a deal on her, for sure. But father does the like, I see. And Mary even. It’s not often hoo’s stirred up to notice much.”\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII\n\n“My heart revolts within me, and two voices Make themselves audible within my bosom.” WALLENSTEIN.\n\nOn Margaret’s return home she found two letters on the table: one was a note for her mother,—the other, which had come by the post, was evidently from her aunt Shaw—covered with foreign post-marks—thin, silvery, and rustling. She took up the other, and was examining it, when her father came in suddenly:\n\n“So your mother is tired, and gone to bed early! I’m afraid, such a thundery day was not the best in the world for the doctor to see her. What did he say? Dixon tells me he spoke to you about her.”\n\nMargaret hesitated. Her father’s looks became more grave and anxious:\n\n“He does not think her seriously ill?”\n\n“Not at present; she needs care, he says; he was very kind, and he said he would call again, and see how his medicines worked.”\n\n“Only care?—he did not recommend change of air?—he did not say this smoky town was doing her any harm, did he, Margaret?”\n\n“No! not a word,” she replied, gravely. “He was anxious, I think.”\n\n“Doctors have that anxious manner; it’s professional,” said he.\n\nMargaret saw, in her father’s nervous ways, that the first impression of possible danger was made upon his mind, in spite of all his making light of what she told him. He could not forget the subject,—could not pass from it to other things; he kept recurring to it through the evening, with an unwillingness to receive even the slightest unfavourable idea, which made Margaret inexpressibly sad.\n\n“This letter is from Aunt Shaw, papa. She has got to Naples, and finds it too hot, so she has taken apartments at Sorrento. But I don’t think she likes Italy.”\n\n“He did not say anything about diet, did he?”\n\n“It was to be nourishing, and digestible. Mamma’s appetite is pretty good, I think.”\n\n“Yes! and that makes it all the more strange he should have thought of speaking about diet.”\n\n“I asked him, papa.” Another pause. Then Margaret went on: “Aunt Shaw says she has sent me some coral ornaments, papa: but,” added Margaret, half smiling, “she’s afraid the Milton dissenters won’t appreciate them. She has got all her ideas of Dissenters from the Quakers, has she not?”\n\n“If ever you hear or notice that your mother wishes for anything, be sure you let me know. I am so afraid she does not tell me always what she would like. Pray, see after that girl Mrs. Thornton named. If we had a good, efficient house-servant, Dixon could be constantly with her, and I’d answer for it we’d soon set her up amongst us, if care will do it. She’s been very much tired of late, with the hot weather, and the difficulty of getting a servant. A little rest will put her quite to rights—eh, Margaret?”\n\n“I hope so,” said Margaret,—but so sadly, that her father took notice of it. He pinched her cheek.\n\n“Come; if you look so pale as this, I must rouge you up a little. Take care of yourself, child, or you’ll be wanting the doctor next.”\n\nBut he could not settle to anything that evening. He was continually going backwards and forwards, on laborious tiptoe, to see if his wife was still asleep. Margaret’s heart ached at his restlessness—his trying to stifle and strangle the hideous fear that was looming out of the dark places of his heart.\n\nHe came back at last, somewhat comforted.\n\n“She’s awake now, Margaret. She quite smiled as she saw me standing by her. Just her old smile. And she says she feels refreshed, and ready for tea. Where’s the note for her? She want’s to see it. I’ll read it to her while you make tea.”\n\nThe note proved to be a formal invitation from Mrs. Thornton, to Mr., Mrs., and Miss Hale to dinner, on the twenty-first instant. Margaret was surprised to find an acceptance contemplated, after all she had learnt of sad probabilities during the day. But so it was. The idea of her husband’s and daughter’s going to this dinner had quite captivated Mrs. Hale’s fancy, even before Margaret had heard the contents of the note. It was an event to diversify the monotony of the invalid’s life; and she clung to the idea of their going, with even fretful pertinacity, when Margaret objected.\n\n“Nay, Margaret: if she wishes it, I’m sure we’ll both go willingly. She never would wish it unless she felt herself really stronger—really better than we thought she was, eh, Margaret?” said Mr. Hale, anxiously, as she prepared to write the note of acceptance, the next day.\n\n“Eh! Margaret?” questioned he, with a nervous motion of his hands. It seemed cruel to refuse him the comfort he craved for. And besides, his passionate refusal to admit the existence of fear, almost inspired Margaret herself with hope.\n\n“I do think she is better since last night,” said she. “Her eyes look brighter, and her complexion clearer.”\n\n“God bless you,” said her father earnestly. “But is it true? Yesterday was so sultry every one felt ill. It was a most unlucky day for Mr. Donaldson to see her on.”\n\nSo he went away to his day’s duties, now increased by the preparation of some lectures he had promised to deliver to the working people at a neighbouring Lyceum. He had chosen Ecclesiastical Architecture as his subject, rather more in accordance with his own taste and knowledge than as falling in with the character of the place or the desire for particular kinds of information among those to whom he was to lecture. And the institution itself, being in debt, was only too glad to get a gratis course from an educated and accomplished man like Mr. Hale, let the subject be what it might.\n\n“Well, mother,” asked Mr. Thornton that night, “who have accepted your invitations for the twenty-first?”\n\n“Fanny, where are the notes? The Slicksons accept, Collingbrooks accept, Stephenses accept, Browns decline. Hales—father and daughter come—mother too great an invalid—Macphersons come, and Mr. Horsfall, and Mr. Young. I was thinking of asking the Porters, as the Browns can’t come.”\n\n“Very good. Do you know, I’m really afraid Mrs. Hale is very far from well, from what Dr. Donaldson says.”\n\n“It’s strange of them to accept a dinner-invitation if she’s very ill,” said Fanny.\n\n“I didn’t say very ill,” said her brother, rather sharply. “I only said far from well. They may not know it either.” And then he suddenly remembered that, from what Dr. Donaldson had told him, Margaret, at any rate, must be aware of the exact state of the case.\n\n“Very probably they are quite aware of what you said yesterday, John—of the great advantage it would be to them—to Mr. Hale, I mean, to be introduced to such people as the Stephenses and the Collingbrooks.”\n\n“I’m sure that motive would not influence them. No! I think I understand how it is.”\n\n“John!” said Fanny, laughing in her little, weak, nervous way. “How you profess to understand these Hales, and how you never will allow that we can know anything about them. Are they really so very different to most people one meets with?”\n\nShe did not mean to vex him; but if she had intended it, she could not have done it more thoroughly. He chafed in silence, however, not deigning to reply to her question.\n\n“They do not seem to me out of the common way,” said Mrs. Thornton. “He appears a worthy kind of man enough; rather too simple for trade—so it’s perhaps as well he should have been a clergyman first, and now a teacher. She’s a bit of a fine lady, with her invalidism; and as for the girl—she’s the only one who puzzles me when I think about her,—which I don’t often do. She seems to have a great notion of giving herself airs; and I can’t make out why. I could almost fancy she thinks herself too good for her company at times. And yet they’re not rich; from all I can hear they never have been.”\n\n“And she’s not accomplished, mamma. She can’t play.”\n\n“Go on, Fanny. What else does she want to bring her up to your standard?”\n\n“Nay! John,” said his mother, “that speech of Fanny’s did no harm. I myself heard Miss Hale say she could not play. If you would let us alone, we could perhaps like her, and see her merits.”\n\n“I’m sure I never could!” murmured Fanny, protected by her mother. Mr. Thornton heard, but did not care to reply. He was walking up and down the dining-room, wishing that his mother would order candles, and allow him to set to work at either reading or writing, and so put a stop to the conversation. But he never thought of interfering in any of the small domestic regulations that Mrs. Thornton observed, in habitual remembrance of her old economies.\n\n“Mother,” said he, stopping, and bravely speaking out the truth, “I wish you would like Miss Hale.”\n\n“Why,” asked she, startled by his earnest, yet tender manner. “You’re never thinking of marrying her?—a girl without a penny.”\n\n“She would never have me,” said he, with a short laugh.\n\n“No, I don’t think she would,” answered his mother. “She laughed in my face, when I praised her for speaking out something Mr. Bell had said in your favour. I liked the girl for doing so frankly, for it made me sure she had no thought of you; and the next minute she vexed me so by seeming to think—— Well, never mind! Only you’re right in saying she’s too good an opinion of herself to think of you! The saucy jade! I should like to know where she’d find a better!”\n\n“Well, as I’m just as much convinced of the truth of what you have been saying as you can be; and as I have no thought or expectation of ever asking her to be my wife, you’ll believe me for the future that I’m quite disinterested in speaking about her. I foresee trouble for that girl—perhaps, want of motherly care—and I only wish you to be ready to be a friend to her, in case she needs one. Now, Fanny,” said he, “I trust you have delicacy enough to understand, that it is as great an injury to Miss Hale as to me—in fact, she would think it a greater—to suppose that I have any reason, more than I now give, for begging you and my mother to show her every kindly attention.”\n\n“I cannot forgive her her pride,” said his mother; “I will befriend her, if there is need, for your asking, John. I would befriend Jezebel herself if you asked me. But this girl, who turns up her nose at us all—who turns up her nose at you——”\n\n“Nay, mother; I have never yet put myself, and I mean never to put myself, within reach of her contempt.”\n\n“Contempt, indeed!”—(One of Mrs. Thornton’s expressive snorts.)—“Don’t go on speaking of Miss Hale, John, if I’ve to be kind to her. When I’m with her, I don’t know if I like or dislike her most; but when I think of her, and hear you talk of her, I hate her. I can see she’s given herself airs to you as well as if you’d told me out.”\n\n“And if she has,” said he—and then he paused for a moment—then went on: “I’m not a lad, to be cowed by a proud look from a woman, or to care for her misunderstanding me and my position. I can laugh at it!”\n\n“To be sure! and at her too, with her fine notions, and haughty tosses!”\n\n“I only wonder why you talk so much about her, then,” said Fanny. “I’m sure, I’m tired enough of the subject.”\n\n“Well!” said her brother, with a shade of bitterness. “Suppose we find some more agreeable subject. What do you say to a strike, by way of something pleasant to talk about?”\n\n“Have the hands actually turned out?” asked Mrs. Thornton, with vivid interest.\n\n“Hamper’s men are actually out. Mine are working out their week, through fear of being prosecuted for breach of contract. I’d have had every one of them up and punished for it, that left his work before his time was out.”\n\n“The law expenses would have been more than the hands themselves were worth—a set of ungrateful naughts!” said his mother.\n\n“To be sure. But I’d have shown them how to keep my word, and how I mean them to keep their’s. They know me by this time. Slickson’s men are off—pretty certain he won’t spend money in getting them punished. We’re in for a turn-out, mother.”\n\n“I hope there are not many orders in hand?”\n\n“Of course there are. They know that well enough. But they don’t quite understand all, though they think they do.”\n\n“What do you mean, John?”\n\nCandles had been brought, and Fanny had taken up her interminable piece of worsted-work, over which she was yawning; throwing herself back in her chair, from time to time to gaze at vacancy, and think of nothing at her ease.\n\n“Why,” said he, “the Americans are getting their yarns so into the general market, that our only chance is producing them at a lower rate. If we can’t, we may shut up shop at once, and hands and masters alike go on tramp. Yet these fools go back to the prices paid three years ago—nay, some of their leaders quote Dickinson’s price now—though they know as well as we do that, what with fines pressed out of their wages, as no honourable man would extort them, the real rate of wage paid at Dickinson’s is less than at ours. Upon my word, mother, I wish the old combination laws were in force. It is too bad to find out that fools—ignorant, wayward men like these—just by uniting their silly heads, are to rule over the fortunes of those who bring all the wisdom that knowledge and experience, and often painful thought and anxiety, can give. The next thing will be—indeed, we’ve all but come to it now—that we shall have to go and ask—stand hat in hand—and humbly ask the secretary of the Spinner’s Union to be so kind as to furnish us with labour at their own price. That’s what they want—they, who haven’t the sense to see that, if we don’t get a fair share of the profits to compensate us for our wear and tear here in England, we can move off to some other country; and that, what with home and foreign competition, we are none of us likely to make above a fair share, and may be thankful enough if we can get that, in an average number of years.”\n\n“Can’t you get hands from Ireland? I wouldn’t keep these fellows a day. I’d teach them that I was master, and could employ what servants I liked.”\n\n“Yes! to be sure, I can; and will, too, if they go on long. It will be trouble and expense, and I fear there will be some danger; but I will do it, rather than give in.”\n\n“If there is to be all this extra expense, I’m sorry we’re giving a dinner just now.”\n\n“So am I,—not because of the expense, but because I shall have much to think about, and many unexpected calls on my time. But we must have had Mr. Horsfall, and he does not stay in Milton long. And as for the others, we owe them dinners, and it’s all one trouble.”\n\nHe kept on with his restless walk—not speaking any more, but drawing a deep breath from time to time, as if endeavouring to throw off some annoying thought. Fanny asked her mother numerous small questions, all having nothing to do with the subject, which a wiser person would have perceived was occupying her attention. Consequently, she received many short answers. She was not sorry when, at ten o’clock, the servants filed in to prayers. These her mother always read,—first reading a chapter. They were now working steadily through the Old Testament. When prayers were ended, and his mother had wished him good-night, with that long steady look of hers which conveyed no expression of the tenderness that was in her heart, but yet had the intensity of a blessing, Mr. Thornton continued his walk. All his business plans had received a check, a sudden pull-up, from this approaching turn-out. The forethought of many anxious hours was thrown away, utterly wasted by their insane folly, which would injure themselves even more than him, though no one could set any limit to the mischief they were doing. And these were the men who thought themselves fitted to direct the masters in the disposal of their capital; Hamper had said, only this very day, that if he were ruined by the strike, he would start life again, comforted by the conviction that those who had brought it on were in a worse predicament than he himself,—for he had head as well as hands, while they had only hands; and if they drove away their market, they could not follow it, nor turn to anything else. But this thought was no consolation to Mr. Thornton. It might be that revenge gave him no pleasure; it might be that he valued the position he had earned with the sweat of his brow, so much that he keenly felt its being endangered by the ignorance or folly of others,—so keenly that he had no thoughts to spare for what would be the consequences of their conduct to themselves. He paced up and down, setting his teeth now and then. At last it struck two. The candles were flickering in their sockets. He lighted his own, muttering to himself:\n\n“Once for all they shall know whom they have got to deal with. I can give them a fortnight,—no more. If they don’t see their madness before the end of that time, I must have hands from Ireland. I believe it’s Slickson’s doing,—confound him and his dodges! He thought he was overstocked; so he seemed to yield at first, when the deputation came to him,—and of course, he only confirmed them in their folly, as he meant to do. That’s where it spread from.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIX\n\n“As angels in some brighter dreams Call to the soul when man doth sleep, So some strange thoughts transcend our wonted themes, And into glory peep.” HENRY VAUGHAN.\n\nMrs. Hale was curiously amused and interested by the idea of the Thornton dinner party. She kept wondering about the details, with something of the simplicity of a little child, who wants to have all its anticipated pleasure described beforehand. But the monotonous life led by invalids often makes them like children, inasmuch as they have neither of them any sense of proportion in events, and seem each to believe that the walls and curtains which shut in their world, and shut out everything else, must of necessity be larger than anything hidden beyond. Besides Mrs. Hale had had her vanities as a girl; had perhaps unduly felt their mortification when she became a poor clergyman’s wife;—they had been smothered and kept down; but they were not extinct; and she liked to think of seeing Margaret dressed for a party, and discussed what she should wear, with an unsettled anxiety that amused Margaret, who had been more accustomed to society in her one year in Harley Street than her mother in five and twenty years of Helstone.\n\n“Then you think you shall wear your white silk. Are you sure it will fit? It’s nearly a year since Edith was married!”\n\n“Oh, yes, mamma! Mrs. Murray made it, and it’s sure to be right; it may be a straw’s breadth shorter or longer-waisted, according to my having grown fat or thin. But I don’t think I’ve altered in the least.”\n\n“Hadn’t you better let Dixon see it? It may have gone yellow with lying by.”\n\n“If you like, mamma. But if the worst comes to the worst, I’ve a very nice pink gauze which Aunt Shaw gave me, only two or three months before Edith was married. That can’t have gone yellow.”\n\n“No! but it may have faded.”\n\n“Well! then I’ve a green silk. I feel more as if it was the embarrassment of riches.”\n\n“I wish I knew what you ought to wear,” said Mrs. Hale, nervously.\n\nMargaret’s manner changed instantly. “Shall I go and put them on one after another, mamma, and then you could see which you liked best?”\n\n“But—yes! perhaps that will be best.”\n\nSo off Margaret went. She was very much inclined to play some pranks when she was dressed up at such an unusual hour; to make her rich white silk balloon out into a cheese, to retreat backwards from her mother as if she were the queen; but when she found that these freaks of hers were regarded as interruptions to the serious business, and as such annoyed her mother, she became grave and sedate. What had possessed the world (her world) to fidget so about her dress, she could not understand; but that very afternoon, on naming her engagement to Bessy Higgins (apropos of the servant that Mrs. Thornton had promised to enquire about), Bessy quite roused up at the intelligence.\n\n“Dear! and are you going to dine at Thornton’s at Marlborough Mills?”\n\n“Yes, Bessy. Why are you so surprised?”\n\n“Oh, I dunno. But they visit wi’ a’ th’ first folk in Milton.”\n\n“And you don’t think we’re quite the first folk in Milton, eh, Bessy?”\n\nBessy’s cheeks flushed a little at her thought being thus easily read.\n\n“Well,” said she, “yo’ see, they thinken a deal o’ money here; and I reckon yo’ve not getten much.”\n\n“No,” said Margaret, “that’s very true. But we are educated people, and have lived amongst educated people. Is there anything so wonderful, in our being asked out to dinner by a man who owns himself inferior to my father by coming to him to be instructed? I don’t mean to blame Mr. Thornton. Few drapers’ assistants, as he was once, could have made themselves what he is.”\n\n“But can yo’ give dinners back, in yo’re small house? Thornton’s house is three times as big.”\n\n“Well, I think we could manage to give Mr. Thornton a dinner back, as you call it. Perhaps not in such a large room, nor with so many people. But I don’t think we’ve thought about it at all in that way.”\n\n“I never thought yo’d be dining with Thorntons,” repeated Bessy. “Why, the mayor hissel’ dines there; and the members of Parliament and all.”\n\n“I think, I could support the honour of meeting the mayor of Milton.”\n\n“But them ladies dress so grand!” said Bessy, with an anxious look at Margaret’s print gown, which her Milton eyes appraised at sevenpence a yard.\n\nMargaret’s face dimpled up into a merry laugh. “Thank you, Bessy, for thinking so kindly about my looking nice among all the smart people. But I’ve plenty of grand gowns,—a week ago, I should have said they were far too grand for anything I should ever want here. But as I’m to dine at Mr. Thornton’s, and perhaps to meet the mayor, I shall put on my very best gown, you may be sure.”\n\n“What win yo’ wear?” asked Bessy, somewhat relieved.\n\n“White silk,” said Margaret. “A gown I had for a cousin’s wedding, a year ago.”\n\n“That’ll do!” said Bessy, falling back in her chair. “I should be loth to have yo’ looked down upon.”\n\n“Oh! I’ll be fine enough, if that will save me from being looked down upon in Milton.”\n\n“I wish I could see you dressed up,” said Bessy. “I reckon, yo’re not what folk would ca’ pretty; yo’ve not red and white enough for that. But dun yo’ know, I ha’ dreamt of yo’, long afore ever I seed yo’.”\n\n“Nonsense, Bessy!”\n\n“Ay, but I did. Yo’r very face,—looking wi’ yo’r clear steadfast eyes out o’ th’ darkness, wi’ yo’r hair blown off from yo’r brow, and going out like rays round yo’r forehead, which was just as smooth and straight as it is now,—and yo’ always came to give me strength, which I seemed to gather out o’ yo’r deep comforting eyes,—and yo’ was drest in shining raiment—just as yo’r going to be drest. So, yo’ see, it was yo’!”\n\n“Nay, Bessy,” said Margaret, gently, “it was but a dream.”\n\n“And why might na I dream a dream in my affliction as well as others? Did not many a one i’ the Bible? Ay, and see visions too! Why, even my father thinks a deal o’ dreams! I tell yo’ again, I saw yo’ as plainly, coming swiftly towards me, wi’ yo’r hair blown back wi’ the very swiftness o’ the motion, just like the way it grows, a little standing off like; and the white shining dress on yo’ve getten to wear. Let me come and see yo’ in it. I want to see yo’ and touch yo’ as in very deed yo’ were in my dream.”\n\n“My dear Bessy, it is quite a fancy of yours.”\n\n“Fancy or no fancy,—yo’ve come, as I knew yo’ would, when I saw yo’r movement in my dream,—and when yo’re here about me, I reckon I feel easier in my mind, and comforted, just as a fire comforts one on a dree day. Yo’ said it were on th’ twenty-first; please God, I’ll come and see yo’.”\n\n“Oh Bessy! you may come and welcome; but don’t talk so—it really makes me sorry. It does indeed.”\n\n“Then I’ll keep it to mysel’, if I bite my tongue out. Not but what it’s true for all that.”\n\nMargaret was silent. At last she said, “Let us talk about it sometimes, if you think it true. But not now. Tell me, has your father turned out?”\n\n“Ay!” said Bessy heavily—in a manner very different from that she had spoken in but a minute or two before. “He and many another,—all Hamper’s men,—and many a one besides. Th’ women are as bad as th’ men, in their savageness, this time. Food is high,—and they mun have food for their childer, I reckon. Suppose Thorntons sent ’em their dinner out,—th’ same money, spent on potatoes and meal, would keep many a crying baby quiet, and hush up its mother’s heart for a bit!”\n\n“Don’t speak so!” said Margaret. “You’ll make me feel wicked and guilty in going to this dinner.”\n\n“No!” said Bessy. “Some’s pre-elected to sumptuous feasts, and purple and fine linen,—may be yo’re one on ’em. Others toil and moil all their lives long—and the very dogs are not pitiful in our days, as they were in the days of Lazarus. But if yo’ ask me to cool yo’re tongue wi’ th’ tip of my finger, I’ll come across the great gulf to yo’ just for th’ thought o’ what yo’ve been to me here.”\n\n“Bessy; you’re very feverish! I can tell it in the touch of your hand, as well us in what you’re saying. It won’t be division enough, in that awful day, that some of us have been beggars here, and some of us have been rich,—we shall not be judged by that poor accident, but by our faithful following of Christ.”\n\nMargaret got up, and found some water; and soaking her pocket-handkerchief in it, she laid the cool wetness on Bessy’s forehead, and began to chafe the stone-cold feet. Bessy shut her eyes, and allowed herself to be soothed. At last she said,\n\n“Yo’d ha’ been deaved out o’ yo’r five wits, as well as me, if yo’d had one body after another coming in to ask for father, and staying to tell me each one their tale. Some spoke o’ deadly hatred, and made my blood run cold wi’ the terrible things they said o’ th’ masters,—but more, being women, kept plaining, plaining (wi’ the tears running down their cheeks, and never wiped away, nor heeded), of the price o’ meat, and how their childer could na sleep at nights for th’ hunger.”\n\n“And do they think the strike will mend this?” asked Margaret.\n\n“They say so,” replied Bessy. “They do say trade has been good for long, and the masters has made no end o’ money; how much father doesn’t know, but, in course, th’ Union does; and, as it is natural, they wanted their share o’ th’ profits, now that food is getting dear; and th’ Union says they’ll not be doing their duty if they don’t make th’ masters give ’em their share. But masters has getten th’ upper hand somehow; and I’m feared they’ll keep it now and evermore. It’s like th’ great battle o’ Armageddon, the way they keep on, grinning and fighting at each other, till even while they fight, they are picked off into the pit.”\n\nJust then, Nicholas Higgins came in. He caught his daughter’s last words.\n\n“Ay! and I’ll fight on too; and I’ll got it this time. It’ll not take long for to make ’em give in, for they’ve getten a pretty lot of orders, all under contract; and they’ll soon find out they’d better give us our five per cent. than lose the profit they’ll gain; let alone the fine for not fulfilling the contract. Aha my masters! I know who’ll win.”\n\nMargaret fancied from his manner that he must have been drinking, not so much from what he said, as from the excited way in which he spoke; and she was rather confirmed in this idea by the evident anxiety Bessie showed to hasten her departure. Bessy said to her,—\n\n“The twenty-first—that’s Thursday week. I may come and see yo’ dressed for Thornton’s, I reckon. What time is yo’r dinner?”\n\nBefore Margaret could answer, Higgins broke out,—\n\n“Thornton’s! Ar’ t’ going to dine at Thornton’s? Ask him to give yo’ a bumper to the success of his orders. By th’ twenty-first, I reckon, he’ll be pottered in his brains how to get ’em done in time. Tell him, there’s seven hundred ’ll come marching into Marlborough Mills, the morning after he gives the five per cent., and will help him through his contract in no time. You’ll have ’em all there. My master, Hamper. He’s one o’ th’ oud-fashioned sort. Ne’er meets a man bout an oath or a curse; I should think he were going to die if he spoke me civil; but arter all, his bark’s waur than his bite, and yo’ may tell him one o’ his turn-outs said so, if yo’ like. Eh! but yo’ll have a lot of prize mill-owners at Thornton’s! I should like to get speech o’ them, when they’re a bit inclined to sit still after dinner, and could na run for the life on ’em. I’d tell ’em my mind. I’d speak up again th’ hard way they’re driving on us!”\n\n“Good-bye!” said Margaret, hastily. “Good-bye, Bessy! I shall look to see you on the twenty-first, if you’re well enough.”\n\nThe medicines and treatment which Dr. Donaldson had ordered for Mrs. Hale, did her so much good at first that not only she herself, but Margaret, began to hope that he might have been mistaken, and that she could recover permanently. As for Mr. Hale, although he had never had an idea of the serious nature of their apprehensions, he triumphed over their fears with an evident relief, which proved how much his glimpse into the nature of them had affected him. Only Dixon croaked for ever into Margaret’s ear. However, Margaret defied the raven, and would hope.\n\nThey needed this gleam of brightness in-doors, for out-of-doors, even to their uninstructed eyes, there was a gloomy brooding appearance of discontent. Mr. Hale had his own acquaintances among the working men, and was depressed with their earnestly-told tales of suffering and long-endurance. They would have scorned to speak of what they had to bear to any one who might, from his position, have understood it without their words. But here was this man, from a distant county, who was perplexed by the workings of a system into the midst of which he was thrown, and each was eager to make him a judge, and to bring witness of his own causes for irritation. Then Mr. Hale brought all his budget of grievances, and laid it before Mr. Thornton, for him, with his experience as a master, to arrange them, and explain their origin; which he always did, on sound economical principles; showing that, as trade was conducted, there must always be a waxing and waning of commercial prosperity; and that in the waning a certain number of masters, as well as of men, must go down into ruin, and be no more seen among the ranks of the happy and prosperous. He spoke as if this consequence were so entirely logical, that neither employers nor employed had any right to complain if it became their fate: the employer to turn aside from the race he could no longer run, with a bitter sense of incompetency and failure—wounded in the struggle—trampled down by his fellows in their haste to get rich—slighted where he was once honoured—humbly asking for, instead of bestowing, employment with a lordly hand. Of course, speaking so of the fate that, as a master, might be his own in the fluctuations of commerce, he was not likely to have more sympathy with that of the workmen, who were passed by in the swift merciless improvement or alteration; who would fain lie down and quietly die out of the world that needed them not, but felt as they could never rest in their graves for the clinging cries of the beloved and helpless they would leave behind; who envied the power of the wild bird, that can feed her young with her very heart’s blood. Margaret’s whole soul rose up against him while he reasoned in this way—as if commerce were everything and humanity nothing. She could hardly thank him for the individual kindness, which brought him that very evening to offer her—for the delicacy which made him understand that he must offer her privately—every convenience for illness that his own wealth or his mother’s foresight had caused them to accumulate in their household, and which, as he learnt from Dr. Donaldson, Mrs. Hale might possibly require. His presence, after the way he had spoken—his bringing before her the doom, which she was vainly trying to persuade herself might yet be averted from her mother—all conspired to set Margaret’s teeth on edge, as she looked at him, and listened to him. What business had he to be the only person, except Dr. Donaldson and Dixon, admitted to the awful secret, which she held shut up in the most dark and sacred recess of her heart—not daring to look at it, unless she invoked heavenly strength to bear the sight—that, some day soon, she should cry aloud for her mother, and no answer would come out of the blank, dumb darkness? Yet he knew all. She saw it in his pitying eyes. She heard it in his grave and tremulous voice. How reconcile those eyes, that voice, with the hard, reasoning, dry, merciless way in which he laid down axioms of trade, and serenely followed them out to their full consequences? The discord jarred upon her inexpressibly. The more because of the gathering woe of which she heard from Bessy. To be sure, Nicholas Higgins, the father, spoke differently. He had been appointed a committee-man, and said that he knew secrets of which the exoteric knew nothing. He said this more expressly and particularly, on the very day before Mrs. Thornton’s dinner-party. When Margaret, going in to speak to Bessy, found him arguing the point with Boucher, the neighbour of whom she had frequently heard mention, as by turns exciting Higgins’s compassion, as an unskilful workman with a large family depending upon him for support, and at other times enraging his more energetic and sanguine neighbour by his want of what the latter called spirit. It was very evident that Higgins was in a passion when Margaret entered. Boucher stood, with both hands on the rather high mantelpiece, swaying himself a little on the support which his arms, thus placed, gave him, and looking wildly into the fire, with a kind of despair that irritated Higgins, even while it went to his heart. Bessy was rocking herself violently backwards and forwards, as was her wont (Margaret knew by this time) when she was agitated. Her sister Mary was tying on her bonnet (in great clumsy bows, as suited her great clumsy fingers), to go to her fustian-cutting, blubbering out loud the while, and evidently longing to be away from a scene that distressed her.\n\nMargaret came in upon this scene. She stood for a moment at the door—then, her finger on her lips, she stole to a seat on the squab near Bessy. Nicholas saw her come in, and greeted her with a gruff, but not unfriendly nod. Mary hurried out of the house, catching gladly at the open door, and crying aloud when she got away from her father’s presence. It was only John Boucher that took no notice whatever who came in and who went out.\n\n“It’s no use, Higgins. Hoo cannot live long a’ this’n. Hoo’s just sinking away—not for want o’ meat hersel’—but because hoo cannot stand th’ sight o’ the little ones clemming. Ay, clemming! Five shilling a week may do well enough for thee, wi’ but two mouths to fill, and one on ’em a wench who can well earn her own meat. But it’s clemming to us. An’ I tell thee plain—if hoo dies, as I’m ’feard hoo will afore we’ve getten th’ five per cent., I’ll fling the money back i’ th’ master’s face, and say, ‘Be domned to yo’; be domned to th’ whole cruel world o’ yo’; that could na leave me th’ best wife that ever bore childer to a man. An’ look thee lad, I’ll hate thee, and th’ whole pack o’ th’ Union. Ay, an’ chase yo’ through heaven wi’ my hatred,—I will, lad! I will,—if yo’re leading me astray i’ this matter. Thou saidst, Nicholas, on Wednesday sennight—and it’s now Tuesday i’ th’ second week—that afore a fortnight we’d ha’ the masters coming a-begging to us to take back our work, at our own wage—and time’s nearly up,—and there’s our lile Jack lying a-bed, too weak to cry, but just every now and then sobbing up his heart for want o’ food,—our lile Jack, I tell thee, lad! Hoo’s never looked up sin’ he were born, and hoo loves him as if he were her very life,—as he is,—for I reckon he’ll ha’ cost me that precious price,—our lile Jack, who wakened me each morn wi’ putting his sweet little lips to my great rough fou’ face, a-seeking a smooth place to kiss,—an’ he lies clemming.” Here the deep sobs choked the poor man and Nicholas looked up, with eyes brimful of tears, to Margaret, before he could gain courage to speak.\n\n“Hou’d up, man. Thy lile Jack shall na’ clem. I ha’ gotten brass, and we’ll go buy the chap a sup o’ milk an’ a good four-pounder this very minute. What’s mine’s thine, sure enough, i’ thou’st i’ want. Only, dunnot lose heart, man!” continued he, as he fumbled in a tea-pot for what money he had. “I lay yo my heart and soul we’ll win for a’ this: it’s but bearing on one more week, and yo’ just see th’ way th’ masters ’ll come round, praying on us to come back to our mills. An’ the Union—that’s to say, I—will take care yo’ve enough for th’ childer and th’ missus. So dunnot turn faint-heart, and go to th’ tyrants a-seeking work.”\n\nThe man turned round at these words,—turned round a face so white, and gaunt, and tear-furrowed, and hopeless, that its very calm forced Margaret to weep.\n\n“Yo’ know well, that a worser tyrant than e’er th’ masters were says, ‘Clem to death, and see ’em a’ clem to death, ere yo’ dare go again th’ Union.’ Yo’ know it well, Nicholas, for a’ yo’re one on e’m. Yo’ may be kind hearts, each separate; but once banded together, yo’ve no more pity for a man than a wild hunger-maddened wolf.”\n\nNicholas had his hand on the lock of the door—he stopped and turned round on Boucher, close following:\n\n“So help me God! man alive—if I think not I’m doing best for thee, and for all on us. If I’m going wrong when I think I’m going right, it’s their sin, who ha’ left me where I am, in my ignorance. I ha’ thought till my brains ached,—Beli’ me, John, I have. An’ I say again, there’s no help for us but having faith i’ th’ Union. They’ll win the day, see if they dunnot!”\n\nNot one word had Margaret or Bessy spoken. They had hardly uttered the sighing, that the eyes of each called to the other to bring up from the depths of her heart. At last Bessy said,\n\n“I never thought to hear father call on God again. But yo’ heard him say, ‘So help me God!’”\n\n“Yes!” said Margaret. “Let me bring you what money I can spare,—let me bring you a little food for that poor man’s children. Don’t let them know it comes from any one but your father. It will be but little.”\n\nBessy lay back without taking any notice of what Margaret said. She did not cry—she only quivered up her breath.\n\n“My heart’s drained dry o’ tears,” she said. “Boucher’s been in these days past, a telling me of his fears and his troubles. He’s but a weak kind of chap, I know, but he’s a man for a’ that; and tho’ I’ve been angry, many a time afore now, wi’ him an’ his wife, as knew no more nor him how to manage, yet yo’ see, all folks isn’t wise, yet God lets ’em live—ay, an’ gives ’em some one to love, and be loved by, just as good as Solomon. An’ if sorrow comes to them they love, it hurts ’em as sore as e’er it did Solomon. I can’t make it out. Perhaps it’s as well such a one as Boucher has th’ Union to see after him. But I’d just like for to see th’ men as make th’ Union, and put ’em one by one face to face wi’ Boucher. I reckon, if they heard him, the’d tell him (if I cotched ’em one by one), he might go back and get what he could for his work, even if it weren’t so much as they ordered.”\n\nMargaret sat utterly silent. How was she ever to go away into comfort and forget that man’s voice, with the tone of unutterable agony, telling more by far than his words of what he had to suffer? She took out her purse; she had not much in it of what she could call her own, but what she had she put into Bessy’s hands without speaking.\n\n“Thank yo’. There’s many on ’em gets no more, and is not so bad off,—leastways does not show it as he does. But father won’t let ’em want, now he knows. Yo’ see, Boucher’s been pulled down wi’ his childer,—and her being so cranky, and a’ they could pawn has gone this last twelvemonth. Yo’re not to think we’d ha’ letten ’em clem, for all we’re a bit pressed oursel’; if neighbours doesn’t see after neighbours, I dunno who will.” Bessy seemed almost afraid lest Margaret should think they had not the will, and, to a certain degree, the power of helping one whom she evidently regarded as having a claim upon them. “Besides,” she went on, “father is sure and positive the masters must give in within these next few days,—that they canna hould on much longer. But I thank yo’ all the same,—I thank yo’ for mysel’, as much as for Boucher, for it jus makes my heart warm to yo’ more and more.”\n\nBessy seemed much quieter to-day, but fearfully languid and exhausted. As she finished speaking, she looked so faint and weary that Margaret became alarmed.\n\n“It’s nout,” said Bessy. “It’s not death yet. I had a fearfu’ night wi’ dreams—or somewhat like dreams, for I were wide awake—and I’m all in a swounding daze to-day,—only yon poor chap made me alive again. No! it’s not death yet, but death is not far off. Ay. Cover me up, and I’ll may be sleep, if th’ cough will let me. Good night—good afternoon, m’appen I should say—but th’ light is dim an’ misty to-day.”\n\n# CHAPTER XX\n\n“Old and young, boy, let ’em all eat, I have it; Let ’em have ten tire of teeth a-piece, I care not.” ROLLO, DUKE OF NORMANDY.\n\nMargaret went home so painfully occupied with what she had heard and seen that she hardly knew how to rouse herself up to the duties which awaited her; the necessity for keeping up a constant flow of cheerful conversation for her mother, who, now that she was unable to go out, always looked to Margaret’s return from the shortest walk as bringing in some news.\n\n“And can your factory friend come on Thursday to see you dressed?”\n\n“She was so ill I never thought of asking her,” said Margaret, dolefully.\n\n“Dear! Everybody is ill now, I think,” said Mrs. Hale, with a little of the jealousy which one invalid is apt to feel of another. “But it must be very sad to be ill in one of those little back streets.” (Her kindly nature prevailing, and the old Helstone habits of thought returning.) “It’s bad enough here. What could you do for her, Margaret? Mr. Thornton has sent me some of his old port wine since you went out. Would a bottle of that do her good, think you?”\n\n“No, mamma! I don’t believe they are very poor,—at least, they don’t speak as if they were; and, at any rate, Bessy’s illness is consumption—she won’t want wine. Perhaps, I might take her a little preserve, made of our dear Helstone fruit. No! there’s another family to whom I should like to give—Oh mamma, mamma! how am I to dress up in my finery, and go off and away to smart parties, after the sorrow I’ve seen to-day?” exclaimed Margaret, bursting the bounds she had preordained for herself before she came in, and telling her mother what she had seen and heard at Higgins’s cottage.\n\nIt distressed Mrs. Hale excessively. It made her restlessly irritated till she could do something. She directed Margaret to pack up a basket in the very drawing-room, to be sent there and then to the family; and was almost angry with her for saying, that it would not signify if it did not go till morning, as she knew Higgins had provided for their immediate wants, and she herself had left money with Bessy. Mrs. Hale called her unfeeling for saying this; and never gave herself breathing-time till the basket was sent out of the house. Then she said:\n\n“After all, we may have been doing wrong. It was only the last time Mr. Thornton was here that he said, those were no true friends who helped to prolong the struggle by assisting the turn-outs. And this Boucher-man was a turn-out, was he not?”\n\nThe question was referred to Mr. Hale by his wife, when he came upstairs, fresh from giving a lesson to Mr. Thornton, which had ended in conversation, as was their wont. Margaret did not care if their gifts had prolonged the strife; she did not think far enough for that, in her present excited state.\n\nMr. Hale listened, and tried to be as calm as a judge; he recalled all that had seemed so clear not half-an-hour before, as it came out of Mr. Thornton’s lips; and then he made an unsatisfactory compromise. His wife and daughter had not only done quite right in this instance, but he did not see for a moment how they could have done otherwise. Nevertheless, as a general rule, it was very true what Mr. Thornton said, that as the strike, if prolonged, must end in the masters bringing hands from a distance (if, indeed, the final result were not, as it had often been before, the invention of some machine which would diminish the need of hands at all), why, it was clear enough that the kindest thing was to refuse all help which might bolster them up in their folly. But as to this Boucher, he would go and see him the first thing in the morning, and try and find out what could be done for him.\n\nMr. Hale went the next morning, as he proposed. He did not find Boucher at home, but he had a long talk with his wife; promised to ask for an Infirmary order for her; and, seeing the plenty provided by Mrs. Hale, and somewhat lavishly used by the children, who were masters downstairs in their father’s absence, he came back with a more consoling and cheerful account than Margaret had dared to hope for; indeed, what she had said the night before had prepared her father for so much worse a state of things that, by a re-action of his imagination, he described all as better than it really was.\n\n“But I will go again, and see the man himself,” said Mr. Hale. “I hardly know as yet how to compare one of these houses with our Helstone cottages. I see furniture here which our labourers would never have thought of buying, and food commonly used which they would consider luxuries; yet for these very families there seems no other resource now that their weekly wages are stopped, but the pawn-shop. One had need to learn a different standard, up here in Milton.”\n\nBessy, too, was rather better this day. Still she was so weak that she seemed to have entirely forgotten her wish to see Margaret dressed—if, indeed, that had not been the feverish desire of a half delirious state.\n\nMargaret could not help comparing this strange dressing of hers, to go where she did not care to be—her heart heavy with various anxieties—with the old, merry, girlish toilettes that she and Edith had performed scarcely more than a year ago. Her only pleasure now in decking herself out was in thinking that her mother would take delight in seeing her dressed. She blushed when Dixon, throwing the drawing-room door open, made an appeal for admiration.\n\n“Miss Hale looks well, ma’am,—doesn’t she? Mrs. Shaw’s coral couldn’t have come in better. It just gives the right touch of colour, ma’am. Otherwise, Miss Margaret, you would have been too pale.”\n\nMargaret’s black hair was too thick to be plaited; it needed rather to be twisted round and round, and have its fine silkiness compressed into massive coils, that encircled her head like a crown, and then were gathered into a large spiral knot behind. She kept its weight together by two large coral pins, like small arrows for length. Her white silk sleeves were looped up with strings of the same material, and on her neck, just below the base of her curved and milk-white throat, there lay heavy coral beads.\n\n“Oh, Margaret! how I should like to be going with you to one of the old Barrington assemblies,—taking you as Lady Beresford used to take me.”\n\nMargaret kissed her mother for this little burst of maternal vanity; but she could hardly smile at it, she felt so much out of spirits.\n\n“I would rather stay at home with you,—much rather, mamma.”\n\n“Nonsense, darling! Be sure you notice the dinner well. I shall like to hear how they manage these things in Milton. Particularly the second course, dear. Look what they have instead of game.”\n\nMrs. Hale would have been more than interested,—she would have been astonished, if she had seen the sumptuousness of the dinner-table and its appointments. Margaret, with her London cultivated taste, felt the number of delicacies to be oppressive; one half of the quantity would have been enough, and the effect tighter and more elegant. But it was one of Mrs. Thornton’s rigorous laws of hospitality, that of each separate dainty enough should be provided for all the guests to partake, if they felt inclined. Careless to abstemiousness in her daily habits, it was part of her pride to set a feast before such of her guests as cared for it. Her son shared this feeling. He had never known—though he might have imagined, and had the capability to relish—any kind of society but that which depended on an exchange of superb meals: and even now, though he was denying himself the personal expenditure of an unnecessary sixpence, and had more than once regretted that the invitations for this dinner had been sent out, still, as it was to be, he was glad to see the old magnificence of preparation.\n\nMargaret and her father were the first to arrive. Mr. Hale was anxiously punctual to the time specified. There was no one upstairs in the drawing-room but Mrs. Thornton and Fanny. Every cover was taken off, and the apartment blazed forth in yellow silk damask and a brilliantly flowered carpet. Every corner seemed filled up with ornament, until it became a weariness to the eye, and presented a strange contrast to the bald ugliness of the look-out into the great mill-yard, where wide folding gates were thrown open for the admission of carriages. The mill loomed high on the left-hand side of the windows, casting a shadow down from its many stories, which darkened the summer evening before its time.\n\n“My son was engaged up to the last moment on business. He will be here directly, Mr. Hale. May I beg you to take a seat?”\n\nMr. Hale was standing at one of the windows as Mrs. Thornton spoke. He turned away, saying,\n\n“Don’t you find such close neighbourhood to the mill rather unpleasant at times?”\n\nShe drew herself up:\n\n“Never. I am not become so fine as to desire to forget the source of my son’s wealth and power. Besides, there is not such another factory in Milton. One room alone is two hundred and twenty square yards.”\n\n“I meant that the smoke and the noise—the constant going out and coming in of the workpeople, might be annoying!”\n\n“I agree with you, Mr. Hale!” said Fanny. “There is a continual smell of steam and oily machinery—and the noise is perfectly deafening.”\n\n“I have heard noise that was called music far more deafening. The engine-room is at the street-end of the factory; we hardly hear it, except in summer weather, when all the windows are open; and as for the continual murmur of the workpeople, it disturbs me no more than the humming of a hive of bees. If I think of it at all, I connect it with my son, and feel how all belongs to him, and that his is the head that directs it. Just now, there are no sounds to come from the mill; the hands have been ungrateful enough to turn out, as perhaps you have heard. But the very business (of which I spoke when you entered), had reference to the steps he is going to take to make them learn their place.” The expression on her face, always stern, deepened into dark anger, as she said this. Nor did it clear away when Mr. Thornton entered the room; for she saw in an instant, the weight of care and anxiety which he could not shake off, although his guests received from him a greeting that appeared both cheerful and cordial. He shook hands with Margaret. He knew it was the first time their hands had met, though she was perfectly unconscious of the fact. He inquired after Mrs. Hale, and heard Mr. Hale’s sanguine, hopeful account; and, glancing to Margaret, to understand how far she agreed with her father, he saw that no dissenting shadow crossed her face. And as he looked with this intention, he was struck anew with her great beauty. He had never seen her in such dress before; and yet now it appeared that such elegance of attire was so befitting her noble figure and lofty serenity of countenance that she ought to go always thus apparelled. She was talking to Fanny; about what he could not hear; but he saw his sister’s restless way of continually arranging some part of her gown, her wandering eyes, now glancing here, now there, but without any purpose in her observation; and he contrasted them uneasily with the large soft eyes that looked forth steadily at one object, as if from out their light beamed some gentle influence of repose: the curving lines of the red lips, just parted in the interest of listening to what her companion said—the head a little bent forwards, so as to make a long sweeping line from the summit, where the light caught on the glossy raven hair, to the smooth ivory tip of the shoulder; the round white arms, and taper hands, laid lightly across each other, but perfectly motionless in their pretty attitude. Mr. Thornton sighed as he took in all this with one of his sudden comprehensive glances. And then he turned his back to the young ladies, and threw himself, with an effort, but with all his heart and soul, into a conversation with Mr. Hale.\n\nMore people came—more and more. Fanny left Margaret’s side and helped her mother to receive her guests. Mr. Thornton felt that in this influx no one was speaking to Margaret, and was restless under this apparent neglect. But he never went near her himself; he did not look at her. Only, he knew what she was doing—or not doing—better than he knew the movements of any one else in the room. Margaret was so unconscious of herself, and so much amused by watching other people, that she never thought whether she was left unnoticed or not. Somebody took her down to dinner; she did not catch the name; nor did he seem much inclined to talk to her. There was a very animated conversation going on among the gentlemen; the ladies, for the most part, were silent, employing themselves in taking notes of the dinner and criticising each other’s dresses. Margaret caught the clue to the general conversation, grew interested and listened attentively. Mr. Horsfall, the stranger, whose visit to the town was the original germ of the party, was asking questions relative to the trade and manufactures of the place; and the rest of the gentlemen—all Milton men—were giving him answers and explanations. Some dispute arose, which was warmly contested; it was referred to Mr. Thornton, who had hardly spoken before; but who now gave an opinion, the grounds of which were so clearly stated that even the opponents yielded. Margaret’s attention was thus called to her host; his whole manner, as master of the house, and entertainer of his friends, was so straightforward, yet simple and modest, as to be thoroughly dignified. Margaret thought she had never seen him to so much advantage. When he had come to their house, there had been always something, either of over-eagerness or of that kind of vexed annoyance which seemed ready to pre-suppose that he was unjustly judged, and yet felt too proud to try and make himself better understood. But now, among his fellows, there was no uncertainty as to his position. He was regarded by them as a man of great force of character; of power in many ways. There was no need to struggle for their respect. He had it, and he knew it; and the security of this gave a fine grand quietness to his voice and ways, which Margaret had missed before.\n\nHe was not in the habit of talking to ladies; and what he did say was a little formal. To Margaret herself he hardly spoke at all. She was surprised to think how much she enjoyed this dinner. She knew enough now to understand many local interests—nay, even some of the technical words employed by the eager millowners. She silently took a very decided part in the question they were discussing. At any rate, they talked in desperate earnest,—not in the used-up style that wearied her so in the old London parties. She wondered that, with all this dwelling on the manufacturers and trade of the place, no allusion was made to the strike then pending. She did not yet know how coolly such things were taken by the masters, as having only one possible end. To be sure, the men were cutting their own throats, as they had done many a time before; but if they would be fools, and put themselves into the hands of a rascally set of paid delegates, they must take the consequence. One or two thought Thornton looked out of spirits; and, of course, he must lose by this turn-out. But it was an accident that might happen to themselves any day; and Thornton was as good to manage a strike as any one; for he was as iron a chap as any in Milton. The hands had mistaken their man in trying that dodge on him. And they chuckled inwardly at the idea of the workmen’s discomfiture and defeat, in their attempt to alter one iota of what Thornton had decreed.\n\nIt was rather dull for Margaret after dinner. She was glad when the gentlemen came, not merely because she caught her father’s eye to brighten her sleepiness up; but because she could listen to something larger and grander than the petty interests which the ladies had been talking about. She liked the exultation in the sense of power which these Milton men had. It might be rather rampant in its display, and savour of boasting; but still they seemed to defy the old limits of possibility, in a kind of fine intoxication, caused by the recollection of what had been achieved, and what yet should be. If in her cooler moments she might not approve of the spirit in all things, still there was much to admire in their forgetfulness of themselves and the present, in their anticipated triumphs over all inanimate matter at some future time which none of them would live to see. She was rather startled when Mr. Thornton spoke to her close at her elbow:\n\n“I could see you were on our side in our discussion at dinner,—were you not, Miss Hale?”\n\n“Certainly. But then I know so little about it. I was surprised, however, to find from what Mr. Horsfall said, that there were others who thought in so diametrically opposite a manner, as the Mr. Morison he spoke about. He cannot be a gentleman—is he?”\n\n“I am not quite the person to decide on another’s gentlemanliness, Miss Hale. I mean, I don’t quite understand your application of the word. But I should say that this Morison is no true man. I don’t know who he is; I merely judge him from Mr. Horsfall’s account.”\n\n“I suspect my ‘gentleman’ includes your ‘true man.’”\n\n“And a great deal more, you would imply. I differ from you. A man is to me a higher and a completer being than a gentleman.”\n\n“What do you mean?” asked Margaret. “We must understand the words differently.”\n\n“I take it that ‘gentleman’ is a term that only describes a person in his relation to others; but when we speak of him as ‘a man,’ we consider him not merely with regard to his fellow-men, but in relation to himself,—to life—to time—to eternity. A cast-away lonely as Robinson Crusoe—a prisoner immured in a dungeon for life—nay, even a saint in Patmos, has his endurance, his strength, his faith, best described by being spoken of as ‘a man.’ I am rather weary of this word ‘gentlemanly,’ which seems to me to be often inappropriately used, and often, too, with such exaggerated distortion of meaning, while the full simplicity of the noun ‘man,’ and the adjective ‘manly’ are acknowledged—that I am induced to class it with the cant of the day.”\n\nMargaret thought a moment—but before she could speak her slow conviction, he was called away by some of the eager manufacturers, whose speeches she could not hear, though she could guess at their import by the short clear answers Mr. Thornton gave, which came steady and firm as the boom of a distant minute gun. They were evidently talking of the turn-out, and suggesting what course had best be pursued. She heard Mr. Thornton say:\n\n“That has been done.” Then came a hurried murmur, in which two or three joined.\n\n“All those arrangements have been made.”\n\nSome doubts were implied, some difficulties named by Mr. Slickson, who took hold of Mr. Thornton’s arm, the better to impress his words. Mr. Thornton moved slightly away, lifted his eyebrows a very little, and then replied:\n\n“I take the risk. You need not join in it unless you choose.” Still some more fears were urged.\n\n“I’m not afraid of anything so dastardly as incendiarism. We are open enemies; and I can protect myself from any violence that I apprehend. And I will assuredly protect all others who come to me for work. They know my determination by this time, as well and as fully as you do.”\n\nMr. Horsfall took him a little on one side, as Margaret conjectured, to ask him some other question about the strike; but, in truth, it was to inquire who she herself was—so quiet, so stately, and so beautiful.\n\n“A Milton lady?” asked he, as the name was given.\n\n“No! from the south of England—Hampshire, I believe,” was the cold, indifferent answer.\n\nMrs. Slickson was catechising Fanny on the same subject.\n\n“Who is that fine distinguished-looking girl? a sister of Mr. Horsfall’s?”\n\n“Oh dear, no! That is Mr. Hale, her father, talking now to Mr. Stephens. He gives lessons; that is to say, he reads with young men. My brother John goes to him twice a week, and so he begged mamma to ask them here, in hopes of getting him known. I believe we have some of their prospectuses, if you would like to have one.”\n\n“Mr. Thornton! Does he really find time to read with a tutor, in the midst of all his business,—and this abominable strike in hand as well?”\n\nFanny was not sure, from Mrs. Slickson’s manner, whether she ought to be proud or ashamed of her brother’s conduct; and, like all people who try and take other people’s “ought” for the rule of their feelings, she was inclined to blush for any singularity of action. Her shame was interrupted by the dispersion of the guests.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI\n\n“On earth is known to none The smile that is not sister to a tear.” ELLIOTT.\n\nMargaret and her father walked home. The night was fine, the streets clean, and with her pretty white silk, like Leezie Lindsay’s gown o’ green satin, in the ballad, “kilted up to her knee,” she was off with her father—ready to dance along with the excitement of the cool, fresh night air.\n\n“I rather think Thornton is not quite easy in his mind about this strike. He seemed very anxious to-night.”\n\n“I should wonder if he were not. But he spoke with his usual coolness to the others, when they suggested different things, just before we came away.”\n\n“So he did after dinner as well. It would take a good deal to stir him from his cool manner of speaking; but his face strikes me as anxious.”\n\n“I should be, if I were he. He must know of the growing anger and hardly smothered hatred of his workpeople, who all looked upon him as what the Bible calls a ‘hard man,’—not so much unjust as unfeeling; clear in judgment, standing upon his ‘rights’ as no human being ought to stand, considering what we and all our petty rights are in the sight of the Almighty. I am glad you think he looks anxious. When I remember Boucher’s half mad words and ways, I cannot bear to think how coolly Mr. Thornton spoke.”\n\n“In the first place, I am not so convinced as you are about that man Boucher’s utter distress; for the moment, he was badly off, I don’t doubt. But there is always a mysterious supply of money from these Unions; and, from what you said, it was evident the man was of a passionate, demonstrative nature, and gave strong expression to all he felt.”\n\n“Oh, papa!”\n\n“Well! I only want you to do justice to Mr. Thornton, who is, I suspect, of an exactly opposite nature—a man who is far too proud to show his feelings. Just the character I should have thought beforehand, you would have admired, Margaret.”\n\n“So I do—so I should; but I don’t feel quite so sure as you do of the existence of those feelings. He is a man of great strength of character—of unusual intellect, considering the few advantages he has had.”\n\n“Not so few. He has led a practical life from a very early age, has been called upon to exercise judgment and self-control. All that developes one part of the intellect. To be sure, he needs some of the knowledge of the past, which gives the truest basis for conjecture as to the future; but he knows this need—he perceives it, and that is something. You are quite prejudiced against Mr. Thornton, Margaret.”\n\n“He is the first specimen of a manufacturer—of a person engaged in trade—that I had ever the opportunity of studying, papa. He is my first olive: let me make a face while I swallow it. I know he is good of his kind, and by and by I shall like the kind. I rather think I am already beginning to do so. I was very much interested by what the gentlemen were talking about, although I did not understand half of it. I was quite sorry when Miss Thornton came to take me to the other end of the room, saying she was sure I should be uncomfortable at being the only lady among so many gentlemen. I had never thought about it, I was so busy listening; and the ladies were so dull, papa—oh, so dull! Yet I think it was clever too. It reminded me of our old game of having each so many nouns to introduce into a sentence.”\n\n“What do you mean, child?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\n“Why, they took nouns that were signs of things which gave evidence of wealth,—housekeepers, under-gardeners, extent of glass, valuable lace, diamonds, and all such things; and each one formed her speech so as to bring them all in, in the prettiest accidental manner possible.”\n\n“You will be as proud of your one servant when you get her, if all is true about her that Mrs. Thornton says.”\n\n“To be sure, I shall. I felt like a great hypocrite to-night, sitting there in my white silk gown, with my idle hands before me, when I remembered all the good, thorough, house-work they had done to-day. They took me for a fine lady, I’m sure.”\n\n“Even I was mistaken enough to think you looked like a lady, my dear,” said Mr. Hale, quietly smiling.\n\nBut smiles were changed to white and trembling looks when they saw Dixon’s face, as she opened the door.\n\n“Oh, master!—Oh, Miss Margaret! Thank God you are come! Dr. Donaldson is here. The servant next door went for him, for the charwoman is gone home. She’s better now; but, oh sir! I thought she’d have died an hour ago.”\n\nMr. Hale caught Margaret’s arm to steady himself from falling. He looked at her face, and saw an expression upon it of surprise and extremest sorrow, but not the agony of terror that contracted his own unprepared heart. She knew more than he did, and yet she listened with that hopeless expression of awed apprehension.\n\n“Oh! I should not have left her—wicked daughter that I am!” moaned forth Margaret, as she supported her trembling father’s hasty steps upstairs. Dr. Donaldson met them on the landing.\n\n“She is better now,” he whispered. “The opiate has taken effect. The spasms were very bad: no wonder they frightened your maid; but she’ll rally this time.”\n\n“This time! Let me go to her!” Half an hour ago Mr. Hale was a middle-aged man; now his sight was dim, his senses wavering, his walk tottering, as if he were seventy years of age.\n\nDr. Donaldson took his arm, and led him into the bedroom. Margaret followed close. There lay her mother, with an unmistakeable look on her face. She might be better now; she was sleeping, but Death had signed her for his own, and it was clear that ere long he would return to take possession. Mr. Hale looked at her for some time without a word. Then he began to shake all over, and, turning away from Dr. Donaldson’s anxious care, he groped to find the door; he could not see it, although several candles, brought in the sudden affright, were burning and flaring there. He staggered into the drawing-room, and felt about for a chair. Dr. Donaldson wheeled one to him, and placed him in it. He felt his pulse.\n\n“Speak to him, Miss Hale. We must rouse him.”\n\n“Papa!” said Margaret, with a crying voice that was wild with pain. “Papa! Speak to me!” The speculation came again into his eyes, and he made a great effort.\n\n“Margaret, did you know of this? Oh, it was cruel of you!”\n\n“No, sir, it was not cruel!” replied Dr. Donaldson, with quick decision. “Miss Hale acted under my directions. There may have been a mistake, but it was not cruel. Your wife will be a different creature to-morrow, I trust. She has had spasms, as I anticipated, though I did not tell Miss Hale of my apprehensions. She has taken the opiate I brought with me; she will have a good long sleep; and to-morrow that look which has alarmed you so much will have passed away.”\n\n“But not the disease?”\n\nDr. Donaldson glanced at Margaret. Her bent head, her face raised with no appeal for a temporary reprieve, showed that quick observer of human nature that she thought it better that the whole truth should be told.\n\n“Not the disease. We cannot touch the disease, with all our poor vaunted skill. We can only delay its progress—alleviate the pain it causes. Be a man, sir—a Christian. Have faith in the immortality of the soul, which no pain, no mortal disease, can assail or touch!”\n\nBut all the reply he got was in the choked words, “You have never been married, Dr. Donaldson; you don’t know what it is,” and in the deep, manly sobs, which went through the stillness of the night like heavy pulses of agony.\n\nMargaret knelt by him, caressing him with tearful caresses. No one, not even Dr. Donaldson, knew how the time went by. Mr. Hale was the first to dare to speak of the necessities of the present moment.\n\n“What must we do?” asked he. “Tell us both. Margaret is my staff—my right hand.”\n\nDr. Donaldson gave his clear, sensible directions. No fear for to-night—nay, even peace for to-morrow, and for many days yet. But no enduring hope of recovery. He advised Mr. Hale to go to bed, and leave only one to watch the slumber, which he hoped would be undisturbed. He promised to come again early in the morning. And, with a warm and kindly shake of the hand, he left them.\n\nThey spoke but few words; they were too much exhausted by their terror to do more than decide upon the immediate course of action. Mr. Hale was resolved to sit up through the night, and all that Margaret could do was to prevail upon him to rest on the drawing-room sofa. Dixon stoutly and bluntly refused to go to bed; and, as for Margaret, it was simply impossible that she should leave her mother, let all the doctors in the world speak of “husbanding resources,” and “one watcher only being required.” So, Dixon sat, and stared, and winked, and drooped, and picked herself up again with a jerk, and finally gave up the battle, and fairly snored. Margaret had taken off her gown and tossed it aside with a sort of impatient disgust, and put on her dressing-gown. She felt as if she never could sleep again; as if her whole senses were acutely vital, and all endued with double keenness, for the purpose of watching. Every sight and sound—nay, even every thought, touched some nerve to the very quick. For more than two hours, she heard her father’s restless movements in the next room. He came perpetually to the door of her mother’s chamber, pausing there to listen, till she, not hearing his close unseen presence, went and opened it to tell him how all went on, in reply to the questions his baked lips could hardly form. At last he, too, fell asleep, and all the house was still. Margaret sat behind the curtain thinking. Far away in time, far away in space, seemed all the interests of past days. Not more than thirty-six hours ago, she cared for Bessy Higgins and her father, and her heart was wrung for Boucher; now, that was all like a dreaming memory of some former life;—everything that had passed out of doors seemed dissevered from her mother, and therefore unreal. Even Harley Street appeared more distinct; there she remembered, as if it were yesterday, how she had pleased herself with tracing out her mother’s features in her Aunt Shaw’s face,—and how letters had come, making her dwell on the thoughts of home with all the longing of love. Helstone, itself, was in the dim past. The dull gray days of the preceding winter and spring, so uneventless and monotonous, seemed more associated with what she cared for now above all price. She would fain have caught at the skirts of that departing time, and prayed it to return, and give her back what she had too little valued while it was yet in her possession. What a vain show Life seemed! How unsubstantial, and flickering, and flitting! It was as if from some aërial belfry, high up above the stir and jar of the earth, there was a bell continually tolling, “All are shadows!—all are passing!—all is past!” And when the morning dawned, cool and gray, like many a happier morning before—when Margaret looked one by one at the sleepers, it seemed as if the terrible night were unreal as a dream; it, too, was a shadow. It, too, was past.\n\nMrs. Hale herself was not aware when she awoke, how ill she had been the night before. She was rather surprised at Dr. Donaldson’s early visit, and perplexed by the anxious faces of husband and child. She consented to remain in bed that day, saying she certainly was tired; but, the next, she insisted on getting up; and Dr. Donaldson gave his consent to her returning into the drawing-room. She was restless and uncomfortable in every position, and before night she became very feverish. Mr. Hale was utterly listless, and incapable of deciding on anything.\n\n“What can we do to spare mamma such another night?” asked Margaret on the third day.\n\n“It is to a certain degree, the reaction after the powerful opiates I have been obliged to use. It is more painful for you to see than for her to bear, I believe. But, I think, if we could get a water-bed it might be a good thing. Not but what she will be better to-morrow; pretty much like herself as she was before this attack. Still, I should like her to have a water-bed. Mrs. Thornton has one, I know. I’ll try and call there this afternoon. Stay,” said he, his eye catching on Margaret’s face, blanched with watching in a sick room, “I’m not sure whether I can go; I’ve a long round to take. It would do you no harm to have a brisk walk to Marlborough Street, and ask Mrs. Thornton if she can spare it.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Margaret. “I could go while mamma is asleep this afternoon. I’m sure Mrs. Thornton would lend it to us.”\n\nDr. Donaldson’s experience told him rightly. Mrs. Hale seemed to shake off the consequences of her attack, and looked brighter and better this afternoon than Margaret had ever hoped to see her again. Her daughter left her after dinner, sitting in her easy chair, with her hand lying in her husband’s, who looked more worn and suffering than she by far. Still, he could smile now—rather slowly, rather faintly, it is true; but a day or two before, Margaret never thought to see him smile again.\n\nIt was about two miles from their house in Crampton Crescent to Marlborough Street. It was too hot to walk very quickly. An August sun beat straight down into the street at three o’clock in the afternoon. Margaret went along, without noticing anything very different from usual in the first mile and a half of her journey; she was absorbed in her own thoughts, and had learnt by this time to thread her way through the irregular stream of human beings that flowed through Milton streets. But, by and by, she was struck with an unusual heaving among the mass of people in the crowded road on which she was entering. They did not appear to be moving on, so much as talking, and listening, and buzzing with excitement without much stirring from the spot where they might happen to be. Still, as they made way for her, and, wrapt up in the purpose of her errand, and the necessities that suggested it, she was less quick of observation than she might have been, if her mind had been at ease. She had got into Marlborough Street before the full conviction forced itself upon her, that there was a restless, oppressive sense of irritation abroad among the people; a thunderous atmosphere, morally as well as physically, around her. From every narrow lane opening out on Marlborough Street came up a low distant roar, as of myriads of fierce indignant voices. The inhabitants of each poor squalid dwelling were gathered round the doors and windows, if indeed they were not actually standing in the middle of the narrow ways—all with looks intent towards one point. Marlborough Street itself was the focus of all those human eyes, that betrayed intensest interest of various kinds; some fierce with anger, some lowering with relentless threats! some dilated with fear, or imploring entreaty; and as Margaret reached the small side-entrance by the folding doors, in the great dead wall of Marlborough mill-yard, and waited the porter’s answer to the bell, she looked round and heard the first long far-off roll of the tempest;—saw the first slow-surging wave of the dark crowd come, with its threatening crest, tumble over, and retreat, at the far end of the street, which a moment ago seemed so full of repressed noise, but which now was ominously still; all these circumstances forced themselves on Margaret’s notice, but did not sink down into her pre-occupied heart. She did not know what they meant—what was their deep significance; while she did know, did feel the keen sharp pressure of the knife that was soon to stab her through and through by leaving her motherless. She was trying to realise that, in order that, when it came, she might be ready to comfort her father.\n\nThe porter opened the door cautiously, not nearly wide enough to admit her.\n\n“It’s you, is it, ma’am?” said he, drawing a long breath, and widening the entrance, but still not opening it fully. Margaret went in. He hastily bolted it behind her.\n\n“Th’ folk are all coming up here, I reckon?” asked he.\n\n“I don’t know. Something unusual seemed going on; but this street is quite empty, I think.”\n\nShe went across the yard and put up the steps to the house door. There was no near sound,—no steam engine at work with beat and pant,—no click of machinery, no mingling and clashing of many sharp voices; but far away, the ominous gathering roar, deep-clamouring.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\n“But work grew scarce, while bread grew dear, And wages lessened, too; For Irish hordes were bidders here, Our half-paid work to do.” CORN LAW RHYMES.\n\nMargaret was shown into the drawing-room. It had returned into its normal state of bag and covering. The windows were half open because of the heat, and the Venetian blinds covered the glass,—so that a gray grim light, reflected from the pavement below, threw all the shadows wrong, and combined with the green-tinged upper light to make even Margaret’s own face, as she caught it in the mirrors, look ghastly and wan. She sat and waited; no one came. Every now and then, the wind seemed to bear the distant multitudinous sound nearer; and yet there was no wind! It died away into profound stillness between whiles.\n\nFanny came in at last.\n\n“Mamma will come directly, Miss Hale. She desired me to apologise to you as it is. Perhaps you know my brother has imported hands from Ireland, and it has irritated the Milton people excessively—as if he hadn’t a right to get labour where he could; and the stupid wretches here wouldn’t work for him; and now they’ve frightened these poor Irish starvelings so with their threats, that we daren’t let them out. You may see them huddled in that top room in the mill,—and they’re to sleep there, to keep them safe from those brutes, who will neither work or let them work. And mamma is seeing about their food, and John is speaking to them, for some of the women are crying to go back. Ah! here’s mamma!”\n\nMrs. Thornton came in with a look of black sternness on her face, which made Margaret feel she had arrived at a bad time to trouble her with her request. However, it was only in compliance with Mrs. Thornton’s expressed desire, that she would ask for whatever they might want in the progress of her mother’s illness. Mrs. Thornton’s brow contracted, and her mouth grew set, while Margaret spoke with gentle modesty of her mother’s restlessness, and Dr. Donaldson’s wish that she should have the relief of a water-bed. She ceased. Mrs. Thornton did not reply immediately. Then she started up and exclaimed—\n\n“They’re at the gates! Call John, Fanny,—call him in from the mill! They’re at the gates! They’ll batter them in! Call John, I say!”\n\nAnd simultaneously, the gathering tramp—to which she had been listening, instead of heeding Margaret’s words—was heard just right outside the wall, and an increasing din of angry voices raged behind the wooden barrier, which shook as if the unseen maddened crowd made battering rams of their bodies, and retreated a short space only to come with more united steady impetus against it, till their great beats made the strong gates quiver, like reeds before the wind.\n\nThe women gathered round the windows, fascinated to look on the scene which terrified them. Mrs. Thornton, the women-servants, Margaret,—all were there. Fanny had returned, screaming upstairs as if pursued at every step, and had thrown herself in hysterical sobbing on the sofa. Mrs. Thornton watched for her son, who was still in the mill. He came out, looked up at them—the pale cluster of faces—and smiled good courage to them, before he locked the factory door. Then he called to one of the women to come down and undo his own door, which Fanny had fastened behind her in her mad flight. Mrs. Thornton herself went. And the sound of his well-known and commanding voice, seemed to have been like the taste of blood to the infuriated multitude outside. Hitherto they had been voiceless, wordless, needing all their breath for their hard-laboured efforts to break down the gates. But now, hearing him speak inside, they set up such a fierce, unearthly groan, that even Mrs. Thornton was white with fear as she preceded him into the room. He came in a little flushed, but his eyes gleamed, as in answer to the trumpet-call of danger, and with a proud look of defiance on his face, that made him a noble, if not a handsome man. Margaret had always dreaded lest her courage should fail her in any emergency, and she should be proved to be, what she dreaded lest she was—a coward. But now, in this real great time of reasonable fear and nearness of terror, she forgot herself, and felt only an intense sympathy—intense to painfulness—in the interests of the moment.\n\nMr. Thornton came frankly forwards:\n\n“I’m sorry, Miss Hale, you have visited us at this unfortunate moment, when, I fear, you may be involved in whatever risk we have to bear. Mother! hadn’t you better go into the back rooms? I’m not sure whether they may not have made their way from Pinner’s Lane into the stable-yard; but if not, you will be safer there than here. Go, Jane!” continued he, addressing the upper-servant. And she went, followed by the others.\n\n“I stop here!” said his mother. “Where you are, there I stay.” And indeed, retreat into the back rooms was of no avail; the crowd had surrounded the outbuildings at the rear, and were sending forth their awful threatening roar behind. The servants retreated into the garrets, with many a cry and shriek. Mr. Thornton smiled scornfully as he heard them. He glanced at Margaret, standing all by herself at the window nearest the factory. Her eyes glittered, her colour was deepened on cheek and lip. As if she felt his look, she turned to him and asked a question that had been for some time in her mind:\n\n“Where are the poor imported workpeople? In the factory there?”\n\n“Yes! I left them cowed up in a small room, at the head of a back flight of stairs; bidding them run all risks, and escape down there, if they heard any attack made on the mill doors. But it is not them—it is me they want.”\n\n“When can the soldiers be here?” asked his mother, in a low but not unsteady voice.\n\nHe took out his watch with the same steady composure with which he did everything. He made some little calculation:—\n\n“Supposing Williams got straight off when I told him, and hadn’t to dodge about amongst them—it must be twenty minutes yet.”\n\n“Twenty minutes!” said his mother, for the first time showing her terror in the tones of her voice.\n\n“Shut down the windows instantly, mother,” exclaimed he: “the gates won’t bear such another shock. Shut down that window, Miss Hale.”\n\nMargaret shut down her window, and then went to assist Mrs. Thornton’s trembling fingers.\n\nFrom some cause or other, there was a pause of several minutes in the unseen street. Mrs. Thornton looked with wild anxiety at her son’s countenance, as if to gain the interpretation of the sudden stillness from him. His face was set into rigid lines of contemptuous defiance; neither hope nor fear could be read there.\n\nFanny raised herself up:\n\n“Are they gone?” asked she, in a whisper.\n\n“Gone!” replied he. “Listen!”\n\nShe did listen; they all could hear the one great straining breath; the creak of wood slowly yielding; the wrench of iron; the mighty fall of the ponderous gates. Fanny stood up tottering—made a step or two towards her mother, and fell forwards into her arms in a fainting fit. Mrs. Thornton lifted her up with a strength that was as much that of the will as of the body, and carried her away.\n\n“Thank God!” said Mr. Thornton, as he watched her out. “Had you not better go upstairs, Miss Hale?”\n\nMargaret’s lips formed a “No!”—but he could not hear her speak, for the tramp of innumerable steps right under the very wall of the house, and the fierce growl of low deep angry voices that had a ferocious murmur of satisfaction in them, more dreadful than their baffled cries not many minutes before.\n\n“Never mind!” said he, thinking to encourage her. “I am very sorry that you should have been entrapped into all this alarm; but it cannot last long now; a few minutes more, and the soldiers will be here.”\n\n“Oh, God!” cried Margaret, suddenly; “there is Boucher. I know his face, though he is livid with rage,—he is fighting to get to the front—look! look!”\n\n“Who is Boucher?” asked Mr. Thornton coolly, and coming close to the window to discover the man in whom Margaret took such an interest. As soon as they saw Mr. Thornton, they set up a yell, to call it not human is nothing,—it was as the demoniac desire of some terrible wild beast for the food that is withheld from his ravening. Even he drew back for a moment, dismayed at the intensity of hatred he had provoked.\n\n“Let them yell!” said he. “In five minutes more—. I only hope my poor Irishmen are not terrified out of their wits by such a fiendlike noise. Keep up your courage for five minutes, Miss Hale.”\n\n“Don’t be afraid for me,” she said hastily. “But what in five minutes? Can you do nothing to soothe these poor creatures? It is awful to see them.”\n\n“The soldiers will be here directly, and that will bring them to reason.”\n\n“To reason!” said Margaret, quickly. “What kind of reason?”\n\n“The only reason that does with men that make themselves into wild beasts. By heaven! they’ve turned to the mill-door!”\n\n“Mr. Thornton,” said Margaret, shaking all over with her passion, “go down this instant, if you are not a coward. Go down and face them like a man. Save these poor strangers, whom you have decoyed here. Speak to your workmen as if they were human beings. Speak to them kindly. Don’t let the soldiers come in and cut down poor creatures who are driven mad. I see one there who is. If you have any courage or noble quality in you, go out and speak to them, man to man!”\n\nHe turned and looked at her while she spoke. A dark cloud came over his face while he listened. He set his teeth as he heard her words.\n\n“I will go. Perhaps I may ask you to accompany me downstairs, and bar the door behind me; my mother and sister will need that protection.”\n\n“Oh! Mr. Thornton! I do not know—I may be wrong—only—”\n\nBut he was gone; he was downstairs in the hall; he had unbarred the front door; all she could do, was to follow him quickly, and fasten it behind him, and clamber up the stairs again with a sick heart and a dizzy head. Again she took her place by the farthest window. He was on the steps below; she saw that by the direction of a thousand angry eyes; but she could neither see nor hear anything save the savage satisfaction of the rolling angry murmur. See threw the window wide open. Many in the crowd were mere boys; cruel and thoughtless,—cruel because they were thoughtless; some were men, gaunt as wolves, and mad for prey. She knew how it was; they were like Boucher,—with starving children at home—relying on ultimate success in their efforts to get higher wages, and enraged beyond measure at discovering that Irishmen were to be brought in to rob their little ones of bread. Margaret knew it all; she read it in Boucher’s face, forlornly desperate and livid with rage. If Mr. Thornton would but say something to them—let them hear his voice only—it seemed as if it would be better than this wild beating and raging against the stony silence that vouchsafed them no word, even of anger or reproach. But perhaps he was speaking now; there was a momentary hush of their noise, inarticulate as that of a troop of animals. She tore her bonnet off, and bent forward to hear. She could only see; for if Mr. Thornton had indeed made the attempt to speak, the momentary instinct to listen to him was past and gone, and the people were raging worse than ever. He stood with his arms folded; still as a statue; his face pale with repressed excitement. They were trying to intimidate him—to make him flinch; each was urging the other on to some immediate act of personal violence. Margaret felt intuitively, that in an instant all would be uproar; the first touch would cause an explosion, in which, among such hundreds of infuriated men and reckless boys, even Mr. Thornton’s life would be unsafe,—that in another instant the stormy passions would have passed their bounds, and swept away all barriers of reason, or apprehension of consequence. Even while she looked, she saw lads in the background stooping to take off their heavy wooden clogs—the readiest missile they could find; she saw it was the spark to the gunpowder, and, with a cry, which no one heard, she rushed out of the room, down stairs,—she had lifted the great iron bar of the door with an imperious force—had thrown the door open wide—and was there, in face of that angry sea of men, her eyes smiting them with flaming arrows of reproach. The clogs were arrested in the hands that held them—the countenances, so fell not a moment before, now looked irresolute, and as if asking what this meant. For she stood between them and their enemy. She could not speak, but held out her arms towards them till she could recover breath.\n\n“Oh, do not use violence! He is one man, and you are many”; but her words died away, for there was no tone in her voice; it was but a hoarse whisper. Mr. Thornton stood a little on one side; he had moved away from behind her, as if jealous of anything that should come between him and danger.\n\n“Go!” said she, once more (and now her voice was like a cry). “The soldiers are sent for—are coming. Go peaceably. Go away. You shall have relief from your complaints, whatever they are.”\n\n“Shall them Irish blackguards be packed back again?” asked one from out the crowd, with fierce threatening in his voice.\n\n“Never, for your bidding!” exclaimed Mr. Thornton. And instantly the storm broke. The hootings rose and filled the air,—but Margaret did not hear them. Her eye was on the group of lads who had armed themselves with their clogs some time before. She saw their gesture—she knew its meaning—she read their aim. Another moment, and Mr. Thornton might be smitten down,—he whom she had urged and goaded to come to this perilous place. She only thought how she could save him. She threw her arms around him; she made her body into a shield from the fierce people beyond. Still, with his arms folded, he shook her off.\n\n“Go away,” said he, in his deep voice. “This is no place for you.”\n\n“It is,” said she. “You did not see what I saw.” If she thought her sex would be a protection,—if, with shrinking eyes she had turned away from the terrible anger of these men, in any hope that ere she looked again they would have paused and reflected, and slunk away, and vanished,—she was wrong. Their reckless passion had carried them too far to stop—at least had carried some of them too far; for it is always the savage lads, with their love of cruel excitement, who head the riot—reckless to what bloodshed it may lead. A clog whizzed through the air. Margaret’s fascinated eyes watched its progress; it missed its aim, and she turned sick with affright, but changed not her position, only hid her face on Mr. Thornton’s arm. Then she turned and spoke again:\n\n“For God’s sake! do not damage your cause by this violence. You do not know what you are doing.” She strove to make her words distinct.\n\nA sharp pebble flew by her, grazing forehead and cheek, and drawing a blinding sheet of light before her eyes. She lay like one dead on Mr. Thornton’s shoulder. Then he unfolded his arms, and held her encircled in one for an instant:\n\n“You do well!” said he. “You come to oust the innocent stranger. You fall—you hundreds—on one man; and when a woman comes before you, to ask you for your own sakes to be reasonable creatures, your cowardly wrath falls upon her! You do well!” They were silent while he spoke. They were watching, open-eyed and open-mouthed, the thread of dark-red blood which wakened them up from their trance of passion. Those nearest the gate stole out ashamed; there was a movement through all the crowd—a retreating movement. Only one voice called out:\n\n“Th’ stone was meant for thee; but thou wert sheltered behind a woman!”\n\nMr. Thornton quivered with rage. The blood-flowing had made Margaret conscious—dimly, vaguely conscious. He placed her gently on the door-step, her head leaning against the frame.\n\n“Can you rest there?” he asked. But without waiting for her answer, he went slowly down the steps right into the middle of the crowd. “Now kill me, if it is your brutal will. There is no woman to shield me here. You may beat me to death—you will never move me from what I have determined upon—not you!” He stood amongst them with his arms folded, in precisely the same attitude as he had been in on the steps.\n\nBut the retrograde movement towards the gate had begun—as unreasoningly, perhaps as blindly, as the simultaneous anger. Or, perhaps, the idea of the approach of the soldiers, and the sight of that pale, upturned face, with closed eyes, still and sad as marble, though the tears welled out of the long entanglement of eyelashes, and dropped down; and, heavier, slower plash than even tears, came the drip of blood from her wound. Even the most desperate—Boucher himself—drew back, faltered away, scowled, and finally went off, muttering curses on the master, who stood in his unchanging attitude, looking after their retreat with defiant eyes. The moment that retreat had changed into a flight (as it was sure from its very character to do), he darted up the steps to Margaret.\n\nShe tried to rise without his help.\n\n“It is nothing,” she said, with a sickly smile. “The skin is grazed, and I was stunned at the moment. Oh, I am so thankful they are gone!” And she cried without restraint.\n\nHe could not sympathise with her. His anger had not abated; it was rising the more as his sense of immediate danger was passing away. The distant clank of the soldiers was heard: just five minutes too late to make this vanished mob feel the power of authority and order. He hoped they would see the troops, and be quelled by the thought of their narrow escape. While these thoughts crossed his mind, Margaret clung to the doorpost to steady herself: but a film came over her eyes—he was only just in time to catch her. “Mother—mother!” cried he; “Come down—they are gone, and Miss Hale is hurt!” He bore her into the dining-room, and laid her on the sofa there; laid her down softly, and looking on her pure white face, the sense of what she was to him came upon him so keenly that he spoke it out in his pain:\n\n“Oh, my Margaret—my Margaret! no one can tell what you are to me! Dead—cold as you lie there, you are the only woman I ever loved! Oh, Margaret—Margaret!”\n\nInarticulately as he spoke, kneeling by her, and rather moaning than saying the words, he started up, ashamed of himself, as his mother came in. She saw nothing, but her son a little paler, a little sterner than usual.\n\n“Miss Hale is hurt, mother. A stone has grazed her temple. She has lost a good deal of blood, I’m afraid.”\n\n“She looks very seriously hurt,—I could almost fancy her dead,” said Mrs. Thornton, a good deal alarmed.\n\n“It is only a fainting-fit. She has spoken to me since.” But all the blood in his body seemed to rush inwards to his heart as he spoke, and he absolutely trembled.\n\n“Go and call Jane,—she can find me the things I want; and do you go to your Irish people, who are crying and shouting as if they were mad with fright.”\n\nHe went. He went away as if weights were tied to every limb that bore him from her. He called Jane; he called his sister. She should have all womanly care, all gentle tendance. But every pulse beat in him as he remembered how she had come down and placed herself in foremost danger—could it be to save him? At the time, he had pushed her aside, and spoken gruffly; he had seen nothing but the unnecessary danger she had placed herself in. He went to his Irish people, with every nerve in his body thrilling at the thought of her, and found it difficult to understand enough of what they were saying to soothe and comfort away their fears. There, they declared, they would not stop; they claimed to be sent back.\n\nAnd so he had to think, and talk, and reason.\n\nMrs. Thornton bathed Margaret’s temples with eau de Cologne. As the spirit touched the wound, which till then neither Mrs. Thornton nor Jane had perceived, Margaret opened her eyes; but it was evident she did not know where she was, nor who they were. The dark circles deepened, the lips quivered and contracted, and she became insensible once more.\n\n“She has had a terrible blow,” said Mrs. Thornton. “Is there any one who will go for a doctor?”\n\n“Not me, ma’am, if you please,” said Jane, shrinking back. “Them rabble may be all about; I don’t think the cut is so deep, ma’am, as it looks.”\n\n“I will not run the chance. She was hurt in our house. If you are a coward, Jane, I am not. I will go.”\n\n“Pray, ma’am, let me send one of the police. There’s ever so many come up, and soldiers too.”\n\n“And yet you’re afraid to go! I will not have their time taken up with our errands. They’ll have enough to do to catch some of the mob. You will not be afraid to stop in this house,” she asked contemptuously, “and go on bathing Miss Hale’s forehead, shall you? I shall not be ten minutes away.”\n\n“Couldn’t Hannah go, ma’am?”\n\n“Why Hannah? Why any but you? No, Jane, if you don’t go, I do.”\n\nMrs. Thornton went first to the room in which she had left Fanny stretched on the bed. She started up as her mother entered.\n\n“Oh, mamma, how you terrified me! I thought you were a man that had got into the house.”\n\n“Nonsense! The men are all gone away. There are soldiers all round the place, seeking for their work now it is too late. Miss Hale is lying on the dining-room sofa badly hurt. I am going for the doctor.”\n\n“Oh! don’t, mamma! they’ll murder you.” She clung to her mother’s gown. Mrs. Thornton wrenched it away with no gentle hand.\n\n“Find me some one else to go; but that girl must not bleed to death.”\n\n“Bleed! oh, how horrid! How has she got hurt?”\n\n“I don’t know,—I have no time to ask. Go down to her, Fanny, and do try to make yourself of use. Jane is with her; and I trust it looks worse than it is. Jane has refused to leave the house, cowardly woman! And I won’t put myself in the way of any more refusals from my servants, so I am going myself.”\n\n“Oh, dear, dear!” said Fanny, crying, and preparing to go down rather than be left alone, with the thought of wounds and bloodshed in the very house.\n\n“Oh, Jane!” said she, creeping into the dining-room, “What is the matter? How white she looks! How did she get hurt? Did they throw stones into the drawing-room?”\n\nMargaret did indeed look white and wan, although her senses were beginning to return to her. But the sickly daze of the swoon made her still miserably faint. She was conscious of movement around her, and of refreshment from the eau de Cologne, and a craving for the bathing to go on without intermission; but when they stopped to talk, she could no more have opened her eyes, or spoken to ask for more bathing, than the people who lie in death-like trance can move, or utter sound, to arrest the awful preparations for their burial, while they are yet fully aware, not merely of the actions of those around them, but of the idea that is the motive for such actions.\n\nJane paused in her bathing, to reply to Miss Thornton’s question.\n\n“She’d have been safe enough, miss, if she’d stayed in the drawing-room, or come up to us; we were in the front garret, and could see it all, out of harm’s way.”\n\n“Where was she then?” said Fanny, drawing nearer by slow degrees, as she became accustomed to the sight of Margaret’s pale face.\n\n“Just before the front door—with master!” said Jane, significantly.\n\n“With John! with my brother! How did she get there?”\n\n“Nay, miss, that’s not for me to say,” answered Jane, with a slight toss of her head. “Sarah did——”\n\n“Sarah what?” said Fanny, with impatient curiosity.\n\nJane resumed her bathing, as if what Sarah did or said was not exactly the thing she liked to repeat.\n\n“Sarah what?” asked Fanny, sharply. “Don’t speak in these half sentences, or I can’t understand you.”\n\n“Well, miss, since you will have it—Sarah, you see, was in the best place for seeing, being at the right-hand window; and she says, and said at the very time too, that she saw Miss Hale with her arms about master’s neck, hugging him before all the people.”\n\n“I don’t believe it,” said Fanny. “I know she cares for my brother; any one can see that; and I dare say, she’d give her eyes if he’d marry her,—which he never will, I can tell her. But I don’t believe she’d be so bold and forward as to put her arms round his neck.”\n\n“Poor young lady! she’s paid for it dearly if she did. It’s my belief, that the blow has given her such an ascendancy of blood to the head as she’ll never get the better from. She looks like a corpse now.”\n\n“Oh, I wish mamma would come!” said Fanny, wringing her hands. “I never was in the room with a dead person before.”\n\n“Stay, miss! She’s not dead: her eye-lids are quivering, and here’s wet tears a-coming down her cheeks. Speak to her, Miss Fanny!”\n\n“Are you better now?” asked Fanny, in a quavering voice.\n\nNo answer; no sign of recognition; but a faint pink colour returned to her lips, although the rest of her face was ashen pale.\n\nMrs. Thornton came hurriedly in, with the nearest surgeon she could find.\n\n“How is she? Are you better, my dear?” as Margaret opened her filmy eyes, and gazed dreamily at her. “Here is Mr. Lowe come to see you.”\n\nMrs. Thornton spoke loudly and distinctly, as to a deaf person. Margaret tried to rise, and drew her ruffled, luxuriant hair instinctively over the cut.\n\n“I am better now,” said she, in a very low, faint voice. “I was a little sick.”\n\nShe let him take her hand and feel her pulse. The bright colour came for a moment into her face, when he asked to examine the wound in her forehead; and she glanced up at Jane, as if shrinking from her inspection more than from the doctor’s.\n\n“It is not much, I think. I am better now. I must go home.”\n\n“Not until I have applied some strips of plaster, and you have rested a little.”\n\nShe sat down hastily, without another word, and allowed it to be bound up.\n\n“Now, if you please,” said she, “I must go. Mamma will not see it, I think. It is under the hair, is it not?”\n\n“Quite; no one could tell.”\n\n“But you must not go,” said Mrs. Thornton, impatiently. “You are not fit to go.”\n\n“I must,” said Margaret, decidedly. “Think of mamma. If they should hear—— Besides, I must go,” said she, vehemently. “I cannot stay here. May I ask for a cab?”\n\n“You are quite flushed and feverish,” observed Mr. Lowe.\n\n“It is only with being here, when I do so want to go. The air—getting away, would do me more good than anything,” pleaded she.\n\n“I really believe it is as she says,” Mr. Lowe replied. “If her mother is so ill as you told me on the way here, it may be very serious if she hears of this riot, and does not see her daughter back at the time she expects. The injury is not deep. I will fetch a cab, if your servants are still afraid to go out.”\n\n“Oh, thank you!” said Margaret. “It will do me more good than anything. It is the air of this room that makes me feel so miserable.”\n\nShe leant back on the sofa, and closed her eyes. Fanny beckoned her mother out of the room, and told her something that made her equally anxious with Margaret for the departure of the latter. Not that she fully believed Fanny’s statement; but she credited enough to make her manner to Margaret appear very much constrained, at wishing her good-bye.\n\nMr. Lowe returned in the cab.\n\n“If you will allow me, I will see you home, Miss Hale. The streets are not very quiet yet.”\n\nMargaret’s thoughts were quite alive enough to the present to make her desirous of getting rid of both Mr. Lowe and the cab before she reached Crampton Crescent, for fear of alarming her father and mother. Beyond that one aim she would not look. That ugly dream of insolent words spoken about herself, could never be forgotten—but could be put aside till she was stronger—for, oh! she was very weak; and her mind sought for some present fact to steady itself upon, and keep it from utterly losing consciousness in another hideous, sickly swoon.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\n“Which when his mother saw, she in her mind Was troubled sore, he wist well what to ween.” SPENSER.\n\nMargaret had not been gone five minutes when Mr. Thornton came in, his face all a-glow.\n\n“I could not come sooner: the superintendent would—— Where is she?” He looked round the dining-room, and then almost fiercely at his mother, who was quietly re-arranging the disturbed furniture, and did not instantly reply. “Where is Miss Hale?” asked he again.\n\n“Gone home,” said she, rather shortly.\n\n“Gone home!”\n\n“Yes. She was a great deal better. Indeed, I don’t believe it was so very much of a hurt; only some people faint at the least thing.”\n\n“I am sorry she has gone home,” said he, walking uneasily about. “She could not have been fit for it.”\n\n“She said she was; and Mr. Lowe said she was. I went for him myself.”\n\n“Thank you, mother.” He stopped, and partly held out his hand to give her a grateful shake. But she did not notice the movement.\n\n“What have you done with your Irish people?”\n\n“Sent to the Dragon, for a good meal for them, poor wretches. And then, luckily, I caught Father Grady, and I’ve asked him in to speak to them, and dissuade them from going off in a body. How did Miss Hale go home? I’m sure she could not walk.”\n\n“She had a cab. Everything was done properly, even to the paying. Let us talk of something else. She has caused disturbance enough.”\n\n“I don’t know where I should have been but for her.”\n\n“Are you become so helpless as to have to be defended by a girl?” asked Mrs. Thornton scornfully.\n\nHe reddened. “Not many girls would have taken the blows on herself which were meant for me;—meant with right down goodwill, too.”\n\n“A girl in love will do a good deal,” replied Mrs. Thornton, shortly.\n\n“Mother!” He made a step forwards; stood still; heaved with passion.\n\nShe was a little startled at the evident force he used to keep himself calm. She was not sure of the nature of the emotions she had provoked. It was only their violence that was clear. Was it anger? His eyes glowed, his figure was dilated, his breath came thick and fast. It was a mixture of joy, of anger, of pride, of glad surprise, of panting doubt; but she could not read it. Still it made her uneasy,—as the presence of all strong feeling, of which the cause is not fully understood or sympathised in, always has this effect. She went to the side-board, opened a drawer, and took out a duster, which she kept there for any occasional purpose. She had seen a drop of eau de Cologne on the polished arm of the sofa, and instinctively sought to wipe it off. But she kept her back turned to her son much longer than was necessary; and when she spoke, her voice seemed unusual and constrained.\n\n“You have taken some steps about the rioters, I suppose? You don’t apprehend anymore violence, do you? Where were the police? Never at hand when they’re wanted!”\n\n“On the contrary, I saw three or four of them, when the gates gave way, struggling and beating about in fine fashion; and more came running up just when the yard was clearing. I might have given some of the fellows in charge then, if I had had my wits about me. But there will be no difficulty, plenty of people can identify them.”\n\n“But won’t they come back to-night?”\n\n“I’m going to see about a sufficient guard for the premises. I have appointed to meet Captain Hanbury in half an hour at the station.”\n\n“You must have some tea first.”\n\n“Tea! Yes, I suppose I must. It’s half-past six, and I may be out for some time. Don’t sit up for me, mother.”\n\n“You expect me to go to bed before I have seen you safe, do you?”\n\n“Well, perhaps not.” He hesitated for a moment. “But if I’ve time, I shall go round by Crampton, after I’ve arranged with the police and seen Hamper and Clarkson.” Their eyes met; they looked at each other intently for a minute. Then she asked:\n\n“Why are you going round by Crampton?”\n\n“To ask after Miss Hale.”\n\n“I will send. Williams must take the water-bed she came to ask for. He shall inquire how she is.”\n\n“I must go myself.”\n\n“Not merely to ask how Miss Hale is?”\n\n“No, not merely for that. I want to thank her for the way in which she stood between me and the mob.”\n\n“What made you go down at all? It was putting your head into the lion’s mouth!”\n\nHe glanced sharply at her; saw that she did not know what had passed between him and Margaret in the drawing-room: and replied by another question:\n\n“Shall you be afraid to be left without me, until I can get some of the police; or had we better send Williams for them now, and they could be here by the time we have done tea? There’s no time to be lost. I must be off in a quarter of an hour.”\n\nMrs. Thornton left the room. Her servants wondered at her directions, usually so sharply-cut and decided, now confused and uncertain. Mr. Thornton remained in the dining-room, trying to think of the business he had to do at the police-office, and in reality thinking of Margaret. Everything seemed dim and vague beyond—behind—besides the touch of her arms round his neck—the soft clinging which made the dark colour come and go in his cheek as he thought of it.\n\nThe tea would have been very silent, but for Fanny’s perpetual description of her own feelings; how she had been alarmed—and then thought they were gone—and then felt sick and faint and trembling in every limb.\n\n“There, that’s enough,” said her brother, rising from the table. “The reality was enough for me.” He was going to leave the room, when his mother stopped him with her hand upon his arm.\n\n“You will come back here before you go to the Hales’,” said she, in a low, anxious voice.\n\n“I know what I know,” said Fanny to herself.\n\n“Why? Will it be too late to disturb them?”\n\n“John, come back to me for this one evening. It will be late for Mrs. Hale. But that is not it. To-morrow, you will—— Come back to-night, John!” She had seldom pleaded with her son at all—she was too proud for that; but she had never pleaded in vain.\n\n“I will return straight here after I have done my business. You will be sure to enquire after them?—after her?”\n\nMrs. Thornton was by no means a talkative companion to Fanny, nor yet a good listener while her son was absent. But on his return, her eyes and ears were keen to see and to listen to all the details which he could give, as to the steps he had taken to secure himself, and those whom he chose to employ, from any repetition of the day’s outrages. He clearly saw his object. Punishment and suffering were the natural consequences to those who had taken part in the riot. All that was necessary, in order that property should be protected, and that the will of the proprietor might cut to his end, clean and sharp as a sword.\n\n“Mother! You know what I have got to say to Miss Hale, to-morrow?”\n\nThe question came upon her suddenly, during a pause in which she, at least, had forgotten Margaret.\n\nShe looked up at him.\n\n“Yes! I do. You can hardly do otherwise.”\n\n“Do otherwise! I don’t understand you.”\n\n“I mean that, after allowing her feelings so to overcome her, I consider you bound in honour—”\n\n“Bound in honour,” said he scornfully. “I’m afraid honour has nothing to do with it. ‘Her feelings overcome her!’ What feelings do you mean?”\n\n“Nay, John, there is no need to be angry. Did she not rush down and cling to you to save you from danger?”\n\n“She did!” said he. “But, mother,” continued he, stopping short in his walk right in front of her. “I dare not hope. I never was faint-hearted before; but I cannot believe such a creature cares for me.”\n\n“Don’t be foolish, John. Such a creature! Why, she might be a duke’s daughter, to hear you speak. And what proof more would you have, I wonder, of her caring for you? I can believe she has had a struggle with her aristocratic way of viewing things; but I like her the better for seeing clearly at last. It is a good deal for me to say,” said Mrs. Thornton, smiling slowly, while the tears stood in her eyes; “for after to-night, I stand second. It was to have you to myself, all to myself, a few hours longer, that I begged you not to go till to-morrow.”\n\n“Dearest mother!” (Still love is selfish, and in an instant he reverted to his own hopes and fears in a way that drew the cold creeping shadow over Mrs. Thornton’s heart.) “But I know she does not care for me. I shall put myself at her feet—I must. If it were but one chance in a thousand—or a million—I should do it.”\n\n“Don’t fear!” said his mother, crushing down her own personal mortification at the little notice he had taken of the rare ebullition of her maternal feelings—of the pang of jealousy that betrayed the intensity of her disregarded love. “Don’t be afraid,” she said, coldly. “As far as love may go she may be worthy of you. It must have taken a good deal to overcome her pride. Don’t be afraid, John,” said she, kissing him, as she wished him good-night. And she went slowly and majestically out of the room. But when she got into her own, she locked the door, and sate down to cry unwonted tears.\n\nMargaret entered the room (where her father and mother still sat, holding low conversation together), looking very pale and white. She came close up to them before she could trust herself to speak.\n\n“Mrs. Thornton will send the water-bed, mamma.”\n\n“Dear, how tired you look! Is it very hot, Margaret?”\n\n“Very hot, and the streets are rather rough with the strike.”\n\nMargaret’s colour came back vivid and bright as ever; but it faded away instantly.\n\n“Here has been a message from Bessy Higgins, asking you to go to her,” said Mrs. Hale. “But I’m sure you look too tired.”\n\n“Yes!” said Margaret. “I am tired, I cannot go.”\n\nShe was very silent and trembling while she made tea. She was thankful to see her father so much occupied with her mother as not to notice her looks. Even after her mother went to bed, he was not content to be absent from her, but undertook to read her to sleep. Margaret was alone.\n\n“Now I will think of it—now I will remember it all. I could not before—I dared not.” She sat still in her chair, her hands clasped on her knees, her lips compressed, her eyes fixed as one who sees a vision. She drew a deep breath.\n\n“I, who hate scenes—I, who have despised people for showing emotion—who have thought them wanting in self-control—I went down and must needs throw myself into the melée, like a romantic fool! Did I do any good? They would have gone away without me, I dare say.” But this was overleaping the rational conclusion,—as in an instant her well-poised judgment felt. “No, perhaps they would not. I did some good. But what possessed me to defend that man as if he were a helpless child! Ah!” said she, clenching her hands together, “it is no wonder those people thought I was in love with him, after disgracing myself in that way. I in love—and with him too!” Her pale cheeks suddenly became one flame of fire; and she covered her face with her hands. When she took them away, her palms were wet with scalding tears.\n\n“Oh how low I am fallen that they should say that of me! I could not have been so brave for any one else, just because he was so utterly indifferent to me—if, indeed, I do not positively dislike him. It made me the more anxious that there should be fair play on each side; and I could see what fair play was. It was not fair,” said she vehemently, “that he should stand there—sheltered, awaiting the soldiers, who might catch those poor maddened creatures as in a trap—without an effort on his part, to bring them to reason. And it was worse than unfair for them to set on him as they threatened. I would do it again, let who will say what they like of me. If I saved one blow, one cruel, angry action that might otherwise have been committed, I did a woman’s work. Let them insult my maiden pride as they will—I walk pure before God!”\n\nShe looked up, and a noble peace seemed to descend and calm her face, till it was “stiller than chiselled marble.”\n\nDixon came in:\n\n“If you please, Miss Margaret, here’s the water-bed from Mrs. Thornton’s. It’s too late for to-night, I’m afraid, for missus is nearly asleep: but it will do nicely for to-morrow.”\n\n“Very,” said Margaret. “You must send our best thanks.”\n\nDixon left the room for a moment.\n\n“If you please, Miss Margaret, he says he’s to ask particular how you are. I think he must mean missus; but he says his last words were, to ask how Miss Hale was.”\n\n“Me!” said Margaret, drawing herself up. “I am quite well. Tell him I am perfectly well.” But her complexion was as deadly white as her handkerchief; and her head ached intensely.\n\nMr. Hale now came in. He had left his sleeping wife; and wanted, as Margaret saw, to be amused and interested by something that she was to tell him. With sweet patience did she bear her pain, without a word of complaint; and rummaged up numberless small subjects for conversation—all except the riot, and that she never named once. It turned her sick to think of it.\n\n“Good-night, Margaret. I have every chance of a good night myself, and you are looking very pale with your watching. I shall call Dixon if your mother needs anything. Do you go to bed and sleep like a top; for I’m sure you need it, poor child!”\n\n“Good-night, papa!”\n\nShe let her colour go—the false smile fade away—the eyes grow dull with heavy pain. She released her strong will from its laborious task. Till morning she might feel ill and weary.\n\nShe lay down and never stirred. To move hand or foot, or even so much as one finger, would have been beyond the powers of either volition or motion. She was so tired, so stunned, that she thought she never slept at all; her feverish thoughts passed and repassed the boundary between sleeping and waking, and kept their own miserable identity. She could not be alone, prostrate, powerless as she was,—a cloud of faces looked up at her, giving her no idea of fierce vivid anger, or of personal danger, but a deep sense of shame that she should thus be the object of universal regard—a sense of shame so acute that it seemed as if she would fain have burrowed into the earth to hide herself, and yet she could not escape out of that unwinking glare of many eyes.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV\n\n“Your beauty was the first that won the place And scal’d the walls of my undaunted heart, Which, captive now, pines in a caitive case, Unkindly met with rigour for desert:— Yet not the less your servant shall abide, In spite of rude repulse or silent pride.” WILLIAM FOWLER.\n\nThe next morning, Margaret dragged herself up, thankful that the night was over,—unrefreshed, yet rested. All had gone well through the house; her mother had only wakened once. A little breeze was stirring in the hot air, and though there were no trees to show the playful tossing movement caused by the wind among the leaves, Margaret knew how, somewhere or another, by wayside, in copses, or in thick green woods, there was a pleasant, murmuring dancing sound,—a rushing and falling noise, the very thought of which was an echo of distinct gladness in her heart.\n\nShe sat at her work in Mrs. Hale’s room. As soon as that forenoon slumber was over, she would help her mother to dress; after dinner, she would go and see Bessy Higgins. She would banish all recollection of the Thornton family—no need to think of them till they absolutely stood before her in flesh and blood. But, of course, the effort not to think of them brought them only the more strongly before her; and from time to time, the hot flush came over her pale face sweeping it into colour, as a sunbeam from between watery clouds comes swiftly moving over the sea.\n\nDixon opened the door very softly, and stole on tiptoe up to Margaret, sitting by the shaded window.\n\n“Mr. Thornton, Miss Margaret. He is in the drawing-room.”\n\nMargaret dropped her sewing.\n\n“Did he ask for me? Isn’t papa come in?”\n\n“He asked for you, miss; and master is out.”\n\n“Very well, I will come,” said Margaret, quietly. But she lingered strangely.\n\nMr. Thornton stood by one of the windows, with his back to the door apparently absorbed in watching something in the street. But, in truth, he was afraid of himself. His heart beat thick at the thought of her coming. He could not forget the touch of her arms around his neck, impatiently felt as it had been at the time; but now the recollection of her clinging defence of him, seemed to thrill him through and through,—to melt away every resolution, all power of self-control, as if it were wax before a fire. He dreaded lest he should go forwards to meet her, with his arms held out in mute entreaty that she would come and nestle there, as she had done, all unheeded, the day before, but never unheeded again. His heart throbbed loud and quick. Strong man as he was, he trembled at the anticipation of what he had to say, and how it might be received. She might droop, and flush, and flutter to his arms, as to her natural home and resting-place. One moment he glowed with impatience at the thought that she might do this,—the next he feared a passionate rejection, the idea of which withered up his future with so deadly a blight that he refused to think of it. He was startled by the sense of the presence of some one else in the room. He turned round. She had come in so gently, that he had never heard her; the street noises had been more distinct to his inattentive ear than her slow movements, in her soft muslin gown.\n\nShe stood by the table, not offering to sit down. Her eyelids were dropped half over her eyes; her teeth were shut, not compressed; her lips were just parted over them, allowing the white line to be seen between their curve. Her slow deep breathings dilated her chin and beautiful nostrils; it was the only motion visible on her countenance. The fine-grained skin, the oval cheek, the rich outline of her mouth, its corners deep set in dimples,—were all wan and pale to-day; the loss of their usual natural healthy colour being made more evident by the heavy shadow of the dark hair, brought down upon her temples, to hide all sign of the blow she had received. Her head, for all its drooping eyes, was thrown a little back, in the old proud attitude. Her long arms hung motionless by her sides. Altogether she looked like some prisoner, falsely accused of a crime that she loathed and despised, and from which she was too indignant to justify herself.\n\nMr. Thornton made a hasty step or two forwards; recovered himself, and went with quiet firmness to the door (which she had left open), and shut it. Then he came back, and stood opposite to her for a moment, receiving the general impression of her beautiful presence, before he dared to disturb it, perhaps to repel it, by what he had to say.\n\n“Miss Hale, I was very ungrateful yesterday—”\n\n“You had nothing to be grateful for,” said she raising her eyes, and looking full and straight at him. “You mean, I suppose, that you believe you ought to thank me for what I did.” In spite of herself—in defiance of her anger—the thick blushes came all over her face, and burnt into her very eyes; which fell not nevertheless from their grave and steady look. “It was only a natural instinct; any woman would have done just the same. We all feel the sanctity of our sex as a high privilege when we see danger. I ought rather,” said she, hastily, “to apologise to you, for having said thoughtless words which sent you down into the danger.”\n\n“It was not your words; it was the truth they conveyed, pungently as it was expressed. But you shall not drive me off upon that, and so escape the expression of my deep gratitude, my—” he was on the verge now; he would not speak in the haste of his hot passion; he would weigh each word. He would; and his will was triumphant. He stopped in mid career.\n\n“I do not try to escape from anything,” said she. “I simply say, that you owe me no gratitude; and I may add, that any expression of it will be painful to me, because I do not feel that I deserve it. Still, if it will relieve you from even a fancied obligation, speak on.”\n\n“I do not want to be relieved from any obligation,” said he, goaded by her calm manner. “Fancied, or not fancied—I question not myself to know which—I choose to believe that I owe my very life to you—ay—smile, and think it an exaggeration if you will. I believe it, because it adds a value to that life to think—oh, Miss Hale!” continued he, lowering his voice to such a tender intensity of passion that she shivered and trembled before him, “to think circumstances so wrought, that whenever I exult in existence henceforward, I may say to myself, ‘All this gladness in life, all honest pride in doing my work in the world, all this keen sense of being, I owe to her!’ And it doubles the gladness, it makes the pride glow, it sharpens the sense of existence till I hardly know if it is pain or pleasure to think that I owe it to one—nay, you must, you shall hear,”—said he, stepping forward with stern determination—“to one whom I love, as I do not believe man ever loved woman before.” He held her hand tight in his. He panted as he listened for what should come. He threw the hand away with indignation, as he heard her icy tone; for icy it was, though the words came faltering out, as if she knew not where to find them.\n\n“Your way of speaking shocks me. It is blasphemous. I cannot help it, if that is my first feeling. It might not be so, I dare say, if I understood the kind of feeling you describe. I do not want to vex you; and besides, we must speak gently, for mamma is asleep; but your whole manner offends me—”\n\n“How!” exclaimed he. “Offends you! I am indeed most unfortunate.”\n\n“Yes!” said she with recovered dignity. “I do feel offended; and, I think, justly. You seem to fancy that my conduct of yesterday”—again the deep carnation blush, but this time with eyes kindling with indignation rather than shame—“was a personal act between you and me; and that you may come and thank me for it, instead of perceiving, as a gentleman would—yes! a gentleman,” she repeated, in allusion to their former conversation about that word, “that any woman, worthy of the name of woman, would come forward to shield, with her reverenced helplessness, a man in danger from the violence of numbers.”\n\n“And the gentleman thus rescued is forbidden the relief of thanks!” he broke in contemptuously. “I am a man. I claim the right of expressing my feelings.”\n\n“And I yielded to the right; simply saying that you gave me pain by insisting upon it,” she replied, proudly. “But you seem to have imagined, that I was not merely guided by womanly instinct, but”—and here the passionate tears (kept down for long—struggled with vehemently) came up into her eyes and choked her voice—“but that I was prompted by some particular feeling for you—you! Why, there was not a man—not a poor desperate man in all that crowd—for whom I had not more sympathy—for whom I should not have done what little I could more heartily.”\n\n“You may speak on, Miss Hale. I am aware of these misplaced sympathies of yours. I now believe that it was only your innate sense of oppression—(yes; I, though a master, may be oppressed)—that made you act so nobly as you did. I know you despise me; allow me to say, it is because you do not understand me.”\n\n“I do not care to understand,” she replied, taking hold of the table to steady herself; for she thought him cruel—as, indeed, he was—and she was weak with her indignation.\n\n“No, I see you do not. You are unfair and unjust.”\n\nMargaret compressed her lips. She would not speak in answer to such accusations. But, for all that—for all his savage words, he could have thrown himself at her feet, and kissed the hem of her garment. She did not speak; she did not move. The tears of wounded pride fell hot and fast. He waited awhile, longing for her to say something, even a taunt, to which he might reply. But she was silent. He took up his hat.\n\n“One word more. You look as if you thought it tainted you to be loved by me. You cannot avoid it. Nay, I, if I would, cannot cleanse you from it. But I would not if I could. I have never loved any woman before: my life has been too busy, my thoughts too much absorbed with other things. Now I love, and will love. But do not be afraid of too much expression on my part.”\n\n“I am not afraid,” she replied, lifting herself straight up. “No one yet has ever dared to be impertinent to me, and no one ever shall. But, Mr. Thornton, you have been very kind to my father,” said she, changing her whole tone and bearing to a most womanly softness. “Don’t let us go on making each other angry. Pray don’t!” He took no notice of her words; he occupied himself in smoothing the nap of his hat with his coat-sleeve, for half a minute or so; and then, rejecting her offered hand, and making as if he did not see her grave look of regret, he turned abruptly away, and left the room. Margaret caught one glance at his face before he went.\n\nWhen he was gone she thought she had seen the gleam of washed tears in his eyes; and that turned her proud dislike into something different and kinder, if nearly as painful—self-reproach for having caused such mortification to any one.\n\n“But how could I help it?” asked she of herself. “I never liked him. I was civil; but I took no trouble to conceal my indifference. Indeed, I never thought about myself or him, so my manners must have shown the truth. All that yesterday, he might mistake. But that is his fault, not mine. I would do it again, if need were, though it does lead me into all this shame and trouble.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXV\n\n“Revenge may have her own; Roused discipline aloud proclaims their cause, And injured navies urge their broken laws.” BYRON.\n\nMargaret began to wonder whether all offers were as unexpected beforehand,—as distressing at the time of their occurrence, as the two she had had. An involuntary comparison between Mr. Lennox and Mr. Thornton arose in her mind. She had been sorry, that an expression of any other feeling than friendship had been lured out by circumstances from Henry Lennox. That regret was the predominant feeling, on the first occasion of her receiving a proposal. She had not felt so stunned—so impressed as she did now, when echoes of Mr. Thornton’s voice yet lingered about the room. In Lennox’s case, he seemed for a moment to have slid over the boundary between friendship and love; and the instant afterwards, to regret it nearly as much as she did, although for different reasons. In Mr. Thornton’s case, as far as Margaret knew, there was no intervening stage of friendship. Their intercourse had been one continued series of opposition. Their opinions clashed; and indeed, she had never perceived that he had cared for her opinions, as belonging to her, the individual. As far as they defied his rock-like power of character, his passion-strength, he seemed to throw them off from him with contempt, until she felt the weariness of the exertion of making useless protests; and now, he had come, in this strange wild passionate way, to make known his love! For, although at first it had struck her, that his offer was forced and goaded out of him by sharp compassion for the exposure she had made of herself,—which he, like others, might misunderstand—yet, even before he left the room,—and certainly not five minutes after, the clear conviction dawned upon her, shined bright upon her, that he did love her; that he had loved her; that he would love her. And she shrank and shuddered as under the fascination of some great power, repugnant to her whole previous life. She crept away, and hid from his idea. But it was of no use. To parody a line out of Fairfax’s Tasso—\n\n“His strong idea wandered through her thought.”\n\nShe disliked him the more for having mastered her inner will. How dared he say that he would love her still, even though she shook him off with contempt? She wished she had spoken more—stronger. Sharp, decisive speeches came thronging into her mind, now that it was too late to utter them. The deep impression made by the interview, was like that of a horror in a dream; that will not leave the room although we waken up, and rub our eyes, and force a stiff rigid smile upon our lips. It is there—there, cowering and gibbering, with fixed ghastly eyes, in some corner of the chamber, listening to hear whether we dare to breathe of its presence to any one. And we dare not; poor cowards that we are!\n\nAnd so she shuddered away from the threat of his enduring love. What did he mean? Had she not the power to daunt him? She would see. It was more daring than became a man to threaten her so. Did he ground it upon the miserable yesterday? If need were, she would do the same to-morrow,—by a crippled beggar, willingly and gladly,—but by him, she would do it, just as bravely, in spite of his deductions, and the cold slime of woman’s impertinence. She did it because it was right, and simple, and true to save where she could save; even to try to save. “Fais ce que dois, advienne que pourra.”\n\nHitherto she had not stirred from where he had left her; no outward circumstances had roused her out of the trance of thought in which she had been plunged by his last words, and by the look of his deep intent passionate eyes, as their flames had made her own fall before them. She went to the window, and threw it open, to dispel the oppression which hung around her. Then she went and opened the door, with a sort of impetuous wish to shake off the recollection of the past hour, in the company of others, or in active exertion. But all was profoundly hushed in the noonday stillness of a house, where an invalid catches the unrefreshing sleep that is denied to the night-hours. Margaret would not be alone. What should she do? “Go and see Bessy Higgins, of course,” thought she, as the recollection of the message sent the night before flashed into her mind. And away she went.\n\nWhen she got there, she found Bessy lying on the settle, moved close to the fire, though the day was sultry and oppressive. She was laid down quite flat, as if resting languidly after some paroxysm of pain. Margaret felt sure she ought to have the greater freedom of breathing which a more sitting posture would procure; and, without a word, she raised her up, and so arranged the pillows, that Bessy was more at ease, though very languid.\n\n“I thought I should na’ ha’ seen yo’ again,” said she, at last, looking wistfully in Margaret’s face.\n\n“I’m afraid you’re much worse. But I could not have come yesterday, my mother was so ill—for many reasons,” said Margaret, colouring.\n\n“Yo’d m’appen think I went beyond my place in sending Mary for yo’. But the wraglin’ and the loud voices had just torn me to pieces, and I thought when father left, oh! if I could just hear her voice, reading me some words o’ peace and promise, I could die away into the silence and rest o’ God, just as a baby is hushed up to sleep by its mother’s lullaby.”\n\n“Shall I read you a chapter, now?”\n\n“Ay, do! M’appen I shan’t listen to th’ sense, at first; it will seem far away—but when yo’ come to words I like—to th’ comforting texts—it’ll seem close in my ear, and going through me as it were.”\n\nMargaret began. Bessy tossed to and fro. If, by an effort, she attended for one moment, it seemed as though she were convulsed into double restlessness the next. At last, she burst out: “Don’t go on reading. It’s no use. I’m blaspheming all the time in my mind, wi’ thinking angrily on what canna be helped.—Yo’d hear of th’ riot, m’appen, yesterday at Marlborough Mills? Thornton’s factory, yo’ know.”\n\n“Your father was not there, was he?” said Margaret colouring deeply.\n\n“Not he. He’d ha’ given his right hand if it had never come to pass. It’s that that’s fretting me. He’s fairly knocked down in his mind by it. It’s no use telling him, fools will always break out o’ bounds. Yo’ never saw a man so downhearted as he is.”\n\n“But why?” asked Margaret. “I don’t understand.”\n\n“Why, yo’ see, he’s a committee-man on this special strike. Th’ Union appointed him because, though I say it as shouldn’t say it, he’s reckoned a deep chap, and true to th’ back-bone. And he and t’other committee-men laid their plans. They were to hou’d together through thick and thin; what the major part thought, t’others were to think, whether they would or no. And above all there was to be no going again the law of the land. Folk would go with them if they saw them striving and starving wi’ dumb patience; but if there was once any noise o’ fighting and struggling—even wi’ knobsticks—all was up, as they know by th’ experience of many, and many a time before. They would try and get speech o’ th’ knobsticks, and coax ’em, and reason wi’ ’em, and m’appen warn ’em off; but whatever came, the Committee charged all members o’ th’ Union to lie down and die, if need were, without striking a blow; and then they reckoned they were sure o’ carrying th’ public with them. And besides all that, Committee knew they were right in their demand, and they didn’t want to have right all mixed up wi’ wrong, till folk can’t separate it, no more nor I can the physic-powder from th’ jelly yo’ gave me to mix it in; jelly is much the biggest, but powder tastes it all through. Well, I’ve told yo’ at length about this’n, but I am tired out. Yo’ just think for yor’sel, what it mun be for father to have a’ his work undone, and by such a fool as Boucher, who must needs go right again the orders of Committee, and ruin th’ strike, just as bad as if he meant to be a Judas. Eh! but father giv’d it him last night! He went so far as to say, he’d go and tell police where they might find th’ ringleader o’ th’ riot; he’d give him up to th’ mill-owners to do what they would wi’ him. He’d show the world that th’ real leaders o’ the strike were not such as Boucher, but steady thoughtful men; good hands, and good citizens, who were friendly to law and judgment, and would uphold order; who only wanted their right wage, and wouldn’t work, even though they starved, till they got ’em; but who would ne’er injure property or life. For,” dropping her voice, “they do say, that Boucher threw a stone at Thornton’s sister, that welly killed her.”\n\n“That’s not true,” said Margaret. “It was not Boucher that threw the stone”—she went first red, then white.\n\n“Yo’d be there then, were yo’?” asked Bessie languidly: for indeed, she had spoken with many pauses, as if speech was unusually difficult to her.\n\n“Yes. Never mind. Go on. Only it was not Boucher that threw the stone. But what did he answer to your father?”\n\n“He did na’ speak words. He were all in such a tremble wi’ spent passion, I could na’ bear to look at him. I heard his breath coming quick, and at one time I thought he were sobbing. But when father said he’d give him up to police, he gave a great cry, and struck father on th’ face wi’ his closed fist, and he off like lightning. Father were stunned wi’ the blow at first, for all Boucher were weak wi’ passion and wi’ clemming. He sat down a bit, and put his hand afore his eyes; and then made for th’ door. I dunno’ where I got strength, but I threw mysel’ oh th’ settle and clung to him. ‘Father, father!’ said I. ‘Thou’ll never go peach on that poor clemmed man. I’ll never leave go on thee, till thou sayst thou wunnot.’ ‘Dunnot be a fool,’ says he, ‘words come readier than deeds to most men. I never thought o’ telling th’ police on him; though by G—, he deserves it, and I should na’ ha’ minded if some one else had done the dirty work, and got him clapped up. But now he has strucken me, I could do it less nor ever, for it would be getting other men to take up my quarrel. But if ever he gets well o’er this clemming, and is in good condition, he and I’ll have an up and down fight, purring an’ a’, and I’ll see what I can do for him.’ And so father shook me off,—for indeed I was low and faint enough, and his face was all clay white, where it weren’t bloody, and turned me sick to look at. And I know not if I slept or waked, or were in a dead swoon, till Mary come in; and I telled her to fetch yo’ to me. And now dunnot talk to me, but just read out th’ chapter. I’m easier in my mind for having spit it out; but I want some thoughts of the world that’s far away to take the weary taste of it out of my mouth. Read me not a sermon chapter, but a story chapter; they’ve pictures in them, which I see when my eyes are shut. Read about the New Heavens, and the New Earth; and m’appen I’ll forget this.”\n\nMargaret read in her soft low voice. Though Bessy’s eyes were shut, she was listening for some time, for the moisture of tears gathered heavy on her eyelashes. At last she slept; with many starts, and muttered pleadings. Margaret covered her up, and left her, for she had an uneasy consciousness that she might be wanted at home, and yet, until now, it seemed cruel to leave the dying girl.\n\nMrs. Hale was in the drawing-room on her daughter’s return. It was one of her better days, and she was full of praises of the water-bed. It had been more like the beds at Sir John Beresford’s than anything she had slept on since. She did not know how it was, but people seemed to have lost the art of making the same kind of beds as they used to do in her youth. One would think it was easy enough; there was the same kind of feathers to be had, and yet somehow, till this last night she did not know when she had had a good sound resting sleep.\n\nMr. Hale suggested, that something of the merits of the feather-beds of former days might be attributed to the activity of youth, which gave a relish to rest; but this idea was not kindly received by his wife.\n\n“No, indeed, Mr. Hale, it was those beds at Sir John’s. Now, Margaret, you’re young enough, and go about in the day; are the beds comfortable? I appeal to you. Do they give you a feeling of perfect repose when you lie down upon them; or rather, don’t you toss about, and try in vain to find an easy position, and wake in the morning as tired as when you went to bed?”\n\nMargaret laughed. “To tell the truth, mamma, I’ve never thought about my bed at all, what kind it is. I’m so sleepy at night, that if I only lie down anywhere, I nap off directly. So I don’t think I’m a competent witness. But then, you know, I never had the opportunity of trying Sir John Beresford’s beds. I never was at Oxenham.”\n\n“Were you not? Oh, no! to be sure. It was poor darling Fred I took with me, I remember. I only went to Oxenham once after I was married,—to your Aunt Shaw’s wedding; and poor little Fred was the baby then. And I know Dixon did not like changing from lady’s maid to nurse, and I was afraid that if I took her near her old home, and amongst her own people, she might want to leave me. But poor baby was taken ill at Oxenham, with his teething; and, what with my being a great deal with Anna just before her marriage, and not being very strong myself, Dixon had more of the charge of him than she ever had before; and it made her so fond of him, and she was so proud when he would turn away from every one and cling to her, that I don’t believe she ever thought of leaving me again; though it was very different from what she’d been accustomed to. Poor Fred! Everybody loved him. He was born with the gift of winning hearts. It makes me think very badly of Captain Reid when I know that he disliked my own dear boy. I think it a certain proof he had a bad heart. Ah! Your poor father, Margaret. He has left the room. He can’t bear to hear Fred spoken of.”\n\n“I love to hear about him, mamma. Tell me all you like; you never can tell me too much. Tell me what he was like as a baby.”\n\n“Why, Margaret, you must not be hurt, but he was much prettier than you were. I remember, when I first saw you in Dixon’s arms, I said, ‘Dear, what an ugly little thing!’ And she said, ‘It’s not every child that’s like Master Fred, bless him!’ Dear! how well I remember it. Then I could have had Fred in my arms every minute of the day, and his cot was close by my bed; and now, now—Margaret—I don’t know where my boy is, and sometimes I think I shall never see him again.”\n\nMargaret sat down by her mother’s sofa on a little stool, and softly took hold of her hand, caressing it and kissing it, as if to comfort. Mrs. Hale cried without restraint. At last, she sat straight, stiff up on the sofa, and turning round to her daughter, she said with tearful, almost solemn earnestness, “Margaret, if I can get better,—if God lets me have a chance of recovery, it must be through seeing my son Frederick once more. It will waken up all the poor springs of health left in me.”\n\nShe paused, and seemed to try and gather strength for something more yet to be said. Her voice was choked as she went on—was quavering as with the contemplation of some strange, yet closely-present idea.\n\n“And, Margaret, if I am to die—if I am one of those appointed to die before many weeks are over—I must see my child first. I cannot think how it must be managed; but I charge you, Margaret, as you yourself hope for comfort in your last illness, bring him to me that I may bless him. Only for five minutes, Margaret. There could be no danger in five minutes. Oh, Margaret, let me see him before I die!”\n\nMargaret did not think of anything that might be utterly unreasonable in this speech: we do not look for reason or logic in the passionate entreaties of those who are sick unto death; we are stung with the recollection of a thousand slighted opportunities of fulfilling the wishes of those who will soon pass away from among us: and do they ask us for the future happiness of our lives, we lay it at their feet, and will it away from us. But this wish of Mrs. Hale’s was so natural, so just, so right to both parties, that Margaret felt as if, on Frederick’s account as well as on her mother’s, she ought to overlook all intermediate chances of danger, and pledge herself to do everything in her power for its realization. The large, pleading, dilated eyes were fixed upon her wistfully, steady in their gaze, though the poor white lips quivered like those of a child. Margaret gently rose up and stood opposite to her frail mother; so that she might gather the secure fulfilment of her wish from the calm steadiness of her daughter’s face.\n\n“Mamma, I will write to-night, and tell Frederick what you say. I am as sure that he will come directly to us, as I am sure of my life. Be easy, mamma, you shall see him as far as anything earthly can be promised.”\n\n“You will write to-night? Oh, Margaret! the post goes out at five—you will write by it, won’t you? I have so few hours left—I feel dear, as if I should not recover, though sometimes your father over-persuades me into hoping; you will write directly, won’t you? Don’t lose a single post; for just by that very post I may miss him.”\n\n“But, mamma, papa is out.”\n\n“Papa is out! and what then? Do you mean that he would deny me this last wish, Margaret? Why, I should not be ill, be dying—if he had not taken me away from Helstone, to this unhealthy, smoky, sunless place.”\n\n“Oh, mamma!” said Margaret.\n\n“Yes; it is so, indeed. He knows it himself; he has said so many a time. He would do anything for me; you don’t mean he would refuse me this last wish—prayer, if you will. And, indeed, Margaret, the longing to see Frederick stands between me and God. I cannot pray till I have this one thing; indeed, I cannot. Don’t lose time, dear, dear Margaret. Write by this very next post. Then he may be here—here in twenty-two days! For he is sure to come. No cords or chains can keep him. In twenty-two days I shall see my boy.” She fell back, and for a short time she took no notice of the fact that Margaret sat motionless, her hand shading her eyes.\n\n“You are not writing!” said her mother at last. “Bring me some pens and paper; I will try and write myself.” She sat up, trembling all over with feverish eagerness. Margaret took her hand down, and looked at her mother sadly.\n\n“Only wait till papa comes in. Let us ask him how best to do it?”\n\n“You promised, Margaret, not a quarter of an hour ago,—you said he should come.”\n\n“And so he shall, mamma; don’t cry, my own dear mother. I’ll write here, now—you shall see me write—and it shall go by this very post; and if papa thinks fit he can write again when he comes in—it is only a day’s delay. Oh, mamma, don’t cry so pitifully, it cuts me to the heart.”\n\nMrs. Hale could not stop her tears; they came hysterically; and, in truth, she made no effort to control them, but rather called up all the pictures of the happy past, and the probable future—painting the scene when she should lie a corpse, with the son she had longed to see in life weeping over her, and she unconscious of his presence—till she was melted by self-pity into a state of sobbing and exhaustion that made Margaret’s heart ache. But at last she was calm, and greedily watched her daughter, as she began her letter; wrote it with swift urgent entreaty; sealed it up hurriedly, for fear her mother should ask to see it: and then to make security most sure, at Mrs. Hale’s own bidding, took it herself to the post-office. She was coming home when her father overtook her.\n\n“And where have you been, my pretty maid?” asked he.\n\n“To the post-office—with a letter; a letter to Frederick. Oh, papa, perhaps I have done wrong: but mamma was seized with such a passionate yearning to see him—she said it would make her well again—and then she said that she must see him before she died—I cannot tell you how urgent she was! Did I do wrong?”\n\nMr. Hale did not reply at first. Then he said:\n\n“You should have waited till I came in, Margaret.”\n\n“I tried to persuade her—” and then she was silent.\n\n“I don’t know,” said Mr. Hale, after a pause. “She ought to see him if she wishes it so much; for I believe it would do her much more good than all the doctor’s medicine—and, perhaps, set her up altogether; but the danger to him, I’m afraid, is very great.”\n\n“All these years since the mutiny, papa?”\n\n“Yes; it is necessary, of course, for government to take very stringent measures for the repression of offences against authority, more particularly in the navy, where a commanding officer needs to be surrounded in his men’s eyes with a vivid consciousness of all the power there is at home to back him, and take up his cause, and avenge any injuries offered to him, if need be. Ah! it’s no matter to them how far their authorities have tyrannised—galled hasty tempers to madness—or, if that can be any excuse afterwards, it is never allowed for in the first instance; they spare no expense, they send out ships—they scour the seas to lay hold of the offenders—the lapse of years does not wash out the memory of the offence—it is a fresh and vivid crime on the Admiralty books till it is blotted out by blood.”\n\n“Oh, papa, what have I done! And yet it seemed so right at the time. I’m sure Frederick himself, would run the risk.”\n\n“So he would; so he should! Nay, Margaret, I’m glad it is done, though I durst not have done it myself. I’m thankful it is as it is; I should have hesitated till, perhaps, it might have been too late to do any good. Dear Margaret, you have done what is right about it; and the end is beyond our control.”\n\nIt was all very well; but her father’s account of the relentless manner in which mutinies were punished made Margaret shiver and creep. If she had decoyed her brother home to blot out the memory of his error by his blood! She saw her father’s anxiety lap deeper than the source of his latter cheering words. She took his arm and walked home pensively and wearily by his side.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI\n\n“I have found that holy place of rest Still changeless.” MRS. HEMANS.\n\nWhen Mr. Thornton had left the house that morning he was almost blinded by his baffled passion. He was as dizzy as if Margaret, instead of looking, and speaking, and moving like a tender graceful woman, had been a sturdy fish-wife, and given him a sound blow with her fists. He had positive bodily pain—a violent headache, and a throbbing intermittent pulse. He could not bear the noise, the garish light, the continued rumble and movement of the street. He called himself a fool for suffering so; and yet he could not, at the moment, recollect the cause of his suffering, and whether it was adequate to the consequences it had produced. It would have been a relief to him, if he could have sat down and cried on a door-step by a little child, who was raging and storming, through his passionate tears, at some injury he had received. He said to himself, that he hated Margaret, but a wild, sharp sensation of love cleft his dull, thunderous feeling like lightning, even as he shaped the words expressive of hatred. His greatest comfort was in hugging his torment and in feeling, as he had indeed said to her, that though she might despise him, contemn him, treat him with her proud sovereign indifference, he did not change one whit. She could not make him change. He loved her, and would love her; and defy her, and this miserable bodily pain.\n\nHe stood still for a moment, to make this resolution firm and clear. There was an omnibus passing—going into the country; the conductor thought he was wishing for a place, and stopped near the pavement. It was too much trouble to apologise and explain; so he mounted upon it, and was borne away—past long rows of houses—then past detached villas with trim gardens, till they came to real country hedge-rows, and, by-and-by, to a small country town. Then everybody got down; and so did Mr. Thornton, and because they walked away he did so too. He went into the fields, walking briskly, because the sharp motion relieved his mind. He could remember all about it now; the pitiful figure he must have cut; the absurd way in which he had gone and done the very thing he had so often agreed with himself in thinking would be the most foolish thing in the world; and had met with exactly the consequences which, in these wise moods, he had always foretold were certain to follow, if he ever did make such a fool of himself. Was he bewitched by those beautiful eyes, that soft, half-open, sighing mouth which lay so close upon his shoulder only yesterday? He could not even shake off the recollection that she had been there; that her arms had been round him, once—if never again. He only caught glimpses of her; he did not understand her altogether. At one time she was so brave, and at another so timid; now so tender, and then so haughty and regal proud. And then he thought over every time he had ever seen her once again, by way of finally forgetting her. He saw her in every dress, in every mood, and did not know which became her best. Even this morning how magnificent she had looked—her eyes flashing out upon him at the idea that, because she had shared his danger yesterday, she had cared for him the least!\n\nIf Mr. Thornton was a fool in the morning, as he assured himself at least twenty times he was, he did not grow much wiser in the afternoon. All that he gained for his sixpenny omnibus ride, was a more vivid conviction that there never was, never could be, any one like Margaret; that she did not love him and never would; but that she—no! nor the whole world—should never hinder him from loving her. And so he returned to the little market-place, and remounted the omnibus to return to Milton.\n\nIt was late in the afternoon when he was set down, near his warehouse. The accustomed places brought back the accustomed habits and trains of thought. He knew how much he had to do—more than his usual work, owing to the commotion of the day before. He had to see his brother magistrates; he had to complete the arrangements, only half made in the morning, for the comfort and safety of his newly imported Irish hands; he had to secure them from all chance of communication with the discontented workpeople of Milton. Last of all, he had to go home and encounter his mother.\n\nMrs. Thornton had sat in the dining-room all day, every moment expecting the news of her son’s acceptance by Miss Hale. She had braced herself up many and many a time, at some sudden noise in the house; had caught up the half-dropped work, and begun to ply her needle diligently, though through dimmed spectacles, and with an unsteady hand! and many times had the door opened, and some indifferent person entered on some insignificant errand. Then her rigid face unstiffened from its gray frost-bound expression, and the features dropped into the relaxed look of despondency, so unusual to their sternness. She wrenched herself away from the contemplation of all the dreary changes that would be brought about to herself by her son’s marriage; she forced her thoughts into the accustomed household grooves. The newly-married couple-to-be would need fresh household stocks of linen: and Mrs. Thornton had clothes-basket upon clothes-basket, full of table-cloths and napkins, brought in, and began to reckon up the store. There was some confusion between what was hers, and consequently marked G. H. T. (for George and Hannah Thornton), and what was her son’s—bought with his money, marked with his initials. Some of those marked G. H. T. were Dutch damask of the old kind, exquisitely fine; none were like them now. Mrs. Thornton stood looking at them long,—they had been her pride when she was first married. Then she knit her brows, and pinched and compressed her lips tight, and carefully unpicked the G.H. She went so far as to search for the Turkey-red marking-thread to put in the new initials; but it was all used,—and she had no heart to send for any more just yet. So she looked fixedly at vacancy; a series of visions passing before her, in all of which her son was the principal, the sole object,—her son, her pride, her property. Still he did not come. Doubtless he was with Miss Hale. The new love was displacing her already from her place as first in his heart. A terrible pain—a pang of vain jealousy—shot through her: she hardly knew whether it was more physical or mental; but it forced her to sit down. In a moment, she was up again as straight as ever,—a grim smile upon her face for the first time that day, ready for the door opening, and the rejoicing triumphant one, who should never know the sore regret his mother felt at his marriage. In all this, there was little thought enough of the future daughter-in-law as an individual. She was to be John’s wife. To take Mrs. Thornton’s place as mistress of the house, was only one of the rich consequences which decked out the supreme glory: all household plenty and comfort, all purple and fine linen, honour, love, obedience, troops of friends, would all come as naturally as jewels on a king’s robe, and be as little thought of for their separate value. To be chosen by John, would separate a kitchen-wench from the rest of the world. And Miss Hale was not so bad. If she had been a Milton lass, Mrs. Thornton would have positively liked her. She was pungent, and had taste, and spirit, and flavour in her. True, she was sadly prejudiced, and very ignorant; but that was to be expected from her southern breeding. A strange sort of mortified comparison of Fanny with her, went on in Mrs. Thornton’s mind; and for once she spoke harshly to her daughter; abused her roundly; and then, by way of penance, she took up Henry’s Commentaries, and tried to fix her attention on it, instead of pursuing the employment she took pride in, and continuing her inspection of the table-linen.\n\nHis step at last! She heard him, even while she thought she was finishing a sentence; while her eye did pass over it, and her memory could mechanically have repeated it word for word, she heard him come in at the hall-door. Her quickened sense could interpret every sound of motion: now he was at the hat-stand—now at the very room-door. Why did he pause? Let her know the worst.\n\nYet her head was down over the book; she did not look up. He came close to the table, and stood still there, waiting till she should have finished the paragraph which apparently absorbed her. By an effort she looked up. “Well, John?”\n\nHe knew what that little speech meant. But he had steeled himself. He longed to reply with a jest; the bitterness of his heart could have uttered one, but his mother deserved better of him. He came round behind her, so that she could not see his looks, and, bending back her gray, stony face, he kissed it, murmuring:\n\n“No one loves me,—no one cares for me, but you, mother.”\n\nHe turned away and stood leaning his head against the mantelpiece, tears forcing themselves into his manly eyes. She stood up,—she tottered. For the first time in her life, the strong woman tottered. She put her hands on his shoulders; she was a tall woman. She looked into his face: she made him look at her.\n\n“Mother’s love is given by God, John. It holds fast for ever and ever. A girl’s love is like a puff of smoke,—it changes with every wind. And she would not have you, my own lad, would not she?” She set her teeth; she showed them like a dog for the whole length of her mouth. He shook his head.\n\n“I am not fit for her, mother; I knew I was not.”\n\nShe ground out words between her closed teeth. He could not hear what she said; but the look in her eyes interpreted it to be a curse,—if not as coarsely worded, as fell in intent as ever was uttered. And yet her heart leapt up light, to know he was her own again.\n\n“Mother!” said he, hurriedly, “I cannot hear a word against her. Spare me,—spare me! I am very weak in my sore heart;—I love her yet; I love her more than ever.”\n\n“And I hate her,” said Mrs. Thornton, in a low fierce voice. “I tried not to hate her, when she stood between you and me, because,—I said to myself,—she will make him happy; and I would give my heart’s blood to do that. But now, I hate her for your misery’s sake. Yes, John, it’s no use hiding up your aching heart from me. I am the mother that bore you, and your sorrow is my agony; and if you don’t hate her, I do.”\n\n“Then, mother, you make me love her more. She is unjustly treated by you, and I must make the balance even. But why do we talk of love or hatred? She does not care for me, and that is enough, too much. Let us never name the subject again. It is the only thing you can do for me in the matter. Let us never name her.”\n\n“With all my heart. I only wish that she, and all belonging to her, were swept back to the place they came from.”\n\nHe stood still, gazing into the fire for a minute or two longer. Her dry dim eyes filled with unwonted tears as she looked at him; but she seemed just as grim and quiet as usual when he next spoke.\n\n“Warrants are out against three men for conspiracy, mother. The riot yesterday helped to knock up the strike.”\n\nAnd Margaret’s name was no more mentioned between Mrs. Thornton and her son. They fell back into their usual mode of talk,—about facts, not opinions, far less feelings. Their voices and tones were calm and cold; a stranger might have gone away and thought that he had never seen such frigid indifference of demeanour between such near relations.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII\n\n“For never any thing can be amiss When simpleness and duty tender it.” MIDSUMMER NIGHT’S DREAM.\n\nMr. Thornton went straight and clear into all the interests of the following day. There was a slight demand for finished goods; and as it affected his branch of the trade, he took advantage of it, and drove hard bargains. He was sharp to the hour at the meeting of his brother magistrates,—giving them the best assistance of his strong sense, and his power of seeing consequences at a glance, and so coming to a rapid decision. Older men, men of long standing in the town, men of far greater wealth—realised and turned into land, while his was all floating capital, engaged in his trade—looked to him for prompt, ready wisdom. He was the one deputed to see and arrange with the police—to lead in all the requisite steps. And he cared for their unconscious deference no more than for the soft west wind, that scarcely made the smoke from the great tall chimneys swerve in its straight upward course. He was not aware of the silent respect paid to him. If it had been otherwise, he would have felt it as an obstacle in his progress to the object he had in view. As it was, he looked to the speedy accomplishment of that alone. It was his mother’s greedy ears that sucked in, from the womenkind of these magistrates and wealthy men, how highly Mr. This or Mr. That thought of Mr. Thornton: that if he had not been there, things would have gone on very differently,—very badly, indeed. He swept off his business right and left that day. It seemed as though his deep mortification of yesterday, and the stunned purposeless course of the hours afterwards, had cleared away all the mists from his intellect. He felt his power and revelled in it. He could almost defy his heart. If he had known it, he could have sang the song of the miller who lived by the river Dee:—\n\n“I care for nobody— Nobody cares for me.”\n\nThe evidence against Boucher, and other ring-leaders of the riot, was taken before him: that against the three others, for conspiracy, failed. But he sternly charged the police to be on the watch; for the swift right arm of the law should be in readiness to strike, as soon as they could prove a fault. And then he left the hot reeking room in the borough court, and went out into the fresher, but still sultry street. It seemed as though he gave way all at once; he was so languid that he could not control his thoughts; they would wander to her: they would bring back the scene,—not of his repulse and rejection the day before, but the looks, the actions of the day before that. He went along the crowded streets mechanically, winding in and out among the people, but never seeing them,—almost sick with longing for that one half-hour—that one brief space of time when she clung to him, and her heart beat against his—to come once again.\n\n“Why, Mr. Thornton! you’re cutting me very coolly, I must say. And how is Mrs. Thornton? Brave weather this! We doctors don’t like it, I can tell you!”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Dr. Donaldson. I really didn’t see you. My mother’s quite well, thank you. It is a fine day, and good for the harvest, I hope. If the wheat is well got in, we shall have a brisk trade next year, whatever you doctors have.”\n\n“Ay, ay. Each man for himself. Your bad weather, and your bad times, are my good ones. When trade is bad, there’s more undermining of health, and preparation for death, going on among you Milton men than you’re aware of.”\n\n“Not with me, Doctor. I’m made of iron. The news of the very worst bad debt I ever had, never made my pulse vary. This strike, which affects me more than any one else in Milton,—more than Hamper,—never comes near my appetite. You must go elsewhere for a patient, Doctor.”\n\n“By the way, you’ve recommended me a good patient, poor lady! Not to go on talking in this heartless way, I seriously believe that Mrs. Hale—that lady in Crampton, you know—hasn’t many weeks to live. I never had any hope of cure, as I think I told you; but I’ve been seeing her to-day, and I think very badly of her.”\n\nMr. Thornton was silent. The vaunted steadiness of pulse failed him for an instant.\n\n“Can I do anything, Doctor?” he asked, in an altered voice. “You know—you would see, that money is not very plentiful, are there any comforts or dainties she ought to have?”\n\n“No,” replied the Doctor, shaking his head. “She craves for fruit,—she has a constant fever on her; but jargonelle pears will do as well as anything, and there are quantities of them in the market.”\n\n“You will tell me if there is anything I can do, I’m sure,” replied Mr. Thornton. “I rely upon you.”\n\n“Oh! never fear! I’ll not spare your purse,—I know it’s deep enough. I wish you’d give me a carte-blanche for all my patients, and all their wants.”\n\nBut Mr. Thornton had no general benevolence,—no universal philanthropy; few even would have given him credit for strong affections. But he went straight to the first fruit-shop in Milton, and chose out the bunch of purple grapes with the most delicate bloom upon them,—the richest-coloured peaches,—the freshest vine-leaves. They were packed into a basket, and the shopman awaited the answer to his inquiry, “Where shall we send them to, sir?”\n\nThere was no reply. “To Marlborough Mills, I suppose, sir?”\n\n“No!” Mr. Thornton said. “Give the basket to me,—I’ll take it.”\n\nIt took up both his hands to carry it; and he had to pass through the busiest part of the town for feminine shopping. Many a young lady of his acquaintance turned to look after him, and thought it strange to see him occupied just like a porter or an errand-boy.\n\nHe was thinking, “I will not be daunted from doing as I choose by the thought of her. I like to take this fruit to the poor mother, and it is simply right that I should. She shall never scorn me out of doing what I please. A pretty joke, indeed, if, for fear of a haughty girl, I failed in doing a kindness to a man I liked! I do it for Mr. Hale; I do it in defiance of her.”\n\nHe went at an unusual pace, and was soon at Crampton. He went upstairs two steps at a time, and entered the drawing-room before Dixon could announce him,—his face flushed, his eyes shining with kindly earnestness. Mrs. Hale lay on the sofa, heated with fever. Mr. Hale was reading aloud. Margaret was working on a low stool by her mother’s side. Her heart fluttered, if his did not at this interview. But he took no notice of her,—hardly of Mr. Hale himself; he went up straight with his basket to Mrs. Hale, and said, in that subdued and gentle tone, which is so touching when used by a robust man in full health, speaking to a feeble invalid,—\n\n“I met Dr. Donaldson, ma’am, and as he said fruit would be good for you, I have taken the liberty—the great liberty—of bringing you some that seemed to me fine.” Mrs. Hale was excessively pleased; quite in a tremble of eagerness. Mr. Hale with fewer words expressed a deeper gratitude.\n\n“Fetch a plate, Margaret—a basket—anything.” Margaret stood up by the table, half afraid of moving or making any noise to arouse Mr. Thornton into a consciousness of her being in the room. She thought it would be awkward for both to be brought into conscious collision: and fancied that, from her being on a low seat at first, and now standing behind her father, he had overlooked her in his haste. As if he did not feel the consciousness of her presence all over, though his eyes had never rested on her!\n\n“I must go,” said he, “I cannot stay. If you will forgive this liberty,—my rough ways,—too abrupt, I fear—but I will be more gentle next time. You will allow me the pleasure of bringing you some fruit again, if I should see any that is tempting. Good afternoon, Mr. Hale. Good-bye, ma’am.”\n\nHe was gone. Not one word: not one look to Margaret. She believed that he had not seen her. She went for a plate in silence, and lifted the fruit out tenderly, with the points of her delicate taper fingers. It was good of him to bring it; and after yesterday too!\n\n“Oh! it is so delicious!” said Mrs. Hale, in a feeble voice. “How kind of him to think of me! Margaret love, only taste these grapes! Was it not good of him?”\n\n“Yes!” said Margaret, quietly.\n\n“Margaret!” said Mrs. Hale, rather querulously, “you won’t like anything Mr. Thornton does. I never saw anybody so prejudiced.”\n\nMr. Hale had been peeling a peach for his wife; and, cutting off a small piece for himself, he said:\n\n“If I had any prejudices, the gift of such delicious fruit as this would melt them away. I have not tasted such fruit—no! not even in Hampshire—since I was a boy; and, to boys, I fancy, all fruit is good. I remember eating sloes and crabs with a relish. Do you remember the matted-up currant bushes, Margaret, at the corner of the west-wall at the garden at home?”\n\nDid she not? Did she not remember every weather-stain on the old stone wall; the gray and yellow lichens that marked it like a map; the little crane’s-bill that grew in the crevices? She had been shaken by the events of the last two days; her whole life just now was a strain upon her fortitude; and, somehow, these careless words of her father’s, touching on the remembrance of the sunny times of old, made her start up, and, dropping her sewing on the ground, she went hastily out of the room into her own little chamber. She had hardly given way to the first choking sob, when she became aware of Dixon standing at her drawers, and evidently searching for something.\n\n“Bless me, miss! How you startled me! Missus is not worse, is she? Is anything the matter?”\n\n“No, nothing. Only I’m silly, Dixon, and want a glass of water. What are you looking for? I keep my muslins in that drawer.”\n\nDixon did not speak, but went on rummaging. The scent of lavender came out and perfumed the room.\n\nAt last Dixon found what she wanted; what it was Margaret could not see. Dixon faced round, and spoke to her:\n\n“Now I don’t like telling what I wanted, because you’ve fretting enough to go through, and I know you’ll fret about this. I meant to have kept it from you till night, may be, or such times as that.”\n\n“What is the matter? Pray, tell me, Dixon, at once.”\n\n“That young woman you go to see—Higgins I mean.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well! she died this morning, and her sister is here—come to beg a strange thing. It seems, the young woman who died had a fancy for being buried in something of yours, and so the sister’s come to ask for it,—and I was looking for a night-cap that wasn’t too good to give away.”\n\n“Oh! let me find one,” said Margaret, in the midst of her tears. “Poor Bessy! I never thought I should not see her again.”\n\n“Why, that’s another thing. This girl downstairs wanted me to ask you, if you would like to see her.”\n\n“But she’s dead!” said Margaret, turning a little pale. “I never saw a dead person. No! I would rather not.”\n\n“I should never have asked you, if you hadn’t come in. I told her you wouldn’t.”\n\n“I will go down and speak to her,” said Margaret, afraid lest Dixon’s harshness of manner might wound the poor girl. So, taking the cap in her hand, she went to the kitchen. Mary’s face was all swollen with crying, and she burst out afresh when she saw Margaret.\n\n“Oh, ma’am, she loved yo’, she loved yo’, she did indeed!” And for a long time, Margaret could not get her to say anything more than this. At last, her sympathy, and Dixon’s scolding, forced out a few facts. Nicholas Higgins had gone out in the morning, leaving Bessy as well as on the day before. But in an hour she was taken worse; some neighbour ran to the room where Mary was working; they did not know where to find her father; Mary had only come in a few minutes before she died.\n\n“It were a day or two ago she axed to be buried in somewhat o’ yourn. She were never tired o’ talking o’ yo’. She used to say yo’ were the prettiest thing she ever clapped eyes on. She loved yo’ dearly. Her last words were ‘Give her my affectionate respects; and keep father fro’ drink.’ Yo’ll come and see her ma’am. She would ha’ thought it a great compliment, I know.”\n\nMargaret shrank a little from answering.\n\n“Yes, perhaps I may. Yes, I will. I’ll come before tea. But where’s your father, Mary?”\n\nMary shook her head, and stood up to be going.\n\n“Miss Hale,” said Dixon, in a low voice, “where’s the use o’ your going to see the poor thing laid out? I’d never say a word against it, if it could do the girl any good; and I wouldn’t mind a bit going myself, if that would satisfy her. They’ve just a notion, these common folks, of its being a respect to the departed. Here,” said she, turning sharply round, “I’ll come and see your sister. Miss Hale is busy, and she can’t come, or else she would.”\n\nThe girl looked wistfully at Margaret. Dixon’s coming might be a compliment, but it was not the same thing to the poor sister, who had had her little pangs of jealousy, during Bessy’s lifetime, at the intimacy between her and the young lady.\n\n“No, Dixon!” said Margaret with decision. “I will go. Mary, you shall see me this afternoon.” And for fear of her own cowardice, she went away, in order to take from herself any chance of changing her determination.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII\n\n“Through cross to crown!—And though thy spirit’s life Trials untold assail with giant strength, Good cheer! good cheer! Soon ends the bitter strife, And thou shalt reign in peace with Christ at length.” KOSEGARTEN.\n\n“Ay sooth, we feel too strong in weal, to need Thee on that road; But woe being come, the soul is dumb, that crieth not on ‘God.’” MRS. BROWNING.\n\nThat afternoon she walked swiftly to the Higgins’s house. Mary was looking out for her, with a half-distrustful face. Margaret smiled into her eyes to re-assure her. They passed quickly through the house-place, upstairs, and into the quiet presence of the dead. Then Margaret was glad that she had come. The face, so often weary with pain, so restless with troublous thoughts, had now the faint soft smile of eternal rest upon it. The slow tears gathered into Margaret’s eyes, but a deep calm entered into her soul. And that was death! It looked more peaceful than life. All beautiful scriptures came into her mind. “They rest from their labours.” “The weary are at rest.” “He giveth his beloved sleep.”\n\nSlowly, slowly Margaret turned away from the bed. Mary was humbly sobbing in the back-ground. They went downstairs without a word.\n\nResting his hand upon the house-table, Nicholas Higgins stood in the midst of the floor; his great eyes startled open by the news he had heard, as he came along the court, from many busy tongues. His eyes were dry and fierce; studying the reality of her death; bringing himself to understand that her place should know her no more. For she had been sickly, dying so long, that he had persuaded himself she would not die; that she would “pull through.”\n\nMargaret felt as if she had no business to be there, familiarly acquainting herself with the surroundings of death, which he, the father, had only just learnt. There had been a pause of an instant on the steep crooked stair, when she first saw him; but now she tried to steal past his abstracted gaze, and to leave him in the solemn circle of his household misery.\n\nMary sat down on the first chair she came to, and throwing her apron over her head, began to cry.\n\nThe noise appeared to rouse him. He took sudden hold of Margaret’s arm, and held her till he could gather words to speak. His throat seemed dry; they came up thick, and choked, and hoarse:\n\n“Were yo’ with her? Did yo’ see her die?”\n\n“No!” replied Margaret, standing still with the utmost patience, now she found herself perceived. It was some time before he spoke again, but he kept his hold on her arm.\n\n“All men must die,” said he at last, with a strange sort of gravity, which first suggested to Margaret the idea that he had been drinking—not enough to intoxicate himself, but enough to make his thoughts bewildered. “But she were younger than me.” Still he pondered over the event, not looking at Margaret, though he grasped her tight. Suddenly, he looked up at her with a wild searching inquiry in his glance. “Yo’re sure and certain she’s dead—not in a dwam, a faint? she’s been so before, often.”\n\n“She is dead,” replied Margaret. She felt no fear in speaking to him, though he hurt her arm with his gripe, and wild gleams came across the stupidity of his eyes.\n\n“She is dead!” she said.\n\nHe looked at her still with that searching look, which seemed to fade out of his eyes as he gazed. Then he suddenly let go his hold of Margaret, and, throwing his body half across the table, he shook it and every piece of furniture in the room, with his violent sobs. Mary came trembling towards him.\n\n“Get thee gone!—get thee gone!” he cried, striking wildly and blindly at her. “What do I care for thee?” Margaret took her hand and held it softly in hers. He tore his hair, he beat his head against the hard wood, then he lay exhausted and stupid. Still his daughter and Margaret did not move. Mary trembled from head to foot.\n\nAt last—it might have been a quarter of an hour, it might have been an hour—he lifted himself up. His eyes were swollen and bloodshot, and he seemed to have forgotten that any one was by; he scowled at the watchers when he saw them. He shook himself heavily, gave them one more sullen look, spoke never a word, but made for the door.\n\n“Oh, father, father!” said Mary, throwing herself upon his arm,—“not to-night! Any night but to-night. Oh, help me! he’s going out to drink again! Father, I’ll not leave yo’. Yo’ may strike, but I’ll not leave yo’. She told me last of all to keep yo’ fro’ drink!”\n\nBut Margaret stood in the doorway, silent yet commanding. He looked up at her defyingly.\n\n“It’s my own house. Stand out o’ the way, wench, or I’ll make yo’!” He had shaken off Mary with violence; he looked ready to strike Margaret. But she never moved a feature—never took her deep, serious eyes off him. He stared back on her with gloomy fierceness. If she had stirred hand or foot, he would have thrust her aside with even more violence than he had used to his own daughter, whose face was bleeding from her fall against a chair.\n\n“What are yo’ looking at me in that way for?” asked he at last, daunted and awed by her severe calm. “If yo’ think for to keep me from going what gait I choose, because she loved yo’—and in my own house, too, where I never asked yo’ to come, yo’re mista’en. It’s very hard upon a man that he can’t go to the only comfort left.”\n\nMargaret felt that he acknowledged her power. What could she do next? He had seated himself on a chair, close to the door; half-conquered half-resenting; intending to go out as soon as she left her position, but unwilling to use the violence he had threatened not five minutes before. Margaret laid her hand on his arm.\n\n“Come with me,” she said. “Come and see her!”\n\nThe voice in which she spoke was very low and solemn; but there was no fear or doubt expressed in it, either of him or of his compliance. He sullenly rose up. He stood uncertain, with dogged irresolution upon his face. She waited him there; quietly and patiently waited for his time to move. He had a strange pleasure in making her wait; but at last he moved towards the stairs.\n\nShe and he stood by the corpse.\n\n“Her last words to Mary were, ‘Keep my father fro’ drink.’”\n\n“It canna hurt her now,” muttered he. “Nought can hurt her now.” Then, raising his voice to a wailing cry, he went on: “We may quarrel and fall out—we may make peace and be friends—we may clem to skin and bone—and nought o’ all our griefs will ever touch her more. Hoo’s had her portion on ’em. What wi’ hard work first, and sickness at last, hoo’ led the life of a dog. And to die without knowing one good piece o’ rejoicing in all her days! Nay, wench, whatever hoo said, hoo can know nought about it now, and I mun ha’ a sup o’ drink just to steady me again sorrow.”\n\n“No,” said Margaret, softening with his softened manner. “You shall not. If her life has been what you say, at any rate she did not fear death as some do. Oh, you should have heard her speak of the life to come—the life hidden with God, that she is now gone to.”\n\nHe shook his head, glancing sideways up at Margaret as he did so. His pale haggard face struck her painfully.\n\n“You are sorely tried. Where have you been all day—not at work?”\n\n“Not at work, sure enough,” said he, with a short, grim laugh. “Not at what you call work. I were at the Committee, till I were sickened out wi’ trying to make fools hear reason. I were fetched to Boucher’s wife afore seven this morning. She’s bed-fast, but she were raving and raging to know where her dunner-headed brute of a chap was, as if I’d to keep him—as if he were fit to be ruled by me. The d—d fool, who has put his foot in all our plans! And I’ve walked my feet sore wi’ going about for to see men who wouldn’t be seen, now the law is raised against us. And I were sore-hearted, too, which is worse than sore-footed; and if I did see a friend who ossed to treat me, I never knew hoo lay a-dying here. Bess, lass, thou’d believe me, thou wouldst—wouldstn’t thou?” turning to the poor dumb form with wild appeal.\n\n“I am sure,” said Margaret, “I am sure you did not know; it was quite sudden. But, now, you see, it would be different; you do know; you do see her lying there; you hear what she said with her last breath. You will not go?”\n\nNo answer. In fact, where was he to look for comfort?\n\n“Come home with me,” said she at last, with a bold venture, half trembling at her own proposal as she made it. “At least you shall have some comfortable food, which I’m sure you need.”\n\n“Yo’r father’s a parson?” asked he, with a sudden turn in his ideas.\n\n“He was,” said Margaret, shortly.\n\n“I’ll go and take a dish o’ tea with him, since yo’ve asked me. I’ve many a thing I often wished to say to a parson, and I’m not particular as to whether he’s preaching now, or not.”\n\nMargaret was perplexed; his drinking tea with her father, who would be totally unprepared for his visitor—her mother so ill—seemed utterly out of the question; and yet, if she drew back now, it would be worse than ever—sure to drive him to the gin-shop. She thought that if she could only get him to their own house, it was so great a step gained that she would trust to the chapter of accidents for the next.\n\n“Good-bye, ou’d wench! We’ve parted company at last, we have! But thou’st been a blessin’ to thy father ever sin’ thou wert born. Bless thy white lips, lass,—they’ve a smile on ’em now! and I’m glad to see it once again, though I’m lone and forlorn for evermore.”\n\nHe stooped down and fondly kissed his daughter; covered up her face, and turned to follow Margaret. She had hastily gone down stairs to tell Mary of the arrangement; to say it was the only place she could think of to keep him from the gin-palace; to urge Mary to come too, for her heart smote her at the idea of leaving the poor affectionate girl alone. But Mary had friends amongst the neighbours, she said, who would come in and sit a bit with her; it was all right; but father—\n\nHe was there by them or she would have spoken more. He had shaken off his emotion, as if he was ashamed of having ever given way to it; and had even o’erleaped himself so much that he assumed a sort of bitter mirth, like the crackling of thorns under a pot.\n\n“I’m going to take my tea wi’ her father, I am.”\n\nBut he slouched his cap low down over his brow as he went out into the street, and looked neither to the right nor to the left, while he tramped along by Margaret’s side; he feared being upset by the words, still more the looks, of sympathising neighbours. So he and Margaret walked in silence.\n\nAs he got near the street in which he knew she lived, he looked down at his clothes, his hands, his shoes.\n\n“I should m’appen ha’ cleaned mysel’, first.”\n\nIt certainly would have been desirable, but Margaret assured him he should be allowed to go into the yard, and have soap and towel provided; she would not let him slip out of her hands just then.\n\nWhile he followed the house-servant along the passage, and through the kitchen, stepping cautiously on every dark mark in the pattern of the oil-cloth, in order to conceal his dirty foot-prints, Margaret ran upstairs. She met Dixon on the landing.\n\n“How is mamma?—where is papa?”\n\nMissus was tired, and gone into her own room. She had wanted to go to bed, but Dixon had persuaded her to lie down on the sofa, and have her tea brought to her there; it would be better than getting restless by being too long in bed.\n\nSo far, so good. But where was Mr. Hale? In the drawing-room. Margaret went in half breathless with the hurried story she had to tell. Of course, she told it incompletely; and her father was rather “taken aback” by the idea of the drunken weaver awaiting him in his quiet study, with whom he was expected to drink tea, and on whose behalf Margaret was anxiously pleading. The meek, kind-hearted Mr. Hale would have readily tried to console him in his grief, but, unluckily, the point Margaret dwelt upon most forcibly was the fact of his having been drinking, and her having brought him home with her as a last expedient to keep him from the gin-shop. One little event had come out of another so naturally that Margaret was hardly conscious of what she had done, till she saw the slight look of repugnance on her father’s face.\n\n“Oh, papa! he really is a man you will not dislike—if you won’t be shocked to begin with.”\n\n“But, Margaret, to bring a drunken man home—and your mother so ill!”\n\nMargaret’s countenance fell. “I am sorry, papa. He is very quiet—he is not tipsy at all. He was only rather strange at first, but that might be the shock of poor Bessy’s death.” Margaret’s eyes filled with tears. Mr. Hale took hold of her sweet pleading face in both his hands, and kissed her forehead.\n\n“It is all right, dear. I’ll go and make him as comfortable as I can, and do you attend to your mother. Only, if you can come in and make a third in the study, I shall be glad.”\n\n“Oh, yes—thank you.” But as Mr. Hale was leaving the room, she ran after him.\n\n“Papa—you must not wonder at what he says: he’s an——, I mean he does not believe much in what we do.”\n\n“Oh dear! a drunken infidel weaver!” said Mr. Hale to himself, in dismay. But to Margaret he only said, “If your mother goes to sleep, be sure you come directly.”\n\nMargaret went into her mother’s room. Mrs. Hale lifted herself up from a doze.\n\n“When did you write to Frederick, Margaret? Yesterday, or the day before?”\n\n“Yesterday, mamma.”\n\n“Yesterday, and the letter went?”\n\n“Yes. I took it myself.”\n\n“Oh, Margaret, I’m so afraid of his coming! If he should be recognized! If he should be taken! If he should be executed, after all these years that he has kept away and lived in safety! I keep falling asleep and dreaming that he is caught and being tried.”\n\n“Oh, mamma, don’t be afraid. There will be some risk, no doubt; but we will lessen it as much as ever we can. And it is so little! Now, if we were at Helstone, there would be twenty—a hundred times as much. There, everybody would remember him; and if there was a stranger known to be in the house, they would be sure to guess it was Frederick; while here, nobody knows or cares for us enough to notice what we do. Dixon will keep the door like a dragon—won’t you, Dixon—while he is here?”\n\n“They’ll be clever if they come in past me!” said Dixon, showing her teeth at the bare idea.\n\n“And he need not go out, except in the dusk, poor fellow!”\n\n“Poor fellow!” echoed Mrs. Hale. “But I almost wish you had not written. Would it be too late to stop him if you wrote again, Margaret?”\n\n“I’m afraid it would, mamma,” said Margaret, remembering the urgency with which she had entreated him to come directly, if he wished to see his mother alive.\n\n“I always dislike that doing things in such a hurry,” said Mrs. Hale.\n\nMargaret was silent.\n\n“Come now, ma’am,” said Dixon, with a kind of cheerful authority, “you know seeing Master Frederick is just the very thing of all others you’re longing for. And I’m glad Miss Margaret wrote off straight, without shilly-shallying. I’ve had a great mind to do it myself. And we’ll keep him snug, depend upon it. There’s only Martha in the house that would not do a good deal to save him on a pinch; and I’ve been thinking she might go and see her mother just at that very time. She’s been saying once or twice she should like to go, for her mother has had a stroke since she came here; only she didn’t like to ask. But I’ll see about her being safe off, as soon as we know when he comes, God bless him! So take your tea, ma’am, in comfort, and trust to me.”\n\nMrs. Hale did trust in Dixon more than in Margaret. Dixon’s words quieted her for the time. Margaret poured out the tea in silence, trying to think of something agreeable to say; but her thoughts made answer something like Daniel O’Rourke, when the man-in-the moon asked him to get off his reaping-hook. “The more you ax us, the more we won’t stir.” The more she tried to think of something—anything besides the danger to which Frederick would be exposed—the more closely her imagination clung to the unfortunate idea presented to her. Her mother prattled with Dixon, and seemed to have utterly forgotten the possibility of Frederick being tried and executed—utterly forgotten that at her wish, if by Margaret’s deed, he was summoned into this danger. Her mother was one of those who throw out terrible possibilities, miserable probabilities, unfortunate chances of all kinds, as a rocket throws out sparks; but if the sparks light on some combustible matter, they smoulder first, and burst out into a frightful flame at last. Margaret was glad when, her filial duties gently and carefully performed, she could go down into the study. She wondered how her father and Higgins had got on.\n\nIn the first place, the decorous, kind-hearted, simple, old-fashioned gentleman, had unconsciously called out, by his own refinement and courteousness of manner, all the latent courtesy in the other.\n\nMr. Hale treated all his fellow-creatures alike: it never entered into his head to make any difference because of their rank. He placed a chair for Nicholas; stood up till he, at Mr. Hale’s request, took a seat; and called him, invariably, “Mr. Higgins,” instead of the curt “Nicholas” or “Higgins,” to which the “drunken infidel weaver” had been accustomed. But Nicholas was neither an habitual drunkard nor a thorough infidel. He drank to drown care, as he would have himself expressed it: and he was infidel so far as he had never yet found any form of faith to which he could attach himself, heart and soul.\n\nMargaret was a little surprised, and very much pleased, when she found her father and Higgins in earnest conversation—each speaking with gentle politeness to the other, however their opinions might clash. Nicholas—clean, tidied (if only at the pump-trough), and quiet spoken—was a new creature to her, who had only seen him in the rough independence of his own hearthstone. He had “slicked” his hair down with the fresh water; he had adjusted his neck-handkerchief, and borrowed an odd candle-end to polish his clogs with; and there he sat, enforcing some opinion on her father, with a strong Darkshire accent, it is true, but with a lowered voice, and a good, earnest composure on his face. Her father, too, was interested in what his companion was saying. He looked round as she came in, smiled, and quietly gave her his chair, and then sat down afresh as quickly as possible, and with a little bow of apology to his guest for the interruption. Higgins nodded to her as a sign of greeting; and she softly adjusted her working materials on the table, and prepared to listen.\n\n“As I was a-saying, sir, I reckon yo’d not ha’ much belief in yo’ if yo’ lived here,—if yo’d been bred here. I ax your pardon if I use wrong words; but what I mean by belief just now, is a-thinking on sayings and maxims and promises made by folk yo’ never saw, about the things and the life yo’ never saw, nor no one else. Now, yo’ say these are true things, and true sayings, and a true life. I just say, where’s the proof? There’s many and many a one wiser, and scores better learned than I am around me,—folk who’ve had time to think on these things,—while my time has had to be gi’en up to getting my bread. Well, I sees these people. Their lives is pretty much open to me. They’re real folk. They don’t believe i’ the Bible,—not they. They may say they do, for form’s sake; but Lord, sir, d’ye think their first cry i’ th’ morning is, ‘What shall I do to get hold on eternal life?’ or ‘What shall I do to fill my purse this blessed day? Where shall I go? What bargains shall I strike?’ The purse and the gold and the notes is real things; things as can be felt and touched; them’s realities; and eternal life is all a talk, very fit for—I ax your pardon, sir; yo’r a parson out o’ work, I believe. Well! I’ll never speak disrespectful of a man in the same fix as I’m in mysel. But I’ll just ax yo’ another question, sir, and I dunnot want yo’ to answer it, only to put in yo’r pipe, and smoke it, afore yo’ go for to set down us, who only believe in what we see, as fools and noddies. If salvation, and life to come, and what not, was true—not in men’s words, but in men’s hearts’ core—dun yo’ not think they’d din us wi’ it as they do wi’ political ’conomy? They’re mighty anxious to come round us wi’ that piece o’ wisdom; but t’other would be a greater convarsion, if it were true.”\n\n“But the masters have nothing to do with your religion. All that they are connected with you in is trade,—so they think,—and all that it concerns them, therefore, to rectify your opinions in is the science of trade.”\n\n“I’m glad, sir,” said Higgins, with a curious wink of his eye, “that yo’ put in, ‘so they think.’ I’d ha’ thought yo’ a hyprocrite, I’m afeard, if yo’ hadn’t, for all yo’r a parson, or rayther because yo’r a parson. Yo’ see, if yo’d spoken o’ religion as a thing that, if it was true, it didn’t concern all men to press on all men’s attention, above everything else in this ’varsal earth, I should ha’ thought yo’ a knave for to be a parson; and I’d rather think yo’ a fool than a knave. No offence, I hope, sir.”\n\n“None at all. You consider me mistaken, and I consider you far more fatally mistaken. I don’t expect to convince you in a day,—not in one conversation; but let us know each other, and speak freely to each other about these things, and the truth will prevail. I should not believe in God if I did not believe that. Mr. Higgins, I trust, whatever else you have given up, you believe”—(Mr. Hale’s voice dropped low in reverence)—“you believe in Him.”\n\nNicholas Higgins suddenly stood straight, stiff up. Margaret started to her feet,—for she thought, by the working of his face, he was going into convulsions. Mr. Hale looked at her dismayed. At last Higgins found words:\n\n“Man! I could fell yo’ to the ground for tempting me. Whatten business have yo’ to try me wi’ your doubts? Think o’ her lying theere, after the life hoo’s led; and think then how yo’d deny me the one sole comfort left—that there is a God, and that He set her her life. I dunnot believe she’ll ever live again,” said he, sitting down, and drearily going on, as if to the unsympathising fire. “I dunnot believe in any other life than this, in which she dreed such trouble, and had such never-ending care; and I cannot bear to think it were all a set o’ chances, that might ha’ been altered wi’ a breath o’ wind. There’s many a time when I’ve thought I didna believe in God, but I’ve never put it fair out before me in words, as many men do. I may ha’ laughed at those who did, to brave it out like—but I have looked round at after, to see if He heard me, if so be there was a He; but to-day, when I’m left desolate, I wunnot listen to yo’ wi’ yo’r questions, and yo’r doubts. There’s but one thing steady and quiet i’ all this reeling world, and, reason or no reason, I’ll cling to that. It’s a’ very well for happy folk”——\n\nMargaret touched his arm very softly. She had not spoken before, nor had he heard her rise.\n\n“Nicholas, we do not want to reason; you misunderstand my father. We do not reason—we believe; and so do you. It is the one sole comfort in such times.”\n\nHe turned round and caught her hand. “Ay! it is, it is”—(brushing away the tears with the back of his hand).—“But yo’ know, she’s lying dead at home; and I’m welly dazed wi’ sorrow, and at times I hardly know what I’m saying. It’s as if speeches folk ha’ made—clever and smart things as I’ve thought at the time—come up now my heart’s welly brossen. Th’ strike’s failed as well; dun yo’ know that, miss? I were coming whoam to ask her, like a beggar as I am, for a bit o’ comfort i’ that trouble; and I were knocked down by one who telled me she were dead—just dead. That were all; but that were enough for me.”\n\nMr. Hale blew his nose, and got up to snuff the candles in order to conceal his emotion. “He’s not an infidel, Margaret; how could you say so?” muttered he reproachfully. “I’ve a good mind to read him the fourteenth chapter of Job.”\n\n“Not yet, papa, I think. Perhaps not at all. Let us ask him about the strike, and give him all the sympathy he needs, and hoped to have from poor Bessy.”\n\nSo they questioned and listened. The workmen’s calculations were based (like too many of the masters’) on false premises. They reckoned on their fellow-men as if they possessed the calculable powers of machines, no more, no less; no allowance for human passions getting the better of reason, as in the case of Boucher and the rioters; and believing that the representations of their injuries would have the same effect on strangers far away, as the injuries (fancied or real) had upon themselves. They were consequently surprised and indignant at the poor Irish, who had allowed themselves to be imported and brought over to take their places. This indignation was tempered in some degree, by contempt for “them Irishers,” and by pleasure at the idea of the bungling way in which they would set to work, and perplex their new masters with their ignorance and stupidity, strange exaggerated stories of which were already spreading through the town. But the most cruel cut of all was that of the Milton workmen, who had defied and disobeyed the commands of the Union to keep the peace, whatever came; who had originated discord in the camp, and spread the panic of the law being arrayed against them.\n\n“And so the strike is at an end,” said Margaret.\n\n“Ay, miss. It’s save as save can. Th’ factory doors will need open wide to-morrow to let in all who’ll be axing for work; if it’s only just to show they’d nought to do wi’ a measure, which if we’d been made o’ th’ right stuff would ha’ brought wages up to a point they’n not been at this ten year.”\n\n“You’ll get work, shan’t you?” asked Margaret. “You’re a famous workman, are not you?”\n\n“Hamper’ll let me work at his mill, when he cuts off his right hand—not before, and not after,” said Nicholas, quietly. Margaret was silenced and sad.\n\n“About the wages,” said Mr. Hale. “You’ll not be offended, but I think you make some sad mistakes. I should like to read you some remarks in a book I have.” He got up and went to his bookshelves.\n\n“Yo’ needn’t trouble yoursel’, sir,” said Nicholas. “Their book-stuff goes in at one ear and out at t’other. I can make nought on’t. Afore Hamper and me had this split, th’ overlooker telled him I were stirring up th’ men to ask for higher wages; and Hamper met me one day in th’ yard. He’d a thin book i’ his hand, and says he, ‘Higgins, I’m told you’re one o’ those damned fools that think you can get higher wages for asking for ’em; ay, and keep ’em up too, when you’ve forced ’em up. Now, I’ll give yo’ a chance and try if yo’ve any sense in yo’. Here’s a book written by a friend o’ mine, and if yo’ll read it yo’ll see how wages find their own level, without either masters or men having aught to do with them; except the men cut their own throats wi’ striking, like the confounded noodles they are.’ Well, now, sir, I put it to yo’, being a parson, and having been in th’ preaching line, and having had to try and bring folk o’er to what yo’ thought was a right way o’ thinking—did yo’ begin by calling ’em fools and such like, or didn’t yo’ rayther give ’em such kind words at first, to make ’em ready for to listen and be convinced, if they could; and in yo’r preaching, did yo’ stop every now and then, and say, half to them and half to yo’rsel’, ‘But yo’r such a pack o’ fools, that I’ve a strong notion it’s no use my trying to put sense into yo’?’ I were not i’ th’ best state, I’ll own, for taking in what Hamper’s friend had to say—I were so vexed at the way it were put to me;—but I thought, ‘Come, I’ll see what these chaps has got to say, and try if it’s them or me as is th’ noodle.’ So I took th’ book and tugged at it; but, Lord bless yo’, it went on about capital and labour, and labour and capital, till it fair sent me off to sleep. I ne’er could rightly fix i’ my mind which was which; and it spoke on ’em as if they was vartues or vices; and what I wanted for to know were the rights o’ men, whether they were rich or poor—so be they only were men.”\n\n“But for all that,” said Mr. Hale, “and granting to the full the offensiveness, the folly, the unchristianness of Mr. Hamper’s way of speaking to you in recommending his friend’s book, yet if it told you what he said it did, that wages find their own level, and that the most successful strike can only force them up for a moment, to sink in far greater proportion afterwards, in consequence of that very strike, the book would have told you the truth.”\n\n“Well, sir,” said Higgins, rather doggedly; “it might, or it might not. There’s two opinions go to settling that point. But suppose it was truth double strong, it were no truth to me if I couldna take it in. I daresay there’s great truth in yon Latin book on your shelves; but it’s gibberish and not truth to me, unless I know the meaning o’ the words. If yo’, sir, or any other knowledgable, patient man comes to me, and says he’ll larn me what the words mean, and not blow me up if I’m a bit stupid, or forget how one thing hangs on another—why, in time I may get to see the truth of it; or I may not. I’ll not be bound to say I shall end in thinking the same as any man. And I’m not one who think truth can be shaped out in words, all neat and clean, as th’ men at th’ foundry cut out sheet-iron. Same bones won’t go down wi’ every one. It’ll stick here i’ this man’s throat, and there i’ t’others. Let alone that, when down, it may be too strong for this one, too weak for that. Folk who sets up to doctor th’ world wi’ their truth, mun suit different for different minds; and be a bit tender i’ th’ way of giving it too, or the poor sick fools may spit it out i’ their faces. Now Hamper first gi’es me a box on my ear, and then he throws his big bolus at me, and says he reckons it’ll do me no good, I’m such a fool, but there it is.”\n\n“I wish some of the kindest and wisest of the masters would meet some of you men, and have a good talk on these things; it would, surely, be the best way of getting over your difficulties, which, I do believe, arise from your ignorance—excuse me, Mr. Higgins—on subjects which it is for the mutual interests of both masters and men should be well understood by both. I wonder”—(half to his daughter) “if Mr. Thornton might not be induced to do such thing?”\n\n“Remember, papa,” said she in a very low voice, “what he said one day,—about governments, you know.” She was unwilling to make any clearer allusion to the conversation they had held on the mode of governing work-people—by giving men intelligence enough to rule themselves, or by a wise despotism on the part of the master—for she saw that Higgins had caught Mr. Thornton’s name, if not the whole of the speech: indeed, he began to speak of him.\n\n“Thornton! He’s the chap as wrote off at once for these Irishers; and led to th’ riot that ruined th’ strike. Even Hamper wi’ all his bullying, would ha’ waited a while—but it’s a word and a blow wi’ Thornton. And, now, when th’ Union would ha’ thanked him for following up th’ chase after Boucher, and them chaps as went right again our commands, it’s Thornton who steps forrard and coolly says that, as th’ strike’s at an end, he, as party injured, doesn’t want to press the charge again the rioters. I thought he’d ha’ carried his point, and had his revenge in an open way; but says he (one in court telled me his very words) ‘they are well known; they will find the natural punishment of their conduct, in the difficulty they will meet wi’ in getting employment. That will be severe enough.’ I only wish they’d cotched Boucher, and had him up before Hamper. I see th’ oud tiger setting on him! would he ha’ let him off? Not he!”\n\n“Mr. Thornton was right,” said Margaret. “You are angry against Boucher, Nicholas; or else you would be the first to see, that where the natural punishment would be severe enough for the offence, any farther punishment would be something like revenge.”\n\n“My daughter is no great friend of Mr. Thornton’s,” said Mr. Hale, smiling at Margaret; while she, as red as any carnation, began to work with double diligence, “but I believe what she says is the truth. I like him for it.”\n\n“Well, sir, this strike has been a weary bit o’ business to me; and yo’ll not wonder if I’m a bit put out wi’ seeing it fail, just for a few men who would na suffer in silence, and aou’d out, brave and firm.”\n\n“You forget!” said Margaret. “I don’t know much of Boucher; but the only time I saw him it was not his own sufferings he spoke of, but those of his sick wife—his little children.”\n\n“True! but he were not made of iron himsel’. He’d ha’ cried out for his own sorrows, next. He were not one to bear.”\n\n“How came he into the Union?” asked Margaret innocently. “You don’t seem to have much respect for him; nor gained much good from having him in.”\n\nHiggins’s brow clouded. He was silent for a minute or two. Then he said, shortly enough:\n\n“It’s not for me to speak o’ th’ Union. What they does they does. Them that is of a trade mun hang together; and if they’re not willing to take their chance along wi’ th’ rest, th’ Union has ways and means.”\n\nMr. Hale saw that Higgins was vexed at the turn the conversation had taken, and was silent. Not so Margaret, though she saw Higgins’s feeling as clearly as he did. By instinct she felt that if he could but be brought to express himself in plain words, something clear would be gained on which to argue for the right and the just.\n\n“And what are the Union’s ways and means?”\n\nHe looked up at her, as if on the point of dogged resistance to her wish for information. But her calm face, fixed on his, patient and trustful, compelled him to answer.\n\n“Well! If a man doesn’t belong to th’ Union, them as works next looms as orders not to speak to him—if he’s sorry or ill it’s a’ the same; he’s out o’ bounds; he’s none o’ us; he comes among us, he works among us, but he’s none o’ us. I’ some places them’s fined who speaks to him. Yo’ try that, miss; try living a year or two among them as looks away if yo’ look at ’em; try working within two yards o’ crowds o’ men, who, yo’ know, have a grinding grudge at yo’ in their hearts—to whom if yo’ say yo’r glad, not an eye brightens, nor a lip moves,—to whom if your heart’s heavy, yo’ can never say nought, because they’ll ne’er take notice on your sighs or sad looks (and a man’s no man who’ll groan out loud ’bout folk asking him what’s the matter?)—just yo’ try that, miss—ten hours for three hundred days, and yo’ll know a bit what the Union is.”\n\n“Why!” said Margaret, “what tyranny this is! Nay, Higgins, I don’t care one straw for your anger. I know you can’t be angry with me if you would, and I must tell you the truth: that I never read, in all the history I have read, of a more slow, lingering torture than this. And you belong to the Union! And you talk of the tyranny of the masters!”\n\n“Nay,” said Higgins, “yo’ may say what yo’ like. The dead stand between yo’ and every angry word o’ mine. D’ye think I forget who’s lying there, and how hoo loved yo’? And it’s th’ masters as has made us sin, if th’ Union is a sin. Not this generation maybe, but their fathers. Their fathers ground our fathers to the very dust; ground us to powder! Parson! I reckon I’ve heerd my mother read out a text, ‘The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and th’ chidren’s teeth are set on edge.’ Its so wi’ them. In those days of sore oppression th’ Unions began; it were a necessity. It’s a necessity now, according to me. It’s a withstanding of injustice, past, present, or to come. It may be like war; along wi’ it come crimes; but I think it were a greater crime to let it alone. Our only chance is binding men together in one common interest; and if some are cowards and some are fools, they mun come along and join the great march, whose only strength is in numbers.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mr. Hale, sighing, “your Union in itself would be beautiful, glorious—it would be Christianity itself—if it were but for an end which affected the good of all, instead of that of merely one class as opposed to another.”\n\n“I reckon it’s time for me to be going, sir,” said Higgins, as the clock struck ten.\n\n“Home?” said Margaret very softly. He understood her, and took her offered hand. “Home, miss. Yo’ may trust me, tho’ I am one o’ th’ Union.”\n\n“I do trust you most thoroughly, Nicholas.”\n\n“Stay!” said Mr. Hale, hurrying to the bookshelves. “Mr. Higgins! I’m sure you’ll join us in family prayer?”\n\nHiggins looked at Margaret, doubtfully. Her grave sweet eyes met his; there was no compulsion, only deep interest in them. He did not speak, but he kept his place.\n\nMargaret the Churchwoman, her father the Dissenter, Higgins the Infidel, knelt down together. It did them no harm.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX\n\n“Some wishes crossed my mind and dimly cheered it, And one or two poor melancholy pleasures, Each in the pale unwarming light of hope, Silvering its flimsy wing, flew silent by— Moths in the moonbeam!” COLERIDGE.\n\nThe next morning brought Margaret a letter from Edith. It was affectionate and inconsequent like the writer. But the affection was charming to Margaret’s own affectionate nature; and she had grown up with the inconsequence, so she did not perceive it. It was as follows:—\n\n“Oh, Margaret, it is worth a journey from England to see my boy! He is a superb little fellow, especially in his caps, and most especially in the one you sent him, you good, dainty-fingered, persevering little lady! Having made all the mothers here envious, I want to show him to somebody new, and hear a fresh set of admiring expressions; perhaps, that’s all the reason; perhaps it is not—nay, possibly, there is just a little cousinly love mixed with it; but I do want you so much to come here, Margaret! I’m sure it would be the very best thing for Aunt Hale’s health; everybody here is young and well, and our skies are always blue, and our sun always shines, and the band plays deliciously from morning till night; and, to come back to the burden of my ditty, my baby always smiles. I am constantly wanting you to draw him from me, Margaret. It does not signify what he is doing; that very thing is prettiest, gracefullest, best. I think I love him a great deal better than my husband, who is getting stout, and grumpy—what he calls ‘busy.’ No! he is not. He has just come in with news of such a charming picnic, given by the officers of the Hazard, at anchor in the bay below. Because he has brought in such a pleasant piece of news, I retract all I said just now. Did not somebody burn his hand for having said or done something he was sorry for? Well, I can’t burn mine, because it would hurt me, and the scar would be ugly; but I’ll retract all I said as fast as I can. Cosmo is quite as great a darling as baby, and not a bit stout, and as ungrumpy as ever husband was; only, sometimes he is very, very busy. I may say that without love—wifely duty— where was I?—I had something very particular to say, I know, once. Oh, it is this—Dearest Margaret!—you must come and see me; it would do Aunt Hale good, as I said before. Get the doctor to order it for her. Tell him that it’s the smoke of Milton that does her harm. I have no doubt it is that, really. Three months (you must not come for less) of this delicious climate—all sunshine, and grapes as common as blackberries, would quite cure her. I don’t ask my uncle”—(Here the letter became more constrained, and better written. Mr. Hale was in the corner, like a naughty child, for having given up his living.)—“because, I dare say, he disapproves of war, soldiers, and bands of music; at least, I know that many Dissenters are members of the Peace Society, and I am afraid he would not like to come; but, if he would, dear, pray say that Cosmo and I will do our best to make him happy; and I’ll hide up Cosmo’s red coat and sword, and make the band play all sorts of grave, solemn things; or, if they do play pomps and vanities, it shall be in double slow time. Dear Margaret, if he would like to accompany you and Aunt Hale, we will try and make it pleasant, though I’m rather afraid of any one who has done anything for conscience’ sake. You never did, I hope. Tell Aunt Hale not to bring any warm clothes, though I’m afraid it will be late in the year before you can come. But you have no idea of the heat here! I tried to wear my great beauty Indian shawl at a picnic. I kept myself up with proverbs as long as I could; ‘Pride must abide,’—and such wholesome pieces of pith; but it was no use. I was like mamma’s little dog Tiny with an elephant’s trappings on; smothered, hidden, killed with my finery; so I made it into a capital carpet for us all to sit down upon. Here’s this boy of mine, Margaret—if you don’t pack up your things as soon as you get this letter, and come straight off to see him, I shall think you’re descended from King Herod!”\n\nMargaret did long for a day of Edith’s life—her freedom from care, her cheerful home, her sunny skies. If a wish could have transported her, she would have gone off; just for one day. She yearned for the strength which such a change would give—even for a few hours to be in the midst of that bright life, and to feel young again. Not yet twenty! and she had had to bear up against such hard pressure that she felt quite old. That was her first feeling after reading Edith’s letter. Then she read it again, and, forgetting herself, was amused at its likeness to Edith’s self, and was laughing merrily over it when Mrs. Hale came into the drawing-room, leaning on Dixon’s arm. Margaret flew to adjust the pillows. Her mother seemed more than usually feeble.\n\n“What were you laughing at, Margaret?” asked she, as soon as she had recovered from the exertion of settling herself on the sofa.\n\n“A letter I have had this morning from Edith. Shall I read it to you, mamma?”\n\nShe read it aloud, and for a time it seemed to interest her mother, who kept wondering what name Edith had given to her boy, and suggesting all probable names, and all possible reasons why each and all of these names should be given. Into the very midst of these wonders Mr. Thornton came, bringing another offering of fruit for Mrs. Hale. He could not—say rather, he would not—deny himself the chance of the pleasure of seeing Margaret. He had no end in this but the present gratification. It was the sturdy wilfulness of a man usually most reasonable and self-controlled. He entered the room, taking in at a glance the fact of Margaret’s presence; but after the first cold distant bow, he never seemed to let his eyes fall on her again. He only stayed to present his peaches—to speak some gentle kindly words—and then his cold offended eyes met Margaret’s with a grave farewell, as he left the room. She sat down silent and pale.\n\n“Do you know, Margaret, I really begin to like Mr. Thornton.”\n\nNo answer at first. Then Margaret forced out an icy “Do you?”\n\n“Yes! I think he is really getting quite polished in his manners.”\n\nMargaret’s voice was more in order now. She replied, “He is very kind and attentive—there is no doubt of that.”\n\n“I wonder Mrs. Thornton never calls. She must know I am ill, because of the water-bed.”\n\n“I dare say, she hears how you are from her son.”\n\n“Still, I should like to see her. You have so few friends here, Margaret.”\n\nMargaret felt what was in her mother’s thoughts—a tender craving to bespeak the kindness of some woman towards the daughter that might soon be left motherless. But she could not speak.\n\n“Do you think,” said Mrs. Hale, after a pause, “that you could go and ask Mrs. Thornton to come and see me? Only once—I don’t want to be troublesome.”\n\n“I will do anything, if you wish it, mamma—but if—but when Frederick comes——”\n\n“Ah, to be sure! we must keep our doors shut—we must let no one in. I hardly know whether I dare wish him to come or not. Sometimes I think I would rather not. Sometimes I have such frightful dreams about him.”\n\n“Oh, mamma! we’ll take good care. I will put my arm in the bolt sooner than he should come to the slightest harm. Trust the care of him to me, mamma. I will watch over him like a lioness over her young.”\n\n“When can we hear from him?”\n\n“Not for a week yet, certainly—perhaps more.”\n\n“We must send Martha away in good time. It would never do to have her here when he comes, and then send her off in a hurry.”\n\n“Dixon is sure to remind us of that. I was thinking that, if we wanted any help in the house while he is here, we could perhaps get Mary Higgins. She is very slack of work, and is a good girl, and would take pains to do her best, I am sure, and would sleep at home, and need never come upstairs, so as to know who is in the house.”\n\n“As you please. As Dixon pleases. But, Margaret, don’t get to use these horrid Milton words. ‘Slack of work:’ it is a provincialism. What will your aunt Shaw say, if she hears you use it on her return?”\n\n“Oh, mamma! don’t try and make a bugbear of aunt Shaw,” said Margaret, laughing. “Edith picked up all sorts of military slang from Captain Lennox, and aunt Shaw never took any notice of it.”\n\n“But yours is factory slang.”\n\n“And if I live in a factory town, I must speak factory language when I want it. Why, mamma, I could astonish you with a great many words you never heard in your life. I don’t believe you know what a knobstick is.”\n\n“Not I, child. I only know it has a very vulgar sound; and I don’t want to hear you using it.”\n\n“Very well, dearest mother, I won’t. Only I shall have to use a whole explanatory sentence instead.”\n\n“I don’t like this Milton,” said Mrs. Hale. “Edith is right enough in saying it’s the smoke that has made me so ill.”\n\nMargaret started up as her mother said this. Her father had just entered the room, and she was most anxious that the faint impression she had seen on his mind that the Milton air had injured her mother’s health, should not be deepened—should not receive any confirmation. She could not tell whether he had heard what Mrs. Hale had said or not; but she began speaking hurriedly of other things, unaware that Mr. Thornton was following him.\n\n“Mamma is accusing me of having picked up a great deal of vulgarity since we came to Milton.”\n\nThe “vulgarity” Margaret spoke of, referred purely to the use of local words, and the expression arose out of the conversation they had just been holding. But Mr. Thornton’s brow darkened; and Margaret suddenly felt how her speech might be misunderstood by him; so, in the natural sweet desire to avoid giving unnecessary pain, she forced herself to go forwards with a little greeting, and continue what she was saying, addressing herself to him expressly.\n\n“Now, Mr. Thornton, though ‘knobstick’ has not a very pretty sound, is it not expressive? Could I do without it, in speaking of the thing it represents? If using local words is vulgar, I was very vulgar in the Forest,—was I not, mamma?”\n\nIt was unusual with Margaret to obtrude her own subject of conversation on others; but, in this case, she was so anxious to prevent Mr. Thornton from feeling annoyance at the words he had accidentally overheard, that it was not until she had done speaking that she coloured all over with consciousness, more especially as Mr. Thornton seemed hardly to understand the exact gist or bearing of what she was saying, but passed her by, with a cold reserve of ceremonious movement, to speak to Mrs. Hale.\n\nThe sight of him reminded her of the wish to see his mother, and commend Margaret to her care. Margaret, sitting in burning silence, vexed and ashamed of her difficulty in keeping her right place, and her calm unconsciousness of heart, when Mr. Thornton was by, heard her mother’s slow entreaty that Mrs. Thornton would come and see her; see her soon; to-morrow, if it were possible. Mr. Thornton promised that she should—conversed a little, and then took his leave; and Margaret’s movements and voice seemed at once released from some invisible chains. He never looked at her; and yet, the careful avoidance of his eyes betokened that in some way he knew exactly where, if they fell by chance, they would rest on her. If she spoke, he gave no sign of attention, and yet his next speech to any one else was modified by what she had said; sometimes there was an express answer to what she had remarked, but given to another person as though unsuggested by her. It was not the bad manners of ignorance; it was the wilful bad manners arising from deep offence. It was wilful at the time; repented of afterwards. But no deep plan, no careful cunning could have stood him in such good stead. Margaret thought about him more than she had ever done before; not with any tinge of what is called love, but with regret that she had wounded him so deeply,—and with a gentle, patient striving to return to their former position of antagonistic friendship; for a friend’s position was what she found that he had held in her regard, as well as in that of the rest of the family. There was a pretty humility in her behaviour to him, as if mutely apologising for the over-strong words which were the reaction from the deeds of the day of the riot.\n\nBut he resented those words bitterly. They rung in his ears; and he was proud of the sense of justice which made him go in every kindness he could offer to her parents. He exulted in the power he showed in compelling himself to face her, whenever he could think of any action which might give her father or mother pleasure. He thought that he disliked seeing one who had mortified him so keenly; but he was mistaken. It was a stinging pleasure to be in the room with her, and feel her presence. But he was no great analyser of his own motives and was mistaken, as I have said.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX\n\n“The saddest birds a season find to sing.” SOUTHWELL.\n\n“Never to fold the robe o’er secret pain, Never, weighed down by memory’s clouds again, To bow thy head! Thou art gone home!” MRS. HEMANS.\n\nMrs. Thornton came to see Mrs. Hale the next morning. She was much worse. One of those sudden changes—those great visible strides towards death, had been taken in the night, and her own family were startled by the gray sunken look her features had assumed in that one twelve hours of suffering. Mrs. Thornton—who had not seen her for weeks—was softened all at once. She had come because her son asked it from her as a personal favour, but with all the proud bitter feelings of her nature in arms against that family of which Margaret formed one. She doubted the reality of Mrs. Hale’s illness; she doubted any want beyond a momentary fancy on that lady’s part, which should take her out of her previously settled course of employment for the day. She told her son that she wished they had never come near the place; that he had never got acquainted with them; that there had been no such useless languages as Latin and Greek ever invented. He bore all this pretty silently; but when she had ended her invective against the dead languages, he quietly returned to the short, curt, decided expression of his wish that she should go and see Mrs. Hale at the time appointed, as most likely to be convenient to the invalid. Mrs. Thornton submitted with as bad a grace as she could to her son’s desire, all the time liking him the better for having it; and exaggerating in her own mind the same notion that he had of extraordinary goodness on his part in so perseveringly keeping up with the Hales.\n\nHis goodness verging on weakness (as all the softer virtues did in her mind), and her own contempt for Mr. and Mrs. Hale, and positive dislike to Margaret, were the ideas which occupied Mrs. Thornton, till she was struck into nothingness before the dark shadow of the wings of the angel of death. There lay Mrs. Hale—a mother like herself—a much younger woman than she was,—on the bed from which there was no sign of hope that she might ever rise again. No more variety of light and shade for her in that darkened room; no power of action, scarcely change of movement; faint alternations of whispered sound and studious silence; and yet that monotonous life seemed almost too much! When Mrs. Thornton, strong and prosperous with life, came in, Mrs. Hale lay still, although from the look on her face she was evidently conscious of who it was. But she did not even open her eyes for a minute or two. The heavy moisture of tears stood on her eyelashes before she looked up; then, with her hand groping feebly over the bed-clothes, for the touch of Mrs. Thornton’s large firm fingers, she said, scarcely above her breath.—Mrs. Thornton had to stoop from her erectness to listen,—\n\n“Margaret—you have a daughter—my sister is in Italy. My child will be without a mother;—in a strange place,—if I die— will you”——\n\nAnd her filmy wandering eyes fixed themselves with an intensity of wistfulness on Mrs. Thornton’s face. For a minute there was no change in its rigidness; it was stern and unmoved;—nay, but that the eyes of the sick woman were growing dim with the slow-gathering tears, she might have seen a dark cloud cross the cold features. And it was no thought of her son, or of her living daughter Fanny, that stirred her heart at last; but a sudden remembrance, suggested by something in the arrangement of the room,—of a little daughter—dead in infancy—long years ago—that, like a sudden sunbeam, melted the icy crust, behind which there was a real tender woman.\n\n“You wish me to be a friend to Miss Hale,” said Mrs. Thornton, in her measured voice, that would not soften with her heart, but came out distinct and clear.\n\nMrs. Hale, her eyes still fixed on Mrs. Thornton’s face, pressed the hand that lay below hers on the coverlet. She could not speak. Mrs. Thornton sighed, “I will be a true friend, if circumstances require it. Not a tender friend. That I cannot be,”—(“to her,” she was on the point of adding, but she relented at the sight of that poor, anxious face.)—“It is not my nature to show affection even where I feel it, nor do I volunteer advice in general. Still, at your request,—if it will be any comfort to you, I will promise you.” Then came a pause. Mrs. Thornton was too conscientious to promise what she did not mean to perform; and to perform anything in the way of kindness on behalf of Margaret, more disliked at this moment than ever, was difficult; almost impossible.\n\n“I promise,” said she, with grave severity; which, after all, inspired the dying woman with faith as in something more stable than life itself,—flickering, flittering, wavering life! “I promise that in any difficulty in which Miss Hale”——\n\n“Call her Margaret!” gasped Mrs. Hale.\n\n“In which she comes to me for help, I will help her with every power I have, as if she were my own daughter. I also promise that if ever I see her doing what I think is wrong”——\n\n“But Margaret never does wrong—not wilfully wrong,” pleaded Mrs. Hale. Mrs. Thornton went on as before; as if she had not heard:\n\n“If ever I see her doing what I believe to be wrong—such wrong not touching me or mine, in which case I might be supposed to have an interested motive—I will tell her of it, faithfully and plainly, as I should wish my own daughter to be told.”\n\nThere was a long pause. Mrs. Hale felt that this promise did not include all; and yet it was much. It had reservations in it which she did not understand: but then she was weak, dizzy, and tired. Mrs. Thornton was reviewing all the probable cases in which she had pledged herself to act. She had a fierce pleasure in the idea of telling Margaret unwelcome truths, in the shape of performance of duty. Mrs. Hale began to speak:\n\n“I thank you. I pray God to bless you. I shall never see you again in this world. But my last words are, I thank you for your promise of kindness to my child.”\n\n“Not kindness!” testified Mrs. Thornton, ungraciously truthful to the last. But having eased her conscience by saying these words, she was not sorry that they were not heard. She pressed Mrs. Hale’s soft languid hand; and rose up and went her way out of the house without seeing a creature.\n\nDuring the time that Mrs. Thornton was having this interview with Mrs. Hale, Margaret and Dixon were laying their heads together and consulting how they should keep Frederick’s coming a profound secret to all out of the house. A letter from him might now be expected any day; and he would assuredly follow quickly on its heels. Martha must be sent away on her holiday; Dixon must keep stern guard on the front door, only admitting the few visitors that ever came to the house into Mr. Hale’s room down stairs—Mrs. Hale’s extreme illness giving her a good excuse for this. If Mary Higgins was required as a help to Dixon in the kitchen, she was to hear and see as little of Frederick as possible; and he was, if necessary, to be spoken of to her under the name of Mr. Dickinson. But her sluggish and incurious nature was the greatest safeguard of all.\n\nThey resolved that Martha should leave them that very afternoon for this visit to her mother. Margaret wished that she had been sent away on the previous day, as she fancied it might be thought strange to give a servant a holiday when her mistress’s state required so much attendance.\n\nPoor Margaret! All that afternoon she had to act the part of a Roman daughter, and give strength out of her own scanty stock to her father. Mr. Hale would hope, would not despair, between the attacks of his wife’s malady; he buoyed himself up in every respite from her pain, and believed that it was the beginning of ultimate recovery. And so, when the paroxysms came on, each more severe than the last, they were fresh agonies, and greater disappointments to him. This afternoon, he sat in the drawing-room, unable to bear the solitude of his study, or to employ himself in any way. He buried his head in his arms, which lay folded on the table. Margaret’s heart ached to see him; yet, as he did not speak, she did not like to volunteer any attempt at comfort. Martha was gone. Dixon sat with Mrs. Hale while she slept. The house was very still and quiet, and darkness came on, without any movement to procure candles. Margaret sat at the window, looking out at the lamps and the street, but seeing nothing,—only alive to her father’s heavy sighs. She did not like to go down for lights, lest the tacit restraint of her presence being withdrawn, he might give way to more violent emotion, without her being at hand to comfort him. Yet she was just thinking that she ought to go and see after the well-doing of the kitchen fire, which there was nobody but herself to attend to, when she heard the muffled door-bell ring with so violent a pull, that the wires jingled all through the house, though the positive sound was not great. She started up, passed her father, who had never moved at the veiled, dull sound,—returned, and kissed him tenderly. And still he never moved, nor took any notice of her fond embrace. Then she went down softly, through the dark, to the door. Dixon would have put the chain on before she opened it, but Margaret had not a thought of fear in her pre-occupied mind. A man’s tall figure stood between her and the luminous street. He was looking away; but at the sound of the latch he turned quickly round.\n\n“Is this Mr. Hale’s?” said he, in a clear, full, delicate voice.\n\nMargaret trembled all over; at first she did not answer. In a moment she sighed out,\n\n“Frederick!” and stretched out both her hands to catch his, and draw him in.\n\n“Oh, Margaret!” said he, holding her off by her shoulders, after they had kissed each other, as if even in that darkness he could see her face, and read in its expression a quicker answer to his question than words could give,—\n\n“My mother! is she alive!”\n\n“Yes, she is alive, dear, dear brother! She—as ill as she can be she is; but alive! She is alive!”\n\n“Thank God!” said he.\n\n“Papa is utterly prostrate with this great grief.”\n\n“You expect me, don’t you?”\n\n“No, we have had no letter.”\n\n“Then I have come before it. But my mother knows I am coming.”\n\n“Oh! we all knew you would come. But wait a little! Step in here. Give me your hand. What is this? Oh! your carpet-bag. Dixon has shut the shutters; but this is papa’s study, and I can take you to a chair to rest yourself for a few minutes; while I go and tell him.”\n\nShe groped her way to the taper and the lucifer matches. She suddenly felt shy when the little feeble light made them visible. All she could see was, that her brother’s face was unusually dark in complexion, and she caught the stealthy look of a pair of remarkably long-cut blue eyes, that suddenly twinkled up with a droll consciousness of their mutual purpose of inspecting each other. But though the brother and sister had an instant of sympathy in their reciprocal glances, they did not exchange a word; only, Margaret felt sure that she should like her brother as a companion as much as she already loved him as a near relation. Her heart was wonderfully lighter as she went up stairs; the sorrow was no less in reality, but it became less oppressive from having some one in precisely the same relation to it as that in which she stood. Not her father’s desponding attitude had power to damp her now. He lay across the table, helpless as ever; but she had the spell by which to rouse him. She used it perhaps too violently in her own great relief.\n\n“Papa,” said she, throwing her arms fondly round his neck; pulling his weary head up in fact with her gentle violence, till it rested in her arms, and she could look into his eyes, and let them gain strength and assurance from hers.\n\n“Papa! guess who is here!”\n\nHe looked at her; she saw the idea of the truth glimmer into their filmy sadness, and be dismissed thence as a wild imagination.\n\nHe threw himself forward, and hid his face once more in his stretched-out arms, resting upon the table as heretofore. She heard him whisper; she bent tenderly down to listen. “I don’t know; don’t tell me it is Frederick—not Frederick. I cannot bear it,—I am too weak. And his mother is dying!”\n\nHe began to cry and wail like a child. It was so different to all which Margaret had hoped and expected, that she turned sick with disappointment, and was silent for an instant. Then she spoke again—very differently—not so exultingly, far more tenderly and carefully.\n\n“Papa, it is Frederick! Think of mamma, how glad she will be! And oh, for her sake, how glad we ought to be! For his sake, too,—our poor, poor boy!”\n\nHer father did not change his attitude, but he seemed to be trying to understand the fact.\n\n“Where is he?” asked he at last, his face still hidden in his prostrate arms.\n\n“In your study, quite alone. I lighted the taper, and ran up to tell you. He is quite alone, and will be wondering why—”\n\n“I will go to him,” broke in her father; and he lifted himself up and leant on her arm as on that of a guide.\n\nMargaret led him to the study door, but her spirits were so agitated that she felt she could not bear to see the meeting. She turned away, and ran up stairs, and cried most heartily. It was the first time she had dared to allow herself this relief for days. The strain had been terrible, as she now felt. But Frederick was come! He, the one precious brother, was there, safe, amongst them again! She could hardly believe it. She stopped her crying, and opened her bedroom door. She heard no sound of voices, and almost feared she might have dreamt. She went down stairs, and listened at the study door. She heard the buzz of voices; and that was enough. She went into the kitchen, and stirred up the fire, and lighted the house, and prepared for the wanderer’s refreshment. How fortunate it was that her mother slept! She knew that she did, from the candle-lighter thrust through the keyhole of her bedroom door. The traveller could be refreshed and bright, and the first excitement of the meeting with his father all be over, before her mother became aware of anything unusual.\n\nWhen all was ready, Margaret opened the study door, and went in like a serving-maiden, with a heavy tray held in her extended arms. She was proud of serving Frederick. But he, when he saw her, sprang up in a minute, and relieved her of her burden. It was a type, a sign, of all the coming relief which his presence would bring. The brother and sister arranged the table together, saying little, but their hands touching, and their eyes speaking the natural language of expression, so intelligible to those of the same blood. The fire had gone out; and Margaret applied herself to light it, for the evenings had begun to be chilly; and yet it was desirable to make all noises as distant as possible from Mrs. Hale’s room.\n\n“Dixon says it is a gift to light a fire; not an art to be acquired.”\n\n“Poeta nascitur, non fit,” murmured Mr. Hale; and Margaret was glad to hear a quotation once more, however languidly given.\n\n“Dear old Dixon! How we shall kiss each other!” said Frederick. “She used to kiss me, and then look in my face to be sure I was the right person, and then set to again! But, Margaret, what a bungler you are! I never saw such a little awkward, good-for-nothing pair of hands. Run away, and wash them, ready to cut bread-and-butter for me, and leave the fire. I’ll manage it. Lighting fires is one of my natural accomplishments.”\n\nSo Margaret went away; and returned; and passed in and out of the room, in a glad restlessness that could not be satisfied with sitting still. The more wants Frederick had, the better she was pleased; and he understood all this by instinct. It was a joy snatched in the house of mourning, and the zest of it was all the more pungent, because they knew in the depth of their hearts what irremediable sorrow awaited them.\n\nIn the middle, they heard Dixon’s foot on the stairs. Mr. Hale started from his languid posture in his great arm-chair, from which he had been watching his children in a dreamy way, as if they were acting some drama of happiness, which it was pretty to look at, but which was distinct from reality, and in which he had no part. He stood up, and faced the door, showing such a strange, sudden anxiety to conceal Frederick from the sight of any person entering, even though it were the faithful Dixon, that a shiver came over Margaret’s heart: it reminded her of the new fear in their lives. She caught at Frederick’s arm, and clutched it tight, while a stern thought compressed her brow, and caused her to set her teeth. And yet they knew it was only Dixon’s measured tread. They heard her walk the length of the passage,—into the kitchen. Margaret rose up.\n\n“I will go to her, and tell her. And I shall hear how mamma is.” Mrs. Hale was awake. She rambled at first; but after they had given her some tea she was refreshed, though not disposed to talk. It was better that the night should pass over before she was told of her son’s arrival. Dr. Donaldson’s appointed visit would bring nervous excitement enough for the evening; and he might tell them how to prepare her for seeing Frederick. He was there, in the house; could be summoned at any moment.\n\nMargaret could not sit still. It was a relief to her to aid Dixon in all her preparations for “Master Frederick.” It seemed as though she never could be tired again. Each glimpse into the room where he sate by his father, conversing with him, about, she knew not what, nor cared to know,—was increase of strength to her. Her own time for talking and hearing would come at last, and she was too certain of this to feel in a hurry to grasp it now. She took in his appearance and liked it. He had delicate features, redeemed from effeminacy by the swarthiness of his complexion, and his quick intensity of expression. His eyes were generally merry-looking, but at times they and his mouth so suddenly changed, and gave her such an idea of latent passion, that it almost made her afraid. But this look was only for an instant; and had in it no doggedness, no vindictiveness; it was rather the instantaneous ferocity of expression that comes over the countenances of all natives of wild or southern countries—a ferocity which enhances the charm of the childlike softness into which such a look may melt away. Margaret might fear the violence of the impulsive nature thus occasionally betrayed, but there was nothing in it to make her distrust, or recoil in the least, from the new-found brother. On the contrary, all their intercourse was peculiarly charming to her from the very first. She knew then how much responsibility she had had to bear, from the exquisite sensation of relief which she felt in Frederick’s presence. He understood his father and mother—their characters and their weaknesses, and went along with a careless freedom, which was yet most delicately careful not to hurt or wound any of their feelings. He seemed to know instinctively when a little of the natural brilliancy of his manner and conversation would not jar on the deep depression of his father, or might relieve his mother’s pain. Whenever it would have been out of tune, and out of time, his patient devotion and watchfulness came into play, and made him an admirable nurse. Then Margaret was almost touched into tears by the allusions which he often made to their childish days in the New Forest; he had never forgotten her—or Helstone either—all the time he had been roaming among distant countries and foreign people. She might talk to him of the old spot, and never feared tiring him. She had been afraid of him before he came, even while she had longed for his coming; seven or eight years had, she felt, produced such great changes in herself that, forgetting how much of the original Margaret was left, she had reasoned that if her tastes and feelings had so materially altered, even in her stay-at-home life, his wild career, with which she was but imperfectly acquainted, must have almost substituted another Frederick for the tall stripling in his middy’s uniform, whom she remembered looking up to with such admiring awe. But in their absence they had grown nearer to each other in age, as well as in many other things. And so it was that the weight, this sorrowful time, was lightened to Margaret. Other light than that of Frederick’s presence she had none. For a few hours, the mother rallied on seeing her son. She sate with his hand in hers; she would not part with it even while she slept; and Margaret had to feed him like a baby, rather than he should disturb her mother by removing a finger. Mrs. Hale wakened while they were thus engaged; she slowly moved her head round on the pillow, and smiled at her children, as she understood what they were doing, and why it was done.\n\n“I am very selfish,” said she; “but it will not be for long.” Frederick bent down and kissed the feeble hand that imprisoned his.\n\nThis state of tranquility could not endure for many days, nor perhaps for many hours; so Dr. Donaldson assured Margaret. After the kind doctor had gone away, she stole down to Frederick, who, during the visit, had been adjured to remain quietly concealed in the back parlour, usually Dixon’s bedroom, but now given up to him.\n\nMargaret told him what Dr. Donaldson said.\n\n“I don’t believe it,” he exclaimed. “She is very ill; she may be dangerously ill, and in immediate danger, too; but I can’t imagine that she could be as she is, if she were on the point of death. Margaret! she should have some other advice—some London doctor. Have you never thought of that?”\n\n“Yes,” said Margaret, “more than once. But I don’t believe it would do any good. And, you know, we have not the money to bring any great London surgeon down, and I am sure Dr. Donaldson is only second in skill to the very best,—if, indeed, he is to them.”\n\nFrederick began to walk up and down the room impatiently.\n\n“I have credit in Cadiz,” said he, “but none here, owing to this wretched change of name. Why did my father leave Helstone? That was the blunder.”\n\n“It was no blunder,” said Margaret gloomily. “And above all possible chances, avoid letting papa hear anything like what you have just been saying. I can see that he is tormenting himself already with the idea that mamma would never have been ill if we had stayed at Helstone, and you don’t know papa’s agonising power of self-reproach!”\n\nFrederick walked away as if he were on the quarter-deck. At last he stopped right opposite to Margaret, and looked at her drooping and desponding attitude for an instant.\n\n“My little Margaret!” said he, caressing her. “Let us hope as long as we can. Poor little woman! what! is this face all wet with tears? I will hope. I will, in spite of a thousand doctors. Bear up, Margaret, and be brave enough to hope!”\n\nMargaret choked in trying to speak, and when she did it was very low.\n\n“I must try to be meek enough to trust. Oh, Frederick! mamma was getting to love me so! And I was getting to understand her. And now comes death to snap us asunder!”\n\n“Come, come, come! Let us go upstairs, and do something, rather than waste time that may be so precious. Thinking has, many a time, made me sad, darling; but doing never did in all my life. My theory is a sort of parody on the maxim of ‘Get money, my son, honestly if you can; but get money.’ My precept is, ‘Do something my sister, do good if you can; but, at any rate, do something.’”\n\n“Not excluding mischief,” said Margaret, smiling faintly through her tears.\n\n“By no means. What I do exclude is the remorse afterwards. Blot your misdeeds out (if you are particularly conscientious), by a good deed, as soon as you can; just as we did a correct sum at school on the slate, where an incorrect one was only half rubbed out. It was better than wetting our sponge with our tears; both less loss of time where tears had to be waited for, and a better effect at last.”\n\nIf Margaret thought Frederick’s theory rather a rough one at first, she saw how he worked it out into continual production of kindness in fact. After a bad night with his mother (for he insisted on taking his turn as a sitter-up) he was busy next morning before breakfast, contriving a leg-rest for Dixon, who was beginning to feel the fatigues of watching. At breakfast-time, he interested Mr. Hale with vivid, graphic, rattling accounts of the wild life he had led in Mexico, South America, and elsewhere. Margaret would have given up the effort in despair to rouse Mr. Hale out of his dejection; it would even have affected herself and rendered her incapable of talking at all. But Fred, true to his theory, did something perpetually; and talking was the only thing to be done, besides eating, at breakfast.\n\nBefore the night of that day, Dr. Donaldson’s opinion was proved to be too well founded. Convulsions came on; and when they ceased, Mrs. Hale was unconscious. Her husband might lie by her shaking the bed with his sobs; her son’s strong arms might lift her tenderly up into a comfortable position; her daughter’s hands might bathe her face; but she knew them not. She would never recognise them again, till they met in Heaven.\n\nBefore the morning came all was over.\n\nThen Margaret rose from her trembling and despondency, and became as a strong angel of comfort to her father and brother. For Frederick had broken down now, and all his theories were of no use to him. He cried so violently when shut up in his little room at night, that Margaret and Dixon came down in affright to warn him to be quiet: for the house partitions were but thin, and the next door neighbours might easily hear his youthful passionate sobs, so different from the slower trembling agony of after-life, when we become inured to grief, and dare not be rebellious against the inexorable doom, knowing who it is that decrees.\n\nMargaret sate with her father in the room of the dead. If he had cried, she would have been thankful. But he sate by the bed quite quietly; only, from time to time, he uncovered the face, and stroked it gently, making a kind of soft inarticulate noise, like that of some mother-animal caressing her young. He took no notice of Margaret’s presence. Once or twice she came up to kiss him; and he submitted to it, giving her a little push away when she had done, as if her affection disturbed him from his absorption in the dead. He started when he heard Frederick’s cries, and shook his head:—“Poor boy! poor boy!” he said, and then took no more notice. Margaret’s heart ached within her. She could not think of her own loss in thinking of her father’s case. The night was wearing away, and the day was at hand, when, without a word of preparation, Margaret’s voice broke upon the stillness of the room, with a clearness of sound that startled even herself: “Let not your heart be troubled,” it said; and she went steadily on through all that chapter of unspeakable consolation.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI\n\n“SHOULD AULD ACQUAINTANCE BE FORGOT.”\n\n“Show not that manner, and these features all, The serpent’s cunning, and the sinner’s fall?” CRABBE.\n\nThe chill, shivery October morning came; not the October morning of the country, with soft, silvery mists, clearing off before the sunbeams that bring out all the gorgeous beauty of colouring, but the October morning of Milton, whose silvery mists were heavy fogs, and where the sun could only show long dusky streets when he did break through and shine. Margaret went languidly about, assisting Dixon in her task of arranging the house. Her eyes were continually blinded by tears, but she had no time to give way to regular crying. The father and brother depended upon her; while they were giving way to grief, she must be working, planning, considering. Even the necessary arrangements for the funeral seemed to devolve upon her.\n\nWhen the fire was bright and crackling—when everything was ready for breakfast, and the tea-kettle was singing away, Margaret gave a last look round the room before going to summon Mr. Hale and Frederick. She wanted everything to look as cheerful as possible; and yet, when it did so, the contrast between it and her own thoughts forced her into sudden weeping. She was kneeling by the sofa, hiding her face in the cushions that no one might hear her cry, when she was touched on the shoulder by Dixon.\n\n“Come, Miss Hale—come, my dear! You must not give way, or where shall we all be? There is not another person in the house fit to give a direction of any kind, and there is so much to be done. There’s who’s to manage the funeral; and who’s to come to it; and where it’s to be; and all to be settled: and Master Frederick’s like one crazed with crying, and master never was a good one for settling; and, poor gentleman, he goes about now as if he was lost. It’s bad enough, my dear, I know; but death comes to us all; and you’re well off never to have lost any friend till now.”\n\nPerhaps so. But this seemed a loss by itself; not to bear comparison with any other event in the world. Margaret did not take any comfort from what Dixon said, but the unusual tenderness of the prim old servant’s manner touched her to the heart; and, more from a desire to show her gratitude for this than for any other reason, she roused herself up, and smiled in answer to Dixon’s anxious look at her; and went to tell her father and brother that breakfast was ready.\n\nMr. Hale came—as if in a dream, or rather with the unconscious motion of a sleep-walker, whose eyes and mind perceive other things than what are present. Frederick came briskly in, with a forced cheerfulness, grasped her hand, looked into her eyes and burst into tears. She had to try and think of little nothings to say all breakfast-time, in order to prevent the recurrence of her companions’ thoughts too strongly to the last meal they had taken together, when there had been a continual strained listening for some sound or signal from the sick-room.\n\nAfter breakfast, she resolved to speak to her father about the funeral. He shook his head, and assented to all she proposed, though many of her propositions absolutely contradicted one another. Margaret gained no real decision from him; and was leaving the room languidly, to have a consultation with Dixon, when Mr. Hale motioned her back to his side.\n\n“Ask Mr. Bell,” said he in a hollow voice.\n\n“Mr. Bell!” said she, a little surprised. “Mr. Bell of Oxford?”\n\n“Mr. Bell,” he repeated. “Yes. He was my groom’s-man.”\n\nMargaret understood the association.\n\n“I will write to-day,” said she. He sank again into listlessness. All morning she toiled on, longing for rest, but in a continual whirl of melancholy business.\n\nTowards evening Dixon said to her:\n\n“I’ve done it, miss. I was really afraid for master, that he’d have a stroke with grief. He’s been all this day with poor missus; and when I’ve listened at the door, I’ve heard him talking to her, and talking to her, as if she was alive. When I went in he would be quite quiet, but all in a maze like. So I thought to myself, he ought to be roused; and if it gives him a shock at first, it will, maybe, be the better for him afterwards. So I’ve been and told him, that I don’t think it’s safe for Master Frederick to be here. And I don’t. It was only on Tuesday, when I was out, that I met a Southampton man—the first I’ve seen since I came to Milton; they don’t make their way much up here, I think. Well, it was young Leonards, old Leonards the draper’s son, as great a scamp as ever lived—who plagued his father almost to death, and then ran off to sea. I never could abide him. He was in the Orion at the same time as Frederick, I know; though I don’t recollect if he was there at the mutiny.”\n\n“Did he know you?” said Margaret, eagerly.\n\n“Why, that’s the worst of it. I don’t believe he would have known me but for my being such a fool as to call out his name. He were a Southampton man, in a strange place, or else I should never have been so ready to call cousins with him, a nasty, good-for-nothing-fellow. Says he, ‘Miss Dixon! who would ha’ thought of seeing you here? But perhaps I mistake, and you’re Miss Dixon no longer?’ So I told him he might still address me as an unmarried lady, though if I hadn’t been so particular, I’d had good chances of matrimony. He was polite enough: ‘He couldn’t look at me and doubt me.’ But I were not to be caught with such chaff from such a fellow as him, and so I told him; and, by way of being even, I asked him after his father (who I knew had turned him out of doors), as if they were the best friends as ever was. So then, to spite me—for you see we were getting savage, for all we were so civil to each other—he began to inquire after Master Frederick, and said, what a scrape he’d got into (as if Master Frederick’s scrapes would ever wash George Leonards white, or make ’em look otherwise than nasty, dirty black), and how he’d be hung for mutiny if ever he were caught, and how a hundred pound reward had been offered for catching him, and what a disgrace he had been to his family—all to spite me, you see, my dear, because before now I’ve helped old Mr. Leonards to give George a good rating, down in Southampton. So I said, there were other families as I knew, who had far more cause to blush for their sons, and to be thankful if they could think they were earning an honest living far away from home. To which he made answer, like the impudent chap he is, that he were in a confidential situation, and if I knew of any young man who had been so unfortunate as to lead vicious courses, and wanted to turn steady, he’d have no objection to lend him his patronage. He, indeed! Why, he’d corrupt a saint. I’ve not felt so bad myself for years as when I were standing talking to him the other day. I could have cried to think I couldn’t spite him better, for he kept smiling in my face, as if he took all my compliments for earnest; and I couldn’t see that he minded what I said in the least, while I was mad with all his speeches.”\n\n“But you did not tell him anything about us—about Frederick?”\n\n“Not I,” said Dixon. “He had never the grace to ask where I was staying; and I shouldn’t have told him if he had asked. Nor did I ask him what his precious situation was. He was waiting for a bus, and just then it drove up, and he hailed it. But, to plague me to the last, he turned back before he got in, and said, ‘If you can help me to trap Lieutenant Hale, Miss Dixon, we’ll go partners in the reward. I know you’d like to be my partner, now wouldn’t you? Don’t be shy, but say yes.’ And he jumped on the bus, and I saw his ugly face leering at me with a wicked smile to think how he’d had the last word of plaguing.”\n\nMargaret was made very uncomfortable by this account of Dixon’s.\n\n“Have you told Frederick?” asked she.\n\n“No,” said Dixon. “I were uneasy in my mind at knowing that bad Leonards was in town; but there was so much else to think about that I did not dwell on it all. But when I saw master sitting so stiff, and with his eyes so glazed and sad, I thought it might rouse him to have to think of Master Frederick in hiding, he would have to go, poor fellow, before Mr. Bell came.”\n\n“Oh, I’m not afraid of Mr. Bell; but I am afraid of this Leonards. I must tell Frederick. What did Leonards look like?”\n\n“A bad-looking fellow, I can assure you, miss. Whiskers such as I should be ashamed to wear—they are so red. And for all he said he’d got a confidential situation, he was dressed in fustian just like a working-man.”\n\nIt was evident that Frederick must go. Go, too, when he had so completely vaulted into his place in the family, and promised to be such a stay and staff to his father and sister. Go, when his cares for the living mother, and sorrow for the dead, seemed to make him one of those peculiar people who are bound to us by a fellow-love for them that are taken away. Just as Margaret was thinking all this, sitting over the drawing-room fire—her father restless and uneasy under the pressure of this newly-aroused fear, of which he had not as yet spoken—Frederick came in, his brightness dimmed, but the extreme violence of his grief passed away. He came up to Margaret, and kissed her forehead.\n\n“How wan you look, Margaret!” said he in a low voice. “You have been thinking of everybody, and no one has thought of you. Lie on this sofa—there is nothing for you to do.”\n\n“That is the worst,” said Margaret, in a sad whisper. But she went and lay down, and her brother covered her feet with a shawl and then sate on the ground by her side; and the two began to talk together in a subdued tone.\n\nMargaret told him all that Dixon had related of her interview with young Leonards. Frederick’s lips closed with a long whew of dismay.\n\n“I should just like to have it out with that young fellow. A worse sailor was never on board ship—nor a much worse man either. I declare, Margaret—you know the circumstances of the whole affair?”\n\n“Yes, mamma told me.”\n\n“Well, when all the sailors who were good for anything were indignant with our captain, this fellow to curry favour—pah! And to think of his being here! Oh, if he’d a notion I was within twenty miles of him, he’d ferret me out to pay all old grudges. I’d rather anybody had the hundred pounds they think I am worth than that rascal. What a pity poor old Dixon could not be persuaded to give me up, and make a provision for her old age!”\n\n“Oh, Frederick, hush! Don’t talk so.”\n\nMr. Hale came towards them, eager and trembling. He had overheard what they were saying. He took Frederick’s hand in both of his:\n\n“My boy, you must go. It is very bad—but I see you must. You have done all you could—you have been a comfort to her.”\n\n“Oh, papa, must he go?” said Margaret, pleading against her own conviction of necessity.\n\n“I declare, I’ve a good mind to face it out, and stand my trial. If I could only pick up my evidence! I cannot endure the thought of being in the power of such a blackguard as Leonards. I could almost have enjoyed—in other circumstances—this stolen visit: it has had all the charm which the Frenchwoman attributed to forbidden pleasures.”\n\n“One of the earliest things I can remember,” said Margaret, “was your being in some great disgrace, Fred, for stealing apples. We had plenty of our own—trees loaded with them; but some one had told you that stolen fruit tasted sweetest, which you took au pied de la lettre, and off you went a-robbing. You have not changed your feelings much since then.”\n\n“Yes—you must go,” repeated Mr. Hale, answering Margaret’s question, which she had asked some time ago. His thoughts were fixed on one subject, and it was an effort to him to follow the zigzag remarks of his children—an effort which he did not make.\n\nMargaret and Frederick looked at each other. That quick momentary sympathy would be theirs no longer if he went away. So much was understood through eyes that could not be put into words. Both coursed the same thought till it was lost in sadness. Frederick shook it off first:\n\n“Do you know, Margaret, I was very nearly giving both Dixon and myself a good fright this afternoon. I was in my bedroom; I had heard a ring at the front door, but I thought the ringer must have done his business and gone away long ago; so I was on the point of making my appearance in the passage, when, as I opened my room door, I saw Dixon coming downstairs; and she frowned and kicked me into hiding again. I kept the door open, and heard a message given to some man that was in my father’s study, and that then went away. What could it have been? Some of the shopmen?”\n\n“Very likely,” said Margaret indifferently. “There was a little quiet man who came up for orders about two o’clock.”\n\n“But this was not a little man—a great powerful fellow; and it was past four when he was here.”\n\n“It was Mr. Thornton,” said Mr. Hale. They were glad to have drawn him into the conversation.\n\n“Mr. Thornton!” said Margaret, a little surprised. “I thought——”\n\n“Well, little one, what did you think?” asked Frederick, as she did not finish her sentence.\n\n“Oh, only,” said she, reddening and looking straight at him, “I fancied you meant some one of a different class, not a gentleman; somebody come on an errand.”\n\n“He looked like someone of that kind,” said Frederick, carelessly. “I took him for a shopman, and he turns out a manufacturer.”\n\nMargaret was silent. She remembered how at first, before she knew his character, she had spoken and thought of him just as Frederick was doing. It was but a natural impression that was made upon him, and yet she was a little annoyed by it. She was unwilling to speak; she wanted to make Frederick understand what kind of person Mr. Thornton was—but she was tongue-tied.\n\nMr. Hale went on. “He came to offer any assistance in his power, I believe. But I could not see him. I told Dixon to ask him if he would like to see you—I think I asked her to find you, and you would go to him. I don’t know what I said.”\n\n“He has been a very agreeable acquaintance, has he not?” asked Frederick, throwing the question like a ball for any one to catch who chose.\n\n“A very kind friend,” said Margaret, when her father did not answer.\n\nFrederick was silent for a time. At last he spoke:\n\n“Margaret, it is painful to think I can never thank those who have shown you kindness. Your acquaintances and mine must be separate. Unless, indeed, I run the chances of a court-martial, or unless you and my father would come to Spain.” He threw out this last suggestion as a kind of feeler; and then suddenly made the plunge. “You don’t know how I wish you would. I have a good position—the chance of a better,” continued he, reddening like a girl. “That Dolores Barbour that I was telling you of, Margaret—I only wish you knew her; I am sure you would like—no, love is the right word, like is so poor—you would love her, father, if you knew her. She is not eighteen; but if she is in the same mind another year, she is to be my wife. Mr. Barbour won’t let us call it an engagement. But if you would come you would find friends everywhere, besides Dolores. Think of it, father. Margaret be on my side.”\n\n“No—no more removals for me,” said Mr. Hale. “One has cost me my wife. No more removals in this life. She will be here; and here will I stay out my appointed time.”\n\n“Oh, Frederick,” said Margaret, “tell us more about her. I never thought of this; but I am so glad. You will have some one to love and care for you out there. Tell us all about it.”\n\n“In the first place, she is a Roman Catholic. That’s the only objection I anticipated. But my father’s change of opinion—nay, Margaret, don’t sigh.”\n\nMargaret had reason to sigh a little more before the conversation ended. Frederick himself was Roman Catholic in fact, though not in profession as yet. This was, then, the reason why his sympathy in her extreme distress at her father’s leaving the church had been so faintly expressed in his letters. She had thought it was the carelessness of a sailor; but the truth was, that even then he was himself inclined to give up the form of religion into which had been baptized, only that his opinions were tending in exactly the opposite direction to those of his father. How much love had to do with this change not even Frederick himself could have told. Margaret gave up talking about this branch of the subject at last; and, returning to the fact of the engagement, she began to consider it in some fresh light.\n\n“But for her sake, Fred, you surely will try and clear yourself of the exaggerated charges brought against you, even if the charge of mutiny itself be true. If there were to be a court-martial, and you could find your witnesses, you might, at any rate, show your disobedience to authority was because that authority was unworthily exercised.”\n\nMr. Hale roused himself up to listen to his son’s answer.\n\n“In the first place, Margaret, who is to hunt up my witnesses? All of them are sailors, drafted off to other ships, except those whose evidence would go for very little, as they took part or sympathised in the affair. In the next place allow me to tell you, you don’t know what a court-martial is, and consider it as an assembly where justice is administered, instead of what it really is—a court where authority weighs nine-tenths in the balance, and evidence forms only the other tenth. In such cases, evidence itself can hardly escape being influenced by the prestige of authority.”\n\n“But is it not worth trying, to see how much evidence might be discovered and arrayed on your behalf? At present, all those who knew you formerly, believe you guilty without any shadow of excuse. You have never tried to justify yourself, and we have never known where to seek for proofs of your justification. Now, for Miss Barbour’s sake, make your conduct as clear as you can in the eye of the world. She may not care for it; she has, I am sure, that trust in you that we all have; but you ought not to let her ally herself to one under such a serious charge, without showing the world exactly how it is you stand. You disobeyed authority—that was bad; but to have stood by, without word or act, while the authority was brutally used, would have been infinitely worse. People know what you did; but not the motives that elevate it out of a crime into an heroic protection of the weak. For Dolores’ sake, they ought to know.”\n\n“But how must I make them know? I am not sufficiently sure of the purity and justice of those who would be my judges, to give myself up to a court-martial, even if I could bring a whole array of truth-speaking witnesses. I can’t send a bell-man about, to cry aloud and proclaim in the streets what you are pleased to call my heroism. No one would read a pamphlet of self-justification so long after the deed, even if I put one out.”\n\n“Will you consult a lawyer as to your chances of exculpation?” asked Margaret, looking up, and turning very red.\n\n“I must first catch my lawyer, and have a look at him, and see how I like him, before I make him into my confidant. Many a briefless barrister might twist his conscience into thinking, that he could earn a hundred pounds very easily by doing a good action—in giving me, a criminal, up to justice.”\n\n“Nonsense, Frederick!—because I know a lawyer on whose honour I can rely; of whose cleverness in his profession people speak very highly; and who would, I think, take a good deal of trouble for any of—Aunt Shaw’s relations. Mr. Henry Lennox, papa.”\n\n“I think it is a good idea,” said Mr. Hale. “But don’t propose anything which will detain Frederick in England. Don’t, for your mother’s sake.”\n\n“You could go to London by a night-train,” continued Margaret, warming up into her plan. “He must go to-morrow, I’m afraid, papa,” said she, tenderly; “we fixed that, because of Mr. Bell, and Dixon’s disagreeable acquaintance.”\n\n“Yes; I must go to-morrow,” said Frederick, decidedly.\n\nMr. Hale groaned. “I can’t bear to part with you, and yet I am miserable with anxiety as long as you stop here.”\n\n“Well then,” said Margaret, “listen to my plan. He gets to London on Friday morning. I will—you might—no! it would be better to give him a note to Mr. Lennox. You will find him at his chambers in the Temple.”\n\n“I will write down a list of all the names I can remember on board the Orion. I could leave it with him to ferret them out. He is Edith’s husband’s brother, isn’t he? I remember your naming him in your letters. I have money in Barbour’s hands. I can pay a pretty long bill, if there’s any chance of success. Money, dear father, that I had meant for a different purpose; so I shall only consider it as borrowed from you and Margaret.”\n\n“Don’t do that,” said Margaret. “You won’t risk it if you do. And it will be as risk; only it is worth trying. You can sail from London as well as from Liverpool?”\n\n“To be sure, little goose. Wherever I feel water heaving under a plank, there I feel at home. I’ll pick up some craft or other to take me off, never fear. I won’t stay twenty-four hours in London, away from you on the one hand, and from somebody else on the other.”\n\nIt was rather a comfort to Margaret that Frederick took it into his head to look over her shoulder as she wrote to Mr. Lennox. If she had not been thus compelled to write steadily and concisely on, she might have hesitated over many a word, and been puzzled to choose between many an expression, in the awkwardness of being the first to resume the intercourse of which the concluding event had been so unpleasant to both sides. However, the note was taken from her before she had even had time to look it over, and treasured up in a pocket-book, out of which fell a long lock of black hair, the sight of which caused Frederick’s eyes to glow with pleasure.\n\n“Now you would like to see that, wouldn’t you?” said he. “No! you must wait till you see her herself. She is too perfect to be known by fragments. No mean brick shall be a specimen of the building of my palace.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\n“What! remain to be Denounced—dragged, it may be, in chains.” WERNER.\n\nAll the next day they sate together—they three. Mr. Hale hardly ever spoke but when his children asked him questions, and forced him, as it were, into the present. Frederick’s grief was no more to be seen or heard; the first paroxysm had passed over, and now he was ashamed of having been so battered down by emotion; and though his sorrow for the loss of his mother was a deep real feeling, and would last out his life, it was never to be spoken of again. Margaret, not so passionate at first, was more suffering now. At times she cried a good deal; and her manner, even when speaking on different things, had a mournful tenderness about it, which was deepened whenever her looks fell upon Frederick, and she thought of his rapidly approaching departure. She was glad he was going, on her father’s account, however much she might grieve over it on her own. The anxious terror in which Mr. Hale lived lest his son should be detected and captured, far outweighed the pleasure he derived from his presence. The nervousness had increased since Mrs. Hale’s death, probably because he dwelt upon it more exclusively. He started at every unusual sound, and was never comfortable unless Frederick sate out of the immediate view of any one entering the room. Towards evening he said:\n\n“You will go with Frederick to the station, Margaret? I shall want to know he is safely off. You will bring me word that he is clear of Milton, at any rate?”\n\n“Certainly,” said Margaret. “I shall like it, if you won’t be lonely without me, papa.”\n\n“No, no! I should always be fancying some one had known him, and that he had been stopped, unless you could tell me you had seen him off. And go to the Outwood station. It is quite as near, and not so many people about. Take a cab there. There is less risk of his being seen. What time is your train, Fred?”\n\n“Ten minutes past six; very nearly dark. So what will you do, Margaret?”\n\n“Oh, I can manage. I am getting very brave and very hard. It is a well-lighted road all the way home, if it should be dark. But I was out last week much later.”\n\nMargaret was thankful when the parting was over—the parting from the dead mother and the living father. She hurried Frederick into the cab, in order to shorten a scene which she saw was so bitterly painful to her father, who would accompany his son as he took his last look at his mother. Partly in consequence of this, and partly owing to one of the very common mistakes in the “Railway Guide” as to the times when trains arrive at the smaller stations, they found, on reaching Outwood, that they had nearly twenty minutes to spare. The booking-office was not open, so they could not even take the ticket. They accordingly went down the flight of steps that led to the level of the ground below the railway. There was a broad cinder-path diagonally crossing a field which lay alongside of the carriage-road, and they went there to walk backwards and forwards for the few minutes they had to spare.\n\nMargaret’s hand lay in Frederick’s arm. He took hold of it affectionately.\n\n“Margaret! I am going to consult Mr. Lennox as to the chance of exculpating myself so that I may return to England whenever I choose, more for your sake than for the sake of anyone else. I can’t bear to think of your lonely position if any thing should happen to my father. He looks sadly changed—terribly shaken. I wish you could get him to think of the Cadiz plan, for many reasons. What could you do if he were taken away? You have no friend near. We are curiously bare of relations.”\n\nMargaret could hardly keep from crying at the tender anxiety with which Frederick was bringing before her an event which she herself felt was not very improbable, so severely had the cares of the last few months told upon Mr. Hale. But she tried to rally as she said:\n\n“There have been such strange unexpected changes in my life during these last two years, that I feel more than ever that it is not worth while to calculate too closely what I should do if any future event took place. I try to think only upon the present.” She paused; they were standing still for a moment, close on the field side of the stile leading into the road; the setting sun fell on their faces. Frederick held her hand in his, and looked with wistful anxiety into her face, reading there more care and trouble than she would betray by words. She went on:\n\n“We shall write often to one another, and I will promise—for I see it will set your mind at ease—to tell you every worry I have. Papa is”—she started a little, a hardly visible start—but Frederick felt the sudden motion of the hand he held, and turned his full face to the road, along which a horseman was slowly riding, just passing the very stile where they stood. Margaret bowed; her bow was stiffly returned.\n\n“Who is that?” said Frederick, almost before he was out of hearing.\n\nMargaret was a little drooping, a little flushed, as she replied: “Mr. Thornton; you saw him before, you know.”\n\n“Only his back. He is an unprepossessing-looking fellow. What a scowl he has!”\n\n“Something has happened to vex him,” said Margaret apologetically. “You would not have thought him unprepossessing if you had seen him with mamma.”\n\n“I fancy it must be time to go and take my ticket. If I had known how dark it would be, we wouldn’t have sent back the cab, Margaret.”\n\n“Oh, don’t fidget about that. I can take a cab here, if I like; or go back by the rail-road, when I should have shops and people and lamps all the way from the Milton station-house. Don’t think of me; take care of yourself. I am sick with the thought that Leonards may be in the same train with you. Look well into the carriage before you get in.”\n\nThey went back to the station. Margaret insisted on going into the full light of the flaring gas inside to take the ticket. Some idle-looking young men were lounging about with the station-master. Margaret thought she had seen the face of one of them before, and returned him a proud look of offended dignity for his somewhat impertinent stare of undisguised admiration. She went hastily to her brother, who was standing outside, and took hold of his arm. “Have you got your bag? Let us walk about here on the platform,” said she, a little flurried at the idea of so soon being left alone, and her bravery oozing out rather faster than she liked to acknowledge even to herself. She heard a step following them along the flags; it stopped when they stopped, looking out along the line and hearing the whizz of the coming train. They did not speak; their hearts were too full. Another moment, and the train would be here; a minute more, and he would be gone. Margaret almost repented the urgency with which she had entreated him to go to London; it was throwing more chances of detection in his way. If he had sailed for Spain by Liverpool, he might have been off in two or three hours.\n\nFrederick turned round, right facing the lamp, where the gas darted up in vivid anticipation of the train. A man in the dress of a railway porter started forward; a bad-looking man, who seemed to have drunk himself into a state of brutality, although his senses were in perfect order.\n\n“By your leave, miss!” said he, pushing Margaret rudely on one side, and seizing Frederick by the collar.\n\n“Your name is Hale, I believe?”\n\nIn an instant, how, Margaret did not see, for everything danced before her eyes—but by some sleight of wrestling, Frederick had tripped him up, and he fell from the height of three or four feet, which the platform was elevated above the space of soft ground, by the side of the railroad. There he lay.\n\n“Run, run!” gasped Margaret. “The train is here. It was Leonards, was it? oh, run! I will carry your bag.” And she took him by the arm to push him along with all her feeble force. A door was opened in a carriage—he jumped in; and as he leant out to say, “God bless you, Margaret!” the train rushed past her; and she was left standing alone. She was so terribly sick and faint that she was thankful to be able to turn into the ladies’ waiting-room, and sit down for an instant. At first she could do nothing but gasp for breath. It was such a hurry; such a sickening alarm; such a near chance. If the train had not been there at the moment, the man would have jumped up again and called for assistance to arrest him. She wondered if the man had got up: she tried to remember if she had seen him move: she wondered if he could have been seriously hurt. She ventured out; the platform was all alight, but still quite deserted; she went to the end, and looked over, somewhat fearfully. No one was there; and then she was glad she had made herself go, and inspect, for otherwise terrible thoughts would have haunted her dreams. And even as it was, she was so trembling and affrighted that she felt she could not walk home along the road, which did indeed seem lonely and dark, as she gazed down upon it from the blaze of the station. She would wait till the down train passed and take her seat in it. But what if Leonards recognized her as Frederick’s companion! She peered about, before venturing into the booking-office to take her ticket. There were only some railway officials standing about; and talking loud to one another.\n\n“So Leonards has been drinking again!” said one, seemingly in authority. “He’ll need all his boasted influence to keep his place this time.”\n\n“Where is he?” asked another, while Margaret, her back towards them, was counting her change with trembling fingers, not daring to turn round until she heard the answer to this question.\n\n“I don’t know. He came in not five minutes ago, with some long story or other about a fall he’d had, swearing awfully: and wanted to borrow some money from me to go to London by the next up-train. He made all sorts of tipsy promises, but I’d something else to do than listen to him; I told him to go about his business; and he went off at the front door.”\n\n“He’s at the nearest vaults, I’ll be bound,” said the first speaker. “Your money would have gone there too, if you’d been such a fool as to lend it.”\n\n“Catch me! I knew better what his London meant. Why, he has never paid me off that five shillings”—and so they went on.\n\nAnd now all Margaret’s anxiety was for the train to come. She hid herself once more in the ladies’ waiting-room, and fancied every noise was Leonards’ step—every loud and boisterous voice was his. But no one came near her until the train drew up; when she was civilly helped into a carriage by a porter, into whose face she durst not look till they were in motion, and then she saw that it was not Leonards’.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\n“Sleep on, my love, in thy cold bed, Never to be disquieted! My last Good Night—thou wilt not wake Till I thy fate shall overtake.” DR. KING.\n\nHome seemed unnaturally quiet after all this terror and noisy commotion. Her father had seen all due preparation made for her refreshment on her return; and then sate down again in his accustomed chair, to fall into one of his sad waking dreams. Dixon had got Mary Higgins to scold and direct in the kitchen; and her scolding was not the less energetic because it was delivered in an angry whisper; for, speaking above her breath she would have thought irreverent, as long as there was any one lying dead in the house. Margaret had resolved not to mention the crowning and closing affright to her father. There was no use in speaking about it; it had ended well; the only thing to be feared was lest Leonards should in some way borrow money enough to effect his purpose of following Frederick to London, and hunting him out there. But there were immense chances against the success of any such plan; and Margaret determined not to torment herself by thinking of what she could do nothing to prevent. Frederick would be as much on his guard as she could put him; and in a day or two at most he would be safely out of England.\n\n“I suppose we shall hear from Mr. Bell to-morrow,” said Margaret.\n\n“Yes,” replied her father. “I suppose so.”\n\n“If he can come, he will be here to-morrow evening, I should think.”\n\n“If he cannot come, I shall ask Mr. Thornton to go with me to the funeral. I cannot go alone. I should break down utterly.”\n\n“Don’t ask Mr. Thornton, papa. Let me go with you,” said Margaret, impetuously.\n\n“You! My dear, women do not generally go.”\n\n“No; because they can’t control themselves. Women of our class don’t go, because they have no power over their emotions, and yet are ashamed of showing them. Poor women go, and don’t care if they are seen overwhelmed with grief. But I promise you, papa, that if you will let me go, I will be no trouble. Don’t have a stranger, and leave me out. Dear papa! if Mr. Bell cannot come, I shall go. I won’t urge my wish against your will, if he does.”\n\nMr. Bell could not come. He had the gout. It was a most affectionate letter, and expressed great and true regret for his inability to attend. He hoped to come and pay them a visit soon, if they would have him; his Milton property required some looking after, and his agent had written to him to say that his presence was absolutely necessary; or else he had avoided coming near Milton as long as he could, and now the only thing that would reconcile him to this necessary visit was the idea that he should see, and might possibly be able to comfort his old friend.\n\nMargaret had all the difficulty in the world to persuade her father not to invite Mr. Thornton. She had an indescribable repugnance to this step being taken. The night before the funeral, came a stately note from Mrs. Thornton to Miss Hale, saying that, at her son’s desire, their carriage should attend the funeral, if it would not be disagreeable to the family. Margaret tossed the note to her father.\n\n“Oh, don’t let us have these forms,” said she. “Let us go alone—you and me, papa. They don’t care for us, or else he would have offered to go himself, and not have proposed this sending an empty carriage.”\n\n“I thought you were so extremely averse to his going, Margaret,” said Mr. Hale in some surprise.\n\n“And so I am. I don’t want him to come at all; and I should especially dislike the idea of our asking him. But this seems such a mockery of mourning that I did not expect it from him.” She startled her father by bursting into tears. She had been so subdued in her grief, so thoughtful for others, so gentle and patient in all things, that he could not understand her impatient ways to-night; she seemed agitated and restless; and at all the tenderness which her father in his turn now lavished upon her, she only cried the more.\n\nShe passed so bad a night that she was ill prepared for the additional anxiety caused by a letter received from Frederick. Mr. Lennox was out of town; his clerk said that he would return by the following Tuesday at the latest; that he might possibly be home on Monday. Consequently, after some consideration, Frederick had determined upon remaining in London a day or two longer. He had thought of coming down to Milton again; the temptation had been very strong; but the idea of Mr. Bell domesticated in his father’s house, and the alarm he had received at the last moment at the railway station, had made him resolve to stay in London. Margaret might be assured he would take every precaution against being tracked by Leonards. Margaret was thankful that she received this letter while her father was absent in her mother’s room. If he had been present, he would have expected her to read it aloud to him, and it would have raised in him a state of nervous alarm which she would have found it impossible to soothe away. There was not merely the fact, which disturbed her excessively, of Frederick’s detention in London, but there were allusions to the recognition at the last moment at Milton, and the possibility of a pursuit, which made her blood run cold; and how then would it have affected her father? Many a time did Margaret repent of having suggested and urged on the plan of consulting Mr. Lennox. At the moment, it had seemed as if it would occasion so little delay—add so little to the apparently small chances of detection; and yet everything that had since occurred had tended to make it so undesirable. Margaret battled hard against this regret of hers for what could not now be helped; this self-reproach for having said what at the time appeared to be wise, but which after events were proving to have been so foolish. But her father was in too depressed a state of mind and body to struggle healthily; he would succumb to all these causes for morbid regret over what could not be recalled. Margaret summoned up all her forces to her aid. Her father seemed to have forgotten that they had any reason to expect a letter from Frederick that morning. He was absorbed in one idea—that the last visible token of the presence of his wife was to be carried away from him, and hidden from his sight. He trembled pitifully as the undertaker’s man was arranging his crape draperies around him. He looked wistfully at Margaret; and, when released, he tottered towards her, murmuring, “Pray for me, Margaret. I have no strength left in me. I cannot pray. I give her up because I must. I try to bear it: indeed I do. I know it is God’s will. But I cannot see why she died. Pray for me, Margaret, that I may have faith to pray. It is a great strait, my child.”\n\nMargaret sat by him in the coach, almost supporting him in her arms; and repeating all the noble verses of holy comfort, or texts expressive of faithful resignation, that she could remember. Her voice never faltered; and she herself gained strength by doing this. Her father’s lips moved after her, repeating the well-known texts as her words suggested them; it was terrible to see the patient struggling effort to obtain the resignation which he had not strength to take into his heart as part of himself.\n\nMargaret’s fortitude nearly gave way as Dixon, with a slight motion of her hand, directed her notice to Nicholas Higgins and his daughter, standing a little aloof, but deeply attentive to the ceremonial. Nicholas wore his usual fustian clothes, but had a bit of black stuff sown round his hat—a mark of mourning which he had never shown to his daughter Bessy’s memory. But Mr. Hale saw nothing. He went on repeating to himself, mechanically as it were, all the funeral service as it was read by the officiating clergyman; he sighed twice or thrice when all was ended; and then, putting his hand on Margaret’s arm, he mutely entreated to be led away, as if he were blind, and she his faithful guide.\n\nDixon sobbed aloud; she covered her face with her handkerchief, and was so absorbed in her own grief that she did not perceive that the crowd, attracted on such occasions, was dispersing, till she was spoken to by some one close at hand. It was Mr. Thornton. He had been present all the time, standing, with bent head, behind a group of people, so that, in fact, no one had recognised him.\n\n“I beg your pardon,—but, can you tell me how Mr. Hale is? And Miss Hale, too? I should like to know how they both are.”\n\n“Of course, sir. They are much as is to be expected. Master is terribly broke down. Miss Hale bears up better than likely.”\n\nMr. Thornton would rather have heard that she was suffering the natural sorrow. In the first place, there was selfishness enough in him to have taken pleasure in the idea that his great love might come in to comfort and console her; much the same kind of passionate pleasure which comes stinging through a mother’s heart, when her drooping infant nestles close to her, and is dependent upon her for everything. But this delicious vision of what might have been—in which, in spite of all Margaret’s repulse, he would have indulged only a few days ago—was miserably disturbed by the recollection of what he had seen near the Outwood station. “Miserably disturbed!” that is not strong enough. He was haunted by the remembrance of the handsome young man, with whom she stood in an attitude of such familiar confidence; and the remembrance shot through him like an agony, till it made him clench his hands tight in order to subdue the pain. At that late hour, so far from home! It took a great moral effort to galvanise his trust—erewhile so perfect—in Margaret’s pure and exquisite maidenliness into life; as soon as the effort ceased, his trust dropped down dead and powerless: and all sorts of wild fancies chased each other like dreams, through his mind. Here was a little bit of miserable, gnawing confirmation. “She bore up better than likely” under this grief. She had then some hope to look to, so bright that even in her affectionate nature it could come in to lighten the dark hours of a daughter newly made motherless. Yes! he knew how she would love. He had not loved her without gaining that instinctive knowledge of what capabilities were in her. Her soul would walk in glorious sunlight if any man was worthy, by his power of loving, to win back her love. Even in her mourning she would rest with a peaceful faith upon his sympathy. His sympathy! Whose? That other man’s. And that it was another was enough to make Mr. Thornton’s pale grave face grow doubly wan and stern at Dixon’s answer.\n\n“I suppose I may call,” said he coldly. “On Mr. Hale, I mean. He will perhaps admit me after to-morrow or so.”\n\nHe spoke as if the answer were a matter of indifference to him. But it was not so. For all his pain, he longed to see the author of it. Although he hated Margaret at times, when he thought of that gentle, familiar attitude and all the attendant circumstances, he had a restless desire to renew her picture in his mind—a longing for the very atmosphere she breathed. He was in the Charybdis of passion, and must perforce circle and circle ever nearer round the fatal centre.\n\n“I dare say, sir, master will see you. He was very sorry to have to deny you the other day; but circumstances was not agreeable just then.”\n\nFor some reason or other, Dixon never named this interview that she had had with Mr. Thornton to Margaret. It might have been mere chance, but so it was that Margaret never heard that he had attended her poor mother’s funeral.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV\n\n“Truth will fail thee never, never! Though thy bark be tempest-driven, Though each plank be rent and riven, Truth will bear thee on for ever!” ANON.\n\nThe “bearing up better than likely” was a terrible strain upon Margaret. Sometimes she thought she must give way, and cry out with pain, as the sudden sharp thought came across her, even during her apparently cheerful conversations with her father, that she had no longer a mother. About Frederick, too, there was great uneasiness. The Sunday post intervened, and interfered with their London letters; and on Tuesday Margaret was surprised and disheartened to find that there was still no letter. She was quite in the dark as to his plans, and her father was miserable at all this uncertainty. It broke in upon his lately acquired habit of sitting still in one easy chair for half a day together. He kept pacing up and down the room; then out of it; and she heard him upon the landing opening and shutting the bed-room doors, without any apparent object. She tried to tranquillise him by reading aloud; but it was evident he could not listen for long together. How thankful she was then, that she had kept to herself the additional cause for anxiety\n\nThornton announced. His visit would force her father’s thoughts into another channel.\n\nHe came up straight to her father, whose hands he took and wrung without a word—holding them in his for a minute or two, during which time his face, his eyes, his look, told of more sympathy than could be put into words. Then he turned to Margaret. Not “better than likely” did she look. Her stately beauty was dimmed with much watching and with many tears. The expression on her countenance was of gentle patient sadness—nay of positive present suffering. He had not meant to greet her otherwise than with his late studied coldness of demeanour; but he could not help going up to her, as she stood a little aside, rendered timid by the uncertainty of his manner of late, and saying the few necessary common-place words in so tender a voice, that her eyes filled with tears, and she turned away to hide her emotion. She took her work and sate down very quiet and silent. Mr. Thornton’s heart beat quick and strong, and for the time he utterly forgot the Outwood lane. He tried to talk to Mr. Hale: and—his presence always a certain kind of pleasure to Mr. Hale, as his power and decision made him, and his opinions, a safe, sure port—was unusually agreeable to her father, as Margaret saw.\n\nPresently Dixon came to the door and said, “Miss Hale, you are wanted.”\n\nDixon’s manner was so flurried that Margaret turned sick at heart. Something had happened to Fred. She had no doubt of that. It was well that her father and Mr. Thornton were so much occupied by their conversation.\n\n“What is it, Dixon?” asked Margaret, the moment she had shut the drawing-room door.\n\n“Come this way, miss,” said Dixon, opening the door of what had been Mrs. Hale’s bed-chamber, now Margaret’s, for her father refused to sleep there again after his wife’s death. “It’s nothing, miss,” said Dixon, choking a little. “Only a police-inspector. He wants to see you, miss. But I dare say, it’s about nothing at all.”\n\n“Did he name—” asked Margaret, almost inaudibly.\n\n“No, miss; he named nothing. He only asked if you lived here, and if he could speak to you. Martha went to the door, and let him in; she has shown him into master’s study. I went to him myself, to try if that would do; but no—it’s you, miss, he wants.”\n\nMargaret did not speak again till her hand was on the lock of the study door. Here she turned round and said, “Take care papa does not come down. Mr. Thornton is with him now.”\n\nThe inspector was almost daunted by the haughtiness of her manner as she entered. There was something of indignation expressed in her countenance, but so kept down and controlled, that it gave her a superb air of disdain. There was no surprise, no curiosity. She stood awaiting the opening of his business there. Not a question did she ask.\n\n“I beg your pardon, ma’am, but my duty obliges me to ask you a few plain questions. A man has died at the Infirmary, in consequence of a fall, received at Outwood station, between the hours of five and six on Thursday evening, the twenty-sixth instant. At the time, this fall did not seem of much consequence; but it was rendered fatal, the doctors say, by the presence of some internal complaint, and the man’s own habit of drinking.”\n\nThe large dark eyes, gazing straight into the inspector’s face, dilated a little. Otherwise there was no motion perceptible to his experienced observation. Her lips swelled out into a richer curve than ordinary, owing to the enforced tension of the muscles, but he did not know what was their usual appearance, so as to recognise the unwonted sullen defiance of the firm sweeping lines. She never blenched or trembled. She fixed him with her eye. Now—as he paused before going on, she said, almost as if she would encourage him in telling his tale—“Well—go on!”\n\n“It is supposed that an inquest will have to be held; there is some slight evidence to prove that the blow, or push, or scuffle that caused the fall, was provoked by this poor fellow’s half-tipsy impertinence to a young lady, walking with the man who pushed the deceased over the edge of the platform. This much was observed by some one on the platform, who, however, thought no more about the matter, as the blow seemed of slight consequence. There is also some reason to identify the lady with yourself; in which case—”\n\n“I was not there,” said Margaret, still keeping her expressionless eyes fixed on his face, with the unconscious look of a sleep-walker.\n\nThe inspector bowed but did not speak. The lady standing before him showed no emotion, no fluttering fear, no anxiety, no desire to end the interview. The information he had received was very vague; one of the porters, rushing out to be in readiness for the train, had seen a scuffle, at the other end of the platform, between Leonards and a gentleman accompanied by a lady, but heard no noise; and before the train had got to its full speed after starting, he had been almost knocked down by the headlong run of the enraged half-intoxicated Leonards, swearing and cursing awfully. He had not thought any more about it, till his evidence was routed out by the inspector, who, on making some farther inquiry at the railroad station, had heard from the station-master that a young lady and gentleman had been there about that hour—the lady remarkably handsome—and said, by some grocer’s assistant present at the time, to be a Miss Hale, living at Crampton, whose family dealt at his shop. There was no certainty that the one lady and gentleman were identical with the other pair, but there was great probability. Leonards himself had gone, half-mad with rage and pain, to the nearest gin-palace for comfort; and his tipsy words had not been attended to by the busy waiters there; they, however, remembered his starting up and cursing himself for not having sooner thought of the electric telegraph, for some purpose unknown; and they believed that he left with the idea of going there. On his way, overcome by pain or drink, he had lain down in the road, where the police had found him and taken him to the Infirmary: there he had never recovered sufficient consciousness to give any distinct account of his fall, although once or twice he had had glimmerings of sense sufficient to make the authorities send for the nearest magistrate, in hopes that he might be able to take down the dying man’s deposition of the cause of his death. But when the magistrate had come, he was rambling about being at sea, and mixing up names of captains and lieutenants in an indistinct manner with those of his fellow porters at the railway; and his last words were a curse on the “Cornish trick” which had, he said, made him a hundred pounds poorer than he ought to have been. The inspector ran all this over in his mind—the vagueness of the evidence to prove that Margaret had been at the station—the unflinching, calm denial which she gave to such a supposition. She stood awaiting his next word with a composure that appeared supreme.\n\n“Then, madam, I have your denial that you were the lady accompanying the gentleman who struck the blow, or gave the push, which caused the death of this poor man?”\n\nA quick, sharp pain went through Margaret’s brain. “Oh God! that I knew Frederick were safe!” A deep observer of human countenances might have seen the momentary agony shoot out of her great gloomy eyes, like the torture of some creature brought to bay. But the inspector though a very keen, was not a very deep observer. He was a little struck, notwithstanding, by the form of the answer, which sounded like a mechanical repetition of her first reply—not changed and modified in shape so as to meet his last question.\n\n“I was not there,” said she, slowly and heavily. And all this time she never closed her eyes, or ceased from that glassy, dream-like stare. His quick suspicions were aroused by this dull echo of her former denial. It was as if she had forced herself to one untruth, and had been stunned out of all power of varying it.\n\nHe put up his book of notes in a very deliberate manner. Then he looked up; she had not moved any more than if she had been some great Egyptian statue.\n\n“I hope you will not think me impertinent when I say, that I may have to call on you again. I may have to summon you to appear on the inquest, and prove an alibi, if my witnesses” (it was but one who had recognised her) “persist in deposing to your presence at the unfortunate event.” He looked at her sharply. She was still perfectly quiet—no change of colour, or darker shadow of guilt, on her proud face. He thought to have seen her wince: he did not know Margaret Hale. He was a little abashed by her real composure. It must have been a mistake of identity. He went on:\n\n“It is very unlikely, ma’am, that I shall have to do anything of the kind. I hope you will excuse me for doing what is only my duty, although it may appear impertinent.”\n\nMargaret bowed her head as he went towards the door. Her lips were stiff and dry. She could not speak even the common words of farewell. But suddenly she walked forwards, and opened the study door, and preceded him to the door of the house, which she threw wide open for his exit. She kept her eyes upon him in the same dull, fixed manner, until he was fairly out of the house. She shut the door, and went half-way into the study; then turned back, as if moved by some passionate impulse, and locked the door inside.\n\nThen she went into the study, paused—tottered forward—paused again—swayed for an instant where she stood, and fell prone on the floor in a dead swoon.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV\n\n“There’s nought so finely spun But it cometh to the sun.”\n\nMr. Thornton sate on and on. He felt that his company gave pleasure to Mr. Hale; and was touched by the half-spoken wishful entreaty that he would remain a little longer—the plaintive “Don’t go yet,” which his poor friend put forth from time to time. He wondered Margaret did not return; but it was with no view of seeing her that he lingered. For the hour—and in the presence of one who was so thoroughly feeling the nothingness of earth—he was reasonable and self-controlled. He was deeply interested in all her father said\n\n“Of death, and of the heavy lull, And of the brain that has grown dull.”\n\nIt was curious how the presence of Mr. Thornton had power over Mr. Hale to make him unlock the secret thoughts which he kept shut up even from Margaret. Whether it was that her sympathy would be so keen, and show itself in so lively a manner, that he was afraid of the reaction upon himself, or whether it was that to his speculative mind all kinds of doubts presented themselves at such a time, pleading and crying aloud to be resolved into certainties, and that he knew she would have shrunk from the expression of any such doubts—nay, from him himself as capable of conceiving them—whatever was the reason, he could unburden himself better to Mr. Thornton than to her of all the thoughts and fancies and fears that had been frost-bound in his brain till now. Mr. Thornton said very little; but every sentence he uttered added to Mr. Hale’s reliance and regard for him. Was it that he paused in the expression of some remembered agony, Mr. Thornton’s two or three words would complete the sentence, and show how deeply its meaning was entered into. Was it a doubt—a fear—a wandering uncertainty seeking rest, but finding none—so tear-blinded were its eyes—Mr. Thornton, instead of being shocked, seemed to have passed through that very stage of thought himself, and could suggest where the exact ray of light was to be found, which should make the dark places plain. Man of action as he was, busy in the world’s great battle, there was a deeper religion binding him to God in his heart, in spite of his strong wilfulness, through all his mistakes, than Mr. Hale had ever dreamed. They never spoke of such things again, as it happened; but this one conversation made them peculiar people to each other; knit them together, in a way which no loose indiscriminate talking about sacred things can ever accomplish. When all are admitted, how can there be a Holy of Holies?\n\nAnd all this while, Margaret lay as still and white as death on the study floor! She had sunk under her burden. It had been heavy in weight and long carried; and she had been very meek and patient, till all at once her faith had given way, and she had groped in vain for help! There was a pitiful contraction of suffering upon her beautiful brows, although there was no other sign of consciousness remaining. The mouth—a little while ago, so sullenly projected in defiance—was relaxed and livid.\n\n“E par che de la sua labbia si mova Uno spirto soave e pien d’amore, Chi va dicendo a l’anima: sospira!”\n\nThe first symptom of returning life was a quivering about the lips—a little mute soundless attempt at speech; but the eyes were still closed; and the quivering sank into stillness. Then, feebly leaning on her arms for an instant to steady herself, Margaret gathered herself up, and rose. Her comb had fallen out of her hair; and with an intuitive desire to efface the traces of weakness, and bring herself into order again, she sought for it, although from time to time, in the course of the search, she had to sit down and recover strength. Her head drooped forwards—her hands meekly laid one upon the other—she tried to recall the force of her temptation, by endeavouring to remember the details which had thrown her into such deadly fright; but she could not. She only understood two facts—that Frederick had been in danger of being pursued and detected in London, as not only guilty of manslaughter, but as the more unpardonable leader of the mutiny, and that she had lied to save him. There was one comfort; her lie had saved him, if only by gaining some additional time. If the inspector came again to-morrow, after she had received the letter she longed for to assure her of her brother’s safety, she would brave shame, and stand in her bitter penance—she the lofty Margaret—acknowledging before a crowded justice-room, if need were, that she had been as “a dog, and done this thing.” But if he came before she heard from Frederick; if he returned, as he had half threatened, in a few hours, why! she would tell that lie again; though how the words would come out, after all this terrible pause for reflection and self-reproach, without betraying her falsehood, she did not know, she could not tell. But her repetition of it would gain time—time for Frederick.\n\nShe was roused by Dixon’s entrance into the room; she had just been letting out Mr. Thornton.\n\nHe had hardly gone ten steps in the street, before a passing omnibus stopped close by him, and a man got down, and came up to him, touching his hat as he did so. It was the police-inspector.\n\nMr. Thornton had obtained for him his first situation in the police, and had heard from time to time of the progress of his protégé, but they had not often met, and at first Mr. Thornton did not remember him.\n\n“My name is Watson—George Watson, sir, that you got——”\n\n“Ah, yes! Why you are getting on famously, I hear.”\n\n“Yes, sir. I ought to thank you, sir. But it is on a little matter of business I made so bold as to speak to you now. I believe you were the magistrate who attended to take down the deposition of a poor man who died in the Infirmary last night.”\n\n“Yes,” replied Mr. Thornton. “I went and heard some kind of a rambling statement, which the clerk said was of no great use. I’m afraid he was but a drunken fellow, though there is no doubt he came to his death by violence at last. One of my mother’s servants was engaged to him, I believe, and she is in great distress to-day. What about him?”\n\n“Why, sir, his death is oddly mixed up with somebody in the house I saw you coming out of just now; it was a Mr. Hales’s I believe.”\n\n“Yes!” said Mr. Thornton, turning sharp round and looking into the inspector’s face with sudden interest. “What about it?”\n\n“Why, sir, it seems to me that I have got a pretty distinct chain of evidence, inculpating a gentleman who was walking with Miss Hale that night at the Outwood station, as the man who struck or pushed Leonards off the platform and so caused his death. But the young lady denies that she was there at the time.”\n\n“Miss Hale denies she was there!” repeated Mr. Thornton in an altered voice. “Tell me, what evening was it? What time?”\n\n“About six o’clock, on the evening of Thursday, the twenty-sixth.”\n\nThey walked on, side by side, in silence for a minute or two. The inspector was the first to speak.\n\n“You see, sir, there is like to be a coroner’s inquest; and I’ve got a young man who is pretty positive,—at least he was at first;—since he has heard of the young lady’s denial, he says he should not like to swear; but still he’s pretty positive that he saw Miss Hale at the station, walking about with a gentleman, not five minutes before the time, when one of the porters saw a scuffle, which he set down to some of Leonards’ impudence—but which led to the fall which caused his death. And seeing you come out of the very house, sir, I thought I might make bold to ask if—you see, it’s always awkward having to do with cases of disputed identity, and one doesn’t like to doubt the word of a respectable young woman unless one has strong proof to the contrary.”\n\n“And she denied having been at the station that evening!” repeated Mr. Thornton, in a low, brooding tone.\n\n“Yes, sir, twice over, as distinct as could be. I told her I should call again, but seeing you just as I was on my way back from questioning the young man who said it was her, I thought I would ask your advice, both as the magistrate who saw Leonards on his death-bed, and as the gentleman who got me my berth in the force.”\n\n“You were quite right,” said Mr. Thornton. “Don’t take any steps till you have seen me again.”\n\n“The young lady will expect me to call, from what I said.”\n\n“I only want to delay you an hour. It’s now three. Come to my warehouse at four.”\n\n“Very well, sir!”\n\nAnd they parted company. Mr. Thornton hurried to his warehouse, and, sternly forbidding his clerks to allow any one to interrupt him, he went his way to his own private room, and locked the door. Then he indulged himself in the torture of thinking it all over, and realising every detail. How could he have lulled himself into the unsuspicious calm in which her tearful image had mirrored itself not two hours before, till he had weakly pitied her and yearned towards her, and forgotten the savage, distrustful jealousy with which the sight of her—and that unknown to him—at such an hour—in such a place—had inspired him! How could one so pure have stooped from her decorous and noble manner of bearing! But was it decorous—was it? He hated himself for the idea that forced itself upon him, just for an instant—no more—and yet, while it was present, thrilled him with its old potency of attraction towards her image. And then this falsehood—how terrible must be some dread of shame to be revealed—for, after all, the provocation given by such a man as Leonards was, when excited by drinking, might, in all probability, be more than enough to justify any one who came forward to state the circumstances openly and without reserve! How creeping and deadly that fear which could bow down the truthful Margaret to falsehood! He could almost pity her. What would be the end of it? She could not have considered all she was entering upon; if there was an inquest and the young man came forward. Suddenly he started up. There should be no inquest. He would save Margaret. He would take the responsibility of preventing the inquest, the issue of which, from the uncertainty of the medical testimony (which he had vaguely heard the night before, from the surgeon in attendance), could be but doubtful; the doctors had discovered an internal disease far advanced, and sure to prove fatal; they had stated that death might have been accelerated by the fall, or by the subsequent drinking and exposure to cold. If he had but known how Margaret would have become involved in the affair—if he had but foreseen that he would have stained her whiteness by a falsehood, he could have saved her by a word; for the question, of inquest or no inquest, had hung trembling in the balance only the night before. Miss Hale might love another—was indifferent and contemptuous to him—but he would yet do her faithful acts of service of which she should never know. He might despise her, but the woman whom he had once loved should be kept from shame; and shame it would be to pledge herself to a lie in a public court, or otherwise to stand and acknowledge her reason for desiring darkness rather than light.\n\nVery gray and stern did Mr. Thornton look, as he passed out through his wondering clerks. He was away about half an hour; and scarcely less stern did he look when he returned, although his errand had been successful.\n\nHe wrote two lines on a slip of paper, put it in an envelope, and sealed it up. This he gave to one of the clerks, saying:—\n\n“I appointed Watson—he who was a packer in the warehouse, and who went into the police—to call on me at four o’clock. I have just met a gentleman from Liverpool who wishes to see me before he leaves town. Take care to give this note to Watson when he calls.”\n\nThe note contained these words:\n\n“There will be no inquest. Medical evidence not sufficient to justify it. Take no further steps. I have not seen the coroner; but I will take the responsibility.”\n\n“Well,” thought Watson, “it relieves me from an awkward job. None of my witnesses seemed certain of anything except the young woman. She was clear and distinct enough; the porter at the railroad had seen a scuffle! or when he found it was likely to bring him in as a witness, then it might not have been a scuffle, only a little larking, and Leonards might have jumped off the platform himself;—he would not stick firm to anything. And Jennings, the grocer’s shopman,—well, he was not quite so bad, but I doubt if I could have got him up to an oath after he heard that Miss Hale flatly denied it. It would have been a troublesome job and no satisfaction. And now I must go and tell them they won’t be wanted.”\n\nHe accordingly presented himself again at Mr. Hale’s that evening. Her father and Dixon would fain have persuaded Margaret to go to bed; but they, neither of them, knew the reason for her low continued refusals to do so. Dixon had learnt part of the truth—but only part. Margaret would not tell any human being of what she had said, and she did not reveal the fatal termination to Leonards’ fall from the platform. So Dixon’s curiosity combined with her allegiance to urge Margaret to go to rest, which her appearance, as she lay on the sofa, showed but too clearly that she required. She did not speak except when spoken to; she tried to smile back in reply to her father’s anxious looks and words of tender inquiry; but, instead of a smile, the wan lips resolved themselves into a sigh. He was so miserably uneasy that, at last, she consented to go into her own room, and prepare for going to bed. She was indeed inclined to give up the idea that the inspector would call again that night, as it was already past nine o’clock.\n\nShe stood by her father, holding on to the back of his chair.\n\n“You will go to bed soon, papa, won’t you? Don’t sit up alone!”\n\nWhat his answer was she did not hear; the words were lost in the far smaller sound that magnified itself to her fears, and filled her brain. There was a low ring at the door-bell.\n\nShe kissed her father and glided down stairs, with a rapidity of motion of which no one would have thought her capable, who had seen her the minute before. She put aside Dixon.\n\n“Don’t come; I will open the door. I know it is him—I can—I must manage it all myself.”\n\n“As you please, miss!” said Dixon, testily; but in a moment afterwards, she added, “But you’re not fit for it. You are more dead than alive.”\n\n“Am I?” said Margaret, turning round and showing her eyes all aglow with strange fire, her cheeks flushed, though her lips were baked and livid still.\n\nShe opened the door to the Inspector, and preceded him into the study. She placed the candle on the table, and snuffed it carefully, before she turned round and faced him.\n\n“You are late!” said she. “Well?” She held her breath for the answer.\n\n“I am sorry to have given any unnecessary trouble, ma’am; for, after all, they’ve given up all thoughts of holding an inquest. I have had other work to do and other people to see, or I should have been here before now.”\n\n“Then it is ended,” said Margaret. “There is to be no further enquiry.”\n\n“I believe I’ve got Mr. Thornton’s note about me,” said the Inspector, fumbling in his pocket-book.\n\n“Mr. Thornton’s!” said Margaret.\n\n“Yes! he’s a magistrate—ah! here it is.”\n\nShe could not see to read it—no, not although she was close to the candle. The words swam before her. But she held it in her hand, and looked at it as if she were intently studying it.\n\n“I’m sure, ma’am, it’s a great weight off my mind; for the evidence was so uncertain, you see, that the man had received any blow at all, and if any question of identity came in, it so complicated the case, as I told Mr. Thornton——”\n\n“Mr. Thornton!” said Margaret again.\n\n“I met him this morning, just as he was coming out of this house, and, as he’s an old friend of mine, besides being the magistrate who saw Leonards last night, I made bold to tell him of my difficulty.”\n\nMargaret sighed deeply. She did not want to hear any more; she was afraid alike of what she had heard, and of what she might hear. She wished that the man would go. She forced herself to speak.\n\n“Thank you for calling. It is very late. I dare say it is past ten o’clock. Oh! here is the note!” she continued, suddenly interpreting the meaning of the hand held out to receive it. He was putting it up, when she said, “I think it is a cramped, dazzling sort of writing. I could not read it; will you just read it to me?”\n\nHe read it aloud to her.\n\n“Thank you. You told Mr. Thornton that I was not there?”\n\n“Oh, of course, ma’am. I’m sorry now that I acted upon information, which seems to have been so erroneous. At first the young man was so positive; and now he says that he doubted all along, and hopes that his mistake won’t have occasioned you such annoyance as to lose their shop your custom. Good night, ma’am.”\n\n“Good night.” She rang the bell for Dixon to show him out. As Dixon returned up the passage Margaret passed her swiftly.\n\n“It is all right!” said she, without looking at Dixon; and before the woman could follow her with further questions she had sped upstairs, and entered her bed-chamber, and bolted her door.\n\nShe threw herself, dressed as she was, upon her bed. She was too much exhausted to think. Half-an-hour or more elapsed before the cramped nature of her position, and the chilliness, supervening upon great fatigue, had the power to rouse her numbed faculties. Then she began to recall, to combine, to wonder. The first idea that presented itself to her was, that all this sickening alarm on Frederick’s behalf was over; that the strain was past. The next was a wish to remember every word of the Inspector’s which related to Mr. Thornton. When had he seen him? What had he said? What had Mr. Thornton done? What were the exact words of his note? And until she could recollect, even to the placing or omitting an article, the very expressions which he had used in the note, her mind refused to go on with its progress. But the next conviction she came to was clear enough;—Mr. Thornton had seen her close to Outwood station on the fatal Thursday night, and had been told of her denial that she was there. She stood a liar in his eyes. She was a liar. But she had no thought of penitence before God; nothing but chaos and night surrounded the one lurid fact that, in Mr. Thornton’s eyes, she was degraded. She cared not to think, even to herself, of how much of excuse she might plead. That had nothing to do with Mr. Thornton; she never dreamed that he, or any one else, could find cause for suspicion in what was so natural as her accompanying her brother; but what was really false and wrong was known to him, and he had a right to judge her. “Oh, Frederick! Frederick!” she cried, “what have I not sacrificed for you!” Even when she fell asleep her thoughts were compelled to travel the same circle, only with exaggerated and monstrous circumstances of pain.\n\nWhen she awoke a new idea flashed upon her with all the brightness of the morning. Mr. Thornton had learnt her falsehood before he went to the coroner; that suggested the thought, that he had possibly been influenced so to do with a view of sparing her the repetition of her denial. But she pushed this notion on one side with the sick wilfulness of a child. If it were so, she felt no gratitude to him, as it only showed her how keenly he must have seen that she was disgraced already, before he took such unwonted pains to spare her any further trial of truthfulness, which had already failed so signally. She would have gone through the whole—she would have perjured herself to save Frederick, rather—far rather—than Mr. Thornton should have had the knowledge that prompted him to interfere to save her. What ill-fate brought him in contact with the Inspector? What made him be the very magistrate sent for to receive Leonards’ deposition? What had Leonards said? How much of it was intelligible to Mr. Thornton, who might already, for aught she knew, be aware of the old accusation against Frederick, through their mutual friend, Mr. Bell? If so, he had striven to save the son, who came in defiance of the law to attend his mother’s death-bed. And under this idea she could feel grateful—not yet, if ever she should, if his interference had been prompted by contempt. Oh! had any one just cause to feel contempt for her? Mr. Thornton, above all people, on whom she had looked down from her imaginary heights till now! She suddenly found herself at his feet, and was strangely distressed at her fall. She shrank from following out the premises to their conclusion, and so acknowledging to herself how much she valued his respect and good opinion. Whenever this idea presented itself to her at the end of a long avenue of thoughts, she turned away from following that path—she would not believe in it.\n\nIt was later than she fancied, for in the agitation of the previous night, she had forgotten to wind up her watch; and Mr. Hale had given especial orders that she was not to be disturbed by the usual awakening. By and by the door opened cautiously, and Dixon put her head in. Perceiving that Margaret was awake, she came forwards with a letter.\n\n“Here’s something to do you good, miss. A letter from Master Frederick.”\n\n“Thank you, Dixon. How late it is!”\n\nShe spoke very languidly, and suffered Dixon to lay it on the counterpane before her, without putting out a hand to take it.\n\n“You want your breakfast, I’m sure. I will bring it you in a minute. Master has got the tray all ready, I know.”\n\nMargaret did not reply; she let her go; she felt that she must be alone before she could open that letter. She opened it at last. The first thing that caught her eye was the date two days earlier than she received it. He had then written when he had promised, and their alarm might have been spared. But she would read the letter and see. It was hasty enough, but perfectly satisfactory. He had seen Henry Lennox, who knew enough of the case to shake his head over it, in the first instance, and tell him he had done a very daring thing in returning to England, with such an accusation, backed by such powerful influence, hanging over him. But when they had come to talk it over, Mr. Lennox had acknowledged that there might be some chance of his acquittal, if he could but prove his statements by credible witnesses—that in such case it might be worth while to stand his trial, otherwise it would be a great risk. He would examine—he would take every pains. “It struck me,” said Frederick, “that your introduction, little sister of mine, went a long way. Is it so? He made so many inquiries, I can assure you. He seemed a sharp, intelligent fellow, and in good practice too, to judge from the signs of business and the number of clerks about him. But these may be only lawyer’s dodges. I have just caught a packet on the point of sailing—I am off in five minutes. I may have to come back to England again on this business, so keep my visit secret. I shall send my father some rare old sherry, such as you cannot buy in England (such stuff as I’ve got in the bottle before me)! He needs something of the kind—my dear love to him—God bless him. I’m sure—here’s my cab. P.S.—What an escape that was! Take care you don’t breathe of my having been—not even to the Shaws.”\n\nMargaret turned to the envelope: it was marked “Too late.” The letter had probably been trusted to some careless waiter, who had forgotten to post it. Oh! what slight cobwebs of chances stand between us and Temptation! Frederick had been safe, and out of England twenty, nay, thirty hours ago; and it was only about seventeen hours since she had told a falsehood to baffle pursuit, which even then would have been vain. How faithless she had been! Where now was her proud motto, “Fais ce que dois, advienne que pourra?” If she had but dared to bravely tell the truth as regarded herself, defying them to find out what she refused to tell concerning another, how light of heart she would now have felt! Not humbled before God, as having failed in trust towards Him; not degraded and abased in Mr. Thornton’s sight. She caught herself up at this with a miserable tremor; here was she classing his low opinion of her alongside with the displeasure of God. How was it that he haunted her imagination so persistently? What could it be? Why did she care for what he thought, in spite of all her pride; in spite of herself? She believed that she could have borne the sense of Almighty displeasure, because He knew all, and could read her penitence, and hear her cries for help in time to come. But Mr. Thornton—why did she tremble, and hide her face in the pillow? What strong feeling had overtaken her at last?\n\nShe sprang out of bed and prayed long and earnestly. It soothed and comforted her so to open her heart. But as soon as she reviewed her position she found the sting was still there; that she was not good enough, nor pure enough to be indifferent to the lowered opinion of a fellow creature: that the thought of how he must be looking upon her with contempt, stood between her and her sense of wrong-doing. She took her letter in to her father as soon as she was drest. There was so slight an allusion to their alarm at the railroad station, that Mr. Hale passed over it without paying any attention to it. Indeed, beyond the mere fact of Frederick having sailed undiscovered and unsuspected, he did not gather much from the letter at the time, he was so uneasy about Margaret’s pallid looks. She seemed continually on the point of weeping.\n\n“You are sadly overdone, Margaret. It is no wonder. But you must let me nurse you now.”\n\nHe made her lie down on the sofa, and went for a shawl to cover her with. His tenderness released her tears; and she cried bitterly.\n\n“Poor child!—poor child!” said he, looking fondly at her, as she lay with her face to the wall, shaking with her sobs. After a while they ceased, and she began to wonder whether she durst give herself the relief of telling her father of all her trouble. But there were more reasons against it than for it. The only one for it was the relief to herself; and against it was the thought that it would add materially to her father’s nervousness, if it were indeed necessary for Frederick to come to England again; that he would dwell on the circumstance of his son’s having caused the death of a man, however unwittingly and unwillingly; that this knowledge would perpetually recur to trouble him, in various shapes of exaggeration and distortion from the simple truth. And about her own great fault—he would be distressed beyond measure at her want of courage and faith, yet perpetually troubled to make excuses for her. Formerly Margaret would have come to him as priest as well as father, to tell him of her temptation and her sin; but latterly they had not spoken much on such subjects; and she knew not how, in his change of opinions, he would reply if the depth of her soul called unto his. No; she would keep her secret, and bear the burden alone. Alone she would go before God, and cry for His absolution. Alone she would endure her disgraced position in the opinion of Mr. Thornton. She was unspeakably touched by the tender efforts of her father to think of cheerful subjects on which to talk, and so to take her thoughts away from dwelling on all that had happened of late. It was some months since he had been so talkative as he was this day. He would not let her sit up, and offended Dixon desperately by insisting on waiting upon her himself.\n\nAt last she smiled; a poor, weak little smile; but it gave him the truest pleasure.\n\n“It seems strange to think, that what gives us most hope for the future should be called Dolores,” said Margaret. The remark was more in character with her father than with her usual self; but to-day they seemed to have changed natures.\n\n“Her mother was a Spaniard, I believe: that accounts for her religion. Her father was a stiff Presbyterian when I knew him. But it is a very soft and pretty name.”\n\n“How young she is!—younger by fourteen months than I am. Just the age that Edith was when she was engaged to Captain Lennox. Papa, we will go and see them in Spain.”\n\nHe shook his head. But he said, “If you wish it Margaret. Only let us come back here. It would seem unfair—unkind to your mother, who always, I’m afraid, disliked Milton so much, if we left it now she is lying here, and cannot go with us. No, dear; you shall go and see them, and bring me back a report of my Spanish daughter.”\n\n“No, papa, I won’t go without you. Who is to take care of you when I am gone?”\n\n“I should like to know which of us is taking care of the other. But if you went, I should persuade Mr. Thornton to let me give him double lessons. We would work up the classics famously. That would be a perpetual interest. You might go on, and see Edith at Corfu, if you liked.”\n\nMargaret did not speak all at once. Then she said rather gravely: “Thank you, papa. But I don’t want to go. We will hope that Mr. Lennox will manage so well, that Frederick may bring Dolores to see us when they are married. And as for Edith, the regiment won’t remain much longer in Corfu. Perhaps we shall see both of them here before another year is out.”\n\nMr. Hale’s cheerful subjects had come to an end. Some painful recollections had stolen across his mind, and driven him into silence. By and by Margaret said:\n\n“Papa—did you see Nicholas Higgins at the funeral? He was there, and Mary too. Poor fellow! it was his way of showing sympathy. He has a good warm heart under his bluff abrupt ways.”\n\n“I am sure of it,” replied Mr. Hale. “I saw it all along, even while you tried to persuade me that he was all sorts of bad things. We will go and see them to-morrow, if you are strong enough to walk so far.”\n\n“Oh yes. I want to see them. We did not pay Mary—or rather she refused to take it, Dixon says. We will go so as to catch him just after his dinner, and before he goes to his work.”\n\nTowards evening Mr. Hale said:\n\n“I half expected Mr. Thornton would have called. He spoke of a book yesterday which he had, and which I wanted to see. He said he would try and bring it to-day.”\n\nMargaret sighed. She knew he would not come. He would be too delicate to run the chance of meeting her, while her shame must be so fresh in his memory. The very mention of his name renewed her trouble, and produced a relapse into the feeling of depressed, pre-occupied exhaustion. She gave way to listless languor. Suddenly it struck her that this was a strange manner to show her patience, or to reward her father for his watchful care of her all through the day. She sate up, and offered to read aloud. His eyes were failing, and he gladly accepted her proposal. She read well: she gave the due emphasis; but had any one asked her, when she had ended, the meaning of what she had been reading, she could not have told. She was smitten with a feeling of ingratitude to Mr. Thornton, inasmuch as, in the morning, she had refused to accept the kindness he had shown her in making further inquiry from the medical men, so as to obviate any inquest being held. Oh! she was grateful! She had been cowardly and false, and had shown her cowardliness and falsehood in action that could not be recalled; but she was not ungrateful. It sent a glow to her heart, to know she could feel towards one who had reason to despise her. His cause for contempt was so just, that she should have respected him less if she had thought he did not feel contempt. It was a pleasure to feel how thoroughly she respected him. He could not prevent her doing that; it was the one comfort in all this misery.\n\nLate in the evening, the expected book arrived, “with Mr. Thornton’s kind regards, and wishes to know how Mr. Hale is.”\n\n“Say that I am much better, Dixon, but that Miss Hale—”\n\n“No, papa,” said Margaret, eagerly—“don’t say anything about me. He does not ask.”\n\n“My dear child, how you are shivering!” said her father, a few minutes afterwards. “You must go to bed directly. You have turned quite pale!”\n\nMargaret did not refuse to go, though she was loth to leave her father alone. She needed the relief of solitude after a day of busy thinking, and busier repenting.\n\nBut she seemed much as usual the next day; the lingering gravity and sadness, and the occasional absence of mind, were not unnatural symptoms in the early days of grief. And almost in proportion to her re-establishment in health, was her father’s relapse into his abstracted musings upon the wife he had lost, and the past era in his life that was closed to him for ever.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI\n\n“The steps of the bearers, heavy and slow, The sobs of the mourners, deep and low.” SHELLEY.\n\nAt the time arranged the previous day, they set out on their walk to see Nicholas Higgins and his daughter. They both were reminded of their recent loss, by a strange kind of shyness in their new habiliments, and in the fact that it was the first time, for many weeks, that they had deliberately gone out together. They drew very close to each other in unspoken sympathy.\n\nNicholas was sitting by the fire-side in his accustomed corner; but he had not his accustomed pipe. He was leaning his head upon his hand, his arm resting on his knee. He did not get up when he saw them, though Margaret could read the welcome in his eye.\n\n“Sit ye down, sit ye down. Fire’s welly out,” said he, giving it a vigorous poke, as if to turn attention away from himself. He was rather disorderly, to be sure, with a black unshaven beard of several days’ growth, making his pale face look yet paler, and a jacket which would have been all the better for patching.\n\n“We thought we should have a good chance of finding you, just after dinner-time,” said Margaret.\n\n“We have had our sorrows too, since we saw you,” said Mr. Hale.\n\n“Ay, ay. Sorrows is more plentiful than dinners just now; I reckon, my dinner hour stretches all o’er the day; yo’re pretty sure of finding me.”\n\n“Are you out of work?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Ay,” he replied shortly. Then, after a moment’s silence, he added, looking up for the first time: “I’m not wanting brass. Dunno yo’ think it. Bess, poor lass, had a little stock under her pillow, ready to slip into my hand, last moment, and Mary is fustian-cutting. But I’m out of work a’ the same.”\n\n“We owe Mary some money,” said Mr. Hale, before Margaret’s sharp pressure on his arm could arrest the words.\n\n“If hoo takes it, I’ll turn her out o’ doors. I’ll bide inside these four walls, and she’ll bide out. That’s a’.”\n\n“But we owe her many thanks for her kind service,” began Mr. Hale again.\n\n“I ne’er thanken your daughter theer for her deeds o’ love to my poor wench. I ne’er could find th’ words. I’se have to begin to try now, if yo’ start making an ado about what little Mary could sarve yo’.”\n\n“Is it because of the strike you’re out of work?” asked Margaret gently.\n\n“Strike’s ended. It’s o’er for this time. I’m out o’ work because I ne’er asked for it. And I ne’er asked for it, because good words is scarce, and bad words is plentiful.”\n\nHe was in a mood to take a surly pleasure in giving answers that were like riddles. But Margaret saw that he would like to be asked for the explanation.\n\n“And good words are—?”\n\n“Asking for work. I reckon them’s almost the best words that men can say. ‘Gi’ me work’ means ‘and I’ll do it like a man.’ Them’s good words.”\n\n“And bad words are refusing you work when you ask for it.”\n\n“Ay. Bad words is saying ‘Aha, my fine chap! Yo’ve been true to yo’r order, and I’ll be true to mine. Yo’ did the best yo’ could for them as wanted help; that’s yo’re way of being true to yo’re kind: and I’ll be true to mine. Yo’ve been a poor fool, as knowed no better nor be a true faithful fool. So go and be d—d to yo’. There’s no work for yo’ here.’ Them’s bad words. I’m not a fool; and if I was, folk ought to ha’ taught me how to be wise after their fashion. I could m’appen ha’ learnt, if any one had tried to teach me.”\n\n“Would it not be worth while,” said Mr. Hale, “to ask your old master if he would take you back again? It might be a poor chance, but it would be a chance.”\n\nHe looked up again, with a sharp glance at the questioner; and then tittered a low and bitter laugh.\n\n“Measter! if it’s no offence, I’ll ask yo’ a question or two in my turn.”\n\n“You’re quite welcome,” said Mr. Hale.\n\n“I reckon yo’n some way of earning your bread. Folk seldom lives i’ Milton just for pleasure, if they can live anywhere else.”\n\n“You are quite right. I have some independent property, but my intention in settling in Milton was to become a private tutor.”\n\n“To teach folk. Well! I reckon they pay yo’ for teaching them, dunnot they?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Mr. Hale, smiling. “I teach in order to get paid.”\n\n“And them that pays yo’, dun they tell yo’ whatten to do, or whatten not to do wi’ the money they gives you in just payment for your pains—in fair exchange like?”\n\n“No; to be sure not!”\n\n“They dunnot say, ‘Yo’ may have a brother, or a friend as dear as a brother, who wants this here brass for a purpose both yo’ and he think right; but yo’ mun promise not give it to him. Yo’ may see a good use, as yo’ think, to put yo’r money to; but we don’t think it good, and so if yo’ spend it a-thatens we’ll just leave off dealing with yo’.’ They dunnot say that, dun they?”\n\n“No: to be sure not!”\n\n“Would yo’ stand it if they did?”\n\n“It would be some very hard pressure that would make me even think of submitting to such dictation.”\n\n“There’s not the pressure on all the broad earth that would make me,” said Nicholas Higgins. “Now yo’ve got it. Yo’ve hit the bull’s eye. Hampers—that’s where I worked—makes their men pledge ’emselves they’ll not give a penny to help th’ Union or keep turn-outs fro’ clemming. They may pledge and make pledge,” continued he, scornfully; “they nobbut make liars and hypocrites. And that’s a less sin, to my mind, to make men’s hearts so hard that they’ll not do a kindness to them as needs it, or help on the right and just cause, though it goes again the strong hand. But I’ll ne’er forswear mysel’ for a’ the work the king could gi’e me. I’m a member o’ the Union; and I think it’s the only thing to do the workmen any good. And I’ve been a turn-out, and known what it was to clem; so if I get a shilling, sixpence shall go to them if they axe it from me. Consequence is, I dunnot see where I’m to get a shilling.”\n\n“Is that rule about not contributing to the Union in force at all the mills?” asked Margaret.\n\n“I cannot say. It’s a new regulation at ours; and I reckon they’ll find that they cannot stick to it. But it’s in force now. By-and-by they’ll find out, tyrants makes liars.”\n\nThere was a little pause. Margaret was hesitating whether she should say what was in her mind; she was unwilling to irritate one who was always gloomy and despondent enough. At last out it came. But in her soft tones, and with her reluctant manner, shewing that she was unwilling to say anything unpleasant, it did not seem to annoy Higgins, only to perplex him.\n\n“Do you remember poor Boucher saying that the Union was a tyrant? I think he said it was the worse tyrant of all. And I remember, at the time I agreed with him.”\n\nIt was a long while before he spoke. He was resting his head on his two hands, and looking down into the fire, so she could not read the expression on his face.\n\n“I’ll not deny but what th’ Union finds it necessary to force a man into his own good. I’ll speak truth. A man leads a dree life who’s not i’ th’ Union. But once i’ th’ Union, his interests are taken care on better nor he could do it for himsel’, or by himsel’, for that matter. It’s the only way working men can get their rights, by all joining together. More the members, more chance for each one separate man having justice done him. Government takes care o’ fools and madmen; and if any man is inclined to do himsel’ or his neighbour a hurt, it puts a bit of a check on him, whether he likes it or no. That’s all we do i’ th’ Union. We can’t clap folk into prison; but we can make a man’s life so heavy to be borne, that he’s obliged to come in, and be wise and helpful in spite of himsel’. Boucher were a fool all along, and ne’er a worse fool than at th’ last.”\n\n“He did you harm?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Aye, that did he. We had public opinion on our side, till he and his sort began rioting and breaking laws. It were all o’er wi’ the strike then.”\n\n“Then would it not have been far better to have left him alone, and not forced him to join the Union? He did you no good; and you drove him mad.”\n\n“Margaret,” said her father, in a low and warning tone, for he saw the cloud gathering on Higgins’s face.\n\n“I like her,” said Higgins, suddenly. “Hoo speaks plain out what’s in her mind. Hoo doesn’t comprehend th’ Union for all that. It’s a great power: it’s our only power. I ha’ read a bit o’ poetry about a plough going o’er a daisy, as made tears come into my eyes, afore I’d other cause for crying. But the chap ne’er stopped driving the plough, I’se warrant, for all he was pitiful about the daisy. He’d too much mother-wit for that. Th’ Union’s the plough, making ready the land for harvest-time. Such as Boucher—’twould be settin’ him up too much to liken him to a daisy; he’s liker a weed lounging over the ground—mun just made up their mind to be put out o’ the way. I’m sore vexed wi’ him just now. So m’appen, I dunnot speak him fair. I could go o’er him wi’ a plough mysel’, wi’ a’ the pleasure in life.”\n\n“Why? What has he been doing? Anything fresh?”\n\n“Ay, to be sure. He’s ne’er out o’ mischief, that man. First of a’ he must go raging like a mad fool, and kick up yon riot. Then he’d to go into hiding, where he’d a been yet, if Thornton had followed him out as I’d hoped he would ha’ done. But Thornton, having got his own purpose, didn’t care to go on wi’ the prosecution for the riot. So Boucher slunk back again to his house. He ne’er showed himsel’ abroad for a day or two. He had that grace. And then, where think ye that he went? Why, to Hampers’. Damn him! He went wi’ his mealy-mouthed face, that turns me sick to look at, a-asking for work, though he knowed well enough the new rule, o’ pledging themselves to give nought to th’ Unions; nought to help the starving turn-out! Why he’d a clemmed to death, if th’ Union had na helped him in his pinch. There he went, ossing to promise aught, and pledge himsel’ to aught—to tell a’ he know’d on our proceedings, the good-for-nothing Judas! But I’ll say this for Hamper, and thank him for it at my dying day, he drove Boucher away, and would na listen to him—ne’er a word—though folk standing by, says the traitor cried like a babby!”\n\n“Oh! how shocking! how pitiful!” exclaimed Margaret. “Higgins, I don’t know you to-day. Don’t you see how you’ve made Boucher what he is, by driving him into the Union against his will—without his heart going with it. You have made him what he is!”\n\nMade him what he is! What was he?\n\nGathering, gathering along the narrow street, came a hollow, measured sound; now forcing itself on their attention. Many voices were hushed and low: many steps were heard, not moving onwards, at least not with any rapidity or steadiness of motion, but as if circling round one spot. Yes, there was one distinct, slow tramp of feet, which made itself a clear path through the air, and reached their ears; the measured laboured walk of men carrying a heavy burden. They were all drawn towards the house-door by some irresistible impulse; impelled thither—not by a poor curiosity, but as if by some solemn blast.\n\nSix men walked in the middle of the road, three of them being policemen. They carried a door, taken off its hinges, upon their shoulders, on which lay some dead human creature; and from each side of the door there were constant droppings. All the street turned out to see, and, seeing, to accompany the procession, each one questioning the bearers, who answered almost reluctantly at last, so often had they told the tale.\n\n“We found him i’ th’ brook in the field beyond there.”\n\n“Th’ brook!—why there’s not water enough to drown him!”\n\n“He was a determined chap. He lay with his face downwards. He was sick enough o’ living, choose what cause he had for it.”\n\nHiggins crept up to Margaret’s side, and said in a weak piping kind of voice: “It’s not John Boucher? He had na spunk enough. Sure! It’s not John Boucher! Why, they are a’ looking this way! Listen! I’ve a singing in my head, and I cannot hear.”\n\nThey put the door down carefully upon the stones, and all might see the poor drowned wretch—his glassy eyes, one half-open, staring right upwards to the sky. Owing to the position in which he had been found lying, his face was swollen and discoloured; besides, his skin was stained by the water in the brook, which had been used for dyeing purposes. The fore part of his head was bald; but the hair grew thin and long behind, and every separate lock was a conduit for water. Through all these disfigurements, Margaret recognised John Boucher. It seemed to her so sacrilegious to be peering into that poor distorted, agonised face, that, by a flash of instinct, she went forwards and softly covered the dead man’s countenance with her handkerchief. The eyes that saw her do this followed her, as she turned away from her pious office, and were thus led to the place where Nicholas Higgins stood, like one rooted to the spot. The men spoke together, and then one of them came up to Higgins, who would have fain shrunk back into his house.\n\n“Higgins, thou knowed him! Thou mun go tell the wife. Do it gently, man, but do it quick, for we canna leave him here long.”\n\n“I canna go,” said Higgins. “Dunnot ask me. I canna face her.”\n\n“Thou knows her best,” said the man. “We’n done a deal in bringing him here—thou take thy share.”\n\n“I canna do it,” said Higgins. “I’m welly felled wi’ seeing him. We wasn’t friends; and now he’s dead.”\n\n“Well, if thou wunnot thou wunnot. Some one mun though. It’s a dree task; but it’s a chance, every minute, as she doesn’t hear on it in some rougher way nor a person going to make her let on by degrees, as it were.”\n\n“Papa, do you go,” said Margaret, in a low voice.\n\n“If I could—if I had time to think of what I had better say; but all at once——” Margaret saw that her father was indeed unable. He was trembling from head to foot.\n\n“I will go,” said she.\n\n“Bless yo’ miss, it will be a kind act; for she’s been but a sickly sort o’ body, I hear, and few hereabouts know much on her.”\n\nMargaret knocked at the closed door; but there was such a noise, as of many little ill-ordered children, that she could hear no reply; indeed, she doubted if she was heard, and as every moment of delay made her recoil from her task more and more, she opened the door and went in, shutting it after her, and even, unseen by the woman, fastening the bolt.\n\nMrs. Boucher was sitting in a rocking-chair, on the other side of the ill-redd-up fireplace; it looked as if the house had been untouched for days by any effort at cleanliness.\n\nMargaret said something, she hardly knew what, her throat and mouth were so dry, and the children’s noise completely prevented her from being heard. She tried again.\n\n“How are you, Mrs. Boucher? But very poorly, I’m afraid.”\n\n“I’ve chance o’ being well,” said she querulously. “I’m left alone to manage these childer, and nought for to give ’em for to keep ’em quiet. John should na ha’ left me, and me so poorly.”\n\n“How long is it since he went away?”\n\n“Four days sin’. No one would give him work here, and he’d to go on tramp toward Greenfield. But he might ha’ been back afore this, or sent me some word if he’d getten work. He might——”\n\n“Oh, don’t blame him,” said Margaret. “He felt it deeply, I’m sure——”\n\n“Willto’ hold thy din, and let me hear the lady speak!” addressing herself, in no very gentle voice, to a little urchin of about a year old. She apologetically continued to Margaret, “He’s always mithering me for ‘daddy’ and ‘butty’; and I ha’ no butties to give him, and daddy’s away, and forgotten us a’, I think. He’s his father’s darling, he is,” said she, with a sudden turn of mood, and, dragging the child up to her knee, she began kissing it fondly.\n\nMargaret laid her hand on the woman’s arm to arrest her attention. Their eyes met.\n\n“Poor little fellow!” said Margaret, slowly; “he was his father’s darling.”\n\n“He is his father’s darling,” said the woman, rising hastily, and standing face to face with Margaret. Neither of them spoke for a moment or two. Then Mrs. Boucher began in a low growling tone, gathering in wildness as she went on: “He is his father’s darling, I say. Poor folk can love their childer as well as rich. Why dunno yo’ speak? Why dun yo’ stare at me wi’ your great pitiful eyes? Where’s John?” Weak as she was, she shook Margaret to force out an answer. “Oh, my God!” said she, understanding the meaning of that tearful look. She sank back into the chair. Margaret took up the child and put him into her arms.\n\n“He loved him,” said she.\n\n“Ay,” said the woman, shaking her head, “he loved us a’. We had some one to love us once. It’s a long time ago; but when he were in life and with us he did love us, he did. He loved this babby m’appen the best on us; but he loved me and I loved him, though I was calling him five minutes agone. Are yo’ sure he’s dead?” said she, trying to get up. “If it’s only that he’s ill and like to die, they may bring him round yet. I’m but an ailing creature mysel’—I’ve been ailing this long time.”\n\n“But he is dead—he is drowned!”\n\n“Folk are brought round after they’re dead-drowned. Whatten was I thinking of, to sit still when I should be stirring mysel’? Here, whisth thee, child—whisth thee! tak’ this, tak’ aught to play wi’, but dunnot cry while my heart’s breaking! Oh, where is my strength gone to? Oh John—husband!”\n\nMargaret saved her from falling by catching her in her arms. She sate down in the rocking chair, and held the woman upon her knees, her head lying on Margaret’s shoulder. The other children, clustered together in affright, began to understand the mystery of the scene; but the ideas came slowly, for their brains were dull and languid of perception. They set up such a cry of despair as they guessed the truth, that Margaret knew not how to bear it. Johnny’s cry was loudest of them all, though he knew not why he cried, poor little fellow.\n\nThe mother quivered as she lay in Margaret’s arms. Margaret heard a noise at the door.\n\n“Open it. Open it quick,” said she to the eldest child. “It’s bolted; make no noise—be very still. Oh, papa, let them go upstairs very softly and carefully, and perhaps she will not hear them. She has fainted—that’s all.”\n\n“It’s as well for her, poor creature,” said a woman following in the wake of the bearers of the dead. “But yo’re not fit to hold her. Stay, I’ll run fetch a pillow, and we’ll let her down easy on the floor.”\n\nThis helpful neighbour was a great relief to Margaret; she was evidently a stranger to the house, a new-comer in the district, indeed; but she was so kind and thoughtful that Margaret felt she was no longer needed; and that it would be better, perhaps, to set an example of clearing the house, which was filled with idle, if sympathising gazers.\n\nShe looked round for Nicholas Higgins. He was not there. So she spoke to the woman who had taken the lead in placing Mrs. Boucher on the floor.\n\n“Can you give all these people a hint that they had better leave in quietness? So that when she comes round, she could only find one or two that she knows about her. Papa, will you speak to the men, and get them to go away. She cannot breathe, poor thing, with this crowd about her.”\n\nMargaret was kneeling down by Mrs. Boucher and bathing her face with vinegar; but in a few minutes she was surprised at the gush of fresh air. She looked round, and saw a smile pass between her father and the woman.\n\n“What is it?” asked she.\n\n“Only our good friend here,” replied her father, “hit on a capital expedient for clearing the place.”\n\n“I bid ’em begone, and each take a child with ’em, and to mind that they were orphans, and their mother a widow. It was who could do most, and the childer are sure of a bellyful to-day, and of kindness too. Does hoo know how he died?”\n\n“No,” said Margaret; “I could not tell her all at once.”\n\n“Hoo mun be told because of th’ Inquest. See! Hoo’s coming round; shall you or I do it? or m’appen your father would be best?”\n\n“No; you, you,” said Margaret.\n\nThey awaited her perfect recovery in silence. Then the neighbour woman sat down on the floor, and took Mrs. Boucher’s head and shoulders on her lap.\n\n“Neighbour,” said she, “your man is dead. Guess yo’ how he died?”\n\n“He were drowned,” said Mrs. Boucher, feebly, beginning to cry for the first time, at this rough probing of her sorrows.\n\n“He were found drowned. He were coming home very hopeless o’ aught on earth. He thought God could na be harder than men; m’appen not so hard; m’appen as tender as a mother; m’appen tenderer. I’m not saying he did right, and I’m not saying he did wrong. All I say is, may neither me nor mine ever have his sore heart, or we may do like things.”\n\n“He has left me alone wi’ a’ these children!” moaned the widow, less distressed at the manner of the death than Margaret expected; but it was of a piece with her helpless character to feel his loss as principally affecting herself and her children.\n\n“Not alone,” said Mr. Hale, solemnly. “Who is with you? Who will take up your cause?” The widow opened her eyes wide, and looked at the new speaker, of whose presence she had not been aware till then.\n\n“Who has promised to be a father to the fatherless?” continued he.\n\n“But I’ve getten six children, sir, and the eldest not eight years of age. I’m not meaning for to doubt His power, sir,—only it needs a deal o’ trust”; and she began to cry afresh.\n\n“Hoo’ll be better able to talk to-morrow, sir,” said the neighbour. “Best comfort now would be the feel of a child at her heart. I’m sorry they took the babby.”\n\n“I’ll go for it,” said Margaret. And in a few minutes she returned, carrying Johnnie, his face all smeared with eating, and his hands loaded with treasures in the shape of shells, and bits of crystal, and the head of a plaster figure. She placed him on his mother’s arms.\n\n“There!” said the woman, “now you go. They’ll cry together, and comfort together, better nor any one but a child can do. I’ll stop with her as long as I’m needed, and if you come to-morrow, you’ can have a deal o’ wise talk with her, that she’s not up to to-day.”\n\nAs Margaret and her father went slowly up the street, she paused at Higgins’s closed door.\n\n“Shall we go in?” asked her father. “I was thinking of him too.”\n\nThey knocked. There was no answer, so they tried the door. It was bolted, but they thought they heard him moving within.\n\n“Nicholas!” said Margaret. There was no answer, and they might have gone away, believing the house to be empty, if there had not been some accidental fall, as of a book, within.\n\n“Nicholas!” said Margaret again. “It is only us. Won’t you let us come in?”\n\n“No,” said he. “I spoke as plain as I could, ’bout using words, when I bolted th’ door. Let me be, this day.”\n\nMr. Hale would have urged their desire, but Margaret placed her finger on his lips.\n\n“I don’t wonder at it,” said she. “I myself long to be alone. It seems the only thing to do one good after a day like this.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII\n\n“A spade! a rake! a hoe! A pickaxe or a bill! A hook to reap, or a scythe to mow, A flail or what ye will— And here’s a ready hand To ply the needful tool, And skill’d enough, by lessons rough, In Labour’s rugged school.” HOOD.\n\nHiggins’s door was locked the next day, when they went to pay their call on the widow Boucher: but they learnt this time from an officious neighbour, that he was really from home. He had, however, been in to see Mrs. Boucher, before starting on his day’s business, whatever that was. It was but an unsatisfactory visit to Mrs. Boucher; she considered herself an ill-used woman by her poor husband’s suicide; and there was quite germ of truth enough in this idea to make it a very difficult one to refute. Still, it was unsatisfactory to see how completely her thoughts were turned upon herself and her own position, and this selfishness extended even to her relations with her children, whom she considered as incumbrances, even in the very midst of her somewhat animal affection for them. Margaret tried to make acquaintances with one or two of them, while her father strove to raise the widow’s thoughts into some higher channel than that of mere helpless querulousness. She found that the children were truer and simpler mourners than the widow. Daddy had been a kind daddy to them; each could tell, in their eager stammering way, of some tenderness shown, some indulgence granted by the lost father.\n\n“Is yon thing upstairs really him? it doesna look like him. I’m feared on it, and I never was feared o’ daddy.”\n\nMargaret’s heart bled to hear that the mother, in her selfish requirement of sympathy, had taken her children upstairs to see their dead disfigured father. It was intermingling the coarseness of horror with the profoundness of natural grief. She tried to turn their thoughts in some other direction; on what they could do for mother; on what—for this was a more efficacious way of putting it—what father would have wished them to do. Margaret was more successful than Mr. Hale in her efforts. The children seeing their little duties lie in action close around them, began to try each one to do something that she suggested towards redding up the slatternly room. But her father set too high a standard, and too abstract a view, before the indolent invalid. She could not rouse her torpid mind into any vivid imagination of what her husband’s misery might have been, before he had resorted to the last terrible step; she could only look upon it as it affected herself; she could not enter into the enduring mercy of the God who had not specially interposed to prevent the water from drowning her prostrate husband; and although she was secretly blaming her husband for having fallen into such drear despair, and denying that he had any excuse for his last rash act, she was inveterate in her abuse of all who could by any possibility be supposed to have driven him to such desperation. The masters—Mr. Thornton in particular, whose mill had been attacked by Boucher, and who, after the warrant had been issued for his apprehension on the charge of rioting, had caused it to be withdrawn,—the Union, of which Higgins was a representative to the poor woman,—the children so numerous, so hungry, and so noisy—all made up one great army of personal enemies, whose fault it was that she was now a helpless widow.\n\nMargaret heard enough of this unreasonableness to dishearten her; and when they came away she found it impossible to cheer her father.\n\n“It is the town life,” said she. “Their nerves are quickened by the haste and bustle and speed of everything around them, to say nothing of the confinement in these pent-up houses, which of itself is enough to induce depression and worry of spirits. Now in the country, people live so much more out of doors, even children, and even in the winter.”\n\n“But people must live in towns. And in the country some get such stagnant habits of mind that they are almost fatalists.”\n\n“Yes; I acknowledge that. I suppose each mode of life produces its own trials and its own temptations. The dweller in towns must find it as difficult to be patient, as the country-bred man must find it to be active, and equal to unwonted emergencies. Both must find it hard to realise a future of any kind; the one because the present is so living and hurrying and close around him; the other because his life tempts him to revel in the mere sense of animal existence, not knowing of, and consequently not caring for any pungency of pleasure, for the attainment of which he can plan, and deny himself and look forward.”\n\n“And thus both the necessity for engrossment, and the stupid content in the present, produce the same effects. But this poor Mrs. Boucher! how little we can do for her.”\n\n“And yet we dare not leave her without our efforts, although they may seem so useless. Oh papa! it’s a hard world to live in!”\n\n“So it is, my child. We feel it so just now, at any rate; but we have been very happy, even in the midst of our sorrow. What a pleasure Frederick’s visit was!”\n\n“Yes, that it was,” said Margaret, brightly. “It was such a charming, snatched, forbidden thing.” But she suddenly stopped speaking. She had spoiled the remembrance of Frederick’s visit to herself by her own cowardice. Of all faults the one she most despised in others was the want of bravery; the meanness of heart which leads to untruth. And here had she been guilty of it! Then came the thought of Mr. Thornton’s cognisance of her falsehood. She wondered if she should have minded detection half so much from any one else. She tried herself in imagination with her Aunt Shaw and Edith; with her father; with Captain and Mr. Lennox; with Frederick. The thought of the last knowing what she had done, even in his own behalf, was the most painful, for the brother and sister were in the first flush of their mutual regard and love; but even any fall in Frederick’s opinion was as nothing to the shame, the shrinking shame she felt at the thought of meeting Mr. Thornton again. And yet she longed to see him, to get it over; to understand where she stood in his opinion. Her cheeks burnt as she recollected how proudly she had implied an objection to trade (in the early days of their acquaintance), because it too often led to the deceit of passing off inferior for superior goods, in the one branch; of assuming credit for wealth and resources not possessed, in the other. She remembered Mr. Thornton’s look of calm disdain, as in few words he gave her to understand that, in the great scheme of commerce, all dishonourable ways of acting were sure to prove injurious in the long run, and that, testing such actions simply according to the poor standard of success, there was folly and not wisdom in all such, and every kind of deceit in trade, as well as in other things. She remembered—she, then strong in her own untempted truth—asking him, if he did not think that buying in the cheapest and selling in the dearest market proved some want of the transparent justice which is so intimately connected with the idea of truth: and she had used the word chivalric—and her father had corrected her with the higher word, Christian; and so drawn the argument upon himself, while she sate silent by with a slight feeling of contempt.\n\nNo more contempt for her!—no more talk about the chivalric! Henceforward she must feel humiliated and disgraced in his sight. But when should she see him? Her heart leaped up in apprehension at every ring of the door-bell; and yet, when it fell down to calmness, she felt strangely saddened and sick at heart at each disappointment. It was very evident that her father expected to see him, and was surprised that he did not come. The truth was, that there were points in their conversation the other night on which they had no time then to enlarge; but it had been understood that if possible on the succeeding evening—if not then, at least the very first evening that Mr. Thornton could command,—they should meet for further discussion. Mr. Hale had looked forward to this meeting ever since they had parted. He had not yet resumed the instruction to his pupils, which he had relinquished at the commencement of his wife’s more serious illness, so he had fewer occupations than usual; and the great interest of the last day or so (Boucher’s suicide) had driven him back with more eagerness than ever upon his speculations. He was restless all the evening. He kept saying, “I quite expected to have seen Mr. Thornton. I think the messenger who brought the book last night must have had some note, and forgot to deliver it. Do you think there has been any message left to-day?”\n\n“I will go and inquire, papa,” said Margaret, after the changes on these sentences had been rung once or twice, “Stay, there’s a ring!” She sat down instantly, and bent her head attentively over her work. She heard a step on the stairs, but it was only one, and she knew it was Dixon’s. She lifted up her head and sighed, and believed she felt glad.\n\n“It’s that Higgins, sir. He wants to see you, or else Miss Hale. Or it might be Miss Hale first, and then you, sir; for he’s in a strange kind of way.”\n\n“He had better come up here, Dixon; and then he can see us both, and choose which he likes for his listener.”\n\n“Oh! very well, sir. I’ve no wish to hear what he’s got to say, I’m sure; only, if you could see his shoes, I’m sure you’d say the kitchen was the fitter place.”\n\n“He can wipe them, I suppose,” said Mr. Hale. So Dixon flung off, to bid him walk up-stairs. She was a little mollified, however, when he looked at his feet with a hesitating air; and then, sitting down on the bottom stair, he took off the offending shoes, and without a word walked up-stairs.\n\n“Sarvant, sir!” said he, slicking his hair down when he came into the room: “If hoo’l excuse me (looking at Margaret) for being i’ my stockings; I’se been tramping a’ day, and streets is none o’ th’ cleanest.”\n\nMargaret thought that fatigue might account for the change in his manner, for he was unusually quiet and subdued; and he had evidently some difficulty in saying what he came to say.\n\nMr. Hale’s ever-ready sympathy with anything of shyness or hesitation, or want of self-possession, made him come to his aid.\n\n“We shall have tea up directly, and then you’ll take a cup with us, Mr. Higgins. I am sure you are tired, if you’ve been out much this wet relaxing day. Margaret, my dear, can’t you hasten tea?”\n\nMargaret could only hasten tea by taking the preparation of it into her own hands, and so offending Dixon, who was emerging out of her sorrow for her late mistress into a very touchy, irritable state. But Martha, like all who came in contact with Margaret—even Dixon herself, in the long run—felt it a pleasure and an honour to forward any of her wishes; and her readiness, and Margaret’s sweet forbearance, soon made Dixon ashamed of herself.\n\n“Why master and you must always be asking the lower classes up-stairs, since we came to Milton, I cannot understand. Folk at Helstone were never brought higher than the kitchen; and I’ve let one or two of them know before now that they might think it an honour to be even there.”\n\nHiggins found it easier to unburden himself to one than to two. After Margaret left the room, he went to the door and assured himself that it was shut. Then he came and stood close to Mr. Hale.\n\n“Master,” said he, “yo’d not guess easy what I’ve been tramping after to-day. Special if yo’d remember my manner o’ talk yesterday. I’ve been a seeking work. I have,” said he. “I said to mysel’, I’d keep a civil tongue in my head, let who would say what ’em would. I’d set my teeth into my tongue sooner nor speak i’ haste. For that man’s sake—yo’ understand,” jerking his thumb back in some unknown direction.\n\n“No, I don’t,” said Mr. Hale, seeing he waited for some kind of assent, and completely bewildered as to who “that man” could be.\n\n“That chap as lies theer,” said he, with another jerk. “Him as went and drownded himself, poor chap! I did na think he’d got it in him to lie still and let the water creep o’er him till he died. Boucher, yo’ know.”\n\n“Yes, I know now,” said Mr. Hale. “Go back to what you were saying: you’d not speak in haste——”\n\n“For his sake. Yet not for his sake; for where’er he is, and whate’er, he’ll ne’er know other clemming or cold again; but for the wife’s sake, and the bits o’ childer.”\n\n“God bless you!” said Mr. Hale, starting up; then, calming down, he said, breathlessly, “What do you mean? Tell me out.”\n\n“I have telled yo’,” said Higgins, a little surprised at Mr. Hale’s agitation. “I would na ask for work for mysel’; but them’s left as a charge on me. I reckon, I would ha guided Boucher to a better end; but I set him off o’ th’ road, and so I mun answer for him.”\n\nMr. Hale got hold of Higgins’s hand and shook it heartily, without speaking. Higgins looked awkward and ashamed.\n\n“Theer, theer, master! Theer’s ne’er a man, to call a man, amongst us, but what would do th’ same; ay, and better too; for, belie’ me, Is’e ne’er got a stroke o’ work, nor yet a sight of any. For all I telled Hamper that, let alone his pledge—which I would not sign—no, I could na, not e’en for this—he’d ne’er ha’ such a worker on his mill as I would be—he’d ha’ none o’ me—no more would none o’ th’ others. I’m a poor black feckless sheep—childer may clem for ought I can do, unless, parson, yo’d help me?”\n\n“Help you! How? I would do anything,—but what can I do?”\n\n“Miss there”—for Margaret had re-entered the room, and stood silent, listening—“has often talked grand o’ the South, and the ways down there. Now I dunnot know how far off it is, but I been thinking if I could get ’em down theer, where food is cheap and wages good, and all the folk, rich and poor, master and man, friendly like; yo’ could, may be, help me to work. I’m not forty-five, and I’ve a deal o’ strength in me, measter.”\n\n“But what kind of work could you do, my man?”\n\n“Well, I reckon I could spade a bit——”\n\n“And for that,” said Margaret, stepping forwards, “for anything you could do, Higgins, with the best will in the world, you would, may be, get nine shillings a week; may be ten, at the outside. Food is much the same as here, except that you might have a little garden——”\n\n“The childer could work at that,” said he. “I’m sick o’ Milton anyways, and Milton is sick o’ me.”\n\n“You must not go the South,” said Margaret, “for all that. You could not stand it. You would have to be out all weathers. It would kill you with rheumatism. The mere bodily work at your time of life would break you down. The fare is far different to what you have been accustomed to.”\n\n“I’se nought particular about my meat,” said he, as if offended.\n\n“But you’ve reckoned on having butcher’s meat once a day, if you’re in work; pay for that out of your ten shillings, and keep those poor children if you can. I owe it to you—since it’s my way of talking that has set you off on this idea—to put it all clear before you. You would not bear the dulness of the life; you don’t know what it is; it would eat you away like rust. Those that have lived there all their lives, are used to soaking in the stagnant waters. They labour on from day to day, in the great solitude of steaming fields—never speaking or lifting up their poor, bent, downcast heads. The hard spadework robs their brain of life; the sameness of their toil deadens their imagination; they don’t care to meet to talk over thoughts and speculations, even of the weakest, wildest kind, after their work is done; they go home brutishly tired, poor creatures! caring for nothing but food and rest. You could not stir them up into any companionship, which you get in a town as plentiful as the air you breathe, whether it be good or bad—and that I don’t know; but I do know, that you of all men are not one to bear a life among such labourers. What would be peace to them, would be eternal fretting to you. Think no more of it, Nicholas, I beg. Besides, you could never pay to get mother and children all there—that’s one good thing.”\n\n“I’ve reckoned for that. One house mun do for us a’, and the furniture o’ t’other would go a good way. And men theer mun have their families to keep—m’appen six or seven childer. God help ’em!” said he, more convinced by his own presentation of the facts than by all Margaret had said, and suddenly renouncing the idea, which had but recently formed itself in a brain worn out by the day’s fatigue and anxiety. “God help ’em! North an’ South have each getten their own troubles. If work’s sure and steady theer, labour’s paid at starvation wages; while here we’n rucks o’ money coming in one quarter, and ne’er a farthing th’ next. For sure, th’ world is in a confusion that passes me or any other man to understand; it needs fettling, and who’s to fettle it if it’s as yon folks say, and there’s nought but what we see?”\n\nMr. Hale was busy cutting bread and butter; Margaret was glad of this, for she saw that Higgins was better left to himself: that if her father began to speak ever so mildly on the subject of Higgins’s thoughts, the latter would consider himself challenged to an argument, and would feel himself bound to maintain his own ground. She and her father kept up an indifferent conversation until Higgins, scarcely aware whether he ate or not, had made a very substantial meal. Then he pushed his chair away from the table, and tried to take an interest in what they were saying; but it was of no use; and he fell back into dreamy gloom. Suddenly, Margaret said (she had been thinking of it for some time, but the words had stuck in her throat), “Higgins have you been to Marlborough Mills to seek for work?”\n\n“Thornton’s?” asked he. “Ay, I’ve been at Thornton’s”\n\n“And what did he say?”\n\n“Such a chap as me is not like to see the measter. Th’ o’erlooker bid me go and be d——d.”\n\n“I wish you had seen Mr. Thornton,” said Mr. Hale. “He might not have given you work, but he would not have used such language.”\n\n“As to th’ language, I’m welly used to it; it dunnot matter to me. I’m not nesh mysel’ when I’m put out. It were th’ fact that I were na wanted theer, no more nor ony other place, as I minded.”\n\n“But I wish you had seen Mr. Thornton,” repeated Margaret. “Would you go again—it’s a good deal to ask, I know—but would you go to-morrow and try him? I should be so glad if you would.”\n\n“I’m afraid it would be of no use,” said Mr. Hale, in a low voice. “It would be better to let me speak to him.” Margaret still looked at Higgins for his answer. Those grave soft eyes of hers were difficult to resist. He gave a great sigh.\n\n“It would tax my pride above a bit; if it were for mysel’, I could stand a deal o’ clemming first; I’d sooner knock him down than ask a favour from him. I’d a deal sooner be flogged mysel’; but yo’re not a common wench, axing yo’r pardon, nor yet have yo’ common ways about yo’. I’ll e’en make a wry face, and go at it to-morrow. Dunna yo’ think that he’ll do it. That man has it in him to be burnt at the stake afore he’ll give in. I’ll do it for yo’r sake, Miss Hale, and it’s first time in my life as e’er I give way to a woman. Neither my wife nor Bess could e’er say that much again me.”\n\n“All the more do I thank you,” said Margaret, smiling. “Though I don’t believe you: I believe you have just given way to wife and daughter as much as most men.”\n\n“And as to Mr. Thornton,” said Mr. Hale, “I’ll give you a note to him, which, I think I may venture say, will ensure you a hearing.”\n\n“I thank yo’ kindly, sir, but I’d as lief stand on my own bottom. I dunnot stomach the notion of having favour curried for me, by one as doesn’t know the ins and outs of the quarrel. Meddling ’twixt master and man is liker meddling ’twixt husband and wife than aught else: it takes a deal of wisdom to do ony good. I’ll stand guard at the lodge door. I’ll stand there fro’ six in the morning till I get speech on him. But I’d liefer sweep th’ streets, if paupers had na’ got hold on that work. Dunna yo’ hope, miss. There’ll be more chance o’ getting milk out of a flint. I wish yo’ a very good night, and many thanks to yo’.”\n\n“You’ll find your shoes by the kitchen fire; I took them there to dry,” said Margaret.\n\nHe turned round and looked at her steadily, and then he brushed his lean hand across his eyes and went his way.\n\n“How proud that man is!” said her father, who was a little annoyed at the manner in which Higgins had declined his intercession with Mr. Thornton.\n\n“He is,” said Margaret; “but what grand makings of a man there are in him, pride and all.”\n\n“It’s amusing to see how he evidently respects the part in Mr. Thornton’s character which is like his own.”\n\n“There’s granite in all these northern people, papa, is there not?”\n\n“There was none in poor Boucher, I am afraid; none in his wife either.”\n\n“I should guess by their tones that they had Irish blood in them. I wonder what success he’ll have to-morrow. If he and Mr. Thornton would speak out together as man to man—if Higgins would forget that Mr. Thornton was a master, and speak to him as he does to us—and if Mr. Thornton would be patient enough to listen to him with his human heart, not with his master’s ears—”\n\n“You are getting to do Mr. Thornton justice at last, Margaret,” said her father, pinching her ear.\n\nMargaret had a strange choking at her heart, which made her unable to answer, “Oh!” thought she, “I wish I were a man, that I could go and force him to express his disapprobation, and tell him honestly that I knew I deserved it. It seems hard to lose him as a friend just when I had begun to feel his value. How tender he was with dear mamma! If it were only for her sake, I wish he would come, and then at least I should know how much I was abased in his eyes.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII\n\n“Then proudly, proudly up she rose, Tho’ the tear was in her e’e, Whate’er ye say, think what ye may, Ye’s get na word frae me!” SCOTCH BALLAD.\n\nIt was not merely that Margaret was known to Mr. Thornton to have spoken falsely,—though she imagined that for this reason only was she so turned in his opinion,—but that this falsehood of hers bore a distinct reference in his mind to some other lover. He could not forget the fond and earnest look that had passed between her and some other man—the attitude of familiar confidence, if not of positive endearment. The thought of this perpetually stung him; it was a picture before his eyes, wherever he went and whatever he was doing. In addition to this (and he ground his teeth as he remembered it), was the hour, dusky twilight; the place, so far away from home, and comparatively unfrequented. His nobler self had said at first, that all this last might be accidental, innocent, justifiable; but once allow her right to love and be beloved (and had he any reason to deny her right?—had not her words been severely explicit when she cast his love away from her?), she might easily have been beguiled into a longer walk, on to a later hour than she had anticipated. But that falsehood! which showed a fatal consciousness of something wrong, and to be concealed, which was unlike her. He did her that justice, though all the time it would have been a relief to believe her utterly unworthy of his esteem. It was this that made the misery—that he passionately loved her, and thought her, even with all her faults, more lovely and more excellent than any other woman; yet he deemed her so attached to some other man, so led away by her affection for him, as to violate her truthful nature. The very falsehood that stained her, was a proof how blindly she loved another—this dark, slight, elegant, handsome man—while he himself was rough, and stern, and strongly made. He lashed himself into an agony of fierce jealousy. He thought of that look, that attitude!—how he would have laid his life at her feet for such tender glances, such fond detention! He mocked at himself, for having valued the mechanical way in which she had protected him from the fury of the mob; now he had seen how soft and bewitching she looked when with a man she really loved. He remembered, point by point, the sharpness of her words—“There was not a man in all that crowd for whom she would not have done as much, far more readily than for him.” He shared with the mob, in her desire of averting bloodshed from them; but this man, this hidden lover, shared with nobody; he had looks, words, hand-cleavings, lies, concealment, all to himself.\n\nMr. Thornton was conscious that he had never been so irritable as he was now, in all his life long; he felt inclined to give a short abrupt answer, more like a bark than a speech, to every one that asked him a question; and this consciousness hurt his pride: he had always piqued himself on his self-control, and control himself he would. So the manner was subdued to a quiet deliberation, but the matter was even harder and sterner than common. He was more than usually silent at home; employing his evenings in a continual pace backwards and forwards, which would have annoyed his mother exceedingly if it had been practised by any one else; and did not tend to promote any forbearance on her part even to this beloved son.\n\n“Can you stop—can you sit down for a moment? I have something to say to you, if you would give up that everlasting walk, walk, walk.”\n\nHe sat down instantly, on a chair against the wall.\n\n“I want to speak to you about Betsy. She says she must leave us; that her lover’s death has so affected her spirits she can’t give her heart to her work.”\n\n“Very well. I suppose other cooks are to be met with.”\n\n“That’s so like a man. It’s not merely the cooking, it is that she knows all the ways of the house. Besides, she tells me something about your friend Miss Hale.”\n\n“Miss Hale is no friend of mine. Mr. Hale is my friend.”\n\n“I am glad to hear you say so, for if she had been your friend, what Betsy says would have annoyed you.”\n\n“Let me hear it,” said he, with the extreme quietness of manner he had been assuming for the last few days.\n\n“Betsy says, that the night on which her lover—I forget his name—for she always calls him ‘he’——”\n\n“Leonards.”\n\n“The night on which Leonards was last seen at the station—when he was last seen on duty, in fact—Miss Hale was there, walking about with a young man who, Bessy believes, killed Leonards by some blow or push.”\n\n“Leonards was not killed by any blow or push.”\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“Because I distinctly put the question to the surgeon of the Infirmary. He told me there was an internal disease of long standing, caused by Leonards’ habit of drinking to excess; that the fact of his becoming rapidly worse while in a state of intoxication, settled the question as to whether the last fatal attack was caused by excess of drinking, or the fall.”\n\n“The fall! What fall?”\n\n“Caused by the blow or push of which Betsy speaks.”\n\n“Then there was a blow or push?”\n\n“I believe so.”\n\n“And who did it?”\n\n“As there was no inquest, in consequence of the doctor’s opinion, I cannot tell you.”\n\n“But Miss Hale was there?”\n\nNo answer.\n\n“And with a young man?”\n\nStill no answer. At last he said: “I tell you, mother, that there was no inquest—no inquiry. No judicial inquiry, I mean.”\n\n“Betsy says that Woolmer (some man she knows, who is in a grocer’s shop out at Crampton) can swear that Miss Hale was at the station at that hour, walking backwards and forwards with a young man.”\n\n“I don’t see what we have to do with that. Miss Hale is at liberty to please herself.”\n\n“I’m glad to hear you say so,” says Mrs. Thornton, eagerly. “It certainly signifies very little to us—not at all to you, after what has passed! but I—I made a promise to Mrs. Hale, that I would not allow her daughter to go wrong without advising and remonstrating with her. I shall certainly let her know my opinion of such conduct.”\n\n“I do not see any harm in what she did that evening,” said Mr. Thornton, getting up, and coming near to his mother; he stood by the chimney-piece with his face turned away from the room.\n\n“You would not have approved of Fanny’s being seen out, after dark, in rather a lonely place, walking about with a young man. I say nothing of the taste which could choose the time, when her mother lay unburied, for such a promenade. Should you have liked your sister to have been noticed by a grocer’s assistant for doing so?”\n\n“In the first place, as it is not many years since I myself was a draper’s assistant, the mere circumstance of a grocer’s assistant noticing any act does not alter the character of the act to me. And in the next place, I see a great deal of difference between Miss Hale and Fanny. I can imagine that the one may have weighty reasons, which may and ought to make her overlook any seeming impropriety in her conduct. I never knew Fanny have weighty reasons for anything. Other people must guard her. I believe Miss Hale is a guardian to herself.”\n\n“A pretty character of your sister, indeed! Really, John, one would have thought Miss Hale had done enough to make you clear-sighted. She drew you on to an offer, by a bold display of pretended regard for you,—to play you off against this very young man, I’ve no doubt. Her whole conduct is clear to me now. You believe he is her lover, I suppose—you agree to that.”\n\nHe turned round to his mother; his face was very gray and grim. “Yes, mother. I do believe he is her lover.” When he had spoken, he turned round again; he writhed himself about, like one in bodily pain. He leant his face against his hand. Then before she could speak, he turned sharp again:\n\n“Mother. He is her lover, whoever he is; but she may need help and womanly counsel;—there may be difficulties or temptations which I don’t know. I fear there are. I don’t want to know what they are; but as you have ever been a good—ay! and a tender mother to me, go to her, and gain her confidence, and tell her what is best to be done. I know that something is wrong; some dread, which must be a terrible torture to her.”\n\n“For God’s sake, John!” said his mother, now really shocked, “what do you mean? What do you mean? What do you know?”\n\nHe did not reply to her.\n\n“John! I don’t know what I shan’t think unless you speak. You have no right to say what you have done against her.”\n\n“Not against her, mother! I could not speak against her.”\n\n“Well! you have no right to say what you have done, unless you say more. These half-expressions are what ruin a woman’s character.”\n\n“Her character! Mother, you do not dare—” he faced about, and looked into her face with his flaming eyes. Then, drawing himself up into determined composure and dignity, he said, “I will not say any more than this, which is neither more nor less than the simple truth, and I am sure you believe me,—I have good reason to believe, that Miss Hale is in some strait and difficulty connected with an attachment which, of itself, from my knowledge of Miss Hale’s character, is perfectly innocent and right. What my reason is, I refuse to tell. But never let me hear any one say a word against her, implying any more serious imputation than that she now needs the counsel of some kind and gentle woman. You promised Mrs. Hale to be that woman!”\n\n“No!” said Mrs. Thornton. “I am happy to say, I did not promise kindness and gentleness, for I felt at the time that it might be out of my power to render these to one of Miss Hale’s character and disposition. I promised counsel and advice, such as I would give to my own daughter; I shall speak to her as I would do to Fanny, if she had gone gallivanting with a young man in the dusk. I shall speak with relation to the circumstances I know, without being influenced either one way or another by the ‘strong reasons’ which you will not confide to me. Then I shall have fulfilled my promise, and done my duty.\n\n“She will never bear it,” said he passionately.\n\n“She will have to bear it, if I speak in her dead mother’s name.”\n\n“Well!” said he, breaking away, “don’t tell me any more about it. I cannot endure to think of it. It will be better that you should speak to her any way, than that she should not be spoken to at all.—Oh! that look of love!” continued he, between his teeth, as he bolted himself into his own private room. “And that cursed lie; which showed some terrible shame in the background, to be kept from the light in which I thought she lived perpetually! Oh, Margaret, Margaret! Mother, how you have tortured me! Oh! Margaret, could you not have loved me? I am but uncouth and hard, but I would never have led you into any falsehood for me.”\n\nThe more Mrs. Thornton thought over what her son had said, in pleading for a merciful judgment for Margaret’s indiscretion, the more bitterly she felt inclined towards her. She took a savage pleasure in the idea of “speaking her mind” to her, in the guise of fulfilment of a duty. She enjoyed the thought of showing herself untouched by the “glamour,” which she was well aware Margaret had the power of throwing over many people. She snorted scornfully over the picture of the beauty of her victim; her jet black hair, her clear smooth skin, her lucid eyes would not help to save her one word of the just and stern reproach which Mrs. Thornton spent half the night in preparing to her mind.\n\n“Is Miss Hale within?” She knew she was, for she had seen her at the window, and she had her feet inside the little hall before Martha had half answered the question.\n\nMargaret was sitting alone, writing to Edith, and giving her many particulars of her mother’s last days. It was a softening employment, and she had to brush away the unbidden tears as Mrs. Thornton was announced.\n\nShe was so gentle and ladylike in her mode of reception that her visitor was somewhat daunted; and it became impossible to utter the speech, so easy of arrangement with no one to address it to. Margaret’s low rich voice was softer than usual; her manner more gracious, because in her heart she was feeling very grateful to Mrs. Thornton for the courteous attention of her call. She exerted herself to find subjects of interest for conversation; praised Martha, the servant whom Mrs. Thornton had found for them: had asked Edith for a little Greek air, about which she had spoken to Miss Thornton. Mrs. Thornton was fairly discomfited. Her sharp Damascus blade seemed out of place, and useless among rose-leaves. She was silent because she was trying to task herself up to her duty. At last she stung herself into its performance by a suspicion which, in spite of all probability, she allowed to cross her mind, that all this sweetness was put on with a view of propitiating Mr. Thornton; that, somehow, the other attachment had fallen through, and that it suited Miss Hale’s purpose to recall her rejected lover. Poor Margaret! there was perhaps so much truth in the suspicion as this; that Mrs. Thornton was the mother of one whose regard she valued, and feared to have lost; and this thought unconsciously added to her natural desire of pleasing one who was showing her kindness by her visit. Mrs. Thornton stood up to go, but yet she seemed to have something more to say. She cleared her throat and began:\n\n“Miss Hale, I have a duty to perform. I promised your poor mother that, as far as my poor judgment went, I would not allow you to act in any way wrongly, or (she softened her speech down a little here) inadvertently, without remonstrating; at least without offering advice, whether you took it or not.”\n\nMargaret stood before her, blushing like any culprit, with her eyes dilating as she gazed at Mrs. Thornton. She thought she had come to speak to her about the falsehood she had told—that Mr. Thornton had employed her to explain the danger she had exposed herself to, of being confuted in full court! and although her heart sank to think he had not rather chosen to come himself, and upbraid her, and receive her penitence, and restore her again to his good opinion, yet she was too much humbled not to bear any blame on this subject patiently and meekly.\n\nMrs. Thornton went on:\n\n“At first, when I heard from one of my servants, that you had been seen walking about with a gentleman, so far from home as the Outwood station, at such a time of the evening, I could hardly believe it. But my son, I am sorry to say, confirmed her story. It was indiscreet, to say the least; many a young woman has lost her character before now——”\n\nMargaret’s eyes flashed fire. This was a new idea—this was too insulting. If Mrs. Thornton had spoken to her about the lie she had told, well and good—she would have owned it, and humiliated herself. But to interfere with her conduct—to speak of her character! she—Mrs. Thornton, a mere stranger—it was too impertinent! She would not answer her—not one word. Mrs. Thornton saw the battle-spirit in Margaret’s eyes, and it called up her combativeness also.\n\n“For your mother’s sake, I have thought it right to warn you against such improprieties; they must degrade you in the long run in the estimation of the world, even if in fact they do not lead you to positive harm.”\n\n“For my mother’s sake,” said Margaret, in a tearful voice, “I will bear much; but I cannot bear everything. She never meant me to be exposed to insult, I am sure.”\n\n“Insult, Miss Hale!”\n\n“Yes, madam,” said Margaret more steadily, “it is insult. What do you know of me that should lead you to suspect—Oh!” said she, breaking down, and covering her face with her hands—“I know now, Mr. Thornton has told you——”\n\n“No, Miss Hale,” said Mrs. Thornton, her truthfulness causing her to arrest the confession Margaret was on the point of making, though her curiosity was itching to hear it. “Stop. Mr. Thornton has told me nothing. You do not know my son. You are not worthy to know him. He said this. Listen, young lady, that you may understand, if you can, what sort of a man you rejected. This Milton manufacturer, his great tender heart scorned as it was scorned, said to me only last night, ‘Go to her. I have good reason to know that she is in some strait arising out of some attachment; and she needs womanly counsel.’ I believe those were his very words. Farther than that—beyond admitting the fact of your being at the Outwood station with a gentleman, on the evening of the twenty-sixth—he has said nothing—not one word against you. If he has knowledge of anything which should make you sob so, he keeps it to himself.”\n\nMargaret’s face was still hidden in her hands, the fingers of which were wet with tears. Mrs. Thornton was a little mollified.\n\n“Come, Miss Hale. There may be circumstances, I’ll allow, that, if explained, may take off from the seeming impropriety.”\n\nStill no answer. Margaret was considering what to say; she wished to stand well with Mrs. Thornton; and yet she could not, might not, give any explanation. Mrs. Thornton grew impatient.\n\n“I shall be sorry to break off an acquaintance; but for Fanny’s sake—as I told my son, if Fanny had done so we should consider it a great disgrace—and Fanny might be led away——”\n\n“I can give you no explanation,” said Margaret, in a low voice. “I have done wrong, but not in the way you think or know about. I think Mr. Thornton judges me more mercifully than you;”—she had hard work to keep herself from choking with her tears—“but, I believe, madam, you mean to do rightly.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Mrs. Thornton, drawing herself up; “I was not aware that my meaning was doubted. It is the last time I shall interfere. I was unwilling to consent to do it, when your mother asked me. I had not approved of my son’s attachment to you, while I only suspected it. You did not appear to me worthy of him. But when you compromised yourself as you did at the time of the riot, and exposed yourself to the comments of servants and workpeople, I felt it was no longer right to set myself against my son’s wish of proposing to you—a wish, by the way, which he had always denied entertaining until the day of the riot.” Margaret winced, and drew in her breath with a long, hissing sound; of which, however, Mrs. Thornton took no notice. “He came; you had apparently changed your mind. I told my son yesterday, that I thought it possible, short as was the interval, you might have heard or learnt something of this other lover——”\n\n“What must you think of me, madam?” asked Margaret, throwing her head back with proud disdain, till her throat curved outwards like a swan’s. “You can say nothing more, Mrs. Thornton. I decline every attempt to justify myself for anything. You must allow me to leave the room.”\n\nAnd she swept out of it with the noiseless grace of an offended princess. Mrs. Thornton had quite enough of natural humour to make her feel the ludricrousness of the position in which she was left. There was nothing for it but to show herself out. She was not particularly annoyed at Margaret’s way of behaving. She did not care enough for her for that. She had taken Mrs. Thornton’s remonstrance to the full as keenly to heart as that lady expected; and Margaret’s passion at once mollified her visitor, far more than any silence or reserve could have done. It showed the effect of her words. “My young lady,” thought Mrs. Thornton to herself; “you’ve a pretty good temper of your own. If John and you had come together, he would have had to keep a tight hand over you, to make you know your place. But I don’t think you will go a-walking again with your beau, at such an hour of the day, in a hurry. You’ve too much pride and spirit in you for that. I like to see a girl fly out at the notion of being talked about. It shows they’re neither giddy, nor bold by nature. As for that girl, she might be bold, but she’d never be giddy. I’ll do her that justice. Now as to Fanny, she’d be giddy, and not bold. She’s no courage in her, poor thing!”\n\nMr. Thornton was not spending the morning so satisfactorily as his mother. She, at any rate, was fulfilling her determined purpose. He was trying to understand where he stood; what damage the strike had done him. A good deal of his capital was locked up in new and expensive machinery; and he had also bought cotton largely, with a view to some great orders which he had in hand. The strike had thrown him terribly behindhand, as to the completion of these orders. Even with his own accustomed and skilled workpeople, he would have had some difficulty in fulfilling his engagements; as it was, the incompetence of the Irish hands, who had to be trained to their work, at a time requiring unusual activity, was a daily annoyance.\n\nIt was not a favourable hour for Higgins to make his request. But he had promised Margaret to do it at any cost. So, though every moment added to his repugnance, his pride, and his sullenness of temper, he stood leaning against the dead wall, hour after hour, first on one leg, then on the other. At last the latch was sharply lifted, and out came Mr. Thornton.\n\n“I want for to speak to yo’, sir.”\n\n“Can’t stay now, my man. I’m too late as it is.”\n\n“Well, sir, I reckon I can wait till yo’ come back.”\n\nMr. Thornton was half way down the street. Higgins sighed. But it was no use. To catch him in the street, was his only chance of seeing “the measter!” if he had rung the lodge bell or even gone up to the house to ask for him, he would have been referred to the overlooker. So he stood still again, vouchsafing no answer, but a short nod of recognition to the few men who knew and spoke to him, as the crowd drove out the millyard at dinner-time, and scowling with all his might at the Irish “knobsticks” who had just been imported. At last Mr. Thornton returned.\n\n“What! you there still!”\n\n“Ay, sir, I mun speak to yo’.”\n\n“Come in here, then. Stay, we’ll go across the yard; the men are not come back, and we shall have it to ourselves. These good people, I see, are at dinner,” said he, closing the door of the porter’s lodge.\n\nHe stopped to speak to the overlooker. The latter said in a low tone:\n\n“I suppose you know, sir, that that man is Higgins, one of the leaders of the Union; he that made that speech in Hurstfield.”\n\n“No, I didn’t,” said Mr. Thornton, looking round sharply at his follower. Higgins was known to him by name as a turbulent spirit.\n\n“Come along,” said he, and his tone was rougher than before. “It is men such as this,” thought he, “who interrupt commerce and injure the very town they live in: mere demagogues, lovers of power at whatever cost to others.”\n\n“Well, sir! what do you want with me?” said Mr. Thornton, facing round at him, as soon as they were in the counting-house of the mill.\n\n“My name is Higgins”——\n\n“I know that,” broke in Mr. Thornton. “What do you want, Mr. Higgins? That’s the question.”\n\n“I want work.”\n\n“Work! You’re a pretty chap to come asking me for work. You don’t want impudence, that’s very clear.”\n\n“I’ve getten enemies and backbiters, like my betters; but I ne’er heerd o’ ony of them calling me o’er-modest,” said Higgins. His blood was a little roused by Mr. Thornton’s manner, more than by his words.\n\nMr. Thornton saw a letter addressed to himself on the table. He took it up and read it through. At the end, he looked up and said, “What are you waiting for?”\n\n“An answer to th’ question I axed.”\n\n“I gave it you before. Don’t waste any more of your time.”\n\n“Yo’ made a remark, sir, on my impudence: but I were taught that it was manners to say either ‘yes’ or ‘no,’ when I were axed a civil question. I should be thankfu’ to yo’ if yo’d give me work. Hamper will speak to my being a good hand.”\n\n“I’ve a notion you’d better not send me to Hamper to ask for a character, my man. I might hear more than you’d like.”\n\n“I’d take th’ risk. Worst they could say of me is, that I did what I thought best, even to my own wrong.”\n\n“You’d better go and try them, then, and see whether they’ll give you work. I’ve turned off upwards of a hundred of my best hands, for no other fault than following you and such as you; and d’ye think I’ll take you on? I might as well put a fire-brand into the midst of the cotton-waste.”\n\nHiggins turned away: then the recollection of Boucher came over him, and he faced round with the greatest concession he could persuade himself to make.\n\n“I’d promise yo’, measter, I’d not speak a word as could do harm, if so be yo’ did right by us; and I’d promise more: I’d promise that when I seed yo’ going wrong, and acting unfair, I’d speak to yo’ in private first; and that would be a fair warning. If yo’ and I did na agree in our opinion o’ your conduct, yo’ might turn me off at an hour’s notice.”\n\n“Upon my word, you don’t think small beer of yourself! Hamper has had a loss of you. How came he to let you and your wisdom go?”\n\n“Well, we parted wi’ mutual dissatisfaction. I wouldn’t gi’e the pledge they were asking; and they wouldn’t have me at no rate. So I’m free to make another engagement; and as I said before, though I should na’ say it, I’m a good hand, measter, and a steady man—specially when I can keep fro’ drink; and that I shall do now, if I ne’er did afore.”\n\n“That you may have more money laid up for another strike, I suppose?”\n\n“No! I’d be thankful if I was free to do that; it’s for to keep th’ widow and childer of a man who was drove mad by them knobsticks o’ yourn; put out of his place by a Paddy that did na know weft fro’ warp.”\n\n“Well! you’d better turn to something else, if you’ve any such good intention in your head. I shouldn’t advise you to stay in Milton: you’re too well known here.”\n\n“If it were summer,” said Higgins, “I’d take to Paddy’s work, and go as a navvy, or haymaking, or summat, and ne’er see Milton again. But it’s winter, and th’ childer will clem.”\n\n“A pretty navvy you’d make! why you couldn’t do half a day’s work at digging against an Irishman.”\n\n“I’d only charge half-a-day for th’ twelve hours, if I could only do half-a-day’s work in th’ time. Yo’re not knowing of any place, where they could gi’ me a trial, away fro’ the mills, if I’m such a firebrand? I’d take any wage they thought I was worth, for the sake of those childer.”\n\n“Don’t you see what you would be? You’d be a knobstick. You’d be taking less wages than the other labourers—all for the sake of another man’s children. Think how you’d abuse any poor fellow who was willing to take what he could get to keep his own children. You and your Union would soon be down upon him. No! no! if it’s only for the recollection of the way in which you’ve used the poor knobsticks before now, I say No! to your question. I’ll not give you work. I won’t say, I don’t believe your pretext for coming and asking for work; I know nothing about it. It may be true, or it may not. It’s a very unlikely story, at any rate. Let me pass. I’ll not give you work. There’s your answer.”\n\n“I hear, sir. I would na ha’ troubled you’, but that I were bid to come, by one as seemed to think yo’d getten some place in your heart. Hoo were mistook, and I were misled. But I’m not the first man as is misled by a woman.”\n\n“Tell her to mind her own business the next time, instead of taking up your time and mine too. I believe women are at the bottom of every plague in this world. Be off with you.”\n\n“I’m obleeged to you for a’ yo’r kindness, measter, and most of a’ for yo’r civil way o’ saying good-bye.”\n\nMr. Thornton did not deign a reply. But, looking out of the window a minute later, he was struck with the lean, bent figure going out of the yard; the heavy walk was in strange contrast with the resolute, clear determination of the man to speak to him. He crossed to the porter’s lodge:\n\n“How long has that man Higgins been waiting to speak to me?”\n\n“He was outside the gate before eight o’clock, sir. I think he’s been there ever since.”\n\n“And it is now—?”\n\n“Just one, sir.”\n\n“Five hours,” thought Mr. Thornton; “it’s a long time for a man to wait, doing nothing but first hoping and then fearing.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX\n\n“Nay, I have done; you get no more of me; And I am glad, yea glad with all my heart, That thus so clearly I myself am free.” DRAYTON.\n\nMargaret shut herself up in her own room, after she had quitted Mrs. Thornton. She began to walk backwards and forwards, in her old habitual way of showing agitation; but, then, remembering that in that slightly-built house every step was heard from one room to another, she sate down until she heard Mrs. Thornton go safely out of the house. She forced herself to recollect all the conversation that had passed between them; speech by speech, she compelled her memory to go through with it. At the end, she rose up, and said to herself, in a melancholy tone:\n\n“At any rate, her words do not touch me; they fall off from me; for I am innocent of all the motives she attributes to me. But still, it is hard to think that any one—any woman—can believe all this of another so easily. It is hard and sad. Where I have done wrong, she does not accuse me—she does not know. He never told her: I might have known he would not!”\n\nShe lifted up her head, as if she took pride in any delicacy of feeling which Mr. Thornton had shown. Then, as a new thought came across her, she pressed her hands tightly together:\n\n“He, too, must take poor Frederick for some lover.” (She blushed as the word passed through her mind.) “I see it now. It is not merely that he knows of my falsehood, but he believes that some one else cares for me; and that I—— Oh dear!—oh dear! What shall I do? What do I mean? Why do I care what he thinks, beyond the mere loss of his good opinion as regards my telling the truth or not? I cannot tell. But I am very miserable! Oh, how unhappy this last year has been! I have passed out of childhood into old age. I have had no youth—no womanhood; the hopes of womanhood have closed for me—for I shall never marry; and I anticipate cares and sorrows just as if I were an old woman, and with the same tearful spirit. I am weary of this continual call upon me for strength. I could bear up for papa; because that is a natural, pious duty. And I think I could bear up against—at any rate, I could have the energy to resent, Mrs. Thornton’s unjust, impertinent suspicions. But it is hard to feel how completely he must misunderstand me. What has happened to make me so morbid to-day? I do not know. I only know I cannot help it. I must give way sometimes. No, I will not though,” said she, springing to her feet. “I will not—I will not think of myself and my own position. I won’t examine into my own feelings. It would be of no use now. Some time, if I live to be an old woman, I may sit over the fire, and looking into the embers, see the life that might have been.”\n\nAll this time she was hastily putting on her things to go out, only stopping from time to time to wipe her eyes, with an impatience of gesture at the tears that would come again, in spite of all her bravery.\n\n“I dare say there’s many a woman makes as sad a mistake as I have done, and only finds it out too late. And how proudly and impertinently I spoke to him that day! But I did not know then. It has come upon me little by little, and I don’t know where it began. Now I won’t give way. I shall find it difficult to behave in the same way to him, with this miserable consciousness upon me; but I will be very calm and very quiet, and say very little. But, to be sure, I may not see him; he keeps out of our way evidently. That would be worse than all. And yet no wonder that he avoids me, believing what he must about me.”\n\nShe went out, going rapidly towards the country, and trying to drown reflection by swiftness of motion.\n\nAs she stood on the door-step, at her return, her father came up:\n\n“Good girl!” said he. “You’ve been to Mrs. Boucher’s. I was just meaning to go there, if I had time, before dinner.”\n\n“No, papa; I have not,” said Margaret, reddening. “I never thought about her. But I will go directly after dinner; I will go while you are taking your nap.”\n\nAccordingly Margaret went. Mrs. Boucher was very ill; really ill—not merely ailing. The kind and sensible neighbour, who had come in the other day, seemed to have taken charge of everything. Some of the children were gone to the neighbours. Mary Higgins had come for the three youngest at dinner-time; and since then Nicholas had gone for the doctor. He had not come as yet; Mrs. Boucher was dying; and there was nothing to do but wait. Margaret thought that she should like to hear his opinion, and that she could not do better than go and see the Higginses in the meantime. She might then possibly hear whether Nicholas had been able to make his application to Mr. Thornton.\n\nShe found Nicholas busily engaged in making a penny spin on the dresser, for the amusement of three little children, who were clinging to him in a fearless manner. He, as well as they, was smiling at a good long spin; and Margaret thought, that the happy look of interest in his occupation was a good sign. When the penny stopped spinning, “lile Johnnie” began to cry.\n\n“Come to me,” said Margaret, taking him off the dresser, and holding him in her arms; she held her watch to his ear, while she asked Nicholas if he had seen Mr. Thornton.\n\nThe look on his face changed instantly.\n\n“Ay!” said he. “I’ve seen and heerd too much on him.”\n\n“He refused you, then?” said Margaret, sorrowfully.\n\n“To be sure. I knew he’d do it all along. It’s no good expecting marcy at the hands o’ them measters. Yo’r a stranger and a foreigner, and aren’t likely to know their ways; but I knowed it.”\n\n“I am sorry I asked you. Was he angry? He did not speak to you as Hamper did, did he?”\n\n“He weren’t o’er-civil!” said Nicholas, spinning the penny again, as much for his own amusement as for that of the children. “Never yo’ fret, I’m only where I was. I’ll go on tramp to-morrow. I gave him as good as I got. I telled him, I’d not that good opinion on him that I’d ha’ come a second time of mysel’; but yo’d advised me for to come, and I were beholden to yo’.”\n\n“You told him I sent you?”\n\n“I dunno, if I ca’d yo’ by your name. I dunnot think I did. I said a woman who knew no better had advised me for to come and see if there was a soft place in his heart.”\n\n“And he—?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Said I were to tell yo’ to mind yo’r own business.—That’s the longest spin yet, my lads.—And them’s civil words to what he used to me. But ne’er mind. We’re but where we was; and I’ll break stones on the road afore I let these little uns clem.”\n\nMargaret put the struggling Johnnie out of her arms, back into his former place on the dresser.\n\n“I am sorry I asked you to go to Mr. Thornton’s. I am disappointed in him.”\n\nThere was a slight noise behind her. Both she and Nicholas turned round at the same moment, and there stood Mr. Thornton, with a look of displeased surprise upon his face. Obeying her swift impulse, Margaret passed out before him, saying not a word, only bowing low to hide the sudden paleness that she felt had come over her face. He bent equally low in return, and then closed the door after her. As she hurried to Mrs. Boucher’s, she heard the clang, and it seemed to fill up the measure of her mortification. He too was annoyed to find her there. He had tenderness in his heart—“a soft place,” as Nicholas Higgins called it; but he had some pride in concealing it; he kept it very sacred and safe, and was jealous of every circumstance that tried to gain admission. But if he dreaded exposure of his tenderness, he was equally desirous that all men should recognise his justice; and he felt that he had been unjust in giving so scornful a hearing to any one who had waited, with humble patience, for five hours, to speak to him. That the man had spoken saucily to him when he had the opportunity, was nothing to Mr. Thornton. He rather liked him for it; and he was conscious of his own irritability of temper at the time, which probably made them both quits. It was the five hours of waiting that struck Mr. Thornton. He had not five hours to spare, himself; but one hour—two hours, of his hard penetrating intellectual, as well as bodily labour, did he give up to going about collecting evidence as to the truth of Higgins’s story, the nature of his character, the tenor of his life. He tried not to be, but was convinced that all that Higgins had said was true. And then the conviction went in, as if by some spell, and touched the latent tenderness of his heart; the patience of the man, the simple generosity of the motive (for he had learnt about the quarrel between Boucher and Higgins), made him forget entirely the mere reasonings of justice, and overleap them by a diviner instinct. He came to tell Higgins he would give him work; and he was more annoyed to find Margaret there than by hearing her last words; for then he understood that she was the woman who had urged Higgins to come to him; and he dreaded the admission of any thought of her, as a motive to what he was doing solely because it was right.\n\n“So that was the lady you spoke of as a woman?” said he indignantly to Higgins. “You might have told me who she was.”\n\n“And then, maybe, yo’d have spoken of her more civil than yo’ did; yo’d getten a mother who might ha’ kept yo’r tongue in check when yo’ were talking o’ women being at the root of all the plagues.”\n\n“Of course you told that to Miss Hale?”\n\n“In coorse I did. Leastways, I reckon I did. I telled her she weren’t to meddle again in aught that concerned yo’.”\n\n“Whose children are those—yours?” Mr. Thornton had a pretty good notion whose they were, from what he had heard; but he felt awkward in turning the conversation round from this unpromising beginning.\n\n“They’re not mine, and they are mine.”\n\n“They are the children you spoke of to me this morning?”\n\n“When yo’ said,” replied Higgins, turning round, with ill-smothered fierceness, “that my story might be true or might not, but it were a very unlikely one. Measter, I’ve not forgotten.”\n\nMr. Thornton was silent for a moment; then he said: “No more have I. I remember what I said. I spoke to you about those children in a way I had no business to. I did not believe you. I could not have taken care of another man’s children myself, if he had acted towards me as I hear Boucher did towards you. But I know now you spoke truth. I beg your pardon.”\n\nHiggins did not turn round, or immediately respond to this. But when he did speak, it was in a softened tone, although the words were gruff enough.\n\n“Yo’ve no business to go prying into what happened between Boucher and me. He’s dead, and I’m sorry. That’s enough.”\n\n“So it is. Will you take work with me? That’s what I came to ask.”\n\nHiggins’s obstinacy wavered, recovered strength, and stood firm. He would not speak. Mr. Thornton would not ask again. Higgins’s eye fell on the children.\n\n“Yo’ve called me impudent, and a liar, and a mischief-maker, and yo’ might ha’ said wi’ some truth, as I were now and then given to drink. An’, I ha’ called you a tyrant, an’ an oud bull dog, and a hard, cruel master; that’s where it stands. But for th’ childer, Measter, do yo’ think we can e’er get on together?”\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Thornton, half-laughing, “it was not my proposal that we should go together. But there’s one comfort on your own showing. We neither of us can think much worse of the other than we do now.”\n\n“That’s true,” said Higgins, reflectively. “I’ve been thinking ever sin’ I saw you, what a marcy it were yo’ did na take me on, for that I ne’er saw a man whom I could less abide. But that’s maybe been a hasty judgment; and work’s work to such as me. So, measter, I’ll come; and what’s more, I thank yo’; and that’s a deal fro’ me,” said he, more frankly, suddenly turning round and facing Mr. Thornton fully for the first time.\n\n“And this is a deal from me,” said Mr. Thornton, giving Higgins’s hand a good grip. “Now mind you come sharp to your time,” continued he, resuming the master. “I’ll have no laggards at my mill. What fines we have, we keep pretty sharply. And the first time I catch you making mischief, off you go. So now you know where you are.”\n\n“Yo’ spoke of my wisdom this morning. I reckon I may bring it wi’ me; or would yo’ rayther have me ’bout my brains?”\n\n“’Bout your brains if you use them for meddling with my business; with your brains if you can keep to your own.”\n\n“I shall need a deal o’ brains to settle where my business ends and yo’rs begins.”\n\n“Your business has not begun yet, and mine stands still for me. So good afternoon.”\n\nJust before Mr. Thornton came up to Mrs. Boucher’s door, Margaret came out of it. She did not see him; and he followed her for several yards, admiring her light and easy walk, and her tall and graceful figure. But, suddenly, this simple emotion of pleasure was tainted, poisoned by jealousy. He wished to overtake her, and speak to her, to see how she would receive him, now she must know he was aware of some other attachment. He wished too, but of this wish he was rather ashamed, that she should know that he had justified her wisdom in sending Higgins to him to ask for work, and had repented him of his morning’s decision. He came up to her. She started.\n\n“Allow me to say, Miss Hale, that you were rather premature in expressing your disappointment. I have taken Higgins on.”\n\n“I am glad of it,” said she coldly.\n\n“He tells me, he repeated to you, what I said this morning about—,” Mr. Thornton hesitated. Margaret took it up:\n\n“About women not meddling. You had a perfect right to express your opinion, which was a very correct one, I have no doubt. But,” she went on a little more eagerly, “Higgins did not quite tell you the exact truth.” The word “truth,” reminded her of her own untruth, and she stopped short, feeling exceedingly uncomfortable.\n\nMr. Thornton at first was puzzled to account for her silence; and then he remembered the lie she had told, and all that was foregone. “The exact truth!” said he. “Very few people do speak the exact truth. I have given up hoping for it. Miss Hale, have you no explanation to give me? You must perceive what I cannot but think.”\n\nMargaret was silent. She was wondering whether an explanation of any kind would be consistent with her loyalty to Frederick.\n\n“Nay,” said he, “I will ask no farther. I may be putting temptation in your way. At present, believe me, your secret is safe with me. But you run great risks, allow me to say, in being so indiscreet. I am only speaking as a friend of your father’s: if I had any other thought or hope, of course that is at an end. I am quite disinterested.”\n\n“I am aware of that,” said Margaret, forcing herself to speak in an indifferent, careless way. “I am aware of what I must appear to you, but the secret is another person’s, and I cannot explain it without doing him harm.”\n\n“I have not the slightest wish to pry into the gentleman’s secrets,” he said, with growing anger. “My own interest in you is—simply that of a friend. You may not believe me, Miss Hale, but it is—in spite of the persecution I’m afraid I threatened you with at one time—but that is all given up; all passed away. You believe me, Miss Hale?”\n\n“Yes,” said Margaret, quietly and sadly.\n\n“Then, really, I don’t see any reason for us to go on walking together. I thought, perhaps, you might have had something to say, but I see we are nothing to each other. If you’re quite convinced, that any foolish passion on my part is entirely over, I wish you good afternoon.” He walked off very hastily.\n\n“What can he mean?” thought Margaret,—“what could he mean by speaking so, as if I were always thinking that he cared for me, when I know he does not; he cannot. His mother will have said all those cruel things about me to him. But I won’t care for him. I surely am mistress enough of myself to control this wild, strange, miserable feeling, which tempted me even to betray my own dear Frederick, so that I might but regain his good opinion—the good opinion of a man who takes such pains to tell me that I am nothing to him. Come! poor little heart! be cheery and brave. We’ll be a great deal to one another, if we are thrown off and left desolate.”\n\nHer father was almost startled by her merriment this morning. She talked incessantly, and forced her natural humour to an unusual pitch; and if there was a tinge of bitterness in much of what she said; if her accounts of the old Harley Street set were a little sarcastic, her father could not bear to check her, as he would have done at another time—for he was glad to see her shake off her cares. In the middle of the evening, she was called down to speak to Mary Higgins; and when she came back, Mr. Hale imagined that he saw traces of tears on her cheeks. But that could not be, for she brought good news—that Higgins had got work at Mr. Thornton’s mill. Her spirits were damped, at any rate, and she found it very difficult to go on talking at all, much more in the wild way that she had done. For some days her spirits varied strangely; and her father was beginning to be anxious about her, when news arrived from one or two quarters that promised some change and variety for her. Mr. Hale received a letter from Mr. Bell, in which that gentleman volunteered a visit to them; and Mr. Hale imagined that the promised society of his old Oxford friend would give as agreeable a turn to Margaret’s ideas as it did to his own. Margaret tried to take an interest in what pleased her father; but she was too languid to care about any Mr. Bell, even though he were twenty times her godfather. She was more roused by a letter from Edith, full of sympathy about her aunt’s death; full of details about herself, her husband, and child; and at the end saying, that as the climate did not suit the baby, and as Mrs. Shaw was talking of returning to England, she thought it probable that Captain Lennox might sell out, and that they might all go and live again in the old Harley Street house; which, however, would seem very incomplete without Margaret. Margaret yearned after that old house, and the placid tranquility of that well-ordered, monotonous life. She had found it occasionally tiresome while it lasted; but since then she had been buffeted about, and felt so exhausted by this recent struggle with herself, that she thought that even stagnation would be a rest and a refreshment. So she began to look towards a long visit to the Lennoxes, on their return to England, as to a point—no, not of hope—but of leisure, in which she could regain her power and command over herself. At present it seemed to her as if all subjects tended towards Mr. Thornton; as if she could not forget him with all her endeavours. If she went to see the Higginses, she heard of him there; her father had resumed their readings together, and quoted his opinions perpetually; even Mr. Bell’s visit brought his tenant’s name upon the tapis; for he wrote word, that he believed he must be occupied some great part of his time with Mr. Thornton, as a new lease was in preparation, and the terms of it must be agreed upon.\n\n# CHAPTER XL\n\n“I have no wrong, where I can claim no right, Naught ta’en me fro, where I have nothing had, Yet of my woe I cannot so be quite; Namely, since that another may be glad With that, that thus in sorrow makes me sad.” WYATT.\n\nMargaret had not expected much pleasure to herself from Mr. Bell’s visit—she had only looked forward to it on her father’s account, but when her godfather came, she at once fell into the most natural position of friendship in the world. He said she had no merit in being what she was, a girl so entirely after his own heart: it was an hereditary power which she had, to walk in and take possession of his regard; while she, in reply, gave him much credit for being so fresh and young under his Fellow’s cap and gown.\n\n“Fresh and young in warmth and kindness, I mean. I’m afraid I must own, that I think your opinions are the oldest and mustiest I have met with this long time.”\n\n“Hear this daughter of yours, Hale! Her residence in Milton has quite corrupted her. She’s a democrat, a red republican, a member of the Peace Society, a socialist—”\n\n“Papa, it’s all because I’m standing up for the progress of commerce. Mr. Bell would have had it keep still at exchanging wild-beast skins for acorns.”\n\n“No, no. I’d dig the ground and grow potatoes. And I’d shave the wild-beast skin and make the wool into broadcloth. Don’t exaggerate, missy. But I’m tired of this bustle. Everybody rushing over everybody, in their hurry to get rich.”\n\n“It is not everyone who can sit comfortably in a set of college rooms and let his riches grow without any exertion of his own. No doubt there is many a man here who would be thankful if his property would increase as yours has done, without his taking any trouble about it,” said Mr. Hale.\n\n“I don’t believe they would. It’s the bustle and the struggle they like. As for sitting still, and learning from the past, or shaping out the future by faithful work done in a prophetic spirit—Why! Pooh! I don’t believe there’s a man in Milton who knows how to sit still; and it is a great art.”\n\n“Milton people, I suspect, think Oxford men don’t know how to move. It would be a very good thing if they mixed a little more.”\n\n“It might be good for the Miltoners. Many things might be good for them which would be very disagreeable for other people.”\n\n“Are you not a Milton man yourself?” asked Margaret. “I should have thought you would have been proud of your town.”\n\n“I confess, I don’t see what there is to be proud of. If you’ll only come to Oxford, Margaret, I will show you a place to glory in.”\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Hale, “Mr. Thornton is coming to drink tea with us to-night, and he is as proud of Milton as you of Oxford. You two must try and make each other a little more liberal-minded.”\n\n“I don’t want to be more liberal-minded, thank you,” said Mr. Bell.\n\n“Is Mr. Thornton coming to tea, papa!” asked Margaret in a low voice.\n\n“Either to tea or soon after. He could not tell. He told us not to wait.”\n\nMr. Thornton had determined that he would make no inquiry of his mother as to how far she had put her project into execution of speaking to Margaret about the impropriety of her conduct. He felt pretty sure that, if this interview took place, his mother’s account of what passed at it would only annoy and chagrin him, though he would all the time be aware of the colouring which it received by passing through her mind. He shrank from hearing Margaret’s very name mentioned; he, while he blamed her—while he was jealous of her—while he renounced her—he loved her sorely, in spite of himself. He dreamt of her; he dreamt she came dancing towards him with outspread arms, and with a lightness and gaiety which made him loathe her, even while it allured him. But the impression of this figure of Margaret—with all Margaret’s character taken out of it, as completely as if some evil spirit had got possession of her form—was so deeply stamped upon his imagination, that when he wakened he felt hardly able to separate the Una from the Duessa; and the dislike he had to the latter seemed to envelop and disfigure the former. Yet he was too proud to acknowledge his weakness by avoiding the sight of her. He would neither seek an opportunity of being in her company nor avoid it. To convince himself of his power of self-control, he lingered over every piece of business this afternoon; he forced every movement into unnatural slowness and deliberation; and it was consequently past eight o’clock before he reached Mr. Hale’s. Then there were business arrangements to be transacted in the study with Mr. Bell; and the latter kept on, sitting over the fire, and talking wearily, long after all business was transacted, and when they might just as well have gone upstairs. But Mr. Thornton would not say a word about moving their quarters; he chafed and chafed, and thought Mr. Bell a most prosy companion; while Mr. Bell returned the compliment in secret, by considering Mr. Thornton about as brusque and curt a fellow as he had ever met with, and terribly gone off both in intelligence and manner. At last some slight noise in the room above suggested the desirableness of moving there. They found Margaret with a letter open before her, eagerly discussing its contents with her father. On the entrance of the gentlemen, it was immediately put aside; but Mr. Thornton’s eager senses caught some few words of Mr. Hale’s to Mr. Bell.\n\n“A letter from Henry Lennox. It makes Margaret very hopeful.”\n\nMr. Bell nodded. Margaret was red as a rose when Mr. Thornton looked at her. He had the greatest mind in the world to get up and go out of the room that very instant, and never set foot in the house again.\n\n“We were thinking,” said Mr. Hale, “that you and Mr. Thornton had taken Margaret’s advice, and were each trying to convert the other, you were so long in the study.”\n\n“And you thought there would be nothing left of us but an opinion, like the Kilkenny cat’s tail. Pray whose opinion did you think would have the most obstinate vitality?”\n\nMr. Thornton had not a notion what they were talking about, and disdained to inquire. Mr. Hale politely enlightened him.\n\n“Mr. Thornton, we were accusing Mr. Bell this morning of a kind of Oxonian mediæval bigotry against his native town; and we—Margaret, I believe—suggested that it would do him good to associate a little with Milton manufacturers.”\n\n“I beg your pardon. Margaret thought it would do the Milton manufacturers good to associate a little more with Oxford men. Now wasn’t it so, Margaret?”\n\n“I believe I thought it would do both good to see a little more of the other,—I did not know it was my idea any more than papa’s.”\n\n“And so you see, Mr. Thornton, we ought to have been improving each other down-stairs, instead of talking over vanished families of Smith’s and Harrison’s. However, I am willing to do my part now. I wonder when you Milton men intend to live. All your lives seem to be spent in gathering together the materials for life.”\n\n“By living, I suppose you mean enjoyment.”\n\n“Yes, enjoyment,—I don’t specify of what, because I trust we should both consider mere pleasure as very poor enjoyment.”\n\n“I would rather have the nature of the enjoyment defined.”\n\n“Well! enjoyment or leisure—enjoyment of the power and influence which money gives. You are all striving for money. What do you want it for?”\n\nMr. Thornton was silent. Then he said, “I really don’t know. But money is not what I strive for.”\n\n“What then?”\n\n“It is a home question. I shall have to lay myself open to such a catechist, and I am not sure that I am prepared to do it.”\n\n“No!” said Mr. Hale; “don’t let us be personal in our catechism. You are neither of you representative men; you are each of you too individual for that.”\n\n“I am not sure whether to consider that as a compliment or not. I should like to be the representative of Oxford, with its beauty and its learning, and its proud old history. What do you say, Margaret; ought I to be flattered?”\n\n“I don’t know Oxford. But there is a difference between being the representative of a city and the representative man of its inhabitants.”\n\n“Very true, Miss Margaret. Now I remember, you were against me this morning, and were quite Miltonian and manufacturing in your preferences.”\n\nMargaret saw the quick glance of surprise that Mr. Thornton gave her, and she was annoyed at the construction which he might put on this speech of Mr. Bell’s. Mr. Bell went on—\n\n“Ah! I wish I could show you our High Street—our Radcliffe Square. I am leaving out our colleges, just as I give Mr. Thornton leave to omit his factories in speaking of the charms of Milton. I have a right to abuse my birth-place. Remember I am a Milton man.”\n\nMr. Thornton was annoyed more than he ought to have been at all that Mr. Bell was saying. He was not in a mood for joking. At another time, he could have enjoyed Mr. Bell’s half testy condemnation of a town where the life was so at variance with every habit he had formed; but now, he was galled enough to attempt to defend what was never meant to be seriously attacked.\n\n“I don’t set up Milton as a model of a town.”\n\n“Not in architecture?” slyly asked Mr. Bell.\n\n“No! We’ve been too busy to attend to mere outward appearances.”\n\n“Don’t say mere outward appearances,” said Mr. Hale, gently. “They impress us all, from childhood upward—every day of our life.”\n\n“Wait a little while,” said Mr. Thornton. “Remember we are of a different race from the Greeks, to whom beauty was everything, and to whom Mr. Bell might speak of a life of leisure and serene enjoyment, much of which entered in through their outward senses. I don’t mean to despise them, any more than I would ape them. But I belong to Teutonic blood; it is little mingled in this part of England to what it is in others; we retain much of their language; we retain more of their spirit; we do not look upon life as a time for enjoyment, but as a time for action and exertion. Our glory and our beauty arise out of our outward strength, which makes us victorious over material resistance, and over greater difficulties still. We are Teutonic up here in Darkshire in another way. We hate to have laws made for us at a distance. We wish people would allow us to right ourselves, instead of continually meddling, with their imperfect legislation. We stand up for self-government, and oppose centralisation.”\n\n“In short, you would like the Heptarchy back again. Well, at any rate, I revoke what I said this morning—that you Milton people did not reverence the past. You are regular worshippers of Thor.”\n\n“If we do not reverence the past as you do in Oxford, it is because we want something which can apply to the present more directly. It is fine when the study of the past leads to a prophecy of the future. But to men groping in new circumstances, it would be finer if the words of experience could direct us how to act in what concerns us most intimately and immediately; which is full of difficulties to be encountered; and upon the mode in which they are met and conquered—not merely pushed aside for the time—depends our future. But no! People can speak of Utopia much more easily than of the next day’s duty; and yet when that duty is all done by others, who so ready to cry, ‘Fie, for shame!’”\n\n“And all this time I don’t see what you are talking about. Would you Milton men condescend to send up your to-day’s difficulty to Oxford? You have not tried us yet.”\n\nMr. Thornton laughed outright at this. “I believe I was talking with reference to a good deal that has been troubling us of late; I was thinking of the strikes we have gone through, which are troublesome and injurious things enough, as I am finding to my cost. And yet this last strike, under which I am smarting, has been respectable.”\n\n“A respectable strike!” said Mr. Bell. “That sounds as if you were far gone in the worship of Thor.”\n\nMargaret felt, rather than saw, that Mr. Thornton was chagrined by the repeated turning into jest of what he was feeling as very serious. She tried to change the conversation from a subject about which one party cared little, while, to the other, it was deeply, because personally, interesting. She forced herself to say something.\n\n“Edith says she finds the printed calicoes in Corfu better and cheaper than in London.”\n\n“Does she?” said her father, “I think that must be one of Edith’s exaggerations. Are you sure of it, Margaret?”\n\n“I am sure she says so, papa.”\n\n“Then I am sure of the fact,” said Mr. Bell. “Margaret, I go so far in my idea of your truthfulness, that it shall cover your cousin’s character. I don’t believe a cousin of yours could exaggerate.”\n\n“Is Miss Hale so remarkable for truth?” said Mr. Thornton, bitterly. The moment he had done so, he could have bitten his tongue out. What was he? And why should he stab her with her shame in this way? How evil he was to-night: possessed by ill-humour at being detained so long from her; irritated by the mention of some name, because he thought it belonged to a more successful lover; now ill-tempered because he had been unable to cope, with a light heart, against one who was trying, by gay and careless speeches, to make the evening pass pleasantly away,—the kind old friend to all parties, whose manner by this time might be well known to Mr. Thornton, who had been acquainted with him for many years. And then to speak to Margaret as he had done! She did not get up and leave the room, as she had done in former days, when his abruptness or his temper had annoyed her. She sat quite still, after the first momentary glance of grieved surprise, that made her eyes look like some child’s who has met with an unexpected rebuff; they slowly dilated into mournful, reproachful sadness; and then they fell, and she bent over her work, and did not speak again. But he could not help looking at her, and he saw a sigh tremble over her body, as if she quivered in some unwonted chill. He felt as the mother would have done, in the midst of “her rocking it, and rating it,” had she been called away before her slow confiding smile, implying perfect trust in mother’s love, had proved the renewing of its love. He gave short sharp answers; he was uneasy and cross, unable to discern between jest and earnest; anxious only for a look, a word of hers, before which to prostrate himself in penitent humility. But she neither looked nor spoke. Her round taper fingers flew in and out of her sewing, as steadily and swiftly as if that were the business of her life. She could not care for him, he thought, or else the passionate fervour of his wish would have forced her to raise those eyes, if but for an instant, to read the late repentance in his. He could have struck her before he left, in order that by some strange overt act of rudeness, he might earn the privilege of telling her the remorse that gnawed at his heart. It was well that the long walk in the open air wound up this evening for him. It sobered him back into grave resolution that henceforth he would see as little of her as possible,—since the very sight of that face and form, the very sounds of that voice (like the soft winds of pure melody) had such power to move him from his balance. Well! He had known what love was—a sharp pang, a fierce experience, in the midst of whose flames he was struggling! but, through the furnace he would fight his way out into the serenity of middle age,—all the richer and more human for having known this great passion.\n\nWhen he had somewhat abruptly left the room, Margaret rose from her seat, and began silently to fold up her work. The long seams were heavy, and had an unusual weight for her languid arms. The round lines in her face took a lengthened, straighter form, and her whole appearance was that of one who had gone through a day of great fatigue. As the three prepared for bed, Mr. Bell muttered a little condemnation of Mr. Thornton.\n\n“I never saw a fellow so spoiled by success. He can’t bear a word; a jest of any kind. Everything seems to touch on the soreness of his high dignity. Formerly, he was as simple and noble as the open day; you could not offend him, because he had no vanity.”\n\n“He is not vain now,” said Margaret, turning round from the table, and speaking with quiet distinctness. “To-night he has not been like himself. Something must have annoyed him before he came here.”\n\nMr. Bell gave her one of his sharp glances from above his spectacles. She stood it quite calmly; but, after she had left the room, he suddenly asked,—\n\n“Hale! did it ever strike you that Thornton and your daughter have what the French call a tendresse for each other?”\n\n“Never!” said Mr. Hale, first startled and then flurried by the new idea. “No, I am sure you are wrong, I am almost certain you are mistaken. If there is anything, it is all on Mr. Thornton’s side. Poor fellow! I hope and trust he is not thinking of her, for I am sure she would not have him.”\n\n“Well! I’m a bachelor, and have steered clear of love affairs all my life; so perhaps my opinion is not worth having. Or else I should say there were very pretty symptoms about her!”\n\n“Then I am sure you are wrong,” said Mr. Hale. “He may care for her, though she really has been almost rude to him at times. But she!—why, Margaret would never think of him, I’m sure. Such a thing has never entered her head.”\n\n“Entering her heart would do. But I merely threw out a suggestion of what might be. I dare say I was wrong. And whether I was wrong or right, I’m very sleepy; so, having disturbed your night’s rest (as I can see) with my untimely fancies, I’ll betake myself with an easy mind to my own.”\n\nBut Mr. Hale resolved that he would not be disturbed by any such nonsensical idea; so he lay awake, determining not to think about it.\n\nMr. Bell took his leave the next day, bidding Margaret look to him as one who had a right to help and protect her in all her troubles, of whatever nature they might be. To Mr. Hale he said,—\n\n“That Margaret of yours has gone deep into my heart. Take care of her, for she is a very precious creature,—a great deal too good for Milton—only fit for Oxford, in fact. The town, I mean; not the men. I can’t match her yet. When I can, I shall bring my young man to stand side by side with your young woman, just as the genii in the Arabian Nights brought Prince Caralmazan to match with the fairy’s Princess Badoura.”\n\n“I beg you’ll do no such thing. Remember the misfortunes that ensued; and besides, I can’t spare Margaret.”\n\n“No; on second thoughts, we’ll have her to nurse us ten years hence, when we shall be two cross old invalids. Seriously, Hale! I wish you’d leave Milton; which is a most unsuitable place for you, though it was my recommendation in the first instance. If you would, I’d swallow my shadows of doubts, and take a college living; and you and Margaret should come and live at the parsonage—you to be a sort of lay curate, and take the unwashed off my hands; and she to be our housekeeper—the village Lady Bountiful—by day; and read us to sleep in the evenings. I could be very happy in such a life. What do you think of it?”\n\n“Never,” said Mr. Hale, decidedly. “My one great change has been made and my price of suffering paid. Here I stay out my life; and here will I be buried, and lost in the crowd.”\n\n“I don’t give up my plan yet. Only I won’t bait you with it any more just now. Where’s the Pearl. Come, Margaret, give me a farewell kiss; and remember, my dear, where you may find a true friend, as far as his capability goes. You are my child, Margaret. Remember that, and God bless you!”\n\nSo they fell back into the monotony of the quiet life they would henceforth lead. There was no invalid to hope and fear about; even the Higginses—so long a vivid interest—seemed to have receded from any need of immediate thought. The Boucher children, left motherless orphans, claimed what of Margaret’s care she could bestow; and she went pretty often to see Mary Higgins, who had charge of them. The two families were living in one house: the elder children were at humble schools, the younger ones were tended, in Mary’s absence at her work, by the kind neighbour whose good sense had struck Margaret at the time of Boucher’s death. Of course she was paid for her trouble; and, indeed, in all his little plans and arrangements for these orphan children, Nicholas showed a sober judgment, and regulated method of thinking, which were at variance with his former more eccentric jerks of action. He was so steady at his work, that Margaret did not often see him during these winter months; but when she did, she saw that he winced away from any reference to the father of those children, whom he had so fully and heartily taken under his care. He did not speak easily of Mr. Thornton.\n\n“To tell the truth,” said he, “he fairly bamboozles me. He’s two chaps. One chap I knowed of old as were measter all o’er. T’other chap hasn’t an ounce of measter’s flesh about him. How them two chaps is bound up in one body, is a craddy for me to find out. I’ll not be beat by it, though. Meanwhile he comes here pretty often; that’s how I know the chap that’s a man, not a measter. And I reckon he’s taken aback by me pretty much as I am by him; for he sits and listens and stares, as if I were some strange beast newly caught in some of the zones. But I’m none daunted. It would take a deal to daunt me in my own house, as he sees. And I tell him some of my mind that I reckon he’d ha’ been the better of hearing when he were a younger man.”\n\n“And does he not answer you?” asked Mr. Hale.\n\n“Well! I’ll not say th’ advantage is all on his side, for all I take credit for improving him above a bit. Sometimes he says a rough thing or two, which is not agreeable to look at at first, but has a queer smack o’ truth in it when yo’ come to chew it. He’ll be coming to-night, I reckon, about them childer’s schooling. He’s not satisfied wi’ the make of it, and wants for t’ examine ’em.”\n\n“What are they”—began Mr. Hale; but Margaret, touching his arm, showed him her watch.\n\n“It is nearly seven,” she said. “The evenings are getting longer now. Come, papa.” She did not breathe freely till they were some distance from the house. Then, as she became more calm, she wished that she had not been in so great a hurry; for, somehow, they saw Mr. Thornton but very seldom now; and he might have come to see Higgins, and for the old friendship’s sake she should like to have seen him to-night.\n\nYes! he came very seldom, even for the dull cold purpose of lessons. Mr. Hale was disappointed in his pupil’s lukewarmness about Greek literature, which had but a short time ago so great an interest for him. And now it often happened that a hurried note from Mr. Thornton would arrive, just at the last moment, saying that he was so much engaged that he could not come to read with Mr. Hale that evening. And though other pupils had taken more than his place as to time, no one was like his first scholar in Mr. Hale’s heart. He was depressed and sad at this partial cessation of an intercourse which had become dear to him; and he used to sit pondering over the reason that could have occasioned this change.\n\nHe startled Margaret, one evening as she sate at her work, by suddenly asking:\n\n“Margaret! had you ever any reason for thinking that Mr. Thornton cared for you?”\n\nHe almost blushed as he put this question; but Mr. Bell’s scouted idea recurred to him, and the words were out of his mouth before he well knew what he was about.\n\nMargaret did not answer immediately; but by the bent drooping of her head, he guessed what her reply would be.\n\n“Yes; I believe—oh, papa, I should have told you.” And she dropped her work, and hid her face in her hands.\n\n“No, dear; don’t think that I am impertinently curious. I am sure you would have told me if you had felt that you could return his regard. Did he speak to you about it?”\n\nNo answer at first; but by-and-by a little gentle reluctant “Yes.”\n\n“And you refused him?”\n\nA long sigh: a more helpless, nerveless attitude, and another “Yes.” But before her father could speak, Margaret lifted up her face, rosy with some beautiful shame, and, fixing her eyes upon him, said:\n\n“Now, papa, I have told you all this, and I cannot tell you more; and then the whole thing is so painful to me; every word and action connected with it is so unspeakably bitter, that I cannot bear to think of it. Oh, papa, I am sorry to have lost you this friend, but I could not help it—but oh! I am very sorry.” She sate down on the ground, and laid her head on his knees.\n\n“I, too, am sorry, my dear. Mr. Bell quite startled me when he said, some idea of the kind—”\n\n“Mr. Bell! Oh did Mr. Bell see it?”\n\n“A little; but he took it into his head that you—how shall I say it?—that you were not ungraciously disposed towards Mr. Thornton. I knew that could never be. I hoped the whole thing was but an imagination; but I knew too well what your real feelings were to suppose that you could ever like Mr. Thornton in that way. But I am very sorry.”\n\nThey were very quiet and still for some minutes. But, on stroking her cheek in a caressing way soon after, he was almost shocked to find her face wet with tears. As he touched her, she sprang up, and smiling with forced brightness, began to talk of the Lennoxes with such a vehement desire to turn the conversation, that Mr. Hale was too tender-hearted to try to force it back into the old channel.\n\n“To-morrow—yes, to-morrow they will be back in Harley Street. Oh, how strange it will be! I wonder what room they will make into the nursery? Aunt Shaw will be happy with the baby. Fancy Edith a mamma! And Captain Lennox—I wonder what he will do with himself now he has sold out!”\n\n“I’ll tell you what,” said her father, anxious to indulge her in this fresh subject of interest, “I think I must spare you for a fortnight just to run up to town and see the travellers. You could learn more by half an hour’s conversation with Mr. Henry Lennox about Frederick’s chances, than in a dozen of these letters of his; so it would, in fact, be uniting business with pleasure.”\n\n“No, papa, you cannot spare me, and what’s more, I won’t be spared.” Then after a pause, she added: “I am losing hope sadly about Frederick; he is letting us down gently, but I can see that Mr. Lennox himself has no hope of hunting up the witnesses under years and years of time. No,” said she, “that bubble was very pretty, and very dear to our hearts; but it has burst like many another; and we must console ourselves with being glad that Frederick is so happy, and with being a great deal to each other. So don’t offend me by talking of being able to spare me, papa, for I assure you you can’t.”\n\nBut the idea of a change took root and germinated in Margaret’s heart, although not in the way in which her father proposed it at first. She began to consider how desirable something of the kind would be to her father, whose spirits, always feeble, now became too frequently depressed, and whose health, though he never complained, had been seriously affected by his wife’s illness and death. There were the regular hours of reading with his pupils, but that all giving and no receiving could no longer be called companionship, as in the old days when Mr. Thornton came to study under him. Margaret was conscious of the want under which he was suffering, unknown to himself; the want of a man’s intercourse with men. At Helstone there had been perpetual occasion for an interchange of visits with neighbouring clergymen; and the poor labourers in the fields, or leisurely tramping home at eve, or tending their cattle in the forest, were always at liberty to speak or be spoken to. But in Milton every one was too busy for quiet speech, or any ripened intercourse of thought; what they said was about business, very present and actual; and when the tension of mind relating to their daily affairs was over, they sunk into fallow rest until next morning. The workman was not to be found after the day’s work was done; he had gone away to some lecture, or some club, or some beershop, according to his degree of character. Mr. Hale thought of trying to deliver a course of lectures at some of the institutions, but he contemplated doing this so much as an effort of duty, and with so little of the genial impulse of love towards his work and its end, that Margaret was sure that it would not be well done until he could look upon it with some kind of zest.\n\n# CHAPTER XLI\n\nI see my way as birds their trackless way— I shall arrive! what time, what circuit first, I ask not: but unless God sends his hail Or blinding fire-balls, sleet, or stifling snow, In some time—his good time—I shall arrive; He guides me and the bird. In His good time! BROWNING’S PARACELSUS.\n\nSo the winter was getting on, and the days were beginning to lengthen, without bringing with them any of the brightness of hope which usually accompanies the rays of a February sun. Mrs. Thornton had of course entirely ceased to come to the house. Mr. Thornton came occasionally, but his visits were addressed to her father, and were confined to the study. Mr. Hale spoke of him as always the same; indeed, the very rarity of their intercourse seemed to make Mr. Hale set only the higher value on it. And from what Margaret could gather of what Mr. Thornton had said, there was nothing in the cessation of his visits which could arise from any umbrage or vexation. His business affairs had become complicated during the strike, and required closer attention than he had given to them last winter. Nay, Margaret could even discover that he spoke from time to time of her, and always, as far as she could learn, in the same calm friendly way, never avoiding and never seeking any mention of her name.\n\nShe was not in spirits to raise her father’s tone of mind. The dreary peacefulness of the present time had been preceded by so long a period of anxiety and care—even intermixed with storms—that her mind had lost its elasticity. She tried to find herself occupation in teaching the two younger Boucher children, and worked hard at goodness; hard, I say most truly, for her heart seemed dead to the end of all her efforts; and though she made them punctually and painfully, yet she stood as far off as ever from any cheerfulness; her life seemed still bleak and dreary. The only thing she did well, was what she did out of unconscious piety, the silent comforting and consoling of her father. Not a mood of his but what found a ready sympathiser in Margaret; not a wish of his that she did not strive to forecast, and to fulfil. They were quiet wishes to be sure, and hardly named without hesitation and apology. All the more complete and beautiful was her meek spirit of obedience. March brought the news of Frederick’s marriage. He and Dolores wrote; she in Spanish-English, as was but natural, and he with little turns and inversions of words which proved how far the idioms of his bride’s country were infecting him.\n\nOn the receipt of Henry Lennox’s letter, announcing how little hope there was of his ever clearing himself at a court-martial, in the absence of the missing witnesses, Frederick had written to Margaret a pretty vehement letter, containing his renunciation of England as his country; he wished he could unnative himself, and declared that he would not take his pardon if it were offered him, nor live in the country if he had permission to do so. All of which made Margaret cry sorely, so unnatural did it seem to her at the first opening; but on consideration, she saw rather in such expression the poignancy of the disappointment which had thus crushed his hopes; and she felt that there was nothing for it but patience. In the next letter Frederick spoke so joyfully of the future that he had no thought for the past; and Margaret found a use in herself for the patience she had been craving for him. She would have to be patient. But the pretty, timid, girlish letters of Dolores were beginning to have a charm for both Margaret and her father. The young Spaniard was so evidently anxious to make a favourable impression upon her lover’s English relations, that her feminine care peeped out at every erasure; and the letters announcing the marriage, were accompanied by a splendid black lace mantilla, chosen by Dolores herself for her unseen sister-in-law, whom Frederick had represented as a paragon of beauty, wisdom and virtue. Frederick’s worldly position was raised by this marriage on to as high a level as they could desire. Barbour and Co. was one of the most extensive Spanish houses, and into it he was received as a junior partner. Margaret smiled a little and then sighed as she remembered afresh her old tirades against trade. Here was her preux chevalier of a brother turned merchant, trader! But then she rebelled against herself, and protested silently against the confusion implied between a Spanish merchant and a Milton millowner. Well, trade or no trade, Frederick was very, very happy. Dolores must be charming, and the mantilla was exquisite! And then she returned to the present life.\n\nHer father had occasionally experienced a difficulty in breathing this spring, which had for the time distressed him exceedingly. Margaret was less alarmed, as this difficulty went off completely in the intervals; but she still was so desirous of his shaking off the liability altogether, as to make her very urgent that he should accept Mr. Bell’s invitation to visit him at Oxford this April. Mr. Bell’s invitation included Margaret. Nay more, he wrote a special letter commanding her to come; but she felt as if it would be a greater relief to her to remain quietly at home, entirely free from any responsibility whatever, and so to rest her mind and heart in a manner which she had not been able to do for more than two years past.\n\nWhen her father had driven off on his way to the railroad, Margaret felt how great and long had been the pressure on her time and her spirits. It was astonishing, almost stunning, to feel herself so much at liberty; no one depending on her for cheering care, if not for positive happiness; no invalid to plan and think for; she might be idle, and silent and forgetful,—and what seemed worth more than all the other privileges—she might be unhappy if she liked. For months past, all her own personal cares and troubles had had to be stuffed away into a dark cupboard; but now she had leisure to take them out, and mourn over them, and study their nature, and seek the true method of subduing them into the elements of peace. All these weeks she had been conscious of their existence in a dull kind of way, though they were hidden out of sight. Now, once for all she would consider them and appoint to each of them its right work in her life. So she sat almost motionless for hours in the drawing-room, going over the bitterness of every remembrance with an unwincing resolution. Only once she cried aloud, at the stinging thought of the faithlessness which gave birth to that abasing falsehood.\n\nShe now would not even acknowledge the force of the temptation; her plans for Frederick had all failed, and the temptation lay there a dead mockery,—a mockery which had never had life in it; the lie had been so despicably foolish, seen by the light of the ensuing events, and faith in the power of truth so infinitely the greater wisdom!\n\nIn her nervous agitation, she unconsciously opened a book of her father’s that lay upon the table,—the words that caught her eye in it, seemed almost made for her present state of acute self-abasement:—\n\n“Je ne voudrois pas reprendre mon cœur en ceste sorte: meurs de honte, aveugle, impudent, traistre et desloyal à ton Dieu, et sembables choses; mais je voudrois le corriger par voye de compassion. Or sus, mon pauvre cœur, nous voilà tombe dans la fosse, laquelle nous avions tant resolu d’eschapper. Ah! revelons-nous, et quittons-la pour jamais, reclamons la misericorde de Dieu, et esperons en elle qu’elle nous assistera pour desormais estra plus fermes; et remettons-nous au chemin de l’humilitè. Courage, soyons meshuy sur nos gardes, Dieu nous aydera.”\n\n“The way of humility. Ah,” thought Margaret, “that is what I have missed! But courage, little heart. We will turn back, and by God’s help we may find the lost path.”\n\nSo she rose up, and determined at once to set to on some work which should take her out of herself. To begin with, she called in Martha, as she passed the drawing-room door in going up-stairs, and tried to find out what was below the grave, respectful, servant-like manner, which crusted over her individual character with an obedience which was almost mechanical. She found it difficult to induce Martha to speak of any of her personal interests; but at last she touched the right chord, in naming Mrs. Thornton. Martha’s whole face brightened, and, on a little encouragement, out came a long story, of how her father had been in early life connected with Mrs. Thornton’s husband—nay, had even been in a position to show him some kindness; what, Martha hardly knew, for it had happened when she was quite a little child; and circumstances had intervened to separate the two families until Martha was nearly grown up, when, her father having sunk lower and lower from his original occupation as clerk in a warehouse, and her mother being dead, she and her sister, to use Martha’s own expression, would have been “lost” but for Mrs. Thornton; who sought them out, and thought for them, and cared for them.\n\n“I had had the fever, and was but delicate; and Mrs. Thornton and Mr. Thornton too, they never rested till they had nursed me up in their own house, and sent me to the sea and all. The doctor said the fever was catching, but they cared none for that—only Miss Fanny, and she went a-visiting these folk that she is going to marry into. So though she was afraid at the time, it has all ended well.”\n\n“Miss Fanny going to be married!” exclaimed Margaret.\n\n“Yes; and to a rich gentleman, too, only he’s a deal older than she is. His name is Watson; and his mills are somewhere out beyond Hayleigh; it’s a very good marriage, for all he’s got such gray hair.”\n\nAt this piece of information, Margaret was silent long enough for Martha to recover her propriety, and, with it, her habitual shortness of answer. She swept up the hearth, asked at what time she should prepare tea, and quitted the room with the same wooden face with which she had entered it. Margaret had to pull herself up from indulging a bad trick, which she had lately fallen into, of trying to imagine how every event that she heard of in relation to Mr. Thornton would affect him: whether he would like it or dislike it.\n\nThe next day she had the little Boucher children for their lessons, and took a long walk, and ended by a visit to Mary Higgins. Somewhat to Margaret’s surprise, she found Nicholas already come home from his work; the lengthening light had deceived her as to the lateness of the evening. He too seemed, by his manners, to have entered a little more on the way of humility; he was quieter, and less self-asserting.\n\n“So th’ oud gentleman’s away on his travels, is he?” said he. “Little ’uns telled me so. Eh! but the’re sharp ’uns they are; I a’most think they beat my own wenches for sharpness, though m’appen it’s wrong to say so, and one on ’em in her grave. There’s summut in th’ weather, I reckon, as sets folk a-wandering. My measter, him at th’ shop yonder, is spinning about th’ world somewhere.”\n\n“Is that the reason you’re so soon at home to-night?” asked Margaret innocently.\n\n“Thou know’st nought about it, that’s all,” said he, contemptuously. “I’m not one wi’ two faces—one for my measter, and t’other for his back. I counted a’ th’ clocks in the town striking afore I’d leave my work. No! yon Thornton’s good enough for to fight wi’, but too good for to be cheated. It were you as getten me the place, and I thank yo’ for it. Thornton’s is not a bad mill, as times go. Stand down, lad, and say yo’r pretty hymn to Miss Marget. That’s right; steady on thy legs, and right arm out as straight as a skewer. One to stop, two to stay, three mak’ ready, and four away!”\n\nThe little fellow repeated a Methodist hymn, far above his comprehension in point of language, but of which the swinging rhythm had caught his ear, and which he repeated with all the developed cadence of a member of parliament. When Margaret had duly applauded, Nicholas called for another, and yet another, much to her surprise, as she found him thus oddly and unconsciously led to take an interest in the sacred things which he had formerly scouted.\n\nIt was past the usual tea-time when she reached home; but she had the comfort of feeling that no one had been kept waiting for her; and of thinking her own thoughts while she rested, instead of anxiously watching another person to learn whether to be grave or gay. After tea she resolved to examine a large packet of letters, and pick out those that were to be destroyed.\n\nAmong them she came to four or five of Mr. Henry Lennox’s, relating to Frederick’s affairs; and she carefully read them over again, with the sole intention, when she began, to ascertain exactly on how fine a chance the justification of her brother hung. But when she had finished the last, and weighed the pros and cons, the little personal revelation of character contained in them forced itself on her notice. It was evident enough, from the stiffness of the wording, that Mr. Lennox had never forgotten his relation to her in any interest he might feel in the subject of the correspondence. They were clever letters; Margaret saw that in a twinkling; but she missed out of them all hearty and genial atmosphere. They were to be preserved, however, as valuable; so she laid them carefully on one side. When this little piece of business was ended, she fell into a reverie; and the thought of her absent father ran strangely in Margaret’s head that night. She almost blamed herself for having felt her solitude (and consequently his absence) as a relief; but these two days had set her up afresh, with new strength and brighter hope. Plans which had lately appeared to her in the guise of tasks, now appeared like pleasures. The morbid scales had fallen from her eyes, and she saw her position and her work more truly. If only Mr. Thornton would restore her the lost friendship,—nay, if he would only come from time to time to cheer her father as in former days,—though she should never see him, she felt as if the course of her future life, though not brilliant in prospect, might lie clear and even before her. She sighed as she rose up to go to bed. In spite of the “One step’s enough for me,”—in spite of the one plain duty of devotion to her father,—there lay at her heart an anxiety and a pang of sorrow.\n\nAnd Mr. Hale thought of Margaret, that April evening, just as strangely and as persistently as she was thinking of him. He had been fatigued by going about among his old friends and old familiar places. He had had exaggerated ideas of the change which his altered opinions might make in his friends’ reception of him; but although some of them might have felt shocked or grieved, or indignant at his falling off in the abstract, as soon as they saw the face of the man whom they had once loved, they forgot his opinions in himself; or only remembered them enough to give an additional tender gravity to their manner. For Mr. Hale had not been known to many; he had belonged to one of the smaller colleges, and had always been shy and reserved; but those who in youth had cared to penetrate to the delicacy of thought and feeling that lay below his silence and indecision, took him to their hearts, with something of the protecting kindness which they would have shown to a woman. And the renewal of this kindliness, after the lapse of years, and an interval of so much change, overpowered him more than any roughness or expression of disapproval could have done.\n\n“I’m afraid we’ve done too much,” said Mr. Bell. “You’re suffering now from having lived so long in that Milton air.”\n\n“I am tired,” said Mr. Hale. “But it is not Milton air. I’m fifty-five years of age, and that little fact of itself accounts for any loss of strength.”\n\n“Nonsense! I’m upwards of sixty, and feel no loss of strength, either bodily or mental. Don’t let me hear you talking so. Fifty-five! why, you’re quite a young man.”\n\nMr. Hale shook his head. “These last few years!” said he. But after a minute’s pause, he raised himself from his half recumbent position, in one of Mr. Bell’s luxurious easy-chairs, and said with a kind of trembling earnestness:\n\n“Bell! you’re not to think, that if I could have foreseen all that would come of my change of opinion, and my resignation of my living—no! not even if I could have known how she would have suffered,—that I would undo it—the act of open acknowledgment that I no longer held the same faith as the church in which I was a priest. As I think now, even if I could have foreseen that cruellest martyrdom of suffering, through the sufferings of one whom I loved, I would have done just the same as far as that step of openly leaving the church went. I might have done differently, and acted more wisely, in all that I subsequently did for my family. But I don’t think God endued me with over-much wisdom or strength,” he added, falling back into his old position.\n\nMr. Bell blew his nose ostentatiously before answering. Then he said:\n\n“He gave you strength to do what your conscience told you was right; and I don’t see that we need any higher or holier strength than that; or wisdom either. I know I have not that much; and yet men set me down in their fool’s books as a wise man; an independent character; strong-minded, and all that cant. The veriest idiot who obeys his own simple law of right, if it be but in wiping his shoes on a door-mat, is wiser and stronger than I. But what gulls men are!”\n\nThere was a pause. Mr. Hale spoke first, in continuation of his thought:\n\n“About Margaret.”\n\n“Well! about Margaret. What then?”\n\n“If I die——”\n\n“Nonsense!”\n\n“What will become of her—I often think? I suppose the Lennoxes will ask her to live with them. I try to think they will. Her Aunt Shaw loved her very well in her own quiet way; but she forgets to love the absent.”\n\n“A very common fault. What sort of people are the Lennoxes?”\n\n“He, handsome, fluent, and agreeable. Edith, a sweet spoiled beauty. Margaret loves her with all her heart, and Edith with as much of her heart as she can spare.”\n\n“Now, Hale; you know that girl of yours has got pretty nearly all my heart. I told you that before. Of course, as your daughter, as my god-daughter, I took great interest in her before I saw her the last time. But this visit that I paid to you at Milton made me her slave. I went, a willing old victim, following the car of the conqueror. For, indeed, she looks as grand and serene as one who has struggled, and may be struggling, and yet has the victory secure in sight. Yes, in spite of all her present anxieties, that was the look on her face. And so, all I have is at her service, if she needs it; and will be hers, whether she will or no, when I die. Moreover, I myself, will be her preux chevalier, sixty and gouty though I be. Seriously, old friend, your daughter shall be my principal charge in life, and all the help that either my wit or my wisdom or my willing heart can give, shall be hers. I don’t choose her out as a subject for fretting. Something, I know of old, you must have to worry yourself about, or you wouldn’t be happy. But you’re going to outlive me by many a long year. You, spare, thin men are always tempting and always cheating Death! It’s the stout, florid fellows like me, that always go off first.”\n\nIf Mr. Bell had had a prophetic eye he might have seen the torch all but inverted, and the angel with the grave and composed face standing very nigh, beckoning to his friend. That night Mr. Hale laid his head down upon the pillow on which it never more should stir with life. The servant who entered his room in the morning, received no answer to his speech; drew near the bed, and saw the calm, beautiful face lying white and cold under the ineffaceable seal of death. The attitude was exquisitely easy; there had been no pain—no struggle. The action of the heart must have ceased as he lay down.\n\nMr. Bell was stunned by the shock; and only recovered when the time came for being angry at every suggestion of his man’s.\n\n“A coroner’s inquest? Pooh. You don’t think I poisoned him! Dr. Forbes says it is just the natural end of a heart complaint. Poor old Hale! You wore out that tender heart of yours before its time. Poor old friend! how he talked of his—— Wallis, pack up a carpet-bag for me in five minutes. Here have I been talking. Pack it up, I say. I must go to Milton by the next train.”\n\nThe bag was packed, the cab ordered, the railway reached, in twenty minutes from the time of this decision. The London train whizzed by, drew back some yards, and in Mr. Bell was hurried by the impatient guard. He threw himself back in his seat, to try, with closed eyes, to understand how one in life yesterday could be dead to-day; and shortly tears stole out between his grizzled eye-lashes, at the feeling of which he opened his keen eyes, and looked as severely cheerful as his set determination could make him. He was not going to blubber before a set of strangers. Not he!\n\nThere was no set of strangers, only one sitting far from him on the same side. By-and-by Mr. Bell peered at him, to discover what manner of man it was that might have been observing his emotion; and behind the great sheet of the outspread “Times,” he recognized Mr. Thornton.\n\n“Why, Thornton! is that you?” said he, removing hastily to a closer proximity. He shook Mr. Thornton vehemently by the hand, until the gripe ended in a sudden relaxation, for the hand was wanted to wipe away tears. He had last seen Mr. Thornton in his friend Hale’s company.\n\n“I’m going to Milton, bound on a melancholy errand. Going to break to Hale’s daughter the news of his sudden death!”\n\n“Death! Mr. Hale dead!”\n\n“Ay; I keep saying it to myself, ‘Hale is dead!’ but it doesn’t make it any the more real. Hale is dead for all that. He went to bed well, to all appearance, last night, and was quite cold this morning when my servant went to call him.”\n\n“Where? I don’t understand!”\n\n“At Oxford. He came to stay with me: hadn’t been in Oxford this seventeen years—and this is the end of it.”\n\nNot one word was spoken for above a quarter of an hour. Then Mr. Thornton said:\n\n“And she!” and stopped full short.\n\n“Margaret you mean. Yes! I am going to tell her. Poor fellow! how full his thoughts were of her all last night! Good God! Last night only. And how immeasurably distant he is now! But I take Margaret as my child for his sake. I said last night I would take her for her own sake. Well, I take her for both.”\n\nMr. Thornton made one or two fruitless attempts to speak, before he could get out the words:\n\n“What will become of her?”\n\n“I rather fancy there will be two people waiting for her: myself for one. I would take a live dragon into my house to live, if by hiring such a chaperon, and setting up an establishment of my own, I could make my old age happy with having Margaret for a daughter. But there are those Lennoxes!”\n\n“Who are they?” asked Mr. Thornton with trembling interest.\n\n“Oh, smart London people, who very likely will think that they’ve the best right to her. Captain Lennox married her cousin—the girl she was brought up with. Good enough people, I dare say. And there’s her Aunt, Mrs. Shaw. There might be a way open, perhaps, by my offering to marry that worthy lady! but that would be quite a pis aller. And then there’s that brother!”\n\n“What brother? A brother of her aunt’s?”\n\n“No, no; a clever Lennox (the captain’s a fool, you must understand); a young barrister, who will be setting his cap at Margaret. I know he has had her in his mind these five years or more; one of his chums told me as much; and he was only kept back by want of fortune. Now that will be done away with.”\n\n“How?” asked Mr. Thornton, too earnestly curious to be aware of the impertinence of his question.\n\n“Why, she’ll have my money at my death. And if this Henry Lennox is half good enough for her, and she likes him—well! I might find another way of getting a home through a marriage. I’m dreadfully afraid of being tempted, at an unguarded moment, by her aunt.”\n\nNeither Mr. Bell nor Mr. Thornton was in a laughing humour; so the oddity of any of the speeches which the former made was unnoticed by them. Mr. Bell whistled, without emitting any sound beyond a long hissing breath; changed his seat, without finding comfort or rest; while Mr. Thornton sat immovably still, his eyes fixed on one spot in the newspaper, which he had taken up in order to give himself leisure to think.\n\n“Where have you been?” asked Mr. Bell, at length.\n\n“To Havre. Trying to detect the secret of the great rise in the price of cotton.”\n\n“Ugh! Cotton, and speculations, and smoke, well-cleansed and well-cared-for machinery, and unwashed and neglected hands. Poor old Hale! Poor old Hale! If you could have known the change which it was to him from Helstone. Do you know the New Forest at all?”\n\n“Yes.” (Very shortly.)\n\n“Then you can fancy the difference between it and Milton. What part were you in? Were you ever at Helstone? a little picturesque village, like some in the Odenwald? You know Helstone?”\n\n“I have seen it. It was a great change to leave it and come to Milton.”\n\nHe took up his newspaper with a determined air, as if resolved to avoid further conversation; and Mr. Bell was fain to resort to his former occupation of trying to find out how he could best break the news to Margaret.\n\nShe was at an upstairs window; she saw him alight; she guessed the truth with an instinctive flash. She stood in the middle of the drawing-room, as if arrested in her first impulse to rush downstairs, and as if by the same restraining thought she had been turned to stone; so white and immovable was she.\n\n“Oh! don’t tell me! I know it from your face! You would have sent—you would not have left him—if he were alive! Oh, papa, papa!”\n\n# CHAPTER XLII\n\n“When some beloved voice that was to you Both sound and sweetness, faileth suddenly, And silence, against which you dare not cry, Aches round you like a strong disease and new,— What hope? what help? what music will undo That silence to your sense?” MRS. BROWNING.\n\nThe shock had been great. Margaret fell into a state of prostration, which did not show itself in sobs and tears, or even find the relief of words. She lay on the sofa with her eyes shut, never speaking but when spoken to, and then replying in whispers. Mr. Bell was perplexed. He dared not leave her; he dared not ask her to accompany him back to Oxford, which had been one of the plans he had formed on the journey to Milton, her physical exhaustion was evidently too complete for her to undertake any such fatigue—putting the sight that she would have to encounter out of the question. Mr. Bell sate over the fire, considering what he had better do. Margaret lay motionless, and almost breathless by him. He would not leave her, even for the dinner which Dixon had prepared for him downstairs, and, with sobbing hospitality, would fain have tempted him to eat. He had a plateful of something brought up to him. In general, he was particular and dainty enough, and knew well each shade of flavour in his food, but now the devilled chicken tasted like sawdust. He minced up some of the fowl for Margaret, and peppered and salted it well; but when Dixon, following his directions, tried to feed her, the languid shake of head, proved that in such a state as Margaret was in, food would only choke, not nourish her.\n\nMr. Bell gave a great sigh; lifted up his stout old limbs (stiff with travelling) from their easy position, and followed Dixon out of the room.\n\n“I can’t leave her. I must write to them at Oxford, to see that the preparations are made: they can be getting on with these till I arrive. Can’t Mrs. Lennox come to her? I’ll write and tell her she must. The girl must have some woman-friend about her, if only to talk her into a good fit of crying.”\n\nDixon was crying—enough for two; but, after wiping her eyes and steadying her voice, she managed to tell Mr. Bell, that Mrs. Lennox was too near her confinement to be able to undertake any journey at present.\n\n“Well! I suppose we must have Mrs. Shaw; she’s come back to England, isn’t she?”\n\n“Yes, sir, she’s come back; but I don’t think she will like to leave Mrs. Lennox at such an interesting time,” said Dixon, who did not much approve of a stranger entering the household, to share with her in her ruling care of Margaret.\n\n“Interesting time be——.” Mr. Bell restricted himself by coughing over the end of his sentence. “She could be content to be at Venice or Naples, or some of those Popish places, at the last ‘interesting time,’ which took place in Corfu, I think. And what does that little prosperous woman’s ‘interesting time’ signify, in comparison with that poor creature there,—that helpless, homeless, friendless, Margaret—lying as still on that sofa as if it were an altar-tomb, and she the stone statue on it. I tell you, Mrs. Shaw shall come. See that a room, or whatever she wants, is got ready for her by to-morrow night. I’ll take care she comes.”\n\nAccordingly Mr. Bell wrote a letter, which Mrs. Shaw declared, with many tears, to be so like one of the dear general’s when he was going to have a fit of the gout, that she should always value and preserve it. If he had given her the option, by requesting or urging her, as if a refusal were possible, she might not have come—true and sincere as was her sympathy with Margaret. It needed the sharp uncourteous command to make her conquer her vis inertiæ and allow herself to be packed by her maid, after the latter had completed the boxes. Edith, all cap, shawl, and tears, came out to the top of the stairs, as Captain Lennox was taking her mother down to the carriage:\n\n“Don’t forget, mamma; Margaret must come and live with us. Sholto will go to Oxford on Wednesday, and you must send word by Mr. Bell to him when we’re to expect you. And if you want Sholto, he can go on from Oxford to Milton. Don’t forget, mamma; you are to bring back Margaret.”\n\nEdith re-entered the drawing-room. Mr. Henry Lennox was there, cutting open the pages of a new Review. Without lifting his head, he said, “If you don’t like Sholto to be so long absent from you, Edith, I hope you will let me go down to Milton, and give what assistance I can.”\n\n“Oh, thank you,” said Edith, “I dare say old Mr. Bell will do everything he can, and more help may not be needed. Only one does not look for much savoir-faire from a resident Fellow. Dear, darling Margaret; won’t it be nice to have her here, again? You were both great allies, years ago.”\n\n“Were we?” asked he indifferently, with an appearance of being interested in a passage in the Review.\n\n“Well, perhaps not—I forget. I was so full of Sholto. But doesn’t it fall out well, that if my uncle was to die, it should be just now, when we are come home, and settled in the old house, and quite ready to receive Margaret? Poor thing! what a change it will be to her from Milton! I’ll have new chintz for her bed-room, and make it look new and bright, and cheer her up a little.”\n\nIn the same spirit of kindness, Mrs. Shaw journeyed to Milton, occasionally dreading the first meeting, and wondering how it would be got over; but more frequently planning how soon she could get Margaret away from “that horrid place,” and back in the pleasant comforts of Harley Street.\n\n“Oh dear!” she said to her maid; “look at those chimneys! My poor sister Hale! I don’t think I could have rested at Naples, if I had known what it was! I must have come and fetched her and Margaret away.” And to herself she acknowledged, that she had always thought her brother-in-law rather a weak man, but never so weak as now, when she saw for what a place he had exchanged the lovely Helstone home.\n\nMargaret had remained in the same state; white, motionless, speechless, tearless. They had told her that her aunt Shaw was coming; but she had not expressed either surprise or pleasure, or dislike to the idea. Mr. Bell, whose appetite had returned, and who appreciated Dixon’s endeavours to gratify it, in vain urged upon her to taste some sweetbreads stewed with oysters; she shook her head with the same quiet obstinacy as on the previous day; and he was obliged to console himself for her rejection, by eating them all himself. But Margaret was the first to hear the stopping of the cab that brought her aunt from the railway station. Her eyelids quivered, her lips coloured and trembled. Mr. Bell went down to meet Mrs. Shaw; and when they came up, Margaret was standing, trying to steady her dizzy self; and when she saw her aunt, she went forward to the arms open to receive her, and first found the passionate relief of tears on her aunt’s shoulder. All thoughts of quiet habitual love, of tenderness for years, of relationship to the dead, all that inexplicable likeness in look, tone, and gesture, that seem to belong to one family, and which reminded Margaret so forcibly at this moment of her mother, came in to melt and soften her numbed heart into the overflow of warm tears.\n\nMr. Bell stole out of the room, and went down into the study, where he ordered a fire, and tried to divert his thoughts by taking down and examining the different books. Each volume brought a remembrance or a suggestion of his dead friend. It might be a change of employment from his two days’ work of watching Margaret, but it was no change of thought. He was glad to catch the sound of Mr. Thornton’s voice, making enquiry at the door. Dixon was rather cavalierly dismissing him; for with the appearance of Mrs. Shaw’s maid, came visions of former grandeur, of the Beresford blood, of the “station” (so she was pleased to term it) from which her young lady had been ousted, and to which she was now, please God, to be restored. These visions, which she had been dwelling on with complacency in her conversation with Mrs. Shaw’s maid (skilfully eliciting meanwhile all the circumstances of state and consequence connected with the Harley Street establishment, for the edification of the listening Martha), made Dixon rather inclined to be supercilious in her treatment of any inhabitant of Milton; so, though she always stood rather in awe of Mr. Thornton, she was as curt as she durst be in telling him that he could see none of the inmates of the house that night. It was rather uncomfortable to be contradicted in her statement by Mr. Bell’s opening the study-door, and calling out:\n\n“Thornton! is that you? Come in for a minute or two; I want to speak to you.” So Mr. Thornton went into the study, and Dixon had to retreat into the kitchen, and reinstate herself in her own esteem by a prodigious story of Sir John Beresford’s coach and six, when he was high sheriff.\n\n“I don’t know what I wanted to say to you after all. Only it’s dull enough to sit in a room where everything speaks to you of a dead friend. Yet Margaret and her aunt must have the drawing-room to themselves!”\n\n“Is Mrs.—is her aunt come?” asked Mr. Thornton.\n\n“Come? Yes! maid and all. One would have thought she might have come by herself at such a time! And now I shall have to turn out and find my way to the Clarendon.”\n\n“You must not go to the Clarendon. We have five or six empty bed-rooms at home.”\n\n“Well aired?”\n\n“I think you may trust my mother for that.”\n\n“Then I’ll only run upstairs and wish that wan girl good-night, and make my bow to her aunt, and go off with you straight.”\n\nMr. Bell was some time upstairs. Mr. Thornton began to think it long, for he was full of business, and had hardly been able to spare the time for running up to Crampton, and enquiring how Miss Hale was.\n\nWhen they had set out upon their walk, Mr. Bell said:\n\n“I was kept by those women in the drawing-room. Mrs. Shaw is anxious to get home—on account of her daughter, she says—and wants Margaret to go off with her at once. Now she is no more fit for travelling than I am for flying. Besides, she says, and very justly, that she has friends she must see—that she must wish good-bye to several people; and then her aunt worried her about old claims, and was she forgetful of old friends? And she said, with a great burst of crying, she should be glad enough to go from a place where she had suffered so much. Now I must return to Oxford to-morrow, and I don’t know on which side of the scale to throw in my voice.”\n\nHe paused, as if asking a question; but he received no answer from his companion, the echo of whose thoughts kept repeating—\n\n“Where she had suffered so much.” Alas! and that was the way in which this eighteen months in Milton—to him so unspeakably precious, down to its very bitterness, which was worth all the rest of life’s sweetness—would be remembered. Neither loss of father, nor loss of mother, dear as she was to Mr. Thornton, could have poisoned the remembrance of the weeks, the days, the hours, when a walk of two miles, every step of which was pleasant, as it brought him nearer and nearer to her, took him to her sweet presence—every step of which was rich, as each recurring moment that bore him away from her made him recall some fresh grace in her demeanour, or pleasant pungency in her character. Yes! whatever had happened to him, external to his relation to her, he could never have spoken of that time, when he could have seen her every day—when he had her within his grasp, as it were—as a time of suffering. It had been a royal time of luxury to him, with all its stings and contumelies, compared to the poverty that crept round and clipped the anticipation of the future down to sordid fact, and life without an atmosphere of either hope or fear.\n\nMrs. Thornton and Fanny were in the dining-room; the latter in a flutter of small exultation, as the maid held up one glossy material after another, to try the effect of the wedding-dresses by candle-light. Her mother really tried to sympathise with her, but could not. Neither taste nor dress were in her line of subjects, and she heartily wished that Fanny had accepted her brother’s offer of having the wedding clothes provided by some first-rate London dressmaker, without the endless troublesome discussions, and unsettled waverings, that arose out of Fanny’s desire to choose and superintend everything herself. Mr. Thornton was only too glad to mark his grateful approbation of any sensible man, who could be captivated by Fanny’s second-rate airs and graces, by giving her ample means for providing herself with the finery, which certainly rivalled, if it did not exceed, the lover in her estimation. When her brother and Mr. Bell came in, Fanny blushed and simpered, and fluttered over the signs of her employment, in a way which could not have failed to draw attention from any one else but Mr. Bell. If he thought about her and her silks and satins at all, it was to compare her and them with the pale sorrow he had left behind him, sitting motionless, with bent head and folded hands, in a room where the stillness was so great that you might almost fancy the rush in your straining ears was occasioned by the spirits of the dead, yet hovering round their beloved. For when Mr. Bell had first gone upstairs, Mrs. Shaw lay asleep on the sofa; and no sound broke the silence.\n\nMrs. Thornton gave Mr. Bell her formal, hospitable welcome. She was never so gracious as when receiving her son’s friends in her son’s house; and the more unexpected they were, the more honour to her admirable housekeeping preparations for comfort.\n\n“How is Miss Hale?” she asked.\n\n“About as broken down by this last stroke as she can be.”\n\n“I am sure it is very well for her that she has such a friend as you.”\n\n“I wish I were her only friend, madam. I daresay it sounds very brutal; but here have I been displaced, and turned out of my post of comforter and adviser by a fine lady aunt; and there are cousins and what not claiming her in London, as if she were a lap-dog belonging to them. And she is too weak and miserable to have a will of her own.”\n\n“She must indeed be weak,” said Mrs. Thornton, with an implied meaning which her son understood well. “But where,” continued Mrs. Thornton, “have these relations been all this time that Miss Hale has appeared almost friendless, and has certainly had a good deal of anxiety to bear?” But she did not feel interest enough in the answer to her question to wait for it. She left the room to make her household arrangements.\n\n“They have been living abroad. They have some kind of claim upon her. I will do them that justice. The aunt brought her up, and she and the cousin have been like sisters. The thing vexing me, you see, is that I wanted to take her for a child of my own; and I am jealous of these people, who don’t seem to value the privilege of their right. Now it would be different if Frederick claimed her.”\n\n“Frederick!” exclaimed Mr. Thornton. “Who is he? What right——?” He stopped short in his vehement question.\n\n“Frederick,” said Mr. Bell in surprise. “Why, don’t you know? He’s her brother. Have you not heard——”\n\n“I never heard his name before. Where is he? Who is he?”\n\n“Surely I told you about him, when the family first came to Milton—the son who was concerned in that mutiny.”\n\n“I never heard of him till this moment. Where does he live?”\n\n“In Spain. He’s liable to be arrested the moment he sets foot on English ground. Poor fellow! he will grieve at not being able to attend his father’s funeral. We must be content with Captain Lennox; for I don’t know of any other relation.”\n\n“I hope I may be allowed to go?”\n\n“Certainly; thankfully. You’re a good fellow, after all, Thornton. Hale liked you. He spoke to me, only the other day, at Oxford. He regretted he had seen so little of you lately. I am obliged to you for wishing to show him respect.”\n\n“But about Frederick. Does he never come to England?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“He was not over here about the time of Mrs. Hale’s death?”\n\n“No. Why, I was here then. I hadn’t seen Hale for years and years: and, if you remember, I came—No, it was some time after that that I came. But poor Frederick Hale was not here then. What made you think he was?”\n\n“I saw a young man walking with Miss Hale one day,” replied Mr. Thornton, “and I think it was about that time.”\n\n“Oh, that would be this young Lennox, the Captain’s brother. He’s a lawyer, and they were in pretty constant correspondence with him; and I remember Mr. Hale told me he thought he would come down. Do you know,” said Mr. Bell, wheeling round, and shutting one eye, the better to bring the forces of the other to bear with keen scrutiny on Mr. Thornton’s face, “that I once fancied you had a little tenderness for Margaret?”\n\nNo answer. No change of countenance.\n\n“And so did poor Hale. Not at first, and not till I had put it into his head.”\n\n“I admired Miss Hale. Every one must do so. She is a beautiful creature,” said Mr. Thornton, driven to bay by Mr. Bell’s pertinacious questioning.\n\n“Is that all! You can speak of her in that measured way, as simply a ‘beautiful creature’—only something to catch the eye. I did hope you had had nobleness enough in you to make you pay her the homage of the heart. Though I believe—in fact I know, she would have rejected you, still to have loved her without return would have lifted you higher than all those, be they who they may, that have ever known her to love. ‘Beautiful creature’ indeed! Do you speak of her as you would of a horse or a dog?”\n\nMr. Thornton’s eyes glowed like red embers.\n\n“Mr. Bell,” said he, “before you speak so, you should remember that all men are not as free to express what they feel as you are. Let us talk of something else.” For though his heart leaped up, as at a trumpet-call, to every word that Mr. Bell had said, and though he knew that what he had said would henceforward bind the thought of the old Oxford Fellow closely up with the most precious things of his heart, yet he would not be forced into any expression of what he felt towards Margaret. He was no mocking-bird of praise to try, because another extolled what he reverenced and passionately loved, to outdo him in laudation. So he turned to some of the dry matters of business that lay between Mr. Bell and him, as landlord and tenant.\n\n“What is that heap of brick and mortar we came against in the yard? Any repairs wanted?”\n\n“No, none, thank you.”\n\n“Are you building on your own account? If you are, I’m very much obliged to you.”\n\n“I’m building a dining-room—for the men I mean—the hands.”\n\n“I thought you were hard to please, if this room wasn’t good enough to satisfy you, a bachelor.”\n\n“I’ve got acquainted with a strange kind of chap, and I put one or two children in whom he is interested to school. So, as I happened to be passing near his house one day, I just went there about some trifling payment to be made; and I saw such a miserable black frizzle of a dinner—a greasy cinder of meat, as first set me a-thinking. But it was not till provisions grew so high this winter that I bethought me how, by buying things wholesale, and cooking a good quantity of provisions together, much money might be saved, and much comfort gained. So I spoke to my friend—or my enemy—the man I told you of—and he found fault with every detail of my plan; and in consequence I laid it aside, both as impracticable, and also because if I forced it into operation I should be interfering with the independence of my men; when, suddenly, this Higgins came to me and graciously signified his approval of a scheme so nearly the same as mine, that I might fairly have claimed it; and, moreover, the approval of several of his fellow-workmen, to whom he had spoken. I was a little ‘riled,’ I confess, by his manner, and thought of throwing the whole thing overboard to sink or swim. But it seemed childish to relinquish a plan which I had once thought wise and well-laid, just because I myself did not receive all the honour and consequence due to the originator. So I coolly took the part assigned to me, which is something like that of a steward to a club. I buy in the provisions wholesale, and provide a fitting matron or cook.”\n\n“I hope you give satisfaction in your new capacity. Are you a good judge of potatoes and onions? But I suppose Mrs. Thornton assists you in your marketing.”\n\n“Not a bit,” replied Mr. Thornton. “She disapproves of the whole plan, and now we never mention it to each other. But I manage pretty well, getting in great stocks from Liverpool, and being served in butcher’s meat by our own family butcher. I can assure you, the hot dinners the matron turns out are not to be despised.”\n\n“Do you taste each dish as it goes in, in virtue of your office? I hope you have a white wand.”\n\n“I was very scrupulous, at first, in confining myself to the mere purchasing part, and even in that I rather obeyed the men’s orders, conveyed through the housekeeper, than went by my own judgment. At one time, the beef was too large, at another the mutton was not fat enough. I think they saw how careful I was to leave them free, and not to intrude my own ideas upon them; so, one day, two or three of the men—my friend Higgins among them—asked me if I would not come in and take a snack. It was a very busy day, but I saw that the men would be hurt if, after making the advance, I didn’t meet them half-way, so I went in, and I never made a better dinner in my life. I told them (my next neighbours I mean, for I’m no speech-maker) how much I enjoyed it; and for some time, whenever that especial dinner recurred in their dietary, I was sure to be met by these men, with a ‘Master, there’s hot-pot for dinner to-day, win yo’ come in?’ If they had not asked me, I would no more have intruded on them than I’d have gone to the mess at the barracks without invitation.”\n\n“I should think you were rather a restraint on your hosts’ conversation. They can’t abuse the masters while you’re there. I expect they take it out on non-hot-pot days.”\n\n“Well! hitherto we’ve steered clear of all vexed questions. But if any of the old disputes came up again, I would certainly speak out my mind next hot-pot day. But you are hardly acquainted with our Darkshire fellows, for all you’re a Darkshire man yourself. They have such a sense of humour, and such a racy mode of expression! I am getting really to know some of them now, and they talk pretty freely before me.”\n\n“Nothing like the act of eating for equalising men. Dying is nothing to it. The philosopher dies sententiously—the pharisee ostentatiously—the simple-hearted humbly—the poor idiot blindly, as the sparrow falls to the ground; the philosopher and idiot, publican and pharisee, all eat after the same fashion—given an equally good digestion. There’s theory for theory for you!”\n\n“Indeed I have no theory; I hate theories.”\n\n“I beg your pardon. To show my penitence, will you accept a ten pound note towards your marketing, and give the poor fellows a feast?”\n\n“Thank you; but I’d rather not. They pay me rent for the oven and cooking-places at the back of the mill: and will have to pay more for the new dining-room. I don’t want it to fall into a charity. I don’t want donations. Once let in the principle, and I should have people going and talking, and spoiling the simplicity of the whole thing.”\n\n“People will talk about any new plan. You can’t help that.”\n\n“My enemies, if I have any, may make a philanthropic fuss about this dinner-scheme; but you are a friend, and I expect that you will now pay my experiment the respect of silence. It is but a new broom at present, and sweeps clean enough. But by-an-bye we shall meet with plenty of stumbling-blocks, no doubt.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII\n\n“The meanest thing to which we bid adieu, Loses its meanness in the parting hour.” ELLIOTT.\n\nMrs. Shaw took as vehement a dislike as it was possible for one of her gentle nature to do, against Milton. It was noisy, and smoky, and the poor people whom she saw in the streets were dirty, and the rich ladies over-dressed, and not a man that she saw, high or low, had his clothes made to fit him. She was sure Margaret would never regain her lost strength while she stayed in Milton; and she herself was afraid of one of her old attacks of the nerves. Margaret must return with her, and that quickly. This, if not the exact force of her words, was at any rate the spirit of what she urged on Margaret, till the latter, weak, weary, and broken-spirited, yielded a reluctant promise that, as soon as Wednesday was over, she would prepare to accompany her aunt back to town, leaving Dixon in charge of all the arrangements for paying bills, disposing of furniture, and shutting up the house. Before that Wednesday—that mournful Wednesday, when Mr. Hale was to be interred, far away from either of the homes he had known in life, and far away from the wife who lay lonely among strangers (and this last was Margaret’s great trouble, for she thought that if she had not given way to that overwhelming stupor during the first sad days, she could have arranged things otherwise)—before that Wednesday, Margaret received a letter from Mr. Bell.\n\n“MY DEAR MARGARET:—I did mean to have returned to Milton on Thursday, but unluckily it turns out to be one of the rare occasions when we, Plymouth Fellows, are called upon to perform any kind of duty, and I must not be absent from my post. Captain Lennox and Mr. Thornton are here. The former seems a smart, well-meaning man; and has proposed to go over to Milton, and assist you in any search for the will; of course there is none, or you would have found it by this time, if you followed my directions. Then the Captain declares he must take you and his mother-in-law home; and, in his wife’s present state, I don’t see how you can expect him to remain away longer than Friday. However, that Dixon of yours is trusty; and can hold her, or your own, till I come. I will put matters into the hands of my Milton attorney if there is no will; for I doubt this smart Captain is no great man of business. Nevertheless, his moustachios are splendid. There will have to be a sale, so select what things you wish to be reserved. Or you can send a list afterwards. Now two things more, and I have done. You know, or if you don’t, your poor father did, that you are to have my money and goods when I die. Not that I mean to die yet; but I name this just to explain what is coming. These Lennoxes seem very fond of you now; and perhaps may continue to be; perhaps not. So it is best to start with a formal agreement; namely, that you are to pay to them two hundred and fifty pounds a year, as long as you and they find it pleasant to live together. (This, of course, includes Dixon; mind you don’t be cajoled into paying more for her.) Then you won’t be thrown adrift, if some day the captain wishes to have his house to himself, but you can carry yourself and your two hundred and fifty pounds off somewhere else; if, indeed, I have not claimed you to come and keep house for me first. Then as to dress, and Dixon, and personal expenses, and confectionery (all young ladies eat confectionery till wisdom comes by age), I shall consult some lady of my acquaintance, and see how much you will have from your father before fixing this. Now, Margaret, have you flown out before you have read this far, and wondered what right the old man has to settle your affairs for you so cavalierly? I make no doubt you have. Yet the old man has a right. He has loved your father for five and thirty years; he stood beside him on his wedding-day; he closed his eyes in death. Moreover, he is your godfather; and as he cannot do you much good spiritually, having a hidden consciousness of your superiority in such things, he would fain do you the poor good of endowing you materially. And the old man has not a known relation on earth; ‘who is there to mourn for Adam Bell?’ and his whole heart is set and bent upon this one thing, and Margaret Hale is not the girl to say him nay. Write by return if only two lines, to tell me your answer. But no thanks.”\n\nMargaret took up a pen and scrawled with trembling hand, “Margaret Hale is not the girl to say him nay.” In her weak state she could not think of any other words, and yet, she was vexed to use these. But she was so much fatigued even by this slight exertion, that if she could have thought of another form of acceptance, she could not have sate up to write a syllable of it. She was obliged to lie down again and try not to think.\n\n“My dearest child! Has that letter vexed or troubled you?”\n\n“No!” said Margaret feebly. “I shall be better when to-morrow is over.”\n\n“I feel sure, darling, you won’t be better till I get you out of this horrid air. How you can have borne it these two years I can’t imagine.”\n\n“Where could I go to? I could not leave papa and mamma.”\n\n“Well, don’t distress yourself, my dear. I dare say it was all for the best, only I had no conception of how you were living. Our butler’s wife lives in a better house than this.”\n\n“It is sometimes very pretty—in summer; you can’t judge by what it is now. I have been very happy here,” and Margaret closed her eyes by way of stopping the conversation.\n\nThe house teemed with comfort now, compared to what it had been. The evenings were chilly, and by Mrs. Shaw’s directions fires were lighted in every bedroom. She petted Margaret in every possible way, and bought every delicacy, or soft luxury in which she herself would have burrowed and sought comfort. But Margaret was indifferent to all these things; or, if they forced themselves upon her attention, it was simply as causes for gratitude to her aunt, who was putting herself so much out of the way to think of her. She was restless, though so weak. All the day long, she kept herself from thinking of the ceremony which was going on at Oxford, by wandering from room to room, and languidly setting aside such articles as she wished to retain. Dixon followed her by Mrs. Shaw’s desire, ostensibly to receive instructions, but with a private injunction to soothe her into repose as soon as might be.\n\n“These books, Dixon, I will keep. All the rest will you send to Mr. Bell? They are of a kind that he will value for themselves, as well as for papa’s sake. This—— I should like you to take this to Mr. Thornton, after I am gone. Stay; I will write a note with it.” And she sate down hastily, as if afraid of thinking, and wrote:\n\n“DEAR SIR,—The accompanying book I am sure will be valued by you for the sake of my father, to whom it belonged.\n\n“Yours sincerely,\n\nShe set out again upon her travels through the house, turning over articles, known to her from her childhood, with a sort of caressing reluctance to leave them—old-fashioned, worn and shabby, as they might be. But she hardly spoke again; and Dixon’s report to Mrs. Shaw was, that “she doubted whether Miss Hale heard a word of what she said, though she talked the whole time, in order to divert her intention.” The consequence of her being on her feet all day was excessive bodily weariness in the evening, and a better night’s rest than she had had since she heard of Mr. Hale’s death.\n\nAt breakfast time the next day, she expressed her wish to go and bid one or two friends good-bye. Mrs. Shaw objected:\n\n“I am sure, my dear, you can have no friends here with whom you are sufficiently intimate to justify you in calling upon them so soon; before you have been at church.”\n\n“But to-day is my only day; if Captain Lennox comes this afternoon, and if we must—if I must really go to-morrow——”\n\n“Oh, yes, we shall go to-morrow. I am more and more convinced that this air is bad for you, and makes you look so pale and ill; besides, Edith expects us; and she may be waiting for me; and you cannot be left alone, my dear, at your age. No; if you must make these calls, I will go with you. Dixon can get us a coach, I suppose?”\n\nSo Mrs. Shaw went to take care of Margaret and took her maid with her to take care of the shawls and air-cushions. Margaret’s face was too sad to lighten up into a smile at all this preparation for paying two visits, that she had often made by herself at all hours of the day. She was half afraid of owning that one place to which she was going was Nicholas Higgins’; all she could do was to hope her aunt would be indisposed to get out of the coach and walk up the court, and at every breath of wind have her face slapped by wet clothes, hanging out to dry on ropes stretched from house to house.\n\nThere was a little battle in Mrs. Shaw’s mind between ease and a sense of matronly propriety; but the former gained the day; and with many an injunction to Margaret to be careful of herself, and not to catch any fever, such as was always lurking in such places, her aunt permitted her to go where she had often been before without taking any precaution or requiring any permission.\n\nNicholas was out; only Mary and one or two of the Boucher children at home. Margaret was vexed with herself for not having timed her visit better. Mary had a very blunt intellect, although her feelings were warm and kind; and the instant she understood what Margaret’s purpose was in coming to see them, she began to cry and sob with so little restraint that Margaret found it useless to say any of the thousand little things which had suggested themselves to her as she was coming along in the coach. She could only try to comfort her a little by suggesting the vague chance of their meeting again, at some possible time, in some possible place, and bid her tell her father how much she wished, if he could manage it, that he should come to see her when he had done his work in the evening.\n\nAs she was leaving the place, she stopped and looked around; then hesitated a little before she said:\n\n“I should like to have some little thing to remind me of Bessy.”\n\nInstantly Mary’s generosity was keenly alive. What could they give? And on Margaret’s singling out a little common drinking-cup, which she remembered as the one always standing by Bessy’s side with a drink for her feverish lips, Mary said:\n\n“Oh, take summat better; that only cost fourpence!”\n\n“That will do, thank you,” said Margaret; and she went quickly away, while the light caused by the pleasure of having something to give yet lingered on Mary’s face.\n\n“Now to Mrs. Thornton’s,” thought she to herself. “It must be done.” But she looked rather rigid and pale at the thoughts of it, and had hard work to find the exact words in which to explain to her aunt who Mrs. Thornton was, and why she should go to bid her farewell.\n\nThey (for Mrs. Shaw alighted here) were shown into the drawing-room, in which a fire had only just been kindled. Mrs. Shaw huddled herself up in her shawl and shivered.\n\n“What an icy room!” she said.\n\nThey had to wait for some time before Mrs. Thornton entered. There was some softening in her heart towards Margaret, now that she was going away out of her sight. She remembered her spirit, as shown at various times and places, even more than the patience with which she had endured long and wearing cares. Her countenance was blander than usual, as she greeted her; there was even a shade of tenderness in her manner, as she noticed the white, tear-swollen face, and the quiver in the voice which Margaret tried to make so steady.\n\n“Allow me to introduce my aunt, Mrs. Shaw. I am going away from Milton to-morrow; I do not know if you are aware of it, but I wanted to see you once again, Mrs. Thornton, to—to apologize for my manner the last time I saw you; and to say that I am sure you meant kindly—however much we may have misunderstood each other.”\n\nMrs. Shaw looked extremely perplexed by what Margaret had said. Thanks for kindness! and apologies for failure in good manners! But Mrs. Thornton replied:\n\n“Miss Hale, I am glad you do me justice. I did no more than I believed to be my duty in remonstrating with you as I did. I have always desired to act the part of a friend to you. I am glad you do me justice.”\n\n“And,” said Margaret, blushing excessively as she spoke, “will you do me justice, and believe that though I cannot—I do not choose—to give explanations of my conduct, I have not acted in the unbecoming way you apprehended?”\n\nMargaret’s voice was so soft, and her eyes so pleading, that Mrs. Thornton was for once affected by the charm of manner to which she had hitherto proved herself invulnerable.\n\n“Yes, I do believe you. Let us say no more about it. Where are you going to reside, Miss Hale? I understood from Mr. Bell that you were going to leave Milton. You never liked Milton, you know,” said Mrs. Thornton, with a sort of grim smile; “but for all that, you must not expect me to congratulate you on quitting it. Where shall you live?”\n\n“With my aunt,” replied Margaret, turning towards Mrs. Shaw.\n\n“My niece will reside with me in Harley Street. She is almost like a daughter to me,” said Mrs. Shaw, looking fondly at Margaret; “and I am glad to acknowledge my own obligation for any kindness that has been shown to her. If you and your husband ever come to the town, my son and daughter, Captain and Mrs. Lennox, will, I am sure, join with me in wishing to do anything in our power to show you attention.”\n\nMrs. Thornton thought in her own mind, that Margaret had not taken much care to enlighten her aunt as to the relationship between the Mr. and Mrs. Thornton, towards whom the fine-lady aunt was extending her soft patronage; so she answered shortly,\n\n“My husband is dead. Mr. Thornton is my son. I never go to London; so I am not likely to be able to avail myself of your polite offers.”\n\nAt this instant Mr. Thornton entered the room; he had only just returned from Oxford. His mourning suit spoke of the reason that had called him there.\n\n“John,” said his mother, “this lady is Mrs. Shaw, Miss Hale’s aunt. I am sorry to say, that Miss Hale’s call is to wish us good-bye.”\n\n“You are going then!” said he in a low voice.\n\n“Yes,” said Margaret. “We leave to-morrow.”\n\n“My son-in-law comes this evening to escort us,” said Mrs. Shaw.\n\nMr. Thornton turned away. He had not sat down, and now he seemed to be examining something on the table, almost as if he had discovered an unopened letter, which had made him forget the present company. He did not even seem to be aware when they got up to take leave. He started forwards, however, to hand Mrs. Shaw down to the carriage. As it drove up, he and Margaret stood close together on the door-step, and it was impossible but that the recollection of the day of the riot should force itself into both their minds. Into his it came associated with the speeches of the following day; her passionate declaration that there was not a man in all that violent and a desperate crowd, for whom she did not care as much as for him. And at the remembrance of her taunting words, his brow grew stern, though his heart beat thick with longing love. “No!” said he, “I put it to the touch once, and I lost it all. Let her go,—with her stony heart, and her beauty;—how set and terrible her look is now, for all her loveliness of feature! She is afraid I shall speak what will require some stern repression. Let her go. Beauty and heiress as she may be, she will find it hard to meet with a truer heart than mine. Let her go!”\n\nAnd there was no tone of regret, or emotion of any kind in the voice with which he said good-bye; and the offered hand was taken with a resolute calmness, and dropped as carelessly as if it had been a dead and withered flower. But none in his household saw Mr. Thornton again that day. He was busily engaged; or so he said.\n\nMargaret’s strength was so utterly exhausted by these visits, that she had to submit to much watching and petting, and sighing “I-told-you-so’s,” from her aunt. Dixon said she was quite as bad as she had been on the first day she heard of her father’s death; and she and Mrs. Shaw consulted as to the desirableness of delaying the morrow’s journey. But when her aunt reluctantly proposed a few days’ delay to Margaret, the latter writhed her body as if in acute suffering, and said:\n\n“Oh! let us go. I cannot be patient here. I shall not get well here. I want to forget.”\n\nSo the arrangements went on: and Captain Lennox came, and with him news of Edith and the little boy; and Margaret found that the indifferent, careless conversation of one who, however kind, was not too warm and anxious a sympathiser, did her good. She roused up; and by the time that she knew she might expect Higgins, she was able to leave the room quietly, and await in her own chamber the expected summons.\n\n“Eh!” said he, as she came in, “to think of th’ oud gentleman dropping off as he did! Yo’ might ha’ knocked me down wi’ a straw when they telled me. ‘Mr. Hale?’ said I; ‘him as was th’ parson?’ ‘Ay,’ said they. ‘Then,’ said I, ‘there’s as good a man gone as ever lived on this earth, let who will be t’other!’ And I came to see yo’, and tell yo’ how grieved I were, but them women in th’ kitchen wouldn’t tell yo’ I were there. They said yo’ were ill,—and butter me, but yo’ dunnot look like the same wench. And yo’re going to be a grand lady up i’ Lunnon, aren’t yo’?”\n\n“Not a grand lady,” said Margaret, half smiling.\n\n“Well! Thornton said—says he, a day or two ago, ‘Higgins, have yo’ seen Miss Hale?’ ‘No,’ says I; ‘there’s a pack o’ women who won’t let me at her. But I can bide my time, if she’s ill. She and I knows each other pretty well; and hoo’l not go doubting that I’m main sorry for th’ oud gentleman’s death, just because I can’t get at her and tell her so.’ And says he, ‘Yo’ll not have much time for to try and see her, my fine chap. She’s not for staying with us a day longer nor she can help. She’s got grand relations, and they’re carrying her off; and we shan’t see her no more.’ ‘Measter,’ said I, ‘if I dunnot see her afore hoo goes, I’ll strive to get up to Lunnon next Whitsuntide, that I will. I’ll not be baulked of saying her good-bye by any relations whatsomdever. But bless yo’, I knowed yo’d come. It were only for to humour the measter, I let on as if I thought yo’d m’appen leave Milton without seeing me.”\n\n“You’re quite right,” said Margaret. “You only do me justice. And you’ll not forget me, I’m sure. If no one else in Milton remembers me, I’m certain you will; and papa too. You know how good and tender he was. Look, Higgins! here is his Bible. I have kept it for you. I can ill spare it; but I know he would have liked you to have it. I’m sure you’ll care for it, and study what is in it, for his sake.”\n\n“Yo’ may say that. If it were the deuce’s own scribble, and yo’ axed me to read in it for yo’r sake and the oud gentleman’s, I’d do it. Whatten’s this, wench! I’m not going for to take yo’r brass, so dunnot think it. We’ve been great friends, ’bout the sound o’ money passing between us.”\n\n“For the children—for Boucher’s children,” said Margaret, hurriedly. “They may need it. You’ve no right to refuse it for them. I would not give you one penny,” she said, smiling; “don’t think there’s any of it for you.”\n\n“Well, wench! I can nobbut say, Bless yo’! and bless yo’!—and amen.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV\n\n“A dull rotation, never at a stay, Yesterday’s face twin image of to-day.” COWPER.\n\n“Of what each one should be, he sees the form and rule, And till he reach to that, his joy can ne’er be full.” RÜCKERT.\n\nIt was very well for Margaret that the extreme quiet of the Harley Street house, during Edith’s recovery from her confinement, gave her the natural rest which she needed. It gave her time to comprehend the sudden change which had taken place in her circumstances within the last two months. She found herself at once an inmate of a luxurious house, where the bare knowledge of the existence of every trouble or care seemed scarcely to have penetrated. The wheels of the machinery of daily life were well oiled, and went along with delicious smoothness. Mrs. Shaw and Edith could hardly make enough of Margaret, on her return to what they persisted in calling her home. And she felt that it was almost ungrateful in her to have a secret feeling that the Helstone vicarage—nay, even the poor little house at Milton, with her anxious father and her invalid mother, and all the small household cares of comparative poverty, composed her idea of home. Edith was impatient to get well, in order to fill Margaret’s bed-room with all the soft comforts, and pretty knick-knacks, with which her own abounded. Mrs. Shaw and her maid found plenty of occupation in restoring Margaret’s wardrobe to a state of elegant variety. Captain Lennox was easy, kind, and gentlemanly; sate with his wife in her dressing-room an hour or two every day; played with his little boy for another hour, and lounged away the rest of his time at his club, when he was not engaged out to dinner. Just before Margaret had recovered from her necessity for quiet and repose—before she had begun to feel her life wanting and dull—Edith came downstairs and resumed her usual part in the household; and Margaret fell into the old habit of watching, and admiring, and ministering to her cousin. She gladly took all charge of the semblances of duties off Edith’s hands; answered notes, reminded her of engagements, tended her when no gaiety was in prospect, and she was consequently rather inclined to fancy herself ill. But all the rest of the family were in the full business of the London season, and Margaret was often left alone. Then her thoughts went back to Milton, with a strange sense of the contrast between the life there, and here. She was getting surfeited of the eventless ease in which no struggle or endeavour was required. She was afraid lest she should even become sleepily deadened into forgetfulness of anything beyond the life which was lapping her round with luxury. There might be toilers and moilers there in London, but she never saw them; the very servants lived in an underground world of their own, of which she knew neither the hopes nor the fears; they only seemed to start into existence when some want or whim of their master and mistress needed them. There was a strange unsatisfied vacuum in Margaret’s heart and mode of life; and, once when she had dimly hinted this to Edith, the latter, wearied with dancing the night before, languidly stroked Margaret’s cheek as she sat by her in the old attitude,—she on a footstool by the sofa where Edith lay.\n\n“Poor child!” said Edith. “It is a little sad for you to be left, night after night, just at this time when all the world is so gay. But we shall be having our dinner-parties soon—as soon as Henry comes back from circuit—and then there will be a little pleasant variety for you. No wonder it is moped, poor darling!”\n\nMargaret did not feel as if the dinner-parties would be a panacea. But Edith piqued herself on her dinner-parties; “so different,” as she said, “from the old dowager dinners under mamma’s régime”; and Mrs. Shaw herself seemed to take exactly the same kind of pleasure in the very different arrangements and circle of acquaintances which were to Captain and Mrs. Lennox’s taste, as she did in the more formal and ponderous entertainments which she herself used to give. Captain Lennox was always extremely kind and brotherly to Margaret. She was really very fond of him, excepting when he was anxiously attentive to Edith’s dress and appearance, with a view to her beauty making a sufficient impression on the world. Then all the latent Vashti in Margaret was roused, and she could hardly keep herself from expressing her feelings.\n\nThe course of Margaret’s day was this: a quiet hour or two before a late breakfast; an unpunctual meal, lazily eaten by weary and half-awake people, but yet at which, in all its dragged out length, she was expected to be present, because, directly afterwards, came a discussion of plans, at which, although they none of them concerned her, she was expected to give her sympathy, if she could not assist with her advice; an endless number of notes to write, which Edith invariably left to her, with many caressing compliments as to her eloquence du billet; a little play with Sholto as he returned from his morning’s walk; besides the care of the children during the servants’ dinner; a drive or callers; and some dinner or morning engagement for her aunt and cousins, which left Margaret free, it is true, but rather wearied with the inactivity of the day, coming upon depressed spirits and delicate health.\n\nShe looked forward with longing, though unspoken interest to the homely object of Dixon’s return from Milton; where, until now, the old servant had been busily engaged in winding up all the affairs of the Hale family. It had appeared a sudden famine to her heart, this entire cessation of any news respecting the people among whom she had lived so long. It was true, that Dixon, in her business-like letters, quoted, every now and then, an opinion of Mr. Thornton’s as to what she had better do about the furniture, or how act in regard to the landlord of the Crampton Terrace house. But it was only here and there that the name came in, or any Milton name, indeed; and Margaret was sitting one evening, all alone in the Lennox’s drawing-room, not reading Dixon’s letters, which yet she held in her hand, but thinking over them, and recalling the days which had been, and picturing the busy life out of which her own had been taken and never missed; wondering if all went on in that whirl just as if she and her father had never been; questioning within herself, if no one in all the crowd missed her (not Higgins, she was not thinking of him), when, suddenly Mr. Bell was announced; and Margaret hurried the letters into her work-basket, and started up, blushing as if she had been doing some guilty thing.\n\n“Oh, Mr. Bell! I never thought of seeing you!”\n\n“But you give me a welcome, I hope, as well as that very pretty start of surprise.”\n\n“Have you dined? How did you come? Let me order you some dinner.”\n\n“If you’re going to have any. Otherwise, you know, there is no one who cares less for eating than I do. But where are the others? Gone out to dinner? Left you alone?”\n\n“Oh yes! and it is such a rest. I was just thinking—But will you run the risk of dinner? I don’t know if there is anything in the house.”\n\n“Why, to tell you the truth, I dined at my club. Only they don’t cook as well as they did, so I thought, if you were going to dine, I might try and make out my dinner. But never mind, never mind! There aren’t ten cooks in England to be trusted at impromptu dinners. If their skill and their fires will stand it, their tempers won’t. You shall make me some tea, Margaret. And now, what were you thinking of? you were going to tell me. Whose letters were those, god-daughter, that you hid away so speedily?”\n\n“Only Dixon’s,” replied Margaret, growing very red.\n\n“Whew! is that all? Who do you think came up in the train with me?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said Margaret, resolved against making a guess.\n\n“Your what d’ye call him? What’s the right name for a cousin-in-law’s brother?”\n\n“Mr. Henry Lennox?” asked Margaret.\n\n“Yes,” replied Mr. Bell. “You knew him formerly, didn’t you? What sort of a person is he, Margaret?”\n\n“I liked him long ago,” said Margaret, glancing down for a moment. And then she looked straight up and went on in her natural manner. “You know we have been corresponding about Frederick since; but I have not seen him for nearly three years, and he may be changed. What did you think of him?”\n\n“I don’t know. He was so busy trying to find out who I was, in the first instance, and what I was in the second, that he never let out what he was; unless indeed that veiled curiosity of his as to what manner of man he had to talk to was not a good piece, and a fair indication of his character. Do you call him good-looking, Margaret?”\n\n“No! certainly not. Do you?”\n\n“Not I. But I thought perhaps you might. Is he a great deal here?”\n\n“I fancy he is when he is in town. He has been on circuit now since I came. But—Mr. Bell—have you come from Oxford or from Milton?”\n\n“From Milton. Don’t you see I’m smoke-dried?”\n\n“Certainly. But I thought that it might be the effect of the antiquities of Oxford.”\n\n“Come now, be a sensible woman! In Oxford, I could have managed all the landlords in the place, and had my own way, with half the trouble your Milton landlord has given me, and defeated me after all. He won’t take the house off our hands till next June twelve-month. Luckily, Mr. Thornton found a tenant for it. Why don’t you ask after Mr. Thornton, Margaret? He has proved himself a very active friend of yours, I can tell you. Taken more than half the trouble off my hands.”\n\n“And how is he? How is Mrs. Thornton?” asked Margaret hurriedly and below her breath, though she tried to speak out.\n\n“I suppose they’re well. I’ve been staying at their house till I was driven out of it by the perpetual clack about that Thornton girl’s marriage. It was too much for Thornton himself, though she was his sister. He used to go and sit in his own room perpetually. He’s getting past the age for caring for such things, either as principal or accessory. I was surprised to find the old lady falling into the current, and carried away by her daughter’s enthusiasm for orange-blossoms and lace. I thought Mrs. Thornton had been made of sterner stuff.”\n\n“She would put on any assumption of feeling to veil her daughter’s weakness,” said Margaret in a low voice.\n\n“Perhaps so. You’ve studied her, have you? She doesn’t seem over fond of you, Margaret.”\n\n“I know it,” said Margaret. “Oh, here is tea, at last!” exclaimed she, as if relieved. And with tea came Mr. Henry Lennox, who had walked up to Harley Street after a late dinner, and had evidently expected to find his brother and sister-in-law at home. Margaret suspected him of being as thankful as she was at the presence of a third party, on this their first meeting since the memorable day of his offer, and her refusal at Helstone. She could hardly tell what to say at first, and was thankful for all the tea-table occupations, which gave her an excuse for keeping silence, and him an opportunity for recovering himself. For, to tell the truth, he had rather forced himself up to Harley Street this evening, with a view of getting over an awkward meeting, awkward even in the presence of Captain Lennox and Edith, and doubly awkward now that he found her the only lady there, and the person to whom he must naturally and perforce address a great part of his conversation. She was the first to recover her self-possession. She began to talk on the subject which came uppermost in her mind, after the first flush of awkward shyness.\n\n“Mr. Lennox, I have been so much obliged to you for all you have done about Frederick.”\n\n“I am only sorry it has been so unsuccessful,” replied he, with a quick glance towards Mr. Bell, as if reconnoitring how much he might say before him. Margaret, as if she read his thought, addressed herself to Mr. Bell, both including him in the conversation, and implying that he was perfectly aware of the endeavours that had been made to clear Frederick.\n\n“That Horrocks—that very last witness of all, has proved as unavailing as all the others. Mr. Lennox has discovered that he sailed for Australia only last August; only two months before Frederick was in England, and gave us the names of——”\n\n“Frederick in England! you never told me that!” exclaimed Mr. Bell in surprise.\n\n“I thought you knew. I never doubted you had been told. Of course, it was a great secret, and perhaps I should not have named it now,” said Margaret, a little dismayed.\n\n“I have never named it to either my brother or your cousin,” said Mr. Lennox, with a little professional dryness of implied reproach.\n\n“Never mind, Margaret. I am not living in a talking, babbling world, nor yet among people who are trying to worm facts out of me; you needn’t look so frightened because you have let the cat out of the bag to a faithful old hermit like me. I shall never name his having been in England; I shall be out of temptation, for no one will ask me. Stay!” (interrupting himself rather abruptly). “Was it at your mother’s funeral?”\n\n“He was with mamma when she died,” said Margaret, softly.\n\n“To be sure! To be sure! Why, some one asked me if he had not been over then, and I denied it stoutly—not many weeks ago—who could it have been? Oh! I recollect!”\n\nBut he did not say the name; and although Margaret would have given much to know if her suspicions were right, and it had been Mr. Thornton who had made the enquiry, she could not ask the question of Mr. Bell, much as she longed to do so.\n\nThere was a pause for a moment or two. Then Mr. Lennox said, addressing himself to Margaret, “I suppose as Mr. Bell is now acquainted with all the circumstances attending your brother’s unfortunate dilemma, I cannot do better than inform him exactly how the research into the evidence we once hoped to produce in his favour stands at present. So, if he will do me the honour to breakfast with me to-morrow, we will go over the names of these missing gentry.”\n\n“I should like to hear all the particulars, if I may. Cannot you come here? I dare not ask you both to breakfast, though I am sure you would be welcome. But let me know all you can about Frederick, even though there may be no hope at present.”\n\n“I have an engagement at half-past eleven. But I will certainly come if you wish it,” replied Mr. Lennox with a little after-thought of extreme willingness, which made Margaret shrink into herself, and almost wish that she had not proposed her natural request. Mr. Bell got up and looked around him for his hat, which had been removed to make room for tea.\n\n“Well!” said he, “I don’t know what Mr. Lennox is inclined to do, but I’m disposed to be moving off homewards. I’ve been a journey to-day, and journeys begin to tell upon my sixty and odd years.”\n\n“I believe I shall stay and see my brother and sister,” said Mr. Lennox, making no movement of departure. Margaret was seized with a shy awkward dread of being left alone with him. The scene on the little terrace in the Helstone garden was so present to her, that she could hardly help believing it was so with him.\n\n“Don’t go yet, please Mr. Bell,” said she, hastily. “I want you to see Edith; and I want Edith to know you. Please!” said she, laying a light but determined hand on his arm. He looked at her, and saw the confusion stirring in her countenance; he sate down again, as if her little touch had been possessed of resistless strength.\n\n“You see how she overpowers me, Mr. Lennox,” said he. “And I hope you noticed the happy choice of her expressions; she wants me to ‘see’ this cousin Edith, who, I am told, is a great beauty; but she has the honesty to change her word when she comes to me—Mrs. Lennox is to ‘know’ me. I suppose I am not much to ‘see,’ eh, Margaret?”\n\nHe joked, to give her time to recover from the slight flutter which he had detected in her manner on his proposal to leave; and she caught the tone, and threw the ball back. Mr. Lennox wondered how his brother, the Captain, could have reported her as having lost all her good looks. To be sure, in her quiet black dress, she was a contrast to Edith, dancing in her white crape mourning, and long floating golden hair, all softness and glitter. She dimpled and blushed most becomingly when introduced to Mr. Bell, conscious that she had her reputation as a beauty to keep up, and that it would not do to have a Mordecai refusing to worship and admire, even in the shape of an old Fellow of a College, which nobody had ever heard of. Mrs. Shaw and Captain Lennox, each in their separate way, gave Mr. Bell a kind and sincere welcome, winning him over to like them almost in spite of himself, especially when he saw how naturally Margaret took her place as sister and daughter of the house.\n\n“What a shame that we were not at home to receive you,” said Edith. “You, too, Henry! though I don’t know that we should have stayed at home for you. And for Mr. Bell! for Margaret’s Mr. Bell——”\n\n“There is no knowing what sacrifices you would not have made,” said her brother-in-law. “Even a dinner-party! and the delight of wearing this very becoming dress.”\n\nEdith did not know whether to frown or to smile, but it did not suit Mr. Lennox to drive her to the first of these alternatives; so he went on.\n\n“Will you show your readiness to make sacrifices to-morrow morning, first by asking me to breakfast, to meet Mr. Bell, and secondly, by being so kind as to order it at half-past nine, instead of at ten o’clock? I have some letters and papers that I want to show to Miss Hale and Mr. Bell.”\n\n“I hope Mr. Bell will make our house his own during his stay in London,” said Captain Lennox. “I am only so sorry we cannot offer him a bedroom.”\n\n“Thank you. I am much obliged to you. You would only think me a churl if you had, for I should decline it, I believe, in spite of all the temptations of such agreeable company,” said Mr. Bell, bowing all round, and secretly congratulating himself on the neat turn he had given to his sentence, which, if put into plain language, would have been more to this effect: “I couldn’t stand the restraints of such a proper-behaved and civil-spoken people as these are: it would be like meat without salt. I’m thankful they haven’t a bed. And how well I rounded my sentence! I’m absolutely catching the trick of good manners.”\n\nHis self-satisfaction lasted him till he was fairly out in the streets, walking side by side with Henry Lennox. Here he suddenly remembered Margaret’s little look of entreaty as she urged him to stay longer, and he also recollected a few hints given him long ago by an acquaintance of Mr. Lennox’s, as to his admiration of Margaret. It gave a new direction to his thoughts. “You have known Miss Hale for a long time, I believe. How do you think her looking? She strikes me as pale and ill.”\n\n“I thought her looking remarkably well. Perhaps not when I first came—now I think of. But certainly, when she grew animated, she looked as well as ever I saw her do.”\n\n“She has had a great deal to go through,” said Mr. Bell.\n\n“Yes! I have been sorry to hear of all she has had to bear; not merely the common and universal sorrow arising from death, but all the annoyance which her father’s conduct must have caused her, and then——”\n\n“Her father’s conduct!” said Mr. Bell, in an accent of surprise. “You must have heard some wrong statement. He behaved in the most conscientious manner. He showed more resolute strength than I should ever have given him credit for formerly.”\n\n“Perhaps I have been wrongly informed. But I have been told, by his successor in the living—a clever sensible man, and a thoroughly active clergyman—that there was no call upon Mr. Hale to do what he did, relinquish the living, and throw himself and his family on the tender mercies of private teaching in a manufacturing town; the bishop had offered him another living, it is true, but if he had come to entertain certain doubts, he could have remained where he was, and so had no occasion to resign. But the truth is, these country clergymen live such isolated lives—isolated, I mean, from all intercourse with men of equal cultivation with themselves, by whose minds they might regulate their own, and discover when they were going either too fast or too slow—that they are very apt to disturb themselves with imaginary doubts as to the articles of faith, and throw up certain opportunities of doing good for very uncertain fancies of their own.”\n\n“I differ from you. I do not think they are very apt to do as my poor friend Hale did.” Mr. Bell was inwardly chafing.\n\n“Perhaps I used too general an expression in saying ‘very apt.’ But certainly, their lives are such as very often to produce either inordinate self-sufficiency, or a morbid state of conscience,” replied Mr. Lennox with perfect coolness.\n\n“You don’t meet with any self-sufficiency among the lawyers, for instance?” asked Mr. Bell. “And seldom, I imagine, any cases of morbid conscience.” He was becoming more and more vexed, and forgetting his lately-caught trick of good manners. Mr. Lennox saw now that he had annoyed his companion; and as he had talked pretty much for the sake of saying something, and so passing the time away while their road lay together, he was very indifferent as to the exact side he took upon the question, and quietly came round by saying: “To be sure, there is something fine in a man of Mr. Hale’s age leaving his home of twenty years, and giving up all settled habits, for an idea which was probably erroneous—but that does not matter—an untangible thought. One cannot help admiring him, with a mixture of pity in one’s admiration, something like what one feels for Don Quixote. Such a gentleman as he was too! I shall never forget the refined and simple hospitality he showed to me that last day at Helstone.”\n\nOnly half-mollified, and yet anxious, in order to lull certain qualms of his own conscience, to believe that Mr. Hale’s conduct had a tinge of Quixotism in it, Mr. Bell growled out—“Aye! And you don’t know Milton. Such a change from Helstone! It is years since I have been at Helstone—but I’ll answer for it, it is standing there yet—every stick and every stone as it has done for the last century, while Milton! I go there every four or five years—and I was born there—yet I do assure you, I often lose my way—aye, among the very piles of warehouses that are built upon my father’s orchard. Do we part here? Well, good-night, sir; I suppose we shall meet in Harley Street to-morrow morning.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLV\n\n“Where are the sounds that swam along The buoyant air when I was young; The last vibration now is o’er, And they who listened are no more; Ah! let me close my eyes and dream.” W. S. LANDOR.\n\nThe idea of Helstone had been suggested to Mr. Bell’s waking mind by his conversation with Mr. Lennox, and all night long it ran riot through his dreams. He was again the tutor in the college where he now held the rank of Fellow; it was again a long vacation, and he was staying with his newly-married friend, the proud husband, and happy Vicar of Helstone. Over babbling brooks they took impossible leaps, which seemed to keep them whole days suspended in the air. Time and space were not, though all other things seemed real. Every event was measured by the emotions of the mind, not by its actual existence, for existence it had none. But the trees were gorgeous in their autumnal leafiness—the warm odours of flower and herb came sweet upon the sense—the young wife moved about the house with just that mixture of annoyance at her position, as regarded wealth, with pride in her handsome and devoted husband, which Mr. Bell had noticed in real life a quarter of a century ago. The dream was so like life that, when he awoke, his present life seemed a dream. Where was he? In the close, handsomely furnished room of a London hotel! Where were those who spoke to him, moved around him, touched him, not an instant ago? Dead! buried! lost for evermore, as far as earth’s for evermore would extend. He was an old man, so lately exultant in the full strength of manhood. The utter loneliness of his life was insupportable to think about. He got up hastily, and tried to forget what never more might be, in a hurried dressing for the breakfast in Harley Street.\n\nHe could not attend to all the lawyer’s details, which, as he saw, made Margaret’s eyes dilate, and her lips grow pale, as one by one fate decreed, or so it seemed, every morsel of evidence which would exonerate Frederick, should fall from beneath her feet and disappear. Even Mr. Lennox’s well-regulated professional voice took a softer, tenderer tone, as he drew near to the extinction of the last hope. It was not that Margaret had not been perfectly aware of the result before. It was only that the details of each successive disappointment came with such relentless minuteness to quench all hope, that she at last fairly gave way to tears. Mr. Lennox stopped reading.\n\n“I had better not go on,” said he, in a concerned voice. “It was a foolish proposal of mine. Lieutenant Hale,” and even this giving him the title of the service from which he had so harshly been expelled, was soothing to Margaret. “Lieutenant Hale is happy now; more secure in fortune and future prospects than he could ever have been in the navy; and has, doubtless, adopted his wife’s country as his own.”\n\n“That is it,” said Margaret. “It seems so selfish in me to regret it,” trying to smile, “and yet he is lost to me, and I am so lonely.” Mr. Lennox turned over his papers, and wished that he were as rich and prosperous as he believed he should be some day. Mr. Bell blew his nose, but, otherwise, he also kept silence; and Margaret, in a minute or two, had apparently recovered her usual composure. She thanked Mr. Lennox very courteously for his trouble; all the more courteously and graciously because she was conscious that, by her behaviour, he might probably be led to imagine that he had given her needless pain. Yet it was pain she would not have been without.\n\nMr. Bell came up to wish her good-bye.\n\n“Margaret!” said he, as he fumbled with his gloves, “I am going down to Helstone to-morrow, to look at the old place. Would you like to come with me? Or would it give you too much pain? Speak out, don’t be afraid.”\n\n“Oh, Mr. Bell,” said she—and could say no more. But she took his old gouty hand, and kissed it.\n\n“Come, come; that’s enough,” said he, reddening with awkwardness. “I suppose your aunt Shaw will trust you with me. We’ll go to-morrow morning, and we shall get there about two o’clock, I fancy. We’ll take a snack, and order dinner at the little inn—the Lennard Arms, it used to be—and go and get an appetite in the forest. Can you stand it, Margaret? It will be a trial, I know, to both of us, but it will be a pleasure to me, at least. And there we’ll dine—it will be but doe-venison, if we can get it at all—and then I’ll take my nap, while you go out and see old friends. I’ll give you back safe and sound, barring railway accidents, and I’ll insure your life for a thousand pounds before starting, which may be some comfort to your relations; but otherwise, I’ll bring you back to Mrs. Shaw by lunch time on Friday. So, if you say yes, I’ll just go upstairs and propose it.”\n\n“It’s no use my trying to say how much I shall like it,” said Margaret, through her tears.\n\n“Well, then, prove your gratitude by keeping those fountains of yours dry for the next two days. If you don’t, I shall feel queer myself about the lachrymal ducts, and I don’t like that.”\n\n“I won’t cry a drop,” said Margaret, winking her eyes to shake the tears off her eyelashes, and forcing a smile.\n\n“There’s my good girl. Then we’ll go upstairs and settle it all.” Margaret was in a state of almost trembling eagerness, while Mr. Bell discussed his plan with her aunt Shaw, who was first startled, then doubtful and perplexed, and in the end, yielding rather to the rough force of Mr. Bell’s words than to her own conviction; for to the last, whether it was right or wrong, proper or improper, she could not settle to her own satisfaction, till Margaret’s safe return, the happy fulfilment of the project, gave her decision enough to say, “she was sure it had been a very kind thought of Mr. Bell’s, and just what she herself had been wishing for Margaret, as giving her the very change which she required, after all the anxious time she had had.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI\n\n“So on those happy days of yore Oft as I dare to dwell once more, Still must I miss the friends so tried, Whom Death has severed from my side.\n\nBut ever when true friendship binds, Spirit it is that spirit finds; In spirit then our bliss we found, In spirit yet to them I’m bound.” UHLAND.\n\nMargaret was ready long before the appointed time, and had leisure enough to cry a little, quietly, when unobserved, and to smile brightly when any one looked at her. Her last alarm was lest they should be too late and miss the train; but no! they were all in time; and she breathed freely and happily at length, seated in the carriage opposite to Mr. Bell, and whirling away past the well-known stations; seeing the old south country-towns and hamlets sleeping in the warm light of the pure sun, which gave a yet ruddier colour to their tiled roofs, so different to the cold slates of the north. Broods of pigeons hovered around these peaked quaint gables, slowly settling here and there, and ruffling their soft, shiny feathers, as if exposing every fibre to the delicious warmth. There were few people about at the stations, it almost seemed as if they were too lazily content to wish to travel; none of the bustle and stir that Margaret had noticed in her two journeys on the London and North-Western line. Later on in the year, this line of railway should be stirring and alive with rich pleasure-seekers; but as to the constant going to and fro of busy tradespeople it would always be widely different from the northern lines. Here a spectator or two stood lounging at nearly every station, with his hands in his pockets, so absorbed in the simple act of watching, that it made the travellers wonder what he could find to do when the train whirled away, and only the blank of a railway, some sheds, and a distant field or two were left for him to gaze upon. The hot air danced over the golden stillness of the land, farm after farm was left behind, each reminding Margaret of German Idylls—of Herman and Dorothea—of Evangeline. From this waking dream she was roused. It was the place to leave the train and take the fly to Helstone. And now sharper feelings came shooting through her heart, whether pain or pleasure she could hardly tell. Every mile was redolent of associations, which she would not have missed for the world, but each of which made her cry upon “the days that are no more,” with ineffable longing. The last time she had passed along this road was when she had left it with her father and mother—the day, the season, had been gloomy, and she herself hopeless, but they were there with her. Now she was alone, an orphan, and they, strangely, had gone away from her, and vanished from the face of the earth. It hurt her to see the Helstone road so flooded in the sunlight, and every turn and every familiar tree so precisely the same in its summer glory as it had been in former years. Nature felt no change and was ever young.\n\nMr. Bell knew something of what would be passing through her mind, and wisely and kindly held his tongue. They drove up to the Lennard Arms; half farm-house, half-inn, standing a little apart from the road, as much as to say, that the host did not so depend on the custom of travellers, as to have to court it by any obtrusiveness; they, rather, must seek him out. The house fronted the village green; and right before it stood an immemorial lime-tree benched all round, in some hidden recesses of whose leafy wealth hung the grim escutcheon of the Lennards. The door of the inn stood wide open, but there was no hospitable hurry to receive the travellers. When the landlady did appear—and they might have abstracted many an article first—she gave them a kind welcome, almost as if they had been invited guests, and apologised for her coming having been so delayed, by saying, that it was hay-time, and the provisions for the men had to be sent a-field, and she had been too busy packing up the baskets to hear the noise of wheels over the road, which, since they left the highway, ran over soft short turf.\n\n“Why, bless me!” exclaimed she, as at the end of her apology a glint of sunshine showed her Margaret’s face, hitherto unobserved in that shady parlour. “It’s Miss Hale, Jenny,” said she, running to the door, and calling to her daughter. “Come here, come directly, it’s Miss Hale!” And then she went up to Margaret, and shook hands with motherly fondness.\n\n“And how are you all? How’s the Vicar and Miss Dixon? The Vicar above all! God bless him! We’ve never ceased to be sorry that he left.”\n\nMargaret tried to speak and tell her of her father’s death; of her mother’s it was evident that Mrs. Purkis was aware, from her omission of her name. But she choked in the effort, and could only touch her deep mourning, and say the one word, “Papa.”\n\n“Surely, sir, it’s never so!” said Mrs. Purkis, turning to Mr. Bell for confirmation of the sad suspicion that now entered her mind. “There was a gentleman here in the spring—might have been as long ago as last winter—who told us a deal of Mr. Hale and Miss Margaret; and he said Mrs. Hale was gone, poor lady. But never a word of the Vicar’s being ailing!”\n\n“It is so, however,” said Mr. Bell. “He died quite suddenly, when on a visit to me at Oxford. He was a good man, Mrs. Purkis, and there’s many of us that might be thankful to have as calm an end as his. Come, Margaret, my dear! Her father was my oldest friend, and she’s my god-daughter, so I thought we would just come down together and see the old place; and I know of old you can give us comfortable rooms and a capital dinner. You don’t remember me I see, but my name is Bell, and once or twice when the parsonage has been full, I’ve slept here, and tasted your good ale.”\n\n“To be sure; I ask your pardon; but you see I was taken up with Miss Hale. Let me show you to a room, Miss Margaret, where you can take off your bonnet, and wash your face. It’s only this very morning I plunged some fresh-gathered roses head downward in the water-jug, for thought I, perhaps some one will be coming, and there’s nothing so sweet as spring-water scented by a musk rose or two. To think of the Vicar being dead! Well, to be sure, we must all die; only that gentleman said, he was quite picking up after his trouble about Mrs. Hale’s death.”\n\n“Come down to me, Mrs. Purkis, after you have attended to Miss Hale. I want to have a consultation with you about dinner.”\n\nThe little casement window in Margaret’s bed-chamber was almost filled up with rose and vine branches; but, pushing them aside, and stretching a little out, she could see the tops of the parsonage chimneys above the trees, and distinguish many a well-known line through the leaves.\n\n“Aye!” said Mrs. Purkis, smoothing down the bed, and despatching Jenny for an armful of lavender-scented towels, “times is changed, miss; our new Vicar has seven children, and is building a nursery ready for more, just where the arbour and tool-house used to be in old times. And he has had new grates put in, and a plate-glass window in the drawing-room. He and his wife are stirring people, and have done a deal of good; at least they say it’s doing good; if it were not, I should call it turning things upside down for very little purpose. The new Vicar is a teetotaller, miss, and a magistrate, and his wife has a deal of receipts for economical cooking, and is for making bread without yeast; and they both talk so much, and both at a time, that they knock one down as it were, and it’s not till they’re gone, and one’s a little at peace, that one can think that there were things one might have said on one’s own side of the question. He’ll be after the men’s cans in the hay-field, and peeping in; and then there’ll be an ado because it’s not ginger-beer, but I can’t help it. My mother and my grandmother before me sent good malt liquor to hay-makers, and took salts and senna when anything ailed them; and I must e’en go on in their ways, though Mrs. Hepworth does want to give me comfits instead of medicine, which, as she says, is a deal pleasanter, only I’ve no faith in it. But I must go, miss, though I’m wanting to hear many a thing; I’ll come back to you before long.”\n\nMr. Bell had strawberries and cream, a loaf of brown bread, and a jug of milk (together with a Stilton cheese and a bottle of port for his own private refreshment), ready for Margaret on her coming down stairs; and after this rustic luncheon they set out for a walk, hardly knowing in what direction to turn, so many old familiar inducements were there in each.\n\n“Shall we go past the vicarage?” asked Mr. Bell.\n\n“No, not yet. We will go this way, and make a round so as to come back by it,” said Margaret.\n\nHere and there old trees had been felled the autumn before; or a squatter’s cottage roughly-built and decaying cottage had disappeared. Margaret missed them each and all, and grieved over them like old friends. They came past the spot where she and Mr. Lennox had sketched. The white, lightning-scarred trunk of the venerable beech, among whose roots they had sate down was there no more; the old man, the inhabitant of the ruinous cottage, was dead; the cottage had been pulled down, and a new one, tidy and respectable, had been built in its stead. There was a small garden on the place where the beech-tree had been.\n\n“I did not think I had been so old,” said Margaret after a pause of silence; and she turned away sighing.\n\n“Yes!” said Mr. Bell. “It is the first changes among familiar things that make such a mystery of time to the young, afterwards we lose the sense of the mysterious. I take changes in all I see as a matter of course. The instability of all human things is familiar to me, to you it is new and oppressive.”\n\n“Let us go on to see little Susan,” said Margaret, drawing her companion up a grassy road-way, leading under the shadow of a forest glade.\n\n“With all my heart, though I have not an idea who little Susan may be. But I have a kindness for all Susans, for simple Susan’s sake.”\n\n“My little Susan was disappointed when I left without wishing her good-bye; and it has been on my conscience ever since, that I gave her pain which a little more exertion on my part might have prevented. But it is a long way. Are you sure you will not be tired?”\n\n“Quite sure. That is, if you don’t walk so fast. You see, here there are no views that can give one an excuse for stopping to take breath. You would think it romantic to be walking with a person ‘fat and scant o’ breath’ if I were Hamlet, Prince of Denmark. Have compassion on my infirmities for his sake.”\n\n“I will walk slower for your own sake. I like you twenty times better than Hamlet.”\n\n“On the principle that a living ass is better than a dead lion?”\n\n“Perhaps so. I don’t analyse my feelings.”\n\n“I am content to take your liking me, without examining too curiously into the materials it is made of. Only we need not walk at a snail’s pace.”\n\n“Very well. Walk at your own pace, and I will follow. Or stop still and meditate, like the Hamlet you compare yourself to, if I go too fast.”\n\n“Thank you. But as my mother has not murdered my father, and afterwards married my uncle, I shouldn’t know what to think about, unless it were balancing the chances of our having a well-cooked dinner or not. What do you think?”\n\n“I am in good hopes. She used to be considered a famous cook as far as Helstone opinion went.”\n\n“But have you considered the distraction of mind produced by all this haymaking?”\n\nMargaret felt all Mr. Bell’s kindness in trying to make cheerful talk about nothing, to endeavour to prevent her from thinking too curiously about the past. But she would rather have gone over these dear-loved walks in silence, if indeed she were not ungrateful enough to wish that she might have been alone.\n\nThey reached the cottage where Susan’s widowed mother lived. Susan was not there. She was gone to the parochial school. Margaret was disappointed, and the poor woman saw it, and began to make a kind of apology.\n\n“Oh! it is quite right,” said Margaret. “I am very glad to hear it. I might have thought of it. Only she used to stop at home with you.”\n\n“Yes, she did; and I miss her sadly. I used to teach her what little I knew at nights. It were not much to be sure. But she were getting such a handy girl, that I miss her sore. But she’s a deal above me in learning now.” And the mother sighed.\n\n“I’m all wrong,” growled Mr. Bell. “Don’t mind what I say. I’m a hundred years behind the world. But I should say, that the child was getting a better and simpler, and more natural education stopping at home, and helping her mother, and learning to read a chapter in the New Testament every night by her side, than from all the schooling under the sun.”\n\nMargaret did not want to encourage him to go on by replying to him, and so prolonging the discussion before the mother. So she turned to her and asked,\n\n“How is old Betty Barnes?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said the woman rather shortly. “We’se not friends.”\n\n“Why not?” asked Margaret, who had formerly been the peace-maker of the village.\n\n“She stole my cat.”\n\n“Did she know it was yours?”\n\n“I don’t know. I reckon not.”\n\n“Well! could not you get it back again when you told her it was yours?”\n\n“No! for she’d burnt it.”\n\n“Burnt it!” exclaimed both Margaret and Mr. Bell.\n\n“Roasted it!” explained the woman.\n\nIt was no explanation. By dint of questioning, Margaret extracted from her the horrible fact that Betty Barnes, having been induced by a gipsy fortune-teller to lend the latter her husband’s Sunday clothes, on promise of having them faithfully returned on the Saturday night before Goodman Barnes should have missed them, became alarmed by their non-appearance, and her consequent dread of her husband’s anger, and as, according to one of the savage country superstitions, the cries of a cat, in the agonies of being boiled or roasted alive; compelled (as it were) the powers of darkness to fulfil the wishes of the executioner, resort had been had to the charm. The poor woman evidently believed in its efficacy; her only feeling was indignation that her cat had been chosen out from all others for a sacrifice. Margaret listened in horror; and endeavoured in vain to enlighten the woman’s mind; but she was obliged to give it up in despair. Step by step she got the woman to admit certain facts, of which the logical connexion and sequence was perfectly clear to Margaret; but at the end, the bewildered woman simply repeated her first assertion, namely, that “it were very cruel for sure, and she should not like to do it; but that there was nothing like it for giving a person what they wished for; she had heard it all her life; but it were very cruel for all that.” Margaret gave it up in despair, and walked away sick at heart.\n\n“You are a good girl not to triumph over me,” said Mr. Bell.\n\n“How? What do you mean?”\n\n“I own I am wrong about schooling. Anything rather than have that child brought up in such practical paganism.”\n\n“Oh! I remember. Poor little Susan! I must go and see her; would you mind calling at the school?”\n\n“Not a bit. I am curious to see something of the teaching she is to receive.”\n\nThey did not speak much more, but thridded their way through many a bosky dell, whose soft green influence could not charm away the shock and the pain in Margaret’s heart, caused by the recital of such cruelty; a recital too, the manner of which betrayed such utter want of imagination, and therefore of any sympathy with the suffering animal.\n\nThe buzz of voices, like the murmur of a hive of busy human bees, made itself heard as soon as they emerged from the forest on the more open village-green on which the school was situated. The door was wide open, and they entered. A brisk lady in black, here, there, and everywhere, perceived them, and bade them welcome with somewhat of the hostess-air which, Margaret remembered, her mother was wont to assume, only in a more soft and languid manner, when any rare visitors strayed in to inspect the school. She knew at once it was the present Vicar’s wife, her mother’s successor; and she would have drawn back from the interview had it been possible; but in an instant she had conquered this feeling, and modestly advanced, meeting many a bright glance of recognition, and hearing many a half-suppressed murmur of “It’s Miss Hale.” The Vicar’s lady heard the name, and her manner at once became more kindly. Margaret wished she could have helped feeling that it also became more patronizing. The lady held out a hand to Mr. Bell, with,—\n\n“Your father, I presume, Miss Hale. I see it by the likeness. I am sure I am very glad to see you, sir, and so will the Vicar be.”\n\nMargaret explained that it was not her father, and stammered out the fact of his death; wondering all the time how Mr. Hale could have borne coming to revisit Helstone, if it had been as the Vicar’s lady supposed. She did not hear what Mrs. Hepworth was saying, and left it to Mr. Bell to reply, looking round, meanwhile, for her old acquaintances.\n\n“Ah! I see you would like to take a class, Miss Hale. I know it by myself. First class stand up for a parsing lesson with Miss Hale.”\n\nPoor Margaret, whose visit was sentimental, not in any degree inspective, felt herself taken in; but as in some way bringing her in contact with little eager faces, once well-known, and who had received the solemn rite of baptism from her father, she sate down, half losing herself in tracing out the changing features of the girls, and holding Susan’s hand for a minute or two, unobserved by all, while the first class sought for their books, and the Vicar’s lady went as near as a lady could towards holding Mr. Bell by the button, while she explained the Phonetic system to him, and gave him a conversation she had had with the Inspector about it.\n\nMargaret bent over her book, and seeing nothing but that—hearing the buzz of children’s voices, old times rose up, and she thought of them, and her eyes filled with tears, till all at once there was a pause—one of the girls was stumbling over the apparently simple word “a,” uncertain what to call it.\n\n“A, an indefinite article,” said Margaret, mildly.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” said the Vicar’s wife, all eyes and ears; “but we are taught by Mr. Milsome to call ‘a’ an—who can remember?”\n\n“An adjective absolute,” said half-a-dozen voices at once. And Margaret sate abashed. The children knew more than she did. Mr. Bell turned away, and smiled.\n\nMargaret spoke no more during the lesson. But after it was over, she went quietly round to one or two old favourites, and talked to them a little. They were growing out of children into great girls; passing out of their recollection in their rapid development, as she, by her three year’s absence, was vanishing from theirs. Still she was glad to have seen them all again, though a tinge of sadness mixed itself with her pleasure. When school was over for the day, it was yet early in the summer afternoon; and Mrs. Hepworth proposed to Margaret that she and Mr. Bell should accompany her to the parsonage, and see—the word “improvements” had half slipped out of her mouth, but she substituted the more cautious term “alterations” which the present Vicar was making. Margaret did not care a straw about seeing the alterations, which jarred upon her fond recollection of what her home had been; but she longed to see the old place once more, even though she shivered away from the pain which she knew she should feel.\n\nThe parsonage was so altered, both inside and out, that the real pain was less than she anticipated. It was not like the same place. The garden, the grass-plat, formerly so daintily trim that even a stray rose-leaf seemed like a fleck on the exquisite arrangement and propriety, was strewed with children’s things; a bag of marbles here, a hoop there; a straw-hat forced down upon a rose-tree as on a peg, to the destruction of a long beautiful tender branch laden with flowers, which in former days would have been trained up tenderly, as if beloved. The little square matted hall was equally filled with signs of merry healthy rough childhood.\n\n“Ah!” said Mrs. Hepworth, “you must excuse this untidiness, Miss Hale. When the nursery is finished, I shall insist upon a little order. We are building a nursery out of your room, I believe. How did you manage, Miss Hale, without a nursery?”\n\n“We were but two,” said Margaret. “You have many children, I presume?”\n\n“Seven. Look here! we are throwing out a window to the road on this side. Mr. Hepworth is spending an immense deal of money on this house; but really it was scarcely habitable when we came—for so large a family as ours I mean, of course.” Every room in the house was changed, besides the one of which Mrs. Hepworth spoke, which had been Mr. Hale’s study formerly; and where the green gloom and delicious quiet of the place had conduced, as he had said, to a habit of meditation, but, perhaps, in some degree to the formation of a character more fitted for thought than action. The new window gave a view of the road, and had many advantages, as Mrs. Hepworth pointed out. From it the wandering sheep of her husband’s flock might be seen, who straggled to the tempting beer-house, unobserved as they might hope, but not unobserved in reality; for the active Vicar kept his eye on the road, even during the composition of his most orthodox sermons, and had a hat and stick hanging ready at hand to seize, before sallying out after his parishioners, who had need of quick legs if they could take refuge in the “Jolly Forester” before the teetotal Vicar had arrested them. The whole family were quick, brisk, loud-talking, kind-hearted, and not troubled with much delicacy of perception. Margaret feared that Mrs. Hepworth would find out that Mr. Bell was playing upon her, in the admiration he thought fit to express for everything that especially grated on his taste. But no! she took it all literally, and with such good faith, that Margaret could not help remonstrating with him as they walked slowly away from the parsonage back to their inn.\n\n“Don’t scold, Margaret. It was all because of you. If she had not shown you every change with such evident exultation in their superior sense, in perceiving what an improvement this and that would be, I could have behaved well. But if you must go on preaching, keep it till after dinner, when it will send me to sleep, and help my digestion.”\n\nThey were both of them tired, and Margaret herself so much so, that she was unwilling to go out as she had proposed to do, and have another ramble among the woods and fields so close to the home of her childhood. And, somehow, this visit to Helstone had not been all—had not been exactly what she had expected. There was change everywhere; slight, yet pervading all. Households were changed by absence, or death, or marriage, or the natural mutations brought by days and months and years, which carry us on imperceptibly from childhood to youth, and thence through manhood to age, whence we drop like fruit, fully ripe, into the quiet mother earth. Places were changed—a tree gone here, a bough there, bringing in a long ray of light where no light was before—a road was trimmed and narrowed, and the green straggling pathway by its side enclosed and cultivated. A great improvement it was called; but Margaret sighed over the old picturesqueness, the old gloom, and the grassy way-side of former days. She sate by the window on the little settle, sadly gazing out upon the gathering shades of night, which harmonized well with her pensive thought. Mr. Bell slept soundly, after his unusual exercise through the day. At last he was roused by the entrance of the tea-tray, brought in by a flushed-looking country-girl, who had evidently been finding some variety from her usual occupation of waiter, in assisting this day in the hay-field.\n\n“Hallo! Who’s there! Where are we? Who’s that,—Margaret? Oh, now I remember all. I could not imagine what woman was sitting there in such a doleful attitude, with her hands clasped straight out upon her knees, and her face looking so steadfastly before her. What are you looking at?” asked Mr. Bell, coming to the window, and standing behind Margaret.\n\n“Nothing,” said she, rising up quickly, and speaking as cheerfully as she could at a moment’s notice.\n\n“Nothing indeed! A bleak back-ground of trees, some white linen hung out on the sweet-briar hedge, and a great waft of damp air. Shut the window, and come in and make tea.”\n\nMargaret was silent for some time. She played with her teaspoon, and did not attend particularly to what Mr. Bell said. He contradicted her, and she took the same sort of smiling notice of his opinion as if he had agreed with her. Then she sighed, and putting down her spoon, she began, apropos of nothing at all, and in the high-pitched voice which usually shows that the speaker has been thinking for some time on the subject that they wish to introduce—“Mr. Bell, you remember what we were saying about Frederick last night, don’t you?”\n\n“Last night. Where was I? Oh, I remember! Why, it seems a week ago. Yes, to be sure, I recollect we talked about him, poor fellow.”\n\n“Yes—and do you remember that Mr. Lennox spoke about his having been in England about the time of dear mamma’s death?” asked Margaret, her voice now lower than usual.\n\n“I recollect. I hadn’t heard of it before.”\n\n“And I thought—I always thought that papa had told you about it.”\n\n“No! he never did. But what about it, Margaret?”\n\n“I want to tell you of something I did that was very wrong, about that time,” said Margaret, suddenly looking up at him with her clear honest eyes. “I told a lie!” and her face became scarlet.\n\n“True, that was bad I own; not but what I have told a pretty round number in my life, not all in downright words, as I suppose you did, but in actions, or in some shabby circumlocutory way, leading people either to disbelieve the truth, or believe a falsehood. You know who is the father of lies, Margaret? Well! a great number of folk, thinking themselves very good, have odd sorts of connexion with lies, left-hand marriages, and second-cousins-once-removed. The tainting blood of falsehood runs through us all. I should have guessed you as far from it as most people. What! crying, child? Nay, now we’ll not talk of it, if it ends in this way. I dare say you have been sorry for it, and that you won’t do it again, and it’s long ago now, and in short I want you to be very cheerful, and not very sad, this evening.”\n\nMargaret wiped her eyes, and tried to talk about something else, but suddenly she burst out afresh.\n\n“Please, Mr. Bell, let me tell you about it—you could perhaps help me a little; no, not help me, but if you knew the truth, perhaps you could put me to rights—that is not it, after all,” said she, in despair at not being able to express herself more exactly as she wished.\n\nMr. Bell’s whole manner changed. “Tell me all about it, child,” said he.\n\n“It’s a long story; but when Fred came, mamma was very ill, and I was undone with anxiety, and afraid, too, that I might have drawn him into danger; and we had an alarm just after her death, for Dixon met some one in Milton—a man called Leonards—who had known Fred, and who seemed to owe him a grudge, or at any rate to be tempted by the recollection of the reward offered for his apprehension; and with this new fright, I thought I had better hurry off Fred to London, where, as you would understand from what we said the other night, he was to go to consult Mr. Lennox as to his chances if he stood trial. So we—that is, he and I,—went to the railway station; it was one evening, and it was just getting rather dusk, but still light enough to recognize and be recognized, and we were too early, and went out to walk in a field just close by; I was always in a panic about this Leonards, who was, I knew, somewhere in the neighbourhood; and then, when we were in the field, the low red sunlight just in my face, some one came by on horseback in the road just below the field-style on which we stood.—I saw him look at me, but I did not know who it was at first, the sun was so in my eyes, but in an instant the dazzle went off, and I saw it was Mr. Thornton, and we bowed”——\n\n“And he saw Frederick of course,” said Mr. Bell, helping her on with her story, as he thought.\n\n“Yes; and then at the station a man came up—tipsy and reeling—and he tried to collar Fred, and over-balanced himself as Fred wrenched himself away, and fell over the hedge of the platform; not far, not deep; not above three feet; but oh! Mr. Bell, somehow that fall killed him!”\n\n“How awkward. It was this Leonards, I suppose. And how did Fred get off?”\n\n“Oh! he went off immediately after the fall, which we never thought could have done the poor fellow any harm, it seemed so slight an injury.”\n\n“Then he did not die directly?”\n\n“No! not for two or three days. And then—oh, Mr. Bell! now comes the bad part,” said she, nervously twining her fingers together. “A police inspector came and taxed me with having been a companion of the young man, whose push or blow had occasioned Leonards’ death; that was a false accusation, you know, but we had not heard that Fred had sailed, he might still be in London and liable to be arrested on this false charge, and his identity with the Lieutenant Hale, accused of causing the mutiny, discovered, he might be shot; all this flashed through my mind, and I said it was not me. I was not at the railway station that night. I knew nothing about it. I had no conscience or thought but to save Frederick.”\n\n“I say it was right. I should have done the same. You forgot yourself in thought for another. I hope I should have done the same.”\n\n“No, you would not. It was wrong, disobedient, faithless. At that very time Fred was safely out of England, and in my blindness I forgot that there was another witness who could testify to my being there.”\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Mr. Thornton. You know he had seen me close to the station; we had bowed to each other.”\n\n“Well! he would know nothing of this riot, about the drunken fellow’s death. I suppose the injury never came to anything.”\n\n“No! the proceedings they had begun to talk about on the inquest were stopped. Mr. Thornton did know all about it. He was a magistrate, and he found out that it was not the fall that had caused the death. But not before he knew what I had said. Oh, Mr. Bell!” She suddenly covered her face with her hands, as if wishing to hide herself from the presence of the recollection.\n\n“Did you have any explanation with him? Did you ever tell him the strong, instinctive motive?”\n\n“The instinctive want of faith, and clutching at a sin to keep myself from sinking,” said she bitterly. “No! How could I? He knew nothing of Frederick. To put myself to rights in his good opinion, was I to tell him the secrets of our family, involving, as they seemed to do, the chances of poor Frederick’s entire exculpation? Fred’s last words had been to enjoin me to keep his visit a secret from all. You see, papa never told, even you. No! I could bear the shame—I thought I could at least. I did bear it. Mr. Thornton has never respected me since.”\n\n“He respects you, I am sure,” said Mr. Bell. “To be sure it accounts a little for——. But he always speaks of you with regard and esteem, though now I understand certain reservations in his manner.”\n\nMargaret did not speak; did not attend to what Mr. Bell had to say; lost all sense of it. By-and-by she said:\n\n“Will you tell me what you refer to about ‘reservations’ in his manner of speaking to me?”\n\n“Oh! simply he has annoyed me by not joining in my praises of you. Like an old fool, I thought that every one would have the same opinion as I had; and he evidently could not agree with me. I was puzzled at the time. But he must be perplexed, if the affair has never been in the least explained. There was first your walking out with a young man in the dark——”\n\n“But it was my brother!” said Margaret, surprised.\n\n“True. But how was he to know that?”\n\n“I don’t know. I never thought anything of that kind,” said Margaret, reddening, and looking hurt and offended.\n\n“And perhaps he never would, but for the lie,—which, under the circumstances, I maintain, was necessary.”\n\n“It was not. I know it now. I bitterly repent it.”\n\nThere was a long pause of silence. Margaret was the first to speak.\n\n“I am not likely ever to see Mr. Thornton again,”—and there she stopped.\n\n“There are many things more unlikely, I should say,” replied Mr. Bell.\n\n“But I believe I never shall. Still, somehow one does not like to have sunk so low in—in a friend’s opinion as I have done in his.” Her eyes were full of tears, but her voice was steady, and Mr. Bell was not looking at her. “And now that Frederick has given up all hope, and almost all wish of ever clearing himself, and returning to England, it would be only doing myself justice to have all this explained. If you please, and if you can, if there is a good opportunity, (don’t force an explanation upon him, pray,) but if you can, will you tell him the whole circumstances, and tell him also that I gave you leave to do so, because I felt that for poor papa’s sake I should not like to lose his respect, though we may never be likely to meet again?”\n\n“Certainly. I think he ought to know. I do not like you to rest even under a shadow of an impropriety; he would not know what to think of seeing you alone with a young man.”\n\n“As for that,” said Margaret, rather haughtily, “I hold it is ‘Honi soit qui mal y pense,’ Yet still I should choose to have it explained, if any natural opportunity for easy explanation occurs. But it is not to clear myself of any suspicion of improper conduct that I wish to have him told—if I thought that he had suspected me, I should not care for his good opinion—no! it is that he may learn how I was tempted, and how I fell into the snare; why I told that falsehood, in short.”\n\n“Which I don’t blame you for. It is no partiality of mine, I assure you.”\n\n“What other people may think of the rightness or wrongness is nothing in comparison to my own deep knowledge, my innate conviction that it was wrong. But we will not talk of that any more, if you please. It is done—my sin is sinned. I have now to put it behind me, and be truthful for evermore, if I can.”\n\n“Very well. If you like to be uncomfortable and morbid, be so. I always keep my conscience as tight shut up as a jack-in-a-box, for when it jumps into existence it surprises me by its size. So I coax it down again, as the fisherman coaxed the genii. ‘Wonderful,’ says I, ‘to think that you have been concealed so long, and in so small a compass, that I really did not know of your existence. Pray, sir, instead of growing larger and larger every instant, and bewildering me with your misty outlines, would you once more compress yourself into your former dimensions?’ And when I’ve got him down, don’t I clap the seal on the vase, and take good care how I open it again, and how I go against Solomon, wisest of men, who confined him there.”\n\nBut it was no smiling matter to Margaret. She hardly attended to what Mr. Bell was saying. Her thoughts ran upon the idea, before entertained, but which now had assumed the strength of a conviction, that Mr. Thornton no longer held his former good opinion of her—that he was disappointed in her. She did not feel as if any explanation could ever reinstate her—not in his love, for that and any return on her part she had resolved never to dwell upon, and she kept rigidly to her resolution—but in the respect and high regard which she had hoped would have ever made him willing, in the spirit of Gerald Griffin’s beautiful lines,\n\n“To turn and look back when thou hearest The sound of my name.”\n\nShe kept choking and swallowing all the time that she thought about it. She tried to comfort herself with the idea, that what he imagined her to be, did not alter the fact of what she was. But it was a truism, a phantom, and broke down under the weight of her regret. She had twenty questions on the tip of her tongue to ask Mr. Bell, but not one of them did she utter. Mr. Bell thought that she was tired, and sent her early to her room, where she sate long hours by the open window, gazing out on the purple dome above, where the stars arose, and twinkled and disappeared behind the great umbrageous trees before she went to bed. All night long too, there burnt a little light on earth; a candle in her old bedroom, which was the nursery with the present inhabitants of the parsonage, until the new one was built. A sense of change, of individual nothingness, of perplexity and disappointment, overpowered Margaret. Nothing had been the same; and this slight, all-pervading instability, had given her greater pain than if all had been too entirely changed for her to recognise it.\n\n“I begin to understand now what heaven must be—and, oh! the grandeur and repose of the words—‘The same yesterday, to-day, and for ever.’ Everlasting! ‘From everlasting to everlasting, Thou art God.’ That beautiful sky above me looks as though it could not change, and yet it will. I am so tired—so tired of being whirled on through all these phases of my life, in which nothing abides by me, no creature, no place; it is like the circle in which the victims of earthly passion eddy continually. I am in the mood in which women of another religion take the veil. I seek heavenly steadfastness in earthly monotony. If I were a Roman Catholic and could deaden my heart, stun it with some great blow, I might become a nun. But I should pine after my kind; no, not my kind, for love for my species could never fill my heart to the utter exclusion of love for individuals. Perhaps it ought to be so, perhaps not; I cannot decide to-night.”\n\nWearily she went to bed, wearily she arose in four or five hours’ time. But with the morning came hope, and a brighter view of things.\n\n“After all it is right,” said she, hearing the voices of children at play while she was dressing. “If the world stood still, it would retrograde and become corrupt, if that is not Irish. Looking out of myself and my own painful sense of change, the progress of all around me is right and necessary. I must not think so much of how circumstances affect me myself, but how they affect others, if I wish to have a right judgment, or a hopeful trustful heart.” And with a smile ready in her eyes to quiver down to her lips, she went into the parlour and greeted Mr. Bell.\n\n“Ah, Missy! you were up late last night, and so you’re late this morning. Now I’ve got a little piece of news for you. What do you think of an invitation to dinner? a morning call, literally in the dewy morning. Why, I’ve had the Vicar here already, on his way to the school. How much the desire of giving our hostess a teetotal lecture for the benefit of the hay-makers, had to do with his earliness, I don’t know; but here he was, when I came down just before nine; and we are asked to dine there to-day.”\n\n“But Edith expects me back—I cannot go,” said Margaret, thankful to have so good an excuse.\n\n“Yes! I know; so I told him. I thought you would not want to go. Still it is open, if you would like it.”\n\n“Oh, no!” said Margaret. “Let us keep to our plan. Let us start at twelve. It is very good and kind of them; but indeed I could not go.”\n\n“Very well. Don’t fidget yourself, and I’ll arrange it all.”\n\nBefore they left, Margaret stole round to the back of the Vicarage garden, and gathered a little straggling piece of honeysuckle. She would not take a flower the day before, for fear of being observed, and her motives and feelings commented upon. But as she returned across the common, the place was reinvested with the old enchanting atmosphere. The common sounds of life were more musical there than anywhere else in the whole world, the light more golden, the life more tranquil and full of dreamy delight. As Margaret remembered her feelings yesterday, she said to herself:\n\n“And I too change perpetually—now this, now that—now disappointment and peevish because all is not exactly as I had pictured it, and now suddenly discovering that the reality is far more beautiful than I had imagined it. Oh, Helstone! I shall never love any place like you.”\n\nA few days afterwards, she had found her level, and decided that she was very glad to have been there, and that she had seen it again, and that to her it would always be the prettiest spot in the world, but that it was so full of associations with former days, and especially with her father and mother, that if it were all to come over again, she should shrink back from such another visit as that which she had paid with Mr. Bell.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII\n\n“Experience, like a pale musician, holds A dulcimer of patience in his hand; Whence harmonies we cannot understand, Of God’s will in His worlds, the strain unfolds In sad, perplexed minors.” MRS. BROWNING.\n\nAbout this time Dixon returned from Milton, and assumed her post as Margaret’s maid. She brought endless pieces of Milton gossip: How Martha had gone to live with Miss Thornton, on the latter’s marriage; with an account of the bridesmaids, dresses and breakfasts, at that interesting ceremony; how people thought that Mr. Thornton had made too grand a wedding of it, considering he had lost a deal by the strike, and had had to pay so much for the failure of his contracts; how little money articles of furniture—long cherished by Dixon—had fetched at the sale, which was a shame considering how rich folks were at Milton; how Mrs. Thornton had come one day and got two or three good bargains, and Mr. Thornton had come the next, and in his desire to obtain one or two things, had bid against himself, much to the enjoyment of the bystanders, so as Dixon observed, that made things even; if Mrs. Thornton paid too little, Mr. Thornton paid too much. Mr. Bell had sent all sorts of orders about the books; there was no understanding him, he was so particular; if he had come himself it would have been all right, but letters always were and always will be more puzzling than they are worth. Dixon had not much to tell about the Higginses. Her memory had an aristocratic bias, and was very treacherous whenever she tried to recall any circumstance connected with those below her in life. Nicholas was very well she believed. He had been several times at the house asking for news of Miss Margaret—the only person who ever did ask, except once Mr. Thornton. And Mary? oh! of course she was very well, a great, stout, slatternly thing! She did hear, or perhaps it was only a dream of hers, though it would be strange if she had dreamt of such people as the Higginses, that Mary had gone to work at Mr. Thornton’s mill, because her father wished her to know how to cook; but what nonsense that could mean she didn’t know. Margaret rather agreed with her that the story was incoherent enough to be like a dream. Still it was pleasant to have some one now with whom she could talk of Milton, and Milton people. Dixon was not over-fond of the subject, rather wishing to leave that part of her life in shadow. She liked much more to dwell upon speeches of Mr. Bell’s, which had suggested an idea to her of what was really his intention—making Margaret his heiress. But her young lady gave her no encouragement, nor in any way gratified her insinuating enquiries, however disguised in the form of suspicions or assertions.\n\nAll this time Margaret had a strange undefined longing to hear that Mr. Bell had gone to pay one of his business visits to Milton; for it had been well understood between them, at the time of their conversation at Helstone, that the explanation she had desired should only be given to Mr. Thornton by word of mouth, and even in that manner should be in nowise forced upon him. Mr. Bell was no great correspondent, but he wrote from time to time long or short letters, as the humour took him, and although Margaret was not conscious of any definite hope, on receiving them, yet she always put away his notes with a little feeling of disappointment. He was not going to Milton; he said nothing about it at any rate. Well! she must be patient. Sooner or later the mists would be cleared away. Mr. Bell’s letters were hardly like his usual self; they were short, and complaining, with every now and then a little touch of bitterness that was unusual. He did not look forward to the future; he rather seemed to regret the past, and be weary of the present. Margaret fancied that he could not be well; but in answer to some enquiry of hers as to his health, he sent her a short note, saying there was an old-fashioned complaint called the spleen; that he was suffering from that, and it was for her to decide if it was more mental or physical; but that he should like to indulge himself in grumbling, without being obliged to send a bulletin every time.\n\nIn consequence of this note, Margaret made no more enquiries about his health. One day Edith let out accidentally a fragment of a conversation which she had had with Mr. Bell, when he was last in London, which possessed Margaret with the idea that he had some notion of taking her to pay a visit to her brother and new sister-in-law, at Cadiz, in the autumn. She questioned and cross-questioned Edith, till the latter was weary, and declared that there was nothing more to remember; all he had said was that he half-thought he should go, and hear for himself what Frederick had to say about the mutiny; and that it would be a good opportunity for Margaret to become acquainted with her new sister-in-law; that he always went somewhere during the long vacation, and did not see why he should not go to Spain as well as anywhere else. That was all. Edith hoped Margaret did not want to leave them, that she was so anxious about all this. And then, having nothing else particular to do, she cried, and said that she knew she cared much more for Margaret than Margaret did for her. Margaret comforted her as well as she could, but she could hardly explain to her how this idea of Spain, mere Château en Espagne as it might be, charmed and delighted her. Edith was in the mood to think that any pleasure enjoyed away from her was a tacit affront, or at best a proof of indifference. So Margaret had to keep her pleasure to herself, and could only let it escape by the safety-valve of asking Dixon, when she dressed for dinner, if she would not like to see Master Frederick and his new wife very much indeed?\n\n“She’s a Papist, Miss, isn’t she?”\n\n“I believe—oh yes, certainly!” said Margaret, a little damped for an instant at this recollection.\n\n“And they live in a Popish country?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then I’m afraid I must say, that my soul is dearer to me than even Master Frederick, his own dear self. I should be in a perpetual terror, Miss, lest I should be converted.”\n\n“Oh,” said Margaret, “I do not know that I am going; and if I go, I am not such a fine lady as to be unable to travel without you. No! dear old Dixon, you shall have a long holiday, if we go. But I’m afraid it is a long ‘if.’”\n\nNow Dixon did not like this speech. In the first place, she did not like Margaret’s trick of calling her ‘dear old Dixon’ whenever she was particularly demonstrative. She knew that Miss Hale was apt to call all people that she liked “old,” as a sort of term of endearment; but Dixon always winced away from the application of the word to herself, who, being not much past fifty, was, she thought, in the very prime of life. Secondly, she did not like being so easily taken at her word; she had, with all her terror, a lurking curiosity about Spain, the Inquisition, and Popish mysteries. So, after clearing her throat, as if to show her willingness to do away with difficulties, she asked Miss Hale whether she thought, if she took care never to see a priest, or enter into one of their churches, there would be so very much danger of her being converted? Master Frederick, to be sure, had gone over unaccountably.\n\n“I fancy it was love that first predisposed him to conversion,” said Margaret, sighing.\n\n“Indeed, Miss!” said Dixon; “well! I can preserve myself from priests, and from churches; but love steals in unawares! I think it’s as well I should not go.”\n\nMargaret was afraid of letting her mind run too much upon this Spanish plan. But it took off her thoughts from too impatiently dwelling upon her desire to have all explained to Mr. Thornton. Mr. Bell appeared for the present to be stationary at Oxford, and to have no immediate purpose of going to Milton, and some secret restraint seemed to hang over Margaret, and prevent her from even asking, or alluding again to any probability of such a visit on his part. Nor did she feel at liberty to name what Edith had told her of the idea he had entertained,—it might be but for five minutes,—of going to Spain. He had never named it at Helstone, during all that sunny day of leisure; it was very probably but the fancy of a moment,—but if it were true, what a bright outlet it would be from the monotony of her present life, which was beginning to fall upon her.\n\nOne of the great pleasures of Margaret’s life at this time was in Edith’s boy. He was the pride and plaything of both father and mother, as long as he was good; but he had a strong will of his own, and as soon as he burst out into one of his stormy passions, Edith would throw herself back in despair and fatigue, and sigh out, “Oh dear, what shall I do with him! Do, Margaret, please ring the bell for Hanley.”\n\nBut Margaret almost liked him better in these manifestations of character than in his good blue-sashed moods. She would carry him off into a room, where they two alone battled it out; she with a firm power which subdued him into peace, while every sudden charm and wile she possessed was exerted on the side of right, until he would rub his little hot and tear-smeared face all over hers, kissing and caressing till he often fell asleep in her arms or on her shoulder. Those were Margaret’s sweetest moments. They gave her a taste of the feeling that she believed would be denied to her for ever.\n\nMr. Henry Lennox added a new and not disagreeable element to the course of the household life by his frequent presence. Margaret thought him colder, if more brilliant than formerly; but there were strong intellectual tastes, and much and varied knowledge, which gave flavour to the otherwise rather insipid conversation. Margaret saw glimpses in him of a slight contempt for his brother and sister-in-law, and for their mode of life, which he seemed to consider as frivolous and purposeless. He once or twice spoke to his brother, in Margaret’s presence, in a pretty sharp tone of enquiry, as to whether he meant entirely to relinquish his profession; and on Captain Lennox’s reply, that he had quite enough to live upon, she had seen Mr. Lennox’s curl of the lip as he said, “And is that all you live for?”\n\nBut the brothers were much attached to each other, in the way that any two persons are, when the one is cleverer and always leads the other, and this last is patiently content to be led. Mr. Lennox was pushing on in his profession; cultivating, with profound calculation, all those connections that might eventually be of service to him; keen-sighted, far-seeing, intelligent, sarcastic, and proud. Since the one long conversation relating to Frederick’s affairs, which she had with him the first evening in Mr. Bell’s presence, she had had no great intercourse with him, further than that which arose out of their close relations with the same household. But this was enough to wear off the shyness on her side, and any symptoms of mortified pride and vanity on his. They met, continually, of course, but she thought that he rather avoided being alone with her; she fancied that he, as well as she, perceived that they had drifted strangely apart from their former anchorage, side by side, in many of their opinions, and all their tastes.\n\nAnd yet, when he had spoken unusually well, or with remarkable epigrammatic point, she felt that his eye sought the expression of her countenance first of all, if but for an instant; and that, in the family intercourse which constantly threw them together, her opinion was the one to which he listened with a deference,—the more complete, because it was reluctantly paid, and concealed as much as possible.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII\n\n“NE’ER TO BE FOUND AGAIN.”\n\n“My own, my father’s friend! I cannot part with thee! I ne’er have shown, thou ne’er hast known, How dear thou art to me.” ANON.\n\nThe elements of the dinner-parties which Mrs. Lennox gave, were these; her friends contributed the beauty, Captain Lennox the easy knowledge of the subjects of the day; and Mr. Henry Lennox, and the sprinkling of rising men who were received as his friends, brought the wit, the cleverness, the keen and extensive knowledge of which they knew well enough how to avail themselves without seeming pedantic, or burdening the rapid flow of conversation.\n\nThese dinners were delightful; but even here Margaret’s dissatisfaction found her out. Every talent, every feeling, every acquirement; nay, even every tendency towards virtue, was used up as materials for fireworks; the hidden, sacred fire, exhausted itself in sparkle and crackle. They talked about art in a merely sensuous way, dwelling on outside effects, instead of allowing themselves to learn what it has to teach. They lashed themselves up into an enthusiasm about high subjects in company, and never thought about them when they were alone; they squandered their capabilities of appreciation into a mere flow of appropriate words. One day, after the gentlemen had come up into the drawing-room, Mr. Lennox drew near to Margaret, and addressed her in almost the first voluntary words he had spoken to her since she had returned to live in Harley Street.\n\n“You did not look pleased at what Shirley was saying at dinner.”\n\n“Didn’t I? My face must be very expressive,” replied Margaret.\n\n“It always was. It has not lost the trick of being eloquent.”\n\n“I did not like,” said Margaret, hastily, “his way of advocating what he knew to be wrong—so glaringly wrong—even in jest.”\n\n“But it was very clever. How every word told! Did you remember the happy epithets?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And despise them, you would like to add. Pray don’t scruple, though he is my friend.”\n\n“There! that is the exact tone in you, that”—she stopped short.\n\nHe listened for a moment to see if she would finish her sentence; but she only reddened, and turned away; before she did so, however, she heard him say, in a very low, clear voice,—\n\n“If my tones, or modes of thought, are what you dislike, will you do me the justice to tell me so, and so give me the chance of learning to please you.”\n\nAll these weeks there was no intelligence of Mr. Bell’s going to Milton. He had spoken of it at Helstone as of a journey which he might have to take in a very short time from then; but he must have transacted his business by writing. Margaret thought, ere now, and she knew that if he could, he would avoid going to a place which he disliked, and moreover would little understand the secret importance which she affixed to the explanation that could only be given by word of mouth. She knew that he would feel that it was necessary that it should be done; but whether in summer, autumn, or winter, it would signify very little. It was now August, and there had been no mention of the Spanish journey to which he had alluded to Edith, and Margaret tried to reconcile herself to the fading away of this illusion.\n\nBut one morning she received a letter, saying that next week he meant to come up to town; he wanted to see her about a plan which he had in his head; and, moreover, he intended to treat himself to a little doctoring, as he had begun to come round to her opinion, that it would be pleasanter to think that his health was more in fault than he, when he found himself irritable and cross. There was altogether a tone of forced cheerfulness in the letter, as Margaret noticed afterwards; but at the time her attention was taken up by Edith’s exclamations.\n\n“Coming up to town! Oh dear! and I am so worn out by the heat that I don’t believe I have strength enough in me for another dinner. Besides, everybody has left but our dear stupid selves, who can’t settle where to go to. There would be nobody to meet him.”\n\n“I’m sure he would much rather come and dine with us quite alone than with the most agreeable strangers you could pick up. Besides, if he is not well he won’t wish for invitations. I am glad he has owned it at last. I was sure he was ill from the whole tone of his letters, and yet he would not answer me when I asked him, and I had no third person to whom I could apply for news.”\n\n“Oh! he is not very ill, or he would not think of Spain.”\n\n“He never mentions Spain.”\n\n“No! but his plan that is to be proposed evidently relates to that. But would you really go in such weather as this?”\n\n“Oh, it will get cooler every day. Yes! Think of it. I am only afraid I have thought and wished too much—in that absorbing wilful way which is sure to be disappointed—or else gratified, to the letter, while in the spirit it gives no pleasure.”\n\n“But that’s superstitious, I’m sure, Margaret.”\n\n“No, I don’t think it is. Only it ought to warn me, and check me from giving way to such passionate wishes. It is a sort of ‘Give me children, or else I die.’ I’m afraid my cry is, ‘Let me go to Cadiz, or else I die.’”\n\n“My dear Margaret! You’ll be persuaded to stay there; and then what shall I do? Oh! I wish I could find somebody for you to marry here, that I could be sure of you!”\n\n“I shall never marry.”\n\n“Nonsense, and double nonsense! Why, as Sholto says, you’re such an attraction to the house, that he knows ever so many men who will be glad to visit here next year for your sake.”\n\nMargaret drew herself up haughtily. “Do you know, Edith, I sometimes think your Corfu life has taught you——”\n\n“Well!”\n\n“Just a shade or two of coarseness.”\n\nEdith began to sob so bitterly, and to declare so vehemently that Margaret had lost all love for her, and no longer looked upon her as a friend, that Margaret came to think that she had expressed too harsh an opinion for the relief of her own wounded pride, and ended by being Edith’s slave for the rest of the day; while that little lady, overcome by wounded feeling, lay like a victim on the sofa, heaving occasionally a profound sigh, till at last she fell asleep.\n\nMr. Bell did not make his appearance even on the day to which he had for a second time deferred his visit. The next morning there came a letter from Wallis, his servant, stating that his master had not been feeling well for some time, which had been the true reason of his putting off his journey; and that at the very time when he should have set out for London, he had been seized with an apoplectic fit; it was, indeed, Wallis added, the opinion of the medical men—that he could not survive the night; and more than probable, that by the time Miss Hale received this letter his poor master would be no more.\n\nMargaret received this letter at breakfast-time, and turned very pale as she read it; then, silently putting into Edith’s hands, she left the room.\n\nEdith was terribly shocked as she read it, and cried in a sobbing, frightened, childish way, much to her husband’s distress. Mrs. Shaw was breakfasting in her own room, and upon him devolved the task of reconciling his wife to the near contact into which she seemed to be brought with death, for the first time that she could remember in her life. Here was a man who was to have dined with them to-day lying dead or dying instead! It was some time before she could think of Margaret. Then she started up, and followed her upstairs into her room. Dixon was packing up a few toilette articles, and Margaret was hastily putting on her bonnet, shedding tears all the time, and her hands trembling so that she could hardly tie the strings.\n\n“Oh, dear Margaret! how shocking! What are you doing? Are you going out? Sholto would telegraph or do anything you like.”\n\n“I am going to Oxford. There is a train in half-an-hour. Dixon has offered to go with me, but I could have gone by myself. I must see him again. Besides, he may be better, and want some care. He has been like a father to me. Don’t stop me, Edith.”\n\n“But I must. Mamma won’t like it at all. Come and ask her about it, Margaret. You don’t know where you’re going. I should not mind if he had a house of his own; but in his Fellow’s rooms! Come to mamma, and do ask her before you go. It will not take a minute.”\n\nMargaret yielded, and lost her train. In the suddenness of the event, Mrs. Shaw became bewildered and hysterical, and so the precious time slipped by. But there was another train in a couple of hours; and after various discussions on propriety and impropriety, it was decided that Captain Lennox should accompany Margaret, as the one thing to which she was constant was her resolution to go, alone or otherwise, by the next train, whatever might be said of the propriety or impropriety of the step. Her father’s friend, her own friend, was lying at the point of death; and the thought of this came upon her with such vividness, that she was surprised herself at the firmness with which she asserted something of her right to independence of action; and five minutes before the time of starting, she found herself sitting in a railway carriage opposite to Captain Lennox. It was always a comfort to her to think that she had gone, though it was only to hear that he had died in the night. She saw the rooms that he had occupied, and associated them ever after most fondly in her memory with the idea of her father, and his one cherished and faithful friend.\n\nThey had promised Edith before starting, that if all had ended as they feared, they would return to dinner; so that long, lingering look around the room in which her father had died, had to be interrupted, and a quiet farewell taken of the kind old face that had so often come out with pleasant words, and merry quips and cranks.\n\nCaptain Lennox fell asleep on their journey home; and Margaret could cry at leisure, and bethink her of this fatal year, and all the woes it had brought to her. No sooner was she fully aware of one loss than another came—not to supersede her grief for the one before, but to re-open wounds and feelings scarcely healed. But at the sound of the tender voices of her aunt and Edith, of merry little Sholto’s glee at her arrival, and at the sight of the well-lighted rooms, with their mistress, pretty in her paleness and her eager sorrowful interest, Margaret roused herself from her heavy trance of almost superstitious hopelessness, and began to feel that even around her joy and gladness might gather. She had Edith’s place on the sofa; Sholto was taught to carry Aunt Margaret’s cup of tea very carefully to her; and by the time she went up to dress, she could thank God for having spared her dear old friend a long or a painful illness.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX\n\n“And down the sunny beach she paces slowly, With many doubtful pauses by the way; Grief hath an influence so hushed and holy.” HOOD.\n\n“Is not Margaret the heiress?” whispered Edith to her husband, as they were in their room alone at night after the sad journey to Oxford. She had pulled his tall head down, and stood upon tiptoe, and implored him not to be shocked, before she ventured to ask this question. Captain Lennox was, however, quite in the dark; if he had ever heard, he had forgotten; it could not be much that a Fellow of a small College had to leave; but he had never wanted her to pay for her board; and two hundred and fifty pounds a year was something ridiculous, considering that she did not take wine. Edith came down upon her feet a little bit sadder; with a romance blown to pieces.\n\nA week afterwards, she came prancing towards her husband, and made him a low curtsey:\n\n“I am right, and you are wrong, most noble Captain. Margaret has had a lawyer’s letter, and she is residuary legatee—the legacies being about two thousand pounds, and the remainder about forty thousand, at the present value of property in Milton.”\n\n“Indeed! and how does she take her good fortune?”\n\n“Oh, it seems she knew she was to have it all along; only she had no idea it was so much. She looks very white and pale, and says she’s afraid of it; but that’s nonsense, you know, and will soon go off. I left mamma pouring congratulations down her throat, and stole away to tell you.”\n\nIt seemed to be supposed, by general consent, that the most natural thing was to consider Mr. Lennox henceforward as Margaret’s legal adviser. She was so entirely ignorant of all forms of business that in nearly everything she had to refer to him. He chose out her attorney; he came to her with papers to be signed. He was never so happy as when teaching her of what all these mysteries of the law were the signs and types.\n\n“Henry,” said Edith, one day, archly; “do you know what I hope and expect all these long conversations with Margaret will end in?”\n\n“No, I don’t,” said he, reddening. “And I desire you not to tell me.”\n\n“Oh, very well; then I need not tell Sholto not to ask Mr. Montagu so often to the house.”\n\n“Just as you choose,” said he with forced coolness. “What you are thinking of may or may not happen; but this time, before I commit myself, I will see my ground clear. Ask whom you choose. It may not be very civil, Edith, but if you meddle in it you will mar it. She has been very farouche with me for a long time; and is only just beginning to thaw a little from her Zenobia ways. She has the making of a Cleopatra in her, if only she were a little more pagan.”\n\n“For my part,” said Edith, a little maliciously, “I am very glad she is a Christian. I know so very few!”\n\nThere was no Spain for Margaret that autumn; although to the last she hoped that some fortunate occasion would call Frederick to Paris, whither she could easily have met with a convoy. Instead of Cadiz, she had to content herself with Cromer. To that place her aunt Shaw and the Lennoxes were bound. They had all along wished her to accompany them, and, consequently, with their characters, they made but lazy efforts to forward her own separate wish. Perhaps Cromer was, in one sense of the expression, the best for her. She needed bodily strengthening and bracing as well as rest.\n\nAmong other hopes that had vanished, was the hope, the trust she had had, that Mr. Bell would have given Mr. Thornton the simple facts of the family circumstances which had preceded the unfortunate accident that led to Leonards’ death. Whatever opinion—however changed it might be from what Mr. Thornton had once entertained, she had wished it to be based upon a true understanding of what she had done, and why she had done it. It would have been a pleasure to her: would have given her rest on a point on which she should now all her life be restless, unless she could resolve not to think upon it. It was now so long after the time of these occurrences, that there was no possible way of explaining them save the one which she had lost by Mr. Bell’s death. She might just submit, like many another, to be misunderstood; but, though reasoning herself into the belief that in this hers was no uncommon lot, her heart did not ache the less with longing that some time—years and years hence—before he died at any rate, he might know how much she had been tempted. She thought that she did not want to hear that all was explained to him, if only she could be sure that he would know. But this wish was vain, like so many others; and when she had schooled herself into this conviction, she turned with all her heart and strength to the life that lay immediately before her, and resolved to strive and make the best of that.\n\nShe used to sit long hours upon the beach, gazing intently on the waves as they chafed with perpetual motion against the pebbly shore,—or she looked out upon the more distant heave, and sparkle against the sky, and heard, without being conscious of hearing, the eternal psalm, which went up continually. She was soothed without knowing how or why. Listlessly she sat there, on the ground, her hands clasped round her knees, while her aunt Shaw did small shoppings, and Edith and Captain Lennox rode far and wide on shore and inland. The nurses, sauntering on with their charges, would pass and repass her, and wonder in whispers what she could find to look at so long, day after day. And when the family gathered at dinner-time, Margaret was so silent and absorbed that Edith voted her moped, and hailed a proposal of her husband’s with great satisfaction, that Mr. Henry Lennox should be asked to take Cromer for a week, on his return from Scotland in October.\n\nBut all this time for thought enabled Margaret to put events in their right places, as to origin and significance, both as regarded her past life and her future. Those hours by the seaside were not lost, as any one might have seen who had had the perception to read, or the care to understand, the look that Margaret’s face was gradually acquiring. Mr. Henry Lennox was excessively struck by the change.\n\n“The sea has done Miss Hale an immense deal of good, I should fancy,” said he, when she first left the room after his arrival in their family circle. “She looks ten years younger than she did in Harley Street.”\n\n“That’s the bonnet I got her!” said Edith, triumphantly. “I knew it would suit her the moment I saw it.”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” said Mr. Lennox, in the half-contemptuous, half-indulgent tone he generally used to Edith. “But I believe I know the difference between the charms of a dress and the charms of a woman. No mere bonnet would have made Miss Hale’s eyes so lustrous and yet so soft, or her lips so ripe and red—and her face altogether so full of peace and light.—She is like, and yet more,”—he dropped his voice,—“like the Margaret Hale of Helstone.”\n\nFrom this time the clever and ambitious man bent all his powers to gaining Margaret. He loved her sweet beauty. He saw the latent sweep of her mind, which could easily (he thought) be led to embrace all the objects on which he had set his heart. He looked upon her fortune only as a part of the complete and superb character of herself and her position; yet he was fully aware of the rise which it would immediately enable him, the poor barrister, to take. Eventually he would earn such success, and such honours, as would enable him to pay her back, with interest, that first advance in wealth which he should owe to her. He had been to Milton on business connected with her property, on his return from Scotland; and with the quick eye of a skilled lawyer, ready ever to take in and weigh contingencies, he had seen that much additional value was yearly accruing to the lands and tenements which she owned in that prosperous and increasing town. He was glad to find that the present relationship between Margaret and himself, of client and legal adviser, was gradually superseding the recollection of that unlucky, mismanaged day at Helstone. He had thus unusual opportunities of intimate intercourse with her, besides those that arose from the connection between the families.\n\nMargaret was only too willing to listen as long as he talked of Milton, though he had seen none of the people whom she more especially knew. It had been the tone with her aunt and cousin to speak of Milton with dislike and contempt; just such feelings as Margaret was ashamed to remember she had expressed and felt on first going to live there. But Mr. Lennox almost exceeded Margaret in his appreciation of the character of Milton and its inhabitants. Their energy, their power, their indomitable courage in struggling and fighting; their lurid vividness of existence, captivated and arrested his attention. He was never tired of talking about them; and had never perceived how selfish and material were too many of the ends they proposed to themselves as the result of all their mighty, untiring endeavour, till Margaret, even in the midst of her gratification, had the candour to point this out, as the tainting sin in so much that was noble and to be admired. Still, when other subjects palled upon her, and she gave but short answers to many questions, Henry Lennox found out that an enquiry as to some Darkshire peculiarity of character called back the light into her eye, the glow into her cheek.\n\nWhen they returned to town, Margaret fulfilled one of her sea-side resolves, and took her life into her own hands. Before they went to Cromer, she had been as docile to her aunt’s laws as if she were still the scared little stranger who cried herself to sleep that first night in the Harley Street nursery. But she had learnt, in those solemn hours of thought, that she herself must one day answer for her own life, and what she had done with it; and she tried to settle that most difficult problem for woman, how much was to be utterly merged in obedience to authority, and how much might be set apart for freedom in working. Mrs. Shaw was as good-tempered as could be; and Edith had inherited this charming domestic quality; Margaret herself had probably the worst temper of the three, for her quick perceptions and over-lively imagination made her hasty, and her early isolation from sympathy had made her proud: but she had an indescribable childlike sweetness of heart, which made her manners, even in her rarely wilful moods, irresistible of old; and now, chastened even by what the world called her good fortune, she charmed her reluctant aunt into acquiescence with her will. So Margaret gained the acknowledgment of her right to follow her own ideas of duty.\n\n“Only don’t be strong-minded,” pleaded Edith. “Mamma wants you to have a footman of your own; and I’m sure you’re very welcome, for they’re great plagues. Only to please me, darling, don’t go and have a strong mind; it’s the only thing I ask. Footman or no footman, don’t be strong-minded.”\n\n“Don’t be afraid, Edith. I’ll faint on your hands at the servants’ dinner-time, the very first opportunity; and then, what with Sholto playing with the fire, and the baby crying, you’ll begin to wish for a strong-minded woman, equal to any emergency.”\n\n“And you’ll not grow too good to joke and be merry?”\n\n“Not I. I shall be merrier than I have ever been, now I have got my own way.”\n\n“And you’ll not go a figure, but let me buy your dresses for you?”\n\n“Indeed I mean to buy them for myself. You shall come with me if you like; but no one can please me but myself.”\n\n“Oh! I was afraid you’d dress in brown and dust-colour, not to show the dirt you’ll pick up in all those places. I’m glad you’re going to keep one or two vanities, just by way of specimens of the old Adam.”\n\n“I am going to be just the same, Edith, if you and my aunt could but fancy so. Only as I have neither husband nor child to give me natural duties, I must make myself some, in addition to ordering my gowns.”\n\nIn the family conclave, which was made up of Edith, her mother, and her husband, it was decided that perhaps all these plans of hers would only secure her the more for Henry Lennox. They kept her out of the way of other friends who might have been eligible sons or brothers; and it was also agreed that she never seemed to take much pleasure in the society of any one but Henry, out of their own family. The other admirers, attracted by her appearance or the reputation of her fortune, were swept away, by her unconscious smiling disdain, into the paths frequented by other beauties less fastidious, or other heiresses with a larger amount of gold. Henry and she grew slowly into closer intimacy; but neither he nor she were people to brook the slightest notice of their proceedings.\n\n# CHAPTER L\n\n“Here we go up, up, up; And here we go down, down, downee!” NURSERY SONG.\n\nMeanwhile at Milton the chimneys smoked, the ceaseless roar and mighty beat and dazzling whirr of machinery struggled and strove perpetually. Senseless and purposeless were wood and iron and steam in their endless labours; but the persistence of their monotonous work was rivalled in tireless endurance by the strong crowds, who, with sense and with purpose, were busy and restless in seeking after—What? In the streets there were few loiterers,—none walking for mere pleasure; every man’s face was set in lines of eagerness or anxiety; news was sought for with fierce avidity; and men jostled each other aside in the Mart and in the Exchange, as they did in life, in the deep selfishness of competition. There was gloom over the town. Few came to buy, and those who did were looked at suspiciously by the sellers; for credit was insecure, and the most stable might have their fortunes affected by the sweep in the great neighbouring port among the shipping houses. Hitherto there had been no failures in Milton; but from the immense speculations that had come to light in making a bad end in America, and yet nearer home, it was known that some Milton houses of business must suffer so severely that every day men’s faces asked, if their tongues did not, “What news? Who is gone? How will it affect me?” And if two or three spoke together, they dwelt rather on the names of those who were safe than dared to hint at those likely, in their opinion, to go: for idle breath may, at such times, cause the downfall of some who might otherwise weather the storm; and one going down drags many after. “Thornton is safe,” say they. “His business is large—extending every year; but such a head as he has, and so prudent with all his daring!” Then one man draws another aside, and walks a little apart, and with head inclined into his neighbour’s ear, he says, “Thornton’s business is large; but he has spent his profits in extending it; he has no capital laid by; his machinery is new within these two years, and has cost him—we won’t say what!—a word to the wise!” But that Mr. Harrison was a croaker,—a man who had succeeded to his father’s trade-made fortune, which he had feared to lose by altering his mode of business to any having a larger scope; yet he grudged every penny made by others more daring and far-sighted.\n\nBut the truth was, Mr. Thornton was hard pressed. He felt it acutely in his vulnerable point—his pride in the commercial character which he had established for himself. Architect of his own fortunes, he attributed this to no special merit or qualities of his own, but to the power, which he believed that commerce gave to every brave, honest, and persevering man, to raise himself to a level from which he might see and read the great game of worldly success, and honestly, by such far-sightedness, command more power and influence than in any other mode of life. Far away, in the East and in the West, where his person would never be known, his name was to be regarded, and his wishes to be fulfilled, and his word pass like gold. That was the idea of merchant-life with which Mr. Thornton had started. “Her merchants be like princes,” said his mother, reading the text aloud, as if it were a trumpet-call to invite her boy to the struggle. He was but like many others—men, women, and children—alive to distant, and dead to near things. He sought to possess the influence of a name in foreign countries and far-away seas,—to become the head of a firm that should be known for generations; and it had taken him long silent years to come even to a glimmering of what he might be now, to-day, here in his own town, his own factory, among his own people. He and they had led parallel lives—very close, but never touching—till the accident (or so it seemed) of his acquaintance with Higgins. Once brought face to face, man to man, with an individual of the masses around him, and (take notice) out of the character of master and workman, in the first instance, they had each begun to recognise that “we have all of us one human heart.” It was the fine point of the wedge; and until now, when the apprehension of losing his connection with two or three of the workmen whom he had so lately begun to know as men,—of having a plan or two, which were experiments lying very close to his heart, roughly nipped off without trial,—gave a new poignancy to the subtle fear that came over him from time to time; until now, he had never recognised how much and how deep was the interest he had grown of late to feel in his position of manufacturer, simply because it led him into such close contact, and gave him the opportunity of so much power, among a race of people strange, shrewd, ignorant; but, above all, full of character and strong human feeling.\n\nHe reviewed his position as a Milton manufacturer. The strike a year and a half ago,—or more, for it was now untimely wintry weather, in a late spring,—that strike, when he was young, and he now was old—had prevented his completing some of the large orders he had then on hand. He had locked up a good deal of his capital in new and expensive machinery, and he had also bought cotton largely, for the fulfilment of these orders, taken under contract. That he had not been able to complete them, was owing in some degree to the utter want of skill on the part of the Irish hands whom he had imported; much of their work was damaged and unfit to be sent forth by a house which prided itself on turning out nothing but first-rate articles. For many months, the embarrassment caused by the strike had been an obstacle in Mr. Thornton’s way; and often, when his eye fell on Higgins, he could have spoken angrily to him without any present cause, just from feeling how serious was the injury that had arisen from this affair in which he was implicated. But when he became conscious of this sudden, quick resentment, he resolved to curb it. It would not satisfy him to avoid Higgins; he must convince himself that he was master over his own anger, by being particularly careful to allow Higgins access to him, whenever the strict rules of business, or Mr. Thornton’s leisure permitted. And by-and-bye, he lost all sense of resentment in wonder how it was, or could be, that two men like himself and Higgins, living by the same trade, working in their different ways at the same object, could look upon each other’s position and duties in so strangely different a way. And thence arose that intercourse, which, though it might not have the effect of preventing all future clash of opinion and action, when the occasion arose, would, at any rate, enable both master and man to look upon each other with far more charity and sympathy, and bear with each other more patiently and kindly. Besides this improvement of feeling, both Mr. Thornton and his workmen found out their ignorance as to positive matters of fact, known heretofore to one side, but not to the other.\n\nBut now had come one of those periods of bad trade, when the market falling brought down the value of all large stocks; Mr. Thornton’s fell to nearly half. No orders were coming in; so he lost the interest of the capital he had locked up in machinery; indeed, it was difficult to get payment for the orders completed; yet there was the constant drain of expenses for working the business. Then the bills became due for the cotton he had purchased; and money being scarce, he could only borrow at exorbitant interest, and yet he could not realise any of his property. But he did not despair; he exerted himself day and night to foresee and to provide for all emergencies; he was as calm and gentle to the women in his home as ever; to the workmen in his mill he spoke not many words, but they knew him by this time; and many a curt, decided answer was received by them rather with sympathy for the care they saw pressing upon him, than with the suppressed antagonism which had formerly been smouldering, and ready for hard words and hard judgments on all occasions. “Th’ measter’s a deal to potter him,” said Higgins, one day, as he heard Mr. Thornton’s short, sharp inquiry, why such a command had not been obeyed; and caught the sound of the suppressed sigh which he heaved in going past the room where some of the men were working. Higgins and another man stopped over-hours that night, unknown to any one, to get the neglected piece of work done; and Mr. Thornton never knew but that the overlooker, to whom he had given the command in the first instance, had done it himself.\n\n“Eh! I reckon I know who’d ha’ been sorry for to see our measter sitting so like a piece o’ grey calico! Th’ oud parson would ha’ fretted his woman’s heart out, if he’d seen the woeful looks I have seen on our measter’s face,” thought Higgins, one day, as he was approaching Mr. Thornton in Marlborough Street.\n\n“Measter,” said he, stopping his employer in his quick resolved walk, and causing that gentleman to look up with a sudden annoyed start, as if his thoughts had been far away.\n\n“Have yo’ heerd aught of Miss Marget lately?”\n\n“Miss—who?” replied Mr. Thornton.\n\n“Miss Marget—Miss Hale—th’ oud parson’s daughter—yo’ known who I mean well enough—if yo’ll only think a bit—” (there was nothing disrespectful in the tone in which this was said).\n\n“Oh yes!” and suddenly the wintry frost-bound look of care had left Mr. Thornton’s face, as if some soft summer gale had blown all anxiety away from his mind; and though his mouth was as much compressed as before, his eyes smiled out benignly on his questioner.\n\n“She’s my landlord now, you know, Higgins. I hear of her through her agent here, every now and then. She’s well and among friends—thank you, Higgins.” That “thank you” lingered after the other words, and yet came with so much warmth of feeling, let in a new light to the acute Higgins. It might be but a will-o’-th’-wisp, but he thought he would follow it and ascertain whither it would lead him.\n\n“And she’s not getten married, measter?”\n\n“Not yet.” The face was cloudy once more. “There is some talk of it, as I understand, with a connection of the family.”\n\n“Then she’ll not be for coming to Milton again, I reckon.”\n\n“No!”\n\n“Stop a minute, measter.” Then going up confidentially close, he said, “Is th’ young gentleman cleared?” He enforced the depth of his intelligence by a wink of the eye, which only made things more mysterious to Mr. Thornton.\n\n“Th’ young gentleman, I mean—Master Frederick, they ca’ed him—her brother as was over here, yo’ known.”\n\n“Over here.”\n\n“Ay, to be sure, at th’ missus’s death. Yo’ need na be feared of my telling; for Mary and me, we knowed it all along, only we held our peace, for we got it through Mary working in th’ house.”\n\n“And he was over. It was her brother.”\n\n“Sure enough, and I reckoned yo’ knowed it, or I’d never ha’ let on. Yo’ knowed she had a brother?”\n\n“Yes, I know all about him. And he was over at Mrs. Hale’s death?”\n\n“Nay! I’m not going for to tell more. I’ve maybe getten them into mischief already, for they kept it very close. I nobbut wanted to know if they’d getten him cleared?”\n\n“Not that I know of. I know nothing. I only hear of Miss Hale, now, as my landlord, and through her lawyer.”\n\nHe broke off from Higgins, to follow the business on which he had been bent when the latter first accosted him; leaving Higgins baffled in his endeavour.\n\n“It was her brother,” said Mr. Thornton to himself. “I am glad. I may never see her again; but it is a comfort—a relief—to know that much. I knew she could not be unmaidenly; and yet I yearned for conviction. Now I am glad!”\n\nIt was a little golden thread running through the dark web of his present fortunes; which were growing ever gloomier and more gloomy. His agent had largely trusted a house in the American trade, which went down, along with several others, just at this time, like a pack of cards, the fall of one compelling other failures. What were Mr. Thornton’s engagements? Could he stand?\n\nNight after night he took books and papers into his own private room, and sate up there long after the family were gone to bed. He thought no one knew of this occupation of the hours he should have spent in sleep. One morning, when daylight was stealing in through the crevices of his shutters, and he had never been in bed, and, in hopeless indifference of mind, was thinking that he could do without the hour or two of rest, which was all that he should be able to take before the stir of daily labour began again, the door of his room opened, and his mother stood there, dressed as she had been the day before. She had never laid herself down to slumber any more than he. Their eyes met. Their faces were cold and rigid, and wan, from long watching.\n\n“Mother! why are you not in bed?”\n\n“Son John,” said she, “do you think I can sleep with an easy mind, while you keep awake full of care? You have not told me what your trouble is; but sore trouble you have had these many days past.”\n\n“Trade is bad.”\n\n“And you dread——”\n\n“I dread nothing,” replied he, drawing up his head, and holding it erect. “I know that no man will suffer by me. That was my anxiety.”\n\n“But how do you stand? Shall you—will it be a failure?” her steady voice trembling in an unwonted manner.\n\n“Not a failure. I must give up business, but I pay all men. I might redeem myself—I am sorely tempted—”\n\n“How? Oh, John! keep up your name—try all risks for that. How redeem it?”\n\n“By a speculation offered to me, but full of risk; but, if successful, placing me high above water-mark, so that no one need ever know the strait I am in. Still, if it fails—”\n\n“And if it fails,” said she, advancing, and laying her hand on his arm, her eyes full of eager light. She held her breath to hear the end of his speech.\n\n“Honest men are ruined by a rogue,” said he gloomily. “As I stand now, my creditors’ money is safe—every farthing of it; but I don’t know where to find my own—it may be all gone, and I penniless at this moment. Therefore, it is my creditors’ money that I should risk.”\n\n“But if it succeeded, they need never know. Is it so desperate a speculation? I am sure it is not, or you would never have thought of it. If it succeeded—”\n\n“I should be a rich man, and my peace of conscience would be gone!”\n\n“Why! You would have injured no one.”\n\n“No; but I should have run the risk of ruining many for my own paltry aggrandisement. Mother, I have decided! You won’t much grieve over our leaving this house, shall you, dear mother?”\n\n“No! but to have you other than what you are will break my heart. What can you do?”\n\n“Be always the same John Thornton in whatever circumstances; endeavouring to do right, and making great blunders; and then trying to be brave in setting to afresh. But it is hard, mother. I have worked and planned. I have discovered new powers in my situation too late—and now all is over. I am too old to begin again with the same heart. It is hard, mother.”\n\nHe turned away from her, and covered his face with his hands.\n\n“I can’t think,” said she, with gloomy defiance in her tone, “how it comes about. Here is my boy—good son, just man, tender heart—and he fails in all he sets his mind upon: he finds a woman to love, and she cares no more for his affection than if he had been a common man; he labours, and his labour comes to nought. Other people grow rich, and hold their paltry names high and dry above shame.”\n\n“Shame never touched me,” said he, in a low tone: but she went on.\n\n“I sometimes have wondered where justice was gone to, and now I don’t believe there is such a thing in the world—now you are come to this; you, my own John Thornton, though you and I may be beggars together—my own dear son!”\n\nShe fell upon his neck, and kissed him through her tears.\n\n“Mother!” said he, holding her gently in his arms, “who has sent me my lot in life, both of good and evil?”\n\nShe shook her head. She would have nothing to do with religion just then.\n\n“Mother,” he went on, seeing that she would not speak. “I, too, have been rebellious; but I’m striving to be so no longer. Help me, as you helped me when I was a child. Then you said many good words—when my father died, and we were sometimes sorely short of comforts—which we shall never be now; you said brave, noble, trustful words then, mother, which I have never forgotten, though they may have lain dormant. Speak to me again in the old way, mother. Do not let us have to think that the world has too much hardened our hearts. If you would say the old good words, it would make me feel something of the pious simplicity of my childhood. I say them to myself, but they would come differently from you, remembering all the cares and trials you have had to bear.”\n\n“I have had a many,” said she, sobbing, “but none so sore as this. To see you cast down from your rightful place! I could say it for myself, John, but not for you. Not for you! God has seen fit to be very hard on you, very.”\n\nShe shook with the sobs that come so convulsively when an old person weeps. The silence around her struck her at last; and she quieted herself to listen. No sound. She looked. Her son sate by the table, his arms thrown half across it, his head bent face downwards.\n\n“Oh, John!” she said, and she lifted his face up. Such a strange, pallid look of gloom was on it, that for a moment it struck her that this look was the forerunner of death; but as the rigidity melted out of the countenance and the natural colour returned, and she saw that he was himself once again, all worldly mortification sank to nothing before the consciousness of the great blessing that he himself, by his simple existence was to her. She thanked God for this, and this alone, with a fervour that swept away all rebellious feelings from her mind.\n\nHe did not speak readily; but he went and opened the shutters, and let the ruddy light of dawn flood the room. But the wind was in the east; the weather was piercing cold, as it had been for weeks; there would be no demand for light summer goods this year. That hope for the revival of trade must utterly be given up.\n\nIt was a great comfort to have had this conversation with his mother; and to feel sure that, however they might henceforward keep silence on all these anxieties, they yet understood each other’s feelings, and were, if not in harmony, at least in discord with each other, in their way of viewing them. Fanny’s husband was vexed at Thornton’s refusal to take any share in the speculation which he had offered to him, and withdrew from any possibility of being supposed able to assist him with the ready money, which indeed the speculator needed for his own venture.\n\nThere was nothing for it at last, but that which Mr. Thornton had dreaded for many weeks; he had to give up the business in which he had been so long engaged with so much honour and success; and look out for a subordinate situation. Marlborough Mills and the adjacent dwelling were held under a long lease; they must if possible be relet. There was an immediate choice of situations offered to Mr. Thornton. Mr. Hamper would have been only too glad to have secured him as a steady and experienced partner for his son, whom he was setting up with a large capital in a neighbouring town; but the young man was half-educated as regarded information, and wholly uneducated as regarded any other responsibility than that of getting money, and brutalised both as to his pleasures and his pains. Mr. Thornton declined having any share in a partnership, which would frustrate what few plans he had that survived the wreck of his fortunes. He would sooner consent to be only a manager, where he could have a certain degree of power beyond the mere money-getting part, than have to fall in with the tyrannical humours of a moneyed partner with whom he felt sure that he should quarrel in a few months.\n\nSo he waited, and stood on one side with profound humility, as the news swept through the Exchange of the enormous fortune which his brother-in-law had made by his daring speculation. It was a nine days’ wonder. Success brought with it its worldly consequence of extreme admiration. No one was considered so wise and far-seeing as Mr. Watson.\n\n# CHAPTER LI\n\n“Bear up, brave heart! we will be calm and strong; Sure, we can master eyes, or cheek, or tongue, Nor let the smallest tell-tale sign appear She ever was, and is, and will be dear.” RHYMING PLAY.\n\nIt was a hot summer’s evening. Edith came into Margaret’s bed-room, the first time in her habit, the second ready dressed for dinner. No one was there at first; the next time Edith found Dixon laying out Margaret’s dress on the bed; but no Margaret. Edith remained to fidget about.\n\n“Oh, Dixon! not those horrid blue flowers to that dead gold-coloured gown. What taste! Wait a minute, and I will bring you some pomegranate blossoms.”\n\n“It’s not a dead gold-colour ma’am. It’s a straw-colour. And blue always goes with straw-colour.” But Edith had brought the brilliant scarlet flowers before Dixon had got half through her remonstrance.\n\n“Where is Miss Hale?” asked Edith, as soon as she had tried the effect of the garniture. “I can’t think,” she went on, pettishly, “how my aunt allowed her to get into such rambling habits in Milton! I’m sure I’m always expecting to hear of her having met with something horrible among all those wretched places she pokes herself into. I should never dare to go down some of those streets without a servant. They’re not fit for ladies.”\n\nDixon was still huffed about her despised taste; so she replied rather shortly:\n\n“It’s no wonder to my mind, when I hear ladies talk such a deal about ladies—and when they’re such fearful, delicate, dainty ladies too—I say it’s no wonder to me that there are no longer any saints on earth——”\n\n“Oh, Margaret! here you are! I have been so wanting you. But how your cheeks are flushed with the heat, poor child! But only think what that tiresome Henry has done; really, he exceeds brother-in-law’s limits. Just when my party was made up so beautifully—fitted in so precisely for Mr. Colthurst—there has Henry come, with an apology it is true, and making use of your name for an excuse, and asked me if he may bring that Mr. Thornton of Milton—your tenant, you know—who is in London about some law business. It will spoil my number, quite.”\n\n“I don’t mind dinner. I don’t want any,” said Margaret, in a low voice. “Dixon can get me a cup of tea here, and I will be in the drawing-room by the time you come up. I shall really be glad to lie down.”\n\n“No, no! that will never do. You do look wretchedly white, to be sure; but that is just the heat, and we can’t do without you possibly. (Those flowers a little lower, Dixon. They look glorious flames, Margaret, in your black hair.) You know we planned you to talk about Milton to Mr. Colthurst. Oh! to be sure! and this man comes from Milton. I believe it will be capital, after all. Mr. Colthurst can pump him well on all the subjects in which he is interested, and it will be great fun to trace out your experiences, and this Mr. Thornton’s wisdom, in Mr. Colthurst’s next speech in the House. Really, I think it is a happy hit of Henry’s. I asked him if he was a man one would be ashamed of; and he replied, ‘Not if you’ve any sense in you, my little sister.’ So I suppose he is able to sound his h’s, which is not a common Darkshire accomplishment—eh, Margaret?”\n\n“Mr. Lennox did not say why Mr. Thornton was come up to town? Was it law business connected with the property?” asked Margaret, in a constrained voice.\n\n“Oh! he’s failed, or something of the kind, that Henry told you of that day you had such a headache—what was it? (There, that’s capital, Dixon. Miss Hale does us credit, does she not?) I wish I was as tall as a queen, and as brown as a gipsy, Margaret.”\n\n“But about Mr. Thornton?”\n\n“Oh! I really have such a terrible head for law business. Henry will like nothing better than to tell you all about it. I know the impression he made upon me was, that Mr. Thornton is very badly off, and a very respectable man, and that I’m to be very civil to him: and as I did not know how, I came to you to ask you to help me. And now come down with me, and rest on the sofa for a quarter of an hour.”\n\nThe privileged brother-in-law came early; and Margaret, reddening as she spoke, began to ask him the questions she wanted to hear answered about Mr. Thornton.\n\n“He came up about this sub-letting the property—Marlborough Mills, and the house and premises adjoining, I mean. He is unable to keep it on; and there are deeds and leases to be looked over and agreements to be drawn up. I hope Edith will receive him properly; but she was rather put out, as I could see, by the liberty I had taken in begging for an invitation for him. But I thought you would like to have some attention shown him: and one would be particularly scrupulous in paying every respect to a man who is going down in the world.” He had dropped his voice to speak to Margaret, by whom he was sitting; but as he ended he sprang up, and introduced Mr. Thornton, who had that moment entered, to Edith and Captain Lennox.\n\nMargaret looked with an anxious eye at Mr. Thornton while he was thus occupied. It was considerably more than a year since she had seen him; and events had occurred to change him much in that time. His fine figure yet bore him above the common height of men; and gave him a distinguished appearance, from the ease of motion which arose out of it, and was natural to him; but his face looked older and careworn; yet a noble composure sate upon it, which impressed those who had just been hearing of his changed position, with a sense of inherent dignity and manly strength. He was aware, from the first glance he had given round the room, that Margaret was there; he had seen her intent look of occupation as she listened to Mr. Henry Lennox; and he came up to her with the perfectly regulated manner of an old friend. With his first calm words a vivid colour flashed into her cheeks, which never left them again during the evening. She did not seem to have much to say to him. She disappointed him by the quiet way in which she asked what seemed to him to be the merely necessary questions respecting her old acquaintances, in Milton; but others came in—more intimate in the house than he—and he fell into the background, where he and Mr. Lennox talked together from time to time.\n\n“You think Miss Hale looking well,” said Mr. Lennox, “don’t you? Milton didn’t agree with her, I imagine; for when she first came to London, I thought I had never seen any one so much changed. To-night she is looking radiant. But she is much stronger. Last autumn she was fatigued with a walk of a couple of miles. On Friday evening we walked up to Hampstead and back. Yet on Saturday she looked as well as she does now.”\n\n“We!” Who? They two alone?\n\nMr. Colthurst was a very clever man, and a rising member of Parliament. He had a quick eye at discerning character, and was struck by a remark which Mr. Thornton made at dinner-time. He enquired from Edith who that gentleman was; and, rather to her surprise, she found, from the tone of his “Indeed!” that Mr. Thornton of Milton was not such an unknown name to him as she had imagined it would be. Her dinner was going off well. Henry was in good humour, and brought out his dry caustic wit admirably. Mr. Thornton and Mr. Colthurst found one or two mutual subjects of interest, which they could only touch upon then, reserving them for more private after-dinner talk. Margaret looked beautiful in the pomegranate flowers; and if she did lean back in her chair and speak but little, Edith was not annoyed, for the conversation flowed on smoothly without her. Margaret was watching Mr. Thornton’s face. He never looked at her: so she might study him unobserved, and note the changes which even this short time had wrought in him. Only at some unexpected mot of Mr. Lennox’s, his face flashed out into the old look of intense enjoyment: the merry brightness returned to his eyes, the lips just parted to suggest the brilliant smile of former days; and for an instant, his glance instinctively sought hers, as if he wanted her sympathy. But when their eyes met, his whole countenance changed; he was grave and anxious once more; and he resolutely avoided even looking near her again during dinner.\n\nThere were only two ladies beside their own party, and as these were occupied in conversation by her aunt and Edith, when they went up into the drawing-room, Margaret languidly employed herself about some work. Presently the gentlemen came up, Mr. Colthurst and Mr. Thornton in close conversation. Mr. Lennox drew near to Margaret, and said in a low voice:\n\n“I really think Edith owes me thanks for my contribution to her party. You’ve no idea what an agreeable, sensible fellow this tenant of yours is. He has been the very man to give Colthurst all the facts he wanted coaching in. I can’t conceive how he contrived to mismanage his affairs.”\n\n“With his powers and opportunities you would have succeeded,” said Margaret. He did not quite relish the tone in which she spoke, although the words but expressed a thought which had passed through his own mind. As he was silent, they caught a swell in the sound of conversation going on near the fire-place between Mr. Colthurst and Mr. Thornton.\n\n“I assure you, I heard it spoken of with great interest—curiosity as to its result, perhaps I should rather say. I heard your name frequently mentioned during my short stay in the neighbourhood.” Then they lost some words; and when next they could hear Mr. Thornton was speaking.\n\n“I have not the elements for popularity—if they spoke of me in that way, they were mistaken. I fall slowly into new projects; and I find it difficult to let myself be known, even by those whom I desire to know, and with whom I would fain have no reserve. Yet, even with all these drawbacks, I felt that I was on the right path, and that, starting from a kind of friendship with one, I was becoming acquainted with many. The advantages were mutual: we were both unconsciously and consciously teaching each other.”\n\n“You say ‘were.’ I trust you are intending to pursue the same course?”\n\n“I must stop Colthurst,” said Henry Lennox, hastily. And by an abrupt, yet apropos question, he turned the current of the conversation, so as not to give Mr. Thornton the mortification of acknowledging his want of success and consequent change of position. But as soon as the newly-started subject had come to a close, Mr. Thornton resumed the conversation just where it had been interrupted, and gave Mr. Colthurst the reply to his inquiry.\n\n“I have been unsuccessful in business, and have had to give up my position as a master. I am on the look-out for a situation in Milton, where I may meet with employment under some one who will be willing to let me go along my own way in such matters as these. I can depend upon myself for having no go-ahead theories that I would rashly bring into practice. My only wish is to have the opportunity of cultivating some intercourse with the hands beyond the mere ‘cash nexus.’ But it might be the point Archimedes sought from which to move the earth, to judge by the importance attached to it by some of our manufacturers, who shake their heads and look grave as soon as I name the one or two experiments that I should like to try.”\n\n“You call them ‘experiments’ I notice,” said Mr. Colthurst, with a delicate increase of respect in his manner.\n\n“Because I believe them to be such. I am not sure of the consequences that may result from them. But I am sure they ought to be tried. I have arrived at the conviction that no mere institutions, however wise, and however much thought may have been required to organise and arrange them, can attach class to class as they should be attached, unless the working out of such institutions bring the individuals of the different classes into actual personal contact. Such intercourse is the very breath of life. A working man can hardly be made to feel and know how much his employer may have laboured in his study at plans for the benefit of his workpeople. A complete plan emerges like a piece of machinery, apparently fitted for every emergency. But the hands accept it as they do machinery, without understanding the intense mental labour and forethought required to bring it to such perfection. But I would take an idea, the working out of which would necessitate personal intercourse; it might not go well at first, but at every hitch interest would be felt by an increasing number of men, and at last its success in working come to be desired by all, as all had borne a part in the formation of the plan; and even then I am sure that it would lose its vitality, cease to be living, as soon as it was no longer carried on by that sort of common interest which invariably makes people find means and ways of seeing each other, and becoming acquainted with each others’ characters and persons, and even tricks of temper and modes of speech. We should understand each other better, and I’ll venture to say we should like each other more.”\n\n“And you think they may prevent recurrence of strikes?”\n\n“Not at all. My utmost expectation only goes as far as this—that they may render strikes not the bitter, venomous sources of hatred they have hitherto been. A more hopeful man might imagine that a closer and more genial intercourse between classes might do away with strikes. But I am not a hopeful man.”\n\nSuddenly, as if a new idea had struck him, he crossed over to where Margaret was sitting, and began, without preface, as if he knew she had been listening to all that had passed:\n\n“Miss Hale, I had a round-robin from some of my men—I suspect in Higgins’ handwriting—stating their wish to work for me, if ever I was in a position to employ men again on my own behalf. That was good, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Yes. Just right. I am glad of it,” said Margaret, looking up straight into his face with her speaking eyes, and then dropping them under his eloquent glance. He gazed back at her for a minute, as if he did not know exactly what he was about. Then sighed; and saying, “I knew you would like it,” he turned away, and never spoke to her again until he bid her a formal “good night.”\n\nAs Mr. Lennox took his departure, Margaret said, with a blush that she could not repress, and with some hesitation,\n\n“Can I speak to you to-morrow? I want your help about—something.”\n\n“Certainly. I will come at whatever time you name. You cannot give me a greater pleasure than by making me of any use. At eleven? Very well.”\n\nHis eye brightened with exultation. How she was learning to depend upon him! It seemed as if any day now might give him the certainty, without having which he had determined never to offer to her again.\n\n# CHAPTER LII\n\n“PACK CLOUDS AWAY.”\n\n“For joy or grief, for hope or fear, For all hereafter, as for here, In peace or strife, in storm or shine.” ANON.\n\nEdith went about on tip-toe, and checked Sholto in all loud speaking that next morning, as if any sudden noise would interrupt the conference that was taking place in the drawing-room. Two o’clock came; and they still sate there with closed doors. Then there was a man’s footstep running down stairs; and Edith peeped out of the drawing-room.\n\n“Well, Henry?” said she, with a look of interrogation.\n\n“Well!” said he, rather shortly.\n\n“Come in to lunch!”\n\n“No, thank you, I can’t. I’ve lost too much time here already.”\n\n“Then it’s not all settled?” said Edith, despondingly.\n\n“No! not at all. It never will be settled, if the ‘it’ is what I conjecture you mean. That will never be, Edith, so give up thinking about it.”\n\n“But it would be so nice for us all,” pleaded Edith. “I should always feel comfortable about the children, if I had Margaret settled down near to me. As it is, I am always afraid of her going off to Cadiz.”\n\n“I will try, when I marry, to look out for a young lady who has a knowledge of the management of children. That is all I can do. Miss Hale will not have me. And I shall not ask her.”\n\n“Then what have you been talking about?”\n\n“A thousand things you would not understand: Investments, and leases, and value of land.”\n\n“Oh, go away if that’s all. You and she will be unbearably stupid, if you’ve been talking all this time about such weary things.”\n\n“Very well. I’m coming again to-morrow, and bringing Mr. Thornton with me, to have some more talk with Miss Hale.”\n\n“Mr. Thornton! What has he to do with it?”\n\n“He is Miss Hale’s tenant,” said Mr. Lennox, turning away. “And he wishes to give up his lease.”\n\n“Oh! very well. I can’t understand details, so don’t give them me.”\n\n“The only detail I want you to understand is, to let us have the back drawing-room undisturbed, as it was to-day. In general, the children and servants are so in and out, that I can never get any business satisfactorily explained; and the arrangements we have to make to-morrow are of importance.”\n\nNo one ever knew why Mr. Lennox did not keep to his appointment on the following day. Mr. Thornton came true to his time; and after keeping him waiting for nearly an hour, Margaret came in looking very white and anxious.\n\nShe began hurriedly:\n\n“I am so sorry Mr. Lennox is not here,—he could have done it so much better than I can. He is my adviser in this”——\n\n“I am sorry that I came, if it troubles you. Shall I go to Mr. Lennox’s chambers and try and find him?”\n\n“No, thank you. I wanted to tell you, how grieved I was to find that I am to lose you as a tenant. But, Mr. Lennox says, things are sure to brighten”——\n\n“Mr. Lennox knows little about it,” said Mr. Thornton quietly. “Happy and fortunate in all a man cares for, he does not understand what it is to find oneself no longer young—yet thrown back to the starting-point which requires the hopeful energy of youth—to feel one half of life gone, and nothing done—nothing remaining of wasted opportunity, but the bitter recollection that it has been. Miss Hale, I would rather not hear Mr. Lennox’s opinion of my affairs. Those who are happy and successful themselves are too apt to make light of the misfortunes of others.”\n\n“You are unjust,” said Margaret, gently. “Mr. Lennox has only spoken of the great probability which he believes there to be of your redeeming—your more than redeeming what you have lost—don’t speak till I have ended—pray don’t.” And collecting herself once more, she went on rapidly turning over some law papers, and statement of accounts in a trembling hurried manner. “Oh! here it is! and—he drew me out a proposal—I wish he was here to explain it—showing that if you would take some money of mine, eighteen hundred and fifty-seven pounds, lying just at this moment unused in the bank, and bringing me in only two and a half per cent.—you could pay me much better interest, and might go on working Marlborough Mills.” Her voice had cleared itself and become more steady. Mr. Thornton did not speak, and she went on looking for some paper on which were written down the proposals for security; for she was most anxious to have it all looked upon in the light of a mere business arrangement, in which the principal advantage would be on her side. While she sought for this paper, her very heart-pulse was arrested by the tone in which Mr. Thornton spoke. His voice was hoarse, and trembling with tender passion, as he said:—\n\n“Margaret!”\n\nFor an instant she looked up; and then sought to veil her luminous eyes by dropping her forehead on her hands. Again, stepping nearer, he besought her with another tremulous eager call upon her name.\n\n“Margaret!”\n\nStill lower went the head; more closely hidden was the face, almost resting on the table before her. He came close to her. He knelt by her side, to bring his face to a level with her ear; and whispered—panted out the words:—\n\n“Take care.—If you do not speak—I shall claim you as my own in some strange presumptuous way.—Send me away at once, if I must go;—Margaret!—”\n\nAt that third call she turned her face, still covered with her small white hands, towards him, and laid it on his shoulder, even hiding it there; and it was too delicious to feel her soft cheek against his, for him to wish to see either deep blushes or loving eyes. He clasped her close. But they both kept silence. At length she murmured in a broken voice:\n\n“Oh, Mr. Thornton, I am not good enough!”\n\n“Not good enough! Don’t mock my own deep feeling of unworthiness.”\n\nAfter a minute or two he gently disengaged her hands from her face, and laid her arms as they had once before been placed to protect him from the rioters.\n\n“Do you remember, love?” he murmured. “And how I requited you with my insolence the next day?”\n\n“I remember how wrongly I spoke to you,—that is all.”\n\n“Look here! Lift up your head. I have something to show you!” She slowly faced him, glowing with beautiful shame.\n\n“Do you know these roses?” he said, drawing out his pocket-book, in which were treasured up some dead flowers.\n\n“No!” she replied, with innocent curiosity. “Did I give them to you?”\n\n“No! Vanity; you did not. You may have worn sister roses very probably.”\n\nShe looked at them, wondering for a minute, then she smiled a little as she said—\n\n“They are from Helstone, are they not? I know the deep indentations round the leaves. Oh! have you been there? When were you there?”\n\n“I wanted to see the place where Margaret grew to what she is, even at the worst time of all, when I had no hope of ever calling her mine. I went there on my return from Havre.”\n\n“You must give them to me,” she said, trying to take them out of his hand with gentle violence.\n\n“Very well. Only you must pay me for them!”\n\n“How shall I ever tell Aunt Shaw?” she whispered, after some time of delicious silence.\n\n“Let me speak to her.”\n\n“Oh, no! I owe to her,—but what will she say?”\n\n“I can guess. Her first exclamation will be, ‘That man!’”\n\n“Hush!” said Margaret, “or I shall try and show you your mother’s indignant tones as she says, ‘That woman!’”";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-4276"]="# 제1장\n\n「혼례를 서둘러라」\n\n“구애하고 혼인하고 만사가 이루어졌네.”\n\n“이디스!” 마거릿이 나직이 불렀다. “이디스!”\n\n그러나 마거릿이 반쯤 짐작한 대로 이디스는 잠들어 있었다. 할리 가 안쪽 응접실 소파에 몸을 웅크린 채 누운 그녀는 흰 모슬린 드레스에 파란 리본을 달고 있어 무척이나 어여뻤다. 티타니아가 흰 모슬린과 파란 리본을 걸치고 안쪽 응접실의 진홍빛 다마스크 소파에서 잠든 적이 있었다면, 이디스를 그 요정으로 착각했을지도 모른다. 마거릿은 사촌의 아름다움에 새삼 마음이 흔들렸다. 둘은 어릴 적부터 함께 자랐고, 마거릿만 빼고는 누구나 이디스의 예쁘장함을 입에 올렸다. 그러나 마거릿은 요 며칠 전까지 그것을 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 곧 벗을 잃게 되리라는 예감이 이디스가 지닌 온갖 사랑스러운 자질과 매력에 한층 힘을 실어 주는 듯했다. 두 사람은 신부 드레스와 혼례 절차 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. 레녹스 대위 이야기도, 그가 이디스에게 들려준 코르푸에서의 앞날 이야기도 나왔다. 그의 연대가 그곳에 주둔해 있었다. 피아노를 제대로 조율해 두기가 얼마나 어려운가 하는 이야기도 나왔는데, 이디스는 그것을 결혼 생활에 닥칠 수 있는 가장 무시무시한 곤란 가운데 하나로 여기는 눈치였다. 혼례 뒤 곧바로 이어질 스코틀랜드 방문에 어떤 드레스가 필요할지도 이야기했다. 그러나 소곤거리던 말소리는 어느새 나른해졌고, 마거릿은 몇 분쯤 말이 끊긴 뒤에야 알아차렸다. 옆방의 왁자한 소리에도 아랑곳없이 이디스가 모슬린과 리본과 비단결 곱슬머리로 이루어진 보드라운 공처럼 몸을 말고는 저녁 식사 뒤의 평화로운 낮잠에 빠져들었음을.\n\n마거릿은 앞으로 아버지와 어머니가 사는 시골 목사관에서 살아갈 자신의 삶에 대해 품고 있던 계획과 공상을 사촌에게 털어놓으려던 참이었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 늘 화창한 방학을 보냈다. 물론 지난 10년 동안은 쇼 이모의 집이 그녀의 집으로 여겨졌지만. 그러나 들어 줄 사람이 없으니 그녀는 여느 때처럼 자기 삶의 변화를 홀로 곱씹을 수밖에 없었다. 다정한 이모와 사랑스러운 사촌과 기약 없이 헤어진다는 아쉬움이 배어 있기는 해도 그것은 행복한 되새김이었다. 헬스톤 목사관에서 외동딸이라는 중요한 자리를 채우는 기쁨을 떠올리는 사이, 옆방에서 오가는 대화의 조각들이 그녀의 귀에 들어왔다. 쇼 이모는 그날 저녁 식사에 왔던 대여섯 명의 부인들과 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 그 남편들은 아직 식당에 남아 있었다. 그들은 이 집에 낯익은 지인들이었다. 쇼 부인이 친구라고 부르는 이웃들이었는데, 다른 어떤 이들보다 그들과 자주 식사를 함께했기 때문이고, 또 그녀나 이디스가 그들에게서 무엇인가 필요하거나 그들이 그녀에게서 무엇인가 필요할 때 점심 전에 서로의 집을 스스럼없이 찾아가는 사이였기 때문이다. 이 부인들과 그 남편들은 친구 자격으로 초대받아 이디스의 다가오는 혼인을 기려 송별 만찬을 함께하는 것이었다. 이디스는 이 자리를 그리 달가워하지 않았다. 바로 그날 저녁 늦은 기차로 레녹스 대위가 도착할 예정이었기 때문이다. 그러나 응석받이로 자라기는 했어도 그녀는 너무 무심하고 게을러 자기 뜻을 강하게 내세우지 못했고, 어머니가 송별 만찬에서 지나친 슬픔을 막는 데 늘 효험이 있다고들 하는 제철의 별미들을 이미 단단히 일러 두었다는 것을 알고는 물러서고 말았다. 그녀는 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 접시의 음식을 깨작거리며 침울하고 넋 나간 표정을 짓는 것으로 만족했다. 그러는 동안 주위 사람들은 그레이 씨의 재담을 즐기고 있었다. 쇼 부인의 만찬에서 늘 식탁 맨 아랫자리를 차지하는 그 신사는 이디스에게 응접실에서 음악을 들려 달라고 청했다. 그레이 씨는 이 송별 만찬에서 유난히 유쾌했고, 신사들은 여느 때보다 오래 아래층에 머물렀다. 마거릿이 어쩌다 엿들은 대화의 조각들로 미루어 보건대, 그들이 그러기를 잘한 셈이었다.\n\n“나야 너무 많이 겪었지요. 가엾은 장군님과 더없이 행복하지 않았다는 말은 아니지만, 그래도 나이 차이는 흠이에요. 이디스만은 그런 일을 겪게 하지 않으리라 마음먹었답니다. 물론 어미의 치우친 마음을 빼고 보아도, 그 애가 일찍 혼인하리라는 것쯤은 진작에 알았어요. 정말이지 열아홉이 되기 전에 시집갈 게 틀림없다고 여러 번 말하곤 했지요. 레녹스 대위가……” 여기서 목소리가 속삭임으로 잦아들었지만, 마거릿은 그 빈자리를 어렵지 않게 메울 수 있었다. 이디스의 경우 진정한 사랑의 길은 놀랄 만큼 순탄하게 흘러왔다. 쇼 부인은 스스로 표현한 대로 그 예감에 순순히 따랐고, 오히려 혼사를 서두른 편이었다. 젊고 예쁜 상속녀인 이디스에게 지인들이 걸고 있던 기대에는 못 미치는 혼처였는데도 말이다. 그러나 쇼 부인은 하나뿐인 자식은 사랑으로 시집가야 한다고 말하고는 힘주어 한숨을 내쉬었다. 마치 사랑이 자신이 장군과 혼인한 이유가 아니었다는 듯이. 쇼 부인은 지금의 이 약혼이 지닌 낭만을 딸보다 더 즐겼다. 이디스가 제대로 깊이 사랑에 빠지지 않았다는 말은 아니다. 그래도 그녀라면 레녹스 대위가 그려 보이는 코르푸 생활의 온갖 그림 같은 정취보다는 벨그레이비어의 번듯한 저택을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 마거릿을 듣는 내내 달아오르게 만든 바로 그 대목에서 이디스는 몸서리치고 진저리 치는 시늉을 했다. 얼마쯤은 애틋한 연인이 그 싫증을 어르고 달래 주는 즐거움 때문이었고, 얼마쯤은 집시 살림이나 임시변통 같은 것이 정말로 그녀의 취향에 맞지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 누군가 번듯한 저택과 번듯한 영지에 번듯한 작위까지 지니고 나타났더라도, 그 유혹이 이어지는 동안 이디스는 여전히 레녹스 대위에게 매달렸을 것이다. 다만 그 유혹이 지나가고 나면, 레녹스 대위가 바랄 만한 모든 것을 한 몸에 지니지 못했다는 데 대해 잘 감추지 못한 아쉬움을 이따금 느꼈을지도 모른다. 이 점에서 그녀는 어머니의 딸일 뿐이었다. 어머니 역시 인품과 재산에 대한 존경 말고는 그보다 따뜻한 감정 없이 신중하게 쇼 장군과 혼인해 놓고는, 사랑할 수 없는 사람과 맺어진 자신의 모진 팔자를 늘 조용히 한탄해 왔던 것이다.\n\n“혼수에는 아까울 것이 없었어요.” 다음으로 마거릿의 귀에 들어온 말은 그것이었다. “장군님이 내게 주셨지만 내가 다시는 두르지 않을 그 아름다운 인도 숄과 스카프를 다 그 애에게 주었답니다.”\n\n“복 받은 아가씨네요.” 또 다른 목소리가 대꾸했다. 마거릿은 그것이 깁슨 부인의 목소리임을 알아차렸다. 몇 주 전에 딸 하나를 시집보낸 터라 이 대화에 갑절로 관심이 있는 부인이었다. “헬렌도 인도 숄을 몹시 갖고 싶어 했지만, 값을 알아보니 어찌나 터무니없던지 거절할 수밖에 없었어요. 이디스가 인도 숄을 가졌다는 말을 들으면 아주 부러워하겠네요. 어떤 것인가요? 델리 것인가요? 그 곱다란 자잘한 테두리가 있는?”\n\n마거릿은 이모의 목소리를 다시 들었다. 그런데 이번에는 반쯤 누워 있던 자세에서 몸을 일으켜 어스름한 안쪽 응접실을 들여다보는 듯한 목소리였다. “이디스! 이디스!” 이모가 소리쳐 부르고는, 그 수고에 지친 듯 도로 몸을 묻었다. 마거릿이 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n“이디스는 자고 있어요, 쇼 이모. 제가 할 일이 있나요?”\n\n이디스에 관한 이 딱한 소식을 듣고 부인들은 모두 “가엾어라!” 하고 말했고, 쇼 부인의 팔에 안긴 자그마한 애완견이 그 갑작스러운 동정에 흥이 났는지 짖어 대기 시작했다.\n\n“쉿, 타이니! 이 못된 것! 네 주인을 깨우겠구나. 이디스한테 뉴턴을 시켜 숄을 내려오게 하라고 이르려던 것뿐이란다. 마거릿, 네가 좀 가 주겠니?”\n\n마거릿은 집 꼭대기에 있는 옛 아이방으로 올라갔다. 그곳에서는 뉴턴이 혼례에 쓸 레이스를 손질하느라 분주했다. 뉴턴이 그날만 벌써 네댓 번은 펼쳐 보인 숄들을 다시 풀러 가는 동안, 마거릿은 아이방을 둘러보았다. 아홉 해 전 숲에서 갓 잡혀 온 야생의 아이처럼 이 집에 와서 사촌 이디스와 살림도 놀이도 공부도 함께하게 되었을 때, 그녀가 처음으로 낯익어진 방이 바로 여기였다. 그녀는 그 어둡고 침침하던 런던의 아이방을, 깨끗한 손과 찢어진 원피스에 무섭도록 까다롭던 엄격하고 격식 차린 유모의 다스림을 떠올렸다. 위층에서 처음 마신 차도 생각났다. 아버지와 이모와 따로 마신 차였다. 두 사람은 저 아래 어딘가에서, 끝없이 깊은 계단 아래에서 식사를 하고 있었다. 자기가 하늘 위에 있는 것이 아니라면 그들은 땅속 깊은 곳에 있는 것이 틀림없다고 아이는 생각했다. 할리 가에 와서 살기 전 집에서는 어머니의 화장방이 그녀의 아이방이었다. 시골 목사관에서는 이른 시간에 일과를 마쳤으므로 마거릿은 늘 아버지 어머니와 함께 밥을 먹었다. 아, 열여덟 살의 훤칠하고 의젓한 처녀는 아홉 살짜리 어린것이 그 첫날 밤 이불 속에 얼굴을 파묻고 그토록 사납게 슬퍼하며 흘리던 눈물을 또렷이 기억했다. 유모는 이디스 아가씨가 깨니 울지 말라고 했고, 아이는 그래도 여전히 서럽게, 다만 더 조용히 울었다. 그러다 처음 본 크고 어여쁜 이모가 헤일 씨를 데리고 잠든 어린 딸을 보여 주려고 살며시 위층으로 올라왔다. 그러자 어린 마거릿은 흐느낌을 삼키고 잠든 척 가만히 누워 있으려 애썼다. 자기 슬픔으로 아버지를 마음 아프게 할까 두려웠던 것이다. 그 슬픔은 이모 앞에서는 감히 드러낼 수 없었고, 게다가 그 슬픔을 느끼는 것 자체가 잘못이라는 생각마저 들었다. 집에서 그토록 오래 바라고 궁리하고 애쓴 끝에야 그녀의 옷가지가 한결 번듯해진 처지에 걸맞게 갖추어졌고, 아버지가 며칠만이라도 교구를 떠나 런던에 올라올 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이제 그녀는 헐벗은 자리가 되어 버린 그 옛 아이방을 사랑하게 되어 있었고, 사흘 뒤면 이곳을 영영 떠난다는 생각에 고양이 같은 미련을 품고 사방을 둘러보았다.\n\n“아, 뉴턴!” 그녀가 말했다. “우리 모두 이 정든 방을 떠나기가 섭섭할 거예요.”\n\n“정말이지 아가씨, 저는 아니랍니다요. 눈이 예전 같지 않은 데다 여기는 빛이 어찌나 나쁜지, 창가가 아니면 레이스를 깁는 게 보이지가 않아요. 그런데 창가는 늘 지독한 웃풍이라 고뿔로 죽어도 이상하지 않을 정도지요.”\n\n“그렇겠네요. 나폴리에 가면 좋은 빛도 넉넉한 온기도 다 있을 거예요. 기울 것들은 되도록 그때까지 모아 두세요. 고마워요, 뉴턴. 내가 들고 내려갈게요. 바쁘시잖아요.”\n\n그리하여 마거릿은 숄을 잔뜩 안고 내려가며 그 향긋한 동방의 냄새를 들이마셨다. 이디스가 아직 잠들어 있으니 이모는 그녀에게 숄을 펼쳐 보일 인형 노릇을 해 달라고 청했다. 아무도 마음에 두지 않았지만, 아버지 쪽 먼 친척의 상중이라 입고 있던 검은 비단 드레스 차림의 훤칠하고 반듯한 마거릿의 몸매는 이디스라면 반쯤 파묻혔을 그 화려한 숄의 길고 아름다운 주름을 한껏 살려 주었다. 마거릿은 샹들리에 바로 아래에 아무 말 없이 가만히 서 있었고, 이모는 그 늘어진 천을 매만졌다. 이따금 몸을 돌릴 때면 벽난로 위 거울에 비친 자신의 모습이 눈에 들어왔고, 그녀는 그 모습에 미소를 지었다. 낯익은 이목구비가 공주의 옷차림을 하고 있었다. 그녀는 몸에 두른 숄들을 가만히 만져 보며 그 부드러운 감촉과 눈부신 빛깔을 즐겼고, 그토록 화려하게 차려입는 것이 제법 마음에 들었다. 아이가 그러하듯 입가에 조용하고 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채로. 바로 그때 문이 열리더니 헨리 레녹스 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 불쑥 들려왔다. 부인들 몇몇은 옷치레에 쏠린 여자다운 관심이 반쯤 부끄럽다는 듯 뒤로 물러났다. 쇼 부인은 새로 온 이에게 손을 내밀었다. 마거릿은 아직 숄걸이로 쓰일지도 모른다는 생각에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 다만 이렇게 들킨 우스꽝스러움을 그가 함께 웃어 주리라 믿는 듯 환하고 재미있어하는 얼굴로 레녹스 씨를 바라보았다.\n\n이모는 만찬에 오지 못했던 헨리 레녹스 씨에게 신랑인 그의 형에 대해, 신부 들러리인 그의 누이에 대해, 그 밖의 레녹스 집안사람들에 대해 온갖 것을 묻느라 정신이 없었다. 신부 들러리는 이번 일을 위해 대위와 함께 스코틀랜드에서 오는 길이었다. 마거릿은 자기가 더는 숄걸이로 필요하지 않다는 것을 알고, 이모가 잠시 잊고 있던 다른 손님들을 즐겁게 하는 일에 매달렸다. 곧바로 이디스가 안쪽 응접실에서 나왔다. 밝아진 빛에 눈을 껌뻑이며 살짝 헝클어진 곱슬머리를 뒤로 넘기는 그 모습이 영락없이 꿈에서 갓 깨어난 잠자는 숲속의 공주 같았다. 잠결에도 그녀는 레녹스라는 사람이라면 몸을 일으킬 만하다는 것을 본능적으로 알아차렸던 것이다. 그녀에게는 아직 만나 보지 못한 미래의 시누이 재닛에 대해 물을 것이 산더미였다. 그 애정을 어찌나 드러내 놓고 표하던지, 마거릿이 자존심이 세지 않았더라면 하루아침에 나타난 그 경쟁자에게 질투를 느꼈을지도 몰랐다. 이모가 대화에 끼어들자 마거릿이 다소 뒤로 물러나는데, 헨리 레녹스가 그녀 곁의 빈자리로 눈길을 보내는 것이 보였다. 이디스가 그를 질문에서 풀어 주는 즉시 그가 그 의자를 차지하리라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 이모가 그의 일정을 다소 헷갈리게 전한 탓에 그가 그날 밤 올지 확신하지 못하던 참이라 그를 보게 된 것이 거의 뜻밖이었다. 이제 즐거운 저녁이 되리라는 것이 분명했다. 그가 좋아하고 싫어하는 것은 그녀의 그것과 거의 같았다. 마거릿의 얼굴이 솔직하고 툭 트인 환함으로 밝아졌다. 이윽고 그가 다가왔다. 그녀는 수줍음이나 자의식이라고는 티끌만큼도 없는 미소로 그를 맞았다.\n\n“그래, 다들 한창 일에 파묻혀 계신 모양이군요. 부인네들 일 말입니다. 제 일과는 아주 다르지요. 제 것은 진짜 법률 일이니까요. 숄을 가지고 노는 것은 재산 계약서를 작성하는 일과는 영 다른 일이고요.”\n\n“아, 우리가 이렇게 다 같이 고운 것에 홀려 있는 걸 보시면 재미있어하실 줄 알았어요. 하지만 인도 숄은 정말이지 그 종류로는 더할 나위 없는 물건인걸요.”\n\n“그렇겠지요. 값도 더할 나위 없고요. 모자란 데가 없습니다.”\n\n신사들이 하나둘 들어오면서 웅성거림과 소란이 한층 짙어졌다.\n\n“이번이 마지막 만찬이지요? 목요일 전에는 더 없고요?”\n\n“네. 오늘 저녁이 지나면 우리도 좀 쉴 수 있을 것 같아요. 저는 몇 주째 그러지 못했거든요. 적어도 손이 더 할 일이 없어지고, 머리와 마음을 온통 차지할 수밖에 없는 일에 대한 준비가 모두 끝났을 때 찾아오는 그런 쉼 말이에요. 생각할 시간이 생기면 기쁘겠어요. 이디스도 그럴 거예요.”\n\n“이디스는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 헤일 양은 그러시리라 짐작이 갑니다. 요즘 뵐 때마다 남이 일으킨 회오리에 휩쓸려 계시더군요.”\n\n“그렇죠.” 마거릿이 다소 서글프게 말했다. 한 달 넘게 이어져 온 사소한 일들의 끝없는 소동을 떠올린 것이다. “혼인 앞에는 늘 그런 회오리라는 게 있어야 하는 걸까요? 아니면 어떤 경우에는 바로 앞에 잔잔하고 평화로운 시간이 있을 수도 있는 걸까요?”\n\n“이를테면 신데렐라의 요정 대모가 혼수를 마련해 주고, 혼례 피로연을 준비하고, 청첩장을 써 준다면 말이지요.” 레녹스 씨가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“하지만 이 모든 게 정말 꼭 필요한 수고인가요?” 마거릿이 대답을 구하듯 그를 똑바로 올려다보며 물었다. 지난 여섯 주 동안 이디스가 최고 권위자로 매달려 온, 그럴싸한 겉모양을 위한 온갖 준비에 대한 말로 다 할 수 없는 피로가 바로 그때 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그녀는 정말이지 누군가가 혼인에 얽힌 유쾌하고 조용한 생각 몇 가지를 찾도록 도와주기를 바랐다.\n\n“아, 물론입니다.” 그가 목소리를 진지하게 바꾸어 대답했다. “거쳐야 할 형식과 예식이 있지요. 자기 자신을 만족시키기 위해서라기보다 세상의 입을 막기 위해서 말입니다. 그 입을 막지 못하면 삶에 만족이란 게 거의 남지 않으니까요. 그런데 헤일 양이라면 혼례를 어떻게 치르시겠습니까?”\n\n“아, 깊이 생각해 본 적은 없어요. 다만 아주 좋은 여름날 아침이었으면 좋겠고, 나무 그늘을 지나 걸어서 교회에 갔으면 좋겠어요. 들러리는 그리 많지 않았으면 하고, 피로연은 없었으면 해요. 지금 저를 가장 힘들게 하는 바로 그것들을 안 하겠다고 마음먹고 있는 셈이네요.”\n\n“아니요, 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다. 의젓한 간소함이라는 발상은 헤일 양의 성품과 잘 어울리니까요.”\n\n마거릿은 이 말이 썩 마음에 들지 않았다. 그가 전에도 그녀 자신의 성품과 처신에 대한 이야기로 그녀를 끌어들이려 했던 일들이 떠올라 더욱 움츠러들었다. 그때마다 칭찬하는 쪽은 그의 몫이었다. 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그의 말을 다소 짧게 끊었다.\n\n“저로서는 포장된 거리 한복판의 런던 교회에 마차를 타고 가는 것보다 헬스톤 교회와 그리로 가는 길을 떠올리는 게 자연스러워요.”\n\n“헬스톤 이야기를 해 주세요. 한 번도 제게 그려 보여 주신 적이 없잖습니까. 할리 가 96번지가 침침하고 지저분하고 따분하게 닫혀 있을 동안 헤일 양이 살아갈 그곳이 어떤 데인지 좀 알고 싶습니다. 우선, 헬스톤은 마을입니까, 읍입니까?”\n\n“아, 그저 작은 촌락이에요. 마을이라고 부를 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 교회가 있고, 잔디밭 가에 집이 몇 채 있지요. 오두막이라고 하는 편이 낫겠네요. 온통 장미가 뒤덮인 오두막들이요.”\n\n“그리고 일 년 내내, 특히 성탄절에도 꽃이 핀다고 하면 그림이 완성되겠군요.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요.” 마거릿이 다소 언짢아하며 대꾸했다. “저는 그림을 그리는 게 아니에요. 헬스톤을 있는 그대로 이야기하려는 거예요. 그런 말씀은 하지 말았어야 해요.”\n\n“뉘우치겠습니다.” 그가 대답했다. “다만 실제 삶 속의 마을이라기보다 이야기 속의 마을처럼 들렸을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“정말 그런걸요.” 마거릿이 열을 내며 대답했다. “뉴포리스트를 보고 나면 제가 본 잉글랜드의 다른 곳은 다 딱딱하고 멋대가리 없어 보여요. 헬스톤은 시 속의 마을 같아요. 테니슨의 시 속 같은 마을이요. 하지만 더는 그려 보이지 않겠어요. 제가 그곳을 어떻게 생각하는지, 그곳이 정말 어떤 곳인지 말씀드리면 그저 저를 비웃으실 테니까요.”\n\n“정말이지 그러지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 아주 단단히 마음을 정하신 모양이군요. 그러면 제가 더 알고 싶은 것을 말씀해 주십시오. 그 목사관은 어떤 곳입니까?”\n\n“아, 우리 집은 그려 보일 수가 없어요. 그건 집이고, 저는 그 매력을 말로 옮길 수가 없어요.”\n\n“졌습니다. 오늘 밤은 꽤 매정하시군요, 마거릿 양.”\n\n“어째서요?” 그녀가 크고 부드러운 눈을 돌려 그를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. “저는 그런 줄 몰랐는데요.”\n\n“제가 실없는 말을 한 번 했다고 헬스톤이 어떤 곳인지도 말씀해 주지 않고, 댁에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않으시니까요. 제가 그 두 가지를, 특히 뒤엣것을 얼마나 듣고 싶어 하는지 말씀드렸는데도 말입니다.”\n\n“하지만 정말이지 제 집에 대해서는 말씀드릴 수가 없어요. 직접 알지 못하는 분께 이야기할 만한 것이 아니라는 생각이 들어서요.”\n\n“그러면……” 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다. “거기서 무엇을 하며 지내실지 말씀해 주십시오. 여기서는 한낮까지 책을 읽거나 수업을 받거나 어떻게든 머리를 갈고닦으시지요. 점심 전에 산책을 하고, 그 뒤에는 이모님과 마차를 타고 나가고, 저녁에는 무슨 약속이 있고요. 자, 이제 헬스톤에서의 하루를 채워 보십시오. 말을 타시겠습니까, 마차를 타시겠습니까, 걸으시겠습니까?”\n\n“단연코 걷지요. 우리 집에는 말이 없어요. 아버지 몫도요. 아버지는 교구 끝까지 걸어 다니세요. 길이 어찌나 아름다운지 마차를 타는 건 아까울 지경이에요. 말을 타는 것도 거의 아까울 정도고요.”\n\n“밭일은 많이 하십니까? 그건 시골 아가씨에게 어울리는 일거리라고 알고 있습니다만.”\n\n“글쎄요. 저는 그렇게 고된 일은 좋아하지 못할 것 같아요.”\n\n“활쏘기 모임이라든가, 소풍이라든가, 경마 무도회, 사냥 무도회는요?”\n\n“어머, 아니에요!” 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. “아버지의 성직록은 아주 적어요. 게다가 그런 것들이 가까이 있다 해도 제가 갈 것 같지 않고요.”\n\n“보아하니 아무것도 말씀해 주지 않으시는군요. 이건 안 하고 저건 안 한다는 말씀만 하시니까요. 방학이 끝나기 전에 한번 찾아뵙고 대체 무엇을 하며 지내시는지 봐야겠습니다.”\n\n“그래 주시면 좋겠어요. 그러면 헬스톤이 얼마나 아름다운지 직접 보시게 될 테니까요. 이제 가 봐야겠어요. 이디스가 연주하러 앉는데, 저는 악보를 넘겨 줄 만큼은 음악을 아니까요. 게다가 쇼 이모가 우리 둘이 이야기하는 걸 달가워하지 않으실 거예요.”\n\n이디스는 눈부시게 연주했다. 곡 중간에 문이 반쯤 열리더니, 들어올까 말까 망설이는 레녹스 대위가 이디스의 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀는 악보를 내던지고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 마거릿은 어리둥절해하는 손님들에게 이디스의 갑작스러운 도피를 부른 것이 어떤 광경이었는지 설명하느라 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 서 있었다. 레녹스 대위가 예정보다 일찍 온 것이었다. 아니면 정말로 그렇게 늦은 시각이었던가? 사람들은 시계를 들여다보고 마땅히 놀란 뒤 자리를 떴다.\n\n이윽고 이디스가 기쁨으로 상기된 채, 반쯤은 수줍게 반쯤은 자랑스럽게 훤칠하고 잘생긴 대위를 이끌고 돌아왔다. 그의 동생이 그와 악수를 나누었고, 쇼 부인은 늘 어딘가 애처로운 기색이 배어 있는 그 부드럽고 다정한 태도로 그를 맞았다. 그 애처로움은 자신을 마음 맞지 않는 혼인의 희생자로 여겨 온 오랜 습관에서 나온 것이었다. 이제 장군이 세상을 떠나고 나서 그녀는 삶의 온갖 좋은 것을 누리며 흠이랄 것이 거의 없어졌으니, 슬픔까지는 아니더라도 걱정거리 하나를 찾아내기가 오히려 난감했다. 그러나 요즈음 그녀는 자신의 건강을 근심의 원천으로 삼기로 했다. 그 생각을 할 때마다 신경성 잔기침이 나왔고, 어느 상냥한 의사가 그녀가 바라던 바로 그것을 처방해 주었다. 이탈리아에서의 겨울 말이다. 쇼 부인은 여느 사람 못지않게 바라는 바가 뚜렷했지만, 자신의 선의와 즐거움이라는 드러난 동기로 무엇인가를 하는 것은 결코 좋아하지 않았다. 그녀는 다른 사람의 명령이나 바람에 떠밀려 자기 뜻을 채우는 쪽을 좋아했다. 그녀는 정말로 자신이 어떤 모진 외부의 필요에 굴복하고 있다고 스스로를 설득했고, 그리하여 실은 하고 싶은 대로 다 하면서도 그 내내 그 부드러운 태도로 신음하고 한탄할 수 있었다.\n\n그런 식으로 그녀는 레녹스 대위에게 자신의 여행 이야기를 꺼냈고, 대위는 도리에 맞게 미래의 장모가 하는 말마다 맞장구를 쳤다. 그러면서도 그의 눈은 이디스를 좇았다. 이디스는 두 시간 안에 식사를 마치고 왔다는 그의 말에도 아랑곳없이 찻상을 다시 차리고 온갖 좋은 것을 내오라 이르느라 분주했다.\n\n헨리 레녹스 씨는 벽난로 선반에 몸을 기댄 채 이 집안 풍경을 재미있어하며 서 있었다. 그는 잘생긴 형 바로 곁에 있었다. 유난히 인물 좋은 집안에서 그는 못난 축이었다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 총명하고 예리하며 표정이 살아 있었다. 그가 잠자코 있으면서도 이디스와 자신이 하는 모든 일을 살짝 비꼬는 듯한 관심으로 지켜보는 것이 분명했으므로, 마거릿은 이따금 그가 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해졌다. 그 비꼬는 마음은 쇼 부인이 그의 형과 나누는 대화가 불러낸 것이었고, 그가 보고 있는 것에서 일어난 관심과는 별개였다. 그는 두 사촌이 찻상을 두고 자잘하게 부산을 떠는 모습을 어여쁜 광경이라 여겼다. 이디스는 대부분을 손수 하겠다고 나섰다. 자기가 군인의 아내 노릇을 얼마나 잘할 수 있는지 연인에게 보여 주는 것이 즐거운 기분이었던 것이다. 그녀는 주전자의 물이 식은 것을 알아내고는 부엌의 커다란 찻주전자를 올려 오라 일렀다. 그 결과라고는 문간에서 그것을 받아 들고 안으로 옮기려다 너무 무거워 감당하지 못한 것뿐이었다. 그녀는 모슬린 드레스에 검은 자국을 묻히고 자그맣고 동그란 흰 손에 손잡이 자국이 팬 채 입을 삐죽이며 들어왔고, 다친 아이가 그러하듯 그것을 레녹스 대위에게 보여 주었다. 물론 처방도 아이의 경우와 다르지 않았다. 마거릿이 재빨리 손본 알코올램프가 가장 쓸모 있는 방책이었다. 다만 이디스가 어떤 기분일 때 병영 생활에 가장 가깝다고 여기곤 하던 그 집시 야영지 같은 정취는 덜했지만.\n\n이날 저녁 뒤로는 혼례가 끝날 때까지 온통 부산스러웠다.\n\n# 제2장\n\n“숲속 빈터에 어리는 부드러운 초록빛으로, 네 어린 시절이 뛰놀던 이끼 둔덕으로, 네 눈이 처음으로 여름 하늘을 사랑스레 올려다보던 그 집 앞의 나무로.” — 헤먼스 부인.\n\n마거릿은 다시 평상복 차림이 되어, 혼례를 거들러 올라왔던 아버지와 함께 조용히 집으로 돌아가는 길이었다. 어머니는 어정쩡한 이유 여럿에 붙들려 집에 남아 있었는데, 그 가운데 어느 것도 온전히 알아들은 사람이 없었다. 다만 헤일 씨만은 훤히 알고 있었다. 낡지도 새롭지도 않은 어중간한 회색 새틴 드레스를 두둔하던 자신의 온갖 논변이 끝내 소용없었다는 것을, 그리고 아내를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 새로 차려입힐 돈이 없는 이상 아내는 하나뿐인 언니의 하나뿐인 자식의 혼례에 모습을 드러내지 않으리라는 것을. 헤일 부인이 남편을 따라나서지 않은 진짜 까닭을 쇼 부인이 짐작이라도 했더라면 드레스를 쏟아붓듯 보내 주었을 것이다. 그러나 쇼 부인이 가난하고 예쁘장한 베러스퍼드 양이던 시절로부터 어느덧 20년 가까이 흘렀고, 그녀는 부부 사이의 나이 차이에서 비롯되는 불행 말고는 온갖 서러움을 정말로 잊어버린 뒤였다. 그 한 가지에 대해서라면 반 시간이고 늘어놓을 수 있었지만 말이다. 더없이 사랑스러운 마리아는 제 마음이 고른 남자와 혼인했다. 자기보다 8살 위일 뿐인 데다 더없이 순한 성품에, 좀처럼 보기 힘든 그 푸른빛 도는 검은 머리를 지닌 사람이었다. 헤일 씨는 그녀가 들어 본 가장 흐뭇한 설교자 가운데 하나였고, 교구 목사의 완벽한 본보기였다. 이 모든 전제에서 끌어낸 결론이 그리 논리적이지는 않았을지 모르나, 어쨌든 그것은 동생의 팔자를 곱씹을 때마다 나오는 쇼 부인 특유의 결론이었다. “사랑해서 혼인했는데, 우리 마리아가 이 세상에서 더 바랄 게 무엇이 있겠어요?” 헤일 부인이 참말을 했더라면 미리 마련해 둔 목록으로 대답할 수 있었을 것이다. “은회색 글라세 비단 한 벌, 흰 밀짚 보닛 하나, 아, 혼례에 쓸 물건 수십 가지에, 살림에 쓸 물건 수백 가지요.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니가 오기 어려운 사정이었다는 것만 알았을 뿐이고, 두 사람의 재회와 인사가 지난 이삼일의 그 북새통 속 할리 가의 집에서가 아니라 헬스톤 목사관에서 이루어지게 된 것을 오히려 다행으로 여겼다. 그 집에서 그녀는 피가로 노릇을 해야 했고, 온 사방에서 동시에 찾는 사람이 되어 있었다. 지난 48시간 동안 자기가 하고 말한 모든 것을 떠올리기만 해도 이제 머리와 몸이 다 아팠다. 다른 온갖 작별 인사에 섞여 그토록 서둘러 치른, 오래도록 한집에서 살아온 이들과의 이별은 다시 오지 않을 시절에 대한 서글픈 아쉬움으로 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그 시절이 어떠했는지는 상관없었다. 이미 가 버렸고 다시는 돌아오지 않을 시절이었다. 마거릿의 마음은 그토록 그리던 제 집으로, 몇 해나 바라 온 그 자리와 그 삶으로 돌아가는 길에서 스스로 상상조차 못 했을 만큼 무거웠다. 그리움과 갈망이 가장 사무치는 그 시각, 곧 예리한 감각이 잠 속에서 윤곽을 잃기 직전의 그 시각이었으니 더욱 그러했다. 그녀는 지난날의 회상에서 억지로 마음을 떼어 내어 희망찬 앞날의 밝고 고요한 관조 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그녀의 눈에 들어오기 시작한 것은 지나간 것들의 환영이 아니라 바로 눈앞의 광경이었다. 기차 객실에 몸을 기댄 채 잠든 사랑하는 아버지였다. 푸른빛 도는 검던 머리는 이제 희끗해져 이마 위에 성글게 놓여 있었다. 얼굴뼈가 또렷이 드러나 보였다. 이목구비가 조금만 덜 곱게 다듬어졌더라면 아름답다고 하기에는 지나칠 만큼 또렷했겠지만, 실제로는 준수함까지는 아니어도 그 나름의 기품이 있었다. 얼굴은 쉬고 있었으나, 그것은 잔잔하고 만족스러운 삶을 살아온 이의 얼굴에 깃드는 평온한 고요라기보다 고단함 끝에 찾아온 휴식에 가까웠다. 마거릿은 그 지치고 근심 어린 표정에 가슴이 아팠다. 그리고 몸에 밴 괴로움과 침울을 그토록 뚜렷이 말해 주는 그 주름의 까닭을 찾아보려고 아버지의 삶에서 드러나 있고 알려져 있는 사정들을 하나하나 되짚어 보았다.\n\n“가엾은 프레더릭 오빠!” 그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다. “아, 오빠가 해군에 들어가 우리 모두에게서 사라지는 대신 성직자가 되었더라면! 그 일을 다 알 수 있으면 좋겠는데. 쇼 이모한테서는 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었어. 그 끔찍한 일 때문에 오빠가 영국으로 돌아올 수 없다는 것밖에는. 가엾은 아버지! 어쩌면 저리 슬퍼 보이실까! 집으로 돌아가 곁에서 아버지와 어머니를 위로해 드릴 수 있으니 참 다행이야.”\n\n아버지가 잠에서 깨어났을 때 그녀는 피로의 기색이라고는 조금도 없는 환한 미소로 그를 맞을 준비가 되어 있었다. 그도 마주 웃어 주었으나 희미했다. 마치 익숙지 않은 힘을 쓰는 일이라도 되는 듯이. 그의 얼굴은 이내 몸에 밴 근심의 주름으로 되돌아갔다. 그에게는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입을 반쯤 벌리는 버릇이 있었는데, 그 탓에 입술의 모양이 늘 흐트러져 얼굴에 우유부단한 표정이 서렸다. 그러나 그에게는 딸과 똑같은 크고 부드러운 눈이 있었다. 눈구멍 안에서 느릿하게, 거의 위엄 있다 할 만큼 움직이며 투명하고 흰 눈꺼풀에 잘 가려진 눈이었다. 마거릿은 어머니보다 아버지를 닮았다. 이토록 인물 좋은 부모에게서 어찌 저리 정연한 아름다움과는 거리가 먼 딸이 나왔을까 의아해하는 사람들도 이따금 있었다. 아예 아름답지 않다는 말까지 나올 때도 있었다. 입은 컸다. “네”와 “아니요”와 “나리, 그러시다면요” 정도만 겨우 나올 만큼 벌어지는 장미 봉오리 같은 입이 아니었다. 그러나 그 큰 입은 짙은 붉은 입술이 부드럽게 그린 하나의 곡선이었고, 살결은 희고 고운 편은 아니어도 상아처럼 매끄럽고 섬세했다. 평소 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 표정이 그 나이답지 않게 너무 의젓하고 조심스러웠다면, 지금 아버지와 이야기하는 그 얼굴은 아침처럼 환했다. 보조개가 가득했고, 어린아이 같은 기쁨과 앞날에 대한 끝없는 희망을 말해 주는 눈빛이 넘쳤다.\n\n마거릿이 집으로 돌아온 것은 7월 하순이었다. 숲의 나무들은 온통 짙고 무성한 검푸른 초록이었고, 그 아래 고사리는 비스듬히 드는 햇살을 남김없이 받아 안았다. 날씨는 후덥지근하고 무겁게 고요했다. 마거릿은 아버지 곁에서 성큼성큼 걷곤 했다. 가벼운 발밑에서 고사리가 눌리며 특유의 향기를 뿜어 올리는 것을 느끼며 짓궂은 즐거움으로 그것을 밟아 갔다. 그렇게 넓은 공유지로 나가 따뜻하고 향기로운 빛 속에 들어서면, 햇빛과 그 햇빛이 불러낸 풀과 꽃을 마음껏 누리는 야생의 자유로운 생명들이 무수히 보였다. 이 삶은, 적어도 이 산책만은 마거릿이 품었던 모든 기대를 그대로 이루어 주었다. 그녀는 자기 숲에 자부심을 느꼈다. 그곳 사람들은 그녀의 사람들이었다. 그녀는 그들과 진심으로 벗이 되었고, 그들만의 말을 배워 쓰는 것을 즐거워했다. 그들 사이에서 스스럼없이 지냈고, 그들의 아기를 돌보았고, 늙은이들과는 천천히 또박또박 이야기하거나 책을 읽어 주었고, 앓는 이들에게는 정성 들인 죽을 날라다 주었다. 머잖아 아버지가 정해진 소임처럼 날마다 나가는 학교에서 가르치기로 마음먹고 있었지만, 그녀는 늘 딴 데로 이끌려 숲의 푸른 그늘에 자리한 어느 오두막의 벗 하나를 — 남자든 여자든 아이든 — 보러 가곤 했다. 바깥에서의 삶은 나무랄 데가 없었다. 집 안에서의 삶에는 흠이 있었다. 아이다운 건강한 부끄러움으로 그녀는 그 집 안의 모든 것이 마땅히 있어야 할 대로 있지 않다는 것을 알아채는 자기 눈의 밝음을 스스로 나무랐다. 어머니는, 늘 그토록 다정하고 살뜰하던 어머니는 이따금 자기네 처지에 몹시 못마땅해하는 듯 보였다. 주교가 헤일 씨에게 더 나은 성직록을 주지 않는 것을 두고 주교 직분을 이상하리만치 소홀히 한다고 여겼고, 이 교구를 떠나 더 큰 교구를 맡고 싶다는 말을 차마 입에 올리지 못하는 남편을 나무라다시피 했다. 그러면 그는 소리 내어 한숨을 쉬며, 이 작은 헬스톤에서 제 할 도리만 다할 수 있어도 고마운 일이라고 대답했다. 그러나 날이 갈수록 그는 더 짓눌렸고, 세상은 더 갈피를 잡을 수 없어졌다. 아내가 무슨 승진 자리라도 알아보라고 거듭 몰아붙일 때마다 마거릿은 아버지가 점점 더 움츠러드는 것을 보았다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 어머니를 헬스톤과 화해시키려 애썼다. 헤일 부인은 나무가 이렇게 많은 곳 가까이 사는 것이 건강에 좋지 않다고 말했고, 마거릿은 볕이 줄무늬처럼 들고 구름 그림자가 지나가는 그 아름답고 널따란 고지의 공유지로 어머니를 꾀어내려 했다. 어머니가 집 안에서만 지내는 데 너무 익숙해져 산책이라고 해야 교회와 학교와 이웃 오두막 언저리를 좀처럼 벗어나지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다. 한동안은 효험이 있었다. 그러나 가을이 짙어지고 날씨가 더 변덕스러워지자 이곳이 건강에 나쁘다는 어머니의 생각은 더 커졌다. 그리고 흄 씨보다 학식이 깊고 홀즈워스 씨보다 나은 교구 목사인 남편이 어째서 그 두 옛 이웃이 얻은 승진을 얻지 못했느냐고 더욱 자주 한탄했다.\n\n기나긴 불평의 시간으로 집안의 평화가 이렇게 상해 가는 것에 마거릿은 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 할리 가에서는 그저 성가심이자 자유를 옭아매는 굴레였을 뿐인 여러 호사를 포기해야 하리라는 것은 알고 있었고, 오히려 그 생각을 즐긴 편이었다. 감각의 온갖 즐거움을 예민하게 누리는 그녀의 성정은 필요하다면 그 모든 것 없이도 살 수 있다는 자각에서 오는 자부심으로 알맞게, 아니 어쩌면 넘칠 만큼 균형이 잡혀 있었다. 그러나 구름은 우리가 지켜보고 있는 쪽 하늘에서는 결코 오지 않는 법이다. 예전에 집에서 방학을 보낼 때도 헬스톤과 그곳에서의 아버지 처지에 얽힌 사소한 일들을 두고 어머니가 조금씩 불평하거나 지나가듯 아쉬워한 적은 있었다. 그러나 그 시절을 떠올릴 때의 전체적인 행복감 속에서 그녀는 그리 유쾌하지 않았던 자잘한 대목들을 잊고 있었던 것이다.\n\n9월 후반에 접어들자 가을비와 폭풍이 몰아쳤고, 마거릿은 그때까지보다 집 안에 더 머물러야 했다. 헬스톤은 자기네와 같은 교양 수준의 이웃이라면 어느 집에서든 제법 떨어져 있었다.\n\n“여기는 두말할 것 없이 잉글랜드에서 가장 외진 곳 가운데 하나예요.” 헤일 부인이 그 한탄 섞인 기분에 잠긴 어느 날 말했다. “아버지가 여기서는 정말이지 어울릴 사람 하나 없다는 게 늘 안타까워 견딜 수가 없구나. 그이는 이렇게 버려져 있잖니. 한 주가 가고 또 한 주가 가도록 농부와 품팔이꾼 말고는 아무도 만나지 못하고. 교구 저쪽 편에만 살았어도 좀 나았을 텐데. 거기라면 스탠스필드 댁까지 거의 걸어갈 만한 거리이고, 고먼 댁이야 분명 걸어갈 만한 거리이니 말이다.”\n\n“고먼이라니요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “사우샘프턴에서 장사로 재산을 모았다는 그 고먼 댁 말씀이세요? 아, 그 집과 왕래하지 않아 다행이에요. 저는 장사치들이 마음에 들지 않아요. 오두막 사람들과 품팔이꾼들, 잘난 체할 줄 모르는 사람들만 알고 지내는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각해요.”\n\n“그렇게 까다롭게 굴면 못쓴다, 마거릿아.” 어머니가 말했다. 언젠가 흄 씨 댁에서 만난 적이 있는 젊고 인물 좋은 고먼 씨를 속으로 떠올리면서였다.\n\n“아니에요! 제 취향은 아주 너른걸요. 저는 땅과 관련된 일을 하는 사람이라면 다 좋아요. 군인도 뱃사람도 좋고, 사람들이 말하는 그 세 가지 학식 있는 직업도 좋아요. 설마 어머니가 저더러 푸주한이며 빵장수며 촛대장이까지 우러러보라고 하시지는 않겠지요?”\n\n“하지만 고먼 댁은 푸주한도 빵장수도 아니고, 아주 번듯한 마차 제작자였단다.”\n\n“그렇군요. 마차 제작도 어차피 장사이고, 제 생각에는 푸주한이나 빵장수보다 훨씬 쓸데없는 장사인걸요. 아, 쇼 이모의 마차를 타고 날마다 나다니는 게 얼마나 넌더리 났는지 몰라요. 그리고 얼마나 걷고 싶었는지도요!”\n\n그리하여 마거릿은 날씨야 어떻든 걸었다. 아버지 곁에서 바깥에 나서면 어찌나 행복한지 거의 춤을 추다시피 했다. 어느 히스 벌판을 가로지를 때 서풍이 뒤에서 부드럽게 몰아붙이면, 그녀는 가을바람에 실려 가는 낙엽처럼 가볍고 수월하게 앞으로 떠밀려 가는 듯했다. 그러나 저녁 시간은 유쾌하게 채우기가 제법 어려웠다. 차를 마시자마자 아버지는 자기 작은 서재로 물러갔고, 그녀와 어머니만 남았다. 헤일 부인은 애초에 책을 그리 좋아한 적이 없었고, 신혼 초에 이미 자기가 바느질하는 동안 소리 내어 책을 읽어 주고 싶어 하던 남편의 뜻을 꺾어 버렸다. 한때는 방편으로 주사위판 놀이를 해 보기도 했다. 그러나 헤일 씨가 학교와 교구민들에게 점점 더 마음을 쏟게 되면서, 그 소임에서 생기는 방해를 아내가 성직자의 당연한 조건으로 받아들이기는커녕 그때그때 안타까워하며 맞서 싸울 고생거리로 여긴다는 것을 그는 알게 되었다. 그래서 그는 아이들이 아직 어릴 적에 서재로 물러나, 집에 있는 저녁이면 자기가 즐기는 사변적이고 형이상학적인 책들을 읽으며 시간을 보내게 된 것이다.\n\n전에 이곳에 와 있을 때 마거릿은 선생들이나 가정교사가 권한 책을 큰 상자로 하나 가득 들고 내려왔고, 도시로 돌아가기 전에 읽어야 할 분량을 다 해내기에는 여름 하루가 너무 짧다고 여겼다. 그런데 이제는 응접실의 작은 책장을 채우려고 아버지의 서재에서 골라낸, 장정만 훌륭하고 거의 읽히지 않은 영국 고전들뿐이었다. 톰슨의 『사계』, 헤일리가 쓴 카우퍼 전기, 미들턴의 키케로 전기가 그중에서는 단연 가볍고 새롭고 재미있는 축이었다. 책장에서 기대할 것은 그리 많지 않았다. 마거릿은 런던 생활을 시시콜콜 어머니에게 들려주었고, 헤일 부인은 그 모든 이야기를 흥미롭게 들었다. 때로는 재미있어하며 이것저것 물었고, 때로는 언니의 넉넉하고 편안한 형편을 헬스톤 목사관의 옹색한 살림과 견주고 싶어 하는 기색도 있었다. 그런 저녁이면 마거릿은 이야기를 다소 뚝 그치고, 자그마한 활 모양 창의 납 지붕 위로 떨어지는 빗방울 소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했다. 한두 번은 자기도 모르게 그 단조로운 소리가 되풀이되는 횟수를 기계적으로 세고 있었다. 그러면서 마음속 깊이 걸려 있는 문제를 감히 여쭈어도 될까, 프레더릭 오빠가 지금 어디에 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지, 소식을 들은 지 얼마나 되었는지 물어도 될까 망설였다. 그러나 어머니의 약한 몸과 헬스톤을 노골적으로 싫어하는 마음이 모두 프레더릭이 휘말렸던 그 반란 때부터 비롯되었다는 자각이 — 그 일의 자초지종을 마거릿은 한 번도 들은 적이 없었고 이제는 슬픈 망각 속에 묻혀 버릴 운명인 듯했다 — 그 이야기에 다가갈 때마다 그녀를 멈칫하게 하고 발길을 돌리게 했다. 어머니와 있을 때는 아버지가 물어보기에 가장 나은 사람 같았고, 아버지와 있을 때는 어머니에게라면 더 쉽게 말을 꺼낼 수 있을 것 같았다. 아마 새로 들을 만한 이야기도 별로 없었을 것이다. 할리 가를 떠나기 전에 받은 편지 가운데 하나에서 아버지는 프레더릭에게서 소식이 왔다고, 아직 리우에 있고 몸은 아주 건강하며 그녀에게 사랑을 전한다고 알려 왔다. 그것은 앙상한 뼈다귀였을 뿐, 그녀가 갈망하던 살아 있는 소식은 아니었다. 프레더릭의 이름이 드물게 입에 오를 때면 그는 늘 “가엾은 프레더릭”이라고 불렸다. 그의 방은 그가 떠날 때 그대로 보존되었고, 헤일 부인의 하녀 딕슨이 꼬박꼬박 먼지를 털고 정돈했다. 딕슨은 집안일의 다른 일에는 손도 대지 않았으나, 베러스퍼드 부인에게 고용되어 존 경의 피후견인들, 곧 러틀랜드셔의 미인이던 어여쁜 베러스퍼드 자매의 시녀가 되던 그날을 언제나 기억하고 있었다. 딕슨은 헤일 씨를 늘 자기 아가씨의 앞날에 떨어진 재앙이라고 여겼다. 베러스퍼드 양이 그렇게 서둘러 가난한 시골 성직자와 혼인하지만 않았더라면 무엇이 되었을지 누가 알겠는가. 그러나 딕슨은 너무 충직해서 아가씨의 고난과 몰락(아, 그 결혼 생활 말이다) 속에서 그녀를 저버릴 수 없었다. 그녀는 곁에 남아 아가씨의 이익에 헌신했고, 언제나 자기를 착하고 지켜 주는 요정으로, 사악한 거인 헤일 씨의 훼방을 막아 내는 것이 제 소임인 존재로 여겼다. 프레더릭 도련님은 그녀가 아끼는 자랑거리였다. 그래서 그 위엄 있는 얼굴과 몸가짐을 조금은 누그러뜨린 채 주마다 그 방에 들어가, 마치 바로 그날 저녁에라도 도련님이 돌아올 듯 정성스레 정돈해 놓곤 했다.\n\n마거릿은 어머니가 모르는 프레더릭의 최근 소식이 있어서 그것이 아버지를 초조하고 불안하게 만드는 것이라는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. 헤일 부인은 남편의 얼굴빛이나 몸가짐에 달라진 데가 있다는 것을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 그의 마음결은 여리고 부드러워서 남의 안부에 관한 자그마한 소식에도 쉬이 흔들렸다. 임종을 지켜보거나 무슨 범죄 이야기를 들으면 여러 날 침울해하곤 했다. 그러나 지금 마거릿이 알아챈 것은 넋이 나가 있는 기색이었다. 마치 무언가에 생각이 사로잡혀 있는데, 그 짓눌림은 남은 이들을 위로한다든가 다음 세대의 악을 줄여 보려는 마음으로 학교에서 가르친다든가 하는 일상의 어떤 행동으로도 덜어지지 않는 듯했다. 헤일 씨는 여느 때만큼 교구민들 사이로 나가지 않았고, 서재에 틀어박혀 있는 때가 더 많았다. 그리고 마을 우체부를 기다리며 안절부절못했다. 우체부가 집에 오는 신호는 뒤채 부엌 창 덧문을 두드리는 소리였는데, 한때는 집 안의 누군가가 그것이 무엇인지 알아듣고 응대할 만큼 시각을 가늠하기까지 몇 번이고 되풀이해 두드려야 하는 일이 잦았다. 이제 헤일 씨는 아침 날씨가 좋으면 뜰을 서성였고, 그렇지 않으면 우체부가 다녀가기 전까지 서재 창가에 꿈꾸는 듯 서 있었다. 우체부는 반쯤 공손하고 반쯤 은근한 몸짓으로 목사에게 고개를 까딱하고는 골목길로 내려갔고, 목사는 들장미 울타리 너머로, 커다란 소귀나무 옆을 지나 그가 멀어지는 것을 지켜본 뒤에야 방으로 들어와 무거운 마음과 사로잡힌 정신의 온갖 기색을 띤 채 그날의 일을 시작하곤 했다.\n\n그러나 마거릿은 사실의 앎에 온전히 기대지 않은 근심이라면 화창하고 볕 좋은 하루나 무슨 즐거운 바깥일 하나로 한동안 쉬이 떨쳐지는 나이였다. 그리고 10월의 눈부신 14일 동안 좋은 날씨가 이어지자 그녀의 근심은 엉겅퀴 솜털처럼 가볍게 다 날아가 버렸고, 그녀는 숲의 영광 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 고사리 거두기는 끝났고, 이제 비가 그치자 7월과 8월의 날씨에는 그저 들여다보기만 했던 깊은 빈터 여러 곳에 들어갈 수 있게 되었다. 그녀는 이디스와 함께 그림을 배운 적이 있었다. 그리고 궂은 날씨의 우울 속에서, 날이 좋았을 때 숲의 아름다움을 그저 게으르게 누리기만 한 것을 어찌나 후회했던지, 겨울이 제대로 오기 전에 그릴 수 있는 것은 다 그려 두리라 마음먹게 되었다. 그리하여 어느 날 아침 화판을 부지런히 챙기고 있는데, 하녀 세라가 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 아뢰었다. “헨리 레녹스 씨 오셨습니다.”\n\n# 제3장\n\n「급할수록 돌아가라」\n\n“여인의 믿음을 얻는 법을 배우라, 그 일이 고귀한 만큼 고귀하게. 생사를 건 일인 양 용감하게, 한결같은 진중함으로.\n\n잔치의 식탁에서 그녀를 이끌어 내라, 별 뜬 하늘을 가리켜 보이라, 그대의 참된 말로 그녀를 지키라, 구애의 아첨에 물들지 않도록. — 브라우닝 부인.\n\n“헨리 레녹스 씨 오셨습니다.” 마거릿은 바로 조금 전까지 그를 떠올리며 집에서 무엇을 하며 지낼 것이냐고 묻던 그의 말을 되새기던 참이었다. 그야말로 “해 이야기를 하니 햇살이 보인다”는 격이었다. 화판을 내려놓고 그와 악수하려 앞으로 나서는 마거릿의 얼굴 위로 그 햇살의 환함이 번졌다. “어머니께 여쭈어라, 세라.” 그녀가 말했다. “어머니와 저는 이디스에 대해 여쭐 것이 산더미랍니다. 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요.”\n\n“그러겠다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?” 그가 아까보다 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“하지만 저 멀리 하일랜드에 계신다는 소식을 들었던 터라 햄프셔까지 오실 줄은 생각도 못 했어요.”\n\n“아!” 그가 한결 가볍게 말했다. “우리 젊은 부부가 어찌나 어리석은 장난을 치는지, 온갖 위험한 짓을 다 하더군요. 이 산에 오르고 저 호수에서 배를 타고. 곁에서 돌봐 줄 멘토르가 정말로 필요하겠다 싶었지요. 실제로 그랬고요. 숙부님으로서는 도무지 감당이 되지 않아서, 그 노신사가 하루 24시간 중 16시간을 노심초사하며 지내셨습니다. 정말이지 그 둘만 믿고 맡겨 둘 수 없다는 것을 한번 보고 나니, 플리머스에서 무사히 배에 오르는 것을 볼 때까지 곁을 떠나지 않는 것이 제 도리라고 여겨졌습니다.”\n\n“플리머스에 다녀오셨어요? 어머, 이디스는 그 말은 하지 않았는데요. 하기야 요즘은 워낙 서둘러 편지를 썼으니까요. 정말 화요일에 떠났나요?”\n\n“정말로 떠났고, 덕분에 저는 여러 책임에서 놓여났습니다. 이디스가 아가씨께 전해 달라며 온갖 말을 남겼지요. 어딘가에 자그마한 쪽지가 하나 있을 텐데요. 그렇지, 여기 있습니다.”\n\n“어머, 고맙습니다.” 마거릿이 외쳤다. 그러고는 아무도 보지 않는 데서 혼자 읽고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들어, 레녹스 씨가 왔다고 어머니께 다시 알리러 간다는 핑계를 댔다(세라가 필시 무엇인가 잘못 전했을 터였다).\n\n그녀가 방을 나가자 그는 특유의 살피는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다. 자그마한 응접실은 아침 햇살이 쏟아져 들어와 가장 좋은 모습을 하고 있었다. 활 모양으로 튀어나온 창의 가운데 것이 열려 있어 송이송이 핀 장미와 진홍빛 인동덩굴이 모퉁이로 고개를 내밀었고, 작은 잔디밭은 온갖 밝은 빛깔의 버베나와 제라늄으로 화사했다. 그러나 바깥이 그토록 환한 탓에 안쪽 빛깔들은 초라하고 바래 보였다. 양탄자는 새것과는 거리가 멀었고, 사라사 천은 여러 번 빨아 쓴 것이었다. 방 전체가 그가 예상했던 것보다 작고 남루했다. 그토록 여왕 같은 마거릿을 담을 배경이자 틀치고는 말이다. 그는 탁자에 놓인 책 한 권을 집어 들었다. 흰 양피지와 금박으로 된 제대로 된 옛 이탈리아식 장정의 단테 『천국편』이었다. 그 곁에는 사전이 한 권 놓여 있었고, 마거릿의 필체로 옮겨 적은 낱말들이 있었다. 밋밋한 낱말 목록일 뿐이었으나 어쩐지 그는 그것을 들여다보는 것이 좋았다. 그는 한숨을 쉬며 그것을 내려놓았다.\n\n“성직록이 그녀 말대로 정말 적은 모양이군. 베러스퍼드 집안이 좋은 가문인 것을 생각하면 이상한 일이야.”\n\n그러는 사이 마거릿은 어머니를 찾아냈다. 그날은 헤일 부인이 변덕을 부리는 날 가운데 하나여서 무슨 일이든 곤란하고 고생스럽게 여겨졌다. 레녹스 씨의 방문도 그런 모양새를 띠었다. 속으로는 그가 일부러 찾아올 만하다고 여겨 준 것이 흐뭇했지만 말이다.\n\n“정말 하필이면! 오늘은 하인들이 다림질을 마칠 수 있도록 일찍, 그것도 찬 고기밖에 없이 밥을 먹기로 했는데. 그런데도 물론 그이를 만찬에 청해야지. 이디스의 시동생인데. 게다가 네 아버지는 오늘 아침 무엇 때문인지 저리도 기가 죽어 계시고. 무엇 때문인지도 모르겠구나. 방금 서재에 들어가 보니 얼굴을 탁자에 대고 두 손으로 감싸고 계시더구나. 헬스톤 공기가 나한테 그렇듯이 당신께도 맞지 않는 게 분명하다고 말씀드렸더니, 갑자기 고개를 들고는 헬스톤을 나쁘게 말하는 소리를 한마디도 더 하지 말아 달라고, 그건 못 견디겠다고 하시더구나. 이 세상에 사랑하는 곳이 한 군데 있다면 그건 헬스톤이라면서. 하지만 아무리 그래도 이건 분명 축축하고 사람 늘어지게 하는 공기 탓이야.”\n\n마거릿은 자기와 해 사이에 얇고 차가운 구름 한 조각이 끼어든 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그녀는 어머니가 속을 털어놓는 것이 조금이나마 위로가 되기를 바라며 참을성 있게 들었다. 그러나 이제는 어머니를 레녹스 씨에게로 돌려세울 때였다.\n\n“아버지도 레녹스 씨를 좋아하시잖아요. 혼례 피로연에서 두 분이 아주 잘 어울리셨는걸요. 그이가 온 것이 아버지께도 도움이 될 거예요. 그리고 만찬은 걱정 마세요, 어머니. 찬 고기는 점심으로는 아주 훌륭해요. 레녹스 씨도 2시 만찬을 십중팔구 점심으로 여기실 거고요.”\n\n“하지만 그때까지 그이를 어떻게 하니? 이제 겨우 10시 반인데.”\n\n“제가 함께 사생하러 나가자고 청할게요. 그이가 그림을 그리는 걸 알거든요. 그러면 어머니께 거치적거리지도 않을 거예요. 그러니 지금은 들어가 주세요. 안 그러시면 그이가 아주 이상하게 여길 테니까요.”\n\n헤일 부인은 검은 비단 앞치마를 벗고 얼굴을 폈다. 거의 친척이나 다름없는 사람에게 걸맞은 다정함으로 레녹스 씨를 맞이하는 그녀는 무척이나 어여쁘고 기품 있는 부인으로 보였다. 그는 하루를 함께 지내자는 청을 받으리라 여기는 눈치였고, 어찌나 반갑게 선뜻 그 청을 받아들이던지 헤일 부인은 찬 쇠고기에 무엇이라도 하나 보탤 수 있으면 좋겠다는 생각이 들 정도였다. 그는 무엇에나 만족했다. 함께 사생하러 나가자는 마거릿의 생각을 아주 반가워했고, 곧 만찬에서 뵐 텐데 헤일 씨를 성가시게 해 드릴 수는 없다고 했다. 마거릿은 그가 고를 수 있도록 화구를 내왔고, 종이와 붓을 제대로 고른 뒤 두 사람은 더없이 유쾌한 기분으로 길을 나섰다.\n\n“자, 여기서 잠깐만 서 주세요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “비 오던 두 주 내내 저를 따라다니며 왜 자기들을 그리지 않느냐고 나무라던 오두막이 바로 이것들이거든요.”\n\n“무너져 내려 자취도 없어지기 전에 말이지요. 정말이지 그릴 작정이라면, 게다가 아주 그림 같기도 하니, 내년으로 미루지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 그런데 어디에 앉지요?”\n\n“어머! 두 달을 하일랜드에서 지내신 게 아니라 템플의 사무실에서 곧장 나오신 것 같으시네요. 이 아름다운 나무둥치를 보세요. 나무꾼들이 빛이 꼭 알맞게 드는 자리에 남겨 두었잖아요. 제가 격자무늬 담요를 그 위에 덮을 테니, 어엿한 숲속의 옥좌가 될 거예요.”\n\n“발은 저 웅덩이에 담근 채로 왕좌의 발판을 삼으시고요! 잠깐, 제가 옮길 테니 이쪽으로 더 가까이 오시지요. 이 오두막에는 누가 삽니까?”\n\n“50, 60년 전에 무단 정착민들이 지은 것들이에요. 하나는 비어 있고요. 산림지기들이 다른 쪽 사람이 세상을 뜨는 대로 헐어 낼 거래요. 가엾은 노인이지요. 보세요, 저기 계시네요. 가서 인사드리고 와야겠어요. 귀가 어찌나 어두우신지 우리 비밀 이야기도 다 들으실 거예요.”\n\n노인은 오두막 앞에서 지팡이에 기댄 채 맨머리로 볕을 쬐고 서 있었다. 마거릿이 다가가 말을 건네자 굳어 있던 그의 얼굴이 천천히 미소로 풀렸다. 레녹스 씨는 서둘러 그 두 사람을 자기 그림 속에 집어넣고는, 풍경은 그들에게 딸린 배경으로 삼아 마무리했다. 자리에서 일어나 물과 종이 부스러기를 치우고 서로의 그림을 보여 줄 때가 되어서야 마거릿은 그것을 알아차렸다. 그녀는 웃으며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 레녹스 씨는 그 표정을 지켜보았다.\n\n“이건 정말 배신이라고 해야겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저더러 저 오두막의 내력을 여쭈어보라고 하셨을 때, 늙은 아이작과 저를 그림감으로 삼고 계신 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐거든요.”\n\n“도저히 참을 수가 없었습니다. 그 유혹이 얼마나 강했는지는 모르실 겁니다. 제가 이 그림을 얼마나 아낄지는 감히 말씀드리지도 못하겠군요.”\n\n그녀가 팔레트를 씻으러 개울로 가기 전에 이 뒷말을 들었는지 그로서는 확신할 수 없었다. 그녀는 얼굴이 다소 상기된 채 돌아왔으나 더없이 천진하고 아무것도 모르는 표정이었다. 그는 그것이 다행스러웠다. 그 말이 자기도 모르게 새어 나왔기 때문인데, 헨리 레녹스처럼 자기 행동을 미리 재어 두는 사람에게는 드문 일이었다.\n\n두 사람이 돌아왔을 때 집안 풍경은 아무 탈 없이 환했다. 어머니의 이마에 끼어 있던 구름은 이웃이 더없이 때맞추어 보내온 잉어 두 마리 덕에 말끔히 걷혀 있었다. 헤일 씨는 아침 순방에서 돌아와, 정원으로 이어지는 쪽문 바로 바깥에서 손님을 기다리고 있었다. 다소 낡은 상의에 오래 쓴 모자 차림이었지만 그는 영락없는 신사로 보였다. 마거릿은 아버지가 자랑스러웠다. 아버지가 낯선 사람마다 얼마나 좋은 인상을 남기는지 볼 때마다 그녀는 늘 새롭고 애틋한 자부심을 느꼈다. 그러면서도 그녀의 빠른 눈은 아버지의 얼굴을 훑어 여느 때와 다른 어떤 동요의 자취를 찾아냈다. 걷힌 것이 아니라 그저 한쪽으로 밀쳐 둔 동요였다.\n\n헤일 씨가 두 사람의 그림을 보여 달라고 청했다.\n\n“초가지붕 빛깔을 너무 어둡게 낸 것 같구나, 안 그러니?” 그는 마거릿의 그림을 돌려주며 그렇게 말하고는 레녹스 씨의 그림을 향해 손을 내밀었다. 그 그림은 잠시, 아주 잠시 건네지지 않았다.\n\n“아니에요, 아버지. 저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 지붕에 난 바위솔과 돌나물이 비에 젖어 훨씬 짙어졌는걸요. 닮지 않았나요, 아버지?” 그녀는 레녹스 씨의 그림 속 인물들을 들여다보는 아버지의 어깨 너머로 고개를 내밀며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 아주 닮았구나. 네 몸매하며 서 있는 자세가 일품이야. 그리고 이건 딱 가엾은 늙은 아이작이 류머티즘 든 기다란 등을 뻣뻣하게 구부리는 그 모양이고. 나뭇가지에 매달린 이건 무어냐? 설마 새 둥지는 아닐 테고.”\n\n“어머, 아니에요! 그건 제 보닛이에요. 저는 보닛을 쓰고는 도무지 그림을 못 그리겠어요. 머리가 너무 더워지거든요. 제가 인물도 그릴 수 있으려나 모르겠어요. 이 근방에는 그려 보고 싶은 사람이 참 많은데요.”\n\n“꼭 그리고 싶은 사람의 모습이라면 언제든 성공하실 것 같은데요.” 레녹스 씨가 말했다. “저는 의지의 힘을 아주 믿습니다. 제 딴에는 아가씨를 그리는 데 제법 성공했다고 생각하고요.” 헤일 씨는 먼저 집으로 들어갔고, 마거릿은 만찬 때 아침 나들이옷에 꽂을 장미를 몇 송이 꺾느라 뒤에 남았다.\n\n‘여느 런던 아가씨라면 그 말에 숨은 뜻을 알아들었을 텐데.’ 레녹스 씨는 생각했다. ‘젊은 남자가 건네는 말마다 그 속에 찬사의 저의가 없는지 낱낱이 들여다보았을 텐데. 하지만 마거릿은 아무래도……. 잠깐만요!” 그가 소리쳤다. “제가 도와드리지요.” 그러고는 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 핀 벨벳 같은 진홍빛 장미를 몇 송이 꺾어 주었고, 그런 다음 그 전리품을 나누어 두 송이를 자기 단춧구멍에 꽂고는 흐뭇하고 즐거워하는 그녀를 꽃 꽂으러 안으로 들여보냈다.\n\n만찬 자리의 대화는 조용하고 기분 좋게 흘러갔다. 양쪽 다 물을 것이 많았고, 이탈리아에 있는 쇼 부인의 소식 가운데 저마다 아는 가장 최근의 것을 주고받았다. 오가는 이야기가 재미있었고 목사관의 소박하고 꾸밈없는 살림살이도 그러했으며, 무엇보다 마거릿이 곁에 있었으므로 레녹스 씨는 아버지의 성직록이 아주 적다던 그녀의 말이 조금의 과장도 없는 사실임을 처음 알아차렸을 때 느꼈던 자그마한 실망을 잊어버렸다.\n\n“마거릿아, 후식으로 배를 좀 따 왔더라면 좋았을 것을.” 갓 병에서 옮겨 따른 포도주가 손님을 대접하는 호사로 탁자에 놓이자 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n헤일 부인은 마음이 급해졌다. 목사관에서는 후식이 즉흥적이고 흔치 않은 것이기라도 한 듯한 말투였다. 그러나 헤일 씨가 뒤만 돌아보았더라면 비스킷과 마멀레이드며 이것저것이 찬장 위에 격식대로 가지런히 차려진 것을 보았을 것이다. 그러나 배 생각이 헤일 씨의 머리를 사로잡아 좀처럼 떨어지지 않았다.\n\n“남쪽 담벼락에 뵈레 배가 몇 알 익어 있는데, 그건 외국 과일이며 설탕절임을 다 합친 것보다 낫지. 어서 가서 좀 따 오려무나, 마거릿.”\n\n“정원으로 자리를 옮겨 거기서 먹자고 제안하고 싶군요.” 레녹스 씨가 말했다. “햇볕에 데워져 향기가 밴 아삭하고 물 많은 과일에 이를 박아 넣는 것만큼 맛있는 일도 없지요. 다만 곤란한 건 말벌들이 뻔뻔하게도 그 즐거움이 절정에 이른 바로 그 순간에까지 달려들어 다툰다는 겁니다.”\n\n그는 창문 너머로 사라진 마거릿을 따라나서려는 듯 자리에서 일어섰다. 헤일 부인의 허락만 기다릴 뿐이었다. 그녀로서는 만찬을 격식대로, 여태 그토록 매끄럽게 이어져 온 온갖 절차를 갖추어 마무리하고 싶었다. 더구나 쇼 장군 미망인의 동생답게 제대로 차리려고 딕슨과 함께 일부러 곳간에서 손 씻는 그릇까지 꺼내 놓은 참이었으니 말이다. 그러나 헤일 씨가 곧바로 일어나 손님을 따라나설 채비를 하니 그녀로서는 따르는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“나는 칼로 무장을 해야겠네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “자네가 말한 것처럼 그리 원시적으로 과일을 먹던 시절은 내게는 다 지나갔지. 껍질을 벗기고 네 쪽으로 갈라야 비로소 맛을 볼 수 있다네.”\n\n마거릿은 비트 잎으로 배를 담을 접시를 만들었는데, 그 잎이 배의 갈색 도는 금빛을 더없이 돋보이게 했다. 레녹스 씨는 배보다 그녀를 더 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀의 아버지는 근심에서 훔쳐 낸 이 한때의 묘미와 완벽함을 까다롭게 골라내고 싶은 마음에 가장 잘 익은 것을 정성 들여 고르고는 정원 벤치에 앉아 느긋하게 즐겼다. 마거릿과 레녹스 씨는 남쪽 담벼락 아래 자그마한 테라스 길을 따라 거닐었다. 그곳에서는 벌들이 여전히 벌통 속에서 붕붕대며 부지런히 일하고 있었다.\n\n“여기서 얼마나 완벽한 삶을 사시는지요! 저는 그동안 시인들을, 그 ‘내게는 언덕 곁의 오두막 한 채를’ 같은 소망을 다소 업신여겨 왔습니다. 그런데 이제는 아무래도 제가 도회지 물만 든 촌뜨기였을 뿐이라는 게 사실인 듯싶어 두렵군요. 지금 당장은 20년간 법을 고되게 파고든 대가로 이토록 더없이 맑고 고요한 삶을 1년만 누릴 수 있다면 넉넉히 값을 받은 셈이겠다 싶습니다. 이런 하늘이라니!” 그는 고개를 들며 말했다. “저토록 진홍과 호박빛으로 물든 잎사귀들이, 저리도 미동조차 없다니!” 그러면서 마치 둥지처럼 정원을 에워싼 커다란 숲의 나무 몇 그루를 가리켰다.\n\n“우리 하늘이 늘 지금처럼 짙푸르지는 않다는 것도 기억해 주세요. 비도 오고, 나뭇잎도 떨어져 흠뻑 젖는걸요. 그래도 헬스톤은 세상 어느 곳 못지않게 완벽한 데라고 생각하지만요. 할리 가에서 어느 날 저녁 제 이야기를 다소 업신여기셨던 걸 떠올려 보세요. ‘이야기 속의 마을’이라고 하셨죠.”\n\n“업신여기다니요, 마거릿 양! 그건 좀 매몰찬 말인데요.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르죠. 다만 그때 저는 마음 가득한 것을 선생님께 이야기하고 싶었는데, 선생님은…… 그럼 무어라고 해야 하나요? 헬스톤을 그저 이야기 속의 마을이라며 대수롭잖게 말씀하셨죠.”\n\n“다시는 그러지 않겠습니다.” 그가 다정하게 말했다. 두 사람은 길모퉁이를 돌았다.\n\n“저로서는 이런 소원이라도 품고 싶은 심정입니다, 마거릿 양……” 그가 말을 멈추고 머뭇거렸다. 말 잘하는 이 변호사가 머뭇거리는 일은 하도 드문 터라 마거릿은 조금 어리둥절해하며 그를 올려다보았다. 그러나 그 순간, 그의 무엇을 보고 그랬는지는 스스로도 알 수 없었으나, 그녀는 어머니 곁으로, 아버지 곁으로, 그가 없는 어디로든 돌아가고 싶어졌다. 그가 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없는 말을 하려 한다는 것을 그녀는 확신했던 것이다. 다음 순간 그녀 안에 있던 강한 자존심이 그 갑작스러운 동요를 눌러 이겼다. 그가 그것을 알아차리지 못했기를 바라면서. 물론 그녀는 대답할 수 있고, 옳은 대답을 할 수 있다. 처녀다운 드높은 품위로 능히 끝맺을 수 있는 말을 듣기가 두려워 움츠러든다면 그것이야말로 옹졸하고 못난 짓이었다.\n\n“마거릿 양.” 그가 불시에 그녀의 손을 덥석 잡으며 말했으므로 그녀는 가만히 서서 들을 수밖에 없었고, 그러는 내내 두근거리는 제 가슴이 스스로 못마땅했다. “마거릿 양, 나는 아가씨가 헬스톤을 이토록 좋아하지 않았더라면, 여기서 이토록 더없이 평온하고 행복해 보이지 않았더라면 좋았겠다 싶습니다. 지난 석 달 동안 나는 아가씨가 런던을, 런던의 벗들을 조금이나마 그리워하기를 바라 왔습니다. 내놓을 것이 많지 않은 사람의 말을, 사실 앞날의 전망 말고는 아무것도 없는 사람의 말을 조금 더 다정하게 들어 줄 만큼은 말이지요.” 그녀가 조용히, 그러나 단호하게 그의 손아귀에서 제 손을 빼내려 애쓰고 있었으므로 그는 이어 말했다. “그러나 아가씨를 사랑하는 사람입니다, 마거릿 양. 제 자신도 어쩌지 못할 만큼요. 마거릿 양, 내가 너무 놀라게 했습니까? 말씀을 해 보세요!” 그녀의 입술이 곧 울음이라도 터뜨릴 듯 떨리는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 애써 마음을 가라앉혔다. 목소리를 다잡기 전에는 입을 열지 않으려 했고, 이윽고 말했다.\n\n“놀랐어요. 저를 그런 식으로 마음에 두고 계신 줄은 몰랐어요. 저는 늘 선생님을 벗으로 생각해 왔고, 부디 앞으로도 그렇게 생각하고 싶어요. 방금 하신 것 같은 말씀을 듣는 건 싫어요. 바라시는 대로 대답해 드릴 수가 없어요. 그러면서도 제가 선생님 마음을 언짢게 해 드렸다면 몹시 죄송할 거예요.”\n\n“마거릿 양.” 그가 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다. 그 눈은 더없는 진심과 남을 아프게 하기 싫어하는 마음을 담아 툭 트인 곧은 눈길로 그의 눈을 마주 보았다. “혹시” 하고 그는 “다른 누군가를 사랑하십니까?”라고 물으려 했다. 그러나 그 물음은 저 맑고 고요한 눈에 대한 모욕이 될 것만 같았다. “용서하십시오! 내가 너무 성급했습니다. 벌을 받는 셈이지요. 다만 바랄 수 있게만은 해 주십시오. 여태 그럴 만한 사람을 한 번도 본 적이 없다고 말해 주는 그 가엾은 위안만이라도……” 다시 말이 끊겼다. 그는 문장을 맺지 못했다. 마거릿은 그의 괴로움의 원인이 자기라는 데 대해 뼈아프게 자책했다.\n\n“아, 이런 마음을 아예 품지 않으셨더라면! 선생님을 벗으로 생각하는 것이 그렇게나 큰 즐거움이었는데요.”\n\n“하지만 바랄 수는 있겠지요, 마거릿 양. 언젠가는 나를 연인으로 생각해 주시리라고요. 지금은 아니라는 걸 압니다. 서두를 것 없지요. 그러나 언젠가는……”\n\n그녀는 대답하기 전에 자기 마음속에 있는 진실을 찾아보려 애쓰며 1, 2분 말이 없었다. 그러고는 말했다.\n\n“저는 선생님을 벗 말고 다르게 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없어요. 그렇게 생각하는 것이 좋고요. 하지만 그 밖의 무엇으로도 결코 생각할 수 없으리라는 것을 확신해요. 부디 우리 둘 다 이 모든” 그녀는 “불쾌한”이라고 말하려다가 딱 멈추었다. “대화가 있었다는 것을 잊기로 해요.”\n\n그는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 대답했다. 그러고는 여느 때의 그 차가운 어조로 말했다.\n\n“물론이지요. 아가씨 마음이 그렇게 확고하고 이 대화가 아가씨께 그토록 뚜렷이 불쾌한 것이었다면, 기억하지 않는 편이 나을 겁니다. 괴로운 것은 무엇이든 잊는다는 그 방책은 이론으로는 아주 그럴듯하지요. 하지만 적어도 제게는 실행에 옮기기가 다소 어려울 것 같군요.”\n\n“마음이 상하셨군요.” 그녀가 서글프게 말했다. “하지만 저더러 어쩌라는 말씀이신가요?”\n\n이렇게 말하는 그녀가 어찌나 진심으로 슬퍼 보이는지, 그는 잠시 제 진짜 실망과 씨름하고는 한결 밝은 목소리로, 그러나 여전히 어조에 조금 모난 데를 남긴 채 대답했다.\n\n“이 굴욕을 좀 헤아려 주셔야 합니다, 마거릿 양. 한 연인의 굴욕만이 아니라 본디 낭만과는 거리가 먼 사람의, 남들이 신중하다느니 세속적이라느니 하는 사람의 굴욕 말입니다. 그런 사람이 정념의 힘에 떠밀려 제 평소 습관 바깥으로 끌려 나왔지요. 뭐, 그 이야기는 그만두지요. 다만 제 천성의 더 깊고 더 나은 감정을 위해 스스로 낸 그 하나뿐인 물꼬에서 거절과 퇴짜를 맞은 겁니다. 제 어리석음을 비웃는 것으로 스스로를 달래는 수밖에요. 앞길이 고달픈 법정 변호사 주제에 혼인을 생각하다니!”\n\n마거릿은 이 말에 대답할 수 없었다. 그 어조 전체가 그녀를 언짢게 했다. 그것은 그에게서 종종 그녀를 밀어내곤 하던 차이의 지점들을 죄다 건드려 불러내는 듯했다. 그러면서도 그는 할리 가에서 가장 유쾌한 남자였고, 가장 마음을 헤아려 주는 벗이었으며, 누구보다 그녀를 잘 이해하는 사람이었다. 그녀는 그를 거절한 데서 오는 아픔에 한 자락 경멸이 섞여 드는 것을 느꼈다. 그 아름다운 입술이 옅은 멸시로 살짝 비틀렸다. 다행히도 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아온 두 사람은 헤일 씨와 불쑥 마주쳤다. 그가 어디 있는지는 두 사람의 머릿속에서 까맣게 잊혀 있었다. 그는 은박지처럼 얇게 한 줄로 정성껏 벗겨 낸 그 배를 아직 다 먹지 못하고 느긋하게 음미하는 중이었다. 그것은 마법사가 시키는 대로 물동이에 머리를 담갔다가 미처 빼기도 전에 한평생의 일을 다 겪었다는 동방의 왕 이야기와도 같았다. 마거릿은 얼떨떨했고, 아버지와 레녹스 씨 사이에 이어지는 시시한 대화에 낄 만큼 마음을 추스르지 못했다. 그녀는 심각한 얼굴로 좀처럼 말이 없었다. 레녹스 씨가 언제쯤 가서 지난 15분간의 일을 마음 놓고 곱씹게 해 줄까 하는 생각만 가득했다. 그 또한 그녀가 그가 떠나기를 바라는 만큼이나 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다. 그러나 아무리 애를 써서라도 몇 분쯤 가볍고 대수롭잖은 이야기를 이어 가는 것은 상처 입은 허영심에, 혹은 자존심에 그가 치러야 할 값이었다. 그는 이따금 그녀의 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 얼굴을 흘깃거렸다.\n\n‘저 사람이 여기는 것만큼 내가 저 사람에게 아무렇지 않은 존재는 아니지.’ 그는 속으로 생각했다. ‘나는 희망을 버리지 않겠어.’\n\n15분이 채 지나기도 전에 그는 조용한 비아냥을 섞어 이야기하는 투로 빠져들어 있었다. 마치 자신을 비웃는 또 하나의 자아를 의식하면서도 제 풍자가 두렵기라도 한 듯 런던의 삶과 시골의 삶을 논했다. 헤일 씨는 어리둥절했다. 이 손님은 전에 혼례 피로연에서, 또 그날 만찬 자리에서 보았던 사람과는 다른 사람이었다. 더 가볍고 더 약삭빠르고 더 세속적인 사람이었으며, 그런 만큼 헤일 씨와는 잘 맞지 않았다. 레녹스 씨가 5시 기차를 타려면 곧장 떠나야겠다고 말했을 때 그것은 세 사람 모두에게 안도였다. 그들은 헤일 부인을 찾아 작별을 고하러 집으로 향했다. 마지막 순간, 헨리 레녹스의 진짜 자아가 그 껍데기를 뚫고 나왔다.\n\n“마거릿 양, 나를 업신여기지 마십시오. 이런 시답잖은 말투로 지껄이기는 했어도 내게도 마음이란 게 있습니다. 그 증거로, 지난 30분 동안 아가씨가 나를 멸시하며 들어 준 그 태도 때문에 나는 아가씨를 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑하는 것 같습니다. 미워하는 게 아니라면 말이지요. 안녕히 계십시오, 마거릿 양…… 마거릿 양!”\n\n# 제4장\n\n“나를 헐벗은 어느 물가에 내던지라, 거기서 내가 더듬을 것이라고는 어느 슬픈 난파의 자취뿐일지라도, 그대만 그곳에 있다면 바다가 울부짖는들 나는 더 부드러운 고요를 구하지 않으리.” — 해빙턴.\n\n그는 떠났다. 집은 저녁을 맞아 문을 닫아걸었다. 짙푸른 하늘도, 진홍과 호박빛 노을도 이제 없었다. 마거릿은 이른 차 시간에 맞춰 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갔다가, 바쁜 날에 손님이 찾아온 탓에 일이 끊겨 딕슨이 잔뜩 심통이 나 있는 것을 보았다. 딕슨은 헤일 부인에게 급히 가 봐야 한다는 핑계를 대며 마거릿의 머리를 사납게 빗어 대는 것으로 그 심사를 드러냈다. 그러나 정작 마거릿은 어머니가 내려오기까지 응접실에서 한참을 기다려야 했다. 그녀는 뒤쪽 탁자에 불 켜지 않은 초를 둔 채 난롯가에 홀로 앉아 그날 하루를 곱씹었다. 즐거웠던 산책과 즐거웠던 그림 그리기, 유쾌하고 흐뭇했던 저녁 식사, 그리고 정원에서의 그 거북하고 참담했던 산책을.\n\n남자와 여자가 어쩌면 이리도 다를까! 여기 이쪽에서는 본능이 거절 말고는 어떤 대답도 할 수 없게 만들었다는 이유로 그녀가 마음이 어지러워 괴로워하고 있는데, 저쪽에서는 그가 제 삶에서 가장 깊고 가장 거룩한 청혼이었어야 할 것을 거절당한 지 몇 분도 지나지 않아, 사건 수임과 성공과 그것에 딸려 오는 온갖 겉치레—번듯한 집과 영리하고 유쾌한 교제—가 자기 욕망의 유일하게 내세울 만한 목표인 양 말할 수 있었다. 아, 이런! 그가 조금만 달랐더라면 그녀는 그를 얼마나 사랑할 수 있었을까. 그런데 돌이켜 생각해 보니 그 다름이란 밑바닥까지, 아주 깊은 데까지 닿는 것이었다. 그러다가 문득 이런 생각이 들었다. 어쩌면 그 가벼움은 꾸며 낸 것이고, 그 아래에는 쓰디쓴 실망이 감추어져 있는지도 모른다고. 만약 그녀가 사랑했다가 거절당했더라면 바로 그런 실망이 그녀 가슴에 낙인처럼 찍혔을 터였다.\n\n이 회오리치는 생각들이 그럴듯한 갈피를 잡기도 전에 어머니가 방으로 들어왔다. 마거릿은 그날 있었던 일과 오간 말들에 대한 기억을 떨쳐 내고, 딕슨이 다림질용 천이 또 눌어붙었다고 투덜대더라는 이야기며, 수전 라이트풋이 보닛에 조화를 달고 다니는 것이 눈에 띄었으니 그것이야말로 허영스럽고 들뜬 성품의 증거라는 이야기에 맞장구를 치는 다정한 청자가 되어야 했다. 헤일 씨는 넋을 놓은 채 말없이 차를 홀짝였고, 대꾸는 오롯이 마거릿의 몫이었다. 그녀는 아버지와 어머니가 어찌 이토록 잊어버릴 수 있는지, 하루를 함께 보낸 사람을 이토록 아랑곳하지 않고 이름 한 번 입에 올리지 않을 수 있는지 의아했다. 그가 두 분께 청혼한 것은 아니라는 사실을 그녀는 잊고 있었다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤 헤일 씨는 자리에서 일어나 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 얹고 손에 머리를 괸 채 무언가를 골똘히 생각하며 서 있었고, 이따금 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다. 헤일 부인은 가난한 이들에게 줄 겨울옷 문제로 딕슨과 의논하러 나갔다. 마거릿은 어머니의 털실 자수감을 챙겨 놓으며 긴 저녁 시간을 생각하니 좀 움츠러들었고, 어서 잠자리에 들 시간이 되어 그날 있었던 일들을 다시 되짚어 볼 수 있기를 바랐다.\n\n“마거릿!” 마침내 헤일 씨가 말했다. 어딘지 갑작스럽고 절박한 투여서 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다. “그 자수 일이 당장 급한 것이냐? 그러니까, 그건 두고 내 서재로 좀 올 수 있겠니? 우리 모두에게 아주 중대한 일을 이야기하고 싶구나.”\n\n“우리 모두에게 아주 중대한 일.” 레녹스 씨는 그녀가 거절한 뒤로 아버지와 단둘이 이야기할 기회가 전혀 없었다. 그럴 기회가 있었다면 그야말로 아주 중대한 일이 되었을 것이다. 우선 마거릿은 자기가 혼인 이야기가 오갈 만큼 여자로 자랐다는 것이 죄스럽고 부끄러웠다. 그리고 또 하나, 아버지가 자기 마음대로 레녹스 씨의 청혼을 물리친 것을 언짢아하시지는 않을까 알 수 없었다. 그러나 아버지가 그녀에게 하려는 말이 최근에 갑자기 벌어져 이런저런 복잡한 생각을 불러일으켰을 만한 그런 일에 관한 것이 아님을 그녀는 곧 알아차렸다. 아버지는 그녀를 자기 곁 의자에 앉히고는 불을 뒤적이고 촛불의 심지를 자르고 한두 번 한숨을 쉬고 나서야 겨우 말을 꺼낼 결심을 했는데, 그나마도 툭 튀어나오듯 나왔다. “마거릿! 나는 헬스톤을 떠나려 한다.”\n\n“헬스톤을 떠나신다니요, 아버지! 어째서요?”\n\n헤일 씨는 한두 마디 동안 대답하지 않았다. 그는 초조하고 어수선한 몸짓으로 탁자 위의 서류를 만지작거리며 몇 번이나 말하려고 입을 열었다가는 한마디도 내뱉을 용기가 나지 않아 다시 다물었다. 마거릿은 그 애타는 기다림을 차마 보고 있을 수 없었다. 그것은 그녀보다 아버지에게 더 괴로운 일이었다.\n\n“어째서요, 아버지? 어서 말씀해 주세요!”\n\n그는 문득 그녀를 올려다보더니, 느리고 억지로 자아낸 침착함으로 말했다.\n\n“내가 더는 국교회의 성직자로 남을 수 없기 때문이다.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니가 그토록 바라던 승진 가운데 하나가 마침내 아버지께 내려왔으려니 하고만 생각했었다. 그렇다면 아름답고 사랑하는 헬스톤을 떠나야 할 테고, 어쩌면 마거릿이 이따금 대성당이 있는 도시에서 보았던 그 위엄 있고 고요한 경내 관사 어딘가로 가서 살아야 할지도 몰랐다. 웅장하고 당당한 곳이기는 했으나 그리로 가기 위해 헬스톤을 삶터로서 영영 떠나야 한다면, 그것은 슬프고 길게 끄는 아픔이었을 것이다. 그러나 헤일 씨의 마지막 말이 안긴 충격에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 무슨 뜻일까? 알 수 없다는 점이 더욱 나빴다. 자식에게서 자비롭고 너그러운 판단을 구걸하다시피 하는 그 애처롭고 괴로운 표정에 그녀는 갑자기 속이 메스꺼워졌다. 아버지가 프레더릭이 저지른 일에 얽혀 든 것일까? 프레더릭은 법 밖의 사람이었다. 아버지가 아들에 대한 당연한 사랑으로 무언가를 눈감아 주기라도…….\n\n“아, 무슨 일이에요? 말씀해 주세요, 아버지! 다 말씀해 주세요! 어째서 더는 성직자로 계실 수 없다는 거예요? 프레더릭에 대해 우리가 아는 것을 전부, 그 모질고 부당한 처사를 주교님께 말씀드리기만 하면…….”\n\n“프레더릭과는 아무 상관 없는 일이다. 주교님도 그 일과는 아무 관계가 없어. 온전히 나 자신의 문제야. 마거릿, 내가 다 이야기해 주마. 이번 한 번은 무엇이든 물어보는 대로 대답해 주겠다만, 오늘 밤이 지나면 다시는 그 이야기를 하지 말자꾸나. 나는 이 괴롭고 참담한 회의가 불러올 결과를 감당할 수 있다. 그러나 나를 이토록 괴롭혀 온 그 원인을 입에 올리는 것은 내 힘에 부치는 일이구나.”\n\n“회의라니요, 아버지! 신앙에 대한 회의 말씀이세요?” 마거릿이 그 어느 때보다 큰 충격을 받고 물었다.\n\n“아니다! 신앙에 대한 회의는 아니야. 거기에는 티끌만 한 손상도 없다.”\n\n그는 말을 멈추었다. 마거릿은 어떤 새로운 공포의 벼랑 끝에 선 사람처럼 한숨을 쉬었다. 그가 정해진 과업을 서둘러 해치우려는 듯 빠르게 말을 이었다.\n\n“내가 말한들 너는 다 이해하지 못할 게다. 내가 내 성직록을 지닐 자격이 있기는 한가를 알아내려고 여러 해 동안 애태워 온 것도, 교회의 권위로 내 속에서 타들어 가는 의심을 꺼 보려고 애써 온 것도. 아, 마거릿, 이제 내가 쫓겨나야 할 그 거룩한 교회를 나는 얼마나 사랑하는지!” 그는 한동안 말을 잇지 못했다. 마거릿은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀에게는 아버지가 마호메트교로 개종하겠다는 말이라도 하는 것처럼 무섭고 알 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다.\n\n“오늘은 제 교회에서 쫓겨난 2,000명에 대해 읽고 있었다.” 헤일 씨가 희미하게 웃으며 말을 이었다. “그이들의 용기를 조금이나마 훔쳐 보려고 말이다. 그러나 소용이 없구나. 소용이 없어. 사무치게 아픈 것을 어쩔 수가 없다.”\n\n“하지만 아버지, 깊이 헤아려 보셨어요? 아, 너무나 끔찍하고 너무나 놀라운 일이에요.” 마거릿이 이렇게 말하며 갑자기 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그녀의 집을 떠받치던, 사랑하는 아버지에 대한 그녀의 생각을 떠받치던 그 든든한 주춧돌이 휘청거리며 흔들리는 듯했다. 무슨 말을 할 수 있을까? 무엇을 해야 할까? 딸의 괴로워하는 모습을 보자 헤일 씨는 그녀를 달래 보려고 마음을 다잡았다. 그는 여태 가슴에서 치밀어 오르던 마른 흐느낌을 삼키고는 책장으로 가서 요즈음 자주 읽던 책을 한 권 꺼내 들었다. 지금 자기가 들어선 이 길로 나아갈 힘을 그 책에서 얻었다고 그는 생각하고 있었다.\n\n“들어 보렴, 마거릿.” 그가 한 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감싸며 말했다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡아 꼭 쥐었지만 고개를 들 수는 없었다. 속이 어찌나 요동치는지 그가 읽는 것에 마음을 기울일 수도 없었다.\n\n“이것은 나처럼 시골 교구의 성직자였던 사람의 독백이다. 160여 년 전에 더비셔 카싱턴의 목사였던 올드필드 씨가 쓴 것이지. 그분의 시련은 끝났다. 그분은 선한 싸움을 싸우셨어.” 이 마지막 두 문장은 혼잣말하듯 나직이 말했다. 그러고는 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n“하나님께 욕을 돌리지 않고, 신앙을 더럽히지 않고, 네 성실을 저버리지 않고, 양심을 다치게 하지 않고, 네 평안을 망치지 않고, 네 구원을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 네 일을 더 이어 갈 수 없을 때, 한마디로 네가 그 직분을 계속하려면 지켜야 할 조건이 죄스럽고 하나님의 말씀에 어긋날 때, 너는 하나님께서 바로 그 침묵과 정직(停職)과 박탈과 물러남을 당신의 영광과 복음의 유익을 위해 돌리시리라 믿어도 좋으며, 아니 반드시 믿어야 하리라. 하나님께서 너를 한 가지 방식으로 쓰지 않으신다면 다른 방식으로 쓰시리라. 그분을 섬기고 높이기를 바라는 영혼은 결코 그럴 기회가 없지 않으리라. 또한 너는 이스라엘의 거룩하신 이를 좁혀 놓아, 그분께서 너를 통해 스스로를 영화롭게 하실 길이 오직 하나뿐이라고 여겨서는 안 되리라. 그분은 네 설교로써 하시듯 네 침묵으로써도 하실 수 있고, 네가 일을 이어 감으로써 하시듯 네가 물러남으로써도 하실 수 있느니라. 하나님께 가장 큰 봉사를 드린다거나 가장 무거운 의무를 다한다는 구실로는 아무리 작은 죄라도 변명되지 않으리니, 설령 그 죄가 그 의무를 행할 힘이나 기회를 우리에게 주었다 하여도 그러하니라. 오 내 영혼아, 네가 하나님의 예배를 더럽히고 네 서원을 저버렸다는 책망을 들을 때, 성직에 머무르기 위해 그리할 수밖에 없었노라 둘러댄다면 너에게 돌아올 감사는 거의 없으리라.”\n\n이 대목을 읽고, 또 읽지 않은 훨씬 많은 대목에 눈길을 주는 사이 그는 스스로 결심이 굳어져, 자기도 옳다고 믿는 바를 행하는 데 용감하고 굳셀 수 있을 것 같은 기분이 들었다. 그러나 읽기를 마치자 마거릿의 나직하고 경련하는 듯한 흐느낌이 들려왔고, 그 사무치는 고통 앞에서 그의 용기는 도로 무너져 내렸다.\n\n“마거릿, 얘야!” 그가 딸을 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. “초기의 순교자들을 생각해 보렴. 고난을 겪은 수천의 사람들을 생각해 보아라.”\n\n“하지만 아버지.” 그녀가 상기되고 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 갑자기 들며 말했다. “초기의 순교자들은 진리를 위해 고난을 받았어요. 그런데 아버지는……. 아, 아버지, 아버지!”\n\n“나는 양심을 위해 고난을 받는 것이란다, 얘야.” 그가 말했다. 그 목소리에는 위엄이 있었으나 예민한 성품 탓에 떨림이 섞여 있었다. “나는 내 양심이 시키는 대로 해야 한다. 나만큼 무디고 비겁하지 않은 마음이었다면 진작 떨쳐 일어났을 그런 자책을 나는 오래도 견뎌 왔구나.” 그는 말을 이으며 고개를 내저었다. “네 가엾은 어머니가 애타게 바라던 것이, 지나치게 애타는 소망이 흔히 그러하듯 비웃는 방식으로 마침내 이루어졌다. 소돔의 사과 같은 것이지. 그 일이 이 고비를 불러왔고, 나는 그것을 감사해야 마땅하며 또 감사하고 있다고 믿는다. 주교님이 내게 다른 성직록을 내려 주신 것이 한 달도 되지 않았다. 내가 그것을 받았다면 취임할 때 기도서에 대한 순종을 새로 서약해야 했을 게다. 마거릿, 나는 그렇게 해 보려고 애썼다. 그저 그 승진만 사양하고 여기 조용히 머무는 것으로 스스로를 다독여 보려고 했지. 전에 내 양심을 억눌러 왔듯이 이번에도 목을 졸라 가면서 말이다. 하나님, 나를 용서하소서!”\n\n그는 일어나 방 안을 오가며 자책과 부끄러움의 말을 나직이 되뇌었는데, 마거릿은 그 가운데 몇 마디만 들려온 것을 다행으로 여겼다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“마거릿, 다시 그 오래고 슬픈 짐으로 돌아가자꾸나. 우리는 헬스톤을 떠나야 한다.”\n\n“네! 알겠어요. 그런데 언제요?”\n\n“주교님께 편지를 썼다. 아마 네게 말한 것 같기도 한데, 요즘은 자꾸 잊어버리는구나.” 헤일 씨가 이렇게 말했다. 딱딱한 사실을 하나하나 이야기하려니 이내 그 풀 죽은 태도로 주저앉아 버렸다. “이 교구 목사직을 그만두겠다는 뜻을 알려 드린 게지. 주교님은 더할 나위 없이 인자하셨다. 이런저런 논리로 타이르고 말리셨지만 다 헛일이었다. 헛일이었어. 그건 내가 이미 나 자신에게 써 보았다가 아무 소용 없었던 것들뿐이니까. 이제 사임서를 들고 주교님을 직접 찾아뵙고 작별 인사를 드려야 한다. 그것도 시련이겠지. 그러나 그보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더한 것은 사랑하는 교구민들과 헤어지는 일이다. 예배를 맡을 부목사가 정해졌다. 브라운이라는 분이야. 내일 우리 집에 와서 머물게 될 게다. 다음 주일에 나는 고별 설교를 한다.”\n\n이렇게 갑작스러운 일이었단 말인가, 하고 마거릿은 생각했다. 그러나 어쩌면 그편이 나을지도 몰랐다. 질질 끄는 것은 아픔에 가시만 더할 뿐이었다. 자기에게 알려지기도 전에 거의 다 끝나 있는 듯한 이 모든 채비를 한꺼번에 듣고 얼얼하게 마비되는 편이 나았다. “어머니는 뭐라고 하세요?” 그녀가 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 물었다.\n\n뜻밖에도 아버지는 대답하기 전에 다시 방 안을 서성이기 시작했다. 마침내 그가 걸음을 멈추고 대답했다.\n\n“마거릿, 나는 어차피 못난 겁쟁이란다. 남에게 아픔을 주는 것을 견디지 못해. 네 어머니의 결혼 생활이 그이가 바라던 대로가, 마땅히 기대할 권리가 있었던 대로가 되지 못했다는 것을 나는 너무나 잘 안다. 그러니 이 일은 그이에게 큰 타격일 텐데, 나는 도무지 말할 마음도, 힘도 나지 않았다. 그래도 이제는 알려야겠지.” 그가 딸을 안쓰럽게 바라보며 말했다. 마거릿은 어머니가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는데 일이 이토록 진척되었다는 생각에 거의 넋이 나갈 지경이었다.\n\n“네, 정말 알려 드려야 해요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “어쩌면 어머니도 그리…….. 아, 아니에요! 어머니도 놀라실 거예요, 놀라실 수밖에 없어요.” 남이라면 이 일을 어떻게 받아들일까 헤아려 보려는 사이 그 충격이 도로 자기에게 밀려온 것이었다. “우리 어디로 가나요?” 마침내 그녀가 물었다. 앞으로의 계획이, 아버지에게 계획이라는 것이 있기나 하다면, 새삼 궁금해졌던 것이다.\n\n“밀턴노던으로 간다.” 그가 무덤덤한 무관심으로 대답했다. 딸의 사랑이 자기에게 매달리게 하고 잠시나마 그 사랑으로 자기를 다독이려 애쓰게 했음에도, 그 아픔의 날카로움은 딸의 마음속에 여전히 처음처럼 생생하다는 것을 그는 알아차렸던 것이다.\n\n“밀턴노던이라니요! 다크셔에 있는 그 공업 도시요?”\n\n“그래.” 그가 여전히 풀 죽은 무심한 투로 말했다.\n\n“왜 하필 그곳인가요, 아버지?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“거기서라면 식구들 먹을 빵을 벌 수 있으니까. 거기에는 내가 아는 사람이 하나도 없고, 헬스톤을 아는 사람도, 그 이야기를 내게 꺼낼 사람도 없을 테니까.”\n\n“식구들 먹을 빵이라니요! 저는 아버지와 어머니께 있는 줄로…….” 그러다 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 아버지의 이마에 어둠이 모여드는 것을 보고 앞날의 살림에 대한 당연한 관심을 접은 것이다. 그러나 그는 특유의 빠르고 직관적인 공감으로 딸의 얼굴에서 거울에 비치듯 자기 자신의 침울한 우울이 되비친 것을 읽어 내고는 애써 그것을 털어 냈다.\n\n“다 이야기해 주마, 마거릿. 다만 네 어머니께 알리는 일만 좀 도와다오. 그것 말고는 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것 같은데, 그이가 괴로워할 생각만 하면 두려움에 속이 메스꺼워진다. 내가 다 이야기해 주면 네가 내일 그이에게 조심스레 알릴 수 있지 않겠니. 나는 하루 종일 나가 있을 참이다. 도브슨 농부와 브레이시 커먼의 가난한 사람들에게 작별 인사를 하러 말이야. 그 말을 꺼내는 것이 아주 싫으냐, 마거릿?”\n\n마거릿은 정말로 그것이 싫었고, 지금껏 살아오며 해야 했던 그 어떤 일보다 더 움츠러들었다. 그녀는 얼른 말이 나오지 않았다. 아버지가 말했다. “아주 싫은 게로구나, 마거릿?” 그러자 그녀는 스스로를 이겨 내고 얼굴에 밝고 굳센 빛을 띠며 말했다.\n\n“괴로운 일이지만 해야 할 일이잖아요. 제가 할 수 있는 한 잘해 볼게요. 아버지야말로 괴로운 일을 많이 하셔야 할 텐데요.”\n\n헤일 씨는 낙담한 듯 고개를 저었다. 그러고는 고마움의 표시로 딸의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 마거릿은 하마터면 다시 울음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다. 생각을 돌리려고 그녀가 말했다. “이제 말씀해 주세요, 아버지. 우리 계획이 어떻게 되는지요. 아버지와 어머니께는 성직록에서 나오는 수입 말고도 따로 돈이 좀 있으시죠? 쇼 이모께는 있다는 걸 알거든요.”\n\n“그래. 우리 몫으로 한 해에 120파운드쯤 되지 싶다. 그중 70파운드는 프레더릭이 외국에 나간 뒤로 늘 그 아이에게 갔다. 그 아이가 그걸 다 필요로 하는지는 모르겠구나.” 그가 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다. “스페인 군대에서 복무하니 봉급이 좀 있기는 할 텐데.”\n\n“프레더릭 오빠가 아쉬워서는 안 돼요.” 마거릿이 단호히 말했다. “낯선 나라에서, 제 나라에서 그토록 부당한 대우를 받고 있는데요. 그러면 100파운드가 남네요. 아버지와 저와 어머니가 잉글랜드 어딘가 아주 값싸고 아주 조용한 곳에서 한 해 100파운드로 살 수는 없을까요? 아, 저는 할 수 있을 것 같은데요.”\n\n“안 된다!” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “그건 안 될 말이야. 나는 무언가를 해야 한다! 병든 생각을 물리치려면 나를 바쁘게 만들어야 해. 게다가 시골 교구에 있으면 헬스톤과 여기서의 내 소임이 사무치게 떠오를 거다. 나는 그걸 견딜 수 없어, 마거릿. 그리고 한 해 100파운드로는 살림에 꼭 드는 것을 대고 나면, 네 어머니가 여태 익숙해져 있고 또 마땅히 누려야 할 온갖 편안함을 마련해 드리기에 턱없이 모자란다. 아니야, 우리는 밀턴으로 가야 한다. 그건 정해진 일이다. 나는 사랑하는 이들에게 휘둘리지 않고 혼자 있을 때 늘 더 잘 결정하거든.” 식구 누구에게도 뜻을 밝히기 전에 이토록 많은 것을 정해 버린 데 대한 반쯤의 변명으로 그가 말했다. “나는 반대를 견디지 못한다. 반대를 들으면 마음이 도무지 정해지지 않아.”\n\n마거릿은 잠자코 있기로 마음먹었다. 어차피 그 한 가지 무서운 변화에 견주면 어디로 가느냐가 무슨 대수이겠는가?\n\n헤일 씨가 말을 이었다. “몇 달 전에, 회의로 인한 괴로움이 말하지 않고는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 커졌을 때 나는 벨 씨에게 편지를 썼다. 벨 씨 기억하지, 마거릿?”\n\n“아니요, 뵌 적은 없는 것 같아요. 하지만 어떤 분인지는 알아요. 프레더릭 오빠의 대부시고, 옥스퍼드에서 아버지를 가르치신 옛 스승 아니신가요?”\n\n“그래. 거기 플리머스 칼리지의 펠로시다. 밀턴노던 태생이라고 하더구나. 어쨌든 거기에 재산이 있는데, 밀턴이 그렇게 큰 공업 도시가 된 뒤로 값이 아주 많이 올랐다지. 그런데 말이다, 나는 짐작 가는 데가 있었어. 아니, 그건 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 낫겠구나. 아무튼 벨 씨라면 나를 헤아려 주리라고 믿었다. 그분이 내게 큰 힘을 주었는지는 모르겠구나. 평생을 그 대학에서 편안히 사신 분이니까. 그래도 더할 수 없이 다정하셨다. 우리가 밀턴으로 가는 것도 그분 덕이야.”\n\n“어째서요?” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“그분이 거기 소작인과 집과 공장을 가지고 계시거든. 그래서 그곳을 싫어하시면서도—그분 성미에는 너무 부산스러운 곳이지—어떻게든 연을 이어 갈 수밖에 없으신 게다. 그런데 그분이 그러시더구나, 거기 가정교사 자리가 하나 괜찮게 났다는 이야기를 들었다고.”\n\n“가정교사라니요!” 마거릿이 경멸 어린 얼굴로 말했다. “공장주들이 도대체 고전이나 문학이나 신사의 소양 같은 게 무슨 소용이 있다고요?”\n\n“아니다.” 아버지가 말했다. “그 사람들 중에는 정말로 훌륭한 이들이 있는 모양이더구나. 제 부족함을 아는 사람들 말이다. 옥스퍼드의 숱한 사람들보다 나은 셈이지. 어떤 이들은 이미 어른이 되었는데도 굳게 배우려 든다더라. 또 어떤 이들은 제 자식만은 자기보다 잘 가르치고 싶어 하고. 어쨌든 내가 말했듯이 가정교사 자리가 하나 있다. 벨 씨가 나를 손턴이라는 분에게 소개해 주셨는데, 그분의 소작인이고, 편지로 미루어 판단하기로는 아주 총명한 사람이다. 그리고 밀턴에서라면, 마거릿, 나는 행복하지는 않더라도 바쁜 삶을 얻을 게다. 사람도 풍경도 워낙 달라서 헬스톤이 떠오를 일은 결코 없을 테고.”\n\n바로 거기에 숨은 동기가 있었다. 마거릿은 자기 마음으로 그것을 알아차렸다. 그곳은 다를 것이다. 잉글랜드 북부에 대해, 공장주들에 대해, 그곳 사람들에 대해, 거칠고 황량한 고장에 대해 들어 온 모든 것을 거의 혐오하다시피 하는 그녀에게 그것이 아무리 거슬리는 일이라 해도, 거기에는 이 한 가지 이점이 있었다. 그곳은 헬스톤과 다를 것이고, 그 사랑하는 곳을 결코 떠올리게 하지 않으리라는 것.\n\n“우리 언제 가나요?” 잠시 말이 없다가 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“정확히는 모르겠다. 너와 의논하고 싶었어. 알다시피 네 어머니는 아직 아무것도 모르지 않니. 하지만 2주쯤 뒤가 아닐까 싶다. 사임서를 내고 나면 나는 여기 머무를 권리가 없어지니까.”\n\n마거릿은 넋이 나갈 지경이었다.\n\n“2주 뒤라니요!”\n\n“아니, 아니다. 날을 딱 잡은 건 아니야. 정해진 것은 없다.” 아버지가 근심스레 머뭇거리며 말했다. 딸의 눈에 어리는 뿌연 슬픔과 갑자기 달라진 안색을 알아차린 것이었다. 그러나 그녀는 곧바로 마음을 추슬렀다.\n\n“아니에요, 아버지. 말씀하신 대로 어서 확실히 정하는 편이 좋아요. 다만 어머니만 아무것도 모르신다니! 그게 정말 난감한 일이에요.”\n\n“가엾은 마리아!” 헤일 씨가 애틋하게 대답했다. “가엾고 가엾은 마리아! 아, 내가 혼인하지 않았더라면, 이 세상에 나 하나뿐이었다면 얼마나 쉬웠을까! 이러하니……. 마거릿, 나는 도저히 그이에게 말하지 못하겠구나!”\n\n“괜찮아요.” 마거릿이 서글프게 말했다. “제가 할게요. 언제가 좋을지 고를 수 있게 내일 저녁까지만 시간을 주세요. 아, 아버지.” 그녀가 문득 북받쳐 애원하듯 외쳤다. “말씀해 주세요, 이건 악몽이라고, 무서운 꿈일 뿐 깨어 있는 현실이 아니라고 말씀해 주세요! 정말로 국교회를 떠나시겠다는 뜻은 아니겠죠. 헬스톤을 버리고, 저와 어머니에게서 영영 갈라서겠다는 뜻은요. 어떤 망상에, 어떤 유혹에 끌려서 말이에요! 정말로 그러시겠다는 뜻은 아니죠!”\n\n마거릿이 말하는 동안 헤일 씨는 굳은 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다.\n\n이윽고 그는 딸의 얼굴을 똑바로 보며 느리고 쉬고 또박또박한 투로 말했다. “정말로 그러겠다는 뜻이다, 마거릿. 내 말이, 내 굳은 뜻과 결심이 참인지를 의심하며 스스로를 속여서는 안 된다.” 말을 마치고도 그는 한동안 그 흔들림 없는 돌 같은 눈길로 딸을 바라보았다. 그녀 역시 이것이 되돌릴 수 없는 일임을 믿기 전에 애원하는 눈으로 마주 바라보았다. 그러고는 자리에서 일어나 한마디 말도, 한 번의 눈길도 남기지 않고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 손잡이에 손가락이 닿았을 때 그가 그녀를 불러 세웠다. 그는 벽난로 곁에 오그라들어 구부정하게 서 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 다가오자 몸을 곧게 펴 온전한 키로 일어서서는 그녀의 머리에 두 손을 얹고 엄숙히 말했다.\n\n“하나님의 복이 네게 임하기를, 내 아이야!”\n\n“그리고 그분께서 아버지를 그분의 교회로 돌려보내 주시기를.” 그녀가 가슴 가득한 마음으로 대답했다. 다음 순간 그녀는 축복에 대한 이 대꾸가 불경스럽고 그릇된 것은 아닐까, 딸의 입에서 나온 말이라 아버지를 아프게 하지는 않을까 겁이 나서 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았다. 그는 한동안 딸을 품에 안고 있었다. 그가 혼잣말로 중얼거리는 소리가 그녀에게 들렸다. “순교자들과 증거자들은 이보다 더한 아픔도 견뎠다. 나는 물러서지 않으리라.”\n\n헤일 부인이 딸을 찾는 소리에 두 사람은 흠칫 놀랐다. 앞에 놓인 모든 일을 뼈저리게 깨달으며 그들은 서로에게서 떨어졌다. 헤일 씨가 서둘러 말했다. “가 보아라, 마거릿, 어서. 나는 내일 하루 종일 나가 있을 게다. 밤이 되기 전에 네가 어머니께 말씀드리게 될 게야.”\n\n“네.” 그녀가 대답하고는 얼얼하고 어질어질한 상태로 응접실로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제5장\n\n“사려 깊은 사랑을 당신께 구하나이다. 늘 지켜보는 데서 나오는 지혜로, 기쁜 이는 즐거운 미소로 맞이하고 우는 이의 눈물은 닦아 주게 하시고, 스스로에게서 벗어나 한가로운 마음으로 위로하고 함께 아파하게 하소서.” — 작자 미상.\n\n마거릿은 가장 가난한 교구민들의 처지에 자그마한 안락을 보태려는 어머니의 온갖 소소한 계획을 잘 들어 주었다. 새 계획 하나하나가 가슴을 찌르는데도 그녀는 듣지 않을 수 없었다. 서리가 내릴 무렵이면 그들은 헬스톤에서 멀리 떠나 있을 터였다. 늙은 사이먼의 류머티즘은 더 심해지고 눈은 더 어두워질지도 모른다. 그런데 그를 찾아가 책을 읽어 주고 자그마한 죽 사발과 좋은 붉은 플란넬로 위로해 줄 사람은 아무도 없을 것이다. 혹 있다 해도 낯선 사람일 테고, 노인은 헛되이 그녀를 기다릴 것이다. 메리 돔빌의 다리 저는 어린 아들은 헛되이 문가로 기어 나와 숲을 지나 오는 그녀를 찾을 것이다. 이 가난한 벗들은 그녀가 왜 자기들을 저버렸는지 결코 이해하지 못하리라. 그런 이들이 그 밖에도 많았다. “아버지는 늘 성직록에서 나오는 수입을 교구에 쓰셨단다. 어쩌면 내가 다음 몫까지 당겨 쓰는 것인지도 모르지만, 올겨울은 모질 듯하니 우리 가난한 사람들은 도와야 하지 않겠니.”\n\n“아, 어머니, 할 수 있는 건 다 해요.” 마거릿이 간절히 말했다. 신중함이라는 쪽은 보지 못한 채, 그저 이런 도움을 베푸는 것이 마지막일지도 모른다는 생각만 붙들고 있었던 것이다. “우리가 여기 오래 있지 못할지도 모르잖아요.”\n\n“어디 아프니, 얘야?” 헤일 부인이 걱정스레 물었다. 헬스톤에 머무는 것이 불확실하다는 마거릿의 암시를 잘못 알아들은 것이었다. “창백하고 피곤해 보이는구나. 이 부드럽고 축축한, 몸에 안 좋은 공기 탓이야.”\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요, 어머니. 그게 아니에요. 공기는 참 좋은걸요. 할리 가의 그 매캐한 공기를 겪고 나니 더없이 신선하고 맑은 향기가 나요. 그래도 피곤하긴 해요. 잘 시간이 다 됐나 봐요.”\n\n“머지않았구나. 9시 반이다. 얘야, 당장 자리에 눕는 게 좋겠다. 딕슨한테 죽을 좀 달라고 하렴. 네가 눕는 대로 내가 가 보마. 감기가 든 게 아닐까 싶구나. 아니면 고인 못에서 나오는 나쁜 공기가……”\n\n“아, 어머니.” 마거릿이 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 희미하게 웃었다. “저는 아주 멀쩡해요. 제 걱정은 마세요. 그저 피곤할 뿐이에요.”\n\n마거릿은 위층으로 올라갔다. 어머니의 걱정을 달래려고 죽 한 사발을 순순히 받아 먹었다. 헤일 부인이 마지막으로 몇 가지를 묻고 입을 맞춘 뒤 자기 방으로 물러가려고 올라왔을 때, 그녀는 나른하게 침대에 누워 있었다. 그러나 어머니의 방문이 잠기는 소리를 듣는 순간 그녀는 침대에서 뛰쳐나와 실내복을 걸치고 방 안을 오락가락 거닐기 시작했다. 그러다 마룻널 하나가 삐걱대는 소리에 소리를 내서는 안 된다는 것을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 깊숙이 들어앉은 자그마한 창의 창가 자리로 가 몸을 웅크렸다. 그날 아침 밖을 내다보았을 때는 교회 탑에 비치는 밝고 맑은 빛에 가슴이 뛰었다. 화창하고 볕 좋은 날을 알리는 빛이었다. 그런데 이 저녁, 길어야 16시간이 지났을 뿐인데 그녀는 울기에도 너무 벅찬 슬픔을 안고 앉아 있었다. 가슴에서 젊음과 생기를 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않게 짜내 버린 듯한 차갑고 둔한 아픔을 안고서. 헨리 레녹스 씨의 방문과 그의 청혼은 꿈만 같았다. 실제 자기 삶의 곁을 스쳐 간 무엇 같았다. 모진 현실은 아버지가 마음에 유혹하는 의심을 들여놓은 나머지 분리파가 되고 버림받은 자가 되었다는 것이었다. 거기서 비롯된 온갖 변화가 그 하나의 크고 모든 것을 시들게 하는 사실을 둘러싸고 무리 지어 있었다.\n\n그녀는 시야 한복판에 네모지고 곧게 선 짙은 잿빛 교회 탑들의 윤곽을 내다보았다. 그 너머 깊고 푸른 투명한 심연을 배경으로 도려낸 듯한 그 선들을. 그녀는 그 심연을 들여다보며 영원히 들여다볼 수도 있겠다고 느꼈다. 순간순간 더 먼 거리가 드러나는데도 하느님의 흔적이라고는 보이지 않는 그곳을. 그 순간 그녀에게는 세상이 쇠로 된 둥근 지붕에 둘러싸여 있을 때보다 더 철저히 황량한 것처럼 느껴졌다. 그런 지붕이라면 그 뒤에 전능하신 분의 지워지지 않는 평화와 영광이 있을 수도 있으련만. 그 끝없는 공간의 심연은 고요한 평온 속에서 어떤 물질의 경계보다도 더 그녀를 비웃는 듯했다. 물질의 경계라면 땅 위에서 고통받는 이들의 부르짖음을 가두어 두기라도 하련만, 이제 그 부르짖음은 저 무한한 광대함의 광휘 속으로 올라가 사라져 버릴지도 몰랐다. 그분의 보좌에 이르기도 전에 영영 사라져 버릴지도. 이런 심경에 잠겨 있을 때 아버지가 기척 없이 들어왔다. 달빛이 밝아 딸이 여느 때와 다른 자리에 여느 때와 다른 자세로 있는 것이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 그가 다가와 어깨에 손을 얹고 나서야 그녀는 그가 거기 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n“마거릿, 네가 일어나 있다는 말을 들었다. 함께 기도하자고 청하러 오지 않을 수가 없었구나. 주기도문을 외자꾸나. 우리 둘 다에게 좋을 게다.”\n\n헤일 씨와 마거릿은 창가 자리 옆에 무릎을 꿇었다. 그는 위를 우러렀고, 그녀는 부끄러움에 고개를 숙였다. 하느님은 거기, 그들 바로 곁에 계셔서 아버지가 속삭이는 말을 듣고 계셨다. 아버지는 이단자일지도 모른다. 그러나 채 5분도 되기 전에 절망에 찬 의심에 잠겨 스스로 훨씬 더 철저한 회의론자임을 드러낸 것은 그녀 자신이 아니었던가? 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 아버지가 나간 뒤 잘못을 저지르고 부끄러워하는 아이처럼 살며시 잠자리로 들었다. 세상이 알 수 없는 문제로 가득하다 해도 그녀는 믿고 맡기며, 그저 지금 이 시간에 필요한 한 걸음만 보여 달라고 구하리라. 레녹스 씨, 그의 방문, 그의 청혼은 그날 뒤이어 벌어진 일들에 무참히 밀려나 있었는데, 그 기억이 그날 밤 그녀의 꿈을 어지럽혔다. 그는 그녀의 보닛이 걸린 가지에 닿으려고 터무니없이 높은 나무를 기어오르고 있었다. 그가 떨어지고 있었고 그녀는 그를 구하려 몸부림쳤으나 보이지 않는 억센 손에 붙들려 있었다. 그는 죽어 있었다. 그런데 장면이 바뀌자 그녀는 다시 할리 가의 응접실에서 예전처럼 그와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그러면서도 내내 그가 그 끔찍한 추락으로 죽는 것을 보았다는 의식을 지니고 있었다.\n\n비참하고 잠 못 이룬 밤이었다! 다가올 날을 맞기에는 형편없는 준비였다! 그녀는 개운치 않은 몸으로 화들짝 잠에서 깼고, 열에 들뜬 꿈보다 더 나쁜 어떤 현실을 의식했다. 모든 것이 되살아났다. 슬픔만이 아니라 그 슬픔 속의 끔찍한 불협화음까지. 그녀에게는 악한 자의 유혹인 그 의심에 이끌려 아버지는 어디로, 얼마나 멀리 헤매어 간 것일까? 그녀는 묻고 싶었으나, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 대답만은 듣고 싶지 않았다.\n\n맑고 서늘한 아침 덕에 어머니는 아침 식사 자리에서 유난히 몸도 마음도 좋았다. 어머니는 남편의 침묵과 마거릿의 짤막한 대답에도 아랑곳없이 마을에 베풀 선행을 궁리하며 이야기를 이어 갔다. 상을 치우기도 전에 헤일 씨가 일어섰다. 그는 몸을 가누려는 듯 한 손을 식탁에 짚었다.\n\n“저녁까지는 못 돌아올 거요. 브레이시 커먼에 갈 참인데, 돕슨 농부한테 요기할 것을 좀 달라고 하지요. 7시 차 시간에는 돌아오리다.”\n\n그는 두 사람 중 누구도 쳐다보지 않았으나 마거릿은 그 뜻을 알았다. 7시까지 어머니에게 알려야 한다는 것이었다. 헤일 씨라면 그 일을 미루었겠지만 마거릿은 다른 사람이었다. 그녀는 하루 종일 마음을 짓누르는 무게를 견딜 수 없었다. 최악을 먼저 넘기는 편이 나았다. 어머니를 위로하기에는 하루도 짧을 터였다. 그러나 그녀가 창가에 서서 어떻게 말을 꺼낼지 궁리하며 하녀가 방에서 나가기를 기다리는 사이, 어머니는 학교에 가려고 채비를 갖추러 위층으로 올라가 버렸다. 어머니는 차림을 다 갖추고 여느 때보다 활기찬 기분으로 내려왔다.\n\n“어머니, 오늘 아침에 저와 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아요. 딱 한 바퀴만요.” 마거릿이 헤일 부인의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 열린 창을 지나 밖으로 나섰다. 헤일 부인이 무슨 말인가를 했지만 마거릿은 그것이 무슨 말인지 알 수 없었다. 그녀의 눈은 종처럼 깊은 꽃 속으로 들어가는 벌 한 마리에 붙들려 있었다. 저 벌이 전리품을 물고 날아 나오면 말을 꺼내리라. 그것을 신호로 삼으리라. 벌이 나왔다.\n\n“어머니! 아버지가 헬스톤을 떠나실 거예요!” 그녀가 불쑥 내뱉었다. “국교회를 떠나 밀턴노던에서 사실 거예요.” 그렇게 세 가지 모진 사실이 겨우 입 밖에 나왔다.\n\n“어째서 그런 말을 하니?” 헤일 부인이 놀라 믿기지 않는다는 목소리로 물었다. “누가 너한테 그런 터무니없는 소리를 하더냐?”\n\n“아버지가 직접 하셨어요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 무언가 부드럽고 위로가 될 말을 하고 싶었지만 정말이지 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 두 사람은 정원 의자 가까이에 있었다. 헤일 부인이 앉더니 울기 시작했다.\n\n“무슨 말인지 모르겠구나.” 어머니가 말했다. “네가 무언가 크게 잘못 안 것이든지, 아니면 내가 네 말을 제대로 못 알아들은 것이든지.”\n\n“아니에요, 어머니. 저는 잘못 안 게 아니에요. 아버지는 주교님께 편지를 쓰셨어요. 의심이 너무 커서 양심상 잉글랜드 국교회의 사제로 남을 수 없다고, 그래서 헬스톤을 내놓아야 한다고요. 벨 씨와도 의논하셨어요. 프레더릭 오빠의 대부님 말이에요, 어머니. 그래서 우리가 밀턴노던에 가서 살기로 정해졌어요.” 이 말을 하는 내내 헤일 부인은 마거릿의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그 얼굴에 드리운 그늘이, 적어도 딸만은 자기가 하는 말이 사실이라고 믿고 있음을 말해 주었다.\n\n“사실일 리 없다.” 헤일 부인이 마침내 말했다. “이 지경이 되기 전에 분명 내게 말했을 텐데.”\n\n어머니에게 알렸어야 했다는 생각이 마거릿의 마음을 세게 쳤다. 어머니에게 불평과 한탄이라는 흠이 있었다 한들, 자기 생각이 바뀐 것과 곧 닥칠 삶의 변화를 더 잘 아는 딸에게서 듣게 내버려 둔 것은 아버지의 잘못이었다. 마거릿은 어머니 곁에 앉아 저항 없이 기대 오는 머리를 가슴에 안고, 자신의 보드라운 뺨을 숙여 어루만지듯 어머니의 얼굴에 댔다.\n\n“사랑하는 어머니! 저희는 어머니께 아픔을 드릴까 봐 너무 무서웠어요. 아버지도 몹시 괴로워하셨고요. 어머니가 튼튼하지 못하신 걸 아시잖아요. 게다가 견뎌야 할 그 끔찍한 조바심도 있었고요.”\n\n“언제 네게 말하셨니, 마거릿?”\n\n“어제요, 바로 어제요.” 마거릿이 대답하며 그 물음을 부추긴 질투를 알아차렸다. “가엾은 아버지!” 어머니의 생각을 아버지가 겪어 온 모든 일에 대한 가엾은 마음으로 돌리려는 것이었다. 헤일 부인이 고개를 들었다.\n\n“의심이 든다는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?” 어머니가 물었다. “설마 네 아버지가 국교회와 다르게 생각한다는, 국교회보다 더 잘 안다는 뜻은 아니겠지.”\n\n마거릿은 고개를 저었고, 어머니가 자기 회한의 벌거벗은 신경을 건드리자 눈에 눈물이 고였다.\n\n“주교님이 바로잡아 주실 수는 없니?” 헤일 부인이 반쯤 조바심을 내며 물었다.\n\n“안 될 것 같아요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “하지만 저는 여쭙지 않았어요. 아버지가 어떤 대답을 하실지 차마 들을 수가 없었거든요. 어쨌든 다 정해진 일이에요. 아버지는 2주 뒤에 헬스톤을 떠나실 거예요. 사임서를 이미 내셨다고 하신 것도 같고요.”\n\n“2주 뒤라니!” 헤일 부인이 외쳤다. “정말이지 이건 아주 이상하구나. 조금도 온당하지 않아. 나는 이게 아주 매정하다고 본다.” 어머니는 눈물에서 위안을 찾기 시작했다. “의심이 든다면서 성직록을 내놓다니, 그것도 다 나와 한마디 의논 없이. 처음에 내게 그 의심을 말했더라면 내가 싹부터 잘라 낼 수도 있었을 텐데.”\n\n아버지의 처사가 잘못되었다고 느끼면서도 마거릿은 어머니가 그것을 나무라는 말을 차마 들을 수 없었다. 아버지의 그 말없음이야말로 어머니를 아끼는 마음에서 비롯되었음을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그것이 비겁했을지는 몰라도 매정하지는 않았다.\n\n“어머니는 헬스톤을 떠나는 걸 반가워하실지도 모른다고 저는 은근히 바랐어요.” 잠시 뒤 그녀가 말했다. “어머니는 이곳 공기에서는 한 번도 건강하신 적이 없었잖아요.”\n\n“밀턴노던처럼 온통 굴뚝과 먼지뿐인 공장 도시의 매캐한 공기가 이곳 공기보다 나으리라고는 생각할 수 없구나. 여기 공기는 너무 눅눅하고 사람을 늘어지게 하긴 해도 맑고 달콤하지 않니. 공장과 공장 사람들 한복판에서 사는 걸 생각해 보렴! 하기야 네 아버지가 국교회를 떠나면 우리는 어디서도 사교계에 받아들여지지 못하겠지. 우리에게 얼마나 큰 수치냐! 가엾은 존 경! 네 아버지가 이 지경이 된 것을 보지 않고 돌아가신 게 다행이다. 내가 처녀 적에 베러스퍼드 저택에서 네 쇼 이모와 살 때, 존 경은 저녁 식사 뒤면 날마다 첫 축배로 이렇게 외치곤 하셨어. ‘국교회와 국왕을 위하여, 잔당 의회는 물러가라.’”\n\n마거릿은 어머니의 생각이 남편이 자기에게 침묵했다는 사실에서 벗어난 것이 다행이었다. 그 문제는 남편의 마음에 무척이나 가까웠을 것이다. 아버지의 의심이 어떤 성질의 것인가 하는 심각하고도 절실한 근심 다음으로, 마거릿에게 가장 큰 아픔을 준 것이 바로 이 사정이었다.\n\n“어머니, 아시다시피 우리는 여기서도 사교랄 게 거의 없잖아요. 가장 가까운 이웃인 고먼 집안도, 그걸 사교라고 부를 수 있다면 말이지만, 게다가 우리는 그분들을 거의 뵙지도 못하지만, 그 밀턴노던 사람들 못지않게 장사를 해 온 분들이고요.”\n\n“그렇지.” 헤일 부인이 거의 분개하듯 말했다. “하지만 어쨌든 고먼 집안은 이 주의 신사들 절반에게 마차를 만들어 주었고, 그러면서 그분들과 어느 정도 교류도 하지 않았니. 그런데 그 공장 사람들이라니, 대체 아마포를 살 형편이 되는데 무명을 걸치는 사람이 어디 있단 말이냐?”\n\n“그럼요, 어머니. 면방적업자는 제가 포기할게요. 저도 그 사람들 편을 드는 게 아니에요. 다른 장사치들 편을 들지 않는 것과 마찬가지로요. 다만 우리가 그 사람들과 엮일 일은 거의 없을 거예요.”\n\n“대체 네 아버지는 왜 하필 밀턴노던을 살 곳으로 정하셨다니?”\n\n“한편으로는 그곳이 헬스톤과 아주 다르기 때문이에요.” 마거릿이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “한편으로는 벨 씨가 그곳에 개인 교습 자리가 있다고 하셔서고요.”\n\n“밀턴에서 개인 교사라니! 옥스퍼드로 가서 신사들을 가르치면 안 되는 거니?”\n\n“잊으셨어요, 어머니! 아버지는 당신 생각 때문에 국교회를 떠나시는 거예요. 그 의심으로는 옥스퍼드에서 아무것도 못 하세요.”\n\n헤일 부인은 한동안 말없이 조용히 울었다. 마침내 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“그런데 세간은…… 대체 이사를 어떻게 감당한단 말이냐? 나는 평생 이사라는 걸 해 본 적이 없는데, 궁리할 시간이라고는 2주뿐이라니!”\n\n마거릿은 어머니의 근심과 괴로움이 이 정도로 낮아진 것에 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓였다. 자신에게는 하찮은 문제였고, 자기가 크게 도울 수 있는 문제이기도 했다. 그녀는 이런저런 계획을 세우고 약속했으며, 헤일 씨가 무엇을 하려는지 조금 더 분명히 알기 전에 정할 수 있는 것은 다 정하도록 어머니를 이끌었다. 그날 하루 종일 마거릿은 어머니 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 어머니의 감정이 이리저리 옮아 갈 때마다 온 마음을 기울여 함께 느껴 주었다. 특히 저녁이 가까워지면서 그녀는 아버지가 지치고 괴로운 하루를 보내고 돌아왔을 때 마음을 달래 주는 맞이가 집에서 기다리기를 점점 더 간절히 바랐다. 그녀는 아버지가 오랫동안 남몰래 견뎌야 했을 것들을 이야기했다. 어머니는 그렇다면 자기에게 말했어야 했다고, 그랬으면 적어도 조언해 줄 사람은 있었을 것 아니냐고 차갑게 대꾸할 뿐이었다. 그러다 현관에서 아버지의 발소리가 들리자 마거릿은 가슴이 아득해졌다. 그녀는 어머니의 질투 어린 언짢음이 두려워 감히 아버지를 맞으러 나가 하루 종일 무엇을 했는지 말할 수 없었다. 아버지가 자기를 기다리는 듯, 혹은 무슨 기척이라도 기다리는 듯 서성이는 소리가 들렸으나 그녀는 감히 움직이지 못했다. 어머니의 씰룩이는 입술과 변하는 낯빛으로 보아 어머니 역시 남편이 돌아온 것을 알고 있었다. 이윽고 그가 방문을 열고 들어올까 말까 망설이며 서 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 잿빛으로 창백했다. 눈에는 겁먹고 두려워하는 기색이 어려 있었다. 사내의 얼굴에서 보기에는 거의 딱할 지경이었다. 그러나 낙담한 그 막막함이, 그 심신의 무기력이 아내의 마음을 움직였다. 그녀는 남편에게 가서 그의 가슴에 몸을 던지며 울부짖었다.\n\n“아, 리처드, 리처드, 진작에 내게 말했어야지요!”\n\n그러자 마거릿은 눈물을 흘리며 어머니 곁을 떠났다. 위층으로 달려 올라가 침대에 몸을 던지고 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 하루 종일 굳게 자신을 다잡은 끝에 끝내 터져 나오고야 마는 발작 같은 흐느낌을 눌렀다.\n\n그렇게 얼마나 누워 있었는지 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 하녀가 방을 치우러 들어왔는데도 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다. 놀란 하녀는 발끝으로 다시 빠져나가 딕슨에게 헤일 아가씨가 가슴이 미어지도록 울고 있다고, 저러다가는 큰 병이 나고 말 것이 틀림없다고 일렀다. 그 바람에 마거릿은 누군가 자기를 건드리는 것을 느끼고 화들짝 일어나 앉았다. 낯익은 방이 눈에 들어왔고, 그림자 진 딕슨의 모습이 보였다. 딕슨은 부어오르고 앞이 안 보일 만큼 상한 헤일 아가씨의 눈에 빛이 해로울까 봐 촛불을 조금 뒤쪽에 들고 서 있었다.\n\n“어머, 딕슨! 방에 들어오는 소리를 못 들었어요.” 마거릿이 떨리는 몸을 다시 다잡으며 말했다. “많이 늦었나요?” 그녀는 나른하게 몸을 일으키면서도 발이 바닥에 닿기만 할 뿐 온전히 일어서지는 않은 채, 젖어 헝클어진 머리칼을 얼굴에서 걷어 내고 아무 일도 없었던 듯, 잠들어 있었던 듯 보이려 애쓰며 말을 이었다.\n\n“몇 시인지 저도 잘 모르겠구먼요.” 딕슨이 서운함이 묻은 목소리로 대답했다. “차 드실 때 옷을 갈아입혀 드리면서 마님한테서 그 끔찍한 소식을 들은 뒤로는 시간 가는 줄을 통 모르겠어요. 정말이지 우리 모두가 어찌 될지 모르겠습니다요. 아까 샬럿이 아가씨가 흐느껴 우신다고 하기에 저는 그럴 만도 하다, 딱하기도 하시지, 했지요. 주인 어른께서 그 연세에 비국교도가 되실 생각을 하시다니요. 국교회에서 잘되셨다고까지는 못 해도 그리 못되지도 않으셨는데 말입니다요. 저한테 사촌이 하나 있었는데요, 아가씨, 50이 넘은 나이에 감리교 설교자가 되었답니다. 평생 재봉사였는데요. 하기야 그 일을 그렇게 오래 했으면서도 몸에 맞는 바지 한 벌을 못 지었으니 이상할 것도 없지요. 그렇지만 주인 어른께서야! 제가 마님께 이렇게 말씀드렸어요. ‘가엾은 존 경께서 뭐라고 하셨을까요? 그분은 마님이 헤일 씨와 혼인하시는 걸 도무지 좋아하지 않으셨는데, 일이 이 지경까지 오리라는 걸 아셨더라면 그보다 더한 욕이 있을 수 있다면 그런 욕까지 퍼부으셨을 겁니다요.’”\n\n딕슨은 헤일 씨의 처사를 두고 마님에게 이러쿵저러쿵 말하는 데 워낙 익숙해져 있었으므로(마님은 기분에 따라 들어 주기도 하고 흘려버리기도 했다), 마거릿의 번뜩이는 눈과 벌름거리는 콧구멍을 알아채지 못했다. 하인이 자기 면전에서 아버지를 그런 식으로 입에 올리다니!\n\n“딕슨.” 그녀가 몹시 격했을 때면 늘 쓰는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그 목소리에는 멀리서 이는 소란이나 저 멀리서 터지는 폭풍 같은 울림이 있었다. “딕슨! 지금 누구에게 말하고 있는지 잊었군요.” 이제 그녀는 몸을 곧추세우고 단단히 발을 딛고 서서 하녀를 마주 보며 흔들림 없이 꿰뚫는 눈으로 그녀를 붙들었다. “나는 헤일 씨의 딸이에요. 나가요! 딕슨은 이상한 잘못을 저질렀고, 곰곰이 생각해 보면 그 착한 심성으로 틀림없이 뉘우치게 될 잘못이에요.”\n\n딕슨은 1, 2분쯤 망설이며 방 안을 서성였다. 마거릿이 다시 말했다. “나가도 좋아요, 딕슨. 나가 주었으면 해요.” 딕슨은 이 단호한 말에 성을 내야 할지 울어야 할지 알 수 없었다. 마님이라면 어느 쪽이든 통했을 것이다. 그러나 딕슨은 속으로 이렇게 생각했다. ‘마거릿 아가씨한테는 어딘가 옛 어른의 기질이 있어. 가엾은 프레더릭 도련님처럼 말이야. 대체 그걸 누구한테서 물려받았을까?’ 그리하여 태도가 덜 도도하고 덜 단호한 사람에게서 그런 말을 들었더라면 발끈했을 그녀도 반쯤은 겸손하고 반쯤은 서운한 목소리로 이렇게 말할 만큼 누그러졌다.\n\n“가운을 벗겨 드리고 머리를 손봐 드리면 안 될까요, 아가씨?”\n\n“아니요! 오늘 밤은 됐어요, 고마워요.” 그러고 나서 마거릿은 진중하게 촛불로 앞을 비추어 그녀를 방 밖으로 내보내고는 문을 잠갔다. 그때부터 딕슨은 마거릿에게 복종했고 그녀를 우러렀다. 딕슨은 아가씨가 가엾은 프레더릭 도련님과 너무 닮아서라고 말했지만, 사실은 딕슨도 여느 많은 사람들처럼 힘 있고 단호한 성품에 다스려지는 느낌을 좋아했던 것이다.\n\n마거릿에게는 딕슨의 손이 하는 온갖 도움과 그 입이 지키는 침묵이 다 필요했다. 한동안 딕슨은 모욕당했다는 자기감정을 드러내는 것이 도리라고 여겨 아가씨에게 되도록 말을 아꼈으므로, 그 기운이 말 대신 일로 뻗어 나왔던 것이다. 2주는 이토록 중대한 이사를 준비하기에는 몹시 짧은 시간이었다. 딕슨의 말대로 “신사가 아닌 사람이라면 누구라도, 아니 웬만한 다른 신사라도……” 하다가 마거릿의 곧고 엄한 이맛살을 흘끗 보고는 남은 말을 기침 속에 흘려보냈고, “가슴이 좀 간질간질해서요, 아가씨” 하는 그 증세를 멎게 하라며 마거릿이 건네는 쓴박하 사탕을 얌전히 받아 먹었다. 그러나 헤일 씨가 아닌 웬만한 사람이라면, 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 밀턴노던이든 다른 어디든 헬스톤 목사관에서 어차피 빼내야 할 세간을 옮겨 넣을 집을 정하기가 어려우리라는 것쯤은 알아볼 현실 감각이 있었을 것이다.\n\n헤일 부인은 한꺼번에 밀려드는 것만 같은 온갖 근심과 당장 정해야 할 살림살이 문제에 짓눌려 정말로 몸져누웠다. 어머니가 아예 자리에 눕고 살림을 자기에게 맡겼을 때 마거릿은 오히려 마음이 놓이기까지 했다. 딕슨은 호위병이라는 제 자리에 충실하게 마님을 더없이 정성껏 보살폈고, 헤일 부인의 침실에서 나오는 것은 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 혼자 무어라 중얼거리기 위해서일 뿐이었는데 마거릿은 그 소리를 듣지 않기로 했다. 그녀 앞에 분명하고 곧게 놓인 한 가지는 헬스톤을 떠나야 한다는 것이었기 때문이다. 헤일 씨의 성직록을 이을 후임자가 정해졌다. 게다가 아버지가 결정을 내린 이상, 아버지를 위해서도 다른 온갖 사정을 보아서도 이제 더는 미적거려서는 안 되었다. 아버지는 교구민 한 사람 한 사람과 반드시 작별 인사를 나누겠다고 마음먹은 터라, 저녁마다 점점 더 낙심한 채 집에 돌아왔던 것이다. 처리해야 할 온갖 현실적인 일에 서툴기만 한 마거릿은 누구에게 조언을 구해야 할지 몰랐다. 요리사와 샬럿은 기꺼운 팔과 굳센 마음으로 짐을 옮기고 싸는 일을 해냈고, 그 일에 관한 한 마거릿의 훌륭한 분별력 덕에 무엇이 최선인지 알아보고 어떻게 할지 일러 줄 수 있었다. 그러나 어디로 간단 말인가? 1주일 뒤면 떠나야 했다. 곧장 밀턴으로 갈 것인가, 아니면 어디로 갈 것인가? 이 한 가지에 달린 준비가 너무 많았으므로 마거릿은 어느 날 저녁 아버지의 뚜렷한 피로와 침울함에도 불구하고 여쭈어 보기로 마음먹었다. 아버지는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n“얘야! 나는 정말이지 생각할 것이 너무 많아 그것까지는 정하지 못했구나. 네 어머니는 뭐라시더냐? 무엇을 바라신다더냐? 가엾은 마리아!”\n\n그의 한숨보다 더 큰 메아리가 돌아왔다. 마침 딕슨이 헤일 부인에게 드릴 차를 한 잔 더 가지러 방에 들어왔다가 헤일 씨의 마지막 말을 듣고는, 그가 있는 덕에 마거릿의 나무라는 눈길로부터 보호받는다는 생각에 용기를 내어 이렇게 말했다. “가엾은 우리 마님!”\n\n“오늘은 더 나빠진 것 같지는 않지?” 헤일 씨가 황급히 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n“저야 뭐라 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다요, 나리. 제가 판단할 일이 아니지요. 병이 몸보다는 마음에 있는 것 같아서요.”\n\n헤일 씨의 얼굴에 한없는 괴로움이 어렸다.\n\n“식기 전에 어머니께 차를 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠어요, 딕슨.” 마거릿이 조용한 위엄이 실린 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“어머! 죄송합니다, 아가씨! 제 생각이 온통 가엾은 우리…… 헤일 부인께 가 있어서요.”\n\n“아버지!” 마거릿이 말했다. “두 분 다 이 어정쩡함이 나쁜 거예요. 물론 어머니는 아버지의 생각이 바뀐 걸 마음 아파하실 수밖에 없어요. 그건 어쩔 수 없는 일이고요.” 그녀가 부드럽게 말을 이었다. “하지만 이제 적어도 어느 정도까지는 길이 분명해졌잖아요. 그리고 아버지, 무엇을 준비해야 하는지 말씀해 주시면 어머니도 제가 계획 세우는 걸 거들게 할 수 있을 것 같아요. 어머니는 어떤 식으로든 바라는 걸 말씀하신 적이 없고, 어쩔 수 없는 일만 생각하고 계세요. 우리는 곧장 밀턴으로 가는 건가요? 거기 집은 구하셨어요?”\n\n“아니다.” 그가 대답했다. “셋방에 들어가 지내면서 집을 알아봐야 할 것 같구나.”\n\n“그러면 세간은 집을 구할 때까지 기차역에 맡겨 둘 수 있게 꾸려 두고요?”\n\n“그래야겠지. 네가 가장 좋다고 생각하는 대로 하렴. 다만 앞으로는 쓸 돈이 훨씬 적으리라는 것만 기억하고.”\n\n마거릿이 알기로 그들에게 여유라 할 것이 넉넉했던 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그녀는 갑자기 자기 어깨에 큰 무게가 실렸다고 느꼈다. 4개월 전만 해도 그녀가 내려야 할 결정이라고는 만찬에 어떤 드레스를 입을까 하는 것과, 집에서 여는 만찬에서 누가 누구를 데리고 내려갈지 이디스가 명단을 짜는 걸 돕는 일뿐이었다. 그녀가 살던 집안도 결정이랄 것이 그리 필요한 곳이 아니었다. 레녹스 대위의 청혼이라는 단 하나의 큰일을 빼면 모든 것이 시계태엽처럼 규칙적으로 굴러갔다. 한 해에 한 번 이모와 이디스 사이에 와이트섬으로 갈지, 외국으로 갈지, 스코틀랜드로 갈지를 두고 긴 의논이 오갔지만, 그럴 때에도 마거릿은 아무 애도 쓰지 않고 고향이라는 고요한 항구로 떠밀려 갈 것이 확실했다. 그런데 레녹스 씨가 찾아와 그녀를 화들짝 결정 앞에 세운 그날 이후로는, 날마다 그녀에게도 그녀가 사랑하는 이들에게도 중대한 문제가 하나씩 다가와 결정을 기다렸다.\n\n아버지는 차를 마신 뒤 아내와 함께 있으려고 위층으로 올라갔다. 마거릿은 응접실에 혼자 남았다. 문득 그녀는 촛불을 들고 아버지의 서재로 가 커다란 지도책을 가져왔고, 그것을 낑낑대며 응접실로 끌고 와 잉글랜드 지도를 들여다보기 시작했다. 아버지가 아래층으로 내려왔을 때 그녀는 환하게 고개를 들 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n“아주 근사한 계획이 떠올랐어요. 여기 보세요. 다크셔에, 밀턴에서 제 손가락 너비도 안 되는 데에 헤스턴이 있어요. 북쪽에 사는 사람들한테서 아주 좋은 자그마한 해수욕장이라고 여러 번 들었거든요. 자, 우리가 어머니를 딕슨과 함께 저기로 보내 드리고, 그동안 아버지와 제가 집을 보러 다니며 밀턴에 어머니 지내실 집을 다 마련해 두면 어떨까요? 어머니는 바닷바람을 쐬어 겨울을 날 기운을 얻으시고 고생은 하나도 안 하시게 되고, 딕슨은 어머니를 돌보는 걸 즐거워할 거예요.”\n\n“딕슨도 우리와 함께 가는 거냐?” 헤일 씨가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 낭패한 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n“그럼요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “딕슨은 그럴 작정인걸요. 게다가 딕슨 없이 어머니가 어떻게 지내실지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“하지만 앞으로는 아주 다른 살림살이를 견뎌야 할 텐데 걱정이구나. 도시에서는 모든 것이 훨씬 비싸다. 딕슨이 편히 지낼 수 있을지 모르겠구나. 솔직히 말하면 마거릿, 나는 이따금 그 사람이 유세를 부린다는 생각이 든다.”\n\n“물론 그렇지요, 아버지.” 마거릿이 대답했다. “그리고 딕슨이 다른 방식의 살림을 견뎌야 한다면, 우리는 그 유세를 견뎌야 할 텐데 그게 더 고약하겠지요. 하지만 딕슨은 정말로 우리 모두를 사랑하고, 우리를 떠나면 비참해할 게 틀림없어요. 특히 이런 변화를 앞두고는요. 그러니 어머니를 위해서도, 그 충직함을 보아서도 저는 딕슨이 꼭 가야 한다고 생각해요.”\n\n“좋다, 얘야. 계속하렴. 나는 받아들이마. 헤스턴은 밀턴에서 얼마나 머냐? 네 손가락 하나의 너비로는 거리가 잘 그려지지 않는구나.”\n\n“음, 그러면 30마일쯤 되는 것 같아요. 그리 멀지 않네요!”\n\n“거리로야 그렇지만, 다른 걸로 치면…… 아니, 됐다! 정말로 네 어머니에게 좋을 것 같으면 그렇게 정하자꾸나.”\n\n이것은 커다란 진전이었다. 이제 마거릿은 본격적으로 일하고 움직이고 계획할 수 있었다. 이제 헤일 부인도 나른함에서 몸을 일으켜 바닷가로 간다는 기쁨과 즐거움을 생각하며 진짜 고통을 잊을 수 있었다. 그녀의 단 하나 아쉬움은 그곳에서 지낼 2주 내내 헤일 씨가 함께 있어 줄 수 없다는 것이었다. 약혼 시절 그녀가 존 베러스퍼드 경 부부와 함께 토키에 머물 때 그가 꼬박 2주를 함께 있어 준 적이 있었는데 말이다.\n\n# 제6장\n\n아무도 지켜보지 않는 가운데 정원의 가지는 흔들리고, 여린 꽃송이는 하늘하늘 떨어지리라. 사랑받지 못한 채 저 너도밤나무는 갈색으로 물들고, 단풍나무는 스스로를 태워 사위어 가리라.\n\n사랑받지 못한 채 해바라기는 곱게 빛나며 씨앗의 둥근 판 둘레로 불꽃을 뻗치고, 수많은 장미빛 카네이션은 벌 소리 잉잉거리는 대기를 여름의 향내로 채우리라.\n\n마침내 정원에서도 들판에서도 새로운 인연이 불어와, 해가 갈수록 그 풍경이 낯선 이의 아이에게 낯익어질 때까지.\n\n해마다 일꾼이 늘 갈던 밭을 갈고 숲의 빈터를 쳐 내는 동안, 해마다 우리에 대한 기억은 저 언덕들이 두른 둘레에서 스러져 가리라. — 테니슨.\n\n마지막 날이 왔다. 집 안은 포장 상자로 가득했고, 그것들은 현관 앞에서 짐수레에 실려 가장 가까운 기차역으로 옮겨지고 있었다. 집 옆의 어여쁘던 잔디밭조차 열린 문과 창으로 날려 나온 짚 때문에 보기 흉하고 어수선해졌다. 방마다 낯선 울림이 돌았고, 커튼을 걷어 낸 창으로 빛이 모질고 세차게 들이쳐 벌써부터 낯설고 서먹해 보였다. 헤일 부인의 화장방만은 맨 마지막까지 손대지 않고 두었는데, 거기서 부인과 딕슨은 옷가지를 꾸리다가 이따금 서로 말을 끊고는 아이들이 아직 어리던 시절의 유물 같은, 잊고 있던 보물을 발견하고 탄성을 지르며 애틋한 눈길로 뒤적이곤 했다. 두 사람의 일은 그리 진척되지 않았다. 아래층에서는 마거릿이 침착하고 차분하게 서서, 요리사와 샬럿을 도우라고 불러들인 일꾼들에게 지시하고 조언할 채비를 하고 있었다. 요리사와 샬럿은 틈틈이 울면서 아가씨가 이 마지막 날을 어떻게 저리 견뎌 내는지 의아해했고, 그토록 오래 런던에 있었으니 헬스톤에 그리 마음을 두지 않는 모양이라고 둘이서 결론을 지었다. 마거릿은 그곳에 아주 창백하고 조용하게 서서, 크고 진지한 눈으로 모든 것을, 아무리 사소한 일이라도 눈앞의 온갖 사정을 살피고 있었다. 두 사람은 그동안 내내 그녀의 가슴이 어떤 한숨으로도 들어 올리거나 덜어 낼 수 없는 무거운 압박에 얼마나 아파하고 있었는지, 그리고 지각하는 능력을 끊임없이 부려 쓰는 것만이 아파서 울부짖지 않을 유일한 길이었음을 알 수 없었다. 게다가 그녀가 무너지면 누가 나서서 일을 처리한단 말인가? 아버지는 성구실에서 서기와 함께 서류며 책이며 교적부며 온갖 것을 살피고 있었고, 안으로 들어오면 자기 책들을 꾸려야 했는데 그것은 그의 마음에 들게 해낼 수 있는 사람이 그 자신 말고는 없었다. 게다가 마거릿이 낯선 사내들 앞에서, 아니 요리사나 샬럿처럼 집안 식구나 다름없는 이들 앞에서라도 무너질 사람이던가? 그럴 리가! 그러나 마침내 짐 꾸리던 네 사람이 차를 마시러 부엌으로 들어갔고, 마거릿은 그토록 오래 서 있던 현관의 그 자리에서 뻣뻣하고 느릿하게 몸을 옮겨, 텅 빈 채 울리는 응접실을 지나 11월 초저녁의 어스름 속으로 나갔다. 부드럽고 흐릿한 안개가 얇은 너울처럼 드리워 온갖 것을 흐릿하게 했으나 감추지는 못했고, 해가 아직 완전히 지지 않은 터라 그 모든 것에 연보랏빛을 입혀 놓았다. 울새 한 마리가 노래하고 있었다. 아마도, 하고 마거릿은 생각했다, 아버지가 겨울철 애완 새라며 그토록 자주 이야기하던 바로 그 울새일 것이다. 아버지는 서재 창가에 손수 울새 집 같은 것을 지어 주기까지 했다. 나뭇잎은 그 어느 때보다 화려했다. 첫서리가 한번 스치면 잎들은 죄다 땅에 내려앉으리라. 벌써 한두 잎은 낮게 비껴 드는 햇살에 호박빛으로, 금빛으로 물든 채 쉼 없이 떠내려 오고 있었다.\n\n마거릿은 배나무를 올린 담 아래 오솔길을 따라 걸었다. 헨리 레녹스 곁에서 그 길을 거닌 뒤로 한 번도 거기를 지나지 않았다. 여기, 이 백리향 화단께에서 그는 그녀가 지금은 생각해서도 안 될 이야기를 꺼냈다. 대답하려 애쓰는 동안 그녀의 눈은 늦게 핀 저 장미에 가 있었고, 그가 마지막 말을 하는 한복판에서 그녀는 당근의 깃털 같은 잎이 지닌 선명한 아름다움에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 겨우 두 주 전의 일이었다! 그런데 모든 것이 이토록 달라지다니! 그는 지금 어디에 있을까? 런던에서 예전의 그 쳇바퀴를 돌고 있겠지. 할리 가의 옛 무리와, 아니면 좀 더 흥겨운 제 또래 친구들과 저녁을 먹고 있겠지. 지금 이 순간에도, 그녀가 온갖 것이 떨어지고 시들고 썩어 가는 이 축축하고 스산한 정원을 어스름 속에 서글피 거니는 동안, 그는 흡족한 하루의 노고를 마치고 기꺼이 법률 책을 치우고는, 늘 그런다고 그녀에게 말한 대로 템플 정원을 한 바퀴 뛰며 몸을 개운하게 하고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그러면서 바로 곁에 있으나 보이지는 않는 수만 명 분주한 사람들의 웅장하고 알아들을 수 없는 거대한 포효를 들이마시고, 몸을 재게 돌릴 때마다 강물 깊은 곳에서 솟아오르는 도시의 불빛을 흘끗흘끗 붙잡으면서. 그는 공부와 저녁 식사 사이의 짬을 훔쳐 하는 그 급한 산책 이야기를 마거릿에게 자주 했다. 가장 좋은 시절에, 가장 좋은 기분일 때 그는 그 이야기를 했고, 그 생각은 그녀의 공상에 인상을 남겼다. 이곳에는 아무 소리도 없었다. 울새는 밤의 광막한 고요 속으로 사라져 버렸다. 이따금 멀리서 오두막 문이 열렸다 닫혔다. 마치 지친 일꾼을 제집으로 들이는 듯이. 그러나 그 소리는 아주 멀게 들렸다. 정원 너머 숲의 바스락거리는 낙엽 사이로 살금살금 기어드는 듯 서걱대는 소리가 거의 코앞인 양 들려왔다. 마거릿은 그것이 밀렵꾼이라는 것을 알았다. 지난가을 침실에 앉아 촛불을 끈 채 하늘과 땅의 엄숙한 아름다움에 오롯이 취해 있을 때, 그녀는 밀렵꾼들이 소리 없이 가볍게 정원 울타리를 뛰어넘는 것을, 이슬 맺힌 달빛 잔디밭을 재게 가로지르는 것을, 그 너머 검고 고요한 그늘 속으로 사라지는 것을 여러 번 보았다. 그들의 삶이 지닌 거칠고 모험적인 자유는 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았다. 그녀는 그들이 잘되기를 바라고 싶어졌다. 그들이 무섭지 않았다. 그러나 오늘 밤은 무서웠고, 왜 그런지는 알 수 없었다. 샬럿이 누군가 정원에 나갔다는 것도 모른 채 창을 닫고 밤을 대비해 단속하는 소리가 들렸다. 작은 가지 하나가, 썩은 나뭇가지였는지 힘으로 꺾인 것이었는지, 숲에서 가장 가까운 자리에서 묵직하게 떨어졌다. 마거릿은 카밀라처럼 날래게 창가로 달려가 창을 두드렸는데, 그 다급하고 떨리는 소리에 안에 있던 샬럿이 화들짝 놀랐다.\n\n“들여보내 줘요! 들여보내 줘요! 나예요, 샬럿!” 창을 닫아걸고 빗장을 지른 응접실 안, 낯익은 벽이 사방을 에워싸 그녀를 감싸 준 그곳에 안전히 들어서고 나서야 그녀의 심장은 두근거림을 멈추었다. 그녀는 포장 상자 하나에 주저앉았다. 살림을 걷어 낸 그 을씨년스러운 방은 쓸쓸하고 차가웠다. 불도 없고, 샬럿이 오래 심지를 자르지 않은 촛불 말고는 다른 빛도 없었다. 샬럿이 놀란 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았다. 마거릿은 그것을 보았다기보다 느끼고는 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n“나를 아주 밖에 내버려 두고 잠가 버리는 줄 알았어요, 샬럿.” 그녀가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다. “그랬으면 부엌에서는 내가 부르는 소리를 영영 듣지 못했을 테고, 골목과 묘지로 통하는 문은 진작에 잠겼을 텐데요.”\n\n“어머나, 아가씨, 저야 곧 아가씨가 안 계신 걸 알아챘을 거예요. 일꾼들이 어떻게 하면 되는지 여쭈러 아가씨를 찾았을 테니까요. 그리고 차는 나리 서재에 차려 두었어요. 말하자면 그 방이 제일 편하니까요.”\n\n“고마워요, 샬럿. 착한 아이로군요. 헤어지려니 섭섭해요. 내가 자그마한 도움이나 좋은 조언이라도 줄 수 있을 것 같거든 편지를 써 보내 봐요. 헬스톤에서 오는 편지라면 나는 언제나 반가울 테니까요. 주소를 알게 되면 꼭 알려 줄게요.”\n\n서재는 차를 마실 준비가 다 되어 있었다. 불이 활활 잘 타올랐고, 탁자 위에는 아직 불을 붙이지 않은 초가 놓여 있었다. 마거릿은 깔개 위에 앉았다. 저녁의 습기가 옷에 배어 있었고 지나친 피로로 몸이 으슬으슬했으므로 얼마쯤은 몸을 녹이기 위해서였다. 그녀는 두 손을 맞잡아 무릎을 감싸 몸을 지탱했다. 고개가 가슴 쪽으로 조금 떨구어졌다. 마음이야 어떠했든 그 자세는 낙심한 사람의 것이었다. 그러나 바깥 자갈길에서 아버지의 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 숱 많은 검은 머리를 서둘러 뒤로 넘기고, 어느 결에 뺨에 흘러내렸는지 모를 눈물 몇 방울을 닦고는 문을 열어 드리러 나갔다. 아버지는 그녀보다 훨씬 더 침울해 보였다. 그녀는 매번 이번이 마지막이겠거니 싶은 안간힘을 써 가며 아버지가 관심을 가질 만한 이야기를 꺼내 보았지만, 좀처럼 말을 하게 만들 수 없었다.\n\n“오늘은 아주 멀리 걸으셨어요?” 그가 어떤 음식에도 손대기를 마다하는 것을 보고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“포덤 너도밤나무 숲까지 갔다 왔다. 몰트비 과부댁을 보러 갔지. 네게 작별 인사를 못 했다고 몹시 서운해하더구나. 어린 수전이가 며칠째 골목을 내다보며 기다렸다는구나. 아니, 마거릿, 왜 그러니, 얘야?” 자기를 기다리다 번번이 실망했을 그 어린아이 생각은, 그것이 자기가 잊어서가 아니라 도무지 집을 비울 수 없어서였는데도, 가엾은 마거릿의 잔을 넘치게 하는 마지막 한 방울이 되었다. 그녀는 가슴이 미어질 듯 흐느껴 울었다. 헤일 씨는 딱하리만큼 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그는 일어나 방 안을 초조하게 오갔다. 마거릿은 스스로를 다잡으려 했으나 단단한 목소리로 말할 수 있게 되기 전에는 입을 열려 하지 않았다. 아버지가 혼잣말하듯 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n“견딜 수가 없구나. 남이 괴로워하는 것을 보는 걸 견딜 수가 없어. 내 몫이라면 참고 지나갈 수 있을 것 같은데. 아, 돌이킬 길은 없는 것인가?”\n\n“없어요, 아버지.” 마거릿이 그를 똑바로 바라보며 낮고 흔들림 없는 목소리로 말했다. “아버지가 그르쳤다고 믿는 것도 괴로운 일이에요. 하지만 아버지를 위선자로 알게 되는 건 그보다 한없이 더 나쁠 거예요.” 마지막 몇 마디에서 그녀는 목소리를 낮추었다. 위선이라는 생각을 아버지와 결부해 한순간이라도 떠올리는 것이 불경스럽게 느껴지기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n“게다가,” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그저 제가 오늘 밤 지쳤을 뿐이에요. 아버지가 하신 일 때문에 제가 괴로워한다고는 생각하지 마세요, 아버지. 오늘 밤은 우리 둘 다 그 이야기를 할 수 없을 것 같아요.” 눈물과 흐느낌이 자기 뜻과 상관없이 터져 나오는 것을 알고 그녀가 말했다. “어머니께 이 차나 한 잔 갖다 드리는 게 낫겠어요. 제가 너무 바빠 못 가 뵐 때 아주 일찍 드셨으니, 지금 한 잔 더 드리면 반기실 거예요.”\n\n이튿날 아침, 기찻길의 시간은 매정하게도 그들을 사랑스럽고 정든 헬스톤에서 떼어 냈다. 그들은 떠났다. 낮고 길게 뻗은, 중국장미와 피라칸타로 반쯤 덮인 그 목사관 집을 마지막으로 보았다. 창마다 저마다 사랑하던 방에 딸린 그 집은 유리창을 반짝이게 하는 아침 햇살 속에서 그 어느 때보다 정겨워 보였다. 역까지 그들을 데려가려고 사우샘프턴에서 보내온 마차에 미처 자리를 잡기도 전에, 그들은 다시는 돌아오지 못할 길을 떠나 버렸다. 마거릿은 가슴을 찌르는 아픔에, 숲 나무들의 물결 위로 옛 교회 탑이 보일 만한 그 모퉁이에서 마지막으로 그것을 한 번 붙잡아 보려 애써 밖을 내다보려 했다. 그러나 아버지도 그것을 기억하고 있었고, 그녀는 그것이 보이는 단 하나의 창에 대해 아버지가 더 큰 권리를 지녔음을 말없이 인정했다. 그녀는 몸을 뒤로 기대고 눈을 감았다. 눈물이 솟아올라 그늘진 속눈썹에 한순간 반짝이며 매달렸다가 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내려, 아무도 모르는 채 옷 위에 떨어졌다.\n\n그들은 런던의 어느 조용한 여관에서 하룻밤을 묵기로 되어 있었다. 가엾은 헤일 부인은 하루 종일 제 나름으로 거의 내내 울었고, 딕슨은 지독한 심통으로 자기 슬픔을 드러냈으며, 이 모든 고통의 근원이라 여기는 아무것도 모르는 헤일 씨에게 자기 치맛자락이 스치기라도 할까 봐 짜증스럽게 연신 애를 썼다.\n\n그들은 낯익은 거리들을 지나갔다. 자주 찾아가던 집들을 지나고, 이모가 무엇인가 중대하고 끝없는 결정을 내리는 동안 그 곁에서 조바심을 내며 서성이던 가게들을 지나고, 아니 거리에서 아는 얼굴들과 실제로 스쳐 지나가기까지 했다. 그들에게 그날 아침은 헤아릴 수 없이 길었고 벌써 오래전에 어둠의 안식을 위해 저물었어야 마땅하게 느껴졌지만, 그들이 그곳에 닿은 때는 11월 런던 오후의 가장 분주한 시각이었던 것이다. 헤일 부인이 런던에 와 본 지는 오래된 터였다. 그녀는 거의 아이처럼 기운을 차리고 이 거리 저 거리를 둘러보았고, 가게며 마차를 눈으로 좇으며 탄성을 질렀다.\n\n“어머, 저기 해리슨 상점이 있네. 내 혼수를 저기서 참 많이 샀는데. 세상에! 어쩌면 저렇게 달라졌을까! 사우샘프턴의 크로퍼드 상점보다 더 큰 어마어마한 판유리 창을 달았구나. 아, 그리고 저기, 틀림없어. 아니, 아닌가. 아니야, 맞아. 마거릿, 우리 방금 헨리 레녹스 씨를 지나쳤어. 저이가 이 가게들 사이에서 대체 어디를 가는 걸까?”\n\n마거릿은 앞으로 몸을 내밀었다가 그만큼 빨리 도로 물러앉으며 그 갑작스러운 몸짓을 스스로 반쯤 웃어넘겼다. 그때는 이미 100야드나 떨어져 있었다. 그러나 그는 헬스톤의 유물 같았다. 그는 어느 환한 아침과, 사연 많던 하루와 얽혀 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 그를 보고 싶었다. 그가 그녀를 보지 않은 채로, 서로 말을 나눌 일 없이.\n\n여관 높은 층의 방에서 할 일 없이 보낸 저녁은 길고 무거웠다. 헤일 씨는 단골 서적상에게 다녀오고 친구 한둘을 찾아보러 나갔다. 집 안에서든 거리에서든 그들이 보는 사람은 모두 어딘가 약속 자리로 서둘러 가는 듯했고, 누군가에게 기다려지거나 누군가를 기다리는 듯했다. 오직 그들만이 낯설고 벗도 없고 쓸쓸해 보였다. 그러나 1마일 안에도 마거릿은 집이란 집을 알고 있었다. 그녀 자신을 보아서, 또 어머니는 쇼 이모를 보아서 환영받을 집들이었다. 다만 그들이 기쁜 마음으로, 아니 적어도 마음이 편안한 채로 찾아갔을 때의 이야기였다. 슬픔을 안고, 지금 같은 얽히고설킨 곤경에 동정을 구하며 찾아간다면, 벗이 아니라 가까운 지인일 뿐인 그 모든 집에서 그들은 하나의 그늘로 느껴질 것이었다. 런던의 삶은 너무 어지럽게 돌아가고 너무 꽉 차 있어서, 욥의 벗들이 “이레 밤낮을 그와 함께 땅에 앉았으되 그의 괴로움이 심함을 보았으므로 한마디도 말하는 자가 없었더라” 할 때 보여 준 그 깊은 감정의 침묵을 단 한 시간도 허락하지 못한다.\n\n# 제7장\n\n「안개가 햇빛을 가로막고, 연기에 그은 난쟁이 같은 집들이 사방에서 나를 에워싸는구나.」 — 매슈 아널드.\n\n이튿날 오후, 밀턴노던에서 20마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 그들은 헤스턴으로 이어지는 작은 지선 철도로 들어섰다. 헤스턴은 그 자체가 바닷가와 나란히 뻗은 길고 산만한 거리 하나였다. 그곳에는 저만의 기질이 있어서, 잉글랜드 남부의 작은 해수욕 마을들과는 사뭇 달랐다. 그 남부 마을들이 대륙의 것들과 다른 만큼이나 말이다. 스코틀랜드 말을 빌리자면 무엇이든 더 “쓸모 있어” 보였다. 시골 짐수레는 마구에 쇠가 더 많고 나무와 가죽이 덜 들어갔다. 거리의 사람들은 놀이 삼아 나온 길인데도 마음만은 분주했다. 빛깔은 한결 잿빛으로 보였다. 더 오래갈 듯하고, 그리 밝지도 곱지도 않았다. 시골 사람들 사이에서도 헐렁한 작업복 웃옷은 보이지 않았다. 그것은 몸놀림을 더디게 하고 기계에 걸리기 십상이어서 입는 버릇이 아주 사라져 버린 것이다. 잉글랜드 남부의 그런 읍내에서 마거릿은 가게 사람들이 장사가 한가할 때면 문간에 느긋이 서서 맑은 공기를 쐬고 거리를 오르내리는 풍경을 바라보는 것을 보아 왔다. 이곳에서는 손님이 없어 짬이 나면 그들은 가게 안에서 스스로 일거리를 만들어 냈다. 마거릿이 보기에는 굳이 그럴 것도 없이 리본을 풀었다가 도로 감기까지 했다. 이튿날 아침 어머니와 함께 셋집을 구하러 나섰을 때, 이 모든 차이가 그녀의 마음에 하나하나 와 닿았다.\n\n여관에서 보낸 이틀 밤은 헤일 씨가 짐작했던 것보다 비용이 더 들었으므로, 그들은 맨 처음 만난 깨끗하고 밝은 방을, 마침 들어갈 수 있는 곳이라 하여 반갑게 잡았다. 그곳에서 마거릿은 여러 날 만에 처음으로 쉼을 느꼈다. 게다가 그 쉼에는 꿈결 같은 데가 있어서, 몸을 누이기에 한층 더 온전하고 호사스러웠다. 멀리 바다는 고른 소리로 모랫가를 핥았다. 좀 더 가까이에서는 나귀 몰이 아이들의 외침이 들려왔다. 낯선 풍경들은 그림처럼 눈앞을 지나갔는데, 나른한 그녀는 그것들이 지나가 버리기 전에 굳이 다 알아보려 들지도 않았다. 바닷바람을 쐬려 해변까지 걸어 내려가면 11월 끝자락인데도 그 모래톱의 바람은 부드럽고 따뜻했다. 아득히 길게 흐린 수평선은 여린 빛깔의 하늘에 맞닿아 있었다. 멀리 뜬 배의 흰 돛은 창백한 햇살 한 줄기에 은빛으로 물들었다. 이런 호사스러운 시름 속에서라면 평생을 꿈꾸듯 흘려보낼 수도 있을 것 같았다. 그 속에서 그녀는 지난 일을 감히 떠올리지도, 앞일을 헤아려 보고 싶지도 않았기에 오직 지금 이 순간만을 전부로 삼았다.\n\n그러나 앞일이란 아무리 모질고 쇠 같더라도 맞닥뜨려야 하는 법이다. 어느 저녁, 마거릿과 아버지가 이튿날 밀턴노던으로 가서 집을 알아보기로 정해졌다. 헤일 씨는 벨 씨에게서 편지를 여러 통 받았고 손턴 씨에게서도 한두 통 받은 터라, 그곳에서 자신의 처지와 성공할 가망에 관한 여러 가지 사정을 당장 알아보고 싶어 했다. 그것은 뒤엣분을 직접 만나야만 알 수 있는 일이었다. 마거릿은 이사를 서둘러야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 공장 도시라는 생각 자체가 싫었고, 어머니가 헤스턴의 공기 덕을 보고 있다고 여겼으므로, 밀턴 나들이를 기꺼이 미루고 싶었다.\n\n밀턴에 닿기 몇 마일 전부터 그들은 그 도시가 있는 쪽 지평선 위에 짙은 납빛 구름이 걸려 있는 것을 보았다. 겨울 하늘의 옅은 잿빛 도는 푸른색과 대비되어 그것은 한층 더 어두워 보였다. 헤스턴에는 이미 첫서리의 조짐이 있었던 것이다. 도시에 가까워지자 공기에는 연기의 맛과 냄새가 희미하게 배어 있었다. 어쩌면 그것은 무슨 뚜렷한 맛이나 냄새라기보다 풀과 초목의 향기가 사라진 자리에 가까웠는지도 모른다. 그들은 길고 곧고 희망이라고는 없는 거리들을 휙휙 지나쳤다. 하나같이 작고 벽돌로 지은, 판에 박은 듯한 집들이 늘어선 거리였다. 여기저기 창이 숱하게 뚫린 커다랗고 길쭉한 공장이 병아리 떼 가운데 선 암탉처럼 솟아 있었고, “법을 어긴” 검은 연기를 뿜어내고 있었다. 마거릿이 비를 부르는 조짐이라 여겼던 그 구름의 정체가 그것으로 넉넉히 설명되었다. 역에서 여관까지 좀 더 크고 넓은 거리를 지나는 동안 그들은 쉴 새 없이 멈춰야 했다. 짐을 잔뜩 실은 큰 수레들이 그리 넓지도 않은 큰길을 가로막고 있었던 것이다. 마거릿도 이모와 마차를 타고 나갔다가 이따금 런던 시내에 들어가 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그곳의 육중하고 굼뜬 수레들은 저마다 쓰임과 목적이 다양해 보였다. 이곳에서는 짐마차든 짐수레든 화물차든 하나같이 면화를 실어 날랐다. 자루에 담긴 날것 그대로거나, 옥양목 꾸러미로 짜인 것이거나 둘 중 하나였다. 사람들이 인도에 몰려다녔는데, 대부분 옷감으로 보아서는 잘 차려입은 편이었으나 어딘가 헐렁하고 아무렇게나 걸친 데가 있었다. 마거릿에게는 그것이 런던의 같은 계층이 지닌 낡고 해졌으나 말쑥한 맵시와는 다르게 느껴졌다.\n\n“뉴 스트리트로구나.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “이게 밀턴의 중심 거리인 모양이다. 벨이 내게 여러 번 이야기했지. 30년 전에 이 거리가 골목에서 큰길로 뚫린 덕에 그 사람 재산 값이 그토록 뛰어올랐다는구나. 손턴 씨 공장도 그리 멀지 않은 데 있을 게다. 그가 벨 씨의 세입자니까 말이야. 하지만 편지에 적는 주소는 창고 쪽인 듯싶더구나.”\n\n“우리 여관은 어디예요, 아버지?”\n\n“이 거리 끝머리 가까이일 게다. 점심을 먹고 나서 「밀턴 타임스」에 표시해 둔 집들을 보러 갈까, 아니면 먼저 보고 올까?”\n\n“아, 일부터 마쳐요.”\n\n“그러자꾸나. 그럼 손턴 씨한테서 온 쪽지나 편지가 있는지만 보마. 그 집들에 관해 무엇이든 듣는 대로 알려 주겠다고 했으니 말이다. 그러고 나서 곧장 나서자. 마차는 붙들어 두자꾸나. 길을 헤매다가 오늘 오후 기차를 놓치는 것보다는 그편이 안전할 테니.”\n\n그를 기다리는 편지는 없었다. 그들은 집을 구하러 나섰다. 낼 수 있는 돈은 연 30파운드가 고작이었는데, 햄프셔라면 그 돈으로 넉넉한 집과 아담한 뜰까지 얻을 수 있었을 것이다. 이곳에서는 거실 2개와 침실 4개라는 최소한의 살림 공간조차 얻을 수 없어 보였다. 그들은 적어 온 목록을 훑으며 한 집씩 찾아가 보고는 차례로 물리쳤다. 그러고는 낙담한 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n“두 번째 집으로 돌아가는 수밖에 없을 것 같아요. 그 집 말이에요. 크램프턴이라고 하던가요, 그 변두리 동네? 거실이 3개나 있었잖아요. 침실도 3개인데 거실이 그만큼이나 된다고 우리가 웃었던 것 기억나세요? 그런데 제가 다 궁리해 놓았어요. 아래층 앞방은 아버지 서재 겸 우리 식당으로 쓰기로 해요. 가엾은 아버지! 어머니께는 되도록 밝은 거실을 드려야 한다고 우리끼리 정해 두었잖아요. 그리고 그 흉한 파랑 분홍 벽지에 육중한 처마 장식이 달린 위층 앞방은 저 아래 벌판이 정말 예쁘게 내다보였어요. 강인지 운하인지 뭔지가 크게 굽어 도는 것도 보이고요. 저는 그 뒤쪽 작은 침실을 쓰면 돼요. 부엌 위쪽, 첫 층계참 끝에 튀어나온 그 방 있잖아요. 아버지와 어머니는 응접실 뒤쪽 방을 쓰시고요. 지붕 밑의 그 골방은 아버지께 훌륭한 옷방이 되어 줄 거예요.”\n\n“그럼 딕슨은? 또 일손을 도우라고 둘 아이는?”\n\n“아, 잠깐만요. 제 살림 솜씨가 얼마나 대단한지 깨닫고 제가 다 얼떨떨하네요. 딕슨은 어디 보자, 아까는 생각해 두었는데, 뒤쪽 거실을 쓰기로 해요. 딕슨도 마음에 들어 할 거예요. 헤스턴에서는 층계 때문에 어찌나 투덜대는지 몰라요. 그리고 그 아이는 아버지와 어머니 방 위의 비스듬한 다락을 쓰면 되고요. 그만하면 되지 않아요?”\n\n“그렇겠구나. 하지만 그 벽지 말이다. 무슨 취향이 그러냐! 그런 집에 색을 그토록 처바르고 처마 장식은 또 그렇게 육중하게 두르다니!”\n\n“괜찮아요, 아버지! 집주인을 잘 구슬러서 방 한둘쯤은 도배를 다시 하게 하실 수 있을 거예요. 응접실과 아버지 침실 말이에요. 어머니가 가장 많이 계실 곳이니까요. 그리고 식당의 그 요란한 무늬는 아버지 책장이 상당 부분 가려 줄 거고요.”\n\n“그럼 네 생각에는 그 집이 제일 낫다는 게냐? 그렇다면 당장 가서 광고에 적힌 그 돈킨이라는 사람을 만나 보는 게 좋겠구나. 너는 여관까지 데려다주마. 거기서 점심을 시켜 놓고 쉬고 있으려무나. 준비가 될 때쯤이면 나도 돌아와 있을 게다. 벽지는 새로 얻어 낼 수 있으면 좋겠구나.”\n\n마거릿도 그러기를 바랐지만 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 아무리 흉한 것이라도 장식이라면 좋아하는 취향, 그 자체로 우아함의 뼈대가 되는 담백함과 소박함보다 장식을 더 좋아하는 취향과 제대로 부딪쳐 본 적이 없었다.\n\n아버지는 그녀를 데리고 여관 현관을 지나 층계 아래에 남겨 둔 채, 그들이 정한 집의 주인이 산다는 주소로 떠났다. 마거릿이 막 그들의 거실 문에 손을 얹었을 때, 종종걸음으로 뒤따라온 종업원이 말을 걸었다.\n\n“실례합니다, 아씨. 그 어른께서 하도 빨리 나가시는 바람에 여쭐 틈이 없었습니다. 두 분이 나가신 직후에 손턴 씨가 찾아오셨습니다. 그 어른 말씀으로 미루어 한 시간이면 돌아오시겠거니 해서 그렇게 전해 드렸더니, 5분쯤 전에 다시 오셔서 헤일 씨를 기다리시겠다고 하셨습니다. 지금 방에 계십니다, 아씨.”\n\n“고마워요. 아버지가 곧 돌아오실 테니 그때 말씀드리면 되겠네요.”\n\n마거릿은 문을 열고 몸에 밴 그 곧고 두려움 없고 의젓한 태도로 들어섰다. 그녀는 어색함을 느끼지 않았다. 그러기에는 사교의 몸가짐이 너무 익어 있었다. 아버지에게 볼일이 있어 온 사람이 여기 있었다. 게다가 그는 이미 호의를 보여 준 이였으므로, 그녀는 그에게 충분한 예의를 갖추어 대할 마음이었다. 손턴 씨 쪽이 그녀보다 훨씬 더 놀라고 당황했다. 조용한 중년의 성직자 대신 젊은 아가씨가 거리낌 없는 위엄을 갖추고 앞으로 나섰던 것이다. 그가 평소 보아 오던 여자들 대부분과는 다른 부류의 아가씨였다. 옷차림은 아주 수수했다. 가장 좋은 감과 모양으로 지어 흰 리본을 두른 몸에 꼭 맞는 밀짚 보닛, 그리고 길고 무거운 주름을 이루며 몸에 드리운 커다란 인도 숄. 그녀는 그것을 마치 여제가 옷자락을 걸치듯 두르고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 누구인지 알아차리지 못했다. 그가 거기 있다는 사실이 그 아름다운 얼굴에는 아무 상관도 없는 일이며 상아처럼 흰 낯빛에 놀란 기색 하나 불러일으키지 못한다는 것을 보여 주는, 그 담박하고 곧고 주눅 들지 않은 눈길을 마주하면서도. 헤일 씨에게 딸이 있다는 말은 들었지만, 그는 어린 여자아이일 줄로만 알고 있었다.\n\n“손턴 씨시군요.” 그가 미처 말을 찾지 못하고 있던 그 반 순간의 침묵 뒤에 마거릿이 말했다. “앉으시지요. 아버지가 채 일 분도 안 되어 저를 문 앞까지 데려다주셨는데, 안타깝게도 손턴 씨가 와 계신 줄 모르고 볼일이 있어 나가셨어요. 하지만 곧 돌아오실 거예요. 두 번이나 걸음하시게 해서 죄송합니다.”\n\n손턴 씨 자신도 남을 부리는 데 익숙한 사람이었으나, 그녀는 대뜸 그를 다스리는 듯한 태도를 지녔다. 그녀가 나타나기 직전만 해도 그는 장이 서는 날에 시간을 허비하는 것이 슬슬 못마땅해지던 참이었다. 그런데 이제 그는 그녀가 시키는 대로 잠자코 자리에 앉았다.\n\n“헤일 씨가 어디로 가셨는지 아십니까? 어쩌면 제가 찾아낼 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“커누트 가의 돈킨 씨라는 분에게 가셨어요. 아버지가 얻으려는 크램프턴의 집 주인이랍니다.”\n\n손턴 씨는 그 집을 알고 있었다. 광고를 보고 직접 가서 살펴본 적도 있었다. 헤일 씨를 힘닿는 데까지 도와 달라는 벨 씨의 부탁에 따른 것이기도 했고, 헤일 씨와 같은 사정으로 성직록을 내놓은 성직자의 처지에 스스로 관심이 동한 까닭이기도 했다. 손턴 씨는 크램프턴의 그 집이 정말 딱 맞겠다고 여겼었다. 그러나 이제 마거릿의 그 당당한 몸가짐과 눈길을 보고 나니, 둘러볼 당시에도 걸렸던 어딘가 천박한 구석이 있는 그 집을 헤일 집안에 아주 알맞겠다고 생각한 것이 부끄러워지기 시작했다.\n\n마거릿은 제 생김새를 어쩔 수 없었다. 그러나 짧게 도드라진 윗입술, 둥글고 다부지게 치켜든 턱, 고개를 가누는 자세, 부드러운 여자다운 반항이 가득한 몸짓은 낯선 이들에게 늘 오만하다는 인상을 주었다. 그녀는 지금 지쳐 있었고, 차라리 잠자코 있으면서 아버지가 마련해 준 휴식을 취하고 싶었다. 그러나 물론 스스로에게 부끄럽지 않으려면 숙녀답게 굴어야 했고 이 낯선 이에게 이따금 정중한 말을 건네야 했다. 밀턴의 거리와 인파에 부대끼고 온 그 사람은 솔직히 말해 옷매무새도 그리 단정하지 않고 몸가짐도 그리 다듬어져 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그가 한번 말했던 대로 그만 가 주기를 바랐다. 저렇게 앉아서 그녀가 하는 말마다 짧은 문장으로 툭툭 대답만 하고 있지 말고. 그녀는 숄을 벗어 의자 등받이에 걸쳐 두었다. 그녀는 그를 마주하고, 또한 빛을 마주하고 앉아 있었다. 그녀의 아름다움이 온전히 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 풍만하면서도 나긋한 몸매 위로 솟아오른 둥글고 흰 유연한 목, 말할 때조차 거의 움직이지 않아 그 곱고 도도한 곡선 하나를 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 않는 입술, 그리하여 차갑도록 고요한 얼굴빛을 조금도 바꾸지 않는 그 입매, 부드러운 그늘을 머금은 채 처녀다운 거리낌 없음으로 그의 눈길을 마주 받는 두 눈. 대화가 끝나기도 전에 그는 그녀가 마음에 들지 않는다고 스스로에게 되뇌다시피 했다. 그는 그렇게 해서 굴욕감을 달래 보려 했다. 그는 억누를 수 없는 감탄으로 그녀를 바라보는데, 그녀는 도도한 무심함으로 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 자기를 무엇으로 여기고 있다고 생각했다. 화가 치민 김에 스스로에게 이르던 그것, 곧 우아함이라고는 세련됨이라고는 한 점도 없는 크고 거친 사내로 말이다. 그는 그녀의 조용하고 냉랭한 태도를 업신여김으로 받아들였고, 마음속으로 어찌나 분했던지 자리를 박차고 나가 이 헤일 집안 사람들과 그 콧대와는 다시는 상관하지 말까 하는 생각까지 들 지경이었다.\n\n마거릿이 마지막 화젯거리마저 바닥냈을 바로 그때, 그토록 적고 짧은 말들로 이루어진 것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면 말이지만, 아버지가 들어왔다. 그리고 그 유쾌하고 신사다운 정중한 사과로 자기 이름과 가족에 대한 손턴 씨의 호감을 되돌려 놓았다.\n\n헤일 씨와 손님은 두 사람의 공통된 벗인 벨 씨에 관해 나눌 이야기가 많았다. 마거릿은 손님을 상대하는 자기 몫이 끝난 것이 반가워 창가로 가서 낯선 거리 풍경에 좀 더 익숙해져 보려 했다. 밖에서 벌어지는 일을 지켜보는 데 어찌나 골똘했던지 아버지가 말을 걸어도 거의 듣지 못했고, 아버지는 했던 말을 되풀이해야 했다.\n\n“마거릿! 집주인이 그 흉측한 벽지를 극구 좋다고 우기는구나. 아무래도 그냥 두어야 할 것 같다.”\n\n“어머나! 안됐네요!” 그녀가 대답하고는, 적어도 일부라도 자기 그림 몇 점으로 가려 볼 수 있을까 마음속으로 궁리하기 시작했다. 그러나 그러다가는 되레 더 볼썽사나워지기 십상이겠다 싶어 끝내 그 생각을 접었다. 그러는 동안 아버지는 시골 사람다운 다정한 손님 대접으로 손턴 씨에게 점심을 함께 하고 가라고 권하고 있었다. 그로서는 그러기가 몹시 곤란했으나, 마거릿이 말로든 눈길로든 아버지의 권유에 한마디만 보탰더라면 자기가 그 청을 받아들였으리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 그녀가 그러지 않은 것이 다행스러우면서도, 그러지 않았다는 것에 화가 났다. 그가 떠날 때 그녀는 나직하고 정중한 목례를 건넸고, 그는 평생 어느 때보다도 손발이 어색하고 스스로가 의식되었다.\n\n“자, 마거릿, 이제 되도록 빨리 점심을 먹자꾸나. 시켜 두었니?”\n\n“아니요, 아버지. 제가 돌아왔을 때 그분이 여기 계셔서 그럴 틈이 없었어요.”\n\n“그럼 나오는 대로 아무거나 먹어야겠구나. 아무래도 그 사람이 한참을 기다렸을 것 같은데.”\n\n“저한테는 지독하게 길게 느껴졌어요. 아버지가 들어오셨을 때 저는 막 숨이 넘어가기 직전이었다고요. 무슨 이야기든 이어 가는 법이 없고 짤막하고 뚝뚝 끊기는 대답만 하더군요.”\n\n“그래도 요점은 아주 잘 짚는 것 같더구나. 머리가 맑은 사람이야. 그가 말하기를, 너도 들었니? 크램프턴은 자갈땅 위에 있어서 밀턴 근방에서 단연 가장 건강에 좋은 변두리라더구나.”\n\n헤스턴으로 돌아오자 그날 있었던 일을 헤일 부인에게 들려주어야 했다. 부인은 묻고 싶은 것이 잔뜩 있었고, 두 사람은 차를 마시는 틈틈이 그 물음에 답했다.\n\n“그래, 당신이 편지를 주고받는다는 그 손턴 씨라는 분은 어떤 사람이에요?”\n\n“마거릿에게 물어보구려.” 남편이 말했다. “내가 집주인을 만나러 나가 있는 동안 저 애하고 그 사람이 한참 이야기를 나눠 보려 애썼으니.”\n\n“아, 저도 그분이 어떤 분인지 잘 모르겠어요.” 마거릿이 나른하게 말했다. 너무 지쳐서 묘사에 힘을 쏟기가 어려웠던 것이다. 그러다 정신을 가다듬고 말했다. “키가 크고 어깨가 벌어진 분이에요. 나이는, 몇쯤 될까요, 아버지?”\n\n“내 짐작에는 30쯤 되지 싶구나.”\n\n“30쯤이에요. 얼굴은 딱히 못생기지도 잘생기지도 않았고, 눈에 띄는 데라곤 없어요. 신사라고 하기는 좀 그렇고요. 하지만 그건 애초에 바랄 수 없는 일이었겠지요.”\n\n“그렇다고 천박하거나 상스럽지는 않지.” 아버지가 끼어들었다. 밀턴에서 유일한 벗이 깎이는 것이 다소 못마땅했던 것이다.\n\n“아, 그럼요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “그런 결단과 힘이 서린 표정을 지녔다면 아무리 이목구비가 못났더라도 천박하거나 상스러울 수는 없지요. 저라면 그분과 흥정을 해야 하는 처지는 사양하겠어요. 아주 굽힐 줄 모르는 사람으로 보여요. 한마디로 제 자리에 꼭 맞게 태어난 사람 같아요, 어머니. 큰 장사꾼답게 눈치 빠르고 억센 분이지요.”\n\n“밀턴의 공장주들을 장사꾼이라고 부르지 말거라, 마거릿.” 아버지가 말했다. “그들은 아주 다르단다.”\n\n“그런가요? 저는 손에 잡히는 무엇인가를 파는 사람에게는 다 그 말을 쓰거든요. 하지만 아버지가 맞지 않는 말이라고 여기신다면 쓰지 않을게요. 그런데 어머니! 천박하고 상스러운 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 우리 응접실 벽지에 마음의 준비를 하셔야 해요. 노란 잎사귀에 분홍 장미 파란 장미라니까요! 게다가 방을 빙 두른 그 육중한 처마 장식이라니!”\n\n그러나 그들이 밀턴의 새집으로 옮겨 갔을 때 그 밉살스러운 벽지는 사라지고 없었다. 집주인은 그들의 인사를 아주 태연하게 받았고, 다시 도배는 하지 않겠다고 못 박았던 자기 뜻을 누그러뜨린 것이라고 그들이 여기고 싶어 한다면 그러도록 내버려 두었다. 밀턴에서 이름 없는 헤일 목사를 위해서라면 하고 싶지 않았을 일을, 부유한 공장주 손턴 씨가 짧고 매섭게 한마디 하자 기꺼이 해치웠다는 것을 굳이 그들에게 알릴 까닭은 없었던 것이다.\n\n# 제8장\n\n「그리고 고향으로, 고향으로, 고향으로, 고향으로 가고 싶어라.」\n\n방마다 발라 놓은 어여쁜 밝은 벽지가 아니었다면 그들은 밀턴과 화해할 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그것만으로는 부족했다. 더 필요했지만 얻을 수 없는 것들이 있었다. 누렇고 짙은 11월의 안개가 몰려들었고, 강이 크게 굽이돌며 만들어 놓은 골짜기의 평지 풍경은 헤일 부인이 새집에 닿았을 무렵 온통 가려져 보이지 않았다.\n\n마거릿과 딕슨이 이틀 동안 짐을 풀고 정리하며 일했건만 집 안은 여전히 온통 어수선해 보였다. 밖에서는 짙은 안개가 창문 바로 앞까지 기어올랐고, 열린 문마다 숨 막히는 흰 화환 같은 해로운 안개가 밀려 들어왔다.\n\n“아, 마거릿! 우리가 여기서 살아야 하는 거니?” 헤일 부인이 넋을 잃은 낙담 속에서 물었다.\n\n이 물음이 던져진 말투의 스산함이 마거릿의 가슴에도 그대로 울렸다. 그녀는 간신히 자신을 다스려 이렇게 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다. “어머, 런던의 안개도 때로는 훨씬 더 심한걸요!”\n\n“하지만 그때는 런던이라는 곳이, 그리고 친구들이 뒤에 있다는 걸 알고 있었지. 여기는…… 그래! 우리는 아주 외톨이야. 아, 딕슨, 여기는 대체 어떤 곳이니!”\n\n“정말이지 마님, 머잖아 마님이 여기서 돌아가시고 말 겁니다요. 그러면 제가 누구를 원망할지…… 잠깐만요! 헤일 아가씨, 그건 아가씨가 드시기엔 너무 무겁습니다.”\n\n“괜찮아요, 고마워요, 딕슨.” 마거릿이 쌀쌀하게 대꾸했다. “지금 어머니를 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 어머니가 바로 자리에 드실 수 있도록 방을 다 치워 놓는 거예요. 그동안 저는 가서 커피를 한 잔 가져올게요.”\n\n헤일 씨도 마찬가지로 기운을 잃었고, 마찬가지로 마거릿에게서 위로를 구했다.\n\n“마거릿, 여기는 정말이지 몸에 해로운 곳인 것 같구나. 네 어머니나 네 건강이 상하기라도 하면 어쩌지. 차라리 웨일스의 어느 시골로 갈 것을 그랬다. 이건 정말 끔찍하구나.” 그가 창가로 다가가며 말했다.\n\n건넬 위로란 없었다. 그들은 밀턴에 자리를 잡았고, 한 계절 동안 연기와 안개를 견뎌야 했다. 실로 다른 모든 삶이 그만큼 짙은 처지의 안개에 가려 그들에게서 닫혀 버린 듯했다. 바로 전날 헤일 씨는 이사와 헤스턴에서의 두 주가 얼마나 들었는지를 낙담 속에 셈해 보다가, 그것이 얼마 되지 않던 현금을 거의 다 삼켜 버렸다는 것을 알아냈다. 그렇다! 그들은 여기 와 있었고, 여기 머물러야만 했다.\n\n밤이 되어 마거릿이 이 사실을 실감했을 때, 그녀는 절망의 멍한 상태로 주저앉고 싶은 심정이었다. 무겁고 그을린 공기가 그녀의 침실에 감돌았다. 그 방은 집 뒤편으로 길고 좁게 튀어나온 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 그 길쭉한 방의 옆면에 난 창은 열 자도 채 떨어지지 않은 똑같은 돌출부의 민민한 벽을 마주 보고 있었다. 그것은 안개 너머로 희망을 가로막는 커다란 장벽처럼 어렴풋이 솟아 있었다. 방 안은 온통 어지러웠다. 그들의 온 노력은 어머니의 방을 편하게 만드는 데로만 쏠려 있었던 것이다. 마거릿은 상자 하나에 걸터앉았는데, 거기 붙은 주소 쪽지가 헬스톤에서 쓰인 것이라는 사실이 문득 그녀의 마음을 찔렀다. 아름답고 사랑하는 헬스톤! 그녀는 침울한 생각에 빠져들었다. 그러나 마침내 마음을 지금 이곳에서 떼어 놓기로 마음먹었고, 문득 아침의 부산함 속에서 반밖에 읽지 못한 이디스의 편지가 있다는 것이 떠올랐다. 편지에는 코르푸에 닿았다는 소식이 담겨 있었다. 지중해를 따라온 항해, 배 위에서의 음악과 춤, 그녀 앞에 열린 화사한 새 생활, 격자 시렁을 두른 발코니가 있고 흰 절벽과 짙푸른 바다가 내려다보이는 그녀의 집 이야기였다.\n\n이디스는 생생하게 그려 내지는 못해도 술술 잘 썼다. 그녀는 어떤 장면의 두드러지고 특징적인 대목을 붙잡아 낼 뿐 아니라, 이것저것 가릴 것 없는 자잘한 것들을 마거릿이 스스로 그림을 그려 볼 수 있을 만큼 넉넉히 늘어놓을 줄도 알았다. 레녹스 대위와 최근에 결혼한 또 다른 장교가 별장 하나를 함께 쓰고 있었다. 바다 위로 아름답게 깎아지른 바위 높은 곳에 있는 집이었다. 그해가 저물어 가는데도 그들의 나날은 뱃놀이나 들놀이로 채워지는 듯했다. 온통 바깥에서, 즐거움을 좇으며 흥겹게 지내니, 이디스의 삶은 그녀 위에 펼쳐진 푸른 하늘의 깊은 궁륭 같아 보였다. 자유롭고, 티끌 하나 구름 한 점 없이 온전히 자유로웠다. 남편은 훈련에 나가야 했고, 그곳에서 가장 음악에 밝은 장교의 아내인 그녀는 군악대장을 위해 가장 최근의 영국 악보에서 새롭고 인기 있는 곡들을 베껴 적어야 했다. 그것이 그들의 가장 고되고 힘든 임무인 듯했다. 그녀는 연대가 코르푸에 한 해 더 머무르게 되면 마거릿이 건너와 오래 묵어 가면 좋겠다는 다정한 바람을 적었다. 그녀는 자기가 편지를 쓰고 있는 이날로부터 꼭 한 해 전의 일을 마거릿이 기억하는지 물었다. 할리 가에 온종일 비가 내리던 그날, 시시한 만찬에 가는데 새 드레스를 입지 않겠다고, 마차까지 걸어가다가 다 젖고 흙탕물이 튈 거라고 고집을 부리던 일, 그리고 바로 그 만찬에서 그들이 레녹스 대위를 처음 만난 일 말이다.\n\n그렇다! 마거릿은 그날을 또렷이 기억했다. 이디스와 쇼 부인이 만찬에 갔고, 마거릿은 저녁에야 그 자리에 합류했다. 온갖 준비의 넉넉한 호사, 가구의 위엄 있는 아름다움, 집의 크기, 손님들의 평온하고 근심 없는 안락함이 모두 그녀 앞에 생생히 떠올라 지금 이때와 야릇한 대조를 이루었다. 그 옛 삶의 잔잔한 바다는 그들이 모두 어디에 있었는지 알려 줄 자국 하나 남기지 않은 채 닫혀 버렸다. 늘 있던 만찬과 방문과 춤추는 저녁 모임은 쇼 이모와 이디스가 더는 그 자리에 없는데도 여전히, 영원히 이어지고 있었다. 그리고 그녀야 물론 더더욱 아쉬워하는 이가 없었다. 그녀는 그 옛 무리 가운데 헨리 레녹스 말고 누구 하나 자기를 떠올리는 이가 있을까 싶었다. 그 사람 역시 자기가 안긴 아픔 때문에 자기를 잊으려 애쓰리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 언짢은 생각이라면 무엇이든 멀찍이 밀어내는 재주가 있다고 그가 곧잘 자랑하던 것을 그녀는 여러 번 들었다. 그러다 그녀는 있을 수도 있었던 일 속으로 더 깊이 파고들었다. 만일 그녀가 그를 연인으로 마음에 두고 청혼을 받아들였는데 아버지의 신념에 이런 변화가 일어나고 그에 따라 처지까지 이렇게 되었다면, 레녹스 씨가 그것을 참지 못하고 받아들였으리라는 것을 그녀는 의심할 수 없었다. 어떤 뜻에서 그것은 그녀에게 쓰라린 굴욕이었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 꾹 참고 견딜 수 있었다. 아버지의 뜻이 순수하다는 것을 알았고, 그 사실이 아버지의 잘못을, 그녀가 보기에도 무겁고 심각한 그 잘못을 견뎌 낼 힘을 주었기 때문이다. 그러나 세상이 그 거칠고 뭉뚱그린 판단으로 아버지를 격이 떨어진 사람으로 여긴다는 사실은 레녹스 씨를 짓누르고 짜증 나게 했을 것이다. 있을 수도 있었던 일을 깨달으면서 그녀는 지금 있는 그대로에 고마워하게 되었다. 그들은 이제 맨 밑바닥에 있었다. 더 나빠질 수는 없었다. 이디스의 놀라움과 쇼 이모의 낙담은 그들의 편지가 오면 씩씩하게 맞이해야 할 터였다. 그리하여 마거릿은 몸을 일으켜 천천히 옷을 벗기 시작했다. 시각이 늦기는 했어도 하루 종일 이어진 부산함 끝에 느긋하게 움직이는 호사를 한껏 누리면서. 그녀는 안에서든 밖에서든 어떤 밝음이 찾아오기를 바라며 잠이 들었다. 그러나 그 밝음이 오기까지 얼마나 오래 걸릴지 알았더라면 그녀의 가슴은 밑바닥까지 가라앉았을 것이다. 그 계절은 몸에도 마음에도 더없이 좋지 않은 때였다. 어머니는 심한 고뿔에 걸렸고, 딕슨 자신도 몸이 성치 않은 것이 분명했다. 그렇다고 그녀의 수고를 덜어 주려 하거나 그녀를 돌보려 드는 것보다 더한 모욕은 딕슨에게 없었지만. 딕슨을 도울 처녀아이는 하나도 구할 수 없었다. 다들 공장에서 일하고 있었다. 적어도 찾아온 아이들은 자기 같은 것들이 신사의 집에서 일하도록 믿음을 받을 수 있으리라 여겼다는 이유로 딕슨에게 실컷 꾸중을 들었다. 그래서 그들은 거의 늘 삯일하는 여자를 하나 두어야 했다. 마거릿은 샬럿을 불러오고 싶었지만, 이제 그들이 둘 수 있는 형편보다 나은 하녀라는 걸림돌 말고도 거리가 너무 멀었다.\n\n헤일 씨는 벨 씨의 추천으로, 또는 더 가까이에 있는 손턴 씨의 주선으로 몇몇 학생을 만나게 되었다. 그들은 대개 여느 소년이라면 아직 학교에 다니고 있을 나이였다. 그러나 밀턴에 널리 퍼져 있고 보아하니 근거도 있어 보이는 생각에 따르면, 사내아이를 훌륭한 상인으로 만들려면 어릴 때 붙잡아 공장이나 사무실이나 창고의 생활에 길들여야 했다. 스코틀랜드의 대학에라도 보내면 상업에 마음을 붙이지 못한 채 돌아왔다. 열여덟이 되기 전에는 들어갈 수도 없는 옥스퍼드나 케임브리지에 간다면 얼마나 더 그렇겠는가? 그래서 공장주들은 대개 열넷이나 열다섯 살 나이부터 아들을 어느 자리에 앉히고, 문학이나 높은 지적 교양 쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 곁가지는 사정없이 쳐 냈다. 그 나무의 온 힘과 기운을 장사 쪽으로 몰아넣으려는 바람에서였다. 그래도 더 슬기로운 부모들이 없지는 않았고, 자신의 부족함을 알아차리고 그것을 메우려 애쓸 만큼 분별이 있는 이들도 있었다. 아니, 이제 젊은이가 아니라 한창때의 장년이면서도 자신의 무지를 인정하고 일찍 배웠어야 할 것을 늦게라도 배우는 준엄한 지혜를 지닌 사람도 몇 있었다. 손턴 씨는 헤일 씨의 학생들 가운데 아마 가장 나이가 많았을 것이다. 그리고 분명 가장 마음에 드는 학생이었다. 헤일 씨가 어찌나 자주, 또 어찌나 대단하게 여기며 그의 견해를 인용하는 버릇이 들었던지, 가르치기로 정해 놓은 그 한 시간 가운데 순전한 공부에 쓰이는 시간이 대체 얼마나 될까 하는 것이 집안의 자그마한 농담이 되었다. 그 시간의 그토록 많은 부분이 이야기로 흘러가 버린 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n마거릿은 아버지와 손턴 씨의 교분을 이렇게 가볍고 유쾌하게 바라보는 태도를 오히려 부추겼다. 어머니가 남편의 이 새로운 우정을 질투 어린 눈으로 바라보는 기색이 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 헬스톤에서처럼 남편의 시간이 오로지 책과 교구민에게만 쓰이는 동안에는 그를 자주 보든 못 보든 어머니는 별로 개의치 않는 듯했다. 그러나 이제 남편이 손턴 씨와의 만남이 거듭될 때마다 그것을 애타게 기다리자, 어머니는 처음으로 자기와 함께 있는 것을 남편이 가벼이 여기기라도 한 듯 상하고 언짢아하는 것 같았다. 헤일 씨의 지나친 칭찬은 듣는 이들에게 지나친 칭찬이 늘 미치는 그 효과를 냈다. 아리스티데스를 언제나 의인이라 부르는 데 대해 그들은 조금쯤 반기를 들고 싶어졌던 것이다.\n\n시골 목사관에서 이십 년 넘게 조용히 살아온 헤일 씨에게는 엄청난 어려움을 손쉽게 정복해 내는 그 활력에 눈부신 데가 있었다. 밀턴의 기계가 지닌 힘, 밀턴 사람들이 지닌 힘은 그에게 웅장함이라는 느낌을 새겨 주었고, 그는 그 힘이 어떻게 쓰이는지 낱낱이 캐물을 마음도 없이 거기에 굴복했다. 그러나 마거릿은 기계와 사람들 사이로 나다니는 일이 적었고, 그 힘이 공적으로 드러나는 모습을 덜 보았다. 그리고 마침 그녀는 수많은 사람에게 영향을 미치는 온갖 조치 속에서 다수의 이익을 위해 뼈아프게 고통받을 수밖에 없는 한둘의 사람과 마주치게 되었다. 언제나 문제는 이것이다. 이런 예외적인 이들의 고통을 되도록 작게 하기 위해 모든 것이 다 이루어졌는가? 아니면 몰려가는 행렬의 개가 속에서, 힘없는 이들은 정복자가 나아가는 길에서 부드럽게 옆으로 들어 옮겨지는 대신 짓밟히고 말았는가? 그 행렬을 따라 걸을 힘조차 없는 이들이 말이다.\n\n딕슨을 도울 하녀를 찾아 나서는 일은 마거릿의 몫이 되었다. 처음에는 딕슨이 집안의 궂은일을 도맡을 딱 알맞은 사람을 자기가 찾아내겠다고 나섰던 터였다. 그러나 쓸모 있는 처녀아이에 대한 딕슨의 생각은 헬스톤 학교의 얌전한 상급생들에 대한 기억에 뿌리를 두고 있었다. 그 아이들은 바쁜 날 목사관에 오라는 말을 들으면 더없이 자랑스러워했고, 헤일 씨 부부에게 바치는 것과 똑같은 공경에다 훨씬 더한 두려움까지 얹어 딕슨 부인을 대했다. 딕슨은 자기에게 바쳐지는 그 경외 어린 공경을 모르지 않았고, 싫어하지도 않았다. 루이 14세가 자신의 눈부신 빛 앞에서 신하들이 눈을 가리는 것에 우쭐했듯 그것은 그녀를 자못 우쭐하게 했다. 그러나 하녀 자리에 지원한 밀턴 처녀아이들이 자격을 묻는 그녀의 물음에 대꾸하는 그 거칠고 독립적인 태도를 견디게 한 것은 헤일 부인을 향한 충직한 사랑 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 아이들은 심지어 되받아 캐묻기까지 했다. 일 년에 30파운드짜리 집에 살면서도 거드름을 피우고 하녀를 둘이나 두는, 게다가 그중 하나는 그토록 도도하고 위세 부리는 집안이 과연 셈을 치를 형편이 되는지 저희 나름의 의심과 걱정을 품었던 것이다. 헤일 씨는 더 이상 헬스톤의 교구 목사로 여겨지지 않고, 다만 어느 정도의 돈을 쓰는 사람으로만 여겨졌다. 마거릿은 딕슨이 이 하녀 지망생들의 행실에 대해 끊임없이 헤일 부인에게 늘어놓는 이야기에 지치고 진저리가 났다. 마거릿이 이 사람들의 거칠고 무례한 태도에 밀려나지 않았다는 말은 아니다. 스스럼없이 대뜸 말을 붙이는 데에 까다로운 자존심으로 움츠러들지 않았다는 말도 아니고, 밀턴에 살면서도 무슨 장사에 매여 있지 않은 집안의 형편과 지위에 대해 드러내 놓고 캐묻는 그들의 호기심에 몹시 언짢아하지 않았다는 말도 아니다. 다만 마거릿은 그 무례를 느끼면 느낄수록 그 일에 대해 입을 다물기 쉬웠다. 그리고 어쨌든 그녀가 하녀 구하는 일을 스스로 떠맡는다면, 그 모든 실망과 상상이든 실제든 모욕당한 이야기를 어머니가 듣지 않아도 되게 할 수 있었다.\n\n그리하여 마거릿은 더할 나위 없는 처녀아이 하나를 찾아 푸줏간과 식료품 가게를 오르내렸고, 공장에서 일하는 편이 삯도 좋고 훨씬 자유롭다는 이유로 그것을 마다할 사람을 공업 도시에서 만나기가 얼마나 어려운지 알아 가면서 매주 바라는 바와 기대를 낮추어 갔다. 이 바쁘고 부산한 곳에서 혼자 나다니는 것은 마거릿에게 제법 시련이었다. 쇼 부인은 법도에 대한 생각과 남에게 기대는 자신의 무력함 탓에, 이디스와 마거릿이 할리 가나 바로 가까운 동네를 벗어나면 반드시 하인 하나가 따라붙어야 한다고 늘 고집했다. 이모의 그 규칙이 마거릿의 자유를 옭아매던 그 울타리에 그녀는 그때도 말없이 반기를 들었었다. 그리고 그 대비 덕분에 숲에서의 삶에서 누리던 자유로운 산책과 나들이를 갑절로 즐겼었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 거리낌도 두려움도 없는 걸음으로 나아갔고, 급할 때면 이따금 달음질로 터져 나오기도 했으며, 이따금은 잎이 우거진 뜰에서 노래하거나 낮은 덤불과 뒤엉킨 가시금작화 사이에서 날카롭고 반짝이는 눈으로 내다보는 야생의 것들에 귀 기울이고 눈길을 주느라 완전한 고요 속에 멎어 서기도 했다. 오로지 자기 마음이 이끄는 대로 움직이거나 멈추던 그런 삶에서 거리에서 지켜야 하는 고르고 점잖은 걸음걸이로 내려앉는 것은 시련이었다. 그러나 그 변화를 마음에 두는 자신을 그녀는 웃어넘길 수도 있었을 것이다. 그것에 더 심각한 성가심이 따라붙지만 않았더라면.\n\n크램프턴이 자리 잡은 쪽의 시내는 특히 공장 사람들이 오가는 길목이었다. 그들 주위의 뒷골목에는 공장이 많았고, 하루에 두세 번씩 남자와 여자의 물결이 거기서 쏟아져 나왔다. 마거릿은 그들이 들고 나는 시각을 알기 전까지 늘 그들과 맞닥뜨리는 운 나쁜 일을 겪었다. 그들은 대담하고 겁 없는 얼굴로, 특히 자기들보다 신분이나 지위가 높아 보이는 이들을 겨냥한 요란한 웃음과 농지거리를 던지며 우르르 몰려왔다. 거리낌 없는 그 목소리의 가락과 거리에서 지켜야 할 예의라는 통념 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 태도가 처음에는 마거릿을 조금 겁먹게 했다. 처녀아이들은 거칠기는 해도 악의는 없는 스스럼없음으로 그녀의 옷차림을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵했고, 감이 정확히 무엇인지 알아보려고 숄이나 드레스를 만져 보기까지 했다. 아니, 한두 번은 유난히 마음에 드는 물건에 대해 이것저것 묻기도 했다. 옷을 좋아하는 자기들의 마음에 여자로서 공감해 주리라는 것과 그녀의 상냥함에 대한 그 소박한 믿음이 어찌나 곧았던지, 그녀는 물음을 알아듣기가 무섭게 기꺼이 대답해 주었고 그들의 말에 살며시 미소로 답하기도 했다. 그녀는 아무리 많은 처녀아이들과 마주쳐도, 아무리 목소리가 크고 왁자하더라도 개의치 않았다. 그러나 그녀의 옷차림이 아니라 얼굴을 두고 똑같이 거리낌 없고 겁 없는 태도로 말을 던지는 남자 직공들은 두려워지기도 하고 발끈하게 되기도 했다. 자기 용모에 대한 말이라면 아무리 점잖은 것이라도 무례라고 여겨 온 그녀가 이 거침없는 사내들의 숨김없는 감탄을 견뎌야 했다. 그러나 바로 그 거침없음이야말로 그녀의 섬세한 마음을 다치게 할 뜻이 그들에게 없다는 것을 드러내는 증거였고, 그 어수선한 소란에 덜 겁먹었더라면 그녀도 그것을 알아보았을 것이다. 그 겁먹음에서 분노의 불꽃이 튀어나와, 그들 중 몇몇이 하는 말을 들을 때면 그녀의 얼굴이 새빨개지고 검은 눈에 불이 모였다. 그러나 그들이 하는 말 가운데는 조용하고 안전한 집에 이르고 나면 그녀를 언짢게 하면서도 웃음이 나게 하는 것들도 있었다.\n\n이를테면 어느 날, 여러 사내들을 지나쳐 온 뒤였다. 그중 몇몇은 그녀가 자기 애인이었으면 좋겠다는 그리 드물지 않은 찬사를 바쳤는데, 뒤에 처져 있던 하나가 이렇게 덧붙였다. “아가씨 고운 얼굴을 보니 오늘 하루가 다 환해 보이는구먼.” 또 어느 날은 그녀가 스치는 생각에 저도 모르게 미소 짓고 있는데, 허름한 옷차림의 중년 직공 하나가 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. “웃을 만도 하지, 아가씨. 그런 고운 얼굴을 가졌으면 누군들 안 웃겠소.” 이 사내가 어찌나 고생에 찌들어 보였던지, 마거릿은 그에게 미소로 답하지 않을 수 없었다. 자기 얼굴이 어떻든 그것이 유쾌한 생각 하나를 불러일으킬 힘을 지녔다는 것이 기뻤던 것이다. 그는 알아보았다는 그녀의 눈길을 알아차린 듯했고, 그날그날의 우연이 서로의 길을 엇갈리게 할 때마다 두 사람 사이에는 말 없는 인사가 오가게 되었다. 그들은 한마디도 나눈 적이 없었다. 그 첫 찬사 말고는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 그런데도 어쩐지 마거릿은 밀턴의 다른 누구보다 이 사내를 더 마음에 두고 바라보게 되었다. 일요일이면 한두 번 그가 어떤 처녀아이와 함께 걷는 것을 보았다. 분명 그의 딸이었고, 있을 수 있는 일이라면 그보다도 더 몸이 성치 않아 보였다.\n\n어느 날 마거릿과 아버지는 시내를 둘러싼 들판까지 나갔다. 이른 봄이었고, 그녀는 남쪽의 그 흐드러진 꽃들에 대한 말 없는 아쉬움을 가슴에 품은 채 산울타리와 도랑가에 핀 꽃들, 개제비꽃이며 애기미나리아재비 따위를 꺾어 모았다. 아버지는 볼일이 있어 밀턴 시내로 들어가느라 그녀와 헤어졌다. 그리고 집으로 돌아오는 길에 그녀는 그 보잘것없는 벗들을 만났다. 처녀아이가 꽃을 물끄러미 바라보자, 마거릿은 문득 이는 마음에 이끌려 꽃을 내밀었다. 꽃을 받아 드는 아이의 옅은 푸른 눈이 환해졌고, 아버지가 아이를 대신해 입을 열었다.\n\n“고맙수, 아가씨. 베시가 저 꽃을 아주 소중히 여길 거요. 정말 그럴 거구먼. 나도 아가씨의 그 마음씨를 소중히 여기겠소. 아가씨는 이 고장 사람이 아닌 것 같은데, 맞소?”\n\n“네!” 마거릿이 반쯤 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “저는 남쪽에서 왔어요. 햄프셔에서요.” 그녀는 그가 알지 못하는 이름을 대었다가 그의 무지를 건드릴까 조금 조심스러워하며 말을 이었다.\n\n“런던 너머 아니오? 나는 번리 쪽에서 왔소. 북쪽으로 사십 마일이지. 그런데도 이렇게, 이 크고 그을린 곳에서 북과 남이 서로 만나 얼추 친구가 되었구먼.”\n\n마거릿은 걸음을 늦추어 사내와 그 딸 곁에서 나란히 걸었다. 두 사람의 걸음은 딸의 쇠약함에 맞추어져 있었다. 이제 그녀가 처녀아이에게 말을 건넸는데, 그때 그녀의 목소리에 서린 다정한 연민의 울림이 아버지의 가슴에 곧장 가서 닿았다.\n\n“몸이 그리 튼튼하지 않으신가 봐요.”\n\n“네.” 처녀아이가 말했다. “앞으로도 튼튼해지지 않을 거요.”\n\n“봄이 오고 있잖아요.” 마거릿이 유쾌하고 희망찬 생각을 일깨우려는 듯 말했다.\n\n“봄도 여름도 나한테는 소용없어요.” 처녀아이가 조용히 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 사내를 올려다보았다. 그에게서 무슨 반박이라도, 적어도 딸의 그 완전한 절망을 누그러뜨릴 만한 말이라도 나오기를 은근히 기대하면서였다. 그러나 그는 도리어 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n“내 딸이 참말을 하는 것 같아 두렵소. 저 애가 너무 멀리 시들어 버린 것 같아 두렵구먼.”\n\n“내가 가려는 곳에는 봄이 있을 거요. 꽃도 있고, 시들지 않는 꽃도 있고, 빛나는 옷도 있을 거고.”\n\n“가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!” 아버지가 낮은 소리로 말했다. “나야 그런 게 있는지 잘 모르겠소만, 네게 위로가 된다면야, 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것. 가엾은 애비! 이제 곧이겠구먼.”\n\n마거릿은 그의 말에 충격을 받았다. 충격을 받았으나 물러서지는 않았다. 오히려 마음이 끌리고 관심이 갔다.\n\n“어디 사세요? 이 길에서 이토록 자주 마주치는 걸 보면 우리는 이웃인가 봐요.”\n\n“프랜시스 가 9번지에 묵고 있소. 황금 용 여관을 지나서 왼쪽으로 두 번째 골목이오.”\n\n“성함은요? 잊으면 안 되니까요.”\n\n“내 이름을 부끄러워할 것 없소. 니컬러스 히긴스요. 얘는 베시 히긴스라 하고. 그건 뭣하러 묻소?”\n\n마거릿은 이 마지막 물음에 놀랐다. 헬스톤에서라면 그렇게 물어본 다음에 이름과 사는 곳을 물은 가난한 이웃을 찾아가 볼 뜻이라는 것쯤은 말 안 해도 아는 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n“저는…… 저는 찾아뵐 생각이었어요.” 그녀는 문득 그 방문을 청하는 것이 조금 겸연쩍어졌다. 낯선 이에게 품은 다정한 관심 말고는 그러고 싶은 이유를 댈 것이 없었기 때문이다. 그것이 갑자기 자기 쪽의 주제넘은 짓처럼 여겨졌다. 그 사내의 눈에서도 그녀는 그런 뜻을 읽어 냈다.\n\n“나는 낯선 사람이 우리 집에 오는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않소.” 그러나 그녀의 붉어진 얼굴빛을 보고는 마음이 누그러져 그가 덧붙였다. “아가씨는 말하자면 타관 사람이고, 여기서 아는 이도 별로 없겠지. 게다가 우리 딸애한테 손수 꽃을 주지 않았소. 오고 싶으면 오시구려.”\n\n마거릿은 이 대답에 반쯤은 우스웠고 반쯤은 언짢았다. 이렇게 은혜라도 베풀 듯 내주는 허락을 받고 찾아가야 할지 마음이 서지 않았다. 그러나 프랜시스 가로 꺾어 드는 길목에 이르자 처녀아이가 잠깐 멈춰 서서 말했다.\n\n“우리 보러 오시기로 한 거 잊지 마시우.”\n\n“그럼, 그럼.” 아버지가 성마르게 말했다. “이 아가씨는 올 거다. 지금은 좀 뻗대는 거야. 내가 더 곱게 말할 수 있었는데 싶으니까. 그래도 다시 생각해 보고 올 거다. 나는 저 도도하고 고운 얼굴을 책처럼 읽는다. 어서 가자, 베스. 공장 종이 울리는구나.”\n\n마거릿은 집으로 돌아가며 새로 사귄 벗들을 신기해했고, 자기 마음속에서 오가던 것을 꿰뚫어 본 사내의 눈썰미에 미소 지었다. 그날부터 밀턴은 그녀에게 한결 밝은 곳이 되었다. 그것은 길고 스산하고 볕 좋은 봄날들 때문도 아니었고, 세월이 그녀를 사는 곳과 화해시키고 있었기 때문도 아니었다. 그곳에서 그녀가 사람에 대한 관심을 찾아냈기 때문이었다.\n\n# 제9장\n\n“빛깔 고운 물감으로 짙어지고, 금으로 그려지고, 하늘빛 무늬가 뻗은 중국의 흙이여, 인도 잎사귀의 고마운 향기를, 아니면 모카의 볕에 그을린 열매를 기꺼이 받아들이라.” — 바볼드 부인.\n\n히긴스 부녀를 만난 이튿날, 헤일 씨는 여느 때와 다른 시각에 위층 작은 응접실로 올라왔다. 그는 방 안의 이런저런 물건 앞으로 다가가 그것들을 살펴보는 듯했지만, 마거릿은 그것이 그저 초조할 때 나오는 버릇임을 알아보았다. 말하고 싶으면서도 말하기가 두려운 무엇인가를 미루는 방식이었던 것이다. 마침내 그것이 튀어나왔다.\n\n“여보! 오늘 저녁에 차 마시러 오시라고 손턴 씨에게 청해 두었소.”\n\n헤일 부인은 안락의자에 몸을 기댄 채 눈을 감고 있었고, 얼굴에는 요즈음 들어 버릇처럼 자리 잡은 고통스러운 기색이 어려 있었다. 그러나 남편의 이 말에 그녀는 몸을 일으키며 투덜대기 시작했다.\n\n“손턴 씨라니요! 그것도 오늘 저녁에! 대체 그 사람이 무엇하러 여기까지 온답니까? 딕슨은 내 모슬린이며 레이스를 빨고 있고, 이 지긋지긋한 동풍 탓에 단물도 없는데. 밀턴에서는 이 바람이 일 년 내내 불겠지요.”\n\n“바람이 방향을 바꾸고 있소, 여보.” 헤일 씨가 창밖의 연기를 내다보며 말했다. 연기는 곧장 동쪽에서 흘러오고 있었지만, 그는 아직 방위를 제대로 알지 못해 형편에 따라 제멋대로 정하곤 했다.\n\n“그런 말씀 마세요!” 헤일 부인이 몸을 떨며 숄을 한층 단단히 여미고 말했다. “하지만 동풍이든 서풍이든 그 사람은 오겠지요.”\n\n“어머니, 그건 어머니가 손턴 씨를 못 뵈어서 하시는 말씀이에요. 그분은 맞닥뜨릴 수 있는 온갖 궂은 것과 싸우기를 즐길 사람처럼 보이거든요. 적이든 바람이든 형편이든 말이에요. 비바람이 거셀수록 그분은 틀림없이 오실 거예요. 그건 그렇고 저는 가서 딕슨을 돕겠어요. 저도 이제 풀 먹여 다리는 솜씨가 제법이랍니다. 그리고 그분은 아버지와 이야기하는 것 말고는 다른 심심풀이가 필요 없으실 거예요. 아버지, 저는 정말이지 아버지의 다몬에게 짝이 되는 피티아스를 보고 싶어요. 아시다시피 저는 그분을 딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐이고, 그때는 서로 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 쩔쩔매느라 그리 잘 어울리지 못했잖아요.”\n\n“네가 그 사람을 좋아하게 될지, 상냥한 사람이라고 여기게 될지는 모르겠구나, 마거릿. 그는 부인네들 비위를 맞추는 사람이 아니야.”\n\n마거릿은 경멸하는 듯한 곡선으로 목을 젖혔다.\n\n“저는 부인네들 비위나 맞추는 남자를 딱히 좋아하지 않아요, 아버지. 하지만 손턴 씨는 아버지의 벗으로 오시는 거잖아요. 아버지의 진가를 알아본 분으로서요.”\n\n“밀턴에서 유일한 사람이지요.” 헤일 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러니 우리도 그분을 반갑게 맞아 드려요. 코코넛 과자도 좀 내고요. 딕슨한테 만들어 달라고 부탁하면 우쭐해할 거예요. 그리고 어머니 모자는 제가 다려 놓겠어요.”\n\n그날 아침 마거릿은 손턴 씨가 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으면 하고 몇 번이나 바랐다. 그녀는 다른 일거리를 계획해 두었던 것이다. 이디스에게 보낼 편지, 단테를 한 대목 읽는 일, 히긴스네 방문. 그러나 그 대신 그녀는 딕슨의 푸념을 들어 주며 다림질만 해 댔다. 넘칠 만큼 맞장구를 쳐 주면 딕슨이 그 서러운 사연을 헤일 부인에게까지 늘어놓지 않으리라 바랄 뿐이었다. 이따금 마거릿은 아버지가 손턴 씨를 얼마나 아끼는지를 스스로에게 일깨워야 했다. 슬며시 밀려드는 고단함의 짜증을 눌러야 했기 때문이고, 그 짜증이 요즈음 들어 곧잘 앓게 된 그 지독한 두통 가운데 하나를 불러오고 있었기 때문이다. 마침내 자리에 앉았을 때 그녀는 말할 기운조차 거의 없었다. 그래도 그녀는 어머니에게, 이제 자기는 빨래하는 페기가 아니라 숙녀 마거릿 헤일이라고 말했다. 자그마한 농담으로 한 말이었는데, 어머니가 그것을 진담으로 받아들이는 것을 보고는 부산한 제 혀가 어지간히 원망스러웠다.\n\n“그래! 내가 베러스퍼드 양이던 시절, 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 미인이던 시절에 누가 나더러 이런 말을 했다면 어땠을까. 내 자식이 반나절이나 비좁고 답답한 부엌에 서서 하녀나 다름없이 일을 해야 한다고, 그것도 장사꾼 하나를 제대로 맞이할 채비를 하느라 그래야 한다고, 게다가 그 장사꾼이 유일한……”\n\n“어머니!” 마거릿이 몸을 일으키며 말했다. “그렇게 생각 없이 한 말로 저를 벌하지 마세요. 저는 다림질도, 어머니와 아버지를 위한 어떤 일도 싫지 않아요. 바닥을 문질러 닦고 설거지를 하게 된다 해도, 저는 그 내내 나면서부터 자라기까지 숙녀인걸요. 지금은 잠깐 지쳤을 뿐이에요. 반 시간만 지나면 다시 똑같은 일을 할 수 있을 거예요. 그리고 손턴 씨가 장사를 한다는 것으로 말하자면, 그분도 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이잖아요, 딱하게도. 그분이 받은 교육으로는 다른 일에 그리 어울리지도 않았을 테고요.” 마거릿은 천천히 몸을 일으켜 자기 방으로 갔다. 바로 지금은 더 견딜 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n바로 그 시각, 손턴 씨의 집에서는 비슷하면서도 사뭇 다른 정경이 펼쳐지고 있었다. 중년을 훌쩍 넘긴 뼈대 굵은 부인이 을씨년스럽되 훌륭하게 꾸며진 식당에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다. 그녀의 이목구비는 체구와 마찬가지로 둔중하다기보다 강하고 굵직했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 하나의 단호한 표정에서 그에 못지않게 단호한 또 하나의 표정으로 천천히 옮겨 갔다. 그 얼굴에 이렇다 할 변화는 없었지만, 한 번 그것을 본 사람은 대개 다시 한번 보게 되었다. 거리의 행인들조차 고개를 반쯤 돌려 그 굳세고 엄격하고 위엄 있는 여인을 한순간 더 바라보곤 했다. 길에서 남에게 양보하는 법이 없고, 스스로 정한 또렷한 목적지를 향해 곧장 나아가는 걸음을 멈추는 법도 없는 여인이었다.\n\n그녀는 올이 닳거나 빛이 바랜 데라고는 한 올도 없는 두툼한 검은 비단옷을 훌륭하게 차려입고 있었다. 그녀는 더없이 고운 올로 짠 크고 긴 식탁보를 깁는 중이었는데, 이따금 그것을 빛에 비추어 보며 그 섬세한 손길이 필요한 얇아진 자리를 찾아냈다. 방 안에는 책이라고는 없었다. 매슈 헨리의 성경 주석만은 예외여서, 그 여섯 권이 묵직한 찬장 한가운데 놓인 채 한쪽에는 차 항아리를, 다른 쪽에는 등을 거느리고 있었다. 저 멀리 어느 방에서는 피아노 연습이 한창이었다. 누군가 살롱풍의 소품을 익히는 중이었는데, 어찌나 빠르게 치는지 평균 세 음마다 하나는 흐릿하거나 아예 빠졌고, 끝의 우렁찬 화음도 절반은 틀렸다. 그렇다고 치는 이의 흡족함이 덜해지지는 않았지만. 손턴 부인은 단호한 성격에서 제 것을 닮은 발소리가 식당 문 앞을 지나가는 것을 들었다.\n\n“존! 너냐?”\n\n아들이 문을 열고 모습을 보였다.\n\n“무슨 일로 이렇게 일찍 왔느냐? 벨 씨의 그 친구, 헤일 씨라는 사람과 차를 마시러 가는 줄 알았는데.”\n\n“가는 길입니다, 어머니. 옷을 갈아입으러 온 거예요.”\n\n“옷을 갈아입어! 흥! 내가 처녀 적에는 젊은 사내들이 하루에 한 번 차려입는 것으로 만족했다. 늙은 목사와 차 한잔 마시러 가면서 무엇하러 옷을 갈아입는단 말이냐?”\n\n“헤일 씨는 신사이고, 그 부인과 딸은 숙녀입니다.”\n\n“부인과 딸이라! 그 사람들도 가르치느냐? 무엇을 하는 사람들이냐? 너는 한 번도 그들 이야기를 한 적이 없구나.”\n\n“안 했지요, 어머니. 저는 헤일 부인을 뵌 적이 없고, 헤일 양도 반 시간 보았을 뿐이니까요.”\n\n“한 푼 없는 처녀에게 걸려들지 않도록 조심해라, 존.”\n\n“저는 쉽게 걸려드는 사람이 아닙니다, 어머니. 어머니도 아시잖습니까. 하지만 헤일 양을 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것은 참을 수 없습니다. 아시다시피 그건 제게 거슬리는 일이니까요. 저는 지금껏 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 저를 낚으려 애쓰는 것을 본 적이 없고, 누군가 그런 쓸데없는 수고를 했으리라고 믿지도 않습니다.”\n\n손턴 부인은 그 점을 아들에게 순순히 양보할 마음이 없었다. 아니면 그녀에게는 대체로 여자라는 편을 위한 자부심이 넘칠 만큼 있었던 것이거나.\n\n“좋다! 나는 그저 조심하라는 것뿐이다. 우리 밀턴 처녀들이야 기개와 좋은 마음씨가 있어 남편을 낚으러 다니지 않을지도 모르지. 하지만 이 헤일 양이라는 아이는 귀족 티 나는 고장에서 왔지 않느냐. 소문이 다 사실이라면 그곳에서는 돈 많은 남편을 상품으로 친다더구나.”\n\n손턴 씨의 이마가 찌푸려졌고, 그는 방 안으로 한 걸음 들어섰다.\n\n“어머니.” 그가 짤막하게 경멸 어린 웃음을 흘리며 말했다. “기어이 제 입으로 털어놓게 만드시는군요. 제가 헤일 양을 본 그 단 한 번, 그 아가씨는 저를 도도한 예의로 대했고 거기에는 멸시의 기색이 짙게 배어 있었습니다. 그녀는 마치 자기가 여왕이고 제가 씻지도 않은 미천한 종복이기라도 한 것처럼 저를 멀리했지요. 마음 놓으세요, 어머니.”\n\n“아니다! 나는 마음이 놓이지도 않고 흡족하지도 않다. 국교회를 저버린 목사의 딸년이 무슨 자격으로 네 앞에서 코를 치켜든단 말이냐! 내가 너라면 그런 것들을 위해서는 옷 한 벌 갈아입지 않겠다. 건방진 것들 같으니!” 그가 방을 나서며 말했다.\n\n“헤일 씨는 선하고 온화하고 학식이 깊은 분입니다. 건방지지 않아요. 헤일 부인으로 말하자면, 들으실 마음이 있으시면 오늘 밤 그분이 어떤 분인지 말씀드리지요.” 그는 문을 닫고 나가 버렸다.\n\n“내 아들을 멸시해! 그 애를 제 종복 취급하다니, 원! 흥! 그런 아들을 대체 어디서 또 찾을 수 있는지 나야말로 알고 싶구나! 어릴 적에도 어른이 되어서도 저 애는 내가 아는 가장 고귀하고 굳센 심장이다. 내가 제 어미라서 하는 소리라 해도 상관없다. 나는 무엇이 무엇인지 볼 줄 알고, 눈이 멀지도 않았다. 나는 패니가 어떤 아이인지 알고, 존이 어떤 아이인지 안다. 그 애를 멸시해! 나는 그 계집애가 밉다!”\n\n# 제10장\n\n「우리는 흔들릴수록 더욱 단단히 뿌리를 내리는 나무다.」 — 조지 허버트.\n\n손턴 씨는 식당에 다시 들르지 않고 그 집을 나섰다. 시간이 좀 늦은 터라 그는 크램프턴까지 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다. 무례하게 시간을 어겨 새로 사귄 벗을 소홀히 대하는 일이 없기를 바랐던 것이다. 문 앞에 서서 딕슨의 굼뜬 움직임을 기다리는 동안 교회 종이 7시 반을 쳤다. 딕슨은 초인종에 응하느라 제 몸을 낮춰야 할 때면 늘 갑절로 느릿느릿했다. 그는 자그마한 응접실로 안내되었고, 헤일 씨가 반갑게 맞아 부인에게로 데려갔다. 부인의 창백한 얼굴과 숄을 두른 몸매가 그 차고 나른한 인사를 말없이 변명해 주었다. 그가 들어섰을 때 마거릿은 등불을 켜고 있었다. 어둠이 내려앉고 있었던 것이다. 등불은 어스름한 방 한가운데로 고운 빛을 던졌다. 시골 습관대로 그들은 그 방에서 밤하늘도, 바깥 공기의 어둠도 굳이 막아 두지 않았다. 어쩐지 그 방은 그가 조금 전에 떠나온 방과 견주어졌다. 그쪽은 번듯하고 육중했으나, 어머니가 앉는 그 한 자리를 빼고는 여자가 사는 흔적이라고는 없었고, 먹고 마시는 것 말고 다른 일에 쓸 만한 편의라고는 하나도 없었다. 하기야 그곳은 식당이었다. 어머니가 거기 앉기를 좋아했고, 그 뜻이 곧 그 집안의 법이었다. 그러나 그 집 응접실도 이 방 같지는 않았다. 그쪽이 두 배는, 아니 스무 배는 더 훌륭했다. 다만 아늑하기로는 4분의 1도 되지 못했다. 이곳에는 거울이 없었다. 빛을 되비쳐 풍경 속의 물과 같은 구실을 해 줄 유리 조각 하나 없었다. 금박도 없었다. 따뜻하고 차분한 빛깔이 넉넉하게 깔려 있었고, 정든 헬스톤 시절의 사라사 커튼과 의자 덮개가 그것을 잘 받쳐 주었다. 문 맞은편 창가에는 뚜껑을 열어 둔 책상이 놓여 있었고, 다른 창가에는 받침대 위에 키 큰 흰 도자기 꽃병이 있어 잉글랜드 담쟁이덩굴과 연둣빛 자작나무 잎과 구릿빛 너도밤나무 잎이 늘어져 있었다. 여기저기에 고운 반짇고리가 놓여 있었다. 겉장 때문에만 아껴지는 것이 아닌 책들이 방금 내려놓은 듯 탁자 하나에 놓여 있었다. 문 뒤에는 차를 위해 흰 식탁보로 차려 놓은 또 다른 탁자가 있었고, 그 위에는 코코넛 과자가 넉넉히 담겨 있었으며, 잎사귀를 깔고 오렌지와 발그레한 아메리카 사과를 수북이 쌓은 바구니가 놓여 있었다.\n\n손턴 씨가 보기에 이 우아한 손길들은 이 집안에 몸에 밴 것이었고, 특히 마거릿과 한 폭을 이루고 있었다. 그녀는 분홍빛이 많이 도는 밝은 색 모슬린 드레스 차림으로 찻상 곁에 서 있었다. 그녀는 대화에 마음을 두지 않고 오직 찻잔에만 골몰한 듯 보였는데, 그 사이로 상아처럼 둥근 두 손이 곱고 소리 없이 사뿐사뿐 움직였다. 가느다란 한쪽 팔에 팔찌를 하나 끼고 있었는데, 그것이 자꾸만 둥근 손목 위로 흘러내렸다. 손턴 씨는 그 성가신 장신구를 도로 밀어 올리는 모습을 그녀 아버지의 말을 듣는 것보다 훨씬 더 열심히 지켜보았다. 그녀가 부드러운 살이 조일 만큼 참을성 없이 팔찌를 밀어 올리는 것을, 그러고 나면 그것이 느슨해지다가 마침내 흘러내리는 것을 지켜보는 일에 그는 홀린 듯했다. 하마터면 “또 흘러내리는군!” 하고 소리칠 뻔했다. 그가 온 뒤로 차 준비에 남은 일이 얼마 없어서, 먹고 마셔야 하는 의무가 이토록 빨리 다가와 마거릿을 지켜보는 것을 막는 것이 그는 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다. 그녀는 마지못해 부림을 받는 사람처럼 도도한 태도로 그에게 찻잔을 건넸다. 그러면서도 그가 한 잔 더 마실 때가 되었다는 것을 놓치지 않고 알아차렸다. 그는 그녀가 아버지를 위해 마지못해 해 주는 그 일을 자기에게도 해 달라고 청하고 싶은 마음이 들 뻔했다. 아버지는 사내다운 손으로 그녀의 새끼손가락과 엄지를 잡아 각설탕 집게 대신으로 삼았던 것이다. 손턴 씨는 아무도 보지 못하려니 여기며 두 사람 사이에 오가는 그 자그마한 무언극이 이어지는 동안, 그녀의 아름다운 두 눈이 빛과 반쯤의 웃음과 반쯤의 사랑을 가득 담고 아버지를 올려다보는 것을 보았다. 마거릿은 여전히 머리가 아팠고, 창백한 낯빛과 말수 없음이 그것을 말해 주었을 법도 했다. 그러나 그녀는 어색한 침묵이 길게 이어지면 아버지의 벗이자 제자이자 손님인 이가 조금이라도 홀대받았다고 여기게 하느니 차라리 자기가 그 틈을 메우겠다고 마음먹고 있었다. 그러나 이야기는 끊이지 않고 이어졌다. 찻그릇을 물린 뒤 마거릿은 바느질감을 들고 어머니 곁 구석으로 물러났고, 갑자기 빈자리를 채우라고 불려 나갈 걱정 없이 마음껏 생각에 잠겨도 되겠다고 느꼈다.\n\n손턴 씨와 헤일 씨는 지난번 만남에서 꺼냈던 어떤 이야기를 이어 가는 데 둘 다 푹 빠져 있었다. 마거릿은 어머니가 나직이 건넨 대수롭지 않은 한마디에 다시 지금 이 자리로 돌아왔다. 그리고 문득 바느질감에서 눈을 들다가, 그토록 뚜렷이 맞선 두 천성을 드러내는 아버지와 손턴 씨의 겉모습 차이에 눈길을 빼앗겼다. 아버지는 여윈 몸매여서 실제보다 키가 커 보였다. 지금처럼 다른 사람의 크고 육중한 체구와 나란히 놓이지만 않는다면 말이다. 아버지 얼굴의 주름은 부드럽게 물결졌고, 그 위로 자주 일렁이듯 떨리는 움직임이 지나가 온갖 흔들리는 감정을 그대로 드러냈다. 눈꺼풀은 크고 활처럼 휘어 있어 두 눈에 거의 여자 같다 할 나른한 아름다움을 주었다. 눈썹은 곱게 휘어 있었으나, 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈꺼풀이 워낙 커서 눈에서 한참 위로 밀려 올라가 있었다. 그런데 손턴 씨의 얼굴에서는 곧은 눈썹이 맑고 깊이 박힌 진지한 두 눈 위로 낮게 드리워 있었다. 그 눈은 불쾌하리만치 날카롭지는 않으면서도, 바라보는 것의 속과 알맹이까지 꿰뚫을 만큼 골똘해 보였다. 얼굴의 주름은 몇 줄 없었으나 대리석에 새긴 듯 단단했고, 주로 입가에 몰려 있었다. 그 입술은 살짝 다물려 있었고, 그 안의 이는 흠 하나 없이 아름다워서, 좀처럼 보기 힘든 환한 미소가 순식간에 떠올라 두 눈에서 빛나 나올 때면 마치 갑자기 햇살이 비치는 듯한 느낌을 주었다. 그 미소는 무엇이든 하고 무엇이든 감행할 각오가 된 사내의 엄하고 단호한 표정을, 아이들 말고는 좀처럼 그토록 거리낌 없이 대뜸 드러내는 법이 없는, 예리하고 정직한 그 순간의 즐거움으로 온통 바꾸어 놓았다. 마거릿은 그 미소가 마음에 들었다. 아버지의 이 새 벗에게서 그녀가 처음으로 감탄한 것이 그것이었다. 그리고 방금 눈여겨본 겉모습의 온갖 세부에 드러난 그 성품의 맞섬이, 두 사람이 서로에게 느끼는 것이 분명한 그 끌림을 설명해 주는 듯했다.\n\n그녀는 어머니의 털실 수예를 다시 매만지고는 자기 생각 속으로 돌아갔다. 손턴 씨에게는 그녀가 이 방에 아예 없는 것이나 다름없이 잊혀 있었다. 그만큼 그는 증기 해머의 그 어마어마한 힘과 섬세한 조절을 헤일 씨에게 설명하는 데 골몰해 있었다. 그 이야기는 헤일 씨에게 아라비안나이트에 나오는 순종하는 정령들의 그 놀라운 이야기를 떠올리게 했다. 한순간에는 땅에서 하늘까지 뻗어 지평선 너비를 온통 채우다가, 다음 순간에는 고분고분 오그라들어 아이의 손에 들릴 만큼 작은 항아리 속으로 들어가는 그 정령들 말이다.\n\n“힘에 대한 이 상상, 거대한 생각의 이 실제적 구현이 우리 좋은 도시의 한 사람 머리에서 나왔습니다. 바로 그 사람 안에는 자기가 이루어 낸 경이 하나하나를 딛고 한 걸음씩 더 높은 경이로 올라설 힘이 있습니다. 게다가 감히 말씀드리건대, 설령 그 사람이 사라진다 해도 그 자리를 메우고 나서서 모든 물질의 힘을 과학에 굴복시키는, 또 앞으로도 굴복시킬 그 싸움을 이어 갈 사람이 우리 가운데 숱하게 있습니다.”\n\n“그 자랑을 들으니 옛 시구가 떠오르는군요.”\n\n“‘내게는 잉글랜드에 100명의 대장이 있소.’ 그가 말했다. ‘하나같이 그 사람에 못지않은 이들이오.’”\n\n아버지가 시를 읊자 마거릿이 문득 눈을 들었다. 두 눈에 묻고 싶어 하는 놀라움이 어려 있었다. 대체 어떻게 톱니바퀴에서 체비체이스까지 흘러왔단 말인가?\n\n“제 자랑이 아닙니다.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “그저 있는 그대로의 사실이지요. 저는 이 도시에, 아니 차라리 이 고장이라고 해야 하겠군요, 그런 장대한 구상을 낳게 하는 절박함을 지닌 이 고장에 속해 있다는 것이 자랑스럽다는 것을 부인하지 않겠습니다. 저는 남쪽에서 선생님이 더 귀족적이라 부르시는 그 사회의 낡아 빠진 홈을 따라, 태평하고 느긋한 나날을 보내며 따분하게 번영하는 삶을 사느니, 차라리 여기서 애쓰고 고생하는 사람이, 아니 실패하고 아무것도 이루지 못하는 사람이 되겠습니다. 꿀에 발이 붙들려 일어나 날지 못하는 수가 있으니까요.”\n\n“잘못 아셨어요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 사랑하는 남부를 헐뜯는 말에 발끈해 그것을 감싸려는 애틋한 격렬함이 일었고, 그 바람에 두 뺨에는 핏기가, 두 눈에는 성난 눈물이 어렸다. “손턴 씨는 남부에 대해 아무것도 모르세요. 그 놀라운 발명을 억지로 끌어내는 데 꼭 필요하다는 장사의 도박 같은 기질에서 나오는 모험이나 진보가 그곳에 덜하다면, 흥분이 덜하다고는 말하면 안 되겠지요, 그렇다면 고통 또한 덜한 거예요. 저는 이곳 거리에서 무슨 옥죄는 슬픔이나 근심에 짓눌린 듯한 얼굴로 다니는 사람들을 봅니다. 그저 고통받는 사람이 아니라 미워하는 사람들이지요. 남부에도 가난한 이들은 있어요. 하지만 제가 여기서 보는 그런 무언의 부당함에 대한 뿌루퉁한 감정이 얼굴에 그토록 무섭게 드러나 있지는 않아요. 손턴 씨는 남부를 모르세요.” 그녀는 이렇게 말을 맺고는 단단히 입을 다물어 버렸고, 그토록 많은 말을 한 자기 자신에게 화가 났다.\n\n“그러면 저도 헤일 양은 북부를 모르신다고 말씀드려도 되겠습니까?” 자기가 그녀에게 정말로 상처를 주었다는 것을 알아차린 그가 말할 수 없이 부드러운 목소리로 물었다. 그녀는 굳게 입을 다문 채였다. 저 멀리 햄프셔에 두고 온 그 사랑스러운 곳들이 사무치게 그리워, 입을 열면 목소리가 흔들리고 떨릴 것만 같았던 것이다.\n\n“어쨌든 손턴 씨,” 헤일 부인이 말했다. “밀턴이 남부에서 만나 볼 어떤 도시보다도 훨씬 연기 많고 더러운 도시라는 것만은 인정하시겠지요.”\n\n“깨끗함만은 포기해야 할 듯싶습니다.” 손턴 씨가 그 빠르게 번쩍이는 미소를 지으며 말했다. “하지만 우리는 우리가 낸 연기를 우리가 태우라는 의회의 분부를 받고 있습니다. 그러니 착한 아이들처럼 시키는 대로 하겠지요, 언젠가는 말입니다.”\n\n“그런데 굴뚝을 고쳐 연기를 태우게 하셨다고 말씀하지 않으셨던가요?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n“제 굴뚝은 의회가 이 일에 참견하기 전에 제 뜻으로 고쳤습니다. 당장은 돈이 나갔지만 석탄이 절약되니 되돌아옵니다. 법이 통과될 때까지 기다렸다면 과연 제가 그렇게 했을지 모르겠습니다. 어쨌든 저는 고발당해 벌금을 물 때까지 기다렸을 테고, 법이 허락하는 한도까지 온갖 애를 먹인 뒤에야 굽혔을 겁니다. 하지만 고발과 벌금에 기대어 시행되는 법은 그 방식이 하도 밉살스러워서 죄다 흐지부지되고 맙니다. 지난 5년 동안 밀턴에서 고발당한 굴뚝이 하나라도 있었을까 싶습니다. 여기서 이른바 의회를 어긴 연기라 부르는 그 꼴로 석탄의 3분의 1을 끊임없이 내뿜는 굴뚝이 더러 있는데도 말이지요.”\n\n“제가 아는 것이라곤 여기서는 모슬린 커튼을 일주일도 깨끗하게 두지 못한다는 것뿐이에요. 헬스톤에서는 한 달이 넘도록 걸어 두어도 그때까지 더러워 보이지 않았거든요. 손으로 말하자면, 마거릿, 오늘 아침 12시도 되기 전에 손을 몇 번이나 씻었다고 했지? 3번이었던가?”\n\n“네, 어머니.”\n\n“여기 밀턴에서 사업을 꾸리는 방식에 영향을 주는 의회법이며 온갖 입법에 아주 강하게 반대하시는 모양입니다그려.” 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다. 저 말고도 그런 사람이 많고요. 게다가 그럴 만하다고 생각합니다. 면업의 그 모든 기계는, 지금은 나무와 쇠로 된 기계를 말하는 것이 아닙니다만, 워낙 새것이어서 모든 부분이 한꺼번에 잘 돌아가지 않는다 해도 이상할 것이 없습니다. 70년 전에는 그것이 무엇이었습니까? 그런데 지금은 무엇이 아닙니까? 다듬어지지 않은 거친 재료들이 한데 모였습니다. 교육과 신분으로 보아 같은 자리에 있던 사람들이 갑자기 공장주와 직공이라는 서로 다른 자리를 차지하게 되었지요. 기회와 가망을 알아보는 타고난 눈치가 몇몇을 남달리 만들었고, 리처드 아크라이트 경의 그 투박한 모형 안에 얼마나 큰 앞날이 숨어 있는지 멀리 내다보게 해 준 덕분입니다. 새 업종이라 할 만한 것이 빠르게 자라나면서 그 초창기 공장주들에게 어마어마한 부와 권세를 안겨 주었습니다. 직공들에 대해서만 하는 말이 아닙니다. 사는 이들에 대해, 온 세계의 시장에 대해 하는 말입니다. 이를테면 50년도 채 되지 않은 옛날에 밀턴의 어느 신문에 실린 광고를 하나 말씀드리지요. 아무개가, 당시 예닐곱 되던 옥양목 날염업자 가운데 하나입니다만, 날마다 정오에 창고 문을 닫겠으니 사려는 사람은 모두 그 전에 오라는 내용이었습니다. 언제 팔고 언제 팔지 않을지를 그런 식으로 통고하는 사람을 한번 생각해 보십시오. 지금은 좋은 손님이 한밤중에 오겠다고 하면 제가 자리에서 일어나 모자를 손에 들고 서서 주문을 받으리라 봅니다.”\n\n마거릿은 입술을 삐죽 올렸으나, 어쩐지 듣지 않을 수 없었다. 더는 제 생각 속으로 물러나 있을 수가 없었다.\n\n“이런 이야기를 꺼내는 것은 이 세기가 시작될 무렵 공장주들이 거의 무한에 가까운 힘을 가지고 있었다는 것을 보여 드리기 위해서일 뿐입니다. 그 사람들은 그 힘에 어지러워졌지요. 어떤 사람이 자기 사업에서 성공했다고 해서 다른 모든 일에서도 마음이 고르게 잡혀 있으리라는 법은 없습니다. 오히려 그 정의감과 소박함은 쏟아져 내리는 부의 홍수에 아주 짓눌려 버리기 일쑤였습니다. 그 초창기 면화왕들이 잔칫날이면 얼마나 거칠게 흥청거리며 살았는지 이상한 이야기가 전해 옵니다. 그들이 직공들에게 휘두른 횡포 또한 의심할 여지가 없고요. 헤일 선생님도 그 속담을 아시겠지요. ‘거지를 말에 태워 놓으면 지옥까지 달려간다.’ 그렇습니다, 그 초창기 공장주들 가운데 더러는 정말이지 거창한 폼으로 지옥까지 달려갔습니다. 사람의 뼈와 살을 말발굽 아래 짓밟으면서도 뉘우칠 줄 몰랐지요. 그러나 머잖아 되돌림이 왔습니다. 공장이 늘고 공장주가 늘자 사람이 더 필요해졌던 겁니다. 공장주와 직공의 힘은 한결 고르게 맞서게 되었고, 이제 그 싸움은 우리 사이에서 제법 공평하게 벌어지고 있습니다. 우리는 심판의 판정에도 좀처럼 따르지 않을 텐데, 하물며 일의 실상을 어설피 주워들었을 뿐인 참견꾼의 간섭이야 말할 것도 없지요. 설령 그 참견꾼을 의회라는 고등법정이라 부른다 해도 말입니다.”\n\n“그것을 굳이 두 계급 사이의 싸움이라 불러야 할 까닭이 있소?” 헤일 씨가 물었다. “그 말을 쓰시는 것으로 보아, 그것이 자네 마음에는 일의 참모습을 제대로 보여 주는 말이라는 것은 알겠소만.”\n\n“그렇습니다. 그리고 저는 그것이 신중한 지혜와 바른 처신이 언제나 무지와 무분별에 맞서 싸우기 마련인 것과 꼭 같은 필연이라고 믿습니다. 노동자도 제 노력과 처신으로 공장주의 힘과 자리에 오를 수 있다는 것, 사실 제 몸을 단정하고 절제 있게 다스리며 제 할 일에 마음을 쏟는 사람은 누구나 우리 쪽으로 넘어온다는 것, 그것이 우리 체제의 큰 아름다움 가운데 하나입니다. 언제나 공장주로 오는 것은 아니겠지만, 감독으로든 출납으로든 부기로든 사무원으로든, 권위와 질서 편에 선 사람으로 오는 것이지요.”\n\n“그러면 제가 제대로 알아들었다면, 무슨 까닭에서든 세상에서 스스로를 일으켜 세우지 못한 사람은 모두 손턴 씨의 적이라고 여기신다는 말씀이군요.” 마거릿이 맑고 차가운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“제 적이 아니라 자기 자신의 적이지요, 분명히.” 그가 얼른 대답했다. 그녀의 말투와 표현에 배어 있는 도도한 못마땅함에 적잖이 부아가 났던 것이다. 그러나 이내 그의 곧은 정직함이 그로 하여금 그 말이 그녀의 말에 대한 옹색하고 말장난 같은 대답일 뿐임을 깨닫게 했다. 그녀가 아무리 업신여기든, 자기가 정말로 뜻한 바를 될 수 있는 대로 참되게 밝히는 것은 스스로에게 진 빚이었다. 그러나 그녀의 풀이를 떼어 내어 자기 뜻과 갈라 두기란 몹시 어려웠다. 하고 싶은 말을 가장 잘 보여 주는 길은 자기 삶의 이야기를 조금 들려주는 것이었다. 그러나 그것은 낯선 이들에게 말하기에는 너무 사사로운 이야기가 아닐까? 그래도 그것이 제 뜻을 밝히는 단순하고 곧은 길이었다. 그리하여 그는 검은 뺨에 잠시 붉은빛을 돌게 한 그 수줍음의 기색을 밀쳐 두고 말했다.\n\n“근거 없이 하는 말이 아닙니다. 16년 전에 제 아버지가 아주 비참한 사정 속에서 세상을 떠났습니다. 저는 학교에서 끌려 나와 며칠 만에 어른이 되어야 했지요, 될 수 있는 데까지는 말입니다. 저에게는 좀처럼 받기 힘든 복이라 할 어머니가 있었습니다. 굳센 힘과 단단한 결심을 지닌 여자였지요. 우리는 밀턴보다 살림살이가 싼 작은 시골 읍내로 갔고, 저는 거기서 포목점에 일자리를 얻었습니다. 물건에 대한 지식을 얻기에는 더없이 좋은 자리였지요. 우리 수입은 주에 15실링이었고, 그것으로 3명이 먹고살아야 했습니다. 어머니는 살림을 꾸려 제가 그 15실링에서 3실링을 꼬박꼬박 떼어 두게 하셨습니다. 그것이 시작이었고, 그것이 제게 자기 절제를 가르쳤습니다. 이제 어머니께 그 연세에, 어머니 자신의 바람이라기보다는 연세에 필요한 만큼의 편안함을 마련해 드릴 수 있게 되고 보니, 저는 그때마다 어릴 적에 그런 가르침을 주신 어머니께 말없이 감사드립니다. 이제 제 경우를 두고 보면 그것이 운도 아니고 공로도 재주도 아니며, 그저 제대로 벌지 않은 호사를 업신여기도록, 아니 아예 두 번 생각조차 하지 않도록 가르친 삶의 습관일 뿐이라고 느낍니다. 그래서 저는 헤일 양이 밀턴 사람들 얼굴에 새겨져 있다고 하신 그 고통이, 지난날 어느 때인가 정직하지 못하게 누린 쾌락에 대한 마땅한 벌일 뿐이라고 믿습니다. 저는 제 몸을 함부로 놀리고 감각에 젖어 사는 사람들을 미워할 만한 가치가 있다고 보지 않습니다. 그저 그 사람됨의 옹색함을 딱하게 여기며 업신여길 뿐입니다.”\n\n“하지만 자네는 좋은 교육의 밑바탕을 지니고 있네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “지금 호메로스를 읽는 그 빠르고 신나는 맛을 보면, 자네가 그것을 모르는 책으로 대하고 있지 않다는 것을 알 수 있지. 전에 읽어 본 적이 있고, 그저 옛 지식을 되살리고 있을 뿐이야.”\n\n“그건 그렇습니다. 학교에서 더듬더듬 읽어 나갔지요. 그 시절에는 제법 괜찮은 고전 학생으로 여겨졌던 것 같기도 합니다. 그 뒤로 라틴어도 그리스어도 다 빠져나가 버렸지만요. 그러나 여쭙겠습니다. 제가 살아야 했던 그런 삶에 그것들이 무슨 준비가 되어 주었겠습니까? 아무것도 아니었습니다. 정말이지 아무것도 아니었지요. 교육이라는 점에서 보면, 그때 제가 지녔던 참으로 쓸모 있는 지식의 양으로는 읽고 쓸 줄 아는 사람이면 누구나 저와 같은 자리에서 출발하는 셈입니다.”\n\n“글쎄! 나는 동의하지 않네. 하지만 그 점에서는 내가 다소 책상물림인지도 모르지. 호메로스 시절 삶의 그 영웅적인 소박함을 떠올리며 힘을 얻지는 않았나?”\n\n“조금도 아닙니다!” 손턴 씨가 웃으며 소리쳤다. “저는 너무 바빠서 죽은 사람들 생각을 할 겨를이 없었습니다. 산 사람들이 빵을 두고 다투며 제 옆에 바짝 붙어 밀려들고 있었으니까요. 이제 어머니를 그 연세에 어울리고 지난날의 애씀에 마땅히 값하는 조용한 평안 속에 편히 모시고 나니, 저도 그 옛이야기로 눈을 돌려 실컷 즐길 수 있게 되었습니다.”\n\n“내 말이 제 신 파는 사람 눈에는 가죽만 한 게 없다는 식의 직업적인 마음에서 나왔는지도 모르겠군.” 헤일 씨가 대답했다.\n\n손턴 씨가 자리를 뜨려고 일어나 헤일 씨 부부와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 마거릿에게도 같은 식으로 작별 인사를 하려고 손을 내밀었다. 그것은 이 고장의 스스럼없고 허물없는 풍습이었다. 그러나 마거릿은 그럴 마음의 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그저 목례로 작별을 대신했다. 반쯤 내밀었다가 얼른 거두어들이는 그 손을 보는 순간, 그 뜻을 미리 알아차리지 못한 것이 못내 미안했지만 말이다. 그러나 손턴 씨는 그 미안함을 조금도 알지 못한 채 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 걸어 나갔고, 그 집을 나서며 이렇게 중얼거렸다.\n\n“저보다 더 도도하고 밉살스러운 아가씨는 본 적이 없다. 저 대단한 아름다움조차 저 업신여기는 태도 때문에 기억에서 지워져 버리는군.”\n\n# 제11장\n\n“우리 모두의 피에는 쇠가 들어 있다고들 하지, 한두 알갱이쯤은 어쩌면 좋은 것이리라. 그러나 그의 피는, 나로 하여금 모질게 느끼게 만드는 그 피는, 강철이 조금 지나치게 많은 듯하구나.” — 작자 미상.\n\n“마거릿!” 손님을 아래층까지 배웅하고 돌아온 헤일 씨가 말했다. “손턴 씨가 가게 점원 노릇을 했다고 털어놓을 때, 나는 적잖이 조마조마한 마음으로 네 얼굴을 지켜볼 수밖에 없더구나. 벨 씨한테서 진작 다 들어 알고 있었으니 무슨 말이 나올지는 짐작하고 있었다만, 네가 자리에서 일어나 방을 나가 버리지나 않을까 반쯤은 그렇게 여겼단다.”\n\n“어머, 아버지! 저를 그토록 어리석은 아이로 여기셨다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요? 저는 정말이지 그분이 하신 말씀 가운데 자기 자신에 대한 그 이야기가 무엇보다 마음에 들었어요. 다른 것은 다 그 모진 데가 거슬렸지만, 자기 이야기만은 어찌나 담백하게 하시던지요. 가게 사람들을 천박하게 만드는 그 허세라고는 거의 없이, 어머니에 대한 그토록 애틋한 존경을 담아서 말이에요. 그러니 제가 그때 방을 나갈 일은, 그분이 세상에 밀턴 같은 곳은 또 없다는 듯 밀턴을 자랑하실 때나, 사람들이 앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모르고 헤프다며 조용히 업신여기실 때보다 오히려 적었지요. 그 사람들을 달라지게 하려고 애쓰는 것이 자기 할 일이라고는 조금도 여기지 않으시면서요. 어머니가 당신에게 주신 그 가르침을, 지금의 자리가 어떤 것이든 그 자리를 얻게 해 준 것이 분명한 그 가르침을 그 사람들에게도 조금은 나누어 줄 생각은 없이 말이에요. 아니에요! 가게 점원이었다는 그 말씀이야말로 제가 무엇보다 마음에 들어 한 것이었어요.”\n\n“네가 놀랍구나, 마거릿.” 어머니가 말했다. “헬스톤에서는 늘 사람들더러 장사치 티가 난다고 나무라던 네가 말이다. 헤일 씨, 나는 당신이 그런 사람을 우리한테 소개하면서 그가 어떤 사람이었는지 말해 주지 않은 건 온당하지 않았다고 봐요. 그 사람 말 가운데 몇 대목에는 정말이지 얼마나 놀랐는지 내색하지 않으려고 무척 애를 썼답니다. 아버지가 ‘비참한 처지에서 돌아가셨다’니. 아니, 구빈원에서였을지도 모르잖아요.”\n\n“구빈원에 있는 것보다 더 나빴을지도 모르오.” 남편이 대답했다. “여기 오기 전에 벨 씨한테서 그 사람의 지난 삶을 꽤 많이 들었다오. 그가 일부를 이야기했으니 빠뜨린 데는 내가 채워 주리다. 그의 아버지는 무모하게 투기를 하다가 망했고, 그 치욕을 견디지 못해 스스로 목숨을 끊었소. 예전 친구들은 하나같이 그의 부정한 노름이 드러날 판이 되자 몸을 사렸다오. 제 몫의 그리 대단찮은 재산을 되찾겠다고 남의 돈으로 벌인 무모하고 가망 없는 발버둥이었지. 어머니와 이 아이를 도우러 나선 사람은 아무도 없었소. 아이가 하나 더 있었던 모양이오. 딸아이인데, 돈을 벌기에는 너무 어렸지만 그래도 먹여 살려야 했지. 적어도 곧바로 나서 준 벗은 없었고, 손턴 부인은 뒤늦은 친절이 저를 찾아올 때까지 기다릴 사람은 아닌 듯싶소. 그래서 그들은 밀턴을 떠났다오. 나는 그가 가게에 들어갔고, 그 벌이에 어머니 앞으로 남겨진 얼마 안 되는 재산을 보태 오랫동안 식구들을 건사했다는 것까지는 알고 있었소. 벨 씨 말로는 그들이 몇 해 동안 오로지 멀건 죽만 먹고 살았다는데, 어떻게 그랬는지는 그도 모른다더군. 그런데 채권자들이 늙은 손턴 씨의 빚을 받을 가망을 접은 지 한참 뒤에—하기야 그가 스스로 목숨을 끊은 마당에 애초에 무슨 가망을 걸기나 했겠소만—이 젊은이가 밀턴으로 돌아와 채권자를 하나하나 조용히 찾아다니며 갚아야 할 돈의 첫 몫을 치렀다오. 떠들썩할 것도, 채권자들을 한자리에 모을 것도 없이 아주 조용하고 잠잠하게 해냈고, 끝내 다 갚았소. 채권자 가운데 하나가—벨 씨 말로는 성마른 늙은이라는데—손턴 씨를 일종의 동업자로 받아들여 준 덕을 크게 보기도 했고 말이오.”\n\n“정말 훌륭하네요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “그런 천성이 밀턴 공장주라는 자리 때문에 물들어 버렸다니 안타까워요.”\n\n“어떻게 물들었다는 게냐?” 아버지가 물었다.\n\n“아, 아버지, 모든 것을 부라는 잣대로 재는 그 태도 말이에요. 기계의 힘에 대해 말씀하실 때도 그분은 그것을 오로지 장사를 넓히고 돈을 버는 새로운 방법으로만 보시는 게 분명했어요. 그리고 그분 둘레의 가난한 사람들은요, 그 사람들은 못됐기 때문에 가난한 것이고, 그분 같은 쇠 같은 천성도 그것이 그분에게 주는 부자가 될 능력도 없으니 그분의 동정 밖에 있는 것이지요.”\n\n“못됐다고는 하지 않았다. 그런 말은 한 적이 없어. 앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모르고 제 욕심을 못 이긴다고 했지.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니의 바느질감을 챙기며 잠자리에 들 채비를 하고 있었다. 방을 막 나서려다 그녀는 머뭇거렸다. 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드릴 만하다고 여겨지는 고백을 하고 싶었으나, 그것을 온전히 정직하게 하려면 약간의 언짢음까지 함께 담아야 했다. 그래도 결국 말이 튀어나왔다.\n\n“아버지, 저도 손턴 씨가 아주 대단한 분이라고는 생각해요. 하지만 사람으로서는 조금도 좋아지지가 않아요.”\n\n“나는 좋더구나!” 아버지가 웃으며 말했다. “네 말대로 사람으로서도, 무엇으로서도 말이다. 그를 영웅이니 뭐니 하는 자리에 올려놓는 건 아니다만. 그건 그렇고 잘 자거라, 얘야. 오늘 밤 네 어머니가 몹시 지쳐 보이는구나, 마거릿.”\n\n마거릿은 얼마 전부터 어머니의 지친 낯빛을 근심스럽게 지켜보고 있던 터라, 아버지의 이 말에 어렴풋한 두려움이 무게처럼 가슴에 얹힌 채 잠자리로 올라갔다. 밀턴의 삶은 헤일 부인이 헬스톤에서 익숙하게 살아온 삶과 너무나 달랐다. 그곳에서는 언제든 신선하고 트인 바깥 공기를 드나들었다. 공기 자체가 이토록 달라서, 이곳의 공기에는 사람을 되살리는 기운이라고는 하나도 없는 듯했다. 살림살이의 시름은 이 집 여자들 모두에게 너무나 바짝, 그것도 이제껏 겪어 보지 못한 궁색한 모습으로 밀려들었다. 그러니 어머니의 건강이 심각하게 상하고 있는 것은 아닐까 두려워할 만한 까닭이 넉넉했다. 헤일 부인에게 무언가 잘못되었다는 조짐은 그 밖에도 여럿 있었다. 어머니와 딕슨은 침실에서 은밀한 의논을 나누었고, 그러고 나면 딕슨은 울면서 심통이 난 얼굴로 나오곤 했다. 안주인의 괴로움이 제 동정을 부를 때면 늘 그러던 버릇이었다. 한번은 딕슨이 나간 직후에 마거릿이 방으로 들어갔다가 어머니가 무릎을 꿇고 있는 것을 보았고, 살그머니 물러나면서 몇 마디를 얻어들었는데, 그것은 분명 모진 육신의 고통을 견딜 힘과 인내를 구하는 기도였다. 마거릿은 쇼 이모 댁에서 오래 지내는 동안 끊어져 버린 그 살가운 신뢰의 끈을 다시 잇고 싶어 애가 탔고, 부드러운 손길과 다정한 말로 어머니 마음의 가장 따뜻한 자리에 파고들려 애썼다. 그러나 예전 같았으면 기뻤을 만큼 넘치도록 손길과 다정한 말이 되돌아왔는데도, 그녀는 자기에게 감춰진 비밀이 있음을 느꼈고, 그것이 어머니의 건강과 무겁게 얽혀 있으리라 믿었다. 이날 밤 그녀는 아주 오래도록 잠들지 못한 채, 밀턴의 삶이 어머니에게 끼치는 나쁜 영향을 어떻게 하면 줄일 수 있을까 궁리했다. 딕슨을 늘 거들어 줄 하녀를 하나 구해야 했다. 온종일 그 일에만 매달리는 한이 있더라도. 그러면 적어도 어머니는 평생 익숙했고 또 필요로 하는 시중을 다 받을 수 있을 터였다.\n\n직업소개소를 돌고, 도무지 어울릴 성싶지 않은 온갖 사람들을 만나고, 그중 그럴듯한 사람은 몇 되지 않는 일로 마거릿의 시간과 마음은 며칠 동안 온통 붙들려 있었다. 어느 날 오후 그녀는 거리에서 베시 히긴스를 만나 걸음을 멈추고 말을 건넸다.\n\n“베시, 어떻게 지내요? 바람이 바뀌었으니 좀 나아졌기를 바라요.”\n\n“나아지기도 하고 안 나아지기도 했구먼요. 그게 무슨 말인지 아신다면요.”\n\n“잘 모르겠어요.” 마거릿이 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n“밤마다 기침에 몸이 갈가리 찢기지는 않으니 나아진 거지만, 밀턴이 지긋지긋하고 넌더리가 나서 뷸라 땅으로 떠나고만 싶은데, 거기서 자꾸 멀어지고만 있구나 싶으면 가슴이 내려앉으니 나아진 게 아니라 되레 나빠진 거우.”\n\n마거릿은 몸을 돌려, 힘없이 집으로 향하는 처녀와 나란히 걸었다. 그러나 일이 분 동안 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 마침내 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“베시, 죽고 싶어요?” 그녀 자신은 죽음 앞에서 움츠러들었기 때문이다. 젊고 건강한 사람에게 그토록 자연스러운, 삶에 대한 온갖 애착을 지닌 채로.\n\n이번에는 베시가 일이 분 동안 잠자코 있었다. 그러고는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n“댁이 내가 산 것 같은 삶을 살았고, 나만큼 그 삶에 넌더리가 났고, 이따금 ‘어쩌면 이게 50년이나 60년은 갈지도 모르지, 더러는 그러니까’ 하는 생각이 들었다면, 그리고 그 60년이 하나하나 눈앞에서 빙빙 돌며 그 긴 시간과 분과 끝도 없는 자투리로 나를 놀려 대는 것 같아 어지럽고 아뜩하고 속이 메스꺼웠다면, 아이고 아가씨, 내 말하지만 의사가 자네는 다음 겨울을 못 볼 것 같다고 했을 때 댁도 반가웠을 거우.”\n\n“아니, 베시, 어떤 삶을 살아온 거예요?”\n\n“남들 것보다 더 나쁠 것도 없지 싶수. 다만 나는 그것에 안달을 했고 남들은 안 했다는 것뿐이우.”\n\n“그런데 그게 어떤 것이었어요? 알다시피 나는 여기 낯선 사람이라, 밀턴에서 평생을 살았다면 모를까, 말뜻을 얼른 알아듣지 못할지도 몰라요.”\n\n“오시겠다고 하셨을 때 우리 집에 오셨더라면 아마 말해 드릴 수 있었을 거우. 하지만 아버지는 댁도 다른 사람들과 매한가지라고 하십디다. 눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어지는 사람이라고요.”\n\n“다른 사람들이 누군지는 모르겠어요. 그리고 나는 몹시 바빴고, 사실대로 말하자면 약속을 잊고 있었어요…”\n\n“댁이 먼저 하신 말이지, 우리가 청한 게 아니우.”\n\n“한동안 내가 한 말을 잊고 있었어요.” 마거릿이 조용히 말을 이었다. “덜 바빠지면 다시 떠올렸을 거예요. 지금 같이 가도 될까요?”\n\n베시는 그 말에 담긴 바람이 정말 진심인지 보려고 마거릿의 얼굴을 얼른 훔쳐보았다. 마거릿의 부드럽고 다정한 눈길과 마주치자 그 눈에 서렸던 날카로움이 애틋한 그리움으로 바뀌었다.\n\n“나를 마음에 두어 주는 사람이 그리 많지도 않으니, 댁이 마음이 있다면 오시구려.”\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 말없이 함께 걸었다. 지저분한 거리에서 벗어나 작은 골목 안뜰로 접어들 때 베시가 말했다.\n\n“아버지가 집에 계셔서 처음에 좀 퉁명스럽게 굴더라도 겁먹지 마시우. 아시다시피 아버지가 댁을 마음에 들어 하셨고, 우리 집에 와 주시는 걸 대단히 여기셨거든요. 그러니 좋아했던 만큼이나 심사가 상하고 뒤틀리셨던 거우.”\n\n“걱정 말아요, 베시.”\n\n그러나 그들이 들어섰을 때 니컬러스는 집에 없었다. 베시보다는 어리지만 키도 크고 힘도 센, 덩치 크고 칠칠치 못한 처녀가 빨래통 앞에서 부산을 떨며 거칠지만 야무진 손놀림으로 가구를 이리저리 부딪치고 있었는데, 그 통에 소리가 어찌나 요란한지 마거릿은 가엾은 베시가 딱해 절로 움츠러들었다. 베시는 걸어오느라 완전히 기진한 듯 문가의 첫 의자에 주저앉아 있었다. 마거릿은 동생에게 물 한 잔을 청했고, 그 아이가 물을 가지러 뛰어가는 동안(가는 길에 부지깽이를 넘어뜨리고 의자에 걸려 넘어졌다) 베시의 가쁜 숨을 덜어 주려고 모자 끈을 풀어 주었다.\n\n“이런 삶도 아낄 만한 것 같수?” 마침내 베시가 헐떡이며 말했다. 마거릿은 아무 말 없이 물잔을 그녀의 입에 대 주었다. 베시는 열에 들뜬 듯 길게 들이켜고는 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 눈을 감았다. 마거릿은 그녀가 혼자 중얼거리는 소리를 들었다. “그들이 다시는 주리지 아니하며 다시는 목마르지 아니하고, 해나 아무 뜨거운 기운에 상하지 아니하리니.”\n\n마거릿은 몸을 숙이고 말했다. “베시, 삶이 어떤 것이든, 또 어떤 것이었든 그렇게 조바심 내지 말아요. 누가 그 삶을 주었고 지금의 모습으로 만들었는지 기억해요!”\n\n등 뒤에서 니컬러스가 말하는 소리에 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다. 그가 들어오는 것을 알아차리지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“이보시오, 내 딸한테 설교하는 건 못 참겠구려. 그러잖아도 저 애는 꿈이니 감리교 냄새 나는 공상이니, 황금 문과 보석으로 지은 도시의 환영이니 하는 것들로 이미 몹쓸 지경이오. 그래도 그게 저 애 낙이라면 내버려 두겠소만, 거기다 무엇을 더 부어 넣는 건 두고 볼 생각이 없소.”\n\n“하지만 분명히요.” 마거릿이 돌아서며 말했다. “제가 한 말은 믿으시겠지요. 하느님이 저 사람에게 삶을 주셨고, 그 삶이 어떤 것이 될지도 정해 두셨다는 말 말이에요.”\n\n“나는 내가 보는 것만 믿고 그 이상은 안 믿소. 그게 내가 믿는 거요, 젊은 아가씨. 듣는 걸 다 믿지는 않소. 아니고말고, 어림도 없지. 어떤 젊은 처자가 우리 사는 데를 알아 놓고 찾아오겠다고 야단을 떠는 소리는 나도 들었소. 우리 딸애는 그걸 두고 어지간히 마음을 썼고, 내가 저를 보고 있는 줄도 모르고 낯선 발소리가 날 때마다 몇 번이나 낯을 붉히더구먼. 그래도 끝내 오셨으니 오신 건 반갑소. 아무것도 모르는 일로 설교만 하지 않는다면 말이오.”\n\n베시는 마거릿의 얼굴을 지켜보고 있었다. 이제 그녀는 무슨 말을 하려고 몸을 반쯤 일으켜 애원하듯 마거릿의 팔에 손을 얹었다. “아버지한테 언짢아하지 마시우. 아버지처럼 생각하는 사람이 한둘이 아니라우. 여기는 그런 사람이 숱하고 숱해요. 그 사람들 말하는 걸 들으시면 아버지쯤은 놀랍지도 않으실 거우. 우리 아버지는 참으로 드물게 좋은 사람이라우. 하지만 아이고!” 그녀가 절망에 잠겨 몸을 뒤로 떨구며 말했다. “가끔 아버지가 하는 말을 들으면 그 어느 때보다 죽고만 싶어지우. 알고 싶은 게 하도 많은데 궁금한 것에 이리 휘둘리기만 하니 말이우.”\n\n“가엾은 것, 가엾은 내 딸아, 너를 속상하게 하고 싶지야 않지, 정말 그렇지. 하지만 사내라면 진실을 위해 할 말은 해야 하는 법이다. 요즘 세상이 온통 그릇 돌아가는 걸 볼 때는 더 그렇지. 아무것도 모르는 일로 제 속을 끓이면서, 바로 코앞에 어질러진 채 놓인 일은 죄다 손도 대지 않고 있으니 말이다. 그러니 내 말하는데, 종교니 뭐니 하는 이런 이야기는 다 내버려 두고 눈에 보이고 아는 것부터 손을 대라 이 말이오. 그게 내 믿음이오. 단순하고, 멀리 가서 찾을 것도 없고, 지키기 어렵지도 않고.”\n\n그러나 처녀는 마거릿에게 더욱 간곡히 매달릴 뿐이었다.\n\n“아버지를 나쁘게 여기지 마시우. 좋은 사람이라우, 정말이오. 나는 가끔 아버지가 거기 없으면 하느님의 도성에서조차 슬픔에 잠겨 넋을 놓고 있겠구나 싶수.” 열에 들뜬 빛이 그녀의 뺨에 올랐고, 열에 들뜬 불꽃이 그녀의 눈에 어렸다. “하지만 아버지도 거기 계실 거예요! 꼭 그러실 거예요! 아, 내 가슴!” 그녀는 가슴에 손을 얹었고, 무섭도록 창백해졌다.\n\n마거릿은 그녀를 품에 안고 지친 머리를 제 가슴에 뉘었다. 관자놀이에서 가늘고 부드러운 머리카락을 걷어 올리고 물로 적셔 주었다. 니컬러스는 이것저것 필요한 물건을 가리키는 그녀의 손짓을 사랑이 주는 빠릿함으로 죄다 알아들었고, 눈이 동그래진 동생조차 마거릿의 “쉿!” 소리에 안간힘을 써 가며 조심스럽게 움직였다. 이윽고 죽음을 미리 알리는 듯하던 그 발작이 지나갔고, 베시는 몸을 추스르며 말했다.\n\n“자리에 누워야겠수. 그게 제일 낫지. 그런데……” 그녀가 마거릿의 옷자락을 붙들며 말했다. “또 오실 거지요. 오실 줄 알아요. 그래도 말로 한 번만 해 주시우!”\n\n“내일 다시 올게요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n베시는 아버지에게 몸을 기댔고, 아버지는 딸을 안아 위층으로 올릴 채비를 했다. 그런데 마거릿이 가려고 일어서자 그는 무슨 말인가를 하려고 애쓰더니 이렇게 말했다. “하느님이 계셨으면 싶구려. 그저 댁에게 복을 내려 달라고 빌기 위해서라도.”\n\n마거릿은 몹시 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 채 그곳을 떠났다.\n\n집에서는 차 시간에 늦었다. 헬스톤에서라면 식사 시간을 지키지 않는 것은 어머니 눈에 큰 잘못이었다. 그러나 이제는 이것도, 그 밖의 자잘한 흐트러짐도 화를 돋우는 힘을 잃어버린 듯했고, 마거릿은 차라리 예전의 그 잔소리가 그리울 지경이었다.\n\n“하녀는 구했니, 얘야?”\n\n“아니요, 어머니. 그 앤 버클리라는 사람은 도무지 안 되겠더라고요.”\n\n“내가 한번 해 볼까.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “이 크나큰 난제를 다들 한 번씩은 맡아 보았으니, 이제 내 차례를 다오. 어쩌면 끝내 유리 구두를 신는 신데렐라가 내가 될지도 모르지.”\n\n마거릿은 이 자그마한 농담에 웃음조차 짓기 어려웠다. 히긴스네를 다녀온 일이 그토록 그녀를 짓누르고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“어떻게 하시려고요, 아버지? 어디서부터 시작하시겠어요?”\n\n“글쎄, 살림 잘하는 안주인을 하나 찾아가서 자기나 자기 집 하인들이 아는 사람을 하나 천거해 달라고 하겠지.”\n\n“좋은 생각이에요. 하지만 그 안주인부터 붙잡아야 하잖아요.”\n\n“이미 붙잡았단다. 아니, 제 발로 덫에 걸어 들어오고 있으니, 네가 솜씨만 좋다면 내일 붙잡게 될 게다.”\n\n“그게 무슨 말이에요, 헤일 씨?” 궁금해진 아내가 물었다.\n\n“아, 마거릿 말마따나 나의 그 본보기 제자가 그럽디다. 자기 어머니가 내일 헤일 부인과 헤일 양을 찾아뵐 생각이라고 말이오.”\n\n“손턴 부인이!” 헤일 부인이 소리쳤다.\n\n“저희한테 이야기하시던 그 어머니 말인가요?” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“손턴 부인이지. 그에게 어머니라고는 그분 한 분뿐일 게다.” 헤일 씨가 조용히 말했다.\n\n“나도 만나 보고 싶구나. 예사롭지 않은 사람인 게 분명해.” 어머니가 덧붙였다. “어쩌면 우리 집에 맞을 만한, 또 이 자리를 반길 만한 친척이 있을지도 모르지. 어찌나 알뜰하고 살림에 야무진 사람 같던지, 같은 집안에서 나온 사람이라면 누구라도 좋겠구나.”\n\n“여보.” 헤일 씨가 놀라 말했다. “제발 그런 생각은 접어 두시오. 내 보기에 손턴 부인은 여기 우리 마거릿이 제 나름으로 그렇듯 자기 나름으로 도도하고 자존심이 센 사람이오. 그리고 그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 이야기하는 그 시절의 고생과 가난과 절약을 그분은 아예 없었던 일로 여기고 있소. 어쨌든 낯선 사람들이 그 일을 조금이라도 아는 것을 달가워하지 않으리라는 건 분명하오.”\n\n“그건 제 도도함과는 다른 종류라는 걸 알아주세요, 아버지. 제게 그런 게 조금이라도 있다면 말이지만요. 저는 인정하지 않는데도 아버지는 늘 저를 그렇게 나무라시잖아요.”\n\n“그게 그분의 것이라고 딱 잘라 말할 수 있는 것도 아니다. 다만 그에게서 주워들은 자잘한 것들로 미루어 그러려니 짐작할 뿐이지.”\n\n그들은 아들이 어머니에 대해 어떤 식으로 이야기했는지 물어볼 만큼 마음을 쓰지도 않았다. 마거릿은 다만 이 방문을 맞으려고 집에 붙어 있어야 하는지가 궁금할 뿐이었다. 그렇게 되면 이른 아침은 늘 집안일에 매여 있으니 베시가 어떤지 보러 가는 일이 늦은 시각으로 밀릴 터였기 때문이다. 그러고는 어머니에게 손님 접대의 무게를 온전히 떠맡길 수는 없다는 데 생각이 미쳤다.\n\n# 제12장\n\n「글쎄요, 아무래도 우리가 가야만 하겠지요.」 — 『회의하는 벗들』.\n\n손턴 씨는 어머니를 바라던 만큼의 예의 있는 태도로 이끌어 내는 데 적잖은 애를 먹었다. 그녀는 남의 집을 찾아다니는 일이 드물었다. 어쩌다 나설 때면 무겁고 엄숙한 격식을 갖추어 그 의무를 치렀다. 아들이 어머니에게 마차를 한 대 사 주었지만, 그녀는 그 마차에 딸린 말까지 두게 하는 것은 한사코 마다했다. 말은 그녀가 오전이나 저녁 방문을 다니는 엄숙한 행사 때에만 빌렸다. 채 2주도 되지 않은 얼마 전에 그녀는 3일 동안 말을 빌려 두고 아는 사람들을 하나도 남김없이 “해치워” 버린 참이었다. 이제는 그들이 차례로 수고와 비용을 치를 차례였다. 그런데 크램프턴은 걸어가기에는 너무 멀었다. 그래서 그녀는 헤일 집안을 찾아가 주기를 바라는 아들의 마음이 삯마차 삯을 치를 만큼 간절한 것이냐고 거듭 캐물었다. 그렇지 않다면 그녀로서는 고마울 일이었다. 그녀의 말대로 “밀턴의 온갖 교사며 선생들과 우정이니 친분이니 하는 것을 맺어 무슨 쓸모가 있는지 알 수 없었기” 때문이다. “그러다가는 다음에는 패니의 무용 선생 부인한테까지 인사를 다니라고 하겠구나!”\n\n“저라면 그러겠습니다, 어머니. 메이슨 씨 부부가 헤일 집안처럼 낯선 곳에서 의지할 데 없이 지낸다면 말입니다.”\n\n“아이참, 그렇게 성급하게 말할 것 없다. 내일 갈 참이야. 그저 네 뜻을 정확히 알아 두고 싶었을 뿐이지.”\n\n“내일 가신다면 말을 부르겠습니다.”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리, 존. 남들이 보면 네가 돈으로 지어진 줄 알겠구나.”\n\n“아직 그 정도는 아니지요. 하지만 말 문제만큼은 제 뜻대로 하겠습니다. 지난번에 삯마차를 타고 나가셨을 때는 덜컹거리는 통에 머리가 아프다며 돌아오셨잖습니까.”\n\n“나는 그런 일로 투정한 적 없다.”\n\n“그럼요! 제 어머니는 투정하시는 분이 아니지요.” 그가 조금 자랑스러운 투로 말했다. “하지만 그러니 더더욱 제가 어머니를 살펴 드려야지요. 저기 저 패니로 말하자면, 고생을 좀 해 보는 편이 오히려 약이 될 텐데 말입니다.”\n\n“저 애는 너와 같은 재목이 아니다, 존. 견뎌 내지 못할 게야.”\n\n손턴 부인은 그 말끝에 입을 다물었다. 마지막 말이 그녀를 부끄럽게 하는 문제와 맞닿아 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 약한 성품을 저도 모르게 경멸했다. 그런데 패니는 하필 어머니와 오빠가 강한 바로 그 지점에서 약했다. 손턴 부인은 이치를 따지기를 즐기는 여자가 아니었다. 빠른 판단과 굳은 결심이 스스로와 벌이는 긴 논쟁과 토론을 대신해 그녀에게 든든한 구실을 해 주었다. 그녀는 본능적으로 느꼈다. 무엇으로도 패니를 강하게 만들어 고생을 참을성 있게 견디게 하거나 어려움을 용감하게 마주하게 할 수는 없다는 것을. 딸에 대해 이렇게 자인하면서 그녀는 마음이 찔렸지만, 그것은 다만 딸을 대하는 태도에 가엾어하는 다정함 같은 것을 배게 했을 뿐이다. 어머니들이 약하고 병치레 잦은 아이를 대할 때 흔히 보이는 그런 태도와 크게 다르지 않았다. 낯선 사람이나 무심한 구경꾼이라면 손턴 부인이 자식들을 대하는 태도를 두고 존보다 패니를 훨씬 더 사랑한다고 여겼을지도 모른다. 그러나 그런 사람은 크게 잘못 안 것이다. 어머니와 아들이 서로에게 듣기 거북한 진실을 거침없이 내뱉는 그 대담함이야말로 서로의 영혼이 지닌 굳건한 중심에 대한 믿음을 보여 주는 것이었다. 반면 딸을 대하는 손턴 부인의 불안한 다정함, 그리고 자신은 저도 모르게 지녔고 남에게서는 그토록 높이 치던 온갖 훌륭한 자질이 제 아이에게는 없다는 사실을 감추려는 그 부끄러움— 되풀이하거니와 바로 그 부끄러움이야말로, 그녀의 애정이 마음 놓고 쉴 자리를 얻지 못했음을 드러내고 있었다. 그녀는 아들을 존이라는 이름 말고 다른 무엇으로도 부르지 않았다. “사랑아”, “귀여운 것” 따위의 말은 패니의 몫으로 남겨 두었다. 그러나 그녀의 마음은 밤낮으로 아들을 두고 감사드렸고, 그녀는 아들 덕에 여인들 사이에서 당당하게 걸었다.\n\n“패니야! 오늘 마차에 말을 매어 그 헤일 집안을 찾아가려 한다. 너는 유모를 보러 가는 게 어떻겠니? 같은 방향이고, 유모도 늘 너를 보면 그렇게 반가워하잖니. 내가 헤일 부인 댁에 있는 동안 너는 거기까지 가 있으면 되겠구나.”\n\n“아이, 어머니, 그렇게 먼 길인데다 저는 몸이 너무 나른해요.”\n\n“무엇 때문에?” 손턴 부인이 눈살을 살짝 찌푸리며 물었다.\n\n“모르겠어요. 날씨 탓인가 봐요. 어찌나 사람을 늘어지게 하는지. 유모를 이리로 데려오면 안 될까요, 어머니? 마차로 모셔 오면 되잖아요. 그러면 남은 하루를 여기서 보낼 수 있을 테고, 유모도 그편을 좋아할 게 뻔한걸요.”\n\n손턴 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다만 하던 바느질감을 탁자에 내려놓고 생각에 잠기는 듯했다.\n\n“밤에 걸어서 돌아가기에는 먼 길이겠구나.” 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아이, 하지만 제가 삯마차로 보내 드릴 거예요. 걸어가시게 할 생각은 해 본 적도 없어요.”\n\n바로 이때 손턴 씨가 공장으로 나가기 직전에 들어왔다.\n\n“어머니! 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다만, 병자인 헤일 부인께 도움이 될 만한 사소한 것이라도 있거든 챙겨 드리시겠지요.”\n\n“알아낼 수만 있다면 그러마. 하지만 나는 앓아 본 적이 없어서 병자들이 바라는 것에는 밝지 못하구나.”\n\n“그럼요! 그렇다면 여기 패니가 있지 않습니까. 어디 한 군데 안 아픈 날이 드무니까요. 뭔가 일러 줄 수 있을 겁니다. 그렇지 않니, 팬?”\n\n“저는 늘 아픈 게 아니에요.” 패니가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “그리고 저는 어머니와 함께 가지 않을 거예요. 오늘은 머리가 아파서 나가지 않겠어요.”\n\n손턴 씨는 언짢은 기색이었다. 어머니의 눈은 바느질감에 가 있었고, 그녀는 이제 부지런히 바늘을 놀리고 있었다.\n\n“패니! 나는 네가 가기를 바란다.” 그가 위엄 있는 어조로 말했다. “해가 되기는커녕 너한테 이로울 게다. 더 이상 이 일로 말하게 하지 말고 가 주면 고맙겠다.”\n\n이 말을 하고 그는 불쑥 방에서 나가 버렸다.\n\n그가 1분만 더 머물렀더라면 패니는 그 명령하는 어조에 울음을 터뜨렸을 것이다. “가 주면 고맙겠다”는 말을 쓸 때조차 그러했다. 그가 나가 버린 터라 그녀는 투덜거리는 데 그쳤다.\n\n“오빠는 늘 내가 아픈 척한다는 듯이 말한다니까요. 나는 정말이지 그런 척한 적이 한 번도 없는데. 오빠가 저렇게 야단스레 구는 그 헤일 집안이 대체 누구예요?”\n\n“패니, 오라비를 두고 그렇게 말하는 게 아니다. 저 애한테는 무슨 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 게지. 아니면 우리더러 가라고 하지 않았을 게다. 어서 채비나 하려무나.”\n\n그러나 아들과 딸 사이에 오간 그 작은 실랑이는 손턴 부인의 마음을 “그 헤일 집안” 쪽으로 조금도 더 기울게 하지 못했다. 시샘 많은 그녀의 마음은 딸의 물음을 그대로 되뇌었다. “대체 누구기에 저 애가 우리더러 이렇게까지 마음을 써 주기를 바라는 걸까?” 그것은 노래의 후렴처럼 자꾸만 되살아났다. 패니가 새 보닛이 거울에 비치는 모양을 살피는 즐거운 설렘 속에 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버린 뒤로도 오래도록.\n\n손턴 부인은 낯을 가렸다. 그녀가 사교계에 나설 여유를 얻은 것은 근래 몇 해에 지나지 않았다. 그리고 사교 그 자체로는 그것을 즐기지 않았다. 만찬을 베푸는 일로서, 또 남들의 만찬을 품평하는 일로서라면 만족을 느꼈다. 그러나 낯선 이들과 안면을 트러 가는 일은 전혀 다른 문제였다. 그녀는 마음이 편치 않았고, 헤일 집안의 작은 응접실에 들어설 때 여느 때보다 더 준엄하고 가까이하기 어려운 얼굴이었다.\n\n마거릿은 이디스가 곧 낳을 아기의 자잘한 옷가지에 쓸 고운 아마포 조각에 수를 놓느라 바빴다. 손턴 부인이 속으로 이르기를 “하늘거리기만 하고 쓸모없는 일감”이었다. 그녀는 헤일 부인의 두 겹 뜨개질이 훨씬 마음에 들었다. 그것은 그 나름으로 실속이 있었다. 방은 통틀어 자잘한 장식품으로 가득했는데, 먼지를 터는 데만도 한참 걸릴 물건들이었다. 그리고 수입이 빤한 사람들에게 시간은 곧 돈이었다.\n\n그녀는 헤일 부인에게 위엄 있는 투로 말을 건네며, 대개의 사람들이 감각을 눈감은 채로도 찾아낼 수 있는 그 판에 박힌 상투어들을 늘어놓는 동안 이런 생각들을 하고 있었다. 헤일 부인 쪽은 대답에 제법 더 공을 들이고 있었는데, 손턴 부인이 두른 진짜 오래된 레이스에 마음을 빼앗긴 탓이었다. 뒷날 딕슨에게 이르기를 “70년 동안 만들어진 적이 없어 돈을 주고도 살 수 없는 그 옛 잉글랜드 바늘 레이스”였다. “집안 대대로 물려받은 물건임이 틀림없고, 그분에게 조상이 있다는 증거지.” 그리하여 그 유서 깊은 레이스의 주인은 손님에게 상냥하게 굴어 주는 나른한 수고 이상의 대접을 받을 만한 사람이 되었다. 그렇지 않았더라면 헤일 부인의 대화 노력은 딱 거기까지에서 그쳤을 것이다. 그리고 곧 마거릿은 패니와 무슨 말을 나눌지 머리를 쥐어짜다가, 어머니와 손턴 부인이 하인 이야기라는 끝없는 화제 속으로 빠져드는 것을 들었다.\n\n“피아노가 보이지 않는 걸 보니 음악은 안 하시나 봐요.” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“좋은 음악을 듣는 건 좋아해요. 제가 잘 치지는 못하고요. 아버지와 어머니도 음악에 그리 마음을 두지 않으셔서, 이리로 올 때 쓰던 피아노를 팔았어요.”\n\n“피아노 없이 어떻게 지내시는지 모르겠어요. 저한테는 거의 살아가는 데 없어서는 안 될 물건 같은데요.”\n\n“주급 15실링에, 그중에서 3실링을 떼어 모았다지!” 마거릿은 속으로 생각했다. “하지만 저 사람은 그때 아주 어렸겠지. 아마 제가 겪은 일을 잊어버린 거야. 그래도 그 시절을 모르지는 않을 텐데.” 다음에 입을 열었을 때 마거릿의 태도에는 냉랭한 기운이 한 겹 더해져 있었다.\n\n“여기 음악회가 훌륭하다고 들었어요.”\n\n“아, 그럼요! 근사해요! 너무 붐비는 게 흠이지만요. 주최하는 분들이 아무나 다 들여보내거든요. 그래도 거기서는 가장 새로운 곡을 들을 수 있는 건 확실해요. 저는 음악회 다음 날이면 늘 존슨네에 잔뜩 주문을 넣는답니다.”\n\n“그럼 새 음악을 그저 새롭다는 이유만으로 좋아하시는 건가요?”\n\n“아, 런던에서 유행하는 곡이라는 걸 아니까요. 안 그러면 가수들이 여기까지 들고 내려오지도 않을 테고요. 물론 런던에는 가 보셨겠죠.”\n\n“네.” 마거릿이 말했다. “여러 해를 그곳에서 살았어요.”\n\n“어머! 런던과 알람브라, 그 두 곳이 제가 가장 보고 싶은 데예요!”\n\n“런던과 알람브라라니!”\n\n“네! 『알람브라 이야기』를 읽고 나서부터 죽 그랬어요. 그 책을 모르세요?”\n\n“읽어 보지 못한 것 같네요. 하지만 런던이야 아주 쉽게 다녀올 수 있는 길이잖아요.”\n\n“그렇긴 한데, 그게 어쩐지요.” 패니가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. “어머니는 런던에 한 번도 가 보신 적이 없어서 제 그리움을 이해하지 못하세요. 어머니는 밀턴을 무척 자랑스러워하시거든요. 저한테는 더럽고 연기투성이인 곳으로만 느껴지는데 말이에요. 어머니는 바로 그런 점 때문에 오히려 더 좋아하시는 것 같아요.”\n\n“손턴 부인께서 여러 해 동안 여기서 사셨다면, 그분이 이곳을 사랑하시는 마음도 저는 잘 알 것 같아요.” 마거릿이 맑은 종소리 같은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“나에 대해 무슨 이야기를 하고 있나요, 헤일 양? 물어봐도 될까요?”\n\n마거릿은 조금 뜻밖의 물음에 답할 말이 얼른 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 손턴 양이 대신 대답했다.\n\n“아이, 어머니! 저희는 그저 어머니가 왜 밀턴을 그토록 좋아하시는지 헤아려 보고 있었을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“고맙구나.” 손턴 부인이 말했다. “하지만 내가 태어나고 자란 곳, 그 뒤로도 여러 해 살아온 곳을 좋아하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이니 헤아려 볼 것도 없다고 생각한다만.”\n\n마거릿은 언짢았다. 패니가 그렇게 말해 놓고 보니 정말로 그들이 손턴 부인의 심정을 주제넘게 논한 것처럼 들렸다. 그러나 기분이 상했음을 저런 식으로 드러내는 그 부인의 태도에도 마음이 곤두섰다.\n\n손턴 부인은 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다.\n\n“밀턴에 대해 아는 게 있습니까, 헤일 양? 우리 공장을 하나라도 보셨나요? 우리의 그 웅장한 창고들은요?”\n\n“아니요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “아직 그런 곳은 하나도 보지 못했어요.”\n\n그러고는 그런 곳들에 대한 자신의 철저한 무관심을 숨기는 것이 정직하게 말하는 것이 못 된다는 생각이 들어 말을 이었다.\n\n“제가 마음이 있었다면 아버지께서 벌써 데려가 주셨을 거예요. 하지만 저는 공장을 둘러보는 데서 그리 큰 즐거움을 느끼지 못해서요.”\n\n“아주 신기한 곳이긴 하지요.” 헤일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 늘 그렇게 시끄럽고 지저분해서요. 언젠가 연보라색 비단옷을 입고 초 만드는 것을 보러 갔다가 옷을 아주 망쳐 버린 적이 있답니다.”\n\n“그러셨겠지요.” 손턴 부인이 짧고 못마땅한 투로 말했다. “나는 그저, 이 고장 특유의 사업이 지닌 성격과 그 발전으로 온 나라에서 손꼽히게 된 도시에 새로 와서 사시는 분들이니, 그 일이 이루어지는 곳들을 한번 보고 싶어 하실까 싶었을 뿐입니다. 듣기로는 이 왕국 안에서 그만한 데가 또 없다더군요. 헤일 양이 마음을 바꾸어 밀턴의 제조업에 궁금증을 내어 주신다면, 나로서는 날염 공장이든 바디 만드는 곳이든, 아니면 내 아들 공장에서 하는 좀 더 단순한 방적 작업이든 들어가 보시도록 기꺼이 주선해 드리겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 것이 없습니다. 기계라는 기계의 온갖 개량은 다 거기서 가장 완벽한 모습으로 볼 수 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“저는 아가씨가 공장이니 제조업이니 그런 것들을 좋아하지 않으신다니 참 다행이에요.” 패니가 반쯤 속삭이듯 말하며 어머니를 따라 일어섰다. 어머니는 옷자락을 사각거리며 위엄 있게 헤일 부인에게 작별을 고하고 있었다.\n\n“제가 아가씨라면 그런 것들을 다 알아 두고 싶을 것 같은데요.” 마거릿이 조용히 대답했다.\n\n“패니야!” 마차가 떠날 때 어머니가 말했다. “저 헤일 집안에 예의는 갖추도록 하자. 하지만 저 딸과 네 버릇대로 성급한 친분을 맺지는 마라. 너한테 이로울 게 없는 아이더구나. 어미 쪽은 무척 아파 보이고, 얌전하고 조용한 사람 같더라만.”\n\n“저는 헤일 양과 친해질 마음 없어요, 어머니.” 패니가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. “저는 그저 말동무가 되어 주고 즐겁게 해 주려고 애쓰는 것이 제 도리인 줄 알았을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“그래! 어쨌든 이제 존도 만족하겠지.”\n\n# 제13장\n\n“의심과 곤경도, 두려움과 아픔도, 그 모든 번민까지도 다 헛된 그림자일 뿐이며, 죽음 그 자체조차 끝내 남아 있지 않으리라는 것.\n\n우리가 고달픈 사막을 밟고 지날지라도, 음침한 미로를 더듬어 헤맬지라도, 어두운 땅속 길로 이끌려 갈지라도,\n\n그럼에도 우리가 오직 한 분 인도자께 순종하기만 한다면, 가장 스산한 길도 가장 어두운 길도 마침내 하늘의 빛으로 나서게 되리라는 것.\n\n그리고 지금은 서로 다른 기슭에 던져진 우리도, 위태로운 항해를 다 마치고 나면 마침내 아버지의 집에서 다 함께 만나게 되리라는 것!” — R. C. 트렌치.\n\n마거릿은 손님들이 돌아가자마자 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 보닛과 숄을 걸쳤다. 베시 히긴스가 어떤지 물어보러 달려가서 저녁 식사 전까지 되도록 오래 곁에 앉아 있을 참이었다. 붐비는 좁은 거리들을 지나가면서 그녀는 그곳에 사는 한 사람을 마음에 두게 되었다는 그 단순한 사실만으로 그 거리들이 얼마나 많은 흥미를 얻게 되었는지 느꼈다.\n\n칠칠치 못한 동생 메리 히긴스는 손님이 오기로 되어 있으니 나름대로 애를 써서 집 안을 치워 놓았다. 바닥 한가운데는 거친 돌로 문질러 닦아 놓았지만, 의자와 탁자 밑이며 벽을 두른 자리의 판석들은 씻지 않은 채 거뭇한 그대로였다. 날이 더운데도 벽난로에는 불이 크게 타올라 집 안 전체가 화덕 속 같았다. 마거릿은 석탄을 그렇게 아낌없이 때는 것이 메리 나름의 극진한 환대의 표시라는 것을 알지 못했고, 어쩌면 그 숨 막히는 열기가 베시에게 필요한 것인지도 모른다고 생각했다. 베시는 창 밑에 놓인 스쿼브라는 짧은 소파에 누워 있었다. 그녀는 전날보다 훨씬 더 기운이 없었고, 발소리가 날 때마다 몸을 일으켜 마거릿이 오는지 내다보느라 지쳐 있었다. 이제 마거릿이 와서 곁에 의자를 놓고 앉자, 베시는 말없이 등을 기댄 채 마거릿의 얼굴을 바라보고 그 옷가지를 만져 보는 것만으로 흡족해했다. 그 감의 고운 결에 아이 같은 감탄을 품고서.\n\n“전에는 성경에 나오는 사람들이 어째서 부드러운 옷을 그리 아꼈는지 몰랐구먼. 그런데 댁처럼 차려입는 건 참 좋은 일이겠수. 여느 것하고는 다르니 말이오. 곱게 차려입은 사람들은 대개 그 빛깔 때문에 내 눈이 아픈데, 댁의 것은 어쩐지 눈이 편해지는구먼. 이 옷은 어디서 났수?”\n\n“런던에서요.” 마거릿이 무척 재미있어하며 말했다.\n\n“런던! 댁이 런던에 있었수?”\n\n“네! 몇 해 살았어요. 하지만 우리 집은 숲속에 있었어요. 시골에요.”\n\n“그 얘기 좀 해 주우.” 베시가 말했다. “나는 시골이랑 나무랑 그런 것들 얘기 듣는 게 좋소.” 그녀는 몸을 뒤로 기대고 눈을 감은 채 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고서, 마거릿이 들려줄 온갖 것을 다 받아들이려는 듯 더없이 편안하게 누워 있었다.\n\n마거릿은 헬스톤을 떠난 뒤로 그곳을 입에 올린 적이 없었다. 그저 지나는 말로 지명을 부른 것이 전부였다. 그녀는 실제보다 더 생생하게 꿈속에서 그곳을 보았고, 밤이면 잠에 빠져들면서 기억이 그 정다운 곳들을 두루 헤매었다. 그러나 이 처녀에게는 마음이 열렸다. “아, 베시, 나는 우리가 떠나온 그 집을 정말 끔찍이도 사랑했어요! 당신이 그곳을 볼 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 그 아름다움은 반의반도 말로 옮길 수가 없어요. 집 둘레에 온통 큰 나무들이 서 있고, 그 가지들이 길고 평평하게 뻗어 나가 한낮에도 깊고 편안한 그늘을 드리워요. 그런데도 잎사귀 하나하나는 다 멎어 있는 것 같은데, 사방에서 무언가 끊임없이 술렁이며 움직이는 소리가 나요. 바로 곁이 아니라 저만치서요. 그러다가 어떤 데는 잔디가 벨벳처럼 보드랍고 곱고, 또 어떤 데는 가까이 숨어서 졸졸거리는 작은 시내가 늘 적셔 주어 아주 무성해요. 또 다른 데에는 물결치는 고사리가, 온통 고사리 벌판이 펼쳐져 있어요. 어떤 것은 푸른 그늘 속에 있고, 어떤 것 위에는 금빛 햇살이 길게 줄무늬를 이루며 누워 있죠. 꼭 바다처럼요.”\n\n“나는 바다를 본 적이 없구먼.” 베시가 나직이 말했다. “그래도 더 얘기해 주우.”\n\n“그리고 여기저기에 널따란 벌판이 있어요. 꼭 나무 꼭대기보다 더 높은 데 있는 것처럼 높다랗게요……”\n\n“그건 반가운 얘기구먼. 나는 저 아래에 있으면 숨이 막히는 것 같았소. 나들이를 나갈 때면 늘 높은 데 올라가서 멀리 바라보고 그 공기를 가슴 가득 깊이 들이마시고 싶었지. 밀턴에서는 숨이 막혀 죽을 지경이오. 그리고 댁이 말한 그 나무들 사이의 소리, 그게 끝도 없이 이어진다면 나는 정신이 아득해질 것 같구먼. 공장에서 내 머리가 그렇게 아팠던 것도 바로 그것 때문이었소. 그런데 그 벌판에는 소리가 별로 없겠구려?”\n\n“네.” 마거릿이 말했다. “여기저기 높은 하늘에 종달새가 있을 뿐 아무것도 없어요. 이따금 농부가 일꾼들에게 날카롭고 크게 소리치는 게 들리기도 했지만, 하도 멀어서 그저 어딘가 먼 데서 다른 사람들이 열심히 일하고 있구나 하는 생각이 기분 좋게 떠오를 뿐이었어요. 나는 그냥 히스 덤불에 앉아 아무것도 하지 않고 있었고요.”\n\n“예전엔 이런 생각을 했었소. 아무것도 안 하고 쉬는 날이 하루만 있으면, 댁이 말한 그런 조용한 데서 하루만 지낼 수 있으면 몸이 좀 나아지려나 하고. 그런데 이제는 한가한 날을 숱하게 보내고 나니, 일이 지겨웠던 만큼이나 그것도 지겹구먼. 어떨 땐 하도 지쳐서, 먼저 좀 쉬지 않고는 천국도 즐길 수 없겠다 싶소. 무덤에서 한숨 푹 자고 기운을 차리지도 못한 채 곧장 그리로 가는 게 좀 겁이 나우.”\n\n“겁내지 말아요, 베시.” 마거릿이 그녀의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. “하느님은 이 땅의 한가함이나 무덤의 죽은 잠보다 더 온전한 쉼을 주실 수 있어요.”\n\n베시는 불안하게 몸을 뒤척이더니 말했다.\n\n“아버지가 나한테 그렇게 말하지 않았으면 좋겠구먼. 어제도 말했고 몇 번이고 다시 말하지만, 아버지는 좋은 뜻으로 그러는 거요. 그렇지만 있잖수, 낮에는 그 말을 조금도 믿지 않다가도 밤이 되면, 열이 올라 반쯤 자고 반쯤 깬 채로 있으면 그게 도로 나한테 밀려오는데, 아이고, 그렇게 지독할 수가 없소! 그러면 이런 생각이 드는 거요. 이게 다 끝이라면, 내가 태어난 게 고작 심장과 목숨을 갈아 넣어 일하다가 이 지긋지긋한 데서 병들라고 그런 거라면, 그만 좀 멈추라고 조금만이라도 조용하게 해 달라고 비명을 지르고 싶어질 만큼 공장 소리를 귓속에 영영 담고 살라고 그런 거라면, 솜먼지가 허파를 가득 메워서 댁이 말한 그 맑은 공기를 한 번만이라도 길고 깊게 들이마시고 싶어 목이 타 죽을 지경이 되라고 그런 거라면, 게다가 어머니는 가 버려서 내가 얼마나 사랑했는지, 내 온갖 괴로움이 어땠는지 다시는 말도 못 해 드리는데, 이 삶이 끝이고 모든 눈에서 모든 눈물을 씻어 주실 하느님이 없다면 말이오. 이 사람아, 이 사람아!” 그녀가 몸을 일으켜 마거릿의 손을 사납다 싶을 만큼 거세게 움켜쥐며 말했다. “나는 미쳐 버릴 수도 있고, 댁을 죽여 버릴 수도 있소, 정말 그럴 수 있단 말이오.” 그녀는 그 격정에 아주 기진하여 뒤로 쓰러졌다. 마거릿이 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n“베시, 우리에게는 하늘에 계신 아버지가 있어요.”\n\n“알아요! 알고말고.” 그녀가 머리를 이쪽저쪽으로 불안하게 돌리며 신음했다. “나는 몹시 나쁜 사람이오. 아주 못된 소리를 했구먼. 아이고! 나 때문에 놀라서 다시는 오지 않는 일은 없게 해 주우. 나는 댁의 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 않을 거요. 그리고,” 그녀가 눈을 뜨고 마거릿을 간절하게 바라보며 말했다. “나는 앞으로 올 일에 대해서는 어쩌면 댁보다 더 많이 믿고 있소. 요한계시록을 하도 읽어서 외울 지경이오. 깨어 있고 제정신일 때는 내가 가게 될 그 온갖 영광을 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없구먼.”\n\n“열이 오를 때 머릿속에 떠오르는 공상 이야기는 하지 말기로 해요. 그보다 건강했을 때 무슨 일을 하며 지냈는지 듣고 싶어요.”\n\n“어머니가 돌아가셨을 땐 내가 성했던 것 같은데, 그 무렵 언저리부터 영 제대로 튼튼했던 적이 없구먼. 얼마 안 있어 소면실에서 일을 시작했는데, 솜먼지가 허파로 들어가서 나를 망쳐 놓았소.”\n\n“솜먼지요?” 마거릿이 묻듯이 말했다.\n\n“솜먼지 말이오.” 베시가 되풀이했다. “면화를 소면할 때 거기서 날아오르는 자잘한 부스러기 말이오. 공기 중에 가득 차서 온통 고운 흰 먼지처럼 보이지. 그게 허파를 칭칭 감아서 죄어 버린다더구먼. 어쨌거나 소면실에서 일하다가 몸이 쇠하는 사람이 한둘이 아니오. 기침을 하고 피를 뱉지. 그저 솜먼지에 중독된 거요.”\n\n“하지만 어떻게 손을 쓸 수는 없나요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“모르겠소. 어떤 데는 소면실 한쪽 끝에 커다란 바퀴를 달아서 바람을 일으켜 먼지를 빼낸다더구먼. 그런데 그 바퀴 값이 어마어마해서, 아마 500이나 600파운드는 들 텐데 벌이가 되는 것도 아니니 그걸 달아 주는 공장주는 몇 안 되지. 게다가 바퀴가 있는 데서 일하기 싫어한 사람들 얘기도 들었소. 오래도록 솜먼지를 삼키는 데 익어 놓으니 그게 없으면 배가 고파진다는 거요. 그런 데서 일할 거면 품삯을 올려 줘야 한다고 하더구먼. 그러니 공장주와 일꾼 사이에서 그 바퀴는 흐지부지되고 마는 거지. 그래도 우리 공장에 그 바퀴가 있었더라면 좋았을 거라고 나는 생각하오.”\n\n“아버님은 그 일을 모르셨나요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“알았소! 그리고 안타까워했지. 그렇지만 우리 공장은 대체로 괜찮은 데였소. 사람들도 착실하고 그만하면 반듯했고. 아버지는 나를 낯선 데로 보내기를 겁냈소. 지금은 그렇게 안 보이겠지만, 그때만 해도 나더러 꽤 참한 처녀라고 하는 사람이 한둘이 아니었으니 말이오. 게다가 나도 비실비실하고 무르다는 소리는 듣기 싫었고, 메리 학교는 계속 보내야 한다고 어머니가 그랬고, 아버지는 늘 책 사는 걸 좋아하고 이런저런 강연에 다니기를 좋아했는데 그게 다 돈이 드는 일이었소. 그래서 나는 그냥 일했고, 이제 이 세상에서는 귓속의 그 윙윙거리는 소리도, 목구멍의 솜먼지도 영영 걷어 내지 못하게 되었구먼. 그게 다요.”\n\n“몇 살이에요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“7월이면 19살이 되오.”\n\n“저도 19살이에요.” 그녀는 두 사람 사이의 대비를 베시보다 더 서글프게 곱씹었다. 그녀는 억누르려 애쓰는 감정 때문에 한두 순간 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n“메리 말인데.” 베시가 말했다. “댁이 그 애한테 벗이 되어 주었으면 해서 부탁하고 싶었소. 그 애는 17살인데 우리 중에 마지막 남은 아이요. 나는 그 애가 공장에 가지 않았으면 좋겠는데, 그렇다고 무슨 일에 맞을지도 모르겠구먼.”\n\n“그 애는……” 마거릿은 자기도 모르게 방 안의 치우지 않은 구석들을 흘긋 보았다. “하녀 자리를 맡기는 어렵지 않을까요? 우리 집에는 벗이나 다름없는 오래되고 충직한 하인이 있는데, 일손이 필요하기는 하지만 사람이 몹시 까다로워요. 정말로 성가시고 짜증스러운 일이 될 도움을 주어서 그이를 괴롭히는 건 옳지 않겠지요.”\n\n“그렇소, 알겠구먼. 댁 말이 맞소. 우리 메리는 착한 애지만, 살림살이를 어찌하는지 그 애한테 가르쳐 준 사람이 누가 있었겠수? 어머니도 없고, 나는 공장에 있었으니. 나야 내가 조금도 할 줄 모르는 일을 그 애가 서툴게 한다고 야단이나 칠 줄 알았지. 그래도 그 애가 댁네와 함께 살 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데.”\n\n“하지만 그 애가 하녀로 와서 우리와 함께 살기에 꼭 맞지는 않더라도, 그건 저도 잘 모르겠지만요, 저는 당신을 봐서라도 언제까지나 그 애에게 벗이 되어 주려고 애쓸 거예요, 베시. 이제 가 봐야겠어요. 되도록 빨리 다시 올게요. 하지만 그게 내일이나 모레가 아니더라도, 일주일이나 보름 뒤가 되더라도 내가 당신을 잊었다고 생각하지는 말아요. 바쁠지도 모르니까요.”\n\n“댁이 다시는 나를 잊지 않으리라는 걸 알겠소. 이제 다시는 댁을 못 미더워하지 않겠구먼. 그렇지만 일주일이나 보름 뒤면 내가 죽어서 묻혀 있을지도 모른다는 걸 잊지 마시우!”\n\n“되도록 빨리 다시 올게요, 베시.” 마거릿이 그녀의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다. “하지만 더 나빠지면 나한테 알려 줘요.”\n\n“그럼요, 그러겠소.” 베시가 마주 손을 쥐며 말했다.\n\n그날부터 헤일 부인은 갈수록 더 병으로 고통받는 몸이 되어 갔다. 어느덧 이디스의 결혼 1주년이 가까워지고 있었고, 그 한 해 동안 쌓인 곤경의 더미를 돌아보며 마거릿은 그것들을 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지 의아했다. 미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면 그녀는 얼마나 움츠러들어 다가올 시간으로부터 몸을 숨겼을 것인가! 그런데도 하루하루는 그것 하나하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 충분히 견딜 만했다. 작고 날카롭고 환한, 순전한 기쁨의 점들이 슬픔의 한복판으로 반짝이며 들어왔던 것이다. 한 해 전, 아니 헬스톤에 처음 갔을 때, 그리고 어머니의 성미에 밴 불평기를 처음으로 말없이 알아차렸을 때였다면, 그녀는 낯설고 황량하고 시끄럽고 분주한 곳에서, 집안 살림의 온갖 안락이 사방으로 줄어든 가운데 긴 병을 견뎌야 한다는 생각에 몹시도 신음했을 것이다. 그러나 불평할 진지하고 정당한 근거가 늘어나자 어머니의 마음속에는 새로운 종류의 인내가 돋아났다. 그녀는 슬퍼할 실제 이유가 없을 때 안절부절못하고 우울해했던 것과 거의 같은 정도로, 극심한 육신의 고통 속에서는 온순하고 조용했다. 헤일 씨는 그와 같은 성정의 남자들에게서 고집스러운 눈감기의 형태로 나타나는 바로 그 근심의 단계에 있었다. 그는 딸이 드러내는 걱정에 마거릿이 여태 본 적 없을 만큼 짜증을 냈다.\n\n“정말이지, 마거릿, 너는 점점 공상이 심해지는구나! 네 어머니가 정말 아프다면 하느님이 아시다시피 제일 먼저 놀랄 사람은 나다. 헬스톤에서도 우리는 네 어머니가 말하지 않아도 두통이 온 것을 늘 알아보지 않았느냐. 네 어머니는 아프면 얼굴이 아주 창백하고 하얘진다. 그런데 지금은 뺨에 건강하고 환한 혈색이 돌지 않느냐. 내가 처음 알았을 때 그랬던 것처럼 말이다.”\n\n“하지만 아버지.” 마거릿이 머뭇거리며 말했다. “저는 그게 고통 때문에 오르는 홍조가 아닐까 싶어요.”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리다, 마거릿. 내가 말하지 않았느냐, 너는 공상이 지나치다고. 내가 보기엔 정작 성치 않은 사람은 너다. 내일 너를 위해 의사를 부르려무나. 그러고 나서 네 마음이 편해질 것 같으면 그 사람이 네 어머니도 봐 주면 되지 않겠느냐.”\n\n“고마워요, 아버지. 그러면 정말 마음이 놓이겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 입을 맞추려고 아버지에게 다가갔다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 밀어냈다. 부드럽기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀가 불쾌한 생각들을 불러일으켰으니 그녀가 곁에서 없어져 주기를 바라는 만큼이나 그 생각들도 얼른 떨쳐 버리고 싶다는 듯한 몸짓이었다. 그는 불안하게 방 안을 오르내리며 서성거렸다.\n\n“가엾은 마리아!” 그가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다. “남을 희생시키지 않고도 옳은 일을 할 수 있으면 좋으련만. 그 사람이 만일……. 그렇게 되면 나는 이 도시도, 나 자신도 미워하게 될 게다. 그런데 마거릿, 네 어머니가 옛 고장 이야기를 자주 하더냐? 헬스톤 말이다.”\n\n“아니요, 아버지.” 마거릿이 서글프게 말했다.\n\n“그것 보아라, 그러니 그곳을 그리워하며 애태우는 게 아니지 않느냐, 응? 네 어머니가 워낙 소탈하고 숨김이 없어서 그이가 품은 자잘한 불만까지 내가 다 안다고 생각하는 것이 늘 내게 위안이 되어 왔다. 그이는 자기 건강에 심각하게 관계되는 일을 나에게 숨길 사람이 결코 아니야. 안 그러냐, 응, 마거릿? 나는 그러지 않으리라고 아주 확신한다. 그러니 그런 어리석고 병적인 생각은 다시 들려주지 말아라. 자, 입 맞춰 주고 어서 자러 가거라.”\n\n그러나 그녀는 아버지가 방 안을 서성거리는 소리를 들었다. 그녀와 이디스가 예전에 “너구리 짓”이라 부르던 그 걸음질이었다. 그녀가 느릿느릿 나른하게 옷을 다 벗고 난 뒤로도 한참 동안, 침대에 누워 귀를 기울이기 시작한 뒤로도 한참 동안 그 소리는 이어졌다.\n\n# 제14장\n\n“나는 밤이면 어린아이처럼 달게 잠들곤 했다. 이제는 바람이 거칠게 불면 소스라쳐 깨어, 울부짖는 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들리는 가엾은 내 아이를 떠올린다. 그러고는 그토록 작은 허물 하나로 그 아이를 내게서 앗아 간 것이 모질다고 느끼는 것이다.” — 사우디.\n\n이 무렵 마거릿에게 위안이 된 것은 어머니가 어린 시절 이후로 한 번도 보인 적 없을 만큼 다정하고 살가운 마음으로 자기에게 다가온다는 사실이었다. 어머니는 그녀를 속내를 털어놓는 벗으로 가슴에 품었다. 마거릿이 늘 채우고 싶어 하던 자리, 딕슨이 자기보다 더 사랑받는다고 시샘하게 만들던 바로 그 자리였다. 마거릿은 자기에게 공감을 청해 오는 부름 하나하나에 애써 응했다. 그런 부름은 수없이 많았고, 개중에는 그녀 자신이라면 결코 눈여겨보지도 마음에 두지도 않았을 하찮은 일들도 있었다. 마치 코끼리가 제 발치에 떨어진 작은 핀을 알아볼 리 없으면서도 조련사가 시키면 조심스레 집어 올리듯이. 그러는 사이 마거릿은 자신도 모르게 어떤 보답에 다가가고 있었다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 헤일 씨가 자리를 비운 사이, 어머니가 오빠 프레더릭 이야기를 꺼냈다. 마거릿이 그토록 묻고 싶어 하던 바로 그 이야기였고, 타고난 솔직함마저 수줍음에 눌리고 마는 거의 유일한 화제이기도 했다. 그에 대해 듣고 싶은 마음이 클수록 그녀는 입을 열지 못했다.\n\n“아, 마거릿, 어젯밤은 어찌나 바람이 세던지! 우리 방 굴뚝으로 울부짖으며 몰아쳐 내려오더구나! 나는 잠을 이룰 수가 없었단다. 그런 무서운 바람이 불면 도무지 잠을 자지 못해. 가엾은 프레더릭이 바다에 나가 있을 때 잠 못 드는 버릇이 들었지. 이제는 대번에 깨어나지는 않더라도 폭풍우 이는 바다에 있는 그 아이 꿈을 꾼단다. 배 양옆으로 유리처럼 맑고 푸른 물결의 벽이 솟는데, 돛대보다도 훨씬 높이 올라 그 모질고 무서운 흰 거품을 뿜으며 배 위로 말려 넘어오는 거야. 무슨 거대한 볏 달린 뱀처럼 말이다. 오래된 꿈이지만 바람 부는 밤이면 늘 되살아나서, 무서움에 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 침대에 일어나 앉은 채로 깨어난 것이 고맙기까지 하단다. 가엾은 프레더릭! 이제 그 아이는 뭍에 있으니 바람이 해칠 수야 없겠지. 그래도 저 높다란 굴뚝들쯤은 흔들어 무너뜨릴지도 모르겠다는 생각은 들더구나.”\n\n“오빠는 지금 어디 있어요, 어머니? 우리 편지는 카디스의 바버 상회 앞으로 보내잖아요. 그건 알아요. 그런데 오빠 자신은 어디 있는 거예요?”\n\n“그 고장 이름은 기억이 나지 않는구나. 그런데 그 아이는 헤일이라 불리지 않는단다. 그것만은 잊지 말아라, 마거릿. 편지마다 구석에 적힌 F. D.를 눈여겨보렴. 그 아이는 디킨슨이라는 이름을 쓰고 있단다. 나는 베러스퍼드라 부르게 하고 싶었어. 그 아이에게 그럴 권리가 어느 정도는 있었으니까. 하지만 네 아버지가 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 하셨지. 내 친정 성을 쓰면 정체가 드러날 수도 있으니 말이다.”\n\n“어머니,” 마거릿이 말했다. “그 일이 벌어졌을 때 저는 쇼 이모 댁에 있었고, 아마 사정을 곧이곧대로 들려주기에는 너무 어렸겠지요. 하지만 이제는 알고 싶어요. 괜찮으시다면요. 이야기하시는 것이 어머니께 너무 아프지만 않다면요.”\n\n“아프다니! 아니다.” 헤일 부인이 뺨을 붉히며 대답했다. “하지만 내 귀한 아들을 다시는 못 볼지도 모른다는 생각은 아프구나. 그것만 아니라면 그 아이는 옳은 일을 한 거란다, 마거릿. 사람들이야 뭐라고 하든 내게는 그 아이가 손수 쓴 편지가 있어. 내 아들이라 하는 말이 아니라, 나는 이 세상 어떤 군법회의보다 그 아이를 먼저 믿는다. 얘야, 내 자그마한 옻칠 장으로 가서 왼쪽 두 번째 서랍을 열어 보렴. 편지 한 묶음이 있을 게다.”\n\n마거릿이 가 보았다. 거기에는 누렇게 바래고 바닷물에 얼룩진 편지들이, 바다를 건너온 편지 특유의 냄새를 품은 채 들어 있었다. 마거릿은 그것을 어머니에게 가져다주었고, 어머니는 떨리는 손가락으로 비단 끈을 풀어 날짜를 살펴본 뒤 딸에게 읽으라고 건넸다. 그러면서 딸이 그 내용을 채 알아보기도 전에 조급하고 안달스러운 설명을 늘어놓았다.\n\n“보렴, 마거릿. 그 아이가 맨 처음부터 리드 함장을 얼마나 싫어했는지 말이다. 프레더릭이 맨 처음 배를 탔을 때 그 사람은 오라이언호의 이등 위관이었단다. 가엾은 것, 사관후보생 옷을 입은 모습이 어찌나 잘 어울리던지. 단검을 손에 들고 그것을 종이칼 삼아 신문지를 죄다 갈라 놓곤 했지! 그런데 그때는 그저 리드 씨였던 그 사람이 처음부터 프레더릭을 미워하는 눈치더구나. 그리고, 가만있자! 이건 그 아이가 러셀호에서 쓴 편지들이다. 그 배에 발령을 받고 옛 원수인 리드 함장이 지휘를 맡고 있는 것을 알았을 때, 그 아이는 그 사람의 횡포를 다 참고 견디려고 했단다. 보렴! 이게 그 편지야. 어디 읽어 보아라, 마거릿. 그 아이가 뭐라고 했더라. 가만있자. ‘아버지께서는 저를 믿으셔도 좋습니다. 장교이자 신사인 사람이 다른 장교에게서 받을 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 마땅한 인내로 견디겠습니다. 다만 지금 함장에 대해 전에 알던 바로 미루어, 러셀호에서 오래도록 이어질 횡포를 걱정스러운 마음으로 내다볼 수밖에 없음을 고백합니다.’ 보렴, 참고 견디겠다고 약속하지 않았니. 나는 그 아이가 정말 그랬으리라 믿는다. 그 아이는 부아만 나지 않으면 세상에 다시없이 성품이 고운 아이였으니까. 리드 함장이 어벤저호만큼 배를 빨리 다루지 못한다고 병사들에게 성을 냈다는 이야기가 나오는 것이 저 편지였던가? 보렴, 러셀호에는 새로 들어온 사람이 많았는데 어벤저호는 그 해역에 3년 가까이 머물면서 노예선을 쫓는 일 말고는 할 일이 없어 사람들을 부려 먹었으니, 다들 쥐나 원숭이처럼 삭구를 오르내렸다고 하는구나.”\n\n마거릿은 잉크가 바래 반쯤은 알아보기 어려운 그 편지를 천천히 읽었다. 그것은 아마도, 아니 십중팔구는 사소한 일에서 드러난 리드 함장의 오만함을 적은 글이었고, 다툼의 현장에서 갓 빠져나와 뜨거운 김이 가시기도 전에 펜을 든 사람의 손에서 몹시 부풀려진 것이었다. 선원 몇이 큰돛대 윗돛 삭구에 올라가 있는데 함장이 서둘러 내려오라 명하며 맨 나중에 닿는 자는 아홉 갈래 채찍으로 다스리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 활대 끝에 가장 멀리 나가 있던 사람은 동료들을 앞질러 갈 수 없다는 것을 깨닫고, 그러면서도 매질의 치욕이 미치도록 두려워, 한참 아래에 있는 밧줄을 붙잡으려고 죽을힘을 다해 몸을 던졌다가 놓치고 갑판 위에 정신을 잃은 채 떨어졌다. 그는 겨우 몇 시간을 더 버티다 숨을 거두었고, 젊은 헤일이 그 편지를 쓸 무렵 배 안의 분노는 끓어오를 대로 끓어오른 상태였다.\n\n“그런데 우리는 반란 소식을 듣고도 한참, 한참 지나서야 이 편지를 받았단다. 가엾은 프레드! 부치는 방법도 몰랐을 텐데, 그래도 이것을 쓰는 것이 그 아이에게는 위안이 되었겠지, 가엾은 것! 그러다 우리는 신문에서 기사를 보았어. 그러니까 프레드의 편지가 우리에게 닿기 훨씬 전이지. 러셀호에서 흉악한 반란이 일어났고, 반란자들이 배를 차지한 채 떠나가 버렸다고, 아마 해적이 되려는 것이라고 하더구나. 그리고 리드 함장은 몇 사람과 함께 보트에 실려 표류하게 되었는데, 장교인지 무엇인지 하는 그 사람들 이름이 죄다 실려 있었단다. 서인도제도에서 온 기선에 구조되었으니까. 아, 마거릿! 그 명단에 프레더릭 헤일이라는 이름이 없는 것을 보고 네 아버지와 내가 얼마나 속이 뒤집혔는지. 우리는 무슨 착오려니 했다. 가엾은 프레드는 참 훌륭한 아이였고, 그저 조금 성미가 급했을 뿐이니까. 그래서 명단에 있던 카라는 이름이 헤일의 오식이기를 바랐지. 신문이란 것이 워낙 엉성하니 말이다. 이튿날 우편 시간이 다가오자 네 아버지가 신문을 구하러 사우샘프턴까지 걸어가셨어. 나는 집에 가만있을 수가 없어서 마중을 나갔단다. 아버지는 아주 늦으셨어. 내가 생각한 것보다 훨씬 늦으셨지. 나는 산울타리 아래 앉아 기다렸다. 마침내 아버지가 오셨는데, 두 팔을 축 늘어뜨리고 고개를 떨군 채 한 걸음 한 걸음이 고역이고 짐인 것처럼 무겁게 걸어오시더구나. 마거릿, 나는 지금도 그 모습이 눈에 선하다.”\n\n“그만하세요, 어머니. 저도 다 알겠어요.” 마거릿이 어머니 곁에 살며시 몸을 기대고 그 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n“아니다, 너는 모른다, 마거릿. 그때 아버지를 보지 못한 사람은 아무도 모른단다. 나는 마중을 가려고 몸을 일으키기도 힘들었어. 별안간 사방이 빙빙 도는 것 같았거든. 내가 다가갔을 때 아버지는 아무 말도 하지 않으셨고, 집에서 3마일도 넘게 떨어진 올덤 너도밤나무 아래에서 나를 보고도 놀라는 기색조차 없으셨다. 다만 내 팔을 당신 팔에 끼우고는 내 손을 자꾸 쓸어 주셨어. 무슨 크고 무거운 벼락이 떨어질 테니 조용히 견디라고 달래는 것처럼 말이다. 내가 온몸을 떨며 말도 못 하게 되자 아버지는 나를 끌어안고 내 머리 위로 고개를 숙이시더니, 이상하게 목이 막힌 신음 같은 소리로 몸을 떨며 우시더구나. 나는 너무 무서워서 그저 가만히 서서, 무슨 소식을 들으셨느냐고 여쭙기만 했어. 그러자 아버지가, 누가 억지로 그 손을 움직이기라도 하는 듯 손을 덜덜 떨면서 그 몹쓸 신문을 읽어 보라고 내미셨단다. 거기서는 우리 프레더릭을 ‘더할 수 없이 시커먼 역적’이라고, ‘제 직분을 욕보인 비열하고 배은망덕한 자’라고 했어. 아! 그 사람들이 쓰지 않은 험한 말이 없었단다. 나는 다 읽자마자 그 신문을 손에 들고 갈기갈기 찢었어. 찢고 또 찢었지. 아, 마거릿, 나는 이로 물어뜯기까지 한 것 같구나. 울지는 않았어. 울 수가 없었다. 뺨은 불덩이처럼 뜨겁고 눈알까지 타는 것 같았지. 네 아버지가 나를 근심스레 바라보시는 것이 보였다. 나는 그건 거짓말이라고 했고, 정말 거짓말이었어. 몇 달이 지나 이 편지가 왔으니, 프레더릭이 어떤 일을 당했는지 너도 알겠지. 그 아이가 들고일어난 것은 제 자신을 위해서도, 제가 입은 억울함 때문도 아니었어. 다만 리드 함장에게 할 말은 하겠다는 것이었고, 그러다 일이 자꾸 험해진 거란다. 그리고 보다시피 선원들 대부분이 프레더릭 편에 섰지.\n\n“내 생각에는 말이다, 마거릿.” 그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 가냘프게 떨리는 지친 목소리로 말을 이었다. “나는 그것이 기쁘구나. 그 아이가 그저 훌륭한 장교이기만 했을 때보다, 불의에 맞서 일어선 프레더릭이 더 자랑스럽단다.”\n\n“저야말로 그래요.” 마거릿이 단단하고 또렷한 어조로 말했다. “지혜와 정의에 충성하고 따르는 것은 훌륭한 일이지요. 하지만 부당하고 모질게 휘두르는 제멋대로의 힘에 맞서는 것은 더욱 훌륭한 일이에요. 그것도 우리 자신을 위해서가 아니라 더 힘없는 이들을 위해서라면요.”\n\n“그래도 나는 프레더릭을 한 번만 더 보았으면 싶구나. 딱 한 번만. 그 아이는 내 첫아이였단다, 마거릿.” 헤일 부인은 아쉬운 듯이, 그리고 그 사무치는 간절한 소망이 곁에 남은 자식을 깎아내리기라도 하는 양 거의 미안해하듯이 말했다. 그러나 마거릿의 머릿속에는 그런 생각이 스치지도 않았다. 그녀는 어머니의 소원을 어떻게 하면 이룰 수 있을까 궁리하고 있었다.\n\n“6, 7년 전 일이잖아요. 지금도 오빠를 기소할까요, 어머니? 돌아와서 재판을 받는다면 형벌이 어떻게 될까요? 얼마나 심한 일을 당했는지 증거를 댈 수도 있을 텐데요.”\n\n“소용없단다.” 헤일 부인이 대답했다. “프레더릭과 함께했던 선원 몇이 붙잡혀서 어미시아호에서 군법회의를 받았어. 나는 그 가엾은 사람들이 변호하며 한 말을 다 믿었단다. 프레더릭이 한 이야기와 꼭 맞아떨어졌으니까. 하지만 아무 소용이 없었지.” 그러고는 이 대화가 시작된 뒤로 처음으로 헤일 부인이 울기 시작했다. 그런데도 마거릿은 무엇에 씐 듯, 이미 짐작하면서도 두려워하던 그 사실을 어머니에게서 기어이 끌어냈다.\n\n“그 사람들은 어떻게 되었어요, 어머니?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“활대 끝에 매달렸단다.” 헤일 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다. “그리고 무엇보다 나빴던 것은, 재판정이 그들에게 사형을 내리면서 상관에게 이끌려 제 본분에서 벗어나도록 스스로를 내버려 두었다고 한 것이었어.”\n\n두 사람은 오랫동안 말이 없었다.\n\n“그리고 오빠는 몇 해 동안 남아메리카에 있었지요?”\n\n“그래. 지금은 스페인에 있단다. 카디스나 그 근처에. 잉글랜드로 오면 그 아이는 교수형을 당한다. 나는 그 얼굴을 다시는 못 볼 게다. 잉글랜드로 오면 교수형을 당할 테니 말이다.”\n\n건넬 위로가 없었다. 헤일 부인은 얼굴을 벽으로 돌리고, 어미의 절망에 잠긴 채 미동도 없이 누워 있었다. 달랠 말이라고는 한마디도 있을 수 없었다. 그녀는 아들에 대한 기억과 단둘이 남고 싶다는 듯 조바심 어린 작은 몸짓으로 마거릿의 손에서 제 손을 빼냈다. 헤일 씨가 들어오자 마거릿은 방을 나왔다. 우울에 짓눌린 채, 지평선 어느 쪽에서도 밝아 올 기미를 볼 수 없었다.\n\n# 제15장\n\n“생각은 생각과 싸우고, 칼과 방패가 부딪는 자리에서 진리의 불꽃이 튀어 오른다.” — W. S. 랜더.\n\n“마거릿,” 이튿날 아버지가 말했다. “손턴 부인의 방문에 답례를 해야겠구나. 네 어머니는 몸이 좋지 않아 그렇게 멀리는 걷지 못하겠다고 하니, 너와 내가 오늘 오후에 다녀오자.”\n\n가는 길에 헤일 씨는 아내의 건강 이야기를 꺼냈다. 어딘가 감추어 둔 듯한 근심이 배어 있었고, 마거릿은 마침내 그것이 깨어난 것을 보고 반가웠다.\n\n“의사와 상의해 보았니, 마거릿? 사람을 보내 불러오게 했느냐?”\n\n“아니요, 아버지. 아버지는 그분이 저를 보러 오시는 이야기를 하셨잖아요. 그때 저는 다 나은 뒤였고요. 하지만 좋은 의사를 알기만 한다면 오늘 오후에라도 찾아가 와 달라고 청하겠어요. 어머니가 정말 심하게 편찮으신 게 분명하니까요.”\n\n그녀가 이렇게 사실을 분명하고 힘주어 내놓은 것은, 지난번 자신의 두려움을 입에 올렸을 때 아버지가 그 생각에 마음을 완전히 닫아 버렸기 때문이었다. 그러나 이제 사정이 달라져 있었다. 그는 낙담한 어조로 대답했다.\n\n“네 어머니에게 무슨 숨은 병이 있다고 생각하니? 정말로 몹시 아프다고 생각해? 딕슨이 무슨 말이라도 했느냐? 아, 마거릿! 우리가 밀턴에 온 것이 네 어머니를 죽인 게 아닌가 하는 두려움이 나를 놓아주지 않는구나. 가엾은 마리아!”\n\n“아, 아버지! 그런 생각은 하지 마세요.” 마거릿이 충격을 받아 말했다. “어머니는 몸이 좀 안 좋으신 것뿐이에요. 한동안 몸이 안 좋은 사람은 많고요. 좋은 진찰만 받으면 전보다 더 나아지고 튼튼해지는걸요.”\n\n“하지만 딕슨이 네 어머니에 대해 무슨 말이라도 했느냐?”\n\n“아니요! 딕슨은 아무것도 아닌 일로 수수께끼 만드는 걸 즐기잖아요. 어머니 건강에 대해 좀 알쏭달쏭하게 굴어서 제가 조금 놀란 것뿐이에요. 아마 아무 까닭도 없을 거예요. 아버지도 아시잖아요, 요전 날 제가 공상이 심해진다고 하셨죠.”\n\n“정말 그러기를 바라고 또 믿는다. 하지만 그때 내가 한 말은 마음에 두지 마라. 네가 어머니 건강에 대해 공상하는 건 좋구나! 네 공상을 이야기하기를 두려워하지 마라. 나는 그걸 듣고 싶다. 그때는 내가 성가셔하는 것처럼 말했겠지만 말이다. 아무튼 손턴 부인에게 좋은 의사를 아는지 물어보자꾸나. 일류가 아닌 사람에게 돈을 헛되이 쓰지는 않을 게다. 가만, 이 거리로 꺾어야겠구나.”\n\n그 거리는 손턴 부인이 살 만큼 큰 집을 품고 있을 곳으로는 보이지 않았다. 아들의 모습만으로는 그가 어떤 집에 사는지 짐작할 만한 인상을 받은 적이 없었다. 그러나 마거릿은 저도 모르게, 그 훤칠하고 육중하고 잘 차려입은 손턴 부인이라면 제 자신과 같은 성격의 집에 살고 있으리라 상상해 왔다. 그런데 말버러 가는 작은 집들이 길게 늘어선 줄로 이루어져 있었고, 군데군데 창 하나 없는 담이 서 있었다. 적어도 그들이 들어선 지점에서 보이는 것은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n“그가 말버러 가에 산다고 말한 게 분명한데.” 헤일 씨가 몹시 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“아주 작은 집에 사는 것도 그분이 아직 지키고 있는 절약 가운데 하나일지 모르죠. 하지만 여기 사람들이 많으니 제가 물어볼게요.”\n\n그리하여 그녀는 지나가는 사람에게 물었고, 손턴 씨는 공장 바로 옆에 산다는 대답을 들었다. 그리고 그들이 눈여겨보았던 그 길고 밋밋한 담 끝에 있는 공장 수위실 문을 손가락질로 안내받았다.\n\n수위실 문은 여느 정원 문 같았다. 그 한쪽에는 짐수레와 화물마차가 드나드는 커다란 대문이 닫혀 있었다. 수위가 그들을 널따란 직사각형 마당으로 들여보냈는데, 한쪽에는 사무를 보는 방들이 있었고 맞은편에는 창이 수없이 달린 거대한 공장이 있었다. 거기서는 기계의 끊임없는 쨍그렁 소리와 증기기관의 길게 신음하는 굉음이 흘러나와, 그 담장 안에 사는 이들의 귀를 먹먹하게 할 지경이었다. 거리가 지나가는 그 담의 맞은편, 직사각형의 짧은 변 한쪽에는 돌로 처마를 두른 훤칠한 집이 서 있었다. 물론 매연에 검게 그을려 있었지만, 페인트와 창문과 층계는 나무랄 데 없이 깨끗하게 건사되어 있었다. 지은 지 50, 60년쯤 된 집임이 분명했다. 돌로 마감한 겉면, 길고 좁은 창들과 그 많은 수, 양옆에서 올라와 난간으로 둘러싸인 현관 앞 층계 — 모든 것이 그 집의 나이를 말해 주었다. 마거릿은 다만 이토록 좋은 집에 살며 이토록 완벽하게 건사할 형편이 되는 사람들이 어째서 시골이나 하다못해 교외의 훨씬 작은 살림집을 더 좋아하지 않는지 의아했다. 공장의 끊임없는 소용돌이와 소음 속이 아니라 말이다. 문이 열리기를 기다리며 층계에 서 있는 동안, 익숙하지 못한 그녀의 귀는 아버지의 목소리조차 알아듣기 어려웠다. 게다가 창 없는 담의 커다란 대문을 경계로 삼은 그 마당은 집 안 거실에서 내다보기에 음울하기 짝이 없는 풍경이었다. 마거릿은 옛날식 계단을 올라 응접실로 안내되었을 때 그것을 알았다. 응접실의 창 세 개는 현관문과 그 오른쪽 방 위로 나 있었다. 응접실에는 아무도 없었다. 마치 이 집이 용암에 뒤덮여 천 년 뒤에나 발견되기라도 할 듯 정성스레 가구에 덮개를 씌우던 그날 이후로 아무도 이 방에 들어온 적이 없는 것 같았다. 벽은 분홍빛과 금빛이었다. 양탄자의 무늬는 밝은 바탕에 꽃다발을 그린 것이었으나, 한가운데는 윤을 낸 빛깔 없는 아마포 깔개로 조심스레 덮여 있었다. 창의 커튼은 레이스였고, 의자와 소파마다 저마다의 그물뜨기나 뜨개 덮개를 걸치고 있었다. 커다란 설화석고 군상들이 평평한 자리마다 차지하고 서서 유리 덮개 아래 먼지를 피하고 있었다. 방 한가운데, 덮개를 씌운 샹들리에 바로 아래에는 커다란 둥근 탁자가 있었고, 말쑥하게 장정한 책들이 윤나는 상판 둘레에 일정한 간격으로 놓여 바퀴의 알록달록한 살처럼 보였다. 모든 것이 빛을 되비쳤고 무엇 하나 빛을 빨아들이지 않았다. 방 전체가 아프도록 얼룩덜룩하고 반짝반짝하고 점점이 박힌 듯한 인상을 주었고, 그것이 마거릿에게 어찌나 불쾌하게 다가왔던지 그녀는 이런 공기 속에서 모든 것을 이토록 희고 정갈하게 지키는 데 필요한 남다른 청결함도, 그 얼음 같고 눈 같은 불편함을 이루려고 기꺼이 쏟아부었을 수고도 거의 의식하지 못했다. 어디를 보아도 정성과 노동의 자취가 있었지만, 그것은 편안함을 얻기 위한, 조용한 가정의 일과를 돕기 위한 정성과 노동이 아니었다. 오로지 꾸미기 위한, 그리고 그 꾸밈을 먼지와 훼손으로부터 지키기 위한 것이었다.\n\n손턴 부인이 나타나기 전에 두 사람은 방을 살펴보고 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나눌 겨를이 있었다. 그들이 하는 이야기는 온 세상이 들어도 좋을 것이었으나, 이런 방은 흔히 사람으로 하여금 낮게 말하게 만드는 법이다. 마치 쓰인 적 없는 메아리를 깨우기 싫다는 듯이.\n\n마침내 손턴 부인이 여느 때처럼 훌륭한 검은 비단을 사각거리며 들어왔다. 그녀의 모슬린과 레이스는 방 안의 모슬린과 그물뜨기의 순백에 견줄 만했으나 그것을 넘어서지는 않았다. 마거릿은 어머니가 손턴 부인의 방문에 답례하러 함께 오지 못한 사정을 설명했다. 그러나 아버지의 두려움을 너무 생생하게 되살리지 않으려는 조바심 탓에 그녀는 어설픈 설명을 하고 말았고, 손턴 부인의 마음에는 헤일 부인의 병이 무슨 일시적이거나 공상적인, 귀부인 티 나는 몸살 같은 것이어서 동기가 충분히 강했더라면 제쳐 둘 수도 있었으리라는 인상을, 혹은 그날 외출을 못 할 만큼 심한 것이었다면 방문을 미룰 수도 있었으리라는 인상을 남겼다. 게다가 헤일 가를 찾아갈 때 제 마차에 세내었던 말들과, 그들에게 온갖 예를 다하도록 손턴 씨가 패니에게 함께 가라고 일렀던 일까지 떠올리자 손턴 부인은 다소 기분이 상해 몸을 곧추세웠고, 마거릿에게 아무런 동정도 보이지 않았다. 실은 어머니가 편찮다는 그 말조차 거의 곧이듣지 않았다.\n\n“손턴 씨는 어떠십니까?” 헤일 씨가 물었다. “어제 서둘러 쓴 쪽지를 보고 몸이 좋지 않으신가 걱정했습니다.”\n\n“제 아들은 좀처럼 앓지 않습니다. 앓더라도 그것을 입에 올리거나 무슨 일을 못 하는 핑계로 삼는 법이 없지요. 어젯밤에는 선생님과 함께 읽을 짬을 낼 수 없다고 하더군요. 아쉬워한 것이 분명합니다. 그 애는 선생님과 보내는 시간을 소중히 여기니까요.”\n\n“저에게도 똑같이 즐거운 시간입니다.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “고전 문학의 아름다움을 그토록 즐기고 알아보는 모습을 보면 저까지 다시 젊어지는 기분이 듭니다.”\n\n“한가한 사람들에게야 고전이 아주 바람직한 것이겠지요. 그러나 솔직히 말해 아들이 다시 고전 공부를 시작한 것은 제 판단에 어긋나는 일이었습니다. 그 애가 사는 시대와 장소는 그 애의 온 기운과 관심을 요구하는 듯이 보이니까요. 고전은 시골이나 대학에서 인생을 빈둥거리며 흘려보내는 사내들에게나 어울릴 것입니다. 그러나 밀턴 사람이라면 생각도 힘도 오늘의 일에 쏟아부어야 하지요. 적어도 제 의견은 그렇습니다.” 이 마지막 구절을 그녀는 “겸손을 흉내 내는 자부심”으로 내뱉었다.\n\n“하지만 정신이 한 가지 대상에만 너무 오래 매여 있으면 굳고 뻣뻣해져서 여러 관심사를 받아들이지 못하게 되지 않을까요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“정신이 굳고 뻣뻣해진다는 게 무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠군요. 그리고 나는 오늘은 이것에 푹 빠졌다가 내일이면 새 관심거리에 그것을 까맣게 잊어버리는 팔랑개비 같은 사람들을 좋게 보지 않습니다. 관심사가 많은 것은 밀턴 공장주의 삶에 맞지 않아요. 그에게는 큰 소망 하나를 품고 제 삶의 모든 목적을 그 성취에 쏟는 것으로 충분하고, 또 충분해야 합니다.”\n\n“그것은……?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n누런 뺨이 달아오르고 눈이 빛나며 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“제 나라의 상인들 사이에서, 제 고장의 사내들 사이에서 높고 명예로운 자리를 얻고 지켜 내는 것이지요. 제 아들은 그런 자리를 제 힘으로 얻었습니다. 어디를 가 보셔도 — 잉글랜드만 두고 하는 말이 아니라 유럽 어디를 가 보셔도 — 밀턴의 존 손턴이라는 이름은 사업하는 사람이라면 누구나 알고 존경합니다. 물론 상류 사교계에서야 알려지지 않았지요.” 그녀가 경멸하듯 이어 말했다. “한가한 신사 숙녀들이 밀턴 공장주에 대해 많이 알 리가 없지요. 의회에 들어가거나 귀족의 딸과 혼인하지 않는 한은요.”\n\n헤일 씨도 마거릿도, 벨 씨가 손턴 씨야말로 밀턴에서 사귀어 두면 좋을 벗이라고 편지에 써 보내기 전까지 그 위대한 이름을 들어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 어색하고도 우스꽝스럽게 의식하고 있었다. 그 자부심 강한 어머니의 세계는 한편으로 할리 가의 점잖은 사람들의 세계도, 다른 한편으로 시골 목사와 햄프셔 지주들의 세계도 아니었다. 그저 듣고만 있는 표정을 지키려는 온갖 애씀에도 마거릿의 얼굴은 예민한 손턴 부인에게 그 마음을 일러 주고 말았다.\n\n“제 아들이라는 이 놀라운 인물에 대해서는 들어 본 적이 없다고 생각하시는군요, 헤일 양. 나를 생각이 밀턴에 갇혀 있고 제 까마귀가 세상에서 가장 희다고 여기는 늙은 여편네로 보고 계시고요.”\n\n“아니에요.” 마거릿이 다소 기운차게 말했다. “밀턴에 오기 전에는 손턴 씨의 이름을 거의 들어 본 적이 없다고 생각하고 있었던 것은 사실일 거예요. 하지만 이곳에 온 뒤로는 그분을 존경하고 우러러보게 될 만큼, 그리고 부인께서 그분에 대해 하신 말씀이 얼마나 옳고 참된지 느낄 만큼 넉넉히 들었어요.”\n\n“누가 그 애 이야기를 하던가요?” 손턴 부인이 다소 누그러져 물었으나, 남의 말이 아들을 온전히 평가하지 못했을까 하는 질투가 배어 있었다.\n\n마거릿은 대답하기 전에 망설였다. 이런 위압적인 캐물음이 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다. 헤일 씨가 제 딴에는 구원자로 나섰다.\n\n“손턴 씨가 어떤 사람인지 알게 된 것은 그분이 스스로 하신 말씀 덕분이었지요. 그렇지 않니, 마거릿?”\n\n손턴 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다.\n\n“제 아들은 제 행적을 떠벌릴 사람이 아닙니다. 다시 한번 여쭙겠습니다만, 헤일 양, 누구의 이야기를 듣고 그 애를 좋게 여기게 되셨는지요? 어미란 제 자식 칭찬이라면 궁금해하고 탐내는 법이니까요.”\n\n마거릿이 대답했다. “벨 씨에게서 들었던 그분의 지난 삶에 대한 이야기를 손턴 씨가 도리어 감추셨다는 것이, 하신 말씀보다도 그 감추심이, 부인께서 그분을 자랑스러워하실 까닭이 얼마나 큰지를 저희 모두에게 느끼게 해 주었어요.”\n\n“벨 씨라니! 그 사람이 존에 대해 뭘 알겠습니까? 졸음에 겨운 대학에서 게으른 삶을 사는 사람이. 하지만 고맙군요, 헤일 양. 어지간한 아가씨들 같으면 아들이 좋은 평판을 듣는다는 이야기로 늙은 여자를 기쁘게 해 주기를 꺼렸을 텐데요.”\n\n“어째서요?” 마거릿이 어리둥절해하며 손턴 부인을 똑바로 바라보고 물었다.\n\n“어째서라니요! 아들의 마음에 무슨 속셈이 있는 경우, 늙은 어미를 제 편드는 사람으로 만들고 있다는 것을 스스로 얼마나 뻔히 아는지, 그런 양심이 있을 테니까요.”\n\n그녀는 험상궂은 미소를 지었다. 마거릿의 솔직함이 마음에 들었던 것이다. 그리고 어쩌면 제게 무슨 심문할 권리라도 있는 양 지나치게 캐물었다고 느꼈는지도 몰랐다. 마거릿은 눈앞에 놓인 그 발상에 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 어찌나 유쾌하게 웃던지, 그 웃음을 불러낸 말이 온통 통째로 우스꽝스럽기라도 한 듯이 들려 손턴 부인의 귀에 거슬렸다.\n\n마거릿은 손턴 부인의 언짢은 낯빛을 보자마자 웃음을 그쳤다.\n\n“죄송합니다, 부인. 하지만 손턴 씨의 마음에 무슨 속셈을 품었다는 혐의를 벗겨 주시니 정말 감사할 따름이에요.”\n\n“젊은 아가씨들이 전에도 그런 적이 있으니까요.” 손턴 부인이 뻣뻣하게 말했다.\n\n“손턴 양은 잘 지내시겠지요.” 헤일 씨가 대화의 흐름을 바꾸고 싶어 끼어들었다.\n\n“늘 그렇듯 잘 지냅니다. 튼튼하지는 못하지요.” 손턴 부인이 짧게 대답했다.\n\n“손턴 씨는요? 목요일에는 뵐 수 있으리라 바라도 되겠지요?”\n\n“아들의 일정은 제가 장담할 수 없습니다. 시내에 어수선한 일이 벌어지고 있어요. 파업 조짐이 있습니다. 그리되면 그 애의 경험과 판단을 벗들이 많이들 물어 오겠지요. 그래도 목요일에는 갈 수 있을 듯싶습니다. 어쨌든 못 가게 되면 반드시 알려 드릴 겁니다.”\n\n“파업이라니요!” 마거릿이 물었다. “무엇 때문에요? 무엇 때문에 파업을 하려는 건가요?”\n\n“남의 재산을 다스리고 차지하려고요.” 손턴 부인이 사납게 코웃음을 치며 말했다. “늘 그것 때문에 파업을 하지요. 제 아들의 직공들이 파업을 한다면 저는 그들이 은혜를 모르는 개떼라고밖에 말하지 않겠습니다. 하지만 틀림없이 할 겁니다.”\n\n“임금을 더 달라는 것이겠지요?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n“겉으로야 그렇습니다. 그러나 실상은 저희가 주인이 되고 공장주들을 제 땅에서 종으로 만들고 싶은 것이지요. 늘 그것을 노립니다. 늘 마음에 품고 있고요. 그래서 5, 6년마다 공장주와 직공들 사이에 싸움이 벌어집니다. 이번에는 저희가 잘못 짚었다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다. 셈이 좀 어긋났다는 것을요. 일단 나가고 나면 다시 들어오기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 것도 알게 되겠지요. 이번에 저들이 해 본다면, 다시는 함부로 파업할 생각을 못 하도록 가르쳐 줄 무엇인가를 공장주들이 머릿속에 품고 있다고 저는 믿습니다.”\n\n“그러면 시내가 아주 험악해지나요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“물론이지요. 하지만 설마 겁쟁이는 아니시겠지요? 밀턴은 겁쟁이가 살 곳이 아닙니다. 매킨슨이 제 공장 밖으로 코빼기라도 내미는 즉시 그 피를 보겠다고 저마다 욕설을 퍼붓는, 얼굴이 하얗게 질린 성난 사내들의 무리를 헤치고 길을 뚫어야 했던 때를 나는 압니다. 그런데 그는 아무것도 모르고 있었으니 누군가 가서 일러 주어야 했지요. 그러지 않으면 죽은 목숨이었으니까요. 그리고 그 일은 여자가 해야만 했습니다. 그래서 내가 갔지요. 그런데 들어가고 나니 나올 수가 없었습니다. 목숨을 건 일이었지요. 그래서 나는 지붕으로 올라갔습니다. 거기에는 무리가 공장 문을 부수려 들면 그 머리 위로 떨어뜨리려고 쌓아 둔 돌들이 있었어요. 그리고 나는 그 무거운 돌들을 들어 올려 거기 있는 어느 사내 못지않게 정확히 겨누어 떨어뜨렸을 겁니다. 그때까지 겪은 열기에 정신을 잃지만 않았더라면요. 밀턴에서 살려거든 용감한 마음을 지니는 법을 배우셔야 합니다, 헤일 양.”\n\n“최선을 다하겠어요.” 마거릿이 다소 창백해져 말했다. “겪어 보기 전에는 제가 용감한지 아닌지 모르겠어요. 하지만 아무래도 겁쟁이일 것 같아요.”\n\n“남부 사람들은 우리 다크셔의 남녀가 그저 살아가고 버텨 나가는 일이라고 부르는 것에 곧잘 겁을 먹지요. 하지만 늘 윗사람에게 앙심을 품고 그것을 갚을 기회만 노리는 사람들 틈에서 10년을 지내 보면, 자기가 겁쟁이인지 아닌지 알게 될 겁니다. 내 말을 믿으세요.”\n\n그날 저녁 손턴 씨가 헤일 씨의 집에 왔다. 그는 응접실로 안내되었는데, 마침 헤일 씨가 아내와 딸에게 소리 내어 책을 읽어 주고 있던 참이었다.\n\n“어머니의 쪽지를 전해 드릴 겸, 어제 시간을 지키지 못한 것을 사과드릴 겸 왔습니다. 쪽지에 청하신 주소가 적혀 있습니다. 도널드슨 의사입니다.”\n\n“고맙습니다!” 마거릿이 서둘러 말하며 쪽지를 받으려 손을 내밀었다. 의사를 수소문했다는 이야기를 어머니가 듣기를 바라지 않았기 때문이다. 손턴 씨가 그 마음을 곧바로 알아차린 듯한 것이 반가웠다. 그는 아무 설명도 덧붙이지 않고 쪽지를 건네주었다.\n\n헤일 씨가 파업 이야기를 꺼냈다. 손턴 씨의 얼굴이 그 어머니의 가장 고약한 표정을 닮아 갔고, 지켜보던 마거릿은 곧바로 거부감을 느꼈다.\n\n“예, 그 어리석은 자들이 기어이 파업을 하겠답니다. 하라지요. 우리에게는 나쁠 것도 없습니다. 그래도 우리는 기회를 주었지요. 저들은 장사가 작년처럼 잘되는 줄 압니다. 우리는 수평선에 몰려오는 폭풍을 보고 돛을 거두고 있고요. 그런데 우리가 까닭을 설명하지 않는다고 해서 저들은 우리가 사리에 맞게 행동하고 있다는 것을 믿으려 들지 않습니다. 우리가 우리 돈을 어떻게 쓰든 어떻게 아끼든 저들에게 조목조목 근거를 대야 한다는 겁니다. 애슐리의 헨더슨이 제 직공들에게 수를 써 보았다가 실패했지요. 그는 오히려 파업을 바랐습니다. 그편이 그의 장부에는 알맞았으니까요. 그래서 직공들이 요구하는 5퍼센트를 달라고 찾아오자, 생각해 보고 봉급날에 답을 주겠다고 했습니다. 물론 제 답이 무엇일지 내내 알면서도, 저들이 제 방식에 우쭐해지도록 부추기려던 것이었지요. 그런데 저들이 그보다 한 수 위여서, 장사 형편이 나빠진다는 이야기를 어디서 들었지 뭡니까. 그래서 금요일에 몰려와 요구를 거두어들였고, 이제 그는 어쩔 수 없이 계속 공장을 돌려야 하게 되었습니다. 그러나 우리 밀턴 공장주들은 오늘 우리 결정을 통보했습니다. 한 푼도 올리지 않겠다고요. 임금을 내려야 할지도 모르지만 올릴 여력은 없다고 말했습니다. 그러니 이제 우리는 여기 서서 저들의 다음 공격을 기다리는 겁니다.”\n\n“그것이 무엇이겠습니까?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n“동시 파업이라고 짐작합니다. 며칠 안에 연기 없는 밀턴을 보시게 될 겁니다, 헤일 양.”\n\n“하지만 어째서,” 그녀가 물었다. “장사가 나빠지리라 내다보시는 그럴 만한 까닭을 설명해 주실 수는 없나요? 제가 알맞은 말을 쓰고 있는지는 모르겠지만, 무슨 뜻인지는 아시겠지요.”\n\n“댁의 하인들에게 씀씀이나 절약에 대해 까닭을 대십니까? 우리, 자본을 가진 사람들에게는 그것으로 무엇을 할지 고를 권리가 있습니다.”\n\n“인간의 권리지요.” 마거릿이 아주 낮게 말했다.\n\n“죄송합니다, 뭐라고 하셨는지 못 들었습니다.”\n\n“되풀이하고 싶지 않아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “선생님께서는 함께 느끼지 않으실 감정에 관한 것이었으니까요.”\n\n“한번 저를 시험해 보시지 않겠습니까?” 그가 청했다. 그녀가 한 말을 알아내는 데 갑자기 온 생각이 쏠린 것이었다. 그녀는 그의 집요함이 언짢았으나, 제 말에 지나친 무게를 실어 주고 싶지도 않았다.\n\n“인간의 권리라고 말했어요. 종교적인 까닭 말고는, 선생님이 선생님의 것으로 마음대로 하지 못할 이유가 없어 보인다는 뜻이었어요.”\n\n“우리가 종교적 견해에서 다르다는 것은 압니다. 하지만 제게도, 비록 헤일 양과 같지는 않을지언정 나름의 견해가 있다고는 인정해 주시지 않겠습니까?”\n\n그는 마치 그녀에게만 말하듯 낮춘 목소리로 말하고 있었다. 그녀는 그렇게 자기만 두고 이야기하는 것이 싫었다. 그녀는 여느 때의 목소리로 또렷하게 대답했다.\n\n“이 일에서 선생님의 특별한 종교적 견해를 헤아려야 할 까닭은 없다고 생각해요. 제가 말하려던 것은 그저, 고용주가 마음만 먹으면 제 돈을 죄다 허비하거나 내던져 버리는 것을 막을 인간의 법은 없지만, 그렇게 하면 청지기로서의 의무를 저버리는 것이라고 — 적어도 제게는 그렇게 — 넌지시 일러 주는 구절들이 성경에 있다는 것이었어요. 하지만 저는 파업이니 임금 수준이니 자본이니 노동이니 하는 것을 너무나 모르니, 선생님 같은 정치경제학자와는 이야기하지 않는 편이 낫겠네요.”\n\n“아니요, 오히려 더 그러셔야 할 까닭이지요.” 그가 열을 내며 말했다. “낯선 사람에게 이상하거나 알 수 없어 보이는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 설명해 드리겠습니다. 특히 지금처럼 펜을 잡을 줄 아는 글쟁이라면 누구나 우리가 하는 일을 물고 늘어질 시기에는요.”\n\n“고맙습니다.” 그녀가 차갑게 대답했다. “물론 이 낯선 사회에서 살아가며 어리둥절해질 때면 저는 우선 아버지께 여쭈어 알 수 있는 것을 얻겠지만요.”\n\n“낯설다고 생각하시는군요. 어째서요?”\n\n“모르겠어요. 아마도 겉으로 보기에만도 서로 온갖 방식으로 기대고 있는 두 계급이 보이는데, 저마다 상대의 이익을 제 이익과 맞선 것으로 여기는 것이 분명해 보이기 때문일 거예요. 두 무리의 사람들이 늘 서로를 헐뜯는 곳에서는 살아 본 적이 없어서요.”\n\n“공장주들을 헐뜯는 이야기를 누구에게서 들으셨습니까? 직공들을 욕하는 이야기를 누구에게서 들으셨는지는 묻지 않겠습니다. 요전 날 제가 한 말을 여전히 오해하고 계신 것이 보이니까요. 그러나 공장주들을 욕하는 이야기는 누구에게서 들으셨습니까?”\n\n마거릿이 붉어졌다가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n“저는 심문받는 것을 좋아하지 않아요. 그 물음에는 대답하지 않겠어요. 게다가 그건 사실과 아무 상관이 없고요. 제 말을 그냥 믿으셔야 해요. 저는 어떤 사람들이, 어쩌면 직공들 가운데 어느 한 사람이었을지도 모르지만, 그들이 돈을 모으지 못하게 하는 것이 고용주들에게 이롭다는 듯이 말하는 것을 들었어요. 저축은행에 돈이 좀 쌓이면 그들이 너무 자립하게 되리라고요.”\n\n“틀림없이 그 히긴스라는 사람이 네게 이런 이야기를 다 했겠구나.” 헤일 부인이 말했다. 손턴 씨는 마거릿이 자기에게 알리고 싶어 하지 않는 것을 듣지 못한 척했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것을 놓치지 않았다.\n\n“게다가 무지한 직공을 두는 것이 공장주들에게 이롭다고들 여긴다는 이야기도 들었어요. 명령마다 캐묻고 그 까닭을 알려 드는 제 중대의 사내들을 레녹스 대위가 부르던 말로 하자면, 어설픈 법률가가 아닌 사람들 말이에요.”\n\n문장의 이 뒷부분을 그녀는 손턴 씨보다 아버지 쪽에 대고 말했다. 레녹스 대위가 누구지? 손턴 씨는 야릇한 종류의 불쾌감과 함께 스스로에게 물었고, 그 때문에 잠시 그녀에게 대꾸하지 못했다. 그녀의 아버지가 이야기를 이어받았다.\n\n“너는 학교에 마음을 둔 적이 없지, 마거릿. 그렇지 않았다면 밀턴에서 교육을 위해 얼마나 많은 일이 이루어지고 있는지 벌써 보고 알았을 텐데.”\n\n“그러네요!” 그녀가 갑자기 온순해져 말했다. “제가 학교에 충분히 마음을 두지 않는다는 건 알아요. 하지만 제가 말한 앎과 무지는 읽고 쓰기에 관한 게 아니었어요. 아이에게 줄 수 있는 가르침이나 지식 말이에요. 분명 그 말뜻은 남녀를 이끌어 갈 지혜에 대한 무지였어요. 그게 무엇인지는 저도 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 그 사람은, 그러니까 제게 말해 준 사람은, 공장주들이 자기 직공들을 그저 키만 크고 몸집만 큰 아이들로 두고 싶어 한다는 듯이 말했어요. 지금 이 순간만 살아가면서 눈멀고 따지지 않는 복종을 하는 아이들로요.”\n\n“요컨대, 헤일 양, 헤일 양께 그런 이야기를 해 준 사람은 공장주들을 헐뜯으려고 늘어놓는 온갖 험담을 아주 선뜻 들어 줄 귀를 찾아냈던 것이 분명하군요.” 손턴 씨가 기분 상한 어조로 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 대답하지 않았다. 손턴 씨가 제 말에 인신공격의 성격을 붙인 것이 언짢았다.\n\n다음으로 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“고백하건대, 저는 마거릿만큼 어느 직공과도 가깝게 알고 지내게 되지는 않았습니다만, 사물의 겉면에서부터 고용하는 쪽과 고용되는 쪽 사이의 반목이 몹시 눈에 띄더군요. 손턴 씨가 이따금 하신 말씀에서도 저는 그런 인상을 받습니다.”\n\n손턴 씨는 입을 열기 전에 잠시 뜸을 들였다. 마거릿이 방금 방을 나간 참이었고, 그는 자기와 그녀 사이의 감정 상태에 속이 상해 있었다. 그러나 그 작은 언짢음이 그를 더 차분하고 사려 깊게 만들어, 그의 말에 한결 더한 품위를 실어 주었다.\n\n“제 이론은 제 이익이 제 직공들의 이익과 하나이며 그 반대도 마찬가지라는 것입니다. 헤일 양이 사람을 ‘직공’이라 부르는 것을 듣기 싫어한다는 것을 알기에 그 말은 쓰지 않겠습니다. 제 입에는 그것이 가장 쉽게 오르는 전문 용어이고, 그 유래가 무엇이든 제가 태어나기 전부터 있던 말이지만요. 언젠가, 어느 천년왕국에서, 유토피아에서라면 이 하나 됨이 실제로 이루어질지도 모르겠습니다. 제가 공화정을 가장 완전한 정치 형태로 상상할 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로요.”\n\n“호메로스를 마치는 대로 플라톤의 국가론을 읽읍시다.”\n\n“글쎄요, 플라톤의 대년(大年)에는 우리 모두가 — 남자도 여자도 아이도 — 공화정에 어울리게 될 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 지금 우리의 도덕과 지성 상태에서라면 저는 입헌군주정을 택하겠습니다. 어릴 적에는 우리를 다스릴 현명한 전제가 필요합니다. 실은 어린 시절이 한참 지난 뒤에도 아이들과 젊은이들은 분별 있고 단호한 권위의 흔들림 없는 법 아래에서 가장 행복하지요. 저는 우리 사람들이 아이의 처지에 있다고 보는 데까지는 헤일 양과 뜻을 같이합니다. 다만 우리 공장주들이 그들을 그렇게 만들거나 그렇게 붙들어 두는 데 무슨 관계가 있다는 것은 부인합니다. 저는 그들에게는 전제가 가장 좋은 통치 방식이라고 주장합니다. 그러므로 제가 그들과 마주하는 시간 동안 저는 필연코 전제군주일 수밖에 없습니다. 저는 제 최선의 분별을 쓰겠습니다. 북부에 다소 지나치게 많았던 그 겉치레나 박애 감정에서가 아니라, 제 사업을 이끌어 가는 데 현명한 법을 세우고 올바른 결정을 내리기 위해서요. 첫째로는 제 자신의 이익을 위해, 둘째로는 그들의 이익을 위해 움직이는 법과 결정 말입니다. 그러나 저는 제 까닭을 대라고 강요당하지도 않을 것이고, 한번 제 결심이라고 밝힌 것에서 물러서지도 않을 것입니다. 나가라지요! 저도 그들 못지않게 고통을 겪을 것입니다. 그러나 끝내 그들은 제가 한 치도 깎지 않았고 바꾸지도 않았음을 알게 될 것입니다.”\n\n마거릿이 방에 다시 들어와 바느질감을 들고 앉아 있었으나 말은 하지 않았다. 헤일 씨가 대답했다.\n\n“제가 아주 모르면서 하는 말인지도 모르겠습니다만, 아는 것이 얼마 없는 대로 보건대 대중은 이미 빠르게 그 성가신 단계로 접어들고 있는 듯합니다. 개인의 삶에서든 무리의 삶에서든 어린 시절과 어른 시절 사이에 끼어드는 그 단계 말입니다. 그런데 이때 개인을 다루면서 많은 부모가 저지르는 잘못은, ‘부르면 오너라’, ‘시키는 대로 하여라’ 하는 단순한 법을 따르는 것이 의무의 전부였을 때와 똑같이 따지지 않는 복종을 고집한다는 것이지요. 그러나 현명한 부모는 스스로 하고자 하는 마음을 어루만져 주어, 제 절대적인 다스림이 끝났을 때 벗이자 조언자가 되는 법입니다. 제 논리가 어긋난다면, 그 비유를 끌어들인 것은 손턴 씨라는 걸 기억해 주십시오.”\n\n“아주 최근에,” 마거릿이 말했다. “불과 3, 4년 전 뉘른베르크에서 있었던 일에 대한 이야기를 들었어요. 그곳의 어느 부자가 예전에는 살림집이자 창고이기도 했던 거대한 저택 가운데 하나에서 혼자 살았대요. 그에게 자식이 하나 있다는 소문이 돌았지만 아무도 확실히 알지는 못했고요. 40년 동안 그 소문은 일었다 잦아들었다 하면서 아주 사라지지는 않았어요. 그가 죽은 뒤에야 그것이 사실로 드러났지요. 그에게는 아들이 있었어요. 몸만 자란 사내였는데 지성은 쓰인 적 없는 아이의 것이었어요. 아버지가 유혹과 잘못에서 지켜 주려고 그런 이상한 방식으로 가두어 키운 것이었지요. 하지만 물론 이 다 큰 아이가 세상에 풀려나자 온갖 나쁜 조언자가 그를 마음대로 휘둘렀어요. 그는 선과 악을 가릴 줄 몰랐어요. 아버지는 그를 무지 속에서 키우고는 그것을 순진함으로 착각하는 잘못을 저질렀던 거예요. 그리고 14개월 동안 방탕하게 산 끝에, 그가 굶어 죽지 않도록 시 당국이 그를 맡아야 했지요. 그는 구걸을 제대로 해낼 만큼도 말을 부릴 줄 몰랐어요.”\n\n“저는 (헤일 양이 일러 준) 공장주의 처지를 부모의 처지에 견주는 비유를 썼으니, 그 비유를 저를 치는 무기로 삼으신다고 해서 불평해서는 안 되겠지요. 하지만 헤일 씨, 현명한 부모를 우리의 본보기로 세우시면서 그런 부모는 자식들이 스스로 하고자 하는 마음을 어루만져 준다고 하셨습니다. 그런데 분명히 말씀드리지만, 일하는 시간 동안 직공들이 무엇이든 스스로 알아서 할 때는 오지 않았습니다. 그때 그것이 무슨 뜻일지 저로서는 짐작조차 어렵습니다. 그리고 제가 말씀드리건대, 우리가 그들이 공장 밖에서 사는 삶에 지나치게 참견한다면 그것은 공장주가 직공들의 자립을 침범하는 일이 될 것이고, 적어도 저로서는 그것이 정당하다고 느끼지 못할 것입니다. 그들이 우리를 위해 하루에 10시간 일한다고 해서 나머지 시간까지 우리가 그들에게 걸음마 끈을 채울 권리가 있다고는 보지 않으니까요. 저는 제 자신의 자립을 몹시 소중히 여기기에, 다른 사람이 끊임없이 제 행동을 지시하고 충고하고 훈계하는 것, 심지어 어떤 식으로든 너무 세세히 계획해 주는 것보다 더 큰 굴욕은 상상할 수 없습니다. 그가 세상에서 가장 현명한 사람이든 가장 힘 있는 사람이든, 저는 똑같이 반발하고 그 간섭에 분개할 것입니다. 이런 감정은 잉글랜드 남부보다 북부에서 더 강하리라 짐작합니다.”\n\n“실례입니다만, 그것은 조언하는 계급과 조언받는 계급 사이에 벗으로서의 대등함이 전혀 없었기 때문 아닐까요? 사람마다 제 형제 된 사람과 떨어져 그를 시샘하는, 그리스도인답지 못하고 외따로 떨어진 자리에 서야 했기 때문에요. 제 권리가 침범당할까 늘 두려워하면서요.”\n\n“저는 사실만을 말씀드립니다. 안타깝게도 8시에 약속이 있어서, 오늘 밤은 사실을 있는 그대로 받아들일 뿐 그 까닭을 밝히려 애쓰지는 못하겠습니다. 사실 그것을 밝힌들 지금 형편에서 어떻게 행동할지를 정하는 데는 아무런 차이도 없을 테고요. 사실은 사실대로 인정해야 하니까요.”\n\n“하지만,” 마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “제게는 그것이 세상의 온갖 차이를 만드는 것처럼 보이는데요.” 아버지가 그녀에게 조용히 하고 손턴 씨가 할 말을 마치게 하라는 신호를 보냈다. 그는 이미 일어서서 갈 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n“이 한 가지는 인정해 주셔야 합니다. 다크셔 사람이라면 누구에게나 강한 자립심이 있다고 할 때, 그가 어떻게 행동해야 하는가에 대한 제 견해를 다른 사람에게 들이밀 권리가 제게 있습니까? 제 자신이라면 그런 일을 더없이 격렬하게 싫어할 텐데, 그저 그에게는 팔 노동이 있고 제게는 살 자본이 있다는 이유만으로 말입니다.”\n\n“조금도 없어요.” 마거릿이 이 한마디만은 하겠다고 마음먹고 말했다. “선생님의 노동과 자본의 처지가 어떻든 그것 때문이 아니라, 선생님이 한 사람의 인간으로서 여러 사람을 상대하고 계시기 때문에 조금도 없는 거예요. 선생님이 그 힘을 쓰기를 마다하시든 아니든, 선생님은 그들에게 엄청난 힘을 지니고 계세요. 바로 선생님의 삶과 안녕이 그들의 것과 그토록 끊임없이 그리고 깊이 얽혀 있기 때문이지요. 하느님은 우리가 서로에게 기대며 살도록 우리를 만드셨어요. 우리는 우리 자신이 기대고 있음을 모른 척할 수도 있고, 주급을 치르는 일 말고도 여러 면에서 남들이 우리에게 기대고 있음을 인정하지 않을 수도 있어요. 그래도 그것은 엄연한 사실이에요. 선생님도 다른 어느 공장주도 어쩌지 못하는 일이지요. 가장 자랑스럽게 홀로 선 사람도 제 성품과 제 삶에 미치는 주위 사람들의 알 수 없는 영향에 기대고 있어요. 그리고 다크셔의 그 온갖 자아 가운데 가장 외따로 선 자아에게도 사방에서 매달리는 사람들이 있어요. 그는 그들을 떨쳐 낼 수 없어요. 그가 닮은 그 커다란 바위가 떨쳐 낼 수 없듯이……”\n\n“비유는 그만두어라, 마거릿. 벌써 한 번 우리를 옆길로 이끌지 않았니.” 아버지가 웃으며 말했으나, 손턴 씨를 그의 뜻과 달리 붙들고 있다는 생각에 마음이 편치 않았다. 그것은 오해였다. 마거릿이 이야기를 하는 한 그는 오히려 그것이 좋았기 때문이다. 그녀가 하는 말이 그를 짜증 나게 할 뿐이었는데도.\n\n“한 가지만 말씀해 주십시오, 헤일 양. 헤일 양 자신은 누군가에게 영향을 받은 적이 — 아니, 그렇게 묻는 것은 공정하지 않겠군요 — 그러니까 사정 때문이 아니라 다른 사람들 때문에 영향을 받고 있다고 느끼신 적이 있다면, 그 사람들은 직접 움직였습니까, 아니면 에둘러 움직였습니까? 그들이 타이르고 당부하고 본보기가 되려고 애써 올바르게 행동했습니까? 아니면 소박하고 참된 사람들로서 제 의무를 떠맡아 흔들림 없이 해냈을 뿐, 제 행동이 이 사람을 부지런하게 만들고 저 사람을 알뜰하게 만들지 어떨지는 생각조차 하지 않았습니까? 아니, 제가 직공이라면, 일하는 시간 밖의 제 살아가는 방식에 대한 어떤 참견보다도 — 아무리 좋은 뜻이라 해도 — 제 주인이 하는 모든 일에서 정직하고 시간을 지키고 재빠르고 단호하다는 것을 아는 데서 스무 배는 더 깊은 감명을 받을 것입니다. 직공은 시종보다도 눈이 밝은 염탐꾼이니까요. 저는 제 자신이 어떤 사람인지 너무 세세히 따져 볼 마음은 없습니다. 그러나 저는 제 직공들의 올곧은 정직함과 그들이 맞서는 방식의 떳떳함에 기대고 있다고 믿습니다. 어떤 공장에서 이번 파업이 다루어질 방식과는 사뭇 다르게 말이지요. 오로지 제가 단 한 번도 떳떳하지 못한 이득을 취하거나 뒷구멍으로 무슨 짓을 하는 것을 스스로 경멸한다는 것을 그들이 알기 때문입니다. 그것은 ‘정직이 최선의 방책’이라는 강의를 통째로 듣는 것보다 훨씬 멀리 갑니다. 말로 묽어진 삶보다 말이지요. 아니, 아닙니다! 공장주가 어떤 사람이냐, 그것이 곧 직공들이 어떤 사람이 되느냐입니다. 그가 지나치게 마음 쓰지 않아도 말입니다.”\n\n“대단한 인정이시네요.” 마거릿이 웃으며 말했다. “사내들이 제 권리를 좇아 사납고 고집스럽게 구는 것을 보면, 저는 공장주도 마찬가지라고 마음 놓고 미루어 짐작해도 되겠군요. 오래 참고 온유하며 제 것을 구하지 않는 그 마음을 그가 조금 모른다고요.”\n\n“헤일 양은 우리 제도가 어떻게 돌아가는지 모르는 낯선 사람들과 꼭 같으시군요.” 그가 서둘러 말했다. “우리 직공들이 우리가 원하는 어떤 곱상한 모양으로든 빚어낼 수 있는 밀가루 반죽 인형이라고 생각하시는군요. 우리가 그들의 삶의 3분의 1도 안 되는 시간만 상대한다는 것을 잊고 계십니다. 그리고 공장주의 의무가 그저 노동을 부리는 사람의 의무보다 훨씬 크고 넓다는 것을 알아보지 못하시는 듯합니다. 우리에게는 지켜 나가야 할 넓은 상업적 인격이 있고, 그것이 우리를 문명의 위대한 개척자로 만듭니다.”\n\n“제 생각에는,” 헤일 씨가 웃으며 말했다. “집안부터 조금 개척하셔도 좋을 듯합니다. 손턴 씨의 그 밀턴 사람들은 거칠고 이교도 같은 무리들이니까요.”\n\n“그렇지요.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “그들에게 장미수 같은 외과술은 통하지 않습니다. 크롬웰이라면 훌륭한 공장주가 되었을 겁니다, 헤일 양. 이 파업을 눌러 줄 크롬웰이 우리에게 있었으면 좋겠군요.”\n\n“크롬웰은 제 영웅이 아니에요.” 그녀가 차갑게 말했다. “다만 저는 전제에 대한 선생님의 찬탄과 다른 사람의 자립적인 성품에 대한 존중을 어떻게 맞추어야 할지 애쓰고 있을 뿐이에요.”\n\n그는 그녀의 말투에 얼굴이 붉어졌다. “저는 그들이 저를 위해 일하는 시간 동안에는 제 직공들의, 따져 물을 수 없고 책임지지 않는 주인이기를 택합니다. 그러나 그 시간이 지나면 우리 관계는 끝나고, 그때는 제 자신이 요구하는 것과 똑같은 자립에 대한 존중이 들어섭니다.”\n\n그는 잠시 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 너무 속이 상했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 그것을 떨쳐 내고 헤일 씨 부부에게 밤 인사를 했다. 그러고는 마거릿에게 다가가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다.\n\n“오늘 저녁에 한 번 성급하게, 그리고 아무래도 다소 무례하게 말씀드렸군요. 하지만 아시다시피 저는 투박한 밀턴 공장주일 뿐입니다. 용서해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“물론이죠.” 그녀가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 미소 지었다. 그 얼굴은 어딘가 근심스럽고 짓눌린 표정이었고, 두 사람의 논쟁이 남긴 북풍의 기색이 말끔히 사라진 그녀의 다정하고 환한 얼굴을 마주하고도 좀처럼 개지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀지 않았고, 그는 다시 그 빠뜨림을 느끼고는 그것을 자존심 탓으로 돌렸다.\n\n# 제16장\n\n“가려진 그 손을 믿으라, 어느 누구도 스스로 가고자 하는 길로는 이끌지 않는 그 손을. 그리고 늘 변화에 대비하라, 세상의 법이란 밀물과 썰물이니.” — 아라비아 시에서.\n\n이튿날 오후 도널드슨 의사가 헤일 부인을 처음으로 진찰하러 왔다. 요사이 부쩍 가까워진 살림살이가 그 비밀스러움을 허물어 주었으려니 마거릿은 바랐지만, 비밀스러움은 다시 되살아났다. 그녀는 방에서 밀려났고 딕슨이 대신 들어갔다. 마거릿은 쉬이 정을 주는 사람이 아니었으나, 한번 사랑하면 뜨겁게 사랑했고 거기에는 적잖은 질투심도 섞여 있었다.\n\n그녀는 응접실 바로 뒤에 있는 어머니의 침실로 들어가, 의사가 나오기를 기다리며 방 안을 서성거렸다. 이따금 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다. 신음 소리가 들리는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 숨을 죽였다. 분명 신음 소리였다. 그러고는 몇 분 더 모든 것이 조용했다. 이윽고 의자 움직이는 소리, 높아진 목소리, 작별 인사에 따르는 온갖 자잘한 부산함이 들려왔다.\n\n문 열리는 소리가 나자 그녀는 얼른 침실에서 나왔다.\n\n“아버지는 지금 집에 안 계세요, 도널드슨 선생님. 이 시간에는 학생을 가르치러 나가셔야 하거든요. 번거로우시겠지만 아래층 아버지 방으로 내려가 주시겠어요?”\n\n딕슨이 앞을 가로막느라 놓는 온갖 훼방을 그녀는 알아차렸고, 또 이겨 냈다. 이 집 딸로서 마땅히 누릴 자리를 맏이의 기개 같은 것으로 내세우자 늙은 하녀의 참견은 아주 효험 있게 잦아들었다. 딕슨을 상대로 이 예사롭지 않은 위엄을 스스로 알면서 꾸며 보인 것이, 걱정 한복판에서 잠깐이나마 그녀를 재미있게 했다. 딕슨의 놀란 얼굴빛을 보고 그녀는 자기가 얼마나 우스꽝스럽도록 위풍당당해 보였을지 알 수 있었다. 그 생각에 실려 그녀는 아래층 방까지 내려갔고, 그 덕분에 지금 눈앞에 놓인 일에 대한 서슬 퍼런 기억을 그만큼 잊고 있을 수 있었다. 이제 그것이 되돌아와 숨을 앗아 가는 듯했다. 한마디 말을 꺼내기까지 잠깐의 시간이 필요했다.\n\n그러나 물음을 던지는 그녀의 목소리에는 명령하는 기운이 서려 있었다.\n\n“어머니가 어떠신가요? 있는 그대로의 사실만 말씀해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.” 그러고는 의사가 살짝 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n“저는 어머니의 하나뿐인 자식이에요. 여기서는 그렇다는 말씀입니다. 아버지는 아직 사태를 충분히 걱정하지 않으시는 듯해요. 그러니 정말 심각한 우려가 있다면 아버지께는 조심스럽게 알려 드려야 합니다. 그 일은 제가 할 수 있어요. 어머니 병간호도 제가 할 수 있고요. 부디 말씀해 주세요, 선생님. 선생님 얼굴을 보면서도 그 뜻을 읽어 내지 못하는 것이, 선생님 입에서 나올 어떤 말보다도 저를 더 두렵게 합니다.”\n\n“아가씨, 어머님께는 더없이 살뜰하고 유능한 하녀가 있는 것 같더군요. 하녀라기보다 벗에 가까운……”\n\n“저는 어머니의 딸입니다, 선생님.”\n\n“하지만 어머님께서 아가씨에게는 알리지 말라고 분명히 당부하셨다고 말씀드린다면……”\n\n“저는 그런 금지에 순순히 따를 만큼 착하지도 참을성 있지도 못합니다. 게다가 선생님은 그 비밀을 지키겠다고 약속하시기에는 너무 지혜롭고 겪으신 일도 많은 분이라고 믿습니다.”\n\n“그렇군요.” 그가 슬픔이 짙게 배어 있으면서도 반쯤 웃음을 띠며 말했다. “그 점은 아가씨 말이 옳습니다. 나는 약속하지 않았어요. 사실은 내가 밝히지 않더라도 그 비밀이 머잖아 저절로 알려질 것 같아 걱정입니다.”\n\n그가 말을 멈추었다. 그러자 마거릿은 얼굴이 몹시 하얘지며 입술을 조금 더 굳게 다물었다. 그 밖에는 이목구비 하나 움직이지 않았다. 사람 됨됨이를 재빨리 꿰뚫어 보는 눈, 그것 없이는 어떤 의사도 도널드슨 의사만 한 명성에 오를 수 없는 그 눈으로 그는 알아보았다. 이 아가씨는 온전한 진실을 요구하리라는 것을, 티끌만 한 것 하나라도 감추면 알아채리라는 것을, 그리고 감추는 것이야말로 아는 것보다 더 지독한 고문이 되리라는 것을. 그는 낮은 목소리로 짧은 두 문장을 말하며 내내 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그녀의 눈동자는 검은 공포로 커졌고, 흰 낯빛은 납빛으로 질렸다. 그가 말을 그쳤다. 그는 그 표정이 걷히기를, 그녀의 헐떡이는 숨결이 돌아오기를 기다렸다. 이윽고 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“선생님, 이렇게 믿고 말씀해 주셔서 진심으로 감사합니다. 그 두려움은 몇 주째 저를 따라다녔어요. 정말이지 참되고 실제적인 고통이었습니다. 가엾은, 가엾은 어머니!” 이제 그녀의 입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 그는 그녀가 눈물로 마음을 덜어 내도록 내버려 두었다. 스스로 그것을 그칠 힘이 있는 사람임을 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n몇 방울의 눈물, 그것이 그녀가 흘린 전부였다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 묻고 싶던 숱한 물음들을 떠올렸다.\n\n“많이 아프실까요?”\n\n그가 고개를 저었다. “그건 우리도 알 수 없습니다. 타고난 체질에 달렸고, 또 수천 가지 것들에 달렸지요. 하지만 근래 의학이 알아낸 것들 덕분에 우리에게는 고통을 덜어 줄 힘이 꽤 커졌습니다.”\n\n“아버지는요!” 마거릿이 온몸을 떨며 말했다.\n\n“나는 헤일 씨를 잘 모릅니다. 그러니까 조언을 드리기가 어렵다는 말입니다. 그래도 이렇게는 말씀드리지요. 아가씨가 나에게서 억지로 끌어낸 그 앎을, 이토록 갑작스레 알게 된 그 앎을 견디며 버티세요. 내가 감출 수 없었던 그 사실이 아가씨에게 어느 정도 익숙해질 때까지 말입니다. 그래야 지나치게 애쓰지 않고도 아버님께 드릴 수 있는 위로를 드릴 수 있을 테니까요. 그때가 오기 전에, 물론 나는 앞으로도 이따금 다시 찾아오겠습니다만, 고통을 덜어 드리는 것 말고는 아무것도 해 드릴 수 없을까 걱정이군요, 아무튼 그전에 사소한 일들이 수천 가지나 일어나 아버님의 불안을 깨우고 또 깊게 할 겁니다. 그러니 그만큼 마음의 준비가 되실 테지요. 아니요, 아가씨, 아닙니다, 그러지 마세요. 나는 손턴 씨를 만나 보았습니다. 그리고 아버님이 아무리 잘못 생각하신 것이라 내가 여기더라도, 그분이 치른 그 희생만은 존경합니다. 그래요, 이번 한 번은, 그것이 아가씨 마음에 든다면 그렇게 하지요. 다만 기억해 두세요. 내가 다시 올 때는 벗으로서 오는 겁니다. 그리고 아가씨도 나를 그런 사람으로 여기는 법을 익혀야 합니다. 이런 때에 서로를 보고 서로를 알아 가는 것은 여러 해 동안의 아침 인사보다 값진 일이니까요.”\n\n마거릿은 울음 때문에 말을 할 수 없었다. 다만 헤어질 때 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다.\n\n“내가 말하는 훌륭한 아가씨란 바로 저런 사람이지!” 도널드슨 의사는 마차에 올라앉아, 그녀의 악력에 조금 시달린 반지 낀 손을 살펴볼 여유가 생기자 이렇게 생각했다. “저 작은 손이 그토록 세게 쥘 수 있으리라고 누가 생각이나 했겠나? 그렇지만 뼈대가 야무지게 짜여 있으니 그것이 엄청난 힘을 내는 게지. 참으로 여왕 같은 아가씨야! 처음에는 나에게서 진실을 끌어내려고 고개를 뒤로 젖히더니, 그다음에는 들으려고 그토록 간절하게 몸을 앞으로 숙이더군. 가엾은 것! 저 아가씨가 제 몸을 너무 혹사하지 않도록 살펴야겠어. 하기야 이런 순종 같은 사람들이 얼마나 많은 일을 해내고 또 얼마나 견뎌 내는지는 놀랍기 그지없지. 저 아가씨는 뼛속까지 배짱이 있어. 다른 사람이 그런 죽음 같은 낯빛이 되었다면 실신하거나 발작을 일으키지 않고서는 결코 제정신으로 돌아오지 못했을 게야. 그런데 저 아가씨는 둘 다 하지 않았어. 절대로! 오직 제 의지의 힘만으로 스스로를 되돌려 놓았지. 저런 아가씨라면 내가 30년만 젊었어도 내 마음을 사로잡았을 텐데. 이제는 너무 늦었지. 아, 벌써 아처 씨 댁에 다 왔군.” 그러고는 생각과 지혜와 경험과 연민을 지닌 채, 마치 이 세상에 이 집 말고는 다른 집이 없기라도 한 듯 이 집안이 그것들에 요구하는 무엇에든 응할 채비를 하고서 그는 마차에서 뛰어내렸다.\n\n그러는 동안 마거릿은 위층 어머니 앞에 나서기 전에 기운을 되찾으려고 잠시 아버지의 서재로 돌아가 있었다.\n\n“오, 하느님, 하느님! 이건 너무나 끔찍해요. 제가 어떻게 견디죠? 그런 죽을병이라니! 희망도 없다니! 아, 어머니, 어머니, 쇼 이모 댁에 가지 말걸, 그 소중한 세월을 어머니 곁을 떠나 보내지 말걸! 가엾은 어머니! 얼마나 참아 오셨을까! 오, 하느님, 비오니 어머니의 고통이 너무 모질지 않게, 너무 무섭지 않게 해 주세요. 그것을 지켜보는 일을 제가 어떻게 견디죠? 아버지의 괴로움을 제가 어떻게 견디죠? 아직 아버지께 알려서는 안 돼. 한꺼번에는 안 돼. 그러면 아버지가 죽고 말 거야. 하지만 내 소중하고 귀한 어머니와 보낼 시간을 이제 한순간도 잃지 않을 테야.”\n\n그녀는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 딕슨은 방에 없었다. 헤일 부인은 안락의자에 몸을 기대고 있었다. 부드러운 흰 숄을 두르고 어울리는 실내 모자를 쓴 채였는데, 의사의 방문을 기다려 그렇게 차려입은 것이었다. 얼굴에는 옅은 혈색이 조금 돌았고, 진찰 뒤의 그 지친 기색이 오히려 얼굴을 평온해 보이게 했다. 마거릿은 어머니가 그토록 차분해 보이는 데 놀랐다.\n\n“아니, 마거릿, 얼굴이 왜 그러니! 무슨 일이야?” 그러고는 실제 사정이 어떠한지가 마음에 슬며시 떠오르자, 조금 언짢은 듯이 덧붙였다. “너 설마 도널드슨 선생을 만나서 이것저것 캐물은 건 아니겠지, 얘야?” 마거릿은 대답하지 않고 그저 애타는 눈으로 어머니를 바라볼 뿐이었다. 헤일 부인은 더 언짢아졌다. “그 사람이 나에게 한 약속을 어겼을 리는 없을 텐데, 그럼”—\n\n“아니에요, 어머니, 말씀해 주셨어요. 제가 억지로 말하게 했어요. 저예요, 저를 나무라세요.” 그녀는 어머니 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그 손을 붙잡았다. 헤일 부인이 손을 빼내려 했지만 그녀는 놓아 주지 않았다. 그녀는 그 손에 입을 맞추고 또 맞추었고, 뜨거운 눈물이 흘러내려 그 손을 적셨다.\n\n“마거릿, 그건 아주 잘못한 일이야. 내가 네게 알리고 싶어 하지 않는 걸 알았잖니.” 그러나 실랑이에 지친 듯 부인은 마거릿이 쥔 손을 그대로 내버려 두었고, 이윽고 희미하게나마 그 손을 마주 쥐어 주었다. 그것에 힘을 얻어 마거릿이 입을 열었다.\n\n“아, 어머니! 제가 어머니를 간호하게 해 주세요. 딕슨이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 건 무엇이든 배울게요. 하지만 저는 어머니의 자식이잖아요. 저에게는 어머니를 위해 무엇이든 할 권리가 있다고 정말로 믿어요.”\n\n“네가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 모르는구나.” 헤일 부인이 몸서리치며 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 알아요. 어머니가 아시는 것보다 훨씬 많이 알아요. 제가 간호하게 해 주세요. 어쨌든 해 보게라도 해 주세요. 제가 애쓰는 만큼 애쓴 사람은 여태 아무도 없었고 앞으로도 없을 거예요. 그러면 얼마나 마음이 놓이겠어요, 어머니.”\n\n“가엾은 것! 그래, 해 보려무나. 마거릿, 그거 아니? 딕슨과 나는 네가 알게 되면 나를 아주 꺼릴 거라고 생각했단다—”\n\n“딕슨이 그렇게 생각했다고요!” 마거릿이 입술을 비죽이며 말했다. “딕슨은 제가 참된 사랑을 그만큼 지녔다는 걸, 자기만큼이나 지녔다는 걸 인정해 주지 못한 거로군요! 아마 저를 장미 잎사귀 위에 누워 온종일 부채질이나 받고 싶어 하는 그 딱하고 병약한 여자들 가운데 하나로 여긴 모양이지요. 어머니, 다시는 딕슨의 그런 억측이 어머니와 저 사이에 끼어들게 하지 마세요. 제발요!” 그녀가 애원했다.\n\n“딕슨한테 화내지 마라.” 헤일 부인이 걱정스레 말했다. 마거릿은 마음을 가라앉혔다.\n\n“네! 화내지 않을게요. 겸손해지려고 애쓰고 그이의 방식도 배울게요. 어머니를 위해 제가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하게만 해 주신다면요. 저를 첫째 자리에 놓아 주세요, 어머니. 저는 그것이 욕심나요. 제가 쇼 이모 댁에 가 있는 동안 어머니가 저를 잊으실 거라고 공연히 상상하곤 했어요. 그 생각을 머리에 담은 채 밤마다 울다 잠들었고요.”\n\n“나는 이런 생각을 하곤 했지. 할리 가의 그 온갖 안락과 사치를 누리다 왔는데 마거릿이 우리의 궁색한 살림을 어떻게 견딜까 하고. 그래서 헬스톤에서 우리가 궁여지책으로 마련해 놓은 것들을 낯모르는 남이 알아채는 것보다 네가 보는 것이 더 부끄러웠던 적이 여러 번이었단다.”\n\n“아, 어머니! 저는 그것들이 정말 좋았어요. 할리 가의 그 따분하고 늘 똑같은 방식보다 훨씬 재미있었는걸요. 큰일이 있을 때면 저녁 쟁반 노릇을 하던, 손잡이 달린 옷장 선반이라니! 속을 채우고 천을 씌워 오토만으로 쓰던 낡은 차 상자들이라니! 정든 헬스톤에서 어머니가 궁여지책이라 부르시는 그 재간들이야말로 그곳 살림의 아주 사랑스러운 한 부분이었다고 저는 생각해요.”\n\n“나는 다시는 헬스톤을 보지 못할 거다, 마거릿.” 헤일 부인이 두 눈에 눈물을 그렁그렁 담고 말했다. 마거릿은 대답할 수 없었다. 헤일 부인이 말을 이었다. “거기 있을 때는 내내 떠나고만 싶었지. 어디든 다른 곳이 더 좋아 보였어. 그런데 이제 나는 그곳에서 멀리 떨어져 죽게 되겠구나. 마땅한 벌을 받는 거야.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀하지 마세요.” 마거릿이 참지 못하고 말했다. “선생님이 몇 해는 더 사실 수 있다고 하셨어요. 아, 어머니! 우리는 어머니를 다시 헬스톤으로 모셔 갈 거예요.”\n\n“아니야, 절대로! 그건 마땅한 속죄로 받아들여야지. 그런데 마거릿, 프레더릭이!”\n\n그 한마디를 입에 올린 순간, 부인은 무슨 날카로운 고통에라도 찔린 듯 갑자기 큰 소리로 울부짖었다. 아들 생각이 그녀의 침착함을 모조리 무너뜨리고 평온을 부수고 지친 기운마저 이겨 낸 듯했다. 사납고 격한 외침이 잇달아 터져 나왔다. “프레더릭! 프레더릭! 나에게 오너라. 내가 죽어 간다. 첫아이야, 한 번만 더 나에게 와 다오!”\n\n부인은 심한 발작에 빠졌다. 마거릿은 겁에 질려 딕슨을 부르러 갔다. 딕슨은 잔뜩 골이 나서 들어오더니 마거릿이 어머니를 너무 흥분시켰다고 나무랐다. 마거릿은 아버지가 돌아오지 않기만을 바라며 모든 것을 고분고분 받아들였다. 사태가 요구하는 것보다도 더 큰 놀라움에 사로잡혀 있었으면서도, 그녀는 변명 한마디 없이 딕슨의 지시를 하나하나 재빠르고 야무지게 따랐다. 그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 자기를 나무라던 사람을 누그러뜨렸다. 두 사람은 어머니를 침대에 눕혔고, 마거릿은 어머니가 잠들 때까지, 그러고도 딕슨이 방 밖으로 손짓해 부를 때까지 곁을 지켰다. 딕슨은 마음에 없는 일을 하는 사람처럼 시큰둥한 얼굴로, 응접실에 마련해 둔 커피를 마시라고 이르고는 그녀가 마시는 동안 명령하는 자세로 옆에 버티고 서 있었다.\n\n“그렇게 캐묻지 말았어야죠, 아가씨. 그랬으면 때도 되기 전에 속을 태울 일도 없었을 텐데요. 어차피 머잖아 다 알게 될 일이었어요. 그런데 이제는 주인어른께도 이르시겠지요. 그럼 제 살림 꼴이 아주 볼만하겠구먼요!”\n\n“아니야, 딕슨.” 마거릿이 서글프게 말했다. “아버지께는 말씀드리지 않을 거야. 아버지는 나만큼 견디지 못하실 테니까.” 그러고는 자기가 얼마나 잘 견디는지 증명이라도 하듯 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“저런! 이럴 줄 알았지요. 이제 마님을 깨우시겠어요, 그렇게 곱게 잠드신 지 얼마 되지도 않았는데. 마거릿 아가씨, 저는 이걸 몇 주째 속에 눌러 담고 지냈답니다. 제가 아가씨만큼 마님을 사랑한다고 우길 생각은 없습니다만, 그래도 저는 그 어떤 남자보다도 여자보다도 아이보다도 마님을 더 사랑했어요. 제 마음속에서 마님에 견줄 만한 사람은 프레더릭 도련님밖에 없었지요. 베러스퍼드 부인의 하녀가 처음으로 저를 데려가, 흰 크레이프 옷에 이삭과 진홍빛 양귀비로 단장하신 마님을 보여 주었을 때부터 그랬습니다. 그때 제가 바늘을 손가락에 찔러 부러뜨렸는데, 사람들이 그것을 도려낸 뒤에 마님이 손수 수놓으신 손수건을 찢어 감아 주셨고, 무도회에서 돌아오시자, 그날 밤 모인 이들 가운데 가장 어여쁜 아가씨셨지요, 다시 들러 붕대를 물약으로 적셔 주셨답니다. 그 뒤로 저는 마님만큼 누구를 사랑해 본 적이 없어요. 그때는 마님이 이토록 기울어지시는 것을 제 눈으로 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐지요. 누구를 탓하려는 게 아닙니다. 아가씨더러도 예쁘다느니 곱다느니 하는 사람이 많지요. 눈이 멀 만큼 매캐한 이 고장에서도 올빼미쯤이면 그것쯤은 알아보겠지요. 하지만 아가씨는 아름다움에서만큼은 결코 어머님 같지 못할 겁니다. 결코요. 100살까지 사신다 해도요.”\n\n“어머니는 지금도 아주 고우신걸. 가엾은 어머니!”\n\n“자, 또 시작하지 마세요. 안 그러면 저마저 끝내 무너지고 말 테니까요.” (훌쩍이며) “이래서야 주인어른이 돌아와 이것저것 물으실 때 못 버티십니다. 나가서 좀 걷다가 사람 꼴을 하고 들어오세요. 저도 마님이 어떤 병인지, 그것이 어떻게 끝날 수밖에 없는지 하는 생각을 걸어서 떨쳐 버리고 싶던 적이 한두 번이 아니었답니다.”\n\n“아, 딕슨!” 마거릿이 말했다. “딕슨이 얼마나 무서운 비밀을 안고 있는 줄도 모르고 내가 얼마나 자주 딕슨한테 짜증을 냈던지!”\n\n“아이고, 아가씨! 저는 아가씨가 그렇게 기개를 보이시는 게 좋습니다요. 그게 바로 훌륭한 베러스퍼드 집안의 피지요. 아, 세 대 위의 존 경께서는 소작인들을 쥐어짠다고 말했다는 이유 하나로 집사를 그 자리에서 쏘아 죽이셨답니다. 그러고도 부싯돌에서 가죽을 벗겨 낼 수 없듯 더는 한 푼도 나오지 않을 때까지 소작인들을 쥐어짜셨지요.”\n\n“그래, 딕슨, 나는 딕슨을 쏘지 않을 거야. 그리고 다시는 짜증 내지 않도록 애써 볼게.”\n\n“아가씨는 그런 적 없으십니다. 제가 이따금 그런 말을 했다면 그건 늘 혼잣말이었어요. 그저 혼자 조금 재미난 이야기라도 나누어 보자고 한 것뿐이지요. 여기서는 말을 나눌 만한 사람이 없으니까요. 그리고 아가씨가 발끈하실 때면 영락없이 프레더릭 도련님을 빼닮으셨답니다. 저는 아무 날이나 아가씨를 성나게 해서, 그 폭풍우 같은 표정이 커다란 구름장처럼 얼굴 위로 몰려오는 걸 보고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같을 정도예요. 하지만 이제는 나가세요, 아가씨. 마님은 제가 지켜보겠습니다. 주인어른이야 들어오신다 해도 책이면 충분한 벗이 되어 드리지요.”\n\n“나갈게.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그녀는 두려운 듯 망설이는 듯 잠깐 딕슨 곁을 서성이더니, 불쑥 딕슨에게 입을 맞추고는 얼른 방을 나갔다.\n\n“복 받으실 거야!” 딕슨이 말했다. “참으로 알차고 여문 아가씨지. 내가 사랑하는 사람은 셋뿐이야. 마님과 프레더릭 도련님과 저 아가씨. 딱 그 셋이지. 그게 다야. 나머지는 다 목매달아도 좋아. 그 사람들이 대체 뭐 하러 세상에 나왔는지 나는 모르겠으니까. 주인어른이야 마님과 혼인하시려고 태어나셨겠지. 그분이 마님을 제대로 사랑하신다고 믿을 수만 있다면 나도 언젠가는 그분을 사랑하게 될지 몰라. 하지만 그분은 마님을 훨씬 더 위해 드렸어야 했어. 늘 읽고 또 읽고, 생각하고 또 생각하지만 말고. 그래서 어떤 꼴이 되었는지 보라지. 읽지도 생각하지도 않는 사람들 가운데도 교구 목사가 되고 사제장이 되고 뭐가 되고 하는 이가 숱하잖아. 주인어른도 마님 말씀만 새겨듣고 그 지긋지긋한 읽기와 생각하기를 내버려 두셨더라면 그렇게 되셨을지도 모르지. — 저기 나가시는군.” (현관문 닫히는 소리를 듣고 창밖을 내다보며) “가엾은 아가씨! 한 해 전 헬스톤에 오셨을 때에 견주면 옷차림이 초라해 보이는구나. 그때는 옷장에 기운 양말 한 짝, 세탁한 장갑 한 켤레도 없었는데. 그런데 지금은—!”\n\n# 제17장\n\n“길마다 가시덤불이 가로놓여 있어 참을성 있는 보살핌을 부르고, 어떤 처지에나 십자가가 하나씩 있어 간절히 기도할 일이 있네.” — 무명씨.\n\n마거릿은 무거운 마음으로 마지못해 집을 나섰다. 그러나 거리 하나를 지나는 동안, 그렇다, 밀턴 거리의 공기가 첫 모퉁이에 이르기도 전에 그녀의 젊은 피를 북돋웠다. 걸음이 가벼워지고 입술이 붉어졌다. 그토록 안으로만 향해 있던 생각 대신 그녀는 주위를 살피기 시작했다. 거리에는 여느 때와 달리 어정거리는 사람들이 보였다. 주머니에 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁거리는 사내들, 무리 지어 몰려다니며 큰 소리로 웃고 떠드는 처녀들이 잔뜩 들뜬 기분과 거리낌 없는 기질과 몸가짐으로 흥청거리고 있었다. 사내들 가운데 더 험상궂은 축, 그 낯부끄러운 소수는 맥줏집과 진 가게 계단께에 죽치고 서서 담배를 피우며 지나가는 사람마다 제법 거리낌 없이 품평을 해 댔다. 마거릿은 가려고 마음먹었던 들판에 이르기까지 이 거리들을 오래 걸어야 한다는 것이 마뜩잖았다. 그러느니 베시 히긴스를 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다. 조용한 시골 산책만큼 상쾌하지는 않겠지만, 그래도 어쩌면 그편이 더 친절한 일일 터였다.\n\n그녀가 들어섰을 때 니컬러스 히긴스는 난롯가에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있었다. 베시는 맞은편에서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었다.\n\n니컬러스는 입에서 파이프를 빼고 일어서서 자기 의자를 마거릿 쪽으로 밀어 주었다. 그러고는 벽난로 선반에 느긋하게 몸을 기댔고, 그동안 마거릿은 베시에게 몸이 어떠냐고 물었다.\n\n“쟤가 기분으로 치면 좀 처져 있소만, 몸은 나아졌구먼. 쟤는 이 파업이 마음에 안 드는 게지. 무슨 값을 치르든 조용하고 편한 것만 지나치게 바라니까.”\n\n“이게 내가 본 세 번째 파업이에요.” 그녀가 그것으로 대답도 설명도 다 되었다는 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n“흥, 세 번째면 다 갚아 주는 법이지. 이번에야말로 공장주들을 혼내 주는지 두고 보라구. 저들이 와서 우리가 부르는 값에 돌아와 달라고 사정하는지 두고 보라니까. 그뿐이오. 전에는 그르쳤다는 것, 그건 나도 인정하겠소. 하지만 이번엔 계획을 아주 지독하게 깊이 짜 놨소.”\n\n“왜 파업을 하시죠?” 마거릿이 물었다. “파업이란 자기가 바라는 삯을 받을 때까지 일을 그만두는 것 아닌가요? 제가 아무것도 몰라도 이상하게 여기지 마세요. 제가 살던 데서는 파업이라는 말을 들어 본 적이 없거든요.”\n\n“나도 거기 살았으면 좋겠어요.” 베시가 지친 듯 말했다. “하지만 내가 파업에 넌더리를 낼 것도 없지요. 이번이 내가 볼 마지막이니까. 이게 끝나기 전에 나는 큰 도성에 가 있을 거예요. 거룩한 예루살렘에요.”\n\n“쟤는 저승 이야기로 꽉 차 있어서 이승 생각은 못 하오. 그런데 나는, 보시다시피, 여기서 할 수 있는 걸 다 해야 하는 사람이오. 손에 쥔 새 한 마리가 덤불 속 두 마리보다 낫다고 여기지. 그래서 파업 문제를 두고 우리 둘의 생각이 이렇게 다른 거요.”\n\n“하지만,” 마거릿이 말했다. “제가 살던 데서 사람들이 말씀하시는 그 파업이라는 걸 한다면, 거기는 거의 다 밭일하는 사람들이니 씨도 못 뿌리고 건초도 못 거두고 곡식도 못 벨 거예요.”\n\n“그래서?” 그가 말했다. 그는 다시 파이프를 물고는 그 “그래서”를 되묻는 투로 던졌다.\n\n“아니,” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그러면 농부들은 어떻게 되겠어요?”\n\n그가 연기를 뿜었다. “내 보기엔 농장을 내놓든지, 아니면 제대로 된 삯을 주든지 해야겠지요.”\n\n“그런데 뒤엣것을 할 수 없거나 하려 들지 않는다고 해 봐요. 아무리 그러고 싶어도 농장을 하루아침에 내놓을 수는 없잖아요. 그런데 그해에는 팔 건초도 곡식도 없을 거고요. 그러면 이듬해 일꾼들 삯을 줄 돈은 어디서 나오겠어요?”\n\n여전히 연기를 뿜을 뿐이었다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“나는 남쪽 사는 법에 대해선 아무것도 모르오. 듣기로는 거기 사람들은 기죽고 짓밟힌 무리라더군. 굶어 죽기 직전까지 가고, 굶주림에 넋이 나가서 자기가 짓밟히는 줄도 모른다더구먼. 그런데 여기는 안 그렇소. 우리는 짓밟히면 짓밟히는 줄 알고, 그걸 가만 참고 있기엔 피가 너무 뜨겁소. 우리는 그저 베틀에서 손을 떼고 이렇게 말하는 거요. ‘굶겨 죽여도 좋소, 하지만 우릴 짓밟지는 못하오, 공장주 나리들!’ 그리고 빌어먹을, 이번엔 저들 뜻대로 안 될 거요!”\n\n“나는 남쪽에서 살았으면 좋겠어요.” 베시가 말했다.\n\n“거기도 견딜 것이 많아요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “어디에나 견뎌야 할 슬픔은 있어요. 힘을 낼 만한 먹을거리도 변변찮은데 몸으로 하는 아주 고된 일을 해내야 하죠.”\n\n“그래도 바깥이잖아요.” 베시가 말했다. “끝도 없고 끝도 없는 이 소음과 사람 잡는 열기에서 멀리 떨어져 있고요.”\n\n“어떤 때는 억수 같은 빗속에서, 어떤 때는 살을 에는 추위 속에서 하는 일이에요. 젊은 사람은 견디죠. 하지만 나이 든 사람은 류머티즘으로 뒤틀리고 제 나이보다 먼저 굽고 시들어요. 그런데도 그저 똑같이 일을 이어 가야 해요. 안 그러면 구빈원으로 가야 하고요.”\n\n“아가씨는 남쪽 고장 사는 법을 그리도 좋아하는 줄 알았는데요.”\n\n“좋아하고말고요.” 마거릿이 이렇게 붙들린 것을 알고 살짝 웃으며 말했다. “다만 베시, 내 말은 이 세상 무엇에나 좋은 면과 나쁜 면이 있다는 거예요. 베시가 여기서 나쁜 면을 겪었으니 저기의 나쁜 면도 알아 두는 게 공평하겠다 싶었을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“그래서 거기서는 파업이란 걸 절대 안 한단 말이오?” 니컬러스가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n“안 해요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “다들 분별이 있어서 그런 것 같아요.”\n\n“그런데 내 생각에는,” 그가 파이프의 재를 어찌나 세차게 털어 냈던지 파이프가 부러졌다. “분별이 지나쳐서가 아니라 기개가 모자라서요.”\n\n“아이고, 아버지!” 베시가 말했다. “파업해서 얻은 게 뭐가 있어요? 어머니가 돌아가시던 그 첫 파업을 생각해 봐요. 우리 모두 얼마나 굶었어요. 아버지가 제일 심했고요. 그런데도 매주 여럿이 똑같은 삯을 받고 들어갔고, 일자리가 있는 만큼 다 들어가고 나니 그만이었잖아요. 그러고 나서 평생 거지로 산 사람도 있고요.”\n\n“그래,” 그가 말했다. “그 파업은 형편없이 굴러갔지. 그걸 이끌겠다고 나선 것들이 바보 아니면 참되지 못한 놈들이었으니까. 두고 보라구, 이번엔 다를 테니.”\n\n“그런데 지금까지 무엇 때문에 파업하시는지는 말씀 안 해 주셨어요.” 마거릿이 다시 말했다.\n\n“아, 그러니까 말이오, 공장주 대여섯이 지난 두 해 동안 주던 삯을, 그걸 주면서도 잘나가고 더 부자가 되었으면서 이제는 못 주겠다고 버티고 나선 거요. 그러고는 우리한테 와서 더 적게 받으라 하지. 우린 싫소. 우리가 먼저 저들을 굶겨 죽일 거요. 그러고 나면 누가 저들을 위해 일해 주나 두고 봅시다. 내 보기엔 저들이 황금 알을 낳아 주던 거위를 죽인 셈이 되겠지.”\n\n“그래서 저들에게 앙갚음하려고 죽을 작정을 하신다는 건가요!”\n\n“아니오,” 그가 말했다. “그런 건 아니오. 나는 그저 굽히느니 내 자리에서 죽는 쪽을 바라볼 뿐이오. 군인이 그러면 사람들이 훌륭하다, 명예롭다 하지 않소. 그럼 가난한 베짜기 놈은 왜 안 되오?”\n\n“하지만,” 마거릿이 말했다. “군인은 나라를 위해, 남을 위해 죽는걸요.”\n\n그가 씁쓸하게 웃었다. “아가씨,” 그가 말했다. “아가씨야 아직 어린 처녀지만, 내가 주급 16실링으로 세 사람을, 그러니까 베시하고 메리하고 나를 먹여 살릴 수 있을 것 같소? 내가 이번에 나 자신을 위해 일손을 놓은 줄 아시오? 이것도 저 군인만큼이나 남을 위한 일이오. 다만 다른 게 있다면, 그 군인이 위해서 죽는 상대는 어쩌면 평생 한 번 본 적도 들은 적도 없는 누군가일 텐데, 나는 두 집 건너 사는 존 바우처의 일을 떠맡는다는 거요. 그 사람은 병든 아내에 자식이 여덟인데 하나도 공장 다닐 나이가 안 됐소. 그리고 나는 그 사람 일만 떠맡는 게 아니오. 하기야 그 사람은 한 번에 베틀 두 대밖에 못 다루는 딱한 쓸모없는 위인이지만. 나는 정의라는 것의 일을 떠맡는 거요. 왜 우리가 두 해 전보다 지금 삯을 덜 받아야 하느냐, 나는 그걸 묻는 거요.”\n\n“저한테 묻지 마세요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “저는 아무것도 몰라요. 공장주들한테 물어보세요. 분명 그 까닭을 대 줄 거예요. 아무 이유 없이 제멋대로 내린 결정은 아닐 테니까요.”\n\n“아가씨는 그저 타관 사람일 뿐이구먼, 그 이상은 아니고.” 그가 업신여기듯 말했다. “뭘 안다고. 공장주들한테 물어보라니! 그자들은 우리더러 우리 일이나 신경 쓰라 할 거요, 자기들은 자기들 일 신경 쓸 테니. 우리 일이란 게 뭐냐 하면, 아시겠소, 깎인 삯을 받고 고마워하는 거지. 그리고 저들 일이란 건 제 잇속을 불리려고 우릴 굶어 죽을 지경까지 깎아 내리는 거고. 그런 거요.”\n\n“하지만,” 마거릿이 그를 언짢게 하고 있다는 것을 알면서도 물러서지 않기로 마음먹고 말했다. “장사 형편이 여러분에게 예전과 같은 삯을 줄 수 없는 지경일 수도 있잖아요.”\n\n“장사 형편이라! 그건 그저 공장주들이 늘어놓는 허튼소리요. 내가 말하는 건 삯이오. 공장주들이 장사 형편이란 걸 제 손아귀에 쥐고 있다가, 말 안 듣는 아이한테 겁을 줘서 말 잘 듣게 하려고 내세우는 시커먼 도깨비처럼 그저 앞세워 걸어 보이는 거지. 내 말하리다, 저들 몫은, 어떤 사람들 말로는 저들이 맡은 배역이지, 제 재산을 불리려고 우릴 깎아 내리는 거고, 우리 몫은 일어서서 힘껏 싸우는 거요. 우리만을 위해서가 아니라 우리 둘레 사람들을 위해서, 정의와 공정을 위해서 말이오. 우리가 저들 이익을 만드는 걸 거들었으니 그걸 쓰는 것도 거들어야 마땅하지. 이번엔 전에 여러 번 그랬던 것처럼 저들 돈이 그렇게 급한 것도 아니오. 우리는 모아 둔 돈이 있고, 함께 서고 함께 쓰러지기로 뜻을 모았소. 조합이 우리 몫이라고 하는 것보다 적은 삯을 받고 들어갈 사람은 우리 중에 하나도 없소. 그러니 나는 ‘파업 만세’라고 하겠소. 손턴이든 슬릭슨이든 햄퍼든 그 패거리든 어디 두고 보라지!”\n\n“손턴이요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “말버러 가의 손턴 씨요?”\n\n“그렇소! 우리끼리는 말버러 공장의 손턴이라 부르지.”\n\n“그분도 여러분이 맞서 싸우는 공장주 가운데 하나인가요? 어떤 공장주인가요?”\n\n“불도그를 본 적 있소? 불도그를 뒷다리로 세워 놓고 저고리에 바지를 입혀 보시오. 그게 딱 존 손턴이오.”\n\n“아니에요.” 마거릿이 웃으며 말했다. “그건 아니라고 하겠어요. 손턴 씨가 잘생기지는 않았지만, 짧고 넓적한 코에 으르렁대는 윗입술을 한 불도그 같지는 않아요.”\n\n“아니, 생김새 말은 아니오, 그건 인정하겠소. 하지만 존 손턴이 어떤 생각을 한번 붙들었다 하면 불도그처럼 물고 늘어질 거요. 쇠스랑으로 끌어내려 해도 놓지 않을 사람이지. 존 손턴은 싸워 볼 만한 상대요. 슬릭슨으로 말하자면, 내 보기에 그자는 언젠가 그럴싸한 약속으로 제 직공들을 구슬려 데려갈 거요. 그러고는 다시 제 손아귀에 들어오기가 무섭게 그 약속에서 감쪽같이 속여 먹겠지. 벌금을 톡톡히 우려낼 게 뻔하오. 뱀장어처럼 미끄러운 자요. 고양이 같지, 매끈하고 교활하고 사납고. 그자하고는 손턴처럼 정직하게 맞붙어 싸우는 일은 결코 없을 거요. 손턴은 대못처럼 뻣뻣한 사람이오. 발끝까지 고집불통이지. 그 늙은 불도그!”\n\n“가엾은 베시!” 마거릿이 그녀 쪽으로 돌아앉으며 말했다. “이 모든 일에 한숨만 쉬는군요. 아버지처럼 겨루고 싸우는 걸 좋아하지 않죠, 그렇죠?”\n\n“네!” 그녀가 무겁게 말했다. “넌더리가 나요. 나는 마지막 날들에 다른 이야기를 들으며 지냈으면 했어요. 일이니 삯이니 공장주니 직공이니 파업 파괴자니 하며 부딪히고 쨍강거리고 덜컹거리는, 평생 나를 지치게만 한 그 소리 말고요.”\n\n“가엾은 것, 마지막 날들은 무슨 마지막 날들이냐! 조금 부산해지고 달라지니 벌써 훨씬 나아 보이는구먼. 게다가 내가 여기 많이 있어 줄 테니 더 신이 날 거다.”\n\n“담배 연기 때문에 숨이 막혀요!” 그녀가 짜증스레 말했다.\n\n“그럼 다시는 집 안에서 담배를 안 피우마!” 그가 다정하게 대꾸했다. “한데 왜 진작 말하지 않았누, 이 어리석은 것아?”\n\n그녀는 한참 말이 없다가, 마거릿에게만 들릴 만큼 낮은 소리로 말했다.\n\n“아버지는 이 일이 끝나기 전에 파이프에서든 술에서든 얻을 수 있는 위로란 위로는 다 필요하실 거예요.”\n\n아버지는 파이프를 마저 피우려는 듯 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n베시가 격하게 말했다.\n\n“내가 바보 아니에요? 그렇죠, 아가씨? 저것 봐요, 나는 아버지를 집에 붙들어 두어야 한다는 걸, 파업 때면 언제든 사내를 꾀어 술 마시게 하려 드는 사람들한테서 떼어 놓아야 한다는 걸 알고 있었어요. 그런데 하필 내 혀가 아버지의 저 파이프를 두고 시비를 걸다니. 그러니 아버지는 나가시겠죠. 그럴 게 뻔해요. 담배 피우고 싶을 때마다요. 그러다 어디까지 갈지는 아무도 몰라요. 차라리 내가 먼저 숨이 막히게 두었더라면 좋았을걸.”\n\n“그런데 아버님이 술을 드시나요?” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“아니요, 술을 마신다고 할 정도는 아니에요.” 그녀가 여전히 그 사납고 들뜬 어조로 대답했다. “하지만 어쩌겠어요? 아가씨한테도 다른 사람들처럼 그런 날이 있겠죠. 아침에 일어나 하루를 지나가면서 그저 뭔가 좀 달라지기를, 말하자면 뭔가 좀 신나는 일이 있기를 바라는 그런 날이요. 나도 그런 날이면 늘 다니던 데가 아닌 다른 빵집에서 4파운드짜리 빵을 사 온 적이 있어요. 그저 날마다 언제까지나 똑같은 광경이 눈에 들어오고, 똑같은 소리가 귀에 들리고, 똑같은 맛이 입에 남고, 똑같은 생각이, 아니 생각이랄 것도 없지만, 머릿속에 있는 걸 견딜 수가 없어서요. 나도 사내였으면 좋겠다고, 나가서 신나게 놀았으면 좋겠다고 바란 적이 있어요. 그게 그저 일자리 찾아 새로운 데로 떠도는 것뿐이라도요. 그런데 아버지는, 아니 사내들은 다, 언제까지나 똑같은 것과 일에 물리는 마음이 나보다 더 강해요. 그러니 어쩌겠어요? 피가 좀 더 빨리, 더 신나게 돌게 하려고, 다른 때는 못 보는 것들을, 그림이니 거울이니 그런 것들을 보려고 진 가게에 들어간다 해도 그걸 크게 탓할 수는 없어요. 하지만 아버지는 술꾼이었던 적이 없어요. 이따금 술 때문에 더 나빠지기는 했겠지만요. 다만 아시다시피,” 이제 그녀의 목소리가 구슬프고 애원하는 어조를 띠었다. “파업 때는 사내를 쓰러뜨릴 일이 많아요. 다들 그렇게 희망차게 시작하는데도요. 그러니 위로는 어디서 오겠어요? 아버지도 화가 나고 미칠 것 같아지겠죠. 다들 그러니까요. 그러다 화내고 미쳐 있는 것에도 지치고, 어쩌면 그 홧김에 나중에 잊고 싶어질 일을 저지르기도 하고요. 아가씨의 그 곱고 애처로운 얼굴에 복이 있기를! 하지만 아가씨는 아직 파업이 뭔지 몰라요.”\n\n“자, 베시,” 마거릿이 말했다. “과장한다고는 하지 않을게요. 내가 잘 모르니까요. 하지만 어쩌면 몸이 안 좋으니 한쪽만 보고 있는지도 몰라요. 바라볼 만한 다른 면, 더 밝은 면도 있고요.”\n\n“평생 기분 좋은 푸른 곳에서 살아서 궁핍도 근심도, 하기야 악한 것도 몰랐을 아가씨야 그렇게 말하기 쉽겠죠.”\n\n“조심해요.” 마거릿이 뺨을 붉히고 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다. “함부로 판단하지 말아요, 베시. 나는 이제 집으로 돌아가면 몹시 편찮으신 어머니를 뵈어요. 어찌나 편찮으신지, 베시, 그 큰 고통의 감옥에서 벗어날 길이라고는 죽음밖에 없을 만큼이에요. 그런데도 나는 아버지께 밝게 말씀드려야 해요. 아버지는 어머니의 진짜 상태를 짐작도 못 하시고, 그 사실은 조금씩 알려 드려야 하니까요. 나와 마음을 나누고 나를 도와줄 수 있는 단 한 사람, 이 세상 무엇보다 어머니에게 큰 위로가 될 그 사람은 억울한 누명을 쓰고 있어요. 돌아가시는 어머니를 뵈러 오려면 죽을 위험을 무릅써야 하고요. 이건 베시한테만, 오직 베시한테만 하는 이야기예요. 다른 데 옮기면 안 돼요. 밀턴의 다른 누구도, 잉글랜드의 거의 어느 누구도 모르는 일이니까요. 나에게 근심이 없나요? 내가 옷을 잘 차려입고 먹을 것이 넉넉하다고 해서 애태우는 것을 모르나요? 아, 베시, 하느님은 공정하시고, 우리 몫은 그분이 알맞게 나누어 주신 거예요. 우리 영혼의 쓰라림을 아시는 분은 오직 그분뿐이지만요.”\n\n“용서를 빌어요.” 베시가 겸손하게 대답했다. “가끔은 내 삶을, 그 안에서 누린 얼마 안 되는 즐거움을 생각하면 나도 어쩌면 하늘에서 떨어지는 별에 맞아 죽도록 정해진 사람 가운데 하나인가 보다 하고 믿게 돼요. ‘그 별의 이름은 쑥이라 하더라. 물의 삼분의 일이 쑥이 되고, 그 물이 쓰게 된 까닭에 많은 사람이 죽더라.’ 오래전부터 자기를 두고 미리 말해진 것이라고 생각하면 아픔도 슬픔도 더 잘 견딜 수 있어요. 어쩐지 그러면 내 아픔이 그 말이 이루어지는 데 꼭 필요했던 것처럼 여겨지거든요. 안 그러면 다 아무것도 아닌 일로 보내진 것 같고요.”\n\n“아니에요, 베시, 생각해 봐요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “하느님은 즐겨 아픔을 내리시는 분이 아니에요. 예언에만 그렇게 매달리지 말고 성경에서 더 분명한 부분을 읽어요.”\n\n“그러는 게 더 지혜롭겠지요. 하지만 이 쓸쓸한 세상과, 무엇보다 이 도시와 그토록 다른 무엇에 대한 이야기를, 그렇게 웅장한 약속의 말씀을 요한계시록 말고 어디서 들을 수 있겠어요? 나는 일곱째 장의 구절들을 그저 그 소리가 좋아서 몇 번이고 혼자 되뇌었어요. 오르간 소리만큼이나 좋고, 여느 날들과는 또 그만큼 다르니까요. 아니에요, 나는 요한계시록을 놓을 수 없어요. 성경의 다른 어느 책보다 나에게 큰 위로를 주거든요.”\n\n“내가 와서 좋아하는 장들을 몇 개 읽어 줄게요.”\n\n“네,” 그녀가 게걸스레 말했다. “오세요. 아버지도 어쩌면 들으실 거예요. 아버지는 내 말에는 귀가 먹으셨어요. 그런 건 다 오늘 일과는 아무 상관이 없고, 자기 일은 오늘 일이라면서요.”\n\n“동생은 어디 갔어요?”\n\n“퍼스티언 천 깎으러 갔어요. 보내기 싫었지만 그래도 먹고는 살아야 하니까요. 조합이 우리한테 많이 줄 형편도 못 되고요.”\n\n“이제 가 봐야겠어요. 베시 덕분에 좋았어요.”\n\n“내가 아가씨한테 좋은 일을 했다고요!”\n\n“네, 나는 아주 슬픈 마음으로 여기 왔고, 내 슬픔의 까닭만이 세상에 하나뿐인 것처럼 여기기 쉬웠어요. 그런데 이제 베시가 여러 해 동안 무엇을 견뎌 왔는지 들으니 그게 나를 더 단단하게 해 줘요.”\n\n“아이고! 나는 좋은 일 하는 건 다 양반들 쪽 몫인 줄 알았어요. 내가 아가씨한테 좋은 일을 할 수 있다고 생각하면 우쭐해지겠는데요.”\n\n“그걸 마음에 두면 못 하게 될 거예요. 하지만 마음에 둬 봐야 스스로 헷갈리기만 할 테니, 그게 하나 다행이네요.”\n\n“아가씨는 내가 본 어느 누구와도 달라요. 아가씨를 어떻게 봐야 할지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“나도 나를 모르는걸요. 잘 있어요!”\n\n베시는 몸 흔들기를 멈추고 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보았다.\n\n“남쪽에는 저 아가씨 같은 사람이 많을까. 어쩐지 시골 바람 한 줄기 같아. 나를 제법 상쾌하게 해 주는구나. 내가 꿈에서 보는 천사만큼이나 환하고 굳센 저 얼굴이 자기가 말하는 그런 슬픔을 알고 있었으리라고 누가 생각이나 했겠어. 저 아가씨는 어떻게 죄를 지을까. 우리는 다 죄를 짓게 마련이니. 정말이지 저 아가씨 생각이 많이 나. 그런데 아버지도 그런가 보더라. 메리까지도. 저 애가 무엇을 눈여겨보려고 마음이 동하는 일은 흔치 않은데.”\n\n# 제18장\n\n“내 마음이 속에서 들고일어나, 두 목소리가 내 가슴속에서 저마다 소리를 내는구나.” — 발렌슈타인.\n\n마거릿이 집에 돌아와 보니 탁자 위에 편지 두 통이 놓여 있었다. 하나는 어머니 앞으로 온 짧은 편지였고, 다른 하나는 우편으로 온 것으로 보아하니 쇼 이모가 보낸 것이 분명했다. 외국 소인이 잔뜩 찍힌, 얇고 은빛이 도는 바스락거리는 편지였다. 그녀가 그 편지를 집어 들고 이리저리 살펴보고 있는데 아버지가 불쑥 들어왔다.\n\n“어머니가 피곤해서 일찍 잠자리에 드셨다더구나! 이렇게 천둥 치는 날에 의사가 어머니를 보러 온 것은 아무래도 세상에서 가장 좋지 못한 일이었던 것 같다. 뭐라고 하더냐? 딕슨 말로는 그 사람이 너한테 어머니 이야기를 했다던데.”\n\n마거릿은 머뭇거렸다. 아버지의 얼굴빛이 한층 심각하고 불안해졌다.\n\n“중한 병이라고 여기는 건 아니겠지?”\n\n“지금 당장은 아니라고 하셨어요. 몸조리가 필요하다고요. 아주 친절하셨고, 약이 어떻게 듣는지 보러 다시 들르시겠다고 하셨어요.”\n\n“몸조리뿐이라고?—공기 좋은 데로 옮기라는 말은 없었고?—이 연기투성이 도시가 어머니한테 해롭다는 말도 하지 않았지, 마거릿?”\n\n“네! 한마디도 없으셨어요.” 그녀가 무겁게 대답했다. “걱정하시는 것 같기는 했지만요.”\n\n“의사들은 다들 그렇게 걱정스러운 태도를 짓는 법이지. 직업이 그래서 그렇다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 아버지의 안절부절못하는 모습에서, 자기가 전한 말을 아버지가 아무리 대수롭지 않게 넘기려 해도 위험할지 모른다는 첫 예감이 이미 그 마음에 새겨졌음을 알아보았다. 아버지는 그 문제를 잊지 못했다. 거기서 다른 이야기로 넘어가지 못했다. 저녁 내내 그는 그 이야기로 자꾸만 되돌아왔고, 조금이라도 나쁜 쪽의 생각은 한사코 받아들이려 하지 않았다. 그것이 마거릿을 이루 말할 수 없이 슬프게 했다.\n\n“이 편지는 쇼 이모한테서 온 거예요, 아버지. 나폴리에 이르셨는데 너무 덥다고 소렌토에 방을 얻으셨대요. 그렇지만 이모는 이탈리아가 마음에 들지 않으시는 것 같아요.”\n\n“먹는 것에 대해서는 아무 말도 없었느냐?”\n\n“자양분이 있고 소화가 잘되는 것으로 하라고요. 어머니는 입맛이 꽤 좋으신 편이잖아요.”\n\n“그래! 그러니 그 사람이 음식 이야기를 꺼냈다는 게 더욱 이상하구나.”\n\n“제가 여쭤본 거예요, 아버지.” 또 한 번 말이 끊겼다. 이윽고 마거릿이 말을 이었다. “쇼 이모가 저한테 산호 장신구를 보내셨대요, 아버지. 그런데,” 마거릿이 반쯤 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. “밀턴의 비국교도들이 그 진가를 알아보지 못할까 봐 걱정이시래요. 이모는 비국교도에 대한 생각을 죄다 퀘이커교도한테서 얻으신 거죠, 그렇죠?”\n\n“어머니가 무엇인가 바라는 낌새를 듣거나 눈치채거든 꼭 나한테 알려 다오. 어머니가 바라는 것을 늘 나한테 말해 주지는 않는 것 같아 그게 걱정이구나. 부디 손턴 부인이 일러 준 그 아이 일도 알아보아라. 착실하고 야무진 하녀를 하나 두면 딕슨이 늘 어머니 곁에 붙어 있을 수 있을 테고, 몸조리로 될 일이라면 우리 힘으로 곧 일으켜 세울 수 있으리라고 나는 장담한다. 요즘 더운 날씨에다 하녀 구하는 일까지 겹쳐 어머니가 몹시 지쳤어. 좀 쉬면 아주 좋아질 게다—그렇지 않으냐, 마거릿?”\n\n“그러기를 바라요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그러나 그 말이 어찌나 슬프게 들렸던지 아버지가 알아차렸다. 그는 딸의 뺨을 가만히 꼬집었다.\n\n“자자. 그렇게 창백한 얼굴을 하고 있으면 내가 연지라도 발라 주어야겠구나. 몸조심해라, 얘야. 안 그러면 다음번엔 네가 의사를 부르게 될 테니.”\n\n그러나 그날 저녁 그는 무엇에도 마음을 붙이지 못했다. 아내가 아직 자고 있는지 보려고 애써 발끝으로 걸으며 끊임없이 오갔다. 마거릿은 그 안절부절못하는 모습에 가슴이 아팠다. 마음의 어두운 구석에서 어렴풋이 솟아오르는 그 끔찍한 두려움을 짓누르고 목 졸라 죽이려는 안간힘이었다.\n\n마침내 그가 얼마쯤 마음이 놓인 얼굴로 돌아왔다.\n\n“이제 깨어났다, 마거릿. 내가 곁에 서 있는 걸 보고 활짝 웃더구나. 예전 그 웃음 그대로였어. 개운하다고, 차를 마실 준비가 되었다고 하는구나. 어머니한테 온 편지는 어디 있느냐? 보고 싶어 하신다. 네가 차를 준비하는 동안 내가 읽어 드리마.”\n\n그 편지는 손턴 부인이 헤일 씨와 헤일 부인과 헤일 양을 이달 21일 만찬에 초대하는 정식 초대장이었다. 마거릿은 그날 하루 동안 서글픈 가능성들에 대해 알게 된 것이 있는데도 초대를 받아들일 궁리를 하는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그러나 사실이 그러했다. 남편과 딸이 그 만찬에 간다는 생각은, 마거릿이 편지 내용을 듣기도 전에 이미 헤일 부인의 마음을 온통 사로잡아 버렸다. 그것은 병자의 단조로운 나날에 변화를 주는 사건이었다. 그래서 마거릿이 반대하자 그녀는 보채는 듯한 고집까지 부리며 두 사람이 가야 한다는 생각에 매달렸다.\n\n“아니다, 마거릿. 어머니가 그러기를 바란다면 우리 둘 다 기꺼이 가야지. 정말로 기운이 나지 않고서야—우리가 생각했던 것보다 정말로 나아지지 않고서야 그런 걸 바랄 리가 없지 않으냐, 그렇지, 마거릿?” 이튿날 그녀가 승낙의 답장을 쓰려고 채비하는데 헤일 씨가 불안한 투로 말했다.\n\n“그렇지 않으냐, 마거릿?” 그가 손을 초조하게 놀리며 물었다. 아버지가 그토록 애타게 구하는 위안을 거절하는 것은 잔인한 일로 여겨졌다. 게다가 두려움이 있다는 것을 한사코 인정하지 않으려는 그 격한 몸짓은 마거릿에게마저 거의 희망을 불어넣을 지경이었다.\n\n“저도 어머니가 어젯밤보다 나아지셨다고 생각해요.” 그녀가 말했다. “눈빛도 더 맑아지시고 안색도 더 환해지셨어요.”\n\n“네게 하느님의 축복이 있기를.” 아버지가 간절하게 말했다. “그런데 정말이냐? 어제는 어찌나 무더웠는지 누구나 다 몸이 좋지 않았어. 도널드슨 씨가 어머니를 보러 오기에는 참으로 운 나쁜 날이었지.”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 그날의 일과를 보러 나갔다. 요즘은 이웃 라이시엄에서 노동자들에게 해 주기로 약속한 몇 차례의 강연을 준비하는 일까지 더해져 있었다. 그는 교회 건축을 주제로 골랐는데, 그 고장의 성격이나 강연을 들을 사람들이 알고 싶어 하는 종류의 지식에 맞추었다기보다는 자신의 취향과 학식에 어울리는 쪽이었다. 그리고 그 기관 자체가 빚을 지고 있던 터라, 주제가 무엇이든 헤일 씨처럼 교양 있고 학식 있는 사람에게서 무료 강좌를 얻어 낸 것만으로도 더없이 반가운 일이었다.\n\n“그래, 어머니.” 그날 밤 손턴 씨가 물었다. “21일 초대에 누가 오겠다고 했습니까?”\n\n“패니야, 답장들이 어디 있느냐? 슬릭슨 집안은 온다고 했고, 콜링브룩 집안도 온다고 했고, 스티븐스 집안도 온다고 했고, 브라운 집안은 못 온다는구나. 헤일 집안은—아버지와 딸이 오고—어머니는 몸이 너무 안 좋다고 하고—맥퍼슨 집안이 오고, 호스폴 씨와 영 씨도 온다. 브라운 집안이 못 온다니 포터 집안을 부를까 생각하던 참이다.”\n\n“좋습니다. 그런데 말이지요, 저는 도널드슨 의사가 하는 말로 미루어 헤일 부인이 정말 많이 편찮으신 게 아닌가 걱정입니다.”\n\n“그렇게 많이 아프시다면서 만찬 초대에 응하다니 이상하네요.” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“많이 아프다고는 하지 않았다.” 오라비가 조금 날카롭게 말했다. “몸이 성치 않다고만 했지. 그리고 그 집 사람들도 모르고 있을지 모른다.” 그러고서 그는 도널드슨 의사가 들려준 말로 미루어 적어도 마거릿만은 병세가 정확히 어떠한지 알고 있을 수밖에 없다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐다.\n\n“아마 그 사람들도 네가 어제 한 말을 잘 알고 있을 게다, 존. 스티븐스 집안이나 콜링브룩 집안 같은 사람들에게 소개받는 것이 그들에게—그러니까 헤일 씨에게 얼마나 큰 이득이 되겠느냐는 그 말 말이다.”\n\n“그런 속셈이 그분들을 움직였을 리 없습니다. 아니에요! 저는 어찌 된 일인지 알 것 같습니다.”\n\n“오빠도 참!” 패니가 특유의 가냘프고 신경질적인 웃음을 지으며 말했다. “오빠는 그 헤일 집안을 어쩌면 그렇게 잘 아는 척하면서, 우리는 그 사람들에 대해 아무것도 알 수 없다고만 하시네요. 정말로 그 사람들이 흔히 만나는 사람들과 그렇게나 다른가요?”\n\n그녀는 오라비를 성나게 할 뜻이 없었다. 그러나 설령 그럴 뜻이 있었더라도 그보다 더 철저히 해내지는 못했을 것이다. 그는 말없이 속을 끓일 뿐, 누이의 물음에 대꾸할 가치조차 두지 않았다.\n\n“내가 보기에는 별날 것 없는 사람들이더라.” 손턴 부인이 말했다. “그 사람은 어지간히 점잖은 축인 듯하더구나. 장사를 하기에는 너무 순진해 보이니, 처음에는 성직자였다가 이제 교사가 된 것이 오히려 잘된 일이겠지. 그 부인은 저 병치레로 제법 귀부인 티를 내고 있고. 그 딸로 말하자면—생각할 때마다 나를 알 수 없게 만드는 유일한 아이다. 하기야 자주 생각하지도 않는다만. 그 애는 잘난 체하는 데 대단한 재주가 있는 것 같은데, 왜 그러는지를 모르겠구나. 이따금은 자기가 함께 있는 사람들에게는 제가 너무 과분하다고 여기는 게 아닌가 싶을 지경이다. 게다가 그들은 부유하지도 않아. 내가 들은 바로는 부유했던 적도 없고.”\n\n“게다가 그 애는 재주도 없어요, 어머니. 피아노도 못 친대요.”\n\n“계속해 보렴, 패니. 네 수준까지 올라오려면 그분한테 또 무엇이 더 필요하냐?”\n\n“그러지 마라, 존.” 어머니가 말했다. “패니가 한 말이 해될 것은 없지 않으냐. 헤일 양이 피아노를 못 친다고 말하는 것을 나도 직접 들었다. 네가 우리를 좀 내버려 두면 어쩌면 우리도 그 애를 좋아하고 그 애의 좋은 점도 알아볼 수 있을 게다.”\n\n“저는 정말이지 절대로 못 그럴 거예요!” 어머니의 비호 아래 패니가 중얼거렸다. 손턴 씨는 들었으나 굳이 대꾸하려 들지 않았다. 그는 식당 안을 왔다 갔다 하며 어머니가 촛불을 가져오라고 일러 자기가 읽든 쓰든 일을 시작할 수 있게 해 주기를, 그리하여 이 대화가 끝나기를 바라고 있었다. 그러나 손턴 부인이 지난날의 절약을 늘 잊지 않고 지켜 오는 그 자잘한 살림 규칙들에 간섭할 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n“어머니.” 그가 걸음을 멈추고 용감하게 속마음을 털어놓았다. “저는 어머니가 헤일 양을 좋아해 주셨으면 합니다.”\n\n“어째서냐?” 그녀가 아들의 간절하면서도 부드러운 태도에 놀라 물었다. “설마 그 애와 혼인할 생각을 하는 건 아니겠지?—땡전 한 푼 없는 처녀와.”\n\n“그분은 절대로 저를 받아 주지 않을 겁니다.” 그가 짧게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 나도 그러지 않으리라 본다.” 어머니가 대꾸했다. “벨 씨가 너를 좋게 말한 것을 그 애가 그대로 옮겨 준 일을 두고 내가 칭찬했더니, 그 애는 내 면전에서 웃더구나. 그렇게 솔직하게 구는 것이 나는 마음에 들었다. 그 덕에 그 애가 너를 마음에 두고 있지 않다는 확신이 섰으니까. 그런데 바로 다음 순간에는 그 애가 무슨 생각을 하는 것처럼 굴어서 나를 어찌나 언짢게 하던지—그래, 됐다! 다만 그 애가 제 잘난 맛에 겨워 너 따위는 생각도 하지 않는다는 네 말만은 옳다! 그 건방진 계집애! 저보다 나은 사람을 대체 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 나도 좀 알고 싶구나!”\n\n“그런데 저 역시 어머니 말씀이 옳다는 것을 어머니만큼이나 굳게 믿고 있고, 그분에게 제 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 생각도 기대도 전혀 없으니, 앞으로는 제가 그분 이야기를 하더라도 아무 사심이 없다는 것을 믿어 주십시오. 저는 그 아가씨에게 어려움이 닥치리라는 것을—어쩌면 어머니의 보살핌을 잃게 되리라는 것을 내다보고 있습니다. 그러니 그분에게 벗이 필요할 때 어머니가 그 벗이 되어 주실 준비만 하고 계시기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 그리고 패니야.” 그가 말했다. “네게도 그만한 분별은 있으리라 믿는다. 내가 지금 말한 것 말고 다른 무슨 까닭이 있어서 너와 어머니에게 그분을 정성껏 대해 달라고 부탁하는 것이라고 여기는 것은, 나한테도 그렇지만 헤일 양에게도 그만큼 큰 모욕이다—사실 그분은 더 큰 모욕으로 여길 게다.”\n\n“나는 그 애의 오만만은 용서할 수 없다.” 어머니가 말했다. “네가 부탁하니, 필요할 때가 오면 그 애의 벗이 되어 주마, 존. 네가 부탁한다면 이세벨의 벗이라도 되어 주겠다. 하지만 우리를 모두 깔보는 저 애가—너까지 깔보는 저 애가……”\n\n“아닙니다, 어머니. 저는 아직 한 번도 그분의 경멸이 닿을 자리에 저를 둔 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 생각이 없습니다.”\n\n“경멸이라니, 참!”—(손턴 부인 특유의 뜻이 담긴 콧방귀 가운데 하나였다.)—“내가 그 애한테 잘해 주어야 한다면, 존, 헤일 양 이야기는 더 하지 마라. 그 애와 마주 앉아 있으면 내가 그 애를 좋아하는지 싫어하는지 알 수가 없다. 그런데 그 애를 떠올리고 네가 그 애 이야기를 하는 것을 들으면 밉기만 하다. 네가 나한테 대놓고 말해 준 것이나 다름없이, 그 애가 너한테도 잘난 체했다는 게 훤히 보이는구나.”\n\n“설령 그랬다 해도,” 그가 말하다 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 이었다. “저는 여자의 오만한 눈길에 주눅 들거나, 그 사람이 저와 제 처지를 오해하는 것을 마음에 두거나 할 애송이가 아닙니다. 웃어넘길 수 있어요!”\n\n“물론이지! 그 애까지도 웃어넘기려무나. 그 잘난 생각이며 도도하게 고개 젖히는 꼴까지도!”\n\n“그럼 어머니는 왜 그렇게 그 사람 이야기를 많이 하시는지 모르겠어요.” 패니가 말했다. “저는 정말이지 그 이야기라면 이제 지겨워요.”\n\n“그래!” 오라비가 씁쓸한 기색을 띠고 말했다. “그럼 좀 더 마음에 드는 이야깃거리를 찾아보자. 즐겁게 이야기할 거리로 파업은 어떠냐?”\n\n“직공들이 정말로 일손을 놓았느냐?” 손턴 부인이 눈에 띄게 관심을 보이며 물었다.\n\n“햄퍼 씨네 사람들은 정말로 나갔습니다. 우리 쪽은 계약 위반으로 고소당할까 두려워 남은 한 주를 채우고 있고요. 저라면 기한을 채우기 전에 일을 놓고 나간 놈은 하나도 남김없이 잡아다 벌을 받게 했을 겁니다.”\n\n“소송 비용이 그 직공들 값어치보다 더 나갔을 게다—배은망덕한 쓸모없는 것들 같으니!” 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지요. 하지만 저는 제가 어떻게 약속을 지키는지, 또 저들에게 저들의 약속을 어떻게 지키게 할 셈인지 보여 주었을 겁니다. 이제쯤은 저들도 저를 알지요. 슬릭슨 씨네 사람들도 나갔습니다—그 사람이 저들을 벌주는 데 돈을 쓰지 않으리라는 건 거의 틀림없고요. 우리는 파업을 맞게 됐습니다, 어머니.”\n\n“받아 놓은 주문이 많지 않으면 좋겠구나.”\n\n“물론 많지요. 저들도 그건 잘 압니다. 하지만 저들은 다 안다고 여기면서도 정작 전부를 이해하지는 못합니다.”\n\n“그게 무슨 말이냐, 존?”\n\n촛불이 들어와 있었고, 패니는 그 끝없이 이어지는 털실 자수를 집어 들고는 그 위에서 하품을 하고 있었다. 이따금 의자에 몸을 젖히고 허공을 바라보며 마음 편히 아무 생각도 하지 않았다.\n\n“그러니까요.” 그가 말했다. “미국 사람들이 자기네 실을 세계 시장에 어찌나 밀어 넣는지, 우리한테 남은 유일한 길은 더 낮은 값에 실을 만들어 내는 것뿐입니다. 그러지 못하면 당장 문을 닫고 직공이며 공장주며 다 같이 떠돌이 신세가 되는 거지요. 그런데도 이 바보들은 3년 전에 주던 값으로 돌아가자고 합니다—아니, 그 우두머리들 가운데 몇은 지금 디킨슨네 값을 들먹입니다. 명예를 아는 사람이라면 짜내지 않을 벌금을 임금에서 떼어 내는 것까지 셈하면 디킨슨네에서 실제로 주는 임금이 우리 쪽보다 적다는 것을 저들도 우리만큼이나 잘 알면서 말입니다. 정말이지 어머니, 저는 옛 단결금지법이 아직 살아 있었으면 싶습니다. 바보들이—저런 무지하고 제멋대로인 자들이—그저 그 어리석은 머리들을 한데 모았다는 것만으로, 지식과 경험이, 또 흔히는 괴로운 궁리와 근심이 줄 수 있는 온갖 지혜를 다 갖춘 사람들의 운명을 좌우하게 된다는 것은 너무하지 않습니까. 다음 차례는—사실 이제 거의 거기까지 왔습니다만—우리가 찾아가서, 모자를 벗어 손에 들고 서서, 방적공 조합 서기에게 부디 저희 쪽에 저희가 정한 값으로 일손을 대어 주십사 하고 굽실거리며 청하게 되는 겁니다. 저들이 바라는 게 바로 그것이지요—여기 잉글랜드에서 우리가 닳아 없어지는 대가로 이익의 정당한 몫을 얻지 못한다면 우리는 다른 나라로 옮겨 갈 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 국내외의 경쟁을 다 겪고 나면 우리 가운데 누구도 정당한 몫 이상을 벌기는 어렵고 여러 해를 평균 내어 그만큼이라도 건지면 다행이라는 것을 헤아릴 분별조차 없는 자들이 말입니다.”\n\n“아일랜드에서 직공을 데려올 수는 없느냐? 나 같으면 저런 자들을 하루도 데리고 있지 않겠다. 주인이 누구인지, 내가 원하는 일꾼을 마음대로 쓸 수 있다는 것을 가르쳐 주겠다.”\n\n“네! 물론 데려올 수 있고, 저들이 오래 버티면 그렇게 할 겁니다. 성가시고 돈도 들 테고, 위험도 좀 따르지 않을까 싶습니다만, 굽히느니 그렇게 하겠습니다.”\n\n“이렇게 여분의 비용이 다 들게 생겼다면, 하필 지금 만찬을 여는 것이 아쉽구나.”\n\n“저도 그렇습니다—비용 때문이 아니라, 생각할 일이 많고 예상치 못한 일들이 제 시간을 자꾸 빼앗을 테니까요. 하지만 호스폴 씨는 꼭 모셔야 했고, 그분은 밀턴에 오래 머무르지 않습니다. 나머지 사람들로 말하면 우리가 만찬을 빚지고 있는 처지이고, 어차피 수고는 한 번이니까요.”\n\n그는 여전히 안절부절못하는 걸음을 이어 갔다. 더 말은 하지 않았으나 이따금 깊은 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 마치 성가신 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰는 것 같았다. 패니는 어머니에게 자잘한 것들을 잔뜩 물었는데, 그 어느 것도 어머니의 마음을 붙들고 있는 문제와는 상관이 없었다. 조금이라도 눈치 있는 사람이었다면 알아차렸을 문제였다. 그래서 그녀는 짧은 대답만 잔뜩 받았다. 10시에 하인들이 기도를 하러 줄지어 들어왔을 때 그녀는 반가웠다. 기도는 늘 어머니가 읽었고, 그에 앞서 성경 한 장을 먼저 읽었다. 그들은 그즈음 구약을 차근차근 읽어 나가고 있었다. 기도가 끝나고 어머니가 그 특유의 길고 흔들림 없는 눈길로—마음속에 든 다정함은 조금도 내비치지 않으면서도 축복과 같은 간절함을 담은 그 눈길로—아들에게 잘 자라고 인사한 뒤에도 손턴 씨는 걸음을 멈추지 않았다. 그의 사업 계획은 다가오는 이 파업 때문에 모조리 제동이 걸렸고 갑작스레 멈춰 서 버렸다. 근심 어린 여러 시간에 걸친 앞선 궁리가 다 헛일이 되었고, 저들의 미친 어리석음에 완전히 낭비되고 말았다. 그 어리석음은 그 자신보다 저들에게 더 큰 해를 입힐 터였지만, 그들이 저지르고 있는 손해가 어디까지 미칠지는 아무도 가늠할 수 없었다. 그런데도 이런 자들이 공장주들에게 자본을 어떻게 쓸지 지시할 자격이 자기들에게 있다고 여기는 것이었다. 바로 그날 햄퍼 씨는 이렇게 말했다. 만일 자기가 이 파업으로 망한다 해도 다시 인생을 시작하겠으며, 이 일을 일으킨 자들이 자기보다 더 딱한 처지에 놓여 있다는 확신에서 위안을 얻겠노라고. 자기에게는 손 말고 머리도 있지만 저들에게는 손밖에 없고, 저들이 시장을 쫓아 버리고 나면 그 시장을 따라갈 수도, 다른 일로 돌아설 수도 없다는 것이었다. 그러나 이런 생각도 손턴 씨에게는 위안이 되지 않았다. 복수가 그에게 아무 기쁨도 주지 않았기 때문일 수도 있고, 이마의 땀으로 얻어 낸 자기 자리를 너무나 소중히 여긴 나머지 남들의 무지나 어리석음 탓에 그것이 위태로워지는 것을 뼈아프게 느꼈기 때문일 수도 있다. 어찌나 뼈아프게 느꼈던지 저들의 행동이 저들 자신에게 어떤 결과를 가져올지 헤아려 볼 마음의 여유조차 없었다. 그는 이따금 이를 악물며 방 안을 오갔다. 마침내 2시를 알리는 소리가 났다. 촛불들이 촛대 안에서 가물거리고 있었다. 그는 제 몫의 초에 불을 붙이며 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n“이번만은 저들도 자기들이 누구를 상대하고 있는지 알게 될 거다. 2주일은 줄 수 있다—그 이상은 안 된다. 그때까지도 제 미친 짓을 깨닫지 못한다면 나는 아일랜드에서 직공을 데려와야 한다. 이건 슬릭슨이 벌인 짓이 틀림없어—그 인간과 그 잔꾀들을 어쩌면 좋을까! 자기 물건이 남아돈다고 여긴 게지. 그래서 대표단이 찾아왔을 때 처음엔 양보하는 척했고—물론 자기가 노린 대로 저들을 그 어리석음 속에 더욱 굳혀 놓은 거야. 거기서부터 번진 거다.”\n\n# 제19장\n\n“사람이 잠든 사이 한결 밝은 꿈결 속에서 천사들이 그 영혼을 부르듯, 어떤 낯선 생각들은 우리의 익숙한 상념을 훌쩍 넘어서서 영광 속을 들여다본다.” — 헨리 본.\n\n헤일 부인은 손턴가의 만찬이라는 발상에 묘하게 재미를 느끼고 마음이 쏠렸다. 그녀는 자잘한 것까지 자꾸만 궁금해했는데, 미리 맛볼 즐거움을 낱낱이 말로 들어 두고 싶어 하는 어린아이 같은 순진함이 어딘가 배어 있었다. 그러나 병자들이 보내는 단조로운 삶은 흔히 그들을 아이처럼 만든다. 둘 다 일의 경중을 가늠하는 감각이 없고, 자기 세계를 가두고 그 밖의 모든 것을 막아 주는 벽과 커튼이 그 너머에 감춰진 무엇보다도 반드시 더 클 것이라고 저마다 믿는 듯하다는 점에서 그렇다. 게다가 헤일 부인에게도 처녀 시절의 허영이 있었고, 가난한 목사의 아내가 되면서 그 허영이 꺾이는 굴욕을 지나치다 싶게 느꼈을 것이다. 그것들은 눌리고 잠재워졌을 뿐 아주 꺼지지는 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 마거릿이 만찬을 위해 차려입은 모습을 볼 생각이 좋았고, 무엇을 입어야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 조바심을 내며 이야기했다. 마거릿은 그것이 재미있었다. 할리 가에서 보낸 1년 동안 사교계에 익숙해진 정도가 어머니가 헬스톤에서 보낸 25년보다 더했으니 말이다.\n\n“그러면 흰 비단옷을 입겠다는 거지. 정말 몸에 맞을까? 이디스가 혼인한 지 거의 1년이 다 되어 가는데!”\n\n“아, 그럼요, 어머니! 머리 부인이 지은 옷이니 틀림없이 맞을 거예요. 제가 살이 붙었느냐 빠졌느냐에 따라 허리 길이가 지푸라기 하나쯤 짧거나 길지도 모르지만요. 그렇지만 저는 조금도 달라지지 않은 것 같아요.”\n\n“그래도 딕슨한테 한번 보여 주는 게 낫지 않겠니? 오래 개켜 두어서 누렇게 바랬을지도 모르잖니.”\n\n“어머니가 원하시면요. 하지만 정 안 되면 쇼 이모가 주신 아주 고운 분홍 망사 옷이 있어요. 이디스가 혼인하기 겨우 두세 달 전에 받은 거예요. 그건 누렇게 바랬을 리가 없어요.”\n\n“그렇겠지! 하지만 빛이 바랬을 수도 있잖니.”\n\n“그럼요! 그럼 초록 비단옷도 있어요. 저는 오히려 너무 넘쳐서 곤란한 쪽인 것 같은데요.”\n\n“네가 뭘 입어야 좋을지 알면 좋으련만.” 헤일 부인이 안절부절못하며 말했다.\n\n마거릿의 태도가 곧바로 달라졌다. “제가 가서 하나씩 차례로 입어 볼까요, 어머니? 그러면 어느 것이 제일 마음에 드시는지 보실 수 있잖아요.”\n\n“하지만…… 그래! 아마 그게 제일 좋겠구나.”\n\n그리하여 마거릿은 자리를 떴다. 이런 엉뚱한 시간에 차려입고 나니 그녀는 짓궂은 장난을 치고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 값진 흰 비단 치마를 빙 돌려 치즈 덩이처럼 부풀려 보기도 하고, 여왕이라도 되는 양 어머니 앞에서 뒷걸음질로 물러나 보기도 하고 싶었다. 그러나 그런 장난이 진지한 일을 방해하는 것으로 여겨지고 그 때문에 어머니가 짜증을 낸다는 것을 알고는 정색을 하고 얌전해졌다. 세상이(자기 세계가) 어쩌자고 자기 옷차림 하나로 이토록 부산을 떠는지 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 그런데 바로 그날 오후, 손턴 부인이 알아봐 주기로 한 하녀 이야기가 나온 김에 베시 히긴스에게 그 초대 이야기를 꺼내자, 베시는 그 소식에 아주 눈이 번쩍 뜨였다.\n\n“어머나! 말버러 공장의 손턴 댁에 만찬을 하러 간다는 말이오?”\n\n“네, 베시. 왜 그렇게 놀라요?”\n\n“아, 모르겠구먼. 그렇지만 그 댁은 밀턴에서 제일가는 사람들하고만 오간단 말이오.”\n\n“그러면 베시는 우리가 밀턴에서 제일가는 축은 못 된다고 생각하는군요?”\n\n베시는 제 속마음을 그렇게 쉽게 들킨 것에 뺨이 조금 붉어졌다.\n\n“그게 말이오.” 그녀가 말했다. “여기서는 돈을 대단하게 치는데, 댁네는 돈이 그리 많지 않은 것 같아서 말이오.”\n\n“그래요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “정말 그래요. 하지만 우리는 배운 사람들이고 배운 사람들 틈에서 살아왔어요. 우리 아버지한테 배우러 오면서 스스로 아버지보다 못하다고 인정한 사람이 우리를 만찬에 부른 것이 그렇게 놀랄 일인가요? 손턴 씨를 흉보려는 건 아니에요. 한때 그가 그랬듯 포목상 점원 노릇을 하다가 지금의 그처럼 될 수 있는 사람은 몇 안 되니까요.”\n\n“그렇지만 그 좁은 집에서 답례로 만찬을 낼 수 있겠수? 손턴 댁 집은 세 배는 큰데.”\n\n“글쎄요, 베시 말대로 손턴 씨한테 답례로 만찬을 내는 정도는 어떻게 해낼 수 있을 것 같아요. 그렇게 큰 방에서, 그렇게 많은 사람을 부르지는 못하겠지만요. 그런데 우리는 그런 식으로는 전혀 생각해 보지도 않았어요.”\n\n“나는 댁이 손턴 댁에서 만찬을 하리라고는 생각도 못 했구먼.” 베시가 되뇌었다. “아니, 거기는 시장님까지 만찬을 하러 오고, 국회의원이며 다들 온단 말이오.”\n\n“밀턴 시장님을 만나는 영광쯤은 저도 감당할 수 있을 것 같은데요.”\n\n“그렇지만 그 부인네들은 어찌나 으리으리하게 차려입는지!” 베시가 마거릿의 무명옷을 걱정스럽게 바라보며 말했다. 그 옷을 두고 그녀의 밀턴 눈썰미는 야드당 7펜스짜리라고 어림했다.\n\n마거릿의 얼굴에 보조개가 지며 유쾌한 웃음이 터졌다. “고마워요, 베시. 내가 그 말쑥한 사람들 틈에서 근사해 보일지 그렇게 마음 써 주다니. 하지만 나한테는 으리으리한 옷이 얼마든지 있어요. 일주일 전만 해도 여기서 내가 쓸 만한 어떤 자리에도 너무 과하다고 말했을 옷들이지요. 하지만 손턴 씨 댁에서 만찬을 하고 어쩌면 시장님까지 만나게 될 테니, 내가 가진 제일 좋은 옷을 입을 거예요. 그건 믿어도 좋아요.”\n\n“어떤 걸 입을 거요?” 베시가 얼마쯤 마음이 놓인 듯 물었다.\n\n“흰 비단옷이요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “1년 전에 사촌의 혼례 때 입으려고 지은 옷이에요.”\n\n“그거면 되겠구먼!” 베시가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. “나는 댁이 업신여김을 받는 건 정말 싫소.”\n\n“아! 밀턴에서 업신여김을 받지 않을 수만 있다면 나도 얼마든지 근사하게 차려입을게요.”\n\n“댁이 차려입은 걸 볼 수 있으면 좋겠구먼.” 베시가 말했다. “댁은 사람들이 예쁘다고 할 만한 얼굴은 아닌 것 같소. 그러기엔 홍백이 곱게 어리지 않았으니 말이오. 그렇지만 있잖수, 나는 댁을 만나기 훨씬 전부터 꿈에서 댁을 보았소.”\n\n“말도 안 돼요, 베시!”\n\n“아니, 정말 그랬소. 바로 그 얼굴이었소. 어둠 속에서 그 맑고 흔들림 없는 눈으로 내다보고, 머리카락은 이마에서 뒤로 날려 지금처럼 매끈하고 반듯한 이마 둘레로 빛살처럼 뻗어 나가고. 그리고 댁은 언제나 나에게 힘을 주러 왔고, 나는 그 깊고 다독여 주는 눈에서 그 힘을 길어 올리는 것 같았소. 그리고 댁은 빛나는 옷을 입고 있었지. 바로 지금 입으려는 그 옷처럼 말이오. 그러니 보시우, 그게 댁이었단 말이오!”\n\n“아니에요, 베시.” 마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다. “그건 그저 꿈이었을 뿐이에요.”\n\n“그러면 어째서 내가 이 괴로움 중에 남들처럼 꿈 하나 못 꾼단 말이오? 성경에도 꿈꾼 사람이 한둘이 아니지 않수? 그럼, 환상도 보았지! 아니, 우리 아버지도 꿈을 대단하게 치는데! 다시 말하지만 나는 댁을 똑똑히 보았소. 나를 향해 성큼성큼 다가오는데, 그 걸음이 하도 빨라 머리카락이 뒤로 날렸소. 꼭 원래 나 있는 결대로, 조금 들뜬 것처럼 말이오. 그리고 댁이 입게 될 그 희고 빛나는 옷을 입고 있었소. 내가 가서 그 옷 입은 댁을 보게 해 주우. 꿈에서 정말로 그랬던 것처럼 댁을 보고 만져 보고 싶소.”\n\n“베시, 그건 아무래도 당신의 공상이에요.”\n\n“공상이든 아니든, 댁은 왔잖수. 꿈에서 댁이 다가오는 모습을 보았을 때 내가 알았던 그대로 말이오. 그리고 댁이 내 곁에 있으면 마음이 한결 편안해지고 위로가 되는구먼. 을씨년스러운 날에 불이 사람을 위로해 주듯이 말이오. 그게 21일이라고 했지요. 하느님이 허락하시면 내가 가서 댁을 보겠소.”\n\n“아, 베시! 오면 반갑게 맞을게요. 하지만 그런 말은 하지 말아요. 정말이지 마음이 아파요. 정말로요.”\n\n“그럼 혀를 깨물어서라도 혼자 담아 두겠소. 그래도 그게 사실이기는 하지만.”\n\n마거릿은 잠자코 있었다. 마침내 그녀가 말했다. “그게 사실이라고 여긴다면 언젠가 그 이야기를 나누기로 해요. 하지만 지금은 아니에요. 그런데 아버님도 파업에 나가셨나요?”\n\n“그럼요!” 베시가 무겁게 말했다. 겨우 한두 순간 전에 말하던 것과는 아주 다른 투였다. “아버지도 다른 사람들도 다요. 햄퍼 씨네 일꾼들이 죄다, 그리고 그 밖에도 숱하게. 이번에는 여자들도 사나운 걸로 치면 남자들 못지않소. 먹을 게 비싸니 자식들 먹일 것을 구해야 하지 않겠수. 손턴 댁이 저녁 한 끼 값을 밖으로 내보내 준다면, 그 돈으로 감자와 곡식가루를 사서 우는 아기 여럿을 달래고 그 어미들 마음도 잠시나마 잠재울 텐데!”\n\n“그런 말 말아요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “그러면 이 만찬에 가는 것이 못되고 죄스럽게 느껴질 거예요.”\n\n“아니오!” 베시가 말했다. “어떤 사람은 진수성찬과 자주색 옷과 고운 베옷을 누리도록 미리 정해져 있소. 어쩌면 댁도 그중 하나겠지. 다른 사람들은 한평생 죽도록 애쓰며 살고. 게다가 요즘 세상 개들은 나사로 시절의 그 개들만큼도 가엾이 여길 줄을 모르오. 그렇지만 댁이 나더러 손가락 끝으로 혀를 식혀 달라고 청한다면, 나는 그 큰 구렁을 건너서라도 댁에게 가겠소. 여기서 댁이 나에게 어떤 사람이었는지 그 생각만으로도 말이오.”\n\n“베시, 열이 몹시 오르고 있어요! 손을 만져 봐도 알겠고, 하는 말을 들어도 알겠어요. 그 무서운 날에 우리 가운데 누구는 여기서 거지였고 누구는 부자였다는 것이 갈라 세우는 기준이 되지는 못할 거예요. 우리는 그런 가엾은 우연으로 심판받는 게 아니라 그리스도를 얼마나 성실히 따랐는가로 심판받을 테니까요.”\n\n마거릿은 일어나 물을 찾아다가 손수건을 적셔 그 시원한 물기를 베시의 이마에 얹어 주고, 돌처럼 차가운 발을 문질러 주기 시작했다. 베시는 눈을 감고 다독여지는 대로 몸을 맡겼다. 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“댁도 나처럼 정신이 아주 나가 버렸을 거요. 아버지를 찾는다고 한 사람 한 사람 줄줄이 들어와서는 저마다 제 사연을 늘어놓고 앉아 있었으면 말이오. 어떤 이들은 사무치는 미움을 이야기하며 공장주들에 대해 하도 끔찍한 말을 해서 내 피가 다 얼어붙었소. 그렇지만 여자들이 더 많았는데, 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러도 닦지도 않고 아랑곳도 않은 채 고기값이 어떻고 자식들이 배가 고파 밤에 잠을 못 잔다고 하소연하고 또 하소연했다오.”\n\n“그럼 그이들은 파업이 그걸 고쳐 주리라고 생각하는 건가요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“그렇다고들 하오.” 베시가 대답했다. “오래도록 장사가 잘되어서 공장주들이 돈을 어마어마하게 벌었다고 하더구먼. 얼마나 벌었는지 아버지는 모르지만 조합이야 물론 알고 있지. 그러니 먹을 게 비싸지는 마당에 그 이문에서 제 몫을 받고 싶은 게 당연하지 않겠수. 그리고 조합은 공장주들한테서 그 몫을 받아 내지 못하면 제 할 일을 못 한 거라고 하고. 그렇지만 어찌 된 셈인지 공장주들이 윗자리를 쥐고 있으니, 나는 그이들이 이제도 앞으로도 영영 그 자리를 놓지 않을까 겁이 나오. 서로 이를 드러내고 싸우고 또 싸우다가, 싸우는 그 와중에 하나씩 구덩이로 끌려 들어가는 꼴이 꼭 아마겟돈의 큰 싸움 같구먼.”\n\n바로 그때 니컬러스 히긴스가 들어왔다. 그는 딸의 마지막 말을 들었다.\n\n“그럼! 나도 계속 싸울 거고, 이번에는 기어이 얻어 낼 거요. 그들을 굽히게 하는 데는 오래 안 걸릴 거요. 계약으로 묶인 주문을 잔뜩 받아 놓았으니 말이오. 그러니 우리한테 5퍼센트를 주는 편이 벌어들일 이문을 놓치는 것보다 낫다는 걸 곧 알게 될 거요. 계약을 못 채워서 무는 위약금은 말할 것도 없고. 아하, 공장주 나리들! 누가 이길지는 내가 알지.”\n\n마거릿은 그의 태도로 미루어 그가 술을 마셨나 보다고 짐작했다. 말의 내용보다도 흥분해서 말하는 투 때문이었다. 그리고 베시가 그녀를 어서 보내려고 애쓰는 기색이 뚜렷한 것을 보고 그 짐작에 얼마쯤 확신이 섰다. 베시가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“21일이면 다음 주 목요일이구먼. 손턴 댁에 가려고 차려입은 댁을 보러 가도 되겠지요. 만찬이 몇 시요?”\n\n마거릿이 대답하기도 전에 히긴스가 불쑥 끼어들었다.\n\n“손턴 댁이라고! 손턴 댁에 만찬을 하러 간다고? 그 사람더러 주문이 잘되기를 비는 잔을 가득 채워 들라고 하시우. 21일쯤이면 그걸 제때 어떻게 다 해내나 골머리가 터질 테니까. 그리고 이렇게 전하시우. 5퍼센트를 주고 나면 그다음 날 아침에 700명이 말버러 공장으로 줄지어 들어가 눈 깜짝할 새에 계약을 끝내 주겠다고. 거기 가면 그자들이 다 모여 있을 거요. 우리 공장주 햄퍼 씨도. 그이는 옛날식 축에 드는 사람이오. 사람을 만나면 욕지거리나 악담 없이는 말을 안 하지. 나한테 점잖게 말을 걸면 나는 저이가 죽을 때가 됐나 보다 할 거요. 그래도 따지고 보면 짖는 소리가 무는 것보다 사납지. 그이 밑에서 파업한 놈 하나가 그러더라고 전해도 좋소. 아이고! 손턴 댁에 가면 내로라하는 공장주들이 잔뜩 있겠구먼! 나는 그자들이 만찬 뒤에 좀 앉아 쉴 마음이 들어서 죽어도 달아나지 못할 때 한번 말을 붙여 보고 싶소. 내 속을 다 털어놓겠소. 그자들이 우리를 몰아붙이는 그 모진 방식에 대고 대놓고 따지겠소!”\n\n“안녕히 계세요!” 마거릿이 서둘러 말했다. “잘 있어요, 베시! 몸이 괜찮으면 21일에 보기로 해요.”\n\n도널드슨 의사가 헤일 부인에게 처방한 약과 치료가 처음에는 워낙 효험이 있어서 그녀 자신은 물론이고 마거릿까지도 의사가 잘못 본 것이 아닐까, 어머니가 아주 회복될 수도 있지 않을까 하는 희망을 품기 시작했다. 헤일 씨로 말하자면 그들의 근심이 얼마나 심각한 성격의 것인지 한 번도 헤아려 본 적이 없었으면서도, 그 두려움을 눌러 이겼다는 안도를 뚜렷이 드러냈다. 그것은 그 근심의 성격을 언뜻 엿본 것만으로도 그가 얼마나 크게 흔들렸는지를 말해 주었다. 오직 딕슨만이 노상 마거릿의 귀에 대고 까마귀처럼 불길한 소리를 해 댔다. 그러나 마거릿은 그 까마귀에게 맞서며 기어이 희망을 품으려 했다.\n\n집 안에는 이 한 줄기 밝은 빛이 필요했다. 밖에서는, 아직 사정에 어두운 그들의 눈에도 불만이 음침하게 서려 뒤끓는 기색이 보였기 때문이다. 헤일 씨에게는 노동자들 가운데 나름의 아는 사람들이 있었는데, 그들이 진지하게 들려주는 고통과 오랜 인내의 이야기에 그는 마음이 무거워졌다. 그들은 처지로 보아 말하지 않아도 알아들었을 사람에게라면 자기들이 견뎌야 하는 것을 입에 올리기를 자존심 상해했을 것이다. 그러나 여기 이 사람은 먼 고장에서 온 이였고, 자기가 한복판으로 던져진 그 체제가 어떻게 돌아가는지 몰라 어리둥절해하고 있었다. 그래서 저마다 그를 재판관으로 삼고 싶어 했고, 제 나름의 분한 사연을 증언으로 가져오고 싶어 했다. 그러면 헤일 씨는 그 불평의 보따리를 모조리 챙겨서 손턴 씨 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다. 공장주로서의 경험을 지닌 그가 그것들을 정리해 주고 그 뿌리를 설명해 주기를 바라서였다. 손턴 씨는 언제나 그렇게 해 주었는데, 늘 견실한 경제 원리에 따라서였다. 장사가 굴러가는 방식상 상업의 번영에는 반드시 차오름과 이지러짐이 있게 마련이고, 이지러질 때에는 일꾼들뿐 아니라 일정한 수의 공장주도 몰락하여 행복하고 번창하는 대열에서 자취를 감출 수밖에 없다는 것이었다. 그는 이 결과가 너무도 논리적이어서, 그것이 제 운명이 되더라도 고용주든 피고용인이든 불평할 권리가 없다는 듯이 말했다. 고용주는 더 이상 달릴 수 없게 된 경주에서 비켜서야 한다. 무능과 실패의 쓰라린 감각을 안고서, 다툼 속에서 상처 입고, 부자가 되려고 서두르는 동료들에게 짓밟히고, 한때 존경받던 자리에서 무시당하고, 예전에는 왕처럼 베풀던 일자리를 이제는 겸손히 구걸하면서 말이다. 물론 공장주로서 상업의 부침 속에 자신에게도 닥칠 수 있는 운명을 그렇게 말하는 사람이었으니, 그 무자비하고 빠른 개선이나 변화 속에서 밀려난 노동자들의 운명에는 더 많은 동정을 품었을 리가 없다. 그 노동자들은 자기들을 필요로 하지 않는 이 세상에서 그저 드러누워 조용히 사라져 버리고 싶으면서도, 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 사랑하는 무력한 이들의 매달리는 울음소리 때문에 무덤에서조차 결코 쉬지 못하리라고 느꼈다. 제 심장의 피로 새끼를 먹일 수 있는 들새의 힘을 부러워한 사람들이었다. 손턴 씨가 이런 식으로 논리를 펼 때면, 마치 상업이 전부이고 인간다움은 아무것도 아니라는 듯한 그 말투에 마거릿의 온 영혼이 들고일어났다. 그녀는 그가 개인으로서 베푼 친절에 대해서도 고마워하기가 어려웠다. 그는 바로 그날 저녁에 찾아와 자기 재산과 어머니의 앞을 내다보는 마음 덕분에 그 집에 갖춰져 있던 병자를 위한 온갖 편의를, 도널드슨 의사에게서 들은 대로 헤일 부인에게 필요할지도 모르는 그것들을 내주겠다고 했다. 남몰래 권해야 한다는 것을 헤아릴 만큼 섬세하기까지 했다. 그러나 그렇게 말해 놓은 뒤에 나타난 그의 존재, 어머니에게서 아직 비켜 갈지도 모른다고 헛되이 자신을 설득하려 애쓰던 그 운명을 눈앞에 들이미는 그의 존재, 그 모든 것이 어우러져 그를 바라보고 그의 말을 듣는 마거릿의 신경을 곤두세웠다. 도대체 무슨 자격으로 그가, 도널드슨 의사와 딕슨을 빼고는 유일하게 그 끔찍한 비밀을 아는 사람이 되었단 말인가? 그녀가 마음속 가장 어둡고 성스러운 구석에 가둬 둔 비밀, 그것을 감당할 하늘의 힘을 구하지 않고서는 감히 마주 볼 수조차 없던 비밀, 머지않아 어느 날 어머니를 소리쳐 부르게 되겠지만 그 텅 비고 말 없는 어둠에서는 아무 대답도 오지 않으리라는 비밀을. 그런데도 그는 다 알고 있었다. 그녀는 그것을 그의 가엾어하는 눈에서 보았다. 그의 무겁고 떨리는 목소리에서 들었다. 그 눈, 그 목소리를 어떻게 저 딱딱하고 이치만 따지고 메마르고 무자비한 태도와, 장사의 공리를 늘어놓고는 태연히 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나가는 태도와 나란히 놓을 수 있단 말인가? 그 어긋남이 그녀에게는 말할 수 없이 거슬렸다. 베시에게서 전해 듣는 재앙이 쌓여 가는 터라 더욱 그러했다. 물론 아버지 니컬러스 히긴스는 다르게 말했다. 그는 위원으로 뽑혔고, 바깥사람들은 전혀 모르는 속사정을 자기는 알고 있다고 했다. 그가 그 말을 유난히 또렷하고 자세하게 한 것은 바로 손턴 부인의 만찬 전날이었다. 마거릿이 베시와 이야기하려고 들어갔을 때 그는 바우처와 그 문제로 언쟁을 벌이고 있었다. 마거릿이 여러 번 이야기를 들은 그 이웃은, 딸린 식구는 많은데 솜씨가 없는 일꾼이라 어떤 때는 히긴스의 동정을 자아냈고, 또 어떤 때는 히긴스가 기개라고 부르는 것이 없다는 이유로 그 활기차고 낙천적인 이웃을 격분시키곤 했다. 마거릿이 들어섰을 때 히긴스가 몹시 화가 나 있는 것은 누가 보아도 분명했다. 바우처는 꽤 높다란 벽난로 선반에 두 손을 얹고 서서, 그렇게 짚은 팔이 받쳐 주는 힘에 몸을 조금씩 흔들며 불을 물끄러미 사납게 들여다보고 있었다. 그 얼굴에 서린 일종의 절망은 히긴스의 마음을 저리게 하면서도 동시에 그를 짜증 나게 했다. 베시는 몸을 앞뒤로 세차게 흔들고 있었는데, 마거릿은 이제 그것이 그녀가 흔들릴 때 나오는 버릇임을 알고 있었다. 동생 메리는 코르덴 재단 일을 하러 가려고 보닛 끈을 매고 있었다. 굵고 뭉툭한 손가락에 어울리게 크고 서투른 매듭이었다. 그러면서 내내 소리 내어 훌쩍였고, 자기를 괴롭게 하는 그 자리를 어서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 것이 뚜렷했다.\n\n마거릿은 이런 광경 속으로 들어섰다. 그녀는 잠시 문가에 서 있다가 손가락을 입술에 대고는 베시 곁의 스쿼브 자리로 살그머니 다가가 앉았다. 니컬러스는 그녀가 들어오는 것을 보고 퉁명스럽기는 해도 쌀쌀맞지는 않은 고갯짓으로 인사했다. 메리는 열린 문을 반갑게 붙잡고 집 밖으로 서둘러 나갔고, 아버지의 눈앞에서 벗어나자 소리 내어 울었다. 누가 들어오고 누가 나가는지 도무지 아랑곳하지 않은 사람은 존 바우처뿐이었다.\n\n“소용없소, 히긴스. 우리 집사람은 이대로는 오래 못 살아. 그냥 사그라들고 있단 말이오. 제가 먹을 게 없어서가 아니라, 어린것들이 굶는 꼴을 차마 못 보아서 그런 거요. 그래, 굶는단 말이오! 주급 5실링이면 자네야 넉넉하겠지. 먹일 입이 둘뿐인 데다 그중 하나는 제 먹을 것쯤 넉넉히 벌어 오는 계집애니 말이오. 그렇지만 우리한테는 그게 굶는 거요. 내 대놓고 말하겠소. 만약 집사람이 죽으면, 나는 우리가 5퍼센트를 받아 내기도 전에 그렇게 될까 봐 겁이 나는데, 그러면 나는 그 돈을 공장주 낯짝에 도로 내던지면서 이렇게 말할 거요. ‘너희 따위 다 뒈져라. 사내한테 자식을 낳아 준 가장 좋은 아내마저 남겨 두지 못한 이 모진 세상 다 뒈져라.’ 그리고 이보게, 나는 자네도 조합 무리도 다 미워할 거요. 그래, 내 미움을 안고 천국까지라도 자네를 쫓아다닐 거요. 정말 그럴 거요, 이 사람아! 정말이오. 만약 자네가 이 일로 나를 그르치는 거라면 말이오. 자네가 그랬잖소, 니컬러스, 일주일 전 수요일에. 지금은 둘째 주 화요일이오. 보름 안에 공장주들이 우리한테 빌러 와서 우리가 부르는 품삯으로 일을 도로 맡아 달라고 할 거라고 했지. 그런데 그 기한이 다 되어 가오. 그리고 우리 어린 잭은 자리에 누워, 울 기운도 없이 그저 이따금 배가 고파 흐느껴 목이 멜 뿐이오. 우리 어린 잭 말이오, 이 사람아! 집사람은 그 애가 태어난 뒤로 고개 한번 들어 보지 못했고, 제 목숨인 양 그 애를 사랑하오. 실제로 그렇기도 하고. 그 애가 나한테 그 귀한 값을 치르게 할 것 같으니 말이오. 우리 어린 잭 말이오. 아침마다 그 곱고 조그만 입술을 내 크고 거칠고 지저분한 얼굴에 대고 입 맞출 매끈한 자리를 찾으며 나를 깨우던 아이요. 그 애가 굶고 누워 있단 말이오.” 여기서 깊은 흐느낌이 이 가엾은 사내의 목을 막았고, 니컬러스는 말을 꺼낼 용기를 내기 전에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈을 들어 마거릿을 쳐다보았다.\n\n“기운 차리게, 이 사람아. 자네 어린 잭은 굶기지 않을 걸세. 나한테 돈이 좀 있으니 지금 당장 가서 그 아이 먹일 우유하고 4파운드짜리 빵 한 덩이를 사 오세. 자네가 아쉬우면 내 것이 곧 자네 것이지, 그야 두말할 것 없고. 그저 낙심하지만 말게, 이 사람아!” 그는 찻주전자를 뒤져 가진 돈을 찾으면서 말을 이었다. “내 심장과 영혼을 걸고 말하는데 이 모든 일에도 우리가 이길 걸세. 딱 한 주만 더 버티면 되네. 그러면 공장주들이 어떻게 돌아서서 제발 공장으로 돌아와 달라고 우리한테 비는지 보게 될 걸세. 그리고 조합이, 그러니까 내가 자네 아이들과 안사람이 굶지 않도록 챙기겠네. 그러니 겁먹고 마음 약해져서 폭군들한테 일자리를 구하러 가지는 말게.”\n\n그 말에 사내가 몸을 돌렸다. 돌아선 얼굴은 어찌나 창백하고 여위고 눈물로 골이 파이고 희망이 없어 보이던지, 그 고요함 자체가 마거릿을 울게 만들었다.\n\n“자네도 잘 알잖소. 공장주들보다 더한 폭군이 이러고 있다는 걸. ‘조합에 맞설 엄두를 내느니 굶어 죽어라, 네 식구들이 다 굶어 죽는 꼴을 보아라.’ 자네도 잘 알잖소, 니컬러스. 자네가 그 조합의 하나인데도 말이오. 하나하나 떼어 놓고 보면 다들 마음씨 고운 사람들이겠지. 그렇지만 일단 한데 뭉치고 나면, 굶주림에 미친 들이리보다도 사람을 가엾이 여길 줄 모르오.”\n\n니컬러스는 문고리에 손을 얹은 참이었다. 그는 걸음을 멈추고 바짝 뒤따라오는 바우처를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n“하느님이 나를 도우시기를! 이 사람아, 내가 자네를 위해서, 우리 모두를 위해서 최선을 다하고 있다고 여기지 않는다면 말일세. 내가 옳게 가고 있다고 여기면서 그르게 가고 있는 거라면, 그건 나를 이 무지 속에 이대로 내버려 둔 자들의 죄일세. 나는 머리가 지끈거리도록 생각했네. 정말일세, 존, 정말로 그랬네. 그리고 다시 말하지만 우리에게는 조합을 믿는 것 말고는 살길이 없네. 그들이 이길 걸세, 못 이기나 두고 보게!”\n\n마거릿도 베시도 한마디도 하지 않았다. 서로의 눈이 상대에게 마음 깊은 곳에서 길어 올리라고 청하던 그 한숨조차 겨우 내뱉었을 뿐이었다. 마침내 베시가 말했다.\n\n“아버지가 다시 하느님을 부르는 걸 듣게 될 줄은 몰랐구먼. 그렇지만 댁도 들었잖수, ‘하느님이 나를 도우시기를!’ 하는 말을.”\n\n“네!” 마거릿이 말했다. “내가 낼 수 있는 만큼 돈을 가져오게 해 줘요. 저 가엾은 분의 아이들에게 먹을 것도 조금 가져오게 해 줘요. 그게 당신 아버님 말고 다른 데서 왔다는 건 그이들이 모르게 하고요. 얼마 안 되겠지만요.”\n\n베시는 마거릿의 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 뒤로 몸을 기댔다. 그녀는 울지 않았다. 그저 숨결을 떨었을 뿐이었다.\n\n“내 마음은 눈물이 다 말라붙었소.” 그녀가 말했다. “바우처 씨가 요 며칠 여기 와서 제 두려움이며 걱정거리를 나한테 죄다 이야기했구먼. 나도 그이가 좀 무른 축이라는 건 아오. 그렇지만 그래도 사내는 사내요. 그리고 나는 예전부터 여러 번 그이한테도, 그이 못지않게 살림을 꾸릴 줄 모르는 그 안사람한테도 화를 냈지만, 있잖수, 사람이 다 지혜로운 건 아닌데도 하느님은 그이들을 살게 하시잖수. 그럼, 사랑할 사람도 주시고 사랑받게도 해 주시지. 솔로몬한테 하신 것만큼이나 좋게 말이오. 그러니 그이들이 사랑하는 사람한테 슬픔이 닥치면 그게 솔로몬한테 그랬던 것만큼이나 사무치게 아픈 거요. 나는 그 이치를 모르겠구먼. 어쩌면 바우처 씨 같은 사람은 조합이 돌봐 주는 게 잘된 일인지도 모르지. 그렇지만 나는 그 조합을 만드는 사람들을 좀 보고 싶소. 그이들을 하나씩 바우처 씨와 얼굴을 맞대게 해 놓고 말이오. 내 생각엔 그 사람들이 그이 말을 들으면, 내가 하나씩 따로 붙잡아 놓기만 한다면, 정한 만큼은 못 되더라도 도로 가서 제 일삯을 받을 수 있는 대로 받으라고 할 거요.”\n\n마거릿은 아무 말도 못 하고 앉아 있었다. 그녀가 어떻게 편안한 곳으로 돌아가 저 사내의 목소리를, 말보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 그가 견뎌야 하는 것을 말해 주던 그 이루 말할 수 없이 참담한 어조를 잊을 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 지갑을 꺼냈다. 그 안에는 제 것이라 부를 만한 돈이 그리 많지 않았지만, 있는 대로 다 말없이 베시의 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n“고맙수. 그이보다 더 못 받으면서도 그만큼 딱하지 않은 사람이 숱하다오. 적어도 그이처럼 티를 내지는 않으니 말이오. 그렇지만 아버지가 알게 되었으니 그이들을 굶기지는 않을 거요. 있잖수, 바우처 씨는 자식들 때문에 주저앉은 사람이오. 게다가 안사람은 그렇게 성마르고, 전당 잡힐 만한 건 지난 열두 달 동안 죄다 잡혀 버렸고. 우리도 좀 쪼들리기는 하지만 그렇다고 그이들을 굶게 두었으리라고는 생각하지 마시우. 이웃이 이웃을 돌보지 않으면 누가 돌보겠수.” 베시는 자기들에게 마땅히 도와야 할 몫이 있다고 여기는 그 사람을 도울 뜻이, 그리고 어느 정도는 그럴 힘이 없다고 마거릿이 생각할까 봐 거의 겁을 내는 듯했다. “게다가.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “아버지는 며칠 안에 공장주들이 반드시 굽히고 들어올 거라고, 그이들이 더는 오래 못 버틴다고 확신하고 있소. 그래도 어쨌든 고맙수. 바우처 씨를 위해서만큼이나 나 자신을 위해서도 고맙소. 이러니 댁한테 마음이 자꾸만 더 따뜻해지는구먼.”\n\n베시는 오늘 훨씬 조용해 보였지만 무섭게 기운이 없고 지쳐 있었다. 말을 마쳤을 때 그녀가 하도 창백하고 고단해 보여서 마거릿은 겁이 났다.\n\n“아무것도 아니오.” 베시가 말했다. “아직 죽음은 아니오. 간밤에 꿈 때문에, 아니 꿈 비슷한 것 때문에 몹시 시달렸소. 나는 말짱히 깨어 있었으니 말이오. 그래서 오늘은 온통 아뜩한 혼미 속에 잠겨 있구먼. 다만 저 가엾은 사내가 나를 도로 살아나게 했지. 아니오! 아직 죽음은 아니오. 그래도 죽음이 멀지 않았소. 그래. 나를 덮어 주우. 기침이 놓아주면 어쩌면 잠이 들지도 모르겠소. 잘 자시우. 아니, 안녕히 가시라고 해야겠구먼. 그래도 오늘은 빛이 침침하고 뿌옇기만 하오.”\n\n# 제20장\n\n“늙은이든 젊은이든, 얘야, 다들 먹게 두어라, 내 뜻이 그러하다. 저마다 이빨을 열 줄씩 갖게 두어도 나는 개의치 않는다.” — 노르망디 공작 롤로.\n\n마거릿은 보고 들은 것에 마음이 아프도록 사로잡힌 채 집으로 돌아왔고, 자기를 기다리는 의무를 감당하려면 어떻게 기운을 내야 할지 알 수 없었다. 이제 밖에 나가지 못하게 된 어머니는 마거릿이 아무리 짧은 산책에서 돌아와도 늘 무슨 소식을 가져오리라 기대했으므로, 어머니를 위해 끊임없이 즐거운 이야깃거리를 흘려보내야 하는 의무였다.\n\n“그래, 네 공장 친구는 목요일에 네가 차려입은 걸 보러 올 수 있다더냐?”\n\n“그 애가 너무 아파서 물어볼 생각조차 못 했어요.” 마거릿이 서글프게 말했다.\n\n“어머나! 요즘은 다들 아픈 모양이구나.” 헤일 부인이 병자가 다른 병자에게 품기 쉬운 시샘을 조금 섞어 말했다. “그래도 그런 뒷골목 좁은 집에서 앓는다면 참 딱한 일이겠지.” (타고난 다정한 성품이 이기고, 옛 헬스톤 시절의 생각하던 버릇이 되돌아온 것이었다.) “여기서도 힘든 판인데. 마거릿, 네가 그 애한테 무얼 해 줄 수 있겠니? 네가 나간 사이에 손턴 씨가 오래 묵힌 포트와인을 좀 보내 주셨단다. 그걸 한 병 주면 그 애한테 좋을까?”\n\n“아니에요, 어머니! 그 집이 아주 가난한 것 같지는 않아요. 적어도 그런 식으로 말하지는 않던걸요. 게다가 베시의 병은 폐병이라 포도주를 바라지도 않을 거예요. 우리 헬스톤의 귀한 과일로 만든 잼이라면 조금 가져다줄 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 아니! 제가 무언가 드리고 싶은 집은 따로 있어요. 아, 어머니, 어머니! 오늘 그런 슬픔을 보고 와서 제가 어떻게 곱게 차려입고 화려한 잔치에 나가 놀 수 있겠어요?” 마거릿은 집에 들어오기 전에 스스로 정해 둔 선을 무너뜨리며 이렇게 외쳤고, 히긴스의 오두막에서 보고 들은 것을 어머니에게 이야기했다.\n\n그 이야기는 헤일 부인을 몹시 괴롭혔다. 무엇이든 하지 않고서는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 안절부절못하게 만들었다. 부인은 마거릿에게 바로 그 응접실에서 바구니를 꾸리게 하여 당장 그 집으로 보내라고 일렀고, 히긴스가 당장 필요한 것은 마련해 두었고 자기도 베시에게 돈을 남기고 왔으니 아침에 보내도 상관없다고 말하는 딸에게 하마터면 화를 낼 뻔했다. 헤일 부인은 그런 말을 한다고 딸을 무정하다 나무랐고, 바구니가 집을 나설 때까지 한숨 돌릴 틈도 자신에게 주지 않았다. 그러고 나서 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래도 어쩌면 우리가 잘못한 것인지도 모르겠구나. 바로 지난번에 손턴 씨가 여기 오셨을 때, 파업하는 사람들을 도와 싸움을 길게 끄는 데 손을 보태는 이들은 참된 벗이 아니라고 하셨잖니. 그런데 그 바우처라는 사람도 파업꾼이 아니냐?”\n\n헤일 부인은 남편이 위층으로 올라오자 이 물음을 그에게 넘겼다. 그는 손턴 씨에게 막 수업을 하고 온 참이었는데, 늘 그렇듯 수업은 대화로 끝나 있었다. 마거릿은 자기들의 도움이 다툼을 길게 끌었는지 어떤지 개의치 않았다. 지금처럼 마음이 격해진 상태에서는 그렇게까지 멀리 생각이 미치지 않았다.\n\n헤일 씨는 이야기를 듣고 판관처럼 침착하려 애썼다. 그는 반 시간도 채 되기 전에 손턴 씨의 입에서 흘러나올 때는 그토록 분명해 보이던 이야기를 모두 떠올렸다. 그러고는 흡족하지 못한 절충안을 내놓았다. 이번 경우에 아내와 딸은 아주 옳게 행동했을 뿐 아니라, 달리 어떻게 할 수 있었을지 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 것이었다. 그렇더라도 일반론으로는 손턴 씨의 말이 참으로 옳다고 했다. 파업이 길어지면 결국 공장주들이 먼 데서 직공을 데려오는 것으로 끝날 수밖에 없고(아니면 예전에도 여러 번 그랬듯 아예 직공의 필요를 줄여 줄 어떤 기계의 발명으로 끝나거나), 그렇다면 그들을 어리석음 속에 버티게 떠받쳐 줄 온갖 도움을 거절하는 것이야말로 가장 인정 있는 일임이 분명하다는 것이었다. 다만 이 바우처라는 사람에 대해서는, 아침에 제일 먼저 찾아가 보고 그를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알아보겠다고 했다.\n\n헤일 씨는 말한 대로 이튿날 아침에 갔다. 바우처는 집에 없었으나 그 아내와 오래 이야기를 나누었고, 그녀를 위해 병원 진료권을 청해 보겠다고 약속했다. 그리고 헤일 부인이 보낸 넉넉한 물건들을, 아버지가 없는 사이 아래층의 주인 노릇을 하는 아이들이 다소 헤프게 쓰고 있는 것을 보고는, 마거릿이 감히 바라던 것보다 한결 위안이 되고 밝은 소식을 들고 돌아왔다. 사실 전날 밤 딸이 한 이야기가 아버지에게 훨씬 더 나쁜 형편을 각오하게 만든 터라, 상상이 되돌아오는 반동으로 그는 모든 것을 실제보다 좋게 그려 말한 것이었다.\n\n“그래도 다시 가서 그 사람을 직접 만나 봐야겠구나.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “여기 이런 집들을 우리 헬스톤 오두막과 어떻게 견주어야 할지 아직도 잘 모르겠다. 여기서는 우리 고장 일꾼들이라면 살 생각조차 못 했을 세간이 보이고, 그들이라면 사치라 여길 음식을 예사로 먹더구나. 그런데 정작 그 집들은 주급이 끊기고 나면 전당포 말고는 아무 방도가 없는 것 같으니 말이다. 이 밀턴에서는 다른 잣대를 배워야겠어.”\n\n베시도 이날은 좀 나았다. 그래도 어찌나 기운이 없는지 차려입은 마거릿을 보고 싶다던 소원을 아주 잊어버린 듯했다. 실은 그것이 반쯤 헛소리를 하던 상태에서 나온 들뜬 바람이 아니었다면 말이다.\n\n마거릿은 가고 싶지도 않은 곳에 가려고 이렇게 야릇하게 몸단장을 하면서, 온갖 근심으로 마음이 무거운 채로, 채 일 년도 되지 않은 옛날 이디스와 함께하던 그 즐겁고 처녀다운 몸단장을 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 이제 그녀가 자신을 꾸미며 느끼는 유일한 기쁨은 차려입은 딸을 보며 어머니가 즐거워하리라는 생각뿐이었다. 딕슨이 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 감탄을 청했을 때 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n“헤일 양이 곱지 않습니까요, 마님? 쇼 부인의 산호가 이보다 더 잘 어울릴 수는 없겠어요. 딱 알맞게 빛깔을 더해 주는걸요, 마님. 그게 아니었으면 마거릿 아가씨는 너무 창백해 보였을 겁니다요.”\n\n마거릿의 검은 머리는 너무 숱이 많아 땋을 수가 없었다. 오히려 몇 번이고 감아 돌려 그 고운 비단결을 묵직한 타래로 눌러 붙여야 했고, 그것이 왕관처럼 머리를 둘러싼 뒤 뒤쪽에서 커다란 소용돌이 모양으로 틀어 올려졌다. 그녀는 그 무게를 큼직한 산호 핀 두 개로 여며 두었는데, 길이가 작은 화살만 했다. 흰 비단 소매는 같은 감의 끈으로 걷어 올려 묶었고, 우유처럼 흰 목덜미가 곡선을 그리며 내려앉는 바로 아래에는 묵직한 산호 구슬이 걸려 있었다.\n\n“아, 마거릿! 너를 데리고 옛날 배링턴 무도회에 가면 얼마나 좋을까. 베러스퍼드 부인이 나를 데리고 다니셨듯이 너를 데리고 말이다.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니가 터뜨린 이 자그마한 모성의 허영에 입을 맞추었다. 그러나 마음이 너무 가라앉아 미소조차 짓기 어려웠다.\n\n“저는 차라리 어머니와 집에 있고 싶어요. 훨씬 더요, 어머니.”\n\n“무슨 소리냐, 얘야! 만찬을 잘 눈여겨보아야 한다. 밀턴에서는 그런 것을 어떻게 차리는지 듣고 싶구나. 특히 두 번째 코스를 잘 보렴, 얘야. 사냥한 고기 대신 무엇을 내는지 보아라.”\n\n헤일 부인이 그 만찬 식탁의 호사스러움과 갖추어진 것들을 보았더라면 관심 정도가 아니라 깜짝 놀랐을 것이다. 런던에서 길러진 안목을 지닌 마거릿은 진미의 가짓수가 숨 막힐 지경이라고 느꼈다. 절반만 되어도 넉넉했을 테고, 그 편이 한결 짜임새 있고 우아했을 것이다. 그러나 손님들이 마음이 내키면 누구나 맛볼 수 있도록 진미마다 넉넉히 마련해 두는 것이 손턴 부인의 엄격한 접대 원칙 가운데 하나였다. 일상에서는 절제를 넘어 무심할 만큼 검소했지만, 원하는 손님 앞에 잔칫상을 차려 내는 것은 그녀의 자부심의 한 부분이었다. 아들도 이 마음을 같이했다. 그는 훌륭한 식사를 주고받는 데 기대는 사교 말고 다른 교제를 알아 본 적이 없었다. 그런 것을 그려 볼 수도 있었고 즐길 능력도 있었지만 말이다. 그리고 지금은 쓸데없는 6펜스 한 닢도 제 몫으로는 쓰지 않고 있었고 이 만찬의 초대장을 보낸 것을 여러 번 후회하기까지 했지만, 그래도 이왕 치를 일이라면 옛날 그대로의 성대한 준비를 보는 것이 기뻤다.\n\n마거릿과 아버지가 가장 먼저 도착했다. 헤일 씨는 정해진 시간을 지키려고 애타게 마음을 썼다. 위층 응접실에는 손턴 부인과 패니밖에 없었다. 덮개는 모두 벗겨져 있었고, 방은 노란 비단 다마스크와 화려한 꽃무늬 양탄자로 눈부시게 빛났다. 구석마다 장식으로 가득 채워져 눈이 피곤할 지경이었고, 마차를 들이려고 넓은 접이문이 활짝 열려 있는 커다란 공장 마당 쪽의 헐벗고 흉한 풍경과 야릇한 대조를 이루었다. 창문 왼편으로는 공장이 높이 솟아 여러 층 높이에서 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 그늘이 여름 저녁을 제 시간보다 일찍 어둡게 만들었다.\n\n“아들이 마지막 순간까지 일에 매여 있었답니다. 곧 올 겁니다, 헤일 씨. 앉으시지요.”\n\n손턴 부인이 말할 때 헤일 씨는 창가에 서 있었다. 그는 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n“공장이 이렇게 가까이 붙어 있으면 이따금 불편하지 않으십니까?”\n\n그녀는 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다.\n\n“전혀요. 저는 아들의 부와 힘이 어디서 나오는지 잊고 싶어질 만큼 고상해지지는 않았습니다. 게다가 밀턴에 저만한 공장은 또 없지요. 방 하나만 해도 220평방야드입니다.”\n\n“제 말은 연기와 소음이, 직공들이 끊임없이 드나드는 것이 성가시지 않겠느냐는 뜻이었습니다!”\n\n“저도 같은 생각이에요, 헤일 씨!” 패니가 말했다. “늘 증기와 기름 친 기계 냄새가 나고, 소음은 정말이지 귀가 먹먹할 지경이거든요.”\n\n“음악이라 부르는 소리 가운데도 그보다 훨씬 더 귀먹게 하는 것을 저는 들어 보았습니다. 기관실은 공장에서 길 쪽 끝에 있어서, 창문을 다 여는 여름철이 아니면 소리가 거의 들리지 않아요. 직공들의 끊임없는 웅성거림으로 말하자면, 벌통에서 벌이 붕붕대는 것만큼도 저를 방해하지 않습니다. 굳이 그 소리를 생각한다면, 저는 그것을 아들과 이어서 생각하고, 그 모든 것이 아들의 것이며 그것을 이끄는 머리가 아들의 머리라는 것을 느낍니다. 지금은 공장에서 아무 소리도 나지 않지요. 아마 들으셨겠지만 직공들이 은혜도 모르고 파업에 들어갔으니까요. 그런데 댁이 들어오실 때 제가 말씀드린 바로 그 일이라는 게, 그자들에게 제 자리를 알게 하려고 아들이 밟으려는 절차에 관한 것이었습니다.” 이 말을 하는 동안 언제나 준엄한 그녀의 얼굴빛이 어두운 분노로 깊어졌다. 손턴 씨가 방에 들어왔을 때에도 그 표정은 걷히지 않았다. 손님들은 그에게서 밝고 다정해 보이는 인사를 받았지만, 그녀는 아들이 떨쳐 내지 못하는 근심과 걱정의 무게를 한눈에 알아보았던 것이다. 그는 마거릿과 악수를 했다. 그는 두 사람의 손이 맞닿은 것이 이번이 처음임을 알고 있었으나, 그녀는 그 사실을 조금도 의식하지 못했다. 그는 헤일 부인의 안부를 물었고 헤일 씨의 낙관적이고 희망 섞인 이야기를 들었다. 그리고 마거릿이 아버지의 말에 어디까지 동의하는지 알아보려고 그녀 쪽을 흘긋 보았을 때, 그 얼굴에는 반대하는 그늘이 조금도 스치지 않는 것을 보았다. 그렇게 마음먹고 바라보는 사이 그는 그녀의 빼어난 아름다움에 새삼 마음을 얻어맞았다. 그는 그런 차림의 그녀를 전에 본 적이 없었다. 그런데 이제 보니 그토록 우아한 옷차림이 그녀의 기품 있는 자태와 드높이 고요한 얼굴에 어찌나 잘 어울리는지, 그녀는 늘 그렇게 입고 다녀야 마땅할 것 같았다. 그녀는 패니와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 무슨 이야기인지는 들리지 않았다. 그러나 그는 누이가 잠시도 가만있지 못하고 드레스 여기저기를 매만지는 모습을, 이리저리 헤매는 눈길이 아무 목적 없이 여기를 보았다 저기를 보았다 하는 것을 보았다. 그러고는 그것을, 마치 그 빛 속에서 어떤 부드러운 안식의 기운이 뻗어 나오기라도 하듯 한곳만을 잔잔히 바라보는 크고 부드러운 눈과 불편하게 견주었다. 상대의 말을 흥미롭게 듣느라 살짝 벌어진 붉은 입술의 굽은 선, 조금 앞으로 숙인 머리, 그래서 빛이 윤나는 까마귀빛 머리에 닿는 정수리에서 매끄러운 상앗빛 어깨 끝까지 길게 쓸어내리는 선을 그리는 모습, 둥글고 흰 팔과 가느다란 손이 서로 가볍게 포개진 채 그 어여쁜 자세 그대로 조금도 움직이지 않는 것을. 손턴 씨는 특유의 재빠르고 두루 살피는 눈길 한 번으로 이 모든 것을 받아들이고는 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러고 나서 그는 젊은 여자들에게 등을 돌리고, 애를 써서, 그러나 온 마음과 정신을 다해 헤일 씨와의 대화에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n사람들이 더 왔다. 자꾸자꾸 늘어났다. 패니는 마거릿 곁을 떠나 어머니를 도와 손님을 맞았다. 손턴 씨는 이 밀려드는 사람들 속에서 아무도 마거릿에게 말을 걸지 않는 것을 느꼈고, 이 눈에 띄는 홀대에 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그 자신은 결코 그녀 가까이 가지 않았다. 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았다. 다만 그는 그녀가 무엇을 하고 있는지, 혹은 하지 않고 있는지를 방 안의 다른 누구의 움직임보다 잘 알고 있었다. 마거릿은 자기 자신을 조금도 의식하지 않았고 다른 사람들을 구경하는 것이 하도 재미있어서, 자기가 눈길 밖에 밀려나 있는지 아닌지 생각조차 하지 않았다. 누군가 그녀를 만찬 자리로 이끌었다. 그녀는 그 이름을 알아듣지 못했고, 그 사람도 그녀에게 말을 붙일 마음이 별로 없어 보였다. 신사들 사이에서는 아주 활기찬 대화가 오가고 있었다. 부인들은 대개 말이 없었고, 만찬 차림을 눈여겨보고 서로의 옷을 품평하는 일에 몰두했다. 마거릿은 오가는 이야기의 실마리를 붙잡고는 흥미가 생겨 귀를 기울였다. 이 모임의 애초 씨앗이 된 방문객, 낯선 손님 호스폴 씨는 이 고장의 상업과 제조업에 관해 이것저것 묻고 있었고, 나머지 신사들은 모두 밀턴 사람이라 그에게 대답과 설명을 해 주고 있었다. 어떤 논쟁이 일어나 뜨겁게 다투어졌고, 그것이 그때까지 거의 말이 없던 손턴 씨에게 넘어갔다. 그러나 그가 이제 내놓은 의견은 그 근거가 어찌나 또렷하게 밝혀졌는지 반대하던 이들조차 물러섰다. 그리하여 마거릿의 눈길이 주인에게 쏠렸다. 집주인이자 벗들을 대접하는 사람으로서 그의 태도 전체가 어찌나 거침없으면서도 꾸밈없고 겸손한지, 그야말로 품위 있었다. 마거릿은 그가 이토록 돋보이는 모습을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다. 그가 자기들 집에 왔을 때는 늘 무언가가 있었다. 지나친 열의이거나, 자기가 부당하게 판단받고 있다고 미리 넘겨짚을 태세이면서도 자신을 더 잘 이해시키려 애쓰기에는 너무 자존심이 센 사람 특유의 짜증 섞인 언짢음이거나. 그러나 지금 자기 동료들 사이에서는 그의 자리에 대해 아무런 의심이 없었다. 그들은 그를 성품의 힘이 강한 사람, 여러 면에서 능력 있는 사람으로 여겼다. 그들의 존경을 얻으려고 애쓸 필요가 없었다. 그는 이미 그것을 가지고 있었고 스스로도 알았다. 그리고 그 든든함이 그의 목소리와 태도에 마거릿이 전에는 보지 못했던 훌륭하고 당당한 고요함을 주었다.\n\n그는 여자들과 이야기하는 데 익숙하지 않았고, 하는 말도 조금 딱딱했다. 마거릿 자신에게는 거의 말을 걸지 않았다. 그녀는 자기가 이 만찬을 이토록 즐기고 있다는 사실에 놀랐다. 이제 그녀는 이 고장의 여러 관심사를, 아니 열을 올리는 공장주들이 쓰는 전문 용어까지 얼마쯤 알아들을 만큼은 알고 있었다. 그녀는 그들이 논하는 문제에 대해 말없이 아주 뚜렷한 편을 들었다. 어쨌든 그들은 필사적일 만큼 진지하게 이야기했다. 옛날 런던 모임에서 그녀를 그토록 지치게 하던 닳고 닳은 투가 아니었다. 그녀는 이 고장의 제조업과 상업을 이렇게나 물고 늘어지면서도 그때 걸려 있는 파업 이야기는 한마디도 나오지 않는 것이 이상했다. 그런 일을 공장주들이 얼마나 태연하게, 있을 수 있는 결말은 하나뿐이라는 듯 받아들이는지 그녀는 아직 몰랐다. 물론 그자들은 전에도 여러 번 그랬듯 제 목을 제가 조르고 있는 것이지만, 굳이 바보짓을 해서 삯 받는 파렴치한 대표들 손에 제 몸을 맡기겠다면 그 결과는 저희가 감당해야 한다는 것이었다. 한둘은 손턴이 기운이 없어 보인다고 여겼다. 물론 그도 이 파업으로 손해를 볼 것이었다. 그러나 그것은 자기들에게도 언제든 닥칠 수 있는 사고였고, 손턴은 누구 못지않게 파업을 잘 다룰 사람이었다. 밀턴의 누구 못지않게 쇠 같은 사내였으니까. 직공들은 그런 수작을 그에게 부리다가 사람을 잘못 골랐다. 그리고 그들은 손턴이 정해 놓은 것을 털끝만큼이라도 바꿔 보려다 낭패를 보고 패배할 직공들을 떠올리며 속으로 낄낄거렸다.\n\n만찬 뒤 마거릿에게는 다소 따분한 시간이었다. 그녀는 신사들이 들어오자 반가웠다. 아버지와 눈이 마주쳐 졸음을 떨칠 수 있어서만이 아니라, 부인들이 이야기하던 자잘한 관심사보다 더 크고 더 웅장한 무언가에 귀를 기울일 수 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 이 밀턴 사람들이 지닌 힘의 자각에서 오는 그 의기양양함이 마음에 들었다. 드러내는 방식이 다소 거칠고 자랑처럼 들릴 수도 있었다. 그러나 그들은 이미 이룬 것과 앞으로 이룰 것을 떠올리며 생기는 어떤 훌륭한 도취 속에서 가능성의 오랜 한계에 맞서는 듯했다. 차분해진 순간이라면 그 기개를 모든 점에서 인정하지는 못할지라도, 자기 자신과 지금을 잊어버리는 그 태도에는, 그들 가운데 아무도 살아서 보지 못할 어느 훗날 온갖 무생물을 이겨 내리라 미리 내다보는 그 마음에는 감탄할 것이 많았다. 손턴 씨가 바로 팔꿈치 옆에서 말을 걸었을 때 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n“만찬 자리의 논쟁에서 헤일 양이 우리 편이라는 걸 알 수 있었습니다. 그렇지 않았습니까?”\n\n“물론이에요. 그렇지만 저는 그 일에 대해 아는 것이 너무 적어요. 그래도 호스폴 씨의 말씀에서, 그분이 이야기한 모리슨 씨처럼 정반대로 생각하는 이들이 있다는 것을 알게 되어 놀랐습니다. 그분은 신사일 리가 없어요. 그렇지요?”\n\n“남의 신사다움을 판정하기에 저는 그리 알맞은 사람이 아닙니다, 헤일 양. 제 말은, 그 말을 헤일 양이 어떻게 쓰시는지 잘 모르겠다는 뜻입니다. 다만 저라면 이 모리슨이 참된 사람은 아니라고 하겠습니다. 그가 누구인지 저는 모릅니다. 그저 호스폴 씨의 이야기로 판단할 뿐이지요.”\n\n“제가 말하는 ‘신사’ 안에 손턴 씨가 말씀하시는 ‘참된 사람’이 들어 있는 것 같은데요.”\n\n“게다가 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것이 들어 있다는 말씀이시겠지요. 저는 생각이 다릅니다. 제게는 사람이 신사보다 더 높고 더 온전한 존재입니다.”\n\n“무슨 뜻이신가요?” 마거릿이 물었다. “우리가 그 말들을 서로 다르게 이해하고 있는 모양이에요.”\n\n“제가 보기에 ‘신사’란 어떤 사람을 남들과의 관계에서만 그려 내는 말입니다. 그러나 그를 ‘사람’이라 말할 때 우리는 그를 동료 인간과의 관계에서만이 아니라 자기 자신과의 관계에서, 삶과의, 시간과의, 영원과의 관계에서 헤아리게 됩니다. 로빈슨 크루소처럼 홀로 버려진 조난자도, 평생 지하 감옥에 갇힌 죄수도, 아니 밧모섬의 성자까지도, 그 인내와 힘과 믿음은 ‘사람’이라 불릴 때 가장 잘 드러납니다. 저는 이 ‘신사답다’는 말에 다소 물렸습니다. 제게는 그 말이 흔히 어울리지도 않는 자리에 쓰이고, 흔히 뜻이 지나치게 뒤틀린 채로 쓰이는 것처럼 보이니까요. 그동안 ‘사람’이라는 명사와 ‘사내답다’는 형용사가 지닌 온전한 단순함은 인정받고 있는데도 말입니다. 그래서 저는 그 말을 요즘의 겉치레 유행어에 넣고 싶어집니다.”\n\n마거릿은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러나 천천히 굳어 가는 자신의 확신을 말하기도 전에 그는 열을 올리는 공장주 몇 사람에게 불려 갔다. 그들의 말은 들리지 않았지만, 손턴 씨가 내놓는 짧고 또렷한 대답으로 무슨 이야기인지 짐작할 수 있었다. 그 대답은 멀리서 1분마다 울리는 조포 소리처럼 한결같고 굳세게 들려왔다. 그들은 분명 파업 이야기를 하며 어떤 길을 밟는 것이 가장 나은지 궁리하고 있었다. 그녀는 손턴 씨가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n“그건 이미 해 두었습니다.” 그러자 두어 사람이 끼어든 다급한 웅성거림이 뒤따랐다.\n\n“그 준비는 모두 마쳤습니다.”\n\n슬릭슨 씨가 몇 가지 의심을 내비치고 몇 가지 어려움을 들었다. 그는 자기 말에 무게를 실으려고 손턴 씨의 팔을 붙잡았다. 손턴 씨는 살짝 몸을 비켜 서며 눈썹을 아주 조금 치켜올리고는 대답했다.\n\n“위험은 제가 집니다. 원치 않으시면 함께하지 않으셔도 됩니다.” 그래도 몇 가지 두려움이 더 제기되었다.\n\n“저는 방화 같은 비열한 짓 따위는 두렵지 않습니다. 우리는 드러내 놓고 맞선 적수이고, 저는 제가 짐작할 수 있는 어떤 폭력으로부터도 제 몸을 지킬 수 있습니다. 그리고 일자리를 찾아 제게 오는 다른 사람들도 반드시 지켜 줄 것입니다. 지금쯤은 그자들도 제 결심을 여러분만큼이나 잘, 그리고 낱낱이 알고 있을 겁니다.”\n\n호스폴 씨가 그를 한쪽으로 조금 데려갔다. 마거릿은 파업에 대해 무언가 다른 것을 물으려는 줄 짐작했으나, 실은 그토록 조용하고 그토록 기품 있고 그토록 아름다운 그녀 자신이 누구인지 묻기 위해서였다.\n\n“밀턴 아가씨인가요?” 이름을 듣고 그가 물었다.\n\n“아닙니다! 잉글랜드 남부에서 왔지요. 햄프셔라고 알고 있습니다.” 차갑고 무심한 대답이었다.\n\n슬릭슨 부인은 패니를 붙들고 같은 것을 캐묻고 있었다.\n\n“저 곱고 기품 있어 보이는 아가씨는 누구인가요? 호스폴 씨의 누이인가요?”\n\n“어머, 아니에요! 저기 지금 스티븐스 씨와 이야기하고 계신 헤일 씨가 저 아가씨의 아버지랍니다. 수업을 하세요. 그러니까 젊은 사람들과 함께 책을 읽으신다는 뜻이에요. 우리 오빠 존이 일주일에 두 번 그분께 다니는데, 그분을 알리려고 어머니께 이 자리에 초대해 달라고 부탁했지 뭐예요. 그분들 안내장이 우리 집에 몇 장 있을 텐데, 원하시면 하나 드릴게요.”\n\n“손턴 씨가요! 그 많은 일 가운데, 게다가 이 지긋지긋한 파업까지 안고 있으면서 정말로 선생을 두고 책 읽을 시간을 내신다고요?”\n\n패니는 슬릭슨 부인의 태도로 미루어 오빠의 처신을 자랑스러워해야 할지 부끄러워해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그리고 남들의 “마땅히”를 제 감정의 잣대로 삼으려 드는 모든 사람이 그렇듯, 그녀는 남다른 행동이라면 무엇이든 얼굴을 붉힐 마음이 되어 있었다. 그녀의 부끄러움은 손님들이 흩어지는 바람에 중단되었다.\n\n# 제21장\n\n“이 땅에서 아는 이는 아무도 없다, 눈물과 자매가 아닌 미소를.” — 엘리엇.\n\n마거릿은 아버지와 함께 걸어서 집으로 돌아왔다. 밤은 맑고 거리는 깨끗했으며, 발라드 속 리지 린지의 초록 새틴 드레스처럼 “무릎까지 걷어 올린” 고운 흰 비단을 걸치고 그녀는 아버지와 함께 길을 나섰다. 서늘하고 상쾌한 밤공기의 들뜸에 실려 춤이라도 추며 갈 듯한 마음이었다.\n\n“아무래도 손턴은 이번 파업 때문에 마음이 그리 편치 않은 모양이더구나. 오늘 밤에는 무척 근심스러워 보였어.”\n\n“편하시다면 오히려 이상한 일이겠지요. 그래도 우리가 자리를 뜨기 직전에 다른 분들이 이런저런 방안을 내놓았을 때, 그분은 여느 때와 다름없이 침착하게 말씀하시던걸요.”\n\n“만찬 뒤에도 그랬지. 그 침착한 말투를 흔들어 놓으려면 어지간한 일로는 안 될 게다. 그래도 얼굴만은 근심스러워 보이더구나.”\n\n“제가 그분이라면 저도 근심스러울 거예요. 그분은 직공들 사이에 자라나는 분노와 겨우 눌러 둔 미움을 모를 리 없어요. 그들은 하나같이 그분을 성경에서 말하는 ‘모진 사람’으로 여기잖아요. 불의하다기보다 인정이 없는 사람이요. 판단은 분명하되, 우리와 우리의 하찮은 권리라는 것이 전능하신 분 앞에서 무엇인지를 생각하면 사람으로서 그렇게까지 버텨서는 안 될 만큼 제 ‘권리’ 위에 버티고 선 사람이요. 저는 아버지께서 그분이 근심스러워 보인다고 하시니 다행이에요. 바우처의 반쯤 미친 듯한 말과 몸짓을 떠올리면, 손턴 씨가 얼마나 냉랭하게 말했는지 견딜 수가 없어요.”\n\n“우선 첫째로, 나는 그 바우처라는 사람의 지독한 곤경을 너만큼 확신하지는 않는다. 당장은 형편이 나빴겠지, 그건 의심하지 않아. 하지만 이 조합이라는 데서는 늘 어디선가 알 수 없이 돈이 나오는 법이다. 게다가 네 말로 미루어 보면 그 사람은 성정이 격하고 드러내기를 잘하는 부류라 느끼는 것을 죄다 강하게 터뜨리는 사람인 게 분명하더구나.”\n\n“아, 아버지!”\n\n“그래! 나는 다만 네가 손턴 씨에게 공정하기를 바랄 뿐이다. 내가 짐작하기로 그 사람은 정확히 그 반대되는 성정이야. 제 감정을 드러내기에는 자존심이 너무 센 사람이지. 미리 이야기만 들었더라면 네가 감탄했을 법한 바로 그런 성품이 아니냐, 마거릿.”\n\n“감탄해요. 감탄할 거고요. 하지만 그런 감정이 정말 있는지는 아버지만큼 확신이 서지 않아요. 그분은 성품의 힘이 아주 강한 사람이고, 누려 온 이점이 얼마 없었던 것을 생각하면 보기 드문 지성을 지녔지요.”\n\n“그리 적지도 않다. 그 사람은 아주 어릴 때부터 실제적인 삶을 살아왔고, 판단력과 자제력을 쓰도록 요구받아 왔어. 그 모두가 지성의 한 부분을 길러 준다. 물론 그에게는 과거에 대한 지식이 얼마쯤 필요하지. 그것이야말로 앞날을 헤아리는 데 가장 참된 바탕이 되니까. 하지만 그는 그 필요를 알고 있다. 그것을 스스로 알아차리고 있고, 그것만 해도 대단한 일이야. 너는 손턴 씨에게 아주 편견을 품고 있구나, 마거릿.”\n\n“그분은 제가 여태 살펴볼 기회를 가진 첫 번째 제조업자, 장사에 몸담은 사람의 표본이에요, 아버지. 제 첫 올리브인 셈이지요. 삼키면서 얼굴 한 번 찡그리는 것쯤은 봐주세요. 그분이 그 부류에서 훌륭한 사람이라는 건 알아요. 그리고 차차 저도 그 부류를 좋아하게 되겠지요. 아무래도 벌써 그렇게 되기 시작한 것 같아요. 저는 신사분들이 나누는 이야기가 아주 흥미로웠어요. 절반도 알아듣지 못했지만요. 손턴 양이 와서, 그 많은 신사들 사이에 여자라고는 저 혼자뿐이니 분명 불편할 거라며 저를 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려갔을 때는 정말 아쉬웠어요. 저는 듣느라 바빠서 그런 건 생각도 못 하고 있었거든요. 그리고 부인들은 어찌나 따분하던지요, 아버지. 아, 정말 따분했어요! 그래도 영리하다는 생각은 들었어요. 우리가 예전에 하던 놀이가 떠올랐거든요. 저마다 정해진 명사 몇 개를 문장 속에 다 집어넣는 놀이요.”\n\n“무슨 말이냐, 얘야?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그러니까요, 그분들은 부유함을 드러내 보이는 것들을 가리키는 명사를 고르는 거예요. 가정부, 아래 정원사, 온실 유리의 넓이, 값진 레이스, 다이아몬드, 그런 것들이요. 그리고 저마다 그것들을 되도록 예쁘게, 되도록 우연인 척하는 투로 다 끌어들이도록 말을 지어내는 거지요.”\n\n“너도 하녀를 하나 두게 되면 그 한 사람을 그만큼 자랑스러워하게 될 게다. 손턴 부인이 그 애에 대해 하는 말이 다 사실이라면 말이야.”\n\n“그럼요, 그럴 거예요. 오늘 밤엔 제가 대단한 위선자 같았어요. 흰 비단 드레스를 입고 손을 놀린 채 앉아 있으면서, 우리 집에서 오늘 해낸 그 알차고 빈틈없는 집안일을 다 떠올리자니 말이에요. 그분들은 분명 저를 귀부인으로 여겼을 거예요.”\n\n“나조차도 네가 귀부인처럼 보인다고 잘못 생각할 뻔했다, 얘야.” 헤일 씨가 조용히 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n그러나 딕슨이 문을 열었을 때 그 얼굴을 보고 미소는 창백하게 떨리는 낯빛으로 바뀌었다.\n\n“아, 나리! 아, 마거릿 아가씨! 돌아오셔서 정말 다행입니다요! 도널드슨 의사 선생님이 와 계세요. 파출부는 집으로 가 버려서 옆집 하인이 모시러 갔습니다요. 마님은 이제 좀 나아지셨어요. 하지만 아이고, 나리, 저는 한 시간 전만 해도 마님이 돌아가시는 줄 알았습니다요.”\n\n헤일 씨는 쓰러지지 않으려고 마거릿의 팔을 붙들었다. 그는 딸의 얼굴을 보았고, 거기서 놀람과 극심한 슬픔의 빛을 읽었으나 아무 준비 없던 자기 심장을 옥죄는 그런 공포의 고통은 보지 못했다. 딸은 자기보다 더 많이 알고 있었다. 그러면서도 두려움에 눌린 그 절망적인 예감의 표정으로 이야기를 듣고 있었다.\n\n“아, 어머니 곁을 떠나지 말았어야 했는데. 저는 못된 딸이에요!” 마거릿은 떨리는 아버지의 다급한 걸음을 부축해 위층으로 오르며 신음하듯 내뱉었다. 도널드슨 의사가 층계참에서 두 사람을 맞았다.\n\n“이제 좀 나아지셨습니다.” 그가 속삭였다. “아편이 들었어요. 경련이 아주 심했습니다. 하녀가 놀란 것도 무리가 아니지요. 하지만 이번에는 기운을 되찾으실 겁니다.”\n\n“이번에는이라니! 아내에게 가게 해 주시오!” 30분 전만 해도 헤일 씨는 중년의 남자였다. 그런데 지금은 눈이 흐릿하고 감각이 흔들리고 걸음이 비틀거려 마치 70세 노인 같았다.\n\n도널드슨 의사가 그의 팔을 잡고 침실로 이끌었다. 마거릿이 바짝 뒤따랐다. 거기 어머니가 누워 있었는데, 그 얼굴에는 오해할 수 없는 빛이 어려 있었다. 지금은 나아졌을지도 몰랐다. 어머니는 잠들어 있었다. 그러나 죽음이 이미 그녀를 제 것으로 표해 두었고, 머지않아 다시 돌아와 차지하리라는 것이 분명했다. 헤일 씨는 한동안 말없이 아내를 바라보았다. 그러다 온몸을 떨기 시작했고, 도널드슨 의사의 근심 어린 보살핌을 뿌리치고 돌아서서 손으로 더듬어 문을 찾았다. 갑작스러운 소동에 들여온 촛불 몇 자루가 거기서 타오르며 일렁이고 있었건만 그의 눈에는 문이 보이지 않았다. 그는 비틀거리며 응접실로 들어가 손을 더듬어 의자를 찾았다. 도널드슨 의사가 의자 하나를 밀어다 그를 앉혔다. 그러고는 맥을 짚었다.\n\n“말을 걸어 보십시오, 헤일 양. 정신을 차리게 해 드려야 합니다.”\n\n“아버지!” 마거릿이 고통으로 사나워진 울음 섞인 목소리로 말했다. “아버지! 저한테 말씀 좀 해 보세요!” 그의 눈에 생각하는 빛이 다시 돌아왔고, 그는 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n“마거릿, 너 이걸 알고 있었느냐? 아, 어쩌면 그렇게 모질 수가 있느냐!”\n\n“아닙니다, 선생님, 모진 것이 아니었습니다!” 도널드슨 의사가 재빨리 단호하게 대답했다. “헤일 양은 제 지시에 따라 행동했습니다. 잘못된 판단이었을지는 몰라도 모진 일은 아니었습니다. 부인께서는 내일이면 딴사람이 되어 계실 겁니다, 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 제가 예상한 대로 경련이 왔지요. 제 우려를 헤일 양께 말씀드리지는 않았습니다만. 부인께서는 제가 가져온 아편을 드셨습니다. 오래 푹 주무실 겁니다. 그리고 내일이면 선생을 그토록 놀라게 한 그 얼굴빛도 가셔 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“하지만 병은 그대로겠지요?”\n\n도널드슨 의사가 마거릿을 흘긋 보았다. 숙인 고개와, 잠시나마 유예를 구하는 기색 없이 들어 올린 얼굴은, 사람의 마음을 재빨리 읽는 그에게 그녀가 온전한 진실을 말하는 편이 낫다고 여긴다는 것을 알려 주었다.\n\n“병은 그대로입니다. 우리의 보잘것없는 자랑거리인 온갖 의술로도 그 병에는 손을 댈 수 없습니다. 우리는 다만 그 진행을 늦추고 그것이 주는 고통을 덜어 드릴 수 있을 뿐입니다. 사내답게 견디십시오, 선생. 그리스도인답게요. 어떤 고통도, 어떤 죽을병도 건드리거나 침범할 수 없는 영혼의 불멸을 믿으십시오!”\n\n그러나 그가 얻은 대답이라고는 목이 멘 몇 마디, “선생은 결혼해 본 적이 없지 않소, 도널드슨 선생. 이게 어떤 것인지 모르시오”라는 말과, 밤의 정적을 무겁게 뛰는 고통의 맥박처럼 꿰뚫고 지나가는 깊고 사내다운 흐느낌뿐이었다.\n\n마거릿은 아버지 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 눈물 어린 손길로 그를 어루만졌다. 아무도, 도널드슨 의사조차도 시간이 어떻게 흘렀는지 알지 못했다. 지금 이 순간에 해야 할 일들을 감히 먼저 입에 올린 사람은 헤일 씨였다.\n\n“우리가 어떻게 해야 하오?” 그가 물었다. “우리 둘에게 다 말해 주시오. 마거릿은 내 지팡이이자 오른손이니.”\n\n도널드슨 의사는 분명하고 사리에 맞는 지시를 내렸다. 오늘 밤은 걱정할 것 없다고 했다. 아니, 내일도, 그 뒤로도 여러 날은 평온하리라고 했다. 그러나 회복에 대한 지속적인 희망은 없다고 했다. 그는 헤일 씨에게 잠자리에 들라고 권했고, 방해받지 않기를 바라는 그 잠을 지킬 사람은 하나만 남기라고 했다. 그는 아침 일찍 다시 오겠다고 약속했다. 그리고 따뜻하고 다정하게 손을 굳게 잡아 준 뒤 두 사람 곁을 떠났다.\n\n그들은 말을 거의 나누지 않았다. 공포에 너무 지친 나머지 당장 해야 할 일을 정하는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 헤일 씨는 밤을 새우겠다고 마음먹었고, 마거릿이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 응접실 소파에서라도 쉬시라고 설득하는 것뿐이었다. 딕슨은 잠자리에 들기를 완강하고 퉁명스럽게 거절했다. 마거릿으로 말하자면, 세상의 모든 의사가 “힘을 아껴 두라”느니 “지킬 사람은 하나면 된다”느니 말한들 어머니 곁을 떠난다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그리하여 딕슨은 앉아서 눈을 부릅뜨다가 껌뻑이다가 고개를 떨구다가 화들짝 다시 몸을 일으키기를 되풀이하더니, 마침내 싸움을 포기하고 아주 대놓고 코를 골았다. 마거릿은 드레스를 벗어 성마른 역겨움 같은 것과 함께 옆으로 던져 놓고 실내복을 걸쳤다. 그녀는 다시는 잠들 수 없을 것 같았다. 온 감각이 날카롭게 살아나, 지켜본다는 그 목적 하나를 위해 하나같이 갑절의 예민함을 얻은 것 같았다. 보이는 것 들리는 것 하나하나가, 아니 떠오르는 생각 하나하나까지도 어떤 신경을 아프도록 건드렸다. 2시간이 넘도록 그녀는 옆방에서 아버지가 뒤척이는 소리를 들었다. 그는 끊임없이 어머니 방문 앞으로 와서 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였고, 그러면 가까이 다가온 그 보이지 않는 기척을 미처 듣지 못한 그녀가 문을 열고 나가, 바싹 마른 입술로 겨우 만들어 내는 그의 물음에 지금 어떤지를 알려 주곤 했다. 마침내 그도 잠이 들었고 온 집 안이 고요해졌다. 마거릿은 휘장 뒤에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다. 지난 나날의 온갖 관심사가 시간으로도 아득히 멀고 공간으로도 아득히 멀게 느껴졌다. 불과 36시간 전만 해도 그녀는 베시 히긴스와 그 아버지를 마음에 두었고 바우처를 생각하며 가슴이 미어졌다. 그런데 지금은 그 모든 것이 전생의 어떤 꿈결 같은 기억처럼 여겨졌다. 문밖에서 지나간 일은 무엇이든 어머니와 동떨어진 것이었고, 그래서 실감이 나지 않았다. 오히려 할리 가가 더 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 거기서 쇼 이모의 얼굴에 어린 어머니의 이목구비를 더듬어 내며 혼자 흐뭇해하던 일이, 편지가 와서 사랑의 온갖 그리움으로 집 생각에 잠기게 하던 일이 어제 일처럼 기억났다. 헬스톤 자체는 어스름한 옛일이 되어 있었다. 아무 일도 없이 단조롭기만 하던 지난겨울과 봄의 흐리고 잿빛인 나날이, 이제 그녀가 무엇보다 값지게 여기는 것과 더 가깝게 이어져 있는 듯했다. 그녀는 저 떠나가는 시간의 옷자락이라도 붙들고, 돌아와 달라고, 아직 제 손안에 있을 때 너무도 하찮게 여겼던 것을 돌려 달라고 빌고 싶었다. 삶이란 얼마나 헛된 구경거리인가! 얼마나 실체 없고 깜박이고 스쳐 지나가는 것인가! 마치 땅의 소란과 삐걱임보다 훨씬 높은 어느 하늘의 종탑에서 종이 끊임없이 울리는 것 같았다. “모두가 그림자다! 모두가 지나간다! 모든 것이 지나갔다!” 그리고 아침이 밝아 올 때, 그 전의 숱한 더 행복했던 아침처럼 서늘하고 잿빛으로 밝아 올 때, 마거릿이 잠든 이들을 하나하나 바라보았을 때, 그 끔찍한 밤도 꿈처럼 실체 없는 것으로 느껴졌다. 그것 또한 그림자였다. 그것 또한 지나갔다.\n\n헤일 부인 자신은 잠에서 깨었을 때 지난밤 자기가 얼마나 위중했는지 알지 못했다. 도널드슨 의사가 이른 아침에 찾아온 것을 좀 의아해했고, 남편과 딸의 근심 어린 얼굴에 어리둥절해했다. 그녀는 확실히 피곤하다며 그날은 자리에 누워 있는 데 동의했다. 그러나 이튿날에는 기어이 일어나겠다고 우겼고, 도널드슨 의사도 응접실로 나오는 것을 허락했다. 그녀는 어떤 자세로도 안절부절못하고 불편해했으며, 밤이 되기 전에 열이 몹시 올랐다. 헤일 씨는 완전히 넋이 나가 아무것도 결정하지 못했다.\n\n“어머니가 그런 밤을 또 겪지 않으시게 하려면 우리가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?” 사흘째 되는 날 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“어느 정도는 제가 어쩔 수 없이 써야 했던 강한 아편의 반작용입니다. 부인께서 견디시는 것보다 지켜보시는 쪽이 더 괴로우실 겁니다, 제 생각에는요. 다만 물침대를 구할 수 있다면 좋을 듯싶습니다. 물론 내일이면 나아지실 겁니다. 이번 발작 전과 거의 같은 모습으로요. 그래도 물침대를 쓰시게 해 드리고 싶군요. 손턴 부인 댁에 하나 있는 걸로 압니다. 오늘 오후에 들러 보지요. 잠깐만요.” 그가 병실을 지키느라 핏기가 가신 마거릿의 얼굴에 눈길이 닿자 말했다. “제가 갈 수 있을지 모르겠군요. 돌아야 할 길이 멉니다. 말버러 가까지 씩씩하게 걸어가서 손턴 부인께 빌려 주실 수 있는지 여쭤 보는 것도 헤일 양께 나쁠 것 없겠습니다.”\n\n“물론이지요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “오늘 오후에 어머니가 주무시는 동안 다녀올 수 있어요. 손턴 부인이라면 분명 빌려 주실 거예요.”\n\n도널드슨 의사의 경험은 옳았다. 헤일 부인은 발작의 여파를 털어 낸 듯했고, 그날 오후에는 마거릿이 다시 보리라고 감히 바랐던 것보다 훨씬 밝고 좋아 보였다. 딸은 만찬을 마치고 어머니를 두고 나섰다. 어머니는 안락의자에 앉아 남편의 손에 제 손을 얹고 있었는데, 남편 쪽이 아내보다 훨씬 더 여위고 괴로워 보였다. 그래도 그는 이제 미소 지을 수 있었다. 느리게, 희미하게이긴 했지만 말이다. 하루 이틀 전만 해도 마거릿은 아버지가 다시 웃는 모습을 보리라고는 생각조차 못 했다.\n\n크램프턴 크레센트의 집에서 말버러 가까지는 2마일쯤 되었다. 너무 더워서 빨리 걸을 수가 없었다. 8월의 해가 오후 3시의 거리로 곧장 내리쬐었다. 마거릿은 길의 첫 1.5마일 동안 여느 때와 크게 다른 것을 알아차리지 못한 채 걸어갔다. 그녀는 제 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 이제는 밀턴 거리를 흘러가는 그 고르지 않은 사람의 물결 사이로 길을 헤쳐 나가는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그러나 이윽고 그녀는 접어들고 있던 붐비는 길 위에서 사람들 무리가 여느 때와 다르게 술렁이는 것에 마음이 걸렸다. 그들은 앞으로 나아간다기보다 저마다 서 있던 자리에서 별로 움직이지도 않은 채 말하고 듣고 흥분해서 웅성거리는 듯했다. 그래도 사람들이 그녀에게 길을 내주었고, 심부름의 목적과 그것을 부른 절박한 사정에 마음을 온통 빼앗긴 터라 그녀는 마음이 편했을 때만큼 재빠르게 살피지 못했다. 사람들 사이에 불안하고 짓눌린 짜증의 기운이 감돌고 있다는 것, 자기 둘레에 물리적으로나 정신적으로나 천둥이 몰려오는 듯한 대기가 서려 있다는 것을 온전히 깨닫기 전에 그녀는 이미 말버러 가에 들어서 있었다. 말버러 가로 트인 좁은 골목마다 성난 목소리가 수없이 뒤엉킨 듯한 낮고 먼 울림이 올라왔다. 초라하고 누추한 집마다 사람들이 문간과 창가에 모여 있었고, 아예 좁은 길 한복판에 나와 서 있는 이들도 있었다. 모두가 한 지점을 향해 골똘한 눈길을 던지고 있었다. 말버러 가야말로 갖가지 격렬한 관심을 드러내는 그 모든 사람의 눈이 모이는 초점이었다. 어떤 눈은 분노로 사나웠고, 어떤 눈은 가차 없는 위협으로 험악했다. 어떤 눈은 두려움으로 커져 있었고, 어떤 눈은 애원하듯 빌고 있었다. 그리고 마거릿이 말버러 공장 마당의 크고 밋밋한 담에 난 접이문 옆 작은 쪽문에 이르러 종을 울리고 문지기의 응답을 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 둘레를 둘러보았고 폭풍의 첫 길고 아득한 우렛소리를 들었다. 어두운 군중의 첫 물결이 위협하는 물마루를 세운 채 느릿느릿 밀려와 무너지고 물러가는 것을, 조금 전만 해도 눌린 소음으로 가득했으나 이제는 불길하리만치 고요해진 길 저편 끝에서 보았다. 이 모든 정황이 마거릿의 눈에 억지로 밀고 들어왔으나, 다른 데 사로잡힌 그녀의 마음속까지 가라앉지는 못했다. 그녀는 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그 깊은 의미가 무엇인지 알지 못했다. 그러면서도 곧 자기를 어머니 없는 아이로 만들어 자기를 꿰뚫고 또 꿰뚫을 그 칼의 날카롭고 매서운 압박은 알고 있었고 느끼고 있었다. 그녀는 그것을 실감하려 애쓰고 있었다. 그것이 닥쳤을 때 아버지를 위로할 준비가 되어 있으려고.\n\n문지기가 그녀를 들이기에도 턱없이 좁게, 조심스레 문을 열었다.\n\n“아씨셨구먼요, 그렇지요?” 그는 길게 숨을 들이쉬며 입구를 조금 넓혔으나 여전히 활짝 열지는 않은 채 말했다. 마거릿이 들어갔다. 그는 서둘러 뒤에서 빗장을 질렀다.\n\n“사람들이 죄다 이리로 몰려오고 있는 게지요, 아마?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“모르겠어요. 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어지는 것 같긴 했어요. 그렇지만 이 거리는 아주 텅 비어 있는데요.”\n\n그녀는 마당을 가로질러 집 문으로 오르는 계단을 올라갔다. 가까이에서는 아무 소리도 나지 않았다. 쿵쿵대고 헐떡이며 돌아가는 증기기관도, 기계가 딸깍이는 소리도, 날카로운 목소리들이 뒤섞여 부딪는 소리도 없었다. 다만 저 멀리서 불길하게 모여드는 함성이 깊게 아우성치고 있었다.\n\n# 제22장\n\n“그러나 일감은 귀해지고 빵값은 비싸졌으며, 품삯 또한 줄어들었다. 아일랜드 무리가 여기 와서 값을 불렀으니, 우리가 반값에 하던 일을 하겠다면서.” — 곡물법 시가.\n\n마거릿은 응접실로 안내되었다. 방은 다시 덮개와 커버를 씌운 평소 모습으로 돌아가 있었다. 더위 때문에 창문은 반쯤 열려 있었고 베네치아식 블라인드가 유리창을 가리고 있어서, 아래쪽 포장도로에서 반사된 잿빛의 스산한 빛이 그림자란 그림자를 죄다 엉뚱한 자리에 드리웠고, 초록빛이 감도는 위쪽 빛과 어우러져 거울에 언뜻 비친 마거릿 자신의 얼굴마저 섬뜩하고 파리해 보이게 했다. 그녀는 앉아서 기다렸다. 아무도 오지 않았다. 이따금 바람이 멀리서 웅성대는 무수한 소리를 더 가까이 실어 오는 듯했다. 그런데 바람은 불지 않았다! 그 소리는 사이사이 잦아들어 깊은 정적이 되었다.\n\n마침내 패니가 들어왔다.\n\n“어머니가 곧 오실 거예요, 헤일 양. 사정이 이러하니 저더러 대신 사과를 드리라고 하셨어요. 아실지 모르겠지만 오빠가 아일랜드에서 직공들을 데려왔는데, 그것 때문에 밀턴 사람들이 아주 길길이 뛰고 있어요. 마치 오빠한테는 일손을 구할 수 있는 데서 구할 권리조차 없다는 듯이 말이에요. 여기 그 멍청한 것들은 오빠 밑에서 일하려 들지도 않았으면서요. 그러더니 이제는 그 딱한 아일랜드 굶주린 사람들을 어찌나 위협해서 겁을 주었는지, 우리가 그 사람들을 밖으로 내보내지도 못해요. 공장 맨 위층 방에 다들 몰려 있는 게 보이실 거예요. 자기들은 일도 안 하면서 남이 일하는 것도 못 하게 하는 저 짐승 같은 것들한테서 지키려고 거기서 재울 참이에요. 어머니는 그 사람들 먹을 것을 챙기고 계시고, 오빠는 그 사람들한테 이야기를 하고 있어요. 여자들 몇이 돌아가겠다고 울고 있거든요. 아! 어머니 오셨네요!”\n\n손턴 부인은 시커멓게 굳은 얼굴로 들어왔고, 그 표정을 본 마거릿은 부탁을 꺼내기에는 좋지 않은 때에 왔구나 싶었다. 그러나 그것은 어머니의 병이 진행되는 동안 필요한 것이 있으면 무엇이든 청하라던 손턴 부인 자신의 뜻을 따른 것일 뿐이었다. 마거릿이 어머니가 잠을 이루지 못한다는 것과, 물침대를 써서 편하게 해 주었으면 한다는 도널드슨 의사의 뜻을 부드럽고 조심스럽게 이야기하는 동안 손턴 부인의 이마는 찌푸려졌고 입은 꾹 다물렸다. 마거릿이 말을 마쳤다. 손턴 부인은 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러더니 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다.\n\n“저것들이 문 앞에 왔다! 존을 불러라, 패니. 공장에서 불러들여! 문 앞에 왔다니까! 문을 부수고 들어올 게다! 존을 부르래도!”\n\n그와 동시에, 마거릿의 말을 귀담아듣는 대신 부인이 내내 듣고 있던 그 몰려드는 발소리가 바로 담장 밖에서 들려왔고, 성난 목소리들의 소란이 나무 문 뒤에서 점점 커졌다. 문은 보이지 않는 성난 무리가 제 몸뚱이를 공성 망치 삼아 부딪쳤다가 잠시 물러섰다가 더 한마음으로 꾸준히 힘을 모아 다시 달려드는 듯 흔들렸고, 그 거센 타격에 튼튼한 문이 바람 앞의 갈대처럼 떨었다.\n\n여인들은 창가로 모여들어, 자기들을 겁에 질리게 하는 그 광경에 홀린 듯 눈을 떼지 못했다. 손턴 부인, 하녀들, 마거릿, 모두가 거기 있었다. 패니는 걸음마다 쫓기기라도 하듯 비명을 지르며 위층으로 돌아와 발작하듯 흐느끼며 소파에 몸을 던졌다. 손턴 부인은 아직 공장에 있는 아들을 지켜보았다. 그가 밖으로 나와 창가에 모인 이들을, 그 창백한 얼굴 무리를 올려다보더니 기운을 내라는 듯 웃어 보이고는 공장 문을 잠갔다. 그러고는 여자들 가운데 하나를 불러 내려와서 제집 문을 열라고 했다. 패니가 정신없이 달아나며 뒤로 걸어 잠갔던 것이다. 손턴 부인이 몸소 내려갔다. 그런데 익히 알려진 그의 위엄 있는 목소리가 밖에 있는 광란한 무리에게는 피 맛이라도 본 것처럼 작용한 모양이었다. 그때까지 그들은 소리도 말도 없이, 문을 부수느라 안간힘을 쓰는 데에 숨을 다 쏟고 있었다. 그러나 이제 그가 안에서 말하는 소리를 듣자 어찌나 사납고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 신음을 내질렀는지, 손턴 부인마저 아들을 앞세우고 방으로 들어올 때에는 두려움으로 하얗게 질려 있었다. 그는 얼굴이 조금 붉어진 채 들어왔으나 눈은 위험이라는 나팔 소리에 응답하듯 번득였고, 얼굴에는 당당한 반항의 빛이 서려서, 잘생긴 사내는 못 될지언정 기품 있는 사내로 보이게 했다. 마거릿은 언제나 위급한 일이 닥칠 때 자신의 용기가 무너져, 스스로 두려워하던 그것, 곧 겁쟁이임이 드러날까 봐 겁내 왔다. 그러나 지금, 두려워하는 것이 마땅하고 공포가 코앞에 닥친 이 진짜 큰일 앞에서 그녀는 자신을 잊었고, 오직 이 순간의 일들에 대한 강렬한, 아플 만큼 강렬한 공감만을 느꼈다.\n\n손턴 씨가 거리낌 없이 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n“헤일 양, 하필 이런 불행한 때에 저희를 찾아오시게 되어 죄송합니다. 저희가 감당해야 할 위험에 함께 휘말리시게 될까 걱정입니다. 어머니! 뒤쪽 방으로 가시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까? 저들이 피너 골목에서 마구간 마당으로 들어왔는지는 알 수 없습니다만, 아직 아니라면 여기보다 그쪽이 안전할 겁니다. 가게, 제인!” 그는 우두머리 하녀에게 이렇게 말했다. 그녀가 나가자 다른 이들도 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n“나는 여기 있겠다!” 어머니가 말했다. “네가 있는 곳에 나도 있는다.” 사실 뒤쪽 방으로 물러나 봐야 소용이 없었다. 무리는 뒤편의 별채까지 에워싸고 뒤에서도 무시무시한 위협의 함성을 내지르고 있었다. 하녀들은 울며 비명을 지르며 다락으로 달아났다. 손턴 씨는 그 소리를 들으며 경멸하듯 웃었다. 그는 공장과 가장 가까운 창가에 홀로 서 있는 마거릿을 흘긋 보았다. 그녀의 눈은 반짝였고 뺨과 입술의 빛은 짙어져 있었다. 그의 시선을 느끼기라도 한 듯 그녀는 그를 돌아보며 한동안 마음에 담아 두었던 것을 물었다.\n\n“그 딱한 외지 직공들은 어디 있나요? 저 공장 안에요?”\n\n“그렇습니다! 뒤쪽 계단 위의 작은 방에 잔뜩 웅크린 채로 두고 왔습니다. 공장 문을 부수는 소리가 들리면 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그리로 달아나라고 일러 두었지요. 그렇지만 저들이 노리는 건 그 사람들이 아니라 저입니다.”\n\n“군대는 언제쯤 올 수 있느냐?” 어머니가 낮지만 떨리지 않는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n그는 무슨 일이든 그렇게 하듯 한결같이 침착하게 시계를 꺼냈다. 잠시 셈을 해 보았다.\n\n“윌리엄스가 제가 이르는 대로 곧장 떠났고, 저들 사이를 요리조리 피해 다닐 필요가 없었다면 아직 20분은 더 걸릴 겁니다.”\n\n“20분이라니!” 어머니가 말했다. 처음으로 목소리에 두려움이 배어 나왔다.\n\n“어머니, 지금 당장 창문을 닫으세요.” 그가 외쳤다. “문은 저런 충격을 한 번 더 못 견딥니다. 헤일 양, 그 창문을 닫으십시오.”\n\n마거릿은 자기 쪽 창문을 닫고, 이어 떨리는 손가락으로 애를 먹는 손턴 부인을 도우러 갔다.\n\n무슨 까닭에서인지, 보이지 않는 거리에서는 몇 분 동안 소리가 멎었다. 손턴 부인은 갑작스러운 정적의 뜻을 아들에게서 읽어 내기라도 하려는 듯 미친 듯한 불안으로 그의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 그의 얼굴은 경멸 어린 반항의 딱딱한 선으로 굳어 있었다. 거기에서는 희망도 두려움도 읽을 수 없었다.\n\n패니가 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n“다들 갔나요?” 그녀가 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n“갔다고!” 그가 대답했다. “들어 봐라!”\n\n그녀는 귀를 기울였다. 모두가 들을 수 있었다. 크게 힘을 모으는 한 번의 숨소리, 나무가 천천히 무너지며 내는 삐걱거림, 쇠붙이가 비틀려 뜯기는 소리, 그리고 육중한 문이 우렁차게 쓰러지는 소리를. 패니는 비틀거리며 일어서서 어머니 쪽으로 한두 걸음 내딛다가 앞으로 고꾸라져 어머니 품에서 정신을 잃었다. 손턴 부인은 몸의 힘이라기보다 의지의 힘으로 딸을 안아 올려 데리고 나갔다.\n\n“다행이군!” 손턴 씨가 어머니가 나가는 것을 지켜보며 말했다. “헤일 양, 위층으로 올라가시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?”\n\n마거릿의 입술이 “아니요!”라는 모양을 지었으나 그는 그 말을 들을 수 없었다. 바로 집 담장 아래에서 수없이 많은 발이 짓밟는 소리와, 몇 분 전 헛물켜던 외침보다 더 무시무시한, 사나운 만족의 웅얼거림이 섞인 낮고 깊은 성난 목소리들의 으르렁거림이 들려왔기 때문이다.\n\n“염려 마십시오!” 그가 그녀를 북돋우려는 마음에 말했다. “이 소동에 휘말리시게 해서 정말 죄송합니다. 그러나 이제 오래가지 않을 겁니다. 몇 분만 더 있으면 군대가 도착합니다.”\n\n“아, 하느님!” 마거릿이 별안간 외쳤다. “저기 바우처가 있어요. 분노로 시퍼렇게 질려 있지만 저는 저 얼굴을 알아요. 앞으로 나가려고 몸싸움을 하고 있어요. 보세요! 보세요!”\n\n“바우처가 누구입니까?” 손턴 씨가 차갑게 물으며, 마거릿이 그토록 마음을 쓰는 사내를 찾아보려고 창가로 바싹 다가섰다. 손턴 씨를 보자마자 그들은 괴성을 질렀는데, 사람의 소리가 아니라고 하는 것으로는 어림도 없었다. 그것은 굶주린 아가리에서 먹이를 빼앗긴 어떤 무서운 야수의 악마 같은 갈망이었다. 그마저도 자기가 불러일으킨 증오의 격렬함에 잠시 흠칫 물러섰다.\n\n“소리를 지르라지!” 그가 말했다. “5분만 더 있으면. 다만 저 딱한 아일랜드 사람들이 저 악귀 같은 소리에 넋이 나가지나 않았으면 좋겠군요. 헤일 양, 5분만 용기를 내십시오.”\n\n“제 걱정은 마세요.” 그녀가 다급히 말했다. “그런데 5분 뒤에 어떻게 되는 건가요? 저 딱한 사람들을 달랠 방법이 정말 없나요? 보고 있기가 끔찍해요.”\n\n“곧 군대가 올 겁니다. 그러면 저들도 정신을 차리겠지요.”\n\n“정신을 차린다고요!” 마거릿이 재빨리 말했다. “어떤 식으로 정신을 차린다는 말인가요?”\n\n“스스로 들짐승이 된 자들에게 통하는 유일한 방식이지요. 맙소사! 공장 문 쪽으로 몰려갑니다!”\n\n“손턴 씨.” 마거릿이 격정으로 온몸을 떨며 말했다. “겁쟁이가 아니라면 지금 당장 내려가세요. 내려가서 사내답게 저 사람들과 마주 서세요. 당신이 꾀어 온 저 딱한 외지 사람들을 구하세요. 당신의 직공들에게 사람을 대하듯 말을 거세요. 다정하게 말을 거세요. 군대가 들어와 미쳐 버린 딱한 사람들을 베어 넘기게 하지 마세요. 저기 정말로 미쳐 있는 사람이 하나 보여요. 당신 안에 조금이라도 용기나 고결함이 있다면, 나가서 사람 대 사람으로 이야기하세요!”\n\n그는 그녀가 말하는 동안 몸을 돌려 그녀를 바라보았다. 듣는 사이 그의 얼굴에는 검은 구름이 드리웠다. 그는 그녀의 말을 들으며 이를 악물었다.\n\n“가겠습니다. 아래층까지 저와 함께 내려가셔서 제 뒤로 문을 잠가 주시기를 청해야 할지도 모르겠군요. 어머니와 누이에게는 그 정도 보호는 있어야 하니까요.”\n\n“아! 손턴 씨! 저는 모르겠어요. 제가 틀렸을지도 몰라요. 다만……”\n\n그러나 그는 이미 가고 없었다. 아래층 현관에 있었고, 앞문의 빗장을 풀었다. 그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 서둘러 뒤따라가 그의 뒤로 문을 걸어 잠그고, 메스꺼운 가슴과 어지러운 머리로 다시 계단을 기어오르는 것뿐이었다. 그녀는 다시 가장 먼 창가의 제자리로 갔다. 그가 아래 계단에 서 있었다. 성난 눈 수천 개가 향한 방향으로 그것을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 굽이치는 성난 웅얼거림의 야만스러운 만족 말고는 아무것도 보이지도 들리지도 않았다. 그녀는 창문을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 무리 가운데 많은 수는 어린 사내아이들이었다. 잔인하고 생각이 없었으며, 생각이 없기에 잔인했다. 더러는 이리처럼 여윈, 먹잇감에 미친 어른들이었다. 그녀는 사정을 알고 있었다. 그들은 바우처와 같았다. 집에는 굶주린 아이들이 있고, 품삯을 올리려는 노력이 끝내 성공하리라 믿었으며, 제 아이들의 빵을 빼앗을 아일랜드 사람들이 들어온다는 것을 알고는 이루 말할 수 없이 격분한 것이다. 마거릿은 그 모든 것을 알았다. 그녀는 그것을 바우처의 얼굴에서, 처량하리만치 절박하고 분노로 시퍼렇게 질린 그 얼굴에서 읽었다. 손턴 씨가 저들에게 한마디만 해 준다면, 그의 목소리만이라도 듣게 해 준다면, 노여움이나 꾸짖음의 말조차 한마디 내주지 않는 저 돌 같은 침묵을 향해 미친 듯 두드리고 날뛰는 것보다는 나을 성싶었다. 그러나 어쩌면 그가 지금 말하고 있는지도 몰랐다. 짐승 떼의 소리처럼 알아들을 수 없던 그 소음이 잠시 잦아들었기 때문이다. 그녀는 보닛을 벗어 던지고 몸을 앞으로 내밀어 들으려 했다. 그러나 보이기만 할 뿐이었다. 손턴 씨가 정말로 말을 해 보려 했다 하더라도, 그의 말을 들으려던 순간의 본능은 이미 지나가 버렸고 사람들은 어느 때보다 사납게 날뛰고 있었으니까. 그는 팔짱을 낀 채 조각상처럼 미동도 없이 서 있었고, 억눌린 흥분으로 얼굴이 창백했다. 그들은 그를 위협해서 움츠러들게 만들려 했다. 저마다 서로를 부추겨 당장 손찌검이라도 하게 하려 들었다. 마거릿은 직감으로 알았다. 한순간이면 모든 것이 아수라장이 되리라는 것을, 첫 손길이 폭발을 일으키리라는 것을, 그렇게 격분한 사내 수백 명과 앞뒤 가리지 않는 아이들 틈에서는 손턴 씨의 목숨조차 위태로우리라는 것을, 다음 순간이면 폭풍 같은 격정이 한계를 넘어 이성이나 뒷일에 대한 두려움이라는 둑을 모조리 쓸어 가리라는 것을. 그렇게 바라보는 사이에도 그녀는 뒤쪽에서 사내아이들이 몸을 굽혀 무거운 나막신을 벗는 것을 보았다. 손에 잡히는 가장 만만한 무기였다. 그녀는 그것이 화약에 붙은 불꽃임을 알아차렸고, 아무도 듣지 못한 외마디 소리와 함께 방을 뛰쳐나가 계단을 내려갔다. 그녀는 위압적인 힘으로 문의 커다란 쇠 빗장을 들어 올렸고, 문을 활짝 열어젖혔고, 성난 사내들의 바다와 마주 서서 꾸짖음의 불화살 같은 눈길로 그들을 쳤다. 나막신은 그것을 쥔 손안에 멈추었고, 조금 전까지 그토록 험악하던 얼굴들이 이제는 머뭇거리며 이게 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 듯했다. 그녀가 그들과 그들의 적 사이에 서 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 말을 할 수 없었으나, 숨을 고를 수 있을 때까지 그들을 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있었다.\n\n“아, 폭력을 쓰지 마세요! 저분은 한 사람이고 여러분은 여럿이잖아요.” 그러나 그 말은 사그라졌다. 목소리에 힘이 실리지 않아 쉰 속삭임에 지나지 않았던 것이다. 손턴 씨는 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서 있었다. 그는 자기와 위험 사이에 무엇이 끼어드는 것이 못마땅하다는 듯 그녀 뒤에서 물러나 있었다.\n\n“가세요!” 그녀가 다시 한번 말했다. 이제 그 목소리는 부르짖음에 가까웠다. “군대를 부르러 보냈어요. 오고 있어요. 순순히 가세요. 돌아가세요. 여러분의 하소연이 무엇이든 풀릴 겁니다.”\n\n“그 아일랜드 놈들을 도로 짐짝처럼 싸서 돌려보낼 테요?” 무리 가운데서 하나가 목소리에 사나운 위협을 담아 물었다.\n\n“결코 못 한다, 너희가 시킨다고 해서는!” 손턴 씨가 외쳤다. 그러자 곧바로 폭풍이 터졌다. 야유가 솟구쳐 허공을 채웠으나 마거릿에게는 들리지 않았다. 그녀의 눈은 아까부터 나막신으로 무장한 사내아이들 무리에 가 있었다. 그녀는 그들의 몸짓을 보았고, 그 뜻을 알았고, 그들의 겨냥을 읽었다. 한순간만 지나면 손턴 씨가 얻어맞고 쓰러질지도 몰랐다. 그녀 자신이 몰아세우고 부추겨 이 위험한 자리로 나오게 한 사람이었다. 그녀는 오직 어떻게 그를 구할까만 생각했다. 그녀는 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안았다. 저 너머의 사나운 사람들로부터 그를 막는 방패로 제 몸을 삼았다. 그러나 그는 여전히 팔짱을 낀 채 그녀를 뿌리쳤다.\n\n“비키십시오.” 그가 깊은 목소리로 말했다. “여기는 당신이 있을 자리가 아닙니다.”\n\n“여기가 제 자리예요.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신은 제가 본 것을 보지 못했어요.” 여자라는 것이 방패가 되어 주리라고 그녀가 생각했다면, 움츠러든 눈으로 저 사내들의 무서운 분노에서 고개를 돌린 채 다시 눈을 들었을 때에는 그들이 멈춰 서서 돌이켜 보고 슬그머니 물러나 사라져 있으리라 조금이라도 바랐다면, 그것은 잘못이었다. 그들의 무모한 격정은 이미 멈출 수 없을 만큼 멀리 그들을 데려갔다. 적어도 그중 몇몇은 그러했다. 잔인한 흥분을 좋아하는 사나운 아이들이야말로 어떤 유혈로 이어지든 아랑곳없이 언제나 폭동의 앞장을 서는 법이니까. 나막신 하나가 허공을 가르며 날았다. 마거릿의 눈은 홀린 듯 그것의 궤적을 좇았다. 그것은 빗나갔고, 그녀는 두려움에 속이 뒤집혔으나 자리를 옮기지는 않고 다만 손턴 씨의 팔에 얼굴을 묻었다. 그러고는 몸을 돌려 다시 말했다.\n\n“제발! 이런 폭력으로 여러분의 명분을 망치지 마세요. 여러분은 지금 자기가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 모르고 있어요.” 그녀는 말을 또렷하게 하려고 애썼다.\n\n날카로운 조약돌 하나가 그녀의 곁을 스치며 이마와 뺨을 긁었고, 눈앞에 눈부신 빛의 장막을 드리웠다. 그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 손턴 씨의 어깨에 늘어졌다. 그러자 그는 팔짱을 풀고 한쪽 팔로 그녀를 잠시 감싸 안았다.\n\n“잘들 하는 짓이다!” 그가 말했다. “죄 없는 타지 사람을 쫓아내겠다고 몰려오고, 수백 명이 한 사람에게 달려들고, 그러다 여자 하나가 너희 자신을 위해서 사리를 아는 인간이 되어 달라고 앞에 나서니, 그 비겁한 분풀이를 그 여자에게 퍼붓는구나! 잘들 하는 짓이다!” 그가 말하는 동안 그들은 잠잠했다. 그들은 눈과 입을 벌린 채, 격정의 몽롱함에서 그들을 깨워 놓은 검붉은 핏줄기를 바라보고 있었다. 문에 가까이 있던 자들은 부끄러워 슬그머니 빠져나갔다. 무리 전체에 움직임이 일었다. 물러나는 움직임이었다. 오직 한 목소리만이 외쳤다.\n\n“그 돌은 댁을 맞히려던 거였어. 그런데 댁은 계집 뒤에 숨었구먼!”\n\n손턴 씨는 분노로 부르르 떨었다. 피가 흐른 덕분에 마거릿은 정신이 들었다. 어렴풋하고 아득하게나마 정신이 들었다. 그는 그녀를 문간 계단에 조심스레 앉히고 머리를 문틀에 기대게 했다.\n\n“여기서 좀 쉬실 수 있겠습니까?” 그가 물었다. 그러나 대답을 기다리지도 않고 그는 천천히 계단을 내려가 무리 한가운데로 들어갔다. “자, 짐승 같은 마음이 그렇다면 나를 죽여라. 여기에는 나를 막아 줄 여자가 없다. 나를 때려죽일 수는 있어도, 내가 마음먹은 바에서 나를 물러나게 하지는 못한다. 너희 따위는!” 그는 계단 위에서 그랬던 것과 똑같은 자세로 팔짱을 낀 채 그들 가운데 서 있었다.\n\n그러나 문 쪽으로 물러나는 움직임은 이미 시작되어 있었다. 아마도 한꺼번에 타올랐던 분노만큼이나 까닭 없이, 눈먼 것처럼. 아니, 어쩌면 군대가 다가온다는 생각과, 눈을 감은 채 대리석처럼 고요하고 슬프게 위를 향한 그 창백한 얼굴 때문인지도 몰랐다. 길게 엉킨 속눈썹 사이로 눈물이 배어 나와 떨어졌고, 눈물보다 더 무겁고 느린 소리로 상처에서 피가 방울져 떨어지고 있었다. 가장 절박한 자들마저, 바우처 자신마저 뒷걸음질 치고 비틀거리며 물러나 인상을 찌푸리다가 끝내 공장주에게 저주를 퍼부으며 떠나갔다. 그는 조금도 변하지 않은 자세로 서서 반항하는 눈으로 그들이 물러나는 것을 지켜보았다. 그 후퇴가 (그 성질상 그렇게 되기 마련이었듯) 달아남으로 바뀌는 순간, 그는 계단 위 마거릿에게로 달려 올라갔다.\n\n그녀는 그의 도움 없이 일어나려 했다.\n\n“아무것도 아니에요.” 그녀가 힘없는 미소를 지으며 말했다. “살갗이 좀 벗겨졌을 뿐이고, 그 순간 잠깐 정신이 아득했던 거예요. 아, 저 사람들이 갔다니 정말 다행이에요!” 그러고는 참지 못하고 울었다.\n\n그는 그녀와 같은 마음이 될 수 없었다. 그의 노여움은 가라앉지 않았다. 눈앞의 위험이 지나가면서 오히려 치밀어 오르고 있었다. 멀리서 군인들의 쇠붙이 부딪는 소리가 들려왔다. 이 흩어져 버린 무리에게 권위와 질서의 힘을 느끼게 하기에는 딱 5분이 늦었다. 그는 그들이 군대를 보고, 자기들이 아슬아슬하게 화를 면했음을 깨닫고 기가 꺾이기를 바랐다. 이런 생각들이 머릿속을 스치는 사이, 마거릿은 몸을 가누려고 문설주를 붙잡았다. 그러나 눈앞에 막이 드리웠고, 그는 간신히 때맞춰 그녀를 받아 안았다. “어머니, 어머니!” 그가 외쳤다. “내려오세요. 저들은 갔고, 헤일 양이 다쳤습니다!” 그는 그녀를 안아 식당으로 옮겨 소파에 눕혔다. 부드럽게 눕히고 나서 그 새하얀 얼굴을 내려다보는데, 그녀가 자기에게 어떤 존재인지가 어찌나 사무치게 밀려오던지 그는 그 아픔을 소리 내어 말하고 말았다.\n\n“아, 나의 마거릿, 나의 마거릿! 당신이 나에게 어떤 사람인지는 아무도 모릅니다! 죽은 듯 차갑게 여기 누워 있는 당신은, 내가 사랑한 단 하나의 여인입니다! 아, 마거릿, 마거릿!”\n\n그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채 말이라기보다 신음에 가까운 소리로 그렇게 더듬거리다가, 어머니가 들어오자 그는 스스로가 부끄러워 벌떡 일어섰다. 어머니는 아무것도 보지 못했고, 다만 아들이 평소보다 조금 창백하고 조금 굳어 있다는 것만 보았다.\n\n“헤일 양이 다쳤습니다, 어머니. 돌에 관자놀이를 스쳤어요. 피를 꽤 많이 흘린 것 같습니다.”\n\n“몹시 심하게 다친 것 같구나. 거의 죽은 사람처럼 보이는구나.” 손턴 부인이 적잖이 놀라서 말했다.\n\n“기절한 것뿐입니다. 그 뒤로 저와 말도 했는걸요.” 그러나 그렇게 말하는 동안 몸속의 피가 모조리 심장으로 몰려드는 것 같았고, 그는 정말로 떨고 있었다.\n\n“가서 제인을 불러라. 내게 필요한 것들을 찾아다 줄 테니. 그리고 너는 아일랜드 사람들한테 가 보아라. 겁에 질려 미친 것처럼 울고 소리치고 있잖니.”\n\n그는 갔다. 그녀에게서 자기를 떼어 놓는 팔다리마다 추가 매달린 것처럼 무겁게 갔다. 그는 제인을 불렀고 누이를 불렀다. 그녀는 여자의 손으로 하는 온갖 보살핌과 다정한 시중을 받아야 마땅했다. 그러나 그녀가 내려와 가장 앞선 위험 속에 제 몸을 세우던 일을 떠올리자 온몸의 맥박이 뛰었다. 그것이 자기를 구하려는 것이었을까? 그때 그는 그녀를 밀쳐 내고 퉁명스럽게 말했다. 그녀가 공연히 자초한 위험밖에는 아무것도 보이지 않았던 것이다. 그는 그녀 생각에 온몸의 신경이 떨리는 채로 아일랜드 사람들에게 갔고, 그들의 두려움을 달래고 어루만져 줄 만큼 그들이 하는 말을 알아듣기가 어렵다는 것을 알았다. 그들은 여기 있지 않겠다고, 돌려보내 달라고 우겼다.\n\n그리하여 그는 생각하고, 말하고, 설득해야 했다.\n\n손턴 부인은 오드콜로뉴로 마거릿의 관자놀이를 적셨다. 그때까지 손턴 부인도 제인도 알아채지 못했던 상처에 알코올이 닿자 마거릿은 눈을 떴다. 그러나 자기가 어디 있는지도, 저들이 누구인지도 모르는 것이 분명했다. 눈가의 검은 그늘이 짙어지고 입술이 떨리며 오므라들더니, 그녀는 다시 정신을 잃었다.\n\n“심하게 얻어맞았어.” 손턴 부인이 말했다. “의사를 부르러 갈 사람이 없느냐?”\n\n“저는 아닙니다, 마님. 부디 봐주세요.” 제인이 뒷걸음질 치며 말했다. “그 폭도들이 아직 사방에 있을지도 몰라요. 그리고 상처가 보기만큼 깊지는 않은 것 같습니다요, 마님.”\n\n“나는 요행을 바라지 않겠다. 저 아가씨는 우리 집에서 다쳤어. 네가 겁쟁이라면 나는 아니다. 내가 가마.”\n\n“마님, 제발 순경을 하나 보내게 해 주세요. 잔뜩 올라와 있고 군인들도 있습니다요.”\n\n“그러면서도 나가기는 무섭다는 거냐! 그 사람들 시간을 우리 심부름에 쓰게 할 수는 없다. 폭도 몇이라도 잡으려면 그들도 할 일이 많을 테니. 이 집에 남아 있는 건 무섭지 않겠지.” 부인이 경멸하듯 물었다. “남아서 헤일 양 이마를 계속 적셔 주는 건 할 수 있겠지? 나는 10분도 안 걸려 돌아올 게다.”\n\n“해나가 가면 안 될까요, 마님?”\n\n“왜 해나냐? 왜 너만 빼고 다른 사람이냐? 안 된다, 제인. 네가 안 가면 내가 간다.”\n\n손턴 부인은 먼저 패니를 침대에 눕혀 두고 나왔던 방으로 갔다. 어머니가 들어서자 패니가 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n“어머, 어머니, 얼마나 놀랐는지 몰라요! 집 안에 웬 남자가 들어온 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리! 사내들은 다 갔다. 이제 와서야 할 일을 찾겠다고 군인들이 집 둘레에 온통 깔려 있고. 헤일 양이 식당 소파에 심하게 다쳐 누워 있다. 나는 의사를 부르러 간다.”\n\n“아! 가지 마세요, 어머니! 그자들이 어머니를 죽일 거예요.” 그녀가 어머니의 옷자락에 매달렸다. 손턴 부인은 사정없는 손길로 그것을 뿌리쳤다.\n\n“대신 갈 사람을 찾아 오너라. 그러나 저 아가씨를 피 흘려 죽게 둘 수는 없다.”\n\n“피라니! 아, 끔찍해라! 어쩌다 다쳤어요?”\n\n“모른다. 물어볼 겨를도 없었다. 패니, 내려가서 조금이라도 쓸모 있게 굴어 보아라. 제인이 곁에 있다. 보기보다는 덜한 것이기를 바랄 뿐이다. 제인은 집 밖으로 나가기를 마다했어, 겁 많은 것! 하인들한테서 더는 거절당하고 싶지 않으니 내가 직접 가는 게다.”\n\n“어머, 어쩌면 좋아!” 패니가 울면서, 상처와 유혈이 바로 이 집 안에 있다는 생각에 혼자 남아 있느니 차라리 내려가겠다며 채비를 했다.\n\n“어머나, 제인!” 그녀가 식당으로 슬그머니 들어서며 말했다. “어떻게 된 거야? 얼굴이 왜 이렇게 하얗지! 어쩌다 다친 거야? 응접실로 돌을 던져 넣기라도 했어?”\n\n마거릿은 정말로 창백하고 초췌해 보였다. 정신이 조금씩 돌아오고 있기는 했지만. 그러나 실신의 메스꺼운 몽롱함 때문에 그녀는 여전히 비참하리만치 기운이 없었다. 그녀는 주위에서 사람들이 움직이는 것도, 오드콜로뉴가 주는 시원함도 느끼고 있었고, 적시는 손길이 쉬지 말고 계속되기를 간절히 바랐다. 그러나 그들이 멈추고 이야기를 나눌 때에도 그녀는 눈을 뜨거나 더 적셔 달라고 말할 수가 없었다. 죽은 듯한 혼수에 빠진 사람이, 주위 사람들의 행동뿐 아니라 그런 행동을 하게 만든 생각까지 또렷이 알면서도, 자기 장례의 그 무서운 준비를 막으려 몸을 움직이거나 소리를 낼 수 없는 것과 똑같았다.\n\n제인은 적시던 손을 멈추고 손턴 아가씨의 물음에 대답했다.\n\n“아가씨, 응접실에 그대로 계셨거나 저희한테 올라오셨으면 아무 일도 없으셨을 겁니다요. 저희는 앞쪽 다락에 있어서 다칠 일 없이 다 내다보았거든요.”\n\n“그럼 이 사람은 어디 있었는데?” 패니가 마거릿의 창백한 얼굴에 차츰 익숙해지면서 조금씩 다가서며 물었다.\n\n“앞문 바로 앞에요. 주인님하고요!” 제인이 뜻을 담아 말했다.\n\n“존하고! 우리 오빠하고! 어쩌다 거기까지 갔대?”\n\n“글쎄요, 아가씨, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 일이 아니지요.” 제인이 고개를 살짝 치켜들며 대답했다. “세라가 그러는데……”\n\n“세라가 뭐?” 패니가 참지 못하고 궁금해하며 물었다.\n\n제인은 세라가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 했든 그것을 옮기기가 썩 내키지는 않는다는 듯 다시 적시는 일을 계속했다.\n\n“세라가 뭐라고?” 패니가 날카롭게 물었다. “그렇게 말을 반 토막씩 하지 마. 그러면 알아들을 수가 없잖아.”\n\n“그럼, 아가씨, 정 그러시다면 말씀드리죠. 세라는 오른쪽 창가에 있어서 보기에 제일 좋은 자리였거든요. 그런데 세라 말이, 바로 그때도 그렇게 말했습니다만, 헤일 양이 주인님 목에 두 팔을 두르고 사람들이 다 보는 앞에서 끌어안고 있는 걸 봤다지 뭡니까.”\n\n“난 안 믿어.” 패니가 말했다. “이 사람이 우리 오빠를 마음에 두고 있는 건 알아. 그건 누구 눈에나 보이니까. 오빠가 청혼만 해 준다면 눈이라도 내줄 사람이지. 하지만 오빠는 절대 그러지 않을 거야, 내 장담해. 그래도 목에 팔을 두를 만큼 그렇게 뻔뻔하고 들이대는 사람이라고는 믿지 않아.”\n\n“가엾은 아가씨! 정말 그러셨다면 값을 톡톡히 치르신 거지요. 제 생각에는 그 충격 때문에 머리로 피가 몰려서 다시는 회복하지 못하실 것 같습니다요. 지금 꼭 송장 같잖아요.”\n\n“아, 어머니가 얼른 오셨으면!” 패니가 두 손을 쥐어짜며 말했다. “나는 죽은 사람하고 한방에 있어 본 적이 없단 말이야.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 아가씨! 돌아가신 게 아니에요. 눈꺼풀이 떨리고, 여기 뺨으로 젖은 눈물이 흘러내리고 있잖습니까. 말을 걸어 보세요, 패니 아가씨!”\n\n“이제 좀 나아졌어요?” 패니가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n대답도 없었고 알아보는 기색도 없었다. 다만 얼굴의 나머지는 잿빛으로 창백한데도 입술에는 옅은 분홍빛이 돌아왔다.\n\n손턴 부인이 가장 가까이서 찾을 수 있었던 외과의를 데리고 서둘러 들어왔다.\n\n“좀 어떠냐? 나아졌니, 얘야?” 마거릿이 흐릿한 눈을 뜨고 꿈결처럼 부인을 바라보자 그렇게 물었다. “로 선생님이 널 보러 오셨다.”\n\n손턴 부인은 귀먹은 사람에게 하듯 크고 또렷하게 말했다. 마거릿은 일어나려 하면서 흐트러진 숱 많은 머리카락을 무의식중에 상처 위로 끌어당겼다.\n\n“이제 좀 나았어요.” 그녀가 아주 낮고 힘없는 목소리로 말했다. “잠깐 속이 좋지 않았을 뿐이에요.”\n\n그녀는 그가 손을 잡고 맥을 짚도록 내버려 두었다. 그가 이마의 상처를 살펴봐도 되겠느냐고 묻자 그녀의 얼굴에 잠시 화색이 돌았다. 그리고 그녀는 의사보다 오히려 제인의 눈길을 피하려는 듯 제인을 흘긋 올려다보았다.\n\n“대단치 않은 것 같아요. 이제 나았어요. 집에 가야 해요.”\n\n“반창고를 몇 조각 붙이고 좀 쉬시기 전에는 안 됩니다.”\n\n그녀는 더 말하지 않고 서둘러 앉아서 상처를 싸매도록 두었다.\n\n“이제 됐으면요.” 그녀가 말했다. “가야겠어요. 어머니는 못 보실 것 같은데요. 머리카락에 가려지지요?”\n\n“그럼요. 아무도 모를 겁니다.”\n\n“하지만 가면 안 돼요.” 손턴 부인이 못마땅하다는 듯 말했다. “갈 만한 몸이 아니에요.”\n\n“가야 해요.” 마거릿이 단호하게 말했다. “어머니를 생각해 주세요. 어머니가 이 일을 들으시기라도 하면…… 게다가 저는 가야 해요.” 그녀가 격하게 말했다. “여기 있을 수가 없어요. 마차를 좀 불러 주시겠어요?”\n\n“얼굴이 아주 상기되고 열이 있군요.” 로 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그건 이렇게 가고 싶은데 여기 있어야 해서 그런 거예요. 바깥바람이, 여기서 벗어나는 것이 무엇보다 제게 좋을 거예요.” 그녀가 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n“정말 저분 말씀대로인 것 같습니다.” 로 씨가 대답했다. “오는 길에 말씀하신 대로 어머니가 그토록 편찮으시다면, 이 소동을 전해 듣고도 기다리던 시간에 따님이 돌아오지 않는다면 아주 위중해질 수 있습니다. 상처는 깊지 않습니다. 댁의 하인들이 아직 나가기를 겁내한다면 제가 마차를 불러오지요.”\n\n“아, 고맙습니다!” 마거릿이 말했다. “그게 무엇보다 제게 좋을 거예요. 이 방의 공기 때문에 이렇게 견디기 힘든 거예요.”\n\n그녀는 소파에 등을 기대고 눈을 감았다. 패니가 어머니에게 눈짓해 방 밖으로 불러내더니 무슨 이야기를 했고, 그 말에 부인도 마거릿 못지않게 그녀가 어서 떠나기를 바라게 되었다. 패니의 말을 다 믿어서가 아니라, 작별 인사를 할 때 마거릿을 대하는 태도가 몹시 서먹해 보일 만큼은 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n로 씨가 마차를 타고 돌아왔다.\n\n“괜찮으시다면 댁까지 모셔다드리겠습니다, 헤일 양. 거리가 아직 그리 잠잠하지 않으니까요.”\n\n마거릿의 머릿속은 지금 이 순간을 충분히 헤아릴 만큼은 또렷해서, 아버지와 어머니를 놀라게 할까 봐 크램프턴 크레센트에 닿기 전에 로 씨와 마차를 둘 다 떼어 놓고 싶어 했다. 그 한 가지 목표 너머로는 눈을 돌리려 하지 않았다. 자기를 두고 오간 무례한 말이라는 그 흉한 꿈은 결코 잊을 수 없겠지만 좀 더 기운을 차릴 때까지 미뤄 둘 수는 있었다. 아, 그녀는 몹시도 약해져 있었으니까. 그리고 그녀의 마음은 또 한 번의 끔찍하고 메스꺼운 실신 속으로 아주 가라앉아 버리지 않도록 자신을 붙들어 줄 눈앞의 무언가를 찾고 있었다.\n\n# 제23장\n\n“그것을 보고 어머니는 마음속으로 몹시 근심하였으나, 그는 무엇을 헤아려야 할지 잘 알고 있었더라.” — 스펜서.\n\n마거릿이 떠난 지 5분도 되지 않아 손턴 씨가 들어왔다. 얼굴이 온통 달아올라 있었다.\n\n“더 일찍 올 수가 없었습니다. 서장이 자꾸—— 그분은 어디 계십니까?” 그는 식당을 둘러보고는, 흐트러진 가구를 조용히 제자리에 돌려놓으며 얼른 대답하지 않는 어머니를 거의 사납다시피 바라보았다. “헤일 양은 어디 계십니까?” 그가 다시 물었다.\n\n“집에 갔다.” 어머니가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n“집에 갔다니요!”\n\n“그래. 훨씬 나아졌더구나. 사실 그리 크게 다친 것 같지도 않다. 그저 사소한 일에도 정신을 놓는 사람이 있는 법이지.”\n\n“집에 갔다니 안타깝군요.” 그가 불안하게 서성이며 말했다. “그럴 만한 몸이 아니었을 텐데요.”\n\n“본인이 괜찮다고 했고, 로 씨도 괜찮다고 했다. 내가 직접 그이를 데리러 갔어.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 어머니.” 그는 걸음을 멈추고 고마움을 담아 손을 맞잡으려는 듯 손을 반쯤 내밀었다. 그러나 어머니는 그 몸짓을 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n“그 아일랜드 사람들은 어떻게 했느냐?”\n\n“드래건 여관으로 보냈습니다. 그 딱한 것들에게 제대로 된 끼니라도 먹이려고요. 그러고는 다행히 그래디 신부를 붙잡아서, 들어와 그들에게 말씀을 좀 해 달라고, 다 함께 떠나 버리지 않도록 말려 달라고 부탁해 두었습니다. 헤일 양은 어떻게 집에 갔습니까? 걸어갈 수는 없었을 텐데요.”\n\n“삯마차를 탔다. 삯을 치르는 것까지 하나도 빠짐없이 제대로 해 두었어. 다른 이야기를 하자꾸나. 그 애 때문에 겪은 소란은 그만하면 됐다.”\n\n“그분이 아니었으면 제가 어떻게 됐을지 모릅니다.”\n\n“너는 계집아이한테 감싸여야 할 만큼 그렇게 무력해졌느냐?” 손턴 부인이 경멸하듯 물었다.\n\n그는 얼굴을 붉혔다. “저를 노리고 날아온 매질을, 그것도 아주 작심하고 날린 매질을 제 몸으로 받아 낼 여자는 흔치 않습니다.”\n\n“사랑에 빠진 계집아이는 별짓을 다 하는 법이다.” 손턴 부인이 짧게 대꾸했다.\n\n“어머니!” 그는 한 걸음 앞으로 나서다가 멈춰 섰고, 격정에 겨워 가슴이 들썩였다.\n\n어머니는 그가 스스로를 가라앉히려고 쓰는 힘이 그토록 뚜렷이 드러나는 것에 조금 놀랐다. 자신이 불러일으킨 감정이 어떤 것인지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다. 분명한 것은 그 격렬함뿐이었다. 노여움일까? 그의 눈이 이글거렸고, 몸은 부풀어 오른 듯했으며, 숨결이 굵고 가빴다. 그것은 기쁨과 노여움과 자존심과 반가운 놀라움과 헐떡이는 의심이 뒤섞인 것이었으나, 그녀는 그것을 읽어 낼 수 없었다. 그래도 그것은 그녀를 불안하게 만들었다. 까닭을 온전히 헤아리지도 못하고 함께 느끼지도 못하는 강한 감정이 곁에 있을 때면 늘 그런 법이다. 그녀는 찬장으로 가서 서랍을 열고, 이런저런 일에 쓰려고 넣어 둔 마른걸레를 꺼냈다. 소파의 반들반들한 팔걸이에 오드콜로뉴 한 방울이 떨어져 있는 것을 보고 저도 모르게 그것을 닦아 내려 한 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 필요한 것보다 훨씬 오래 아들에게 등을 돌린 채 있었다. 그리고 입을 열었을 때 그 목소리는 여느 때와 달리 억지로 눌린 듯 들렸다.\n\n“폭도들에 대해서는 무슨 조치를 취했겠지? 더 이상 난동이 있으리라고 여기는 건 아니겠지? 경찰은 다들 어디 있었더냐? 필요할 때는 한 번도 곁에 없구나!”\n\n“그렇지 않습니다. 문이 무너질 때 서너 명이 한창 밀치고 두들기며 애쓰는 걸 봤고, 마당이 비어 갈 무렵에는 더 달려왔습니다. 제가 정신만 차렸더라면 그때 몇 놈은 넘길 수 있었을 겁니다. 하지만 어려울 건 없습니다. 얼굴을 알아볼 사람이야 얼마든지 있으니까요.”\n\n“그렇지만 오늘 밤에 다시 오지는 않겠느냐?”\n\n“공장을 지킬 경비를 넉넉히 세우려고 알아보러 갈 참입니다. 30분 뒤에 경찰서에서 핸버리 대위를 만나기로 해 두었습니다.”\n\n“차부터 좀 마셔야지.”\n\n“차라니요! 그렇군요, 그래야겠습니다. 6시 반이고, 한동안 나가 있게 될지도 모르니까요. 저를 기다리느라 앉아 계시지 마세요, 어머니.”\n\n“네가 무사히 돌아온 걸 보기도 전에 내가 자리에 들 성싶으냐?”\n\n“글쎄요, 아마 아니겠지요.” 그는 잠시 망설였다. “하지만 시간이 되면, 경찰과 이야기를 끝내고 햄퍼와 클라크슨을 만난 뒤에 크램프턴에 들러 볼까 합니다.” 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 그들은 한참 동안 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러고 나서 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“크램프턴에는 왜 들르려는 게냐?”\n\n“헤일 양의 안부를 물으려고요.”\n\n“내가 사람을 보내마. 윌리엄스가 그 애가 얻으러 온 물침대를 가져가야 하니, 그 편에 안부를 묻게 하마.”\n\n“제가 직접 가야겠습니다.”\n\n“헤일 양이 어떤지 묻기만 하려는 건 아니겠지?”\n\n“네, 그것만은 아닙니다. 그분이 저와 폭도들 사이를 막아선 데 대해 고맙다는 말을 하고 싶습니다.”\n\n“애초에 왜 내려갔던 게냐? 사자 아가리에 제 머리를 들이민 꼴이 아니냐!”\n\n그는 어머니를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다. 응접실에서 자신과 마거릿 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 어머니가 모른다는 것을 알아채고는, 다른 물음으로 대답을 대신했다.\n\n“제가 경찰을 좀 데려올 때까지 저 없이 남아 계시는 게 두려우십니까? 아니면 지금 윌리엄스를 보내는 편이 낫겠습니까? 그러면 우리가 차를 다 마실 즈음에는 와 있을 텐데요. 지체할 시간이 없습니다. 저는 15분 안에 나가야 합니다.”\n\n손턴 부인이 방을 나갔다. 하인들은 여느 때 같으면 그토록 딱 부러지고 단호하던 그녀의 지시가 이번에는 어수선하고 갈피를 못 잡는 것을 이상하게 여겼다. 손턴 씨는 식당에 남아 경찰서에서 처리해야 할 일을 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 실은 마거릿을 생각하고 있었다. 자기 목을 감싸던 그녀의 팔의 감촉, 떠올릴 때마다 뺨에 검붉은 빛이 들었다 가시게 하는 그 부드럽게 매달리던 느낌—그 너머도, 그 뒤도, 그 곁도 모두 흐릿하고 어렴풋할 뿐이었다.\n\n패니가 제 심경을 쉴 새 없이 늘어놓지 않았더라면 차 시간은 무척 조용했을 것이다. 자기가 얼마나 놀랐는지, 그러다 이제 다들 갔거니 여겼는데, 그다음에는 속이 메스껍고 아득해지며 온몸이 사지마다 떨렸다는 이야기였다.\n\n“자, 그만하면 됐다.” 오라비가 식탁에서 일어서며 말했다. “실제로 겪은 것만으로도 나는 충분해.” 그가 방을 나서려는데 어머니가 그의 팔에 손을 얹어 붙들었다.\n\n“헤일 씨 댁에 가기 전에 여기로 돌아오너라.” 그녀가 낮고 애타는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n‘나는 알 건 다 알지.’ 패니가 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n“왜요? 그 시간에 찾아가면 폐가 될 만큼 늦을까요?”\n\n“존, 오늘 저녁만은 나에게 돌아와 다오. 헤일 부인에게는 늦은 시각이겠지. 하지만 그 때문이 아니다. 내일이면 너는—— 오늘 밤은 돌아오너라, 존!” 그녀가 아들에게 무엇을 애원한 적은 좀처럼 없었다. 그러기에는 자존심이 너무 강했다. 그러나 그 애원이 헛되었던 적도 없었다.\n\n“일을 마치면 곧장 여기로 돌아오겠습니다. 그 댁 안부는 꼭 물어봐 주시겠지요? 그분 안부를요.”\n\n손턴 부인은 패니에게 결코 말이 많은 상대가 아니었고, 아들이 없는 동안에는 잘 들어 주는 상대도 못 되었다. 그러나 아들이 돌아오자 그녀의 눈과 귀는 예리하게 살아나, 그가 자신과 자신이 쓰기로 한 사람들을 그날의 난동이 되풀이되는 일에서 지키려고 취한 조치들을 낱낱이 보고 들으려 했다. 그는 자기 목적을 뚜렷이 알고 있었다. 폭동에 가담한 자들에게 처벌과 고통이 따르는 것은 당연한 귀결이었다. 재산이 지켜지고 소유주의 뜻이 칼처럼 깨끗하고 날카롭게 제 목적을 향해 파고들려면 필요한 것은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n“어머니! 제가 내일 헤일 양에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 아시지요?”\n\n그 물음은 갑작스레 그녀를 덮쳤다. 적어도 그녀로서는 마거릿을 잊고 있던 침묵의 한때였다.\n\n그녀가 아들을 올려다보았다.\n\n“그래! 안다. 달리 어쩔 수도 없겠지.”\n\n“달리 어쩔 수 없다니요! 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“그 애가 감정에 그토록 휘둘리도록 내버려 둔 이상, 너는 명예를 걸고 매인 몸이라는 뜻이다—”\n\n“명예를 걸고 매였다니요.” 그가 경멸하듯 말했다. “유감스럽지만 명예 따위와는 아무 상관도 없습니다. ‘감정에 휘둘렸다’니요! 무슨 감정을 말씀하시는 겁니까?”\n\n“아니다, 존, 성낼 것 없다. 그 애가 너를 위험에서 구하겠다고 달려 내려와 네게 매달리지 않았느냐?”\n\n“그랬지요!” 그가 말했다. “하지만 어머니,” 그는 어머니 바로 앞에서 걸음을 뚝 멈추며 말을 이었다. “저는 감히 바라지 못하겠습니다. 전에는 한 번도 마음이 약해진 적이 없었는데, 그런 사람이 저를 마음에 두고 있으리라고는 도저히 믿을 수가 없습니다.”\n\n“어리석은 소리 마라, 존. 그런 사람이라니! 네 말만 들으면 그 애가 공작의 딸이라도 되는 줄 알겠구나. 그 애가 너를 마음에 두었다는 증거가 대체 얼마나 더 있어야 하겠느냐? 그 애가 세상을 보는 제 귀족 같은 눈길과 씨름했으리라는 건 나도 믿는다. 하지만 마침내 제대로 보게 되었으니 나는 오히려 그 애가 더 마음에 든다. 내가 이런 말을 하는 것만도 대단한 일이지.” 손턴 부인이 천천히 미소 지으며 말했는데, 그 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었다. “오늘 밤이 지나면 나는 둘째가 될 테니 말이다. 너를 몇 시간이라도 더 나 혼자, 온전히 나 혼자 가지고 싶어서 내일까지는 가지 말라고 부탁했던 게다.”\n\n“더없이 좋은 어머니!” (그래도 사랑은 이기적인 것이어서, 그는 순식간에 제 희망과 두려움으로 되돌아갔고, 그 바람에 손턴 부인의 가슴에는 차가운 그림자가 스멀스멀 드리웠다.) “하지만 저는 그분이 저를 마음에 두지 않는다는 걸 압니다. 그래도 그분의 발치에 저를 내던지겠습니다. 그래야만 합니다. 천에 하나, 아니 백만에 하나의 가망이라도 있다면 저는 그렇게 하겠습니다.”\n\n“두려워 마라!” 어머니가 말했다. 어미로서의 감정이 좀처럼 없이 터져 나온 그 순간을, 그리고 돌아보아 주지 않는 제 사랑의 깊이를 드러낸 그 질투의 아픔을 아들이 거의 알아차리지도 못한 데 대한 서운함을 짓눌러 삼키면서였다. “두려워하지 마라.” 그녀가 차갑게 말했다. “사랑으로 말하자면 그 애는 네게 어울리는 사람일지도 모르지. 그 애의 자존심을 꺾는 데는 어지간한 것으로는 안 되었을 게다. 두려워 마라, 존.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 잘 자라는 인사와 함께 아들에게 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 천천히 위엄 있게 방을 나갔다. 그러나 제 방에 들어서자 그녀는 문을 잠그고 앉아, 좀처럼 흘리지 않던 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n마거릿은 아버지와 어머니가 여전히 앉아 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있는 방으로 들어섰다. 얼굴은 몹시 창백하고 핏기가 없었다. 그녀는 목소리를 낼 자신이 생길 때까지 두 사람 바로 앞으로 다가섰다.\n\n“손턴 부인이 물침대를 보내 주실 거예요, 어머니.”\n\n“얘야, 어쩌면 그렇게 지쳐 보이니! 많이 덥더냐, 마거릿?”\n\n“아주 더워요. 그리고 파업 때문에 거리가 좀 험해요.”\n\n마거릿의 얼굴빛이 여느 때처럼 선명하고 환하게 돌아왔다가 이내 스러졌다.\n\n“베시 히긴스한테서 너더러 와 달라는 전갈이 왔더구나.” 헤일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 아무래도 너는 너무 지쳐 보인다.”\n\n“네!” 마거릿이 말했다. “저는 지쳤어요. 갈 수 없어요.”\n\n그녀는 차를 끓이는 내내 말이 없었고 몸이 떨렸다. 아버지가 어머니에게 온통 마음을 쏟느라 자신의 안색을 알아차리지 못하는 것이 고마웠다. 어머니가 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 아버지는 곁을 떠나려 하지 않고 어머니가 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 주겠다고 나섰다. 마거릿은 혼자가 되었다.\n\n“이제 그 일을 생각해 봐야지. 이제 다 떠올려 봐야지. 아까는 그럴 수가 없었어. 감히 그러지 못했어.” 그녀는 의자에 가만히 앉아 두 손을 무릎 위에 모으고 입술을 꼭 다문 채, 환영을 보는 사람처럼 눈길을 한곳에 붙박았다. 그녀는 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n“소란이라면 질색인 내가, 감정을 드러내는 사람들을 경멸해 온 내가, 그런 이들은 자제력이 모자란다고 여겨 온 내가, 기어이 내려가서 낭만적인 바보처럼 그 아수라장 속으로 몸을 던지고 말았어. 내가 무슨 도움이라도 됐을까? 내가 없었어도 그 사람들은 물러갔겠지.” 그러나 이것은 이치에 맞는 결론을 넘어선 생각이었고, 그녀의 잘 잡힌 판단력이 곧바로 그것을 느꼈다. “아니야, 어쩌면 물러가지 않았을지도 몰라. 나는 얼마쯤 도움이 됐어. 하지만 무엇에 씌었기에 그 사람을 무력한 아이라도 되는 양 감싸고 나섰을까! 아!” 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡아 쥐며 말했다. “그렇게 스스로를 욕보였으니, 그 사람들이 내가 그를 사랑한다고 여긴 것도 무리가 아니지. 내가 사랑을, 그것도 그 사람을!” 창백하던 두 뺨이 별안간 온통 불길처럼 달아올랐다. 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 감쌌다. 손을 떼었을 때 손바닥은 뜨겁게 타는 눈물로 젖어 있었다.\n\n“아, 사람들이 나를 두고 그런 말을 하다니, 내가 얼마나 낮은 데까지 떨어졌는가! 다른 누구를 위해서였다면 나는 그토록 용감할 수 없었을 거야. 바로 그가 나에게는 아무것도 아닌 사람이었기 때문에, 아니 정말이지 내가 그를 싫어하지 않는다고는 할 수 없기 때문에 그럴 수 있었어. 그래서 나는 양쪽 모두에게 공정한 처사가 있어야 한다는 마음이 더 간절했고, 무엇이 공정한 처사인지도 알아볼 수 있었어. 공정하지 않았어.” 그녀가 격하게 말했다. “그가 몸을 숨긴 채 거기 서서, 저 딱하고 미쳐 버린 사람들을 덫에 걸린 짐승처럼 잡아갈 군인들을 기다리기만 하고, 그들을 타일러 정신 차리게 하려는 노력은 조금도 하지 않은 것은 공정하지 않았어. 그리고 그들이 으르대던 대로 그에게 달려든 것은 공정하지 않은 것보다 더 나빴어. 나는 다시 그렇게 할 거야. 누가 나를 두고 무슨 말을 하든 상관없어. 그러지 않았더라면 저질러졌을 매질 한 번을, 잔인하고 성난 짓 하나를 막았다면, 나는 여자로서 할 일을 한 거야. 저 사람들이 내 처녀의 자존심을 마음껏 모욕하라지. 나는 하느님 앞에 깨끗하게 걸어간다!”\n\n그녀가 고개를 들자 고귀한 평화가 내려앉아 그 얼굴을 가라앉히는 듯했고, 마침내 그 얼굴은 “정으로 다듬은 대리석보다 더 고요”해졌다.\n\n딕슨이 들어왔다.\n\n“저, 마거릿 아가씨, 손턴 부인 댁에서 물침대가 왔습니다요. 아무래도 오늘 밤에는 너무 늦은 것 같아요. 마님이 거의 잠드셨으니까요. 그렇지만 내일이면 요긴하게 쓰이겠죠.”\n\n“그렇겠네.” 마거릿이 말했다. “우리가 아주 고마워하더라고 꼭 전해 줘.”\n\n딕슨이 잠깐 방을 나갔다.\n\n“저, 마거릿 아가씨, 그 사람 말이 아가씨 안부를 각별히 여쭈라고 했답니다. 마님을 말하는 거겠거니 싶은데, 헤일 양이 어떠신지 여쭈라는 게 마지막 당부였다고 하네요.”\n\n“나를?” 마거릿이 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우며 말했다. “나는 아주 멀쩡해. 더없이 잘 있다고 전해 줘.” 그러나 그녀의 안색은 손수건처럼 죽은 듯이 새하얬고, 머리가 몹시 아팠다.\n\n이제 헤일 씨가 들어왔다. 잠든 아내를 두고 온 그는, 마거릿이 보기에 딸이 들려줄 무슨 이야기로 재미와 흥미를 얻고 싶어 했다. 그녀는 불평 한마디 없이 고운 인내로 제 아픔을 견디며, 이야깃거리가 될 만한 자잘한 것들을 헤아릴 수 없이 끌어냈다. 폭동만 빼고 모든 것을 말이다. 그것만은 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다. 생각만 해도 속이 뒤집혔다.\n\n“잘 자거라, 마거릿. 나는 오늘 밤 푹 잘 수 있을 것 같구나. 너는 간호하느라 얼굴이 아주 창백하다. 네 어머니한테 무슨 일이 있으면 내가 딕슨을 부르마. 너는 가서 세상모르고 자거라. 정말이지 그래야 할 게다, 가엾은 것!”\n\n“안녕히 주무세요, 아버지!”\n\n그녀는 얼굴빛이 가시게 두었고, 거짓 미소가 스러지게 두었고, 두 눈이 무거운 아픔으로 흐려지게 두었다. 그녀는 고된 소임에서 제 굳센 의지를 놓아주었다. 아침이 올 때까지는 아프고 지쳐 있어도 괜찮았다.\n\n그녀는 자리에 누워 조금도 뒤척이지 않았다. 손이나 발을, 아니 손가락 하나조차 움직이는 일은 의지로든 몸으로든 할 수 없는 것이었다. 너무 지치고 너무 얼떨떨해서 그녀는 자신이 도무지 잠들지 못한 것으로 여겼다. 열에 들뜬 생각들이 잠과 깨어 있음의 경계를 넘나들며 저희의 비참한 정체를 그대로 지니고 있었다. 그렇게 엎드려 힘없이 누워 있으면서도 그녀는 혼자일 수가 없었다. 얼굴들의 구름이 그녀를 올려다보았다. 그것은 사납고 생생한 노여움이나 제 몸에 닥친 위험을 떠올리게 하지는 않았고, 다만 자신이 그렇게 뭇사람의 눈길을 받는 대상이 되었다는 깊은 수치심을 안겨 주었다. 그 수치심이 어찌나 날카로운지 땅속으로 파고들어 몸을 숨기고 싶을 지경이었으나, 그러면서도 그녀는 깜박이지 않는 그 수많은 눈의 번득임에서 벗어날 수가 없었다.\n\n# 제24장\n\n“그대의 아름다움이 맨 먼저 그 자리를 차지하고 굽힐 줄 모르던 내 심장의 성벽을 기어올랐으니, 이제 사로잡힌 그 마음은 비천한 옥에 갇혀 시들며, 공을 세우고도 매정한 가혹함만을 만났구나. 그러나 그럼에도 그대의 종은 머무르리라, 무례한 물리침에도 말 없는 자존심에도 아랑곳없이.” — 윌리엄 파울러.\n\n이튿날 아침, 마거릿은 밤이 지나갔다는 데 감사하며 몸을 억지로 일으켰다. 개운하지는 않았으나 쉬기는 한 셈이었다. 집 안은 온통 별일 없이 지나갔다. 어머니는 딱 한 번 깼을 뿐이었다. 뜨거운 공기 속에 실바람이 일고 있었고, 잎사귀 사이로 바람이 일으키는 장난스러운 흔들림을 보여 줄 나무는 한 그루도 없었지만, 마거릿은 알고 있었다. 어딘가에서, 길가에서, 잡목숲에서, 혹은 짙푸른 숲속에서 기분 좋게 소곤거리며 춤추는 소리가, 밀려왔다 잦아드는 그 소리가 일고 있으리라는 것을. 그것을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그녀의 마음속에는 또렷한 기쁨의 메아리가 울렸다.\n\n그녀는 헤일 부인의 방에서 바느질감을 들고 앉아 있었다. 오전의 선잠이 끝나는 대로 어머니가 옷 입는 것을 도울 참이었다. 저녁 식사 뒤에는 베시 히긴스를 보러 갈 생각이었다. 손턴 집안에 대한 기억은 모조리 몰아내리라. 그들이 정말로 피와 살을 지닌 채 눈앞에 서기 전까지는 생각할 까닭이 없었다. 그러나 물론 생각하지 않으려는 애씀은 오히려 그들을 더욱 또렷이 눈앞에 불러올 뿐이었다. 그리하여 이따금 뜨거운 홍조가 그녀의 창백한 얼굴 위로 번져 물을 들이곤 했다. 비구름 사이로 새어 나온 햇살이 바다 위를 빠르게 훑고 지나가듯이.\n\n딕슨이 아주 조용히 문을 열고, 그늘진 창가에 앉아 있는 마거릿에게 발끝으로 살금살금 다가왔다.\n\n“손턴 씨예요, 마거릿 아가씨. 응접실에 계십니다.”\n\n마거릿은 바느질감을 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n“나를 찾으셨어요? 아버지는 아직 안 들어오셨고요?”\n\n“아가씨를 찾으셨어요. 나리는 나가 계시고요.”\n\n“알았어요, 내려갈게요.” 마거릿이 조용히 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 이상하리만치 머뭇거렸다.\n\n손턴 씨는 창가에 서서 문을 등진 채, 겉보기에는 거리의 무엇인가를 지켜보는 데 골몰해 있었다. 그러나 실은 자기 자신이 두려웠던 것이다. 그녀가 온다는 생각에 심장이 무겁게 뛰었다. 그는 자기 목을 감싸던 그녀의 팔의 감촉을 잊을 수 없었다. 그때는 그저 성가시게만 느꼈으면서도. 그러나 이제 매달리듯 그를 지켜 주던 그 기억은 온몸을 저릿하게 훑고 지나가는 듯했고, 불 앞의 밀랍처럼 모든 결심과 온갖 자제력을 죄다 녹여 버리는 듯했다. 그는 자기가 앞으로 나아가 두 팔을 벌린 채, 어제 아무 헤아림도 받지 못한 채 그랬듯이 그녀가 와서 그 품에 안기기를 말없이 애원하게 될까 봐 두려웠다. 이제 다시는 헤아림 없이 넘기지 않을 터였다. 심장이 크고 빠르게 두근거렸다. 강인한 사내였으나 그는 자기가 해야 할 말과 그것이 어떻게 받아들여질지를 미리 그려 보며 떨었다. 그녀가 고개를 숙이고 얼굴을 붉히며, 마치 제 타고난 집이자 쉼터인 양 그의 품으로 떨려 들지도 몰랐다. 어느 순간에는 그녀가 그럴지도 모른다는 생각에 조바심으로 달아올랐고, 다음 순간에는 격렬한 거절이 두려웠다. 그 생각은 그의 앞날을 어찌나 지독하게 시들려 놓는지 그는 아예 생각하기를 거부했다. 그는 방 안에 다른 누군가가 있다는 느낌에 흠칫 놀랐다. 그가 돌아섰다. 그녀는 너무나 조용히 들어와서 그는 인기척조차 듣지 못했던 것이다. 딴 데 팔린 그의 귀에는 부드러운 모슬린 드레스 차림의 느린 걸음보다 거리의 소음이 더 또렷했다.\n\n그녀는 탁자 곁에 선 채 앉으려 하지 않았다. 눈꺼풀은 눈을 반쯤 덮고 있었다. 이는 앙다물지는 않았으나 다물려 있었고, 입술이 그 위로 살짝 벌어져 곡선 사이로 흰 줄이 보였다. 느리고 깊은 숨결이 턱과 아름다운 콧방울을 부풀렸다. 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 보이는 유일한 움직임이었다. 결 고운 살결도, 갸름한 뺨도, 양끝이 보조개로 깊이 팬 도톰한 입매도 오늘은 온통 핏기 없이 창백했다. 여느 때의 건강한 혈색이 사라진 것은 관자놀이까지 내려 빗은 검은 머리의 짙은 그늘 때문에 한층 두드러졌는데, 그것은 그녀가 맞은 상처의 흔적을 죄다 감추려는 것이었다. 눈은 내리깔았어도 고개는 예의 그 도도한 자세로 살짝 뒤로 젖혀져 있었다. 긴 팔은 옆구리에 미동도 없이 늘어져 있었다. 통틀어 보면 그녀는 자기가 혐오하고 경멸하는 죄를 뒤집어쓴 채, 너무도 분해서 스스로를 변호할 마음조차 없는 죄수처럼 보였다.\n\n손턴 씨는 급히 한두 걸음 앞으로 나섰다가 마음을 다잡고는, 조용하고도 단호하게 문께로 가서(그녀가 열어 둔 채였다) 문을 닫았다. 그러고는 돌아와 잠시 그녀 맞은편에 서서, 자기가 해야 할 말로 그것을 흐트러뜨리기 전에, 어쩌면 밀쳐 내기 전에, 그 아름다운 존재가 주는 전체의 인상을 받아들였다.\n\n“헤일 양, 어제 저는 참으로 배은망덕했습니다……”\n\n“감사하실 일이 아무것도 없었어요.” 그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 똑바로 마주 보며 말했다. “제가 한 일에 대해 제게 고마워해야 한다고 여기신다는 말씀이시겠지요.” 스스로도 어쩌지 못한 채, 분노를 거스르며 짙은 홍조가 온 얼굴에 번져 두 눈까지 타올랐다. 그럼에도 그 눈은 진지하고 흔들림 없는 시선을 거두지 않았다. “그저 자연스러운 본능이었을 뿐이에요. 어떤 여자라도 똑같이 했을 거예요. 위험을 보면 우리 여자들은 누구나 제 성(性)의 신성함을 높은 특권으로 느끼니까요. 오히려 제가……” 그녀가 서둘러 말했다. “위험 속으로 내려가시게 만든 그 경솔한 말에 대해 사과드려야 할 거예요.”\n\n“말씀이 문제가 아니었습니다. 아무리 신랄하게 표현되었다 해도, 그 말이 담고 있던 진실이 문제였지요. 그러나 그 이야기로 저를 밀쳐 내고 제 깊은 감사의 표현을, 제……를 피해 가시지는 못합니다.” 그는 이제 벼랑 끝에 서 있었다. 그는 뜨거운 격정에 휩쓸려 서둘러 말하지 않으려 했다. 말 한 마디 한 마디를 저울질하려 했다. 그러겠다고 마음먹었고, 그의 의지가 이겼다. 그는 내달리던 한가운데서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n“저는 무엇에서도 달아나려 하지 않아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그저 제게 감사하실 이유가 없다고 말씀드리는 것뿐이에요. 그리고 덧붙이자면, 그런 표현은 제게 괴로울 거예요. 제가 그럴 자격이 있다고 느끼지 않으니까요. 그래도 그것이 상상 속의 빚에서나마 손턴 씨를 놓여나게 해 준다면, 말씀하세요.”\n\n“저는 어떤 빚에서도 놓여나고 싶지 않습니다.” 그녀의 침착한 태도에 자극받아 그가 말했다. “상상이든 아니든, 어느 쪽인지 저 자신에게 캐묻지 않겠습니다. 저는 제 목숨을 헤일 양에게 빚졌다고 믿기로 했습니다. 그래요, 웃으십시오. 과장이라 여기고 싶으면 그러십시오. 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 그렇게 생각하는 것이 이 목숨에 값을 더해 주니까요. 아, 헤일 양!” 그가 목소리를 낮추어, 그녀가 그 앞에서 몸을 떨 만큼 부드럽고도 격렬한 열정을 담아 말을 이었다. “일이 이렇게 얽힌 덕분에, 앞으로 제가 살아 있음에 기뻐할 때마다 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 수 있으리라는 것 말입니다. ‘이 삶의 모든 기쁨도, 세상에서 내 일을 해내는 온갖 정직한 자부심도, 살아 있다는 이 예리한 감각도 모두 그 사람에게 빚진 것이다!’ 그리고 그것은 기쁨을 갑절로 만들고, 자부심을 타오르게 하고, 살아 있다는 감각을 어찌나 날카롭게 벼려 놓는지, 이것을 한 사람에게 빚졌다고 생각하는 것이 고통인지 즐거움인지조차 알 수 없을 지경입니다. 아니, 들으셔야 합니다, 들으실 겁니다.” 그가 엄한 결의를 품고 앞으로 나서며 말했다. “일찍이 어떤 사내도 여자를 그렇게 사랑한 적이 없으리라 믿을 만큼 제가 사랑하는 한 사람에게 말입니다.” 그가 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 꽉 쥐었다. 그는 무슨 말이 나올지 귀 기울이며 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 그러다 그녀의 얼음장 같은 목소리를 듣고 분해하며 그 손을 뿌리쳤다. 말은 어디서 찾아야 할지 모르겠다는 듯 더듬더듬 나왔지만, 그 목소리는 정말이지 얼음장 같았다.\n\n“그런 말씀은 제게 충격이에요. 그건 신성모독이에요. 그것이 제 첫 느낌이라면 저로서도 어쩔 수가 없어요. 말씀하시는 그런 감정을 제가 안다면 그렇지 않을지도 모르지요. 저는 손턴 씨를 괴롭히고 싶지 않아요. 게다가 우리는 조용히 말해야 해요. 어머니가 주무시고 계시니까요. 하지만 그 태도 전체가 제게는 모욕이에요……”\n\n“뭐라고요!” 그가 소리쳤다. “모욕이라니요! 저는 참으로 운이 없군요.”\n\n“그래요!” 그녀가 위엄을 되찾고 말했다. “저는 모욕을 느껴요. 그리고 그것이 마땅하다고 생각해요. 손턴 씨는 어제 제 행동이”—다시 짙은 다홍빛 홍조가 올랐으나 이번에는 부끄러움보다 분노로 눈이 이글거렸다—“손턴 씨와 저 사이의 사사로운 행동이었다고, 그래서 찾아와 그 일로 제게 감사할 수 있다고 여기시는 모양이군요. 신사라면 마땅히 알아보았을 것을 알아보지 못하시고요. 그래요, 신사라면요.” 그녀는 전에 그 단어를 두고 나누었던 대화를 넌지시 가리키며 되풀이했다. “여자라는 이름에 값하는 여자라면 누구나 나서서, 존중받는 제 무력함으로 뭇사람의 폭력 앞에 놓인 사내를 감싸 주리라는 것을요.”\n\n“그리고 그렇게 구원받은 신사는 감사할 위안조차 금지당하는군요!” 그가 경멸조로 끼어들었다. “저는 사내입니다. 제 감정을 표현할 권리를 주장합니다.”\n\n“그리고 저는 그 권리를 인정했어요. 다만 그것을 고집하셔서 제게 고통을 준다고 말씀드렸을 뿐이에요.” 그녀가 도도하게 대답했다. “하지만 손턴 씨는 제가 그저 여자다운 본능에 이끌린 것이 아니라”—그리고 여기서 오래 눌러 두고 격렬하게 싸워 온 격정의 눈물이 두 눈에 차올라 목소리를 막았다—“무슨 특별한 감정에 이끌려 그랬다고, 손턴 씨에 대한 감정 말이에요, 그렇게 상상하신 모양이군요! 아니, 그 무리 속에 제가 더 큰 동정을 느끼지 않은 사내는 하나도 없었어요. 그 가엾고 절박한 사내들 가운데 제가 할 수 있는 그 작은 일을 더 진심으로 해 주지 않았을 사람은 하나도 없었다고요.”\n\n“계속 말씀하십시오, 헤일 양. 저는 헤일 양의 그 엉뚱한 동정심들을 잘 알고 있습니다. 이제 저는 헤일 양이 그토록 고결하게 행동하게 만든 것이 억압에 대한 타고난 감각뿐이었다고 믿습니다. 그래요, 저도 공장주이지만 억압당할 수 있지요. 저를 경멸하시는 줄 압니다. 그것은 저를 이해하지 못하시기 때문이라고 말씀드리게 해 주십시오.”\n\n“이해하고 싶지 않아요.” 그녀가 몸을 지탱하려고 탁자를 붙들며 대답했다. 그가 잔인하다고 여겼기 때문이었다. 실제로 그러했다. 그리고 그녀는 분노로 기운이 빠져 있었다.\n\n“그렇겠지요, 그러고 싶지 않으신 게 보입니다. 헤일 양은 불공평하고 부당합니다.”\n\n마거릿은 입술을 앙다물었다. 그런 비난에 대꾸할 생각이 없었다. 그러나 그 모든 것에도, 그의 그 모진 말들에도 불구하고 그는 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던져 옷자락 끝에 입 맞출 수도 있었다. 그녀는 말하지 않았다. 움직이지도 않았다. 상한 자존심의 눈물이 뜨겁고 빠르게 떨어졌다. 그는 그녀가 무슨 말이든, 대꾸할 수 있는 비아냥이라도 해 주기를 바라며 잠시 기다렸다. 그러나 그녀는 잠자코 있었다. 그는 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n“한마디만 더 하지요. 헤일 양은 제게 사랑받는 것이 스스로를 더럽히기라도 하는 것처럼 여기는 얼굴이시군요. 그것을 피하실 수는 없습니다. 아니, 제가 원한다 한들 그 더럽힘에서 헤일 양을 씻어 드릴 수는 없습니다. 그러나 할 수 있다 해도 그러지 않겠습니다. 저는 이제껏 어떤 여자도 사랑한 적이 없습니다. 제 삶은 너무 바빴고, 제 생각은 다른 것들에 너무 사로잡혀 있었으니까요. 이제 저는 사랑하고, 앞으로도 사랑할 것입니다. 그러나 제 쪽에서 그것을 지나치게 드러낼까 두려워하지는 마십시오.”\n\n“두렵지 않아요.” 그녀가 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다. “여태 아무도 감히 제게 무례하게 군 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 사람은 없을 거예요. 하지만 손턴 씨, 제 아버지께는 참 잘해 주셨어요.” 그녀가 목소리와 태도를 더없이 여자다운 부드러움으로 온통 바꾸며 말했다. “우리 서로 화나게 하는 일은 그만두어요. 제발 그러지 말아요!” 그는 그녀의 말을 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그는 반 분쯤 웃옷 소매로 모자의 보풀을 쓸어 다듬는 일에 몰두했다. 그러고는 그녀가 내민 손을 뿌리치고, 그녀의 진지한 뉘우침의 눈빛을 보지 못한 척하며 홱 돌아서서 방을 나갔다. 마거릿은 그가 나가기 전에 그의 얼굴을 얼핏 보았다.\n\n그가 가 버린 뒤, 그녀는 그의 눈에 씻긴 듯한 눈물의 반짝임을 보았다고 생각했다. 그러자 그녀의 도도한 반감이 무엇인가 다르고 한결 다정한 것으로, 그러나 거의 그만큼 괴로운 것으로 바뀌었다. 누구에게든 그런 굴욕을 안겼다는 데 대한 자책이었다.\n\n“하지만 내가 어쩔 수 있었겠어?” 그녀가 스스로에게 물었다. “나는 그를 좋아한 적이 없어. 예의는 지켰지만 내 무관심을 감추려 애쓰지도 않았지. 정말이지 나는 나 자신에 대해서도 그에 대해서도 생각해 본 적이 없으니, 내 태도가 그 진실을 드러냈을 수밖에 없어. 어제 있었던 그 모든 일은 그가 오해할 수도 있겠지. 하지만 그건 그의 잘못이지 내 잘못이 아니야. 필요하다면 나는 또 그렇게 할 거야. 그것이 이 온갖 부끄러움과 곤란으로 나를 끌고 간다 해도.”\n\n# 제25장\n\n“복수가 제 몫을 차지할지도 모른다. 깨어난 군율이 그들의 대의를 소리 높여 외치고, 상처 입은 함대들이 저희가 깨어진 법을 다그친다.” — 바이런.\n\n마거릿은 모든 청혼이 미리 짐작조차 못 할 만큼 뜻밖이고, 벌어지는 그 순간에는 이토록 괴로운 것인지 궁금해지기 시작했다. 자기가 받은 두 번의 청혼이 꼭 그러했으니 말이다. 레녹스 씨와 손턴 씨를 견주어 보는 마음이 저도 모르게 일었다. 헨리 레녹스에게서 우정 아닌 다른 감정의 표현이 상황에 이끌려 나와 버린 것이 그때는 안타까웠다. 첫 청혼을 받았을 때 그녀를 지배한 감정은 그 안타까움이었다. 그러나 지금처럼 얼떨떨하고 이토록 강한 자국이 남은 적은 없었다. 손턴 씨의 목소리의 여운이 아직 방 안을 맴돌고 있는 지금처럼은. 레녹스의 경우, 그는 한순간 우정과 사랑 사이의 경계를 미끄러져 넘은 듯했고, 바로 다음 순간에는 이유는 달랐으나 그녀 못지않게 그것을 후회하는 듯했다. 손턴 씨의 경우, 마거릿이 아는 한 그 사이에 우정이라는 단계는 없었다. 두 사람의 교제는 하나로 이어진 대립의 연속이었다. 의견은 부딪쳤다. 그리고 실은, 그가 그녀의 의견을 그녀라는 한 사람에게 속한 것으로서 마음에 두었다고 느낀 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그 의견들이 바위 같은 그의 성품의 힘에, 그 정열의 힘에 맞서는 한, 그는 경멸하듯 그것들을 제 몸에서 떨쳐 냈고, 마침내 그녀는 쓸데없는 항의를 애써 내놓는 일에 지쳐 버렸다. 그런데 이제 그가 이렇게 야릇하고 걷잡을 수 없는 정열의 방식으로 자기 사랑을 알리러 온 것이다! 처음에는 그의 청혼이 그녀가 스스로를 드러내 보인 그 일—그도 남들처럼 오해했을지 모를 그 일—에 대한 날카로운 연민에 떠밀리고 내몰려 나온 것이라는 생각이 얼핏 들기는 했다. 그러나 그가 방을 나서기도 전에, 아니 적어도 나선 지 5분도 되지 않아, 그가 그녀를 사랑한다는, 사랑해 왔다는, 앞으로도 사랑하리라는 또렷한 확신이 그녀 위로 밝아 왔고 환하게 비쳐 들었다. 그리하여 그녀는 지난 삶 전체와 어긋나는 어떤 커다란 힘에 홀린 듯 몸을 움츠리고 떨었다. 그녀는 슬며시 물러나 그의 생각으로부터 몸을 숨겼다. 그러나 아무 소용이 없었다. 페어팩스가 옮긴 타소의 한 구절을 비틀어 말하자면—\n\n“그의 강한 모습이 그녀의 생각 속을 헤매고 다녔다.”\n\n그녀는 그가 자기 속마음의 의지를 사로잡았다는 이유로 그를 더 싫어했다. 자기가 경멸하며 뿌리쳤는데도 여전히 사랑하겠다고 감히 말하다니. 더 많이, 더 세게 말했어야 했다는 후회가 들었다. 이제는 입 밖에 내기에 너무 늦어 버린 지금에야 날카롭고 단호한 말들이 마음속으로 몰려들었다. 그 만남이 남긴 깊은 자국은 꿈속의 공포와 같았다. 잠에서 깨어 눈을 비비고 굳은 웃음을 억지로 입가에 지어 보아도 방을 떠나지 않는 그런 공포. 그것은 거기에, 바로 거기에 있다. 방 어느 구석에 웅크린 채 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 내며, 붙박인 무시무시한 눈으로, 우리가 감히 그것이 거기 있다는 말을 누구에게 흘리지나 않을까 귀를 기울이면서. 그리고 우리는 감히 그러지 못한다. 우리는 참으로 가엾은 겁쟁이인 것이다!\n\n그렇게 그녀는 그의 변치 않는 사랑이라는 위협에서 몸서리치며 물러났다. 그게 무슨 뜻인가? 그녀에게 그를 주눅 들게 할 힘이 없단 말인가? 두고 볼 일이었다. 그런 식으로 그녀를 위협하다니 남자로서 지나치게 뻔뻔했다. 그는 그 비참한 어제 일을 근거로 삼는 것인가? 필요하다면 그녀는 내일도 똑같이 할 것이었다. 앉은뱅이 거지를 위해서라도 기꺼이 즐거이 그리할 것이고, 그를 위해서라도 그의 억측과 여자의 주제넘음이라는 차가운 진창을 무릅쓰고 꼭 그만큼 용감하게 그리할 것이었다. 그녀가 그렇게 한 것은 그것이 옳고 단순하고 참되었기 때문이다. 구할 수 있는 곳에서 구하는 일, 구해 보려 애쓰기라도 하는 일이 그러했다. “해야 할 일을 하라, 그리고 오는 것은 오는 대로 두라.”\n\n그때까지 그녀는 그가 두고 간 그 자리에서 한 걸음도 움직이지 않았다. 그의 마지막 말에, 그리고 그 불꽃 앞에서 그녀 자신의 눈이 떨구어지게 만들었던 그 깊고 골똘하고 정열적인 눈빛에 빠져든 생각의 혼미에서 그녀를 흔들어 깨울 바깥일이 아무것도 없었다. 그녀는 창가로 가서 창을 활짝 열어 자기를 에워싼 답답함을 몰아냈다. 그러고는 문으로 가서 문을 열었다. 다른 사람들과 어울리든 몸을 부지런히 놀리든 지난 한 시간의 기억을 떨쳐 버리고 싶다는 조급한 마음이 일었던 것이다. 그러나 밤 시간에는 허락되지 않는 개운찮은 잠을 병자가 겨우 붙드는 집의 한낮 고요 속에, 모든 것이 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 마거릿은 혼자 있고 싶지 않았다. 무엇을 해야 하나? “물론 베시 히긴스를 보러 가야지.” 어젯밤 전해진 전갈이 문득 떠오르며 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다. 그리고 곧 집을 나섰다.\n\n그곳에 이르니 베시가 긴 나무의자에 누워 있었다. 날이 후텁지근하고 숨 막히는데도 의자는 불 가까이로 옮겨져 있었다. 그녀는 어떤 고통의 발작 뒤에 기운 없이 쉬는 사람처럼 아주 반듯이 눕혀져 있었다. 마거릿은 좀 더 앉은 자세를 취하면 숨쉬기가 한결 자유로우리라고 확신했다. 그래서 아무 말 없이 그녀를 일으켜 베개를 고쳐 놓아 주었고, 베시는 몹시 기운은 없어도 한결 편안해졌다.\n\n“다시는 댁을 못 볼 줄 알았구먼요.” 마침내 베시가 마거릿의 얼굴을 애타게 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n“많이 나빠지신 것 같아 걱정이에요. 하지만 어제는 올 수가 없었어요. 어머니가 너무 편찮으셨거든요. 여러 가지 사정도 있었고요.” 마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n“메리를 보내 댁을 부른 게 제 분수에 넘친 짓이라 여기실지도 모르겠구먼요. 그렇지만 그 다투는 소리하고 큰소리가 저를 갈가리 찢어 놓아서, 아버지가 나가고 나니 이런 생각이 들었지 뭐예요. 아, 그저 그이 목소리로 평화와 약속의 말씀을 읽어 주는 걸 들을 수만 있다면, 아기가 어미 자장가에 잠들듯이 나도 하느님의 고요와 안식 속으로 스러져 갈 수 있을 텐데 하고요.”\n\n“지금 한 장 읽어 드릴까요?”\n\n“그럼요, 읽어 주세요! 처음에는 아마 뜻은 귀에 안 들어올 거예요. 아주 멀리서 들리는 것 같겠지요. 그러다가 제가 좋아하는 말씀이 나오면, 위로가 되는 구절이 나오면, 바로 귓가에 대고 하는 것처럼 들리면서 제 속을 뚫고 지나갈 거구먼요.”\n\n마거릿이 읽기 시작했다. 베시는 이리저리 몸을 뒤척였다. 애를 써서 한순간 귀를 기울이면, 그다음 순간에는 배로 안절부절못하는 몸부림에 사로잡히는 듯했다. 마침내 그녀가 터뜨렸다. “그만 읽으세요. 소용없구먼요. 어쩔 도리 없는 일을 두고 성이 나서 생각하느라 저는 내내 마음속으로 하느님을 욕하고 있는걸요. 어제 말버러 공장에서 난리가 났다는 이야기는 들으셨겠지요? 손턴네 공장 말이에요.”\n\n“아버지는 거기 안 계셨지요?” 마거릿이 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 말했다.\n\n“아버지는 아니지요. 그런 일이 아예 없었다면 오른손이라도 내주었을 사람이에요. 저를 애태우는 게 바로 그거예요. 아버지는 그 일로 마음이 아주 무너져 버렸어요. 바보들은 언제나 선을 넘게 마련이라고 말해 봐야 소용없구먼요. 저이처럼 낙심한 사람은 여태 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“그런데 왜요?” 마거릿이 물었다. “저는 잘 모르겠어요.”\n\n“그게요, 아버지가 이번 파업의 위원이거든요. 조합에서 아버지를 뽑았지요. 제 입으로 할 말은 아니지만, 아버지는 속이 깊은 사람으로 통하고 뼛속까지 정직하다고들 하니까요. 그래서 아버지하고 다른 위원들이 계획을 세웠어요. 무슨 일이 있어도 한 덩어리로 뭉치자, 다수가 생각하는 대로 나머지도 좋든 싫든 따라 생각하자고요. 그리고 무엇보다 나라의 법을 거스르는 일은 절대 없어야 한다고 했구먼요. 사람들이 보기에 저희가 벙어리처럼 참으면서 굶주리며 버틴다면 세상 인심이 저희 편을 들 테지만, 싸우고 부딪치는 소란이 한 번이라도 나면, 그게 설령 파업 파괴자를 상대로 한 것이라도 다 끝장이라는 걸 저희는 전에도 여러 번 겪어서 알거든요. 파업 파괴자들한테는 말을 붙여 보고, 달래 보고, 이치를 따져 보고, 어쩌면 물러가라고 으름장을 놓을 수는 있어도, 무슨 일이 닥치든 위원회는 조합원 모두에게 필요하다면 주먹 한 번 휘두르지 말고 그냥 누워서 죽으라고 명령했어요. 그래야 세상 사람들을 제 편으로 확실히 끌어올 수 있다고 셈한 거지요. 게다가 위원회는 저희 요구가 옳다는 걸 알고 있었고, 옳은 것이 그른 것과 뒤섞여서 사람들이 갈라낼 수 없게 되는 걸 바라지 않았어요. 댁이 약가루 타 먹으라고 주신 그 젤리에서 제가 가루를 갈라낼 수 없는 것처럼요. 젤리가 훨씬 많은데도 가루 맛이 온통 배어 있잖아요. 자, 이 일을 길게도 이야기했구먼요. 그런데 저는 기진맥진했어요. 댁이 한번 생각해 보세요. 아버지로서는 제 일이 몽땅 헛것이 되어 버린 게, 그것도 바우처 같은 바보 때문에 그리된 게 어떻겠나 하고요. 그 사람은 굳이 위원회 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 파업을 망쳐 놓았으니, 아예 유다 노릇을 하려고 작정한 거나 마찬가지지 뭐예요. 어이구! 어젯밤에 아버지가 그 사람한테 퍼부었어요! 난리를 일으킨 주모자가 어디 있는지 경찰에 가서 일러바치겠다고까지 하더구먼요. 공장주들한테 넘겨서 마음대로 하게 두겠다고요. 파업의 진짜 우두머리는 바우처 같은 자가 아니라 진중하고 생각 깊은 사람들이라는 걸, 법과 재판을 존중하고 질서를 지킬 좋은 직공이자 좋은 시민이라는 걸, 그저 제 몫의 삯을 바랄 뿐이고 그걸 받기 전에는 굶어 죽더라도 일하지 않겠지만 남의 재산이나 목숨은 결코 해치지 않을 사람들이라는 걸 세상에 보여 주겠다고요. 왜냐하면요.” 그녀가 목소리를 낮추었다. “바우처가 손턴 씨 누이한테 돌을 던져서 하마터면 죽일 뻔했다는 말이 돌거든요.”\n\n“그건 사실이 아니에요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “돌을 던진 건 바우처가 아니었어요.” 그녀는 처음에 얼굴이 붉어졌다가 이내 하얘졌다.\n\n“그럼 댁이 거기 있었나 봐요?” 베시가 힘없이 물었다. 실은 그녀는 말하는 것이 유난히 힘든 듯 여러 번 말을 끊어 가며 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n“네. 그건 됐어요. 계속하세요. 다만 돌을 던진 건 바우처가 아니었어요. 그런데 그 사람이 아버지께 뭐라고 대답했나요?”\n\n“말은 없었어요. 정열이 다 타 버린 사람처럼 온몸을 어찌나 떨던지 저는 차마 쳐다볼 수가 없었구먼요. 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬는 소리가 들렸고, 한때는 흐느끼는 줄 알았어요. 그런데 아버지가 경찰에 넘기겠다고 하니까 그 사람이 크게 소리를 지르면서 주먹으로 아버지 얼굴을 치고는 번개같이 달아나 버렸어요. 아버지는 처음엔 그 주먹에 얼이 빠져 있었지요. 바우처가 성이 다 빠지고 굶주려서 힘도 없는 사람이었는데도요. 아버지는 잠깐 앉아서 손으로 눈을 가리더니 문 쪽으로 가더구먼요. 어디서 그런 힘이 났는지 모르겠는데, 저는 몸을 의자에서 던지듯 일으켜 아버지한테 매달렸어요. ‘아버지, 아버지!’ 하고 제가 말했지요. ‘그 불쌍한 굶주린 사람을 절대 고자질하시면 안 돼요. 안 하겠다고 말할 때까지 저는 절대 안 놓을 거예요.’ 그러자 아버지가 ‘바보 같은 소리 마라’ 하시더군요. ‘사람들은 대개 행동보다 말이 앞서는 법이다. 나는 그놈을 경찰에 이를 생각은 없었다. 하기야 맹세코, 그놈은 그래 싸지. 누가 다른 사람이 그 궂은일을 맡아서 그놈을 잡아넣었다 해도 나는 개의치 않았을 거다. 그렇지만 이제 그놈이 나를 쳤으니 더더욱 그럴 수가 없구나. 그건 내 싸움을 남의 손에 맡기는 짓이 될 테니까. 다만 언제고 그놈이 이 굶주림을 이겨 내고 몸이 성해지면, 그놈하고 나하고 한 판 제대로 붙을 거다. 발길질까지 곁들여서 말이다. 내가 그놈한테 무얼 해 줄 수 있는지 그때 보자꾸나.’ 그러고는 아버지가 저를 떨쳐 냈어요. 사실 저도 기운이 다 빠져 까무러칠 지경이었고, 아버지 얼굴은 피가 묻지 않은 데는 온통 흙빛으로 하얘서 보기만 해도 속이 메스꺼웠구먼요. 그러고 나서 메리가 들어올 때까지 제가 잤는지 깼는지 아주 정신을 잃었는지 알 수가 없어요. 그래서 메리한테 댁을 모셔 오라고 했지요. 자, 이제 저한테 말 걸지 마시고 그 장을 읽어만 주세요. 속에 있는 걸 다 뱉어 내니 마음은 좀 편해졌구먼요. 그렇지만 이 지긋지긋한 뒷맛을 입에서 씻어 낼 만한, 저 멀리 있는 세상 생각이 좀 있었으면 해요. 설교 같은 장 말고 이야기가 있는 장을 읽어 주세요. 거기엔 그림이 들어 있어서 눈을 감으면 보이거든요. 새 하늘과 새 땅 이야기를 읽어 주세요. 그러면 어쩌면 이걸 잊을 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n마거릿이 부드럽고 낮은 목소리로 읽었다. 베시는 눈을 감고 있었지만 한동안 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 눈물의 물기가 속눈썹에 무겁게 맺혔기 때문이다. 마침내 그녀는 잠이 들었다. 여러 번 흠칫거리고 무어라 애원하는 말을 중얼거리면서. 마거릿은 그녀에게 이불을 덮어 주고 자리를 떴다. 집에서 자기를 찾을지도 모른다는 불안한 마음이 있었기 때문이다. 그러면서도 그때까지는 죽어 가는 처녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 잔인하게 여겨졌었다.\n\n마거릿이 돌아오니 헤일 부인이 응접실에 있었다. 그날은 부인의 상태가 좋은 날이었고, 부인은 물침대 칭찬으로 가득했다. 그것은 그 뒤로 자 본 어떤 잠자리보다도 존 베러스퍼드 경 댁의 침대와 비슷했다. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만 요즘 사람들은 제 젊은 시절에 만들던 것과 같은 침대를 만드는 재주를 잃어버린 모양이라고 했다. 그리 어려운 일도 아닐 것 같은데 말이다. 깃털도 그때와 같은 것을 구할 수 있는데 어쩐 일인지, 어젯밤에야 비로소 언제 그렇게 푹 자 보았는지 기억나지 않을 만큼 깊은 잠을 잤다는 것이었다.\n\n헤일 씨는 옛날 깃털 침대의 좋은 점 가운데 얼마쯤은 쉬는 맛을 돋우어 주던 젊음의 활기 덕이 아니겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다. 그러나 이 생각은 아내에게 곱게 받아들여지지 않았다.\n\n“아니에요, 헤일 씨. 그건 존 경 댁의 그 침대들이었어요. 자, 마거릿, 너는 젊고 낮에는 이리저리 돌아다니지 않니. 침대가 편하더냐? 네게 물어보마. 누웠을 때 온전히 쉬는 느낌이 들더냐? 아니면 오히려 이리 뒤척 저리 뒤척 하면서 편한 자세를 찾으려다 헛수고하고, 아침에는 잠자리에 들 때만큼 지쳐서 깨지 않더냐?”\n\n마거릿이 웃었다. “사실대로 말씀드리면 저는 제 침대가 어떤 것인지 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없어요, 어머니. 밤이면 어찌나 졸린지 아무 데나 눕기만 하면 곧바로 곯아떨어지거든요. 그러니 저는 제대로 된 증인이 못 될 것 같아요. 게다가 아시다시피 저는 존 베러스퍼드 경 댁의 침대를 써 볼 기회가 아예 없었잖아요. 저는 옥슨햄에 가 본 적이 없는걸요.”\n\n“그랬니? 아, 그렇구나! 그래 맞다. 내가 데리고 간 건 가엾은 우리 프레드였지. 이제 기억나는구나. 결혼한 뒤로 옥슨햄에 간 건 딱 한 번, 네 쇼 이모 결혼식 때였다. 그때 가엾은 어린 프레드는 갓난아기였지. 딕슨이 몸종에서 유모로 바뀌는 걸 싫어했던 걸 나도 안다. 그래서 그 애를 옛집 가까이, 제 고향 사람들 사이로 데려가면 나를 떠나겠다고 할까 봐 겁이 났었지. 그런데 가엾은 아기가 옥슨햄에서 이가 나느라 앓았단다. 게다가 나는 결혼을 앞둔 애나 곁에 오래 붙어 있어야 했고 나 자신도 그리 튼튼하지 못했으니, 딕슨이 전에 없이 그 애를 도맡게 되었지. 그러다 보니 그 애를 어찌나 예뻐하게 되었는지, 프레드가 다른 사람은 다 마다하고 저한테만 매달리면 얼마나 자랑스러워하던지, 그 뒤로는 나를 떠날 생각은 다시 하지 않았을 게다. 그전에 익숙하던 일과는 아주 딴판이었는데도 말이야. 가엾은 프레드! 다들 그 애를 사랑했지. 그 애는 사람 마음을 얻는 재주를 타고났단다. 리드 함장이 내 사랑하는 아들을 싫어했다는 걸 알고 나면 그 사람을 아주 나쁘게 여기게 돼. 나는 그게 그 사람 심보가 나빴다는 확실한 증거라고 본다. 아! 네 가엾은 아버지 좀 보아라, 마거릿. 방을 나가 버리셨구나. 프레드 이야기가 나오는 걸 못 견디시니까.”\n\n“저는 오빠 이야기 듣는 게 좋아요, 어머니. 하고 싶은 이야기는 다 해 주세요. 아무리 많이 해 주셔도 지나치지 않아요. 오빠가 아기였을 때 어땠는지 이야기해 주세요.”\n\n“저런, 마거릿, 서운해하면 안 된다만, 그 애가 너보다 훨씬 예뻤단다. 딕슨의 품에 안긴 너를 처음 보았을 때 내가 이렇게 말했던 게 기억난다. ‘어머나, 참 못생긴 아기로구나!’ 그러자 딕슨이 이러더구나. ‘아이가 다 프레드 도련님 같을 수야 있나요, 그 도련님께 복이 있기를!’ 어머나! 참 또렷하게도 기억나는구나. 그때는 하루 종일 언제든 프레드를 품에 안을 수 있었고, 그 애 아기 침대가 내 침대 바로 옆에 있었지. 그런데 지금은, 지금은, 마거릿, 내 아들이 어디 있는지도 모르고, 어떤 때는 다시는 그 애를 못 보겠구나 싶단다.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니의 소파 옆 낮은 발판에 앉아 어머니의 손을 가만히 붙잡고, 위로라도 하려는 듯 쓰다듬고 입을 맞추었다. 헤일 부인은 거리낌 없이 울었다. 마침내 부인은 소파에 꼿꼿이 몸을 세워 앉더니 딸 쪽으로 돌아앉아, 눈물 어린, 거의 엄숙하다 할 만한 간절함으로 말했다. “마거릿, 내가 나을 수 있다면, 하느님이 내게 회복할 기회를 주신다면, 그건 반드시 내 아들 프레더릭을 한 번 더 보는 일을 통해서일 게다. 그러면 내게 남은 그 가엾은 건강의 샘이 모두 깨어날 거야.”\n\n부인은 말을 멈추고, 아직 해야 할 무언가를 위해 힘을 그러모으려는 듯했다. 말을 이어 갈 때 부인의 목소리는 메어 있었고, 야릇하면서도 바싹 다가와 있는 어떤 생각을 헤아리기라도 하듯 떨렸다.\n\n“그리고 마거릿, 내가 죽어야 한다면, 몇 주도 지나기 전에 죽도록 정해진 사람들 가운데 하나라면, 나는 먼저 내 아이를 보아야 한다. 그 일을 어떻게 해내야 할지는 나도 모르겠구나. 그렇지만 마거릿, 네가 마지막 병상에서 위로받기를 바라거든, 내 간절히 부탁하니 그 애를 내게 데려와 내가 축복해 줄 수 있게 해 다오. 5분만이라도 좋다, 마거릿. 5분이면 위험할 것도 없겠지. 아, 마거릿, 내가 죽기 전에 그 애를 보게 해 다오!”\n\n마거릿은 이 말에 도무지 이치에 닿지 않는 구석이 있으리라는 생각은 하지 않았다. 죽을병에 든 이들의 정열적인 간청에서 우리는 이치나 논리를 찾지 않는다. 곧 우리 곁을 떠날 이들의 바람을 들어줄 기회를 수없이 흘려보냈다는 기억이 우리를 찌른다. 그리고 그들이 우리 삶의 앞날의 행복을 달라고 한다면, 우리는 그것을 그들 발치에 내려놓고 우리에게서 떠나보내 버린다. 그러나 헤일 부인의 이 바람은 너무나 자연스럽고 너무나 마땅하고 두 사람 모두에게 너무나 옳은 것이어서, 마거릿은 어머니를 위해서만이 아니라 프레더릭을 위해서도 그 사이에 놓인 온갖 위험의 가능성을 눈감아 주고, 그것을 이루기 위해 제 힘이 닿는 모든 일을 하겠다고 스스로 약속해야 할 것 같았다. 크게 뜬 채 애원하는 눈이 애타게 그녀에게 붙박여 흔들림 없이 바라보고 있었다. 그 가엾은 흰 입술은 어린아이의 입술처럼 떨렸지만. 마거릿은 가만히 일어나 연약한 어머니와 마주 섰다. 어머니가 딸의 얼굴에 어린 차분한 굳건함에서 제 소원이 반드시 이루어지리라는 확신을 얻을 수 있도록.\n\n“어머니, 오늘 밤에 편지를 써서 프레더릭 오빠에게 어머니 말씀을 전할게요. 오빠가 곧바로 우리에게 오리라는 걸 저는 제 목숨만큼이나 확신해요. 마음 놓으세요, 어머니. 이 세상에서 약속할 수 있는 한 어머니는 오빠를 보게 되실 거예요.”\n\n“오늘 밤에 쓴다고? 아, 마거릿! 우편은 5시에 나가는데, 그 편으로 부치겠지? 응? 내게 남은 시간이 너무 적구나. 얘야, 네 아버지가 이따금 나를 설득해 희망을 갖게 하시지만, 나는 아무래도 낫지 못할 것 같은 기분이 든다. 곧바로 쓰겠지, 그렇지? 우편 한 번도 놓치지 말아라. 바로 그 한 번 때문에 그 애를 놓칠 수도 있으니.”\n\n“그렇지만 어머니, 아버지가 나가셨는걸요.”\n\n“아버지가 나가셨다고! 그래서 어떻다는 게냐? 아버지가 이 마지막 소원을 내게 거절하시리라는 뜻이냐, 마거릿? 애초에 나를 헬스톤에서 이 병들고 매캐하고 볕 없는 곳으로 데려오지만 않았어도 내가 앓지도, 죽어 가지도 않았을 것을.”\n\n“아, 어머니!” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“그래, 정말 그렇다. 그이도 스스로 알고 있어. 여러 번 그렇게 말했으니까. 그이는 나를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 사람이다. 이 마지막 소원을, 원한다면 마지막 기도라 해도 좋겠구나, 그것을 거절하리라는 뜻은 아니겠지. 그리고 마거릿, 참말이지 프레더릭을 보고 싶은 그리움이 나와 하느님 사이를 가로막고 있단다. 이 한 가지가 이루어지기 전에는 기도를 할 수가 없구나. 정말이지 할 수가 없어. 시간을 놓치지 말아라, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 마거릿. 바로 다음 우편에 부쳐라. 그러면 그 애가 여기 올 수 있어. 22일이면 여기 올 수 있단다! 그 애는 틀림없이 올 테니까. 어떤 밧줄도 사슬도 그 애를 붙들지 못한다. 22일이면 내 아들을 보는 거야.” 부인은 도로 몸을 눕혔고, 잠시 동안 마거릿이 한 손으로 눈을 가린 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있는 것을 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n“안 쓰고 있구나!” 마침내 어머니가 말했다. “펜과 종이를 좀 가져오너라. 내가 직접 써 보마.” 부인은 열에 들뜬 조바심으로 온몸을 떨며 몸을 일으켰다. 마거릿은 눈을 가렸던 손을 내리고 슬프게 어머니를 바라보았다.\n\n“아버지가 들어오실 때까지만 기다려요. 어떻게 하는 게 가장 좋을지 여쭈어보아요, 네?”\n\n“마거릿, 너 15분도 지나기 전에 약속하지 않았니. 그 애가 올 거라고 네가 말했잖니.”\n\n“그럼요, 오빠는 올 거예요, 어머니. 울지 마세요, 사랑하는 우리 어머니. 지금 여기서 쓸게요. 제가 쓰는 걸 보고 계세요. 그리고 바로 이번 우편으로 부칠게요. 아버지가 그러는 게 좋겠다 하시면 들어오셔서 다시 쓰시면 되고요. 하루 늦어질 뿐이잖아요. 아, 어머니, 그렇게 애처롭게 울지 마세요. 제 가슴이 미어져요.”\n\n헤일 부인은 눈물을 그칠 수 없었다. 눈물은 발작하듯 터져 나왔다. 게다가 사실 부인은 그것을 다스리려는 노력조차 하지 않고, 오히려 행복했던 지난날과 있음직한 앞날의 온갖 장면을 불러냈다. 자기가 주검이 되어 누워 있고, 살아생전 그토록 보고 싶어 하던 아들이 그 위에서 울고 있는데 자기는 그가 곁에 있는 줄도 모르는 그림을 그려 가면서. 그러다 자기 연민에 녹아내려 흐느끼며 기진하는 지경에 이르렀고, 마거릿의 가슴은 아팠다. 그러나 마침내 부인은 잠잠해졌고, 딸이 편지를 쓰기 시작하자 게걸스럽게 그 모습을 지켜보았다. 마거릿은 빠르고 다급한 간청을 담아 편지를 썼고, 어머니가 보여 달라고 할까 봐 서둘러 봉했다. 그러고는 더욱 확실히 하려고 헤일 부인이 시키는 대로 손수 우체국에 가지고 갔다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에 아버지가 그녀를 따라잡았다.\n\n“어디에 다녀오는 길이냐, 우리 예쁜 아가씨?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“우체국에요. 편지를 부치러요. 프레더릭 오빠에게 보내는 편지예요. 아, 아버지, 제가 잘못한 것인지도 모르겠어요. 그렇지만 어머니가 오빠를 보고 싶다는 그토록 격한 그리움에 사로잡히셨어요. 그러면 다시 건강해질 거라고 하셨고요. 그러고는 죽기 전에 반드시 오빠를 보아야겠다고 하셨어요. 얼마나 간절하셨는지 말로 다 못 해요! 제가 잘못했나요?”\n\n헤일 씨는 처음에는 대답하지 않았다. 그러다 이렇게 말했다.\n\n“내가 들어올 때까지 기다렸어야지, 마거릿.”\n\n“어머니를 말려 보려고 했어요.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 입을 다물었다.\n\n“나도 모르겠구나.” 헤일 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했다. “그토록 간절히 바란다면 네 어머니는 그 애를 보아야 하겠지. 나는 그것이 의사의 어떤 약보다도 훨씬 더 어머니에게 이로우리라고 믿는다. 어쩌면 아주 일으켜 세울지도 모르지. 그렇지만 그 애가 지는 위험이 아주 크지 않을까 걱정이구나.”\n\n“반란이 있고 나서 그 오랜 세월이 지났는데도요, 아버지?”\n\n“그렇다. 정부로서는 권위를 거스르는 죄를 억누르려고 아주 엄한 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없지. 해군에서는 더욱 그렇다. 지휘관은 부하들의 눈에, 본국에 그를 뒷받침하고 그의 편을 들어 주며 필요하다면 그가 입은 어떤 해라도 갚아 줄 온갖 힘이 있다는 생생한 의식에 둘러싸여 있어야 하니까. 아! 그들에게는 상관들이 얼마나 폭압을 부렸는지, 성마른 성미를 미치도록 긁어 놓았는지 따위는 아무 상관이 없다. 설령 나중에 그것이 얼마쯤 변명이 될 수 있다 하더라도, 처음에는 결코 참작해 주지 않지. 그들은 비용을 아끼지 않는다. 배를 내보내고 바다를 샅샅이 뒤져 범인을 붙잡으려 든다. 세월이 흘러도 그 죄의 기억은 씻기지 않아. 피로 지워질 때까지 해군성 장부에는 그것이 새롭고 생생한 범죄로 남아 있는 게다.”\n\n“아, 아버지, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요! 그런데도 그때는 그게 그토록 옳게 여겨졌어요. 프레더릭 오빠라면 스스로 그 위험을 무릅쓸 거라고 저는 믿어요.”\n\n“그럴 게다. 그래야 하고! 아니, 마거릿, 나는 일이 이렇게 된 것이 기쁘다. 내 손으로는 감히 하지 못했을 일이지만 말이다. 이렇게 되어서 고맙구나. 나였다면 망설이다가 어쩌면 손을 써도 아무 소용이 없을 때가 되어 버렸을지도 모르지. 사랑하는 마거릿, 너는 이 일에서 옳은 일을 했다. 그리고 그 끝은 우리 손에 있지 않아.”\n\n다 좋은 말이었다. 그러나 반란이 얼마나 가차 없이 처벌되는지에 대한 아버지의 이야기는 마거릿을 몸서리치고 오싹하게 만들었다. 제가 오빠를 집으로 꾀어들여, 오빠의 잘못의 기억을 그 피로 지우게 만든 것이라면! 그녀는 나중에 한 아버지의 위로 섞인 말이 나온 자리보다 아버지의 불안이 훨씬 더 깊은 데까지 닿아 있음을 알아보았다. 그녀는 아버지의 팔을 잡고, 생각에 잠긴 채 지쳐서 아버지 곁에서 집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n# 제26장\n\n“나는 그 성스러운 안식의 자리를 찾았노라, 여전히 변함없는 그곳을.” — 헤먼스 부인.\n\n그날 아침 그 집을 나설 때 손턴 씨는 좌절된 격정에 눈이 멀 지경이었다. 마거릿이 여리고 우아한 여인처럼 바라보고 말하고 움직인 것이 아니라 억센 생선 장수 아낙이 되어 주먹으로 호되게 한 대 갈기기라도 한 듯 머리가 어질어질했다. 그는 실제로 몸에 통증을 느꼈다. 격심한 두통과 끊겼다 이어지는 맥박이었다. 소음도, 눈부신 햇빛도, 거리의 끊임없는 덜컹거림과 움직임도 견딜 수 없었다. 이렇게 괴로워하는 자신을 두고 그는 스스로 바보라고 불렀다. 그러면서도 그 순간에는 자기 괴로움의 원인이 무엇인지, 그것이 빚어낸 결과에 걸맞은 것이기는 한지조차 떠올릴 수 없었다. 무슨 억울한 일을 당해 격정의 눈물을 흘리며 길길이 날뛰고 울부짖는 어린아이 곁에, 어느 집 문간 계단에 주저앉아 함께 울 수만 있었다면 차라리 후련했을 것이다. 그는 마거릿이 밉다고 스스로에게 말했지만, 미움을 담은 그 말을 입 모양으로 지어내는 순간에도 사납고 날카로운 사랑의 감각이 번개처럼 그 둔중하고 우레 같은 감정을 갈랐다. 그에게 가장 큰 위안은 자신의 고통을 끌어안는 것, 그리고 실제로 그녀에게 말한 대로 그녀가 자기를 업신여기고 경멸하고 그 도도한 여왕 같은 무관심으로 대할지언정 자기는 털끝만큼도 변하지 않는다고 느끼는 것이었다. 그녀는 그를 변하게 할 수 없었다. 그는 그녀를 사랑했고 앞으로도 사랑할 것이었다. 그리고 그녀에게도, 이 비참한 육신의 고통에도 맞설 것이었다.\n\n그는 이 결심을 굳고 또렷하게 다지려고 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 서 있었다. 마침 승합마차 한 대가 시골 쪽으로 지나가고 있었다. 차장은 그가 자리를 원하는 줄 알고 보도 가까이에 마차를 세웠다. 사과하고 설명하기가 너무 번거로워서 그는 그냥 올라탔고, 그대로 실려 갔다. 길게 늘어선 집들의 줄을 지나고, 그다음에는 잘 손질된 정원이 딸린 외딴 별장들을 지나, 이윽고 진짜 시골의 산울타리에 이르렀고, 얼마 뒤에는 자그마한 시골 읍내에 닿았다. 그러자 모두 내렸고 손턴 씨도 내렸다. 사람들이 걸어가기에 그도 그렇게 했다. 그는 들판으로 들어가 성큼성큼 걸었다. 격한 움직임이 마음을 눅여 주었기 때문이다. 이제 그는 그 일을 전부 떠올릴 수 있었다. 자기가 지었을 딱한 몰골, 세상에서 가장 어리석은 짓이라고 그토록 여러 번 혼자 되뇌며 수긍했던 바로 그 일을 기어이 찾아가서 저지른 그 터무니없는 꼴, 그리고 만약 자기가 그런 바보짓을 저지른다면 반드시 뒤따르리라고 그 분별 있는 순간마다 늘 예언했던 바로 그 결과를 고스란히 맞닥뜨린 일까지. 어제만 해도 자기 어깨에 그토록 가까이 놓여 있던 그 아름다운 눈에, 그 부드럽고 반쯤 벌어진 채 한숨짓던 입에 홀리기라도 했단 말인가? 그녀가 그곳에 있었다는 기억을, 다시는 그런 일이 없을지라도 한때 그녀의 두 팔이 자기를 감쌌다는 기억을 그는 떨쳐 낼 수조차 없었다. 그는 그녀를 언뜻언뜻 볼 뿐이었고, 그녀를 온전히 이해하지는 못했다. 어느 때는 그토록 용감했다가 또 어느 때는 그토록 겁이 많았고, 지금은 그토록 다정했다가 이내 그토록 도도하고 여왕처럼 오만했다. 그러고는 그녀를 끝내 잊어버리자는 심산으로, 여태껏 그녀를 보았던 모든 순간을 하나하나 다시 떠올렸다. 온갖 옷차림의 그녀, 온갖 기분의 그녀를 보았지만 어느 쪽이 그녀에게 가장 어울리는지 알 수 없었다. 오늘 아침만 해도 그녀는 얼마나 당당해 보였던가. 어제 자기 위험을 함께 나누었으니 조금이라도 자기를 마음에 두었으리라는 그 생각에 그녀의 두 눈은 그를 향해 불꽃을 뿜었다!\n\n손턴 씨가 아침에 바보였다면, 그리고 그는 스스로에게 적어도 20번은 그렇다고 다짐했는데, 오후에도 그리 현명해지지는 못했다. 6펜스짜리 승합마차 나들이로 그가 얻은 것이라고는 한층 생생해진 확신뿐이었다. 마거릿 같은 사람은 이제껏 없었고 앞으로도 있을 수 없다는 것, 그녀는 자기를 사랑하지 않으며 앞으로도 사랑하지 않으리라는 것, 그러나 그녀가, 아니 온 세상이 나선다 해도 자기가 그녀를 사랑하는 것만은 결코 막지 못하리라는 확신이었다. 그리하여 그는 그 자그마한 장터로 돌아가 다시 승합마차에 올라 밀턴으로 향했다.\n\n그가 자기 창고 근처에서 내려섰을 때는 늦은 오후였다. 늘 다니던 곳들이 늘 하던 버릇과 생각의 흐름을 되돌려 놓았다. 그는 자기가 해야 할 일이 얼마나 많은지 알고 있었다. 전날의 소동 때문에 평소 일보다 더 많았다. 동료 치안판사들을 만나야 했고, 아침에 절반만 해 둔 채비, 곧 새로 데려온 아일랜드 직공들의 편의와 안전을 위한 채비를 마무리해야 했다. 그들이 밀턴의 불만에 찬 직공들과 접촉할 여지를 아예 없애야 했다. 그리고 마지막으로 집에 돌아가 어머니를 마주해야 했다.\n\n손턴 부인은 하루 종일 식당에 앉아 아들이 헤일 양에게서 승낙을 받았다는 소식을 시시각각 기다렸다. 집 안에서 무슨 소리가 갑자기 날 때마다 그녀는 몇 번이고 몸을 곧추세웠고, 반쯤 떨어뜨렸던 바느질감을 집어 들고는 흐려진 안경 너머로 떨리는 손을 놀려 부지런히 바늘을 움직이곤 했다! 그러고는 여러 번 문이 열렸지만, 대수롭지 않은 심부름으로 들어오는 아무 상관 없는 사람들뿐이었다. 그러면 그녀의 굳은 얼굴은 잿빛 서리 같던 표정에서 풀어져, 그 엄격함에는 그토록 어울리지 않는 낙담한 기색으로 이목구비가 늘어졌다. 그녀는 아들의 혼인이 자기 자신에게 가져올 온갖 쓸쓸한 변화를 곱씹던 생각에서 억지로 몸을 빼내어, 늘 하던 살림살이의 골로 생각을 밀어 넣었다. 곧 혼인할 새 부부에게는 새 리넨 살림이 필요할 터였다. 그래서 손턴 부인은 식탁보와 냅킨이 가득 든 빨래 바구니를 몇 개나 들여오게 해서 세어 보기 시작했다. 자기 것이라 G. H. T.(조지와 해나 손턴)라고 표시된 것과, 아들의 돈으로 산 것이라 아들의 머리글자가 새겨진 것 사이에 얼마쯤 뒤섞임이 있었다. G. H. T.라고 표시된 것 가운데 더러는 옛날식 네덜란드 다마스크였는데 곱기가 이를 데 없었다. 요즘은 그런 것이 없었다. 손턴 부인은 오래도록 서서 그것들을 바라보았다. 갓 혼인했을 적 그녀의 자랑이던 물건들이었다. 이윽고 그녀는 눈살을 찌푸리고 입술을 앙다물어 꼭 오므린 채 조심스레 G. H.를 뜯어냈다. 새 머리글자를 넣으려고 터키레드 표시실을 찾기까지 했지만 실은 다 써 버리고 없었다. 그리고 당장 더 사 오라고 사람을 보낼 마음은 나지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 허공을 물끄러미 바라보았다. 여러 환영이 눈앞을 잇달아 스쳐 지나갔고, 그 모두에서 아들이 으뜸가는, 아니 유일한 대상이었다. 그녀의 아들, 그녀의 자랑, 그녀의 소유물. 그런데도 그는 오지 않았다. 필시 헤일 양과 함께 있으리라. 새로운 사랑이 벌써 그의 마음속 첫자리에서 그녀를 밀어내고 있었다. 무서운 아픔이, 부질없는 질투의 발작이 그녀를 꿰뚫었다. 그것이 몸의 것인지 마음의 것인지 그녀 자신도 잘 알 수 없었으나, 그 때문에 그녀는 주저앉을 수밖에 없었다. 잠시 뒤 그녀는 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하게 다시 일어섰다. 그날 처음으로 그 얼굴에 굳은 미소를 띠고, 문이 열리는 순간을, 그리고 자기 혼인에 어머니가 느끼는 쓰라린 회한을 결코 알아서는 안 될 그 기쁨에 찬 승리자를 맞을 채비를 갖추었다. 이 모든 것에서 장래의 며느리를 한 개인으로 생각하는 마음은 거의 없었다. 그 여자는 존의 아내가 될 사람이었다. 손턴 부인의 자리를 이어 이 집의 안주인이 되는 것은, 그 지고한 영광을 꾸며 주는 풍성한 결과들 가운데 하나일 뿐이었다. 살림의 온갖 넉넉함과 안락, 온갖 자줏빛 옷과 고운 리넨, 명예와 사랑과 순종과 무리 지은 벗들이 왕의 옷에 박힌 보석처럼 자연스레 따라올 것이고, 그 낱낱의 값어치는 그만큼 대수롭지 않게 여겨질 것이었다. 존에게 선택받는다는 것은 부엌데기 하나라도 나머지 온 세상에서 따로 떼어 놓는 일이었다. 게다가 헤일 양은 그리 나쁘지도 않았다. 그 아가씨가 밀턴 처녀였다면 손턴 부인은 진심으로 그녀를 좋아했을 것이다. 그 여자에게는 톡 쏘는 맛이 있었고, 안목과 기개와 풍미가 있었다. 물론 딱하게도 편견에 사로잡혀 있고 몹시 무지하기는 했다. 그러나 그것은 남부에서 자란 탓이니 그러려니 할 일이었다. 손턴 부인의 마음속에서는 패니를 그녀와 견주어 보는 언짢은 비교가 야릇하게 이어졌다. 그래서 그녀는 그날따라 딸에게 모질게 말했고 대놓고 나무랐다. 그러고는 속죄하는 셈으로 헨리의 주석서를 집어 들고 거기에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애썼다. 자기가 자랑스러워하던 일, 곧 식탁 리넨을 살펴보는 일을 계속하는 대신에.\n\n드디어 그의 발소리! 그녀는 자기가 한 문장을 다 읽어 가고 있다고 여기는 동안에도, 눈은 정말로 그 문장을 훑고 있었고 기억은 그것을 한 마디 한 마디 기계적으로 되뇔 수도 있었지만, 그가 현관문으로 들어서는 소리를 들었다. 예민해진 감각은 움직임의 소리 하나하나를 읽어 냈다. 이제 그는 모자걸이 앞에 있고, 이제 바로 방문 앞에 있다. 왜 멈춰 섰을까? 최악이 무엇이든 어서 알려 다오.\n\n그런데도 그녀의 머리는 책 위에 숙인 채였다. 그녀는 고개를 들지 않았다. 그가 탁자 가까이 다가와 그 자리에 가만히 서서, 어머니가 분명 몰두해 있는 듯한 그 문단을 다 읽기를 기다렸다. 그녀는 애써 고개를 들었다. “그래, 존?”\n\n그는 그 짧은 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았다. 그러나 그는 마음을 다잡아 둔 터였다. 농담으로 대답하고 싶었고, 가슴속 쓰디쓴 것이 농담 하나쯤 뱉어 낼 수도 있었으나, 어머니는 그보다 나은 대접을 받을 분이었다. 그는 어머니 뒤로 돌아가 자기 표정이 보이지 않게 한 다음, 그 잿빛의 돌 같은 얼굴을 뒤로 젖혀 입을 맞추며 중얼거렸다.\n\n“저를 사랑하는 사람은 아무도 없습니다. 저를 아껴 주는 사람은 어머니뿐이에요.”\n\n그는 돌아서서 벽난로 선반에 머리를 기댄 채 서 있었고, 사내다운 두 눈에 눈물이 밀려 나왔다. 그녀는 일어섰다. 그러다 휘청했다. 평생 처음으로 그 강인한 여자가 휘청거렸다. 그녀는 아들의 어깨에 두 손을 얹었다. 키가 큰 여자였다. 그녀는 아들의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 아들이 자기를 보게 만들었다.\n\n“어미의 사랑은 하느님이 주신 것이다, 존. 그것은 영원토록 굳게 붙들어 준다. 계집아이의 사랑은 한 줄기 연기 같아서 바람 따라 변하는 법이야. 그래, 그 애가 너를 마다했구나, 내 아들아. 그렇지?” 그녀는 이를 악물었다. 개처럼 입 끝에서 끝까지 이를 드러내 보였다. 그는 고개를 저었다.\n\n“저는 그분에게 걸맞지 않습니다, 어머니. 걸맞지 않다는 것을 알고 있었어요.”\n\n그녀는 앙다문 이 사이로 말을 갈아 냈다. 그는 어머니가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 수 없었다. 그러나 두 눈의 표정이 그것을 저주라고 일러 주었다. 말씨는 그처럼 상스럽지 않을지언정 그 속뜻은 여태 입 밖에 나온 어떤 저주 못지않게 모진 저주였다. 그러면서도 아들이 다시 자기 것이 되었다는 것을 알자 그녀의 가슴은 가볍게 뛰어올랐다.\n\n“어머니!” 그가 다급하게 말했다. “저는 그분을 헐뜯는 말은 한 마디도 들을 수 없습니다. 저를 봐주세요, 봐주세요! 제 쓰라린 마음은 몹시 약해져 있습니다. 저는 아직도 그분을 사랑합니다. 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑합니다.”\n\n“나는 그 애가 밉다.” 손턴 부인이 낮고 사나운 목소리로 말했다. “그 애가 너와 나 사이에 서 있을 때는 미워하지 않으려고 애썼다. 저 애가 내 아들을 행복하게 해 주겠지, 하고 스스로에게 말했으니까. 그리고 나는 그 일을 위해서라면 내 심장의 피라도 내어 줄 테니까. 하지만 이제는 네 비참함 때문에 그 애가 밉다. 그래, 존, 아픈 가슴을 내게 숨겨 봐야 소용없다. 나는 너를 낳은 어미고 네 슬픔은 곧 내 고통이야. 네가 그 애를 미워하지 않겠다면 내가 미워하마.”\n\n“그러시면, 어머니, 저는 그분을 더 사랑하게 됩니다. 어머니가 그분을 부당하게 대하시니 제가 저울을 고르게 맞춰야지요. 그런데 우리가 왜 사랑이니 미움이니 하는 이야기를 합니까? 그분은 저를 마음에 두지 않으십니다. 그거면 충분합니다. 아니, 넘치지요. 이 이야기는 다시는 입에 올리지 맙시다. 이 일에서 어머니가 저를 위해 해 주실 수 있는 일은 그것뿐입니다. 다시는 그분 이름을 부르지 맙시다.”\n\n“그러자꾸나, 얼마든지. 나는 다만 그 애도, 그 애에게 딸린 사람들도 모두 저희가 온 곳으로 쓸려 돌아가기만을 바랄 뿐이다.”\n\n그는 일이 분쯤 더 가만히 서서 불을 들여다보았다. 아들을 바라보는 그녀의 마르고 흐릿하던 두 눈에 여느 때 없던 눈물이 차올랐다. 그러나 그가 다음 말을 꺼냈을 때 그녀는 여느 때처럼 험상궂고 조용해 보였다.\n\n“공모 혐의로 세 사람에게 영장이 나왔습니다, 어머니. 어제의 폭동이 파업을 무너뜨리는 데 한몫했습니다.”\n\n그리고 마거릿의 이름은 손턴 부인과 아들 사이에서 두 번 다시 입에 오르지 않았다. 두 사람은 늘 하던 대화 방식으로 되돌아갔다. 사실에 대한 이야기였지 의견에 대한 것이 아니었고, 감정에 대한 것은 더더욱 아니었다. 두 사람의 목소리와 어조는 차분하고 차가웠다. 낯선 사람이라면 그 자리를 떠나면서, 그렇게 가까운 사이에 그토록 싸늘하게 무심한 태도는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했을 것이다.\n\n# 제27장\n\n“단순한 마음과 도리로 바치는 것이라면 그 무엇도 잘못될 수 없나니.” — 한여름 밤의 꿈.\n\n손턴 씨는 이튿날의 온갖 용무 속으로 곧장, 또렷한 정신으로 파고들었다. 완제품에 약간의 수요가 일었고, 그것이 자기 업종에 영향을 미치는 터라 그는 그 기회를 붙들어 야무진 흥정을 붙였다. 동료 치안판사들의 회합에도 시간에 딱 맞추어 나타나, 강인한 분별력과 결과를 한눈에 꿰뚫어 보고 신속하게 결단을 내리는 능력으로 최선의 도움을 주었다. 그보다 나이 많은 이들, 이 도시에서 오래 자리를 지켜 온 이들, 그보다 훨씬 큰 재산을 — 그의 것은 모두 사업에 묶인 유동 자본이었던 반면 저들의 것은 이미 실현되어 토지로 바뀐 재산이었다 — 가진 이들이 그에게서 즉각적이고 준비된 지혜를 구했다. 경찰을 만나 일을 조율하고 필요한 모든 절차를 앞장서 밟도록 위임받은 사람도 그였다. 그런데 그는 저들이 무심결에 보내는 그 경의를, 커다랗고 높다란 굴뚝에서 곧게 솟는 연기를 겨우 비스듬히 밀어낼까 말까 한 부드러운 서풍만큼도 마음에 두지 않았다. 자기에게 바쳐지는 그 말 없는 존경을 그는 알아차리지도 못했다. 만약 알아차렸더라면, 그는 그것을 자기가 마음에 둔 목적으로 나아가는 길의 걸림돌로 여겼을 것이다. 실제로 그는 오직 그 목적을 신속히 이루는 것만을 바라보고 있었다. 이러이러한 분이 손턴 씨를 얼마나 높이 평가하는지, 그가 그 자리에 없었더라면 일이 아주 다르게 — 정말이지 아주 고약하게 — 흘러갔으리라는 이야기를, 저 치안판사들과 부자들의 부녀자들에게서 게걸스레 빨아들인 것은 그의 어머니의 귀였다. 그날 그는 좌우로 일을 척척 쓸어치웠다. 어제의 그 깊은 굴욕과 그 뒤 몇 시간 동안의 얼이 빠진 목적 없는 방황이 그의 지력에서 안개를 죄다 걷어내 준 듯했다. 그는 자기 힘을 느꼈고 그 안에서 마음껏 즐겼다. 자기 심장마저 거의 물리칠 수 있을 것 같았다. 스스로 알았더라면 그는 디 강가에 살던 방앗간 주인의 노래를 불렀을 것이다.\n\n“나는 누구도 아랑곳하지 않고, 누구도 나를 아랑곳하지 않네.”\n\n바우처를 비롯한 폭동 주모자들에 대한 증언이 그의 앞에서 채택되었다. 나머지 세 사람에 대한 공모 혐의 증언은 무산되었다. 그러나 그는 경찰에게 엄중히 감시를 지시했다. 잘못이 입증되는 즉시 법의 날랜 오른팔이 내리칠 채비를 갖추고 있어야 했으니까. 그러고 나서 그는 자치구 법정의 후끈하고 퀴퀴한 방을 나와, 한결 신선하지만 여전히 후텁지근한 거리로 나섰다. 그 순간 그는 단번에 무너져 내린 듯했다. 어찌나 맥이 풀렸는지 생각을 다스릴 수가 없었다. 생각은 자꾸만 그녀에게로 흘러갔다. 생각은 그 장면을 되살려 냈다 — 어제 그가 퇴짜를 맞고 거절당한 장면이 아니라, 그 전날의 눈빛과 몸짓을. 그는 붐비는 거리를 기계적으로 걸어가며 사람들 사이를 요리조리 헤집었으나 아무도 보지 못했다. 그 반 시간, 그녀가 그에게 매달리고 그녀의 심장이 그의 심장에 맞부딪히던 그 짧은 한때가 한 번만 더 돌아왔으면 하는 그리움으로 그는 병이 날 지경이었다.\n\n“아니, 손턴 씨! 나를 아주 쌀쌀맞게 지나치시는구려. 어머님은 안녕하시고? 날씨 한번 훌륭하지요! 우리 의사들이야 이런 날씨를 반기지 않소만!”\n\n“이거 실례했습니다, 도널드슨 선생님. 정말 못 뵈었습니다. 어머니는 아주 잘 계십니다, 감사합니다. 좋은 날씨지요, 추수에도 좋았으면 합니다. 밀만 잘 거둬들이면 내년에는 거래가 활발할 겁니다, 선생님네 의사들 사정이야 어떻든 말이지요.”\n\n“그래요, 그래. 저마다 제 앞가림이지. 당신네한테 나쁜 날씨와 나쁜 시절이 나한테는 좋은 시절이오. 장사가 안 될 때면 당신들 밀턴 사람들 사이에서 건강이 좀먹고 죽을 채비가 되어 가는 일이 당신이 아는 것보다 훨씬 많이 벌어진다오.”\n\n“저는 아닙니다, 선생님. 저는 쇠로 만들어졌으니까요. 제가 겪은 최악의 악성 부채 소식을 들었을 때도 맥박 하나 흐트러진 적이 없습니다. 밀턴에서 누구보다도, 햄퍼보다도 저를 더 괴롭히는 이번 파업조차 제 입맛 근처에도 오지 못합니다. 환자를 구하시려면 다른 데를 알아보셔야겠습니다, 선생님.”\n\n“그러고 보니 당신이 나한테 좋은 환자를 하나 소개해 주었지요, 가엾은 부인! 이렇게 매정한 투로만 말하지 않으려니 하는 얘긴데, 나는 진심으로 헤일 부인이 — 아시죠, 크램프턴에 사는 그 부인 말이오 — 몇 주도 못 넘길 거라고 봅니다. 전에도 말했지만 나는 애초에 나을 가망을 본 적이 없어요. 그런데 오늘 진찰을 하고 나니 정말 좋지 않아 보입니다.”\n\n손턴 씨는 말이 없었다. 그토록 자랑하던 맥박의 흔들림 없음이 한순간 그를 저버렸다.\n\n“제가 뭐든 할 수 있는 일이 있겠습니까, 선생님?” 그가 달라진 목소리로 물었다. “선생님도 아시겠지만 — 보시면 아시겠지만, 그 댁 형편이 그리 넉넉지 않습니다. 부인께 꼭 있어야 할 편의품이나 별미 같은 것이 있을까요?”\n\n“없소.” 의사가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. “과일을 몹시 탐하기는 합디다 — 열이 끊이질 않으니까요. 하지만 자르고넬 배면 무엇 못지않게 좋을 텐데, 시장에 널려 있는 물건이오.”\n\n“제가 할 수 있는 일이 있으면 꼭 말씀해 주시겠지요.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “선생님만 믿겠습니다.”\n\n“아, 걱정 마시오! 당신 지갑을 아껴 줄 생각은 없으니까 — 두둑한 줄 다 아오. 내 환자들 전부와 그들이 원하는 것 전부에 대해 백지 위임장이나 하나 써 주었으면 좋겠구려.”\n\n그러나 손턴 씨에게는 두루 미치는 자비심도, 만인을 향한 박애도 없었다. 그에게 강한 애정이 있다고 인정해 줄 사람조차 몇 없었을 것이다. 그런데도 그는 밀턴에서 제일 좋은 과일 가게로 곧장 가서, 고운 분이 가장 여리게 서린 자줏빛 포도 한 송이와, 빛깔이 가장 짙은 복숭아와, 가장 싱싱한 포도 잎을 골랐다. 그것들은 바구니에 담겼고, 가게 주인은 자기가 던진 물음의 답을 기다렸다. “어디로 보내 드릴까요, 나리?”\n\n대답이 없었다. “말버러 공장으로 보내면 되겠지요, 나리?”\n\n“아니오!” 손턴 씨가 말했다. “바구니를 내게 주시오. 내가 들고 가겠소.”\n\n바구니는 두 손으로 들어야 했다. 게다가 그는 여인네들이 물건을 사러 나오는, 시내에서 가장 붐비는 구역을 지나야 했다. 그가 아는 젊은 아가씨들 여럿이 돌아서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며, 그가 짐꾼이나 심부름꾼처럼 손을 놀리고 있는 것이 이상하다고 생각했다.\n\n그는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. “그 여자 생각 때문에 내가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 일을 그만두지는 않겠다. 나는 이 과일을 저 가엾은 어머니에게 가져다 드리고 싶고, 그렇게 하는 것이 그저 옳은 일이다. 그 여자가 나를 업신여긴다고 해서 내가 원하는 일을 못 하게 되지는 않는다. 도도한 처녀 하나가 무서워서 내가 좋아하는 사람에게 친절 하나 베풀지 못한다면, 그거야말로 우스운 노릇이 아닌가! 나는 헤일 씨를 위해 이 일을 하는 것이다. 나는 그 여자에게 맞서서 이 일을 하는 것이다.”\n\n그는 여느 때 아닌 걸음걸이로 걸어 이내 크램프턴에 닿았다. 계단을 두 칸씩 뛰어올라, 딕슨이 그를 알릴 겨를도 없이 응접실로 들어섰다. 얼굴은 상기되고 두 눈은 따뜻한 진심으로 빛나고 있었다. 헤일 부인은 열에 달아오른 채 소파에 누워 있었다. 헤일 씨는 소리 내어 책을 읽고 있었다. 마거릿은 어머니 곁 낮은 걸상에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다. 이 만남에서 그의 심장은 어땠는지 몰라도 그녀의 심장은 파닥거렸다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 아랑곳하지 않았고, 헤일 씨조차 거의 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그는 바구니를 들고 곧장 헤일 부인에게로 가서, 건장하고 정정한 남자가 쇠약한 병자에게 말할 때 쓰면 그토록 마음을 울리는 그 낮고 부드러운 어조로 말했다.\n\n“도널드슨 선생을 만났는데 과일이 부인께 좋겠다고 하기에, 제 눈에 좋아 보이는 것을 좀 가져다 드리는 무례를 — 큰 무례를 — 저질렀습니다.” 헤일 부인은 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다. 반가움에 몸이 다 떨릴 지경이었다. 헤일 씨는 말수는 적었으나 더 깊은 고마움을 나타냈다.\n\n“접시를 가져오너라, 마거릿. 바구니든 무엇이든.” 마거릿은 탁자 옆에 일어서 있었다. 움직이거나 소리를 내어 손턴 씨에게 자기가 이 방에 있다는 것을 일깨울까 봐 절반쯤 겁이 났던 것이다. 둘이 서로를 의식하며 맞부딪히게 되는 것은 양쪽 모두에게 거북하리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그리고 처음에는 자기가 낮은 자리에 앉아 있었고 지금은 아버지 뒤에 서 있으니, 그가 서두르는 통에 자기를 못 보고 지나쳤으려니 짐작했다. 마치 그의 눈길이 한 번도 그녀에게 머물지 않았다 해서 그가 온몸으로 그녀의 존재를 느끼지 않기라도 하는 것처럼!\n\n“가 봐야겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “더 머물 수가 없군요. 이 무례를 — 제 거친 방식을 — 너무 갑작스러웠지요, 그것을 용서해 주신다면, 다음번에는 좀 더 점잖게 하겠습니다. 눈에 띄는 좋은 과일이 있거든 다시 가져다 드리는 즐거움을 허락해 주십시오. 안녕히 계십시오, 헤일 씨. 안녕히 계십시오, 부인.”\n\n그는 가 버렸다. 마거릿에게는 한마디도, 한 번의 눈길도 없이. 그녀는 그가 자기를 보지 못했다고 믿었다. 그녀는 말없이 접시를 가지러 갔고, 가늘고 고운 손가락 끝으로 과일을 조심스레 들어냈다. 이것을 가져다준 것은 참 고마운 일이었다. 더구나 어제 그런 일이 있고 나서!\n\n“아! 어쩌면 이렇게 맛있을까!” 헤일 부인이 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다. “나를 생각해 주다니 얼마나 고마운 분이니! 마거릿아, 이 포도를 좀 맛보렴! 참 고마운 분이지 않니?”\n\n“네!” 마거릿이 조용히 말했다.\n\n“마거릿!” 헤일 부인이 다소 투정 섞인 투로 말했다. “너는 손턴 씨가 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 마음에 들어 하지 않는구나. 그렇게 치우친 사람은 내 평생 처음 본다.”\n\n헤일 씨는 아내를 위해 복숭아 껍질을 벗기고 있었다. 그러고는 자기 몫으로 한 조각을 잘라 내면서 말했다.\n\n“내게 무슨 편견이 있었더라도, 이렇게 맛난 과일을 선물로 받으면 죄다 녹아 없어질 걸세. 이런 과일은 소년 시절 이후로 먹어 본 적이 없어 — 아니, 햄프셔에서도 못 먹어 봤지. 하기야 소년들에게는 무슨 과일이든 다 맛있는 법이겠지만. 나는 야생 자두며 돌사과를 아주 맛나게 먹던 기억이 나는구나. 마거릿, 고향 집 정원 서쪽 담 모퉁이에 얽히고설켜 있던 까치밥나무 덤불을 기억하니?”\n\n어찌 기억하지 못하겠는가? 그 낡은 돌담에 남은 비바람 자국 하나하나를, 지도처럼 담을 얼룩덜룩 물들이던 잿빛과 노란빛 이끼를, 틈새마다 자라던 자그마한 쥐손이풀을 어찌 잊었겠는가? 그녀는 지난 이틀 동안의 사건들로 뒤흔들려 있었다. 지금 그녀의 삶 전체가 그녀의 굳센 마음을 짓누르는 짐이었다. 그런데 어쩐 일인지, 옛날의 햇살 가득하던 시절의 기억을 건드리는 아버지의 이 무심한 말들이 그녀를 벌떡 일어서게 만들었고, 그녀는 바느질감을 바닥에 떨어뜨린 채 서둘러 방을 나가 자기의 자그마한 방으로 들어갔다. 첫 흐느낌이 목을 메우도록 몸을 내맡기기가 무섭게 그녀는 딕슨이 자기 서랍장 앞에 서서 무엇인가를 뒤지고 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n“어머나, 아가씨! 깜짝 놀랐네요! 마님이 더 나빠지신 건 아니지요? 무슨 일이라도 있어요?”\n\n“아니, 아무 일도 없어. 그냥 내가 바보 같아서 그래, 딕슨. 물 한 잔 줘요. 뭘 찾고 있어요? 그 서랍에는 내 모슬린이 들어 있는데.”\n\n딕슨은 말없이 계속 뒤졌다. 라벤더 향이 흘러나와 방 안에 퍼졌다.\n\n마침내 딕슨은 찾던 것을 찾아냈다. 그것이 무엇인지 마거릿은 볼 수 없었다. 딕슨은 몸을 홱 돌려 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“제가 뭘 찾았는지 말씀드리기가 싫습니다요. 아가씨는 이미 애태울 일이 넘치는데, 이 일로도 마음 졸이실 게 뻔하니까요. 밤에나, 아니면 그런 때쯤에나 말씀드리려고 했었는걸요.”\n\n“무슨 일이에요? 부탁이니 지금 당장 말해 줘요, 딕슨.”\n\n“아가씨가 보러 다니시는 그 젊은 여자 말이에요 — 히긴스네 말입니다요.”\n\n“그런데요?”\n\n“글쎄, 오늘 아침에 죽었답니다. 그 애 동생이 와 있어요. 이상한 부탁을 하러 왔지 뭐예요. 죽은 그 젊은 여자가 아가씨 물건 하나를 걸치고 묻히고 싶어 했다나 봐요. 그래서 동생이 그걸 얻으러 온 거예요. 저는 남 줘도 아깝지 않을 만한 나이트캡을 찾고 있던 참이고요.”\n\n“아! 내가 찾을게.” 마거릿이 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. “가엾은 베시! 다시는 못 볼 줄은 생각도 못 했는데.”\n\n“그런데 말이죠, 또 한 가지가 있어요. 아래층에 있는 그 애가 아가씨한테 여쭤 봐 달라던데요, 언니를 보러 가시겠느냐고요.”\n\n“하지만 이미 죽었잖아!” 마거릿이 조금 창백해지며 말했다. “나는 죽은 사람을 본 적이 없어. 아니! 나는 안 보는 게 낫겠어.”\n\n“아가씨가 들어오시지만 않았어도 여쭙지도 않았을 거예요. 그 애한테는 안 가실 거라고 말해 두었고요.”\n\n“내가 내려가서 그 애하고 얘기할게.” 마거릿이 말했다. 딕슨의 모진 말투가 그 가엾은 소녀에게 상처를 줄까 봐 걱정이 되었던 것이다. 그래서 모자를 손에 든 채 그녀는 부엌으로 갔다. 메리의 얼굴은 울어서 온통 부어 있었고, 마거릿을 보자 다시 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“아이고, 아가씨, 우리 언니가 아가씨를 사랑했다우, 사랑했다우, 정말이라니까요!” 그리고 한참 동안 마거릿은 그 애에게서 이 말 말고는 아무 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 마침내 마거릿의 동정과 딕슨의 다그침이 몇 가지 사실을 끄집어냈다. 니컬러스 히긴스는 아침에 나갔는데, 그때 베시는 전날과 다름없이 괜찮아 보였다. 그런데 한 시간 만에 상태가 나빠졌다. 이웃 사람이 메리가 일하는 곳으로 달려갔다. 아버지가 어디 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 메리는 언니가 숨을 거두기 겨우 몇 분 전에야 들어섰다.\n\n“하루 이틀쯤 전에 언니가 아가씨 물건 뭐라도 하나 걸치고 묻히고 싶다고 했다우. 아가씨 얘기라면 아무리 해도 지칠 줄을 몰랐구먼요. 여태 눈에 담아 본 사람 중에 아가씨가 제일 어여쁘다고 늘 말했지요. 언니는 아가씨를 끔찍이 좋아했다우. 마지막으로 한 말이 ‘아가씨한테 내 다정한 문안 전해 드려라, 그리고 아버지 술 못 드시게 해라’였어요. 아가씨도 와서 언니를 봐 주시구려. 언니가 알면 굉장한 영광으로 여겼을 거라우, 내 알지요.”\n\n마거릿은 대답하기를 조금 꺼렸다.\n\n“그래, 아마 갈 수 있을 거야. 그래, 갈게. 차 마시기 전에 가마. 그런데 너희 아버지는 어디 계시니, 메리?”\n\n메리는 고개를 젓고는 가려고 일어섰다.\n\n“헤일 아가씨.” 딕슨이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “그 가엾은 것 염해 놓은 걸 보러 가신들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 그게 저 애한테 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 저야 한마디도 반대하지 않겠어요. 저 애 마음이 풀린다면 제가 대신 가는 것도 조금도 마다하지 않고요. 이런 상것들은 그저 그게 죽은 이한테 예를 갖추는 거라는 생각뿐이거든요. 얘야.” 그녀가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. “내가 가서 네 언니를 보마. 헤일 아가씨는 바빠서 못 가신단다. 안 그러면 가셨을 텐데.”\n\n소녀는 아쉬운 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았다. 딕슨이 와 주는 것도 영광일 수는 있었으나, 언니가 살아 있는 동안 언니와 저 젊은 아가씨 사이의 다정함에 자잘한 질투를 느껴 온 그 가엾은 동생에게 그것은 결코 같은 일이 아니었다.\n\n“아니에요, 딕슨!” 마거릿이 단호하게 말했다. “내가 갈 거예요. 메리, 오늘 오후에 나를 보게 될 거야.” 그러고는 자기 안의 비겁함이 두려워, 마음을 바꿀 어떤 여지도 스스로에게 남기지 않으려고 그 자리를 떠났다.\n\n# 제28장\n\n“십자가를 지나 면류관으로! 네 영혼의 삶을 이루 다 말할 수 없는 시련들이 거인의 힘으로 덮쳐 온다 해도, 기운을 내어라! 기운을 내어라! 쓰라린 싸움은 머잖아 끝나고, 너는 마침내 그리스도와 함께 평화 속에 다스리리라.” — 코제가르텐.\n\n“참으로 그러하옵니다. 평안할 때에는 스스로 너무 강하다 여겨 그 길에서 당신이 필요하지 않다 하지요. 그러나 재앙이 닥치면, ‘하느님’을 부르짖지 않는 영혼은 벙어리가 되고 맙니다.” — 브라우닝 부인.\n\n그날 오후 그녀는 히긴스네 집으로 빠르게 걸어갔다. 메리가 반쯤 미심쩍어하는 얼굴로 그녀를 기다리고 있었다. 마거릿은 그 눈을 들여다보며 안심시키려 미소 지었다. 두 사람은 거실을 서둘러 지나 위층으로, 죽은 이가 누운 고요한 자리로 들어갔다. 그제야 마거릿은 오기를 잘했다고 여겼다. 그토록 자주 고통에 지쳐 있고 어지러운 생각으로 뒤척이던 그 얼굴에 이제는 영원한 안식의 희미하고 부드러운 미소가 어려 있었다. 마거릿의 눈에 눈물이 천천히 고였으나, 깊은 평온이 그녀의 영혼으로 들어왔다. 이것이 죽음이었다! 삶보다 더 평화로워 보였다. 아름다운 성경 말씀들이 모두 마음에 떠올랐다. “그들은 수고를 그치고 쉬리라.” “고단한 이들이 안식에 들었다.” “그가 사랑하시는 자에게는 잠을 주시는도다.”\n\n마거릿은 천천히, 아주 천천히 침대에서 몸을 돌렸다. 메리가 뒤쪽에서 조용히 흐느끼고 있었다. 두 사람은 한마디도 없이 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n니컬러스 히긴스는 식탁에 손을 짚은 채 방 한가운데 서 있었다. 안뜰을 지나오는 길에 여러 부산한 입에서 들은 소식에 그 커다란 눈이 놀라 크게 벌어져 있었다. 눈은 메마르고 사나웠다. 딸의 죽음이 정말인지 헤아리며, 그 자리가 다시는 그 애를 알아보지 못하리라는 것을 스스로 납득시키려 애쓰는 눈이었다. 딸이 하도 오래 병약한 채 죽어 가고 있었던 터라, 그는 딸이 죽지 않으리라고, “이겨 낼” 것이라고 스스로를 설득해 두었던 것이다.\n\n마거릿은 자기가 그곳에 있을 자격이 없다고 느꼈다. 아버지인 그가 이제야 막 알게 된 죽음의 자리를 자기가 먼저 스스럼없이 익혀 버렸으니 말이다. 그를 처음 보았을 때 가파르고 굽은 계단에서 잠깐 걸음이 멎었지만, 이제 그녀는 넋 나간 그의 시선을 몰래 지나쳐, 그를 집안의 슬픔이라는 엄숙한 울타리 안에 남겨 두고 나가려 했다.\n\n메리는 눈에 띄는 첫 의자에 주저앉아 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 울기 시작했다.\n\n그 소리에 그가 정신을 차린 듯했다. 그는 갑자기 마거릿의 팔을 붙들고, 말이 나올 때까지 놓지 않았다. 목이 바싹 마른 모양이었다. 말은 걸걸하고 막히고 쉰 소리로 올라왔다.\n\n“댁도 그 애 곁에 있었소? 그 애가 죽는 걸 봤소?”\n\n“아니요!” 마거릿은 이제 자기가 그의 눈에 띄었음을 알고, 더없이 참을성 있게 가만히 선 채 대답했다. 그가 다시 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸으나, 그동안에도 그녀의 팔은 놓지 않았다.\n\n“사람은 다 죽는 법이지.” 마침내 그가 이상하게 묵직한 어조로 말했는데, 마거릿은 그 말투에서 처음으로 그가 술을 마셨구나 하는 생각이 들었다. 취할 만큼은 아니되 생각을 어지럽힐 만큼은 마신 것이었다. “허나 그 애는 나보다 어렸잖소.” 그는 여전히 그 일을 곱씹으며, 마거릿을 꽉 붙든 채로도 그녀를 보지는 않았다. 그러다 문득 사납게 캐묻는 눈길로 그녀를 올려다보았다. “정말로 틀림없이 죽은 거요, 혼절한 것도 까무러친 것도 아니고? 전에도 자주 그랬는데.”\n\n“돌아가셨어요.” 마거릿이 대답했다. 그의 손아귀가 팔을 아프게 죄었고 흐리멍덩한 눈에 사나운 빛이 스쳤지만, 그에게 말하는 것이 두렵지는 않았다.\n\n“돌아가셨어요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 여전히 캐묻는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 바라보는 사이 그 눈빛은 서서히 사그라드는 듯했다. 그러더니 그는 갑자기 마거릿을 놓아 주고, 몸을 던지듯 식탁에 반쯤 엎어져 격렬한 흐느낌으로 식탁을, 방 안의 온갖 세간을 뒤흔들었다. 메리가 떨면서 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n“저리 가! 저리 가라니까!” 그는 딸을 향해 마구잡이로, 앞도 보지 않고 손을 휘두르며 소리쳤다. “네깟 게 나한테 무슨 소용이야?” 마거릿은 메리의 손을 잡아 자기 손안에 가만히 감쌌다. 그는 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 딱딱한 나무에 머리를 찧더니, 기진하여 넋을 놓고 늘어졌다. 그래도 딸과 마거릿은 움직이지 않았다. 메리는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨었다.\n\n마침내, 15분쯤이었을 수도 있고 한 시간쯤이었을 수도 있었는데, 그가 몸을 일으켰다. 눈은 붓고 핏발이 서 있었고, 누가 곁에 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다. 지켜보고 선 두 사람을 보자 그는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 그는 몸을 무겁게 한 번 털고, 두 사람에게 다시 한 번 뚱한 눈길을 던지고는, 한마디도 없이 문 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n“아, 아버지, 아버지!” 메리가 그의 팔에 매달리며 말했다. “오늘 밤만은 안 돼요! 다른 날은 몰라도 오늘 밤은 안 돼요. 아, 좀 도와주세요! 아버지가 또 술 마시러 나가려고 해요! 아버지, 저는 안 놓을 거예요. 때리셔도 안 놓을 거예요. 그 애가 맨 마지막으로 나한테 아버지 술 못 드시게 하라고 했단 말이에요!”\n\n그러나 마거릿이 문간에 서 있었다. 말은 없어도 위엄이 서린 자세였다. 그가 대드는 눈으로 그녀를 올려다보았다.\n\n“여긴 내 집이오. 비켜서시오, 아가씨. 안 그러면 내가 비키게 만들 테니!” 그는 메리를 거칠게 떨쳐 냈고, 마거릿도 칠 기세였다. 그러나 그녀는 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않았고, 깊고 진지한 눈을 그에게서 떼지 않았다. 그는 침울한 사나움으로 그녀를 마주 노려보았다. 그녀가 손이나 발을 조금이라도 움직였더라면, 그는 의자에 부딪혀 얼굴에서 피가 흐르는 제 딸에게 쓴 것보다 더한 힘으로 그녀를 밀쳐 냈을 것이다.\n\n“왜 그런 눈으로 날 보는 거요?” 마침내 그가 그녀의 엄한 침착함에 기가 꺾이고 눌려 물었다. “그 애가 댁을 좋아했다고 해서, 그것도 오라고 청한 적도 없는 내 집에서 내가 가고 싶은 길을 못 가게 막을 셈이면 잘못 짚었소. 사내한테 하나 남은 위안에 가지도 못하게 하다니 너무 모진 일이오.”\n\n마거릿은 그가 자기 힘을 인정했음을 느꼈다. 다음으로 무엇을 해야 하나? 그는 문 가까이 놓인 의자에 앉아 있었다. 반쯤은 굴복하고 반쯤은 분해하며, 그녀가 자리를 뜨는 즉시 나가려는 심산이면서도, 채 5분도 전에 을러댔던 그 폭력은 쓰고 싶지 않은 것이었다. 마거릿은 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다.\n\n“저와 함께 가세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “가서 그 애를 보세요!”\n\n그녀가 말한 목소리는 아주 낮고 엄숙했다. 그러나 그 목소리에는 그에 대한, 또 그가 따르리라는 것에 대한 두려움이나 의심이 조금도 담겨 있지 않았다. 그는 뚱하니 일어섰다. 그는 완고한 망설임을 얼굴에 띤 채 우두커니 서 있었다. 그녀는 그 자리에서 그를 기다렸다. 그가 움직일 때가 되기를 조용히, 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 그는 그녀를 기다리게 하는 데서 묘한 즐거움을 느꼈으나, 마침내 계단 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그와 그녀는 주검 곁에 섰다.\n\n“그 애가 메리에게 남긴 마지막 말은 ‘아버지 술 못 드시게 해라’였어요.”\n\n“이제 와서 그게 그 애를 아프게 하진 않소.” 그가 중얼거렸다. “이제 그 무엇도 그 애를 아프게 못 하지.” 그러다 목소리를 높여 울부짖듯 이어 갔다. “우린 다투고 틀어지기도 하고, 화해하고 다시 좋아지기도 하고, 뼈만 남도록 굶기도 하겠지만, 우리 온갖 설움 가운데 어느 하나도 이제 다시는 그 애한테 닿지 못하오. 제 몫은 이미 다 치렀으니. 처음엔 고된 일, 나중엔 병, 개 같은 삶을 살았지. 그러고는 평생에 기뻐할 일 하나 제대로 알지도 못한 채 죽었소! 아니야, 아가씨, 그 애가 무슨 말을 했든 이제 그 애는 아무것도 모르오. 그러니 나는 이 설움에 몸을 가누려면 한 모금 마셔야겠소.”\n\n“안 됩니다.” 마거릿은 누그러진 그의 태도에 따라 부드러워지며 말했다. “그러시면 안 돼요. 그 애의 삶이 정말 그러했다면, 적어도 그 애는 남들처럼 죽음을 두려워하지는 않았어요. 아, 그 애가 다가올 삶에 대해, 하느님 안에 감추어진 삶, 이제 그 애가 간 그 삶에 대해 말하는 걸 들으셨어야 했는데.”\n\n그는 고개를 저으며 곁눈으로 마거릿을 올려다보았다. 창백하고 초췌한 그 얼굴이 그녀의 마음을 아프게 찔렀다.\n\n“몹시 시달리셨군요. 하루 종일 어디 계셨어요? 일하러 가신 건 아니고요?”\n\n“일하러 간 건 아니지, 암.” 그가 짧고 험한 웃음을 지으며 말했다. “댁이 말하는 그런 일 말이오. 나는 위원회에 있었소. 멍청이들한테 이치를 알아듣게 하려다 넌더리가 날 때까지 말이오. 오늘 아침 일곱 시도 되기 전에 바우처 마누라한테 불려 갔소. 자리보전을 하고 누웠으면서도 제 서방 그 대가리 빈 짐승이 어디 갔느냐고 악을 쓰고 길길이 뛰더군. 내가 그자를 맡아 두기라도 한 것처럼, 그자가 내 말을 들어먹을 위인이라도 되는 것처럼! 우리 계획을 죄다 망쳐 놓은 그 빌어먹을 멍청이가! 그러고는 법이 우리를 겨누고 있는 판에 만나 주려 하지도 않는 사람들을 찾아다니느라 발이 부르트도록 걸었소. 그리고 발보다 더한 게 마음이 아팠소. 술 한잔 사겠다는 벗을 만났다 한들, 그 애가 여기서 죽어 가는 줄 내가 어찌 알았겠소. 베스, 얘야, 너는 나를 믿어 주겠지, 그렇지 않으냐?” 그는 말 못 하는 가여운 형체를 향해 사납게 하소연하며 돌아섰다.\n\n“틀림없이,” 마거릿이 말했다. “틀림없이 모르셨을 거예요. 아주 갑작스러웠으니까요. 하지만 이제는 다르지요. 이제는 아시잖아요. 그 애가 저기 누워 있는 걸 보고 계시잖아요. 그 애가 마지막 숨으로 한 말도 들으셨고요. 안 나가실 거죠?”\n\n대답이 없었다. 사실, 그가 위안을 어디서 찾을 수 있었겠는가?\n\n“저희 집으로 같이 가세요.” 마침내 그녀가 대담하게 나서며, 그 말을 하는 스스로에게 반쯤 떨면서 말했다. “적어도 든든한 음식은 드실 수 있을 거예요. 그게 꼭 필요하실 테니까요.”\n\n“댁 아버지가 목사요?” 그가 생각을 홱 돌려 물었다.\n\n“그러셨지요.” 마거릿이 짧게 말했다.\n\n“청하시니 가서 차나 한 잔 마시겠소. 목사한테 한번 말해 보고 싶던 게 여럿 있었소. 지금 설교를 하고 있든 말든 나는 개의치 않으니.”\n\n마거릿은 난감했다. 손님이 오리라고는 전혀 대비가 없을 아버지와 그가 차를 마신다는 것, 어머니는 저리 편찮으신데, 도무지 될 법한 일이 아니었다. 그러나 여기서 물러선다면 전보다 더 나빠질 터, 그를 술집으로 몰아넣는 것이나 다름없었다. 그녀는 그를 집까지만 데려갈 수 있다면 그것만으로도 큰 걸음을 뗀 셈이니, 그다음은 일이 되어 가는 대로 맡겨 보자고 생각했다.\n\n“잘 가라, 우리 딸! 우린 마침내 갈라섰구나, 그래! 허나 너는 태어난 날부터 네 아비한테 복이었다. 네 하얀 입술에 복이 있기를, 얘야. 지금 그 입술에 웃음이 어렸구나! 나야 이제 영영 외롭고 쓸쓸하겠지만, 그걸 한 번 더 보게 되어 기쁘다.”\n\n그는 몸을 굽혀 딸에게 다정히 입을 맞추고, 얼굴을 덮은 뒤 마거릿을 따라 나서려 돌아섰다. 그녀는 서둘러 아래층으로 내려가 메리에게 그렇게 하기로 했다고 일러 두었다. 아버지를 술집에서 떼어 놓을 방도로 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 곳이 그것뿐이었다고, 메리도 함께 가자고 채근했다. 정 많은 그 가여운 아이를 혼자 두고 간다는 생각에 마음이 찔렸던 것이다. 그러나 메리는 이웃에 벗들이 있어 와서 함께 앉아 있어 줄 거라고, 자기는 괜찮다고 했다. 다만 아버지가—\n\n그가 곁에 와 있지 않았더라면 메리는 더 말했을 것이다. 그는 자기 감정을 떨쳐 낸 참이었다. 마치 한때 거기에 무너졌다는 것이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯이. 게다가 도가 지나칠 만큼 스스로를 몰아붙여, 솥 밑에서 가시나무가 타닥거리는 것 같은 씁쓸한 흥까지 꾸며 냈다.\n\n“나는 저 아가씨 아버지랑 차를 마시러 간다, 암.”\n\n그러나 그는 거리로 나서면서 모자를 눈썹까지 푹 눌러썼고, 마거릿 곁에서 터벅터벅 걷는 동안 오른쪽도 왼쪽도 보지 않았다. 동정하는 이웃들의 말에, 그보다도 그 눈길에 마음이 무너질까 두려웠던 것이다. 그리하여 그와 마거릿은 말없이 걸었다.\n\n그녀가 사는 줄로 아는 거리에 가까워지자, 그는 자기 옷과 손과 신발을 내려다보았다.\n\n“아무래도 몸을 좀 씻고 왔어야 했는데.”\n\n그러는 편이 분명 나았을 것이나, 마거릿은 뒤뜰에 들어가도 좋고 비누와 수건을 내어 주겠다고 그를 안심시켰다. 지금 이 순간 그를 손아귀에서 놓칠 수는 없었다.\n\n그가 하녀를 따라 복도를 지나 부엌을 통과하며, 더러운 발자국을 감추려고 유포 무늬의 어두운 자국마다 조심조심 발을 디디는 동안, 마거릿은 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 층계참에서 딕슨과 마주쳤다.\n\n“어머니는 어떠세요? 아버지는 어디 계시고요?”\n\n마님은 지치셔서 방으로 들어가셨다고 했다. 자리에 누우려 하셨지만 딕슨이 소파에 기대어 계시라고, 차도 거기로 가져다 드리겠다고 설득했다는 것이다. 너무 오래 자리에 누워 있다가 안절부절못하시는 것보다는 그편이 나을 터였다.\n\n거기까지는 다행이었다. 그런데 헤일 씨는 어디 계신가? 응접실에 계셨다. 마거릿은 서둘러 전해야 할 이야기로 숨이 반쯤 찬 채 들어갔다. 물론 이야기는 온전치 못했다. 아버지는 자기 조용한 서재에서 자기를 기다리고 있다는, 함께 차를 마셔야 한다는, 그리고 마거릿이 애타게 두둔하고 있는 그 술 취한 직조공이라는 생각에 적잖이 “당황”했다. 온순하고 마음씨 고운 헤일 씨였다면 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 기꺼이 위로하려 했겠지만, 하필 마거릿이 가장 힘주어 말한 대목이 그가 술을 마셨다는 것, 그리고 그를 술집에서 떼어 놓을 마지막 방편으로 집까지 데려왔다는 것이었다. 사소한 일들이 하도 자연스럽게 꼬리를 물고 이어진 터라, 마거릿은 아버지 얼굴에 스치는 옅은 혐오의 빛을 보고서야 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 겨우 깨달았다.\n\n“아, 아버지! 처음에만 놀라지 않으시면, 정말이지 아버지가 싫어하실 사람이 아니에요.”\n\n“하지만 마거릿, 술 취한 사람을 집에 데려오다니. 어머니는 저렇게 편찮으신데!”\n\n마거릿의 얼굴이 어두워졌다. “죄송해요, 아버지. 아주 조용한 분이에요. 전혀 취하지 않았어요. 처음에만 좀 이상했을 뿐인데, 그것도 가엾은 베시가 죽은 충격 때문일 거예요.” 마거릿의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 헤일 씨는 애원하는 그 어여쁜 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고 이마에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“괜찮다, 얘야. 내가 가서 될 수 있는 대로 편히 해 주마. 너는 어머니를 돌봐 드려라. 다만 네가 서재에 들어와 셋이 함께해 준다면 나야 기쁘겠구나.”\n\n“아, 네, 고마워요, 아버지.” 그러나 헤일 씨가 방을 나서려 할 때 그녀가 뒤쫓아 갔다.\n\n“아버지, 저분이 하는 말에 놀라지 마세요. 저분은 무신—, 그러니까 우리가 믿는 것을 그리 믿지 않는 분이에요.”\n\n“저런! 술 취한 무신론자 직조공이라니!” 헤일 씨는 낙담하여 혼잣말을 했다. 그러나 마거릿에게는 이렇게만 말했다. “어머니가 잠드시거든 곧바로 내려오너라.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니 방으로 들어갔다. 헤일 부인이 선잠에서 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n“프레더릭한테 언제 편지를 썼지, 마거릿? 어제였니, 그저께였니?”\n\n“어제요, 어머니.”\n\n“어제, 그래서 편지는 부쳤고?”\n\n“네. 제가 직접 가지고 갔어요.”\n\n“아, 마거릿, 그 애가 온다니 얼마나 무서운지! 혹시라도 알아보는 사람이 있으면! 붙잡히기라도 하면! 그 오랜 세월 멀리 떨어져 무사히 살았는데 끝내 처형이라도 당하면! 자꾸 깜빡 잠이 들면 그 애가 붙잡혀 재판받는 꿈을 꾼단다.”\n\n“아, 어머니, 두려워 마세요. 위험이야 어느 정도 있겠지요. 하지만 저희가 할 수 있는 데까지 줄일 거예요. 게다가 위험은 아주 적어요! 지금 저희가 헬스톤에 있었다면 스무 배, 백 배는 됐을걸요. 거기서는 다들 오빠를 기억할 테고, 집에 낯선 사람이 있다는 걸 알면 틀림없이 프레더릭이라고 짐작할 거예요. 그런데 여기서는 저희가 무얼 하든 눈여겨볼 만큼 저희를 알거나 관심 두는 사람이 아무도 없잖아요. 딕슨이 용처럼 문을 지킬 거예요. 그렇죠, 딕슨? 오빠가 여기 있는 동안 말이에요.”\n\n“저를 지나쳐 들어온다면 여간내기가 아니겠죠!” 딕슨이 그런 생각만으로도 이를 드러내며 말했다.\n\n“게다가 오빠는 어스름 말고는 나갈 일도 없잖아요, 가여운 사람.”\n\n“가여운 것!” 헤일 부인이 따라 말했다. “그래도 네가 편지를 쓰지 않았더라면 싶구나. 다시 편지를 쓴다면 그 애를 말리기에 너무 늦었을까, 마거릿?”\n\n“늦었을 것 같아요, 어머니.” 마거릿이 말했다. 어머니가 살아 계실 때 뵙고 싶거든 곧바로 오시라고 얼마나 다급하게 애원했는지를 떠올리면서.\n\n“나는 그렇게 서둘러 일을 하는 게 늘 싫더구나.” 헤일 부인이 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 말이 없었다.\n\n“자, 마님.” 딕슨이 밝은 목소리로 짐짓 단호하게 말했다. “프레더릭 도련님을 뵙는 게 세상 무엇보다 마님이 바라시던 일이잖습니까요. 저는 마거릿 아가씨가 우물쭈물하지 않고 곧장 편지를 써 보내신 게 다행이라고 생각합니다요. 저도 제가 쓰고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았거든요. 그리고 도련님은 저희가 꼭꼭 숨겨 드릴 테니 믿으세요. 이 집에서 급할 때 도련님을 구하려고 힘써 주지 않을 사람은 마사뿐인데, 저는 바로 그때 그 애를 제 어미한테 다녀오라고 보낼까 생각하고 있었습니다요. 그 애가 한두 번 가고 싶다고 했거든요. 여기 온 뒤로 어미가 중풍을 맞았다니까요. 다만 차마 청하지를 못했던 게지요. 도련님이 언제 오시는지 알게 되는 대로 제가 그 애를 무사히 내보낼 궁리를 하겠습니다요. 하느님이 도련님을 지켜 주시기를! 그러니 마님, 마음 편히 차나 드시고 저만 믿으세요.”\n\n헤일 부인은 마거릿보다 딕슨을 더 믿었다. 딕슨의 말이 한동안 그녀를 가라앉혔다. 마거릿은 말없이 차를 따르며 무언가 기분 좋은 이야깃거리를 떠올리려 애썼다. 그러나 그녀의 생각은 달 속 사내가 낫에서 내려가 달라고 했을 때의 대니얼 오루크처럼 대꾸했다. 청하면 청할수록 우리는 더 안 움직인다고. 프레더릭이 처하게 될 위험 말고 다른 무엇이든 떠올리려 애쓸수록, 그녀의 상상은 눈앞에 떠오른 그 불행한 생각에 더욱 바싹 달라붙었다. 어머니는 딕슨과 재잘거리며, 프레더릭이 재판을 받고 처형될 수도 있다는 것을 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 어머니의 바람으로, 비록 마거릿의 손을 빌려서지만, 아들이 이 위험 속으로 불려 왔다는 것도 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 어머니는 로켓이 불꽃을 뿌리듯 무시무시한 가능성과 참담한 개연성과 온갖 불운한 우연을 내던지는 부류의 사람이었다. 그런데 그 불꽃이 불붙기 쉬운 것에 떨어지면, 처음에는 속으로만 타다가 끝내 끔찍한 불길로 터져 나오는 법이다. 마거릿은 딸의 도리를 다정하고 조심스레 마친 뒤 서재로 내려갈 수 있게 되자 마음이 놓였다. 아버지와 히긴스가 어떻게 지내고 있을지 궁금했다.\n\n우선, 예의 바르고 마음씨 곱고 소박하고 구식인 그 신사는 자기 자신의 세련되고 정중한 태도로 상대 안에 잠들어 있던 온갖 예의를 저도 모르게 불러내 놓았다.\n\n헤일 씨는 모든 사람을 똑같이 대했다. 신분 때문에 차이를 두어야 한다는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그는 니컬러스에게 의자를 내주었고, 자기 청에 따라 니컬러스가 앉을 때까지 서 있었다. 그리고 “술 취한 무신론자 직조공”이 익숙해져 있던 퉁명스러운 “니컬러스”나 “히긴스” 대신 한결같이 “히긴스 씨”라고 불렀다. 그러나 니컬러스는 습관적인 술꾼도 아니었고 철저한 무신론자도 아니었다. 그는 자기 말로 하자면 근심을 잠재우려고 마셨을 뿐이고, 마음과 영혼을 다해 매달릴 만한 신앙의 형태를 아직 찾지 못했다는 점에서만 무신론자였다.\n\n마거릿은 아버지와 히긴스가 진지한 대화를 나누고 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐고 무척 기뻤다. 의견이 아무리 부딪쳐도 둘은 서로에게 부드럽고 정중하게 말하고 있었다. 씻고 매무새를 다듬은 채, 비록 펌프 물통에서였을 뿐이지만, 조용조용 말하는 니컬러스는 제 집 화롯가의 투박한 자립 속에서만 그를 보아 온 그녀에게 새로운 사람이었다. 그는 찬물로 머리를 착 붙여 빗어 넘겼고, 목수건을 매만졌으며, 남는 양초 토막을 빌려 나막신을 문질러 광을 내 놓았다. 그러고는 거기 앉아, 물론 짙은 다크셔 사투리로였지만, 낮춘 목소리와 얼굴에 어린 착실하고 진지한 침착함으로 아버지에게 어떤 의견을 힘주어 펴고 있었다. 아버지 또한 상대의 말에 흥미를 느끼고 있었다. 아버지는 그녀가 들어오자 돌아보며 미소 짓고는 조용히 자기 의자를 내주었다. 그러고는 되도록 빨리 다시 앉으며, 이야기를 끊은 데 대해 손님에게 가볍게 고개 숙여 사과했다. 히긴스는 인사 삼아 그녀에게 고개를 끄덕였고, 그녀는 바느질감을 조용히 탁자 위에 놓고 들을 채비를 했다.\n\n“아까 말씀드린 대로요, 선생님, 만일 선생님이 여기서 사셨다면, 여기서 나고 자라셨다면 믿음이란 게 그리 크지 않으셨을 거요. 말을 잘못 쓰거든 용서하시고. 지금 내가 믿음이라 하는 건, 본 적도 없는 사람들이 본 적도 없는 것들과 삶에 대해 해 놓은 말이며 격언이며 약속을 곱씹는 걸 두고 하는 소리요. 아무도 본 적이 없는 것들 말이오. 그런데 선생님은 그게 참된 것이고 참된 말이고 참된 삶이라 하시지. 나는 그저 묻겠소. 증거가 어디 있소? 내 둘레에는 나보다 지혜롭고 나보다 몇 곱절 배운 이가 수두룩하오. 그런 것들을 생각할 짬이 있던 사람들 말이오. 나야 밥벌이에 짬을 다 바쳐야 했지만. 그래, 나는 그 사람들을 보오. 그 삶이 내게 훤히 드러나 있소. 진짜 사람들이오. 그이들은 성경을 믿지 않소. 천만에. 격식으로야 믿는다고들 하겠지. 허나 선생님, 그이들이 아침에 눈뜨자마자 첫마디로 ‘영생을 얻으려면 무엇을 할까?’ 한다고 보시오? 아니면 ‘오늘 이 좋은 날 내 지갑을 채우려면 무엇을 할까? 어디로 갈까? 어떤 흥정을 붙일까?’ 하겠소? 지갑과 금과 지폐는 진짜요. 만질 수 있고 손에 잡히는 것이오. 그게 실물이지. 영생이란 건 죄다 말뿐이고, 그거야 딱 어울리는 게, 용서하시오, 선생님, 선생님은 일자리 없는 목사시라지. 원! 나와 같은 처지에 놓인 사람을 두고 함부로 말하지는 않겠소. 다만 한 가지만 더 여쭙겠소, 선생님. 대답은 바라지 않소. 그저 눈에 보이는 것만 믿는 우리를 바보요 얼간이라 못 박기 전에, 담뱃대에 담아 한번 피워 보시란 말이오. 구원이며 내세며 그 밖의 것들이 참말이라면, 사람들 입으로가 아니라 마음속 깊은 데서 참말이라면, 그이들이 정치경제학을 두고 그러듯 그 이야기로 우리 귀를 때리지 않겠소? 그이들은 그 지혜 나부랭이를 우리한테 들이밀려고 안달이 났소. 허나 그게 참말이라면 저쪽이야말로 더 큰 회심이 아니겠소.”\n\n“하지만 공장주들은 당신들의 신앙과 아무 상관이 없지요. 그들이 당신들과 얽혀 있는 것은 오직 장사뿐이니까요, 그들 생각으로는 말입니다. 그러니 그들이 당신들의 견해를 바로잡아야 할 영역도 장사의 학문뿐인 셈이고요.”\n\n“선생님이 ‘그들 생각으로는’이라고 덧붙여 주시니 다행이오.” 히긴스가 묘하게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. “그러지 않으셨다면 나는 선생님을 위선자로 여겼을지 모르겠소. 목사시라도, 아니 오히려 목사시니까 더욱. 보시오, 만일 선생님이 종교를 두고, 그게 참말이라 해도 이 온 세상 무엇보다 앞서 모든 사람이 모든 사람에게 힘주어 알릴 일은 아니라는 식으로 말씀하셨다면, 나는 선생님을 목사질을 하는 사기꾼으로 여겼을 거요. 그리고 나는 선생님을 사기꾼으로 여기느니 바보로 여기는 편이 낫소. 언짢게 듣지 마시오, 선생님.”\n\n“조금도요. 당신은 나를 그르다 여기고, 나는 당신을 훨씬 더 치명적으로 그르다 여기지요. 하루 만에, 한 번의 대화로 당신을 설득하리라고는 바라지 않습니다. 다만 서로 알아 가고 이런 것들에 대해 서로 거리낌 없이 말합시다. 그러면 진리가 이길 겁니다. 그것을 믿지 않는다면 나는 하느님도 믿지 않을 겁니다. 히긴스 씨, 다른 무엇을 버렸든 당신은 믿고 계시리라 믿습니다.” (헤일 씨의 목소리가 경건하게 낮아졌다.) “당신은 그분을 믿고 계시지요.”\n\n니컬러스 히긴스가 갑자기 꼿꼿이, 뻣뻣하게 일어섰다. 마거릿도 벌떡 일어섰다. 씰룩거리는 그의 얼굴을 보고 발작이라도 일으키는 줄 알았던 것이다. 헤일 씨가 당황하여 딸을 바라보았다. 마침내 히긴스가 말을 찾아냈다.\n\n“이보시오! 나를 시험한다고 당신을 땅바닥에 메어꽂을 수도 있소. 무슨 권리로 당신 의심으로 나를 떠보는 거요? 저기 누워 있는 그 애를 생각해 보시오. 그런 삶을 살고 간 그 애를. 그러고 나서 생각해 보시오, 당신이 나한테 남은 단 하나뿐인 위안을 어떻게 빼앗으려는지를. 하느님이 계시고 그분이 그 애한테 그 삶을 정해 주셨다는 그 위안 말이오. 나는 그 애가 다시 살아나리라고는 믿지 않소.” 그가 자리에 앉으며, 아랑곳하지 않는 불더러 하는 말처럼 침울하게 이어 갔다. “그 애가 그토록 시달리고 끝없이 근심하며 살았던 이 삶 말고 다른 삶이 있다고는 믿지 않소. 허나 그게 다 우연들이 모인 것뿐이어서 바람 한 줄기에도 달라질 수 있었다고는 차마 생각 못 하겠소. 내가 하느님을 안 믿는다고 여긴 적도 여러 번 있었지. 허나 남들처럼 그걸 말로 똑바로 내놓아 본 적은 한 번도 없소. 그리하는 사람들을 비웃어 준 적은 있겠지, 배짱 좋은 척하느라고. 허나 그러고 나서는 늘 둘레를 둘러보았소. 그분이 계신다면 그분이 내 말을 들으셨을까 하고. 그런데 오늘, 이렇게 홀로 남겨진 날에, 나는 당신 물음도 당신 의심도 듣지 않겠소. 이 비틀거리는 세상에서 흔들리지 않고 고요한 건 오직 하나뿐이오. 이치가 서든 안 서든 나는 거기 매달리겠소. 복 받은 사람들이야 얼마든지”——\n\n마거릿이 그의 팔을 아주 부드럽게 건드렸다. 그녀는 그때까지 말이 없었고, 그는 그녀가 일어서는 소리도 듣지 못했다.\n\n“니컬러스, 저희는 따지려는 게 아니에요. 아버지를 오해하셨어요. 저희는 따지지 않아요, 믿을 뿐이지요. 당신도 그렇고요. 이런 때에 그것이 단 하나뿐인 위안이에요.”\n\n그는 돌아서서 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다. “옳소! 그렇소, 그렇소.” (손등으로 눈물을 훔치며.) “허나 아시겠지만, 그 애가 집에 죽어 누워 있소. 나는 설움에 넋이 반쯤 나갔고, 이따금 내가 무슨 소리를 하는지도 잘 모르겠소. 남들이 해 놓은 말들이, 그때는 영리하고 그럴싸하다 여겼던 말들이, 이제 내 가슴이 다 터져 버린 판에 되살아나는 것 같소. 파업도 무너졌소. 그건 아시오, 아가씨? 나는 거지꼴로 그 애한테 그 시름을 좀 달래 달라고 하려고 집으로 오던 참이었소. 그런데 그 애가 죽었다고, 방금 죽었다고 일러 주는 사람한테 얻어맞은 셈이지. 그뿐이오. 허나 그것으로 내겐 충분했소.”\n\n헤일 씨는 코를 풀고는, 북받치는 마음을 감추려고 일어나 촛불 심지를 잘랐다. “저이는 무신론자가 아니로구나, 마거릿. 어떻게 그런 말을 했느냐?” 그가 나무라듯 중얼거렸다. “욥기 14장을 읽어 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같구나.”\n\n“아직은 아니에요, 아버지. 어쩌면 아예 하지 마세요. 파업에 대해 여쭤보고, 저분이 필요로 하는, 가엾은 베시에게서 얻으려 했던 위로를 다 드리기로 해요.”\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 묻고 들었다. 일꾼들의 셈은, 너무 많은 공장주들의 셈이 그렇듯, 잘못된 전제 위에 서 있었다. 그들은 동료 인간을 마치 기계처럼 계산 가능한 힘만 지닌 존재로, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 것으로 셈했다. 바우처와 폭동을 일으킨 자들의 경우처럼 인간의 격정이 이성을 이겨 버릴 여지는 조금도 두지 않았다. 그리고 자기들이 입은 피해를 알리기만 하면 멀리 있는 낯선 이들에게도 그 피해가, 실제든 상상이든, 자기들에게 일으킨 것과 똑같은 효과를 낳으리라 믿었다. 그래서 그들은 자기 자리를 대신하려고 실려 오도록 스스로를 내맡긴 가여운 아일랜드 사람들에게 놀라고 분개했다. 이 분개는 “그 아일랜드 것들”에 대한 멸시로, 또 그들이 서투르게 일을 벌여 무지와 아둔함으로 새 주인들을 곤란하게 만들리라는 생각의 즐거움으로 얼마쯤 누그러졌다. 그런 이야기가 벌써 이상하게 부풀려진 채 온 도시에 퍼지고 있었다. 그러나 무엇보다 모진 상처는 밀턴 일꾼들 자신에게서 온 것이었다. 무슨 일이 있어도 평온을 지키라는 조합의 명령을 거스르고 어긴 자들, 진영 안에 불화를 일으키고 법이 자기들을 겨누고 있다는 공포를 퍼뜨린 자들이었다.\n\n“그래서 파업은 끝났군요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“그렇소, 아가씨. 살고 볼 일이지. 내일이면 일자리를 달라고 몰려드는 사람들을 다 들이려고 공장 문을 활짝 열어젖혀야 할 거요. 우리가 제대로 된 재목이었더라면 지난 10년 동안 닿아 본 적 없는 데까지 삯을 끌어올렸을 그 조치와 자기들은 아무 상관이 없었노라 보이려는 것뿐이라 해도 말이오.”\n\n“일자리는 얻으시겠지요?” 마거릿이 물었다. “이름난 일꾼이시잖아요?”\n\n“햄퍼가 제 오른손을 잘라 내면 그때는 나를 제 공장에서 일하게 해 주겠지. 그전에도, 그 뒤에도 아니고.” 니컬러스가 조용히 말했다. 마거릿은 할 말을 잃고 서글퍼졌다.\n\n“삯 이야기 말인데요.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “언짢게 듣지 마시고요, 내 생각에 당신들은 안타까운 잘못을 좀 저지르는 것 같습니다. 내가 가진 책에 나오는 대목을 좀 읽어 드리고 싶군요.” 그는 일어나 책장으로 갔다.\n\n“수고하실 것 없소, 선생님.” 니컬러스가 말했다. “그이들 책 나부랭이는 한 귀로 들어와 한 귀로 나가오. 나는 도무지 알아먹지 못하겠소. 햄퍼와 내가 갈라서기 전에, 십장이 그이한테 내가 사람들을 부추겨 삯을 더 달라 하게 만든다고 일렀소. 그래서 햄퍼가 어느 날 마당에서 나를 만났지. 손에 얇은 책 한 권을 들고서 이러더군. ‘히긴스, 자네는 달라고만 하면 삯을 더 받을 수 있고, 억지로 올려놓으면 그게 유지되기까지 한다고 여기는 그 빌어먹을 바보 가운데 하나라던데. 자, 자네한테 기회를 한 번 주지. 자네한테 머리라는 게 있는지 보세. 여기 내 친구가 쓴 책이 있네. 이걸 읽으면 삯이란 게 공장주도 일꾼도 손댈 것 없이 제 수준을 찾아간다는 걸 알게 될 걸세. 다만 일꾼들이 저 못난 얼간이들답게 파업으로 제 목을 긋는 일만 없다면 말이지.’ 자, 선생님, 목사시고 설교를 해 오셨고 사람들을 옳다고 여기는 길로 데려오려 애써 보신 분이니 여쭙겠소. 선생님은 먼저 그들을 바보니 뭐니 부르는 것으로 시작하셨소, 아니면 그들이 들을 마음이 생기고 될 수 있으면 수긍하도록 처음에는 오히려 좋은 말을 해 주셨소? 그리고 설교하시면서 이따금 멈추어, 반은 그들에게 반은 스스로에게 이렇게 말씀하셨소? ‘허나 너희가 워낙 바보 천지라, 내가 이치를 넣어 주려 애써 봐야 소용없지 싶구나.’ 나도 햄퍼의 친구가 하는 말을 받아들일 만한 마음 상태는 아니었소. 그건 인정하오. 말이 그렇게 나오니 어찌나 부아가 나던지. 허나 그래도 생각했소. ‘그래, 이 양반들이 무슨 소리를 하는지 보자. 얼간이가 그이들인지 나인지 가려 보자.’ 그래서 책을 들고 씨름했소. 헌데 원, 자본이니 노동이니, 노동이니 자본이니 하는 소리가 끝없이 이어져 그만 잠이 들어 버립디다. 나는 끝내 어느 것이 어느 것인지 머릿속에 제대로 붙잡지 못했소. 게다가 그것들을 무슨 미덕이나 악덕처럼 이야기하더군. 내가 알고 싶은 건 사람의 권리였는데 말이오. 부자든 가난뱅이든, 사람이기만 하면 누리는 그 권리 말이오.”\n\n“그럼에도 불구하고,” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “햄퍼 씨가 친구의 책을 권하며 당신에게 말한 그 방식이 무례하고 어리석고 그리스도인답지 못하다는 것을 남김없이 인정하더라도, 만일 그 책이 그가 말한 대로 삯은 제 수준을 찾아간다고, 그리고 아무리 성공한 파업이라도 그 삯을 잠깐 밀어 올릴 뿐이며 바로 그 파업 때문에 나중에는 훨씬 더 크게 떨어진다고 말했다면, 그 책은 당신에게 진실을 말한 셈입니다.”\n\n“글쎄올시다, 선생님.” 히긴스가 다소 완고하게 말했다. “그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠지. 그 점을 매듭짓자면 두 가지 의견이 맞서니까. 허나 그게 갑절로 굳센 진실이라 칩시다. 내가 그걸 받아들이지 못한다면 내게는 진실이 아니오. 선생님 책장에 있는 저 라틴어 책에도 큰 진실이 들었겠지. 허나 낱말 뜻을 모르는 내게는 진실이 아니라 횡설수설일 뿐이오. 만일 선생님이든 다른 아는 것 많고 참을성 있는 분이든 내게 와서 그 낱말들이 무슨 뜻인지 가르쳐 주겠다고, 내가 좀 아둔하거나 한 가지가 다른 것에 어떻게 걸려 있는지 잊어버려도 나무라지 않겠다고 하면, 그야 시간이 가면 나도 그 진실을 볼 수 있게 되겠지. 못 볼 수도 있고. 내가 끝내 어느 누구와 똑같이 생각하게 되리라고 장담하지는 않겠소. 그리고 나는 주물공장 사내들이 철판을 오려 내듯 진실을 말로 깔끔하고 말끔하게 잘라 낼 수 있다고 여기는 사람이 아니오. 같은 뼈다귀라도 아무나 다 삼키지는 못하오. 이 사람 목에도 걸리고 저 사람 목에도 걸리지. 그건 그렇다 치고, 삼켜 넘겼다 해도 이 사람에겐 너무 세고 저 사람에겐 너무 약할 수 있소. 제 진실로 세상을 고쳐 보겠다고 나선 사람들은 서로 다른 마음에 맞게 다르게 지어 주어야 하고, 건네는 방식도 좀 다정해야 하오. 안 그러면 그 가엾은 병든 바보들이 그이들 얼굴에 도로 뱉어 낼 테니. 그런데 햄퍼는 먼저 내 귀싸대기를 한 대 올려붙이고, 그러고서 제 큼직한 알약을 던지고는, 내가 하도 바보라 아무 소용도 없겠다만 어쨌든 여기 있다고 하더란 말이오.”\n\n“공장주들 가운데 가장 마음씨 곱고 지혜로운 몇 사람이 당신들 가운데 몇 사람과 만나 이런 것들에 대해 제대로 이야기를 나누었으면 좋겠군요. 그것이야말로 당신들의 어려움을 넘어서는 가장 좋은 길일 겁니다. 그 어려움은, 정말이지 내가 보기에는, 공장주와 일꾼 양쪽 모두의 이익을 위해 양쪽 모두가 잘 알아야 할 문제들에 대한 당신들의 무지에서 나오는 것이니까요. 실례했습니다, 히긴스 씨. 혹시” (반은 딸에게) “손턴 씨라면 그런 일을 해 볼 마음이 들지 않을까?”\n\n“잊지 마세요, 아버지.” 그녀가 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “언젠가 그분이 하신 말씀 말이에요. 정부에 관한 것 있잖아요.” 그녀는 일하는 사람들을 다스리는 방식, 곧 사람들에게 스스로를 다스릴 만한 지성을 주는 길이냐 공장주 쪽의 지혜로운 전제냐를 두고 나누었던 대화를 더 또렷이 들추고 싶지 않았다. 히긴스가 그 말 전부는 아니라도 손턴 씨의 이름만은 알아들었음을 눈치챘기 때문이다. 과연 그가 손턴 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n“손턴! 그자가 바로 그 아일랜드 것들을 데려오라고 대뜸 편지를 써 보내, 파업을 망친 그 폭동을 불러온 자요. 햄퍼도 그 온갖 윽박지름에도 얼마쯤은 기다렸을 텐데, 손턴은 말 떨어지기 무섭게 후려치는 자란 말이오. 그런데 이제, 조합이 바우처와 우리 명령을 정면으로 어긴 그 작자들을 끝까지 쫓아 준 데 대해 고마워했을 판에, 앞으로 나서서 태연히 말하기를, 파업이 끝났으니 피해 당사자로서 폭동을 일으킨 자들에 대한 고발을 밀어붙이고 싶지 않다는 거요. 나는 그자가 제 뜻을 관철해서 드러내 놓고 앙갚음할 줄 알았소. 그런데 이러더란 말이오. 법정에 있던 사람이 그자가 한 말 그대로 일러 줬소. ‘그들은 이미 잘 알려져 있소. 그들은 일자리를 얻는 데 겪을 어려움에서 제 행실의 자연스러운 벌을 찾게 될 것이오. 그것으로 충분히 가혹하오.’ 나는 다만 그자들이 바우처를 붙잡아 햄퍼 앞에 세웠으면 싶소. 그 늙은 호랑이가 그자를 물고 늘어지는 꼴이 눈에 선하오! 그자가 놓아주었겠소? 어림도 없지!”\n\n“손턴 씨가 옳았어요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “니컬러스, 당신은 바우처에게 화가 나 있어요. 그렇지 않다면, 자연스러운 벌만으로도 그 잘못에 충분히 가혹한 자리에서 그 이상의 벌은 앙갚음이나 다름없다는 걸 누구보다 먼저 알아보셨을 텐데요.”\n\n“내 딸은 손턴 씨와 그리 사이가 좋지 않지요.” 헤일 씨가 마거릿을 보고 미소 지으며 말했다. 그러는 동안 그녀는 카네이션처럼 새빨개져 갑절로 부지런히 바느질을 하기 시작했다. “하지만 나는 저 애 말이 옳다고 믿습니다. 그래서 그분이 마음에 들고요.”\n\n“저기, 선생님, 이번 파업은 내게 참 진저리 나는 일이었소. 그러니 몇 안 되는 사람들 때문에 그게 무너지는 걸 보고 내가 좀 부아가 났다 해도 이상할 것 없지 않소. 잠자코 견디지 못하고, 씩씩하고 굳세게 버텨 내지도 못한 자들 말이오.”\n\n“잊으셨군요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “저는 바우처를 잘 몰라요. 하지만 제가 그 사람을 본 단 한 번, 그는 자기 고통을 이야기한 게 아니라 병든 아내와 어린아이들의 고통을 이야기했어요.”\n\n“옳소! 허나 그자도 쇠로 만들어진 건 아니었소. 다음엔 제 설움을 두고 울부짖었을 거요. 견딜 위인이 못 되었소.”\n\n“그 사람은 어떻게 조합에 들어왔나요?” 마거릿이 천진하게 물었다. “그를 그리 존중하시는 것 같지도 않고, 그를 들여서 좋은 일이 있었던 것 같지도 않은데요.”\n\n히긴스의 이마가 흐려졌다. 그는 일이 분쯤 말이 없었다. 그러더니 아주 짧게 말했다.\n\n“조합에 대해서는 내가 말할 게 아니오. 그들이 하는 일은 그들이 하는 것이오. 한 직종에 몸담은 사람들은 뭉쳐야 하오. 그러고도 나머지와 운명을 같이할 마음이 없다면, 조합에는 조합대로 방도와 수단이 있소.”\n\n헤일 씨는 히긴스가 대화가 흘러간 방향에 언짢아하는 것을 보고 잠자코 있었다. 마거릿은 그렇지 않았다. 그녀도 아버지만큼이나 또렷이 히긴스의 심정을 알아보았지만 말이다. 그를 어떻게든 알기 쉬운 말로 털어놓게만 할 수 있다면, 옳고 바른 것을 두고 따져 볼 무언가 분명한 것이 얻어지리라고 그녀는 본능으로 느꼈다.\n\n“그럼 조합의 방도와 수단이란 어떤 건가요?”\n\n그는 그녀를 올려다보았다. 알고 싶어 하는 그녀의 바람에 완고하게 버티려는 참인 듯했다. 그러나 그를 향해 붙박인 참을성 있고 믿음 어린 그 고요한 얼굴이 그를 대답하게 만들었다.\n\n“그러니까! 어떤 사내가 조합에 들지 않으면, 옆 베틀에서 일하는 사람들은 그자에게 말을 걸지 말라는 명을 받소. 그자가 슬픈 일을 당했든 아프든 매한가지요. 그자는 울타리 밖이오. 우리 사람이 아니오. 우리 틈에 끼어들고 우리 틈에서 일하지만 우리 사람이 아니오. 어떤 데선 그자에게 말을 거는 사람에게 벌금을 매기오. 아가씨, 한번 그리 살아 보시오. 댁이 쳐다보면 눈길을 돌려 버리는 사람들 틈에서 한두 해 살아 보시오. 마음속에 댁에 대한 갈리는 원한을 품은 줄 뻔히 아는 사람들 무리에서 두 자 거리를 두고 일해 보시오. 댁이 기쁘다고 말해도 눈 하나 반짝이지 않고 입술 하나 움직이지 않는 사람들, 댁의 마음이 무거워도 아무 말도 못 할 상대인 사람들 말이오. 어차피 그이들은 댁의 한숨도 슬픈 낯빛도 알은체하지 않을 테니. (게다가 무슨 일이냐고 물어 달라고 큰 소리로 앓는 소리를 내는 자는 사내도 아니지.) 한번 그리 살아 보시오, 아가씨. 하루 10시간씩 300일을. 그러면 조합이 무엇인지 좀 알게 될 거요.”\n\n“세상에!” 마거릿이 말했다. “이 무슨 폭정인가요! 아니에요, 히긴스, 저는 당신이 화내셔도 조금도 개의치 않아요. 당신이 아무리 그러려 해도 제게 화내실 수 없다는 걸 알고, 그러니 진실을 말씀드려야겠어요. 제가 읽은 온갖 역사를 통틀어 이보다 더 더디고 질질 끄는 고문은 읽어 본 적이 없어요. 그런데 당신은 조합에 속해 계시잖아요! 그러면서 공장주들의 폭정을 이야기하시고요!”\n\n“아니오.” 히긴스가 말했다. “아가씨는 하고 싶은 말을 다 하시오. 죽은 이가 아가씨와 내 모든 성난 말 사이에 서 있으니. 내가 저기 누가 누워 있는지, 그 애가 아가씨를 얼마나 좋아했는지 잊었을 것 같소? 그리고 조합이 죄라면, 우리를 죄짓게 만든 건 공장주들이오. 이 세대는 아닐지 몰라도 그 아비들이 그랬소. 그 아비들이 우리 아비들을 흙먼지가 되도록 갈았소. 우리를 가루로 갈아 놓았지! 목사님! 우리 어머니가 이런 구절을 읽어 주시던 걸 들은 것 같소. ‘아비가 신 포도를 먹었으므로 아들의 이가 시다.’ 그이들이 꼭 그 짝이오. 그 모진 억압의 시절에 조합이 시작되었소. 그건 어쩔 수 없는 일이었소. 지금도 어쩔 수 없다는 게 내 생각이오. 그건 지난 불의든 지금의 불의든 앞으로 올 불의든 그것을 버텨 내는 일이오. 전쟁 같을지도 모르지. 거기 따라 죄도 생기고. 허나 그냥 내버려 두는 게 더 큰 죄라고 나는 생각하오. 우리한테 남은 유일한 길은 사람들을 하나의 공통된 이해로 묶는 것이오. 그중 더러가 겁쟁이고 더러가 바보라 해도, 그들도 함께 와서 저 큰 행진에 끼어야 하오. 그 힘이라고는 오직 머릿수뿐인 행진에 말이오.”\n\n“아!” 헤일 씨가 한숨지으며 말했다. “당신들의 조합은 그 자체로는 아름답고 영광스러울 겁니다. 그리스도교 그 자체겠지요. 다만 그 목적이 한 계급에 맞선 한 계급의 이익이 아니라 모두의 선을 향한 것이기만 하다면요.”\n\n“이제 그만 가 볼 때가 된 것 같소, 선생님.” 시계가 열 시를 치자 히긴스가 말했다.\n\n“집으로요?” 마거릿이 아주 나직이 말했다. 그는 그녀의 뜻을 알아듣고 내민 손을 잡았다. “집으로 가오, 아가씨. 나를 믿으셔도 좋소, 내가 조합 사람이긴 해도.”\n\n“저는 당신을 아주 온전히 믿어요, 니컬러스.”\n\n“잠깐만요!” 헤일 씨가 서둘러 책장으로 가며 말했다. “히긴스 씨! 우리 가정 기도에 함께해 주시겠지요?”\n\n히긴스가 미심쩍은 눈으로 마거릿을 보았다. 그녀의 진지하고 다정한 눈이 그의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 안에 강요는 없고 오직 깊은 관심만 있었다. 그는 말은 없었으나 자리를 지켰다.\n\n국교회 사람인 마거릿, 비국교도인 그녀의 아버지, 무신론자인 히긴스가 함께 무릎을 꿇었다. 그것은 그들에게 아무런 해도 되지 않았다.\n\n# 제29장\n\n“몇몇 소망이 내 마음을 스치며 어렴풋이 그것을 북돋았고, 가엾고 서글픈 즐거움 한두 가지가, 저마다 희망의 창백하고 온기 없는 빛 속에서 여린 날개를 은빛으로 물들인 채, 소리 없이 스쳐 지나갔다 — 달빛 속의 나방들이여!” — 콜리지.\n\n이튿날 아침 마거릿에게 이디스의 편지가 왔다. 그 편지는 쓴 사람을 닮아 다정하고 두서가 없었다. 그러나 그 다정함은 마거릿 자신의 다정한 천성에 매혹적이었고, 두서없음은 그녀가 어릴 적부터 함께 자라 온 것이라 알아차리지도 못했다. 편지는 이러했다.\n\n“오, 마거릿, 우리 아기를 보러 영국에서 여기까지 오는 것도 아깝지 않을 거야! 정말 근사한 꼬마란다. 특히 모자를 썼을 때가 그렇고, 그중에서도 네가 보내 준 그 모자를 썼을 때가 제일이야. 착하고 손끝 야무지고 끈기 있는 우리 아가씨! 여기 어머니들을 죄다 부러워하게 만들어 놓았으니, 이제는 새로운 사람한테 보여 주고 새로운 감탄의 말들을 들어 보고 싶어. 어쩌면 그게 다일지도 몰라. 어쩌면 아닐지도 모르지. 아니, 어쩌면 사촌 사이의 정이 조금은 섞여 있을 거야. 아무튼 나는 네가 여기 와 주기를 정말 바라, 마거릿! 헤일 이모의 건강에도 그게 더없이 좋을 거라고 확신해. 여기 사람들은 다들 젊고 건강하고, 하늘은 늘 파랗고, 해는 늘 빛나고, 악단은 아침부터 밤까지 감미롭게 연주한단다. 그리고 내 노랫가락의 후렴으로 돌아오자면, 우리 아기는 늘 웃어. 나는 늘 네가 이 아이를 그려 주었으면 해, 마거릿. 아이가 무얼 하고 있든 상관없어. 그 모습 그대로가 가장 예쁘고 가장 우아하고 가장 훌륭하니까. 나는 남편보다 이 아이를 훨씬 더 사랑하는 것 같아. 남편은 점점 뚱뚱해지고 심술궂어지고 있거든. 본인은 그걸 ‘바쁘다’고 하지만. 아니야! 그렇지 않아. 방금 들어왔는데, 아래 만에 정박한 해저드호 장교들이 여는 아주 멋진 소풍 소식을 가져왔지 뭐야. 그렇게 반가운 소식을 가져왔으니 방금 한 말은 모두 거두어들일게. 누군가는 말이나 행동을 후회한 나머지 제 손을 불에 태웠다지? 글쎄, 나는 내 손을 태울 수는 없어. 아플 테고 흉터도 보기 흉할 테니까. 그래도 방금 한 말은 최대한 빨리 거두어들일게. 코즈모는 아기 못지않게 사랑스럽고, 조금도 뚱뚱하지 않고, 세상 어느 남편만큼이나 심술궂지 않아. 다만 가끔 아주아주 바쁠 뿐이지. 사랑이 없어서 하는 말은 아니야. 아내의 도리로 하는 말이지. 내가 어디까지 말했더라? 뭔가 아주 중요한 할 말이 있었는데, 분명히 있었어. 아, 이거야. 사랑하는 마거릿! 너는 꼭 와서 나를 봐야 해. 아까 말했듯이 헤일 이모께도 좋을 거야. 의사한테 그렇게 처방하라고 해. 이모를 상하게 하는 건 밀턴의 매연이라고 말씀드려. 정말 그렇다고 나는 믿어 의심치 않아. 이 감미로운 기후에서 3개월만 지내면(그보다 짧게 오면 안 돼) — 온통 햇빛에, 포도는 검은딸기만큼이나 흔해 — 이모는 말끔히 나으실 거야. 이모부께는 청하지 않겠어” — (여기서부터 편지는 한결 조심스러워지고 글씨도 나아졌다. 헤일 씨는 성직록을 버렸다는 이유로 말썽꾸러기 아이처럼 구석에 세워져 있었던 것이다.) — “왜냐하면 아마 이모부는 전쟁이며 군인이며 군악대를 못마땅해하실 테니까. 적어도 나는 많은 비국교도가 평화협회 회원이라는 걸 알고 있고, 그래서 이모부가 오고 싶어 하지 않으실까 봐 걱정이야. 하지만 만약 오시겠다면, 코즈모와 내가 이모부를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 최선을 다하겠다고 꼭 말씀드려 줘. 나는 코즈모의 붉은 군복과 검을 감춰 두고, 악단에는 온갖 진중하고 엄숙한 곡만 연주하게 할게. 아니면 화려하고 허영스러운 곡을 연주하더라도 두 배로 느리게 하도록 할 거야. 사랑하는 마거릿, 이모부가 너와 헤일 이모를 모시고 오시겠다면 우리는 즐겁게 해 드리려고 애쓸게. 나야 양심을 위해 무언가를 해낸 사람이라면 좀 겁이 나긴 하지만. 너는 그런 적이 없기를 바라. 헤일 이모께는 따뜻한 옷은 가져오지 마시라고 전해 드려. 너희가 올 수 있을 때는 아무래도 늦은 계절이겠지만. 그래도 여기 더위가 어떤지 너는 상상도 못 할 거야! 소풍 자리에서 내 그 훌륭한 인도 숄을 둘러 보려고 했어. 나는 버틸 수 있는 데까지 속담으로 버텼지. ‘멋을 부리려면 견뎌야 한다’느니 하는, 몸에 좋은 알맹이 같은 말들로 말이야. 하지만 소용없었어. 나는 코끼리 마구를 뒤집어쓴 어머니의 작은 개 타이니 꼴이었어. 그 화려함에 파묻히고 가려지고 숨이 막혀 죽을 지경이었지. 그래서 그걸 우리 모두가 앉을 근사한 깔개로 만들어 버렸단다. 여기 내 아들이 있어, 마거릿. 이 편지를 받는 대로 짐을 싸서 곧장 이 아이를 보러 오지 않는다면, 나는 네가 헤롯 왕의 후손이라고 생각할 거야!”\n\n마거릿은 이디스의 삶을 단 하루라도 살아 보고 싶었다. 근심 없는 자유, 명랑한 가정, 햇살 가득한 하늘을. 소망 하나로 몸이 옮겨질 수 있었다면 그녀는 떠났을 것이다. 단 하루만이라도. 그런 변화가 줄 힘이 그녀는 간절했다. 단 몇 시간만이라도 그 눈부신 삶 한복판에 있으면서 다시 젊어진 기분을 느끼고 싶었다. 아직 20세도 되지 않았는데! 그런데도 그토록 모진 압박을 견뎌 내야 했던 탓에 그녀는 자신이 아주 늙어 버린 듯했다. 이디스의 편지를 읽고 난 뒤의 첫 느낌이 그것이었다. 그러고 나서 다시 읽어 보니, 자기 자신은 잊은 채 그 편지가 이디스를 그대로 빼닮았다는 것이 재미있어졌다. 그래서 그녀가 편지를 두고 유쾌하게 웃고 있을 때, 헤일 부인이 딕슨의 팔에 기대어 응접실로 들어왔다. 마거릿은 얼른 달려가 베개를 바로 놓았다. 어머니는 여느 때보다 더 기운이 없어 보였다.\n\n“무엇 때문에 웃고 있었니, 마거릿?” 소파에 자리를 잡느라 애쓴 뒤 겨우 숨을 돌리자마자 어머니가 물었다.\n\n“오늘 아침 이디스한테서 온 편지 때문이에요. 읽어 드릴까요, 어머니?”\n\n그녀는 편지를 소리 내어 읽었다. 한동안 그것은 어머니의 관심을 끄는 듯했다. 어머니는 이디스가 아들에게 어떤 이름을 지어 주었을까 계속 궁금해하며, 있을 법한 이름을 죄다 대 보고, 그 이름들 하나하나가 붙여졌을 만한 온갖 이유를 들어 보았다. 이 궁금증이 한창일 때 손턴 씨가 헤일 부인에게 줄 과일을 또 들고 들어왔다. 그는 마거릿을 볼 수 있다는 기쁨의 기회를 스스로 마다할 수 없었다. 아니, 마다하려 하지 않았다고 하는 편이 옳겠다. 거기에는 지금 이 순간의 만족 말고 다른 목적이 없었다. 여느 때는 더없이 이성적이고 자제력 있는 남자가 부리는 완고한 고집이었다. 그는 방에 들어서면서 한눈에 마거릿이 있음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 차갑고 서먹한 첫 목례를 한 뒤로는 다시는 그녀에게 눈길을 떨구지 않는 듯했다. 그는 복숭아를 건네고 다정하고 부드러운 말 몇 마디를 건넬 만큼만 머물렀다. 그러고는 방을 나서면서, 차갑게 상한 그의 눈이 엄숙한 작별의 인사로 마거릿의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 말없이 창백한 얼굴로 앉아 있었다.\n\n“얘, 마거릿, 나는 정말 손턴 씨가 좋아지기 시작하는구나.”\n\n처음에는 대답이 없었다. 이윽고 마거릿이 얼음장 같은 “그러세요?”를 겨우 밀어냈다.\n\n“그럼! 그이는 몸가짐이 정말 꽤 세련되어 가는 것 같아.”\n\n이제 마거릿의 목소리는 한결 제자리를 찾아 있었다. 그녀가 대답했다. “참 친절하고 자상한 분이에요. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.”\n\n“손턴 부인이 한 번도 찾아오지 않는 게 이상하구나. 물침대 일이 있었으니 내가 아픈 걸 알 텐데 말이다.”\n\n“아마 아드님한테서 어머니 소식을 듣고 계실 거예요.”\n\n“그래도 나는 그이를 만나 보고 싶구나. 너는 여기에 벗이 너무 없잖니, 마거릿.”\n\n마거릿은 어머니의 마음속에 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 머잖아 어미 없는 아이가 될지도 모르는 딸에게 어떤 여인의 친절을 미리 청해 두고 싶은 애틋한 갈망이었다. 그러나 그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n“네 생각에는,” 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 헤일 부인이 말했다. “네가 손턴 부인을 찾아가 나를 보러 와 달라고 청해 볼 수 있겠니? 딱 한 번만이면 돼. 성가시게 굴고 싶지는 않으니까.”\n\n“어머니가 바라시면 무엇이든 하겠어요, 어머니. 하지만 만약에… 그러니까 오빠가 오면……”\n\n“아, 그렇지! 우리는 문을 닫아걸어야 하지. 아무도 들이면 안 되고. 그 애가 오기를 감히 바라야 할지 말아야 할지 나도 잘 모르겠구나. 어떤 때는 차라리 오지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어. 어떤 때는 그 애에 대해 어찌나 무서운 꿈을 꾸는지 모른다.”\n\n“어머니! 우리가 잘 지킬 거예요. 오빠가 조금이라도 다치게 두느니 차라리 제 팔을 빗장 대신 문에 끼워 넣겠어요. 오빠를 지키는 일은 제게 맡기세요, 어머니. 새끼를 지키는 암사자처럼 오빠를 살피겠어요.”\n\n“언제쯤 그 애 소식을 들을 수 있을까?”\n\n“적어도 1주일은 지나야 해요. 어쩌면 더 걸릴지도 모르고요.”\n\n“마사는 미리 내보내야 한다. 그 애가 왔을 때 마사가 여기 있다가 부랴부랴 내보내는 일은 결코 있어서는 안 되지.”\n\n“딕슨이 틀림없이 그걸 일깨워 줄 거예요. 저는 이런 생각을 했어요. 오빠가 여기 있는 동안 집안일에 손이 필요하면 메리 히긴스를 부르면 어떨까 하고요. 그 애는 요새 일이 아주 뜸하거든요. 착한 아이고, 제 딴에는 힘껏 애쓸 게 분명하고, 잠은 제 집에서 잘 테고, 위층에 올라올 일이 전혀 없으니 집 안에 누가 있는지 알 일도 없을 거예요.”\n\n“네 좋을 대로 하렴. 딕슨이 좋을 대로 하고. 하지만 마거릿, 이 끔찍한 밀턴 말을 쓰는 버릇은 들이지 마라. ‘일이 뜸하다’라니, 그건 지방 사투리다. 쇼 이모가 돌아와서 네가 그런 말을 쓰는 걸 들으면 뭐라고 하시겠니?”\n\n“어머니도 참! 쇼 이모를 도깨비처럼 만들지 마세요.” 마거릿이 웃으며 말했다. “이디스는 레녹스 대위한테서 온갖 군대 은어를 배웠는데도 쇼 이모는 조금도 개의치 않으셨잖아요.”\n\n“하지만 네 것은 공장 말투잖니.”\n\n“공장 도시에 살고 있으니 필요할 때는 공장 말을 써야죠. 어머니, 저는 어머니가 평생 들어 보지 못한 말들로 어머니를 깜짝 놀라게 해 드릴 수도 있어요. ‘파업 파괴자’가 무엇인지도 모르실걸요.”\n\n“난 모른다, 얘야. 그저 아주 상스럽게 들린다는 것만 알 뿐이야. 그리고 나는 네가 그 말을 쓰는 걸 듣고 싶지 않구나.”\n\n“알겠어요, 사랑하는 어머니. 쓰지 않을게요. 다만 그 대신 설명하는 문장을 통째로 써야 할 거예요.”\n\n“나는 이 밀턴이 싫구나.” 헤일 부인이 말했다. “나를 이렇게 아프게 한 게 매연이라는 이디스의 말이 옳아.”\n\n어머니가 이렇게 말하자 마거릿은 벌떡 일어섰다. 아버지가 막 방에 들어선 참이었다. 밀턴의 공기가 어머니의 건강을 해쳤다는 희미한 인상이 아버지의 마음에 어려 있는 것을 그녀는 본 적이 있었고, 그 인상이 더 깊어지지 않기를, 어떤 확증도 얻지 않기를 몹시 바랐다. 아버지가 헤일 부인의 말을 들었는지 아닌지는 알 수 없었지만, 그녀는 서둘러 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 손턴 씨가 아버지 뒤를 따라 들어오고 있는 줄은 모른 채.\n\n“어머니는 제가 밀턴에 온 뒤로 상스러운 말을 잔뜩 배웠다고 나무라시는 중이에요.”\n\n마거릿이 말한 “상스러움”이란 오로지 그 고장 말을 쓰는 것을 가리켰고, 그 표현은 방금 나눈 대화에서 나온 것이었다. 그러나 손턴 씨의 이마가 어두워졌다. 마거릿은 문득 자기 말이 그에게 어떻게 오해될 수 있는지 깨달았다. 그래서 불필요한 아픔을 주지 않으려는 타고난 고운 마음에서, 그녀는 애써 앞으로 나서서 가볍게 인사를 건네고는 그를 향해 대놓고 말을 이었다.\n\n“그런데 손턴 씨, ‘파업 파괴자’라는 말이 그리 곱게 들리지는 않지만 그래도 실감 나지 않나요? 그 말이 가리키는 것을 이야기하면서 그 말을 안 쓸 수가 있을까요? 고장 말을 쓰는 것이 상스러운 일이라면, 저는 숲에서도 몹시 상스러웠어요. 그렇지 않았나요, 어머니?”\n\n마거릿이 자기 이야깃거리를 남에게 들이대는 것은 좀처럼 없는 일이었다. 그러나 이번에는 우연히 엿들은 말 때문에 손턴 씨가 언짢아하지 않기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 말을 다 마치고 나서야 그녀는 자신을 의식하고 온통 얼굴을 붉혔다. 더욱이 손턴 씨는 그녀가 하는 말의 정확한 요지나 뜻을 거의 알아듣지 못한 듯, 격식 차린 몸짓의 차가운 서먹함으로 그녀를 지나쳐 헤일 부인에게 말을 걸었기에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n그를 보자 헤일 부인은 그의 어머니를 만나 마거릿을 그 보살핌에 맡기고 싶다는 바람이 떠올랐다. 마거릿은 타는 듯한 침묵 속에 앉아, 손턴 씨가 곁에 있을 때면 제자리를 지키고 마음의 고요한 무심함을 지키기가 어렵다는 사실에 짜증스럽고 부끄러워하면서, 손턴 부인이 자기를 보러 와 주었으면 한다는 어머니의 느릿한 간청을 들었다. 곧, 되도록이면 내일이라도 와 주었으면 한다는 간청이었다. 손턴 씨는 어머니가 그러실 거라고 약속하고, 잠시 이야기를 나누다가 물러갔다. 그러자 마거릿의 몸짓과 목소리는 단번에 보이지 않던 사슬에서 풀려난 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 한 번도 쳐다보지 않았다. 그런데도 그 눈길을 애써 피하는 태도는, 혹시라도 눈길이 무심코 떨어진다면 어디에 가 닿을지를 그가 정확히 알고 있음을 드러내고 있었다. 그녀가 말을 하면 그는 귀 기울이는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았지만, 다른 사람에게 건네는 그의 다음 말은 그녀가 한 말에 따라 달라져 있었다. 때로는 그녀가 한 말에 대한 분명한 대답이 있었으나, 그것은 그녀가 꺼낸 이야기가 아닌 양 다른 사람에게 주어졌다. 그것은 무지에서 나온 무례가 아니었다. 깊은 모욕감에서 비롯된 고의적인 무례였다. 그때는 고의였고, 나중에는 뉘우쳤다. 그러나 어떤 깊은 계략도, 어떤 용의주도한 술책도 그에게 그만큼 이롭게 작용하지는 못했을 것이다. 마거릿은 전에 없이 그를 자주 생각했다. 사랑이라 불리는 것의 기미가 어려 있어서가 아니라, 그를 그토록 깊이 상처 입혔다는 뉘우침 때문이었고, 또 서로 맞서던 예전의 우정 관계로 돌아가려는 부드럽고 참을성 있는 애씀 때문이었다. 그녀가 알아차린 바로는, 그가 자기 마음속에서도, 또 집안의 다른 식구들 마음속에서도 차지하고 있던 자리가 바로 벗의 자리였던 것이다. 그를 대하는 그녀의 태도에는 어여쁜 겸손이 있었다. 폭동이 있던 날의 행동에 대한 반발로 튀어나왔던 지나치게 모진 말들을 말없이 사과하기라도 하듯이.\n\n그러나 그는 그 말들을 쓰라리게 곱씹었다. 그 말들이 귓가에 울렸다. 그러면서도 그는 그녀의 부모에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 친절을 마다하지 않게 만드는 자신의 정의감을 자랑스러워했다. 그녀의 아버지나 어머니를 기쁘게 할 만한 일이 떠오를 때마다 스스로를 다그쳐 그녀와 마주하게 하는 자신의 힘에 그는 우쭐했다. 자기를 그토록 사무치게 모욕한 사람을 보는 것이 싫다고 그는 생각했다. 그러나 그것은 착각이었다. 그녀와 한방에 있으면서 그 존재를 느끼는 것은 따끔하게 쓰라린 기쁨이었다. 그러나 그는 제 동기를 잘 분석하는 사람이 아니었고, 앞서 말했듯이 착각하고 있었다.\n\n# 제30장\n\n“가장 슬픈 새들도 노래할 한철을 찾아낸다.” — 사우스웰.\n\n“은밀한 아픔 위로 옷자락을 여미는 일도 다시는 없고, 기억의 구름에 짓눌려 머리를 떨구는 일도 다시는 없으리! 그대는 집으로 돌아갔으니!” — 헤먼스 부인.\n\n손턴 부인은 이튿날 아침 헤일 부인을 보러 왔다. 부인의 병세는 훨씬 나빠져 있었다. 밤사이 그 갑작스러운 변화 가운데 하나가, 죽음을 향해 눈에 띄게 성큼 내딛는 그 걸음이 일어났고, 열두 시간의 고통 속에 잿빛으로 푹 꺼져 버린 얼굴에 식구들마저 소스라쳤다. 몇 주 동안 그녀를 보지 못했던 손턴 부인은 대번에 마음이 누그러졌다. 그녀가 온 것은 아들이 개인적인 부탁으로 청했기 때문이었고, 마거릿이 속한 그 집안에 대해서는 타고난 오만하고 쓰디쓴 감정을 죄다 곤두세운 채였다. 그녀는 헤일 부인의 병이 진짜인지 의심했다. 그날 하루의 정해진 일과에서 자신을 끌어내려는 그 부인의 한순간의 변덕 말고 무슨 절실한 사정이 있으랴 의심했다. 그녀는 아들에게, 저 사람들이 이 고장 근처에 오지나 말았더라면, 네가 저들과 알고 지내지나 않았더라면, 라틴어니 그리스어니 하는 쓸데없는 언어가 애초에 생겨나지나 않았더라면 좋았겠다고 말했다. 아들은 그 말을 꽤 잠자코 들었다. 그러나 죽은 언어들에 대한 그녀의 성토가 끝나자, 그는 병자에게 가장 편할 만한 시각에 어머니가 헤일 부인을 찾아가 주었으면 한다는 뜻을 짧고 무뚝뚝하고 단호한 말로 조용히 되풀이했다. 손턴 부인은 낼 수 있는 한 가장 언짢은 낯빛으로 아들의 뜻에 따랐으나, 그러면서도 내내 그런 뜻을 품은 아들이 더 좋았다. 그리고 헤일 집안과 그토록 끈질기게 어울려 주는 것이 아들의 유별난 선의라는, 아들 자신이 품고 있는 것과 똑같은 생각을 마음속에서 부풀렸다.\n\n약함에 가까운 아들의 선의(그녀의 마음속에서는 부드러운 덕목이란 다 그러했다), 헤일 씨 내외에 대한 자신의 경멸, 마거릿에 대한 노골적인 반감, 이런 생각들이 손턴 부인을 차지하고 있었다. 죽음의 천사가 드리운 날개의 검은 그림자 앞에서 그 모든 것이 아무것도 아닌 것으로 무너져 내리기 전까지는. 저기 헤일 부인이 누워 있었다. 자기와 똑같은 어머니가, 자기보다 훨씬 젊은 여자가, 다시는 일어날 가망이라고는 보이지 않는 침상 위에. 그 어두운 방에서 그녀에게는 더 이상 빛과 그늘의 변화가 없었다. 무엇을 할 힘도, 몸을 뒤척일 여지조차 거의 없었다. 속삭이는 소리와 조심스러운 침묵이 희미하게 번갈아 들 뿐이었다. 그런데도 그 단조로운 삶조차 감당하기 벅차 보였다! 삶으로 튼튼하고 번성한 손턴 부인이 들어섰을 때 헤일 부인은 가만히 누워 있었다. 얼굴빛을 보면 누가 왔는지 분명히 알고 있었는데도. 그러나 그녀는 한두 분 동안 눈조차 뜨지 않았다. 눈을 들었을 때는 이미 눈물의 무거운 물기가 속눈썹에 맺혀 있었다. 이윽고 손턴 부인의 크고 단단한 손가락을 더듬어 찾느라 이불 위로 힘없이 손을 헤매며, 그녀는 숨결보다 조금 높을까 말까 한 소리로 말했다. 손턴 부인은 꼿꼿한 몸을 굽혀야 들을 수 있었다.\n\n“마거릿을요. 댁에게도 딸이 있지요. 내 언니는 이탈리아에 있어요. 내 아이는 어미 없이 남게 됩니다. 낯선 곳에서. 내가 죽으면. 그래 주시겠어요”……\n\n그리고 흐릿하게 헤매던 두 눈이 애타는 간절함으로 손턴 부인의 얼굴에 못 박혔다. 잠시 동안 그 얼굴의 굳음에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 매섭고 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 아니, 병든 여인의 눈이 천천히 고이는 눈물로 흐려지고 있지만 않았더라면, 차가운 이목구비 위로 검은 구름 한 조각이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 보았을지도 몰랐다. 그리고 마침내 그 마음을 흔든 것은 아들 생각도, 살아 있는 딸 패니 생각도 아니었다. 방 안의 무언가가 문득 떠올려 준 기억, 오래전 갓난쟁이 적에 죽은 어린 딸의 기억이었다. 그것이 갑작스러운 햇살처럼 얼음 껍질을 녹였고, 그 뒤에는 진짜로 다정한 여인이 있었다.\n\n“헤일 양의 벗이 되어 달라는 말씀이군요.” 손턴 부인이 절도 있는 목소리로 말했다. 마음과 달리 누그러지지 못하고 또렷하고 맑게 나오는 목소리였다.\n\n헤일 부인은 여전히 손턴 부인의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 채, 이불 위에 놓인 제 손 밑의 그 손을 꼭 눌렀다. 말은 나오지 않았다. 손턴 부인이 한숨을 쉬었다. “사정이 그것을 요구한다면 참된 벗이 되겠습니다. 다정한 벗은 아니고요. 그것은 제가 될 수 없습니다.”(“저 아이에게는” 하고 덧붙이려다가, 그 가엾고 애타는 얼굴을 보고는 마음을 누그러뜨렸다.) “저는 애정을 느끼는 자리에서도 그것을 내보이는 성미가 아니고, 남에게 먼저 나서서 충고하는 편도 아닙니다. 그래도 청하시니, 그것이 조금이라도 위로가 된다면 약속드리지요.” 그러고 나서 사이가 있었다. 손턴 부인은 지킬 뜻이 없는 것을 약속하기에는 너무 양심적이었다. 그런데 그 어느 때보다 밉살스러운 마거릿을 위해 친절이랄 것을 실행한다는 것은 어려운 일이었다. 거의 불가능했다.\n\n“약속합니다.” 그녀가 엄숙하고 준엄하게 말했다. 그 준엄함이 오히려 죽어 가는 여인에게 삶 자체보다, 깜박이고 나풀거리고 흔들리는 저 삶보다 더 든든한 무엇에 대한 믿음을 불어넣었다. “헤일 양이 어떤 어려움에 처하든”……\n\n“마거릿이라고 불러 주세요!” 헤일 부인이 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n“저 아이가 도움을 청하러 오는 어떤 어려움에서든, 제 딸이기라도 한 것처럼 제가 지닌 온 힘을 다해 돕겠습니다. 또 한 가지 약속드리지요. 저 아이가 제 생각에 그른 일을 하는 것을 보게 되면”……\n\n“하지만 마거릿은 그른 일을 하지 않아요. 일부러 그르게는요.” 헤일 부인이 애원하듯 말했다. 손턴 부인은 듣지 못한 듯 하던 말을 그대로 이어 갔다.\n\n“저 아이가 제가 그르다고 믿는 일을 하는 것을 보게 되면, 저나 제 식구와 얽히지 않은 그른 일 말입니다. 얽혀 있다면 제게 딴 속셈이 있다고 여겨질 테니까요. 그럴 때에는 제 딸이라면 이렇게 일러 주었으면 싶은 그대로, 성실하고 솔직하게 일러 주겠습니다.”\n\n긴 침묵이 흘렀다. 헤일 부인은 이 약속이 모든 것을 담고 있지는 않다고 느꼈다. 그래도 이만해도 대단했다. 그 안에는 그녀가 헤아리지 못할 유보가 들어 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 약하고 어지럽고 지쳐 있었다. 손턴 부인은 자기가 그리하겠다고 맹세한 있을 법한 경우들을 하나하나 헤아려 보고 있었다. 의무를 이행한다는 모양새로 마거릿에게 반갑지 않은 진실을 일러 준다는 생각에 그녀는 사나운 쾌감을 느꼈다. 헤일 부인이 입을 열었다.\n\n“고맙습니다. 하느님의 복을 비옵니다. 이승에서 다시는 뵙지 못하겠지요. 그래도 마지막으로 이 말은 하겠어요. 내 아이에게 친절을 베풀겠다 약속해 주셔서 고맙습니다.”\n\n“친절은 아닙니다!” 손턴 부인이 마지막까지 무뚝뚝하리만치 정직하게 못 박았다. 그러나 그 말로 양심을 덜고 나자, 그 말이 상대에게 들리지 않았다는 것이 서운하지는 않았다. 그녀는 헤일 부인의 부드럽고 힘없는 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그러고는 일어나 사람 하나 마주치지 않고 그 집을 나섰다.\n\n손턴 부인이 헤일 부인과 이런 만남을 갖고 있는 동안, 마거릿과 딕슨은 머리를 맞대고 프레더릭이 온다는 것을 집 밖의 모두에게 어떻게 철저히 감출지 궁리하고 있었다. 이제 그의 편지는 언제 와도 이상하지 않았고, 편지 뒤를 바짝 따라 그가 올 것이 틀림없었다. 마사는 휴가를 주어 내보내야 했다. 딕슨은 현관을 매섭게 지키면서, 어쩌다 찾아오는 몇 안 되는 손님을 아래층 헤일 씨의 방으로만 들여야 했다. 헤일 부인의 병이 워낙 위중하니 그럴 구실은 충분했다. 부엌에서 딕슨을 거들 사람으로 메리 히긴스가 필요해지면, 그 애가 프레더릭에 대해 되도록 적게 보고 듣게 해야 했다. 그리고 꼭 필요하다면 그 애 앞에서는 그를 디킨슨 씨라는 이름으로 불러야 했다. 그러나 무엇보다 든든한 방패는 그 애의 굼뜨고 무심한 성미였다.\n\n그들은 마사를 바로 그날 오후에 어머니를 찾아뵙는 길로 떠나보내기로 정했다. 마거릿은 차라리 전날 내보냈더라면 싶었다. 안주인의 병세가 이토록 손이 많이 가는 판에 하녀에게 휴가를 주는 것이 이상하게 보일지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서였다.\n\n가엾은 마거릿! 그날 오후 내내 그녀는 로마의 딸 노릇을 하며 얼마 남지 않은 제 몫의 기운을 덜어 아버지에게 나누어 주어야 했다. 헤일 씨는 아내의 병이 발작과 발작 사이에 놓일 때마다 희망을 품으려 했고 절망하지 않으려 했다. 아내의 고통이 잠시 물러설 때마다 그는 마음을 부풀렸고, 이것이 끝내 나아지는 시작이라고 믿었다. 그래서 앞것보다 더 모진 발작이 닥칠 때마다 그것은 새로운 고뇌였고, 더 큰 낙담이었다. 이날 오후 그는 응접실에 앉아 있었다. 서재의 적막을 견딜 수도, 무엇으로든 마음을 붙일 수도 없었기 때문이다. 그는 탁자 위에 포갠 두 팔에 얼굴을 파묻었다. 그를 보는 마거릿의 가슴이 아팠다. 그러나 그가 말이 없으니 그녀도 굳이 나서서 위로해 보고 싶지 않았다. 마사는 떠났다. 딕슨은 헤일 부인이 잠든 곁에 앉아 있었다. 집 안은 몹시 고요하고 조용했고, 촛불을 가지러 움직이는 이도 없이 어둠이 내렸다. 마거릿은 창가에 앉아 가로등과 거리를 내다보았으나 아무것도 보고 있지 않았다. 오직 아버지의 무거운 한숨에만 살아 있었다. 그녀는 불을 가지러 내려가기가 싫었다. 자기가 곁에 있음으로 해서 말없이 눌려 있던 것이 풀리면, 위로해 줄 사람도 곁에 없는 자리에서 아버지가 더 격한 감정에 무너질까 봐서였다. 그래도 자기 말고는 돌볼 사람이 없는 부엌 불이 잘 있는지 가 보아야겠다는 생각이 막 들던 참에, 헝겊을 감은 초인종이 어찌나 세게 당겨졌는지 소리 자체는 크지 않은데도 줄이 온 집 안을 울리며 딸랑거렸다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 아버지 곁을 지나쳤다. 그 먹먹하고 둔한 소리에도 아버지는 꿈쩍하지 않았다. 그녀는 되돌아와 그에게 살며시 입을 맞추었다. 그래도 그는 움직이지도, 딸의 애틋한 포옹을 알은체하지도 않았다. 그러자 그녀는 어둠을 지나 살그머니 문간으로 내려갔다. 딕슨이었다면 문을 열기 전에 걸쇠 사슬을 걸었겠지만, 마음이 온통 딴 데 가 있는 마거릿에게는 두려움이라는 생각이 없었다. 그녀와 환한 거리 사이로 키 큰 남자의 형체가 서 있었다. 그는 딴 데를 보고 있다가 걸쇠 소리에 홱 돌아섰다.\n\n“여기가 헤일 씨 댁입니까?” 그가 맑고 우렁차면서도 고운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 온몸을 떨었다. 처음에는 대답하지 못했다. 이윽고 그녀는 한숨처럼 내뱉었다.\n\n“오빠!” 그러고는 두 손을 뻗어 그의 손을 붙잡고 안으로 끌어들였다.\n\n“아, 마거릿!” 그가 서로 입을 맞춘 뒤 그녀의 어깨를 잡아 몸을 떼어 놓으며 말했다. 그 어둠 속에서도 그녀의 얼굴이 보이기라도 하는 듯이, 그 표정에서 말보다 빠른 대답을 읽어 내려는 듯이.\n\n“어머니! 살아 계셔?”\n\n“네, 살아 계세요, 그립고 그리운 오빠! 더할 수 없이 위중하시지만, 그래도 살아 계세요! 살아 계셔요!”\n\n“하느님, 감사합니다!” 그가 말했다.\n\n“아버지는 이 큰 슬픔에 완전히 주저앉으셨어요.”\n\n“내가 올 줄 알고 있었지?”\n\n“아니요, 편지는 못 받았어요.”\n\n“그럼 편지보다 내가 먼저 왔구나. 그래도 어머니는 내가 온다는 걸 아셔.”\n\n“아! 오빠가 오리라는 건 우리 모두 알고 있었어요. 그래도 잠깐만요! 이리 들어오세요. 손 주세요. 이건 뭐예요? 아, 여행 가방이군요. 딕슨이 덧문을 닫아 두었지만 여기가 아버지 서재예요. 제가 올라가서 알려 드리는 동안 잠깐 앉아 쉬시게 의자로 모실게요.”\n\n그녀는 더듬더듬 가느다란 초와 성냥을 찾아갔다. 그 희미한 작은 불빛에 서로의 모습이 드러나자 그녀는 문득 수줍어졌다. 그녀가 볼 수 있었던 것이라고는 오빠의 얼굴빛이 유난히 검다는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 눈꼬리가 유난히 길게 째진 푸른 두 눈이 슬며시 건네는 눈길을 붙들었는데, 그 눈은 서로를 살펴보려는 속셈이 같다는 것을 익살스럽게 알아채고는 반짝 빛났다. 그러나 오누이는 마주친 눈길 속에 한순간 마음이 통했으면서도 한마디도 주고받지 않았다. 다만 마거릿은 이미 가까운 피붙이로 사랑하고 있는 만큼이나 이 오빠를 동무로서도 좋아하게 되리라고 확신했다. 위층으로 올라가는 그녀의 마음은 놀랍도록 가벼웠다. 슬픔이 실제로 줄어든 것은 아니었으나, 그 슬픔과 꼭 같은 자리에 선 사람이 생겼다는 것만으로 짓누르는 힘이 덜해졌다. 이제는 아버지의 낙담한 모습도 그녀를 가라앉힐 힘이 없었다. 아버지는 여전히 어쩔 줄 모르는 채로 탁자에 엎드려 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그를 깨울 주문이 있었다. 크나큰 안도에 겨워 그녀는 그 주문을 어쩌면 너무 세차게 썼는지도 몰랐다.\n\n“아버지.” 그녀가 그의 목을 다정하게 끌어안으며 말했다. 부드러우면서도 억지스러운 힘으로 그 지친 머리를 실제로 끌어올려 자기 팔에 기대게 하고, 그의 눈을 들여다보며 그 눈이 자기 눈에서 힘과 확신을 얻게 하면서.\n\n“아버지! 누가 왔는지 맞혀 보세요!”\n\n그가 딸을 바라보았다. 그 흐릿한 슬픔 속으로 진실의 짐작이 어렴풋이 스며들었다가, 터무니없는 공상으로 내쫓기는 것을 그녀는 보았다.\n\n그는 몸을 앞으로 던지듯 숙여 아까처럼 탁자에 얹은 두 팔 사이에 다시 얼굴을 묻었다. 그가 속삭이는 소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 다정하게 몸을 굽혀 귀를 기울였다. “모르겠다. 프레더릭이라고는 말하지 마라. 프레더릭은 안 된다. 나는 견딜 수가 없어. 나는 너무 약하다. 게다가 그 애 어미가 죽어 가고 있는데!”\n\n그는 아이처럼 울며 통곡하기 시작했다. 마거릿이 바라고 기대했던 것과는 너무나 달라서 그녀는 낙담에 속이 메슥거렸고, 한순간 말을 잃었다. 그러고는 다시 입을 열었다. 아주 다르게, 그리 들뜨지 않게, 훨씬 다정하고 조심스럽게.\n\n“아버지, 프레더릭 오빠예요! 어머니를 생각해 보세요, 얼마나 기뻐하시겠어요! 아, 어머니를 위해서라도 우리가 얼마나 기뻐해야 하는지요! 오빠를 위해서도요. 가엾고 가엾은 우리 오빠!”\n\n아버지는 자세를 바꾸지 않았으나, 그 사실을 헤아려 보려 애쓰는 듯했다.\n\n“그 애가 어디 있느냐?” 마침내 그가 물었다. 얼굴은 여전히 엎드린 팔에 묻은 채였다.\n\n“아버지 서재에요, 아주 혼자요. 제가 초를 켜 놓고 아버지께 알리려 뛰어 올라왔어요. 혼자 있으니 왜 이러나 싶어 할 텐데요……”\n\n“내가 그 애한테 가마.” 아버지가 말을 자르고 나섰다. 그러고는 몸을 일으켜 길잡이의 팔에 기대듯 딸의 팔에 기댔다.\n\n마거릿은 그를 서재 문까지 이끌었다. 그러나 마음이 하도 뒤흔들려 그 만남을 차마 지켜볼 수 없을 것 같았다. 그녀는 돌아서서 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 실컷 울었다. 며칠 만에 처음으로 스스로에게 허락한 위안이었다. 그동안의 긴장이 얼마나 끔찍했는지 그녀는 이제야 느꼈다. 그러나 프레더릭이 왔다! 하나뿐인 그 소중한 오빠가 무사히 다시 식구들 틈에 와 있다! 그녀는 좀처럼 믿기지 않았다. 그녀는 울음을 그치고 침실 문을 열었다. 아무 말소리도 들리지 않아 혹시 꿈을 꾼 것은 아닌가 겁이 날 지경이었다. 그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 서재 문에서 귀를 기울였다. 웅얼거리는 말소리가 들렸고, 그것으로 충분했다. 그녀는 부엌으로 들어가 불을 지피고 집 안에 불을 밝히고 나그네가 요기할 것을 마련했다. 어머니가 잠들어 계신 것이 얼마나 다행인가! 침실 문 열쇠 구멍에 꽂아 둔 불쏘시개를 보고 그녀는 어머니가 잠든 것을 알았다. 어머니가 무슨 심상찮은 낌새를 알아채기 전에 나그네는 배를 채우고 기운을 차릴 수 있을 테고, 아버지와의 첫 만남이 주는 흥분도 다 가라앉을 것이었다.\n\n채비가 다 되자 마거릿은 서재 문을 열고 하녀처럼 무거운 쟁반을 두 팔로 받쳐 들고 들어갔다. 프레더릭의 시중을 드는 것이 그녀는 자랑스러웠다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 보자마자 벌떡 일어나 그 짐을 받아 들었다. 그것은 그가 있음으로 해서 앞으로 찾아올 온갖 덜어짐의 한 본보기이자 조짐이었다. 오누이는 함께 상을 차렸다. 말은 별로 없었으나 손이 서로 닿았고, 눈은 같은 피를 나눈 이들에게는 그토록 잘 통하는 저 자연스러운 표정의 언어로 말하고 있었다. 불이 꺼져 있어서 마거릿이 불을 붙이는 일에 매달렸다. 저녁이 쌀쌀해지기 시작한 데다, 그러면서도 헤일 부인의 방에서 소리를 되도록 멀리 두는 편이 좋았기 때문이다.\n\n“딕슨은 불 붙이는 건 타고나는 재주지 익혀서 되는 기술이 아니라고 하던데요.”\n\n“시인은 태어나는 것이지 만들어지는 것이 아니다.” 헤일 씨가 중얼거렸다. 아무리 맥없이 나왔을망정 마거릿은 다시 인용구를 듣게 된 것이 반가웠다.\n\n“그리운 딕슨! 우리 서로 얼마나 입을 맞출까!” 프레더릭이 말했다. “나한테 입을 맞추고는 사람을 제대로 봤나 하고 얼굴을 들여다보고 나서 또 맞추곤 했지! 그런데 마거릿, 너 참 서투르구나! 이렇게 작고 어설프고 아무짝에도 못 쓸 손은 처음 본다. 저리 가서 손 씻고 와서 나한테 버터 바른 빵이나 썰어 주고, 불은 놔둬라. 내가 하마. 불 붙이는 건 내 타고난 재주 가운데 하나야.”\n\n그래서 마거릿은 나갔다가 돌아왔고, 가만히 앉아 있어서는 성에 차지 않는 기쁜 들썩임으로 방을 들락날락했다. 프레더릭이 아쉬운 것이 많으면 많을수록 그녀는 더 기뻤고, 그는 그 마음을 본능으로 다 알았다. 그것은 상가에서 낚아챈 기쁨이었고, 마음 깊은 곳에서 돌이킬 수 없는 슬픔이 기다리고 있음을 알기에 그 맛은 한층 더 알싸했다.\n\n한창때에 딕슨의 발소리가 계단에서 들렸다. 헤일 씨는 큰 안락의자에 맥없이 기대 있던 자세에서 흠칫 몸을 일으켰다. 그는 거기서 두 자식을 꿈결처럼 지켜보고 있던 참이었다. 마치 그들이, 보기에는 예쁘장하나 현실과는 동떨어진, 자기 몫이라고는 없는 행복의 연극을 연기하고 있기라도 한 것처럼. 그는 일어서서 문을 마주 보았다. 들어오는 사람이 누구든, 그것이 충직한 딕슨이라 할지라도 프레더릭을 그 눈에 띄지 않게 감추려는 그 이상하고 갑작스러운 조바심에 마거릿의 가슴에 소름이 돋았다. 그것은 그들의 삶에 새로 들어선 두려움을 일깨웠다. 그녀는 프레더릭의 팔을 잡고 꽉 붙들었고, 그러는 동안 준엄한 생각이 이마를 찌푸리게 하고 이를 악물게 했다. 그러면서도 그들은 그것이 딕슨의 절도 있는 발소리일 뿐임을 알고 있었다. 딕슨이 복도를 지나 부엌으로 들어가는 소리가 들렸다. 마거릿이 일어섰다.\n\n“제가 가서 말할게요. 어머니가 어떠신지도 듣고 올게요.” 헤일 부인은 깨어 있었다. 처음에는 헛소리를 했으나, 차를 조금 드리고 나자 기운을 차렸다. 그래도 말을 하고 싶어 하지는 않았다. 아들이 왔다는 이야기는 하룻밤을 넘긴 뒤에 하는 편이 나았다. 도널드슨 의사가 오기로 한 왕진만으로도 그날 저녁의 신경 쓰이는 흥분으로는 충분할 터였다. 게다가 그가 어머니에게 프레더릭을 만나게 할 채비를 어떻게 해야 할지 일러 줄지도 몰랐다. 프레더릭은 여기, 이 집에 있었다. 언제든 부르면 될 일이었다.\n\n마거릿은 가만히 앉아 있을 수 없었다. “프레더릭 도련님”을 위한 딕슨의 온갖 채비를 거드는 것이 그녀에게는 오히려 위안이었다. 다시는 지치는 일이 없을 것만 같았다. 그가 아버지 곁에 앉아 무엇인지 알 수도 없고 알고 싶지도 않은 이야기를 나누고 있는 방을 한 번씩 들여다볼 때마다 그것이 그녀에게는 힘이 되었다. 이야기하고 들을 그녀의 차례도 언젠가는 올 터였고, 그것을 너무나 확신했기에 지금 서둘러 붙들고 싶은 마음이 들지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 모습을 눈에 담았고, 마음에 들었다. 그는 이목구비가 고왔는데, 가무잡잡한 얼굴빛과 재빠르고 강렬한 표정 덕분에 여자 같아 보이지는 않았다. 눈은 대개 유쾌해 보였으나, 이따금 눈과 입이 어찌나 갑자기 달라지며 속에 잠긴 격정을 짐작하게 하는지 그녀는 겁이 날 지경이었다. 그러나 그 표정은 한순간일 뿐이었고, 거기에는 고집스러움도 앙심도 없었다. 그것은 오히려 거칠거나 남쪽에 있는 나라에서 나고 자란 사람들의 얼굴에 스치는 그 순간적인 사나움이었다. 그런 눈빛이 녹아들어 갈 수 있는 아이 같은 부드러움의 매력을 도리어 돋우어 주는 사나움이었다. 마거릿은 그렇게 이따금 드러나는 충동적인 천성의 격렬함이 두려웠을지언정, 그것 때문에 새로 얻은 오빠를 못 미더워하거나 조금이라도 물러설 일은 없었다. 오히려 그와 주고받는 모든 것이 처음부터 그녀에게는 유난히 매력 있었다. 프레더릭이 곁에 있음으로 해서 느끼는 그 절묘한 안도감으로 그녀는 자기가 그동안 얼마나 무거운 책임을 져 왔는지 비로소 알았다. 그는 아버지와 어머니를 알았다. 두 분의 성품과 약함을 알았고, 무심할 만큼 거리낌 없이 굴면서도 그 감정을 다치게 하거나 상하게 하지 않도록 더없이 섬세하게 마음을 썼다. 그는 타고난 활기가 밴 태도와 이야기를 조금 내보여도 아버지의 깊은 침울함에 거슬리지 않을 때가, 혹은 어머니의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있을 때가 언제인지 본능으로 아는 듯했다. 가락도 때도 맞지 않을 자리에서는 참을성 있는 정성과 살핌이 나서서 그를 훌륭한 간병인으로 만들었다. 그리고 그가 뉴 포리스트에서 보낸 어린 시절을 자주 들먹일 때면 마거릿은 눈물이 날 만큼 마음이 움직였다. 그는 먼 나라와 낯선 사람들 사이를 떠도는 내내 그녀도, 헬스톤도 잊은 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그 옛 자리 이야기를 그에게 마음껏 할 수 있었고, 그를 지루하게 할까 걱정할 일이 없었다. 그녀는 그가 오기 전, 그의 도착을 그토록 바라면서도 한편으로는 그를 두려워했다. 일고여덟 해 동안 자기 안에 그토록 큰 변화가 있었다고 느꼈기에, 본래의 마거릿이 얼마나 많이 남아 있는지는 잊은 채로, 집 안에만 있던 자기 삶에서도 취향과 감정이 이렇게 크게 달라졌다면, 자기가 어렴풋이만 알고 있는 그 거친 삶은 사관후보생 제복을 입고 우러러보며 감탄하던 그 훤칠한 소년 대신 아예 다른 프레더릭을 갖다 놓았으리라고 짐작했던 것이다. 그러나 떨어져 지내는 동안 두 사람은 나이로 보나 다른 여러 가지로 보나 서로 더 가까워져 있었다. 그리하여 이 슬픈 시절의 무게가 마거릿에게는 한결 가벼워졌다. 프레더릭이 곁에 있다는 것 말고 그녀에게 다른 빛은 없었다. 몇 시간 동안 어머니는 아들을 보고 기운을 되찾았다. 그녀는 아들의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채 앉아 있었고, 잠든 동안에도 그 손을 놓으려 하지 않았다. 그래서 마거릿은 그가 손가락 하나 움직여 어머니를 방해하는 일이 없도록 그에게 아기처럼 음식을 떠먹여 주어야 했다. 그러고 있는 중에 헤일 부인이 깨어났다. 그녀는 베개 위에서 천천히 고개를 돌려, 두 자식이 무엇을 왜 하고 있는지 알아차리고는 그들에게 미소 지었다.\n\n“내가 참 이기적이구나.” 그녀가 말했다. “그래도 오래가지는 않을 게다.” 프레더릭은 몸을 굽혀 제 손을 가두어 놓은 그 힘없는 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n이 평온한 상태는 여러 날 갈 수 없었고, 어쩌면 여러 시간도 못 갈 것이었다. 도널드슨 의사는 마거릿에게 그렇게 못 박았다. 그 친절한 의사가 돌아간 뒤, 그녀는 왕진 동안 안쪽 뒷방에 조용히 숨어 있으라고 신신당부를 들은 프레더릭에게 살며시 내려갔다. 본래 딕슨의 침실이던 그 방은 이제 그에게 내준 것이었다.\n\n마거릿은 도널드슨 의사가 한 말을 그에게 전했다.\n\n“난 못 믿겠다.” 그가 소리쳤다. “어머니는 몹시 편찮으시지. 위독하실 수도 있고, 당장 위험할 수도 있어. 하지만 정말 돌아가실 지경이라면 지금 저러실 수는 없다고 본다. 마거릿! 다른 사람 소견도 들어 봐야 해. 런던 의사 말이야. 그런 생각은 해 보지 않았니?”\n\n“했어요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “여러 번요. 하지만 그래 봐야 소용이 있을 것 같지 않아요. 그리고 아시다시피 런던의 이름난 외과의를 모셔 올 돈이 우리에겐 없어요. 게다가 도널드슨 선생님은 가장 훌륭한 분들 바로 다음가는 실력이라고 저는 믿어요. 아니, 어쩌면 그분들과 나란할지도 모르고요.”\n\n프레더릭은 안달이 나서 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하기 시작했다.\n\n“카디스에는 내 신용이 있는데.” 그가 말했다. “여기서는 이 몹쓸 개명 때문에 아무것도 안 되는구나. 아버지는 왜 헬스톤을 떠나셨을까? 그게 잘못이었어.”\n\n“잘못이 아니에요.” 마거릿이 침울하게 말했다. “그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 방금 하신 그런 말을 아버지가 듣게 하지 마세요. 우리가 헬스톤에 남았더라면 어머니가 편찮으실 일도 없었으리라는 생각으로 아버지가 벌써 스스로를 괴롭히고 계신 게 보여요. 오빠는 아버지의 그 무서운 자책하는 힘을 모르시잖아요!”\n\n프레더릭은 후갑판을 걷듯 저편으로 걸어갔다. 마침내 그는 마거릿의 바로 맞은편에 멈춰 서서 축 처지고 낙담한 그 모습을 잠시 바라보았다.\n\n“우리 작은 마거릿!” 그가 그녀를 어루만지며 말했다. “할 수 있는 데까지는 희망을 품자. 가엾은 것! 아니, 얼굴이 온통 눈물범벅이구나? 나는 희망을 품겠다. 의사가 천 명이 와도 그러겠다. 견뎌 내라, 마거릿. 희망을 품을 만큼 용감해져라!”\n\n마거릿은 말을 하려다 목이 메었고, 겨우 나온 목소리는 아주 낮았다.\n\n“믿을 수 있을 만큼 온순해지도록 애써 볼게요. 아, 오빠! 어머니가 저를 그렇게 사랑하게 되어 가시던 참이었어요! 저도 어머니를 이해하게 되어 가고 있었고요. 그런데 이제 죽음이 와서 우리를 뚝 잘라 놓는군요!”\n\n“자, 자, 자! 이토록 귀할지 모를 시간을 헛되이 보내느니 위층으로 올라가서 뭐라도 하자. 생각은 여러 번 나를 슬프게 했지만, 무엇을 하는 일이 나를 슬프게 한 적은 평생 한 번도 없었다. 내 지론은 ‘돈을 벌어라, 아들아. 될 수 있으면 정직하게. 그러나 어쨌든 돈을 벌어라’ 하는 격언을 살짝 비튼 거야. 내 계율은 이렇다. ‘무엇이든 해라, 누이야. 될 수 있으면 좋은 일을. 그러나 어쨌든 무엇이든 해라.’”\n\n“못된 짓도 빼지 않고요?” 마거릿이 눈물 사이로 희미하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“빼지 않고말고. 내가 빼는 것은 나중에 오는 뉘우침이야. 잘못을 저질렀거든(네가 유난히 양심적이라면 말이다) 되도록 빨리 착한 일로 그걸 지워 버려라. 학교에서 석판에 틀린 셈을 반만 지운 자리에 맞는 셈을 새로 써넣었던 것처럼 말이야. 그게 눈물로 지우개를 적시는 것보다 나았어. 눈물이 나오기를 기다려야 하니 시간도 덜 버리고, 결과도 더 나았지.”\n\n마거릿은 처음에는 프레더릭의 지론이 다소 거칠다고 여겼을지 몰라도, 그가 그것을 실제로 끊임없는 친절의 생산으로 풀어내는 것을 보았다. 어머니 곁에서 나쁜 밤을 보낸 뒤에도(그는 밤을 새워 지키는 차례를 제 몫으로 맡겠다고 우겼다) 그는 이튿날 아침 식사 전부터 밤샘의 피로를 느끼기 시작한 딕슨을 위해 다리받침을 궁리하느라 분주했다. 아침 식사 때에는 멕시코와 남아메리카를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 보낸 거친 삶의 이야기를 생생하고 그림 같고 거침없이 늘어놓아 헤일 씨의 흥미를 끌었다. 마거릿이었다면 헤일 씨를 낙심에서 끌어내려는 노력을 절망 속에 접었을 것이다. 아니, 그런 것이 그녀 자신에게까지 옮아 아예 말을 할 수 없게 만들었을 것이다. 그러나 프레드는 제 지론에 충실하게 쉼 없이 무엇인가를 했고, 아침 식탁에서 먹는 것 말고 할 수 있는 일이라고는 말하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그날 밤이 되기 전에 도널드슨 의사의 소견은 지나치리만치 근거가 있었음이 드러났다. 경련이 일었고, 그것이 멎었을 때 헤일 부인은 의식이 없었다. 남편이 곁에 누워 흐느낌으로 침상을 흔들어도, 아들의 억센 팔이 그녀를 다정하게 들어 편한 자세로 옮겨 놓아도, 딸의 손이 그 얼굴을 닦아 주어도 그녀는 그들을 알지 못했다. 그녀는 하늘에서 다시 만날 때까지 다시는 그들을 알아보지 못할 터였다.\n\n아침이 오기 전에 모든 것이 끝났다.\n\n그러자 마거릿은 떨림과 낙담에서 일어나 아버지와 오빠에게 위로의 굳센 천사가 되었다. 이제는 프레더릭이 무너져 버렸고, 그의 온갖 지론도 그에게는 아무 쓸모가 없었기 때문이다. 밤에 제 작은 방에 갇혀 어찌나 격하게 우는지, 마거릿과 딕슨이 놀라 내려가 조용히 하라고 일러야 했다. 집의 칸막이가 얇아서 옆집 사람들이 그 젊고 격정적인 흐느낌을 쉽게 들을 수 있었기 때문이다. 슬픔에 길이 들고, 누가 그것을 정하는지 알기에 감히 그 가차 없는 운명에 거스르지 못하는 뒷날의, 더 느리고 떨리는 고통과는 사뭇 다른 흐느낌이었다.\n\n마거릿은 죽은 이의 방에서 아버지와 함께 앉아 있었다. 아버지가 울기라도 했더라면 그녀는 고마웠을 것이다. 그러나 그는 침상 곁에 아주 조용히 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 다만 이따금 얼굴을 덮은 것을 걷고 부드럽게 쓸어 주면서, 새끼를 어루만지는 어미 짐승 같은 나직하고 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 냈다. 그는 마거릿이 있다는 것도 알은체하지 않았다. 한두 번 그녀가 다가가 입을 맞추었고, 그는 그것을 가만히 받아 주었으나 끝나고 나면 살짝 밀어내는 것이었다. 마치 딸의 애정이 죽은 이에게 골몰한 자기를 흐트러뜨리기라도 하는 듯이. 그는 프레더릭의 울음소리가 들리자 흠칫하고 고개를 저었다. “가엾은 것, 가엾은 것.” 그러고는 더 이상 알은체하지 않았다. 마거릿의 가슴은 속으로 아팠다. 아버지의 처지를 생각하느라 자신이 잃은 것은 생각할 수도 없었다. 밤이 저물어 가고 날이 밝아 오려는 때에, 아무런 예고도 없이 마거릿의 목소리가 방의 고요를 깨뜨렸다. 그 소리가 어찌나 맑았는지 그녀 자신마저 놀랐다. “너희는 마음에 근심하지 말라.” 목소리는 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 그 이루 말할 수 없는 위로의 장을 처음부터 끝까지 흔들림 없이 읽어 나갔다.\n\n# 제31장\n\n「옛 친구를 어이 잊으리」\n\n“저 태도와 저 모든 이목구비가 드러내지 않던가, 뱀의 교활함과 죄인의 몰락을?” — 크래브.\n\n쌀쌀하고 으슬으슬한 10월 아침이 왔다. 부드럽고 은빛 도는 안개가 햇살에 걷히며 온갖 찬란한 빛깔의 아름다움을 드러내는 시골의 10월 아침이 아니라, 은빛 안개라는 것이 곧 짙은 매연 안개인 밀턴의 10월 아침이었다. 이곳에서는 해가 구름을 뚫고 나와 비친다 해도 길게 뻗은 침침한 거리들밖에 보여 줄 것이 없었다. 마거릿은 집 안을 정돈하는 딕슨의 일을 도우며 맥없이 오갔다. 눈은 쉴 새 없이 눈물로 흐려졌지만 제대로 울고 있을 겨를이 없었다. 아버지와 오빠가 그녀에게 기대고 있었다. 그들이 슬픔에 몸을 맡기고 있는 동안 그녀는 일하고 계획하고 헤아려야 했다. 장례에 필요한 준비마저 그녀의 몫으로 떨어지는 듯했다.\n\n불이 환하게 타닥거리고, 아침 식사 채비가 모두 갖추어지고, 주전자가 노래하듯 끓을 무렵, 마거릿은 헤일 씨와 프레더릭을 부르러 가기 전에 방 안을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보았다. 그녀는 무엇이든 되도록 밝아 보이기를 바랐다. 그런데 정말로 그렇게 되고 보니, 그것과 자기 마음속 생각 사이의 대비가 그녀를 불쑥 울음으로 몰아넣었다. 우는 소리를 아무도 듣지 못하도록 소파 옆에 무릎을 꿇고 방석에 얼굴을 파묻고 있는데, 딕슨이 그녀의 어깨를 건드렸다.\n\n“자, 헤일 아가씨, 자, 우리 아가씨! 이러시면 안 됩니다요. 아가씨가 무너지면 저희는 다 어찌 되겠어요? 이 집에 무슨 지시 하나 내릴 만한 사람이 달리 없는데 할 일은 산더미랍니다. 장례를 누가 맡을지, 누가 올지, 어디서 치를지, 다 정해야 하잖아요. 프레더릭 도련님은 울다가 넋이 나간 사람 같고, 주인어른은 원래 무슨 일을 정하시는 분이 아니고요. 딱하기도 하시지, 지금은 넋을 잃으신 사람처럼 돌아다니시기만 하니. 얼마나 힘드신지 저도 압니다요. 그렇지만 죽음은 누구에게나 오는 것이고, 아가씨는 여태 벗 하나 잃어 본 적이 없으니 복이 많으신 거예요.”\n\n그럴지도 몰랐다. 그러나 이 상실은 그 자체로 따로 떨어져 있는 듯했고, 세상 어떤 일과도 견줄 수 없을 것 같았다. 마거릿은 딕슨의 말에서 아무 위로도 얻지 못했지만, 그 깐깐한 늙은 하녀가 보인 유별난 다정함이 마음에 사무쳤다. 그리고 다른 무엇보다 그 고마움을 보이고 싶은 마음에서 그녀는 몸을 추스르고, 자기를 근심스레 바라보는 딕슨의 눈길에 미소로 답한 뒤, 아버지와 오빠에게 아침 식사가 준비되었다고 알리러 갔다.\n\n헤일 씨가 왔다. 꿈속에 있는 사람처럼, 아니 그보다는 눈과 마음이 눈앞의 것이 아닌 다른 것을 보고 있는 몽유병자의 무의식한 몸짓으로. 프레더릭은 억지로 꾸민 쾌활함을 띠고 성큼 들어와 그녀의 손을 잡고 눈을 들여다보다가 왈칵 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 그녀는 아침 식사 내내 이런저런 사소한 이야깃거리를 짜내야 했다. 함께했던 마지막 식사 때로 두 사람의 생각이 너무 강하게 되돌아가지 않도록 하기 위해서였다. 그때는 병실에서 무슨 소리나 기별이 오지 않을까 줄곧 마음 졸이며 귀를 기울이고 있었다.\n\n아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 그녀는 아버지에게 장례 이야기를 꺼내기로 마음먹었다. 아버지는 고개를 저으면서도 그녀가 내놓는 모든 제안에 동의했다. 그 제안들 가운데 여럿은 서로 정면으로 어긋나는데도 그랬다. 마거릿은 아버지에게서 아무 실질적인 결정도 얻어 내지 못했다. 딕슨과 의논하려고 맥없이 방을 나서려는데, 헤일 씨가 손짓으로 그녀를 다시 곁으로 불렀다.\n\n“벨 씨에게 물어보아라.” 아버지가 공허한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“벨 씨요!” 그녀가 조금 놀라 말했다. “옥스퍼드의 벨 씨 말씀이세요?”\n\n“벨 씨.” 아버지가 되풀이했다. “그래. 그 사람이 내 신랑 들러리였다.”\n\n마거릿은 그 연상의 까닭을 알아차렸다.\n\n“오늘 편지 쓸게요.” 그녀가 말했다. 아버지는 다시 무기력 속으로 가라앉았다. 아침 내내 그녀는 쉬기를 간절히 바라면서도 서글픈 일거리의 소용돌이 속에서 쉬지 않고 애썼다.\n\n저녁 무렵 딕슨이 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“해 버렸습니다요, 아가씨. 저는 정말이지 주인어른이 슬픔에 겨워 졸도라도 하실까 겁이 났어요. 온종일 가엾은 마님 곁에만 계셨거든요. 제가 문에 귀를 대 보면 마님이 살아 계시기라도 한 듯 자꾸자꾸 말을 거시는 소리가 들렸고요. 제가 들어가면 아주 조용해지시지만 넋이 나간 것 같았지요. 그래서 저 혼자 생각했답니다. 어른을 좀 흔들어 깨워 드려야겠다, 처음에는 충격이 될지 몰라도 나중에는 오히려 나으실지 모른다고요. 그래서 가서 말씀드렸어요. 프레더릭 도련님이 여기 계시는 게 안전할 것 같지 않다고요. 사실 그렇고요. 바로 화요일에, 제가 밖에 나갔다가 사우샘프턴 사람을 하나 만났지 뭐예요. 밀턴에 온 뒤로 처음 본 사우샘프턴 사람이었어요. 이 위쪽까지는 잘들 안 올라오는 모양이더군요. 그런데 그게 하필 젊은 레너즈, 포목상 늙은 레너즈의 아들이었어요. 세상에 다시없을 건달이지요. 제 아비를 죽을 지경으로 들볶다가 바다로 달아난 놈이에요. 저는 도무지 그놈을 견딜 수가 없었어요. 프레더릭 도련님과 같은 때에 오라이언호에 타고 있었던 건 분명한데, 반란 때도 거기 있었는지는 기억이 안 나네요.”\n\n“그 사람이 딕슨을 알아보았어요?” 마거릿이 다급하게 물었다.\n\n“그게 바로 제일 나쁜 대목이랍니다. 제가 바보같이 그 이름을 불러 대지만 않았어도 저를 알아보지 못했을 거예요. 낯선 데서 만난 사우샘프턴 사람이니 그랬지, 아니었다면 그런 고약하고 쓸모없는 놈에게 그렇게 선뜻 아는 척을 했겠어요? 놈이 그러더군요. ‘딕슨 양! 여기서 뵐 줄 누가 알았겠습니까? 아니, 제가 잘못 알았나요, 이제는 딕슨 양이 아니신가요?’ 그래서 저는 아직 처녀로 불러도 좋다고, 다만 내가 그렇게 까다롭게 굴지만 않았어도 혼인할 기회는 얼마든지 있었다고 일러 주었지요. 놈은 제법 공손했어요. ‘저를 보고서야 그 말씀을 의심할 수가 있겠습니까’ 하더군요. 그렇지만 그런 놈이 던지는 그런 겨 같은 소리에 넘어갈 제가 아니지요. 그렇게 말해 주었고요. 그러고는 앙갚음 삼아 그놈 아비 안부를 물었답니다. 아비가 그놈을 집에서 내쫓은 걸 뻔히 알면서, 세상에 둘도 없는 사이라도 되는 양 말이지요. 그랬더니 저를 약 올리려고, 아시겠지만 서로 그렇게 점잖게 굴면서도 속으로는 점점 독이 오르던 참이었거든요, 프레더릭 도련님 안부를 캐묻기 시작하더군요. 도련님이 얼마나 큰일에 휘말렸는지 하면서요. 마치 프레더릭 도련님이 저지른 일이 조지 레너즈를 희게 씻어 주기라도 한다는 듯이, 아니면 그 시커멓고 더러운 꼴을 달리 보이게 해 주기라도 한다는 듯이 말이에요. 잡히기만 하면 반란죄로 목이 매달릴 거라는 둥, 붙잡는 사람에게 100파운드 현상금이 걸렸다는 둥, 집안에 얼마나 먹칠을 했느냐는 둥. 다 저를 약 올리려는 거였어요, 아가씨. 예전에 사우샘프턴에서 제가 늙은 레너즈 씨를 도와 조지를 톡톡히 나무란 적이 있거든요. 그래서 제가 말해 주었지요. 내가 아는 집안 중에는 제 자식 때문에 낯을 붉힐 까닭이 그보다 훨씬 많은 집도 있고, 그 자식이 집에서 멀리 떨어져 정직하게 벌어먹고 산다고만 여길 수 있어도 감사할 집이 있다고요. 그랬더니 그 뻔뻔한 놈이 뭐라는 줄 아세요? 자기는 남이 믿고 맡기는 자리에 있으니, 못된 길로 빠졌다가 마음을 잡고 싶어 하는 젊은이를 제가 알거든 기꺼이 뒤를 봐주겠다지 뭐예요. 저놈이요! 성인군자도 물들일 놈이에요. 요 며칠 전 그놈과 마주 서서 말을 섞을 때만큼 속이 상한 적이 여러 해 만에 처음이었답니다. 그놈을 더 매섭게 쏘아붙이지 못한 게 분해서 눈물이 다 날 뻔했어요. 놈은 제 얼굴에다 대고 계속 웃기만 했거든요. 제 빈정거림을 죄다 참말로 받아들이기라도 하는 것처럼요. 저는 그놈의 말마다 속이 뒤집히는데, 놈은 제 말은 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않는 눈치였어요.”\n\n“그렇지만 우리 이야기는 하지 않았지요? 프레더릭 오빠 이야기도요?”\n\n“안 했지요.” 딕슨이 말했다. “그놈은 제가 어디 묵는지 물어볼 염치도 없었고, 물었다 한들 말해 주지 않았을 거예요. 저도 그 잘난 자리가 뭔지 묻지 않았고요. 놈은 승합마차를 기다리고 있었는데 마침 마차가 와서 손을 흔들어 세우더군요. 그런데 끝까지 저를 약 올리려고 올라타기 전에 돌아서서 이러는 거예요. ‘딕슨 양, 헤일 중위를 잡는 걸 도와주시면 현상금을 반씩 나눕시다. 저와 짝이 되고 싶으시죠, 안 그렇습니까? 부끄러워 마시고 그러자고 하세요.’ 그러고는 마차에 훌쩍 올라탔어요. 마지막까지 약을 올렸다고 좋아하며 그 흉한 얼굴로 심술궂게 히죽거리며 저를 곁눈질하는 게 보이더군요.”\n\n딕슨의 이 이야기에 마거릿은 몹시 불안해졌다.\n\n“오빠에게 말했어요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“아니요.” 딕슨이 말했다. “그 못된 레너즈가 이 도시에 있다는 걸 알고 마음이 편치 않았지만, 달리 생각할 일이 하도 많아서 거기까지 마음을 쓰지 못했어요. 그런데 주인어른이 그렇게 굳은 채로 앉아 계시고 눈은 그렇게 흐릿하고 서글픈 것을 보니, 프레더릭 도련님이 숨어 지내는 일을 생각하시게 하면 정신이 드실까 싶었지요. 도련님은 가셔야 해요, 딱하시게도. 벨 씨가 오시기 전에요.”\n\n“아, 벨 씨는 두렵지 않아요. 그렇지만 그 레너즈라는 사람은 두려워요. 오빠에게 말해야겠어요. 레너즈는 어떻게 생겼던가요?”\n\n“못되게 생긴 놈이지요, 아가씨. 정말이랍니다. 구레나룻은 어찌나 빨간지 제가 다 부끄러울 지경이고요. 남이 믿고 맡기는 자리에 있다고 큰소리는 쳤어도, 옷차림은 영락없는 노동자처럼 퍼스티언 천이더군요.”\n\n프레더릭이 떠나야 한다는 것은 분명했다. 그것도 그가 그토록 온전히 제자리로 뛰어들어 아버지와 누이에게 든든한 기둥이자 지팡이가 되어 주겠다고 약속한 마당에. 살아 계실 적 어머니를 향한 보살핌과 돌아가신 어머니를 향한 슬픔이 그를, 우리에게서 떠나간 이를 함께 사랑함으로써 우리와 맺어지는 그 각별한 사람들 가운데 하나로 만들어 준 마당에. 마거릿이 응접실 난롯가에 앉아 이 모든 것을 생각하고 있을 때—아버지는 아직 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 새로 일깨워진 이 두려움에 눌려 안절부절못하고 있었다—프레더릭이 들어왔다. 밝은 빛은 흐려졌지만 슬픔의 사나운 물결은 지나간 뒤였다. 그는 마거릿에게 다가와 이마에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“얼굴이 어쩌면 그리 핼쑥하니, 마거릿!” 그가 나직한 목소리로 말했다. “너는 모두를 생각해 주었는데 아무도 너를 생각해 주지 않았구나. 이 소파에 누우렴. 네가 할 일은 아무것도 없다.”\n\n“그게 제일 견디기 힘든 일이에요.” 마거릿이 서글픈 속삭임으로 말했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 가서 누웠고, 오빠는 숄로 그녀의 발을 덮어 준 뒤 곁의 바닥에 앉았다. 그리고 두 사람은 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n마거릿은 딕슨이 젊은 레너즈와 마주친 이야기를 들려준 그대로 오빠에게 옮겼다. 프레더릭의 입술이 길게 휘파람 같은 낭패의 소리를 내며 다물렸다.\n\n“그 젊은 놈과 한번 담판을 지어 보고 싶구나. 그놈보다 못한 뱃사람은 배에 타 본 적이 없고, 사람됨도 그만큼이나 못했지. 정말이지, 마거릿, 너 그 일이 어떻게 된 건지 전부 알고 있지?”\n\n“네, 어머니가 말씀해 주셨어요.”\n\n“그래, 쓸 만한 뱃사람이라면 다들 우리 함장에게 분개하고 있을 때 그놈은 비위를 맞추겠다고, 쳇! 그런 놈이 여기 와 있다니! 아, 내가 20마일 안에 있다는 낌새라도 챘다간 묵은 원한을 죄다 갚으려고 나를 기어이 캐낼 거다. 저 악당이 가져가느니 차라리 다른 누구라도 사람들이 내 목에 걸었다는 100파운드를 챙기는 게 낫겠어. 가엾은 늙은 딕슨을 구슬려 나를 넘기게 하고 노후 밑천이나 마련해 주지 못하는 게 안타깝군!”\n\n“아, 오빠, 그만해요! 그런 말 하지 마세요.”\n\n헤일 씨가 다급하게 몸을 떨며 그들에게 다가왔다. 그는 두 사람이 하는 말을 엿들었던 것이다. 그는 두 손으로 프레더릭의 손을 잡았다.\n\n“얘야, 너는 가야 한다. 참으로 괴로운 일이다만, 아무래도 가야겠구나. 너는 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다. 네가 그 사람에게 위안이 되어 주었어.”\n\n“아, 아버지, 오빠가 꼭 가야 하나요?” 마거릿이 그럴 수밖에 없다는 자신의 확신을 거스르며 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n“정말이지 차라리 맞서서 재판을 받아 보고 싶은 심정입니다. 증거만 그러모을 수 있다면! 레너즈 같은 악당의 손아귀에 놓인다는 생각은 견딜 수가 없어요. 다른 사정만 아니었다면 이 몰래 온 방문이 즐거울 뻔했는데요. 어느 프랑스 부인이 금지된 즐거움에 있다고 한 그 온갖 매력이 이 방문에 다 있었으니까요.”\n\n“제가 기억하는 가장 오래된 일 가운데 하나가,” 마거릿이 말했다. “오빠가 사과를 훔쳐서 크게 야단맞던 일이에요, 프레드 오빠. 우리 집에도 사과가 잔뜩 있었잖아요. 나무마다 주렁주렁 달렸는데. 그런데 누가 훔친 과일이 제일 달다고 오빠에게 일러 주었고, 오빠는 그 말을 글자 그대로 받아들여 훔치러 나갔지요. 오빠는 그때 이후로 마음 씀씀이가 별로 달라지지 않았네요.”\n\n“그래, 너는 가야 한다.” 헤일 씨가 마거릿이 한참 전에 던진 물음에 답하며 되풀이했다. 그의 생각은 한 가지에만 붙박여 있었고, 자식들의 종잡을 수 없는 이야기를 좇는 것은 그에게 애를 써야 하는 일이었다. 그리고 그는 그런 애를 쓰지 않았다.\n\n마거릿과 프레더릭은 서로를 바라보았다. 오빠가 떠나고 나면 그 순간적으로 통하는 마음은 더 이상 둘의 것이 아닐 터였다. 말로 옮길 수 없는 그 많은 것이 눈길을 통해 오갔다. 두 사람은 같은 생각을 좇다가 슬픔 속에서 그것을 놓쳤다. 프레더릭이 먼저 그것을 떨쳐 냈다.\n\n“있잖니, 마거릿, 오늘 오후에 딕슨과 나를 둘 다 크게 놀라게 할 뻔했다. 나는 내 방에 있었는데, 현관에서 초인종 소리가 난 것은 들었지만 벨을 누른 사람이 볼일을 마치고 진작에 갔으려니 했지. 그래서 막 복도로 나서려던 참이었는데, 방문을 여니 딕슨이 계단을 내려오고 있더구나. 딕슨은 얼굴을 찌푸리며 나를 도로 숨으라고 밀어 넣었지. 나는 문을 열어 둔 채로 있었는데, 아버지 서재에 있던 어떤 사람에게 전갈을 전하는 소리가 들리더니 그 사람이 가더라. 대체 무슨 일이었을까? 가게 사람인가?”\n\n“그럴 거예요.” 마거릿이 무심하게 말했다. “2시쯤에 주문을 받으러 온 자그맣고 조용한 사람이 있었어요.”\n\n“그런데 이건 자그마한 사람이 아니었어. 덩치가 크고 힘깨나 쓰게 생긴 사내였고, 여기 왔을 때는 4시가 넘어서였다.”\n\n“손턴 씨였다.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. 두 사람은 아버지를 대화 속으로 끌어들이게 되어 반가웠다.\n\n“손턴 씨요!” 마거릿이 조금 놀라 말했다. “저는 또…….”\n\n“그래, 우리 꼬마 아가씨, 뭐라고 생각했는데?” 그녀가 말을 맺지 않자 프레더릭이 물었다.\n\n“아, 그냥,” 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 똑바로 바라보고 말했다. “오빠가 다른 계층의 사람, 신사가 아닌 사람, 무슨 심부름으로 온 사람을 말하는 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“그런 부류로 보이던걸.” 프레더릭이 대수롭지 않게 말했다. “나는 가게 사람인 줄 알았는데 공장주라니.”\n\n마거릿은 잠자코 있었다. 그녀는 처음에, 그의 사람됨을 알기 전에 자기도 꼭 프레더릭처럼 그를 말하고 그렇게 생각했던 일을 떠올렸다. 오빠가 받은 것은 자연스러운 인상일 뿐이었으나, 그래도 그녀는 그것이 조금 언짢았다. 그녀는 말하고 싶지 않았다. 손턴 씨가 어떤 사람인지 프레더릭에게 알려 주고 싶었다. 그러나 혀가 굳은 듯 말이 나오지 않았다.\n\n헤일 씨가 말을 이었다. “자기가 할 수 있는 도움이라면 무엇이든 주겠다고 온 모양이더라. 그렇지만 나는 그를 만날 수가 없었다. 딕슨더러 그에게 너를 보고 싶은지 물어보라고 했지. 너를 찾아보라고, 그러면 네가 그에게 가겠다고 한 것 같기도 하고. 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠구나.”\n\n“아주 마음에 맞는 지인이었나 보군요?” 프레더릭이 누구든 잡고 싶은 사람이 잡으라는 듯 공을 던지듯 물었다.\n\n“아주 친절한 벗이지요.” 아버지가 대답하지 않자 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n프레더릭은 한동안 말이 없었다. 마침내 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n“마거릿, 너에게 친절을 베푼 사람들에게 내가 영영 고맙다는 말을 할 수 없다니 마음이 아프구나. 네 지인과 내 지인은 따로일 수밖에 없겠지. 내가 군법회의를 무릅쓰지 않는 한, 아니면 너와 아버지가 스페인으로 오시지 않는 한.” 그는 이 마지막 말을 슬쩍 떠보듯 던져 놓고는 불쑥 뛰어들었다. “두 분이 와 주시기를 제가 얼마나 바라는지 모르실 겁니다. 저는 자리도 괜찮고 더 나아질 가망도 있어요.” 그는 처녀처럼 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 이었다. “마거릿, 내가 말한 그 돌로레스 바버 말이다. 네가 그 사람을 알기만 하면 좋겠구나. 너도 분명 좋아할 거야. 아니, 사랑이라는 말이 맞겠다, 좋아한다는 말은 너무 약하니까. 아버지도 그 사람을 아시면 사랑하실 겁니다. 아직 18살도 되지 않았어요. 그렇지만 한 해가 더 지나도 마음이 그대로면 제 아내가 될 거예요. 바버 씨가 아직 약혼이라고는 부르지 못하게 하지만요. 그래도 오시기만 하면 돌로레스 말고도 곳곳에서 벗을 만나실 겁니다. 생각해 보세요, 아버지. 마거릿, 너도 내 편을 들어라.”\n\n“아니다. 나는 더는 옮겨 다니지 않겠다.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “한 번 옮긴 것이 내게서 아내를 앗아 갔다. 이 생에서 더 이상의 이사는 없다. 그 사람이 여기 있을 테니, 나도 여기서 내게 정해진 때를 다 채우겠다.”\n\n“아, 오빠,” 마거릿이 말했다. “그분 이야기를 더 해 주세요. 이런 일은 생각도 못 했는데, 정말 기뻐요. 저쪽에서 오빠를 사랑하고 보살펴 줄 사람이 생기는 거잖아요. 다 이야기해 주세요.”\n\n“우선, 그 사람은 로마 가톨릭이란다. 그게 내가 예상한 유일한 걸림돌이었지. 하지만 아버지의 생각이 바뀌셨으니……. 아니다, 마거릿, 한숨 쉬지 마라.”\n\n마거릿은 대화가 끝나기 전에 한숨을 조금 더 쉴 만한 까닭을 얻었다. 프레더릭 자신이 아직 공표하지는 않았을 뿐 사실상 로마 가톨릭이었던 것이다. 아버지가 국교회를 떠난 일로 그녀가 극심한 괴로움을 겪을 때 그가 편지에서 그토록 희미하게만 동정을 표했던 까닭이 바로 이것이었다. 그녀는 그것을 뱃사람다운 무심함이라고 여겼었다. 그러나 진실은, 그때 이미 그 자신도 세례를 받은 종교의 형식을 버릴 쪽으로 마음이 기울어 있었다는 것, 다만 그의 견해가 아버지의 그것과는 정반대 방향으로 향하고 있었다는 것이었다. 이 변화에 사랑이 얼마나 관여했는지는 프레더릭 자신도 말할 수 없었을 것이다. 마거릿은 마침내 이 갈래의 이야기는 그만두었다. 그리고 약혼이라는 사실로 돌아와 그것을 새로운 빛 아래에서 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n“그렇지만 그분을 위해서라도, 프레드 오빠, 오빠에게 씌워진 부풀려진 혐의만큼은 벗으려고 애써야 하지 않겠어요? 반란이라는 혐의 자체는 사실이라 하더라도요. 군법회의가 열리고 증인을 찾을 수만 있다면, 적어도 오빠가 상관의 명령을 거스른 것은 그 권한이 부당하게 행사되었기 때문이라는 것쯤은 밝힐 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n헤일 씨가 아들의 대답을 들으려고 몸을 일으켰다.\n\n“우선, 마거릿, 내 증인들을 누가 찾아다니겠니? 그들은 죄다 뱃사람이고 다른 배로 흩어졌다. 남은 건 그 일에 가담했거나 동조한 자들뿐인데, 그들의 증언은 거의 값이 없지. 다음으로, 이런 말을 해도 될지 모르겠다만, 너는 군법회의라는 것이 어떤 자리인지 모른다. 정의가 집행되는 자리라고 여기는 모양인데 실은 그렇지 않아. 저울에서 권위가 10분의 9를 차지하고 증거는 나머지 10분의 1밖에 되지 않는 법정이란다. 그런 자리에서는 증거 자체도 권위의 위세에 휘둘리는 것을 피하기 어렵지.”\n\n“그래도 오빠를 위해 얼마나 많은 증거를 찾아내어 늘어놓을 수 있을지 해 볼 만한 일 아닌가요? 지금은 예전에 오빠를 알던 사람들 모두가 오빠를 변명의 여지도 없는 죄인으로 여기고 있어요. 오빠는 한 번도 스스로를 변호하려 하지 않았고, 우리도 오빠의 결백을 밝힐 증거를 어디서 찾아야 할지 알지 못했고요. 이제 바버 양을 위해서라도 오빠의 행동을 세상의 눈앞에 되도록 분명하게 밝히세요. 그분은 그런 것을 개의치 않을지도 몰라요. 우리 모두가 그러하듯 그분도 오빠를 믿고 있을 테니까요. 그렇지만 이렇게 무거운 혐의를 쓴 사람과 인연을 맺게 하면서, 오빠의 처지가 정확히 어떤 것인지 세상에 보이지 않는 것은 도리가 아니에요. 오빠는 상관의 명령을 거슬렀어요. 그건 나쁜 일이지요. 그렇지만 그 권한이 잔인하게 휘둘리는 동안 말 한마디 몸짓 하나 없이 지켜보고만 있었다면 그건 비할 데 없이 더 나빴을 거예요. 사람들은 오빠가 무슨 일을 했는지는 알지만, 그것을 죄가 아니라 약한 이를 지켜 낸 영웅적인 행동으로 끌어올려 주는 그 동기는 모르잖아요. 돌로레스를 위해서라도 그들이 알아야 해요.”\n\n“그렇지만 그들에게 어떻게 알린단 말이냐? 나는 나를 재판할 사람들의 결백과 공정을 그다지 믿지 못하니, 진실을 말해 줄 증인을 한 줄로 세울 수 있다 해도 군법회의에 나를 내맡길 수는 없다. 네가 좋게도 영웅적인 행동이라고 부르는 것을 거리에서 크게 외치고 다니라고 포고꾼을 보낼 수도 없는 노릇이고. 일이 있고 나서 이렇게 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에 자기변호의 소책자를 낸다 한들 읽어 줄 사람도 없을 거다.”\n\n“죄를 벗을 가망이 얼마나 되는지 변호사와 상의해 보시겠어요?” 마거릿이 고개를 들며 얼굴이 몹시 붉어져 물었다.\n\n“먼저 그 변호사를 붙잡아 얼굴을 보고 마음에 드는지 살펴본 다음에야 속을 털어놓겠지. 사건 하나 못 맡은 법정 변호사라면 제 양심을 이리저리 비틀어, 나 같은 죄인을 법에 넘기는 착한 일로 100파운드를 손쉽게 벌 수 있다고 여길지도 모르니까.”\n\n“말도 안 돼요, 오빠! 저는 명예를 믿고 맡길 수 있는 변호사를 알고 있어요. 사람들이 그 일에 아주 밝다고 입을 모으고, 쇼 이모의 친척이라면 누구를 위해서든 수고를 아끼지 않을 분이에요. 헨리 레녹스 씨예요, 아버지.”\n\n“좋은 생각인 것 같구나.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “하지만 프레더릭을 영국에 붙들어 두는 일은 제안하지 마라. 네 어머니를 생각해서라도 그러지 마라.”\n\n“밤 기차로 런던에 가시면 돼요.” 마거릿이 자기 계획에 열이 올라 말을 이었다. “아무래도 오빠는 내일 떠나야 해요, 아버지.” 그녀가 다정하게 말했다. “벨 씨 일도 있고 딕슨이 만난 그 고약한 사람 일도 있어서 그렇게 정했어요.”\n\n“그래, 나는 내일 가야 한다.” 프레더릭이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n헤일 씨가 신음했다. “너를 보내는 것도 견딜 수 없는데, 네가 여기 머무는 동안은 불안해서 비참하구나.”\n\n“그럼 이렇게 해요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “제 계획을 들어 보세요. 오빠는 금요일 아침에 런던에 도착해요. 제가, 아니 오빠가, 아니에요! 레녹스 씨에게 보낼 쪽지를 제가 써 드리는 편이 낫겠어요. 템플에 있는 그분 사무실로 가시면 만날 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n“오라이언호에 탔던 사람들 가운데 기억나는 이름을 다 적어 두마. 그걸 그 사람에게 맡겨 찾아내게 하면 되겠지. 그 사람이 이디스 남편의 동생이지? 네 편지에서 이름을 본 기억이 나는구나. 바버 씨에게 맡겨 둔 돈이 있으니 성공할 가망만 있다면 꽤 긴 계산서라도 치를 수 있다. 아버지, 다른 데 쓰려던 돈이니 아버지와 마거릿에게서 빌린 셈만 치겠습니다.”\n\n“그러지 마세요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “그러면 오빠는 위험을 무릅쓰지 않을 테니까요. 그리고 이건 위험을 무릅쓰는 일이 될 거예요. 다만 해 볼 만한 일이고요. 리버풀에서만이 아니라 런던에서도 배를 탈 수 있지요?”\n\n“물론이지, 우리 바보 아가씨. 널빤지 밑에서 물이 출렁이는 것이 느껴지는 곳이면 어디든 내 집 같으니까. 나를 태워 갈 배야 어떻게든 구할 테니 걱정 마라. 런던에는 24시간도 머물지 않을 거다. 한쪽으로는 너와 떨어져, 다른 한쪽으로는 또 다른 누군가와 떨어져서 말이야.”\n\n마거릿이 레녹스 씨에게 편지를 쓰는 동안 프레더릭이 어깨너머로 들여다볼 생각을 한 것은 그녀에게 오히려 다행이었다. 그렇게 흔들림 없이 간결하게 계속 써 내려가지 않을 수 없었기에 망정이지, 아니었다면 그녀는 숱한 낱말 앞에서 머뭇거렸을 테고 숱한 표현 가운데 무엇을 고를지 몰라 애를 먹었을 것이다. 마지막 일이 양쪽 모두에게 그토록 언짢았던 그 왕래를 먼저 다시 트는 어색함 속에서 말이다. 그러나 쪽지는 그녀가 다시 훑어볼 겨를도 없이 손에서 건네졌고, 수첩 안에 소중히 간직되었다. 그 수첩에서 검은 머리카락 한 타래가 길게 떨어졌고, 그것을 본 프레더릭의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였다.\n\n“자, 이걸 보고 싶겠지?” 그가 말했다. “안 된다! 그 사람을 직접 볼 때까지 기다려야 해. 그 사람은 조각으로 알기에는 너무나 완전하니까. 내 궁전을 짓는 데는 어떤 볼품없는 벽돌 하나도 본보기가 될 수 없어.”\n\n# 제32장\n\n「무엇이라! 남아서 고발당하고, 어쩌면 쇠사슬에 묶인 채 끌려가라는 말인가.」 — 베르너.\n\n이튿날 온종일 그들은 함께 앉아 있었다 — 세 사람이. 헤일 씨는 자식들이 물음을 던져 그를 억지로 현재로 끌어낼 때 말고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았다. 프레더릭의 슬픔은 이제 보이지도 들리지도 않았다. 첫 발작은 지나갔고, 이제 그는 감정에 그토록 무너져 내렸던 것이 부끄러웠다. 어머니를 잃은 슬픔은 깊고 참된 것이어서 평생을 갈 것이었지만, 다시는 입에 올리지 않을 것이었다. 마거릿은 처음에는 그만큼 격하지 않았으나 지금은 더 괴로웠다. 이따금 그녀는 한참을 울었다. 다른 이야기를 할 때조차 그녀의 태도에는 애처로운 다정함이 배어 있었고, 눈길이 프레더릭에게 닿아 머지않아 다가올 그의 떠남을 떠올릴 때면 그것이 한층 짙어졌다. 아버지를 생각하면 그가 떠나는 것이 다행이었다. 그녀 자신으로서는 아무리 그것이 서럽더라도 그랬다. 아들이 발각되어 붙잡히지나 않을까 하는 두려움 속에서 헤일 씨가 살고 있던 그 조바심은 아들이 곁에 있는 데서 얻는 기쁨을 훨씬 웃돌았다. 그 초조함은 헤일 부인이 세상을 떠난 뒤로 더해졌는데, 아마도 그가 그 생각만을 골똘히 붙들고 있었기 때문이리라. 그는 낯선 소리만 나도 흠칫했고, 프레더릭이 방에 들어오는 사람의 눈에 곧바로 띄지 않는 자리에 앉아 있지 않으면 도무지 마음을 놓지 못했다. 저녁 무렵 그가 말했다.\n\n“마거릿, 네가 프레더릭과 함께 역에 가 주겠니? 저 애가 무사히 떠나는 것을 알아야겠구나. 적어도 저 애가 밀턴을 벗어났다는 소식은 네가 전해 주겠지?”\n\n“물론이지요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “저도 그러고 싶어요. 제가 없어도 아버지가 외롭지 않으시다면요.”\n\n“아니다, 아니야! 네가 배웅했다고 말해 주지 않으면, 나는 누군가 저 애를 알아보고 붙잡아 세운 것은 아닐까 하는 상상만 하고 있을 게다. 그리고 아웃우드 역으로 가거라. 거리도 비슷하고 사람도 그리 많지 않으니. 거기까지는 마차를 타고 가려무나. 눈에 띌 위험이 덜하겠지. 기차가 몇 시냐, 프레드?”\n\n“6시 10분이에요. 거의 캄캄해질 무렵이지요. 그러면 마거릿, 너는 어떻게 할 거니?”\n\n“아, 저는 어떻게든 할 수 있어요. 요즘은 아주 용감하고 아주 단단해졌는걸요. 어두워진다 해도 집까지 내내 등불이 환한 길이에요. 게다가 지난주에는 이보다 훨씬 늦게까지 밖에 있었는걸요.”\n\n마거릿은 작별이 끝났을 때 고마운 마음이 들었다 — 죽은 어머니와의 작별, 그리고 살아 있는 아버지와의 작별이. 그녀는 프레더릭을 서둘러 마차에 태웠다. 아버지에게 그토록 쓰라리게 괴로워 보이는 장면을 조금이라도 줄이기 위해서였다. 아버지는 아들이 어머니를 마지막으로 보러 갈 때 굳이 따라 들어갔던 것이다. 얼마쯤은 그 때문에, 또 얼마쯤은 작은 역에 기차가 닿는 시각에 대해 “철도 안내서”에 흔히 있는 오류 가운데 하나 때문에, 두 사람은 아웃우드에 이르러 20분 가까이 시간이 남는다는 것을 알았다. 매표소는 아직 열려 있지 않아 표조차 끊을 수 없었다. 그래서 그들은 철길 아래 지면으로 이어지는 계단을 따라 내려갔다. 마찻길과 나란히 놓인 들판을 비스듬히 가로지르는 널찍한 잿길이 있었고, 두 사람은 남은 몇 분 동안 그곳을 오락가락 거닐려고 그리로 갔다.\n\n마거릿의 손이 프레더릭의 팔에 놓여 있었다. 그가 다정하게 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n“마거릿! 나는 레녹스 씨를 찾아가서 내 혐의를 벗을 가망이 있는지 의논해 볼 참이야. 그래야 내가 원할 때 언제든 영국으로 돌아올 수 있을 테니까. 다른 누구보다도 너를 위해서야. 아버지께 무슨 일이라도 생기면 네가 얼마나 외로운 처지가 될지, 생각만 해도 견딜 수가 없구나. 아버지는 몰라보게 변하셨어. 몹시 흔들리고 계셔. 여러 가지로 보아 아버지가 카디스 계획을 생각해 보시도록 네가 해 드리면 좋겠는데. 아버지가 떠나시고 나면 너는 어떻게 하겠니? 가까이 벗도 없잖아. 우리 집안은 이상하리만치 친척이 없으니 말이야.”\n\n프레더릭이 다정한 근심으로 그녀 앞에 그려 보이는 그 일을 마거릿 자신도 그리 있음직하지 않다고 여기지는 않던 터라, 그녀는 눈물을 참기가 어려웠다. 지난 몇 달의 근심이 헤일 씨를 그토록 심하게 갉아먹었던 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 애써 기운을 차리며 말했다.\n\n“지난 두 해 동안 제 삶에는 참으로 이상하고 뜻밖인 변화들이 있었어요. 그래서 저는 어느 때보다도 이렇게 느껴요. 앞으로 어떤 일이 생기면 어떻게 할까를 너무 꼼꼼히 따져 보는 건 부질없다고요. 저는 오직 지금만 생각하려고 해요.” 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 두 사람은 길로 이어지는 층계 담의 들판 쪽 바로 곁에 잠시 가만히 서 있었다. 지는 해가 그들의 얼굴에 내렸다. 프레더릭은 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채 애틋한 근심을 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 거기서 그녀가 말로는 드러내지 않을 근심과 시름을 읽어 냈다. 그녀가 말을 이었다.\n\n“우리 자주 편지해요. 그리고 약속할게요 — 그래야 오빠 마음이 놓일 테니까 — 제 걱정거리를 하나도 빼놓지 않고 다 알려 드릴게요. 아버지는…” 그녀는 흠칫했다. 거의 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 작은 움찔거림이었지만, 프레더릭은 자기가 쥐고 있던 손의 갑작스러운 움직임을 느끼고 길 쪽으로 얼굴을 온전히 돌렸다. 그 길을 따라 말 탄 사람이 천천히 지나가고 있었다. 마침 두 사람이 서 있는 바로 그 층계 담을 스쳐 지나는 참이었다. 마거릿이 목례했다. 그 인사는 뻣뻣하게 돌아왔다.\n\n“저 사람은 누구야?” 그가 미처 소리 닿지 않는 곳으로 가기도 전에 프레더릭이 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 조금 고개를 떨구고 조금 낯을 붉힌 채 대답했다. “손턴 씨예요. 오빠도 전에 뵌 적 있잖아요.”\n\n“뒷모습만 봤지. 볼품없이 생긴 사람이더구나. 저 찌푸린 얼굴 좀 봐!”\n\n“무슨 언짢은 일이 있었나 봐요.” 마거릿이 감싸듯 말했다. “어머니 곁에 있을 때의 그분을 보셨다면 볼품없다고 여기지 않으셨을 거예요.”\n\n“이제 가서 표를 끊어야 할 때가 된 모양이구나. 이렇게 어두워질 줄 알았더라면 마차를 돌려보내지 않았을 텐데, 마거릿.”\n\n“아, 그런 건 마음 쓰지 마세요. 원하면 여기서 마차를 잡을 수도 있고, 아니면 기차로 돌아가도 돼요. 밀턴 역사에서부터는 내내 가게며 사람이며 등불이 있을 테니까요. 제 걱정은 마시고 오빠나 조심하세요. 레너즈가 오빠와 같은 기차에 타고 있을지도 모른다는 생각만 해도 속이 메스꺼워요. 타기 전에 객실을 잘 들여다보세요.”\n\n두 사람은 역으로 돌아갔다. 마거릿은 표를 끊으러 가스등이 활활 타오르는 환한 안쪽으로 자기가 들어가겠다고 우겼다. 빈둥거리는 듯한 젊은 사내 몇이 역장 곁에서 어슬렁대고 있었다. 마거릿은 그중 한 사람의 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같았고, 감탄을 감추지도 않은 채 다소 무례하게 빤히 쳐다보는 그에게 모욕당한 이의 도도한 눈길을 돌려주었다. 그녀는 바깥에 서 있던 오빠에게 서둘러 다가가 그의 팔을 붙들었다. “가방은 챙기셨어요? 여기 승강장을 좀 걸어요.” 그녀가 말했다. 머지않아 혼자 남게 된다는 생각에 조금 당황했고, 스스로에게조차 인정하고 싶지 않을 만큼 빠르게 용기가 새어 나가고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 포석을 따라 자기들 뒤를 밟는 발소리를 들었다. 두 사람이 멈추자 그 발소리도 멈추었다. 그들은 선로를 따라 멀리 내다보며 다가오는 기차의 쇳소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 두 사람은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 가슴이 너무 벅찼던 것이다. 잠깐이면 기차가 이곳에 닿을 것이고, 다시 1분이면 그는 떠나고 없을 터였다. 마거릿은 그에게 런던으로 가라고 그토록 다급히 졸랐던 것을 거의 뉘우쳤다. 그것은 발각될 기회를 그의 앞길에 더 많이 던져 놓는 일이었다. 리버풀에서 스페인으로 가는 배를 탔더라면 두세 시간 안에 떠날 수 있었을 텐데.\n\n프레더릭이 몸을 돌려 등불을 정면으로 마주 보았다. 기차가 온다는 기별에 가스 불꽃이 생생하게 솟구치던 참이었다. 철도 짐꾼 차림의 사내 하나가 앞으로 나섰다. 험상궂은 사내였는데, 술을 마셔 사나운 지경에 이른 듯하면서도 정신만은 멀쩡해 보였다.\n\n“실례합니다, 아가씨!” 그가 이렇게 말하며 마거릿을 한쪽으로 거칠게 밀치고 프레더릭의 멱살을 붙잡았다.\n\n“당신 이름이 헤일이지, 아마?”\n\n눈앞의 모든 것이 어지럽게 흔들려 마거릿은 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 보지 못했다. 그러나 순식간에, 어떤 씨름 솜씨로 프레더릭이 그의 발을 걸어 넘어뜨렸고, 승강장은 철길 옆 물렁한 땅바닥보다 3, 4피트쯤 높이 솟아 있었는데 그는 그 높이에서 떨어졌다. 그는 그대로 거기 누워 있었다.\n\n“어서 뛰어요, 어서요!” 마거릿이 헐떡이며 말했다. “기차가 왔어요. 레너즈였죠, 그렇죠? 아, 어서요! 가방은 제가 들게요.” 그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡아 가녀린 힘을 다해 그를 밀어 보냈다. 객실 문 하나가 열렸다. 그가 뛰어올랐다. 그리고 그가 몸을 내밀어 “신의 가호가 있기를, 마거릿!” 하고 말하는 순간 기차가 그녀 옆을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그녀는 홀로 남겨졌다. 그녀는 지독히 메스껍고 어지러워서 부인 대합실로 들어가 잠깐 앉을 수 있는 것이 고마울 지경이었다. 처음에는 숨을 몰아쉬는 것 말고 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 너무나 황급했고, 너무나 속을 뒤집는 놀라움이었으며, 너무나 아슬아슬했다. 그 순간에 기차가 와 있지 않았더라면 그 사내는 다시 일어나 그를 붙잡으라고 사람들을 불렀을 것이다. 그녀는 그 사내가 일어났을까 궁금했다. 그가 움직이는 것을 보았는지 떠올려 보려 애썼다. 그가 크게 다쳤을 수도 있을까 궁금했다. 그녀는 큰맘 먹고 밖으로 나갔다. 승강장은 온통 환했지만 여전히 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 끝까지 가서 다소 겁먹은 채 아래를 내려다보았다. 아무도 없었다. 그러자 그녀는 스스로를 다그쳐 가서 살펴보기를 잘했다고 여겼다. 그러지 않았더라면 끔찍한 생각들이 그녀의 꿈자리를 따라다녔을 테니까. 그런데도 그녀는 어찌나 떨리고 겁이 나는지, 길을 따라 집까지 걸어갈 수는 없겠다고 느꼈다. 역의 불빛 속에서 내려다보는 그 길은 정말이지 외지고 캄캄해 보였다. 그녀는 하행 열차가 올 때까지 기다렸다가 거기 자리를 잡기로 했다. 그러나 레너즈가 그녀를 프레더릭의 일행으로 알아본다면 어쩐단 말인가! 그녀는 표를 끊으러 매표소에 들어가기 전에 사방을 살폈다. 철도 직원 몇 사람만이 서서 서로 큰 소리로 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n“레너즈가 또 술을 마셨다지!” 윗사람인 듯한 하나가 말했다. “이번에는 자리를 지키려면 그 잘난 뒷배를 죄다 끌어대야 할걸.”\n\n“그자는 어디 있나?” 다른 하나가 물었다. 그동안 마거릿은 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 떨리는 손가락으로 거스름돈을 세고 있었고, 이 물음에 대한 답을 들을 때까지는 감히 돌아설 엄두를 내지 못했다.\n\n“몰라. 5분도 채 안 돼서 들어왔지. 넘어졌다나 어쨌다나 하는 이야기를 길게 늘어놓으면서 험하게 욕을 퍼붓더군. 그러더니 다음 상행 열차로 런던에 간다면서 내게 돈을 좀 꾸어 달라지 뭔가. 술김에 온갖 약속을 다 하는데, 나야 그런 소리나 듣고 있을 만큼 한가하지 않아서 네 볼일이나 보라고 했지. 그랬더니 정문으로 나가 버리더군.”\n\n“가까운 술집에 가 있을 게 뻔하지.” 처음에 말한 이가 말했다. “자네가 어리석게 빌려주었더라면 자네 돈도 거기로 갔을 거야.”\n\n“내가 그럴까 봐! 그자가 말하는 런던이 무슨 뜻인지 나는 잘 알지. 아니 글쎄, 그 5실링도 아직 갚지 않았다니까.” 그들의 이야기는 그렇게 이어졌다.\n\n이제 마거릿의 온 마음은 기차가 오기만을 바라고 있었다. 그녀는 다시 한번 부인 대합실에 몸을 숨긴 채, 무슨 소리든 다 레너즈의 발소리로, 크고 왁자한 목소리는 다 그의 것으로 여겨졌다. 그러나 기차가 들어설 때까지 아무도 그녀 곁에 오지 않았다. 그때 짐꾼 하나가 정중하게 그녀를 객실에 태워 주었는데, 그녀는 기차가 움직일 때까지 감히 그의 얼굴을 쳐다보지 못하다가 그제야 그것이 레너즈의 얼굴이 아님을 알았다.\n\n# 제33장\n\n“잠들어 있으라, 내 사랑이여, 그대의 차가운 잠자리에서, 다시는 어지럽혀지지 않기를! 나의 마지막 밤 인사 — 그대는 내가 그대의 운명을 뒤따를 때까지 깨어나지 않으리라.” — 킹 박사.\n\n그 모든 공포와 요란한 소동이 지나간 뒤라 집 안은 부자연스러울 만큼 조용해 보였다. 아버지는 돌아온 딸이 요기할 수 있도록 준비를 빠짐없이 시켜 두었고, 그러고는 늘 앉던 의자에 다시 앉아 서글픈 백일몽 가운데 하나에 잠겨 들었다. 딕슨은 부엌에서 메리 히긴스를 붙들고 나무라고 이것저것 시키는 중이었다. 그 잔소리는 성난 속삭임으로 전해졌지만 그렇다고 기세가 덜하지도 않았다. 집 안에 죽은 이가 누워 있는 동안 목소리를 숨결 위로 올리는 것은 불경하다고 여겼기 때문이다. 마거릿은 마지막에 겪은 그 절정의 놀라움을 아버지에게 말하지 않기로 마음먹었다. 이야기해 봐야 소용이 없었다. 잘 끝난 일이었다. 다만 두려운 것은 레너즈가 어떻게든 돈을 마련해 프레더릭을 뒤따라 런던으로 가서 그를 찾아내려는 뜻을 이루지나 않을까 하는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 그런 계획이 성공하지 못할 가능성이 압도적으로 컸다. 마거릿은 자기가 막을 수 없는 일을 생각하며 스스로를 괴롭히지 않기로 결심했다. 프레더릭은 그녀가 일러 줄 수 있는 만큼 경계하고 있을 터였고, 길어야 하루 이틀이면 무사히 잉글랜드를 벗어날 것이었다.\n\n“내일이면 벨 씨한테서 소식이 오겠지요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“그래.” 아버지가 대답했다. “그렇겠지.”\n\n“오실 수 있다면 내일 저녁에는 여기 닿으시겠네요.”\n\n“오시지 못하면 손턴 씨한테 장례에 함께 가 달라고 부탁해야겠구나. 나 혼자서는 못 간다. 아주 무너져 버릴 게다.”\n\n“손턴 씨한테 부탁하지 마세요, 아버지. 제가 함께 갈게요.” 마거릿이 다급하게 말했다.\n\n“네가! 얘야, 여자들은 보통 가지 않는단다.”\n\n“가지 않는 건 스스로를 다스리지 못해서예요. 우리 같은 계층의 여자들이 가지 않는 건 감정을 억누를 힘이 없으면서도 그것을 드러내는 걸 부끄러워하기 때문이고요. 가난한 여자들은 가요. 슬픔에 무너진 모습을 남이 보아도 개의치 않지요. 하지만 약속드릴게요, 아버지. 저를 가게 해 주시면 조금도 폐가 되지 않겠어요. 남을 데려가고 저를 빼놓지 마세요. 아버지! 벨 씨가 오시지 못하면 제가 가겠어요. 오신다면 아버지 뜻을 거슬러 제 바람을 내세우지 않을게요.”\n\n벨 씨는 올 수 없었다. 통풍이 도졌던 것이다. 더없이 애정 어린 편지였고, 참석하지 못하는 데 대한 크고 진심 어린 아쉬움을 담고 있었다. 그는 받아 준다면 곧 찾아가 머물다 오고 싶다고 했다. 밀턴의 소유지를 좀 돌보아야 하는데 대리인이 편지를 보내 그가 반드시 와야 한다고 알려 왔다는 것이었다. 그렇지 않았다면 그는 될 수 있는 한 오래 밀턴 근처에 가지 않았을 것이고, 이제 이 피할 수 없는 걸음을 그나마 받아들일 만하게 해 주는 것은 오랜 벗을 만나 어쩌면 위로할 수 있을지 모른다는 생각뿐이라고 했다.\n\n마거릿은 손턴 씨를 부르지 말자고 아버지를 설득하느라 세상에 없을 애를 먹었다. 그녀는 그런 조치를 취한다는 데 말로 다 할 수 없는 거부감을 느꼈다. 장례 전날 밤, 손턴 부인에게서 헤일 양 앞으로 격식 차린 쪽지가 왔다. 아들의 뜻에 따라, 유족에게 불쾌하지 않다면 자기네 마차를 장례에 보내겠다는 내용이었다. 마거릿은 그 쪽지를 아버지에게 던지듯 건넸다.\n\n“아, 이런 형식은 그만두어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “우리끼리 가요, 아버지와 저 둘이서요. 저 사람들은 우리를 마음에 두지 않아요. 그랬다면 손턴 씨가 직접 가겠다고 했을 테고, 빈 마차를 보내겠다는 이런 말을 꺼내지도 않았을 거예요.”\n\n“나는 네가 그분이 오시는 걸 그토록 몹시 꺼리는 줄 알았는데, 마거릿.” 헤일 씨가 다소 놀라서 말했다.\n\n“꺼려요. 그분이 오시는 건 조금도 바라지 않고, 우리가 부탁한다는 생각은 특히나 싫어요. 하지만 이건 애도를 이렇게 우롱하는 것 같아서, 그분에게서 이런 걸 기대하지는 않았어요.” 그러고는 갑자기 울음을 터뜨려 아버지를 놀라게 했다. 그녀는 슬픔 속에서도 그토록 자신을 눌러 왔고, 남을 그토록 헤아렸으며, 모든 일에 그토록 온순하고 참을성이 있었기에, 아버지는 그날 밤 그녀의 조급한 태도를 이해할 수 없었다. 그녀는 동요하고 안절부절못하는 듯 보였다. 이번에는 아버지가 그녀에게 온갖 다정함을 쏟아부었지만, 그럴수록 그녀는 더 울기만 했다.\n\n밤을 어찌나 나쁘게 보냈던지, 그녀는 프레더릭에게서 온 편지가 안겨 준 새로운 근심을 감당할 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 레녹스 씨는 런던을 떠나 있었다. 사무원 말로는 늦어도 다음 화요일까지는 돌아올 것이고, 어쩌면 월요일에 올 수도 있다고 했다. 그래서 프레더릭은 얼마간 궁리한 끝에 하루 이틀 더 런던에 머물기로 정했다는 것이었다. 다시 밀턴으로 내려갈까 생각도 했고 그 유혹이 무척 강했지만, 벨 씨가 아버지 집에 묵고 있으리라는 생각과 마지막 순간 기차역에서 겪은 놀라움 때문에 런던에 남기로 마음먹었다고 했다. 레너즈에게 뒤를 밟히지 않도록 온갖 조심을 다할 테니 마거릿은 안심해도 좋다고 했다. 마거릿은 아버지가 어머니 방에 가 있는 동안 이 편지를 받은 것이 고마웠다. 아버지가 옆에 있었다면 소리 내어 읽어 주기를 바랐을 테고, 그러면 그녀로서는 도저히 달랠 수 없는 신경질적인 불안에 빠졌을 것이다. 그녀를 몹시 뒤흔든 프레더릭의 런던 체류라는 사실만이 아니었다. 밀턴에서 마지막 순간에 알아보인 일과 추적 가능성에 대한 언급들이 있었고, 그것은 그녀의 피를 얼어붙게 했다. 하물며 아버지에게는 어떤 영향을 미쳤겠는가? 마거릿은 레녹스 씨와 상의하자는 계획을 자기가 꺼내고 밀어붙인 것을 몇 번이고 뉘우쳤다. 그때는 지체될 일도 거의 없고, 얼마 되지 않아 보이던 발각의 위험을 더할 것도 거의 없어 보였다. 그런데 그 뒤로 벌어진 모든 일이 그 계획을 그토록 바람직하지 않은 것으로 만들어 놓았다. 마거릿은 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일에 대한 이 후회와, 그때는 지혜로워 보였으나 나중 일이 어리석었음을 증명해 가고 있는 그 말을 했다는 자책과 힘껏 싸웠다. 그러나 아버지는 정신도 몸도 너무 가라앉아 건강하게 싸울 수 없었다. 그는 돌이킬 수 없는 일에 대한 병적인 후회의 온갖 빌미에 굴복하고 말 터였다. 마거릿은 있는 힘을 모두 그러모아 스스로를 도왔다. 아버지는 그날 아침 프레더릭에게서 편지가 올 만한 이유가 있었다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다. 그는 한 가지 생각에 사로잡혀 있었다. 아내가 곁에 있었다는 마지막 눈에 보이는 표적이 자기에게서 실려 나가 눈앞에서 감추어지리라는 생각이었다. 장의사 일꾼이 그의 몸에 검은 상장(喪章) 천을 둘러 주는 동안 그는 가엾도록 떨었다. 그는 애타는 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았고, 몸이 풀려나자 비틀거리며 그녀에게 다가와 중얼거렸다. “나를 위해 기도해 다오, 마거릿. 내게는 힘이 남아 있지 않구나. 기도를 할 수가 없다. 어쩔 수 없으니 그이를 내어 놓는다. 견디려 애쓰고 있어. 정말 그러고 있단다. 하느님의 뜻인 줄도 안다. 그러나 왜 그이가 죽어야 했는지는 알 수가 없구나. 나를 위해 기도해 다오, 마거릿. 기도할 믿음을 갖게 되도록. 참으로 험한 고비로구나, 얘야.”\n\n마거릿은 마차 안에서 아버지 곁에 앉아 거의 두 팔로 그를 떠받치다시피 하며, 기억나는 대로 거룩한 위로가 담긴 고귀한 구절들과 믿음으로 순종함을 나타내는 성구들을 하나하나 읊었다. 그녀의 목소리는 한 번도 흔들리지 않았고, 그렇게 하는 동안 그녀 자신도 힘을 얻었다. 아버지의 입술은 그녀를 따라 움직이며 그녀의 말이 일깨워 주는 대로 낯익은 성구들을 되뇌었다. 스스로의 일부로 마음에 받아들일 힘이 없는 그 순종을 얻어 보려고 참을성 있게 애쓰는 그 몸부림을 지켜보는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다.\n\n딕슨이 손을 살짝 움직여 니컬러스 히긴스와 그의 딸을 눈짓해 보였을 때, 마거릿의 굳센 마음은 하마터면 무너질 뻔했다. 두 사람은 조금 떨어져 서 있었지만 예식을 깊이 눈여겨보고 있었다. 니컬러스는 늘 입는 퍼스티언 옷차림이었으나 모자 둘레에 검은 천 조각을 꿰매어 달고 있었다. 제 딸 베시를 기릴 때도 한 번 보인 적이 없는 애도의 표시였다. 그러나 헤일 씨는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그는 집전하는 목사가 읽어 나가는 장례 예식을 마치 기계처럼 혼자 따라 되뇌기만 했다. 모든 것이 끝나자 그는 두세 번 한숨을 쉬었고, 그러고는 마거릿의 팔에 손을 얹고 자기를 데려가 달라고 말없이 청했다. 마치 자기는 눈먼 사람이고 그녀는 그의 충실한 길잡이라는 듯이.\n\n딕슨은 소리 내어 흐느꼈다. 그녀는 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 자기 슬픔에 너무 빠져 있어서, 그런 자리면 으레 몰려드는 구경꾼들이 흩어지고 있는 것도 알아차리지 못했다. 바로 곁에서 누군가 말을 걸고 나서야 알았다. 손턴 씨였다. 그는 내내 그 자리에 있었다. 사람들 무리 뒤에 고개를 숙이고 서 있었던 터라 사실 아무도 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“실례합니다만, 헤일 씨는 어떠신지 말씀해 주시겠습니까? 헤일 양도요. 두 분 다 어떠신지 알고 싶습니다.”\n\n“그럼요, 나리. 짐작하시는 그대로지요. 주인어른은 아주 무너지셨습니다요. 헤일 아가씨는 뜻밖에도 잘 견디시고요.”\n\n손턴 씨는 그녀가 마땅한 슬픔에 젖어 있다는 말을 듣는 편이 나았을 것이다. 우선 그의 안에는 자기의 크나큰 사랑이 그녀를 달래고 위로해 줄 수 있으리라는 생각에서 기쁨을 얻을 만큼의 이기심이 있었다. 시들시들한 젖먹이가 제 어미에게 바싹 파고들어 모든 것을 어미에게 기댈 때, 어미의 가슴을 찌르며 밀려드는 그 격렬한 기쁨과 거의 같은 종류의 것이었다. 그러나 있었을 수도 있었을 일에 대한 이 달콤한 환상은 — 마거릿에게 그토록 거절당하고서도 며칠 전만 해도 그가 기꺼이 빠져들었을 환상은 — 아웃우드 역 근처에서 본 것에 대한 기억으로 비참하게 흐트러졌다. “비참하게 흐트러졌다”는 말로는 부족하다. 그토록 스스럼없는 신뢰의 몸짓으로 그녀와 나란히 서 있던 그 잘생긴 젊은이의 기억이 그를 따라다녔고, 그 기억은 고통처럼 그를 꿰뚫어, 그는 아픔을 억누르려고 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐어야 했다. 그 늦은 시각에, 집에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서! 마거릿의 순결하고 빼어난 처녀다움에 대해 얼마 전까지 그토록 완전했던 자신의 믿음을 되살려 내려면 엄청난 도덕적 노력이 필요했다. 그 노력을 멈추는 순간 그의 믿음은 죽은 듯 무력하게 주저앉았다. 그리고 온갖 걷잡을 수 없는 상상이 꿈처럼 그의 머릿속을 앞서거니 뒤서거니 스쳐 갔다. 여기 비참하게 속을 갉아먹는 자그마한 확증이 있었다. 그녀가 이 슬픔 속에서 “뜻밖에도 잘 견디고” 있다는 것. 그렇다면 그녀에게는 바라볼 무슨 희망이 있는 것이었다. 어찌나 밝은 희망이었으면, 정 많은 그녀의 천성에서조차 갓 어머니를 잃은 딸의 어두운 시간을 밝혀 줄 만하겠는가. 그렇다! 그는 그녀가 어떻게 사랑할지 알고 있었다. 그녀 안에 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있는지 본능적으로 알아채지 못한 채 그녀를 사랑했던 것이 아니었다. 사랑하는 힘으로 그녀의 사랑을 얻어 낼 만한 자격을 갖춘 사내가 있다면, 그녀의 영혼은 눈부신 햇빛 속을 거닐 것이었다. 애도 중에도 그녀는 그의 공감에 평온한 믿음을 얹고 쉴 것이었다. 그의 공감이라니! 누구의? 그 다른 사내의 것이지. 그리고 그것이 다른 사내의 것이라는 사실만으로도, 딕슨의 대답을 들은 손턴 씨의 창백하고 굳은 얼굴은 갑절로 핏기를 잃고 험악해지기에 충분했다.\n\n“찾아뵈어도 되겠지요.” 그가 차갑게 말했다. “헤일 씨를 말입니다. 내일쯤 지나면 아마 저를 들여 주시겠지요.”\n\n그는 마치 그 대답이 자기에게는 아무래도 상관없다는 투로 말했다. 그러나 그렇지 않았다. 그 모든 고통에도 불구하고 그는 그 고통을 지어낸 사람을 보고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 그 다정하고 스스럼없던 몸짓과 거기에 딸린 온갖 정황을 떠올릴 때면 이따금 마거릿을 미워하기도 했지만, 그는 그녀의 모습을 마음속에 새로 그려 보고 싶은 안절부절못하는 갈망을, 그녀가 숨 쉬는 바로 그 공기에 대한 그리움을 품고 있었다. 그는 정념의 카리브디스에 빠져 있었고, 어쩔 수 없이 그 치명적인 중심 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 자꾸만 가까이 다가갈 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“아무렴요, 나리. 주인어른께서 만나 주실 겁니다요. 지난번에 나리를 물리치실 수밖에 없었던 걸 아주 미안해하셨어요. 다만 그때는 사정이 좋질 못했지요.”\n\n무슨 까닭에서인지 딕슨은 손턴 씨와 나눈 이 대화를 마거릿에게 한 번도 이야기하지 않았다. 그저 우연이었을 수도 있으나, 어쨌든 마거릿은 그가 가엾은 어머니의 장례에 참석했다는 것을 끝내 듣지 못했다.\n\n# 제34장\n\n“진리는 결코, 결코 그대를 저버리지 않으리! 그대의 배가 폭풍에 떠밀려 가도, 널빤지마다 찢기고 갈라져도, 진리는 그대를 영원히 실어 가리라!” — 작자 미상.\n\n“생각보다 잘 견디고 있다”는 그 말은 마거릿에게 끔찍한 부담이었다. 때때로 그녀는 그만 무너져 고통에 소리치고 말 것 같았다. 아버지와 겉으로는 명랑하게 이야기를 나누는 중에도, 이제 자기에게 어머니가 없다는 생각이 문득 날카롭게 스치고 지나갔던 것이다. 프레더릭에 대해서도 큰 불안이 있었다. 일요일 우편이 끼어드는 바람에 런던에서 오는 편지가 늦어졌고, 화요일이 되어서도 여전히 편지가 없다는 것을 알고 마거릿은 놀랍고도 낙담했다. 그녀는 오빠의 계획에 대해 아무것도 알지 못했고, 아버지는 이 모든 불확실함에 비참해했다. 그것은 한나절 내내 안락의자 하나에 가만히 앉아 있는, 근래에 몸에 밴 그의 습관마저 깨뜨려 놓았다. 그는 방 안을 이리저리 서성이다가 밖으로 나갔고, 계단참에서 아무 뚜렷한 목적도 없이 침실 문들을 여닫는 소리가 그녀의 귀에 들려왔다. 그녀는 소리 내어 책을 읽어 아버지를 가라앉히려 했지만, 그가 오래 귀를 기울이지 못한다는 것이 분명했다. 그러니 걱정거리 하나를 더 자기 혼자만 간직해 둔 것을 그때 그녀가 얼마나 다행으로 여겼던가.\n\n그때 손턴 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들려왔다. 그의 방문은 아버지의 생각을 다른 물길로 돌려놓을 터였다.\n\n그는 곧장 그녀의 아버지에게 다가가 말없이 두 손을 잡고 꼭 쥐었다. 한두 분 동안 그 손을 자기 손 안에 쥐고 있는 사이, 그의 얼굴과 눈과 그 눈길이 말로는 담을 수 없는 깊은 위로를 전했다. 그러고서 그는 마거릿에게로 돌아섰다. 그녀는 “생각보다 잘” 견디고 있는 얼굴이 아니었다. 그 당당한 아름다움은 오랜 간병과 수많은 눈물로 빛이 바래 있었다. 얼굴에 어린 표정은 온순하게 견디는 슬픔이었다. 아니, 지금 이 순간에도 또렷한 괴로움이었다. 그는 요즈음 애써 지켜 온 차가운 태도 말고 달리 그녀를 맞을 생각이 없었다. 그러나 근래 그의 태도를 종잡을 수 없어 주눅이 든 채 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서 있는 그녀에게 다가가지 않을 수 없었고, 꼭 필요한 몇 마디 상투적인 인사말을 어찌나 부드러운 목소리로 건넸던지 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 차올랐고 그녀는 감정을 감추려 몸을 돌렸다. 그녀는 바느질감을 집어 들고 아주 조용히, 말없이 앉았다. 손턴 씨의 심장은 빠르고 세차게 뛰었고, 그 순간만큼은 그는 아웃우드 오솔길을 까맣게 잊었다. 그는 헤일 씨와 이야기를 나누려 애썼다. 그의 존재는 늘 헤일 씨에게 어떤 즐거움이었는데, 그의 힘과 결단력이 그와 그의 견해를 안전하고 확실한 항구로 만들어 주었기 때문이다. 마거릿이 보기에 그날 그는 여느 때보다도 아버지의 마음에 드는 듯했다.\n\n이윽고 딕슨이 문간으로 와서 말했다. “헤일 아가씨, 좀 나와 보셔야겠습니다.”\n\n딕슨의 태도가 어찌나 허둥대던지 마거릿은 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 프레드에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것이다. 그 점에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 아버지와 손턴 씨가 대화에 그토록 몰두해 있는 것이 다행이었다.\n\n“무슨 일이에요, 딕슨?” 응접실 문을 닫자마자 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“이쪽으로 오세요, 아가씨.” 딕슨은 헤일 부인의 침실이었다가 이제는 마거릿의 방이 된 곳의 문을 열며 말했다. 헤일 씨가 아내를 잃은 뒤로 다시는 그 방에서 자기를 마다했기 때문이었다. “아무 일도 아닙니다요, 아가씨.” 딕슨이 살짝 목이 메어 말했다. “그저 경찰 조사관이 하나 왔을 뿐이에요. 아가씨를 뵙자고 합니다. 하지만 아마 아무것도 아닐 거예요.”\n\n“그 사람이 이름을 대던가요……” 마거릿이 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 소리로 물었다.\n\n“아니요, 아가씨. 아무 이름도 대지 않았어요. 그저 아가씨가 여기 사시는지, 말씀을 나눌 수 있는지만 묻더군요. 마사가 문으로 나가서 들여보냈고, 주인어른 서재로 안내했습니다. 제가 직접 가서 저로 되겠느냐고 해 보았지만 안 된다더군요. 아가씨라야 한답니다.”\n\n마거릿은 서재 문의 손잡이에 손을 얹을 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 여기서 그녀는 돌아서서 말했다. “아버지가 내려오시지 않게 잘 봐요. 지금 손턴 씨가 함께 계시니까.”\n\n그녀가 들어설 때의 그 오만한 태도에 조사관은 거의 기가 죽었다. 얼굴에는 분노 같은 것이 드러나 있었으나 어찌나 눌리고 다스려져 있던지, 그것이 그녀에게 도도한 경멸의 기품을 주었다. 놀라움도 호기심도 없었다. 그녀는 그가 용건을 꺼내기를 기다리며 서 있었다. 한마디도 묻지 않았다.\n\n“실례합니다, 부인. 하오나 직무상 몇 가지 간단한 질문을 드려야겠습니다. 이달 26일 목요일 저녁 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이에 아웃우드 역에서 넘어진 사람이 그 일로 병원에서 죽었습니다. 당시에는 그 낙상이 그리 대수롭지 않아 보였습니다만, 의사들 말로는 어떤 내장의 병과 그자 자신의 술버릇 탓에 목숨을 잃게 되었다고 합니다.”\n\n조사관의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하던 크고 검은 눈이 조금 커졌다. 그 밖에는 노련한 그의 관찰로도 알아챌 만한 움직임이 없었다. 근육이 억지로 팽팽해진 탓에 그녀의 입술은 여느 때보다 도톰한 곡선으로 부풀었으나, 그는 그 입술의 평소 모습을 알지 못했으므로 그 굳고 시원스러운 선에 어린 뜻밖의 뿌루퉁한 반항을 알아보지 못했다. 그녀는 움츠리지도 떨지도 않았다. 그녀는 눈으로 그를 붙들었다. 이제 그가 말을 잇기 전에 뜸을 들이자, 그녀는 마치 그가 이야기를 계속하도록 부추기기라도 하듯 말했다. “그래서요, 계속하세요!”\n\n“검시가 열려야 하리라 봅니다. 그 낙상을 부른 타격이나 떠밀기, 혹은 몸싸움이 이 가엾은 자가 반쯤 취해 어떤 젊은 아가씨에게 무례를 범한 데서 비롯되었다는 정황 증거가 얼마간 있습니다. 그 아가씨는 죽은 자를 승강장 가장자리 너머로 떠민 남자와 함께 걷고 있었지요. 승강장에 있던 누군가가 이만큼을 보았습니다만, 그 타격이 대수롭지 않아 보였으므로 그는 그 일에 더는 마음을 두지 않았습니다. 또한 그 아가씨를 당신으로 볼 만한 근거도 얼마간 있습니다. 그렇다면……”\n\n“저는 그곳에 없었습니다.” 마거릿이 말했다. 몽유병자처럼 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 눈빛으로, 여전히 아무 표정 없는 눈을 그의 얼굴에 붙박은 채였다.\n\n조사관은 고개를 숙였을 뿐 말이 없었다. 앞에 선 아가씨는 아무런 감정도, 떨리는 두려움도, 불안도, 이 면담을 끝내려는 기색도 보이지 않았다. 그가 얻은 정보는 매우 모호했다. 짐꾼 하나가 기차를 맞을 채비를 하려고 뛰어나갔다가 승강장 저쪽 끝에서 레너즈와 어떤 아가씨를 동반한 신사 사이에 몸싸움이 벌어지는 것을 보았으나 소리는 듣지 못했다. 그리고 기차가 출발해 속도를 다 내기 전에, 성이 나 반쯤 취한 레너즈가 무섭게 욕설과 저주를 퍼부으며 곤두박질치듯 달려오는 바람에 그는 하마터면 떠밀려 넘어질 뻔했다. 그는 조사관이 그 증언을 캐낼 때까지 그 일에 더는 마음을 두지 않았다. 조사관이 철도역에서 더 캐물었더니, 역장에게서 그 시각쯤 젊은 아가씨와 신사가 거기 있었다는 말을, 그 아가씨가 유난히 아름다웠다는 말을 들었다. 그리고 그때 그 자리에 있던 어느 식료품점 점원이 그 아가씨는 크램프턴에 사는 헤일 양이며 그 집안이 자기 가게에서 물건을 산다고 말했다는 것이었다. 그 아가씨와 신사가 앞서의 두 사람과 같은 이들이라는 확증은 없었으나, 그럴 개연성은 컸다. 레너즈 자신은 분노와 통증으로 반쯤 미쳐서 위안을 얻으려 가장 가까운 술집으로 갔다. 그가 취해서 지껄인 말에 그곳의 바쁜 종업원들은 귀를 기울이지 않았다. 다만 그가 벌떡 일어나 전기 전신을 진작에 떠올리지 못했다며 스스로를 저주하던 것은 기억했는데, 무슨 속셈이었는지는 알 수 없었다. 그리고 그가 거기로 갈 작정으로 나갔으리라고들 여겼다. 가는 길에 통증인지 술인지에 못 이겨 그는 길바닥에 드러누웠고, 경찰이 그를 발견해 병원으로 데려갔다. 그곳에서 그는 자신이 어떻게 넘어졌는지 또렷이 이야기할 만큼 의식을 되찾지 못했다. 다만 한두 번 정신이 어렴풋이 돌아온 적이 있어, 당국은 죽어 가는 자에게서 사인에 관한 진술을 받아 둘 수 있을까 하여 가장 가까운 치안판사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그러나 치안판사가 왔을 때 그는 바다에 있다며 횡설수설했고, 함장과 부관들의 이름을 철도의 동료 짐꾼들의 이름과 알아들을 수 없게 뒤섞고 있었다. 그리고 그의 마지막 말은 “콘월식 속임수”에 대한 저주였는데, 그 때문에 자기가 마땅히 가졌어야 할 것보다 100파운드나 가난해졌다는 것이었다. 조사관은 이 모든 것을 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았다. 마거릿이 그 역에 있었음을 증명하기에는 증거가 모호하다는 것, 그리고 그런 추정에 대해 그녀가 내놓은 흔들림 없이 차분한 부인. 그녀는 더할 나위 없어 보이는 침착함으로 그의 다음 말을 기다리며 서 있었다.\n\n“그러면 부인, 이 가엾은 사람을 죽음에 이르게 한 타격을 가했거나 떠민 그 신사와 함께 있던 아가씨가 자신이 아니라고 부인하시는 것으로 알겠습니다?”\n\n빠르고 날카로운 통증이 마거릿의 머릿속을 관통했다. “아, 하느님, 프레더릭 오빠가 무사하다는 것만 알 수 있다면!” 사람의 얼굴을 깊이 읽을 줄 아는 이라면 그 크고 어두운 눈에서 한순간 솟구친 고뇌를, 궁지에 몰린 짐승의 고통 같은 그것을 보았을지도 모른다. 그러나 조사관은 매우 예리하기는 해도 그리 깊이 보는 사람은 아니었다. 그래도 그는 그 대답의 형태에 조금 마음이 걸렸다. 그것은 첫 대답을 기계적으로 되풀이한 것처럼 들렸고, 그의 마지막 질문에 맞추어 모양을 바꾸거나 다듬은 데가 없었다.\n\n“저는 그곳에 없었습니다.” 그녀가 느리고 무겁게 말했다. 그러는 내내 그녀는 눈을 감지도, 그 유리알 같은 꿈결의 응시를 거두지도 않았다. 앞선 부인의 이 둔탁한 메아리에 그의 재빠른 의심이 일었다. 마치 그녀가 스스로에게 거짓 하나를 억지로 밀어붙여 놓고는, 그것을 조금이라도 바꿀 힘마저 잃도록 얼이 빠져 버린 것 같았다.\n\n그는 아주 신중한 태도로 수첩을 집어넣었다. 그러고서 고개를 들었다. 그녀는 무슨 거대한 이집트 조각상이기라도 한 듯 조금도 움직이지 않고 있었다.\n\n“무례하다고 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다만, 제가 다시 찾아뵈어야 할지도 모르겠습니다. 제 증인들이”(그녀를 알아본 사람은 하나뿐이었다) “그 불행한 사건 현장에 당신이 있었다고 계속 진술한다면, 검시에 출두하셔서 그곳에 없었음을 증명해 주십사 소환해야 할지도 모릅니다.” 그는 그녀를 날카롭게 바라보았다. 그녀는 여전히 완벽하게 고요했다. 그 도도한 얼굴에는 낯빛의 변화도, 죄를 짐작케 하는 어두운 그늘도 없었다. 그는 그녀가 움찔하는 것을 보게 되리라 여겼으나, 마거릿 헤일을 몰랐던 것이다. 그는 그녀의 진짜 침착함에 조금 무안해졌다. 사람을 잘못 본 것이 틀림없었다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n“부인, 제가 그런 일을 해야 할 가능성은 아주 낮습니다. 무례해 보일지 모르나 그저 제 직무를 다하는 것이니 너그러이 보아 주시기 바랍니다.”\n\n그가 문 쪽으로 가는 동안 마거릿은 고개를 숙였다. 그녀의 입술은 뻣뻣하게 말라 있었다. 그녀는 흔한 작별 인사조차 입에 올릴 수 없었다. 그러나 문득 그녀는 앞으로 걸어 나가 서재 문을 열었고, 그를 앞질러 현관문까지 가서 그가 나가도록 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 그녀는 그가 완전히 집 밖으로 나갈 때까지 여전히 그 둔하고 붙박인 눈길로 그를 지켜보았다. 그녀는 문을 닫고 서재로 반쯤 들어갔다. 그러다 무슨 격정에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 되돌아가 안에서 문을 잠갔다.\n\n그러고서 그녀는 서재로 들어가 멈춰 섰다. 비틀거리며 앞으로 나아가다 다시 멈춰 섰고, 선 자리에서 한순간 휘청이더니 바닥에 엎어져 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다.\n\n# 제35장\n\n「아무리 곱게 자아낸 실이라 해도 끝내는 햇빛 아래로 드러나 오기 마련이라네.」\n\n손턴 씨는 자리를 뜨지 못하고 하염없이 앉아 있었다. 자기가 곁에 있는 것이 헤일 씨에게 위안이 된다는 것을 느꼈고, 조금만 더 있어 달라는 반쯤 삼킨 간절한 청에 마음이 움직였다. 가엾은 벗이 이따금 내뱉는 “아직 가지 마시게” 하는 그 애처로운 말에. 그는 마거릿이 돌아오지 않는 것을 이상히 여겼지만, 그녀를 보려고 머무는 것은 아니었다. 그 시간만큼은—그리고 이 세상의 덧없음을 뼛속까지 느끼고 있는 사람 앞에서는—그도 이성적이고 절제되어 있었다. 그는 그녀의 아버지가 하는 모든 말에 깊이 마음을 쏟고 있었다.\n\n「죽음에 대하여, 그 무거운 정적에 대하여, 그리고 무디어져 버린 머릿골에 대하여.」\n\n손턴 씨가 곁에 있으면 헤일 씨가 마거릿에게조차 닫아 두었던 은밀한 생각들을 풀어놓게 되는 것은 이상한 일이었다. 딸의 공감이 너무나 예민하고 또 너무나 생생하게 드러나는 터라 그 반동이 자기에게 되돌아올까 두려웠기 때문인지, 아니면 사변적인 그의 머릿속에는 이런 때면 온갖 의심이 떠올라 확신으로 풀어 달라고 아우성치며 매달리는데 딸이라면 그런 의심을 입에 담는 것 자체를—아니, 그런 의심을 품을 수 있는 아버지라는 사람 자체를—움츠러들며 피하리라는 것을 알았기 때문인지, 이유가 무엇이었든 그는 지금껏 머릿속에 얼어붙어 있던 온갖 생각과 공상과 두려움을 딸보다 손턴 씨에게 훨씬 잘 털어놓을 수 있었다. 손턴 씨는 말을 거의 하지 않았다. 그러나 그가 내뱉는 한마디 한마디가 헤일 씨의 신뢰와 존경을 더해 갔다. 기억 속의 어떤 고통을 이야기하다 그가 말을 멈추면, 손턴 씨가 던지는 두어 마디가 그 문장을 마저 완성해 주었고, 그 뜻을 얼마나 깊이 헤아리고 있는지를 보여 주었다. 의심이든 두려움이든, 안식을 찾아 헤매지만 눈이 눈물에 가려 끝내 찾지 못하는 갈피 없는 불안이든, 손턴 씨는 충격을 받기는커녕 스스로도 바로 그 단계를 지나온 사람처럼, 어두운 곳을 환히 밝혀 줄 빛줄기가 정확히 어디에 있는지 일러 줄 수 있었다. 세상의 큰 싸움터에서 분주한 행동가였으되, 그의 마음속에는 그 억센 고집에도 불구하고, 그가 저지른 온갖 잘못을 지나서도, 그를 하느님께 붙들어 매는 더 깊은 신앙이 헤일 씨가 꿈에도 짐작하지 못한 만큼 자리 잡고 있었다. 공교롭게도 두 사람은 다시는 그런 이야기를 나누지 않았다. 그러나 이 한 번의 대화가 서로를 각별한 사람으로 만들었고, 거룩한 것에 대해 아무렇게나 늘어놓는 헐거운 말로는 결코 이루지 못할 방식으로 두 사람을 하나로 묶었다. 누구나 다 들어올 수 있다면 어찌 지성소가 있을 수 있겠는가?\n\n그러는 내내 마거릿은 서재 바닥에 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게, 미동도 없이 누워 있었다! 그녀는 짐에 짓눌려 무너진 것이었다. 그 짐은 무거웠고 오래 지고 온 것이었다. 그녀는 참으로 온순하고 참을성 있게 견뎌 왔으나, 마침내 한순간에 믿음이 무너졌고, 도움을 찾아 더듬었으나 헛일이었다! 의식이 남아 있다는 다른 기색은 없었건만, 아름다운 이마에는 고통스러운 주름이 애처롭게 잡혀 있었다. 조금 전까지 반항하듯 뾰로통하게 내밀고 있던 입은 힘없이 풀린 채 파리했다.\n\n「그리고 그 얼굴에서 부드럽고 사랑으로 가득한 정령이 하나 움직여 나와, 영혼을 향해 이렇게 말하는 듯하구나. 한숨지어라!」\n\n되살아나는 생명의 첫 징후는 입술의 떨림이었다. 소리 없는 작은 말의 시늉이었다. 그러나 눈은 여전히 감긴 채였고, 떨림도 이내 잦아들어 고요해졌다. 이윽고 마거릿은 잠시 두 팔로 몸을 짚어 가누고는 몸을 추슬러 일어났다. 머리에서 빗이 빠져 떨어져 있었다. 나약함의 흔적을 지우고 다시 몸가짐을 가다듬으려는 본능적인 마음에 그녀는 빗을 찾았다. 다만 찾는 동안에도 이따금 주저앉아 기운을 되찾아야 했다. 고개를 앞으로 떨구고 두 손을 얌전히 포갠 채, 그녀는 자기를 그토록 무서운 공포로 몰아넣었던 세세한 일들을 떠올려 봄으로써 자기가 받은 유혹의 힘이 무엇이었는지 되새겨 보려 했다. 그러나 되지 않았다. 그녀가 알아들은 것은 두 가지 사실뿐이었다. 프레더릭이 런던에서 쫓기고 발각될 위험에 처했었다는 것—사람을 죽인 죄만이 아니라 더 용서받지 못할 반란의 주모자로서—그리고 자기가 오빠를 구하려고 거짓말을 했다는 것. 위안이 하나 있었다. 비록 시간을 조금 더 벌어 준 것뿐일지라도 그 거짓말이 오빠를 구했다는 것. 오빠가 무사하다는 것을 알려 줄, 그토록 애타게 기다리는 편지를 받은 뒤에 경위가 내일 다시 온다면, 그때는 수치를 무릅쓰고 쓰디쓴 참회의 자리에 서리라. 그 도도한 마거릿이, 필요하다면 사람들로 가득 찬 재판정 앞에서 자기가 “개처럼 이런 짓을 저질렀노라”고 시인하리라. 그러나 프레더릭에게서 소식을 듣기 전에 그가 온다면, 반쯤 으름장을 놓았듯 몇 시간 안에 그가 돌아온다면, 그때는—그때는 그 거짓말을 또 하리라. 다만 이 무서운 반성과 자책의 시간을 다 겪고 난 뒤에 어떻게 그 말이 거짓임을 드러내지 않고 입 밖으로 나올 수 있을지, 그녀는 알지 못했고 짐작할 수도 없었다. 그러나 거듭 거짓말을 하면 시간을 벌 수 있을 터였다. 프레더릭을 위한 시간을.\n\n딕슨이 방에 들어오는 바람에 그녀는 정신이 들었다. 딕슨은 막 손턴 씨를 배웅하고 온 참이었다.\n\n그가 거리로 나서 열 걸음도 채 못 갔을 때, 지나가던 승합마차가 그의 곁에 멎더니 한 사내가 내려 다가와 모자에 손을 얹어 인사했다. 경찰 경위였다.\n\n손턴 씨는 그에게 경찰의 첫 자리를 얻어 주었고, 이따금 그 피후견인이 어떻게 되어 가는지 소식을 들어 왔다. 그러나 자주 만나지는 못한 터라 처음에는 그를 알아보지 못했다.\n\n“저는 왓슨입니다. 조지 왓슨이라고, 나리께서 자리를 마련해 주신——”\n\n“아, 그렇지! 아주 잘해 나가고 있다고 들었네.”\n\n“예, 나리. 감사드려야 마땅합니다. 하오나 지금 이렇게 감히 말씀을 올리는 것은 조그마한 일 때문입니다. 어젯밤 시립병원에서 죽은 가엾은 사내의 진술을 받으러 나오신 치안판사가 나리시라고 알고 있습니다만.”\n\n“그렇네.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “가서 두서없는 진술 비슷한 것을 듣기는 했네. 서기는 별 쓸모가 없다고 하더군. 아무래도 술에 절어 있던 자 같았네. 하기야 끝내 폭력으로 죽음에 이른 것만은 틀림없지만. 우리 어머니 댁 하녀 하나가 그자와 약혼한 사이였다는데, 오늘 몹시 상심해 있네. 그자가 어쨌다는 건가?”\n\n“실은 나리, 그자의 죽음이 방금 나리께서 나오시는 것을 제가 뵌 그 댁 사람과 묘하게 얽혀 있습니다. 헤일 씨 댁이지요, 아마.”\n\n“그렇네!” 손턴 씨가 홱 돌아서서 갑작스러운 관심으로 경위의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. “그게 어쨌다는 건가?”\n\n“실은 나리, 제가 보기에는 증거의 사슬이 꽤 또렷하게 잡힙니다. 그날 밤 아웃우드 역에서 헤일 양과 함께 걷고 있던 신사가 레너즈를 승강장에서 치거나 밀어 떨어뜨려 죽음에 이르게 한 자라는 것이지요. 그런데 그 아가씨는 그 시각에 자기가 거기 없었다고 부인합니다.”\n\n“헤일 양이 거기 없었다고 부인한다고!” 손턴 씨가 달라진 목소리로 되뇌었다. “말해 보게, 그게 어느 날 저녁이었나? 몇 시였고?”\n\n“26일 목요일 저녁, 여섯 시쯤입니다.”\n\n두 사람은 나란히 걸으며 일이 분 동안 말이 없었다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 경위였다.\n\n“보십시오, 나리, 검시가 열릴 듯싶습니다. 그런데 꽤 확신하는 젊은이가 하나 있습니다. 적어도 처음에는 그랬지요. 그 아가씨가 부인했다는 말을 듣고는 선서까지는 하고 싶지 않다고 합니다만, 그래도 헤일 양이 역에서 어떤 신사와 함께 거니는 것을 보았다고 꽤 확신합니다. 짐꾼 하나가 몸싸움을 본 시각으로부터 채 오 분도 안 되기 전에 말입니다. 짐꾼은 그 몸싸움을 레너즈가 늘 부리던 건방진 수작쯤으로 여겼습니다만, 그 일로 그자가 떨어져 죽게 된 것이지요. 그런데 나리께서 바로 그 댁에서 나오시는 것을 뵈니 감히 여쭈어도 될까 싶었습니다. 아시다시피 사람이 누구인지를 두고 다투는 사건은 늘 거북하고, 어지간한 반증이 없이는 점잖은 젊은 아가씨의 말을 의심하고 싶지도 않으니까요.”\n\n“그런데 그 저녁에 역에 있지 않았다고 부인했다고!” 손턴 씨가 낮게 잠긴 목소리로 되뇌었다.\n\n“예, 나리. 두 번이나, 더없이 또렷하게요. 제가 다시 찾아뵙겠다고 말해 두었습니다만, 그 아가씨가 맞다고 한 젊은이를 캐묻고 돌아오는 길에 마침 나리를 뵙게 되니, 레너즈의 임종 자리를 지켜보신 치안판사로서도 그렇고 제게 경찰 자리를 마련해 주신 어른으로서도 그렇고, 조언을 여쭙고 싶었습니다.”\n\n“잘했네.” 손턴 씨가 말했다. “나를 다시 만나기 전에는 아무 조치도 취하지 말게.”\n\n“제가 그리 말씀드렸으니 그 아가씨는 제가 찾아올 줄 알고 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“한 시간만 늦추라는 것일세. 지금이 세 시로군. 네 시에 내 창고로 오게.”\n\n“알겠습니다, 나리!”\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 손턴 씨는 서둘러 창고로 가서 서기들에게 누구도 자기를 방해하지 못하게 하라고 엄히 이르고는, 자기 집무실로 들어가 문을 잠갔다. 그러고는 모든 일을 하나하나 되짚으며 세세한 대목까지 실감하는 고문을 스스로에게 실컷 안겼다. 어떻게 자기가 두 시간도 채 되기 전에 눈물 젖은 그녀의 모습이 마음에 비쳐 들던 그 아무 의심 없는 평온에 젖어 들었단 말인가. 그리하여 나약하게도 그녀를 가엾이 여기고 그녀에게 마음이 끌렸으며, 그런 시각에, 그런 곳에서, 그것도 자기가 모르는 사이에 그녀를 본 것이 불러일으켰던 그 사납고 불신에 찬 질투를 잊고 있었다니! 그토록 순결한 사람이 어떻게 그 단정하고 고결한 몸가짐에서 그렇게 내려앉을 수 있단 말인가! 그런데 그것은 단정한 것이었던가—과연 그러했던가? 한순간, 그 이상은 아니었으나, 억지로 밀고 들어오는 그 생각 때문에 그는 자기 자신이 미웠다. 그러면서도 그 생각이 머무는 동안 그녀의 모습을 향한 옛 이끌림이 그 힘을 되찾아 그를 떨리게 했다. 게다가 이 거짓말—드러나는 것이 그토록 두려운 수치라면 대체 얼마나 무서운 것이란 말인가. 따지고 보면 레너즈 같은 자가, 그것도 술에 취해 달아올랐을 때 걸어온 시비라면, 앞에 나서서 사정을 숨김없이 밝히는 사람 누구에게든 넉넉히 명분이 될 법도 하지 않은가! 정직한 마거릿을 거짓말 앞에 무릎 꿇게 만든 그 두려움은 얼마나 은밀하고 치명적인가! 그는 그녀가 가엾다는 마음마저 들었다. 이 일의 끝은 어찌 될 것인가? 검시가 열리고 그 젊은이가 나선다면, 자기가 무슨 일에 발을 들이는지 그녀는 미처 헤아리지 못했을 터였다. 문득 그가 벌떡 일어섰다. 검시는 열려서는 안 된다. 자기가 마거릿을 구하리라. 검시를 막는 책임을 자기가 지리라. 어차피 그 결말도 의학적 소견이 불확실하니 미덥지 못할 것이었다. 전날 밤 진료를 맡은 외과의에게서 어렴풋이 들은 바로는, 의사들은 이미 깊이 진행되어 틀림없이 목숨을 앗아 갈 내부 질환을 발견했고, 추락 때문에, 혹은 그 뒤의 음주와 추위에 노출된 탓으로 죽음이 앞당겨졌을 수도 있다고 밝혔다는 것이었다. 마거릿이 이 일에 어떻게 얽혀 들지 알기만 했더라면, 그녀가 거짓말로 자기 결백을 더럽히게 되리라 내다보기만 했더라면, 그는 한마디로 그녀를 구할 수 있었을 것이다. 검시를 할 것인가 말 것인가는 바로 전날 밤만 해도 저울 위에서 떨고 있었으니 말이다. 헤일 양이 다른 사람을 사랑할지도 모른다. 자기에게는 무심하고 경멸하는지도 모른다. 그래도 그는 그녀가 결코 알지 못할 충직한 도움을 베풀 것이었다. 그녀를 경멸할지언정, 한때 사랑했던 여인이 수치를 당하게 두지는 않으리라. 공개된 법정에서 거짓말에 자기를 걸어야 하는 것도, 그러지 않으려면 어찌하여 빛보다 어둠을 바라는지 그 까닭을 서서 시인해야 하는 것도, 수치임에 틀림없었다.\n\n어리둥절해하는 서기들 사이를 지나 나가는 손턴 씨의 얼굴은 몹시 잿빛이고 굳어 있었다. 그는 반 시간쯤 자리를 비웠다. 돌아왔을 때도, 볼일은 뜻대로 되었건만, 얼굴은 조금도 덜 굳어 보이지 않았다.\n\n그는 종잇조각에 두 줄을 적어 봉투에 넣고 봉했다. 그리고 그것을 서기 하나에게 건네며 말했다.\n\n“창고에서 짐 싸는 일을 하다가 경찰로 간 왓슨이라는 사람에게 네 시에 나를 찾아오라고 일러 두었네. 그런데 방금 리버풀에서 온 신사를 만났는데, 이 도시를 떠나기 전에 나를 만나고 싶어 하는군. 왓슨이 오거든 이 쪽지를 꼭 전해 주게.”\n\n쪽지에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다.\n\n“검시는 없을 것이오. 의학적 증거가 검시를 할 만큼 충분치 않소. 더는 아무 조치도 취하지 마시오. 검시관을 만나지는 않았으나 책임은 내가 지겠소.”\n\n“거참.” 왓슨은 생각했다. “거북한 일을 덜었군. 그 젊은 아가씨 말고는 내 증인 가운데 무엇 하나 확실한 자가 없었지. 아가씨는 더없이 분명하고 또렷했고. 철도 짐꾼은 몸싸움을 보았다고 했다가, 그 일로 자기가 증인으로 불려 나가게 생겼다 싶으니 몸싸움이 아니라 그저 장난질이었을 수도 있다, 레너즈가 제 발로 승강장에서 뛰어내렸을 수도 있다 하더군. 무엇 하나 딱 붙들고 말하는 게 없었어. 식료품 가게 점원 제닝스는, 글쎄, 그만큼 나쁘지는 않았지만, 헤일 양이 딱 잘라 부인했다는 말을 들은 뒤로 선서까지 시킬 수 있었을지는 모르겠군. 골치만 아프고 소득은 없는 일이었을 거야. 이제 가서 이 사람들한테 부를 일 없게 됐다고 알려야겠군.”\n\n그리하여 그는 그날 저녁 다시 헤일 씨 댁을 찾았다. 마거릿의 아버지와 딕슨은 어떻게든 그녀를 잠자리에 들게 하려 했으나, 두 사람 다 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 한사코 마다하는 까닭을 알지 못했다. 딕슨은 진실의 일부를 알고 있었다. 다만 일부뿐이었다. 마거릿은 자기가 무슨 말을 했는지 어떤 사람에게도 말하지 않으려 했고, 레너즈가 승강장에서 떨어진 일이 죽음으로 끝났다는 것도 밝히지 않았다. 그래서 딕슨은 호기심에 충직함까지 더해 마거릿에게 쉬라고 다그쳤다. 소파에 누운 그녀의 모습은 쉬어야 한다는 것을 너무나 분명히 보여 주고 있었다. 그녀는 누가 말을 걸 때가 아니면 입을 열지 않았다. 아버지가 걱정스레 바라보며 다정히 묻는 말에 미소로 답하려 애썼으나, 미소 대신 핏기 없는 입술은 한숨이 되어 버렸다. 아버지가 하도 안절부절못하는 통에 그녀는 마침내 제 방으로 들어가 잘 채비를 하겠다고 했다. 하기야 이미 아홉 시가 넘었으니 그날 밤 경위가 다시 오지는 않으리라는 쪽으로 그녀의 마음도 기울고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 아버지 곁에 서서 의자 등받이를 붙들고 있었다.\n\n“아버지도 곧 주무실 거죠? 혼자 앉아 계시지 마세요!”\n\n아버지의 대답이 무엇이었는지 그녀는 듣지 못했다. 그 말은 훨씬 작은 소리에 묻혀 버렸다. 그 소리는 그녀의 두려움 속에서 크게 부풀어 올라 머릿속을 가득 채웠다. 현관 초인종이 나직하게 울린 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 아버지에게 입을 맞추고 아래층으로 미끄러지듯 내려갔다. 조금 전의 그녀를 본 사람이라면 그런 몸놀림이 가능하리라고는 생각지 못했을 만큼 빠른 걸음이었다. 그녀는 딕슨을 옆으로 물렸다.\n\n“오지 마요. 내가 문을 열 테니. 그 사람인 줄 알아요. 내가 할 수 있어요. 내가 다 알아서 해야 해요.”\n\n“좋으실 대로 하세요, 아가씨!” 딕슨이 퉁명스레 말했다. 그러나 곧이어 덧붙였다. “하지만 아가씨는 그럴 몸이 아니에요. 산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람 꼴이라고요.”\n\n“내가요?” 마거릿이 돌아서며 말했다. 그 눈은 야릇한 불길로 온통 이글거렸고 두 뺨은 달아올라 있었다. 다만 입술만은 여전히 마르고 파리했다.\n\n그녀는 경위에게 문을 열어 주고 앞장서 서재로 들어갔다. 촛불을 탁자 위에 놓고 심지를 조심스레 자른 다음에야 몸을 돌려 그와 마주 섰다.\n\n“늦으셨군요!” 그녀가 말했다. “그래서요?” 대답을 기다리며 그녀는 숨을 죽였다.\n\n“공연한 수고를 끼쳐 드려 죄송합니다, 아가씨. 결국 검시를 열 생각은 다들 접었습니다. 다른 일도 보고 만날 사람도 있어서 늦었지, 아니었으면 진작에 왔을 겁니다.”\n\n“그럼 끝난 거군요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “더 이상 조사는 없는 거고요.”\n\n“손턴 씨가 제게 보낸 쪽지를 갖고 있을 겁니다.” 경위가 수첩을 뒤적이며 말했다.\n\n“손턴 씨가요!” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“예! 그분이 치안판사시니까요. 아, 여기 있군요.”\n\n그녀는 눈으로 그것을 읽을 수가 없었다. 촛불 바로 곁에 서 있었는데도 그랬다. 글자가 눈앞에서 헤엄쳤다. 그래도 그녀는 그것을 손에 들고 열심히 들여다보는 척했다.\n\n“정말이지 아가씨, 저로서는 큰 짐을 던 셈입니다. 아시다시피 그 사람이 맞기는 맞았는지조차 증거가 하도 불확실해서, 거기에 신원을 두고 다투는 문제까지 끼어들면 사건이 여간 복잡해지는 게 아니거든요. 그래서 손턴 씨께도 말씀을 드렸——”\n\n“손턴 씨께요!” 마거릿이 다시 말했다.\n\n“오늘 아침에 그분이 바로 이 댁에서 나오시는 참에 마주쳤습니다. 제 오랜 은인이신 데다 어젯밤 레너즈를 보신 치안판사이기도 하시니, 감히 제 곤란한 사정을 말씀드렸지요.”\n\n마거릿은 깊이 한숨지었다. 그녀는 더 듣고 싶지 않았다. 이미 들은 것도, 앞으로 들을지 모르는 것도 다 같이 두려웠다. 이 사람이 어서 가 주기를 바랐다. 그녀는 억지로 입을 열었다.\n\n“찾아와 주셔서 고맙습니다. 아주 늦었네요. 열 시가 넘었을 거예요. 아, 여기 쪽지요!” 그녀는 그것을 받으려고 내민 손의 뜻을 문득 알아차리고 말을 이었다. 그가 그것을 도로 넣으려 하자 그녀가 말했다. “글씨가 빽빽하고 어지러워서 읽을 수가 없었어요. 제게 읽어 주시겠어요?”\n\n그는 그것을 소리 내어 읽어 주었다.\n\n“고맙습니다. 제가 거기 없었다고 손턴 씨께 말씀드리셨나요?”\n\n“아, 물론입니다, 아가씨. 그렇게나 잘못된 정보를 믿고 움직인 것이 이제 와 죄송할 따름입니다. 처음에는 그 젊은이가 하도 확신하기에 그랬지요. 그런데 이제 와서는 처음부터 미심쩍었다고 하면서, 자기 잘못 때문에 아가씨가 언짢으셔서 그 가게 단골을 끊으시는 일은 없기를 바란다더군요. 안녕히 주무십시오, 아가씨.”\n\n“안녕히 가세요.” 그녀는 딕슨에게 그를 배웅하라고 종을 울렸다. 딕슨이 복도를 되돌아오는데 마거릿이 재빨리 그 곁을 지나쳤다.\n\n“다 잘됐어요!” 그녀는 딕슨을 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다. 그리고 딕슨이 더 캐물으며 따라오기도 전에 그녀는 위층으로 달아나 침실에 들어가서는 문에 빗장을 질렀다.\n\n그녀는 옷도 갈아입지 않은 채 침대에 몸을 던졌다. 생각하기에는 너무나 지쳐 있었다. 반 시간 남짓 지나서야 옹송그린 자세와, 심한 피로 끝에 밀려든 한기가 마비된 그녀의 정신을 흔들어 깨울 힘을 얻었다. 그제야 그녀는 되짚어 보고, 이어 맞추어 보고, 의아해하기 시작했다. 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각은 프레더릭 때문에 애간장을 태우던 이 모든 두려움이 끝났다는 것, 그 긴장이 지나갔다는 것이었다. 다음에 든 것은 손턴 씨와 관계된 경위의 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 떠올리고 싶다는 마음이었다. 그가 언제 손턴 씨를 만났다고 했던가? 무슨 말을 했던가? 손턴 씨는 무엇을 했던가? 그 쪽지의 말은 정확히 무엇이었던가? 그리고 관사 하나를 넣고 뺀 것까지, 그가 쪽지에 쓴 바로 그 표현을 떠올려 낼 때까지 그녀의 마음은 앞으로 나아가기를 거부했다. 그러나 이어서 다다른 확신만은 더없이 또렷했다. 손턴 씨는 그 운명의 목요일 밤 아웃우드 역 가까이에서 자기를 보았고, 자기가 거기 없었다고 부인했다는 말을 들은 것이다. 그의 눈에 자기는 거짓말쟁이로 서 있었다. 자기는 거짓말쟁이였다. 그러나 그녀는 하느님 앞에서 뉘우칠 생각은 하지 못했다. 손턴 씨의 눈에 자기가 비천해졌다는 그 한 가지 섬뜩한 사실 둘레에는 오직 혼돈과 어둠뿐이었다. 자기가 얼마나 변명할 거리를 댈 수 있는지는 스스로에게조차 생각하고 싶지 않았다. 그것은 손턴 씨와 아무 상관이 없었다. 오빠와 함께 걷는 것처럼 자연스러운 일에서 그가, 혹은 누구라도 의심할 거리를 찾아내리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 않았다. 그러나 정말로 거짓되고 그릇된 그 일을 그는 알고 있었고, 그에게는 자기를 판단할 자격이 있었다. “아, 프레더릭! 프레더릭!” 그녀가 부르짖었다. “오빠를 위해 내가 무엇을 희생하지 않았던가!” 잠이 들어서도 그녀의 생각은 같은 자리를 맴돌아야 했다. 다만 고통의 정황이 부풀려지고 흉측해졌을 뿐이었다.\n\n잠에서 깨었을 때 아침 햇살처럼 환한 새로운 생각이 그녀에게 번쩍 떠올랐다. 손턴 씨는 검시관에게 가기 전에 그녀의 거짓말을 알았다. 그렇다면 그가 그렇게 한 것은 그녀가 그 부인을 되풀이하지 않아도 되도록 감싸 주려는 뜻에서였을지도 모른다는 생각이었다. 그러나 그녀는 병든 아이 같은 고집으로 이 생각을 밀쳐 냈다. 정말 그렇다면 그것은 고마울 일이 아니었다. 이미 그렇게도 참담하게 무너져 버린 정직함을 더 시험당하지 않도록 그가 그토록 유별난 수고를 하기에 앞서, 자기가 이미 욕된 처지에 놓였음을 그가 얼마나 뼈저리게 알아보았는지를 드러낼 뿐이었으니까. 차라리 그 모든 것을 다 겪는 편이 나았을 것이다. 프레더릭을 구하려고 위증까지 했을 것이다. 손턴 씨가 자기를 구하려고 나서게 만든 그 앎을 그가 가지느니, 차라리, 훨씬 더 차라리 그편이 나았을 것이다. 무슨 몹쓸 운수가 그를 경위와 마주치게 했단 말인가? 무엇 때문에 하필 그가 레너즈의 진술을 받으러 불려 나온 치안판사였단 말인가? 레너즈는 무슨 말을 했을까? 그중 얼마나가 손턴 씨에게 알아들을 만한 것이었을까? 그는 두 사람의 공통된 벗인 벨 씨를 통해 프레더릭에게 걸린 옛 혐의를 이미 알고 있을지도 모르지 않는가? 그렇다면 그는 어머니의 임종을 지키려고 법을 어기고 돌아온 아들을 구해 주려 애쓴 셈이었다. 그런 생각이라면 그녀도 고마움을 느낄 수 있었다. 그러나 그의 개입이 경멸에서 나온 것이라면, 언젠가는 몰라도 아직은 아니었다. 아, 자기를 경멸할 만한 정당한 까닭이 누구엔들 있었단 말인가? 하필 손턴 씨에게라니. 지금까지 자기가 상상 속의 높은 자리에서 내려다보던 바로 그 사람에게! 문득 그녀는 자기가 그의 발밑에 서 있음을 깨달았고, 자기가 떨어진 그 자리에 야릇하게도 마음이 아팠다. 그녀는 이 전제를 끝까지 밀고 가서 그의 존중과 좋은 평판을 자기가 얼마나 소중히 여기는지 스스로 인정하는 데까지 나아가기를 움츠러들며 피했다. 긴 생각의 오솔길 끝에서 이 생각이 떠오를 때마다 그녀는 그 길을 따라가기를 그만두고 돌아섰다. 그녀는 그것을 믿으려 하지 않았다.\n\n그녀가 짐작한 것보다 시간이 늦어 있었다. 전날 밤의 소란 통에 시계 태엽 감는 것을 잊었기 때문이고, 헤일 씨가 여느 때처럼 깨우지 말라고 각별히 일러 두었기 때문이다. 이윽고 문이 조심스레 열리며 딕슨이 고개를 들이밀었다. 마거릿이 깨어 있는 것을 보고 그녀는 편지 한 통을 들고 앞으로 나왔다.\n\n“아가씨한테 약이 될 게 왔어요. 프레더릭 도련님한테서 온 편지랍니다.”\n\n“고마워요, 딕슨. 시간이 이렇게나 됐네!”\n\n그녀는 몹시 힘없이 말했고, 편지를 받으려 손을 내밀지도 않은 채 딕슨이 그것을 침대보 위에 놓아두게 두었다.\n\n“아침을 드셔야죠, 암요. 곧 가져다 드릴게요. 주인어른께서 쟁반을 다 차려 놓으신 걸 제가 압니다요.”\n\n마거릿은 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 딕슨을 그대로 내보냈다. 저 편지를 열어 보려면 혼자여야 한다는 느낌이 들었다. 마침내 그녀는 편지를 열었다. 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 받은 날보다 이틀이나 앞선 날짜였다. 그렇다면 오빠는 약속한 대로 편지를 썼던 것이고, 그 놀람과 두려움은 겪지 않아도 되었을 것이었다. 그러나 어쨌든 읽어 보아야 알 일이었다. 편지는 급히 쓴 티가 났지만 더없이 안심되는 내용이었다. 오빠는 헨리 레녹스를 만났다. 레녹스는 이 사건을 꽤 알고 있어서 처음에는 고개를 저으며, 그런 혐의가, 그것도 그토록 막강한 세력의 뒷받침을 받는 혐의가 걸려 있는 몸으로 영국에 돌아온 것은 참으로 무모한 짓이라고 말했다. 그러나 이야기를 나누다 보니 레녹스 씨도, 믿을 만한 증인들로 진술을 입증할 수만 있다면 무죄로 풀려날 가망이 얼마쯤은 있으리라고 인정했다. 그런 경우라면 재판을 받아 볼 만하겠지만, 그러지 못한다면 큰 모험이 되리라는 것이었다. 그는 알아보겠다고, 온갖 수고를 아끼지 않겠다고 했다. “내 짐작에는,” 프레더릭은 이렇게 썼다. “귀여운 내 동생아, 네 소개가 큰 몫을 한 것 같더구나. 그런 게냐? 정말이지 그 사람이 어찌나 이것저것 묻던지. 날카롭고 영리한 사람 같았고, 사무실의 분주한 기색이며 서기들 수로 보아 일도 잘 풀리는 모양이더라. 하기야 이런 건 변호사의 술수일 뿐인지도 모르지. 마침 막 떠나려는 배편을 잡았다. 오 분 뒤면 떠난다. 이 일 때문에 다시 영국에 와야 할지도 모르니 내가 다녀갔다는 것은 비밀로 해 다오. 아버지께는 영국에서는 살 수 없는 진귀한 옛 셰리주를 보내 드리마(지금 내 앞에 놓인 병에 든 그런 것 말이다)! 아버지께는 그런 게 필요하시다. 사랑을 담아 안부 전해 다오. 하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를. 정말이지—아, 마차가 왔구나. 추신. 참으로 아슬아슬했지! 내가 다녀갔다는 말은 절대 입 밖에 내지 마라. 쇼 이모네에도 말이다.”\n\n마거릿은 봉투를 살펴보았다. 봉투에는 “발송 지연”이라는 표시가 찍혀 있었다. 아마 어느 덤벙대는 급사에게 맡겼는데 그 사람이 부치는 것을 잊은 모양이었다. 아, 우리와 유혹 사이를 가로막고 선 우연이란 얼마나 가느다란 거미줄인가! 프레더릭은 스무 시간, 아니 서른 시간 전에 이미 무사히 영국을 떠나 있었다. 그런데 그녀가 추격을 따돌리려고 거짓말을 한 것은 겨우 열일곱 시간쯤 전이었으니, 그때도 이미 헛된 짓이었던 것이다. 그녀는 얼마나 믿음이 없었던가! 이제 “마땅히 할 바를 하라, 그 뒤는 될 대로 되게 하라”던 그 자랑스러운 좌우명은 어디로 갔는가? 자기 일에 관한 한 용감하게 사실대로 말하고, 남에 관해 말하기를 거부하는 것은 알아낼 테면 알아내 보라고 맞섰더라면, 지금 그녀의 마음은 얼마나 가벼웠을 것인가! 하느님을 향한 믿음에 실패한 자로서 그분 앞에 낮추어질 일도 없었을 것이고, 손턴 씨의 눈에 비천해지고 욕될 일도 없었을 것이다. 여기까지 생각하다 그녀는 비참한 떨림과 함께 스스로를 붙들었다. 자기가 그의 낮아진 평가를 하느님의 노여움과 나란히 놓고 있었던 것이다. 어찌하여 그가 이토록 끈질기게 그녀의 마음을 떠나지 않는단 말인가? 대체 무엇 때문인가? 그 온갖 자존심에도 불구하고, 자기 자신에게 맞서면서까지, 어찌하여 그가 어떻게 생각하는지를 마음에 두는 것인가? 전능하신 분의 노여움이라면 견딜 수 있으리라고 그녀는 믿었다. 그분은 모든 것을 아시고, 그녀의 뉘우침을 읽으실 수 있으며, 앞으로 도움을 구하는 그녀의 부르짖음을 들으실 수 있으니까. 그러나 손턴 씨는—어찌하여 그녀는 떨면서 베개에 얼굴을 묻는 것인가? 마침내 어떤 거센 감정이 그녀를 사로잡은 것인가?\n\n그녀는 침대에서 뛰어내려 오래도록 간절히 기도했다. 그렇게 마음을 열고 나니 위로가 되고 마음이 가라앉았다. 그러나 자기 처지를 다시 돌아보자마자 가시가 여전히 거기 박혀 있음을 알았다. 자기는 한낱 사람의 낮아진 평가에 아랑곳하지 않을 만큼 착하지도, 순결하지도 못하다는 것. 그가 자기를 경멸하는 눈으로 보고 있으리라는 생각이 자기와 죄를 뉘우치는 마음 사이를 가로막고 서 있다는 것. 그녀는 옷을 갈아입자마자 편지를 들고 아버지에게 갔다. 역에서 겪은 놀람에 대한 언급이 하도 스치듯 짧아서 헤일 씨는 그 대목을 눈여겨보지 않고 지나쳤다. 사실 그때 그는 프레더릭이 들키지도 의심받지도 않고 배를 탔다는 사실 말고는 편지에서 별로 알아낸 것이 없었다. 마거릿의 핏기 없는 얼굴이 그토록 마음에 걸렸기 때문이다. 그녀는 자꾸만 금세라도 울음을 터뜨릴 것만 같아 보였다.\n\n“너무 무리했구나, 마거릿. 그럴 만도 하지. 하지만 이제는 내가 너를 돌보게 해 다오.”\n\n그는 딸을 소파에 눕히고 덮어 줄 숄을 가지러 갔다. 아버지의 다정함에 눈물이 터져 나왔고, 그녀는 서럽게 울었다.\n\n“가엾은 것! 가엾은 것!” 딸이 벽 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린 채 흐느낌으로 몸을 떠는 것을 애틋하게 바라보며 그가 말했다. 이윽고 울음이 그쳤고, 그녀는 자기 괴로움을 아버지에게 다 털어놓는 위안을 감히 누려도 될지 생각해 보기 시작했다. 그러나 그러지 말아야 할 이유가 그래야 할 이유보다 많았다. 그래야 할 이유는 자기 마음이 가벼워진다는 것 하나뿐이었다. 그러지 말아야 할 이유로는, 만일 정말로 프레더릭이 다시 영국에 와야 한다면 아버지의 불안이 크게 더해지리라는 생각이 있었다. 아무리 모르고 한 일, 바라지 않은 일이라 해도 아들이 사람을 죽게 만들었다는 정황을 아버지는 곱씹을 것이었다. 그 앎이 단순한 사실에서 부풀려지고 뒤틀린 온갖 모습으로 끊임없이 되살아나 아버지를 괴롭힐 것이었다. 그리고 자기가 저지른 큰 잘못에 대해서는—아버지는 딸의 용기 없음과 믿음 없음에 한없이 마음 아파하면서도, 딸을 위해 변명거리를 찾느라 내내 애를 태울 것이었다. 예전 같았으면 마거릿은 아버지이자 사제인 그에게 가서 자기가 받은 유혹과 자기 죄를 말했을 것이다. 그러나 근래에는 그런 문제로 이야기를 나눈 적이 별로 없었고, 생각이 바뀐 그가 자기 영혼의 깊은 곳이 그의 영혼을 부를 때 무어라 답할지 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 아니, 그녀는 자기 비밀을 지키고 그 짐을 홀로 지리라. 홀로 하느님 앞에 나아가 용서를 구하리라. 손턴 씨의 눈에 욕된 처지가 된 것도 홀로 견디리라. 그녀는 아버지가 즐거운 이야깃거리를 찾아내어 요즘 일어난 일들에서 딸의 생각을 돌려놓으려고 다정히 애쓰는 것에 말할 수 없이 마음이 움직였다. 아버지가 이날처럼 말이 많았던 것은 몇 달 만이었다. 그는 딸을 일어나 앉게 두지 않았고, 손수 시중을 들겠다고 고집하여 딕슨의 비위를 몹시 상하게 했다.\n\n마침내 그녀가 미소를 지었다. 힘없고 가녀린 미소였으나 아버지에게는 더없는 기쁨이었다.\n\n“우리에게 앞날의 희망을 가장 크게 주는 사람의 이름이 돌로레스라니, 생각하면 이상해요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그것은 평소의 그녀보다 아버지에게 더 어울리는 말이었다. 그러나 그날은 두 사람의 성정이 뒤바뀐 듯했다.\n\n“그 애 어머니가 스페인 사람이었다더구나. 그래서 종교도 그런 게지. 아버지는 내가 알던 무렵에는 꼿꼿한 장로교도였다. 어쨌든 아주 부드럽고 예쁜 이름이야.”\n\n“참 어리네요! 저보다 열네 달이나 아래잖아요. 이디스가 레녹스 대위와 약혼했을 때가 딱 그 나이였어요. 아버지, 우리 스페인에 가서 그 사람들을 만나요.”\n\n그는 고개를 저었다. 그러나 이렇게 말했다. “네가 바란다면야, 마거릿. 다만 여기로 돌아오자꾸나. 네 어머니에게 온당치 못한 일, 매정한 일로 여겨질 게다. 아무래도 어머니는 늘 밀턴을 몹시 싫어했던 것 같지만, 그래도 어머니가 여기 누워 있고 우리와 함께 갈 수 없는데 지금 이곳을 떠난다면 말이다. 아니다, 얘야. 너 혼자 가서 그들을 만나고 내 스페인 딸 이야기를 전해 다오.”\n\n“아니에요, 아버지. 아버지 없이는 안 가요. 제가 가면 누가 아버지를 돌보나요?”\n\n“우리 둘 중에 누가 누구를 돌보고 있는지 나도 알고 싶구나. 하지만 네가 간다면 손턴 씨를 졸라 수업을 갑절로 해 달라고 하마. 우리 둘이서 고전을 신나게 파고들 게다. 그거야 언제까지고 재미있는 일이지. 원한다면 코르푸까지 가서 이디스도 만나고 오려무나.”\n\n마거릿은 곧바로 대꾸하지 않았다. 그러다 자못 진지하게 말했다. “고마워요, 아버지. 하지만 저는 가고 싶지 않아요. 레녹스 씨가 일을 잘 처리해서 오빠가 혼인한 뒤에 돌로레스를 데리고 우리를 보러 올 수 있게 되기를 바라요. 이디스로 말하면, 연대가 코르푸에 그리 오래 머물지는 않을 거예요. 어쩌면 한 해가 가기 전에 두 사람 다 여기서 보게 될지도 모르죠.”\n\n헤일 씨의 즐거운 이야깃거리는 바닥이 났다. 괴로운 기억들이 슬며시 마음을 스치고 지나가 그를 침묵으로 몰아넣었다. 이윽고 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“아버지, 장례식에서 니컬러스 히긴스를 보셨어요? 그 사람도 왔었고 메리도 왔었어요. 가엾은 사람! 그게 그 사람 나름의 조의 표시였던 거예요. 무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 겉모습 아래 따뜻하고 좋은 마음을 지닌 사람이에요.”\n\n“나도 그렇게 믿는다.” 헤일 씨가 대답했다. “네가 그 사람을 온갖 못된 사람으로 몰아붙이려 하던 때에도 나는 줄곧 그걸 알고 있었지. 네 몸이 그만큼 걸을 만큼 튼튼하다면 내일 가서 만나 보자꾸나.”\n\n“네, 좋아요. 저도 만나고 싶어요. 메리에게 삯을 못 치렀어요. 아니, 딕슨 말로는 메리가 받지 않겠다고 했다더군요. 그 사람이 끼니를 든 뒤, 일 나가기 전에 딱 맞춰 가도록 해요.”\n\n저녁 무렵 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“손턴 씨가 들르지 않을까 반쯤 기대했다만. 어제 자기가 가진 책 이야기를 하기에 내가 보고 싶다고 했더니, 오늘 가져와 보겠다고 했거든.”\n\n마거릿은 한숨을 쉬었다. 그녀는 그가 오지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 자기가 당한 수치가 그의 기억에 아직 그토록 생생할 텐데, 그는 너무나 조심스러운 사람이라 자기와 마주칠 위험을 무릅쓰지 않을 터였다. 그의 이름이 나오는 것만으로도 괴로움이 되살아났고, 그녀는 다시 짓눌린 채 무언가에 사로잡힌 듯한 탈진 상태로 빠져들었다. 그녀는 맥없는 나른함에 자기를 내맡겼다. 그러다 문득, 이것이야말로 인내를 보이는 데도, 하루 종일 자기를 살뜰히 보살펴 준 아버지에게 보답하는 데도 어울리지 않는 태도라는 생각이 들었다. 그녀는 몸을 일으켜 앉아 소리 내어 책을 읽어 드리겠다고 했다. 아버지는 눈이 침침해진 터라 딸의 제안을 반갑게 받아들였다. 그녀는 잘 읽었다. 강약을 알맞게 주었다. 그러나 다 읽고 났을 때 누가 방금 읽은 것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었더라면 그녀는 대답하지 못했을 것이다. 그녀는 손턴 씨에게 은혜를 모르는 사람이 되었다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 아침에 그녀는, 검시가 열리지 않도록 의사들에게 다시 알아봐 준 그의 친절을 받아들이기를 마다했던 것이다. 아, 그녀는 고마웠다! 자기는 비겁하고 거짓되었으며, 그 비겁함과 거짓됨을 돌이킬 수 없는 행동으로 드러내고 말았다. 그러나 은혜를 모르는 사람은 아니었다. 자기를 경멸할 만한 까닭이 있는 사람에게 이런 마음을 품을 수 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 그녀의 가슴은 뜨거워졌다. 그가 경멸할 까닭은 너무나 정당해서, 만일 그가 경멸을 느끼지 않는다고 생각했다면 그녀는 그를 덜 존경했을 것이다. 자기가 그를 얼마나 온전히 존경하는지를 느끼는 것은 기쁨이었다. 그것만은 그가 막을 수 없었다. 그것이 이 모든 비참함 속에서 단 하나의 위안이었다.\n\n저녁 늦게 기다리던 책이 도착했다. “손턴 씨가 안부를 전하며 헤일 씨께서 어떠하신지 여쭙습니다” 하는 말과 함께였다.\n\n“훨씬 나아졌다고 전해 주게, 딕슨. 다만 헤일 양이——”\n\n“아니에요, 아버지.” 마거릿이 다급하게 말했다. “제 이야기는 하지 마세요. 저를 묻지도 않았잖아요.”\n\n“얘야, 이렇게 떨고 있구나!” 몇 분 뒤 아버지가 말했다. “당장 자리에 들어야겠다. 얼굴이 아주 새하얘졌어!”\n\n마거릿은 아버지를 혼자 두기가 마음에 걸리기는 했으나 가기를 마다하지 않았다. 하루 종일 분주히 생각하고 더 분주히 뉘우친 끝에 홀로 있는 위안이 필요했다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 그녀는 여느 때와 별로 다르지 않아 보였다. 가시지 않은 무거움과 슬픔, 이따금씩의 넋 나감은 상을 당한 지 얼마 안 된 사람에게 이상할 것 없는 기색이었다. 그리고 그녀가 건강을 되찾는 것과 거의 나란히, 아버지는 잃어버린 아내와 그에게 영영 닫혀 버린 인생의 지난 시절에 대한 넋 나간 상념 속으로 도로 빠져들었다.\n\n# 제36장\n\n“관을 멘 이들의 걸음은 무겁고 더디며, 애도하는 이들의 흐느낌은 깊고 나직하다.” — 셸리.\n\n전날 정해 둔 시각에 두 사람은 니컬러스 히긴스와 그의 딸을 만나러 걸어서 길을 나섰다. 새로 지어 입은 상복이 어쩐지 낯설고 서먹한 데다, 여러 주 만에 처음으로 마음먹고 함께 밖에 나선 것이어서, 둘은 얼마 전의 상실을 새삼 떠올렸다. 그들은 말 없는 연민 속에서 서로에게 바싹 붙어 걸었다.\n\n니컬러스는 늘 앉던 구석 자리 난롯가에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 늘 물던 파이프는 물고 있지 않았다. 그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 팔을 무릎에 얹고 있었다. 두 사람을 보고도 일어서지 않았지만, 마거릿은 그의 눈에 어린 반가움을 읽을 수 있었다.\n\n“앉으슈, 앉으슈. 불이 다 죽었구먼.” 그가 이렇게 말하며 불을 힘차게 쑤셨다. 마치 자기에게서 눈길을 돌려놓으려는 듯이. 과연 그의 몰골은 어수선했다. 며칠씩 깎지 않은 시커먼 수염이 창백한 얼굴을 한층 더 창백해 보이게 했고, 웃옷은 기워 입는 편이 훨씬 나을 지경이었다.\n\n“식사 때가 막 지난 참이면 계실 것 같아서 왔어요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n“우리도 자네를 본 뒤로 슬픈 일을 겪었네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“예, 예. 요즘은 슬픔이 끼니보다 흔하지요. 내 끼니때는 하루 온종일 늘어져 있으니, 언제 오셔도 나를 만나기는 어렵잖을 거요.”\n\n“일자리를 잃으셨어요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“그렇소.” 그가 짧게 대답했다. 그러고는 잠시 잠잠하다가 처음으로 고개를 들며 덧붙였다. “돈이 궁한 건 아니오. 그리 생각지 마슈. 베스가, 가엾은 것이, 마지막 순간에 내 손에 쥐여 주려고 베개 밑에 조금 모아 두었더랬고, 메리는 퍼스티언 천을 자르고 있으니까. 그래도 일자리가 없는 건 매한가지지만.”\n\n“우리가 메리에게 갚을 돈이 좀 있네.” 마거릿이 팔을 세게 눌러 그 말을 막기도 전에 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그 애가 그걸 받는 날엔 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리겠소. 나는 이 네 벽 안에 있고 그 애는 밖에 있게 될 거요. 그뿐이오.”\n\n“하지만 그 애의 고마운 수고에 우리는 큰 신세를 졌는데.” 헤일 씨가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n“나는 저기 댁의 따님이 내 가엾은 딸년한테 베푼 사랑에 고맙단 말 한마디 한 적이 없소. 도무지 말이 떠오르질 않더구먼. 메리가 댁들한테 해 드린 그 하찮은 일로 이러쿵저러쿵 소란을 피우실 양이면, 나도 이제부터 그 말을 찾아보기 시작해야겠소.”\n\n“파업 때문에 일자리를 잃으신 건가요?” 마거릿이 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n“파업은 끝났소. 이번 판은 다 끝난 거요. 내가 일자리가 없는 건 달라고 하질 않았기 때문이오. 그리고 달라고 하지 않은 건, 좋은 말은 귀하고 나쁜 말은 흔해 빠졌기 때문이고.”\n\n그는 수수께끼 같은 대답을 내놓으며 뿌루퉁한 즐거움을 맛보는 기분이었다. 그러나 마거릿은 그가 그 뜻을 물어봐 주기를 바란다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n“좋은 말이란 건……?”\n\n“일 좀 달라고 하는 거요. 사람이 입에 담을 수 있는 말 중에 그만한 게 또 없지 싶소. ‘일을 주슈’ 하는 건 ‘사내답게 해내겠소’ 하는 뜻이니까. 그게 좋은 말이오.”\n\n“그러면 나쁜 말이란, 그렇게 청하는데 일을 주지 않는 것이겠네요.”\n\n“그렇소. 나쁜 말이란 이런 거요. ‘아하, 이 잘난 친구! 자네는 자네 패거리한테 의리를 지켰으니, 나는 내 패거리한테 의리를 지키겠네. 자네는 도움이 필요한 자들을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 다 했지. 그게 자네가 자네 부류한테 의리를 지키는 방식이렷다. 그러니 나도 내 부류한테 의리를 지키겠네. 자네는 더 나은 걸 몰라 우직하게 충실한 바보 노릇이나 한 딱한 바보야. 그러니 썩 꺼져 버리게. 여기 자네한테 줄 일은 없네.’ 그게 나쁜 말이오. 나는 바보가 아니외다. 설령 바보였다 한들, 사람들이 저희 방식대로 똑똑해지는 법을 나한테 가르쳤어야지. 누구든 가르쳐 보려고나 했으면 나도 배웠을지 모를 일 아니오.”\n\n“옛 주인에게 다시 받아 달라고 청해 보는 것이 그래도 해 볼 만한 일 아니겠나?” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “가망이야 적겠지만, 그래도 가망은 가망이니.”\n\n그는 다시 고개를 들어 묻는 이를 날카롭게 흘긋 보더니, 나직하고 씁쓸한 웃음을 흘렸다.\n\n“선생. 언짢게 여기지 않으신다면, 이번엔 내가 한두 가지 여쭤 보겠소.”\n\n“얼마든지 물어보게.” 헤일 씨가 말했다.\n\n“선생도 밥벌이할 방도가 있으실 것 아니오. 다른 데서 살 수 있다면야, 그저 좋아서 밀턴에 사는 사람은 드무니까.”\n\n“자네 말이 옳네. 따로 물려받은 재산이 좀 있기는 하네만, 내가 밀턴에 자리를 잡은 것은 개인 교사가 되기 위해서였네.”\n\n“사람을 가르친다. 흠! 그럼 가르쳐 준 값으로 돈을 받으시겠구려, 안 그렇소?”\n\n“그렇지.” 헤일 씨가 미소 지으며 대답했다. “돈을 받으려고 가르치는 걸세.”\n\n“그럼 그 돈을 주는 자들이, 수고한 값으로 정당하게, 말하자면 공평한 맞바꿈으로 준 그 돈을 가지고 뭘 해라 뭘 하지 마라 이래라저래라 합디까?”\n\n“아니, 그럴 리가 있나!”\n\n“‘당신한테 형제가, 아니면 형제만큼이나 소중한 벗이 있어서 그 사람이 이 돈을 어떤 일에 쓰려 하고 당신도 그 사람도 그게 옳다고 여긴다 칩시다. 하지만 그 사람한테는 주지 않겠다고 약조하시오. 당신 눈엔 그 돈을 쓸 좋은 데가 보일지 몰라도, 우리 눈엔 그게 좋아 보이질 않소. 그러니 그런 식으로 쓴다면 우리는 당신과 거래를 끊겠소.’ 이렇게 말하진 않는단 말이지요, 그렇지 않소?”\n\n“물론 그럴 리가 없지!”\n\n“만약 그렇게 한다면 참고 견디시겠소?”\n\n“그런 지시에 따를 생각이라도 하게 되려면 어지간히 심한 압박이 있어야 할 걸세.”\n\n“이 넓은 땅덩이 어디에도 나를 그렇게 만들 압박은 없소.” 니컬러스 히긴스가 말했다. “자, 이제 아셨구려. 정통으로 맞히셨소. 햄퍼네가—내가 일하던 데요—직공들한테 조합을 돕거나 파업한 사람들이 굶주리지 않게 하는 데 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고 서약을 시킨단 말이오. 서약이야 시키든 말든.” 그가 비웃듯 말을 이었다. “거짓말쟁이하고 위선자를 만들어 낼 뿐이지. 그리고 그건 내 생각엔 그래도 작은 죄요. 사람 마음을 모질게 굳혀서 도움이 필요한 이한테 인정 한번 못 베풀게 하고, 힘센 손에 거스른다는 이유로 옳고 바른 일에 손을 못 보태게 하는 것에 견주면 말이오. 하지만 나는 임금님이 주실 수 있는 일자리를 죄다 준다 해도 내 맹세를 저버리진 않겠소. 나는 조합원이오. 그리고 노동자한테 이로운 건 그것뿐이라고 생각하오. 나도 파업을 해 봤고 굶주림이 어떤 건지도 겪어 봤소. 그러니 1실링이 생기면 그중 6펜스는 달라는 사람한테 갈 거요. 그러다 보니 1실링을 어디서 벌지 알 수가 없게 된 거지.”\n\n“조합에 돈을 내지 못하게 하는 그 규칙이 모든 공장에서 시행되고 있나요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“그건 모르겠소. 우리 공장에선 새로 생긴 규정이오. 그리고 내가 보기엔 그자들도 그걸 끝까지 밀고 가진 못할 거요. 하지만 지금은 시행 중이지. 머잖아 알게 되겠지, 폭군이 거짓말쟁이를 만든다는 걸.”\n\n잠깐 말이 끊겼다. 마거릿은 마음에 있는 말을 해야 할지 망설였다. 언제나 충분히 우울하고 낙담해 있는 사람의 심기를 건드리고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 마침내 말이 나오고 말았다. 그러나 그 부드러운 어조와 머뭇거리는 태도가 언짢은 말은 하고 싶지 않다는 마음을 드러내 보였으므로, 히긴스는 화가 나기보다 그저 어리둥절해하는 듯했다.\n\n“가엾은 바우처가 조합이 폭군이라고 하던 말 기억하세요? 그중에서도 가장 나쁜 폭군이라고 했던 것 같아요. 그리고 그때 저도 그 말에 동의했던 게 기억나요.”\n\n그가 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그는 두 손에 머리를 괸 채 불 속을 내려다보고 있어서, 그녀는 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 읽을 수 없었다.\n\n“조합이 사람을 억지로 제 이익 쪽으로 몰아붙이는 걸 필요하다고 여긴다는 건 나도 부인하지 않겠소. 곧이곧대로 말하리다. 조합에 들지 않은 사람은 고달픈 삶을 살지요. 하지만 일단 조합에 들면, 저 혼자 제 앞가림을 하는 것보다, 아니 제 힘만으로 하는 것보다 훨씬 낫게 제 몫을 챙기게 되오. 노동자가 제 권리를 얻는 길은 그것뿐이오. 다 함께 뭉치는 것 말이오. 조합원이 많을수록 사람 하나하나가 정당한 대접을 받을 가망도 커지지. 나라에서도 바보와 미친 사람을 돌보지 않소. 그리고 누가 저 자신이나 이웃을 해치려 들면, 좋든 싫든 고삐를 채우지 않소. 조합에서 하는 일도 그게 다요. 우리는 사람을 옥에 처넣을 수야 없지. 하지만 한 사람의 삶을 견디기 어려울 만큼 무겁게 만들어서, 제 뜻과 상관없이 들어와 슬기롭고 쓸모 있는 사람이 되게 할 수는 있소. 바우처는 처음부터 끝까지 바보였고, 마지막이 제일 지독한 바보짓이었소.”\n\n“그가 여러분에게 해를 끼쳤나요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“그렇소, 끼쳤소. 그자와 그 패거리가 소동을 벌이고 법을 어기기 전까지는 여론이 우리 편이었소. 그때 파업은 다 끝장이 난 거요.”\n\n“그렇다면 그를 그냥 내버려 두고 억지로 조합에 들이지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫지 않았을까요? 그는 여러분에게 아무 보탬도 되지 않았고, 여러분은 그를 미치게 만들었잖아요.”\n\n“마거릿.” 아버지가 낮고 조심스러운 목소리로 불렀다. 히긴스의 얼굴에 먹구름이 몰려드는 것을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n“나는 이 아가씨가 마음에 드오.” 히긴스가 불쑥 말했다. “마음에 있는 걸 툭 터놓고 말하니 말이오. 그래도 조합이 뭔지는 모르지만. 그건 대단한 힘이오. 우리한테 있는 유일한 힘이지. 쟁기가 데이지 꽃 위로 지나가는 이야기를 읊은 시를 조금 읽은 적이 있는데, 내가 울 다른 까닭이 생기기도 전에 눈물이 다 나옵디다. 그렇지만 그 사람도 데이지를 가엾어했을 뿐, 쟁기질을 멈추진 않았을 거요, 내 장담하리다. 그럴 만큼 어리석진 않았을 테니. 조합이 그 쟁기요. 수확 때를 위해 땅을 갈아 놓는 거지. 바우처 같은 자는—데이지에 견주는 것도 너무 치켜세우는 거요. 그자는 땅바닥에 널브러진 잡초에 가깝지—그저 치워질 각오를 해야 하는 거요. 지금은 그자한테 심사가 아주 뒤틀려 있소. 그러니 그자를 공평하게 말하고 있지 않은지도 모르지. 나 같으면 신이 나서 그자 위로 쟁기를 몰고 지나갈 것 같소.”\n\n“왜요? 그가 무슨 일을 했나요? 또 무슨 일이 있었어요?”\n\n“있고말고요. 그 인간은 사고를 안 치고는 못 배기지. 우선은 미친 바보처럼 날뛰며 그 소동을 일으켰소. 그러곤 숨어 지내야 했지. 손턴이 내 바람대로 끝까지 쫓았더라면 아직도 숨어 있었을 거요. 하지만 손턴은 제 목적을 이뤘으니 폭동 건으로 계속 기소할 마음이 없었던 게지. 그래서 바우처는 슬금슬금 제 집으로 기어 들어왔소. 하루 이틀은 밖에 얼굴을 안 내밀더군. 그만한 염치는 있었던 거요. 그러고는, 그자가 어디로 갔을 것 같소? 아, 햄퍼네로 갔지 뭐요. 빌어먹을 놈! 보고만 있어도 속이 뒤집히는 그 굽실거리는 낯짝을 하고 가서 일을 달라고 했단 말이오. 조합에 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고, 굶주리는 파업자를 돕지 않겠다고 서약하는 새 규칙을 뻔히 알면서도! 아니, 궁할 때 조합이 돕지 않았으면 그자는 굶어 죽었을 텐데! 그런 주제에 가서는 무슨 약조든 하겠다, 무슨 서약이든 하겠다, 우리가 하는 일을 죄다 일러바치겠다고 굽실거렸다지 뭐요. 그 쓸모없는 유다 같은 놈! 하지만 햄퍼에 대해선 이 말만은 해 주겠고, 죽는 날까지 그 일로 고마워하겠소. 그자가 바우처를 쫓아 버리고 한마디도 들어주지 않았다는 거요. 곁에 있던 사람들 말로는 그 배신자가 어린애처럼 울었다는데도 말이오!”\n\n“아! 어쩌면 그렇게 끔찍한! 어쩌면 그렇게 딱한!” 마거릿이 외쳤다. “히긴스, 오늘은 당신을 모르겠어요. 바우처를 지금의 저 사람으로 만든 게 바로 당신들이라는 걸 모르시겠어요? 마음이 따라가지도 않는데 억지로 조합에 몰아넣어서 말이에요. 당신들이 그를 지금의 저 사람으로 만든 거예요!”\n\n그를 지금의 저 사람으로 만들었다니! 그는 지금 무엇이 되어 있었던가?\n\n좁은 거리를 따라 모여들고 또 모여들며, 공허하고 일정한 소리가 다가왔다. 이제는 그 소리가 억지로 그들의 주의를 끌었다. 숱한 목소리가 낮게 잦아들어 있었고, 숱한 발소리가 들렸다. 앞으로 나아가는 것이 아니라, 적어도 빠르거나 고른 걸음으로 나아가는 것이 아니라, 한 지점을 빙 둘러싸고 도는 듯한 발소리였다. 그렇다, 그중에는 또렷하고 느린 발소리가 하나 있어, 허공에 제 길을 훤히 내며 그들의 귀에 닿았다. 무거운 짐을 진 사람들의 일정하고 고된 걸음이었다. 두 사람은 거역할 수 없는 어떤 충동에 이끌려 집 문간으로 나갔다. 하찮은 호기심 때문이 아니라, 무슨 엄숙한 나팔 소리에 떠밀리듯 그리로 나선 것이었다.\n\n6명의 남자가 길 한가운데를 걸어오고 있었고, 그중 3명은 경관이었다. 그들은 돌쩌귀에서 떼어 낸 문짝 하나를 어깨에 메고 있었고, 그 위에는 어떤 주검이 놓여 있었다. 문짝 양옆에서는 끊임없이 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌다. 거리의 사람들이 모두 쏟아져 나와 구경하고, 구경하다가는 그 행렬을 따라나섰다. 저마다 멘 이들에게 물었고, 그들은 하도 여러 번 이야기를 되풀이한 터라 나중에는 마지못한 듯 대답했다.\n\n“저 너머 들판 개울에서 찾았소.”\n\n“개울이라니! 아니, 거기 사람이 빠져 죽을 만큼 물이 있나?”\n\n“독한 사람이었지. 얼굴을 아래로 하고 엎드려 있었소. 무슨 까닭이 있었든, 사는 게 어지간히 지겨웠던 게요.”\n\n히긴스가 마거릿 곁으로 슬금슬금 다가와 가늘게 새는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. “존 바우처는 아니겠지요? 그자는 그럴 배짱도 없었소. 아무렴! 존 바우처일 리 없소! 아니, 다들 왜 이쪽을 보는 거요! 들어 보슈! 머릿속에서 웅웅 소리가 나서 들리질 않소.”\n\n사람들이 문짝을 돌바닥에 조심스레 내려놓자, 물에 빠져 죽은 그 가엾은 사내가 모두의 눈에 들어왔다. 유리알 같은 두 눈은 한쪽이 반쯤 열린 채 곧장 하늘을 올려다보고 있었다. 엎드린 자세로 발견된 탓에 얼굴은 부어오르고 빛이 변해 있었다. 게다가 염색에 쓰이던 개울물에 살갗이 물들어 있었다. 앞머리는 벗어졌고 뒷머리는 성글고 길게 자라 있어, 갈래갈래 늘어진 머리카락마다 물이 타고 흘러내렸다. 그렇게 일그러진 모습 속에서도 마거릿은 존 바우처를 알아보았다. 그 가엾게 뒤틀리고 고통에 찬 얼굴을 들여다보는 것이 신성모독처럼 느껴져, 그녀는 문득 이는 마음결에 이끌려 앞으로 나아가 손수건으로 죽은 이의 얼굴을 가만히 덮어 주었다. 그녀가 그렇게 하는 것을 본 눈길들이, 그 경건한 소임을 마치고 돌아서는 그녀를 따라갔고, 그리하여 니컬러스 히긴스가 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 사람처럼 서 있는 곳에 이르렀다. 남자들이 저희끼리 이야기를 나누더니, 그중 하나가 히긴스에게 다가왔다. 히긴스는 어떻게든 제 집 안으로 물러나고만 싶었다.\n\n“히긴스, 자네가 저 사람을 알잖나! 자네가 가서 부인한테 알려야 하네. 부드럽게 말하게, 이 사람아. 그렇지만 서둘러야 해. 여기 오래 눕혀 둘 수는 없으니까.”\n\n“나는 못 가오.” 히긴스가 말했다. “나한테 시키지 마슈. 그 여자 얼굴을 볼 낯이 없소.”\n\n“자네가 그 여자를 제일 잘 알잖나.” 사내가 말했다. “우린 저 사람을 여기까지 옮겨 오느라 할 만큼 했네. 자네도 자네 몫을 하게.”\n\n“나는 못 하오.” 히긴스가 말했다. “저 사람을 보고 나니 다리에 힘이 다 빠졌소. 우린 사이가 좋지 않았는데, 이제 저렇게 죽어 버렸구먼.”\n\n“뭐, 안 하겠다면 안 하는 거지. 그래도 누군가는 해야 해. 고달픈 일이긴 하네만, 이렇게 차차 알아차리게 해 줄 사람이 가지 않으면 저 여자가 더 험한 식으로 소식을 듣게 될 판이란 말일세. 지금 이 순간에도 말이야.”\n\n“아버지, 아버지가 가 주세요.” 마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“내가 할 수 있다면—무슨 말을 하는 게 나을지 생각할 틈이라도 있다면 모르겠다만, 이렇게 갑자기는——” 마거릿은 아버지가 정말로 할 수 없는 상태임을 알아보았다. 그는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었다.\n\n“제가 갈게요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“고맙수, 아가씨. 인정 많은 일이오. 듣자니 그 여자가 워낙 병약한 사람인 데다, 이 근방에 그 여자를 잘 아는 이도 몇 없으니 말이오.”\n\n마거릿은 닫힌 문을 두드렸다. 그러나 안에서는 제멋대로 구는 어린것들이 여럿 있는 듯한 소란이 어찌나 시끄럽던지 대답이 들리지 않았다. 아니, 자기 소리가 들리기나 했을지도 의심스러웠다. 그리고 지체하는 순간순간마다 이 일에서 더욱더 뒷걸음질 치게 되었으므로, 그녀는 문을 열고 들어가 뒤로 닫았다. 게다가 그 여인이 보지 못하는 사이에 빗장까지 걸어 두었다.\n\n바우처 부인은 제대로 치우지 않은 벽난로 건너편에서 흔들의자에 앉아 있었다. 며칠 동안 집 안에 청소라고는 손 한 번 대지 않은 듯한 꼴이었다.\n\n마거릿은 무슨 말인가를 했지만 스스로도 무슨 말인지 알지 못했다. 목과 입이 바싹 말라 있었고, 아이들의 소란 때문에 그녀의 말은 도무지 들리지 않았다. 그녀는 다시 말해 보았다.\n\n“좀 어떠세요, 바우처 부인? 많이 편찮으신가 봐요.”\n\n“내가 성할 턱이 있겠수.” 그녀가 푸념하듯 말했다. “이 어린것들을 나 혼자 건사하는데, 조용히 시키려 해도 쥐여 줄 게 있나. 존은 날 두고 가지 말았어야 했수. 이렇게 몸도 성치 않은데.”\n\n“나가신 지 얼마나 되셨나요?”\n\n“4일 됐수. 여기선 아무도 그이한테 일을 안 주니 그린필드 쪽으로 떠돌러 가야 했지. 그래도 벌써 돌아왔거나, 일자리를 잡았으면 기별이라도 보냈어야 하는 거 아뉴. 그이는——”\n\n“아, 그분을 탓하지 마세요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “그분도 뼈저리게 느끼셨을 거예요, 분명히——”\n\n“시끄러운 소리 좀 그치고 이 아가씨 말씀 좀 듣게 해 주련!” 한 살쯤 된 조무래기에게 그리 곱지 않은 목소리로 하는 말이었다. 그러고는 미안한 듯 마거릿에게 말을 이었다. “이 녀석이 노상 ‘아빠’ ‘빵’ 하고 졸라 대서 성가시게 구는데, 줄 빵도 없고 아빠는 나가서는 우릴 죄다 잊어버린 모양이지 뭐유. 얘가 제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라우.” 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 갑자기 기분이 바뀌더니, 아이를 제 무릎께로 끌어당겨 애틋하게 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n마거릿은 여인의 팔에 손을 얹어 주의를 끌었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다.\n\n“가엾은 것.” 마거릿이 천천히 말했다. “제 아버지가 끔찍이 아끼던 아이였군요.”\n\n“제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라니까요.” 여인이 황급히 일어서서 마거릿과 얼굴을 맞대고 서며 말했다. 한동안 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 이윽고 바우처 부인이 낮게 으르렁대는 어조로 입을 열더니, 말을 이어 갈수록 사나워졌다. “제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라고 하잖수. 가난한 사람도 제 자식을 부자만큼 사랑할 줄 안다우. 왜 말이 없수? 왜 그 큰 눈에 가엾다는 빛을 담고 날 빤히 쳐다보는 거유? 존은 어디 있수?” 몸이 약한데도 그녀는 대답을 끌어내려고 마거릿을 흔들었다. “아이고, 하느님!” 눈물 어린 그 눈빛의 뜻을 알아차리고 그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 의자에 도로 주저앉았다. 마거릿은 아이를 들어 올려 그녀의 품에 안겨 주었다.\n\n“그분은 이 아이를 사랑하셨어요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그래요.” 여인이 고개를 흔들며 말했다. “그이는 우릴 다 사랑했다우. 우리한테도 한때는 우릴 사랑해 주는 사람이 있었지. 오래전 일이지만, 살아서 우리 곁에 있을 적엔 정말이지 우릴 사랑했다우, 정말로. 우리 중에 이 갓난것을 제일 사랑했는지도 모르지. 그래도 그이는 날 사랑했고 나도 그일 사랑했수. 5분 전에는 욕을 하고 있었지만서두. 그이가 정말 죽었수?” 그녀가 일어서려 애쓰며 말했다. “그저 앓아누워 죽게 됐을 뿐이라면 아직 살려 낼 수 있을지도 모르잖수. 나도 앓는 몸이라우—오래도록 앓아 왔다우.”\n\n“하지만 돌아가셨어요—물에 빠지셨어요!”\n\n“물에 빠져 죽었다가도 되살아나는 사람이 있잖수. 내가 무슨 정신으로 가만히 앉아 있었담, 서둘러 나서야 할 판에? 자, 뚝 그쳐라, 아가—뚝 그쳐! 이걸 받아라, 아무거나 가지고 놀아라. 그렇지만 내 가슴이 미어지는데 울지는 말아 다오! 아아, 내 기운이 다 어디로 갔누? 아아, 존—여보!”\n\n마거릿은 그녀를 팔로 붙들어 쓰러지지 않게 했다. 그녀는 흔들의자에 앉아 여인을 무릎에 눕히고, 그 머리를 제 어깨에 기대게 했다. 겁에 질려 옹기종기 모여 있던 다른 아이들이 이 광경의 수수께끼를 알아차리기 시작했다. 그러나 머리가 둔하고 알아차림이 굼떠서 생각은 더디게 왔다. 사실을 짐작한 아이들이 어찌나 절망에 찬 울음을 터뜨렸던지 마거릿은 견딜 재간이 없었다. 조니의 울음소리가 그중 제일 컸는데, 정작 그 가엾은 것은 제가 왜 우는지도 몰랐다.\n\n어머니는 마거릿의 품에 안긴 채 부들부들 떨었다. 마거릿은 문간에서 나는 소리를 들었다.\n\n“저걸 열어라. 얼른 열어.” 그녀가 맏이에게 말했다. “빗장이 걸려 있단다. 소리는 내지 말고, 아주 가만가만히. 아, 아버지, 저분들을 아주 조용하고 조심스럽게 위층으로 올려 보내세요. 그러면 어쩌면 이 사람이 못 들을지도 몰라요. 기절했을 뿐이니까요.”\n\n“차라리 잘된 거요, 가엾은 사람.” 주검을 멘 이들의 뒤를 따라 들어온 여인이 말했다. “그렇지만 아가씨가 붙들고 있기엔 무리요. 가만있어 보슈, 내가 뛰어가서 베개를 가져올 테니 바닥에 살며시 내려놓읍시다.”\n\n이 고마운 이웃 여인은 마거릿에게 큰 도움이 되었다. 그 여인은 이 집에 낯선 사람인 것이 분명했고, 실은 이 동네에 새로 온 사람이었다. 그러나 어찌나 친절하고 사려 깊던지 마거릿은 이제 자기가 없어도 되겠다고 느꼈다. 그리고 딱하게 여기기는 하나 할 일 없이 구경만 하는 사람들로 가득 찬 집을 비우는 본을 보이는 편이 나으리라 생각했다.\n\n그녀는 니컬러스 히긴스를 찾아 둘러보았다. 그는 거기에 없었다. 그래서 바우처 부인을 바닥에 눕히는 일을 앞장서서 한 여인에게 말했다.\n\n“이 사람들에게 조용히 돌아가는 게 좋겠다고 넌지시 일러 주시겠어요? 이분이 깨어났을 때 곁에 아는 사람 한둘만 보이도록요. 아버지, 저 남자분들께 말씀하셔서 돌아가시게 해 주세요. 이렇게 사람들이 둘러싸고 있으면 이분이 숨을 못 쉬어요, 가엾게도.”\n\n마거릿은 바우처 부인 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 식초로 그 얼굴을 씻어 주고 있었는데, 몇 분도 지나지 않아 신선한 바람이 확 밀려드는 데 놀랐다. 둘러보니 아버지와 그 여인 사이에 미소가 오가는 것이 보였다.\n\n“무슨 일이에요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“다름이 아니라 여기 이 고마운 분이,” 아버지가 대답했다. “집을 비우게 할 아주 훌륭한 방책을 생각해 내셨구나.”\n\n“다들 돌아가라고 했지요. 가는 김에 아이를 하나씩 데려가라고, 이 아이들이 아비 없는 자식이고 저 어미는 과부라는 걸 잊지 말라고 했다우. 서로 더 하겠다고 나서더군요. 그러니 오늘은 아이들이 배불리 먹고 인정도 받을 게 틀림없수. 저 사람은 남편이 어떻게 죽었는지 아우?”\n\n“아니요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “한꺼번에 다 말씀드릴 수는 없었어요.”\n\n“검시가 있을 테니 알려 줘야 하우. 보슈! 정신이 돌아오고 있구려. 아가씨가 하겠수, 내가 하리까? 아니면 아가씨 아버님이 제일 나을까?”\n\n“아니요, 아주머니가, 아주머니가 해 주세요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 그녀가 완전히 깨어나기를 잠자코 기다렸다. 이윽고 이웃 여인이 바닥에 앉아 바우처 부인의 머리와 어깨를 제 무릎에 받쳐 안았다.\n\n“이보우.” 그녀가 말했다. “댁의 남편이 죽었수. 어떻게 죽었는지 짐작이 가우?”\n\n“물에 빠졌지요.” 바우처 부인이 힘없이 말하며, 이렇게 거칠게 슬픔을 헤집는 말에 비로소 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“물에 빠져 죽은 채로 발견됐수. 이 세상 아무것에도 희망을 잃은 채 집으로 오던 길이었지. 하느님이 사람보다야 모질지 않으리라 여겼겠지. 어쩌면 그만큼 모질지도 않고, 어쩌면 어미만큼 자애로우리라고, 어쩌면 그보다 더하리라고 여겼겠지. 나는 그이가 옳게 했다고도, 그르게 했다고도 말하지 않겠수. 내가 하는 말은 이것뿐이우. 나도 내 식구도 부디 그이 같은 쓰라린 마음일랑 겪지 않기를. 안 그러면 우리도 똑같은 짓을 할지 모르니까.”\n\n“그이가 이 아이들을 다 남겨 놓고 나만 혼자 두고 갔구려!” 과부가 신음하듯 말했다. 죽은 방식에 대해서는 마거릿이 예상한 것만큼 괴로워하지 않았다. 그러나 남편을 잃은 일을 무엇보다 저와 제 아이들에게 미치는 일로 느끼는 것은 그 무력한 성정에 걸맞은 일이었다.\n\n“혼자가 아닙니다.” 헤일 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. “누가 부인과 함께 계십니까? 누가 부인의 사정을 떠맡아 주시겠습니까?” 과부는 눈을 크게 뜨고, 그제야 있는 줄 알게 된 새 목소리의 주인을 바라보았다.\n\n“아비 없는 자에게 아버지가 되어 주겠다고 약속하신 분이 누구입니까?” 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n“하지만 나리, 저는 아이가 6명이나 되고 맏이가 8살도 안 되었답니다. 그분의 능력을 의심하려는 게 아니에요, 나리. 다만 그러려면 믿음이 아주 많이 있어야 하는걸요.” 그러고는 다시 울기 시작했다.\n\n“내일이면 말을 더 잘할 수 있을 거예요, 나리.” 이웃 여인이 말했다. “지금 제일 위로가 될 건 가슴에 아이를 안는 느낌일 텐데. 갓난것을 데려가 버려서 안됐구먼.”\n\n“제가 데려올게요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그리고 몇 분 뒤 조니를 안고 돌아왔다. 아이의 얼굴은 먹은 것으로 온통 범벅이었고, 두 손에는 조개껍데기며 수정 조각이며 석고 인형 머리 같은 보물이 가득했다. 그녀는 아이를 어머니의 품에 안겨 주었다.\n\n“자!” 여인이 말했다. “이제 가 보슈. 둘이서 함께 울고 함께 위로할 거요. 아이 말고는 아무도 못 해 줄 위로를 말이오. 나는 필요할 때까지 곁에 있겠수. 그리고 내일 오시면 오늘은 감당하지 못할 지혜로운 말씀을 실컷 나눌 수 있을 거유.”\n\n마거릿과 아버지가 천천히 거리를 올라가는데, 그녀는 히긴스의 닫힌 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n“들어가 볼까?” 아버지가 물었다. “나도 그 사람 생각을 하고 있었단다.”\n\n두 사람은 문을 두드렸다. 대답이 없어 문을 밀어 보았다. 빗장이 걸려 있었지만, 안에서 그가 움직이는 소리가 들리는 듯했다.\n\n“니컬러스!” 마거릿이 불렀다. 대답이 없었다. 안에서 책 같은 것이 우연히 떨어지는 소리가 나지 않았더라면, 두 사람은 집이 비었다고 여기고 돌아갔을 것이다.\n\n“니컬러스!” 마거릿이 다시 불렀다. “저희예요. 좀 들어가게 해 주시겠어요?”\n\n“안 되오.” 그가 말했다. “문에 빗장을 걸었을 때, 말을 쓰지 않고도 할 수 있는 한 분명히 말한 셈이오. 오늘은 나를 좀 내버려 두슈.”\n\n헤일 씨는 들어가고 싶다는 뜻을 더 밀어붙이려 했지만, 마거릿이 그의 입술에 손가락을 얹었다.\n\n“그럴 만도 해요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저도 혼자 있고 싶은걸요. 이런 하루를 보낸 뒤엔 마음에 도움이 될 일이 그것밖에 없는 것 같아요.”\n\n# 제37장\n\n“삽! 갈퀴! 괭이! 곡괭이나 낫자루! 거두는 낫이든 베어 넘기는 큰낫이든, 도리깨든 무엇이든 좋다— 여기 그 필요한 연장을 부릴 채비된 손이 있으니, 노동의 거친 학교에서 모진 수업으로 익힐 만큼 익힌 손이다.” — 후드.\n\n이튿날 그들이 과부가 된 바우처 부인을 찾아갔을 때 히긴스의 문은 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 이번에는 참견하기 좋아하는 이웃에게서 그가 정말로 집을 비웠다는 말을 들었다. 그래도 그는 그날의 볼일이 무엇이든 그것에 나서기 전에 바우처 부인을 들여다보고 갔다고 했다. 바우처 부인을 찾아간 것은 그리 흡족한 방문이 못 되었다. 그녀는 가엾은 남편의 자살로 자기가 몹쓸 꼴을 당했다고 여기고 있었는데, 그 생각에는 반박하기가 몹시 어려울 만큼의 진실의 씨앗이 들어 있었다. 그렇더라도 그녀의 생각이 온통 자기 자신과 자기 처지에만 쏠려 있는 것을 보는 일은 마음 편치 않았다. 그 이기심은 아이들과의 관계에까지 뻗어 있어서, 짐승 같은 애정을 한창 쏟는 와중에도 그녀는 아이들을 짐스러운 것으로 여겼다. 마거릿은 아이들 한둘과 낯을 익히려 애썼고, 그동안 그녀의 아버지는 그저 속수무책의 푸념에 지나지 않는 과부의 생각을 좀 더 높은 물길로 끌어올리려 애썼다. 마거릿이 보기에 아이들은 과부보다 더 진실하고 소박한 상주였다. 아빠는 그들에게 다정한 아빠였다. 아이들은 저마다 조급하게 더듬거리는 말투로, 잃어버린 아버지가 베풀어 준 어떤 다정함이며 어떤 너그러움을 이야기할 수 있었다.\n\n“위층에 있는 저게 정말 아빠유? 아빠처럼 보이질 않는데. 난 저게 무섭구먼. 아빠가 무서웠던 적은 한 번도 없었는데.”\n\n어머니가 이기적으로 동정을 갈구한 나머지 아이들을 위층으로 데려가 죽어 일그러진 아버지를 보게 했다는 것을 듣고 마거릿은 가슴이 미어졌다. 그것은 자연스러운 슬픔의 깊이에 공포의 조야함을 뒤섞는 짓이었다. 그녀는 아이들의 생각을 다른 쪽으로 돌리려 애썼다. 어머니를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지로, 그리고—이 편이 더 효험 있는 말투였는데—아버지라면 무엇을 하기를 바랐을지로. 마거릿은 그런 노력에서 헤일 씨보다 성공적이었다. 아이들은 자기가 할 자그마한 도리가 바로 곁에 놓여 있음을 보고, 저마다 그녀가 일러 준 대로 어질러진 방을 치우는 일에 무엇이든 해 보려 들기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그 게으른 병자 앞에 너무 높은 기준과 너무 추상적인 견해를 내놓았다. 그녀는 자신의 무딘 마음을 일깨워 남편이 그 마지막 무서운 걸음을 내딛기 전에 어떤 비참함을 겪었을지 생생히 그려 보지 못했다. 그녀는 오직 그 일이 자기에게 미친 영향으로만 그것을 바라볼 수 있었다. 엎어진 남편을 물이 덮어 삼키는 것을 특별히 막아 주지 않으신 하느님의 오래 참으시는 자비를 그녀는 헤아리지 못했다. 그리고 남편이 그런 암담한 절망에 빠진 것을 속으로는 나무라고 그 마지막 무모한 짓에 아무 변명거리도 없다고 하면서도, 어떻게든 그를 그런 절망으로 몰아넣었을 법한 이들 모두를 헐뜯는 데는 지독히도 끈질겼다. 공장주들—그중에서도 특히, 바우처의 습격을 받은 공장의 주인이면서 폭동 혐의로 그를 잡아들일 영장이 발부된 뒤에 그것을 거두게 한 손턴 씨—그리고 그 가엾은 여인에게는 히긴스가 곧 대표자였던 노동조합, 그리고 그토록 수도 많고 배고프고 시끄러운 아이들, 이 모두가 하나의 커다란 사적인 원수의 군대를 이루었고, 그녀가 지금 속수무책의 과부가 된 것은 다 그들의 잘못이었다.\n\n마거릿은 이 터무니없는 소리를 낙심할 만큼 실컷 들었다. 그리고 그 집을 나섰을 때 아버지를 도무지 위로할 수가 없다는 것을 알았다.\n\n“도시 생활 탓이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “주위의 모든 것이 서두르고 북적이고 빨라서 신경이 곤두서는 거예요. 이 답답한 집에 갇혀 지내는 것만 해도 우울과 근심을 불러오기에 충분하고요. 시골에서는 사람들이 훨씬 더 바깥에서 지내잖아요. 아이들도 그렇고, 겨울에도 그렇고요.”\n\n“하지만 사람들은 도시에 살아야 하지 않느냐. 그리고 시골에서는 마음이 어찌나 고여 버리는지 거의 숙명론자가 되어 버리는 이들도 있단다.”\n\n“네, 그건 인정해요. 저마다의 삶의 방식이 저마다의 시련과 저마다의 유혹을 낳는 거겠지요. 도시에 사는 사람은 참는 것이 어려울 테고, 시골에서 자란 사람은 부지런히 움직이며 익숙지 않은 위급한 일에 맞서는 것이 어려울 테고요. 둘 다 어떤 종류의 미래든 실감하기가 어려울 거예요. 한쪽은 현재가 너무 생생하고 급하고 바싹 다가와 있기 때문이고, 다른 쪽은 그 삶이 그저 짐승처럼 살아 있다는 느낌에 젖어 들도록 부추기는 탓에 계획하고 자신을 억누르고 앞을 내다보아 얻어 낼 수 있는 어떤 짜릿한 기쁨도 알지 못하고, 따라서 마음에 두지도 않기 때문이지요.”\n\n“그러니까 몰두하지 않을 수 없는 처지나 현재에 대한 우둔한 만족이나 같은 결과를 낳는 셈이구나. 하지만 저 가엾은 바우처 부인! 우리가 저 사람을 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이 어찌 이리 적은지.”\n\n“그래도 소용없어 보인다고 해서 애쓰기를 그만두고 저분을 내버려 둘 수는 없어요. 아, 아버지! 살아가기에 참 모진 세상이에요!”\n\n“그렇구나, 얘야. 적어도 지금 우리는 그렇게 느끼지. 하지만 우리는 슬픔 한가운데서도 아주 행복했단다. 프레더릭이 다녀간 것이 얼마나 큰 기쁨이었느냐!”\n\n“네, 정말 그랬어요.” 마거릿이 환하게 말했다. “그렇게 매혹적이고, 몰래 훔쳐 낸, 금지된 일이었어요.” 그러나 그녀는 문득 말을 멈추었다. 그녀는 자신의 비겁함으로 프레더릭이 다녀간 기억을 스스로 망쳐 버렸다. 남에게서 그녀가 가장 경멸하는 결점이 바로 용기의 부족이었다. 거짓으로 이끄는 마음의 비루함이었다. 그런데 그녀 자신이 그 죄를 저지른 것이다. 그러자 손턴 씨가 그녀의 거짓을 알고 있다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 다른 누구에게 들켰더라도 이만큼이나 마음이 쓰였을까 싶었다. 그녀는 상상 속에서 스스로를 시험해 보았다. 쇼 이모와 이디스에게, 아버지에게, 레녹스 대위와 레녹스 씨에게, 프레더릭에게. 마지막 사람이, 그것도 자기를 위해 한 일인데도, 그녀가 한 짓을 안다고 생각하면 가장 괴로웠다. 오누이는 서로에 대한 정과 사랑이 처음으로 활짝 피어난 참이었으니까. 그러나 프레더릭의 평가가 떨어지는 것조차, 손턴 씨를 다시 만난다고 생각할 때 느끼는 부끄러움, 몸이 움츠러드는 그 부끄러움에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그러면서도 그녀는 그를 보고 싶었다. 얼른 겪어 치우고 싶었다. 그의 눈에 자기가 어디쯤 서 있는지 알고 싶었다. 두 사람이 알게 된 초기에 자기가 얼마나 도도하게 장사에 대한 반감을 내비쳤던가를 떠올리자 뺨이 화끈거렸다. 장사란 한쪽에서는 못한 물건을 좋은 물건으로 속여 넘기는 기만으로, 다른 쪽에서는 가지지도 않은 재산과 자력을 가진 척하는 기만으로 너무 자주 이어진다는 이유에서였다. 그녀는 손턴 씨의 차분한 경멸의 표정을 기억했다. 그는 몇 마디 말로, 상업이라는 커다란 얼개 안에서 명예롭지 못한 온갖 행위는 길게 보면 반드시 해가 되고 만다는 것을, 그리고 그런 행위를 그저 성공이라는 초라한 잣대로만 재어 보아도 장사에서의 온갖 기만은 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로 지혜가 아니라 어리석음이라는 것을 그녀에게 알려 주었다. 그녀는 기억했다. 그때 아직 시험받지 않은 자신의 진실함에 힘입어 당당하던 그녀가, 가장 싼 데서 사서 가장 비싼 데 파는 것은 진실이라는 관념과 그토록 긴밀히 이어진 투명한 정의가 얼마쯤 모자란다는 뜻이 아니겠느냐고 그에게 물었던 것을. 그리고 그녀는 기사도적이라는 말을 썼고, 아버지가 그것을 더 높은 말인 그리스도교적이라는 말로 바로잡아 주었으며, 그리하여 그 논쟁을 자기 쪽으로 끌어갔고, 그동안 그녀는 얼마쯤 경멸을 느끼며 곁에 잠자코 앉아 있었다는 것을.\n\n이제 그녀에게 경멸 따위는 없었다! 기사도 운운하는 말도 없었다! 이제부터 그녀는 그의 눈앞에서 굴욕스럽고 부끄러운 사람으로 있어야 했다. 그런데 언제 그를 보게 될까? 현관 종이 울릴 때마다 그녀의 가슴은 겁먹은 듯 튀어 올랐다. 그러다 도로 잠잠해지면 그 실망마다 이상하게 서글프고 속이 메스꺼웠다. 아버지가 그를 보기를 기다리고 있으며 그가 오지 않는 것을 이상히 여기고 있는 것은 아주 분명했다. 사실인즉 지난밤 두 사람의 대화에는 그때 미처 넓혀 나갈 겨를이 없었던 대목들이 있었다. 그래서 될 수 있으면 그다음 날 저녁에, 그것이 안 되면 적어도 손턴 씨가 시간을 낼 수 있는 가장 이른 저녁에 만나 더 이야기하기로 되어 있었다. 헤일 씨는 헤어진 뒤로 줄곧 이 만남을 고대해 왔다. 그는 아내의 병이 더 위중해지기 시작하면서 그만두었던 제자들 수업을 아직 다시 시작하지 않았으므로 여느 때보다 할 일이 적었다. 게다가 요 하루 이틀 사이의 큰 사건, 곧 바우처의 자살이 그를 어느 때보다 더 간절히 자기 사색으로 밀어 넣었다. 그는 저녁 내내 안절부절못했다. 그는 자꾸만 말했다. “나는 손턴 씨를 보게 되리라 아주 믿고 있었는데. 어젯밤 책을 가져온 심부름꾼이 쪽지도 함께 받아 놓고는 전하는 것을 잊어버린 게 아닌가 싶구나. 오늘 무슨 전갈이 와 있지 않겠니?”\n\n“가서 여쭤 보고 올게요, 아버지.” 이 말들을 한두 번 이리저리 바꿔 되풀이한 끝에 마거릿이 말했다. “잠깐, 종이 울려요!” 그녀는 곧바로 앉아 하던 바느질 위로 열심히 고개를 숙였다. 계단에서 발소리가 들렸으나 하나뿐이었고, 그녀는 그것이 딕슨의 발소리임을 알았다. 그녀는 고개를 들고 한숨을 쉬었고, 자기가 기쁘다고 믿었다.\n\n“그 히긴스라는 사람이에요, 나리. 나리를 뵙자는데, 아니면 헤일 아가씨를 뵙자는 건지. 아니면 헤일 아가씨를 먼저 뵙고 그다음에 나리를 뵙자는 건지도 모르겠어요. 사람이 어째 이상하게 굴어서요.”\n\n“이리로 올라오시라고 하게, 딕슨. 그러면 우리 둘을 다 볼 수 있으니 마음에 드는 쪽을 골라 이야기하면 되지 않겠나.”\n\n“아! 그러시죠, 나리. 저야 그 사람이 무슨 말을 하든 듣고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없습니다요. 다만 그 신발을 보셨더라면 부엌이 더 어울리는 자리라고 하셨을 겁니다요.”\n\n“닦으면 되겠지.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. 그래서 딕슨은 그에게 위층으로 올라가라고 이르러 홱 나가 버렸다. 그러나 그가 머뭇거리는 낯빛으로 자기 발을 내려다보더니 맨 아래 계단에 앉아 그 말썽 많은 신발을 벗고는 말없이 위층으로 올라가는 것을 보고 그녀의 마음도 조금은 풀렸다.\n\n“안녕하십쇼, 나리!” 방으로 들어서면서 그가 머리를 매만져 내리며 말했다. “아가씨가 (마거릿을 보며) 양해해 주신다면 양말 바람으로 있겠소. 하루 종일 돌아다녔는데 거리가 도무지 깨끗하질 않아서요.”\n\n마거릿은 그의 태도가 달라진 것이 고단함 탓인가 보다 생각했다. 그는 여느 때와 달리 조용하고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그리고 하려고 온 말을 꺼내기가 분명 어려운 눈치였다.\n\n수줍음이나 머뭇거림, 침착함을 잃은 기색이라면 무엇에나 늘 곧바로 마음을 써 주는 헤일 씨가 그를 거들고 나섰다.\n\n“곧 차가 올라올 걸세. 그러면 우리와 한 잔 하게, 히긴스 씨. 이렇게 축축하고 나른한 날에 밖에 오래 있었다면 분명 고단할 테지. 마거릿, 얘야, 차를 좀 서둘러 줄 수 있겠니?”\n\n마거릿이 차를 서두를 수 있는 길은 준비를 직접 떠맡는 것뿐이었고, 그러면 딕슨의 심기를 건드리게 되었다. 딕슨은 돌아가신 안주인에 대한 슬픔에서 빠져나오면서 무척 예민하고 짜증스러운 상태가 되어 있었다. 그러나 마사도 마거릿과 마주치는 모든 이가 그러하듯—길게 보면 딕슨 자신까지도—그녀의 바람이라면 무엇이든 앞장서 이루어 주는 것을 기쁨이자 영광으로 여겼다. 그리고 마사의 선선함과 마거릿의 상냥한 참을성은 이내 딕슨이 스스로를 부끄러워하게 만들었다.\n\n“밀턴에 온 뒤로 어째서 나리와 아가씨가 늘 아랫것들을 위층으로 불러올리셔야 하는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다요. 헬스톤에서는 그런 사람들을 부엌보다 높은 데로 들인 적이 없었어요. 게다가 전에는 한둘한테 부엌에 들어온 것만도 영광인 줄 알라고 일러 준 적도 있는걸요.”\n\n히긴스는 둘보다 하나에게 속을 털어놓는 편이 더 쉽다는 것을 알았다. 마거릿이 방을 나가자 그는 문께로 가서 문이 닫혔는지 확인했다. 그러고는 돌아와 헤일 씨 가까이에 섰다.\n\n“나리.” 그가 말했다. “오늘 내가 무얼 찾아 쏘다녔는지는 쉽게 짐작 못 하실 거요. 어제 내 말투를 기억하신다면 더더욱 그렇겠지. 나는 일자리를 찾아다녔소. 그랬소.” 그가 말했다. “나는 속으로 다짐했소. 누가 무슨 소리를 하든 입은 공손히 놀리자고. 급하게 말을 뱉느니 차라리 혓바닥을 이로 물어 버리자고. 그 사람을 봐서 말이오. 무슨 말인지 아시겠소.” 그러면서 알 수 없는 방향으로 엄지를 뒤로 홱 젖혔다.\n\n“아니, 모르겠네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. 상대가 무언가 수긍하는 대답을 기다리는 눈치였으나, “그 사람”이 누구인지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없었던 것이다.\n\n“저기 누워 있는 그 친구 말이오.” 그가 또 한 번 엄지를 젖히며 말했다. “가서 제 몸을 물에 빠뜨린 사람 말이오, 가엾은 친구! 나는 그 사람한테 가만히 누워 물이 제 몸을 덮어 죽을 때까지 견딜 배짱이 있을 줄은 몰랐소. 바우처 말이오, 아시잖소.”\n\n“그래, 이제 알겠네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “하던 말로 돌아가세. 급하게 말하지 않겠다고 했지—”\n\n“그 사람을 봐서 말이오. 아니, 그 사람을 봐서는 아니지. 그가 어디에 있든 무엇이 되었든 이제 다시는 굶주림도 추위도 모를 테니까. 그 안사람과 그 어린것들을 봐서요.”\n\n“하느님이 자네에게 복 주시기를!” 헤일 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. 그러고는 마음을 가라앉히고 숨 가쁘게 말했다. “무슨 뜻인가? 다 털어놓게.”\n\n“벌써 말씀드렸잖소.” 헤일 씨가 흥분하는 것에 조금 놀라며 히긴스가 말했다. “내 앞가림하자고 일자리를 구걸하진 않소. 하지만 그 사람들이 내게 맡겨진 짐으로 남았으니 말이오. 나라면 바우처를 더 나은 끝으로 이끌 수도 있었을 거요. 그런데 내가 그를 길에서 벗어나게 했으니, 그러니 내가 그를 책임져야 하는 거요.”\n\n헤일 씨는 히긴스의 손을 붙잡고 아무 말 없이 힘껏 흔들었다. 히긴스는 어색하고 겸연쩍은 낯빛이었다.\n\n“아이고, 아이고, 나리! 우리네 가운데 사람이라고 부를 만한 사람이라면 누구든 똑같이 했을 거요. 아니, 더 잘했을 거요. 정말이지 나는 일감을 한 손도 못 얻었고, 일감 구경조차 못 했으니 말이오. 햄퍼한테 그렇게 말했는데도 그렇소. 그 서약이야 접어 두더라도—나는 그건 서명 못 하오, 아니, 못 하겠소, 이 일을 위해서라도—내가 될 만한 일꾼만 한 일꾼은 그 공장에 다시 없을 거라고 했는데도, 그는 나를 안 쓰겠다는 거요. 다른 사람들도 다 마찬가지고. 나는 가엾고 시커먼 못난 양이오. 내가 무슨 수를 써도 어린것들은 굶어야 할 판이오. 목사님, 나를 좀 도와주시지 않겠소?”\n\n“자네를 돕는다고! 어떻게? 무엇이든 하겠네만—내가 무얼 할 수 있겠나?”\n\n“저기 아가씨가”—마거릿이 방에 다시 들어와 말없이 서서 듣고 있었다—“남쪽 이야기며 거기 사는 방식을 곧잘 대단하게 말했잖소. 그게 얼마나 먼지는 모르겠소만, 저것들을 거기로 데려갈 수만 있다면 하고 생각해 봤소. 먹을 것이 싸고 품삯이 좋고, 부자든 가난뱅이든 공장주든 일꾼이든 다들 다정하다는 그곳으로 말이오. 나리가 일자리를 얻게 도와주실 수 있을지도 모르잖소. 나는 아직 45살이 안 됐고 힘도 넉넉히 남았소, 나리.”\n\n“하지만 자네가 할 수 있는 일이 어떤 것이겠나?”\n\n“글쎄, 삽질쯤은 할 수 있을 것 같소만——”\n\n“그런데 그것으로는,” 마거릿이 앞으로 나서며 말했다. “히긴스, 세상에서 가장 좋은 마음으로 무슨 일을 하시든, 아마 주급으로 9실링쯤 받을 거예요. 잘해야 10실링이겠지요. 먹을 것은 여기와 별로 다르지 않고요. 텃밭을 조금 가질 수 있다는 것 말고는——”\n\n“그건 어린것들이 할 수 있소.” 그가 말했다. “나는 어차피 밀턴이 지긋지긋하고, 밀턴도 내가 지긋지긋하고.”\n\n“그래도 남쪽으로 가시면 안 돼요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “견디지 못하실 거예요. 어떤 날씨에도 바깥에 나가 있어야 해요. 류머티즘으로 죽고 말 거예요. 그 나이에 하는 몸일만으로도 몸이 망가질 거고요. 먹는 것도 익숙해진 것과는 아주 달라요.”\n\n“나는 먹는 것 가지고 까다롭게 굴지 않소.” 그가 기분이 상한 듯 말했다.\n\n“하지만 일을 하고 있으면 하루 한 번은 푸줏간 고기를 먹으리라 셈하셨잖아요. 그 10실링에서 그 값을 치르고, 그러고도 저 가엾은 아이들을 건사해 보세요. 저는 그것을 다 낱낱이 말씀드릴 빚이 있어요. 이런 생각을 하게 만든 것이 바로 제가 말하던 방식이니까요. 그 삶의 따분함을 견디지 못하실 거예요. 그게 어떤 것인지 모르시잖아요. 그것은 녹처럼 사람을 갉아먹어요. 거기서 평생 살아온 이들은 고인 물에 젖어 지내는 데 익숙해요. 그들은 김이 오르는 들판의 그 커다란 적막 속에서 날마다 일할 뿐이에요. 말 한마디 나누는 법도, 가엾게 구부러져 숙인 고개를 드는 법도 없이요. 고된 삽질은 그들의 머리에서 생기를 앗아 가고, 늘 똑같은 노역은 상상력을 죽여 버려요. 일이 끝난 뒤에 아무리 시시하고 터무니없는 것이라도 생각이며 궁리를 나누자고 모일 마음조차 없어요. 그들은 짐승처럼 지쳐서 집으로 돌아가요, 가엾은 사람들! 먹을 것과 쉴 것 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳하지 않고요. 도시에서라면 숨 쉬는 공기만큼이나 넉넉히 얻는 그 어울림으로 그들을 일으켜 세우지는 못하실 거예요. 그것이 좋은 것인지 나쁜 것인지는 저도 모르겠어요. 하지만 이것만은 알아요. 다른 누구도 아닌 당신 같은 분이야말로 그런 일꾼들 틈에서 살아갈 수 있는 사람이 아니라는 것을요. 그들에게 평온인 것이 당신에게는 끝없는 속앓이가 될 거예요. 그러니 부디 더는 생각하지 마세요, 니컬러스. 게다가 어머니와 아이들을 모두 그리로 데려갈 여비도 결코 못 대실 테고요. 그건 그나마 다행한 일이네요.”\n\n“그것도 셈해 봤소. 집 한 채면 우리 모두 지낼 수 있고, 다른 집 세간을 팔면 꽤 보탬이 될 거요. 그리고 거기 사람들도 먹여 살릴 식구가 있을 테지, 어쩌면 어린것이 여섯 일곱은 될 테고. 하느님이 그들을 도우시기를!” 그는 마거릿이 한 온갖 말보다 자기가 스스로 늘어놓은 사실들에 더 설득되어 말했고, 하루의 고단함과 근심으로 지친 머릿속에 이제 막 생겨났던 그 생각을 문득 접어 버렸다. “하느님이 그들을 도우시기를! 북쪽이나 남쪽이나 저마다 제 고생을 안고 있구먼. 거기서는 일감이 확실하고 꾸준하다면 품삯이 굶어 죽을 만큼밖에 안 되고, 여기서는 한 철에 돈이 무더기로 들어오다가 다음 철에는 한 푼도 안 들어오고. 정말이지 세상은 나나 다른 어떤 사람이나 알아먹을 수 없을 만큼 뒤죽박죽이오. 손을 봐야 하는데, 저 사람들 말대로 우리 눈에 보이는 것 말고는 아무것도 없다면 대체 누가 손을 본단 말이오?”\n\n헤일 씨는 빵에 버터를 바르느라 바빴다. 마거릿은 그것이 다행이었다. 히긴스는 혼자 내버려 두는 편이 낫다는 것을, 아버지가 히긴스의 생각에 대해 아무리 부드럽게 말을 꺼내더라도 히긴스는 자기에게 논쟁이 걸려 왔다고 여기고 제 자리를 지켜야 한다고 생각하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그녀와 아버지는 히긴스가 먹는지 마는지도 거의 모르는 채 아주 든든하게 한 끼를 다 먹을 때까지 심상한 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러고 나서 그는 의자를 식탁에서 밀어내고 두 사람이 하는 이야기에 관심을 가져 보려 애썼다. 그러나 소용없었다. 그는 다시 몽롱한 우울 속으로 가라앉았다. 문득 마거릿이 말했다. 한동안 생각하고 있었으나 말이 목에 걸려 나오지 않던 것이었다. “히긴스, 일자리를 구하러 말버러 공장에 가 보셨어요?”\n\n“손턴네 말이오?” 그가 물었다. “그럼, 손턴네도 가 봤소.”\n\n“그래서 그분이 뭐라고 하셨어요?”\n\n“나 같은 놈이 공장주 얼굴을 볼 성싶소. 감독이 나더러 꺼져 버리라며 욕지거리를 퍼붓더군.”\n\n“자네가 손턴 씨를 만났더라면 좋았을걸.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “그가 일자리를 주지는 않았을지 몰라도 그런 말을 쓰지는 않았을 걸세.”\n\n“그런 말이야 이골이 났소. 나한테는 아무렇지도 않소. 나도 성이 나면 무른 사람은 아니고. 내가 마음에 걸린 건 거기서도 나를 원치 않는다는 사실, 다른 어느 데서도 마찬가지라는 사실이었소.”\n\n“하지만 저도 손턴 씨를 만나 보셨으면 좋았겠어요.” 마거릿이 되풀이했다. “한 번 더 가 주시겠어요? 큰 부탁이라는 건 알아요. 그래도 내일 가서 그분에게 청해 보시겠어요? 그래 주시면 정말 기쁘겠어요.”\n\n“소용없을 것 같구나.” 헤일 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “내가 그에게 말하는 편이 낫겠다.” 마거릿은 여전히 히긴스의 대답을 기다리며 그를 바라보았다. 진지하고 부드러운 그 눈길은 뿌리치기 어려웠다. 그가 크게 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n“그건 내 자존심에 여간 힘든 일이 아니오. 나 자신을 위해서라면 굶는 걸 한참 더 견디고 말지. 그 사람한테 은혜를 청하느니 차라리 한 대 갈기겠소. 차라리 내가 매를 맞고 말겠소. 하지만 실례되는 말이지만 아가씨는 흔한 계집아이가 아니고, 아가씨가 하는 짓도 흔한 짓이 아니오. 나는 얼굴을 찡그리면서라도 내일 그 일에 나서 보겠소. 그 사람이 들어줄 거라고는 생각지 마시오. 그 사람은 굽히느니 화형대에서 타 죽을 위인이오. 아가씨를 봐서 하는 거요, 헤일 아가씨. 그리고 내 평생 여자한테 진 건 이번이 처음이오. 내 안사람도 베스도 그런 소리는 못 했지.”\n\n“그러니 더욱 고마워요.” 마거릿이 미소 지으며 말했다. “그래도 저는 그 말을 믿지 않아요. 히긴스도 여느 남자들만큼은 아내와 딸에게 져 주셨을 거예요.”\n\n“그리고 손턴 씨 일이라면,” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “내가 그에게 보내는 쪽지를 하나 써 주겠네. 감히 말하건대 그것이면 적어도 자네 말을 들어 주기는 할 걸세.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 나리. 하지만 나는 내 발로 서는 편이 낫겠소. 싸움의 속사정을 모르는 사람을 통해 남의 비위를 맞춰 얻어 내는 건 내 속이 받질 않소. 공장주와 일꾼 사이에 끼어드는 건 남편과 아내 사이에 끼어드는 것보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지 않소. 조금이라도 좋게 하려면 여간한 지혜가 드는 게 아니오. 나는 정문 수위실 앞에 지키고 서 있겠소. 아침 6시부터 그와 말을 나눌 때까지 거기 서 있겠소. 하지만 거리 청소 일이 거지들 차지만 아니었어도 차라리 그 일을 하고 말았을 거요. 아가씨, 기대는 마시오. 부싯돌에서 젖을 짜내는 편이 더 가망 있을 거요. 아주 편안한 밤 되시고, 여러모로 고맙소.”\n\n“신발은 부엌 난롯가에 있어요. 제가 말리려고 거기 가져다 놓았거든요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n그는 돌아서서 그녀를 물끄러미 바라보았다. 그러고는 여윈 손으로 눈가를 훔치고 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n“저 사람은 어찌 저리 자존심이 센지!” 손턴 씨에게 나서 주겠다는 자기 제안을 히긴스가 물리친 태도에 조금 언짢아진 아버지가 말했다.\n\n“그러네요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “하지만 자존심까지 다 합쳐서, 저 사람 안에는 얼마나 훌륭한 사람이 될 재목이 들어 있는지요.”\n\n“손턴 씨의 성품 가운데 자기와 닮은 부분을 저 사람이 저렇게 대놓고 존경하는 걸 보면 재미있구나.”\n\n“이 북쪽 사람들에게는 다들 화강암 같은 데가 있어요, 아버지. 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“가엾은 바우처에게는 없었던 것 같구나. 그 아내에게도 없고.”\n\n“말투로 미루어 보면 아일랜드 피가 섞였을 것 같아요. 저분이 내일 얼마나 잘될지 궁금해요. 저분과 손턴 씨가 사람 대 사람으로 툭 터놓고 이야기한다면, 히긴스가 손턴 씨는 공장주라는 것을 잊고 우리에게 말하듯 그분에게 말한다면, 그리고 손턴 씨가 공장주의 귀가 아니라 사람의 마음으로 저분의 말을 들어 줄 만큼 참을성이 있다면—”\n\n“드디어 네가 손턴 씨를 공정하게 보게 되었구나, 마거릿.” 아버지가 그녀의 귀를 꼬집으며 말했다.\n\n마거릿은 가슴이 이상하게 메어 대답할 수가 없었다. “아,” 그녀는 생각했다. “내가 남자였더라면. 그러면 그분에게 찾아가서 못마땅하다는 말을 하게 만들고, 내가 그 말을 들어 마땅한 줄 안다고 정직하게 말할 수 있을 텐데. 그분의 진가를 이제 막 느끼기 시작한 참에 벗으로서 그분을 잃는다는 건 참 모진 일이야. 그분은 사랑하는 어머니에게 얼마나 다정했던가! 어머니를 봐서라도 그분이 와 주었으면. 그러면 적어도 내가 그분의 눈에 얼마나 낮아졌는지는 알 수 있을 텐데.”\n\n# 제38장\n\n“그러자 그 처녀는 도도하게, 도도하게 몸을 일으켰네, 두 눈에는 눈물이 어렸어도. 그대들이 무슨 말을 하든, 무슨 생각을 하든, 내게서는 한마디도 듣지 못하리라!” — 스코틀랜드 발라드.\n\n마거릿이 거짓말을 했다는 것을 손턴 씨가 알고 있다는 사실만이 문제는 아니었다. 그녀는 오직 그 이유 하나로 자신이 그의 눈 밖에 났다고 여겼지만, 정작 문제는 그 거짓말이 그의 마음속에서 어떤 다른 연인과 또렷이 결부되어 있다는 데 있었다. 그는 그녀와 어떤 낯선 사내 사이에 오간 그 애틋하고 간절한 눈길을 잊을 수가 없었다. 노골적인 애무까지는 아니라 해도 허물없이 기대는 그 몸짓을. 그 생각이 끊임없이 그를 찔러 댔다. 어디를 가든 무엇을 하든 그것은 눈앞에 걸린 한 폭의 그림이었다. 게다가 (그는 그것을 떠올리며 이를 갈았다) 시각은 땅거미 지는 황혼이었고, 장소는 집에서 그토록 멀고 인적도 드문 곳이었다. 그의 더 고결한 자아는 처음에 이 모든 정황이 우연일 수도, 결백할 수도, 해명될 수도 있다고 말했다. 그러나 일단 그녀에게 사랑하고 사랑받을 권리가 있음을 인정하고 나면 — 그리고 그가 그 권리를 부정할 무슨 근거가 있단 말인가? 그녀가 그의 사랑을 내던질 때 그 말은 냉혹하리만치 분명하지 않았던가? — 그녀가 예상보다 긴 산책에, 예상보다 늦은 시각까지 이끌려 갔다는 것쯤은 얼마든지 있을 수 있는 일이었다. 그러나 그 거짓말! 무언가 잘못된 것이, 감추어야 할 것이 있음을 치명적으로 의식하고 있음을 드러낸, 그녀답지 않은 그 거짓말. 그는 그녀에게 그만한 공정함은 베풀었다. 물론 내내 그녀가 자기 존경을 받을 자격이 조금도 없는 여자라고 믿을 수 있었다면 차라리 마음이 놓였겠지만. 바로 이것이 그의 비참함을 이루었다. 그는 그녀를 뜨겁게 사랑했고, 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 그녀가 다른 어떤 여자보다 사랑스럽고 뛰어나다고 여겼다. 그러면서도 그녀가 어떤 다른 사내에게 그토록 매여 있고 그에 대한 애정에 그토록 끌려간 나머지 진실한 천성마저 저버렸다고 생각했다. 그녀를 더럽힌 그 거짓말이야말로 그녀가 다른 사내를 얼마나 눈멀게 사랑하는지에 대한 증거였다. 그 검은 머리에 호리호리하고 우아하고 잘생긴 사내를. 그에 비해 자신은 거칠고 굳세고 우락부락하게 생긴 사내였다. 그는 스스로를 채찍질해 사나운 질투의 고통 속으로 몰아넣었다. 그 눈길, 그 몸짓을 떠올렸다! 그런 다정한 눈길, 그런 애틋한 붙듦을 받을 수만 있다면 그는 목숨을 그녀의 발치에 내려놓았으리라! 그는 폭도의 광기로부터 그녀가 기계적으로 자기를 감싸 준 일을 소중히 여겼던 자신을 비웃었다. 이제 그는 그녀가 진정으로 사랑하는 사내와 함께 있을 때 얼마나 부드럽고 홀리는 얼굴이 되는지를 보아 버린 것이다. 그는 그녀의 모진 말들을 한마디 한마디 되새겼다. “그 군중 속 어느 누구를 위해서라도 그만큼은, 아니 훨씬 더 기꺼이 했을 것”이라던 말을. 그는 그들에게서 유혈을 막고자 하는 그녀의 바람을 폭도와 나누어 가졌을 뿐이었다. 그러나 이 사내, 이 숨겨진 연인은 누구와도 나누지 않았다. 눈길도, 말도, 맞잡은 손도, 거짓말도, 은폐도 모두 그 사내 혼자만의 것이었다.\n\n손턴 씨는 평생 지금처럼 신경이 곤두선 적이 없었음을 스스로 알고 있었다. 무엇이든 묻는 사람마다 말이라기보다 짖는 소리에 가까운 짧고 퉁명스러운 대답을 던지고 싶어졌다. 그리고 그런 자각이 그의 자존심을 상하게 했다. 그는 늘 자제력을 자랑으로 삼아 왔고, 기어이 자신을 다스릴 작정이었다. 그리하여 태도는 차분한 신중함으로 눌러 두었으나, 내용은 여느 때보다 오히려 더 딱딱하고 매서웠다. 집에서 그는 평소보다 더 말이 없었다. 저녁이면 내내 방 안을 오락가락 서성이며 보냈는데, 다른 사람이 그랬다면 어머니를 지독히 짜증 나게 했을 짓이었다. 그리고 그것은 그 사랑하는 아들에게조차 어머니의 인내심을 북돋우는 데 도움이 되지 않았다.\n\n“좀 멈출 수 없겠니 — 잠깐 앉을 수 없겠어? 그 끝없는 걷기, 걷기, 걷기를 그만둔다면 네게 할 말이 있다.”\n\n그는 즉시 벽에 붙은 의자에 앉았다.\n\n“벳시 이야기를 하고 싶구나. 그 애가 우리를 떠나야겠다는구나. 연인이 죽은 뒤로 마음이 너무 상해서 일에 마음을 붙일 수가 없다고 말이야.”\n\n“그러시지요. 요리사야 다른 사람도 구할 수 있을 테니까요.”\n\n“꼭 남자다운 소리로구나. 요리만이 문제가 아니라, 그 애가 이 집안 사정을 속속들이 안다는 게 문제야. 게다가 그 애가 네 친구 헤일 양에 대해 뭔가 이야기를 하더구나.”\n\n“헤일 양은 제 친구가 아닙니다. 헤일 씨가 제 친구지요.”\n\n“그런 말을 들으니 다행이다. 그 애가 네 친구였다면 벳시의 말이 널 언짢게 했을 테니 말이야.”\n\n“들어 보지요.” 그가 요 며칠 몸에 익혀 온 그 극도로 차분한 태도로 말했다.\n\n“벳시 말로는, 그 애 연인이 — 이름은 잊었구나, 그 애는 늘 ‘그이’라고만 부르니 —”\n\n“레너즈요.”\n\n“레너즈가 역에서 마지막으로 목격된 그날 밤 — 사실은 근무 중에 마지막으로 목격된 그날 밤에 — 헤일 양이 거기 있었고, 어떤 젊은 남자와 함께 거닐고 있었다는구나. 벳시는 그 남자가 무언가로 치거나 밀어서 레너즈를 죽였다고 믿고 있어.”\n\n“레너즈는 어떤 타격이나 밀침으로 죽은 것이 아닙니다.”\n\n“그걸 네가 어떻게 아느냐?”\n\n“제가 병원 외과의에게 똑똑히 물어보았으니까요. 그가 말하기를, 레너즈에게는 지나치게 술을 마시는 버릇 때문에 생긴 오래된 내장 질환이 있었다고 했습니다. 그리고 그가 취한 상태에서 급격히 나빠졌다는 사실이, 마지막 치명적인 발작이 과음 때문인지 낙상 때문인지 하는 문제를 결론지어 준다고 했습니다.”\n\n“낙상이라니! 무슨 낙상 말이냐?”\n\n“벳시가 말한 그 타격이나 밀침으로 생긴 낙상 말입니다.”\n\n“그러면 타격이나 밀침이 있기는 있었단 말이구나?”\n\n“그랬다고 봅니다.”\n\n“그럼 그건 누가 한 짓이냐?”\n\n“의사의 소견 때문에 검시가 없었으니, 저도 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.”\n\n“하지만 헤일 양이 그 자리에 있었지?”\n\n대답이 없었다.\n\n“게다가 젊은 남자와 함께?”\n\n여전히 대답이 없었다. 마침내 그가 말했다. “어머니, 검시는 없었다고 말씀드리지 않습니까. 아무 조사도 없었습니다. 사법적인 조사 말입니다.”\n\n“벳시 말로는 울머라는 사람이 — 그 애가 아는 사람인데, 크램프턴 저쪽 식료품 가게에 있다더구나 — 그 시각에 헤일 양이 역에서 젊은 남자와 함께 이리저리 거닐고 있었다고 맹세할 수 있다는구나.”\n\n“그것이 저희와 무슨 상관인지 모르겠습니다. 헤일 양은 자기 뜻대로 할 자유가 있으니까요.”\n\n“그런 말을 들으니 다행이구나.” 손턴 부인이 열을 올리며 말했다. “그야 우리에게는 거의 아무 의미도 없는 일이지. 그런 일이 있었으니 네게는 더더욱 아무것도 아니고! 하지만 나는 — 나는 헤일 부인에게 약속을 했다. 그 딸이 그릇된 길로 가는 것을 충고와 나무람 없이 그냥 두지는 않겠다고 말이야. 나는 반드시 그 아이에게 그런 행실에 대한 내 생각을 알려 줄 참이다.”\n\n“그날 저녁 그분이 한 일에서 저는 아무 해로움도 보지 못하겠습니다.” 손턴 씨가 일어나 어머니 곁으로 다가서며 말했다. 그는 벽난로 선반 옆에 서서 방 쪽으로부터 얼굴을 돌리고 있었다.\n\n“패니가 어두워진 뒤에 꽤나 외진 곳에서 젊은 남자와 거니는 모습을 남에게 보였다면 너는 좋게 여기지 않았을 게다. 제 어미가 아직 땅에 묻히지도 않은 때를 그런 산책에 골라잡을 수 있는 그 취향에 대해서는 아무 말도 않으마. 네 누이가 그런 짓을 하다가 식료품점 점원의 눈에 띄었다면 너는 좋았겠느냐?”\n\n“우선, 저 자신이 포목상 점원 노릇을 한 지 그리 여러 해 되지 않았으니, 식료품점 점원이 어떤 행동을 눈여겨보았다고 해서 그 행동의 성격이 제게 달라지지는 않습니다. 그리고 다음으로, 저는 헤일 양과 패니 사이에 아주 큰 차이가 있다고 봅니다. 한쪽은 자기 행실에 겉으로 드러나는 부적절함이 있더라도 그것을 넘어서게 만들 만한, 또 마땅히 그래야 할 무거운 이유를 지녔을 수 있다고 상상할 수 있습니다. 패니가 무슨 일에든 무거운 이유를 지녔던 적은 없지요. 그 애는 남들이 지켜 주어야 합니다. 헤일 양은 스스로 자신을 지키는 사람이라고 저는 믿습니다.”\n\n“참으로 네 누이에 대한 훌륭한 평이로구나! 정말이지 존, 헤일 양이 네 눈을 뜨게 하기에 충분한 짓을 했으리라고 여겼을 텐데 말이다. 그 아이는 널 좋아하는 척 대담하게 내보여 청혼을 이끌어 낸 게야. 틀림없이 바로 그 젊은 남자를 상대로 널 이용해 먹으려고 말이지. 이제 그 아이의 행실이 내게는 훤히 보인다. 너도 그 남자가 그 아이의 연인이라고 믿는 게지. 그것만은 인정하겠지.”\n\n그가 어머니 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 얼굴은 몹시 잿빛이고 험했다. “예, 어머니. 저도 그 사람이 그분의 연인이라고 믿습니다.” 그렇게 말하고 나서 그는 다시 몸을 돌렸다. 몸이 아픈 사람처럼 몸부림쳤다. 얼굴을 손에 기댔다. 그러고는 어머니가 입을 열기도 전에 홱 돌아섰다.\n\n“어머니. 그 사람이 누구든 그분의 연인인 것은 맞습니다. 그러나 그분에게는 도움과 여인의 조언이 필요할지도 모릅니다. 제가 모르는 어려움이나 유혹이 있을지도 모르지요. 저는 있다고 봅니다. 그것이 무엇인지는 알고 싶지 않습니다. 다만 어머니께서 늘 제게 좋은 — 예! 다정한 어머니셨으니, 그분에게 가셔서 그분의 신뢰를 얻고 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 일러 주십시오. 무언가 잘못되어 있다는 것을 저는 압니다. 그분에게는 끔찍한 고문일 것이 분명한 어떤 두려움이 있습니다.”\n\n“맙소사, 존!” 이제야 정말로 충격을 받은 어머니가 말했다. “그게 무슨 소리냐? 무슨 소리야? 넌 무엇을 알고 있는 게냐?”\n\n그는 어머니에게 대답하지 않았다.\n\n“존! 네가 말하지 않으면 내가 무슨 생각인들 안 하겠느냐. 방금 그 아이에게 불리한 말을 해 놓고 그대로 둘 권리는 네게 없다.”\n\n“그분에게 불리한 말이 아닙니다, 어머니! 저는 그분에게 불리한 말은 할 수가 없습니다.”\n\n“그렇다면! 더 말하지 않을 거면 방금 한 말도 할 권리가 없는 게다. 이런 어중간한 말들이 여자의 평판을 망치는 법이야.”\n\n“그분의 평판이라니요! 어머니, 감히 —” 그가 홱 돌아서서 이글거리는 눈으로 어머니의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그러고는 단호한 침착과 위엄으로 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “이보다 더는 말씀드리지 않겠습니다. 이것은 그저 있는 그대로의 진실일 뿐이고, 어머니께서도 저를 믿으시리라 확신합니다. 저는 헤일 양이 어떤 애착과 얽힌 곤경과 어려움에 처해 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있습니다. 그리고 그 애착 자체는, 제가 아는 헤일 양의 성품으로 보아 조금도 흠 없고 옳은 것입니다. 그 근거가 무엇인지는 말씀드리기를 거부합니다. 다만 누구든 그분에 대해, 지금 어질고 온화한 여인의 조언이 필요하다는 것 이상의 무거운 혐의를 넌지시 비추는 말을 하는 것은 결코 듣지 않겠습니다. 어머니께서는 헤일 부인에게 그런 여인이 되어 주겠다고 약속하셨습니다!”\n\n“아니다!” 손턴 부인이 말했다. “다행히도 나는 어짊과 온화함을 약속하지는 않았다. 그때도 헤일 양 같은 성품과 기질을 지닌 사람에게 그런 것을 베푸는 일은 내 힘 밖일지 모른다고 느꼈으니까. 내가 약속한 것은 내 딸에게 해 주었을 법한 충고와 조언이었다. 패니가 어스름에 젊은 남자와 쏘다녔다면 내가 그 애에게 했을 말을 그 아이에게도 하겠다. 나는 내가 아는 정황에 관해서만 말할 것이고, 네가 내게 털어놓지 않으려는 그 ‘충분한 근거’에 이리로도 저리로도 휘둘리지 않겠다. 그러면 나는 내 약속을 다한 것이고 내 의무를 한 것이다.”\n\n“그분은 결코 견디지 못할 겁니다.” 그가 격하게 말했다.\n\n“내가 죽은 제 어미의 이름으로 말한다면 견뎌야만 하겠지.”\n\n“그만!” 그가 몸을 빼며 말했다. “더는 그 이야기를 제게 하지 마십시오. 생각하는 것조차 견딜 수가 없습니다. 아예 아무 말도 듣지 못하느니 어떤 식으로든 어머니께서 말씀해 주시는 편이 낫겠지요. — 아, 그 사랑에 찬 눈길!” 그는 제 방에 들어가 빗장을 지르며 이를 악물고 말을 이었다. “그리고 그 저주스러운 거짓말! 내가 늘 그 사람이 살고 있다고 믿었던 그 빛으로부터 감추어야 할 어떤 무서운 수치가 뒤에 있음을 드러낸 그 거짓말! 아, 마거릿, 마거릿! 어머니, 어머니가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히셨는지! 아, 마거릿, 당신은 나를 사랑할 수 없었단 말입니까? 나는 그저 투박하고 무뚝뚝한 사내일 뿐이지만, 나를 위해 당신을 어떤 거짓말로도 이끌지는 않았을 텐데.”\n\n손턴 부인은 아들이 마거릿의 경솔함에 대해 너그러운 판단을 청하며 한 말을 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록 그 아가씨에게 더 모질어졌다. 그녀는 의무의 이행이라는 탈을 쓰고 “속에 있는 말을 다 해 주는” 생각에 사나운 쾌감을 느꼈다. 마거릿이 많은 사람들에게 뒤집어씌우는 힘을 지녔음을 익히 아는 그 “홀림”에 자신은 조금도 물들지 않았음을 보여 준다는 생각이 즐거웠다. 그녀는 제 희생자의 아름다움을 그리며 경멸스럽게 코웃음을 쳤다. 그 새까만 머리도, 맑고 매끄러운 살결도, 투명한 눈도, 손턴 부인이 밤을 반이나 새워 마음속으로 다듬어 놓은 정당하고 준엄한 질책의 한마디도 덜어 주지는 못하리라.\n\n“헤일 양은 안에 계신가?” 그녀는 그녀가 안에 있음을 알고 있었다. 창가에서 그 모습을 보았기 때문이다. 그리고 마사가 그 물음에 절반쯤 대답했을 때 이미 그녀의 발은 작은 현관 안에 들어와 있었다.\n\n마거릿은 혼자 앉아 이디스에게 편지를 쓰며 어머니의 마지막 날들에 대해 이런저런 자세한 이야기를 들려주던 참이었다. 마음을 누그러뜨리는 일이었으므로, 손턴 부인이 오셨다는 전갈을 받았을 때 그녀는 절로 흐른 눈물을 훔쳐 내야 했다.\n\n그녀의 맞이하는 태도가 어찌나 부드럽고 기품 있었던지 손님은 다소 기가 꺾였다. 그리고 들려줄 사람이 없을 때는 그토록 손쉽게 꾸며 놓았던 그 연설을 입 밖에 내기가 불가능해졌다. 마거릿의 낮고 풍부한 목소리는 여느 때보다 부드러웠고, 태도는 한결 상냥했다. 마음속으로 손턴 부인이 예의를 갖추어 찾아와 준 것을 무척 고맙게 여기고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 애를 써서 이야깃거리를 찾아냈다. 손턴 부인이 구해 준 하녀 마사를 칭찬했고, 손턴 양에게 이야기했던 그리스 소곡을 이디스에게 부탁해 두었다고 말했다. 손턴 부인은 완전히 당황했다. 그 날카로운 다마스쿠스 칼날은 장미 꽃잎들 사이에서 자리에 맞지 않고 쓸모도 없어 보였다. 그녀는 스스로를 채근해 제 의무에 이르게 하려고 애쓰느라 잠자코 있었다. 마침내 그녀는 온갖 정황에 어긋나는데도 마음에 스치도록 내버려 둔 어떤 의심으로 스스로를 찔러 그 일을 해내게 만들었다. 이 모든 상냥함이 손턴 씨의 마음을 달래려는 속셈으로 꾸민 것이며, 어찌 된 셈인지 그 다른 애착이 깨어지는 바람에 헤일 양이 물리쳤던 구혼자를 도로 불러들이는 편이 제 속셈에 맞게 되었으리라는 의심이었다. 가엾은 마거릿! 그 의심에는 어쩌면 이만한 진실은 담겨 있었는지도 모른다. 손턴 부인은 마거릿이 소중히 여기던, 그리고 잃었을까 두려워하던 존경의 주인공의 어머니라는 것. 그리고 그 생각이 자기도 모르게, 찾아옴으로써 친절을 베풀어 준 사람을 기쁘게 해 주고 싶다는 자연스러운 마음에 보태졌다는 것. 손턴 부인은 가려고 일어섰으나 아직 무언가 더 할 말이 있는 듯했다. 그녀는 목을 가다듬고 입을 열었다.\n\n“헤일 양, 나는 이행해야 할 의무가 있어요. 나는 가엾은 그대 어머니에게, 내 얕은 판단이 미치는 한, 그대가 어떤 식으로든 그릇되게, 혹은 (그녀는 여기서 말을 조금 눅였다) 무심코 행동하는 것을 나무람 없이 그냥 두지는 않겠다고 약속했습니다. 적어도 그대가 받아들이든 아니든 조언은 하겠다고 말이오.”\n\n마거릿은 죄인처럼 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그녀 앞에 서서 손턴 부인을 바라보며 눈을 크게 떴다. 그녀는 부인이 자신이 한 거짓말에 대해 이야기하러 온 줄로 알았다. 손턴 씨가 부인을 시켜, 법정에서 만인 앞에 그 말이 뒤집힐 위험에 스스로를 내던졌음을 일깨우려는 것이라고 생각한 것이다! 그리고 그가 차라리 몸소 찾아와 자신을 꾸짖고 뉘우침을 받아들여 다시 자기를 좋게 보아 주기를 택하지 않았다는 생각에 가슴이 내려앉기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀는 이 문제에 관해서라면 어떤 책망도 참을성 있게 온순히 받아들이지 않을 수 없을 만큼 겸허해져 있었다.\n\n손턴 부인이 말을 이었다.\n\n“처음에 내 하인 중 하나에게서, 그대가 아웃우드 역이라는 집에서 그토록 먼 곳에서 그런 저녁 시각에 어떤 신사와 함께 거니는 모습을 보였다는 말을 들었을 때, 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요. 그런데 안타깝게도 내 아들이 그 말을 확인해 주더군요. 아무리 좋게 말해도 그것은 경솔한 짓이었소. 여태껏 많은 젊은 여자들이 그렇게 해서 평판을 잃었지요 —”\n\n마거릿의 눈에서 불꽃이 튀었다. 이것은 생각지도 못한 이야기였다. 이것은 너무나 모욕적이었다. 손턴 부인이 자신이 한 거짓말에 대해 말했다면 좋다, 그녀는 그것을 인정하고 스스로를 낮추었을 것이다. 그러나 자신의 행실에 참견하다니, 자신의 평판을 입에 올리다니! 그것도 손턴 부인이, 한낱 남남인 사람이! 너무나 무례했다! 그녀는 대답하지 않으리라, 단 한마디도. 손턴 부인은 마거릿의 눈에 서린 싸움의 기운을 보았고, 그것이 그녀의 호전성 또한 불러일으켰다.\n\n“그대 어머니를 생각해서, 나는 그런 무분별한 짓에 대해 그대에게 경고하는 것이 옳다고 여겼소. 설령 실제로 그것이 그대를 확실한 해악으로 이끌지는 않는다 해도, 길게 보면 세상의 평가 속에서 그대를 깎아내리고 말 테니까요.”\n\n“제 어머니를 생각해서라면,” 마거릿이 눈물 젖은 목소리로 말했다, “저는 많은 것을 견디겠습니다. 그러나 모든 것을 견딜 수는 없어요. 어머니는 결코 제가 모욕당하기를 바라지 않으셨을 겁니다, 분명히요.”\n\n“모욕이라니요, 헤일 양!”\n\n“그렇습니다, 부인.” 마거릿이 한결 단단해진 목소리로 말했다. “모욕입니다. 저에 대해 무엇을 아시기에 그런 의심을 — 아!” 그녀는 무너져 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 말했다. “이제 알겠어요, 손턴 씨가 말씀하셨군요 —”\n\n“아닙니다, 헤일 양.” 손턴 부인이 말했다. 호기심은 그 말을 듣고 싶어 근질거렸으나, 정직함이 그녀로 하여금 마거릿이 막 하려던 고백을 가로막게 했다. “멈추시오. 손턴 씨는 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았소. 그대는 내 아들을 모르오. 그대는 그를 알 자격이 없어요. 그 애는 이렇게 말했소. 젊은 아가씨, 들어 보시오. 그대가 물리친 사람이 어떤 사내인지 알 수 있다면 알라고 말이오. 이 밀턴의 공장주는, 그 크고 다정한 마음이 그토록 멸시당하고도, 바로 어젯밤 내게 이렇게 말했소. ‘어머니, 그분에게 가 보십시오. 그분이 어떤 애착에서 비롯된 곤경에 처해 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 제게 있습니다. 그분에게는 여인의 조언이 필요합니다.’ 그것이 그 애가 한 바로 그 말이었다고 나는 믿소. 그 이상은 — 26일 저녁에 그대가 어떤 신사와 함께 아웃우드 역에 있었다는 사실을 인정한 것 말고는 — 그 애는 아무 말도 하지 않았소. 그대에게 불리한 말은 한마디도. 그대를 그토록 흐느끼게 만들 무언가를 그 애가 알고 있다 해도, 그 애는 그것을 혼자 간직하고 있어요.”\n\n마거릿의 얼굴은 여전히 두 손에 가려져 있었고, 그 손가락들은 눈물에 젖어 있었다. 손턴 부인은 조금 누그러졌다.\n\n“자, 헤일 양. 설명만 된다면 겉보기의 부적절함을 덜어 줄 만한 사정이 있을 수도 있겠지요, 그건 인정하리다.”\n\n여전히 대답이 없었다. 마거릿은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 궁리하고 있었다. 그녀는 손턴 부인과 좋게 지내고 싶었다. 그러면서도 어떤 설명도 할 수 없었고, 해서도 안 되었다. 손턴 부인은 참을성을 잃어 갔다.\n\n“알고 지내던 사이를 끊게 되는 것은 유감이오. 하지만 패니를 위해서는 — 내가 아들에게도 말했지만, 패니가 그런 짓을 했다면 우리는 그것을 큰 수치로 여겼을 거요 — 그리고 패니가 그릇된 길로 이끌릴 수도 있으니 —”\n\n“저는 아무 설명도 드릴 수 없습니다.” 마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “저는 잘못을 저질렀습니다. 그러나 부인께서 생각하시거나 알고 계신 그런 식으로는 아닙니다. 손턴 씨가 저를 부인보다 더 자비롭게 판단하시는 것 같군요.” 그녀는 눈물에 목이 메지 않도록 애를 써야 했다. “하지만 부인, 저는 부인께서 옳게 하려 하신다는 것은 믿습니다.”\n\n“고맙군요.” 손턴 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “내 뜻이 의심받고 있는 줄은 몰랐소. 내가 참견하는 것은 이번이 마지막이오. 그대 어머니가 부탁했을 때도 나는 그러겠다고 하기가 내키지 않았어요. 나는 내 아들이 그대에게 마음을 두었다는 것을 짐작만 하고 있던 동안에도 그것을 못마땅해했소. 그대는 내게 그 애에게 어울리는 사람으로 보이지 않았으니까. 그러나 그대가 폭동 때 그렇게 스스로를 위태롭게 하고 하인들과 직공들의 입방아에 오르게 되었을 때, 나는 그대에게 청혼하겠다는 아들의 뜻을 더는 거스르는 것이 옳지 않다고 느꼈소. 덧붙이자면, 그 애는 폭동이 있던 날까지 그런 뜻을 품은 적이 없다고 늘 부인해 왔지요.” 마거릿은 움찔하며 길게 쉭 하는 소리를 내며 숨을 들이켰다. 그러나 손턴 부인은 아랑곳하지 않았다. “그 애가 갔지요. 그런데 그대는 마음이 바뀐 듯 보였소. 나는 어제 아들에게, 그 짧은 사이에도 그대가 이 다른 연인에 대해 무언가 듣거나 알게 되었을 수 있겠다고 말했소 —”\n\n“저를 어떻게 보시는 겁니까, 부인?” 마거릿이 도도한 경멸로 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 물었다. 목이 백조의 목처럼 바깥으로 휘었다. “더는 하실 말씀이 없으실 겁니다, 손턴 부인. 저는 무엇에 대해서든 저를 변호하려는 시도를 일절 사양하겠습니다. 이 방에서 나가도록 허락해 주십시오.”\n\n그러고는 모욕당한 공주처럼 소리 없이 우아하게 방을 빠져나갔다. 손턴 부인은 자기가 남겨진 처지의 우스꽝스러움을 느낄 만큼은 타고난 해학을 갖추고 있었다. 스스로 걸어 나가는 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 마거릿의 태도에 딱히 화가 나지도 않았다. 그만큼 그 아가씨를 마음에 두고 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그 아가씨는 손턴 부인의 나무람을 부인이 기대한 만큼 뼈아프게 가슴에 새겼다. 그리고 마거릿의 격정은 어떤 침묵이나 삼감보다 훨씬 더 손님의 마음을 곧바로 누그러뜨렸다. 그것은 제 말이 먹혔음을 보여 주었으니까. “아가씨,” 손턴 부인은 속으로 생각했다. “성깔이 꽤나 대단하시군. 만일 존과 그대가 맺어졌더라면 그 애는 그대를 단단히 틀어쥐고 제 분수를 알게 만들어야 했겠지. 하지만 그대가 하루 중 그런 시각에 그 사내와 다시 거니는 일은 서둘러 하지 않을 성싶군. 그러기에는 자존심과 기개가 너무 세니까. 나는 남의 입에 오른다는 생각에 발끈하는 처녀가 보기 좋더라. 그건 천성이 들뜨지도 뻔뻔하지도 않다는 뜻이니까. 저 처녀로 말하자면 뻔뻔할지는 몰라도 들뜨지는 않을 게야. 그만한 공정함은 베풀어 주마. 그런데 패니로 말하자면, 그 애는 들뜨기는 해도 뻔뻔하지는 않겠지. 가엾은 것, 그 애에게는 배짱이라고는 없으니!”\n\n손턴 씨는 어머니만큼 흡족하게 아침을 보내고 있지 못했다. 어머니는 어쨌든 작정한 목적을 이루어 가는 중이었다. 그는 자기가 어디에 서 있는지, 파업이 자기에게 얼마만 한 손해를 입혔는지 헤아려 보려 애쓰고 있었다. 그의 자본 가운데 상당한 몫이 새로 들여온 값비싼 기계에 묶여 있었다. 게다가 그는 손에 쥔 몇몇 큰 주문을 내다보고 면화도 대량으로 사들여 놓은 참이었다. 파업은 그 주문들을 마무리하는 데에 그를 끔찍하게 뒤처지게 만들었다. 손에 익고 숙련된 제 직공들을 데리고서도 그는 계약을 지키는 데 얼마간 애를 먹었을 터였다. 그런 마당에, 유난한 활기가 필요한 때에 일부터 가르쳐야 하는 아일랜드 직공들의 서투름은 날마다의 골칫거리였다.\n\n히긴스가 청을 넣기에 좋은 때는 아니었다. 그러나 그는 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 그렇게 하겠다고 마거릿에게 약속한 터였다. 그래서 그는 매 순간 반감과 자존심과 뾰로통한 심사가 더해 가는데도 막다른 담벼락에 기대선 채 몇 시간이고 이 다리로 섰다 저 다리로 섰다 했다. 마침내 걸쇠가 날카롭게 들리더니 손턴 씨가 나왔다.\n\n“나리께 드릴 말씀이 있소.”\n\n“지금은 시간이 없네. 이미 늦었어.”\n\n“그러면 나리께서 돌아오실 때까지 기다리면 되겠구먼요.”\n\n손턴 씨는 이미 길 저만치 가 있었다. 히긴스는 한숨을 쉬었다. 그러나 별수 없었다. 길에서 붙드는 것이 “공장주 나리”를 만날 유일한 기회였다. 수위실 종을 울리거나 집으로 찾아가 그를 찾았더라면 감독에게 넘겨졌을 것이다. 그래서 그는 다시 가만히 섰다. 점심때가 되어 사람들이 공장 마당에서 우르르 몰려나올 때 그를 알아보고 말을 거는 몇몇에게는 대답 없이 짧게 고개만 끄덕여 보이고, 갓 들여온 아일랜드 “파업 파괴자들”에게는 있는 힘껏 험한 눈길을 던지면서. 마침내 손턴 씨가 돌아왔다.\n\n“아니! 자네 아직도 거기 있나!”\n\n“예, 나리. 꼭 드릴 말씀이 있소.”\n\n“그럼 이리 들어오게. 아니, 마당을 가로질러 가지. 사람들이 아직 돌아오지 않았으니 우리끼리 조용히 이야기할 수 있겠군. 보아하니 이 착한 사람들이 밥을 먹고 있구먼.” 그가 수위실 문을 닫으며 말했다.\n\n그는 걸음을 멈추고 감독에게 말을 건넸다. 감독이 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“나리, 저 사람이 히긴스라는 거 아시지요? 조합의 우두머리 중 하나입니다. 허스트필드에서 그 연설을 한 자예요.”\n\n“몰랐네.” 손턴 씨가 뒤따라오는 사내를 홱 돌아보며 말했다. 히긴스는 소란스러운 자로 그에게 이름만은 알려져 있었다.\n\n“따라오게.” 그가 말했다. 말투는 아까보다 더 거칠어져 있었다. ‘상업을 가로막고 제가 사는 바로 그 도시를 해치는 것이 이런 자들이지.’ 그는 생각했다. ‘한낱 선동가들, 남이 어떤 대가를 치르든 권력만 좋아하는 자들.’\n\n“자, 이 사람아! 내게 무슨 볼일인가?” 공장 사무실에 들어서자마자 손턴 씨가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n“제 이름은 히긴스라 하는데—”\n\n“그건 알아.” 손턴 씨가 말을 잘랐다. “무엇을 원하나, 히긴스 씨? 그게 문제지.”\n\n“일자리를 원하오.”\n\n“일자리라! 내게 일자리를 청하러 오다니 참 대단한 사람이군. 뻔뻔함만은 모자라지 않는 게 아주 분명해.”\n\n“나도 윗분들처럼 원수도 있고 험담꾼도 있소만, 그중 누구도 나더러 너무 겸손하다고 하는 소리는 못 들어 봤소.” 히긴스가 말했다. 손턴 씨의 말보다도 그 태도에 그의 피가 조금 끓어올랐다.\n\n손턴 씨는 탁자 위에 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 놓인 것을 보았다. 그는 그것을 집어 끝까지 읽었다. 다 읽고 나서 고개를 들고 말했다. “뭘 기다리고 있나?”\n\n“내가 여쭌 물음에 대한 답을 기다리오.”\n\n“아까 주지 않았나. 더는 자네 시간 낭비하지 말게.”\n\n“나리께서 제 뻔뻔함에 대해 한마디 하셨지만, 나는 예의를 갖춘 물음을 받으면 ‘예’든 ‘아니오’든 대답하는 것이 예절이라고 배웠소. 일자리를 주신다면 고맙겠구먼요. 내가 좋은 직공이라는 건 햄퍼 씨가 말해 줄 거요.”\n\n“내 생각에는 자네 인물평을 물으라고 나를 햄퍼에게 보내지 않는 편이 좋겠는데. 자네가 좋아할 것 이상을 듣게 될지도 모르니.”\n\n“그 위험은 감수하겠소. 그 사람들이 나에 대해 할 수 있는 최악의 말이라야, 내 손해를 무릅쓰고서라도 옳다고 여긴 대로 했다는 것뿐일 거요.”\n\n“그럼 가서 그들에게 청해 보고 일자리를 주는지 보게. 나는 자네와 자네 같은 자들을 따랐다는 것 말고 아무 잘못도 없는 가장 좋은 직공을 백 명 넘게 내보냈네. 그런데 내가 자네를 들일 성싶은가? 그럴 바에야 솜 부스러기 한가운데에 불붙은 장작을 던져 넣는 편이 낫지.”\n\n히긴스는 돌아섰다. 그러다 바우처의 기억이 밀려들었고, 그는 스스로를 설득해 할 수 있는 최대의 양보를 안고 다시 돌아섰다.\n\n“나리, 약속드리리다. 나리가 우리에게 옳게 대해 주시는 한 해가 될 말은 한마디도 하지 않겠소. 그리고 더 약속하리다. 나리가 그릇된 길로 가시거나 불공정하게 하시는 걸 보면 먼저 사사로이 나리께 말씀드리겠소. 그러면 공정한 경고가 되겠지요. 나리의 처사를 두고 나리와 내 생각이 맞지 않는다면 한 시간 전에 통보하고 나를 내보내셔도 좋소.”\n\n“이런, 자네는 스스로를 대단히 높이 치는군! 햄퍼가 자네를 잃어 손해가 컸겠어. 어쩌다 자네와 자네의 그 지혜를 놓아 보냈다던가?”\n\n“글쎄, 우리는 서로 못마땅해하며 갈라섰소. 나는 그들이 요구하는 서약을 해 줄 수 없었고, 그들은 어떤 조건으로도 나를 두려 하지 않았지요. 그러니 나는 다른 데 자유로이 매일 수 있는 몸이오. 아까도 말했듯이, 내 입으로 할 소리는 아니오만 나는 좋은 직공이오, 나리. 그리고 착실한 사람이오. 특히 술만 멀리할 수 있으면 말이오. 그리고 전에는 한 번도 못 했더라도 이제는 그렇게 하겠소.”\n\n“다음 파업에 대비해 돈을 더 모아 두려고 그러겠지?”\n\n“아니오! 그럴 여유가 있다면 고맙기나 하겠소. 나리네 그 파업 파괴자들 때문에 미쳐 버린 사람의 과부와 아이들을 먹여 살리려는 거요. 날실 씨실도 구별 못 하는 아일랜드 놈한테 제자리를 빼앗긴 사람 말이오.”\n\n“그래! 그런 갸륵한 뜻을 품고 있다면 다른 일로 돌아서는 게 낫겠군. 밀턴에 머물라고 권하고 싶지는 않네. 여기서는 자네가 너무 잘 알려져 있어.”\n\n“여름이라면,” 히긴스가 말했다, “나도 아일랜드 사람들 하는 일을 붙잡아 토목 인부로든 건초 일로든 뭐로든 나가서 밀턴은 다시 쳐다보지도 않겠소. 하지만 지금은 겨울이고, 아이들이 굶주릴 거요.”\n\n“자네가 무슨 토목 인부가 되겠나! 땅 파는 일로는 아일랜드 사람 상대로 반나절 몫도 못 해낼 텐데.”\n\n“열두 시간에 반나절 몫밖에 못 한다면 반나절 삯만 받으리다. 내가 그렇게 위험한 불씨라면, 공장 말고 어디 시켜나 볼 만한 자리를 아시는 데는 없소? 그 아이들을 위해서라면 그들이 매기는 대로 어떤 삯이라도 받겠소.”\n\n“자네가 무엇이 될지 모르겠나? 자네가 바로 파업 파괴자가 되는 걸세. 남의 아이들을 위한답시고 다른 일꾼들보다 적은 삯을 받게 되는 거야. 제 자식을 먹이려고 받을 수 있는 만큼만 받겠다는 딱한 사람을 자네가 어떻게 욕할지 생각해 보게. 자네와 자네 조합이 곧바로 그 사람에게 달려들 테지. 안 돼! 안 돼! 자네가 지금껏 그 딱한 파업 파괴자들을 어떻게 다루어 왔는지 그것만 떠올려도, 나는 자네 물음에 아니오라고 말하겠네. 자네에게 일자리를 주지 않겠어. 자네가 일자리를 청하러 온 구실을 믿지 않는다는 말은 하지 않겠네. 나는 그에 대해 아무것도 모르니까. 참말일 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지. 어쨌든 있음 직하지 않은 이야기야. 비켜 주게. 자네에게 일자리를 주지 않겠네. 그게 자네가 받을 답일세.”\n\n“알아들었소, 나리. 나리 마음속에 무언가 자리가 있으리라 여기는 듯한 사람이 가 보라고 시키지만 않았어도 나리를 성가시게 하지 않았을 거요. 그 사람이 잘못 안 거고, 나는 잘못 이끌린 거지요. 하지만 여자한테 잘못 이끌린 사내가 내가 처음도 아니잖소.”\n\n“다음번에는 자네 시간 내 시간 다 잡아먹지 말고 제 앞가림이나 하라고 그 여자에게 이르게. 나는 이 세상 모든 재앙의 밑바닥에는 여자가 있다고 믿네. 물러가게.”\n\n“나리의 온갖 친절에, 그중에서도 그 정중한 작별 인사에 참으로 고맙구먼요, 나리.”\n\n손턴 씨는 대꾸할 가치조차 두지 않았다. 그러나 잠시 뒤 창밖을 내다보다가 마당을 나서는 그 여위고 굽은 모습에 마음이 찔렸다. 그 무거운 걸음걸이는 자기에게 말을 걸겠다던 그 사내의 단호하고 또렷한 결의와 묘하게 어긋났다. 그는 수위실로 건너갔다.\n\n“저 히긴스라는 사람이 나와 이야기하려고 얼마나 기다렸나?”\n\n“여덟 시도 되기 전부터 문밖에 있었습니다, 나리. 그때부터 내내 거기 있었던 것 같습니다.”\n\n“그런데 지금이 —?”\n\n“막 한 시입니다, 나리.”\n\n‘다섯 시간이라.’ 손턴 씨는 생각했다. ‘사람이 아무것도 하지 않고 그저 바랐다 두려워했다 하며 기다리기에는 긴 시간이지.’\n\n# 제39장\n\n“아니, 나는 다 끝냈다. 그대는 더는 나를 얻지 못하리라. 그리고 나는 기쁘다, 그렇다, 온 마음으로 기쁘다, 이토록 또렷이 내가 나 자신으로서 자유로우니.” — 드레이턴.\n\n마거릿은 손턴 부인 앞에서 물러나자마자 자기 방에 틀어박혔다. 그녀는 마음이 흔들릴 때면 늘 그러하듯 방 안을 이리저리 오가기 시작했다. 그러나 이 허술하게 지은 집에서는 한 방의 발소리가 다른 방까지 죄다 들린다는 것이 떠올라, 손턴 부인이 무사히 집을 나서는 소리가 들릴 때까지 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화를 하나도 빠짐없이 떠올리도록 스스로를 몰아붙였다. 한마디 한마디, 그녀는 기억을 다그쳐 그것을 끝까지 되짚게 했다. 마침내 그녀는 몸을 일으키며 침울한 어조로 혼잣말했다.\n\n“어쨌든 그분의 말은 나를 건드리지 못해. 그 말들은 나에게서 그대로 미끄러져 떨어질 뿐이야. 나는 그분이 내게 갖다 붙인 그 온갖 속셈과 아무 상관이 없으니까. 그래도 누군가가, 그것도 여자가 다른 여자에 대해 이 모든 것을 그토록 쉽게 믿어 버릴 수 있다는 걸 생각하면 견디기 힘들어. 힘들고 서글퍼. 내가 정말로 잘못한 일에 대해서는 그분이 나를 나무라지 않아. 모르시는 거야. 그가 어머니께 말하지 않은 거야. 그러지 않으리라는 걸 나도 알 수 있었을 텐데!”\n\n그녀는 손턴 씨가 보여 준 그 섬세한 마음씨를 자랑스러워하기라도 하듯 고개를 들었다. 그러다 새로운 생각이 스치고 지나가자 두 손을 꽉 맞잡았다.\n\n“그분도 가엾은 오빠를 무슨 연인으로 여기고 있는 게 틀림없어.” (그 말이 머릿속을 지나가자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔다.) “이제야 알겠어. 그분은 내가 거짓말을 했다는 걸 아는 것만이 아니라, 다른 누군가가 나를 마음에 두고 있다고, 그리고 내가…… 아, 어쩌면 좋아! 아, 어쩌면 좋아! 나는 어떻게 해야 하지? 내가 무슨 소리를 하고 있는 거지? 내가 진실을 말했는지 아닌지에 관해 그분의 좋은 평가를 잃는 것 말고, 어째서 나는 그분이 나를 어떻게 생각하는지 마음을 쓰는 걸까? 알 수 없어. 하지만 나는 너무나 비참해! 아, 지난 한 해가 얼마나 불행했던지! 나는 어린 시절에서 곧장 늙은 나이로 건너와 버렸어. 젊음도 없었고, 여자로서의 시절도 없었어. 여자로서 품을 희망은 내게서 닫혀 버렸어. 나는 결코 혼인하지 않을 테니까. 그리고 나는 마치 늙은 여인이라도 된 듯이, 그와 똑같이 눈물겨운 마음으로 근심과 슬픔을 미리 맞이하고 있어. 힘을 내라는 이 끝없는 요구에 나는 지쳤어. 아버지를 위해서라면 버틸 수 있어. 그건 자연스럽고 경건한 도리니까. 그리고 손턴 부인의 부당하고 무례한 의심에도 버틸 수 있을 것 같아. 적어도 그것에 분개할 기운은 낼 수 있어. 하지만 그분이 나를 얼마나 완전히 오해하고 있을지 느끼는 것은 견디기 힘들어. 오늘 나에게 무슨 일이 있었기에 이렇게 병적이 되었을까? 모르겠어. 다만 어쩔 수 없다는 것만 알아. 나도 이따금은 무너져야 해. 아니, 그래도 그러지 않겠어.” 그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “그러지 않을 거야. 나 자신과 내 처지를 생각하지 않겠어. 내 감정을 들여다보지 않겠어. 이제 와서 아무 소용도 없어. 언젠가 늙은 여인이 될 때까지 살아 있다면, 그때는 난롯가에 앉아 잉걸불을 들여다보며 있었을지도 모를 삶을 볼 수 있겠지.”\n\n그러는 내내 그녀는 밖에 나갈 채비를 서둘러 갖추고 있었다. 다만 이따금 손을 멈추고 눈물을 훔쳤다. 온갖 용기를 다 내어도 자꾸만 다시 차오르는 눈물에 조바심 난 몸짓이었다.\n\n“나만큼이나 서글픈 잘못을 저지르고도 너무 늦어서야 그것을 깨닫는 여자가 많겠지. 그날 나는 그분에게 얼마나 오만하고 무례하게 말했던가! 하지만 그때는 몰랐어. 이 마음은 조금씩 조금씩 내게로 다가왔고, 어디서 시작되었는지도 모르겠어. 이제 나는 무너지지 않겠어. 이 비참한 자각을 안은 채 그분을 예전과 똑같이 대하기는 어렵겠지. 그래도 나는 아주 침착하고 아주 조용하게 있으면서 말을 아주 적게 하겠어. 하기야 그분을 못 볼지도 모르지. 그분은 분명히 우리를 피하고 있으니까. 그건 무엇보다도 더 견디기 힘들 거야. 그렇지만 나에 대해 그렇게 믿고 있으니 나를 피하는 것도 이상할 게 없지.”\n\n그녀는 밖으로 나가 들판 쪽으로 빠르게 걸으며, 걸음의 빠르기로 생각을 잠재우려 했다.\n\n돌아와 현관 계단에 서 있는데 아버지가 다가왔다.\n\n“착하구나.” 그가 말했다. “바우처 부인 댁에 다녀왔구나. 나도 시간이 나면 식사 전에 가 볼 참이었단다.”\n\n“아니에요, 아버지. 안 갔어요.” 마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. “그분 생각을 미처 못 했어요. 그렇지만 식사 마치고 바로 갈게요. 아버지가 낮잠 주무시는 동안에 다녀올게요.”\n\n그리하여 마거릿은 다녀왔다. 바우처 부인은 몹시 아팠다. 정말로 앓고 있었다. 그저 몸이 편찮은 정도가 아니었다. 며칠 전에 들렀던 그 친절하고 사려 깊은 이웃 여자가 모든 일을 도맡은 듯했다. 아이들 몇은 이웃집에 가 있었다. 메리 히긴스가 낮 식사 때 어린 세 아이를 데리러 왔고, 그 뒤로 니컬러스가 의사를 부르러 갔다. 의사는 아직 오지 않았다. 바우처 부인은 죽어 가고 있었고, 기다리는 것 말고는 할 수 있는 일이 없었다. 마거릿은 의사의 소견을 듣고 싶었고, 그동안에는 히긴스네에 가 보는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했다. 그러면 니컬러스가 손턴 씨에게 부탁을 넣어 볼 수 있었는지도 어쩌면 들을 수 있을 터였다.\n\n가 보니 니컬러스는 찬장 위에서 동전 하나를 팽이처럼 돌리느라 여념이 없었다. 겁도 없이 그에게 매달려 있는 어린아이 셋을 즐겁게 해 주기 위해서였다. 동전이 길게 잘 돌아가자 아이들과 마찬가지로 그도 웃고 있었다. 마거릿은 하는 일에 그렇게 재미를 붙인 그 행복한 표정이 좋은 조짐이라고 생각했다. 동전이 돌기를 멈추자 “꼬맹이 조니”가 울기 시작했다.\n\n“이리 오렴.” 마거릿이 아이를 찬장에서 내려 품에 안으며 말했다. 그녀는 시계를 아이의 귀에 대 주면서 니컬러스에게 손턴 씨를 만났느냐고 물었다.\n\n그의 얼굴빛이 대번에 달라졌다.\n\n“봤수!” 그가 말했다. “보기도 하고 듣기도 너무 많이 들었지.”\n\n“그러면 거절당하셨군요.” 마거릿이 서글프게 말했다.\n\n“그럼요. 처음부터 그럴 줄 알았수. 그런 공장주들 손에서 자비를 바라 봐야 헛일이오. 댁은 낯선 사람이고 외지 사람이니 저들 하는 짓을 알 리가 없겠지만, 나는 알고 있었소.”\n\n“여쭤 보시라고 한 게 죄송해요. 그분이 화를 내던가요? 햄퍼 씨가 그랬던 것처럼 말하지는 않았지요?”\n\n“딱히 예의 바르지도 않습디다!” 니컬러스가 아이들을 위해서만큼이나 제 재미로도 다시 동전을 돌리며 말했다. “아가씨는 신경 쓰지 마시우. 나야 있던 자리에 그대로 있을 뿐이오. 내일은 일자리 찾아 떠돌아다닐 거요. 나도 받은 만큼 되돌려 줬소. 내 발로 두 번씩이나 찾아올 만큼 그를 대단하게 여기지는 않는다고, 다만 아가씨가 가 보라 일렀고 나는 아가씨한테 신세를 진 게 있어서 왔다고 말해 줬지.”\n\n“제가 보냈다고 말씀하셨어요?”\n\n“아가씨 이름까지 댔는지는 모르겠소. 대지 않은 것 같은데. 그저 뭘 잘 모르는 여자 하나가 그 사람 마음에 무른 데가 있는지 가서 보라고 권하더라고 했소.”\n\n“그랬더니 그분은요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“아가씨더러 남의 일에 참견 말라고 전하라더군.—이번 게 제일 오래 도네, 얘들아.—그것도 나한테 쓴 말에 대면 점잖은 축이오. 하지만 아무래도 좋소. 우리야 있던 자리 그대로고, 나는 이 어린것들 굶기느니 길바닥에서 돌을 깨겠소.”\n\n마거릿은 버둥거리는 조니를 품에서 내려 찬장 위 제자리에 도로 놓았다.\n\n“손턴 씨에게 가 보시라고 한 게 죄송해요. 그분에게 실망했어요.”\n\n그녀의 등 뒤에서 자그마한 기척이 났다. 그녀와 니컬러스가 동시에 돌아보니, 언짢은 놀라움이 어린 얼굴로 손턴 씨가 서 있었다. 마거릿은 순간의 충동에 따라 한마디도 없이 그의 앞을 지나 밖으로 나갔다. 얼굴에 갑자기 번진 것이 느껴지는 창백함을 감추려고 깊이 고개를 숙였을 뿐이었다. 그도 똑같이 깊이 고개를 숙여 답하고는 그녀의 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 바우처 부인 댁으로 서둘러 가는 길에 그 문 닫히는 소리가 들려왔고, 그것이 그녀의 굴욕을 가득 채우는 듯했다. 그 또한 그녀가 거기 있는 것을 보고 언짢았다. 그의 가슴에는 다정함이 있었다. 니컬러스 히긴스가 말한 그 “무른 데”가. 그러나 그는 얼마간의 자존심으로 그것을 감추었다. 그는 그것을 아주 신성하고 안전하게 간직했고, 그 안으로 비집고 들어오려는 온갖 사정에 날을 세웠다. 그러나 자기 다정함이 드러나는 것을 두려워한 만큼이나, 그는 자기가 공정하다는 것을 모든 사람이 알아주기를 바랐다. 그런데 자기와 말 한마디 나누려고 겸손하게 참으며 다섯 시간을 기다린 사람에게 그토록 업신여기는 태도로 귀를 내주었으니 자기가 공정하지 못했다고 그는 느꼈다. 그 사람이 기회를 잡았을 때 자기에게 건방지게 굴었다는 것은 손턴 씨에게 아무것도 아니었다. 오히려 그 때문에 그가 마음에 들었다. 게다가 그는 그때 자기 성미가 곤두서 있었다는 것도 알고 있었으니, 그것으로 아마 서로 비긴 셈이었다. 손턴 씨의 마음을 친 것은 그 다섯 시간의 기다림이었다. 자기에게는 다섯 시간의 여유가 없었다. 그러나 그는 몸과 머리를 모두 갈아 넣는 그 고된 노동 가운데 한 시간, 두 시간을 떼어, 히긴스가 한 이야기가 사실인지, 그 사람됨이 어떠한지, 그가 살아온 내력이 어떠한지 증거를 모으러 다녔다. 그는 그러지 않으려 애썼으나 히긴스가 한 말이 모두 사실이라고 확신하게 되었다. 그러자 그 확신이 무슨 주문에라도 걸린 듯 안으로 스며들어 그의 마음속에 잠자던 다정함을 건드렸다. 그 사람의 참을성과 그 동기의 꾸밈없는 너그러움은—그는 바우처와 히긴스 사이의 다툼에 대해서도 알아냈던 것이다—공정함을 따지는 이치 따위를 그가 아주 잊어버리게 만들었고, 더 신성한 본능으로 그것을 훌쩍 뛰어넘게 했다. 그는 히긴스에게 일자리를 주겠다고 말하러 온 것이었다. 그런데 그녀의 마지막 말을 들은 것보다도 마거릿이 거기 있다는 사실이 그를 더 언짢게 했다. 그 말로 히긴스에게 자기를 찾아가라고 권한 여자가 바로 그녀임을 알게 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 오로지 옳기 때문에 하고 있는 이 일에 그녀에 대한 어떤 생각이 동기로 끼어드는 것이 두려웠다.\n\n“그러면 아까 여자라고 말한 그 사람이 저 아가씨였다는 건가?” 그가 히긴스에게 발끈하여 말했다. “누구인지 말해 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.”\n\n“그랬으면 아마 댁도 그이를 두고 좀 더 점잖게 말했겠구먼. 댁한테도 어머니가 계실 테니, 온갖 재앙의 뿌리에 여자가 있다느니 떠들 때 그 혀를 붙들어 줬을지도 모르지.”\n\n“물론 그 말을 헤일 양에게 전했겠지?”\n\n“그럼 전했지. 아무튼 전한 것 같소. 댁에 관한 일에는 다시는 참견하지 말라고 일러 뒀소.”\n\n“저 아이들은 누구 아이들인가? 자네 아이들인가?” 손턴 씨는 들은 이야기로 미루어 그 아이들이 누구인지 어지간히 짐작하고 있었다. 그러나 이 답답한 첫머리에서 이야기를 돌려놓기가 어색했다.\n\n“내 아이들이 아니면서 내 아이들이오.”\n\n“오늘 아침에 내게 말한 그 아이들인가?”\n\n“댁이 말했지.” 히긴스가 잘 감추지 못한 사나움으로 돌아서며 대답했다. “내 얘기가 사실일 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지만 아무래도 있을 법하지 않다고. 나리, 나는 안 잊었소.”\n\n손턴 씨는 잠시 잠자코 있다가 말했다. “나도 잊지 않았네. 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 기억하고 있어. 나는 그 아이들에 대해 그렇게 말할 자격이 없었네. 나는 자네 말을 믿지 않았어. 내가 듣기로 바우처가 자네에게 한 것처럼 누가 내게 그리했다면, 나라면 남의 아이들을 떠맡지 못했을 걸세. 하지만 이제는 자네가 진실을 말했다는 걸 아네. 미안하네.”\n\n히긴스는 돌아서지도, 곧바로 대꾸하지도 않았다. 그러나 마침내 입을 열었을 때 그 어조는 누그러져 있었다. 말은 여전히 퉁명스러웠지만.\n\n“바우처와 나 사이에 있었던 일을 캐고 다닐 자격은 댁한테도 없수. 그는 죽었고, 나는 마음이 아프오. 그걸로 됐소.”\n\n“그렇지. 나와 함께 일하겠나? 그걸 물으러 왔네.”\n\n히긴스의 고집이 흔들렸다가 다시 기운을 차리고 버텨 섰다. 그는 말하려 하지 않았다. 손턴 씨도 두 번 묻지 않았다. 히긴스의 눈길이 아이들에게 떨어졌다.\n\n“댁은 나를 뻔뻔하다고, 거짓말쟁이라고, 말썽꾼이라고 불렀고, 이따금 술에 빠진다고 했더라도 그건 얼마쯤 사실이오. 그리고 나는 댁을 폭군이라고, 늙은 불도그라고, 모질고 잔인한 공장주라고 불렀지. 사정이 그렇소. 하지만 이 아이들을 봐서, 나리, 우리가 언제고 함께 지낼 수 있으리라 보시오?”\n\n“허!” 손턴 씨가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다. “함께 지내자고 한 건 내 제안이 아니었네. 그렇지만 자네 말대로라면 한 가지 위안은 있군. 우리 둘 다 상대를 지금보다 더 나쁘게 여기기는 어려울 테니까.”\n\n“그건 그렇소.” 히긴스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. “댁을 본 뒤로 줄곧 생각했지. 나를 안 받아 준 게 얼마나 다행이냐고. 그토록 견디기 힘든 사람은 처음 봤으니까. 하지만 그건 아마 성급한 판단이었겠지. 게다가 나 같은 놈한테는 일은 일이오. 그러니 나리, 가겠소. 그뿐 아니라 고맙다는 말도 하겠소. 나한테는 그게 대단한 말이오.” 그가 한결 스스럼없이 말하며, 갑자기 돌아서서 처음으로 손턴 씨를 똑바로 마주 보았다.\n\n“나에게도 이건 대단한 일이네.” 손턴 씨가 히긴스의 손을 힘주어 잡으며 말했다. “자, 시간은 꼭 맞춰 나오게.” 그가 다시 공장주로 돌아가 말을 이었다. “내 공장에 게으름뱅이는 두지 않네. 벌금을 물릴 일이 있으면 우리는 꽤 매섭게 물리지. 그리고 자네가 말썽을 부리는 걸 처음으로 잡아내는 날 자네는 나가는 거네. 자, 이제 자네 처지를 알았겠지.”\n\n“오늘 아침에 내 지혜 이야기를 하셨지. 그것도 데려가도 되겠소? 아니면 머리는 두고 오는 편이 낫겠소?”\n\n“내 일에 참견하는 데 쓸 거라면 머리는 두고 오고, 자네 일만 지킬 수 있다면 머리도 데려오게.”\n\n“내 일이 어디서 끝나고 나리 일이 어디서 시작되는지 가리려면 머리가 꽤나 많이 필요하겠수.”\n\n“자네 일은 아직 시작도 안 했고, 내 일은 나를 기다리며 멈춰 서 있네. 그러면 잘 가게.”\n\n손턴 씨가 바우처 부인 댁 문 앞에 이르기 바로 전에 마거릿이 그 문에서 나왔다. 그녀는 그를 보지 못했다. 그는 몇 야드쯤 그녀를 뒤따르며 가볍고 편안한 걸음걸이와 훤칠하고 우아한 자태를 감탄하며 바라보았다. 그러나 이 소박한 기쁨의 감정은 문득 질투에 물들고 병들어 버렸다. 그는 그녀를 따라잡아 말을 걸고 싶었다. 다른 애착이 있다는 것을 자기가 알고 있음을 그녀도 이제 알 터인데, 그녀가 자기를 어떻게 맞이하는지 보고 싶었던 것이다. 그는 또한, 이 바람에 대해서는 다소 부끄러웠지만, 히긴스를 자기에게 보내 일자리를 청하게 한 그녀의 판단이 옳았음을 자기가 인정했다는 것을, 그리고 아침의 결정을 뉘우쳤다는 것을 그녀가 알아주기를 바랐다. 그가 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n“한 말씀 드리자면, 헤일 양, 실망하시기에는 좀 일렀습니다. 히긴스를 받아들였습니다.”\n\n“다행이네요.” 그녀가 쌀쌀하게 말했다.\n\n“오늘 아침에 제가 한 말을 그가 당신에게 옮겼다고 하더군요. 그러니까……” 손턴 씨가 말을 머뭇거렸다. 마거릿이 받았다.\n\n“여자는 참견하지 말라는 말씀이지요. 선생님께는 의견을 말씀하실 온전한 권리가 있고, 그 의견은 아주 옳았을 거예요. 저도 의심하지 않아요. 하지만,” 그녀가 조금 더 열을 내어 말을 이었다. “히긴스 씨가 정확한 진실을 다 말씀드리지는 않았어요.” “진실”이라는 낱말이 자기가 한 거짓말을 떠올리게 했고, 그녀는 몹시 거북해져 말을 뚝 그쳤다.\n\n손턴 씨는 처음에는 그녀의 침묵을 어떻게 새겨야 할지 몰라 어리둥절했다. 그러다 그녀가 한 거짓말과 그전에 있었던 모든 일이 떠올랐다. “정확한 진실이라!” 그가 말했다. “정확한 진실을 말하는 사람은 아주 드물지요. 저는 그런 것을 바라기를 그만두었습니다. 헤일 양, 제게 해 주실 설명은 없습니까? 제가 어떻게 생각할 수밖에 없는지 아실 텐데요.”\n\n마거릿은 잠자코 있었다. 그녀는 어떤 종류의 설명이든 그것이 오빠에 대한 의리와 어긋나지 않을지 헤아리고 있었다.\n\n“아닙니다.” 그가 말했다. “더는 묻지 않겠습니다. 제가 당신을 시험에 들게 하고 있는지도 모르지요. 지금으로서는, 믿어 주십시오, 당신의 비밀은 제게 안전합니다. 그러나 한 말씀 드리자면, 그렇게 조심성 없이 구시면 큰 위험을 무릅쓰게 됩니다. 저는 그저 당신 아버님의 친구로서 말씀드리는 것입니다. 제게 다른 어떤 생각이나 희망이 있었다 해도 그것은 물론 끝났습니다. 저는 아무런 사심이 없습니다.”\n\n“알고 있어요.” 마거릿이 아무렇지도 않고 무심한 투로 말하려고 애쓰며 말했다. “제가 선생님께 어떻게 보일지도 알고 있어요. 하지만 그 비밀은 다른 사람의 것이고, 그 사람을 해치지 않고서는 설명할 수가 없어요.”\n\n“그분의 비밀을 캐고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없습니다.” 그가 점점 화가 치미는 채로 말했다. “당신에 대한 제 관심은 그저 친구로서의 것입니다. 믿지 않으실지 모르지만, 헤일 양, 정말 그렇습니다. 한때 제가 당신을 괴롭히겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것은 사실이지만, 그것은 다 접었습니다. 다 지나갔습니다. 믿으시겠습니까, 헤일 양?”\n\n“네.” 마거릿이 조용하고 서글프게 말했다.\n\n“그러면 정말이지 우리가 계속 함께 걸을 이유가 없군요. 어쩌면 하실 말씀이 있으실까 했습니다만, 우리는 서로에게 아무것도 아니라는 걸 알겠습니다. 제 쪽의 어리석은 연정이 완전히 끝났다는 것을 확실히 아셨다면, 이만 실례하겠습니다.” 그는 몹시 서둘러 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n“저분이 무슨 뜻으로 그러시는 걸까?” 마거릿은 생각했다. “내가 늘 그분이 나를 마음에 두고 있다고 여기기라도 하는 듯이 그렇게 말씀하시다니, 대체 무슨 뜻일까? 나는 그렇지 않다는 걸 아는데. 그럴 수가 없는데. 어머니가 나에 대해 그 모진 말들을 다 그분에게 했겠지. 하지만 나는 그분을 마음에 두지 않겠어. 나는 분명 나 자신을 다스릴 만큼은 되니까, 이 사납고 낯설고 비참한 감정도 억누를 수 있어. 그분의 좋은 평가를 되찾아 보겠다고 사랑하는 오빠를 저버리라고까지 나를 꾀던 이 감정을. 그것도 나는 자기에게 아무것도 아니라고 그토록 애써 일러 주는 사람의 평가를. 자! 가엾은 작은 마음아, 기운을 내고 씩씩해지렴. 우리가 내쳐져 홀로 남겨진다면, 우리 둘이 서로에게 아주 큰 존재가 되어 주자.”\n\n아버지는 그날 아침 그녀의 명랑함에 거의 놀랄 지경이었다. 그녀는 쉴 새 없이 이야기했고, 타고난 유머를 여느 때보다 높은 가락으로 끌어올렸다. 그녀가 하는 말 가운데 많은 것에 씁쓸한 기색이 배어 있었다 해도, 옛 할리 가 사람들에 대한 이야기가 다소 비꼬는 투였다 해도, 아버지는 다른 때 같으면 그랬을 것처럼 딸을 나무랄 마음이 나지 않았다. 딸이 근심을 떨쳐 내는 것이 반가웠기 때문이다. 저녁이 한창일 때 그녀는 메리 히긴스와 이야기하러 아래층으로 불려 내려갔다. 그리고 돌아왔을 때 헤일 씨는 딸의 뺨에 눈물 자국이 있는 것을 본 듯했다. 그러나 그럴 리가 없었다. 딸이 좋은 소식을 가져왔으니 말이다. 히긴스가 손턴 씨의 공장에 일자리를 얻었다는 소식이었다. 아무튼 그녀의 기운은 가라앉았고, 그녀는 아예 말을 이어 가기가 몹시 어려워졌다. 아까처럼 들뜬 투로 말하기는 더더욱 어려웠다. 며칠 동안 그녀의 기분은 이상하리만큼 오르내렸다. 아버지가 딸을 걱정하기 시작할 무렵, 그녀에게 얼마간의 변화와 새로움을 약속하는 소식이 한두 군데에서 날아들었다. 헤일 씨는 벨 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 그 노신사는 자청해서 그들을 찾아오겠다고 했다. 헤일 씨는 옛 옥스퍼드 친구와 함께 지내는 것이 자기에게 그러하듯 마거릿의 생각에도 유쾌한 전환을 가져다주리라 여겼다. 마거릿은 아버지를 기쁘게 하는 일에 관심을 가져 보려 애썼다. 그러나 그녀는 너무 기운이 없어서, 벨 씨가 스무 번쯤 그녀의 대부라 한들 그가 어떤 사람이든 마음에 두지 못했다. 그녀의 마음을 더 흔든 것은 이디스에게서 온 편지였다. 이모의 죽음에 대한 위로가 가득했고, 자기와 남편과 아이에 대한 이야기가 잔뜩 적혀 있었으며, 끝에 가서는 그곳 기후가 아기에게 맞지 않고 쇼 부인도 잉글랜드로 돌아가자는 이야기를 하고 있으니 레녹스 대위가 군직을 팔고 나와 모두가 옛 할리 가 집에서 다시 함께 살게 될지도 모르겠다고 적혀 있었다. 다만 마거릿이 없으면 그 집은 몹시 허전할 것이라고 했다. 마거릿은 그 옛집이, 그리고 잘 짜인 단조로운 삶의 잔잔한 고요가 사무치게 그리웠다. 그 삶을 살고 있을 때는 이따금 지겨웠다. 그러나 그 뒤로 그녀는 이리저리 시달렸고, 최근 자기 자신과 벌인 이 싸움으로 어찌나 지쳤던지, 고여 있는 것조차 쉼이고 새 기운이 되리라 여겨졌다. 그리하여 그녀는 레녹스 부부가 잉글랜드로 돌아오면 그들 집에 오래 머무는 일을 하나의 지점으로 바라보기 시작했다. 희망의 지점이 아니라 여유의 지점으로, 그 안에서 자기 자신에 대한 힘과 다스림을 되찾을 수 있을 그런 지점으로. 지금으로서는 모든 화제가 손턴 씨에게로 흘러가는 것만 같았다. 아무리 애를 써도 그를 잊을 수 없는 것만 같았다. 히긴스네에 가면 거기서 그의 이야기를 들었다. 아버지는 그와의 독서를 다시 시작해 그의 견해를 끊임없이 인용했다. 벨 씨의 방문마저도 그 세입자의 이름을 화제에 올려놓았다. 새 임대차 계약이 준비 중이고 그 조건을 합의해야 하니 자기 시간의 상당 부분을 손턴 씨와 보내야 할 것 같다고 편지에 적어 보냈기 때문이다.\n\n# 제40장\n\n“권리를 주장할 수 없는 곳에서는 억울함도 없고, 가져 본 적 없는 것을 빼앗겼을 리도 없건만, 그래도 나는 내 슬픔에서 놓여나지 못하네. 하물며 나를 이토록 슬픔에 잠기게 하는 바로 그것으로 다른 이가 기뻐할 수 있음에랴.” — 와이엇.\n\n마거릿은 벨 씨의 방문에서 자신이 큰 즐거움을 얻으리라고는 기대하지 않았다. 오직 아버지를 생각해서 그날을 기다렸을 뿐이다. 그러나 막상 대부가 오자 그녀는 곧바로 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 우정의 자리로 들어섰다. 벨 씨는 그녀가 그토록 제 마음에 꼭 맞는 처녀인 데에는 그녀 자신의 공이 없다고 말했다. 성큼 걸어 들어와 그의 호의를 통째로 차지해 버리는 것은 그녀가 물려받은 힘이라는 것이었다. 그러자 그녀는 답례로, 펠로의 사각모와 가운을 걸치고도 그토록 싱싱하고 젊음을 잃지 않은 것은 대단한 일이라며 그를 추어올렸다.\n\n“따뜻함과 다정함이 싱싱하고 젊다는 말이에요. 하지만 솔직히 털어놓아야겠는데, 대부님의 의견들은 제가 오랫동안 만나 본 것 가운데 가장 낡고 곰팡내 나는 것 같아요.”\n\n“자네 딸이 하는 말 좀 들어 보게, 헤일! 밀턴에 살더니 아주 물이 들었구먼. 민주주의자에, 붉은 공화주의자에, 평화협회 회원에, 사회주의자에—”\n\n“아버지, 다 제가 상업의 진보를 편들었기 때문이에요. 벨 씨는 상업이 짐승 가죽과 도토리를 맞바꾸던 시절에 그대로 멈춰 서 있기를 바라시거든요.”\n\n“아니, 아니지. 나라면 땅을 파서 감자를 심겠네. 그리고 그 짐승 가죽의 털을 깎아서 나사를 짜겠지. 과장은 말게, 아가씨. 다만 나는 이 부산스러움에 진력이 났을 뿐이야. 다들 부자가 되겠다고 서두르느라 서로를 짓밟고 달려들지 않나.”\n\n“누구나 대학 숙사에 편히 앉아 제 손 하나 까딱하지 않고 재산이 불어나도록 내버려 둘 수 있는 것은 아니라네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “이곳에도 아무 수고 없이 자네 재산처럼 불어나 준다면 고맙게 여길 사람이 수두룩할 걸세.”\n\n“그럴 리 없어. 그자들이 좋아하는 건 그 부산함과 아등바등하는 다툼이니까. 가만히 앉아서 지난 시대에서 배운다거나, 예언자의 정신으로 성실히 일하며 앞날의 모양을 잡아 나간다거나 하는 것으로 말하자면—원! 흥! 밀턴에 가만히 앉아 있을 줄 아는 사내가 하나라도 있을 성싶지 않네. 그런데 그건 대단한 기술이란 말이야.”\n\n“밀턴 사람들은 아마 옥스퍼드 사람들이 움직일 줄을 모른다고 여길 걸세. 서로 조금 더 어울린다면 아주 좋은 일이 되겠지.”\n\n“밀턴 사람들에게야 좋을지 모르지. 그자들에게 좋을 일 가운데는 다른 사람들에게는 아주 못마땅한 일이 많으니까.”\n\n“대부님도 밀턴 사람이 아니신가요?” 마거릿이 물었다. “저는 대부님이 고향 도시를 자랑스러워하실 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“고백하건대, 나는 자랑할 만한 것이 무엇인지 모르겠구나. 마거릿, 옥스퍼드에 한 번만 와 보렴. 자랑스러워할 만한 곳이 어떤 것인지 보여 주마.”\n\n“그런데 말일세,” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “오늘 저녁에 손턴 씨가 우리와 차를 마시러 오기로 했네. 자네가 옥스퍼드를 자랑스러워하는 만큼 그는 밀턴을 자랑스러워하지. 두 사람이 서로를 조금 더 너그러운 사람으로 만들어 보게나.”\n\n“나는 더 너그러워지고 싶은 생각이 없네, 고맙지만.” 벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n“손턴 씨가 차 마시러 오시나요, 아버지?” 마거릿이 나직한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“차 시간에 오거나 곧 뒤에 오거나 할 게다. 자기도 잘 모르겠다더구나. 기다리지 말라고 했다.”\n\n손턴 씨는 어머니가 마거릿에게 그 행실의 부적절함을 이야기하겠다던 계획을 얼마나 실행에 옮겼는지 어머니에게 묻지 않기로 마음먹고 있었다. 만일 그 면담이 실제로 있었다면 거기서 오간 말에 대한 어머니의 전언은 자신을 성가시고 언짢게 만들 뿐이리라고 그는 거의 확신했다. 그러면서도 그 이야기가 어머니의 마음을 거치며 어떤 빛깔로 물들었을지 내내 의식하게 되리라는 것도 알았다. 그는 마거릿의 이름이 입에 오르는 것조차 듣기 싫었다. 그녀를 탓하면서도, 그녀를 질투하면서도, 그녀를 단념하면서도, 그는 스스로도 어쩌지 못한 채 그녀를 사무치게 사랑했다. 그는 그녀의 꿈을 꾸었다. 꿈속에서 그녀는 두 팔을 벌린 채 춤추듯 그에게 다가왔는데, 그 가벼움과 들뜸은 그를 홀리면서도 동시에 그녀를 역겹게 여기게 만들었다. 그러나 마거릿의 성품이라고는 깡그리 빠져나간—마치 어떤 악령이 그 모습만 차지해 버린 듯한—그 마거릿의 형상이 그의 상상에 어찌나 깊이 찍혔던지, 잠에서 깨어난 뒤에도 그는 우나와 두에사를 좀처럼 갈라놓지 못할 지경이었다. 그리고 뒤의 것에 대한 혐오가 앞의 것까지 휘감아 일그러뜨리는 듯했다. 그러면서도 그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 피함으로써 제 나약함을 인정하기에는 자존심이 너무 셌다. 그녀와 한자리에 있을 기회를 일부러 찾지도, 일부러 피하지도 않겠다는 것이었다. 자기에게 자제력이 있음을 스스로 확인하려고 그는 그날 오후 모든 일을 하나하나 질질 끌었다. 몸짓 하나하나를 부자연스러울 만큼 느리고 신중하게 만들었다. 그리하여 그가 헤일 씨 댁에 닿았을 때는 이미 여덟 시가 넘어 있었다. 그다음에는 서재에서 벨 씨와 처리할 사업상의 일이 있었다. 그런데 벨 씨는 볼일이 다 끝나고 위층으로 올라가도 좋았을 때가 한참 지나도록 난롯가에 앉아 지루하게 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그러나 손턴 씨는 자리를 옮기자는 말을 한마디도 꺼내려 하지 않았다. 그는 속으로 애가 타고 또 탔고, 벨 씨야말로 더없이 따분한 말상대라고 생각했다. 한편 벨 씨는 벨 씨대로 속으로 그 인사를 고스란히 되돌려 주어, 손턴 씨야말로 여태 만나 본 가운데 가장 무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 사내이며 머리도 태도도 형편없이 나빠졌다고 여겼다. 마침내 위층 방에서 무슨 기척이 들려 그리로 올라가는 편이 좋겠다는 생각이 들었다. 두 사람은 마거릿이 편지를 펼쳐 놓고 그 내용을 아버지와 열심히 이야기하고 있는 것을 보았다. 신사들이 들어서자 편지는 곧 치워졌다. 그러나 곤두서 있던 손턴 씨의 감각은 헤일 씨가 벨 씨에게 건네는 몇 마디를 붙잡고 말았다.\n\n“헨리 레녹스에게서 온 편지라네. 마거릿이 아주 희망에 부풀었지.”\n\n벨 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다. 손턴 씨가 바라보았을 때 마거릿의 얼굴은 장미처럼 붉었다. 그는 그 자리에서 당장 일어나 방을 나가서 다시는 이 집에 발을 들이지 않고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다.\n\n“우리는 이렇게들 생각했다네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “자네와 손턴 씨가 마거릿의 충고를 받아들여 서로를 개종시키려 애쓰고 있는 모양이라고 말이야. 서재에 그렇게 오래 있었으니.”\n\n“그래서 킬케니 고양이 꼬리처럼 우리에게서도 의견 하나밖에 남지 않으리라 생각했다는 건가. 그래, 누구 의견이 가장 질기게 살아남을 것 같던가?”\n\n손턴 씨는 두 사람이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 도무지 알 수 없었으나 물어보는 것은 자존심이 상해서 하지 않았다. 헤일 씨가 정중하게 사정을 일러 주었다.\n\n“손턴 씨, 우리는 오늘 아침에 벨 씨가 고향 도시에 대해 일종의 옥스퍼드식 중세적 편견을 품고 있다고 나무라던 참이었습니다. 그러면서 우리가—아마 마거릿이었지 싶은데—밀턴의 공장주들과 조금 어울려 보는 것이 벨 씨에게 이로우리라고 말했지요.”\n\n“실례지만 그게 아닐세. 마거릿은 밀턴의 공장주들이 옥스퍼드 사람들과 조금 더 어울리는 것이 그들에게 이로우리라고 했네. 안 그러냐, 마거릿?”\n\n“제 생각에는 서로가 서로를 조금 더 겪어 보는 것이 양쪽 모두에게 좋으리라고 말한 것 같아요. 그게 아버지 생각이 아니라 제 생각이었는지도 몰랐고요.”\n\n“그러니 보십시오, 손턴 씨. 우리는 아래층에서 사라져 버린 스미스 집안이니 해리슨 집안이니 하는 이야기를 늘어놓을 게 아니라 서로를 갈고닦았어야 했던 겁니다. 하지만 이제라도 내 몫은 하지요. 대체 밀턴 사람들은 언제쯤 살아 볼 작정인지 궁금하군요. 당신들 삶은 통째로 삶의 재료를 그러모으는 데만 쓰이는 것 같으니 말입니다.”\n\n“산다는 것으로 즐김을 말씀하시는 모양이군요.”\n\n“그렇지요, 즐김. 무엇을 즐기느냐까지는 따지지 않겠습니다. 당신이나 나나 한낱 쾌락 따위는 아주 보잘것없는 즐김으로 여기리라 믿으니까요.”\n\n“저로서는 그 즐김이라는 것의 성격을 좀 더 분명히 해 주셨으면 합니다.”\n\n“그럼 이렇게 말하지요. 즐김 또는 여유—돈이 가져다주는 힘과 영향력을 누리는 일 말입니다. 당신들은 모두 돈을 얻으려고 애쓰지요. 대체 무엇에 쓰려는 겁니까?”\n\n손턴 씨는 잠자코 있었다. 그러다 입을 열었다. “정말이지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다. 다만 제가 얻으려고 애쓰는 것은 돈이 아닙니다.”\n\n“그럼 무엇입니까?”\n\n“그건 속을 파고드는 물음입니다. 그런 심문관 앞에서는 제 속을 다 드러내야 할 텐데, 제가 그럴 준비가 되어 있는지는 잘 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“안 되지요!” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “우리 문답에서 개인적인 이야기는 빼기로 합시다. 두 분 다 대표자가 될 만한 사람이 아니에요. 각자가 너무 개성이 강해서 말이지요.”\n\n“그걸 칭찬으로 여겨야 할지 어떨지 모르겠구먼. 나는 아름다움과 학문과 자랑스러운 옛 역사를 지닌 옥스퍼드를 대표하고 싶은데. 어떻게 생각하느냐, 마거릿. 내가 우쭐해해도 되겠니?”\n\n“저는 옥스퍼드를 몰라요. 하지만 어떤 도시를 대표하는 것과 그 주민들을 대표하는 사람이 되는 것은 다르지요.”\n\n“지당한 말이다, 마거릿 아가씨. 그러고 보니 오늘 아침에도 너는 내 편이 아니었지. 취향이 아주 밀턴 사람 같고 공장 사람 같더구나.”\n\n마거릿은 손턴 씨가 자기에게 재빨리 던지는 놀란 눈길을 알아차렸고, 벨 씨의 이 말을 그가 어떻게 새길지 생각하니 마음이 언짢았다. 벨 씨가 말을 이었다.\n\n“아! 우리 하이 스트리트와 래드클리프 광장을 보여 드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만. 대학 건물들은 빼고 말하는 겁니다. 손턴 씨가 밀턴의 매력을 말할 때 공장들을 빼도 좋다고 허락하는 것처럼요. 나는 내 고향을 헐뜯을 권리가 있지요. 나도 밀턴 사람이라는 걸 잊지 마십시오.”\n\n손턴 씨는 벨 씨의 말 하나하나에 필요 이상으로 언짢아졌다. 농담을 받아 줄 기분이 아니었던 것이다. 다른 때였다면 자기가 몸에 익힌 온갖 습관과 그토록 어긋나는 삶이 이루어지는 도시를 두고 벨 씨가 짐짓 심술궂게 나무라는 것을 재미있게 들었을 것이다. 그러나 지금은 속이 하도 쓰라려서 애초에 진지한 공격이 아니었던 것을 굳이 막아서려 들었다.\n\n“저는 밀턴을 도시의 본보기라고 내세우지 않습니다.”\n\n“건축에서도 아니고?” 벨 씨가 짓궂게 물었다.\n\n“그럼요! 우리는 너무 바빠서 겉모습 따위에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었습니다.”\n\n“겉모습 ‘따위’라고는 하지 마십시오.” 헤일 씨가 부드럽게 말했다. “그것은 어릴 적부터 우리 모두에게, 살아가는 하루하루에 자국을 남기니까요.”\n\n“조금만 기다려 보십시오.” 손턴 씨가 말했다. “우리는 그리스인들과는 다른 핏줄임을 잊지 마십시오. 그들에게는 아름다움이 전부였고, 그들에게라면 벨 씨께서 여유와 고요한 즐김의 삶을 말씀하실 만도 하지요. 그 즐김의 상당 부분은 바깥 감각을 통해 들어오는 것이었으니까요. 저는 그들을 흉내 낼 생각이 없는 만큼이나 그들을 업신여길 생각도 없습니다. 다만 저는 게르만의 피를 이었습니다. 잉글랜드의 다른 지방에 비하면 이 지방에서는 그 피가 덜 섞였지요. 우리는 그들의 말을 많이 간직하고 있고, 그들의 정신은 더 많이 간직하고 있습니다. 우리는 삶을 즐길 시간이 아니라 행하고 힘쓸 시간으로 봅니다. 우리의 영광과 아름다움은 우리의 바깥 힘에서 솟아납니다. 그 힘이 우리를 물질의 저항 앞에서, 나아가 더 큰 어려움 앞에서 이기게 하지요. 우리 다크셔 사람들은 또 다른 뜻에서도 게르만 사람입니다. 우리는 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 우리를 위한 법이 만들어지는 것을 몹시 싫어합니다. 어설픈 입법으로 끊임없이 참견할 게 아니라, 우리 스스로 우리 일을 바로잡게 내버려 두었으면 합니다. 우리는 자치를 내세우고 중앙집권에 반대합니다.”\n\n“요컨대 칠왕국 시절로 돌아가고 싶다는 말씀이군요. 아무튼 오늘 아침에 내가 한 말은 거두어들이겠습니다. 밀턴 사람들이 과거를 존중하지 않는다고 했던 말 말입니다. 당신들은 어엿한 토르 숭배자로군요.”\n\n“우리가 옥스퍼드 사람들처럼 과거를 받들지 않는다면, 그것은 현재에 좀 더 곧바로 쓰일 무언가를 원하기 때문입니다. 과거를 연구해 미래를 내다보는 예언에 이른다면 훌륭한 일이지요. 그러나 새로운 형편 속을 더듬어 나가는 사람들에게는, 경험에서 나온 말이 우리와 가장 가깝고 가장 급한 일에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지를 일러 준다면 그편이 더 훌륭할 겁니다. 그 일에는 부딪쳐야 할 어려움이 가득하고, 그것을 어떤 식으로 맞아 이겨 내느냐에—당장만 모면하려고 옆으로 밀어 두는 것이 아니라—우리의 앞날이 달려 있으니까요. 그런데 그렇지가 않아요! 사람들은 내일 해야 할 일보다 유토피아를 이야기하기가 훨씬 쉽지요. 그러면서도 그 일을 남들이 다 해 놓고 나면 ‘아이고, 부끄러운 줄 알아라!’ 하고 외칠 준비는 누구보다 잘 되어 있습니다.”\n\n“그런데도 나는 여태 당신이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 모르겠군요. 밀턴 사람들께서는 오늘의 어려움을 옥스퍼드로 올려 보내 주실 생각은 없으신지요? 아직 우리를 시험해 보지도 않으셨잖습니까.”\n\n이 말에 손턴 씨는 소리 내어 웃었다. “요즘 우리를 꽤나 괴롭혀 온 일들을 떠올리며 말한 듯합니다. 우리가 겪어 온 파업들을 생각하고 있었지요. 제가 손해를 보며 깨닫고 있듯이 성가시고 해로운 일입니다. 그런데도 제가 지금 쓰라림을 겪고 있는 이 마지막 파업은 훌륭했습니다.”\n\n“훌륭한 파업이라!” 벨 씨가 말했다. “토르 숭배가 꽤나 깊어졌다는 소리로 들리는군요.”\n\n마거릿은 자기로서는 아주 진지하게 느끼는 일이 자꾸만 농담거리로 바뀌는 데 손턴 씨가 속상해하고 있음을 눈으로 보기보다 마음으로 느꼈다. 그녀는 한쪽은 대수롭지 않게 여기지만 다른 한쪽에게는 자기 일이기에 깊이 사무치는 이 화제에서 이야기를 돌려 보려 애썼다. 그녀는 억지로 무슨 말이든 꺼냈다.\n\n“이디스가 그러는데 코르푸에서는 무늬 놓은 옥양목이 런던보다 좋고 값도 싸대요.”\n\n“그러더냐?” 아버지가 말했다. “그건 이디스가 늘 하는 과장 가운데 하나일 게다. 확실하냐, 마거릿?”\n\n“그 애가 그렇게 말한 건 확실해요, 아버지.”\n\n“그럼 그건 사실일세.” 벨 씨가 말했다. “마거릿, 나는 네 정직함을 어찌나 믿는지 그 믿음이 네 사촌의 인품까지 덮어 주는구나. 네 사촌이라면 과장을 할 리 없지.”\n\n“헤일 양이 그렇게 정직하기로 이름났습니까?” 손턴 씨가 씁쓸하게 말했다. 그 말을 내뱉은 순간 그는 제 혀를 물어뜯고 싶었다. 자기가 무엇이라고? 무엇 때문에 그녀의 수치를 들추어 이런 식으로 그녀를 찌른단 말인가? 오늘 밤 그는 어찌 이리 못돼 먹었는가. 그토록 오래 그녀에게서 붙들려 있었다는 심술에 사로잡히고, 어떤 이름이 나왔다고 해서—그것이 자기보다 운 좋은 연적의 이름이라 여겨져서—짜증이 나고, 흥겹고 스스럼없는 말로 저녁 한때를 즐겁게 흘려보내려 애쓰는 사람을 가벼운 마음으로 상대해 주지 못한 탓에 심사가 뒤틀렸다. 상대는 모두에게 다정한 옛 벗이고, 여러 해 알고 지낸 손턴 씨라면 그 사람됨을 이미 잘 알 만도 했는데 말이다. 그러고서 마거릿에게 그런 말을 하다니! 그녀는 예전처럼 자리에서 일어나 방을 나가지는 않았다. 그의 퉁명스러움이나 성미가 그녀를 언짢게 할 때면 늘 그러던 것과 달리. 그녀는 뜻밖의 매몰찬 대접을 받은 아이처럼 서글피 놀란 눈길을 한순간 던졌을 뿐, 그대로 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그 눈은 천천히 커지며 구슬프고 원망 어린 슬픔으로 차올랐다. 그러다 눈길이 떨어졌고, 그녀는 바느질감 위로 몸을 숙인 채 다시는 입을 열지 않았다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바라보지 않을 수 없었고, 낯선 한기에 떨기라도 하듯 그녀의 몸을 훑고 지나가는 한숨을 보았다. 그는 아기를 “흔들어 어르고 나무라던” 어머니가, 어머니의 사랑을 온전히 믿는다는 뜻으로 아기가 천천히 지어 보이는 그 미소로 사랑이 되살아났음을 확인하기도 전에 불려 나가야 했을 때의 심정이었다. 그는 짤막하고 날 선 대답만 했다. 마음이 편치 않고 뾰족해져서 농담과 진담을 가려 듣지도 못했다. 오직 그녀의 눈길 한 번, 말 한마디를 바랐다. 그 앞에 뉘우침으로 겸허히 엎드릴 수 있도록. 그러나 그녀는 바라보지도 말하지도 않았다. 그녀의 동그랗고 갸름한 손가락이 마치 그것이 평생의 일이기라도 한 듯 한결같고 빠르게 바느질감을 들락거렸다. 그녀는 자기를 마음에 둘 리 없다고 그는 생각했다. 그렇지 않다면 자기 바람이 이토록 뜨거우니 그녀도 단 한순간이나마 그 눈을 들어 자기 눈 속의 뒤늦은 뉘우침을 읽지 않을 수 없었으리라고. 그는 떠나기 전에 그녀를 때리기라도 하고 싶었다. 그런 터무니없이 무례한 짓이라도 저질러야 제 가슴을 갉아먹는 회한을 그녀에게 털어놓을 자격을 얻을 것 같아서였다. 그날 저녁의 마무리가 바깥바람을 맞으며 오래 걷는 일이었던 것은 다행이었다. 그 걸음이 그를 차분하게 가라앉혀, 앞으로는 되도록 그녀를 보지 않겠다는 무거운 결심으로 되돌려 놓았다. 그 얼굴과 자태를 보는 것만으로도, 그 목소리를 듣는 것만으로도—맑은 가락처럼 부드러운 바람 같은 그 목소리—그의 중심이 이토록 흔들리니 말이다. 그렇다! 그는 사랑이 무엇인지 알게 되었다. 날카로운 아픔, 사나운 겪음이었다. 그는 그 불길 한가운데서 몸부림치고 있었다. 그러나 그 용광로를 지나 중년의 고요함으로 싸워 나갈 참이었다. 이 큰 열정을 겪었기에 한결 더 풍요롭고 한결 더 사람다워진 채로.\n\n그가 다소 갑작스레 방을 나가자 마거릿은 자리에서 일어나 말없이 바느질감을 개기 시작했다. 길게 이어진 시접이 무겁게 느껴졌고, 힘 빠진 그녀의 팔에는 여느 때와 달리 묵직했다. 그녀의 동그스름하던 얼굴선이 길고 곧게 뻗은 모양으로 바뀌었고, 온몸이 하루 종일 몹시 고단한 일을 겪어 낸 사람 같았다. 세 사람이 잠자리에 들 채비를 하는 동안 벨 씨는 손턴 씨를 조금 나무라는 말을 웅얼거렸다.\n\n“성공에 저렇게 망가진 사내는 처음 보네. 말 한마디를 못 견뎌. 무슨 농담이든 못 견디고. 무엇이든 그 잘난 위엄의 아픈 데를 건드리는 모양이야. 예전에는 훤한 대낮처럼 소탈하고 늠름한 사람이었는데. 허영이라고는 없어서 기분을 상하게 할 수도 없었지.”\n\n“지금도 허영은 없어요.” 마거릿이 탁자에서 몸을 돌리며 조용하고 또렷하게 말했다. “오늘 밤에는 평소의 그분답지 않았을 뿐이에요. 여기 오시기 전에 무슨 언짢은 일이 있었던 게 분명해요.”\n\n벨 씨는 안경 너머로 그 날카로운 눈길 가운데 하나를 그녀에게 던졌다. 그녀는 아주 침착하게 그 눈길을 받아 냈다. 그러나 그녀가 방을 나간 뒤 그가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n“헤일! 손턴과 자네 딸 사이에 프랑스 사람들이 말하는 ‘탕드레스’가 있다는 생각이 든 적은 없나?”\n\n“한 번도 없네!” 헤일 씨가 말했다. 처음에는 놀라더니 이내 그 새로운 생각에 허둥댔다. “아니야, 자네가 틀렸다고 확신하네. 거의 틀림없이 잘못 본 걸세. 무언가 있다면 그건 다 손턴 씨 쪽 이야기지. 딱한 사람! 그가 그 애를 마음에 두고 있지 않기를 바라고 또 바라네. 그 애가 그를 받아들일 리 없다는 걸 잘 아니까.”\n\n“글쎄! 나야 독신이고 평생 연애라고는 비껴서 살았으니 내 견해는 값어치가 없을지도 모르지. 그렇지만 않다면, 그 애 쪽에도 아주 그럴듯한 낌새가 있더라고 말하겠네만!”\n\n“그렇다면 자네가 틀린 것이 분명하네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “그가 그 애를 마음에 둘 수는 있겠지. 사실 그 애가 때로는 그에게 거의 무례하다시피 했는데도 말이야. 하지만 그 애가! 아니, 마거릿이 그를 두고 그런 생각을 할 리 없네. 그런 일은 그 애 머릿속에 들어온 적도 없을 걸세.”\n\n“머리 말고 가슴에 들어왔으면 되는 거지. 하지만 나야 그럴 수도 있겠다는 짐작을 던져 보았을 뿐이네. 아마 내가 틀렸겠지. 틀렸든 맞았든 나는 몹시 졸리네. 그러니 철없는 공상으로 자네 밤잠을 흩어 놓았으니(그런 눈치가 보이는군) 나는 홀가분한 마음으로 내 잠자리에나 가겠네.”\n\n그러나 헤일 씨는 그런 실없는 생각에 흔들리지 않겠다고 마음먹었다. 그래서 그 생각을 하지 않기로 작정한 채 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었다.\n\n이튿날 벨 씨는 작별을 고하면서, 어떤 종류의 어려움이든 그 속에서 자기를 도와주고 지켜 줄 권리가 있는 사람으로 여겨 달라고 마거릿에게 당부했다. 헤일 씨에게는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n“자네의 그 마거릿이 내 가슴 깊이 들어와 버렸네. 잘 보살피게. 아주 귀한 아이야. 밀턴에는 너무나 과분하고, 사실 옥스퍼드에나 어울리지. 도시 말일세, 사람들 말고. 아직은 짝을 못 찾겠네. 찾게 되면 내 젊은이를 데려와 자네 젊은 처자와 나란히 세워 보겠네. 아라비안나이트의 정령들이 카랄마잔 왕자를 데려와 요정의 바두라 공주와 견주게 했듯이 말이야.”\n\n“제발 그런 짓은 하지 말게. 그다음에 닥친 불행들을 떠올려 보게나. 게다가 나는 마거릿을 내줄 수 없네.”\n\n“아니지. 다시 생각해 보니 10년 뒤에 우리 둘이 심술궂은 늙은 병자가 되었을 때 그 애더러 우리를 돌보게 하세. 진담일세, 헤일! 자네가 밀턴을 떠났으면 좋겠네. 애초에 내가 권하기는 했다만 자네에게는 더없이 맞지 않는 곳이야. 그래 준다면 나는 내 미심쩍은 그늘들을 삼켜 버리고 대학에서 주는 성직록을 받겠네. 그러면 자네와 마거릿이 목사관에 와서 사는 거지. 자네는 평신도 부목사쯤 되어 씻지도 않은 무리를 내 손에서 덜어 가고, 그 애는 낮에는 우리 살림꾼—마을의 자선 부인—노릇을 하고, 저녁에는 우리에게 책을 읽어 주어 잠들게 하고. 그런 삶이라면 나는 아주 행복하겠네. 어떤가?”\n\n“절대로 안 되네.” 헤일 씨가 단호하게 말했다. “내 인생의 큰 변화는 이미 한 번 치렀고, 그 값으로 치를 고통도 다 치렀네. 나는 여기서 남은 생을 살고, 여기 묻혀 사람들 틈에 묻혀 사라질 걸세.”\n\n“나는 아직 내 계획을 접지 않았네. 다만 지금은 더 이상 그것으로 자네를 들쑤시지 않겠네. 우리 진주는 어디 있나. 이리 오너라, 마거릿, 작별의 입맞춤을 다오. 그리고 얘야, 힘이 닿는 데까지 참된 벗을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 잊지 마라. 너는 내 아이다, 마거릿. 그것을 기억하렴. 그리고 하느님의 축복이 있기를!”\n\n그렇게 하여 그들은 앞으로 이어 갈 조용한 삶의 단조로움 속으로 되돌아갔다. 이제 마음 졸이며 바라고 두려워할 병자도 없었다. 그토록 오래 생생한 관심거리였던 히긴스 집안 사람들조차 당장 마음 쓸 일에서는 멀어진 듯했다. 어미를 잃고 고아가 된 바우처네 아이들이 마거릿이 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 보살핌을 요구했고, 그녀는 아이들을 맡고 있는 메리 히긴스를 자주 보러 갔다. 두 집안은 한집에서 살고 있었다. 큰 아이들은 변변찮은 학교에 다녔고, 메리가 일하러 나가 자리를 비우면 작은 아이들은 바우처가 죽었을 때 그 분별력이 마거릿의 마음에 남았던 그 친절한 이웃 여자가 돌보았다. 물론 그 수고에는 삯이 치러졌다. 실제로 니컬러스는 이 고아들을 위한 자잘한 계획과 처리에서 하나같이 차분한 판단력과 정연한 사고방식을 보였는데, 이는 예전의 별스럽고 들쭉날쭉하던 행동거지와는 사뭇 달랐다. 그는 어찌나 꾸준히 일했던지 마거릿은 그 겨울 몇 달 동안 그를 자주 보지 못했다. 그러나 만날 때면, 그토록 온전히 진심으로 떠맡은 그 아이들의 아버지 이야기가 나오면 그가 움찔하며 물러선다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 손턴 씨 이야기도 쉽사리 하지 못했다.\n\n“솔직히 말해서요,” 그가 말했다. “그 양반은 나를 아주 헷갈리게 만들어 놓습니다. 사람이 둘이란 말이오. 하나는 내가 옛날부터 알던, 뼛속까지 공장주인 놈이지요. 다른 하나는 공장주의 살점이라고는 한 점도 없는 사람이고. 그 두 사람이 어떻게 한 몸에 묶여 있는지가 내가 풀어야 할 수수께끼요. 그래도 나는 거기 지지 않을 참이오. 그 사이에도 그 양반이 여기 꽤 자주 오는데, 그래서 공장주가 아니라 사람인 쪽을 내가 아는 거요. 그리고 내가 그 양반한테 어리둥절한 만큼 그 양반도 나한테 어리둥절한 모양이오. 앉아서 듣고 빤히 쳐다보는 게, 꼭 내가 무슨 낯선 지방에서 갓 잡혀 온 짐승이라도 되는 것 같단 말이오. 하지만 나는 조금도 주눅 들지 않소. 내 집에서 나를 주눅 들게 하려면 어지간해서는 안 되지요, 그 양반도 보다시피. 그러고는 그 양반이 젊었을 때 들어 두었으면 좋았을 만한 내 생각을 좀 들려주지요.”\n\n“그러면 그 사람이 대꾸를 하지 않던가?” 헤일 씨가 물었다.\n\n“글쎄! 내가 그 양반을 제법 낫게 만들었다고 자부하기는 해도, 득이 온통 그 양반 쪽에만 있다고는 못 하겠소. 이따금 거친 소리를 한두 마디 하는데, 처음 들으면 보기 좋지 않아도 곱씹어 보면 묘하게 진실의 맛이 나거든요. 오늘 밤에도 올 거요, 아마. 그 아이들 학교 문제로요. 학교 돌아가는 모양이 마음에 안 든다고 아이들을 좀 살펴보겠다는구먼.”\n\n“그 아이들이 무엇을—” 하고 헤일 씨가 말을 꺼내는데, 마거릿이 그의 팔을 건드리며 자기 시계를 보여 주었다.\n\n“일곱 시가 다 됐어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “이제 저녁이 길어지고 있어요. 가요, 아버지.” 그녀는 그 집에서 어느 정도 멀어질 때까지 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬지 못했다. 그러다 마음이 가라앉자 그렇게까지 서두르지 않았더라면 좋았으리라는 생각이 들었다. 어쩐 일인지 요즘은 손턴 씨를 아주 드물게밖에 보지 못했고, 그가 히긴스를 보러 왔을지도 모르는데, 오랜 우정을 생각하면 오늘 밤 그를 보았더라면 좋았을 것이었다.\n\n그렇다! 그는 아주 드물게 왔다. 수업이라는 무미건조하고 서늘한 볼일을 위해서조차 그랬다. 헤일 씨는 얼마 전만 해도 그토록 큰 관심을 보이던 그리스 문학에 대해 제자가 미지근해진 것에 실망했다. 그리고 요즘은 마지막 순간에야 손턴 씨의 급한 쪽지가 도착해 일이 너무 많아 오늘 저녁에는 함께 읽을 수 없겠다고 알려 오는 일이 잦았다. 시간으로 따지자면 다른 제자들이 그의 자리를 넘치도록 채웠지만, 헤일 씨의 마음속에서 첫 제자만 한 사람은 없었다. 그는 어느새 소중해진 그 교류가 반쯤 끊긴 것에 풀이 죽고 서글퍼졌고, 이런 변화를 불러왔을 까닭을 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있곤 했다.\n\n어느 저녁 마거릿이 바느질감을 붙들고 앉아 있는데 그가 불쑥 이렇게 물어 그녀를 놀라게 했다.\n\n“마거릿! 손턴 씨가 너를 마음에 두고 있다고 여길 만한 까닭이 혹시 있었느냐?”\n\n이 물음을 던지면서 그는 거의 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그러나 물리쳐 두었던 벨 씨의 생각이 다시 떠올랐고, 그는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 채 깨닫기도 전에 말을 뱉고 말았다.\n\n마거릿은 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 고개를 수그린 모양새로 그는 그 대답이 무엇일지 짐작했다.\n\n“네, 그런 것 같아요—아, 아버지, 진작 말씀드렸어야 했어요.” 그러고는 바느질감을 떨어뜨리고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n“아니다, 얘야. 내가 주제넘게 캐묻는 것이라고는 생각지 마라. 네가 그의 마음에 응할 수 있다고 느꼈다면 나에게 말했으리라고 믿는다. 그가 너에게 그 이야기를 하더냐?”\n\n처음에는 대답이 없었다. 그러나 이윽고 나직하고 마지못한 작은 “네.”가 들려왔다.\n\n“그래서 거절했고?”\n\n긴 한숨. 한층 더 힘없이 늘어진 자세. 그리고 또 한 번의 “네.” 그러나 아버지가 미처 입을 열기 전에 마거릿이 얼굴을 들었다. 어여쁜 부끄러움으로 발그레해진 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 아버지에게 눈길을 붙박고 말했다.\n\n“아버지, 이제 이 이야기는 다 말씀드렸고 그 이상은 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 게다가 이 모든 일이 저에게는 너무 아파요. 거기 얽힌 말 한마디 몸짓 하나가 이루 말할 수 없이 쓰라려서 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없어요. 아, 아버지, 아버지께 이 친구분을 잃어 드려서 죄송해요. 하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그래도 아, 정말 죄송해요.” 그녀는 바닥에 앉아 아버지의 무릎에 머리를 얹었다.\n\n“나도 안타깝구나, 얘야. 벨 씨가 그런 짐작을 말했을 때 나는 무척 놀랐단다—”\n\n“벨 씨가요! 아, 벨 씨가 눈치채셨어요?”\n\n“조금은. 하지만 그는 네가—뭐라고 해야 하나?—네가 손턴 씨에게 싫지 않은 마음을 품고 있다고 짐작했더구나. 나는 그럴 리 없다는 것을 알고 있었지. 나는 이 모든 것이 그저 그의 공상이기를 바랐다만, 네 진짜 마음을 너무 잘 알고 있었으니 네가 손턴 씨를 그런 식으로 좋아할 수 있으리라고는 생각할 수 없었지. 그래도 참으로 안타깝구나.”\n\n두 사람은 몇 분 동안 아주 조용히 가만히 있었다. 그러나 얼마 뒤 아버지가 어루만지듯 그녀의 뺨을 쓸어내리다가 그 얼굴이 눈물로 젖어 있는 것을 알고는 거의 충격을 받았다. 그가 손을 대자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 억지로 환하게 웃으며 레녹스 집안 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 화제를 돌리려는 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 마음 여린 헤일 씨는 이야기를 도로 본래의 물길로 돌리려 하지 못했다.\n\n“내일—그래요, 내일이면 그 사람들이 할리 가로 돌아오겠네요. 아, 얼마나 낯설까! 어느 방을 아기방으로 만들까요? 쇼 이모는 아기와 함께 행복하시겠죠. 이디스가 엄마가 되다니! 그리고 레녹스 대위는—군직을 팔았으니 이제 뭘 하며 지낼지 궁금해요!”\n\n“이렇게 하자꾸나.” 아버지가 그녀의 새로운 관심거리를 북돋아 주고 싶은 마음에 말했다. “두 주쯤 너를 보내 주어야겠다. 런던에 올라가 여행에서 돌아온 사람들을 보고 오려무나. 프레더릭의 앞날에 대해서는 헨리 레녹스 씨와 반 시간만 이야기해도 그가 보낸 편지 열두 통보다 더 많이 알게 될 게다. 그러니 사실 볼일과 즐거움을 겸하는 셈이지.”\n\n“아니에요, 아버지. 아버지는 저를 보내실 수 없고, 게다가 저도 보내지지 않을 거예요.” 그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. “저는 프레더릭 오빠 일에 대해서는 슬프게도 희망을 잃어 가고 있어요. 오빠는 우리에게 부드럽게 알려 주고 있지만, 레녹스 씨 자신도 여러 해가 지나기 전에는 증인들을 찾아낼 가망이 없다고 여긴다는 걸 알 수 있어요. 아니요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그 물거품은 참 예뻤고 우리 마음에 참 소중했지만, 다른 숱한 물거품처럼 터져 버렸어요. 우리는 프레더릭 오빠가 그토록 행복하다는 데서 위안을 얻고, 서로에게 큰 사람이 되어 주는 데서 위안을 얻어야 해요. 그러니 저를 보내 줄 수 있다는 말씀으로 저를 서운하게 하지 마세요, 아버지. 정말이지 그러실 수 없으니까요.”\n\n그러나 변화라는 생각은 마거릿의 마음에 뿌리를 내리고 싹을 틔웠다. 처음에 아버지가 내놓았던 방식으로는 아니었지만 말이다. 그녀는 그런 종류의 무언가가 아버지에게 얼마나 필요할지 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 아버지의 기운은 늘 약했지만 이제는 너무 자주 가라앉았고, 건강도 결코 불평하는 법은 없었으나 아내의 병과 죽음으로 심하게 상해 있었다. 제자들과 정해진 시간에 책을 읽기는 했다. 그러나 주기만 하고 받는 것이 없는 그 일은, 손턴 씨가 그에게 배우러 오던 옛날처럼 벗함이라고 부를 수 없었다. 마거릿은 아버지 자신은 알지 못하는 결핍을 겪고 있음을 알아차렸다. 남자가 남자들과 나누는 교류의 결핍이었다. 헬스톤에서는 이웃 목사들과 오가며 왕래할 일이 끊이지 않았고, 들에서 일하는 가난한 일꾼들이나 저녁에 느릿느릿 집으로 걸어가는 이들이나 숲에서 소를 돌보는 이들에게는 언제든 말을 걸고 말을 받을 여유가 있었다. 그러나 밀턴에서는 누구나 너무 바빠서 조용한 이야기도, 무르익은 생각의 오고 감도 없었다. 그들이 하는 말은 장사에 관한 것이었고 아주 눈앞의 실제적인 것이었다. 그리고 하루하루의 일에 매인 긴장이 풀리고 나면 그들은 다음 날 아침까지 묵정밭 같은 휴식 속으로 가라앉았다. 하루 일이 끝난 뒤에는 노동자를 찾을 수 없었다. 그는 사람됨의 정도에 따라 어떤 강연으로, 어떤 모임으로, 어떤 선술집으로 가 버렸다. 헤일 씨는 몇몇 학회에서 연속 강연을 해 볼까 생각했다. 그러나 그것을 하려는 마음이 너무나 의무를 다하려는 노력에 가깝고, 그 일과 그 목적을 향한 다정한 사랑의 충동은 너무나 적었으므로, 마거릿은 아버지가 그 일을 어떤 흥으로 바라볼 수 있게 되기 전에는 잘해 내지 못하리라고 확신했다.\n\n# 제41장\n\n나는 새들이 길 없는 제 길을 알듯 내 길을 안다— 나는 다다르리라! 언제일지, 어느 굽이를 먼저 돌지, 나는 묻지 않는다. 다만 하느님이 우박을, 눈멀게 하는 불덩이를, 진눈깨비를, 숨 막히는 눈을 내려보내지 않으신다면, 언젠가는—그분의 좋은 때에—나는 다다르리라. 그분이 나를 인도하시고 새를 인도하신다. 그분의 좋은 때에! — 브라우닝, 파라켈수스.\n\n그리하여 겨울은 깊어 가고 낮은 길어지기 시작했으나, 2월의 햇살에 으레 따라오는 희망의 밝음은 조금도 함께 오지 않았다. 손턴 부인은 물론 이 집에 오는 일을 아주 그만두었다. 손턴 씨는 이따금 왔지만 그의 방문은 그녀의 아버지를 향한 것이었고 서재를 벗어나지 않았다. 헤일 씨는 그가 늘 한결같다고 말했다. 실은 두 사람의 왕래가 드물어졌다는 바로 그 사실이 헤일 씨로 하여금 그 왕래를 한층 더 귀하게 여기게 만드는 듯했다. 그리고 손턴 씨가 한 말이라며 아버지에게서 전해 들을 수 있는 것으로 미루어, 그의 발길이 뜸해진 데에는 어떤 노여움이나 불쾌함에서 비롯된 구석이 조금도 없었다. 파업 동안 그의 사업이 복잡하게 얽혀, 지난겨울에 기울이던 것보다 더 세심한 주의를 요하게 된 것이었다. 아니, 마거릿은 그가 이따금 자기 이야기를 한다는 것까지도 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 알 수 있는 한 언제나 똑같이 차분하고 다정한 말투로, 그녀의 이름을 입에 올리기를 피하지도 않고 굳이 찾지도 않으면서였다.\n\n그녀는 아버지의 마음을 북돋울 만한 기운이 없었다. 지금의 이 음울한 평온에 앞서, 때때로 폭풍까지 섞인 불안과 근심의 시기가 너무나 길게 이어졌던 터라 그녀의 마음은 탄력을 잃어버렸다. 그녀는 어린 바우처 아이 둘을 가르치는 일에서 소일거리를 찾으려 했고, 선하게 살고자 힘껏 애썼다. 힘껏이라는 말을 나는 참으로 진실하게 쓴다. 그 모든 노력의 끝에서 그녀의 마음은 죽은 듯 무감각했기 때문이다. 그리고 그녀가 그 노력을 어김없이, 또 고통스럽게 기울였음에도 그녀는 여전히 어떤 명랑함에서도 예전만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그녀의 삶은 여전히 황량하고 음울해 보였다. 그녀가 잘해낸 단 한 가지는 스스로 의식하지 못한 경건함에서 나온 것, 곧 말없이 아버지를 위로하고 달래는 일이었다. 아버지의 어떤 기분도 마거릿에게서 선뜻 공감을 얻지 못하는 법이 없었고, 아버지의 어떤 바람도 그녀가 미리 헤아려 이루어 드리려 애쓰지 않는 법이 없었다. 물론 그것은 조용한 바람들이었고, 망설임과 미안함 없이는 입에 담기지도 않는 것들이었다. 그럴수록 그녀의 온순한 순종의 마음은 더욱 온전하고 아름다웠다. 3월에 프레더릭의 결혼 소식이 왔다. 그와 돌로레스가 나란히 편지를 썼다. 그녀는 당연하게도 스페인식 영어로 썼고, 그는 신부의 나라 말투가 그에게 얼마나 깊이 스며들고 있는지를 보여 주는 자잘한 어순과 표현의 뒤틀림을 섞어 썼다.\n\n사라진 증인들이 없는 마당에 군법회의에서 스스로를 밝힐 가망이 얼마나 희박한지를 알리는 헨리 레녹스의 편지를 받고서, 프레더릭은 마거릿에게 꽤나 격렬한 편지를 써 보냈다. 영국을 제 조국으로 여기기를 그만두겠다는 선언이 담긴 편지였다. 그는 자기가 그 나라 태생이 아니게 될 수 있기를 바랐고, 사면을 준다 해도 받지 않겠으며 허락을 받는다 해도 그 나라에서는 살지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그 모든 것이 마거릿을 몹시 울게 했으니, 처음 펼쳐 보았을 때에는 그것이 그녀에게 너무나 인정에 어긋나 보였던 것이다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 표현에서 오히려 그의 희망을 이렇게 짓뭉개 버린 실망의 사무침이 보였다. 그리하여 그녀는 참는 수밖에 다른 길이 없다고 느꼈다. 다음 편지에서 프레더릭은 앞날에 대해 어찌나 기쁘게 이야기했던지 지난 일은 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그리고 마거릿은 오빠를 위해 바라던 그 인내를 정작 자기 자신에게 쓸 곳이 있음을 알았다. 그녀야말로 참아야 할 터였다. 그러나 돌로레스의 어여쁘고 수줍은 소녀다운 편지들은 마거릿과 아버지 두 사람 모두에게 매력을 지니기 시작했다. 그 젊은 스페인 아가씨는 연인의 영국 친척들에게 좋은 인상을 주고 싶어 하는 마음이 너무나 뚜렷해서, 지워 고쳐 쓴 자리마다 여자다운 조심스러움이 내비쳤다. 그리고 결혼을 알리는 편지에는 한 번도 본 적 없는 시누이를 위해 돌로레스가 손수 고른 훌륭한 검은 레이스 만틸라가 함께 왔다. 프레더릭이 그 시누이를 아름다움과 지혜와 덕의 본보기라고 일러 준 터였다. 이 결혼으로 프레더릭의 세속적인 처지는 그들이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 높은 자리로 올라섰다. 바버 상회는 스페인에서 가장 큰 상사 가운데 하나였고, 그는 그곳에 젊은 동업자로 받아들여졌다. 마거릿은 장사를 두고 예전에 늘어놓던 열변이 새삼 떠올라 조금 웃었다가 이내 한숨을 지었다. 그녀의 늠름한 기사였던 오빠가 상인이, 장사꾼이 되다니! 그러나 곧 그녀는 자기 자신에게 반기를 들고, 스페인 상인과 밀턴 공장주를 한데 뒤섞는 그 생각에 소리 없이 항의했다. 그래, 장사면 어떻고 아니면 어떤가, 프레더릭은 아주아주 행복했다. 돌로레스는 분명 사랑스러운 사람일 테고, 만틸라는 더없이 아름다웠다! 그러고 나서 그녀는 지금의 삶으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그해 봄 아버지는 이따금 숨쉬기가 힘든 증세를 겪었고, 그럴 때마다 몹시 괴로워했다. 그 증세가 사이사이 말끔히 가시곤 했으므로 마거릿은 덜 놀랐다. 그러나 아버지가 그 병기를 아예 떨쳐 버리기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 그녀는 이번 4월에 옥스퍼드로 와 달라는 벨 씨의 초대를 받아들이시라고 몹시 채근했다. 벨 씨의 초대에는 마거릿도 들어 있었다. 아니, 그뿐 아니라 그는 그녀에게 꼭 오라고 명하는 특별한 편지까지 따로 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 아무 책임도 지지 않은 채 조용히 집에 남아, 지난 2년 남짓 도무지 할 수 없었던 방식으로 마음과 가슴을 쉬게 하는 편이 자기에게는 더 큰 안식이 되리라고 느꼈다.\n\n아버지가 철도역으로 가는 마차를 타고 떠나자, 마거릿은 자기의 시간과 정신에 얼마나 크고 오랜 압박이 있었는지를 실감했다. 스스로가 그토록 자유롭다고 느끼는 것은 놀랍다 못해 얼떨떨할 지경이었다. 뚜렷한 행복까지는 아니더라도 기운을 북돋우는 보살핌을 그녀에게 기대는 사람이 아무도 없었다. 계획을 세우고 생각을 기울여야 할 환자도 없었다. 그녀는 게을러도 되고 말이 없어도 되고 잊어버려도 되었다. 그리고 다른 모든 특권보다 더 값져 보이는 것, 곧 마음 내키면 불행해해도 되었다. 지난 몇 달 동안 그녀 자신의 온갖 근심과 괴로움은 어두운 벽장 속에 밀어 넣어 두어야 했다. 그러나 이제 그녀에게는 그것들을 꺼내 놓고 슬퍼하고, 그 본성을 살피고, 그것들을 평화의 요소로 다스릴 참된 방법을 찾을 여유가 있었다. 그 몇 주 내내 그녀는 그것들이 눈에 보이지 않게 감춰져 있었음에도 그 존재를 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었다. 이제 단번에 아주, 그녀는 그것들을 하나하나 살펴 자기 삶에서 저마다 마땅한 자리를 정해 줄 참이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 응접실에서 몇 시간이고 거의 꼼짝도 하지 않고 앉아, 움츠러들지 않는 결의로 기억마다의 쓰라림을 하나하나 되짚어 나갔다. 다만 한 번, 그 굴욕적인 거짓말을 낳은 믿음 없음을 떠올리며 찌르는 듯한 아픔에 소리 내어 울었다.\n\n그녀는 이제 그 유혹의 힘조차 인정하려 들지 않았다. 프레더릭을 위한 계획은 모두 어그러졌고, 유혹은 죽은 조롱으로, 애초에 생명이라고는 없었던 조롱으로 거기 놓여 있었다. 뒤이어 벌어진 일들에 비추어 보면 그 거짓말은 그토록 비열하고 어리석었으며, 진실의 힘에 대한 믿음이야말로 비할 데 없이 더 큰 지혜였던 것이다!\n\n신경이 곤두선 채로 그녀는 무심코 탁자 위에 놓인 아버지의 책 한 권을 펼쳤다. 그 안에서 눈에 들어온 구절은 사무치게 스스로를 낮추고 있는 지금의 그녀를 위해 마련된 것만 같았다.\n\n“나는 내 마음을 이런 식으로 나무라고 싶지는 않다. 부끄러워 죽어라, 눈먼 것아, 뻔뻔한 것아, 네 하느님을 배반하고 저버린 것아, 하는 식으로는. 나는 오히려 연민의 길로 그것을 고치고 싶다. 자, 나의 가엾은 마음아, 우리가 그토록 벗어나기로 굳게 마음먹었던 그 구덩이에 우리가 이렇게 빠지고 말았구나. 아! 다시 일어나자, 그리고 그곳을 영영 떠나자. 하느님의 자비를 구하고, 그 자비가 이제부터 우리를 도와 더 굳세게 서게 하시리라 바라자. 그리고 겸손의 길로 다시 돌아서자. 용기를 내자, 이제부터 우리 자신을 살피자, 하느님께서 우리를 도우시리라.”\n\n“겸손의 길. 아,” 마거릿은 생각했다. “그것이 바로 내가 놓쳐 버린 것이구나! 그러나 용기를 내렴, 작은 마음아. 우리는 되돌아설 것이고, 하느님의 도우심으로 잃어버린 길을 찾을 수 있으리라.”\n\n그리하여 그녀는 일어나, 자기 자신에게서 빠져나오게 해 줄 어떤 일에 당장 착수하기로 마음먹었다. 우선 그녀는 마사가 위층으로 올라가느라 응접실 문 앞을 지나갈 때 그녀를 불러 세워, 거의 기계적인 순종으로 제 개성을 딱딱하게 덮어 싸고 있는 그 진지하고 공손한 하인다운 태도 아래에 무엇이 있는지 알아보려 했다. 마사에게 자기 자신에 관한 이야기를 하게 만들기는 어려웠다. 그러나 마침내 손턴 부인의 이름을 대자 옳은 줄이 울렸다. 마사의 얼굴이 온통 환해졌고, 조금 부추기자 긴 이야기가 흘러나왔다. 자기 아버지가 젊은 시절 손턴 부인의 남편과 인연이 있었고, 아니 그에게 어떤 친절을 베풀 만한 처지이기까지 했다는 것이었다. 무슨 친절이었는지는 마사도 잘 알지 못했다. 자기가 아주 어린아이였을 때 있었던 일이기 때문이었다. 그리고 이런저런 사정이 끼어들어 두 집안은 마사가 거의 다 자랄 때까지 서로 떨어져 지냈는데, 그 무렵 창고 서기라는 본래의 일자리에서 아버지가 점점 더 아래로 가라앉고 어머니마저 세상을 떠나, 마사 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 그녀와 언니는 손턴 부인이 아니었더라면 “못쓰게 되었을” 것이라고 했다. 손턴 부인이 그들을 찾아내어 그들을 위해 생각해 주고 그들을 돌보아 주었던 것이다.\n\n“저는 열병을 앓고 나서 몸이 약했습니다. 그런데 손턴 부인이며 손턴 씨며, 저를 당신들 집에서 간호해 몸을 추스르게 하고 바닷가까지 보내 주시고 하기 전에는 도무지 쉬지를 않으셨어요. 의사는 그 열병이 옮는다고 했지만 그분들은 그런 건 아랑곳도 않으셨지요. 다만 패니 아가씨만은 달라서, 이번에 시집가실 그 댁에 가 계셨답니다. 그러니 그때는 겁을 내셨어도 결국은 다 잘된 셈이에요.”\n\n“패니 아가씨가 결혼을 하신다고요!” 마거릿이 소리쳤다.\n\n“네, 그것도 부자 신사분한테요. 다만 아가씨보다 나이가 한참 위지요. 왓슨 씨라고 하는데, 공장은 헤일리 너머 어디쯤에 있답니다. 머리가 그렇게 희끗희끗해도 아주 좋은 혼처예요.”\n\n이 소식에 마거릿은 한참 말이 없었고, 그동안 마사는 다시 제 몸가짐을 되찾았으며 그와 함께 늘 하던 대로 짧게 대답하는 버릇도 되찾았다. 마사는 난롯가를 쓸고, 차를 몇 시에 준비할지 물은 뒤, 들어올 때와 똑같이 나무토막 같은 얼굴로 방을 나갔다. 마거릿은 요즘 자기가 빠져든 나쁜 버릇, 곧 손턴 씨와 관련해 듣게 되는 일마다 그것이 그에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지, 그가 그것을 좋아할지 싫어할지 그려 보려는 버릇에 젖어 들지 않으려고 스스로를 다잡아야 했다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 어린 바우처 아이들을 데리고 공부를 시켰고, 오래 걸었으며, 마지막으로 메리 히긴스를 찾아갔다. 마거릿은 니컬러스가 벌써 일을 마치고 집에 와 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐다. 길어진 해가 저녁이 얼마나 늦었는지를 그녀에게 잘못 알려 준 것이었다. 그 역시 태도로 보아 겸손의 길에 조금은 들어선 듯했다. 그는 전보다 조용했고 제 주장을 덜 내세웠다.\n\n“그래, 그 노신사 어른이 여행을 떠나셨다지요?” 그가 말했다. “꼬맹이들이 나한테 그럽디다. 어유! 그것들 어찌나 야무진지, 우리 집 계집애들보다 더 똑똑한 것 같다는 생각까지 든다니까. 그런 말 하면 못쓰는 건지도 모르고, 게다가 하나는 무덤에 누워 있으니 더 그렇소만. 날씨에 뭔가가 있나 보오, 사람을 자꾸 떠돌게 만드는 뭔가가. 우리 공장주 양반도, 저기 공장에 있는 그 사람 말이오, 지금 세상 어디께를 빙빙 돌아다니고 있다지 뭐요.”\n\n“그래서 오늘 밤 이렇게 일찍 집에 계신 거예요?” 마거릿이 아무 뜻 없이 물었다.\n\n“댁은 그 일을 하나도 모르니 하는 소리요.” 그가 업신여기듯 말했다. “나는 얼굴이 둘인 사람이 아니외다. 공장주 앞에서 쓰는 얼굴 하나, 그 등 뒤에서 쓰는 얼굴 하나, 그런 사람 말이오. 나는 일을 놓기 전에 온 동네 시계가 치는 소리를 다 세었소. 아니지! 저 손턴이라는 사람은 맞붙어 싸울 만은 해도 속여먹기에는 너무 아까운 사람이오. 그 자리를 나한테 얻어 준 게 댁이었으니, 그건 고맙게 여기고 있소. 손턴네 공장은 요즘 세상에 나쁜 공장은 아니지. 내려서라, 얘야, 그리고 마거릿 아가씨한테 그 고운 찬송을 읊어 드려라. 옳지. 다리에 힘 딱 주고, 오른팔은 꼬챙이처럼 쭉 뻗고. 하나 서고, 둘 멈추고, 셋 준비, 넷 출발!”\n\n그 어린 녀석은 말뜻으로 보자면 제 이해를 훌쩍 넘어서는 감리교 찬송가 한 편을 읊었다. 그러나 그 흔들리는 가락이 아이의 귀에 붙어 있었고, 아이는 국회의원이나 됨 직한 완연한 억양으로 그것을 읊어 냈다. 마거릿이 마땅히 손뼉을 쳐 주자 니컬러스는 또 하나를, 다시 또 하나를 청했다. 그가 예전에는 비웃던 거룩한 것들에 이렇게 묘하게, 저도 모르게 마음을 두게 된 것을 알고 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n그녀가 집에 닿았을 때는 여느 때의 차 시간이 지나 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 아무도 자기를 기다리느라 붙들려 있지 않았다는 위안이 있었고, 다른 사람을 애태우며 살펴서 심각해야 할지 명랑해야 할지를 알아내는 대신 쉬면서 자기 생각을 할 수 있다는 위안이 있었다. 차를 마시고 나서 그녀는 큼직한 편지 꾸러미를 살펴 없애 버릴 것들을 골라내기로 했다.\n\n그 가운데서 그녀는 프레더릭의 일에 관한 헨리 레녹스 씨의 편지 네댓 통을 찾아냈다. 그리고 처음 시작할 때에는 오로지 오빠의 무죄를 밝힐 가망이 얼마나 가느다란 실낱에 걸려 있는지를 정확히 알아보려는 뜻으로 그것들을 꼼꼼히 다시 읽었다. 그러나 마지막 장을 다 읽고 이로운 점과 불리한 점을 저울질하고 나자, 그 편지들에 담긴 자그마한 인품의 드러남이 저절로 눈에 들어왔다. 문면의 뻣뻣함으로 보아, 레녹스 씨가 그 편지들의 사안에 어떤 관심을 느끼든 자기와 그녀 사이의 관계를 한 번도 잊은 적이 없다는 것이 충분히 뚜렷했다. 영리한 편지들이었다. 마거릿은 그것을 대번에 알아보았다. 그러나 그 편지들에는 진심 어린 따뜻한 기운이 없었다. 그래도 값진 것이니 간직해야 했다. 그래서 그녀는 그것들을 조심스레 한쪽에 놓아두었다. 이 자잘한 일이 끝나자 그녀는 생각에 잠겼다. 그리고 그날 밤 마거릿의 머릿속에는 멀리 있는 아버지 생각이 이상하리만치 맴돌았다. 그녀는 자기의 혼자 있음을, 따라서 아버지의 부재를 안식으로 느꼈던 것을 스스로 나무랄 뻔했다. 그러나 이 이틀이 그녀를 새로운 힘과 더 밝은 희망으로 다시 일으켜 세웠다. 얼마 전까지 짐처럼 보이던 계획들이 이제는 즐거움처럼 보였다. 병든 비늘이 그녀의 눈에서 떨어져 나가, 그녀는 제 처지와 제 할 일을 더 참되게 보았다. 손턴 씨가 잃어버린 우정을 그녀에게 되돌려 주기만 한다면, 아니 그가 예전처럼 이따금 찾아와 아버지의 마음을 밝혀 주기만 한다면, 설령 그녀가 그를 다시는 보지 못한다 해도 앞날의 삶은 눈부시지는 않을지언정 그녀 앞에 맑고 고르게 놓일 수 있을 것 같았다. 그녀는 잠자리에 들려고 일어서며 한숨을 쉬었다. “한 걸음이면 내게는 충분하다”는 말에도 불구하고, 아버지에게 헌신한다는 그 하나의 분명한 본분에도 불구하고, 그녀의 가슴속에는 불안과 슬픔의 아픔이 놓여 있었다.\n\n그리고 그 4월 저녁, 헤일 씨도 마거릿이 자기를 생각하는 것만큼이나 이상하고 끈질기게 마거릿을 생각하고 있었다. 그는 옛 친구들과 옛 낯익은 자리들을 두루 찾아다니느라 지쳐 있었다. 그는 자기 견해가 달라진 것이 친구들이 자기를 맞이하는 태도에 어떤 변화를 가져올지를 지나치게 부풀려 생각해 왔다. 그러나 그중 몇몇은 그가 신앙에서 떨어져 나갔다는 사실 자체에 충격을 받거나 마음 아파하거나 분개했을지 몰라도, 한때 사랑했던 사람의 얼굴을 보는 순간 그의 견해를 잊고 그 사람만을 보았다. 아니면 그 견해를 기억하더라도 다만 태도에 한층 더 부드러운 무게를 더할 만큼만 기억했다. 헤일 씨를 아는 사람은 많지 않았기 때문이다. 그는 작은 칼리지 가운데 하나에 속해 있었고 늘 수줍고 말이 없었다. 그러나 젊은 시절 그의 침묵과 우유부단함 아래에 놓인 섬세한 생각과 감정에까지 파고들 마음을 냈던 이들은 여자에게나 보였을 법한 감싸는 다정함으로 그를 가슴에 품었다. 그리고 여러 해가 흐르고 그토록 많은 변화의 세월이 사이에 놓인 뒤 이 다정함이 되살아난 것은, 어떤 거친 말이나 못마땅한 표현보다도 그를 더욱 무너뜨렸다.\n\n“우리가 너무 무리를 한 모양이네.” 벨 씨가 말했다. “자네가 그 밀턴 공기 속에서 그리 오래 산 탓에 지금 고생을 하는 걸세.”\n\n“피곤하긴 하네.” 헤일 씨가 말했다. “하지만 밀턴 공기 탓은 아닐세. 나는 55세야. 그 사소한 사실 하나만으로도 기운이 줄어든 것쯤은 다 설명이 되지.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리! 나는 60이 넘었어도 몸이든 마음이든 기운이 줄어든 것을 느끼지 못하네. 자네 입에서 그런 소리가 나오게 하지 말게. 55라니! 아니, 자네는 아주 젊은 사람일세.”\n\n헤일 씨는 고개를 저었다. “지난 몇 해가 말일세!” 그가 말했다. 그러나 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그는 벨 씨의 더없이 안락한 안락의자 하나에 반쯤 누워 있던 몸을 일으켜 세우고 어딘가 떨리는 간절함을 담아 말했다.\n\n“벨! 자네는 이렇게 생각해서는 안 되네. 내 견해가 바뀌고 성직록을 내놓은 데서 비롯될 모든 일을 내가 미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면, 아니, 그 사람이 얼마나 고통을 겪게 될지 알 수 있었더라 해도, 내가 그 일을, 곧 내가 사제로 있던 교회와 더는 같은 믿음을 지니지 않았음을 드러내어 밝힌 그 행위를 되돌리겠다고는 생각지 말게. 지금 생각해도, 내가 사랑하는 사람의 고통을 통해 겪게 될 그 더없이 잔인한 순교의 아픔을 미리 내다볼 수 있었다 해도, 교회를 드러내 놓고 떠나는 그 한 걸음에 관한 한 나는 똑같이 했을 걸세. 그 뒤로 가족을 위해 한 모든 일에서는 다르게, 더 지혜롭게 할 수도 있었겠지. 하지만 하느님이 내게 넘칠 만큼의 지혜나 힘을 주셨다고는 생각지 않네.” 그는 이렇게 덧붙이며 아까의 자세로 도로 몸을 뉘었다.\n\n벨 씨는 대답하기 전에 요란하게 코를 풀었다. 그러고서 말했다.\n\n“하느님은 자네 양심이 옳다고 이르는 바를 행할 힘을 자네에게 주셨네. 그리고 나는 우리에게 그보다 더 높거나 더 거룩한 힘이 필요하다고는 보지 않아. 지혜도 마찬가지고. 나에게 그만한 것이 없다는 건 내가 알지. 그런데도 사람들은 저희 바보 장부에 나를 지혜로운 사람이라고, 줏대 있는 인물이라고, 심지가 굳다느니 하는 그 온갖 허튼소리로 적어 놓는단 말일세. 제 앞의 깔개에 신발을 문질러 닦는 일에 지나지 않을망정 제 나름의 단순한 옳음의 법을 지키는 얼간이가 나보다 더 지혜롭고 더 강하네. 사람이란 어찌 이리도 잘 속아 넘어가는지!”\n\n잠시 말이 끊겼다. 헤일 씨가 제 생각을 이어 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n“마거릿 말인데.”\n\n“그래! 마거릿 말이지. 그래서?”\n\n“내가 죽으면……”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리!”\n\n“그 애가 어떻게 될까, 나는 자주 생각한다네. 레녹스네가 함께 살자고 청하겠지. 그러리라고 생각하려 애쓰네. 쇼 처형은 그 나름의 조용한 방식으로 그 애를 무척 사랑했지. 하지만 눈앞에 없는 사람을 사랑하는 일은 잊어버리는 사람이야.”\n\n“아주 흔한 허물이지. 레녹스네는 어떤 사람들인가?”\n\n“그 사람은 잘생기고 말주변 좋고 붙임성이 있네. 이디스는 곱게 자란 어여쁜 미인이고. 마거릿은 온 마음을 다해 이디스를 사랑하고, 이디스는 남는 만큼의 마음으로 마거릿을 사랑하지.”\n\n“여보게, 헤일. 자네도 알다시피 자네 그 딸아이가 내 마음을 거의 다 가져갔네. 전에도 자네한테 말했지. 물론 자네 딸이자 내 대녀이니, 지난번에 만나기 전부터도 나는 그 애에게 큰 관심이 있었네. 하지만 밀턴으로 자네를 찾아갔던 그 방문이 나를 그 애의 종으로 만들어 버렸어. 나는 기꺼운 늙은 제물이 되어 정복자의 수레를 따라갔지. 정말이지 그 애는 싸워 왔고 아마 지금도 싸우고 있으면서도 승리를 눈앞에 확실히 두고 있는 사람처럼 당당하고 고요해 보이거든. 그래, 지금 그 애가 안고 있는 온갖 근심에도 불구하고 얼굴에 어린 것은 바로 그런 표정이었네. 그러니 내가 가진 모든 것은 그 애가 필요로 한다면 그 애를 위한 것이고, 그 애가 원하든 원치 않든 내가 죽으면 그 애 것이 될 걸세. 게다가 나 자신이 그 애의 늠름한 기사가 되겠네, 60에 통풍까지 앓는 몸일망정. 진심일세, 오랜 벗이여, 자네 딸은 내 남은 생의 으뜸가는 소임이 될 것이고, 내 재치든 내 지혜든 내 기꺼운 마음이든 줄 수 있는 모든 도움은 그 애의 것이 될 걸세. 나는 그 애를 자네가 애태울 거리로 골라 주려는 게 아니야. 자네에게는 무언가 걱정거리가 있어야만 한다는 걸 내가 예전부터 알지. 그렇지 않으면 행복하지가 않으니까. 하지만 자네는 나보다 여러 해를 더 살 걸세. 자네처럼 마르고 여윈 사람들은 늘 죽음을 꾀어 놓고는 늘 속여 넘기지! 먼저 가는 건 언제나 나같이 살집 좋고 혈색 좋은 친구들이야.”\n\n벨 씨에게 앞을 내다보는 눈이 있었더라면, 그는 횃불이 이미 거의 거꾸로 뒤집힌 것을, 그리고 진지하고 고요한 얼굴의 천사가 아주 가까이 서서 제 벗을 손짓해 부르는 것을 보았을 것이다. 그날 밤 헤일 씨는 다시는 살아서 들지 못할 베개 위에 머리를 뉘었다. 아침에 그의 방으로 들어간 하인은 제 인사에 아무 대답도 듣지 못하자 침대 가까이 다가갔고, 지울 수 없는 죽음의 봉인 아래 희고 차갑게 누워 있는 고요하고 아름다운 얼굴을 보았다. 자세는 더없이 편안했고, 아픔도 몸부림도 없었다. 심장은 그가 몸을 눕히던 그 순간에 멎었음이 틀림없었다.\n\n벨 씨는 충격에 얼이 빠져 있다가, 하인이 무슨 말을 꺼낼 때마다 화를 낼 때가 되어서야 겨우 정신을 차렸다.\n\n“검시라고? 쳇. 자네는 내가 그를 독살했다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지! 포브스 의사는 그저 심장병의 자연스러운 끝이라고 하네. 가엾은 헤일! 자네는 그 여린 심장을 제때가 오기도 전에 다 닳게 만들었구먼. 가엾은 옛 벗! 그가 어젯밤에 제 딸 이야기를 얼마나…… 월리스, 5분 안에 내 여행 가방을 꾸리게. 나는 여기서 이렇게 떠들고만 있었군. 꾸리라니까. 다음 기차로 밀턴에 가야 하네.”\n\n가방이 꾸려지고 마차가 불려지고 철도역에 닿기까지, 이 결심을 한 때로부터 20분이 걸렸다. 런던행 기차가 휙 지나갔다가 몇 야드 뒤로 물러섰고, 성마른 차장이 벨 씨를 떠밀듯 태웠다. 그는 좌석에 몸을 던지고 눈을 감은 채, 어제까지 살아 있던 사람이 어떻게 오늘 죽어 있을 수 있는지를 이해해 보려 했다. 이윽고 희끗한 속눈썹 사이로 눈물이 새어 나왔고, 그것이 느껴지자 그는 날카로운 눈을 뜨고 굳게 다잡은 결심이 낼 수 있는 한도까지 짐짓 엄격하고 명랑한 얼굴을 지었다. 낯모르는 사람들 앞에서 훌쩍거릴 생각은 없었다. 그럴 그가 아니었다!\n\n낯모르는 사람들이라고 할 것도 없었다. 같은 쪽 저 멀리에 한 사람이 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 이윽고 벨 씨는 제 감정을 지켜보았을지 모를 사람이 어떤 사람인지 알아보려고 그쪽을 유심히 보았다. 그리고 활짝 펼쳐진 큼직한 타임스 신문 뒤에서 손턴 씨를 알아보았다.\n\n“아니, 손턴! 자넨가?” 그가 서둘러 가까운 자리로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다. 그는 손턴 씨의 손을 세차게 흔들었고, 이윽고 그 손아귀가 문득 풀렸다. 그 손으로 눈물을 닦아야 했기 때문이다. 그가 손턴 씨를 마지막으로 본 것은 벗 헤일과 함께 있는 자리에서였다.\n\n“나는 밀턴으로 가는 길일세, 서글픈 볼일을 안고서. 헤일의 딸에게 아버지가 갑작스레 세상을 떠났다는 소식을 전하러 가네!”\n\n“죽었다니요! 헤일 씨가 돌아가셨다고요!”\n\n“그렇다네. 나는 자꾸만 속으로 되뇐다네, ‘헤일이 죽었다!’ 하고. 하지만 그런다고 조금도 더 실감이 나지는 않아. 그래도 헤일은 죽었네. 어젯밤에는 보기에 멀쩡하게 잠자리에 들었는데, 오늘 아침 내 하인이 깨우러 갔을 때는 이미 싸늘하게 식어 있었어.”\n\n“어디서요? 도무지 모르겠습니다!”\n\n“옥스퍼드에서. 나한테 머물러 있으려고 왔었네. 17년 만에 처음 옥스퍼드에 온 것이었는데, 그 끝이 이 모양일세.”\n\n15분이 넘도록 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 그러다 손턴 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그분은!” 그러고는 뚝 멈추었다.\n\n“마거릿 말이지. 그래! 내가 가서 알릴 걸세. 가엾은 사람! 어젯밤 그의 생각은 온통 그 애로 가득했는데! 맙소사! 겨우 어젯밤이었어. 그런데 지금 그는 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 멀리 있는지! 하지만 나는 그를 위해서 마거릿을 내 자식으로 삼겠네. 어젯밤에 나는 그 애 자신을 위해 그 애를 맡겠다고 말했지. 그러니 이제 나는 두 사람 모두를 위해 그 애를 맡는 걸세.”\n\n손턴 씨는 이 말을 겨우 입 밖에 내기까지 한두 번 헛되이 말을 하려 애썼다.\n\n“그분은 어떻게 되는 겁니까?”\n\n“내 짐작에는 그 애를 기다리는 사람이 둘 있을 것 같네. 하나는 나고. 그런 보호자를 하나 들이고 내 살림을 차려서 마거릿을 딸로 두고 늘그막을 행복하게 보낼 수만 있다면, 나는 살아 있는 용이라도 집에 들여 함께 살겠네. 그런데 그 레녹스네가 있단 말일세!”\n\n“그들은 누구입니까?” 손턴 씨가 떨리는 관심으로 물었다.\n\n“아, 런던의 세련된 사람들이지. 십중팔구 자기들에게 그 애에 대한 가장 큰 권리가 있다고 여길 사람들이야. 레녹스 대위가 그 애의 사촌과 결혼했네. 그 애와 함께 자란 아가씨지. 어지간히 괜찮은 사람들이겠지. 그리고 그 애의 이모인 쇼 부인이 있고. 어쩌면 내가 그 훌륭한 부인에게 청혼을 하면 길이 열릴지도 모르지! 하지만 그건 참으로 궁여지책일 걸세. 그리고 또 그 동생이 있단 말이야!”\n\n“무슨 동생 말입니까? 그 이모의 동생인가요?”\n\n“아니, 아니. 영리한 쪽 레녹스일세. 대위는 얼간이라는 걸 알아 두게나. 마거릿에게 마음을 두려 드는 젊은 법정 변호사지. 그가 5년도 넘게 그 애를 마음에 품어 왔다는 걸 나는 알고 있네. 그의 친구 하나가 그만큼을 내게 일러 주었지. 다만 재산이 없어서 물러서 있었을 뿐이야. 이제 그 걸림돌이 없어질 걸세.”\n\n“어떻게요?” 손턴 씨가 물었다. 너무나 간절히 궁금한 나머지 그는 제 물음이 무례하다는 것도 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n“내가 죽으면 그 애가 내 재산을 갖게 되니까. 그리고 이 헨리 레녹스라는 사람이 그 애에게 절반이라도 걸맞은 사람이고 그 애도 그를 좋아한다면, 뭐! 그러면 나는 혼인을 통해 집을 얻을 또 다른 길을 찾아야 할지도 모르지. 방심한 틈에 그 애의 이모에게 넘어가고 말까 봐 나는 무섭기 짝이 없다네.”\n\n벨 씨도 손턴 씨도 웃을 기분이 아니었다. 그래서 앞사람이 한 말들 가운데 어떤 것이 얼마나 별났는지는 두 사람 다 알아채지 못했다. 벨 씨는 길게 새는 숨소리 말고는 아무 소리도 나지 않는 휘파람을 불었고, 자리를 옮겨 보았으나 편안함도 쉼도 찾지 못했다. 그동안 손턴 씨는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 앉아, 생각할 겨를을 얻으려고 집어 들었던 신문의 한 지점에 눈을 못 박고 있었다.\n\n“어디에 다녀오는 길인가?” 마침내 벨 씨가 물었다.\n\n“아브르에요. 면화 값이 크게 오른 까닭을 알아내 보려고요.”\n\n“어휴! 면화니 투기니 연기니, 잘 닦이고 잘 보살펴진 기계, 그리고 씻지도 못하고 돌봄도 받지 못하는 직공들이라니. 가엾은 헤일! 가엾은 헤일! 헬스톤에서 그리로 옮겨 온 것이 그에게 얼마나 큰 변화였는지 자네가 알 수 있었더라면. 자네 뉴 포리스트를 좀 아는가?”\n\n“압니다.” (아주 짧게.)\n\n“그럼 그곳과 밀턴의 차이를 짐작할 수 있겠군. 어느 쪽에 있었나? 헬스톤에 가 본 적이 있는가? 오덴발트의 어느 마을 같은 그림 같은 작은 마을 말일세. 헬스톤을 아는가?”\n\n“본 적이 있습니다. 그곳을 떠나 밀턴으로 오는 것은 큰 변화였겠지요.”\n\n그는 더 이상의 대화를 피하기로 작정한 듯 단호한 태도로 신문을 집어 들었다. 그리하여 벨 씨는 마거릿에게 이 소식을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 전할 수 있을까 궁리하던 아까의 일로 어쩔 수 없이 되돌아갔다.\n\n그녀는 위층 창가에 있었다. 그녀는 그가 마차에서 내리는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 본능이 번쩍이듯 진실을 알아차렸다. 그녀는 아래층으로 뛰어 내려가려던 첫 충동에 붙들린 듯, 그리고 바로 그 붙드는 생각에 돌로 변해 버린 듯 응접실 한가운데에 서 있었다. 그만큼 그녀는 희고 미동도 없었다.\n\n“아! 말씀하지 마세요! 얼굴만 봐도 알겠어요! 살아 계셨다면 사람을 보내셨겠지요. 살아 계셨다면 곁을 떠나오지 않으셨겠지요! 아, 아버지, 아버지!”\n\n# 제42장\n\n“그대에게 소리이자 감미로움이던 어느 사랑하는 목소리가 문득 끊기고, 감히 맞서 부르짖을 수도 없는 침묵이 새로 얻은 모진 병처럼 그대를 에워싸고 아려 올 때—무슨 희망이 있으랴? 무슨 도움이 있으랴? 어떤 음악이 그대의 감각에서 그 침묵을 풀어 주랴?” — 브라우닝 부인.\n\n충격은 컸다. 마거릿은 극도의 탈진 상태에 빠졌는데, 그것은 흐느낌이나 눈물로 드러나지도 않고 말로써 위안을 얻지도 못하는 종류의 것이었다. 그녀는 눈을 감은 채 소파에 누워, 누가 말을 걸 때가 아니면 입을 열지 않았고, 그때에도 속삭임으로만 대답했다. 벨 씨는 난감했다. 그녀를 두고 떠날 수도 없었고, 밀턴으로 오는 길에 세워 두었던 계획 가운데 하나였던 대로 자기와 함께 옥스퍼드로 가자고 청할 수도 없었다. 그녀가 마주해야 할 광경은 논외로 하더라도, 그런 고생을 감당하기에는 몸의 기진함이 너무나 완연했다. 벨 씨는 난롯가에 앉아 어떻게 하는 것이 좋을지 궁리했다. 마거릿은 그의 곁에서 꼼짝도 않고, 숨소리조차 들리지 않는 채로 누워 있었다. 딕슨이 아래층에 차려 놓고 흐느끼는 환대로 기어이 권하려 드는 저녁 식사에조차 그는 그녀를 두고 내려가려 하지 않았다. 그는 접시에 담은 음식을 위층으로 가져오게 했다. 평소의 그는 입이 꽤 까다롭고 세심하여 음식의 맛 하나하나를 잘 가려냈지만, 지금은 양념한 닭고기가 톱밥 같은 맛이었다. 그는 마거릿을 위해 닭고기를 잘게 다지고 후추와 소금을 잘 쳤다. 그러나 딕슨이 그의 지시대로 그녀에게 먹이려 하자, 힘없이 젓는 고갯짓이 지금 마거릿 같은 상태에서는 음식이 목을 메게 할 뿐 몸을 살리지는 못하리라는 것을 말해 주었다.\n\n벨 씨는 크게 한숨을 내쉬고는, 여행으로 뻣뻣해진 튼실한 늙은 팔다리를 편안한 자세에서 일으켜 딕슨을 따라 방을 나섰다.\n\n“그 애를 두고 갈 수가 없네. 옥스퍼드로 편지를 써서 준비가 되도록 해야겠어. 내가 갈 때까지 그쪽에서 일을 진행해 놓을 수 있겠지. 레녹스 부인이 저 애에게 와 줄 수는 없나? 내가 편지를 써서 꼭 와야 한다고 이르겠네. 저 아가씨 곁에는 여자 친구가 하나 있어야 해. 실컷 한바탕 울게 만들어 주기 위해서라도 말이야.”\n\n딕슨은 울고 있었다—두 사람 몫으로도 넉넉할 만큼. 그러나 눈물을 훔치고 목소리를 가다듬은 뒤, 그녀는 레녹스 부인이 해산이 너무 가까워 지금은 어떤 여행도 할 수 없다는 것을 벨 씨에게 겨우 전했다.\n\n“그래! 그렇다면 쇼 부인을 모셔 와야겠구먼. 그분은 영국으로 돌아오셨지, 안 그런가?”\n\n“네, 나리, 돌아오셨습니다. 하지만 이런 중요한 시기에 레녹스 부인 곁을 떠나고 싶어 하실 것 같지는 않은데요.” 딕슨이 말했다. 낯선 사람이 이 집에 들어와, 마거릿을 돌보는 자신의 지배권을 나누어 갖는 것이 그리 달갑지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“중요한 시기는 무슨 빌어먹을—.” 벨 씨는 문장 끝을 기침으로 덮으며 스스로를 억눌렀다. “지난번 그 ‘중요한 시기’ 때는—내 기억에 코르푸에서였지—베네치아나 나폴리, 아니면 그 교황쟁이들 사는 곳 어디쯤에 태평하게 머물러 계셨으면서. 게다가 그 팔자 좋은 부인네의 ‘중요한 시기’가 저기 저 가엾은 것에 비하면 대체 무슨 대수란 말인가—저 무력하고, 집도 없고, 의지할 이도 없는 마거릿 말일세. 소파에 저렇게 꼼짝없이 누워 있는 꼴이 꼭 석관 위에 얹힌 돌 조각상 같지 않은가. 내 말하는데, 쇼 부인은 반드시 오셔야 하네. 내일 밤까지 방이든 뭐든 그분께 필요한 것을 다 갖춰 놓게. 오시게 하는 건 내가 책임지지.”\n\n그리하여 벨 씨는 편지를 한 통 썼는데, 쇼 부인은 눈물을 쏟으며 그 편지가 돌아가신 장군이 통풍 발작을 일으키려 할 때 쓰던 것과 어찌나 똑같은지 평생 소중히 간직하겠노라고 말했다. 만일 그가 거절이 가능하기라도 한 듯 청하거나 권하는 식으로 선택의 여지를 주었더라면, 마거릿을 향한 그녀의 동정이 아무리 진실하고 참되었다 해도 그녀는 오지 않았을지도 모른다. 그 무뚝뚝하고 무례한 명령이 있고서야 그녀는 타고난 게으름의 관성을 이겨 내고, 하녀가 짐 상자를 다 꾸린 뒤 자신마저 꾸려 실려 가도록 몸을 맡길 수 있었다. 레녹스 대위가 장모를 마차까지 데려다주려 할 때, 모자에 숄에 눈물로 뒤범벅이 된 이디스가 층계참 꼭대기까지 나왔다.\n\n“잊지 마세요, 어머니. 마거릿은 꼭 우리와 함께 살러 와야 해요. 숄토가 수요일에 옥스퍼드에 갈 테니까, 언제쯤 오실지 벨 씨 편에 말씀을 전해 주세요. 그리고 숄토가 필요하시면 옥스퍼드에서 밀턴으로 곧장 갈 수 있어요. 잊지 마세요, 어머니. 마거릿을 데리고 돌아오셔야 해요.”\n\n이디스는 응접실로 다시 들어갔다. 그곳에는 헨리 레녹스 씨가 새로 나온 평론지의 책장을 자르고 있었다. 그는 고개도 들지 않은 채 말했다. “숄토가 그렇게 오래 곁을 비우는 게 싫으시다면, 이디스, 제가 밀턴에 내려가서 할 수 있는 도움을 드리게 해 주시죠.”\n\n“아, 고마워요.” 이디스가 말했다. “늙은 벨 씨가 하실 수 있는 건 다 해 주실 테고, 더 손이 필요하지는 않을 거예요. 다만 대학에 눌러앉은 펠로한테 무슨 세상 물정을 기대하기는 어렵겠지만요. 사랑하는 우리 마거릿. 그 애가 다시 여기 와 있으면 얼마나 좋을까요? 두 분은 몇 해 전에 아주 각별한 사이였잖아요.”\n\n“그랬던가요?” 그는 평론지의 어느 대목에 마음이 가 있는 듯한 기색으로 무심하게 물었다.\n\n“글쎄요, 아니었을지도 모르죠—잘 기억이 안 나요. 그땐 온통 숄토 생각뿐이었으니까. 그런데 이모부가 돌아가실 거였다면 하필 지금이라는 게 오히려 잘된 일 아닌가요? 우리가 귀국해서 옛집에 자리를 잡고, 마거릿을 맞을 준비가 다 되어 있는 바로 이때 말이에요. 가엾어라! 밀턴에 있다가 오면 얼마나 큰 변화일까요! 그 애 침실에는 새 사라사 천을 들여서 산뜻하고 화사하게 꾸며 주고, 기운도 좀 북돋아 줘야겠어요.”\n\n쇼 부인도 그와 똑같은 종류의 친절한 마음으로 밀턴을 향해 길을 떠났다. 이따금 첫 대면이 두려워지고 그 순간을 어떻게 넘길까 걱정도 되었지만, 그보다는 마거릿을 얼마나 빨리 “그 끔찍한 곳”에서 데리고 나와 할리 가의 안락한 살림 속으로 되돌려 놓을 수 있을까 하는 궁리를 더 자주 했다.\n\n“어머나!” 그녀가 하녀에게 말했다. “저 굴뚝들 좀 봐라! 가엾은 내 동생 헤일! 여기가 이런 곳인 줄 알았더라면 나폴리에서 마음 편히 지내지 못했을 게다. 진작 와서 동생과 마거릿을 데려갔어야 했어.” 그리고 속으로는 제부가 늘 좀 무른 사람이라고 여겨 왔지만, 그 아름다운 헬스톤의 집을 무엇과 맞바꾸었는지 눈으로 확인한 지금만큼 무르게 여겨진 적은 없다고 인정했다.\n\n마거릿은 여전히 같은 상태였다. 창백하고, 미동도 없고, 말이 없고, 눈물도 없었다. 쇼 이모가 온다는 이야기를 들었지만, 그녀는 놀라움도 기쁨도, 싫은 기색도 내비치지 않았다. 입맛이 돌아온 벨 씨는 그것을 채워 주려는 딕슨의 노력을 고맙게 여기면서도, 굴과 함께 조린 흉선 요리를 좀 들어 보라고 그녀에게 아무리 권해 보아도 소용이 없었다. 그녀는 전날과 똑같이 조용한 고집으로 고개를 저었다. 그는 그 거절을 스스로 위로하기 위해 그것을 혼자 다 먹는 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 이모를 기차역에서 태워 온 마차가 멎는 소리를 맨 먼저 들은 사람은 마거릿이었다. 그녀의 눈꺼풀이 떨렸고, 입술에 핏기가 돌며 떨렸다. 벨 씨가 쇼 부인을 맞으러 아래층으로 내려갔다. 두 사람이 올라왔을 때 마거릿은 어지러운 몸을 가누려 애쓰며 서 있었다. 그리고 이모를 보자 자신을 안으려 벌린 두 팔 속으로 걸어 들어가, 이모의 어깨에 기대어 비로소 눈물이라는 격렬한 위안을 찾았다. 조용하고 몸에 밴 사랑, 오랜 세월의 다정함, 죽은 이와 이어진 핏줄, 그리고 한 집안 사람에게만 있는 듯한 그 설명할 길 없는 눈빛과 목소리와 몸짓의 닮음—바로 그 순간 마거릿에게 어머니를 그토록 사무치게 떠올리게 한 그 모든 것이 밀려들어, 얼어붙었던 그녀의 마음을 녹이고 풀어 따뜻한 눈물이 넘쳐흐르게 했다.\n\n벨 씨는 방에서 살그머니 빠져나와 아래층 서재로 내려갔다. 그는 그곳에 불을 지피게 하고, 이런저런 책을 꺼내 들여다보며 생각을 딴 데로 돌리려 애썼다. 책 한 권 한 권이 죽은 벗의 기억이나 그를 떠올리게 하는 실마리를 가져왔다. 이틀 동안 마거릿을 지켜보던 일에서 하는 일이야 바뀌었을지 몰라도, 생각이 바뀐 것은 아니었다. 현관에서 안부를 묻는 손턴 씨의 목소리가 들려오자 그는 반가웠다. 딕슨은 그를 다소 매몰차게 돌려보내려 하고 있었다. 쇼 부인의 하녀가 나타나면서 지난날의 위세, 베러스퍼드 가의 핏줄, 그리고 자기 아가씨가 밀려났다가 이제 하느님 뜻이라면 되돌아가게 될 그 “지위”(그녀는 그렇게 부르기를 좋아했다)의 환영이 함께 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그녀는 쇼 부인의 하녀와 이야기를 나누며—그러는 사이 할리 가 살림살이의 격식과 위세에 관한 사정을 솜씨 좋게 죄다 캐내어, 옆에서 듣고 있던 마사를 교화하기도 하면서—이런 환영들을 흡족하게 곱씹던 참이라, 밀턴 사람이라면 누구에게든 거만하게 굴고 싶어졌다. 그래서 늘 손턴 씨 앞에서는 다소 주눅이 들던 그녀도, 오늘 밤에는 이 집 사람 누구도 만날 수 없다고 감히 낼 수 있는 최대한 퉁명스러운 말투로 일러 주었다. 그런데 벨 씨가 서재 문을 열고 이렇게 외치는 바람에 자기 말이 뒤집히고 만 것은 여간 민망한 일이 아니었다.\n\n“손턴인가? 자넨가? 잠깐만 들어오게. 자네한테 할 말이 있네.” 그리하여 손턴 씨는 서재로 들어갔고, 딕슨은 부엌으로 물러나 존 베러스퍼드 경이 주 장관이던 시절 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차 이야기를 거창하게 늘어놓음으로써 스스로에 대한 자존심을 되찾아야 했다.\n\n“막상 자네한테 무슨 말을 하려 했는지 모르겠구먼. 다만 온통 죽은 벗을 이야기하는 물건들뿐인 방에 앉아 있자니 여간 적적해야 말이지. 그렇다고 마거릿과 그 이모에게 응접실을 내주지 않을 수도 없고!”\n\n“그—이모님이 오셨습니까?” 손턴 씨가 물었다.\n\n“왔지! 하녀까지 데리고. 이런 때에는 혼자 올 법도 하건만! 그래서 이제 내가 나가서 클래런던 여관을 찾아가야 할 판이라네.”\n\n“클래런던에 가시면 안 됩니다. 저희 집에 빈 침실이 대여섯은 있습니다.”\n\n“바람은 잘 통하게 해 두었고?”\n\n“그건 저희 어머니를 믿으셔도 됩니다.”\n\n“그럼 위층에 잠깐 올라가서 그 핏기 없는 아가씨에게 잘 자라고 인사하고, 이모에게 인사도 드린 다음 자네와 곧장 나서겠네.”\n\n벨 씨는 위층에서 한참을 머물렀다. 손턴 씨는 시간이 길게 느껴지기 시작했다. 그는 일이 산더미 같아서 크램프턴까지 올라와 헤일 양의 안부를 묻는 데 쓸 시간조차 겨우 냈던 것이다.\n\n두 사람이 걷기 시작했을 때 벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n“응접실에서 여자들한테 붙들려 있었네. 쇼 부인은 딸 때문이라면서 어서 집에 돌아가고 싶어 하고, 마거릿을 당장 데려가려 하네. 그런데 저 애가 여행을 할 만한 몸이라면 나는 하늘도 날 수 있을 걸세. 게다가 저 애 말도 지당한 것이, 만나야 할 벗들이 있고 몇 사람에게는 작별 인사를 해야 한다는 게야. 그러자 이모가 오래된 인연을 들먹이며 옛 친구들은 잊었느냐고 저 애를 들볶더군. 그러니까 저 애가 한바탕 울음을 터뜨리면서, 그토록 많은 고통을 겪은 곳을 떠날 수 있다면 오히려 기쁘겠다고 하지 않겠나. 나는 내일 옥스퍼드로 돌아가야 하는데, 저울 어느 쪽에 내 목소리를 얹어야 할지 모르겠네.”\n\n그는 마치 묻기라도 하듯 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 동행에게서는 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았다. 그의 머릿속에서는 그 말의 메아리가 되풀이되고 있었다—\n\n“그토록 많은 고통을 겪은 곳.” 아아! 그에게는 그 쓰디쓴 밑바닥까지도—그리고 그 쓴맛이야말로 인생의 다른 모든 단맛을 합친 것보다 값졌는데—말할 수 없이 소중했던 밀턴에서의 그 열여덟 달이, 그녀에게는 그렇게 기억되는구나. 아버지를 잃는 일도, 손턴 씨에게 그토록 소중한 어머니를 잃는 일도, 그 몇 주, 그 며칠, 그 몇 시간의 기억만은 더럽히지 못했으리라. 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그녀에게 가까워지기에 즐거웠던 2마일의 길이 그를 그녀의 다정한 앞으로 데려다주던 시간, 그리고 그녀에게서 멀어지는 순간순간이 그녀의 몸가짐에서 새로운 우아함을, 성품에서 유쾌하게 톡 쏘는 맛을 자꾸만 떠올리게 해 주어 돌아오는 걸음마다 넉넉하던 시간을. 그렇다! 그녀와의 관계 바깥에서 그에게 무슨 일이 일어났든, 그녀를 날마다 볼 수 있었던 그때—말하자면 그녀가 손 닿는 곳에 있었던 그때—를 그는 결코 고통의 시간이라 부를 수 없었다. 그것은 온갖 가시와 모욕을 안고서도 그에게는 호사스러운 왕의 시절이었다. 슬금슬금 다가와 앞날에 대한 기대를 초라한 사실로 깎아내리고, 희망도 두려움도 없는 공기 속에 삶을 남겨 둔 지금의 빈곤에 비하면 말이다.\n\n손턴 부인과 패니는 식당에 있었다. 패니는 하녀가 번쩍이는 옷감을 하나씩 들어 올려 촛불 아래서 혼례복이 어떻게 보이는지 살펴 주는 동안 자잘한 우쭐함으로 들떠 있었다. 어머니는 정말로 딸의 마음에 맞춰 주려 애썼지만 그럴 수가 없었다. 취향이니 옷차림이니 하는 것은 그녀의 관심사가 아니었고, 그녀는 패니가 모든 것을 제 손으로 고르고 감독하겠다고 고집하는 바람에 생기는 끝없이 성가신 상의와 갈팡질팡하는 마음 바꾸기 없이, 런던의 일류 재봉사에게 혼수옷 일체를 맡기라는 오라비의 제안을 딸이 받아들였더라면 하고 진심으로 바랐다. 손턴 씨로 말하자면, 패니의 이류짜리 애교와 우아 떠는 태도에 홀릴 만큼 분별 있는 남자에게 고마운 마음을 표시할 기회를 얻은 것이 그저 반가웠기에, 누이가 마음껏 치장거리를 장만할 수 있도록 돈을 넉넉히 대 주었다. 패니의 마음속에서 그 치장거리들은 신랑감을 넘어서지는 못할지언정 적어도 어깨를 겨루기는 하는 것이 분명했다. 오라비와 벨 씨가 들어서자 패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 새침하게 웃고는, 자기가 하던 일의 흔적들 위로 부산을 떨었다. 벨 씨가 아닌 다른 사람이었다면 누구라도 눈길을 주지 않을 수 없었을 만한 태도였다. 그가 패니와 그 비단과 공단을 조금이라도 생각했다면, 그것은 그것들을 자기가 두고 온 창백한 슬픔과 견주어 보기 위해서였을 뿐이다. 고개를 숙이고 두 손을 포갠 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있던 그 슬픔, 그리고 어찌나 고요한지 곤두선 귓속에서 울리는 소리마저 아직 사랑하는 이의 곁을 맴도는 죽은 자들의 영혼이 내는 것이 아닌가 싶을 지경이던 그 방을. 벨 씨가 처음 위층에 올라갔을 때 쇼 부인은 소파에 누워 잠들어 있었고, 어떤 소리도 그 고요를 깨뜨리지 않았던 것이다.\n\n손턴 부인은 벨 씨에게 격식 있는 환대의 인사를 건넸다. 그녀는 아들의 집에서 아들의 벗을 맞이할 때만큼 상냥한 적이 없었다. 그리고 손님이 뜻밖일수록, 편안함을 위해 빈틈없이 갖춰 둔 그녀의 살림 솜씨에 돌아오는 영예도 커지는 법이었다.\n\n“헤일 양은 어떻습니까?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“이번 일격에 무너질 수 있는 데까지 무너졌지요.”\n\n“그 아가씨에게 선생 같은 벗이 있다는 건 참으로 다행한 일입니다.”\n\n“내가 그 애의 유일한 벗이었으면 좋겠소이다, 부인. 아주 매정하게 들리겠지만, 나로 말하자면 어디서 굴러온 귀부인 이모한테 위로자며 조언자 자리에서 밀려나 쫓겨난 신세라오. 게다가 런던에는 사촌이니 뭐니 하는 것들이 그 애를 제 집 애완견이라도 되는 양 제 것이라 주장하고 있고 말이오. 그런데 그 애는 너무 약해지고 비참해져서 제 뜻이라는 걸 세울 힘도 없구려.”\n\n“정말이지 약해졌겠군요.” 손턴 부인이 말했는데, 그 속뜻을 아들은 잘 알아들었다. “그런데 말이지요,” 손턴 부인이 말을 이었다. “헤일 양이 벗 하나 없는 사람처럼 지내고, 또 분명 적잖은 걱정을 견뎌 내는 동안 그 친척이라는 사람들은 다들 어디 있었답니까?” 그러나 그녀는 자기가 던진 물음의 답을 기다릴 만큼 관심이 있지도 않았다. 그녀는 집안일을 챙기러 방을 나갔다.\n\n“외국에 나가 살았지요. 그래도 그 애에 대해 어느 정도 연고를 주장할 만은 하오. 그 점은 인정해 주리다. 이모가 그 애를 길렀고, 그 애와 사촌은 자매나 다름없이 지냈으니까. 다만 나를 언짢게 하는 건 말이오, 나는 그 애를 내 자식으로 삼고 싶었는데, 제 권리의 고마움도 모르는 듯한 저 사람들에게 질투가 난다는 거요. 프레더릭이 그 애를 제 것이라 한다면 이야기가 다르겠지만.”\n\n“프레더릭이라니!” 손턴 씨가 소리쳤다. “그게 누굽니까? 무슨 권리로——” 그는 격해진 물음을 뚝 끊었다.\n\n“프레더릭 말일세.” 벨 씨가 놀라며 말했다. “아니, 자네 모르나? 그 애 오라비일세. 못 들었단 말인가——”\n\n“그런 이름은 처음 듣습니다. 어디 있습니까? 어떤 사람입니까?”\n\n“그 집안이 처음 밀턴에 왔을 때 내가 분명 그 아이 이야기를 했을 텐데—그 반란에 연루된 아들 말이야.”\n\n“이 순간까지 한 번도 들은 적이 없습니다. 어디 삽니까?”\n\n“스페인일세. 영국 땅에 발을 딛는 순간 체포당할 몸이지. 딱한 녀석! 제 아비 장례에 참석할 수 없으니 얼마나 애가 타겠나. 우리로서는 레녹스 대위로 만족하는 수밖에. 달리 친척이라고는 아는 이가 없으니.”\n\n“저도 참석해도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“물론이지, 고마운 일일세. 자네도 알고 보면 좋은 사람이야, 손턴. 헤일이 자네를 좋아했네. 요전 날에도 옥스퍼드에서 내게 자네 이야기를 하더군. 요즘 자네를 너무 못 봐서 아쉽다고 말이야. 그를 예우하겠다니 나로서는 고맙네.”\n\n“그런데 프레더릭 말입니다. 그는 영국에 한 번도 오지 않았습니까?”\n\n“한 번도.”\n\n“헤일 부인이 돌아가실 무렵에도 이곳에 와 있지 않았습니까?”\n\n“아닐세. 아니, 그때는 내가 여기 있었네. 몇 해, 아니 여러 해 동안 헤일을 못 보다가, 자네도 기억하겠지만 내가 온 게—아니, 내가 온 건 그로부터 한참 뒤였지. 아무튼 가엾은 프레더릭 헤일은 그때 여기 없었네. 왜 그가 있었다고 생각했나?”\n\n“어느 날 한 젊은이가 헤일 양과 걷고 있는 것을 보았습니다.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “그리고 그게 그 무렵이었던 것 같습니다.”\n\n“아, 그건 그 젊은 레녹스일 걸세. 대위의 동생 말이야. 변호사인데, 그 집안과 꽤 꾸준히 편지를 주고받았지. 헤일 씨가 그 사람이 내려올 것 같다고 하던 게 기억나는군. 그런데 말일세,” 벨 씨는 몸을 홱 돌리고 한쪽 눈을 감았다. 다른 쪽 눈의 힘을 온통 모아 손턴 씨의 얼굴을 날카롭게 살피기 위해서였다. “한때 나는 자네가 마거릿에게 조금은 마음이 있는 줄로 여겼다네.”\n\n대답이 없었다. 낯빛에도 아무 변화가 없었다.\n\n“가엾은 헤일도 그렇게 여겼지. 처음부터는 아니고, 내가 그 생각을 심어 준 뒤에야 그랬지만.”\n\n“저는 헤일 양을 흠모했습니다. 누구라도 그럴 수밖에 없지요. 아름다운 사람이니까요.” 벨 씨의 집요한 물음에 몰린 손턴 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그게 다인가! 자네는 그 애를 그런 잰 듯한 말투로, 그저 ‘아름다운 사람’이라고—눈길이나 끄는 무엇이라고 말할 수 있단 말이지. 나는 자네에게 마음의 경의를 바칠 만한 고귀함이 있기를 정말로 바랐네. 하기야 나는 믿네—아니, 사실 알고 있네, 그 애가 자네를 거절했으리라는 걸. 그렇더라도 보답 없이 그 애를 사랑했다면, 여태 그 애를 사랑해 본 이가 누구든 간에 그 모든 이들보다 자네가 더 높은 곳에 올랐을 걸세. ‘아름다운 사람’이라니, 원! 자네는 말이나 개를 두고 말하듯 그 애를 말하는 겐가?”\n\n손턴 씨의 두 눈이 벌겋게 달아오른 잉걸불처럼 이글거렸다.\n\n“벨 씨,” 그가 말했다. “그렇게 말씀하시기 전에, 세상 모든 사람이 어르신처럼 제 감정을 자유로이 드러낼 수 있는 것은 아니라는 점을 기억해 주셔야 합니다. 다른 이야기를 하시지요.” 벨 씨가 한 말 한마디 한마디에 나팔 소리를 들은 듯 그의 가슴이 뛰어올랐고, 방금 들은 그 말들이 앞으로 저 늙은 옥스퍼드 펠로에 대한 생각을 제 마음속 가장 귀한 것들과 단단히 묶어 놓으리라는 것을 알았지만, 그럼에도 그는 마거릿을 향한 제 감정을 어떤 말로도 억지로 드러내려 하지 않았다. 그는 남이 제가 우러르고 애타게 사랑하는 것을 치켜세운다고 해서 그보다 더 크게 칭송하려 드는, 남의 찬사나 흉내 내는 앵무새가 아니었다. 그리하여 그는 지주와 세입자로서 벨 씨와 자기 사이에 놓인 무미건조한 사업 이야기로 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n“마당에서 부딪칠 뻔한 저 벽돌하고 회 반죽 더미는 뭔가? 수리할 데라도 있나?”\n\n“아닙니다, 없습니다. 고맙습니다.”\n\n“자네 돈으로 무얼 짓고 있는 건가? 그렇다면 나야 아주 고마운 일이지.”\n\n“식당을 짓고 있습니다—사람들을 위해서요, 그러니까 직공들 말입니다.”\n\n“독신 사내인 자네한테 이 방으로도 성에 차지 않는다면 자네 입맛이 여간 까다로운 게 아니라고 생각하던 참이었네.”\n\n“제가 좀 별난 친구 하나를 알게 되었는데, 그 사람이 마음을 쓰는 아이 한둘을 학교에 넣어 주었습니다. 그래서 어느 날 그 집 근처를 지나는 길에 얼마 안 되는 돈을 치를 일이 있어 들렀지요. 그런데 시커멓게 타 버린 초라한 저녁 식사를—기름 덩어리처럼 눌어붙은 고기 한 점을 보고서 처음으로 생각이란 걸 하게 되었습니다. 그러나 이번 겨울에 식료품값이 그토록 뛰고 나서야, 물건을 도매로 사들이고 많은 양을 한꺼번에 조리하면 돈도 많이 아끼고 편의도 크게 얻을 수 있겠다는 데에 생각이 미쳤습니다. 그래서 제 친구—아니 제 적이라고 해야 하나—아까 말씀드린 그 사람에게 이야기를 꺼냈더니, 제 계획의 세세한 대목마다 트집을 잡더군요. 그래서 저는 그 계획을 접어 두었습니다. 실행이 어렵기도 하거니와, 억지로 밀어붙인다면 제 사람들의 독립성을 침해하는 셈이 될 테니까요. 그런데 느닷없이 이 히긴스라는 사람이 저를 찾아와서는, 제 것이라고 주장해도 좋을 만큼 제 것과 거의 똑같은 계획을 황송하게도 승인해 주더군요. 게다가 자기가 이야기해 둔 동료 직공 여럿의 승인까지 말입니다. 솔직히 그 태도에 좀 ‘부아가’ 나서, 죄다 물속에 내던져 뜨든 가라앉든 내버려 둘까 하는 생각도 했습니다. 하지만 한때 현명하고 잘 짜였다고 여겼던 계획을, 단지 그것을 처음 낸 사람으로서 마땅한 영예와 대접을 제가 다 받지 못한다는 이유로 내던지는 건 어린애 같은 짓 같았습니다. 그래서 저는 제게 맡겨진 몫을 담담히 떠맡았지요. 클럽의 관리인쯤 되는 자리랄까요. 저는 식료품을 도매로 사들이고, 알맞은 관리인이나 요리사를 구해 주는 겁니다.”\n\n“새 직분에서도 만족스러운 평판을 얻고 있기를 바라네. 자네가 감자와 양파를 볼 줄은 아는가? 하기야 장 보는 일이야 손턴 부인이 거들어 주시겠지.”\n\n“전혀 아닙니다.” 손턴 씨가 대답했다. “어머니는 그 계획 전체를 못마땅해하시고, 이제 우리는 서로 그 이야기를 입에 올리지도 않습니다. 하지만 저는 꽤 잘해 나가고 있어요. 리버풀에서 물건을 대량으로 들여오고, 정육은 우리 집에 대는 푸줏간에서 받고 있습니다. 장담하는데, 그 관리인이 내놓는 따뜻한 밥상은 얕볼 만한 것이 아닙니다.”\n\n“직분에 걸맞게 들어가는 요리마다 맛을 보나? 흰 지팡이라도 하나 짚었으면 좋겠구먼.”\n\n“처음에는 아주 조심스럽게 물건 사는 일에만 저를 한정했고, 그것마저도 제 판단대로 하기보다는 가정부를 통해 전해지는 사람들의 주문에 따랐습니다. 어떤 때는 쇠고기가 너무 크다고 하고, 또 어떤 때는 양고기에 기름기가 모자란다고 하더군요. 제가 그들을 자유롭게 두려고, 제 생각을 그들에게 들이밀지 않으려고 얼마나 조심하는지 그들도 알아보았던 모양입니다. 그래서 어느 날 두세 사람이—그중에는 제 친구 히긴스도 있었지요—저에게 들어와서 한술 뜨지 않겠느냐고 묻더군요. 무척 바쁜 날이었지만, 그들이 먼저 다가온 마당에 제가 절반쯤이라도 마주 나아가지 않으면 마음이 상하겠구나 싶어서 들어갔습니다. 그리고 평생 그보다 좋은 식사를 해 본 적이 없습니다. 저는 그들에게—그러니까 제 양옆에 앉은 사람들에게 말이지요, 저는 연설 같은 건 못하니까요—얼마나 맛있게 먹었는지 이야기했습니다. 그 뒤로 한동안은 그 특별한 요리가 식단에 오르는 날이면 어김없이 그 사람들이 저를 붙들고 이렇게 말하곤 했지요. ‘공장주님, 오늘 저녁은 핫포트인데 들어오시려우?’ 그들이 청하지 않았다면, 저는 초대도 없이 병영 식당에 끼어드는 것만큼이나 그들 자리에 끼어들 생각이 없었습니다.”\n\n“자네는 자네를 부른 사람들의 이야기판에 꽤나 걸림돌이 되겠구먼. 자네가 있으면 공장주들 흉을 볼 수가 없으니 말이야. 아마 핫포트 없는 날에 그 몫까지 실컷 풀어놓을 걸세.”\n\n“글쎄요! 지금까지는 말썽 나는 문제들은 다 비껴갔습니다. 하지만 옛날의 그 다툼거리가 다시 나온다면, 다음 핫포트 날에는 저도 분명 제 생각을 털어놓을 겁니다. 그런데 어르신은 다크셔 사람이면서도 우리 다크셔 사내들을 잘 모르시는군요. 그들에게는 대단한 해학이 있고, 얼마나 구수한 말솜씨가 있는지 모릅니다! 이제야 저도 몇 사람을 정말로 알아 가고 있는데, 그들도 제 앞에서 꽤 거리낌 없이 이야기합니다.”\n\n“사람을 고르게 만드는 데는 먹는 일만 한 것이 없지. 죽는 일도 거기에는 못 미치네. 철학자는 잠언을 읊으며 죽고, 바리새인은 남의 눈을 의식하며 죽고, 마음이 단순한 자는 겸손하게 죽고, 가엾은 바보는 참새가 땅에 떨어지듯 아무것도 모른 채 죽지. 그러나 철학자든 바보든, 세리든 바리새인이든 소화력만 똑같이 좋다면 먹는 모양새는 다 한가지라네. 자네 이론에 맞서는 내 이론일세!”\n\n“저는 정말이지 이론이라고는 없습니다. 저는 이론이라면 질색입니다.”\n\n“미안하네. 뉘우치는 뜻으로, 장 보는 데 보태라고 10파운드 지폐 한 장을 받아 주겠나? 그 딱한 사람들에게 잔치라도 한번 베풀어 주게.”\n\n“고맙습니다만, 사양하겠습니다. 그들은 공장 뒤편의 화덕과 조리 시설에 대해 제게 세를 내고 있고, 새 식당에 대해서는 더 많이 내야 할 겁니다. 저는 이 일이 자선으로 굴러떨어지는 걸 원하지 않습니다. 기부도 원하지 않습니다. 한번 그 원칙을 들이면, 사람들이 드나들며 이러쿵저러쿵 말을 만들어 이 일의 단순함을 망쳐 놓을 테니까요.”\n\n“새로운 일에는 무슨 일이든 사람들이 말을 하는 법일세. 그건 어쩔 수 없어.”\n\n“제게 적이 있다면, 그들이야 이 식사 계획을 두고 박애니 뭐니 하며 소란을 피울 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 어르신은 벗이시니, 이제부터는 제 시도에 침묵이라는 예우를 베풀어 주시리라 기대합니다. 아직은 새 빗자루라 제법 잘 쓸릴 뿐입니다. 하지만 머지않아 걸림돌을 숱하게 만나게 되겠지요.”\n\n# 제43장\n\n“우리가 작별을 고하는 가장 하찮은 것도 헤어지는 그 시각에는 그 하찮음을 잃는다.” — 엘리엇.\n\n쇼 부인은 그 온순한 천성으로 지을 수 있는 한 가장 격렬한 반감을 밀턴에 품었다. 밀턴은 시끄럽고 매캐했으며, 거리에서 보이는 가난한 사람들은 꾀죄죄했고 부유한 부인들은 지나치게 차려입었으며, 위아래를 막론하고 그녀가 본 남자들 가운데 제 몸에 맞게 지은 옷을 걸친 이가 하나도 없었다. 마거릿이 밀턴에 머무는 한 잃어버린 기력을 결코 되찾지 못하리라고 그녀는 확신했다. 게다가 자기도 예전의 신경 발작이 도질까 겁이 났다. 마거릿은 자기와 함께 돌아가야 한다, 그것도 서둘러. 그녀가 쓴 말이 정확히 그러하지는 않았더라도 마거릿에게 채근한 바의 정신은 적어도 그러했다. 그리하여 마거릿은 쇠약하고 지치고 기가 꺾인 채, 수요일만 지나면 이모를 따라 런던으로 갈 채비를 하겠노라고 마지못한 약속을 내주었다. 청구서를 치르고 가구를 처분하고 집을 닫아거는 온갖 뒤처리는 딕슨에게 맡기기로 하고. 그 수요일이 오기 전에—헤일 씨가 생전에 알던 두 집 어느 쪽에서도 멀리 떨어진 곳에, 낯선 이들 틈에 홀로 누운 아내에게서도 멀리 떨어진 곳에 묻히게 될 그 서글픈 수요일이 오기 전에(그리고 이 마지막 대목이 마거릿의 큰 괴로움이었다. 처음 며칠의 그 압도적인 망연자실에 몸을 내맡기지만 않았더라면 일을 달리 처리할 수도 있었으리라 여겼기 때문이다)—마거릿은 벨 씨에게서 편지 한 통을 받았다.\n\n“친애하는 마거릿에게—나는 목요일에 밀턴으로 돌아갈 작정이었네만, 공교롭게도 우리 플리머스 펠로들이 무슨 직무라도 수행하라고 불려 나가는 드문 경우 가운데 하나가 하필 그때라, 자리를 비울 수가 없게 되었네. 레녹스 대위와 손턴 씨가 여기 와 있네. 앞사람은 영리하고 선의를 지닌 사람으로 보이는데, 밀턴으로 건너가 유언장을 찾는 일이라면 무엇이든 도와주겠다고 나섰네. 물론 유언장은 없네. 있었다면 내가 일러 준 대로 했을 테니 지금쯤 벌써 찾아냈을 걸세. 그러고는 대위가 자네와 장모를 집으로 데려가야 한다고 우기는데, 아내가 지금 저런 형편이니 그가 금요일보다 더 오래 떠나 있으리라고는 기대하기 어렵겠지. 하지만 자네의 그 딕슨은 믿을 만하니, 내가 갈 때까지 제 자리든 자네 자리든 지켜 줄 수 있을 걸세. 유언장이 없다면 밀턴의 내 변호사에게 일을 넘기겠네. 이 영리한 대위가 사무에 밝은 사람 같지는 않으니 말일세. 그래도 콧수염 하나는 훌륭하더군. 경매를 붙여야 할 테니 남겨 두고 싶은 물건을 골라 두게. 아니면 나중에 목록을 보내도 되네. 이제 두 가지만 더 말하면 내 할 말은 끝이네. 자네도 알 테고, 모른다 해도 자네의 가엾은 아버지는 알고 있었네만, 내가 죽으면 내 돈과 재물은 자네 것이 되네. 아직 죽을 작정이라는 말은 아닐세. 다만 앞으로 할 말을 설명하려고 짚어 두는 걸세. 저 레녹스 사람들은 지금 자네를 무척 아끼는 듯하고, 앞으로도 그럴지 모르지. 아닐 수도 있고. 그러니 처음부터 격식을 갖춘 약조로 시작하는 편이 낫네. 곧, 자네와 그들이 함께 사는 것을 서로 좋게 여기는 동안 자네가 그들에게 해마다 250파운드를 치르는 걸세. (물론 여기에는 딕슨 몫도 들었네. 그 사람 몫으로 더 내라는 말에 넘어가지 않도록 조심하게.) 그러면 언젠가 대위가 제 집을 저희끼리만 쓰고 싶어 하더라도 자네가 내쫓기는 신세가 되지 않고, 자네 몸과 자네의 250파운드를 챙겨 다른 데로 옮겨 갈 수 있네. 하기야 그 전에 내가 먼저 와서 살림을 맡아 달라고 자네를 데려가지 않는다면 말일세. 그다음 옷이며 딕슨이며 개인 비용이며 과자 값은(젊은 아가씨는 나이 들어 철이 들 때까지 다들 과자를 먹는 법이지) 내가 아는 부인 한 분께 여쭈어 보고, 자네가 아버지에게서 얼마나 받게 될지 알아본 뒤에 정하겠네. 자, 마거릿, 여기까지 읽기도 전에 발끈해서 저 늙은이가 무슨 권리로 이렇게 함부로 남의 일을 정해 주느냐고 따지지는 않았는가? 그랬으리라고 나는 믿어 의심치 않네. 그러나 늙은이에게는 권리가 있네. 그는 자네 아버지를 35년 동안 사랑했고, 혼례날 그의 곁에 섰으며, 죽음의 자리에서 그의 눈을 감겨 주었네. 게다가 그는 자네의 대부일세. 그리고 그런 일에서는 자네가 자기보다 낫다는 것을 속으로 알고 있어 영적으로는 자네에게 크게 보탬이 될 수 없으니, 물질로나마 자네를 넉넉하게 해 주는 변변찮은 선행이라도 기꺼이 하고 싶은 걸세. 게다가 늙은이에게는 이 세상에 알려진 친척이 하나도 없네. ‘애덤 벨을 위해 슬퍼해 줄 이가 어디 있는가?’ 그의 온 마음이 이 한 가지에 붙박여 기울어 있으니, 마거릿 헤일은 그에게 아니라고 말할 아가씨가 아닐세. 답장은 곧바로 부치게. 단 두 줄이라도 좋으니 자네 답을 알려 주게. 다만 고맙다는 말은 말고.”\n\n마거릿은 펜을 들어 떨리는 손으로 “마거릿 헤일은 그에게 아니라고 말할 아가씨가 아닙니다.”라고 갈겨썼다. 쇠약한 상태라 다른 말을 떠올릴 수가 없었지만, 그래도 이 말을 쓰는 것이 언짢았다. 그러나 이 사소한 수고만으로도 어찌나 지쳤던지, 설령 다른 형태의 승낙을 생각해 낼 수 있었더라도 그것을 한 자라도 쓰려고 몸을 일으켜 앉아 있지는 못했을 것이다. 그녀는 다시 자리에 누워 아무 생각도 하지 않으려 애쓸 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“내 사랑하는 아가! 그 편지가 널 언짢게 하거나 속상하게 하더냐?”\n\n“아니에요!” 마거릿이 힘없이 말했다. “내일만 지나면 나아질 거예요.”\n\n“아무래도 이 끔찍한 공기에서 널 데리고 나가기 전에는 나아지지 않을 게 틀림없다, 얘야. 이 년 동안 어떻게 견뎠는지 나는 짐작도 안 간다.”\n\n“제가 어디로 갈 수 있었겠어요? 아버지 어머니를 두고 떠날 수는 없었잖아요.”\n\n“그래, 속상해하지 마라, 얘야. 다 그게 최선이었겠지. 다만 네가 어떻게 살고 있는지 나는 짐작도 못 했구나. 우리 집사 아내도 이보다는 나은 집에 산단다.”\n\n“여기도 가끔은 아주 예뻐요—여름이면요. 지금 모습만 보고 판단하실 수는 없어요. 저는 여기서 아주 행복했어요.” 그러고서 마거릿은 이야기를 그만두려는 뜻으로 눈을 감았다.\n\n집은 예전에 견주면 이제 안락함으로 넘쳐 났다. 저녁이면 쌀쌀했으므로 쇼 부인의 지시로 침실마다 불이 지펴졌다. 그녀는 온갖 방법으로 마거릿을 어루만졌고, 자기 같으면 파묻혀 위안을 찾았을 진미며 보드라운 사치품을 죄다 사들였다. 그러나 마거릿은 그 모든 것에 무심했다. 혹 그것들이 억지로 그녀의 눈에 들어오더라도, 그것은 오직 자기를 생각하느라 그토록 애를 쓰는 이모에게 고마움을 느낄 까닭이 될 뿐이었다. 그토록 쇠약한데도 그녀는 가만히 있지 못했다. 하루 종일 그녀는 옥스퍼드에서 치러지고 있을 예식을 생각하지 않으려고 이 방 저 방을 거닐며 간직하고 싶은 물건들을 힘없이 따로 골라 놓았다. 딕슨은 쇼 부인의 뜻에 따라 그녀를 좇아다녔다. 겉으로는 지시를 받으려는 것이었으나, 되도록 빨리 그녀를 달래어 쉬게 하라는 은밀한 분부를 받은 터였다.\n\n“이 책들은, 딕슨, 내가 간직할게요. 나머지는 전부 벨 씨께 보내 주겠어요? 그분이 책 자체로도, 또 아버지를 생각해서도 소중히 여기실 만한 것들이에요. 이건—— 내가 떠난 뒤에 이걸 손턴 씨께 가져다 드렸으면 해요. 잠깐만요. 쪽지를 함께 써 둘게요.” 그러고서 그녀는 생각하기가 두려운 사람처럼 서둘러 앉아 이렇게 썼다.\n\n“선생님께—여기 함께 보내는 책은 제 아버지의 것이었으니, 아버지를 생각하셔서 선생님께서 소중히 여겨 주시리라 믿습니다.\n\n“그럼 이만 줄입니다.\n\n그녀는 다시 집 안을 돌아다니기 시작해, 어린 시절부터 낯익은 물건들을 하나하나 뒤집어 보았다. 낡고 닳고 초라할지언정 그것들을 두고 떠나기가 아쉬워 쓰다듬듯 매만지면서. 그러나 그녀는 좀처럼 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 쇼 부인에게 올린 딕슨의 보고는 이러했다. 마음을 딴 데로 돌려 드리려고 내내 말을 걸었습니다만, 헤일 아가씨가 제 말을 한 마디라도 들으셨는지 모르겠습니다요, 하고. 하루 종일 서서 지낸 결과 저녁에는 몸이 지독히도 고단해졌고, 아버지의 부고를 들은 이래 가장 나은 밤잠을 잤다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그녀는 친구 한둘에게 작별 인사를 하러 가고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 쇼 부인은 반대했다.\n\n“얘야, 이렇게 서둘러—교회에 한 번 나가 보기도 전에—찾아갈 만큼 가까운 벗이 여기에 네게 있을 리 없다고 나는 생각한다.”\n\n“하지만 오늘밖에 없는걸요. 레녹스 대위가 오늘 오후에 오고, 우리가 정말로—제가 정말로 내일 떠나야 한다면——”\n\n“오, 그럼, 내일 떠나야지. 이 공기가 네게 나쁘고 널 그렇게 창백하고 아파 보이게 만든다는 확신이 점점 굳어지는구나. 게다가 이디스가 우릴 기다리고, 나를 기다리고 있을 게다. 그리고 얘야, 네 나이에 널 혼자 둘 수는 없지. 아니다, 꼭 그 인사를 다녀야겠다면 내가 함께 가마. 딕슨이 마차를 불러 줄 수 있겠지?”\n\n그리하여 쇼 부인은 마거릿을 돌보러 나섰고, 숄과 공기 방석을 챙길 하녀까지 데리고 갔다. 마거릿이 하루 중 아무 때고 혼자서 곧잘 다녀오곤 하던 두 곳을 방문하는 데 이런 채비가 벌어지는데도, 그녀의 얼굴은 너무 슬퍼서 미소로도 밝아지지 않았다. 가려는 곳 하나가 니컬러스 히긴스의 집이라는 것을 밝히기가 그녀는 반쯤 두려웠다. 이모가 마차에서 내려 그 골목을 걸어 올라가고, 바람이 불 때마다 집과 집 사이에 걸린 줄에 널린 젖은 빨래에 얼굴을 얻어맞는 일을 내키지 않아 하기를 바라는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n쇼 부인의 마음속에서는 편안함과 부인다운 체면 사이에 자그마한 싸움이 벌어졌으나 앞의 것이 이겼다. 그리하여 몸조심하라고, 그런 데는 늘 열병이 도사리고 있으니 옮지 않도록 하라고 마거릿에게 몇 번이고 당부한 끝에, 이모는 그녀가 아무런 조심도 하지 않고 아무 허락도 구하지 않은 채 곧잘 드나들던 곳에 가도록 허락해 주었다.\n\n니컬러스는 나가고 없었다. 집에는 메리와 바우처네 아이 한둘뿐이었다. 마거릿은 방문 시간을 더 잘 맞추지 못한 것이 스스로 언짢았다. 메리는 마음이 따뜻하고 착하기는 해도 머리가 몹시 둔했다. 그리고 마거릿이 자기들을 보러 온 뜻이 무엇인지 알아차린 순간, 그녀는 어찌나 걷잡을 수 없이 울며 흐느끼기 시작했는지, 마거릿은 마차를 타고 오면서 떠올려 둔 자잘한 말 천 가지 가운데 어느 하나도 소용없다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 그저 언젠가 어느 곳에선가 다시 만날지도 모른다는 막연한 기대를 내비치며 조금이나마 위로하려 애쓸 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 아버지가 어떻게든 시간을 낼 수 있다면 저녁에 일을 마치고 자기를 보러 와 주기를 얼마나 바라는지 전해 달라고 일렀다.\n\n그곳을 나서면서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 조금 망설이다가 말했다.\n\n“베시를 기억할 만한 자그마한 물건 하나를 가졌으면 해요.”\n\n그 순간 메리의 인심이 대뜸 되살아났다. 무엇을 드릴 수 있을까? 그리고 마거릿이 흔해빠진 자그마한 물잔 하나를 골라내자—열에 들뜬 베시의 입술을 적실 물을 담고 늘 그 곁에 놓여 있던 것으로 그녀가 기억하는 잔이었다—메리가 말했다.\n\n“아이고, 더 좋은 걸 가져가시우. 그건 4펜스밖에 안 하는 거요!”\n\n“이거면 돼요, 고마워요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그러고는 무언가 줄 것이 있다는 기쁨이 밝혀 놓은 빛이 아직 메리의 얼굴에 어른거리는 사이 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다.\n\n“이제 손턴 부인 댁으로.” 그녀는 속으로 생각했다. “해야 할 일이야.” 그러나 그 생각에 그녀의 낯빛은 자못 굳고 창백해졌으며, 손턴 부인이 어떤 사람이고 왜 작별 인사를 하러 가야 하는지 이모에게 설명할 정확한 말을 찾느라 애를 먹었다.\n\n두 사람은(쇼 부인도 여기서는 마차에서 내렸다) 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에는 불이 이제 막 지펴진 참이었다. 쇼 부인은 숄을 껴안고 몸을 웅크린 채 떨었다.\n\n“어쩌면 이렇게 얼음장 같은 방이 다 있담!” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n손턴 부인이 들어오기까지 두 사람은 한참을 기다려야 했다. 마거릿이 이제 자기 눈앞에서 떠나 버린다고 하니 그녀의 마음에도 다소 누그러진 데가 있었다. 그녀는 마거릿이 길고 고단한 근심을 견뎌 낸 인내보다도 이런저런 때와 자리에서 보여 준 그 기개를 더 또렷이 기억했다. 그녀를 맞이하는 부인의 낯빛은 여느 때보다 부드러웠다. 눈물에 부어오른 창백한 얼굴과, 마거릿이 그토록 가다듬으려 애쓰는 목소리의 떨림을 알아차렸을 때는 그 태도에 다정함의 그림자마저 어렸다.\n\n“제 이모 쇼 부인을 소개해 드리겠습니다. 저는 내일 밀턴을 떠납니다. 알고 계신지 모르겠습니다만, 손턴 부인, 떠나기 전에 한 번 더 뵙고 싶었어요. 지난번에 뵈었을 때의 제 태도를 사과드리려고요. 그리고 우리가 아무리 서로를 오해했다 하더라도 부인께서 좋은 뜻으로 하신 일이라는 것을 제가 잘 안다고 말씀드리고 싶었습니다.”\n\n쇼 부인은 마거릿의 말에 몹시 어리둥절한 낯빛이었다. 친절에 대한 감사라니! 예의를 갖추지 못한 데 대한 사과라니! 그러나 손턴 부인은 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n“헤일 양, 나를 공정하게 보아 주니 기쁘군요. 나는 그때 그렇게 충고한 것이 내 도리라고 믿었기에 그리했을 뿐, 그 이상은 하지 않았습니다. 나는 늘 아가씨에게 벗의 도리를 다하고자 했어요. 나를 공정하게 보아 주니 기쁩니다.”\n\n“그러면,” 마거릿이 말하면서 몹시 붉어졌다. “부인께서도 저를 공정하게 보아 주시겠어요? 제가 제 행동을 설명할 수 없다 해도—설명하지 않기로 한다 해도—부인께서 염려하신 그런 온당치 못한 처신을 하지는 않았다는 것을 믿어 주시겠어요?”\n\n마거릿의 목소리는 어찌나 부드럽고 그 눈은 어찌나 간절했던지, 여태껏 스스로 끄떡없음을 증명해 보이던 그 태도의 매력에 손턴 부인도 이번만은 마음이 움직였다.\n\n“그래요, 믿습니다. 그 일은 더 이야기하지 맙시다. 어디에 가서 살 겁니까, 헤일 양? 벨 씨에게서 아가씨가 밀턴을 떠난다는 말은 들었어요. 아가씨는 밀턴을 좋아한 적이 없지요.” 손턴 부인이 어딘가 씁쓸한 미소를 띠며 말했다. “그렇더라도 밀턴을 떠나는 것을 내가 축하해 주리라고는 기대하지 마세요. 어디서 살게 됩니까?”\n\n“이모와 함께요.” 마거릿이 쇼 부인 쪽으로 돌아서며 대답했다.\n\n“제 조카는 저와 함께 할리 가에서 살 거예요. 저에게는 거의 딸이나 다름없답니다.” 쇼 부인이 마거릿을 애틋하게 바라보며 말했다. “그리고 이 아이에게 베풀어 주신 어떤 친절에 대해서든 제가 신세를 졌다고 인정하게 되어 기쁩니다. 부인과 바깥어른께서 언제라도 런던에 오시게 되면, 제 아들딸인 레녹스 대위 내외도 저와 뜻을 같이해 두 분께 저희가 할 수 있는 예를 다하고자 할 것입니다.”\n\n손턴 부인은 속으로 생각했다. 저 귀부인 티 나는 이모가 부드러운 후의를 베풀고 있는 손턴 씨와 손턴 부인이 서로 어떤 사이인지, 마거릿이 이모에게 굳이 밝혀 두지는 않았구나, 하고. 그래서 그녀는 짧게 대답했다.\n\n“제 남편은 죽었습니다. 손턴 씨는 제 아들이고요. 저는 런던에 가는 일이 없으니 부인의 정중한 제안을 이용할 일도 없을 듯합니다.”\n\n바로 그 순간 손턴 씨가 방에 들어왔다. 옥스퍼드에서 이제 막 돌아온 참이었다. 그의 상복이 그를 그곳으로 부른 까닭을 말해 주고 있었다.\n\n“존,” 어머니가 말했다. “이분은 헤일 양의 이모 되시는 쇼 부인이시다. 안됐지만, 헤일 양이 찾아온 것은 우리에게 작별 인사를 하기 위해서라는구나.”\n\n“그럼 떠나시는군요!” 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“네.” 마거릿이 말했다. “내일 떠납니다.”\n\n“제 사위가 오늘 저녁에 저희를 데리러 옵니다.” 쇼 부인이 말했다.\n\n손턴 씨는 돌아섰다. 그는 앉지도 않은 채 이제 탁자 위의 무언가를 살펴보는 듯했다. 마치 뜯지 않은 편지라도 발견해 지금 자리에 있는 사람들을 잊어버린 사람처럼. 그는 두 사람이 작별하려고 일어섰을 때조차 알아차리지 못하는 것 같았다. 그러나 그는 앞으로 나서서 쇼 부인을 마차까지 모셔다 주었다. 마차가 다가오는 동안 그와 마거릿은 문간 계단에 바짝 붙어 서 있었고, 폭동이 있던 날의 기억이 두 사람의 마음속에 떠오르지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 마음속에서 그 기억은 이튿날의 말들과 얽혀 있었다. 그 사납고 절박한 무리 가운데 자기가 그를 아끼는 만큼 아끼지 않을 사람은 하나도 없다던 그녀의 격정 어린 선언과 함께. 그리고 자기를 비웃던 그 말이 떠오르자 그의 이마는 굳어졌다. 가슴은 그리움 어린 사랑으로 세차게 뛰고 있었으나. “아니다.” 그는 생각했다. “나는 한 번 운을 걸었고 모두 잃었다. 보내 주자—저 돌 같은 마음과 저 아름다움을 지닌 채로. 이목구비가 그토록 사랑스러운데도 지금 저 표정은 어찌 저리 굳고 무섭단 말인가! 저 사람은 내가 무슨 말을 해서 모질게 눌러야 할 일이 생길까 두려워하는구나. 보내 주자. 미인이든 상속녀든 간에, 내 마음보다 더 참된 마음을 만나기는 어려울 것이다. 보내 주자!”\n\n그리고 그가 작별 인사를 하는 목소리에는 아쉬움의 기색도, 어떤 종류의 감정도 없었다. 내밀어진 손은 단호한 침착함으로 잡혔다가, 시들어 죽은 꽃이기라도 한 듯 무심하게 놓였다. 그러나 그날 그의 집안에서 손턴 씨를 다시 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그는 바쁜 일이 있었다. 적어도 그는 그렇게 말했다.\n\n이 방문들로 마거릿의 기력은 완전히 바닥나서, 그녀는 이모의 극진한 간호와 어루만짐과 “그러게 내가 뭐랬니” 하는 한숨을 고스란히 견뎌야 했다. 딕슨은 아가씨가 아버지의 부고를 들은 첫날만큼이나 상태가 나쁘다고 했다. 그리고 딕슨과 쇼 부인은 이튿날의 여정을 미루는 편이 좋겠는지 의논했다. 그러나 이모가 마지못해 며칠 미루자고 말을 꺼내자, 마거릿은 심한 고통이라도 겪는 사람처럼 몸을 비틀며 말했다.\n\n“오! 떠나요. 저는 여기서는 견딜 수가 없어요. 여기서는 낫지 않을 거예요. 잊고 싶어요.”\n\n그리하여 채비는 계속되었다. 레녹스 대위가 왔고, 그와 함께 이디스와 어린 아들의 소식도 왔다. 그리고 마거릿은 아무리 친절하다 해도 지나치게 따뜻하고 안달하는 동정자는 아닌 사람의 심상하고 무심한 대화가 자기에게 도움이 된다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 기운을 차렸다. 그리고 히긴스가 올 만한 때가 되었다 싶자 조용히 방을 나와 자기 방에서 기다리던 기별을 기다릴 수 있었다.\n\n“이런!” 그녀가 들어오자 그가 말했다. “그 어르신이 그렇게 훌쩍 가 버리시다니! 사람들이 그 말을 전했을 때 지푸라기로 밀어도 넘어질 판이었소. ‘헤일 씨라고?’ 내가 그랬지. ‘그 목사 하시던 분?’ ‘그렇다네.’ 사람들이 그러더군. ‘그럼,’ 내가 그랬소. ‘이 땅에 살았던 누구 못지않게 좋은 사람이 갔구먼. 다른 누가 어떻든 간에!’ 그러고서 아가씨를 보러 와서 얼마나 슬픈지 말하려 했는데, 부엌에 있는 그 여편네들이 내가 왔다는 걸 아가씨한테 전해 주질 않더군. 아가씨가 아프다길래—그런데 이런, 참말로 그전 그 처녀 같아 보이질 않는구먼. 그리고 이제 런던에 가서 훌륭한 마님이 되신다지, 안 그렇소?”\n\n“훌륭한 마님은 아니에요.” 마거릿이 반쯤 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n“원! 손턴이 그러더군—하루 이틀 전에 이러는 거요. ‘히긴스, 헤일 양을 만나 봤나?’ ‘아니오.’ 내가 그랬지. ‘그 아가씨한테 나를 못 가게 막는 여편네들이 한 무더기요. 하지만 아프다니 내가 때를 기다릴 수 있소. 그 아가씨하고 나는 서로 꽤 잘 아는 사이니, 내가 곁에 못 가서 말을 못 했다고 해서 그 어르신 돌아가신 걸 내가 몹시 슬퍼한다는 걸 의심할 사람은 아니오.’ 그랬더니 이러는 거요. ‘자네가 그 사람을 만나려 애써 볼 시간은 많지 않을 걸세, 이 사람아. 그 사람은 어쩔 수 없는 하루라도 우리와 더 머물 사람이 아닐세. 훌륭한 친척들이 있어서 그이들이 데려가 버릴 테고, 우리는 다시는 못 볼 걸세.’ ‘나리,’ 내가 그랬소. ‘그 아가씨가 가기 전에 내가 못 만난다면, 다음 성령강림절에 런던까지 올라가고 말겠소. 정말 그러겠소. 어떤 친척이 됐든 내가 그 아가씨한테 작별 인사 하는 걸 막지는 못하오.’ 하지만 이거 참, 나는 아가씨가 부르시리라는 걸 알고 있었소. 그저 나리 비위를 맞추느라, 아가씨가 나를 안 보고 밀턴을 떠날지도 모른다고 여기는 척한 것뿐이지.”\n\n“옳으신 말씀이에요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “저를 제대로 보아 주셨어요. 그리고 저를 잊지 않으시리라 믿어요. 밀턴에서 아무도 저를 기억하지 않는다 해도 히긴스는 분명 기억해 주실 거예요. 아버지도요. 아버지가 얼마나 선하고 다정한 분이었는지 아시잖아요. 보세요, 히긴스! 여기 아버지의 성경이에요. 히긴스를 드리려고 간직해 두었어요. 저도 내주기가 쉽지는 않지만, 아버지라면 히긴스가 갖기를 바라셨을 거예요. 히긴스가 이걸 아끼고 그 안에 담긴 것을 아버지를 생각해서 공부하시리라 믿어요.”\n\n“그렇게 말해도 좋소. 그게 마귀가 갈겨쓴 것이라 해도, 아가씨와 그 어르신을 생각해서 읽어 달라고 하면 나는 읽겠소. 이건 뭐요, 아가씨! 나는 아가씨 돈은 안 받을 참이니 그런 생각일랑 마시오. 우리는 사이에 돈 소리 나는 일 없이도 좋은 벗으로 지내 왔잖소.”\n\n“아이들을 위해서예요—바우처네 아이들을 위해서요.” 마거릿이 서둘러 말했다. “그 애들에게는 필요할지도 몰라요. 그 애들 몫을 거절할 권리는 없으세요. 히긴스한테라면 한 푼도 드리지 않을 거예요.” 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. “이 가운데 조금이라도 히긴스 몫이라고는 생각하지 마세요.”\n\n“허, 아가씨! 나는 그저 이 말밖에 못 하겠소. 복 받으시오! 또 복 받으시오!—그리고 아멘.”\n\n# 제44장\n\n“한자리에 머무는 법 없는 따분한 되풀이, 어제의 얼굴은 오늘의 얼굴과 쌍둥이일 뿐.” — 카우퍼.\n\n“저마다 마땅히 되어야 할 바의 모습과 법도를 그는 보나니, 거기에 이르기까지 그의 기쁨은 결코 온전할 수 없으리.” — 뤼케르트.\n\n이디스가 해산 뒤 몸을 추스르는 동안 할리 가 저택이 더없이 고요했던 것은 마거릿에게 참으로 다행한 일이었다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 필요했던 자연스러운 휴식을 얻었다. 지난 두 달 사이에 자신의 처지에 닥친 급작스러운 변화를 헤아려 볼 시간도 얻었다. 그녀는 어느새 호화로운 저택의 식구가 되어 있었다. 온갖 근심과 걱정이라는 것이 이 세상에 있다는 사실조차 좀처럼 스며들어 본 적 없는 듯한 집이었다. 일상이라는 기계의 바퀴에는 기름이 잘 쳐져 있어 감미로울 만큼 매끄럽게 굴러갔다. 쇼 부인과 이디스는 마거릿이 그들이 한사코 그녀의 집이라 부르는 곳으로 돌아오자 아무리 잘해 주어도 성에 차지 않는 듯했다. 그런 만큼 헬스톤 목사관—아니, 근심에 찬 아버지와 병든 어머니와 가난에 가까운 살림의 자잘한 걱정거리들이 있던 밀턴의 그 초라하고 작은 집마저—이야말로 자기 마음속 집의 모습이라는 남모를 감정을 품고 있다는 것이 그녀 자신에게도 배은망덕하게 느껴졌다. 이디스는 어서 나아서 자기 방에 넘쳐 나는 온갖 포근한 살림살이와 예쁜 자잘한 장식품으로 마거릿의 침실을 채워 주고 싶어 안달이었다. 쇼 부인과 그녀의 하녀는 마거릿의 옷장을 우아하고 다채로운 상태로 되돌리는 일로 넉넉히 소일거리를 찾아냈다. 레녹스 대위는 느긋하고 다정하며 신사다운 사람이었다. 날마다 한두 시간은 아내의 화장실에서 함께 앉아 있었고, 또 한 시간은 어린 아들과 놀아 주었으며, 나머지 시간은 저녁 초대가 없는 날이면 클럽에서 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다. 마거릿이 고요와 안식을 필요로 하던 상태에서 막 벗어날 무렵—자기 삶이 허전하고 따분하다고 느끼기 시작하기 직전에—이디스가 아래층으로 내려와 집안에서 늘 맡던 몫을 다시 떠맡았다. 그러자 마거릿도 사촌을 지켜보고 감탄하고 시중드는 옛 습관으로 되돌아갔다. 그녀는 이디스의 손에서 의무라는 이름의 겉치레들을 기꺼이 모두 떠맡았다. 편지에 답장을 쓰고, 약속을 일깨워 주고, 즐거운 일이 앞에 없어 이디스가 곧잘 자기가 아프다고 여기고 싶어 할 때면 곁에서 보살폈다. 그러나 나머지 식구들은 런던 사교철의 한복판에 있었고, 마거릿은 자주 홀로 남겨졌다. 그럴 때면 그녀의 생각은 밀턴으로 돌아갔고, 그곳의 삶과 이곳의 삶이 얼마나 다른가 하는 야릇한 느낌이 밀려왔다. 아무런 싸움도 애씀도 요구되지 않는, 사건 없는 안락에 그녀는 물려 가고 있었다. 자기를 호사로 감싸고 도는 이 삶 너머의 무엇도 다 잊어버릴 만큼 나른하게 무뎌지지나 않을까 두려웠다. 런던에도 뼈 빠지게 일하고 애쓰는 사람들이 있을 터였지만 그녀는 그들을 본 적이 없었다. 하인들조차 자기들만의 지하 세계에서 살았고, 그녀는 그들의 희망도 두려움도 알지 못했다. 그들은 주인 내외의 어떤 필요나 변덕이 부를 때에만 불쑥 존재하기 시작하는 것 같았다. 마거릿의 마음에도 살아가는 방식에도 채워지지 않는 야릇한 공백이 있었다. 언젠가 그것을 이디스에게 어렴풋이 내비쳤을 때, 간밤에 춤을 추느라 지쳐 있던 이디스는 예전과 같은 자세로—이디스가 누운 소파 옆 발판에 마거릿이 앉아 있는 그 자세로—곁에 앉은 마거릿의 뺨을 나른하게 쓰다듬었다.\n\n“가엾은 것!” 이디스가 말했다. “온 세상이 이렇게 흥청거리는 이 철에 밤마다 혼자 남겨지니 조금 서글프기도 하겠지. 그래도 곧 만찬회를 열 거야. 헨리가 순회재판에서 돌아오는 대로. 그러면 너한테도 자그마한 즐거운 변화가 생기겠지. 시무룩해질 만도 하지, 가엾은 것!”\n\n마거릿은 만찬회가 만병통치약이 되리라고는 느껴지지 않았다. 그러나 이디스는 자기 만찬회를 자랑스러워했다. 스스로 말하기를 “어머니 시절의 그 케케묵은 노부인 만찬과는 아주 다르다”는 것이었다. 그리고 쇼 부인 자신도 레녹스 대위 부부의 취향에 맞춘 사뭇 다른 차림새와 손님 무리에서, 예전에 자기가 베풀던 더 격식 차리고 묵직한 연회에서 느끼던 것과 똑같은 종류의 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다. 레녹스 대위는 언제나 마거릿에게 더없이 다정하고 오라비처럼 굴었다. 그녀도 그를 정말 좋아했다. 다만 그가 이디스의 아름다움이 세상에 충분한 인상을 남기도록 하려고 아내의 옷차림과 겉모습에 안달하며 신경 쓸 때만은 예외였다. 그럴 때면 마거릿 안에 잠자던 와스디가 온통 깨어나, 그녀는 자기 감정을 드러내지 않으려고 애를 써야 했다.\n\n마거릿의 하루는 이렇게 흘러갔다. 늦은 아침 식사 전의 조용한 한두 시간. 그다음은 시간을 지키지 않는 식사였는데, 지치고 반쯤 잠에서 덜 깬 사람들이 게으르게 먹는 자리였지만 그 지루하게 늘어지는 내내 그녀는 자리를 지켜야 했다. 곧바로 이어서 계획을 논하는 자리가 있었기 때문이다. 하나같이 그녀와는 무관한 일들이었으나, 조언으로 거들지 못한다면 공감이라도 보태야 했다. 그다음은 끝도 없이 써야 하는 편지였다. 이디스는 그녀의 “서한의 능변”을 어루만지듯 한껏 추어올리며 그 일을 어김없이 그녀에게 떠넘겼다. 그리고 아침 산책에서 돌아온 숄토와 잠깐 놀아 주기, 그 밖에 하인들이 식사하는 동안 아이들 돌보기, 마차 산책이나 손님맞이. 그러고 나면 이모와 사촌 내외는 만찬 약속이나 오전 약속으로 나가 버려 마거릿은 자유로워졌다. 그것은 사실이었으나, 가라앉은 마음과 약해진 몸 위로 겹쳐 오는 그 하루의 무위(無爲)에 그녀는 오히려 지쳐 있었다.\n\n그녀는 딕슨이 밀턴에서 돌아오는 그 소박한 일을 말은 못 해도 애타는 마음으로 기다렸다. 지금까지 그 늙은 하녀는 헤일 집안의 온갖 뒷일을 정리하느라 바삐 매여 있었다. 오래도록 함께 살아온 사람들에 관한 소식이 이렇게 뚝 끊긴 것은 그녀의 마음에 별안간 닥친 굶주림 같았다. 물론 딕슨은 사무적인 편지 속에서 이따금 손턴 씨의 의견을 옮기기는 했다. 가구를 어떻게 하는 것이 좋을지, 크램프턴 테라스 집의 집주인에게는 어떻게 대응할지 하는 것들이었다. 그러나 그 이름이, 아니 밀턴의 어떤 이름이라도 나오는 것은 여기저기 띄엄띄엄일 뿐이었다. 어느 저녁 마거릿은 레녹스 집안 응접실에 혼자 앉아 있었다. 딕슨의 편지를 손에 쥐고 있었으나 읽지는 않고 그저 그 내용을 곱씹으며, 지나간 날들을 떠올리고, 자기 삶이 뽑혀 나왔는데도 아무도 그 빈자리를 아쉬워하지 않은 그 분주한 삶을 그려 보고 있었다. 자기와 아버지가 애초에 없었던 것처럼 그 소용돌이가 그대로 돌아가고 있을까 궁금해하고, 그 많은 사람 가운데 아무도 자기를 그리워하지 않는지 마음속으로 물어보고 있었다(히긴스는 아니었다, 그녀가 생각하고 있던 사람은). 그때 갑자기 벨 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다. 마거릿은 편지를 반짇고리에 급히 밀어 넣고 벌떡 일어섰다. 무슨 죄라도 지은 것처럼 얼굴을 붉히면서.\n\n“어머, 벨 씨! 뵈리라고는 생각도 못 했어요!”\n\n“그래도 그 아주 예쁜 놀란 몸짓뿐 아니라 반갑다는 인사도 해 주는 거겠지.”\n\n“저녁은 드셨어요? 어떻게 오셨어요? 저녁을 좀 차리라고 할게요.”\n\n“자네도 뭘 먹을 참이라면 그러게. 안 그러면, 알다시피 나만큼 먹는 데 관심 없는 사람도 없으니까. 그런데 다른 사람들은 어디 있나? 저녁 초대를 받아 나갔나? 자네만 혼자 두고?”\n\n“네, 그래요. 그게 어찌나 편한지요. 마침 생각하고 있던 참인데—그런데 저녁을 한번 걸어 보시겠어요? 집에 뭐가 있는지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“실은 말이지, 클럽에서 저녁을 먹었네. 다만 예전만큼 요리를 잘하지 못하기에, 자네가 저녁을 먹을 참이면 나도 한번 저녁을 채워 볼까 했던 게지. 하지만 됐네, 됐어! 잉글랜드를 통틀어도 갑작스러운 저녁상을 믿고 맡길 요리사는 열 명도 못 되네. 솜씨와 불이 견뎌 준다 해도 성깔이 못 견디지. 차나 좀 끓여 주게, 마거릿. 그래, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었나? 말해 주려던 참이었잖은가. 그건 누구 편지인가, 대녀야? 그렇게 잽싸게 감춘 것 말이야.”\n\n“그냥 딕슨의 편지예요.” 마거릿이 얼굴이 새빨개져서 대답했다.\n\n“허! 그게 다인가? 나랑 같이 기차를 타고 올라온 사람이 누구였을 것 같나?”\n\n“모르겠어요.” 마거릿은 짐작 따위는 하지 않겠다고 마음먹고 말했다.\n\n“그 뭐라고 하더라? 사촌의 남편의 동생은 뭐라고 부르는 게 옳은가?”\n\n“헨리 레녹스 씨요?” 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“그래.” 벨 씨가 대답했다. “예전부터 알던 사이였지, 안 그런가? 어떤 사람인가, 마거릿?”\n\n“오래전에는 좋아했어요.” 마거릿은 잠깐 눈을 내리깔며 말했다. 그러고는 곧장 고개를 들고 여느 때의 태도로 말을 이었다. “그 뒤로 프레더릭 일로 편지를 주고받아 온 건 아시잖아요. 하지만 삼 년 가까이 만나지 못했으니 달라졌을지도 모르죠. 어떻게 보셨어요?”\n\n“모르겠네. 처음에는 내가 누구인지 알아내려고, 다음에는 내가 무엇 하는 사람인지 알아내려고 어찌나 바쁘던지, 정작 자기가 어떤 사람인지는 끝내 내비치지 않더군. 하기야 무슨 사람을 상대로 이야기하는 것인지 알아보려는 그 감춰진 호기심 자체가 제법 볼만한 것이었고, 그 성품을 꽤 잘 보여 준 셈이라면 몰라도. 자네는 그 사람이 잘생겼다고 보나, 마거릿?”\n\n“아니요! 전혀요. 그렇게 보세요?”\n\n“나야 아니지. 다만 자네는 혹시 그럴까 싶었네. 여기 자주 오나?”\n\n“런던에 있을 때는 그런 것 같아요. 제가 온 뒤로는 순회재판에 나가 있었고요. 그런데—벨 씨—옥스퍼드에서 오셨어요, 밀턴에서 오셨어요?”\n\n“밀턴에서지. 내가 연기에 절어 있는 게 안 보이나?”\n\n“그러네요. 하지만 저는 옥스퍼드의 그 고색창연한 것들 탓인 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“이보게, 철든 여인답게 굴어야지! 옥스퍼드에서라면 나는 그 동네 집주인들쯤은 다 주무르고 내 뜻대로 했을 걸세. 자네네 밀턴 집주인이 나한테 안긴 수고의 절반만 들이고도 말이야. 그런데 그 사람은 결국 나를 이겨 먹었네. 내년 유월이 되어야 그 집을 우리 손에서 거두어 가겠다는 거야. 다행히 손턴 씨가 세를 들 사람을 찾아 주었지. 자네는 왜 손턴 씨 안부는 묻지 않나, 마거릿? 그 사람이 자네한테 아주 발 벗고 나선 친구 노릇을 했다는 걸 내가 말해 주지. 내 수고의 절반도 넘게 덜어 주었네.”\n\n“그분은 어떠세요? 손턴 부인도 안녕하시고요?” 마거릿은 소리 내어 말하려 애썼으나 서둘러 숨죽인 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“다들 잘 있는 것 같더군. 그 집에 묵고 있다가 손턴네 딸 혼사 이야기를 쉴 새 없이 재잘거리는 통에 쫓겨나다시피 나왔네. 누이인데도 손턴 자신조차 견디기 어려워하더구먼. 늘 제 방에 들어가 앉아 있곤 했지. 그 사람도 이제 그런 일에 당사자로든 곁다리로든 마음 쓸 나이는 지났으니까. 나는 그 노부인이 그 물살에 휩쓸려 딸의 오렌지 꽃과 레이스 타령에 함께 들떠 있는 걸 보고 놀랐네. 손턴 부인은 더 단단한 재료로 빚어진 사람인 줄 알았거든.”\n\n“딸의 약한 구석을 가리기 위해서라면 어떤 감정이든 꾸며 내실 분이에요.” 마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그럴지도 모르지. 자네가 그 부인을 연구해 본 모양이군? 그 부인은 자네를 그리 좋아하지 않는 것 같더구먼, 마거릿.”\n\n“알아요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “아, 드디어 차가 왔네요!” 그녀는 마음이 놓인 듯 외쳤다. 그리고 차와 함께 헨리 레녹스 씨가 들어왔다. 늦은 저녁 식사를 마치고 할리 가까지 걸어 올라온 그는 형과 형수가 집에 있으리라고 여긴 것이 분명했다. 헬스톤에서 그가 청혼하고 그녀가 거절했던 그 잊지 못할 날 이후 처음 마주하는 자리인 만큼, 제삼자가 있다는 것을 그도 자기만큼이나 고맙게 여기리라고 마거릿은 짐작했다. 그녀는 처음에 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 침묵을 지킬 구실을 주고 그에게는 마음을 추스를 여유를 주는 찻상의 온갖 일거리가 고마웠다. 사실을 말하자면 그는 이날 저녁 어색한 만남을 넘겨 버릴 셈으로 억지로 몸을 일으켜 할리 가로 온 것이었다. 레녹스 대위와 이디스가 있어도 어색할 자리인데, 그녀가 그곳의 유일한 부인이고 자연히 또 어쩔 수 없이 대화의 많은 부분을 그녀에게 건네야 하는 처지가 되고 보니 어색함이 갑절이 되었다. 먼저 평정을 되찾은 쪽은 그녀였다. 어색한 수줍음의 첫 물결이 지나가자 그녀는 마음에 가장 먼저 떠오른 화제를 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n“레녹스 씨, 프레더릭 일로 애써 주신 모든 것에 정말 감사드립니다.”\n\n“그렇게 성과가 없었던 것이 안타까울 뿐입니다.” 그가 대답하면서 벨 씨 앞에서 어디까지 말해도 좋을지 살피듯 그쪽으로 재빨리 눈길을 던졌다. 마거릿은 그의 속내를 읽은 듯 벨 씨를 향해 말을 건넸다. 그를 대화 안으로 끌어들이는 동시에, 프레더릭의 누명을 벗기려 해 온 노력들을 그가 이미 훤히 알고 있다는 뜻을 내비친 것이었다.\n\n“그 호럭스라는 사람—맨 마지막 증인이었던 그 사람도 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 아무 소용이 없는 것으로 드러났어요. 레녹스 씨가 알아내신 바로는 그 사람은 바로 지난 팔월에 오스트레일리아로 떠났대요. 프레더릭이 잉글랜드에 와서 우리에게 그 이름들을 알려 주기 겨우 두 달 전에요——”\n\n“프레더릭이 잉글랜드에 왔었다고! 그런 말은 한 번도 안 했잖은가!” 벨 씨가 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n“아시는 줄 알았어요. 들으셨으리라고 믿어 의심치 않았어요. 물론 큰 비밀이었으니 지금 입에 올리지 말았어야 했는지도 모르겠네요.” 마거릿이 조금 당황하여 말했다.\n\n“저는 형에게도 형수에게도 그 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 없습니다.” 레녹스 씨가 나무라는 뜻을 담은 직업적인 무미건조함으로 말했다.\n\n“괜찮네, 마거릿. 나는 떠들썩하게 수다 떠는 세상에 사는 것도 아니고, 나한테서 사실을 캐내려 드는 사람들 틈에 있는 것도 아니야. 나 같은 미덥고 늙은 은둔자한테 비밀을 흘렸다고 그렇게 겁먹은 얼굴을 할 것 없네. 나는 그 사람이 잉글랜드에 왔었다는 말은 결코 입에 담지 않을 걸세. 아무도 나한테 묻지 않을 테니 유혹에 빠질 일도 없지. 가만!” (그가 다소 불쑥 자기 말을 끊었다.) “자네 어머니 장례 때였나?”\n\n“오빠는 어머니가 돌아가실 때 곁에 있었어요.” 마거릿이 나직이 말했다.\n\n“그렇지! 그렇고말고! 아, 누군가 나한테 그 사람이 그때 건너왔던 게 아니냐고 묻기에 내가 딱 잘라 아니라고 했는데—몇 주 되지도 않았어—그게 누구였더라? 아! 이제 생각나는군!”\n\n그러나 그는 그 이름을 말하지 않았다. 마거릿은 자기 짐작이 맞는지, 그 질문을 한 사람이 손턴 씨였는지 알기 위해서라면 많은 것을 내놓을 수도 있었지만, 그토록 묻고 싶었으면서도 벨 씨에게 그 물음을 던질 수 없었다.\n\n잠깐 말이 끊겼다. 그러다가 레녹스 씨가 마거릿을 향해 말했다. “벨 선생님께서 이제 오라버님의 그 딱한 처지에 얽힌 사정을 다 알고 계시다면, 한때 그분께 유리하게 내놓을 수 있으리라 바랐던 증거를 찾는 일이 지금 어디까지 와 있는지 정확히 알려 드리는 것이 가장 좋겠군요. 그러니 내일 아침 저와 함께 식사해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 그 행방이 묘연한 사람들의 이름을 하나씩 짚어 보겠습니다.”\n\n“괜찮다면 저도 그 자세한 사정을 다 듣고 싶어요. 이리로 오실 수는 없나요? 두 분을 아침 식사에 부르자고 감히 말씀드리지는 못하겠어요. 물론 두 분 다 환영받으시겠지만요. 그래도 프레더릭에 대해 아시는 것은 다 알려 주세요. 지금은 아무 희망이 없다 해도요.”\n\n“열한 시 반에 약속이 있습니다. 하지만 원하신다면 반드시 오지요.” 레녹스 씨가 뒤늦게 지나칠 만큼 기꺼운 기색을 덧붙여 대답하는 바람에, 마거릿은 몸이 움츠러들며 그 자연스러운 부탁을 꺼내지 말걸 그랬다고 거의 후회할 뻔했다. 벨 씨가 일어나서 자기 모자를 찾아 둘러보았다. 차를 놓을 자리를 내느라 치워 둔 모자였다.\n\n“자!” 그가 말했다. “레녹스 씨는 어쩔 생각인지 모르겠네만, 나는 슬슬 집으로 갈까 하네. 오늘 여행을 했더니, 예순 몇 해나 먹은 나이에는 여행이 몸에 배어 나오기 시작하는군.”\n\n“저는 남아서 형과 형수를 보고 갈까 합니다.” 레녹스 씨가 떠날 기색을 조금도 보이지 않고 말했다. 마거릿은 그와 단둘이 남겨진다는 데 수줍고 어색한 두려움에 사로잡혔다. 헬스톤 정원의 그 작은 테라스에서 있었던 장면이 그녀에게 너무도 생생해서, 그에게도 그러리라고 믿지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“아직 가지 마세요, 벨 씨.” 그녀가 서둘러 말했다. “이디스를 보셨으면 해요. 이디스도 벨 씨를 알았으면 하고요. 제발요!” 그러면서 그녀는 가볍지만 단호한 손을 그의 팔에 얹었다. 그가 그녀를 바라보고는 그 얼굴에 일렁이는 당혹을 알아차렸다. 그는 그 작은 손길에 거스를 수 없는 힘이라도 실린 듯 도로 앉았다.\n\n“이 아이가 나를 이렇게 휘어잡는군요, 레녹스 씨.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 이 아이가 고른 말이 얼마나 절묘한지도 알아채셨기 바랍니다. 대단한 미인이라고 하는 이 사촌 이디스는 나더러 ‘보라’고 하고서는, 나한테 이르러서는 솔직하게 말을 바꾸었으니 말이오. 레녹스 부인은 나를 ‘알아야’ 한다지요. 나는 별로 ‘볼’ 것이 못 되는 모양이지, 응, 마거릿?”\n\n그는 자기가 가겠다고 하자 그녀의 태도에서 감지한 자그마한 동요를 가라앉힐 틈을 주려고 농을 던졌고, 그녀도 그 어조를 받아 공을 되던졌다. 레녹스 씨는 형인 대위가 어떻게 그녀가 고운 모습을 다 잃었다고 전할 수 있었는지 의아했다. 물론 조용한 검은 드레스 차림의 그녀는 하늘하늘한 흰 크레이프 상복을 입고 길게 늘어뜨린 금빛 머리칼을 나부끼며 온통 부드러움과 광채로 춤추듯 다니는 이디스와는 대조를 이루었다. 이디스는 벨 씨에게 소개받자 더없이 어울리게 보조개를 지으며 얼굴을 붉혔다. 미인이라는 평판을 지켜야 한다는 것을, 그리고 아무도 들어 본 적 없는 어느 대학의 늙은 펠로의 모습을 하고 있을망정 절하고 우러르기를 거부하는 모르드개가 있어서는 안 된다는 것을 그녀는 의식하고 있었다. 쇼 부인과 레녹스 대위도 저마다의 방식으로 벨 씨를 다정하고 진심 어린 태도로 맞아, 그가 자기도 모르게 그들을 좋아하게 만들었다. 특히 마거릿이 이 집의 딸이자 누이로서 얼마나 자연스럽게 제자리를 잡고 있는지를 보고 나서는 더욱 그러했다.\n\n“집에서 맞아 드리지 못했다니 정말 죄송해요.” 이디스가 말했다. “헨리, 도련님도요! 하기야 도련님을 위해서라면 집에 남아 있었을 것 같지는 않지만요. 하지만 벨 씨를 위해서라면! 마거릿의 벨 씨를 위해서라면——”\n\n“형수님이 어떤 희생인들 못 치렀겠습니까.” 시동생이 말했다. “만찬회까지도요! 그리고 이 아주 잘 어울리는 드레스를 입는 즐거움까지도요.”\n\n이디스는 이맛살을 찌푸려야 할지 웃어야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그러나 레녹스 씨는 그녀를 앞엣것 쪽으로 몰고 갈 생각이 없었으므로 말을 이었다.\n\n“내일 아침에 희생할 각오가 되어 있다는 것을 보여 주시겠습니까? 우선 벨 선생님을 만나뵐 수 있게 저를 아침 식사에 불러 주시고, 다음으로는 열 시가 아니라 아홉 시 반에 상을 차리도록 일러 주시는 친절을 베풀어서요. 헤일 양과 벨 선생님께 보여 드리고 싶은 편지와 서류가 있습니다.”\n\n“벨 선생님께서 런던에 머무시는 동안 저희 집을 제 집처럼 여기셨으면 합니다.” 레녹스 대위가 말했다. “침실을 내어 드릴 수 없는 것이 그저 죄송할 따름입니다.”\n\n“고맙습니다. 참으로 감사한 말씀이오. 하지만 내어 주셨다면 나를 무뚝뚝한 사람으로만 여기셨을 겁니다. 이토록 유쾌한 벗들이라는 온갖 유혹에도 불구하고 사양했을 테니까요.” 벨 씨는 좌중을 향해 두루 고개를 숙이며 이렇게 말하고는, 자기 문장을 이토록 말끔하게 마무리한 것을 남몰래 대견해했다. 그 말을 꾸밈없는 말로 옮겼더라면 이런 뜻에 더 가까웠을 것이다. “이렇게 예의 바르고 점잖게 말하는 사람들 틈의 갑갑함은 나는 못 견디지. 소금 없는 고기 같을 게야. 침대가 없다니 고마운 일이군. 그런데 문장을 참 잘도 둥글렸어! 내가 아주 좋은 예절의 요령을 익히고 있구먼.”\n\n그 자기만족은 그가 거리로 나와 헨리 레녹스와 나란히 걸을 때까지 이어졌다. 여기서 그는 문득 더 머물러 달라고 조르던 마거릿의 애원 어린 눈빛을 떠올렸고, 오래전 레녹스 씨의 어느 지인에게서 들었던, 그가 마거릿을 흠모한다는 몇 마디 귀띔도 생각났다. 그것이 그의 생각에 새로운 방향을 주었다. “헤일 양을 오래 알고 지내셨겠지요. 그 아이 안색이 어떻습니까? 나한테는 창백하고 아파 보이는군요.”\n\n“제 눈에는 대단히 좋아 보이던데요. 처음 들어갔을 때는 아니었는지도 모르겠습니다. 지금 생각해 보니 말입니다. 하지만 생기가 돌기 시작하니 제가 본 어느 때 못지않게 좋아 보였습니다.”\n\n“겪어 낸 일이 참 많았지요.” 벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다! 그분이 견뎌야 했던 온갖 일을 전해 듣고 마음이 아팠습니다. 죽음에서 오는 그 흔하고 누구에게나 닥치는 슬픔만이 아니라, 아버님의 처신이 그분께 안겼을 온갖 괴로움까지도요. 게다가——”\n\n“아버지의 처신이라니!” 벨 씨가 놀란 어조로 말했다. “뭔가 잘못된 이야기를 들으신 게 분명합니다. 그분은 더없이 양심에 따라 행동했습니다. 예전의 그분을 두고는 내가 도저히 인정해 주지 않았을 만큼 굳센 결단력을 보여 주셨지요.”\n\n“제가 잘못 전해 들었는지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 그분의 성직록을 이어받은 분—영리하고 사리 밝은 데다 아주 부지런한 성직자였습니다—에게서 들은 바로는, 헤일 씨가 그렇게까지 하실 까닭은 없었다는 겁니다. 성직록을 내놓고 자신과 가족을 공업 도시에서 개인 교습이나 하는 팔자에 내던지실 까닭 말입니다. 주교가 다른 성직록을 제의한 것은 사실이지만, 어떤 의심을 품게 되었다 하더라도 그 자리에 그대로 머무를 수 있었으니 사임할 이유가 없었다는 것이지요. 사실인즉 이 시골 성직자들은 워낙 고립된 삶을 살지 않습니까. 고립이란 자기와 같은 수준의 교양을 갖춘 사람들과의 왕래가 없다는 뜻입니다. 그런 이들의 생각에 견주어 제 생각을 다스리기도 하고, 자기가 너무 앞서가는지 너무 뒤처지는지 알아차리기도 할 텐데 말입니다. 그러다 보니 신앙의 조항을 두고 있지도 않은 의심으로 스스로를 어지럽히고, 선을 행할 수 있는 확실한 기회를 아주 불확실한 제 공상 때문에 내던지기가 아주 쉬운 것이지요.”\n\n“나는 생각이 다릅니다. 그들이 내 가엾은 친구 헤일처럼 하기가 아주 쉽다고는 보지 않아요.” 벨 씨는 속으로 부아가 치밀고 있었다.\n\n“‘아주 쉽다’고 한 것은 제가 너무 두루뭉술한 표현을 쓴 것인지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 그들의 삶이란 것이 지나친 자만이나 병적인 양심 상태 가운데 하나를 아주 자주 낳는 것만은 분명합니다.” 레녹스 씨가 더없이 태연하게 대답했다.\n\n“이를테면 법률가들 사이에서는 자만이라는 것을 만나 보신 적이 없습니까?” 벨 씨가 물었다. “그리고 병적인 양심의 사례도 좀처럼 없겠지요.” 그는 점점 더 부아가 나서, 얼마 전에 익혀 둔 좋은 예절의 요령도 잊어 가고 있었다. 레녹스 씨는 이제 자기가 동행을 언짢게 했음을 알아차렸다. 그리고 그는 무슨 말이든 하려고, 그래서 길이 같이 이어지는 동안 시간이나 보내려고 이야기했을 뿐이므로 이 문제에서 어느 편에 서든 아무래도 좋았고, 그래서 조용히 이렇게 말하며 태도를 돌렸다. “물론 헤일 씨 같은 연세의 분이 이십 년 살던 집을 떠나고 자리 잡힌 습관을 다 버리는 데에는, 그것도 아마 그릇된 생각—아니, 그건 상관없지요—손에 잡히지 않는 어떤 관념 때문에 그러는 데에는 훌륭한 데가 있습니다. 감탄하지 않을 수 없지요. 다만 그 감탄에는 연민이 섞이는데, 돈키호테에게 느끼는 것과 비슷한 감정입니다. 게다가 얼마나 신사다운 분이었는지요! 헬스톤에서 보낸 그 마지막 날에 저에게 베풀어 주신 고상하고 소박한 환대를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다.”\n\n절반쯤밖에 누그러지지 않았으면서도, 자기 양심의 어떤 가책을 달래기 위해서라도 헤일 씨의 처신에 돈키호테 같은 데가 있었다고 믿고 싶었던 벨 씨는 이렇게 으르렁거리듯 내뱉었다. “그렇지요! 그리고 선생은 밀턴을 모릅니다. 헬스톤에서 그리로 옮겨 갔으니 얼마나 큰 변화였겠소! 내가 헬스톤에 가 본 지도 여러 해 되었소만, 장담하건대 그곳은 아직도 그대로 서 있을 겁니다. 나뭇가지 하나 돌멩이 하나까지 지난 백 년 동안 그래 왔듯이 말이오. 그런데 밀턴은! 나는 사오 년마다 그리로 가고, 게다가 거기서 태어났소. 그런데도 분명히 말하는데 나는 곧잘 길을 잃는다오. 그렇소, 우리 아버지 과수원 위에 세워진 바로 그 창고 더미들 틈에서 말이오. 여기서 헤어지는 겁니까? 그럼 안녕히 가시오, 선생. 내일 아침 할리 가에서 뵙겠지요.”\n\n# 제45장\n\n“내가 젊던 날 하늘거리는 대기를 타고 흐르던 그 소리들은 어디에 있는가. 마지막 떨림마저 이제는 스러졌고, 귀 기울이던 이들도 이제는 없구나. 아! 눈을 감고 꿈이나 꾸게 해 다오.” — W. S. 랜더.\n\n헬스톤 생각은 레녹스 씨와 나눈 대화를 통해 깨어 있는 벨 씨의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 밤새도록 그 생각은 그의 꿈속을 마구 헤집고 다녔다. 그는 다시 대학의 지도 교사였다. 지금은 펠로의 자리에 오른 바로 그 대학에서. 다시 긴 방학이었고, 그는 갓 혼인한 벗의 집에 머물고 있었다. 자랑스러운 남편이자 행복한 헬스톤 교구 목사의 집에. 재잘거리는 시내 위로 두 사람은 있을 수 없는 도약을 했고, 그 도약은 며칠씩이나 그들을 공중에 매달아 두는 듯했다. 시간도 공간도 없었으나 다른 모든 것은 실재하는 것만 같았다. 모든 일은 실제로 있고 없음이 아니라 마음의 감정으로 재어졌으니, 실제로 있는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 나무들은 가을빛 잎사귀로 화려했고, 꽃과 풀의 따스한 향내가 감각에 달콤하게 스몄으며, 젊은 아내는 재산에 관한 한 제 처지가 못마땅하다는 기색과 잘생기고 헌신적인 남편에 대한 자부심이 꼭 그만큼씩 뒤섞인 몸짓으로 집 안을 오갔다. 25년 전 실제 삶에서 벨 씨가 눈여겨보았던 바로 그 모습이었다. 꿈이 어찌나 삶을 닮았던지, 깨어나 보니 도리어 지금의 삶이 꿈처럼 느껴졌다. 그는 어디에 있는가? 런던 호텔의 답답하고 번듯하게 꾸며진 방 안에! 조금 전까지 그에게 말을 걸고 그의 곁을 오가고 그를 만지던 이들은 어디에 있는가? 죽었다! 묻혔다! 이 땅의 영원이 미치는 데까지 영영 사라져 버렸다. 그는 노인이었다. 얼마 전만 해도 장년의 온전한 힘으로 의기양양하던 그가. 제 삶의 그 지독한 외로움은 떠올리는 것만으로도 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 서둘러 자리에서 일어나, 다시는 있을 수 없게 된 것을 잊어 보려고 할리 가의 아침 식사에 갈 채비를 부산히 했다.\n\n그는 변호사가 늘어놓는 세세한 대목을 다 좇을 수 없었다. 그가 보기에 그 대목들은 마거릿의 눈을 커지게 하고 입술을 창백하게 만들었다. 프레더릭의 누명을 벗겨 줄 증거라는 증거가 하나씩 차례로, 운명이 그렇게 정해 놓기라도 한 듯, 그녀의 발밑에서 무너져 사라져 갔기 때문이다. 마지막 희망이 꺼져 가는 대목에 이르자 레녹스 씨의 절제된 직업적 목소리마저 한결 부드럽고 다정한 어조를 띠었다. 마거릿이 그 결말을 미리 온전히 알지 못했던 것은 아니다. 다만 잇따른 낙담의 세세한 대목들이 어찌나 가차 없이 촘촘하게 다가와 온갖 희망을 꺼뜨렸던지, 그녀는 마침내 눈물을 쏟고야 말았다. 레녹스 씨가 읽기를 멈추었다.\n\n“더 읽지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.” 그가 걱정스러운 목소리로 말했다. “제 어리석은 제안이었습니다. 헤일 중위는,” 그토록 모질게 내쫓긴 그 군대의 계급으로 오빠를 불러 주는 것만으로도 마거릿에게는 위로가 되었다. “헤일 중위는 지금 행복합니다. 해군에 남아 있었더라면 결코 누리지 못했을 만큼 재산도 앞날도 든든하고, 틀림없이 아내의 나라를 제 나라로 삼았을 겁니다.”\n\n“그게 바로 그거예요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “그걸 아쉬워하다니 제가 너무 이기적인 것 같아요.” 그녀는 웃어 보이려 애썼다. “그래도 오빠는 제게서 사라졌고, 저는 이렇게 외로워요.” 레녹스 씨는 서류를 뒤적이며, 언젠가는 그리되리라 믿는 만큼 지금 제가 부유하고 번듯했으면 하고 바랐다. 벨 씨는 코를 풀었으나, 그 밖에는 그 역시 잠자코 있었다. 그리고 마거릿은 일이 분 만에 겉으로는 여느 때의 침착함을 되찾은 듯했다. 그녀는 수고해 준 레녹스 씨에게 무척 정중하게 고마움을 표했다. 제 태도 때문에 그가 공연히 그녀에게 아픔을 주었다고 여길지도 모른다는 것을 의식했기에 더욱 정중하고 상냥하게. 그러나 그것은 겪지 않았더라면 좋았을 아픔은 아니었다.\n\n벨 씨가 작별 인사를 하려고 다가왔다.\n\n“마거릿!” 그가 장갑을 만지작거리며 말했다. “나는 내일 헬스톤에 내려가 옛 고장을 좀 볼 참이다. 나와 함께 가겠니? 아니면 너한테 너무 괴로운 일이겠느냐? 솔직히 말해 보렴, 겁내지 말고.”\n\n“오, 벨 씨.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 더 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러나 통풍으로 마디가 굵어진 그의 늙은 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“자, 자, 그만하면 됐다.” 그가 겸연쩍어 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. “네 쇼 이모도 너를 내게 맡겨 주겠지. 내일 아침에 떠나면 두 시쯤 닿을 게다. 요기를 좀 하고 작은 여관에 저녁을 시켜 두자꾸나. 예전에는 레너드 암스라고 했지. 그러고는 숲으로 나가 입맛을 돋우는 거야. 견딜 수 있겠느냐, 마거릿? 우리 둘 다에게 시련이 되리라는 건 나도 안다만, 적어도 내게는 기쁨이 될 게다. 거기서 저녁을 먹고, 구할 수만 있다면 암사슴 고기밖에 없겠지만, 그런 다음 나는 낮잠을 잘 테니 너는 옛 벗들을 만나러 나가거라. 기차 사고만 없다면 성한 몸으로 무사히 돌려보내 주마. 떠나기 전에 네 목숨에 1,000파운드 보험을 들어 두마. 그러면 네 친척들에게도 얼마쯤 위안이 되겠지. 아무 일 없으면 금요일 점심때까지 쇼 부인에게 데려다주마. 그러니 네가 좋다고 하면 나는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 그 얘기를 꺼내겠다.”\n\n“얼마나 좋을지 말로는 도무지 표현할 수가 없어요.” 마거릿이 눈물 사이로 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 앞으로 이틀 동안 그 눈물샘을 마른 채로 두어서 고마움을 증명해 보렴. 그러지 않으면 나도 눈물샘이 이상해질 것 같은데, 나는 그런 게 싫으니 말이다.”\n\n“한 방울도 울지 않을게요.” 마거릿이 속눈썹에 맺힌 눈물을 떨구려고 눈을 깜빡이며 억지로 미소를 지은 채 말했다.\n\n“그래야 착한 아이지. 그럼 위층으로 올라가 다 정해 놓자꾸나.” 벨 씨가 쇼 이모와 그 계획을 의논하는 동안 마거릿은 거의 떨릴 만큼 들뜬 상태였다. 쇼 이모는 처음에는 깜짝 놀랐고, 이어 미심쩍어하며 난감해했으며, 끝내는 제 확신보다는 벨 씨가 내뱉는 말의 거친 힘에 못 이겨 물러서고 말았다. 그것이 옳은지 그른지, 온당한지 온당치 않은지 그녀는 끝까지 스스로 흡족할 만큼 정하지 못했으니, 마거릿이 무사히 돌아와 그 계획이 흐뭇하게 이루어지고 나서야 비로소 이렇게 말할 만한 결단이 섰던 것이다. 그것은 참으로 벨 씨다운 고마운 생각이었고, 그동안 그 애가 겪은 온갖 마음 졸이는 시간 끝에 꼭 필요로 하던 바로 그 변화를 준다는 점에서 자기 역시 마거릿에게 바라 마지않던 바로 그것이었다고 확신한다고.\n\n# 제46장\n\n“그리하여 지난날의 그 행복하던 나날에 다시 한 번 마음을 머무르려 할 때마다, 나는 여전히 그리워해야 하네. 죽음이 내 곁에서 갈라놓은, 그토록 미덥던 벗들을.”\n\n“그러나 참된 우정이 맺어질 때는 언제나 영혼이 영혼을 찾아내는 법. 그때 우리는 영혼으로 기쁨을 얻었으니, 지금도 나는 영혼으로 그들에게 매여 있네.” — 울란트.\n\n마거릿은 정해진 시각보다 한참 전에 채비를 마쳤고, 아무도 보지 않을 때 조용히 조금 울고 누군가 쳐다보면 환하게 웃을 만한 여유까지 있었다. 마지막 걱정은 늦어서 기차를 놓치지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그러나 아니었다. 모두 제시간에 닿았다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내 편안하고 행복하게 숨을 쉬었다. 벨 씨와 마주 보고 객차에 앉아 낯익은 역들을 지나 쏜살같이 달려가면서, 남부의 오래된 소읍과 마을들이 맑은 햇살의 따스한 빛 속에 잠들어 있는 것을 바라보면서. 그 햇빛은 기와지붕에 한층 더 붉은 빛을 더해 주었으니, 북부의 차가운 슬레이트와는 어찌나 다른지. 비둘기 떼가 뾰족하고 예스러운 박공 언저리를 맴돌다 여기저기 천천히 내려앉아 부드럽고 반들거리는 깃털을 부풀렸다. 마치 깃털 한 올 한 올을 그 감미로운 온기에 내맡기려는 듯이. 역마다 사람은 별로 없었다. 어쩐지 너무 나른하게 만족하여 여행할 마음조차 없는 듯이 보였다. 마거릿이 런던·노스웨스턴 선을 두 차례 오가며 보았던 그 부산함과 술렁임은 하나도 없었다. 해가 더 깊어지면 이 노선도 넉넉한 유람객들로 북적이며 활기를 띠겠지만, 바쁜 장사치들이 끊임없이 오가는 것으로 치자면 북부 노선과는 언제까지나 크게 다를 터였다. 이곳에서는 거의 모든 역마다 구경꾼 한둘이 주머니에 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁거리며 서 있었는데, 그저 바라보는 그 단순한 행위에 어찌나 몰두해 있는지, 기차가 휙 떠나 버리고 텅 빈 철길과 헛간 몇 채와 저 멀리 밭 한두 뙈기만 바라볼 거리로 남으면 저 사람은 대체 무엇을 하며 지낼까 하고 나그네들이 궁금해할 지경이었다. 더운 공기가 황금빛으로 고요한 대지 위에서 아른거렸고 농가가 하나둘 뒤로 흘러갔는데, 그 하나하나가 마거릿에게 독일의 목가를, 「헤르만과 도로테아」를, 「에반젤린」을 떠올리게 했다. 그 깨어 있는 꿈에서 그녀는 깨어났다. 기차에서 내려 헬스톤행 삯마차를 탈 곳이었다. 이제 한결 날카로운 감정이 그녀의 가슴을 꿰뚫었는데, 그것이 아픔인지 기쁨인지 그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다. 1마일 1마일마다 추억이 배어 있었다. 세상 무엇과도 바꾸고 싶지 않은 추억이었으나, 그 하나하나가 이루 말할 수 없는 그리움으로 “이제는 없는 날들”을 부르짖게 만들었다. 그녀가 이 길을 마지막으로 지난 것은 아버지 어머니와 함께 이곳을 떠나던 때였다. 그날도 그 계절도 음울했고 그녀 자신도 앞이 캄캄했지만, 두 분은 그녀 곁에 있었다. 이제 그녀는 혼자였다. 고아였다. 두 분은 이상하게도 그녀 곁을 떠나 이 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸다. 헬스톤 가는 길이 그토록 햇빛에 잠겨 있는 것이, 굽이굽이마다 낯익은 나무 하나하나가 지난 세월과 조금도 다름없이 여름의 영광 속에 그대로인 것이 그녀에게는 아프게 다가왔다. 자연은 아무 변화도 느끼지 않았고 언제까지나 젊었다.\n\n벨 씨는 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 지나가고 있을지 어느 정도 알았기에 슬기롭고도 자상하게 입을 다물고 있었다. 두 사람은 레너드 암스로 마차를 몰아 올라갔다. 반은 농가요 반은 여관인 그곳은 길에서 조금 물러나 서 있었는데, 마치 주인이 나그네들의 발길에 그리 매여 있지 않아 나서서 붙잡을 것도 없으니 오히려 나그네 쪽에서 찾아와야 한다고 말하는 듯했다. 집은 마을 공터를 마주 보고 있었고, 바로 앞에는 아득한 옛날부터 서 있던 보리수 한 그루가 둘레에 온통 걸상을 두른 채 서 있었으며, 그 무성한 잎사귀의 어느 은밀한 구석에는 레너드 집안의 음침한 문장이 걸려 있었다. 여관 문은 활짝 열려 있었으나 나그네를 맞으려는 살가운 서두름은 없었다. 마침내 안주인이 나타났을 때에는 — 그 사이에 물건 여러 개쯤은 슬쩍해 갈 수도 있었으리라 — 마치 초대받은 손님이라도 되는 양 따뜻이 맞아 주었고, 이렇게 늦은 것을 사과하며 지금이 건초 때라 일꾼들 먹을 것을 들로 내보내야 해서 바구니를 꾸리느라 너무 바빠 길 위로 굴러오는 바퀴 소리를 못 들었다고 했다. 큰길을 벗어난 뒤로는 바퀴가 부드럽고 짧은 잔디 위를 달렸던 것이다.\n\n“아이고, 세상에!” 사과가 끝날 무렵 한 줄기 햇살이 그늘진 응접실에서 여태 눈에 띄지 않던 마거릿의 얼굴을 비추자 안주인이 소리쳤다. “헤일 아가씨야, 제니!” 그녀는 문으로 달려가며 딸을 불렀다. “이리 온, 어서 와 봐, 헤일 아가씨셔!” 그러고는 마거릿에게 다가와 어머니 같은 다정함으로 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n“다들 어떻게 지내세요? 목사님하고 딕슨 아주머니는요? 무엇보다 목사님은요! 하느님의 복이 있으시길! 그분이 떠나신 게 우리는 내내 서운했다니까요.”\n\n마거릿은 아버지의 죽음을 말하려 애썼다. 어머니의 죽음은 퍼키스 부인이 그 이름을 빼놓은 것으로 보아 이미 알고 있는 것이 분명했다. 그러나 그녀는 말을 꺼내려다 목이 메었고, 그저 자기의 짙은 상복을 만져 보이며 “아버지”라는 한마디밖에 하지 못했다.\n\n“설마, 나리, 그럴 리가요!” 퍼키스 부인은 방금 머릿속에 떠오른 그 슬픈 짐작을 확인받으려고 벨 씨를 돌아보며 말했다. “봄에 어떤 신사분이 여기 오셨었는데, 하기야 지난겨울만큼 오래전이었을지도 모르지요, 헤일 씨하고 마거릿 아가씨 이야기를 잔뜩 해 주셨어요. 헤일 부인께서 돌아가셨다고, 가엾은 마님이라고요. 그렇지만 목사님이 편찮으시다는 말씀은 한마디도 없었는데요!”\n\n“그렇지만 사실이오.” 벨 씨가 말했다. “옥스퍼드로 나를 찾아오셨다가 아주 갑작스레 세상을 떠나셨소. 좋은 분이었지요, 퍼키스 부인. 우리 가운데 그분처럼 평온한 마지막을 맞을 수 있다면 고마워할 사람이 많을 게요. 자, 마거릿, 얘야! 이 아이 아버지가 내 가장 오랜 벗이고 이 아이는 내 대녀라, 우리 둘이 함께 내려와 옛 고장을 좀 보자 싶었소. 여기서 편안한 방과 훌륭한 저녁상을 내준다는 건 내가 예전부터 알고 있고. 나를 기억하지 못하시는 모양인데, 내 이름은 벨이오. 목사관이 꽉 찼을 때 한두 번 여기서 자고 댁의 맛좋은 에일도 맛본 적이 있소.”\n\n“아이고, 그럼요. 용서하세요. 그런데 보시다시피 제가 헤일 아가씨한테 정신이 팔려서요. 마거릿 아가씨, 방으로 안내해 드릴게요. 거기서 보닛도 벗으시고 세수도 하시고요. 바로 오늘 아침에 갓 딴 장미를 물병에 거꾸로 꽂아 두었답니다. 혹시 누가 오실지도 모른다 싶어서요. 사향장미 한두 송이 향이 밴 샘물만큼 향긋한 게 없거든요. 목사님이 돌아가시다니! 하기야 우리 모두 죽기야 하지요. 그런데 그 신사분 말씀으로는 헤일 부인 상을 당하신 뒤로 많이 추스르셨다던데요.”\n\n“퍼키스 부인, 헤일 양을 살펴 드린 다음에 내게로 내려오시오. 저녁상 일로 상의 좀 합시다.”\n\n마거릿의 침실에 난 자그마한 여닫이창은 장미와 포도 덩굴 가지로 거의 다 막혀 있었다. 그러나 그것을 옆으로 밀치고 몸을 조금 내밀자 나무들 위로 목사관 굴뚝 꼭대기가 보였고, 잎사귀 사이로 낯익은 윤곽이 여럿 분간되었다.\n\n“아유!” 퍼키스 부인은 침대를 매만지고 제니를 시켜 라벤더 향이 밴 수건을 한 아름 가져오게 하며 말했다. “세상이 변했어요, 아가씨. 새로 오신 목사님은 아이가 일곱이나 되는데 더 낳으려고 아이방을 짓고 계세요. 옛날에 정자하고 연장 광이 있던 바로 그 자리에다요. 화덕도 새것으로 갈아 넣으시고 응접실에는 판유리 창을 다셨고요. 그분하고 사모님은 부지런한 분들이라 좋은 일도 많이 하셨어요. 하기야 좋은 일이라고들 하니까요. 그게 아니라면 저는 별것도 아닌 일로 죄다 뒤집어엎었다고 하겠지만요. 새 목사님은 금주가시고요, 아가씨, 치안판사이기도 하시고요. 사모님은 알뜰한 요리법을 잔뜩 아셔서 효모 없이 빵을 만들자고 하세요. 두 분 다 어찌나 말씀이 많은지, 그것도 한꺼번에 하시니 사람을 아주 쓰러뜨려 놓는다니까요. 그러다 두 분이 가시고 좀 조용해지고 나서야 내 쪽에서도 할 말이 있었구나 하는 생각이 든다니까요. 그분은 건초밭 일꾼들 술통을 쫓아다니며 들여다보실 거예요. 그러면 그게 생강 맥주가 아니라고 한바탕 소동이 나겠지만, 저야 어쩌겠어요. 우리 어머니도 그 앞의 우리 할머니도 건초 일꾼들한테 좋은 맥주를 내보냈고, 어디가 아프면 소금하고 센나를 먹었지요. 저도 그 길을 그대로 갈 수밖에요. 헵워스 사모님은 약 대신 사탕을 주고 싶어 하시고 그게 훨씬 기분 좋다고 하시지만, 저는 그런 건 못 믿겠어요. 그런데 가 봐야겠네요, 아가씨. 여쭙고 싶은 게 많은데도요. 이따 곧 다시 올게요.”\n\n마거릿이 아래층에 내려오니 벨 씨는 그녀를 위해 딸기와 크림, 통밀빵 한 덩이, 우유 한 병을 준비해 두고 있었다(자기 혼자 즐길 것으로는 스틸턴 치즈와 포트와인 한 병도 곁들여). 이 소박한 점심을 마치고 두 사람은 산책을 나섰는데, 어느 쪽으로 발길을 돌려야 할지 좀처럼 정하지 못했다. 어느 길이든 정겹고 낯익은 것들이 그리로 오라 손짓했기 때문이다.\n\n“목사관 쪽으로 지나가 볼까?” 벨 씨가 물었다.\n\n“아뇨, 아직요. 이쪽으로 가서 한 바퀴 돌아 그리로 돌아오는 게 좋겠어요.” 마거릿이 말했다.\n\n여기저기 지난가을에 베어 낸 늙은 나무들이 있었고, 무단 정착민이 엉성하게 지어 놓았던 다 쓰러져 가는 오두막도 사라지고 없었다. 마거릿은 그 하나하나가 아쉬웠고 오랜 벗을 잃은 듯 서러웠다. 두 사람은 그녀와 레녹스 씨가 그림을 그리던 자리를 지났다. 그 뿌리 사이에 앉았던, 벼락 맞은 자국이 허옇게 남은 늙은 너도밤나무 둥치는 이제 없었다. 다 허물어져 가던 오두막에 살던 노인은 죽었고, 오두막은 헐렸으며, 그 자리에 말끔하고 번듯한 새 오두막이 들어서 있었다. 너도밤나무가 서 있던 자리에는 자그마한 텃밭이 생겨 있었다.\n\n“제가 이렇게 나이를 먹은 줄은 몰랐어요.” 한동안 말이 없다가 마거릿이 말했다. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 돌아섰다.\n\n“그렇지!” 벨 씨가 말했다. “젊은이에게 시간을 그토록 알 수 없는 것으로 만드는 건 낯익은 것들 사이에 일어나는 첫 변화라네. 그 뒤로는 그 알 수 없다는 느낌마저 잃어버리지. 나는 눈에 보이는 모든 변화를 당연한 일로 받아들이네. 인간사 모두가 덧없다는 건 내게는 익숙한 일이지만, 자네에게는 새롭고 짓누르는 일이지.”\n\n“꼬마 수전을 보러 가요.” 마거릿이 숲 속 빈터의 그늘 아래로 이어지는 풀 덮인 길로 동행을 이끌며 말했다.\n\n“기꺼이. 꼬마 수전이 누구인지는 전혀 짐작도 안 가지만 말일세. 그래도 나는 「소박한 수전」을 봐서라도 수전이라는 이름은 다 좋아하네.”\n\n“제 꼬마 수전은 제가 작별 인사도 없이 떠나서 서운해했어요. 조금만 더 애썼으면 막을 수 있었을 아픔을 그 애에게 준 것이 그 뒤로 내내 마음에 걸렸고요. 그런데 길이 멀어요. 정말 힘들지 않으시겠어요?”\n\n“아무렴. 자네가 그렇게 빨리 걷지만 않는다면 말이지. 보다시피 여기엔 숨 돌리려고 멈춰 설 핑계가 될 만한 경치가 없단 말이야. 내가 덴마크 왕자 햄릿이라면 ‘살찌고 숨 가쁜’ 사람과 함께 걷는 것이 낭만적이라고 여기겠지. 그를 봐서라도 이 늙은이의 병약함을 좀 가엾이 여겨 주게.”\n\n“벨 씨를 위해서 천천히 걸을게요. 저는 햄릿보다 벨 씨가 스무 배는 더 좋아요.”\n\n“산 나귀가 죽은 사자보다 낫다는 이치로?”\n\n“아마 그렇겠죠. 저는 제 마음을 따져 보지 않아요.”\n\n“나는 자네가 나를 좋아한다는 것만으로 만족하네. 그것이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 지나치게 캐묻지 않고 말이야. 다만 달팽이 걸음으로 걸을 것까지는 없겠지.”\n\n“좋아요. 벨 씨 걸음대로 걸으세요, 제가 따라갈게요. 아니면 제가 너무 빨리 가거든 스스로 견주어 보신 그 햄릿처럼 가만히 멈춰 서서 사색에 잠기시든가요.”\n\n“고맙네. 그렇지만 내 어머니가 내 아버지를 죽이고 나서 내 숙부와 혼인한 것도 아니니, 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 알 수가 있어야지. 저녁상이 잘 차려질 가망이 얼마나 되는지 저울질하는 것 말고는 말이야. 자네 생각은 어떤가?”\n\n“저는 기대가 커요. 헬스톤 사람들이 보기에 그분은 이름난 요리 솜씨였거든요.”\n\n“하지만 이 건초 만들기 소동 때문에 마음이 산란해진 것은 생각해 봤나?”\n\n마거릿은 아무것도 아닌 일로 유쾌한 이야기를 꾸며 내며 자기가 지난날을 너무 골똘히 곱씹지 않게 하려는 벨 씨의 배려를 모두 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 그녀로서는 이 정겨운 길들을 차라리 말없이 걷고 싶었다. 혼자였으면 좋겠다고 바랄 만큼 은혜를 모르는 것은 아니라 하더라도.\n\n두 사람은 수전의 홀어머니가 사는 오두막에 이르렀다. 수전은 없었다. 교구 학교에 가고 없었다. 마거릿은 실망했고, 그 가엾은 여인은 그것을 알아채고는 변명 비슷한 것을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n“아니에요, 잘된 일이죠.” 마거릿이 말했다. “정말 반가운 소식이에요. 제가 미처 생각했어야 했는데. 다만 예전에는 아주머니 곁에 붙어 있었잖아요.”\n\n“네, 그랬죠. 그래서 어찌나 허전한지 몰라요. 밤이면 제가 아는 얼마 안 되는 걸 가르치곤 했지요. 대단할 건 없었지만요. 그래도 그 애가 어찌나 야무진 아이가 되어 가던지, 없으니 사무치게 아쉽구먼요. 그래도 이젠 배운 게 저보다 한참 위지요.” 그러고는 어머니가 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n“내가 다 틀렸구먼.” 벨 씨가 투덜거렸다. “내 말은 신경 쓰지 마시오. 나는 세상보다 100년은 뒤처진 사람이니. 그래도 내 말은, 그 아이가 집에 남아 어머니를 도우며 밤마다 곁에서 신약 한 장씩 읽는 법을 배우는 편이 세상의 온갖 학교 교육을 다 받는 것보다 더 낫고 더 소박하고 더 자연스러운 가르침이었으리라는 게요.”\n\n마거릿은 대꾸를 해서 그가 말을 이어 가도록 부추기고 싶지 않았고, 어머니 앞에서 논쟁을 길게 끌고 싶지도 않았다. 그래서 여인 쪽으로 돌아서서 물었다.\n\n“베티 반스 할머니는 어떻게 지내세요?”\n\n“모르겠구먼요.” 여인이 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다. “우린 사이가 틀어졌어요.”\n\n“왜요?” 예전에 마을의 화해꾼 노릇을 하던 마거릿이 물었다.\n\n“내 고양이를 훔쳐 갔거든요.”\n\n“그게 아주머니 것인 줄 알았대요?”\n\n“모르죠. 몰랐지 싶어요.”\n\n“그럼 아주머니 것이라고 말하고 도로 찾아올 수는 없었어요?”\n\n“없죠! 태워 버렸으니까요.”\n\n“태워요!” 마거릿과 벨 씨가 동시에 소리쳤다.\n\n“구웠다고요!” 여인이 설명했다.\n\n그것은 설명이 되지 못했다. 마거릿은 거듭 캐물은 끝에 그 끔찍한 사실을 알아냈다. 베티 반스가 어느 집시 점쟁이의 꾐에 넘어가 남편의 나들이옷을 빌려준 일이 있었는데, 반스 영감이 옷이 없어진 것을 알아채기 전인 토요일 밤에 틀림없이 돌려주겠다는 약속이었다. 그런데 옷이 나타나지 않자 겁이 났고 남편의 노여움이 두려워진 나머지 그 주술에 기댔던 것이다. 시골의 모진 미신 가운데 하나에 따르면, 산 채로 삶기거나 구워지는 고통 속에서 지르는 고양이의 울음소리가 어둠의 힘으로 하여금 그 짓을 저지른 자의 소원을 들어주지 않을 수 없게 만든다는 것이었다. 그 가엾은 여인은 그 효험을 정말로 믿고 있는 것이 분명했다. 그녀의 유일한 감정은 하필 자기 고양이가 다른 모든 고양이를 제쳐 두고 제물로 골라졌다는 데 대한 분함뿐이었다. 마거릿은 소름이 끼친 채 듣고 있었고 그 여인의 생각을 깨우쳐 보려 애썼으나 헛일이었다. 결국 절망하여 단념할 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 한 걸음 한 걸음 여인에게서 몇 가지 사실을 인정받아 냈고, 그 논리적인 연결과 순서는 마거릿에게 더없이 분명했다. 그러나 끝에 가서 그 어리둥절해진 여인은 그저 처음에 한 말을 되풀이할 뿐이었다. 즉 “그야 참말 모진 짓이고 나 같으면 하고 싶지 않지만, 사람 소원 들어주는 데는 그만한 게 없다고, 평생을 두고 그리 들어 왔다고, 그래도 참 모진 짓이기는 하다고.” 마거릿은 절망 속에 단념하고 가슴이 아려 오는 채로 걸어 나왔다.\n\n“나를 두고 우쭐거리지 않다니 착한 아가씨로군.” 벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n“네? 그게 무슨 말씀이세요?”\n\n“학교 교육에 대해 내가 틀렸다는 것을 인정하네. 저런 실속 있는 이교 속에서 저 아이를 키우느니 무엇이든 낫지.”\n\n“아! 생각났어요. 가엾은 꼬마 수전! 그 애를 보러 가야겠어요. 학교에 들르는 것 괜찮으시겠어요?”\n\n“조금도 싫지 않네. 나도 그 아이가 받는다는 가르침이 어떤 것인지 좀 보고 싶으니.”\n\n두 사람은 더 말을 나누지 않고 나무 우거진 골짜기를 여럿 헤치며 나아갔다. 그 부드러운 초록빛도 그런 잔혹한 이야기를 들은 마거릿의 가슴에 남은 충격과 아픔을 씻어 주지는 못했다. 게다가 그 이야기를 전하는 태도에는 상상력이라고는 도무지 없다는 것이, 따라서 고통받는 짐승에 대한 어떤 연민도 없다는 것이 그대로 드러나 있었다.\n\n바쁜 인간 벌들의 벌통이 웅웅거리는 듯한 말소리가, 숲을 벗어나 학교가 자리한 좀 더 트인 마을 공터로 나서자마자 들려왔다. 문은 활짝 열려 있었고 두 사람은 안으로 들어섰다. 여기저기 사방을 누비던 검은 옷차림의 활달한 부인이 두 사람을 알아보고 어딘가 안주인 같은 태도로 맞아들였다. 마거릿은 어쩌다 학교를 둘러보러 오는 손님이 들르면 어머니도 그런 태도를 지었던 것을 기억했다. 다만 어머니 쪽이 훨씬 부드럽고 나른했을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 그 부인이 지금의 목사 부인, 즉 어머니의 후임이라는 것을 대뜸 알아차렸다. 그리고 될 수만 있었다면 그 만남에서 물러섰을 것이다. 그러나 곧 그 마음을 이겨 내고 얌전히 앞으로 나아갔고, 알아보는 반가운 눈길을 여럿 마주쳤으며 반쯤 죽인 “헤일 아가씨야” 하는 수군거림도 여럿 들었다. 목사 부인은 그 이름을 듣고 대뜸 한결 다정한 태도가 되었다. 마거릿은 그 태도가 동시에 한결 생색내는 투가 되었다고 느끼지 않을 수 있었으면 싶었다. 부인은 벨 씨에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n“아버님이시군요, 헤일 양. 닮은 데를 보니 알겠네요. 뵙게 되어 정말 반갑습니다, 선생님. 목사님도 그러실 거예요.”\n\n마거릿은 아버지가 아니라고 설명하고는 아버지가 세상을 떠났다는 사실을 더듬더듬 말했다. 그러면서 내내, 만일 목사 부인이 짐작한 대로였다면 아버지가 헬스톤을 다시 찾는 일을 어떻게 견뎠을까 생각했다. 그녀는 헵워스 부인이 하는 말을 듣지 못했고, 대꾸는 벨 씨에게 맡긴 채 옛 낯익은 얼굴들을 찾아 둘러보았다.\n\n“아! 수업을 하나 맡고 싶으신 게로군요, 헤일 양. 제가 그 마음을 잘 알지요. 1반, 일어서서 헤일 양과 문장 분석 수업을 하세요.”\n\n가엾은 마거릿은 이번 방문이 감상에서 온 것이지 조금도 시찰하러 온 것이 아니었으므로 꼼짝없이 걸려들었다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그것이 어떤 식으로든 그녀를 한때 잘 알던, 아버지에게서 엄숙한 세례를 받았던 그 조그맣고 초롱초롱한 얼굴들과 맞닿게 해 주었으므로 그녀는 자리에 앉았고, 아이들의 달라진 이목구비를 더듬어 보느라 반쯤 넋을 잃은 채 아무도 보지 못하게 수전의 손을 잠깐 잡고 있었다. 그동안 1반 아이들은 책을 찾았고, 목사 부인은 숙녀로서 할 수 있는 한 벨 씨의 단추라도 붙들 듯이 바싹 다가서서 그에게 발음 교수법을 설명하고 그 문제로 장학관과 나눈 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n마거릿은 책 위로 몸을 숙인 채 그것 말고는 아무것도 보지 않았다. 그리고 아이들 목소리의 웅성거림을 들으니 옛 시절이 떠올라 그것을 생각하는 사이 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그러다 별안간 수업이 멈추었다. 여자아이 하나가 보기에 단순하기 짝이 없는 “a”라는 낱말에서 걸려, 그것을 무어라 불러야 할지 몰라 하고 있었다.\n\n“a는 부정관사예요.” 마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n“죄송하지만요.” 눈과 귀를 온통 곤두세우고 있던 목사 부인이 말했다. “저희는 밀섬 선생님께 ‘a’를 무엇이라고 부르라고 배웠지요, 누가 기억하고 있나요?”\n\n“절대 형용사요.” 대여섯 목소리가 한꺼번에 말했다. 그러자 마거릿은 무안해져서 앉아 있었다. 아이들이 그녀보다 더 많이 알고 있었다. 벨 씨는 고개를 돌리고 웃었다.\n\n마거릿은 수업 내내 더는 말하지 않았다. 그러나 수업이 끝난 뒤 조용히 옛날에 유난히 예뻐하던 아이 한둘에게 돌아가 잠시 이야기를 나누었다. 아이들은 어린것에서 어엿한 처녀로 자라 가고 있었다. 그 빠른 성장 속에 아이들이 그녀의 기억에서 지워져 가듯, 그녀 또한 3년의 부재로 아이들의 기억에서 사라져 가고 있었다. 그래도 그녀는 아이들을 모두 다시 본 것이 기뻤다. 그 기쁨에 슬픔의 빛이 섞이기는 했지만. 그날 수업이 끝났을 때는 아직 여름 오후의 이른 시간이었다. 헵워스 부인은 마거릿에게 벨 씨와 함께 목사관으로 가서 지금 목사가 하고 있는 “개량”을 보고 가라고 청했다. 그 말이 입에서 반쯤 흘러나오다가 그녀는 좀 더 조심스러운 “변경”이라는 말로 바꾸었다. 마거릿은 그 변경 따위를 보는 데는 털끝만큼도 관심이 없었다. 그것은 제 집이 어떠했는가에 대한 애틋한 기억에 거슬리기만 했다. 그러나 옛 그 자리를 한 번 더 보고 싶었다. 느끼게 되리라는 것을 알고 있는 그 아픔에 몸이 움츠러들면서도.\n\n목사관은 안팎으로 어찌나 달라졌던지 실제로 느낀 아픔은 예상보다 덜했다. 같은 곳 같지가 않았다. 예전에는 장미 잎사귀 하나만 떨어져 있어도 그 흠잡을 데 없는 정돈과 단정함에 얼룩처럼 보일 만큼 곱게 손질되어 있던 뜰과 잔디밭은 아이들의 물건으로 어질러져 있었다. 여기엔 구슬 주머니, 저기엔 굴렁쇠. 밀짚모자 하나가 장미나무에 걸이라도 되는 양 눌러 씌워져 있어, 꽃이 잔뜩 달린 길고 아름다운 여린 가지 하나가 상해 있었다. 지난날 같았으면 사랑스러운 것인 양 조심스레 매어 길렀을 가지였다. 돗자리 깔린 자그마한 네모난 현관도 명랑하고 튼튼하고 거친 아이들의 흔적으로 가득했다.\n\n“아유!” 헵워스 부인이 말했다. “이 어수선한 꼴을 용서하세요, 헤일 양. 아이방이 다 되면 좀 정돈을 하자고 우길 참이에요. 아이방을 아가씨가 쓰시던 방을 헐어 짓고 있는 것 같은데요. 헤일 양, 아이방도 없이 어떻게 지내셨어요?”\n\n“저희는 둘뿐이었으니까요.” 마거릿이 말했다. “아이들이 많으신 모양이지요?”\n\n“일곱이에요. 여기 좀 보세요! 이쪽으로 길 쪽에 창을 내고 있답니다. 헵워스 씨가 이 집에 어마어마한 돈을 쓰고 있어요. 그렇지만 정말이지 저희가 왔을 때는 사람이 살 만한 집이 못 되었거든요. 저희처럼 식구가 많은 집에는요, 물론 그런 뜻이지요.” 헵워스 부인이 말한 그 방 말고도 집 안의 방이란 방은 모두 달라져 있었다. 그 방은 예전에 헤일 씨의 서재였다. 그곳의 초록빛 어스름과 감미로운 고요는 그 자신이 말한 대로 사색하는 버릇을 길러 주었으나, 어쩌면 어느 정도는 행동보다 생각에 더 알맞은 성품을 빚어 놓기도 했을 것이다. 새로 낸 창은 길이 내다보였고, 헵워스 부인이 짚어 준 대로 이로운 점이 많았다. 그 창으로는 남편의 양 떼 가운데 길 잃은 것들이 저희 딴에는 남의 눈에 안 띄기를 바라며 유혹적인 술집으로 슬금슬금 새어 나가는 모습이 보였다. 실은 남의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 부지런한 목사는 가장 정통적인 설교문을 지으면서도 길에서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 모자와 지팡이를 손 닿는 곳에 걸어 두었다가 낚아채고는 교구민들을 뒤쫓아 나섰기 때문이다. 그러니 금주가 목사에게 붙들리기 전에 “유쾌한 삼림지기”로 피신하려면 다리가 재빨라야 했다. 온 식구가 재바르고 활달하고 목소리가 크고 마음씨가 고왔으되, 섬세한 눈치 따위로 괴로워하는 법은 없었다. 마거릿은 벨 씨가 자기 취향에 특히 거슬리는 것마다 짐짓 감탄해 보이는 것으로 헵워스 부인을 놀리고 있다는 것을 부인이 알아채지나 않을까 걱정했다. 그러나 아니었다! 부인은 그것을 죄다 곧이곧대로, 그토록 순진하게 받아들였고, 그래서 목사관에서 여관 쪽으로 천천히 걸어 나오면서 마거릿은 그에게 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“꾸짖지 말게, 마거릿. 다 자네 때문이었네. 그 부인이 이러이러하게 고치면 얼마나 나아지는지 안다는 우월한 안목에 그토록 대놓고 우쭐거리며 자네에게 변화란 변화를 죄다 보여 주지만 않았어도 나는 얌전히 굴 수 있었을 걸세. 그래도 굳이 설교를 늘어놓아야겠거든 저녁 먹은 뒤까지 아껴 두게. 그때라면 나를 잠들게 해 주고 소화도 도와줄 테니.”\n\n두 사람 다 지쳐 있었고 마거릿은 특히 그러해서, 어릴 적 집에서 그토록 가까운 숲과 들판을 한 번 더 거닐어 보겠다던 애초의 생각을 접고 나가고 싶어 하지 않았다. 그리고 어쩐지 이번 헬스톤 방문은 전부가, 그녀가 기대했던 것과 꼭 그대로는 아니었다. 어디에나 변화가 있었다. 사소하되 모든 것에 스며 있는 변화였다. 집집마다 누가 떠나거나 죽거나 혼인하거나, 아니면 나날과 달과 해가 가져오는 자연스러운 변천으로 달라져 있었다. 그 변천은 우리를 어린 시절에서 젊음으로, 다시 장년을 거쳐 노년으로 알아채지 못하게 실어 가고, 마침내 우리는 무르익은 열매처럼 고요한 어머니 대지로 떨어진다. 장소도 달라져 있었다. 여기서 나무 한 그루가 없어지고 저기서 가지 하나가 사라져 전에는 빛이 들지 않던 곳에 긴 빛줄기가 들어왔다. 길은 반듯이 다듬어져 좁아졌고, 그 곁에 제멋대로 뻗어 있던 푸른 오솔길은 울타리가 쳐지고 밭이 되어 있었다. 사람들은 그것을 큰 개량이라 불렀다. 그러나 마거릿은 옛날의 그 그림 같은 정취를, 옛날의 그 어스름을, 지난날 길가의 풀밭을 두고 한숨지었다. 그녀는 창가의 작은 걸상에 앉아 몰려드는 밤의 그림자를 서글피 내다보았다. 그 그림자는 그녀의 시름겨운 생각과 잘 어울렸다. 벨 씨는 그날 하루 평소 없던 운동을 한 탓에 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 마침내 그는 차 쟁반이 들어오는 바람에 깨어났다. 상기된 얼굴의 시골 처녀가 그것을 들고 왔는데, 이날 건초밭 일을 거들면서 여느 때의 시중드는 일에서 벗어나 색다른 재미를 맛본 것이 분명했다.\n\n“어! 거기 누구요! 여기가 어디지? 저건 누구야, 마거릿인가? 아, 이제 다 생각났네. 대체 어떤 여자가 저렇게 처량한 자세로, 두 손을 무릎 위에 곧게 맞잡고 얼굴을 저리 꼼짝 않고 앞으로 향한 채 앉아 있나 했지. 무얼 보고 있는 겐가?” 벨 씨가 창가로 다가와 마거릿 뒤에 서며 물었다.\n\n“아무것도 아니에요.” 그녀가 얼른 일어서며, 갑작스러운 순간에 낼 수 있는 한 가장 밝은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“아무것도 아니라니! 을씨년스러운 나무들이 배경이고, 들장미 울타리에 흰 빨래가 널려 있고, 눅눅한 바람이 한 자락 불어오는군. 창을 닫고 이리 와서 차를 따르게.”\n\n마거릿은 한동안 말이 없었다. 찻숟가락을 만지작거리며 벨 씨가 하는 말에 별로 귀 기울이지 않았다. 그가 그녀의 말을 반박해도 그녀는 그가 동의하기라도 한 양 웃음 띤 얼굴로 그의 의견을 받아 넘겼다. 이윽고 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 숟가락을 내려놓더니, 아무 맥락도 없이, 그리고 말하는 이가 꺼내고 싶은 화제를 얼마 동안 마음에 두고 있었음을 흔히 드러내는 그 높다란 목소리로 말을 꺼냈다. “벨 씨, 어젯밤에 우리가 프레더릭 오빠 이야기를 한 것 기억하시죠?”\n\n“어젯밤이라. 내가 어디 있었더라? 아, 생각났네! 아니, 일주일은 지난 것 같군. 그래, 그럼, 그 딱한 사람 이야기를 한 것이 기억나네.”\n\n“네. 그리고 레녹스 씨가 어머니께서 돌아가실 무렵에 오빠가 영국에 와 있었다고 말한 것도 기억하세요?” 마거릿의 목소리가 이제 여느 때보다 낮았다.\n\n“기억하네. 그전에는 들은 적이 없었지.”\n\n“저는 아버지께서 그 이야기를 하신 줄로만, 늘 그런 줄로만 알았어요.”\n\n“아니야! 한 번도 안 하셨네. 그런데 그게 어쨌다는 건가, 마거릿?”\n\n“그 무렵에 제가 아주 잘못한 일이 있는데, 그 이야기를 하고 싶어요.” 마거릿이 맑고 정직한 눈으로 갑자기 그를 올려다보며 말했다. “저는 거짓말을 했어요!” 그러고는 얼굴이 새빨개졌다.\n\n“그렇군, 그건 나쁜 일이었지, 나도 인정하네. 하기야 나도 평생 꽤 많은 거짓말을 했지만 말이야. 자네처럼 대놓고 말로 한 것은 아니어도 행동으로, 또는 어딘가 옹색하게 에두르는 방식으로 남들이 참을 믿지 않게 하거나 거짓을 믿게 만들었지. 거짓의 아비가 누구인지 알지, 마거릿? 그래! 스스로 무척 착하다고 여기는 사람들이 참 많은데, 그들도 거짓과 묘한 인연을 맺고 있다네. 떳떳지 못한 혼인이며 육촌쯤 되는 인연 말이야. 거짓이라는 더러운 피는 우리 모두에게 흐르고 있어. 자네만은 여느 사람보다 그것에서 멀리 있으리라 여겼네만. 아니, 우는 겐가, 얘야? 이런 식으로 끝날 이야기라면 그만두세. 자네가 그 일을 뉘우쳤고 다시는 그러지 않으리라는 것은 짐작이 가네. 게다가 이젠 오래전 일이고. 요컨대 나는 오늘 저녁 자네가 아주 유쾌하고 조금도 슬프지 않기를 바라네.”\n\n마거릿은 눈을 닦고 다른 이야기를 해 보려 애썼으나 갑자기 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“제발, 벨 씨, 그 이야기를 하게 해 주세요. 어쩌면 저를 조금 도와주실 수 있을지도 몰라요. 아니, 도와주시는 게 아니라, 진실을 아시면 어쩌면 저를 바로잡아 주실 수도, 결국 그것도 아니네요.” 그녀는 뜻하는 대로 좀 더 정확하게 표현하지 못하는 것이 안타까워 이렇게 말했다.\n\n벨 씨의 태도가 온통 달라졌다. “다 이야기해 보게, 얘야.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“긴 이야기예요. 하지만 프레드 오빠가 왔을 때 어머니는 몹시 편찮으셨고 저는 걱정으로 넋이 나가 있었어요. 게다가 제가 오빠를 위험으로 끌어들인 게 아닐까 두렵기도 했고요. 그리고 어머니께서 돌아가신 직후에 놀랄 일이 있었어요. 딕슨이 밀턴에서 어떤 사람을 만났거든요. 레너즈라는 남자였는데, 오빠를 알던 사람이었고 오빠에게 무슨 원한이 있는 듯했어요. 아니면 적어도 오빠를 잡으면 준다는 현상금 생각에 마음이 동한 듯했고요. 이 새로운 겁 때문에 저는 오빠를 서둘러 런던으로 보내는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 며칠 전 밤에 우리가 한 이야기로 아시겠지만, 오빠는 거기서 재판을 받을 경우 가망이 어떻겠는지 레녹스 씨와 상의하기로 되어 있었으니까요. 그래서 우리는, 그러니까 오빠와 저는 기차역으로 갔어요. 어느 날 저녁이었고 막 어스름이 내리려던 참이었지만, 그래도 서로 알아보고 알아보일 만큼은 밝았어요. 그리고 우리는 너무 일찍 갔던 터라 바로 옆에 있는 들판으로 걸어 나갔어요. 저는 그 레너즈라는 사람 때문에 늘 조마조마했어요. 그가 이 근처 어딘가에 있다는 걸 알고 있었으니까요. 그런데 우리가 들판에 있을 때, 나직한 붉은 햇빛이 바로 제 얼굴에 비치고 있는데, 우리가 서 있던 들판 층계 바로 아래 길로 누군가 말을 타고 지나갔어요. 그가 저를 보는 것이 보였지만 저는 처음엔 그가 누구인지 몰랐어요. 해가 눈에 너무 비쳐서요. 그런데 순간 눈부심이 걷히고 저는 그가 손턴 씨라는 걸 알았어요. 그리고 우리는 인사를 나누었죠——”\n\n“그리고 그가 물론 프레더릭을 보았겠지.” 벨 씨가 이야기를 거들어 준다고 생각하며 말했다.\n\n“네. 그리고 역에서 한 남자가 다가왔어요. 술에 취해 비틀거리면서요. 그가 프레드 오빠의 멱살을 잡으려 했는데, 오빠가 몸을 홱 뿌리치는 바람에 중심을 잃고 승강장 울타리 너머로 떨어졌어요. 멀지도 깊지도 않았어요. 3피트가 채 안 됐고요. 그런데, 아, 벨 씨! 어쩐 일인지 그 추락이 그를 죽게 했어요!”\n\n“저런 딱한 일이. 그게 그 레너즈였겠군. 그런데 프레드는 어떻게 빠져나갔나?”\n\n“아! 오빠는 그 사람이 떨어진 직후에 바로 떠났어요. 우리는 그 일로 그 딱한 사람이 다치리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했고요. 아주 가벼운 상처로 보였거든요.”\n\n“그럼 그가 그 자리에서 죽은 것은 아니고?”\n\n“아니에요! 이삼일 뒤였어요. 그리고 그다음이, 아, 벨 씨! 이제 나쁜 대목이 나와요.” 그녀는 손가락을 초조하게 맞비틀며 말했다. “경찰 경위가 와서, 레너즈를 죽음에 이르게 한 그 젊은 남자와 제가 동행이었다고 저를 몰아세웠어요. 그건 거짓 혐의였지만, 아시다시피 우리는 프레드 오빠가 배를 탔다는 소식을 듣지 못한 상태였어요. 오빠가 아직 런던에 있을 수도 있고 이 거짓 혐의로 붙잡힐 수도 있고, 그러다 반란을 일으켰다고 고발당한 헤일 중위와 같은 사람이라는 게 드러나면 총살당할 수도 있었어요. 이 모든 것이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔고, 저는 제가 아니라고 말했어요. 그날 밤 저는 기차역에 있지 않았다고요. 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모른다고요. 저에게는 프레더릭 오빠를 구하는 것 말고는 아무 양심도 생각도 없었어요.”\n\n“나는 그게 옳았다고 말하겠네. 나라도 똑같이 했을 걸세. 자네는 남을 생각하느라 자기를 잊은 것이야. 나도 그렇게 했기를 바라네.”\n\n“아니에요, 그러지 않으셨을 거예요. 그건 그릇된 일이었고 불순종이었고 믿음 없는 짓이었어요. 바로 그때 프레드 오빠는 무사히 영국을 떠나 있었고, 저는 눈이 멀어서 제가 그곳에 있었다는 것을 증언할 수 있는 또 다른 증인이 있다는 걸 잊고 있었어요.”\n\n“누구?”\n\n“손턴 씨요. 아시다시피 그분은 역 가까이에서 저를 보았어요. 우리는 서로 인사를 나누었고요.”\n\n“허! 그 사람이 이 소동에 대해서, 그 취한 자의 죽음에 대해서 알 리가 없지 않은가. 그 상처가 아무 일도 아니었던 모양이지.”\n\n“아니에요! 검시에서 이야기가 나오기 시작하던 절차는 중단되었어요. 손턴 씨는 그 일을 다 알고 계셨어요. 그분은 치안판사였고, 죽음의 원인이 그 추락이 아니라는 것을 밝혀내셨어요. 하지만 그건 제가 한 말을 알고 난 뒤였어요. 아, 벨 씨!” 그녀는 그 기억이 있는 자리에서 자신을 숨기고 싶기라도 한 듯 갑자기 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n“그와 무슨 해명이라도 했나? 그 강한 본능적인 동기를 그에게 이야기한 적이 있나?”\n\n“본능적으로 믿음이 없어서, 가라앉지 않으려고 죄를 붙잡은 거죠.” 그녀가 씁쓸하게 말했다. “아니요! 어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠어요? 그분은 프레더릭 오빠에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어요. 그분이 저를 좋게 보도록 제 자리를 바로잡자고, 가엾은 프레더릭 오빠가 완전히 혐의를 벗을 가망까지 걸려 있는 듯한 우리 집안의 비밀을 그분에게 이야기해야 했겠어요? 프레드 오빠의 마지막 말은 자기가 왔다 간 것을 누구에게도 비밀로 하라는 당부였어요. 보세요, 아버지도 벨 씨에게조차 말씀하지 않으셨잖아요. 아니에요! 저는 그 부끄러움을 견딜 수 있었어요. 적어도 견딜 수 있으리라 생각했어요. 그리고 견뎠고요. 손턴 씨는 그 뒤로 저를 존중한 적이 없어요.”\n\n“그는 자네를 존중하네, 틀림없이.” 벨 씨가 말했다. “하기야 그것으로 얼마간 설명이 되는군——. 그래도 그는 늘 자네를 존경과 흠모로 이야기한다네. 이제야 그의 태도에 어딘가 유보하는 데가 있었던 까닭을 알겠네만.”\n\n마거릿은 말이 없었다. 벨 씨가 하는 말에 귀 기울이지도, 그것을 알아듣지도 못했다. 이윽고 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그분이 저에 대해 말할 때 태도에 ‘유보하는 데’가 있었다는 게 무슨 말씀이신지 말씀해 주시겠어요?”\n\n“아! 그저 내가 자네를 칭찬하는데 그가 맞장구를 치지 않아서 언짢았다는 게지. 늙은 바보처럼 나는 누구나 나와 같은 생각일 줄 알았는데, 그는 분명히 내 말에 동의할 수 없었던 게야. 그때는 어리둥절했지. 그렇지만 그 일이 조금도 해명되지 않았다면 그도 어리둥절할밖에. 우선 자네가 어둠 속에서 젊은 남자와 함께 걸어 나갔다는 것부터가——”\n\n“하지만 그건 제 오빠였어요!” 마거릿이 놀라서 말했다.\n\n“그렇지. 그렇지만 그가 그것을 어떻게 알겠나?”\n\n“모르겠어요. 저는 그런 것은 조금도 생각해 보지 않았어요.” 마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 상하고 언짢은 표정으로 말했다.\n\n“아마 그도 끝내 그런 생각은 하지 않았을 게야. 그 거짓말만 아니었더라면 말이지. 그 거짓말은 그 처지에서는 어쩔 수 없었다고 나는 여전히 주장하네만.”\n\n“어쩔 수 없지 않았어요. 이제는 알아요. 저는 그것을 뼈저리게 뉘우쳐요.”\n\n오랫동안 말이 없었다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 마거릿이었다.\n\n“저는 아마 손턴 씨를 다시 만날 일이 없을 거예요.” 그러고는 거기서 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“그보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 많다고 나는 말하겠네.” 벨 씨가 대답했다.\n\n“그래도 저는 다시는 뵐 일이 없으리라 믿어요. 그래도 어쩐지 사람이란, 어떤 벗의 눈에 제가 그분의 눈에 그리된 것처럼 그토록 낮게 떨어지는 것은 좋아하지 않잖아요.” 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했으나 목소리는 흔들리지 않았고, 벨 씨는 그녀를 보고 있지 않았다. “그리고 이제 프레더릭 오빠가 자기 혐의를 벗고 영국으로 돌아올 모든 희망을, 거의 모든 바람마저 버린 마당이니, 이 모든 것을 해명하는 것이 저 자신에게 공정한 일일 뿐이겠지요. 괜찮으시다면, 그리고 하실 수 있다면, 좋은 기회가 생기거든, 억지로 해명을 들이대지는 마세요, 부디, 하지만 하실 수 있다면 그분에게 사정을 다 말씀해 주시겠어요? 그리고 제가 그렇게 해도 좋다고 허락했다고도 말씀해 주세요. 다시 만날 일이 없을지라도 가엾은 아버지를 봐서라도 그분의 존중을 잃고 싶지는 않다고 느꼈으니까요.”\n\n“물론이지. 그는 알아야 마땅하다고 생각하네. 나는 자네가 부적절하다는 그림자 밑에 조금이라도 놓여 있는 게 싫으이. 그도 자네가 젊은 남자와 단둘이 있는 것을 보고 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 몰랐을 게야.”\n\n“그 점에 대해서라면,” 마거릿이 자못 도도하게 말했다. “저는 ‘악을 생각하는 자에게 화 있을진저’라고 봐요. 그래도 손쉽게 해명할 자연스러운 기회가 생긴다면 해명이 되기를 바라요. 하지만 제가 그분에게 이야기가 전해지기를 바라는 것은 부적절한 처신을 했다는 의심을 벗기 위해서가 아니에요. 그분이 저를 의심했다고 생각했다면 저는 그분이 저를 좋게 보든 말든 개의치 않았을 거예요. 아니에요! 제가 어떻게 유혹을 받았고 어떻게 그 덫에 빠졌는지, 요컨대 제가 왜 그 거짓말을 했는지 그분이 알게 되기를 바라는 거예요.”\n\n“나는 그 일로 자네를 탓하지 않네. 내가 자네 편을 들어서 하는 말이 아닐세, 정말이야.”\n\n“다른 사람들이 그것이 옳으냐 그르냐를 어떻게 생각하는지는, 그것이 그르다는 저 자신의 깊은 앎, 타고난 확신에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요. 하지만 괜찮으시다면 그 이야기는 더 하지 않겠어요. 이미 지난 일이에요. 제 죄는 이미 저질러졌어요. 이제는 그것을 뒤로 하고, 할 수만 있다면 앞으로는 언제까지나 진실하게 살아야지요.”\n\n“좋아. 자네가 불편하고 병적으로 지내고 싶다면 그러게. 나는 내 양심을 늘 도깨비 상자처럼 꽉 닫아 두네. 그게 튀어나오면 그 덩치에 내가 놀라거든. 그래서 어부가 정령을 구슬리듯 나도 그것을 구슬려 도로 집어넣지. ‘놀랍구나’ 하고 나는 말하네. ‘그토록 오래, 그토록 작은 틈에 숨어 있었다니, 나는 정말 네가 있는 줄도 몰랐구나. 부디, 나리, 순간마다 점점 더 커져서 그 자욱한 윤곽으로 나를 어리둥절하게 하지 말고, 한 번만 더 예전 크기로 몸을 줄여 주지 않겠소?’ 그렇게 해서 그놈을 들여보내고 나면 내가 항아리에 봉인을 찍고, 다시 열 때는 얼마나 조심하는지, 그리고 그놈을 거기 가둔, 사람 가운데 가장 지혜로운 솔로몬을 거스르지 않으려고 얼마나 애쓰는지 아나.”\n\n그러나 마거릿에게는 웃을 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 벨 씨가 하는 말에 거의 귀를 기울이지 않았다. 그녀의 생각은 전부터 품고 있었으나 이제는 확신의 힘을 얻은 그 생각에 머물러 있었다. 손턴 씨가 더는 예전처럼 자기를 좋게 여기지 않는다는 것, 그가 자기에게 실망했다는 것이었다. 그녀는 어떤 해명으로도 자신이 제자리를 되찾을 수 있으리라고 느껴지지 않았다. 그의 사랑 안에서가 아니었다. 그것도, 그에 대한 자기 쪽의 어떤 응답도 결코 마음에 두지 않겠다고 그녀는 다짐했고 그 다짐을 엄격히 지켰다. 다만 제럴드 그리핀의 아름다운 시구에 담긴 그 마음으로 그가 언제까지나 기꺼이 이렇게 해 주기를 바랐던, 그 존경과 높은 평가 안에서였다.\n\n“그대 내 이름 소리를 들을 때 돌아서서 뒤를 돌아보아 주기를.”\n\n그것을 생각하는 내내 그녀는 자꾸만 목이 메어 삼켰다. 그가 자기를 어떤 사람으로 상상하든 그것이 자기가 어떤 사람인가 하는 사실을 바꾸지는 못한다는 생각으로 스스로를 달래 보려 했다. 그러나 그것은 뻔한 말이었고 허깨비였으며, 그녀의 회한의 무게 아래 무너져 내렸다. 그녀는 벨 씨에게 묻고 싶은 물음을 스무 가지나 혀끝에 달고 있었으나 하나도 입 밖에 내지 못했다. 벨 씨는 그녀가 피곤한 줄 알고 일찍 방으로 올려 보냈다. 그녀는 그곳에서 오랜 시간 열린 창가에 앉아, 별들이 떠올랐다가 반짝이다가 그늘 짙은 큰 나무들 뒤로 사라질 때까지 머리 위의 자줏빛 하늘을 내다보다가 잠자리에 들었다. 밤새도록 땅 위에도 작은 불빛 하나가 타고 있었다. 그녀의 옛 침실에 켜진 촛불이었는데, 그곳은 새 아이방이 지어질 때까지 지금 목사관 식구들의 아이방으로 쓰이고 있었다. 변화의 느낌, 개인의 하찮음, 당혹과 실망이 마거릿을 짓눌렀다. 어느 것도 예전과 같지 않았다. 그리고 이 사소하고 온 데 스며든 불안정함은 모든 것이 아주 달라져 알아볼 수 없게 되었을 경우보다 그녀에게 더 큰 아픔을 주었다.\n\n“이제야 천국이 어떤 것인지 알 것 같아. 아, 그 말씀의 장엄함과 안식이라니. ‘어제나 오늘이나 영원토록 동일하시니라.’ 영원하다! ‘영원부터 영원까지 주는 하나님이시니이다.’ 내 머리 위의 저 아름다운 하늘은 결코 변하지 않을 듯이 보이지만 그래도 변하겠지. 나는 너무 지쳤어. 아무것도 내 곁에 머물지 않는 이 온갖 삶의 국면을 지나 휘둘려 가는 것이 너무 지쳐. 어떤 사람도, 어떤 장소도. 그것은 지상의 정욕에 사로잡힌 자들이 끝없이 맴도는 그 굴레와 같아. 나는 다른 종교의 여자들이 수도복을 입는 그런 심정이야. 지상의 단조로움 속에서 천상의 굳건함을 찾는 거지. 내가 로마 가톨릭 신자여서 큰 충격 하나로 마음을 마비시키고 감각을 잃게 할 수 있다면 수녀가 될 수도 있을 텐데. 하지만 나는 같은 사람들이 그리워 애가 탈 거야. 아니, 같은 사람들이라기보다는, 인류에 대한 사랑이 개개인에 대한 사랑을 아주 몰아낼 만큼 내 마음을 가득 채우지는 결코 못할 테니까. 어쩌면 그래야 하는지도, 어쩌면 그러지 않아야 하는지도 모르지. 오늘 밤에는 결정할 수가 없어.”\n\n지친 몸으로 그녀는 잠자리에 들었고, 지친 몸으로 네다섯 시간 뒤에 일어났다. 그러나 아침과 더불어 희망이 찾아왔고 사물이 한결 밝게 보였다.\n\n“그래도 이것이 옳은 거야.” 옷을 입으면서 노는 아이들의 목소리를 들으며 그녀가 말했다. “세상이 멈춰 선다면 뒷걸음질 쳐서 썩어 버리겠지. 그것이 앞뒤가 안 맞는 말이 아니라면 말이야. 나 자신과 변화에 대한 나 자신의 아픈 느낌 밖으로 나가서 보면, 내 둘레의 모든 것이 나아가는 것은 옳고 필요한 일이야. 바른 판단을, 또는 희망차고 믿음 있는 마음을 갖고 싶다면, 상황이 나 자신에게 어떤 영향을 주는가보다 다른 사람들에게 어떤 영향을 주는가를 더 생각해야 해.” 그러고는 곧 입가로 번져 내릴 듯한 웃음을 눈가에 머금은 채 그녀는 응접실로 들어가 벨 씨에게 인사했다.\n\n“아, 아가씨! 어젯밤에 늦게까지 안 잤으니 아침에도 늦었구먼. 자, 자네에게 전할 소식이 하나 있네. 저녁 초대를 받았다면 어떻겠나? 아침 방문을 받았지, 그야말로 이슬 맺힌 아침에 말이야. 아, 내가 아까 목사님을 여기서 만났다네. 학교로 가는 길이었지. 건초 일꾼들을 위해 우리 안주인한테 금주 강연을 하고 싶은 마음이 그가 그토록 일찍 온 것과 얼마나 관계가 있는지는 모르겠네만, 아무튼 아홉 시가 채 안 되어 내가 내려왔을 때 여기 와 있었네. 그래서 우리가 오늘 거기서 저녁을 먹자고 청함을 받았지.”\n\n“하지만 이디스가 저를 기다리고 있어서 갈 수 없어요.” 마거릿이 그렇게 좋은 핑계가 있는 것을 고맙게 여기며 말했다.\n\n“그래! 나도 아네. 그래서 그렇게 말했지. 자네가 가고 싶어 하지 않으리라 생각했네. 그래도 자네가 원한다면 아직 열려 있는 이야기야.”\n\n“아뇨, 괜찮아요!” 마거릿이 말했다. “우리 계획대로 해요. 열두 시에 떠나요. 참 좋고 고마운 분들이지만, 정말이지 저는 갈 수 없어요.”\n\n“좋아. 안달하지 말게, 내가 다 알아서 하지.”\n\n떠나기 전에 마거릿은 목사관 뜰 뒤쪽으로 몰래 돌아가서 제멋대로 뻗은 인동덩굴 한 가닥을 꺾었다. 전날에는 남의 눈에 띄어 제 마음과 심정이 이러쿵저러쿵 이야기되는 것이 두려워 꽃 한 송이 꺾지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 공터를 가로질러 돌아오는 길에 그곳은 다시금 옛날의 그 홀리는 듯한 정취를 되찾고 있었다. 삶의 흔한 소리들이 세상 어느 곳에서보다 그곳에서 더 음악 같았고, 빛은 더 금빛이었으며, 삶은 더 고요하고 꿈결 같은 기쁨으로 가득했다. 마거릿은 어제의 자기 심정을 떠올리며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n“나도 끊임없이 변하는구나. 이랬다 저랬다. 모든 것이 내가 그려 본 그대로가 아니라고 실망하고 짜증을 내다가, 이제는 문득 현실이 내가 상상했던 것보다 훨씬 아름답다는 것을 깨닫고. 아, 헬스톤! 나는 결코 어떤 곳도 너처럼 사랑하지 못할 거야.”\n\n며칠 뒤 그녀는 마음의 균형을 되찾고 이렇게 결론지었다. 그곳에 다녀온 것이, 그곳을 다시 본 것이 무척 기쁘다고, 자기에게 그곳은 언제까지나 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 곳이리라고, 그러나 그곳은 지난날의, 특히 아버지 어머니와 얽힌 추억으로 어찌나 가득한지, 만일 모든 것을 다시 겪어야 한다면 벨 씨와 함께 다녀온 것과 같은 그런 방문은 뒷걸음질 치며 피하리라고.\n\n# 제47장\n\n“경험은 창백한 악사처럼 제 손에 인내의 덜시머를 들고 있으니, 거기에서 우리가 알아들을 수 없는 화음이, 그분의 세계 속에 담긴 하느님 뜻의 가락이 슬프고 어지러운 단조로 풀려 나온다.” — 브라우닝 부인.\n\n이 무렵 딕슨이 밀턴에서 돌아와 마거릿의 몸종 자리를 다시 맡았다. 그녀는 밀턴의 소문을 끝도 없이 가져왔다. 마사가 손턴 양이 혼인하면서 그 댁으로 들어가 살게 되었다는 이야기며, 그 흥미로운 예식에서 신부 들러리와 드레스와 피로연이 어떠했는지 하는 이야기며, 파업으로 큰 손해를 보고 계약이 어그러진 값까지 그토록 많이 물어야 했던 처지에 손턴 씨가 혼례를 너무 성대하게 치렀다고들 하더라는 이야기며, 딕슨이 오래도록 아껴 온 세간붙이들이 경매에서 얼마나 헐값에 넘어갔는지, 밀턴 사람들이 그토록 부유한 것을 생각하면 부끄러운 일이라는 이야기며, 어느 날은 손턴 부인이 와서 두세 가지를 아주 싸게 사 갔는데 이튿날은 손턴 씨가 와서 한두 가지를 꼭 손에 넣고 싶은 마음에 제가 부른 값에 제가 또 값을 얹어 불러 구경꾼들을 크게 즐겁게 했다는 이야기까지. 그러니 딕슨이 보기에는 그것으로 셈이 맞은 셈이었다. 손턴 부인이 너무 적게 냈다면 손턴 씨가 너무 많이 냈으니까. 벨 씨는 책에 관해 온갖 지시를 보내왔는데, 어찌나 까다로운지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다. 직접 왔더라면 다 잘되었을 것을, 편지란 언제나 그 값어치보다 사람을 더 헷갈리게 만들었고 앞으로도 그럴 것이었다. 히긴스네에 대해서는 딕슨이 들려줄 이야기가 별로 없었다. 그녀의 기억력은 귀족 취향으로 기울어 있어서, 제 아래 신분인 사람들에 얽힌 일을 떠올리려 들 때면 여간 미덥지 않았다. 니컬러스는 아주 잘 지내는 것 같다고 했다. 그는 마거릿 아가씨의 소식을 물으러 몇 번이나 집에 들렀다. 손턴 씨가 한 번 물은 것 말고는 그런 것을 물어 온 사람이라고는 그뿐이었다. 그러면 메리는? 아, 물론 아주 잘 지내지요, 그 덩치 크고 뚱뚱하고 칠칠맞은 것이! 그녀는 들은 것 같기도 했다. 아니면 그저 제가 꾼 꿈이었는지도 모르지만 — 하기야 히긴스네 같은 사람들 꿈을 꾸었다면 그것도 이상한 일이었다 — 아버지가 요리를 배우게 하고 싶어 해서 메리가 손턴 씨 공장에 일하러 갔다던가. 그렇지만 그게 무슨 말도 안 되는 소린지는 자기도 모르겠다고 했다. 마거릿도 그 이야기가 꿈이라 해도 좋을 만큼 앞뒤가 안 맞는다는 데 동의하는 편이었다. 그래도 이제 밀턴과 밀턴 사람들 이야기를 나눌 상대가 생겼다는 것은 즐거운 일이었다. 딕슨은 그 화제를 그리 좋아하지 않았다. 제 삶의 그 대목은 그늘 속에 두고 싶어 하는 쪽이었다. 그녀는 벨 씨가 한 말들을 되뇌는 쪽을 훨씬 좋아했으니, 그 말들이 그의 진짜 속뜻이 무엇인지에 대한 생각 하나를 그녀에게 심어 주었기 때문이다. 마거릿을 제 상속인으로 삼으려 한다는 것. 그러나 그녀의 아가씨는 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았고, 의심이나 단정의 형태로 아무리 그럴듯하게 감춘들 그 넌지시 캐묻는 말에 조금도 응해 주지 않았다.\n\n이 내내 마거릿은 벨 씨가 사업차 밀턴을 찾아갔다는 소식을 듣고 싶다는, 이상하게 뭐라 이름 붙일 수 없는 갈망을 품고 있었다. 헬스톤에서 이야기를 나눌 때 두 사람 사이에는, 그녀가 바라는 그 해명은 오직 말로만 손턴 씨에게 전해져야 하고 그렇게 하더라도 결코 억지로 떠맡겨서는 안 된다는 것이 잘 이해되어 있었기 때문이다. 벨 씨는 부지런한 편지 상대가 못 되었으나 기분 내키는 대로 이따금 길거나 짧은 편지를 보내왔고, 마거릿은 그것을 받으며 어떤 뚜렷한 기대를 품고 있다고 스스로 의식하지는 않으면서도 늘 조금은 실망스러운 마음으로 그 쪽지를 치워 두곤 했다. 그는 밀턴에 갈 생각이 없었다. 적어도 그에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그래! 참아야지. 이르든 늦든 안개는 걷힐 것이다. 벨 씨의 편지는 여느 때의 그답지 않았다. 짧고 투정이 섞였으며, 이따금 그답지 않은 씁쓸함이 살짝 배어 있었다. 그는 앞날을 내다보지 않았다. 오히려 지난날을 아쉬워하고 지금을 지겨워하는 듯했다. 마거릿은 그가 편치 않은 것이 아닐까 싶었다. 그러나 건강이 어떠냐는 그녀의 물음에 그는 짤막한 쪽지를 보내, 비장증이라 부르는 옛날식 병이 있는데 자기가 그것을 앓고 있으며 그것이 마음의 것인지 몸의 것인지는 그녀가 정할 일이라고, 다만 자기는 투덜거릴 때마다 병세 보고서를 보내야 하는 의무 없이 마음껏 투덜거리고 싶다고 했다.\n\n이 쪽지 때문에 마거릿은 그의 건강에 대해 더는 묻지 않았다. 어느 날 이디스가 벨 씨와 나눈 대화의 한 토막을 무심코 흘렸다. 그가 마지막으로 런던에 왔을 때의 일이었는데, 그 이야기가 마거릿의 마음을 사로잡아, 그가 가을에 그녀를 카디스로 데려가 오빠와 새 올케를 찾아보게 할 생각을 품고 있다는 생각에 빠지게 했다. 그녀는 이디스가 지쳐서 더 기억날 것이 없다고 잘라 말할 때까지 묻고 또 캐물었다. 그가 한 말이라고는 자기가 갈까 반쯤 생각 중이고 프레더릭이 반란에 대해 무어라 하는지 직접 들어 보고 싶다는 것, 마거릿이 새 올케와 알고 지내기에도 좋은 기회가 되리라는 것, 자기는 긴 방학이면 늘 어디든 가는데 다른 어디나 마찬가지로 스페인이라고 못 갈 까닭이 없지 않으냐는 것뿐이었다. 그것이 전부였다. 이디스는 마거릿이 이 일에 그토록 애를 태우는 것이 자기들을 떠나고 싶어서는 아니기를 바랐다. 그러고는 달리 딱히 할 일도 없던 참이라 울음을 터뜨리며, 마거릿이 자기를 생각하는 것보다 자기가 마거릿을 훨씬 더 아낀다는 것을 안다고 말했다. 마거릿은 힘닿는 대로 그녀를 달랬으나, 이 스페인 이야기가 그저 스페인의 성일 뿐일지라도 얼마나 제 마음을 사로잡고 들뜨게 하는지는 도무지 설명할 수가 없었다. 이디스는 제 곁을 떠나 누리는 즐거움이라면 무엇이든 말 없는 모욕이거나 잘 봐줘야 무관심의 증거라고 여길 기분이었다. 그래서 마거릿은 그 기쁨을 혼자 간직할 수밖에 없었고, 저녁 식사 채비로 옷을 갈아입을 때 딕슨에게 프레더릭 도련님과 그 새색시를 정말이지 몹시 보고 싶지 않으냐고 묻는 안전밸브로나 겨우 그것을 흘려보낼 수 있었다.\n\n“그 아씨는 교황파 아닙니까요, 아가씨?”\n\n“그럴 거예요. 아, 그래요, 틀림없이요!” 마거릿이 그 생각이 떠올라 잠깐 김이 빠진 채 말했다.\n\n“그리고 두 분은 교황파 나라에서 사시고요?”\n\n“그래요.”\n\n“그렇다면 죄송하지만 이렇게 말씀드려야겠습니다요. 제 영혼은 제게 프레더릭 도련님보다도, 그 귀하신 몸소보다도 더 소중하니까요. 저는 노상 벌벌 떨며 지낼 겁니다요, 아가씨. 혹시라도 제가 개종당할까 봐서요.”\n\n“어머,” 마거릿이 말했다. “내가 간다고 정해진 것도 아니에요. 그리고 간다 해도 나는 딕슨 없이는 여행도 못 할 만큼 대단한 귀부인은 아니고요. 아니에요! 정다운 딕슨 할멈, 우리가 가게 되면 딕슨은 긴 휴가를 얻는 거예요. 하지만 아무래도 그건 아주 긴 ‘만약’인 것 같네요.”\n\n그런데 딕슨은 이 말이 마음에 들지 않았다. 우선 그녀는 마거릿이 유난히 다정해질 때면 자기를 ‘정다운 딕슨 할멈’이라고 부르는 버릇이 싫었다. 헤일 양이 좋아하는 사람이면 누구에게나 애정의 표시 삼아 ‘할멈’이니 ‘영감’이니 하는 말을 붙이곤 한다는 것은 그녀도 알고 있었다. 그러나 딕슨은 그 말이 제게 붙는 데는 늘 움찔하며 물러났으니, 쉰을 얼마 넘기지 않은 자기야말로 인생의 한창때에 있다고 여겼기 때문이다. 둘째로 그녀는 제 말이 그렇게 선뜻 곧이곧대로 받아들여지는 것도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그 온갖 겁에도 불구하고 그녀에게는 스페인과 종교재판과 교황파의 신비에 대한 은근한 호기심이 도사리고 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 어려움을 없애 볼 뜻이 있음을 내보이기라도 하듯 헛기침을 하고 나서, 사제를 절대로 보지 않고 그들의 교회에도 들어가지 않도록 조심만 한다면 그래도 제가 개종당할 위험이 그렇게까지 크겠느냐고 헤일 양에게 물었다. 하기야 프레더릭 도련님이야 알 수 없게도 넘어가 버리셨지만.\n\n“오빠를 먼저 개종 쪽으로 기울게 한 건 사랑이었다고 생각해요.” 마거릿이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니까요, 아가씨!” 딕슨이 말했다. “그렇다면! 저야 사제한테서도 교회한테서도 저를 지킬 수 있습니다만, 사랑이란 건 저도 모르는 새에 스며드는 것이잖습니까요! 아무래도 저는 가지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다요.”\n\n마거릿은 이 스페인 계획에 마음을 너무 많이 내달리게 두는 것이 겁났다. 그러나 그 덕분에 손턴 씨에게 모든 것을 밝히고 싶다는 소망을 지나치게 조바심 내며 곱씹지 않을 수 있었다. 벨 씨는 당분간 옥스퍼드에 붙박여 있는 듯했고 밀턴에 갈 당장의 뜻은 없어 보였는데, 어떤 은밀한 억제 같은 것이 마거릿을 짓눌러 그가 그런 걸음을 할 가망이 있는지 묻는 것조차, 다시 그 이야기를 넌지시 꺼내는 것조차 하지 못하게 막았다. 이디스가 전해 준 이야기, 그가 스페인에 갈 생각을 — 어쩌면 5분 동안만 품었을 그 생각을 — 했다는 것을 입에 올릴 자유도 느끼지 못했다. 그는 헬스톤에서, 그 볕 좋고 한가롭던 하루 내내 그런 말을 한 번도 하지 않았다. 아마도 한순간의 공상에 지나지 않았으리라. 그러나 만약 그것이 참이라면, 이제 그녀를 짓누르기 시작한 지금 삶의 단조로움에서 얼마나 환한 출구가 되어 줄 것인가.\n\n이 무렵 마거릿의 삶에서 큰 즐거움 가운데 하나는 이디스의 아들이었다. 아이는 얌전할 때는 아버지와 어머니 양쪽의 자랑이자 노리개였다. 그러나 아이에게는 제 나름의 억센 고집이 있었고, 그 사나운 성깔을 한바탕 터뜨릴 때면 이디스는 절망과 피로에 젖어 몸을 뒤로 던지고는 한숨을 쉬며 말하곤 했다. “어머나, 얘를 어떡하면 좋아! 마거릿, 제발 종을 울려서 핸리 좀 불러 줘.”\n\n그러나 마거릿은 파란 띠를 두른 얌전한 기분일 때보다 이렇게 성깔을 드러낼 때의 아이를 오히려 더 좋아하는 편이었다. 그녀는 아이를 안고 어느 방으로 들어가 단둘이서 그것을 겨루어 냈다. 그녀는 아이를 평온으로 다스려 눌러 앉히는 흔들림 없는 힘을 지녔고, 제가 지닌 온갖 불시의 매력과 재주를 옳은 편에 실어 다 쏟아부었다. 그러면 아이는 마침내 뜨겁고 눈물로 얼룩진 작은 얼굴을 그녀의 얼굴에 온통 비벼 대며 입 맞추고 어루만지다가, 그녀의 품이나 어깨에서 잠들어 버리기 일쑤였다. 그것이 마거릿에게는 가장 달콤한 순간이었다. 그 순간은 그녀가 자기에게는 영영 허락되지 않으리라 믿고 있던 감정을 조금이나마 맛보게 해 주었다.\n\n헨리 레녹스 씨는 자주 드나들며 이 집안의 살아가는 흐름에 새롭고 싫지 않은 요소를 더해 주었다. 마거릿이 보기에 그는 예전보다 한결 빛나기는 해도 더 차가워진 듯했다. 그러나 그에게는 강한 지적 취향과 넓고 다양한 지식이 있어서, 그것이 없었더라면 다소 밍밍했을 대화에 맛을 내 주었다. 마거릿은 그가 제 형과 형수를, 그리고 그들의 살아가는 방식을 살짝 경멸하는 기색을 언뜻언뜻 엿보았다. 그는 그 삶을 경박하고 목적 없는 것으로 여기는 눈치였다. 그는 마거릿이 있는 자리에서 한두 번 제 형에게 자못 날카로운 물음의 어조로, 정말로 직업을 아주 놓아 버릴 작정이냐고 물은 적이 있었다. 그리고 먹고살 만큼은 넉넉하다는 레녹스 대위의 대답에 레녹스 씨가 입술을 비틀며 이렇게 말하는 것을 그녀는 보았다. “그러면 형님이 사는 목적이 그게 전부입니까?”\n\n그러나 두 형제는 서로에게 무척 정이 깊었다. 한쪽이 더 영리해 늘 앞장서 이끌고 다른 한쪽은 이끌리는 데 참을성 있게 만족하는 두 사람 사이에 흔히 그러하듯이. 레녹스 씨는 제 직업에서 앞으로 밀고 나가는 중이었다. 언젠가 자기에게 도움이 될 만한 연줄이라면 무엇이든 깊은 계산 아래 가꾸었고, 눈이 밝고 멀리 내다보며 총명하고 빈정거리기 좋아하고 자존심이 강했다. 벨 씨가 있는 자리에서 첫날 저녁에 그와 나누었던, 프레더릭의 일에 관한 그 한 차례 긴 대화 이후로 그녀는 같은 집안과 가까이 얽힌 데서 생기는 왕래 말고는 그와 별다른 교류가 없었다. 그러나 그것만으로도 그녀 쪽의 수줍음과 그의 쪽에 남아 있던 상한 자존심과 허영의 기색은 다 씻겨 나갔다. 물론 두 사람은 끊임없이 마주쳤으나, 그녀는 그가 자기와 단둘이 있는 것을 오히려 피한다고 여겼다. 그도 자기와 마찬가지로, 두 사람이 나란히 매여 있던 예전의 정박지에서 이상하리만치 멀리 떠내려왔음을, 많은 의견에서 그리고 온갖 취향에서 그러함을 알아차리고 있는 것이라고 그녀는 짐작했다.\n\n그러면서도 그가 유난히 말을 잘했을 때나 놀랄 만큼 날 선 경구를 던졌을 때면, 그의 눈이 무엇보다 먼저 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 찾는다는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다. 한순간에 지나지 않더라도 그러했다. 그리고 두 사람을 끊임없이 한자리에 몰아넣는 그 집안의 왕래 속에서, 그가 공손함을 담아 귀 기울이는 의견은 바로 그녀의 것이었다. 그 공손함은 마지못해 바쳐지고 될 수 있는 대로 감추어졌기에 더욱 온전한 것이었다.\n\n# 제48장\n\n「다시는 찾을 수 없는 것」\n\n“내 벗이여, 내 아버지의 벗이여! 나는 그대와 헤어질 수 없어라! 나는 한 번도 내보인 적 없고, 그대는 한 번도 알지 못했으니, 그대가 내게 얼마나 소중한지를.” — 작자 미상.\n\n레녹스 부인이 여는 만찬을 이루는 요소는 이러했다. 그녀의 친구들은 아름다움을 보탰고, 레녹스 대위는 그날그날의 화제에 대한 여유로운 식견을 보탰다. 그리고 헨리 레녹스 씨와, 그의 벗으로 받아들여진 몇몇 떠오르는 인사들은 재치와 영민함과 예리하고 폭넓은 지식을 가져왔는데, 그들은 현학적으로 보이지도 않고 빠르게 흘러가는 대화에 부담을 주지도 않으면서 그것을 활용하는 법을 아주 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n이 만찬들은 즐거웠다. 그러나 여기서도 마거릿의 불만은 기어이 그녀를 찾아냈다. 온갖 재능, 온갖 감정, 온갖 소양, 아니 미덕을 향한 온갖 성향까지도 불꽃놀이의 재료로 소모되었다. 감추어져 있어야 할 신성한 불이 반짝임과 탁탁거림 속에 저를 다 태워 없앴다. 그들은 예술을 두고 오로지 감각적인 방식으로만 이야기했고, 예술이 가르쳐 주는 바를 배우려 하는 대신 겉으로 드러난 효과에만 머물렀다. 그들은 사람들 앞에서는 고상한 주제를 두고 스스로를 채찍질하듯 열광에 몰아넣었으나, 혼자 있을 때에는 그런 것을 한 번도 생각하지 않았다. 그들은 감상할 줄 아는 능력을 그저 알맞은 말이 흘러나오게 하는 데 탕진해 버렸다. 어느 날 신사들이 응접실로 올라온 뒤, 레녹스 씨가 마거릿에게 다가와 말을 건넸다. 그녀가 할리 가로 돌아와 살게 된 뒤로 그가 자진해서 그녀에게 건넨 거의 첫마디였다.\n\n“저녁 식사 때 셜리가 하는 말이 마음에 들지 않으시는 눈치더군요.”\n\n“그랬나요? 제 얼굴이 어지간히 속을 다 드러내나 봐요.” 마거릿이 대답했다.\n\n“늘 그랬지요. 아직도 웅변하는 재주를 잃지 않았군요.”\n\n“저는 마음에 들지 않았어요.” 마거릿이 서둘러 말했다. “틀린 줄 뻔히 알면서도, 그토록 빤히 틀린 것을 농담으로라도 두둔하는 그 방식이요.”\n\n“하지만 아주 영리했지요. 한마디 한마디가 얼마나 잘 들어맞던지! 그 절묘한 표현들을 기억하시나요?”\n\n“네.”\n\n“그리고 그것들을 경멸한다고 덧붙이고 싶으시겠지요. 그가 제 벗이라 해도 개의치 마십시오.”\n\n“바로 그거예요! 당신의 그 말투가 바로—” 그녀는 말을 뚝 끊었다.\n\n그는 그녀가 문장을 끝맺을지 잠시 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그녀는 얼굴만 붉힌 채 돌아서 버렸다. 다만 돌아서기 전에 그가 아주 낮고 또렷한 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n“제 말투나 사고방식이 당신께서 싫어하시는 것이라면, 저에게 그렇다고 말해 주는 공정함을 베풀어 주시겠습니까? 그래서 당신 마음에 들도록 배울 기회를 저에게 주시겠습니까.”\n\n그 몇 주 내내 벨 씨가 밀턴에 간다는 소식은 없었다. 그는 헬스톤에서 그것을 머지않아 떠나야 할지도 모를 여행처럼 말했었다. 그러나 이제 와 생각해 보면 그는 편지로 그 일을 처리해 버렸을 것이라고 마거릿은 여겼다. 그리고 그가 할 수만 있다면 싫어하는 곳에 가는 일을 피하리라는 것을, 게다가 오직 직접 말로만 해 줄 수 있는 그 해명에 자신이 남몰래 부여한 중대함을 그가 거의 이해하지 못하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그가 그 일을 꼭 해야 한다고 느끼기는 하겠지만, 그것이 여름이든 가을이든 겨울이든 그에게는 별로 대수롭지 않으리라는 것도 그녀는 알았다. 이제 8월이었고, 그가 이디스에게 넌지시 비쳤던 스페인 여행 이야기도 전혀 없었다. 마거릿은 이 헛된 꿈이 스러져 가는 것에 스스로를 체념시키려 애썼다.\n\n그러나 어느 날 아침 그녀는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 다음 주에 런던으로 올라올 작정이라는 것이었다. 머릿속에 품고 있는 계획 하나를 두고 그녀를 만나고 싶다고 했다. 게다가 자신이 짜증스럽고 심통이 날 때면 자기 탓이라기보다 건강 탓이라고 여기는 편이 마음 편하리라는 그녀의 의견에 이제 동의하게 되었으므로, 진료를 좀 받아 보는 호사를 누릴 참이라고도 했다. 나중에 마거릿이 알아차린 대로 그 편지 전체에는 억지로 꾸며 낸 쾌활함의 어조가 배어 있었다. 그러나 그때는 이디스의 탄성에 정신이 팔려 있었다.\n\n“런던으로 올라오신다고! 어머나! 난 더위에 어찌나 지쳤는지 만찬을 한 번 더 치를 기운이 남아 있을 것 같지도 않은데. 게다가 어디로 갈지 정하지도 못한 우리 어리석은 식구들만 빼고는 다들 떠나 버렸잖아. 그분을 맞아 드릴 사람이 아무도 없겠는걸.”\n\n“네가 그러모을 수 있는 가장 유쾌한 낯선 분들과 함께하시느니 우리끼리 조용히 저녁을 드시는 편을 훨씬 더 좋아하실 게 틀림없어. 게다가 편찮으시다면 초대받고 싶어 하지도 않으시겠지. 마침내 털어놓으셔서 다행이야. 편지의 온 어조로 보아 편찮으신 게 틀림없다고 여겼는데, 내가 여쭈어도 대답을 안 하셨고, 소식을 물어볼 만한 제삼자도 없었으니까.”\n\n“어머! 아주 편찮으신 건 아닐 거야. 그렇다면 스페인 생각을 하실 리가 없지.”\n\n“스페인 말씀은 한 번도 없으셨어.”\n\n“아니야! 하지만 제안하시겠다는 그 계획이란 게 분명 그 일과 관계있는 거지. 그런데 이런 날씨에 정말로 갈 거야?”\n\n“아, 날마다 시원해질 거야. 그럼! 생각해 봐. 다만 내가 너무 많이 생각하고 너무 많이 바란 게 아닌가 두려울 뿐이야. 꼭 실망하고 마는 그 사로잡힌 듯한 고집스러운 방식으로 말이야. 아니면 글자 그대로는 이루어지되 정작 마음에는 아무 기쁨도 주지 않는 식이 되든지.”\n\n“하지만 그건 미신이야, 마거릿, 틀림없이.”\n\n“아니야, 그렇게 생각하지 않아. 다만 그것이 나를 일깨워서, 그런 격렬한 소망에 나를 내맡기지 않도록 막아 주어야 해. ‘내게 자식을 주소서, 그렇지 않으면 내가 죽겠나이다’ 하는 것과 같은 거지. 내 외침은 ‘카디스로 가게 해 주소서, 그렇지 않으면 내가 죽겠나이다’인 것 같아 두려워.”\n\n“마거릿, 얘! 넌 거기 눌러앉으라고 설득당하고 말 거야. 그럼 난 어쩌라고? 아, 여기서 네가 결혼할 사람을 하나 찾아낼 수만 있다면, 그래서 너를 붙잡아 둘 수만 있다면 좋겠어!”\n\n“나는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거야.”\n\n“말도 안 돼, 두 번 말도 안 돼! 아니, 숄토 말로는 네가 이 집에 어찌나 큰 매력이 되는지, 내년에 너 때문에 기꺼이 찾아올 남자들을 여럿 알고 있다던걸.”\n\n마거릿이 도도하게 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다. “있잖아, 이디스, 나는 가끔 네 코르푸 생활이 너에게 가르쳐 준 게——”\n\n“뭔데!”\n\n“한두 겹쯤의 상스러움이라는 생각이 들어.”\n\n이디스가 어찌나 서럽게 흐느끼기 시작하고, 마거릿이 자기에 대한 사랑을 다 잃었으며 더는 자기를 벗으로 여기지 않는다고 어찌나 격하게 우겨 대던지, 마거릿은 상처받은 제 자존심을 달래려고 너무 모진 말을 했다고 여기게 되었고, 결국 그날 남은 내내 이디스의 종노릇을 하고 말았다. 그러는 동안 그 자그마한 부인은 상한 감정에 짓눌려 제물처럼 소파에 드러누운 채 이따금 깊은 한숨을 내쉬다가 마침내 잠이 들었다.\n\n벨 씨는 두 번째로 방문을 미룬 그 날짜에도 모습을 나타내지 않았다. 이튿날 아침 그의 하인 월리스에게서 편지가 왔다. 주인어른이 얼마 전부터 몸이 편치 않으셨고 그것이 여행을 미룬 진짜 까닭이었다는 것, 그리고 런던으로 떠나야 할 바로 그때에 졸중 발작을 일으키셨다는 것이었다. 월리스는 덧붙이기를, 의사들의 소견으로는 그분이 이 밤을 넘기지 못하시리라 하며, 헤일 양이 이 편지를 받을 무렵이면 가엾은 주인어른은 이미 이 세상 사람이 아닐 공산이 크다고 했다.\n\n마거릿은 아침 식사 시간에 이 편지를 받았고, 읽으면서 얼굴이 몹시 창백해졌다. 그러고는 말없이 이디스의 손에 편지를 쥐여 주고 방을 나갔다.\n\n이디스는 편지를 읽고 몹시 충격을 받아 흐느끼며 겁먹은 아이처럼 울었고, 남편은 크게 난처해졌다. 쇼 부인은 자기 방에서 아침을 들고 있었으므로, 아내가 평생 기억하기로는 처음으로 죽음과 이토록 가까이 맞닥뜨리게 된 것을 받아들이도록 다독이는 일이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌다. 오늘 자기들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 했던 사람이 그 대신 죽어 가거나 이미 죽어 누워 있다니! 그녀가 마거릿을 떠올리기까지는 얼마쯤 시간이 걸렸다. 이윽고 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 위층 마거릿의 방으로 뒤따라 올라갔다. 딕슨은 세면도구 몇 가지를 챙기고 있었고, 마거릿은 줄곧 눈물을 흘리며 서둘러 보닛을 쓰고 있었는데, 손이 어찌나 떨리는지 끈을 제대로 매지도 못했다.\n\n“아, 마거릿! 이 무슨 끔찍한 일이니! 뭘 하는 거야? 나가려고? 숄토가 전보를 치든 뭐든 네가 원하는 대로 해 줄 텐데.”\n\n“옥스퍼드에 갈 거야. 30분 뒤에 기차가 있어. 딕슨이 같이 가 주겠다고 했지만 나 혼자서도 갈 수 있었어. 그분을 다시 뵈어야 해. 게다가 차도가 있으셔서 누가 돌봐 드려야 할지도 모르잖아. 그분은 내게 아버지 같은 분이셨어. 붙잡지 마, 이디스.”\n\n“하지만 붙잡아야겠어. 어머니가 도무지 좋아하지 않으실 거야. 가서 여쭈어보자, 마거릿. 넌 네가 어디로 가는지도 모르잖아. 그분이 당신 집을 갖고 계시다면 나도 개의치 않겠지만, 펠로 숙소라니! 어머니께 가서 떠나기 전에 꼭 여쭈어봐. 잠깐이면 될 거야.”\n\n마거릿은 물러섰고, 기차를 놓쳤다. 갑작스러운 일에 쇼 부인은 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 신경질을 부렸고, 그렇게 귀한 시간이 흘러가 버렸다. 그러나 두 시간 뒤에 또 기차가 있었다. 그리고 온당하다느니 온당하지 않다느니 하는 갖가지 논의 끝에, 레녹스 대위가 마거릿과 동행하기로 결정되었다. 그녀가 한결같이 붙들고 있는 단 하나는 그 처신이 온당하다는 말을 듣든 온당하지 않다는 말을 듣든 간에 혼자서든 누구와 함께든 다음 기차로 가겠다는 결심이었기 때문이다. 아버지의 벗이자 그녀 자신의 벗이 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있었다. 그 생각이 어찌나 생생하게 밀려들었던지, 그녀는 행동의 독립에 대한 제 권리를 얼마간 주장하는 자신의 단호함에 스스로도 놀랐다. 그리고 출발 5분 전, 그녀는 기차간에서 레녹스 대위와 마주 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했다. 비록 그분이 간밤에 돌아가셨다는 말을 듣기 위해 간 것에 지나지 않았지만, 자기가 갔었다는 사실은 언제나 그녀에게 위안이 되었다. 그녀는 그가 지내던 방을 보았고, 그 뒤로 언제까지나 그 방을 아버지에 대한 생각과, 아버지의 하나뿐이던 소중하고 신실한 벗에 대한 생각과 더불어 더없이 애틋하게 기억 속에 간직했다.\n\n두 사람은 떠나기 전에 이디스에게 약속했었다. 만일 모든 일이 두려워하던 대로 끝나 있다면 저녁 식사 시간에 맞추어 돌아오겠다고. 그래서 아버지가 숨을 거둔 그 방을 길게 미련 두어 둘러보던 눈길도 중간에 거두어야 했고, 그토록 자주 다정한 말과 유쾌한 농지거리와 익살을 건네던 그 정겨운 노인의 얼굴에 조용히 작별을 고해야 했다.\n\n레녹스 대위는 돌아오는 길에 잠이 들었다. 그래서 마거릿은 마음껏 울 수 있었고, 이 모진 한 해와 그것이 자기에게 안겨 준 온갖 슬픔을 곱씹을 수 있었다. 한 가지 상실을 온전히 깨닫기가 무섭게 또 다른 상실이 찾아왔다. 앞선 슬픔을 밀어내기 위해서가 아니라, 아물지도 못한 상처와 감정을 다시 헤집기 위해서였다. 그러나 이모와 이디스의 다정한 목소리를 듣고, 그녀가 온 것을 반기는 어린 숄토의 환호를 듣고, 환하게 불 밝힌 방과 창백한 낯빛으로 오히려 어여쁜, 애틋하고 슬픈 관심을 담은 그 안주인을 보자, 마거릿은 미신에 가까운 절망의 무거운 혼미에서 몸을 일으켰고, 자기 주위에도 기쁨과 즐거움이 모여들 수 있으리라는 것을 느끼기 시작했다. 그녀는 소파의 이디스 자리를 차지했다. 숄토는 마거릿 이모의 찻잔을 아주 조심스럽게 날라다 드리도록 배웠다. 그리고 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갈 무렵에는, 소중한 옛 벗에게 길거나 고통스러운 병을 면하게 해 주신 데 대해 하느님께 감사드릴 수 있었다.\n\n# 제49장\n\n“그리고 볕 드는 바닷가를 그녀는 천천히 거닌다, 길 위에서 여러 번 머뭇거려 멈추면서. 슬픔에는 그토록 고요하고 거룩한 힘이 있으니.” — 후드.\n\n“마거릿이 상속녀 아니에요?” 옥스퍼드로 다녀온 서글픈 여행이 끝난 그날 밤, 방에 단둘이 남게 되자 이디스가 남편에게 속삭였다. 그녀는 그의 훤칠한 머리를 끌어내리고 발끝으로 선 채, 감히 이 물음을 꺼내기 전에 부디 놀라지 말아 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 레녹스 대위는 도무지 아는 바가 없었다. 들은 적이 있었다 해도 잊어버린 터였다. 작은 칼리지의 펠로가 남길 것이 그리 많을 리 없었다. 그러나 그는 마거릿에게 하숙비를 내라고 한 적이 한 번도 없었고, 그녀가 포도주도 마시지 않는 것을 생각하면 1년에 250파운드라는 것은 터무니없는 액수였다. 이디스는 낭만이 산산조각 난 채 조금 더 서글퍼져서 발뒤꿈치를 내렸다.\n\n일주일 뒤 그녀는 깡충거리며 남편에게 다가와 무릎을 깊숙이 굽혀 인사했다.\n\n“제 말이 맞고 당신이 틀렸어요, 고귀하신 대위님. 마거릿한테 변호사 편지가 왔는데, 그 애가 잔여재산 수유자래요. 따로 정해진 유증이 2,000파운드쯤 되고, 나머지가 밀턴의 지금 재산 시세로 40,000파운드쯤 된다지 뭐예요.”\n\n“그것참! 그래, 마거릿은 그 행운을 어떻게 받아들이던가?”\n\n“아, 진작부터 다 자기 몫이 되리라는 걸 알고 있었나 봐요. 다만 그렇게 많을 줄은 몰랐대요. 얼굴이 아주 하얗게 질려서는 그게 겁이 난다고 하지 뭐예요. 하지만 그건 말도 안 되는 소리고, 곧 사라질 거예요. 어머니가 그 애 목구멍에 축하를 퍼붓고 계시길래 저는 살짝 빠져나와 당신한테 알려 주러 왔어요.”\n\n이제부터 레녹스 씨를 마거릿의 법률 고문으로 여기는 것이 가장 자연스러운 일이라고 다들 암묵적으로 동의한 듯했다. 그녀는 사무라는 것의 온갖 절차에 워낙 어두워서 거의 모든 일을 그에게 물어보아야 했다. 그가 그녀의 변호사를 골라 주었고, 서명할 서류를 들고 그녀를 찾아왔다. 이 온갖 법률의 신비가 무엇을 가리키고 무엇을 뜻하는지 그녀에게 가르쳐 줄 때만큼 그가 행복한 때는 없었다.\n\n“헨리 도련님.” 어느 날 이디스가 짓궂게 말했다. “마거릿과 나누는 그 긴 대화들이 결국 어떻게 끝나기를 제가 바라고 또 기대하는지 아세요?”\n\n“아니요, 모르겠습니다.” 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. “그리고 말씀하지 말아 주시기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“아, 그러세요. 그럼 숄토한테 몬터규 씨를 그렇게 자주 집에 부르지 말라고 이를 필요도 없겠네요.”\n\n“좋으실 대로 하십시오.” 그가 억지로 냉정한 척하며 말했다. “형수님이 생각하시는 일은 일어날 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지요. 하지만 이번에는 몸을 내던지기 전에 발밑을 분명히 살펴볼 참입니다. 누구든 부르고 싶은 대로 부르십시오. 이런 말씀이 그리 예의 바르지는 않겠습니다만, 이디스, 끼어드시면 오히려 망칠 겁니다. 그분은 오랫동안 저를 몹시 사납게 대했고, 이제야 겨우 그 제노비아 같은 태도를 조금 누그러뜨리기 시작했으니까요. 조금만 더 이교도다웠다면 클레오파트라가 되고도 남을 자질이 그분 안에 있습니다.”\n\n“저로 말하자면,” 이디스가 조금 짓궂게 말했다. “그 애가 기독교도라서 정말 다행이에요. 제가 아는 기독교도가 워낙 적어서요!”\n\n그해 가을 마거릿에게 스페인은 없었다. 마지막 순간까지 그녀는 무슨 다행스러운 일이 생겨 프레더릭이 파리로 불려 가기를 바랐다. 파리라면 함께 갈 일행을 어렵지 않게 구해 그를 만나러 갈 수 있었을 것이다. 카디스 대신 그녀는 크로머로 만족해야 했다. 쇼 이모와 레녹스 부부가 그리로 갈 참이었다. 그들은 처음부터 그녀가 함께 가기를 바랐고, 그런 만큼 그들의 성정대로 그녀 자신의 소망을 이루어 주는 일에는 게으른 노력밖에 기울이지 않았다. 어쩌면 어떤 뜻에서는 크로머가 그녀에게 가장 좋았을지도 모른다. 그녀에게는 쉼만이 아니라 몸을 튼튼히 하고 다잡는 일도 필요했으니까.\n\n사라져 버린 여러 희망 가운데는 벨 씨가 손턴 씨에게 레너즈의 죽음으로 이어진 그 불행한 사고에 앞서 있었던 집안 사정을 있는 그대로 이야기해 주었으리라는 희망이, 그런 믿음이 있었다. 손턴 씨가 한때 품었던 것과 아무리 달라졌다 한들 그가 어떤 판단을 내리든, 그녀는 그 판단이 자기가 무엇을 했고 왜 그렇게 했는지에 대한 참된 이해 위에 서기를 바랐다. 그것은 그녀에게 기쁨이 되었을 것이다. 이제 평생 마음이 놓이지 않을 그 한 가지 점에서 그녀에게 쉼을 주었을 것이다. 아예 그 일을 생각하지 않기로 마음먹을 수 있다면 모르지만. 그 일들이 있은 지 워낙 오래되어, 이제는 벨 씨의 죽음으로 잃어버린 그 한 가지 길 말고는 그것을 설명할 방도가 없었다. 그녀는 다른 숱한 사람들처럼 그저 오해받는 것을 감수할 수도 있었다. 그러나 이런 것이 자기에게만 있는 드문 팔자가 아니라고 스스로를 타이르면서도, 언젠가—몇 해가, 아주 여러 해가 흐른 뒤라도—적어도 그가 죽기 전에는 자기가 얼마나 큰 유혹을 받았는지 그가 알아주었으면 하는 그리움에 그녀의 가슴은 조금도 덜 아프지 않았다. 모든 것이 그에게 설명되었다는 말을 듣고 싶은 것은 아니라고, 그가 알게 되기만 한다면 그것으로 되었다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 이 바람도 다른 수많은 바람들처럼 헛되었다. 그리하여 그런 확신을 스스로에게 가르쳐 넣고 나자, 그녀는 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 바로 눈앞에 놓인 삶으로 돌아서서 그 삶을 힘껏 살아 내기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그녀는 바닷가에 오래도록 앉아, 파도가 자갈 깔린 물가에 끊임없이 밀려와 부딪히는 모습을 골똘히 바라보곤 했다. 아니면 더 먼 곳의 너울과 하늘을 배경으로 반짝이는 빛을 내다보며, 쉼 없이 올라가는 그 영원한 시편 소리를 듣는 줄도 모른 채 들었다. 어떻게 왜 그런지도 모르는 채 그녀의 마음은 달래졌다. 그녀는 두 손으로 무릎을 감싸 안고 땅바닥에 넋 놓고 앉아 있었고, 그동안 쇼 이모는 자잘한 물건을 사러 다녔으며 이디스와 레녹스 대위는 바닷가로 내륙으로 멀리까지 말을 달렸다. 아이들을 데리고 어슬렁거리던 유모들이 그녀 앞을 오가면서, 날마다 저렇게 오래도록 대체 무엇을 보고 있는 것일까 하고 소곤소곤 궁금해했다. 그리고 저녁 식사 때 식구들이 모이면 마거릿이 어찌나 말이 없고 골똘한지, 이디스는 그녀가 우울증에 빠졌다고 단정해 버리고는 10월에 스코틀랜드에서 돌아오는 헨리 레녹스 씨에게 크로머에서 일주일을 지내자고 청하자는 남편의 제안을 크게 반겼다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 생각의 시간 덕분에 마거릿은 지난 삶에 대해서도 앞날에 대해서도 여러 일들을 그 근원과 뜻에 따라 제자리에 놓을 수 있었다. 바닷가에서의 그 시간들은 헛되지 않았다. 마거릿의 얼굴이 조금씩 얻어 가고 있던 그 표정을 읽어 낼 눈이 있거나 그것을 이해하려 마음 쓰는 사람이라면 누구나 알아보았을 것이다. 헨리 레녹스 씨는 그 변화에 몹시 놀랐다.\n\n“바다가 헤일 양에게 이루 말할 수 없이 좋았던 모양입니다.” 그가 이 집안 자리에 온 뒤 그녀가 처음으로 방을 나서자 말했다. “할리 가에 계실 때보다 10년은 젊어 보이시는군요.”\n\n“그건 제가 사 준 보닛이에요!” 이디스가 의기양양하게 말했다. “보는 순간 그 애한테 어울릴 줄 알았다니까요.”\n\n“죄송합니다만,” 레녹스 씨가 이디스에게 늘 쓰는 반은 깔보는 듯 반은 너그러운 어조로 말했다. “저도 옷의 매력과 여자의 매력의 차이쯤은 안다고 생각합니다. 한낱 보닛 따위가 헤일 양의 눈을 저토록 빛나면서도 부드럽게, 입술을 저토록 무르익어 붉게, 얼굴 전체를 저토록 평화와 빛으로 가득하게 만들 수는 없지요. 저분은 닮았습니다, 아니 그 이상입니다.” 그가 목소리를 낮추었다. “헬스톤의 마거릿 헤일을 닮았어요.”\n\n이때부터 그 영리하고 야심 있는 사내는 마거릿을 얻는 데 온 힘을 기울였다. 그는 그녀의 다정한 아름다움을 사랑했다. 그녀의 마음에 잠재된 넓은 폭을 보았고, 자기가 마음에 품은 온갖 목표를 끌어안도록 그것을 쉽게 이끌 수 있으리라고 (그는 그렇게 생각했다) 여겼다. 그는 그녀의 재산을 그녀 자신과 그 처지가 이루는 완전하고 훌륭한 됨됨이의 한 부분으로만 보았다. 그러면서도 가난한 법정 변호사인 자신을 그 재산이 당장 얼마나 끌어올려 줄지 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 언젠가는 그도 성공과 영예를 거두어, 그녀에게 빚지게 될 그 첫 재산의 도움을 이자까지 붙여 갚을 수 있으리라. 그는 스코틀랜드에서 돌아오는 길에 그녀의 재산에 관한 일로 밀턴에 다녀왔다. 그리고 늘 만일의 사정을 헤아리고 가늠할 채비가 되어 있는 숙련된 법률가의 빠른 눈으로, 번창하며 커 가는 그 도시에 그녀가 소유한 토지와 건물의 값이 해마다 크게 오르고 있음을 보았다. 그는 마거릿과 자신 사이의 지금 이 의뢰인과 법률 고문이라는 관계가 헬스톤에서의 그 운 없고 서투르게 그르친 하루의 기억을 조금씩 밀어내고 있음을 알고 기뻤다. 그리하여 그는 두 집안의 연고에서 생기는 것 말고도 그녀와 가깝게 오갈 흔치 않은 기회를 얻었다.\n\n마거릿은 그가 밀턴 이야기를 하는 한 얼마든지 기꺼이 귀를 기울였다. 그가 그녀와 유난히 가까웠던 사람들은 하나도 만나지 않았는데도 그랬다. 이모와 사촌은 밀턴을 싫어하고 업신여기며 말하는 것이 버릇이었다. 마거릿이 그곳에 처음 살러 갔을 때 스스로 드러내고 또 느꼈던, 이제는 떠올리기조차 부끄러운 바로 그런 감정이었다. 그러나 레녹스 씨는 밀턴과 그곳 사람들의 기질을 높이 사는 데서 마거릿을 거의 앞질렀다. 그들의 활력, 그들의 힘, 싸우고 겨루는 데서 보이는 굽힐 줄 모르는 용기, 그 이글거리듯 생생한 삶이 그의 관심을 사로잡고 붙들었다. 그는 그들 이야기를 하는 데 지칠 줄 몰랐다. 그리고 그 모든 굳세고 지칠 줄 모르는 애씀의 결과로 그들이 스스로 내건 목표 가운데 너무나 많은 것이 얼마나 이기적이고 물질적인지 미처 알아차리지 못했다. 마거릿이 그 이야기에 흐뭇해하는 와중에도 솔직하게, 그토록 고귀하고 우러를 만한 것들을 더럽히는 죄가 바로 그것이라고 짚어 주기 전까지는. 그래도 다른 이야깃거리가 시들해져 그녀가 여러 물음에 짧은 대답만 내놓을 때면, 다크셔 특유의 기질에 대해 물어보기만 하면 그녀의 눈에 다시 빛이, 뺨에 다시 홍조가 돌아온다는 것을 헨리 레녹스는 알아냈다.\n\n런던으로 돌아왔을 때 마거릿은 바닷가에서 한 결심 가운데 하나를 지켜 자기 삶을 자기 손에 쥐었다. 크로머에 가기 전까지 그녀는, 할리 가 아이방에서 첫날 밤 울다 잠들었던 그 겁먹은 어린 이방인이 아직도 그대로인 양 이모의 규율에 고분고분했다. 그러나 그 엄숙한 사색의 시간 동안 그녀는 언젠가 자기 자신의 삶에 대해, 그 삶으로 무엇을 했는지에 대해 스스로 답해야 한다는 것을 배웠다. 그리하여 여자에게 가장 어려운 저 문제를, 곧 얼마만큼을 권위에 대한 순종에 온전히 맡기고 얼마만큼을 일하는 자유로 떼어 둘 것인가를 정리해 보려 애썼다. 쇼 부인은 더할 나위 없이 성품이 좋았고, 이디스도 이 사랑스러운 집안의 미덕을 물려받았다. 마거릿 자신은 아마 셋 가운데 성미가 가장 나빴을 것이다. 재빠른 지각과 지나치게 활발한 상상력이 그녀를 성급하게 만들었고, 어릴 적부터 공감에서 떨어져 지낸 탓에 자존심이 세졌으니까. 그러나 그녀에게는 뭐라 말할 수 없는 어린아이 같은 마음의 다정함이 있어서, 드물게 고집을 부릴 때조차 예부터 그 태도에는 아무도 거스를 수가 없었다. 그리고 이제 세상이 행운이라 부르는 것으로 오히려 다듬어진 그녀는 내키지 않아 하는 이모를 사로잡아 자기 뜻을 따르게 만들었다. 그리하여 마거릿은 자기가 생각하는 의무를 따를 권리를 인정받았다.\n\n“그저 드센 여자만은 되지 마.” 이디스가 사정했다. “어머니는 네가 네 하인을 하나 두기를 바라셔. 나야 얼마든지 좋아. 그것들은 어찌나 골칫거리인지 몰라. 그저 나를 봐서라도, 얘, 드센 마음일랑 갖지 마. 내가 부탁하는 건 그것뿐이야. 하인이 있든 없든, 드센 여자는 되지 마.”\n\n“걱정 마, 이디스. 첫 기회가 오는 대로 하인들 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 네 팔에 안겨 쓰러져 줄게. 그러면 숄토는 불장난을 하고 아기는 울어 대는 통에, 너도 어떤 급한 일에도 끄떡없는 드센 여자가 하나 있었으면 하고 바라기 시작할걸.”\n\n“그리고 너무 착해져서 농담도 안 하고 즐거워하지도 않게 되진 않을 거지?”\n\n“천만에. 이제 내 뜻대로 하게 되었으니 그 어느 때보다 더 즐거워질 거야.”\n\n“그리고 꼴사납게 하고 다니지 말고, 내가 네 옷을 사 주게 해 줄 거지?”\n\n“정말이지 내 옷은 내가 사려고 해. 원하면 같이 가도 좋아. 하지만 나를 만족시킬 수 있는 사람은 나뿐이야.”\n\n“어머! 나는 네가 온통 갈색이나 흙빛 옷만 입을까 봐 걱정했어. 그런 데를 돌아다니며 묻혀 오는 때가 안 보이도록 말이야. 옛 아담의 표본 삼아 허영을 한두 가지 남겨 두겠다니 다행이다.”\n\n“나는 예전과 똑같이 지낼 거야, 이디스. 너와 이모가 그렇게 생각해 주기만 한다면. 다만 나는 자연히 주어지는 의무를 안겨 줄 남편도 자식도 없으니, 옷을 맞추는 일 말고도 무언가 할 일을 스스로 만들어야 해.”\n\n이디스와 그 어머니와 남편으로 이루어진 집안 회의에서는, 어쩌면 그녀의 이 모든 계획들이 오히려 그녀를 헨리 레녹스에게 더욱 단단히 붙들어 매어 줄 뿐이라는 결론이 났다. 그 계획들은 신랑감이 될 만한 아들이나 형제인 다른 벗들에게서 그녀를 떼어 놓았다. 또한 그녀가 자기네 집안사람 가운데 헨리 말고는 그 누구와 어울리는 데서도 별로 즐거움을 얻는 것 같지 않다는 데도 뜻이 모였다. 그녀의 외모나 재산에 대한 소문에 끌린 다른 구애자들은 그녀의 무심하고 웃는 듯한 경멸에 밀려, 덜 까다로운 다른 미인들이나 금붙이를 더 많이 지닌 다른 상속녀들이 다니는 길로 쓸려 갔다. 헨리와 그녀는 천천히 더 가까운 사이로 자라 갔다. 그러나 그도 그녀도 자기들의 일에 남이 조금이라도 참견하는 것을 참아 낼 사람은 아니었다.\n\n# 제50장\n\n“여기서 우리는 올라가네, 올라, 올라가네. 그리고 여기서 우리는 내려가네, 내려, 아래로 내려가네!” — 동요.\n\n그러는 동안 밀턴에서는 굴뚝이 연기를 뿜었고, 기계의 그침 없는 굉음과 육중한 박동과 눈부신 회전이 쉼 없이 다투고 겨루었다. 나무와 쇠와 증기는 그 끝없는 노역 속에서 아무 감각도 목적도 없었다. 그러나 그 단조로운 일이 지닌 끈질김에 못지않게 지칠 줄 모르는 인내를 보인 것은, 감각과 목적을 지닌 채 무엇인가를 좇아 바쁘고 쉴 새 없이 움직이던 억센 무리였다. 무엇을 좇아서였을까? 거리에는 어슬렁거리는 이가 드물었고, 그저 즐거움을 위해 걷는 이는 하나도 없었다. 사람마다 얼굴에 열의나 근심의 선이 굳어 있었다. 소식은 사나운 탐욕으로 구해졌고, 사람들은 삶에서 그러하듯 경쟁의 깊은 이기심 속에서 시장에서도 거래소에서도 서로를 밀쳐 냈다. 도시 위에 침울함이 드리워 있었다. 사러 오는 이가 드물었고, 오는 이들조차 파는 이들에게 의심스러운 눈길을 받았다. 신용이 불안했고, 가장 탄탄한 이라도 이웃한 큰 항구의 선박 상회들을 휩쓴 몰락에 재산이 흔들릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 지금까지 밀턴에는 파산이 없었다. 그러나 미국에서, 또 그보다 더 가까운 곳에서 나쁘게 끝나며 드러난 엄청난 투기들 탓에 밀턴의 몇몇 상회가 몹시 심하게 타격을 입으리라는 것이 알려져 있었다. 그리하여 날마다 사람들의 얼굴은, 혀가 묻지 않더라도 이렇게 묻고 있었다. “무슨 소식인가? 누가 무너졌나? 그것이 나에게 어떤 영향을 미칠까?” 그리고 두셋이 모여 이야기할 때면, 그들은 자기들 생각에 무너질 성싶은 이들의 이름을 감히 입에 올리기보다 무사한 이들의 이름을 되뇌었다. 그런 때에는 부질없는 말 한마디가, 그러지만 않았다면 폭풍을 견뎌 냈을 이들을 무너뜨릴 수도 있고, 하나가 무너지면 여럿이 뒤따라 끌려 들어가기 때문이다. “손턴은 안전하지.” 그들은 말했다. “그 사람 사업은 크고 해마다 커지고 있어. 하지만 그만한 머리를 지녔고, 그 대담함에도 그토록 신중하지 않은가!” 그러면 한 사람이 다른 사람을 한쪽으로 데려가 조금 떨어져 걸으며, 이웃의 귀에 고개를 기울이고는 이렇게 말한다. “손턴의 사업은 크지. 하지만 그는 이익을 사업을 키우는 데 다 써 버렸어. 쟁여 둔 자본이 없다고. 기계는 이 2년 안에 새로 들인 것인데, 그 값이 얼마였는지는 말하지 않겠네! 아는 사람에게는 한마디면 족하지!” 그러나 그렇게 말한 해리슨 씨는 투덜이였다. 아버지가 장사로 이룬 재산을 물려받고서, 사업 방식을 조금이라도 넓은 쪽으로 바꾸었다가 그것을 잃을까 두려워한 사람이었다. 그러면서도 더 대담하고 멀리 보는 이들이 벌어들이는 한 푼 한 푼을 아까워했다.\n\n그러나 사실인즉 손턴 씨는 몹시 쪼들리고 있었다. 그는 그것을 자신의 약한 곳에서 뼈아프게 느꼈다. 스스로 세워 놓은 상인으로서의 평판에 대한 자부심이 바로 그것이었다. 제 운명의 설계자였던 그는 그것을 자신의 특별한 공로나 자질 덕분으로 돌리지 않았다. 그는 상업이 용감하고 정직하고 끈기 있는 모든 사람에게 힘을 준다고 믿었다. 세속적 성공이라는 큰 판을 내려다보고 읽어 낼 수 있는 자리까지 스스로를 끌어올리고, 그렇게 멀리 봄으로써 정직하게, 다른 어떤 삶의 방식에서보다 더 큰 힘과 영향력을 부릴 수 있게 해 주는 힘이었다. 멀리 동방에서도 서방에서도, 제 사람됨은 결코 알려지지 않을 곳에서 제 이름이 존중받고, 제 뜻이 이루어지고, 제 말이 금처럼 통하게 되는 것. 그것이 손턴 씨가 출발점으로 삼았던 상인의 삶이라는 관념이었다. “그 상인들은 왕후와 같으니.” 어머니가 그 구절을 소리 내어 읽으며 말하곤 했다. 마치 그것이 제 아들을 싸움터로 부르는 나팔 소리이기라도 한 듯이. 그도 다른 많은 이들과 다르지 않았다. 남자든 여자든 아이든, 먼 것에는 깨어 있고 가까운 것에는 죽어 있는 사람들과 다르지 않았다. 그는 낯선 나라와 아득한 바다에서 통하는 이름의 영향력을 지니기를, 대대로 알려질 상회의 우두머리가 되기를 구했다. 그리고 지금, 오늘, 여기 제 고장에서, 제 공장에서, 제 사람들 사이에서 자신이 어떤 존재가 될 수 있는지를 어렴풋이나마 알아차리기까지 그에게는 오랜 침묵의 세월이 걸렸다. 그와 그들은 나란히 뻗은 삶을 살아왔다. 아주 가까웠으나 결코 맞닿지 않는 삶이었다. 히긴스와 알게 된 그 우연한 일이 있기까지는, 적어도 우연으로 보였던 그 일이 있기까지는 그러했다. 한 번 얼굴을 마주하고 사람 대 사람으로 제 둘레의 대중 가운데 한 개인과 마주 선 뒤로, 그리고 눈여겨보라, 처음부터 공장주와 직공이라는 자격을 벗어나 마주 선 뒤로, 두 사람은 저마다 “우리 모두에게는 하나의 사람 마음이 있다”는 것을 알아보기 시작했다. 그것이 쐐기의 뾰족한 끝이었다. 그리고 지금에 이르러서야 그는 비로소 깨달았다. 사람으로서 이제 막 알게 된 두어 명의 직공과 맺은 인연을 잃을지도 모른다는 걱정, 그의 마음에 아주 가까이 놓인 실험과도 같은 계획 한두 가지가 시험도 해 보지 못한 채 거칠게 잘려 나가리라는 걱정이, 이따금 그를 덮치던 그 미묘한 두려움에 새로운 아픔을 더했을 때에야 비로소, 자신이 공장주라는 자리에 대해 요사이 얼마나 크고 깊은 관심을 품게 되었는지를 깨달았던 것이다. 그저 그 자리가 자신을 그토록 가까운 접촉으로 이끌어 주고, 낯설고 약삭빠르고 무지하지만 무엇보다 개성과 강한 인간적 감정으로 가득한 한 무리의 사람들 사이에서 그토록 큰 힘을 쓸 기회를 주기 때문이었다.\n\n그는 밀턴 공장주로서 자신의 처지를 되짚어 보았다. 1년 반 전의 그 파업은, 아니 그보다 더 오래되었다. 지금은 늦봄인데도 때 아닌 겨울 날씨였으니 말이다. 그 파업이 있을 때 그는 젊었고, 지금 그는 늙어 있었다. 그 파업 탓에 그는 그때 손에 쥐고 있던 큰 주문 몇 건을 끝내지 못했다. 그는 새롭고 값비싼 기계에 자본을 꽤 많이 묶어 두었고, 계약으로 받아 둔 그 주문들을 채우려고 면화도 많이 사들였던 터였다. 그것들을 끝내지 못한 데에는 그가 들여온 아일랜드 직공들의 솜씨가 형편없이 모자랐던 탓도 얼마쯤 있었다. 그들이 한 일은 상당 부분 망가져 있어서, 일류 물건만 내놓는다고 자부하는 상회가 내보낼 만한 것이 못 되었다. 여러 달 동안 파업이 불러온 곤경은 손턴 씨의 앞길을 가로막는 장애였다. 그리고 히긴스에게 눈길이 가 닿을 때면 그는 당장은 아무 까닭이 없는데도 그에게 성난 말을 던질 뻔한 적이 여러 번이었다. 히긴스가 얽혀 있던 그 일에서 비롯된 손해가 얼마나 심각한지를 절감했기 때문이다. 그러나 이 갑작스럽고 재빠른 분함을 스스로 알아차렸을 때 그는 그것을 억누르기로 마음먹었다. 히긴스를 피하는 것으로는 만족할 수 없었다. 그는 사업의 엄격한 규칙이나 자신의 여가가 허락하는 한 언제든 히긴스가 자신에게 다가올 수 있도록 각별히 마음을 씀으로써, 자신이 제 분노의 주인임을 스스로에게 납득시켜야 했다. 그러다 이윽고 그는 분함을 다 잊고 놀라움에 빠져들었다. 자기와 히긴스처럼 같은 업으로 먹고살고 저마다 다른 방식으로 같은 목적을 향해 일하는 두 사람이 어떻게 서로의 처지와 의무를 그토록 이상하리만치 다르게 볼 수 있는가 하는 놀라움이었다. 그리하여 그 왕래가 생겨났다. 그 왕래가 훗날 일이 생겼을 때 의견과 행동의 충돌을 모조리 막아 주지는 못하더라도, 적어도 공장주와 직공이 서로를 훨씬 더 너그럽고 따뜻한 눈으로 바라보고, 서로를 더 참을성 있고 친절하게 견뎌 낼 수 있게 해 주기는 할 터였다. 이렇게 감정이 나아진 것 말고도, 손턴 씨와 그의 직공들은 사실에 관한 문제에서 자신들이 얼마나 무지한지를 알아차리게 되었다. 지금까지 한쪽은 알고 다른 쪽은 모르던 사실들이었다.\n\n그러나 이제 불황의 시기 가운데 하나가 닥쳐, 시세가 떨어지면서 쌓여 있는 모든 재고의 값이 함께 내려앉았다. 손턴 씨의 것은 거의 절반으로 떨어졌다. 주문은 들어오지 않았다. 그래서 그는 기계에 묶어 둔 자본의 이자를 잃었다. 아니, 이미 마친 주문의 대금을 받아 내는 일조차 어려웠다. 그런데도 사업을 돌리는 비용은 끊임없이 새어 나갔다. 그러다 사들인 면화의 어음 만기가 돌아왔다. 돈이 귀한 때라 그는 터무니없는 이자로밖에 빌릴 수 없었는데, 그러면서도 제 재산은 무엇 하나 현금으로 바꾸지 못했다. 그러나 그는 절망하지 않았다. 그는 낮이고 밤이고 애를 써서 온갖 급한 일을 미리 내다보고 대비했다. 집안의 여자들에게는 여느 때와 다름없이 차분하고 부드러웠다. 공장의 직공들에게 그는 많은 말을 하지 않았으나, 그들도 이제는 그를 알고 있었다. 그가 내놓는 짧고 단호한 대답을 그들은 예전처럼 속으로 부글거리며 걸핏하면 모진 말과 모진 판단을 쏟아 낼 태세로 받아들이기보다, 그를 짓누르는 근심을 알아보고 안쓰러워하며 받아들이는 일이 많았다. “나리는 속 썩일 일이 많구먼.” 어느 날 히긴스는 어째서 그런 지시를 따르지 않았느냐고 묻는 손턴 씨의 짧고 날카로운 물음을 듣고, 또 직공 몇이 일하고 있는 방을 지나가며 그가 삼키던 한숨 소리를 듣고는 그렇게 말했다. 그날 밤 히긴스와 또 한 사람은 아무도 모르게 시간을 넘겨 남아, 미뤄져 있던 그 일을 끝내 놓았다. 그리고 손턴 씨는 처음에 그 지시를 내렸던 감독이 손수 한 것이려니 여겼을 뿐 다른 것은 알지 못했다.\n\n“에! 우리 나리가 잿빛 옥양목 쪼가리처럼 저리 앉아 있는 꼴을 봤으면 누가 안쓰러워했을지 나는 알지! 우리 나리 얼굴에 서린 그 서글픈 낯빛을 그 늙은 목사 양반이 봤더라면 여자 같은 그 속을 다 태웠을 거여.” 어느 날 말버러 가에서 손턴 씨에게 다가가며 히긴스는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n“나리.” 그가 말하며, 빠르고 단호한 걸음으로 걷던 고용주를 멈춰 세웠다. 그 신사는 생각이 멀리 가 있기라도 했던 듯 갑자기 언짢게 흠칫하며 고개를 들었다.\n\n“요새 마거릿 아가씨 소식 들으신 게 있소?”\n\n“아가씨라니, 누구 말인가?” 손턴 씨가 되물었다.\n\n“마거릿 아가씨요, 헤일 양 말이오. 그 늙은 목사님 따님 말이오. 조금만 생각해 보시면 내가 누굴 말하는지 잘 아실 텐데……” (이렇게 말하는 그의 말투에는 무례한 구석이 조금도 없었다.)\n\n“아, 그렇지!” 그러자 대뜸 겨울 서리에 얼어붙은 듯하던 근심의 빛이 손턴 씨의 얼굴에서 사라졌다. 마치 부드러운 여름 바람이 그의 마음에서 온갖 걱정을 다 날려 보내기라도 한 듯했다. 입은 여전히 아까처럼 굳게 다물려 있었으나, 그의 눈은 묻는 이를 향해 인자하게 웃고 있었다.\n\n“그분은 이제 내 집주인일세, 히긴스. 이곳에 있는 그분 대리인을 통해 이따금 소식을 듣지. 잘 지내고 계시고, 좋은 이들 곁에 계시다네. 고맙네, 히긴스.” 그 “고맙네”는 다른 말들보다 뒤에 남아 맴돌았고, 그러면서도 어찌나 따뜻한 마음이 담겨 나왔던지, 눈치 빠른 히긴스에게 새로운 빛을 던져 주었다. 도깨비불에 지나지 않을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그는 그것을 따라가 어디로 데려가는지 알아보리라 마음먹었다.\n\n“그러면 그 아가씨는 아직 시집을 안 갔소, 나리?”\n\n“아직은.” 얼굴이 다시 흐려졌다. “내가 듣기로는 집안과 인연이 닿는 사람과 그런 이야기가 오간다더군.”\n\n“그러면 다시는 밀턴에 오실 일이 없겠구먼요.”\n\n“없겠지!”\n\n“잠깐만요, 나리.” 그러고는 은밀하게 바짝 다가서며 그가 말했다. “그 젊은 나리는 혐의를 벗었소?” 그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이며 자기가 아는 바가 얼마나 깊은지를 힘주어 알렸는데, 그 바람에 손턴 씨에게는 일이 더 알쏭달쏭해질 뿐이었다.\n\n“그 젊은 나리 말이오. 다들 프레더릭 도련님이라 부르던, 여기 와 있었던 아가씨 오라버니 말이오. 아시잖소.”\n\n“여기 와 있었다고.”\n\n“그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 마님 돌아가셨을 때 말이오. 내가 떠벌릴까 봐 걱정하실 것 없소. 메리하고 나는 처음부터 다 알고 있었지만 입을 다물었으니까. 메리가 그 댁에서 일한 덕에 알게 된 일이라서요.”\n\n“정말로 와 있었군. 그 사람이 그분의 오라버니였고.”\n\n“암, 그렇지요. 나는 나리도 아시는 줄 알았소. 안 그랬으면 입 밖에 내지도 않았을 거요. 아가씨한테 오라버니가 있는 건 아셨지요?”\n\n“그래, 그 사람에 대해서는 다 알고 있네. 그런데 그 사람이 헤일 부인이 돌아가실 때 여기 와 있었단 말인가?”\n\n“아니오! 더는 말하지 않으려오. 그들이 아주 꽁꽁 숨겨 온 일인데 내가 벌써 곤란하게 만들었을지도 모르니까. 나는 그저 그 사람 혐의를 벗겨 냈는지 그것만 알고 싶었소.”\n\n“내가 아는 바로는 아닐세. 나는 아무것도 모른다네. 이제는 헤일 양 소식도 내 집주인으로서, 또 그분 변호사를 통해서 들을 뿐이야.”\n\n그는 히긴스에게서 말을 끊고, 히긴스가 처음 그를 붙들었을 때 향하고 있던 볼일을 보러 갔다. 히긴스는 애쓴 보람도 없이 어리둥절한 채로 남겨졌다.\n\n“그 사람은 그분의 오라버니였구나.” 손턴 씨가 속으로 말했다. “다행이다. 나는 그분을 다시는 못 볼지도 모른다. 그러나 그것만이라도 알게 되어 위안이 된다. 아니, 마음이 놓인다. 그분이 처녀답지 못한 짓을 할 리 없다는 것은 알고 있었다. 그런데도 확신이 그토록 간절했다. 이제 다행이다!”\n\n그것은 갈수록 더 어두워져만 가는 그의 현재 운수라는 검은 거미줄을 가로지르는 조그만 황금 실 한 오라기였다. 그의 대리인은 미국 무역을 하는 어느 상회에 큰돈을 믿고 맡겨 두었는데, 바로 이 무렵 그 상회가 다른 여러 곳과 함께 카드로 쌓은 집처럼 무너졌다. 하나가 무너지면서 다른 것들의 파산을 불러온 것이었다. 손턴 씨가 짊어진 채무는 얼마인가? 그는 버텨 낼 수 있을까?\n\n밤이면 밤마다 그는 장부와 서류를 제 서재로 가져가 식구들이 다 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 오래도록 앉아 있었다. 잠으로 보내야 할 시간을 그렇게 쓰고 있다는 것을 아무도 모르리라고 그는 생각했다. 어느 날 아침, 덧문 틈으로 햇빛이 스며들고, 그는 잠자리에 든 적조차 없이 절망적인 무심함에 잠겨, 날마다의 노동이 다시 시작되기 전에 겨우 얻을 수 있는 한두 시간의 휴식쯤은 없어도 되겠다고 생각하고 있을 때였다. 방문이 열리더니 어머니가 어제와 똑같은 옷차림으로 거기 서 있었다. 그녀 역시 그와 마찬가지로 잠들려고 몸을 누인 적이 없었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 오래 깨어 있었던 탓에 두 얼굴은 차갑고 굳어 있었으며 핏기가 없었다.\n\n“어머니! 어째서 주무시지 않으셨습니까?”\n\n“존아.” 그녀가 말했다. “네가 근심에 싸여 뜬눈으로 지새우는데 내가 마음 편히 잘 수 있겠느냐? 무엇이 걱정인지 네가 내게 말해 주지는 않았다만, 요 며칠 네가 지독한 걱정거리를 안고 있다는 것쯤은 안다.”\n\n“장사가 나쁩니다.”\n\n“그래서 네가 두려워하는 것은……”\n\n“두려워하는 것은 없습니다.” 그가 고개를 들어 꼿꼿이 세우며 대답했다. “저 때문에 손해 볼 사람은 아무도 없다는 것을 압니다. 제 걱정은 그것뿐이었습니다.”\n\n“그런데 형편이 어떠냐? 설마…… 파산하게 되는 것이냐?” 늘 흔들림 없던 그녀의 목소리가 여느 때와 달리 떨렸다.\n\n“파산은 아닙니다. 사업은 접어야겠지만 사람들에게는 다 갚아 줍니다. 저를 다시 일으킬 수도 있습니다. 몹시 마음이 흔들립니다만……”\n\n“어떻게? 오, 존! 네 이름은 지켜야 한다. 그것을 위해서라면 어떤 위험이든 무릅써라. 어떻게 다시 일으킨다는 말이냐?”\n\n“제게 들어온 투기 이야기가 있습니다. 위험천만하지요. 그러나 성공하면 저를 물 위 높은 곳에 올려놓아, 제가 처한 궁지를 아무도 알 필요가 없게 될 겁니다. 그래도 실패하면……”\n\n“실패하면?” 그녀가 앞으로 나서며 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다. 두 눈에 간절한 빛이 가득했다. 그녀는 그의 말끝을 들으려고 숨을 죽였다.\n\n“정직한 사람들이 한 사기꾼 때문에 망하는 것이지요.” 그가 침울하게 말했다. “지금 제 형편으로는 채권자들의 돈은 한 푼도 빠짐없이 안전합니다. 그러나 제 몫이 어디에 있는지는 모르겠습니다. 다 사라졌을지도 모르고, 저는 이 순간 무일푼일지도 모릅니다. 그러니 제가 걸어야 할 것은 채권자들의 돈입니다.”\n\n“하지만 성공하면 그들은 알 필요도 없지 않느냐. 그렇게까지 무모한 투기냐? 그럴 리 없다. 그랬다면 너는 애초에 생각도 하지 않았을 테지. 성공한다면……”\n\n“저는 부자가 되겠지요. 그리고 제 양심의 평화는 사라질 겁니다!”\n\n“어째서! 네가 누구를 해친 것도 아닌데.”\n\n“해친 것은 아니지요. 그러나 제 하찮은 영달을 위해 여러 사람을 망하게 할 위험을 무릅쓴 것이 됩니다. 어머니, 저는 마음을 정했습니다! 이 집을 떠나는 일로 그리 슬퍼하지는 않으시겠지요, 어머니?”\n\n“그렇다! 하지만 네가 지금의 너 아닌 다른 무엇이 되는 것은 내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 게다. 이제 어찌하려느냐?”\n\n“어떤 형편에 놓이든 늘 같은 존 손턴으로 있으렵니다. 옳은 일을 하려 애쓰고, 크게 헛디디고, 그러고 나서 다시 시작하는 데 용감해지려 애쓰면서요. 그러나 힘듭니다, 어머니. 저는 일했고 계획했습니다. 제 자리에서 새로운 힘을 발견했는데 너무 늦었고, 이제 모든 것이 끝났습니다. 같은 마음으로 다시 시작하기에 저는 너무 늙었습니다. 힘듭니다, 어머니.”\n\n그는 어머니에게서 몸을 돌리고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n“나는 알 수가 없구나.” 그녀가 침울한 반항이 서린 말투로 말했다. “어쩌다 이렇게 되었는지. 여기 내 아들이 있다. 착한 아들이요 올바른 사람이요 따뜻한 마음을 지녔건만, 마음먹은 일마다 실패하는구나. 사랑할 여자를 만나면 그 여자는 흔해 빠진 사내의 마음이나 다름없이 그 애정을 대수롭지 않게 여기고, 힘써 일하면 그 수고는 헛것이 되고 말지. 다른 사람들은 부자가 되어 그 하찮은 이름을 부끄러움이 닿지 않는 높고 마른 자리에 올려놓는데.”\n\n“부끄러움은 저를 건드린 적이 없습니다.” 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n“나는 이따금 정의라는 것이 대체 어디로 갔는가 싶었다만, 이제는 그런 것이 이 세상에 있다고 믿지 않는다. 네가 이 지경이 되고 보니 말이다. 다른 누구도 아닌 내 아들 존 손턴이, 너와 내가 함께 거지가 될지언정, 내 사랑하는 아들이!”\n\n그녀는 아들의 목을 끌어안고 눈물 사이로 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“어머니!” 그가 어머니를 두 팔로 부드럽게 안으며 말했다. “좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 제 몫의 운명을 저에게 보내신 분이 누구입니까?”\n\n그녀는 고개를 저었다. 그때만큼은 종교와 아무 상관도 맺고 싶지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“어머니.” 어머니가 말하지 않으려는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었다. “저도 거역해 왔습니다. 그러나 이제 더는 그러지 않으려고 애쓰고 있습니다. 제가 어릴 적에 도와주셨듯이 저를 도와주십시오. 그때 어머니는 좋은 말씀을 많이 해 주셨습니다. 아버지가 돌아가시고 우리에게 필요한 것들이 몹시 모자라던 때에요. 이제 다시는 그렇게 모자라지 않을 테지만요. 그때 어머니는 용감하고 고결하고 믿음이 담긴 말씀을 해 주셨고, 저는 그 말씀을 한 번도 잊은 적이 없습니다. 그동안 잠들어 있었을지는 몰라도요. 다시 옛날처럼 제게 말씀해 주십시오, 어머니. 세상이 우리 마음을 너무 굳게 만들었다는 생각을 하게 두지 마십시오. 그 옛날의 좋은 말씀을 해 주신다면 제 어린 시절의 경건한 소박함을 조금이나마 느낄 수 있을 것 같습니다. 저 혼자 되뇌어 보기도 합니다만, 어머니가 견뎌 오신 온갖 근심과 시련을 생각하면 어머니 입에서 나오는 말은 다르게 들릴 것입니다.”\n\n“근심이야 많았지.” 그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다. “하지만 이만큼 쓰라린 것은 없었다. 네가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리에서 내던져지는 것을 보다니! 나 자신을 두고라면 그 말을 할 수 있겠다만, 너를 두고는 못 하겠구나. 너를 두고는! 하느님이 너에게는 아주 모질기로 작정하신 게야, 아주.”\n\n그녀는 늙은 사람이 울 때면 그렇게도 발작처럼 터져 나오는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었다. 마침내 둘레의 고요가 그녀의 마음을 쳤다. 그녀는 귀를 기울이려고 스스로를 가라앉혔다. 아무 소리도 없었다. 그녀가 바라보았다. 아들은 탁자 곁에 앉아 두 팔을 탁자 위에 반쯤 걸치고 고개를 숙여 얼굴을 묻고 있었다.\n\n“오, 존!” 그녀가 말하며 아들의 얼굴을 들어 올렸다. 그 얼굴에 어찌나 낯설고 창백한 침울함이 서려 있던지, 한순간 그녀는 이 낯빛이 죽음의 전조가 아닌가 하는 생각에 가슴이 철렁했다. 그러나 얼굴에서 굳은 기운이 녹아내리고 본디의 핏기가 돌아와 아들이 다시 제 모습을 되찾은 것을 보자, 세상의 온갖 굴욕은 그가 그저 살아 있다는 것만으로 자신에게 얼마나 큰 축복인가 하는 깨달음 앞에서 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었다. 그녀는 오직 이 한 가지를 두고 하느님께 감사드렸고, 그 뜨거움이 그녀의 마음에서 온갖 거역하는 감정을 쓸어 갔다.\n\n그는 선뜻 입을 열지 않았다. 다만 가서 덧문을 열어 붉은 새벽빛이 방 안에 넘쳐흐르게 했다. 그러나 바람은 동쪽에서 불어왔다. 몇 주째 그러했듯 날씨는 살을 에듯 추웠다. 올해에는 가벼운 여름 옷감을 찾는 수요가 없을 터였다. 장사가 되살아나리라는 그 희망은 아주 접어야 했다.\n\n어머니와 이런 대화를 나눈 것은 큰 위안이었다. 앞으로 이 모든 근심에 대해 둘이서 아무리 입을 다물게 되더라도 서로의 마음만은 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 그것을 바라보는 방식에서 화음까지는 아닐지언정 적어도 서로 어우러지는 불협화음을 이루고 있다는 것을 확신할 수 있어 큰 위안이었다. 패니의 남편은 자기가 내놓은 투기에 손턴이 한몫 끼기를 거절한 것에 부아가 났고, 현금으로 그를 도울 수 있으리라고 여겨질 만한 여지에서 아예 물러나 버렸다. 사실 그 투기꾼에게는 제 사업에 쓸 현금이 필요하기도 했다.\n\n마침내 손턴 씨가 여러 주 동안 두려워해 온 그 일 말고는 달리 길이 없게 되었다. 그는 그토록 오랫동안 그토록 큰 명예와 성공을 누리며 해 온 사업을 접고 남의 밑에 들어갈 자리를 알아보아야 했다. 말버러 공장과 거기 딸린 집은 장기 임차로 얻어 둔 것이었으니, 되도록 다시 세를 놓아야 했다. 손턴 씨에게는 곧바로 여러 자리가 제안되어 고를 수 있었다. 햄퍼 씨는 이웃 도시에서 큰 자본으로 개업시키려는 자기 아들에게 든든하고 노련한 동업자로 그를 붙들 수만 있다면 더없이 반가워했을 것이다. 그러나 그 젊은이는 지식이라는 면에서 어중간하게 배웠고, 돈 버는 일 말고 다른 어떤 책임이라는 면에서는 아예 배우지 못했으며, 즐거움에서나 괴로움에서나 거칠고 무디었다. 손턴 씨는 그 동업에 끼기를 거절했다. 그것은 제 재산의 난파에서 살아남은 몇 안 되는 계획마저 헛되이 만들 터였다. 몇 달 안에 반드시 다투게 되리라 확신하는 돈 가진 동업자의 횡포한 변덕에 맞춰 사는 것보다는, 차라리 그저 지배인에 지나지 않더라도 돈 버는 일 너머로 어느 정도 힘을 쓸 수 있는 자리에 앉는 편이 나았다.\n\n그리하여 그는 기다렸고, 처남이 대담한 투기로 어마어마한 재산을 벌었다는 소식이 거래소를 휩쓸고 지나가는 동안 깊은 겸손으로 한쪽에 비켜서 있었다. 그것은 아흐레짜리 소문거리였다. 성공에는 극진한 찬탄이라는 세속의 결과가 따라왔다. 왓슨 씨만큼 지혜롭고 멀리 내다보는 사람은 없다고들 했다.\n\n# 제51장\n\n“견뎌 다오, 용감한 마음이여! 우리는 침착하고 굳세리라. 정녕 우리는 눈도, 뺨도, 혀도 다스릴 수 있으리니, 그이가 예전에도 소중했고 지금도 소중하며 앞으로도 소중하리라는 것을 일러바치는 아주 작은 기색조차 내비치지 않으리라.” — 운문극.\n\n무더운 여름 저녁이었다. 이디스가 마거릿의 침실로 들어왔다. 처음에는 승마복 차림이었고, 두 번째에는 만찬 채비를 다 갖춘 차림이었다. 처음에는 아무도 없었다. 두 번째로 왔을 때 이디스는 딕슨이 침대 위에 마거릿의 드레스를 펼쳐 놓고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 마거릿은 없었다. 이디스는 남아서 안절부절못하며 서성거렸다.\n\n“어머, 딕슨! 그 칙칙한 금빛 드레스에 저 흉한 파란 꽃은 안 돼요. 무슨 취향이 그래요! 잠깐만 기다려요, 내가 석류꽃을 좀 가져올 테니.”\n\n“칙칙한 금빛이 아닙니다요, 마님. 밀짚색이지요. 그리고 파랑은 밀짚색과 언제나 어울립니다요.” 그러나 딕슨이 항변을 절반도 마치기 전에 이디스는 이미 눈부신 진홍빛 꽃을 가져와 있었다.\n\n“헤일 양은 어디 있어요?” 장식이 어떤지 대 보자마자 이디스가 물었다. “도무지 알 수가 없다니까요.” 그녀는 짜증스레 말을 이었다. “이모는 어쩌자고 밀턴에서 그 애가 그렇게 쏘다니는 버릇을 들이도록 내버려 두셨을까! 그 애가 기어드는 그 형편없는 곳들에서 무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 당했다는 소식을 듣게 될 것만 같아 늘 조마조마해요. 나라면 하인도 없이 그런 거리를 지나갈 엄두조차 못 낼 거예요. 숙녀가 다닐 만한 데가 아니라고요.”\n\n딕슨은 무시당한 자기 안목 때문에 아직 심통이 나 있었다. 그래서 다소 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다.\n\n“제 생각에는 조금도 이상할 게 없습니다요. 숙녀분들이 숙녀에 대해 그렇게나 말이 많은 걸 듣고 있으면요. 게다가 그렇게나 겁 많고 가녀리고 까다로운 숙녀분들일 때는 더요. 이 땅에 성인이 더는 남아 있지 않은 것도 저한테는 조금도 이상할 게 없다, 이 말씀입니다요——”\n\n“아, 마거릿! 여기 있었구나! 너를 얼마나 찾았다고. 그런데 더위에 뺨이 온통 달아올랐네, 가엾은 것! 그건 그렇고 그 성가신 헨리가 무슨 일을 벌였는지 좀 들어 봐. 정말이지 시동생의 도리를 넘어섰다니까. 내 자리가 그렇게나 아름답게 짜였는데, 콜서스트 씨에게 꼭 맞게 딱 들어맞았는데, 헨리가 나타나서는, 사과를 하기는 했지만, 게다가 네 이름까지 핑계 삼아 들먹이면서, 밀턴의 그 손턴 씨라는 사람을 데려와도 되겠냐고 묻지 뭐야. 네 세입자 말이야, 알지. 무슨 법률 일로 런던에 와 있다는구나. 인원수가 아주 엉망이 되겠어.”\n\n“저는 만찬은 괜찮아요. 먹고 싶지도 않고요.” 마거릿이 나직한 목소리로 말했다. “여기서 딕슨한테 차나 한 잔 얻어 마실게요. 언니가 올라올 때쯤이면 응접실에 나가 있을게요. 정말이지 좀 누워 있었으면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“안 돼, 안 돼! 그건 절대 안 되지. 하기야 얼굴이 지독하게 창백해 보이기는 해. 하지만 그건 그저 더위 탓이고, 우리는 도저히 너 없이는 안 돼. (그 꽃은 조금 더 아래로, 딕슨. 그 꽃이 네 검은 머리에서 눈부신 불꽃처럼 보이는구나, 마거릿.) 네가 콜서스트 씨에게 밀턴 이야기를 해 주기로 우리가 계획해 둔 거 알잖니. 아! 그러고 보니! 이 사람도 밀턴에서 왔네. 결국 아주 잘된 일일 것 같아. 콜서스트 씨가 관심 있는 온갖 주제에 대해 그 사람한테서 실컷 캐낼 수 있을 테고, 콜서스트 씨가 다음번에 의회에서 하는 연설 속에서 네 경험과 이 손턴 씨의 식견을 짚어 내는 것도 아주 재미있겠지. 정말이지 헨리의 묘수였다는 생각이 들어. 부끄러워할 만한 사람이냐고 내가 물었더니 이렇게 대답하더구나. ‘누나한테 분별이란 게 조금이라도 있다면 그럴 리 없죠.’ 그러니 h 발음쯤은 제대로 낼 줄 아는 사람인가 봐. 다크셔에서는 흔한 재주가 아니라던데, 그렇지, 마거릿?”\n\n“레녹스 씨가 손턴 씨는 무슨 일로 상경했는지는 말하지 않던가요? 그 부동산과 관련된 법률 문제였나요?” 마거릿이 억눌린 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“아! 그 사람 파산했다든가 뭐 그런 거래. 네가 그렇게 머리가 아팠던 날 헨리가 너한테 말했다던 그거 말이야. 뭐였더라? (그래, 아주 훌륭해, 딕슨. 헤일 양이 우리 체면을 세워 주겠지, 안 그래?) 나도 너처럼 여왕같이 훤칠하고 집시처럼 볕에 그을렸으면 좋겠구나, 마거릿.”\n\n“그런데 손턴 씨 이야기는요?”\n\n“아! 나는 정말이지 법률 일에는 머리가 형편없이 안 돌아가서. 헨리가 너한테 전부 이야기해 주는 걸 무엇보다 좋아할걸. 내가 받은 인상이라면, 손턴 씨가 형편이 아주 나빠졌고, 아주 존경할 만한 사람이며, 내가 그분께 아주 깍듯이 대해야 한다는 것 정도야. 그런데 어떻게 해야 할지 몰라서 너한테 도와 달라고 부탁하러 온 거고. 자, 이제 나랑 같이 내려가서 15분쯤 소파에 누워 쉬자.”\n\n특권을 누리는 그 시동생은 일찍 왔다. 마거릿은 말을 꺼내면서 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 손턴 씨에 대해 듣고 싶었던 것들을 그에게 묻기 시작했다.\n\n“그분은 그 부동산을 전대하는 문제로 올라왔습니다. 말버러 공장과 거기 딸린 집과 부지 말입니다. 계속 유지할 수가 없게 된 거지요. 그래서 살펴봐야 할 증서와 임차 계약이 있고, 작성해야 할 합의서도 있습니다. 이디스가 그분을 제대로 맞아 주면 좋겠는데, 제가 주제넘게 초대를 청한 것 때문에 형수가 다소 언짢아하는 눈치더군요. 하지만 당신이 그분에게 어느 정도 대접을 해 드리고 싶어 하리라 생각했습니다. 게다가 세상에서 몰락해 가는 사람에게는 유난히 조심스럽게 온갖 예를 다하고 싶어지는 법이지요.” 그는 옆자리에 앉은 마거릿에게 말하느라 목소리를 낮추고 있었다. 그러나 말을 마치자마자 벌떡 일어나, 그 순간 막 들어선 손턴 씨를 이디스와 레녹스 대위에게 소개했다.\n\n마거릿은 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 근심 어린 눈으로 손턴 씨를 바라보았다. 그를 마지막으로 본 지 일 년이 훌쩍 넘었고, 그사이 그를 크게 바꾸어 놓을 만한 일들이 일어났다. 훤칠한 체구는 여전히 그를 보통 남자들보다 크게 보이게 했고, 거기서 우러나 그에게 자연스레 배어 있는 여유로운 몸놀림이 그에게 남다른 풍모를 주었다. 그러나 얼굴은 더 늙고 근심에 시달린 듯 보였다. 그런데도 그 위에는 고결한 평정이 어려 있어서, 그가 처지가 달라졌다는 이야기를 방금 들은 이들에게 타고난 위엄과 사내다운 굳셈을 느끼게 했다. 그는 방 안을 처음 한 번 둘러보았을 때부터 마거릿이 거기 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀가 헨리 레녹스 씨의 말에 귀 기울이며 골똘히 몰두한 표정을 짓고 있는 것도 보았다. 그리고 그는 오랜 벗의 완벽하게 절제된 태도로 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그의 첫 담담한 말과 함께 선연한 붉은빛이 그녀의 뺨으로 확 번졌고, 그 빛은 그날 저녁 내내 다시 가시지 않았다. 그녀는 그에게 딱히 할 말이 많지 않은 듯했다. 그녀는 밀턴의 옛 지인들에 대해 그가 보기에는 그저 형식적으로 필요한 정도의 질문들을 조용히 던지는 것으로 그를 실망시켰다. 그러나 다른 사람들이 들어왔다. 그보다 이 집안과 가까운 이들이었다. 그리하여 그는 뒤로 물러나게 되었고, 거기서 이따금 레녹스 씨와 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n“헤일 양의 안색이 좋아 보이지요.” 레녹스 씨가 말했다. “그렇지 않습니까? 밀턴은 저 사람과 맞지 않았던 모양입니다. 처음 런던에 왔을 때는 사람이 그토록 변한 것을 본 적이 없다 싶었으니까요. 오늘 밤은 환히 빛나는군요. 게다가 몸도 훨씬 튼튼해졌습니다. 지난가을만 해도 3킬로미터쯤 걷고는 지쳤는데, 금요일 저녁에는 우리가 햄프스테드까지 걸어갔다가 돌아왔지요. 그런데도 토요일에 지금처럼 좋아 보이더군요.”\n\n“우리라니!” 누구와? 두 사람만이?\n\n콜서스트 씨는 매우 영민한 사람이었고, 떠오르는 하원 의원이었다. 그는 사람됨을 알아보는 눈이 빨랐는데, 만찬 자리에서 손턴 씨가 한 말 한마디에 깊은 인상을 받았다. 그는 저 신사가 누구냐고 이디스에게 물었다. 그리고 이디스는 다소 놀랍게도 그의 “아, 그렇습니까!” 하는 어조에서 밀턴의 손턴 씨가 자기가 짐작했던 것처럼 그에게 낯선 이름이 아니라는 것을 알아차렸다. 그녀의 만찬은 잘 굴러가고 있었다. 헨리는 기분이 좋았고, 그 특유의 메마르고 신랄한 재치를 훌륭하게 펼쳐 보였다. 손턴 씨와 콜서스트 씨는 서로 관심이 겹치는 주제를 한두 가지 찾아냈는데, 그 자리에서는 잠깐 건드리기만 하고 만찬 뒤의 좀 더 사사로운 이야기 시간으로 미루어 두었다. 마거릿은 석류꽃을 꽂은 모습이 아름다웠다. 그리고 그녀가 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 말을 거의 하지 않아도 이디스는 언짢아하지 않았으니, 그녀 없이도 대화가 매끄럽게 흘러갔기 때문이다. 마거릿은 손턴 씨의 얼굴을 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 한 번도 그녀를 보지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 그를 살필 수 있었고, 이 짧은 시간이 그에게 새겨 놓은 변화를 헤아릴 수 있었다. 다만 레녹스 씨의 예기치 못한 한마디가 나올 때면, 그의 얼굴에 예전의 그 짙은 즐거움이 확 번졌다. 유쾌한 광채가 눈으로 돌아왔고, 입술이 살짝 벌어지며 지난날의 눈부신 미소를 예고했다. 그리고 한순간 그의 시선은 마치 그녀의 공감을 구하기라도 하듯 본능적으로 그녀의 시선을 찾았다. 그러나 두 사람의 눈이 마주치자 그의 온 낯빛이 달라졌다. 그는 다시금 무겁고 근심스러워졌고, 만찬이 끝날 때까지 그녀 쪽으로 눈길을 주는 것조차 단호히 피했다.\n\n그들 일행 말고는 부인이 둘밖에 없었는데, 응접실로 올라갔을 때 그 두 사람은 쇼 이모와 이디스가 붙들고 이야기를 나누는 통에, 마거릿은 나른하게 바느질감을 붙들고 앉았다. 이윽고 신사들이 올라왔다. 콜서스트 씨와 손턴 씨는 열띤 대화를 나누는 중이었다. 레녹스 씨가 마거릿 가까이 다가와 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“이디스가 자기 만찬에 내가 보탠 몫에 대해 정말 고마워해야 할 것 같군요. 당신의 저 세입자가 얼마나 유쾌하고 분별 있는 사람인지 당신은 모르실 겁니다. 콜서스트가 배워 두고 싶어 하던 사실들을 전부 알려 주기에 더없이 알맞은 사람이었어요. 그런 사람이 어떻게 제 일을 그르쳤는지 나로서는 짐작이 안 갑니다.”\n\n“그분이 지녔던 역량과 기회가 있었다면 당신은 성공했겠지요.” 마거릿이 말했다. 그 말이 그 자신의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각을 그대로 옮긴 것이었는데도, 그는 그녀가 말한 어조가 썩 마음에 들지 않았다. 그가 잠자코 있는 사이, 두 사람은 벽난로 옆에서 콜서스트 씨와 손턴 씨가 나누는 대화가 한결 높아지는 것을 들었다.\n\n“정말이지, 사람들이 그 일을 대단한 관심을 가지고 이야기하는 것을 들었습니다. 어쩌면 결과가 어찌 될지 궁금해한다고 말하는 편이 낫겠군요. 그 근방에 잠깐 머무는 동안 선생 성함이 자주 오르내리는 것을 들었습니다.” 그러고는 몇 마디를 놓쳤다. 다시 들리기 시작했을 때는 손턴 씨가 말하고 있었다.\n\n“저에게는 인기를 끌 만한 자질이 없습니다. 사람들이 저를 그런 식으로 말했다면 잘못 본 것이지요. 저는 새 계획에 더디게 들어서고, 저 자신을 남에게 알리는 일을 어려워합니다. 제가 알고 지내고 싶은 사람들, 아무런 거리낌도 두고 싶지 않은 사람들에게조차 그렇습니다. 그런데도 이런 온갖 결점을 안고서도 제가 옳은 길 위에 있다는 것을 느꼈고, 한 사람과의 우정 비슷한 것에서 출발해 여러 사람과 아는 사이가 되어 가고 있다는 것을 느꼈습니다. 이로움은 양쪽 모두에게 돌아갔지요. 우리는 저도 모르게, 또 알면서도 서로를 가르치고 있었습니다.”\n\n“‘있었다’고 말씀하시는군요. 앞으로도 같은 길을 이어 가실 생각이겠지요?”\n\n“콜서스트를 막아야겠습니다.” 헨리 레녹스가 급히 말했다. 그러고는 불쑥 던지면서도 때에 맞는 물음으로 대화의 물줄기를 돌려놓아, 손턴 씨가 자신의 실패와 그로 인한 처지의 변화를 인정하는 굴욕을 겪지 않도록 했다. 그러나 새로 꺼낸 화제가 마무리되자마자 손턴 씨는 끊겼던 바로 그 자리에서 대화를 다시 이어, 콜서스트 씨의 물음에 답을 주었다.\n\n“저는 사업에 실패했고, 공장주라는 자리를 내놓아야 했습니다. 지금은 밀턴에서 일자리를 찾고 있습니다. 이런 일들에 관해서만은 제 방식대로 하도록 놓아둘 뜻이 있는 누군가 밑에서 일할 수 있는 자리 말입니다. 함부로 실행에 옮길 만한 앞서 나간 이론 따위는 제가 품지 않으리라는 것은 저 자신이 보증할 수 있습니다. 제 유일한 바람은 단순한 ‘현금 관계’를 넘어 직공들과 얼마간 교류를 가꿀 기회를 얻는 것뿐입니다. 그런데 제가 시도해 보고 싶은 한두 가지 실험을 입에 올리기가 무섭게 고개를 젓고 심각한 낯빛을 짓는 우리 공장주들이 거기에 부여하는 무게로 보자면, 그것은 아르키메데스가 지구를 움직이려고 찾던 그 받침점쯤 되는 모양입니다.”\n\n“‘실험’이라고 부르시는군요.” 콜서스트 씨가 태도에 은근히 존경을 더하며 말했다.\n\n“그것이 실험이라고 믿기 때문입니다. 저는 거기서 어떤 결과가 나올지 확신하지 못합니다. 그러나 시도해 보아야 마땅하다는 것만은 확신합니다. 저는 이런 확신에 이르렀습니다. 아무리 지혜롭고, 그것을 조직하고 정비하는 데 아무리 많은 궁리가 들어갔다 하더라도, 제도 그 자체만으로는 계급과 계급을 마땅히 이어져야 할 만큼 이어 놓을 수 없다는 것입니다. 그 제도를 실제로 굴려 나가는 과정이 서로 다른 계급의 사람들을 참으로 사람 대 사람으로 맞닿게 하지 않는 한 말이지요. 그런 교류야말로 삶의 숨결 그 자체입니다. 노동자에게, 자기 고용주가 일꾼들의 이익을 위한 계획을 세우느라 서재에서 얼마나 애를 썼는지를 느끼고 알게 만들기란 좀처럼 어려운 일입니다. 완성된 계획은 마치 기계 한 대처럼 튀어나옵니다. 겉보기에는 어떤 상황에도 들어맞도록 짜여 있지요. 그러나 직공들은 그것을 기계를 받아들이듯 받아들일 뿐, 그것을 그만한 완성도로 끌어올리기까지 필요했던 극심한 정신노동과 미리 헤아린 수고는 알지 못합니다. 하지만 저라면 실행하자면 반드시 사람 대 사람의 교류가 있어야만 하는 그런 착상을 택하겠습니다. 처음에는 잘 굴러가지 않을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 걸림돌이 생길 때마다 점점 더 많은 사람이 관심을 갖게 될 것이고, 마침내는 모두가 그 계획을 만드는 데 한몫을 했으니 그것이 잘 굴러가기를 모두가 바라게 될 것입니다. 그리고 그때조차도 저는 확신합니다. 서로 얼굴을 볼 방도와 길을 어김없이 찾아내게 하고, 서로의 성품과 사람됨을, 나아가 성미의 버릇과 말투까지도 알게 만드는 그런 공통의 관심이 더는 그것을 떠받치지 않게 되는 순간, 그 계획은 생기를 잃고 살아 있기를 그치리라는 것을요. 우리는 서로를 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이고, 감히 말하건대 서로를 더 좋아하게 될 것입니다.”\n\n“그러면 그것이 파업의 재발을 막아 주리라고 보십니까?”\n\n“전혀 아닙니다. 제가 최대한으로 기대하는 것은 여기까지입니다. 파업이 지금까지 그래 왔던 것처럼 쓰라리고 독한 증오의 원천이 되지는 않게 하리라는 것 말입니다. 저보다 희망을 품은 사람이라면 계급 사이의 더 가깝고 더 다정한 교류가 파업을 아예 없애 주리라고 상상할지도 모르지요. 그러나 저는 희망을 품은 사람이 아닙니다.”\n\n문득 새로운 생각이 떠오르기라도 한 듯 그는 마거릿이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 건너가더니, 그녀가 오간 이야기를 전부 듣고 있었음을 안다는 듯 아무런 서두도 없이 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“헤일 양, 제 일꾼들 몇몇이 보낸 연명 편지를 받았습니다. 히긴스의 필체인 것 같더군요. 제가 다시 제 이름으로 사람을 부릴 처지가 되기만 하면 저를 위해 일하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝힌 것이었습니다. 좋은 일이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“네. 참으로 마땅한 일이에요. 저도 기뻐요.” 마거릿이 말하는 듯한 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 올려다보며 말했다. 그러고는 무언가를 말하는 듯한 그의 눈길 아래 시선을 떨구었다. 그는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 정확히 알지 못하는 사람처럼 잠시 그녀를 마주 바라보았다. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬었다. 그리고 “좋아하시리라 알고 있었습니다.” 하고 말하고는 돌아섰고, 격식을 차린 “안녕히 계십시오.”라는 인사를 건넬 때까지 그녀에게 다시는 말을 걸지 않았다.\n\n레녹스 씨가 돌아가려 할 때, 마거릿은 억누를 수 없는 홍조를 띤 채 얼마간 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n“내일 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있을까요? 어떤 일에 대해 도움이 필요해서요.”\n\n“물론입니다. 시간을 정해 주시면 언제든 오지요. 저를 어디엔가 쓸모 있게 해 주시는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨을 제게 주실 수는 없습니다. 11시에요? 좋습니다.”\n\n그의 눈이 의기양양하게 빛났다. 그녀가 자신에게 기대는 법을 배워 가고 있지 않은가! 이제는 어느 날엔가 그 확신이 주어질 것만 같았다. 그 확신 없이는 그녀에게 다시는 청혼하지 않겠다고 그는 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n# 제52장\n\n「구름아 걷혀라」\n\n“기쁨에도 슬픔에도, 희망에도 두려움에도, 여기서와 마찬가지로 앞으로 올 모든 날에도, 평화에도 다툼에도, 폭풍에도 햇살에도.” — 작자 미상.\n\n이디스는 이튿날 아침 내내 발끝으로 걸어 다녔고, 숄토가 큰 소리로 말할 때마다 제지했다. 마치 갑작스러운 소리라도 나면 응접실에서 벌어지고 있는 담판이 끊기기라도 할 것처럼. 두 시가 되었다. 그런데도 두 사람은 여전히 문을 닫아건 채 그 안에 앉아 있었다. 이윽고 층계를 뛰어 내려오는 남자의 발소리가 났고, 이디스가 응접실 밖으로 고개를 내밀었다.\n\n“그래서, 헨리?” 그녀가 묻는 듯한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n“그래서라니요!” 그가 다소 퉁명스럽게 대답했다.\n\n“들어와서 점심 들어요!”\n\n“아뇨, 고맙지만 그럴 수 없습니다. 여기서 이미 너무 많은 시간을 허비했어요.”\n\n“그럼 아직 다 정해진 게 아니군요?” 이디스가 낙담해서 말했다.\n\n“아뇨! 전혀 아닙니다. 그 ‘다’라는 것이 제 짐작대로 형수님이 뜻하는 그것이라면, 그건 영영 정해지지 않을 겁니다. 절대 그렇게 되지 않을 테니, 이디스, 그 생각은 그만두세요.”\n\n“하지만 우리 모두에게 얼마나 좋겠어요.” 이디스가 매달리듯 말했다. “마거릿이 내 가까이에 자리를 잡아 준다면 아이들 걱정은 늘 놓을 수 있을 텐데. 지금 같아서는 그 애가 카디스로 훌쩍 가 버릴까 봐 늘 조마조마해요.”\n\n“저는 결혼할 때가 되면 아이 다루는 법을 아는 아가씨를 찾아보도록 하지요. 제가 할 수 있는 건 그것뿐입니다. 헤일 양은 저를 받아 주지 않을 테고, 저도 청하지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“그럼 무슨 이야기를 그렇게 한 거예요?”\n\n“형수님은 알아듣지 못할 천 가지 것들이지요. 투자며 임대차며 땅값 같은 것들.”\n\n“아, 그게 다라면 어서 가 봐요. 그렇게 오래도록 그런 따분한 이야기만 했다면, 도련님이나 그 애나 못 견디게 재미없는 사람들이네요.”\n\n“좋습니다. 내일 다시 오지요. 손턴 씨를 모시고 와서 헤일 양과 이야기를 좀 더 나눌 겁니다.”\n\n“손턴 씨요! 그분이 그 일과 무슨 상관이에요?”\n\n“그분은 헤일 양의 세입자니까요.” 레녹스 씨가 돌아서며 말했다. “그리고 임차를 그만두고 싶어 합니다.”\n\n“아! 알겠어요. 자세한 건 알아듣지도 못하니 나한테 늘어놓지 말아요.”\n\n“제가 알아주셨으면 하는 자세한 사정은 딱 하나입니다. 오늘처럼 안쪽 응접실을 방해받지 않게 내어 주십사 하는 것이지요. 평소에는 아이들과 하인들이 어찌나 들락날락하는지 무슨 일이든 제대로 설명해 볼 수가 없습니다. 내일 우리가 매듭지어야 할 일들은 중요한 것들이에요.”\n\n이튿날 레녹스 씨가 왜 그 약속을 지키지 않았는지는 아무도 알지 못했다. 손턴 씨는 시간에 맞춰 왔다. 그리고 한 시간 가까이 그를 기다리게 한 끝에, 마거릿이 몹시 창백하고 불안한 얼굴로 들어왔다.\n\n그녀는 서둘러 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“레녹스 씨가 안 계셔서 정말 죄송해요. 저보다 훨씬 잘 해내셨을 텐데. 이 일에서는 그분이 제 조언자시거든요……”\n\n“제가 와서 번거롭게 해 드렸다면 죄송합니다. 레녹스 씨 사무실로 가서 찾아볼까요?”\n\n“아뇨, 괜찮습니다. 제가 드리고 싶었던 말씀은, 세입자로 모시던 손턴 씨를 잃게 되었다는 걸 알고 얼마나 마음이 아팠는지 하는 거예요. 하지만 레녹스 씨 말씀으로는 사정이 틀림없이 나아질 거라고……”\n\n“레녹스 씨는 그 일을 잘 모릅니다.” 손턴 씨가 조용히 말했다. “사람이 바랄 만한 모든 것에서 행복하고 운이 좋은 이는, 자신이 더 이상 젊지 않다는 것을 깨닫는 일이 어떤 것인지 모릅니다. 그러면서도 젊음의 희망찬 기운을 요구하는 출발점으로 도로 내던져지는 것이, 인생의 절반이 가 버렸는데 이룬 것은 하나도 없다고 느끼는 것이, 허비한 기회에서 남은 것이라고는 그런 일이 있었다는 쓰디쓴 기억뿐임을 아는 것이 어떤 것인지를 모른단 말입니다. 헤일 양, 제 일에 대한 레녹스 씨의 의견은 차라리 듣고 싶지 않습니다. 스스로 행복하고 성공한 사람들은 남의 불운을 너무나 쉽게 가벼이 여기니까요.”\n\n“그건 부당하세요.” 마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다. “레녹스 씨는 다만 손턴 씨가 잃으신 것을 되찾을, 아니 되찾고도 남을 가능성이 크다고 믿기에 그 말씀을 하셨을 뿐이에요. 제 말이 끝날 때까지는 아무 말씀 마세요. 부디요.” 그러고는 다시 마음을 가다듬고, 떨리는 손으로 조급하게 법률 서류와 계산서를 뒤적이며 빠르게 말을 이었다. “아! 여기 있네요. 그리고 그분이 제게 제안서를 하나 써 주셨어요. 설명해 주실 수 있게 그분이 여기 계셨으면 좋았을 텐데. 지금 이 순간 은행에 쓰이지 않은 채 놓여 있으면서 저에게 겨우 2.5퍼센트밖에 가져다주지 않는 제 돈 1,857파운드를 손턴 씨가 받아 주신다면, 저에게 훨씬 나은 이자를 주실 수 있고 말버러 공장도 계속 돌리실 수 있다는 것이었어요.” 그녀의 목소리는 어느새 맑아지고 한결 차분해져 있었다. 손턴 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그녀는 담보 조건이 적힌 종이를 계속 찾았다. 이 모든 것이 순전한 사업상의 약정으로만, 그것도 이득의 대부분이 자기 쪽에 있는 거래로만 보이기를 그녀는 무엇보다 간절히 바랐던 것이다. 그 종이를 찾고 있는 사이, 손턴 씨가 입을 여는 그 어조에 그녀의 심장 박동이 그대로 멎어 버렸다. 그의 목소리는 쉬어 있었고, 애틋한 정열로 떨리고 있었다.\n\n“마거릿!”\n\n한순간 그녀는 눈을 들었다. 그러고는 두 손에 이마를 묻어 빛나는 눈을 가리려 했다. 그가 한 걸음 더 다가서서, 떨리면서도 간절한 부름으로 그녀의 이름을 또 한 번 불렀다.\n\n“마거릿!”\n\n고개는 더욱 낮게 떨구어졌고, 얼굴은 더욱 깊이 감추어져 앞의 탁자에 거의 닿을 듯했다. 그가 그녀에게 바싹 다가섰다. 그는 그녀의 곁에 무릎을 꿇어 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 귀 높이에 맞추고는, 속삭이듯 헐떡이듯 말을 뱉었다.\n\n“조심하세요. 당신이 아무 말도 하지 않으면, 저는 무언가 이상하고 주제넘은 방식으로 당신을 제 사람이라 주장하고 말 겁니다. 제가 가야 한다면 지금 당장 저를 내치세요. 마거릿!”\n\n그 세 번째 부름에 그녀는 여전히 작고 흰 두 손으로 가린 얼굴을 그에게로 돌려 그의 어깨에 기댔고, 아예 그 안에 묻어 버렸다. 그녀의 보드라운 뺨이 자기 뺨에 닿는 느낌이 너무나 감미로워, 그는 짙게 물든 홍조도 사랑에 젖은 눈도 굳이 보고 싶지 않았다. 그는 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다. 그러나 두 사람 다 말이 없었다. 이윽고 그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 중얼거렸다.\n\n“아, 손턴 씨, 저는 그럴 만한 사람이 못 돼요!”\n\n“그럴 만하지 않다니요! 스스로 자격이 없다고 느끼는 제 깊은 마음을 놀리지 마세요.”\n\n1, 2분쯤 지난 뒤, 그는 그녀의 두 손을 얼굴에서 부드럽게 떼어 내고, 언젠가 폭도들로부터 그를 지키려 놓였던 그대로 그녀의 두 팔을 놓아 주었다.\n\n“기억하시나요, 내 사랑?” 그가 나직이 말했다. “그리고 제가 그 이튿날 어떤 무례로 그 은혜를 갚았는지도요?”\n\n“제가 얼마나 잘못된 말을 했는지가 기억날 뿐이에요.”\n\n“여기 보세요! 고개를 드세요. 보여 드릴 것이 있습니다!” 그녀는 아름다운 부끄러움으로 발갛게 달아오른 채 천천히 그를 마주 보았다.\n\n“이 장미들을 아시겠습니까?” 그가 말하며 수첩을 꺼냈다. 그 안에는 시든 꽃 몇 송이가 소중히 간직되어 있었다.\n\n“아뇨!” 그녀가 천진한 호기심으로 대답했다. “제가 드린 건가요?”\n\n“아닙니다. 우쭐하셨군요. 당신이 주신 게 아닙니다. 아마 그 자매쯤 되는 장미를 몸에 다신 적은 있겠지요.”\n\n그녀는 잠시 들여다보며 궁금해하다가, 이윽고 살며시 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“헬스톤 장미로군요, 그렇죠? 잎 가장자리의 깊은 톱니를 알아보겠어요. 아! 거기에 가셨었나요? 언제 가셨어요?”\n\n“마거릿이 지금의 마거릿으로 자라난 곳을 보고 싶었습니다. 그녀를 제 사람이라 부를 희망이라고는 조금도 없던, 가장 힘든 그때조차도 말입니다. 아브르에서 돌아오는 길에 그곳에 갔습니다.”\n\n“저에게 주셔야 해요.” 그녀가 말하며 부드러운 힘으로 그의 손에서 그것을 빼앗으려 했다.\n\n“좋습니다. 다만 값을 치르셔야 합니다!”\n\n“쇼 이모에게는 어떻게 말씀드리죠?” 감미로운 침묵이 얼마쯤 흐른 뒤 그녀가 속삭였다.\n\n“제가 말씀드리게 해 주세요.”\n\n“아, 안 돼요! 제가 직접 말씀드리는 것이 도리인걸요. 그런데 뭐라고 하실까요?”\n\n“짐작이 갑니다. 첫마디는 이러시겠지요. ‘그 남자가!’”\n\n“쉿!” 마거릿이 말했다. “안 그러면 어머님께서 ‘그 여자가!’ 하실 때의 그 노기 띤 어조를 흉내 내 보일 거예요.”";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-4276"]=[[["# CHAPTER I", "# 제1장"]], [["“HASTE TO THE WEDDING.”", "「혼례를 서둘러라」"]], [["“Wooed and married and a’.”", "“구애하고 혼인하고 만사가 이루어졌네.”"]], [["“Edith!”", "“이디스!”"], ["said Margaret, gently, “Edith!”", "마거릿이 나직이 불렀다. “이디스!”"]], [["But as Margaret half suspected, Edith had fallen asleep. She lay curled up on the sofa in the back drawing-room in Harley Street looking very lovely in her white muslin and blue ribbons. If Titania had ever been dressed in white muslin and blue ribbons, and had fallen asleep on a crimson damask sofa in a back drawing-room, Edith might have been taken for her. Margaret was struck afresh by her cousin’s beauty. They had grown up together from childhood, and all along Edith had been remarked upon by every one, except Margaret, for her prettiness; but Margaret had never thought about it until the last few days, when the prospect of soon losing her companion seemed to give force to every sweet quality and charm which Edith possessed. They had been talking about wedding dresses and wedding ceremonies; and Captain Lennox, and what he had told Edith about her future life at Corfu, where his regiment was stationed; and the difficulty of keeping a piano in good tune (a difficulty which Edith seemed to consider as one of the most formidable that could befall her in her married life), and what gowns she should want in the visits to Scotland, which would immediately succeed her marriage; but the whispered tone had latterly become more drowsy; and Margaret, after a pause of a few minutes, found, as she fancied, that in spite of the buzz in the next room, Edith had rolled herself up into a soft ball of muslin and ribbon, and silken curls, and gone off into a peaceful little after-dinner nap.", "그러나 마거릿이 반쯤 짐작한 대로 이디스는 잠들어 있었다. 할리 가 안쪽 응접실 소파에 몸을 웅크린 채 누운 그녀는 흰 모슬린 드레스에 파란 리본을 달고 있어 무척이나 어여뻤다. 티타니아가 흰 모슬린과 파란 리본을 걸치고 안쪽 응접실의 진홍빛 다마스크 소파에서 잠든 적이 있었다면, 이디스를 그 요정으로 착각했을지도 모른다. 마거릿은 사촌의 아름다움에 새삼 마음이 흔들렸다. 둘은 어릴 적부터 함께 자랐고, 마거릿만 빼고는 누구나 이디스의 예쁘장함을 입에 올렸다. 그러나 마거릿은 요 며칠 전까지 그것을 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 곧 벗을 잃게 되리라는 예감이 이디스가 지닌 온갖 사랑스러운 자질과 매력에 한층 힘을 실어 주는 듯했다. 두 사람은 신부 드레스와 혼례 절차 이야기를 나누던 참이었다. 레녹스 대위 이야기도, 그가 이디스에게 들려준 코르푸에서의 앞날 이야기도 나왔다. 그의 연대가 그곳에 주둔해 있었다. 피아노를 제대로 조율해 두기가 얼마나 어려운가 하는 이야기도 나왔는데, 이디스는 그것을 결혼 생활에 닥칠 수 있는 가장 무시무시한 곤란 가운데 하나로 여기는 눈치였다. 혼례 뒤 곧바로 이어질 스코틀랜드 방문에 어떤 드레스가 필요할지도 이야기했다. 그러나 소곤거리던 말소리는 어느새 나른해졌고, 마거릿은 몇 분쯤 말이 끊긴 뒤에야 알아차렸다. 옆방의 왁자한 소리에도 아랑곳없이 이디스가 모슬린과 리본과 비단결 곱슬머리로 이루어진 보드라운 공처럼 몸을 말고는 저녁 식사 뒤의 평화로운 낮잠에 빠져들었음을."]], [["Margaret had been on the point of telling her cousin of some of the plans and visions which she entertained as to her future life in the country parsonage, where her father and mother lived; and where her bright holidays had always been passed, though for the last ten years her aunt Shaw’s house had been considered as her home.", "마거릿은 앞으로 아버지와 어머니가 사는 시골 목사관에서 살아갈 자신의 삶에 대해 품고 있던 계획과 공상을 사촌에게 털어놓으려던 참이었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 늘 화창한 방학을 보냈다."], ["But in default of a listener, she had to brood over the change in her life silently as heretofore.", "물론 지난 10년 동안은 쇼 이모의 집이 그녀의 집으로 여겨졌지만."], ["It was a happy brooding, although tinged with regret at being separated for an indefinite time from her gentle aunt and dear cousin.", "그러나 들어 줄 사람이 없으니 그녀는 여느 때처럼 자기 삶의 변화를 홀로 곱씹을 수밖에 없었다."], ["As she thought of the delight of filling the important post of only daughter in Helstone parsonage, pieces of the conversation out of the next room came upon her ears.", "다정한 이모와 사랑스러운 사촌과 기약 없이 헤어진다는 아쉬움이 배어 있기는 해도 그것은 행복한 되새김이었다. 헬스톤 목사관에서 외동딸이라는 중요한 자리를 채우는 기쁨을 떠올리는 사이, 옆방에서 오가는 대화의 조각들이 그녀의 귀에 들어왔다."], ["Her aunt Shaw was talking to the five or six ladies who had been dining there, and whose husbands were still in the dining-room.", "쇼 이모는 그날 저녁 식사에 왔던 대여섯 명의 부인들과 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 그 남편들은 아직 식당에 남아 있었다."], ["They were the familiar acquaintances of the house; neighbours whom Mrs.", "그들은 이 집에 낯익은 지인들이었다."], ["Shaw called friends, because she happened to dine with them more frequently than with any other people, and because if she or Edith wanted anything from them, or they from her, they did not scruple to make a call at each other’s houses before luncheon.", "쇼 부인이 친구라고 부르는 이웃들이었는데, 다른 어떤 이들보다 그들과 자주 식사를 함께했기 때문이고, 또 그녀나 이디스가 그들에게서 무엇인가 필요하거나 그들이 그녀에게서 무엇인가 필요할 때 점심 전에 서로의 집을 스스럼없이 찾아가는 사이였기 때문이다."], ["These ladies and their husbands were invited in their capacity of friends, to eat a farewell dinner in honour of Edith’s approaching marriage.", "이 부인들과 그 남편들은 친구 자격으로 초대받아 이디스의 다가오는 혼인을 기려 송별 만찬을 함께하는 것이었다. 이디스는 이 자리를 그리 달가워하지 않았다."], ["Edith had rather objected to this arrangement, for Captain Lennox was expected to arrive by a late train this very evening; but, although she was a spoiled child, she was too careless and idle to have a very strong will of her own, and gave way when she found that her mother had absolutely ordered those extra delicacies of the season which are always supposed to be efficacious against immoderate grief at farewell dinners.", "바로 그날 저녁 늦은 기차로 레녹스 대위가 도착할 예정이었기 때문이다. 그러나 응석받이로 자라기는 했어도 그녀는 너무 무심하고 게을러 자기 뜻을 강하게 내세우지 못했고, 어머니가 송별 만찬에서 지나친 슬픔을 막는 데 늘 효험이 있다고들 하는 제철의 별미들을 이미 단단히 일러 두었다는 것을 알고는 물러서고 말았다."], ["She contented herself by leaning back in her chair, merely playing with the food on her plate, and looking grave and absent; while all around her were enjoying the mots of Mr.", "그녀는 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 접시의 음식을 깨작거리며 침울하고 넋 나간 표정을 짓는 것으로 만족했다. 그러는 동안 주위 사람들은 그레이 씨의 재담을 즐기고 있었다."], ["Grey, the gentleman who always took the bottom of the table at Mrs. Shaw’s dinner parties, and asked Edith to give them some music in the drawing-room.", "쇼 부인의 만찬에서 늘 식탁 맨 아랫자리를 차지하는 그 신사는 이디스에게 응접실에서 음악을 들려 달라고 청했다."], ["Mr. Grey was particularly agreeable over this farewell dinner, and the gentlemen staid downstairs longer than usual.", "그레이 씨는 이 송별 만찬에서 유난히 유쾌했고, 신사들은 여느 때보다 오래 아래층에 머물렀다."], ["It was very well they did—to judge from the fragments of conversation which Margaret overheard.", "마거릿이 어쩌다 엿들은 대화의 조각들로 미루어 보건대, 그들이 그러기를 잘한 셈이었다."]], [["“I suffered too much myself; not that I was not extremely happy with the poor dear General, but still disparity of age is a drawback; one that I was resolved Edith should not have to encounter.", "“나야 너무 많이 겪었지요. 가엾은 장군님과 더없이 행복하지 않았다는 말은 아니지만, 그래도 나이 차이는 흠이에요."], ["Of course, without any maternal partiality, I foresaw that the dear child was likely to marry early; indeed, I had often said that I was sure she would be married before she was nineteen.", "이디스만은 그런 일을 겪게 하지 않으리라 마음먹었답니다. 물론 어미의 치우친 마음을 빼고 보아도, 그 애가 일찍 혼인하리라는 것쯤은 진작에 알았어요."], ["I had quite prophetic feeling when Captain Lennox”—and here the voice dropped into a whisper, but Margaret could easily supply the blank.", "정말이지 열아홉이 되기 전에 시집갈 게 틀림없다고 여러 번 말하곤 했지요. 레녹스 대위가……”"], ["The course of true love in Edith’s case had run remarkably smooth. Mrs.", "여기서 목소리가 속삭임으로 잦아들었지만, 마거릿은 그 빈자리를 어렵지 않게 메울 수 있었다."], ["Shaw had given way to the presentiment, as she expressed it; and had rather urged on the marriage, although it was below the expectations which many of Edith’s acquaintances had formed for her, a young and pretty heiress.", "이디스의 경우 진정한 사랑의 길은 놀랄 만큼 순탄하게 흘러왔다. 쇼 부인은 스스로 표현한 대로 그 예감에 순순히 따랐고, 오히려 혼사를 서두른 편이었다."], ["But Mrs.", "젊고 예쁜 상속녀인 이디스에게 지인들이 걸고 있던 기대에는 못 미치는 혼처였는데도 말이다."], ["Shaw said that her only child should marry for love,—and sighed emphatically, as if love had not been her motive for marrying the General.", "그러나 쇼 부인은 하나뿐인 자식은 사랑으로 시집가야 한다고 말하고는 힘주어 한숨을 내쉬었다. 마치 사랑이 자신이 장군과 혼인한 이유가 아니었다는 듯이."], ["Mrs. Shaw enjoyed the romance of the present engagement rather more than her daughter.", "쇼 부인은 지금의 이 약혼이 지닌 낭만을 딸보다 더 즐겼다."], ["Not but that Edith was very thoroughly and properly in love; still she would certainly have preferred a good house in Belgravia, to all the picturesqueness of the life which Captain Lennox described at Corfu.", "이디스가 제대로 깊이 사랑에 빠지지 않았다는 말은 아니다. 그래도 그녀라면 레녹스 대위가 그려 보이는 코르푸 생활의 온갖 그림 같은 정취보다는 벨그레이비어의 번듯한 저택을 더 좋아했을 것이다."], ["The very parts which made Margaret glow as she listened, Edith pretended to shiver and shudder at; partly for the pleasure she had in being coaxed out of her dislike by her fond lover, and partly because anything of a gipsy or make-shift life was really distasteful to her.", "마거릿을 듣는 내내 달아오르게 만든 바로 그 대목에서 이디스는 몸서리치고 진저리 치는 시늉을 했다. 얼마쯤은 애틋한 연인이 그 싫증을 어르고 달래 주는 즐거움 때문이었고, 얼마쯤은 집시 살림이나 임시변통 같은 것이 정말로 그녀의 취향에 맞지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Yet had any one come with a fine house, and a fine estate, and a fine title to boot, Edith would still have clung to Captain Lennox while the temptation lasted; when it was over, it is possible she might have had little qualms of ill-concealed regret that Captain Lennox could not have united in his person everything that was desirable.", "그러나 누군가 번듯한 저택과 번듯한 영지에 번듯한 작위까지 지니고 나타났더라도, 그 유혹이 이어지는 동안 이디스는 여전히 레녹스 대위에게 매달렸을 것이다. 다만 그 유혹이 지나가고 나면, 레녹스 대위가 바랄 만한 모든 것을 한 몸에 지니지 못했다는 데 대해 잘 감추지 못한 아쉬움을 이따금 느꼈을지도 모른다."], ["In this she was but her mother’s child; who, after deliberately marrying General Shaw with no warmer feeling than respect for his character and establishment, was constantly, though quietly, bemoaning her hard lot in being united to one whom she could not love.", "이 점에서 그녀는 어머니의 딸일 뿐이었다. 어머니 역시 인품과 재산에 대한 존경 말고는 그보다 따뜻한 감정 없이 신중하게 쇼 장군과 혼인해 놓고는, 사랑할 수 없는 사람과 맺어진 자신의 모진 팔자를 늘 조용히 한탄해 왔던 것이다."]], [["“I have spared no expense in her trousseau,”", "“혼수에는 아까울 것이 없었어요.”"], ["were the next words Margaret heard.", "다음으로 마거릿의 귀에 들어온 말은 그것이었다."], ["“She has all the beautiful Indian shawls and scarfs the General gave to me, but which I shall never wear again.”", "“장군님이 내게 주셨지만 내가 다시는 두르지 않을 그 아름다운 인도 숄과 스카프를 다 그 애에게 주었답니다.”"]], [["“She is a lucky girl,”", "“복 받은 아가씨네요.”"], ["replied another voice, which Margaret knew to be that of Mrs.", "또 다른 목소리가 대꾸했다."], ["Gibson, a lady who was taking a double interest in the conversation, from the fact of one of her daughters having been married within the last few weeks.", "마거릿은 그것이 깁슨 부인의 목소리임을 알아차렸다. 몇 주 전에 딸 하나를 시집보낸 터라 이 대화에 갑절로 관심이 있는 부인이었다."], ["“Helen had set her heart upon an Indian shawl, but really when I found what an extravagant price was asked, I was obliged to refuse her.", "“헬렌도 인도 숄을 몹시 갖고 싶어 했지만, 값을 알아보니 어찌나 터무니없던지 거절할 수밖에 없었어요."], ["She will be quite envious when she hears of Edith having Indian shawls.", "이디스가 인도 숄을 가졌다는 말을 들으면 아주 부러워하겠네요. 어떤 것인가요?"], ["What kind are they?", "델리 것인가요?"], ["Delhi? with the lovely little borders?”", "그 곱다란 자잘한 테두리가 있는?”"]], [["Margaret heard her aunt’s voice again, but this time it was as if she had raised herself up from her half-recumbent position, and were looking into the more dimly lighted back drawing-room.", "마거릿은 이모의 목소리를 다시 들었다. 그런데 이번에는 반쯤 누워 있던 자세에서 몸을 일으켜 어스름한 안쪽 응접실을 들여다보는 듯한 목소리였다."], ["“Edith!", "“이디스!"], ["Edith!”", "이디스!”"], ["cried she; and then she sank as if wearied by the exertion.", "이모가 소리쳐 부르고는, 그 수고에 지친 듯 도로 몸을 묻었다."], ["Margaret stepped forward.", "마거릿이 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“Edith is asleep, Aunt Shaw.", "“이디스는 자고 있어요, 쇼 이모."], ["Is it anything I can do?”", "제가 할 일이 있나요?”"]], [["All the ladies said “poor child!”", "이디스에 관한 이 딱한 소식을 듣고 부인들은 모두 “가엾어라!”"], ["on receiving this distressing intelligence about Edith; and the minute lap-dog in Mrs. Shaw’s arms began to bark, as if excited by the burst of pity.", "하고 말했고, 쇼 부인의 팔에 안긴 자그마한 애완견이 그 갑작스러운 동정에 흥이 났는지 짖어 대기 시작했다."]], [["“Hush, Tiny!", "“쉿, 타이니!"], ["you naughty little girl!", "이 못된 것!"], ["you will waken your mistress.", "네 주인을 깨우겠구나."], ["It was only to ask Edith if she would tell Newton to bring down her shawls; perhaps you would go, Margaret dear?”", "이디스한테 뉴턴을 시켜 숄을 내려오게 하라고 이르려던 것뿐이란다. 마거릿, 네가 좀 가 주겠니?”"]], [["Margaret went up into the old nursery at the very top of the house, where Newton was busy getting up some laces which were required for the wedding.", "마거릿은 집 꼭대기에 있는 옛 아이방으로 올라갔다. 그곳에서는 뉴턴이 혼례에 쓸 레이스를 손질하느라 분주했다."], ["While Newton went (not without a muttered grumbling) to undo the shawls, which had already been exhibited four or five times that day, Margaret looked round upon the nursery; the first room in that house with which she had become familiar nine years ago, when she was brought, all untamed from the forest, to share the home, the play, and the lessons of her cousin Edith.", "뉴턴이 그날만 벌써 네댓 번은 펼쳐 보인 숄들을 다시 풀러 가는 동안, 마거릿은 아이방을 둘러보았다. 아홉 해 전 숲에서 갓 잡혀 온 야생의 아이처럼 이 집에 와서 사촌 이디스와 살림도 놀이도 공부도 함께하게 되었을 때, 그녀가 처음으로 낯익어진 방이 바로 여기였다."], ["She remembered the dark, dim look of the London nursery, presided over by an austere and ceremonious nurse, who was terribly particular about clean hands and torn frocks.", "그녀는 그 어둡고 침침하던 런던의 아이방을, 깨끗한 손과 찢어진 원피스에 무섭도록 까다롭던 엄격하고 격식 차린 유모의 다스림을 떠올렸다. 위층에서 처음 마신 차도 생각났다."], ["She recollected the first tea up there—separate from her father and aunt, who were dining somewhere down below, an infinite depth of stairs; for unless she were up in the sky (the child thought), they must be deep down in the bowels of the earth.", "아버지와 이모와 따로 마신 차였다. 두 사람은 저 아래 어딘가에서, 끝없이 깊은 계단 아래에서 식사를 하고 있었다."], ["At home—before she came to live in Harley Street—her mother’s dressing-room had been her nursery; and as they kept early hours in the country parsonage, Margaret had always had her meals with her father and mother.", "자기가 하늘 위에 있는 것이 아니라면 그들은 땅속 깊은 곳에 있는 것이 틀림없다고 아이는 생각했다. 할리 가에 와서 살기 전 집에서는 어머니의 화장방이 그녀의 아이방이었다."], ["Oh!", "시골 목사관에서는 이른 시간에 일과를 마쳤으므로 마거릿은 늘 아버지 어머니와 함께 밥을 먹었다. 아, 열여덟 살의 훤칠하고 의젓한 처녀는 아홉 살짜리 어린것이 그 첫날 밤 이불 속에 얼굴을 파묻고 그토록 사납게 슬퍼하며 흘리던 눈물을 또렷이 기억했다."], ["well did the tall stately girl of eighteen remember the tears shed with such wild passion of grief by the little girl of nine, as she hid her face under the bed-clothes in that first night; and how she was bidden not to cry by the nurse, because it would disturb Miss Edith; and how she had cried as bitterly, but more quietly, till her newly-seen, grand, pretty aunt had come softly upstairs with Mr.", "유모는 이디스 아가씨가 깨니 울지 말라고 했고, 아이는 그래도 여전히 서럽게, 다만 더 조용히 울었다. 그러다 처음 본 크고 어여쁜 이모가 헤일 씨를 데리고 잠든 어린 딸을 보여 주려고 살며시 위층으로 올라왔다."], ["Hale to show him his little sleeping daughter.", "그러자 어린 마거릿은 흐느낌을 삼키고 잠든 척 가만히 누워 있으려 애썼다. 자기 슬픔으로 아버지를 마음 아프게 할까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["Then the little Margaret had hushed her sobs, and tried to lie quiet as if asleep, for fear of making her father unhappy by her grief, which she dared not express before her aunt, and which she rather thought it was wrong to feel at all after the long hoping, and planning, and contriving they had gone through at home, before her wardrobe could be arranged to suit her grander circumstances, and before papa could leave his parish to come up to London, even for a few days.", "그 슬픔은 이모 앞에서는 감히 드러낼 수 없었고, 게다가 그 슬픔을 느끼는 것 자체가 잘못이라는 생각마저 들었다. 집에서 그토록 오래 바라고 궁리하고 애쓴 끝에야 그녀의 옷가지가 한결 번듯해진 처지에 걸맞게 갖추어졌고, 아버지가 며칠만이라도 교구를 떠나 런던에 올라올 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Now she had got to love the old nursery, though it was but a dismantled place; and she looked all round with a kind of cat-like regret, at the idea of leaving it for ever in three days.", "이제 그녀는 헐벗은 자리가 되어 버린 그 옛 아이방을 사랑하게 되어 있었고, 사흘 뒤면 이곳을 영영 떠난다는 생각에 고양이 같은 미련을 품고 사방을 둘러보았다."]], [["“Ah Newton!”", "“아, 뉴턴!”"], ["said she, “I think we shall all be sorry to leave this dear old room.”", "그녀가 말했다. “우리 모두 이 정든 방을 떠나기가 섭섭할 거예요.”"]], [["“Indeed, miss, I shan’t for one.", "“정말이지 아가씨, 저는 아니랍니다요."], ["My eyes are not so good as they were, and the light here is so bad that I can’t see to mend laces except just at the window, where there’s always a shocking draught—enough to give one one’s death of cold.”", "눈이 예전 같지 않은 데다 여기는 빛이 어찌나 나쁜지, 창가가 아니면 레이스를 깁는 게 보이지가 않아요. 그런데 창가는 늘 지독한 웃풍이라 고뿔로 죽어도 이상하지 않을 정도지요.”"]], [["“Well, I dare say you will have both good light and plenty of warmth at Naples.", "“그렇겠네요. 나폴리에 가면 좋은 빛도 넉넉한 온기도 다 있을 거예요."], ["You must keep as much of your darning as you can till then.", "기울 것들은 되도록 그때까지 모아 두세요. 고마워요, 뉴턴."], ["Thank you, Newton, I can take them down—you’re busy.”", "내가 들고 내려갈게요. 바쁘시잖아요.”"]], [["So Margaret went down laden with shawls, and snuffing up their spicy Eastern smell.", "그리하여 마거릿은 숄을 잔뜩 안고 내려가며 그 향긋한 동방의 냄새를 들이마셨다."], ["Her aunt asked her to stand as a sort of lay figure on which to display them, as Edith was still asleep.", "이디스가 아직 잠들어 있으니 이모는 그녀에게 숄을 펼쳐 보일 인형 노릇을 해 달라고 청했다."], ["No one thought about it; but Margaret’s tall, finely made figure, in the black silk dress which she was wearing as mourning for some distant relative of her father’s, set off the long beautiful folds of the gorgeous shawls that would have half-smothered Edith.", "아무도 마음에 두지 않았지만, 아버지 쪽 먼 친척의 상중이라 입고 있던 검은 비단 드레스 차림의 훤칠하고 반듯한 마거릿의 몸매는 이디스라면 반쯤 파묻혔을 그 화려한 숄의 길고 아름다운 주름을 한껏 살려 주었다."], ["Margaret stood right under the chandelier, quite silent and passive, while her aunt adjusted the draperies.", "마거릿은 샹들리에 바로 아래에 아무 말 없이 가만히 서 있었고, 이모는 그 늘어진 천을 매만졌다."], ["Occasionally, as she was turned round, she caught a glimpse of herself in the mirror over the chimney-piece, and smiled at her own appearance there—the familiar features in the usual garb of a princess.", "이따금 몸을 돌릴 때면 벽난로 위 거울에 비친 자신의 모습이 눈에 들어왔고, 그녀는 그 모습에 미소를 지었다. 낯익은 이목구비가 공주의 옷차림을 하고 있었다."], ["She touched the shawls gently as they hung around her, and took a pleasure in their soft feel and their brilliant colours, and rather liked to be dressed in such splendour—enjoying it much as a child would do, with a quiet pleased smile on her lips.", "그녀는 몸에 두른 숄들을 가만히 만져 보며 그 부드러운 감촉과 눈부신 빛깔을 즐겼고, 그토록 화려하게 차려입는 것이 제법 마음에 들었다. 아이가 그러하듯 입가에 조용하고 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채로."], ["Just then the door opened, and Mr. Henry Lennox was suddenly announced.", "바로 그때 문이 열리더니 헨리 레녹스 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 불쑥 들려왔다."], ["Some of the ladies started back, as if half-ashamed of their feminine interest in dress. Mrs.", "부인들 몇몇은 옷치레에 쏠린 여자다운 관심이 반쯤 부끄럽다는 듯 뒤로 물러났다."], ["Shaw held out her hand to the new-comer; Margaret stood perfectly still, thinking she might be yet wanted as a sort of block for the shawls; but looking at Mr.", "쇼 부인은 새로 온 이에게 손을 내밀었다. 마거릿은 아직 숄걸이로 쓰일지도 모른다는 생각에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["Lennox with a bright, amused face, as if sure of his sympathy in her sense of the ludicrousness at being thus surprised.", "다만 이렇게 들킨 우스꽝스러움을 그가 함께 웃어 주리라 믿는 듯 환하고 재미있어하는 얼굴로 레녹스 씨를 바라보았다."]], [["Her aunt was so much absorbed in asking Mr.", "이모는 만찬에 오지 못했던 헨리 레녹스 씨에게 신랑인 그의 형에 대해, 신부 들러리인 그의 누이에 대해, 그 밖의 레녹스 집안사람들에 대해 온갖 것을 묻느라 정신이 없었다."], ["Henry Lennox—who had not been able to come to dinner—all sorts of questions about his brother the bridegroom, his sister the bridesmaid (coming with the Captain from Scotland for the occasion), and various other members of the Lennox family, that Margaret saw she was no more wanted as shawl-bearer, and devoted herself to the amusement of the other visitors, whom her aunt had for the moment forgotten.", "신부 들러리는 이번 일을 위해 대위와 함께 스코틀랜드에서 오는 길이었다. 마거릿은 자기가 더는 숄걸이로 필요하지 않다는 것을 알고, 이모가 잠시 잊고 있던 다른 손님들을 즐겁게 하는 일에 매달렸다."], ["Almost immediately, Edith came in from the back drawing-room, winking and blinking her eyes at the stronger light, shaking back her slightly-ruffled curls, and altogether looking like the Sleeping Beauty just startled from her dreams.", "곧바로 이디스가 안쪽 응접실에서 나왔다. 밝아진 빛에 눈을 껌뻑이며 살짝 헝클어진 곱슬머리를 뒤로 넘기는 그 모습이 영락없이 꿈에서 갓 깨어난 잠자는 숲속의 공주 같았다."], ["Even in her slumber she had instinctively felt that a Lennox was worth rousing herself for; and she had a multitude of questions to ask about dear Janet, the future, unseen sister-in-law, for whom she professed so much affection, that if Margaret had not been very proud she might have almost felt jealous of the mushroom rival.", "잠결에도 그녀는 레녹스라는 사람이라면 몸을 일으킬 만하다는 것을 본능적으로 알아차렸던 것이다. 그녀에게는 아직 만나 보지 못한 미래의 시누이 재닛에 대해 물을 것이 산더미였다."], ["As Margaret sank rather more into the background on her aunt’s joining the conversation, she saw Henry Lennox directing his look towards a vacant seat near her; and she knew perfectly well that as soon as Edith released him from her questioning, he would take possession of that chair.", "그 애정을 어찌나 드러내 놓고 표하던지, 마거릿이 자존심이 세지 않았더라면 하루아침에 나타난 그 경쟁자에게 질투를 느꼈을지도 몰랐다. 이모가 대화에 끼어들자 마거릿이 다소 뒤로 물러나는데, 헨리 레녹스가 그녀 곁의 빈자리로 눈길을 보내는 것이 보였다."], ["She had not been quite sure, from her aunt’s rather confused account of his engagements, whether he would come that night; it was almost a surprise to see him; and now she was sure of a pleasant evening.", "이디스가 그를 질문에서 풀어 주는 즉시 그가 그 의자를 차지하리라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 이모가 그의 일정을 다소 헷갈리게 전한 탓에 그가 그날 밤 올지 확신하지 못하던 참이라 그를 보게 된 것이 거의 뜻밖이었다."], ["He liked and disliked pretty nearly the same things that she did.", "이제 즐거운 저녁이 되리라는 것이 분명했다. 그가 좋아하고 싫어하는 것은 그녀의 그것과 거의 같았다."], ["Margaret’s face was lightened up into an honest, open brightness.", "마거릿의 얼굴이 솔직하고 툭 트인 환함으로 밝아졌다."], ["By-and-by he came.", "이윽고 그가 다가왔다."], ["She received him with a smile which had not a tinge of shyness or self-consciousness in it.", "그녀는 수줍음이나 자의식이라고는 티끌만큼도 없는 미소로 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Well, I suppose you are all in the depth of business—ladies’ business, I mean.", "“그래, 다들 한창 일에 파묻혀 계신 모양이군요. 부인네들 일 말입니다."], ["Very different to my business, which is the real true law business.", "제 일과는 아주 다르지요. 제 것은 진짜 법률 일이니까요."], ["Playing with shawls is very different work to drawing up settlements.”", "숄을 가지고 노는 것은 재산 계약서를 작성하는 일과는 영 다른 일이고요.”"]], [["“Ah, I knew how you would be amused to find us all so occupied in admiring finery.", "“아, 우리가 이렇게 다 같이 고운 것에 홀려 있는 걸 보시면 재미있어하실 줄 알았어요."], ["But really Indian shawls are very perfect things of their kind.”", "하지만 인도 숄은 정말이지 그 종류로는 더할 나위 없는 물건인걸요.”"]], [["“I have no doubt they are.", "“그렇겠지요."], ["Their prices are very perfect, too.", "값도 더할 나위 없고요."], ["Nothing wanting.”", "모자란 데가 없습니다.”"]], [["The gentlemen came dropping in one by one, and the buzz and noise deepened in tone.", "신사들이 하나둘 들어오면서 웅성거림과 소란이 한층 짙어졌다."]], [["“This is your last dinner-party, is it not?", "“이번이 마지막 만찬이지요?"], ["There are no more before Thursday?”", "목요일 전에는 더 없고요?”"]], [["“No.", "“네. 오늘 저녁이 지나면 우리도 좀 쉴 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["I think after this evening we shall feel at rest, which I am sure I have not done for many weeks; at least, that kind of rest when the hands have nothing more to do, and all the arrangements are complete for an event which must occupy one’s head and heart.", "저는 몇 주째 그러지 못했거든요. 적어도 손이 더 할 일이 없어지고, 머리와 마음을 온통 차지할 수밖에 없는 일에 대한 준비가 모두 끝났을 때 찾아오는 그런 쉼 말이에요."], ["I shall be glad to have time to think, and I am sure Edith will.”", "생각할 시간이 생기면 기쁘겠어요. 이디스도 그럴 거예요.”"]], [["“I am not so sure about her, but I can fancy that you will.", "“이디스는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 헤일 양은 그러시리라 짐작이 갑니다."], ["Whenever I have seen you lately, you have been carried away by a whirlwind of some other person’s making.”", "요즘 뵐 때마다 남이 일으킨 회오리에 휩쓸려 계시더군요.”"]], [["“Yes,” said Margaret, rather sadly, remembering the never-ending commotion about trifles that had been going on for more than a month past: “I wonder if a marriage must always be preceded by what you call a whirlwind, or whether in some cases there might not rather be a calm and peaceful time just before it.”", "“그렇죠.” 마거릿이 다소 서글프게 말했다. 한 달 넘게 이어져 온 사소한 일들의 끝없는 소동을 떠올린 것이다. “혼인 앞에는 늘 그런 회오리라는 게 있어야 하는 걸까요? 아니면 어떤 경우에는 바로 앞에 잔잔하고 평화로운 시간이 있을 수도 있는 걸까요?”"]], [["“Cinderella’s godmother ordering the trousseau, the wedding-breakfast, writing the notes of invitation, for instance,”", "“이를테면 신데렐라의 요정 대모가 혼수를 마련해 주고, 혼례 피로연을 준비하고, 청첩장을 써 준다면 말이지요.”"], ["said Mr. Lennox, laughing.", "레녹스 씨가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“But are all these quite necessary troubles?”", "“하지만 이 모든 게 정말 꼭 필요한 수고인가요?”"], ["asked Margaret, looking up straight at him for an answer.", "마거릿이 대답을 구하듯 그를 똑바로 올려다보며 물었다."], ["A sense of indescribable weariness of all the arrangements for a pretty effect, in which Edith had been busied as supreme authority for the last six weeks, oppressed her just now; and she really wanted some one to help her to a few pleasant, quiet ideas connected with a marriage.", "지난 여섯 주 동안 이디스가 최고 권위자로 매달려 온, 그럴싸한 겉모양을 위한 온갖 준비에 대한 말로 다 할 수 없는 피로가 바로 그때 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그녀는 정말이지 누군가가 혼인에 얽힌 유쾌하고 조용한 생각 몇 가지를 찾도록 도와주기를 바랐다."]], [["“Oh, of course,”", "“아, 물론입니다.”"], ["he replied with a change to gravity in his tone.", "그가 목소리를 진지하게 바꾸어 대답했다. “거쳐야 할 형식과 예식이 있지요."], ["“There are forms and ceremonies to be gone through, not so much to satisfy oneself, as to stop the world’s mouth, without which stoppage there would be very little satisfaction in life.", "자기 자신을 만족시키기 위해서라기보다 세상의 입을 막기 위해서 말입니다. 그 입을 막지 못하면 삶에 만족이란 게 거의 남지 않으니까요."], ["But how would you have a wedding arranged?”", "그런데 헤일 양이라면 혼례를 어떻게 치르시겠습니까?”"]], [["“Oh, I have never thought much about it; only I should like it to be a very fine summer morning; and I should like to walk to church through the shade of trees; and not to have so many bridesmaids, and to have no wedding-breakfast.", "“아, 깊이 생각해 본 적은 없어요. 다만 아주 좋은 여름날 아침이었으면 좋겠고, 나무 그늘을 지나 걸어서 교회에 갔으면 좋겠어요."], ["I dare say I am resolving against the very things that have given me the most trouble just now.”", "들러리는 그리 많지 않았으면 하고, 피로연은 없었으면 해요. 지금 저를 가장 힘들게 하는 바로 그것들을 안 하겠다고 마음먹고 있는 셈이네요.”"]], [["“No, I don’t think you are.", "“아니요, 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다."], ["The idea of stately simplicity accords well with your character.”", "의젓한 간소함이라는 발상은 헤일 양의 성품과 잘 어울리니까요.”"]], [["Margaret did not quite like this speech; she winced away from it more, from remembering former occasions on which he had tried to lead her into a discussion (in which he took the complimentary part) about her own character and ways of going on.", "마거릿은 이 말이 썩 마음에 들지 않았다. 그가 전에도 그녀 자신의 성품과 처신에 대한 이야기로 그녀를 끌어들이려 했던 일들이 떠올라 더욱 움츠러들었다."], ["She cut his speech rather short by saying:", "그때마다 칭찬하는 쪽은 그의 몫이었다. 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 그의 말을 다소 짧게 끊었다."]], [["“It is natural for me to think of Helstone church, and the walk to it, rather than of driving up to a London church in the middle of a paved street.”", "“저로서는 포장된 거리 한복판의 런던 교회에 마차를 타고 가는 것보다 헬스톤 교회와 그리로 가는 길을 떠올리는 게 자연스러워요.”"]], [["“Tell me about Helstone.", "“헬스톤 이야기를 해 주세요."], ["You have never described it to me.", "한 번도 제게 그려 보여 주신 적이 없잖습니까."], ["I should like to have some idea of the place you will be living in, when ninety-six Harley Street will be looking dingy and dirty, and dull, and shut up.", "할리 가 96번지가 침침하고 지저분하고 따분하게 닫혀 있을 동안 헤일 양이 살아갈 그곳이 어떤 데인지 좀 알고 싶습니다."], ["Is Helstone a village, or a town, in the first place?”", "우선, 헬스톤은 마을입니까, 읍입니까?”"]], [["“Oh, only a hamlet; I don’t think I could call it a village at all. There is the church and a few houses near it on the green—cottages, rather—with roses growing all over them.”", "“아, 그저 작은 촌락이에요. 마을이라고 부를 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 교회가 있고, 잔디밭 가에 집이 몇 채 있지요. 오두막이라고 하는 편이 낫겠네요. 온통 장미가 뒤덮인 오두막들이요.”"]], [["“And flowering all the year round, especially at Christmas—make your picture complete,”", "“그리고 일 년 내내, 특히 성탄절에도 꽃이 핀다고 하면 그림이 완성되겠군요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니에요.”"], ["replied Margaret, somewhat annoyed, “I am not making a picture.", "마거릿이 다소 언짢아하며 대꾸했다. “저는 그림을 그리는 게 아니에요."], ["I am trying to describe Helstone as it really is.", "헬스톤을 있는 그대로 이야기하려는 거예요."], ["You should not have said that.”", "그런 말씀은 하지 말았어야 해요.”"]], [["“I am penitent,”", "“뉘우치겠습니다.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Only it really sounded like a village in a tale rather than in real life.”", "“다만 실제 삶 속의 마을이라기보다 이야기 속의 마을처럼 들렸을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“And so it is,”", "“정말 그런걸요.”"], ["replied Margaret, eagerly.", "마거릿이 열을 내며 대답했다."], ["“All the other places in England that I have seen seem so hard and prosaic-looking, after the New Forest.", "“뉴포리스트를 보고 나면 제가 본 잉글랜드의 다른 곳은 다 딱딱하고 멋대가리 없어 보여요."], ["Helstone is like a village in a poem—in one of Tennyson’s poems.", "헬스톤은 시 속의 마을 같아요. 테니슨의 시 속 같은 마을이요."], ["But I won’t try and describe it any more.", "하지만 더는 그려 보이지 않겠어요."], ["You would only laugh at me if I told you what I think of it—what it really is.”", "제가 그곳을 어떻게 생각하는지, 그곳이 정말 어떤 곳인지 말씀드리면 그저 저를 비웃으실 테니까요.”"]], [["“Indeed, I would not.", "“정말이지 그러지 않을 겁니다."], ["But I see you are going to be very resolved.", "하지만 아주 단단히 마음을 정하신 모양이군요."], ["Well, then, tell me that which I should like still better to know: what the parsonage is like.”", "그러면 제가 더 알고 싶은 것을 말씀해 주십시오. 그 목사관은 어떤 곳입니까?”"]], [["“Oh, I can’t describe my home.", "“아, 우리 집은 그려 보일 수가 없어요."], ["It is home, and I can’t put its charm into words.”", "그건 집이고, 저는 그 매력을 말로 옮길 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“I submit.", "“졌습니다."], ["You are rather severe to-night, Margaret.”", "오늘 밤은 꽤 매정하시군요, 마거릿 양.”"]], [["“How?”", "“어째서요?”"], ["said she, turning her large soft eyes round full upon him.", "그녀가 크고 부드러운 눈을 돌려 그를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I did not know I was.”", "“저는 그런 줄 몰랐는데요.”"]], [["“Why, because I made an unlucky remark, you will neither tell me what Helstone is like, nor will you say anything about your home, though I have told you how much I want to hear about both, the latter especially.”", "“제가 실없는 말을 한 번 했다고 헬스톤이 어떤 곳인지도 말씀해 주지 않고, 댁에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않으시니까요. 제가 그 두 가지를, 특히 뒤엣것을 얼마나 듣고 싶어 하는지 말씀드렸는데도 말입니다.”"]], [["“But indeed I cannot tell you about my own home.", "“하지만 정말이지 제 집에 대해서는 말씀드릴 수가 없어요."], ["I don’t quite think it is a thing to be talked about, unless you knew it.”", "직접 알지 못하는 분께 이야기할 만한 것이 아니라는 생각이 들어서요.”"]], [["“Well, then”—pausing for a moment—“tell me what you do there.", "“그러면……” 그가 잠시 뜸을 들였다."], ["Here you read, or have lessons, or otherwise improve your mind, till the middle of the day; take a walk before lunch, go a drive with your aunt after, and have some kind of engagement in the evening.", "“거기서 무엇을 하며 지내실지 말씀해 주십시오. 여기서는 한낮까지 책을 읽거나 수업을 받거나 어떻게든 머리를 갈고닦으시지요."], ["There, now fill up your day at Helstone.", "점심 전에 산책을 하고, 그 뒤에는 이모님과 마차를 타고 나가고, 저녁에는 무슨 약속이 있고요. 자, 이제 헬스톤에서의 하루를 채워 보십시오."], ["Shall you ride, drive, or walk?”", "말을 타시겠습니까, 마차를 타시겠습니까, 걸으시겠습니까?”"]], [["“Walk, decidedly.", "“단연코 걷지요."], ["We have no horse, not even for papa.", "우리 집에는 말이 없어요. 아버지 몫도요."], ["He walks to the very extremity of his parish.", "아버지는 교구 끝까지 걸어 다니세요."], ["The walks are so beautiful, it would be a shame to drive—almost a shame to ride.”", "길이 어찌나 아름다운지 마차를 타는 건 아까울 지경이에요. 말을 타는 것도 거의 아까울 정도고요.”"]], [["“Shall you garden much?", "“밭일은 많이 하십니까?"], ["That, I believe, is a proper employment for young ladies in the country.”", "그건 시골 아가씨에게 어울리는 일거리라고 알고 있습니다만.”"]], [["“I don’t know.", "“글쎄요."], ["I am afraid I shan’t like such hard work.”", "저는 그렇게 고된 일은 좋아하지 못할 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Archery parties—picnics—race balls—hunt balls?”", "“활쏘기 모임이라든가, 소풍이라든가, 경마 무도회, 사냥 무도회는요?”"]], [["“Oh no!”", "“어머, 아니에요!”"], ["said she, laughing.", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Papa’s living is very small; and even if we were near such things, I doubt if I should go to them.”", "“아버지의 성직록은 아주 적어요. 게다가 그런 것들이 가까이 있다 해도 제가 갈 것 같지 않고요.”"]], [["“I see, you won’t tell me anything.", "“보아하니 아무것도 말씀해 주지 않으시는군요."], ["You will only tell me that you are not going to do this and that.", "이건 안 하고 저건 안 한다는 말씀만 하시니까요."], ["Before the vacation ends, I think I shall pay you a call, and see what you really do employ yourself in.”", "방학이 끝나기 전에 한번 찾아뵙고 대체 무엇을 하며 지내시는지 봐야겠습니다.”"]], [["“I hope you will.", "“그래 주시면 좋겠어요."], ["Then you will see for yourself how beautiful Helstone is.", "그러면 헬스톤이 얼마나 아름다운지 직접 보시게 될 테니까요."], ["Now I must go.", "이제 가 봐야겠어요."], ["Edith is sitting down to play, and I just know enough of music to turn over the leaves for her; and, besides, Aunt Shaw won’t like us to talk.”", "이디스가 연주하러 앉는데, 저는 악보를 넘겨 줄 만큼은 음악을 아니까요. 게다가 쇼 이모가 우리 둘이 이야기하는 걸 달가워하지 않으실 거예요.”"]], [["Edith played brilliantly.", "이디스는 눈부시게 연주했다."], ["In the middle of the piece the door half-opened, and Edith saw Captain Lennox hesitating whether to come in.", "곡 중간에 문이 반쯤 열리더니, 들어올까 말까 망설이는 레녹스 대위가 이디스의 눈에 들어왔다."], ["She threw down her music, and rushed out of the room, leaving Margaret standing confused and blushing to explain to the astonished guests what vision had shown itself to cause Edith’s sudden flight.", "그녀는 악보를 내던지고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔고, 마거릿은 어리둥절해하는 손님들에게 이디스의 갑작스러운 도피를 부른 것이 어떤 광경이었는지 설명하느라 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 얼굴을 붉힌 채 서 있었다."], ["Captain Lennox had come earlier than was expected; or was it really so late?", "레녹스 대위가 예정보다 일찍 온 것이었다. 아니면 정말로 그렇게 늦은 시각이었던가?"], ["They looked at their watches, were duly shocked, and took their leave.", "사람들은 시계를 들여다보고 마땅히 놀란 뒤 자리를 떴다."]], [["Then Edith came back, glowing with pleasure, half-shyly, half-proudly leading in her tall handsome Captain. His brother shook hands with him, and Mrs.", "이윽고 이디스가 기쁨으로 상기된 채, 반쯤은 수줍게 반쯤은 자랑스럽게 훤칠하고 잘생긴 대위를 이끌고 돌아왔다."], ["Shaw welcomed him in her gentle kindly way, which had always something plaintive in it, arising from the long habit of considering herself a victim to an uncongenial marriage.", "그의 동생이 그와 악수를 나누었고, 쇼 부인은 늘 어딘가 애처로운 기색이 배어 있는 그 부드럽고 다정한 태도로 그를 맞았다. 그 애처로움은 자신을 마음 맞지 않는 혼인의 희생자로 여겨 온 오랜 습관에서 나온 것이었다."], ["Now that, the General being gone, she had every good of life, with as few drawbacks as possible, she had been rather perplexed to find an anxiety, if not a sorrow.", "이제 장군이 세상을 떠나고 나서 그녀는 삶의 온갖 좋은 것을 누리며 흠이랄 것이 거의 없어졌으니, 슬픔까지는 아니더라도 걱정거리 하나를 찾아내기가 오히려 난감했다. 그러나 요즈음 그녀는 자신의 건강을 근심의 원천으로 삼기로 했다."], ["She had, however, of late settled upon her own health as a source of apprehension; she had a nervous little cough whenever she thought about it; and some complaisant doctor ordered her just what she desired—a winter in Italy.", "그 생각을 할 때마다 신경성 잔기침이 나왔고, 어느 상냥한 의사가 그녀가 바라던 바로 그것을 처방해 주었다. 이탈리아에서의 겨울 말이다."], ["Mrs. Shaw had as strong wishes as most people, but she never liked to do anything from the open and acknowledged motive of her own good will and pleasure; she preferred being compelled to gratify herself by some other person’s command or desire.", "쇼 부인은 여느 사람 못지않게 바라는 바가 뚜렷했지만, 자신의 선의와 즐거움이라는 드러난 동기로 무엇인가를 하는 것은 결코 좋아하지 않았다."], ["She really did persuade herself that she was submitting to some hard external necessity; and thus she was able to moan and complain in her soft manner, all the time she was in reality doing just what she liked.", "그녀는 다른 사람의 명령이나 바람에 떠밀려 자기 뜻을 채우는 쪽을 좋아했다. 그녀는 정말로 자신이 어떤 모진 외부의 필요에 굴복하고 있다고 스스로를 설득했고, 그리하여 실은 하고 싶은 대로 다 하면서도 그 내내 그 부드러운 태도로 신음하고 한탄할 수 있었다."]], [["It was in this way she began to speak of her own journey to Captain Lennox, who assented, as in duty bound, to all his future mother-in-law said, while his eyes sought Edith, who was busying herself in re-arranging the tea-table, and ordering up all sorts of good things in spite of his assurances that he had dined within the last two hours.", "그런 식으로 그녀는 레녹스 대위에게 자신의 여행 이야기를 꺼냈고, 대위는 도리에 맞게 미래의 장모가 하는 말마다 맞장구를 쳤다. 그러면서도 그의 눈은 이디스를 좇았다. 이디스는 두 시간 안에 식사를 마치고 왔다는 그의 말에도 아랑곳없이 찻상을 다시 차리고 온갖 좋은 것을 내오라 이르느라 분주했다."]], [["Mr. Henry Lennox stood leaning against the chimney-piece, amused with the family scene.", "헨리 레녹스 씨는 벽난로 선반에 몸을 기댄 채 이 집안 풍경을 재미있어하며 서 있었다."], ["He was close by his handsome brother; he was the plain one in a singularly good-looking family; but his face was intelligent, keen, and mobile; and now and then Margaret wondered what it was that he could be thinking about, while he kept silence, but was evidently observing, with an interest that was slightly sarcastic, all that Edith and she were doing.", "그는 잘생긴 형 바로 곁에 있었다. 유난히 인물 좋은 집안에서 그는 못난 축이었다."], ["The sarcastic feeling was called out by Mrs.", "그러나 그의 얼굴은 총명하고 예리하며 표정이 살아 있었다."], ["Shaw’s conversation with his brother; it was separate from the interest which was excited by what he saw.", "그가 잠자코 있으면서도 이디스와 자신이 하는 모든 일을 살짝 비꼬는 듯한 관심으로 지켜보는 것이 분명했으므로, 마거릿은 이따금 그가 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해졌다. 그 비꼬는 마음은 쇼 부인이 그의 형과 나누는 대화가 불러낸 것이었고, 그가 보고 있는 것에서 일어난 관심과는 별개였다."], ["He thought it a pretty sight to see the two cousins so busy in their little arrangements about the table.", "그는 두 사촌이 찻상을 두고 자잘하게 부산을 떠는 모습을 어여쁜 광경이라 여겼다. 이디스는 대부분을 손수 하겠다고 나섰다."], ["Edith chose to do most herself.", "자기가 군인의 아내 노릇을 얼마나 잘할 수 있는지 연인에게 보여 주는 것이 즐거운 기분이었던 것이다."], ["She was in a humour to enjoy showing her lover how well she could behave as a soldier’s wife.", "그녀는 주전자의 물이 식은 것을 알아내고는 부엌의 커다란 찻주전자를 올려 오라 일렀다. 그 결과라고는 문간에서 그것을 받아 들고 안으로 옮기려다 너무 무거워 감당하지 못한 것뿐이었다."], ["She found out that the water in the urn was cold, and ordered up the great kitchen tea-kettle; the only consequence of which was that when she met it at the door, and tried to carry it in, it was too heavy for her, and she came in pouting, with a black mark on her muslin gown, and a little round white hand indented by the handle, which she took to show to Captain Lennox, just like a hurt child, and, of course, the remedy was the same in both cases.", "그녀는 모슬린 드레스에 검은 자국을 묻히고 자그맣고 동그란 흰 손에 손잡이 자국이 팬 채 입을 삐죽이며 들어왔고, 다친 아이가 그러하듯 그것을 레녹스 대위에게 보여 주었다. 물론 처방도 아이의 경우와 다르지 않았다."], ["Margaret’s quickly-adjusted spirit-lamp was the most efficacious contrivance, though not so like the gypsy-encampment which Edith, in some of her moods, chose to consider the nearest resemblance to a barrack-life.", "마거릿이 재빨리 손본 알코올램프가 가장 쓸모 있는 방책이었다. 다만 이디스가 어떤 기분일 때 병영 생활에 가장 가깝다고 여기곤 하던 그 집시 야영지 같은 정취는 덜했지만."]], [["After this evening all was bustle till the wedding was over.", "이날 저녁 뒤로는 혼례가 끝날 때까지 온통 부산스러웠다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# 제2장"]], [["“By the soft green light in the woody glade, On the banks of moss where thy childhood played By the household tree, thro’ which thine eye First looked in love to the summer sky.”", "“숲속 빈터에 어리는 부드러운 초록빛으로, 네 어린 시절이 뛰놀던 이끼 둔덕으로, 네 눈이 처음으로 여름 하늘을 사랑스레 올려다보던 그 집 앞의 나무로.”"], ["MRS. HEMANS.", "— 헤먼스 부인."]], [["Margaret was once more in her morning dress, travelling quietly home with her father, who had come up to assist at the wedding.", "마거릿은 다시 평상복 차림이 되어, 혼례를 거들러 올라왔던 아버지와 함께 조용히 집으로 돌아가는 길이었다."], ["Her mother had been detained at home by a multitude of half-reasons, none of which anybody fully understood, except Mr.", "어머니는 어정쩡한 이유 여럿에 붙들려 집에 남아 있었는데, 그 가운데 어느 것도 온전히 알아들은 사람이 없었다. 다만 헤일 씨만은 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["Hale, who was perfectly aware that all his arguments in favour of a grey satin gown, which was midway between oldness and newness, had proved unavailing; and that, as he had not the money to equip his wife afresh, from top to toe, she would not show herself at her only sister’s only child’s wedding. If Mrs.", "낡지도 새롭지도 않은 어중간한 회색 새틴 드레스를 두둔하던 자신의 온갖 논변이 끝내 소용없었다는 것을, 그리고 아내를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 새로 차려입힐 돈이 없는 이상 아내는 하나뿐인 언니의 하나뿐인 자식의 혼례에 모습을 드러내지 않으리라는 것을."], ["Shaw had guessed at the real reason why Mrs. Hale did not accompany her husband, she would have showered down gowns upon her; but it was nearly twenty years since Mrs.", "헤일 부인이 남편을 따라나서지 않은 진짜 까닭을 쇼 부인이 짐작이라도 했더라면 드레스를 쏟아붓듯 보내 주었을 것이다."], ["Shaw had been the poor pretty Miss Beresford, and she had really forgotten all grievances except that of the unhappiness arising from disparity of age in married life, on which she could descant by the half-hour.", "그러나 쇼 부인이 가난하고 예쁘장한 베러스퍼드 양이던 시절로부터 어느덧 20년 가까이 흘렀고, 그녀는 부부 사이의 나이 차이에서 비롯되는 불행 말고는 온갖 서러움을 정말로 잊어버린 뒤였다."], ["Dearest Maria had married the man of her heart, only eight years older than herself, with the sweetest temper, and that blue black hair one so seldom sees.", "그 한 가지에 대해서라면 반 시간이고 늘어놓을 수 있었지만 말이다. 더없이 사랑스러운 마리아는 제 마음이 고른 남자와 혼인했다."], ["Mr. Hale was one of the most delightful preachers she had ever heard, and a perfect model of a parish priest.", "자기보다 8살 위일 뿐인 데다 더없이 순한 성품에, 좀처럼 보기 힘든 그 푸른빛 도는 검은 머리를 지닌 사람이었다."], ["Perhaps it was not quite a logical deduction from all these premises, but it was still Mrs.", "헤일 씨는 그녀가 들어 본 가장 흐뭇한 설교자 가운데 하나였고, 교구 목사의 완벽한 본보기였다."], ["Shaw’s characteristic conclusion, as she thought over her sister’s lot: “Married for love, what can dearest Maria have to wish for in this world?” Mrs.", "이 모든 전제에서 끌어낸 결론이 그리 논리적이지는 않았을지 모르나, 어쨌든 그것은 동생의 팔자를 곱씹을 때마다 나오는 쇼 부인 특유의 결론이었다."], ["Hale, if she spoke truth, might have answered with a ready-made list, “a silver-grey glacé silk, a white chip bonnet, oh!", "“사랑해서 혼인했는데, 우리 마리아가 이 세상에서 더 바랄 게 무엇이 있겠어요?” 헤일 부인이 참말을 했더라면 미리 마련해 둔 목록으로 대답할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["dozens of things for the wedding, and hundreds of things for the house.”", "“은회색 글라세 비단 한 벌, 흰 밀짚 보닛 하나, 아, 혼례에 쓸 물건 수십 가지에, 살림에 쓸 물건 수백 가지요.”"]], [["Margaret only knew that her mother had not found it convenient to come, and she was not sorry to think that their meeting and greeting would take place at Helstone parsonage, rather than, during the confusion of the last two or three days, in the house in Harley Street, where she herself had had to play the part of Figaro, and was wanted everywhere at one and the same time.", "마거릿은 어머니가 오기 어려운 사정이었다는 것만 알았을 뿐이고, 두 사람의 재회와 인사가 지난 이삼일의 그 북새통 속 할리 가의 집에서가 아니라 헬스톤 목사관에서 이루어지게 된 것을 오히려 다행으로 여겼다. 그 집에서 그녀는 피가로 노릇을 해야 했고, 온 사방에서 동시에 찾는 사람이 되어 있었다."], ["Her mind and body ached now with the recollection of all she had done and said within the last forty-eight hours.", "지난 48시간 동안 자기가 하고 말한 모든 것을 떠올리기만 해도 이제 머리와 몸이 다 아팠다."], ["The farewells so hurriedly taken, amongst all the other good-byes, of those she had lived with so long, oppressed her now with a sad regret for the times that were no more; it did not signify what those times had been, they were gone never to return.", "다른 온갖 작별 인사에 섞여 그토록 서둘러 치른, 오래도록 한집에서 살아온 이들과의 이별은 다시 오지 않을 시절에 대한 서글픈 아쉬움으로 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그 시절이 어떠했는지는 상관없었다."], ["Margaret’s heart felt more heavy than she could ever have thought it possible in going to her own dear home, the place and the life she had longed for for years—at that time of all times for yearning and longing, just before the sharp senses lose their outlines in sleep.", "이미 가 버렸고 다시는 돌아오지 않을 시절이었다. 마거릿의 마음은 그토록 그리던 제 집으로, 몇 해나 바라 온 그 자리와 그 삶으로 돌아가는 길에서 스스로 상상조차 못 했을 만큼 무거웠다."], ["She took her mind away with a wrench from the recollection of the past to the bright serene contemplation of the hopeful future.", "그리움과 갈망이 가장 사무치는 그 시각, 곧 예리한 감각이 잠 속에서 윤곽을 잃기 직전의 그 시각이었으니 더욱 그러했다. 그녀는 지난날의 회상에서 억지로 마음을 떼어 내어 희망찬 앞날의 밝고 고요한 관조 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["Her eyes began to see, not visions of what had been, but the sight actually before her; her dear father leaning back asleep in the railway carriage.", "그녀의 눈에 들어오기 시작한 것은 지나간 것들의 환영이 아니라 바로 눈앞의 광경이었다. 기차 객실에 몸을 기댄 채 잠든 사랑하는 아버지였다."], ["His blue-black hair was grey now, and lay thinly over his brows.", "푸른빛 도는 검던 머리는 이제 희끗해져 이마 위에 성글게 놓여 있었다."], ["The bones of his face were plainly to be seen—too plainly for beauty, if his features had been less finely cut; as it was, they had a grace if not a comeliness of their own.", "얼굴뼈가 또렷이 드러나 보였다. 이목구비가 조금만 덜 곱게 다듬어졌더라면 아름답다고 하기에는 지나칠 만큼 또렷했겠지만, 실제로는 준수함까지는 아니어도 그 나름의 기품이 있었다."], ["The face was in repose; but it was rather rest after weariness than the serene calm of the countenance of one who led a placid, contented life.", "얼굴은 쉬고 있었으나, 그것은 잔잔하고 만족스러운 삶을 살아온 이의 얼굴에 깃드는 평온한 고요라기보다 고단함 끝에 찾아온 휴식에 가까웠다."], ["Margaret was painfully struck by the worn, anxious expression; and she went back over the open and avowed circumstances of her father’s life, to find the cause for the lines that spoke so plainly of habitual distress and depression.", "마거릿은 그 지치고 근심 어린 표정에 가슴이 아팠다. 그리고 몸에 밴 괴로움과 침울을 그토록 뚜렷이 말해 주는 그 주름의 까닭을 찾아보려고 아버지의 삶에서 드러나 있고 알려져 있는 사정들을 하나하나 되짚어 보았다."]], [["“Poor Frederick!”", "“가엾은 프레더릭 오빠!”"], ["thought she, sighing. “Oh!", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다."], ["if Frederick had but been a clergyman, instead of going into the navy, and being lost to us all!", "“아, 오빠가 해군에 들어가 우리 모두에게서 사라지는 대신 성직자가 되었더라면!"], ["I wish I knew all about it.", "그 일을 다 알 수 있으면 좋겠는데."], ["I never understood it from Aunt Shaw; I only knew he could not come back to England because of that terrible affair.", "쇼 이모한테서는 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었어. 그 끔찍한 일 때문에 오빠가 영국으로 돌아올 수 없다는 것밖에는."], ["Poor dear papa!", "가엾은 아버지!"], ["how sad he looks!", "어쩌면 저리 슬퍼 보이실까!"], ["I am so glad I am going home, to be at hand to comfort him and mamma.”", "집으로 돌아가 곁에서 아버지와 어머니를 위로해 드릴 수 있으니 참 다행이야.”"]], [["She was ready with a bright smile, in which there was not a trace of fatigue, to greet her father when he awakened.", "아버지가 잠에서 깨어났을 때 그녀는 피로의 기색이라고는 조금도 없는 환한 미소로 그를 맞을 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["He smiled back again, but faintly, as if it were an unusual exertion.", "그도 마주 웃어 주었으나 희미했다. 마치 익숙지 않은 힘을 쓰는 일이라도 되는 듯이."], ["His face returned into its lines of habitual anxiety.", "그의 얼굴은 이내 몸에 밴 근심의 주름으로 되돌아갔다."], ["He had a trick of half-opening his mouth as if to speak, which constantly unsettled the form of the lips, and gave the face an undecided expression.", "그에게는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 입을 반쯤 벌리는 버릇이 있었는데, 그 탓에 입술의 모양이 늘 흐트러져 얼굴에 우유부단한 표정이 서렸다."], ["But he had the same large, soft eyes as his daughter,—eyes which moved slowly and almost grandly round in their orbits, and were well veiled by their transparent white eyelids.", "그러나 그에게는 딸과 똑같은 크고 부드러운 눈이 있었다. 눈구멍 안에서 느릿하게, 거의 위엄 있다 할 만큼 움직이며 투명하고 흰 눈꺼풀에 잘 가려진 눈이었다."], ["Margaret was more like him than like her mother.", "마거릿은 어머니보다 아버지를 닮았다."], ["Sometimes people wondered that parents so handsome should have a daughter who was so far regularly beautiful; not beautiful at all, was occasionally said.", "이토록 인물 좋은 부모에게서 어찌 저리 정연한 아름다움과는 거리가 먼 딸이 나왔을까 의아해하는 사람들도 이따금 있었다."], ["Her mouth was wide; no rosebud that could only open just enough to let out a “yes”", "아예 아름답지 않다는 말까지 나올 때도 있었다. 입은 컸다."], ["and “no,” and “an’t please you, sir.”", "“네”와 “아니요”와 “나리, 그러시다면요”"], ["But the wide mouth was one soft curve of rich red lips; and the skin, if not white and fair, was of an ivory smoothness and delicacy.", "정도만 겨우 나올 만큼 벌어지는 장미 봉오리 같은 입이 아니었다. 그러나 그 큰 입은 짙은 붉은 입술이 부드럽게 그린 하나의 곡선이었고, 살결은 희고 고운 편은 아니어도 상아처럼 매끄럽고 섬세했다."], ["If the look on her face was, in general, too dignified and reserved for one so young, now, talking to her father, it was bright as the morning,—full of dimples, and glances that spoke of childish gladness, and boundless hope in the future.", "평소 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 표정이 그 나이답지 않게 너무 의젓하고 조심스러웠다면, 지금 아버지와 이야기하는 그 얼굴은 아침처럼 환했다. 보조개가 가득했고, 어린아이 같은 기쁨과 앞날에 대한 끝없는 희망을 말해 주는 눈빛이 넘쳤다."]], [["It was the latter part of July when Margaret returned home.", "마거릿이 집으로 돌아온 것은 7월 하순이었다. 숲의 나무들은 온통 짙고 무성한 검푸른 초록이었고, 그 아래 고사리는 비스듬히 드는 햇살을 남김없이 받아 안았다."], ["The forest trees were all one dark, full, dusky green; the fern below them caught all the slanting sunbeams; the weather was sultry and broodingly still.", "날씨는 후덥지근하고 무겁게 고요했다. 마거릿은 아버지 곁에서 성큼성큼 걷곤 했다."], ["Margaret used to tramp along by her father’s side, crushing down the fern with a cruel glee, as she felt it yield under her light foot, and send up the fragrance peculiar to it—out on the broad commons into the warm scented light, seeing multitudes of wild, free, living creatures, revelling in the sunshine, and the herbs and flowers it called forth.", "가벼운 발밑에서 고사리가 눌리며 특유의 향기를 뿜어 올리는 것을 느끼며 짓궂은 즐거움으로 그것을 밟아 갔다. 그렇게 넓은 공유지로 나가 따뜻하고 향기로운 빛 속에 들어서면, 햇빛과 그 햇빛이 불러낸 풀과 꽃을 마음껏 누리는 야생의 자유로운 생명들이 무수히 보였다."], ["This life—at least these walks—realised all Margaret’s anticipations.", "이 삶은, 적어도 이 산책만은 마거릿이 품었던 모든 기대를 그대로 이루어 주었다. 그녀는 자기 숲에 자부심을 느꼈다."], ["She took a pride in her forest.", "그곳 사람들은 그녀의 사람들이었다."], ["Its people were her people.", "그녀는 그들과 진심으로 벗이 되었고, 그들만의 말을 배워 쓰는 것을 즐거워했다."], ["She made hearty friends with them; learned and delighted in using their peculiar words; took up her freedom amongst them; nursed their babies; talked or read with slow distinctness to their old people; carried dainty messes to their sick; resolved before long to teach at the school where her father went regularly every day as to an appointed task, but she was continually tempted off to go and see some individual friend—man, woman, or child—in some cottage in the green shade of the forest.", "그들 사이에서 스스럼없이 지냈고, 그들의 아기를 돌보았고, 늙은이들과는 천천히 또박또박 이야기하거나 책을 읽어 주었고, 앓는 이들에게는 정성 들인 죽을 날라다 주었다. 머잖아 아버지가 정해진 소임처럼 날마다 나가는 학교에서 가르치기로 마음먹고 있었지만, 그녀는 늘 딴 데로 이끌려 숲의 푸른 그늘에 자리한 어느 오두막의 벗 하나를 — 남자든 여자든 아이든 — 보러 가곤 했다."], ["Her out-of-doors life was perfect.", "바깥에서의 삶은 나무랄 데가 없었다."], ["Her in-doors life had its drawbacks.", "집 안에서의 삶에는 흠이 있었다."], ["With the healthy shame of a child, she blamed herself for her keenness of sight in perceiving that all was not as it should be there.", "아이다운 건강한 부끄러움으로 그녀는 그 집 안의 모든 것이 마땅히 있어야 할 대로 있지 않다는 것을 알아채는 자기 눈의 밝음을 스스로 나무랐다."], ["Her mother—her mother always so kind and tender towards her—seemed now and then so much discontented with their situation; thought that the bishop strangely neglected his episcopal duties, in not giving Mr.", "어머니는, 늘 그토록 다정하고 살뜰하던 어머니는 이따금 자기네 처지에 몹시 못마땅해하는 듯 보였다. 주교가 헤일 씨에게 더 나은 성직록을 주지 않는 것을 두고 주교 직분을 이상하리만치 소홀히 한다고 여겼고, 이 교구를 떠나 더 큰 교구를 맡고 싶다는 말을 차마 입에 올리지 못하는 남편을 나무라다시피 했다."], ["Hale a better living; and almost reproached her husband because he could not bring himself to say that he wished to leave the parish, and undertake the charge of a larger.", "그러면 그는 소리 내어 한숨을 쉬며, 이 작은 헬스톤에서 제 할 도리만 다할 수 있어도 고마운 일이라고 대답했다. 그러나 날이 갈수록 그는 더 짓눌렸고, 세상은 더 갈피를 잡을 수 없어졌다."], ["He would sigh aloud as he answered, that if he could do what he ought in little Helstone, he should be thankful; but every day he was more overpowered; the world became more bewildering.", "아내가 무슨 승진 자리라도 알아보라고 거듭 몰아붙일 때마다 마거릿은 아버지가 점점 더 움츠러드는 것을 보았다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 어머니를 헬스톤과 화해시키려 애썼다."], ["At each repeated urgency of his wife, that he would put himself in the way of seeking some preferment, Margaret saw that her father shrank more and more; and she strove at such times to reconcile her mother to Helstone. Mrs.", "헤일 부인은 나무가 이렇게 많은 곳 가까이 사는 것이 건강에 좋지 않다고 말했고, 마거릿은 볕이 줄무늬처럼 들고 구름 그림자가 지나가는 그 아름답고 널따란 고지의 공유지로 어머니를 꾀어내려 했다."], ["Hale said that the near neighbourhood of so many trees affected her health; and Margaret would try to tempt her forth on to the beautiful, broad, upland, sun-streaked, cloud-shadowed common; for she was sure that her mother had accustomed herself too much to an in-doors life, seldom extending her walks beyond the church, the school, and the neighbouring cottages.", "어머니가 집 안에서만 지내는 데 너무 익숙해져 산책이라고 해야 교회와 학교와 이웃 오두막 언저리를 좀처럼 벗어나지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["This did good for a time; but when the autumn drew on, and the weather became more changeable, her mother’s idea of the unhealthiness of the place increased; and she repined even more frequently that her husband, who was more learned than Mr.", "한동안은 효험이 있었다. 그러나 가을이 짙어지고 날씨가 더 변덕스러워지자 이곳이 건강에 나쁘다는 어머니의 생각은 더 커졌다."], ["Hume, a better parish priest than Mr. Houldsworth, should not have met with the preferment that these two former neighbours of theirs had done.", "그리고 흄 씨보다 학식이 깊고 홀즈워스 씨보다 나은 교구 목사인 남편이 어째서 그 두 옛 이웃이 얻은 승진을 얻지 못했느냐고 더욱 자주 한탄했다."]], [["This marring of the peace of home, by long hours of discontent, was what Margaret was unprepared for.", "기나긴 불평의 시간으로 집안의 평화가 이렇게 상해 가는 것에 마거릿은 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["She knew, and had rather revelled in the idea, that she should have to give up many luxuries, which had only been troubles and trammels to her freedom in Harley Street.", "할리 가에서는 그저 성가심이자 자유를 옭아매는 굴레였을 뿐인 여러 호사를 포기해야 하리라는 것은 알고 있었고, 오히려 그 생각을 즐긴 편이었다."], ["Her keen enjoyment of every sensuous pleasure was balanced finely, if not overbalanced, by her conscious pride in being able to do without them all, if need were.", "감각의 온갖 즐거움을 예민하게 누리는 그녀의 성정은 필요하다면 그 모든 것 없이도 살 수 있다는 자각에서 오는 자부심으로 알맞게, 아니 어쩌면 넘칠 만큼 균형이 잡혀 있었다."], ["But the cloud never comes in that quarter of the horizon from which we watch for it.", "그러나 구름은 우리가 지켜보고 있는 쪽 하늘에서는 결코 오지 않는 법이다."], ["There had been slight complaints and passing regrets on her mother’s part, over some trifle connected with Helstone, and her father’s position there, when Margaret had been spending her holidays at home before; but in the general happiness of the recollection of those times, she had forgotten the small details which were not so pleasant.", "예전에 집에서 방학을 보낼 때도 헬스톤과 그곳에서의 아버지 처지에 얽힌 사소한 일들을 두고 어머니가 조금씩 불평하거나 지나가듯 아쉬워한 적은 있었다. 그러나 그 시절을 떠올릴 때의 전체적인 행복감 속에서 그녀는 그리 유쾌하지 않았던 자잘한 대목들을 잊고 있었던 것이다."]], [["In the latter half of September, the autumnal rains and storms came on, and Margaret was obliged to remain more in the house than she had hitherto done.", "9월 후반에 접어들자 가을비와 폭풍이 몰아쳤고, 마거릿은 그때까지보다 집 안에 더 머물러야 했다."], ["Helstone was at some distance from any neighbours of their own standard of cultivation.", "헬스톤은 자기네와 같은 교양 수준의 이웃이라면 어느 집에서든 제법 떨어져 있었다."]], [["“It is undoubtedly one of the most out-of-the-way places in England,”", "“여기는 두말할 것 없이 잉글랜드에서 가장 외진 곳 가운데 하나예요.” 헤일 부인이 그 한탄 섞인 기분에 잠긴 어느 날 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Hale, in one of her plaintive moods.", "“아버지가 여기서는 정말이지 어울릴 사람 하나 없다는 게 늘 안타까워 견딜 수가 없구나."], ["“I can’t help regretting constantly that papa has really no one to associate with here; he is so thrown away; seeing no one but farmers and labourers from week’s end to week’s end.", "그이는 이렇게 버려져 있잖니. 한 주가 가고 또 한 주가 가도록 농부와 품팔이꾼 말고는 아무도 만나지 못하고."], ["If we only lived at the other side of the parish, it would be something; there we should be almost within walking distance of the Stansfields; certainly the Gormons would be within a walk.”", "교구 저쪽 편에만 살았어도 좀 나았을 텐데. 거기라면 스탠스필드 댁까지 거의 걸어갈 만한 거리이고, 고먼 댁이야 분명 걸어갈 만한 거리이니 말이다.”"]], [["“Gormons,”", "“고먼이라니요.”"], ["said Margaret, “Are those the Gormons who made their fortunes in trade at Southampton?", "마거릿이 말했다. “사우샘프턴에서 장사로 재산을 모았다는 그 고먼 댁 말씀이세요?"], ["Oh! I’m glad we don’t visit them.", "아, 그 집과 왕래하지 않아 다행이에요."], ["I don’t like shoppy people.", "저는 장사치들이 마음에 들지 않아요."], ["I think we are far better off, knowing only cottagers and labourers, and people without pretence.”", "오두막 사람들과 품팔이꾼들, 잘난 체할 줄 모르는 사람들만 알고 지내는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“You must not be so fastidious, Margaret, dear!”", "“그렇게 까다롭게 굴면 못쓴다, 마거릿아.”"], ["said her mother, secretly thinking of a young and handsome Mr.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["Gormon whom she had once met at Mr. Hume’s.", "언젠가 흄 씨 댁에서 만난 적이 있는 젊고 인물 좋은 고먼 씨를 속으로 떠올리면서였다."]], [["“No!", "“아니에요!"], ["I call mine a very comprehensive taste; I like all people whose occupations have to do with land; I like soldiers and sailors, and the three learned professions, as they call them.", "제 취향은 아주 너른걸요. 저는 땅과 관련된 일을 하는 사람이라면 다 좋아요."], ["I’m sure you don’t want me to admire butchers and bakers, and candlestick-makers, do you, mamma?”", "군인도 뱃사람도 좋고, 사람들이 말하는 그 세 가지 학식 있는 직업도 좋아요. 설마 어머니가 저더러 푸주한이며 빵장수며 촛대장이까지 우러러보라고 하시지는 않겠지요?”"]], [["“But the Gormons were neither butchers nor bakers, but very respectable coach-builders.”", "“하지만 고먼 댁은 푸주한도 빵장수도 아니고, 아주 번듯한 마차 제작자였단다.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“그렇군요."], ["Coach-building is a trade all the same, and I think a much more useless one than that of butchers or bakers. Oh!", "마차 제작도 어차피 장사이고, 제 생각에는 푸주한이나 빵장수보다 훨씬 쓸데없는 장사인걸요."], ["how tired I used to be of the drives every day in Aunt Shaw’s carriage, and how I longed to walk!”", "아, 쇼 이모의 마차를 타고 날마다 나다니는 게 얼마나 넌더리 났는지 몰라요. 그리고 얼마나 걷고 싶었는지도요!”"]], [["And walk Margaret did, in spite of the weather.", "그리하여 마거릿은 날씨야 어떻든 걸었다."], ["She was so happy out of doors, at her father’s side, that she almost danced; and with the soft violence of the west wind behind her, as she crossed some heath, she seemed to be borne onwards, as lightly and easily as the fallen leaf that was wafted along by the autumnal breeze.", "아버지 곁에서 바깥에 나서면 어찌나 행복한지 거의 춤을 추다시피 했다. 어느 히스 벌판을 가로지를 때 서풍이 뒤에서 부드럽게 몰아붙이면, 그녀는 가을바람에 실려 가는 낙엽처럼 가볍고 수월하게 앞으로 떠밀려 가는 듯했다."], ["But the evenings were rather difficult to fill up agreeably.", "그러나 저녁 시간은 유쾌하게 채우기가 제법 어려웠다."], ["Immediately after tea her father withdrew into his small library, and she and her mother were left alone.", "차를 마시자마자 아버지는 자기 작은 서재로 물러갔고, 그녀와 어머니만 남았다."], ["Mrs. Hale had never cared much for books, and had discouraged her husband, very early in their married life, in his desire of reading aloud to her, while she worked.", "헤일 부인은 애초에 책을 그리 좋아한 적이 없었고, 신혼 초에 이미 자기가 바느질하는 동안 소리 내어 책을 읽어 주고 싶어 하던 남편의 뜻을 꺾어 버렸다."], ["At one time they had tried backgammon as a resource; but as Mr.", "한때는 방편으로 주사위판 놀이를 해 보기도 했다."], ["Hale grew to take an increasing interest in his school and his parishioners, he found that the interruptions which arose out of these duties were regarded as hardships by his wife, not to be accepted as the natural conditions of his profession, but to be regretted and struggled against by her as they severally arose.", "그러나 헤일 씨가 학교와 교구민들에게 점점 더 마음을 쏟게 되면서, 그 소임에서 생기는 방해를 아내가 성직자의 당연한 조건으로 받아들이기는커녕 그때그때 안타까워하며 맞서 싸울 고생거리로 여긴다는 것을 그는 알게 되었다."], ["So he withdrew, while the children were yet young, into his library, to spend his evenings (if he were at home), in reading the speculative and metaphysical books which were his delight.", "그래서 그는 아이들이 아직 어릴 적에 서재로 물러나, 집에 있는 저녁이면 자기가 즐기는 사변적이고 형이상학적인 책들을 읽으며 시간을 보내게 된 것이다."]], [["When Margaret had been here before, she had brought down with her a great box of books, recommended by masters or governess, and had found the summer’s day all too short to get through the reading she had to do before her return to town.", "전에 이곳에 와 있을 때 마거릿은 선생들이나 가정교사가 권한 책을 큰 상자로 하나 가득 들고 내려왔고, 도시로 돌아가기 전에 읽어야 할 분량을 다 해내기에는 여름 하루가 너무 짧다고 여겼다."], ["Now there were only the well-bound, little-read English Classics, which were weeded out of her father’s library to fill up the small book-shelves in the drawing-room.", "그런데 이제는 응접실의 작은 책장을 채우려고 아버지의 서재에서 골라낸, 장정만 훌륭하고 거의 읽히지 않은 영국 고전들뿐이었다."], ["Thomson’s Season’s, Hayley’s Cowper, Middleton’s Cicero, were by far the lightest, newest, and most amusing.", "톰슨의 『사계』, 헤일리가 쓴 카우퍼 전기, 미들턴의 키케로 전기가 그중에서는 단연 가볍고 새롭고 재미있는 축이었다."], ["The book-shelves did not afford much resource.", "책장에서 기대할 것은 그리 많지 않았다."], ["Margaret told her mother every particular of her London life, to all of which Mrs.", "마거릿은 런던 생활을 시시콜콜 어머니에게 들려주었고, 헤일 부인은 그 모든 이야기를 흥미롭게 들었다."], ["Hale listened with interest, sometimes amused and questioning, at others a little inclined to compare her sister’s circumstances of ease and comfort with the narrower means at Helstone Vicarage.", "때로는 재미있어하며 이것저것 물었고, 때로는 언니의 넉넉하고 편안한 형편을 헬스톤 목사관의 옹색한 살림과 견주고 싶어 하는 기색도 있었다."], ["On such evenings Margaret was apt to stop talking rather abruptly, and listen to the drip-drip of the rain upon the leads of the little bow window.", "그런 저녁이면 마거릿은 이야기를 다소 뚝 그치고, 자그마한 활 모양 창의 납 지붕 위로 떨어지는 빗방울 소리에 귀를 기울이곤 했다."], ["Once or twice Margaret found herself mechanically counting the repetition of the monotonous sound, while she wondered if she might venture to put a question on a subject very near to her heart, and ask where Frederick was now; what he was doing; how long it was since they had heard from him.", "한두 번은 자기도 모르게 그 단조로운 소리가 되풀이되는 횟수를 기계적으로 세고 있었다. 그러면서 마음속 깊이 걸려 있는 문제를 감히 여쭈어도 될까, 프레더릭 오빠가 지금 어디에 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지, 소식을 들은 지 얼마나 되었는지 물어도 될까 망설였다."], ["But a consciousness that her mother’s delicate health and positive dislike to Helstone, all dated from the time of the mutiny in which Frederick had been engaged—the full account of which Margaret had never heard, and which now seemed doomed to be buried in sad oblivion,—made her pause and turn away from the subject each time she approached it.", "그러나 어머니의 약한 몸과 헬스톤을 노골적으로 싫어하는 마음이 모두 프레더릭이 휘말렸던 그 반란 때부터 비롯되었다는 자각이 — 그 일의 자초지종을 마거릿은 한 번도 들은 적이 없었고 이제는 슬픈 망각 속에 묻혀 버릴 운명인 듯했다 — 그 이야기에 다가갈 때마다 그녀를 멈칫하게 하고 발길을 돌리게 했다."], ["When she was with her mother, her father seemed the best person to apply to for information; and when with him, she thought that she could speak more easily to her mother.", "어머니와 있을 때는 아버지가 물어보기에 가장 나은 사람 같았고, 아버지와 있을 때는 어머니에게라면 더 쉽게 말을 꺼낼 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["Probably there was nothing much to be heard that was new.", "아마 새로 들을 만한 이야기도 별로 없었을 것이다."], ["In one of the letters she had received before leaving Harley Street, her father had told her that they had heard from Frederick; he was still at Rio, and very well in health, and sent his best love to her; which was dry bones, but not the living intelligence she longed for.", "할리 가를 떠나기 전에 받은 편지 가운데 하나에서 아버지는 프레더릭에게서 소식이 왔다고, 아직 리우에 있고 몸은 아주 건강하며 그녀에게 사랑을 전한다고 알려 왔다. 그것은 앙상한 뼈다귀였을 뿐, 그녀가 갈망하던 살아 있는 소식은 아니었다."], ["Frederick was always spoken of, in the rare times when his name was mentioned, as “Poor Frederick.”", "프레더릭의 이름이 드물게 입에 오를 때면 그는 늘 “가엾은 프레더릭”이라고 불렸다."], ["His room was kept exactly as he had left it; and was regularly dusted, and put into order by Dixon, Mrs.", "그의 방은 그가 떠날 때 그대로 보존되었고, 헤일 부인의 하녀 딕슨이 꼬박꼬박 먼지를 털고 정돈했다."], ["Hale’s maid, who touched no other part of the household work, but always remembered the day when she had been engaged by Lady Beresford as ladies’ maid to Sir John’s wards, the pretty Miss Beresfords, the belles of Rutlandshire. Dixon had always considered Mr.", "딕슨은 집안일의 다른 일에는 손도 대지 않았으나, 베러스퍼드 부인에게 고용되어 존 경의 피후견인들, 곧 러틀랜드셔의 미인이던 어여쁜 베러스퍼드 자매의 시녀가 되던 그날을 언제나 기억하고 있었다."], ["Hale as the blight which had fallen upon her young lady’s prospects in life.", "딕슨은 헤일 씨를 늘 자기 아가씨의 앞날에 떨어진 재앙이라고 여겼다."], ["If Miss Beresford had not been in such a hurry to marry a poor country clergyman there was no knowing what she might have become.", "베러스퍼드 양이 그렇게 서둘러 가난한 시골 성직자와 혼인하지만 않았더라면 무엇이 되었을지 누가 알겠는가."], ["But Dixon was too loyal to desert her in her affliction and downfall (alas, her married life).", "그러나 딕슨은 너무 충직해서 아가씨의 고난과 몰락(아, 그 결혼 생활 말이다) 속에서 그녀를 저버릴 수 없었다."], ["She remained with her, and was devoted to her interests; always considering herself as the good and protecting fairy, whose duty it was to baffle the malignant giant, Mr. Hale.", "그녀는 곁에 남아 아가씨의 이익에 헌신했고, 언제나 자기를 착하고 지켜 주는 요정으로, 사악한 거인 헤일 씨의 훼방을 막아 내는 것이 제 소임인 존재로 여겼다."], ["Master Frederick had been her favourite and pride; and it was with a little softening of her dignified look and manner, that she went in weekly to arrange the chamber as carefully as if he might be coming home that very evening.", "프레더릭 도련님은 그녀가 아끼는 자랑거리였다. 그래서 그 위엄 있는 얼굴과 몸가짐을 조금은 누그러뜨린 채 주마다 그 방에 들어가, 마치 바로 그날 저녁에라도 도련님이 돌아올 듯 정성스레 정돈해 놓곤 했다."]], [["Margaret could not help believing that there had been some late intelligence of Frederick, unknown to her mother, which was making her father anxious and uneasy.", "마거릿은 어머니가 모르는 프레더릭의 최근 소식이 있어서 그것이 아버지를 초조하고 불안하게 만드는 것이라는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. 헤일 부인은 남편의 얼굴빛이나 몸가짐에 달라진 데가 있다는 것을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Mrs. Hale did not seem to perceive any alteration in her husband’s looks or ways.", "그의 마음결은 여리고 부드러워서 남의 안부에 관한 자그마한 소식에도 쉬이 흔들렸다."], ["His spirits were tender and gentle, readily affected by any small piece of intelligence concerning the welfare of others.", "임종을 지켜보거나 무슨 범죄 이야기를 들으면 여러 날 침울해하곤 했다. 그러나 지금 마거릿이 알아챈 것은 넋이 나가 있는 기색이었다."], ["He would be depressed for many days after witnessing a death-bed, or hearing of any crime.", "마치 무언가에 생각이 사로잡혀 있는데, 그 짓눌림은 남은 이들을 위로한다든가 다음 세대의 악을 줄여 보려는 마음으로 학교에서 가르친다든가 하는 일상의 어떤 행동으로도 덜어지지 않는 듯했다. 헤일 씨는 여느 때만큼 교구민들 사이로 나가지 않았고, 서재에 틀어박혀 있는 때가 더 많았다."], ["But now Margaret noticed an absence of mind, as if his thoughts were pre-occupied by some subject, the oppression of which could not be relieved by any daily action, such as comforting the survivors, or teaching at the school in hope of lessening the evils in the generation to come.", "그리고 마을 우체부를 기다리며 안절부절못했다. 우체부가 집에 오는 신호는 뒤채 부엌 창 덧문을 두드리는 소리였는데, 한때는 집 안의 누군가가 그것이 무엇인지 알아듣고 응대할 만큼 시각을 가늠하기까지 몇 번이고 되풀이해 두드려야 하는 일이 잦았다."], ["Mr. Hale did not go out among his parishioners as much as usual; he was more shut up in his study; was anxious for the village postman, whose summons to the household was a rap on the back-kitchen window shutter—a signal which at one time had often to be repeated before any one was sufficiently alive to the hour of the day to understand what it was, and attend to him.", "이제 헤일 씨는 아침 날씨가 좋으면 뜰을 서성였고, 그렇지 않으면 우체부가 다녀가기 전까지 서재 창가에 꿈꾸는 듯 서 있었다."], ["Now Mr. Hale loitered about the garden if the morning was fine, and if not, stood dreamily by the study window until the postman had called, or gone down the lane, giving a half-respectful, half-confidential shake of the head to the parson, who watched him away from the sweet-briar hedge, and past the great arbutus, before he turned into the room to begin his day’s work, with all the signs of a heavy heart and an occupied mind.", "우체부는 반쯤 공손하고 반쯤 은근한 몸짓으로 목사에게 고개를 까딱하고는 골목길로 내려갔고, 목사는 들장미 울타리 너머로, 커다란 소귀나무 옆을 지나 그가 멀어지는 것을 지켜본 뒤에야 방으로 들어와 무거운 마음과 사로잡힌 정신의 온갖 기색을 띤 채 그날의 일을 시작하곤 했다."]], [["But Margaret was at an age when any apprehension, not absolutely based on a knowledge of facts, is easily banished for a time by a bright sunny day, or some happy outward circumstance.", "그러나 마거릿은 사실의 앎에 온전히 기대지 않은 근심이라면 화창하고 볕 좋은 하루나 무슨 즐거운 바깥일 하나로 한동안 쉬이 떨쳐지는 나이였다."], ["And when the brilliant fourteen fine days of October came on, her cares were all blown away as lightly as thistledown, and she thought of nothing but the glories of the forest.", "그리고 10월의 눈부신 14일 동안 좋은 날씨가 이어지자 그녀의 근심은 엉겅퀴 솜털처럼 가볍게 다 날아가 버렸고, 그녀는 숲의 영광 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다."], ["The fern-harvest was over; and now that the rain was gone, many a deep glade was accessible, into which Margaret had only peeped in July and August weather.", "고사리 거두기는 끝났고, 이제 비가 그치자 7월과 8월의 날씨에는 그저 들여다보기만 했던 깊은 빈터 여러 곳에 들어갈 수 있게 되었다."], ["She had learnt drawing with Edith; and she had sufficiently regretted, during the gloom of the bad weather, her idle revelling in the beauty of the woodlands, while it had yet been fine, to make her determined to sketch what she could before winter fairly set in.", "그녀는 이디스와 함께 그림을 배운 적이 있었다. 그리고 궂은 날씨의 우울 속에서, 날이 좋았을 때 숲의 아름다움을 그저 게으르게 누리기만 한 것을 어찌나 후회했던지, 겨울이 제대로 오기 전에 그릴 수 있는 것은 다 그려 두리라 마음먹게 되었다."], ["Accordingly, she was busy preparing her board one morning, when Sarah, the housemaid, threw wide open the drawing-room door, and announced, “Mr.", "그리하여 어느 날 아침 화판을 부지런히 챙기고 있는데, 하녀 세라가 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히며 아뢰었다."], ["Henry Lennox.”", "“헨리 레녹스 씨 오셨습니다.”"]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# 제3장"]], [["“THE MORE HASTE THE WORSE SPEED.”", "「급할수록 돌아가라」"]], [["“Learn to win a lady’s faith Nobly, as the thing is high; Bravely, as for life and death— With a loyal gravity.", "“여인의 믿음을 얻는 법을 배우라, 그 일이 고귀한 만큼 고귀하게. 생사를 건 일인 양 용감하게, 한결같은 진중함으로."]], [["Lead her from the festive boards, Point her to the starry skies, Guard her, by your truthful words, Pure from courtship’s flatteries.”", "잔치의 식탁에서 그녀를 이끌어 내라, 별 뜬 하늘을 가리켜 보이라, 그대의 참된 말로 그녀를 지키라, 구애의 아첨에 물들지 않도록."], ["MRS. BROWNING.", "— 브라우닝 부인."]], [["“Mr. Henry Lennox.”", "“헨리 레녹스 씨 오셨습니다.”"], ["Margaret had been thinking of him only a moment before, and remembering his inquiry into her probable occupations at home.", "마거릿은 바로 조금 전까지 그를 떠올리며 집에서 무엇을 하며 지낼 것이냐고 묻던 그의 말을 되새기던 참이었다."], ["It was “parler du soleil et l’on en voit les rayons”; and the brightness of the sun came over Margaret’s face as she put down her board, and went forward to shake hands with him.", "그야말로 “해 이야기를 하니 햇살이 보인다”는 격이었다. 화판을 내려놓고 그와 악수하려 앞으로 나서는 마거릿의 얼굴 위로 그 햇살의 환함이 번졌다."], ["“Tell mamma, Sarah,”", "“어머니께 여쭈어라, 세라.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Mamma and I want to ask you so many questions about Edith; I am so much obliged to you for coming.”", "“어머니와 저는 이디스에 대해 여쭐 것이 산더미랍니다. 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요.”"]], [["“Did not I say that I should?”", "“그러겠다고 말씀드리지 않았습니까?”"], ["asked he, in a lower tone than that in which he had spoken.", "그가 아까보다 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“But I heard of you so far away in the Highlands that I never thought Hampshire could come in.”", "“하지만 저 멀리 하일랜드에 계신다는 소식을 들었던 터라 햄프셔까지 오실 줄은 생각도 못 했어요.”"]], [["“Oh!”", "“아!” 그가 한결 가볍게 말했다."], ["said he, more lightly, “our young couple were playing such foolish pranks, running all sorts of risks, climbing this mountain, sailing on that lake, that I really thought they needed a Mentor to take care of them.", "“우리 젊은 부부가 어찌나 어리석은 장난을 치는지, 온갖 위험한 짓을 다 하더군요. 이 산에 오르고 저 호수에서 배를 타고."], ["And indeed they did: they were quite beyond my uncle’s management, and kept the old gentleman in a panic for sixteen hours out of the twenty-four.", "곁에서 돌봐 줄 멘토르가 정말로 필요하겠다 싶었지요. 실제로 그랬고요."], ["Indeed, when I once saw how unfit they were to be trusted alone, I thought it my duty not to leave them till I had seen them safely embarked at Plymouth.”", "숙부님으로서는 도무지 감당이 되지 않아서, 그 노신사가 하루 24시간 중 16시간을 노심초사하며 지내셨습니다. 정말이지 그 둘만 믿고 맡겨 둘 수 없다는 것을 한번 보고 나니, 플리머스에서 무사히 배에 오르는 것을 볼 때까지 곁을 떠나지 않는 것이 제 도리라고 여겨졌습니다.”"]], [["“Have you been at Plymouth?", "“플리머스에 다녀오셨어요?"], ["Oh! Edith never named that.", "어머, 이디스는 그 말은 하지 않았는데요."], ["To be sure, she has written in such a hurry lately.", "하기야 요즘은 워낙 서둘러 편지를 썼으니까요."], ["Did they really sail on Tuesday?”", "정말 화요일에 떠났나요?”"]], [["“Really sailed, and relieved me from many responsibilities.", "“정말로 떠났고, 덕분에 저는 여러 책임에서 놓여났습니다."], ["Edith gave me all sorts of messages for you.", "이디스가 아가씨께 전해 달라며 온갖 말을 남겼지요."], ["I believe I have a little diminutive note somewhere; yes, here it is.”", "어딘가에 자그마한 쪽지가 하나 있을 텐데요. 그렇지, 여기 있습니다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머, 고맙습니다.”"], ["thank you,”", "마거릿이 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Margaret; and then, half wishing to read it alone and unwatched, she made the excuse of going to tell her mother again (Sarah surely had made some mistake) that Mr. Lennox was there.", "그러고는 아무도 보지 않는 데서 혼자 읽고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 들어, 레녹스 씨가 왔다고 어머니께 다시 알리러 간다는 핑계를 댔다(세라가 필시 무엇인가 잘못 전했을 터였다)."]], [["When she had left the room, he began in his scrutinising way to look about him.", "그녀가 방을 나가자 그는 특유의 살피는 눈길로 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["The little drawing-room was looking its best in the streaming light of the morning sun.", "자그마한 응접실은 아침 햇살이 쏟아져 들어와 가장 좋은 모습을 하고 있었다."], ["The middle window in the bow was opened, and clustering roses and the scarlet honeysuckle came peeping round the corner; the small lawn was gorgeous with verbenas and geraniums of all bright colours.", "활 모양으로 튀어나온 창의 가운데 것이 열려 있어 송이송이 핀 장미와 진홍빛 인동덩굴이 모퉁이로 고개를 내밀었고, 작은 잔디밭은 온갖 밝은 빛깔의 버베나와 제라늄으로 화사했다. 그러나 바깥이 그토록 환한 탓에 안쪽 빛깔들은 초라하고 바래 보였다."], ["But the very brightness outside made the colours within seem poor and faded.", "양탄자는 새것과는 거리가 멀었고, 사라사 천은 여러 번 빨아 쓴 것이었다. 방 전체가 그가 예상했던 것보다 작고 남루했다."], ["The carpet was far from new; the chintz had been often washed; the whole apartment was smaller and shabbier than he had expected, as back-ground and frame-work for Margaret, herself so queenly.", "그토록 여왕 같은 마거릿을 담을 배경이자 틀치고는 말이다. 그는 탁자에 놓인 책 한 권을 집어 들었다."], ["He took up one of the books lying on the table; it was the Paradise of Dante, in the proper old Italian binding of white vellum and gold; by it lay a dictionary, and some words copied out in Margaret’s handwriting.", "흰 양피지와 금박으로 된 제대로 된 옛 이탈리아식 장정의 단테 『천국편』이었다. 그 곁에는 사전이 한 권 놓여 있었고, 마거릿의 필체로 옮겨 적은 낱말들이 있었다."], ["They were a dull list of words, but somehow he liked looking at them.", "밋밋한 낱말 목록일 뿐이었으나 어쩐지 그는 그것을 들여다보는 것이 좋았다."], ["He put them down with a sigh.", "그는 한숨을 쉬며 그것을 내려놓았다."]], [["“The living is evidently as small as she said.", "“성직록이 그녀 말대로 정말 적은 모양이군."], ["It seems strange, for the Beresfords belong to a good family.”", "베러스퍼드 집안이 좋은 가문인 것을 생각하면 이상한 일이야.”"]], [["Margaret meanwhile had found her mother.", "그러는 사이 마거릿은 어머니를 찾아냈다."], ["It was one of Mrs. Hale’s fitful days, when everything was a difficulty and a hardship: and Mr.", "그날은 헤일 부인이 변덕을 부리는 날 가운데 하나여서 무슨 일이든 곤란하고 고생스럽게 여겨졌다."], ["Lennox’s appearance took this shape, although secretly she felt complimented by his thinking it worth while to call.", "레녹스 씨의 방문도 그런 모양새를 띠었다. 속으로는 그가 일부러 찾아올 만하다고 여겨 준 것이 흐뭇했지만 말이다."]], [["“It is most unfortunate!", "“정말 하필이면!"], ["We are dining early to-day, and having nothing but cold meat, in order that the servants may get on with their ironing; and yet, of course, we must ask him to dinner—Edith’s brother-in-law and all.", "오늘은 하인들이 다림질을 마칠 수 있도록 일찍, 그것도 찬 고기밖에 없이 밥을 먹기로 했는데. 그런데도 물론 그이를 만찬에 청해야지."], ["And your papa is in such low spirits this morning about something—I don’t know what.", "이디스의 시동생인데. 게다가 네 아버지는 오늘 아침 무엇 때문인지 저리도 기가 죽어 계시고."], ["I went into the study just now, and he had his face on the table, covering it with his hands.", "무엇 때문인지도 모르겠구나. 방금 서재에 들어가 보니 얼굴을 탁자에 대고 두 손으로 감싸고 계시더구나."], ["I told him I was sure Helstone air did not agree with him any more than with me, and he suddenly lifted up his head, and begged me not to speak a word more against Helstone, he could not bear it; if there was one place he loved on earth it was Helstone.", "헬스톤 공기가 나한테 그렇듯이 당신께도 맞지 않는 게 분명하다고 말씀드렸더니, 갑자기 고개를 들고는 헬스톤을 나쁘게 말하는 소리를 한마디도 더 하지 말아 달라고, 그건 못 견디겠다고 하시더구나. 이 세상에 사랑하는 곳이 한 군데 있다면 그건 헬스톤이라면서."], ["But I am sure, for all that, it is the damp and relaxing air.”", "하지만 아무리 그래도 이건 분명 축축하고 사람 늘어지게 하는 공기 탓이야.”"]], [["Margaret felt as if a thin cold cloud had come between her and the sun.", "마거릿은 자기와 해 사이에 얇고 차가운 구름 한 조각이 끼어든 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그녀는 어머니가 속을 털어놓는 것이 조금이나마 위로가 되기를 바라며 참을성 있게 들었다."], ["She had listened patiently, in hopes that it might be some relief to her mother to unburden herself; but now it was time to draw her back to Mr. Lennox.", "그러나 이제는 어머니를 레녹스 씨에게로 돌려세울 때였다."]], [["“Papa likes Mr. Lennox; they got on together famously at the wedding breakfast.", "“아버지도 레녹스 씨를 좋아하시잖아요."], ["I daresay his coming will do papa good.", "혼례 피로연에서 두 분이 아주 잘 어울리셨는걸요."], ["And never mind the dinner, dear mamma.", "그이가 온 것이 아버지께도 도움이 될 거예요."], ["Cold meat will do capitally for a lunch, which is the light in which Mr.", "그리고 만찬은 걱정 마세요, 어머니. 찬 고기는 점심으로는 아주 훌륭해요."], ["Lennox will most likely look upon a two o’clock dinner.”", "레녹스 씨도 2시 만찬을 십중팔구 점심으로 여기실 거고요.”"]], [["“But what are we to do with him till then?", "“하지만 그때까지 그이를 어떻게 하니?"], ["It is only half-past ten now.”", "이제 겨우 10시 반인데.”"]], [["“I’ll ask him to go out sketching with me.", "“제가 함께 사생하러 나가자고 청할게요."], ["I know he draws, and that will take him out of your way, mamma.", "그이가 그림을 그리는 걸 알거든요. 그러면 어머니께 거치적거리지도 않을 거예요."], ["Only do come in now; he will think it so strange if you don’t.”", "그러니 지금은 들어가 주세요. 안 그러시면 그이가 아주 이상하게 여길 테니까요.”"]], [["Mrs. Hale took off her black silk apron, and smoothed her face.", "헤일 부인은 검은 비단 앞치마를 벗고 얼굴을 폈다."], ["She looked a very pretty lady-like woman, as she greeted Mr. Lennox with the cordiality due to one who was almost a relation.", "거의 친척이나 다름없는 사람에게 걸맞은 다정함으로 레녹스 씨를 맞이하는 그녀는 무척이나 어여쁘고 기품 있는 부인으로 보였다."], ["He evidently expected to be asked to spend the day, and accepted the invitation with a glad readiness that made Mrs.", "그는 하루를 함께 지내자는 청을 받으리라 여기는 눈치였고, 어찌나 반갑게 선뜻 그 청을 받아들이던지 헤일 부인은 찬 쇠고기에 무엇이라도 하나 보탤 수 있으면 좋겠다는 생각이 들 정도였다."], ["Hale wish she could add something to the cold beef.", "그는 무엇에나 만족했다."], ["He was pleased with everything; delighted with Margaret’s idea of going out sketching together; would not have Mr. Hale disturbed for the world, with the prospect of so soon meeting him at dinner.", "함께 사생하러 나가자는 마거릿의 생각을 아주 반가워했고, 곧 만찬에서 뵐 텐데 헤일 씨를 성가시게 해 드릴 수는 없다고 했다."], ["Margaret brought out her drawing materials for him to choose from; and after the paper and brushes had been duly selected, the two set out in the merriest spirits in the world.", "마거릿은 그가 고를 수 있도록 화구를 내왔고, 종이와 붓을 제대로 고른 뒤 두 사람은 더없이 유쾌한 기분으로 길을 나섰다."]], [["“Now, please, just stop here for a minute or two,”", "“자, 여기서 잠깐만 서 주세요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“These are the cottages that haunted me so during the rainy fortnight, reproaching me for not having sketched them.”", "“비 오던 두 주 내내 저를 따라다니며 왜 자기들을 그리지 않느냐고 나무라던 오두막이 바로 이것들이거든요.”"]], [["“Before they tumbled down and were no more seen.", "“무너져 내려 자취도 없어지기 전에 말이지요."], ["Truly, if they are to be sketched—and they are very picturesque—we had better not put it off till next year.", "정말이지 그릴 작정이라면, 게다가 아주 그림 같기도 하니, 내년으로 미루지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["But where shall we sit?”", "그런데 어디에 앉지요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["You might have come straight from chambers in the Temple, instead of having been two months in the Highlands!", "두 달을 하일랜드에서 지내신 게 아니라 템플의 사무실에서 곧장 나오신 것 같으시네요."], ["Look at this beautiful trunk of a tree, which the woodcutters have left just in the right place for the light.", "이 아름다운 나무둥치를 보세요. 나무꾼들이 빛이 꼭 알맞게 드는 자리에 남겨 두었잖아요."], ["I will put my plaid over it, and it will be a regular forest throne.”", "제가 격자무늬 담요를 그 위에 덮을 테니, 어엿한 숲속의 옥좌가 될 거예요.”"]], [["“With your feet in that puddle for a regal footstool!", "“발은 저 웅덩이에 담근 채로 왕좌의 발판을 삼으시고요!"], ["Stay, I will move, and then you can come nearer this way.", "잠깐, 제가 옮길 테니 이쪽으로 더 가까이 오시지요."], ["Who lives in these cottages?”", "이 오두막에는 누가 삽니까?”"]], [["“They were built by squatters fifty or sixty years ago.", "“50, 60년 전에 무단 정착민들이 지은 것들이에요. 하나는 비어 있고요."], ["One is uninhabited; the foresters are going to take it down, as soon as the other is dead, poor old fellow!", "산림지기들이 다른 쪽 사람이 세상을 뜨는 대로 헐어 낼 거래요. 가엾은 노인이지요."], ["Look—there he is—I must go and speak to him.", "보세요, 저기 계시네요. 가서 인사드리고 와야겠어요."], ["He is so deaf you will hear all our secrets.”", "귀가 어찌나 어두우신지 우리 비밀 이야기도 다 들으실 거예요.”"]], [["The old man stood bareheaded in the sun, leaning on his stick at the front of his cottage.", "노인은 오두막 앞에서 지팡이에 기댄 채 맨머리로 볕을 쬐고 서 있었다."], ["His stiff features relaxed into a slow smile as Margaret went up and spoke to him. Mr.", "마거릿이 다가가 말을 건네자 굳어 있던 그의 얼굴이 천천히 미소로 풀렸다."], ["Lennox hastily introduced the two figures into his sketch, and finished up the landscape with a subordinate reference to them—as Margaret perceived, when the time came for getting up, putting away water, and scraps of paper, and exhibiting to each other their sketches.", "레녹스 씨는 서둘러 그 두 사람을 자기 그림 속에 집어넣고는, 풍경은 그들에게 딸린 배경으로 삼아 마무리했다. 자리에서 일어나 물과 종이 부스러기를 치우고 서로의 그림을 보여 줄 때가 되어서야 마거릿은 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["She laughed and blushed: Mr. Lennox watched her countenance.", "그녀는 웃으며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 레녹스 씨는 그 표정을 지켜보았다."]], [["“Now, I call that treacherous,” said she.", "“이건 정말 배신이라고 해야겠어요.”"], ["“I little thought that you were making old Isaac and me into subjects, when you told me to ask him the history of these cottages.”", "그녀가 말했다. “저더러 저 오두막의 내력을 여쭈어보라고 하셨을 때, 늙은 아이작과 저를 그림감으로 삼고 계신 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐거든요.”"]], [["“It was irresistible.", "“도저히 참을 수가 없었습니다."], ["You can’t know how strong a temptation it was.", "그 유혹이 얼마나 강했는지는 모르실 겁니다."], ["I hardly dare tell you how much I shall like this sketch.”", "제가 이 그림을 얼마나 아낄지는 감히 말씀드리지도 못하겠군요.”"]], [["He was not quite sure whether she heard this latter sentence before she went to the brook to wash her palette.", "그녀가 팔레트를 씻으러 개울로 가기 전에 이 뒷말을 들었는지 그로서는 확신할 수 없었다."], ["She came back rather flushed, but looking perfectly innocent and unconscious.", "그녀는 얼굴이 다소 상기된 채 돌아왔으나 더없이 천진하고 아무것도 모르는 표정이었다."], ["He was glad of it, for the speech had slipped from him unawares—a rare thing in the case of a man who premeditated his actions so much as Henry Lennox.", "그는 그것이 다행스러웠다. 그 말이 자기도 모르게 새어 나왔기 때문인데, 헨리 레녹스처럼 자기 행동을 미리 재어 두는 사람에게는 드문 일이었다."]], [["The aspect of home was all right and bright when they reached it.", "두 사람이 돌아왔을 때 집안 풍경은 아무 탈 없이 환했다."], ["The clouds on her mother’s brow had cleared off under the propitious influence of a brace of carp, most opportunely presented by a neighbour. Mr.", "어머니의 이마에 끼어 있던 구름은 이웃이 더없이 때맞추어 보내온 잉어 두 마리 덕에 말끔히 걷혀 있었다."], ["Hale had returned from his morning’s round, and was awaiting his visitor just outside the wicket gate that led into the garden.", "헤일 씨는 아침 순방에서 돌아와, 정원으로 이어지는 쪽문 바로 바깥에서 손님을 기다리고 있었다. 다소 낡은 상의에 오래 쓴 모자 차림이었지만 그는 영락없는 신사로 보였다."], ["He looked a complete gentleman in his rather threadbare coat and well-worn hat.", "마거릿은 아버지가 자랑스러웠다. 아버지가 낯선 사람마다 얼마나 좋은 인상을 남기는지 볼 때마다 그녀는 늘 새롭고 애틋한 자부심을 느꼈다."], ["Margaret was proud of her father; she had always a fresh and tender pride in seeing how favourably he impressed every stranger; still her quick eye sought over his face and found there traces of some unusual disturbance, which was only put aside, not cleared away.", "그러면서도 그녀의 빠른 눈은 아버지의 얼굴을 훑어 여느 때와 다른 어떤 동요의 자취를 찾아냈다. 걷힌 것이 아니라 그저 한쪽으로 밀쳐 둔 동요였다."]], [["Mr. Hale asked to look at their sketches.", "헤일 씨가 두 사람의 그림을 보여 달라고 청했다."]], [["“I think you have made the tints on the thatch too dark, have you not?”", "“초가지붕 빛깔을 너무 어둡게 낸 것 같구나, 안 그러니?”"], ["as he returned Margaret’s to her, and held out his hand for Mr.", "그는 마거릿의 그림을 돌려주며 그렇게 말하고는 레녹스 씨의 그림을 향해 손을 내밀었다."], ["Lennox’s, which was withheld from him one moment, no more.", "그 그림은 잠시, 아주 잠시 건네지지 않았다."]], [["“No, papa! I don’t think I have.", "“아니에요, 아버지."], ["The house-leek and stone-crop have grown so much darker in the rain.", "저는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요. 지붕에 난 바위솔과 돌나물이 비에 젖어 훨씬 짙어졌는걸요."], ["Is it not like, papa?”", "닮지 않았나요, 아버지?”"], ["said she, peeping over his shoulder, as he looked at the figures in Mr. Lennox’s drawing.", "그녀는 레녹스 씨의 그림 속 인물들을 들여다보는 아버지의 어깨 너머로 고개를 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, very like.", "“그래, 아주 닮았구나."], ["Your figure and way of holding yourself is capital.", "네 몸매하며 서 있는 자세가 일품이야."], ["And it is just poor old Isaac’s stiff way of stooping his long rheumatic back.", "그리고 이건 딱 가엾은 늙은 아이작이 류머티즘 든 기다란 등을 뻣뻣하게 구부리는 그 모양이고."], ["What is this hanging from the branch of the tree?", "나뭇가지에 매달린 이건 무어냐?"], ["Not a bird’s nest, surely.”", "설마 새 둥지는 아닐 테고.”"]], [["“Oh no! that is my bonnet.", "“어머, 아니에요!"], ["I never can draw with my bonnet on; it makes my head so hot.", "그건 제 보닛이에요. 저는 보닛을 쓰고는 도무지 그림을 못 그리겠어요."], ["I wonder if I could manage figures.", "머리가 너무 더워지거든요."], ["There are so many people about here whom I should like to sketch.”", "제가 인물도 그릴 수 있으려나 모르겠어요. 이 근방에는 그려 보고 싶은 사람이 참 많은데요.”"]], [["“I should say that a likeness you very much wish to take you would always succeed in,”", "“꼭 그리고 싶은 사람의 모습이라면 언제든 성공하실 것 같은데요.”"], ["said Mr. Lennox.", "레녹스 씨가 말했다."], ["“I have great faith in the power of will.", "“저는 의지의 힘을 아주 믿습니다."], ["I think myself I have succeeded pretty well in yours.” Mr.", "제 딴에는 아가씨를 그리는 데 제법 성공했다고 생각하고요.”"], ["Hale had preceded them into the house, while Margaret was lingering to pluck some roses, with which to adorn her morning gown for dinner.", "헤일 씨는 먼저 집으로 들어갔고, 마거릿은 만찬 때 아침 나들이옷에 꽂을 장미를 몇 송이 꺾느라 뒤에 남았다."]], [["“A regular London girl would understand the implied meaning of that speech,”", "‘여느 런던 아가씨라면 그 말에 숨은 뜻을 알아들었을 텐데.’ 레녹스 씨는 생각했다. ‘젊은 남자가 건네는 말마다 그 속에 찬사의 저의가 없는지 낱낱이 들여다보았을 텐데."], ["thought Mr. Lennox.", "하지만 마거릿은 아무래도……."], ["“She would be up to looking through every speech that a young man made her for the arrière-pensée of a compliment.", "잠깐만요!”"], ["But I don’t believe Margaret,—Stay!”", "그가 소리쳤다. “제가 도와드리지요.”"], ["exclaimed he, “Let me help you”; and he gathered for her some velvety cramoisy roses that were above her reach, and then dividing the spoil he placed two in his button-hole, and sent her in, pleased and happy, to arrange her flowers.", "그러고는 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 핀 벨벳 같은 진홍빛 장미를 몇 송이 꺾어 주었고, 그런 다음 그 전리품을 나누어 두 송이를 자기 단춧구멍에 꽂고는 흐뭇하고 즐거워하는 그녀를 꽃 꽂으러 안으로 들여보냈다."]], [["The conversation at dinner flowed on quietly and agreeably.", "만찬 자리의 대화는 조용하고 기분 좋게 흘러갔다."], ["There were plenty of questions to be asked on both sides—the latest intelligence which each could give of Mrs.", "양쪽 다 물을 것이 많았고, 이탈리아에 있는 쇼 부인의 소식 가운데 저마다 아는 가장 최근의 것을 주고받았다."], ["Shaw’s movements in Italy to be exchanged; and in the interest of what was said, the unpretending simplicity of the parsonage-ways—above all in the neighbourhood of Margaret, Mr. Lennox forgot the little feeling of disappointment with which he had at first perceived that she had spoken but the simple truth when she had described her father’s living as very small.", "오가는 이야기가 재미있었고 목사관의 소박하고 꾸밈없는 살림살이도 그러했으며, 무엇보다 마거릿이 곁에 있었으므로 레녹스 씨는 아버지의 성직록이 아주 적다던 그녀의 말이 조금의 과장도 없는 사실임을 처음 알아차렸을 때 느꼈던 자그마한 실망을 잊어버렸다."]], [["“Margaret, my child, you might have gathered us some pears for our dessert,”", "“마거릿아, 후식으로 배를 좀 따 왔더라면 좋았을 것을.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, as the hospitable luxury of a freshly-decanted bottle of wine was placed on the table.", "갓 병에서 옮겨 따른 포도주가 손님을 대접하는 호사로 탁자에 놓이자 헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Hale was hurried.", "헤일 부인은 마음이 급해졌다."], ["It seemed as if desserts were impromptu and unusual things at the parsonage; whereas, if Mr.", "목사관에서는 후식이 즉흥적이고 흔치 않은 것이기라도 한 듯한 말투였다."], ["Hale would only have looked behind him, he would have seen biscuits and marmalade, and what not, all arranged in formal order on the side-board.", "그러나 헤일 씨가 뒤만 돌아보았더라면 비스킷과 마멀레이드며 이것저것이 찬장 위에 격식대로 가지런히 차려진 것을 보았을 것이다."], ["But the idea of pears had taken possession of Mr. Hale’s mind, and was not to be got rid of.", "그러나 배 생각이 헤일 씨의 머리를 사로잡아 좀처럼 떨어지지 않았다."]], [["“There are a few brown beurrés against the south wall which are worth all foreign fruits and preserves.", "“남쪽 담벼락에 뵈레 배가 몇 알 익어 있는데, 그건 외국 과일이며 설탕절임을 다 합친 것보다 낫지."], ["Run, Margaret, and gather some.”", "어서 가서 좀 따 오려무나, 마거릿.”"]], [["“I propose that we adjourn into the garden, and eat them there,”", "“정원으로 자리를 옮겨 거기서 먹자고 제안하고 싶군요.”"], ["said Mr. Lennox.", "레녹스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Nothing is so delicious as to set one’s teeth into the crisp, juicy fruit, warm and scented by the sun.", "“햇볕에 데워져 향기가 밴 아삭하고 물 많은 과일에 이를 박아 넣는 것만큼 맛있는 일도 없지요."], ["The worst is, the wasps are impudent enough to dispute it with one, even at the very crisis and summit of enjoyment.”", "다만 곤란한 건 말벌들이 뻔뻔하게도 그 즐거움이 절정에 이른 바로 그 순간에까지 달려들어 다툰다는 겁니다.”"]], [["He rose, as if to follow Margaret, who had disappeared through the window: he only awaited Mrs.", "그는 창문 너머로 사라진 마거릿을 따라나서려는 듯 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["Hale’s permission.", "헤일 부인의 허락만 기다릴 뿐이었다."], ["She would rather have wound up the dinner in the proper way, and with all the ceremonies which had gone on so smoothly hitherto, especially as she and Dixon had got out the finger-glasses from the store-room on purpose to be as correct as became General Shaw’s widow’s sister; but as Mr.", "그녀로서는 만찬을 격식대로, 여태 그토록 매끄럽게 이어져 온 온갖 절차를 갖추어 마무리하고 싶었다. 더구나 쇼 장군 미망인의 동생답게 제대로 차리려고 딕슨과 함께 일부러 곳간에서 손 씻는 그릇까지 꺼내 놓은 참이었으니 말이다."], ["Hale got up directly, and prepared to accompany his guest, she could only submit.", "그러나 헤일 씨가 곧바로 일어나 손님을 따라나설 채비를 하니 그녀로서는 따르는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“I shall arm myself with a knife,” said Mr.", "“나는 칼로 무장을 해야겠네.”"], ["Hale: “the days of eating fruit so primitively as you describe are over with me.", "헤일 씨가 말했다. “자네가 말한 것처럼 그리 원시적으로 과일을 먹던 시절은 내게는 다 지나갔지."], ["I must pare it and quarter it before I can enjoy it.”", "껍질을 벗기고 네 쪽으로 갈라야 비로소 맛을 볼 수 있다네.”"]], [["Margaret made a plate for the pears out of a beet-root leaf, which threw up their brown gold colour admirably. Mr.", "마거릿은 비트 잎으로 배를 담을 접시를 만들었는데, 그 잎이 배의 갈색 도는 금빛을 더없이 돋보이게 했다."], ["Lennox looked more at her than at the pears; but her father, inclined to cull fastidiously the very zest and perfection of the hour he had stolen from his anxiety, chose daintily the ripest fruit, and sat down on the garden bench to enjoy it at his leisure.", "레녹스 씨는 배보다 그녀를 더 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀의 아버지는 근심에서 훔쳐 낸 이 한때의 묘미와 완벽함을 까다롭게 골라내고 싶은 마음에 가장 잘 익은 것을 정성 들여 고르고는 정원 벤치에 앉아 느긋하게 즐겼다."], ["Margaret and Mr.", "마거릿과 레녹스 씨는 남쪽 담벼락 아래 자그마한 테라스 길을 따라 거닐었다."], ["Lennox strolled along the little terrace-walk under the south wall, where the bees still hummed and worked busily in their hives.", "그곳에서는 벌들이 여전히 벌통 속에서 붕붕대며 부지런히 일하고 있었다."]], [["“What a perfect life you seem to live here!", "“여기서 얼마나 완벽한 삶을 사시는지요!"], ["I have always felt rather contemptuously towards the poets before, with their wishes, ‘Mine be a cot beside a hill,’ and that sort of thing; but now I am afraid that the truth is, I have been nothing better than a Cockney.", "저는 그동안 시인들을, 그 ‘내게는 언덕 곁의 오두막 한 채를’ 같은 소망을 다소 업신여겨 왔습니다. 그런데 이제는 아무래도 제가 도회지 물만 든 촌뜨기였을 뿐이라는 게 사실인 듯싶어 두렵군요."], ["Just now I feel as if twenty years’ hard study of law would be amply rewarded by one year of such an exquisite serene life as this—such skies!”", "지금 당장은 20년간 법을 고되게 파고든 대가로 이토록 더없이 맑고 고요한 삶을 1년만 누릴 수 있다면 넉넉히 값을 받은 셈이겠다 싶습니다. 이런 하늘이라니!”"], ["looking up—“such crimson and amber foliage, so perfectly motionless as that!”", "그는 고개를 들며 말했다. “저토록 진홍과 호박빛으로 물든 잎사귀들이, 저리도 미동조차 없다니!”"], ["pointing to some of the great forest trees which shut in the garden as if it were a nest.", "그러면서 마치 둥지처럼 정원을 에워싼 커다란 숲의 나무 몇 그루를 가리켰다."]], [["“You must please to remember that our skies are not always as deep a blue as they are now.", "“우리 하늘이 늘 지금처럼 짙푸르지는 않다는 것도 기억해 주세요."], ["We have rain, and our leaves do fall, and get sodden: though I think Helstone is about as perfect a place as any in the world.", "비도 오고, 나뭇잎도 떨어져 흠뻑 젖는걸요. 그래도 헬스톤은 세상 어느 곳 못지않게 완벽한 데라고 생각하지만요."], ["Recollect how you rather scorned my description of it one evening in Harley Street: ‘a village in a tale.’”", "할리 가에서 어느 날 저녁 제 이야기를 다소 업신여기셨던 걸 떠올려 보세요. ‘이야기 속의 마을’이라고 하셨죠.”"]], [["“Scorned, Margaret!", "“업신여기다니요, 마거릿 양!"], ["That is rather a hard word.”", "그건 좀 매몰찬 말인데요.”"]], [["“Perhaps it is.", "“그럴지도 모르죠. 다만 그때 저는 마음 가득한 것을 선생님께 이야기하고 싶었는데, 선생님은……"], ["Only I know I should have liked to have talked to you of what I was very full at the time, and you—what must I call it then?—spoke disrespectfully of Helstone as a mere village in a tale.”", "그럼 무어라고 해야 하나요? 헬스톤을 그저 이야기 속의 마을이라며 대수롭잖게 말씀하셨죠.”"]], [["“I will never do so again,”", "“다시는 그러지 않겠습니다.”"], ["said he, warmly.", "그가 다정하게 말했다."], ["They turned the corner of the walk.", "두 사람은 길모퉁이를 돌았다."]], [["“I could almost wish, Margaret——”", "“저로서는 이런 소원이라도 품고 싶은 심정입니다, 마거릿 양……” 그가 말을 멈추고 머뭇거렸다."], ["he stopped and hesitated.", "말 잘하는 이 변호사가 머뭇거리는 일은 하도 드문 터라 마거릿은 조금 어리둥절해하며 그를 올려다보았다."], ["It was so unusual for the fluent lawyer to hesitate that Margaret looked up at him, in a little state of questioning wonder; but in an instant—from what about him she could not tell—she wished herself back with her mother—her father—anywhere away from him, for she was sure he was going to say something to which she should not know what to reply.", "그러나 그 순간, 그의 무엇을 보고 그랬는지는 스스로도 알 수 없었으나, 그녀는 어머니 곁으로, 아버지 곁으로, 그가 없는 어디로든 돌아가고 싶어졌다. 그가 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없는 말을 하려 한다는 것을 그녀는 확신했던 것이다."], ["In another moment the strong pride that was in her came to conquer her sudden agitation, which she hoped he had not perceived.", "다음 순간 그녀 안에 있던 강한 자존심이 그 갑작스러운 동요를 눌러 이겼다. 그가 그것을 알아차리지 못했기를 바라면서."], ["Of course she could answer, and answer the right thing; and it was poor and despicable of her to shrink from hearing any speech, as if she had not power to put an end to it with her high maidenly dignity.", "물론 그녀는 대답할 수 있고, 옳은 대답을 할 수 있다. 처녀다운 드높은 품위로 능히 끝맺을 수 있는 말을 듣기가 두려워 움츠러든다면 그것이야말로 옹졸하고 못난 짓이었다."]], [["“Margaret,”", "“마거릿 양.”"], ["said he, taking her by surprise, and getting sudden possession of her hand, so that she was forced to stand still and listen, despising herself for the fluttering at her heart all the time; “Margaret, I wish you did not like Helstone so much—did not seem so perfectly calm and happy here.", "그가 불시에 그녀의 손을 덥석 잡으며 말했으므로 그녀는 가만히 서서 들을 수밖에 없었고, 그러는 내내 두근거리는 제 가슴이 스스로 못마땅했다. “마거릿 양, 나는 아가씨가 헬스톤을 이토록 좋아하지 않았더라면, 여기서 이토록 더없이 평온하고 행복해 보이지 않았더라면 좋았겠다 싶습니다."], ["I have been hoping for these three months past to find you regretting London—and London friends, a little—enough to make you listen more kindly”", "지난 석 달 동안 나는 아가씨가 런던을, 런던의 벗들을 조금이나마 그리워하기를 바라 왔습니다. 내놓을 것이 많지 않은 사람의 말을, 사실 앞날의 전망 말고는 아무것도 없는 사람의 말을 조금 더 다정하게 들어 줄 만큼은 말이지요.”"], ["(for she was quietly, but firmly, striving to extricate her hand from his grasp) “to one who has not much to offer, it is true—nothing but prospects in the future—but who does love you, Margaret, almost in spite of himself.", "그녀가 조용히, 그러나 단호하게 그의 손아귀에서 제 손을 빼내려 애쓰고 있었으므로 그는 이어 말했다. “그러나 아가씨를 사랑하는 사람입니다, 마거릿 양."], ["Margaret, have I startled you too much?", "제 자신도 어쩌지 못할 만큼요. 마거릿 양, 내가 너무 놀라게 했습니까?"], ["Speak!”", "말씀을 해 보세요!”"], ["For he saw her lips quivering almost as if she were going to cry.", "그녀의 입술이 곧 울음이라도 터뜨릴 듯 떨리는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["She made a strong effort to be calm; she would not speak till she had succeeded in mastering her voice, and then she said:", "그녀는 애써 마음을 가라앉혔다. 목소리를 다잡기 전에는 입을 열지 않으려 했고, 이윽고 말했다."]], [["“I was startled.", "“놀랐어요."], ["I did not know that you cared for me in that way.", "저를 그런 식으로 마음에 두고 계신 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["I have always thought of you as a friend; and, please, I would rather go on thinking of you so.", "저는 늘 선생님을 벗으로 생각해 왔고, 부디 앞으로도 그렇게 생각하고 싶어요."], ["I don’t like to be spoken to as you have been doing.", "방금 하신 것 같은 말씀을 듣는 건 싫어요."], ["I cannot answer you as you want me to do, and yet I should feel so sorry if I vexed you.”", "바라시는 대로 대답해 드릴 수가 없어요. 그러면서도 제가 선생님 마음을 언짢게 해 드렸다면 몹시 죄송할 거예요.”"]], [["“Margaret,”", "“마거릿 양.”"], ["said he, looking into her eyes, which met his with their open, straight look, expressive of the utmost good faith and reluctance to give pain, “Do you”—he was going to say—“love any one else?”", "그가 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다. 그 눈은 더없는 진심과 남을 아프게 하기 싫어하는 마음을 담아 툭 트인 곧은 눈길로 그의 눈을 마주 보았다."], ["But it seemed as if this question would be an insult to the pure serenity of those eyes.", "“혹시” 하고 그는 “다른 누군가를 사랑하십니까?”라고 물으려 했다."], ["“Forgive me! I have been too abrupt.", "그러나 그 물음은 저 맑고 고요한 눈에 대한 모욕이 될 것만 같았다."], ["I am punished.", "“용서하십시오!"], ["Only let me hope.", "내가 너무 성급했습니다. 벌을 받는 셈이지요."], ["Give me the poor comfort of telling me you have never seen any one whom you could——”", "다만 바랄 수 있게만은 해 주십시오. 여태 그럴 만한 사람을 한 번도 본 적이 없다고 말해 주는 그 가엾은 위안만이라도……”"], ["Again a pause.", "다시 말이 끊겼다."], ["He could not end his sentence.", "그는 문장을 맺지 못했다."], ["Margaret reproached herself acutely as the cause of his distress.", "마거릿은 그의 괴로움의 원인이 자기라는 데 대해 뼈아프게 자책했다."]], [["“Ah! if you had but never got this fancy into your head!", "“아, 이런 마음을 아예 품지 않으셨더라면!"], ["It was such a pleasure to think of you as a friend.”", "선생님을 벗으로 생각하는 것이 그렇게나 큰 즐거움이었는데요.”"]], [["“But I may hope, may I not, Margaret, that some time you will think of me as a lover? Not yet, I see—there is no hurry—but some time——”", "“하지만 바랄 수는 있겠지요, 마거릿 양. 언젠가는 나를 연인으로 생각해 주시리라고요. 지금은 아니라는 걸 압니다. 서두를 것 없지요. 그러나 언젠가는……”"]], [["She was silent for a minute or two, trying to discover the truth as it was in her own heart, before replying, then she said:", "그녀는 대답하기 전에 자기 마음속에 있는 진실을 찾아보려 애쓰며 1, 2분 말이 없었다. 그러고는 말했다."]], [["“I have never thought of—you, but as a friend.", "“저는 선생님을 벗 말고 다르게 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없어요."], ["I like to think of you so; but I am sure I could never think of you as anything else.", "그렇게 생각하는 것이 좋고요. 하지만 그 밖의 무엇으로도 결코 생각할 수 없으리라는 것을 확신해요."], ["Pray let us both forget that all this” (“disagreeable,”", "부디 우리 둘 다 이 모든”"], ["she was going to say, but stopped short) “conversation has taken place.”", "그녀는 “불쾌한”이라고 말하려다가 딱 멈추었다. “대화가 있었다는 것을 잊기로 해요.”"]], [["He paused before he replied.", "그는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 대답했다."], ["Then, in his habitual coldness of tone, he answered:", "그러고는 여느 때의 그 차가운 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Of course, as your feelings are so decided, and as this conversation has been so evidently unpleasant to you, it had better not be remembered.", "“물론이지요. 아가씨 마음이 그렇게 확고하고 이 대화가 아가씨께 그토록 뚜렷이 불쾌한 것이었다면, 기억하지 않는 편이 나을 겁니다."], ["That is all very fine in theory, that plan of forgetting whatever is painful, but it will be somewhat difficult for me, at least, to carry it into execution.”", "괴로운 것은 무엇이든 잊는다는 그 방책은 이론으로는 아주 그럴듯하지요. 하지만 적어도 제게는 실행에 옮기기가 다소 어려울 것 같군요.”"]], [["“You are vexed,”", "“마음이 상하셨군요.”"], ["said she, sadly; “yet how can I help it?”", "그녀가 서글프게 말했다. “하지만 저더러 어쩌라는 말씀이신가요?”"]], [["She looked so truly grieved as she said this, that he struggled for a moment with his real disappointment, and then answered more cheerfully, but still with a little hardness in his tone:", "이렇게 말하는 그녀가 어찌나 진심으로 슬퍼 보이는지, 그는 잠시 제 진짜 실망과 씨름하고는 한결 밝은 목소리로, 그러나 여전히 어조에 조금 모난 데를 남긴 채 대답했다."]], [["“You should make allowances for the mortification, not only of a lover, Margaret, but of a man not given to romance in general—prudent, worldly, as some people call me—who has been carried out of his usual habits by the force of a passion—well, we will say no more of that; but in the one outlet which he has formed for the deeper and better feelings of his nature, he meets with rejection and repulse. I shall have to console myself with scorning my own folly. A struggling barrister to think of matrimony!”", "“이 굴욕을 좀 헤아려 주셔야 합니다, 마거릿 양. 한 연인의 굴욕만이 아니라 본디 낭만과는 거리가 먼 사람의, 남들이 신중하다느니 세속적이라느니 하는 사람의 굴욕 말입니다. 그런 사람이 정념의 힘에 떠밀려 제 평소 습관 바깥으로 끌려 나왔지요. 뭐, 그 이야기는 그만두지요. 다만 제 천성의 더 깊고 더 나은 감정을 위해 스스로 낸 그 하나뿐인 물꼬에서 거절과 퇴짜를 맞은 겁니다. 제 어리석음을 비웃는 것으로 스스로를 달래는 수밖에요. 앞길이 고달픈 법정 변호사 주제에 혼인을 생각하다니!”"]], [["Margaret could not answer this.", "마거릿은 이 말에 대답할 수 없었다."], ["The whole tone of it annoyed her.", "그 어조 전체가 그녀를 언짢게 했다."], ["It seemed to touch on and call out all the points of difference which had often repelled her in him; while yet he was the pleasantest man, the most sympathising friend, the person of all others who understood her best in Harley Street.", "그것은 그에게서 종종 그녀를 밀어내곤 하던 차이의 지점들을 죄다 건드려 불러내는 듯했다. 그러면서도 그는 할리 가에서 가장 유쾌한 남자였고, 가장 마음을 헤아려 주는 벗이었으며, 누구보다 그녀를 잘 이해하는 사람이었다."], ["She felt a tinge of contempt mingle itself with her pain at having refused him.", "그녀는 그를 거절한 데서 오는 아픔에 한 자락 경멸이 섞여 드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Her beautiful lip curled in a slight disdain.", "그 아름다운 입술이 옅은 멸시로 살짝 비틀렸다."], ["It was well that, having made the round of the garden, they came suddenly upon Mr.", "다행히도 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아온 두 사람은 헤일 씨와 불쑥 마주쳤다."], ["Hale, whose whereabouts had been quite forgotten by them.", "그가 어디 있는지는 두 사람의 머릿속에서 까맣게 잊혀 있었다."], ["He had not yet finished the pear, which he had delicately peeled in one long strip of silver-paper thinness, and which he was enjoying in a deliberate manner.", "그는 은박지처럼 얇게 한 줄로 정성껏 벗겨 낸 그 배를 아직 다 먹지 못하고 느긋하게 음미하는 중이었다."], ["It was like the story of the eastern king, who dipped his head into a basin of water, at the magician’s command, and ere he instantly took it out went through the experience of a lifetime.", "그것은 마법사가 시키는 대로 물동이에 머리를 담갔다가 미처 빼기도 전에 한평생의 일을 다 겪었다는 동방의 왕 이야기와도 같았다."], ["Margaret felt stunned, and unable to recover her self-possession enough to join in the trivial conversation that ensued between her father and Mr. Lennox.", "마거릿은 얼떨떨했고, 아버지와 레녹스 씨 사이에 이어지는 시시한 대화에 낄 만큼 마음을 추스르지 못했다."], ["She was grave, and little disposed to speak; full of wonder when Mr.", "그녀는 심각한 얼굴로 좀처럼 말이 없었다."], ["Lennox would go, and allow her to relax into thought on the events of the last quarter of an hour.", "레녹스 씨가 언제쯤 가서 지난 15분간의 일을 마음 놓고 곱씹게 해 줄까 하는 생각만 가득했다."], ["He was almost as anxious to take his departure as she was for him to leave; but a few minutes light and careless talking, carried on at whatever effort, was a sacrifice which he owed to his mortified vanity, or his self-respect.", "그 또한 그녀가 그가 떠나기를 바라는 만큼이나 떠나고 싶어 안달이었다. 그러나 아무리 애를 써서라도 몇 분쯤 가볍고 대수롭잖은 이야기를 이어 가는 것은 상처 입은 허영심에, 혹은 자존심에 그가 치러야 할 값이었다."], ["He glanced from time to time at her sad and pensive face.", "그는 이따금 그녀의 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 얼굴을 흘깃거렸다."]], [["“I am not so indifferent to her as she believes,” thought he to himself.", "‘저 사람이 여기는 것만큼 내가 저 사람에게 아무렇지 않은 존재는 아니지.’ 그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["“I do not give up hope.”", "‘나는 희망을 버리지 않겠어.’"]], [["Before a quarter of an hour was over, he had fallen into a way of conversing with quiet sarcasm; speaking of life in London and life in the country, as if he were conscious of his second mocking self, and afraid of his own satire.", "15분이 채 지나기도 전에 그는 조용한 비아냥을 섞어 이야기하는 투로 빠져들어 있었다. 마치 자신을 비웃는 또 하나의 자아를 의식하면서도 제 풍자가 두렵기라도 한 듯 런던의 삶과 시골의 삶을 논했다."], ["Mr. Hale was puzzled.", "헤일 씨는 어리둥절했다."], ["His visitor was a different man to what he had seen him before at the wedding-breakfast, and at dinner to-day; a lighter, cleverer, more worldly man, and, as such, dissonant to Mr.", "이 손님은 전에 혼례 피로연에서, 또 그날 만찬 자리에서 보았던 사람과는 다른 사람이었다."], ["Hale. It was a relief to all three when Mr.", "더 가볍고 더 약삭빠르고 더 세속적인 사람이었으며, 그런 만큼 헤일 씨와는 잘 맞지 않았다."], ["Lennox said that he must go directly if he meant to catch the five o’clock train.", "레녹스 씨가 5시 기차를 타려면 곧장 떠나야겠다고 말했을 때 그것은 세 사람 모두에게 안도였다."], ["They proceeded to the house to find Mrs. Hale, and wish her good-bye.", "그들은 헤일 부인을 찾아 작별을 고하러 집으로 향했다."], ["At the last moment, Henry Lennox’s real self broke through the crust.", "마지막 순간, 헨리 레녹스의 진짜 자아가 그 껍데기를 뚫고 나왔다."]], [["“Margaret, don’t despise me; I have a heart, notwithstanding all this good-for-nothing way of talking.", "“마거릿 양, 나를 업신여기지 마십시오. 이런 시답잖은 말투로 지껄이기는 했어도 내게도 마음이란 게 있습니다."], ["As a proof of it, I believe I love you more than ever—if I do not hate you—for the disdain with which you have listened to me during this last half-hour.", "그 증거로, 지난 30분 동안 아가씨가 나를 멸시하며 들어 준 그 태도 때문에 나는 아가씨를 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑하는 것 같습니다. 미워하는 게 아니라면 말이지요."], ["Good-bye, Margaret—Margaret!”", "안녕히 계십시오, 마거릿 양…… 마거릿 양!”"]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# 제4장"]], [["“Cast me upon some naked shore, Where I may tracke Only the print of some sad wracke, If thou be there though the seas roare, I shall no gentler calm implore.”", "“나를 헐벗은 어느 물가에 내던지라, 거기서 내가 더듬을 것이라고는 어느 슬픈 난파의 자취뿐일지라도, 그대만 그곳에 있다면 바다가 울부짖는들 나는 더 부드러운 고요를 구하지 않으리.”"], ["HABINGTON.", "— 해빙턴."]], [["He was gone.", "그는 떠났다."], ["The house was shut up for the evening.", "집은 저녁을 맞아 문을 닫아걸었다."], ["No more deep blue skies or crimson and amber tints.", "짙푸른 하늘도, 진홍과 호박빛 노을도 이제 없었다."], ["Margaret went up to dress for the early tea, finding Dixon in a pretty temper from the interruption which a visitor had naturally occasioned on a busy day.", "마거릿은 이른 차 시간에 맞춰 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갔다가, 바쁜 날에 손님이 찾아온 탓에 일이 끊겨 딕슨이 잔뜩 심통이 나 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She showed it by brushing away viciously at Margaret’s hair, under pretence of being in a great hurry to go to Mrs. Hale.", "딕슨은 헤일 부인에게 급히 가 봐야 한다는 핑계를 대며 마거릿의 머리를 사납게 빗어 대는 것으로 그 심사를 드러냈다."], ["Yet, after all, Margaret had to wait a long time in the drawing-room before her mother came down.", "그러나 정작 마거릿은 어머니가 내려오기까지 응접실에서 한참을 기다려야 했다."], ["She sat by herself at the fire, with unlighted candles on the table behind her, thinking over the day, the happy walk, happy sketching, cheerful pleasant dinner, and the uncomfortable, miserable walk in the garden.", "그녀는 뒤쪽 탁자에 불 켜지 않은 초를 둔 채 난롯가에 홀로 앉아 그날 하루를 곱씹었다. 즐거웠던 산책과 즐거웠던 그림 그리기, 유쾌하고 흐뭇했던 저녁 식사, 그리고 정원에서의 그 거북하고 참담했던 산책을."]], [["How different men were to women!", "남자와 여자가 어쩌면 이리도 다를까!"], ["Here was she disturbed and unhappy, because her instinct had made anything but a refusal impossible; while he, not many minutes after he had met with a rejection of what ought to have been the deepest, holiest proposal of his life, could speak as if briefs, success, and all its superficial consequences of a good house, clever and agreeable society, were the sole avowed objects of his desires.", "여기 이쪽에서는 본능이 거절 말고는 어떤 대답도 할 수 없게 만들었다는 이유로 그녀가 마음이 어지러워 괴로워하고 있는데, 저쪽에서는 그가 제 삶에서 가장 깊고 가장 거룩한 청혼이었어야 할 것을 거절당한 지 몇 분도 지나지 않아, 사건 수임과 성공과 그것에 딸려 오는 온갖 겉치레—번듯한 집과 영리하고 유쾌한 교제—가 자기 욕망의 유일하게 내세울 만한 목표인 양 말할 수 있었다. 아, 이런!"], ["Oh dear!", "그가 조금만 달랐더라면 그녀는 그를 얼마나 사랑할 수 있었을까."], ["how she could have loved him if he had but been different, with a difference which she felt, on reflection, to be one that went low—deep down.", "그런데 돌이켜 생각해 보니 그 다름이란 밑바닥까지, 아주 깊은 데까지 닿는 것이었다. 그러다가 문득 이런 생각이 들었다."], ["Then she took it into her head that, after all, his lightness might be but assumed, to cover a bitterness of disappointment which would have been stamped on her own heart if she had loved and been rejected.", "어쩌면 그 가벼움은 꾸며 낸 것이고, 그 아래에는 쓰디쓴 실망이 감추어져 있는지도 모른다고. 만약 그녀가 사랑했다가 거절당했더라면 바로 그런 실망이 그녀 가슴에 낙인처럼 찍혔을 터였다."]], [["Her mother came into the room before this whirl of thoughts was adjusted into anything like order.", "이 회오리치는 생각들이 그럴듯한 갈피를 잡기도 전에 어머니가 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Margaret had to shake off the recollections of what had been done and said through the day, and turn a sympathising listener to the account of how Dixon had complained that the ironing-blanket had been burnt again; and how Susan Lightfoot had been seen with artificial flowers in her bonnet, thereby giving evidence of a vain and giddy character.", "마거릿은 그날 있었던 일과 오간 말들에 대한 기억을 떨쳐 내고, 딕슨이 다림질용 천이 또 눌어붙었다고 투덜대더라는 이야기며, 수전 라이트풋이 보닛에 조화를 달고 다니는 것이 눈에 띄었으니 그것이야말로 허영스럽고 들뜬 성품의 증거라는 이야기에 맞장구를 치는 다정한 청자가 되어야 했다."], ["Mr. Hale sipped his tea in abstracted silence; Margaret had the responses all to herself.", "헤일 씨는 넋을 놓은 채 말없이 차를 홀짝였고, 대꾸는 오롯이 마거릿의 몫이었다."], ["She wondered how her father and mother could be so forgetful, so regardless of their companion through the day, as never to mention his name.", "그녀는 아버지와 어머니가 어찌 이토록 잊어버릴 수 있는지, 하루를 함께 보낸 사람을 이토록 아랑곳하지 않고 이름 한 번 입에 올리지 않을 수 있는지 의아했다."], ["She forgot that he had not made them an offer.", "그가 두 분께 청혼한 것은 아니라는 사실을 그녀는 잊고 있었다."]], [["After tea Mr. Hale got up, and stood with his elbow on the chimney-piece, leaning his head on his hand, musing over something, and from time to time sighing deeply.", "차를 마신 뒤 헤일 씨는 자리에서 일어나 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 얹고 손에 머리를 괸 채 무언가를 골똘히 생각하며 서 있었고, 이따금 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Mrs. Hale went out to consult with Dixon about some winter clothing for the poor.", "헤일 부인은 가난한 이들에게 줄 겨울옷 문제로 딕슨과 의논하러 나갔다."], ["Margaret was preparing her mother’s worsted work, and rather shrinking from the thought of the long evening, and wishing bed-time were come that she might go over the events of the day again.", "마거릿은 어머니의 털실 자수감을 챙겨 놓으며 긴 저녁 시간을 생각하니 좀 움츠러들었고, 어서 잠자리에 들 시간이 되어 그날 있었던 일들을 다시 되짚어 볼 수 있기를 바랐다."]], [["“Margaret!” said Mr.", "“마거릿!”"], ["Hale, at last, in a sort of sudden desperate way, that made her start.", "마침내 헤일 씨가 말했다. 어딘지 갑작스럽고 절박한 투여서 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["“Is that tapestry thing of immediate consequence?", "“그 자수 일이 당장 급한 것이냐?"], ["I mean, can you leave it and come into my study?", "그러니까, 그건 두고 내 서재로 좀 올 수 있겠니?"], ["I want to speak to you about something very serious to us all.”", "우리 모두에게 아주 중대한 일을 이야기하고 싶구나.”"]], [["“Very serious to us all.” Mr.", "“우리 모두에게 아주 중대한 일.”"], ["Lennox had never had the opportunity of having any private conversation with her father after her refusal, or else that would indeed be a very serious affair.", "레녹스 씨는 그녀가 거절한 뒤로 아버지와 단둘이 이야기할 기회가 전혀 없었다. 그럴 기회가 있었다면 그야말로 아주 중대한 일이 되었을 것이다."], ["In the first place, Margaret felt guilty and ashamed of having grown so much into a woman as to be thought of in marriage; and secondly, she did not know if her father might not be displeased that she had taken upon herself to decline Mr.", "우선 마거릿은 자기가 혼인 이야기가 오갈 만큼 여자로 자랐다는 것이 죄스럽고 부끄러웠다. 그리고 또 하나, 아버지가 자기 마음대로 레녹스 씨의 청혼을 물리친 것을 언짢아하시지는 않을까 알 수 없었다."], ["Lennox’s proposal. But she soon felt it was not about anything, which having only lately and suddenly occurred, could have given rise to any complicated thoughts, that her father wished to speak to her.", "그러나 아버지가 그녀에게 하려는 말이 최근에 갑자기 벌어져 이런저런 복잡한 생각을 불러일으켰을 만한 그런 일에 관한 것이 아님을 그녀는 곧 알아차렸다."], ["He made her take a chair by him; he stirred the fire, snuffed the candles, and sighed once or twice before he could make up his mind to say—and it came out with a jerk after all—“Margaret!", "아버지는 그녀를 자기 곁 의자에 앉히고는 불을 뒤적이고 촛불의 심지를 자르고 한두 번 한숨을 쉬고 나서야 겨우 말을 꺼낼 결심을 했는데, 그나마도 툭 튀어나오듯 나왔다. “마거릿!"], ["I am going to leave Helstone.”", "나는 헬스톤을 떠나려 한다.”"]], [["“Leave Helstone, papa!", "“헬스톤을 떠나신다니요, 아버지!"], ["But why?”", "어째서요?”"]], [["Mr. Hale did not answer for a minute or two.", "헤일 씨는 한두 마디 동안 대답하지 않았다."], ["He played with some papers on the table in a nervous and confused manner, opening his lips to speak several times, but closing them again without having the courage to utter a word.", "그는 초조하고 어수선한 몸짓으로 탁자 위의 서류를 만지작거리며 몇 번이나 말하려고 입을 열었다가는 한마디도 내뱉을 용기가 나지 않아 다시 다물었다."], ["Margaret could not bear the sight of the suspense, which was even more distressing to her father than to herself.", "마거릿은 그 애타는 기다림을 차마 보고 있을 수 없었다. 그것은 그녀보다 아버지에게 더 괴로운 일이었다."]], [["“But why, dear papa?", "“어째서요, 아버지?"], ["Do tell me!”", "어서 말씀해 주세요!”"]], [["He looked up at her suddenly, and then said with a slow and enforced calmness,", "그는 문득 그녀를 올려다보더니, 느리고 억지로 자아낸 침착함으로 말했다."]], [["“Because I must no longer be a minister in the Church of England.”", "“내가 더는 국교회의 성직자로 남을 수 없기 때문이다.”"]], [["Margaret had imagined nothing less than that some of the preferments which her mother had so much desired had befallen her father at last—something that would force him to leave beautiful, beloved Helstone, and perhaps compel him to go and live in some of the stately and silent Closes which Margaret had seen from time to time in Cathedral towns.", "마거릿은 어머니가 그토록 바라던 승진 가운데 하나가 마침내 아버지께 내려왔으려니 하고만 생각했었다. 그렇다면 아름답고 사랑하는 헬스톤을 떠나야 할 테고, 어쩌면 마거릿이 이따금 대성당이 있는 도시에서 보았던 그 위엄 있고 고요한 경내 관사 어딘가로 가서 살아야 할지도 몰랐다."], ["They were grand and imposing places, but if, to go there, it was necessary to leave Helstone as a home for ever, that would have been a sad, long, lingering pain.", "웅장하고 당당한 곳이기는 했으나 그리로 가기 위해 헬스톤을 삶터로서 영영 떠나야 한다면, 그것은 슬프고 길게 끄는 아픔이었을 것이다."], ["But nothing to the shock she received from Mr. Hale’s last speech.", "그러나 헤일 씨의 마지막 말이 안긴 충격에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["What could he mean?", "무슨 뜻일까?"], ["It was all the worse for being so mysterious.", "알 수 없다는 점이 더욱 나빴다."], ["The aspect of piteous distress on his face, almost imploring a merciful and kind judgment from his child, gave her a sudden sickening.", "자식에게서 자비롭고 너그러운 판단을 구걸하다시피 하는 그 애처롭고 괴로운 표정에 그녀는 갑자기 속이 메스꺼워졌다."], ["Could he have become implicated in anything Frederick had done?", "아버지가 프레더릭이 저지른 일에 얽혀 든 것일까?"], ["Frederick was an outlaw.", "프레더릭은 법 밖의 사람이었다."], ["Had her father, out of a natural love for his son, connived at any—", "아버지가 아들에 대한 당연한 사랑으로 무언가를 눈감아 주기라도……."]], [["“Oh! what is it?", "“아, 무슨 일이에요?"], ["do speak, papa!", "말씀해 주세요, 아버지!"], ["tell me all!", "다 말씀해 주세요!"], ["Why can you no longer be a clergyman?", "어째서 더는 성직자로 계실 수 없다는 거예요?"], ["Surely, if the bishop were told all we know about Frederick, and the hard unjust—”", "프레더릭에 대해 우리가 아는 것을 전부, 그 모질고 부당한 처사를 주교님께 말씀드리기만 하면…….”"]], [["“It is nothing about Frederick; the bishop would have nothing to do with that.", "“프레더릭과는 아무 상관 없는 일이다. 주교님도 그 일과는 아무 관계가 없어."], ["It is all myself.", "온전히 나 자신의 문제야."], ["Margaret, I will tell you about it.", "마거릿, 내가 다 이야기해 주마."], ["I will answer any questions this once, but after to-night let us never speak of it again.", "이번 한 번은 무엇이든 물어보는 대로 대답해 주겠다만, 오늘 밤이 지나면 다시는 그 이야기를 하지 말자꾸나."], ["I can meet the consequences of my painful, miserable doubts; but it is an effort beyond me to speak of what has caused me so much suffering.”", "나는 이 괴롭고 참담한 회의가 불러올 결과를 감당할 수 있다. 그러나 나를 이토록 괴롭혀 온 그 원인을 입에 올리는 것은 내 힘에 부치는 일이구나.”"]], [["“Doubts, papa!", "“회의라니요, 아버지!"], ["Doubts as to religion?”", "신앙에 대한 회의 말씀이세요?”"], ["asked Margaret, more shocked than ever.", "마거릿이 그 어느 때보다 큰 충격을 받고 물었다."]], [["“No!", "“아니다!"], ["not doubts as to religion; not the slightest injury to that.”", "신앙에 대한 회의는 아니야. 거기에는 티끌만 한 손상도 없다.”"]], [["He paused.", "그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["Margaret sighed, as if standing on the verge of some new horror.", "마거릿은 어떤 새로운 공포의 벼랑 끝에 선 사람처럼 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["He began again, speaking rapidly, as if to get over a set task:", "그가 정해진 과업을 서둘러 해치우려는 듯 빠르게 말을 이었다."]], [["“You could not understand it all, if I told you—my anxiety, for years past, to know whether I had any right to hold my living—my efforts to quench my smouldering doubts by the authority of the Church.", "“내가 말한들 너는 다 이해하지 못할 게다. 내가 내 성직록을 지닐 자격이 있기는 한가를 알아내려고 여러 해 동안 애태워 온 것도, 교회의 권위로 내 속에서 타들어 가는 의심을 꺼 보려고 애써 온 것도."], ["Oh! Margaret, how I love the holy Church from which I am to be shut out!”", "아, 마거릿, 이제 내가 쫓겨나야 할 그 거룩한 교회를 나는 얼마나 사랑하는지!”"], ["He could not go on for a moment or two.", "그는 한동안 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["Margaret could not tell what to say; it seemed to her as terribly mysterious as if her father were about to turn Mahometan.", "마거릿은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀에게는 아버지가 마호메트교로 개종하겠다는 말이라도 하는 것처럼 무섭고 알 수 없는 일로 여겨졌다."]], [["“I have been reading to-day of the two thousand who were ejected from their churches,”—continued Mr. Hale, smiling faintly,—“trying to steal some of their bravery; but it is of no use—no use—I cannot help feeling it acutely.”", "“오늘은 제 교회에서 쫓겨난 2,000명에 대해 읽고 있었다.” 헤일 씨가 희미하게 웃으며 말을 이었다. “그이들의 용기를 조금이나마 훔쳐 보려고 말이다. 그러나 소용이 없구나. 소용이 없어. 사무치게 아픈 것을 어쩔 수가 없다.”"]], [["“But, papa, have you well considered?", "“하지만 아버지, 깊이 헤아려 보셨어요?"], ["Oh! it seems so terrible, so shocking,”", "아, 너무나 끔찍하고 너무나 놀라운 일이에요.”"], ["said Margaret, suddenly bursting into tears.", "마거릿이 이렇게 말하며 갑자기 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The one staid foundation of her home, of her idea of her beloved father, seemed reeling and rocking.", "그녀의 집을 떠받치던, 사랑하는 아버지에 대한 그녀의 생각을 떠받치던 그 든든한 주춧돌이 휘청거리며 흔들리는 듯했다."], ["What could she say?", "무슨 말을 할 수 있을까?"], ["What was to be done?", "무엇을 해야 할까?"], ["The sight of her distress made Mr. Hale nerve himself, in order to try and comfort her.", "딸의 괴로워하는 모습을 보자 헤일 씨는 그녀를 달래 보려고 마음을 다잡았다."], ["He swallowed down the dry choking sobs which had been heaving up from his heart hitherto, and going to his bookcase he took down a volume, which he had often been reading lately, and from which he thought he had derived strength to enter upon the course in which he was now embarked.", "그는 여태 가슴에서 치밀어 오르던 마른 흐느낌을 삼키고는 책장으로 가서 요즈음 자주 읽던 책을 한 권 꺼내 들었다. 지금 자기가 들어선 이 길로 나아갈 힘을 그 책에서 얻었다고 그는 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“Listen, dear Margaret,”", "“들어 보렴, 마거릿.”"], ["said he, putting one arm round her waist.", "그가 한 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감싸며 말했다."], ["She took his hand in hers and grasped it tight, but she could not lift up her head; nor indeed could she attend to what he read, so great was her internal agitation.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡아 꼭 쥐었지만 고개를 들 수는 없었다. 속이 어찌나 요동치는지 그가 읽는 것에 마음을 기울일 수도 없었다."]], [["“This is the soliloquy of one who was once a clergyman in a country parish, like me; it was written by Mr.", "“이것은 나처럼 시골 교구의 성직자였던 사람의 독백이다."], ["Oldfield, minister of Carsington, in Derbyshire, a hundred and sixty years ago, or more.", "160여 년 전에 더비셔 카싱턴의 목사였던 올드필드 씨가 쓴 것이지."], ["His trials are over.", "그분의 시련은 끝났다."], ["He fought the good fight.”", "그분은 선한 싸움을 싸우셨어.”"], ["These last two sentences he spoke low, as if to himself.", "이 마지막 두 문장은 혼잣말하듯 나직이 말했다."], ["Then he read aloud,—", "그러고는 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“When thou canst no longer continue in thy work without dishonour to God, discredit to religion, foregoing thy integrity, wounding conscience, spoiling thy peace, and hazarding the loss of thy salvation; in a word, when the conditions upon which thou must continue (if thou wilt continue) in thy employments are sinful, and unwarranted by the word of God, thou mayest, yea, thou must believe that God will turn thy very silence, suspension, deprivation, and laying aside, to His glory, and the advancement of the Gospel’s interest.", "“하나님께 욕을 돌리지 않고, 신앙을 더럽히지 않고, 네 성실을 저버리지 않고, 양심을 다치게 하지 않고, 네 평안을 망치지 않고, 네 구원을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 네 일을 더 이어 갈 수 없을 때, 한마디로 네가 그 직분을 계속하려면 지켜야 할 조건이 죄스럽고 하나님의 말씀에 어긋날 때, 너는 하나님께서 바로 그 침묵과 정직(停職)과 박탈과 물러남을 당신의 영광과 복음의 유익을 위해 돌리시리라 믿어도 좋으며, 아니 반드시 믿어야 하리라."], ["When God will not use thee in one kind, yet He will in another.", "하나님께서 너를 한 가지 방식으로 쓰지 않으신다면 다른 방식으로 쓰시리라."], ["A soul that desires to serve and honour Him shall never want opportunity to do it; nor must thou so limit the Holy One of Israel, as to think He hath but one way in which He can glorify Himself by thee.", "그분을 섬기고 높이기를 바라는 영혼은 결코 그럴 기회가 없지 않으리라. 또한 너는 이스라엘의 거룩하신 이를 좁혀 놓아, 그분께서 너를 통해 스스로를 영화롭게 하실 길이 오직 하나뿐이라고 여겨서는 안 되리라."], ["He can do it by thy silence as well as by thy preaching; the laying aside as well as thy continuance in thy work.", "그분은 네 설교로써 하시듯 네 침묵으로써도 하실 수 있고, 네가 일을 이어 감으로써 하시듯 네가 물러남으로써도 하실 수 있느니라."], ["It is not pretence of doing God the greatest service, or performing the weightiest duty, that will excuse the least sin, though that sin capacitated or gave us the opportunity for doing that duty.", "하나님께 가장 큰 봉사를 드린다거나 가장 무거운 의무를 다한다는 구실로는 아무리 작은 죄라도 변명되지 않으리니, 설령 그 죄가 그 의무를 행할 힘이나 기회를 우리에게 주었다 하여도 그러하니라."], ["Thou wilt have little thanks, O my soul! if, when thou art charged with corrupting God’s worship, falsifying thy vows, thou pretendest a necessity for it in order to a continuance in the ministry.”", "오 내 영혼아, 네가 하나님의 예배를 더럽히고 네 서원을 저버렸다는 책망을 들을 때, 성직에 머무르기 위해 그리할 수밖에 없었노라 둘러댄다면 너에게 돌아올 감사는 거의 없으리라.”"]], [["As he read this, and glanced at much more which he did not read, he gained resolution for himself, and felt as if he too could be brave and firm in doing what he believed to be right; but as he ceased he heard Margaret’s low convulsive sob; and his courage sank down under the keen sense of suffering.", "이 대목을 읽고, 또 읽지 않은 훨씬 많은 대목에 눈길을 주는 사이 그는 스스로 결심이 굳어져, 자기도 옳다고 믿는 바를 행하는 데 용감하고 굳셀 수 있을 것 같은 기분이 들었다. 그러나 읽기를 마치자 마거릿의 나직하고 경련하는 듯한 흐느낌이 들려왔고, 그 사무치는 고통 앞에서 그의 용기는 도로 무너져 내렸다."]], [["“Margaret, dear!”", "“마거릿, 얘야!” 그가 딸을 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["said he, drawing her closer, “think of the early martyrs; think of the thousands who have suffered.”", "“초기의 순교자들을 생각해 보렴. 고난을 겪은 수천의 사람들을 생각해 보아라.”"]], [["“But, father,”", "“하지만 아버지.”"], ["said she, suddenly lifting up her flushed, tear-wet face, “the early martyrs suffered for the truth, while you—oh!", "그녀가 상기되고 눈물에 젖은 얼굴을 갑자기 들며 말했다. “초기의 순교자들은 진리를 위해 고난을 받았어요."], ["dear, dear papa!”", "그런데 아버지는……. 아, 아버지, 아버지!”"]], [["“I suffer for conscience’ sake, my child,”", "“나는 양심을 위해 고난을 받는 것이란다, 얘야.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, with a dignity that was only tremulous from the acute sensitiveness of his character; “I must do what my conscience bids.", "그 목소리에는 위엄이 있었으나 예민한 성품 탓에 떨림이 섞여 있었다. “나는 내 양심이 시키는 대로 해야 한다."], ["I have borne long with self-reproach that would have roused any mind less torpid and cowardly than mine.”", "나만큼 무디고 비겁하지 않은 마음이었다면 진작 떨쳐 일어났을 그런 자책을 나는 오래도 견뎌 왔구나.” 그는 말을 이으며 고개를 내저었다."], ["He shook his head as he went on.", "“네 가엾은 어머니가 애타게 바라던 것이, 지나치게 애타는 소망이 흔히 그러하듯 비웃는 방식으로 마침내 이루어졌다. 소돔의 사과 같은 것이지."], ["“Your poor mother’s fond wish, gratified at last in the mocking way in which over-fond wishes are too often fulfilled—Sodom apples as they are—has brought on this crisis, for which I ought to be, and I hope I am thankful.", "그 일이 이 고비를 불러왔고, 나는 그것을 감사해야 마땅하며 또 감사하고 있다고 믿는다. 주교님이 내게 다른 성직록을 내려 주신 것이 한 달도 되지 않았다."], ["It is not a month since the bishop offered me another living; if I had accepted it, I should have had to make a fresh declaration of conformity to the Liturgy at my institution.", "내가 그것을 받았다면 취임할 때 기도서에 대한 순종을 새로 서약해야 했을 게다. 마거릿, 나는 그렇게 해 보려고 애썼다."], ["Margaret, I tried to do it; I tried to content myself with simply refusing the additional preferment, and stopping quietly here,—strangling my conscience now, as I had strained it before.", "그저 그 승진만 사양하고 여기 조용히 머무는 것으로 스스로를 다독여 보려고 했지. 전에 내 양심을 억눌러 왔듯이 이번에도 목을 졸라 가면서 말이다."], ["God forgive me!”", "하나님, 나를 용서하소서!”"]], [["He rose and walked up and down the room, speaking low words of self-reproach and humiliation, of which Margaret was thankful to hear but few.", "그는 일어나 방 안을 오가며 자책과 부끄러움의 말을 나직이 되뇌었는데, 마거릿은 그 가운데 몇 마디만 들려온 것을 다행으로 여겼다."], ["At last he said,", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“Margaret, I return to the old sad burden: we must leave Helstone.”", "“마거릿, 다시 그 오래고 슬픈 짐으로 돌아가자꾸나. 우리는 헬스톤을 떠나야 한다.”"]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["I see.", "알겠어요."], ["But when?”", "그런데 언제요?”"]], [["“I have written to the bishop—I dare say I have told you so, but I forget things just now,”", "“주교님께 편지를 썼다. 아마 네게 말한 것 같기도 한데, 요즘은 자꾸 잊어버리는구나.”"], ["said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 이렇게 말했다."], ["Hale, collapsing into his depressed manner as soon as he came to talk of hard matter-of-fact details, “informing him of my intention to resign this vicarage.", "딱딱한 사실을 하나하나 이야기하려니 이내 그 풀 죽은 태도로 주저앉아 버렸다. “이 교구 목사직을 그만두겠다는 뜻을 알려 드린 게지."], ["He has been most kind; he has used arguments and expostulations, all in vain—in vain.", "주교님은 더할 나위 없이 인자하셨다. 이런저런 논리로 타이르고 말리셨지만 다 헛일이었다."], ["They are but what I have tried upon myself, without avail.", "헛일이었어. 그건 내가 이미 나 자신에게 써 보았다가 아무 소용 없었던 것들뿐이니까."], ["I shall have to take my deed of resignation, and wait upon the bishop myself, to bid him farewell.", "이제 사임서를 들고 주교님을 직접 찾아뵙고 작별 인사를 드려야 한다."], ["That will be a trial.", "그것도 시련이겠지."], ["But worse, far worse, will be the parting from my dear people.", "그러나 그보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더한 것은 사랑하는 교구민들과 헤어지는 일이다."], ["There is a curate appointed to read prayers—a Mr.", "예배를 맡을 부목사가 정해졌다. 브라운이라는 분이야."], ["Brown. He will come to stay with us to-morrow.", "내일 우리 집에 와서 머물게 될 게다."], ["Next Sunday I preach my farewell sermon.”", "다음 주일에 나는 고별 설교를 한다.”"]], [["Was it to be so sudden then?", "이렇게 갑작스러운 일이었단 말인가, 하고 마거릿은 생각했다."], ["thought Margaret; and yet perhaps it was as well.", "그러나 어쩌면 그편이 나을지도 몰랐다."], ["Lingering would only add stings to the pain; it was better to be stunned into numbness by hearing of all these arrangements, which seemed to be nearly completed before she had been told.", "질질 끄는 것은 아픔에 가시만 더할 뿐이었다. 자기에게 알려지기도 전에 거의 다 끝나 있는 듯한 이 모든 채비를 한꺼번에 듣고 얼얼하게 마비되는 편이 나았다."], ["“What does mamma say?”", "“어머니는 뭐라고 하세요?”"], ["asked she, with a deep sigh.", "그녀가 깊은 한숨을 쉬며 물었다."]], [["To her surprise, her father began to walk about again before he answered.", "뜻밖에도 아버지는 대답하기 전에 다시 방 안을 서성이기 시작했다."], ["At length he stopped and replied:", "마침내 그가 걸음을 멈추고 대답했다."]], [["“Margaret, I am a poor coward after all.", "“마거릿, 나는 어차피 못난 겁쟁이란다."], ["I cannot bear to give pain.", "남에게 아픔을 주는 것을 견디지 못해."], ["I know so well your mother’s married life has not been all she hoped—all she had a right to expect—and this will be such a blow to her, that I have never had the heart, the power to tell her.", "네 어머니의 결혼 생활이 그이가 바라던 대로가, 마땅히 기대할 권리가 있었던 대로가 되지 못했다는 것을 나는 너무나 잘 안다. 그러니 이 일은 그이에게 큰 타격일 텐데, 나는 도무지 말할 마음도, 힘도 나지 않았다."], ["She must be told though, now,”", "그래도 이제는 알려야겠지.”"], ["said he, looking wistfully at his daughter.", "그가 딸을 안쓰럽게 바라보며 말했다."], ["Margaret was almost overpowered with the idea that her mother knew nothing of it all, and yet the affair was so far advanced!", "마거릿은 어머니가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는데 일이 이토록 진척되었다는 생각에 거의 넋이 나갈 지경이었다."]], [["“Yes, indeed she must,”", "“네, 정말 알려 드려야 해요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“Perhaps, after all, she may not—Oh yes!", "“어쩌면 어머니도 그리…….. 아, 아니에요!"], ["she will, she must be shocked”—as the force of the blow returned upon herself in trying to realise how another would take it.", "어머니도 놀라실 거예요, 놀라실 수밖에 없어요.” 남이라면 이 일을 어떻게 받아들일까 헤아려 보려는 사이 그 충격이 도로 자기에게 밀려온 것이었다."], ["“Where are we to go to?”", "“우리 어디로 가나요?”"], ["said she at last, struck with a fresh wonder as to their future plans, if plans indeed her father had.", "마침내 그녀가 물었다. 앞으로의 계획이, 아버지에게 계획이라는 것이 있기나 하다면, 새삼 궁금해졌던 것이다."]], [["“To Milton-Northern,”", "“밀턴노던으로 간다.”"], ["he answered, with a dull indifference, for he had perceived that, although his daughter’s love had made her cling to him, and for a moment strive to soothe him with her love, yet the keenness of the pain was as fresh as ever in her mind.", "그가 무덤덤한 무관심으로 대답했다. 딸의 사랑이 자기에게 매달리게 하고 잠시나마 그 사랑으로 자기를 다독이려 애쓰게 했음에도, 그 아픔의 날카로움은 딸의 마음속에 여전히 처음처럼 생생하다는 것을 그는 알아차렸던 것이다."]], [["“Milton-Northern!", "“밀턴노던이라니요!"], ["The manufacturing town in Darkshire?”", "다크셔에 있는 그 공업 도시요?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["said he, in the same despondent, indifferent way.", "그가 여전히 풀 죽은 무심한 투로 말했다."]], [["“Why there, papa?”", "“왜 하필 그곳인가요, 아버지?”"], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Because there I can earn bread for my family.", "“거기서라면 식구들 먹을 빵을 벌 수 있으니까."], ["Because I know no one there, and no one knows Helstone, or can ever talk to me about it.”", "거기에는 내가 아는 사람이 하나도 없고, 헬스톤을 아는 사람도, 그 이야기를 내게 꺼낼 사람도 없을 테니까.”"]], [["“Bread for your family!", "“식구들 먹을 빵이라니요!"], ["I thought you and mamma had”—and then she stopped, checking her natural interest regarding their future life, as she saw the gathering gloom on her father’s brow.", "저는 아버지와 어머니께 있는 줄로…….” 그러다 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."], ["But he, with his quick intuitive sympathy, read in her face, as in a mirror, the reflections of his own moody depression, and turned it off with an effort.", "아버지의 이마에 어둠이 모여드는 것을 보고 앞날의 살림에 대한 당연한 관심을 접은 것이다. 그러나 그는 특유의 빠르고 직관적인 공감으로 딸의 얼굴에서 거울에 비치듯 자기 자신의 침울한 우울이 되비친 것을 읽어 내고는 애써 그것을 털어 냈다."]], [["“You shall be told all, Margaret.", "“다 이야기해 주마, 마거릿."], ["Only help me to tell your mother.", "다만 네 어머니께 알리는 일만 좀 도와다오."], ["I think I could do anything but that: the idea of her distress turns me sick with dread.", "그것 말고는 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것 같은데, 그이가 괴로워할 생각만 하면 두려움에 속이 메스꺼워진다."], ["If I tell you all, perhaps you could break it to her to-morrow.", "내가 다 이야기해 주면 네가 내일 그이에게 조심스레 알릴 수 있지 않겠니."], ["I am going out for the day, to bid farmer Dobson and the poor people on Bracy Common good-bye.", "나는 하루 종일 나가 있을 참이다. 도브슨 농부와 브레이시 커먼의 가난한 사람들에게 작별 인사를 하러 말이야."], ["Would you dislike breaking it to her very much, Margaret?”", "그 말을 꺼내는 것이 아주 싫으냐, 마거릿?”"]], [["Margaret did dislike it, did shrink from it more than from anything she had ever had to do in her life before.", "마거릿은 정말로 그것이 싫었고, 지금껏 살아오며 해야 했던 그 어떤 일보다 더 움츠러들었다."], ["She could not speak, all at once.", "그녀는 얼른 말이 나오지 않았다."], ["Her father said, “You dislike it very much don’t you, Margaret?”", "아버지가 말했다. “아주 싫은 게로구나, 마거릿?”"], ["Then she conquered herself, and said, with a bright strong look on her face:", "그러자 그녀는 스스로를 이겨 내고 얼굴에 밝고 굳센 빛을 띠며 말했다."]], [["“It is a painful thing, but it must be done, and I will do it as well as ever I can.", "“괴로운 일이지만 해야 할 일이잖아요. 제가 할 수 있는 한 잘해 볼게요."], ["You must have many painful things to do.”", "아버지야말로 괴로운 일을 많이 하셔야 할 텐데요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale shook his head despondingly: he pressed her hand in token of gratitude.", "헤일 씨는 낙담한 듯 고개를 저었다."], ["Margaret was nearly upset again into a burst of crying.", "그러고는 고마움의 표시로 딸의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 마거릿은 하마터면 다시 울음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다."], ["To turn her thoughts, she said: “Now tell me, papa, what our plans are.", "생각을 돌리려고 그녀가 말했다. “이제 말씀해 주세요, 아버지."], ["You and mamma have some money, independent of the income from the living, have you not?", "우리 계획이 어떻게 되는지요. 아버지와 어머니께는 성직록에서 나오는 수입 말고도 따로 돈이 좀 있으시죠?"], ["Aunt Shaw has, I know.”", "쇼 이모께는 있다는 걸 알거든요.”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["I suppose we have about a hundred and twenty pounds a year of our own.", "우리 몫으로 한 해에 120파운드쯤 되지 싶다."], ["Seventy of that has always gone to Frederick, since he has been abroad.", "그중 70파운드는 프레더릭이 외국에 나간 뒤로 늘 그 아이에게 갔다."], ["I don’t know if he wants it all,”", "그 아이가 그걸 다 필요로 하는지는 모르겠구나.”"], ["he continued in a hesitating manner.", "그가 머뭇거리며 말을 이었다."], ["“He must have some pay for serving with the Spanish army.”", "“스페인 군대에서 복무하니 봉급이 좀 있기는 할 텐데.”"]], [["“Frederick must not suffer,”", "“프레더릭 오빠가 아쉬워서는 안 돼요.”"], ["said Margaret, decidedly; “in a foreign country; so unjustly treated by his own.", "마거릿이 단호히 말했다. “낯선 나라에서, 제 나라에서 그토록 부당한 대우를 받고 있는데요."], ["A hundred is left.", "그러면 100파운드가 남네요."], ["Could not you, and I, and mamma live on a hundred a year in some very cheap—very quiet part of England?", "아버지와 저와 어머니가 잉글랜드 어딘가 아주 값싸고 아주 조용한 곳에서 한 해 100파운드로 살 수는 없을까요?"], ["Oh! I think we could.”", "아, 저는 할 수 있을 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“No!” said Mr.", "“안 된다!”"], ["Hale. “That would not answer.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["I must do something!", "“그건 안 될 말이야."], ["I must make myself busy, to keep off morbid thoughts.", "나는 무언가를 해야 한다! 병든 생각을 물리치려면 나를 바쁘게 만들어야 해."], ["Besides, in a country parish I should be so painfully reminded of Helstone, and my duties here.", "게다가 시골 교구에 있으면 헬스톤과 여기서의 내 소임이 사무치게 떠오를 거다."], ["I could not bear it, Margaret.", "나는 그걸 견딜 수 없어, 마거릿."], ["And a hundred a year would go a very little way, after the necessary wants of housekeeping are met, towards providing your mother with all the comforts she has been accustomed to, and ought to have.", "그리고 한 해 100파운드로는 살림에 꼭 드는 것을 대고 나면, 네 어머니가 여태 익숙해져 있고 또 마땅히 누려야 할 온갖 편안함을 마련해 드리기에 턱없이 모자란다."], ["No: we must go to Milton.", "아니야, 우리는 밀턴으로 가야 한다."], ["That is settled.", "그건 정해진 일이다."], ["I can always decide better by myself, and not influenced by those whom I love,”", "나는 사랑하는 이들에게 휘둘리지 않고 혼자 있을 때 늘 더 잘 결정하거든.”"], ["said he, as a half apology for having arranged so much before he had told any one of his family of his intentions.", "식구 누구에게도 뜻을 밝히기 전에 이토록 많은 것을 정해 버린 데 대한 반쯤의 변명으로 그가 말했다."], ["“I cannot stand objections.", "“나는 반대를 견디지 못한다."], ["They make me so undecided.”", "반대를 들으면 마음이 도무지 정해지지 않아.”"]], [["Margaret resolved to keep silence.", "마거릿은 잠자코 있기로 마음먹었다."], ["After all, what did it signify where they went, compared to the one terrible change?", "어차피 그 한 가지 무서운 변화에 견주면 어디로 가느냐가 무슨 대수이겠는가?"]], [["Mr. Hale continued: “A few months ago, when my misery of doubt became more than I could bear without speaking, I wrote to Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["Bell—you remember Mr.", "“몇 달 전에, 회의로 인한 괴로움이 말하지 않고는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 커졌을 때 나는 벨 씨에게 편지를 썼다."], ["Bell, Margaret?”", "벨 씨 기억하지, 마거릿?”"]], [["“No; I never saw him, I think.", "“아니요, 뵌 적은 없는 것 같아요."], ["But I know who he is.", "하지만 어떤 분인지는 알아요."], ["Frederick’s godfather—your old tutor at Oxford, don’t you mean?”", "프레더릭 오빠의 대부시고, 옥스퍼드에서 아버지를 가르치신 옛 스승 아니신가요?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["He is a Fellow of Plymouth College there.", "거기 플리머스 칼리지의 펠로시다."], ["He is a native of Milton-Northern, I believe.", "밀턴노던 태생이라고 하더구나."], ["At any rate he has property there, which has very much increased in value since Milton has become such a large manufacturing town.", "어쨌든 거기에 재산이 있는데, 밀턴이 그렇게 큰 공업 도시가 된 뒤로 값이 아주 많이 올랐다지."], ["Well; I had reason to suspect—to imagine—I had better say nothing about it, however.", "그런데 말이다, 나는 짐작 가는 데가 있었어. 아니, 그건 아무 말도 하지 않는 편이 낫겠구나."], ["But I felt sure of sympathy from Mr. Bell.", "아무튼 벨 씨라면 나를 헤아려 주리라고 믿었다."], ["I don’t know that he gave me much strength.", "그분이 내게 큰 힘을 주었는지는 모르겠구나."], ["He has lived an easy life in his college all his days.", "평생을 그 대학에서 편안히 사신 분이니까."], ["But he has been as kind as can be.", "그래도 더할 수 없이 다정하셨다."], ["And it is owing to him we are going to Milton.”", "우리가 밀턴으로 가는 것도 그분 덕이야.”"]], [["“How?”", "“어째서요?”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Why he has tenants, and houses, and mills there; so, though he dislikes the place—too bustling for one of his habits—he is obliged to keep up some sort of connection; and he tells me that he hears there is a good opening for a private tutor there.”", "“그분이 거기 소작인과 집과 공장을 가지고 계시거든. 그래서 그곳을 싫어하시면서도—그분 성미에는 너무 부산스러운 곳이지—어떻게든 연을 이어 갈 수밖에 없으신 게다. 그런데 그분이 그러시더구나, 거기 가정교사 자리가 하나 괜찮게 났다는 이야기를 들었다고.”"]], [["“A private tutor!”", "“가정교사라니요!”"], ["said Margaret, looking scornful: “What in the world do manufacturers want with the classics, or literature, or the accomplishments of a gentleman?”", "마거릿이 경멸 어린 얼굴로 말했다. “공장주들이 도대체 고전이나 문학이나 신사의 소양 같은 게 무슨 소용이 있다고요?”"]], [["“Oh,”", "“아니다.”"], ["said her father, “some of them really seem to be fine fellows, conscious of their own deficiencies, which is more than many a man at Oxford is.", "아버지가 말했다. “그 사람들 중에는 정말로 훌륭한 이들이 있는 모양이더구나."], ["Some want resolutely to learn, though they have come to man’s estate.", "제 부족함을 아는 사람들 말이다. 옥스퍼드의 숱한 사람들보다 나은 셈이지."], ["Some want their children to be better instructed than they themselves have been.", "어떤 이들은 이미 어른이 되었는데도 굳게 배우려 든다더라."], ["At any rate, there is an opening, as I have said, for a private tutor.", "또 어떤 이들은 제 자식만은 자기보다 잘 가르치고 싶어 하고."], ["Mr. Bell has recommended me to a Mr.", "어쨌든 내가 말했듯이 가정교사 자리가 하나 있다."], ["Thornton, a tenant of his, and a very intelligent man, as far as I can judge from his letters.", "벨 씨가 나를 손턴이라는 분에게 소개해 주셨는데, 그분의 소작인이고, 편지로 미루어 판단하기로는 아주 총명한 사람이다."], ["And in Milton, Margaret, I shall find a busy life, if not a happy one, and people and scenes so different that I shall never be reminded of Helstone.”", "그리고 밀턴에서라면, 마거릿, 나는 행복하지는 않더라도 바쁜 삶을 얻을 게다. 사람도 풍경도 워낙 달라서 헬스톤이 떠오를 일은 결코 없을 테고.”"]], [["There was the secret motive, as Margaret knew from her own feelings.", "바로 거기에 숨은 동기가 있었다. 마거릿은 자기 마음으로 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["It would be different.", "그곳은 다를 것이다."], ["Discordant as it was—with almost a detestation for all she had ever heard of the North of England, the manufacturers, the people, the wild and bleak country—there was this one recommendation—it would be different to Helstone, and could never remind them of that beloved place.", "잉글랜드 북부에 대해, 공장주들에 대해, 그곳 사람들에 대해, 거칠고 황량한 고장에 대해 들어 온 모든 것을 거의 혐오하다시피 하는 그녀에게 그것이 아무리 거슬리는 일이라 해도, 거기에는 이 한 가지 이점이 있었다. 그곳은 헬스톤과 다를 것이고, 그 사랑하는 곳을 결코 떠올리게 하지 않으리라는 것."]], [["“When do we go?”", "“우리 언제 가나요?”"], ["asked Margaret, after a short silence.", "잠시 말이 없다가 마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“I do not know exactly.", "“정확히는 모르겠다."], ["I wanted to talk it over with you.", "너와 의논하고 싶었어. 알다시피 네 어머니는 아직 아무것도 모르지 않니."], ["You see, your mother knows nothing about it yet: but I think, in a fortnight;—after my deed of resignation is sent in, I shall have no right to remain.”", "하지만 2주쯤 뒤가 아닐까 싶다. 사임서를 내고 나면 나는 여기 머무를 권리가 없어지니까.”"]], [["Margaret was almost stunned.", "마거릿은 넋이 나갈 지경이었다."]], [["“In a fortnight!”", "“2주 뒤라니요!”"]], [["“No—no, not exactly to a day.", "“아니, 아니다. 날을 딱 잡은 건 아니야."], ["Nothing is fixed,”", "정해진 것은 없다.”"], ["said her father, with anxious hesitation, as he noticed the filmy sorrow that came over her eyes, and the sudden change in her complexion.", "아버지가 근심스레 머뭇거리며 말했다. 딸의 눈에 어리는 뿌연 슬픔과 갑자기 달라진 안색을 알아차린 것이었다."], ["But she recovered herself immediately.", "그러나 그녀는 곧바로 마음을 추슬렀다."]], [["“Yes, papa, it had better be fixed soon and decidedly, as you say.", "“아니에요, 아버지. 말씀하신 대로 어서 확실히 정하는 편이 좋아요."], ["Only mamma to know nothing about it!", "다만 어머니만 아무것도 모르신다니!"], ["It is that that is the great perplexity.”", "그게 정말 난감한 일이에요.”"]], [["“Poor Maria!”", "“가엾은 마리아!”"], ["replied Mr. Hale tenderly.", "헤일 씨가 애틋하게 대답했다."], ["“Poor, poor Maria!", "“가엾고 가엾은 마리아!"], ["Oh, if I were not married—if I were but myself in the world, how easy it would be!", "아, 내가 혼인하지 않았더라면, 이 세상에 나 하나뿐이었다면 얼마나 쉬웠을까! 이러하니……."], ["As it is—Margaret, I dare not tell her!”", "마거릿, 나는 도저히 그이에게 말하지 못하겠구나!”"]], [["“No,”", "“괜찮아요.”"], ["said Margaret, sadly, “I will do it.", "마거릿이 서글프게 말했다. “제가 할게요."], ["Give me till to-morrow evening to choose my time.", "언제가 좋을지 고를 수 있게 내일 저녁까지만 시간을 주세요."], ["Oh, papa,”", "아, 아버지.”"], ["cried she with sudden passionate entreaty, “say—tell me it is a nightmare—a horrid dream—not the real waking truth!", "그녀가 문득 북받쳐 애원하듯 외쳤다. “말씀해 주세요, 이건 악몽이라고, 무서운 꿈일 뿐 깨어 있는 현실이 아니라고 말씀해 주세요!"], ["You cannot mean that you are really going to leave the Church—to give up Helstone—to be for ever separate from me, from mamma—led away by some delusion—some temptation!", "정말로 국교회를 떠나시겠다는 뜻은 아니겠죠. 헬스톤을 버리고, 저와 어머니에게서 영영 갈라서겠다는 뜻은요."], ["You do not really mean it!”", "어떤 망상에, 어떤 유혹에 끌려서 말이에요! 정말로 그러시겠다는 뜻은 아니죠!”"]], [["Mr. Hale sat in rigid stillness while she spoke.", "마거릿이 말하는 동안 헤일 씨는 굳은 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다."]], [["Then he looked her in the face, and said in a slow, hoarse, measured way—“I do mean it, Margaret.", "이윽고 그는 딸의 얼굴을 똑바로 보며 느리고 쉬고 또박또박한 투로 말했다. “정말로 그러겠다는 뜻이다, 마거릿."], ["You must not deceive yourself in doubting the reality of my words—my fixed intention and resolve.”", "내 말이, 내 굳은 뜻과 결심이 참인지를 의심하며 스스로를 속여서는 안 된다.”"], ["He looked at her in the same steady, stony manner, for some moments after he had done speaking.", "말을 마치고도 그는 한동안 그 흔들림 없는 돌 같은 눈길로 딸을 바라보았다."], ["She, too, gazed back with pleading eyes before she would believe it was irrevocable.", "그녀 역시 이것이 되돌릴 수 없는 일임을 믿기 전에 애원하는 눈으로 마주 바라보았다."], ["Then she arose and went, without another word or look, towards the door.", "그러고는 자리에서 일어나 한마디 말도, 한 번의 눈길도 남기지 않고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["As her fingers were on the handle he called her back.", "손잡이에 손가락이 닿았을 때 그가 그녀를 불러 세웠다."], ["He was standing by the fireplace, shrunk and stooping; but as she came near he drew himself up to his full height, and, placing his hands on her head, he said, solemnly:", "그는 벽난로 곁에 오그라들어 구부정하게 서 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 다가오자 몸을 곧게 펴 온전한 키로 일어서서는 그녀의 머리에 두 손을 얹고 엄숙히 말했다."]], [["“The blessing of God be upon thee, my child!”", "“하나님의 복이 네게 임하기를, 내 아이야!”"]], [["“And may He restore you to His Church,”", "“그리고 그분께서 아버지를 그분의 교회로 돌려보내 주시기를.”"], ["responded she, out of the fulness of her heart.", "그녀가 가슴 가득한 마음으로 대답했다."], ["The next moment she feared lest this answer to his blessing might be irreverent, wrong—might hurt him as coming from his daughter, and she threw her arms round his neck.", "다음 순간 그녀는 축복에 대한 이 대꾸가 불경스럽고 그릇된 것은 아닐까, 딸의 입에서 나온 말이라 아버지를 아프게 하지는 않을까 겁이 나서 두 팔로 그의 목을 끌어안았다. 그는 한동안 딸을 품에 안고 있었다."], ["He held her to him for a minute or two.", "그가 혼잣말로 중얼거리는 소리가 그녀에게 들렸다."], ["She heard him murmur to himself, “The martyrs and confessors had even more pain to bear—I will not shrink.”", "“순교자들과 증거자들은 이보다 더한 아픔도 견뎠다. 나는 물러서지 않으리라.”"]], [["They were startled by hearing Mrs. Hale inquiring for her daughter.", "헤일 부인이 딸을 찾는 소리에 두 사람은 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["They started asunder in the full consciousness of all that was before them. Mr.", "앞에 놓인 모든 일을 뼈저리게 깨달으며 그들은 서로에게서 떨어졌다."], ["Hale hurriedly said, “Go, Margaret, go.", "헤일 씨가 서둘러 말했다. “가 보아라, 마거릿, 어서."], ["I shall be out all to-morrow.", "나는 내일 하루 종일 나가 있을 게다."], ["Before night you will have told your mother.”", "밤이 되기 전에 네가 어머니께 말씀드리게 될 게야.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["she replied, and she returned to the drawing-room in a stunned and dizzy state.", "그녀가 대답하고는 얼얼하고 어질어질한 상태로 응접실로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# 제5장"]], [["“I ask Thee for a thoughtful love, Through constant watching wise, To meet the glad with joyful smiles, And to wipe the weeping eyes; And a heart at leisure from itself To soothe and sympathise.”", "“사려 깊은 사랑을 당신께 구하나이다. 늘 지켜보는 데서 나오는 지혜로, 기쁜 이는 즐거운 미소로 맞이하고 우는 이의 눈물은 닦아 주게 하시고, 스스로에게서 벗어나 한가로운 마음으로 위로하고 함께 아파하게 하소서.”"], ["ANON.", "— 작자 미상."]], [["Margaret made a good listener to all her mother’s little plans for adding some small comforts to the lot of the poorest parishioners.", "마거릿은 가장 가난한 교구민들의 처지에 자그마한 안락을 보태려는 어머니의 온갖 소소한 계획을 잘 들어 주었다. 새 계획 하나하나가 가슴을 찌르는데도 그녀는 듣지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She could not help listening, though each new project was a stab to her heart.", "서리가 내릴 무렵이면 그들은 헬스톤에서 멀리 떠나 있을 터였다."], ["By the time the frost had set in, they should be far away from Helstone.", "늙은 사이먼의 류머티즘은 더 심해지고 눈은 더 어두워질지도 모른다."], ["Old Simon’s rheumatism might be bad and his eyesight worse; there would be no one to go and read to him, and comfort him with little porringers of broth and good red flannel; or if there was, it would be a stranger, and the old man would watch in vain for her.", "그런데 그를 찾아가 책을 읽어 주고 자그마한 죽 사발과 좋은 붉은 플란넬로 위로해 줄 사람은 아무도 없을 것이다. 혹 있다 해도 낯선 사람일 테고, 노인은 헛되이 그녀를 기다릴 것이다."], ["Mary Domville’s little crippled boy would crawl in vain to the door and look for her coming through the forest.", "메리 돔빌의 다리 저는 어린 아들은 헛되이 문가로 기어 나와 숲을 지나 오는 그녀를 찾을 것이다."], ["These poor friends would never understand why she had forsaken them; and there were many others besides.", "이 가난한 벗들은 그녀가 왜 자기들을 저버렸는지 결코 이해하지 못하리라. 그런 이들이 그 밖에도 많았다."], ["“Papa has always spent the income he derived from his living in the parish.", "“아버지는 늘 성직록에서 나오는 수입을 교구에 쓰셨단다."], ["I am, perhaps, encroaching upon the next dues, but the winter is likely to be severe, and our poor people must be helped.”", "어쩌면 내가 다음 몫까지 당겨 쓰는 것인지도 모르지만, 올겨울은 모질 듯하니 우리 가난한 사람들은 도와야 하지 않겠니.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma, let us do all we can,”", "“아, 어머니, 할 수 있는 건 다 해요.” 마거릿이 간절히 말했다."], ["said Margaret eagerly, not seeing the prudential side of the question, only grasping at the idea that they were rendering such help for the last time; “we may not be here long.”", "신중함이라는 쪽은 보지 못한 채, 그저 이런 도움을 베푸는 것이 마지막일지도 모른다는 생각만 붙들고 있었던 것이다. “우리가 여기 오래 있지 못할지도 모르잖아요.”"]], [["“Do you feel ill, my darling?” asks Mrs.", "“어디 아프니, 얘야?”"], ["Hale, anxiously, misunderstanding Margaret’s hint of the uncertainty of their stay at Helstone.", "헤일 부인이 걱정스레 물었다. 헬스톤에 머무는 것이 불확실하다는 마거릿의 암시를 잘못 알아들은 것이었다."], ["“You look pale and tired.", "“창백하고 피곤해 보이는구나."], ["It is this soft, damp, unhealthy air.”", "이 부드럽고 축축한, 몸에 안 좋은 공기 탓이야.”"]], [["“No—no, mamma, it is not that: it is delicious air.", "“아니에요, 아니에요, 어머니. 그게 아니에요."], ["It smells of the freshest, purest fragrance, after the smokiness of Harley Street.", "공기는 참 좋은걸요. 할리 가의 그 매캐한 공기를 겪고 나니 더없이 신선하고 맑은 향기가 나요."], ["But I am tired: it surely must be near bedtime.”", "그래도 피곤하긴 해요. 잘 시간이 다 됐나 봐요.”"]], [["“Not far off—it is half-past nine.", "“머지않았구나. 9시 반이다."], ["You had better go to bed at once, dear.", "얘야, 당장 자리에 눕는 게 좋겠다."], ["Ask Dixon for some gruel.", "딕슨한테 죽을 좀 달라고 하렴."], ["I will come and see you as soon as you are in bed.", "네가 눕는 대로 내가 가 보마."], ["I am afraid you have taken cold; or the bad air from some of the stagnant ponds—”", "감기가 든 게 아닐까 싶구나. 아니면 고인 못에서 나오는 나쁜 공기가……”"]], [["“Oh, mamma,” said Margaret, faintly smiling as she kissed her mother, “I am quite well—don’t alarm yourself about me; I am only tired.”", "“아, 어머니.” 마거릿이 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 희미하게 웃었다. “저는 아주 멀쩡해요. 제 걱정은 마세요. 그저 피곤할 뿐이에요.”"]], [["Margaret went up stairs.", "마거릿은 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["To soothe her mother’s anxiety she submitted to a basin of gruel.", "어머니의 걱정을 달래려고 죽 한 사발을 순순히 받아 먹었다."], ["She was lying languidly in bed when Mrs. Hale came up to make some last inquiries and kiss her before going to her own room for the night.", "헤일 부인이 마지막으로 몇 가지를 묻고 입을 맞춘 뒤 자기 방으로 물러가려고 올라왔을 때, 그녀는 나른하게 침대에 누워 있었다."], ["But the instant she heard her mother’s door locked, she sprang out of bed, and throwing her dressing-gown on, she began to pace up and down the room, until the creaking of one of the boards reminded her that she must make no noise.", "그러나 어머니의 방문이 잠기는 소리를 듣는 순간 그녀는 침대에서 뛰쳐나와 실내복을 걸치고 방 안을 오락가락 거닐기 시작했다. 그러다 마룻널 하나가 삐걱대는 소리에 소리를 내서는 안 된다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["She went and curled herself upon the window-seat in the small, deeply-recessed window.", "그녀는 깊숙이 들어앉은 자그마한 창의 창가 자리로 가 몸을 웅크렸다. 그날 아침 밖을 내다보았을 때는 교회 탑에 비치는 밝고 맑은 빛에 가슴이 뛰었다."], ["That morning when she had looked out, her heart had danced at seeing the bright clear lights on the church tower, which foretold a fine and sunny day.", "화창하고 볕 좋은 날을 알리는 빛이었다. 그런데 이 저녁, 길어야 16시간이 지났을 뿐인데 그녀는 울기에도 너무 벅찬 슬픔을 안고 앉아 있었다."], ["This evening—sixteen hours at most had past by—she sat down, too full of sorrow to cry, but with a cold dull pain, which seemed to have pressed the youth and buoyancy out of her heart, never to return.", "가슴에서 젊음과 생기를 두 번 다시 돌아오지 않게 짜내 버린 듯한 차갑고 둔한 아픔을 안고서. 헨리 레녹스 씨의 방문과 그의 청혼은 꿈만 같았다."], ["Mr. Henry Lennox’s visit—his offer—was like a dream, a thing beside her actual life.", "실제 자기 삶의 곁을 스쳐 간 무엇 같았다."], ["The hard reality was, that her father had so admitted tempting doubts into his mind as to become a schismatic—an outcast; all the changes consequent upon this grouped themselves around that one great blighting fact.", "모진 현실은 아버지가 마음에 유혹하는 의심을 들여놓은 나머지 분리파가 되고 버림받은 자가 되었다는 것이었다. 거기서 비롯된 온갖 변화가 그 하나의 크고 모든 것을 시들게 하는 사실을 둘러싸고 무리 지어 있었다."]], [["She looked out upon the dark-grey lines of the church towers, square and straight in the centre of the view, cutting against the deep blue transparent depths beyond, into which she gazed, and felt that she might gaze for ever, seeing at every moment some farther distance, and yet no sign of God! It seemed to her at the moment, as if the earth was more utterly desolate than if girt in by an iron dome, behind which there might be the ineffaceable peace and glory of the Almighty: those never-ending depths of space, in their still serenity, were more mocking to her than any material bounds could be—shutting in the cries of earth’s sufferers, which now might ascend into that infinite splendour of vastness and be lost—lost for ever, before they reached His throne. In this mood her father came in unheard. The moonlight was strong enough to let him see his daughter in her unusual place and attitude. He came to her and touched her shoulder before she was aware that he was there.", "그녀는 시야 한복판에 네모지고 곧게 선 짙은 잿빛 교회 탑들의 윤곽을 내다보았다. 그 너머 깊고 푸른 투명한 심연을 배경으로 도려낸 듯한 그 선들을. 그녀는 그 심연을 들여다보며 영원히 들여다볼 수도 있겠다고 느꼈다. 순간순간 더 먼 거리가 드러나는데도 하느님의 흔적이라고는 보이지 않는 그곳을. 그 순간 그녀에게는 세상이 쇠로 된 둥근 지붕에 둘러싸여 있을 때보다 더 철저히 황량한 것처럼 느껴졌다. 그런 지붕이라면 그 뒤에 전능하신 분의 지워지지 않는 평화와 영광이 있을 수도 있으련만. 그 끝없는 공간의 심연은 고요한 평온 속에서 어떤 물질의 경계보다도 더 그녀를 비웃는 듯했다. 물질의 경계라면 땅 위에서 고통받는 이들의 부르짖음을 가두어 두기라도 하련만, 이제 그 부르짖음은 저 무한한 광대함의 광휘 속으로 올라가 사라져 버릴지도 몰랐다. 그분의 보좌에 이르기도 전에 영영 사라져 버릴지도. 이런 심경에 잠겨 있을 때 아버지가 기척 없이 들어왔다. 달빛이 밝아 딸이 여느 때와 다른 자리에 여느 때와 다른 자세로 있는 것이 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 그가 다가와 어깨에 손을 얹고 나서야 그녀는 그가 거기 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Margaret, I heard you were up.", "“마거릿, 네가 일어나 있다는 말을 들었다. 함께 기도하자고 청하러 오지 않을 수가 없었구나."], ["I could not help coming in to ask you to pray with me—to say the Lord’s Prayer; that will do good to both of us.”", "주기도문을 외자꾸나. 우리 둘 다에게 좋을 게다.”"]], [["Mr. Hale and Margaret knelt by the window-seat—he looking up, she bowed down in humble shame.", "헤일 씨와 마거릿은 창가 자리 옆에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["God was there, close around them, hearing her father’s whispered words.", "그는 위를 우러렀고, 그녀는 부끄러움에 고개를 숙였다. 하느님은 거기, 그들 바로 곁에 계셔서 아버지가 속삭이는 말을 듣고 계셨다."], ["Her father might be a heretic; but had not she, in her despairing doubts not five minutes before, shown herself a far more utter sceptic?", "아버지는 이단자일지도 모른다. 그러나 채 5분도 되기 전에 절망에 찬 의심에 잠겨 스스로 훨씬 더 철저한 회의론자임을 드러낸 것은 그녀 자신이 아니었던가?"], ["She spoke not a word, but stole to bed after her father had left her, like a child ashamed of its fault.", "그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 아버지가 나간 뒤 잘못을 저지르고 부끄러워하는 아이처럼 살며시 잠자리로 들었다."], ["If the world was full of perplexing problems she would trust, and only ask to see the one step needful for the hour. Mr.", "세상이 알 수 없는 문제로 가득하다 해도 그녀는 믿고 맡기며, 그저 지금 이 시간에 필요한 한 걸음만 보여 달라고 구하리라."], ["Lennox—his visit, his proposal—the remembrance of which had been so rudely pushed aside by the subsequent events of the day—haunted her dreams that night.", "레녹스 씨, 그의 방문, 그의 청혼은 그날 뒤이어 벌어진 일들에 무참히 밀려나 있었는데, 그 기억이 그날 밤 그녀의 꿈을 어지럽혔다."], ["He was climbing up some tree of fabulous height to reach the branch whereon was slung her bonnet: he was falling and she was struggling to save him, but held back by some invisible powerful hand.", "그는 그녀의 보닛이 걸린 가지에 닿으려고 터무니없이 높은 나무를 기어오르고 있었다. 그가 떨어지고 있었고 그녀는 그를 구하려 몸부림쳤으나 보이지 않는 억센 손에 붙들려 있었다."], ["He was dead.", "그는 죽어 있었다."], ["And yet, with a shifting of the scene, she was once more in the Harley Street drawing-room, talking to him as of old, and still with a consciousness all the time that she had seen him killed by that terrible fall.", "그런데 장면이 바뀌자 그녀는 다시 할리 가의 응접실에서 예전처럼 그와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그러면서도 내내 그가 그 끔찍한 추락으로 죽는 것을 보았다는 의식을 지니고 있었다."]], [["Miserable, unresting night!", "비참하고 잠 못 이룬 밤이었다!"], ["Ill preparation for the coming day!", "다가올 날을 맞기에는 형편없는 준비였다!"], ["She awoke with a start, unrefreshed, and conscious of some reality worse even than her feverish dreams.", "그녀는 개운치 않은 몸으로 화들짝 잠에서 깼고, 열에 들뜬 꿈보다 더 나쁜 어떤 현실을 의식했다."], ["It all came back upon her; not merely the sorrow, but the terrible discord in the sorrow.", "모든 것이 되살아났다. 슬픔만이 아니라 그 슬픔 속의 끔찍한 불협화음까지."], ["Where, to what distance apart, had her father wandered, led by doubts which were to her temptations of the Evil One?", "그녀에게는 악한 자의 유혹인 그 의심에 이끌려 아버지는 어디로, 얼마나 멀리 헤매어 간 것일까?"], ["She longed to ask, and yet would not have heard for all the world.", "그녀는 묻고 싶었으나, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 대답만은 듣고 싶지 않았다."]], [["The fine crisp morning made her mother feel particularly well and happy at breakfast-time.", "맑고 서늘한 아침 덕에 어머니는 아침 식사 자리에서 유난히 몸도 마음도 좋았다."], ["She talked on, planning village kindness, unheeding the silence of her husband and the monosyllabic answers of Margaret.", "어머니는 남편의 침묵과 마거릿의 짤막한 대답에도 아랑곳없이 마을에 베풀 선행을 궁리하며 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["Before the things were cleared away, Mr.", "상을 치우기도 전에 헤일 씨가 일어섰다."], ["Hale got up; he leaned one hand on the table, as if to support himself:", "그는 몸을 가누려는 듯 한 손을 식탁에 짚었다."]], [["“I shall not be home till evening.", "“저녁까지는 못 돌아올 거요."], ["I am going to Bracy Common, and will ask Farmer Dobson to give me something for dinner.", "브레이시 커먼에 갈 참인데, 돕슨 농부한테 요기할 것을 좀 달라고 하지요."], ["I shall be back to tea at seven.”", "7시 차 시간에는 돌아오리다.”"]], [["He did not look at either of them, but Margaret knew what he meant.", "그는 두 사람 중 누구도 쳐다보지 않았으나 마거릿은 그 뜻을 알았다."], ["By seven the announcement must be made to her mother.", "7시까지 어머니에게 알려야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Hale would have delayed making it, but Margaret was of different stuff.", "헤일 씨라면 그 일을 미루었겠지만 마거릿은 다른 사람이었다."], ["She could not bear the impending weight on her mind all day long: better get the worst over; the day would be too short to comfort her mother.", "그녀는 하루 종일 마음을 짓누르는 무게를 견딜 수 없었다. 최악을 먼저 넘기는 편이 나았다."], ["But while she stood by the window, thinking how to begin, and waiting for the servant to have left the room, her mother had gone up stairs to put on her things to go to the school.", "어머니를 위로하기에는 하루도 짧을 터였다. 그러나 그녀가 창가에 서서 어떻게 말을 꺼낼지 궁리하며 하녀가 방에서 나가기를 기다리는 사이, 어머니는 학교에 가려고 채비를 갖추러 위층으로 올라가 버렸다."], ["She came down ready equipped, in a brisker mood than usual.", "어머니는 차림을 다 갖추고 여느 때보다 활기찬 기분으로 내려왔다."]], [["“Mother, come round the garden with me this morning; just one turn,”", "“어머니, 오늘 아침에 저와 정원을 한 바퀴 돌아요. 딱 한 바퀴만요.”"], ["said Margaret, putting her arm round Mrs. Hale’s waist.", "마거릿이 헤일 부인의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다."]], [["They passed through the open window.", "두 사람은 열린 창을 지나 밖으로 나섰다."], ["Mrs. Hale spoke—said something—Margaret could not tell what.", "헤일 부인이 무슨 말인가를 했지만 마거릿은 그것이 무슨 말인지 알 수 없었다."], ["Her eye caught on a bee entering a deep-belled flower: when that bee flew forth with his spoil she would begin—that should be the sign.", "그녀의 눈은 종처럼 깊은 꽃 속으로 들어가는 벌 한 마리에 붙들려 있었다. 저 벌이 전리품을 물고 날아 나오면 말을 꺼내리라."], ["Out he came.", "그것을 신호로 삼으리라. 벌이 나왔다."]], [["“Mamma!", "“어머니!"], ["Papa is going to leave Helstone!”", "아버지가 헬스톤을 떠나실 거예요!”"], ["she blurted forth.", "그녀가 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["“He’s going to leave the Church, and live in Milton-Northern.”", "“국교회를 떠나 밀턴노던에서 사실 거예요.”"], ["There were the three hard facts hardly spoken.", "그렇게 세 가지 모진 사실이 겨우 입 밖에 나왔다."]], [["“What makes you say so?”", "“어째서 그런 말을 하니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Hale, in a surprised, incredulous voice.", "헤일 부인이 놀라 믿기지 않는다는 목소리로 물었다."], ["“Who has been telling you such nonsense?”", "“누가 너한테 그런 터무니없는 소리를 하더냐?”"]], [["“Papa himself,”", "“아버지가 직접 하셨어요.”"], ["said Margaret, longing to say something gentle and consoling, but literally not knowing how.", "마거릿이 말했다. 무언가 부드럽고 위로가 될 말을 하고 싶었지만 정말이지 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["They were close to a garden-bench. Mrs.", "두 사람은 정원 의자 가까이에 있었다."], ["Hale sat down and began to cry.", "헤일 부인이 앉더니 울기 시작했다."]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "“무슨 말인지 모르겠구나.”"], ["she said.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["“Either you have made some great mistake, or I don’t quite understand you.”", "“네가 무언가 크게 잘못 안 것이든지, 아니면 내가 네 말을 제대로 못 알아들은 것이든지.”"]], [["“No, mother, I have made no mistake.", "“아니에요, 어머니. 저는 잘못 안 게 아니에요."], ["Papa has written to the bishop, saying that he has such doubts that he cannot conscientiously remain a priest of the Church of England, and that he must give up Helstone.", "아버지는 주교님께 편지를 쓰셨어요. 의심이 너무 커서 양심상 잉글랜드 국교회의 사제로 남을 수 없다고, 그래서 헬스톤을 내놓아야 한다고요."], ["He has also consulted Mr.", "벨 씨와도 의논하셨어요. 프레더릭 오빠의 대부님 말이에요, 어머니."], ["Bell—Frederick’s godfather, you know, mamma; and it is arranged that we go to live at Milton-Northern.” Mrs.", "그래서 우리가 밀턴노던에 가서 살기로 정해졌어요.”"], ["Hale looked up in Margaret’s face all the time she was speaking these words: the shadow on her countenance told that she, at least, believed in the truth of what she said.", "이 말을 하는 내내 헤일 부인은 마거릿의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 그 얼굴에 드리운 그늘이, 적어도 딸만은 자기가 하는 말이 사실이라고 믿고 있음을 말해 주었다."]], [["“I don’t think it can be true,”", "“사실일 리 없다.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, at length.", "헤일 부인이 마침내 말했다."], ["“He would surely have told me before it came to this.”", "“이 지경이 되기 전에 분명 내게 말했을 텐데.”"]], [["It came strongly upon Margaret’s mind that her mother ought to have been told: that whatever her faults of discontent and repining might have been, it was an error in her father to have left her to learn his change of opinion, and his approaching change of life, from her better-informed child.", "어머니에게 알렸어야 했다는 생각이 마거릿의 마음을 세게 쳤다. 어머니에게 불평과 한탄이라는 흠이 있었다 한들, 자기 생각이 바뀐 것과 곧 닥칠 삶의 변화를 더 잘 아는 딸에게서 듣게 내버려 둔 것은 아버지의 잘못이었다."], ["Margaret sat down by her mother, and took her unresisting head on her breast, bending her own soft cheeks down caressingly to touch her face.", "마거릿은 어머니 곁에 앉아 저항 없이 기대 오는 머리를 가슴에 안고, 자신의 보드라운 뺨을 숙여 어루만지듯 어머니의 얼굴에 댔다."]], [["“Dear, darling mamma!", "“사랑하는 어머니!"], ["we were so afraid of giving you pain.", "저희는 어머니께 아픔을 드릴까 봐 너무 무서웠어요. 아버지도 몹시 괴로워하셨고요."], ["Papa felt so acutely—you know you are not strong, and there must have been such terrible suspense to go through.”", "어머니가 튼튼하지 못하신 걸 아시잖아요. 게다가 견뎌야 할 그 끔찍한 조바심도 있었고요.”"]], [["“When did he tell you, Margaret?”", "“언제 네게 말하셨니, 마거릿?”"]], [["“Yesterday, only yesterday,”", "“어제요, 바로 어제요.”"], ["replied Margaret, detecting the jealousy which prompted the inquiry.", "마거릿이 대답하며 그 물음을 부추긴 질투를 알아차렸다."], ["“Poor papa!”—trying to divert her mother’s thoughts into compassionate sympathy for all her father had gone through.", "“가엾은 아버지!” 어머니의 생각을 아버지가 겪어 온 모든 일에 대한 가엾은 마음으로 돌리려는 것이었다."], ["Mrs. Hale raised her head.", "헤일 부인이 고개를 들었다."]], [["“What does he mean by having doubts?”", "“의심이 든다는 게 무슨 뜻이냐?”"], ["she asked.", "어머니가 물었다."], ["“Surely, he does not mean that he thinks differently—that he knows better than the Church.”", "“설마 네 아버지가 국교회와 다르게 생각한다는, 국교회보다 더 잘 안다는 뜻은 아니겠지.”"]], [["Margaret shook her head, and the tears came into her eyes, as her mother touched the bare nerve of her own regret.", "마거릿은 고개를 저었고, 어머니가 자기 회한의 벌거벗은 신경을 건드리자 눈에 눈물이 고였다."]], [["“Can’t the bishop set him right?”", "“주교님이 바로잡아 주실 수는 없니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Hale half impatiently.", "헤일 부인이 반쯤 조바심을 내며 물었다."]], [["“I’m afraid not,”", "“안 될 것 같아요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“But I did not ask.", "“하지만 저는 여쭙지 않았어요."], ["I could not bear to hear what he might answer.", "아버지가 어떤 대답을 하실지 차마 들을 수가 없었거든요."], ["It is all settled at any rate.", "어쨌든 다 정해진 일이에요."], ["He is going to leave Helstone in a fortnight.", "아버지는 2주 뒤에 헬스톤을 떠나실 거예요."], ["I am not sure if he did not say he had sent in his deed of resignation.”", "사임서를 이미 내셨다고 하신 것도 같고요.”"]], [["“In a fortnight!”", "“2주 뒤라니!”"], ["exclaimed Mrs.", "헤일 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Hale, “I do think this is very strange—not at all right.", "“정말이지 이건 아주 이상하구나. 조금도 온당하지 않아."], ["I call it very unfeeling,”", "나는 이게 아주 매정하다고 본다.”"], ["said she, beginning to take relief in tears.", "어머니는 눈물에서 위안을 찾기 시작했다."], ["“He has doubts you say, and gives up his living, and all without consulting me.", "“의심이 든다면서 성직록을 내놓다니, 그것도 다 나와 한마디 의논 없이."], ["I dare say, if he had told me his doubts at the first I could have nipped them in the bud.”", "처음에 내게 그 의심을 말했더라면 내가 싹부터 잘라 낼 수도 있었을 텐데.”"]], [["Mistaken as Margaret felt her father’s conduct to have been, she could not bear to hear it blamed by her mother.", "아버지의 처사가 잘못되었다고 느끼면서도 마거릿은 어머니가 그것을 나무라는 말을 차마 들을 수 없었다."], ["She knew that his very reserve had originated in a tenderness for her, which might be cowardly, but was not unfeeling.", "아버지의 그 말없음이야말로 어머니를 아끼는 마음에서 비롯되었음을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그것이 비겁했을지는 몰라도 매정하지는 않았다."]], [["“I almost hoped you might be glad to leave Helstone, mamma,”", "“어머니는 헬스톤을 떠나는 걸 반가워하실지도 모른다고 저는 은근히 바랐어요.”"], ["said she, after a pause.", "잠시 뒤 그녀가 말했다."], ["“You have never been well in this air, you know.”", "“어머니는 이곳 공기에서는 한 번도 건강하신 적이 없었잖아요.”"]], [["“You can’t think the smoky air of a manufacturing town, all chimneys and dirt like Milton-Northern, would be better than this air, which is pure and sweet, if it is too soft and relaxing.", "“밀턴노던처럼 온통 굴뚝과 먼지뿐인 공장 도시의 매캐한 공기가 이곳 공기보다 나으리라고는 생각할 수 없구나. 여기 공기는 너무 눅눅하고 사람을 늘어지게 하긴 해도 맑고 달콤하지 않니."], ["Fancy living in the middle of factories, and factory people!", "공장과 공장 사람들 한복판에서 사는 걸 생각해 보렴!"], ["Though, of course, if your father leaves the Church, we shall not be admitted into society anywhere.", "하기야 네 아버지가 국교회를 떠나면 우리는 어디서도 사교계에 받아들여지지 못하겠지."], ["It will be such a disgrace to us!", "우리에게 얼마나 큰 수치냐!"], ["Poor dear Sir John!", "가엾은 존 경!"], ["It is well he is not alive to see what your father has come to!", "네 아버지가 이 지경이 된 것을 보지 않고 돌아가신 게 다행이다."], ["Every day after dinner, when I was a girl, living with your Aunt Shaw, at Beresford Court, Sir John used to give for the first toast—‘Church and King, and down with the Rump.’”", "내가 처녀 적에 베러스퍼드 저택에서 네 쇼 이모와 살 때, 존 경은 저녁 식사 뒤면 날마다 첫 축배로 이렇게 외치곤 하셨어. ‘국교회와 국왕을 위하여, 잔당 의회는 물러가라.’”"]], [["Margaret was glad that her mother’s thoughts were turned away from the fact of her husband’s silence to her on the point which must have been so near his heart.", "마거릿은 어머니의 생각이 남편이 자기에게 침묵했다는 사실에서 벗어난 것이 다행이었다. 그 문제는 남편의 마음에 무척이나 가까웠을 것이다."], ["Next to the serious vital anxiety as to the nature of her father’s doubts, this was the one circumstance of the case that gave Margaret the most pain.", "아버지의 의심이 어떤 성질의 것인가 하는 심각하고도 절실한 근심 다음으로, 마거릿에게 가장 큰 아픔을 준 것이 바로 이 사정이었다."]], [["“You know, we have very little society here, mamma.", "“어머니, 아시다시피 우리는 여기서도 사교랄 게 거의 없잖아요."], ["The Gormans, who are our nearest neighbours (to call society—and we hardly ever see them), have been in trade just as much as these Milton-Northern people.”", "가장 가까운 이웃인 고먼 집안도, 그걸 사교라고 부를 수 있다면 말이지만, 게다가 우리는 그분들을 거의 뵙지도 못하지만, 그 밀턴노던 사람들 못지않게 장사를 해 온 분들이고요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇지.”"], ["says Mrs.", "헤일 부인이 거의 분개하듯 말했다."], ["Hale, almost indignantly, “but at any rate, the Gormans made carriages for half the gentlemen of the county, and were brought into some kind of intercourse with them; but these factory people, who on earth wears cotton that can afford linen?”", "“하지만 어쨌든 고먼 집안은 이 주의 신사들 절반에게 마차를 만들어 주었고, 그러면서 그분들과 어느 정도 교류도 하지 않았니. 그런데 그 공장 사람들이라니, 대체 아마포를 살 형편이 되는데 무명을 걸치는 사람이 어디 있단 말이냐?”"]], [["“Well, mamma, I give up the cotton-spinners; I am not standing up for them, any more than for any other trades-people. Only we shall have little enough to do with them.”", "“그럼요, 어머니. 면방적업자는 제가 포기할게요. 저도 그 사람들 편을 드는 게 아니에요. 다른 장사치들 편을 들지 않는 것과 마찬가지로요. 다만 우리가 그 사람들과 엮일 일은 거의 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“Why on earth has your father fixed on Milton-Northern to live in?”", "“대체 네 아버지는 왜 하필 밀턴노던을 살 곳으로 정하셨다니?”"]], [["“Partly,” said Margaret, sighing, “because it is so very different from Helstone—partly because Mr.", "“한편으로는 그곳이 헬스톤과 아주 다르기 때문이에요.”"], ["Bell says there is an opening there for a private tutor.”", "마거릿이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “한편으로는 벨 씨가 그곳에 개인 교습 자리가 있다고 하셔서고요.”"]], [["“Private tutor in Milton!", "“밀턴에서 개인 교사라니!"], ["Why can’t he go to Oxford, and be a tutor to gentlemen?”", "옥스퍼드로 가서 신사들을 가르치면 안 되는 거니?”"]], [["“You forget, mamma!", "“잊으셨어요, 어머니!"], ["He is leaving the Church on account of his opinions—his doubts would do him no good at Oxford.”", "아버지는 당신 생각 때문에 국교회를 떠나시는 거예요. 그 의심으로는 옥스퍼드에서 아무것도 못 하세요.”"]], [["Mrs. Hale was silent for some time, quietly crying.", "헤일 부인은 한동안 말없이 조용히 울었다."], ["At last she said:—", "마침내 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“And the furniture—How in the world are we to manage the removal?", "“그런데 세간은…… 대체 이사를 어떻게 감당한단 말이냐?"], ["I never removed in my life, and only a fortnight to think about it!”", "나는 평생 이사라는 걸 해 본 적이 없는데, 궁리할 시간이라고는 2주뿐이라니!”"]], [["Margaret was inexpressibly relieved to find that her mother’s anxiety and distress was lowered to this point, so insignificant to herself, and on which she could do so much to help.", "마거릿은 어머니의 근심과 괴로움이 이 정도로 낮아진 것에 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓였다. 자신에게는 하찮은 문제였고, 자기가 크게 도울 수 있는 문제이기도 했다."], ["She planned and promised, and led her mother on to arrange fully as much as could be fixed before they knew somewhat more definitely what Mr.", "그녀는 이런저런 계획을 세우고 약속했으며, 헤일 씨가 무엇을 하려는지 조금 더 분명히 알기 전에 정할 수 있는 것은 다 정하도록 어머니를 이끌었다."], ["Hale intended to do.", "그날 하루 종일 마거릿은 어머니 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["Throughout the day Margaret never left her mother; bending her whole soul to sympathise in all the various turns her feelings took; towards evening especially, as she became more and more anxious that her father should find a soothing welcome home awaiting him, after his return from his day of fatigue and distress.", "어머니의 감정이 이리저리 옮아 갈 때마다 온 마음을 기울여 함께 느껴 주었다. 특히 저녁이 가까워지면서 그녀는 아버지가 지치고 괴로운 하루를 보내고 돌아왔을 때 마음을 달래 주는 맞이가 집에서 기다리기를 점점 더 간절히 바랐다."], ["She dwelt upon what he must have borne in secret for long; her mother only replied coldly that he ought to have told her, and that then at any rate he would have had an adviser to give him counsel; and Margaret turned faint at heart when she heard her father’s step in the hall.", "그녀는 아버지가 오랫동안 남몰래 견뎌야 했을 것들을 이야기했다. 어머니는 그렇다면 자기에게 말했어야 했다고, 그랬으면 적어도 조언해 줄 사람은 있었을 것 아니냐고 차갑게 대꾸할 뿐이었다."], ["She dared not go to meet him, and tell him what she had done all day, for fear of her mother’s jealous annoyance.", "그러다 현관에서 아버지의 발소리가 들리자 마거릿은 가슴이 아득해졌다. 그녀는 어머니의 질투 어린 언짢음이 두려워 감히 아버지를 맞으러 나가 하루 종일 무엇을 했는지 말할 수 없었다."], ["She heard him linger, as if awaiting her, or some sign of her; and she dared not stir; she saw by her mother’s twitching lips, and changing colour, that she too was aware that her husband had returned.", "아버지가 자기를 기다리는 듯, 혹은 무슨 기척이라도 기다리는 듯 서성이는 소리가 들렸으나 그녀는 감히 움직이지 못했다. 어머니의 씰룩이는 입술과 변하는 낯빛으로 보아 어머니 역시 남편이 돌아온 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Presently he opened the room-door, and stood there uncertain whether to come in.", "이윽고 그가 방문을 열고 들어올까 말까 망설이며 서 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 잿빛으로 창백했다."], ["His face was gray and pale; he had a timid fearful look in his eyes; something almost pitiful to see in a man’s face; but that look of despondent uncertainty, of mental and bodily languor, touched his wife’s heart.", "눈에는 겁먹고 두려워하는 기색이 어려 있었다. 사내의 얼굴에서 보기에는 거의 딱할 지경이었다."], ["She went to him, and threw herself on his breast, crying out:—", "그러나 낙담한 그 막막함이, 그 심신의 무기력이 아내의 마음을 움직였다. 그녀는 남편에게 가서 그의 가슴에 몸을 던지며 울부짖었다."]], [["“Oh! Richard, Richard, you should have told me sooner!”", "“아, 리처드, 리처드, 진작에 내게 말했어야지요!”"]], [["And then, in tears, Margaret left her, as she rushed up stairs to throw herself on her bed, and hide her face in the pillows to stifle the hysteric sobs that would force their way out at last, after the rigid self-control of the whole day.", "그러자 마거릿은 눈물을 흘리며 어머니 곁을 떠났다. 위층으로 달려 올라가 침대에 몸을 던지고 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 하루 종일 굳게 자신을 다잡은 끝에 끝내 터져 나오고야 마는 발작 같은 흐느낌을 눌렀다."]], [["How long she lay thus she could not tell. She heard no noise, though the housemaid came in to arrange the room.", "그렇게 얼마나 누워 있었는지 그녀는 알 수 없었다."], ["The affrighted girl stole out again on tip-toe, and went and told Mrs.", "하녀가 방을 치우러 들어왔는데도 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다."], ["Dixon that Miss Hale was crying as if her heart would break: she was sure she would make herself deadly ill if she went on at that rate.", "놀란 하녀는 발끝으로 다시 빠져나가 딕슨에게 헤일 아가씨가 가슴이 미어지도록 울고 있다고, 저러다가는 큰 병이 나고 말 것이 틀림없다고 일렀다. 그 바람에 마거릿은 누군가 자기를 건드리는 것을 느끼고 화들짝 일어나 앉았다."], ["In consequence of this, Margaret felt herself touched, and started up into a sitting posture; she saw the accustomed room, the figure of Dixon in shadow, as the latter stood holding a candle a little behind her, for fear of the effect on Miss Hale’s startled eyes, swollen and blinded as they were.", "낯익은 방이 눈에 들어왔고, 그림자 진 딕슨의 모습이 보였다. 딕슨은 부어오르고 앞이 안 보일 만큼 상한 헤일 아가씨의 눈에 빛이 해로울까 봐 촛불을 조금 뒤쪽에 들고 서 있었다."]], [["“Oh, Dixon!", "“어머, 딕슨!"], ["I did not hear you come into the room!”", "방에 들어오는 소리를 못 들었어요.”"], ["said Margaret, resuming her trembling self-restraint.", "마거릿이 떨리는 몸을 다시 다잡으며 말했다."], ["“Is it very late?”", "“많이 늦었나요?”"], ["continued she, lifting herself languidly off the bed, yet letting her feet touch the ground without fairly standing down, as she shaded her wet ruffled hair off her face, and tried to look as though nothing were the matter; as if she had been asleep.", "그녀는 나른하게 몸을 일으키면서도 발이 바닥에 닿기만 할 뿐 온전히 일어서지는 않은 채, 젖어 헝클어진 머리칼을 얼굴에서 걷어 내고 아무 일도 없었던 듯, 잠들어 있었던 듯 보이려 애쓰며 말을 이었다."]], [["“I can hardly tell what time it is,”", "“몇 시인지 저도 잘 모르겠구먼요.”"], ["replied Dixon in an aggrieved tone of voice.", "딕슨이 서운함이 묻은 목소리로 대답했다."], ["“Since your mamma told me this terrible news, when I dressed her for tea, I’ve lost all count of time.", "“차 드실 때 옷을 갈아입혀 드리면서 마님한테서 그 끔찍한 소식을 들은 뒤로는 시간 가는 줄을 통 모르겠어요."], ["I’m sure I don’t know what is to become of us all.", "정말이지 우리 모두가 어찌 될지 모르겠습니다요."], ["When Charlotte told me just now you were sobbing, Miss Hale, I thought, no wonder, poor thing!", "아까 샬럿이 아가씨가 흐느껴 우신다고 하기에 저는 그럴 만도 하다, 딱하기도 하시지, 했지요. 주인 어른께서 그 연세에 비국교도가 되실 생각을 하시다니요."], ["And master thinking of turning Dissenter at his time of life, when, if it is not to be said he’s done well in the Church, he’s not done badly after all.", "국교회에서 잘되셨다고까지는 못 해도 그리 못되지도 않으셨는데 말입니다요. 저한테 사촌이 하나 있었는데요, 아가씨, 50이 넘은 나이에 감리교 설교자가 되었답니다."], ["I had a cousin, miss, who turned Methodist preacher after he was fifty years of age, and a tailor all his life; but then he had never been able to make a pair of trousers to fit, for as long as he had been in the trade, so it was no wonder; but for master!", "평생 재봉사였는데요. 하기야 그 일을 그렇게 오래 했으면서도 몸에 맞는 바지 한 벌을 못 지었으니 이상할 것도 없지요."], ["as I said to missus, ‘What would poor Sir John have said?", "그렇지만 주인 어른께서야! 제가 마님께 이렇게 말씀드렸어요."], ["he never liked your marrying Mr.", "‘가엾은 존 경께서 뭐라고 하셨을까요?"], ["Hale, but if he could have known it would have come to this, he would have sworn worse oaths than ever, if that was possible!’”", "그분은 마님이 헤일 씨와 혼인하시는 걸 도무지 좋아하지 않으셨는데, 일이 이 지경까지 오리라는 걸 아셨더라면 그보다 더한 욕이 있을 수 있다면 그런 욕까지 퍼부으셨을 겁니다요.’”"]], [["Dixon had been so much accustomed to comment upon Mr. Hale’s proceedings to her mistress (who listened to her, or not, as she was in the humour), that she never noticed Margaret’s flashing eye and dilating nostril.", "딕슨은 헤일 씨의 처사를 두고 마님에게 이러쿵저러쿵 말하는 데 워낙 익숙해져 있었으므로(마님은 기분에 따라 들어 주기도 하고 흘려버리기도 했다), 마거릿의 번뜩이는 눈과 벌름거리는 콧구멍을 알아채지 못했다."], ["To hear her father talked of in this way by a servant to her face!", "하인이 자기 면전에서 아버지를 그런 식으로 입에 올리다니!"]], [["“Dixon,”", "“딕슨.”"], ["she said, in the low tone she always used when much excited, which had a sound in it as of some distant turmoil, or threatening storm breaking far away.", "그녀가 몹시 격했을 때면 늘 쓰는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그 목소리에는 멀리서 이는 소란이나 저 멀리서 터지는 폭풍 같은 울림이 있었다."], ["“Dixon!", "“딕슨!"], ["you forget to whom you are speaking.”", "지금 누구에게 말하고 있는지 잊었군요.”"], ["She stood upright and firm on her feet now, confronting the waiting-maid, and fixing her with her steady discerning eye. “I am Mr.", "이제 그녀는 몸을 곧추세우고 단단히 발을 딛고 서서 하녀를 마주 보며 흔들림 없이 꿰뚫는 눈으로 그녀를 붙들었다."], ["Hale’s daughter. Go!", "“나는 헤일 씨의 딸이에요."], ["You have made a strange mistake, and one that I am sure your own good feeling will make you sorry for when you think about it.”", "나가요! 딕슨은 이상한 잘못을 저질렀고, 곰곰이 생각해 보면 그 착한 심성으로 틀림없이 뉘우치게 될 잘못이에요.”"]], [["Dixon hung irresolutely about the room for a minute or two.", "딕슨은 1, 2분쯤 망설이며 방 안을 서성였다. 마거릿이 다시 말했다."], ["Margaret repeated, “You may leave me, Dixon.", "“나가도 좋아요, 딕슨. 나가 주었으면 해요.”"], ["I wish you to go.”", "딕슨은 이 단호한 말에 성을 내야 할지 울어야 할지 알 수 없었다. 마님이라면 어느 쪽이든 통했을 것이다."], ["Dixon did not know whether to resent these decided words or to cry; either course would have suited her mistress: but as she said to herself, “Miss Margaret has a touch of the old gentleman about her, as well as poor Master Frederick; I wonder where they get it from?”", "그러나 딕슨은 속으로 이렇게 생각했다. ‘마거릿 아가씨한테는 어딘가 옛 어른의 기질이 있어."], ["and she, who would have resented such words from any one less haughty and determined in manner, was subdued enough to say, in a half humble, half injured tone:", "가엾은 프레더릭 도련님처럼 말이야. 대체 그걸 누구한테서 물려받았을까?’ 그리하여 태도가 덜 도도하고 덜 단호한 사람에게서 그런 말을 들었더라면 발끈했을 그녀도 반쯤은 겸손하고 반쯤은 서운한 목소리로 이렇게 말할 만큼 누그러졌다."]], [["“Mayn’t I unfasten your gown, miss, and do your hair?”", "“가운을 벗겨 드리고 머리를 손봐 드리면 안 될까요, 아가씨?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니요!"], ["not to-night, thank you.”", "오늘 밤은 됐어요, 고마워요.”"], ["And Margaret gravely lighted her out of the room, and bolted the door.", "그러고 나서 마거릿은 진중하게 촛불로 앞을 비추어 그녀를 방 밖으로 내보내고는 문을 잠갔다."], ["From henceforth Dixon obeyed and admired Margaret.", "그때부터 딕슨은 마거릿에게 복종했고 그녀를 우러렀다."], ["She said it was because she was so like poor Master Frederick; but the truth was, that Dixon, as do many others, liked to feel herself ruled by a powerful and decided nature.", "딕슨은 아가씨가 가엾은 프레더릭 도련님과 너무 닮아서라고 말했지만, 사실은 딕슨도 여느 많은 사람들처럼 힘 있고 단호한 성품에 다스려지는 느낌을 좋아했던 것이다."]], [["Margaret needed all Dixon’s help in action, and silence in words; for, for some time, the latter thought it her duty to show her sense of affront by saying as little as possible to her young lady; so the energy came out in doing rather than in speaking.", "마거릿에게는 딕슨의 손이 하는 온갖 도움과 그 입이 지키는 침묵이 다 필요했다. 한동안 딕슨은 모욕당했다는 자기감정을 드러내는 것이 도리라고 여겨 아가씨에게 되도록 말을 아꼈으므로, 그 기운이 말 대신 일로 뻗어 나왔던 것이다."], ["A fortnight was a very short time to make arrangements for so serious a removal; as Dixon said, “Anyone but a gentleman—indeed almost any other gentleman—”", "2주는 이토록 중대한 이사를 준비하기에는 몹시 짧은 시간이었다. 딕슨의 말대로 “신사가 아닌 사람이라면 누구라도, 아니 웬만한 다른 신사라도……”"], ["but catching a look at Margaret’s straight, stern brow just here, she coughed the remainder of the sentence away, and meekly took the horehound drop that Margaret offered her, to stop the “little tickling at my chest, miss.”", "하다가 마거릿의 곧고 엄한 이맛살을 흘끗 보고는 남은 말을 기침 속에 흘려보냈고, “가슴이 좀 간질간질해서요, 아가씨” 하는 그 증세를 멎게 하라며 마거릿이 건네는 쓴박하 사탕을 얌전히 받아 먹었다."], ["But almost any one but Mr. Hale would have had practical knowledge enough to see, that in so short a time it would be difficult to fix on any house in Milton-Northern, or indeed elsewhere, to which they could remove the furniture that had of necessity been taken out of Helstone Vicarage.", "그러나 헤일 씨가 아닌 웬만한 사람이라면, 그토록 짧은 시간 안에 밀턴노던이든 다른 어디든 헬스톤 목사관에서 어차피 빼내야 할 세간을 옮겨 넣을 집을 정하기가 어려우리라는 것쯤은 알아볼 현실 감각이 있었을 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Hale, overpowered by all the troubles and necessities for immediate household decisions that seemed to come upon her at once, became really ill, and Margaret almost felt it as a relief when her mother fairly took to her bed, and left the management of affairs to her.", "헤일 부인은 한꺼번에 밀려드는 것만 같은 온갖 근심과 당장 정해야 할 살림살이 문제에 짓눌려 정말로 몸져누웠다."], ["Dixon, true to her post of body-guard, attended most faithfully to her mistress, and only emerged from Mrs.", "어머니가 아예 자리에 눕고 살림을 자기에게 맡겼을 때 마거릿은 오히려 마음이 놓이기까지 했다."], ["Hale’s bedroom to shake her head, and murmur to herself in a manner which Margaret did not choose to hear.", "딕슨은 호위병이라는 제 자리에 충실하게 마님을 더없이 정성껏 보살폈고, 헤일 부인의 침실에서 나오는 것은 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 혼자 무어라 중얼거리기 위해서일 뿐이었는데 마거릿은 그 소리를 듣지 않기로 했다."], ["For, the one thing clear and straight before her, was the necessity for leaving Helstone. Mr.", "그녀 앞에 분명하고 곧게 놓인 한 가지는 헬스톤을 떠나야 한다는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["Hale’s successor in the living was appointed; and, at any rate, after her father’s decision, there must be no lingering now, for his sake, as well as from every other consideration.", "헤일 씨의 성직록을 이을 후임자가 정해졌다. 게다가 아버지가 결정을 내린 이상, 아버지를 위해서도 다른 온갖 사정을 보아서도 이제 더는 미적거려서는 안 되었다."], ["For he came home every evening more and more depressed, after the necessary leave-taking which he had resolved to have with every individual parishioner.", "아버지는 교구민 한 사람 한 사람과 반드시 작별 인사를 나누겠다고 마음먹은 터라, 저녁마다 점점 더 낙심한 채 집에 돌아왔던 것이다."], ["Margaret, inexperienced as she was in all the necessary matter-of-fact business to be got through, did not know to whom to apply for advice.", "처리해야 할 온갖 현실적인 일에 서툴기만 한 마거릿은 누구에게 조언을 구해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["The cook and Charlotte worked away with willing arms and stout hearts at all the moving and packing; and as far as that went, Margaret’s admirable sense enabled her to see what was best, and to direct how it should be done.", "요리사와 샬럿은 기꺼운 팔과 굳센 마음으로 짐을 옮기고 싸는 일을 해냈고, 그 일에 관한 한 마거릿의 훌륭한 분별력 덕에 무엇이 최선인지 알아보고 어떻게 할지 일러 줄 수 있었다. 그러나 어디로 간단 말인가?"], ["But where were they to go to?", "1주일 뒤면 떠나야 했다."], ["In a week they must be gone. Straight to Milton, or where?", "곧장 밀턴으로 갈 것인가, 아니면 어디로 갈 것인가?"], ["So many arrangements depended on this precision that Margaret resolved to ask her father one evening, in spite of his evident fatigue and low spirits.", "이 한 가지에 달린 준비가 너무 많았으므로 마거릿은 어느 날 저녁 아버지의 뚜렷한 피로와 침울함에도 불구하고 여쭈어 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["He answered:", "아버지는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“My dear!", "“얘야!"], ["I have really had too much to think about to settle this.", "나는 정말이지 생각할 것이 너무 많아 그것까지는 정하지 못했구나."], ["What does your mother say?", "네 어머니는 뭐라시더냐?"], ["What does she wish?", "무엇을 바라신다더냐?"], ["Poor Maria!”", "가엾은 마리아!”"]], [["He met with an echo even louder than his sigh.", "그의 한숨보다 더 큰 메아리가 돌아왔다."], ["Dixon had just come into the room for another cup of tea for Mrs. Hale, and catching Mr.", "마침 딕슨이 헤일 부인에게 드릴 차를 한 잔 더 가지러 방에 들어왔다가 헤일 씨의 마지막 말을 듣고는, 그가 있는 덕에 마거릿의 나무라는 눈길로부터 보호받는다는 생각에 용기를 내어 이렇게 말했다."], ["Hale’s last words, and protected by his presence from Margaret’s upbraiding eyes, made bold to say, “My poor mistress!”", "“가엾은 우리 마님!”"]], [["“You don’t think her worse to-day,”", "“오늘은 더 나빠진 것 같지는 않지?”"], ["said Mr. Hale, turning hastily.", "헤일 씨가 황급히 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“I am sure I can’t say, sir. It’s not for me to judge.", "“저야 뭐라 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다요, 나리."], ["The illness seems so much more on the mind than on the body.”", "제가 판단할 일이 아니지요. 병이 몸보다는 마음에 있는 것 같아서요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale looked infinitely distressed.", "헤일 씨의 얼굴에 한없는 괴로움이 어렸다."]], [["“You had better take mamma her tea while it is hot, Dixon,”", "“식기 전에 어머니께 차를 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠어요, 딕슨.”"], ["said Margaret, in a tone of quiet authority.", "마거릿이 조용한 위엄이 실린 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["I beg your pardon, Miss!", "죄송합니다, 아가씨! 제 생각이 온통 가엾은 우리……"], ["My thoughts was otherwise occupied in thinking of my poor—— of Mrs. Hale.”", "헤일 부인께 가 있어서요.”"]], [["“Papa!”", "“아버지!”"], ["said Margaret, “it is this suspense that is bad for you both.", "마거릿이 말했다. “두 분 다 이 어정쩡함이 나쁜 거예요."], ["Of course, mamma must feel your change of opinions: we can’t help that,”", "물론 어머니는 아버지의 생각이 바뀐 걸 마음 아파하실 수밖에 없어요. 그건 어쩔 수 없는 일이고요.”"], ["she continued softly; “but now the course is clear, at least to a certain point.", "그녀가 부드럽게 말을 이었다. “하지만 이제 적어도 어느 정도까지는 길이 분명해졌잖아요."], ["And I think, papa, that I could get mamma to help me in planning, if you could tell me what to plan for.", "그리고 아버지, 무엇을 준비해야 하는지 말씀해 주시면 어머니도 제가 계획 세우는 걸 거들게 할 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["She has never expressed any wish in any way, and only thinks of what can’t be helped.", "어머니는 어떤 식으로든 바라는 걸 말씀하신 적이 없고, 어쩔 수 없는 일만 생각하고 계세요."], ["Are we to go straight to Milton?", "우리는 곧장 밀턴으로 가는 건가요?"], ["Have you taken a house there?”", "거기 집은 구하셨어요?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니다.”"], ["he replied, “I suppose we must go into lodgings, and look about for a house.”", "그가 대답했다. “셋방에 들어가 지내면서 집을 알아봐야 할 것 같구나.”"]], [["“And pack up the furniture so that it can be left at the railway station, till we have met with one?”", "“그러면 세간은 집을 구할 때까지 기차역에 맡겨 둘 수 있게 꾸려 두고요?”"]], [["“I suppose so.", "“그래야겠지."], ["Do what you think best.", "네가 가장 좋다고 생각하는 대로 하렴."], ["Only remember, we shall have much less money to spend.”", "다만 앞으로는 쓸 돈이 훨씬 적으리라는 것만 기억하고.”"]], [["They had never had much superfluity, as Margaret knew.", "마거릿이 알기로 그들에게 여유라 할 것이 넉넉했던 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["She felt that it was a great weight suddenly thrown upon her shoulders.", "그녀는 갑자기 자기 어깨에 큰 무게가 실렸다고 느꼈다."], ["Four months ago, all the decisions she needed to make were what dress she would wear for dinner, and to help Edith to draw out the lists of who should take down whom in the dinner parties at home.", "4개월 전만 해도 그녀가 내려야 할 결정이라고는 만찬에 어떤 드레스를 입을까 하는 것과, 집에서 여는 만찬에서 누가 누구를 데리고 내려갈지 이디스가 명단을 짜는 걸 돕는 일뿐이었다."], ["Nor was the household in which she lived one that called for much decision.", "그녀가 살던 집안도 결정이랄 것이 그리 필요한 곳이 아니었다."], ["Except in the one grand case of Captain Lennox’s offer, everything went on with the regularity of clockwork.", "레녹스 대위의 청혼이라는 단 하나의 큰일을 빼면 모든 것이 시계태엽처럼 규칙적으로 굴러갔다."], ["Once a year, there was a long discussion between her aunt and Edith as to whether they should go to the Isle of Wight, abroad, or to Scotland; but at such times Margaret herself was secure of drifting, without any exertion of her own, into the quiet harbour of home.", "한 해에 한 번 이모와 이디스 사이에 와이트섬으로 갈지, 외국으로 갈지, 스코틀랜드로 갈지를 두고 긴 의논이 오갔지만, 그럴 때에도 마거릿은 아무 애도 쓰지 않고 고향이라는 고요한 항구로 떠밀려 갈 것이 확실했다."], ["Now, since that day when Mr. Lennox came, and startled her into a decision, every day brought some question, momentous to her, and to those whom she loved, to be settled.", "그런데 레녹스 씨가 찾아와 그녀를 화들짝 결정 앞에 세운 그날 이후로는, 날마다 그녀에게도 그녀가 사랑하는 이들에게도 중대한 문제가 하나씩 다가와 결정을 기다렸다."]], [["Her father went up after tea to sit with his wife.", "아버지는 차를 마신 뒤 아내와 함께 있으려고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Margaret remained alone in the drawing-room.", "마거릿은 응접실에 혼자 남았다."], ["Suddenly she took a candle and went into her father’s study for a great atlas, and lugging it back into the drawing-room, she began to pore over the map of England.", "문득 그녀는 촛불을 들고 아버지의 서재로 가 커다란 지도책을 가져왔고, 그것을 낑낑대며 응접실로 끌고 와 잉글랜드 지도를 들여다보기 시작했다."], ["She was ready to look up brightly when her father came down stairs.", "아버지가 아래층으로 내려왔을 때 그녀는 환하게 고개를 들 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“I have hit upon such a beautiful plan.", "“아주 근사한 계획이 떠올랐어요. 여기 보세요."], ["Look here—in Darkshire, hardly the breadth of my finger from Milton, is Heston, which I have often heard of from people living in the north as such a pleasant little bathing-place.", "다크셔에, 밀턴에서 제 손가락 너비도 안 되는 데에 헤스턴이 있어요. 북쪽에 사는 사람들한테서 아주 좋은 자그마한 해수욕장이라고 여러 번 들었거든요."], ["Now, don’t you think we could get mamma there with Dixon, while you and I go and look at houses, and get one all ready for her in Milton?", "자, 우리가 어머니를 딕슨과 함께 저기로 보내 드리고, 그동안 아버지와 제가 집을 보러 다니며 밀턴에 어머니 지내실 집을 다 마련해 두면 어떨까요?"], ["She would get a breath of sea air to set her up for the winter, and be spared all the fatigue, and Dixon would enjoy taking care of her.”", "어머니는 바닷바람을 쐬어 겨울을 날 기운을 얻으시고 고생은 하나도 안 하시게 되고, 딕슨은 어머니를 돌보는 걸 즐거워할 거예요.”"]], [["“Is Dixon to go with us?”", "“딕슨도 우리와 함께 가는 거냐?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale, in a kind of helpless dismay.", "헤일 씨가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 낭패한 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "“그럼요!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“Dixon quite intends it, and I don’t know what mamma would do without her.”", "“딕슨은 그럴 작정인걸요. 게다가 딕슨 없이 어머니가 어떻게 지내실지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“But we shall have to put up with a very different way of living, I am afraid.", "“하지만 앞으로는 아주 다른 살림살이를 견뎌야 할 텐데 걱정이구나."], ["Everything is so much dearer in a town.", "도시에서는 모든 것이 훨씬 비싸다."], ["I doubt if Dixon can make herself comfortable.", "딕슨이 편히 지낼 수 있을지 모르겠구나."], ["To tell you the truth, Margaret, I sometimes feel as if that woman gave herself airs.”", "솔직히 말하면 마거릿, 나는 이따금 그 사람이 유세를 부린다는 생각이 든다.”"]], [["“To be sure she does, papa,”", "“물론 그렇지요, 아버지.” 마거릿이 대답했다."], ["replied Margaret; “and if she has to put up with a different style of living, we shall have to put up with her airs, which will be worse.", "“그리고 딕슨이 다른 방식의 살림을 견뎌야 한다면, 우리는 그 유세를 견뎌야 할 텐데 그게 더 고약하겠지요. 하지만 딕슨은 정말로 우리 모두를 사랑하고, 우리를 떠나면 비참해할 게 틀림없어요."], ["But she really loves us all, and would be miserable to leave us, I am sure—especially in this change; so, for mamma’s sake, and for the sake of her faithfulness, I do think she must go.”", "특히 이런 변화를 앞두고는요. 그러니 어머니를 위해서도, 그 충직함을 보아서도 저는 딕슨이 꼭 가야 한다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Very well, my dear.", "“좋다, 얘야. 계속하렴."], ["Go on. I am resigned.", "나는 받아들이마."], ["How far is Heston from Milton?", "헤스턴은 밀턴에서 얼마나 머냐?"], ["The breadth of one of your fingers does not give me a very clear idea of distance.”", "네 손가락 하나의 너비로는 거리가 잘 그려지지 않는구나.”"]], [["“Well, then, I suppose it is thirty miles; that is not much!”", "“음, 그러면 30마일쯤 되는 것 같아요. 그리 멀지 않네요!”"]], [["“Not in distance, but in—. Never mind!", "“거리로야 그렇지만, 다른 걸로 치면……"], ["If you really think it will do your mother good, let it be fixed so.”", "아니, 됐다! 정말로 네 어머니에게 좋을 것 같으면 그렇게 정하자꾸나.”"]], [["This was a great step.", "이것은 커다란 진전이었다."], ["Now Margaret could work, and act, and plan in good earnest. And now Mrs.", "이제 마거릿은 본격적으로 일하고 움직이고 계획할 수 있었다."], ["Hale could rouse herself from her languor, and forget her real suffering in thinking of the pleasure and the delight of going to the sea-side. Her only regret was that Mr.", "이제 헤일 부인도 나른함에서 몸을 일으켜 바닷가로 간다는 기쁨과 즐거움을 생각하며 진짜 고통을 잊을 수 있었다."], ["Hale could not be with her all the fortnight she was to be there, as he had been for a whole fortnight once, when they were engaged, and she was staying with Sir John and Lady Beresford at Torquay.", "그녀의 단 하나 아쉬움은 그곳에서 지낼 2주 내내 헤일 씨가 함께 있어 줄 수 없다는 것이었다. 약혼 시절 그녀가 존 베러스퍼드 경 부부와 함께 토키에 머물 때 그가 꼬박 2주를 함께 있어 준 적이 있었는데 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# 제6장"]], [["“Unwatch’d the garden bough shall sway, The tender blossom flutter down, Unloved that beech will gather brown, The maple burn itself away;", "아무도 지켜보지 않는 가운데 정원의 가지는 흔들리고, 여린 꽃송이는 하늘하늘 떨어지리라. 사랑받지 못한 채 저 너도밤나무는 갈색으로 물들고, 단풍나무는 스스로를 태워 사위어 가리라."]], [["Unloved, the sun-flower, shining fair, Ray round with flames her disk of seed, And many a rose-carnation feed With summer spice the humming air;", "사랑받지 못한 채 해바라기는 곱게 빛나며 씨앗의 둥근 판 둘레로 불꽃을 뻗치고, 수많은 장미빛 카네이션은 벌 소리 잉잉거리는 대기를 여름의 향내로 채우리라."]], [["Till from the garden and the wild A fresh association blow And year by year the landscape grow Familiar to the stranger’s child;", "마침내 정원에서도 들판에서도 새로운 인연이 불어와, 해가 갈수록 그 풍경이 낯선 이의 아이에게 낯익어질 때까지."]], [["As year by year the labourer tills His wonted glebe, or lops the glades; And year by year our memory fades From all the circle of the hills.”", "해마다 일꾼이 늘 갈던 밭을 갈고 숲의 빈터를 쳐 내는 동안, 해마다 우리에 대한 기억은 저 언덕들이 두른 둘레에서 스러져 가리라."], ["TENNYSON.", "— 테니슨."]], [["The last day came; the house was full of packing-cases, which were being carted off at the front door, to the nearest railway station.", "마지막 날이 왔다. 집 안은 포장 상자로 가득했고, 그것들은 현관 앞에서 짐수레에 실려 가장 가까운 기차역으로 옮겨지고 있었다."], ["Even the pretty lawn at the side of the house, was made unsightly and untidy by the straw that had been wafted upon it through the open door and windows.", "집 옆의 어여쁘던 잔디밭조차 열린 문과 창으로 날려 나온 짚 때문에 보기 흉하고 어수선해졌다."], ["The rooms had a strange echoing sound in them,—and the light came harshly and strongly in through the uncurtained windows,—seeming already unfamiliar and strange.", "방마다 낯선 울림이 돌았고, 커튼을 걷어 낸 창으로 빛이 모질고 세차게 들이쳐 벌써부터 낯설고 서먹해 보였다."], ["Mrs. Hale’s dressing-room was left untouched to the last; and there she and Dixon were packing up clothes, and interrupting each other every now and then to exclaim at, and turn over with fond regard, some forgotten treasure, in the shape of some relic of the children while they were yet little.", "헤일 부인의 화장방만은 맨 마지막까지 손대지 않고 두었는데, 거기서 부인과 딕슨은 옷가지를 꾸리다가 이따금 서로 말을 끊고는 아이들이 아직 어리던 시절의 유물 같은, 잊고 있던 보물을 발견하고 탄성을 지르며 애틋한 눈길로 뒤적이곤 했다."], ["They did not make much progress with their work.", "두 사람의 일은 그리 진척되지 않았다."], ["Down-stairs, Margaret stood calm and collected, ready to counsel or advise the men who had been called in to help the cook and Charlotte.", "아래층에서는 마거릿이 침착하고 차분하게 서서, 요리사와 샬럿을 도우라고 불러들인 일꾼들에게 지시하고 조언할 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["These two last, crying between whiles, wondered how the young lady could keep up so this last day, and settled it between them that she was not likely to care much for Helstone, having been so long in London.", "요리사와 샬럿은 틈틈이 울면서 아가씨가 이 마지막 날을 어떻게 저리 견뎌 내는지 의아해했고, 그토록 오래 런던에 있었으니 헬스톤에 그리 마음을 두지 않는 모양이라고 둘이서 결론을 지었다."], ["There she stood, very pale and quiet, with her large grave eyes observing everything—up to every present circumstance however small.", "마거릿은 그곳에 아주 창백하고 조용하게 서서, 크고 진지한 눈으로 모든 것을, 아무리 사소한 일이라도 눈앞의 온갖 사정을 살피고 있었다."], ["They could not understand how her heart was aching all the time, with a heavy pressure that no sighs could lift off or relieve, and how constant exertion for her perceptive faculties was the only way to keep herself from crying out with pain.", "두 사람은 그동안 내내 그녀의 가슴이 어떤 한숨으로도 들어 올리거나 덜어 낼 수 없는 무거운 압박에 얼마나 아파하고 있었는지, 그리고 지각하는 능력을 끊임없이 부려 쓰는 것만이 아파서 울부짖지 않을 유일한 길이었음을 알 수 없었다."], ["Moreover, if she gave way, who was to act?", "게다가 그녀가 무너지면 누가 나서서 일을 처리한단 말인가?"], ["Her father was examining papers, books, registers, what not, in the vestry with the clerk; and when he came in, there were his own books to pack up, which no one but himself could do to his satisfaction.", "아버지는 성구실에서 서기와 함께 서류며 책이며 교적부며 온갖 것을 살피고 있었고, 안으로 들어오면 자기 책들을 꾸려야 했는데 그것은 그의 마음에 들게 해낼 수 있는 사람이 그 자신 말고는 없었다."], ["Besides, was Margaret one to give way before strange men, or even household friends like the cook and Charlotte?", "게다가 마거릿이 낯선 사내들 앞에서, 아니 요리사나 샬럿처럼 집안 식구나 다름없는 이들 앞에서라도 무너질 사람이던가?"], ["Not she!", "그럴 리가!"], ["But at last the four packers went into the kitchen to their tea; and Margaret moved stiffly and slowly away from the place in the hall where she had been standing so long, out through the bare echoing drawing-room, into the twilight of an early November evening.", "그러나 마침내 짐 꾸리던 네 사람이 차를 마시러 부엌으로 들어갔고, 마거릿은 그토록 오래 서 있던 현관의 그 자리에서 뻣뻣하고 느릿하게 몸을 옮겨, 텅 빈 채 울리는 응접실을 지나 11월 초저녁의 어스름 속으로 나갔다. 부드럽고 흐릿한 안개가 얇은 너울처럼 드리워 온갖 것을 흐릿하게 했으나 감추지는 못했고, 해가 아직 완전히 지지 않은 터라 그 모든 것에 연보랏빛을 입혀 놓았다."], ["There was a filmy veil of soft dull mist obscuring, but not hiding, all objects, giving them a lilac hue, for the sun had not yet fully set; a robin was singing,—perhaps, Margaret thought, the very robin that her father had so often talked of as his winter pet, and for which he had made, with his own hands, a kind of robin-house by his study-window.", "울새 한 마리가 노래하고 있었다. 아마도, 하고 마거릿은 생각했다, 아버지가 겨울철 애완 새라며 그토록 자주 이야기하던 바로 그 울새일 것이다."], ["The leaves were more gorgeous than ever; the first touch of frost would lay them all low on the ground.", "아버지는 서재 창가에 손수 울새 집 같은 것을 지어 주기까지 했다. 나뭇잎은 그 어느 때보다 화려했다."], ["Already one or two kept constantly floating down, amber and golden in the low slanting sun-rays.", "첫서리가 한번 스치면 잎들은 죄다 땅에 내려앉으리라. 벌써 한두 잎은 낮게 비껴 드는 햇살에 호박빛으로, 금빛으로 물든 채 쉼 없이 떠내려 오고 있었다."]], [["Margaret went along the walk under the pear-tree wall.", "마거릿은 배나무를 올린 담 아래 오솔길을 따라 걸었다."], ["She had never been along it since she paced it at Henry Lennox’s side.", "헨리 레녹스 곁에서 그 길을 거닌 뒤로 한 번도 거기를 지나지 않았다."], ["Here, at this bed of thyme, he began to speak of what she must not think of now.", "여기, 이 백리향 화단께에서 그는 그녀가 지금은 생각해서도 안 될 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Her eyes were on that late-blowing rose as she was trying to answer; and she had caught the idea of the vivid beauty of the feathery leaves of the carrots in the very middle of his last sentence.", "대답하려 애쓰는 동안 그녀의 눈은 늦게 핀 저 장미에 가 있었고, 그가 마지막 말을 하는 한복판에서 그녀는 당근의 깃털 같은 잎이 지닌 선명한 아름다움에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 겨우 두 주 전의 일이었다!"], ["Only a fortnight ago! And all so changed!", "그런데 모든 것이 이토록 달라지다니!"], ["Where was he now?", "그는 지금 어디에 있을까?"], ["In London,—going through the old round; dining with the old Harley Street set, or with gayer young friends of his own.", "런던에서 예전의 그 쳇바퀴를 돌고 있겠지. 할리 가의 옛 무리와, 아니면 좀 더 흥겨운 제 또래 친구들과 저녁을 먹고 있겠지."], ["Even now, while she walked sadly through that damp and drear garden in the dusk, with everything falling and fading, and turning to decay around her, he might be gladly putting away his law-books after a day of satisfactory toil, and freshening himself up, as he had told her he often did, by a run in the Temple Gardens, taking in the while the grand inarticulate mighty roar of tens of thousands of busy men, nigh at hand, but not seen, and catching ever, at his quick turns, glimpses of the lights of the city coming up out of the depths of the river.", "지금 이 순간에도, 그녀가 온갖 것이 떨어지고 시들고 썩어 가는 이 축축하고 스산한 정원을 어스름 속에 서글피 거니는 동안, 그는 흡족한 하루의 노고를 마치고 기꺼이 법률 책을 치우고는, 늘 그런다고 그녀에게 말한 대로 템플 정원을 한 바퀴 뛰며 몸을 개운하게 하고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그러면서 바로 곁에 있으나 보이지는 않는 수만 명 분주한 사람들의 웅장하고 알아들을 수 없는 거대한 포효를 들이마시고, 몸을 재게 돌릴 때마다 강물 깊은 곳에서 솟아오르는 도시의 불빛을 흘끗흘끗 붙잡으면서."], ["He had often spoken to Margaret of these hasty walks, snatched in the intervals between study and dinner.", "그는 공부와 저녁 식사 사이의 짬을 훔쳐 하는 그 급한 산책 이야기를 마거릿에게 자주 했다."], ["At his best times and in his best moods had he spoken of them; and the thought of them had struck upon her fancy.", "가장 좋은 시절에, 가장 좋은 기분일 때 그는 그 이야기를 했고, 그 생각은 그녀의 공상에 인상을 남겼다."], ["Here there was no sound.", "이곳에는 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["The robin had gone away into the vast stillness of night.", "울새는 밤의 광막한 고요 속으로 사라져 버렸다."], ["Now and then, a cottage door in the distance was opened and shut, as if to admit the tired labourer to his home; but that sounded very far away.", "이따금 멀리서 오두막 문이 열렸다 닫혔다. 마치 지친 일꾼을 제집으로 들이는 듯이."], ["A stealthy, creeping, cranching sound among the crisp fallen leaves of the forest, beyond the garden, seemed almost close at hand.", "그러나 그 소리는 아주 멀게 들렸다. 정원 너머 숲의 바스락거리는 낙엽 사이로 살금살금 기어드는 듯 서걱대는 소리가 거의 코앞인 양 들려왔다."], ["Margaret knew it was some poacher.", "마거릿은 그것이 밀렵꾼이라는 것을 알았다."], ["Sitting up in her bedroom this past autumn, with the light of her candle extinguished, and purely revelling in the solemn beauty of the heavens and the earth, she had many a time seen the light noiseless leap of the poachers over the garden-fence, their quick tramp across the dewy moonlit lawn, their disappearance in the black still shadow beyond.", "지난가을 침실에 앉아 촛불을 끈 채 하늘과 땅의 엄숙한 아름다움에 오롯이 취해 있을 때, 그녀는 밀렵꾼들이 소리 없이 가볍게 정원 울타리를 뛰어넘는 것을, 이슬 맺힌 달빛 잔디밭을 재게 가로지르는 것을, 그 너머 검고 고요한 그늘 속으로 사라지는 것을 여러 번 보았다."], ["The wild adventurous freedom of their life had taken her fancy; she felt inclined to wish them success; she had no fear of them.", "그들의 삶이 지닌 거칠고 모험적인 자유는 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았다. 그녀는 그들이 잘되기를 바라고 싶어졌다."], ["But to-night she was afraid, she knew not why.", "그들이 무섭지 않았다."], ["She heard Charlotte shutting the windows, and fastening up for the night, unconscious that any one had gone out into the garden.", "그러나 오늘 밤은 무서웠고, 왜 그런지는 알 수 없었다. 샬럿이 누군가 정원에 나갔다는 것도 모른 채 창을 닫고 밤을 대비해 단속하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["A small branch—it might be of rotten wood, or it might be broken by force—came heavily down in the nearest part of the forest; Margaret ran, swift as Camilla, down to the window, and rapped at it with a hurried tremulousness which startled Charlotte within.", "작은 가지 하나가, 썩은 나뭇가지였는지 힘으로 꺾인 것이었는지, 숲에서 가장 가까운 자리에서 묵직하게 떨어졌다. 마거릿은 카밀라처럼 날래게 창가로 달려가 창을 두드렸는데, 그 다급하고 떨리는 소리에 안에 있던 샬럿이 화들짝 놀랐다."]], [["“Let me in! Let me in!", "“들여보내 줘요!"], ["It is only me, Charlotte!”", "들여보내 줘요! 나예요, 샬럿!”"], ["Her heart did not still its fluttering till she was safe in the drawing-room, with the windows fastened and bolted, and the familiar walls hemming her round, and shutting her in.", "창을 닫아걸고 빗장을 지른 응접실 안, 낯익은 벽이 사방을 에워싸 그녀를 감싸 준 그곳에 안전히 들어서고 나서야 그녀의 심장은 두근거림을 멈추었다. 그녀는 포장 상자 하나에 주저앉았다."], ["She had sate down upon a packing-case; cheerless, chill was the dreary and dismantled room—no fire, nor other light, but Charlotte’s long unsnuffed candle.", "살림을 걷어 낸 그 을씨년스러운 방은 쓸쓸하고 차가웠다. 불도 없고, 샬럿이 오래 심지를 자르지 않은 촛불 말고는 다른 빛도 없었다."], ["Charlotte looked at Margaret with surprise; and Margaret, feeling it rather than seeing it, rose up.", "샬럿이 놀란 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았다. 마거릿은 그것을 보았다기보다 느끼고는 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“I was afraid you were shutting me out altogether, Charlotte,”", "“나를 아주 밖에 내버려 두고 잠가 버리는 줄 알았어요, 샬럿.”"], ["said she, half-smiling.", "그녀가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다."], ["“And then you would never have heard me in the kitchen, and the doors into the lane and churchyard are locked long ago.”", "“그랬으면 부엌에서는 내가 부르는 소리를 영영 듣지 못했을 테고, 골목과 묘지로 통하는 문은 진작에 잠겼을 텐데요.”"]], [["“Oh, miss, I should have been sure to have missed you soon.", "“어머나, 아가씨, 저야 곧 아가씨가 안 계신 걸 알아챘을 거예요."], ["The men would have wanted you to tell them how to go on.", "일꾼들이 어떻게 하면 되는지 여쭈러 아가씨를 찾았을 테니까요."], ["And I have put tea in master’s study, as being the most comfortable room, so to speak.”", "그리고 차는 나리 서재에 차려 두었어요. 말하자면 그 방이 제일 편하니까요.”"]], [["“Thank you, Charlotte.", "“고마워요, 샬럿."], ["You are a kind girl.", "착한 아이로군요."], ["I shall be sorry to leave you.", "헤어지려니 섭섭해요."], ["You must try and write to me, if I can ever give you any little help or good advice.", "내가 자그마한 도움이나 좋은 조언이라도 줄 수 있을 것 같거든 편지를 써 보내 봐요."], ["I shall always be glad to get a letter from Helstone, you know.", "헬스톤에서 오는 편지라면 나는 언제나 반가울 테니까요."], ["I shall be sure and send you my address when I know it.”", "주소를 알게 되면 꼭 알려 줄게요.”"]], [["The study was all ready for tea.", "서재는 차를 마실 준비가 다 되어 있었다."], ["There was a good blazing fire, and unlighted candles on the table.", "불이 활활 잘 타올랐고, 탁자 위에는 아직 불을 붙이지 않은 초가 놓여 있었다."], ["Margaret sat down on the rug, partly to warm herself, for the dampness of the evening hung about her dress, and over-fatigue had made her chilly.", "마거릿은 깔개 위에 앉았다. 저녁의 습기가 옷에 배어 있었고 지나친 피로로 몸이 으슬으슬했으므로 얼마쯤은 몸을 녹이기 위해서였다."], ["She kept herself balanced by clasping her hands together round her knees; her head dropped a little towards her chest; the attitude was one of despondency, whatever her frame of mind might be.", "그녀는 두 손을 맞잡아 무릎을 감싸 몸을 지탱했다. 고개가 가슴 쪽으로 조금 떨구어졌다."], ["But when she heard her father’s step on the gravel outside, she started up, and hastily shaking her heavy black hair back, and wiping a few tears away that had come on her cheeks she knew not how, she went out to open the door for him.", "마음이야 어떠했든 그 자세는 낙심한 사람의 것이었다. 그러나 바깥 자갈길에서 아버지의 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 숱 많은 검은 머리를 서둘러 뒤로 넘기고, 어느 결에 뺨에 흘러내렸는지 모를 눈물 몇 방울을 닦고는 문을 열어 드리러 나갔다."], ["He showed far more depression than she did.", "아버지는 그녀보다 훨씬 더 침울해 보였다."], ["She could hardly get him to talk, although she tried to speak on subjects that would interest him, at the cost of an effort every time which she thought would be her last.", "그녀는 매번 이번이 마지막이겠거니 싶은 안간힘을 써 가며 아버지가 관심을 가질 만한 이야기를 꺼내 보았지만, 좀처럼 말을 하게 만들 수 없었다."]], [["“Have you been a very long walk to-day?”", "“오늘은 아주 멀리 걸으셨어요?”"], ["asked she, on seeing his refusal to touch food of any kind.", "그가 어떤 음식에도 손대기를 마다하는 것을 보고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“As far as Fordham Beeches.", "“포덤 너도밤나무 숲까지 갔다 왔다."], ["I went to see Widow Maltby; she is sadly grieved at not having wished you good-bye.", "몰트비 과부댁을 보러 갔지. 네게 작별 인사를 못 했다고 몹시 서운해하더구나."], ["She says little Susan has kept watch down the lane for days past.—Nay, Margaret, what is the matter, dear?”", "어린 수전이가 며칠째 골목을 내다보며 기다렸다는구나. 아니, 마거릿, 왜 그러니, 얘야?”"], ["The thought of the little child watching for her, and continually disappointed—from no forgetfulness on her part, but from sheer inability to leave home—was the last drop in poor Margaret’s cup, and she was sobbing away as if her heart would break.", "자기를 기다리다 번번이 실망했을 그 어린아이 생각은, 그것이 자기가 잊어서가 아니라 도무지 집을 비울 수 없어서였는데도, 가엾은 마거릿의 잔을 넘치게 하는 마지막 한 방울이 되었다. 그녀는 가슴이 미어질 듯 흐느껴 울었다."], ["Mr. Hale was distressingly perplexed.", "헤일 씨는 딱하리만큼 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["He rose, and walked nervously up and down the room.", "그는 일어나 방 안을 초조하게 오갔다."], ["Margaret tried to check herself, but would not speak until she could do so with firmness.", "마거릿은 스스로를 다잡으려 했으나 단단한 목소리로 말할 수 있게 되기 전에는 입을 열려 하지 않았다."], ["She heard him talking, as if to himself.", "아버지가 혼잣말하듯 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“I cannot bear it.", "“견딜 수가 없구나."], ["I cannot bear to see the sufferings of others.", "남이 괴로워하는 것을 보는 걸 견딜 수가 없어."], ["I think I could go through my own with patience.", "내 몫이라면 참고 지나갈 수 있을 것 같은데."], ["Oh, is there no going back?”", "아, 돌이킬 길은 없는 것인가?”"]], [["“No, father,”", "“없어요, 아버지.”"], ["said Margaret, looking straight at him, and speaking low and steadily.", "마거릿이 그를 똑바로 바라보며 낮고 흔들림 없는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It is bad to believe you in error.", "“아버지가 그르쳤다고 믿는 것도 괴로운 일이에요."], ["It would be infinitely worse to have known you a hypocrite.”", "하지만 아버지를 위선자로 알게 되는 건 그보다 한없이 더 나쁠 거예요.”"], ["She dropped her voice at the last few words, as if entertaining the idea of hypocrisy for a moment in connection with her father savoured of irreverence.", "마지막 몇 마디에서 그녀는 목소리를 낮추었다. 위선이라는 생각을 아버지와 결부해 한순간이라도 떠올리는 것이 불경스럽게 느껴지기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["“Besides,”", "“게다가,”"], ["she went on, “it is only that I am tired to-night; don’t think that I am suffering from what you have done, dear papa.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “그저 제가 오늘 밤 지쳤을 뿐이에요."], ["We can’t either of us talk about it to-night, I believe,”", "아버지가 하신 일 때문에 제가 괴로워한다고는 생각하지 마세요, 아버지."], ["said she, finding that tears and sobs would come in spite of herself.", "오늘 밤은 우리 둘 다 그 이야기를 할 수 없을 것 같아요.”"], ["“I had better go and take mamma up this cup of tea.", "눈물과 흐느낌이 자기 뜻과 상관없이 터져 나오는 것을 알고 그녀가 말했다."], ["She had hers very early, when I was too busy to go to her, and I am sure she will be glad of another now.”", "“어머니께 이 차나 한 잔 갖다 드리는 게 낫겠어요. 제가 너무 바빠 못 가 뵐 때 아주 일찍 드셨으니, 지금 한 잔 더 드리면 반기실 거예요.”"]], [["Railroad time inexorably wrenched them away from lovely, beloved Helstone, the next morning.", "이튿날 아침, 기찻길의 시간은 매정하게도 그들을 사랑스럽고 정든 헬스톤에서 떼어 냈다. 그들은 떠났다."], ["They were gone; they had seen the last of the long low parsonage home, half-covered with China-roses and pyracanthus—more homelike than ever in the morning sun that glittered on its windows, each belonging to some well-loved room.", "낮고 길게 뻗은, 중국장미와 피라칸타로 반쯤 덮인 그 목사관 집을 마지막으로 보았다. 창마다 저마다 사랑하던 방에 딸린 그 집은 유리창을 반짝이게 하는 아침 햇살 속에서 그 어느 때보다 정겨워 보였다."], ["Almost before they had settled themselves into the car, sent from Southampton to fetch them to the station, they were gone away to return no more.", "역까지 그들을 데려가려고 사우샘프턴에서 보내온 마차에 미처 자리를 잡기도 전에, 그들은 다시는 돌아오지 못할 길을 떠나 버렸다."], ["A sting at Margaret’s heart made her strive to look out to catch the last glimpse of the old church tower at the turn where she knew it might be seen above a wave of the forest trees; but her father remembered this too, and she silently acknowledged his greater right to the one window from which it could be seen.", "마거릿은 가슴을 찌르는 아픔에, 숲 나무들의 물결 위로 옛 교회 탑이 보일 만한 그 모퉁이에서 마지막으로 그것을 한 번 붙잡아 보려 애써 밖을 내다보려 했다. 그러나 아버지도 그것을 기억하고 있었고, 그녀는 그것이 보이는 단 하나의 창에 대해 아버지가 더 큰 권리를 지녔음을 말없이 인정했다."], ["She leant back and shut her eyes, and the tears welled forth, and hung glittering for an instant on the shadowing eyelashes before rolling slowly down her cheeks, and dropping, unheeded, on her dress.", "그녀는 몸을 뒤로 기대고 눈을 감았다. 눈물이 솟아올라 그늘진 속눈썹에 한순간 반짝이며 매달렸다가 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내려, 아무도 모르는 채 옷 위에 떨어졌다."]], [["They were to stop in London all night at some quiet hotel. Poor Mrs.", "그들은 런던의 어느 조용한 여관에서 하룻밤을 묵기로 되어 있었다."], ["Hale had cried in her way nearly all day long; and Dixon showed her sorrow by extreme crossness, and a continual irritable attempt to keep her petticoats from even touching the unconscious Mr. Hale, whom she regarded as the origin of all this suffering.", "가엾은 헤일 부인은 하루 종일 제 나름으로 거의 내내 울었고, 딕슨은 지독한 심통으로 자기 슬픔을 드러냈으며, 이 모든 고통의 근원이라 여기는 아무것도 모르는 헤일 씨에게 자기 치맛자락이 스치기라도 할까 봐 짜증스럽게 연신 애를 썼다."]], [["They went through the well-known streets, past houses which they had often visited, past shops in which she had lounged, impatient, by her aunt’s side, while that lady was making some important and interminable decision—nay, absolutely passed acquaintances in the streets; for though the morning had been of an incalculable length to them, and they felt as if it ought long ago to have closed in for the repose of darkness, it was the very busiest time of a London afternoon in November when they arrived there.", "그들은 낯익은 거리들을 지나갔다. 자주 찾아가던 집들을 지나고, 이모가 무엇인가 중대하고 끝없는 결정을 내리는 동안 그 곁에서 조바심을 내며 서성이던 가게들을 지나고, 아니 거리에서 아는 얼굴들과 실제로 스쳐 지나가기까지 했다."], ["It was long since Mrs.", "그들에게 그날 아침은 헤아릴 수 없이 길었고 벌써 오래전에 어둠의 안식을 위해 저물었어야 마땅하게 느껴졌지만, 그들이 그곳에 닿은 때는 11월 런던 오후의 가장 분주한 시각이었던 것이다."], ["Hale had been in London; and she roused up, almost like a child, to look about her at the different streets, and to gaze after and exclaim at the shops and carriages,—", "헤일 부인이 런던에 와 본 지는 오래된 터였다. 그녀는 거의 아이처럼 기운을 차리고 이 거리 저 거리를 둘러보았고, 가게며 마차를 눈으로 좇으며 탄성을 질렀다."]], [["“Oh, there’s Harrison’s, where I bought so many of my wedding things.", "“어머, 저기 해리슨 상점이 있네. 내 혼수를 저기서 참 많이 샀는데."], ["Dear! how altered!", "세상에!"], ["They’ve got immense plate-glass windows, larger than Crawford’s in Southampton.", "어쩌면 저렇게 달라졌을까! 사우샘프턴의 크로퍼드 상점보다 더 큰 어마어마한 판유리 창을 달았구나."], ["Oh, and there, I declare—no, it is not—yes, it is—Margaret, we have just passed Mr.", "아, 그리고 저기, 틀림없어. 아니, 아닌가."], ["Henry Lennox.", "아니야, 맞아."], ["Where can he be going, among all these shops?”", "마거릿, 우리 방금 헨리 레녹스 씨를 지나쳤어. 저이가 이 가게들 사이에서 대체 어디를 가는 걸까?”"]], [["Margaret started forwards, and as quickly fell back, half-smiling at herself for the sudden motion. They were a hundred yards away by this time; but he seemed like a relic of Helstone—he was associated with a bright morning, an eventful day, and she should have liked to have seen him, without his seeing her,—without the chance of their speaking.", "마거릿은 앞으로 몸을 내밀었다가 그만큼 빨리 도로 물러앉으며 그 갑작스러운 몸짓을 스스로 반쯤 웃어넘겼다. 그때는 이미 100야드나 떨어져 있었다. 그러나 그는 헬스톤의 유물 같았다. 그는 어느 환한 아침과, 사연 많던 하루와 얽혀 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 그를 보고 싶었다. 그가 그녀를 보지 않은 채로, 서로 말을 나눌 일 없이."]], [["The evening, without employment, passed in a room high up in an hotel, was long and heavy.", "여관 높은 층의 방에서 할 일 없이 보낸 저녁은 길고 무거웠다."], ["Mr. Hale went out to his bookseller’s, and to call on a friend or two.", "헤일 씨는 단골 서적상에게 다녀오고 친구 한둘을 찾아보러 나갔다."], ["Every one they saw, either in the house or out in the streets, appeared hurrying to some appointment, expected by, or expecting somebody.", "집 안에서든 거리에서든 그들이 보는 사람은 모두 어딘가 약속 자리로 서둘러 가는 듯했고, 누군가에게 기다려지거나 누군가를 기다리는 듯했다. 오직 그들만이 낯설고 벗도 없고 쓸쓸해 보였다."], ["They alone seemed strange and friendless, and desolate.", "그러나 1마일 안에도 마거릿은 집이란 집을 알고 있었다."], ["Yet within a mile, Margaret knew of house after house, where she for her own sake, and her mother for her aunt Shaw’s, would be welcomed, if they came in gladness, or even in peace of mind.", "그녀 자신을 보아서, 또 어머니는 쇼 이모를 보아서 환영받을 집들이었다. 다만 그들이 기쁜 마음으로, 아니 적어도 마음이 편안한 채로 찾아갔을 때의 이야기였다."], ["If they came sorrowing, and wanting sympathy in a complicated trouble like the present, then they would be felt as a shadow in all these houses of intimate acquaintances, not friends.", "슬픔을 안고, 지금 같은 얽히고설킨 곤경에 동정을 구하며 찾아간다면, 벗이 아니라 가까운 지인일 뿐인 그 모든 집에서 그들은 하나의 그늘로 느껴질 것이었다."], ["London life is too whirling and full to admit of even an hour of that deep silence of feeling which the friends of Job showed, when “they sat with him on the ground seven days and seven nights, and none spake a word unto him; for they saw that his grief was very great.”", "런던의 삶은 너무 어지럽게 돌아가고 너무 꽉 차 있어서, 욥의 벗들이 “이레 밤낮을 그와 함께 땅에 앉았으되 그의 괴로움이 심함을 보았으므로 한마디도 말하는 자가 없었더라” 할 때 보여 준 그 깊은 감정의 침묵을 단 한 시간도 허락하지 못한다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# 제7장"]], [["“Mist clogs the sunshine, Smoky dwarf houses Hem me round everywhere.”", "「안개가 햇빛을 가로막고, 연기에 그은 난쟁이 같은 집들이 사방에서 나를 에워싸는구나.」"], ["MATTHEW ARNOLD.", "— 매슈 아널드."]], [["The next afternoon, about twenty miles from Milton-Northern, they entered on the little branch railway that led to Heston.", "이튿날 오후, 밀턴노던에서 20마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 그들은 헤스턴으로 이어지는 작은 지선 철도로 들어섰다."], ["Heston itself was one long straggling street, running parallel to the seashore.", "헤스턴은 그 자체가 바닷가와 나란히 뻗은 길고 산만한 거리 하나였다."], ["It had a character of its own, as different from the little bathing-places in the south of England as they again from those of the continent.", "그곳에는 저만의 기질이 있어서, 잉글랜드 남부의 작은 해수욕 마을들과는 사뭇 달랐다. 그 남부 마을들이 대륙의 것들과 다른 만큼이나 말이다."], ["To use a Scotch word, everything looked more “purpose-like.”", "스코틀랜드 말을 빌리자면 무엇이든 더 “쓸모 있어” 보였다."], ["The country carts had more iron, and less wood and leather about the horse-gear; the people in the streets, although on pleasure bent, had yet a busy mind.", "시골 짐수레는 마구에 쇠가 더 많고 나무와 가죽이 덜 들어갔다. 거리의 사람들은 놀이 삼아 나온 길인데도 마음만은 분주했다."], ["The colours looked grayer—more enduring, not so gay and pretty.", "빛깔은 한결 잿빛으로 보였다. 더 오래갈 듯하고, 그리 밝지도 곱지도 않았다."], ["There were no smock-frocks, even among the country-folk; they retarded motion, and were apt to catch on machinery, and so the habit of wearing them had died out.", "시골 사람들 사이에서도 헐렁한 작업복 웃옷은 보이지 않았다. 그것은 몸놀림을 더디게 하고 기계에 걸리기 십상이어서 입는 버릇이 아주 사라져 버린 것이다."], ["In such towns in the south of England, Margaret had seen the shopmen, when not employed in their business, lounging a little at their doors, enjoying the fresh air, and the look up and down the street.", "잉글랜드 남부의 그런 읍내에서 마거릿은 가게 사람들이 장사가 한가할 때면 문간에 느긋이 서서 맑은 공기를 쐬고 거리를 오르내리는 풍경을 바라보는 것을 보아 왔다."], ["Here, if they had any leisure from customers, they made themselves business in the shop—even, Margaret fancied, to the unnecessary unrolling and re-rolling of ribbons.", "이곳에서는 손님이 없어 짬이 나면 그들은 가게 안에서 스스로 일거리를 만들어 냈다. 마거릿이 보기에는 굳이 그럴 것도 없이 리본을 풀었다가 도로 감기까지 했다."], ["All these differences struck upon her mind, as she and her mother went out next morning to look for lodgings.", "이튿날 아침 어머니와 함께 셋집을 구하러 나섰을 때, 이 모든 차이가 그녀의 마음에 하나하나 와 닿았다."]], [["Their two nights at hotels had cost more than Mr. Hale had anticipated, and they were glad to take the first clean, cheerful rooms they met with that were at liberty to receive them. There, for the first time for many days, did Margaret feel at rest. There was a dreaminess in the rest, too, which made it still more perfect and luxurious to repose in. The distant sea, lapped the sandy shore with measured sound; the nearer cries of the donkey-boys; the unusual scenes moving before her like pictures, which she cared not in her laziness to have fully explained before they passed away; the stroll down to the beach to breathe the sea-air, soft and warm on that sandy shore even to the end of November; the great long misty sea-line touching the tender-coloured sky; the white sail of a distant boat turning silver in some pale sunbeam:—it seemed as if she could dream her life away in such luxury of pensiveness, in which she made her present all in all, from not daring to think of the past, or wishing to contemplate the future.", "여관에서 보낸 이틀 밤은 헤일 씨가 짐작했던 것보다 비용이 더 들었으므로, 그들은 맨 처음 만난 깨끗하고 밝은 방을, 마침 들어갈 수 있는 곳이라 하여 반갑게 잡았다. 그곳에서 마거릿은 여러 날 만에 처음으로 쉼을 느꼈다. 게다가 그 쉼에는 꿈결 같은 데가 있어서, 몸을 누이기에 한층 더 온전하고 호사스러웠다. 멀리 바다는 고른 소리로 모랫가를 핥았다. 좀 더 가까이에서는 나귀 몰이 아이들의 외침이 들려왔다. 낯선 풍경들은 그림처럼 눈앞을 지나갔는데, 나른한 그녀는 그것들이 지나가 버리기 전에 굳이 다 알아보려 들지도 않았다. 바닷바람을 쐬려 해변까지 걸어 내려가면 11월 끝자락인데도 그 모래톱의 바람은 부드럽고 따뜻했다. 아득히 길게 흐린 수평선은 여린 빛깔의 하늘에 맞닿아 있었다. 멀리 뜬 배의 흰 돛은 창백한 햇살 한 줄기에 은빛으로 물들었다. 이런 호사스러운 시름 속에서라면 평생을 꿈꾸듯 흘려보낼 수도 있을 것 같았다. 그 속에서 그녀는 지난 일을 감히 떠올리지도, 앞일을 헤아려 보고 싶지도 않았기에 오직 지금 이 순간만을 전부로 삼았다."]], [["But the future must be met, however stern and iron it be.", "그러나 앞일이란 아무리 모질고 쇠 같더라도 맞닥뜨려야 하는 법이다."], ["One evening it was arranged that Margaret and her father should go the next day to Milton-Northern, and look out for a house. Mr.", "어느 저녁, 마거릿과 아버지가 이튿날 밀턴노던으로 가서 집을 알아보기로 정해졌다."], ["Hale had received several letters from Mr. Bell, and one or two from Mr.", "헤일 씨는 벨 씨에게서 편지를 여러 통 받았고 손턴 씨에게서도 한두 통 받은 터라, 그곳에서 자신의 처지와 성공할 가망에 관한 여러 가지 사정을 당장 알아보고 싶어 했다."], ["Thornton, and he was anxious to ascertain at once a good many particulars respecting his position and chances of success there, which he could only do by an interview with the latter gentleman.", "그것은 뒤엣분을 직접 만나야만 알 수 있는 일이었다. 마거릿은 이사를 서둘러야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Margaret knew that they ought to be removing; but she had a repugnance to the idea of a manufacturing town, and believed that her mother was receiving benefit from Heston air, so she would willingly have deferred the expedition to Milton.", "그러나 공장 도시라는 생각 자체가 싫었고, 어머니가 헤스턴의 공기 덕을 보고 있다고 여겼으므로, 밀턴 나들이를 기꺼이 미루고 싶었다."]], [["For several miles before they reached Milton, they saw a deep lead-coloured cloud hanging over the horizon in the direction in which it lay.", "밀턴에 닿기 몇 마일 전부터 그들은 그 도시가 있는 쪽 지평선 위에 짙은 납빛 구름이 걸려 있는 것을 보았다. 겨울 하늘의 옅은 잿빛 도는 푸른색과 대비되어 그것은 한층 더 어두워 보였다."], ["It was all the darker from contrast with the pale gray-blue of the wintry sky; for in Heston there had been the earliest signs of frost.", "헤스턴에는 이미 첫서리의 조짐이 있었던 것이다. 도시에 가까워지자 공기에는 연기의 맛과 냄새가 희미하게 배어 있었다."], ["Nearer to the town, the air had a faint taste and smell of smoke; perhaps, after all, more a loss of the fragrance of grass and herbage than any positive taste or smell.", "어쩌면 그것은 무슨 뚜렷한 맛이나 냄새라기보다 풀과 초목의 향기가 사라진 자리에 가까웠는지도 모른다. 그들은 길고 곧고 희망이라고는 없는 거리들을 휙휙 지나쳤다."], ["Quick they were whirled over long, straight, hopeless streets of regularly-built houses, all small and of brick.", "하나같이 작고 벽돌로 지은, 판에 박은 듯한 집들이 늘어선 거리였다. 여기저기 창이 숱하게 뚫린 커다랗고 길쭉한 공장이 병아리 떼 가운데 선 암탉처럼 솟아 있었고, “법을 어긴”"], ["Here and there a great oblong many-windowed factory stood up, like a hen among her chickens, puffing out black “unparliamentary”", "검은 연기를 뿜어내고 있었다. 마거릿이 비를 부르는 조짐이라 여겼던 그 구름의 정체가 그것으로 넉넉히 설명되었다."], ["smoke, and sufficiently accounting for the cloud which Margaret had taken to foretell rain.", "역에서 여관까지 좀 더 크고 넓은 거리를 지나는 동안 그들은 쉴 새 없이 멈춰야 했다."], ["As they drove through the larger and wider streets, from the station to the hotel, they had to stop constantly; great loaded lurries blocked up the not over-wide thoroughfares.", "짐을 잔뜩 실은 큰 수레들이 그리 넓지도 않은 큰길을 가로막고 있었던 것이다. 마거릿도 이모와 마차를 타고 나갔다가 이따금 런던 시내에 들어가 본 적이 있었다."], ["Margaret had now and then been into the city in her drives with her aunt.", "그러나 그곳의 육중하고 굼뜬 수레들은 저마다 쓰임과 목적이 다양해 보였다."], ["But there the heavy lumbering vehicles seemed various in their purposes and intent; here every van, every waggon and truck, bore cotton, either in the raw shape in bags, or the woven shape in bales of calico.", "이곳에서는 짐마차든 짐수레든 화물차든 하나같이 면화를 실어 날랐다. 자루에 담긴 날것 그대로거나, 옥양목 꾸러미로 짜인 것이거나 둘 중 하나였다."], ["People thronged the footpaths, most of them well-dressed as regarded the material, but with a slovenly looseness which struck Margaret as different from the shabby, threadbare smartness of a similar class in London.", "사람들이 인도에 몰려다녔는데, 대부분 옷감으로 보아서는 잘 차려입은 편이었으나 어딘가 헐렁하고 아무렇게나 걸친 데가 있었다. 마거릿에게는 그것이 런던의 같은 계층이 지닌 낡고 해졌으나 말쑥한 맵시와는 다르게 느껴졌다."]], [["“New Street,”", "“뉴 스트리트로구나.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“This, I believe, is the principal street in Milton.", "“이게 밀턴의 중심 거리인 모양이다."], ["Bell has often spoken to me about it.", "벨이 내게 여러 번 이야기했지."], ["It was the opening of this street from a lane into a great thoroughfare, thirty years ago, which has caused his property to rise so much in value.", "30년 전에 이 거리가 골목에서 큰길로 뚫린 덕에 그 사람 재산 값이 그토록 뛰어올랐다는구나. 손턴 씨 공장도 그리 멀지 않은 데 있을 게다."], ["Mr. Thornton’s mill must be somewhere not very far off, for he is Mr.", "그가 벨 씨의 세입자니까 말이야."], ["Bell’s tenant. But I fancy he dates from his warehouse.”", "하지만 편지에 적는 주소는 창고 쪽인 듯싶더구나.”"]], [["“Where is our hotel, papa?”", "“우리 여관은 어디예요, 아버지?”"]], [["“Close to the end of this street, I believe.", "“이 거리 끝머리 가까이일 게다."], ["Shall we have lunch before or after we have looked at the houses we marked in the Milton Times?”", "점심을 먹고 나서 「밀턴 타임스」에 표시해 둔 집들을 보러 갈까, 아니면 먼저 보고 올까?”"]], [["“Oh, let us get our work done first.”", "“아, 일부터 마쳐요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“그러자꾸나."], ["Then I will only see if there is any note or letter for me from Mr.", "그럼 손턴 씨한테서 온 쪽지나 편지가 있는지만 보마."], ["Thornton, who said he would let me know anything he might hear about these houses, and then we will set off.", "그 집들에 관해 무엇이든 듣는 대로 알려 주겠다고 했으니 말이다. 그러고 나서 곧장 나서자."], ["We will keep the cab; it will be safer than losing ourselves, and being too late for the train this afternoon.”", "마차는 붙들어 두자꾸나. 길을 헤매다가 오늘 오후 기차를 놓치는 것보다는 그편이 안전할 테니.”"]], [["There were no letters awaiting him.", "그를 기다리는 편지는 없었다."], ["They set out on their house-hunting.", "그들은 집을 구하러 나섰다."], ["Thirty pounds a-year was all they could afford to give, but in Hampshire they could have met with a roomy house and pleasant garden for the money.", "낼 수 있는 돈은 연 30파운드가 고작이었는데, 햄프셔라면 그 돈으로 넉넉한 집과 아담한 뜰까지 얻을 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Here, even the necessary accommodation of two sitting-rooms and four bed-rooms seemed unattainable.", "이곳에서는 거실 2개와 침실 4개라는 최소한의 살림 공간조차 얻을 수 없어 보였다."], ["They went through their list, rejecting each as they visited it.", "그들은 적어 온 목록을 훑으며 한 집씩 찾아가 보고는 차례로 물리쳤다."], ["They then looked at each other in dismay.", "그러고는 낙담한 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“We must go back to the second, I think. That one,—in Crampton, don’t they call the suburb? There were three sitting-rooms; don’t you remember how we laughed at the number compared with the three bed-rooms? But I have planned it all. The front room down-stairs is to be your study and our dining-room (poor papa!), for you know, we settled mamma is to have as cheerful a sitting-room as we can get; and that front room up-stairs, with the atrocious blue and pink paper and heavy cornice, had really a pretty view over the plain, with a great bend of river, or canal, or whatever it is, down below. Then I could have the little bed-room behind, in that projection at the head of the first flight of stairs over the kitchen, you know—and you and mamma the room behind the drawing-room, and that closet in the roof will make you a splendid dressing-room.”", "“두 번째 집으로 돌아가는 수밖에 없을 것 같아요. 그 집 말이에요. 크램프턴이라고 하던가요, 그 변두리 동네? 거실이 3개나 있었잖아요. 침실도 3개인데 거실이 그만큼이나 된다고 우리가 웃었던 것 기억나세요? 그런데 제가 다 궁리해 놓았어요. 아래층 앞방은 아버지 서재 겸 우리 식당으로 쓰기로 해요. 가엾은 아버지! 어머니께는 되도록 밝은 거실을 드려야 한다고 우리끼리 정해 두었잖아요. 그리고 그 흉한 파랑 분홍 벽지에 육중한 처마 장식이 달린 위층 앞방은 저 아래 벌판이 정말 예쁘게 내다보였어요. 강인지 운하인지 뭔지가 크게 굽어 도는 것도 보이고요. 저는 그 뒤쪽 작은 침실을 쓰면 돼요. 부엌 위쪽, 첫 층계참 끝에 튀어나온 그 방 있잖아요. 아버지와 어머니는 응접실 뒤쪽 방을 쓰시고요. 지붕 밑의 그 골방은 아버지께 훌륭한 옷방이 되어 줄 거예요.”"]], [["“But Dixon, and the girl we are to have to help?”", "“그럼 딕슨은? 또 일손을 도우라고 둘 아이는?”"]], [["“Oh, wait a minute.", "“아, 잠깐만요."], ["I am overpowered by the discovery of my own genius for management.", "제 살림 솜씨가 얼마나 대단한지 깨닫고 제가 다 얼떨떨하네요."], ["Dixon is to have—let me see, I had it once—the back sitting-room.", "딕슨은 어디 보자, 아까는 생각해 두었는데, 뒤쪽 거실을 쓰기로 해요."], ["I think she will like that.", "딕슨도 마음에 들어 할 거예요."], ["She grumbles so much about the stairs at Heston; and the girl is to have that sloping attic over your room and mamma’s.", "헤스턴에서는 층계 때문에 어찌나 투덜대는지 몰라요. 그리고 그 아이는 아버지와 어머니 방 위의 비스듬한 다락을 쓰면 되고요."], ["Won’t that do?”", "그만하면 되지 않아요?”"]], [["“I dare say it will.", "“그렇겠구나."], ["But the papers. What taste!", "하지만 그 벽지 말이다."], ["And the over-loading such a house with colour and such heavy cornices!”", "무슨 취향이 그러냐! 그런 집에 색을 그토록 처바르고 처마 장식은 또 그렇게 육중하게 두르다니!”"]], [["“Never mind, papa! Surely you can charm the landlord into re-papering one or two of the rooms—the drawing-room and your bed-room—for mamma will come most in contact with them; and your book-shelves will hide a great deal of that gaudy pattern in the dining-room.”", "“괜찮아요, 아버지! 집주인을 잘 구슬러서 방 한둘쯤은 도배를 다시 하게 하실 수 있을 거예요. 응접실과 아버지 침실 말이에요. 어머니가 가장 많이 계실 곳이니까요. 그리고 식당의 그 요란한 무늬는 아버지 책장이 상당 부분 가려 줄 거고요.”"]], [["“Then you think it the best?", "“그럼 네 생각에는 그 집이 제일 낫다는 게냐?"], ["If so, I had better go at once and call on this Mr.", "그렇다면 당장 가서 광고에 적힌 그 돈킨이라는 사람을 만나 보는 게 좋겠구나."], ["Donkin, to whom the advertisement refers me.", "너는 여관까지 데려다주마."], ["I will take you back to the hotel, where you can order lunch, and rest, and by the time it is ready, I shall be with you.", "거기서 점심을 시켜 놓고 쉬고 있으려무나. 준비가 될 때쯤이면 나도 돌아와 있을 게다."], ["I hope I shall be able to get new papers.”", "벽지는 새로 얻어 낼 수 있으면 좋겠구나.”"]], [["Margaret hoped so too, though she said nothing.", "마거릿도 그러기를 바랐지만 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She had never come fairly in contact with the taste that loves ornament, however bad, more than the plainness and simplicity which are of themselves the framework of elegance.", "그녀는 아무리 흉한 것이라도 장식이라면 좋아하는 취향, 그 자체로 우아함의 뼈대가 되는 담백함과 소박함보다 장식을 더 좋아하는 취향과 제대로 부딪쳐 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Her father took her through the entrance of the hotel, and leaving her at the foot of the staircase, went to the address of the landlord of the house they had fixed upon.", "아버지는 그녀를 데리고 여관 현관을 지나 층계 아래에 남겨 둔 채, 그들이 정한 집의 주인이 산다는 주소로 떠났다."], ["Just as Margaret had her hand on the door of their sitting-room, she was followed by a quick-stepping waiter:", "마거릿이 막 그들의 거실 문에 손을 얹었을 때, 종종걸음으로 뒤따라온 종업원이 말을 걸었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, ma’am.", "“실례합니다, 아씨."], ["The gentleman was gone so quickly, I had no time to tell him. Mr.", "그 어른께서 하도 빨리 나가시는 바람에 여쭐 틈이 없었습니다."], ["Thornton called almost directly after you left; and as I understood, from what the gentleman said, you would be back in an hour, I told him so, and he came again about five minutes ago, and said he would wait for Mr.", "두 분이 나가신 직후에 손턴 씨가 찾아오셨습니다. 그 어른 말씀으로 미루어 한 시간이면 돌아오시겠거니 해서 그렇게 전해 드렸더니, 5분쯤 전에 다시 오셔서 헤일 씨를 기다리시겠다고 하셨습니다."], ["Hale. He is in your room now, ma’am.”", "지금 방에 계십니다, 아씨.”"]], [["“Thank you.", "“고마워요."], ["My father will return soon, and then you can tell him.”", "아버지가 곧 돌아오실 테니 그때 말씀드리면 되겠네요.”"]], [["Margaret opened the door and went in with the straight, fearless, dignified presence habitual to her.", "마거릿은 문을 열고 몸에 밴 그 곧고 두려움 없고 의젓한 태도로 들어섰다. 그녀는 어색함을 느끼지 않았다."], ["She felt no awkwardness; she had too much the habits of society for that.", "그러기에는 사교의 몸가짐이 너무 익어 있었다. 아버지에게 볼일이 있어 온 사람이 여기 있었다."], ["Here was a person come on business to her father; and, as he was one who had shown himself obliging, she was disposed to treat him with a full measure of civility.", "게다가 그는 이미 호의를 보여 준 이였으므로, 그녀는 그에게 충분한 예의를 갖추어 대할 마음이었다. 손턴 씨 쪽이 그녀보다 훨씬 더 놀라고 당황했다."], ["Mr. Thornton was a good deal more surprised and discomfited than she.", "조용한 중년의 성직자 대신 젊은 아가씨가 거리낌 없는 위엄을 갖추고 앞으로 나섰던 것이다."], ["Instead of a quiet, middle-aged clergyman, a young lady came forward with frank dignity,—a young lady of a different type to most of those he was in the habit of seeing.", "그가 평소 보아 오던 여자들 대부분과는 다른 부류의 아가씨였다. 옷차림은 아주 수수했다."], ["Her dress was very plain: a close straw bonnet of the best material and shape, trimmed with white ribbon; a large Indian shawl, which hung about her in long heavy folds, and which she wore as an empress wears her drapery.", "가장 좋은 감과 모양으로 지어 흰 리본을 두른 몸에 꼭 맞는 밀짚 보닛, 그리고 길고 무거운 주름을 이루며 몸에 드리운 커다란 인도 숄. 그녀는 그것을 마치 여제가 옷자락을 걸치듯 두르고 있었다."], ["He did not understand who she was, as he caught the simple, straight, unabashed look, which showed that his being there was of no concern to the beautiful countenance, and called up no flush of surprise to the pale ivory of the complexion.", "그는 그녀가 누구인지 알아차리지 못했다. 그가 거기 있다는 사실이 그 아름다운 얼굴에는 아무 상관도 없는 일이며 상아처럼 흰 낯빛에 놀란 기색 하나 불러일으키지 못한다는 것을 보여 주는, 그 담박하고 곧고 주눅 들지 않은 눈길을 마주하면서도."], ["He had heard that Mr. Hale had a daughter, but he had imagined that she was a little girl.", "헤일 씨에게 딸이 있다는 말은 들었지만, 그는 어린 여자아이일 줄로만 알고 있었다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton, I believe!”", "“손턴 씨시군요.”"], ["said Margaret, after a half-instant’s pause, during which his unready words would not come.", "그가 미처 말을 찾지 못하고 있던 그 반 순간의 침묵 뒤에 마거릿이 말했다."], ["“Will you sit down.", "“앉으시지요."], ["My father brought me to the door not a minute ago, but unfortunately he was not told that you were here, and he has gone away on some business.", "아버지가 채 일 분도 안 되어 저를 문 앞까지 데려다주셨는데, 안타깝게도 손턴 씨가 와 계신 줄 모르고 볼일이 있어 나가셨어요."], ["But he will come back almost directly.", "하지만 곧 돌아오실 거예요."], ["I am sorry you have had the trouble of calling twice.”", "두 번이나 걸음하시게 해서 죄송합니다.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was in habits of authority himself, but she seemed to assume some kind of rule over him at once.", "손턴 씨 자신도 남을 부리는 데 익숙한 사람이었으나, 그녀는 대뜸 그를 다스리는 듯한 태도를 지녔다."], ["He had been getting impatient at the loss of his time on a market-day, the moment before she appeared, yet now he calmly took a seat at her bidding.", "그녀가 나타나기 직전만 해도 그는 장이 서는 날에 시간을 허비하는 것이 슬슬 못마땅해지던 참이었다. 그런데 이제 그는 그녀가 시키는 대로 잠자코 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Do you know where it is that Mr.", "“헤일 씨가 어디로 가셨는지 아십니까?"], ["Hale has gone to? Perhaps I might be able to find him.”", "어쩌면 제가 찾아낼 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“He has gone to a Mr. Donkin in Canute Street.", "“커누트 가의 돈킨 씨라는 분에게 가셨어요."], ["He is the landlord of the house my father wishes to take in Crampton.”", "아버지가 얻으려는 크램프턴의 집 주인이랍니다.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton knew the house.", "손턴 씨는 그 집을 알고 있었다."], ["He had seen the advertisement, and been to look at it, in compliance with a request of Mr.", "광고를 보고 직접 가서 살펴본 적도 있었다."], ["Bell’s that he would assist Mr.", "헤일 씨를 힘닿는 데까지 도와 달라는 벨 씨의 부탁에 따른 것이기도 했고, 헤일 씨와 같은 사정으로 성직록을 내놓은 성직자의 처지에 스스로 관심이 동한 까닭이기도 했다."], ["Hale to the best of his power: and also instigated by his own interest in the case of a clergyman who had given up his living under circumstances such as those of Mr. Hale.", "손턴 씨는 크램프턴의 그 집이 정말 딱 맞겠다고 여겼었다."], ["Mr. Thornton had thought that the house in Crampton was really just the thing; but now that he saw Margaret, with her superb ways of moving and looking, he began to feel ashamed of having imagined that it would do very well for the Hales, in spite of a certain vulgarity in it which had struck him at the time of his looking it over.", "그러나 이제 마거릿의 그 당당한 몸가짐과 눈길을 보고 나니, 둘러볼 당시에도 걸렸던 어딘가 천박한 구석이 있는 그 집을 헤일 집안에 아주 알맞겠다고 생각한 것이 부끄러워지기 시작했다."]], [["Margaret could not help her looks; but the short curled upper lip, the round, massive up-turned chin, the manner of carrying her head, her movements, full of a soft feminine defiance, always gave strangers the impression of haughtiness. She was tired now, and would much rather have remained silent, and taken the rest her father had planned for her; but, of course, she owed to herself to be a gentlewoman, and to speak courteously from time to time to this stranger; not over-brushed, nor over-polished, it must be confessed, after his rough encounter with Milton streets and crowds. She wished that he would go, as he had once spoken of doing, instead of sitting there, answering with curt sentences all the remarks she made. She had taken off her shawl, and hung it over the back of her chair. She sat facing him and facing the light; her full beauty met his eye; her round white flexile throat rising out of the full, yet lithe figure; her lips moving so slightly as she spoke, not breaking the cold serene look of her face with any variation from the one lovely haughty curve; her eyes, with their soft gloom, meeting his with quiet maiden freedom. He almost said to himself that he did not like her, before their conversation ended; he tried so to compensate himself for the mortified feeling, that while he looked upon her with an admiration he could not repress, she looked at him with proud indifference, taking him, he thought, for what, in his irritation, he told himself he was—a great rough fellow, with not a grace or a refinement about him. Her quiet coldness of demeanour he interpreted into contemptuousness, and resented it in his heart to the pitch of almost inclining him to get up and go away, and have nothing more to do with these Hales, and their superciliousness.", "마거릿은 제 생김새를 어쩔 수 없었다. 그러나 짧게 도드라진 윗입술, 둥글고 다부지게 치켜든 턱, 고개를 가누는 자세, 부드러운 여자다운 반항이 가득한 몸짓은 낯선 이들에게 늘 오만하다는 인상을 주었다. 그녀는 지금 지쳐 있었고, 차라리 잠자코 있으면서 아버지가 마련해 준 휴식을 취하고 싶었다. 그러나 물론 스스로에게 부끄럽지 않으려면 숙녀답게 굴어야 했고 이 낯선 이에게 이따금 정중한 말을 건네야 했다. 밀턴의 거리와 인파에 부대끼고 온 그 사람은 솔직히 말해 옷매무새도 그리 단정하지 않고 몸가짐도 그리 다듬어져 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그가 한번 말했던 대로 그만 가 주기를 바랐다. 저렇게 앉아서 그녀가 하는 말마다 짧은 문장으로 툭툭 대답만 하고 있지 말고. 그녀는 숄을 벗어 의자 등받이에 걸쳐 두었다. 그녀는 그를 마주하고, 또한 빛을 마주하고 앉아 있었다. 그녀의 아름다움이 온전히 그의 눈에 들어왔다. 풍만하면서도 나긋한 몸매 위로 솟아오른 둥글고 흰 유연한 목, 말할 때조차 거의 움직이지 않아 그 곱고 도도한 곡선 하나를 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 않는 입술, 그리하여 차갑도록 고요한 얼굴빛을 조금도 바꾸지 않는 그 입매, 부드러운 그늘을 머금은 채 처녀다운 거리낌 없음으로 그의 눈길을 마주 받는 두 눈. 대화가 끝나기도 전에 그는 그녀가 마음에 들지 않는다고 스스로에게 되뇌다시피 했다. 그는 그렇게 해서 굴욕감을 달래 보려 했다. 그는 억누를 수 없는 감탄으로 그녀를 바라보는데, 그녀는 도도한 무심함으로 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 자기를 무엇으로 여기고 있다고 생각했다. 화가 치민 김에 스스로에게 이르던 그것, 곧 우아함이라고는 세련됨이라고는 한 점도 없는 크고 거친 사내로 말이다. 그는 그녀의 조용하고 냉랭한 태도를 업신여김으로 받아들였고, 마음속으로 어찌나 분했던지 자리를 박차고 나가 이 헤일 집안 사람들과 그 콧대와는 다시는 상관하지 말까 하는 생각까지 들 지경이었다."]], [["Just as Margaret had exhausted her last subject of conversation—and yet conversation that could hardly be called which consisted of so few and such short speeches—her father came in, and with his pleasant gentlemanly courteousness of apology, reinstated his name and family in Mr.", "마거릿이 마지막 화젯거리마저 바닥냈을 바로 그때, 그토록 적고 짧은 말들로 이루어진 것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면 말이지만, 아버지가 들어왔다."], ["Thornton’s good opinion.", "그리고 그 유쾌하고 신사다운 정중한 사과로 자기 이름과 가족에 대한 손턴 씨의 호감을 되돌려 놓았다."]], [["Mr. Hale and his visitor had a good deal to say respecting their mutual friend, Mr.", "헤일 씨와 손님은 두 사람의 공통된 벗인 벨 씨에 관해 나눌 이야기가 많았다."], ["Bell; and Margaret, glad that her part of entertaining the visitor was over, went to the window to try and make herself more familiar with the strange aspect of the street.", "마거릿은 손님을 상대하는 자기 몫이 끝난 것이 반가워 창가로 가서 낯선 거리 풍경에 좀 더 익숙해져 보려 했다."], ["She got so much absorbed in watching what was going on outside that she hardly heard her father when he spoke to her, and he had to repeat what he said:", "밖에서 벌어지는 일을 지켜보는 데 어찌나 골똘했던지 아버지가 말을 걸어도 거의 듣지 못했고, 아버지는 했던 말을 되풀이해야 했다."]], [["“Margaret!", "“마거릿!"], ["the landlord will persist in admiring that hideous paper, and I am afraid we must let it remain.”", "집주인이 그 흉측한 벽지를 극구 좋다고 우기는구나. 아무래도 그냥 두어야 할 것 같다.”"]], [["“Oh dear!", "“어머나!"], ["I am sorry!”", "안됐네요!”"], ["she replied, and began to turn over in her mind the possibility of hiding part of it, at least, by some of her sketches, but gave up the idea at last, as likely only to make bad worse.", "그녀가 대답하고는, 적어도 일부라도 자기 그림 몇 점으로 가려 볼 수 있을까 마음속으로 궁리하기 시작했다. 그러나 그러다가는 되레 더 볼썽사나워지기 십상이겠다 싶어 끝내 그 생각을 접었다."], ["Her father, meanwhile, with his kindly country hospitality, was pressing Mr. Thornton to stay to luncheon with them.", "그러는 동안 아버지는 시골 사람다운 다정한 손님 대접으로 손턴 씨에게 점심을 함께 하고 가라고 권하고 있었다."], ["It would have been very inconvenient to him to do so, yet he felt that he should have yielded, if Margaret by word or look had seconded her father’s invitation; he was glad she did not, and yet he was irritated at her for not doing so.", "그로서는 그러기가 몹시 곤란했으나, 마거릿이 말로든 눈길로든 아버지의 권유에 한마디만 보탰더라면 자기가 그 청을 받아들였으리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 그녀가 그러지 않은 것이 다행스러우면서도, 그러지 않았다는 것에 화가 났다."], ["She gave him a low, grave bow when he left, and he felt more awkward and self-conscious in every limb than he had ever done in his life before.", "그가 떠날 때 그녀는 나직하고 정중한 목례를 건넸고, 그는 평생 어느 때보다도 손발이 어색하고 스스로가 의식되었다."]], [["“Well, Margaret, now to luncheon as fast as we can.", "“자, 마거릿, 이제 되도록 빨리 점심을 먹자꾸나."], ["Have you ordered it?”", "시켜 두었니?”"]], [["“No, papa; that man was here when I came home, and I have never had an opportunity.”", "“아니요, 아버지. 제가 돌아왔을 때 그분이 여기 계셔서 그럴 틈이 없었어요.”"]], [["“Then we must take anything we can get.", "“그럼 나오는 대로 아무거나 먹어야겠구나."], ["He must have been waiting a long time I’m afraid.”", "아무래도 그 사람이 한참을 기다렸을 것 같은데.”"]], [["“It seemed exceedingly long to me.", "“저한테는 지독하게 길게 느껴졌어요."], ["I was just at the last gasp when you came in.", "아버지가 들어오셨을 때 저는 막 숨이 넘어가기 직전이었다고요."], ["He never went on with any subject, but gave little, short, abrupt answers.”", "무슨 이야기든 이어 가는 법이 없고 짤막하고 뚝뚝 끊기는 대답만 하더군요.”"]], [["“Very much to the point though, I should think.", "“그래도 요점은 아주 잘 짚는 것 같더구나."], ["He is a clear-headed fellow.", "머리가 맑은 사람이야."], ["He said (did you hear?) that Crampton is on gravelly soil, and by far the most healthy suburb in the neighbourhood of Milton.”", "그가 말하기를, 너도 들었니? 크램프턴은 자갈땅 위에 있어서 밀턴 근방에서 단연 가장 건강에 좋은 변두리라더구나.”"]], [["When they returned to Heston, there was the day’s account to be given to Mrs.", "헤스턴으로 돌아오자 그날 있었던 일을 헤일 부인에게 들려주어야 했다."], ["Hale, who was full of questions, which they answered in the intervals of tea-drinking.", "부인은 묻고 싶은 것이 잔뜩 있었고, 두 사람은 차를 마시는 틈틈이 그 물음에 답했다."]], [["“And what is your correspondent, Mr. Thornton, like?”", "“그래, 당신이 편지를 주고받는다는 그 손턴 씨라는 분은 어떤 사람이에요?”"]], [["“Ask Margaret,”", "“마거릿에게 물어보구려.”"], ["said her husband.", "남편이 말했다."], ["“She and he had a long attempt at conversation, while I was away speaking to the landlord.”", "“내가 집주인을 만나러 나가 있는 동안 저 애하고 그 사람이 한참 이야기를 나눠 보려 애썼으니.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아, 저도 그분이 어떤 분인지 잘 모르겠어요.”"], ["I hardly know what he is like,”", "마거릿이 나른하게 말했다."], ["said Margaret, lazily; too tired to tax her powers of description much.", "너무 지쳐서 묘사에 힘을 쏟기가 어려웠던 것이다. 그러다 정신을 가다듬고 말했다."], ["And then rousing herself, she said, “He is a tall, broad-shouldered man, about—how old, papa?”", "“키가 크고 어깨가 벌어진 분이에요. 나이는, 몇쯤 될까요, 아버지?”"]], [["“I should guess about thirty.”", "“내 짐작에는 30쯤 되지 싶구나.”"]], [["“About thirty—with a face that is neither exactly plain, nor yet handsome, nothing remarkable—not quite a gentleman; but that was hardly to be expected.”", "“30쯤이에요. 얼굴은 딱히 못생기지도 잘생기지도 않았고, 눈에 띄는 데라곤 없어요. 신사라고 하기는 좀 그렇고요. 하지만 그건 애초에 바랄 수 없는 일이었겠지요.”"]], [["“Not vulgar or common though,”", "“그렇다고 천박하거나 상스럽지는 않지.”"], ["put in her father, rather jealous of any disparagement of the sole friend he had in Milton.", "아버지가 끼어들었다. 밀턴에서 유일한 벗이 깎이는 것이 다소 못마땅했던 것이다."]], [["“Oh, no!” said Margaret.", "“아, 그럼요!”"], ["“With such an expression of resolution and power, no face, however plain in feature, could be either vulgar or common.", "마거릿이 말했다. “그런 결단과 힘이 서린 표정을 지녔다면 아무리 이목구비가 못났더라도 천박하거나 상스러울 수는 없지요."], ["I should not like to have to bargain with him; he looks very inflexible.", "저라면 그분과 흥정을 해야 하는 처지는 사양하겠어요. 아주 굽힐 줄 모르는 사람으로 보여요."], ["Altogether a man who seems made for his niche, mamma; sagacious, and strong, as becomes a great tradesman.”", "한마디로 제 자리에 꼭 맞게 태어난 사람 같아요, 어머니. 큰 장사꾼답게 눈치 빠르고 억센 분이지요.”"]], [["“Don’t call the Milton manufacturers tradesmen, Margaret,”", "“밀턴의 공장주들을 장사꾼이라고 부르지 말거라, 마거릿.”"], ["said her father.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["“They are very different.”", "“그들은 아주 다르단다.”"]], [["“Are they?", "“그런가요?"], ["I apply the word to all who have something tangible to sell; but if you think the term is not correct, papa, I won’t use it.", "저는 손에 잡히는 무엇인가를 파는 사람에게는 다 그 말을 쓰거든요. 하지만 아버지가 맞지 않는 말이라고 여기신다면 쓰지 않을게요."], ["But, oh mamma!", "그런데 어머니!"], ["speaking of vulgarity and commonness, you must prepare yourself for our drawing-room paper.", "천박하고 상스러운 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 우리 응접실 벽지에 마음의 준비를 하셔야 해요."], ["Pink and blue roses, with yellow leaves!", "노란 잎사귀에 분홍 장미 파란 장미라니까요!"], ["And such a heavy cornice round the room!”", "게다가 방을 빙 두른 그 육중한 처마 장식이라니!”"]], [["But when they removed to their new house in Milton, the obnoxious papers were gone.", "그러나 그들이 밀턴의 새집으로 옮겨 갔을 때 그 밉살스러운 벽지는 사라지고 없었다."], ["The landlord received their thanks very composedly; and let them think, if they liked, that he had relented from his expressed determination not to repaper. There was no particular need to tell them, that what he did not care to do for a Reverend Mr.", "집주인은 그들의 인사를 아주 태연하게 받았고, 다시 도배는 하지 않겠다고 못 박았던 자기 뜻을 누그러뜨린 것이라고 그들이 여기고 싶어 한다면 그러도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Hale, unknown in Milton, he was only too glad to do at the one short sharp remonstrance of Mr. Thornton, the wealthy manufacturer.", "밀턴에서 이름 없는 헤일 목사를 위해서라면 하고 싶지 않았을 일을, 부유한 공장주 손턴 씨가 짧고 매섭게 한마디 하자 기꺼이 해치웠다는 것을 굳이 그들에게 알릴 까닭은 없었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# 제8장"]], [["“And it’s hame, hame, hame, Hame fain wad I be.”", "「그리고 고향으로, 고향으로, 고향으로, 고향으로 가고 싶어라.」"]], [["It needed the pretty light papering of the rooms to reconcile them to Milton.", "방마다 발라 놓은 어여쁜 밝은 벽지가 아니었다면 그들은 밀턴과 화해할 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그것만으로는 부족했다."], ["It needed more—more that could not be had.", "더 필요했지만 얻을 수 없는 것들이 있었다."], ["The thick yellow November fogs had come on; and the view of the plain in the valley, made by the sweeping bend of the river, was all shut out when Mrs. Hale arrived at her new home.", "누렇고 짙은 11월의 안개가 몰려들었고, 강이 크게 굽이돌며 만들어 놓은 골짜기의 평지 풍경은 헤일 부인이 새집에 닿았을 무렵 온통 가려져 보이지 않았다."]], [["Margaret and Dixon had been at work two days, unpacking and arranging, but everything inside the house still looked in disorder; and outside a thick fog crept up to the very windows, and was driven in to every open door in choking white wreaths of unwholsome mist.", "마거릿과 딕슨이 이틀 동안 짐을 풀고 정리하며 일했건만 집 안은 여전히 온통 어수선해 보였다. 밖에서는 짙은 안개가 창문 바로 앞까지 기어올랐고, 열린 문마다 숨 막히는 흰 화환 같은 해로운 안개가 밀려 들어왔다."]], [["“Oh, Margaret!", "“아, 마거릿!"], ["are we to live here?”", "우리가 여기서 살아야 하는 거니?”"], ["asked Mrs. Hale in blank dismay.", "헤일 부인이 넋을 잃은 낙담 속에서 물었다."]], [["Margaret’s heart echoed the dreariness of the tone in which this question was put.", "이 물음이 던져진 말투의 스산함이 마거릿의 가슴에도 그대로 울렸다."], ["She could scarcely command herself enough to say, “Oh, the fogs in London are sometimes far worse!”", "그녀는 간신히 자신을 다스려 이렇게 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다. “어머, 런던의 안개도 때로는 훨씬 더 심한걸요!”"]], [["“But then you knew that London itself, and friends lay behind it.", "“하지만 그때는 런던이라는 곳이, 그리고 친구들이 뒤에 있다는 걸 알고 있었지."], ["Here—well!", "여기는…… 그래!"], ["we are desolate.", "우리는 아주 외톨이야."], ["Oh Dixon, what a place this is!”", "아, 딕슨, 여기는 대체 어떤 곳이니!”"]], [["“Indeed, ma’am, I’m sure it will be your death before long, and then I know who’ll—stay!", "“정말이지 마님, 머잖아 마님이 여기서 돌아가시고 말 겁니다요. 그러면 제가 누구를 원망할지……"], ["Miss Hale, that’s far too heavy for you to lift.”", "잠깐만요! 헤일 아가씨, 그건 아가씨가 드시기엔 너무 무겁습니다.”"]], [["“Not at all, thank you, Dixon,”", "“괜찮아요, 고마워요, 딕슨.”"], ["replied Margaret coldly.", "마거릿이 쌀쌀하게 대꾸했다."], ["“The best thing we can do for mamma is to get her room quite ready for her to go to bed, while I go and bring her a cup of coffee.”", "“지금 어머니를 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 어머니가 바로 자리에 드실 수 있도록 방을 다 치워 놓는 거예요. 그동안 저는 가서 커피를 한 잔 가져올게요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale was equally out of spirits, and equally came upon Margaret for sympathy.", "헤일 씨도 마찬가지로 기운을 잃었고, 마찬가지로 마거릿에게서 위로를 구했다."]], [["“Margaret, I do believe this is an unhealthy place.", "“마거릿, 여기는 정말이지 몸에 해로운 곳인 것 같구나."], ["Only suppose that your mother’s health or yours should suffer.", "네 어머니나 네 건강이 상하기라도 하면 어쩌지."], ["I wish I had gone into some country place in Wales; this is really terrible,”", "차라리 웨일스의 어느 시골로 갈 것을 그랬다. 이건 정말 끔찍하구나.”"], ["said he, going up to the window.", "그가 창가로 다가가며 말했다."]], [["There was no comfort to be given.", "건넬 위로란 없었다."], ["They were settled in Milton, and must endure smoke and fogs for a season; indeed, all other life seemed shut out from them by as thick a fog of circumstance.", "그들은 밀턴에 자리를 잡았고, 한 계절 동안 연기와 안개를 견뎌야 했다. 실로 다른 모든 삶이 그만큼 짙은 처지의 안개에 가려 그들에게서 닫혀 버린 듯했다."], ["Only the day before, Mr. Hale had been reckoning up with dismay how much their removal and fortnight at Heston had cost, and he found it had absorbed nearly all his little stock of ready money.", "바로 전날 헤일 씨는 이사와 헤스턴에서의 두 주가 얼마나 들었는지를 낙담 속에 셈해 보다가, 그것이 얼마 되지 않던 현금을 거의 다 삼켜 버렸다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["No!", "그렇다!"], ["here they were, and here they must remain.", "그들은 여기 와 있었고, 여기 머물러야만 했다."]], [["At night when Margaret realised this, she felt inclined to sit down in a stupor of despair.", "밤이 되어 마거릿이 이 사실을 실감했을 때, 그녀는 절망의 멍한 상태로 주저앉고 싶은 심정이었다."], ["The heavy smoky air hung about her bedroom, which occupied the long narrow projection at the back of the house.", "무겁고 그을린 공기가 그녀의 침실에 감돌았다. 그 방은 집 뒤편으로 길고 좁게 튀어나온 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["The window, placed at the side of the oblong, looked at the blank wall of a similar projection, not above ten feet distant.", "그 길쭉한 방의 옆면에 난 창은 열 자도 채 떨어지지 않은 똑같은 돌출부의 민민한 벽을 마주 보고 있었다."], ["It loomed through the fog like a great barrier to hope.", "그것은 안개 너머로 희망을 가로막는 커다란 장벽처럼 어렴풋이 솟아 있었다."], ["Inside the room everything was in confusion.", "방 안은 온통 어지러웠다."], ["All their efforts had been directed to make her mother’s room comfortable.", "그들의 온 노력은 어머니의 방을 편하게 만드는 데로만 쏠려 있었던 것이다."], ["Margaret sat down on a box, the direction card upon which struck her as having been written at Helstone—beautiful, beloved Helstone!", "마거릿은 상자 하나에 걸터앉았는데, 거기 붙은 주소 쪽지가 헬스톤에서 쓰인 것이라는 사실이 문득 그녀의 마음을 찔렀다. 아름답고 사랑하는 헬스톤!"], ["She lost herself in dismal thought: but at last she determined to take her mind away from the present; and suddenly remembered that she had a letter from Edith which she had only half read in the bustle of the morning.", "그녀는 침울한 생각에 빠져들었다. 그러나 마침내 마음을 지금 이곳에서 떼어 놓기로 마음먹었고, 문득 아침의 부산함 속에서 반밖에 읽지 못한 이디스의 편지가 있다는 것이 떠올랐다."], ["It was to tell of their arrival at Corfu; their voyage along the Mediterranean—their music, and dancing on board ship; the gay new life opening upon her; her house with its trellised balcony, and its views over white cliffs and deep blue sea.", "편지에는 코르푸에 닿았다는 소식이 담겨 있었다. 지중해를 따라온 항해, 배 위에서의 음악과 춤, 그녀 앞에 열린 화사한 새 생활, 격자 시렁을 두른 발코니가 있고 흰 절벽과 짙푸른 바다가 내려다보이는 그녀의 집 이야기였다."]], [["Edith wrote fluently and well, if not graphically.", "이디스는 생생하게 그려 내지는 못해도 술술 잘 썼다."], ["She could not only seize the salient and characteristic points of a scene, but she could enumerate enough of indiscriminate particular for Margaret to make it out for herself.", "그녀는 어떤 장면의 두드러지고 특징적인 대목을 붙잡아 낼 뿐 아니라, 이것저것 가릴 것 없는 자잘한 것들을 마거릿이 스스로 그림을 그려 볼 수 있을 만큼 넉넉히 늘어놓을 줄도 알았다."], ["Captain Lennox and another lately married officer shared a villa, high up on the beautiful precipitous rocks overhanging the sea.", "레녹스 대위와 최근에 결혼한 또 다른 장교가 별장 하나를 함께 쓰고 있었다. 바다 위로 아름답게 깎아지른 바위 높은 곳에 있는 집이었다."], ["Their days, late as it was in the year, seemed spent in boating or land-picnics; all out-of-doors, pleasure-seeking and glad, Edith’s life seemed like the deep vault of blue sky above her, free—utterly free from fleck or cloud.", "그해가 저물어 가는데도 그들의 나날은 뱃놀이나 들놀이로 채워지는 듯했다. 온통 바깥에서, 즐거움을 좇으며 흥겹게 지내니, 이디스의 삶은 그녀 위에 펼쳐진 푸른 하늘의 깊은 궁륭 같아 보였다."], ["Her husband had to attend drill, and she, the most musical officer’s wife there, had to copy the new and popular tunes out of the most recent English music, for the benefit of the bandmaster; those seemed their most severe and arduous duties.", "자유롭고, 티끌 하나 구름 한 점 없이 온전히 자유로웠다. 남편은 훈련에 나가야 했고, 그곳에서 가장 음악에 밝은 장교의 아내인 그녀는 군악대장을 위해 가장 최근의 영국 악보에서 새롭고 인기 있는 곡들을 베껴 적어야 했다."], ["She expressed an affectionate hope that, if the regiment stopped another year at Corfu, Margaret might come out and pay her a long visit.", "그것이 그들의 가장 고되고 힘든 임무인 듯했다. 그녀는 연대가 코르푸에 한 해 더 머무르게 되면 마거릿이 건너와 오래 묵어 가면 좋겠다는 다정한 바람을 적었다."], ["She asked Margaret if she remembered the day twelve-month on which she, Edith, wrote—how it rained all day long in Harley Street; and how she would not put on her new gown to go to a stupid dinner, and get it all wet and splashed in going to the carriage; and how at that very dinner they had first met Captain Lennox.", "그녀는 자기가 편지를 쓰고 있는 이날로부터 꼭 한 해 전의 일을 마거릿이 기억하는지 물었다. 할리 가에 온종일 비가 내리던 그날, 시시한 만찬에 가는데 새 드레스를 입지 않겠다고, 마차까지 걸어가다가 다 젖고 흙탕물이 튈 거라고 고집을 부리던 일, 그리고 바로 그 만찬에서 그들이 레녹스 대위를 처음 만난 일 말이다."]], [["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["Margaret remembered it well. Edith and Mrs.", "마거릿은 그날을 또렷이 기억했다."], ["Shaw had gone to dinner. Margaret had joined the party in the evening.", "이디스와 쇼 부인이 만찬에 갔고, 마거릿은 저녁에야 그 자리에 합류했다."], ["The recollection of the plentiful luxury of all arrangements, the stately handsomeness of the furniture, the size of the house, the peaceful, untroubled ease of the visitors—all came vividly before her, in strange contrast to the present time.", "온갖 준비의 넉넉한 호사, 가구의 위엄 있는 아름다움, 집의 크기, 손님들의 평온하고 근심 없는 안락함이 모두 그녀 앞에 생생히 떠올라 지금 이때와 야릇한 대조를 이루었다."], ["The smooth sea of that old life closed up, without a mark left to tell where they had all been.", "그 옛 삶의 잔잔한 바다는 그들이 모두 어디에 있었는지 알려 줄 자국 하나 남기지 않은 채 닫혀 버렸다."], ["The habitual dinners, the calls, the dancing evenings, were all going on, going on for ever, though her Aunt Shaw and Edith were no longer there; and she, of course, was even less missed.", "늘 있던 만찬과 방문과 춤추는 저녁 모임은 쇼 이모와 이디스가 더는 그 자리에 없는데도 여전히, 영원히 이어지고 있었다. 그리고 그녀야 물론 더더욱 아쉬워하는 이가 없었다."], ["She doubted if any one of that old set ever thought of her, except Henry Lennox.", "그녀는 그 옛 무리 가운데 헨리 레녹스 말고 누구 하나 자기를 떠올리는 이가 있을까 싶었다."], ["He too, she knew, would strive to forget her, because of the pain she had caused him.", "그 사람 역시 자기가 안긴 아픔 때문에 자기를 잊으려 애쓰리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["She had heard him often boast of his power of putting any disagreeable thought far away from him.", "언짢은 생각이라면 무엇이든 멀찍이 밀어내는 재주가 있다고 그가 곧잘 자랑하던 것을 그녀는 여러 번 들었다."], ["Then she penetrated farther into what might have been.", "그러다 그녀는 있을 수도 있었던 일 속으로 더 깊이 파고들었다."], ["If she had cared for him as a lover, and had accepted him, and this change in her father’s opinions and consequent station had taken place, she could not doubt but that it would have been impatiently received by Mr. Lennox.", "만일 그녀가 그를 연인으로 마음에 두고 청혼을 받아들였는데 아버지의 신념에 이런 변화가 일어나고 그에 따라 처지까지 이렇게 되었다면, 레녹스 씨가 그것을 참지 못하고 받아들였으리라는 것을 그녀는 의심할 수 없었다."], ["It was a bitter mortification to her in one sense; but she could bear it patiently, because she knew her father’s purity of purpose, and that strengthened her to endure his errors, grave and serious though in her estimation they were.", "어떤 뜻에서 그것은 그녀에게 쓰라린 굴욕이었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 꾹 참고 견딜 수 있었다."], ["But the fact of the world esteeming her father degraded, in its rough wholesale judgment, would have oppressed and irritated Mr.", "아버지의 뜻이 순수하다는 것을 알았고, 그 사실이 아버지의 잘못을, 그녀가 보기에도 무겁고 심각한 그 잘못을 견뎌 낼 힘을 주었기 때문이다. 그러나 세상이 그 거칠고 뭉뚱그린 판단으로 아버지를 격이 떨어진 사람으로 여긴다는 사실은 레녹스 씨를 짓누르고 짜증 나게 했을 것이다."], ["Lennox. As she realised what might have been, she grew to be thankful for what was.", "있을 수도 있었던 일을 깨달으면서 그녀는 지금 있는 그대로에 고마워하게 되었다."], ["They were at the lowest now; they could not be worse.", "그들은 이제 맨 밑바닥에 있었다. 더 나빠질 수는 없었다."], ["Edith’s astonishment and her Aunt Shaw’s dismay would have to be met bravely, when their letters came.", "이디스의 놀라움과 쇼 이모의 낙담은 그들의 편지가 오면 씩씩하게 맞이해야 할 터였다."], ["So Margaret rose up and began slowly to undress herself, feeling the full luxury of acting leisurely, late as it was, after all the past hurry of the day.", "그리하여 마거릿은 몸을 일으켜 천천히 옷을 벗기 시작했다. 시각이 늦기는 했어도 하루 종일 이어진 부산함 끝에 느긋하게 움직이는 호사를 한껏 누리면서."], ["She fell asleep, hoping for some brightness, either internal or external.", "그녀는 안에서든 밖에서든 어떤 밝음이 찾아오기를 바라며 잠이 들었다."], ["But if she had known how long it would be before the brightness came, her heart would have sunk low down.", "그러나 그 밝음이 오기까지 얼마나 오래 걸릴지 알았더라면 그녀의 가슴은 밑바닥까지 가라앉았을 것이다. 그 계절은 몸에도 마음에도 더없이 좋지 않은 때였다."], ["The time of the year was most unpropitious to health as well as to spirits.", "어머니는 심한 고뿔에 걸렸고, 딕슨 자신도 몸이 성치 않은 것이 분명했다."], ["Her mother caught a severe cold, and Dixon herself was evidently not well, although Margaret could not insult her more than by trying to save her, or by taking any care of her.", "그렇다고 그녀의 수고를 덜어 주려 하거나 그녀를 돌보려 드는 것보다 더한 모욕은 딕슨에게 없었지만. 딕슨을 도울 처녀아이는 하나도 구할 수 없었다."], ["They could hear of no girl to assist her; all were at work in the factories; at least, those who applied were well scolded by Dixon, for thinking that such as they could ever be trusted to work in a gentleman’s house.", "다들 공장에서 일하고 있었다. 적어도 찾아온 아이들은 자기 같은 것들이 신사의 집에서 일하도록 믿음을 받을 수 있으리라 여겼다는 이유로 딕슨에게 실컷 꾸중을 들었다."], ["So they had to keep a charwoman in almost constant employ.", "그래서 그들은 거의 늘 삯일하는 여자를 하나 두어야 했다."], ["Margaret longed to send for Charlotte; but besides the objection of her being a better servant than they now could afford to keep, the distance was too great.", "마거릿은 샬럿을 불러오고 싶었지만, 이제 그들이 둘 수 있는 형편보다 나은 하녀라는 걸림돌 말고도 거리가 너무 멀었다."]], [["Mr. Hale met with several pupils, recommended to him by Mr.", "헤일 씨는 벨 씨의 추천으로, 또는 더 가까이에 있는 손턴 씨의 주선으로 몇몇 학생을 만나게 되었다."], ["Bell, or by the more immediate influence of Mr. Thornton.", "그들은 대개 여느 소년이라면 아직 학교에 다니고 있을 나이였다."], ["They were mostly of the age when many boys would be still at school, but, according to the prevalent, and, apparently well-founded notions of Milton, to make a lad into a good tradesman he must be caught young, and acclimated to the life of the mill, or office, or warehouse.", "그러나 밀턴에 널리 퍼져 있고 보아하니 근거도 있어 보이는 생각에 따르면, 사내아이를 훌륭한 상인으로 만들려면 어릴 때 붙잡아 공장이나 사무실이나 창고의 생활에 길들여야 했다."], ["If he were sent to even the Scotch Universities, he came back unsettled for commercial pursuits: how much more so if he went to Oxford or Cambridge, where he could not be entered till he was eighteen?", "스코틀랜드의 대학에라도 보내면 상업에 마음을 붙이지 못한 채 돌아왔다. 열여덟이 되기 전에는 들어갈 수도 없는 옥스퍼드나 케임브리지에 간다면 얼마나 더 그렇겠는가?"], ["So most of the manufacturers placed their sons in situations at from fourteen or fifteen years of age, unsparingly cutting away all off-shoots in the direction of literature or high mental cultivation, in hopes of throwing the whole strength and vigour of the plant into commerce.", "그래서 공장주들은 대개 열넷이나 열다섯 살 나이부터 아들을 어느 자리에 앉히고, 문학이나 높은 지적 교양 쪽으로 뻗어 나가는 곁가지는 사정없이 쳐 냈다. 그 나무의 온 힘과 기운을 장사 쪽으로 몰아넣으려는 바람에서였다."], ["Still there were some wiser parents; and some who had sense enough to perceive their own deficiencies, and strive to remedy them.", "그래도 더 슬기로운 부모들이 없지는 않았고, 자신의 부족함을 알아차리고 그것을 메우려 애쓸 만큼 분별이 있는 이들도 있었다."], ["Nay, there were a few no longer youths, but men in the prime of life, who had the stern wisdom to acknowledge their own ignorance, and to learn late what they should have learnt early. Mr.", "아니, 이제 젊은이가 아니라 한창때의 장년이면서도 자신의 무지를 인정하고 일찍 배웠어야 할 것을 늦게라도 배우는 준엄한 지혜를 지닌 사람도 몇 있었다."], ["Thornton was perhaps the oldest of Mr. Hale’s pupils.", "손턴 씨는 헤일 씨의 학생들 가운데 아마 가장 나이가 많았을 것이다."], ["He was certainly the favourite. Mr.", "그리고 분명 가장 마음에 드는 학생이었다."], ["Hale got into the habit of quoting his opinions so frequently, and with such regard, that it became a little domestic joke to wonder what time, during the hour appointed for instruction, could be given to absolute learning, so much of it appeared to have been spent in conversation.", "헤일 씨가 어찌나 자주, 또 어찌나 대단하게 여기며 그의 견해를 인용하는 버릇이 들었던지, 가르치기로 정해 놓은 그 한 시간 가운데 순전한 공부에 쓰이는 시간이 대체 얼마나 될까 하는 것이 집안의 자그마한 농담이 되었다. 그 시간의 그토록 많은 부분이 이야기로 흘러가 버린 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["Margaret rather encouraged this light, merry way of viewing her father’s acquaintance with Mr.", "마거릿은 아버지와 손턴 씨의 교분을 이렇게 가볍고 유쾌하게 바라보는 태도를 오히려 부추겼다."], ["Thornton, because she felt that her mother was inclined to look upon this new friendship of her husband’s with jealous eyes.", "어머니가 남편의 이 새로운 우정을 질투 어린 눈으로 바라보는 기색이 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["As long as his time had been solely occupied with his books and his parishioners, as at Helstone, she had appeared to care little whether she saw much of him or not; but now that he looked eagerly forward to each renewal of his intercourse with Mr.", "헬스톤에서처럼 남편의 시간이 오로지 책과 교구민에게만 쓰이는 동안에는 그를 자주 보든 못 보든 어머니는 별로 개의치 않는 듯했다. 그러나 이제 남편이 손턴 씨와의 만남이 거듭될 때마다 그것을 애타게 기다리자, 어머니는 처음으로 자기와 함께 있는 것을 남편이 가벼이 여기기라도 한 듯 상하고 언짢아하는 것 같았다."], ["Thornton, she seemed hurt and annoyed, as if he were slighting her companionship for the first time.", "헤일 씨의 지나친 칭찬은 듣는 이들에게 지나친 칭찬이 늘 미치는 그 효과를 냈다."], ["Mr. Hale’s over-praise had the usual effect of over-praise upon his auditors; they were a little inclined to rebel against Aristides being always called the Just.", "아리스티데스를 언제나 의인이라 부르는 데 대해 그들은 조금쯤 반기를 들고 싶어졌던 것이다."]], [["After a quiet life in a country parsonage for more than twenty years, there was something dazzling to Mr.", "시골 목사관에서 이십 년 넘게 조용히 살아온 헤일 씨에게는 엄청난 어려움을 손쉽게 정복해 내는 그 활력에 눈부신 데가 있었다."], ["Hale in the energy which conquered immense difficulties with ease; the power of the machinery of Milton, the power of the men of Milton, impressed him with a sense of grandeur, which he yielded to without caring to inquire into the details of its exercise.", "밀턴의 기계가 지닌 힘, 밀턴 사람들이 지닌 힘은 그에게 웅장함이라는 느낌을 새겨 주었고, 그는 그 힘이 어떻게 쓰이는지 낱낱이 캐물을 마음도 없이 거기에 굴복했다."], ["But Margaret went less abroad, among machinery and men; saw less of power in its public effect, and, as it happened, she was thrown with one or two of those who, in all measures affecting masses of people, must be acute sufferers for the good of many.", "그러나 마거릿은 기계와 사람들 사이로 나다니는 일이 적었고, 그 힘이 공적으로 드러나는 모습을 덜 보았다. 그리고 마침 그녀는 수많은 사람에게 영향을 미치는 온갖 조치 속에서 다수의 이익을 위해 뼈아프게 고통받을 수밖에 없는 한둘의 사람과 마주치게 되었다."], ["The question always is, has everything been done to make the sufferings of these exceptions as small as possible?", "언제나 문제는 이것이다. 이런 예외적인 이들의 고통을 되도록 작게 하기 위해 모든 것이 다 이루어졌는가?"], ["or, in the triumph of the crowded procession, have the helpless been trampled on, instead of being gently lifted aside out of the roadway of the conqueror, whom they have no power to accompany on his march?", "아니면 몰려가는 행렬의 개가 속에서, 힘없는 이들은 정복자가 나아가는 길에서 부드럽게 옆으로 들어 옮겨지는 대신 짓밟히고 말았는가? 그 행렬을 따라 걸을 힘조차 없는 이들이 말이다."]], [["It fell to Margaret’s share to have to look out for a servant to assist Dixon, who had at first undertaken to find just the person she wanted to do all the rough work of the house.", "딕슨을 도울 하녀를 찾아 나서는 일은 마거릿의 몫이 되었다. 처음에는 딕슨이 집안의 궂은일을 도맡을 딱 알맞은 사람을 자기가 찾아내겠다고 나섰던 터였다."], ["But Dixon’s ideas of helpful girls were founded on the recollection of tidy elder scholars at Helstone school, who were only too proud to be allowed to come to the parsonage on a busy day, and treated Mrs.", "그러나 쓸모 있는 처녀아이에 대한 딕슨의 생각은 헬스톤 학교의 얌전한 상급생들에 대한 기억에 뿌리를 두고 있었다. 그 아이들은 바쁜 날 목사관에 오라는 말을 들으면 더없이 자랑스러워했고, 헤일 씨 부부에게 바치는 것과 똑같은 공경에다 훨씬 더한 두려움까지 얹어 딕슨 부인을 대했다."], ["Dixon with all the respect, and a good deal more of fright, which they paid to Mr. and Mrs.", "딕슨은 자기에게 바쳐지는 그 경외 어린 공경을 모르지 않았고, 싫어하지도 않았다."], ["Hale. Dixon was not unconscious of this awed reverence which was given to her; nor did she dislike it: it flattered her much as Louis the Fourteenth was flattered by his courtiers shading their eyes from the dazzling light of his presence.", "루이 14세가 자신의 눈부신 빛 앞에서 신하들이 눈을 가리는 것에 우쭐했듯 그것은 그녀를 자못 우쭐하게 했다."], ["But nothing short of her faithful love for Mrs. Hale could have made her endure the rough independent way in which all the Milton girls, who made application for the servant’s place, replied to her inquiries respecting their qualifications.", "그러나 하녀 자리에 지원한 밀턴 처녀아이들이 자격을 묻는 그녀의 물음에 대꾸하는 그 거칠고 독립적인 태도를 견디게 한 것은 헤일 부인을 향한 충직한 사랑 말고는 아무것도 없었다."], ["They even went the length of questioning her back again; having doubts and fears of their own, as to the solvency of a family who lived in a house of thirty pounds a-year, and yet gave themselves airs, and kept two servants, one of them so very high and mighty.", "아이들은 심지어 되받아 캐묻기까지 했다. 일 년에 30파운드짜리 집에 살면서도 거드름을 피우고 하녀를 둘이나 두는, 게다가 그중 하나는 그토록 도도하고 위세 부리는 집안이 과연 셈을 치를 형편이 되는지 저희 나름의 의심과 걱정을 품었던 것이다."], ["Mr. Hale was no longer looked upon as Vicar of Helstone, but as a man who only spent at a certain rate.", "헤일 씨는 더 이상 헬스톤의 교구 목사로 여겨지지 않고, 다만 어느 정도의 돈을 쓰는 사람으로만 여겨졌다."], ["Margaret was weary and impatient of the accounts which Dixon perpetually brought to Mrs. Hale of the behaviour of these would-be servants.", "마거릿은 딕슨이 이 하녀 지망생들의 행실에 대해 끊임없이 헤일 부인에게 늘어놓는 이야기에 지치고 진저리가 났다."], ["Not but what Margaret was repelled by the rough uncourteous manners of these people; not but what she shrunk with fastidious pride from their hail-fellow accost, and severely resented their unconcealed curiosity as to the means and position of any family who lived in Milton, and yet were not engaged in trade of some kind.", "마거릿이 이 사람들의 거칠고 무례한 태도에 밀려나지 않았다는 말은 아니다. 스스럼없이 대뜸 말을 붙이는 데에 까다로운 자존심으로 움츠러들지 않았다는 말도 아니고, 밀턴에 살면서도 무슨 장사에 매여 있지 않은 집안의 형편과 지위에 대해 드러내 놓고 캐묻는 그들의 호기심에 몹시 언짢아하지 않았다는 말도 아니다."], ["But the more Margaret felt impertinence, the more likely she was to be silent on the subject; and, at any rate, if she took upon herself to make inquiry for a servant, she could spare her mother the recital of all her disappointments and fancied or real insults.", "다만 마거릿은 그 무례를 느끼면 느낄수록 그 일에 대해 입을 다물기 쉬웠다. 그리고 어쨌든 그녀가 하녀 구하는 일을 스스로 떠맡는다면, 그 모든 실망과 상상이든 실제든 모욕당한 이야기를 어머니가 듣지 않아도 되게 할 수 있었다."]], [["Margaret accordingly went up and down to butchers and grocers, seeking for a nonpareil of a girl; and lowering her hopes and expectations every week, as she found the difficulty of meeting with any one in a manufacturing town who did not prefer the better wages and greater independence of working in a mill.", "그리하여 마거릿은 더할 나위 없는 처녀아이 하나를 찾아 푸줏간과 식료품 가게를 오르내렸고, 공장에서 일하는 편이 삯도 좋고 훨씬 자유롭다는 이유로 그것을 마다할 사람을 공업 도시에서 만나기가 얼마나 어려운지 알아 가면서 매주 바라는 바와 기대를 낮추어 갔다."], ["It was something of a trial to Margaret to go out by herself in this busy bustling place.", "이 바쁘고 부산한 곳에서 혼자 나다니는 것은 마거릿에게 제법 시련이었다."], ["Mrs. Shaw’s ideas of propriety and her own helpless dependence on others, had always made her insist that a footman should accompany Edith and Margaret, if they went beyond Harley Street or the immediate neighbourhood.", "쇼 부인은 법도에 대한 생각과 남에게 기대는 자신의 무력함 탓에, 이디스와 마거릿이 할리 가나 바로 가까운 동네를 벗어나면 반드시 하인 하나가 따라붙어야 한다고 늘 고집했다."], ["The limits by which this rule of her aunt’s had circumscribed Margaret’s independence had been silently rebelled against at the time: and she had doubly enjoyed the free walks and rambles of her forest life from the contrast which they presented.", "이모의 그 규칙이 마거릿의 자유를 옭아매던 그 울타리에 그녀는 그때도 말없이 반기를 들었었다. 그리고 그 대비 덕분에 숲에서의 삶에서 누리던 자유로운 산책과 나들이를 갑절로 즐겼었다."], ["She went along there with a boundless fearless step, that occasionally broke out into a run, if she were in a hurry, and occasionally was stilled into perfect repose, as she stood listening to, or watching any of the wild creatures who sang in the leafy courts, or glanced out with their keen bright eyes from the low brushwood or tangled furze.", "그곳에서 그녀는 거리낌도 두려움도 없는 걸음으로 나아갔고, 급할 때면 이따금 달음질로 터져 나오기도 했으며, 이따금은 잎이 우거진 뜰에서 노래하거나 낮은 덤불과 뒤엉킨 가시금작화 사이에서 날카롭고 반짝이는 눈으로 내다보는 야생의 것들에 귀 기울이고 눈길을 주느라 완전한 고요 속에 멎어 서기도 했다."], ["It was a trial to come down from such motion or such stillness, only guided by her own sweet will, to the even and decorous pace necessary in streets.", "오로지 자기 마음이 이끄는 대로 움직이거나 멈추던 그런 삶에서 거리에서 지켜야 하는 고르고 점잖은 걸음걸이로 내려앉는 것은 시련이었다."], ["But she could have laughed at herself for minding this change, if it had not been accompanied by what was a more serious annoyance.", "그러나 그 변화를 마음에 두는 자신을 그녀는 웃어넘길 수도 있었을 것이다. 그것에 더 심각한 성가심이 따라붙지만 않았더라면."]], [["The side of the town on which Crampton lay was especially a thoroughfare for the factory people.", "크램프턴이 자리 잡은 쪽의 시내는 특히 공장 사람들이 오가는 길목이었다."], ["In the back streets around them there were many mills, out of which poured streams of men and women two or three times a day.", "그들 주위의 뒷골목에는 공장이 많았고, 하루에 두세 번씩 남자와 여자의 물결이 거기서 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["Until Margaret had learnt the times of their ingress and egress, she was very unfortunate in constantly falling in with them.", "마거릿은 그들이 들고 나는 시각을 알기 전까지 늘 그들과 맞닥뜨리는 운 나쁜 일을 겪었다."], ["They came rushing along, with bold, fearless faces, and loud laughs and jests, particularly aimed at all those who appeared to be above them in rank or station.", "그들은 대담하고 겁 없는 얼굴로, 특히 자기들보다 신분이나 지위가 높아 보이는 이들을 겨냥한 요란한 웃음과 농지거리를 던지며 우르르 몰려왔다."], ["The tones of their unrestrained voices, and their carelessness of all common rules of street politeness, frightened Margaret a little at first.", "거리낌 없는 그 목소리의 가락과 거리에서 지켜야 할 예의라는 통념 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 태도가 처음에는 마거릿을 조금 겁먹게 했다."], ["The girls, with their rough, but not unfriendly freedom, would comment on her dress, even touch her shawl or gown to ascertain the exact material; nay, once or twice she was asked questions relative to some article which they particularly admired.", "처녀아이들은 거칠기는 해도 악의는 없는 스스럼없음으로 그녀의 옷차림을 두고 이러쿵저러쿵했고, 감이 정확히 무엇인지 알아보려고 숄이나 드레스를 만져 보기까지 했다. 아니, 한두 번은 유난히 마음에 드는 물건에 대해 이것저것 묻기도 했다."], ["There was such a simple reliance on her womanly sympathy with their love of dress, and on her kindliness, that she gladly replied to these inquiries, as soon as she understood them; and half smiled back at their remarks.", "옷을 좋아하는 자기들의 마음에 여자로서 공감해 주리라는 것과 그녀의 상냥함에 대한 그 소박한 믿음이 어찌나 곧았던지, 그녀는 물음을 알아듣기가 무섭게 기꺼이 대답해 주었고 그들의 말에 살며시 미소로 답하기도 했다."], ["She did not mind meeting any number of girls, loud spoken and boisterous though they might be.", "그녀는 아무리 많은 처녀아이들과 마주쳐도, 아무리 목소리가 크고 왁자하더라도 개의치 않았다."], ["But she alternately dreaded and fired up against the workmen, who commented not on her dress, but on her looks, in the same open, fearless manner.", "그러나 그녀의 옷차림이 아니라 얼굴을 두고 똑같이 거리낌 없고 겁 없는 태도로 말을 던지는 남자 직공들은 두려워지기도 하고 발끈하게 되기도 했다."], ["She, who had hitherto felt that even the most refined remark on her personal appearance was an impertinence, had to endure undisguised admiration from these outspoken men.", "자기 용모에 대한 말이라면 아무리 점잖은 것이라도 무례라고 여겨 온 그녀가 이 거침없는 사내들의 숨김없는 감탄을 견뎌야 했다."], ["But the very outspokenness marked their innocence of any intention to hurt her delicacy, as she would have perceived if she had been less frightened by the disorderly tumult.", "그러나 바로 그 거침없음이야말로 그녀의 섬세한 마음을 다치게 할 뜻이 그들에게 없다는 것을 드러내는 증거였고, 그 어수선한 소란에 덜 겁먹었더라면 그녀도 그것을 알아보았을 것이다."], ["Out of her fright came a flash of indignation which made her face scarlet, and her dark eyes gather flame, as she heard some of their speeches.", "그 겁먹음에서 분노의 불꽃이 튀어나와, 그들 중 몇몇이 하는 말을 들을 때면 그녀의 얼굴이 새빨개지고 검은 눈에 불이 모였다."], ["Yet there were other sayings of theirs, which, when she reached the quiet safety of home, amused her even while they irritated her.", "그러나 그들이 하는 말 가운데는 조용하고 안전한 집에 이르고 나면 그녀를 언짢게 하면서도 웃음이 나게 하는 것들도 있었다."]], [["For instance, one day, after she had passed a number of men, several of whom had paid her the not unusual compliment of wishing she was their sweetheart, one of the lingerers added, “Your bonny face, my lass, makes the day look brighter.” And another day, as she was unconsciously smiling at some passing thought, she was addressed by a poorly-dressed, middle-aged workman, with, “You may well smile, my lass; many a one would smile to have such a bonny face.” This man looked so careworn that Margaret could not help giving him an answering smile, glad to think that her looks, such as they were, should have had the power to call up a pleasant thought. He seemed to understand her acknowledging glance, and a silent recognition was established between them whenever the chances of the day brought them across each other’s paths. They had never exchanged a word; nothing had been said but that first compliment; yet somehow Margaret looked upon this man with more interest than upon any one else in Milton. Once or twice, on Sundays, she saw him walking with a girl, evidently his daughter, and, if possible, still more unhealthy than he was himself.", "이를테면 어느 날, 여러 사내들을 지나쳐 온 뒤였다. 그중 몇몇은 그녀가 자기 애인이었으면 좋겠다는 그리 드물지 않은 찬사를 바쳤는데, 뒤에 처져 있던 하나가 이렇게 덧붙였다. “아가씨 고운 얼굴을 보니 오늘 하루가 다 환해 보이는구먼.” 또 어느 날은 그녀가 스치는 생각에 저도 모르게 미소 짓고 있는데, 허름한 옷차림의 중년 직공 하나가 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. “웃을 만도 하지, 아가씨. 그런 고운 얼굴을 가졌으면 누군들 안 웃겠소.” 이 사내가 어찌나 고생에 찌들어 보였던지, 마거릿은 그에게 미소로 답하지 않을 수 없었다. 자기 얼굴이 어떻든 그것이 유쾌한 생각 하나를 불러일으킬 힘을 지녔다는 것이 기뻤던 것이다. 그는 알아보았다는 그녀의 눈길을 알아차린 듯했고, 그날그날의 우연이 서로의 길을 엇갈리게 할 때마다 두 사람 사이에는 말 없는 인사가 오가게 되었다. 그들은 한마디도 나눈 적이 없었다. 그 첫 찬사 말고는 아무 말도 오가지 않았다. 그런데도 어쩐지 마거릿은 밀턴의 다른 누구보다 이 사내를 더 마음에 두고 바라보게 되었다. 일요일이면 한두 번 그가 어떤 처녀아이와 함께 걷는 것을 보았다. 분명 그의 딸이었고, 있을 수 있는 일이라면 그보다도 더 몸이 성치 않아 보였다."]], [["One day Margaret and her father had been as far as the fields that lay around the town; it was early spring, and she had gathered some of the hedge and ditch flowers, dog-violets, lesser celandines, and the like, with an unspoken lament in her heart for the sweet profusion of the South.", "어느 날 마거릿과 아버지는 시내를 둘러싼 들판까지 나갔다. 이른 봄이었고, 그녀는 남쪽의 그 흐드러진 꽃들에 대한 말 없는 아쉬움을 가슴에 품은 채 산울타리와 도랑가에 핀 꽃들, 개제비꽃이며 애기미나리아재비 따위를 꺾어 모았다."], ["Her father had left her to go into Milton upon some business; and on the road home she met her humble friends.", "아버지는 볼일이 있어 밀턴 시내로 들어가느라 그녀와 헤어졌다. 그리고 집으로 돌아오는 길에 그녀는 그 보잘것없는 벗들을 만났다."], ["The girl looked wistfully at the flowers, and, acting on a sudden impulse, Margaret offered them to her.", "처녀아이가 꽃을 물끄러미 바라보자, 마거릿은 문득 이는 마음에 이끌려 꽃을 내밀었다."], ["Her pale blue eyes lightened up as she took them, and her father spoke for her.", "꽃을 받아 드는 아이의 옅은 푸른 눈이 환해졌고, 아버지가 아이를 대신해 입을 열었다."]], [["“Thank yo, Miss.", "“고맙수, 아가씨."], ["Bessy’ll think a deal o’ them flowers; that hoo will; and I shall think a deal o’ yor kindness.", "베시가 저 꽃을 아주 소중히 여길 거요. 정말 그럴 거구먼."], ["Yo’re not of this country, I reckon?”", "나도 아가씨의 그 마음씨를 소중히 여기겠소. 아가씨는 이 고장 사람이 아닌 것 같은데, 맞소?”"]], [["“No!”", "“네!”"], ["said Margaret, half sighing.", "마거릿이 반쯤 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I come from the South—from Hampshire,”", "“저는 남쪽에서 왔어요. 햄프셔에서요.”"], ["she continued, a little afraid of wounding his consciousness of ignorance, if she used a name which he did not understand.", "그녀는 그가 알지 못하는 이름을 대었다가 그의 무지를 건드릴까 조금 조심스러워하며 말을 이었다."]], [["“That’s beyond London, I reckon?", "“런던 너머 아니오?"], ["And I come fro’ Burnleyways, and forty miles to th’ North.", "나는 번리 쪽에서 왔소. 북쪽으로 사십 마일이지."], ["And yet, yo see, North and South has both met and made kind o’ friends in this big smoky place.”", "그런데도 이렇게, 이 크고 그을린 곳에서 북과 남이 서로 만나 얼추 친구가 되었구먼.”"]], [["Margaret had slackened her pace to a walk alongside of the man and his daughter, whose steps were regulated by the feebleness of the latter.", "마거릿은 걸음을 늦추어 사내와 그 딸 곁에서 나란히 걸었다. 두 사람의 걸음은 딸의 쇠약함에 맞추어져 있었다."], ["She now spoke to the girl, and there was a sound of tender pity in the tone of her voice as she did so that went right to the heart of the father.", "이제 그녀가 처녀아이에게 말을 건넸는데, 그때 그녀의 목소리에 서린 다정한 연민의 울림이 아버지의 가슴에 곧장 가서 닿았다."]], [["“I’m afraid you are not very strong.”", "“몸이 그리 튼튼하지 않으신가 봐요.”"]], [["“No,”", "“네.”"], ["said the girl, “nor never will be.”", "처녀아이가 말했다. “앞으로도 튼튼해지지 않을 거요.”"]], [["“Spring is coming,”", "“봄이 오고 있잖아요.”"], ["said Margaret, as if to suggest pleasant, hopeful thoughts.", "마거릿이 유쾌하고 희망찬 생각을 일깨우려는 듯 말했다."]], [["“Spring nor summer will do me good,”", "“봄도 여름도 나한테는 소용없어요.”"], ["said the girl quietly.", "처녀아이가 조용히 말했다."]], [["Margaret looked up at the man, almost expecting some contradiction from him, or at least some remark that would modify his daughter’s utter hopelessness.", "마거릿은 사내를 올려다보았다. 그에게서 무슨 반박이라도, 적어도 딸의 그 완전한 절망을 누그러뜨릴 만한 말이라도 나오기를 은근히 기대하면서였다."], ["But instead, he added—", "그러나 그는 도리어 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“I’m afeared hoo speaks the truth.", "“내 딸이 참말을 하는 것 같아 두렵소."], ["I’m afeared hoo’s too far gone in a waste.”", "저 애가 너무 멀리 시들어 버린 것 같아 두렵구먼.”"]], [["“I shall have a spring where I am boun’ to, and flowers, and amaranths, and shining robes besides.”", "“내가 가려는 곳에는 봄이 있을 거요. 꽃도 있고, 시들지 않는 꽃도 있고, 빛나는 옷도 있을 거고.”"]], [["“Poor lass, poor lass!”", "“가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!”"], ["said her father in a low tone.", "아버지가 낮은 소리로 말했다."], ["“I’m none so sure o’ that; but it’s a comfort to thee, poor lass, poor lass.", "“나야 그런 게 있는지 잘 모르겠소만, 네게 위로가 된다면야, 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것."], ["Poor father!", "가엾은 애비!"], ["it’ll be soon.”", "이제 곧이겠구먼.”"]], [["Margaret was shocked by his words—shocked but not repelled; rather attracted and interested.", "마거릿은 그의 말에 충격을 받았다. 충격을 받았으나 물러서지는 않았다. 오히려 마음이 끌리고 관심이 갔다."]], [["“Where do you live?", "“어디 사세요?"], ["I think we must be neighbours, we meet so often on this road.”", "이 길에서 이토록 자주 마주치는 걸 보면 우리는 이웃인가 봐요.”"]], [["“We put up at nine Frances Street, second turn to th’ left at after yo’ve past th’ Goulden Dragon.”", "“프랜시스 가 9번지에 묵고 있소. 황금 용 여관을 지나서 왼쪽으로 두 번째 골목이오.”"]], [["“And your name?", "“성함은요?"], ["I must not forget that.”", "잊으면 안 되니까요.”"]], [["“I’m none ashamed o’ my name.", "“내 이름을 부끄러워할 것 없소."], ["It’s Nicholas Higgins.", "니컬러스 히긴스요."], ["Hoo’s called Bessy Higgins.", "얘는 베시 히긴스라 하고."], ["Whatten yo’ asking for?”", "그건 뭣하러 묻소?”"]], [["Margaret was surprised at this last question, for at Helstone it would have been an understood thing, after the enquiries she had made, that she intended to come and call upon any poor neighbour whose name and habitation she had asked for.", "마거릿은 이 마지막 물음에 놀랐다. 헬스톤에서라면 그렇게 물어본 다음에 이름과 사는 곳을 물은 가난한 이웃을 찾아가 볼 뜻이라는 것쯤은 말 안 해도 아는 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["“I thought—I meant to come and see you.”", "“저는…… 저는 찾아뵐 생각이었어요.”"], ["She suddenly felt rather shy of offering the visit, without having any reason to give for her wish to make it, beyond a kindly interest in a stranger.", "그녀는 문득 그 방문을 청하는 것이 조금 겸연쩍어졌다. 낯선 이에게 품은 다정한 관심 말고는 그러고 싶은 이유를 댈 것이 없었기 때문이다."], ["It seemed all at once to take the shape of an impertinence on her part; she read this meaning too in the man’s eyes.", "그것이 갑자기 자기 쪽의 주제넘은 짓처럼 여겨졌다. 그 사내의 눈에서도 그녀는 그런 뜻을 읽어 냈다."]], [["“I’m none so fond of having strange folk in my house.” But then relenting, as he saw her heightened colour, he added, “Yo’re a foreigner, as one may say, and maybe don’t know many folk here, and yo’ve given my wench here flowers out of yo’r own hand;—yo may come if yo like.”", "“나는 낯선 사람이 우리 집에 오는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않소.” 그러나 그녀의 붉어진 얼굴빛을 보고는 마음이 누그러져 그가 덧붙였다. “아가씨는 말하자면 타관 사람이고, 여기서 아는 이도 별로 없겠지. 게다가 우리 딸애한테 손수 꽃을 주지 않았소. 오고 싶으면 오시구려.”"]], [["Margaret was half amused, half-nettled at this answer.", "마거릿은 이 대답에 반쯤은 우스웠고 반쯤은 언짢았다."], ["She was not sure if she would go where permission was given so like a favour conferred.", "이렇게 은혜라도 베풀 듯 내주는 허락을 받고 찾아가야 할지 마음이 서지 않았다."], ["But when they came to the turn into Frances Street, the girl stopped a minute, and said,", "그러나 프랜시스 가로 꺾어 드는 길목에 이르자 처녀아이가 잠깐 멈춰 서서 말했다."]], [["“Yo’ll not forget yo’re to come and see us.”", "“우리 보러 오시기로 한 거 잊지 마시우.”"]], [["“Aye, aye,”", "“그럼, 그럼.”"], ["said the father, impatiently, “hoo’ll come.", "아버지가 성마르게 말했다. “이 아가씨는 올 거다."], ["Hoo’s a bit set up now, because hoo thinks I might ha’ spoken more civilly; but hoo’ll think better on it, and come.", "지금은 좀 뻗대는 거야. 내가 더 곱게 말할 수 있었는데 싶으니까."], ["I can read her proud bonny face like a book.", "그래도 다시 생각해 보고 올 거다. 나는 저 도도하고 고운 얼굴을 책처럼 읽는다."], ["Come along, Bess; there’s the mill bell ringing.”", "어서 가자, 베스. 공장 종이 울리는구나.”"]], [["Margaret went home, wondering at her new friends, and smiling at the man’s insight into what had been passing in her mind.", "마거릿은 집으로 돌아가며 새로 사귄 벗들을 신기해했고, 자기 마음속에서 오가던 것을 꿰뚫어 본 사내의 눈썰미에 미소 지었다."], ["From that day Milton became a brighter place to her.", "그날부터 밀턴은 그녀에게 한결 밝은 곳이 되었다."], ["It was not the long, bleak sunny days of spring, nor yet was it that time was reconciling her to the town of her habitation.", "그것은 길고 스산하고 볕 좋은 봄날들 때문도 아니었고, 세월이 그녀를 사는 곳과 화해시키고 있었기 때문도 아니었다."], ["It was that in it she had found a human interest.", "그곳에서 그녀가 사람에 대한 관심을 찾아냈기 때문이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# 제9장"]], [["“Let China’s earth, enriched with coloured stains, Pencil’d with gold, and streaked with azure veins, The grateful flavour of the Indian leaf, Or Mocha’s sunburnt berry glad receive.”", "“빛깔 고운 물감으로 짙어지고, 금으로 그려지고, 하늘빛 무늬가 뻗은 중국의 흙이여, 인도 잎사귀의 고마운 향기를, 아니면 모카의 볕에 그을린 열매를 기꺼이 받아들이라.”"], ["MRS. BARBOULD.", "— 바볼드 부인."]], [["The day after this meeting with Higgins and his daughter Mr. Hale came upstairs into the little drawing-room at an unusual hour.", "히긴스 부녀를 만난 이튿날, 헤일 씨는 여느 때와 다른 시각에 위층 작은 응접실로 올라왔다."], ["He went up to different objects in the room, as if examining them, but Margaret saw that it was merely a nervous trick—a way of putting off something he wished, yet feared to say.", "그는 방 안의 이런저런 물건 앞으로 다가가 그것들을 살펴보는 듯했지만, 마거릿은 그것이 그저 초조할 때 나오는 버릇임을 알아보았다. 말하고 싶으면서도 말하기가 두려운 무엇인가를 미루는 방식이었던 것이다."], ["Out it came at last—", "마침내 그것이 튀어나왔다."]], [["“My dear!", "“여보!"], ["I’ve asked Mr. Thornton to come to tea to-night.”", "오늘 저녁에 차 마시러 오시라고 손턴 씨에게 청해 두었소.”"]], [["Mrs. Hale was leaning back in her easy chair, with her eyes shut, and an expression of pain on her face which had become habitual to her of late.", "헤일 부인은 안락의자에 몸을 기댄 채 눈을 감고 있었고, 얼굴에는 요즈음 들어 버릇처럼 자리 잡은 고통스러운 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["But she roused up into querulousness at this speech of her husband’s.", "그러나 남편의 이 말에 그녀는 몸을 일으키며 투덜대기 시작했다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton!—and to-night!", "“손턴 씨라니요!"], ["What in the world does the man want to come here for?", "그것도 오늘 저녁에! 대체 그 사람이 무엇하러 여기까지 온답니까?"], ["And Dixon is washing my muslins and laces, and there is no soft water with these horrid east winds, which I suppose we shall have all the year round in Milton.”", "딕슨은 내 모슬린이며 레이스를 빨고 있고, 이 지긋지긋한 동풍 탓에 단물도 없는데. 밀턴에서는 이 바람이 일 년 내내 불겠지요.”"]], [["“The wind is veering round, my dear,” said Mr.", "“바람이 방향을 바꾸고 있소, 여보.”"], ["Hale, looking out at the smoke, which drifted right from the east, only he did not yet understand the points of the compass, and rather arranged them ad libitum according to circumstances.", "헤일 씨가 창밖의 연기를 내다보며 말했다. 연기는 곧장 동쪽에서 흘러오고 있었지만, 그는 아직 방위를 제대로 알지 못해 형편에 따라 제멋대로 정하곤 했다."]], [["“Don’t tell me!” said Mrs.", "“그런 말씀 마세요!”"], ["Hale, shuddering up, and wrapping her shawl about her still more closely.", "헤일 부인이 몸을 떨며 숄을 한층 단단히 여미고 말했다."], ["“But, east or west wind, I suppose this man comes.”", "“하지만 동풍이든 서풍이든 그 사람은 오겠지요.”"]], [["“Oh mamma, that shows you never saw Mr. Thornton.", "“어머니, 그건 어머니가 손턴 씨를 못 뵈어서 하시는 말씀이에요."], ["He looks like a person who would enjoy battling with every adverse thing he could meet with—enemies, winds, or circumstances.", "그분은 맞닥뜨릴 수 있는 온갖 궂은 것과 싸우기를 즐길 사람처럼 보이거든요. 적이든 바람이든 형편이든 말이에요."], ["The more it rains and blows, the more certain we are to have him.", "비바람이 거셀수록 그분은 틀림없이 오실 거예요."], ["But I’ll go and help Dixon.", "그건 그렇고 저는 가서 딕슨을 돕겠어요."], ["I’m getting to be a famous clear-starcher.", "저도 이제 풀 먹여 다리는 솜씨가 제법이랍니다."], ["And he won’t want any amusement beyond talking to Papa.", "그리고 그분은 아버지와 이야기하는 것 말고는 다른 심심풀이가 필요 없으실 거예요."], ["Papa, I am really longing to see the Pythias to your Damon.", "아버지, 저는 정말이지 아버지의 다몬에게 짝이 되는 피티아스를 보고 싶어요."], ["You know I never saw him but once, and then we were so puzzled to know what to say to each other that we did not get on particularly well.”", "아시다시피 저는 그분을 딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐이고, 그때는 서로 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 쩔쩔매느라 그리 잘 어울리지 못했잖아요.”"]], [["“I don’t know that you would ever like him, or think him agreeable, Margaret.", "“네가 그 사람을 좋아하게 될지, 상냥한 사람이라고 여기게 될지는 모르겠구나, 마거릿."], ["He is not a lady’s man.”", "그는 부인네들 비위를 맞추는 사람이 아니야.”"]], [["Margaret wreathed her throat in a scornful curve.", "마거릿은 경멸하는 듯한 곡선으로 목을 젖혔다."]], [["“I don’t particularly admire ladies’ men, papa. But Mr.", "“저는 부인네들 비위나 맞추는 남자를 딱히 좋아하지 않아요, 아버지."], ["Thornton comes here as your friend—as one who has appreciated you”—", "하지만 손턴 씨는 아버지의 벗으로 오시는 거잖아요. 아버지의 진가를 알아본 분으로서요.”"]], [["“The only person in Milton,”", "“밀턴에서 유일한 사람이지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."]], [["“So we will give him a welcome, and some cocoa-nut cakes.", "“그러니 우리도 그분을 반갑게 맞아 드려요. 코코넛 과자도 좀 내고요."], ["Dixon will be flattered if we ask her to make some; and I will undertake to iron your caps, mamma.”", "딕슨한테 만들어 달라고 부탁하면 우쭐해할 거예요. 그리고 어머니 모자는 제가 다려 놓겠어요.”"]], [["Many a time that morning did Margaret wish Mr. Thornton far enough away.", "그날 아침 마거릿은 손턴 씨가 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으면 하고 몇 번이나 바랐다."], ["She had planned other employments for herself: a letter to Edith, a good piece of Dante, a visit to the Higginses.", "그녀는 다른 일거리를 계획해 두었던 것이다. 이디스에게 보낼 편지, 단테를 한 대목 읽는 일, 히긴스네 방문."], ["But, instead, she ironed away, listening to Dixon’s complaints, and only hoping that by an excess of sympathy she might prevent her from carrying the recital of her sorrows to Mrs.", "그러나 그 대신 그녀는 딕슨의 푸념을 들어 주며 다림질만 해 댔다. 넘칠 만큼 맞장구를 쳐 주면 딕슨이 그 서러운 사연을 헤일 부인에게까지 늘어놓지 않으리라 바랄 뿐이었다."], ["Hale. Every now and then, Margaret had to remind herself of her father’s regard for Mr.", "이따금 마거릿은 아버지가 손턴 씨를 얼마나 아끼는지를 스스로에게 일깨워야 했다."], ["Thornton, to subdue the irritation of weariness that was stealing over her, and bringing on one of the bad headaches to which she had lately become liable.", "슬며시 밀려드는 고단함의 짜증을 눌러야 했기 때문이고, 그 짜증이 요즈음 들어 곧잘 앓게 된 그 지독한 두통 가운데 하나를 불러오고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She could hardly speak when she sat down at last, and told her mother that she was no longer Peggy the laundry-maid, but Margaret Hale the lady.", "마침내 자리에 앉았을 때 그녀는 말할 기운조차 거의 없었다. 그래도 그녀는 어머니에게, 이제 자기는 빨래하는 페기가 아니라 숙녀 마거릿 헤일이라고 말했다."], ["She meant this speech for a little joke, and was vexed enough with her busy tongue when she found her mother taking it seriously.", "자그마한 농담으로 한 말이었는데, 어머니가 그것을 진담으로 받아들이는 것을 보고는 부산한 제 혀가 어지간히 원망스러웠다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그래!"], ["if any one had told me, when I was Miss Beresford, and one of the belles of the country, that a child of mine would have to stand half a day, in a little poky kitchen, working away like any servant, that we might prepare properly for the reception of a tradesman, and that this tradesman should be the only”—", "내가 베러스퍼드 양이던 시절, 이 고장에서 손꼽히는 미인이던 시절에 누가 나더러 이런 말을 했다면 어땠을까. 내 자식이 반나절이나 비좁고 답답한 부엌에 서서 하녀나 다름없이 일을 해야 한다고, 그것도 장사꾼 하나를 제대로 맞이할 채비를 하느라 그래야 한다고, 게다가 그 장사꾼이 유일한……”"]], [["“Oh, mamma!”", "“어머니!”"], ["said Margaret, lifting herself up, “don’t punish me for so careless a speech.", "마거릿이 몸을 일으키며 말했다. “그렇게 생각 없이 한 말로 저를 벌하지 마세요."], ["I don’t mind ironing, or any kind of work for you and papa.", "저는 다림질도, 어머니와 아버지를 위한 어떤 일도 싫지 않아요."], ["I am myself a born and bred lady through it all, even though it comes to scouring a floor, or washing dishes.", "바닥을 문질러 닦고 설거지를 하게 된다 해도, 저는 그 내내 나면서부터 자라기까지 숙녀인걸요."], ["I am tired now, just for a little while; but in half an hour I shall be ready to do the same over again.", "지금은 잠깐 지쳤을 뿐이에요. 반 시간만 지나면 다시 똑같은 일을 할 수 있을 거예요."], ["And as to Mr. Thornton’s being in trade, why he can’t help that now, poor fellow.", "그리고 손턴 씨가 장사를 한다는 것으로 말하자면, 그분도 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일이잖아요, 딱하게도."], ["I don’t suppose his education would fit him for much else.”", "그분이 받은 교육으로는 다른 일에 그리 어울리지도 않았을 테고요.”"], ["Margaret lifted herself slowly up, and went to her own room; for just now she could not bear much more.", "마거릿은 천천히 몸을 일으켜 자기 방으로 갔다. 바로 지금은 더 견딜 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["In Mr. Thornton’s house, at this very same time, a similar, yet different scene was going on.", "바로 그 시각, 손턴 씨의 집에서는 비슷하면서도 사뭇 다른 정경이 펼쳐지고 있었다."], ["A large-boned lady, long past middle age, sat at work in a grim handsomely-furnished dining-room.", "중년을 훌쩍 넘긴 뼈대 굵은 부인이 을씨년스럽되 훌륭하게 꾸며진 식당에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다. 그녀의 이목구비는 체구와 마찬가지로 둔중하다기보다 강하고 굵직했다."], ["Her features, like her frame, were strong and massive, rather than heavy.", "그녀의 얼굴은 하나의 단호한 표정에서 그에 못지않게 단호한 또 하나의 표정으로 천천히 옮겨 갔다."], ["Her face moved slowly from one decided expression to another equally decided.", "그 얼굴에 이렇다 할 변화는 없었지만, 한 번 그것을 본 사람은 대개 다시 한번 보게 되었다."], ["There was no great variety in her countenance; but those who looked at it once, generally looked at it again; even the passers by in the street, half-turned their heads to gaze an instant longer at the firm, severe, dignified woman, who never gave way in street-courtesy, or paused in her straight-onward course to the clearly defined end which she proposed to herself.", "거리의 행인들조차 고개를 반쯤 돌려 그 굳세고 엄격하고 위엄 있는 여인을 한순간 더 바라보곤 했다. 길에서 남에게 양보하는 법이 없고, 스스로 정한 또렷한 목적지를 향해 곧장 나아가는 걸음을 멈추는 법도 없는 여인이었다."]], [["She was handsomely dressed in stout black silk, of which not a thread was worn or discoloured.", "그녀는 올이 닳거나 빛이 바랜 데라고는 한 올도 없는 두툼한 검은 비단옷을 훌륭하게 차려입고 있었다."], ["She was mending a large, long table-cloth of the finest texture, holding it up against the light occasionally to discover thin places, which required her delicate care.", "그녀는 더없이 고운 올로 짠 크고 긴 식탁보를 깁는 중이었는데, 이따금 그것을 빛에 비추어 보며 그 섬세한 손길이 필요한 얇아진 자리를 찾아냈다."], ["There was not a book about in the room, with the exception of Matthew Henry’s Bible commentaries, six volumes of which lay in the centre of the massive side-board, flanked by a tea-urn on one side, and a lamp on the other.", "방 안에는 책이라고는 없었다. 매슈 헨리의 성경 주석만은 예외여서, 그 여섯 권이 묵직한 찬장 한가운데 놓인 채 한쪽에는 차 항아리를, 다른 쪽에는 등을 거느리고 있었다."], ["In some remote apartment, there was exercise upon the piano going on.", "저 멀리 어느 방에서는 피아노 연습이 한창이었다."], ["Someone was practising up a morceau de salon, playing it very rapidly, every third note, on an average, being either indistinct, or wholly missed out, and the loud chords at the end being half of them false, but not the less satisfactory to the performer.", "누군가 살롱풍의 소품을 익히는 중이었는데, 어찌나 빠르게 치는지 평균 세 음마다 하나는 흐릿하거나 아예 빠졌고, 끝의 우렁찬 화음도 절반은 틀렸다. 그렇다고 치는 이의 흡족함이 덜해지지는 않았지만."], ["Mrs. Thornton heard a step, like her own in its decisive character, pass the dining-room door.", "손턴 부인은 단호한 성격에서 제 것을 닮은 발소리가 식당 문 앞을 지나가는 것을 들었다."]], [["“John!", "“존!"], ["Is that you?”", "너냐?”"]], [["Her son opened the door, and showed himself.", "아들이 문을 열고 모습을 보였다."]], [["“What has brought you home so early? I thought you were going to tea with that friend of Mr.", "“무슨 일로 이렇게 일찍 왔느냐?"], ["Bell’s; that Mr. Hale?”", "벨 씨의 그 친구, 헤일 씨라는 사람과 차를 마시러 가는 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["“So I am, mother.", "“가는 길입니다, 어머니."], ["I am come home to dress!”", "옷을 갈아입으러 온 거예요.”"]], [["“Dress! humph!", "“옷을 갈아입어!"], ["When I was a girl, young men were satisfied with dressing once in a day.", "흥! 내가 처녀 적에는 젊은 사내들이 하루에 한 번 차려입는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Why should you dress to go and take a cup of tea with an old parson?”", "늙은 목사와 차 한잔 마시러 가면서 무엇하러 옷을 갈아입는단 말이냐?”"]], [["“Mr. Hale is a gentleman, and his wife and daughter are ladies.”", "“헤일 씨는 신사이고, 그 부인과 딸은 숙녀입니다.”"]], [["“Wife and daughter!", "“부인과 딸이라!"], ["Do they teach too?", "그 사람들도 가르치느냐?"], ["What do they do?", "무엇을 하는 사람들이냐?"], ["You have never mentioned them.”", "너는 한 번도 그들 이야기를 한 적이 없구나.”"]], [["“No! mother, because I have never seen Mrs.", "“안 했지요, 어머니."], ["Hale; I have only seen Miss Hale for half an hour.”", "저는 헤일 부인을 뵌 적이 없고, 헤일 양도 반 시간 보았을 뿐이니까요.”"]], [["“Take care you don’t get caught by a penniless girl, John.”", "“한 푼 없는 처녀에게 걸려들지 않도록 조심해라, 존.”"]], [["“I am not easily caught, mother, as I think you know.", "“저는 쉽게 걸려드는 사람이 아닙니다, 어머니. 어머니도 아시잖습니까."], ["But I must not have Miss Hale spoken of in that way, which, you know, is offensive to me.", "하지만 헤일 양을 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것은 참을 수 없습니다. 아시다시피 그건 제게 거슬리는 일이니까요."], ["I never was aware of any young lady trying to catch me yet, nor do I believe that any one has ever given themselves that useless trouble.”", "저는 지금껏 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 저를 낚으려 애쓰는 것을 본 적이 없고, 누군가 그런 쓸데없는 수고를 했으리라고 믿지도 않습니다.”"]], [["Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 그 점을 아들에게 순순히 양보할 마음이 없었다."], ["Thornton did not choose to yield the point to her son; or else she had, in general, pride enough for her sex.", "아니면 그녀에게는 대체로 여자라는 편을 위한 자부심이 넘칠 만큼 있었던 것이거나."]], [["“Well!", "“좋다!"], ["I only say, take care.", "나는 그저 조심하라는 것뿐이다. 우리 밀턴 처녀들이야 기개와 좋은 마음씨가 있어 남편을 낚으러 다니지 않을지도 모르지."], ["Perhaps our Milton girls have too much spirit and good feeling to go angling after husbands; but this Miss Hale comes out of the aristocratic counties, where, if all tales be true, rich husbands are reckoned prizes.”", "하지만 이 헤일 양이라는 아이는 귀족 티 나는 고장에서 왔지 않느냐. 소문이 다 사실이라면 그곳에서는 돈 많은 남편을 상품으로 친다더구나.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton’s brow contracted, and he came a step forward into the room.", "손턴 씨의 이마가 찌푸려졌고, 그는 방 안으로 한 걸음 들어섰다."]], [["“Mother”", "“어머니.”"], ["(with a short scornful laugh), “you will make me confess.", "그가 짤막하게 경멸 어린 웃음을 흘리며 말했다. “기어이 제 입으로 털어놓게 만드시는군요."], ["The only time I saw Miss Hale, she treated me with a haughty civility which had a strong flavour of contempt in it.", "제가 헤일 양을 본 그 단 한 번, 그 아가씨는 저를 도도한 예의로 대했고 거기에는 멸시의 기색이 짙게 배어 있었습니다."], ["She held herself aloof from me as if she had been a queen, and I her humble, unwashed vassal.", "그녀는 마치 자기가 여왕이고 제가 씻지도 않은 미천한 종복이기라도 한 것처럼 저를 멀리했지요."], ["Be easy, mother.”", "마음 놓으세요, 어머니.”"]], [["“No!", "“아니다!"], ["I am not easy nor content either.", "나는 마음이 놓이지도 않고 흡족하지도 않다."], ["What business had she, a renegade clergyman’s daughter, to turn up her nose at you!", "국교회를 저버린 목사의 딸년이 무슨 자격으로 네 앞에서 코를 치켜든단 말이냐!"], ["I would dress for none of them—a saucy set! if I were you.”", "내가 너라면 그런 것들을 위해서는 옷 한 벌 갈아입지 않겠다."], ["As he was leaving the room he said:—", "건방진 것들 같으니!” 그가 방을 나서며 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Hale is good, and gentle, and learned.", "“헤일 씨는 선하고 온화하고 학식이 깊은 분입니다."], ["He is not saucy.", "건방지지 않아요."], ["As for Mrs. Hale, I will tell you what she is like to-night, if you care to hear.”", "헤일 부인으로 말하자면, 들으실 마음이 있으시면 오늘 밤 그분이 어떤 분인지 말씀드리지요.”"], ["He shut the door, and was gone.", "그는 문을 닫고 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Despise my son!", "“내 아들을 멸시해!"], ["treat him as her vassal, indeed!", "그 애를 제 종복 취급하다니, 원!"], ["Humph!", "흥!"], ["I should like to know where she could find such another!", "그런 아들을 대체 어디서 또 찾을 수 있는지 나야말로 알고 싶구나!"], ["Boy and man, he’s the noblest, stoutest heart I ever knew.", "어릴 적에도 어른이 되어서도 저 애는 내가 아는 가장 고귀하고 굳센 심장이다."], ["I don’t care if I am his mother; I can see what’s what, and not be blind.", "내가 제 어미라서 하는 소리라 해도 상관없다. 나는 무엇이 무엇인지 볼 줄 알고, 눈이 멀지도 않았다."], ["I know what Fanny is; and I know what John is.", "나는 패니가 어떤 아이인지 알고, 존이 어떤 아이인지 안다."], ["Despise him!", "그 애를 멸시해!"], ["I hate her!”", "나는 그 계집애가 밉다!”"]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# 제10장"]], [["“We are the trees whom shaking fastens more.”", "「우리는 흔들릴수록 더욱 단단히 뿌리를 내리는 나무다.」"], ["GEORGE HERBERT.", "— 조지 허버트."]], [["Mr. Thornton left the house without coming into the dining-room again.", "손턴 씨는 식당에 다시 들르지 않고 그 집을 나섰다."], ["He was rather late, and walked rapidly out to Crampton.", "시간이 좀 늦은 터라 그는 크램프턴까지 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["He was anxious not to slight his new friend by any disrespectful unpunctuality.", "무례하게 시간을 어겨 새로 사귄 벗을 소홀히 대하는 일이 없기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["The church-clock struck half-past seven as he stood at the door awaiting Dixon’s slow movements; always doubly tardy when she had to degrade herself by answering the door-bell.", "문 앞에 서서 딕슨의 굼뜬 움직임을 기다리는 동안 교회 종이 7시 반을 쳤다. 딕슨은 초인종에 응하느라 제 몸을 낮춰야 할 때면 늘 갑절로 느릿느릿했다."], ["He was ushered into the little drawing-room, and kindly greeted by Mr.", "그는 자그마한 응접실로 안내되었고, 헤일 씨가 반갑게 맞아 부인에게로 데려갔다."], ["Hale, who led him up to his wife, whose pale face, and shawl-draped figure made a silent excuse for the cold languor of her greeting.", "부인의 창백한 얼굴과 숄을 두른 몸매가 그 차고 나른한 인사를 말없이 변명해 주었다. 그가 들어섰을 때 마거릿은 등불을 켜고 있었다."], ["Margaret was lighting the lamp when he entered, for the darkness was coming on.", "어둠이 내려앉고 있었던 것이다. 등불은 어스름한 방 한가운데로 고운 빛을 던졌다."], ["The lamp threw a pretty light into the centre of the dusky room, from which, with country habits, they did not exclude the night-skies, and the outer darkness of air.", "시골 습관대로 그들은 그 방에서 밤하늘도, 바깥 공기의 어둠도 굳이 막아 두지 않았다. 어쩐지 그 방은 그가 조금 전에 떠나온 방과 견주어졌다."], ["Somehow, that room contrasted itself with the one he had lately left; handsome, ponderous, with no sign of female habitation, except in the one spot where his mother sate, and no convenience for any other employment than eating and drinking.", "그쪽은 번듯하고 육중했으나, 어머니가 앉는 그 한 자리를 빼고는 여자가 사는 흔적이라고는 없었고, 먹고 마시는 것 말고 다른 일에 쓸 만한 편의라고는 하나도 없었다. 하기야 그곳은 식당이었다."], ["To be sure, it was a dining-room; his mother preferred to sit in it; and her will was a household law.", "어머니가 거기 앉기를 좋아했고, 그 뜻이 곧 그 집안의 법이었다. 그러나 그 집 응접실도 이 방 같지는 않았다."], ["But the drawing-room was not like this.", "그쪽이 두 배는, 아니 스무 배는 더 훌륭했다."], ["It was twice—twenty times as fine; not one quarter as comfortable.", "다만 아늑하기로는 4분의 1도 되지 못했다. 이곳에는 거울이 없었다."], ["Here were no mirrors, not even a scrap of glass to reflect the light, and answer the same purpose as water in a landscape; no gilding; a warm, sober breadth of colouring, well relieved by the dear old Helstone chintz-curtains and chair covers.", "빛을 되비쳐 풍경 속의 물과 같은 구실을 해 줄 유리 조각 하나 없었다. 금박도 없었다."], ["An open davenport stood in the window opposite the door; in the other there was a stand, with a tall white china vase, from which drooped wreaths of English ivy, pale green birch, and copper-coloured beech-leaves.", "따뜻하고 차분한 빛깔이 넉넉하게 깔려 있었고, 정든 헬스톤 시절의 사라사 커튼과 의자 덮개가 그것을 잘 받쳐 주었다. 문 맞은편 창가에는 뚜껑을 열어 둔 책상이 놓여 있었고, 다른 창가에는 받침대 위에 키 큰 흰 도자기 꽃병이 있어 잉글랜드 담쟁이덩굴과 연둣빛 자작나무 잎과 구릿빛 너도밤나무 잎이 늘어져 있었다."], ["Pretty baskets of work stood about in different places: and books, not cared for on account of their binding solely, lay on one table, as if recently put down.", "여기저기에 고운 반짇고리가 놓여 있었다. 겉장 때문에만 아껴지는 것이 아닌 책들이 방금 내려놓은 듯 탁자 하나에 놓여 있었다."], ["Behind the door was another table decked out for tea, with a white table-cloth, on which flourished the cocoa-nut cakes, and a basket piled with oranges and ruddy American apples, heaped on leaves.", "문 뒤에는 차를 위해 흰 식탁보로 차려 놓은 또 다른 탁자가 있었고, 그 위에는 코코넛 과자가 넉넉히 담겨 있었으며, 잎사귀를 깔고 오렌지와 발그레한 아메리카 사과를 수북이 쌓은 바구니가 놓여 있었다."]], [["It appeared to Mr. Thornton that all these graceful cares were habitual to the family; and especially of a piece with Margaret.", "손턴 씨가 보기에 이 우아한 손길들은 이 집안에 몸에 밴 것이었고, 특히 마거릿과 한 폭을 이루고 있었다."], ["She stood by the tea-table in a light-coloured muslin gown, which had a good deal of pink about it.", "그녀는 분홍빛이 많이 도는 밝은 색 모슬린 드레스 차림으로 찻상 곁에 서 있었다."], ["She looked as if she was not attending to the conversation, but solely busy with the tea-cups, among which her round ivory hands moved with pretty, noiseless, daintiness.", "그녀는 대화에 마음을 두지 않고 오직 찻잔에만 골몰한 듯 보였는데, 그 사이로 상아처럼 둥근 두 손이 곱고 소리 없이 사뿐사뿐 움직였다. 가느다란 한쪽 팔에 팔찌를 하나 끼고 있었는데, 그것이 자꾸만 둥근 손목 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["She had a bracelet on one taper arm, which would fall down over her round wrist. Mr.", "손턴 씨는 그 성가신 장신구를 도로 밀어 올리는 모습을 그녀 아버지의 말을 듣는 것보다 훨씬 더 열심히 지켜보았다."], ["Thornton watched the replacing of this troublesome ornament with far more attention than he listened to her father.", "그녀가 부드러운 살이 조일 만큼 참을성 없이 팔찌를 밀어 올리는 것을, 그러고 나면 그것이 느슨해지다가 마침내 흘러내리는 것을 지켜보는 일에 그는 홀린 듯했다. 하마터면 “또 흘러내리는군!”"], ["It seemed as if it fascinated him to see her push it up impatiently until it tightened her soft flesh; and then to mark the loosening—the fall.", "하고 소리칠 뻔했다. 그가 온 뒤로 차 준비에 남은 일이 얼마 없어서, 먹고 마셔야 하는 의무가 이토록 빨리 다가와 마거릿을 지켜보는 것을 막는 것이 그는 못내 아쉬울 지경이었다."], ["He could almost have exclaimed—“There it goes again!”", "그녀는 마지못해 부림을 받는 사람처럼 도도한 태도로 그에게 찻잔을 건넸다."], ["There was so little left to be done after he arrived at the preparation for tea, that he was almost sorry the obligation of eating and drinking came so soon to prevent him watching Margaret.", "그러면서도 그가 한 잔 더 마실 때가 되었다는 것을 놓치지 않고 알아차렸다. 그는 그녀가 아버지를 위해 마지못해 해 주는 그 일을 자기에게도 해 달라고 청하고 싶은 마음이 들 뻔했다."], ["She handed him his cup of tea with the proud air of an unwilling slave; but her eye caught the moment when he was ready for another cup; and he almost longed to ask her to do for him what he saw her compelled to do for her father, who took her little finger and thumb in his masculine hand, and made them serve as sugar-tongues.", "아버지는 사내다운 손으로 그녀의 새끼손가락과 엄지를 잡아 각설탕 집게 대신으로 삼았던 것이다. 손턴 씨는 아무도 보지 못하려니 여기며 두 사람 사이에 오가는 그 자그마한 무언극이 이어지는 동안, 그녀의 아름다운 두 눈이 빛과 반쯤의 웃음과 반쯤의 사랑을 가득 담고 아버지를 올려다보는 것을 보았다."], ["Mr. Thornton saw her beautiful eyes lifted to her father, full of light, half-laughter, and half-love, as this bit of pantomime went on between the two, unobserved as they fancied, by any.", "마거릿은 여전히 머리가 아팠고, 창백한 낯빛과 말수 없음이 그것을 말해 주었을 법도 했다."], ["Margaret’s head still ached, as the paleness of her complexion, and her silence might have testified; but she was resolved to throw herself into the breach, if there was any long untoward pause, rather than that her father’s friend, pupil, and guest should have cause to think himself in any way neglected.", "그러나 그녀는 어색한 침묵이 길게 이어지면 아버지의 벗이자 제자이자 손님인 이가 조금이라도 홀대받았다고 여기게 하느니 차라리 자기가 그 틈을 메우겠다고 마음먹고 있었다."], ["But the conversation went on; and Margaret drew into a corner, near her mother, with her work, after the tea-things were taken away; and felt that she might let her thoughts roam, without fear of being suddenly wanted to fill up a gap.", "그러나 이야기는 끊이지 않고 이어졌다. 찻그릇을 물린 뒤 마거릿은 바느질감을 들고 어머니 곁 구석으로 물러났고, 갑자기 빈자리를 채우라고 불려 나갈 걱정 없이 마음껏 생각에 잠겨도 되겠다고 느꼈다."]], [["Mr. Thornton and Mr.", "손턴 씨와 헤일 씨는 지난번 만남에서 꺼냈던 어떤 이야기를 이어 가는 데 둘 다 푹 빠져 있었다."], ["Hale were both absorbed in the continuation of some object which had been started at their last meeting.", "마거릿은 어머니가 나직이 건넨 대수롭지 않은 한마디에 다시 지금 이 자리로 돌아왔다."], ["Margaret was recalled to a sense of the present by some trivial, low-spoken remark of her mother’s; and on suddenly looking up from her work, her eye was caught by the difference of outward appearance between her father and Mr.", "그리고 문득 바느질감에서 눈을 들다가, 그토록 뚜렷이 맞선 두 천성을 드러내는 아버지와 손턴 씨의 겉모습 차이에 눈길을 빼앗겼다. 아버지는 여윈 몸매여서 실제보다 키가 커 보였다."], ["Thornton, as betokening such distinctly opposite natures.", "지금처럼 다른 사람의 크고 육중한 체구와 나란히 놓이지만 않는다면 말이다."], ["Her father was of slight figure, which made him appear taller than he really was, when not contrasted, as at this time, with the tall, massive frame of another.", "아버지 얼굴의 주름은 부드럽게 물결졌고, 그 위로 자주 일렁이듯 떨리는 움직임이 지나가 온갖 흔들리는 감정을 그대로 드러냈다. 눈꺼풀은 크고 활처럼 휘어 있어 두 눈에 거의 여자 같다 할 나른한 아름다움을 주었다."], ["The lines in her father’s face were soft and waving, with a frequent undulating kind of trembling movement passing over them, showing every fluctuating emotion; the eyelids were large and arched, giving to the eyes a peculiar languid beauty which was almost feminine.", "눈썹은 곱게 휘어 있었으나, 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈꺼풀이 워낙 커서 눈에서 한참 위로 밀려 올라가 있었다. 그런데 손턴 씨의 얼굴에서는 곧은 눈썹이 맑고 깊이 박힌 진지한 두 눈 위로 낮게 드리워 있었다."], ["The brows were finely arched, but were by the very size of the dreamy lids, raised to a considerable distance from the eyes. Now, in Mr.", "그 눈은 불쾌하리만치 날카롭지는 않으면서도, 바라보는 것의 속과 알맹이까지 꿰뚫을 만큼 골똘해 보였다."], ["Thornton’s face the straight brows fell low over the clear, deep-set, earnest eyes, which without being unpleasantly sharp, seemed intent enough to penetrate into the very heart and core of what he was looking at.", "얼굴의 주름은 몇 줄 없었으나 대리석에 새긴 듯 단단했고, 주로 입가에 몰려 있었다. 그 입술은 살짝 다물려 있었고, 그 안의 이는 흠 하나 없이 아름다워서, 좀처럼 보기 힘든 환한 미소가 순식간에 떠올라 두 눈에서 빛나 나올 때면 마치 갑자기 햇살이 비치는 듯한 느낌을 주었다."], ["The lines in the face were few but firm, as if they were carved in marble, and lay principally about the lips, which were slightly compressed, over a set of teeth so faultless and beautiful as to give the effect of sudden sunlight when the rare bright smile, coming in an instant and shining out of the eyes, changed the whole look from the severe and resolved expression of a man ready to do and dare everything, to the keen, honest, enjoyment of the moment, which is seldom shown so fearlessly and instantaneously except by children.", "그 미소는 무엇이든 하고 무엇이든 감행할 각오가 된 사내의 엄하고 단호한 표정을, 아이들 말고는 좀처럼 그토록 거리낌 없이 대뜸 드러내는 법이 없는, 예리하고 정직한 그 순간의 즐거움으로 온통 바꾸어 놓았다. 마거릿은 그 미소가 마음에 들었다."], ["Margaret liked this smile; it was the first thing she had admired in this new friend of her father’s; and the opposition of character, shown in all these details of appearance she had just been noticing, seemed to explain the attraction they evidently felt towards each other.", "아버지의 이 새 벗에게서 그녀가 처음으로 감탄한 것이 그것이었다. 그리고 방금 눈여겨본 겉모습의 온갖 세부에 드러난 그 성품의 맞섬이, 두 사람이 서로에게 느끼는 것이 분명한 그 끌림을 설명해 주는 듯했다."]], [["She rearranged her mother’s worsted-work, and fell back into her own thoughts—as completely forgotten by Mr.", "그녀는 어머니의 털실 수예를 다시 매만지고는 자기 생각 속으로 돌아갔다."], ["Thornton as if she had not been in the room, so thoroughly was he occupied in explaining to Mr.", "손턴 씨에게는 그녀가 이 방에 아예 없는 것이나 다름없이 잊혀 있었다."], ["Hale the magnificent power, yet delicate adjustment of the might of the steam-hammer, which was recalling to Mr.", "그만큼 그는 증기 해머의 그 어마어마한 힘과 섬세한 조절을 헤일 씨에게 설명하는 데 골몰해 있었다."], ["Hale some of the wonderful stories of subservient genii in the Arabian Nights—one moment stretching from earth to sky and filling all the width of the horizon, at the next obediently compressed into a vase small enough to be borne in the hand of a child.", "그 이야기는 헤일 씨에게 아라비안나이트에 나오는 순종하는 정령들의 그 놀라운 이야기를 떠올리게 했다. 한순간에는 땅에서 하늘까지 뻗어 지평선 너비를 온통 채우다가, 다음 순간에는 고분고분 오그라들어 아이의 손에 들릴 만큼 작은 항아리 속으로 들어가는 그 정령들 말이다."]], [["“And this imagination of power, this practical realisation of a gigantic thought, came out of one man’s brain in our good town.", "“힘에 대한 이 상상, 거대한 생각의 이 실제적 구현이 우리 좋은 도시의 한 사람 머리에서 나왔습니다."], ["That very man has it within him to mount, step by step, on each wonder he achieves to higher marvels still.", "바로 그 사람 안에는 자기가 이루어 낸 경이 하나하나를 딛고 한 걸음씩 더 높은 경이로 올라설 힘이 있습니다."], ["And I’ll be bound to say, we have many among us who, if he were gone, could spring into the breach and carry on the war which compels, and shall compel, all material power to yield to science.”", "게다가 감히 말씀드리건대, 설령 그 사람이 사라진다 해도 그 자리를 메우고 나서서 모든 물질의 힘을 과학에 굴복시키는, 또 앞으로도 굴복시킬 그 싸움을 이어 갈 사람이 우리 가운데 숱하게 있습니다.”"]], [["“Your boast reminds me of the old lines—", "“그 자랑을 들으니 옛 시구가 떠오르는군요.”"]], [["‘I’ve a hundred captains in England,’ he said, ‘As good as ever was he.’”", "“‘내게는 잉글랜드에 100명의 대장이 있소.’ 그가 말했다. ‘하나같이 그 사람에 못지않은 이들이오.’”"]], [["At her father’s quotation Margaret looked suddenly up, with inquiring wonder in her eyes.", "아버지가 시를 읊자 마거릿이 문득 눈을 들었다. 두 눈에 묻고 싶어 하는 놀라움이 어려 있었다."], ["How in the world had they got from the cog-wheels to Chevy Chace?", "대체 어떻게 톱니바퀴에서 체비체이스까지 흘러왔단 말인가?"]], [["“It is no boast of mine,”", "“제 자랑이 아닙니다.”"], ["replied Mr.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["Thornton; “it is plain matter-of-fact.", "“그저 있는 그대로의 사실이지요."], ["I won’t deny that I am proud of belonging to a town—or perhaps I should rather say a district—the necessities of which give birth to such grandeur of conception.", "저는 이 도시에, 아니 차라리 이 고장이라고 해야 하겠군요, 그런 장대한 구상을 낳게 하는 절박함을 지닌 이 고장에 속해 있다는 것이 자랑스럽다는 것을 부인하지 않겠습니다."], ["I would rather be a man toiling, suffering—nay, failing and successless—here, than lead a dull prosperous life in the old worn grooves of what you call more aristocratic society down in the South, with their slow days of careless ease.", "저는 남쪽에서 선생님이 더 귀족적이라 부르시는 그 사회의 낡아 빠진 홈을 따라, 태평하고 느긋한 나날을 보내며 따분하게 번영하는 삶을 사느니, 차라리 여기서 애쓰고 고생하는 사람이, 아니 실패하고 아무것도 이루지 못하는 사람이 되겠습니다."], ["One may be clogged with honey and unable to rise and fly.”", "꿀에 발이 붙들려 일어나 날지 못하는 수가 있으니까요.”"]], [["“You are mistaken,”", "“잘못 아셨어요.”"], ["said Margaret, roused by the aspersion on her beloved South to a fond vehemence of defence, that brought the colour into her cheeks and the angry tears into her eyes.", "마거릿이 말했다. 사랑하는 남부를 헐뜯는 말에 발끈해 그것을 감싸려는 애틋한 격렬함이 일었고, 그 바람에 두 뺨에는 핏기가, 두 눈에는 성난 눈물이 어렸다."], ["“You do not know anything about the South.", "“손턴 씨는 남부에 대해 아무것도 모르세요."], ["If there is less adventure or less progress—I suppose I must not say less excitement—from the gambling spirit of trade, which seems requisite to force out these wonderful inventions, there is less suffering also.", "그 놀라운 발명을 억지로 끌어내는 데 꼭 필요하다는 장사의 도박 같은 기질에서 나오는 모험이나 진보가 그곳에 덜하다면, 흥분이 덜하다고는 말하면 안 되겠지요, 그렇다면 고통 또한 덜한 거예요."], ["I see men here going about in the streets who look ground down by some pinching sorrow or care—who are not only sufferers but haters.", "저는 이곳 거리에서 무슨 옥죄는 슬픔이나 근심에 짓눌린 듯한 얼굴로 다니는 사람들을 봅니다. 그저 고통받는 사람이 아니라 미워하는 사람들이지요."], ["Now, in the South we have our poor, but there is not that terrible expression in their countenances of a sullen sense of injustice which I see here.", "남부에도 가난한 이들은 있어요. 하지만 제가 여기서 보는 그런 무언의 부당함에 대한 뿌루퉁한 감정이 얼굴에 그토록 무섭게 드러나 있지는 않아요."], ["You do not know the South, Mr.", "손턴 씨는 남부를 모르세요.”"], ["Thornton,” she concluded, collapsing into a determined silence, and angry with herself for having said so much.", "그녀는 이렇게 말을 맺고는 단단히 입을 다물어 버렸고, 그토록 많은 말을 한 자기 자신에게 화가 났다."]], [["“And may I say you do not know the North?”", "“그러면 저도 헤일 양은 북부를 모르신다고 말씀드려도 되겠습니까?”"], ["asked he, with an inexpressible gentleness in his tone, as he saw that he had really hurt her.", "자기가 그녀에게 정말로 상처를 주었다는 것을 알아차린 그가 말할 수 없이 부드러운 목소리로 물었다."], ["She continued resolutely silent; yearning after the lovely haunts she had left far away in Hampshire, with a passionate longing that made her feel her voice would be unsteady and trembling if she spoke.", "그녀는 굳게 입을 다문 채였다. 저 멀리 햄프셔에 두고 온 그 사랑스러운 곳들이 사무치게 그리워, 입을 열면 목소리가 흔들리고 떨릴 것만 같았던 것이다."]], [["“At any rate, Mr.", "“어쨌든 손턴 씨,”"], ["Thornton,” said Mrs.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."], ["Hale, “you will allow that Milton is a much more smoky, dirty town than you will ever meet with in the South.”", "“밀턴이 남부에서 만나 볼 어떤 도시보다도 훨씬 연기 많고 더러운 도시라는 것만은 인정하시겠지요.”"]], [["“I’m afraid I must give up its cleanliness,”", "“깨끗함만은 포기해야 할 듯싶습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, with the quick gleaming smile.", "손턴 씨가 그 빠르게 번쩍이는 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“But we are bidden by parliament to burn our own smoke; so I suppose, like good little children, we shall do as we are bid—some time.”", "“하지만 우리는 우리가 낸 연기를 우리가 태우라는 의회의 분부를 받고 있습니다. 그러니 착한 아이들처럼 시키는 대로 하겠지요, 언젠가는 말입니다.”"]], [["“But I think you told me you had altered your chimneys so as to consume the smoke, did you not?”", "“그런데 굴뚝을 고쳐 연기를 태우게 하셨다고 말씀하지 않으셨던가요?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Mine were altered by my own will, before parliament meddled with the affair.", "“제 굴뚝은 의회가 이 일에 참견하기 전에 제 뜻으로 고쳤습니다."], ["It was an immediate outlay, but it repays me in the saving of coal.", "당장은 돈이 나갔지만 석탄이 절약되니 되돌아옵니다."], ["I’m not sure whether I should have done it, if I had waited until the act was passed.", "법이 통과될 때까지 기다렸다면 과연 제가 그렇게 했을지 모르겠습니다."], ["At any rate, I should have waited to be informed against and fined, and given all the trouble in yielding that I legally could.", "어쨌든 저는 고발당해 벌금을 물 때까지 기다렸을 테고, 법이 허락하는 한도까지 온갖 애를 먹인 뒤에야 굽혔을 겁니다."], ["But all laws which depend for their enforcement upon informers and fines, become inert from the odiousness of the machinery.", "하지만 고발과 벌금에 기대어 시행되는 법은 그 방식이 하도 밉살스러워서 죄다 흐지부지되고 맙니다."], ["I doubt if there has been a chimney in Milton informed against for five years past, although some are constantly sending out one-third of their coal in what is called here unparliamentary smoke.”", "지난 5년 동안 밀턴에서 고발당한 굴뚝이 하나라도 있었을까 싶습니다. 여기서 이른바 의회를 어긴 연기라 부르는 그 꼴로 석탄의 3분의 1을 끊임없이 내뿜는 굴뚝이 더러 있는데도 말이지요.”"]], [["“I only know it is impossible to keep the muslin blinds clean here above a week together; and at Helstone we have had them up for a month or more, and they have not looked dirty at the end of that time.", "“제가 아는 것이라곤 여기서는 모슬린 커튼을 일주일도 깨끗하게 두지 못한다는 것뿐이에요. 헬스톤에서는 한 달이 넘도록 걸어 두어도 그때까지 더러워 보이지 않았거든요."], ["And as for hands—Margaret, how many times did you say you had washed your hands this morning before twelve o’clock?", "손으로 말하자면, 마거릿, 오늘 아침 12시도 되기 전에 손을 몇 번이나 씻었다고 했지?"], ["Three times, was it not?”", "3번이었던가?”"]], [["“Yes, mamma.”", "“네, 어머니.”"]], [["“You seem to have a strong objection to acts of parliament and all legislation affecting your mode of management down here at Milton,”", "“여기 밀턴에서 사업을 꾸리는 방식에 영향을 주는 의회법이며 온갖 입법에 아주 강하게 반대하시는 모양입니다그려.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I have; and many others have as well.", "“그렇습니다. 저 말고도 그런 사람이 많고요."], ["And with justice, I think.", "게다가 그럴 만하다고 생각합니다."], ["The whole machinery—I don’t mean the wood and iron machinery now—of the cotton trade is so new that it is no wonder if it does not work well in every part all at once.", "면업의 그 모든 기계는, 지금은 나무와 쇠로 된 기계를 말하는 것이 아닙니다만, 워낙 새것이어서 모든 부분이 한꺼번에 잘 돌아가지 않는다 해도 이상할 것이 없습니다. 70년 전에는 그것이 무엇이었습니까?"], ["Seventy years ago what was it?", "그런데 지금은 무엇이 아닙니까?"], ["And now what it is not?", "다듬어지지 않은 거친 재료들이 한데 모였습니다."], ["Raw, crude materials came together; men of the same level, as regarded education and station, took suddenly the different position of masters and men, owing to the motherwit, as regarded opportunities and probabilities, which distinguished some, and made them far-seeing as to what great future lay concealed in that rude model of Sir Richard Arkwright’s.", "교육과 신분으로 보아 같은 자리에 있던 사람들이 갑자기 공장주와 직공이라는 서로 다른 자리를 차지하게 되었지요. 기회와 가망을 알아보는 타고난 눈치가 몇몇을 남달리 만들었고, 리처드 아크라이트 경의 그 투박한 모형 안에 얼마나 큰 앞날이 숨어 있는지 멀리 내다보게 해 준 덕분입니다."], ["The rapid development of what might be called a new trade, gave those early masters enormous power of wealth and command.", "새 업종이라 할 만한 것이 빠르게 자라나면서 그 초창기 공장주들에게 어마어마한 부와 권세를 안겨 주었습니다."], ["I don’t mean merely over the workmen; I mean over purchasers—over the whole world’s market.", "직공들에 대해서만 하는 말이 아닙니다. 사는 이들에 대해, 온 세계의 시장에 대해 하는 말입니다."], ["Why, I may give you, as an instance, an advertisement, inserted not fifty years ago in a Milton paper, that so-and-so (one of the half-dozen calico-printers of the time) would close his warehouse at noon each day; therefore, that all purchasers must come before that hour.", "이를테면 50년도 채 되지 않은 옛날에 밀턴의 어느 신문에 실린 광고를 하나 말씀드리지요. 아무개가, 당시 예닐곱 되던 옥양목 날염업자 가운데 하나입니다만, 날마다 정오에 창고 문을 닫겠으니 사려는 사람은 모두 그 전에 오라는 내용이었습니다."], ["Fancy a man dictating in this manner the time when he would sell and when he would not sell.", "언제 팔고 언제 팔지 않을지를 그런 식으로 통고하는 사람을 한번 생각해 보십시오."], ["Now, I believe, if a good customer chose to come at midnight, I should get up, and stand hat in hand to receive his orders.”", "지금은 좋은 손님이 한밤중에 오겠다고 하면 제가 자리에서 일어나 모자를 손에 들고 서서 주문을 받으리라 봅니다.”"]], [["Margaret’s lip curled, but somehow she was compelled to listen; she could no longer abstract herself in her own thoughts.", "마거릿은 입술을 삐죽 올렸으나, 어쩐지 듣지 않을 수 없었다. 더는 제 생각 속으로 물러나 있을 수가 없었다."]], [["“I only name such things to show what almost unlimited power the manufacturers had about the beginning of this century.", "“이런 이야기를 꺼내는 것은 이 세기가 시작될 무렵 공장주들이 거의 무한에 가까운 힘을 가지고 있었다는 것을 보여 드리기 위해서일 뿐입니다."], ["The men were rendered dizzy by it.", "그 사람들은 그 힘에 어지러워졌지요."], ["Because a man was successful in his ventures, there was no reason that in all other things his mind should be well-balanced.", "어떤 사람이 자기 사업에서 성공했다고 해서 다른 모든 일에서도 마음이 고르게 잡혀 있으리라는 법은 없습니다."], ["On the contrary, his sense of justice, and his simplicity, were often utterly smothered under the glut of wealth that came down upon him; and they tell strange tales of the wild extravagance of living indulged in on gala-days by those early cotton-lords.", "오히려 그 정의감과 소박함은 쏟아져 내리는 부의 홍수에 아주 짓눌려 버리기 일쑤였습니다. 그 초창기 면화왕들이 잔칫날이면 얼마나 거칠게 흥청거리며 살았는지 이상한 이야기가 전해 옵니다."], ["There can be no doubt, too, of the tyranny they exercised over their work-people.", "그들이 직공들에게 휘두른 횡포 또한 의심할 여지가 없고요."], ["You know the proverb, Mr.", "헤일 선생님도 그 속담을 아시겠지요."], ["Hale, ‘Set a beggar on horseback, and he’ll ride to the devil,’—well, some of these early manufacturers did ride to the devil in a magnificent style—crushing human bone and flesh under their horses’ hoofs without remorse.", "‘거지를 말에 태워 놓으면 지옥까지 달려간다.’ 그렇습니다, 그 초창기 공장주들 가운데 더러는 정말이지 거창한 폼으로 지옥까지 달려갔습니다. 사람의 뼈와 살을 말발굽 아래 짓밟으면서도 뉘우칠 줄 몰랐지요."], ["But by-and-by came a reaction; there were more factories, more masters; more men were wanted.", "그러나 머잖아 되돌림이 왔습니다. 공장이 늘고 공장주가 늘자 사람이 더 필요해졌던 겁니다."], ["The power of masters and men became more evenly balanced; and now the battle is pretty fairly waged between us.", "공장주와 직공의 힘은 한결 고르게 맞서게 되었고, 이제 그 싸움은 우리 사이에서 제법 공평하게 벌어지고 있습니다."], ["We will hardly submit to the decision of an umpire, much less to the interference of a meddler with only a smattering of the knowledge of the real facts of the case, even though that meddler be called the High Court of Parliament.”", "우리는 심판의 판정에도 좀처럼 따르지 않을 텐데, 하물며 일의 실상을 어설피 주워들었을 뿐인 참견꾼의 간섭이야 말할 것도 없지요. 설령 그 참견꾼을 의회라는 고등법정이라 부른다 해도 말입니다.”"]], [["“Is there necessity for calling it a battle between the two classes?”", "“그것을 굳이 두 계급 사이의 싸움이라 불러야 할 까닭이 있소?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."], ["“I know, from your using the term, it is one which gives a true idea of the real state of things to your mind.”", "“그 말을 쓰시는 것으로 보아, 그것이 자네 마음에는 일의 참모습을 제대로 보여 주는 말이라는 것은 알겠소만.”"]], [["“It is true; and I believe it to be as much a necessity as that prudent wisdom and good conduct are always opposed to, and doing battle with ignorance and improvidence.", "“그렇습니다. 그리고 저는 그것이 신중한 지혜와 바른 처신이 언제나 무지와 무분별에 맞서 싸우기 마련인 것과 꼭 같은 필연이라고 믿습니다."], ["It is one of the great beauties of our system, that a working-man may raise himself into the power and position of a master by his own exertions and behaviour; that, in fact, every one who rules himself to decency and sobriety of conduct, and attention to his duties, comes over to our ranks; it may not be always as a master, but as an overlooker, a cashier, a book-keeper, a clerk, one on the side of authority and order.”", "노동자도 제 노력과 처신으로 공장주의 힘과 자리에 오를 수 있다는 것, 사실 제 몸을 단정하고 절제 있게 다스리며 제 할 일에 마음을 쏟는 사람은 누구나 우리 쪽으로 넘어온다는 것, 그것이 우리 체제의 큰 아름다움 가운데 하나입니다. 언제나 공장주로 오는 것은 아니겠지만, 감독으로든 출납으로든 부기로든 사무원으로든, 권위와 질서 편에 선 사람으로 오는 것이지요.”"]], [["“You consider all who are unsuccessful in raising themselves in the world, from whatever cause, as your enemies, then, if I understand you rightly,”", "“그러면 제가 제대로 알아들었다면, 무슨 까닭에서든 세상에서 스스로를 일으켜 세우지 못한 사람은 모두 손턴 씨의 적이라고 여기신다는 말씀이군요.”"], ["said Margaret, in a clear, cold voice.", "마거릿이 맑고 차가운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“As their own enemies, certainly,”", "“제 적이 아니라 자기 자신의 적이지요, 분명히.”"], ["said he, quickly, not a little piqued by the haughty disapproval her form of expression and tone of speaking implied.", "그가 얼른 대답했다. 그녀의 말투와 표현에 배어 있는 도도한 못마땅함에 적잖이 부아가 났던 것이다."], ["But, in a moment, his straightforward honesty made him feel that his words were but a poor and quibbling answer to what she had said, and, be she as scornful as she liked, it was a duty he owed to himself to explain, as truly as he could, what he did mean.", "그러나 이내 그의 곧은 정직함이 그로 하여금 그 말이 그녀의 말에 대한 옹색하고 말장난 같은 대답일 뿐임을 깨닫게 했다. 그녀가 아무리 업신여기든, 자기가 정말로 뜻한 바를 될 수 있는 대로 참되게 밝히는 것은 스스로에게 진 빚이었다."], ["Yet it was very difficult to separate her interpretation, and keep it distinct from his meaning.", "그러나 그녀의 풀이를 떼어 내어 자기 뜻과 갈라 두기란 몹시 어려웠다."], ["He could best have illustrated what he wanted to say by telling them something of his own life; but was it not too personal a subject to speak about to strangers?", "하고 싶은 말을 가장 잘 보여 주는 길은 자기 삶의 이야기를 조금 들려주는 것이었다. 그러나 그것은 낯선 이들에게 말하기에는 너무 사사로운 이야기가 아닐까?"], ["Still, it was the simple straightforward way of explaining his meaning; so, putting aside the touch of shyness that brought a momentary flush of colour into his dark cheek, he said:", "그래도 그것이 제 뜻을 밝히는 단순하고 곧은 길이었다. 그리하여 그는 검은 뺨에 잠시 붉은빛을 돌게 한 그 수줍음의 기색을 밀쳐 두고 말했다."]], [["“I am not speaking without book.", "“근거 없이 하는 말이 아닙니다."], ["Sixteen years ago, my father died under very miserable circumstances.", "16년 전에 제 아버지가 아주 비참한 사정 속에서 세상을 떠났습니다."], ["I was taken from school, and had to become a man (as well as I could) in a few days.", "저는 학교에서 끌려 나와 며칠 만에 어른이 되어야 했지요, 될 수 있는 데까지는 말입니다."], ["I had such a mother as few are blest with; a woman of strong power, and firm resolve.", "저에게는 좀처럼 받기 힘든 복이라 할 어머니가 있었습니다. 굳센 힘과 단단한 결심을 지닌 여자였지요."], ["We went into a small country town, where living was cheaper than in Milton, and where I got employment in a draper’s shop (a capital place, by the way, for obtaining a knowledge of goods).", "우리는 밀턴보다 살림살이가 싼 작은 시골 읍내로 갔고, 저는 거기서 포목점에 일자리를 얻었습니다. 물건에 대한 지식을 얻기에는 더없이 좋은 자리였지요."], ["Week by week, our income came to fifteen shillings, out of which three people had to be kept.", "우리 수입은 주에 15실링이었고, 그것으로 3명이 먹고살아야 했습니다."], ["My mother managed so that I put by three out of these fifteen shillings regularly.", "어머니는 살림을 꾸려 제가 그 15실링에서 3실링을 꼬박꼬박 떼어 두게 하셨습니다."], ["This made the beginning; this taught me self-denial.", "그것이 시작이었고, 그것이 제게 자기 절제를 가르쳤습니다."], ["Now that I am able to afford my mother such comforts as her age, rather than her own wish, requires, I thank her silently on each occasion for the early training she gave me.", "이제 어머니께 그 연세에, 어머니 자신의 바람이라기보다는 연세에 필요한 만큼의 편안함을 마련해 드릴 수 있게 되고 보니, 저는 그때마다 어릴 적에 그런 가르침을 주신 어머니께 말없이 감사드립니다."], ["Now when I feel that in my own case it is no good luck, nor merit, nor talent,—but simply the habits of life which taught me to despise indulgences not thoroughly earned,—indeed, never to think twice about them,—I believe that this suffering, which Miss Hale says is impressed on the countenances of the people of Milton, is but the natural punishment of dishonestly-enjoyed pleasure, at some former period of their lives.", "이제 제 경우를 두고 보면 그것이 운도 아니고 공로도 재주도 아니며, 그저 제대로 벌지 않은 호사를 업신여기도록, 아니 아예 두 번 생각조차 하지 않도록 가르친 삶의 습관일 뿐이라고 느낍니다. 그래서 저는 헤일 양이 밀턴 사람들 얼굴에 새겨져 있다고 하신 그 고통이, 지난날 어느 때인가 정직하지 못하게 누린 쾌락에 대한 마땅한 벌일 뿐이라고 믿습니다."], ["I do not look on self-indulgent, sensual people as worthy of my hatred; I simply look upon them with contempt for their poorness of character.”", "저는 제 몸을 함부로 놀리고 감각에 젖어 사는 사람들을 미워할 만한 가치가 있다고 보지 않습니다. 그저 그 사람됨의 옹색함을 딱하게 여기며 업신여길 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“But you have the rudiments of a good education,”", "“하지만 자네는 좋은 교육의 밑바탕을 지니고 있네.”"], ["remarked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“The quick zest with which you are now reading Homer, shows me that you do not come to it as an unknown book: you have read it before, and are only recalling your old knowledge.”", "“지금 호메로스를 읽는 그 빠르고 신나는 맛을 보면, 자네가 그것을 모르는 책으로 대하고 있지 않다는 것을 알 수 있지. 전에 읽어 본 적이 있고, 그저 옛 지식을 되살리고 있을 뿐이야.”"]], [["“That is true,—I had blundered along it at school; I dare say, I was even considered a pretty fair classic in those days, though my Latin and Greek have slipt away from me since.", "“그건 그렇습니다. 학교에서 더듬더듬 읽어 나갔지요."], ["But I ask you, what preparation were they for such a life as I had to lead?", "그 시절에는 제법 괜찮은 고전 학생으로 여겨졌던 것 같기도 합니다. 그 뒤로 라틴어도 그리스어도 다 빠져나가 버렸지만요."], ["None at all.", "그러나 여쭙겠습니다."], ["Utterly none at all.", "제가 살아야 했던 그런 삶에 그것들이 무슨 준비가 되어 주었겠습니까? 아무것도 아니었습니다."], ["On the point of education, any man who can read and write starts fair with me in the amount of really useful knowledge that I had at that time.”", "정말이지 아무것도 아니었지요. 교육이라는 점에서 보면, 그때 제가 지녔던 참으로 쓸모 있는 지식의 양으로는 읽고 쓸 줄 아는 사람이면 누구나 저와 같은 자리에서 출발하는 셈입니다.”"]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄!"], ["I don’t agree with you.", "나는 동의하지 않네."], ["But there I am perhaps somewhat of a pedant.", "하지만 그 점에서는 내가 다소 책상물림인지도 모르지."], ["Did not the recollection of the heroic simplicity of the Homeric life nerve you up?”", "호메로스 시절 삶의 그 영웅적인 소박함을 떠올리며 힘을 얻지는 않았나?”"]], [["“Not one bit!”", "“조금도 아닙니다!”"], ["exclaimed Mr. Thornton, laughing.", "손턴 씨가 웃으며 소리쳤다."], ["“I was too busy to think about any dead people, with the living pressing alongside of me, neck to neck, in the struggle for bread.", "“저는 너무 바빠서 죽은 사람들 생각을 할 겨를이 없었습니다. 산 사람들이 빵을 두고 다투며 제 옆에 바짝 붙어 밀려들고 있었으니까요."], ["Now that I have my mother safe in the quiet peace that becomes her age, and duly rewards her former exertions, I can turn to all that old narration and thoroughly enjoy it.”", "이제 어머니를 그 연세에 어울리고 지난날의 애씀에 마땅히 값하는 조용한 평안 속에 편히 모시고 나니, 저도 그 옛이야기로 눈을 돌려 실컷 즐길 수 있게 되었습니다.”"]], [["“I dare say, my remark came from the professional feeling of there being nothing like leather,”", "“내 말이 제 신 파는 사람 눈에는 가죽만 한 게 없다는 식의 직업적인 마음에서 나왔는지도 모르겠군.”"], ["replied Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 대답했다."]], [["When Mr. Thornton rose up to go away, after shaking hands with Mr.", "손턴 씨가 자리를 뜨려고 일어나 헤일 씨 부부와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 마거릿에게도 같은 식으로 작별 인사를 하려고 손을 내밀었다."], ["and Mrs. Hale, he made an advance to Margaret to wish her good-bye in a similar manner.", "그것은 이 고장의 스스럼없고 허물없는 풍습이었다."], ["It was the frank familiar custom of the place; but Margaret was not prepared for it.", "그러나 마거릿은 그럴 마음의 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["She simply bowed her farewell; although the instant she saw the hand, half put out, quickly drawn back, she was sorry she had not been aware of the intention.", "그녀는 그저 목례로 작별을 대신했다. 반쯤 내밀었다가 얼른 거두어들이는 그 손을 보는 순간, 그 뜻을 미리 알아차리지 못한 것이 못내 미안했지만 말이다."], ["Mr. Thornton, however, knew nothing of her sorrow, and, drawing himself up to his full height, walked off, muttering as he left the house—", "그러나 손턴 씨는 그 미안함을 조금도 알지 못한 채 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 걸어 나갔고, 그 집을 나서며 이렇게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“A more proud, disagreeable girl I never saw.", "“저보다 더 도도하고 밉살스러운 아가씨는 본 적이 없다."], ["Even her great beauty is blotted out of one’s memory by her scornful ways.”", "저 대단한 아름다움조차 저 업신여기는 태도 때문에 기억에서 지워져 버리는군.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# 제11장"]], [["“There’s iron, they say, in all our blood, And a grain or two perhaps is good; But his, he makes me harshly feel, Has got a little too much of steel.”", "“우리 모두의 피에는 쇠가 들어 있다고들 하지, 한두 알갱이쯤은 어쩌면 좋은 것이리라. 그러나 그의 피는, 나로 하여금 모질게 느끼게 만드는 그 피는, 강철이 조금 지나치게 많은 듯하구나.”"], ["ANON.", "— 작자 미상."]], [["“Margaret!”", "“마거릿!”"], ["said Mr. Hale as he returned from showing his guest downstairs; “I could not help watching your face with some anxiety, when Mr.", "손님을 아래층까지 배웅하고 돌아온 헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Thornton made his confession of having been a shop-boy. I knew it all along from Mr.", "“손턴 씨가 가게 점원 노릇을 했다고 털어놓을 때, 나는 적잖이 조마조마한 마음으로 네 얼굴을 지켜볼 수밖에 없더구나."], ["Bell; so I was aware of what was coming; but I half expected to see you get up and leave the room.”", "벨 씨한테서 진작 다 들어 알고 있었으니 무슨 말이 나올지는 짐작하고 있었다만, 네가 자리에서 일어나 방을 나가 버리지나 않을까 반쯤은 그렇게 여겼단다.”"]], [["“Oh, papa!", "“어머, 아버지! 저를 그토록 어리석은 아이로 여기셨다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요?"], ["you don’t mean that you thought me so silly?", "저는 정말이지 그분이 하신 말씀 가운데 자기 자신에 대한 그 이야기가 무엇보다 마음에 들었어요. 다른 것은 다 그 모진 데가 거슬렸지만, 자기 이야기만은 어찌나 담백하게 하시던지요."], ["I really liked that account of himself better than anything else he said.", "가게 사람들을 천박하게 만드는 그 허세라고는 거의 없이, 어머니에 대한 그토록 애틋한 존경을 담아서 말이에요. 그러니 제가 그때 방을 나갈 일은, 그분이 세상에 밀턴 같은 곳은 또 없다는 듯 밀턴을 자랑하실 때나, 사람들이 앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모르고 헤프다며 조용히 업신여기실 때보다 오히려 적었지요."], ["Everything else revolted me, from its hardness; but he spoke about himself so simply—with so little of the pretence that makes the vulgarity of shop-people, and with such tender respect for his mother, that I was less likely to leave the room then than when he was boasting about Milton, as if there was not such another place in the world; or quietly professing to despise people for careless, wasteful improvidence, without ever seeming to think it his duty to try to make them different,—to give them anything of the training which his mother gave him, and to which he evidently owes his position, whatever that may be.", "그 사람들을 달라지게 하려고 애쓰는 것이 자기 할 일이라고는 조금도 여기지 않으시면서요. 어머니가 당신에게 주신 그 가르침을, 지금의 자리가 어떤 것이든 그 자리를 얻게 해 준 것이 분명한 그 가르침을 그 사람들에게도 조금은 나누어 줄 생각은 없이 말이에요."], ["No!", "아니에요!"], ["his statement of having been a shop-boy was the thing I liked best of all.”", "가게 점원이었다는 그 말씀이야말로 제가 무엇보다 마음에 들어 한 것이었어요.”"]], [["“I am surprised at you, Margaret,”", "“네가 놀랍구나, 마거릿.”"], ["said her mother.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["“You who were always accusing people of being shoppy at Helstone!", "“헬스톤에서는 늘 사람들더러 장사치 티가 난다고 나무라던 네가 말이다."], ["I don’t think, Mr. Hale, you have done quite right in introducing such a person to us without telling us what he had been.", "헤일 씨, 나는 당신이 그런 사람을 우리한테 소개하면서 그가 어떤 사람이었는지 말해 주지 않은 건 온당하지 않았다고 봐요."], ["I really was very much afraid of showing him how much shocked I was at some parts of what he said.", "그 사람 말 가운데 몇 대목에는 정말이지 얼마나 놀랐는지 내색하지 않으려고 무척 애를 썼답니다."], ["His father ‘dying in miserable circumstances.’ Why it might have been in the workhouse.”", "아버지가 ‘비참한 처지에서 돌아가셨다’니. 아니, 구빈원에서였을지도 모르잖아요.”"]], [["“I am not sure it was not worse than being in the workhouse,”", "“구빈원에 있는 것보다 더 나빴을지도 모르오.”"], ["replied her husband.", "남편이 대답했다."], ["“I heard a good deal of his previous life from Mr.", "“여기 오기 전에 벨 씨한테서 그 사람의 지난 삶을 꽤 많이 들었다오."], ["Bell before we came here; and as he has told you a part, I will fill up what he left out.", "그가 일부를 이야기했으니 빠뜨린 데는 내가 채워 주리다."], ["His father speculated wildly, failed, and then killed himself, because he could not bear the disgrace.", "그의 아버지는 무모하게 투기를 하다가 망했고, 그 치욕을 견디지 못해 스스로 목숨을 끊었소."], ["All his former friends shrunk from the disclosures that had to be made of his dishonest gambling—wild, hopeless struggles, made with other people’s money, to regain his own moderate portion of wealth.", "예전 친구들은 하나같이 그의 부정한 노름이 드러날 판이 되자 몸을 사렸다오. 제 몫의 그리 대단찮은 재산을 되찾겠다고 남의 돈으로 벌인 무모하고 가망 없는 발버둥이었지."], ["No one came forward to help the mother and this boy.", "어머니와 이 아이를 도우러 나선 사람은 아무도 없었소."], ["There was another child, I believe, a girl; too young to earn money, but of course she had to be kept.", "아이가 하나 더 있었던 모양이오. 딸아이인데, 돈을 벌기에는 너무 어렸지만 그래도 먹여 살려야 했지."], ["At least, no friend came forward immediately, and Mrs. Thornton is not one, I fancy, to wait till tardy kindness comes to find her out.", "적어도 곧바로 나서 준 벗은 없었고, 손턴 부인은 뒤늦은 친절이 저를 찾아올 때까지 기다릴 사람은 아닌 듯싶소."], ["So they left Milton.", "그래서 그들은 밀턴을 떠났다오."], ["I knew he had gone into a shop, and that his earnings, with some fragment of property secured to his mother, had been made to keep them for a long time. Mr.", "나는 그가 가게에 들어갔고, 그 벌이에 어머니 앞으로 남겨진 얼마 안 되는 재산을 보태 오랫동안 식구들을 건사했다는 것까지는 알고 있었소."], ["Bell said they absolutely lived upon water-porridge for years—how, he did not know; but long after the creditors had given up hope of any payment of old Mr.", "벨 씨 말로는 그들이 몇 해 동안 오로지 멀건 죽만 먹고 살았다는데, 어떻게 그랬는지는 그도 모른다더군."], ["Thornton’s debts (if, indeed, they ever had hoped at all about it, after his suicide), this young man returned to Milton, and went quietly round to each creditor, paying him the first instalment of the money owing to him.", "그런데 채권자들이 늙은 손턴 씨의 빚을 받을 가망을 접은 지 한참 뒤에—하기야 그가 스스로 목숨을 끊은 마당에 애초에 무슨 가망을 걸기나 했겠소만—이 젊은이가 밀턴으로 돌아와 채권자를 하나하나 조용히 찾아다니며 갚아야 할 돈의 첫 몫을 치렀다오."], ["No noise—no gathering together of creditors—it was done very silently and quietly, but all was paid at last; helped on materially by the circumstance of one of the creditors, a crabbed old fellow (Mr.", "떠들썩할 것도, 채권자들을 한자리에 모을 것도 없이 아주 조용하고 잠잠하게 해냈고, 끝내 다 갚았소."], ["Bell says), taking in Mr. Thornton as a kind of partner.”", "채권자 가운데 하나가—벨 씨 말로는 성마른 늙은이라는데—손턴 씨를 일종의 동업자로 받아들여 준 덕을 크게 보기도 했고 말이오.”"]], [["“That really is fine,”", "“정말 훌륭하네요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“What a pity such a nature should be tainted by his position as a Milton manufacturer.”", "“그런 천성이 밀턴 공장주라는 자리 때문에 물들어 버렸다니 안타까워요.”"]], [["“How tainted?”", "“어떻게 물들었다는 게냐?”"], ["asked her father.", "아버지가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, papa, by that testing everything by the standard of wealth.", "“아, 아버지, 모든 것을 부라는 잣대로 재는 그 태도 말이에요."], ["When he spoke of the mechanical powers, he evidently looked upon them only as new ways of extending trade and making money.", "기계의 힘에 대해 말씀하실 때도 그분은 그것을 오로지 장사를 넓히고 돈을 버는 새로운 방법으로만 보시는 게 분명했어요."], ["And the poor men around him—they were poor because they were vicious—out of the pale of his sympathies because they had not his iron nature, and the capabilities that it gives him for being rich.”", "그리고 그분 둘레의 가난한 사람들은요, 그 사람들은 못됐기 때문에 가난한 것이고, 그분 같은 쇠 같은 천성도 그것이 그분에게 주는 부자가 될 능력도 없으니 그분의 동정 밖에 있는 것이지요.”"]], [["“Not vicious; he never said that.", "“못됐다고는 하지 않았다. 그런 말은 한 적이 없어."], ["Improvident and self-indulgent were his words.”", "앞날을 헤아릴 줄 모르고 제 욕심을 못 이긴다고 했지.”"]], [["Margaret was collecting her mother’s working materials, and preparing to go to bed.", "마거릿은 어머니의 바느질감을 챙기며 잠자리에 들 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["Just as she was leaving the room, she hesitated—she was inclined to make an acknowledgment which she thought would please her father, but which to be full and true must include a little annoyance.", "방을 막 나서려다 그녀는 머뭇거렸다. 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드릴 만하다고 여겨지는 고백을 하고 싶었으나, 그것을 온전히 정직하게 하려면 약간의 언짢음까지 함께 담아야 했다."], ["However, out it came.", "그래도 결국 말이 튀어나왔다."]], [["“Papa, I do think Mr.", "“아버지, 저도 손턴 씨가 아주 대단한 분이라고는 생각해요."], ["Thornton a very remarkable man; but personally I don’t like him at all.”", "하지만 사람으로서는 조금도 좋아지지가 않아요.”"]], [["“And I do!”", "“나는 좋더구나!”"], ["said her father laughing.", "아버지가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Personally, as you call it, and all.", "“네 말대로 사람으로서도, 무엇으로서도 말이다."], ["I don’t set him up for a hero, or anything of that kind.", "그를 영웅이니 뭐니 하는 자리에 올려놓는 건 아니다만."], ["But good-night, child.", "그건 그렇고 잘 자거라, 얘야."], ["Your mother looks sadly tired to-night, Margaret.”", "오늘 밤 네 어머니가 몹시 지쳐 보이는구나, 마거릿.”"]], [["Margaret had noticed her mother’s jaded appearance with anxiety for some time past, and this remark of her father’s sent her up to bed with a dim fear lying like a weight on her heart.", "마거릿은 얼마 전부터 어머니의 지친 낯빛을 근심스럽게 지켜보고 있던 터라, 아버지의 이 말에 어렴풋한 두려움이 무게처럼 가슴에 얹힌 채 잠자리로 올라갔다. 밀턴의 삶은 헤일 부인이 헬스톤에서 익숙하게 살아온 삶과 너무나 달랐다."], ["The life in Milton was so different from what Mrs.", "그곳에서는 언제든 신선하고 트인 바깥 공기를 드나들었다."], ["Hale had been accustomed to live in Helstone, in and out perpetually into the fresh and open air; the air itself was so different, deprived of all revivifying principle as it seemed to be here; the domestic worries pressed so very closely, and in so new and sordid a form, upon all the women in the family, that there was good reason to fear that her mother’s health might be becoming seriously affected.", "공기 자체가 이토록 달라서, 이곳의 공기에는 사람을 되살리는 기운이라고는 하나도 없는 듯했다. 살림살이의 시름은 이 집 여자들 모두에게 너무나 바짝, 그것도 이제껏 겪어 보지 못한 궁색한 모습으로 밀려들었다."], ["There were several other signs of something wrong about Mrs. Hale.", "그러니 어머니의 건강이 심각하게 상하고 있는 것은 아닐까 두려워할 만한 까닭이 넉넉했다."], ["She and Dixon held mysterious consultations in her bedroom, from which Dixon would come out crying and cross, as was her custom when any distress of her mistress called upon her sympathy.", "헤일 부인에게 무언가 잘못되었다는 조짐은 그 밖에도 여럿 있었다. 어머니와 딕슨은 침실에서 은밀한 의논을 나누었고, 그러고 나면 딕슨은 울면서 심통이 난 얼굴로 나오곤 했다."], ["Once Margaret had gone into the chamber soon after Dixon left it, and found her mother on her knees, and as Margaret stole out she caught a few words which were evidently a prayer for strength and patience to endure severe bodily suffering.", "안주인의 괴로움이 제 동정을 부를 때면 늘 그러던 버릇이었다. 한번은 딕슨이 나간 직후에 마거릿이 방으로 들어갔다가 어머니가 무릎을 꿇고 있는 것을 보았고, 살그머니 물러나면서 몇 마디를 얻어들었는데, 그것은 분명 모진 육신의 고통을 견딜 힘과 인내를 구하는 기도였다."], ["Margaret yearned to re-unite the bond of intimate confidence which had been broken by her long residence at her aunt Shaw’s, and strove by gentle caresses and softened words to creep into the warmest place in her mother’s heart.", "마거릿은 쇼 이모 댁에서 오래 지내는 동안 끊어져 버린 그 살가운 신뢰의 끈을 다시 잇고 싶어 애가 탔고, 부드러운 손길과 다정한 말로 어머니 마음의 가장 따뜻한 자리에 파고들려 애썼다."], ["But though she received caresses and fond words back again, in such profusion as would have gladdened her formerly, yet she felt that there was a secret withheld from her, and she believed it bore serious reference to her mother’s health.", "그러나 예전 같았으면 기뻤을 만큼 넘치도록 손길과 다정한 말이 되돌아왔는데도, 그녀는 자기에게 감춰진 비밀이 있음을 느꼈고, 그것이 어머니의 건강과 무겁게 얽혀 있으리라 믿었다. 이날 밤 그녀는 아주 오래도록 잠들지 못한 채, 밀턴의 삶이 어머니에게 끼치는 나쁜 영향을 어떻게 하면 줄일 수 있을까 궁리했다."], ["She lay awake very long this night, planning how to lessen the evil influence of their Milton life on her mother.", "딕슨을 늘 거들어 줄 하녀를 하나 구해야 했다. 온종일 그 일에만 매달리는 한이 있더라도."], ["A servant to give Dixon permanent assistance should be got, if she gave up her whole time to the search; and then, at any rate, her mother might have all the personal attention she required, and had been accustomed to her whole life.", "그러면 적어도 어머니는 평생 익숙했고 또 필요로 하는 시중을 다 받을 수 있을 터였다."]], [["Visiting register offices, seeing all manner of unlikely people, and very few in the least likely, absorbed Margaret’s time and thoughts for several days.", "직업소개소를 돌고, 도무지 어울릴 성싶지 않은 온갖 사람들을 만나고, 그중 그럴듯한 사람은 몇 되지 않는 일로 마거릿의 시간과 마음은 며칠 동안 온통 붙들려 있었다."], ["One afternoon she met Bessy Higgins in the street, and stopped to speak to her.", "어느 날 오후 그녀는 거리에서 베시 히긴스를 만나 걸음을 멈추고 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Well, Bessy, how are you?", "“베시, 어떻게 지내요?"], ["Better, I hope, now the wind has changed.”", "바람이 바뀌었으니 좀 나아졌기를 바라요.”"]], [["“Better and not better, if yo’ know what that means.”", "“나아지기도 하고 안 나아지기도 했구먼요. 그게 무슨 말인지 아신다면요.”"]], [["“Not exactly,”", "“잘 모르겠어요.”"], ["replied Margaret, smiling.", "마거릿이 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“I’m better in not being torn to pieces by coughing o’ nights, but I’m weary and tired o’ Milton, and longing to get away to the land o’ Beulah? and when I think I’m farther and farther off, my heart sinks, and I’m no better; I’m worse.”", "“밤마다 기침에 몸이 갈가리 찢기지는 않으니 나아진 거지만, 밀턴이 지긋지긋하고 넌더리가 나서 뷸라 땅으로 떠나고만 싶은데, 거기서 자꾸 멀어지고만 있구나 싶으면 가슴이 내려앉으니 나아진 게 아니라 되레 나빠진 거우.”"]], [["Margaret turned round to walk alongside of the girl in her feeble progress homeward.", "마거릿은 몸을 돌려, 힘없이 집으로 향하는 처녀와 나란히 걸었다."], ["But for a minute or two she did not speak.", "그러나 일이 분 동안 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["At last she said in a low voice,", "마침내 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Bessy, do you wish to die?”", "“베시, 죽고 싶어요?”"], ["For she shrank from death herself, with all the clinging to life so natural to the young and healthy.", "그녀 자신은 죽음 앞에서 움츠러들었기 때문이다. 젊고 건강한 사람에게 그토록 자연스러운, 삶에 대한 온갖 애착을 지닌 채로."]], [["Bessy was silent in her turn for a minute or two.", "이번에는 베시가 일이 분 동안 잠자코 있었다."], ["Then she replied,", "그러고는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“If yo’d led the life I have, and getten as weary of it as I have, and thought at times, ‘maybe it’ll last for fifty or sixty years—it does wi’ some,’—and got dizzy and dazed, and sick, as each of them sixty years seemed to spin about me, and mock me with its length of hours and minutes, and endless bits o’ time—oh, wench! I tell thee thou’d been glad enough when th’ doctor said he feared thou’d never see another winter.”", "“댁이 내가 산 것 같은 삶을 살았고, 나만큼 그 삶에 넌더리가 났고, 이따금 ‘어쩌면 이게 50년이나 60년은 갈지도 모르지, 더러는 그러니까’ 하는 생각이 들었다면, 그리고 그 60년이 하나하나 눈앞에서 빙빙 돌며 그 긴 시간과 분과 끝도 없는 자투리로 나를 놀려 대는 것 같아 어지럽고 아뜩하고 속이 메스꺼웠다면, 아이고 아가씨, 내 말하지만 의사가 자네는 다음 겨울을 못 볼 것 같다고 했을 때 댁도 반가웠을 거우.”"]], [["“Why, Bessy, what kind of a life has yours been?”", "“아니, 베시, 어떤 삶을 살아온 거예요?”"]], [["“Nought worse than many others’, I reckon.", "“남들 것보다 더 나쁠 것도 없지 싶수."], ["Only I fretted against it, and they didn’t.”", "다만 나는 그것에 안달을 했고 남들은 안 했다는 것뿐이우.”"]], [["“But what was it?", "“그런데 그게 어떤 것이었어요?"], ["You know, I’m a stranger here, so perhaps I’m not so quick at understanding what you mean as if I’d lived all my life at Milton.”", "알다시피 나는 여기 낯선 사람이라, 밀턴에서 평생을 살았다면 모를까, 말뜻을 얼른 알아듣지 못할지도 몰라요.”"]], [["“If yo’d ha’ come to our house when yo’ said yo’ would, I could maybe ha’ told you.", "“오시겠다고 하셨을 때 우리 집에 오셨더라면 아마 말해 드릴 수 있었을 거우."], ["But father says yo’re just like th’ rest on ’em; its out o’ sight out o’ mind wi’ you.”", "하지만 아버지는 댁도 다른 사람들과 매한가지라고 하십디다. 눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어지는 사람이라고요.”"]], [["“I don’t know who the rest are; and I’ve been very busy; and, to tell the truth, I had forgotten my promise—”", "“다른 사람들이 누군지는 모르겠어요. 그리고 나는 몹시 바빴고, 사실대로 말하자면 약속을 잊고 있었어요…”"]], [["“Yo’ offered it; we asked none of it.”", "“댁이 먼저 하신 말이지, 우리가 청한 게 아니우.”"]], [["“I had forgotten what I said for the time,”", "“한동안 내가 한 말을 잊고 있었어요.”"], ["continued Margaret quietly.", "마거릿이 조용히 말을 이었다."], ["“I should have thought of it again when I was less busy.", "“덜 바빠지면 다시 떠올렸을 거예요."], ["May I go with you now?”", "지금 같이 가도 될까요?”"]], [["Bessy gave a quick glance at Margaret’s face, to see if the wish expressed was really felt.", "베시는 그 말에 담긴 바람이 정말 진심인지 보려고 마거릿의 얼굴을 얼른 훔쳐보았다."], ["The sharpness in her eye turned to a wistful longing as she met Margaret’s soft and friendly gaze.", "마거릿의 부드럽고 다정한 눈길과 마주치자 그 눈에 서렸던 날카로움이 애틋한 그리움으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“I ha’ none so many to care for me; if yo’ care yo’ may come.”", "“나를 마음에 두어 주는 사람이 그리 많지도 않으니, 댁이 마음이 있다면 오시구려.”"]], [["So they walked on together in silence.", "그리하여 두 사람은 말없이 함께 걸었다."], ["As they turned up into a small court, out of a squalid street, Bessy said,", "지저분한 거리에서 벗어나 작은 골목 안뜰로 접어들 때 베시가 말했다."]], [["“Yo’ll not be daunted if father’s at home, and speaks a bit gruffish at first.", "“아버지가 집에 계셔서 처음에 좀 퉁명스럽게 굴더라도 겁먹지 마시우."], ["He took a mind to ye, yo’ see, and he thought a deal o’ your coming to see us; and just because he liked yo’ he was vexed and put about.”", "아시다시피 아버지가 댁을 마음에 들어 하셨고, 우리 집에 와 주시는 걸 대단히 여기셨거든요. 그러니 좋아했던 만큼이나 심사가 상하고 뒤틀리셨던 거우.”"]], [["“Don’t fear, Bessy.”", "“걱정 말아요, 베시.”"]], [["But Nicholas was not at home when they entered.", "그러나 그들이 들어섰을 때 니컬러스는 집에 없었다."], ["A great slatternly girl, not so old as Bessy, but taller and stronger, was busy at the wash-tub, knocking about the furniture in a rough capable way, but altogether making so much noise that Margaret shrunk, out of sympathy with poor Bessy, who had sat down on the first chair, as if completely tired out with her walk.", "베시보다는 어리지만 키도 크고 힘도 센, 덩치 크고 칠칠치 못한 처녀가 빨래통 앞에서 부산을 떨며 거칠지만 야무진 손놀림으로 가구를 이리저리 부딪치고 있었는데, 그 통에 소리가 어찌나 요란한지 마거릿은 가엾은 베시가 딱해 절로 움츠러들었다. 베시는 걸어오느라 완전히 기진한 듯 문가의 첫 의자에 주저앉아 있었다."], ["Margaret asked the sister for a cup of water, and while she ran to fetch it (knocking down the fire-irons, and tumbling over a chair in her way), she unloosed Bessy’s bonnet strings, to relieve her catching breath.", "마거릿은 동생에게 물 한 잔을 청했고, 그 아이가 물을 가지러 뛰어가는 동안(가는 길에 부지깽이를 넘어뜨리고 의자에 걸려 넘어졌다) 베시의 가쁜 숨을 덜어 주려고 모자 끈을 풀어 주었다."]], [["“Do you think such life as this is worth caring for?”", "“이런 삶도 아낄 만한 것 같수?”"], ["gasped Bessy, at last.", "마침내 베시가 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["Margaret did not speak, but held the water to her lips.", "마거릿은 아무 말 없이 물잔을 그녀의 입에 대 주었다."], ["Bessy took a long and feverish draught, and then fell back and shut her eyes.", "베시는 열에 들뜬 듯 길게 들이켜고는 몸을 뒤로 젖히고 눈을 감았다."], ["Margaret heard her murmur to herself: “They shall hunger no more, neither thirst any more: neither shall the sun light on them, nor any heat.”", "마거릿은 그녀가 혼자 중얼거리는 소리를 들었다. “그들이 다시는 주리지 아니하며 다시는 목마르지 아니하고, 해나 아무 뜨거운 기운에 상하지 아니하리니.”"]], [["Margaret bent over and said, “Bessy don’t be impatient with your life, whatever it is—or may have been.", "마거릿은 몸을 숙이고 말했다. “베시, 삶이 어떤 것이든, 또 어떤 것이었든 그렇게 조바심 내지 말아요."], ["Remember who gave it you, and made it what it is!”", "누가 그 삶을 주었고 지금의 모습으로 만들었는지 기억해요!”"]], [["She was startled by hearing Nicholas speak behind her; he had come in without her noticing him.", "등 뒤에서 니컬러스가 말하는 소리에 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다. 그가 들어오는 것을 알아차리지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“Now, I’ll not have my wench preached to.", "“이보시오, 내 딸한테 설교하는 건 못 참겠구려."], ["She’s bad enough, as it is, with her dreams and her methodee fancies, and her visions of cities with goulden gates and precious stones.", "그러잖아도 저 애는 꿈이니 감리교 냄새 나는 공상이니, 황금 문과 보석으로 지은 도시의 환영이니 하는 것들로 이미 몹쓸 지경이오."], ["But if it amuses her I let it abe, but I’m none going to have more stuff poured into her.”", "그래도 그게 저 애 낙이라면 내버려 두겠소만, 거기다 무엇을 더 부어 넣는 건 두고 볼 생각이 없소.”"]], [["“But surely,”", "“하지만 분명히요.” 마거릿이 돌아서며 말했다."], ["said Margaret, facing round, “you believe in what I said, that God gave her life, and ordered what kind of life it was to be?”", "“제가 한 말은 믿으시겠지요. 하느님이 저 사람에게 삶을 주셨고, 그 삶이 어떤 것이 될지도 정해 두셨다는 말 말이에요.”"]], [["“I believe what I see, and no more.", "“나는 내가 보는 것만 믿고 그 이상은 안 믿소."], ["That’s what I believe, young woman.", "그게 내가 믿는 거요, 젊은 아가씨."], ["I don’t believe all I hear—no!", "듣는 걸 다 믿지는 않소."], ["not by a big deal.", "아니고말고, 어림도 없지."], ["I did hear a young lass make an ado about knowing where we lived, and coming to see us.", "어떤 젊은 처자가 우리 사는 데를 알아 놓고 찾아오겠다고 야단을 떠는 소리는 나도 들었소."], ["And my wench here thought a deal about it, and flushed up many a time, when hoo little knew as I was looking at her, at the sound of a strange step.", "우리 딸애는 그걸 두고 어지간히 마음을 썼고, 내가 저를 보고 있는 줄도 모르고 낯선 발소리가 날 때마다 몇 번이나 낯을 붉히더구먼."], ["But hoo’s come at last,—and hoo’s welcome, as long as hoo’ll keep from preaching on what hoo knows nought about.”", "그래도 끝내 오셨으니 오신 건 반갑소. 아무것도 모르는 일로 설교만 하지 않는다면 말이오.”"]], [["Bessy had been watching Margaret’s face; she half sate up to speak now, laying her hand on Margaret’s arm with a gesture of entreaty. “Don’t be vexed wi’ him—there’s many a one thinks like him: many and many a one here. If yo’ could hear them speak, yo’d not be shocked at him; he’s a rare good man, is father—but oh!” said she, falling back in despair, “what he says at times makes me long to die more than ever, for I want to know so many things, and am so tossed about wi’ wonder.”", "베시는 마거릿의 얼굴을 지켜보고 있었다. 이제 그녀는 무슨 말을 하려고 몸을 반쯤 일으켜 애원하듯 마거릿의 팔에 손을 얹었다. “아버지한테 언짢아하지 마시우. 아버지처럼 생각하는 사람이 한둘이 아니라우. 여기는 그런 사람이 숱하고 숱해요. 그 사람들 말하는 걸 들으시면 아버지쯤은 놀랍지도 않으실 거우. 우리 아버지는 참으로 드물게 좋은 사람이라우. 하지만 아이고!” 그녀가 절망에 잠겨 몸을 뒤로 떨구며 말했다. “가끔 아버지가 하는 말을 들으면 그 어느 때보다 죽고만 싶어지우. 알고 싶은 게 하도 많은데 궁금한 것에 이리 휘둘리기만 하니 말이우.”"]], [["“Poor wench—poor old wench—I’m loth to vex thee, I am; but a man mun speak out for the truth, and when I see the world going all wrong at this time o’ day, bothering itself wi’ things it knows nought about, and leaving undone all the things that lie in disorder close at its hand—why, I say, leave a’ this talk about religion alone, and set to work on what yo’ see and know. That’s my creed. It’s simple, and not far to fetch, nor hard to work.”", "“가엾은 것, 가엾은 내 딸아, 너를 속상하게 하고 싶지야 않지, 정말 그렇지. 하지만 사내라면 진실을 위해 할 말은 해야 하는 법이다. 요즘 세상이 온통 그릇 돌아가는 걸 볼 때는 더 그렇지. 아무것도 모르는 일로 제 속을 끓이면서, 바로 코앞에 어질러진 채 놓인 일은 죄다 손도 대지 않고 있으니 말이다. 그러니 내 말하는데, 종교니 뭐니 하는 이런 이야기는 다 내버려 두고 눈에 보이고 아는 것부터 손을 대라 이 말이오. 그게 내 믿음이오. 단순하고, 멀리 가서 찾을 것도 없고, 지키기 어렵지도 않고.”"]], [["But the girl only pleaded the more with Margaret.", "그러나 처녀는 마거릿에게 더욱 간곡히 매달릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“Don’t think hardly on him—he’s a good man, he is.", "“아버지를 나쁘게 여기지 마시우. 좋은 사람이라우, 정말이오."], ["I sometimes think I shall be moped wi’ sorrow even in the City of God, if father is not there.”", "나는 가끔 아버지가 거기 없으면 하느님의 도성에서조차 슬픔에 잠겨 넋을 놓고 있겠구나 싶수.”"], ["The feverish colour came into her cheek, and the feverish flame into her eye.", "열에 들뜬 빛이 그녀의 뺨에 올랐고, 열에 들뜬 불꽃이 그녀의 눈에 어렸다."], ["“But you will be there, father!", "“하지만 아버지도 거기 계실 거예요!"], ["you shall! Oh!", "꼭 그러실 거예요!"], ["my heart!”", "아, 내 가슴!”"], ["She put her hand to it, and became ghastly pale.", "그녀는 가슴에 손을 얹었고, 무섭도록 창백해졌다."]], [["Margaret held her in her arms, and put the weary head to rest upon her bosom.", "마거릿은 그녀를 품에 안고 지친 머리를 제 가슴에 뉘었다."], ["She lifted the thin soft hair from off the temples, and bathed them with water.", "관자놀이에서 가늘고 부드러운 머리카락을 걷어 올리고 물로 적셔 주었다."], ["Nicholas understood all her signs for different articles with the quickness of love, and even the round-eyed sister moved with laborious gentleness at Margaret’s “hush!”", "니컬러스는 이것저것 필요한 물건을 가리키는 그녀의 손짓을 사랑이 주는 빠릿함으로 죄다 알아들었고, 눈이 동그래진 동생조차 마거릿의 “쉿!” 소리에 안간힘을 써 가며 조심스럽게 움직였다."], ["Presently the spasm that foreshadowed death had passed away, and Bessy roused herself and said,—", "이윽고 죽음을 미리 알리는 듯하던 그 발작이 지나갔고, 베시는 몸을 추스르며 말했다."]], [["“I’ll go to bed,—it’s best place; but,” catching at Margaret’s gown, “yo’ll come again,—I know yo’ will—but just say it!”", "“자리에 누워야겠수. 그게 제일 낫지. 그런데……” 그녀가 마거릿의 옷자락을 붙들며 말했다. “또 오실 거지요. 오실 줄 알아요. 그래도 말로 한 번만 해 주시우!”"]], [["“I will come again to-morrow,”", "“내일 다시 올게요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["Bessy leant back against her father, who prepared to carry her upstairs; but as Margaret rose to go, he struggled to say something; “I could wish there were a God, if it were only to ask Him to bless thee.”", "베시는 아버지에게 몸을 기댔고, 아버지는 딸을 안아 위층으로 올릴 채비를 했다. 그런데 마거릿이 가려고 일어서자 그는 무슨 말인가를 하려고 애쓰더니 이렇게 말했다. “하느님이 계셨으면 싶구려. 그저 댁에게 복을 내려 달라고 빌기 위해서라도.”"]], [["Margaret went away very sad and thoughtful.", "마거릿은 몹시 슬프고 생각에 잠긴 채 그곳을 떠났다."]], [["She was late for tea at home.", "집에서는 차 시간에 늦었다."], ["At Helstone unpunctuality at meal-times was a great fault in her mother’s eyes; but now this, as well as many other little irregularities, seemed to have lost their power of irritation, and Margaret almost longed for the old complainings.", "헬스톤에서라면 식사 시간을 지키지 않는 것은 어머니 눈에 큰 잘못이었다. 그러나 이제는 이것도, 그 밖의 자잘한 흐트러짐도 화를 돋우는 힘을 잃어버린 듯했고, 마거릿은 차라리 예전의 그 잔소리가 그리울 지경이었다."]], [["“Have you met with a servant, dear?”", "“하녀는 구했니, 얘야?”"]], [["“No, mamma; that Anne Buckley would never have done.”", "“아니요, 어머니. 그 앤 버클리라는 사람은 도무지 안 되겠더라고요.”"]], [["“Suppose I try,”", "“내가 한번 해 볼까.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“Everybody else has had their turn at this great difficulty. Now let me try.", "“이 크나큰 난제를 다들 한 번씩은 맡아 보았으니, 이제 내 차례를 다오."], ["I may be the Cinderella to put on the slipper after all.”", "어쩌면 끝내 유리 구두를 신는 신데렐라가 내가 될지도 모르지.”"]], [["Margaret could hardly smile at this little joke, so oppressed was she by her visit to the Higginses.", "마거릿은 이 자그마한 농담에 웃음조차 짓기 어려웠다. 히긴스네를 다녀온 일이 그토록 그녀를 짓누르고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“What would you do, papa?", "“어떻게 하시려고요, 아버지?"], ["How would you set about it?”", "어디서부터 시작하시겠어요?”"]], [["“Why, I should apply to some good house-mother to recommend me one known to herself or her servants.”", "“글쎄, 살림 잘하는 안주인을 하나 찾아가서 자기나 자기 집 하인들이 아는 사람을 하나 천거해 달라고 하겠지.”"]], [["“Very good.", "“좋은 생각이에요."], ["But we must first catch our house-mother.”", "하지만 그 안주인부터 붙잡아야 하잖아요.”"]], [["“You have caught her.", "“이미 붙잡았단다."], ["Or rather she is coming into the snare, and you will catch her to-morrow, if you’re skilful.”", "아니, 제 발로 덫에 걸어 들어오고 있으니, 네가 솜씨만 좋다면 내일 붙잡게 될 게다.”"]], [["“What do you mean, Mr. Hale?”", "“그게 무슨 말이에요, 헤일 씨?”"], ["asked his wife, her curiosity aroused.", "궁금해진 아내가 물었다."]], [["“Why, my paragon pupil (as Margaret calls him) has told me that his mother intends to call on Mrs.", "“아, 마거릿 말마따나 나의 그 본보기 제자가 그럽디다."], ["and Miss Hale to-morrow.”", "자기 어머니가 내일 헤일 부인과 헤일 양을 찾아뵐 생각이라고 말이오.”"]], [["“Mrs. Thornton!”", "“손턴 부인이!”"], ["exclaimed Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 소리쳤다."]], [["“The mother of whom he spoke to us?”", "“저희한테 이야기하시던 그 어머니 말인가요?”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Mrs.", "“손턴 부인이지."], ["Thornton; the only mother he has, I believe,”", "그에게 어머니라고는 그분 한 분뿐일 게다.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, quietly.", "헤일 씨가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“I shall like to see her.", "“나도 만나 보고 싶구나."], ["She must be an uncommon person,”", "예사롭지 않은 사람인 게 분명해.”"], ["her mother added.", "어머니가 덧붙였다."], ["“Perhaps she may have a relation who might suit us, and be glad of our place.", "“어쩌면 우리 집에 맞을 만한, 또 이 자리를 반길 만한 친척이 있을지도 모르지."], ["She sounded to be such a careful economical person, that I should like any one out of the same family.”", "어찌나 알뜰하고 살림에 야무진 사람 같던지, 같은 집안에서 나온 사람이라면 누구라도 좋겠구나.”"]], [["“My dear,”", "“여보.”"], ["said Mr. Hale alarmed.", "헤일 씨가 놀라 말했다."], ["“Pray don’t go off on that idea. I fancy Mrs.", "“제발 그런 생각은 접어 두시오."], ["Thornton is as haughty and proud in her way as our little Margaret here is in hers, and that she completely ignores that old time of trial, and poverty, and economy, of which he speaks so openly.", "내 보기에 손턴 부인은 여기 우리 마거릿이 제 나름으로 그렇듯 자기 나름으로 도도하고 자존심이 센 사람이오. 그리고 그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 이야기하는 그 시절의 고생과 가난과 절약을 그분은 아예 없었던 일로 여기고 있소."], ["I am sure, at any rate, she would not like strangers to know anything about it.”", "어쨌든 낯선 사람들이 그 일을 조금이라도 아는 것을 달가워하지 않으리라는 건 분명하오.”"]], [["“Take notice that is not my kind of haughtiness, papa, if I have any at all; which I don’t agree to, though you’re always accusing me of it.”", "“그건 제 도도함과는 다른 종류라는 걸 알아주세요, 아버지. 제게 그런 게 조금이라도 있다면 말이지만요. 저는 인정하지 않는데도 아버지는 늘 저를 그렇게 나무라시잖아요.”"]], [["“I don’t know positively that it is hers either; but from little things I have gathered from him, I fancy so.”", "“그게 그분의 것이라고 딱 잘라 말할 수 있는 것도 아니다. 다만 그에게서 주워들은 자잘한 것들로 미루어 그러려니 짐작할 뿐이지.”"]], [["They cared too little to ask in what manner her son had spoken about her.", "그들은 아들이 어머니에 대해 어떤 식으로 이야기했는지 물어볼 만큼 마음을 쓰지도 않았다. 마거릿은 다만 이 방문을 맞으려고 집에 붙어 있어야 하는지가 궁금할 뿐이었다."], ["Margaret only wanted to know if she must stay in to receive this call, as it would prevent her going to see how Bessy was, until late in the day, since the early morning was always occupied in household affairs; and then she recollected that her mother must not be left to have the whole weight of entertaining her visitor.", "그렇게 되면 이른 아침은 늘 집안일에 매여 있으니 베시가 어떤지 보러 가는 일이 늦은 시각으로 밀릴 터였기 때문이다. 그러고는 어머니에게 손님 접대의 무게를 온전히 떠맡길 수는 없다는 데 생각이 미쳤다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# 제12장"]], [["“Well—I suppose we must.”", "「글쎄요, 아무래도 우리가 가야만 하겠지요.」"], ["FRIENDS IN COUNCIL.", "— 『회의하는 벗들』."]], [["Mr.", "손턴 씨는 어머니를 바라던 만큼의 예의 있는 태도로 이끌어 내는 데 적잖은 애를 먹었다."], ["Thornton had had some difficulty in working up his mother to the desired point of civility.", "그녀는 남의 집을 찾아다니는 일이 드물었다. 어쩌다 나설 때면 무겁고 엄숙한 격식을 갖추어 그 의무를 치렀다."], ["She did not often make calls; and when she did, it was in heavy state that she went through her duties.", "아들이 어머니에게 마차를 한 대 사 주었지만, 그녀는 그 마차에 딸린 말까지 두게 하는 것은 한사코 마다했다."], ["Her son had given her a carriage; but she refused to let him keep horses for it; they were hired for the solemn occasions, when she paid morning or evening visits.", "말은 그녀가 오전이나 저녁 방문을 다니는 엄숙한 행사 때에만 빌렸다. 채 2주도 되지 않은 얼마 전에 그녀는 3일 동안 말을 빌려 두고 아는 사람들을 하나도 남김없이 “해치워”"], ["She had had horses for three days, not a fortnight before, and had comfortably “killed off”", "버린 참이었다. 이제는 그들이 차례로 수고와 비용을 치를 차례였다."], ["all her acquaintances, who might now put themselves to trouble and expense in their turn.", "그런데 크램프턴은 걸어가기에는 너무 멀었다. 그래서 그녀는 헤일 집안을 찾아가 주기를 바라는 아들의 마음이 삯마차 삯을 치를 만큼 간절한 것이냐고 거듭 캐물었다."], ["Yet Crampton was too far off for her to walk; and she had repeatedly questioned her son as to whether his wish that she should call on the Hales was strong enough to bear the expense of cab-hire.", "그렇지 않다면 그녀로서는 고마울 일이었다. 그녀의 말대로 “밀턴의 온갖 교사며 선생들과 우정이니 친분이니 하는 것을 맺어 무슨 쓸모가 있는지 알 수 없었기”"], ["She would have been thankful if it had not; for, as she said, “she saw no use in making up friendships and intimacies with all the teachers and masters in Milton; why, he would be wanting her to call on Fanny’s dancing-master’s wife, the next thing!”", "때문이다. “그러다가는 다음에는 패니의 무용 선생 부인한테까지 인사를 다니라고 하겠구나!”"]], [["“And so I would, mother, if Mr.", "“저라면 그러겠습니다, 어머니."], ["Mason and his wife were friendless in a strange place, like the Hales.”", "메이슨 씨 부부가 헤일 집안처럼 낯선 곳에서 의지할 데 없이 지낸다면 말입니다.”"]], [["“Oh! you need not speak so hastily.", "“아이참, 그렇게 성급하게 말할 것 없다."], ["I am going to-morrow.", "내일 갈 참이야."], ["I only wanted you exactly to understand about it.”", "그저 네 뜻을 정확히 알아 두고 싶었을 뿐이지.”"]], [["“If you are going to-morrow, I shall order horses.”", "“내일 가신다면 말을 부르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Nonsense, John.", "“쓸데없는 소리, 존."], ["One would think you were made of money.”", "남들이 보면 네가 돈으로 지어진 줄 알겠구나.”"]], [["“Not quite, yet.", "“아직 그 정도는 아니지요."], ["But about the horses I’m determined.", "하지만 말 문제만큼은 제 뜻대로 하겠습니다."], ["The last time you were out in a cab, you came home with a headache from the jolting.”", "지난번에 삯마차를 타고 나가셨을 때는 덜컹거리는 통에 머리가 아프다며 돌아오셨잖습니까.”"]], [["“I never complained of it, I’m sure.”", "“나는 그런 일로 투정한 적 없다.”"]], [["“No!", "“그럼요!"], ["my mother is not given to complaints,”", "제 어머니는 투정하시는 분이 아니지요.”"], ["said he, a little proudly.", "그가 조금 자랑스러운 투로 말했다."], ["“But so much the more I have to watch over you.", "“하지만 그러니 더더욱 제가 어머니를 살펴 드려야지요."], ["Now, as for Fanny there, a little hardship would do her good.”", "저기 저 패니로 말하자면, 고생을 좀 해 보는 편이 오히려 약이 될 텐데 말입니다.”"]], [["“She is not made of the same stuff as you are, John.", "“저 애는 너와 같은 재목이 아니다, 존."], ["She could not bear it.”", "견뎌 내지 못할 게야.”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton was silent after this; for her last words bore relation to a subject which mortified her.", "손턴 부인은 그 말끝에 입을 다물었다."], ["She had an unconscious contempt for a weak character; and Fanny was weak in the very points in which her mother and brother were strong.", "마지막 말이 그녀를 부끄럽게 하는 문제와 맞닿아 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 약한 성품을 저도 모르게 경멸했다."], ["Mrs. Thornton was not a woman much given to reasoning; her quick judgment and firm resolution served her in good stead of any long arguments and discussions with herself; she felt instinctively that nothing could strengthen Fanny to endure hardships patiently, or face difficulties bravely; and though she winced as she made this acknowledgment to herself about her daughter, it only gave her a kind of pitying tenderness of manner towards her; much of the same description of demeanour with which mothers are wont to treat their weak and sickly children.", "그런데 패니는 하필 어머니와 오빠가 강한 바로 그 지점에서 약했다."], ["A stranger, a careless observer might have considered that Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 이치를 따지기를 즐기는 여자가 아니었다. 빠른 판단과 굳은 결심이 스스로와 벌이는 긴 논쟁과 토론을 대신해 그녀에게 든든한 구실을 해 주었다."], ["Thornton’s manner to her children betokened far more love to Fanny than to John.", "그녀는 본능적으로 느꼈다. 무엇으로도 패니를 강하게 만들어 고생을 참을성 있게 견디게 하거나 어려움을 용감하게 마주하게 할 수는 없다는 것을."], ["But such a one would have been deeply mistaken.", "딸에 대해 이렇게 자인하면서 그녀는 마음이 찔렸지만, 그것은 다만 딸을 대하는 태도에 가엾어하는 다정함 같은 것을 배게 했을 뿐이다. 어머니들이 약하고 병치레 잦은 아이를 대할 때 흔히 보이는 그런 태도와 크게 다르지 않았다."], ["The very daringness with which mother and son spoke out unpalatable truths, the one to the other, showed a reliance on the firm centre of each other’s souls, which the uneasy tenderness of Mrs.", "낯선 사람이나 무심한 구경꾼이라면 손턴 부인이 자식들을 대하는 태도를 두고 존보다 패니를 훨씬 더 사랑한다고 여겼을지도 모른다. 그러나 그런 사람은 크게 잘못 안 것이다."], ["Thornton’s manner to her daughter, the shame with which she thought to hide the poverty of her child in all the grand qualities which she herself possessed unconsciously, and which she set so high a value upon in others—this shame, I say, betrayed the want of a secure resting-place for her affection.", "어머니와 아들이 서로에게 듣기 거북한 진실을 거침없이 내뱉는 그 대담함이야말로 서로의 영혼이 지닌 굳건한 중심에 대한 믿음을 보여 주는 것이었다. 반면 딸을 대하는 손턴 부인의 불안한 다정함, 그리고 자신은 저도 모르게 지녔고 남에게서는 그토록 높이 치던 온갖 훌륭한 자질이 제 아이에게는 없다는 사실을 감추려는 그 부끄러움— 되풀이하거니와 바로 그 부끄러움이야말로, 그녀의 애정이 마음 놓고 쉴 자리를 얻지 못했음을 드러내고 있었다."], ["She never called her son by any name but John; “love,” and “dear,”", "그녀는 아들을 존이라는 이름 말고 다른 무엇으로도 부르지 않았다."], ["and such like terms, were reserved for Fanny.", "“사랑아”, “귀여운 것” 따위의 말은 패니의 몫으로 남겨 두었다."], ["But her heart gave thanks for him day and night; and she walked proudly among women for his sake.", "그러나 그녀의 마음은 밤낮으로 아들을 두고 감사드렸고, 그녀는 아들 덕에 여인들 사이에서 당당하게 걸었다."]], [["“Fanny dear!", "“패니야!"], ["I shall have horses to the carriage to-day, to go and call on these Hales.", "오늘 마차에 말을 매어 그 헤일 집안을 찾아가려 한다."], ["Should not you go and see nurse?", "너는 유모를 보러 가는 게 어떻겠니?"], ["It’s in the same direction, and she’s always so glad to see you.", "같은 방향이고, 유모도 늘 너를 보면 그렇게 반가워하잖니."], ["You could go on there while I am at Mrs. Hale’s.”", "내가 헤일 부인 댁에 있는 동안 너는 거기까지 가 있으면 되겠구나.”"]], [["“Oh! mamma, it’s such a long way, and I am so tired.”", "“아이, 어머니, 그렇게 먼 길인데다 저는 몸이 너무 나른해요.”"]], [["“With what?” asked Mrs.", "“무엇 때문에?”"], ["Thornton, her brow slightly contracting.", "손턴 부인이 눈살을 살짝 찌푸리며 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know—the weather, I think.", "“모르겠어요. 날씨 탓인가 봐요."], ["It is so relaxing.", "어찌나 사람을 늘어지게 하는지."], ["Couldn’t you bring nurse here, mamma?", "유모를 이리로 데려오면 안 될까요, 어머니?"], ["The carriage could fetch her, and she could spend the rest of the day here, which I know she would like.”", "마차로 모셔 오면 되잖아요. 그러면 남은 하루를 여기서 보낼 수 있을 테고, 유모도 그편을 좋아할 게 뻔한걸요.”"]], [["Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Thornton did not speak; but she laid her work on the table, and seemed to think.", "다만 하던 바느질감을 탁자에 내려놓고 생각에 잠기는 듯했다."]], [["“It will be a long way for her to walk back at night!”", "“밤에 걸어서 돌아가기에는 먼 길이겠구나.”"], ["she remarked, at last.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, but I will send her home in a cab.", "“아이, 하지만 제가 삯마차로 보내 드릴 거예요."], ["I never thought of her walking.”", "걸어가시게 할 생각은 해 본 적도 없어요.”"]], [["At this point, Mr. Thornton came in, just before going to the mill.", "바로 이때 손턴 씨가 공장으로 나가기 직전에 들어왔다."]], [["“Mother!", "“어머니!"], ["I need hardly say, that if there is any little thing that could serve Mrs. Hale as an invalid, you will offer it, I’m sure.”", "굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다만, 병자인 헤일 부인께 도움이 될 만한 사소한 것이라도 있거든 챙겨 드리시겠지요.”"]], [["“If I can find it out, I will.", "“알아낼 수만 있다면 그러마."], ["But I have never been ill myself, so I am not much up to invalids’ fancies.”", "하지만 나는 앓아 본 적이 없어서 병자들이 바라는 것에는 밝지 못하구나.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그럼요!"], ["here is Fanny then, who is seldom without an ailment.", "그렇다면 여기 패니가 있지 않습니까. 어디 한 군데 안 아픈 날이 드무니까요."], ["She will be able to suggest something, perhaps—won’t you, Fan?”", "뭔가 일러 줄 수 있을 겁니다. 그렇지 않니, 팬?”"]], [["“I have not always an ailment,”", "“저는 늘 아픈 게 아니에요.”"], ["said Fanny, pettishly; “and I am not going with mamma.", "패니가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “그리고 저는 어머니와 함께 가지 않을 거예요."], ["I have a headache to-day, and I shan’t go out.”", "오늘은 머리가 아파서 나가지 않겠어요.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton looked annoyed.", "손턴 씨는 언짢은 기색이었다."], ["His mother’s eyes were bent on her work, at which she was now stitching away busily.", "어머니의 눈은 바느질감에 가 있었고, 그녀는 이제 부지런히 바늘을 놀리고 있었다."]], [["“Fanny!", "“패니!"], ["I wish you to go,”", "나는 네가 가기를 바란다.”"], ["said he, authoritatively.", "그가 위엄 있는 어조로 말했다."], ["“It will do you good, instead of harm.", "“해가 되기는커녕 너한테 이로울 게다."], ["You will oblige me by going, without my saying anything more about it.”", "더 이상 이 일로 말하게 하지 말고 가 주면 고맙겠다.”"]], [["He went abruptly out of the room after saying this.", "이 말을 하고 그는 불쑥 방에서 나가 버렸다."]], [["If he had stayed a minute longer, Fanny would have cried at his tone of command, even when he used the words, “You will oblige me.”", "그가 1분만 더 머물렀더라면 패니는 그 명령하는 어조에 울음을 터뜨렸을 것이다. “가 주면 고맙겠다”는 말을 쓸 때조차 그러했다."], ["As it was, she grumbled.", "그가 나가 버린 터라 그녀는 투덜거리는 데 그쳤다."]], [["“John always speaks as if I fancied I was ill, and I am sure I never do fancy any such thing.", "“오빠는 늘 내가 아픈 척한다는 듯이 말한다니까요. 나는 정말이지 그런 척한 적이 한 번도 없는데."], ["Who are these Hales that he makes such a fuss about?”", "오빠가 저렇게 야단스레 구는 그 헤일 집안이 대체 누구예요?”"]], [["“Fanny, don’t speak so of your brother.", "“패니, 오라비를 두고 그렇게 말하는 게 아니다."], ["He has good reasons of some kind or other, or he would not wish us to go.", "저 애한테는 무슨 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는 게지. 아니면 우리더러 가라고 하지 않았을 게다."], ["Make haste and put your things on.”", "어서 채비나 하려무나.”"]], [["But the little altercation between her son and her daughter did not incline Mrs.", "그러나 아들과 딸 사이에 오간 그 작은 실랑이는 손턴 부인의 마음을 “그 헤일 집안”"], ["Thornton more favourably towards “these Hales.”", "쪽으로 조금도 더 기울게 하지 못했다."], ["Her jealous heart repeated her daughter’s question, “Who are they, that he is so anxious we should pay them all this attention?”", "시샘 많은 그녀의 마음은 딸의 물음을 그대로 되뇌었다. “대체 누구기에 저 애가 우리더러 이렇게까지 마음을 써 주기를 바라는 걸까?”"], ["It came up like a burden to a song, long after Fanny had forgotten all about it in the pleasant excitement of seeing the effect of a new bonnet in the looking-glass.", "그것은 노래의 후렴처럼 자꾸만 되살아났다. 패니가 새 보닛이 거울에 비치는 모양을 살피는 즐거운 설렘 속에 그 일을 까맣게 잊어버린 뒤로도 오래도록."]], [["Mrs. Thornton was shy.", "손턴 부인은 낯을 가렸다."], ["It was only of late years that she had had leisure enough in her life to go into society; and as society she did not enjoy it.", "그녀가 사교계에 나설 여유를 얻은 것은 근래 몇 해에 지나지 않았다. 그리고 사교 그 자체로는 그것을 즐기지 않았다."], ["As dinner-giving, and as criticising other people’s dinners, she took satisfaction in it.", "만찬을 베푸는 일로서, 또 남들의 만찬을 품평하는 일로서라면 만족을 느꼈다."], ["But this going to make acquaintance with strangers was a very different thing.", "그러나 낯선 이들과 안면을 트러 가는 일은 전혀 다른 문제였다."], ["She was ill at ease, and looked more than usually stern and forbidding as she entered the Hales’ little drawing-room.", "그녀는 마음이 편치 않았고, 헤일 집안의 작은 응접실에 들어설 때 여느 때보다 더 준엄하고 가까이하기 어려운 얼굴이었다."]], [["Margaret was busy embroidering a small piece of cambric for some little article of dress for Edith’s expected baby—“Flimsy, useless work,”", "마거릿은 이디스가 곧 낳을 아기의 자잘한 옷가지에 쓸 고운 아마포 조각에 수를 놓느라 바빴다. 손턴 부인이 속으로 이르기를 “하늘거리기만 하고 쓸모없는 일감”이었다."], ["as Mrs. Thornton observed to herself.", "그녀는 헤일 부인의 두 겹 뜨개질이 훨씬 마음에 들었다."], ["She liked Mrs. Hale’s double knitting far better; that was sensible of its kind.", "그것은 그 나름으로 실속이 있었다."], ["The room altogether was full of knick-knacks, which must take a long time to dust; and time to people of limited income was money.", "방은 통틀어 자잘한 장식품으로 가득했는데, 먼지를 터는 데만도 한참 걸릴 물건들이었다. 그리고 수입이 빤한 사람들에게 시간은 곧 돈이었다."]], [["She made all these reflections as she was talking in her stately way to Mrs. Hale, and uttering all the stereotyped commonplaces that most people can find to say with their senses blindfolded.", "그녀는 헤일 부인에게 위엄 있는 투로 말을 건네며, 대개의 사람들이 감각을 눈감은 채로도 찾아낼 수 있는 그 판에 박힌 상투어들을 늘어놓는 동안 이런 생각들을 하고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Hale was making rather more exertion in her answers, captivated by some real old lace which Mrs.", "헤일 부인 쪽은 대답에 제법 더 공을 들이고 있었는데, 손턴 부인이 두른 진짜 오래된 레이스에 마음을 빼앗긴 탓이었다."], ["Thornton wore; “lace,” as she afterwards observed to Dixon, “of that old English point which has not been made for this seventy years, and which cannot be bought.", "뒷날 딕슨에게 이르기를 “70년 동안 만들어진 적이 없어 돈을 주고도 살 수 없는 그 옛 잉글랜드 바늘 레이스”였다."], ["It must have been an heir-loom, and shows that she had ancestors.”", "“집안 대대로 물려받은 물건임이 틀림없고, 그분에게 조상이 있다는 증거지.”"], ["So the owner of the ancestral lace became worthy of something more than the languid exertion to be agreeable to a visitor, by which Mrs.", "그리하여 그 유서 깊은 레이스의 주인은 손님에게 상냥하게 굴어 주는 나른한 수고 이상의 대접을 받을 만한 사람이 되었다."], ["Hale’s efforts at conversation would have been otherwise bounded.", "그렇지 않았더라면 헤일 부인의 대화 노력은 딱 거기까지에서 그쳤을 것이다."], ["And presently, Margaret, racking her brain to talk to Fanny, heard her mother and Mrs. Thornton plunge into the interminable subject of servants.", "그리고 곧 마거릿은 패니와 무슨 말을 나눌지 머리를 쥐어짜다가, 어머니와 손턴 부인이 하인 이야기라는 끝없는 화제 속으로 빠져드는 것을 들었다."]], [["“I suppose you are not musical,”", "“피아노가 보이지 않는 걸 보니 음악은 안 하시나 봐요.”"], ["said Fanny, “as I see no piano.”", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“I am fond of hearing good music; I cannot play well myself; and papa and mamma don’t care much about it; so we sold our old piano when we came here.”", "“좋은 음악을 듣는 건 좋아해요. 제가 잘 치지는 못하고요. 아버지와 어머니도 음악에 그리 마음을 두지 않으셔서, 이리로 올 때 쓰던 피아노를 팔았어요.”"]], [["“I wonder how you can exist without one.", "“피아노 없이 어떻게 지내시는지 모르겠어요."], ["It almost seems to me a necessary of life.”", "저한테는 거의 살아가는 데 없어서는 안 될 물건 같은데요.”"]], [["“Fifteen shillings a week, and three saved out of them!”", "“주급 15실링에, 그중에서 3실링을 떼어 모았다지!”"], ["thought Margaret to herself.", "마거릿은 속으로 생각했다."], ["“But she must have been very young.", "“하지만 저 사람은 그때 아주 어렸겠지."], ["She probably has forgotten her own personal experience.", "아마 제가 겪은 일을 잊어버린 거야."], ["But she must know of those days.”", "그래도 그 시절을 모르지는 않을 텐데.”"], ["Margaret’s manner had an extra tinge of coldness in it when she next spoke.", "다음에 입을 열었을 때 마거릿의 태도에는 냉랭한 기운이 한 겹 더해져 있었다."]], [["“You have good concerts here, I believe.”", "“여기 음악회가 훌륭하다고 들었어요.”"]], [["“Oh, yes!", "“아, 그럼요!"], ["Delicious!", "근사해요!"], ["Too crowded, that is the worst.", "너무 붐비는 게 흠이지만요."], ["The directors admit so indiscriminately.", "주최하는 분들이 아무나 다 들여보내거든요."], ["But one is sure to hear the newest music there.", "그래도 거기서는 가장 새로운 곡을 들을 수 있는 건 확실해요."], ["I always have a large order to give to Johnson’s, the day after a concert.”", "저는 음악회 다음 날이면 늘 존슨네에 잔뜩 주문을 넣는답니다.”"]], [["“Do you like new music simply for its newness, then?”", "“그럼 새 음악을 그저 새롭다는 이유만으로 좋아하시는 건가요?”"]], [["“Oh; one knows it is the fashion in London, or else the singers would not bring it down here.", "“아, 런던에서 유행하는 곡이라는 걸 아니까요. 안 그러면 가수들이 여기까지 들고 내려오지도 않을 테고요."], ["You have been in London, of course.”", "물론 런던에는 가 보셨겠죠.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said Margaret, “I have lived there for several years.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “여러 해를 그곳에서 살았어요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["London and the Alhambra are the two places I long to see!”", "런던과 알람브라, 그 두 곳이 제가 가장 보고 싶은 데예요!”"]], [["“London and the Alhambra!”", "“런던과 알람브라라니!”"]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["ever since I read the Tales of the Alhambra.", "『알람브라 이야기』를 읽고 나서부터 죽 그랬어요."], ["Don’t you know them?”", "그 책을 모르세요?”"]], [["“I don’t think I do.", "“읽어 보지 못한 것 같네요."], ["But surely, it is a very easy journey to London.”", "하지만 런던이야 아주 쉽게 다녀올 수 있는 길이잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes; but somehow,”", "“그렇긴 한데, 그게 어쩐지요.”"], ["said Fanny, lowering her voice, “mamma has never been to London herself, and can’t understand my longing.", "패니가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. “어머니는 런던에 한 번도 가 보신 적이 없어서 제 그리움을 이해하지 못하세요."], ["She is very proud of Milton; dirty, smoky place as I feel it to be.", "어머니는 밀턴을 무척 자랑스러워하시거든요. 저한테는 더럽고 연기투성이인 곳으로만 느껴지는데 말이에요."], ["I believe she admires it the more for those very qualities.”", "어머니는 바로 그런 점 때문에 오히려 더 좋아하시는 것 같아요.”"]], [["“If it has been Mrs. Thornton’s home for some years, I can well understand her loving it,”", "“손턴 부인께서 여러 해 동안 여기서 사셨다면, 그분이 이곳을 사랑하시는 마음도 저는 잘 알 것 같아요.”"], ["said Margaret, in her clear, bell-like voice.", "마거릿이 맑은 종소리 같은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“What are you saying about me, Miss Hale?", "“나에 대해 무슨 이야기를 하고 있나요, 헤일 양?"], ["May I inquire?”", "물어봐도 될까요?”"]], [["Margaret had not the words ready for an answer to this question, which took her a little by surprise, so Miss Thornton replied:", "마거릿은 조금 뜻밖의 물음에 답할 말이 얼른 떠오르지 않았다. 그래서 손턴 양이 대신 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, mamma!", "“아이, 어머니!"], ["we are only trying to account for your being so fond of Milton.”", "저희는 그저 어머니가 왜 밀턴을 그토록 좋아하시는지 헤아려 보고 있었을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“Thank you,”", "“고맙구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton.", "손턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“I do not feel that my very natural liking for the place where I was born and brought up,—and which has since been my residence for some years, requires any accounting for.”", "“하지만 내가 태어나고 자란 곳, 그 뒤로도 여러 해 살아온 곳을 좋아하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이니 헤아려 볼 것도 없다고 생각한다만.”"]], [["Margaret was vexed.", "마거릿은 언짢았다."], ["As Fanny had put it, it did seem as if they had been impertinently discussing Mrs.", "패니가 그렇게 말해 놓고 보니 정말로 그들이 손턴 부인의 심정을 주제넘게 논한 것처럼 들렸다."], ["Thornton’s feelings; but she also rose up against that lady’s manner of showing that she was offended.", "그러나 기분이 상했음을 저런 식으로 드러내는 그 부인의 태도에도 마음이 곤두섰다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton went on after a moment’s pause:", "손턴 부인은 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Do you know anything of Milton, Miss Hale?", "“밀턴에 대해 아는 게 있습니까, 헤일 양?"], ["Have you seen any of our factories?", "우리 공장을 하나라도 보셨나요?"], ["our magnificent warehouses?”", "우리의 그 웅장한 창고들은요?”"]], [["“No!” said Margaret.", "“아니요.”"], ["“I have not seen anything of that description as yet.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “아직 그런 곳은 하나도 보지 못했어요.”"]], [["Then she felt that, by concealing her utter indifference to all such places, she was hardly speaking with truth; so she went on:", "그러고는 그런 곳들에 대한 자신의 철저한 무관심을 숨기는 것이 정직하게 말하는 것이 못 된다는 생각이 들어 말을 이었다."]], [["“I dare say, papa would have taken me before now if I had cared.", "“제가 마음이 있었다면 아버지께서 벌써 데려가 주셨을 거예요."], ["But I really do not find much pleasure in going over manufactories.”", "하지만 저는 공장을 둘러보는 데서 그리 큰 즐거움을 느끼지 못해서요.”"]], [["“They are very curious places,” said Mrs.", "“아주 신기한 곳이긴 하지요.”"], ["Hale, “but there is so much noise and dirt always.", "헤일 부인이 말했다. “하지만 늘 그렇게 시끄럽고 지저분해서요."], ["I remember once going in a lilac silk to see candles made, and my gown was utterly ruined.”", "언젠가 연보라색 비단옷을 입고 초 만드는 것을 보러 갔다가 옷을 아주 망쳐 버린 적이 있답니다.”"]], [["“Very probably,”", "“그러셨겠지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton, in a short displeased manner.", "손턴 부인이 짧고 못마땅한 투로 말했다."], ["“I merely thought, that as strangers newly come to reside in a town which has risen to eminence in the country, from the character and progress of its peculiar business, you might have cared to visit some of the places where it is carried on; places unique in the kingdom, I am informed.", "“나는 그저, 이 고장 특유의 사업이 지닌 성격과 그 발전으로 온 나라에서 손꼽히게 된 도시에 새로 와서 사시는 분들이니, 그 일이 이루어지는 곳들을 한번 보고 싶어 하실까 싶었을 뿐입니다. 듣기로는 이 왕국 안에서 그만한 데가 또 없다더군요."], ["If Miss Hale changes her mind and condescends to be curious as to the manufactures of Milton, I can only say I shall be glad to procure her admission to print-works, or reed-making, or the more simple operations of spinning carried on in my son’s mill.", "헤일 양이 마음을 바꾸어 밀턴의 제조업에 궁금증을 내어 주신다면, 나로서는 날염 공장이든 바디 만드는 곳이든, 아니면 내 아들 공장에서 하는 좀 더 단순한 방적 작업이든 들어가 보시도록 기꺼이 주선해 드리겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 것이 없습니다."], ["Every improvement of machinery is, I believe, to be seen there, in its highest perfection.”", "기계라는 기계의 온갖 개량은 다 거기서 가장 완벽한 모습으로 볼 수 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“I am so glad you don’t like mills and manufactories, and all those kind of things,”", "“저는 아가씨가 공장이니 제조업이니 그런 것들을 좋아하지 않으신다니 참 다행이에요.” 패니가 반쯤 속삭이듯 말하며 어머니를 따라 일어섰다."], ["said Fanny, in a half-whisper, as she rose to accompany her mother, who was taking leave of Mrs. Hale with rustling dignity.", "어머니는 옷자락을 사각거리며 위엄 있게 헤일 부인에게 작별을 고하고 있었다."]], [["“I think I should like to know all about them, if I were you,”", "“제가 아가씨라면 그런 것들을 다 알아 두고 싶을 것 같은데요.”"], ["replied Margaret quietly.", "마거릿이 조용히 대답했다."]], [["“Fanny!”", "“패니야!”"], ["said her mother, as they drove away, “we will be civil to these Hales: but don’t form one of your hasty friendships with the daughter.", "마차가 떠날 때 어머니가 말했다. “저 헤일 집안에 예의는 갖추도록 하자."], ["She will do you no good, I see.", "하지만 저 딸과 네 버릇대로 성급한 친분을 맺지는 마라."], ["The mother looks very ill, and seems a nice, quiet kind of person.”", "너한테 이로울 게 없는 아이더구나. 어미 쪽은 무척 아파 보이고, 얌전하고 조용한 사람 같더라만.”"]], [["“I don’t want to form any friendship with Miss Hale, mamma,”", "“저는 헤일 양과 친해질 마음 없어요, 어머니.”"], ["said Fanny, pouting.", "패니가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다."], ["“I thought I was doing my duty by talking to her, and trying to amuse her.”", "“저는 그저 말동무가 되어 주고 즐겁게 해 주려고 애쓰는 것이 제 도리인 줄 알았을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그래!"], ["at any rate John must be satisfied now.”", "어쨌든 이제 존도 만족하겠지.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# 제13장"]], [["“That doubt and trouble, fear and pain, And anguish, all, are shadows vain, That death itself shall not remain;", "“의심과 곤경도, 두려움과 아픔도, 그 모든 번민까지도 다 헛된 그림자일 뿐이며, 죽음 그 자체조차 끝내 남아 있지 않으리라는 것."]], [["That weary deserts we may tread, A dreary labyrinth may thread. Thro’ dark ways underground be led;", "우리가 고달픈 사막을 밟고 지날지라도, 음침한 미로를 더듬어 헤맬지라도, 어두운 땅속 길로 이끌려 갈지라도,"]], [["Yet, if we will one Guide obey, The dreariest path, the darkest way Shall issue out in heavenly day;", "그럼에도 우리가 오직 한 분 인도자께 순종하기만 한다면, 가장 스산한 길도 가장 어두운 길도 마침내 하늘의 빛으로 나서게 되리라는 것."]], [["And we, on divers shores now cast, Shall meet, our perilous voyage past, All in our Father’s house at last!”", "그리고 지금은 서로 다른 기슭에 던져진 우리도, 위태로운 항해를 다 마치고 나면 마침내 아버지의 집에서 다 함께 만나게 되리라는 것!” — R."], ["R. C.", "C."], ["TRENCH.", "트렌치."]], [["Margaret flew up stairs as soon as their visitors were gone, and put on her bonnet and shawl, to run and inquire how Betsy Higgins was, and sit with her as long as she could before dinner.", "마거릿은 손님들이 돌아가자마자 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 보닛과 숄을 걸쳤다. 베시 히긴스가 어떤지 물어보러 달려가서 저녁 식사 전까지 되도록 오래 곁에 앉아 있을 참이었다."], ["As she went along the crowded narrow streets, she felt how much of interest they had gained by the simple fact of her having learnt to care for a dweller in them.", "붐비는 좁은 거리들을 지나가면서 그녀는 그곳에 사는 한 사람을 마음에 두게 되었다는 그 단순한 사실만으로 그 거리들이 얼마나 많은 흥미를 얻게 되었는지 느꼈다."]], [["Mary Higgins, the slatternly younger sister, had endeavoured as well as she could to tidy up the house for the expected visit.", "칠칠치 못한 동생 메리 히긴스는 손님이 오기로 되어 있으니 나름대로 애를 써서 집 안을 치워 놓았다."], ["There had been rough-stoning done in the middle of the floor, while the flags under the chairs and table and round the walls retained their dark unwashed appearance.", "바닥 한가운데는 거친 돌로 문질러 닦아 놓았지만, 의자와 탁자 밑이며 벽을 두른 자리의 판석들은 씻지 않은 채 거뭇한 그대로였다."], ["Although the day was hot, there burnt a large fire in the grate, making the whole place feel like an oven; Margaret did not understand that the lavishness of coals was a sign of hospitable welcome to her on Mary’s part, and thought that perhaps the oppressive heat was necessary for Bessy.", "날이 더운데도 벽난로에는 불이 크게 타올라 집 안 전체가 화덕 속 같았다. 마거릿은 석탄을 그렇게 아낌없이 때는 것이 메리 나름의 극진한 환대의 표시라는 것을 알지 못했고, 어쩌면 그 숨 막히는 열기가 베시에게 필요한 것인지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["Bessy herself lay on a squab, or short sofa, placed under the window.", "베시는 창 밑에 놓인 스쿼브라는 짧은 소파에 누워 있었다."], ["She was very much more feeble than on the previous day, and tired with raising herself at every step to look out and see if it was Margaret coming.", "그녀는 전날보다 훨씬 더 기운이 없었고, 발소리가 날 때마다 몸을 일으켜 마거릿이 오는지 내다보느라 지쳐 있었다."], ["And now that Margaret was there, and had taken a chair by her, Bessy lay back silent, and content to look at Margaret’s face, and touch her articles of dress, with a childish admiration of their fineness of texture.", "이제 마거릿이 와서 곁에 의자를 놓고 앉자, 베시는 말없이 등을 기댄 채 마거릿의 얼굴을 바라보고 그 옷가지를 만져 보는 것만으로 흡족해했다. 그 감의 고운 결에 아이 같은 감탄을 품고서."]], [["“I never knew why folk in the Bible cared for soft raiment afore.", "“전에는 성경에 나오는 사람들이 어째서 부드러운 옷을 그리 아꼈는지 몰랐구먼."], ["But it must be nice to go dressed as yo’ do.", "그런데 댁처럼 차려입는 건 참 좋은 일이겠수."], ["It’s different fro’ common.", "여느 것하고는 다르니 말이오."], ["Most fine folk tire my eyes out wi’ their colours; but some how yours rest me.", "곱게 차려입은 사람들은 대개 그 빛깔 때문에 내 눈이 아픈데, 댁의 것은 어쩐지 눈이 편해지는구먼."], ["Where did ye get this frock?”", "이 옷은 어디서 났수?”"]], [["“In London,”", "“런던에서요.”"], ["said Margaret, much amused.", "마거릿이 무척 재미있어하며 말했다."]], [["“London!", "“런던!"], ["Have yo’ been in London?”", "댁이 런던에 있었수?”"]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["I lived there for some years.", "몇 해 살았어요."], ["But my home was in a forest; in the country.”", "하지만 우리 집은 숲속에 있었어요. 시골에요.”"]], [["“Tell me about it,” said Bessy.", "“그 얘기 좀 해 주우.”"], ["“I like to hear speak of the country, and trees, and such like things.”", "베시가 말했다. “나는 시골이랑 나무랑 그런 것들 얘기 듣는 게 좋소.”"], ["She leant back, and shut her eyes, and crossed her hands over her breast, lying at perfect rest, as if to receive all the ideas Margaret could suggest.", "그녀는 몸을 뒤로 기대고 눈을 감은 채 두 손을 가슴 위에 포개고서, 마거릿이 들려줄 온갖 것을 다 받아들이려는 듯 더없이 편안하게 누워 있었다."]], [["Margaret had never spoken of Helstone since she left it, except just naming the place incidentally.", "마거릿은 헬스톤을 떠난 뒤로 그곳을 입에 올린 적이 없었다. 그저 지나는 말로 지명을 부른 것이 전부였다."], ["She saw it in dreams more vivid than life, and as she fell away to slumber at nights her memory wandered in all its pleasant places.", "그녀는 실제보다 더 생생하게 꿈속에서 그곳을 보았고, 밤이면 잠에 빠져들면서 기억이 그 정다운 곳들을 두루 헤매었다. 그러나 이 처녀에게는 마음이 열렸다."], ["But her heart was opened to this girl: “Oh, Bessy, I loved the home we have left so dearly!", "“아, 베시, 나는 우리가 떠나온 그 집을 정말 끔찍이도 사랑했어요! 당신이 그곳을 볼 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["I wish you could see it. I cannot tell you half its beauty.", "그 아름다움은 반의반도 말로 옮길 수가 없어요."], ["There are great trees standing all about it, with their branches stretching long and level, and making a deep shade of rest even at noonday.", "집 둘레에 온통 큰 나무들이 서 있고, 그 가지들이 길고 평평하게 뻗어 나가 한낮에도 깊고 편안한 그늘을 드리워요."], ["And yet, though every leaf may seem still, there is a continual rushing sound of movement all around—not close at hand.", "그런데도 잎사귀 하나하나는 다 멎어 있는 것 같은데, 사방에서 무언가 끊임없이 술렁이며 움직이는 소리가 나요. 바로 곁이 아니라 저만치서요."], ["Then sometimes the turf is as soft and fine as velvet; and sometimes quite lush with the perpetual moisture of a little, hidden, tinkling brook near at hand.", "그러다가 어떤 데는 잔디가 벨벳처럼 보드랍고 곱고, 또 어떤 데는 가까이 숨어서 졸졸거리는 작은 시내가 늘 적셔 주어 아주 무성해요. 또 다른 데에는 물결치는 고사리가, 온통 고사리 벌판이 펼쳐져 있어요."], ["And then in other parts there are billowy ferns—whole stretches of fern; some in the green shadow; some with long streaks of golden sunlight lying on them—just like the sea.”", "어떤 것은 푸른 그늘 속에 있고, 어떤 것 위에는 금빛 햇살이 길게 줄무늬를 이루며 누워 있죠. 꼭 바다처럼요.”"]], [["“I have never seen the sea,”", "“나는 바다를 본 적이 없구먼.”"], ["murmured Bessy.", "베시가 나직이 말했다."], ["“But go on.”", "“그래도 더 얘기해 주우.”"]], [["“Then here and there, there are wide commons, high up as if above the very tops of the trees—”", "“그리고 여기저기에 널따란 벌판이 있어요. 꼭 나무 꼭대기보다 더 높은 데 있는 것처럼 높다랗게요……”"]], [["“I am glad of that.", "“그건 반가운 얘기구먼."], ["I felt smothered like down below.", "나는 저 아래에 있으면 숨이 막히는 것 같았소."], ["When I have gone for an out, I’ve always wanted to get high up and see far away, and take a deep breath o’ fulness in that air.", "나들이를 나갈 때면 늘 높은 데 올라가서 멀리 바라보고 그 공기를 가슴 가득 깊이 들이마시고 싶었지. 밀턴에서는 숨이 막혀 죽을 지경이오."], ["I get smothered enough in Milton, and I think the sound yo’ speak of among the trees, going on for ever and ever, would send me dazed; it’s that made my head ache so in the mill.", "그리고 댁이 말한 그 나무들 사이의 소리, 그게 끝도 없이 이어진다면 나는 정신이 아득해질 것 같구먼. 공장에서 내 머리가 그렇게 아팠던 것도 바로 그것 때문이었소."], ["Now on these commons, I reckon, there is but little noise?”", "그런데 그 벌판에는 소리가 별로 없겠구려?”"]], [["“No,”", "“네.”"], ["said Margaret; “nothing but here and there a lark high in the air.", "마거릿이 말했다. “여기저기 높은 하늘에 종달새가 있을 뿐 아무것도 없어요."], ["Sometimes I used to hear a farmer speaking sharp and loud to his servants; but it was so far away that it only reminded me pleasantly that other people were hard at work in some distant place, while I just sat on the heather and did nothing.”", "이따금 농부가 일꾼들에게 날카롭고 크게 소리치는 게 들리기도 했지만, 하도 멀어서 그저 어딘가 먼 데서 다른 사람들이 열심히 일하고 있구나 하는 생각이 기분 좋게 떠오를 뿐이었어요. 나는 그냥 히스 덤불에 앉아 아무것도 하지 않고 있었고요.”"]], [["“I used to think once that if I could have a day of doing nothing, to rest me—a day in some quiet place like that yo’ speak on—it would maybe set me up.", "“예전엔 이런 생각을 했었소. 아무것도 안 하고 쉬는 날이 하루만 있으면, 댁이 말한 그런 조용한 데서 하루만 지낼 수 있으면 몸이 좀 나아지려나 하고."], ["But now I’ve had many days o’ idleness, and I’m just as weary ’o them as I was o’ my work.", "그런데 이제는 한가한 날을 숱하게 보내고 나니, 일이 지겨웠던 만큼이나 그것도 지겹구먼."], ["Sometimes I’m so tired out I think I cannot enjoy heaven without a piece of rest first.", "어떨 땐 하도 지쳐서, 먼저 좀 쉬지 않고는 천국도 즐길 수 없겠다 싶소."], ["I’m rather afeard o’ going straight there without getting a good sleep in the grave to set me up.”", "무덤에서 한숨 푹 자고 기운을 차리지도 못한 채 곧장 그리로 가는 게 좀 겁이 나우.”"]], [["“Don’t be afraid, Bessy,”", "“겁내지 말아요, 베시.”"], ["said Margaret, laying her hand on the girl’s hand; “God can give you more perfect rest than even idleness on earth, or the dead sleep of the grave can do.”", "마거릿이 그녀의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. “하느님은 이 땅의 한가함이나 무덤의 죽은 잠보다 더 온전한 쉼을 주실 수 있어요.”"]], [["Bessy moved uneasily; then she said:", "베시는 불안하게 몸을 뒤척이더니 말했다."]], [["“I wish father would not speak to me as he does.", "“아버지가 나한테 그렇게 말하지 않았으면 좋겠구먼."], ["He means well, as I telled yo’ yesterday, and tell yo’ again and again.", "어제도 말했고 몇 번이고 다시 말하지만, 아버지는 좋은 뜻으로 그러는 거요."], ["But yo’ see, though I don’t believe him a bit by day, yet by night—when I’m in a fever, half asleep and half awake—it comes back upon me—oh! so bad!", "그렇지만 있잖수, 낮에는 그 말을 조금도 믿지 않다가도 밤이 되면, 열이 올라 반쯤 자고 반쯤 깬 채로 있으면 그게 도로 나한테 밀려오는데, 아이고, 그렇게 지독할 수가 없소!"], ["And I think, if this should be th’ end of all, and if all I’ve been born for is just to work my heart and my life away, and to sicken i’ this dree place, wi’ them mill-noises in my ears for ever, until I could scream out for them to stop, and let me have a little piece o’ quiet—and wi’ the fluff filling my lungs, until I thirst to death for one long deep breath o’ the clear air yo’ speak on—and my mother gone, and I never able to tell her again how I loved her, and o’ all my troubles—I think if this life is th’ end, and there’s no God to wipe away all tears from all eyes—yo’ wench, yo’!”", "그러면 이런 생각이 드는 거요. 이게 다 끝이라면, 내가 태어난 게 고작 심장과 목숨을 갈아 넣어 일하다가 이 지긋지긋한 데서 병들라고 그런 거라면, 그만 좀 멈추라고 조금만이라도 조용하게 해 달라고 비명을 지르고 싶어질 만큼 공장 소리를 귓속에 영영 담고 살라고 그런 거라면, 솜먼지가 허파를 가득 메워서 댁이 말한 그 맑은 공기를 한 번만이라도 길고 깊게 들이마시고 싶어 목이 타 죽을 지경이 되라고 그런 거라면, 게다가 어머니는 가 버려서 내가 얼마나 사랑했는지, 내 온갖 괴로움이 어땠는지 다시는 말도 못 해 드리는데, 이 삶이 끝이고 모든 눈에서 모든 눈물을 씻어 주실 하느님이 없다면 말이오."], ["said she, sitting up, and clutching violently, almost fiercely, at Margaret’s hand, “I could go mad, and kill yo, I could.”", "이 사람아, 이 사람아!” 그녀가 몸을 일으켜 마거릿의 손을 사납다 싶을 만큼 거세게 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["She fell back completely worn out with her passion.", "“나는 미쳐 버릴 수도 있고, 댁을 죽여 버릴 수도 있소, 정말 그럴 수 있단 말이오.” 그녀는 그 격정에 아주 기진하여 뒤로 쓰러졌다."], ["Margaret knelt down by her.", "마거릿이 그 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Bessy—we have a Father in Heaven.”", "“베시, 우리에게는 하늘에 계신 아버지가 있어요.”"]], [["“I know it!", "“알아요!"], ["I know it,”", "알고말고.”"], ["moaned she, turning her head uneasily from side to side.", "그녀가 머리를 이쪽저쪽으로 불안하게 돌리며 신음했다."], ["“I’m very wicked.", "“나는 몹시 나쁜 사람이오."], ["I’ve spoken very wickedly.", "아주 못된 소리를 했구먼. 아이고!"], ["Oh! don’t be frightened by me and never come again.", "나 때문에 놀라서 다시는 오지 않는 일은 없게 해 주우."], ["I would not harm a hair of your head.", "나는 댁의 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 않을 거요."], ["And,”", "그리고,”"], ["opening her eyes, and looking earnestly at Margaret, “I believe, perhaps, more than yo’ do o’ what’s to come.", "그녀가 눈을 뜨고 마거릿을 간절하게 바라보며 말했다. “나는 앞으로 올 일에 대해서는 어쩌면 댁보다 더 많이 믿고 있소."], ["I read the book o’ Revelations until I know it off by heart, and I never doubt when I’m waking, and in my senses, of all the glory I’m to come to.”", "요한계시록을 하도 읽어서 외울 지경이오. 깨어 있고 제정신일 때는 내가 가게 될 그 온갖 영광을 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없구먼.”"]], [["“Don’t let us talk of what fancies come into your head when you are feverish.", "“열이 오를 때 머릿속에 떠오르는 공상 이야기는 하지 말기로 해요."], ["I would rather hear something about what you used to do when you were well.”", "그보다 건강했을 때 무슨 일을 하며 지냈는지 듣고 싶어요.”"]], [["“I think I was well when mother died, but I have never been rightly strong sin’ somewhere about that time.", "“어머니가 돌아가셨을 땐 내가 성했던 것 같은데, 그 무렵 언저리부터 영 제대로 튼튼했던 적이 없구먼."], ["I began to work in a carding-room soon after, and the fluff got into my lungs, and poisoned me.”", "얼마 안 있어 소면실에서 일을 시작했는데, 솜먼지가 허파로 들어가서 나를 망쳐 놓았소.”"]], [["“Fluff?”", "“솜먼지요?”"], ["said Margaret, inquiringly.", "마거릿이 묻듯이 말했다."]], [["“Fluff,”", "“솜먼지 말이오.”"], ["repeated Bessy.", "베시가 되풀이했다."], ["“Little bits, as fly off fro’ the cotton, when they’re carding it, and fill the air till it looks all fine white dust.", "“면화를 소면할 때 거기서 날아오르는 자잘한 부스러기 말이오. 공기 중에 가득 차서 온통 고운 흰 먼지처럼 보이지."], ["They say it winds rounds the lungs, and tightens them up.", "그게 허파를 칭칭 감아서 죄어 버린다더구먼. 어쨌거나 소면실에서 일하다가 몸이 쇠하는 사람이 한둘이 아니오."], ["Anyhow, there’s many a one as works in a carding-room, that falls into a waste, coughing and spitting blood, because they’re just poisoned by the fluff.”", "기침을 하고 피를 뱉지. 그저 솜먼지에 중독된 거요.”"]], [["“But can’t it be helped?”", "“하지만 어떻게 손을 쓸 수는 없나요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“I dunno.", "“모르겠소. 어떤 데는 소면실 한쪽 끝에 커다란 바퀴를 달아서 바람을 일으켜 먼지를 빼낸다더구먼."], ["Some folk have a great wheel at one end o’ their carding-rooms to make a draught, and carry off th’ dust; but that wheel costs a deal of money—five or six hundred pounds, maybe, and brings in no profit; so it’s but a few of th’ masters as will put ’em up; and I’ve heard tell o’ men who didn’t like working in places where there was a wheel, because they said as how it made ’em hungry, at after they’d been long used to swallowing fluff, to go without it, and that their wages ought to be raised if they were to work in such places.", "그런데 그 바퀴 값이 어마어마해서, 아마 500이나 600파운드는 들 텐데 벌이가 되는 것도 아니니 그걸 달아 주는 공장주는 몇 안 되지. 게다가 바퀴가 있는 데서 일하기 싫어한 사람들 얘기도 들었소."], ["So between masters and men th’ wheels fall through.", "오래도록 솜먼지를 삼키는 데 익어 놓으니 그게 없으면 배가 고파진다는 거요. 그런 데서 일할 거면 품삯을 올려 줘야 한다고 하더구먼."], ["I know I wish there’d been a wheel in our place, though.”", "그러니 공장주와 일꾼 사이에서 그 바퀴는 흐지부지되고 마는 거지. 그래도 우리 공장에 그 바퀴가 있었더라면 좋았을 거라고 나는 생각하오.”"]], [["“Did not your father know about it?”", "“아버님은 그 일을 모르셨나요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Yes!", "“알았소! 그리고 안타까워했지."], ["And he was sorry.", "그렇지만 우리 공장은 대체로 괜찮은 데였소. 사람들도 착실하고 그만하면 반듯했고."], ["But our factory were a good one on the whole; and a steady likely set o’ people; and father was afeard of letting me go to a strange place, for though yo’ would na think it now, many a one then used to call me a gradely lass enough.", "아버지는 나를 낯선 데로 보내기를 겁냈소. 지금은 그렇게 안 보이겠지만, 그때만 해도 나더러 꽤 참한 처녀라고 하는 사람이 한둘이 아니었으니 말이오."], ["And I did na like to be reckoned nesh and soft, and Mary’s schooling were to be kept up, mother said, and father he were always liking to buy books, and go to lectures o’ one kind and another—all which took money—so I just worked on till I shall ne’er get the whirr out o’ my ears, or the fluff out o’ my throat i’ this world.", "게다가 나도 비실비실하고 무르다는 소리는 듣기 싫었고, 메리 학교는 계속 보내야 한다고 어머니가 그랬고, 아버지는 늘 책 사는 걸 좋아하고 이런저런 강연에 다니기를 좋아했는데 그게 다 돈이 드는 일이었소. 그래서 나는 그냥 일했고, 이제 이 세상에서는 귓속의 그 윙윙거리는 소리도, 목구멍의 솜먼지도 영영 걷어 내지 못하게 되었구먼."], ["That’s all.”", "그게 다요.”"]], [["“How old are you?”", "“몇 살이에요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Nineteen, come July.”", "“7월이면 19살이 되오.”"]], [["“And I too am nineteen.”", "“저도 19살이에요.”"], ["She thought more sorrowfully than Bessy did, of the contrast between them.", "그녀는 두 사람 사이의 대비를 베시보다 더 서글프게 곱씹었다."], ["She could not speak for a moment or two for the emotion she was trying to keep down.", "그녀는 억누르려 애쓰는 감정 때문에 한두 순간 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["“About Mary,”", "“메리 말인데.”"], ["said Bessy.", "베시가 말했다."], ["“I wanted to ask yo’ to be a friend to her.", "“댁이 그 애한테 벗이 되어 주었으면 해서 부탁하고 싶었소."], ["She’s seventeen, but she’s th’ last on us.", "그 애는 17살인데 우리 중에 마지막 남은 아이요."], ["And I don’t want her to go to th’ mill, and yet I dunno what she’s fit for.”", "나는 그 애가 공장에 가지 않았으면 좋겠는데, 그렇다고 무슨 일에 맞을지도 모르겠구먼.”"]], [["“She could not do”—Margaret glanced unconsciously at the uncleaned corners of the room—“She could hardly undertake a servant’s place, could she? We have an old faithful servant, almost a friend, who wants help, but who is very particular; and it would not be right to plague her with giving her any assistance that would really be an annoyance and an irritation.”", "“그 애는……” 마거릿은 자기도 모르게 방 안의 치우지 않은 구석들을 흘긋 보았다. “하녀 자리를 맡기는 어렵지 않을까요? 우리 집에는 벗이나 다름없는 오래되고 충직한 하인이 있는데, 일손이 필요하기는 하지만 사람이 몹시 까다로워요. 정말로 성가시고 짜증스러운 일이 될 도움을 주어서 그이를 괴롭히는 건 옳지 않겠지요.”"]], [["“No, I see.", "“그렇소, 알겠구먼."], ["I reckon yo’re right.", "댁 말이 맞소."], ["Our Mary’s a good wench; but who has she had to teach her what to do about a house?", "우리 메리는 착한 애지만, 살림살이를 어찌하는지 그 애한테 가르쳐 준 사람이 누가 있었겠수?"], ["No mother, and me at the mill till I was good for nothing but scolding her for doing badly what I didn’t know how to do a bit.", "어머니도 없고, 나는 공장에 있었으니. 나야 내가 조금도 할 줄 모르는 일을 그 애가 서툴게 한다고 야단이나 칠 줄 알았지."], ["But I wish she could ha’ lived wi’ yo’, for all that.”", "그래도 그 애가 댁네와 함께 살 수 있었으면 좋았을 텐데.”"]], [["“But even though she may not be exactly fitted to come and live with us as a servant—and I don’t know about that—I will always try to be a friend to her for your sake, Bessy.", "“하지만 그 애가 하녀로 와서 우리와 함께 살기에 꼭 맞지는 않더라도, 그건 저도 잘 모르겠지만요, 저는 당신을 봐서라도 언제까지나 그 애에게 벗이 되어 주려고 애쓸 거예요, 베시."], ["And now I must go.", "이제 가 봐야겠어요."], ["I will come again as soon as I can; but if it should not be to-morrow, or the next day, or even a week or a fortnight hence, don’t think I’ve forgotten you.", "되도록 빨리 다시 올게요. 하지만 그게 내일이나 모레가 아니더라도, 일주일이나 보름 뒤가 되더라도 내가 당신을 잊었다고 생각하지는 말아요."], ["I may be busy.”", "바쁠지도 모르니까요.”"]], [["“I’ll know yo’ won’t forget me again.", "“댁이 다시는 나를 잊지 않으리라는 걸 알겠소."], ["I’ll not mistrust yo’ no more.", "이제 다시는 댁을 못 미더워하지 않겠구먼."], ["But remember, in a week or a fortnight I may be dead and buried!”", "그렇지만 일주일이나 보름 뒤면 내가 죽어서 묻혀 있을지도 모른다는 걸 잊지 마시우!”"]], [["“I’ll come again as soon as I can, Bessy,”", "“되도록 빨리 다시 올게요, 베시.”"], ["said Margaret, squeezing her hand tight.", "마거릿이 그녀의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다."], ["“But you’ll let me know if you get worse.”", "“하지만 더 나빠지면 나한테 알려 줘요.”"]], [["“Ay, that will I,”", "“그럼요, 그러겠소.”"], ["said Bessy, returning the pressure.", "베시가 마주 손을 쥐며 말했다."]], [["From that day forwards Mrs. Hale became more and more of a suffering invalid.", "그날부터 헤일 부인은 갈수록 더 병으로 고통받는 몸이 되어 갔다."], ["It was now drawing near to the anniversary of Edith’s marriage, and looking back upon the year’s accumulated heap of troubles, Margaret wondered how they had been borne.", "어느덧 이디스의 결혼 1주년이 가까워지고 있었고, 그 한 해 동안 쌓인 곤경의 더미를 돌아보며 마거릿은 그것들을 어떻게 다 견뎌 냈는지 의아했다."], ["If she could have anticipated them, how she would have shrunk away and hid herself from the coming time!", "미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면 그녀는 얼마나 움츠러들어 다가올 시간으로부터 몸을 숨겼을 것인가!"], ["And yet day by day had, of itself, and by itself, been very endurable—small, keen, bright little spots of positive enjoyment having come sparkling into the very middle of sorrows.", "그런데도 하루하루는 그것 하나하나만 떼어 놓고 보면 충분히 견딜 만했다. 작고 날카롭고 환한, 순전한 기쁨의 점들이 슬픔의 한복판으로 반짝이며 들어왔던 것이다."], ["A year ago, or when she first went to Helstone, and first became silently conscious of the querulousness in her mother’s temper, she would have groaned bitterly over the idea of a long illness to be borne in a strange, desolate, noisy, busy place, with diminished comforts on every side of the home life.", "한 해 전, 아니 헬스톤에 처음 갔을 때, 그리고 어머니의 성미에 밴 불평기를 처음으로 말없이 알아차렸을 때였다면, 그녀는 낯설고 황량하고 시끄럽고 분주한 곳에서, 집안 살림의 온갖 안락이 사방으로 줄어든 가운데 긴 병을 견뎌야 한다는 생각에 몹시도 신음했을 것이다."], ["But with the increase of serious and just ground of complaint, a new kind of patience had sprung up in her mother’s mind.", "그러나 불평할 진지하고 정당한 근거가 늘어나자 어머니의 마음속에는 새로운 종류의 인내가 돋아났다."], ["She was gentle and quiet in intense bodily suffering, almost in proportion as she had been restless and depressed when there had been no real cause for grief.", "그녀는 슬퍼할 실제 이유가 없을 때 안절부절못하고 우울해했던 것과 거의 같은 정도로, 극심한 육신의 고통 속에서는 온순하고 조용했다."], ["Mr. Hale was in exactly that stage of apprehension which, in men of his stamp, takes the shape of wilful blindness.", "헤일 씨는 그와 같은 성정의 남자들에게서 고집스러운 눈감기의 형태로 나타나는 바로 그 근심의 단계에 있었다."], ["He was more irritated than Margaret had ever known him at his daughter’s expressed anxiety.", "그는 딸이 드러내는 걱정에 마거릿이 여태 본 적 없을 만큼 짜증을 냈다."]], [["“Indeed, Margaret, you are growing fanciful!", "“정말이지, 마거릿, 너는 점점 공상이 심해지는구나! 네 어머니가 정말 아프다면 하느님이 아시다시피 제일 먼저 놀랄 사람은 나다."], ["God knows I should be the first to take the alarm if your mother were really ill; we always saw when she had her headaches at Helstone, even without her telling us.", "헬스톤에서도 우리는 네 어머니가 말하지 않아도 두통이 온 것을 늘 알아보지 않았느냐. 네 어머니는 아프면 얼굴이 아주 창백하고 하얘진다."], ["She looks quite pale and white when she is ill; and now she has a bright healthy colour in her cheeks, just as she used to have when I first knew her.”", "그런데 지금은 뺨에 건강하고 환한 혈색이 돌지 않느냐. 내가 처음 알았을 때 그랬던 것처럼 말이다.”"]], [["“But papa,”", "“하지만 아버지.”"], ["said Margaret, with hesitation, “do you know I think that is the flush of pain.”", "마거릿이 머뭇거리며 말했다. “저는 그게 고통 때문에 오르는 홍조가 아닐까 싶어요.”"]], [["“Nonsense, Margaret.", "“쓸데없는 소리다, 마거릿."], ["I tell you, you are too fanciful.", "내가 말하지 않았느냐, 너는 공상이 지나치다고."], ["You are the person not well, I think.", "내가 보기엔 정작 성치 않은 사람은 너다."], ["Send for the doctor to-morrow for yourself; and then, if it will make your mind easier, he can see your mother.”", "내일 너를 위해 의사를 부르려무나. 그러고 나서 네 마음이 편해질 것 같으면 그 사람이 네 어머니도 봐 주면 되지 않겠느냐.”"]], [["“Thank you, dear papa.", "“고마워요, 아버지."], ["It will make me happier, indeed.”", "그러면 정말 마음이 놓이겠어요.”"], ["And she went up to him to kiss him.", "그러고서 그녀는 입을 맞추려고 아버지에게 다가갔다."], ["But he pushed her away—gently enough, but still as if she had suggested unpleasant ideas, which he should be glad to get rid of as readily as he could of her presence.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 밀어냈다. 부드럽기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀가 불쾌한 생각들을 불러일으켰으니 그녀가 곁에서 없어져 주기를 바라는 만큼이나 그 생각들도 얼른 떨쳐 버리고 싶다는 듯한 몸짓이었다."], ["He walked uneasily up and down the room.", "그는 불안하게 방 안을 오르내리며 서성거렸다."]], [["“Poor Maria!”", "“가엾은 마리아!”"], ["said he, half soliloquising, “I wish one could do right without sacrificing others.", "그가 반쯤 혼잣말로 말했다. “남을 희생시키지 않고도 옳은 일을 할 수 있으면 좋으련만."], ["I shall hate this town, and myself too, if she——.", "그 사람이 만일……. 그렇게 되면 나는 이 도시도, 나 자신도 미워하게 될 게다."], ["Pray, Margaret, does your mother often talk to you of the old places: of Helstone, I mean.”", "그런데 마거릿, 네 어머니가 옛 고장 이야기를 자주 하더냐? 헬스톤 말이다.”"]], [["“No, papa,”", "“아니요, 아버지.”"], ["said Margaret, sadly.", "마거릿이 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“Then, you see, she can’t be fretting after them, eh?", "“그것 보아라, 그러니 그곳을 그리워하며 애태우는 게 아니지 않느냐, 응?"], ["It has always been a comfort to me to think that your mother was so simple and open that I knew every little grievance she had.", "네 어머니가 워낙 소탈하고 숨김이 없어서 그이가 품은 자잘한 불만까지 내가 다 안다고 생각하는 것이 늘 내게 위안이 되어 왔다."], ["She never would conceal anything seriously affecting her health from me: would she, eh, Margaret?", "그이는 자기 건강에 심각하게 관계되는 일을 나에게 숨길 사람이 결코 아니야. 안 그러냐, 응, 마거릿?"], ["I am quite sure she would not.", "나는 그러지 않으리라고 아주 확신한다."], ["So don’t let me hear of these foolish morbid ideas.", "그러니 그런 어리석고 병적인 생각은 다시 들려주지 말아라."], ["Come, give me a kiss, and run off to bed.”", "자, 입 맞춰 주고 어서 자러 가거라.”"]], [["But she heard him pacing about (racooning, as she and Edith used to call it) long after her slow and languid undressing was finished—long after as she began to listen as she lay in bed.", "그러나 그녀는 아버지가 방 안을 서성거리는 소리를 들었다. 그녀와 이디스가 예전에 “너구리 짓”이라 부르던 그 걸음질이었다. 그녀가 느릿느릿 나른하게 옷을 다 벗고 난 뒤로도 한참 동안, 침대에 누워 귀를 기울이기 시작한 뒤로도 한참 동안 그 소리는 이어졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# 제14장"]], [["“I was used To sleep at nights as sweetly as a child,— Now if the wind blew rough, it made me start, And think of my poor boy tossing about Upon the roaring seas.", "“나는 밤이면 어린아이처럼 달게 잠들곤 했다. 이제는 바람이 거칠게 불면 소스라쳐 깨어, 울부짖는 바다 위에서 이리저리 흔들리는 가엾은 내 아이를 떠올린다."], ["And then I seemed To feel that it was hard to take him from me For such a little fault.”", "그러고는 그토록 작은 허물 하나로 그 아이를 내게서 앗아 간 것이 모질다고 느끼는 것이다.”"], ["SOUTHEY.", "— 사우디."]], [["It was a comfort to Margaret about this time, to find that her mother drew more tenderly and intimately towards her than she had ever done since the days of her childhood.", "이 무렵 마거릿에게 위안이 된 것은 어머니가 어린 시절 이후로 한 번도 보인 적 없을 만큼 다정하고 살가운 마음으로 자기에게 다가온다는 사실이었다. 어머니는 그녀를 속내를 털어놓는 벗으로 가슴에 품었다."], ["She took her to her heart as a confidential friend—the post Margaret had always longed to fill, and had envied Dixon for being preferred to.", "마거릿이 늘 채우고 싶어 하던 자리, 딕슨이 자기보다 더 사랑받는다고 시샘하게 만들던 바로 그 자리였다. 마거릿은 자기에게 공감을 청해 오는 부름 하나하나에 애써 응했다."], ["Margaret took pains to respond to every call made upon her for sympathy—and they were many—even when they bore relation to trifles which she would no more have noticed or regarded herself than the elephant would perceive the little pin at his feet, which yet he lifts carefully up at the bidding of his keeper.", "그런 부름은 수없이 많았고, 개중에는 그녀 자신이라면 결코 눈여겨보지도 마음에 두지도 않았을 하찮은 일들도 있었다. 마치 코끼리가 제 발치에 떨어진 작은 핀을 알아볼 리 없으면서도 조련사가 시키면 조심스레 집어 올리듯이."], ["All unconsciously Margaret drew near to a reward.", "그러는 사이 마거릿은 자신도 모르게 어떤 보답에 다가가고 있었다."]], [["One evening, Mr. Hale being absent, her mother began to talk to her about her brother Frederick, the very subject on which Margaret had longed to ask questions, and almost the only one on which her timidity overcame her natural openness.", "어느 날 저녁 헤일 씨가 자리를 비운 사이, 어머니가 오빠 프레더릭 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["The more she wanted to hear about him, the less likely she was to speak.", "마거릿이 그토록 묻고 싶어 하던 바로 그 이야기였고, 타고난 솔직함마저 수줍음에 눌리고 마는 거의 유일한 화제이기도 했다. 그에 대해 듣고 싶은 마음이 클수록 그녀는 입을 열지 못했다."]], [["“Oh, Margaret, it was so windy last night!", "“아, 마거릿, 어젯밤은 어찌나 바람이 세던지!"], ["It came howling down the chimney in our room!", "우리 방 굴뚝으로 울부짖으며 몰아쳐 내려오더구나!"], ["I could not sleep.", "나는 잠을 이룰 수가 없었단다."], ["I never can when there is such a terrible wind.", "그런 무서운 바람이 불면 도무지 잠을 자지 못해. 가엾은 프레더릭이 바다에 나가 있을 때 잠 못 드는 버릇이 들었지."], ["I got into a wakeful habit when poor Frederick was at sea; and now, even if I don’t waken all at once, I dream of him in some stormy sea, with great, clear, glass-green walls of waves on either side his ship, but far higher than her very masts, curling over her with that cruel, terrible white foam, like some gigantic crested serpent.", "이제는 대번에 깨어나지는 않더라도 폭풍우 이는 바다에 있는 그 아이 꿈을 꾼단다. 배 양옆으로 유리처럼 맑고 푸른 물결의 벽이 솟는데, 돛대보다도 훨씬 높이 올라 그 모질고 무서운 흰 거품을 뿜으며 배 위로 말려 넘어오는 거야."], ["It is an old dream, but it always comes back on windy nights, till I am thankful to waken, sitting straight and stiff up in bed with my terror.", "무슨 거대한 볏 달린 뱀처럼 말이다. 오래된 꿈이지만 바람 부는 밤이면 늘 되살아나서, 무서움에 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 침대에 일어나 앉은 채로 깨어난 것이 고맙기까지 하단다."], ["Poor Frederick!", "가엾은 프레더릭!"], ["He is on land now, so wind can do him no harm.", "이제 그 아이는 뭍에 있으니 바람이 해칠 수야 없겠지."], ["Though I did think it might shake down some of those tall chimneys.”", "그래도 저 높다란 굴뚝들쯤은 흔들어 무너뜨릴지도 모르겠다는 생각은 들더구나.”"]], [["“Where is Frederick now, mamma?", "“오빠는 지금 어디 있어요, 어머니?"], ["Our letters are directed to the care of Messrs.", "우리 편지는 카디스의 바버 상회 앞으로 보내잖아요."], ["Barbour, at Cadiz.", "그건 알아요."], ["I know: but where is he himself?”", "그런데 오빠 자신은 어디 있는 거예요?”"]], [["“I can’t remember the name of the place, but he is not called Hale; you must remember that, Margaret.", "“그 고장 이름은 기억이 나지 않는구나. 그런데 그 아이는 헤일이라 불리지 않는단다."], ["Notice the F. D.", "그것만은 잊지 말아라, 마거릿."], ["in every corner of the letters.", "편지마다 구석에 적힌 F. D.를 눈여겨보렴."], ["He has taken the name of Dickenson.", "그 아이는 디킨슨이라는 이름을 쓰고 있단다."], ["I wanted him to have been called Beresford, to which he had a kind of right, but your father thought he had better not.", "나는 베러스퍼드라 부르게 하고 싶었어. 그 아이에게 그럴 권리가 어느 정도는 있었으니까."], ["He might be recognised, you know, if he were called by my name.”", "하지만 네 아버지가 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 하셨지. 내 친정 성을 쓰면 정체가 드러날 수도 있으니 말이다.”"]], [["“Mamma,”", "“어머니,” 마거릿이 말했다."], ["said Margaret, “I was at Aunt Shaw’s when it all happened; and I suppose I was not old enough to be told plainly about it.", "“그 일이 벌어졌을 때 저는 쇼 이모 댁에 있었고, 아마 사정을 곧이곧대로 들려주기에는 너무 어렸겠지요. 하지만 이제는 알고 싶어요."], ["But I should like to know now, if I may—if it does not give you too much pain to speak about it.”", "괜찮으시다면요. 이야기하시는 것이 어머니께 너무 아프지만 않다면요.”"]], [["“Pain! No,”", "“아프다니!"], ["replied Mrs.", "아니다.”"], ["Hale, her cheek flushing.", "헤일 부인이 뺨을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["“Yet it is pain to think that perhaps I may never see my darling boy again.", "“하지만 내 귀한 아들을 다시는 못 볼지도 모른다는 생각은 아프구나."], ["Or else he did right, Margaret.", "그것만 아니라면 그 아이는 옳은 일을 한 거란다, 마거릿."], ["They may say what they like, but I have his own letters to show, and I’ll believe him, though he is my son, sooner than any court-martial on earth.", "사람들이야 뭐라고 하든 내게는 그 아이가 손수 쓴 편지가 있어. 내 아들이라 하는 말이 아니라, 나는 이 세상 어떤 군법회의보다 그 아이를 먼저 믿는다."], ["Go to my little japan cabinet, dear, and in the second left-hand drawer you will find a packet of letters.”", "얘야, 내 자그마한 옻칠 장으로 가서 왼쪽 두 번째 서랍을 열어 보렴. 편지 한 묶음이 있을 게다.”"]], [["Margaret went.", "마거릿이 가 보았다."], ["There were the yellow, sea-stained letters, with the peculiar fragrance which ocean letters have.", "거기에는 누렇게 바래고 바닷물에 얼룩진 편지들이, 바다를 건너온 편지 특유의 냄새를 품은 채 들어 있었다."], ["Margaret carried them back to her mother, who untied the silken string with trembling fingers, and, examining their dates, she gave them to Margaret to read, making her hurried, anxious remarks on their contents, almost before her daughter could have understood what they were.", "마거릿은 그것을 어머니에게 가져다주었고, 어머니는 떨리는 손가락으로 비단 끈을 풀어 날짜를 살펴본 뒤 딸에게 읽으라고 건넸다. 그러면서 딸이 그 내용을 채 알아보기도 전에 조급하고 안달스러운 설명을 늘어놓았다."]], [["“You see, Margaret, how from the very first he disliked Captain Reid.", "“보렴, 마거릿. 그 아이가 맨 처음부터 리드 함장을 얼마나 싫어했는지 말이다."], ["He was second lieutenant in the ship—the Orion—in which Frederick sailed the very first time.", "프레더릭이 맨 처음 배를 탔을 때 그 사람은 오라이언호의 이등 위관이었단다."], ["Poor little fellow, how well he looked in his midshipman’s dress, with his dirk in his hand, cutting open all the newspapers with it as if it were a paper-knife!", "가엾은 것, 사관후보생 옷을 입은 모습이 어찌나 잘 어울리던지. 단검을 손에 들고 그것을 종이칼 삼아 신문지를 죄다 갈라 놓곤 했지!"], ["But this Mr. Reid, as he was then, seemed to take a dislike to Frederick from the very beginning.", "그런데 그때는 그저 리드 씨였던 그 사람이 처음부터 프레더릭을 미워하는 눈치더구나."], ["And then—stay!", "그리고, 가만있자!"], ["these are the letters he wrote on board the Russell.", "이건 그 아이가 러셀호에서 쓴 편지들이다."], ["When he was appointed to her, and found his old enemy Captain Reid in command, he did mean to bear all his tyranny patiently.", "그 배에 발령을 받고 옛 원수인 리드 함장이 지휘를 맡고 있는 것을 알았을 때, 그 아이는 그 사람의 횡포를 다 참고 견디려고 했단다."], ["Look!", "보렴!"], ["this is the letter.", "이게 그 편지야."], ["Just read it, Margaret.", "어디 읽어 보아라, 마거릿. 그 아이가 뭐라고 했더라."], ["Where is it he says—Stop—‘my father may rely upon me, that I will bear with all proper patience everything that one officer and gentleman can take from another.", "가만있자. ‘아버지께서는 저를 믿으셔도 좋습니다."], ["But from my former knowledge of my present captain, I confess I look forward with apprehension to a long course of tyranny on board the Russell.’ You see, he promises to bear patiently, and I am sure he did, for he was the sweetest-tempered boy, when he was not vexed, that could possibly be.", "장교이자 신사인 사람이 다른 장교에게서 받을 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 마땅한 인내로 견디겠습니다. 다만 지금 함장에 대해 전에 알던 바로 미루어, 러셀호에서 오래도록 이어질 횡포를 걱정스러운 마음으로 내다볼 수밖에 없음을 고백합니다.’ 보렴, 참고 견디겠다고 약속하지 않았니."], ["Is that the letter in which he speaks of Captain Reid’s impatience with the men, for not going through the ship’s manœuvres as quickly as the Avenger?", "나는 그 아이가 정말 그랬으리라 믿는다. 그 아이는 부아만 나지 않으면 세상에 다시없이 성품이 고운 아이였으니까."], ["You see, he says that they had many new hands on board the Russell, while the Avenger had been nearly three years on the station, with nothing to do but to keep slavers off, and work her men, till they ran up and down the rigging like rats or monkeys.”", "리드 함장이 어벤저호만큼 배를 빨리 다루지 못한다고 병사들에게 성을 냈다는 이야기가 나오는 것이 저 편지였던가? 보렴, 러셀호에는 새로 들어온 사람이 많았는데 어벤저호는 그 해역에 3년 가까이 머물면서 노예선을 쫓는 일 말고는 할 일이 없어 사람들을 부려 먹었으니, 다들 쥐나 원숭이처럼 삭구를 오르내렸다고 하는구나.”"]], [["Margaret slowly read the letter, half illegible through the fading of the ink.", "마거릿은 잉크가 바래 반쯤은 알아보기 어려운 그 편지를 천천히 읽었다."], ["It might be—it probably was—a statement of Captain Reid’s imperiousness in trifles, very much exaggerated by the narrator, who had written it while fresh and warm from the scene of altercation.", "그것은 아마도, 아니 십중팔구는 사소한 일에서 드러난 리드 함장의 오만함을 적은 글이었고, 다툼의 현장에서 갓 빠져나와 뜨거운 김이 가시기도 전에 펜을 든 사람의 손에서 몹시 부풀려진 것이었다."], ["Some sailors being aloft in the main-topsail rigging, the captain had ordered them to race down, threatening the hindmost with the cat-of-nine tails.", "선원 몇이 큰돛대 윗돛 삭구에 올라가 있는데 함장이 서둘러 내려오라 명하며 맨 나중에 닿는 자는 아홉 갈래 채찍으로 다스리겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["He who was the farthest on the spar, feeling the impossibility of passing his companions, and yet passionately dreading the disgrace of the flogging, threw himself desperately down to catch a rope considerably lower, failed, and fell senseless on the deck.", "활대 끝에 가장 멀리 나가 있던 사람은 동료들을 앞질러 갈 수 없다는 것을 깨닫고, 그러면서도 매질의 치욕이 미치도록 두려워, 한참 아래에 있는 밧줄을 붙잡으려고 죽을힘을 다해 몸을 던졌다가 놓치고 갑판 위에 정신을 잃은 채 떨어졌다."], ["He only survived for a few hours afterwards, and the indignation of the ship’s crew was at boiling point when young Hale wrote.", "그는 겨우 몇 시간을 더 버티다 숨을 거두었고, 젊은 헤일이 그 편지를 쓸 무렵 배 안의 분노는 끓어오를 대로 끓어오른 상태였다."]], [["“But we did not receive this letter till long, long after we heard of the mutiny.", "“그런데 우리는 반란 소식을 듣고도 한참, 한참 지나서야 이 편지를 받았단다. 가엾은 프레드!"], ["Poor Fred!", "부치는 방법도 몰랐을 텐데, 그래도 이것을 쓰는 것이 그 아이에게는 위안이 되었겠지, 가엾은 것!"], ["I dare say it was a comfort to him to write it, even though he could not have known how to send it, poor fellow!", "그러다 우리는 신문에서 기사를 보았어. 그러니까 프레드의 편지가 우리에게 닿기 훨씬 전이지."], ["And then we saw a report in the papers—that’s to say, long before Fred’s letter reached us—of an atrocious mutiny having broken out on board the Russell, and that the mutineers had remained in possession of the ship, which had gone off, it was supposed, to be a pirate; and that Captain Reid was sent adrift in a boat with some men—officers or something—whose names were all given, for they were picked up by a West Indian steamer.", "러셀호에서 흉악한 반란이 일어났고, 반란자들이 배를 차지한 채 떠나가 버렸다고, 아마 해적이 되려는 것이라고 하더구나. 그리고 리드 함장은 몇 사람과 함께 보트에 실려 표류하게 되었는데, 장교인지 무엇인지 하는 그 사람들 이름이 죄다 실려 있었단다."], ["Oh, Margaret!", "서인도제도에서 온 기선에 구조되었으니까. 아, 마거릿!"], ["how your father and I turned sick over that list, when there was no name of Frederick Hale.", "그 명단에 프레더릭 헤일이라는 이름이 없는 것을 보고 네 아버지와 내가 얼마나 속이 뒤집혔는지. 우리는 무슨 착오려니 했다."], ["We thought it must be some mistake; for poor Fred was such a fine fellow, only perhaps rather too passionate; and we hoped that the name of Carr, which was in the list, was a misprint for that of Hale—newspapers are so careless.", "가엾은 프레드는 참 훌륭한 아이였고, 그저 조금 성미가 급했을 뿐이니까. 그래서 명단에 있던 카라는 이름이 헤일의 오식이기를 바랐지."], ["And towards post-time the next day, papa set off to walk to Southampton to get the papers; and I could not stop at home, so I went to meet him.", "신문이란 것이 워낙 엉성하니 말이다. 이튿날 우편 시간이 다가오자 네 아버지가 신문을 구하러 사우샘프턴까지 걸어가셨어."], ["He was very late—much later than I thought he would have been; and I sat down under the hedge to wait for him.", "나는 집에 가만있을 수가 없어서 마중을 나갔단다. 아버지는 아주 늦으셨어."], ["He came at last, his arms hanging loose down, his head sunk, and walking heavily along, as if every step was a labour and a trouble.", "내가 생각한 것보다 훨씬 늦으셨지. 나는 산울타리 아래 앉아 기다렸다."], ["Margaret, I see him now.”", "마침내 아버지가 오셨는데, 두 팔을 축 늘어뜨리고 고개를 떨군 채 한 걸음 한 걸음이 고역이고 짐인 것처럼 무겁게 걸어오시더구나. 마거릿, 나는 지금도 그 모습이 눈에 선하다.”"]], [["“Don’t go on, mamma.", "“그만하세요, 어머니."], ["I can understand it all,”", "저도 다 알겠어요.”"], ["said Margaret, leaning up caressingly against her mother’s side, and kissing her hand.", "마거릿이 어머니 곁에 살며시 몸을 기대고 그 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“No, you can’t, Margaret.", "“아니다, 너는 모른다, 마거릿. 그때 아버지를 보지 못한 사람은 아무도 모른단다."], ["No one can who did not see him then.", "나는 마중을 가려고 몸을 일으키기도 힘들었어. 별안간 사방이 빙빙 도는 것 같았거든."], ["I could hardly lift myself up to go and meet him—everything seemed so to reel around me all at once.", "내가 다가갔을 때 아버지는 아무 말도 하지 않으셨고, 집에서 3마일도 넘게 떨어진 올덤 너도밤나무 아래에서 나를 보고도 놀라는 기색조차 없으셨다. 다만 내 팔을 당신 팔에 끼우고는 내 손을 자꾸 쓸어 주셨어."], ["And when I got to him, he did not speak, or seem surprised to see me there, more than three miles from home, beside the Oldham beech-tree; but he put my arm in his, and kept stroking my hand, as if he wanted to soothe me to be very quiet under some great heavy blow; and when I trembled so all over that I could not speak, he took me in his arms, and stooped down his head on mine, and began to shake and to cry in a strange muffled, groaning voice, till I, for very fright, stood quite still, and only begging him to tell me what he had heard.", "무슨 크고 무거운 벼락이 떨어질 테니 조용히 견디라고 달래는 것처럼 말이다. 내가 온몸을 떨며 말도 못 하게 되자 아버지는 나를 끌어안고 내 머리 위로 고개를 숙이시더니, 이상하게 목이 막힌 신음 같은 소리로 몸을 떨며 우시더구나."], ["And then, with his hand jerking, as if some one else moved it against his will, he gave me a wicked newspaper to read, calling our Frederick a ‘traitor of the blackest dye,’ ‘a base, ungrateful disgrace to his profession.’ Oh!", "나는 너무 무서워서 그저 가만히 서서, 무슨 소식을 들으셨느냐고 여쭙기만 했어. 그러자 아버지가, 누가 억지로 그 손을 움직이기라도 하는 듯 손을 덜덜 떨면서 그 몹쓸 신문을 읽어 보라고 내미셨단다."], ["I cannot tell what bad words they did not use.", "거기서는 우리 프레더릭을 ‘더할 수 없이 시커먼 역적’이라고, ‘제 직분을 욕보인 비열하고 배은망덕한 자’라고 했어. 아!"], ["I took the paper in my hands as soon as I had read it—I tore it up to little bits—I tore it—oh!", "그 사람들이 쓰지 않은 험한 말이 없었단다. 나는 다 읽자마자 그 신문을 손에 들고 갈기갈기 찢었어."], ["I believe, Margaret, I tore it with my teeth.", "찢고 또 찢었지. 아, 마거릿, 나는 이로 물어뜯기까지 한 것 같구나."], ["I did not cry.", "울지는 않았어."], ["I could not.", "울 수가 없었다."], ["My cheeks were as hot as fire, and my very eyes burnt in my head.", "뺨은 불덩이처럼 뜨겁고 눈알까지 타는 것 같았지."], ["I saw your father looking grave at me.", "네 아버지가 나를 근심스레 바라보시는 것이 보였다."], ["I said it was a lie, and so it was.", "나는 그건 거짓말이라고 했고, 정말 거짓말이었어."], ["Months after, this letter came, and you see what provocation Frederick had.", "몇 달이 지나 이 편지가 왔으니, 프레더릭이 어떤 일을 당했는지 너도 알겠지. 그 아이가 들고일어난 것은 제 자신을 위해서도, 제가 입은 억울함 때문도 아니었어."], ["It was not for himself, or his own injuries, he rebelled; but he would speak his mind to Captain Reid, and so it went on from bad to worse; and you see, most of the sailors stuck by Frederick.", "다만 리드 함장에게 할 말은 하겠다는 것이었고, 그러다 일이 자꾸 험해진 거란다. 그리고 보다시피 선원들 대부분이 프레더릭 편에 섰지."]], [["“I think, Margaret,”", "“내 생각에는 말이다, 마거릿.” 그녀가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 가냘프게 떨리는 지친 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["she continued, after a pause, in a weak, trembling, exhausted voice, “I am glad of it—I am prouder of Frederick standing up against injustice, than if he had been simply a good officer.”", "“나는 그것이 기쁘구나. 그 아이가 그저 훌륭한 장교이기만 했을 때보다, 불의에 맞서 일어선 프레더릭이 더 자랑스럽단다.”"]], [["“I am sure I am,”", "“저야말로 그래요.”"], ["said Margaret, in a firm, decided tone.", "마거릿이 단단하고 또렷한 어조로 말했다. “지혜와 정의에 충성하고 따르는 것은 훌륭한 일이지요."], ["“Loyalty and obedience to wisdom and justice are fine; but it is still finer to defy arbitrary power, unjustly and cruelly used—not on behalf of ourselves, but on behalf of others more helpless.”", "하지만 부당하고 모질게 휘두르는 제멋대로의 힘에 맞서는 것은 더욱 훌륭한 일이에요. 그것도 우리 자신을 위해서가 아니라 더 힘없는 이들을 위해서라면요.”"]], [["“For all that, I wish I could see Frederick once more—just once.", "“그래도 나는 프레더릭을 한 번만 더 보았으면 싶구나. 딱 한 번만."], ["He was my first baby, Margaret.” Mrs.", "그 아이는 내 첫아이였단다, 마거릿.”"], ["Hale spoke wistfully, and almost as if apologising for the yearning, craving wish, as though it were a depreciation of her remaining child.", "헤일 부인은 아쉬운 듯이, 그리고 그 사무치는 간절한 소망이 곁에 남은 자식을 깎아내리기라도 하는 양 거의 미안해하듯이 말했다."], ["But such an idea never crossed Margaret’s mind.", "그러나 마거릿의 머릿속에는 그런 생각이 스치지도 않았다."], ["She was thinking how her mother’s desire could be fulfilled.", "그녀는 어머니의 소원을 어떻게 하면 이룰 수 있을까 궁리하고 있었다."]], [["“It is six or seven years ago—would they still prosecute him, mother?", "“6, 7년 전 일이잖아요. 지금도 오빠를 기소할까요, 어머니?"], ["If he came and stood his trial, what would be the punishment?", "돌아와서 재판을 받는다면 형벌이 어떻게 될까요?"], ["Surely, he might bring evidence of his great provocation.”", "얼마나 심한 일을 당했는지 증거를 댈 수도 있을 텐데요.”"]], [["“It would do no good,”", "“소용없단다.”"], ["replied Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Some of the sailors who accompanied Frederick were taken, and there was a court-martial held on them on board the Amicia; I believed all they said in their defence, poor fellows, because it just agreed with Frederick’s story—but it was of no use,—”", "“프레더릭과 함께했던 선원 몇이 붙잡혀서 어미시아호에서 군법회의를 받았어. 나는 그 가엾은 사람들이 변호하며 한 말을 다 믿었단다."], ["and for the first time during the conversation Mrs.", "프레더릭이 한 이야기와 꼭 맞아떨어졌으니까. 하지만 아무 소용이 없었지.”"], ["Hale began to cry; yet something possessed Margaret to force the information she foresaw, yet dreaded, from her mother.", "그러고는 이 대화가 시작된 뒤로 처음으로 헤일 부인이 울기 시작했다. 그런데도 마거릿은 무엇에 씐 듯, 이미 짐작하면서도 두려워하던 그 사실을 어머니에게서 기어이 끌어냈다."]], [["“What happened to them, mamma?”", "“그 사람들은 어떻게 되었어요, 어머니?”"], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“They were hung at the yard-arm,”", "“활대 끝에 매달렸단다.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, solemnly.", "헤일 부인이 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["“And the worst was that the court, in condemning them to death, said they had suffered themselves to be led astray from their duty by their superior officers.”", "“그리고 무엇보다 나빴던 것은, 재판정이 그들에게 사형을 내리면서 상관에게 이끌려 제 본분에서 벗어나도록 스스로를 내버려 두었다고 한 것이었어.”"]], [["They were silent for a long time.", "두 사람은 오랫동안 말이 없었다."]], [["“And Frederick was in South America for several years, was he not?”", "“그리고 오빠는 몇 해 동안 남아메리카에 있었지요?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["And now he is in Spain.", "지금은 스페인에 있단다."], ["At Cadiz, or somewhere near it.", "카디스나 그 근처에."], ["If he comes to England he will be hung.", "잉글랜드로 오면 그 아이는 교수형을 당한다."], ["I shall never see his face again—for if he comes to England he will be hung.”", "나는 그 얼굴을 다시는 못 볼 게다. 잉글랜드로 오면 교수형을 당할 테니 말이다.”"]], [["There was no comfort to be given.", "건넬 위로가 없었다."], ["Mrs. Hale turned her face to the wall, and lay perfectly still in her mother’s despair.", "헤일 부인은 얼굴을 벽으로 돌리고, 어미의 절망에 잠긴 채 미동도 없이 누워 있었다."], ["Nothing could be said to console her.", "달랠 말이라고는 한마디도 있을 수 없었다."], ["She took her hand out of Margaret’s with a little impatient movement, as if she would fain be left alone with the recollection of her son. When Mr.", "그녀는 아들에 대한 기억과 단둘이 남고 싶다는 듯 조바심 어린 작은 몸짓으로 마거릿의 손에서 제 손을 빼냈다."], ["Hale came in, Margaret went out, oppressed with gloom, and seeing no promise of brightness on any side of the horizon.", "헤일 씨가 들어오자 마거릿은 방을 나왔다. 우울에 짓눌린 채, 지평선 어느 쪽에서도 밝아 올 기미를 볼 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV", "# 제15장"]], [["“Thought fights with thought; out springs a spark of truth From the collision of the sword and shield.”", "“생각은 생각과 싸우고, 칼과 방패가 부딪는 자리에서 진리의 불꽃이 튀어 오른다.” — W."], ["W. S.", "S."], ["LANDOR.", "랜더."]], [["“Margaret,”", "“마거릿,”"], ["said her father, the next day, “we must return Mrs.", "이튿날 아버지가 말했다. “손턴 부인의 방문에 답례를 해야겠구나."], ["Thornton’s call. Your mother is not very well, and thinks she cannot walk so far; but you and I will go this afternoon.”", "네 어머니는 몸이 좋지 않아 그렇게 멀리는 걷지 못하겠다고 하니, 너와 내가 오늘 오후에 다녀오자.”"]], [["As they went, Mr.", "가는 길에 헤일 씨는 아내의 건강 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Hale began about his wife’s health, with a kind of veiled anxiety, which Margaret was glad to see awakened at last.", "어딘가 감추어 둔 듯한 근심이 배어 있었고, 마거릿은 마침내 그것이 깨어난 것을 보고 반가웠다."]], [["“Did you consult the doctor, Margaret?", "“의사와 상의해 보았니, 마거릿?"], ["Did you send for him?”", "사람을 보내 불러오게 했느냐?”"]], [["“No, papa, you spoke of his coming to see me.", "“아니요, 아버지. 아버지는 그분이 저를 보러 오시는 이야기를 하셨잖아요."], ["Now I was well.", "그때 저는 다 나은 뒤였고요."], ["But if I only knew of some good doctor, I would go this afternoon, and ask him to come, for I am sure mamma is seriously indisposed.”", "하지만 좋은 의사를 알기만 한다면 오늘 오후에라도 찾아가 와 달라고 청하겠어요. 어머니가 정말 심하게 편찮으신 게 분명하니까요.”"]], [["She put the truth thus plainly and strongly because her father had so completely shut his mind against the idea, when she had last named her fears.", "그녀가 이렇게 사실을 분명하고 힘주어 내놓은 것은, 지난번 자신의 두려움을 입에 올렸을 때 아버지가 그 생각에 마음을 완전히 닫아 버렸기 때문이었다."], ["But now the case was changed.", "그러나 이제 사정이 달라져 있었다."], ["He answered in a despondent tone:", "그는 낙담한 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Do you think she has any hidden complaint?", "“네 어머니에게 무슨 숨은 병이 있다고 생각하니?"], ["Do you think she is really very ill?", "정말로 몹시 아프다고 생각해?"], ["Has Dixon said anything?", "딕슨이 무슨 말이라도 했느냐?"], ["Oh, Margaret!", "아, 마거릿!"], ["I am haunted by the fear that our coming to Milton has killed her.", "우리가 밀턴에 온 것이 네 어머니를 죽인 게 아닌가 하는 두려움이 나를 놓아주지 않는구나."], ["My poor Maria!”", "가엾은 마리아!”"]], [["“Oh, papa!", "“아, 아버지!"], ["don’t imagine such things,”", "그런 생각은 하지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret, shocked.", "마거릿이 충격을 받아 말했다."], ["“She is not well, that is all.", "“어머니는 몸이 좀 안 좋으신 것뿐이에요."], ["Many a one is not well for a time; and with good advice gets better and stronger than ever.”", "한동안 몸이 안 좋은 사람은 많고요. 좋은 진찰만 받으면 전보다 더 나아지고 튼튼해지는걸요.”"]], [["“But has Dixon said anything about her?”", "“하지만 딕슨이 네 어머니에 대해 무슨 말이라도 했느냐?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니요!"], ["You know Dixon enjoys making a mystery out of trifles; and she has been a little mysterious about mamma’s health, which has alarmed me rather, that is all.", "딕슨은 아무것도 아닌 일로 수수께끼 만드는 걸 즐기잖아요. 어머니 건강에 대해 좀 알쏭달쏭하게 굴어서 제가 조금 놀란 것뿐이에요."], ["Without any reason I dare say.", "아마 아무 까닭도 없을 거예요."], ["You know, papa, you said the other day I was getting fanciful.”", "아버지도 아시잖아요, 요전 날 제가 공상이 심해진다고 하셨죠.”"]], [["“I hope and trust you are.", "“정말 그러기를 바라고 또 믿는다."], ["But don’t think of what I said then.", "하지만 그때 내가 한 말은 마음에 두지 마라."], ["I like you to be fanciful about your mother’s health!", "네가 어머니 건강에 대해 공상하는 건 좋구나!"], ["Don’t be afraid of telling me your fancies.", "네 공상을 이야기하기를 두려워하지 마라."], ["I like to hear them, though I dare say, I spoke as if I was annoyed.", "나는 그걸 듣고 싶다. 그때는 내가 성가셔하는 것처럼 말했겠지만 말이다."], ["But we will ask Mrs. Thornton if she can tell us of a good doctor.", "아무튼 손턴 부인에게 좋은 의사를 아는지 물어보자꾸나."], ["We won’t throw away our money on any but some one first-rate.", "일류가 아닌 사람에게 돈을 헛되이 쓰지는 않을 게다."], ["Stay, we turn up this street.”", "가만, 이 거리로 꺾어야겠구나.”"]], [["The street did not look as if it could contain any house large enough for Mrs.", "그 거리는 손턴 부인이 살 만큼 큰 집을 품고 있을 곳으로는 보이지 않았다."], ["Thornton’s habitation. Her son’s presence never gave any impression as to the kind of house he lived in; but, unconsciously, Margaret had imagined that tall, massive, handsomely dressed Mrs.", "아들의 모습만으로는 그가 어떤 집에 사는지 짐작할 만한 인상을 받은 적이 없었다."], ["Thornton must live in a house of the same character as herself.", "그러나 마거릿은 저도 모르게, 그 훤칠하고 육중하고 잘 차려입은 손턴 부인이라면 제 자신과 같은 성격의 집에 살고 있으리라 상상해 왔다."], ["Now Marlborough Street consisted of long rows of small houses, with a blank wall here and there; at least that was all they could see from the point at which they entered it.", "그런데 말버러 가는 작은 집들이 길게 늘어선 줄로 이루어져 있었고, 군데군데 창 하나 없는 담이 서 있었다. 적어도 그들이 들어선 지점에서 보이는 것은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["“He told me he lived in Marlborough Street, I’m sure,”", "“그가 말버러 가에 산다고 말한 게 분명한데.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, with a much perplexed air.", "헤일 씨가 몹시 어리둥절한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps it is one of the economies he still practises, to live in a very small house.", "“아주 작은 집에 사는 것도 그분이 아직 지키고 있는 절약 가운데 하나일지 모르죠."], ["But here are plenty of people about; let me ask.”", "하지만 여기 사람들이 많으니 제가 물어볼게요.”"]], [["She accordingly inquired of a passer-by, and was informed that Mr.", "그리하여 그녀는 지나가는 사람에게 물었고, 손턴 씨는 공장 바로 옆에 산다는 대답을 들었다."], ["Thornton lived close to the mill, and had the factory lodge-door pointed out to her, at the end of the long dead wall they had noticed.", "그리고 그들이 눈여겨보았던 그 길고 밋밋한 담 끝에 있는 공장 수위실 문을 손가락질로 안내받았다."]], [["The lodge-door was like a common garden-door; on one side of it were great closed gates for the ingress and egress of lorries and wagons.", "수위실 문은 여느 정원 문 같았다. 그 한쪽에는 짐수레와 화물마차가 드나드는 커다란 대문이 닫혀 있었다."], ["The lodge-keeper admitted them into a great oblong yard, on one side of which were offices for the transaction of business; on the opposite, an immense many-windowed mill, whence proceeded the continual clank of machinery and the long groaning roar of the steam-engine, enough to deafen those who lived within the enclosure.", "수위가 그들을 널따란 직사각형 마당으로 들여보냈는데, 한쪽에는 사무를 보는 방들이 있었고 맞은편에는 창이 수없이 달린 거대한 공장이 있었다. 거기서는 기계의 끊임없는 쨍그렁 소리와 증기기관의 길게 신음하는 굉음이 흘러나와, 그 담장 안에 사는 이들의 귀를 먹먹하게 할 지경이었다."], ["Opposite to the wall, along which the street ran, on one of the narrow sides of the oblong, was a handsome stone-coped house,—blackened, to be sure, by the smoke, but with paint, windows, and steps kept scrupulously clean.", "거리가 지나가는 그 담의 맞은편, 직사각형의 짧은 변 한쪽에는 돌로 처마를 두른 훤칠한 집이 서 있었다. 물론 매연에 검게 그을려 있었지만, 페인트와 창문과 층계는 나무랄 데 없이 깨끗하게 건사되어 있었다."], ["It was evidently a house which had been built some fifty or sixty years.", "지은 지 50, 60년쯤 된 집임이 분명했다."], ["The stone facings—the long, narrow windows, and the number of them—the flights of steps up to the front door, ascending from either side, and guarded by railing—all witnessed to its age.", "돌로 마감한 겉면, 길고 좁은 창들과 그 많은 수, 양옆에서 올라와 난간으로 둘러싸인 현관 앞 층계 — 모든 것이 그 집의 나이를 말해 주었다."], ["Margaret only wondered why people who could afford to live in so good a house, and keep it in such perfect order, did not prefer a much smaller dwelling in the country, or even some suburb; not in the continual whirl and din of the factory.", "마거릿은 다만 이토록 좋은 집에 살며 이토록 완벽하게 건사할 형편이 되는 사람들이 어째서 시골이나 하다못해 교외의 훨씬 작은 살림집을 더 좋아하지 않는지 의아했다. 공장의 끊임없는 소용돌이와 소음 속이 아니라 말이다."], ["Her unaccustomed ears could hardly catch her father’s voice, as they stood on the steps awaiting the opening of the door.", "문이 열리기를 기다리며 층계에 서 있는 동안, 익숙하지 못한 그녀의 귀는 아버지의 목소리조차 알아듣기 어려웠다."], ["The yard, too, with the great doors in the dead wall as a boundary, was but a dismal look-out for the sitting-rooms of the house—as Margaret found when they had mounted the old-fashioned stairs, and been ushered into the drawing-room, the three windows of which went over the front door and the room on the right-hand side of the entrance.", "게다가 창 없는 담의 커다란 대문을 경계로 삼은 그 마당은 집 안 거실에서 내다보기에 음울하기 짝이 없는 풍경이었다. 마거릿은 옛날식 계단을 올라 응접실로 안내되었을 때 그것을 알았다."], ["There was no one in the drawing-room.", "응접실의 창 세 개는 현관문과 그 오른쪽 방 위로 나 있었다."], ["It seemed as though no one had been in it since the day when the furniture was bagged up with as much care as if the house was to be overwhelmed with lava, and discovered a thousand years hence.", "응접실에는 아무도 없었다. 마치 이 집이 용암에 뒤덮여 천 년 뒤에나 발견되기라도 할 듯 정성스레 가구에 덮개를 씌우던 그날 이후로 아무도 이 방에 들어온 적이 없는 것 같았다."], ["The walls were pink and gold; the pattern on the carpet represented bunches of flowers on a light ground, but it was carefully covered up in the centre by a linen drugget, glazed and colourless.", "벽은 분홍빛과 금빛이었다. 양탄자의 무늬는 밝은 바탕에 꽃다발을 그린 것이었으나, 한가운데는 윤을 낸 빛깔 없는 아마포 깔개로 조심스레 덮여 있었다."], ["The window-curtains were lace; each chair and sofa had its own particular veil of netting or knitting.", "창의 커튼은 레이스였고, 의자와 소파마다 저마다의 그물뜨기나 뜨개 덮개를 걸치고 있었다."], ["Great alabaster groups occupied every flat surface, safe from dust under their glass shades.", "커다란 설화석고 군상들이 평평한 자리마다 차지하고 서서 유리 덮개 아래 먼지를 피하고 있었다."], ["In the middle of the room, right under the bagged-up chandelier, was a large circular table, with smartly-bound books arranged at regular intervals round the circumference of its polished surface, like gaily coloured spokes of a wheel.", "방 한가운데, 덮개를 씌운 샹들리에 바로 아래에는 커다란 둥근 탁자가 있었고, 말쑥하게 장정한 책들이 윤나는 상판 둘레에 일정한 간격으로 놓여 바퀴의 알록달록한 살처럼 보였다."], ["Everything reflected light, nothing absorbed it.", "모든 것이 빛을 되비쳤고 무엇 하나 빛을 빨아들이지 않았다."], ["The whole room had a painfully spotted, spangled, speckled look about it, which impressed Margaret so unpleasantly that she was hardly conscious of the peculiar cleanliness required to keep everything so white and pure in such an atmosphere, or of the trouble that must be willingly expended to secure that effect of icy, snowy discomfort.", "방 전체가 아프도록 얼룩덜룩하고 반짝반짝하고 점점이 박힌 듯한 인상을 주었고, 그것이 마거릿에게 어찌나 불쾌하게 다가왔던지 그녀는 이런 공기 속에서 모든 것을 이토록 희고 정갈하게 지키는 데 필요한 남다른 청결함도, 그 얼음 같고 눈 같은 불편함을 이루려고 기꺼이 쏟아부었을 수고도 거의 의식하지 못했다."], ["Wherever she looked there was evidence of care and labour, but not care and labour to procure ease, to help on habits of tranquil home employment; solely to ornament and then to preserve ornament from dirt or destruction.", "어디를 보아도 정성과 노동의 자취가 있었지만, 그것은 편안함을 얻기 위한, 조용한 가정의 일과를 돕기 위한 정성과 노동이 아니었다. 오로지 꾸미기 위한, 그리고 그 꾸밈을 먼지와 훼손으로부터 지키기 위한 것이었다."]], [["They had leisure to observe, and to speak to each other in low voices, before Mrs. Thornton appeared.", "손턴 부인이 나타나기 전에 두 사람은 방을 살펴보고 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나눌 겨를이 있었다."], ["They were talking of what all the world might hear; but it is a common effect of such a room as this to make people speak low, as if unwilling to awaken the unused echoes.", "그들이 하는 이야기는 온 세상이 들어도 좋을 것이었으나, 이런 방은 흔히 사람으로 하여금 낮게 말하게 만드는 법이다. 마치 쓰인 적 없는 메아리를 깨우기 싫다는 듯이."]], [["At last Mrs. Thornton came in, rustling in handsome black silk, as was her wont; her muslins and laces rivalling, not excelling, the pure whiteness of the muslins and netting of the room.", "마침내 손턴 부인이 여느 때처럼 훌륭한 검은 비단을 사각거리며 들어왔다."], ["Margaret explained how it was that her mother could not accompany them to return Mrs.", "그녀의 모슬린과 레이스는 방 안의 모슬린과 그물뜨기의 순백에 견줄 만했으나 그것을 넘어서지는 않았다."], ["Thornton’s call; but in her anxiety not to bring back her father’s fears too vividly, she gave but a bungling account, and left the impression on Mrs.", "마거릿은 어머니가 손턴 부인의 방문에 답례하러 함께 오지 못한 사정을 설명했다."], ["Thornton’s mind that Mrs. Hale’s was some temporary or fanciful fine-ladyish indisposition, which might have been put aside had there been a strong enough motive; or that if it was too severe to allow her to come out that day, the call might have been deferred.", "그러나 아버지의 두려움을 너무 생생하게 되살리지 않으려는 조바심 탓에 그녀는 어설픈 설명을 하고 말았고, 손턴 부인의 마음에는 헤일 부인의 병이 무슨 일시적이거나 공상적인, 귀부인 티 나는 몸살 같은 것이어서 동기가 충분히 강했더라면 제쳐 둘 수도 있었으리라는 인상을, 혹은 그날 외출을 못 할 만큼 심한 것이었다면 방문을 미룰 수도 있었으리라는 인상을 남겼다."], ["Remembering, too, the horses to her carriage, hired for her own visit to the Hales, and how Fanny had been ordered to go by Mr. Thornton, in order to pay every respect to them, Mrs.", "게다가 헤일 가를 찾아갈 때 제 마차에 세내었던 말들과, 그들에게 온갖 예를 다하도록 손턴 씨가 패니에게 함께 가라고 일렀던 일까지 떠올리자 손턴 부인은 다소 기분이 상해 몸을 곧추세웠고, 마거릿에게 아무런 동정도 보이지 않았다."], ["Thornton drew up slightly offended, and gave Margaret no sympathy—indeed, hardly any credit for the statement of her mother’s indisposition.", "실은 어머니가 편찮다는 그 말조차 거의 곧이듣지 않았다."]], [["“How is Mr. Thornton?”", "“손턴 씨는 어떠십니까?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."], ["“I was afraid he was not well, from his hurried note yesterday.”", "“어제 서둘러 쓴 쪽지를 보고 몸이 좋지 않으신가 걱정했습니다.”"]], [["“My son is rarely ill; and when he is, he never speaks about it, or makes it an excuse for not doing anything.", "“제 아들은 좀처럼 앓지 않습니다. 앓더라도 그것을 입에 올리거나 무슨 일을 못 하는 핑계로 삼는 법이 없지요."], ["He told me he could not get leisure to read with you last night, sir.", "어젯밤에는 선생님과 함께 읽을 짬을 낼 수 없다고 하더군요."], ["He regretted it, I am sure; he values the hours spent with you.”", "아쉬워한 것이 분명합니다. 그 애는 선생님과 보내는 시간을 소중히 여기니까요.”"]], [["“I am sure they are equally agreeable to me,”", "“저에게도 똑같이 즐거운 시간입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“It makes me feel young again to see his enjoyment and appreciation of all that is fine in classical literature.”", "“고전 문학의 아름다움을 그토록 즐기고 알아보는 모습을 보면 저까지 다시 젊어지는 기분이 듭니다.”"]], [["“I have no doubt that classics are very desirable for people who have leisure.", "“한가한 사람들에게야 고전이 아주 바람직한 것이겠지요."], ["But I confess, it was against my judgment that my son renewed his study of them.", "그러나 솔직히 말해 아들이 다시 고전 공부를 시작한 것은 제 판단에 어긋나는 일이었습니다."], ["The time and place in which he lives, seem to me to require all his energy and attention.", "그 애가 사는 시대와 장소는 그 애의 온 기운과 관심을 요구하는 듯이 보이니까요."], ["Classics may do very well for men who loiter away their lives in the country or in colleges; but Milton men ought to have their thoughts and powers absorbed in the work of to-day.", "고전은 시골이나 대학에서 인생을 빈둥거리며 흘려보내는 사내들에게나 어울릴 것입니다. 그러나 밀턴 사람이라면 생각도 힘도 오늘의 일에 쏟아부어야 하지요."], ["At least, that is my opinion.”", "적어도 제 의견은 그렇습니다.”"], ["This last clause she gave out with “the pride that apes humility.”", "이 마지막 구절을 그녀는 “겸손을 흉내 내는 자부심”으로 내뱉었다."]], [["“But, surely, if the mind is too long directed to one object only, it will get stiff and rigid, and unable to take in many interests,”", "“하지만 정신이 한 가지 대상에만 너무 오래 매여 있으면 굳고 뻣뻣해져서 여러 관심사를 받아들이지 못하게 되지 않을까요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“I do not quite understand what you mean by a mind getting stiff and rigid.", "“정신이 굳고 뻣뻣해진다는 게 무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠군요."], ["Nor do I admire those whirligig characters that are full of this thing to-day, to be utterly forgetful of it in their new interest to-morrow.", "그리고 나는 오늘은 이것에 푹 빠졌다가 내일이면 새 관심거리에 그것을 까맣게 잊어버리는 팔랑개비 같은 사람들을 좋게 보지 않습니다."], ["Having many interests does not suit the life of a Milton manufacturer.", "관심사가 많은 것은 밀턴 공장주의 삶에 맞지 않아요."], ["It is or ought to be enough for him to have one great desire, and to bring all the purposes of his life to bear on the fulfilment of that.”", "그에게는 큰 소망 하나를 품고 제 삶의 모든 목적을 그 성취에 쏟는 것으로 충분하고, 또 충분해야 합니다.”"]], [["“And that is—?”", "“그것은……?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["Her sallow cheek flushed, and her eye lightened, as she answered:", "누런 뺨이 달아오르고 눈이 빛나며 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“To hold and maintain a high, honourable place among the merchants of his country—the men of his town.", "“제 나라의 상인들 사이에서, 제 고장의 사내들 사이에서 높고 명예로운 자리를 얻고 지켜 내는 것이지요."], ["Such a place my son has earned for himself.", "제 아들은 그런 자리를 제 힘으로 얻었습니다."], ["Go where you will—I don’t say in England only, but in Europe—the name of John Thornton of Milton is known and respected amongst all men of business.", "어디를 가 보셔도 — 잉글랜드만 두고 하는 말이 아니라 유럽 어디를 가 보셔도 — 밀턴의 존 손턴이라는 이름은 사업하는 사람이라면 누구나 알고 존경합니다."], ["Of course, it is unknown in the fashionable circles,”", "물론 상류 사교계에서야 알려지지 않았지요.”"], ["she continued scornfully.", "그녀가 경멸하듯 이어 말했다."], ["“Idle gentlemen and ladies are not likely to know much of a Milton manufacturer, unless he gets into parliament, or marries a lord’s daughter.”", "“한가한 신사 숙녀들이 밀턴 공장주에 대해 많이 알 리가 없지요. 의회에 들어가거나 귀족의 딸과 혼인하지 않는 한은요.”"]], [["Both Mr. Hale and Margaret had an uneasy, ludicrous consciousness that they had never heard of this great name, until Mr. Bell had written them word that Mr. Thornton would be a good friend to have in Milton. The proud mother’s world was not their world of Harley street gentilities on the one hand, or country clergymen and Hampshire squires on the other. Margaret’s face, in spite of all her endeavours to keep it simply listening in its expression, told the sensitive Mrs. Thornton this feeling of hers.", "헤일 씨도 마거릿도, 벨 씨가 손턴 씨야말로 밀턴에서 사귀어 두면 좋을 벗이라고 편지에 써 보내기 전까지 그 위대한 이름을 들어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 어색하고도 우스꽝스럽게 의식하고 있었다. 그 자부심 강한 어머니의 세계는 한편으로 할리 가의 점잖은 사람들의 세계도, 다른 한편으로 시골 목사와 햄프셔 지주들의 세계도 아니었다. 그저 듣고만 있는 표정을 지키려는 온갖 애씀에도 마거릿의 얼굴은 예민한 손턴 부인에게 그 마음을 일러 주고 말았다."]], [["“You think you never heard of this wonderful son of mine, Miss Hale.", "“제 아들이라는 이 놀라운 인물에 대해서는 들어 본 적이 없다고 생각하시는군요, 헤일 양."], ["You think I’m an old woman whose ideas are hounded by Milton, and whose own crow is the whitest ever seen.”", "나를 생각이 밀턴에 갇혀 있고 제 까마귀가 세상에서 가장 희다고 여기는 늙은 여편네로 보고 계시고요.”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니에요.”"], ["said Margaret, with some spirit.", "마거릿이 다소 기운차게 말했다."], ["“It may be true that I was thinking I had hardly heard Mr. Thornton’s name before I came to Milton.", "“밀턴에 오기 전에는 손턴 씨의 이름을 거의 들어 본 적이 없다고 생각하고 있었던 것은 사실일 거예요."], ["But since I have come here, I have heard enough to make me respect and admire him, and to feel how much justice and truth there is in what you have said of him.”", "하지만 이곳에 온 뒤로는 그분을 존경하고 우러러보게 될 만큼, 그리고 부인께서 그분에 대해 하신 말씀이 얼마나 옳고 참된지 느낄 만큼 넉넉히 들었어요.”"]], [["“Who spoke to you of him?” asked Mrs.", "“누가 그 애 이야기를 하던가요?”"], ["Thornton, a little mollified, yet jealous lest any one else’s words should not have done him full justice.", "손턴 부인이 다소 누그러져 물었으나, 남의 말이 아들을 온전히 평가하지 못했을까 하는 질투가 배어 있었다."]], [["Margaret hesitated before she replied.", "마거릿은 대답하기 전에 망설였다."], ["She did not like this authoritative questioning. Mr.", "이런 위압적인 캐물음이 마음에 들지 않았던 것이다."], ["Hale came in, as he thought, to the rescue.", "헤일 씨가 제 딴에는 구원자로 나섰다."]], [["“It was what Mr. Thornton said himself, that made us know the kind of man he was.", "“손턴 씨가 어떤 사람인지 알게 된 것은 그분이 스스로 하신 말씀 덕분이었지요."], ["Was it not, Margaret?”", "그렇지 않니, 마거릿?”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton drew herself up, and said—", "손턴 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다."]], [["“My son is not the one to tell of his own doings.", "“제 아들은 제 행적을 떠벌릴 사람이 아닙니다."], ["May I again ask you, Miss Hale, from whose account you formed your favourable opinion of him?", "다시 한번 여쭙겠습니다만, 헤일 양, 누구의 이야기를 듣고 그 애를 좋게 여기게 되셨는지요?"], ["A mother is curious and greedy of commendation of her children, you know.”", "어미란 제 자식 칭찬이라면 궁금해하고 탐내는 법이니까요.”"]], [["Margaret replied, “It was as much from what Mr.", "마거릿이 대답했다."], ["Thornton withheld of that which we had been told of his previous life by Mr. Bell,—it was more that than what he said, that made us all feel what reason you have to be proud of him.”", "“벨 씨에게서 들었던 그분의 지난 삶에 대한 이야기를 손턴 씨가 도리어 감추셨다는 것이, 하신 말씀보다도 그 감추심이, 부인께서 그분을 자랑스러워하실 까닭이 얼마나 큰지를 저희 모두에게 느끼게 해 주었어요.”"]], [["“Mr. Bell!", "“벨 씨라니!"], ["What can he know of John?", "그 사람이 존에 대해 뭘 알겠습니까?"], ["He, living a lazy life in a drowsy college.", "졸음에 겨운 대학에서 게으른 삶을 사는 사람이."], ["But I’m obliged to you, Miss Hale.", "하지만 고맙군요, 헤일 양."], ["Many a missy young lady would have shrunk from giving an old woman the pleasure of hearing that her son was well spoken of.”", "어지간한 아가씨들 같으면 아들이 좋은 평판을 듣는다는 이야기로 늙은 여자를 기쁘게 해 주기를 꺼렸을 텐데요.”"]], [["“Why?”", "“어째서요?”"], ["asked Margaret, looking straight at Mrs. Thornton, in bewilderment.", "마거릿이 어리둥절해하며 손턴 부인을 똑바로 바라보고 물었다."]], [["“Why!", "“어째서라니요!"], ["because I suppose they might have consciences that told them how surely they were making the old mother into an advocate for them, in case they had any plans on the son’s heart.”", "아들의 마음에 무슨 속셈이 있는 경우, 늙은 어미를 제 편드는 사람으로 만들고 있다는 것을 스스로 얼마나 뻔히 아는지, 그런 양심이 있을 테니까요.”"]], [["She smiled a grim smile, for she had been pleased by Margaret’s frankness; and perhaps she felt that she had been asking questions too much as if she had a right to catechise.", "그녀는 험상궂은 미소를 지었다. 마거릿의 솔직함이 마음에 들었던 것이다."], ["Margaret laughed outright at the notion presented to her; laughed so merrily that it grated on Mrs.", "그리고 어쩌면 제게 무슨 심문할 권리라도 있는 양 지나치게 캐물었다고 느꼈는지도 몰랐다."], ["Thornton’s ear, as if the words that called forth that laugh, must have been utterly and entirely ludicrous.", "마거릿은 눈앞에 놓인 그 발상에 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 어찌나 유쾌하게 웃던지, 그 웃음을 불러낸 말이 온통 통째로 우스꽝스럽기라도 한 듯이 들려 손턴 부인의 귀에 거슬렸다."]], [["Margaret stopped her merriment as soon as she saw Mrs. Thornton’s annoyed look.", "마거릿은 손턴 부인의 언짢은 낯빛을 보자마자 웃음을 그쳤다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, madam.", "“죄송합니다, 부인."], ["But I really am very much obliged to you for exonerating me from making any plans on Mr. Thornton’s heart.”", "하지만 손턴 씨의 마음에 무슨 속셈을 품었다는 혐의를 벗겨 주시니 정말 감사할 따름이에요.”"]], [["“Young ladies have, before now,” said Mrs.", "“젊은 아가씨들이 전에도 그런 적이 있으니까요.”"], ["Thornton, stiffly.", "손턴 부인이 뻣뻣하게 말했다."]], [["“I hope Miss Thornton is well,” put in Mr.", "“손턴 양은 잘 지내시겠지요.”"], ["Hale, desirous of changing the current of the conversation.", "헤일 씨가 대화의 흐름을 바꾸고 싶어 끼어들었다."]], [["“She is as well as she ever is.", "“늘 그렇듯 잘 지냅니다."], ["She is not strong,”", "튼튼하지는 못하지요.”"], ["replied Mrs. Thornton, shortly.", "손턴 부인이 짧게 대답했다."]], [["“And Mr. Thornton?", "“손턴 씨는요?"], ["I suppose I may hope to see him on Thursday?”", "목요일에는 뵐 수 있으리라 바라도 되겠지요?”"]], [["“I cannot answer for my son’s engagements.", "“아들의 일정은 제가 장담할 수 없습니다."], ["There is some uncomfortable work going on in the town; a threatening of a strike.", "시내에 어수선한 일이 벌어지고 있어요. 파업 조짐이 있습니다."], ["If so, his experience and judgment will make him much consulted by his friends.", "그리되면 그 애의 경험과 판단을 벗들이 많이들 물어 오겠지요."], ["But I should think he could come on Thursday.", "그래도 목요일에는 갈 수 있을 듯싶습니다."], ["At any rate, I am sure he will let you know if he cannot.”", "어쨌든 못 가게 되면 반드시 알려 드릴 겁니다.”"]], [["“A strike!”", "“파업이라니요!”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“What for?", "“무엇 때문에요?"], ["What are they going to strike for?”", "무엇 때문에 파업을 하려는 건가요?”"]], [["“For the mastership and ownership of other people’s property,”", "“남의 재산을 다스리고 차지하려고요.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton, with a fierce snort.", "손턴 부인이 사납게 코웃음을 치며 말했다."], ["“That is what they always strike for.", "“늘 그것 때문에 파업을 하지요."], ["If my son’s work-people strike, I will only say they are a pack of ungrateful hounds.", "제 아들의 직공들이 파업을 한다면 저는 그들이 은혜를 모르는 개떼라고밖에 말하지 않겠습니다."], ["But I have no doubt they will.”", "하지만 틀림없이 할 겁니다.”"]], [["“They are wanting higher wages, I suppose?”", "“임금을 더 달라는 것이겠지요?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["“That is the face of the thing.", "“겉으로야 그렇습니다."], ["But the truth is, they want to be masters, and make the masters into slaves on their own ground.", "그러나 실상은 저희가 주인이 되고 공장주들을 제 땅에서 종으로 만들고 싶은 것이지요. 늘 그것을 노립니다."], ["They are always trying at it; they always have it in their minds; and every five or six years, there comes a struggle between masters and men.", "늘 마음에 품고 있고요. 그래서 5, 6년마다 공장주와 직공들 사이에 싸움이 벌어집니다."], ["They’ll find themselves mistaken this time, I fancy,—a little out of their reckoning.", "이번에는 저희가 잘못 짚었다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다. 셈이 좀 어긋났다는 것을요."], ["If they turn out, they mayn’t find it so easy to go in again.", "일단 나가고 나면 다시 들어오기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 것도 알게 되겠지요."], ["I believe, the masters have a thing or two in their heads which will teach the men not to strike again in a hurry, if they try it this time.”", "이번에 저들이 해 본다면, 다시는 함부로 파업할 생각을 못 하도록 가르쳐 줄 무엇인가를 공장주들이 머릿속에 품고 있다고 저는 믿습니다.”"]], [["“Does it make the town very rough?”", "“그러면 시내가 아주 험악해지나요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Of course it does.", "“물론이지요."], ["But surely you are not a coward, are you?", "하지만 설마 겁쟁이는 아니시겠지요?"], ["Milton is not the place for cowards.", "밀턴은 겁쟁이가 살 곳이 아닙니다."], ["I have known the time when I have had to thread my way through a crowd of white, angry men, all swearing they would have Makinson’s blood as soon as he ventured to show his nose out of his factory; and he, knowing nothing of it, some one had to go and tell him, or he was a dead man; and it needed to be a woman,—so I went.", "매킨슨이 제 공장 밖으로 코빼기라도 내미는 즉시 그 피를 보겠다고 저마다 욕설을 퍼붓는, 얼굴이 하얗게 질린 성난 사내들의 무리를 헤치고 길을 뚫어야 했던 때를 나는 압니다. 그런데 그는 아무것도 모르고 있었으니 누군가 가서 일러 주어야 했지요."], ["And when I got in, I could not get out.", "그러지 않으면 죽은 목숨이었으니까요. 그리고 그 일은 여자가 해야만 했습니다."], ["It was as much as my life was worth.", "그래서 내가 갔지요. 그런데 들어가고 나니 나올 수가 없었습니다."], ["So I went up to the roof, where there were stones piled ready to drop on the heads of the crowd, if they tried to force the factory doors.", "목숨을 건 일이었지요. 그래서 나는 지붕으로 올라갔습니다."], ["And I would have lifted those heavy stones, and dropped them with as good an aim as the best man there, but that I fainted with the heat I had gone through.", "거기에는 무리가 공장 문을 부수려 들면 그 머리 위로 떨어뜨리려고 쌓아 둔 돌들이 있었어요. 그리고 나는 그 무거운 돌들을 들어 올려 거기 있는 어느 사내 못지않게 정확히 겨누어 떨어뜨렸을 겁니다."], ["If you live in Milton, you must learn to have a brave heart, Miss Hale.”", "그때까지 겪은 열기에 정신을 잃지만 않았더라면요. 밀턴에서 살려거든 용감한 마음을 지니는 법을 배우셔야 합니다, 헤일 양.”"]], [["“I would do my best,”", "“최선을 다하겠어요.”"], ["said Margaret rather pale.", "마거릿이 다소 창백해져 말했다."], ["“I do not know whether I am brave or not till I am tried; but I am afraid I should be a coward.”", "“겪어 보기 전에는 제가 용감한지 아닌지 모르겠어요. 하지만 아무래도 겁쟁이일 것 같아요.”"]], [["“South country people are often frightened by what our Darkshire men and women only call living and struggling.", "“남부 사람들은 우리 다크셔의 남녀가 그저 살아가고 버텨 나가는 일이라고 부르는 것에 곧잘 겁을 먹지요."], ["But when you’ve been ten years among a people who are always owing their betters a grudge, and only waiting for an opportunity to pay it off, you’ll know whether you are a coward or not, take my word for it.”", "하지만 늘 윗사람에게 앙심을 품고 그것을 갚을 기회만 노리는 사람들 틈에서 10년을 지내 보면, 자기가 겁쟁이인지 아닌지 알게 될 겁니다. 내 말을 믿으세요.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton came that evening to Mr. Hale’s. He was shown up into the drawing-room, where Mr. Hale was reading aloud to his wife and daughter.", "그날 저녁 손턴 씨가 헤일 씨의 집에 왔다. 그는 응접실로 안내되었는데, 마침 헤일 씨가 아내와 딸에게 소리 내어 책을 읽어 주고 있던 참이었다."]], [["“I am come partly to bring you a note from my mother, and partly to apologise for not keeping to my time yesterday.", "“어머니의 쪽지를 전해 드릴 겸, 어제 시간을 지키지 못한 것을 사과드릴 겸 왔습니다. 쪽지에 청하신 주소가 적혀 있습니다."], ["The note contains the address you asked for; Dr. Donaldson.”", "도널드슨 의사입니다.”"]], [["“Thank you!”", "“고맙습니다!”"], ["said Margaret, hastily, holding out her hand to take the note, for she did not wish her mother to hear that they had been making any inquiry about a doctor.", "마거릿이 서둘러 말하며 쪽지를 받으려 손을 내밀었다. 의사를 수소문했다는 이야기를 어머니가 듣기를 바라지 않았기 때문이다."], ["She was pleased that Mr.", "손턴 씨가 그 마음을 곧바로 알아차린 듯한 것이 반가웠다."], ["Thornton seemed immediately to understand her feeling; he gave her the note without another word of explanation.", "그는 아무 설명도 덧붙이지 않고 쪽지를 건네주었다."]], [["Mr. Hale began to talk about the strike.", "헤일 씨가 파업 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Mr. Thornton’s face assumed a likeness to his mother’s worst expression, which immediately repelled the watching Margaret.", "손턴 씨의 얼굴이 그 어머니의 가장 고약한 표정을 닮아 갔고, 지켜보던 마거릿은 곧바로 거부감을 느꼈다."]], [["“Yes; the fools will have a strike.", "“예, 그 어리석은 자들이 기어이 파업을 하겠답니다."], ["Let them.", "하라지요."], ["It suits us well enough.", "우리에게는 나쁠 것도 없습니다."], ["But we gave them a chance.", "그래도 우리는 기회를 주었지요."], ["They think trade is flourishing as it was last year.", "저들은 장사가 작년처럼 잘되는 줄 압니다."], ["We see the storm on the horizon and draw in our sails.", "우리는 수평선에 몰려오는 폭풍을 보고 돛을 거두고 있고요."], ["But because we don’t explain our reasons, they won’t believe we’re acting reasonably.", "그런데 우리가 까닭을 설명하지 않는다고 해서 저들은 우리가 사리에 맞게 행동하고 있다는 것을 믿으려 들지 않습니다."], ["We must give them line and letter for the way we choose to spend or save our money.", "우리가 우리 돈을 어떻게 쓰든 어떻게 아끼든 저들에게 조목조목 근거를 대야 한다는 겁니다."], ["Henderson tried a dodge with his men, out at Ashley, and failed.", "애슐리의 헨더슨이 제 직공들에게 수를 써 보았다가 실패했지요."], ["He rather wanted a strike; it would have suited his book well enough.", "그는 오히려 파업을 바랐습니다."], ["So, when the men came to ask for the five per cent.", "그편이 그의 장부에는 알맞았으니까요."], ["they are claiming, he told ’em he’d think about it, and give them his answer on the pay day; knowing all the while what his answer would be, of course, but thinking he’d strengthen their conceit of their own way.", "그래서 직공들이 요구하는 5퍼센트를 달라고 찾아오자, 생각해 보고 봉급날에 답을 주겠다고 했습니다. 물론 제 답이 무엇일지 내내 알면서도, 저들이 제 방식에 우쭐해지도록 부추기려던 것이었지요."], ["However, they were too deep for him, and heard something about the bad prospects of trade.", "그런데 저들이 그보다 한 수 위여서, 장사 형편이 나빠진다는 이야기를 어디서 들었지 뭡니까."], ["So in they came on the Friday, and drew back their claim, and now he’s obliged to go on working.", "그래서 금요일에 몰려와 요구를 거두어들였고, 이제 그는 어쩔 수 없이 계속 공장을 돌려야 하게 되었습니다."], ["But we Milton masters have to-day sent in our decision.", "그러나 우리 밀턴 공장주들은 오늘 우리 결정을 통보했습니다."], ["We won’t advance a penny.", "한 푼도 올리지 않겠다고요."], ["We tell them we may have to lower wages; but can’t afford to raise.", "임금을 내려야 할지도 모르지만 올릴 여력은 없다고 말했습니다."], ["So here we stand, waiting for their next attack.”", "그러니 이제 우리는 여기 서서 저들의 다음 공격을 기다리는 겁니다.”"]], [["“And what will that be?”", "“그것이 무엇이겠습니까?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["“I conjecture a simultaneous strike.", "“동시 파업이라고 짐작합니다."], ["You will see Milton without smoke in a few days, I imagine, Miss Hale.”", "며칠 안에 연기 없는 밀턴을 보시게 될 겁니다, 헤일 양.”"]], [["“But why,”", "“하지만 어째서,”"], ["asked she, “could you not explain what good reason you had for expecting a bad trade?", "그녀가 물었다. “장사가 나빠지리라 내다보시는 그럴 만한 까닭을 설명해 주실 수는 없나요?"], ["I don’t know whether I use the right words, but you will understand what I mean.”", "제가 알맞은 말을 쓰고 있는지는 모르겠지만, 무슨 뜻인지는 아시겠지요.”"]], [["“Do you give your servants reasons for your expenditure, or your economy in the use of your own money?", "“댁의 하인들에게 씀씀이나 절약에 대해 까닭을 대십니까?"], ["We, the owners of capital, have a right to choose what we will do with it.”", "우리, 자본을 가진 사람들에게는 그것으로 무엇을 할지 고를 권리가 있습니다.”"]], [["“A human right,”", "“인간의 권리지요.”"], ["said Margaret, very low.", "마거릿이 아주 낮게 말했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, I did not hear what you said.”", "“죄송합니다, 뭐라고 하셨는지 못 들었습니다.”"]], [["“I would rather not repeat it,”", "“되풀이하고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["said she; “it related to a feeling which I do not think you would share.”", "그녀가 말했다. “선생님께서는 함께 느끼지 않으실 감정에 관한 것이었으니까요.”"]], [["“Won’t you try me?”", "“한번 저를 시험해 보시지 않겠습니까?”"], ["pleaded he; his thoughts suddenly bent upon learning what she had said.", "그가 청했다. 그녀가 한 말을 알아내는 데 갑자기 온 생각이 쏠린 것이었다."], ["She was displeased with his pertinacity, but did not choose to affix too much importance to her words.", "그녀는 그의 집요함이 언짢았으나, 제 말에 지나친 무게를 실어 주고 싶지도 않았다."]], [["“I said you had a human right.", "“인간의 권리라고 말했어요."], ["I meant that there seemed no reason but religious ones, why you should not do what you like with your own.”", "종교적인 까닭 말고는, 선생님이 선생님의 것으로 마음대로 하지 못할 이유가 없어 보인다는 뜻이었어요.”"]], [["“I know we differ in our religious opinions; but don’t you give me credit for having some, though not the same as yours?”", "“우리가 종교적 견해에서 다르다는 것은 압니다. 하지만 제게도, 비록 헤일 양과 같지는 않을지언정 나름의 견해가 있다고는 인정해 주시지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["He was speaking in a subdued voice, as if to her alone.", "그는 마치 그녀에게만 말하듯 낮춘 목소리로 말하고 있었다."], ["She did not wish to be so exclusively addressed.", "그녀는 그렇게 자기만 두고 이야기하는 것이 싫었다."], ["She replied out in her usual tone:", "그녀는 여느 때의 목소리로 또렷하게 대답했다."]], [["“I do not think that I have any occasion to consider your special religious opinions in the affair.", "“이 일에서 선생님의 특별한 종교적 견해를 헤아려야 할 까닭은 없다고 생각해요."], ["All I meant to say is, that there is no human law to prevent the employers from utterly wasting or throwing away all their money, if they choose; but that there are passages in the Bible which would rather imply—to me at least—that they neglected their duties as stewards if they did so.", "제가 말하려던 것은 그저, 고용주가 마음만 먹으면 제 돈을 죄다 허비하거나 내던져 버리는 것을 막을 인간의 법은 없지만, 그렇게 하면 청지기로서의 의무를 저버리는 것이라고 — 적어도 제게는 그렇게 — 넌지시 일러 주는 구절들이 성경에 있다는 것이었어요."], ["However, I know so little about strikes and rate of wages, and capital, and labour, that I had better not talk to a political economist like you.”", "하지만 저는 파업이니 임금 수준이니 자본이니 노동이니 하는 것을 너무나 모르니, 선생님 같은 정치경제학자와는 이야기하지 않는 편이 낫겠네요.”"]], [["“Nay, the more reason,”", "“아니요, 오히려 더 그러셔야 할 까닭이지요.”"], ["said he eagerly.", "그가 열을 내며 말했다."], ["“I shall only be too glad to explain to you all that may seem anomalous or mysterious to a stranger; especially at a time like this, when our doings are sure to be canvassed by every scribbler who can hold a pen.”", "“낯선 사람에게 이상하거나 알 수 없어 보이는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 설명해 드리겠습니다. 특히 지금처럼 펜을 잡을 줄 아는 글쟁이라면 누구나 우리가 하는 일을 물고 늘어질 시기에는요.”"]], [["“Thank you,” she answered, coldly.", "“고맙습니다.”"], ["“Of course, I shall apply to my father in the first instance for any information he can give me, if I get puzzled with living here amongst this strange society.”", "그녀가 차갑게 대답했다. “물론 이 낯선 사회에서 살아가며 어리둥절해질 때면 저는 우선 아버지께 여쭈어 알 수 있는 것을 얻겠지만요.”"]], [["“You think it strange.", "“낯설다고 생각하시는군요."], ["Why?”", "어째서요?”"]], [["“I don’t know—I suppose because, on the very face of it, I see two classes dependent on each other in every possible way, yet each evidently regarding the interests of the other as opposed to their own: I never lived in a place before where there were two sets of people always running each other down.”", "“모르겠어요. 아마도 겉으로 보기에만도 서로 온갖 방식으로 기대고 있는 두 계급이 보이는데, 저마다 상대의 이익을 제 이익과 맞선 것으로 여기는 것이 분명해 보이기 때문일 거예요. 두 무리의 사람들이 늘 서로를 헐뜯는 곳에서는 살아 본 적이 없어서요.”"]], [["“Who have you heard running the masters down?", "“공장주들을 헐뜯는 이야기를 누구에게서 들으셨습니까?"], ["I don’t ask who you have heard abusing the men; for I see you persist in misunderstanding what I said the other day.", "직공들을 욕하는 이야기를 누구에게서 들으셨는지는 묻지 않겠습니다. 요전 날 제가 한 말을 여전히 오해하고 계신 것이 보이니까요."], ["But who have you heard abusing the masters?”", "그러나 공장주들을 욕하는 이야기는 누구에게서 들으셨습니까?”"]], [["Margaret reddened; then smiled as she said,", "마거릿이 붉어졌다가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I am not fond of being catechised.", "“저는 심문받는 것을 좋아하지 않아요."], ["I refuse to answer your question.", "그 물음에는 대답하지 않겠어요."], ["Besides, it has nothing to do with the fact.", "게다가 그건 사실과 아무 상관이 없고요. 제 말을 그냥 믿으셔야 해요."], ["You must take my word for it, that I have heard some people, or, it may be, only some one of the workpeople speak as though it were the interest of the employers to keep them from acquiring money—that it would make them too independent if they had a sum in the savings’ bank.”", "저는 어떤 사람들이, 어쩌면 직공들 가운데 어느 한 사람이었을지도 모르지만, 그들이 돈을 모으지 못하게 하는 것이 고용주들에게 이롭다는 듯이 말하는 것을 들었어요. 저축은행에 돈이 좀 쌓이면 그들이 너무 자립하게 되리라고요.”"]], [["“I dare say it was that man Higgins who told you all this,”", "“틀림없이 그 히긴스라는 사람이 네게 이런 이야기를 다 했겠구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."], ["Mr. Thornton did not appear to hear what Margaret evidently did not wish him to know.", "손턴 씨는 마거릿이 자기에게 알리고 싶어 하지 않는 것을 듣지 못한 척했다."], ["But he caught it nevertheless.", "그러나 그럼에도 그것을 놓치지 않았다."]], [["“I heard, moreover, that it was considered to the advantage of the masters to have ignorant workmen—not hedge-lawyers, as Captain Lennox used to call those men in his company who questioned and would know the reason for every order.”", "“게다가 무지한 직공을 두는 것이 공장주들에게 이롭다고들 여긴다는 이야기도 들었어요. 명령마다 캐묻고 그 까닭을 알려 드는 제 중대의 사내들을 레녹스 대위가 부르던 말로 하자면, 어설픈 법률가가 아닌 사람들 말이에요.”"]], [["This latter part of her sentence she addressed rather to her father than to Mr.", "문장의 이 뒷부분을 그녀는 손턴 씨보다 아버지 쪽에 대고 말했다."], ["Thornton. Who is Captain Lennox?", "레녹스 대위가 누구지?"], ["asked Mr. Thornton of himself, with a strange kind of displeasure, that prevented him for the moment from replying to her!", "손턴 씨는 야릇한 종류의 불쾌감과 함께 스스로에게 물었고, 그 때문에 잠시 그녀에게 대꾸하지 못했다."], ["Her father took up the conversation.", "그녀의 아버지가 이야기를 이어받았다."]], [["“You never were fond of schools, Margaret, or you would have seen and known before this how much is being done for education in Milton.”", "“너는 학교에 마음을 둔 적이 없지, 마거릿. 그렇지 않았다면 밀턴에서 교육을 위해 얼마나 많은 일이 이루어지고 있는지 벌써 보고 알았을 텐데.”"]], [["“No!” she said, with sudden meekness.", "“그러네요!”"], ["“I know I do not care enough about schools.", "그녀가 갑자기 온순해져 말했다."], ["But the knowledge and the ignorance of which I was speaking did not relate to reading and writing,—the teaching or information one can give to a child.", "“제가 학교에 충분히 마음을 두지 않는다는 건 알아요. 하지만 제가 말한 앎과 무지는 읽고 쓰기에 관한 게 아니었어요."], ["I am sure, that what was meant was ignorance of the wisdom that shall guide men and women.", "아이에게 줄 수 있는 가르침이나 지식 말이에요. 분명 그 말뜻은 남녀를 이끌어 갈 지혜에 대한 무지였어요."], ["I hardly know what that is.", "그게 무엇인지는 저도 잘 모르겠어요."], ["But he—that is my informant—spoke as if the masters would like their hands to be merely tall, large children—living in the present moment—with a blind unreasoning kind of obedience.”", "하지만 그 사람은, 그러니까 제게 말해 준 사람은, 공장주들이 자기 직공들을 그저 키만 크고 몸집만 큰 아이들로 두고 싶어 한다는 듯이 말했어요. 지금 이 순간만 살아가면서 눈멀고 따지지 않는 복종을 하는 아이들로요.”"]], [["“In short, Miss Hale, it is very evident that your informant found a pretty ready listener to all the slander he chose to utter against the masters,”", "“요컨대, 헤일 양, 헤일 양께 그런 이야기를 해 준 사람은 공장주들을 헐뜯으려고 늘어놓는 온갖 험담을 아주 선뜻 들어 줄 귀를 찾아냈던 것이 분명하군요.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, in an offended tone.", "손턴 씨가 기분 상한 어조로 말했다."]], [["Margaret did not reply.", "마거릿은 대답하지 않았다."], ["She was displeased at the personal character Mr. Thornton affixed to what she had said.", "손턴 씨가 제 말에 인신공격의 성격을 붙인 것이 언짢았다."]], [["Mr. Hale spoke next:", "다음으로 헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I must confess that, although I have not become so intimately acquainted with any workmen as Margaret has, I am very much struck by the antagonism between the employer and the employed, on the very surface of things.", "“고백하건대, 저는 마거릿만큼 어느 직공과도 가깝게 알고 지내게 되지는 않았습니다만, 사물의 겉면에서부터 고용하는 쪽과 고용되는 쪽 사이의 반목이 몹시 눈에 띄더군요."], ["I even gather this impression from what you yourself have from time to time said.”", "손턴 씨가 이따금 하신 말씀에서도 저는 그런 인상을 받습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton paused awhile before he spoke.", "손턴 씨는 입을 열기 전에 잠시 뜸을 들였다."], ["Margaret had just left the room, and he was vexed at the state of feeling between himself and her.", "마거릿이 방금 방을 나간 참이었고, 그는 자기와 그녀 사이의 감정 상태에 속이 상해 있었다."], ["However, the little annoyance, by making him cooler and more thoughtful, gave a greater dignity to what he said:", "그러나 그 작은 언짢음이 그를 더 차분하고 사려 깊게 만들어, 그의 말에 한결 더한 품위를 실어 주었다."]], [["“My theory is, that my interests are identical with those of my workpeople, and vice-versa.", "“제 이론은 제 이익이 제 직공들의 이익과 하나이며 그 반대도 마찬가지라는 것입니다."], ["Miss Hale, I know, does not like to hear men called ‘hands,’ so I won’t use that word, though it comes most readily to my lips as the technical term, whose origin, whatever it was, dates before my time.", "헤일 양이 사람을 ‘직공’이라 부르는 것을 듣기 싫어한다는 것을 알기에 그 말은 쓰지 않겠습니다. 제 입에는 그것이 가장 쉽게 오르는 전문 용어이고, 그 유래가 무엇이든 제가 태어나기 전부터 있던 말이지만요."], ["On some future day—in some millennium—in Utopia, this unity may be brought into practice—just as I can fancy a republic the most perfect form of government.”", "언젠가, 어느 천년왕국에서, 유토피아에서라면 이 하나 됨이 실제로 이루어질지도 모르겠습니다. 제가 공화정을 가장 완전한 정치 형태로 상상할 수 있는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로요.”"]], [["“We will read Plato’s Republic as soon as we have finished Homer.”", "“호메로스를 마치는 대로 플라톤의 국가론을 읽읍시다.”"]], [["“Well, in the Platonic year, it may fall out that we are all—men, women, and children—fit for a republic; but give me a constitutional monarchy in our present state of morals and intelligence.", "“글쎄요, 플라톤의 대년(大年)에는 우리 모두가 — 남자도 여자도 아이도 — 공화정에 어울리게 될 수도 있겠지요. 그러나 지금 우리의 도덕과 지성 상태에서라면 저는 입헌군주정을 택하겠습니다."], ["In our infancy we require a wise despotism to govern us.", "어릴 적에는 우리를 다스릴 현명한 전제가 필요합니다. 실은 어린 시절이 한참 지난 뒤에도 아이들과 젊은이들은 분별 있고 단호한 권위의 흔들림 없는 법 아래에서 가장 행복하지요."], ["Indeed, long past infancy, children and young people are the happiest under the unfailing laws of a discreet firm authority.", "저는 우리 사람들이 아이의 처지에 있다고 보는 데까지는 헤일 양과 뜻을 같이합니다. 다만 우리 공장주들이 그들을 그렇게 만들거나 그렇게 붙들어 두는 데 무슨 관계가 있다는 것은 부인합니다."], ["I agree with Miss Hale so far as to consider our people in the condition of children, while I deny that we, the masters, have anything to do with the making or keeping them so.", "저는 그들에게는 전제가 가장 좋은 통치 방식이라고 주장합니다. 그러므로 제가 그들과 마주하는 시간 동안 저는 필연코 전제군주일 수밖에 없습니다."], ["I maintain that despotism is the best kind of government for them; so that in the hours in which I come in contact with them I must necessarily be an autocrat.", "저는 제 최선의 분별을 쓰겠습니다. 북부에 다소 지나치게 많았던 그 겉치레나 박애 감정에서가 아니라, 제 사업을 이끌어 가는 데 현명한 법을 세우고 올바른 결정을 내리기 위해서요."], ["I will use my best discretion—from no humbug or philanthropic feeling, of which we have had rather too much in the North—to make wise laws and come to just decisions in the conduct of my business—laws and decisions which work for my own good in the first instance—for theirs in the second; but I will neither be forced to give my reasons, nor flinch from what I have once declared to be my resolution.", "첫째로는 제 자신의 이익을 위해, 둘째로는 그들의 이익을 위해 움직이는 법과 결정 말입니다. 그러나 저는 제 까닭을 대라고 강요당하지도 않을 것이고, 한번 제 결심이라고 밝힌 것에서 물러서지도 않을 것입니다."], ["Let them turn out!", "나가라지요!"], ["I shall suffer as well as they: but in the end they will find I have not bated nor altered one jot.”", "저도 그들 못지않게 고통을 겪을 것입니다. 그러나 끝내 그들은 제가 한 치도 깎지 않았고 바꾸지도 않았음을 알게 될 것입니다.”"]], [["Margaret had re-entered the room and was sitting at her work; but she did not speak.", "마거릿이 방에 다시 들어와 바느질감을 들고 앉아 있었으나 말은 하지 않았다."], ["Mr. Hale answered—", "헤일 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“I dare say I am talking in great ignorance; but from the little I know, I should say that the masses were already passing rapidly into the troublesome stage which intervenes between childhood and manhood, in the life of the multitude as well as that of the individual.", "“제가 아주 모르면서 하는 말인지도 모르겠습니다만, 아는 것이 얼마 없는 대로 보건대 대중은 이미 빠르게 그 성가신 단계로 접어들고 있는 듯합니다. 개인의 삶에서든 무리의 삶에서든 어린 시절과 어른 시절 사이에 끼어드는 그 단계 말입니다."], ["Now, the error which many parents commit in the treatment of the individual at this time is, insisting on the same unreasoning obedience as when all he had to do in the way of duty was, to obey the simple laws of ‘Come when you’re called,’ and ‘Do as you’re bid!’ But a wise parent humours the desire for independent action, so as to become the friend and adviser when his absolute rule shall cease.", "그런데 이때 개인을 다루면서 많은 부모가 저지르는 잘못은, ‘부르면 오너라’, ‘시키는 대로 하여라’ 하는 단순한 법을 따르는 것이 의무의 전부였을 때와 똑같이 따지지 않는 복종을 고집한다는 것이지요. 그러나 현명한 부모는 스스로 하고자 하는 마음을 어루만져 주어, 제 절대적인 다스림이 끝났을 때 벗이자 조언자가 되는 법입니다."], ["If I get wrong in my reasoning, recollect, it is you who adopted the analogy.”", "제 논리가 어긋난다면, 그 비유를 끌어들인 것은 손턴 씨라는 걸 기억해 주십시오.”"]], [["“Very lately,”", "“아주 최근에,”"], ["said Margaret, “I heard a story of what happened in Nuremberg only three or four years ago.", "마거릿이 말했다. “불과 3, 4년 전 뉘른베르크에서 있었던 일에 대한 이야기를 들었어요."], ["A rich man there lived alone in one of the immense mansions which were formerly both dwellings and warehouses.", "그곳의 어느 부자가 예전에는 살림집이자 창고이기도 했던 거대한 저택 가운데 하나에서 혼자 살았대요."], ["It was reported that he had a child, but no one knew of it for certain.", "그에게 자식이 하나 있다는 소문이 돌았지만 아무도 확실히 알지는 못했고요."], ["For forty years this rumour kept rising and falling—never utterly dying away.", "40년 동안 그 소문은 일었다 잦아들었다 하면서 아주 사라지지는 않았어요."], ["After his death it was found to be true.", "그가 죽은 뒤에야 그것이 사실로 드러났지요. 그에게는 아들이 있었어요."], ["He had a son—an overgrown man, with the unexercised intellect of a child, whom he had kept up in that strange way, in order to save him from temptation and error.", "몸만 자란 사내였는데 지성은 쓰인 적 없는 아이의 것이었어요. 아버지가 유혹과 잘못에서 지켜 주려고 그런 이상한 방식으로 가두어 키운 것이었지요."], ["But, of course, when this great old child was turned loose in the world, every bad counsellor had power over him.", "하지만 물론 이 다 큰 아이가 세상에 풀려나자 온갖 나쁜 조언자가 그를 마음대로 휘둘렀어요."], ["He did not know good from evil.", "그는 선과 악을 가릴 줄 몰랐어요."], ["His father had made the blunder of bringing him up in ignorance and taking it for innocence; and after fourteen months of riotous living, the city authorities had to take charge of him, in order to save him from starvation.", "아버지는 그를 무지 속에서 키우고는 그것을 순진함으로 착각하는 잘못을 저질렀던 거예요. 그리고 14개월 동안 방탕하게 산 끝에, 그가 굶어 죽지 않도록 시 당국이 그를 맡아야 했지요."], ["He could not even use words effectively enough to be a successful beggar.”", "그는 구걸을 제대로 해낼 만큼도 말을 부릴 줄 몰랐어요.”"]], [["“I used the comparison (suggested by Miss Hale) of the position of the master to that of a parent, so I ought not to complain of your turning the simile into a weapon against me.", "“저는 (헤일 양이 일러 준) 공장주의 처지를 부모의 처지에 견주는 비유를 썼으니, 그 비유를 저를 치는 무기로 삼으신다고 해서 불평해서는 안 되겠지요."], ["But, Mr. Hale, when you were setting up a wise parent as a model for us, you said he humoured his children in their desire for independent action.", "하지만 헤일 씨, 현명한 부모를 우리의 본보기로 세우시면서 그런 부모는 자식들이 스스로 하고자 하는 마음을 어루만져 준다고 하셨습니다."], ["Now certainly, the time is not come for the hands to have any independent action during business hours; I hardly know what you would mean by it then.", "그런데 분명히 말씀드리지만, 일하는 시간 동안 직공들이 무엇이든 스스로 알아서 할 때는 오지 않았습니다. 그때 그것이 무슨 뜻일지 저로서는 짐작조차 어렵습니다."], ["And I say, that the masters would be trenching on the independence of their hands, in a way that I, for one, should not feel justified in doing, if we interfered too much with the life they lead out of the mills.", "그리고 제가 말씀드리건대, 우리가 그들이 공장 밖에서 사는 삶에 지나치게 참견한다면 그것은 공장주가 직공들의 자립을 침범하는 일이 될 것이고, 적어도 저로서는 그것이 정당하다고 느끼지 못할 것입니다."], ["Because they labour ten hours a-day for us, I do not see that we have any right to impose leading-strings upon them for the rest of their time.", "그들이 우리를 위해 하루에 10시간 일한다고 해서 나머지 시간까지 우리가 그들에게 걸음마 끈을 채울 권리가 있다고는 보지 않으니까요."], ["I value my own independence so highly that I can fancy no degradation greater than that of having another man perpetually directing and advising and lecturing me, or even planning too closely in any way about my actions.", "저는 제 자신의 자립을 몹시 소중히 여기기에, 다른 사람이 끊임없이 제 행동을 지시하고 충고하고 훈계하는 것, 심지어 어떤 식으로든 너무 세세히 계획해 주는 것보다 더 큰 굴욕은 상상할 수 없습니다."], ["He might be the wisest of men or the most powerful—I should equally rebel and resent his interference.", "그가 세상에서 가장 현명한 사람이든 가장 힘 있는 사람이든, 저는 똑같이 반발하고 그 간섭에 분개할 것입니다."], ["I imagine this is a stronger feeling in the North of England than in the South.”", "이런 감정은 잉글랜드 남부보다 북부에서 더 강하리라 짐작합니다.”"]], [["“I beg your pardon, but is not that because there has been none of the equality of friendship between the adviser and advised classes?", "“실례입니다만, 그것은 조언하는 계급과 조언받는 계급 사이에 벗으로서의 대등함이 전혀 없었기 때문 아닐까요?"], ["Because every man has had to stand in an unchristian and isolated position, apart from and jealous of his brother-man: constantly afraid of his rights being trenched upon?”", "사람마다 제 형제 된 사람과 떨어져 그를 시샘하는, 그리스도인답지 못하고 외따로 떨어진 자리에 서야 했기 때문에요. 제 권리가 침범당할까 늘 두려워하면서요.”"]], [["“I only state the fact.", "“저는 사실만을 말씀드립니다. 안타깝게도 8시에 약속이 있어서, 오늘 밤은 사실을 있는 그대로 받아들일 뿐 그 까닭을 밝히려 애쓰지는 못하겠습니다."], ["I am sorry to say, I have an appointment at eight o’clock, and I must just take facts as I find them to-night, without trying to account for them; which, indeed, would make no difference in determining how to act as things stand—the facts must be granted.”", "사실 그것을 밝힌들 지금 형편에서 어떻게 행동할지를 정하는 데는 아무런 차이도 없을 테고요. 사실은 사실대로 인정해야 하니까요.”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["said Margaret in a low voice, “it seems to me that it makes all the difference in the world—.”", "마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “제게는 그것이 세상의 온갖 차이를 만드는 것처럼 보이는데요.”"], ["Her father made a sign to her to be silent, and allow Mr. Thornton to finish what he had to say.", "아버지가 그녀에게 조용히 하고 손턴 씨가 할 말을 마치게 하라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["He was already standing up and preparing to go.", "그는 이미 일어서서 갈 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["“You must grant me this one point.", "“이 한 가지는 인정해 주셔야 합니다."], ["Given a strong feeling of independence in every Darkshire man, have I any right to obtrude my views, of the manner in which he shall act, upon another (hating it as I should do most vehemently myself), merely because he has labour to sell and I capital to buy?”", "다크셔 사람이라면 누구에게나 강한 자립심이 있다고 할 때, 그가 어떻게 행동해야 하는가에 대한 제 견해를 다른 사람에게 들이밀 권리가 제게 있습니까? 제 자신이라면 그런 일을 더없이 격렬하게 싫어할 텐데, 그저 그에게는 팔 노동이 있고 제게는 살 자본이 있다는 이유만으로 말입니다.”"]], [["“Not in the least,”", "“조금도 없어요.”"], ["said Margaret, determined to say just this one thing; “not in the least because of your labour and capital positions, whatever they are, but because you are a man, dealing with a set of men over whom you have, whether you reject the use of it or not, immense power, just because your lives and your welfare are so constantly and intimately interwoven.", "마거릿이 이 한마디만은 하겠다고 마음먹고 말했다. “선생님의 노동과 자본의 처지가 어떻든 그것 때문이 아니라, 선생님이 한 사람의 인간으로서 여러 사람을 상대하고 계시기 때문에 조금도 없는 거예요."], ["God has made us so that we must be mutually dependent.", "선생님이 그 힘을 쓰기를 마다하시든 아니든, 선생님은 그들에게 엄청난 힘을 지니고 계세요. 바로 선생님의 삶과 안녕이 그들의 것과 그토록 끊임없이 그리고 깊이 얽혀 있기 때문이지요."], ["We may ignore our own dependence, or refuse to acknowledge that others depend upon us in more respects than the payment of weekly wages; but the thing must be, nevertheless.", "하느님은 우리가 서로에게 기대며 살도록 우리를 만드셨어요. 우리는 우리 자신이 기대고 있음을 모른 척할 수도 있고, 주급을 치르는 일 말고도 여러 면에서 남들이 우리에게 기대고 있음을 인정하지 않을 수도 있어요."], ["Neither you nor any other master can help yourselves.", "그래도 그것은 엄연한 사실이에요. 선생님도 다른 어느 공장주도 어쩌지 못하는 일이지요."], ["The most proudly independent man depends on those around him for their insensible influence on his character—his life.", "가장 자랑스럽게 홀로 선 사람도 제 성품과 제 삶에 미치는 주위 사람들의 알 수 없는 영향에 기대고 있어요. 그리고 다크셔의 그 온갖 자아 가운데 가장 외따로 선 자아에게도 사방에서 매달리는 사람들이 있어요."], ["And the most isolated of all your Darkshire Egos has dependants clinging to him on all sides; he cannot shake them off, any more than the great rock he resembles can shake off—”", "그는 그들을 떨쳐 낼 수 없어요. 그가 닮은 그 커다란 바위가 떨쳐 낼 수 없듯이……”"]], [["“Pray don’t go into similes, Margaret; you have led us off once already,”", "“비유는 그만두어라, 마거릿. 벌써 한 번 우리를 옆길로 이끌지 않았니.”"], ["said her father, smiling, yet uneasy at the thought that they were detaining Mr.", "아버지가 웃으며 말했으나, 손턴 씨를 그의 뜻과 달리 붙들고 있다는 생각에 마음이 편치 않았다. 그것은 오해였다."], ["Thornton against his will, which was a mistake; for he rather liked it, as long as Margaret would talk, although what she said only irritated him.", "마거릿이 이야기를 하는 한 그는 오히려 그것이 좋았기 때문이다. 그녀가 하는 말이 그를 짜증 나게 할 뿐이었는데도."]], [["“Just tell me, Miss Hale, are you yourself ever influenced—no, that is not a fair way of putting it;—but if you are ever conscious of being influenced by others, and not by circumstances, have those others been working directly or indirectly? Have they been labouring to exhort, to enjoin, to act rightly for the sake of example, or have they been simple, true men, taking up their duty, and doing it unflinchingly, without a thought of how their actions were to make this man industrious, that man saving? Why, if I were a workman, I should be twenty times more impressed by the knowledge that my master was honest, punctual, quick, resolute in all his doings (and hands are keener spies even than valets), than by any amount of interference, however kindly meant, with my ways of going on out of work-hours. I do not choose to think too closely on what I am myself; but, I believe, I rely on the straightforward honesty of my hands, and the open nature of their opposition, in contra-distinction to the way in which the turn-out will be managed in some mills, just because they know I scorn to take a single dishonourable advantage, or do an underhand thing myself. It goes farther than a whole course of lectures on ‘Honesty is the Best Policy’—life diluted into words. No, no! What the master is, that will the men be, without over-much taking thought on his part.”", "“한 가지만 말씀해 주십시오, 헤일 양. 헤일 양 자신은 누군가에게 영향을 받은 적이 — 아니, 그렇게 묻는 것은 공정하지 않겠군요 — 그러니까 사정 때문이 아니라 다른 사람들 때문에 영향을 받고 있다고 느끼신 적이 있다면, 그 사람들은 직접 움직였습니까, 아니면 에둘러 움직였습니까? 그들이 타이르고 당부하고 본보기가 되려고 애써 올바르게 행동했습니까? 아니면 소박하고 참된 사람들로서 제 의무를 떠맡아 흔들림 없이 해냈을 뿐, 제 행동이 이 사람을 부지런하게 만들고 저 사람을 알뜰하게 만들지 어떨지는 생각조차 하지 않았습니까? 아니, 제가 직공이라면, 일하는 시간 밖의 제 살아가는 방식에 대한 어떤 참견보다도 — 아무리 좋은 뜻이라 해도 — 제 주인이 하는 모든 일에서 정직하고 시간을 지키고 재빠르고 단호하다는 것을 아는 데서 스무 배는 더 깊은 감명을 받을 것입니다. 직공은 시종보다도 눈이 밝은 염탐꾼이니까요. 저는 제 자신이 어떤 사람인지 너무 세세히 따져 볼 마음은 없습니다. 그러나 저는 제 직공들의 올곧은 정직함과 그들이 맞서는 방식의 떳떳함에 기대고 있다고 믿습니다. 어떤 공장에서 이번 파업이 다루어질 방식과는 사뭇 다르게 말이지요. 오로지 제가 단 한 번도 떳떳하지 못한 이득을 취하거나 뒷구멍으로 무슨 짓을 하는 것을 스스로 경멸한다는 것을 그들이 알기 때문입니다. 그것은 ‘정직이 최선의 방책’이라는 강의를 통째로 듣는 것보다 훨씬 멀리 갑니다. 말로 묽어진 삶보다 말이지요. 아니, 아닙니다! 공장주가 어떤 사람이냐, 그것이 곧 직공들이 어떤 사람이 되느냐입니다. 그가 지나치게 마음 쓰지 않아도 말입니다.”"]], [["“That is a great admission,”", "“대단한 인정이시네요.”"], ["said Margaret, laughing.", "마거릿이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“When I see men violent and obstinate, in pursuit of their rights, I may safely infer that the master is the same; that he is a little ignorant of that spirit which suffereth long, and is kind, and seeketh not her own.”", "“사내들이 제 권리를 좇아 사납고 고집스럽게 구는 것을 보면, 저는 공장주도 마찬가지라고 마음 놓고 미루어 짐작해도 되겠군요. 오래 참고 온유하며 제 것을 구하지 않는 그 마음을 그가 조금 모른다고요.”"]], [["“You are just like all strangers who don’t understand the working of our system, Miss Hale,”", "“헤일 양은 우리 제도가 어떻게 돌아가는지 모르는 낯선 사람들과 꼭 같으시군요.”"], ["said he, hastily.", "그가 서둘러 말했다."], ["“You suppose that our men are puppets of dough, ready to be moulded into any amiable form we please.", "“우리 직공들이 우리가 원하는 어떤 곱상한 모양으로든 빚어낼 수 있는 밀가루 반죽 인형이라고 생각하시는군요. 우리가 그들의 삶의 3분의 1도 안 되는 시간만 상대한다는 것을 잊고 계십니다."], ["You forget we have only to do with them for less than a third of their lives; and you seem not to perceive that the duties of a manufacturer are far larger and wider than those merely of an employer of labour: we have a wide commercial character to maintain, which makes us into the great pioneers of civilisation.”", "그리고 공장주의 의무가 그저 노동을 부리는 사람의 의무보다 훨씬 크고 넓다는 것을 알아보지 못하시는 듯합니다. 우리에게는 지켜 나가야 할 넓은 상업적 인격이 있고, 그것이 우리를 문명의 위대한 개척자로 만듭니다.”"]], [["“It strikes me,” said Mr.", "“제 생각에는,”"], ["Hale, smiling, “that you might pioneer a little at home.", "헤일 씨가 웃으며 말했다. “집안부터 조금 개척하셔도 좋을 듯합니다."], ["They are a rough, heathenish set of fellows, these Milton men of yours.”", "손턴 씨의 그 밀턴 사람들은 거칠고 이교도 같은 무리들이니까요.”"]], [["“They are that,”", "“그렇지요.”"], ["replied Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["“Rose-water surgery won’t do for them.", "“그들에게 장미수 같은 외과술은 통하지 않습니다."], ["Cromwell would have made a capital mill-owner, Miss Hale.", "크롬웰이라면 훌륭한 공장주가 되었을 겁니다, 헤일 양."], ["I wish we had him to put down this strike for us.”", "이 파업을 눌러 줄 크롬웰이 우리에게 있었으면 좋겠군요.”"]], [["“Cromwell is no hero of mine,”", "“크롬웰은 제 영웅이 아니에요.”"], ["she said, coldly.", "그녀가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“But I am trying to reconcile your admiration of despotism with your respect for other men’s independence of character.”", "“다만 저는 전제에 대한 선생님의 찬탄과 다른 사람의 자립적인 성품에 대한 존중을 어떻게 맞추어야 할지 애쓰고 있을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["He reddened at her tone.", "그는 그녀의 말투에 얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["“I choose to be the unquestioned and irresponsible master of my hands, during the hours they labour for me.", "“저는 그들이 저를 위해 일하는 시간 동안에는 제 직공들의, 따져 물을 수 없고 책임지지 않는 주인이기를 택합니다."], ["But those hours past, our relation ceases; and then comes in the same respect for their independence that I myself exact.”", "그러나 그 시간이 지나면 우리 관계는 끝나고, 그때는 제 자신이 요구하는 것과 똑같은 자립에 대한 존중이 들어섭니다.”"]], [["He did not speak again for a minute, he was too much vexed.", "그는 잠시 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 너무 속이 상했던 것이다."], ["But he shook it off, and bade Mr. and Mrs.", "그러나 그는 그것을 떨쳐 내고 헤일 씨 부부에게 밤 인사를 했다."], ["Hale good night. Then, drawing near to Margaret, he said in a lower voice—", "그러고는 마거릿에게 다가가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다."]], [["“I spoke hastily to you once this evening, and I am afraid, rather rudely.", "“오늘 저녁에 한 번 성급하게, 그리고 아무래도 다소 무례하게 말씀드렸군요."], ["But you know I am but an uncouth Milton manufacturer; will you forgive me?”", "하지만 아시다시피 저는 투박한 밀턴 공장주일 뿐입니다. 용서해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론이죠.”"], ["said she, smiling up in his face, the expression of which was somewhat anxious and oppressed, and hardly cleared away as he met her sweet sunny countenance, out of which all the north-wind effect of their discussion had entirely vanished.", "그녀가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 미소 지었다. 그 얼굴은 어딘가 근심스럽고 짓눌린 표정이었고, 두 사람의 논쟁이 남긴 북풍의 기색이 말끔히 사라진 그녀의 다정하고 환한 얼굴을 마주하고도 좀처럼 개지 않았다."], ["But she did not put out her hand to him, and again he felt the omission, and set it down to pride.", "그러나 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀지 않았고, 그는 다시 그 빠뜨림을 느끼고는 그것을 자존심 탓으로 돌렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI", "# 제16장"]], [["“Trust in that veiled hand, which leads None by the path that he would go; And always be for change prepared, For the world’s law is ebb and flow.”", "“가려진 그 손을 믿으라, 어느 누구도 스스로 가고자 하는 길로는 이끌지 않는 그 손을. 그리고 늘 변화에 대비하라, 세상의 법이란 밀물과 썰물이니.”"], ["FROM THE ARABIC.", "— 아라비아 시에서."]], [["The next afternoon Dr. Donaldson came to pay his first visit to Mrs.", "이튿날 오후 도널드슨 의사가 헤일 부인을 처음으로 진찰하러 왔다."], ["Hale. The mystery that Margaret hoped their late habits of intimacy had broken through was resumed.", "요사이 부쩍 가까워진 살림살이가 그 비밀스러움을 허물어 주었으려니 마거릿은 바랐지만, 비밀스러움은 다시 되살아났다."], ["She was excluded from the room, while Dixon was admitted.", "그녀는 방에서 밀려났고 딕슨이 대신 들어갔다."], ["Margaret was not a ready lover, but where she loved she loved passionately, and with no small degree of jealousy.", "마거릿은 쉬이 정을 주는 사람이 아니었으나, 한번 사랑하면 뜨겁게 사랑했고 거기에는 적잖은 질투심도 섞여 있었다."]], [["She went into her mother’s bed-room, just behind the drawing-room, and paced it up and down, while awaiting the doctor’s coming out.", "그녀는 응접실 바로 뒤에 있는 어머니의 침실로 들어가, 의사가 나오기를 기다리며 방 안을 서성거렸다."], ["Every now and then she stopped to listen; she fancied she heard a moan.", "이따금 걸음을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다. 신음 소리가 들리는 것만 같았다."], ["She clenched her hands tight, and held her breath.", "그녀는 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 숨을 죽였다."], ["She was sure she heard a moan.", "분명 신음 소리였다."], ["Then all was still for a few minutes more; and then there was the moving of chairs, the raised voices, all the little disturbances of leave-taking.", "그러고는 몇 분 더 모든 것이 조용했다. 이윽고 의자 움직이는 소리, 높아진 목소리, 작별 인사에 따르는 온갖 자잘한 부산함이 들려왔다."]], [["When she heard the door open, she went quickly out of the bedroom.", "문 열리는 소리가 나자 그녀는 얼른 침실에서 나왔다."]], [["“My father is from home, Dr.", "“아버지는 지금 집에 안 계세요, 도널드슨 선생님."], ["Donaldson; he has to attend a pupil at this hour.", "이 시간에는 학생을 가르치러 나가셔야 하거든요."], ["May I trouble you to come into his room downstairs?”", "번거로우시겠지만 아래층 아버지 방으로 내려가 주시겠어요?”"]], [["She saw, and triumphed over all the obstacles which Dixon threw in her way; assuming her rightful position as daughter of the house in something of the spirit of the Elder Brother, which quelled the old servant’s officiousness very effectually.", "딕슨이 앞을 가로막느라 놓는 온갖 훼방을 그녀는 알아차렸고, 또 이겨 냈다. 이 집 딸로서 마땅히 누릴 자리를 맏이의 기개 같은 것으로 내세우자 늙은 하녀의 참견은 아주 효험 있게 잦아들었다."], ["Margaret’s conscious assumption of this unusual dignity of demeanour towards Dixon, gave her an instant’s amusement in the midst of her anxiety.", "딕슨을 상대로 이 예사롭지 않은 위엄을 스스로 알면서 꾸며 보인 것이, 걱정 한복판에서 잠깐이나마 그녀를 재미있게 했다."], ["She knew, from the surprised expression on Dixon’s face, how ridiculously grand she herself must be looking; and the idea carried her downstairs into the room; it gave her that length of oblivion from the keen sharpness of the recollection of the actual business in hand.", "딕슨의 놀란 얼굴빛을 보고 그녀는 자기가 얼마나 우스꽝스럽도록 위풍당당해 보였을지 알 수 있었다. 그 생각에 실려 그녀는 아래층 방까지 내려갔고, 그 덕분에 지금 눈앞에 놓인 일에 대한 서슬 퍼런 기억을 그만큼 잊고 있을 수 있었다."], ["Now, that came back, and seemed to take away her breath.", "이제 그것이 되돌아와 숨을 앗아 가는 듯했다."], ["It was a moment or two before she could utter a word.", "한마디 말을 꺼내기까지 잠깐의 시간이 필요했다."]], [["But she spoke with an air of command, as she asked—", "그러나 물음을 던지는 그녀의 목소리에는 명령하는 기운이 서려 있었다."]], [["“What is the matter with mamma?", "“어머니가 어떠신가요?"], ["You will oblige me by telling the simple truth.”", "있는 그대로의 사실만 말씀해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.”"], ["Then, seeing a slight hesitation on the doctor’s part, she added—", "그러고는 의사가 살짝 머뭇거리는 것을 보고 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“I am the only child she has—here, I mean.", "“저는 어머니의 하나뿐인 자식이에요. 여기서는 그렇다는 말씀입니다."], ["My father is not sufficiently alarmed, I fear: and, therefore, if there is any serious apprehension, it must be broken to him gently.", "아버지는 아직 사태를 충분히 걱정하지 않으시는 듯해요. 그러니 정말 심각한 우려가 있다면 아버지께는 조심스럽게 알려 드려야 합니다."], ["I can do this.", "그 일은 제가 할 수 있어요."], ["I can nurse my mother.", "어머니 병간호도 제가 할 수 있고요."], ["Pray, speak, sir; to see your face, and not be able to read it, gives me a worse dread than I trust any words of yours will justify.”", "부디 말씀해 주세요, 선생님. 선생님 얼굴을 보면서도 그 뜻을 읽어 내지 못하는 것이, 선생님 입에서 나올 어떤 말보다도 저를 더 두렵게 합니다.”"]], [["“My dear young lady, your mother seems to have a most attentive and efficient servant, who is more like her friend——”", "“아가씨, 어머님께는 더없이 살뜰하고 유능한 하녀가 있는 것 같더군요. 하녀라기보다 벗에 가까운……”"]], [["“I am her daughter, sir.”", "“저는 어머니의 딸입니다, 선생님.”"]], [["“But when I tell you she expressly desired that you might not be told——”", "“하지만 어머님께서 아가씨에게는 알리지 말라고 분명히 당부하셨다고 말씀드린다면……”"]], [["“I am not good or patient enough to submit to the prohibition.", "“저는 그런 금지에 순순히 따를 만큼 착하지도 참을성 있지도 못합니다."], ["Besides, I am sure, you are too wise—too experienced to have promised to keep the secret.”", "게다가 선생님은 그 비밀을 지키겠다고 약속하시기에는 너무 지혜롭고 겪으신 일도 많은 분이라고 믿습니다.”"]], [["“Well,”", "“그렇군요.”"], ["said he, half-smiling, though sadly enough, “there you are right.", "그가 슬픔이 짙게 배어 있으면서도 반쯤 웃음을 띠며 말했다. “그 점은 아가씨 말이 옳습니다."], ["I did not promise.", "나는 약속하지 않았어요."], ["In fact, I fear, the secret will be known soon enough without my revealing it.”", "사실은 내가 밝히지 않더라도 그 비밀이 머잖아 저절로 알려질 것 같아 걱정입니다.”"]], [["He paused.", "그가 말을 멈추었다."], ["Margaret then went very white, and compressed her lips a little more.", "그러자 마거릿은 얼굴이 몹시 하얘지며 입술을 조금 더 굳게 다물었다."], ["Otherwise not a feature moved.", "그 밖에는 이목구비 하나 움직이지 않았다."], ["With the quick insight into character, without which no medical man can rise to the eminence of Dr.", "사람 됨됨이를 재빨리 꿰뚫어 보는 눈, 그것 없이는 어떤 의사도 도널드슨 의사만 한 명성에 오를 수 없는 그 눈으로 그는 알아보았다."], ["Donaldson, he saw that she would exact the full truth; that she would know if one iota was withheld; and that the withholding would be torture more acute than the knowledge of it.", "이 아가씨는 온전한 진실을 요구하리라는 것을, 티끌만 한 것 하나라도 감추면 알아채리라는 것을, 그리고 감추는 것이야말로 아는 것보다 더 지독한 고문이 되리라는 것을."], ["He spoke two short sentences in a low voice, watching her all the time; for the pupils of her eyes dilated into a black horror, and the whiteness of her complexion became livid.", "그는 낮은 목소리로 짧은 두 문장을 말하며 내내 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그녀의 눈동자는 검은 공포로 커졌고, 흰 낯빛은 납빛으로 질렸다."], ["He ceased speaking.", "그가 말을 그쳤다."], ["He waited for that look to go off,—for her gasping breath to come.", "그는 그 표정이 걷히기를, 그녀의 헐떡이는 숨결이 돌아오기를 기다렸다."], ["Then she said:—", "이윽고 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I thank you most truly, sir, for your confidence.", "“선생님, 이렇게 믿고 말씀해 주셔서 진심으로 감사합니다."], ["That dread has haunted me for many weeks.", "그 두려움은 몇 주째 저를 따라다녔어요."], ["It is a true, real agony.", "정말이지 참되고 실제적인 고통이었습니다."], ["My poor, poor mother!”", "가엾은, 가엾은 어머니!”"], ["Her lips now began to quiver, and he let her have the relief of tears, sure of her power of self-control to check them.", "이제 그녀의 입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 그는 그녀가 눈물로 마음을 덜어 내도록 내버려 두었다. 스스로 그것을 그칠 힘이 있는 사람임을 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["A few tears—those were all she shed, before she recollected the many questions she longed to ask.", "몇 방울의 눈물, 그것이 그녀가 흘린 전부였다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 묻고 싶던 숱한 물음들을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Will there be much suffering?”", "“많이 아프실까요?”"]], [["He shook his head.", "그가 고개를 저었다."], ["“That we cannot tell.", "“그건 우리도 알 수 없습니다."], ["It depends on constitution; on a thousand things.", "타고난 체질에 달렸고, 또 수천 가지 것들에 달렸지요."], ["But the late discoveries of medical science have given us large power of alleviation.”", "하지만 근래 의학이 알아낸 것들 덕분에 우리에게는 고통을 덜어 줄 힘이 꽤 커졌습니다.”"]], [["“My father!”", "“아버지는요!”"], ["said Margaret, trembling all over.", "마거릿이 온몸을 떨며 말했다."]], [["“I do not know Mr.", "“나는 헤일 씨를 잘 모릅니다. 그러니까 조언을 드리기가 어렵다는 말입니다."], ["Hale.", "그래도 이렇게는 말씀드리지요. 아가씨가 나에게서 억지로 끌어낸 그 앎을, 이토록 갑작스레 알게 된 그 앎을 견디며 버티세요."], ["I mean, it is difficult to give advice.", "내가 감출 수 없었던 그 사실이 아가씨에게 어느 정도 익숙해질 때까지 말입니다. 그래야 지나치게 애쓰지 않고도 아버님께 드릴 수 있는 위로를 드릴 수 있을 테니까요."], ["But I should say, bear on, with the knowledge you have forced me to give you so abruptly, till the fact which I could not withhold has become in some degree familiar to you, so that you may without too great an effort, be able to give what comfort you can to your father.", "그때가 오기 전에, 물론 나는 앞으로도 이따금 다시 찾아오겠습니다만, 고통을 덜어 드리는 것 말고는 아무것도 해 드릴 수 없을까 걱정이군요, 아무튼 그전에 사소한 일들이 수천 가지나 일어나 아버님의 불안을 깨우고 또 깊게 할 겁니다. 그러니 그만큼 마음의 준비가 되실 테지요."], ["Before then,—my visits, which of course, I shall repeat from time to time, although I fear I can do nothing but alleviate,—a thousand little circumstances will have occurred to awaken his alarm, to deepen it—so that he will be all the better prepared.—Nay, my dear young lady—nay, my dear—I saw Mr.", "아니요, 아가씨, 아닙니다, 그러지 마세요. 나는 손턴 씨를 만나 보았습니다."], ["Thornton, and I honour your father for the sacrifice he has made, however mistaken I may believe him to be.—Well, this once, if it will please you, my dear.", "그리고 아버님이 아무리 잘못 생각하신 것이라 내가 여기더라도, 그분이 치른 그 희생만은 존경합니다. 그래요, 이번 한 번은, 그것이 아가씨 마음에 든다면 그렇게 하지요."], ["Only remember, when I come again, I come as a friend.", "다만 기억해 두세요. 내가 다시 올 때는 벗으로서 오는 겁니다."], ["And you must learn to look upon me as such, because seeing each other—getting to know each other at such times as these, is worth years of morning calls.”", "그리고 아가씨도 나를 그런 사람으로 여기는 법을 익혀야 합니다. 이런 때에 서로를 보고 서로를 알아 가는 것은 여러 해 동안의 아침 인사보다 값진 일이니까요.”"]], [["Margaret could not speak for crying; but she wrang his hand at parting.", "마거릿은 울음 때문에 말을 할 수 없었다. 다만 헤어질 때 그의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“That’s what I call a fine girl!” thought Dr.", "“내가 말하는 훌륭한 아가씨란 바로 저런 사람이지!”"], ["Donaldson, when he was seated in his carriage, and had time to examine his ringed hand, which had slightly suffered from her pressure.", "도널드슨 의사는 마차에 올라앉아, 그녀의 악력에 조금 시달린 반지 낀 손을 살펴볼 여유가 생기자 이렇게 생각했다."], ["“Who would have thought that little hand could have given such a squeeze?", "“저 작은 손이 그토록 세게 쥘 수 있으리라고 누가 생각이나 했겠나?"], ["But the bones were well put together, and that gives immense power.", "그렇지만 뼈대가 야무지게 짜여 있으니 그것이 엄청난 힘을 내는 게지."], ["What a queen she is!", "참으로 여왕 같은 아가씨야!"], ["With her head thrown back at first, to force me into speaking the truth; and then bent so eagerly forward to listen.", "처음에는 나에게서 진실을 끌어내려고 고개를 뒤로 젖히더니, 그다음에는 들으려고 그토록 간절하게 몸을 앞으로 숙이더군."], ["Poor thing!", "가엾은 것!"], ["I must see she does not overstrain herself.", "저 아가씨가 제 몸을 너무 혹사하지 않도록 살펴야겠어."], ["Though it’s astonishing how much these thorough-bred creatures can do and suffer.", "하기야 이런 순종 같은 사람들이 얼마나 많은 일을 해내고 또 얼마나 견뎌 내는지는 놀랍기 그지없지."], ["That girl’s game to the back-bone.", "저 아가씨는 뼛속까지 배짱이 있어."], ["Another, who had gone that deadly colour, could never have come round without either fainting or hysterics.", "다른 사람이 그런 죽음 같은 낯빛이 되었다면 실신하거나 발작을 일으키지 않고서는 결코 제정신으로 돌아오지 못했을 게야."], ["But she wouldn’t do either—not she!", "그런데 저 아가씨는 둘 다 하지 않았어."], ["And the very force of her will brought her round.", "절대로! 오직 제 의지의 힘만으로 스스로를 되돌려 놓았지."], ["Such a girl as that would win my heart, if I were thirty years younger.", "저런 아가씨라면 내가 30년만 젊었어도 내 마음을 사로잡았을 텐데."], ["It’s too late now.", "이제는 너무 늦었지."], ["Ah; here we are at the Archers’.”", "아, 벌써 아처 씨 댁에 다 왔군.”"], ["So out he jumped, with thought, wisdom, experience, sympathy, and ready to attend to any of the calls made upon them by this family, just as if there were none other in the world.", "그러고는 생각과 지혜와 경험과 연민을 지닌 채, 마치 이 세상에 이 집 말고는 다른 집이 없기라도 한 듯 이 집안이 그것들에 요구하는 무엇에든 응할 채비를 하고서 그는 마차에서 뛰어내렸다."]], [["Meanwhile, Margaret had returned into her father’s study for a moment, to recover strength before going upstairs into her mother’s presence.", "그러는 동안 마거릿은 위층 어머니 앞에 나서기 전에 기운을 되찾으려고 잠시 아버지의 서재로 돌아가 있었다."]], [["“Oh, my God, my God!", "“오, 하느님, 하느님!"], ["but this is terrible.", "이건 너무나 끔찍해요."], ["How shall I bear it?", "제가 어떻게 견디죠?"], ["Such a deadly disease!", "그런 죽을병이라니!"], ["no hope!", "희망도 없다니!"], ["Oh, mamma, mamma, I wish I had never gone to aunt Shaw’s, and been all those precious years away from you!", "아, 어머니, 어머니, 쇼 이모 댁에 가지 말걸, 그 소중한 세월을 어머니 곁을 떠나 보내지 말걸!"], ["Poor mamma!", "가엾은 어머니!"], ["how much she must have borne!", "얼마나 참아 오셨을까!"], ["Oh, pray thee, my God, that her sufferings may not be too acute, too dreadful.", "오, 하느님, 비오니 어머니의 고통이 너무 모질지 않게, 너무 무섭지 않게 해 주세요."], ["How shall I bear to see them?", "그것을 지켜보는 일을 제가 어떻게 견디죠?"], ["How can I bear papa’s agony?", "아버지의 괴로움을 제가 어떻게 견디죠?"], ["He must not be told yet; not all at once.", "아직 아버지께 알려서는 안 돼. 한꺼번에는 안 돼."], ["It would kill him.", "그러면 아버지가 죽고 말 거야."], ["But I won’t lose another moment of my own dear, precious mother.”", "하지만 내 소중하고 귀한 어머니와 보낼 시간을 이제 한순간도 잃지 않을 테야.”"]], [["She ran upstairs.", "그녀는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["Dixon was not in the room. Mrs.", "딕슨은 방에 없었다."], ["Hale lay back in an easy chair, with a soft white shawl wrapped around her, and a becoming cap put on, in expectation of the doctor’s visit.", "헤일 부인은 안락의자에 몸을 기대고 있었다. 부드러운 흰 숄을 두르고 어울리는 실내 모자를 쓴 채였는데, 의사의 방문을 기다려 그렇게 차려입은 것이었다."], ["Her face had a little faint colour in it, and the very exhaustion after the examination gave it a peaceful look.", "얼굴에는 옅은 혈색이 조금 돌았고, 진찰 뒤의 그 지친 기색이 오히려 얼굴을 평온해 보이게 했다."], ["Margaret was surprised to see her look so calm.", "마거릿은 어머니가 그토록 차분해 보이는 데 놀랐다."]], [["“Why, Margaret, how strange you look!", "“아니, 마거릿, 얼굴이 왜 그러니!"], ["What is the matter?”", "무슨 일이야?”"], ["And then, as the idea stole into her mind of what was indeed the real state of the case, she added, as if a little displeased: “you have not been seeing Dr.", "그러고는 실제 사정이 어떠한지가 마음에 슬며시 떠오르자, 조금 언짢은 듯이 덧붙였다. “너 설마 도널드슨 선생을 만나서 이것저것 캐물은 건 아니겠지, 얘야?”"], ["Donaldson, and asking him any questions—have you child?” Margaret did not reply—only looked wistfully towards her.", "마거릿은 대답하지 않고 그저 애타는 눈으로 어머니를 바라볼 뿐이었다."], ["Mrs. Hale became more displeased.", "헤일 부인은 더 언짢아졌다."], ["“He would not surely break his word to me, and”—", "“그 사람이 나에게 한 약속을 어겼을 리는 없을 텐데, 그럼”—"]], [["“Oh yes, mamma, he did. I made him.", "“아니에요, 어머니, 말씀해 주셨어요."], ["It was I—blame me.”", "제가 억지로 말하게 했어요."], ["She knelt down by her mother’s side, and caught her hand—she would not let it go, though Mrs.", "저예요, 저를 나무라세요.” 그녀는 어머니 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그 손을 붙잡았다."], ["Hale tried to pull it away.", "헤일 부인이 손을 빼내려 했지만 그녀는 놓아 주지 않았다."], ["She kept kissing it, and the hot tears she shed bathed it.", "그녀는 그 손에 입을 맞추고 또 맞추었고, 뜨거운 눈물이 흘러내려 그 손을 적셨다."]], [["“Margaret, it was very wrong of you.", "“마거릿, 그건 아주 잘못한 일이야."], ["You knew I did not wish you to know.”", "내가 네게 알리고 싶어 하지 않는 걸 알았잖니.”"], ["But, as if tired with the contest, she left her hand in Margaret’s clasp, and by-and-by she returned the pressure faintly.", "그러나 실랑이에 지친 듯 부인은 마거릿이 쥔 손을 그대로 내버려 두었고, 이윽고 희미하게나마 그 손을 마주 쥐어 주었다."], ["That encouraged Margaret to speak.", "그것에 힘을 얻어 마거릿이 입을 열었다."]], [["“Oh, mamma!", "“아, 어머니!"], ["let me be your nurse.", "제가 어머니를 간호하게 해 주세요."], ["I will learn anything Dixon can teach me.", "딕슨이 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 건 무엇이든 배울게요."], ["But you know I am your child, and I do think I have a right to do everything for you.”", "하지만 저는 어머니의 자식이잖아요. 저에게는 어머니를 위해 무엇이든 할 권리가 있다고 정말로 믿어요.”"]], [["“You don’t know what you are asking,”", "“네가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 모르는구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, with a shudder.", "헤일 부인이 몸서리치며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I do.", "“아니에요, 알아요."], ["I know a great deal more than you are aware of.", "어머니가 아시는 것보다 훨씬 많이 알아요."], ["Let me be your nurse.", "제가 간호하게 해 주세요."], ["Let me try, at any rate.", "어쨌든 해 보게라도 해 주세요."], ["No one has ever, shall ever try so hard as I will do.", "제가 애쓰는 만큼 애쓴 사람은 여태 아무도 없었고 앞으로도 없을 거예요."], ["It will be such a comfort, mamma.”", "그러면 얼마나 마음이 놓이겠어요, 어머니.”"]], [["“My poor child!", "“가엾은 것!"], ["Well, you shall try.", "그래, 해 보려무나."], ["Do you know, Margaret, Dixon and I thought you would quite shrink from me if you knew—”", "마거릿, 그거 아니? 딕슨과 나는 네가 알게 되면 나를 아주 꺼릴 거라고 생각했단다—”"]], [["“Dixon thought!”", "“딕슨이 그렇게 생각했다고요!”"], ["said Margaret, her lip curling.", "마거릿이 입술을 비죽이며 말했다."], ["“Dixon could not give me credit for enough true love—for as much as herself!", "“딕슨은 제가 참된 사랑을 그만큼 지녔다는 걸, 자기만큼이나 지녔다는 걸 인정해 주지 못한 거로군요!"], ["She thought, I suppose, that I was one of those poor sickly women who like to lie on rose leaves, and be fanned all day.", "아마 저를 장미 잎사귀 위에 누워 온종일 부채질이나 받고 싶어 하는 그 딱하고 병약한 여자들 가운데 하나로 여긴 모양이지요."], ["Don’t let Dixon’s fancies come any more between you and me, mamma.", "어머니, 다시는 딕슨의 그런 억측이 어머니와 저 사이에 끼어들게 하지 마세요."], ["Don’t please!”", "제발요!”"], ["implored she.", "그녀가 애원했다."]], [["“Don’t be angry with Dixon,”", "“딕슨한테 화내지 마라.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, anxiously.", "헤일 부인이 걱정스레 말했다."], ["Margaret recovered herself.", "마거릿은 마음을 가라앉혔다."]], [["“No!", "“네!"], ["I won’t.", "화내지 않을게요."], ["I will try and be humble, and learn her ways, if you will only let me do all I can for you.", "겸손해지려고 애쓰고 그이의 방식도 배울게요. 어머니를 위해 제가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하게만 해 주신다면요."], ["Let me be in the first place, mother—I am greedy of that.", "저를 첫째 자리에 놓아 주세요, 어머니. 저는 그것이 욕심나요."], ["I used to fancy you would forget me while I was away at aunt Shaw’s, and cry myself to sleep at nights with that notion in my head.”", "제가 쇼 이모 댁에 가 있는 동안 어머니가 저를 잊으실 거라고 공연히 상상하곤 했어요. 그 생각을 머리에 담은 채 밤마다 울다 잠들었고요.”"]], [["“And I used to think, how will Margaret bear our make-shift poverty after the thorough comfort and luxury in Harley Street, till I have many a time been more ashamed of your seeing our contrivances at Helstone than of any stranger finding them out.”", "“나는 이런 생각을 하곤 했지. 할리 가의 그 온갖 안락과 사치를 누리다 왔는데 마거릿이 우리의 궁색한 살림을 어떻게 견딜까 하고. 그래서 헬스톤에서 우리가 궁여지책으로 마련해 놓은 것들을 낯모르는 남이 알아채는 것보다 네가 보는 것이 더 부끄러웠던 적이 여러 번이었단다.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma!", "“아, 어머니!"], ["and I did so enjoy them.", "저는 그것들이 정말 좋았어요."], ["They were so much more amusing than all the jog-trot Harley Street ways.", "할리 가의 그 따분하고 늘 똑같은 방식보다 훨씬 재미있었는걸요."], ["The wardrobe shelf with handles that served as a supper-tray on grand occasions!", "큰일이 있을 때면 저녁 쟁반 노릇을 하던, 손잡이 달린 옷장 선반이라니!"], ["And the old tea-chests stuffed and covered for ottomans!", "속을 채우고 천을 씌워 오토만으로 쓰던 낡은 차 상자들이라니!"], ["I think what you call the make-shift contrivances at dear Helstone were a charming part of the life there.”", "정든 헬스톤에서 어머니가 궁여지책이라 부르시는 그 재간들이야말로 그곳 살림의 아주 사랑스러운 한 부분이었다고 저는 생각해요.”"]], [["“I shall never see Helstone again, Margaret,”", "“나는 다시는 헬스톤을 보지 못할 거다, 마거릿.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, the tears welling up into her eyes.", "헤일 부인이 두 눈에 눈물을 그렁그렁 담고 말했다."], ["Margaret could not reply.", "마거릿은 대답할 수 없었다."], ["Mrs. Hale went on.", "헤일 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["“While I was there, I was for ever wanting to leave it.", "“거기 있을 때는 내내 떠나고만 싶었지."], ["Every place seemed pleasanter.", "어디든 다른 곳이 더 좋아 보였어."], ["And now I shall die far away from it.", "그런데 이제 나는 그곳에서 멀리 떨어져 죽게 되겠구나."], ["I am rightly punished.”", "마땅한 벌을 받는 거야.”"]], [["“You must not talk so,”", "“그렇게 말씀하지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret impatiently.", "마거릿이 참지 못하고 말했다."], ["“He said you might live for years.", "“선생님이 몇 해는 더 사실 수 있다고 하셨어요."], ["Oh, mother!", "아, 어머니!"], ["we will have you back at Helstone yet.”", "우리는 어머니를 다시 헬스톤으로 모셔 갈 거예요.”"]], [["“No, never!", "“아니야, 절대로!"], ["That I must take as a just penance.", "그건 마땅한 속죄로 받아들여야지."], ["But, Margaret—Frederick!”", "그런데 마거릿, 프레더릭이!”"]], [["At the mention of that one word, she suddenly cried out loud, as in some sharp agony.", "그 한마디를 입에 올린 순간, 부인은 무슨 날카로운 고통에라도 찔린 듯 갑자기 큰 소리로 울부짖었다."], ["It seemed as if the thought of him upset all her composure, destroyed the calm, overcame the exhaustion.", "아들 생각이 그녀의 침착함을 모조리 무너뜨리고 평온을 부수고 지친 기운마저 이겨 낸 듯했다."], ["Wild passionate cry succeeded to cry—“Frederick!", "사납고 격한 외침이 잇달아 터져 나왔다."], ["Frederick!", "“프레더릭!"], ["Come to me.", "프레더릭!"], ["I am dying.", "나에게 오너라."], ["Little first-born child, come to me once again!”", "내가 죽어 간다. 첫아이야, 한 번만 더 나에게 와 다오!”"]], [["She was in violent hysterics.", "부인은 심한 발작에 빠졌다."], ["Margaret went and called Dixon in terror.", "마거릿은 겁에 질려 딕슨을 부르러 갔다."], ["Dixon came in a huff, and accused Margaret of having over-excited her mother.", "딕슨은 잔뜩 골이 나서 들어오더니 마거릿이 어머니를 너무 흥분시켰다고 나무랐다."], ["Margaret bore all meekly, only trusting that her father might not return.", "마거릿은 아버지가 돌아오지 않기만을 바라며 모든 것을 고분고분 받아들였다."], ["In spite of her alarm, which was even greater than the occasion warranted, she obeyed all Dixon’s directions promptly and well without a word of self-justification.", "사태가 요구하는 것보다도 더 큰 놀라움에 사로잡혀 있었으면서도, 그녀는 변명 한마디 없이 딕슨의 지시를 하나하나 재빠르고 야무지게 따랐다."], ["By so doing she mollified her accuser.", "그렇게 함으로써 그녀는 자기를 나무라던 사람을 누그러뜨렸다."], ["They put her mother to bed, and Margaret sate by her till she fell asleep, and afterwards till Dixon beckoned her out of the room, and, with a sour face, as if doing something against the grain, she bade her drink a cup of coffee which she had prepared for her in the drawing-room, and stood over her in a commanding attitude as she did so.", "두 사람은 어머니를 침대에 눕혔고, 마거릿은 어머니가 잠들 때까지, 그러고도 딕슨이 방 밖으로 손짓해 부를 때까지 곁을 지켰다. 딕슨은 마음에 없는 일을 하는 사람처럼 시큰둥한 얼굴로, 응접실에 마련해 둔 커피를 마시라고 이르고는 그녀가 마시는 동안 명령하는 자세로 옆에 버티고 서 있었다."]], [["“You shouldn’t have been so curious, Miss, and then you wouldn’t have needed to fret before your time.", "“그렇게 캐묻지 말았어야죠, 아가씨. 그랬으면 때도 되기 전에 속을 태울 일도 없었을 텐데요."], ["It would have come soon enough.", "어차피 머잖아 다 알게 될 일이었어요."], ["And now, I suppose, you’ll tell master, and a pretty household I shall have of you!”", "그런데 이제는 주인어른께도 이르시겠지요. 그럼 제 살림 꼴이 아주 볼만하겠구먼요!”"]], [["“No, Dixon,”", "“아니야, 딕슨.”"], ["said Margaret, sorrowfully, “I will not tell papa.", "마거릿이 서글프게 말했다. “아버지께는 말씀드리지 않을 거야."], ["He could not bear it as I can.”", "아버지는 나만큼 견디지 못하실 테니까.”"], ["And by way of proving how well she bore it, she burst into tears.", "그러고는 자기가 얼마나 잘 견디는지 증명이라도 하듯 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Ay!", "“저런!"], ["I knew how it would be.", "이럴 줄 알았지요."], ["Now you’ll waken your mamma, just after she’s gone to sleep so quietly.", "이제 마님을 깨우시겠어요, 그렇게 곱게 잠드신 지 얼마 되지도 않았는데. 마거릿 아가씨, 저는 이걸 몇 주째 속에 눌러 담고 지냈답니다."], ["Miss Margaret my dear, I’ve had to keep it down this many a week: and though I don’t pretend I can love her as you do, yet I loved her better than any other man, woman, or child—no one but Master Frederick ever came near her in my mind.", "제가 아가씨만큼 마님을 사랑한다고 우길 생각은 없습니다만, 그래도 저는 그 어떤 남자보다도 여자보다도 아이보다도 마님을 더 사랑했어요. 제 마음속에서 마님에 견줄 만한 사람은 프레더릭 도련님밖에 없었지요."], ["Ever since Lady Beresford’s maid first took me in to see her dressed out in white crape, and corn-ears, and scarlet poppies, and I ran a needle down into my finger, and broke it in, and she tore up her worked pocket-handkerchief, after they’d cut it out, and came in to wet the bandages again with lotion when she returned from the ball—where she’d been the prettiest young lady of all—I’ve never loved any one like her.", "베러스퍼드 부인의 하녀가 처음으로 저를 데려가, 흰 크레이프 옷에 이삭과 진홍빛 양귀비로 단장하신 마님을 보여 주었을 때부터 그랬습니다. 그때 제가 바늘을 손가락에 찔러 부러뜨렸는데, 사람들이 그것을 도려낸 뒤에 마님이 손수 수놓으신 손수건을 찢어 감아 주셨고, 무도회에서 돌아오시자, 그날 밤 모인 이들 가운데 가장 어여쁜 아가씨셨지요, 다시 들러 붕대를 물약으로 적셔 주셨답니다."], ["I little thought then that I should live to see her brought so low.", "그 뒤로 저는 마님만큼 누구를 사랑해 본 적이 없어요. 그때는 마님이 이토록 기울어지시는 것을 제 눈으로 보게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐지요."], ["I don’t mean no reproach to nobody.", "누구를 탓하려는 게 아닙니다."], ["Many a one calls you pretty and handsome, and what not.", "아가씨더러도 예쁘다느니 곱다느니 하는 사람이 많지요."], ["Even in this smoky place, enough to blind one’s eyes, the owls can see that.", "눈이 멀 만큼 매캐한 이 고장에서도 올빼미쯤이면 그것쯤은 알아보겠지요. 하지만 아가씨는 아름다움에서만큼은 결코 어머님 같지 못할 겁니다."], ["But you’ll never be like your mother for beauty—never; not if you live to be a hundred.”", "결코요. 100살까지 사신다 해도요.”"]], [["“Mamma is very pretty still.", "“어머니는 지금도 아주 고우신걸."], ["Poor mamma!”", "가엾은 어머니!”"]], [["“Now don’t ye set off again, or I shall give way at last”", "“자, 또 시작하지 마세요. 안 그러면 저마저 끝내 무너지고 말 테니까요.”"], ["(whimpering). “You’ll never stand master’s coming home, and questioning, at this rate.", "(훌쩍이며) “이래서야 주인어른이 돌아와 이것저것 물으실 때 못 버티십니다."], ["Go out and take a walk, and come in something like.", "나가서 좀 걷다가 사람 꼴을 하고 들어오세요."], ["Many’s the time I’ve longed to walk it off—the thought of what was the matter with her, and how it must all end.”", "저도 마님이 어떤 병인지, 그것이 어떻게 끝날 수밖에 없는지 하는 생각을 걸어서 떨쳐 버리고 싶던 적이 한두 번이 아니었답니다.”"]], [["“Oh, Dixon!”", "“아, 딕슨!”"], ["said Margaret, “how often I’ve been cross with you, not knowing what a terrible secret you had to bear!”", "마거릿이 말했다. “딕슨이 얼마나 무서운 비밀을 안고 있는 줄도 모르고 내가 얼마나 자주 딕슨한테 짜증을 냈던지!”"]], [["“Bless you, child!", "“아이고, 아가씨!"], ["I like to see you showing a bit of a spirit.", "저는 아가씨가 그렇게 기개를 보이시는 게 좋습니다요."], ["It’s the good old Beresford blood.", "그게 바로 훌륭한 베러스퍼드 집안의 피지요."], ["Why, the last Sir John but two shot his steward down, there where he stood, for just telling him that he racked the tenants, and he’d racked the tenants till he could get no more money off them than he could get skin off a flint.”", "아, 세 대 위의 존 경께서는 소작인들을 쥐어짠다고 말했다는 이유 하나로 집사를 그 자리에서 쏘아 죽이셨답니다. 그러고도 부싯돌에서 가죽을 벗겨 낼 수 없듯 더는 한 푼도 나오지 않을 때까지 소작인들을 쥐어짜셨지요.”"]], [["“Well, Dixon, I won’t shoot you, and I’ll try not to be cross again.”", "“그래, 딕슨, 나는 딕슨을 쏘지 않을 거야. 그리고 다시는 짜증 내지 않도록 애써 볼게.”"]], [["“You never have.", "“아가씨는 그런 적 없으십니다."], ["If I’ve said it at times, it has always been to myself, just in private, by way of making a little agreeable conversation, for there’s no one here fit to talk to.", "제가 이따금 그런 말을 했다면 그건 늘 혼잣말이었어요. 그저 혼자 조금 재미난 이야기라도 나누어 보자고 한 것뿐이지요."], ["And when you fire up you’re the very image of Master Frederick.", "여기서는 말을 나눌 만한 사람이 없으니까요. 그리고 아가씨가 발끈하실 때면 영락없이 프레더릭 도련님을 빼닮으셨답니다."], ["I could find in my heart to put you in a passion any day just to see his stormy look coming like a great cloud over your face.", "저는 아무 날이나 아가씨를 성나게 해서, 그 폭풍우 같은 표정이 커다란 구름장처럼 얼굴 위로 몰려오는 걸 보고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같을 정도예요."], ["But now you go out, Miss.", "하지만 이제는 나가세요, 아가씨."], ["I’ll watch over Missus; and as for master, his books are company enough for him, if he should come in.”", "마님은 제가 지켜보겠습니다. 주인어른이야 들어오신다 해도 책이면 충분한 벗이 되어 드리지요.”"]], [["“I will go,”", "“나갈게.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["She hung about Dixon for a minute or so, as if afraid and irresolute; then, suddenly kissing her, she went quickly out of the room.", "그녀는 두려운 듯 망설이는 듯 잠깐 딕슨 곁을 서성이더니, 불쑥 딕슨에게 입을 맞추고는 얼른 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Bless her!”", "“복 받으실 거야!”"], ["said Dixon.", "딕슨이 말했다."], ["“She’s as sweet as a nut.", "“참으로 알차고 여문 아가씨지."], ["There are three people I love; its Missus, Master Frederick, and her.", "내가 사랑하는 사람은 셋뿐이야. 마님과 프레더릭 도련님과 저 아가씨."], ["Just them three.", "딱 그 셋이지."], ["That’s all.", "그게 다야."], ["The rest be hanged, for I don’t know what they’re in the world for.", "나머지는 다 목매달아도 좋아. 그 사람들이 대체 뭐 하러 세상에 나왔는지 나는 모르겠으니까."], ["Master was born, I suppose, for to marry missus.", "주인어른이야 마님과 혼인하시려고 태어나셨겠지."], ["If I thought he loved her properly, I might get to love him in time.", "그분이 마님을 제대로 사랑하신다고 믿을 수만 있다면 나도 언젠가는 그분을 사랑하게 될지 몰라."], ["But he should ha’ made a deal more on her, and not been always reading, reading, thinking, thinking.", "하지만 그분은 마님을 훨씬 더 위해 드렸어야 했어. 늘 읽고 또 읽고, 생각하고 또 생각하지만 말고."], ["See what it has brought him to.", "그래서 어떤 꼴이 되었는지 보라지."], ["Many a one who never reads or thinks either, gets to be Rector, and Dean, and what not; and I daresay master might, if he’d just minded missus, and let the weary reading and thinking alone.—There she goes”", "읽지도 생각하지도 않는 사람들 가운데도 교구 목사가 되고 사제장이 되고 뭐가 되고 하는 이가 숱하잖아. 주인어른도 마님 말씀만 새겨듣고 그 지긋지긋한 읽기와 생각하기를 내버려 두셨더라면 그렇게 되셨을지도 모르지."], ["(looking out of the window as she heard the front door shut).", "— 저기 나가시는군.” (현관문 닫히는 소리를 듣고 창밖을 내다보며) “가엾은 아가씨!"], ["“Poor young lady! her clothes look shabby to what they did when she came to Helstone a year ago.", "한 해 전 헬스톤에 오셨을 때에 견주면 옷차림이 초라해 보이는구나."], ["Then she hadn’t so much as a darned stocking or a cleaned pair of gloves in all her wardrobe.", "그때는 옷장에 기운 양말 한 짝, 세탁한 장갑 한 켤레도 없었는데."], ["And now—!”", "그런데 지금은—!”"]], [["# CHAPTER XVII", "# 제17장"]], [["“There are briars besetting every path, Which call for patient care; There is a cross in every lot, And an earnest need for prayer.”", "“길마다 가시덤불이 가로놓여 있어 참을성 있는 보살핌을 부르고, 어떤 처지에나 십자가가 하나씩 있어 간절히 기도할 일이 있네.”"], ["ANON.", "— 무명씨."]], [["Margaret went out heavily and unwillingly enough.", "마거릿은 무거운 마음으로 마지못해 집을 나섰다."], ["But the length of a street—yes, the air of a Milton Street—cheered her young blood before she reached her first turning.", "그러나 거리 하나를 지나는 동안, 그렇다, 밀턴 거리의 공기가 첫 모퉁이에 이르기도 전에 그녀의 젊은 피를 북돋웠다."], ["Her step grew lighter, her lip redder.", "걸음이 가벼워지고 입술이 붉어졌다."], ["She began to take notice, instead of having her thoughts turned so exclusively inward.", "그토록 안으로만 향해 있던 생각 대신 그녀는 주위를 살피기 시작했다."], ["She saw unusual loiterers in the streets: men with their hands in their pockets sauntering along; loud-laughing and loud-spoken girls clustered together, apparently excited to high spirits, and a boisterous independence of temper and behaviour.", "거리에는 여느 때와 달리 어정거리는 사람들이 보였다. 주머니에 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁거리는 사내들, 무리 지어 몰려다니며 큰 소리로 웃고 떠드는 처녀들이 잔뜩 들뜬 기분과 거리낌 없는 기질과 몸가짐으로 흥청거리고 있었다."], ["The more ill-looking of the men—the discreditable minority—hung about on the steps of the beer-houses and gin-shops, smoking, and commenting pretty freely on every passer-by.", "사내들 가운데 더 험상궂은 축, 그 낯부끄러운 소수는 맥줏집과 진 가게 계단께에 죽치고 서서 담배를 피우며 지나가는 사람마다 제법 거리낌 없이 품평을 해 댔다."], ["Margaret disliked the prospect of the long walk through these streets, before she came to the fields which she had planned to reach.", "마거릿은 가려고 마음먹었던 들판에 이르기까지 이 거리들을 오래 걸어야 한다는 것이 마뜩잖았다."], ["Instead, she would go and see Bessy Higgins.", "그러느니 베시 히긴스를 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다."], ["It would not be so refreshing as a quiet country walk, but still it would perhaps be doing the kinder thing.", "조용한 시골 산책만큼 상쾌하지는 않겠지만, 그래도 어쩌면 그편이 더 친절한 일일 터였다."]], [["Nicholas Higgins was sitting by the fire smoking, as she went in.", "그녀가 들어섰을 때 니컬러스 히긴스는 난롯가에 앉아 담배를 피우고 있었다."], ["Bessy was rocking herself on the other side.", "베시는 맞은편에서 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었다."]], [["Nicholas took the pipe out of his mouth, and standing up, pushed his chair towards Margaret; he leant against the chimney-piece in a lounging attitude, while she asked Bessy how she was.", "니컬러스는 입에서 파이프를 빼고 일어서서 자기 의자를 마거릿 쪽으로 밀어 주었다. 그러고는 벽난로 선반에 느긋하게 몸을 기댔고, 그동안 마거릿은 베시에게 몸이 어떠냐고 물었다."]], [["“Hoo’s rather down i’ th’ mouth in regard to spirits, but hoo’s better in health.", "“쟤가 기분으로 치면 좀 처져 있소만, 몸은 나아졌구먼."], ["Hoo doesn’t like this strike.", "쟤는 이 파업이 마음에 안 드는 게지."], ["Hoo’s a deal too much set on piece and quietness at any price.”", "무슨 값을 치르든 조용하고 편한 것만 지나치게 바라니까.”"]], [["“This is th’ third strike I’ve seen,”", "“이게 내가 본 세 번째 파업이에요.”"], ["said she, sighing, as if that was answer and explanation enough.", "그녀가 그것으로 대답도 설명도 다 되었다는 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“Well, third time pays for all.", "“흥, 세 번째면 다 갚아 주는 법이지."], ["See if we don’t dang th’ masters this time.", "이번에야말로 공장주들을 혼내 주는지 두고 보라구."], ["See if they don’t come and beg us to come back at our own price.", "저들이 와서 우리가 부르는 값에 돌아와 달라고 사정하는지 두고 보라니까."], ["That’s all.", "그뿐이오."], ["We’ve missed it afore time, I grant yo’; but this time we’n laid our plans desperate deep.”", "전에는 그르쳤다는 것, 그건 나도 인정하겠소. 하지만 이번엔 계획을 아주 지독하게 깊이 짜 놨소.”"]], [["“Why do you strike?”", "“왜 파업을 하시죠?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“Striking is leaving off work till you get your own rate of wages, is it not?", "“파업이란 자기가 바라는 삯을 받을 때까지 일을 그만두는 것 아닌가요?"], ["You must not wonder at my ignorance; where I come from I never heard of a strike.”", "제가 아무것도 몰라도 이상하게 여기지 마세요. 제가 살던 데서는 파업이라는 말을 들어 본 적이 없거든요.”"]], [["“I wish I were there,”", "“나도 거기 살았으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Bessie, wearily.", "베시가 지친 듯 말했다."], ["“But it’s not for me to get sick and tired o’ strikes.", "“하지만 내가 파업에 넌더리를 낼 것도 없지요."], ["This is the last I’ll see.", "이번이 내가 볼 마지막이니까."], ["Before it’s ended I shall be in the Great City—the Holy Jerusalem.”", "이게 끝나기 전에 나는 큰 도성에 가 있을 거예요. 거룩한 예루살렘에요.”"]], [["“Hoo’s so full of th’ life to come, hoo cannot think of th’ present.", "“쟤는 저승 이야기로 꽉 차 있어서 이승 생각은 못 하오."], ["Now I, yo see, am bound to do the best I can here.", "그런데 나는, 보시다시피, 여기서 할 수 있는 걸 다 해야 하는 사람이오."], ["I think a bird i’ th’ hand is worth two i’ th’ bush.", "손에 쥔 새 한 마리가 덤불 속 두 마리보다 낫다고 여기지."], ["So them’s the different views we take on th’ strike question.”", "그래서 파업 문제를 두고 우리 둘의 생각이 이렇게 다른 거요.”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["said Margaret, “if the people struck, as you call it, where I come from, as they are mostly all field labourers, the seed would not be sown, the hay got in, the corn reaped.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “제가 살던 데서 사람들이 말씀하시는 그 파업이라는 걸 한다면, 거기는 거의 다 밭일하는 사람들이니 씨도 못 뿌리고 건초도 못 거두고 곡식도 못 벨 거예요.”"]], [["“Well?”", "“그래서?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["He had resumed his pipe, and put his “well” in the form of an interrogation.", "그는 다시 파이프를 물고는 그 “그래서”를 되묻는 투로 던졌다."]], [["“Why,”", "“아니,”"], ["she went on, “what would become of the farmers?”", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “그러면 농부들은 어떻게 되겠어요?”"]], [["He puffed away.", "그가 연기를 뿜었다."], ["“I reckon, they’d have either to give up their farms, or to give fair rate of wage.”", "“내 보기엔 농장을 내놓든지, 아니면 제대로 된 삯을 주든지 해야겠지요.”"]], [["“Suppose they could not, or would not do the last; they could not give up their farms all in a minute, however much they might wish to do so; but they would have no hay, nor corn to sell that year; and where would the money come from to pay the labourers’ wages the next?”", "“그런데 뒤엣것을 할 수 없거나 하려 들지 않는다고 해 봐요. 아무리 그러고 싶어도 농장을 하루아침에 내놓을 수는 없잖아요. 그런데 그해에는 팔 건초도 곡식도 없을 거고요. 그러면 이듬해 일꾼들 삯을 줄 돈은 어디서 나오겠어요?”"]], [["Still puffing away.", "여전히 연기를 뿜을 뿐이었다."], ["At last he said:", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“I know nought of your ways down South.", "“나는 남쪽 사는 법에 대해선 아무것도 모르오."], ["I have heerd they’re a pack of spiritless, down-trodden men; welly clemmed to death; too dazed wi’ clemming to know when they’re put upon.", "듣기로는 거기 사람들은 기죽고 짓밟힌 무리라더군. 굶어 죽기 직전까지 가고, 굶주림에 넋이 나가서 자기가 짓밟히는 줄도 모른다더구먼."], ["Now, it’s not so here.", "그런데 여기는 안 그렇소."], ["We known when we’re put upon; and we’en too much blood in us to stand it.", "우리는 짓밟히면 짓밟히는 줄 알고, 그걸 가만 참고 있기엔 피가 너무 뜨겁소."], ["We just take our hands fro’ our looms, and say, ‘Yo’ may clem us, but yo’ll not put upon us, my masters!’ And be danged to ’em, they shan’t this time!”", "우리는 그저 베틀에서 손을 떼고 이렇게 말하는 거요. ‘굶겨 죽여도 좋소, 하지만 우릴 짓밟지는 못하오, 공장주 나리들!’ 그리고 빌어먹을, 이번엔 저들 뜻대로 안 될 거요!”"]], [["“I wished I lived down South,”", "“나는 남쪽에서 살았으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Bessy.", "베시가 말했다."]], [["“There’s a deal to bear there,”", "“거기도 견딜 것이 많아요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“There are sorrows to bear everywhere.", "“어디에나 견뎌야 할 슬픔은 있어요."], ["There is very hard bodily labour to be gone through, with very little food to give strength.”", "힘을 낼 만한 먹을거리도 변변찮은데 몸으로 하는 아주 고된 일을 해내야 하죠.”"]], [["“But it’s out of doors,”", "“그래도 바깥이잖아요.”"], ["said Bessy.", "베시가 말했다."], ["“And away from the endless, endless noise, and sickening heat.”", "“끝도 없고 끝도 없는 이 소음과 사람 잡는 열기에서 멀리 떨어져 있고요.”"]], [["“It’s sometimes in heavy rain, and sometimes in bitter cold. A young person can stand it; but an old man gets racked with rheumatism, and bent and withered before his time; yet he must just work on the same, or else go to the workhouse.”", "“어떤 때는 억수 같은 빗속에서, 어떤 때는 살을 에는 추위 속에서 하는 일이에요. 젊은 사람은 견디죠. 하지만 나이 든 사람은 류머티즘으로 뒤틀리고 제 나이보다 먼저 굽고 시들어요. 그런데도 그저 똑같이 일을 이어 가야 해요. 안 그러면 구빈원으로 가야 하고요.”"]], [["“I thought yo were so taken wi’ the ways of the South country.”", "“아가씨는 남쪽 고장 사는 법을 그리도 좋아하는 줄 알았는데요.”"]], [["“So I am,”", "“좋아하고말고요.”"], ["said Margaret, smiling a little, as she found herself thus caught.", "마거릿이 이렇게 붙들린 것을 알고 살짝 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I only mean, Bessy, there’s good and bad in everything in this world; and as you felt the bad up here, I thought it was but fair you should know the bad down there.”", "“다만 베시, 내 말은 이 세상 무엇에나 좋은 면과 나쁜 면이 있다는 거예요. 베시가 여기서 나쁜 면을 겪었으니 저기의 나쁜 면도 알아 두는 게 공평하겠다 싶었을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“And yo say they never strike down there?”", "“그래서 거기서는 파업이란 걸 절대 안 한단 말이오?”"], ["asked Nicholas abruptly.", "니컬러스가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“No!”", "“안 해요!”"], ["said Margaret; “I think they have too much sense.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “다들 분별이 있어서 그런 것 같아요.”"]], [["“An’ I think,”", "“그런데 내 생각에는,”"], ["replied he, dashing the ashes out of his pipe with so much vehemence that it broke, “it’s not that they’ve too much sense, but that they’ve too little spirit.”", "그가 파이프의 재를 어찌나 세차게 털어 냈던지 파이프가 부러졌다. “분별이 지나쳐서가 아니라 기개가 모자라서요.”"]], [["“Oh, father!” said Bessy, “what have ye gained by striking? Just think of that first strike when mother died—how we all had to clem—you the worst of all; and yet many a one went in every week at the same wage, till all were gone in that there was work for; and some went beggars all their lives at after.”", "“아이고, 아버지!” 베시가 말했다. “파업해서 얻은 게 뭐가 있어요? 어머니가 돌아가시던 그 첫 파업을 생각해 봐요. 우리 모두 얼마나 굶었어요. 아버지가 제일 심했고요. 그런데도 매주 여럿이 똑같은 삯을 받고 들어갔고, 일자리가 있는 만큼 다 들어가고 나니 그만이었잖아요. 그러고 나서 평생 거지로 산 사람도 있고요.”"]], [["“Ay,” said he.", "“그래,”"], ["“That there strike was badly managed.", "그가 말했다. “그 파업은 형편없이 굴러갔지."], ["Folk got into th’ management of it, as were either fools or not true men.", "그걸 이끌겠다고 나선 것들이 바보 아니면 참되지 못한 놈들이었으니까."], ["Yo’ll see, it’ll be different this time.”", "두고 보라구, 이번엔 다를 테니.”"]], [["“But all this time you’ve not told me what you’re striking for,”", "“그런데 지금까지 무엇 때문에 파업하시는지는 말씀 안 해 주셨어요.”"], ["said Margaret, again.", "마거릿이 다시 말했다."]], [["“Why, yo see, there’s five or six masters who have set themselves again paying the wages they’ve been paying these two years past, and flourishing upon, and getting richer upon.", "“아, 그러니까 말이오, 공장주 대여섯이 지난 두 해 동안 주던 삯을, 그걸 주면서도 잘나가고 더 부자가 되었으면서 이제는 못 주겠다고 버티고 나선 거요."], ["And now they come to us, and say we’re to take less.", "그러고는 우리한테 와서 더 적게 받으라 하지."], ["And we won’t.", "우린 싫소."], ["We’ll just clem them to death first; and see who’ll work for ’em then.", "우리가 먼저 저들을 굶겨 죽일 거요. 그러고 나면 누가 저들을 위해 일해 주나 두고 봅시다."], ["They’ll have killed the goose that laid ’em the golden eggs, I reckon.”", "내 보기엔 저들이 황금 알을 낳아 주던 거위를 죽인 셈이 되겠지.”"]], [["“And so you plan dying, in order to be revenged upon them!”", "“그래서 저들에게 앙갚음하려고 죽을 작정을 하신다는 건가요!”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니오,”"], ["said he, “I dunnot.", "그가 말했다."], ["I just look forward to the chance of dying at my post sooner than yield.", "“그런 건 아니오. 나는 그저 굽히느니 내 자리에서 죽는 쪽을 바라볼 뿐이오."], ["That’s what folk call fine and honourable in a soldier, and why not in a poor weaver-chap?”", "군인이 그러면 사람들이 훌륭하다, 명예롭다 하지 않소. 그럼 가난한 베짜기 놈은 왜 안 되오?”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["said Margaret, “a soldier dies in the cause of the Nation—in the cause of others.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “군인은 나라를 위해, 남을 위해 죽는걸요.”"]], [["He laughed grimly.", "그가 씁쓸하게 웃었다. “아가씨,”"], ["“My lass,”", "그가 말했다. “아가씨야 아직 어린 처녀지만, 내가 주급 16실링으로 세 사람을, 그러니까 베시하고 메리하고 나를 먹여 살릴 수 있을 것 같소?"], ["said he, “yo’re but a young wench, but don’t yo think I can keep three people—that’s Bessy, and Mary, and me—on sixteen shillings a week?", "내가 이번에 나 자신을 위해 일손을 놓은 줄 아시오? 이것도 저 군인만큼이나 남을 위한 일이오."], ["Dun yo think it’s for mysel’ I’m striking work at this time?", "다만 다른 게 있다면, 그 군인이 위해서 죽는 상대는 어쩌면 평생 한 번 본 적도 들은 적도 없는 누군가일 텐데, 나는 두 집 건너 사는 존 바우처의 일을 떠맡는다는 거요. 그 사람은 병든 아내에 자식이 여덟인데 하나도 공장 다닐 나이가 안 됐소."], ["It is just as much in the cause of others as yon soldier—only m’appen, the cause he dies for is just that of somebody he never clapt eyes on, nor heerd on all his born days, while I take up John Boucher’s cause, as lives next door but one, wi’ a sickly wife, and eight childer, none on ’em factory age; and I don’t take up his cause only, though he’s a poor good-for-nought, as can only manage two looms at a time, but I take up th’ cause o’ justice.", "그리고 나는 그 사람 일만 떠맡는 게 아니오. 하기야 그 사람은 한 번에 베틀 두 대밖에 못 다루는 딱한 쓸모없는 위인이지만."], ["Why are we to have less wage now, I ask, than two year ago?”", "나는 정의라는 것의 일을 떠맡는 거요. 왜 우리가 두 해 전보다 지금 삯을 덜 받아야 하느냐, 나는 그걸 묻는 거요.”"]], [["“Don’t ask me,”", "“저한테 묻지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret; “I am very ignorant.", "마거릿이 말했다. “저는 아무것도 몰라요."], ["Ask some of your masters.", "공장주들한테 물어보세요."], ["Surely they will give you a reason for it.", "분명 그 까닭을 대 줄 거예요."], ["It is not merely an arbitrary decision of theirs, come to without reason.”", "아무 이유 없이 제멋대로 내린 결정은 아닐 테니까요.”"]], [["“Yo’re just a foreigner, and nothing more,”", "“아가씨는 그저 타관 사람일 뿐이구먼, 그 이상은 아니고.”"], ["said he, contemptuously.", "그가 업신여기듯 말했다. “뭘 안다고."], ["“Much yo know about it.", "공장주들한테 물어보라니!"], ["Ask th’ masters! They’d tell us to mind our own business, and they’d mind theirs.", "그자들은 우리더러 우리 일이나 신경 쓰라 할 거요, 자기들은 자기들 일 신경 쓸 테니."], ["Our business being, yo understand, to take the bated wage, and be thankful; and their business to bate us down to clemming point, to swell their profits.", "우리 일이란 게 뭐냐 하면, 아시겠소, 깎인 삯을 받고 고마워하는 거지. 그리고 저들 일이란 건 제 잇속을 불리려고 우릴 굶어 죽을 지경까지 깎아 내리는 거고."], ["That’s what it is.”", "그런 거요.”"]], [["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["said Margaret, determined not to give way, although she saw she was irritating him, “the state of trade may be such as not to enable them to give you the same remuneration.”", "마거릿이 그를 언짢게 하고 있다는 것을 알면서도 물러서지 않기로 마음먹고 말했다. “장사 형편이 여러분에게 예전과 같은 삯을 줄 수 없는 지경일 수도 있잖아요.”"]], [["“State o’ trade!", "“장사 형편이라!"], ["That’s just a piece o’ masters’ humbug.", "그건 그저 공장주들이 늘어놓는 허튼소리요."], ["It’s rate o’ wages I was talking of.", "내가 말하는 건 삯이오."], ["Th’ masters keep th’ state o’ trade in their own hands; and just walk it forward like a black bug-a-boo, to frighten naughty children with into being good.", "공장주들이 장사 형편이란 걸 제 손아귀에 쥐고 있다가, 말 안 듣는 아이한테 겁을 줘서 말 잘 듣게 하려고 내세우는 시커먼 도깨비처럼 그저 앞세워 걸어 보이는 거지."], ["I’ll tell you it’s their part,—their cue, as some folks call it,—to beat us down, to swell their fortunes; and it’s ours to stand up and fight hard,—not for ourselves alone, but for them round about us—for justice and fair play.", "내 말하리다, 저들 몫은, 어떤 사람들 말로는 저들이 맡은 배역이지, 제 재산을 불리려고 우릴 깎아 내리는 거고, 우리 몫은 일어서서 힘껏 싸우는 거요. 우리만을 위해서가 아니라 우리 둘레 사람들을 위해서, 정의와 공정을 위해서 말이오."], ["We help to make their profits, and we ought to help spend ’em.", "우리가 저들 이익을 만드는 걸 거들었으니 그걸 쓰는 것도 거들어야 마땅하지."], ["It’s not that we want their brass so much this time, as we’ve done many a time afore.", "이번엔 전에 여러 번 그랬던 것처럼 저들 돈이 그렇게 급한 것도 아니오."], ["We’n getten money laid by; and we’re resolved to stand and fall together; not a man on us will go in for less wage than th’ Union says is our due.", "우리는 모아 둔 돈이 있고, 함께 서고 함께 쓰러지기로 뜻을 모았소. 조합이 우리 몫이라고 하는 것보다 적은 삯을 받고 들어갈 사람은 우리 중에 하나도 없소."], ["So I say, ‘hooray for the strike,’ and let Thornton, and Slickson, and Hamper, and their set look to it!”", "그러니 나는 ‘파업 만세’라고 하겠소. 손턴이든 슬릭슨이든 햄퍼든 그 패거리든 어디 두고 보라지!”"]], [["“Thornton!”", "“손턴이요!”"], ["said Margaret. “Mr.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["Thornton of Marlborough Street?”", "“말버러 가의 손턴 씨요?”"]], [["“Aye!", "“그렇소!"], ["Thornton o’ Marlborough Mill, as we call him.”", "우리끼리는 말버러 공장의 손턴이라 부르지.”"]], [["“He is one of the masters you are striving with, is he not?", "“그분도 여러분이 맞서 싸우는 공장주 가운데 하나인가요?"], ["What sort of a master is he?”", "어떤 공장주인가요?”"]], [["“Did yo’ ever see a bulldog?", "“불도그를 본 적 있소?"], ["Set a bulldog on hind legs, and dress him up in coat and breeches, and yo’n just getten John Thornton.”", "불도그를 뒷다리로 세워 놓고 저고리에 바지를 입혀 보시오. 그게 딱 존 손턴이오.”"]], [["“Nay,”", "“아니에요.”"], ["said Margaret, laughing, “I deny that.", "마거릿이 웃으며 말했다. “그건 아니라고 하겠어요."], ["Mr. Thornton is plain enough, but he’s not like a bulldog, with its short broad nose, and snarling upper lip.”", "손턴 씨가 잘생기지는 않았지만, 짧고 넓적한 코에 으르렁대는 윗입술을 한 불도그 같지는 않아요.”"]], [["“No! not in look, I grant yo.", "“아니, 생김새 말은 아니오, 그건 인정하겠소."], ["But let John Thornton get hold on a notion, and he’ll stick to it like a bulldog; yo might pull him away wi’ a pitchfork ere he’d leave go.", "하지만 존 손턴이 어떤 생각을 한번 붙들었다 하면 불도그처럼 물고 늘어질 거요. 쇠스랑으로 끌어내려 해도 놓지 않을 사람이지."], ["He’s worth fighting wi’, is John Thornton.", "존 손턴은 싸워 볼 만한 상대요."], ["As for Slickson, I take it, some o’ these days he’ll wheedle his men back wi’ fair promises; that they’ll just get cheated out of as soon as they’re in his power again.", "슬릭슨으로 말하자면, 내 보기에 그자는 언젠가 그럴싸한 약속으로 제 직공들을 구슬려 데려갈 거요. 그러고는 다시 제 손아귀에 들어오기가 무섭게 그 약속에서 감쪽같이 속여 먹겠지."], ["He’ll work his fines well out on ’em, I’ll warrant.", "벌금을 톡톡히 우려낼 게 뻔하오."], ["He’s as slippery as an eel, he is.", "뱀장어처럼 미끄러운 자요."], ["He’s like a cat,—as sleek, and cunning, and fierce.", "고양이 같지, 매끈하고 교활하고 사납고."], ["It’ll never be an honest up and down fight wi’ him, as it will be wi’ Thornton.", "그자하고는 손턴처럼 정직하게 맞붙어 싸우는 일은 결코 없을 거요. 손턴은 대못처럼 뻣뻣한 사람이오."], ["Thornton’s as dour as a door-nail; an obstinate chap, every inch on him,—th’ oud bulldog!”", "발끝까지 고집불통이지. 그 늙은 불도그!”"]], [["“Poor Bessy!”", "“가엾은 베시!”"], ["said Margaret, turning round to her.", "마거릿이 그녀 쪽으로 돌아앉으며 말했다."], ["“You sigh over it all.", "“이 모든 일에 한숨만 쉬는군요."], ["You don’t like struggling and fighting as your father does, do you?”", "아버지처럼 겨루고 싸우는 걸 좋아하지 않죠, 그렇죠?”"]], [["“No!”", "“네!”"], ["said she, heavily.", "그녀가 무겁게 말했다."], ["“I’m sick on it.", "“넌더리가 나요."], ["I could have wished to have had other talk about me in my latter days, than just the clashing and clanging and clattering that has ever wearied a’ my life long, about work and wages, and masters, and hands, and knobsticks.”", "나는 마지막 날들에 다른 이야기를 들으며 지냈으면 했어요. 일이니 삯이니 공장주니 직공이니 파업 파괴자니 하며 부딪히고 쨍강거리고 덜컹거리는, 평생 나를 지치게만 한 그 소리 말고요.”"]], [["“Poor wench! latter days be farred!", "“가엾은 것, 마지막 날들은 무슨 마지막 날들이냐!"], ["Thou’rt looking a sight better already for a little stir and change.", "조금 부산해지고 달라지니 벌써 훨씬 나아 보이는구먼."], ["Beside, I shall be a deal here to make it more lively for thee.”", "게다가 내가 여기 많이 있어 줄 테니 더 신이 날 거다.”"]], [["“Tobacco-smoke chokes me!”", "“담배 연기 때문에 숨이 막혀요!”"], ["said she, querulously.", "그녀가 짜증스레 말했다."]], [["“Then I’ll never smoke no more i’ the house!”", "“그럼 다시는 집 안에서 담배를 안 피우마!”"], ["he replied, tenderly.", "그가 다정하게 대꾸했다."], ["“But why didst thou not tell me afore, thou foolish wench?”", "“한데 왜 진작 말하지 않았누, 이 어리석은 것아?”"]], [["She did not speak for a while, and then so low that only Margaret heard her:", "그녀는 한참 말이 없다가, 마거릿에게만 들릴 만큼 낮은 소리로 말했다."]], [["“I reckon, he’ll want a’ the comfort he can get out o’ either pipe or drink afore he’s done.”", "“아버지는 이 일이 끝나기 전에 파이프에서든 술에서든 얻을 수 있는 위로란 위로는 다 필요하실 거예요.”"]], [["Her father went out of doors, evidently to finish his pipe.", "아버지는 파이프를 마저 피우려는 듯 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Bessy said passionately,", "베시가 격하게 말했다."]], [["“Now am not I a fool,—am I not, Miss?—there, I knew I ought for to keep father at home, and away fro’ the folk that are always ready for to tempt a man, in time o’ strike, to go drink,—and there my tongue must needs quarrel with this pipe o’ his’n—and he’ll go off, I know he will,—as often as he wants to smoke—and nobody knows where it’ll end. I wish I’d letten myself be choked first.”", "“내가 바보 아니에요? 그렇죠, 아가씨? 저것 봐요, 나는 아버지를 집에 붙들어 두어야 한다는 걸, 파업 때면 언제든 사내를 꾀어 술 마시게 하려 드는 사람들한테서 떼어 놓아야 한다는 걸 알고 있었어요. 그런데 하필 내 혀가 아버지의 저 파이프를 두고 시비를 걸다니. 그러니 아버지는 나가시겠죠. 그럴 게 뻔해요. 담배 피우고 싶을 때마다요. 그러다 어디까지 갈지는 아무도 몰라요. 차라리 내가 먼저 숨이 막히게 두었더라면 좋았을걸.”"]], [["“But does your father drink?”", "“그런데 아버님이 술을 드시나요?”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“No—not to say drink,”", "“아니요, 술을 마신다고 할 정도는 아니에요.”"], ["replied she, still in the same wild excited tone.", "그녀가 여전히 그 사납고 들뜬 어조로 대답했다. “하지만 어쩌겠어요?"], ["“But what win ye have?", "아가씨한테도 다른 사람들처럼 그런 날이 있겠죠."], ["There are days wi’ you as wi’ other folk, I suppose, when yo’ get up and go through th’ hours, just longing for a bit of a change—a bit of a fillip, as it were.", "아침에 일어나 하루를 지나가면서 그저 뭔가 좀 달라지기를, 말하자면 뭔가 좀 신나는 일이 있기를 바라는 그런 날이요. 나도 그런 날이면 늘 다니던 데가 아닌 다른 빵집에서 4파운드짜리 빵을 사 온 적이 있어요."], ["I know I ha’ gone and bought a four-pounder out o’ another baker’s shop to common on such days, just because I sickened at the thought of going on for ever wi’ the same sight in my eyes, and the same sound in my ears, and the same taste i’ my mouth, and the same thought (or no thought, for that matter) in my head, day after day, for ever.", "그저 날마다 언제까지나 똑같은 광경이 눈에 들어오고, 똑같은 소리가 귀에 들리고, 똑같은 맛이 입에 남고, 똑같은 생각이, 아니 생각이랄 것도 없지만, 머릿속에 있는 걸 견딜 수가 없어서요. 나도 사내였으면 좋겠다고, 나가서 신나게 놀았으면 좋겠다고 바란 적이 있어요."], ["I’ve longed for to be a man to go spreeing, even if it were only a tramp to some new place in search o’ work.", "그게 그저 일자리 찾아 새로운 데로 떠도는 것뿐이라도요. 그런데 아버지는, 아니 사내들은 다, 언제까지나 똑같은 것과 일에 물리는 마음이 나보다 더 강해요."], ["And father—all men—have it stronger in ’em than me to get tired o’ sameness and work for ever.", "그러니 어쩌겠어요? 피가 좀 더 빨리, 더 신나게 돌게 하려고, 다른 때는 못 보는 것들을, 그림이니 거울이니 그런 것들을 보려고 진 가게에 들어간다 해도 그걸 크게 탓할 수는 없어요."], ["And what is ’em to do?", "하지만 아버지는 술꾼이었던 적이 없어요."], ["It’s little blame to them if they do go into th’ gin-shop for to make their blood flow quicker, and more lively, and see things they never see at no other time—pictures, and looking-glass, and such like.", "이따금 술 때문에 더 나빠지기는 했겠지만요. 다만 아시다시피,”"], ["But father never was a drunkard, though maybe, he’s got worse for drink, now and then.", "이제 그녀의 목소리가 구슬프고 애원하는 어조를 띠었다. “파업 때는 사내를 쓰러뜨릴 일이 많아요."], ["Only yo’ see,”", "다들 그렇게 희망차게 시작하는데도요."], ["and now her voice took a mournful, pleading tone, “at times o’ strike there’s much to knock a man down, for all they start so hopefully; and where’s the comfort to come fro’?", "그러니 위로는 어디서 오겠어요? 아버지도 화가 나고 미칠 것 같아지겠죠."], ["He’ll get angry and mad—they all do—and then they get tired out wi’ being angry and mad, and maybe ha’ done things in their passion they’d be glad to forget.", "다들 그러니까요. 그러다 화내고 미쳐 있는 것에도 지치고, 어쩌면 그 홧김에 나중에 잊고 싶어질 일을 저지르기도 하고요."], ["Bless yo’r sweet pitiful face!", "아가씨의 그 곱고 애처로운 얼굴에 복이 있기를!"], ["but yo’ dunnot know what a strike is yet.”", "하지만 아가씨는 아직 파업이 뭔지 몰라요.”"]], [["“Come, Bessy,” said Margaret, “I won’t say you’re exaggerating, because I don’t know enough about it: but, perhaps, as you’re not well, you’re only looking on one side, and there is another and a brighter to be looked to.”", "“자, 베시,” 마거릿이 말했다. “과장한다고는 하지 않을게요. 내가 잘 모르니까요. 하지만 어쩌면 몸이 안 좋으니 한쪽만 보고 있는지도 몰라요. 바라볼 만한 다른 면, 더 밝은 면도 있고요.”"]], [["“It’s all well enough for yo’ to say so, who have lived in pleasant green places all your life long, and never known want or care, or wickedness either, for that matter.”", "“평생 기분 좋은 푸른 곳에서 살아서 궁핍도 근심도, 하기야 악한 것도 몰랐을 아가씨야 그렇게 말하기 쉽겠죠.”"]], [["“Take care,”", "“조심해요.” 마거릿이 뺨을 붉히고 눈을 번뜩이며 말했다."], ["said Margaret, her cheek flushing, and her eye lightening, “how you judge, Bessy.", "“함부로 판단하지 말아요, 베시. 나는 이제 집으로 돌아가면 몹시 편찮으신 어머니를 뵈어요."], ["I shall go home to my mother, who is so ill—so ill, Bessy, that there’s no outlet but death for her out of prison of her great suffering; and yet I must speak cheerfully to my father, who has no notion of her real state, and to whom the knowledge must come gradually.", "어찌나 편찮으신지, 베시, 그 큰 고통의 감옥에서 벗어날 길이라고는 죽음밖에 없을 만큼이에요. 그런데도 나는 아버지께 밝게 말씀드려야 해요."], ["The only person—the only one who could sympathise with me and help me—whose presence could comfort my mother more than any other earthly thing—is falsely accused—would run the risk of death if he came to see his dying mother.", "아버지는 어머니의 진짜 상태를 짐작도 못 하시고, 그 사실은 조금씩 알려 드려야 하니까요. 나와 마음을 나누고 나를 도와줄 수 있는 단 한 사람, 이 세상 무엇보다 어머니에게 큰 위로가 될 그 사람은 억울한 누명을 쓰고 있어요."], ["This I tell you—only you, Bessy.", "돌아가시는 어머니를 뵈러 오려면 죽을 위험을 무릅써야 하고요."], ["You must not mention it.", "이건 베시한테만, 오직 베시한테만 하는 이야기예요."], ["No other person in Milton—hardly any other person in England knows.", "다른 데 옮기면 안 돼요. 밀턴의 다른 누구도, 잉글랜드의 거의 어느 누구도 모르는 일이니까요."], ["Have I not care?", "나에게 근심이 없나요?"], ["Do I not know anxiety, though I go about well-dressed, and have food enough?", "내가 옷을 잘 차려입고 먹을 것이 넉넉하다고 해서 애태우는 것을 모르나요?"], ["Oh, Bessy, God is just, and our lots are well portioned out by Him, although none but He knows the bitterness of our souls.”", "아, 베시, 하느님은 공정하시고, 우리 몫은 그분이 알맞게 나누어 주신 거예요. 우리 영혼의 쓰라림을 아시는 분은 오직 그분뿐이지만요.”"]], [["“I ask your pardon,” replied Bessy, humbly. “Sometimes, when I’ve thought o’ my life, and the little pleasure I’ve had in it, I’ve believed that, maybe, I was one of those doomed to die by the falling of a star from heaven; ‘And the name of the star is called Wormwood; and the third part of the waters became wormwood; and men died of the waters, because they were made bitter.’ One can bear pain and sorrow better if one thinks it has been prophesied long before for one: somehow, then it seems as if my pain was needed for the fulfilment; otherways it seems all sent for nothing.”", "“용서를 빌어요.” 베시가 겸손하게 대답했다. “가끔은 내 삶을, 그 안에서 누린 얼마 안 되는 즐거움을 생각하면 나도 어쩌면 하늘에서 떨어지는 별에 맞아 죽도록 정해진 사람 가운데 하나인가 보다 하고 믿게 돼요. ‘그 별의 이름은 쑥이라 하더라. 물의 삼분의 일이 쑥이 되고, 그 물이 쓰게 된 까닭에 많은 사람이 죽더라.’ 오래전부터 자기를 두고 미리 말해진 것이라고 생각하면 아픔도 슬픔도 더 잘 견딜 수 있어요. 어쩐지 그러면 내 아픔이 그 말이 이루어지는 데 꼭 필요했던 것처럼 여겨지거든요. 안 그러면 다 아무것도 아닌 일로 보내진 것 같고요.”"]], [["“Nay, Bessy—think!”", "“아니에요, 베시, 생각해 봐요!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“God does not willingly inflict.", "“하느님은 즐겨 아픔을 내리시는 분이 아니에요."], ["Don’t dwell so much on the prophecies, but read the clearer parts of the Bible.”", "예언에만 그렇게 매달리지 말고 성경에서 더 분명한 부분을 읽어요.”"]], [["“I dare say it would be wiser; but where would I hear such grand words of promise—hear tell o’ anything so far different fro’ this dreary world, and this town above a’ as in Revelations?", "“그러는 게 더 지혜롭겠지요. 하지만 이 쓸쓸한 세상과, 무엇보다 이 도시와 그토록 다른 무엇에 대한 이야기를, 그렇게 웅장한 약속의 말씀을 요한계시록 말고 어디서 들을 수 있겠어요?"], ["Many’s the time I’ve repeated the verses in the seventh chapter to myself, just for the sound.", "나는 일곱째 장의 구절들을 그저 그 소리가 좋아서 몇 번이고 혼자 되뇌었어요."], ["It’s as good as an organ, and as different from every day, too.", "오르간 소리만큼이나 좋고, 여느 날들과는 또 그만큼 다르니까요."], ["No, I cannot give up Revelations.", "아니에요, 나는 요한계시록을 놓을 수 없어요."], ["It gives me more comfort than any other book i’ the Bible.”", "성경의 다른 어느 책보다 나에게 큰 위로를 주거든요.”"]], [["“Let me come and read you some of my favourite chapters.”", "“내가 와서 좋아하는 장들을 몇 개 읽어 줄게요.”"]], [["“Ay,”", "“네,” 그녀가 게걸스레 말했다."], ["said she greedily, “come.", "“오세요. 아버지도 어쩌면 들으실 거예요."], ["Father will maybe hear yo.’ He’s deaved wi’ my talking; he says it’s all nought to do with the things o’ to-day, and that’s his business.”", "아버지는 내 말에는 귀가 먹으셨어요. 그런 건 다 오늘 일과는 아무 상관이 없고, 자기 일은 오늘 일이라면서요.”"]], [["“Where is your sister?”", "“동생은 어디 갔어요?”"]], [["“Gone fustian cutting.", "“퍼스티언 천 깎으러 갔어요."], ["I were loth to let her go; but somehow we must live; and th’ Union can’t afford us much.”", "보내기 싫었지만 그래도 먹고는 살아야 하니까요. 조합이 우리한테 많이 줄 형편도 못 되고요.”"]], [["“Now I must go.", "“이제 가 봐야겠어요."], ["You have done me good, Bessy.”", "베시 덕분에 좋았어요.”"]], [["“I done you good!”", "“내가 아가씨한테 좋은 일을 했다고요!”"]], [["“Yes, I came here very sad, and rather too apt to think my own cause for grief was the only one in the world.", "“네, 나는 아주 슬픈 마음으로 여기 왔고, 내 슬픔의 까닭만이 세상에 하나뿐인 것처럼 여기기 쉬웠어요."], ["And now I hear how you have had to bear for years, and that makes me stronger.”", "그런데 이제 베시가 여러 해 동안 무엇을 견뎌 왔는지 들으니 그게 나를 더 단단하게 해 줘요.”"]], [["“Bless yo’!", "“아이고!"], ["I thought a’ the good-doing was on the side of gentlefolk.", "나는 좋은 일 하는 건 다 양반들 쪽 몫인 줄 알았어요."], ["I shall get proud if I think I can do good to yo’.”", "내가 아가씨한테 좋은 일을 할 수 있다고 생각하면 우쭐해지겠는데요.”"]], [["“You won’t do it if you think about it.", "“그걸 마음에 두면 못 하게 될 거예요."], ["But you’ll only puzzle yourself if you do, that’s one comfort.”", "하지만 마음에 둬 봐야 스스로 헷갈리기만 할 테니, 그게 하나 다행이네요.”"]], [["“You’re not like no one I ever seed.", "“아가씨는 내가 본 어느 누구와도 달라요."], ["I dunno what to make of yo’.”", "아가씨를 어떻게 봐야 할지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Nor I of myself.", "“나도 나를 모르는걸요."], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있어요!”"]], [["Bessy stilled her rocking to gaze after her.", "베시는 몸 흔들기를 멈추고 그녀의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."]], [["“I wonder if there are many folk like her down South.", "“남쪽에는 저 아가씨 같은 사람이 많을까."], ["She’s like a breath of country air, somehow.", "어쩐지 시골 바람 한 줄기 같아."], ["She freshens me up above a bit.", "나를 제법 상쾌하게 해 주는구나."], ["Who’d a thought that face—as bright and as strong as the angel I dream of—could have known the sorrow she speaks on?", "내가 꿈에서 보는 천사만큼이나 환하고 굳센 저 얼굴이 자기가 말하는 그런 슬픔을 알고 있었으리라고 누가 생각이나 했겠어."], ["I wonder how she’ll sin.", "저 아가씨는 어떻게 죄를 지을까."], ["All on us must sin.", "우리는 다 죄를 짓게 마련이니."], ["I think a deal on her, for sure.", "정말이지 저 아가씨 생각이 많이 나."], ["But father does the like, I see.", "그런데 아버지도 그런가 보더라."], ["And Mary even.", "메리까지도."], ["It’s not often hoo’s stirred up to notice much.”", "저 애가 무엇을 눈여겨보려고 마음이 동하는 일은 흔치 않은데.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII", "# 제18장"]], [["“My heart revolts within me, and two voices Make themselves audible within my bosom.”", "“내 마음이 속에서 들고일어나, 두 목소리가 내 가슴속에서 저마다 소리를 내는구나.”"], ["WALLENSTEIN.", "— 발렌슈타인."]], [["On Margaret’s return home she found two letters on the table: one was a note for her mother,—the other, which had come by the post, was evidently from her aunt Shaw—covered with foreign post-marks—thin, silvery, and rustling.", "마거릿이 집에 돌아와 보니 탁자 위에 편지 두 통이 놓여 있었다. 하나는 어머니 앞으로 온 짧은 편지였고, 다른 하나는 우편으로 온 것으로 보아하니 쇼 이모가 보낸 것이 분명했다."], ["She took up the other, and was examining it, when her father came in suddenly:", "외국 소인이 잔뜩 찍힌, 얇고 은빛이 도는 바스락거리는 편지였다. 그녀가 그 편지를 집어 들고 이리저리 살펴보고 있는데 아버지가 불쑥 들어왔다."]], [["“So your mother is tired, and gone to bed early!", "“어머니가 피곤해서 일찍 잠자리에 드셨다더구나!"], ["I’m afraid, such a thundery day was not the best in the world for the doctor to see her.", "이렇게 천둥 치는 날에 의사가 어머니를 보러 온 것은 아무래도 세상에서 가장 좋지 못한 일이었던 것 같다."], ["What did he say?", "뭐라고 하더냐?"], ["Dixon tells me he spoke to you about her.”", "딕슨 말로는 그 사람이 너한테 어머니 이야기를 했다던데.”"]], [["Margaret hesitated.", "마거릿은 머뭇거렸다."], ["Her father’s looks became more grave and anxious:", "아버지의 얼굴빛이 한층 심각하고 불안해졌다."]], [["“He does not think her seriously ill?”", "“중한 병이라고 여기는 건 아니겠지?”"]], [["“Not at present; she needs care, he says; he was very kind, and he said he would call again, and see how his medicines worked.”", "“지금 당장은 아니라고 하셨어요. 몸조리가 필요하다고요. 아주 친절하셨고, 약이 어떻게 듣는지 보러 다시 들르시겠다고 하셨어요.”"]], [["“Only care?—he did not recommend change of air?—he did not say this smoky town was doing her any harm, did he, Margaret?”", "“몸조리뿐이라고?—공기 좋은 데로 옮기라는 말은 없었고?—이 연기투성이 도시가 어머니한테 해롭다는 말도 하지 않았지, 마거릿?”"]], [["“No!", "“네!"], ["not a word,”", "한마디도 없으셨어요.”"], ["she replied, gravely.", "그녀가 무겁게 대답했다."], ["“He was anxious, I think.”", "“걱정하시는 것 같기는 했지만요.”"]], [["“Doctors have that anxious manner; it’s professional,”", "“의사들은 다들 그렇게 걱정스러운 태도를 짓는 법이지. 직업이 그래서 그렇다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Margaret saw, in her father’s nervous ways, that the first impression of possible danger was made upon his mind, in spite of all his making light of what she told him. He could not forget the subject,—could not pass from it to other things; he kept recurring to it through the evening, with an unwillingness to receive even the slightest unfavourable idea, which made Margaret inexpressibly sad.", "마거릿은 아버지의 안절부절못하는 모습에서, 자기가 전한 말을 아버지가 아무리 대수롭지 않게 넘기려 해도 위험할지 모른다는 첫 예감이 이미 그 마음에 새겨졌음을 알아보았다. 아버지는 그 문제를 잊지 못했다. 거기서 다른 이야기로 넘어가지 못했다. 저녁 내내 그는 그 이야기로 자꾸만 되돌아왔고, 조금이라도 나쁜 쪽의 생각은 한사코 받아들이려 하지 않았다. 그것이 마거릿을 이루 말할 수 없이 슬프게 했다."]], [["“This letter is from Aunt Shaw, papa.", "“이 편지는 쇼 이모한테서 온 거예요, 아버지."], ["She has got to Naples, and finds it too hot, so she has taken apartments at Sorrento.", "나폴리에 이르셨는데 너무 덥다고 소렌토에 방을 얻으셨대요."], ["But I don’t think she likes Italy.”", "그렇지만 이모는 이탈리아가 마음에 들지 않으시는 것 같아요.”"]], [["“He did not say anything about diet, did he?”", "“먹는 것에 대해서는 아무 말도 없었느냐?”"]], [["“It was to be nourishing, and digestible.", "“자양분이 있고 소화가 잘되는 것으로 하라고요."], ["Mamma’s appetite is pretty good, I think.”", "어머니는 입맛이 꽤 좋으신 편이잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes!", "“그래!"], ["and that makes it all the more strange he should have thought of speaking about diet.”", "그러니 그 사람이 음식 이야기를 꺼냈다는 게 더욱 이상하구나.”"]], [["“I asked him, papa.”", "“제가 여쭤본 거예요, 아버지.”"], ["Another pause.", "또 한 번 말이 끊겼다."], ["Then Margaret went on: “Aunt Shaw says she has sent me some coral ornaments, papa: but,”", "이윽고 마거릿이 말을 이었다. “쇼 이모가 저한테 산호 장신구를 보내셨대요, 아버지."], ["added Margaret, half smiling, “she’s afraid the Milton dissenters won’t appreciate them.", "그런데,” 마거릿이 반쯤 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["She has got all her ideas of Dissenters from the Quakers, has she not?”", "“밀턴의 비국교도들이 그 진가를 알아보지 못할까 봐 걱정이시래요. 이모는 비국교도에 대한 생각을 죄다 퀘이커교도한테서 얻으신 거죠, 그렇죠?”"]], [["“If ever you hear or notice that your mother wishes for anything, be sure you let me know.", "“어머니가 무엇인가 바라는 낌새를 듣거나 눈치채거든 꼭 나한테 알려 다오."], ["I am so afraid she does not tell me always what she would like.", "어머니가 바라는 것을 늘 나한테 말해 주지는 않는 것 같아 그게 걱정이구나."], ["Pray, see after that girl Mrs. Thornton named.", "부디 손턴 부인이 일러 준 그 아이 일도 알아보아라."], ["If we had a good, efficient house-servant, Dixon could be constantly with her, and I’d answer for it we’d soon set her up amongst us, if care will do it.", "착실하고 야무진 하녀를 하나 두면 딕슨이 늘 어머니 곁에 붙어 있을 수 있을 테고, 몸조리로 될 일이라면 우리 힘으로 곧 일으켜 세울 수 있으리라고 나는 장담한다."], ["She’s been very much tired of late, with the hot weather, and the difficulty of getting a servant.", "요즘 더운 날씨에다 하녀 구하는 일까지 겹쳐 어머니가 몹시 지쳤어."], ["A little rest will put her quite to rights—eh, Margaret?”", "좀 쉬면 아주 좋아질 게다—그렇지 않으냐, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I hope so,”", "“그러기를 바라요.”"], ["said Margaret,—but so sadly, that her father took notice of it.", "마거릿이 말했다. 그러나 그 말이 어찌나 슬프게 들렸던지 아버지가 알아차렸다."], ["He pinched her cheek.", "그는 딸의 뺨을 가만히 꼬집었다."]], [["“Come; if you look so pale as this, I must rouge you up a little.", "“자자. 그렇게 창백한 얼굴을 하고 있으면 내가 연지라도 발라 주어야겠구나."], ["Take care of yourself, child, or you’ll be wanting the doctor next.”", "몸조심해라, 얘야. 안 그러면 다음번엔 네가 의사를 부르게 될 테니.”"]], [["But he could not settle to anything that evening.", "그러나 그날 저녁 그는 무엇에도 마음을 붙이지 못했다."], ["He was continually going backwards and forwards, on laborious tiptoe, to see if his wife was still asleep.", "아내가 아직 자고 있는지 보려고 애써 발끝으로 걸으며 끊임없이 오갔다."], ["Margaret’s heart ached at his restlessness—his trying to stifle and strangle the hideous fear that was looming out of the dark places of his heart.", "마거릿은 그 안절부절못하는 모습에 가슴이 아팠다. 마음의 어두운 구석에서 어렴풋이 솟아오르는 그 끔찍한 두려움을 짓누르고 목 졸라 죽이려는 안간힘이었다."]], [["He came back at last, somewhat comforted.", "마침내 그가 얼마쯤 마음이 놓인 얼굴로 돌아왔다."]], [["“She’s awake now, Margaret.", "“이제 깨어났다, 마거릿."], ["She quite smiled as she saw me standing by her.", "내가 곁에 서 있는 걸 보고 활짝 웃더구나."], ["Just her old smile.", "예전 그 웃음 그대로였어."], ["And she says she feels refreshed, and ready for tea.", "개운하다고, 차를 마실 준비가 되었다고 하는구나."], ["Where’s the note for her?", "어머니한테 온 편지는 어디 있느냐?"], ["She want’s to see it.", "보고 싶어 하신다."], ["I’ll read it to her while you make tea.”", "네가 차를 준비하는 동안 내가 읽어 드리마.”"]], [["The note proved to be a formal invitation from Mrs. Thornton, to Mr., Mrs., and Miss Hale to dinner, on the twenty-first instant.", "그 편지는 손턴 부인이 헤일 씨와 헤일 부인과 헤일 양을 이달 21일 만찬에 초대하는 정식 초대장이었다."], ["Margaret was surprised to find an acceptance contemplated, after all she had learnt of sad probabilities during the day.", "마거릿은 그날 하루 동안 서글픈 가능성들에 대해 알게 된 것이 있는데도 초대를 받아들일 궁리를 하는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그러나 사실이 그러했다."], ["But so it was. The idea of her husband’s and daughter’s going to this dinner had quite captivated Mrs.", "남편과 딸이 그 만찬에 간다는 생각은, 마거릿이 편지 내용을 듣기도 전에 이미 헤일 부인의 마음을 온통 사로잡아 버렸다."], ["Hale’s fancy, even before Margaret had heard the contents of the note.", "그것은 병자의 단조로운 나날에 변화를 주는 사건이었다."], ["It was an event to diversify the monotony of the invalid’s life; and she clung to the idea of their going, with even fretful pertinacity, when Margaret objected.", "그래서 마거릿이 반대하자 그녀는 보채는 듯한 고집까지 부리며 두 사람이 가야 한다는 생각에 매달렸다."]], [["“Nay, Margaret: if she wishes it, I’m sure we’ll both go willingly.", "“아니다, 마거릿. 어머니가 그러기를 바란다면 우리 둘 다 기꺼이 가야지."], ["She never would wish it unless she felt herself really stronger—really better than we thought she was, eh, Margaret?” said Mr.", "정말로 기운이 나지 않고서야—우리가 생각했던 것보다 정말로 나아지지 않고서야 그런 걸 바랄 리가 없지 않으냐, 그렇지, 마거릿?”"], ["Hale, anxiously, as she prepared to write the note of acceptance, the next day.", "이튿날 그녀가 승낙의 답장을 쓰려고 채비하는데 헤일 씨가 불안한 투로 말했다."]], [["“Eh! Margaret?”", "“그렇지 않으냐, 마거릿?”"], ["questioned he, with a nervous motion of his hands.", "그가 손을 초조하게 놀리며 물었다."], ["It seemed cruel to refuse him the comfort he craved for.", "아버지가 그토록 애타게 구하는 위안을 거절하는 것은 잔인한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["And besides, his passionate refusal to admit the existence of fear, almost inspired Margaret herself with hope.", "게다가 두려움이 있다는 것을 한사코 인정하지 않으려는 그 격한 몸짓은 마거릿에게마저 거의 희망을 불어넣을 지경이었다."]], [["“I do think she is better since last night,”", "“저도 어머니가 어젯밤보다 나아지셨다고 생각해요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Her eyes look brighter, and her complexion clearer.”", "“눈빛도 더 맑아지시고 안색도 더 환해지셨어요.”"]], [["“God bless you,”", "“네게 하느님의 축복이 있기를.”"], ["said her father earnestly.", "아버지가 간절하게 말했다."], ["“But is it true?", "“그런데 정말이냐?"], ["Yesterday was so sultry every one felt ill.", "어제는 어찌나 무더웠는지 누구나 다 몸이 좋지 않았어."], ["It was a most unlucky day for Mr. Donaldson to see her on.”", "도널드슨 씨가 어머니를 보러 오기에는 참으로 운 나쁜 날이었지.”"]], [["So he went away to his day’s duties, now increased by the preparation of some lectures he had promised to deliver to the working people at a neighbouring Lyceum.", "그러고 나서 그는 그날의 일과를 보러 나갔다. 요즘은 이웃 라이시엄에서 노동자들에게 해 주기로 약속한 몇 차례의 강연을 준비하는 일까지 더해져 있었다."], ["He had chosen Ecclesiastical Architecture as his subject, rather more in accordance with his own taste and knowledge than as falling in with the character of the place or the desire for particular kinds of information among those to whom he was to lecture.", "그는 교회 건축을 주제로 골랐는데, 그 고장의 성격이나 강연을 들을 사람들이 알고 싶어 하는 종류의 지식에 맞추었다기보다는 자신의 취향과 학식에 어울리는 쪽이었다."], ["And the institution itself, being in debt, was only too glad to get a gratis course from an educated and accomplished man like Mr. Hale, let the subject be what it might.", "그리고 그 기관 자체가 빚을 지고 있던 터라, 주제가 무엇이든 헤일 씨처럼 교양 있고 학식 있는 사람에게서 무료 강좌를 얻어 낸 것만으로도 더없이 반가운 일이었다."]], [["“Well, mother,” asked Mr.", "“그래, 어머니.”"], ["Thornton that night, “who have accepted your invitations for the twenty-first?”", "그날 밤 손턴 씨가 물었다. “21일 초대에 누가 오겠다고 했습니까?”"]], [["“Fanny, where are the notes?", "“패니야, 답장들이 어디 있느냐?"], ["The Slicksons accept, Collingbrooks accept, Stephenses accept, Browns decline.", "슬릭슨 집안은 온다고 했고, 콜링브룩 집안도 온다고 했고, 스티븐스 집안도 온다고 했고, 브라운 집안은 못 온다는구나."], ["Hales—father and daughter come—mother too great an invalid—Macphersons come, and Mr. Horsfall, and Mr.", "헤일 집안은—아버지와 딸이 오고—어머니는 몸이 너무 안 좋다고 하고—맥퍼슨 집안이 오고, 호스폴 씨와 영 씨도 온다."], ["Young. I was thinking of asking the Porters, as the Browns can’t come.”", "브라운 집안이 못 온다니 포터 집안을 부를까 생각하던 참이다.”"]], [["“Very good. Do you know, I’m really afraid Mrs.", "“좋습니다."], ["Hale is very far from well, from what Dr. Donaldson says.”", "그런데 말이지요, 저는 도널드슨 의사가 하는 말로 미루어 헤일 부인이 정말 많이 편찮으신 게 아닌가 걱정입니다.”"]], [["“It’s strange of them to accept a dinner-invitation if she’s very ill,”", "“그렇게 많이 아프시다면서 만찬 초대에 응하다니 이상하네요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“I didn’t say very ill,”", "“많이 아프다고는 하지 않았다.”"], ["said her brother, rather sharply.", "오라비가 조금 날카롭게 말했다."], ["“I only said far from well.", "“몸이 성치 않다고만 했지."], ["They may not know it either.”", "그리고 그 집 사람들도 모르고 있을지 모른다.”"], ["And then he suddenly remembered that, from what Dr. Donaldson had told him, Margaret, at any rate, must be aware of the exact state of the case.", "그러고서 그는 도널드슨 의사가 들려준 말로 미루어 적어도 마거릿만은 병세가 정확히 어떠한지 알고 있을 수밖에 없다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐다."]], [["“Very probably they are quite aware of what you said yesterday, John—of the great advantage it would be to them—to Mr.", "“아마 그 사람들도 네가 어제 한 말을 잘 알고 있을 게다, 존."], ["Hale, I mean, to be introduced to such people as the Stephenses and the Collingbrooks.”", "스티븐스 집안이나 콜링브룩 집안 같은 사람들에게 소개받는 것이 그들에게—그러니까 헤일 씨에게 얼마나 큰 이득이 되겠느냐는 그 말 말이다.”"]], [["“I’m sure that motive would not influence them.", "“그런 속셈이 그분들을 움직였을 리 없습니다. 아니에요!"], ["No! I think I understand how it is.”", "저는 어찌 된 일인지 알 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“John!”", "“오빠도 참!”"], ["said Fanny, laughing in her little, weak, nervous way.", "패니가 특유의 가냘프고 신경질적인 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“How you profess to understand these Hales, and how you never will allow that we can know anything about them.", "“오빠는 그 헤일 집안을 어쩌면 그렇게 잘 아는 척하면서, 우리는 그 사람들에 대해 아무것도 알 수 없다고만 하시네요."], ["Are they really so very different to most people one meets with?”", "정말로 그 사람들이 흔히 만나는 사람들과 그렇게나 다른가요?”"]], [["She did not mean to vex him; but if she had intended it, she could not have done it more thoroughly.", "그녀는 오라비를 성나게 할 뜻이 없었다. 그러나 설령 그럴 뜻이 있었더라도 그보다 더 철저히 해내지는 못했을 것이다."], ["He chafed in silence, however, not deigning to reply to her question.", "그는 말없이 속을 끓일 뿐, 누이의 물음에 대꾸할 가치조차 두지 않았다."]], [["“They do not seem to me out of the common way,”", "“내가 보기에는 별날 것 없는 사람들이더라.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton.", "손턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“He appears a worthy kind of man enough; rather too simple for trade—so it’s perhaps as well he should have been a clergyman first, and now a teacher.", "“그 사람은 어지간히 점잖은 축인 듯하더구나. 장사를 하기에는 너무 순진해 보이니, 처음에는 성직자였다가 이제 교사가 된 것이 오히려 잘된 일이겠지."], ["She’s a bit of a fine lady, with her invalidism; and as for the girl—she’s the only one who puzzles me when I think about her,—which I don’t often do.", "그 부인은 저 병치레로 제법 귀부인 티를 내고 있고. 그 딸로 말하자면—생각할 때마다 나를 알 수 없게 만드는 유일한 아이다."], ["She seems to have a great notion of giving herself airs; and I can’t make out why.", "하기야 자주 생각하지도 않는다만. 그 애는 잘난 체하는 데 대단한 재주가 있는 것 같은데, 왜 그러는지를 모르겠구나."], ["I could almost fancy she thinks herself too good for her company at times.", "이따금은 자기가 함께 있는 사람들에게는 제가 너무 과분하다고 여기는 게 아닌가 싶을 지경이다."], ["And yet they’re not rich; from all I can hear they never have been.”", "게다가 그들은 부유하지도 않아. 내가 들은 바로는 부유했던 적도 없고.”"]], [["“And she’s not accomplished, mamma.", "“게다가 그 애는 재주도 없어요, 어머니."], ["She can’t play.”", "피아노도 못 친대요.”"]], [["“Go on, Fanny.", "“계속해 보렴, 패니."], ["What else does she want to bring her up to your standard?”", "네 수준까지 올라오려면 그분한테 또 무엇이 더 필요하냐?”"]], [["“Nay! John,”", "“그러지 마라, 존.”"], ["said his mother, “that speech of Fanny’s did no harm.", "어머니가 말했다. “패니가 한 말이 해될 것은 없지 않으냐."], ["I myself heard Miss Hale say she could not play.", "헤일 양이 피아노를 못 친다고 말하는 것을 나도 직접 들었다."], ["If you would let us alone, we could perhaps like her, and see her merits.”", "네가 우리를 좀 내버려 두면 어쩌면 우리도 그 애를 좋아하고 그 애의 좋은 점도 알아볼 수 있을 게다.”"]], [["“I’m sure I never could!”", "“저는 정말이지 절대로 못 그럴 거예요!”"], ["murmured Fanny, protected by her mother.", "어머니의 비호 아래 패니가 중얼거렸다."], ["Mr. Thornton heard, but did not care to reply.", "손턴 씨는 들었으나 굳이 대꾸하려 들지 않았다."], ["He was walking up and down the dining-room, wishing that his mother would order candles, and allow him to set to work at either reading or writing, and so put a stop to the conversation.", "그는 식당 안을 왔다 갔다 하며 어머니가 촛불을 가져오라고 일러 자기가 읽든 쓰든 일을 시작할 수 있게 해 주기를, 그리하여 이 대화가 끝나기를 바라고 있었다."], ["But he never thought of interfering in any of the small domestic regulations that Mrs. Thornton observed, in habitual remembrance of her old economies.", "그러나 손턴 부인이 지난날의 절약을 늘 잊지 않고 지켜 오는 그 자잘한 살림 규칙들에 간섭할 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Mother,”", "“어머니.”"], ["said he, stopping, and bravely speaking out the truth, “I wish you would like Miss Hale.”", "그가 걸음을 멈추고 용감하게 속마음을 털어놓았다. “저는 어머니가 헤일 양을 좋아해 주셨으면 합니다.”"]], [["“Why,”", "“어째서냐?”"], ["asked she, startled by his earnest, yet tender manner.", "그녀가 아들의 간절하면서도 부드러운 태도에 놀라 물었다."], ["“You’re never thinking of marrying her?—a girl without a penny.”", "“설마 그 애와 혼인할 생각을 하는 건 아니겠지?—땡전 한 푼 없는 처녀와.”"]], [["“She would never have me,”", "“그분은 절대로 저를 받아 주지 않을 겁니다.”"], ["said he, with a short laugh.", "그가 짧게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“No, I don’t think she would,”", "“그래, 나도 그러지 않으리라 본다.”"], ["answered his mother.", "어머니가 대꾸했다."], ["“She laughed in my face, when I praised her for speaking out something Mr.", "“벨 씨가 너를 좋게 말한 것을 그 애가 그대로 옮겨 준 일을 두고 내가 칭찬했더니, 그 애는 내 면전에서 웃더구나."], ["Bell had said in your favour.", "그렇게 솔직하게 구는 것이 나는 마음에 들었다."], ["I liked the girl for doing so frankly, for it made me sure she had no thought of you; and the next minute she vexed me so by seeming to think—— Well, never mind!", "그 덕에 그 애가 너를 마음에 두고 있지 않다는 확신이 섰으니까. 그런데 바로 다음 순간에는 그 애가 무슨 생각을 하는 것처럼 굴어서 나를 어찌나 언짢게 하던지—그래, 됐다!"], ["Only you’re right in saying she’s too good an opinion of herself to think of you!", "다만 그 애가 제 잘난 맛에 겨워 너 따위는 생각도 하지 않는다는 네 말만은 옳다!"], ["The saucy jade!", "그 건방진 계집애!"], ["I should like to know where she’d find a better!”", "저보다 나은 사람을 대체 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 나도 좀 알고 싶구나!”"]], [["“Well, as I’m just as much convinced of the truth of what you have been saying as you can be; and as I have no thought or expectation of ever asking her to be my wife, you’ll believe me for the future that I’m quite disinterested in speaking about her.", "“그런데 저 역시 어머니 말씀이 옳다는 것을 어머니만큼이나 굳게 믿고 있고, 그분에게 제 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 생각도 기대도 전혀 없으니, 앞으로는 제가 그분 이야기를 하더라도 아무 사심이 없다는 것을 믿어 주십시오. 저는 그 아가씨에게 어려움이 닥치리라는 것을—어쩌면 어머니의 보살핌을 잃게 되리라는 것을 내다보고 있습니다."], ["I foresee trouble for that girl—perhaps, want of motherly care—and I only wish you to be ready to be a friend to her, in case she needs one.", "그러니 그분에게 벗이 필요할 때 어머니가 그 벗이 되어 주실 준비만 하고 계시기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 그리고 패니야.”"], ["Now, Fanny,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I trust you have delicacy enough to understand, that it is as great an injury to Miss Hale as to me—in fact, she would think it a greater—to suppose that I have any reason, more than I now give, for begging you and my mother to show her every kindly attention.”", "“네게도 그만한 분별은 있으리라 믿는다. 내가 지금 말한 것 말고 다른 무슨 까닭이 있어서 너와 어머니에게 그분을 정성껏 대해 달라고 부탁하는 것이라고 여기는 것은, 나한테도 그렇지만 헤일 양에게도 그만큼 큰 모욕이다—사실 그분은 더 큰 모욕으로 여길 게다.”"]], [["“I cannot forgive her her pride,”", "“나는 그 애의 오만만은 용서할 수 없다.”"], ["said his mother; “I will befriend her, if there is need, for your asking, John.", "어머니가 말했다. “네가 부탁하니, 필요할 때가 오면 그 애의 벗이 되어 주마, 존."], ["I would befriend Jezebel herself if you asked me.", "네가 부탁한다면 이세벨의 벗이라도 되어 주겠다."], ["But this girl, who turns up her nose at us all—who turns up her nose at you——”", "하지만 우리를 모두 깔보는 저 애가—너까지 깔보는 저 애가……”"]], [["“Nay, mother; I have never yet put myself, and I mean never to put myself, within reach of her contempt.”", "“아닙니다, 어머니. 저는 아직 한 번도 그분의 경멸이 닿을 자리에 저를 둔 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 생각이 없습니다.”"]], [["“Contempt, indeed!”—(One of Mrs. Thornton’s expressive snorts.)—“Don’t go on speaking of Miss Hale, John, if I’ve to be kind to her.", "“경멸이라니, 참!”—(손턴 부인 특유의 뜻이 담긴 콧방귀 가운데 하나였다.)—“내가 그 애한테 잘해 주어야 한다면, 존, 헤일 양 이야기는 더 하지 마라."], ["When I’m with her, I don’t know if I like or dislike her most; but when I think of her, and hear you talk of her, I hate her.", "그 애와 마주 앉아 있으면 내가 그 애를 좋아하는지 싫어하는지 알 수가 없다. 그런데 그 애를 떠올리고 네가 그 애 이야기를 하는 것을 들으면 밉기만 하다."], ["I can see she’s given herself airs to you as well as if you’d told me out.”", "네가 나한테 대놓고 말해 준 것이나 다름없이, 그 애가 너한테도 잘난 체했다는 게 훤히 보이는구나.”"]], [["“And if she has,”", "“설령 그랬다 해도,”"], ["said he—and then he paused for a moment—then went on: “I’m not a lad, to be cowed by a proud look from a woman, or to care for her misunderstanding me and my position.", "그가 말하다 잠시 멈추었다가 다시 이었다. “저는 여자의 오만한 눈길에 주눅 들거나, 그 사람이 저와 제 처지를 오해하는 것을 마음에 두거나 할 애송이가 아닙니다."], ["I can laugh at it!”", "웃어넘길 수 있어요!”"]], [["“To be sure!", "“물론이지!"], ["and at her too, with her fine notions, and haughty tosses!”", "그 애까지도 웃어넘기려무나. 그 잘난 생각이며 도도하게 고개 젖히는 꼴까지도!”"]], [["“I only wonder why you talk so much about her, then,”", "“그럼 어머니는 왜 그렇게 그 사람 이야기를 많이 하시는지 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“I’m sure, I’m tired enough of the subject.”", "“저는 정말이지 그 이야기라면 이제 지겨워요.”"]], [["“Well!”", "“그래!”"], ["said her brother, with a shade of bitterness.", "오라비가 씁쓸한 기색을 띠고 말했다."], ["“Suppose we find some more agreeable subject.", "“그럼 좀 더 마음에 드는 이야깃거리를 찾아보자."], ["What do you say to a strike, by way of something pleasant to talk about?”", "즐겁게 이야기할 거리로 파업은 어떠냐?”"]], [["“Have the hands actually turned out?”", "“직공들이 정말로 일손을 놓았느냐?”"], ["asked Mrs. Thornton, with vivid interest.", "손턴 부인이 눈에 띄게 관심을 보이며 물었다."]], [["“Hamper’s men are actually out.", "“햄퍼 씨네 사람들은 정말로 나갔습니다."], ["Mine are working out their week, through fear of being prosecuted for breach of contract.", "우리 쪽은 계약 위반으로 고소당할까 두려워 남은 한 주를 채우고 있고요."], ["I’d have had every one of them up and punished for it, that left his work before his time was out.”", "저라면 기한을 채우기 전에 일을 놓고 나간 놈은 하나도 남김없이 잡아다 벌을 받게 했을 겁니다.”"]], [["“The law expenses would have been more than the hands themselves were worth—a set of ungrateful naughts!”", "“소송 비용이 그 직공들 값어치보다 더 나갔을 게다—배은망덕한 쓸모없는 것들 같으니!”"], ["said his mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“To be sure.", "“그렇지요."], ["But I’d have shown them how to keep my word, and how I mean them to keep their’s.", "하지만 저는 제가 어떻게 약속을 지키는지, 또 저들에게 저들의 약속을 어떻게 지키게 할 셈인지 보여 주었을 겁니다."], ["They know me by this time.", "이제쯤은 저들도 저를 알지요."], ["Slickson’s men are off—pretty certain he won’t spend money in getting them punished.", "슬릭슨 씨네 사람들도 나갔습니다—그 사람이 저들을 벌주는 데 돈을 쓰지 않으리라는 건 거의 틀림없고요."], ["We’re in for a turn-out, mother.”", "우리는 파업을 맞게 됐습니다, 어머니.”"]], [["“I hope there are not many orders in hand?”", "“받아 놓은 주문이 많지 않으면 좋겠구나.”"]], [["“Of course there are.", "“물론 많지요."], ["They know that well enough.", "저들도 그건 잘 압니다."], ["But they don’t quite understand all, though they think they do.”", "하지만 저들은 다 안다고 여기면서도 정작 전부를 이해하지는 못합니다.”"]], [["“What do you mean, John?”", "“그게 무슨 말이냐, 존?”"]], [["Candles had been brought, and Fanny had taken up her interminable piece of worsted-work, over which she was yawning; throwing herself back in her chair, from time to time to gaze at vacancy, and think of nothing at her ease.", "촛불이 들어와 있었고, 패니는 그 끝없이 이어지는 털실 자수를 집어 들고는 그 위에서 하품을 하고 있었다. 이따금 의자에 몸을 젖히고 허공을 바라보며 마음 편히 아무 생각도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Why,”", "“그러니까요.”"], ["said he, “the Americans are getting their yarns so into the general market, that our only chance is producing them at a lower rate.", "그가 말했다. “미국 사람들이 자기네 실을 세계 시장에 어찌나 밀어 넣는지, 우리한테 남은 유일한 길은 더 낮은 값에 실을 만들어 내는 것뿐입니다."], ["If we can’t, we may shut up shop at once, and hands and masters alike go on tramp.", "그러지 못하면 당장 문을 닫고 직공이며 공장주며 다 같이 떠돌이 신세가 되는 거지요."], ["Yet these fools go back to the prices paid three years ago—nay, some of their leaders quote Dickinson’s price now—though they know as well as we do that, what with fines pressed out of their wages, as no honourable man would extort them, the real rate of wage paid at Dickinson’s is less than at ours.", "그런데도 이 바보들은 3년 전에 주던 값으로 돌아가자고 합니다—아니, 그 우두머리들 가운데 몇은 지금 디킨슨네 값을 들먹입니다. 명예를 아는 사람이라면 짜내지 않을 벌금을 임금에서 떼어 내는 것까지 셈하면 디킨슨네에서 실제로 주는 임금이 우리 쪽보다 적다는 것을 저들도 우리만큼이나 잘 알면서 말입니다."], ["Upon my word, mother, I wish the old combination laws were in force.", "정말이지 어머니, 저는 옛 단결금지법이 아직 살아 있었으면 싶습니다."], ["It is too bad to find out that fools—ignorant, wayward men like these—just by uniting their silly heads, are to rule over the fortunes of those who bring all the wisdom that knowledge and experience, and often painful thought and anxiety, can give.", "바보들이—저런 무지하고 제멋대로인 자들이—그저 그 어리석은 머리들을 한데 모았다는 것만으로, 지식과 경험이, 또 흔히는 괴로운 궁리와 근심이 줄 수 있는 온갖 지혜를 다 갖춘 사람들의 운명을 좌우하게 된다는 것은 너무하지 않습니까."], ["The next thing will be—indeed, we’ve all but come to it now—that we shall have to go and ask—stand hat in hand—and humbly ask the secretary of the Spinner’s Union to be so kind as to furnish us with labour at their own price.", "다음 차례는—사실 이제 거의 거기까지 왔습니다만—우리가 찾아가서, 모자를 벗어 손에 들고 서서, 방적공 조합 서기에게 부디 저희 쪽에 저희가 정한 값으로 일손을 대어 주십사 하고 굽실거리며 청하게 되는 겁니다."], ["That’s what they want—they, who haven’t the sense to see that, if we don’t get a fair share of the profits to compensate us for our wear and tear here in England, we can move off to some other country; and that, what with home and foreign competition, we are none of us likely to make above a fair share, and may be thankful enough if we can get that, in an average number of years.”", "저들이 바라는 게 바로 그것이지요—여기 잉글랜드에서 우리가 닳아 없어지는 대가로 이익의 정당한 몫을 얻지 못한다면 우리는 다른 나라로 옮겨 갈 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 국내외의 경쟁을 다 겪고 나면 우리 가운데 누구도 정당한 몫 이상을 벌기는 어렵고 여러 해를 평균 내어 그만큼이라도 건지면 다행이라는 것을 헤아릴 분별조차 없는 자들이 말입니다.”"]], [["“Can’t you get hands from Ireland?", "“아일랜드에서 직공을 데려올 수는 없느냐?"], ["I wouldn’t keep these fellows a day.", "나 같으면 저런 자들을 하루도 데리고 있지 않겠다."], ["I’d teach them that I was master, and could employ what servants I liked.”", "주인이 누구인지, 내가 원하는 일꾼을 마음대로 쓸 수 있다는 것을 가르쳐 주겠다.”"]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["to be sure, I can; and will, too, if they go on long.", "물론 데려올 수 있고, 저들이 오래 버티면 그렇게 할 겁니다."], ["It will be trouble and expense, and I fear there will be some danger; but I will do it, rather than give in.”", "성가시고 돈도 들 테고, 위험도 좀 따르지 않을까 싶습니다만, 굽히느니 그렇게 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“If there is to be all this extra expense, I’m sorry we’re giving a dinner just now.”", "“이렇게 여분의 비용이 다 들게 생겼다면, 하필 지금 만찬을 여는 것이 아쉽구나.”"]], [["“So am I,—not because of the expense, but because I shall have much to think about, and many unexpected calls on my time.", "“저도 그렇습니다—비용 때문이 아니라, 생각할 일이 많고 예상치 못한 일들이 제 시간을 자꾸 빼앗을 테니까요."], ["But we must have had Mr. Horsfall, and he does not stay in Milton long.", "하지만 호스폴 씨는 꼭 모셔야 했고, 그분은 밀턴에 오래 머무르지 않습니다."], ["And as for the others, we owe them dinners, and it’s all one trouble.”", "나머지 사람들로 말하면 우리가 만찬을 빚지고 있는 처지이고, 어차피 수고는 한 번이니까요.”"]], [["He kept on with his restless walk—not speaking any more, but drawing a deep breath from time to time, as if endeavouring to throw off some annoying thought.", "그는 여전히 안절부절못하는 걸음을 이어 갔다. 더 말은 하지 않았으나 이따금 깊은 숨을 몰아쉬었다."], ["Fanny asked her mother numerous small questions, all having nothing to do with the subject, which a wiser person would have perceived was occupying her attention.", "마치 성가신 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰는 것 같았다. 패니는 어머니에게 자잘한 것들을 잔뜩 물었는데, 그 어느 것도 어머니의 마음을 붙들고 있는 문제와는 상관이 없었다."], ["Consequently, she received many short answers.", "조금이라도 눈치 있는 사람이었다면 알아차렸을 문제였다."], ["She was not sorry when, at ten o’clock, the servants filed in to prayers.", "그래서 그녀는 짧은 대답만 잔뜩 받았다. 10시에 하인들이 기도를 하러 줄지어 들어왔을 때 그녀는 반가웠다."], ["These her mother always read,—first reading a chapter.", "기도는 늘 어머니가 읽었고, 그에 앞서 성경 한 장을 먼저 읽었다."], ["They were now working steadily through the Old Testament.", "그들은 그즈음 구약을 차근차근 읽어 나가고 있었다."], ["When prayers were ended, and his mother had wished him good-night, with that long steady look of hers which conveyed no expression of the tenderness that was in her heart, but yet had the intensity of a blessing, Mr.", "기도가 끝나고 어머니가 그 특유의 길고 흔들림 없는 눈길로—마음속에 든 다정함은 조금도 내비치지 않으면서도 축복과 같은 간절함을 담은 그 눈길로—아들에게 잘 자라고 인사한 뒤에도 손턴 씨는 걸음을 멈추지 않았다. 그의 사업 계획은 다가오는 이 파업 때문에 모조리 제동이 걸렸고 갑작스레 멈춰 서 버렸다."], ["Thornton continued his walk.", "근심 어린 여러 시간에 걸친 앞선 궁리가 다 헛일이 되었고, 저들의 미친 어리석음에 완전히 낭비되고 말았다."], ["All his business plans had received a check, a sudden pull-up, from this approaching turn-out.", "그 어리석음은 그 자신보다 저들에게 더 큰 해를 입힐 터였지만, 그들이 저지르고 있는 손해가 어디까지 미칠지는 아무도 가늠할 수 없었다."], ["The forethought of many anxious hours was thrown away, utterly wasted by their insane folly, which would injure themselves even more than him, though no one could set any limit to the mischief they were doing.", "그런데도 이런 자들이 공장주들에게 자본을 어떻게 쓸지 지시할 자격이 자기들에게 있다고 여기는 것이었다. 바로 그날 햄퍼 씨는 이렇게 말했다."], ["And these were the men who thought themselves fitted to direct the masters in the disposal of their capital; Hamper had said, only this very day, that if he were ruined by the strike, he would start life again, comforted by the conviction that those who had brought it on were in a worse predicament than he himself,—for he had head as well as hands, while they had only hands; and if they drove away their market, they could not follow it, nor turn to anything else.", "만일 자기가 이 파업으로 망한다 해도 다시 인생을 시작하겠으며, 이 일을 일으킨 자들이 자기보다 더 딱한 처지에 놓여 있다는 확신에서 위안을 얻겠노라고. 자기에게는 손 말고 머리도 있지만 저들에게는 손밖에 없고, 저들이 시장을 쫓아 버리고 나면 그 시장을 따라갈 수도, 다른 일로 돌아설 수도 없다는 것이었다."], ["But this thought was no consolation to Mr. Thornton.", "그러나 이런 생각도 손턴 씨에게는 위안이 되지 않았다."], ["It might be that revenge gave him no pleasure; it might be that he valued the position he had earned with the sweat of his brow, so much that he keenly felt its being endangered by the ignorance or folly of others,—so keenly that he had no thoughts to spare for what would be the consequences of their conduct to themselves.", "복수가 그에게 아무 기쁨도 주지 않았기 때문일 수도 있고, 이마의 땀으로 얻어 낸 자기 자리를 너무나 소중히 여긴 나머지 남들의 무지나 어리석음 탓에 그것이 위태로워지는 것을 뼈아프게 느꼈기 때문일 수도 있다. 어찌나 뼈아프게 느꼈던지 저들의 행동이 저들 자신에게 어떤 결과를 가져올지 헤아려 볼 마음의 여유조차 없었다."], ["He paced up and down, setting his teeth now and then.", "그는 이따금 이를 악물며 방 안을 오갔다."], ["At last it struck two.", "마침내 2시를 알리는 소리가 났다."], ["The candles were flickering in their sockets.", "촛불들이 촛대 안에서 가물거리고 있었다."], ["He lighted his own, muttering to himself:", "그는 제 몫의 초에 불을 붙이며 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Once for all they shall know whom they have got to deal with.", "“이번만은 저들도 자기들이 누구를 상대하고 있는지 알게 될 거다."], ["I can give them a fortnight,—no more.", "2주일은 줄 수 있다—그 이상은 안 된다."], ["If they don’t see their madness before the end of that time, I must have hands from Ireland.", "그때까지도 제 미친 짓을 깨닫지 못한다면 나는 아일랜드에서 직공을 데려와야 한다."], ["I believe it’s Slickson’s doing,—confound him and his dodges!", "이건 슬릭슨이 벌인 짓이 틀림없어—그 인간과 그 잔꾀들을 어쩌면 좋을까!"], ["He thought he was overstocked; so he seemed to yield at first, when the deputation came to him,—and of course, he only confirmed them in their folly, as he meant to do.", "자기 물건이 남아돈다고 여긴 게지. 그래서 대표단이 찾아왔을 때 처음엔 양보하는 척했고—물론 자기가 노린 대로 저들을 그 어리석음 속에 더욱 굳혀 놓은 거야."], ["That’s where it spread from.”", "거기서부터 번진 거다.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XIX", "# 제19장"]], [["“As angels in some brighter dreams Call to the soul when man doth sleep, So some strange thoughts transcend our wonted themes, And into glory peep.”", "“사람이 잠든 사이 한결 밝은 꿈결 속에서 천사들이 그 영혼을 부르듯, 어떤 낯선 생각들은 우리의 익숙한 상념을 훌쩍 넘어서서 영광 속을 들여다본다.”"], ["HENRY VAUGHAN.", "— 헨리 본."]], [["Mrs. Hale was curiously amused and interested by the idea of the Thornton dinner party.", "헤일 부인은 손턴가의 만찬이라는 발상에 묘하게 재미를 느끼고 마음이 쏠렸다."], ["She kept wondering about the details, with something of the simplicity of a little child, who wants to have all its anticipated pleasure described beforehand.", "그녀는 자잘한 것까지 자꾸만 궁금해했는데, 미리 맛볼 즐거움을 낱낱이 말로 들어 두고 싶어 하는 어린아이 같은 순진함이 어딘가 배어 있었다. 그러나 병자들이 보내는 단조로운 삶은 흔히 그들을 아이처럼 만든다."], ["But the monotonous life led by invalids often makes them like children, inasmuch as they have neither of them any sense of proportion in events, and seem each to believe that the walls and curtains which shut in their world, and shut out everything else, must of necessity be larger than anything hidden beyond.", "둘 다 일의 경중을 가늠하는 감각이 없고, 자기 세계를 가두고 그 밖의 모든 것을 막아 주는 벽과 커튼이 그 너머에 감춰진 무엇보다도 반드시 더 클 것이라고 저마다 믿는 듯하다는 점에서 그렇다. 게다가 헤일 부인에게도 처녀 시절의 허영이 있었고, 가난한 목사의 아내가 되면서 그 허영이 꺾이는 굴욕을 지나치다 싶게 느꼈을 것이다."], ["Besides Mrs.", "그것들은 눌리고 잠재워졌을 뿐 아주 꺼지지는 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 마거릿이 만찬을 위해 차려입은 모습을 볼 생각이 좋았고, 무엇을 입어야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 조바심을 내며 이야기했다."], ["Hale had had her vanities as a girl; had perhaps unduly felt their mortification when she became a poor clergyman’s wife;—they had been smothered and kept down; but they were not extinct; and she liked to think of seeing Margaret dressed for a party, and discussed what she should wear, with an unsettled anxiety that amused Margaret, who had been more accustomed to society in her one year in Harley Street than her mother in five and twenty years of Helstone.", "마거릿은 그것이 재미있었다. 할리 가에서 보낸 1년 동안 사교계에 익숙해진 정도가 어머니가 헬스톤에서 보낸 25년보다 더했으니 말이다."]], [["“Then you think you shall wear your white silk.", "“그러면 흰 비단옷을 입겠다는 거지."], ["Are you sure it will fit?", "정말 몸에 맞을까?"], ["It’s nearly a year since Edith was married!”", "이디스가 혼인한 지 거의 1년이 다 되어 가는데!”"]], [["“Oh, yes, mamma! Mrs.", "“아, 그럼요, 어머니!"], ["Murray made it, and it’s sure to be right; it may be a straw’s breadth shorter or longer-waisted, according to my having grown fat or thin.", "머리 부인이 지은 옷이니 틀림없이 맞을 거예요. 제가 살이 붙었느냐 빠졌느냐에 따라 허리 길이가 지푸라기 하나쯤 짧거나 길지도 모르지만요."], ["But I don’t think I’ve altered in the least.”", "그렇지만 저는 조금도 달라지지 않은 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Hadn’t you better let Dixon see it?", "“그래도 딕슨한테 한번 보여 주는 게 낫지 않겠니?"], ["It may have gone yellow with lying by.”", "오래 개켜 두어서 누렇게 바랬을지도 모르잖니.”"]], [["“If you like, mamma.", "“어머니가 원하시면요."], ["But if the worst comes to the worst, I’ve a very nice pink gauze which Aunt Shaw gave me, only two or three months before Edith was married.", "하지만 정 안 되면 쇼 이모가 주신 아주 고운 분홍 망사 옷이 있어요. 이디스가 혼인하기 겨우 두세 달 전에 받은 거예요."], ["That can’t have gone yellow.”", "그건 누렇게 바랬을 리가 없어요.”"]], [["“No!", "“그렇겠지!"], ["but it may have faded.”", "하지만 빛이 바랬을 수도 있잖니.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그럼요!"], ["then I’ve a green silk.", "그럼 초록 비단옷도 있어요."], ["I feel more as if it was the embarrassment of riches.”", "저는 오히려 너무 넘쳐서 곤란한 쪽인 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“I wish I knew what you ought to wear,”", "“네가 뭘 입어야 좋을지 알면 좋으련만.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, nervously.", "헤일 부인이 안절부절못하며 말했다."]], [["Margaret’s manner changed instantly.", "마거릿의 태도가 곧바로 달라졌다."], ["“Shall I go and put them on one after another, mamma, and then you could see which you liked best?”", "“제가 가서 하나씩 차례로 입어 볼까요, 어머니? 그러면 어느 것이 제일 마음에 드시는지 보실 수 있잖아요.”"]], [["“But—yes!", "“하지만……"], ["perhaps that will be best.”", "그래! 아마 그게 제일 좋겠구나.”"]], [["So off Margaret went.", "그리하여 마거릿은 자리를 떴다."], ["She was very much inclined to play some pranks when she was dressed up at such an unusual hour; to make her rich white silk balloon out into a cheese, to retreat backwards from her mother as if she were the queen; but when she found that these freaks of hers were regarded as interruptions to the serious business, and as such annoyed her mother, she became grave and sedate.", "이런 엉뚱한 시간에 차려입고 나니 그녀는 짓궂은 장난을 치고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 값진 흰 비단 치마를 빙 돌려 치즈 덩이처럼 부풀려 보기도 하고, 여왕이라도 되는 양 어머니 앞에서 뒷걸음질로 물러나 보기도 하고 싶었다."], ["What had possessed the world (her world) to fidget so about her dress, she could not understand; but that very afternoon, on naming her engagement to Bessy Higgins (apropos of the servant that Mrs.", "그러나 그런 장난이 진지한 일을 방해하는 것으로 여겨지고 그 때문에 어머니가 짜증을 낸다는 것을 알고는 정색을 하고 얌전해졌다. 세상이(자기 세계가) 어쩌자고 자기 옷차림 하나로 이토록 부산을 떠는지 그녀는 알 수 없었다."], ["Thornton had promised to enquire about), Bessy quite roused up at the intelligence.", "그런데 바로 그날 오후, 손턴 부인이 알아봐 주기로 한 하녀 이야기가 나온 김에 베시 히긴스에게 그 초대 이야기를 꺼내자, 베시는 그 소식에 아주 눈이 번쩍 뜨였다."]], [["“Dear!", "“어머나!"], ["and are you going to dine at Thornton’s at Marlborough Mills?”", "말버러 공장의 손턴 댁에 만찬을 하러 간다는 말이오?”"]], [["“Yes, Bessy.", "“네, 베시."], ["Why are you so surprised?”", "왜 그렇게 놀라요?”"]], [["“Oh, I dunno.", "“아, 모르겠구먼."], ["But they visit wi’ a’ th’ first folk in Milton.”", "그렇지만 그 댁은 밀턴에서 제일가는 사람들하고만 오간단 말이오.”"]], [["“And you don’t think we’re quite the first folk in Milton, eh, Bessy?”", "“그러면 베시는 우리가 밀턴에서 제일가는 축은 못 된다고 생각하는군요?”"]], [["Bessy’s cheeks flushed a little at her thought being thus easily read.", "베시는 제 속마음을 그렇게 쉽게 들킨 것에 뺨이 조금 붉어졌다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그게 말이오.”"], ["said she, “yo’ see, they thinken a deal o’ money here; and I reckon yo’ve not getten much.”", "그녀가 말했다. “여기서는 돈을 대단하게 치는데, 댁네는 돈이 그리 많지 않은 것 같아서 말이오.”"]], [["“No,”", "“그래요.”"], ["said Margaret, “that’s very true.", "마거릿이 말했다. “정말 그래요."], ["But we are educated people, and have lived amongst educated people.", "하지만 우리는 배운 사람들이고 배운 사람들 틈에서 살아왔어요."], ["Is there anything so wonderful, in our being asked out to dinner by a man who owns himself inferior to my father by coming to him to be instructed?", "우리 아버지한테 배우러 오면서 스스로 아버지보다 못하다고 인정한 사람이 우리를 만찬에 부른 것이 그렇게 놀랄 일인가요?"], ["I don’t mean to blame Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨를 흉보려는 건 아니에요."], ["Few drapers’ assistants, as he was once, could have made themselves what he is.”", "한때 그가 그랬듯 포목상 점원 노릇을 하다가 지금의 그처럼 될 수 있는 사람은 몇 안 되니까요.”"]], [["“But can yo’ give dinners back, in yo’re small house?", "“그렇지만 그 좁은 집에서 답례로 만찬을 낼 수 있겠수?"], ["Thornton’s house is three times as big.”", "손턴 댁 집은 세 배는 큰데.”"]], [["“Well, I think we could manage to give Mr. Thornton a dinner back, as you call it.", "“글쎄요, 베시 말대로 손턴 씨한테 답례로 만찬을 내는 정도는 어떻게 해낼 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["Perhaps not in such a large room, nor with so many people.", "그렇게 큰 방에서, 그렇게 많은 사람을 부르지는 못하겠지만요."], ["But I don’t think we’ve thought about it at all in that way.”", "그런데 우리는 그런 식으로는 전혀 생각해 보지도 않았어요.”"]], [["“I never thought yo’d be dining with Thorntons,”", "“나는 댁이 손턴 댁에서 만찬을 하리라고는 생각도 못 했구먼.”"], ["repeated Bessy.", "베시가 되뇌었다."], ["“Why, the mayor hissel’ dines there; and the members of Parliament and all.”", "“아니, 거기는 시장님까지 만찬을 하러 오고, 국회의원이며 다들 온단 말이오.”"]], [["“I think, I could support the honour of meeting the mayor of Milton.”", "“밀턴 시장님을 만나는 영광쯤은 저도 감당할 수 있을 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“But them ladies dress so grand!”", "“그렇지만 그 부인네들은 어찌나 으리으리하게 차려입는지!”"], ["said Bessy, with an anxious look at Margaret’s print gown, which her Milton eyes appraised at sevenpence a yard.", "베시가 마거릿의 무명옷을 걱정스럽게 바라보며 말했다. 그 옷을 두고 그녀의 밀턴 눈썰미는 야드당 7펜스짜리라고 어림했다."]], [["Margaret’s face dimpled up into a merry laugh.", "마거릿의 얼굴에 보조개가 지며 유쾌한 웃음이 터졌다. “고마워요, 베시."], ["“Thank you, Bessy, for thinking so kindly about my looking nice among all the smart people.", "내가 그 말쑥한 사람들 틈에서 근사해 보일지 그렇게 마음 써 주다니. 하지만 나한테는 으리으리한 옷이 얼마든지 있어요."], ["But I’ve plenty of grand gowns,—a week ago, I should have said they were far too grand for anything I should ever want here. But as I’m to dine at Mr.", "일주일 전만 해도 여기서 내가 쓸 만한 어떤 자리에도 너무 과하다고 말했을 옷들이지요."], ["Thornton’s, and perhaps to meet the mayor, I shall put on my very best gown, you may be sure.”", "하지만 손턴 씨 댁에서 만찬을 하고 어쩌면 시장님까지 만나게 될 테니, 내가 가진 제일 좋은 옷을 입을 거예요. 그건 믿어도 좋아요.”"]], [["“What win yo’ wear?”", "“어떤 걸 입을 거요?”"], ["asked Bessy, somewhat relieved.", "베시가 얼마쯤 마음이 놓인 듯 물었다."]], [["“White silk,”", "“흰 비단옷이요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“A gown I had for a cousin’s wedding, a year ago.”", "“1년 전에 사촌의 혼례 때 입으려고 지은 옷이에요.”"]], [["“That’ll do!”", "“그거면 되겠구먼!”"], ["said Bessy, falling back in her chair.", "베시가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다."], ["“I should be loth to have yo’ looked down upon.”", "“나는 댁이 업신여김을 받는 건 정말 싫소.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I’ll be fine enough, if that will save me from being looked down upon in Milton.”", "밀턴에서 업신여김을 받지 않을 수만 있다면 나도 얼마든지 근사하게 차려입을게요.”"]], [["“I wish I could see you dressed up,”", "“댁이 차려입은 걸 볼 수 있으면 좋겠구먼.”"], ["said Bessy.", "베시가 말했다."], ["“I reckon, yo’re not what folk would ca’ pretty; yo’ve not red and white enough for that.", "“댁은 사람들이 예쁘다고 할 만한 얼굴은 아닌 것 같소. 그러기엔 홍백이 곱게 어리지 않았으니 말이오."], ["But dun yo’ know, I ha’ dreamt of yo’, long afore ever I seed yo’.”", "그렇지만 있잖수, 나는 댁을 만나기 훨씬 전부터 꿈에서 댁을 보았소.”"]], [["“Nonsense, Bessy!”", "“말도 안 돼요, 베시!”"]], [["“Ay, but I did. Yo’r very face,—looking wi’ yo’r clear steadfast eyes out o’ th’ darkness, wi’ yo’r hair blown off from yo’r brow, and going out like rays round yo’r forehead, which was just as smooth and straight as it is now,—and yo’ always came to give me strength, which I seemed to gather out o’ yo’r deep comforting eyes,—and yo’ was drest in shining raiment—just as yo’r going to be drest. So, yo’ see, it was yo’!”", "“아니, 정말 그랬소. 바로 그 얼굴이었소. 어둠 속에서 그 맑고 흔들림 없는 눈으로 내다보고, 머리카락은 이마에서 뒤로 날려 지금처럼 매끈하고 반듯한 이마 둘레로 빛살처럼 뻗어 나가고. 그리고 댁은 언제나 나에게 힘을 주러 왔고, 나는 그 깊고 다독여 주는 눈에서 그 힘을 길어 올리는 것 같았소. 그리고 댁은 빛나는 옷을 입고 있었지. 바로 지금 입으려는 그 옷처럼 말이오. 그러니 보시우, 그게 댁이었단 말이오!”"]], [["“Nay, Bessy,”", "“아니에요, 베시.”"], ["said Margaret, gently, “it was but a dream.”", "마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다. “그건 그저 꿈이었을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“And why might na I dream a dream in my affliction as well as others?", "“그러면 어째서 내가 이 괴로움 중에 남들처럼 꿈 하나 못 꾼단 말이오?"], ["Did not many a one i’ the Bible?", "성경에도 꿈꾼 사람이 한둘이 아니지 않수?"], ["Ay, and see visions too!", "그럼, 환상도 보았지!"], ["Why, even my father thinks a deal o’ dreams!", "아니, 우리 아버지도 꿈을 대단하게 치는데! 다시 말하지만 나는 댁을 똑똑히 보았소."], ["I tell yo’ again, I saw yo’ as plainly, coming swiftly towards me, wi’ yo’r hair blown back wi’ the very swiftness o’ the motion, just like the way it grows, a little standing off like; and the white shining dress on yo’ve getten to wear.", "나를 향해 성큼성큼 다가오는데, 그 걸음이 하도 빨라 머리카락이 뒤로 날렸소. 꼭 원래 나 있는 결대로, 조금 들뜬 것처럼 말이오."], ["Let me come and see yo’ in it.", "그리고 댁이 입게 될 그 희고 빛나는 옷을 입고 있었소."], ["I want to see yo’ and touch yo’ as in very deed yo’ were in my dream.”", "내가 가서 그 옷 입은 댁을 보게 해 주우. 꿈에서 정말로 그랬던 것처럼 댁을 보고 만져 보고 싶소.”"]], [["“My dear Bessy, it is quite a fancy of yours.”", "“베시, 그건 아무래도 당신의 공상이에요.”"]], [["“Fancy or no fancy,—yo’ve come, as I knew yo’ would, when I saw yo’r movement in my dream,—and when yo’re here about me, I reckon I feel easier in my mind, and comforted, just as a fire comforts one on a dree day. Yo’ said it were on th’ twenty-first; please God, I’ll come and see yo’.”", "“공상이든 아니든, 댁은 왔잖수. 꿈에서 댁이 다가오는 모습을 보았을 때 내가 알았던 그대로 말이오. 그리고 댁이 내 곁에 있으면 마음이 한결 편안해지고 위로가 되는구먼. 을씨년스러운 날에 불이 사람을 위로해 주듯이 말이오. 그게 21일이라고 했지요. 하느님이 허락하시면 내가 가서 댁을 보겠소.”"]], [["“Oh Bessy!", "“아, 베시!"], ["you may come and welcome; but don’t talk so—it really makes me sorry.", "오면 반갑게 맞을게요. 하지만 그런 말은 하지 말아요."], ["It does indeed.”", "정말이지 마음이 아파요. 정말로요.”"]], [["“Then I’ll keep it to mysel’, if I bite my tongue out.", "“그럼 혀를 깨물어서라도 혼자 담아 두겠소."], ["Not but what it’s true for all that.”", "그래도 그게 사실이기는 하지만.”"]], [["Margaret was silent.", "마거릿은 잠자코 있었다."], ["At last she said, “Let us talk about it sometimes, if you think it true.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다. “그게 사실이라고 여긴다면 언젠가 그 이야기를 나누기로 해요."], ["But not now.", "하지만 지금은 아니에요."], ["Tell me, has your father turned out?”", "그런데 아버님도 파업에 나가셨나요?”"]], [["“Ay!”", "“그럼요!”"], ["said Bessy heavily—in a manner very different from that she had spoken in but a minute or two before.", "베시가 무겁게 말했다. 겨우 한두 순간 전에 말하던 것과는 아주 다른 투였다."], ["“He and many another,—all Hamper’s men,—and many a one besides.", "“아버지도 다른 사람들도 다요. 햄퍼 씨네 일꾼들이 죄다, 그리고 그 밖에도 숱하게."], ["Th’ women are as bad as th’ men, in their savageness, this time.", "이번에는 여자들도 사나운 걸로 치면 남자들 못지않소."], ["Food is high,—and they mun have food for their childer, I reckon.", "먹을 게 비싸니 자식들 먹일 것을 구해야 하지 않겠수."], ["Suppose Thorntons sent ’em their dinner out,—th’ same money, spent on potatoes and meal, would keep many a crying baby quiet, and hush up its mother’s heart for a bit!”", "손턴 댁이 저녁 한 끼 값을 밖으로 내보내 준다면, 그 돈으로 감자와 곡식가루를 사서 우는 아기 여럿을 달래고 그 어미들 마음도 잠시나마 잠재울 텐데!”"]], [["“Don’t speak so!”", "“그런 말 말아요!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You’ll make me feel wicked and guilty in going to this dinner.”", "“그러면 이 만찬에 가는 것이 못되고 죄스럽게 느껴질 거예요.”"]], [["“No!”", "“아니오!”"], ["said Bessy.", "베시가 말했다."], ["“Some’s pre-elected to sumptuous feasts, and purple and fine linen,—may be yo’re one on ’em.", "“어떤 사람은 진수성찬과 자주색 옷과 고운 베옷을 누리도록 미리 정해져 있소. 어쩌면 댁도 그중 하나겠지."], ["Others toil and moil all their lives long—and the very dogs are not pitiful in our days, as they were in the days of Lazarus.", "다른 사람들은 한평생 죽도록 애쓰며 살고. 게다가 요즘 세상 개들은 나사로 시절의 그 개들만큼도 가엾이 여길 줄을 모르오."], ["But if yo’ ask me to cool yo’re tongue wi’ th’ tip of my finger, I’ll come across the great gulf to yo’ just for th’ thought o’ what yo’ve been to me here.”", "그렇지만 댁이 나더러 손가락 끝으로 혀를 식혀 달라고 청한다면, 나는 그 큰 구렁을 건너서라도 댁에게 가겠소. 여기서 댁이 나에게 어떤 사람이었는지 그 생각만으로도 말이오.”"]], [["“Bessy; you’re very feverish!", "“베시, 열이 몹시 오르고 있어요!"], ["I can tell it in the touch of your hand, as well us in what you’re saying.", "손을 만져 봐도 알겠고, 하는 말을 들어도 알겠어요."], ["It won’t be division enough, in that awful day, that some of us have been beggars here, and some of us have been rich,—we shall not be judged by that poor accident, but by our faithful following of Christ.”", "그 무서운 날에 우리 가운데 누구는 여기서 거지였고 누구는 부자였다는 것이 갈라 세우는 기준이 되지는 못할 거예요. 우리는 그런 가엾은 우연으로 심판받는 게 아니라 그리스도를 얼마나 성실히 따랐는가로 심판받을 테니까요.”"]], [["Margaret got up, and found some water; and soaking her pocket-handkerchief in it, she laid the cool wetness on Bessy’s forehead, and began to chafe the stone-cold feet.", "마거릿은 일어나 물을 찾아다가 손수건을 적셔 그 시원한 물기를 베시의 이마에 얹어 주고, 돌처럼 차가운 발을 문질러 주기 시작했다."], ["Bessy shut her eyes, and allowed herself to be soothed.", "베시는 눈을 감고 다독여지는 대로 몸을 맡겼다."], ["At last she said,", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yo’d ha’ been deaved out o’ yo’r five wits, as well as me, if yo’d had one body after another coming in to ask for father, and staying to tell me each one their tale.", "“댁도 나처럼 정신이 아주 나가 버렸을 거요. 아버지를 찾는다고 한 사람 한 사람 줄줄이 들어와서는 저마다 제 사연을 늘어놓고 앉아 있었으면 말이오."], ["Some spoke o’ deadly hatred, and made my blood run cold wi’ the terrible things they said o’ th’ masters,—but more, being women, kept plaining, plaining (wi’ the tears running down their cheeks, and never wiped away, nor heeded), of the price o’ meat, and how their childer could na sleep at nights for th’ hunger.”", "어떤 이들은 사무치는 미움을 이야기하며 공장주들에 대해 하도 끔찍한 말을 해서 내 피가 다 얼어붙었소. 그렇지만 여자들이 더 많았는데, 뺨으로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러도 닦지도 않고 아랑곳도 않은 채 고기값이 어떻고 자식들이 배가 고파 밤에 잠을 못 잔다고 하소연하고 또 하소연했다오.”"]], [["“And do they think the strike will mend this?”", "“그럼 그이들은 파업이 그걸 고쳐 주리라고 생각하는 건가요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“They say so,”", "“그렇다고들 하오.”"], ["replied Bessy.", "베시가 대답했다."], ["“They do say trade has been good for long, and the masters has made no end o’ money; how much father doesn’t know, but, in course, th’ Union does; and, as it is natural, they wanted their share o’ th’ profits, now that food is getting dear; and th’ Union says they’ll not be doing their duty if they don’t make th’ masters give ’em their share.", "“오래도록 장사가 잘되어서 공장주들이 돈을 어마어마하게 벌었다고 하더구먼. 얼마나 벌었는지 아버지는 모르지만 조합이야 물론 알고 있지."], ["But masters has getten th’ upper hand somehow; and I’m feared they’ll keep it now and evermore.", "그러니 먹을 게 비싸지는 마당에 그 이문에서 제 몫을 받고 싶은 게 당연하지 않겠수. 그리고 조합은 공장주들한테서 그 몫을 받아 내지 못하면 제 할 일을 못 한 거라고 하고."], ["It’s like th’ great battle o’ Armageddon, the way they keep on, grinning and fighting at each other, till even while they fight, they are picked off into the pit.”", "그렇지만 어찌 된 셈인지 공장주들이 윗자리를 쥐고 있으니, 나는 그이들이 이제도 앞으로도 영영 그 자리를 놓지 않을까 겁이 나오. 서로 이를 드러내고 싸우고 또 싸우다가, 싸우는 그 와중에 하나씩 구덩이로 끌려 들어가는 꼴이 꼭 아마겟돈의 큰 싸움 같구먼.”"]], [["Just then, Nicholas Higgins came in.", "바로 그때 니컬러스 히긴스가 들어왔다."], ["He caught his daughter’s last words.", "그는 딸의 마지막 말을 들었다."]], [["“Ay!", "“그럼!"], ["and I’ll fight on too; and I’ll got it this time.", "나도 계속 싸울 거고, 이번에는 기어이 얻어 낼 거요."], ["It’ll not take long for to make ’em give in, for they’ve getten a pretty lot of orders, all under contract; and they’ll soon find out they’d better give us our five per cent.", "그들을 굽히게 하는 데는 오래 안 걸릴 거요. 계약으로 묶인 주문을 잔뜩 받아 놓았으니 말이오."], ["than lose the profit they’ll gain; let alone the fine for not fulfilling the contract.", "그러니 우리한테 5퍼센트를 주는 편이 벌어들일 이문을 놓치는 것보다 낫다는 걸 곧 알게 될 거요. 계약을 못 채워서 무는 위약금은 말할 것도 없고."], ["Aha my masters!", "아하, 공장주 나리들!"], ["I know who’ll win.”", "누가 이길지는 내가 알지.”"]], [["Margaret fancied from his manner that he must have been drinking, not so much from what he said, as from the excited way in which he spoke; and she was rather confirmed in this idea by the evident anxiety Bessie showed to hasten her departure.", "마거릿은 그의 태도로 미루어 그가 술을 마셨나 보다고 짐작했다. 말의 내용보다도 흥분해서 말하는 투 때문이었다."], ["Bessy said to her,—", "그리고 베시가 그녀를 어서 보내려고 애쓰는 기색이 뚜렷한 것을 보고 그 짐작에 얼마쯤 확신이 섰다. 베시가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“The twenty-first—that’s Thursday week.", "“21일이면 다음 주 목요일이구먼."], ["I may come and see yo’ dressed for Thornton’s, I reckon.", "손턴 댁에 가려고 차려입은 댁을 보러 가도 되겠지요."], ["What time is yo’r dinner?”", "만찬이 몇 시요?”"]], [["Before Margaret could answer, Higgins broke out,—", "마거릿이 대답하기도 전에 히긴스가 불쑥 끼어들었다."]], [["“Thornton’s!", "“손턴 댁이라고!"], ["Ar’ t’ going to dine at Thornton’s?", "손턴 댁에 만찬을 하러 간다고?"], ["Ask him to give yo’ a bumper to the success of his orders.", "그 사람더러 주문이 잘되기를 비는 잔을 가득 채워 들라고 하시우."], ["By th’ twenty-first, I reckon, he’ll be pottered in his brains how to get ’em done in time.", "21일쯤이면 그걸 제때 어떻게 다 해내나 골머리가 터질 테니까. 그리고 이렇게 전하시우."], ["Tell him, there’s seven hundred ’ll come marching into Marlborough Mills, the morning after he gives the five per cent., and will help him through his contract in no time.", "5퍼센트를 주고 나면 그다음 날 아침에 700명이 말버러 공장으로 줄지어 들어가 눈 깜짝할 새에 계약을 끝내 주겠다고. 거기 가면 그자들이 다 모여 있을 거요."], ["You’ll have ’em all there.", "우리 공장주 햄퍼 씨도."], ["My master, Hamper.", "그이는 옛날식 축에 드는 사람이오."], ["He’s one o’ th’ oud-fashioned sort.", "사람을 만나면 욕지거리나 악담 없이는 말을 안 하지. 나한테 점잖게 말을 걸면 나는 저이가 죽을 때가 됐나 보다 할 거요."], ["Ne’er meets a man bout an oath or a curse; I should think he were going to die if he spoke me civil; but arter all, his bark’s waur than his bite, and yo’ may tell him one o’ his turn-outs said so, if yo’ like. Eh!", "그래도 따지고 보면 짖는 소리가 무는 것보다 사납지."], ["but yo’ll have a lot of prize mill-owners at Thornton’s!", "그이 밑에서 파업한 놈 하나가 그러더라고 전해도 좋소. 아이고!"], ["I should like to get speech o’ them, when they’re a bit inclined to sit still after dinner, and could na run for the life on ’em.", "손턴 댁에 가면 내로라하는 공장주들이 잔뜩 있겠구먼! 나는 그자들이 만찬 뒤에 좀 앉아 쉴 마음이 들어서 죽어도 달아나지 못할 때 한번 말을 붙여 보고 싶소."], ["I’d tell ’em my mind.", "내 속을 다 털어놓겠소."], ["I’d speak up again th’ hard way they’re driving on us!”", "그자들이 우리를 몰아붙이는 그 모진 방식에 대고 대놓고 따지겠소!”"]], [["“Good-bye!” said Margaret, hastily.", "“안녕히 계세요!”"], ["“Good-bye, Bessy!", "마거릿이 서둘러 말했다."], ["I shall look to see you on the twenty-first, if you’re well enough.”", "“잘 있어요, 베시! 몸이 괜찮으면 21일에 보기로 해요.”"]], [["The medicines and treatment which Dr. Donaldson had ordered for Mrs.", "도널드슨 의사가 헤일 부인에게 처방한 약과 치료가 처음에는 워낙 효험이 있어서 그녀 자신은 물론이고 마거릿까지도 의사가 잘못 본 것이 아닐까, 어머니가 아주 회복될 수도 있지 않을까 하는 희망을 품기 시작했다."], ["Hale, did her so much good at first that not only she herself, but Margaret, began to hope that he might have been mistaken, and that she could recover permanently.", "헤일 씨로 말하자면 그들의 근심이 얼마나 심각한 성격의 것인지 한 번도 헤아려 본 적이 없었으면서도, 그 두려움을 눌러 이겼다는 안도를 뚜렷이 드러냈다."], ["As for Mr. Hale, although he had never had an idea of the serious nature of their apprehensions, he triumphed over their fears with an evident relief, which proved how much his glimpse into the nature of them had affected him.", "그것은 그 근심의 성격을 언뜻 엿본 것만으로도 그가 얼마나 크게 흔들렸는지를 말해 주었다."], ["Only Dixon croaked for ever into Margaret’s ear.", "오직 딕슨만이 노상 마거릿의 귀에 대고 까마귀처럼 불길한 소리를 해 댔다."], ["However, Margaret defied the raven, and would hope.", "그러나 마거릿은 그 까마귀에게 맞서며 기어이 희망을 품으려 했다."]], [["They needed this gleam of brightness in-doors, for out-of-doors, even to their uninstructed eyes, there was a gloomy brooding appearance of discontent.", "집 안에는 이 한 줄기 밝은 빛이 필요했다. 밖에서는, 아직 사정에 어두운 그들의 눈에도 불만이 음침하게 서려 뒤끓는 기색이 보였기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Hale had his own acquaintances among the working men, and was depressed with their earnestly-told tales of suffering and long-endurance.", "헤일 씨에게는 노동자들 가운데 나름의 아는 사람들이 있었는데, 그들이 진지하게 들려주는 고통과 오랜 인내의 이야기에 그는 마음이 무거워졌다."], ["They would have scorned to speak of what they had to bear to any one who might, from his position, have understood it without their words.", "그들은 처지로 보아 말하지 않아도 알아들었을 사람에게라면 자기들이 견뎌야 하는 것을 입에 올리기를 자존심 상해했을 것이다. 그러나 여기 이 사람은 먼 고장에서 온 이였고, 자기가 한복판으로 던져진 그 체제가 어떻게 돌아가는지 몰라 어리둥절해하고 있었다."], ["But here was this man, from a distant county, who was perplexed by the workings of a system into the midst of which he was thrown, and each was eager to make him a judge, and to bring witness of his own causes for irritation.", "그래서 저마다 그를 재판관으로 삼고 싶어 했고, 제 나름의 분한 사연을 증언으로 가져오고 싶어 했다. 그러면 헤일 씨는 그 불평의 보따리를 모조리 챙겨서 손턴 씨 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["Then Mr. Hale brought all his budget of grievances, and laid it before Mr.", "공장주로서의 경험을 지닌 그가 그것들을 정리해 주고 그 뿌리를 설명해 주기를 바라서였다."], ["Thornton, for him, with his experience as a master, to arrange them, and explain their origin; which he always did, on sound economical principles; showing that, as trade was conducted, there must always be a waxing and waning of commercial prosperity; and that in the waning a certain number of masters, as well as of men, must go down into ruin, and be no more seen among the ranks of the happy and prosperous.", "손턴 씨는 언제나 그렇게 해 주었는데, 늘 견실한 경제 원리에 따라서였다. 장사가 굴러가는 방식상 상업의 번영에는 반드시 차오름과 이지러짐이 있게 마련이고, 이지러질 때에는 일꾼들뿐 아니라 일정한 수의 공장주도 몰락하여 행복하고 번창하는 대열에서 자취를 감출 수밖에 없다는 것이었다."], ["He spoke as if this consequence were so entirely logical, that neither employers nor employed had any right to complain if it became their fate: the employer to turn aside from the race he could no longer run, with a bitter sense of incompetency and failure—wounded in the struggle—trampled down by his fellows in their haste to get rich—slighted where he was once honoured—humbly asking for, instead of bestowing, employment with a lordly hand.", "그는 이 결과가 너무도 논리적이어서, 그것이 제 운명이 되더라도 고용주든 피고용인이든 불평할 권리가 없다는 듯이 말했다. 고용주는 더 이상 달릴 수 없게 된 경주에서 비켜서야 한다."], ["Of course, speaking so of the fate that, as a master, might be his own in the fluctuations of commerce, he was not likely to have more sympathy with that of the workmen, who were passed by in the swift merciless improvement or alteration; who would fain lie down and quietly die out of the world that needed them not, but felt as they could never rest in their graves for the clinging cries of the beloved and helpless they would leave behind; who envied the power of the wild bird, that can feed her young with her very heart’s blood.", "무능과 실패의 쓰라린 감각을 안고서, 다툼 속에서 상처 입고, 부자가 되려고 서두르는 동료들에게 짓밟히고, 한때 존경받던 자리에서 무시당하고, 예전에는 왕처럼 베풀던 일자리를 이제는 겸손히 구걸하면서 말이다. 물론 공장주로서 상업의 부침 속에 자신에게도 닥칠 수 있는 운명을 그렇게 말하는 사람이었으니, 그 무자비하고 빠른 개선이나 변화 속에서 밀려난 노동자들의 운명에는 더 많은 동정을 품었을 리가 없다."], ["Margaret’s whole soul rose up against him while he reasoned in this way—as if commerce were everything and humanity nothing.", "그 노동자들은 자기들을 필요로 하지 않는 이 세상에서 그저 드러누워 조용히 사라져 버리고 싶으면서도, 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 사랑하는 무력한 이들의 매달리는 울음소리 때문에 무덤에서조차 결코 쉬지 못하리라고 느꼈다."], ["She could hardly thank him for the individual kindness, which brought him that very evening to offer her—for the delicacy which made him understand that he must offer her privately—every convenience for illness that his own wealth or his mother’s foresight had caused them to accumulate in their household, and which, as he learnt from Dr.", "제 심장의 피로 새끼를 먹일 수 있는 들새의 힘을 부러워한 사람들이었다. 손턴 씨가 이런 식으로 논리를 펼 때면, 마치 상업이 전부이고 인간다움은 아무것도 아니라는 듯한 그 말투에 마거릿의 온 영혼이 들고일어났다."], ["Donaldson, Mrs. Hale might possibly require.", "그녀는 그가 개인으로서 베푼 친절에 대해서도 고마워하기가 어려웠다."], ["His presence, after the way he had spoken—his bringing before her the doom, which she was vainly trying to persuade herself might yet be averted from her mother—all conspired to set Margaret’s teeth on edge, as she looked at him, and listened to him.", "그는 바로 그날 저녁에 찾아와 자기 재산과 어머니의 앞을 내다보는 마음 덕분에 그 집에 갖춰져 있던 병자를 위한 온갖 편의를, 도널드슨 의사에게서 들은 대로 헤일 부인에게 필요할지도 모르는 그것들을 내주겠다고 했다. 남몰래 권해야 한다는 것을 헤아릴 만큼 섬세하기까지 했다."], ["What business had he to be the only person, except Dr.", "그러나 그렇게 말해 놓은 뒤에 나타난 그의 존재, 어머니에게서 아직 비켜 갈지도 모른다고 헛되이 자신을 설득하려 애쓰던 그 운명을 눈앞에 들이미는 그의 존재, 그 모든 것이 어우러져 그를 바라보고 그의 말을 듣는 마거릿의 신경을 곤두세웠다."], ["Donaldson and Dixon, admitted to the awful secret, which she held shut up in the most dark and sacred recess of her heart—not daring to look at it, unless she invoked heavenly strength to bear the sight—that, some day soon, she should cry aloud for her mother, and no answer would come out of the blank, dumb darkness?", "도대체 무슨 자격으로 그가, 도널드슨 의사와 딕슨을 빼고는 유일하게 그 끔찍한 비밀을 아는 사람이 되었단 말인가? 그녀가 마음속 가장 어둡고 성스러운 구석에 가둬 둔 비밀, 그것을 감당할 하늘의 힘을 구하지 않고서는 감히 마주 볼 수조차 없던 비밀, 머지않아 어느 날 어머니를 소리쳐 부르게 되겠지만 그 텅 비고 말 없는 어둠에서는 아무 대답도 오지 않으리라는 비밀을."], ["Yet he knew all. She saw it in his pitying eyes.", "그런데도 그는 다 알고 있었다."], ["She heard it in his grave and tremulous voice.", "그녀는 그것을 그의 가엾어하는 눈에서 보았다."], ["How reconcile those eyes, that voice, with the hard, reasoning, dry, merciless way in which he laid down axioms of trade, and serenely followed them out to their full consequences?", "그의 무겁고 떨리는 목소리에서 들었다. 그 눈, 그 목소리를 어떻게 저 딱딱하고 이치만 따지고 메마르고 무자비한 태도와, 장사의 공리를 늘어놓고는 태연히 그것을 끝까지 밀고 나가는 태도와 나란히 놓을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["The discord jarred upon her inexpressibly.", "그 어긋남이 그녀에게는 말할 수 없이 거슬렸다."], ["The more because of the gathering woe of which she heard from Bessy.", "베시에게서 전해 듣는 재앙이 쌓여 가는 터라 더욱 그러했다."], ["To be sure, Nicholas Higgins, the father, spoke differently.", "물론 아버지 니컬러스 히긴스는 다르게 말했다."], ["He had been appointed a committee-man, and said that he knew secrets of which the exoteric knew nothing.", "그는 위원으로 뽑혔고, 바깥사람들은 전혀 모르는 속사정을 자기는 알고 있다고 했다."], ["He said this more expressly and particularly, on the very day before Mrs. Thornton’s dinner-party.", "그가 그 말을 유난히 또렷하고 자세하게 한 것은 바로 손턴 부인의 만찬 전날이었다."], ["When Margaret, going in to speak to Bessy, found him arguing the point with Boucher, the neighbour of whom she had frequently heard mention, as by turns exciting Higgins’s compassion, as an unskilful workman with a large family depending upon him for support, and at other times enraging his more energetic and sanguine neighbour by his want of what the latter called spirit.", "마거릿이 베시와 이야기하려고 들어갔을 때 그는 바우처와 그 문제로 언쟁을 벌이고 있었다. 마거릿이 여러 번 이야기를 들은 그 이웃은, 딸린 식구는 많은데 솜씨가 없는 일꾼이라 어떤 때는 히긴스의 동정을 자아냈고, 또 어떤 때는 히긴스가 기개라고 부르는 것이 없다는 이유로 그 활기차고 낙천적인 이웃을 격분시키곤 했다."], ["It was very evident that Higgins was in a passion when Margaret entered.", "마거릿이 들어섰을 때 히긴스가 몹시 화가 나 있는 것은 누가 보아도 분명했다."], ["Boucher stood, with both hands on the rather high mantelpiece, swaying himself a little on the support which his arms, thus placed, gave him, and looking wildly into the fire, with a kind of despair that irritated Higgins, even while it went to his heart.", "바우처는 꽤 높다란 벽난로 선반에 두 손을 얹고 서서, 그렇게 짚은 팔이 받쳐 주는 힘에 몸을 조금씩 흔들며 불을 물끄러미 사납게 들여다보고 있었다. 그 얼굴에 서린 일종의 절망은 히긴스의 마음을 저리게 하면서도 동시에 그를 짜증 나게 했다."], ["Bessy was rocking herself violently backwards and forwards, as was her wont (Margaret knew by this time) when she was agitated.", "베시는 몸을 앞뒤로 세차게 흔들고 있었는데, 마거릿은 이제 그것이 그녀가 흔들릴 때 나오는 버릇임을 알고 있었다. 동생 메리는 코르덴 재단 일을 하러 가려고 보닛 끈을 매고 있었다."], ["Her sister Mary was tying on her bonnet (in great clumsy bows, as suited her great clumsy fingers), to go to her fustian-cutting, blubbering out loud the while, and evidently longing to be away from a scene that distressed her.", "굵고 뭉툭한 손가락에 어울리게 크고 서투른 매듭이었다. 그러면서 내내 소리 내어 훌쩍였고, 자기를 괴롭게 하는 그 자리를 어서 벗어나고 싶어 하는 것이 뚜렷했다."]], [["Margaret came in upon this scene.", "마거릿은 이런 광경 속으로 들어섰다."], ["She stood for a moment at the door—then, her finger on her lips, she stole to a seat on the squab near Bessy.", "그녀는 잠시 문가에 서 있다가 손가락을 입술에 대고는 베시 곁의 스쿼브 자리로 살그머니 다가가 앉았다."], ["Nicholas saw her come in, and greeted her with a gruff, but not unfriendly nod.", "니컬러스는 그녀가 들어오는 것을 보고 퉁명스럽기는 해도 쌀쌀맞지는 않은 고갯짓으로 인사했다."], ["Mary hurried out of the house, catching gladly at the open door, and crying aloud when she got away from her father’s presence.", "메리는 열린 문을 반갑게 붙잡고 집 밖으로 서둘러 나갔고, 아버지의 눈앞에서 벗어나자 소리 내어 울었다."], ["It was only John Boucher that took no notice whatever who came in and who went out.", "누가 들어오고 누가 나가는지 도무지 아랑곳하지 않은 사람은 존 바우처뿐이었다."]], [["“It’s no use, Higgins. Hoo cannot live long a’ this’n. Hoo’s just sinking away—not for want o’ meat hersel’—but because hoo cannot stand th’ sight o’ the little ones clemming. Ay, clemming! Five shilling a week may do well enough for thee, wi’ but two mouths to fill, and one on ’em a wench who can well earn her own meat. But it’s clemming to us. An’ I tell thee plain—if hoo dies, as I’m ’feard hoo will afore we’ve getten th’ five per cent., I’ll fling the money back i’ th’ master’s face, and say, ‘Be domned to yo’; be domned to th’ whole cruel world o’ yo’; that could na leave me th’ best wife that ever bore childer to a man. An’ look thee lad, I’ll hate thee, and th’ whole pack o’ th’ Union. Ay, an’ chase yo’ through heaven wi’ my hatred,—I will, lad! I will,—if yo’re leading me astray i’ this matter. Thou saidst, Nicholas, on Wednesday sennight—and it’s now Tuesday i’ th’ second week—that afore a fortnight we’d ha’ the masters coming a-begging to us to take back our work, at our own wage—and time’s nearly up,—and there’s our lile Jack lying a-bed, too weak to cry, but just every now and then sobbing up his heart for want o’ food,—our lile Jack, I tell thee, lad! Hoo’s never looked up sin’ he were born, and hoo loves him as if he were her very life,—as he is,—for I reckon he’ll ha’ cost me that precious price,—our lile Jack, who wakened me each morn wi’ putting his sweet little lips to my great rough fou’ face, a-seeking a smooth place to kiss,—an’ he lies clemming.” Here the deep sobs choked the poor man and Nicholas looked up, with eyes brimful of tears, to Margaret, before he could gain courage to speak.", "“소용없소, 히긴스. 우리 집사람은 이대로는 오래 못 살아. 그냥 사그라들고 있단 말이오. 제가 먹을 게 없어서가 아니라, 어린것들이 굶는 꼴을 차마 못 보아서 그런 거요. 그래, 굶는단 말이오! 주급 5실링이면 자네야 넉넉하겠지. 먹일 입이 둘뿐인 데다 그중 하나는 제 먹을 것쯤 넉넉히 벌어 오는 계집애니 말이오. 그렇지만 우리한테는 그게 굶는 거요. 내 대놓고 말하겠소. 만약 집사람이 죽으면, 나는 우리가 5퍼센트를 받아 내기도 전에 그렇게 될까 봐 겁이 나는데, 그러면 나는 그 돈을 공장주 낯짝에 도로 내던지면서 이렇게 말할 거요. ‘너희 따위 다 뒈져라. 사내한테 자식을 낳아 준 가장 좋은 아내마저 남겨 두지 못한 이 모진 세상 다 뒈져라.’ 그리고 이보게, 나는 자네도 조합 무리도 다 미워할 거요. 그래, 내 미움을 안고 천국까지라도 자네를 쫓아다닐 거요. 정말 그럴 거요, 이 사람아! 정말이오. 만약 자네가 이 일로 나를 그르치는 거라면 말이오. 자네가 그랬잖소, 니컬러스, 일주일 전 수요일에. 지금은 둘째 주 화요일이오. 보름 안에 공장주들이 우리한테 빌러 와서 우리가 부르는 품삯으로 일을 도로 맡아 달라고 할 거라고 했지. 그런데 그 기한이 다 되어 가오. 그리고 우리 어린 잭은 자리에 누워, 울 기운도 없이 그저 이따금 배가 고파 흐느껴 목이 멜 뿐이오. 우리 어린 잭 말이오, 이 사람아! 집사람은 그 애가 태어난 뒤로 고개 한번 들어 보지 못했고, 제 목숨인 양 그 애를 사랑하오. 실제로 그렇기도 하고. 그 애가 나한테 그 귀한 값을 치르게 할 것 같으니 말이오. 우리 어린 잭 말이오. 아침마다 그 곱고 조그만 입술을 내 크고 거칠고 지저분한 얼굴에 대고 입 맞출 매끈한 자리를 찾으며 나를 깨우던 아이요. 그 애가 굶고 누워 있단 말이오.” 여기서 깊은 흐느낌이 이 가엾은 사내의 목을 막았고, 니컬러스는 말을 꺼낼 용기를 내기 전에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 눈을 들어 마거릿을 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Hou’d up, man.", "“기운 차리게, 이 사람아."], ["Thy lile Jack shall na’ clem.", "자네 어린 잭은 굶기지 않을 걸세."], ["I ha’ gotten brass, and we’ll go buy the chap a sup o’ milk an’ a good four-pounder this very minute.", "나한테 돈이 좀 있으니 지금 당장 가서 그 아이 먹일 우유하고 4파운드짜리 빵 한 덩이를 사 오세."], ["What’s mine’s thine, sure enough, i’ thou’st i’ want.", "자네가 아쉬우면 내 것이 곧 자네 것이지, 그야 두말할 것 없고."], ["Only, dunnot lose heart, man!”", "그저 낙심하지만 말게, 이 사람아!”"], ["continued he, as he fumbled in a tea-pot for what money he had.", "그는 찻주전자를 뒤져 가진 돈을 찾으면서 말을 이었다. “내 심장과 영혼을 걸고 말하는데 이 모든 일에도 우리가 이길 걸세."], ["“I lay yo my heart and soul we’ll win for a’ this: it’s but bearing on one more week, and yo’ just see th’ way th’ masters ’ll come round, praying on us to come back to our mills.", "딱 한 주만 더 버티면 되네. 그러면 공장주들이 어떻게 돌아서서 제발 공장으로 돌아와 달라고 우리한테 비는지 보게 될 걸세."], ["An’ the Union—that’s to say, I—will take care yo’ve enough for th’ childer and th’ missus.", "그리고 조합이, 그러니까 내가 자네 아이들과 안사람이 굶지 않도록 챙기겠네."], ["So dunnot turn faint-heart, and go to th’ tyrants a-seeking work.”", "그러니 겁먹고 마음 약해져서 폭군들한테 일자리를 구하러 가지는 말게.”"]], [["The man turned round at these words,—turned round a face so white, and gaunt, and tear-furrowed, and hopeless, that its very calm forced Margaret to weep.", "그 말에 사내가 몸을 돌렸다. 돌아선 얼굴은 어찌나 창백하고 여위고 눈물로 골이 파이고 희망이 없어 보이던지, 그 고요함 자체가 마거릿을 울게 만들었다."]], [["“Yo’ know well, that a worser tyrant than e’er th’ masters were says, ‘Clem to death, and see ’em a’ clem to death, ere yo’ dare go again th’ Union.’ Yo’ know it well, Nicholas, for a’ yo’re one on e’m. Yo’ may be kind hearts, each separate; but once banded together, yo’ve no more pity for a man than a wild hunger-maddened wolf.”", "“자네도 잘 알잖소. 공장주들보다 더한 폭군이 이러고 있다는 걸. ‘조합에 맞설 엄두를 내느니 굶어 죽어라, 네 식구들이 다 굶어 죽는 꼴을 보아라.’ 자네도 잘 알잖소, 니컬러스. 자네가 그 조합의 하나인데도 말이오. 하나하나 떼어 놓고 보면 다들 마음씨 고운 사람들이겠지. 그렇지만 일단 한데 뭉치고 나면, 굶주림에 미친 들이리보다도 사람을 가엾이 여길 줄 모르오.”"]], [["Nicholas had his hand on the lock of the door—he stopped and turned round on Boucher, close following:", "니컬러스는 문고리에 손을 얹은 참이었다. 그는 걸음을 멈추고 바짝 뒤따라오는 바우처를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“So help me God!", "“하느님이 나를 도우시기를!"], ["man alive—if I think not I’m doing best for thee, and for all on us.", "이 사람아, 내가 자네를 위해서, 우리 모두를 위해서 최선을 다하고 있다고 여기지 않는다면 말일세."], ["If I’m going wrong when I think I’m going right, it’s their sin, who ha’ left me where I am, in my ignorance.", "내가 옳게 가고 있다고 여기면서 그르게 가고 있는 거라면, 그건 나를 이 무지 속에 이대로 내버려 둔 자들의 죄일세."], ["I ha’ thought till my brains ached,—Beli’ me, John, I have.", "나는 머리가 지끈거리도록 생각했네. 정말일세, 존, 정말로 그랬네."], ["An’ I say again, there’s no help for us but having faith i’ th’ Union.", "그리고 다시 말하지만 우리에게는 조합을 믿는 것 말고는 살길이 없네."], ["They’ll win the day, see if they dunnot!”", "그들이 이길 걸세, 못 이기나 두고 보게!”"]], [["Not one word had Margaret or Bessy spoken.", "마거릿도 베시도 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["They had hardly uttered the sighing, that the eyes of each called to the other to bring up from the depths of her heart.", "서로의 눈이 상대에게 마음 깊은 곳에서 길어 올리라고 청하던 그 한숨조차 겨우 내뱉었을 뿐이었다."], ["At last Bessy said,", "마침내 베시가 말했다."]], [["“I never thought to hear father call on God again.", "“아버지가 다시 하느님을 부르는 걸 듣게 될 줄은 몰랐구먼."], ["But yo’ heard him say, ‘So help me God!’”", "그렇지만 댁도 들었잖수, ‘하느님이 나를 도우시기를!’ 하는 말을.”"]], [["“Yes!”", "“네!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“Let me bring you what money I can spare,—let me bring you a little food for that poor man’s children.", "“내가 낼 수 있는 만큼 돈을 가져오게 해 줘요. 저 가엾은 분의 아이들에게 먹을 것도 조금 가져오게 해 줘요."], ["Don’t let them know it comes from any one but your father.", "그게 당신 아버님 말고 다른 데서 왔다는 건 그이들이 모르게 하고요."], ["It will be but little.”", "얼마 안 되겠지만요.”"]], [["Bessy lay back without taking any notice of what Margaret said.", "베시는 마거릿의 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 뒤로 몸을 기댔다."], ["She did not cry—she only quivered up her breath.", "그녀는 울지 않았다. 그저 숨결을 떨었을 뿐이었다."]], [["“My heart’s drained dry o’ tears,”", "“내 마음은 눈물이 다 말라붙었소.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Boucher’s been in these days past, a telling me of his fears and his troubles.", "“바우처 씨가 요 며칠 여기 와서 제 두려움이며 걱정거리를 나한테 죄다 이야기했구먼. 나도 그이가 좀 무른 축이라는 건 아오."], ["He’s but a weak kind of chap, I know, but he’s a man for a’ that; and tho’ I’ve been angry, many a time afore now, wi’ him an’ his wife, as knew no more nor him how to manage, yet yo’ see, all folks isn’t wise, yet God lets ’em live—ay, an’ gives ’em some one to love, and be loved by, just as good as Solomon.", "그렇지만 그래도 사내는 사내요. 그리고 나는 예전부터 여러 번 그이한테도, 그이 못지않게 살림을 꾸릴 줄 모르는 그 안사람한테도 화를 냈지만, 있잖수, 사람이 다 지혜로운 건 아닌데도 하느님은 그이들을 살게 하시잖수."], ["An’ if sorrow comes to them they love, it hurts ’em as sore as e’er it did Solomon.", "그럼, 사랑할 사람도 주시고 사랑받게도 해 주시지."], ["I can’t make it out.", "솔로몬한테 하신 것만큼이나 좋게 말이오."], ["Perhaps it’s as well such a one as Boucher has th’ Union to see after him.", "그러니 그이들이 사랑하는 사람한테 슬픔이 닥치면 그게 솔로몬한테 그랬던 것만큼이나 사무치게 아픈 거요. 나는 그 이치를 모르겠구먼."], ["But I’d just like for to see th’ men as make th’ Union, and put ’em one by one face to face wi’ Boucher.", "어쩌면 바우처 씨 같은 사람은 조합이 돌봐 주는 게 잘된 일인지도 모르지. 그렇지만 나는 그 조합을 만드는 사람들을 좀 보고 싶소."], ["I reckon, if they heard him, the’d tell him (if I cotched ’em one by one), he might go back and get what he could for his work, even if it weren’t so much as they ordered.”", "그이들을 하나씩 바우처 씨와 얼굴을 맞대게 해 놓고 말이오. 내 생각엔 그 사람들이 그이 말을 들으면, 내가 하나씩 따로 붙잡아 놓기만 한다면, 정한 만큼은 못 되더라도 도로 가서 제 일삯을 받을 수 있는 대로 받으라고 할 거요.”"]], [["Margaret sat utterly silent.", "마거릿은 아무 말도 못 하고 앉아 있었다."], ["How was she ever to go away into comfort and forget that man’s voice, with the tone of unutterable agony, telling more by far than his words of what he had to suffer?", "그녀가 어떻게 편안한 곳으로 돌아가 저 사내의 목소리를, 말보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 그가 견뎌야 하는 것을 말해 주던 그 이루 말할 수 없이 참담한 어조를 잊을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["She took out her purse; she had not much in it of what she could call her own, but what she had she put into Bessy’s hands without speaking.", "그녀는 지갑을 꺼냈다. 그 안에는 제 것이라 부를 만한 돈이 그리 많지 않았지만, 있는 대로 다 말없이 베시의 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Thank yo’.", "“고맙수."], ["There’s many on ’em gets no more, and is not so bad off,—leastways does not show it as he does.", "그이보다 더 못 받으면서도 그만큼 딱하지 않은 사람이 숱하다오. 적어도 그이처럼 티를 내지는 않으니 말이오."], ["But father won’t let ’em want, now he knows.", "그렇지만 아버지가 알게 되었으니 그이들을 굶기지는 않을 거요."], ["Yo’ see, Boucher’s been pulled down wi’ his childer,—and her being so cranky, and a’ they could pawn has gone this last twelvemonth.", "있잖수, 바우처 씨는 자식들 때문에 주저앉은 사람이오. 게다가 안사람은 그렇게 성마르고, 전당 잡힐 만한 건 지난 열두 달 동안 죄다 잡혀 버렸고."], ["Yo’re not to think we’d ha’ letten ’em clem, for all we’re a bit pressed oursel’; if neighbours doesn’t see after neighbours, I dunno who will.”", "우리도 좀 쪼들리기는 하지만 그렇다고 그이들을 굶게 두었으리라고는 생각하지 마시우. 이웃이 이웃을 돌보지 않으면 누가 돌보겠수.”"], ["Bessy seemed almost afraid lest Margaret should think they had not the will, and, to a certain degree, the power of helping one whom she evidently regarded as having a claim upon them.", "베시는 자기들에게 마땅히 도와야 할 몫이 있다고 여기는 그 사람을 도울 뜻이, 그리고 어느 정도는 그럴 힘이 없다고 마거릿이 생각할까 봐 거의 겁을 내는 듯했다. “게다가.”"], ["“Besides,”", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she went on, “father is sure and positive the masters must give in within these next few days,—that they canna hould on much longer.", "“아버지는 며칠 안에 공장주들이 반드시 굽히고 들어올 거라고, 그이들이 더는 오래 못 버틴다고 확신하고 있소. 그래도 어쨌든 고맙수."], ["But I thank yo’ all the same,—I thank yo’ for mysel’, as much as for Boucher, for it jus makes my heart warm to yo’ more and more.”", "바우처 씨를 위해서만큼이나 나 자신을 위해서도 고맙소. 이러니 댁한테 마음이 자꾸만 더 따뜻해지는구먼.”"]], [["Bessy seemed much quieter to-day, but fearfully languid and exhausted.", "베시는 오늘 훨씬 조용해 보였지만 무섭게 기운이 없고 지쳐 있었다."], ["As she finished speaking, she looked so faint and weary that Margaret became alarmed.", "말을 마쳤을 때 그녀가 하도 창백하고 고단해 보여서 마거릿은 겁이 났다."]], [["“It’s nout,”", "“아무것도 아니오.” 베시가 말했다."], ["said Bessy.", "“아직 죽음은 아니오."], ["“It’s not death yet.", "간밤에 꿈 때문에, 아니 꿈 비슷한 것 때문에 몹시 시달렸소. 나는 말짱히 깨어 있었으니 말이오."], ["I had a fearfu’ night wi’ dreams—or somewhat like dreams, for I were wide awake—and I’m all in a swounding daze to-day,—only yon poor chap made me alive again.", "그래서 오늘은 온통 아뜩한 혼미 속에 잠겨 있구먼. 다만 저 가엾은 사내가 나를 도로 살아나게 했지."], ["No!", "아니오!"], ["it’s not death yet, but death is not far off.", "아직 죽음은 아니오. 그래도 죽음이 멀지 않았소."], ["Ay.", "그래. 나를 덮어 주우."], ["Cover me up, and I’ll may be sleep, if th’ cough will let me.", "기침이 놓아주면 어쩌면 잠이 들지도 모르겠소. 잘 자시우."], ["Good night—good afternoon, m’appen I should say—but th’ light is dim an’ misty to-day.”", "아니, 안녕히 가시라고 해야겠구먼. 그래도 오늘은 빛이 침침하고 뿌옇기만 하오.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XX", "# 제20장"]], [["“Old and young, boy, let ’em all eat, I have it; Let ’em have ten tire of teeth a-piece, I care not.”", "“늙은이든 젊은이든, 얘야, 다들 먹게 두어라, 내 뜻이 그러하다. 저마다 이빨을 열 줄씩 갖게 두어도 나는 개의치 않는다.”"], ["ROLLO, DUKE OF NORMANDY.", "— 노르망디 공작 롤로."]], [["Margaret went home so painfully occupied with what she had heard and seen that she hardly knew how to rouse herself up to the duties which awaited her; the necessity for keeping up a constant flow of cheerful conversation for her mother, who, now that she was unable to go out, always looked to Margaret’s return from the shortest walk as bringing in some news.", "마거릿은 보고 들은 것에 마음이 아프도록 사로잡힌 채 집으로 돌아왔고, 자기를 기다리는 의무를 감당하려면 어떻게 기운을 내야 할지 알 수 없었다. 이제 밖에 나가지 못하게 된 어머니는 마거릿이 아무리 짧은 산책에서 돌아와도 늘 무슨 소식을 가져오리라 기대했으므로, 어머니를 위해 끊임없이 즐거운 이야깃거리를 흘려보내야 하는 의무였다."]], [["“And can your factory friend come on Thursday to see you dressed?”", "“그래, 네 공장 친구는 목요일에 네가 차려입은 걸 보러 올 수 있다더냐?”"]], [["“She was so ill I never thought of asking her,”", "“그 애가 너무 아파서 물어볼 생각조차 못 했어요.”"], ["said Margaret, dolefully.", "마거릿이 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“Dear!", "“어머나!"], ["Everybody is ill now, I think,”", "요즘은 다들 아픈 모양이구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, with a little of the jealousy which one invalid is apt to feel of another.", "헤일 부인이 병자가 다른 병자에게 품기 쉬운 시샘을 조금 섞어 말했다."], ["“But it must be very sad to be ill in one of those little back streets.”", "“그래도 그런 뒷골목 좁은 집에서 앓는다면 참 딱한 일이겠지.”"], ["(Her kindly nature prevailing, and the old Helstone habits of thought returning.) “It’s bad enough here.", "(타고난 다정한 성품이 이기고, 옛 헬스톤 시절의 생각하던 버릇이 되돌아온 것이었다.) “여기서도 힘든 판인데."], ["What could you do for her, Margaret? Mr.", "마거릿, 네가 그 애한테 무얼 해 줄 수 있겠니?"], ["Thornton has sent me some of his old port wine since you went out.", "네가 나간 사이에 손턴 씨가 오래 묵힌 포트와인을 좀 보내 주셨단다."], ["Would a bottle of that do her good, think you?”", "그걸 한 병 주면 그 애한테 좋을까?”"]], [["“No, mamma!", "“아니에요, 어머니!"], ["I don’t believe they are very poor,—at least, they don’t speak as if they were; and, at any rate, Bessy’s illness is consumption—she won’t want wine.", "그 집이 아주 가난한 것 같지는 않아요. 적어도 그런 식으로 말하지는 않던걸요."], ["Perhaps, I might take her a little preserve, made of our dear Helstone fruit. No!", "게다가 베시의 병은 폐병이라 포도주를 바라지도 않을 거예요."], ["there’s another family to whom I should like to give—Oh mamma, mamma!", "우리 헬스톤의 귀한 과일로 만든 잼이라면 조금 가져다줄 수 있을지도 모르겠어요. 아니!"], ["how am I to dress up in my finery, and go off and away to smart parties, after the sorrow I’ve seen to-day?”", "제가 무언가 드리고 싶은 집은 따로 있어요. 아, 어머니, 어머니!"], ["exclaimed Margaret, bursting the bounds she had preordained for herself before she came in, and telling her mother what she had seen and heard at Higgins’s cottage.", "오늘 그런 슬픔을 보고 와서 제가 어떻게 곱게 차려입고 화려한 잔치에 나가 놀 수 있겠어요?” 마거릿은 집에 들어오기 전에 스스로 정해 둔 선을 무너뜨리며 이렇게 외쳤고, 히긴스의 오두막에서 보고 들은 것을 어머니에게 이야기했다."]], [["It distressed Mrs. Hale excessively.", "그 이야기는 헤일 부인을 몹시 괴롭혔다."], ["It made her restlessly irritated till she could do something.", "무엇이든 하지 않고서는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 안절부절못하게 만들었다."], ["She directed Margaret to pack up a basket in the very drawing-room, to be sent there and then to the family; and was almost angry with her for saying, that it would not signify if it did not go till morning, as she knew Higgins had provided for their immediate wants, and she herself had left money with Bessy.", "부인은 마거릿에게 바로 그 응접실에서 바구니를 꾸리게 하여 당장 그 집으로 보내라고 일렀고, 히긴스가 당장 필요한 것은 마련해 두었고 자기도 베시에게 돈을 남기고 왔으니 아침에 보내도 상관없다고 말하는 딸에게 하마터면 화를 낼 뻔했다."], ["Mrs. Hale called her unfeeling for saying this; and never gave herself breathing-time till the basket was sent out of the house.", "헤일 부인은 그런 말을 한다고 딸을 무정하다 나무랐고, 바구니가 집을 나설 때까지 한숨 돌릴 틈도 자신에게 주지 않았다."], ["Then she said:", "그러고 나서 부인이 말했다."]], [["“After all, we may have been doing wrong.", "“그래도 어쩌면 우리가 잘못한 것인지도 모르겠구나."], ["It was only the last time Mr. Thornton was here that he said, those were no true friends who helped to prolong the struggle by assisting the turn-outs.", "바로 지난번에 손턴 씨가 여기 오셨을 때, 파업하는 사람들을 도와 싸움을 길게 끄는 데 손을 보태는 이들은 참된 벗이 아니라고 하셨잖니."], ["And this Boucher-man was a turn-out, was he not?”", "그런데 그 바우처라는 사람도 파업꾼이 아니냐?”"]], [["The question was referred to Mr. Hale by his wife, when he came upstairs, fresh from giving a lesson to Mr.", "헤일 부인은 남편이 위층으로 올라오자 이 물음을 그에게 넘겼다."], ["Thornton, which had ended in conversation, as was their wont.", "그는 손턴 씨에게 막 수업을 하고 온 참이었는데, 늘 그렇듯 수업은 대화로 끝나 있었다."], ["Margaret did not care if their gifts had prolonged the strife; she did not think far enough for that, in her present excited state.", "마거릿은 자기들의 도움이 다툼을 길게 끌었는지 어떤지 개의치 않았다. 지금처럼 마음이 격해진 상태에서는 그렇게까지 멀리 생각이 미치지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Hale listened, and tried to be as calm as a judge; he recalled all that had seemed so clear not half-an-hour before, as it came out of Mr.", "헤일 씨는 이야기를 듣고 판관처럼 침착하려 애썼다."], ["Thornton’s lips; and then he made an unsatisfactory compromise.", "그는 반 시간도 채 되기 전에 손턴 씨의 입에서 흘러나올 때는 그토록 분명해 보이던 이야기를 모두 떠올렸다."], ["His wife and daughter had not only done quite right in this instance, but he did not see for a moment how they could have done otherwise.", "그러고는 흡족하지 못한 절충안을 내놓았다. 이번 경우에 아내와 딸은 아주 옳게 행동했을 뿐 아니라, 달리 어떻게 할 수 있었을지 그로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["Nevertheless, as a general rule, it was very true what Mr.", "그렇더라도 일반론으로는 손턴 씨의 말이 참으로 옳다고 했다."], ["Thornton said, that as the strike, if prolonged, must end in the masters bringing hands from a distance (if, indeed, the final result were not, as it had often been before, the invention of some machine which would diminish the need of hands at all), why, it was clear enough that the kindest thing was to refuse all help which might bolster them up in their folly.", "파업이 길어지면 결국 공장주들이 먼 데서 직공을 데려오는 것으로 끝날 수밖에 없고(아니면 예전에도 여러 번 그랬듯 아예 직공의 필요를 줄여 줄 어떤 기계의 발명으로 끝나거나), 그렇다면 그들을 어리석음 속에 버티게 떠받쳐 줄 온갖 도움을 거절하는 것이야말로 가장 인정 있는 일임이 분명하다는 것이었다."], ["But as to this Boucher, he would go and see him the first thing in the morning, and try and find out what could be done for him.", "다만 이 바우처라는 사람에 대해서는, 아침에 제일 먼저 찾아가 보고 그를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알아보겠다고 했다."]], [["Mr. Hale went the next morning, as he proposed.", "헤일 씨는 말한 대로 이튿날 아침에 갔다."], ["He did not find Boucher at home, but he had a long talk with his wife; promised to ask for an Infirmary order for her; and, seeing the plenty provided by Mrs.", "바우처는 집에 없었으나 그 아내와 오래 이야기를 나누었고, 그녀를 위해 병원 진료권을 청해 보겠다고 약속했다."], ["Hale, and somewhat lavishly used by the children, who were masters downstairs in their father’s absence, he came back with a more consoling and cheerful account than Margaret had dared to hope for; indeed, what she had said the night before had prepared her father for so much worse a state of things that, by a re-action of his imagination, he described all as better than it really was.", "그리고 헤일 부인이 보낸 넉넉한 물건들을, 아버지가 없는 사이 아래층의 주인 노릇을 하는 아이들이 다소 헤프게 쓰고 있는 것을 보고는, 마거릿이 감히 바라던 것보다 한결 위안이 되고 밝은 소식을 들고 돌아왔다. 사실 전날 밤 딸이 한 이야기가 아버지에게 훨씬 더 나쁜 형편을 각오하게 만든 터라, 상상이 되돌아오는 반동으로 그는 모든 것을 실제보다 좋게 그려 말한 것이었다."]], [["“But I will go again, and see the man himself,”", "“그래도 다시 가서 그 사람을 직접 만나 봐야겠구나.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“I hardly know as yet how to compare one of these houses with our Helstone cottages.", "“여기 이런 집들을 우리 헬스톤 오두막과 어떻게 견주어야 할지 아직도 잘 모르겠다."], ["I see furniture here which our labourers would never have thought of buying, and food commonly used which they would consider luxuries; yet for these very families there seems no other resource now that their weekly wages are stopped, but the pawn-shop.", "여기서는 우리 고장 일꾼들이라면 살 생각조차 못 했을 세간이 보이고, 그들이라면 사치라 여길 음식을 예사로 먹더구나. 그런데 정작 그 집들은 주급이 끊기고 나면 전당포 말고는 아무 방도가 없는 것 같으니 말이다."], ["One had need to learn a different standard, up here in Milton.”", "이 밀턴에서는 다른 잣대를 배워야겠어.”"]], [["Bessy, too, was rather better this day.", "베시도 이날은 좀 나았다."], ["Still she was so weak that she seemed to have entirely forgotten her wish to see Margaret dressed—if, indeed, that had not been the feverish desire of a half delirious state.", "그래도 어찌나 기운이 없는지 차려입은 마거릿을 보고 싶다던 소원을 아주 잊어버린 듯했다. 실은 그것이 반쯤 헛소리를 하던 상태에서 나온 들뜬 바람이 아니었다면 말이다."]], [["Margaret could not help comparing this strange dressing of hers, to go where she did not care to be—her heart heavy with various anxieties—with the old, merry, girlish toilettes that she and Edith had performed scarcely more than a year ago.", "마거릿은 가고 싶지도 않은 곳에 가려고 이렇게 야릇하게 몸단장을 하면서, 온갖 근심으로 마음이 무거운 채로, 채 일 년도 되지 않은 옛날 이디스와 함께하던 그 즐겁고 처녀다운 몸단장을 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Her only pleasure now in decking herself out was in thinking that her mother would take delight in seeing her dressed.", "이제 그녀가 자신을 꾸미며 느끼는 유일한 기쁨은 차려입은 딸을 보며 어머니가 즐거워하리라는 생각뿐이었다."], ["She blushed when Dixon, throwing the drawing-room door open, made an appeal for admiration.", "딕슨이 응접실 문을 활짝 열어젖히고 감탄을 청했을 때 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“Miss Hale looks well, ma’am,—doesn’t she?", "“헤일 양이 곱지 않습니까요, 마님?"], ["Mrs. Shaw’s coral couldn’t have come in better.", "쇼 부인의 산호가 이보다 더 잘 어울릴 수는 없겠어요."], ["It just gives the right touch of colour, ma’am.", "딱 알맞게 빛깔을 더해 주는걸요, 마님."], ["Otherwise, Miss Margaret, you would have been too pale.”", "그게 아니었으면 마거릿 아가씨는 너무 창백해 보였을 겁니다요.”"]], [["Margaret’s black hair was too thick to be plaited; it needed rather to be twisted round and round, and have its fine silkiness compressed into massive coils, that encircled her head like a crown, and then were gathered into a large spiral knot behind.", "마거릿의 검은 머리는 너무 숱이 많아 땋을 수가 없었다. 오히려 몇 번이고 감아 돌려 그 고운 비단결을 묵직한 타래로 눌러 붙여야 했고, 그것이 왕관처럼 머리를 둘러싼 뒤 뒤쪽에서 커다란 소용돌이 모양으로 틀어 올려졌다."], ["She kept its weight together by two large coral pins, like small arrows for length.", "그녀는 그 무게를 큼직한 산호 핀 두 개로 여며 두었는데, 길이가 작은 화살만 했다."], ["Her white silk sleeves were looped up with strings of the same material, and on her neck, just below the base of her curved and milk-white throat, there lay heavy coral beads.", "흰 비단 소매는 같은 감의 끈으로 걷어 올려 묶었고, 우유처럼 흰 목덜미가 곡선을 그리며 내려앉는 바로 아래에는 묵직한 산호 구슬이 걸려 있었다."]], [["“Oh, Margaret!", "“아, 마거릿!"], ["how I should like to be going with you to one of the old Barrington assemblies,—taking you as Lady Beresford used to take me.”", "너를 데리고 옛날 배링턴 무도회에 가면 얼마나 좋을까. 베러스퍼드 부인이 나를 데리고 다니셨듯이 너를 데리고 말이다.”"]], [["Margaret kissed her mother for this little burst of maternal vanity; but she could hardly smile at it, she felt so much out of spirits.", "마거릿은 어머니가 터뜨린 이 자그마한 모성의 허영에 입을 맞추었다. 그러나 마음이 너무 가라앉아 미소조차 짓기 어려웠다."]], [["“I would rather stay at home with you,—much rather, mamma.”", "“저는 차라리 어머니와 집에 있고 싶어요. 훨씬 더요, 어머니.”"]], [["“Nonsense, darling!", "“무슨 소리냐, 얘야!"], ["Be sure you notice the dinner well.", "만찬을 잘 눈여겨보아야 한다."], ["I shall like to hear how they manage these things in Milton.", "밀턴에서는 그런 것을 어떻게 차리는지 듣고 싶구나."], ["Particularly the second course, dear.", "특히 두 번째 코스를 잘 보렴, 얘야."], ["Look what they have instead of game.”", "사냥한 고기 대신 무엇을 내는지 보아라.”"]], [["Mrs. Hale would have been more than interested,—she would have been astonished, if she had seen the sumptuousness of the dinner-table and its appointments.", "헤일 부인이 그 만찬 식탁의 호사스러움과 갖추어진 것들을 보았더라면 관심 정도가 아니라 깜짝 놀랐을 것이다."], ["Margaret, with her London cultivated taste, felt the number of delicacies to be oppressive; one half of the quantity would have been enough, and the effect tighter and more elegant.", "런던에서 길러진 안목을 지닌 마거릿은 진미의 가짓수가 숨 막힐 지경이라고 느꼈다. 절반만 되어도 넉넉했을 테고, 그 편이 한결 짜임새 있고 우아했을 것이다."], ["But it was one of Mrs. Thornton’s rigorous laws of hospitality, that of each separate dainty enough should be provided for all the guests to partake, if they felt inclined.", "그러나 손님들이 마음이 내키면 누구나 맛볼 수 있도록 진미마다 넉넉히 마련해 두는 것이 손턴 부인의 엄격한 접대 원칙 가운데 하나였다."], ["Careless to abstemiousness in her daily habits, it was part of her pride to set a feast before such of her guests as cared for it.", "일상에서는 절제를 넘어 무심할 만큼 검소했지만, 원하는 손님 앞에 잔칫상을 차려 내는 것은 그녀의 자부심의 한 부분이었다. 아들도 이 마음을 같이했다."], ["Her son shared this feeling.", "그는 훌륭한 식사를 주고받는 데 기대는 사교 말고 다른 교제를 알아 본 적이 없었다."], ["He had never known—though he might have imagined, and had the capability to relish—any kind of society but that which depended on an exchange of superb meals: and even now, though he was denying himself the personal expenditure of an unnecessary sixpence, and had more than once regretted that the invitations for this dinner had been sent out, still, as it was to be, he was glad to see the old magnificence of preparation.", "그런 것을 그려 볼 수도 있었고 즐길 능력도 있었지만 말이다. 그리고 지금은 쓸데없는 6펜스 한 닢도 제 몫으로는 쓰지 않고 있었고 이 만찬의 초대장을 보낸 것을 여러 번 후회하기까지 했지만, 그래도 이왕 치를 일이라면 옛날 그대로의 성대한 준비를 보는 것이 기뻤다."]], [["Margaret and her father were the first to arrive.", "마거릿과 아버지가 가장 먼저 도착했다."], ["Mr. Hale was anxiously punctual to the time specified.", "헤일 씨는 정해진 시간을 지키려고 애타게 마음을 썼다."], ["There was no one upstairs in the drawing-room but Mrs.", "위층 응접실에는 손턴 부인과 패니밖에 없었다."], ["Thornton and Fanny. Every cover was taken off, and the apartment blazed forth in yellow silk damask and a brilliantly flowered carpet.", "덮개는 모두 벗겨져 있었고, 방은 노란 비단 다마스크와 화려한 꽃무늬 양탄자로 눈부시게 빛났다."], ["Every corner seemed filled up with ornament, until it became a weariness to the eye, and presented a strange contrast to the bald ugliness of the look-out into the great mill-yard, where wide folding gates were thrown open for the admission of carriages.", "구석마다 장식으로 가득 채워져 눈이 피곤할 지경이었고, 마차를 들이려고 넓은 접이문이 활짝 열려 있는 커다란 공장 마당 쪽의 헐벗고 흉한 풍경과 야릇한 대조를 이루었다."], ["The mill loomed high on the left-hand side of the windows, casting a shadow down from its many stories, which darkened the summer evening before its time.", "창문 왼편으로는 공장이 높이 솟아 여러 층 높이에서 그늘을 드리웠고, 그 그늘이 여름 저녁을 제 시간보다 일찍 어둡게 만들었다."]], [["“My son was engaged up to the last moment on business.", "“아들이 마지막 순간까지 일에 매여 있었답니다."], ["He will be here directly, Mr. Hale.", "곧 올 겁니다, 헤일 씨."], ["May I beg you to take a seat?”", "앉으시지요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale was standing at one of the windows as Mrs.", "손턴 부인이 말할 때 헤일 씨는 창가에 서 있었다."], ["Thornton spoke. He turned away, saying,", "그는 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t you find such close neighbourhood to the mill rather unpleasant at times?”", "“공장이 이렇게 가까이 붙어 있으면 이따금 불편하지 않으십니까?”"]], [["She drew herself up:", "그녀는 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다."]], [["“Never.", "“전혀요."], ["I am not become so fine as to desire to forget the source of my son’s wealth and power.", "저는 아들의 부와 힘이 어디서 나오는지 잊고 싶어질 만큼 고상해지지는 않았습니다."], ["Besides, there is not such another factory in Milton.", "게다가 밀턴에 저만한 공장은 또 없지요."], ["One room alone is two hundred and twenty square yards.”", "방 하나만 해도 220평방야드입니다.”"]], [["“I meant that the smoke and the noise—the constant going out and coming in of the workpeople, might be annoying!”", "“제 말은 연기와 소음이, 직공들이 끊임없이 드나드는 것이 성가시지 않겠느냐는 뜻이었습니다!”"]], [["“I agree with you, Mr. Hale!”", "“저도 같은 생각이에요, 헤일 씨!”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“There is a continual smell of steam and oily machinery—and the noise is perfectly deafening.”", "“늘 증기와 기름 친 기계 냄새가 나고, 소음은 정말이지 귀가 먹먹할 지경이거든요.”"]], [["“I have heard noise that was called music far more deafening.", "“음악이라 부르는 소리 가운데도 그보다 훨씬 더 귀먹게 하는 것을 저는 들어 보았습니다."], ["The engine-room is at the street-end of the factory; we hardly hear it, except in summer weather, when all the windows are open; and as for the continual murmur of the workpeople, it disturbs me no more than the humming of a hive of bees.", "기관실은 공장에서 길 쪽 끝에 있어서, 창문을 다 여는 여름철이 아니면 소리가 거의 들리지 않아요. 직공들의 끊임없는 웅성거림으로 말하자면, 벌통에서 벌이 붕붕대는 것만큼도 저를 방해하지 않습니다."], ["If I think of it at all, I connect it with my son, and feel how all belongs to him, and that his is the head that directs it.", "굳이 그 소리를 생각한다면, 저는 그것을 아들과 이어서 생각하고, 그 모든 것이 아들의 것이며 그것을 이끄는 머리가 아들의 머리라는 것을 느낍니다. 지금은 공장에서 아무 소리도 나지 않지요."], ["Just now, there are no sounds to come from the mill; the hands have been ungrateful enough to turn out, as perhaps you have heard.", "아마 들으셨겠지만 직공들이 은혜도 모르고 파업에 들어갔으니까요. 그런데 댁이 들어오실 때 제가 말씀드린 바로 그 일이라는 게, 그자들에게 제 자리를 알게 하려고 아들이 밟으려는 절차에 관한 것이었습니다.”"], ["But the very business (of which I spoke when you entered), had reference to the steps he is going to take to make them learn their place.”", "이 말을 하는 동안 언제나 준엄한 그녀의 얼굴빛이 어두운 분노로 깊어졌다. 손턴 씨가 방에 들어왔을 때에도 그 표정은 걷히지 않았다."], ["The expression on her face, always stern, deepened into dark anger, as she said this.", "손님들은 그에게서 밝고 다정해 보이는 인사를 받았지만, 그녀는 아들이 떨쳐 내지 못하는 근심과 걱정의 무게를 한눈에 알아보았던 것이다."], ["Nor did it clear away when Mr.", "그는 마거릿과 악수를 했다."], ["Thornton entered the room; for she saw in an instant, the weight of care and anxiety which he could not shake off, although his guests received from him a greeting that appeared both cheerful and cordial.", "그는 두 사람의 손이 맞닿은 것이 이번이 처음임을 알고 있었으나, 그녀는 그 사실을 조금도 의식하지 못했다. 그는 헤일 부인의 안부를 물었고 헤일 씨의 낙관적이고 희망 섞인 이야기를 들었다."], ["He shook hands with Margaret. He knew it was the first time their hands had met, though she was perfectly unconscious of the fact.", "그리고 마거릿이 아버지의 말에 어디까지 동의하는지 알아보려고 그녀 쪽을 흘긋 보았을 때, 그 얼굴에는 반대하는 그늘이 조금도 스치지 않는 것을 보았다."], ["He inquired after Mrs. Hale, and heard Mr.", "그렇게 마음먹고 바라보는 사이 그는 그녀의 빼어난 아름다움에 새삼 마음을 얻어맞았다."], ["Hale’s sanguine, hopeful account; and, glancing to Margaret, to understand how far she agreed with her father, he saw that no dissenting shadow crossed her face.", "그는 그런 차림의 그녀를 전에 본 적이 없었다. 그런데 이제 보니 그토록 우아한 옷차림이 그녀의 기품 있는 자태와 드높이 고요한 얼굴에 어찌나 잘 어울리는지, 그녀는 늘 그렇게 입고 다녀야 마땅할 것 같았다."], ["And as he looked with this intention, he was struck anew with her great beauty.", "그녀는 패니와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 무슨 이야기인지는 들리지 않았다."], ["He had never seen her in such dress before; and yet now it appeared that such elegance of attire was so befitting her noble figure and lofty serenity of countenance that she ought to go always thus apparelled.", "그러나 그는 누이가 잠시도 가만있지 못하고 드레스 여기저기를 매만지는 모습을, 이리저리 헤매는 눈길이 아무 목적 없이 여기를 보았다 저기를 보았다 하는 것을 보았다."], ["She was talking to Fanny; about what he could not hear; but he saw his sister’s restless way of continually arranging some part of her gown, her wandering eyes, now glancing here, now there, but without any purpose in her observation; and he contrasted them uneasily with the large soft eyes that looked forth steadily at one object, as if from out their light beamed some gentle influence of repose: the curving lines of the red lips, just parted in the interest of listening to what her companion said—the head a little bent forwards, so as to make a long sweeping line from the summit, where the light caught on the glossy raven hair, to the smooth ivory tip of the shoulder; the round white arms, and taper hands, laid lightly across each other, but perfectly motionless in their pretty attitude.", "그러고는 그것을, 마치 그 빛 속에서 어떤 부드러운 안식의 기운이 뻗어 나오기라도 하듯 한곳만을 잔잔히 바라보는 크고 부드러운 눈과 불편하게 견주었다. 상대의 말을 흥미롭게 듣느라 살짝 벌어진 붉은 입술의 굽은 선, 조금 앞으로 숙인 머리, 그래서 빛이 윤나는 까마귀빛 머리에 닿는 정수리에서 매끄러운 상앗빛 어깨 끝까지 길게 쓸어내리는 선을 그리는 모습, 둥글고 흰 팔과 가느다란 손이 서로 가볍게 포개진 채 그 어여쁜 자세 그대로 조금도 움직이지 않는 것을."], ["Mr. Thornton sighed as he took in all this with one of his sudden comprehensive glances.", "손턴 씨는 특유의 재빠르고 두루 살피는 눈길 한 번으로 이 모든 것을 받아들이고는 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["And then he turned his back to the young ladies, and threw himself, with an effort, but with all his heart and soul, into a conversation with Mr. Hale.", "그러고 나서 그는 젊은 여자들에게 등을 돌리고, 애를 써서, 그러나 온 마음과 정신을 다해 헤일 씨와의 대화에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["More people came—more and more.", "사람들이 더 왔다. 자꾸자꾸 늘어났다."], ["Fanny left Margaret’s side and helped her mother to receive her guests.", "패니는 마거릿 곁을 떠나 어머니를 도와 손님을 맞았다."], ["Mr. Thornton felt that in this influx no one was speaking to Margaret, and was restless under this apparent neglect.", "손턴 씨는 이 밀려드는 사람들 속에서 아무도 마거릿에게 말을 걸지 않는 것을 느꼈고, 이 눈에 띄는 홀대에 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["But he never went near her himself; he did not look at her.", "그러나 그 자신은 결코 그녀 가까이 가지 않았다."], ["Only, he knew what she was doing—or not doing—better than he knew the movements of any one else in the room.", "그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았다. 다만 그는 그녀가 무엇을 하고 있는지, 혹은 하지 않고 있는지를 방 안의 다른 누구의 움직임보다 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Margaret was so unconscious of herself, and so much amused by watching other people, that she never thought whether she was left unnoticed or not.", "마거릿은 자기 자신을 조금도 의식하지 않았고 다른 사람들을 구경하는 것이 하도 재미있어서, 자기가 눈길 밖에 밀려나 있는지 아닌지 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["Somebody took her down to dinner; she did not catch the name; nor did he seem much inclined to talk to her.", "누군가 그녀를 만찬 자리로 이끌었다. 그녀는 그 이름을 알아듣지 못했고, 그 사람도 그녀에게 말을 붙일 마음이 별로 없어 보였다."], ["There was a very animated conversation going on among the gentlemen; the ladies, for the most part, were silent, employing themselves in taking notes of the dinner and criticising each other’s dresses.", "신사들 사이에서는 아주 활기찬 대화가 오가고 있었다. 부인들은 대개 말이 없었고, 만찬 차림을 눈여겨보고 서로의 옷을 품평하는 일에 몰두했다."], ["Margaret caught the clue to the general conversation, grew interested and listened attentively.", "마거릿은 오가는 이야기의 실마리를 붙잡고는 흥미가 생겨 귀를 기울였다."], ["Mr. Horsfall, the stranger, whose visit to the town was the original germ of the party, was asking questions relative to the trade and manufactures of the place; and the rest of the gentlemen—all Milton men—were giving him answers and explanations.", "이 모임의 애초 씨앗이 된 방문객, 낯선 손님 호스폴 씨는 이 고장의 상업과 제조업에 관해 이것저것 묻고 있었고, 나머지 신사들은 모두 밀턴 사람이라 그에게 대답과 설명을 해 주고 있었다."], ["Some dispute arose, which was warmly contested; it was referred to Mr.", "어떤 논쟁이 일어나 뜨겁게 다투어졌고, 그것이 그때까지 거의 말이 없던 손턴 씨에게 넘어갔다."], ["Thornton, who had hardly spoken before; but who now gave an opinion, the grounds of which were so clearly stated that even the opponents yielded.", "그러나 그가 이제 내놓은 의견은 그 근거가 어찌나 또렷하게 밝혀졌는지 반대하던 이들조차 물러섰다. 그리하여 마거릿의 눈길이 주인에게 쏠렸다."], ["Margaret’s attention was thus called to her host; his whole manner, as master of the house, and entertainer of his friends, was so straightforward, yet simple and modest, as to be thoroughly dignified.", "집주인이자 벗들을 대접하는 사람으로서 그의 태도 전체가 어찌나 거침없으면서도 꾸밈없고 겸손한지, 그야말로 품위 있었다. 마거릿은 그가 이토록 돋보이는 모습을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."], ["Margaret thought she had never seen him to so much advantage.", "그가 자기들 집에 왔을 때는 늘 무언가가 있었다."], ["When he had come to their house, there had been always something, either of over-eagerness or of that kind of vexed annoyance which seemed ready to pre-suppose that he was unjustly judged, and yet felt too proud to try and make himself better understood.", "지나친 열의이거나, 자기가 부당하게 판단받고 있다고 미리 넘겨짚을 태세이면서도 자신을 더 잘 이해시키려 애쓰기에는 너무 자존심이 센 사람 특유의 짜증 섞인 언짢음이거나."], ["But now, among his fellows, there was no uncertainty as to his position.", "그러나 지금 자기 동료들 사이에서는 그의 자리에 대해 아무런 의심이 없었다."], ["He was regarded by them as a man of great force of character; of power in many ways.", "그들은 그를 성품의 힘이 강한 사람, 여러 면에서 능력 있는 사람으로 여겼다."], ["There was no need to struggle for their respect.", "그들의 존경을 얻으려고 애쓸 필요가 없었다."], ["He had it, and he knew it; and the security of this gave a fine grand quietness to his voice and ways, which Margaret had missed before.", "그는 이미 그것을 가지고 있었고 스스로도 알았다. 그리고 그 든든함이 그의 목소리와 태도에 마거릿이 전에는 보지 못했던 훌륭하고 당당한 고요함을 주었다."]], [["He was not in the habit of talking to ladies; and what he did say was a little formal.", "그는 여자들과 이야기하는 데 익숙하지 않았고, 하는 말도 조금 딱딱했다."], ["To Margaret herself he hardly spoke at all.", "마거릿 자신에게는 거의 말을 걸지 않았다."], ["She was surprised to think how much she enjoyed this dinner.", "그녀는 자기가 이 만찬을 이토록 즐기고 있다는 사실에 놀랐다."], ["She knew enough now to understand many local interests—nay, even some of the technical words employed by the eager millowners.", "이제 그녀는 이 고장의 여러 관심사를, 아니 열을 올리는 공장주들이 쓰는 전문 용어까지 얼마쯤 알아들을 만큼은 알고 있었다."], ["She silently took a very decided part in the question they were discussing.", "그녀는 그들이 논하는 문제에 대해 말없이 아주 뚜렷한 편을 들었다."], ["At any rate, they talked in desperate earnest,—not in the used-up style that wearied her so in the old London parties.", "어쨌든 그들은 필사적일 만큼 진지하게 이야기했다. 옛날 런던 모임에서 그녀를 그토록 지치게 하던 닳고 닳은 투가 아니었다."], ["She wondered that, with all this dwelling on the manufacturers and trade of the place, no allusion was made to the strike then pending.", "그녀는 이 고장의 제조업과 상업을 이렇게나 물고 늘어지면서도 그때 걸려 있는 파업 이야기는 한마디도 나오지 않는 것이 이상했다."], ["She did not yet know how coolly such things were taken by the masters, as having only one possible end.", "그런 일을 공장주들이 얼마나 태연하게, 있을 수 있는 결말은 하나뿐이라는 듯 받아들이는지 그녀는 아직 몰랐다."], ["To be sure, the men were cutting their own throats, as they had done many a time before; but if they would be fools, and put themselves into the hands of a rascally set of paid delegates, they must take the consequence.", "물론 그자들은 전에도 여러 번 그랬듯 제 목을 제가 조르고 있는 것이지만, 굳이 바보짓을 해서 삯 받는 파렴치한 대표들 손에 제 몸을 맡기겠다면 그 결과는 저희가 감당해야 한다는 것이었다."], ["One or two thought Thornton looked out of spirits; and, of course, he must lose by this turn-out.", "한둘은 손턴이 기운이 없어 보인다고 여겼다. 물론 그도 이 파업으로 손해를 볼 것이었다."], ["But it was an accident that might happen to themselves any day; and Thornton was as good to manage a strike as any one; for he was as iron a chap as any in Milton.", "그러나 그것은 자기들에게도 언제든 닥칠 수 있는 사고였고, 손턴은 누구 못지않게 파업을 잘 다룰 사람이었다. 밀턴의 누구 못지않게 쇠 같은 사내였으니까."], ["The hands had mistaken their man in trying that dodge on him.", "직공들은 그런 수작을 그에게 부리다가 사람을 잘못 골랐다."], ["And they chuckled inwardly at the idea of the workmen’s discomfiture and defeat, in their attempt to alter one iota of what Thornton had decreed.", "그리고 그들은 손턴이 정해 놓은 것을 털끝만큼이라도 바꿔 보려다 낭패를 보고 패배할 직공들을 떠올리며 속으로 낄낄거렸다."]], [["It was rather dull for Margaret after dinner.", "만찬 뒤 마거릿에게는 다소 따분한 시간이었다."], ["She was glad when the gentlemen came, not merely because she caught her father’s eye to brighten her sleepiness up; but because she could listen to something larger and grander than the petty interests which the ladies had been talking about.", "그녀는 신사들이 들어오자 반가웠다. 아버지와 눈이 마주쳐 졸음을 떨칠 수 있어서만이 아니라, 부인들이 이야기하던 자잘한 관심사보다 더 크고 더 웅장한 무언가에 귀를 기울일 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["She liked the exultation in the sense of power which these Milton men had.", "그녀는 이 밀턴 사람들이 지닌 힘의 자각에서 오는 그 의기양양함이 마음에 들었다."], ["It might be rather rampant in its display, and savour of boasting; but still they seemed to defy the old limits of possibility, in a kind of fine intoxication, caused by the recollection of what had been achieved, and what yet should be.", "드러내는 방식이 다소 거칠고 자랑처럼 들릴 수도 있었다. 그러나 그들은 이미 이룬 것과 앞으로 이룰 것을 떠올리며 생기는 어떤 훌륭한 도취 속에서 가능성의 오랜 한계에 맞서는 듯했다."], ["If in her cooler moments she might not approve of the spirit in all things, still there was much to admire in their forgetfulness of themselves and the present, in their anticipated triumphs over all inanimate matter at some future time which none of them would live to see.", "차분해진 순간이라면 그 기개를 모든 점에서 인정하지는 못할지라도, 자기 자신과 지금을 잊어버리는 그 태도에는, 그들 가운데 아무도 살아서 보지 못할 어느 훗날 온갖 무생물을 이겨 내리라 미리 내다보는 그 마음에는 감탄할 것이 많았다."], ["She was rather startled when Mr. Thornton spoke to her close at her elbow:", "손턴 씨가 바로 팔꿈치 옆에서 말을 걸었을 때 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["“I could see you were on our side in our discussion at dinner,—were you not, Miss Hale?”", "“만찬 자리의 논쟁에서 헤일 양이 우리 편이라는 걸 알 수 있었습니다. 그렇지 않았습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“물론이에요."], ["But then I know so little about it. I was surprised, however, to find from what Mr.", "그렇지만 저는 그 일에 대해 아는 것이 너무 적어요."], ["Horsfall said, that there were others who thought in so diametrically opposite a manner, as the Mr. Morison he spoke about.", "그래도 호스폴 씨의 말씀에서, 그분이 이야기한 모리슨 씨처럼 정반대로 생각하는 이들이 있다는 것을 알게 되어 놀랐습니다."], ["He cannot be a gentleman—is he?”", "그분은 신사일 리가 없어요. 그렇지요?”"]], [["“I am not quite the person to decide on another’s gentlemanliness, Miss Hale.", "“남의 신사다움을 판정하기에 저는 그리 알맞은 사람이 아닙니다, 헤일 양."], ["I mean, I don’t quite understand your application of the word.", "제 말은, 그 말을 헤일 양이 어떻게 쓰시는지 잘 모르겠다는 뜻입니다."], ["But I should say that this Morison is no true man.", "다만 저라면 이 모리슨이 참된 사람은 아니라고 하겠습니다. 그가 누구인지 저는 모릅니다."], ["I don’t know who he is; I merely judge him from Mr. Horsfall’s account.”", "그저 호스폴 씨의 이야기로 판단할 뿐이지요.”"]], [["“I suspect my ‘gentleman’ includes your ‘true man.’”", "“제가 말하는 ‘신사’ 안에 손턴 씨가 말씀하시는 ‘참된 사람’이 들어 있는 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“And a great deal more, you would imply.", "“게다가 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것이 들어 있다는 말씀이시겠지요."], ["I differ from you.", "저는 생각이 다릅니다."], ["A man is to me a higher and a completer being than a gentleman.”", "제게는 사람이 신사보다 더 높고 더 온전한 존재입니다.”"]], [["“What do you mean?”", "“무슨 뜻이신가요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“We must understand the words differently.”", "“우리가 그 말들을 서로 다르게 이해하고 있는 모양이에요.”"]], [["“I take it that ‘gentleman’ is a term that only describes a person in his relation to others; but when we speak of him as ‘a man,’ we consider him not merely with regard to his fellow-men, but in relation to himself,—to life—to time—to eternity. A cast-away lonely as Robinson Crusoe—a prisoner immured in a dungeon for life—nay, even a saint in Patmos, has his endurance, his strength, his faith, best described by being spoken of as ‘a man.’ I am rather weary of this word ‘gentlemanly,’ which seems to me to be often inappropriately used, and often, too, with such exaggerated distortion of meaning, while the full simplicity of the noun ‘man,’ and the adjective ‘manly’ are acknowledged—that I am induced to class it with the cant of the day.”", "“제가 보기에 ‘신사’란 어떤 사람을 남들과의 관계에서만 그려 내는 말입니다. 그러나 그를 ‘사람’이라 말할 때 우리는 그를 동료 인간과의 관계에서만이 아니라 자기 자신과의 관계에서, 삶과의, 시간과의, 영원과의 관계에서 헤아리게 됩니다. 로빈슨 크루소처럼 홀로 버려진 조난자도, 평생 지하 감옥에 갇힌 죄수도, 아니 밧모섬의 성자까지도, 그 인내와 힘과 믿음은 ‘사람’이라 불릴 때 가장 잘 드러납니다. 저는 이 ‘신사답다’는 말에 다소 물렸습니다. 제게는 그 말이 흔히 어울리지도 않는 자리에 쓰이고, 흔히 뜻이 지나치게 뒤틀린 채로 쓰이는 것처럼 보이니까요. 그동안 ‘사람’이라는 명사와 ‘사내답다’는 형용사가 지닌 온전한 단순함은 인정받고 있는데도 말입니다. 그래서 저는 그 말을 요즘의 겉치레 유행어에 넣고 싶어집니다.”"]], [["Margaret thought a moment—but before she could speak her slow conviction, he was called away by some of the eager manufacturers, whose speeches she could not hear, though she could guess at their import by the short clear answers Mr.", "마거릿은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러나 천천히 굳어 가는 자신의 확신을 말하기도 전에 그는 열을 올리는 공장주 몇 사람에게 불려 갔다."], ["Thornton gave, which came steady and firm as the boom of a distant minute gun.", "그들의 말은 들리지 않았지만, 손턴 씨가 내놓는 짧고 또렷한 대답으로 무슨 이야기인지 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["They were evidently talking of the turn-out, and suggesting what course had best be pursued.", "그 대답은 멀리서 1분마다 울리는 조포 소리처럼 한결같고 굳세게 들려왔다. 그들은 분명 파업 이야기를 하며 어떤 길을 밟는 것이 가장 나은지 궁리하고 있었다."], ["She heard Mr. Thornton say:", "그녀는 손턴 씨가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“That has been done.”", "“그건 이미 해 두었습니다.”"], ["Then came a hurried murmur, in which two or three joined.", "그러자 두어 사람이 끼어든 다급한 웅성거림이 뒤따랐다."]], [["“All those arrangements have been made.”", "“그 준비는 모두 마쳤습니다.”"]], [["Some doubts were implied, some difficulties named by Mr. Slickson, who took hold of Mr.", "슬릭슨 씨가 몇 가지 의심을 내비치고 몇 가지 어려움을 들었다."], ["Thornton’s arm, the better to impress his words. Mr.", "그는 자기 말에 무게를 실으려고 손턴 씨의 팔을 붙잡았다."], ["Thornton moved slightly away, lifted his eyebrows a very little, and then replied:", "손턴 씨는 살짝 몸을 비켜 서며 눈썹을 아주 조금 치켜올리고는 대답했다."]], [["“I take the risk.", "“위험은 제가 집니다."], ["You need not join in it unless you choose.”", "원치 않으시면 함께하지 않으셔도 됩니다.”"], ["Still some more fears were urged.", "그래도 몇 가지 두려움이 더 제기되었다."]], [["“I’m not afraid of anything so dastardly as incendiarism.", "“저는 방화 같은 비열한 짓 따위는 두렵지 않습니다."], ["We are open enemies; and I can protect myself from any violence that I apprehend.", "우리는 드러내 놓고 맞선 적수이고, 저는 제가 짐작할 수 있는 어떤 폭력으로부터도 제 몸을 지킬 수 있습니다."], ["And I will assuredly protect all others who come to me for work.", "그리고 일자리를 찾아 제게 오는 다른 사람들도 반드시 지켜 줄 것입니다."], ["They know my determination by this time, as well and as fully as you do.”", "지금쯤은 그자들도 제 결심을 여러분만큼이나 잘, 그리고 낱낱이 알고 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr.", "호스폴 씨가 그를 한쪽으로 조금 데려갔다."], ["Horsfall took him a little on one side, as Margaret conjectured, to ask him some other question about the strike; but, in truth, it was to inquire who she herself was—so quiet, so stately, and so beautiful.", "마거릿은 파업에 대해 무언가 다른 것을 물으려는 줄 짐작했으나, 실은 그토록 조용하고 그토록 기품 있고 그토록 아름다운 그녀 자신이 누구인지 묻기 위해서였다."]], [["“A Milton lady?”", "“밀턴 아가씨인가요?”"], ["asked he, as the name was given.", "이름을 듣고 그가 물었다."]], [["“No!", "“아닙니다!"], ["from the south of England—Hampshire, I believe,”", "잉글랜드 남부에서 왔지요. 햄프셔라고 알고 있습니다.”"], ["was the cold, indifferent answer.", "차갑고 무심한 대답이었다."]], [["Mrs. Slickson was catechising Fanny on the same subject.", "슬릭슨 부인은 패니를 붙들고 같은 것을 캐묻고 있었다."]], [["“Who is that fine distinguished-looking girl?", "“저 곱고 기품 있어 보이는 아가씨는 누구인가요?"], ["a sister of Mr. Horsfall’s?”", "호스폴 씨의 누이인가요?”"]], [["“Oh dear, no!", "“어머, 아니에요!"], ["That is Mr. Hale, her father, talking now to Mr.", "저기 지금 스티븐스 씨와 이야기하고 계신 헤일 씨가 저 아가씨의 아버지랍니다."], ["Stephens.", "수업을 하세요."], ["He gives lessons; that is to say, he reads with young men.", "그러니까 젊은 사람들과 함께 책을 읽으신다는 뜻이에요."], ["My brother John goes to him twice a week, and so he begged mamma to ask them here, in hopes of getting him known.", "우리 오빠 존이 일주일에 두 번 그분께 다니는데, 그분을 알리려고 어머니께 이 자리에 초대해 달라고 부탁했지 뭐예요."], ["I believe we have some of their prospectuses, if you would like to have one.”", "그분들 안내장이 우리 집에 몇 장 있을 텐데, 원하시면 하나 드릴게요.”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton!", "“손턴 씨가요!"], ["Does he really find time to read with a tutor, in the midst of all his business,—and this abominable strike in hand as well?”", "그 많은 일 가운데, 게다가 이 지긋지긋한 파업까지 안고 있으면서 정말로 선생을 두고 책 읽을 시간을 내신다고요?”"]], [["Fanny was not sure, from Mrs. Slickson’s manner, whether she ought to be proud or ashamed of her brother’s conduct; and, like all people who try and take other people’s “ought”", "패니는 슬릭슨 부인의 태도로 미루어 오빠의 처신을 자랑스러워해야 할지 부끄러워해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["for the rule of their feelings, she was inclined to blush for any singularity of action.", "그리고 남들의 “마땅히”를 제 감정의 잣대로 삼으려 드는 모든 사람이 그렇듯, 그녀는 남다른 행동이라면 무엇이든 얼굴을 붉힐 마음이 되어 있었다."], ["Her shame was interrupted by the dispersion of the guests.", "그녀의 부끄러움은 손님들이 흩어지는 바람에 중단되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI", "# 제21장"]], [["“On earth is known to none The smile that is not sister to a tear.”", "“이 땅에서 아는 이는 아무도 없다, 눈물과 자매가 아닌 미소를.”"], ["ELLIOTT.", "— 엘리엇."]], [["Margaret and her father walked home.", "마거릿은 아버지와 함께 걸어서 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["The night was fine, the streets clean, and with her pretty white silk, like Leezie Lindsay’s gown o’ green satin, in the ballad, “kilted up to her knee,”", "밤은 맑고 거리는 깨끗했으며, 발라드 속 리지 린지의 초록 새틴 드레스처럼 “무릎까지 걷어 올린” 고운 흰 비단을 걸치고 그녀는 아버지와 함께 길을 나섰다."], ["she was off with her father—ready to dance along with the excitement of the cool, fresh night air.", "서늘하고 상쾌한 밤공기의 들뜸에 실려 춤이라도 추며 갈 듯한 마음이었다."]], [["“I rather think Thornton is not quite easy in his mind about this strike.", "“아무래도 손턴은 이번 파업 때문에 마음이 그리 편치 않은 모양이더구나."], ["He seemed very anxious to-night.”", "오늘 밤에는 무척 근심스러워 보였어.”"]], [["“I should wonder if he were not.", "“편하시다면 오히려 이상한 일이겠지요."], ["But he spoke with his usual coolness to the others, when they suggested different things, just before we came away.”", "그래도 우리가 자리를 뜨기 직전에 다른 분들이 이런저런 방안을 내놓았을 때, 그분은 여느 때와 다름없이 침착하게 말씀하시던걸요.”"]], [["“So he did after dinner as well.", "“만찬 뒤에도 그랬지."], ["It would take a good deal to stir him from his cool manner of speaking; but his face strikes me as anxious.”", "그 침착한 말투를 흔들어 놓으려면 어지간한 일로는 안 될 게다. 그래도 얼굴만은 근심스러워 보이더구나.”"]], [["“I should be, if I were he.", "“제가 그분이라면 저도 근심스러울 거예요."], ["He must know of the growing anger and hardly smothered hatred of his workpeople, who all looked upon him as what the Bible calls a ‘hard man,’—not so much unjust as unfeeling; clear in judgment, standing upon his ‘rights’ as no human being ought to stand, considering what we and all our petty rights are in the sight of the Almighty.", "그분은 직공들 사이에 자라나는 분노와 겨우 눌러 둔 미움을 모를 리 없어요. 그들은 하나같이 그분을 성경에서 말하는 ‘모진 사람’으로 여기잖아요."], ["I am glad you think he looks anxious.", "불의하다기보다 인정이 없는 사람이요."], ["When I remember Boucher’s half mad words and ways, I cannot bear to think how coolly Mr.", "판단은 분명하되, 우리와 우리의 하찮은 권리라는 것이 전능하신 분 앞에서 무엇인지를 생각하면 사람으로서 그렇게까지 버텨서는 안 될 만큼 제 ‘권리’ 위에 버티고 선 사람이요. 저는 아버지께서 그분이 근심스러워 보인다고 하시니 다행이에요."], ["Thornton spoke.”", "바우처의 반쯤 미친 듯한 말과 몸짓을 떠올리면, 손턴 씨가 얼마나 냉랭하게 말했는지 견딜 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“In the first place, I am not so convinced as you are about that man Boucher’s utter distress; for the moment, he was badly off, I don’t doubt.", "“우선 첫째로, 나는 그 바우처라는 사람의 지독한 곤경을 너만큼 확신하지는 않는다. 당장은 형편이 나빴겠지, 그건 의심하지 않아."], ["But there is always a mysterious supply of money from these Unions; and, from what you said, it was evident the man was of a passionate, demonstrative nature, and gave strong expression to all he felt.”", "하지만 이 조합이라는 데서는 늘 어디선가 알 수 없이 돈이 나오는 법이다. 게다가 네 말로 미루어 보면 그 사람은 성정이 격하고 드러내기를 잘하는 부류라 느끼는 것을 죄다 강하게 터뜨리는 사람인 게 분명하더구나.”"]], [["“Oh, papa!”", "“아, 아버지!”"]], [["“Well!", "“그래!"], ["I only want you to do justice to Mr.", "나는 다만 네가 손턴 씨에게 공정하기를 바랄 뿐이다."], ["Thornton, who is, I suspect, of an exactly opposite nature—a man who is far too proud to show his feelings.", "내가 짐작하기로 그 사람은 정확히 그 반대되는 성정이야. 제 감정을 드러내기에는 자존심이 너무 센 사람이지."], ["Just the character I should have thought beforehand, you would have admired, Margaret.”", "미리 이야기만 들었더라면 네가 감탄했을 법한 바로 그런 성품이 아니냐, 마거릿.”"]], [["“So I do—so I should; but I don’t feel quite so sure as you do of the existence of those feelings.", "“감탄해요. 감탄할 거고요."], ["He is a man of great strength of character—of unusual intellect, considering the few advantages he has had.”", "하지만 그런 감정이 정말 있는지는 아버지만큼 확신이 서지 않아요. 그분은 성품의 힘이 아주 강한 사람이고, 누려 온 이점이 얼마 없었던 것을 생각하면 보기 드문 지성을 지녔지요.”"]], [["“Not so few.", "“그리 적지도 않다."], ["He has led a practical life from a very early age, has been called upon to exercise judgment and self-control.", "그 사람은 아주 어릴 때부터 실제적인 삶을 살아왔고, 판단력과 자제력을 쓰도록 요구받아 왔어. 그 모두가 지성의 한 부분을 길러 준다."], ["All that developes one part of the intellect.", "물론 그에게는 과거에 대한 지식이 얼마쯤 필요하지. 그것이야말로 앞날을 헤아리는 데 가장 참된 바탕이 되니까."], ["To be sure, he needs some of the knowledge of the past, which gives the truest basis for conjecture as to the future; but he knows this need—he perceives it, and that is something.", "하지만 그는 그 필요를 알고 있다. 그것을 스스로 알아차리고 있고, 그것만 해도 대단한 일이야."], ["You are quite prejudiced against Mr. Thornton, Margaret.”", "너는 손턴 씨에게 아주 편견을 품고 있구나, 마거릿.”"]], [["“He is the first specimen of a manufacturer—of a person engaged in trade—that I had ever the opportunity of studying, papa.", "“그분은 제가 여태 살펴볼 기회를 가진 첫 번째 제조업자, 장사에 몸담은 사람의 표본이에요, 아버지. 제 첫 올리브인 셈이지요."], ["He is my first olive: let me make a face while I swallow it.", "삼키면서 얼굴 한 번 찡그리는 것쯤은 봐주세요."], ["I know he is good of his kind, and by and by I shall like the kind.", "그분이 그 부류에서 훌륭한 사람이라는 건 알아요."], ["I rather think I am already beginning to do so.", "그리고 차차 저도 그 부류를 좋아하게 되겠지요."], ["I was very much interested by what the gentlemen were talking about, although I did not understand half of it.", "아무래도 벌써 그렇게 되기 시작한 것 같아요. 저는 신사분들이 나누는 이야기가 아주 흥미로웠어요."], ["I was quite sorry when Miss Thornton came to take me to the other end of the room, saying she was sure I should be uncomfortable at being the only lady among so many gentlemen.", "절반도 알아듣지 못했지만요. 손턴 양이 와서, 그 많은 신사들 사이에 여자라고는 저 혼자뿐이니 분명 불편할 거라며 저를 방 저쪽 끝으로 데려갔을 때는 정말 아쉬웠어요."], ["I had never thought about it, I was so busy listening; and the ladies were so dull, papa—oh, so dull!", "저는 듣느라 바빠서 그런 건 생각도 못 하고 있었거든요. 그리고 부인들은 어찌나 따분하던지요, 아버지."], ["Yet I think it was clever too.", "아, 정말 따분했어요! 그래도 영리하다는 생각은 들었어요."], ["It reminded me of our old game of having each so many nouns to introduce into a sentence.”", "우리가 예전에 하던 놀이가 떠올랐거든요. 저마다 정해진 명사 몇 개를 문장 속에 다 집어넣는 놀이요.”"]], [["“What do you mean, child?”", "“무슨 말이냐, 얘야?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Why, they took nouns that were signs of things which gave evidence of wealth,—housekeepers, under-gardeners, extent of glass, valuable lace, diamonds, and all such things; and each one formed her speech so as to bring them all in, in the prettiest accidental manner possible.”", "“그러니까요, 그분들은 부유함을 드러내 보이는 것들을 가리키는 명사를 고르는 거예요. 가정부, 아래 정원사, 온실 유리의 넓이, 값진 레이스, 다이아몬드, 그런 것들이요. 그리고 저마다 그것들을 되도록 예쁘게, 되도록 우연인 척하는 투로 다 끌어들이도록 말을 지어내는 거지요.”"]], [["“You will be as proud of your one servant when you get her, if all is true about her that Mrs.", "“너도 하녀를 하나 두게 되면 그 한 사람을 그만큼 자랑스러워하게 될 게다."], ["Thornton says.”", "손턴 부인이 그 애에 대해 하는 말이 다 사실이라면 말이야.”"]], [["“To be sure, I shall.", "“그럼요, 그럴 거예요."], ["I felt like a great hypocrite to-night, sitting there in my white silk gown, with my idle hands before me, when I remembered all the good, thorough, house-work they had done to-day.", "오늘 밤엔 제가 대단한 위선자 같았어요. 흰 비단 드레스를 입고 손을 놀린 채 앉아 있으면서, 우리 집에서 오늘 해낸 그 알차고 빈틈없는 집안일을 다 떠올리자니 말이에요."], ["They took me for a fine lady, I’m sure.”", "그분들은 분명 저를 귀부인으로 여겼을 거예요.”"]], [["“Even I was mistaken enough to think you looked like a lady, my dear,”", "“나조차도 네가 귀부인처럼 보인다고 잘못 생각할 뻔했다, 얘야.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, quietly smiling.", "헤일 씨가 조용히 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["But smiles were changed to white and trembling looks when they saw Dixon’s face, as she opened the door.", "그러나 딕슨이 문을 열었을 때 그 얼굴을 보고 미소는 창백하게 떨리는 낯빛으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“Oh, master!—Oh, Miss Margaret!", "“아, 나리! 아, 마거릿 아가씨!"], ["Thank God you are come!", "돌아오셔서 정말 다행입니다요!"], ["Dr. Donaldson is here.", "도널드슨 의사 선생님이 와 계세요."], ["The servant next door went for him, for the charwoman is gone home.", "파출부는 집으로 가 버려서 옆집 하인이 모시러 갔습니다요. 마님은 이제 좀 나아지셨어요."], ["She’s better now; but, oh sir! I thought she’d have died an hour ago.”", "하지만 아이고, 나리, 저는 한 시간 전만 해도 마님이 돌아가시는 줄 알았습니다요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale caught Margaret’s arm to steady himself from falling.", "헤일 씨는 쓰러지지 않으려고 마거릿의 팔을 붙들었다."], ["He looked at her face, and saw an expression upon it of surprise and extremest sorrow, but not the agony of terror that contracted his own unprepared heart.", "그는 딸의 얼굴을 보았고, 거기서 놀람과 극심한 슬픔의 빛을 읽었으나 아무 준비 없던 자기 심장을 옥죄는 그런 공포의 고통은 보지 못했다."], ["She knew more than he did, and yet she listened with that hopeless expression of awed apprehension.", "딸은 자기보다 더 많이 알고 있었다. 그러면서도 두려움에 눌린 그 절망적인 예감의 표정으로 이야기를 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Oh! I should not have left her—wicked daughter that I am!”", "“아, 어머니 곁을 떠나지 말았어야 했는데."], ["moaned forth Margaret, as she supported her trembling father’s hasty steps upstairs.", "저는 못된 딸이에요!” 마거릿은 떨리는 아버지의 다급한 걸음을 부축해 위층으로 오르며 신음하듯 내뱉었다."], ["Dr. Donaldson met them on the landing.", "도널드슨 의사가 층계참에서 두 사람을 맞았다."]], [["“She is better now,”", "“이제 좀 나아지셨습니다.”"], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."], ["“The opiate has taken effect.", "“아편이 들었어요. 경련이 아주 심했습니다."], ["The spasms were very bad: no wonder they frightened your maid; but she’ll rally this time.”", "하녀가 놀란 것도 무리가 아니지요. 하지만 이번에는 기운을 되찾으실 겁니다.”"]], [["“This time!", "“이번에는이라니!"], ["Let me go to her!” Half an hour ago Mr.", "아내에게 가게 해 주시오!”"], ["Hale was a middle-aged man; now his sight was dim, his senses wavering, his walk tottering, as if he were seventy years of age.", "30분 전만 해도 헤일 씨는 중년의 남자였다. 그런데 지금은 눈이 흐릿하고 감각이 흔들리고 걸음이 비틀거려 마치 70세 노인 같았다."]], [["Dr. Donaldson took his arm, and led him into the bedroom.", "도널드슨 의사가 그의 팔을 잡고 침실로 이끌었다."], ["Margaret followed close.", "마거릿이 바짝 뒤따랐다."], ["There lay her mother, with an unmistakeable look on her face.", "거기 어머니가 누워 있었는데, 그 얼굴에는 오해할 수 없는 빛이 어려 있었다. 지금은 나아졌을지도 몰랐다."], ["She might be better now; she was sleeping, but Death had signed her for his own, and it was clear that ere long he would return to take possession.", "어머니는 잠들어 있었다. 그러나 죽음이 이미 그녀를 제 것으로 표해 두었고, 머지않아 다시 돌아와 차지하리라는 것이 분명했다."], ["Mr. Hale looked at her for some time without a word.", "헤일 씨는 한동안 말없이 아내를 바라보았다."], ["Then he began to shake all over, and, turning away from Dr.", "그러다 온몸을 떨기 시작했고, 도널드슨 의사의 근심 어린 보살핌을 뿌리치고 돌아서서 손으로 더듬어 문을 찾았다."], ["Donaldson’s anxious care, he groped to find the door; he could not see it, although several candles, brought in the sudden affright, were burning and flaring there.", "갑작스러운 소동에 들여온 촛불 몇 자루가 거기서 타오르며 일렁이고 있었건만 그의 눈에는 문이 보이지 않았다."], ["He staggered into the drawing-room, and felt about for a chair. Dr.", "그는 비틀거리며 응접실로 들어가 손을 더듬어 의자를 찾았다."], ["Donaldson wheeled one to him, and placed him in it.", "도널드슨 의사가 의자 하나를 밀어다 그를 앉혔다."], ["He felt his pulse.", "그러고는 맥을 짚었다."]], [["“Speak to him, Miss Hale.", "“말을 걸어 보십시오, 헤일 양."], ["We must rouse him.”", "정신을 차리게 해 드려야 합니다.”"]], [["“Papa!”", "“아버지!”"], ["said Margaret, with a crying voice that was wild with pain.", "마거릿이 고통으로 사나워진 울음 섞인 목소리로 말했다. “아버지!"], ["“Papa! Speak to me!”", "저한테 말씀 좀 해 보세요!”"], ["The speculation came again into his eyes, and he made a great effort.", "그의 눈에 생각하는 빛이 다시 돌아왔고, 그는 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["“Margaret, did you know of this?", "“마거릿, 너 이걸 알고 있었느냐?"], ["Oh, it was cruel of you!”", "아, 어쩌면 그렇게 모질 수가 있느냐!”"]], [["“No, sir, it was not cruel!” replied Dr.", "“아닙니다, 선생님, 모진 것이 아니었습니다!”"], ["Donaldson, with quick decision.", "도널드슨 의사가 재빨리 단호하게 대답했다."], ["“Miss Hale acted under my directions.", "“헤일 양은 제 지시에 따라 행동했습니다."], ["There may have been a mistake, but it was not cruel.", "잘못된 판단이었을지는 몰라도 모진 일은 아니었습니다."], ["Your wife will be a different creature to-morrow, I trust.", "부인께서는 내일이면 딴사람이 되어 계실 겁니다, 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 제가 예상한 대로 경련이 왔지요."], ["She has had spasms, as I anticipated, though I did not tell Miss Hale of my apprehensions.", "제 우려를 헤일 양께 말씀드리지는 않았습니다만. 부인께서는 제가 가져온 아편을 드셨습니다."], ["She has taken the opiate I brought with me; she will have a good long sleep; and to-morrow that look which has alarmed you so much will have passed away.”", "오래 푹 주무실 겁니다. 그리고 내일이면 선생을 그토록 놀라게 한 그 얼굴빛도 가셔 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“But not the disease?”", "“하지만 병은 그대로겠지요?”"]], [["Dr. Donaldson glanced at Margaret.", "도널드슨 의사가 마거릿을 흘긋 보았다."], ["Her bent head, her face raised with no appeal for a temporary reprieve, showed that quick observer of human nature that she thought it better that the whole truth should be told.", "숙인 고개와, 잠시나마 유예를 구하는 기색 없이 들어 올린 얼굴은, 사람의 마음을 재빨리 읽는 그에게 그녀가 온전한 진실을 말하는 편이 낫다고 여긴다는 것을 알려 주었다."]], [["“Not the disease.", "“병은 그대로입니다."], ["We cannot touch the disease, with all our poor vaunted skill.", "우리의 보잘것없는 자랑거리인 온갖 의술로도 그 병에는 손을 댈 수 없습니다."], ["We can only delay its progress—alleviate the pain it causes.", "우리는 다만 그 진행을 늦추고 그것이 주는 고통을 덜어 드릴 수 있을 뿐입니다."], ["Be a man, sir—a Christian.", "사내답게 견디십시오, 선생."], ["Have faith in the immortality of the soul, which no pain, no mortal disease, can assail or touch!”", "그리스도인답게요. 어떤 고통도, 어떤 죽을병도 건드리거나 침범할 수 없는 영혼의 불멸을 믿으십시오!”"]], [["But all the reply he got was in the choked words, “You have never been married, Dr.", "그러나 그가 얻은 대답이라고는 목이 멘 몇 마디, “선생은 결혼해 본 적이 없지 않소, 도널드슨 선생."], ["Donaldson; you don’t know what it is,” and in the deep, manly sobs, which went through the stillness of the night like heavy pulses of agony.", "이게 어떤 것인지 모르시오”라는 말과, 밤의 정적을 무겁게 뛰는 고통의 맥박처럼 꿰뚫고 지나가는 깊고 사내다운 흐느낌뿐이었다."]], [["Margaret knelt by him, caressing him with tearful caresses.", "마거릿은 아버지 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 눈물 어린 손길로 그를 어루만졌다."], ["No one, not even Dr. Donaldson, knew how the time went by.", "아무도, 도널드슨 의사조차도 시간이 어떻게 흘렀는지 알지 못했다."], ["Mr. Hale was the first to dare to speak of the necessities of the present moment.", "지금 이 순간에 해야 할 일들을 감히 먼저 입에 올린 사람은 헤일 씨였다."]], [["“What must we do?”", "“우리가 어떻게 해야 하오?”"], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Tell us both.", "“우리 둘에게 다 말해 주시오."], ["Margaret is my staff—my right hand.”", "마거릿은 내 지팡이이자 오른손이니.”"]], [["Dr. Donaldson gave his clear, sensible directions.", "도널드슨 의사는 분명하고 사리에 맞는 지시를 내렸다."], ["No fear for to-night—nay, even peace for to-morrow, and for many days yet.", "오늘 밤은 걱정할 것 없다고 했다. 아니, 내일도, 그 뒤로도 여러 날은 평온하리라고 했다."], ["But no enduring hope of recovery. He advised Mr.", "그러나 회복에 대한 지속적인 희망은 없다고 했다."], ["Hale to go to bed, and leave only one to watch the slumber, which he hoped would be undisturbed.", "그는 헤일 씨에게 잠자리에 들라고 권했고, 방해받지 않기를 바라는 그 잠을 지킬 사람은 하나만 남기라고 했다."], ["He promised to come again early in the morning.", "그는 아침 일찍 다시 오겠다고 약속했다."], ["And, with a warm and kindly shake of the hand, he left them.", "그리고 따뜻하고 다정하게 손을 굳게 잡아 준 뒤 두 사람 곁을 떠났다."]], [["They spoke but few words; they were too much exhausted by their terror to do more than decide upon the immediate course of action.", "그들은 말을 거의 나누지 않았다. 공포에 너무 지친 나머지 당장 해야 할 일을 정하는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Hale was resolved to sit up through the night, and all that Margaret could do was to prevail upon him to rest on the drawing-room sofa.", "헤일 씨는 밤을 새우겠다고 마음먹었고, 마거릿이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 응접실 소파에서라도 쉬시라고 설득하는 것뿐이었다."], ["Dixon stoutly and bluntly refused to go to bed; and, as for Margaret, it was simply impossible that she should leave her mother, let all the doctors in the world speak of “husbanding resources,”", "딕슨은 잠자리에 들기를 완강하고 퉁명스럽게 거절했다. 마거릿으로 말하자면, 세상의 모든 의사가 “힘을 아껴 두라”느니 “지킬 사람은 하나면 된다”느니 말한들 어머니 곁을 떠난다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["and “one watcher only being required.” So, Dixon sat, and stared, and winked, and drooped, and picked herself up again with a jerk, and finally gave up the battle, and fairly snored.", "그리하여 딕슨은 앉아서 눈을 부릅뜨다가 껌뻑이다가 고개를 떨구다가 화들짝 다시 몸을 일으키기를 되풀이하더니, 마침내 싸움을 포기하고 아주 대놓고 코를 골았다."], ["Margaret had taken off her gown and tossed it aside with a sort of impatient disgust, and put on her dressing-gown.", "마거릿은 드레스를 벗어 성마른 역겨움 같은 것과 함께 옆으로 던져 놓고 실내복을 걸쳤다."], ["She felt as if she never could sleep again; as if her whole senses were acutely vital, and all endued with double keenness, for the purpose of watching.", "그녀는 다시는 잠들 수 없을 것 같았다. 온 감각이 날카롭게 살아나, 지켜본다는 그 목적 하나를 위해 하나같이 갑절의 예민함을 얻은 것 같았다."], ["Every sight and sound—nay, even every thought, touched some nerve to the very quick.", "보이는 것 들리는 것 하나하나가, 아니 떠오르는 생각 하나하나까지도 어떤 신경을 아프도록 건드렸다."], ["For more than two hours, she heard her father’s restless movements in the next room.", "2시간이 넘도록 그녀는 옆방에서 아버지가 뒤척이는 소리를 들었다."], ["He came perpetually to the door of her mother’s chamber, pausing there to listen, till she, not hearing his close unseen presence, went and opened it to tell him how all went on, in reply to the questions his baked lips could hardly form.", "그는 끊임없이 어머니 방문 앞으로 와서 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였고, 그러면 가까이 다가온 그 보이지 않는 기척을 미처 듣지 못한 그녀가 문을 열고 나가, 바싹 마른 입술로 겨우 만들어 내는 그의 물음에 지금 어떤지를 알려 주곤 했다."], ["At last he, too, fell asleep, and all the house was still.", "마침내 그도 잠이 들었고 온 집 안이 고요해졌다."], ["Margaret sat behind the curtain thinking.", "마거릿은 휘장 뒤에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Far away in time, far away in space, seemed all the interests of past days.", "지난 나날의 온갖 관심사가 시간으로도 아득히 멀고 공간으로도 아득히 멀게 느껴졌다. 불과 36시간 전만 해도 그녀는 베시 히긴스와 그 아버지를 마음에 두었고 바우처를 생각하며 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["Not more than thirty-six hours ago, she cared for Bessy Higgins and her father, and her heart was wrung for Boucher; now, that was all like a dreaming memory of some former life;—everything that had passed out of doors seemed dissevered from her mother, and therefore unreal.", "그런데 지금은 그 모든 것이 전생의 어떤 꿈결 같은 기억처럼 여겨졌다. 문밖에서 지나간 일은 무엇이든 어머니와 동떨어진 것이었고, 그래서 실감이 나지 않았다."], ["Even Harley Street appeared more distinct; there she remembered, as if it were yesterday, how she had pleased herself with tracing out her mother’s features in her Aunt Shaw’s face,—and how letters had come, making her dwell on the thoughts of home with all the longing of love.", "오히려 할리 가가 더 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 거기서 쇼 이모의 얼굴에 어린 어머니의 이목구비를 더듬어 내며 혼자 흐뭇해하던 일이, 편지가 와서 사랑의 온갖 그리움으로 집 생각에 잠기게 하던 일이 어제 일처럼 기억났다."], ["Helstone, itself, was in the dim past.", "헬스톤 자체는 어스름한 옛일이 되어 있었다."], ["The dull gray days of the preceding winter and spring, so uneventless and monotonous, seemed more associated with what she cared for now above all price.", "아무 일도 없이 단조롭기만 하던 지난겨울과 봄의 흐리고 잿빛인 나날이, 이제 그녀가 무엇보다 값지게 여기는 것과 더 가깝게 이어져 있는 듯했다."], ["She would fain have caught at the skirts of that departing time, and prayed it to return, and give her back what she had too little valued while it was yet in her possession.", "그녀는 저 떠나가는 시간의 옷자락이라도 붙들고, 돌아와 달라고, 아직 제 손안에 있을 때 너무도 하찮게 여겼던 것을 돌려 달라고 빌고 싶었다. 삶이란 얼마나 헛된 구경거리인가!"], ["What a vain show Life seemed!", "얼마나 실체 없고 깜박이고 스쳐 지나가는 것인가!"], ["How unsubstantial, and flickering, and flitting!", "마치 땅의 소란과 삐걱임보다 훨씬 높은 어느 하늘의 종탑에서 종이 끊임없이 울리는 것 같았다. “모두가 그림자다!"], ["It was as if from some aërial belfry, high up above the stir and jar of the earth, there was a bell continually tolling, “All are shadows!—all are passing!—all is past!”", "모두가 지나간다! 모든 것이 지나갔다!”"], ["And when the morning dawned, cool and gray, like many a happier morning before—when Margaret looked one by one at the sleepers, it seemed as if the terrible night were unreal as a dream; it, too, was a shadow.", "그리고 아침이 밝아 올 때, 그 전의 숱한 더 행복했던 아침처럼 서늘하고 잿빛으로 밝아 올 때, 마거릿이 잠든 이들을 하나하나 바라보았을 때, 그 끔찍한 밤도 꿈처럼 실체 없는 것으로 느껴졌다. 그것 또한 그림자였다."], ["It, too, was past.", "그것 또한 지나갔다."]], [["Mrs. Hale herself was not aware when she awoke, how ill she had been the night before.", "헤일 부인 자신은 잠에서 깨었을 때 지난밤 자기가 얼마나 위중했는지 알지 못했다."], ["She was rather surprised at Dr. Donaldson’s early visit, and perplexed by the anxious faces of husband and child.", "도널드슨 의사가 이른 아침에 찾아온 것을 좀 의아해했고, 남편과 딸의 근심 어린 얼굴에 어리둥절해했다."], ["She consented to remain in bed that day, saying she certainly was tired; but, the next, she insisted on getting up; and Dr.", "그녀는 확실히 피곤하다며 그날은 자리에 누워 있는 데 동의했다."], ["Donaldson gave his consent to her returning into the drawing-room.", "그러나 이튿날에는 기어이 일어나겠다고 우겼고, 도널드슨 의사도 응접실로 나오는 것을 허락했다."], ["She was restless and uncomfortable in every position, and before night she became very feverish. Mr.", "그녀는 어떤 자세로도 안절부절못하고 불편해했으며, 밤이 되기 전에 열이 몹시 올랐다."], ["Hale was utterly listless, and incapable of deciding on anything.", "헤일 씨는 완전히 넋이 나가 아무것도 결정하지 못했다."]], [["“What can we do to spare mamma such another night?”", "“어머니가 그런 밤을 또 겪지 않으시게 하려면 우리가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?”"], ["asked Margaret on the third day.", "사흘째 되는 날 마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“It is to a certain degree, the reaction after the powerful opiates I have been obliged to use.", "“어느 정도는 제가 어쩔 수 없이 써야 했던 강한 아편의 반작용입니다."], ["It is more painful for you to see than for her to bear, I believe.", "부인께서 견디시는 것보다 지켜보시는 쪽이 더 괴로우실 겁니다, 제 생각에는요."], ["But, I think, if we could get a water-bed it might be a good thing.", "다만 물침대를 구할 수 있다면 좋을 듯싶습니다. 물론 내일이면 나아지실 겁니다."], ["Not but what she will be better to-morrow; pretty much like herself as she was before this attack.", "이번 발작 전과 거의 같은 모습으로요. 그래도 물침대를 쓰시게 해 드리고 싶군요."], ["Still, I should like her to have a water-bed. Mrs.", "손턴 부인 댁에 하나 있는 걸로 압니다."], ["Thornton has one, I know.", "오늘 오후에 들러 보지요. 잠깐만요.”"], ["I’ll try and call there this afternoon. Stay,”", "그가 병실을 지키느라 핏기가 가신 마거릿의 얼굴에 눈길이 닿자 말했다."], ["said he, his eye catching on Margaret’s face, blanched with watching in a sick room, “I’m not sure whether I can go; I’ve a long round to take.", "“제가 갈 수 있을지 모르겠군요. 돌아야 할 길이 멉니다."], ["It would do you no harm to have a brisk walk to Marlborough Street, and ask Mrs. Thornton if she can spare it.”", "말버러 가까지 씩씩하게 걸어가서 손턴 부인께 빌려 주실 수 있는지 여쭤 보는 것도 헤일 양께 나쁠 것 없겠습니다.”"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론이지요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“I could go while mamma is asleep this afternoon.", "“오늘 오후에 어머니가 주무시는 동안 다녀올 수 있어요."], ["I’m sure Mrs. Thornton would lend it to us.”", "손턴 부인이라면 분명 빌려 주실 거예요.”"]], [["Dr.", "도널드슨 의사의 경험은 옳았다."], ["Donaldson’s experience told him rightly. Mrs.", "헤일 부인은 발작의 여파를 털어 낸 듯했고, 그날 오후에는 마거릿이 다시 보리라고 감히 바랐던 것보다 훨씬 밝고 좋아 보였다."], ["Hale seemed to shake off the consequences of her attack, and looked brighter and better this afternoon than Margaret had ever hoped to see her again.", "딸은 만찬을 마치고 어머니를 두고 나섰다."], ["Her daughter left her after dinner, sitting in her easy chair, with her hand lying in her husband’s, who looked more worn and suffering than she by far.", "어머니는 안락의자에 앉아 남편의 손에 제 손을 얹고 있었는데, 남편 쪽이 아내보다 훨씬 더 여위고 괴로워 보였다. 그래도 그는 이제 미소 지을 수 있었다."], ["Still, he could smile now—rather slowly, rather faintly, it is true; but a day or two before, Margaret never thought to see him smile again.", "느리게, 희미하게이긴 했지만 말이다. 하루 이틀 전만 해도 마거릿은 아버지가 다시 웃는 모습을 보리라고는 생각조차 못 했다."]], [["It was about two miles from their house in Crampton Crescent to Marlborough Street.", "크램프턴 크레센트의 집에서 말버러 가까지는 2마일쯤 되었다. 너무 더워서 빨리 걸을 수가 없었다."], ["It was too hot to walk very quickly.", "8월의 해가 오후 3시의 거리로 곧장 내리쬐었다."], ["An August sun beat straight down into the street at three o’clock in the afternoon.", "마거릿은 길의 첫 1.5마일 동안 여느 때와 크게 다른 것을 알아차리지 못한 채 걸어갔다."], ["Margaret went along, without noticing anything very different from usual in the first mile and a half of her journey; she was absorbed in her own thoughts, and had learnt by this time to thread her way through the irregular stream of human beings that flowed through Milton streets.", "그녀는 제 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 이제는 밀턴 거리를 흘러가는 그 고르지 않은 사람의 물결 사이로 길을 헤쳐 나가는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그러나 이윽고 그녀는 접어들고 있던 붐비는 길 위에서 사람들 무리가 여느 때와 다르게 술렁이는 것에 마음이 걸렸다."], ["But, by and by, she was struck with an unusual heaving among the mass of people in the crowded road on which she was entering.", "그들은 앞으로 나아간다기보다 저마다 서 있던 자리에서 별로 움직이지도 않은 채 말하고 듣고 흥분해서 웅성거리는 듯했다."], ["They did not appear to be moving on, so much as talking, and listening, and buzzing with excitement without much stirring from the spot where they might happen to be.", "그래도 사람들이 그녀에게 길을 내주었고, 심부름의 목적과 그것을 부른 절박한 사정에 마음을 온통 빼앗긴 터라 그녀는 마음이 편했을 때만큼 재빠르게 살피지 못했다."], ["Still, as they made way for her, and, wrapt up in the purpose of her errand, and the necessities that suggested it, she was less quick of observation than she might have been, if her mind had been at ease.", "사람들 사이에 불안하고 짓눌린 짜증의 기운이 감돌고 있다는 것, 자기 둘레에 물리적으로나 정신적으로나 천둥이 몰려오는 듯한 대기가 서려 있다는 것을 온전히 깨닫기 전에 그녀는 이미 말버러 가에 들어서 있었다."], ["She had got into Marlborough Street before the full conviction forced itself upon her, that there was a restless, oppressive sense of irritation abroad among the people; a thunderous atmosphere, morally as well as physically, around her.", "말버러 가로 트인 좁은 골목마다 성난 목소리가 수없이 뒤엉킨 듯한 낮고 먼 울림이 올라왔다. 초라하고 누추한 집마다 사람들이 문간과 창가에 모여 있었고, 아예 좁은 길 한복판에 나와 서 있는 이들도 있었다."], ["From every narrow lane opening out on Marlborough Street came up a low distant roar, as of myriads of fierce indignant voices.", "모두가 한 지점을 향해 골똘한 눈길을 던지고 있었다. 말버러 가야말로 갖가지 격렬한 관심을 드러내는 그 모든 사람의 눈이 모이는 초점이었다."], ["The inhabitants of each poor squalid dwelling were gathered round the doors and windows, if indeed they were not actually standing in the middle of the narrow ways—all with looks intent towards one point.", "어떤 눈은 분노로 사나웠고, 어떤 눈은 가차 없는 위협으로 험악했다. 어떤 눈은 두려움으로 커져 있었고, 어떤 눈은 애원하듯 빌고 있었다."], ["Marlborough Street itself was the focus of all those human eyes, that betrayed intensest interest of various kinds; some fierce with anger, some lowering with relentless threats!", "그리고 마거릿이 말버러 공장 마당의 크고 밋밋한 담에 난 접이문 옆 작은 쪽문에 이르러 종을 울리고 문지기의 응답을 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 둘레를 둘러보았고 폭풍의 첫 길고 아득한 우렛소리를 들었다."], ["some dilated with fear, or imploring entreaty; and as Margaret reached the small side-entrance by the folding doors, in the great dead wall of Marlborough mill-yard, and waited the porter’s answer to the bell, she looked round and heard the first long far-off roll of the tempest;—saw the first slow-surging wave of the dark crowd come, with its threatening crest, tumble over, and retreat, at the far end of the street, which a moment ago seemed so full of repressed noise, but which now was ominously still; all these circumstances forced themselves on Margaret’s notice, but did not sink down into her pre-occupied heart.", "어두운 군중의 첫 물결이 위협하는 물마루를 세운 채 느릿느릿 밀려와 무너지고 물러가는 것을, 조금 전만 해도 눌린 소음으로 가득했으나 이제는 불길하리만치 고요해진 길 저편 끝에서 보았다. 이 모든 정황이 마거릿의 눈에 억지로 밀고 들어왔으나, 다른 데 사로잡힌 그녀의 마음속까지 가라앉지는 못했다."], ["She did not know what they meant—what was their deep significance; while she did know, did feel the keen sharp pressure of the knife that was soon to stab her through and through by leaving her motherless.", "그녀는 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그 깊은 의미가 무엇인지 알지 못했다. 그러면서도 곧 자기를 어머니 없는 아이로 만들어 자기를 꿰뚫고 또 꿰뚫을 그 칼의 날카롭고 매서운 압박은 알고 있었고 느끼고 있었다."], ["She was trying to realise that, in order that, when it came, she might be ready to comfort her father.", "그녀는 그것을 실감하려 애쓰고 있었다. 그것이 닥쳤을 때 아버지를 위로할 준비가 되어 있으려고."]], [["The porter opened the door cautiously, not nearly wide enough to admit her.", "문지기가 그녀를 들이기에도 턱없이 좁게, 조심스레 문을 열었다."]], [["“It’s you, is it, ma’am?”", "“아씨셨구먼요, 그렇지요?”"], ["said he, drawing a long breath, and widening the entrance, but still not opening it fully.", "그는 길게 숨을 들이쉬며 입구를 조금 넓혔으나 여전히 활짝 열지는 않은 채 말했다."], ["Margaret went in.", "마거릿이 들어갔다."], ["He hastily bolted it behind her.", "그는 서둘러 뒤에서 빗장을 질렀다."]], [["“Th’ folk are all coming up here, I reckon?”", "“사람들이 죄다 이리로 몰려오고 있는 게지요, 아마?”"], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know.", "“모르겠어요."], ["Something unusual seemed going on; but this street is quite empty, I think.”", "무언가 심상치 않은 일이 벌어지는 것 같긴 했어요. 그렇지만 이 거리는 아주 텅 비어 있는데요.”"]], [["She went across the yard and put up the steps to the house door.", "그녀는 마당을 가로질러 집 문으로 오르는 계단을 올라갔다. 가까이에서는 아무 소리도 나지 않았다."], ["There was no near sound,—no steam engine at work with beat and pant,—no click of machinery, no mingling and clashing of many sharp voices; but far away, the ominous gathering roar, deep-clamouring.", "쿵쿵대고 헐떡이며 돌아가는 증기기관도, 기계가 딸깍이는 소리도, 날카로운 목소리들이 뒤섞여 부딪는 소리도 없었다. 다만 저 멀리서 불길하게 모여드는 함성이 깊게 아우성치고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII", "# 제22장"]], [["“But work grew scarce, while bread grew dear, And wages lessened, too; For Irish hordes were bidders here, Our half-paid work to do.”", "“그러나 일감은 귀해지고 빵값은 비싸졌으며, 품삯 또한 줄어들었다. 아일랜드 무리가 여기 와서 값을 불렀으니, 우리가 반값에 하던 일을 하겠다면서.”"], ["CORN LAW RHYMES.", "— 곡물법 시가."]], [["Margaret was shown into the drawing-room.", "마거릿은 응접실로 안내되었다."], ["It had returned into its normal state of bag and covering.", "방은 다시 덮개와 커버를 씌운 평소 모습으로 돌아가 있었다."], ["The windows were half open because of the heat, and the Venetian blinds covered the glass,—so that a gray grim light, reflected from the pavement below, threw all the shadows wrong, and combined with the green-tinged upper light to make even Margaret’s own face, as she caught it in the mirrors, look ghastly and wan.", "더위 때문에 창문은 반쯤 열려 있었고 베네치아식 블라인드가 유리창을 가리고 있어서, 아래쪽 포장도로에서 반사된 잿빛의 스산한 빛이 그림자란 그림자를 죄다 엉뚱한 자리에 드리웠고, 초록빛이 감도는 위쪽 빛과 어우러져 거울에 언뜻 비친 마거릿 자신의 얼굴마저 섬뜩하고 파리해 보이게 했다. 그녀는 앉아서 기다렸다."], ["She sat and waited; no one came.", "아무도 오지 않았다."], ["Every now and then, the wind seemed to bear the distant multitudinous sound nearer; and yet there was no wind!", "이따금 바람이 멀리서 웅성대는 무수한 소리를 더 가까이 실어 오는 듯했다. 그런데 바람은 불지 않았다!"], ["It died away into profound stillness between whiles.", "그 소리는 사이사이 잦아들어 깊은 정적이 되었다."]], [["Fanny came in at last.", "마침내 패니가 들어왔다."]], [["“Mamma will come directly, Miss Hale.", "“어머니가 곧 오실 거예요, 헤일 양. 사정이 이러하니 저더러 대신 사과를 드리라고 하셨어요."], ["She desired me to apologise to you as it is.", "아실지 모르겠지만 오빠가 아일랜드에서 직공들을 데려왔는데, 그것 때문에 밀턴 사람들이 아주 길길이 뛰고 있어요. 마치 오빠한테는 일손을 구할 수 있는 데서 구할 권리조차 없다는 듯이 말이에요."], ["Perhaps you know my brother has imported hands from Ireland, and it has irritated the Milton people excessively—as if he hadn’t a right to get labour where he could; and the stupid wretches here wouldn’t work for him; and now they’ve frightened these poor Irish starvelings so with their threats, that we daren’t let them out.", "여기 그 멍청한 것들은 오빠 밑에서 일하려 들지도 않았으면서요. 그러더니 이제는 그 딱한 아일랜드 굶주린 사람들을 어찌나 위협해서 겁을 주었는지, 우리가 그 사람들을 밖으로 내보내지도 못해요."], ["You may see them huddled in that top room in the mill,—and they’re to sleep there, to keep them safe from those brutes, who will neither work or let them work.", "공장 맨 위층 방에 다들 몰려 있는 게 보이실 거예요. 자기들은 일도 안 하면서 남이 일하는 것도 못 하게 하는 저 짐승 같은 것들한테서 지키려고 거기서 재울 참이에요."], ["And mamma is seeing about their food, and John is speaking to them, for some of the women are crying to go back.", "어머니는 그 사람들 먹을 것을 챙기고 계시고, 오빠는 그 사람들한테 이야기를 하고 있어요. 여자들 몇이 돌아가겠다고 울고 있거든요."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["here’s mamma!”", "어머니 오셨네요!”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton came in with a look of black sternness on her face, which made Margaret feel she had arrived at a bad time to trouble her with her request.", "손턴 부인은 시커멓게 굳은 얼굴로 들어왔고, 그 표정을 본 마거릿은 부탁을 꺼내기에는 좋지 않은 때에 왔구나 싶었다."], ["However, it was only in compliance with Mrs. Thornton’s expressed desire, that she would ask for whatever they might want in the progress of her mother’s illness.", "그러나 그것은 어머니의 병이 진행되는 동안 필요한 것이 있으면 무엇이든 청하라던 손턴 부인 자신의 뜻을 따른 것일 뿐이었다."], ["Mrs. Thornton’s brow contracted, and her mouth grew set, while Margaret spoke with gentle modesty of her mother’s restlessness, and Dr.", "마거릿이 어머니가 잠을 이루지 못한다는 것과, 물침대를 써서 편하게 해 주었으면 한다는 도널드슨 의사의 뜻을 부드럽고 조심스럽게 이야기하는 동안 손턴 부인의 이마는 찌푸려졌고 입은 꾹 다물렸다."], ["Donaldson’s wish that she should have the relief of a water-bed. She ceased.", "마거릿이 말을 마쳤다."], ["Mrs. Thornton did not reply immediately.", "손턴 부인은 곧바로 대답하지 않았다."], ["Then she started up and exclaimed—", "그러더니 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다."]], [["“They’re at the gates!", "“저것들이 문 앞에 왔다!"], ["Call John, Fanny,—call him in from the mill!", "존을 불러라, 패니. 공장에서 불러들여!"], ["They’re at the gates!", "문 앞에 왔다니까!"], ["They’ll batter them in!", "문을 부수고 들어올 게다!"], ["Call John, I say!”", "존을 부르래도!”"]], [["And simultaneously, the gathering tramp—to which she had been listening, instead of heeding Margaret’s words—was heard just right outside the wall, and an increasing din of angry voices raged behind the wooden barrier, which shook as if the unseen maddened crowd made battering rams of their bodies, and retreated a short space only to come with more united steady impetus against it, till their great beats made the strong gates quiver, like reeds before the wind.", "그와 동시에, 마거릿의 말을 귀담아듣는 대신 부인이 내내 듣고 있던 그 몰려드는 발소리가 바로 담장 밖에서 들려왔고, 성난 목소리들의 소란이 나무 문 뒤에서 점점 커졌다. 문은 보이지 않는 성난 무리가 제 몸뚱이를 공성 망치 삼아 부딪쳤다가 잠시 물러섰다가 더 한마음으로 꾸준히 힘을 모아 다시 달려드는 듯 흔들렸고, 그 거센 타격에 튼튼한 문이 바람 앞의 갈대처럼 떨었다."]], [["The women gathered round the windows, fascinated to look on the scene which terrified them. Mrs.", "여인들은 창가로 모여들어, 자기들을 겁에 질리게 하는 그 광경에 홀린 듯 눈을 떼지 못했다."], ["Thornton, the women-servants, Margaret,—all were there.", "손턴 부인, 하녀들, 마거릿, 모두가 거기 있었다."], ["Fanny had returned, screaming upstairs as if pursued at every step, and had thrown herself in hysterical sobbing on the sofa. Mrs.", "패니는 걸음마다 쫓기기라도 하듯 비명을 지르며 위층으로 돌아와 발작하듯 흐느끼며 소파에 몸을 던졌다."], ["Thornton watched for her son, who was still in the mill.", "손턴 부인은 아직 공장에 있는 아들을 지켜보았다."], ["He came out, looked up at them—the pale cluster of faces—and smiled good courage to them, before he locked the factory door.", "그가 밖으로 나와 창가에 모인 이들을, 그 창백한 얼굴 무리를 올려다보더니 기운을 내라는 듯 웃어 보이고는 공장 문을 잠갔다."], ["Then he called to one of the women to come down and undo his own door, which Fanny had fastened behind her in her mad flight.", "그러고는 여자들 가운데 하나를 불러 내려와서 제집 문을 열라고 했다. 패니가 정신없이 달아나며 뒤로 걸어 잠갔던 것이다."], ["Mrs. Thornton herself went.", "손턴 부인이 몸소 내려갔다."], ["And the sound of his well-known and commanding voice, seemed to have been like the taste of blood to the infuriated multitude outside.", "그런데 익히 알려진 그의 위엄 있는 목소리가 밖에 있는 광란한 무리에게는 피 맛이라도 본 것처럼 작용한 모양이었다."], ["Hitherto they had been voiceless, wordless, needing all their breath for their hard-laboured efforts to break down the gates.", "그때까지 그들은 소리도 말도 없이, 문을 부수느라 안간힘을 쓰는 데에 숨을 다 쏟고 있었다."], ["But now, hearing him speak inside, they set up such a fierce, unearthly groan, that even Mrs. Thornton was white with fear as she preceded him into the room.", "그러나 이제 그가 안에서 말하는 소리를 듣자 어찌나 사납고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 신음을 내질렀는지, 손턴 부인마저 아들을 앞세우고 방으로 들어올 때에는 두려움으로 하얗게 질려 있었다."], ["He came in a little flushed, but his eyes gleamed, as in answer to the trumpet-call of danger, and with a proud look of defiance on his face, that made him a noble, if not a handsome man.", "그는 얼굴이 조금 붉어진 채 들어왔으나 눈은 위험이라는 나팔 소리에 응답하듯 번득였고, 얼굴에는 당당한 반항의 빛이 서려서, 잘생긴 사내는 못 될지언정 기품 있는 사내로 보이게 했다."], ["Margaret had always dreaded lest her courage should fail her in any emergency, and she should be proved to be, what she dreaded lest she was—a coward.", "마거릿은 언제나 위급한 일이 닥칠 때 자신의 용기가 무너져, 스스로 두려워하던 그것, 곧 겁쟁이임이 드러날까 봐 겁내 왔다."], ["But now, in this real great time of reasonable fear and nearness of terror, she forgot herself, and felt only an intense sympathy—intense to painfulness—in the interests of the moment.", "그러나 지금, 두려워하는 것이 마땅하고 공포가 코앞에 닥친 이 진짜 큰일 앞에서 그녀는 자신을 잊었고, 오직 이 순간의 일들에 대한 강렬한, 아플 만큼 강렬한 공감만을 느꼈다."]], [["Mr. Thornton came frankly forwards:", "손턴 씨가 거리낌 없이 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“I’m sorry, Miss Hale, you have visited us at this unfortunate moment, when, I fear, you may be involved in whatever risk we have to bear.", "“헤일 양, 하필 이런 불행한 때에 저희를 찾아오시게 되어 죄송합니다. 저희가 감당해야 할 위험에 함께 휘말리시게 될까 걱정입니다."], ["Mother!", "어머니!"], ["hadn’t you better go into the back rooms?", "뒤쪽 방으로 가시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?"], ["I’m not sure whether they may not have made their way from Pinner’s Lane into the stable-yard; but if not, you will be safer there than here.", "저들이 피너 골목에서 마구간 마당으로 들어왔는지는 알 수 없습니다만, 아직 아니라면 여기보다 그쪽이 안전할 겁니다."], ["Go, Jane!”", "가게, 제인!”"], ["continued he, addressing the upper-servant.", "그는 우두머리 하녀에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["And she went, followed by the others.", "그녀가 나가자 다른 이들도 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“I stop here!”", "“나는 여기 있겠다!”"], ["said his mother.", "어머니가 말했다."], ["“Where you are, there I stay.”", "“네가 있는 곳에 나도 있는다.”"], ["And indeed, retreat into the back rooms was of no avail; the crowd had surrounded the outbuildings at the rear, and were sending forth their awful threatening roar behind.", "사실 뒤쪽 방으로 물러나 봐야 소용이 없었다. 무리는 뒤편의 별채까지 에워싸고 뒤에서도 무시무시한 위협의 함성을 내지르고 있었다."], ["The servants retreated into the garrets, with many a cry and shriek.", "하녀들은 울며 비명을 지르며 다락으로 달아났다."], ["Mr. Thornton smiled scornfully as he heard them.", "손턴 씨는 그 소리를 들으며 경멸하듯 웃었다."], ["He glanced at Margaret, standing all by herself at the window nearest the factory.", "그는 공장과 가장 가까운 창가에 홀로 서 있는 마거릿을 흘긋 보았다."], ["Her eyes glittered, her colour was deepened on cheek and lip.", "그녀의 눈은 반짝였고 뺨과 입술의 빛은 짙어져 있었다."], ["As if she felt his look, she turned to him and asked a question that had been for some time in her mind:", "그의 시선을 느끼기라도 한 듯 그녀는 그를 돌아보며 한동안 마음에 담아 두었던 것을 물었다."]], [["“Where are the poor imported workpeople?", "“그 딱한 외지 직공들은 어디 있나요?"], ["In the factory there?”", "저 공장 안에요?”"]], [["“Yes!", "“그렇습니다!"], ["I left them cowed up in a small room, at the head of a back flight of stairs; bidding them run all risks, and escape down there, if they heard any attack made on the mill doors.", "뒤쪽 계단 위의 작은 방에 잔뜩 웅크린 채로 두고 왔습니다. 공장 문을 부수는 소리가 들리면 무슨 위험을 무릅쓰고라도 그리로 달아나라고 일러 두었지요."], ["But it is not them—it is me they want.”", "그렇지만 저들이 노리는 건 그 사람들이 아니라 저입니다.”"]], [["“When can the soldiers be here?”", "“군대는 언제쯤 올 수 있느냐?”"], ["asked his mother, in a low but not unsteady voice.", "어머니가 낮지만 떨리지 않는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["He took out his watch with the same steady composure with which he did everything.", "그는 무슨 일이든 그렇게 하듯 한결같이 침착하게 시계를 꺼냈다."], ["He made some little calculation:—", "잠시 셈을 해 보았다."]], [["“Supposing Williams got straight off when I told him, and hadn’t to dodge about amongst them—it must be twenty minutes yet.”", "“윌리엄스가 제가 이르는 대로 곧장 떠났고, 저들 사이를 요리조리 피해 다닐 필요가 없었다면 아직 20분은 더 걸릴 겁니다.”"]], [["“Twenty minutes!”", "“20분이라니!”"], ["said his mother, for the first time showing her terror in the tones of her voice.", "어머니가 말했다. 처음으로 목소리에 두려움이 배어 나왔다."]], [["“Shut down the windows instantly, mother,”", "“어머니, 지금 당장 창문을 닫으세요.”"], ["exclaimed he: “the gates won’t bear such another shock.", "그가 외쳤다. “문은 저런 충격을 한 번 더 못 견딥니다."], ["Shut down that window, Miss Hale.”", "헤일 양, 그 창문을 닫으십시오.”"]], [["Margaret shut down her window, and then went to assist Mrs. Thornton’s trembling fingers.", "마거릿은 자기 쪽 창문을 닫고, 이어 떨리는 손가락으로 애를 먹는 손턴 부인을 도우러 갔다."]], [["From some cause or other, there was a pause of several minutes in the unseen street.", "무슨 까닭에서인지, 보이지 않는 거리에서는 몇 분 동안 소리가 멎었다."], ["Mrs. Thornton looked with wild anxiety at her son’s countenance, as if to gain the interpretation of the sudden stillness from him.", "손턴 부인은 갑작스러운 정적의 뜻을 아들에게서 읽어 내기라도 하려는 듯 미친 듯한 불안으로 그의 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["His face was set into rigid lines of contemptuous defiance; neither hope nor fear could be read there.", "그의 얼굴은 경멸 어린 반항의 딱딱한 선으로 굳어 있었다. 거기에서는 희망도 두려움도 읽을 수 없었다."]], [["Fanny raised herself up:", "패니가 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“Are they gone?”", "“다들 갔나요?”"], ["asked she, in a whisper.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“Gone!”", "“갔다고!”"], ["replied he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Listen!”", "“들어 봐라!”"]], [["She did listen; they all could hear the one great straining breath; the creak of wood slowly yielding; the wrench of iron; the mighty fall of the ponderous gates.", "그녀는 귀를 기울였다. 모두가 들을 수 있었다."], ["Fanny stood up tottering—made a step or two towards her mother, and fell forwards into her arms in a fainting fit. Mrs.", "크게 힘을 모으는 한 번의 숨소리, 나무가 천천히 무너지며 내는 삐걱거림, 쇠붙이가 비틀려 뜯기는 소리, 그리고 육중한 문이 우렁차게 쓰러지는 소리를."], ["Thornton lifted her up with a strength that was as much that of the will as of the body, and carried her away.", "패니는 비틀거리며 일어서서 어머니 쪽으로 한두 걸음 내딛다가 앞으로 고꾸라져 어머니 품에서 정신을 잃었다. 손턴 부인은 몸의 힘이라기보다 의지의 힘으로 딸을 안아 올려 데리고 나갔다."]], [["“Thank God!”", "“다행이군!”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, as he watched her out.", "손턴 씨가 어머니가 나가는 것을 지켜보며 말했다."], ["“Had you not better go upstairs, Miss Hale?”", "“헤일 양, 위층으로 올라가시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["Margaret’s lips formed a “No!”—but he could not hear her speak, for the tramp of innumerable steps right under the very wall of the house, and the fierce growl of low deep angry voices that had a ferocious murmur of satisfaction in them, more dreadful than their baffled cries not many minutes before.", "마거릿의 입술이 “아니요!”라는 모양을 지었으나 그는 그 말을 들을 수 없었다. 바로 집 담장 아래에서 수없이 많은 발이 짓밟는 소리와, 몇 분 전 헛물켜던 외침보다 더 무시무시한, 사나운 만족의 웅얼거림이 섞인 낮고 깊은 성난 목소리들의 으르렁거림이 들려왔기 때문이다."]], [["“Never mind!”", "“염려 마십시오!”"], ["said he, thinking to encourage her.", "그가 그녀를 북돋우려는 마음에 말했다. “이 소동에 휘말리시게 해서 정말 죄송합니다."], ["“I am very sorry that you should have been entrapped into all this alarm; but it cannot last long now; a few minutes more, and the soldiers will be here.”", "그러나 이제 오래가지 않을 겁니다. 몇 분만 더 있으면 군대가 도착합니다.”"]], [["“Oh, God!”", "“아, 하느님!”"], ["cried Margaret, suddenly; “there is Boucher.", "마거릿이 별안간 외쳤다. “저기 바우처가 있어요."], ["I know his face, though he is livid with rage,—he is fighting to get to the front—look!", "분노로 시퍼렇게 질려 있지만 저는 저 얼굴을 알아요. 앞으로 나가려고 몸싸움을 하고 있어요."], ["look!”", "보세요! 보세요!”"]], [["“Who is Boucher?” asked Mr.", "“바우처가 누구입니까?”"], ["Thornton coolly, and coming close to the window to discover the man in whom Margaret took such an interest. As soon as they saw Mr.", "손턴 씨가 차갑게 물으며, 마거릿이 그토록 마음을 쓰는 사내를 찾아보려고 창가로 바싹 다가섰다."], ["Thornton, they set up a yell, to call it not human is nothing,—it was as the demoniac desire of some terrible wild beast for the food that is withheld from his ravening.", "손턴 씨를 보자마자 그들은 괴성을 질렀는데, 사람의 소리가 아니라고 하는 것으로는 어림도 없었다. 그것은 굶주린 아가리에서 먹이를 빼앗긴 어떤 무서운 야수의 악마 같은 갈망이었다."], ["Even he drew back for a moment, dismayed at the intensity of hatred he had provoked.", "그마저도 자기가 불러일으킨 증오의 격렬함에 잠시 흠칫 물러섰다."]], [["“Let them yell!”", "“소리를 지르라지!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“In five minutes more—.", "“5분만 더 있으면."], ["I only hope my poor Irishmen are not terrified out of their wits by such a fiendlike noise.", "다만 저 딱한 아일랜드 사람들이 저 악귀 같은 소리에 넋이 나가지나 않았으면 좋겠군요."], ["Keep up your courage for five minutes, Miss Hale.”", "헤일 양, 5분만 용기를 내십시오.”"]], [["“Don’t be afraid for me,”", "“제 걱정은 마세요.”"], ["she said hastily.", "그녀가 다급히 말했다."], ["“But what in five minutes?", "“그런데 5분 뒤에 어떻게 되는 건가요?"], ["Can you do nothing to soothe these poor creatures?", "저 딱한 사람들을 달랠 방법이 정말 없나요?"], ["It is awful to see them.”", "보고 있기가 끔찍해요.”"]], [["“The soldiers will be here directly, and that will bring them to reason.”", "“곧 군대가 올 겁니다. 그러면 저들도 정신을 차리겠지요.”"]], [["“To reason!”", "“정신을 차린다고요!”"], ["said Margaret, quickly.", "마거릿이 재빨리 말했다."], ["“What kind of reason?”", "“어떤 식으로 정신을 차린다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“The only reason that does with men that make themselves into wild beasts.", "“스스로 들짐승이 된 자들에게 통하는 유일한 방식이지요."], ["By heaven!", "맙소사!"], ["they’ve turned to the mill-door!”", "공장 문 쪽으로 몰려갑니다!”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton,”", "“손턴 씨.”"], ["said Margaret, shaking all over with her passion, “go down this instant, if you are not a coward.", "마거릿이 격정으로 온몸을 떨며 말했다. “겁쟁이가 아니라면 지금 당장 내려가세요."], ["Go down and face them like a man.", "내려가서 사내답게 저 사람들과 마주 서세요."], ["Save these poor strangers, whom you have decoyed here.", "당신이 꾀어 온 저 딱한 외지 사람들을 구하세요."], ["Speak to your workmen as if they were human beings.", "당신의 직공들에게 사람을 대하듯 말을 거세요."], ["Speak to them kindly.", "다정하게 말을 거세요."], ["Don’t let the soldiers come in and cut down poor creatures who are driven mad.", "군대가 들어와 미쳐 버린 딱한 사람들을 베어 넘기게 하지 마세요."], ["I see one there who is.", "저기 정말로 미쳐 있는 사람이 하나 보여요."], ["If you have any courage or noble quality in you, go out and speak to them, man to man!”", "당신 안에 조금이라도 용기나 고결함이 있다면, 나가서 사람 대 사람으로 이야기하세요!”"]], [["He turned and looked at her while she spoke.", "그는 그녀가 말하는 동안 몸을 돌려 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["A dark cloud came over his face while he listened.", "듣는 사이 그의 얼굴에는 검은 구름이 드리웠다."], ["He set his teeth as he heard her words.", "그는 그녀의 말을 들으며 이를 악물었다."]], [["“I will go.", "“가겠습니다."], ["Perhaps I may ask you to accompany me downstairs, and bar the door behind me; my mother and sister will need that protection.”", "아래층까지 저와 함께 내려가셔서 제 뒤로 문을 잠가 주시기를 청해야 할지도 모르겠군요. 어머니와 누이에게는 그 정도 보호는 있어야 하니까요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["Mr.", "손턴 씨!"], ["Thornton!", "저는 모르겠어요."], ["I do not know—I may be wrong—only—”", "제가 틀렸을지도 몰라요. 다만……”"]], [["But he was gone; he was downstairs in the hall; he had unbarred the front door; all she could do, was to follow him quickly, and fasten it behind him, and clamber up the stairs again with a sick heart and a dizzy head.", "그러나 그는 이미 가고 없었다."], ["Again she took her place by the farthest window.", "아래층 현관에 있었고, 앞문의 빗장을 풀었다."], ["He was on the steps below; she saw that by the direction of a thousand angry eyes; but she could neither see nor hear anything save the savage satisfaction of the rolling angry murmur.", "그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 서둘러 뒤따라가 그의 뒤로 문을 걸어 잠그고, 메스꺼운 가슴과 어지러운 머리로 다시 계단을 기어오르는 것뿐이었다. 그녀는 다시 가장 먼 창가의 제자리로 갔다."], ["See threw the window wide open.", "그가 아래 계단에 서 있었다. 성난 눈 수천 개가 향한 방향으로 그것을 알 수 있었다."], ["Many in the crowd were mere boys; cruel and thoughtless,—cruel because they were thoughtless; some were men, gaunt as wolves, and mad for prey.", "그러나 굽이치는 성난 웅얼거림의 야만스러운 만족 말고는 아무것도 보이지도 들리지도 않았다. 그녀는 창문을 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["She knew how it was; they were like Boucher,—with starving children at home—relying on ultimate success in their efforts to get higher wages, and enraged beyond measure at discovering that Irishmen were to be brought in to rob their little ones of bread.", "무리 가운데 많은 수는 어린 사내아이들이었다. 잔인하고 생각이 없었으며, 생각이 없기에 잔인했다."], ["Margaret knew it all; she read it in Boucher’s face, forlornly desperate and livid with rage.", "더러는 이리처럼 여윈, 먹잇감에 미친 어른들이었다. 그녀는 사정을 알고 있었다."], ["If Mr.", "그들은 바우처와 같았다."], ["Thornton would but say something to them—let them hear his voice only—it seemed as if it would be better than this wild beating and raging against the stony silence that vouchsafed them no word, even of anger or reproach.", "집에는 굶주린 아이들이 있고, 품삯을 올리려는 노력이 끝내 성공하리라 믿었으며, 제 아이들의 빵을 빼앗을 아일랜드 사람들이 들어온다는 것을 알고는 이루 말할 수 없이 격분한 것이다. 마거릿은 그 모든 것을 알았다."], ["But perhaps he was speaking now; there was a momentary hush of their noise, inarticulate as that of a troop of animals.", "그녀는 그것을 바우처의 얼굴에서, 처량하리만치 절박하고 분노로 시퍼렇게 질린 그 얼굴에서 읽었다. 손턴 씨가 저들에게 한마디만 해 준다면, 그의 목소리만이라도 듣게 해 준다면, 노여움이나 꾸짖음의 말조차 한마디 내주지 않는 저 돌 같은 침묵을 향해 미친 듯 두드리고 날뛰는 것보다는 나을 성싶었다."], ["She tore her bonnet off, and bent forward to hear.", "그러나 어쩌면 그가 지금 말하고 있는지도 몰랐다. 짐승 떼의 소리처럼 알아들을 수 없던 그 소음이 잠시 잦아들었기 때문이다."], ["She could only see; for if Mr.", "그녀는 보닛을 벗어 던지고 몸을 앞으로 내밀어 들으려 했다."], ["Thornton had indeed made the attempt to speak, the momentary instinct to listen to him was past and gone, and the people were raging worse than ever.", "그러나 보이기만 할 뿐이었다. 손턴 씨가 정말로 말을 해 보려 했다 하더라도, 그의 말을 들으려던 순간의 본능은 이미 지나가 버렸고 사람들은 어느 때보다 사납게 날뛰고 있었으니까."], ["He stood with his arms folded; still as a statue; his face pale with repressed excitement.", "그는 팔짱을 낀 채 조각상처럼 미동도 없이 서 있었고, 억눌린 흥분으로 얼굴이 창백했다."], ["They were trying to intimidate him—to make him flinch; each was urging the other on to some immediate act of personal violence.", "그들은 그를 위협해서 움츠러들게 만들려 했다. 저마다 서로를 부추겨 당장 손찌검이라도 하게 하려 들었다."], ["Margaret felt intuitively, that in an instant all would be uproar; the first touch would cause an explosion, in which, among such hundreds of infuriated men and reckless boys, even Mr.", "마거릿은 직감으로 알았다. 한순간이면 모든 것이 아수라장이 되리라는 것을, 첫 손길이 폭발을 일으키리라는 것을, 그렇게 격분한 사내 수백 명과 앞뒤 가리지 않는 아이들 틈에서는 손턴 씨의 목숨조차 위태로우리라는 것을, 다음 순간이면 폭풍 같은 격정이 한계를 넘어 이성이나 뒷일에 대한 두려움이라는 둑을 모조리 쓸어 가리라는 것을."], ["Thornton’s life would be unsafe,—that in another instant the stormy passions would have passed their bounds, and swept away all barriers of reason, or apprehension of consequence.", "그렇게 바라보는 사이에도 그녀는 뒤쪽에서 사내아이들이 몸을 굽혀 무거운 나막신을 벗는 것을 보았다. 손에 잡히는 가장 만만한 무기였다."], ["Even while she looked, she saw lads in the background stooping to take off their heavy wooden clogs—the readiest missile they could find; she saw it was the spark to the gunpowder, and, with a cry, which no one heard, she rushed out of the room, down stairs,—she had lifted the great iron bar of the door with an imperious force—had thrown the door open wide—and was there, in face of that angry sea of men, her eyes smiting them with flaming arrows of reproach.", "그녀는 그것이 화약에 붙은 불꽃임을 알아차렸고, 아무도 듣지 못한 외마디 소리와 함께 방을 뛰쳐나가 계단을 내려갔다. 그녀는 위압적인 힘으로 문의 커다란 쇠 빗장을 들어 올렸고, 문을 활짝 열어젖혔고, 성난 사내들의 바다와 마주 서서 꾸짖음의 불화살 같은 눈길로 그들을 쳤다."], ["The clogs were arrested in the hands that held them—the countenances, so fell not a moment before, now looked irresolute, and as if asking what this meant.", "나막신은 그것을 쥔 손안에 멈추었고, 조금 전까지 그토록 험악하던 얼굴들이 이제는 머뭇거리며 이게 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 듯했다."], ["For she stood between them and their enemy.", "그녀가 그들과 그들의 적 사이에 서 있었기 때문이다."], ["She could not speak, but held out her arms towards them till she could recover breath.", "그녀는 말을 할 수 없었으나, 숨을 고를 수 있을 때까지 그들을 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, do not use violence!", "“아, 폭력을 쓰지 마세요! 저분은 한 사람이고 여러분은 여럿이잖아요.”"], ["He is one man, and you are many”; but her words died away, for there was no tone in her voice; it was but a hoarse whisper.", "그러나 그 말은 사그라졌다. 목소리에 힘이 실리지 않아 쉰 속삭임에 지나지 않았던 것이다."], ["Mr.", "손턴 씨는 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서 있었다."], ["Thornton stood a little on one side; he had moved away from behind her, as if jealous of anything that should come between him and danger.", "그는 자기와 위험 사이에 무엇이 끼어드는 것이 못마땅하다는 듯 그녀 뒤에서 물러나 있었다."]], [["“Go!”", "“가세요!”"], ["said she, once more (and now her voice was like a cry).", "그녀가 다시 한번 말했다. 이제 그 목소리는 부르짖음에 가까웠다."], ["“The soldiers are sent for—are coming.", "“군대를 부르러 보냈어요. 오고 있어요."], ["Go peaceably.", "순순히 가세요."], ["Go away.", "돌아가세요."], ["You shall have relief from your complaints, whatever they are.”", "여러분의 하소연이 무엇이든 풀릴 겁니다.”"]], [["“Shall them Irish blackguards be packed back again?”", "“그 아일랜드 놈들을 도로 짐짝처럼 싸서 돌려보낼 테요?”"], ["asked one from out the crowd, with fierce threatening in his voice.", "무리 가운데서 하나가 목소리에 사나운 위협을 담아 물었다."]], [["“Never, for your bidding!”", "“결코 못 한다, 너희가 시킨다고 해서는!”"], ["exclaimed Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 외쳤다."], ["And instantly the storm broke.", "그러자 곧바로 폭풍이 터졌다."], ["The hootings rose and filled the air,—but Margaret did not hear them.", "야유가 솟구쳐 허공을 채웠으나 마거릿에게는 들리지 않았다."], ["Her eye was on the group of lads who had armed themselves with their clogs some time before.", "그녀의 눈은 아까부터 나막신으로 무장한 사내아이들 무리에 가 있었다."], ["She saw their gesture—she knew its meaning—she read their aim. Another moment, and Mr.", "그녀는 그들의 몸짓을 보았고, 그 뜻을 알았고, 그들의 겨냥을 읽었다."], ["Thornton might be smitten down,—he whom she had urged and goaded to come to this perilous place.", "한순간만 지나면 손턴 씨가 얻어맞고 쓰러질지도 몰랐다. 그녀 자신이 몰아세우고 부추겨 이 위험한 자리로 나오게 한 사람이었다."], ["She only thought how she could save him.", "그녀는 오직 어떻게 그를 구할까만 생각했다."], ["She threw her arms around him; she made her body into a shield from the fierce people beyond.", "그녀는 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안았다. 저 너머의 사나운 사람들로부터 그를 막는 방패로 제 몸을 삼았다."], ["Still, with his arms folded, he shook her off.", "그러나 그는 여전히 팔짱을 낀 채 그녀를 뿌리쳤다."]], [["“Go away,”", "“비키십시오.”"], ["said he, in his deep voice.", "그가 깊은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“This is no place for you.”", "“여기는 당신이 있을 자리가 아닙니다.”"]], [["“It is,” said she.", "“여기가 제 자리예요.”"], ["“You did not see what I saw.”", "그녀가 말했다. “당신은 제가 본 것을 보지 못했어요.”"], ["If she thought her sex would be a protection,—if, with shrinking eyes she had turned away from the terrible anger of these men, in any hope that ere she looked again they would have paused and reflected, and slunk away, and vanished,—she was wrong.", "여자라는 것이 방패가 되어 주리라고 그녀가 생각했다면, 움츠러든 눈으로 저 사내들의 무서운 분노에서 고개를 돌린 채 다시 눈을 들었을 때에는 그들이 멈춰 서서 돌이켜 보고 슬그머니 물러나 사라져 있으리라 조금이라도 바랐다면, 그것은 잘못이었다. 그들의 무모한 격정은 이미 멈출 수 없을 만큼 멀리 그들을 데려갔다."], ["Their reckless passion had carried them too far to stop—at least had carried some of them too far; for it is always the savage lads, with their love of cruel excitement, who head the riot—reckless to what bloodshed it may lead.", "적어도 그중 몇몇은 그러했다. 잔인한 흥분을 좋아하는 사나운 아이들이야말로 어떤 유혈로 이어지든 아랑곳없이 언제나 폭동의 앞장을 서는 법이니까."], ["A clog whizzed through the air.", "나막신 하나가 허공을 가르며 날았다."], ["Margaret’s fascinated eyes watched its progress; it missed its aim, and she turned sick with affright, but changed not her position, only hid her face on Mr.", "마거릿의 눈은 홀린 듯 그것의 궤적을 좇았다. 그것은 빗나갔고, 그녀는 두려움에 속이 뒤집혔으나 자리를 옮기지는 않고 다만 손턴 씨의 팔에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["Thornton’s arm. Then she turned and spoke again:", "그러고는 몸을 돌려 다시 말했다."]], [["“For God’s sake!", "“제발!"], ["do not damage your cause by this violence.", "이런 폭력으로 여러분의 명분을 망치지 마세요."], ["You do not know what you are doing.”", "여러분은 지금 자기가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 모르고 있어요.”"], ["She strove to make her words distinct.", "그녀는 말을 또렷하게 하려고 애썼다."]], [["A sharp pebble flew by her, grazing forehead and cheek, and drawing a blinding sheet of light before her eyes.", "날카로운 조약돌 하나가 그녀의 곁을 스치며 이마와 뺨을 긁었고, 눈앞에 눈부신 빛의 장막을 드리웠다."], ["She lay like one dead on Mr. Thornton’s shoulder.", "그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 손턴 씨의 어깨에 늘어졌다."], ["Then he unfolded his arms, and held her encircled in one for an instant:", "그러자 그는 팔짱을 풀고 한쪽 팔로 그녀를 잠시 감싸 안았다."]], [["“You do well!” said he.", "“잘들 하는 짓이다!”"], ["“You come to oust the innocent stranger.", "그가 말했다."], ["You fall—you hundreds—on one man; and when a woman comes before you, to ask you for your own sakes to be reasonable creatures, your cowardly wrath falls upon her!", "“죄 없는 타지 사람을 쫓아내겠다고 몰려오고, 수백 명이 한 사람에게 달려들고, 그러다 여자 하나가 너희 자신을 위해서 사리를 아는 인간이 되어 달라고 앞에 나서니, 그 비겁한 분풀이를 그 여자에게 퍼붓는구나!"], ["You do well!”", "잘들 하는 짓이다!”"], ["They were silent while he spoke.", "그가 말하는 동안 그들은 잠잠했다."], ["They were watching, open-eyed and open-mouthed, the thread of dark-red blood which wakened them up from their trance of passion.", "그들은 눈과 입을 벌린 채, 격정의 몽롱함에서 그들을 깨워 놓은 검붉은 핏줄기를 바라보고 있었다. 문에 가까이 있던 자들은 부끄러워 슬그머니 빠져나갔다."], ["Those nearest the gate stole out ashamed; there was a movement through all the crowd—a retreating movement.", "무리 전체에 움직임이 일었다. 물러나는 움직임이었다."], ["Only one voice called out:", "오직 한 목소리만이 외쳤다."]], [["“Th’ stone was meant for thee; but thou wert sheltered behind a woman!”", "“그 돌은 댁을 맞히려던 거였어. 그런데 댁은 계집 뒤에 숨었구먼!”"]], [["Mr. Thornton quivered with rage.", "손턴 씨는 분노로 부르르 떨었다."], ["The blood-flowing had made Margaret conscious—dimly, vaguely conscious.", "피가 흐른 덕분에 마거릿은 정신이 들었다. 어렴풋하고 아득하게나마 정신이 들었다."], ["He placed her gently on the door-step, her head leaning against the frame.", "그는 그녀를 문간 계단에 조심스레 앉히고 머리를 문틀에 기대게 했다."]], [["“Can you rest there?” he asked.", "“여기서 좀 쉬실 수 있겠습니까?”"], ["But without waiting for her answer, he went slowly down the steps right into the middle of the crowd.", "그가 물었다. 그러나 대답을 기다리지도 않고 그는 천천히 계단을 내려가 무리 한가운데로 들어갔다."], ["“Now kill me, if it is your brutal will.", "“자, 짐승 같은 마음이 그렇다면 나를 죽여라."], ["There is no woman to shield me here.", "여기에는 나를 막아 줄 여자가 없다."], ["You may beat me to death—you will never move me from what I have determined upon—not you!”", "나를 때려죽일 수는 있어도, 내가 마음먹은 바에서 나를 물러나게 하지는 못한다."], ["He stood amongst them with his arms folded, in precisely the same attitude as he had been in on the steps.", "너희 따위는!” 그는 계단 위에서 그랬던 것과 똑같은 자세로 팔짱을 낀 채 그들 가운데 서 있었다."]], [["But the retrograde movement towards the gate had begun—as unreasoningly, perhaps as blindly, as the simultaneous anger.", "그러나 문 쪽으로 물러나는 움직임은 이미 시작되어 있었다. 아마도 한꺼번에 타올랐던 분노만큼이나 까닭 없이, 눈먼 것처럼."], ["Or, perhaps, the idea of the approach of the soldiers, and the sight of that pale, upturned face, with closed eyes, still and sad as marble, though the tears welled out of the long entanglement of eyelashes, and dropped down; and, heavier, slower plash than even tears, came the drip of blood from her wound.", "아니, 어쩌면 군대가 다가온다는 생각과, 눈을 감은 채 대리석처럼 고요하고 슬프게 위를 향한 그 창백한 얼굴 때문인지도 몰랐다. 길게 엉킨 속눈썹 사이로 눈물이 배어 나와 떨어졌고, 눈물보다 더 무겁고 느린 소리로 상처에서 피가 방울져 떨어지고 있었다."], ["Even the most desperate—Boucher himself—drew back, faltered away, scowled, and finally went off, muttering curses on the master, who stood in his unchanging attitude, looking after their retreat with defiant eyes.", "가장 절박한 자들마저, 바우처 자신마저 뒷걸음질 치고 비틀거리며 물러나 인상을 찌푸리다가 끝내 공장주에게 저주를 퍼부으며 떠나갔다. 그는 조금도 변하지 않은 자세로 서서 반항하는 눈으로 그들이 물러나는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["The moment that retreat had changed into a flight (as it was sure from its very character to do), he darted up the steps to Margaret.", "그 후퇴가 (그 성질상 그렇게 되기 마련이었듯) 달아남으로 바뀌는 순간, 그는 계단 위 마거릿에게로 달려 올라갔다."]], [["She tried to rise without his help.", "그녀는 그의 도움 없이 일어나려 했다."]], [["“It is nothing,”", "“아무것도 아니에요.”"], ["she said, with a sickly smile.", "그녀가 힘없는 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“The skin is grazed, and I was stunned at the moment.", "“살갗이 좀 벗겨졌을 뿐이고, 그 순간 잠깐 정신이 아득했던 거예요."], ["Oh, I am so thankful they are gone!”", "아, 저 사람들이 갔다니 정말 다행이에요!”"], ["And she cried without restraint.", "그러고는 참지 못하고 울었다."]], [["He could not sympathise with her.", "그는 그녀와 같은 마음이 될 수 없었다."], ["His anger had not abated; it was rising the more as his sense of immediate danger was passing away.", "그의 노여움은 가라앉지 않았다. 눈앞의 위험이 지나가면서 오히려 치밀어 오르고 있었다."], ["The distant clank of the soldiers was heard: just five minutes too late to make this vanished mob feel the power of authority and order.", "멀리서 군인들의 쇠붙이 부딪는 소리가 들려왔다. 이 흩어져 버린 무리에게 권위와 질서의 힘을 느끼게 하기에는 딱 5분이 늦었다."], ["He hoped they would see the troops, and be quelled by the thought of their narrow escape.", "그는 그들이 군대를 보고, 자기들이 아슬아슬하게 화를 면했음을 깨닫고 기가 꺾이기를 바랐다."], ["While these thoughts crossed his mind, Margaret clung to the doorpost to steady herself: but a film came over her eyes—he was only just in time to catch her.", "이런 생각들이 머릿속을 스치는 사이, 마거릿은 몸을 가누려고 문설주를 붙잡았다. 그러나 눈앞에 막이 드리웠고, 그는 간신히 때맞춰 그녀를 받아 안았다."], ["“Mother—mother!”", "“어머니, 어머니!” 그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he; “Come down—they are gone, and Miss Hale is hurt!”", "“내려오세요. 저들은 갔고, 헤일 양이 다쳤습니다!”"], ["He bore her into the dining-room, and laid her on the sofa there; laid her down softly, and looking on her pure white face, the sense of what she was to him came upon him so keenly that he spoke it out in his pain:", "그는 그녀를 안아 식당으로 옮겨 소파에 눕혔다. 부드럽게 눕히고 나서 그 새하얀 얼굴을 내려다보는데, 그녀가 자기에게 어떤 존재인지가 어찌나 사무치게 밀려오던지 그는 그 아픔을 소리 내어 말하고 말았다."]], [["“Oh, my Margaret—my Margaret!", "“아, 나의 마거릿, 나의 마거릿!"], ["no one can tell what you are to me!", "당신이 나에게 어떤 사람인지는 아무도 모릅니다!"], ["Dead—cold as you lie there, you are the only woman I ever loved!", "죽은 듯 차갑게 여기 누워 있는 당신은, 내가 사랑한 단 하나의 여인입니다!"], ["Oh, Margaret—Margaret!”", "아, 마거릿, 마거릿!”"]], [["Inarticulately as he spoke, kneeling by her, and rather moaning than saying the words, he started up, ashamed of himself, as his mother came in.", "그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇은 채 말이라기보다 신음에 가까운 소리로 그렇게 더듬거리다가, 어머니가 들어오자 그는 스스로가 부끄러워 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["She saw nothing, but her son a little paler, a little sterner than usual.", "어머니는 아무것도 보지 못했고, 다만 아들이 평소보다 조금 창백하고 조금 굳어 있다는 것만 보았다."]], [["“Miss Hale is hurt, mother.", "“헤일 양이 다쳤습니다, 어머니."], ["A stone has grazed her temple.", "돌에 관자놀이를 스쳤어요."], ["She has lost a good deal of blood, I’m afraid.”", "피를 꽤 많이 흘린 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“She looks very seriously hurt,—I could almost fancy her dead,”", "“몹시 심하게 다친 것 같구나. 거의 죽은 사람처럼 보이는구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton, a good deal alarmed.", "손턴 부인이 적잖이 놀라서 말했다."]], [["“It is only a fainting-fit.", "“기절한 것뿐입니다."], ["She has spoken to me since.”", "그 뒤로 저와 말도 했는걸요.”"], ["But all the blood in his body seemed to rush inwards to his heart as he spoke, and he absolutely trembled.", "그러나 그렇게 말하는 동안 몸속의 피가 모조리 심장으로 몰려드는 것 같았고, 그는 정말로 떨고 있었다."]], [["“Go and call Jane,—she can find me the things I want; and do you go to your Irish people, who are crying and shouting as if they were mad with fright.”", "“가서 제인을 불러라. 내게 필요한 것들을 찾아다 줄 테니. 그리고 너는 아일랜드 사람들한테 가 보아라. 겁에 질려 미친 것처럼 울고 소리치고 있잖니.”"]], [["He went.", "그는 갔다."], ["He went away as if weights were tied to every limb that bore him from her.", "그녀에게서 자기를 떼어 놓는 팔다리마다 추가 매달린 것처럼 무겁게 갔다."], ["He called Jane; he called his sister.", "그는 제인을 불렀고 누이를 불렀다."], ["She should have all womanly care, all gentle tendance.", "그녀는 여자의 손으로 하는 온갖 보살핌과 다정한 시중을 받아야 마땅했다."], ["But every pulse beat in him as he remembered how she had come down and placed herself in foremost danger—could it be to save him?", "그러나 그녀가 내려와 가장 앞선 위험 속에 제 몸을 세우던 일을 떠올리자 온몸의 맥박이 뛰었다. 그것이 자기를 구하려는 것이었을까?"], ["At the time, he had pushed her aside, and spoken gruffly; he had seen nothing but the unnecessary danger she had placed herself in.", "그때 그는 그녀를 밀쳐 내고 퉁명스럽게 말했다. 그녀가 공연히 자초한 위험밖에는 아무것도 보이지 않았던 것이다."], ["He went to his Irish people, with every nerve in his body thrilling at the thought of her, and found it difficult to understand enough of what they were saying to soothe and comfort away their fears.", "그는 그녀 생각에 온몸의 신경이 떨리는 채로 아일랜드 사람들에게 갔고, 그들의 두려움을 달래고 어루만져 줄 만큼 그들이 하는 말을 알아듣기가 어렵다는 것을 알았다."], ["There, they declared, they would not stop; they claimed to be sent back.", "그들은 여기 있지 않겠다고, 돌려보내 달라고 우겼다."]], [["And so he had to think, and talk, and reason.", "그리하여 그는 생각하고, 말하고, 설득해야 했다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton bathed Margaret’s temples with eau de Cologne.", "손턴 부인은 오드콜로뉴로 마거릿의 관자놀이를 적셨다."], ["As the spirit touched the wound, which till then neither Mrs.", "그때까지 손턴 부인도 제인도 알아채지 못했던 상처에 알코올이 닿자 마거릿은 눈을 떴다."], ["Thornton nor Jane had perceived, Margaret opened her eyes; but it was evident she did not know where she was, nor who they were.", "그러나 자기가 어디 있는지도, 저들이 누구인지도 모르는 것이 분명했다."], ["The dark circles deepened, the lips quivered and contracted, and she became insensible once more.", "눈가의 검은 그늘이 짙어지고 입술이 떨리며 오므라들더니, 그녀는 다시 정신을 잃었다."]], [["“She has had a terrible blow,”", "“심하게 얻어맞았어.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton.", "손턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“Is there any one who will go for a doctor?”", "“의사를 부르러 갈 사람이 없느냐?”"]], [["“Not me, ma’am, if you please,”", "“저는 아닙니다, 마님. 부디 봐주세요.”"], ["said Jane, shrinking back.", "제인이 뒷걸음질 치며 말했다."], ["“Them rabble may be all about; I don’t think the cut is so deep, ma’am, as it looks.”", "“그 폭도들이 아직 사방에 있을지도 몰라요. 그리고 상처가 보기만큼 깊지는 않은 것 같습니다요, 마님.”"]], [["“I will not run the chance.", "“나는 요행을 바라지 않겠다."], ["She was hurt in our house.", "저 아가씨는 우리 집에서 다쳤어."], ["If you are a coward, Jane, I am not.", "네가 겁쟁이라면 나는 아니다."], ["I will go.”", "내가 가마.”"]], [["“Pray, ma’am, let me send one of the police.", "“마님, 제발 순경을 하나 보내게 해 주세요."], ["There’s ever so many come up, and soldiers too.”", "잔뜩 올라와 있고 군인들도 있습니다요.”"]], [["“And yet you’re afraid to go!", "“그러면서도 나가기는 무섭다는 거냐!"], ["I will not have their time taken up with our errands.", "그 사람들 시간을 우리 심부름에 쓰게 할 수는 없다."], ["They’ll have enough to do to catch some of the mob.", "폭도 몇이라도 잡으려면 그들도 할 일이 많을 테니."], ["You will not be afraid to stop in this house,”", "이 집에 남아 있는 건 무섭지 않겠지.”"], ["she asked contemptuously, “and go on bathing Miss Hale’s forehead, shall you?", "부인이 경멸하듯 물었다. “남아서 헤일 양 이마를 계속 적셔 주는 건 할 수 있겠지?"], ["I shall not be ten minutes away.”", "나는 10분도 안 걸려 돌아올 게다.”"]], [["“Couldn’t Hannah go, ma’am?”", "“해나가 가면 안 될까요, 마님?”"]], [["“Why Hannah?", "“왜 해나냐?"], ["Why any but you?", "왜 너만 빼고 다른 사람이냐?"], ["No, Jane, if you don’t go, I do.”", "안 된다, 제인. 네가 안 가면 내가 간다.”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton went first to the room in which she had left Fanny stretched on the bed.", "손턴 부인은 먼저 패니를 침대에 눕혀 두고 나왔던 방으로 갔다."], ["She started up as her mother entered.", "어머니가 들어서자 패니가 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Oh, mamma, how you terrified me!", "“어머, 어머니, 얼마나 놀랐는지 몰라요!"], ["I thought you were a man that had got into the house.”", "집 안에 웬 남자가 들어온 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“Nonsense!", "“쓸데없는 소리!"], ["The men are all gone away.", "사내들은 다 갔다."], ["There are soldiers all round the place, seeking for their work now it is too late.", "이제 와서야 할 일을 찾겠다고 군인들이 집 둘레에 온통 깔려 있고."], ["Miss Hale is lying on the dining-room sofa badly hurt.", "헤일 양이 식당 소파에 심하게 다쳐 누워 있다."], ["I am going for the doctor.”", "나는 의사를 부르러 간다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["don’t, mamma!", "가지 마세요, 어머니!"], ["they’ll murder you.”", "그자들이 어머니를 죽일 거예요.”"], ["She clung to her mother’s gown.", "그녀가 어머니의 옷자락에 매달렸다."], ["Mrs. Thornton wrenched it away with no gentle hand.", "손턴 부인은 사정없는 손길로 그것을 뿌리쳤다."]], [["“Find me some one else to go; but that girl must not bleed to death.”", "“대신 갈 사람을 찾아 오너라. 그러나 저 아가씨를 피 흘려 죽게 둘 수는 없다.”"]], [["“Bleed!", "“피라니!"], ["oh, how horrid!", "아, 끔찍해라!"], ["How has she got hurt?”", "어쩌다 다쳤어요?”"]], [["“I don’t know,—I have no time to ask.", "“모른다. 물어볼 겨를도 없었다."], ["Go down to her, Fanny, and do try to make yourself of use.", "패니, 내려가서 조금이라도 쓸모 있게 굴어 보아라."], ["Jane is with her; and I trust it looks worse than it is.", "제인이 곁에 있다. 보기보다는 덜한 것이기를 바랄 뿐이다."], ["Jane has refused to leave the house, cowardly woman!", "제인은 집 밖으로 나가기를 마다했어, 겁 많은 것!"], ["And I won’t put myself in the way of any more refusals from my servants, so I am going myself.”", "하인들한테서 더는 거절당하고 싶지 않으니 내가 직접 가는 게다.”"]], [["“Oh, dear, dear!”", "“어머, 어쩌면 좋아!”"], ["said Fanny, crying, and preparing to go down rather than be left alone, with the thought of wounds and bloodshed in the very house.", "패니가 울면서, 상처와 유혈이 바로 이 집 안에 있다는 생각에 혼자 남아 있느니 차라리 내려가겠다며 채비를 했다."]], [["“Oh, Jane!”", "“어머나, 제인!”"], ["said she, creeping into the dining-room, “What is the matter?", "그녀가 식당으로 슬그머니 들어서며 말했다. “어떻게 된 거야?"], ["How white she looks!", "얼굴이 왜 이렇게 하얗지!"], ["How did she get hurt?", "어쩌다 다친 거야?"], ["Did they throw stones into the drawing-room?”", "응접실로 돌을 던져 넣기라도 했어?”"]], [["Margaret did indeed look white and wan, although her senses were beginning to return to her.", "마거릿은 정말로 창백하고 초췌해 보였다. 정신이 조금씩 돌아오고 있기는 했지만."], ["But the sickly daze of the swoon made her still miserably faint.", "그러나 실신의 메스꺼운 몽롱함 때문에 그녀는 여전히 비참하리만치 기운이 없었다. 그녀는 주위에서 사람들이 움직이는 것도, 오드콜로뉴가 주는 시원함도 느끼고 있었고, 적시는 손길이 쉬지 말고 계속되기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["She was conscious of movement around her, and of refreshment from the eau de Cologne, and a craving for the bathing to go on without intermission; but when they stopped to talk, she could no more have opened her eyes, or spoken to ask for more bathing, than the people who lie in death-like trance can move, or utter sound, to arrest the awful preparations for their burial, while they are yet fully aware, not merely of the actions of those around them, but of the idea that is the motive for such actions.", "그러나 그들이 멈추고 이야기를 나눌 때에도 그녀는 눈을 뜨거나 더 적셔 달라고 말할 수가 없었다. 죽은 듯한 혼수에 빠진 사람이, 주위 사람들의 행동뿐 아니라 그런 행동을 하게 만든 생각까지 또렷이 알면서도, 자기 장례의 그 무서운 준비를 막으려 몸을 움직이거나 소리를 낼 수 없는 것과 똑같았다."]], [["Jane paused in her bathing, to reply to Miss Thornton’s question.", "제인은 적시던 손을 멈추고 손턴 아가씨의 물음에 대답했다."]], [["“She’d have been safe enough, miss, if she’d stayed in the drawing-room, or come up to us; we were in the front garret, and could see it all, out of harm’s way.”", "“아가씨, 응접실에 그대로 계셨거나 저희한테 올라오셨으면 아무 일도 없으셨을 겁니다요. 저희는 앞쪽 다락에 있어서 다칠 일 없이 다 내다보았거든요.”"]], [["“Where was she then?”", "“그럼 이 사람은 어디 있었는데?”"], ["said Fanny, drawing nearer by slow degrees, as she became accustomed to the sight of Margaret’s pale face.", "패니가 마거릿의 창백한 얼굴에 차츰 익숙해지면서 조금씩 다가서며 물었다."]], [["“Just before the front door—with master!”", "“앞문 바로 앞에요. 주인님하고요!”"], ["said Jane, significantly.", "제인이 뜻을 담아 말했다."]], [["“With John!", "“존하고!"], ["with my brother!", "우리 오빠하고!"], ["How did she get there?”", "어쩌다 거기까지 갔대?”"]], [["“Nay, miss, that’s not for me to say,”", "“글쎄요, 아가씨, 그건 제가 말씀드릴 일이 아니지요.”"], ["answered Jane, with a slight toss of her head.", "제인이 고개를 살짝 치켜들며 대답했다."], ["“Sarah did——”", "“세라가 그러는데……”"]], [["“Sarah what?”", "“세라가 뭐?”"], ["said Fanny, with impatient curiosity.", "패니가 참지 못하고 궁금해하며 물었다."]], [["Jane resumed her bathing, as if what Sarah did or said was not exactly the thing she liked to repeat.", "제인은 세라가 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 했든 그것을 옮기기가 썩 내키지는 않는다는 듯 다시 적시는 일을 계속했다."]], [["“Sarah what?”", "“세라가 뭐라고?”"], ["asked Fanny, sharply.", "패니가 날카롭게 물었다."], ["“Don’t speak in these half sentences, or I can’t understand you.”", "“그렇게 말을 반 토막씩 하지 마. 그러면 알아들을 수가 없잖아.”"]], [["“Well, miss, since you will have it—Sarah, you see, was in the best place for seeing, being at the right-hand window; and she says, and said at the very time too, that she saw Miss Hale with her arms about master’s neck, hugging him before all the people.”", "“그럼, 아가씨, 정 그러시다면 말씀드리죠. 세라는 오른쪽 창가에 있어서 보기에 제일 좋은 자리였거든요. 그런데 세라 말이, 바로 그때도 그렇게 말했습니다만, 헤일 양이 주인님 목에 두 팔을 두르고 사람들이 다 보는 앞에서 끌어안고 있는 걸 봤다지 뭡니까.”"]], [["“I don’t believe it,”", "“난 안 믿어.” 패니가 말했다."], ["said Fanny.", "“이 사람이 우리 오빠를 마음에 두고 있는 건 알아."], ["“I know she cares for my brother; any one can see that; and I dare say, she’d give her eyes if he’d marry her,—which he never will, I can tell her.", "그건 누구 눈에나 보이니까. 오빠가 청혼만 해 준다면 눈이라도 내줄 사람이지."], ["But I don’t believe she’d be so bold and forward as to put her arms round his neck.”", "하지만 오빠는 절대 그러지 않을 거야, 내 장담해. 그래도 목에 팔을 두를 만큼 그렇게 뻔뻔하고 들이대는 사람이라고는 믿지 않아.”"]], [["“Poor young lady!", "“가엾은 아가씨!"], ["she’s paid for it dearly if she did.", "정말 그러셨다면 값을 톡톡히 치르신 거지요."], ["It’s my belief, that the blow has given her such an ascendancy of blood to the head as she’ll never get the better from.", "제 생각에는 그 충격 때문에 머리로 피가 몰려서 다시는 회복하지 못하실 것 같습니다요."], ["She looks like a corpse now.”", "지금 꼭 송장 같잖아요.”"]], [["“Oh, I wish mamma would come!”", "“아, 어머니가 얼른 오셨으면!”"], ["said Fanny, wringing her hands.", "패니가 두 손을 쥐어짜며 말했다."], ["“I never was in the room with a dead person before.”", "“나는 죽은 사람하고 한방에 있어 본 적이 없단 말이야.”"]], [["“Stay, miss!", "“잠깐만요, 아가씨!"], ["She’s not dead: her eye-lids are quivering, and here’s wet tears a-coming down her cheeks.", "돌아가신 게 아니에요. 눈꺼풀이 떨리고, 여기 뺨으로 젖은 눈물이 흘러내리고 있잖습니까."], ["Speak to her, Miss Fanny!”", "말을 걸어 보세요, 패니 아가씨!”"]], [["“Are you better now?”", "“이제 좀 나아졌어요?”"], ["asked Fanny, in a quavering voice.", "패니가 떨리는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["No answer; no sign of recognition; but a faint pink colour returned to her lips, although the rest of her face was ashen pale.", "대답도 없었고 알아보는 기색도 없었다. 다만 얼굴의 나머지는 잿빛으로 창백한데도 입술에는 옅은 분홍빛이 돌아왔다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton came hurriedly in, with the nearest surgeon she could find.", "손턴 부인이 가장 가까이서 찾을 수 있었던 외과의를 데리고 서둘러 들어왔다."]], [["“How is she?", "“좀 어떠냐?"], ["Are you better, my dear?”", "나아졌니, 얘야?”"], ["as Margaret opened her filmy eyes, and gazed dreamily at her.", "마거릿이 흐릿한 눈을 뜨고 꿈결처럼 부인을 바라보자 그렇게 물었다."], ["“Here is Mr. Lowe come to see you.”", "“로 선생님이 널 보러 오셨다.”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton spoke loudly and distinctly, as to a deaf person.", "손턴 부인은 귀먹은 사람에게 하듯 크고 또렷하게 말했다."], ["Margaret tried to rise, and drew her ruffled, luxuriant hair instinctively over the cut.", "마거릿은 일어나려 하면서 흐트러진 숱 많은 머리카락을 무의식중에 상처 위로 끌어당겼다."]], [["“I am better now,”", "“이제 좀 나았어요.”"], ["said she, in a very low, faint voice.", "그녀가 아주 낮고 힘없는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I was a little sick.”", "“잠깐 속이 좋지 않았을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["She let him take her hand and feel her pulse.", "그녀는 그가 손을 잡고 맥을 짚도록 내버려 두었다."], ["The bright colour came for a moment into her face, when he asked to examine the wound in her forehead; and she glanced up at Jane, as if shrinking from her inspection more than from the doctor’s.", "그가 이마의 상처를 살펴봐도 되겠느냐고 묻자 그녀의 얼굴에 잠시 화색이 돌았다. 그리고 그녀는 의사보다 오히려 제인의 눈길을 피하려는 듯 제인을 흘긋 올려다보았다."]], [["“It is not much, I think.", "“대단치 않은 것 같아요."], ["I am better now.", "이제 나았어요."], ["I must go home.”", "집에 가야 해요.”"]], [["“Not until I have applied some strips of plaster, and you have rested a little.”", "“반창고를 몇 조각 붙이고 좀 쉬시기 전에는 안 됩니다.”"]], [["She sat down hastily, without another word, and allowed it to be bound up.", "그녀는 더 말하지 않고 서둘러 앉아서 상처를 싸매도록 두었다."]], [["“Now, if you please,”", "“이제 됐으면요.”"], ["said she, “I must go.", "그녀가 말했다. “가야겠어요."], ["Mamma will not see it, I think.", "어머니는 못 보실 것 같은데요."], ["It is under the hair, is it not?”", "머리카락에 가려지지요?”"]], [["“Quite; no one could tell.”", "“그럼요. 아무도 모를 겁니다.”"]], [["“But you must not go,”", "“하지만 가면 안 돼요.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton, impatiently.", "손턴 부인이 못마땅하다는 듯 말했다."], ["“You are not fit to go.”", "“갈 만한 몸이 아니에요.”"]], [["“I must,”", "“가야 해요.”"], ["said Margaret, decidedly.", "마거릿이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“Think of mamma.", "“어머니를 생각해 주세요."], ["If they should hear—— Besides, I must go,”", "어머니가 이 일을 들으시기라도 하면…… 게다가 저는 가야 해요.”"], ["said she, vehemently.", "그녀가 격하게 말했다."], ["“I cannot stay here.", "“여기 있을 수가 없어요."], ["May I ask for a cab?”", "마차를 좀 불러 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“You are quite flushed and feverish,”", "“얼굴이 아주 상기되고 열이 있군요.”"], ["observed Mr. Lowe.", "로 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It is only with being here, when I do so want to go.", "“그건 이렇게 가고 싶은데 여기 있어야 해서 그런 거예요."], ["The air—getting away, would do me more good than anything,”", "바깥바람이, 여기서 벗어나는 것이 무엇보다 제게 좋을 거예요.”"], ["pleaded she.", "그녀가 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["“I really believe it is as she says,”", "“정말 저분 말씀대로인 것 같습니다.”"], ["Mr. Lowe replied.", "로 씨가 대답했다."], ["“If her mother is so ill as you told me on the way here, it may be very serious if she hears of this riot, and does not see her daughter back at the time she expects.", "“오는 길에 말씀하신 대로 어머니가 그토록 편찮으시다면, 이 소동을 전해 듣고도 기다리던 시간에 따님이 돌아오지 않는다면 아주 위중해질 수 있습니다."], ["The injury is not deep.", "상처는 깊지 않습니다."], ["I will fetch a cab, if your servants are still afraid to go out.”", "댁의 하인들이 아직 나가기를 겁내한다면 제가 마차를 불러오지요.”"]], [["“Oh, thank you!”", "“아, 고맙습니다!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“It will do me more good than anything.", "“그게 무엇보다 제게 좋을 거예요."], ["It is the air of this room that makes me feel so miserable.”", "이 방의 공기 때문에 이렇게 견디기 힘든 거예요.”"]], [["She leant back on the sofa, and closed her eyes.", "그녀는 소파에 등을 기대고 눈을 감았다."], ["Fanny beckoned her mother out of the room, and told her something that made her equally anxious with Margaret for the departure of the latter.", "패니가 어머니에게 눈짓해 방 밖으로 불러내더니 무슨 이야기를 했고, 그 말에 부인도 마거릿 못지않게 그녀가 어서 떠나기를 바라게 되었다."], ["Not that she fully believed Fanny’s statement; but she credited enough to make her manner to Margaret appear very much constrained, at wishing her good-bye.", "패니의 말을 다 믿어서가 아니라, 작별 인사를 할 때 마거릿을 대하는 태도가 몹시 서먹해 보일 만큼은 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Lowe returned in the cab.", "로 씨가 마차를 타고 돌아왔다."]], [["“If you will allow me, I will see you home, Miss Hale.", "“괜찮으시다면 댁까지 모셔다드리겠습니다, 헤일 양."], ["The streets are not very quiet yet.”", "거리가 아직 그리 잠잠하지 않으니까요.”"]], [["Margaret’s thoughts were quite alive enough to the present to make her desirous of getting rid of both Mr. Lowe and the cab before she reached Crampton Crescent, for fear of alarming her father and mother.", "마거릿의 머릿속은 지금 이 순간을 충분히 헤아릴 만큼은 또렷해서, 아버지와 어머니를 놀라게 할까 봐 크램프턴 크레센트에 닿기 전에 로 씨와 마차를 둘 다 떼어 놓고 싶어 했다."], ["Beyond that one aim she would not look.", "그 한 가지 목표 너머로는 눈을 돌리려 하지 않았다."], ["That ugly dream of insolent words spoken about herself, could never be forgotten—but could be put aside till she was stronger—for, oh!", "자기를 두고 오간 무례한 말이라는 그 흉한 꿈은 결코 잊을 수 없겠지만 좀 더 기운을 차릴 때까지 미뤄 둘 수는 있었다."], ["she was very weak; and her mind sought for some present fact to steady itself upon, and keep it from utterly losing consciousness in another hideous, sickly swoon.", "아, 그녀는 몹시도 약해져 있었으니까. 그리고 그녀의 마음은 또 한 번의 끔찍하고 메스꺼운 실신 속으로 아주 가라앉아 버리지 않도록 자신을 붙들어 줄 눈앞의 무언가를 찾고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII", "# 제23장"]], [["“Which when his mother saw, she in her mind Was troubled sore, he wist well what to ween.”", "“그것을 보고 어머니는 마음속으로 몹시 근심하였으나, 그는 무엇을 헤아려야 할지 잘 알고 있었더라.”"], ["SPENSER.", "— 스펜서."]], [["Margaret had not been gone five minutes when Mr.", "마거릿이 떠난 지 5분도 되지 않아 손턴 씨가 들어왔다."], ["Thornton came in, his face all a-glow.", "얼굴이 온통 달아올라 있었다."]], [["“I could not come sooner: the superintendent would—— Where is she?”", "“더 일찍 올 수가 없었습니다. 서장이 자꾸—— 그분은 어디 계십니까?”"], ["He looked round the dining-room, and then almost fiercely at his mother, who was quietly re-arranging the disturbed furniture, and did not instantly reply.", "그는 식당을 둘러보고는, 흐트러진 가구를 조용히 제자리에 돌려놓으며 얼른 대답하지 않는 어머니를 거의 사납다시피 바라보았다."], ["“Where is Miss Hale?”", "“헤일 양은 어디 계십니까?”"], ["asked he again.", "그가 다시 물었다."]], [["“Gone home,”", "“집에 갔다.”"], ["said she, rather shortly.", "어머니가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Gone home!”", "“집에 갔다니요!”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["She was a great deal better.", "훨씬 나아졌더구나."], ["Indeed, I don’t believe it was so very much of a hurt; only some people faint at the least thing.”", "사실 그리 크게 다친 것 같지도 않다. 그저 사소한 일에도 정신을 놓는 사람이 있는 법이지.”"]], [["“I am sorry she has gone home,”", "“집에 갔다니 안타깝군요.”"], ["said he, walking uneasily about.", "그가 불안하게 서성이며 말했다."], ["“She could not have been fit for it.”", "“그럴 만한 몸이 아니었을 텐데요.”"]], [["“She said she was; and Mr. Lowe said she was.", "“본인이 괜찮다고 했고, 로 씨도 괜찮다고 했다."], ["I went for him myself.”", "내가 직접 그이를 데리러 갔어.”"]], [["“Thank you, mother.”", "“고맙습니다, 어머니.”"], ["He stopped, and partly held out his hand to give her a grateful shake.", "그는 걸음을 멈추고 고마움을 담아 손을 맞잡으려는 듯 손을 반쯤 내밀었다."], ["But she did not notice the movement.", "그러나 어머니는 그 몸짓을 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“What have you done with your Irish people?”", "“그 아일랜드 사람들은 어떻게 했느냐?”"]], [["“Sent to the Dragon, for a good meal for them, poor wretches.", "“드래건 여관으로 보냈습니다. 그 딱한 것들에게 제대로 된 끼니라도 먹이려고요."], ["And then, luckily, I caught Father Grady, and I’ve asked him in to speak to them, and dissuade them from going off in a body.", "그러고는 다행히 그래디 신부를 붙잡아서, 들어와 그들에게 말씀을 좀 해 달라고, 다 함께 떠나 버리지 않도록 말려 달라고 부탁해 두었습니다."], ["How did Miss Hale go home?", "헤일 양은 어떻게 집에 갔습니까?"], ["I’m sure she could not walk.”", "걸어갈 수는 없었을 텐데요.”"]], [["“She had a cab.", "“삯마차를 탔다."], ["Everything was done properly, even to the paying.", "삯을 치르는 것까지 하나도 빠짐없이 제대로 해 두었어."], ["Let us talk of something else.", "다른 이야기를 하자꾸나."], ["She has caused disturbance enough.”", "그 애 때문에 겪은 소란은 그만하면 됐다.”"]], [["“I don’t know where I should have been but for her.”", "“그분이 아니었으면 제가 어떻게 됐을지 모릅니다.”"]], [["“Are you become so helpless as to have to be defended by a girl?”", "“너는 계집아이한테 감싸여야 할 만큼 그렇게 무력해졌느냐?”"], ["asked Mrs. Thornton scornfully.", "손턴 부인이 경멸하듯 물었다."]], [["He reddened.", "그는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“Not many girls would have taken the blows on herself which were meant for me;—meant with right down goodwill, too.”", "“저를 노리고 날아온 매질을, 그것도 아주 작심하고 날린 매질을 제 몸으로 받아 낼 여자는 흔치 않습니다.”"]], [["“A girl in love will do a good deal,”", "“사랑에 빠진 계집아이는 별짓을 다 하는 법이다.”"], ["replied Mrs. Thornton, shortly.", "손턴 부인이 짧게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Mother!”", "“어머니!”"], ["He made a step forwards; stood still; heaved with passion.", "그는 한 걸음 앞으로 나서다가 멈춰 섰고, 격정에 겨워 가슴이 들썩였다."]], [["She was a little startled at the evident force he used to keep himself calm.", "어머니는 그가 스스로를 가라앉히려고 쓰는 힘이 그토록 뚜렷이 드러나는 것에 조금 놀랐다."], ["She was not sure of the nature of the emotions she had provoked.", "자신이 불러일으킨 감정이 어떤 것인지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다."], ["It was only their violence that was clear.", "분명한 것은 그 격렬함뿐이었다."], ["Was it anger?", "노여움일까?"], ["His eyes glowed, his figure was dilated, his breath came thick and fast.", "그의 눈이 이글거렸고, 몸은 부풀어 오른 듯했으며, 숨결이 굵고 가빴다."], ["It was a mixture of joy, of anger, of pride, of glad surprise, of panting doubt; but she could not read it.", "그것은 기쁨과 노여움과 자존심과 반가운 놀라움과 헐떡이는 의심이 뒤섞인 것이었으나, 그녀는 그것을 읽어 낼 수 없었다."], ["Still it made her uneasy,—as the presence of all strong feeling, of which the cause is not fully understood or sympathised in, always has this effect.", "그래도 그것은 그녀를 불안하게 만들었다. 까닭을 온전히 헤아리지도 못하고 함께 느끼지도 못하는 강한 감정이 곁에 있을 때면 늘 그런 법이다."], ["She went to the side-board, opened a drawer, and took out a duster, which she kept there for any occasional purpose.", "그녀는 찬장으로 가서 서랍을 열고, 이런저런 일에 쓰려고 넣어 둔 마른걸레를 꺼냈다."], ["She had seen a drop of eau de Cologne on the polished arm of the sofa, and instinctively sought to wipe it off.", "소파의 반들반들한 팔걸이에 오드콜로뉴 한 방울이 떨어져 있는 것을 보고 저도 모르게 그것을 닦아 내려 한 것이다."], ["But she kept her back turned to her son much longer than was necessary; and when she spoke, her voice seemed unusual and constrained.", "그러나 그녀는 필요한 것보다 훨씬 오래 아들에게 등을 돌린 채 있었다. 그리고 입을 열었을 때 그 목소리는 여느 때와 달리 억지로 눌린 듯 들렸다."]], [["“You have taken some steps about the rioters, I suppose?", "“폭도들에 대해서는 무슨 조치를 취했겠지?"], ["You don’t apprehend anymore violence, do you?", "더 이상 난동이 있으리라고 여기는 건 아니겠지?"], ["Where were the police?", "경찰은 다들 어디 있었더냐?"], ["Never at hand when they’re wanted!”", "필요할 때는 한 번도 곁에 없구나!”"]], [["“On the contrary, I saw three or four of them, when the gates gave way, struggling and beating about in fine fashion; and more came running up just when the yard was clearing.", "“그렇지 않습니다. 문이 무너질 때 서너 명이 한창 밀치고 두들기며 애쓰는 걸 봤고, 마당이 비어 갈 무렵에는 더 달려왔습니다."], ["I might have given some of the fellows in charge then, if I had had my wits about me.", "제가 정신만 차렸더라면 그때 몇 놈은 넘길 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["But there will be no difficulty, plenty of people can identify them.”", "하지만 어려울 건 없습니다. 얼굴을 알아볼 사람이야 얼마든지 있으니까요.”"]], [["“But won’t they come back to-night?”", "“그렇지만 오늘 밤에 다시 오지는 않겠느냐?”"]], [["“I’m going to see about a sufficient guard for the premises.", "“공장을 지킬 경비를 넉넉히 세우려고 알아보러 갈 참입니다."], ["I have appointed to meet Captain Hanbury in half an hour at the station.”", "30분 뒤에 경찰서에서 핸버리 대위를 만나기로 해 두었습니다.”"]], [["“You must have some tea first.”", "“차부터 좀 마셔야지.”"]], [["“Tea!", "“차라니요!"], ["Yes, I suppose I must.", "그렇군요, 그래야겠습니다."], ["It’s half-past six, and I may be out for some time.", "6시 반이고, 한동안 나가 있게 될지도 모르니까요."], ["Don’t sit up for me, mother.”", "저를 기다리느라 앉아 계시지 마세요, 어머니.”"]], [["“You expect me to go to bed before I have seen you safe, do you?”", "“네가 무사히 돌아온 걸 보기도 전에 내가 자리에 들 성싶으냐?”"]], [["“Well, perhaps not.”", "“글쎄요, 아마 아니겠지요.”"], ["He hesitated for a moment.", "그는 잠시 망설였다."], ["“But if I’ve time, I shall go round by Crampton, after I’ve arranged with the police and seen Hamper and Clarkson.”", "“하지만 시간이 되면, 경찰과 이야기를 끝내고 햄퍼와 클라크슨을 만난 뒤에 크램프턴에 들러 볼까 합니다.”"], ["Their eyes met; they looked at each other intently for a minute.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다. 그들은 한참 동안 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Then she asked:", "그러고 나서 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Why are you going round by Crampton?”", "“크램프턴에는 왜 들르려는 게냐?”"]], [["“To ask after Miss Hale.”", "“헤일 양의 안부를 물으려고요.”"]], [["“I will send.", "“내가 사람을 보내마."], ["Williams must take the water-bed she came to ask for. He shall inquire how she is.”", "윌리엄스가 그 애가 얻으러 온 물침대를 가져가야 하니, 그 편에 안부를 묻게 하마.”"]], [["“I must go myself.”", "“제가 직접 가야겠습니다.”"]], [["“Not merely to ask how Miss Hale is?”", "“헤일 양이 어떤지 묻기만 하려는 건 아니겠지?”"]], [["“No, not merely for that.", "“네, 그것만은 아닙니다."], ["I want to thank her for the way in which she stood between me and the mob.”", "그분이 저와 폭도들 사이를 막아선 데 대해 고맙다는 말을 하고 싶습니다.”"]], [["“What made you go down at all?", "“애초에 왜 내려갔던 게냐?"], ["It was putting your head into the lion’s mouth!”", "사자 아가리에 제 머리를 들이민 꼴이 아니냐!”"]], [["He glanced sharply at her; saw that she did not know what had passed between him and Margaret in the drawing-room: and replied by another question:", "그는 어머니를 날카롭게 흘끗 보았다. 응접실에서 자신과 마거릿 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 어머니가 모른다는 것을 알아채고는, 다른 물음으로 대답을 대신했다."]], [["“Shall you be afraid to be left without me, until I can get some of the police; or had we better send Williams for them now, and they could be here by the time we have done tea?", "“제가 경찰을 좀 데려올 때까지 저 없이 남아 계시는 게 두려우십니까? 아니면 지금 윌리엄스를 보내는 편이 낫겠습니까?"], ["There’s no time to be lost.", "그러면 우리가 차를 다 마실 즈음에는 와 있을 텐데요."], ["I must be off in a quarter of an hour.”", "지체할 시간이 없습니다. 저는 15분 안에 나가야 합니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton left the room.", "손턴 부인이 방을 나갔다."], ["Her servants wondered at her directions, usually so sharply-cut and decided, now confused and uncertain. Mr.", "하인들은 여느 때 같으면 그토록 딱 부러지고 단호하던 그녀의 지시가 이번에는 어수선하고 갈피를 못 잡는 것을 이상하게 여겼다."], ["Thornton remained in the dining-room, trying to think of the business he had to do at the police-office, and in reality thinking of Margaret.", "손턴 씨는 식당에 남아 경찰서에서 처리해야 할 일을 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 실은 마거릿을 생각하고 있었다."], ["Everything seemed dim and vague beyond—behind—besides the touch of her arms round his neck—the soft clinging which made the dark colour come and go in his cheek as he thought of it.", "자기 목을 감싸던 그녀의 팔의 감촉, 떠올릴 때마다 뺨에 검붉은 빛이 들었다 가시게 하는 그 부드럽게 매달리던 느낌—그 너머도, 그 뒤도, 그 곁도 모두 흐릿하고 어렴풋할 뿐이었다."]], [["The tea would have been very silent, but for Fanny’s perpetual description of her own feelings; how she had been alarmed—and then thought they were gone—and then felt sick and faint and trembling in every limb.", "패니가 제 심경을 쉴 새 없이 늘어놓지 않았더라면 차 시간은 무척 조용했을 것이다. 자기가 얼마나 놀랐는지, 그러다 이제 다들 갔거니 여겼는데, 그다음에는 속이 메스껍고 아득해지며 온몸이 사지마다 떨렸다는 이야기였다."]], [["“There, that’s enough,”", "“자, 그만하면 됐다.”"], ["said her brother, rising from the table.", "오라비가 식탁에서 일어서며 말했다."], ["“The reality was enough for me.”", "“실제로 겪은 것만으로도 나는 충분해.”"], ["He was going to leave the room, when his mother stopped him with her hand upon his arm.", "그가 방을 나서려는데 어머니가 그의 팔에 손을 얹어 붙들었다."]], [["“You will come back here before you go to the Hales’,”", "“헤일 씨 댁에 가기 전에 여기로 돌아오너라.”"], ["said she, in a low, anxious voice.", "그녀가 낮고 애타는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I know what I know,” said Fanny to herself.", "‘나는 알 건 다 알지.’ 패니가 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Why?", "“왜요?"], ["Will it be too late to disturb them?”", "그 시간에 찾아가면 폐가 될 만큼 늦을까요?”"]], [["“John, come back to me for this one evening.", "“존, 오늘 저녁만은 나에게 돌아와 다오."], ["It will be late for Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인에게는 늦은 시각이겠지."], ["But that is not it.", "하지만 그 때문이 아니다."], ["To-morrow, you will—— Come back to-night, John!”", "내일이면 너는—— 오늘 밤은 돌아오너라, 존!” 그녀가 아들에게 무엇을 애원한 적은 좀처럼 없었다."], ["She had seldom pleaded with her son at all—she was too proud for that; but she had never pleaded in vain.", "그러기에는 자존심이 너무 강했다. 그러나 그 애원이 헛되었던 적도 없었다."]], [["“I will return straight here after I have done my business.", "“일을 마치면 곧장 여기로 돌아오겠습니다."], ["You will be sure to enquire after them?—after her?”", "그 댁 안부는 꼭 물어봐 주시겠지요? 그분 안부를요.”"]], [["Mrs. Thornton was by no means a talkative companion to Fanny, nor yet a good listener while her son was absent.", "손턴 부인은 패니에게 결코 말이 많은 상대가 아니었고, 아들이 없는 동안에는 잘 들어 주는 상대도 못 되었다."], ["But on his return, her eyes and ears were keen to see and to listen to all the details which he could give, as to the steps he had taken to secure himself, and those whom he chose to employ, from any repetition of the day’s outrages.", "그러나 아들이 돌아오자 그녀의 눈과 귀는 예리하게 살아나, 그가 자신과 자신이 쓰기로 한 사람들을 그날의 난동이 되풀이되는 일에서 지키려고 취한 조치들을 낱낱이 보고 들으려 했다."], ["He clearly saw his object.", "그는 자기 목적을 뚜렷이 알고 있었다."], ["Punishment and suffering were the natural consequences to those who had taken part in the riot.", "폭동에 가담한 자들에게 처벌과 고통이 따르는 것은 당연한 귀결이었다."], ["All that was necessary, in order that property should be protected, and that the will of the proprietor might cut to his end, clean and sharp as a sword.", "재산이 지켜지고 소유주의 뜻이 칼처럼 깨끗하고 날카롭게 제 목적을 향해 파고들려면 필요한 것은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“Mother!", "“어머니!"], ["You know what I have got to say to Miss Hale, to-morrow?”", "제가 내일 헤일 양에게 무슨 말을 해야 하는지 아시지요?”"]], [["The question came upon her suddenly, during a pause in which she, at least, had forgotten Margaret.", "그 물음은 갑작스레 그녀를 덮쳤다. 적어도 그녀로서는 마거릿을 잊고 있던 침묵의 한때였다."]], [["She looked up at him.", "그녀가 아들을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그래!"], ["I do.", "안다."], ["You can hardly do otherwise.”", "달리 어쩔 수도 없겠지.”"]], [["“Do otherwise!", "“달리 어쩔 수 없다니요!"], ["I don’t understand you.”", "무슨 말씀인지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“I mean that, after allowing her feelings so to overcome her, I consider you bound in honour—”", "“그 애가 감정에 그토록 휘둘리도록 내버려 둔 이상, 너는 명예를 걸고 매인 몸이라는 뜻이다—”"]], [["“Bound in honour,”", "“명예를 걸고 매였다니요.”"], ["said he scornfully.", "그가 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“I’m afraid honour has nothing to do with it.", "“유감스럽지만 명예 따위와는 아무 상관도 없습니다."], ["‘Her feelings overcome her!’ What feelings do you mean?”", "‘감정에 휘둘렸다’니요! 무슨 감정을 말씀하시는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Nay, John, there is no need to be angry.", "“아니다, 존, 성낼 것 없다."], ["Did she not rush down and cling to you to save you from danger?”", "그 애가 너를 위험에서 구하겠다고 달려 내려와 네게 매달리지 않았느냐?”"]], [["“She did!”", "“그랬지요!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But, mother,”", "“하지만 어머니,”"], ["continued he, stopping short in his walk right in front of her.", "그는 어머니 바로 앞에서 걸음을 뚝 멈추며 말을 이었다. “저는 감히 바라지 못하겠습니다."], ["“I dare not hope. I never was faint-hearted before; but I cannot believe such a creature cares for me.”", "전에는 한 번도 마음이 약해진 적이 없었는데, 그런 사람이 저를 마음에 두고 있으리라고는 도저히 믿을 수가 없습니다.”"]], [["“Don’t be foolish, John.", "“어리석은 소리 마라, 존."], ["Such a creature!", "그런 사람이라니!"], ["Why, she might be a duke’s daughter, to hear you speak.", "네 말만 들으면 그 애가 공작의 딸이라도 되는 줄 알겠구나."], ["And what proof more would you have, I wonder, of her caring for you?", "그 애가 너를 마음에 두었다는 증거가 대체 얼마나 더 있어야 하겠느냐?"], ["I can believe she has had a struggle with her aristocratic way of viewing things; but I like her the better for seeing clearly at last.", "그 애가 세상을 보는 제 귀족 같은 눈길과 씨름했으리라는 건 나도 믿는다. 하지만 마침내 제대로 보게 되었으니 나는 오히려 그 애가 더 마음에 든다."], ["It is a good deal for me to say,” said Mrs.", "내가 이런 말을 하는 것만도 대단한 일이지.”"], ["Thornton, smiling slowly, while the tears stood in her eyes; “for after to-night, I stand second.", "손턴 부인이 천천히 미소 지으며 말했는데, 그 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었다. “오늘 밤이 지나면 나는 둘째가 될 테니 말이다."], ["It was to have you to myself, all to myself, a few hours longer, that I begged you not to go till to-morrow.”", "너를 몇 시간이라도 더 나 혼자, 온전히 나 혼자 가지고 싶어서 내일까지는 가지 말라고 부탁했던 게다.”"]], [["“Dearest mother!”", "“더없이 좋은 어머니!”"], ["(Still love is selfish, and in an instant he reverted to his own hopes and fears in a way that drew the cold creeping shadow over Mrs. Thornton’s heart.) “But I know she does not care for me.", "(그래도 사랑은 이기적인 것이어서, 그는 순식간에 제 희망과 두려움으로 되돌아갔고, 그 바람에 손턴 부인의 가슴에는 차가운 그림자가 스멀스멀 드리웠다.) “하지만 저는 그분이 저를 마음에 두지 않는다는 걸 압니다."], ["I shall put myself at her feet—I must.", "그래도 그분의 발치에 저를 내던지겠습니다."], ["If it were but one chance in a thousand—or a million—I should do it.”", "그래야만 합니다. 천에 하나, 아니 백만에 하나의 가망이라도 있다면 저는 그렇게 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“Don’t fear!”", "“두려워 마라!”"], ["said his mother, crushing down her own personal mortification at the little notice he had taken of the rare ebullition of her maternal feelings—of the pang of jealousy that betrayed the intensity of her disregarded love.", "어머니가 말했다. 어미로서의 감정이 좀처럼 없이 터져 나온 그 순간을, 그리고 돌아보아 주지 않는 제 사랑의 깊이를 드러낸 그 질투의 아픔을 아들이 거의 알아차리지도 못한 데 대한 서운함을 짓눌러 삼키면서였다."], ["“Don’t be afraid,”", "“두려워하지 마라.”"], ["she said, coldly.", "그녀가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“As far as love may go she may be worthy of you.", "“사랑으로 말하자면 그 애는 네게 어울리는 사람일지도 모르지."], ["It must have taken a good deal to overcome her pride.", "그 애의 자존심을 꺾는 데는 어지간한 것으로는 안 되었을 게다."], ["Don’t be afraid, John,”", "두려워 마라, 존.”"], ["said she, kissing him, as she wished him good-night.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 잘 자라는 인사와 함께 아들에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["And she went slowly and majestically out of the room.", "그러고는 천천히 위엄 있게 방을 나갔다."], ["But when she got into her own, she locked the door, and sate down to cry unwonted tears.", "그러나 제 방에 들어서자 그녀는 문을 잠그고 앉아, 좀처럼 흘리지 않던 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["Margaret entered the room (where her father and mother still sat, holding low conversation together), looking very pale and white.", "마거릿은 아버지와 어머니가 여전히 앉아 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있는 방으로 들어섰다. 얼굴은 몹시 창백하고 핏기가 없었다."], ["She came close up to them before she could trust herself to speak.", "그녀는 목소리를 낼 자신이 생길 때까지 두 사람 바로 앞으로 다가섰다."]], [["“Mrs. Thornton will send the water-bed, mamma.”", "“손턴 부인이 물침대를 보내 주실 거예요, 어머니.”"]], [["“Dear, how tired you look!", "“얘야, 어쩌면 그렇게 지쳐 보이니!"], ["Is it very hot, Margaret?”", "많이 덥더냐, 마거릿?”"]], [["“Very hot, and the streets are rather rough with the strike.”", "“아주 더워요. 그리고 파업 때문에 거리가 좀 험해요.”"]], [["Margaret’s colour came back vivid and bright as ever; but it faded away instantly.", "마거릿의 얼굴빛이 여느 때처럼 선명하고 환하게 돌아왔다가 이내 스러졌다."]], [["“Here has been a message from Bessy Higgins, asking you to go to her,”", "“베시 히긴스한테서 너더러 와 달라는 전갈이 왔더구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."], ["“But I’m sure you look too tired.”", "“하지만 아무래도 너는 너무 지쳐 보인다.”"]], [["“Yes!”", "“네!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“I am tired, I cannot go.”", "“저는 지쳤어요. 갈 수 없어요.”"]], [["She was very silent and trembling while she made tea.", "그녀는 차를 끓이는 내내 말이 없었고 몸이 떨렸다."], ["She was thankful to see her father so much occupied with her mother as not to notice her looks.", "아버지가 어머니에게 온통 마음을 쏟느라 자신의 안색을 알아차리지 못하는 것이 고마웠다."], ["Even after her mother went to bed, he was not content to be absent from her, but undertook to read her to sleep.", "어머니가 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 아버지는 곁을 떠나려 하지 않고 어머니가 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 주겠다고 나섰다."], ["Margaret was alone.", "마거릿은 혼자가 되었다."]], [["“Now I will think of it—now I will remember it all.", "“이제 그 일을 생각해 봐야지. 이제 다 떠올려 봐야지."], ["I could not before—I dared not.”", "아까는 그럴 수가 없었어."], ["She sat still in her chair, her hands clasped on her knees, her lips compressed, her eyes fixed as one who sees a vision.", "감히 그러지 못했어.” 그녀는 의자에 가만히 앉아 두 손을 무릎 위에 모으고 입술을 꼭 다문 채, 환영을 보는 사람처럼 눈길을 한곳에 붙박았다."], ["She drew a deep breath.", "그녀는 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["“I, who hate scenes—I, who have despised people for showing emotion—who have thought them wanting in self-control—I went down and must needs throw myself into the melée, like a romantic fool!", "“소란이라면 질색인 내가, 감정을 드러내는 사람들을 경멸해 온 내가, 그런 이들은 자제력이 모자란다고 여겨 온 내가, 기어이 내려가서 낭만적인 바보처럼 그 아수라장 속으로 몸을 던지고 말았어."], ["Did I do any good?", "내가 무슨 도움이라도 됐을까?"], ["They would have gone away without me, I dare say.”", "내가 없었어도 그 사람들은 물러갔겠지.”"], ["But this was overleaping the rational conclusion,—as in an instant her well-poised judgment felt.", "그러나 이것은 이치에 맞는 결론을 넘어선 생각이었고, 그녀의 잘 잡힌 판단력이 곧바로 그것을 느꼈다."], ["“No, perhaps they would not. I did some good.", "“아니야, 어쩌면 물러가지 않았을지도 몰라."], ["But what possessed me to defend that man as if he were a helpless child!", "나는 얼마쯤 도움이 됐어. 하지만 무엇에 씌었기에 그 사람을 무력한 아이라도 되는 양 감싸고 나섰을까!"], ["Ah!”", "아!”"], ["said she, clenching her hands together, “it is no wonder those people thought I was in love with him, after disgracing myself in that way.", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡아 쥐며 말했다. “그렇게 스스로를 욕보였으니, 그 사람들이 내가 그를 사랑한다고 여긴 것도 무리가 아니지."], ["I in love—and with him too!”", "내가 사랑을, 그것도 그 사람을!”"], ["Her pale cheeks suddenly became one flame of fire; and she covered her face with her hands.", "창백하던 두 뺨이 별안간 온통 불길처럼 달아올랐다. 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 감쌌다."], ["When she took them away, her palms were wet with scalding tears.", "손을 떼었을 때 손바닥은 뜨겁게 타는 눈물로 젖어 있었다."]], [["“Oh how low I am fallen that they should say that of me!", "“아, 사람들이 나를 두고 그런 말을 하다니, 내가 얼마나 낮은 데까지 떨어졌는가!"], ["I could not have been so brave for any one else, just because he was so utterly indifferent to me—if, indeed, I do not positively dislike him.", "다른 누구를 위해서였다면 나는 그토록 용감할 수 없었을 거야. 바로 그가 나에게는 아무것도 아닌 사람이었기 때문에, 아니 정말이지 내가 그를 싫어하지 않는다고는 할 수 없기 때문에 그럴 수 있었어."], ["It made me the more anxious that there should be fair play on each side; and I could see what fair play was.", "그래서 나는 양쪽 모두에게 공정한 처사가 있어야 한다는 마음이 더 간절했고, 무엇이 공정한 처사인지도 알아볼 수 있었어."], ["It was not fair,”", "공정하지 않았어.”"], ["said she vehemently, “that he should stand there—sheltered, awaiting the soldiers, who might catch those poor maddened creatures as in a trap—without an effort on his part, to bring them to reason.", "그녀가 격하게 말했다. “그가 몸을 숨긴 채 거기 서서, 저 딱하고 미쳐 버린 사람들을 덫에 걸린 짐승처럼 잡아갈 군인들을 기다리기만 하고, 그들을 타일러 정신 차리게 하려는 노력은 조금도 하지 않은 것은 공정하지 않았어."], ["And it was worse than unfair for them to set on him as they threatened.", "그리고 그들이 으르대던 대로 그에게 달려든 것은 공정하지 않은 것보다 더 나빴어."], ["I would do it again, let who will say what they like of me.", "나는 다시 그렇게 할 거야. 누가 나를 두고 무슨 말을 하든 상관없어."], ["If I saved one blow, one cruel, angry action that might otherwise have been committed, I did a woman’s work.", "그러지 않았더라면 저질러졌을 매질 한 번을, 잔인하고 성난 짓 하나를 막았다면, 나는 여자로서 할 일을 한 거야."], ["Let them insult my maiden pride as they will—I walk pure before God!”", "저 사람들이 내 처녀의 자존심을 마음껏 모욕하라지. 나는 하느님 앞에 깨끗하게 걸어간다!”"]], [["She looked up, and a noble peace seemed to descend and calm her face, till it was “stiller than chiselled marble.”", "그녀가 고개를 들자 고귀한 평화가 내려앉아 그 얼굴을 가라앉히는 듯했고, 마침내 그 얼굴은 “정으로 다듬은 대리석보다 더 고요”해졌다."]], [["Dixon came in:", "딕슨이 들어왔다."]], [["“If you please, Miss Margaret, here’s the water-bed from Mrs.", "“저, 마거릿 아가씨, 손턴 부인 댁에서 물침대가 왔습니다요. 아무래도 오늘 밤에는 너무 늦은 것 같아요."], ["Thornton’s.", "마님이 거의 잠드셨으니까요."], ["It’s too late for to-night, I’m afraid, for missus is nearly asleep: but it will do nicely for to-morrow.”", "그렇지만 내일이면 요긴하게 쓰이겠죠.”"]], [["“Very,”", "“그렇겠네.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You must send our best thanks.”", "“우리가 아주 고마워하더라고 꼭 전해 줘.”"]], [["Dixon left the room for a moment.", "딕슨이 잠깐 방을 나갔다."]], [["“If you please, Miss Margaret, he says he’s to ask particular how you are.", "“저, 마거릿 아가씨, 그 사람 말이 아가씨 안부를 각별히 여쭈라고 했답니다."], ["I think he must mean missus; but he says his last words were, to ask how Miss Hale was.”", "마님을 말하는 거겠거니 싶은데, 헤일 양이 어떠신지 여쭈라는 게 마지막 당부였다고 하네요.”"]], [["“Me!”", "“나를?”"], ["said Margaret, drawing herself up.", "마거릿이 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우며 말했다."], ["“I am quite well.", "“나는 아주 멀쩡해."], ["Tell him I am perfectly well.”", "더없이 잘 있다고 전해 줘.”"], ["But her complexion was as deadly white as her handkerchief; and her head ached intensely.", "그러나 그녀의 안색은 손수건처럼 죽은 듯이 새하얬고, 머리가 몹시 아팠다."]], [["Mr. Hale now came in.", "이제 헤일 씨가 들어왔다."], ["He had left his sleeping wife; and wanted, as Margaret saw, to be amused and interested by something that she was to tell him.", "잠든 아내를 두고 온 그는, 마거릿이 보기에 딸이 들려줄 무슨 이야기로 재미와 흥미를 얻고 싶어 했다."], ["With sweet patience did she bear her pain, without a word of complaint; and rummaged up numberless small subjects for conversation—all except the riot, and that she never named once.", "그녀는 불평 한마디 없이 고운 인내로 제 아픔을 견디며, 이야깃거리가 될 만한 자잘한 것들을 헤아릴 수 없이 끌어냈다. 폭동만 빼고 모든 것을 말이다."], ["It turned her sick to think of it.", "그것만은 한 번도 입에 올리지 않았다. 생각만 해도 속이 뒤집혔다."]], [["“Good-night, Margaret.", "“잘 자거라, 마거릿."], ["I have every chance of a good night myself, and you are looking very pale with your watching.", "나는 오늘 밤 푹 잘 수 있을 것 같구나. 너는 간호하느라 얼굴이 아주 창백하다."], ["I shall call Dixon if your mother needs anything.", "네 어머니한테 무슨 일이 있으면 내가 딕슨을 부르마."], ["Do you go to bed and sleep like a top; for I’m sure you need it, poor child!”", "너는 가서 세상모르고 자거라. 정말이지 그래야 할 게다, 가엾은 것!”"]], [["“Good-night, papa!”", "“안녕히 주무세요, 아버지!”"]], [["She let her colour go—the false smile fade away—the eyes grow dull with heavy pain.", "그녀는 얼굴빛이 가시게 두었고, 거짓 미소가 스러지게 두었고, 두 눈이 무거운 아픔으로 흐려지게 두었다."], ["She released her strong will from its laborious task.", "그녀는 고된 소임에서 제 굳센 의지를 놓아주었다."], ["Till morning she might feel ill and weary.", "아침이 올 때까지는 아프고 지쳐 있어도 괜찮았다."]], [["She lay down and never stirred.", "그녀는 자리에 누워 조금도 뒤척이지 않았다. 손이나 발을, 아니 손가락 하나조차 움직이는 일은 의지로든 몸으로든 할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["To move hand or foot, or even so much as one finger, would have been beyond the powers of either volition or motion.", "너무 지치고 너무 얼떨떨해서 그녀는 자신이 도무지 잠들지 못한 것으로 여겼다. 열에 들뜬 생각들이 잠과 깨어 있음의 경계를 넘나들며 저희의 비참한 정체를 그대로 지니고 있었다."], ["She was so tired, so stunned, that she thought she never slept at all; her feverish thoughts passed and repassed the boundary between sleeping and waking, and kept their own miserable identity.", "그렇게 엎드려 힘없이 누워 있으면서도 그녀는 혼자일 수가 없었다. 얼굴들의 구름이 그녀를 올려다보았다."], ["She could not be alone, prostrate, powerless as she was,—a cloud of faces looked up at her, giving her no idea of fierce vivid anger, or of personal danger, but a deep sense of shame that she should thus be the object of universal regard—a sense of shame so acute that it seemed as if she would fain have burrowed into the earth to hide herself, and yet she could not escape out of that unwinking glare of many eyes.", "그것은 사납고 생생한 노여움이나 제 몸에 닥친 위험을 떠올리게 하지는 않았고, 다만 자신이 그렇게 뭇사람의 눈길을 받는 대상이 되었다는 깊은 수치심을 안겨 주었다. 그 수치심이 어찌나 날카로운지 땅속으로 파고들어 몸을 숨기고 싶을 지경이었으나, 그러면서도 그녀는 깜박이지 않는 그 수많은 눈의 번득임에서 벗어날 수가 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV", "# 제24장"]], [["“Your beauty was the first that won the place And scal’d the walls of my undaunted heart, Which, captive now, pines in a caitive case, Unkindly met with rigour for desert:— Yet not the less your servant shall abide, In spite of rude repulse or silent pride.”", "“그대의 아름다움이 맨 먼저 그 자리를 차지하고 굽힐 줄 모르던 내 심장의 성벽을 기어올랐으니, 이제 사로잡힌 그 마음은 비천한 옥에 갇혀 시들며, 공을 세우고도 매정한 가혹함만을 만났구나. 그러나 그럼에도 그대의 종은 머무르리라, 무례한 물리침에도 말 없는 자존심에도 아랑곳없이.”"], ["WILLIAM FOWLER.", "— 윌리엄 파울러."]], [["The next morning, Margaret dragged herself up, thankful that the night was over,—unrefreshed, yet rested. All had gone well through the house; her mother had only wakened once. A little breeze was stirring in the hot air, and though there were no trees to show the playful tossing movement caused by the wind among the leaves, Margaret knew how, somewhere or another, by wayside, in copses, or in thick green woods, there was a pleasant, murmuring dancing sound,—a rushing and falling noise, the very thought of which was an echo of distinct gladness in her heart.", "이튿날 아침, 마거릿은 밤이 지나갔다는 데 감사하며 몸을 억지로 일으켰다. 개운하지는 않았으나 쉬기는 한 셈이었다. 집 안은 온통 별일 없이 지나갔다. 어머니는 딱 한 번 깼을 뿐이었다. 뜨거운 공기 속에 실바람이 일고 있었고, 잎사귀 사이로 바람이 일으키는 장난스러운 흔들림을 보여 줄 나무는 한 그루도 없었지만, 마거릿은 알고 있었다. 어딘가에서, 길가에서, 잡목숲에서, 혹은 짙푸른 숲속에서 기분 좋게 소곤거리며 춤추는 소리가, 밀려왔다 잦아드는 그 소리가 일고 있으리라는 것을. 그것을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그녀의 마음속에는 또렷한 기쁨의 메아리가 울렸다."]], [["She sat at her work in Mrs.", "그녀는 헤일 부인의 방에서 바느질감을 들고 앉아 있었다."], ["Hale’s room.", "오전의 선잠이 끝나는 대로 어머니가 옷 입는 것을 도울 참이었다."], ["As soon as that forenoon slumber was over, she would help her mother to dress; after dinner, she would go and see Bessy Higgins.", "저녁 식사 뒤에는 베시 히긴스를 보러 갈 생각이었다. 손턴 집안에 대한 기억은 모조리 몰아내리라."], ["She would banish all recollection of the Thornton family—no need to think of them till they absolutely stood before her in flesh and blood.", "그들이 정말로 피와 살을 지닌 채 눈앞에 서기 전까지는 생각할 까닭이 없었다. 그러나 물론 생각하지 않으려는 애씀은 오히려 그들을 더욱 또렷이 눈앞에 불러올 뿐이었다."], ["But, of course, the effort not to think of them brought them only the more strongly before her; and from time to time, the hot flush came over her pale face sweeping it into colour, as a sunbeam from between watery clouds comes swiftly moving over the sea.", "그리하여 이따금 뜨거운 홍조가 그녀의 창백한 얼굴 위로 번져 물을 들이곤 했다. 비구름 사이로 새어 나온 햇살이 바다 위를 빠르게 훑고 지나가듯이."]], [["Dixon opened the door very softly, and stole on tiptoe up to Margaret, sitting by the shaded window.", "딕슨이 아주 조용히 문을 열고, 그늘진 창가에 앉아 있는 마거릿에게 발끝으로 살금살금 다가왔다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton, Miss Margaret.", "“손턴 씨예요, 마거릿 아가씨."], ["He is in the drawing-room.”", "응접실에 계십니다.”"]], [["Margaret dropped her sewing.", "마거릿은 바느질감을 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“Did he ask for me?", "“나를 찾으셨어요?"], ["Isn’t papa come in?”", "아버지는 아직 안 들어오셨고요?”"]], [["“He asked for you, miss; and master is out.”", "“아가씨를 찾으셨어요. 나리는 나가 계시고요.”"]], [["“Very well, I will come,”", "“알았어요, 내려갈게요.”"], ["said Margaret, quietly.", "마거릿이 조용히 말했다."], ["But she lingered strangely.", "그러나 그녀는 이상하리만치 머뭇거렸다."]], [["Mr. Thornton stood by one of the windows, with his back to the door apparently absorbed in watching something in the street.", "손턴 씨는 창가에 서서 문을 등진 채, 겉보기에는 거리의 무엇인가를 지켜보는 데 골몰해 있었다."], ["But, in truth, he was afraid of himself.", "그러나 실은 자기 자신이 두려웠던 것이다."], ["His heart beat thick at the thought of her coming.", "그녀가 온다는 생각에 심장이 무겁게 뛰었다. 그는 자기 목을 감싸던 그녀의 팔의 감촉을 잊을 수 없었다."], ["He could not forget the touch of her arms around his neck, impatiently felt as it had been at the time; but now the recollection of her clinging defence of him, seemed to thrill him through and through,—to melt away every resolution, all power of self-control, as if it were wax before a fire.", "그때는 그저 성가시게만 느꼈으면서도. 그러나 이제 매달리듯 그를 지켜 주던 그 기억은 온몸을 저릿하게 훑고 지나가는 듯했고, 불 앞의 밀랍처럼 모든 결심과 온갖 자제력을 죄다 녹여 버리는 듯했다."], ["He dreaded lest he should go forwards to meet her, with his arms held out in mute entreaty that she would come and nestle there, as she had done, all unheeded, the day before, but never unheeded again.", "그는 자기가 앞으로 나아가 두 팔을 벌린 채, 어제 아무 헤아림도 받지 못한 채 그랬듯이 그녀가 와서 그 품에 안기기를 말없이 애원하게 될까 봐 두려웠다. 이제 다시는 헤아림 없이 넘기지 않을 터였다."], ["His heart throbbed loud and quick.", "심장이 크고 빠르게 두근거렸다."], ["Strong man as he was, he trembled at the anticipation of what he had to say, and how it might be received.", "강인한 사내였으나 그는 자기가 해야 할 말과 그것이 어떻게 받아들여질지를 미리 그려 보며 떨었다."], ["She might droop, and flush, and flutter to his arms, as to her natural home and resting-place.", "그녀가 고개를 숙이고 얼굴을 붉히며, 마치 제 타고난 집이자 쉼터인 양 그의 품으로 떨려 들지도 몰랐다."], ["One moment he glowed with impatience at the thought that she might do this,—the next he feared a passionate rejection, the idea of which withered up his future with so deadly a blight that he refused to think of it.", "어느 순간에는 그녀가 그럴지도 모른다는 생각에 조바심으로 달아올랐고, 다음 순간에는 격렬한 거절이 두려웠다. 그 생각은 그의 앞날을 어찌나 지독하게 시들려 놓는지 그는 아예 생각하기를 거부했다."], ["He was startled by the sense of the presence of some one else in the room.", "그는 방 안에 다른 누군가가 있다는 느낌에 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["He turned round.", "그가 돌아섰다."], ["She had come in so gently, that he had never heard her; the street noises had been more distinct to his inattentive ear than her slow movements, in her soft muslin gown.", "그녀는 너무나 조용히 들어와서 그는 인기척조차 듣지 못했던 것이다. 딴 데 팔린 그의 귀에는 부드러운 모슬린 드레스 차림의 느린 걸음보다 거리의 소음이 더 또렷했다."]], [["She stood by the table, not offering to sit down.", "그녀는 탁자 곁에 선 채 앉으려 하지 않았다."], ["Her eyelids were dropped half over her eyes; her teeth were shut, not compressed; her lips were just parted over them, allowing the white line to be seen between their curve.", "눈꺼풀은 눈을 반쯤 덮고 있었다. 이는 앙다물지는 않았으나 다물려 있었고, 입술이 그 위로 살짝 벌어져 곡선 사이로 흰 줄이 보였다."], ["Her slow deep breathings dilated her chin and beautiful nostrils; it was the only motion visible on her countenance.", "느리고 깊은 숨결이 턱과 아름다운 콧방울을 부풀렸다. 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 보이는 유일한 움직임이었다."], ["The fine-grained skin, the oval cheek, the rich outline of her mouth, its corners deep set in dimples,—were all wan and pale to-day; the loss of their usual natural healthy colour being made more evident by the heavy shadow of the dark hair, brought down upon her temples, to hide all sign of the blow she had received.", "결 고운 살결도, 갸름한 뺨도, 양끝이 보조개로 깊이 팬 도톰한 입매도 오늘은 온통 핏기 없이 창백했다. 여느 때의 건강한 혈색이 사라진 것은 관자놀이까지 내려 빗은 검은 머리의 짙은 그늘 때문에 한층 두드러졌는데, 그것은 그녀가 맞은 상처의 흔적을 죄다 감추려는 것이었다."], ["Her head, for all its drooping eyes, was thrown a little back, in the old proud attitude.", "눈은 내리깔았어도 고개는 예의 그 도도한 자세로 살짝 뒤로 젖혀져 있었다."], ["Her long arms hung motionless by her sides.", "긴 팔은 옆구리에 미동도 없이 늘어져 있었다."], ["Altogether she looked like some prisoner, falsely accused of a crime that she loathed and despised, and from which she was too indignant to justify herself.", "통틀어 보면 그녀는 자기가 혐오하고 경멸하는 죄를 뒤집어쓴 채, 너무도 분해서 스스로를 변호할 마음조차 없는 죄수처럼 보였다."]], [["Mr. Thornton made a hasty step or two forwards; recovered himself, and went with quiet firmness to the door (which she had left open), and shut it.", "손턴 씨는 급히 한두 걸음 앞으로 나섰다가 마음을 다잡고는, 조용하고도 단호하게 문께로 가서(그녀가 열어 둔 채였다) 문을 닫았다."], ["Then he came back, and stood opposite to her for a moment, receiving the general impression of her beautiful presence, before he dared to disturb it, perhaps to repel it, by what he had to say.", "그러고는 돌아와 잠시 그녀 맞은편에 서서, 자기가 해야 할 말로 그것을 흐트러뜨리기 전에, 어쩌면 밀쳐 내기 전에, 그 아름다운 존재가 주는 전체의 인상을 받아들였다."]], [["“Miss Hale, I was very ungrateful yesterday—”", "“헤일 양, 어제 저는 참으로 배은망덕했습니다……”"]], [["“You had nothing to be grateful for,”", "“감사하실 일이 아무것도 없었어요.”"], ["said she raising her eyes, and looking full and straight at him.", "그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 똑바로 마주 보며 말했다."], ["“You mean, I suppose, that you believe you ought to thank me for what I did.”", "“제가 한 일에 대해 제게 고마워해야 한다고 여기신다는 말씀이시겠지요.”"], ["In spite of herself—in defiance of her anger—the thick blushes came all over her face, and burnt into her very eyes; which fell not nevertheless from their grave and steady look.", "스스로도 어쩌지 못한 채, 분노를 거스르며 짙은 홍조가 온 얼굴에 번져 두 눈까지 타올랐다. 그럼에도 그 눈은 진지하고 흔들림 없는 시선을 거두지 않았다."], ["“It was only a natural instinct; any woman would have done just the same.", "“그저 자연스러운 본능이었을 뿐이에요. 어떤 여자라도 똑같이 했을 거예요."], ["We all feel the sanctity of our sex as a high privilege when we see danger.", "위험을 보면 우리 여자들은 누구나 제 성(性)의 신성함을 높은 특권으로 느끼니까요."], ["I ought rather,”", "오히려 제가……”"], ["said she, hastily, “to apologise to you, for having said thoughtless words which sent you down into the danger.”", "그녀가 서둘러 말했다. “위험 속으로 내려가시게 만든 그 경솔한 말에 대해 사과드려야 할 거예요.”"]], [["“It was not your words; it was the truth they conveyed, pungently as it was expressed.", "“말씀이 문제가 아니었습니다. 아무리 신랄하게 표현되었다 해도, 그 말이 담고 있던 진실이 문제였지요."], ["But you shall not drive me off upon that, and so escape the expression of my deep gratitude, my—”", "그러나 그 이야기로 저를 밀쳐 내고 제 깊은 감사의 표현을, 제……를 피해 가시지는 못합니다.” 그는 이제 벼랑 끝에 서 있었다."], ["he was on the verge now; he would not speak in the haste of his hot passion; he would weigh each word.", "그는 뜨거운 격정에 휩쓸려 서둘러 말하지 않으려 했다. 말 한 마디 한 마디를 저울질하려 했다."], ["He would; and his will was triumphant.", "그러겠다고 마음먹었고, 그의 의지가 이겼다."], ["He stopped in mid career.", "그는 내달리던 한가운데서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“I do not try to escape from anything,”", "“저는 무엇에서도 달아나려 하지 않아요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I simply say, that you owe me no gratitude; and I may add, that any expression of it will be painful to me, because I do not feel that I deserve it.", "“그저 제게 감사하실 이유가 없다고 말씀드리는 것뿐이에요. 그리고 덧붙이자면, 그런 표현은 제게 괴로울 거예요."], ["Still, if it will relieve you from even a fancied obligation, speak on.”", "제가 그럴 자격이 있다고 느끼지 않으니까요. 그래도 그것이 상상 속의 빚에서나마 손턴 씨를 놓여나게 해 준다면, 말씀하세요.”"]], [["“I do not want to be relieved from any obligation,” said he, goaded by her calm manner. “Fancied, or not fancied—I question not myself to know which—I choose to believe that I owe my very life to you—ay—smile, and think it an exaggeration if you will. I believe it, because it adds a value to that life to think—oh, Miss Hale!” continued he, lowering his voice to such a tender intensity of passion that she shivered and trembled before him, “to think circumstances so wrought, that whenever I exult in existence henceforward, I may say to myself, ‘All this gladness in life, all honest pride in doing my work in the world, all this keen sense of being, I owe to her!’ And it doubles the gladness, it makes the pride glow, it sharpens the sense of existence till I hardly know if it is pain or pleasure to think that I owe it to one—nay, you must, you shall hear,”—said he, stepping forward with stern determination—“to one whom I love, as I do not believe man ever loved woman before.” He held her hand tight in his. He panted as he listened for what should come. He threw the hand away with indignation, as he heard her icy tone; for icy it was, though the words came faltering out, as if she knew not where to find them.", "“저는 어떤 빚에서도 놓여나고 싶지 않습니다.” 그녀의 침착한 태도에 자극받아 그가 말했다. “상상이든 아니든, 어느 쪽인지 저 자신에게 캐묻지 않겠습니다. 저는 제 목숨을 헤일 양에게 빚졌다고 믿기로 했습니다. 그래요, 웃으십시오. 과장이라 여기고 싶으면 그러십시오. 저는 그렇게 믿습니다. 그렇게 생각하는 것이 이 목숨에 값을 더해 주니까요. 아, 헤일 양!” 그가 목소리를 낮추어, 그녀가 그 앞에서 몸을 떨 만큼 부드럽고도 격렬한 열정을 담아 말을 이었다. “일이 이렇게 얽힌 덕분에, 앞으로 제가 살아 있음에 기뻐할 때마다 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 수 있으리라는 것 말입니다. ‘이 삶의 모든 기쁨도, 세상에서 내 일을 해내는 온갖 정직한 자부심도, 살아 있다는 이 예리한 감각도 모두 그 사람에게 빚진 것이다!’ 그리고 그것은 기쁨을 갑절로 만들고, 자부심을 타오르게 하고, 살아 있다는 감각을 어찌나 날카롭게 벼려 놓는지, 이것을 한 사람에게 빚졌다고 생각하는 것이 고통인지 즐거움인지조차 알 수 없을 지경입니다. 아니, 들으셔야 합니다, 들으실 겁니다.” 그가 엄한 결의를 품고 앞으로 나서며 말했다. “일찍이 어떤 사내도 여자를 그렇게 사랑한 적이 없으리라 믿을 만큼 제가 사랑하는 한 사람에게 말입니다.” 그가 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 꽉 쥐었다. 그는 무슨 말이 나올지 귀 기울이며 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 그러다 그녀의 얼음장 같은 목소리를 듣고 분해하며 그 손을 뿌리쳤다. 말은 어디서 찾아야 할지 모르겠다는 듯 더듬더듬 나왔지만, 그 목소리는 정말이지 얼음장 같았다."]], [["“Your way of speaking shocks me.", "“그런 말씀은 제게 충격이에요."], ["It is blasphemous.", "그건 신성모독이에요."], ["I cannot help it, if that is my first feeling.", "그것이 제 첫 느낌이라면 저로서도 어쩔 수가 없어요. 말씀하시는 그런 감정을 제가 안다면 그렇지 않을지도 모르지요."], ["It might not be so, I dare say, if I understood the kind of feeling you describe.", "저는 손턴 씨를 괴롭히고 싶지 않아요. 게다가 우리는 조용히 말해야 해요."], ["I do not want to vex you; and besides, we must speak gently, for mamma is asleep; but your whole manner offends me—”", "어머니가 주무시고 계시니까요. 하지만 그 태도 전체가 제게는 모욕이에요……”"]], [["“How!”", "“뭐라고요!”"], ["exclaimed he.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“Offends you!", "“모욕이라니요!"], ["I am indeed most unfortunate.”", "저는 참으로 운이 없군요.”"]], [["“Yes!”", "“그래요!”"], ["said she with recovered dignity.", "그녀가 위엄을 되찾고 말했다."], ["“I do feel offended; and, I think, justly.", "“저는 모욕을 느껴요. 그리고 그것이 마땅하다고 생각해요."], ["You seem to fancy that my conduct of yesterday”—again the deep carnation blush, but this time with eyes kindling with indignation rather than shame—“was a personal act between you and me; and that you may come and thank me for it, instead of perceiving, as a gentleman would—yes!", "손턴 씨는 어제 제 행동이”—다시 짙은 다홍빛 홍조가 올랐으나 이번에는 부끄러움보다 분노로 눈이 이글거렸다—“손턴 씨와 저 사이의 사사로운 행동이었다고, 그래서 찾아와 그 일로 제게 감사할 수 있다고 여기시는 모양이군요. 신사라면 마땅히 알아보았을 것을 알아보지 못하시고요."], ["a gentleman,”", "그래요, 신사라면요.”"], ["she repeated, in allusion to their former conversation about that word, “that any woman, worthy of the name of woman, would come forward to shield, with her reverenced helplessness, a man in danger from the violence of numbers.”", "그녀는 전에 그 단어를 두고 나누었던 대화를 넌지시 가리키며 되풀이했다. “여자라는 이름에 값하는 여자라면 누구나 나서서, 존중받는 제 무력함으로 뭇사람의 폭력 앞에 놓인 사내를 감싸 주리라는 것을요.”"]], [["“And the gentleman thus rescued is forbidden the relief of thanks!”", "“그리고 그렇게 구원받은 신사는 감사할 위안조차 금지당하는군요!”"], ["he broke in contemptuously.", "그가 경멸조로 끼어들었다."], ["“I am a man.", "“저는 사내입니다."], ["I claim the right of expressing my feelings.”", "제 감정을 표현할 권리를 주장합니다.”"]], [["“And I yielded to the right; simply saying that you gave me pain by insisting upon it,”", "“그리고 저는 그 권리를 인정했어요. 다만 그것을 고집하셔서 제게 고통을 준다고 말씀드렸을 뿐이에요.”"], ["she replied, proudly.", "그녀가 도도하게 대답했다."], ["“But you seem to have imagined, that I was not merely guided by womanly instinct, but”—and here the passionate tears (kept down for long—struggled with vehemently) came up into her eyes and choked her voice—“but that I was prompted by some particular feeling for you—you!", "“하지만 손턴 씨는 제가 그저 여자다운 본능에 이끌린 것이 아니라”—그리고 여기서 오래 눌러 두고 격렬하게 싸워 온 격정의 눈물이 두 눈에 차올라 목소리를 막았다—“무슨 특별한 감정에 이끌려 그랬다고, 손턴 씨에 대한 감정 말이에요, 그렇게 상상하신 모양이군요!"], ["Why, there was not a man—not a poor desperate man in all that crowd—for whom I had not more sympathy—for whom I should not have done what little I could more heartily.”", "아니, 그 무리 속에 제가 더 큰 동정을 느끼지 않은 사내는 하나도 없었어요. 그 가엾고 절박한 사내들 가운데 제가 할 수 있는 그 작은 일을 더 진심으로 해 주지 않았을 사람은 하나도 없었다고요.”"]], [["“You may speak on, Miss Hale.", "“계속 말씀하십시오, 헤일 양."], ["I am aware of these misplaced sympathies of yours.", "저는 헤일 양의 그 엉뚱한 동정심들을 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["I now believe that it was only your innate sense of oppression—(yes; I, though a master, may be oppressed)—that made you act so nobly as you did.", "이제 저는 헤일 양이 그토록 고결하게 행동하게 만든 것이 억압에 대한 타고난 감각뿐이었다고 믿습니다. 그래요, 저도 공장주이지만 억압당할 수 있지요."], ["I know you despise me; allow me to say, it is because you do not understand me.”", "저를 경멸하시는 줄 압니다. 그것은 저를 이해하지 못하시기 때문이라고 말씀드리게 해 주십시오.”"]], [["“I do not care to understand,” she replied, taking hold of the table to steady herself; for she thought him cruel—as, indeed, he was—and she was weak with her indignation.", "“이해하고 싶지 않아요.” 그녀가 몸을 지탱하려고 탁자를 붙들며 대답했다. 그가 잔인하다고 여겼기 때문이었다. 실제로 그러했다. 그리고 그녀는 분노로 기운이 빠져 있었다."]], [["“No, I see you do not.", "“그렇겠지요, 그러고 싶지 않으신 게 보입니다."], ["You are unfair and unjust.”", "헤일 양은 불공평하고 부당합니다.”"]], [["Margaret compressed her lips.", "마거릿은 입술을 앙다물었다."], ["She would not speak in answer to such accusations.", "그런 비난에 대꾸할 생각이 없었다."], ["But, for all that—for all his savage words, he could have thrown himself at her feet, and kissed the hem of her garment.", "그러나 그 모든 것에도, 그의 그 모진 말들에도 불구하고 그는 그녀의 발치에 몸을 던져 옷자락 끝에 입 맞출 수도 있었다."], ["She did not speak; she did not move.", "그녀는 말하지 않았다. 움직이지도 않았다."], ["The tears of wounded pride fell hot and fast.", "상한 자존심의 눈물이 뜨겁고 빠르게 떨어졌다."], ["He waited awhile, longing for her to say something, even a taunt, to which he might reply.", "그는 그녀가 무슨 말이든, 대꾸할 수 있는 비아냥이라도 해 주기를 바라며 잠시 기다렸다."], ["But she was silent.", "그러나 그녀는 잠자코 있었다."], ["He took up his hat.", "그는 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“One word more.", "“한마디만 더 하지요."], ["You look as if you thought it tainted you to be loved by me.", "헤일 양은 제게 사랑받는 것이 스스로를 더럽히기라도 하는 것처럼 여기는 얼굴이시군요."], ["You cannot avoid it.", "그것을 피하실 수는 없습니다."], ["Nay, I, if I would, cannot cleanse you from it.", "아니, 제가 원한다 한들 그 더럽힘에서 헤일 양을 씻어 드릴 수는 없습니다."], ["But I would not if I could.", "그러나 할 수 있다 해도 그러지 않겠습니다."], ["I have never loved any woman before: my life has been too busy, my thoughts too much absorbed with other things.", "저는 이제껏 어떤 여자도 사랑한 적이 없습니다. 제 삶은 너무 바빴고, 제 생각은 다른 것들에 너무 사로잡혀 있었으니까요."], ["Now I love, and will love.", "이제 저는 사랑하고, 앞으로도 사랑할 것입니다."], ["But do not be afraid of too much expression on my part.”", "그러나 제 쪽에서 그것을 지나치게 드러낼까 두려워하지는 마십시오.”"]], [["“I am not afraid,”", "“두렵지 않아요.”"], ["she replied, lifting herself straight up.", "그녀가 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다."], ["“No one yet has ever dared to be impertinent to me, and no one ever shall.", "“여태 아무도 감히 제게 무례하게 군 적이 없고, 앞으로도 그럴 사람은 없을 거예요. 하지만 손턴 씨, 제 아버지께는 참 잘해 주셨어요.”"], ["But, Mr. Thornton, you have been very kind to my father,”", "그녀가 목소리와 태도를 더없이 여자다운 부드러움으로 온통 바꾸며 말했다."], ["said she, changing her whole tone and bearing to a most womanly softness.", "“우리 서로 화나게 하는 일은 그만두어요. 제발 그러지 말아요!”"], ["“Don’t let us go on making each other angry. Pray don’t!”", "그는 그녀의 말을 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["He took no notice of her words; he occupied himself in smoothing the nap of his hat with his coat-sleeve, for half a minute or so; and then, rejecting her offered hand, and making as if he did not see her grave look of regret, he turned abruptly away, and left the room.", "그는 반 분쯤 웃옷 소매로 모자의 보풀을 쓸어 다듬는 일에 몰두했다. 그러고는 그녀가 내민 손을 뿌리치고, 그녀의 진지한 뉘우침의 눈빛을 보지 못한 척하며 홱 돌아서서 방을 나갔다."], ["Margaret caught one glance at his face before he went.", "마거릿은 그가 나가기 전에 그의 얼굴을 얼핏 보았다."]], [["When he was gone she thought she had seen the gleam of washed tears in his eyes; and that turned her proud dislike into something different and kinder, if nearly as painful—self-reproach for having caused such mortification to any one.", "그가 가 버린 뒤, 그녀는 그의 눈에 씻긴 듯한 눈물의 반짝임을 보았다고 생각했다. 그러자 그녀의 도도한 반감이 무엇인가 다르고 한결 다정한 것으로, 그러나 거의 그만큼 괴로운 것으로 바뀌었다. 누구에게든 그런 굴욕을 안겼다는 데 대한 자책이었다."]], [["“But how could I help it?”", "“하지만 내가 어쩔 수 있었겠어?”"], ["asked she of herself.", "그녀가 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“I never liked him.", "“나는 그를 좋아한 적이 없어."], ["I was civil; but I took no trouble to conceal my indifference.", "예의는 지켰지만 내 무관심을 감추려 애쓰지도 않았지."], ["Indeed, I never thought about myself or him, so my manners must have shown the truth.", "정말이지 나는 나 자신에 대해서도 그에 대해서도 생각해 본 적이 없으니, 내 태도가 그 진실을 드러냈을 수밖에 없어."], ["All that yesterday, he might mistake.", "어제 있었던 그 모든 일은 그가 오해할 수도 있겠지."], ["But that is his fault, not mine.", "하지만 그건 그의 잘못이지 내 잘못이 아니야."], ["I would do it again, if need were, though it does lead me into all this shame and trouble.”", "필요하다면 나는 또 그렇게 할 거야. 그것이 이 온갖 부끄러움과 곤란으로 나를 끌고 간다 해도.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXV", "# 제25장"]], [["“Revenge may have her own; Roused discipline aloud proclaims their cause, And injured navies urge their broken laws.”", "“복수가 제 몫을 차지할지도 모른다. 깨어난 군율이 그들의 대의를 소리 높여 외치고, 상처 입은 함대들이 저희가 깨어진 법을 다그친다.”"], ["BYRON.", "— 바이런."]], [["Margaret began to wonder whether all offers were as unexpected beforehand,—as distressing at the time of their occurrence, as the two she had had.", "마거릿은 모든 청혼이 미리 짐작조차 못 할 만큼 뜻밖이고, 벌어지는 그 순간에는 이토록 괴로운 것인지 궁금해지기 시작했다."], ["An involuntary comparison between Mr. Lennox and Mr.", "자기가 받은 두 번의 청혼이 꼭 그러했으니 말이다."], ["Thornton arose in her mind. She had been sorry, that an expression of any other feeling than friendship had been lured out by circumstances from Henry Lennox.", "레녹스 씨와 손턴 씨를 견주어 보는 마음이 저도 모르게 일었다."], ["That regret was the predominant feeling, on the first occasion of her receiving a proposal.", "헨리 레녹스에게서 우정 아닌 다른 감정의 표현이 상황에 이끌려 나와 버린 것이 그때는 안타까웠다."], ["She had not felt so stunned—so impressed as she did now, when echoes of Mr.", "첫 청혼을 받았을 때 그녀를 지배한 감정은 그 안타까움이었다."], ["Thornton’s voice yet lingered about the room.", "그러나 지금처럼 얼떨떨하고 이토록 강한 자국이 남은 적은 없었다."], ["In Lennox’s case, he seemed for a moment to have slid over the boundary between friendship and love; and the instant afterwards, to regret it nearly as much as she did, although for different reasons.", "손턴 씨의 목소리의 여운이 아직 방 안을 맴돌고 있는 지금처럼은. 레녹스의 경우, 그는 한순간 우정과 사랑 사이의 경계를 미끄러져 넘은 듯했고, 바로 다음 순간에는 이유는 달랐으나 그녀 못지않게 그것을 후회하는 듯했다."], ["In Mr. Thornton’s case, as far as Margaret knew, there was no intervening stage of friendship.", "손턴 씨의 경우, 마거릿이 아는 한 그 사이에 우정이라는 단계는 없었다."], ["Their intercourse had been one continued series of opposition.", "두 사람의 교제는 하나로 이어진 대립의 연속이었다."], ["Their opinions clashed; and indeed, she had never perceived that he had cared for her opinions, as belonging to her, the individual.", "의견은 부딪쳤다. 그리고 실은, 그가 그녀의 의견을 그녀라는 한 사람에게 속한 것으로서 마음에 두었다고 느낀 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["As far as they defied his rock-like power of character, his passion-strength, he seemed to throw them off from him with contempt, until she felt the weariness of the exertion of making useless protests; and now, he had come, in this strange wild passionate way, to make known his love!", "그 의견들이 바위 같은 그의 성품의 힘에, 그 정열의 힘에 맞서는 한, 그는 경멸하듯 그것들을 제 몸에서 떨쳐 냈고, 마침내 그녀는 쓸데없는 항의를 애써 내놓는 일에 지쳐 버렸다. 그런데 이제 그가 이렇게 야릇하고 걷잡을 수 없는 정열의 방식으로 자기 사랑을 알리러 온 것이다!"], ["For, although at first it had struck her, that his offer was forced and goaded out of him by sharp compassion for the exposure she had made of herself,—which he, like others, might misunderstand—yet, even before he left the room,—and certainly not five minutes after, the clear conviction dawned upon her, shined bright upon her, that he did love her; that he had loved her; that he would love her.", "처음에는 그의 청혼이 그녀가 스스로를 드러내 보인 그 일—그도 남들처럼 오해했을지 모를 그 일—에 대한 날카로운 연민에 떠밀리고 내몰려 나온 것이라는 생각이 얼핏 들기는 했다. 그러나 그가 방을 나서기도 전에, 아니 적어도 나선 지 5분도 되지 않아, 그가 그녀를 사랑한다는, 사랑해 왔다는, 앞으로도 사랑하리라는 또렷한 확신이 그녀 위로 밝아 왔고 환하게 비쳐 들었다."], ["And she shrank and shuddered as under the fascination of some great power, repugnant to her whole previous life.", "그리하여 그녀는 지난 삶 전체와 어긋나는 어떤 커다란 힘에 홀린 듯 몸을 움츠리고 떨었다. 그녀는 슬며시 물러나 그의 생각으로부터 몸을 숨겼다."], ["She crept away, and hid from his idea.", "그러나 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["But it was of no use. To parody a line out of Fairfax’s Tasso—", "페어팩스가 옮긴 타소의 한 구절을 비틀어 말하자면—"]], [["“His strong idea wandered through her thought.”", "“그의 강한 모습이 그녀의 생각 속을 헤매고 다녔다.”"]], [["She disliked him the more for having mastered her inner will.", "그녀는 그가 자기 속마음의 의지를 사로잡았다는 이유로 그를 더 싫어했다."], ["How dared he say that he would love her still, even though she shook him off with contempt?", "자기가 경멸하며 뿌리쳤는데도 여전히 사랑하겠다고 감히 말하다니."], ["She wished she had spoken more—stronger.", "더 많이, 더 세게 말했어야 했다는 후회가 들었다."], ["Sharp, decisive speeches came thronging into her mind, now that it was too late to utter them.", "이제는 입 밖에 내기에 너무 늦어 버린 지금에야 날카롭고 단호한 말들이 마음속으로 몰려들었다. 그 만남이 남긴 깊은 자국은 꿈속의 공포와 같았다."], ["The deep impression made by the interview, was like that of a horror in a dream; that will not leave the room although we waken up, and rub our eyes, and force a stiff rigid smile upon our lips.", "잠에서 깨어 눈을 비비고 굳은 웃음을 억지로 입가에 지어 보아도 방을 떠나지 않는 그런 공포. 그것은 거기에, 바로 거기에 있다."], ["It is there—there, cowering and gibbering, with fixed ghastly eyes, in some corner of the chamber, listening to hear whether we dare to breathe of its presence to any one.", "방 어느 구석에 웅크린 채 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 내며, 붙박인 무시무시한 눈으로, 우리가 감히 그것이 거기 있다는 말을 누구에게 흘리지나 않을까 귀를 기울이면서. 그리고 우리는 감히 그러지 못한다."], ["And we dare not; poor cowards that we are!", "우리는 참으로 가엾은 겁쟁이인 것이다!"]], [["And so she shuddered away from the threat of his enduring love.", "그렇게 그녀는 그의 변치 않는 사랑이라는 위협에서 몸서리치며 물러났다."], ["What did he mean?", "그게 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Had she not the power to daunt him?", "그녀에게 그를 주눅 들게 할 힘이 없단 말인가?"], ["She would see.", "두고 볼 일이었다."], ["It was more daring than became a man to threaten her so.", "그런 식으로 그녀를 위협하다니 남자로서 지나치게 뻔뻔했다."], ["Did he ground it upon the miserable yesterday?", "그는 그 비참한 어제 일을 근거로 삼는 것인가?"], ["If need were, she would do the same to-morrow,—by a crippled beggar, willingly and gladly,—but by him, she would do it, just as bravely, in spite of his deductions, and the cold slime of woman’s impertinence.", "필요하다면 그녀는 내일도 똑같이 할 것이었다. 앉은뱅이 거지를 위해서라도 기꺼이 즐거이 그리할 것이고, 그를 위해서라도 그의 억측과 여자의 주제넘음이라는 차가운 진창을 무릅쓰고 꼭 그만큼 용감하게 그리할 것이었다."], ["She did it because it was right, and simple, and true to save where she could save; even to try to save.", "그녀가 그렇게 한 것은 그것이 옳고 단순하고 참되었기 때문이다. 구할 수 있는 곳에서 구하는 일, 구해 보려 애쓰기라도 하는 일이 그러했다."], ["“Fais ce que dois, advienne que pourra.”", "“해야 할 일을 하라, 그리고 오는 것은 오는 대로 두라.”"]], [["Hitherto she had not stirred from where he had left her; no outward circumstances had roused her out of the trance of thought in which she had been plunged by his last words, and by the look of his deep intent passionate eyes, as their flames had made her own fall before them.", "그때까지 그녀는 그가 두고 간 그 자리에서 한 걸음도 움직이지 않았다. 그의 마지막 말에, 그리고 그 불꽃 앞에서 그녀 자신의 눈이 떨구어지게 만들었던 그 깊고 골똘하고 정열적인 눈빛에 빠져든 생각의 혼미에서 그녀를 흔들어 깨울 바깥일이 아무것도 없었다."], ["She went to the window, and threw it open, to dispel the oppression which hung around her.", "그녀는 창가로 가서 창을 활짝 열어 자기를 에워싼 답답함을 몰아냈다."], ["Then she went and opened the door, with a sort of impetuous wish to shake off the recollection of the past hour, in the company of others, or in active exertion.", "그러고는 문으로 가서 문을 열었다. 다른 사람들과 어울리든 몸을 부지런히 놀리든 지난 한 시간의 기억을 떨쳐 버리고 싶다는 조급한 마음이 일었던 것이다."], ["But all was profoundly hushed in the noonday stillness of a house, where an invalid catches the unrefreshing sleep that is denied to the night-hours.", "그러나 밤 시간에는 허락되지 않는 개운찮은 잠을 병자가 겨우 붙드는 집의 한낮 고요 속에, 모든 것이 깊이 잠들어 있었다."], ["Margaret would not be alone.", "마거릿은 혼자 있고 싶지 않았다."], ["What should she do?", "무엇을 해야 하나?"], ["“Go and see Bessy Higgins, of course,”", "“물론 베시 히긴스를 보러 가야지.”"], ["thought she, as the recollection of the message sent the night before flashed into her mind.", "어젯밤 전해진 전갈이 문득 떠오르며 그녀는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["And away she went.", "그리고 곧 집을 나섰다."]], [["When she got there, she found Bessy lying on the settle, moved close to the fire, though the day was sultry and oppressive.", "그곳에 이르니 베시가 긴 나무의자에 누워 있었다. 날이 후텁지근하고 숨 막히는데도 의자는 불 가까이로 옮겨져 있었다."], ["She was laid down quite flat, as if resting languidly after some paroxysm of pain.", "그녀는 어떤 고통의 발작 뒤에 기운 없이 쉬는 사람처럼 아주 반듯이 눕혀져 있었다."], ["Margaret felt sure she ought to have the greater freedom of breathing which a more sitting posture would procure; and, without a word, she raised her up, and so arranged the pillows, that Bessy was more at ease, though very languid.", "마거릿은 좀 더 앉은 자세를 취하면 숨쉬기가 한결 자유로우리라고 확신했다. 그래서 아무 말 없이 그녀를 일으켜 베개를 고쳐 놓아 주었고, 베시는 몹시 기운은 없어도 한결 편안해졌다."]], [["“I thought I should na’ ha’ seen yo’ again,”", "“다시는 댁을 못 볼 줄 알았구먼요.”"], ["said she, at last, looking wistfully in Margaret’s face.", "마침내 베시가 마거릿의 얼굴을 애타게 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["“I’m afraid you’re much worse.", "“많이 나빠지신 것 같아 걱정이에요. 하지만 어제는 올 수가 없었어요."], ["But I could not have come yesterday, my mother was so ill—for many reasons,”", "어머니가 너무 편찮으셨거든요. 여러 가지 사정도 있었고요.”"], ["said Margaret, colouring.", "마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“Yo’d m’appen think I went beyond my place in sending Mary for yo’.", "“메리를 보내 댁을 부른 게 제 분수에 넘친 짓이라 여기실지도 모르겠구먼요."], ["But the wraglin’ and the loud voices had just torn me to pieces, and I thought when father left, oh!", "그렇지만 그 다투는 소리하고 큰소리가 저를 갈가리 찢어 놓아서, 아버지가 나가고 나니 이런 생각이 들었지 뭐예요."], ["if I could just hear her voice, reading me some words o’ peace and promise, I could die away into the silence and rest o’ God, just as a baby is hushed up to sleep by its mother’s lullaby.”", "아, 그저 그이 목소리로 평화와 약속의 말씀을 읽어 주는 걸 들을 수만 있다면, 아기가 어미 자장가에 잠들듯이 나도 하느님의 고요와 안식 속으로 스러져 갈 수 있을 텐데 하고요.”"]], [["“Shall I read you a chapter, now?”", "“지금 한 장 읽어 드릴까요?”"]], [["“Ay, do!", "“그럼요, 읽어 주세요! 처음에는 아마 뜻은 귀에 안 들어올 거예요."], ["M’appen I shan’t listen to th’ sense, at first; it will seem far away—but when yo’ come to words I like—to th’ comforting texts—it’ll seem close in my ear, and going through me as it were.”", "아주 멀리서 들리는 것 같겠지요. 그러다가 제가 좋아하는 말씀이 나오면, 위로가 되는 구절이 나오면, 바로 귓가에 대고 하는 것처럼 들리면서 제 속을 뚫고 지나갈 거구먼요.”"]], [["Margaret began. Bessy tossed to and fro.", "마거릿이 읽기 시작했다."], ["If, by an effort, she attended for one moment, it seemed as though she were convulsed into double restlessness the next.", "베시는 이리저리 몸을 뒤척였다. 애를 써서 한순간 귀를 기울이면, 그다음 순간에는 배로 안절부절못하는 몸부림에 사로잡히는 듯했다."], ["At last, she burst out: “Don’t go on reading.", "마침내 그녀가 터뜨렸다. “그만 읽으세요."], ["It’s no use.", "소용없구먼요."], ["I’m blaspheming all the time in my mind, wi’ thinking angrily on what canna be helped.—Yo’d hear of th’ riot, m’appen, yesterday at Marlborough Mills?", "어쩔 도리 없는 일을 두고 성이 나서 생각하느라 저는 내내 마음속으로 하느님을 욕하고 있는걸요. 어제 말버러 공장에서 난리가 났다는 이야기는 들으셨겠지요?"], ["Thornton’s factory, yo’ know.”", "손턴네 공장 말이에요.”"]], [["“Your father was not there, was he?”", "“아버지는 거기 안 계셨지요?”"], ["said Margaret colouring deeply.", "마거릿이 얼굴을 몹시 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“Not he.", "“아버지는 아니지요."], ["He’d ha’ given his right hand if it had never come to pass.", "그런 일이 아예 없었다면 오른손이라도 내주었을 사람이에요."], ["It’s that that’s fretting me.", "저를 애태우는 게 바로 그거예요."], ["He’s fairly knocked down in his mind by it.", "아버지는 그 일로 마음이 아주 무너져 버렸어요."], ["It’s no use telling him, fools will always break out o’ bounds.", "바보들은 언제나 선을 넘게 마련이라고 말해 봐야 소용없구먼요."], ["Yo’ never saw a man so downhearted as he is.”", "저이처럼 낙심한 사람은 여태 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“But why?”", "“그런데 왜요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“I don’t understand.”", "“저는 잘 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Why, yo’ see, he’s a committee-man on this special strike.", "“그게요, 아버지가 이번 파업의 위원이거든요."], ["Th’ Union appointed him because, though I say it as shouldn’t say it, he’s reckoned a deep chap, and true to th’ back-bone.", "조합에서 아버지를 뽑았지요. 제 입으로 할 말은 아니지만, 아버지는 속이 깊은 사람으로 통하고 뼛속까지 정직하다고들 하니까요."], ["And he and t’other committee-men laid their plans.", "그래서 아버지하고 다른 위원들이 계획을 세웠어요."], ["They were to hou’d together through thick and thin; what the major part thought, t’others were to think, whether they would or no.", "무슨 일이 있어도 한 덩어리로 뭉치자, 다수가 생각하는 대로 나머지도 좋든 싫든 따라 생각하자고요. 그리고 무엇보다 나라의 법을 거스르는 일은 절대 없어야 한다고 했구먼요."], ["And above all there was to be no going again the law of the land.", "사람들이 보기에 저희가 벙어리처럼 참으면서 굶주리며 버틴다면 세상 인심이 저희 편을 들 테지만, 싸우고 부딪치는 소란이 한 번이라도 나면, 그게 설령 파업 파괴자를 상대로 한 것이라도 다 끝장이라는 걸 저희는 전에도 여러 번 겪어서 알거든요."], ["Folk would go with them if they saw them striving and starving wi’ dumb patience; but if there was once any noise o’ fighting and struggling—even wi’ knobsticks—all was up, as they know by th’ experience of many, and many a time before.", "파업 파괴자들한테는 말을 붙여 보고, 달래 보고, 이치를 따져 보고, 어쩌면 물러가라고 으름장을 놓을 수는 있어도, 무슨 일이 닥치든 위원회는 조합원 모두에게 필요하다면 주먹 한 번 휘두르지 말고 그냥 누워서 죽으라고 명령했어요. 그래야 세상 사람들을 제 편으로 확실히 끌어올 수 있다고 셈한 거지요."], ["They would try and get speech o’ th’ knobsticks, and coax ’em, and reason wi’ ’em, and m’appen warn ’em off; but whatever came, the Committee charged all members o’ th’ Union to lie down and die, if need were, without striking a blow; and then they reckoned they were sure o’ carrying th’ public with them.", "게다가 위원회는 저희 요구가 옳다는 걸 알고 있었고, 옳은 것이 그른 것과 뒤섞여서 사람들이 갈라낼 수 없게 되는 걸 바라지 않았어요. 댁이 약가루 타 먹으라고 주신 그 젤리에서 제가 가루를 갈라낼 수 없는 것처럼요."], ["And besides all that, Committee knew they were right in their demand, and they didn’t want to have right all mixed up wi’ wrong, till folk can’t separate it, no more nor I can the physic-powder from th’ jelly yo’ gave me to mix it in; jelly is much the biggest, but powder tastes it all through.", "젤리가 훨씬 많은데도 가루 맛이 온통 배어 있잖아요. 자, 이 일을 길게도 이야기했구먼요."], ["Well, I’ve told yo’ at length about this’n, but I am tired out.", "그런데 저는 기진맥진했어요. 댁이 한번 생각해 보세요."], ["Yo’ just think for yor’sel, what it mun be for father to have a’ his work undone, and by such a fool as Boucher, who must needs go right again the orders of Committee, and ruin th’ strike, just as bad as if he meant to be a Judas.", "아버지로서는 제 일이 몽땅 헛것이 되어 버린 게, 그것도 바우처 같은 바보 때문에 그리된 게 어떻겠나 하고요. 그 사람은 굳이 위원회 명령을 정면으로 거스르고 파업을 망쳐 놓았으니, 아예 유다 노릇을 하려고 작정한 거나 마찬가지지 뭐예요."], ["Eh! but father giv’d it him last night!", "어이구!"], ["He went so far as to say, he’d go and tell police where they might find th’ ringleader o’ th’ riot; he’d give him up to th’ mill-owners to do what they would wi’ him.", "어젯밤에 아버지가 그 사람한테 퍼부었어요! 난리를 일으킨 주모자가 어디 있는지 경찰에 가서 일러바치겠다고까지 하더구먼요."], ["He’d show the world that th’ real leaders o’ the strike were not such as Boucher, but steady thoughtful men; good hands, and good citizens, who were friendly to law and judgment, and would uphold order; who only wanted their right wage, and wouldn’t work, even though they starved, till they got ’em; but who would ne’er injure property or life.", "공장주들한테 넘겨서 마음대로 하게 두겠다고요. 파업의 진짜 우두머리는 바우처 같은 자가 아니라 진중하고 생각 깊은 사람들이라는 걸, 법과 재판을 존중하고 질서를 지킬 좋은 직공이자 좋은 시민이라는 걸, 그저 제 몫의 삯을 바랄 뿐이고 그걸 받기 전에는 굶어 죽더라도 일하지 않겠지만 남의 재산이나 목숨은 결코 해치지 않을 사람들이라는 걸 세상에 보여 주겠다고요."], ["For,”", "왜냐하면요.”"], ["dropping her voice, “they do say, that Boucher threw a stone at Thornton’s sister, that welly killed her.”", "그녀가 목소리를 낮추었다. “바우처가 손턴 씨 누이한테 돌을 던져서 하마터면 죽일 뻔했다는 말이 돌거든요.”"]], [["“That’s not true,”", "“그건 사실이 아니에요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“It was not Boucher that threw the stone”—she went first red, then white.", "“돌을 던진 건 바우처가 아니었어요.” 그녀는 처음에 얼굴이 붉어졌다가 이내 하얘졌다."]], [["“Yo’d be there then, were yo’?”", "“그럼 댁이 거기 있었나 봐요?”"], ["asked Bessie languidly: for indeed, she had spoken with many pauses, as if speech was unusually difficult to her.", "베시가 힘없이 물었다. 실은 그녀는 말하는 것이 유난히 힘든 듯 여러 번 말을 끊어 가며 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["Never mind.", "그건 됐어요."], ["Go on.", "계속하세요."], ["Only it was not Boucher that threw the stone.", "다만 돌을 던진 건 바우처가 아니었어요."], ["But what did he answer to your father?”", "그런데 그 사람이 아버지께 뭐라고 대답했나요?”"]], [["“He did na’ speak words.", "“말은 없었어요."], ["He were all in such a tremble wi’ spent passion, I could na’ bear to look at him.", "정열이 다 타 버린 사람처럼 온몸을 어찌나 떨던지 저는 차마 쳐다볼 수가 없었구먼요."], ["I heard his breath coming quick, and at one time I thought he were sobbing.", "숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬는 소리가 들렸고, 한때는 흐느끼는 줄 알았어요."], ["But when father said he’d give him up to police, he gave a great cry, and struck father on th’ face wi’ his closed fist, and he off like lightning.", "그런데 아버지가 경찰에 넘기겠다고 하니까 그 사람이 크게 소리를 지르면서 주먹으로 아버지 얼굴을 치고는 번개같이 달아나 버렸어요. 아버지는 처음엔 그 주먹에 얼이 빠져 있었지요."], ["Father were stunned wi’ the blow at first, for all Boucher were weak wi’ passion and wi’ clemming.", "바우처가 성이 다 빠지고 굶주려서 힘도 없는 사람이었는데도요. 아버지는 잠깐 앉아서 손으로 눈을 가리더니 문 쪽으로 가더구먼요."], ["He sat down a bit, and put his hand afore his eyes; and then made for th’ door.", "어디서 그런 힘이 났는지 모르겠는데, 저는 몸을 의자에서 던지듯 일으켜 아버지한테 매달렸어요. ‘아버지, 아버지!’ 하고 제가 말했지요."], ["I dunno’ where I got strength, but I threw mysel’ oh th’ settle and clung to him.", "‘그 불쌍한 굶주린 사람을 절대 고자질하시면 안 돼요. 안 하겠다고 말할 때까지 저는 절대 안 놓을 거예요.’ 그러자 아버지가 ‘바보 같은 소리 마라’ 하시더군요."], ["‘Father, father!’ said I.", "‘사람들은 대개 행동보다 말이 앞서는 법이다."], ["‘Thou’ll never go peach on that poor clemmed man.", "나는 그놈을 경찰에 이를 생각은 없었다."], ["I’ll never leave go on thee, till thou sayst thou wunnot.’ ‘Dunnot be a fool,’ says he, ‘words come readier than deeds to most men.", "하기야 맹세코, 그놈은 그래 싸지. 누가 다른 사람이 그 궂은일을 맡아서 그놈을 잡아넣었다 해도 나는 개의치 않았을 거다."], ["I never thought o’ telling th’ police on him; though by G—, he deserves it, and I should na’ ha’ minded if some one else had done the dirty work, and got him clapped up.", "그렇지만 이제 그놈이 나를 쳤으니 더더욱 그럴 수가 없구나. 그건 내 싸움을 남의 손에 맡기는 짓이 될 테니까."], ["But now he has strucken me, I could do it less nor ever, for it would be getting other men to take up my quarrel.", "다만 언제고 그놈이 이 굶주림을 이겨 내고 몸이 성해지면, 그놈하고 나하고 한 판 제대로 붙을 거다. 발길질까지 곁들여서 말이다."], ["But if ever he gets well o’er this clemming, and is in good condition, he and I’ll have an up and down fight, purring an’ a’, and I’ll see what I can do for him.’ And so father shook me off,—for indeed I was low and faint enough, and his face was all clay white, where it weren’t bloody, and turned me sick to look at.", "내가 그놈한테 무얼 해 줄 수 있는지 그때 보자꾸나.’ 그러고는 아버지가 저를 떨쳐 냈어요. 사실 저도 기운이 다 빠져 까무러칠 지경이었고, 아버지 얼굴은 피가 묻지 않은 데는 온통 흙빛으로 하얘서 보기만 해도 속이 메스꺼웠구먼요."], ["And I know not if I slept or waked, or were in a dead swoon, till Mary come in; and I telled her to fetch yo’ to me.", "그러고 나서 메리가 들어올 때까지 제가 잤는지 깼는지 아주 정신을 잃었는지 알 수가 없어요. 그래서 메리한테 댁을 모셔 오라고 했지요."], ["And now dunnot talk to me, but just read out th’ chapter.", "자, 이제 저한테 말 걸지 마시고 그 장을 읽어만 주세요."], ["I’m easier in my mind for having spit it out; but I want some thoughts of the world that’s far away to take the weary taste of it out of my mouth.", "속에 있는 걸 다 뱉어 내니 마음은 좀 편해졌구먼요. 그렇지만 이 지긋지긋한 뒷맛을 입에서 씻어 낼 만한, 저 멀리 있는 세상 생각이 좀 있었으면 해요."], ["Read me not a sermon chapter, but a story chapter; they’ve pictures in them, which I see when my eyes are shut.", "설교 같은 장 말고 이야기가 있는 장을 읽어 주세요. 거기엔 그림이 들어 있어서 눈을 감으면 보이거든요."], ["Read about the New Heavens, and the New Earth; and m’appen I’ll forget this.”", "새 하늘과 새 땅 이야기를 읽어 주세요. 그러면 어쩌면 이걸 잊을 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["Margaret read in her soft low voice.", "마거릿이 부드럽고 낮은 목소리로 읽었다. 베시는 눈을 감고 있었지만 한동안 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Though Bessy’s eyes were shut, she was listening for some time, for the moisture of tears gathered heavy on her eyelashes.", "눈물의 물기가 속눈썹에 무겁게 맺혔기 때문이다. 마침내 그녀는 잠이 들었다."], ["At last she slept; with many starts, and muttered pleadings.", "여러 번 흠칫거리고 무어라 애원하는 말을 중얼거리면서. 마거릿은 그녀에게 이불을 덮어 주고 자리를 떴다."], ["Margaret covered her up, and left her, for she had an uneasy consciousness that she might be wanted at home, and yet, until now, it seemed cruel to leave the dying girl.", "집에서 자기를 찾을지도 모른다는 불안한 마음이 있었기 때문이다. 그러면서도 그때까지는 죽어 가는 처녀를 두고 떠나는 것이 잔인하게 여겨졌었다."]], [["Mrs. Hale was in the drawing-room on her daughter’s return.", "마거릿이 돌아오니 헤일 부인이 응접실에 있었다."], ["It was one of her better days, and she was full of praises of the water-bed.", "그날은 부인의 상태가 좋은 날이었고, 부인은 물침대 칭찬으로 가득했다."], ["It had been more like the beds at Sir John Beresford’s than anything she had slept on since.", "그것은 그 뒤로 자 본 어떤 잠자리보다도 존 베러스퍼드 경 댁의 침대와 비슷했다."], ["She did not know how it was, but people seemed to have lost the art of making the same kind of beds as they used to do in her youth.", "어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만 요즘 사람들은 제 젊은 시절에 만들던 것과 같은 침대를 만드는 재주를 잃어버린 모양이라고 했다."], ["One would think it was easy enough; there was the same kind of feathers to be had, and yet somehow, till this last night she did not know when she had had a good sound resting sleep.", "그리 어려운 일도 아닐 것 같은데 말이다. 깃털도 그때와 같은 것을 구할 수 있는데 어쩐 일인지, 어젯밤에야 비로소 언제 그렇게 푹 자 보았는지 기억나지 않을 만큼 깊은 잠을 잤다는 것이었다."]], [["Mr.", "헤일 씨는 옛날 깃털 침대의 좋은 점 가운데 얼마쯤은 쉬는 맛을 돋우어 주던 젊음의 활기 덕이 아니겠느냐고 넌지시 말했다."], ["Hale suggested, that something of the merits of the feather-beds of former days might be attributed to the activity of youth, which gave a relish to rest; but this idea was not kindly received by his wife.", "그러나 이 생각은 아내에게 곱게 받아들여지지 않았다."]], [["“No, indeed, Mr.", "“아니에요, 헤일 씨."], ["Hale, it was those beds at Sir John’s.", "그건 존 경 댁의 그 침대들이었어요."], ["Now, Margaret, you’re young enough, and go about in the day; are the beds comfortable?", "자, 마거릿, 너는 젊고 낮에는 이리저리 돌아다니지 않니. 침대가 편하더냐?"], ["I appeal to you.", "네게 물어보마."], ["Do they give you a feeling of perfect repose when you lie down upon them; or rather, don’t you toss about, and try in vain to find an easy position, and wake in the morning as tired as when you went to bed?”", "누웠을 때 온전히 쉬는 느낌이 들더냐? 아니면 오히려 이리 뒤척 저리 뒤척 하면서 편한 자세를 찾으려다 헛수고하고, 아침에는 잠자리에 들 때만큼 지쳐서 깨지 않더냐?”"]], [["Margaret laughed.", "마거릿이 웃었다."], ["“To tell the truth, mamma, I’ve never thought about my bed at all, what kind it is.", "“사실대로 말씀드리면 저는 제 침대가 어떤 것인지 생각해 본 적이 한 번도 없어요, 어머니."], ["I’m so sleepy at night, that if I only lie down anywhere, I nap off directly.", "밤이면 어찌나 졸린지 아무 데나 눕기만 하면 곧바로 곯아떨어지거든요."], ["So I don’t think I’m a competent witness.", "그러니 저는 제대로 된 증인이 못 될 것 같아요."], ["But then, you know, I never had the opportunity of trying Sir John Beresford’s beds.", "게다가 아시다시피 저는 존 베러스퍼드 경 댁의 침대를 써 볼 기회가 아예 없었잖아요."], ["I never was at Oxenham.”", "저는 옥슨햄에 가 본 적이 없는걸요.”"]], [["“Were you not?", "“그랬니? 아, 그렇구나!"], ["Oh, no!", "그래 맞다."], ["to be sure.", "내가 데리고 간 건 가엾은 우리 프레드였지."], ["It was poor darling Fred I took with me, I remember.", "이제 기억나는구나. 결혼한 뒤로 옥슨햄에 간 건 딱 한 번, 네 쇼 이모 결혼식 때였다."], ["I only went to Oxenham once after I was married,—to your Aunt Shaw’s wedding; and poor little Fred was the baby then.", "그때 가엾은 어린 프레드는 갓난아기였지. 딕슨이 몸종에서 유모로 바뀌는 걸 싫어했던 걸 나도 안다."], ["And I know Dixon did not like changing from lady’s maid to nurse, and I was afraid that if I took her near her old home, and amongst her own people, she might want to leave me.", "그래서 그 애를 옛집 가까이, 제 고향 사람들 사이로 데려가면 나를 떠나겠다고 할까 봐 겁이 났었지. 그런데 가엾은 아기가 옥슨햄에서 이가 나느라 앓았단다."], ["But poor baby was taken ill at Oxenham, with his teething; and, what with my being a great deal with Anna just before her marriage, and not being very strong myself, Dixon had more of the charge of him than she ever had before; and it made her so fond of him, and she was so proud when he would turn away from every one and cling to her, that I don’t believe she ever thought of leaving me again; though it was very different from what she’d been accustomed to.", "게다가 나는 결혼을 앞둔 애나 곁에 오래 붙어 있어야 했고 나 자신도 그리 튼튼하지 못했으니, 딕슨이 전에 없이 그 애를 도맡게 되었지. 그러다 보니 그 애를 어찌나 예뻐하게 되었는지, 프레드가 다른 사람은 다 마다하고 저한테만 매달리면 얼마나 자랑스러워하던지, 그 뒤로는 나를 떠날 생각은 다시 하지 않았을 게다."], ["Poor Fred! Everybody loved him.", "그전에 익숙하던 일과는 아주 딴판이었는데도 말이야."], ["He was born with the gift of winning hearts.", "가엾은 프레드! 다들 그 애를 사랑했지."], ["It makes me think very badly of Captain Reid when I know that he disliked my own dear boy.", "그 애는 사람 마음을 얻는 재주를 타고났단다. 리드 함장이 내 사랑하는 아들을 싫어했다는 걸 알고 나면 그 사람을 아주 나쁘게 여기게 돼."], ["I think it a certain proof he had a bad heart.", "나는 그게 그 사람 심보가 나빴다는 확실한 증거라고 본다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Your poor father, Margaret.", "네 가엾은 아버지 좀 보아라, 마거릿."], ["He has left the room.", "방을 나가 버리셨구나."], ["He can’t bear to hear Fred spoken of.”", "프레드 이야기가 나오는 걸 못 견디시니까.”"]], [["“I love to hear about him, mamma.", "“저는 오빠 이야기 듣는 게 좋아요, 어머니."], ["Tell me all you like; you never can tell me too much.", "하고 싶은 이야기는 다 해 주세요. 아무리 많이 해 주셔도 지나치지 않아요."], ["Tell me what he was like as a baby.”", "오빠가 아기였을 때 어땠는지 이야기해 주세요.”"]], [["“Why, Margaret, you must not be hurt, but he was much prettier than you were.", "“저런, 마거릿, 서운해하면 안 된다만, 그 애가 너보다 훨씬 예뻤단다. 딕슨의 품에 안긴 너를 처음 보았을 때 내가 이렇게 말했던 게 기억난다."], ["I remember, when I first saw you in Dixon’s arms, I said, ‘Dear, what an ugly little thing!’ And she said, ‘It’s not every child that’s like Master Fred, bless him!’ Dear!", "‘어머나, 참 못생긴 아기로구나!’ 그러자 딕슨이 이러더구나. ‘아이가 다 프레드 도련님 같을 수야 있나요, 그 도련님께 복이 있기를!’ 어머나!"], ["how well I remember it.", "참 또렷하게도 기억나는구나."], ["Then I could have had Fred in my arms every minute of the day, and his cot was close by my bed; and now, now—Margaret—I don’t know where my boy is, and sometimes I think I shall never see him again.”", "그때는 하루 종일 언제든 프레드를 품에 안을 수 있었고, 그 애 아기 침대가 내 침대 바로 옆에 있었지. 그런데 지금은, 지금은, 마거릿, 내 아들이 어디 있는지도 모르고, 어떤 때는 다시는 그 애를 못 보겠구나 싶단다.”"]], [["Margaret sat down by her mother’s sofa on a little stool, and softly took hold of her hand, caressing it and kissing it, as if to comfort.", "마거릿은 어머니의 소파 옆 낮은 발판에 앉아 어머니의 손을 가만히 붙잡고, 위로라도 하려는 듯 쓰다듬고 입을 맞추었다."], ["Mrs. Hale cried without restraint.", "헤일 부인은 거리낌 없이 울었다."], ["At last, she sat straight, stiff up on the sofa, and turning round to her daughter, she said with tearful, almost solemn earnestness, “Margaret, if I can get better,—if God lets me have a chance of recovery, it must be through seeing my son Frederick once more.", "마침내 부인은 소파에 꼿꼿이 몸을 세워 앉더니 딸 쪽으로 돌아앉아, 눈물 어린, 거의 엄숙하다 할 만한 간절함으로 말했다. “마거릿, 내가 나을 수 있다면, 하느님이 내게 회복할 기회를 주신다면, 그건 반드시 내 아들 프레더릭을 한 번 더 보는 일을 통해서일 게다."], ["It will waken up all the poor springs of health left in me.”", "그러면 내게 남은 그 가엾은 건강의 샘이 모두 깨어날 거야.”"]], [["She paused, and seemed to try and gather strength for something more yet to be said.", "부인은 말을 멈추고, 아직 해야 할 무언가를 위해 힘을 그러모으려는 듯했다."], ["Her voice was choked as she went on—was quavering as with the contemplation of some strange, yet closely-present idea.", "말을 이어 갈 때 부인의 목소리는 메어 있었고, 야릇하면서도 바싹 다가와 있는 어떤 생각을 헤아리기라도 하듯 떨렸다."]], [["“And, Margaret, if I am to die—if I am one of those appointed to die before many weeks are over—I must see my child first.", "“그리고 마거릿, 내가 죽어야 한다면, 몇 주도 지나기 전에 죽도록 정해진 사람들 가운데 하나라면, 나는 먼저 내 아이를 보아야 한다."], ["I cannot think how it must be managed; but I charge you, Margaret, as you yourself hope for comfort in your last illness, bring him to me that I may bless him.", "그 일을 어떻게 해내야 할지는 나도 모르겠구나. 그렇지만 마거릿, 네가 마지막 병상에서 위로받기를 바라거든, 내 간절히 부탁하니 그 애를 내게 데려와 내가 축복해 줄 수 있게 해 다오."], ["Only for five minutes, Margaret.", "5분만이라도 좋다, 마거릿."], ["There could be no danger in five minutes.", "5분이면 위험할 것도 없겠지."], ["Oh, Margaret, let me see him before I die!”", "아, 마거릿, 내가 죽기 전에 그 애를 보게 해 다오!”"]], [["Margaret did not think of anything that might be utterly unreasonable in this speech: we do not look for reason or logic in the passionate entreaties of those who are sick unto death; we are stung with the recollection of a thousand slighted opportunities of fulfilling the wishes of those who will soon pass away from among us: and do they ask us for the future happiness of our lives, we lay it at their feet, and will it away from us.", "마거릿은 이 말에 도무지 이치에 닿지 않는 구석이 있으리라는 생각은 하지 않았다. 죽을병에 든 이들의 정열적인 간청에서 우리는 이치나 논리를 찾지 않는다."], ["But this wish of Mrs.", "곧 우리 곁을 떠날 이들의 바람을 들어줄 기회를 수없이 흘려보냈다는 기억이 우리를 찌른다."], ["Hale’s was so natural, so just, so right to both parties, that Margaret felt as if, on Frederick’s account as well as on her mother’s, she ought to overlook all intermediate chances of danger, and pledge herself to do everything in her power for its realization.", "그리고 그들이 우리 삶의 앞날의 행복을 달라고 한다면, 우리는 그것을 그들 발치에 내려놓고 우리에게서 떠나보내 버린다. 그러나 헤일 부인의 이 바람은 너무나 자연스럽고 너무나 마땅하고 두 사람 모두에게 너무나 옳은 것이어서, 마거릿은 어머니를 위해서만이 아니라 프레더릭을 위해서도 그 사이에 놓인 온갖 위험의 가능성을 눈감아 주고, 그것을 이루기 위해 제 힘이 닿는 모든 일을 하겠다고 스스로 약속해야 할 것 같았다."], ["The large, pleading, dilated eyes were fixed upon her wistfully, steady in their gaze, though the poor white lips quivered like those of a child.", "크게 뜬 채 애원하는 눈이 애타게 그녀에게 붙박여 흔들림 없이 바라보고 있었다. 그 가엾은 흰 입술은 어린아이의 입술처럼 떨렸지만."], ["Margaret gently rose up and stood opposite to her frail mother; so that she might gather the secure fulfilment of her wish from the calm steadiness of her daughter’s face.", "마거릿은 가만히 일어나 연약한 어머니와 마주 섰다. 어머니가 딸의 얼굴에 어린 차분한 굳건함에서 제 소원이 반드시 이루어지리라는 확신을 얻을 수 있도록."]], [["“Mamma, I will write to-night, and tell Frederick what you say.", "“어머니, 오늘 밤에 편지를 써서 프레더릭 오빠에게 어머니 말씀을 전할게요."], ["I am as sure that he will come directly to us, as I am sure of my life.", "오빠가 곧바로 우리에게 오리라는 걸 저는 제 목숨만큼이나 확신해요."], ["Be easy, mamma, you shall see him as far as anything earthly can be promised.”", "마음 놓으세요, 어머니. 이 세상에서 약속할 수 있는 한 어머니는 오빠를 보게 되실 거예요.”"]], [["“You will write to-night?", "“오늘 밤에 쓴다고? 아, 마거릿!"], ["Oh, Margaret!", "우편은 5시에 나가는데, 그 편으로 부치겠지?"], ["the post goes out at five—you will write by it, won’t you?", "응? 내게 남은 시간이 너무 적구나."], ["I have so few hours left—I feel dear, as if I should not recover, though sometimes your father over-persuades me into hoping; you will write directly, won’t you?", "얘야, 네 아버지가 이따금 나를 설득해 희망을 갖게 하시지만, 나는 아무래도 낫지 못할 것 같은 기분이 든다. 곧바로 쓰겠지, 그렇지?"], ["Don’t lose a single post; for just by that very post I may miss him.”", "우편 한 번도 놓치지 말아라. 바로 그 한 번 때문에 그 애를 놓칠 수도 있으니.”"]], [["“But, mamma, papa is out.”", "“그렇지만 어머니, 아버지가 나가셨는걸요.”"]], [["“Papa is out!", "“아버지가 나가셨다고!"], ["and what then?", "그래서 어떻다는 게냐?"], ["Do you mean that he would deny me this last wish, Margaret?", "아버지가 이 마지막 소원을 내게 거절하시리라는 뜻이냐, 마거릿?"], ["Why, I should not be ill, be dying—if he had not taken me away from Helstone, to this unhealthy, smoky, sunless place.”", "애초에 나를 헬스톤에서 이 병들고 매캐하고 볕 없는 곳으로 데려오지만 않았어도 내가 앓지도, 죽어 가지도 않았을 것을.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma!”", "“아, 어머니!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Yes; it is so, indeed.", "“그래, 정말 그렇다."], ["He knows it himself; he has said so many a time.", "그이도 스스로 알고 있어. 여러 번 그렇게 말했으니까."], ["He would do anything for me; you don’t mean he would refuse me this last wish—prayer, if you will.", "그이는 나를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 사람이다. 이 마지막 소원을, 원한다면 마지막 기도라 해도 좋겠구나, 그것을 거절하리라는 뜻은 아니겠지."], ["And, indeed, Margaret, the longing to see Frederick stands between me and God.", "그리고 마거릿, 참말이지 프레더릭을 보고 싶은 그리움이 나와 하느님 사이를 가로막고 있단다."], ["I cannot pray till I have this one thing; indeed, I cannot.", "이 한 가지가 이루어지기 전에는 기도를 할 수가 없구나. 정말이지 할 수가 없어."], ["Don’t lose time, dear, dear Margaret.", "시간을 놓치지 말아라, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 마거릿."], ["Write by this very next post.", "바로 다음 우편에 부쳐라."], ["Then he may be here—here in twenty-two days!", "그러면 그 애가 여기 올 수 있어."], ["For he is sure to come.", "22일이면 여기 올 수 있단다!"], ["No cords or chains can keep him.", "그 애는 틀림없이 올 테니까."], ["In twenty-two days I shall see my boy.”", "어떤 밧줄도 사슬도 그 애를 붙들지 못한다."], ["She fell back, and for a short time she took no notice of the fact that Margaret sat motionless, her hand shading her eyes.", "22일이면 내 아들을 보는 거야.” 부인은 도로 몸을 눕혔고, 잠시 동안 마거릿이 한 손으로 눈을 가린 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있는 것을 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“You are not writing!”", "“안 쓰고 있구나!”"], ["said her mother at last.", "마침내 어머니가 말했다."], ["“Bring me some pens and paper; I will try and write myself.”", "“펜과 종이를 좀 가져오너라. 내가 직접 써 보마.”"], ["She sat up, trembling all over with feverish eagerness.", "부인은 열에 들뜬 조바심으로 온몸을 떨며 몸을 일으켰다."], ["Margaret took her hand down, and looked at her mother sadly.", "마거릿은 눈을 가렸던 손을 내리고 슬프게 어머니를 바라보았다."]], [["“Only wait till papa comes in.", "“아버지가 들어오실 때까지만 기다려요."], ["Let us ask him how best to do it?”", "어떻게 하는 게 가장 좋을지 여쭈어보아요, 네?”"]], [["“You promised, Margaret, not a quarter of an hour ago,—you said he should come.”", "“마거릿, 너 15분도 지나기 전에 약속하지 않았니. 그 애가 올 거라고 네가 말했잖니.”"]], [["“And so he shall, mamma; don’t cry, my own dear mother. I’ll write here, now—you shall see me write—and it shall go by this very post; and if papa thinks fit he can write again when he comes in—it is only a day’s delay. Oh, mamma, don’t cry so pitifully, it cuts me to the heart.”", "“그럼요, 오빠는 올 거예요, 어머니. 울지 마세요, 사랑하는 우리 어머니. 지금 여기서 쓸게요. 제가 쓰는 걸 보고 계세요. 그리고 바로 이번 우편으로 부칠게요. 아버지가 그러는 게 좋겠다 하시면 들어오셔서 다시 쓰시면 되고요. 하루 늦어질 뿐이잖아요. 아, 어머니, 그렇게 애처롭게 울지 마세요. 제 가슴이 미어져요.”"]], [["Mrs.", "헤일 부인은 눈물을 그칠 수 없었다."], ["Hale could not stop her tears; they came hysterically; and, in truth, she made no effort to control them, but rather called up all the pictures of the happy past, and the probable future—painting the scene when she should lie a corpse, with the son she had longed to see in life weeping over her, and she unconscious of his presence—till she was melted by self-pity into a state of sobbing and exhaustion that made Margaret’s heart ache.", "눈물은 발작하듯 터져 나왔다. 게다가 사실 부인은 그것을 다스리려는 노력조차 하지 않고, 오히려 행복했던 지난날과 있음직한 앞날의 온갖 장면을 불러냈다."], ["But at last she was calm, and greedily watched her daughter, as she began her letter; wrote it with swift urgent entreaty; sealed it up hurriedly, for fear her mother should ask to see it: and then to make security most sure, at Mrs.", "자기가 주검이 되어 누워 있고, 살아생전 그토록 보고 싶어 하던 아들이 그 위에서 울고 있는데 자기는 그가 곁에 있는 줄도 모르는 그림을 그려 가면서. 그러다 자기 연민에 녹아내려 흐느끼며 기진하는 지경에 이르렀고, 마거릿의 가슴은 아팠다."], ["Hale’s own bidding, took it herself to the post-office.", "그러나 마침내 부인은 잠잠해졌고, 딸이 편지를 쓰기 시작하자 게걸스럽게 그 모습을 지켜보았다. 마거릿은 빠르고 다급한 간청을 담아 편지를 썼고, 어머니가 보여 달라고 할까 봐 서둘러 봉했다."], ["She was coming home when her father overtook her.", "그러고는 더욱 확실히 하려고 헤일 부인이 시키는 대로 손수 우체국에 가지고 갔다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에 아버지가 그녀를 따라잡았다."]], [["“And where have you been, my pretty maid?”", "“어디에 다녀오는 길이냐, 우리 예쁜 아가씨?”"], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“To the post-office—with a letter; a letter to Frederick. Oh, papa, perhaps I have done wrong: but mamma was seized with such a passionate yearning to see him—she said it would make her well again—and then she said that she must see him before she died—I cannot tell you how urgent she was! Did I do wrong?”", "“우체국에요. 편지를 부치러요. 프레더릭 오빠에게 보내는 편지예요. 아, 아버지, 제가 잘못한 것인지도 모르겠어요. 그렇지만 어머니가 오빠를 보고 싶다는 그토록 격한 그리움에 사로잡히셨어요. 그러면 다시 건강해질 거라고 하셨고요. 그러고는 죽기 전에 반드시 오빠를 보아야겠다고 하셨어요. 얼마나 간절하셨는지 말로 다 못 해요! 제가 잘못했나요?”"]], [["Mr. Hale did not reply at first.", "헤일 씨는 처음에는 대답하지 않았다."], ["Then he said:", "그러다 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“You should have waited till I came in, Margaret.”", "“내가 들어올 때까지 기다렸어야지, 마거릿.”"]], [["“I tried to persuade her—”", "“어머니를 말려 보려고 했어요.”"], ["and then she was silent.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“나도 모르겠구나.”"], ["said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했다."], ["Hale, after a pause.", "“그토록 간절히 바란다면 네 어머니는 그 애를 보아야 하겠지. 나는 그것이 의사의 어떤 약보다도 훨씬 더 어머니에게 이로우리라고 믿는다."], ["“She ought to see him if she wishes it so much; for I believe it would do her much more good than all the doctor’s medicine—and, perhaps, set her up altogether; but the danger to him, I’m afraid, is very great.”", "어쩌면 아주 일으켜 세울지도 모르지. 그렇지만 그 애가 지는 위험이 아주 크지 않을까 걱정이구나.”"]], [["“All these years since the mutiny, papa?”", "“반란이 있고 나서 그 오랜 세월이 지났는데도요, 아버지?”"]], [["“Yes; it is necessary, of course, for government to take very stringent measures for the repression of offences against authority, more particularly in the navy, where a commanding officer needs to be surrounded in his men’s eyes with a vivid consciousness of all the power there is at home to back him, and take up his cause, and avenge any injuries offered to him, if need be. Ah! it’s no matter to them how far their authorities have tyrannised—galled hasty tempers to madness—or, if that can be any excuse afterwards, it is never allowed for in the first instance; they spare no expense, they send out ships—they scour the seas to lay hold of the offenders—the lapse of years does not wash out the memory of the offence—it is a fresh and vivid crime on the Admiralty books till it is blotted out by blood.”", "“그렇다. 정부로서는 권위를 거스르는 죄를 억누르려고 아주 엄한 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없지. 해군에서는 더욱 그렇다. 지휘관은 부하들의 눈에, 본국에 그를 뒷받침하고 그의 편을 들어 주며 필요하다면 그가 입은 어떤 해라도 갚아 줄 온갖 힘이 있다는 생생한 의식에 둘러싸여 있어야 하니까. 아! 그들에게는 상관들이 얼마나 폭압을 부렸는지, 성마른 성미를 미치도록 긁어 놓았는지 따위는 아무 상관이 없다. 설령 나중에 그것이 얼마쯤 변명이 될 수 있다 하더라도, 처음에는 결코 참작해 주지 않지. 그들은 비용을 아끼지 않는다. 배를 내보내고 바다를 샅샅이 뒤져 범인을 붙잡으려 든다. 세월이 흘러도 그 죄의 기억은 씻기지 않아. 피로 지워질 때까지 해군성 장부에는 그것이 새롭고 생생한 범죄로 남아 있는 게다.”"]], [["“Oh, papa, what have I done!", "“아, 아버지, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요!"], ["And yet it seemed so right at the time.", "그런데도 그때는 그게 그토록 옳게 여겨졌어요."], ["I’m sure Frederick himself, would run the risk.”", "프레더릭 오빠라면 스스로 그 위험을 무릅쓸 거라고 저는 믿어요.”"]], [["“So he would; so he should!", "“그럴 게다. 그래야 하고!"], ["Nay, Margaret, I’m glad it is done, though I durst not have done it myself.", "아니, 마거릿, 나는 일이 이렇게 된 것이 기쁘다. 내 손으로는 감히 하지 못했을 일이지만 말이다."], ["I’m thankful it is as it is; I should have hesitated till, perhaps, it might have been too late to do any good.", "이렇게 되어서 고맙구나. 나였다면 망설이다가 어쩌면 손을 써도 아무 소용이 없을 때가 되어 버렸을지도 모르지."], ["Dear Margaret, you have done what is right about it; and the end is beyond our control.”", "사랑하는 마거릿, 너는 이 일에서 옳은 일을 했다. 그리고 그 끝은 우리 손에 있지 않아.”"]], [["It was all very well; but her father’s account of the relentless manner in which mutinies were punished made Margaret shiver and creep.", "다 좋은 말이었다. 그러나 반란이 얼마나 가차 없이 처벌되는지에 대한 아버지의 이야기는 마거릿을 몸서리치고 오싹하게 만들었다."], ["If she had decoyed her brother home to blot out the memory of his error by his blood!", "제가 오빠를 집으로 꾀어들여, 오빠의 잘못의 기억을 그 피로 지우게 만든 것이라면!"], ["She saw her father’s anxiety lap deeper than the source of his latter cheering words.", "그녀는 나중에 한 아버지의 위로 섞인 말이 나온 자리보다 아버지의 불안이 훨씬 더 깊은 데까지 닿아 있음을 알아보았다."], ["She took his arm and walked home pensively and wearily by his side.", "그녀는 아버지의 팔을 잡고, 생각에 잠긴 채 지쳐서 아버지 곁에서 집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI", "# 제26장"]], [["“I have found that holy place of rest Still changeless.”", "“나는 그 성스러운 안식의 자리를 찾았노라, 여전히 변함없는 그곳을.”"], ["MRS. HEMANS.", "— 헤먼스 부인."]], [["When Mr. Thornton had left the house that morning he was almost blinded by his baffled passion.", "그날 아침 그 집을 나설 때 손턴 씨는 좌절된 격정에 눈이 멀 지경이었다."], ["He was as dizzy as if Margaret, instead of looking, and speaking, and moving like a tender graceful woman, had been a sturdy fish-wife, and given him a sound blow with her fists.", "마거릿이 여리고 우아한 여인처럼 바라보고 말하고 움직인 것이 아니라 억센 생선 장수 아낙이 되어 주먹으로 호되게 한 대 갈기기라도 한 듯 머리가 어질어질했다."], ["He had positive bodily pain—a violent headache, and a throbbing intermittent pulse.", "그는 실제로 몸에 통증을 느꼈다. 격심한 두통과 끊겼다 이어지는 맥박이었다."], ["He could not bear the noise, the garish light, the continued rumble and movement of the street.", "소음도, 눈부신 햇빛도, 거리의 끊임없는 덜컹거림과 움직임도 견딜 수 없었다."], ["He called himself a fool for suffering so; and yet he could not, at the moment, recollect the cause of his suffering, and whether it was adequate to the consequences it had produced.", "이렇게 괴로워하는 자신을 두고 그는 스스로 바보라고 불렀다. 그러면서도 그 순간에는 자기 괴로움의 원인이 무엇인지, 그것이 빚어낸 결과에 걸맞은 것이기는 한지조차 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["It would have been a relief to him, if he could have sat down and cried on a door-step by a little child, who was raging and storming, through his passionate tears, at some injury he had received.", "무슨 억울한 일을 당해 격정의 눈물을 흘리며 길길이 날뛰고 울부짖는 어린아이 곁에, 어느 집 문간 계단에 주저앉아 함께 울 수만 있었다면 차라리 후련했을 것이다."], ["He said to himself, that he hated Margaret, but a wild, sharp sensation of love cleft his dull, thunderous feeling like lightning, even as he shaped the words expressive of hatred.", "그는 마거릿이 밉다고 스스로에게 말했지만, 미움을 담은 그 말을 입 모양으로 지어내는 순간에도 사납고 날카로운 사랑의 감각이 번개처럼 그 둔중하고 우레 같은 감정을 갈랐다."], ["His greatest comfort was in hugging his torment and in feeling, as he had indeed said to her, that though she might despise him, contemn him, treat him with her proud sovereign indifference, he did not change one whit.", "그에게 가장 큰 위안은 자신의 고통을 끌어안는 것, 그리고 실제로 그녀에게 말한 대로 그녀가 자기를 업신여기고 경멸하고 그 도도한 여왕 같은 무관심으로 대할지언정 자기는 털끝만큼도 변하지 않는다고 느끼는 것이었다."], ["She could not make him change.", "그녀는 그를 변하게 할 수 없었다."], ["He loved her, and would love her; and defy her, and this miserable bodily pain.", "그는 그녀를 사랑했고 앞으로도 사랑할 것이었다. 그리고 그녀에게도, 이 비참한 육신의 고통에도 맞설 것이었다."]], [["He stood still for a moment, to make this resolution firm and clear.", "그는 이 결심을 굳고 또렷하게 다지려고 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 서 있었다."], ["There was an omnibus passing—going into the country; the conductor thought he was wishing for a place, and stopped near the pavement.", "마침 승합마차 한 대가 시골 쪽으로 지나가고 있었다. 차장은 그가 자리를 원하는 줄 알고 보도 가까이에 마차를 세웠다."], ["It was too much trouble to apologise and explain; so he mounted upon it, and was borne away—past long rows of houses—then past detached villas with trim gardens, till they came to real country hedge-rows, and, by-and-by, to a small country town.", "사과하고 설명하기가 너무 번거로워서 그는 그냥 올라탔고, 그대로 실려 갔다. 길게 늘어선 집들의 줄을 지나고, 그다음에는 잘 손질된 정원이 딸린 외딴 별장들을 지나, 이윽고 진짜 시골의 산울타리에 이르렀고, 얼마 뒤에는 자그마한 시골 읍내에 닿았다."], ["Then everybody got down; and so did Mr.", "그러자 모두 내렸고 손턴 씨도 내렸다."], ["Thornton, and because they walked away he did so too.", "사람들이 걸어가기에 그도 그렇게 했다."], ["He went into the fields, walking briskly, because the sharp motion relieved his mind.", "그는 들판으로 들어가 성큼성큼 걸었다. 격한 움직임이 마음을 눅여 주었기 때문이다."], ["He could remember all about it now; the pitiful figure he must have cut; the absurd way in which he had gone and done the very thing he had so often agreed with himself in thinking would be the most foolish thing in the world; and had met with exactly the consequences which, in these wise moods, he had always foretold were certain to follow, if he ever did make such a fool of himself.", "이제 그는 그 일을 전부 떠올릴 수 있었다. 자기가 지었을 딱한 몰골, 세상에서 가장 어리석은 짓이라고 그토록 여러 번 혼자 되뇌며 수긍했던 바로 그 일을 기어이 찾아가서 저지른 그 터무니없는 꼴, 그리고 만약 자기가 그런 바보짓을 저지른다면 반드시 뒤따르리라고 그 분별 있는 순간마다 늘 예언했던 바로 그 결과를 고스란히 맞닥뜨린 일까지."], ["Was he bewitched by those beautiful eyes, that soft, half-open, sighing mouth which lay so close upon his shoulder only yesterday?", "어제만 해도 자기 어깨에 그토록 가까이 놓여 있던 그 아름다운 눈에, 그 부드럽고 반쯤 벌어진 채 한숨짓던 입에 홀리기라도 했단 말인가?"], ["He could not even shake off the recollection that she had been there; that her arms had been round him, once—if never again.", "그녀가 그곳에 있었다는 기억을, 다시는 그런 일이 없을지라도 한때 그녀의 두 팔이 자기를 감쌌다는 기억을 그는 떨쳐 낼 수조차 없었다."], ["He only caught glimpses of her; he did not understand her altogether.", "그는 그녀를 언뜻언뜻 볼 뿐이었고, 그녀를 온전히 이해하지는 못했다."], ["At one time she was so brave, and at another so timid; now so tender, and then so haughty and regal proud.", "어느 때는 그토록 용감했다가 또 어느 때는 그토록 겁이 많았고, 지금은 그토록 다정했다가 이내 그토록 도도하고 여왕처럼 오만했다."], ["And then he thought over every time he had ever seen her once again, by way of finally forgetting her.", "그러고는 그녀를 끝내 잊어버리자는 심산으로, 여태껏 그녀를 보았던 모든 순간을 하나하나 다시 떠올렸다."], ["He saw her in every dress, in every mood, and did not know which became her best.", "온갖 옷차림의 그녀, 온갖 기분의 그녀를 보았지만 어느 쪽이 그녀에게 가장 어울리는지 알 수 없었다."], ["Even this morning how magnificent she had looked—her eyes flashing out upon him at the idea that, because she had shared his danger yesterday, she had cared for him the least!", "오늘 아침만 해도 그녀는 얼마나 당당해 보였던가. 어제 자기 위험을 함께 나누었으니 조금이라도 자기를 마음에 두었으리라는 그 생각에 그녀의 두 눈은 그를 향해 불꽃을 뿜었다!"]], [["If Mr. Thornton was a fool in the morning, as he assured himself at least twenty times he was, he did not grow much wiser in the afternoon.", "손턴 씨가 아침에 바보였다면, 그리고 그는 스스로에게 적어도 20번은 그렇다고 다짐했는데, 오후에도 그리 현명해지지는 못했다."], ["All that he gained for his sixpenny omnibus ride, was a more vivid conviction that there never was, never could be, any one like Margaret; that she did not love him and never would; but that she—no!", "6펜스짜리 승합마차 나들이로 그가 얻은 것이라고는 한층 생생해진 확신뿐이었다. 마거릿 같은 사람은 이제껏 없었고 앞으로도 있을 수 없다는 것, 그녀는 자기를 사랑하지 않으며 앞으로도 사랑하지 않으리라는 것, 그러나 그녀가, 아니 온 세상이 나선다 해도 자기가 그녀를 사랑하는 것만은 결코 막지 못하리라는 확신이었다."], ["nor the whole world—should never hinder him from loving her. And so he returned to the little market-place, and remounted the omnibus to return to Milton.", "그리하여 그는 그 자그마한 장터로 돌아가 다시 승합마차에 올라 밀턴으로 향했다."]], [["It was late in the afternoon when he was set down, near his warehouse.", "그가 자기 창고 근처에서 내려섰을 때는 늦은 오후였다."], ["The accustomed places brought back the accustomed habits and trains of thought.", "늘 다니던 곳들이 늘 하던 버릇과 생각의 흐름을 되돌려 놓았다."], ["He knew how much he had to do—more than his usual work, owing to the commotion of the day before.", "그는 자기가 해야 할 일이 얼마나 많은지 알고 있었다. 전날의 소동 때문에 평소 일보다 더 많았다."], ["He had to see his brother magistrates; he had to complete the arrangements, only half made in the morning, for the comfort and safety of his newly imported Irish hands; he had to secure them from all chance of communication with the discontented workpeople of Milton.", "동료 치안판사들을 만나야 했고, 아침에 절반만 해 둔 채비, 곧 새로 데려온 아일랜드 직공들의 편의와 안전을 위한 채비를 마무리해야 했다. 그들이 밀턴의 불만에 찬 직공들과 접촉할 여지를 아예 없애야 했다."], ["Last of all, he had to go home and encounter his mother.", "그리고 마지막으로 집에 돌아가 어머니를 마주해야 했다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton had sat in the dining-room all day, every moment expecting the news of her son’s acceptance by Miss Hale.", "손턴 부인은 하루 종일 식당에 앉아 아들이 헤일 양에게서 승낙을 받았다는 소식을 시시각각 기다렸다."], ["She had braced herself up many and many a time, at some sudden noise in the house; had caught up the half-dropped work, and begun to ply her needle diligently, though through dimmed spectacles, and with an unsteady hand!", "집 안에서 무슨 소리가 갑자기 날 때마다 그녀는 몇 번이고 몸을 곧추세웠고, 반쯤 떨어뜨렸던 바느질감을 집어 들고는 흐려진 안경 너머로 떨리는 손을 놀려 부지런히 바늘을 움직이곤 했다!"], ["and many times had the door opened, and some indifferent person entered on some insignificant errand.", "그러고는 여러 번 문이 열렸지만, 대수롭지 않은 심부름으로 들어오는 아무 상관 없는 사람들뿐이었다."], ["Then her rigid face unstiffened from its gray frost-bound expression, and the features dropped into the relaxed look of despondency, so unusual to their sternness.", "그러면 그녀의 굳은 얼굴은 잿빛 서리 같던 표정에서 풀어져, 그 엄격함에는 그토록 어울리지 않는 낙담한 기색으로 이목구비가 늘어졌다."], ["She wrenched herself away from the contemplation of all the dreary changes that would be brought about to herself by her son’s marriage; she forced her thoughts into the accustomed household grooves.", "그녀는 아들의 혼인이 자기 자신에게 가져올 온갖 쓸쓸한 변화를 곱씹던 생각에서 억지로 몸을 빼내어, 늘 하던 살림살이의 골로 생각을 밀어 넣었다."], ["The newly-married couple-to-be would need fresh household stocks of linen: and Mrs.", "곧 혼인할 새 부부에게는 새 리넨 살림이 필요할 터였다."], ["Thornton had clothes-basket upon clothes-basket, full of table-cloths and napkins, brought in, and began to reckon up the store.", "그래서 손턴 부인은 식탁보와 냅킨이 가득 든 빨래 바구니를 몇 개나 들여오게 해서 세어 보기 시작했다. 자기 것이라 G."], ["There was some confusion between what was hers, and consequently marked G.", "H. T.(조지와 해나 손턴)라고 표시된 것과, 아들의 돈으로 산 것이라 아들의 머리글자가 새겨진 것 사이에 얼마쯤 뒤섞임이 있었다."], ["H. T.", "G."], ["(for George and Hannah Thornton), and what was her son’s—bought with his money, marked with his initials.", "H. T.라고 표시된 것 가운데 더러는 옛날식 네덜란드 다마스크였는데 곱기가 이를 데 없었다."], ["Some of those marked G. H.", "요즘은 그런 것이 없었다."], ["T. were Dutch damask of the old kind, exquisitely fine; none were like them now.", "손턴 부인은 오래도록 서서 그것들을 바라보았다."], ["Mrs. Thornton stood looking at them long,—they had been her pride when she was first married.", "갓 혼인했을 적 그녀의 자랑이던 물건들이었다."], ["Then she knit her brows, and pinched and compressed her lips tight, and carefully unpicked the G.H.", "이윽고 그녀는 눈살을 찌푸리고 입술을 앙다물어 꼭 오므린 채 조심스레 G. H.를 뜯어냈다."], ["She went so far as to search for the Turkey-red marking-thread to put in the new initials; but it was all used,—and she had no heart to send for any more just yet.", "새 머리글자를 넣으려고 터키레드 표시실을 찾기까지 했지만 실은 다 써 버리고 없었다. 그리고 당장 더 사 오라고 사람을 보낼 마음은 나지 않았다."], ["So she looked fixedly at vacancy; a series of visions passing before her, in all of which her son was the principal, the sole object,—her son, her pride, her property.", "그래서 그녀는 허공을 물끄러미 바라보았다. 여러 환영이 눈앞을 잇달아 스쳐 지나갔고, 그 모두에서 아들이 으뜸가는, 아니 유일한 대상이었다."], ["Still he did not come.", "그녀의 아들, 그녀의 자랑, 그녀의 소유물."], ["Doubtless he was with Miss Hale.", "그런데도 그는 오지 않았다."], ["The new love was displacing her already from her place as first in his heart.", "필시 헤일 양과 함께 있으리라. 새로운 사랑이 벌써 그의 마음속 첫자리에서 그녀를 밀어내고 있었다."], ["A terrible pain—a pang of vain jealousy—shot through her: she hardly knew whether it was more physical or mental; but it forced her to sit down.", "무서운 아픔이, 부질없는 질투의 발작이 그녀를 꿰뚫었다. 그것이 몸의 것인지 마음의 것인지 그녀 자신도 잘 알 수 없었으나, 그 때문에 그녀는 주저앉을 수밖에 없었다."], ["In a moment, she was up again as straight as ever,—a grim smile upon her face for the first time that day, ready for the door opening, and the rejoicing triumphant one, who should never know the sore regret his mother felt at his marriage.", "잠시 뒤 그녀는 여느 때처럼 꼿꼿하게 다시 일어섰다. 그날 처음으로 그 얼굴에 굳은 미소를 띠고, 문이 열리는 순간을, 그리고 자기 혼인에 어머니가 느끼는 쓰라린 회한을 결코 알아서는 안 될 그 기쁨에 찬 승리자를 맞을 채비를 갖추었다."], ["In all this, there was little thought enough of the future daughter-in-law as an individual.", "이 모든 것에서 장래의 며느리를 한 개인으로 생각하는 마음은 거의 없었다."], ["She was to be John’s wife. To take Mrs.", "그 여자는 존의 아내가 될 사람이었다."], ["Thornton’s place as mistress of the house, was only one of the rich consequences which decked out the supreme glory: all household plenty and comfort, all purple and fine linen, honour, love, obedience, troops of friends, would all come as naturally as jewels on a king’s robe, and be as little thought of for their separate value.", "손턴 부인의 자리를 이어 이 집의 안주인이 되는 것은, 그 지고한 영광을 꾸며 주는 풍성한 결과들 가운데 하나일 뿐이었다. 살림의 온갖 넉넉함과 안락, 온갖 자줏빛 옷과 고운 리넨, 명예와 사랑과 순종과 무리 지은 벗들이 왕의 옷에 박힌 보석처럼 자연스레 따라올 것이고, 그 낱낱의 값어치는 그만큼 대수롭지 않게 여겨질 것이었다."], ["To be chosen by John, would separate a kitchen-wench from the rest of the world.", "존에게 선택받는다는 것은 부엌데기 하나라도 나머지 온 세상에서 따로 떼어 놓는 일이었다."], ["And Miss Hale was not so bad.", "게다가 헤일 양은 그리 나쁘지도 않았다."], ["If she had been a Milton lass, Mrs. Thornton would have positively liked her.", "그 아가씨가 밀턴 처녀였다면 손턴 부인은 진심으로 그녀를 좋아했을 것이다."], ["She was pungent, and had taste, and spirit, and flavour in her.", "그 여자에게는 톡 쏘는 맛이 있었고, 안목과 기개와 풍미가 있었다."], ["True, she was sadly prejudiced, and very ignorant; but that was to be expected from her southern breeding.", "물론 딱하게도 편견에 사로잡혀 있고 몹시 무지하기는 했다. 그러나 그것은 남부에서 자란 탓이니 그러려니 할 일이었다."], ["A strange sort of mortified comparison of Fanny with her, went on in Mrs.", "손턴 부인의 마음속에서는 패니를 그녀와 견주어 보는 언짢은 비교가 야릇하게 이어졌다. 그래서 그녀는 그날따라 딸에게 모질게 말했고 대놓고 나무랐다."], ["Thornton’s mind; and for once she spoke harshly to her daughter; abused her roundly; and then, by way of penance, she took up Henry’s Commentaries, and tried to fix her attention on it, instead of pursuing the employment she took pride in, and continuing her inspection of the table-linen.", "그러고는 속죄하는 셈으로 헨리의 주석서를 집어 들고 거기에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애썼다. 자기가 자랑스러워하던 일, 곧 식탁 리넨을 살펴보는 일을 계속하는 대신에."]], [["His step at last!", "드디어 그의 발소리!"], ["She heard him, even while she thought she was finishing a sentence; while her eye did pass over it, and her memory could mechanically have repeated it word for word, she heard him come in at the hall-door.", "그녀는 자기가 한 문장을 다 읽어 가고 있다고 여기는 동안에도, 눈은 정말로 그 문장을 훑고 있었고 기억은 그것을 한 마디 한 마디 기계적으로 되뇔 수도 있었지만, 그가 현관문으로 들어서는 소리를 들었다."], ["Her quickened sense could interpret every sound of motion: now he was at the hat-stand—now at the very room-door.", "예민해진 감각은 움직임의 소리 하나하나를 읽어 냈다. 이제 그는 모자걸이 앞에 있고, 이제 바로 방문 앞에 있다."], ["Why did he pause?", "왜 멈춰 섰을까?"], ["Let her know the worst.", "최악이 무엇이든 어서 알려 다오."]], [["Yet her head was down over the book; she did not look up.", "그런데도 그녀의 머리는 책 위에 숙인 채였다."], ["He came close to the table, and stood still there, waiting till she should have finished the paragraph which apparently absorbed her.", "그녀는 고개를 들지 않았다. 그가 탁자 가까이 다가와 그 자리에 가만히 서서, 어머니가 분명 몰두해 있는 듯한 그 문단을 다 읽기를 기다렸다."], ["By an effort she looked up.", "그녀는 애써 고개를 들었다."], ["“Well, John?”", "“그래, 존?”"]], [["He knew what that little speech meant.", "그는 그 짧은 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았다."], ["But he had steeled himself.", "그러나 그는 마음을 다잡아 둔 터였다."], ["He longed to reply with a jest; the bitterness of his heart could have uttered one, but his mother deserved better of him.", "농담으로 대답하고 싶었고, 가슴속 쓰디쓴 것이 농담 하나쯤 뱉어 낼 수도 있었으나, 어머니는 그보다 나은 대접을 받을 분이었다."], ["He came round behind her, so that she could not see his looks, and, bending back her gray, stony face, he kissed it, murmuring:", "그는 어머니 뒤로 돌아가 자기 표정이 보이지 않게 한 다음, 그 잿빛의 돌 같은 얼굴을 뒤로 젖혀 입을 맞추며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“No one loves me,—no one cares for me, but you, mother.”", "“저를 사랑하는 사람은 아무도 없습니다. 저를 아껴 주는 사람은 어머니뿐이에요.”"]], [["He turned away and stood leaning his head against the mantelpiece, tears forcing themselves into his manly eyes.", "그는 돌아서서 벽난로 선반에 머리를 기댄 채 서 있었고, 사내다운 두 눈에 눈물이 밀려 나왔다."], ["She stood up,—she tottered.", "그녀는 일어섰다."], ["For the first time in her life, the strong woman tottered.", "그러다 휘청했다. 평생 처음으로 그 강인한 여자가 휘청거렸다."], ["She put her hands on his shoulders; she was a tall woman.", "그녀는 아들의 어깨에 두 손을 얹었다. 키가 큰 여자였다."], ["She looked into his face: she made him look at her.", "그녀는 아들의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 아들이 자기를 보게 만들었다."]], [["“Mother’s love is given by God, John.", "“어미의 사랑은 하느님이 주신 것이다, 존."], ["It holds fast for ever and ever.", "그것은 영원토록 굳게 붙들어 준다."], ["A girl’s love is like a puff of smoke,—it changes with every wind.", "계집아이의 사랑은 한 줄기 연기 같아서 바람 따라 변하는 법이야."], ["And she would not have you, my own lad, would not she?”", "그래, 그 애가 너를 마다했구나, 내 아들아. 그렇지?”"], ["She set her teeth; she showed them like a dog for the whole length of her mouth.", "그녀는 이를 악물었다. 개처럼 입 끝에서 끝까지 이를 드러내 보였다."], ["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I am not fit for her, mother; I knew I was not.”", "“저는 그분에게 걸맞지 않습니다, 어머니. 걸맞지 않다는 것을 알고 있었어요.”"]], [["She ground out words between her closed teeth.", "그녀는 앙다문 이 사이로 말을 갈아 냈다. 그는 어머니가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 수 없었다."], ["He could not hear what she said; but the look in her eyes interpreted it to be a curse,—if not as coarsely worded, as fell in intent as ever was uttered.", "그러나 두 눈의 표정이 그것을 저주라고 일러 주었다. 말씨는 그처럼 상스럽지 않을지언정 그 속뜻은 여태 입 밖에 나온 어떤 저주 못지않게 모진 저주였다."], ["And yet her heart leapt up light, to know he was her own again.", "그러면서도 아들이 다시 자기 것이 되었다는 것을 알자 그녀의 가슴은 가볍게 뛰어올랐다."]], [["“Mother!”", "“어머니!”"], ["said he, hurriedly, “I cannot hear a word against her.", "그가 다급하게 말했다. “저는 그분을 헐뜯는 말은 한 마디도 들을 수 없습니다."], ["Spare me,—spare me!", "저를 봐주세요, 봐주세요! 제 쓰라린 마음은 몹시 약해져 있습니다."], ["I am very weak in my sore heart;—I love her yet; I love her more than ever.”", "저는 아직도 그분을 사랑합니다. 그 어느 때보다 더 사랑합니다.”"]], [["“And I hate her,”", "“나는 그 애가 밉다.”"], ["said Mrs. Thornton, in a low fierce voice.", "손턴 부인이 낮고 사나운 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I tried not to hate her, when she stood between you and me, because,—I said to myself,—she will make him happy; and I would give my heart’s blood to do that.", "“그 애가 너와 나 사이에 서 있을 때는 미워하지 않으려고 애썼다. 저 애가 내 아들을 행복하게 해 주겠지, 하고 스스로에게 말했으니까."], ["But now, I hate her for your misery’s sake.", "그리고 나는 그 일을 위해서라면 내 심장의 피라도 내어 줄 테니까."], ["Yes, John, it’s no use hiding up your aching heart from me.", "하지만 이제는 네 비참함 때문에 그 애가 밉다. 그래, 존, 아픈 가슴을 내게 숨겨 봐야 소용없다."], ["I am the mother that bore you, and your sorrow is my agony; and if you don’t hate her, I do.”", "나는 너를 낳은 어미고 네 슬픔은 곧 내 고통이야. 네가 그 애를 미워하지 않겠다면 내가 미워하마.”"]], [["“Then, mother, you make me love her more.", "“그러시면, 어머니, 저는 그분을 더 사랑하게 됩니다."], ["She is unjustly treated by you, and I must make the balance even.", "어머니가 그분을 부당하게 대하시니 제가 저울을 고르게 맞춰야지요."], ["But why do we talk of love or hatred?", "그런데 우리가 왜 사랑이니 미움이니 하는 이야기를 합니까?"], ["She does not care for me, and that is enough, too much.", "그분은 저를 마음에 두지 않으십니다. 그거면 충분합니다."], ["Let us never name the subject again.", "아니, 넘치지요. 이 이야기는 다시는 입에 올리지 맙시다."], ["It is the only thing you can do for me in the matter.", "이 일에서 어머니가 저를 위해 해 주실 수 있는 일은 그것뿐입니다."], ["Let us never name her.”", "다시는 그분 이름을 부르지 맙시다.”"]], [["“With all my heart.", "“그러자꾸나, 얼마든지."], ["I only wish that she, and all belonging to her, were swept back to the place they came from.”", "나는 다만 그 애도, 그 애에게 딸린 사람들도 모두 저희가 온 곳으로 쓸려 돌아가기만을 바랄 뿐이다.”"]], [["He stood still, gazing into the fire for a minute or two longer.", "그는 일이 분쯤 더 가만히 서서 불을 들여다보았다."], ["Her dry dim eyes filled with unwonted tears as she looked at him; but she seemed just as grim and quiet as usual when he next spoke.", "아들을 바라보는 그녀의 마르고 흐릿하던 두 눈에 여느 때 없던 눈물이 차올랐다. 그러나 그가 다음 말을 꺼냈을 때 그녀는 여느 때처럼 험상궂고 조용해 보였다."]], [["“Warrants are out against three men for conspiracy, mother.", "“공모 혐의로 세 사람에게 영장이 나왔습니다, 어머니."], ["The riot yesterday helped to knock up the strike.”", "어제의 폭동이 파업을 무너뜨리는 데 한몫했습니다.”"]], [["And Margaret’s name was no more mentioned between Mrs. Thornton and her son.", "그리고 마거릿의 이름은 손턴 부인과 아들 사이에서 두 번 다시 입에 오르지 않았다."], ["They fell back into their usual mode of talk,—about facts, not opinions, far less feelings.", "두 사람은 늘 하던 대화 방식으로 되돌아갔다. 사실에 대한 이야기였지 의견에 대한 것이 아니었고, 감정에 대한 것은 더더욱 아니었다."], ["Their voices and tones were calm and cold; a stranger might have gone away and thought that he had never seen such frigid indifference of demeanour between such near relations.", "두 사람의 목소리와 어조는 차분하고 차가웠다. 낯선 사람이라면 그 자리를 떠나면서, 그렇게 가까운 사이에 그토록 싸늘하게 무심한 태도는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII", "# 제27장"]], [["“For never any thing can be amiss When simpleness and duty tender it.”", "“단순한 마음과 도리로 바치는 것이라면 그 무엇도 잘못될 수 없나니.”"], ["MIDSUMMER NIGHT’S DREAM.", "— 한여름 밤의 꿈."]], [["Mr. Thornton went straight and clear into all the interests of the following day.", "손턴 씨는 이튿날의 온갖 용무 속으로 곧장, 또렷한 정신으로 파고들었다."], ["There was a slight demand for finished goods; and as it affected his branch of the trade, he took advantage of it, and drove hard bargains.", "완제품에 약간의 수요가 일었고, 그것이 자기 업종에 영향을 미치는 터라 그는 그 기회를 붙들어 야무진 흥정을 붙였다."], ["He was sharp to the hour at the meeting of his brother magistrates,—giving them the best assistance of his strong sense, and his power of seeing consequences at a glance, and so coming to a rapid decision.", "동료 치안판사들의 회합에도 시간에 딱 맞추어 나타나, 강인한 분별력과 결과를 한눈에 꿰뚫어 보고 신속하게 결단을 내리는 능력으로 최선의 도움을 주었다."], ["Older men, men of long standing in the town, men of far greater wealth—realised and turned into land, while his was all floating capital, engaged in his trade—looked to him for prompt, ready wisdom.", "그보다 나이 많은 이들, 이 도시에서 오래 자리를 지켜 온 이들, 그보다 훨씬 큰 재산을 — 그의 것은 모두 사업에 묶인 유동 자본이었던 반면 저들의 것은 이미 실현되어 토지로 바뀐 재산이었다 — 가진 이들이 그에게서 즉각적이고 준비된 지혜를 구했다."], ["He was the one deputed to see and arrange with the police—to lead in all the requisite steps.", "경찰을 만나 일을 조율하고 필요한 모든 절차를 앞장서 밟도록 위임받은 사람도 그였다."], ["And he cared for their unconscious deference no more than for the soft west wind, that scarcely made the smoke from the great tall chimneys swerve in its straight upward course.", "그런데 그는 저들이 무심결에 보내는 그 경의를, 커다랗고 높다란 굴뚝에서 곧게 솟는 연기를 겨우 비스듬히 밀어낼까 말까 한 부드러운 서풍만큼도 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["He was not aware of the silent respect paid to him.", "자기에게 바쳐지는 그 말 없는 존경을 그는 알아차리지도 못했다."], ["If it had been otherwise, he would have felt it as an obstacle in his progress to the object he had in view.", "만약 알아차렸더라면, 그는 그것을 자기가 마음에 둔 목적으로 나아가는 길의 걸림돌로 여겼을 것이다."], ["As it was, he looked to the speedy accomplishment of that alone.", "실제로 그는 오직 그 목적을 신속히 이루는 것만을 바라보고 있었다."], ["It was his mother’s greedy ears that sucked in, from the womenkind of these magistrates and wealthy men, how highly Mr.", "이러이러한 분이 손턴 씨를 얼마나 높이 평가하는지, 그가 그 자리에 없었더라면 일이 아주 다르게 — 정말이지 아주 고약하게 — 흘러갔으리라는 이야기를, 저 치안판사들과 부자들의 부녀자들에게서 게걸스레 빨아들인 것은 그의 어머니의 귀였다."], ["This or Mr. That thought of Mr.", "그날 그는 좌우로 일을 척척 쓸어치웠다."], ["Thornton: that if he had not been there, things would have gone on very differently,—very badly, indeed. He swept off his business right and left that day.", "어제의 그 깊은 굴욕과 그 뒤 몇 시간 동안의 얼이 빠진 목적 없는 방황이 그의 지력에서 안개를 죄다 걷어내 준 듯했다."], ["It seemed as though his deep mortification of yesterday, and the stunned purposeless course of the hours afterwards, had cleared away all the mists from his intellect.", "그는 자기 힘을 느꼈고 그 안에서 마음껏 즐겼다."], ["He felt his power and revelled in it. He could almost defy his heart.", "자기 심장마저 거의 물리칠 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["If he had known it, he could have sang the song of the miller who lived by the river Dee:—", "스스로 알았더라면 그는 디 강가에 살던 방앗간 주인의 노래를 불렀을 것이다."]], [["“I care for nobody— Nobody cares for me.”", "“나는 누구도 아랑곳하지 않고, 누구도 나를 아랑곳하지 않네.”"]], [["The evidence against Boucher, and other ring-leaders of the riot, was taken before him: that against the three others, for conspiracy, failed. But he sternly charged the police to be on the watch; for the swift right arm of the law should be in readiness to strike, as soon as they could prove a fault. And then he left the hot reeking room in the borough court, and went out into the fresher, but still sultry street. It seemed as though he gave way all at once; he was so languid that he could not control his thoughts; they would wander to her: they would bring back the scene,—not of his repulse and rejection the day before, but the looks, the actions of the day before that. He went along the crowded streets mechanically, winding in and out among the people, but never seeing them,—almost sick with longing for that one half-hour—that one brief space of time when she clung to him, and her heart beat against his—to come once again.", "바우처를 비롯한 폭동 주모자들에 대한 증언이 그의 앞에서 채택되었다. 나머지 세 사람에 대한 공모 혐의 증언은 무산되었다. 그러나 그는 경찰에게 엄중히 감시를 지시했다. 잘못이 입증되는 즉시 법의 날랜 오른팔이 내리칠 채비를 갖추고 있어야 했으니까. 그러고 나서 그는 자치구 법정의 후끈하고 퀴퀴한 방을 나와, 한결 신선하지만 여전히 후텁지근한 거리로 나섰다. 그 순간 그는 단번에 무너져 내린 듯했다. 어찌나 맥이 풀렸는지 생각을 다스릴 수가 없었다. 생각은 자꾸만 그녀에게로 흘러갔다. 생각은 그 장면을 되살려 냈다 — 어제 그가 퇴짜를 맞고 거절당한 장면이 아니라, 그 전날의 눈빛과 몸짓을. 그는 붐비는 거리를 기계적으로 걸어가며 사람들 사이를 요리조리 헤집었으나 아무도 보지 못했다. 그 반 시간, 그녀가 그에게 매달리고 그녀의 심장이 그의 심장에 맞부딪히던 그 짧은 한때가 한 번만 더 돌아왔으면 하는 그리움으로 그는 병이 날 지경이었다."]], [["“Why, Mr. Thornton!", "“아니, 손턴 씨!"], ["you’re cutting me very coolly, I must say.", "나를 아주 쌀쌀맞게 지나치시는구려."], ["And how is Mrs. Thornton?", "어머님은 안녕하시고?"], ["Brave weather this!", "날씨 한번 훌륭하지요!"], ["We doctors don’t like it, I can tell you!”", "우리 의사들이야 이런 날씨를 반기지 않소만!”"]], [["“I beg your pardon, Dr. Donaldson.", "“이거 실례했습니다, 도널드슨 선생님."], ["I really didn’t see you.", "정말 못 뵈었습니다."], ["My mother’s quite well, thank you.", "어머니는 아주 잘 계십니다, 감사합니다."], ["It is a fine day, and good for the harvest, I hope.", "좋은 날씨지요, 추수에도 좋았으면 합니다."], ["If the wheat is well got in, we shall have a brisk trade next year, whatever you doctors have.”", "밀만 잘 거둬들이면 내년에는 거래가 활발할 겁니다, 선생님네 의사들 사정이야 어떻든 말이지요.”"]], [["“Ay, ay.", "“그래요, 그래."], ["Each man for himself.", "저마다 제 앞가림이지."], ["Your bad weather, and your bad times, are my good ones.", "당신네한테 나쁜 날씨와 나쁜 시절이 나한테는 좋은 시절이오."], ["When trade is bad, there’s more undermining of health, and preparation for death, going on among you Milton men than you’re aware of.”", "장사가 안 될 때면 당신들 밀턴 사람들 사이에서 건강이 좀먹고 죽을 채비가 되어 가는 일이 당신이 아는 것보다 훨씬 많이 벌어진다오.”"]], [["“Not with me, Doctor.", "“저는 아닙니다, 선생님."], ["I’m made of iron.", "저는 쇠로 만들어졌으니까요."], ["The news of the very worst bad debt I ever had, never made my pulse vary.", "제가 겪은 최악의 악성 부채 소식을 들었을 때도 맥박 하나 흐트러진 적이 없습니다."], ["This strike, which affects me more than any one else in Milton,—more than Hamper,—never comes near my appetite.", "밀턴에서 누구보다도, 햄퍼보다도 저를 더 괴롭히는 이번 파업조차 제 입맛 근처에도 오지 못합니다."], ["You must go elsewhere for a patient, Doctor.”", "환자를 구하시려면 다른 데를 알아보셔야겠습니다, 선생님.”"]], [["“By the way, you’ve recommended me a good patient, poor lady!", "“그러고 보니 당신이 나한테 좋은 환자를 하나 소개해 주었지요, 가엾은 부인!"], ["Not to go on talking in this heartless way, I seriously believe that Mrs. Hale—that lady in Crampton, you know—hasn’t many weeks to live.", "이렇게 매정한 투로만 말하지 않으려니 하는 얘긴데, 나는 진심으로 헤일 부인이 — 아시죠, 크램프턴에 사는 그 부인 말이오 — 몇 주도 못 넘길 거라고 봅니다."], ["I never had any hope of cure, as I think I told you; but I’ve been seeing her to-day, and I think very badly of her.”", "전에도 말했지만 나는 애초에 나을 가망을 본 적이 없어요. 그런데 오늘 진찰을 하고 나니 정말 좋지 않아 보입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was silent.", "손턴 씨는 말이 없었다."], ["The vaunted steadiness of pulse failed him for an instant.", "그토록 자랑하던 맥박의 흔들림 없음이 한순간 그를 저버렸다."]], [["“Can I do anything, Doctor?”", "“제가 뭐든 할 수 있는 일이 있겠습니까, 선생님?”"], ["he asked, in an altered voice.", "그가 달라진 목소리로 물었다."], ["“You know—you would see, that money is not very plentiful, are there any comforts or dainties she ought to have?”", "“선생님도 아시겠지만 — 보시면 아시겠지만, 그 댁 형편이 그리 넉넉지 않습니다. 부인께 꼭 있어야 할 편의품이나 별미 같은 것이 있을까요?”"]], [["“No,”", "“없소.”"], ["replied the Doctor, shaking his head.", "의사가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["“She craves for fruit,—she has a constant fever on her; but jargonelle pears will do as well as anything, and there are quantities of them in the market.”", "“과일을 몹시 탐하기는 합디다 — 열이 끊이질 않으니까요. 하지만 자르고넬 배면 무엇 못지않게 좋을 텐데, 시장에 널려 있는 물건이오.”"]], [["“You will tell me if there is anything I can do, I’m sure,”", "“제가 할 수 있는 일이 있으면 꼭 말씀해 주시겠지요.”"], ["replied Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I rely upon you.”", "“선생님만 믿겠습니다.”"]], [["“Oh! never fear!", "“아, 걱정 마시오!"], ["I’ll not spare your purse,—I know it’s deep enough.", "당신 지갑을 아껴 줄 생각은 없으니까 — 두둑한 줄 다 아오."], ["I wish you’d give me a carte-blanche for all my patients, and all their wants.”", "내 환자들 전부와 그들이 원하는 것 전부에 대해 백지 위임장이나 하나 써 주었으면 좋겠구려.”"]], [["But Mr. Thornton had no general benevolence,—no universal philanthropy; few even would have given him credit for strong affections.", "그러나 손턴 씨에게는 두루 미치는 자비심도, 만인을 향한 박애도 없었다."], ["But he went straight to the first fruit-shop in Milton, and chose out the bunch of purple grapes with the most delicate bloom upon them,—the richest-coloured peaches,—the freshest vine-leaves.", "그에게 강한 애정이 있다고 인정해 줄 사람조차 몇 없었을 것이다. 그런데도 그는 밀턴에서 제일 좋은 과일 가게로 곧장 가서, 고운 분이 가장 여리게 서린 자줏빛 포도 한 송이와, 빛깔이 가장 짙은 복숭아와, 가장 싱싱한 포도 잎을 골랐다."], ["They were packed into a basket, and the shopman awaited the answer to his inquiry, “Where shall we send them to, sir?”", "그것들은 바구니에 담겼고, 가게 주인은 자기가 던진 물음의 답을 기다렸다. “어디로 보내 드릴까요, 나리?”"]], [["There was no reply.", "대답이 없었다."], ["“To Marlborough Mills, I suppose, sir?”", "“말버러 공장으로 보내면 되겠지요, 나리?”"]], [["“No!” Mr.", "“아니오!”"], ["Thornton said.", "손턴 씨가 말했다."], ["“Give the basket to me,—I’ll take it.”", "“바구니를 내게 주시오. 내가 들고 가겠소.”"]], [["It took up both his hands to carry it; and he had to pass through the busiest part of the town for feminine shopping.", "바구니는 두 손으로 들어야 했다. 게다가 그는 여인네들이 물건을 사러 나오는, 시내에서 가장 붐비는 구역을 지나야 했다."], ["Many a young lady of his acquaintance turned to look after him, and thought it strange to see him occupied just like a porter or an errand-boy.", "그가 아는 젊은 아가씨들 여럿이 돌아서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며, 그가 짐꾼이나 심부름꾼처럼 손을 놀리고 있는 것이 이상하다고 생각했다."]], [["He was thinking, “I will not be daunted from doing as I choose by the thought of her.", "그는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. “그 여자 생각 때문에 내가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 일을 그만두지는 않겠다."], ["I like to take this fruit to the poor mother, and it is simply right that I should.", "나는 이 과일을 저 가엾은 어머니에게 가져다 드리고 싶고, 그렇게 하는 것이 그저 옳은 일이다."], ["She shall never scorn me out of doing what I please.", "그 여자가 나를 업신여긴다고 해서 내가 원하는 일을 못 하게 되지는 않는다."], ["A pretty joke, indeed, if, for fear of a haughty girl, I failed in doing a kindness to a man I liked!", "도도한 처녀 하나가 무서워서 내가 좋아하는 사람에게 친절 하나 베풀지 못한다면, 그거야말로 우스운 노릇이 아닌가! 나는 헤일 씨를 위해 이 일을 하는 것이다."], ["I do it for Mr. Hale; I do it in defiance of her.”", "나는 그 여자에게 맞서서 이 일을 하는 것이다.”"]], [["He went at an unusual pace, and was soon at Crampton.", "그는 여느 때 아닌 걸음걸이로 걸어 이내 크램프턴에 닿았다."], ["He went upstairs two steps at a time, and entered the drawing-room before Dixon could announce him,—his face flushed, his eyes shining with kindly earnestness.", "계단을 두 칸씩 뛰어올라, 딕슨이 그를 알릴 겨를도 없이 응접실로 들어섰다. 얼굴은 상기되고 두 눈은 따뜻한 진심으로 빛나고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Hale lay on the sofa, heated with fever.", "헤일 부인은 열에 달아오른 채 소파에 누워 있었다."], ["Mr. Hale was reading aloud.", "헤일 씨는 소리 내어 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["Margaret was working on a low stool by her mother’s side.", "마거릿은 어머니 곁 낮은 걸상에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다."], ["Her heart fluttered, if his did not at this interview.", "이 만남에서 그의 심장은 어땠는지 몰라도 그녀의 심장은 파닥거렸다."], ["But he took no notice of her,—hardly of Mr. Hale himself; he went up straight with his basket to Mrs.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 아랑곳하지 않았고, 헤일 씨조차 거의 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Hale, and said, in that subdued and gentle tone, which is so touching when used by a robust man in full health, speaking to a feeble invalid,—", "그는 바구니를 들고 곧장 헤일 부인에게로 가서, 건장하고 정정한 남자가 쇠약한 병자에게 말할 때 쓰면 그토록 마음을 울리는 그 낮고 부드러운 어조로 말했다."]], [["“I met Dr. Donaldson, ma’am, and as he said fruit would be good for you, I have taken the liberty—the great liberty—of bringing you some that seemed to me fine.”", "“도널드슨 선생을 만났는데 과일이 부인께 좋겠다고 하기에, 제 눈에 좋아 보이는 것을 좀 가져다 드리는 무례를 — 큰 무례를 — 저질렀습니다.”"], ["Mrs.", "헤일 부인은 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다."], ["Hale was excessively pleased; quite in a tremble of eagerness.", "반가움에 몸이 다 떨릴 지경이었다."], ["Mr. Hale with fewer words expressed a deeper gratitude.", "헤일 씨는 말수는 적었으나 더 깊은 고마움을 나타냈다."]], [["“Fetch a plate, Margaret—a basket—anything.”", "“접시를 가져오너라, 마거릿. 바구니든 무엇이든.”"], ["Margaret stood up by the table, half afraid of moving or making any noise to arouse Mr.", "마거릿은 탁자 옆에 일어서 있었다. 움직이거나 소리를 내어 손턴 씨에게 자기가 이 방에 있다는 것을 일깨울까 봐 절반쯤 겁이 났던 것이다."], ["Thornton into a consciousness of her being in the room.", "둘이 서로를 의식하며 맞부딪히게 되는 것은 양쪽 모두에게 거북하리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["She thought it would be awkward for both to be brought into conscious collision: and fancied that, from her being on a low seat at first, and now standing behind her father, he had overlooked her in his haste.", "그리고 처음에는 자기가 낮은 자리에 앉아 있었고 지금은 아버지 뒤에 서 있으니, 그가 서두르는 통에 자기를 못 보고 지나쳤으려니 짐작했다."], ["As if he did not feel the consciousness of her presence all over, though his eyes had never rested on her!", "마치 그의 눈길이 한 번도 그녀에게 머물지 않았다 해서 그가 온몸으로 그녀의 존재를 느끼지 않기라도 하는 것처럼!"]], [["“I must go,”", "“가 봐야겠습니다.”"], ["said he, “I cannot stay.", "그가 말했다. “더 머물 수가 없군요."], ["If you will forgive this liberty,—my rough ways,—too abrupt, I fear—but I will be more gentle next time.", "이 무례를 — 제 거친 방식을 — 너무 갑작스러웠지요, 그것을 용서해 주신다면, 다음번에는 좀 더 점잖게 하겠습니다."], ["You will allow me the pleasure of bringing you some fruit again, if I should see any that is tempting.", "눈에 띄는 좋은 과일이 있거든 다시 가져다 드리는 즐거움을 허락해 주십시오."], ["Good afternoon, Mr.", "안녕히 계십시오, 헤일 씨."], ["Hale. Good-bye, ma’am.”", "안녕히 계십시오, 부인.”"]], [["He was gone.", "그는 가 버렸다."], ["Not one word: not one look to Margaret.", "마거릿에게는 한마디도, 한 번의 눈길도 없이."], ["She believed that he had not seen her.", "그녀는 그가 자기를 보지 못했다고 믿었다."], ["She went for a plate in silence, and lifted the fruit out tenderly, with the points of her delicate taper fingers.", "그녀는 말없이 접시를 가지러 갔고, 가늘고 고운 손가락 끝으로 과일을 조심스레 들어냈다. 이것을 가져다준 것은 참 고마운 일이었다."], ["It was good of him to bring it; and after yesterday too!", "더구나 어제 그런 일이 있고 나서!"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["it is so delicious!”", "어쩌면 이렇게 맛있을까!”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, in a feeble voice.", "헤일 부인이 가냘픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["“How kind of him to think of me!", "“나를 생각해 주다니 얼마나 고마운 분이니!"], ["Margaret love, only taste these grapes!", "마거릿아, 이 포도를 좀 맛보렴!"], ["Was it not good of him?”", "참 고마운 분이지 않니?”"]], [["“Yes!”", "“네!”"], ["said Margaret, quietly.", "마거릿이 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Margaret!” said Mrs.", "“마거릿!”"], ["Hale, rather querulously, “you won’t like anything Mr.", "헤일 부인이 다소 투정 섞인 투로 말했다. “너는 손턴 씨가 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 마음에 들어 하지 않는구나."], ["Thornton does. I never saw anybody so prejudiced.”", "그렇게 치우친 사람은 내 평생 처음 본다.”"]], [["Mr.", "헤일 씨는 아내를 위해 복숭아 껍질을 벗기고 있었다."], ["Hale had been peeling a peach for his wife; and, cutting off a small piece for himself, he said:", "그러고는 자기 몫으로 한 조각을 잘라 내면서 말했다."]], [["“If I had any prejudices, the gift of such delicious fruit as this would melt them away.", "“내게 무슨 편견이 있었더라도, 이렇게 맛난 과일을 선물로 받으면 죄다 녹아 없어질 걸세."], ["I have not tasted such fruit—no! not even in Hampshire—since I was a boy; and, to boys, I fancy, all fruit is good.", "이런 과일은 소년 시절 이후로 먹어 본 적이 없어 — 아니, 햄프셔에서도 못 먹어 봤지."], ["I remember eating sloes and crabs with a relish.", "하기야 소년들에게는 무슨 과일이든 다 맛있는 법이겠지만."], ["Do you remember the matted-up currant bushes, Margaret, at the corner of the west-wall at the garden at home?”", "나는 야생 자두며 돌사과를 아주 맛나게 먹던 기억이 나는구나. 마거릿, 고향 집 정원 서쪽 담 모퉁이에 얽히고설켜 있던 까치밥나무 덤불을 기억하니?”"]], [["Did she not?", "어찌 기억하지 못하겠는가?"], ["Did she not remember every weather-stain on the old stone wall; the gray and yellow lichens that marked it like a map; the little crane’s-bill that grew in the crevices?", "그 낡은 돌담에 남은 비바람 자국 하나하나를, 지도처럼 담을 얼룩덜룩 물들이던 잿빛과 노란빛 이끼를, 틈새마다 자라던 자그마한 쥐손이풀을 어찌 잊었겠는가? 그녀는 지난 이틀 동안의 사건들로 뒤흔들려 있었다."], ["She had been shaken by the events of the last two days; her whole life just now was a strain upon her fortitude; and, somehow, these careless words of her father’s, touching on the remembrance of the sunny times of old, made her start up, and, dropping her sewing on the ground, she went hastily out of the room into her own little chamber.", "지금 그녀의 삶 전체가 그녀의 굳센 마음을 짓누르는 짐이었다. 그런데 어쩐 일인지, 옛날의 햇살 가득하던 시절의 기억을 건드리는 아버지의 이 무심한 말들이 그녀를 벌떡 일어서게 만들었고, 그녀는 바느질감을 바닥에 떨어뜨린 채 서둘러 방을 나가 자기의 자그마한 방으로 들어갔다."], ["She had hardly given way to the first choking sob, when she became aware of Dixon standing at her drawers, and evidently searching for something.", "첫 흐느낌이 목을 메우도록 몸을 내맡기기가 무섭게 그녀는 딕슨이 자기 서랍장 앞에 서서 무엇인가를 뒤지고 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Bless me, miss!", "“어머나, 아가씨!"], ["How you startled me!", "깜짝 놀랐네요!"], ["Missus is not worse, is she?", "마님이 더 나빠지신 건 아니지요?"], ["Is anything the matter?”", "무슨 일이라도 있어요?”"]], [["“No, nothing.", "“아니, 아무 일도 없어."], ["Only I’m silly, Dixon, and want a glass of water.", "그냥 내가 바보 같아서 그래, 딕슨. 물 한 잔 줘요."], ["What are you looking for?", "뭘 찾고 있어요?"], ["I keep my muslins in that drawer.”", "그 서랍에는 내 모슬린이 들어 있는데.”"]], [["Dixon did not speak, but went on rummaging.", "딕슨은 말없이 계속 뒤졌다."], ["The scent of lavender came out and perfumed the room.", "라벤더 향이 흘러나와 방 안에 퍼졌다."]], [["At last Dixon found what she wanted; what it was Margaret could not see.", "마침내 딕슨은 찾던 것을 찾아냈다. 그것이 무엇인지 마거릿은 볼 수 없었다."], ["Dixon faced round, and spoke to her:", "딕슨은 몸을 홱 돌려 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“Now I don’t like telling what I wanted, because you’ve fretting enough to go through, and I know you’ll fret about this.", "“제가 뭘 찾았는지 말씀드리기가 싫습니다요. 아가씨는 이미 애태울 일이 넘치는데, 이 일로도 마음 졸이실 게 뻔하니까요."], ["I meant to have kept it from you till night, may be, or such times as that.”", "밤에나, 아니면 그런 때쯤에나 말씀드리려고 했었는걸요.”"]], [["“What is the matter?", "“무슨 일이에요?"], ["Pray, tell me, Dixon, at once.”", "부탁이니 지금 당장 말해 줘요, 딕슨.”"]], [["“That young woman you go to see—Higgins I mean.”", "“아가씨가 보러 다니시는 그 젊은 여자 말이에요 — 히긴스네 말입니다요.”"]], [["“Well?”", "“그런데요?”"]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄, 오늘 아침에 죽었답니다. 그 애 동생이 와 있어요."], ["she died this morning, and her sister is here—come to beg a strange thing.", "이상한 부탁을 하러 왔지 뭐예요. 죽은 그 젊은 여자가 아가씨 물건 하나를 걸치고 묻히고 싶어 했다나 봐요."], ["It seems, the young woman who died had a fancy for being buried in something of yours, and so the sister’s come to ask for it,—and I was looking for a night-cap that wasn’t too good to give away.”", "그래서 동생이 그걸 얻으러 온 거예요. 저는 남 줘도 아깝지 않을 만한 나이트캡을 찾고 있던 참이고요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["let me find one,”", "내가 찾을게.”"], ["said Margaret, in the midst of her tears.", "마거릿이 눈물을 흘리며 말했다."], ["“Poor Bessy!", "“가엾은 베시!"], ["I never thought I should not see her again.”", "다시는 못 볼 줄은 생각도 못 했는데.”"]], [["“Why, that’s another thing.", "“그런데 말이죠, 또 한 가지가 있어요."], ["This girl downstairs wanted me to ask you, if you would like to see her.”", "아래층에 있는 그 애가 아가씨한테 여쭤 봐 달라던데요, 언니를 보러 가시겠느냐고요.”"]], [["“But she’s dead!”", "“하지만 이미 죽었잖아!”"], ["said Margaret, turning a little pale.", "마거릿이 조금 창백해지며 말했다."], ["“I never saw a dead person.", "“나는 죽은 사람을 본 적이 없어."], ["No!", "아니!"], ["I would rather not.”", "나는 안 보는 게 낫겠어.”"]], [["“I should never have asked you, if you hadn’t come in.", "“아가씨가 들어오시지만 않았어도 여쭙지도 않았을 거예요."], ["I told her you wouldn’t.”", "그 애한테는 안 가실 거라고 말해 두었고요.”"]], [["“I will go down and speak to her,”", "“내가 내려가서 그 애하고 얘기할게.”"], ["said Margaret, afraid lest Dixon’s harshness of manner might wound the poor girl.", "마거릿이 말했다. 딕슨의 모진 말투가 그 가엾은 소녀에게 상처를 줄까 봐 걱정이 되었던 것이다."], ["So, taking the cap in her hand, she went to the kitchen.", "그래서 모자를 손에 든 채 그녀는 부엌으로 갔다."], ["Mary’s face was all swollen with crying, and she burst out afresh when she saw Margaret.", "메리의 얼굴은 울어서 온통 부어 있었고, 마거릿을 보자 다시 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Oh, ma’am, she loved yo’, she loved yo’, she did indeed!”", "“아이고, 아가씨, 우리 언니가 아가씨를 사랑했다우, 사랑했다우, 정말이라니까요!”"], ["And for a long time, Margaret could not get her to say anything more than this.", "그리고 한참 동안 마거릿은 그 애에게서 이 말 말고는 아무 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 마침내 마거릿의 동정과 딕슨의 다그침이 몇 가지 사실을 끄집어냈다."], ["At last, her sympathy, and Dixon’s scolding, forced out a few facts.", "니컬러스 히긴스는 아침에 나갔는데, 그때 베시는 전날과 다름없이 괜찮아 보였다."], ["Nicholas Higgins had gone out in the morning, leaving Bessy as well as on the day before.", "그런데 한 시간 만에 상태가 나빠졌다. 이웃 사람이 메리가 일하는 곳으로 달려갔다."], ["But in an hour she was taken worse; some neighbour ran to the room where Mary was working; they did not know where to find her father; Mary had only come in a few minutes before she died.", "아버지가 어디 있는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 메리는 언니가 숨을 거두기 겨우 몇 분 전에야 들어섰다."]], [["“It were a day or two ago she axed to be buried in somewhat o’ yourn.", "“하루 이틀쯤 전에 언니가 아가씨 물건 뭐라도 하나 걸치고 묻히고 싶다고 했다우."], ["She were never tired o’ talking o’ yo’.", "아가씨 얘기라면 아무리 해도 지칠 줄을 몰랐구먼요."], ["She used to say yo’ were the prettiest thing she ever clapped eyes on.", "여태 눈에 담아 본 사람 중에 아가씨가 제일 어여쁘다고 늘 말했지요."], ["She loved yo’ dearly.", "언니는 아가씨를 끔찍이 좋아했다우."], ["Her last words were ‘Give her my affectionate respects; and keep father fro’ drink.’ Yo’ll come and see her ma’am.", "마지막으로 한 말이 ‘아가씨한테 내 다정한 문안 전해 드려라, 그리고 아버지 술 못 드시게 해라’였어요. 아가씨도 와서 언니를 봐 주시구려."], ["She would ha’ thought it a great compliment, I know.”", "언니가 알면 굉장한 영광으로 여겼을 거라우, 내 알지요.”"]], [["Margaret shrank a little from answering.", "마거릿은 대답하기를 조금 꺼렸다."]], [["“Yes, perhaps I may.", "“그래, 아마 갈 수 있을 거야."], ["Yes, I will.", "그래, 갈게."], ["I’ll come before tea.", "차 마시기 전에 가마."], ["But where’s your father, Mary?”", "그런데 너희 아버지는 어디 계시니, 메리?”"]], [["Mary shook her head, and stood up to be going.", "메리는 고개를 젓고는 가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Miss Hale,”", "“헤일 아가씨.”"], ["said Dixon, in a low voice, “where’s the use o’ your going to see the poor thing laid out?", "딕슨이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “그 가엾은 것 염해 놓은 걸 보러 가신들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["I’d never say a word against it, if it could do the girl any good; and I wouldn’t mind a bit going myself, if that would satisfy her.", "그게 저 애한테 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 저야 한마디도 반대하지 않겠어요. 저 애 마음이 풀린다면 제가 대신 가는 것도 조금도 마다하지 않고요."], ["They’ve just a notion, these common folks, of its being a respect to the departed.", "이런 상것들은 그저 그게 죽은 이한테 예를 갖추는 거라는 생각뿐이거든요."], ["Here,”", "얘야.”"], ["said she, turning sharply round, “I’ll come and see your sister.", "그녀가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. “내가 가서 네 언니를 보마."], ["Miss Hale is busy, and she can’t come, or else she would.”", "헤일 아가씨는 바빠서 못 가신단다. 안 그러면 가셨을 텐데.”"]], [["The girl looked wistfully at Margaret.", "소녀는 아쉬운 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았다."], ["Dixon’s coming might be a compliment, but it was not the same thing to the poor sister, who had had her little pangs of jealousy, during Bessy’s lifetime, at the intimacy between her and the young lady.", "딕슨이 와 주는 것도 영광일 수는 있었으나, 언니가 살아 있는 동안 언니와 저 젊은 아가씨 사이의 다정함에 자잘한 질투를 느껴 온 그 가엾은 동생에게 그것은 결코 같은 일이 아니었다."]], [["“No, Dixon!”", "“아니에요, 딕슨!”"], ["said Margaret with decision.", "마거릿이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“I will go.", "“내가 갈 거예요."], ["Mary, you shall see me this afternoon.”", "메리, 오늘 오후에 나를 보게 될 거야.”"], ["And for fear of her own cowardice, she went away, in order to take from herself any chance of changing her determination.", "그러고는 자기 안의 비겁함이 두려워, 마음을 바꿀 어떤 여지도 스스로에게 남기지 않으려고 그 자리를 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII", "# 제28장"]], [["“Through cross to crown!—And though thy spirit’s life Trials untold assail with giant strength, Good cheer!", "“십자가를 지나 면류관으로! 네 영혼의 삶을 이루 다 말할 수 없는 시련들이 거인의 힘으로 덮쳐 온다 해도, 기운을 내어라!"], ["good cheer!", "기운을 내어라!"], ["Soon ends the bitter strife, And thou shalt reign in peace with Christ at length.”", "쓰라린 싸움은 머잖아 끝나고, 너는 마침내 그리스도와 함께 평화 속에 다스리리라.”"], ["KOSEGARTEN.", "— 코제가르텐."]], [["“Ay sooth, we feel too strong in weal, to need Thee on that road; But woe being come, the soul is dumb, that crieth not on ‘God.’”", "“참으로 그러하옵니다. 평안할 때에는 스스로 너무 강하다 여겨 그 길에서 당신이 필요하지 않다 하지요."], ["MRS.", "그러나 재앙이 닥치면, ‘하느님’을 부르짖지 않는 영혼은 벙어리가 되고 맙니다.”"], ["BROWNING.", "— 브라우닝 부인."]], [["That afternoon she walked swiftly to the Higgins’s house.", "그날 오후 그녀는 히긴스네 집으로 빠르게 걸어갔다."], ["Mary was looking out for her, with a half-distrustful face.", "메리가 반쯤 미심쩍어하는 얼굴로 그녀를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Margaret smiled into her eyes to re-assure her.", "마거릿은 그 눈을 들여다보며 안심시키려 미소 지었다."], ["They passed quickly through the house-place, upstairs, and into the quiet presence of the dead.", "두 사람은 거실을 서둘러 지나 위층으로, 죽은 이가 누운 고요한 자리로 들어갔다."], ["Then Margaret was glad that she had come.", "그제야 마거릿은 오기를 잘했다고 여겼다."], ["The face, so often weary with pain, so restless with troublous thoughts, had now the faint soft smile of eternal rest upon it.", "그토록 자주 고통에 지쳐 있고 어지러운 생각으로 뒤척이던 그 얼굴에 이제는 영원한 안식의 희미하고 부드러운 미소가 어려 있었다."], ["The slow tears gathered into Margaret’s eyes, but a deep calm entered into her soul.", "마거릿의 눈에 눈물이 천천히 고였으나, 깊은 평온이 그녀의 영혼으로 들어왔다."], ["And that was death!", "이것이 죽음이었다!"], ["It looked more peaceful than life.", "삶보다 더 평화로워 보였다."], ["All beautiful scriptures came into her mind.", "아름다운 성경 말씀들이 모두 마음에 떠올랐다."], ["“They rest from their labours.”", "“그들은 수고를 그치고 쉬리라.”"], ["“The weary are at rest.”", "“고단한 이들이 안식에 들었다.”"], ["“He giveth his beloved sleep.”", "“그가 사랑하시는 자에게는 잠을 주시는도다.”"]], [["Slowly, slowly Margaret turned away from the bed.", "마거릿은 천천히, 아주 천천히 침대에서 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Mary was humbly sobbing in the back-ground.", "메리가 뒤쪽에서 조용히 흐느끼고 있었다."], ["They went downstairs without a word.", "두 사람은 한마디도 없이 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["Resting his hand upon the house-table, Nicholas Higgins stood in the midst of the floor; his great eyes startled open by the news he had heard, as he came along the court, from many busy tongues.", "니컬러스 히긴스는 식탁에 손을 짚은 채 방 한가운데 서 있었다. 안뜰을 지나오는 길에 여러 부산한 입에서 들은 소식에 그 커다란 눈이 놀라 크게 벌어져 있었다."], ["His eyes were dry and fierce; studying the reality of her death; bringing himself to understand that her place should know her no more.", "눈은 메마르고 사나웠다. 딸의 죽음이 정말인지 헤아리며, 그 자리가 다시는 그 애를 알아보지 못하리라는 것을 스스로 납득시키려 애쓰는 눈이었다."], ["For she had been sickly, dying so long, that he had persuaded himself she would not die; that she would “pull through.”", "딸이 하도 오래 병약한 채 죽어 가고 있었던 터라, 그는 딸이 죽지 않으리라고, “이겨 낼” 것이라고 스스로를 설득해 두었던 것이다."]], [["Margaret felt as if she had no business to be there, familiarly acquainting herself with the surroundings of death, which he, the father, had only just learnt.", "마거릿은 자기가 그곳에 있을 자격이 없다고 느꼈다. 아버지인 그가 이제야 막 알게 된 죽음의 자리를 자기가 먼저 스스럼없이 익혀 버렸으니 말이다."], ["There had been a pause of an instant on the steep crooked stair, when she first saw him; but now she tried to steal past his abstracted gaze, and to leave him in the solemn circle of his household misery.", "그를 처음 보았을 때 가파르고 굽은 계단에서 잠깐 걸음이 멎었지만, 이제 그녀는 넋 나간 그의 시선을 몰래 지나쳐, 그를 집안의 슬픔이라는 엄숙한 울타리 안에 남겨 두고 나가려 했다."]], [["Mary sat down on the first chair she came to, and throwing her apron over her head, began to cry.", "메리는 눈에 띄는 첫 의자에 주저앉아 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 울기 시작했다."]], [["The noise appeared to rouse him.", "그 소리에 그가 정신을 차린 듯했다."], ["He took sudden hold of Margaret’s arm, and held her till he could gather words to speak.", "그는 갑자기 마거릿의 팔을 붙들고, 말이 나올 때까지 놓지 않았다."], ["His throat seemed dry; they came up thick, and choked, and hoarse:", "목이 바싹 마른 모양이었다. 말은 걸걸하고 막히고 쉰 소리로 올라왔다."]], [["“Were yo’ with her?", "“댁도 그 애 곁에 있었소?"], ["Did yo’ see her die?”", "그 애가 죽는 걸 봤소?”"]], [["“No!”", "“아니요!”"], ["replied Margaret, standing still with the utmost patience, now she found herself perceived.", "마거릿은 이제 자기가 그의 눈에 띄었음을 알고, 더없이 참을성 있게 가만히 선 채 대답했다."], ["It was some time before he spoke again, but he kept his hold on her arm.", "그가 다시 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸으나, 그동안에도 그녀의 팔은 놓지 않았다."]], [["“All men must die,”", "“사람은 다 죽는 법이지.”"], ["said he at last, with a strange sort of gravity, which first suggested to Margaret the idea that he had been drinking—not enough to intoxicate himself, but enough to make his thoughts bewildered.", "마침내 그가 이상하게 묵직한 어조로 말했는데, 마거릿은 그 말투에서 처음으로 그가 술을 마셨구나 하는 생각이 들었다. 취할 만큼은 아니되 생각을 어지럽힐 만큼은 마신 것이었다."], ["“But she were younger than me.”", "“허나 그 애는 나보다 어렸잖소.”"], ["Still he pondered over the event, not looking at Margaret, though he grasped her tight.", "그는 여전히 그 일을 곱씹으며, 마거릿을 꽉 붙든 채로도 그녀를 보지는 않았다."], ["Suddenly, he looked up at her with a wild searching inquiry in his glance.", "그러다 문득 사납게 캐묻는 눈길로 그녀를 올려다보았다."], ["“Yo’re sure and certain she’s dead—not in a dwam, a faint?", "“정말로 틀림없이 죽은 거요, 혼절한 것도 까무러친 것도 아니고?"], ["she’s been so before, often.”", "전에도 자주 그랬는데.”"]], [["“She is dead,”", "“돌아가셨어요.”"], ["replied Margaret.", "마거릿이 대답했다."], ["She felt no fear in speaking to him, though he hurt her arm with his gripe, and wild gleams came across the stupidity of his eyes.", "그의 손아귀가 팔을 아프게 죄었고 흐리멍덩한 눈에 사나운 빛이 스쳤지만, 그에게 말하는 것이 두렵지는 않았다."]], [["“She is dead!”", "“돌아가셨어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He looked at her still with that searching look, which seemed to fade out of his eyes as he gazed.", "그는 여전히 캐묻는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았고, 바라보는 사이 그 눈빛은 서서히 사그라드는 듯했다."], ["Then he suddenly let go his hold of Margaret, and, throwing his body half across the table, he shook it and every piece of furniture in the room, with his violent sobs.", "그러더니 그는 갑자기 마거릿을 놓아 주고, 몸을 던지듯 식탁에 반쯤 엎어져 격렬한 흐느낌으로 식탁을, 방 안의 온갖 세간을 뒤흔들었다."], ["Mary came trembling towards him.", "메리가 떨면서 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Get thee gone!—get thee gone!”", "“저리 가! 저리 가라니까!”"], ["he cried, striking wildly and blindly at her.", "그는 딸을 향해 마구잡이로, 앞도 보지 않고 손을 휘두르며 소리쳤다."], ["“What do I care for thee?”", "“네깟 게 나한테 무슨 소용이야?”"], ["Margaret took her hand and held it softly in hers.", "마거릿은 메리의 손을 잡아 자기 손안에 가만히 감쌌다."], ["He tore his hair, he beat his head against the hard wood, then he lay exhausted and stupid.", "그는 머리를 쥐어뜯고, 딱딱한 나무에 머리를 찧더니, 기진하여 넋을 놓고 늘어졌다."], ["Still his daughter and Margaret did not move.", "그래도 딸과 마거릿은 움직이지 않았다."], ["Mary trembled from head to foot.", "메리는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨었다."]], [["At last—it might have been a quarter of an hour, it might have been an hour—he lifted himself up.", "마침내, 15분쯤이었을 수도 있고 한 시간쯤이었을 수도 있었는데, 그가 몸을 일으켰다."], ["His eyes were swollen and bloodshot, and he seemed to have forgotten that any one was by; he scowled at the watchers when he saw them.", "눈은 붓고 핏발이 서 있었고, 누가 곁에 있다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다. 지켜보고 선 두 사람을 보자 그는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["He shook himself heavily, gave them one more sullen look, spoke never a word, but made for the door.", "그는 몸을 무겁게 한 번 털고, 두 사람에게 다시 한 번 뚱한 눈길을 던지고는, 한마디도 없이 문 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["“Oh, father, father!”", "“아, 아버지, 아버지!”"], ["said Mary, throwing herself upon his arm,—“not to-night!", "메리가 그의 팔에 매달리며 말했다. “오늘 밤만은 안 돼요!"], ["Any night but to-night.", "다른 날은 몰라도 오늘 밤은 안 돼요."], ["Oh, help me!", "아, 좀 도와주세요!"], ["he’s going out to drink again!", "아버지가 또 술 마시러 나가려고 해요!"], ["Father, I’ll not leave yo’.", "아버지, 저는 안 놓을 거예요."], ["Yo’ may strike, but I’ll not leave yo’.", "때리셔도 안 놓을 거예요."], ["She told me last of all to keep yo’ fro’ drink!”", "그 애가 맨 마지막으로 나한테 아버지 술 못 드시게 하라고 했단 말이에요!”"]], [["But Margaret stood in the doorway, silent yet commanding.", "그러나 마거릿이 문간에 서 있었다. 말은 없어도 위엄이 서린 자세였다."], ["He looked up at her defyingly.", "그가 대드는 눈으로 그녀를 올려다보았다."]], [["“It’s my own house.", "“여긴 내 집이오."], ["Stand out o’ the way, wench, or I’ll make yo’!”", "비켜서시오, 아가씨. 안 그러면 내가 비키게 만들 테니!”"], ["He had shaken off Mary with violence; he looked ready to strike Margaret.", "그는 메리를 거칠게 떨쳐 냈고, 마거릿도 칠 기세였다."], ["But she never moved a feature—never took her deep, serious eyes off him.", "그러나 그녀는 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않았고, 깊고 진지한 눈을 그에게서 떼지 않았다."], ["He stared back on her with gloomy fierceness.", "그는 침울한 사나움으로 그녀를 마주 노려보았다."], ["If she had stirred hand or foot, he would have thrust her aside with even more violence than he had used to his own daughter, whose face was bleeding from her fall against a chair.", "그녀가 손이나 발을 조금이라도 움직였더라면, 그는 의자에 부딪혀 얼굴에서 피가 흐르는 제 딸에게 쓴 것보다 더한 힘으로 그녀를 밀쳐 냈을 것이다."]], [["“What are yo’ looking at me in that way for?”", "“왜 그런 눈으로 날 보는 거요?”"], ["asked he at last, daunted and awed by her severe calm.", "마침내 그가 그녀의 엄한 침착함에 기가 꺾이고 눌려 물었다."], ["“If yo’ think for to keep me from going what gait I choose, because she loved yo’—and in my own house, too, where I never asked yo’ to come, yo’re mista’en.", "“그 애가 댁을 좋아했다고 해서, 그것도 오라고 청한 적도 없는 내 집에서 내가 가고 싶은 길을 못 가게 막을 셈이면 잘못 짚었소."], ["It’s very hard upon a man that he can’t go to the only comfort left.”", "사내한테 하나 남은 위안에 가지도 못하게 하다니 너무 모진 일이오.”"]], [["Margaret felt that he acknowledged her power.", "마거릿은 그가 자기 힘을 인정했음을 느꼈다."], ["What could she do next?", "다음으로 무엇을 해야 하나?"], ["He had seated himself on a chair, close to the door; half-conquered half-resenting; intending to go out as soon as she left her position, but unwilling to use the violence he had threatened not five minutes before.", "그는 문 가까이 놓인 의자에 앉아 있었다. 반쯤은 굴복하고 반쯤은 분해하며, 그녀가 자리를 뜨는 즉시 나가려는 심산이면서도, 채 5분도 전에 을러댔던 그 폭력은 쓰고 싶지 않은 것이었다."], ["Margaret laid her hand on his arm.", "마거릿은 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Come with me,”", "“저와 함께 가세요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Come and see her!”", "“가서 그 애를 보세요!”"]], [["The voice in which she spoke was very low and solemn; but there was no fear or doubt expressed in it, either of him or of his compliance.", "그녀가 말한 목소리는 아주 낮고 엄숙했다. 그러나 그 목소리에는 그에 대한, 또 그가 따르리라는 것에 대한 두려움이나 의심이 조금도 담겨 있지 않았다."], ["He sullenly rose up.", "그는 뚱하니 일어섰다."], ["He stood uncertain, with dogged irresolution upon his face.", "그는 완고한 망설임을 얼굴에 띤 채 우두커니 서 있었다."], ["She waited him there; quietly and patiently waited for his time to move.", "그녀는 그 자리에서 그를 기다렸다. 그가 움직일 때가 되기를 조용히, 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["He had a strange pleasure in making her wait; but at last he moved towards the stairs.", "그는 그녀를 기다리게 하는 데서 묘한 즐거움을 느꼈으나, 마침내 계단 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["She and he stood by the corpse.", "그와 그녀는 주검 곁에 섰다."]], [["“Her last words to Mary were, ‘Keep my father fro’ drink.’”", "“그 애가 메리에게 남긴 마지막 말은 ‘아버지 술 못 드시게 해라’였어요.”"]], [["“It canna hurt her now,”", "“이제 와서 그게 그 애를 아프게 하진 않소.”"], ["muttered he.", "그가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Nought can hurt her now.”", "“이제 그 무엇도 그 애를 아프게 못 하지.”"], ["Then, raising his voice to a wailing cry, he went on: “We may quarrel and fall out—we may make peace and be friends—we may clem to skin and bone—and nought o’ all our griefs will ever touch her more.", "그러다 목소리를 높여 울부짖듯 이어 갔다. “우린 다투고 틀어지기도 하고, 화해하고 다시 좋아지기도 하고, 뼈만 남도록 굶기도 하겠지만, 우리 온갖 설움 가운데 어느 하나도 이제 다시는 그 애한테 닿지 못하오."], ["Hoo’s had her portion on ’em.", "제 몫은 이미 다 치렀으니."], ["What wi’ hard work first, and sickness at last, hoo’ led the life of a dog.", "처음엔 고된 일, 나중엔 병, 개 같은 삶을 살았지."], ["And to die without knowing one good piece o’ rejoicing in all her days!", "그러고는 평생에 기뻐할 일 하나 제대로 알지도 못한 채 죽었소!"], ["Nay, wench, whatever hoo said, hoo can know nought about it now, and I mun ha’ a sup o’ drink just to steady me again sorrow.”", "아니야, 아가씨, 그 애가 무슨 말을 했든 이제 그 애는 아무것도 모르오. 그러니 나는 이 설움에 몸을 가누려면 한 모금 마셔야겠소.”"]], [["“No,”", "“안 됩니다.”"], ["said Margaret, softening with his softened manner.", "마거릿은 누그러진 그의 태도에 따라 부드러워지며 말했다."], ["“You shall not.", "“그러시면 안 돼요."], ["If her life has been what you say, at any rate she did not fear death as some do.", "그 애의 삶이 정말 그러했다면, 적어도 그 애는 남들처럼 죽음을 두려워하지는 않았어요."], ["Oh, you should have heard her speak of the life to come—the life hidden with God, that she is now gone to.”", "아, 그 애가 다가올 삶에 대해, 하느님 안에 감추어진 삶, 이제 그 애가 간 그 삶에 대해 말하는 걸 들으셨어야 했는데.”"]], [["He shook his head, glancing sideways up at Margaret as he did so.", "그는 고개를 저으며 곁눈으로 마거릿을 올려다보았다."], ["His pale haggard face struck her painfully.", "창백하고 초췌한 그 얼굴이 그녀의 마음을 아프게 찔렀다."]], [["“You are sorely tried.", "“몹시 시달리셨군요."], ["Where have you been all day—not at work?”", "하루 종일 어디 계셨어요? 일하러 가신 건 아니고요?”"]], [["“Not at work, sure enough,”", "“일하러 간 건 아니지, 암.”"], ["said he, with a short, grim laugh.", "그가 짧고 험한 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“Not at what you call work.", "“댁이 말하는 그런 일 말이오."], ["I were at the Committee, till I were sickened out wi’ trying to make fools hear reason.", "나는 위원회에 있었소. 멍청이들한테 이치를 알아듣게 하려다 넌더리가 날 때까지 말이오."], ["I were fetched to Boucher’s wife afore seven this morning.", "오늘 아침 일곱 시도 되기 전에 바우처 마누라한테 불려 갔소."], ["She’s bed-fast, but she were raving and raging to know where her dunner-headed brute of a chap was, as if I’d to keep him—as if he were fit to be ruled by me.", "자리보전을 하고 누웠으면서도 제 서방 그 대가리 빈 짐승이 어디 갔느냐고 악을 쓰고 길길이 뛰더군. 내가 그자를 맡아 두기라도 한 것처럼, 그자가 내 말을 들어먹을 위인이라도 되는 것처럼!"], ["The d—d fool, who has put his foot in all our plans!", "우리 계획을 죄다 망쳐 놓은 그 빌어먹을 멍청이가!"], ["And I’ve walked my feet sore wi’ going about for to see men who wouldn’t be seen, now the law is raised against us.", "그러고는 법이 우리를 겨누고 있는 판에 만나 주려 하지도 않는 사람들을 찾아다니느라 발이 부르트도록 걸었소."], ["And I were sore-hearted, too, which is worse than sore-footed; and if I did see a friend who ossed to treat me, I never knew hoo lay a-dying here.", "그리고 발보다 더한 게 마음이 아팠소. 술 한잔 사겠다는 벗을 만났다 한들, 그 애가 여기서 죽어 가는 줄 내가 어찌 알았겠소."], ["Bess, lass, thou’d believe me, thou wouldst—wouldstn’t thou?”", "베스, 얘야, 너는 나를 믿어 주겠지, 그렇지 않으냐?”"], ["turning to the poor dumb form with wild appeal.", "그는 말 못 하는 가여운 형체를 향해 사납게 하소연하며 돌아섰다."]], [["“I am sure,” said Margaret, “I am sure you did not know; it was quite sudden. But, now, you see, it would be different; you do know; you do see her lying there; you hear what she said with her last breath. You will not go?”", "“틀림없이,” 마거릿이 말했다. “틀림없이 모르셨을 거예요. 아주 갑작스러웠으니까요. 하지만 이제는 다르지요. 이제는 아시잖아요. 그 애가 저기 누워 있는 걸 보고 계시잖아요. 그 애가 마지막 숨으로 한 말도 들으셨고요. 안 나가실 거죠?”"]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["In fact, where was he to look for comfort?", "사실, 그가 위안을 어디서 찾을 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“Come home with me,”", "“저희 집으로 같이 가세요.”"], ["said she at last, with a bold venture, half trembling at her own proposal as she made it.", "마침내 그녀가 대담하게 나서며, 그 말을 하는 스스로에게 반쯤 떨면서 말했다."], ["“At least you shall have some comfortable food, which I’m sure you need.”", "“적어도 든든한 음식은 드실 수 있을 거예요. 그게 꼭 필요하실 테니까요.”"]], [["“Yo’r father’s a parson?”", "“댁 아버지가 목사요?”"], ["asked he, with a sudden turn in his ideas.", "그가 생각을 홱 돌려 물었다."]], [["“He was,”", "“그러셨지요.”"], ["said Margaret, shortly.", "마거릿이 짧게 말했다."]], [["“I’ll go and take a dish o’ tea with him, since yo’ve asked me.", "“청하시니 가서 차나 한 잔 마시겠소."], ["I’ve many a thing I often wished to say to a parson, and I’m not particular as to whether he’s preaching now, or not.”", "목사한테 한번 말해 보고 싶던 게 여럿 있었소. 지금 설교를 하고 있든 말든 나는 개의치 않으니.”"]], [["Margaret was perplexed; his drinking tea with her father, who would be totally unprepared for his visitor—her mother so ill—seemed utterly out of the question; and yet, if she drew back now, it would be worse than ever—sure to drive him to the gin-shop.", "마거릿은 난감했다. 손님이 오리라고는 전혀 대비가 없을 아버지와 그가 차를 마신다는 것, 어머니는 저리 편찮으신데, 도무지 될 법한 일이 아니었다."], ["She thought that if she could only get him to their own house, it was so great a step gained that she would trust to the chapter of accidents for the next.", "그러나 여기서 물러선다면 전보다 더 나빠질 터, 그를 술집으로 몰아넣는 것이나 다름없었다. 그녀는 그를 집까지만 데려갈 수 있다면 그것만으로도 큰 걸음을 뗀 셈이니, 그다음은 일이 되어 가는 대로 맡겨 보자고 생각했다."]], [["“Good-bye, ou’d wench!", "“잘 가라, 우리 딸!"], ["We’ve parted company at last, we have!", "우린 마침내 갈라섰구나, 그래!"], ["But thou’st been a blessin’ to thy father ever sin’ thou wert born.", "허나 너는 태어난 날부터 네 아비한테 복이었다."], ["Bless thy white lips, lass,—they’ve a smile on ’em now!", "네 하얀 입술에 복이 있기를, 얘야. 지금 그 입술에 웃음이 어렸구나!"], ["and I’m glad to see it once again, though I’m lone and forlorn for evermore.”", "나야 이제 영영 외롭고 쓸쓸하겠지만, 그걸 한 번 더 보게 되어 기쁘다.”"]], [["He stooped down and fondly kissed his daughter; covered up her face, and turned to follow Margaret.", "그는 몸을 굽혀 딸에게 다정히 입을 맞추고, 얼굴을 덮은 뒤 마거릿을 따라 나서려 돌아섰다. 그녀는 서둘러 아래층으로 내려가 메리에게 그렇게 하기로 했다고 일러 두었다."], ["She had hastily gone down stairs to tell Mary of the arrangement; to say it was the only place she could think of to keep him from the gin-palace; to urge Mary to come too, for her heart smote her at the idea of leaving the poor affectionate girl alone.", "아버지를 술집에서 떼어 놓을 방도로 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 곳이 그것뿐이었다고, 메리도 함께 가자고 채근했다. 정 많은 그 가여운 아이를 혼자 두고 간다는 생각에 마음이 찔렸던 것이다."], ["But Mary had friends amongst the neighbours, she said, who would come in and sit a bit with her; it was all right; but father—", "그러나 메리는 이웃에 벗들이 있어 와서 함께 앉아 있어 줄 거라고, 자기는 괜찮다고 했다. 다만 아버지가—"]], [["He was there by them or she would have spoken more.", "그가 곁에 와 있지 않았더라면 메리는 더 말했을 것이다. 그는 자기 감정을 떨쳐 낸 참이었다."], ["He had shaken off his emotion, as if he was ashamed of having ever given way to it; and had even o’erleaped himself so much that he assumed a sort of bitter mirth, like the crackling of thorns under a pot.", "마치 한때 거기에 무너졌다는 것이 부끄럽기라도 한 듯이. 게다가 도가 지나칠 만큼 스스로를 몰아붙여, 솥 밑에서 가시나무가 타닥거리는 것 같은 씁쓸한 흥까지 꾸며 냈다."]], [["“I’m going to take my tea wi’ her father, I am.”", "“나는 저 아가씨 아버지랑 차를 마시러 간다, 암.”"]], [["But he slouched his cap low down over his brow as he went out into the street, and looked neither to the right nor to the left, while he tramped along by Margaret’s side; he feared being upset by the words, still more the looks, of sympathising neighbours.", "그러나 그는 거리로 나서면서 모자를 눈썹까지 푹 눌러썼고, 마거릿 곁에서 터벅터벅 걷는 동안 오른쪽도 왼쪽도 보지 않았다. 동정하는 이웃들의 말에, 그보다도 그 눈길에 마음이 무너질까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["So he and Margaret walked in silence.", "그리하여 그와 마거릿은 말없이 걸었다."]], [["As he got near the street in which he knew she lived, he looked down at his clothes, his hands, his shoes.", "그녀가 사는 줄로 아는 거리에 가까워지자, 그는 자기 옷과 손과 신발을 내려다보았다."]], [["“I should m’appen ha’ cleaned mysel’, first.”", "“아무래도 몸을 좀 씻고 왔어야 했는데.”"]], [["It certainly would have been desirable, but Margaret assured him he should be allowed to go into the yard, and have soap and towel provided; she would not let him slip out of her hands just then.", "그러는 편이 분명 나았을 것이나, 마거릿은 뒤뜰에 들어가도 좋고 비누와 수건을 내어 주겠다고 그를 안심시켰다. 지금 이 순간 그를 손아귀에서 놓칠 수는 없었다."]], [["While he followed the house-servant along the passage, and through the kitchen, stepping cautiously on every dark mark in the pattern of the oil-cloth, in order to conceal his dirty foot-prints, Margaret ran upstairs.", "그가 하녀를 따라 복도를 지나 부엌을 통과하며, 더러운 발자국을 감추려고 유포 무늬의 어두운 자국마다 조심조심 발을 디디는 동안, 마거릿은 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["She met Dixon on the landing.", "층계참에서 딕슨과 마주쳤다."]], [["“How is mamma?—where is papa?”", "“어머니는 어떠세요? 아버지는 어디 계시고요?”"]], [["Missus was tired, and gone into her own room.", "마님은 지치셔서 방으로 들어가셨다고 했다."], ["She had wanted to go to bed, but Dixon had persuaded her to lie down on the sofa, and have her tea brought to her there; it would be better than getting restless by being too long in bed.", "자리에 누우려 하셨지만 딕슨이 소파에 기대어 계시라고, 차도 거기로 가져다 드리겠다고 설득했다는 것이다. 너무 오래 자리에 누워 있다가 안절부절못하시는 것보다는 그편이 나을 터였다."]], [["So far, so good.", "거기까지는 다행이었다."], ["But where was Mr. Hale?", "그런데 헤일 씨는 어디 계신가?"], ["In the drawing-room.", "응접실에 계셨다."], ["Margaret went in half breathless with the hurried story she had to tell.", "마거릿은 서둘러 전해야 할 이야기로 숨이 반쯤 찬 채 들어갔다."], ["Of course, she told it incompletely; and her father was rather “taken aback”", "물론 이야기는 온전치 못했다."], ["by the idea of the drunken weaver awaiting him in his quiet study, with whom he was expected to drink tea, and on whose behalf Margaret was anxiously pleading. The meek, kind-hearted Mr.", "아버지는 자기 조용한 서재에서 자기를 기다리고 있다는, 함께 차를 마셔야 한다는, 그리고 마거릿이 애타게 두둔하고 있는 그 술 취한 직조공이라는 생각에 적잖이 “당황”했다."], ["Hale would have readily tried to console him in his grief, but, unluckily, the point Margaret dwelt upon most forcibly was the fact of his having been drinking, and her having brought him home with her as a last expedient to keep him from the gin-shop.", "온순하고 마음씨 고운 헤일 씨였다면 슬픔에 잠긴 그를 기꺼이 위로하려 했겠지만, 하필 마거릿이 가장 힘주어 말한 대목이 그가 술을 마셨다는 것, 그리고 그를 술집에서 떼어 놓을 마지막 방편으로 집까지 데려왔다는 것이었다."], ["One little event had come out of another so naturally that Margaret was hardly conscious of what she had done, till she saw the slight look of repugnance on her father’s face.", "사소한 일들이 하도 자연스럽게 꼬리를 물고 이어진 터라, 마거릿은 아버지 얼굴에 스치는 옅은 혐오의 빛을 보고서야 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 겨우 깨달았다."]], [["“Oh, papa!", "“아, 아버지!"], ["he really is a man you will not dislike—if you won’t be shocked to begin with.”", "처음에만 놀라지 않으시면, 정말이지 아버지가 싫어하실 사람이 아니에요.”"]], [["“But, Margaret, to bring a drunken man home—and your mother so ill!”", "“하지만 마거릿, 술 취한 사람을 집에 데려오다니. 어머니는 저렇게 편찮으신데!”"]], [["Margaret’s countenance fell.", "마거릿의 얼굴이 어두워졌다."], ["“I am sorry, papa.", "“죄송해요, 아버지."], ["He is very quiet—he is not tipsy at all.", "아주 조용한 분이에요. 전혀 취하지 않았어요."], ["He was only rather strange at first, but that might be the shock of poor Bessy’s death.”", "처음에만 좀 이상했을 뿐인데, 그것도 가엾은 베시가 죽은 충격 때문일 거예요.”"], ["Margaret’s eyes filled with tears.", "마거릿의 눈에 눈물이 고였다."], ["Mr. Hale took hold of her sweet pleading face in both his hands, and kissed her forehead.", "헤일 씨는 애원하는 그 어여쁜 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고 이마에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“It is all right, dear.", "“괜찮다, 얘야."], ["I’ll go and make him as comfortable as I can, and do you attend to your mother.", "내가 가서 될 수 있는 대로 편히 해 주마. 너는 어머니를 돌봐 드려라."], ["Only, if you can come in and make a third in the study, I shall be glad.”", "다만 네가 서재에 들어와 셋이 함께해 준다면 나야 기쁘겠구나.”"]], [["“Oh, yes—thank you.” But as Mr.", "“아, 네, 고마워요, 아버지.”"], ["Hale was leaving the room, she ran after him.", "그러나 헤일 씨가 방을 나서려 할 때 그녀가 뒤쫓아 갔다."]], [["“Papa—you must not wonder at what he says: he’s an——, I mean he does not believe much in what we do.”", "“아버지, 저분이 하는 말에 놀라지 마세요. 저분은 무신—, 그러니까 우리가 믿는 것을 그리 믿지 않는 분이에요.”"]], [["“Oh dear!", "“저런!"], ["a drunken infidel weaver!”", "술 취한 무신론자 직조공이라니!”"], ["said Mr. Hale to himself, in dismay.", "헤일 씨는 낙담하여 혼잣말을 했다."], ["But to Margaret he only said, “If your mother goes to sleep, be sure you come directly.”", "그러나 마거릿에게는 이렇게만 말했다. “어머니가 잠드시거든 곧바로 내려오너라.”"]], [["Margaret went into her mother’s room.", "마거릿은 어머니 방으로 들어갔다."], ["Mrs. Hale lifted herself up from a doze.", "헤일 부인이 선잠에서 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“When did you write to Frederick, Margaret?", "“프레더릭한테 언제 편지를 썼지, 마거릿?"], ["Yesterday, or the day before?”", "어제였니, 그저께였니?”"]], [["“Yesterday, mamma.”", "“어제요, 어머니.”"]], [["“Yesterday, and the letter went?”", "“어제, 그래서 편지는 부쳤고?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["I took it myself.”", "제가 직접 가지고 갔어요.”"]], [["“Oh, Margaret, I’m so afraid of his coming!", "“아, 마거릿, 그 애가 온다니 얼마나 무서운지!"], ["If he should be recognized!", "혹시라도 알아보는 사람이 있으면!"], ["If he should be taken!", "붙잡히기라도 하면!"], ["If he should be executed, after all these years that he has kept away and lived in safety!", "그 오랜 세월 멀리 떨어져 무사히 살았는데 끝내 처형이라도 당하면!"], ["I keep falling asleep and dreaming that he is caught and being tried.”", "자꾸 깜빡 잠이 들면 그 애가 붙잡혀 재판받는 꿈을 꾼단다.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma, don’t be afraid.", "“아, 어머니, 두려워 마세요."], ["There will be some risk, no doubt; but we will lessen it as much as ever we can.", "위험이야 어느 정도 있겠지요. 하지만 저희가 할 수 있는 데까지 줄일 거예요."], ["And it is so little!", "게다가 위험은 아주 적어요!"], ["Now, if we were at Helstone, there would be twenty—a hundred times as much.", "지금 저희가 헬스톤에 있었다면 스무 배, 백 배는 됐을걸요. 거기서는 다들 오빠를 기억할 테고, 집에 낯선 사람이 있다는 걸 알면 틀림없이 프레더릭이라고 짐작할 거예요."], ["There, everybody would remember him; and if there was a stranger known to be in the house, they would be sure to guess it was Frederick; while here, nobody knows or cares for us enough to notice what we do.", "그런데 여기서는 저희가 무얼 하든 눈여겨볼 만큼 저희를 알거나 관심 두는 사람이 아무도 없잖아요. 딕슨이 용처럼 문을 지킬 거예요."], ["Dixon will keep the door like a dragon—won’t you, Dixon—while he is here?”", "그렇죠, 딕슨? 오빠가 여기 있는 동안 말이에요.”"]], [["“They’ll be clever if they come in past me!”", "“저를 지나쳐 들어온다면 여간내기가 아니겠죠!”"], ["said Dixon, showing her teeth at the bare idea.", "딕슨이 그런 생각만으로도 이를 드러내며 말했다."]], [["“And he need not go out, except in the dusk, poor fellow!”", "“게다가 오빠는 어스름 말고는 나갈 일도 없잖아요, 가여운 사람.”"]], [["“Poor fellow!”", "“가여운 것!”"], ["echoed Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 따라 말했다."], ["“But I almost wish you had not written.", "“그래도 네가 편지를 쓰지 않았더라면 싶구나."], ["Would it be too late to stop him if you wrote again, Margaret?”", "다시 편지를 쓴다면 그 애를 말리기에 너무 늦었을까, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I’m afraid it would, mamma,”", "“늦었을 것 같아요, 어머니.”"], ["said Margaret, remembering the urgency with which she had entreated him to come directly, if he wished to see his mother alive.", "마거릿이 말했다. 어머니가 살아 계실 때 뵙고 싶거든 곧바로 오시라고 얼마나 다급하게 애원했는지를 떠올리면서."]], [["“I always dislike that doing things in such a hurry,”", "“나는 그렇게 서둘러 일을 하는 게 늘 싫더구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."]], [["Margaret was silent.", "마거릿은 말이 없었다."]], [["“Come now, ma’am,”", "“자, 마님.”"], ["said Dixon, with a kind of cheerful authority, “you know seeing Master Frederick is just the very thing of all others you’re longing for.", "딕슨이 밝은 목소리로 짐짓 단호하게 말했다. “프레더릭 도련님을 뵙는 게 세상 무엇보다 마님이 바라시던 일이잖습니까요."], ["And I’m glad Miss Margaret wrote off straight, without shilly-shallying.", "저는 마거릿 아가씨가 우물쭈물하지 않고 곧장 편지를 써 보내신 게 다행이라고 생각합니다요."], ["I’ve had a great mind to do it myself.", "저도 제가 쓰고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았거든요."], ["And we’ll keep him snug, depend upon it.", "그리고 도련님은 저희가 꼭꼭 숨겨 드릴 테니 믿으세요."], ["There’s only Martha in the house that would not do a good deal to save him on a pinch; and I’ve been thinking she might go and see her mother just at that very time.", "이 집에서 급할 때 도련님을 구하려고 힘써 주지 않을 사람은 마사뿐인데, 저는 바로 그때 그 애를 제 어미한테 다녀오라고 보낼까 생각하고 있었습니다요. 그 애가 한두 번 가고 싶다고 했거든요."], ["She’s been saying once or twice she should like to go, for her mother has had a stroke since she came here; only she didn’t like to ask.", "여기 온 뒤로 어미가 중풍을 맞았다니까요. 다만 차마 청하지를 못했던 게지요."], ["But I’ll see about her being safe off, as soon as we know when he comes, God bless him!", "도련님이 언제 오시는지 알게 되는 대로 제가 그 애를 무사히 내보낼 궁리를 하겠습니다요. 하느님이 도련님을 지켜 주시기를!"], ["So take your tea, ma’am, in comfort, and trust to me.”", "그러니 마님, 마음 편히 차나 드시고 저만 믿으세요.”"]], [["Mrs. Hale did trust in Dixon more than in Margaret.", "헤일 부인은 마거릿보다 딕슨을 더 믿었다."], ["Dixon’s words quieted her for the time.", "딕슨의 말이 한동안 그녀를 가라앉혔다."], ["Margaret poured out the tea in silence, trying to think of something agreeable to say; but her thoughts made answer something like Daniel O’Rourke, when the man-in-the moon asked him to get off his reaping-hook.", "마거릿은 말없이 차를 따르며 무언가 기분 좋은 이야깃거리를 떠올리려 애썼다. 그러나 그녀의 생각은 달 속 사내가 낫에서 내려가 달라고 했을 때의 대니얼 오루크처럼 대꾸했다."], ["“The more you ax us, the more we won’t stir.”", "청하면 청할수록 우리는 더 안 움직인다고."], ["The more she tried to think of something—anything besides the danger to which Frederick would be exposed—the more closely her imagination clung to the unfortunate idea presented to her.", "프레더릭이 처하게 될 위험 말고 다른 무엇이든 떠올리려 애쓸수록, 그녀의 상상은 눈앞에 떠오른 그 불행한 생각에 더욱 바싹 달라붙었다."], ["Her mother prattled with Dixon, and seemed to have utterly forgotten the possibility of Frederick being tried and executed—utterly forgotten that at her wish, if by Margaret’s deed, he was summoned into this danger.", "어머니는 딕슨과 재잘거리며, 프레더릭이 재판을 받고 처형될 수도 있다는 것을 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 어머니의 바람으로, 비록 마거릿의 손을 빌려서지만, 아들이 이 위험 속으로 불려 왔다는 것도 까맣게 잊은 듯했다."], ["Her mother was one of those who throw out terrible possibilities, miserable probabilities, unfortunate chances of all kinds, as a rocket throws out sparks; but if the sparks light on some combustible matter, they smoulder first, and burst out into a frightful flame at last.", "어머니는 로켓이 불꽃을 뿌리듯 무시무시한 가능성과 참담한 개연성과 온갖 불운한 우연을 내던지는 부류의 사람이었다. 그런데 그 불꽃이 불붙기 쉬운 것에 떨어지면, 처음에는 속으로만 타다가 끝내 끔찍한 불길로 터져 나오는 법이다."], ["Margaret was glad when, her filial duties gently and carefully performed, she could go down into the study.", "마거릿은 딸의 도리를 다정하고 조심스레 마친 뒤 서재로 내려갈 수 있게 되자 마음이 놓였다."], ["She wondered how her father and Higgins had got on.", "아버지와 히긴스가 어떻게 지내고 있을지 궁금했다."]], [["In the first place, the decorous, kind-hearted, simple, old-fashioned gentleman, had unconsciously called out, by his own refinement and courteousness of manner, all the latent courtesy in the other.", "우선, 예의 바르고 마음씨 곱고 소박하고 구식인 그 신사는 자기 자신의 세련되고 정중한 태도로 상대 안에 잠들어 있던 온갖 예의를 저도 모르게 불러내 놓았다."]], [["Mr. Hale treated all his fellow-creatures alike: it never entered into his head to make any difference because of their rank.", "헤일 씨는 모든 사람을 똑같이 대했다."], ["He placed a chair for Nicholas; stood up till he, at Mr.", "신분 때문에 차이를 두어야 한다는 생각은 그의 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["Hale’s request, took a seat; and called him, invariably, “Mr. Higgins,”", "그는 니컬러스에게 의자를 내주었고, 자기 청에 따라 니컬러스가 앉을 때까지 서 있었다."], ["instead of the curt “Nicholas” or “Higgins,”", "그리고 “술 취한 무신론자 직조공”이 익숙해져 있던 퉁명스러운 “니컬러스”나 “히긴스”"], ["to which the “drunken infidel weaver”", "대신 한결같이 “히긴스 씨”라고 불렀다."], ["had been accustomed. But Nicholas was neither an habitual drunkard nor a thorough infidel.", "그러나 니컬러스는 습관적인 술꾼도 아니었고 철저한 무신론자도 아니었다."], ["He drank to drown care, as he would have himself expressed it: and he was infidel so far as he had never yet found any form of faith to which he could attach himself, heart and soul.", "그는 자기 말로 하자면 근심을 잠재우려고 마셨을 뿐이고, 마음과 영혼을 다해 매달릴 만한 신앙의 형태를 아직 찾지 못했다는 점에서만 무신론자였다."]], [["Margaret was a little surprised, and very much pleased, when she found her father and Higgins in earnest conversation—each speaking with gentle politeness to the other, however their opinions might clash.", "마거릿은 아버지와 히긴스가 진지한 대화를 나누고 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐고 무척 기뻤다. 의견이 아무리 부딪쳐도 둘은 서로에게 부드럽고 정중하게 말하고 있었다."], ["Nicholas—clean, tidied (if only at the pump-trough), and quiet spoken—was a new creature to her, who had only seen him in the rough independence of his own hearthstone. He had “slicked”", "씻고 매무새를 다듬은 채, 비록 펌프 물통에서였을 뿐이지만, 조용조용 말하는 니컬러스는 제 집 화롯가의 투박한 자립 속에서만 그를 보아 온 그녀에게 새로운 사람이었다."], ["his hair down with the fresh water; he had adjusted his neck-handkerchief, and borrowed an odd candle-end to polish his clogs with; and there he sat, enforcing some opinion on her father, with a strong Darkshire accent, it is true, but with a lowered voice, and a good, earnest composure on his face.", "그는 찬물로 머리를 착 붙여 빗어 넘겼고, 목수건을 매만졌으며, 남는 양초 토막을 빌려 나막신을 문질러 광을 내 놓았다. 그러고는 거기 앉아, 물론 짙은 다크셔 사투리로였지만, 낮춘 목소리와 얼굴에 어린 착실하고 진지한 침착함으로 아버지에게 어떤 의견을 힘주어 펴고 있었다."], ["Her father, too, was interested in what his companion was saying.", "아버지 또한 상대의 말에 흥미를 느끼고 있었다."], ["He looked round as she came in, smiled, and quietly gave her his chair, and then sat down afresh as quickly as possible, and with a little bow of apology to his guest for the interruption.", "아버지는 그녀가 들어오자 돌아보며 미소 짓고는 조용히 자기 의자를 내주었다. 그러고는 되도록 빨리 다시 앉으며, 이야기를 끊은 데 대해 손님에게 가볍게 고개 숙여 사과했다."], ["Higgins nodded to her as a sign of greeting; and she softly adjusted her working materials on the table, and prepared to listen.", "히긴스는 인사 삼아 그녀에게 고개를 끄덕였고, 그녀는 바느질감을 조용히 탁자 위에 놓고 들을 채비를 했다."]], [["“As I was a-saying, sir, I reckon yo’d not ha’ much belief in yo’ if yo’ lived here,—if yo’d been bred here.", "“아까 말씀드린 대로요, 선생님, 만일 선생님이 여기서 사셨다면, 여기서 나고 자라셨다면 믿음이란 게 그리 크지 않으셨을 거요. 말을 잘못 쓰거든 용서하시고."], ["I ax your pardon if I use wrong words; but what I mean by belief just now, is a-thinking on sayings and maxims and promises made by folk yo’ never saw, about the things and the life yo’ never saw, nor no one else.", "지금 내가 믿음이라 하는 건, 본 적도 없는 사람들이 본 적도 없는 것들과 삶에 대해 해 놓은 말이며 격언이며 약속을 곱씹는 걸 두고 하는 소리요. 아무도 본 적이 없는 것들 말이오."], ["Now, yo’ say these are true things, and true sayings, and a true life.", "그런데 선생님은 그게 참된 것이고 참된 말이고 참된 삶이라 하시지. 나는 그저 묻겠소."], ["I just say, where’s the proof?", "증거가 어디 있소? 내 둘레에는 나보다 지혜롭고 나보다 몇 곱절 배운 이가 수두룩하오."], ["There’s many and many a one wiser, and scores better learned than I am around me,—folk who’ve had time to think on these things,—while my time has had to be gi’en up to getting my bread.", "그런 것들을 생각할 짬이 있던 사람들 말이오. 나야 밥벌이에 짬을 다 바쳐야 했지만."], ["Well, I sees these people.", "그래, 나는 그 사람들을 보오. 그 삶이 내게 훤히 드러나 있소."], ["Their lives is pretty much open to me.", "진짜 사람들이오. 그이들은 성경을 믿지 않소."], ["They’re real folk.", "천만에. 격식으로야 믿는다고들 하겠지."], ["They don’t believe i’ the Bible,—not they.", "허나 선생님, 그이들이 아침에 눈뜨자마자 첫마디로 ‘영생을 얻으려면 무엇을 할까?’ 한다고 보시오? 아니면 ‘오늘 이 좋은 날 내 지갑을 채우려면 무엇을 할까?"], ["They may say they do, for form’s sake; but Lord, sir, d’ye think their first cry i’ th’ morning is, ‘What shall I do to get hold on eternal life?’ or ‘What shall I do to fill my purse this blessed day?", "어디로 갈까? 어떤 흥정을 붙일까?’ 하겠소?"], ["Where shall I go?", "지갑과 금과 지폐는 진짜요. 만질 수 있고 손에 잡히는 것이오."], ["What bargains shall I strike?’ The purse and the gold and the notes is real things; things as can be felt and touched; them’s realities; and eternal life is all a talk, very fit for—I ax your pardon, sir; yo’r a parson out o’ work, I believe.", "그게 실물이지. 영생이란 건 죄다 말뿐이고, 그거야 딱 어울리는 게, 용서하시오, 선생님, 선생님은 일자리 없는 목사시라지."], ["Well!", "원!"], ["I’ll never speak disrespectful of a man in the same fix as I’m in mysel.", "나와 같은 처지에 놓인 사람을 두고 함부로 말하지는 않겠소. 다만 한 가지만 더 여쭙겠소, 선생님."], ["But I’ll just ax yo’ another question, sir, and I dunnot want yo’ to answer it, only to put in yo’r pipe, and smoke it, afore yo’ go for to set down us, who only believe in what we see, as fools and noddies.", "대답은 바라지 않소. 그저 눈에 보이는 것만 믿는 우리를 바보요 얼간이라 못 박기 전에, 담뱃대에 담아 한번 피워 보시란 말이오."], ["If salvation, and life to come, and what not, was true—not in men’s words, but in men’s hearts’ core—dun yo’ not think they’d din us wi’ it as they do wi’ political ’conomy?", "구원이며 내세며 그 밖의 것들이 참말이라면, 사람들 입으로가 아니라 마음속 깊은 데서 참말이라면, 그이들이 정치경제학을 두고 그러듯 그 이야기로 우리 귀를 때리지 않겠소?"], ["They’re mighty anxious to come round us wi’ that piece o’ wisdom; but t’other would be a greater convarsion, if it were true.”", "그이들은 그 지혜 나부랭이를 우리한테 들이밀려고 안달이 났소. 허나 그게 참말이라면 저쪽이야말로 더 큰 회심이 아니겠소.”"]], [["“But the masters have nothing to do with your religion.", "“하지만 공장주들은 당신들의 신앙과 아무 상관이 없지요."], ["All that they are connected with you in is trade,—so they think,—and all that it concerns them, therefore, to rectify your opinions in is the science of trade.”", "그들이 당신들과 얽혀 있는 것은 오직 장사뿐이니까요, 그들 생각으로는 말입니다. 그러니 그들이 당신들의 견해를 바로잡아야 할 영역도 장사의 학문뿐인 셈이고요.”"]], [["“I’m glad, sir,”", "“선생님이 ‘그들 생각으로는’이라고 덧붙여 주시니 다행이오.” 히긴스가 묘하게 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."], ["said Higgins, with a curious wink of his eye, “that yo’ put in, ‘so they think.’ I’d ha’ thought yo’ a hyprocrite, I’m afeard, if yo’ hadn’t, for all yo’r a parson, or rayther because yo’r a parson.", "“그러지 않으셨다면 나는 선생님을 위선자로 여겼을지 모르겠소. 목사시라도, 아니 오히려 목사시니까 더욱."], ["Yo’ see, if yo’d spoken o’ religion as a thing that, if it was true, it didn’t concern all men to press on all men’s attention, above everything else in this ’varsal earth, I should ha’ thought yo’ a knave for to be a parson; and I’d rather think yo’ a fool than a knave.", "보시오, 만일 선생님이 종교를 두고, 그게 참말이라 해도 이 온 세상 무엇보다 앞서 모든 사람이 모든 사람에게 힘주어 알릴 일은 아니라는 식으로 말씀하셨다면, 나는 선생님을 목사질을 하는 사기꾼으로 여겼을 거요. 그리고 나는 선생님을 사기꾼으로 여기느니 바보로 여기는 편이 낫소."], ["No offence, I hope, sir.”", "언짢게 듣지 마시오, 선생님.”"]], [["“None at all.", "“조금도요."], ["You consider me mistaken, and I consider you far more fatally mistaken.", "당신은 나를 그르다 여기고, 나는 당신을 훨씬 더 치명적으로 그르다 여기지요."], ["I don’t expect to convince you in a day,—not in one conversation; but let us know each other, and speak freely to each other about these things, and the truth will prevail.", "하루 만에, 한 번의 대화로 당신을 설득하리라고는 바라지 않습니다. 다만 서로 알아 가고 이런 것들에 대해 서로 거리낌 없이 말합시다."], ["I should not believe in God if I did not believe that.", "그러면 진리가 이길 겁니다. 그것을 믿지 않는다면 나는 하느님도 믿지 않을 겁니다."], ["Mr. Higgins, I trust, whatever else you have given up, you believe”—(Mr.", "히긴스 씨, 다른 무엇을 버렸든 당신은 믿고 계시리라 믿습니다.”"], ["Hale’s voice dropped low in reverence)—“you believe in Him.”", "(헤일 씨의 목소리가 경건하게 낮아졌다.) “당신은 그분을 믿고 계시지요.”"]], [["Nicholas Higgins suddenly stood straight, stiff up.", "니컬러스 히긴스가 갑자기 꼿꼿이, 뻣뻣하게 일어섰다."], ["Margaret started to her feet,—for she thought, by the working of his face, he was going into convulsions.", "마거릿도 벌떡 일어섰다. 씰룩거리는 그의 얼굴을 보고 발작이라도 일으키는 줄 알았던 것이다."], ["Mr. Hale looked at her dismayed.", "헤일 씨가 당황하여 딸을 바라보았다."], ["At last Higgins found words:", "마침내 히긴스가 말을 찾아냈다."]], [["“Man!", "“이보시오!"], ["I could fell yo’ to the ground for tempting me.", "나를 시험한다고 당신을 땅바닥에 메어꽂을 수도 있소. 무슨 권리로 당신 의심으로 나를 떠보는 거요?"], ["Whatten business have yo’ to try me wi’ your doubts?", "저기 누워 있는 그 애를 생각해 보시오. 그런 삶을 살고 간 그 애를."], ["Think o’ her lying theere, after the life hoo’s led; and think then how yo’d deny me the one sole comfort left—that there is a God, and that He set her her life.", "그러고 나서 생각해 보시오, 당신이 나한테 남은 단 하나뿐인 위안을 어떻게 빼앗으려는지를. 하느님이 계시고 그분이 그 애한테 그 삶을 정해 주셨다는 그 위안 말이오."], ["I dunnot believe she’ll ever live again,”", "나는 그 애가 다시 살아나리라고는 믿지 않소.”"], ["said he, sitting down, and drearily going on, as if to the unsympathising fire.", "그가 자리에 앉으며, 아랑곳하지 않는 불더러 하는 말처럼 침울하게 이어 갔다. “그 애가 그토록 시달리고 끝없이 근심하며 살았던 이 삶 말고 다른 삶이 있다고는 믿지 않소."], ["“I dunnot believe in any other life than this, in which she dreed such trouble, and had such never-ending care; and I cannot bear to think it were all a set o’ chances, that might ha’ been altered wi’ a breath o’ wind.", "허나 그게 다 우연들이 모인 것뿐이어서 바람 한 줄기에도 달라질 수 있었다고는 차마 생각 못 하겠소. 내가 하느님을 안 믿는다고 여긴 적도 여러 번 있었지."], ["There’s many a time when I’ve thought I didna believe in God, but I’ve never put it fair out before me in words, as many men do.", "허나 남들처럼 그걸 말로 똑바로 내놓아 본 적은 한 번도 없소. 그리하는 사람들을 비웃어 준 적은 있겠지, 배짱 좋은 척하느라고."], ["I may ha’ laughed at those who did, to brave it out like—but I have looked round at after, to see if He heard me, if so be there was a He; but to-day, when I’m left desolate, I wunnot listen to yo’ wi’ yo’r questions, and yo’r doubts.", "허나 그러고 나서는 늘 둘레를 둘러보았소. 그분이 계신다면 그분이 내 말을 들으셨을까 하고."], ["There’s but one thing steady and quiet i’ all this reeling world, and, reason or no reason, I’ll cling to that.", "그런데 오늘, 이렇게 홀로 남겨진 날에, 나는 당신 물음도 당신 의심도 듣지 않겠소. 이 비틀거리는 세상에서 흔들리지 않고 고요한 건 오직 하나뿐이오."], ["It’s a’ very well for happy folk”——", "이치가 서든 안 서든 나는 거기 매달리겠소. 복 받은 사람들이야 얼마든지”——"]], [["Margaret touched his arm very softly.", "마거릿이 그의 팔을 아주 부드럽게 건드렸다."], ["She had not spoken before, nor had he heard her rise.", "그녀는 그때까지 말이 없었고, 그는 그녀가 일어서는 소리도 듣지 못했다."]], [["“Nicholas, we do not want to reason; you misunderstand my father.", "“니컬러스, 저희는 따지려는 게 아니에요. 아버지를 오해하셨어요."], ["We do not reason—we believe; and so do you.", "저희는 따지지 않아요, 믿을 뿐이지요. 당신도 그렇고요."], ["It is the one sole comfort in such times.”", "이런 때에 그것이 단 하나뿐인 위안이에요.”"]], [["He turned round and caught her hand.", "그는 돌아서서 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["“Ay!", "“옳소!"], ["it is, it is”—(brushing away the tears with the back of his hand).—“But yo’ know, she’s lying dead at home; and I’m welly dazed wi’ sorrow, and at times I hardly know what I’m saying.", "그렇소, 그렇소.” (손등으로 눈물을 훔치며.) “허나 아시겠지만, 그 애가 집에 죽어 누워 있소."], ["It’s as if speeches folk ha’ made—clever and smart things as I’ve thought at the time—come up now my heart’s welly brossen.", "나는 설움에 넋이 반쯤 나갔고, 이따금 내가 무슨 소리를 하는지도 잘 모르겠소. 남들이 해 놓은 말들이, 그때는 영리하고 그럴싸하다 여겼던 말들이, 이제 내 가슴이 다 터져 버린 판에 되살아나는 것 같소."], ["Th’ strike’s failed as well; dun yo’ know that, miss?", "파업도 무너졌소. 그건 아시오, 아가씨?"], ["I were coming whoam to ask her, like a beggar as I am, for a bit o’ comfort i’ that trouble; and I were knocked down by one who telled me she were dead—just dead.", "나는 거지꼴로 그 애한테 그 시름을 좀 달래 달라고 하려고 집으로 오던 참이었소. 그런데 그 애가 죽었다고, 방금 죽었다고 일러 주는 사람한테 얻어맞은 셈이지."], ["That were all; but that were enough for me.”", "그뿐이오. 허나 그것으로 내겐 충분했소.”"]], [["Mr. Hale blew his nose, and got up to snuff the candles in order to conceal his emotion.", "헤일 씨는 코를 풀고는, 북받치는 마음을 감추려고 일어나 촛불 심지를 잘랐다."], ["“He’s not an infidel, Margaret; how could you say so?”", "“저이는 무신론자가 아니로구나, 마거릿. 어떻게 그런 말을 했느냐?”"], ["muttered he reproachfully.", "그가 나무라듯 중얼거렸다."], ["“I’ve a good mind to read him the fourteenth chapter of Job.”", "“욥기 14장을 읽어 주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같구나.”"]], [["“Not yet, papa, I think.", "“아직은 아니에요, 아버지."], ["Perhaps not at all.", "어쩌면 아예 하지 마세요."], ["Let us ask him about the strike, and give him all the sympathy he needs, and hoped to have from poor Bessy.”", "파업에 대해 여쭤보고, 저분이 필요로 하는, 가엾은 베시에게서 얻으려 했던 위로를 다 드리기로 해요.”"]], [["So they questioned and listened.", "그리하여 두 사람은 묻고 들었다."], ["The workmen’s calculations were based (like too many of the masters’) on false premises.", "일꾼들의 셈은, 너무 많은 공장주들의 셈이 그렇듯, 잘못된 전제 위에 서 있었다."], ["They reckoned on their fellow-men as if they possessed the calculable powers of machines, no more, no less; no allowance for human passions getting the better of reason, as in the case of Boucher and the rioters; and believing that the representations of their injuries would have the same effect on strangers far away, as the injuries (fancied or real) had upon themselves.", "그들은 동료 인간을 마치 기계처럼 계산 가능한 힘만 지닌 존재로, 그 이상도 이하도 아닌 것으로 셈했다. 바우처와 폭동을 일으킨 자들의 경우처럼 인간의 격정이 이성을 이겨 버릴 여지는 조금도 두지 않았다."], ["They were consequently surprised and indignant at the poor Irish, who had allowed themselves to be imported and brought over to take their places.", "그리고 자기들이 입은 피해를 알리기만 하면 멀리 있는 낯선 이들에게도 그 피해가, 실제든 상상이든, 자기들에게 일으킨 것과 똑같은 효과를 낳으리라 믿었다."], ["This indignation was tempered in some degree, by contempt for “them Irishers,”", "그래서 그들은 자기 자리를 대신하려고 실려 오도록 스스로를 내맡긴 가여운 아일랜드 사람들에게 놀라고 분개했다."], ["and by pleasure at the idea of the bungling way in which they would set to work, and perplex their new masters with their ignorance and stupidity, strange exaggerated stories of which were already spreading through the town.", "이 분개는 “그 아일랜드 것들”에 대한 멸시로, 또 그들이 서투르게 일을 벌여 무지와 아둔함으로 새 주인들을 곤란하게 만들리라는 생각의 즐거움으로 얼마쯤 누그러졌다. 그런 이야기가 벌써 이상하게 부풀려진 채 온 도시에 퍼지고 있었다."], ["But the most cruel cut of all was that of the Milton workmen, who had defied and disobeyed the commands of the Union to keep the peace, whatever came; who had originated discord in the camp, and spread the panic of the law being arrayed against them.", "그러나 무엇보다 모진 상처는 밀턴 일꾼들 자신에게서 온 것이었다. 무슨 일이 있어도 평온을 지키라는 조합의 명령을 거스르고 어긴 자들, 진영 안에 불화를 일으키고 법이 자기들을 겨누고 있다는 공포를 퍼뜨린 자들이었다."]], [["“And so the strike is at an end,”", "“그래서 파업은 끝났군요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Ay, miss.", "“그렇소, 아가씨."], ["It’s save as save can.", "살고 볼 일이지."], ["Th’ factory doors will need open wide to-morrow to let in all who’ll be axing for work; if it’s only just to show they’d nought to do wi’ a measure, which if we’d been made o’ th’ right stuff would ha’ brought wages up to a point they’n not been at this ten year.”", "내일이면 일자리를 달라고 몰려드는 사람들을 다 들이려고 공장 문을 활짝 열어젖혀야 할 거요. 우리가 제대로 된 재목이었더라면 지난 10년 동안 닿아 본 적 없는 데까지 삯을 끌어올렸을 그 조치와 자기들은 아무 상관이 없었노라 보이려는 것뿐이라 해도 말이오.”"]], [["“You’ll get work, shan’t you?”", "“일자리는 얻으시겠지요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“You’re a famous workman, are not you?”", "“이름난 일꾼이시잖아요?”"]], [["“Hamper’ll let me work at his mill, when he cuts off his right hand—not before, and not after,”", "“햄퍼가 제 오른손을 잘라 내면 그때는 나를 제 공장에서 일하게 해 주겠지. 그전에도, 그 뒤에도 아니고.”"], ["said Nicholas, quietly.", "니컬러스가 조용히 말했다."], ["Margaret was silenced and sad.", "마거릿은 할 말을 잃고 서글퍼졌다."]], [["“About the wages,”", "“삯 이야기 말인데요.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“You’ll not be offended, but I think you make some sad mistakes.", "“언짢게 듣지 마시고요, 내 생각에 당신들은 안타까운 잘못을 좀 저지르는 것 같습니다."], ["I should like to read you some remarks in a book I have.”", "내가 가진 책에 나오는 대목을 좀 읽어 드리고 싶군요.”"], ["He got up and went to his bookshelves.", "그는 일어나 책장으로 갔다."]], [["“Yo’ needn’t trouble yoursel’, sir,” said Nicholas. “Their book-stuff goes in at one ear and out at t’other. I can make nought on’t. Afore Hamper and me had this split, th’ overlooker telled him I were stirring up th’ men to ask for higher wages; and Hamper met me one day in th’ yard. He’d a thin book i’ his hand, and says he, ‘Higgins, I’m told you’re one o’ those damned fools that think you can get higher wages for asking for ’em; ay, and keep ’em up too, when you’ve forced ’em up. Now, I’ll give yo’ a chance and try if yo’ve any sense in yo’. Here’s a book written by a friend o’ mine, and if yo’ll read it yo’ll see how wages find their own level, without either masters or men having aught to do with them; except the men cut their own throats wi’ striking, like the confounded noodles they are.’ Well, now, sir, I put it to yo’, being a parson, and having been in th’ preaching line, and having had to try and bring folk o’er to what yo’ thought was a right way o’ thinking—did yo’ begin by calling ’em fools and such like, or didn’t yo’ rayther give ’em such kind words at first, to make ’em ready for to listen and be convinced, if they could; and in yo’r preaching, did yo’ stop every now and then, and say, half to them and half to yo’rsel’, ‘But yo’r such a pack o’ fools, that I’ve a strong notion it’s no use my trying to put sense into yo’?’ I were not i’ th’ best state, I’ll own, for taking in what Hamper’s friend had to say—I were so vexed at the way it were put to me;—but I thought, ‘Come, I’ll see what these chaps has got to say, and try if it’s them or me as is th’ noodle.’ So I took th’ book and tugged at it; but, Lord bless yo’, it went on about capital and labour, and labour and capital, till it fair sent me off to sleep. I ne’er could rightly fix i’ my mind which was which; and it spoke on ’em as if they was vartues or vices; and what I wanted for to know were the rights o’ men, whether they were rich or poor—so be they only were men.”", "“수고하실 것 없소, 선생님.” 니컬러스가 말했다. “그이들 책 나부랭이는 한 귀로 들어와 한 귀로 나가오. 나는 도무지 알아먹지 못하겠소. 햄퍼와 내가 갈라서기 전에, 십장이 그이한테 내가 사람들을 부추겨 삯을 더 달라 하게 만든다고 일렀소. 그래서 햄퍼가 어느 날 마당에서 나를 만났지. 손에 얇은 책 한 권을 들고서 이러더군. ‘히긴스, 자네는 달라고만 하면 삯을 더 받을 수 있고, 억지로 올려놓으면 그게 유지되기까지 한다고 여기는 그 빌어먹을 바보 가운데 하나라던데. 자, 자네한테 기회를 한 번 주지. 자네한테 머리라는 게 있는지 보세. 여기 내 친구가 쓴 책이 있네. 이걸 읽으면 삯이란 게 공장주도 일꾼도 손댈 것 없이 제 수준을 찾아간다는 걸 알게 될 걸세. 다만 일꾼들이 저 못난 얼간이들답게 파업으로 제 목을 긋는 일만 없다면 말이지.’ 자, 선생님, 목사시고 설교를 해 오셨고 사람들을 옳다고 여기는 길로 데려오려 애써 보신 분이니 여쭙겠소. 선생님은 먼저 그들을 바보니 뭐니 부르는 것으로 시작하셨소, 아니면 그들이 들을 마음이 생기고 될 수 있으면 수긍하도록 처음에는 오히려 좋은 말을 해 주셨소? 그리고 설교하시면서 이따금 멈추어, 반은 그들에게 반은 스스로에게 이렇게 말씀하셨소? ‘허나 너희가 워낙 바보 천지라, 내가 이치를 넣어 주려 애써 봐야 소용없지 싶구나.’ 나도 햄퍼의 친구가 하는 말을 받아들일 만한 마음 상태는 아니었소. 그건 인정하오. 말이 그렇게 나오니 어찌나 부아가 나던지. 허나 그래도 생각했소. ‘그래, 이 양반들이 무슨 소리를 하는지 보자. 얼간이가 그이들인지 나인지 가려 보자.’ 그래서 책을 들고 씨름했소. 헌데 원, 자본이니 노동이니, 노동이니 자본이니 하는 소리가 끝없이 이어져 그만 잠이 들어 버립디다. 나는 끝내 어느 것이 어느 것인지 머릿속에 제대로 붙잡지 못했소. 게다가 그것들을 무슨 미덕이나 악덕처럼 이야기하더군. 내가 알고 싶은 건 사람의 권리였는데 말이오. 부자든 가난뱅이든, 사람이기만 하면 누리는 그 권리 말이오.”"]], [["“But for all that,” said Mr.", "“그럼에도 불구하고,”"], ["Hale, “and granting to the full the offensiveness, the folly, the unchristianness of Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Hamper’s way of speaking to you in recommending his friend’s book, yet if it told you what he said it did, that wages find their own level, and that the most successful strike can only force them up for a moment, to sink in far greater proportion afterwards, in consequence of that very strike, the book would have told you the truth.”", "“햄퍼 씨가 친구의 책을 권하며 당신에게 말한 그 방식이 무례하고 어리석고 그리스도인답지 못하다는 것을 남김없이 인정하더라도, 만일 그 책이 그가 말한 대로 삯은 제 수준을 찾아간다고, 그리고 아무리 성공한 파업이라도 그 삯을 잠깐 밀어 올릴 뿐이며 바로 그 파업 때문에 나중에는 훨씬 더 크게 떨어진다고 말했다면, 그 책은 당신에게 진실을 말한 셈입니다.”"]], [["“Well, sir,”", "“글쎄올시다, 선생님.”"], ["said Higgins, rather doggedly; “it might, or it might not.", "히긴스가 다소 완고하게 말했다. “그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠지."], ["There’s two opinions go to settling that point.", "그 점을 매듭짓자면 두 가지 의견이 맞서니까."], ["But suppose it was truth double strong, it were no truth to me if I couldna take it in.", "허나 그게 갑절로 굳센 진실이라 칩시다. 내가 그걸 받아들이지 못한다면 내게는 진실이 아니오."], ["I daresay there’s great truth in yon Latin book on your shelves; but it’s gibberish and not truth to me, unless I know the meaning o’ the words.", "선생님 책장에 있는 저 라틴어 책에도 큰 진실이 들었겠지. 허나 낱말 뜻을 모르는 내게는 진실이 아니라 횡설수설일 뿐이오."], ["If yo’, sir, or any other knowledgable, patient man comes to me, and says he’ll larn me what the words mean, and not blow me up if I’m a bit stupid, or forget how one thing hangs on another—why, in time I may get to see the truth of it; or I may not.", "만일 선생님이든 다른 아는 것 많고 참을성 있는 분이든 내게 와서 그 낱말들이 무슨 뜻인지 가르쳐 주겠다고, 내가 좀 아둔하거나 한 가지가 다른 것에 어떻게 걸려 있는지 잊어버려도 나무라지 않겠다고 하면, 그야 시간이 가면 나도 그 진실을 볼 수 있게 되겠지. 못 볼 수도 있고."], ["I’ll not be bound to say I shall end in thinking the same as any man.", "내가 끝내 어느 누구와 똑같이 생각하게 되리라고 장담하지는 않겠소."], ["And I’m not one who think truth can be shaped out in words, all neat and clean, as th’ men at th’ foundry cut out sheet-iron.", "그리고 나는 주물공장 사내들이 철판을 오려 내듯 진실을 말로 깔끔하고 말끔하게 잘라 낼 수 있다고 여기는 사람이 아니오."], ["Same bones won’t go down wi’ every one.", "같은 뼈다귀라도 아무나 다 삼키지는 못하오."], ["It’ll stick here i’ this man’s throat, and there i’ t’others.", "이 사람 목에도 걸리고 저 사람 목에도 걸리지."], ["Let alone that, when down, it may be too strong for this one, too weak for that.", "그건 그렇다 치고, 삼켜 넘겼다 해도 이 사람에겐 너무 세고 저 사람에겐 너무 약할 수 있소."], ["Folk who sets up to doctor th’ world wi’ their truth, mun suit different for different minds; and be a bit tender i’ th’ way of giving it too, or the poor sick fools may spit it out i’ their faces.", "제 진실로 세상을 고쳐 보겠다고 나선 사람들은 서로 다른 마음에 맞게 다르게 지어 주어야 하고, 건네는 방식도 좀 다정해야 하오. 안 그러면 그 가엾은 병든 바보들이 그이들 얼굴에 도로 뱉어 낼 테니."], ["Now Hamper first gi’es me a box on my ear, and then he throws his big bolus at me, and says he reckons it’ll do me no good, I’m such a fool, but there it is.”", "그런데 햄퍼는 먼저 내 귀싸대기를 한 대 올려붙이고, 그러고서 제 큼직한 알약을 던지고는, 내가 하도 바보라 아무 소용도 없겠다만 어쨌든 여기 있다고 하더란 말이오.”"]], [["“I wish some of the kindest and wisest of the masters would meet some of you men, and have a good talk on these things; it would, surely, be the best way of getting over your difficulties, which, I do believe, arise from your ignorance—excuse me, Mr.", "“공장주들 가운데 가장 마음씨 곱고 지혜로운 몇 사람이 당신들 가운데 몇 사람과 만나 이런 것들에 대해 제대로 이야기를 나누었으면 좋겠군요. 그것이야말로 당신들의 어려움을 넘어서는 가장 좋은 길일 겁니다."], ["Higgins—on subjects which it is for the mutual interests of both masters and men should be well understood by both.", "그 어려움은, 정말이지 내가 보기에는, 공장주와 일꾼 양쪽 모두의 이익을 위해 양쪽 모두가 잘 알아야 할 문제들에 대한 당신들의 무지에서 나오는 것이니까요."], ["I wonder”—(half to his daughter) “if Mr.", "실례했습니다, 히긴스 씨. 혹시”"], ["Thornton might not be induced to do such thing?”", "(반은 딸에게) “손턴 씨라면 그런 일을 해 볼 마음이 들지 않을까?”"]], [["“Remember, papa,”", "“잊지 마세요, 아버지.”"], ["said she in a very low voice, “what he said one day,—about governments, you know.”", "그녀가 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “언젠가 그분이 하신 말씀 말이에요."], ["She was unwilling to make any clearer allusion to the conversation they had held on the mode of governing work-people—by giving men intelligence enough to rule themselves, or by a wise despotism on the part of the master—for she saw that Higgins had caught Mr.", "정부에 관한 것 있잖아요.” 그녀는 일하는 사람들을 다스리는 방식, 곧 사람들에게 스스로를 다스릴 만한 지성을 주는 길이냐 공장주 쪽의 지혜로운 전제냐를 두고 나누었던 대화를 더 또렷이 들추고 싶지 않았다."], ["Thornton’s name, if not the whole of the speech: indeed, he began to speak of him.", "히긴스가 그 말 전부는 아니라도 손턴 씨의 이름만은 알아들었음을 눈치챘기 때문이다. 과연 그가 손턴 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["“Thornton!", "“손턴!"], ["He’s the chap as wrote off at once for these Irishers; and led to th’ riot that ruined th’ strike.", "그자가 바로 그 아일랜드 것들을 데려오라고 대뜸 편지를 써 보내, 파업을 망친 그 폭동을 불러온 자요."], ["Even Hamper wi’ all his bullying, would ha’ waited a while—but it’s a word and a blow wi’ Thornton.", "햄퍼도 그 온갖 윽박지름에도 얼마쯤은 기다렸을 텐데, 손턴은 말 떨어지기 무섭게 후려치는 자란 말이오."], ["And, now, when th’ Union would ha’ thanked him for following up th’ chase after Boucher, and them chaps as went right again our commands, it’s Thornton who steps forrard and coolly says that, as th’ strike’s at an end, he, as party injured, doesn’t want to press the charge again the rioters.", "그런데 이제, 조합이 바우처와 우리 명령을 정면으로 어긴 그 작자들을 끝까지 쫓아 준 데 대해 고마워했을 판에, 앞으로 나서서 태연히 말하기를, 파업이 끝났으니 피해 당사자로서 폭동을 일으킨 자들에 대한 고발을 밀어붙이고 싶지 않다는 거요. 나는 그자가 제 뜻을 관철해서 드러내 놓고 앙갚음할 줄 알았소."], ["I thought he’d ha’ carried his point, and had his revenge in an open way; but says he (one in court telled me his very words) ‘they are well known; they will find the natural punishment of their conduct, in the difficulty they will meet wi’ in getting employment.", "그런데 이러더란 말이오. 법정에 있던 사람이 그자가 한 말 그대로 일러 줬소."], ["That will be severe enough.’ I only wish they’d cotched Boucher, and had him up before Hamper.", "‘그들은 이미 잘 알려져 있소. 그들은 일자리를 얻는 데 겪을 어려움에서 제 행실의 자연스러운 벌을 찾게 될 것이오."], ["I see th’ oud tiger setting on him!", "그것으로 충분히 가혹하오.’ 나는 다만 그자들이 바우처를 붙잡아 햄퍼 앞에 세웠으면 싶소. 그 늙은 호랑이가 그자를 물고 늘어지는 꼴이 눈에 선하오!"], ["would he ha’ let him off?", "그자가 놓아주었겠소?"], ["Not he!”", "어림도 없지!”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton was right,”", "“손턴 씨가 옳았어요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You are angry against Boucher, Nicholas; or else you would be the first to see, that where the natural punishment would be severe enough for the offence, any farther punishment would be something like revenge.”", "“니컬러스, 당신은 바우처에게 화가 나 있어요. 그렇지 않다면, 자연스러운 벌만으로도 그 잘못에 충분히 가혹한 자리에서 그 이상의 벌은 앙갚음이나 다름없다는 걸 누구보다 먼저 알아보셨을 텐데요.”"]], [["“My daughter is no great friend of Mr.", "“내 딸은 손턴 씨와 그리 사이가 좋지 않지요.”"], ["Thornton’s,” said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 마거릿을 보고 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Hale, smiling at Margaret; while she, as red as any carnation, began to work with double diligence, “but I believe what she says is the truth.", "그러는 동안 그녀는 카네이션처럼 새빨개져 갑절로 부지런히 바느질을 하기 시작했다. “하지만 나는 저 애 말이 옳다고 믿습니다."], ["I like him for it.”", "그래서 그분이 마음에 들고요.”"]], [["“Well, sir, this strike has been a weary bit o’ business to me; and yo’ll not wonder if I’m a bit put out wi’ seeing it fail, just for a few men who would na suffer in silence, and aou’d out, brave and firm.”", "“저기, 선생님, 이번 파업은 내게 참 진저리 나는 일이었소. 그러니 몇 안 되는 사람들 때문에 그게 무너지는 걸 보고 내가 좀 부아가 났다 해도 이상할 것 없지 않소. 잠자코 견디지 못하고, 씩씩하고 굳세게 버텨 내지도 못한 자들 말이오.”"]], [["“You forget!”", "“잊으셨군요!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“I don’t know much of Boucher; but the only time I saw him it was not his own sufferings he spoke of, but those of his sick wife—his little children.”", "“저는 바우처를 잘 몰라요. 하지만 제가 그 사람을 본 단 한 번, 그는 자기 고통을 이야기한 게 아니라 병든 아내와 어린아이들의 고통을 이야기했어요.”"]], [["“True!", "“옳소!"], ["but he were not made of iron himsel’.", "허나 그자도 쇠로 만들어진 건 아니었소."], ["He’d ha’ cried out for his own sorrows, next.", "다음엔 제 설움을 두고 울부짖었을 거요."], ["He were not one to bear.”", "견딜 위인이 못 되었소.”"]], [["“How came he into the Union?”", "“그 사람은 어떻게 조합에 들어왔나요?”"], ["asked Margaret innocently.", "마거릿이 천진하게 물었다."], ["“You don’t seem to have much respect for him; nor gained much good from having him in.”", "“그를 그리 존중하시는 것 같지도 않고, 그를 들여서 좋은 일이 있었던 것 같지도 않은데요.”"]], [["Higgins’s brow clouded.", "히긴스의 이마가 흐려졌다."], ["He was silent for a minute or two.", "그는 일이 분쯤 말이 없었다."], ["Then he said, shortly enough:", "그러더니 아주 짧게 말했다."]], [["“It’s not for me to speak o’ th’ Union.", "“조합에 대해서는 내가 말할 게 아니오."], ["What they does they does.", "그들이 하는 일은 그들이 하는 것이오."], ["Them that is of a trade mun hang together; and if they’re not willing to take their chance along wi’ th’ rest, th’ Union has ways and means.”", "한 직종에 몸담은 사람들은 뭉쳐야 하오. 그러고도 나머지와 운명을 같이할 마음이 없다면, 조합에는 조합대로 방도와 수단이 있소.”"]], [["Mr. Hale saw that Higgins was vexed at the turn the conversation had taken, and was silent.", "헤일 씨는 히긴스가 대화가 흘러간 방향에 언짢아하는 것을 보고 잠자코 있었다."], ["Not so Margaret, though she saw Higgins’s feeling as clearly as he did.", "마거릿은 그렇지 않았다. 그녀도 아버지만큼이나 또렷이 히긴스의 심정을 알아보았지만 말이다."], ["By instinct she felt that if he could but be brought to express himself in plain words, something clear would be gained on which to argue for the right and the just.", "그를 어떻게든 알기 쉬운 말로 털어놓게만 할 수 있다면, 옳고 바른 것을 두고 따져 볼 무언가 분명한 것이 얻어지리라고 그녀는 본능으로 느꼈다."]], [["“And what are the Union’s ways and means?”", "“그럼 조합의 방도와 수단이란 어떤 건가요?”"]], [["He looked up at her, as if on the point of dogged resistance to her wish for information.", "그는 그녀를 올려다보았다. 알고 싶어 하는 그녀의 바람에 완고하게 버티려는 참인 듯했다."], ["But her calm face, fixed on his, patient and trustful, compelled him to answer.", "그러나 그를 향해 붙박인 참을성 있고 믿음 어린 그 고요한 얼굴이 그를 대답하게 만들었다."]], [["“Well! If a man doesn’t belong to th’ Union, them as works next looms as orders not to speak to him—if he’s sorry or ill it’s a’ the same; he’s out o’ bounds; he’s none o’ us; he comes among us, he works among us, but he’s none o’ us. I’ some places them’s fined who speaks to him. Yo’ try that, miss; try living a year or two among them as looks away if yo’ look at ’em; try working within two yards o’ crowds o’ men, who, yo’ know, have a grinding grudge at yo’ in their hearts—to whom if yo’ say yo’r glad, not an eye brightens, nor a lip moves,—to whom if your heart’s heavy, yo’ can never say nought, because they’ll ne’er take notice on your sighs or sad looks (and a man’s no man who’ll groan out loud ’bout folk asking him what’s the matter?)—just yo’ try that, miss—ten hours for three hundred days, and yo’ll know a bit what the Union is.”", "“그러니까! 어떤 사내가 조합에 들지 않으면, 옆 베틀에서 일하는 사람들은 그자에게 말을 걸지 말라는 명을 받소. 그자가 슬픈 일을 당했든 아프든 매한가지요. 그자는 울타리 밖이오. 우리 사람이 아니오. 우리 틈에 끼어들고 우리 틈에서 일하지만 우리 사람이 아니오. 어떤 데선 그자에게 말을 거는 사람에게 벌금을 매기오. 아가씨, 한번 그리 살아 보시오. 댁이 쳐다보면 눈길을 돌려 버리는 사람들 틈에서 한두 해 살아 보시오. 마음속에 댁에 대한 갈리는 원한을 품은 줄 뻔히 아는 사람들 무리에서 두 자 거리를 두고 일해 보시오. 댁이 기쁘다고 말해도 눈 하나 반짝이지 않고 입술 하나 움직이지 않는 사람들, 댁의 마음이 무거워도 아무 말도 못 할 상대인 사람들 말이오. 어차피 그이들은 댁의 한숨도 슬픈 낯빛도 알은체하지 않을 테니. (게다가 무슨 일이냐고 물어 달라고 큰 소리로 앓는 소리를 내는 자는 사내도 아니지.) 한번 그리 살아 보시오, 아가씨. 하루 10시간씩 300일을. 그러면 조합이 무엇인지 좀 알게 될 거요.”"]], [["“Why!”", "“세상에!”"], ["said Margaret, “what tyranny this is!", "마거릿이 말했다. “이 무슨 폭정인가요!"], ["Nay, Higgins, I don’t care one straw for your anger.", "아니에요, 히긴스, 저는 당신이 화내셔도 조금도 개의치 않아요."], ["I know you can’t be angry with me if you would, and I must tell you the truth: that I never read, in all the history I have read, of a more slow, lingering torture than this.", "당신이 아무리 그러려 해도 제게 화내실 수 없다는 걸 알고, 그러니 진실을 말씀드려야겠어요. 제가 읽은 온갖 역사를 통틀어 이보다 더 더디고 질질 끄는 고문은 읽어 본 적이 없어요."], ["And you belong to the Union!", "그런데 당신은 조합에 속해 계시잖아요!"], ["And you talk of the tyranny of the masters!”", "그러면서 공장주들의 폭정을 이야기하시고요!”"]], [["“Nay,”", "“아니오.”"], ["said Higgins, “yo’ may say what yo’ like.", "히긴스가 말했다. “아가씨는 하고 싶은 말을 다 하시오."], ["The dead stand between yo’ and every angry word o’ mine.", "죽은 이가 아가씨와 내 모든 성난 말 사이에 서 있으니."], ["D’ye think I forget who’s lying there, and how hoo loved yo’?", "내가 저기 누가 누워 있는지, 그 애가 아가씨를 얼마나 좋아했는지 잊었을 것 같소?"], ["And it’s th’ masters as has made us sin, if th’ Union is a sin.", "그리고 조합이 죄라면, 우리를 죄짓게 만든 건 공장주들이오."], ["Not this generation maybe, but their fathers.", "이 세대는 아닐지 몰라도 그 아비들이 그랬소."], ["Their fathers ground our fathers to the very dust; ground us to powder!", "그 아비들이 우리 아비들을 흙먼지가 되도록 갈았소. 우리를 가루로 갈아 놓았지!"], ["Parson!", "목사님!"], ["I reckon I’ve heerd my mother read out a text, ‘The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and th’ chidren’s teeth are set on edge.’ Its so wi’ them.", "우리 어머니가 이런 구절을 읽어 주시던 걸 들은 것 같소. ‘아비가 신 포도를 먹었으므로 아들의 이가 시다.’ 그이들이 꼭 그 짝이오."], ["In those days of sore oppression th’ Unions began; it were a necessity.", "그 모진 억압의 시절에 조합이 시작되었소. 그건 어쩔 수 없는 일이었소."], ["It’s a necessity now, according to me.", "지금도 어쩔 수 없다는 게 내 생각이오. 그건 지난 불의든 지금의 불의든 앞으로 올 불의든 그것을 버텨 내는 일이오."], ["It’s a withstanding of injustice, past, present, or to come.", "전쟁 같을지도 모르지. 거기 따라 죄도 생기고."], ["It may be like war; along wi’ it come crimes; but I think it were a greater crime to let it alone.", "허나 그냥 내버려 두는 게 더 큰 죄라고 나는 생각하오. 우리한테 남은 유일한 길은 사람들을 하나의 공통된 이해로 묶는 것이오."], ["Our only chance is binding men together in one common interest; and if some are cowards and some are fools, they mun come along and join the great march, whose only strength is in numbers.”", "그중 더러가 겁쟁이고 더러가 바보라 해도, 그들도 함께 와서 저 큰 행진에 끼어야 하오. 그 힘이라고는 오직 머릿수뿐인 행진에 말이오.”"]], [["“Oh!”", "“아!”"], ["said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 한숨지으며 말했다. “당신들의 조합은 그 자체로는 아름답고 영광스러울 겁니다."], ["Hale, sighing, “your Union in itself would be beautiful, glorious—it would be Christianity itself—if it were but for an end which affected the good of all, instead of that of merely one class as opposed to another.”", "그리스도교 그 자체겠지요. 다만 그 목적이 한 계급에 맞선 한 계급의 이익이 아니라 모두의 선을 향한 것이기만 하다면요.”"]], [["“I reckon it’s time for me to be going, sir,”", "“이제 그만 가 볼 때가 된 것 같소, 선생님.”"], ["said Higgins, as the clock struck ten.", "시계가 열 시를 치자 히긴스가 말했다."]], [["“Home?”", "“집으로요?”"], ["said Margaret very softly.", "마거릿이 아주 나직이 말했다."], ["He understood her, and took her offered hand.", "그는 그녀의 뜻을 알아듣고 내민 손을 잡았다."], ["“Home, miss.", "“집으로 가오, 아가씨."], ["Yo’ may trust me, tho’ I am one o’ th’ Union.”", "나를 믿으셔도 좋소, 내가 조합 사람이긴 해도.”"]], [["“I do trust you most thoroughly, Nicholas.”", "“저는 당신을 아주 온전히 믿어요, 니컬러스.”"]], [["“Stay!” said Mr.", "“잠깐만요!”"], ["Hale, hurrying to the bookshelves.", "헤일 씨가 서둘러 책장으로 가며 말했다."], ["“Mr. Higgins!", "“히긴스 씨!"], ["I’m sure you’ll join us in family prayer?”", "우리 가정 기도에 함께해 주시겠지요?”"]], [["Higgins looked at Margaret, doubtfully.", "히긴스가 미심쩍은 눈으로 마거릿을 보았다."], ["Her grave sweet eyes met his; there was no compulsion, only deep interest in them.", "그녀의 진지하고 다정한 눈이 그의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그 안에 강요는 없고 오직 깊은 관심만 있었다."], ["He did not speak, but he kept his place.", "그는 말은 없었으나 자리를 지켰다."]], [["Margaret the Churchwoman, her father the Dissenter, Higgins the Infidel, knelt down together.", "국교회 사람인 마거릿, 비국교도인 그녀의 아버지, 무신론자인 히긴스가 함께 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["It did them no harm.", "그것은 그들에게 아무런 해도 되지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX", "# 제29장"]], [["“Some wishes crossed my mind and dimly cheered it, And one or two poor melancholy pleasures, Each in the pale unwarming light of hope, Silvering its flimsy wing, flew silent by— Moths in the moonbeam!”", "“몇몇 소망이 내 마음을 스치며 어렴풋이 그것을 북돋았고, 가엾고 서글픈 즐거움 한두 가지가, 저마다 희망의 창백하고 온기 없는 빛 속에서 여린 날개를 은빛으로 물들인 채, 소리 없이 스쳐 지나갔다 — 달빛 속의 나방들이여!”"], ["COLERIDGE.", "— 콜리지."]], [["The next morning brought Margaret a letter from Edith.", "이튿날 아침 마거릿에게 이디스의 편지가 왔다."], ["It was affectionate and inconsequent like the writer.", "그 편지는 쓴 사람을 닮아 다정하고 두서가 없었다."], ["But the affection was charming to Margaret’s own affectionate nature; and she had grown up with the inconsequence, so she did not perceive it.", "그러나 그 다정함은 마거릿 자신의 다정한 천성에 매혹적이었고, 두서없음은 그녀가 어릴 적부터 함께 자라 온 것이라 알아차리지도 못했다."], ["It was as follows:—", "편지는 이러했다."]], [["“Oh, Margaret, it is worth a journey from England to see my boy! He is a superb little fellow, especially in his caps, and most especially in the one you sent him, you good, dainty-fingered, persevering little lady! Having made all the mothers here envious, I want to show him to somebody new, and hear a fresh set of admiring expressions; perhaps, that’s all the reason; perhaps it is not—nay, possibly, there is just a little cousinly love mixed with it; but I do want you so much to come here, Margaret! I’m sure it would be the very best thing for Aunt Hale’s health; everybody here is young and well, and our skies are always blue, and our sun always shines, and the band plays deliciously from morning till night; and, to come back to the burden of my ditty, my baby always smiles. I am constantly wanting you to draw him from me, Margaret. It does not signify what he is doing; that very thing is prettiest, gracefullest, best. I think I love him a great deal better than my husband, who is getting stout, and grumpy—what he calls ‘busy.’ No! he is not. He has just come in with news of such a charming picnic, given by the officers of the Hazard, at anchor in the bay below. Because he has brought in such a pleasant piece of news, I retract all I said just now. Did not somebody burn his hand for having said or done something he was sorry for? Well, I can’t burn mine, because it would hurt me, and the scar would be ugly; but I’ll retract all I said as fast as I can. Cosmo is quite as great a darling as baby, and not a bit stout, and as ungrumpy as ever husband was; only, sometimes he is very, very busy. I may say that without love—wifely duty— where was I?—I had something very particular to say, I know, once. Oh, it is this—Dearest Margaret!—you must come and see me; it would do Aunt Hale good, as I said before. Get the doctor to order it for her. Tell him that it’s the smoke of Milton that does her harm. I have no doubt it is that, really. Three months (you must not come for less) of this delicious climate—all sunshine, and grapes as common as blackberries, would quite cure her. I don’t ask my uncle”—(Here the letter became more constrained, and better written. Mr. Hale was in the corner, like a naughty child, for having given up his living.)—“because, I dare say, he disapproves of war, soldiers, and bands of music; at least, I know that many Dissenters are members of the Peace Society, and I am afraid he would not like to come; but, if he would, dear, pray say that Cosmo and I will do our best to make him happy; and I’ll hide up Cosmo’s red coat and sword, and make the band play all sorts of grave, solemn things; or, if they do play pomps and vanities, it shall be in double slow time. Dear Margaret, if he would like to accompany you and Aunt Hale, we will try and make it pleasant, though I’m rather afraid of any one who has done anything for conscience’ sake. You never did, I hope. Tell Aunt Hale not to bring any warm clothes, though I’m afraid it will be late in the year before you can come. But you have no idea of the heat here! I tried to wear my great beauty Indian shawl at a picnic. I kept myself up with proverbs as long as I could; ‘Pride must abide,’—and such wholesome pieces of pith; but it was no use. I was like mamma’s little dog Tiny with an elephant’s trappings on; smothered, hidden, killed with my finery; so I made it into a capital carpet for us all to sit down upon. Here’s this boy of mine, Margaret—if you don’t pack up your things as soon as you get this letter, and come straight off to see him, I shall think you’re descended from King Herod!”", "“오, 마거릿, 우리 아기를 보러 영국에서 여기까지 오는 것도 아깝지 않을 거야! 정말 근사한 꼬마란다. 특히 모자를 썼을 때가 그렇고, 그중에서도 네가 보내 준 그 모자를 썼을 때가 제일이야. 착하고 손끝 야무지고 끈기 있는 우리 아가씨! 여기 어머니들을 죄다 부러워하게 만들어 놓았으니, 이제는 새로운 사람한테 보여 주고 새로운 감탄의 말들을 들어 보고 싶어. 어쩌면 그게 다일지도 몰라. 어쩌면 아닐지도 모르지. 아니, 어쩌면 사촌 사이의 정이 조금은 섞여 있을 거야. 아무튼 나는 네가 여기 와 주기를 정말 바라, 마거릿! 헤일 이모의 건강에도 그게 더없이 좋을 거라고 확신해. 여기 사람들은 다들 젊고 건강하고, 하늘은 늘 파랗고, 해는 늘 빛나고, 악단은 아침부터 밤까지 감미롭게 연주한단다. 그리고 내 노랫가락의 후렴으로 돌아오자면, 우리 아기는 늘 웃어. 나는 늘 네가 이 아이를 그려 주었으면 해, 마거릿. 아이가 무얼 하고 있든 상관없어. 그 모습 그대로가 가장 예쁘고 가장 우아하고 가장 훌륭하니까. 나는 남편보다 이 아이를 훨씬 더 사랑하는 것 같아. 남편은 점점 뚱뚱해지고 심술궂어지고 있거든. 본인은 그걸 ‘바쁘다’고 하지만. 아니야! 그렇지 않아. 방금 들어왔는데, 아래 만에 정박한 해저드호 장교들이 여는 아주 멋진 소풍 소식을 가져왔지 뭐야. 그렇게 반가운 소식을 가져왔으니 방금 한 말은 모두 거두어들일게. 누군가는 말이나 행동을 후회한 나머지 제 손을 불에 태웠다지? 글쎄, 나는 내 손을 태울 수는 없어. 아플 테고 흉터도 보기 흉할 테니까. 그래도 방금 한 말은 최대한 빨리 거두어들일게. 코즈모는 아기 못지않게 사랑스럽고, 조금도 뚱뚱하지 않고, 세상 어느 남편만큼이나 심술궂지 않아. 다만 가끔 아주아주 바쁠 뿐이지. 사랑이 없어서 하는 말은 아니야. 아내의 도리로 하는 말이지. 내가 어디까지 말했더라? 뭔가 아주 중요한 할 말이 있었는데, 분명히 있었어. 아, 이거야. 사랑하는 마거릿! 너는 꼭 와서 나를 봐야 해. 아까 말했듯이 헤일 이모께도 좋을 거야. 의사한테 그렇게 처방하라고 해. 이모를 상하게 하는 건 밀턴의 매연이라고 말씀드려. 정말 그렇다고 나는 믿어 의심치 않아. 이 감미로운 기후에서 3개월만 지내면(그보다 짧게 오면 안 돼) — 온통 햇빛에, 포도는 검은딸기만큼이나 흔해 — 이모는 말끔히 나으실 거야. 이모부께는 청하지 않겠어” — (여기서부터 편지는 한결 조심스러워지고 글씨도 나아졌다. 헤일 씨는 성직록을 버렸다는 이유로 말썽꾸러기 아이처럼 구석에 세워져 있었던 것이다.) — “왜냐하면 아마 이모부는 전쟁이며 군인이며 군악대를 못마땅해하실 테니까. 적어도 나는 많은 비국교도가 평화협회 회원이라는 걸 알고 있고, 그래서 이모부가 오고 싶어 하지 않으실까 봐 걱정이야. 하지만 만약 오시겠다면, 코즈모와 내가 이모부를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 최선을 다하겠다고 꼭 말씀드려 줘. 나는 코즈모의 붉은 군복과 검을 감춰 두고, 악단에는 온갖 진중하고 엄숙한 곡만 연주하게 할게. 아니면 화려하고 허영스러운 곡을 연주하더라도 두 배로 느리게 하도록 할 거야. 사랑하는 마거릿, 이모부가 너와 헤일 이모를 모시고 오시겠다면 우리는 즐겁게 해 드리려고 애쓸게. 나야 양심을 위해 무언가를 해낸 사람이라면 좀 겁이 나긴 하지만. 너는 그런 적이 없기를 바라. 헤일 이모께는 따뜻한 옷은 가져오지 마시라고 전해 드려. 너희가 올 수 있을 때는 아무래도 늦은 계절이겠지만. 그래도 여기 더위가 어떤지 너는 상상도 못 할 거야! 소풍 자리에서 내 그 훌륭한 인도 숄을 둘러 보려고 했어. 나는 버틸 수 있는 데까지 속담으로 버텼지. ‘멋을 부리려면 견뎌야 한다’느니 하는, 몸에 좋은 알맹이 같은 말들로 말이야. 하지만 소용없었어. 나는 코끼리 마구를 뒤집어쓴 어머니의 작은 개 타이니 꼴이었어. 그 화려함에 파묻히고 가려지고 숨이 막혀 죽을 지경이었지. 그래서 그걸 우리 모두가 앉을 근사한 깔개로 만들어 버렸단다. 여기 내 아들이 있어, 마거릿. 이 편지를 받는 대로 짐을 싸서 곧장 이 아이를 보러 오지 않는다면, 나는 네가 헤롯 왕의 후손이라고 생각할 거야!”"]], [["Margaret did long for a day of Edith’s life—her freedom from care, her cheerful home, her sunny skies.", "마거릿은 이디스의 삶을 단 하루라도 살아 보고 싶었다. 근심 없는 자유, 명랑한 가정, 햇살 가득한 하늘을."], ["If a wish could have transported her, she would have gone off; just for one day.", "소망 하나로 몸이 옮겨질 수 있었다면 그녀는 떠났을 것이다. 단 하루만이라도."], ["She yearned for the strength which such a change would give—even for a few hours to be in the midst of that bright life, and to feel young again.", "그런 변화가 줄 힘이 그녀는 간절했다. 단 몇 시간만이라도 그 눈부신 삶 한복판에 있으면서 다시 젊어진 기분을 느끼고 싶었다."], ["Not yet twenty!", "아직 20세도 되지 않았는데!"], ["and she had had to bear up against such hard pressure that she felt quite old.", "그런데도 그토록 모진 압박을 견뎌 내야 했던 탓에 그녀는 자신이 아주 늙어 버린 듯했다."], ["That was her first feeling after reading Edith’s letter.", "이디스의 편지를 읽고 난 뒤의 첫 느낌이 그것이었다."], ["Then she read it again, and, forgetting herself, was amused at its likeness to Edith’s self, and was laughing merrily over it when Mrs.", "그러고 나서 다시 읽어 보니, 자기 자신은 잊은 채 그 편지가 이디스를 그대로 빼닮았다는 것이 재미있어졌다."], ["Hale came into the drawing-room, leaning on Dixon’s arm.", "그래서 그녀가 편지를 두고 유쾌하게 웃고 있을 때, 헤일 부인이 딕슨의 팔에 기대어 응접실로 들어왔다."], ["Margaret flew to adjust the pillows.", "마거릿은 얼른 달려가 베개를 바로 놓았다."], ["Her mother seemed more than usually feeble.", "어머니는 여느 때보다 더 기운이 없어 보였다."]], [["“What were you laughing at, Margaret?”", "“무엇 때문에 웃고 있었니, 마거릿?”"], ["asked she, as soon as she had recovered from the exertion of settling herself on the sofa.", "소파에 자리를 잡느라 애쓴 뒤 겨우 숨을 돌리자마자 어머니가 물었다."]], [["“A letter I have had this morning from Edith.", "“오늘 아침 이디스한테서 온 편지 때문이에요."], ["Shall I read it to you, mamma?”", "읽어 드릴까요, 어머니?”"]], [["She read it aloud, and for a time it seemed to interest her mother, who kept wondering what name Edith had given to her boy, and suggesting all probable names, and all possible reasons why each and all of these names should be given.", "그녀는 편지를 소리 내어 읽었다."], ["Into the very midst of these wonders Mr.", "한동안 그것은 어머니의 관심을 끄는 듯했다."], ["Thornton came, bringing another offering of fruit for Mrs. Hale.", "어머니는 이디스가 아들에게 어떤 이름을 지어 주었을까 계속 궁금해하며, 있을 법한 이름을 죄다 대 보고, 그 이름들 하나하나가 붙여졌을 만한 온갖 이유를 들어 보았다."], ["He could not—say rather, he would not—deny himself the chance of the pleasure of seeing Margaret.", "이 궁금증이 한창일 때 손턴 씨가 헤일 부인에게 줄 과일을 또 들고 들어왔다. 그는 마거릿을 볼 수 있다는 기쁨의 기회를 스스로 마다할 수 없었다."], ["He had no end in this but the present gratification.", "아니, 마다하려 하지 않았다고 하는 편이 옳겠다."], ["It was the sturdy wilfulness of a man usually most reasonable and self-controlled.", "거기에는 지금 이 순간의 만족 말고 다른 목적이 없었다. 여느 때는 더없이 이성적이고 자제력 있는 남자가 부리는 완고한 고집이었다."], ["He entered the room, taking in at a glance the fact of Margaret’s presence; but after the first cold distant bow, he never seemed to let his eyes fall on her again.", "그는 방에 들어서면서 한눈에 마거릿이 있음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 차갑고 서먹한 첫 목례를 한 뒤로는 다시는 그녀에게 눈길을 떨구지 않는 듯했다."], ["He only stayed to present his peaches—to speak some gentle kindly words—and then his cold offended eyes met Margaret’s with a grave farewell, as he left the room.", "그는 복숭아를 건네고 다정하고 부드러운 말 몇 마디를 건넬 만큼만 머물렀다. 그러고는 방을 나서면서, 차갑게 상한 그의 눈이 엄숙한 작별의 인사로 마거릿의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["She sat down silent and pale.", "그녀는 말없이 창백한 얼굴로 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Do you know, Margaret, I really begin to like Mr. Thornton.”", "“얘, 마거릿, 나는 정말 손턴 씨가 좋아지기 시작하는구나.”"]], [["No answer at first.", "처음에는 대답이 없었다."], ["Then Margaret forced out an icy “Do you?”", "이윽고 마거릿이 얼음장 같은 “그러세요?”를 겨우 밀어냈다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그럼!"], ["I think he is really getting quite polished in his manners.”", "그이는 몸가짐이 정말 꽤 세련되어 가는 것 같아.”"]], [["Margaret’s voice was more in order now.", "이제 마거릿의 목소리는 한결 제자리를 찾아 있었다. 그녀가 대답했다."], ["She replied, “He is very kind and attentive—there is no doubt of that.”", "“참 친절하고 자상한 분이에요. 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.”"]], [["“I wonder Mrs. Thornton never calls.", "“손턴 부인이 한 번도 찾아오지 않는 게 이상하구나."], ["She must know I am ill, because of the water-bed.”", "물침대 일이 있었으니 내가 아픈 걸 알 텐데 말이다.”"]], [["“I dare say, she hears how you are from her son.”", "“아마 아드님한테서 어머니 소식을 듣고 계실 거예요.”"]], [["“Still, I should like to see her.", "“그래도 나는 그이를 만나 보고 싶구나."], ["You have so few friends here, Margaret.”", "너는 여기에 벗이 너무 없잖니, 마거릿.”"]], [["Margaret felt what was in her mother’s thoughts—a tender craving to bespeak the kindness of some woman towards the daughter that might soon be left motherless.", "마거릿은 어머니의 마음속에 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 머잖아 어미 없는 아이가 될지도 모르는 딸에게 어떤 여인의 친절을 미리 청해 두고 싶은 애틋한 갈망이었다."], ["But she could not speak.", "그러나 그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["“Do you think,”", "“네 생각에는,”"], ["said Mrs. Hale, after a pause, “that you could go and ask Mrs.", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 헤일 부인이 말했다."], ["Thornton to come and see me?", "“네가 손턴 부인을 찾아가 나를 보러 와 달라고 청해 볼 수 있겠니?"], ["Only once—I don’t want to be troublesome.”", "딱 한 번만이면 돼. 성가시게 굴고 싶지는 않으니까.”"]], [["“I will do anything, if you wish it, mamma—but if—but when Frederick comes——”", "“어머니가 바라시면 무엇이든 하겠어요, 어머니. 하지만 만약에… 그러니까 오빠가 오면……”"]], [["“Ah, to be sure!", "“아, 그렇지!"], ["we must keep our doors shut—we must let no one in.", "우리는 문을 닫아걸어야 하지. 아무도 들이면 안 되고."], ["I hardly know whether I dare wish him to come or not.", "그 애가 오기를 감히 바라야 할지 말아야 할지 나도 잘 모르겠구나."], ["Sometimes I think I would rather not.", "어떤 때는 차라리 오지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어."], ["Sometimes I have such frightful dreams about him.”", "어떤 때는 그 애에 대해 어찌나 무서운 꿈을 꾸는지 모른다.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma!", "“어머니!"], ["we’ll take good care.", "우리가 잘 지킬 거예요."], ["I will put my arm in the bolt sooner than he should come to the slightest harm.", "오빠가 조금이라도 다치게 두느니 차라리 제 팔을 빗장 대신 문에 끼워 넣겠어요."], ["Trust the care of him to me, mamma.", "오빠를 지키는 일은 제게 맡기세요, 어머니."], ["I will watch over him like a lioness over her young.”", "새끼를 지키는 암사자처럼 오빠를 살피겠어요.”"]], [["“When can we hear from him?”", "“언제쯤 그 애 소식을 들을 수 있을까?”"]], [["“Not for a week yet, certainly—perhaps more.”", "“적어도 1주일은 지나야 해요. 어쩌면 더 걸릴지도 모르고요.”"]], [["“We must send Martha away in good time.", "“마사는 미리 내보내야 한다."], ["It would never do to have her here when he comes, and then send her off in a hurry.”", "그 애가 왔을 때 마사가 여기 있다가 부랴부랴 내보내는 일은 결코 있어서는 안 되지.”"]], [["“Dixon is sure to remind us of that.", "“딕슨이 틀림없이 그걸 일깨워 줄 거예요."], ["I was thinking that, if we wanted any help in the house while he is here, we could perhaps get Mary Higgins.", "저는 이런 생각을 했어요. 오빠가 여기 있는 동안 집안일에 손이 필요하면 메리 히긴스를 부르면 어떨까 하고요."], ["She is very slack of work, and is a good girl, and would take pains to do her best, I am sure, and would sleep at home, and need never come upstairs, so as to know who is in the house.”", "그 애는 요새 일이 아주 뜸하거든요. 착한 아이고, 제 딴에는 힘껏 애쓸 게 분명하고, 잠은 제 집에서 잘 테고, 위층에 올라올 일이 전혀 없으니 집 안에 누가 있는지 알 일도 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“As you please.", "“네 좋을 대로 하렴."], ["As Dixon pleases.", "딕슨이 좋을 대로 하고."], ["But, Margaret, don’t get to use these horrid Milton words.", "하지만 마거릿, 이 끔찍한 밀턴 말을 쓰는 버릇은 들이지 마라."], ["‘Slack of work:’ it is a provincialism.", "‘일이 뜸하다’라니, 그건 지방 사투리다."], ["What will your aunt Shaw say, if she hears you use it on her return?”", "쇼 이모가 돌아와서 네가 그런 말을 쓰는 걸 들으면 뭐라고 하시겠니?”"]], [["“Oh, mamma!", "“어머니도 참!"], ["don’t try and make a bugbear of aunt Shaw,”", "쇼 이모를 도깨비처럼 만들지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret, laughing.", "마거릿이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Edith picked up all sorts of military slang from Captain Lennox, and aunt Shaw never took any notice of it.”", "“이디스는 레녹스 대위한테서 온갖 군대 은어를 배웠는데도 쇼 이모는 조금도 개의치 않으셨잖아요.”"]], [["“But yours is factory slang.”", "“하지만 네 것은 공장 말투잖니.”"]], [["“And if I live in a factory town, I must speak factory language when I want it.", "“공장 도시에 살고 있으니 필요할 때는 공장 말을 써야죠."], ["Why, mamma, I could astonish you with a great many words you never heard in your life.", "어머니, 저는 어머니가 평생 들어 보지 못한 말들로 어머니를 깜짝 놀라게 해 드릴 수도 있어요."], ["I don’t believe you know what a knobstick is.”", "‘파업 파괴자’가 무엇인지도 모르실걸요.”"]], [["“Not I, child.", "“난 모른다, 얘야."], ["I only know it has a very vulgar sound; and I don’t want to hear you using it.”", "그저 아주 상스럽게 들린다는 것만 알 뿐이야. 그리고 나는 네가 그 말을 쓰는 걸 듣고 싶지 않구나.”"]], [["“Very well, dearest mother, I won’t.", "“알겠어요, 사랑하는 어머니."], ["Only I shall have to use a whole explanatory sentence instead.”", "쓰지 않을게요. 다만 그 대신 설명하는 문장을 통째로 써야 할 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t like this Milton,”", "“나는 이 밀턴이 싫구나.”"], ["said Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 말했다."], ["“Edith is right enough in saying it’s the smoke that has made me so ill.”", "“나를 이렇게 아프게 한 게 매연이라는 이디스의 말이 옳아.”"]], [["Margaret started up as her mother said this.", "어머니가 이렇게 말하자 마거릿은 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["Her father had just entered the room, and she was most anxious that the faint impression she had seen on his mind that the Milton air had injured her mother’s health, should not be deepened—should not receive any confirmation.", "아버지가 막 방에 들어선 참이었다. 밀턴의 공기가 어머니의 건강을 해쳤다는 희미한 인상이 아버지의 마음에 어려 있는 것을 그녀는 본 적이 있었고, 그 인상이 더 깊어지지 않기를, 어떤 확증도 얻지 않기를 몹시 바랐다."], ["She could not tell whether he had heard what Mrs. Hale had said or not; but she began speaking hurriedly of other things, unaware that Mr.", "아버지가 헤일 부인의 말을 들었는지 아닌지는 알 수 없었지만, 그녀는 서둘러 다른 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["Thornton was following him.", "손턴 씨가 아버지 뒤를 따라 들어오고 있는 줄은 모른 채."]], [["“Mamma is accusing me of having picked up a great deal of vulgarity since we came to Milton.”", "“어머니는 제가 밀턴에 온 뒤로 상스러운 말을 잔뜩 배웠다고 나무라시는 중이에요.”"]], [["The “vulgarity” Margaret spoke of, referred purely to the use of local words, and the expression arose out of the conversation they had just been holding.", "마거릿이 말한 “상스러움”이란 오로지 그 고장 말을 쓰는 것을 가리켰고, 그 표현은 방금 나눈 대화에서 나온 것이었다."], ["But Mr.", "그러나 손턴 씨의 이마가 어두워졌다."], ["Thornton’s brow darkened; and Margaret suddenly felt how her speech might be misunderstood by him; so, in the natural sweet desire to avoid giving unnecessary pain, she forced herself to go forwards with a little greeting, and continue what she was saying, addressing herself to him expressly.", "마거릿은 문득 자기 말이 그에게 어떻게 오해될 수 있는지 깨달았다. 그래서 불필요한 아픔을 주지 않으려는 타고난 고운 마음에서, 그녀는 애써 앞으로 나서서 가볍게 인사를 건네고는 그를 향해 대놓고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Thornton, though ‘knobstick’ has not a very pretty sound, is it not expressive?", "“그런데 손턴 씨, ‘파업 파괴자’라는 말이 그리 곱게 들리지는 않지만 그래도 실감 나지 않나요?"], ["Could I do without it, in speaking of the thing it represents?", "그 말이 가리키는 것을 이야기하면서 그 말을 안 쓸 수가 있을까요?"], ["If using local words is vulgar, I was very vulgar in the Forest,—was I not, mamma?”", "고장 말을 쓰는 것이 상스러운 일이라면, 저는 숲에서도 몹시 상스러웠어요. 그렇지 않았나요, 어머니?”"]], [["It was unusual with Margaret to obtrude her own subject of conversation on others; but, in this case, she was so anxious to prevent Mr.", "마거릿이 자기 이야깃거리를 남에게 들이대는 것은 좀처럼 없는 일이었다."], ["Thornton from feeling annoyance at the words he had accidentally overheard, that it was not until she had done speaking that she coloured all over with consciousness, more especially as Mr.", "그러나 이번에는 우연히 엿들은 말 때문에 손턴 씨가 언짢아하지 않기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 말을 다 마치고 나서야 그녀는 자신을 의식하고 온통 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Thornton seemed hardly to understand the exact gist or bearing of what she was saying, but passed her by, with a cold reserve of ceremonious movement, to speak to Mrs. Hale.", "더욱이 손턴 씨는 그녀가 하는 말의 정확한 요지나 뜻을 거의 알아듣지 못한 듯, 격식 차린 몸짓의 차가운 서먹함으로 그녀를 지나쳐 헤일 부인에게 말을 걸었기에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["The sight of him reminded her of the wish to see his mother, and commend Margaret to her care.", "그를 보자 헤일 부인은 그의 어머니를 만나 마거릿을 그 보살핌에 맡기고 싶다는 바람이 떠올랐다."], ["Margaret, sitting in burning silence, vexed and ashamed of her difficulty in keeping her right place, and her calm unconsciousness of heart, when Mr.", "마거릿은 타는 듯한 침묵 속에 앉아, 손턴 씨가 곁에 있을 때면 제자리를 지키고 마음의 고요한 무심함을 지키기가 어렵다는 사실에 짜증스럽고 부끄러워하면서, 손턴 부인이 자기를 보러 와 주었으면 한다는 어머니의 느릿한 간청을 들었다."], ["Thornton was by, heard her mother’s slow entreaty that Mrs.", "곧, 되도록이면 내일이라도 와 주었으면 한다는 간청이었다."], ["Thornton would come and see her; see her soon; to-morrow, if it were possible. Mr.", "손턴 씨는 어머니가 그러실 거라고 약속하고, 잠시 이야기를 나누다가 물러갔다."], ["Thornton promised that she should—conversed a little, and then took his leave; and Margaret’s movements and voice seemed at once released from some invisible chains.", "그러자 마거릿의 몸짓과 목소리는 단번에 보이지 않던 사슬에서 풀려난 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 한 번도 쳐다보지 않았다."], ["He never looked at her; and yet, the careful avoidance of his eyes betokened that in some way he knew exactly where, if they fell by chance, they would rest on her.", "그런데도 그 눈길을 애써 피하는 태도는, 혹시라도 눈길이 무심코 떨어진다면 어디에 가 닿을지를 그가 정확히 알고 있음을 드러내고 있었다."], ["If she spoke, he gave no sign of attention, and yet his next speech to any one else was modified by what she had said; sometimes there was an express answer to what she had remarked, but given to another person as though unsuggested by her.", "그녀가 말을 하면 그는 귀 기울이는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았지만, 다른 사람에게 건네는 그의 다음 말은 그녀가 한 말에 따라 달라져 있었다. 때로는 그녀가 한 말에 대한 분명한 대답이 있었으나, 그것은 그녀가 꺼낸 이야기가 아닌 양 다른 사람에게 주어졌다."], ["It was not the bad manners of ignorance; it was the wilful bad manners arising from deep offence.", "그것은 무지에서 나온 무례가 아니었다. 깊은 모욕감에서 비롯된 고의적인 무례였다."], ["It was wilful at the time; repented of afterwards.", "그때는 고의였고, 나중에는 뉘우쳤다."], ["But no deep plan, no careful cunning could have stood him in such good stead.", "그러나 어떤 깊은 계략도, 어떤 용의주도한 술책도 그에게 그만큼 이롭게 작용하지는 못했을 것이다. 마거릿은 전에 없이 그를 자주 생각했다."], ["Margaret thought about him more than she had ever done before; not with any tinge of what is called love, but with regret that she had wounded him so deeply,—and with a gentle, patient striving to return to their former position of antagonistic friendship; for a friend’s position was what she found that he had held in her regard, as well as in that of the rest of the family.", "사랑이라 불리는 것의 기미가 어려 있어서가 아니라, 그를 그토록 깊이 상처 입혔다는 뉘우침 때문이었고, 또 서로 맞서던 예전의 우정 관계로 돌아가려는 부드럽고 참을성 있는 애씀 때문이었다. 그녀가 알아차린 바로는, 그가 자기 마음속에서도, 또 집안의 다른 식구들 마음속에서도 차지하고 있던 자리가 바로 벗의 자리였던 것이다."], ["There was a pretty humility in her behaviour to him, as if mutely apologising for the over-strong words which were the reaction from the deeds of the day of the riot.", "그를 대하는 그녀의 태도에는 어여쁜 겸손이 있었다. 폭동이 있던 날의 행동에 대한 반발로 튀어나왔던 지나치게 모진 말들을 말없이 사과하기라도 하듯이."]], [["But he resented those words bitterly.", "그러나 그는 그 말들을 쓰라리게 곱씹었다."], ["They rung in his ears; and he was proud of the sense of justice which made him go in every kindness he could offer to her parents.", "그 말들이 귓가에 울렸다. 그러면서도 그는 그녀의 부모에게 베풀 수 있는 온갖 친절을 마다하지 않게 만드는 자신의 정의감을 자랑스러워했다."], ["He exulted in the power he showed in compelling himself to face her, whenever he could think of any action which might give her father or mother pleasure.", "그녀의 아버지나 어머니를 기쁘게 할 만한 일이 떠오를 때마다 스스로를 다그쳐 그녀와 마주하게 하는 자신의 힘에 그는 우쭐했다."], ["He thought that he disliked seeing one who had mortified him so keenly; but he was mistaken.", "자기를 그토록 사무치게 모욕한 사람을 보는 것이 싫다고 그는 생각했다. 그러나 그것은 착각이었다."], ["It was a stinging pleasure to be in the room with her, and feel her presence.", "그녀와 한방에 있으면서 그 존재를 느끼는 것은 따끔하게 쓰라린 기쁨이었다."], ["But he was no great analyser of his own motives and was mistaken, as I have said.", "그러나 그는 제 동기를 잘 분석하는 사람이 아니었고, 앞서 말했듯이 착각하고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXX", "# 제30장"]], [["“The saddest birds a season find to sing.”", "“가장 슬픈 새들도 노래할 한철을 찾아낸다.”"], ["SOUTHWELL.", "— 사우스웰."]], [["“Never to fold the robe o’er secret pain, Never, weighed down by memory’s clouds again, To bow thy head!", "“은밀한 아픔 위로 옷자락을 여미는 일도 다시는 없고, 기억의 구름에 짓눌려 머리를 떨구는 일도 다시는 없으리!"], ["Thou art gone home!”", "그대는 집으로 돌아갔으니!”"], ["MRS. HEMANS.", "— 헤먼스 부인."]], [["Mrs. Thornton came to see Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 이튿날 아침 헤일 부인을 보러 왔다."], ["Hale the next morning. She was much worse.", "부인의 병세는 훨씬 나빠져 있었다."], ["One of those sudden changes—those great visible strides towards death, had been taken in the night, and her own family were startled by the gray sunken look her features had assumed in that one twelve hours of suffering.", "밤사이 그 갑작스러운 변화 가운데 하나가, 죽음을 향해 눈에 띄게 성큼 내딛는 그 걸음이 일어났고, 열두 시간의 고통 속에 잿빛으로 푹 꺼져 버린 얼굴에 식구들마저 소스라쳤다."], ["Mrs. Thornton—who had not seen her for weeks—was softened all at once.", "몇 주 동안 그녀를 보지 못했던 손턴 부인은 대번에 마음이 누그러졌다."], ["She had come because her son asked it from her as a personal favour, but with all the proud bitter feelings of her nature in arms against that family of which Margaret formed one. She doubted the reality of Mrs.", "그녀가 온 것은 아들이 개인적인 부탁으로 청했기 때문이었고, 마거릿이 속한 그 집안에 대해서는 타고난 오만하고 쓰디쓴 감정을 죄다 곤두세운 채였다."], ["Hale’s illness; she doubted any want beyond a momentary fancy on that lady’s part, which should take her out of her previously settled course of employment for the day.", "그녀는 헤일 부인의 병이 진짜인지 의심했다. 그날 하루의 정해진 일과에서 자신을 끌어내려는 그 부인의 한순간의 변덕 말고 무슨 절실한 사정이 있으랴 의심했다."], ["She told her son that she wished they had never come near the place; that he had never got acquainted with them; that there had been no such useless languages as Latin and Greek ever invented.", "그녀는 아들에게, 저 사람들이 이 고장 근처에 오지나 말았더라면, 네가 저들과 알고 지내지나 않았더라면, 라틴어니 그리스어니 하는 쓸데없는 언어가 애초에 생겨나지나 않았더라면 좋았겠다고 말했다."], ["He bore all this pretty silently; but when she had ended her invective against the dead languages, he quietly returned to the short, curt, decided expression of his wish that she should go and see Mrs.", "아들은 그 말을 꽤 잠자코 들었다. 그러나 죽은 언어들에 대한 그녀의 성토가 끝나자, 그는 병자에게 가장 편할 만한 시각에 어머니가 헤일 부인을 찾아가 주었으면 한다는 뜻을 짧고 무뚝뚝하고 단호한 말로 조용히 되풀이했다."], ["Hale at the time appointed, as most likely to be convenient to the invalid. Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 낼 수 있는 한 가장 언짢은 낯빛으로 아들의 뜻에 따랐으나, 그러면서도 내내 그런 뜻을 품은 아들이 더 좋았다."], ["Thornton submitted with as bad a grace as she could to her son’s desire, all the time liking him the better for having it; and exaggerating in her own mind the same notion that he had of extraordinary goodness on his part in so perseveringly keeping up with the Hales.", "그리고 헤일 집안과 그토록 끈질기게 어울려 주는 것이 아들의 유별난 선의라는, 아들 자신이 품고 있는 것과 똑같은 생각을 마음속에서 부풀렸다."]], [["His goodness verging on weakness (as all the softer virtues did in her mind), and her own contempt for Mr. and Mrs.", "약함에 가까운 아들의 선의(그녀의 마음속에서는 부드러운 덕목이란 다 그러했다), 헤일 씨 내외에 대한 자신의 경멸, 마거릿에 대한 노골적인 반감, 이런 생각들이 손턴 부인을 차지하고 있었다."], ["Hale, and positive dislike to Margaret, were the ideas which occupied Mrs.", "죽음의 천사가 드리운 날개의 검은 그림자 앞에서 그 모든 것이 아무것도 아닌 것으로 무너져 내리기 전까지는. 저기 헤일 부인이 누워 있었다."], ["Thornton, till she was struck into nothingness before the dark shadow of the wings of the angel of death. There lay Mrs.", "자기와 똑같은 어머니가, 자기보다 훨씬 젊은 여자가, 다시는 일어날 가망이라고는 보이지 않는 침상 위에."], ["Hale—a mother like herself—a much younger woman than she was,—on the bed from which there was no sign of hope that she might ever rise again.", "그 어두운 방에서 그녀에게는 더 이상 빛과 그늘의 변화가 없었다. 무엇을 할 힘도, 몸을 뒤척일 여지조차 거의 없었다."], ["No more variety of light and shade for her in that darkened room; no power of action, scarcely change of movement; faint alternations of whispered sound and studious silence; and yet that monotonous life seemed almost too much!", "속삭이는 소리와 조심스러운 침묵이 희미하게 번갈아 들 뿐이었다. 그런데도 그 단조로운 삶조차 감당하기 벅차 보였다!"], ["When Mrs. Thornton, strong and prosperous with life, came in, Mrs.", "삶으로 튼튼하고 번성한 손턴 부인이 들어섰을 때 헤일 부인은 가만히 누워 있었다."], ["Hale lay still, although from the look on her face she was evidently conscious of who it was.", "얼굴빛을 보면 누가 왔는지 분명히 알고 있었는데도. 그러나 그녀는 한두 분 동안 눈조차 뜨지 않았다."], ["But she did not even open her eyes for a minute or two.", "눈을 들었을 때는 이미 눈물의 무거운 물기가 속눈썹에 맺혀 있었다."], ["The heavy moisture of tears stood on her eyelashes before she looked up; then, with her hand groping feebly over the bed-clothes, for the touch of Mrs. Thornton’s large firm fingers, she said, scarcely above her breath.—Mrs.", "이윽고 손턴 부인의 크고 단단한 손가락을 더듬어 찾느라 이불 위로 힘없이 손을 헤매며, 그녀는 숨결보다 조금 높을까 말까 한 소리로 말했다."], ["Thornton had to stoop from her erectness to listen,—", "손턴 부인은 꼿꼿한 몸을 굽혀야 들을 수 있었다."]], [["“Margaret—you have a daughter—my sister is in Italy. My child will be without a mother;—in a strange place,—if I die— will you”——", "“마거릿을요. 댁에게도 딸이 있지요. 내 언니는 이탈리아에 있어요. 내 아이는 어미 없이 남게 됩니다. 낯선 곳에서. 내가 죽으면. 그래 주시겠어요”……"]], [["And her filmy wandering eyes fixed themselves with an intensity of wistfulness on Mrs.", "그리고 흐릿하게 헤매던 두 눈이 애타는 간절함으로 손턴 부인의 얼굴에 못 박혔다. 잠시 동안 그 얼굴의 굳음에는 아무런 변화가 없었다."], ["Thornton’s face.", "매섭고 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."], ["For a minute there was no change in its rigidness; it was stern and unmoved;—nay, but that the eyes of the sick woman were growing dim with the slow-gathering tears, she might have seen a dark cloud cross the cold features.", "아니, 병든 여인의 눈이 천천히 고이는 눈물로 흐려지고 있지만 않았더라면, 차가운 이목구비 위로 검은 구름 한 조각이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 보았을지도 몰랐다. 그리고 마침내 그 마음을 흔든 것은 아들 생각도, 살아 있는 딸 패니 생각도 아니었다."], ["And it was no thought of her son, or of her living daughter Fanny, that stirred her heart at last; but a sudden remembrance, suggested by something in the arrangement of the room,—of a little daughter—dead in infancy—long years ago—that, like a sudden sunbeam, melted the icy crust, behind which there was a real tender woman.", "방 안의 무언가가 문득 떠올려 준 기억, 오래전 갓난쟁이 적에 죽은 어린 딸의 기억이었다. 그것이 갑작스러운 햇살처럼 얼음 껍질을 녹였고, 그 뒤에는 진짜로 다정한 여인이 있었다."]], [["“You wish me to be a friend to Miss Hale,” said Mrs.", "“헤일 양의 벗이 되어 달라는 말씀이군요.”"], ["Thornton, in her measured voice, that would not soften with her heart, but came out distinct and clear.", "손턴 부인이 절도 있는 목소리로 말했다. 마음과 달리 누그러지지 못하고 또렷하고 맑게 나오는 목소리였다."]], [["Mrs. Hale, her eyes still fixed on Mrs.", "헤일 부인은 여전히 손턴 부인의 얼굴에 눈을 붙박은 채, 이불 위에 놓인 제 손 밑의 그 손을 꼭 눌렀다."], ["Thornton’s face, pressed the hand that lay below hers on the coverlet.", "말은 나오지 않았다."], ["She could not speak. Mrs.", "손턴 부인이 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["Thornton sighed, “I will be a true friend, if circumstances require it.", "“사정이 그것을 요구한다면 참된 벗이 되겠습니다. 다정한 벗은 아니고요."], ["Not a tender friend. That I cannot be,”—(“to her,”", "그것은 제가 될 수 없습니다.”(“저 아이에게는”"], ["she was on the point of adding, but she relented at the sight of that poor, anxious face.)—“It is not my nature to show affection even where I feel it, nor do I volunteer advice in general.", "하고 덧붙이려다가, 그 가엾고 애타는 얼굴을 보고는 마음을 누그러뜨렸다.) “저는 애정을 느끼는 자리에서도 그것을 내보이는 성미가 아니고, 남에게 먼저 나서서 충고하는 편도 아닙니다."], ["Still, at your request,—if it will be any comfort to you, I will promise you.”", "그래도 청하시니, 그것이 조금이라도 위로가 된다면 약속드리지요.” 그러고 나서 사이가 있었다."], ["Then came a pause. Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 지킬 뜻이 없는 것을 약속하기에는 너무 양심적이었다."], ["Thornton was too conscientious to promise what she did not mean to perform; and to perform anything in the way of kindness on behalf of Margaret, more disliked at this moment than ever, was difficult; almost impossible.", "그런데 그 어느 때보다 밉살스러운 마거릿을 위해 친절이랄 것을 실행한다는 것은 어려운 일이었다. 거의 불가능했다."]], [["“I promise,”", "“약속합니다.”"], ["said she, with grave severity; which, after all, inspired the dying woman with faith as in something more stable than life itself,—flickering, flittering, wavering life!", "그녀가 엄숙하고 준엄하게 말했다. 그 준엄함이 오히려 죽어 가는 여인에게 삶 자체보다, 깜박이고 나풀거리고 흔들리는 저 삶보다 더 든든한 무엇에 대한 믿음을 불어넣었다."], ["“I promise that in any difficulty in which Miss Hale”——", "“헤일 양이 어떤 어려움에 처하든”……"]], [["“Call her Margaret!”", "“마거릿이라고 불러 주세요!”"], ["gasped Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["“In which she comes to me for help, I will help her with every power I have, as if she were my own daughter.", "“저 아이가 도움을 청하러 오는 어떤 어려움에서든, 제 딸이기라도 한 것처럼 제가 지닌 온 힘을 다해 돕겠습니다."], ["I also promise that if ever I see her doing what I think is wrong”——", "또 한 가지 약속드리지요. 저 아이가 제 생각에 그른 일을 하는 것을 보게 되면”……"]], [["“But Margaret never does wrong—not wilfully wrong,”", "“하지만 마거릿은 그른 일을 하지 않아요. 일부러 그르게는요.”"], ["pleaded Mrs. Hale.", "헤일 부인이 애원하듯 말했다."], ["Mrs. Thornton went on as before; as if she had not heard:", "손턴 부인은 듣지 못한 듯 하던 말을 그대로 이어 갔다."]], [["“If ever I see her doing what I believe to be wrong—such wrong not touching me or mine, in which case I might be supposed to have an interested motive—I will tell her of it, faithfully and plainly, as I should wish my own daughter to be told.”", "“저 아이가 제가 그르다고 믿는 일을 하는 것을 보게 되면, 저나 제 식구와 얽히지 않은 그른 일 말입니다. 얽혀 있다면 제게 딴 속셈이 있다고 여겨질 테니까요. 그럴 때에는 제 딸이라면 이렇게 일러 주었으면 싶은 그대로, 성실하고 솔직하게 일러 주겠습니다.”"]], [["There was a long pause. Mrs.", "긴 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Hale felt that this promise did not include all; and yet it was much.", "헤일 부인은 이 약속이 모든 것을 담고 있지는 않다고 느꼈다. 그래도 이만해도 대단했다."], ["It had reservations in it which she did not understand: but then she was weak, dizzy, and tired.", "그 안에는 그녀가 헤아리지 못할 유보가 들어 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 약하고 어지럽고 지쳐 있었다."], ["Mrs. Thornton was reviewing all the probable cases in which she had pledged herself to act.", "손턴 부인은 자기가 그리하겠다고 맹세한 있을 법한 경우들을 하나하나 헤아려 보고 있었다."], ["She had a fierce pleasure in the idea of telling Margaret unwelcome truths, in the shape of performance of duty.", "의무를 이행한다는 모양새로 마거릿에게 반갑지 않은 진실을 일러 준다는 생각에 그녀는 사나운 쾌감을 느꼈다."], ["Mrs. Hale began to speak:", "헤일 부인이 입을 열었다."]], [["“I thank you.", "“고맙습니다."], ["I pray God to bless you.", "하느님의 복을 비옵니다."], ["I shall never see you again in this world.", "이승에서 다시는 뵙지 못하겠지요."], ["But my last words are, I thank you for your promise of kindness to my child.”", "그래도 마지막으로 이 말은 하겠어요. 내 아이에게 친절을 베풀겠다 약속해 주셔서 고맙습니다.”"]], [["“Not kindness!”", "“친절은 아닙니다!”"], ["testified Mrs. Thornton, ungraciously truthful to the last.", "손턴 부인이 마지막까지 무뚝뚝하리만치 정직하게 못 박았다."], ["But having eased her conscience by saying these words, she was not sorry that they were not heard. She pressed Mrs.", "그러나 그 말로 양심을 덜고 나자, 그 말이 상대에게 들리지 않았다는 것이 서운하지는 않았다."], ["Hale’s soft languid hand; and rose up and went her way out of the house without seeing a creature.", "그녀는 헤일 부인의 부드럽고 힘없는 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 그러고는 일어나 사람 하나 마주치지 않고 그 집을 나섰다."]], [["During the time that Mrs. Thornton was having this interview with Mrs.", "손턴 부인이 헤일 부인과 이런 만남을 갖고 있는 동안, 마거릿과 딕슨은 머리를 맞대고 프레더릭이 온다는 것을 집 밖의 모두에게 어떻게 철저히 감출지 궁리하고 있었다."], ["Hale, Margaret and Dixon were laying their heads together and consulting how they should keep Frederick’s coming a profound secret to all out of the house.", "이제 그의 편지는 언제 와도 이상하지 않았고, 편지 뒤를 바짝 따라 그가 올 것이 틀림없었다."], ["A letter from him might now be expected any day; and he would assuredly follow quickly on its heels.", "마사는 휴가를 주어 내보내야 했다."], ["Martha must be sent away on her holiday; Dixon must keep stern guard on the front door, only admitting the few visitors that ever came to the house into Mr. Hale’s room down stairs—Mrs.", "딕슨은 현관을 매섭게 지키면서, 어쩌다 찾아오는 몇 안 되는 손님을 아래층 헤일 씨의 방으로만 들여야 했다."], ["Hale’s extreme illness giving her a good excuse for this.", "헤일 부인의 병이 워낙 위중하니 그럴 구실은 충분했다."], ["If Mary Higgins was required as a help to Dixon in the kitchen, she was to hear and see as little of Frederick as possible; and he was, if necessary, to be spoken of to her under the name of Mr.", "부엌에서 딕슨을 거들 사람으로 메리 히긴스가 필요해지면, 그 애가 프레더릭에 대해 되도록 적게 보고 듣게 해야 했다. 그리고 꼭 필요하다면 그 애 앞에서는 그를 디킨슨 씨라는 이름으로 불러야 했다."], ["Dickinson. But her sluggish and incurious nature was the greatest safeguard of all.", "그러나 무엇보다 든든한 방패는 그 애의 굼뜨고 무심한 성미였다."]], [["They resolved that Martha should leave them that very afternoon for this visit to her mother.", "그들은 마사를 바로 그날 오후에 어머니를 찾아뵙는 길로 떠나보내기로 정했다."], ["Margaret wished that she had been sent away on the previous day, as she fancied it might be thought strange to give a servant a holiday when her mistress’s state required so much attendance.", "마거릿은 차라리 전날 내보냈더라면 싶었다. 안주인의 병세가 이토록 손이 많이 가는 판에 하녀에게 휴가를 주는 것이 이상하게 보일지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서였다."]], [["Poor Margaret!", "가엾은 마거릿!"], ["All that afternoon she had to act the part of a Roman daughter, and give strength out of her own scanty stock to her father. Mr.", "그날 오후 내내 그녀는 로마의 딸 노릇을 하며 얼마 남지 않은 제 몫의 기운을 덜어 아버지에게 나누어 주어야 했다."], ["Hale would hope, would not despair, between the attacks of his wife’s malady; he buoyed himself up in every respite from her pain, and believed that it was the beginning of ultimate recovery.", "헤일 씨는 아내의 병이 발작과 발작 사이에 놓일 때마다 희망을 품으려 했고 절망하지 않으려 했다. 아내의 고통이 잠시 물러설 때마다 그는 마음을 부풀렸고, 이것이 끝내 나아지는 시작이라고 믿었다."], ["And so, when the paroxysms came on, each more severe than the last, they were fresh agonies, and greater disappointments to him.", "그래서 앞것보다 더 모진 발작이 닥칠 때마다 그것은 새로운 고뇌였고, 더 큰 낙담이었다."], ["This afternoon, he sat in the drawing-room, unable to bear the solitude of his study, or to employ himself in any way.", "이날 오후 그는 응접실에 앉아 있었다. 서재의 적막을 견딜 수도, 무엇으로든 마음을 붙일 수도 없었기 때문이다."], ["He buried his head in his arms, which lay folded on the table.", "그는 탁자 위에 포갠 두 팔에 얼굴을 파묻었다."], ["Margaret’s heart ached to see him; yet, as he did not speak, she did not like to volunteer any attempt at comfort.", "그를 보는 마거릿의 가슴이 아팠다. 그러나 그가 말이 없으니 그녀도 굳이 나서서 위로해 보고 싶지 않았다."], ["Martha was gone.", "마사는 떠났다."], ["Dixon sat with Mrs. Hale while she slept.", "딕슨은 헤일 부인이 잠든 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["The house was very still and quiet, and darkness came on, without any movement to procure candles.", "집 안은 몹시 고요하고 조용했고, 촛불을 가지러 움직이는 이도 없이 어둠이 내렸다."], ["Margaret sat at the window, looking out at the lamps and the street, but seeing nothing,—only alive to her father’s heavy sighs.", "마거릿은 창가에 앉아 가로등과 거리를 내다보았으나 아무것도 보고 있지 않았다. 오직 아버지의 무거운 한숨에만 살아 있었다."], ["She did not like to go down for lights, lest the tacit restraint of her presence being withdrawn, he might give way to more violent emotion, without her being at hand to comfort him.", "그녀는 불을 가지러 내려가기가 싫었다. 자기가 곁에 있음으로 해서 말없이 눌려 있던 것이 풀리면, 위로해 줄 사람도 곁에 없는 자리에서 아버지가 더 격한 감정에 무너질까 봐서였다."], ["Yet she was just thinking that she ought to go and see after the well-doing of the kitchen fire, which there was nobody but herself to attend to, when she heard the muffled door-bell ring with so violent a pull, that the wires jingled all through the house, though the positive sound was not great.", "그래도 자기 말고는 돌볼 사람이 없는 부엌 불이 잘 있는지 가 보아야겠다는 생각이 막 들던 참에, 헝겊을 감은 초인종이 어찌나 세게 당겨졌는지 소리 자체는 크지 않은데도 줄이 온 집 안을 울리며 딸랑거렸다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 아버지 곁을 지나쳤다."], ["She started up, passed her father, who had never moved at the veiled, dull sound,—returned, and kissed him tenderly.", "그 먹먹하고 둔한 소리에도 아버지는 꿈쩍하지 않았다. 그녀는 되돌아와 그에게 살며시 입을 맞추었다."], ["And still he never moved, nor took any notice of her fond embrace.", "그래도 그는 움직이지도, 딸의 애틋한 포옹을 알은체하지도 않았다."], ["Then she went down softly, through the dark, to the door.", "그러자 그녀는 어둠을 지나 살그머니 문간으로 내려갔다."], ["Dixon would have put the chain on before she opened it, but Margaret had not a thought of fear in her pre-occupied mind.", "딕슨이었다면 문을 열기 전에 걸쇠 사슬을 걸었겠지만, 마음이 온통 딴 데 가 있는 마거릿에게는 두려움이라는 생각이 없었다."], ["A man’s tall figure stood between her and the luminous street.", "그녀와 환한 거리 사이로 키 큰 남자의 형체가 서 있었다."], ["He was looking away; but at the sound of the latch he turned quickly round.", "그는 딴 데를 보고 있다가 걸쇠 소리에 홱 돌아섰다."]], [["“Is this Mr. Hale’s?”", "“여기가 헤일 씨 댁입니까?”"], ["said he, in a clear, full, delicate voice.", "그가 맑고 우렁차면서도 고운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["Margaret trembled all over; at first she did not answer.", "마거릿은 온몸을 떨었다. 처음에는 대답하지 못했다."], ["In a moment she sighed out,", "이윽고 그녀는 한숨처럼 내뱉었다."]], [["“Frederick!”", "“오빠!”"], ["and stretched out both her hands to catch his, and draw him in.", "그러고는 두 손을 뻗어 그의 손을 붙잡고 안으로 끌어들였다."]], [["“Oh, Margaret!”", "“아, 마거릿!”"], ["said he, holding her off by her shoulders, after they had kissed each other, as if even in that darkness he could see her face, and read in its expression a quicker answer to his question than words could give,—", "그가 서로 입을 맞춘 뒤 그녀의 어깨를 잡아 몸을 떼어 놓으며 말했다. 그 어둠 속에서도 그녀의 얼굴이 보이기라도 하는 듯이, 그 표정에서 말보다 빠른 대답을 읽어 내려는 듯이."]], [["“My mother!", "“어머니!"], ["is she alive!”", "살아 계셔?”"]], [["“Yes, she is alive, dear, dear brother!", "“네, 살아 계세요, 그립고 그리운 오빠!"], ["She—as ill as she can be she is; but alive!", "더할 수 없이 위중하시지만, 그래도 살아 계세요!"], ["She is alive!”", "살아 계셔요!”"]], [["“Thank God!”", "“하느님, 감사합니다!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Papa is utterly prostrate with this great grief.”", "“아버지는 이 큰 슬픔에 완전히 주저앉으셨어요.”"]], [["“You expect me, don’t you?”", "“내가 올 줄 알고 있었지?”"]], [["“No, we have had no letter.”", "“아니요, 편지는 못 받았어요.”"]], [["“Then I have come before it.", "“그럼 편지보다 내가 먼저 왔구나."], ["But my mother knows I am coming.”", "그래도 어머니는 내가 온다는 걸 아셔.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["we all knew you would come.", "오빠가 오리라는 건 우리 모두 알고 있었어요."], ["But wait a little!", "그래도 잠깐만요!"], ["Step in here.", "이리 들어오세요."], ["Give me your hand.", "손 주세요."], ["What is this?", "이건 뭐예요?"], ["Oh! your carpet-bag.", "아, 여행 가방이군요."], ["Dixon has shut the shutters; but this is papa’s study, and I can take you to a chair to rest yourself for a few minutes; while I go and tell him.”", "딕슨이 덧문을 닫아 두었지만 여기가 아버지 서재예요. 제가 올라가서 알려 드리는 동안 잠깐 앉아 쉬시게 의자로 모실게요.”"]], [["She groped her way to the taper and the lucifer matches.", "그녀는 더듬더듬 가느다란 초와 성냥을 찾아갔다."], ["She suddenly felt shy when the little feeble light made them visible.", "그 희미한 작은 불빛에 서로의 모습이 드러나자 그녀는 문득 수줍어졌다."], ["All she could see was, that her brother’s face was unusually dark in complexion, and she caught the stealthy look of a pair of remarkably long-cut blue eyes, that suddenly twinkled up with a droll consciousness of their mutual purpose of inspecting each other.", "그녀가 볼 수 있었던 것이라고는 오빠의 얼굴빛이 유난히 검다는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 눈꼬리가 유난히 길게 째진 푸른 두 눈이 슬며시 건네는 눈길을 붙들었는데, 그 눈은 서로를 살펴보려는 속셈이 같다는 것을 익살스럽게 알아채고는 반짝 빛났다."], ["But though the brother and sister had an instant of sympathy in their reciprocal glances, they did not exchange a word; only, Margaret felt sure that she should like her brother as a companion as much as she already loved him as a near relation.", "그러나 오누이는 마주친 눈길 속에 한순간 마음이 통했으면서도 한마디도 주고받지 않았다. 다만 마거릿은 이미 가까운 피붙이로 사랑하고 있는 만큼이나 이 오빠를 동무로서도 좋아하게 되리라고 확신했다."], ["Her heart was wonderfully lighter as she went up stairs; the sorrow was no less in reality, but it became less oppressive from having some one in precisely the same relation to it as that in which she stood.", "위층으로 올라가는 그녀의 마음은 놀랍도록 가벼웠다. 슬픔이 실제로 줄어든 것은 아니었으나, 그 슬픔과 꼭 같은 자리에 선 사람이 생겼다는 것만으로 짓누르는 힘이 덜해졌다."], ["Not her father’s desponding attitude had power to damp her now.", "이제는 아버지의 낙담한 모습도 그녀를 가라앉힐 힘이 없었다."], ["He lay across the table, helpless as ever; but she had the spell by which to rouse him.", "아버지는 여전히 어쩔 줄 모르는 채로 탁자에 엎드려 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그를 깨울 주문이 있었다."], ["She used it perhaps too violently in her own great relief.", "크나큰 안도에 겨워 그녀는 그 주문을 어쩌면 너무 세차게 썼는지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Papa,”", "“아버지.”"], ["said she, throwing her arms fondly round his neck; pulling his weary head up in fact with her gentle violence, till it rested in her arms, and she could look into his eyes, and let them gain strength and assurance from hers.", "그녀가 그의 목을 다정하게 끌어안으며 말했다. 부드러우면서도 억지스러운 힘으로 그 지친 머리를 실제로 끌어올려 자기 팔에 기대게 하고, 그의 눈을 들여다보며 그 눈이 자기 눈에서 힘과 확신을 얻게 하면서."]], [["“Papa!", "“아버지!"], ["guess who is here!”", "누가 왔는지 맞혀 보세요!”"]], [["He looked at her; she saw the idea of the truth glimmer into their filmy sadness, and be dismissed thence as a wild imagination.", "그가 딸을 바라보았다. 그 흐릿한 슬픔 속으로 진실의 짐작이 어렴풋이 스며들었다가, 터무니없는 공상으로 내쫓기는 것을 그녀는 보았다."]], [["He threw himself forward, and hid his face once more in his stretched-out arms, resting upon the table as heretofore.", "그는 몸을 앞으로 던지듯 숙여 아까처럼 탁자에 얹은 두 팔 사이에 다시 얼굴을 묻었다. 그가 속삭이는 소리가 들렸다."], ["She heard him whisper; she bent tenderly down to listen.", "그녀는 다정하게 몸을 굽혀 귀를 기울였다. “모르겠다."], ["“I don’t know; don’t tell me it is Frederick—not Frederick.", "프레더릭이라고는 말하지 마라. 프레더릭은 안 된다."], ["I cannot bear it,—I am too weak.", "나는 견딜 수가 없어. 나는 너무 약하다."], ["And his mother is dying!”", "게다가 그 애 어미가 죽어 가고 있는데!”"]], [["He began to cry and wail like a child.", "그는 아이처럼 울며 통곡하기 시작했다."], ["It was so different to all which Margaret had hoped and expected, that she turned sick with disappointment, and was silent for an instant.", "마거릿이 바라고 기대했던 것과는 너무나 달라서 그녀는 낙담에 속이 메슥거렸고, 한순간 말을 잃었다. 그러고는 다시 입을 열었다."], ["Then she spoke again—very differently—not so exultingly, far more tenderly and carefully.", "아주 다르게, 그리 들뜨지 않게, 훨씬 다정하고 조심스럽게."]], [["“Papa, it is Frederick!", "“아버지, 프레더릭 오빠예요!"], ["Think of mamma, how glad she will be!", "어머니를 생각해 보세요, 얼마나 기뻐하시겠어요!"], ["And oh, for her sake, how glad we ought to be!", "아, 어머니를 위해서라도 우리가 얼마나 기뻐해야 하는지요!"], ["For his sake, too,—our poor, poor boy!”", "오빠를 위해서도요. 가엾고 가엾은 우리 오빠!”"]], [["Her father did not change his attitude, but he seemed to be trying to understand the fact.", "아버지는 자세를 바꾸지 않았으나, 그 사실을 헤아려 보려 애쓰는 듯했다."]], [["“Where is he?”", "“그 애가 어디 있느냐?”"], ["asked he at last, his face still hidden in his prostrate arms.", "마침내 그가 물었다. 얼굴은 여전히 엎드린 팔에 묻은 채였다."]], [["“In your study, quite alone.", "“아버지 서재에요, 아주 혼자요."], ["I lighted the taper, and ran up to tell you.", "제가 초를 켜 놓고 아버지께 알리려 뛰어 올라왔어요."], ["He is quite alone, and will be wondering why—”", "혼자 있으니 왜 이러나 싶어 할 텐데요……”"]], [["“I will go to him,”", "“내가 그 애한테 가마.”"], ["broke in her father; and he lifted himself up and leant on her arm as on that of a guide.", "아버지가 말을 자르고 나섰다. 그러고는 몸을 일으켜 길잡이의 팔에 기대듯 딸의 팔에 기댔다."]], [["Margaret led him to the study door, but her spirits were so agitated that she felt she could not bear to see the meeting.", "마거릿은 그를 서재 문까지 이끌었다. 그러나 마음이 하도 뒤흔들려 그 만남을 차마 지켜볼 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["She turned away, and ran up stairs, and cried most heartily.", "그녀는 돌아서서 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 실컷 울었다."], ["It was the first time she had dared to allow herself this relief for days.", "며칠 만에 처음으로 스스로에게 허락한 위안이었다."], ["The strain had been terrible, as she now felt.", "그동안의 긴장이 얼마나 끔찍했는지 그녀는 이제야 느꼈다."], ["But Frederick was come!", "그러나 프레더릭이 왔다!"], ["He, the one precious brother, was there, safe, amongst them again!", "하나뿐인 그 소중한 오빠가 무사히 다시 식구들 틈에 와 있다!"], ["She could hardly believe it.", "그녀는 좀처럼 믿기지 않았다."], ["She stopped her crying, and opened her bedroom door.", "그녀는 울음을 그치고 침실 문을 열었다."], ["She heard no sound of voices, and almost feared she might have dreamt.", "아무 말소리도 들리지 않아 혹시 꿈을 꾼 것은 아닌가 겁이 날 지경이었다."], ["She went down stairs, and listened at the study door.", "그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 서재 문에서 귀를 기울였다."], ["She heard the buzz of voices; and that was enough.", "웅얼거리는 말소리가 들렸고, 그것으로 충분했다."], ["She went into the kitchen, and stirred up the fire, and lighted the house, and prepared for the wanderer’s refreshment.", "그녀는 부엌으로 들어가 불을 지피고 집 안에 불을 밝히고 나그네가 요기할 것을 마련했다."], ["How fortunate it was that her mother slept!", "어머니가 잠들어 계신 것이 얼마나 다행인가!"], ["She knew that she did, from the candle-lighter thrust through the keyhole of her bedroom door.", "침실 문 열쇠 구멍에 꽂아 둔 불쏘시개를 보고 그녀는 어머니가 잠든 것을 알았다."], ["The traveller could be refreshed and bright, and the first excitement of the meeting with his father all be over, before her mother became aware of anything unusual.", "어머니가 무슨 심상찮은 낌새를 알아채기 전에 나그네는 배를 채우고 기운을 차릴 수 있을 테고, 아버지와의 첫 만남이 주는 흥분도 다 가라앉을 것이었다."]], [["When all was ready, Margaret opened the study door, and went in like a serving-maiden, with a heavy tray held in her extended arms.", "채비가 다 되자 마거릿은 서재 문을 열고 하녀처럼 무거운 쟁반을 두 팔로 받쳐 들고 들어갔다."], ["She was proud of serving Frederick.", "프레더릭의 시중을 드는 것이 그녀는 자랑스러웠다."], ["But he, when he saw her, sprang up in a minute, and relieved her of her burden.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 보자마자 벌떡 일어나 그 짐을 받아 들었다."], ["It was a type, a sign, of all the coming relief which his presence would bring.", "그것은 그가 있음으로 해서 앞으로 찾아올 온갖 덜어짐의 한 본보기이자 조짐이었다. 오누이는 함께 상을 차렸다."], ["The brother and sister arranged the table together, saying little, but their hands touching, and their eyes speaking the natural language of expression, so intelligible to those of the same blood.", "말은 별로 없었으나 손이 서로 닿았고, 눈은 같은 피를 나눈 이들에게는 그토록 잘 통하는 저 자연스러운 표정의 언어로 말하고 있었다. 불이 꺼져 있어서 마거릿이 불을 붙이는 일에 매달렸다."], ["The fire had gone out; and Margaret applied herself to light it, for the evenings had begun to be chilly; and yet it was desirable to make all noises as distant as possible from Mrs. Hale’s room.", "저녁이 쌀쌀해지기 시작한 데다, 그러면서도 헤일 부인의 방에서 소리를 되도록 멀리 두는 편이 좋았기 때문이다."]], [["“Dixon says it is a gift to light a fire; not an art to be acquired.”", "“딕슨은 불 붙이는 건 타고나는 재주지 익혀서 되는 기술이 아니라고 하던데요.”"]], [["“Poeta nascitur, non fit,” murmured Mr.", "“시인은 태어나는 것이지 만들어지는 것이 아니다.”"], ["Hale; and Margaret was glad to hear a quotation once more, however languidly given.", "헤일 씨가 중얼거렸다. 아무리 맥없이 나왔을망정 마거릿은 다시 인용구를 듣게 된 것이 반가웠다."]], [["“Dear old Dixon!", "“그리운 딕슨!"], ["How we shall kiss each other!”", "우리 서로 얼마나 입을 맞출까!”"], ["said Frederick.", "프레더릭이 말했다."], ["“She used to kiss me, and then look in my face to be sure I was the right person, and then set to again!", "“나한테 입을 맞추고는 사람을 제대로 봤나 하고 얼굴을 들여다보고 나서 또 맞추곤 했지!"], ["But, Margaret, what a bungler you are!", "그런데 마거릿, 너 참 서투르구나!"], ["I never saw such a little awkward, good-for-nothing pair of hands.", "이렇게 작고 어설프고 아무짝에도 못 쓸 손은 처음 본다."], ["Run away, and wash them, ready to cut bread-and-butter for me, and leave the fire.", "저리 가서 손 씻고 와서 나한테 버터 바른 빵이나 썰어 주고, 불은 놔둬라."], ["I’ll manage it.", "내가 하마."], ["Lighting fires is one of my natural accomplishments.”", "불 붙이는 건 내 타고난 재주 가운데 하나야.”"]], [["So Margaret went away; and returned; and passed in and out of the room, in a glad restlessness that could not be satisfied with sitting still.", "그래서 마거릿은 나갔다가 돌아왔고, 가만히 앉아 있어서는 성에 차지 않는 기쁜 들썩임으로 방을 들락날락했다."], ["The more wants Frederick had, the better she was pleased; and he understood all this by instinct.", "프레더릭이 아쉬운 것이 많으면 많을수록 그녀는 더 기뻤고, 그는 그 마음을 본능으로 다 알았다."], ["It was a joy snatched in the house of mourning, and the zest of it was all the more pungent, because they knew in the depth of their hearts what irremediable sorrow awaited them.", "그것은 상가에서 낚아챈 기쁨이었고, 마음 깊은 곳에서 돌이킬 수 없는 슬픔이 기다리고 있음을 알기에 그 맛은 한층 더 알싸했다."]], [["In the middle, they heard Dixon’s foot on the stairs. Mr.", "한창때에 딕슨의 발소리가 계단에서 들렸다."], ["Hale started from his languid posture in his great arm-chair, from which he had been watching his children in a dreamy way, as if they were acting some drama of happiness, which it was pretty to look at, but which was distinct from reality, and in which he had no part.", "헤일 씨는 큰 안락의자에 맥없이 기대 있던 자세에서 흠칫 몸을 일으켰다. 그는 거기서 두 자식을 꿈결처럼 지켜보고 있던 참이었다."], ["He stood up, and faced the door, showing such a strange, sudden anxiety to conceal Frederick from the sight of any person entering, even though it were the faithful Dixon, that a shiver came over Margaret’s heart: it reminded her of the new fear in their lives.", "마치 그들이, 보기에는 예쁘장하나 현실과는 동떨어진, 자기 몫이라고는 없는 행복의 연극을 연기하고 있기라도 한 것처럼. 그는 일어서서 문을 마주 보았다."], ["She caught at Frederick’s arm, and clutched it tight, while a stern thought compressed her brow, and caused her to set her teeth.", "들어오는 사람이 누구든, 그것이 충직한 딕슨이라 할지라도 프레더릭을 그 눈에 띄지 않게 감추려는 그 이상하고 갑작스러운 조바심에 마거릿의 가슴에 소름이 돋았다. 그것은 그들의 삶에 새로 들어선 두려움을 일깨웠다."], ["And yet they knew it was only Dixon’s measured tread.", "그녀는 프레더릭의 팔을 잡고 꽉 붙들었고, 그러는 동안 준엄한 생각이 이마를 찌푸리게 하고 이를 악물게 했다."], ["They heard her walk the length of the passage,—into the kitchen.", "그러면서도 그들은 그것이 딕슨의 절도 있는 발소리일 뿐임을 알고 있었다. 딕슨이 복도를 지나 부엌으로 들어가는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Margaret rose up.", "마거릿이 일어섰다."]], [["“I will go to her, and tell her.", "“제가 가서 말할게요."], ["And I shall hear how mamma is.”", "어머니가 어떠신지도 듣고 올게요.”"], ["Mrs. Hale was awake.", "헤일 부인은 깨어 있었다."], ["She rambled at first; but after they had given her some tea she was refreshed, though not disposed to talk.", "처음에는 헛소리를 했으나, 차를 조금 드리고 나자 기운을 차렸다. 그래도 말을 하고 싶어 하지는 않았다."], ["It was better that the night should pass over before she was told of her son’s arrival. Dr.", "아들이 왔다는 이야기는 하룻밤을 넘긴 뒤에 하는 편이 나았다."], ["Donaldson’s appointed visit would bring nervous excitement enough for the evening; and he might tell them how to prepare her for seeing Frederick.", "도널드슨 의사가 오기로 한 왕진만으로도 그날 저녁의 신경 쓰이는 흥분으로는 충분할 터였다. 게다가 그가 어머니에게 프레더릭을 만나게 할 채비를 어떻게 해야 할지 일러 줄지도 몰랐다."], ["He was there, in the house; could be summoned at any moment.", "프레더릭은 여기, 이 집에 있었다. 언제든 부르면 될 일이었다."]], [["Margaret could not sit still.", "마거릿은 가만히 앉아 있을 수 없었다."], ["It was a relief to her to aid Dixon in all her preparations for “Master Frederick.”", "“프레더릭 도련님”을 위한 딕슨의 온갖 채비를 거드는 것이 그녀에게는 오히려 위안이었다."], ["It seemed as though she never could be tired again.", "다시는 지치는 일이 없을 것만 같았다."], ["Each glimpse into the room where he sate by his father, conversing with him, about, she knew not what, nor cared to know,—was increase of strength to her.", "그가 아버지 곁에 앉아 무엇인지 알 수도 없고 알고 싶지도 않은 이야기를 나누고 있는 방을 한 번씩 들여다볼 때마다 그것이 그녀에게는 힘이 되었다."], ["Her own time for talking and hearing would come at last, and she was too certain of this to feel in a hurry to grasp it now.", "이야기하고 들을 그녀의 차례도 언젠가는 올 터였고, 그것을 너무나 확신했기에 지금 서둘러 붙들고 싶은 마음이 들지 않았다."], ["She took in his appearance and liked it.", "그녀는 그의 모습을 눈에 담았고, 마음에 들었다."], ["He had delicate features, redeemed from effeminacy by the swarthiness of his complexion, and his quick intensity of expression.", "그는 이목구비가 고왔는데, 가무잡잡한 얼굴빛과 재빠르고 강렬한 표정 덕분에 여자 같아 보이지는 않았다."], ["His eyes were generally merry-looking, but at times they and his mouth so suddenly changed, and gave her such an idea of latent passion, that it almost made her afraid.", "눈은 대개 유쾌해 보였으나, 이따금 눈과 입이 어찌나 갑자기 달라지며 속에 잠긴 격정을 짐작하게 하는지 그녀는 겁이 날 지경이었다. 그러나 그 표정은 한순간일 뿐이었고, 거기에는 고집스러움도 앙심도 없었다."], ["But this look was only for an instant; and had in it no doggedness, no vindictiveness; it was rather the instantaneous ferocity of expression that comes over the countenances of all natives of wild or southern countries—a ferocity which enhances the charm of the childlike softness into which such a look may melt away.", "그것은 오히려 거칠거나 남쪽에 있는 나라에서 나고 자란 사람들의 얼굴에 스치는 그 순간적인 사나움이었다. 그런 눈빛이 녹아들어 갈 수 있는 아이 같은 부드러움의 매력을 도리어 돋우어 주는 사나움이었다."], ["Margaret might fear the violence of the impulsive nature thus occasionally betrayed, but there was nothing in it to make her distrust, or recoil in the least, from the new-found brother.", "마거릿은 그렇게 이따금 드러나는 충동적인 천성의 격렬함이 두려웠을지언정, 그것 때문에 새로 얻은 오빠를 못 미더워하거나 조금이라도 물러설 일은 없었다."], ["On the contrary, all their intercourse was peculiarly charming to her from the very first.", "오히려 그와 주고받는 모든 것이 처음부터 그녀에게는 유난히 매력 있었다."], ["She knew then how much responsibility she had had to bear, from the exquisite sensation of relief which she felt in Frederick’s presence.", "프레더릭이 곁에 있음으로 해서 느끼는 그 절묘한 안도감으로 그녀는 자기가 그동안 얼마나 무거운 책임을 져 왔는지 비로소 알았다."], ["He understood his father and mother—their characters and their weaknesses, and went along with a careless freedom, which was yet most delicately careful not to hurt or wound any of their feelings.", "그는 아버지와 어머니를 알았다. 두 분의 성품과 약함을 알았고, 무심할 만큼 거리낌 없이 굴면서도 그 감정을 다치게 하거나 상하게 하지 않도록 더없이 섬세하게 마음을 썼다."], ["He seemed to know instinctively when a little of the natural brilliancy of his manner and conversation would not jar on the deep depression of his father, or might relieve his mother’s pain.", "그는 타고난 활기가 밴 태도와 이야기를 조금 내보여도 아버지의 깊은 침울함에 거슬리지 않을 때가, 혹은 어머니의 고통을 덜어 줄 수 있을 때가 언제인지 본능으로 아는 듯했다."], ["Whenever it would have been out of tune, and out of time, his patient devotion and watchfulness came into play, and made him an admirable nurse.", "가락도 때도 맞지 않을 자리에서는 참을성 있는 정성과 살핌이 나서서 그를 훌륭한 간병인으로 만들었다. 그리고 그가 뉴 포리스트에서 보낸 어린 시절을 자주 들먹일 때면 마거릿은 눈물이 날 만큼 마음이 움직였다."], ["Then Margaret was almost touched into tears by the allusions which he often made to their childish days in the New Forest; he had never forgotten her—or Helstone either—all the time he had been roaming among distant countries and foreign people.", "그는 먼 나라와 낯선 사람들 사이를 떠도는 내내 그녀도, 헬스톤도 잊은 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그 옛 자리 이야기를 그에게 마음껏 할 수 있었고, 그를 지루하게 할까 걱정할 일이 없었다."], ["She might talk to him of the old spot, and never feared tiring him.", "그녀는 그가 오기 전, 그의 도착을 그토록 바라면서도 한편으로는 그를 두려워했다."], ["She had been afraid of him before he came, even while she had longed for his coming; seven or eight years had, she felt, produced such great changes in herself that, forgetting how much of the original Margaret was left, she had reasoned that if her tastes and feelings had so materially altered, even in her stay-at-home life, his wild career, with which she was but imperfectly acquainted, must have almost substituted another Frederick for the tall stripling in his middy’s uniform, whom she remembered looking up to with such admiring awe.", "일고여덟 해 동안 자기 안에 그토록 큰 변화가 있었다고 느꼈기에, 본래의 마거릿이 얼마나 많이 남아 있는지는 잊은 채로, 집 안에만 있던 자기 삶에서도 취향과 감정이 이렇게 크게 달라졌다면, 자기가 어렴풋이만 알고 있는 그 거친 삶은 사관후보생 제복을 입고 우러러보며 감탄하던 그 훤칠한 소년 대신 아예 다른 프레더릭을 갖다 놓았으리라고 짐작했던 것이다. 그러나 떨어져 지내는 동안 두 사람은 나이로 보나 다른 여러 가지로 보나 서로 더 가까워져 있었다."], ["But in their absence they had grown nearer to each other in age, as well as in many other things.", "그리하여 이 슬픈 시절의 무게가 마거릿에게는 한결 가벼워졌다. 프레더릭이 곁에 있다는 것 말고 그녀에게 다른 빛은 없었다."], ["And so it was that the weight, this sorrowful time, was lightened to Margaret.", "몇 시간 동안 어머니는 아들을 보고 기운을 되찾았다."], ["Other light than that of Frederick’s presence she had none. For a few hours, the mother rallied on seeing her son.", "그녀는 아들의 손을 제 손에 쥔 채 앉아 있었고, 잠든 동안에도 그 손을 놓으려 하지 않았다."], ["She sate with his hand in hers; she would not part with it even while she slept; and Margaret had to feed him like a baby, rather than he should disturb her mother by removing a finger. Mrs.", "그래서 마거릿은 그가 손가락 하나 움직여 어머니를 방해하는 일이 없도록 그에게 아기처럼 음식을 떠먹여 주어야 했다."], ["Hale wakened while they were thus engaged; she slowly moved her head round on the pillow, and smiled at her children, as she understood what they were doing, and why it was done.", "그러고 있는 중에 헤일 부인이 깨어났다. 그녀는 베개 위에서 천천히 고개를 돌려, 두 자식이 무엇을 왜 하고 있는지 알아차리고는 그들에게 미소 지었다."]], [["“I am very selfish,”", "“내가 참 이기적이구나.”"], ["said she; “but it will not be for long.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그래도 오래가지는 않을 게다.”"], ["Frederick bent down and kissed the feeble hand that imprisoned his.", "프레더릭은 몸을 굽혀 제 손을 가두어 놓은 그 힘없는 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["This state of tranquility could not endure for many days, nor perhaps for many hours; so Dr.", "이 평온한 상태는 여러 날 갈 수 없었고, 어쩌면 여러 시간도 못 갈 것이었다."], ["Donaldson assured Margaret.", "도널드슨 의사는 마거릿에게 그렇게 못 박았다."], ["After the kind doctor had gone away, she stole down to Frederick, who, during the visit, had been adjured to remain quietly concealed in the back parlour, usually Dixon’s bedroom, but now given up to him.", "그 친절한 의사가 돌아간 뒤, 그녀는 왕진 동안 안쪽 뒷방에 조용히 숨어 있으라고 신신당부를 들은 프레더릭에게 살며시 내려갔다. 본래 딕슨의 침실이던 그 방은 이제 그에게 내준 것이었다."]], [["Margaret told him what Dr. Donaldson said.", "마거릿은 도널드슨 의사가 한 말을 그에게 전했다."]], [["“I don’t believe it,”", "“난 못 믿겠다.” 그가 소리쳤다."], ["he exclaimed.", "“어머니는 몹시 편찮으시지."], ["“She is very ill; she may be dangerously ill, and in immediate danger, too; but I can’t imagine that she could be as she is, if she were on the point of death.", "위독하실 수도 있고, 당장 위험할 수도 있어. 하지만 정말 돌아가실 지경이라면 지금 저러실 수는 없다고 본다."], ["Margaret!", "마거릿!"], ["she should have some other advice—some London doctor.", "다른 사람 소견도 들어 봐야 해. 런던 의사 말이야."], ["Have you never thought of that?”", "그런 생각은 해 보지 않았니?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“했어요.”"], ["said Margaret, “more than once.", "마거릿이 말했다. “여러 번요."], ["But I don’t believe it would do any good.", "하지만 그래 봐야 소용이 있을 것 같지 않아요."], ["And, you know, we have not the money to bring any great London surgeon down, and I am sure Dr.", "그리고 아시다시피 런던의 이름난 외과의를 모셔 올 돈이 우리에겐 없어요."], ["Donaldson is only second in skill to the very best,—if, indeed, he is to them.”", "게다가 도널드슨 선생님은 가장 훌륭한 분들 바로 다음가는 실력이라고 저는 믿어요. 아니, 어쩌면 그분들과 나란할지도 모르고요.”"]], [["Frederick began to walk up and down the room impatiently.", "프레더릭은 안달이 나서 방 안을 왔다 갔다 하기 시작했다."]], [["“I have credit in Cadiz,”", "“카디스에는 내 신용이 있는데.”"], ["said he, “but none here, owing to this wretched change of name.", "그가 말했다. “여기서는 이 몹쓸 개명 때문에 아무것도 안 되는구나."], ["Why did my father leave Helstone?", "아버지는 왜 헬스톤을 떠나셨을까?"], ["That was the blunder.”", "그게 잘못이었어.”"]], [["“It was no blunder,”", "“잘못이 아니에요.”"], ["said Margaret gloomily.", "마거릿이 침울하게 말했다."], ["“And above all possible chances, avoid letting papa hear anything like what you have just been saying.", "“그리고 무슨 일이 있어도 방금 하신 그런 말을 아버지가 듣게 하지 마세요."], ["I can see that he is tormenting himself already with the idea that mamma would never have been ill if we had stayed at Helstone, and you don’t know papa’s agonising power of self-reproach!”", "우리가 헬스톤에 남았더라면 어머니가 편찮으실 일도 없었으리라는 생각으로 아버지가 벌써 스스로를 괴롭히고 계신 게 보여요. 오빠는 아버지의 그 무서운 자책하는 힘을 모르시잖아요!”"]], [["Frederick walked away as if he were on the quarter-deck.", "프레더릭은 후갑판을 걷듯 저편으로 걸어갔다."], ["At last he stopped right opposite to Margaret, and looked at her drooping and desponding attitude for an instant.", "마침내 그는 마거릿의 바로 맞은편에 멈춰 서서 축 처지고 낙담한 그 모습을 잠시 바라보았다."]], [["“My little Margaret!”", "“우리 작은 마거릿!”"], ["said he, caressing her.", "그가 그녀를 어루만지며 말했다."], ["“Let us hope as long as we can.", "“할 수 있는 데까지는 희망을 품자."], ["Poor little woman!", "가엾은 것!"], ["what! is this face all wet with tears?", "아니, 얼굴이 온통 눈물범벅이구나?"], ["I will hope.", "나는 희망을 품겠다."], ["I will, in spite of a thousand doctors.", "의사가 천 명이 와도 그러겠다."], ["Bear up, Margaret, and be brave enough to hope!”", "견뎌 내라, 마거릿. 희망을 품을 만큼 용감해져라!”"]], [["Margaret choked in trying to speak, and when she did it was very low.", "마거릿은 말을 하려다 목이 메었고, 겨우 나온 목소리는 아주 낮았다."]], [["“I must try to be meek enough to trust.", "“믿을 수 있을 만큼 온순해지도록 애써 볼게요."], ["Oh, Frederick!", "아, 오빠!"], ["mamma was getting to love me so!", "어머니가 저를 그렇게 사랑하게 되어 가시던 참이었어요!"], ["And I was getting to understand her.", "저도 어머니를 이해하게 되어 가고 있었고요."], ["And now comes death to snap us asunder!”", "그런데 이제 죽음이 와서 우리를 뚝 잘라 놓는군요!”"]], [["“Come, come, come! Let us go upstairs, and do something, rather than waste time that may be so precious. Thinking has, many a time, made me sad, darling; but doing never did in all my life. My theory is a sort of parody on the maxim of ‘Get money, my son, honestly if you can; but get money.’ My precept is, ‘Do something my sister, do good if you can; but, at any rate, do something.’”", "“자, 자, 자! 이토록 귀할지 모를 시간을 헛되이 보내느니 위층으로 올라가서 뭐라도 하자. 생각은 여러 번 나를 슬프게 했지만, 무엇을 하는 일이 나를 슬프게 한 적은 평생 한 번도 없었다. 내 지론은 ‘돈을 벌어라, 아들아. 될 수 있으면 정직하게. 그러나 어쨌든 돈을 벌어라’ 하는 격언을 살짝 비튼 거야. 내 계율은 이렇다. ‘무엇이든 해라, 누이야. 될 수 있으면 좋은 일을. 그러나 어쨌든 무엇이든 해라.’”"]], [["“Not excluding mischief,”", "“못된 짓도 빼지 않고요?”"], ["said Margaret, smiling faintly through her tears.", "마거릿이 눈물 사이로 희미하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“By no means.", "“빼지 않고말고."], ["What I do exclude is the remorse afterwards.", "내가 빼는 것은 나중에 오는 뉘우침이야."], ["Blot your misdeeds out (if you are particularly conscientious), by a good deed, as soon as you can; just as we did a correct sum at school on the slate, where an incorrect one was only half rubbed out.", "잘못을 저질렀거든(네가 유난히 양심적이라면 말이다) 되도록 빨리 착한 일로 그걸 지워 버려라. 학교에서 석판에 틀린 셈을 반만 지운 자리에 맞는 셈을 새로 써넣었던 것처럼 말이야."], ["It was better than wetting our sponge with our tears; both less loss of time where tears had to be waited for, and a better effect at last.”", "그게 눈물로 지우개를 적시는 것보다 나았어. 눈물이 나오기를 기다려야 하니 시간도 덜 버리고, 결과도 더 나았지.”"]], [["If Margaret thought Frederick’s theory rather a rough one at first, she saw how he worked it out into continual production of kindness in fact.", "마거릿은 처음에는 프레더릭의 지론이 다소 거칠다고 여겼을지 몰라도, 그가 그것을 실제로 끊임없는 친절의 생산으로 풀어내는 것을 보았다."], ["After a bad night with his mother (for he insisted on taking his turn as a sitter-up) he was busy next morning before breakfast, contriving a leg-rest for Dixon, who was beginning to feel the fatigues of watching.", "어머니 곁에서 나쁜 밤을 보낸 뒤에도(그는 밤을 새워 지키는 차례를 제 몫으로 맡겠다고 우겼다) 그는 이튿날 아침 식사 전부터 밤샘의 피로를 느끼기 시작한 딕슨을 위해 다리받침을 궁리하느라 분주했다."], ["At breakfast-time, he interested Mr. Hale with vivid, graphic, rattling accounts of the wild life he had led in Mexico, South America, and elsewhere.", "아침 식사 때에는 멕시코와 남아메리카를 비롯한 여러 곳에서 보낸 거친 삶의 이야기를 생생하고 그림 같고 거침없이 늘어놓아 헤일 씨의 흥미를 끌었다."], ["Margaret would have given up the effort in despair to rouse Mr.", "마거릿이었다면 헤일 씨를 낙심에서 끌어내려는 노력을 절망 속에 접었을 것이다."], ["Hale out of his dejection; it would even have affected herself and rendered her incapable of talking at all.", "아니, 그런 것이 그녀 자신에게까지 옮아 아예 말을 할 수 없게 만들었을 것이다."], ["But Fred, true to his theory, did something perpetually; and talking was the only thing to be done, besides eating, at breakfast.", "그러나 프레드는 제 지론에 충실하게 쉼 없이 무엇인가를 했고, 아침 식탁에서 먹는 것 말고 할 수 있는 일이라고는 말하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Before the night of that day, Dr. Donaldson’s opinion was proved to be too well founded.", "그날 밤이 되기 전에 도널드슨 의사의 소견은 지나치리만치 근거가 있었음이 드러났다."], ["Convulsions came on; and when they ceased, Mrs. Hale was unconscious.", "경련이 일었고, 그것이 멎었을 때 헤일 부인은 의식이 없었다."], ["Her husband might lie by her shaking the bed with his sobs; her son’s strong arms might lift her tenderly up into a comfortable position; her daughter’s hands might bathe her face; but she knew them not.", "남편이 곁에 누워 흐느낌으로 침상을 흔들어도, 아들의 억센 팔이 그녀를 다정하게 들어 편한 자세로 옮겨 놓아도, 딸의 손이 그 얼굴을 닦아 주어도 그녀는 그들을 알지 못했다."], ["She would never recognise them again, till they met in Heaven.", "그녀는 하늘에서 다시 만날 때까지 다시는 그들을 알아보지 못할 터였다."]], [["Before the morning came all was over.", "아침이 오기 전에 모든 것이 끝났다."]], [["Then Margaret rose from her trembling and despondency, and became as a strong angel of comfort to her father and brother.", "그러자 마거릿은 떨림과 낙담에서 일어나 아버지와 오빠에게 위로의 굳센 천사가 되었다. 이제는 프레더릭이 무너져 버렸고, 그의 온갖 지론도 그에게는 아무 쓸모가 없었기 때문이다."], ["For Frederick had broken down now, and all his theories were of no use to him.", "밤에 제 작은 방에 갇혀 어찌나 격하게 우는지, 마거릿과 딕슨이 놀라 내려가 조용히 하라고 일러야 했다."], ["He cried so violently when shut up in his little room at night, that Margaret and Dixon came down in affright to warn him to be quiet: for the house partitions were but thin, and the next door neighbours might easily hear his youthful passionate sobs, so different from the slower trembling agony of after-life, when we become inured to grief, and dare not be rebellious against the inexorable doom, knowing who it is that decrees.", "집의 칸막이가 얇아서 옆집 사람들이 그 젊고 격정적인 흐느낌을 쉽게 들을 수 있었기 때문이다. 슬픔에 길이 들고, 누가 그것을 정하는지 알기에 감히 그 가차 없는 운명에 거스르지 못하는 뒷날의, 더 느리고 떨리는 고통과는 사뭇 다른 흐느낌이었다."]], [["Margaret sate with her father in the room of the dead.", "마거릿은 죽은 이의 방에서 아버지와 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["If he had cried, she would have been thankful.", "아버지가 울기라도 했더라면 그녀는 고마웠을 것이다."], ["But he sate by the bed quite quietly; only, from time to time, he uncovered the face, and stroked it gently, making a kind of soft inarticulate noise, like that of some mother-animal caressing her young.", "그러나 그는 침상 곁에 아주 조용히 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 다만 이따금 얼굴을 덮은 것을 걷고 부드럽게 쓸어 주면서, 새끼를 어루만지는 어미 짐승 같은 나직하고 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 냈다."], ["He took no notice of Margaret’s presence.", "그는 마거릿이 있다는 것도 알은체하지 않았다."], ["Once or twice she came up to kiss him; and he submitted to it, giving her a little push away when she had done, as if her affection disturbed him from his absorption in the dead.", "한두 번 그녀가 다가가 입을 맞추었고, 그는 그것을 가만히 받아 주었으나 끝나고 나면 살짝 밀어내는 것이었다. 마치 딸의 애정이 죽은 이에게 골몰한 자기를 흐트러뜨리기라도 하는 듯이."], ["He started when he heard Frederick’s cries, and shook his head:—“Poor boy!", "그는 프레더릭의 울음소리가 들리자 흠칫하고 고개를 저었다. “가엾은 것, 가엾은 것.”"], ["poor boy!” he said, and then took no more notice.", "그러고는 더 이상 알은체하지 않았다."], ["Margaret’s heart ached within her.", "마거릿의 가슴은 속으로 아팠다."], ["She could not think of her own loss in thinking of her father’s case.", "아버지의 처지를 생각하느라 자신이 잃은 것은 생각할 수도 없었다. 밤이 저물어 가고 날이 밝아 오려는 때에, 아무런 예고도 없이 마거릿의 목소리가 방의 고요를 깨뜨렸다."], ["The night was wearing away, and the day was at hand, when, without a word of preparation, Margaret’s voice broke upon the stillness of the room, with a clearness of sound that startled even herself: “Let not your heart be troubled,”", "그 소리가 어찌나 맑았는지 그녀 자신마저 놀랐다. “너희는 마음에 근심하지 말라.”"], ["it said; and she went steadily on through all that chapter of unspeakable consolation.", "목소리는 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 그 이루 말할 수 없는 위로의 장을 처음부터 끝까지 흔들림 없이 읽어 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI", "# 제31장"]], [["“SHOULD AULD ACQUAINTANCE BE FORGOT.”", "「옛 친구를 어이 잊으리」"]], [["“Show not that manner, and these features all, The serpent’s cunning, and the sinner’s fall?”", "“저 태도와 저 모든 이목구비가 드러내지 않던가, 뱀의 교활함과 죄인의 몰락을?”"], ["CRABBE.", "— 크래브."]], [["The chill, shivery October morning came; not the October morning of the country, with soft, silvery mists, clearing off before the sunbeams that bring out all the gorgeous beauty of colouring, but the October morning of Milton, whose silvery mists were heavy fogs, and where the sun could only show long dusky streets when he did break through and shine.", "쌀쌀하고 으슬으슬한 10월 아침이 왔다. 부드럽고 은빛 도는 안개가 햇살에 걷히며 온갖 찬란한 빛깔의 아름다움을 드러내는 시골의 10월 아침이 아니라, 은빛 안개라는 것이 곧 짙은 매연 안개인 밀턴의 10월 아침이었다."], ["Margaret went languidly about, assisting Dixon in her task of arranging the house.", "이곳에서는 해가 구름을 뚫고 나와 비친다 해도 길게 뻗은 침침한 거리들밖에 보여 줄 것이 없었다."], ["Her eyes were continually blinded by tears, but she had no time to give way to regular crying.", "마거릿은 집 안을 정돈하는 딕슨의 일을 도우며 맥없이 오갔다. 눈은 쉴 새 없이 눈물로 흐려졌지만 제대로 울고 있을 겨를이 없었다."], ["The father and brother depended upon her; while they were giving way to grief, she must be working, planning, considering.", "아버지와 오빠가 그녀에게 기대고 있었다. 그들이 슬픔에 몸을 맡기고 있는 동안 그녀는 일하고 계획하고 헤아려야 했다."], ["Even the necessary arrangements for the funeral seemed to devolve upon her.", "장례에 필요한 준비마저 그녀의 몫으로 떨어지는 듯했다."]], [["When the fire was bright and crackling—when everything was ready for breakfast, and the tea-kettle was singing away, Margaret gave a last look round the room before going to summon Mr. Hale and Frederick.", "불이 환하게 타닥거리고, 아침 식사 채비가 모두 갖추어지고, 주전자가 노래하듯 끓을 무렵, 마거릿은 헤일 씨와 프레더릭을 부르러 가기 전에 방 안을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보았다."], ["She wanted everything to look as cheerful as possible; and yet, when it did so, the contrast between it and her own thoughts forced her into sudden weeping.", "그녀는 무엇이든 되도록 밝아 보이기를 바랐다. 그런데 정말로 그렇게 되고 보니, 그것과 자기 마음속 생각 사이의 대비가 그녀를 불쑥 울음으로 몰아넣었다."], ["She was kneeling by the sofa, hiding her face in the cushions that no one might hear her cry, when she was touched on the shoulder by Dixon.", "우는 소리를 아무도 듣지 못하도록 소파 옆에 무릎을 꿇고 방석에 얼굴을 파묻고 있는데, 딕슨이 그녀의 어깨를 건드렸다."]], [["“Come, Miss Hale—come, my dear!", "“자, 헤일 아가씨, 자, 우리 아가씨!"], ["You must not give way, or where shall we all be?", "이러시면 안 됩니다요. 아가씨가 무너지면 저희는 다 어찌 되겠어요?"], ["There is not another person in the house fit to give a direction of any kind, and there is so much to be done.", "이 집에 무슨 지시 하나 내릴 만한 사람이 달리 없는데 할 일은 산더미랍니다. 장례를 누가 맡을지, 누가 올지, 어디서 치를지, 다 정해야 하잖아요."], ["There’s who’s to manage the funeral; and who’s to come to it; and where it’s to be; and all to be settled: and Master Frederick’s like one crazed with crying, and master never was a good one for settling; and, poor gentleman, he goes about now as if he was lost.", "프레더릭 도련님은 울다가 넋이 나간 사람 같고, 주인어른은 원래 무슨 일을 정하시는 분이 아니고요. 딱하기도 하시지, 지금은 넋을 잃으신 사람처럼 돌아다니시기만 하니."], ["It’s bad enough, my dear, I know; but death comes to us all; and you’re well off never to have lost any friend till now.”", "얼마나 힘드신지 저도 압니다요. 그렇지만 죽음은 누구에게나 오는 것이고, 아가씨는 여태 벗 하나 잃어 본 적이 없으니 복이 많으신 거예요.”"]], [["Perhaps so.", "그럴지도 몰랐다."], ["But this seemed a loss by itself; not to bear comparison with any other event in the world.", "그러나 이 상실은 그 자체로 따로 떨어져 있는 듯했고, 세상 어떤 일과도 견줄 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["Margaret did not take any comfort from what Dixon said, but the unusual tenderness of the prim old servant’s manner touched her to the heart; and, more from a desire to show her gratitude for this than for any other reason, she roused herself up, and smiled in answer to Dixon’s anxious look at her; and went to tell her father and brother that breakfast was ready.", "마거릿은 딕슨의 말에서 아무 위로도 얻지 못했지만, 그 깐깐한 늙은 하녀가 보인 유별난 다정함이 마음에 사무쳤다. 그리고 다른 무엇보다 그 고마움을 보이고 싶은 마음에서 그녀는 몸을 추스르고, 자기를 근심스레 바라보는 딕슨의 눈길에 미소로 답한 뒤, 아버지와 오빠에게 아침 식사가 준비되었다고 알리러 갔다."]], [["Mr.", "헤일 씨가 왔다."], ["Hale came—as if in a dream, or rather with the unconscious motion of a sleep-walker, whose eyes and mind perceive other things than what are present.", "꿈속에 있는 사람처럼, 아니 그보다는 눈과 마음이 눈앞의 것이 아닌 다른 것을 보고 있는 몽유병자의 무의식한 몸짓으로."], ["Frederick came briskly in, with a forced cheerfulness, grasped her hand, looked into her eyes and burst into tears.", "프레더릭은 억지로 꾸민 쾌활함을 띠고 성큼 들어와 그녀의 손을 잡고 눈을 들여다보다가 왈칵 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 그녀는 아침 식사 내내 이런저런 사소한 이야깃거리를 짜내야 했다."], ["She had to try and think of little nothings to say all breakfast-time, in order to prevent the recurrence of her companions’ thoughts too strongly to the last meal they had taken together, when there had been a continual strained listening for some sound or signal from the sick-room.", "함께했던 마지막 식사 때로 두 사람의 생각이 너무 강하게 되돌아가지 않도록 하기 위해서였다. 그때는 병실에서 무슨 소리나 기별이 오지 않을까 줄곧 마음 졸이며 귀를 기울이고 있었다."]], [["After breakfast, she resolved to speak to her father about the funeral.", "아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 그녀는 아버지에게 장례 이야기를 꺼내기로 마음먹었다. 아버지는 고개를 저으면서도 그녀가 내놓는 모든 제안에 동의했다."], ["He shook his head, and assented to all she proposed, though many of her propositions absolutely contradicted one another.", "그 제안들 가운데 여럿은 서로 정면으로 어긋나는데도 그랬다. 마거릿은 아버지에게서 아무 실질적인 결정도 얻어 내지 못했다."], ["Margaret gained no real decision from him; and was leaving the room languidly, to have a consultation with Dixon, when Mr. Hale motioned her back to his side.", "딕슨과 의논하려고 맥없이 방을 나서려는데, 헤일 씨가 손짓으로 그녀를 다시 곁으로 불렀다."]], [["“Ask Mr. Bell,”", "“벨 씨에게 물어보아라.”"], ["said he in a hollow voice.", "아버지가 공허한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Bell!”", "“벨 씨요!”"], ["said she, a little surprised.", "그녀가 조금 놀라 말했다."], ["“Mr. Bell of Oxford?”", "“옥스퍼드의 벨 씨 말씀이세요?”"]], [["“Mr. Bell,”", "“벨 씨.”"], ["he repeated.", "아버지가 되풀이했다."], ["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["He was my groom’s-man.”", "그 사람이 내 신랑 들러리였다.”"]], [["Margaret understood the association.", "마거릿은 그 연상의 까닭을 알아차렸다."]], [["“I will write to-day,”", "“오늘 편지 쓸게요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["He sank again into listlessness.", "아버지는 다시 무기력 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["All morning she toiled on, longing for rest, but in a continual whirl of melancholy business.", "아침 내내 그녀는 쉬기를 간절히 바라면서도 서글픈 일거리의 소용돌이 속에서 쉬지 않고 애썼다."]], [["Towards evening Dixon said to her:", "저녁 무렵 딕슨이 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“I’ve done it, miss.", "“해 버렸습니다요, 아가씨."], ["I was really afraid for master, that he’d have a stroke with grief.", "저는 정말이지 주인어른이 슬픔에 겨워 졸도라도 하실까 겁이 났어요."], ["He’s been all this day with poor missus; and when I’ve listened at the door, I’ve heard him talking to her, and talking to her, as if she was alive.", "온종일 가엾은 마님 곁에만 계셨거든요. 제가 문에 귀를 대 보면 마님이 살아 계시기라도 한 듯 자꾸자꾸 말을 거시는 소리가 들렸고요."], ["When I went in he would be quite quiet, but all in a maze like.", "제가 들어가면 아주 조용해지시지만 넋이 나간 것 같았지요."], ["So I thought to myself, he ought to be roused; and if it gives him a shock at first, it will, maybe, be the better for him afterwards.", "그래서 저 혼자 생각했답니다. 어른을 좀 흔들어 깨워 드려야겠다, 처음에는 충격이 될지 몰라도 나중에는 오히려 나으실지 모른다고요."], ["So I’ve been and told him, that I don’t think it’s safe for Master Frederick to be here.", "그래서 가서 말씀드렸어요. 프레더릭 도련님이 여기 계시는 게 안전할 것 같지 않다고요."], ["And I don’t.", "사실 그렇고요."], ["It was only on Tuesday, when I was out, that I met a Southampton man—the first I’ve seen since I came to Milton; they don’t make their way much up here, I think.", "바로 화요일에, 제가 밖에 나갔다가 사우샘프턴 사람을 하나 만났지 뭐예요. 밀턴에 온 뒤로 처음 본 사우샘프턴 사람이었어요."], ["Well, it was young Leonards, old Leonards the draper’s son, as great a scamp as ever lived—who plagued his father almost to death, and then ran off to sea.", "이 위쪽까지는 잘들 안 올라오는 모양이더군요. 그런데 그게 하필 젊은 레너즈, 포목상 늙은 레너즈의 아들이었어요."], ["I never could abide him.", "세상에 다시없을 건달이지요. 제 아비를 죽을 지경으로 들볶다가 바다로 달아난 놈이에요."], ["He was in the Orion at the same time as Frederick, I know; though I don’t recollect if he was there at the mutiny.”", "저는 도무지 그놈을 견딜 수가 없었어요. 프레더릭 도련님과 같은 때에 오라이언호에 타고 있었던 건 분명한데, 반란 때도 거기 있었는지는 기억이 안 나네요.”"]], [["“Did he know you?”", "“그 사람이 딕슨을 알아보았어요?”"], ["said Margaret, eagerly.", "마거릿이 다급하게 물었다."]], [["“Why, that’s the worst of it.", "“그게 바로 제일 나쁜 대목이랍니다. 제가 바보같이 그 이름을 불러 대지만 않았어도 저를 알아보지 못했을 거예요."], ["I don’t believe he would have known me but for my being such a fool as to call out his name.", "낯선 데서 만난 사우샘프턴 사람이니 그랬지, 아니었다면 그런 고약하고 쓸모없는 놈에게 그렇게 선뜻 아는 척을 했겠어요? 놈이 그러더군요."], ["He were a Southampton man, in a strange place, or else I should never have been so ready to call cousins with him, a nasty, good-for-nothing-fellow.", "‘딕슨 양! 여기서 뵐 줄 누가 알았겠습니까?"], ["Says he, ‘Miss Dixon!", "아니, 제가 잘못 알았나요, 이제는 딕슨 양이 아니신가요?’ 그래서 저는 아직 처녀로 불러도 좋다고, 다만 내가 그렇게 까다롭게 굴지만 않았어도 혼인할 기회는 얼마든지 있었다고 일러 주었지요. 놈은 제법 공손했어요."], ["who would ha’ thought of seeing you here?", "‘저를 보고서야 그 말씀을 의심할 수가 있겠습니까’ 하더군요. 그렇지만 그런 놈이 던지는 그런 겨 같은 소리에 넘어갈 제가 아니지요."], ["But perhaps I mistake, and you’re Miss Dixon no longer?’ So I told him he might still address me as an unmarried lady, though if I hadn’t been so particular, I’d had good chances of matrimony.", "그렇게 말해 주었고요. 그러고는 앙갚음 삼아 그놈 아비 안부를 물었답니다."], ["He was polite enough: ‘He couldn’t look at me and doubt me.’ But I were not to be caught with such chaff from such a fellow as him, and so I told him; and, by way of being even, I asked him after his father (who I knew had turned him out of doors), as if they were the best friends as ever was.", "아비가 그놈을 집에서 내쫓은 걸 뻔히 알면서, 세상에 둘도 없는 사이라도 되는 양 말이지요. 그랬더니 저를 약 올리려고, 아시겠지만 서로 그렇게 점잖게 굴면서도 속으로는 점점 독이 오르던 참이었거든요, 프레더릭 도련님 안부를 캐묻기 시작하더군요."], ["So then, to spite me—for you see we were getting savage, for all we were so civil to each other—he began to inquire after Master Frederick, and said, what a scrape he’d got into (as if Master Frederick’s scrapes would ever wash George Leonards white, or make ’em look otherwise than nasty, dirty black), and how he’d be hung for mutiny if ever he were caught, and how a hundred pound reward had been offered for catching him, and what a disgrace he had been to his family—all to spite me, you see, my dear, because before now I’ve helped old Mr.", "도련님이 얼마나 큰일에 휘말렸는지 하면서요. 마치 프레더릭 도련님이 저지른 일이 조지 레너즈를 희게 씻어 주기라도 한다는 듯이, 아니면 그 시커멓고 더러운 꼴을 달리 보이게 해 주기라도 한다는 듯이 말이에요."], ["Leonards to give George a good rating, down in Southampton.", "잡히기만 하면 반란죄로 목이 매달릴 거라는 둥, 붙잡는 사람에게 100파운드 현상금이 걸렸다는 둥, 집안에 얼마나 먹칠을 했느냐는 둥. 다 저를 약 올리려는 거였어요, 아가씨."], ["So I said, there were other families as I knew, who had far more cause to blush for their sons, and to be thankful if they could think they were earning an honest living far away from home.", "예전에 사우샘프턴에서 제가 늙은 레너즈 씨를 도와 조지를 톡톡히 나무란 적이 있거든요. 그래서 제가 말해 주었지요."], ["To which he made answer, like the impudent chap he is, that he were in a confidential situation, and if I knew of any young man who had been so unfortunate as to lead vicious courses, and wanted to turn steady, he’d have no objection to lend him his patronage.", "내가 아는 집안 중에는 제 자식 때문에 낯을 붉힐 까닭이 그보다 훨씬 많은 집도 있고, 그 자식이 집에서 멀리 떨어져 정직하게 벌어먹고 산다고만 여길 수 있어도 감사할 집이 있다고요. 그랬더니 그 뻔뻔한 놈이 뭐라는 줄 아세요?"], ["He, indeed!", "자기는 남이 믿고 맡기는 자리에 있으니, 못된 길로 빠졌다가 마음을 잡고 싶어 하는 젊은이를 제가 알거든 기꺼이 뒤를 봐주겠다지 뭐예요. 저놈이요!"], ["Why, he’d corrupt a saint.", "성인군자도 물들일 놈이에요. 요 며칠 전 그놈과 마주 서서 말을 섞을 때만큼 속이 상한 적이 여러 해 만에 처음이었답니다."], ["I’ve not felt so bad myself for years as when I were standing talking to him the other day.", "그놈을 더 매섭게 쏘아붙이지 못한 게 분해서 눈물이 다 날 뻔했어요. 놈은 제 얼굴에다 대고 계속 웃기만 했거든요."], ["I could have cried to think I couldn’t spite him better, for he kept smiling in my face, as if he took all my compliments for earnest; and I couldn’t see that he minded what I said in the least, while I was mad with all his speeches.”", "제 빈정거림을 죄다 참말로 받아들이기라도 하는 것처럼요. 저는 그놈의 말마다 속이 뒤집히는데, 놈은 제 말은 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않는 눈치였어요.”"]], [["“But you did not tell him anything about us—about Frederick?”", "“그렇지만 우리 이야기는 하지 않았지요? 프레더릭 오빠 이야기도요?”"]], [["“Not I,” said Dixon.", "“안 했지요.”"], ["“He had never the grace to ask where I was staying; and I shouldn’t have told him if he had asked.", "딕슨이 말했다. “그놈은 제가 어디 묵는지 물어볼 염치도 없었고, 물었다 한들 말해 주지 않았을 거예요."], ["Nor did I ask him what his precious situation was.", "저도 그 잘난 자리가 뭔지 묻지 않았고요."], ["He was waiting for a bus, and just then it drove up, and he hailed it.", "놈은 승합마차를 기다리고 있었는데 마침 마차가 와서 손을 흔들어 세우더군요."], ["But, to plague me to the last, he turned back before he got in, and said, ‘If you can help me to trap Lieutenant Hale, Miss Dixon, we’ll go partners in the reward.", "그런데 끝까지 저를 약 올리려고 올라타기 전에 돌아서서 이러는 거예요. ‘딕슨 양, 헤일 중위를 잡는 걸 도와주시면 현상금을 반씩 나눕시다."], ["I know you’d like to be my partner, now wouldn’t you?", "저와 짝이 되고 싶으시죠, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["Don’t be shy, but say yes.’ And he jumped on the bus, and I saw his ugly face leering at me with a wicked smile to think how he’d had the last word of plaguing.”", "부끄러워 마시고 그러자고 하세요.’ 그러고는 마차에 훌쩍 올라탔어요. 마지막까지 약을 올렸다고 좋아하며 그 흉한 얼굴로 심술궂게 히죽거리며 저를 곁눈질하는 게 보이더군요.”"]], [["Margaret was made very uncomfortable by this account of Dixon’s.", "딕슨의 이 이야기에 마거릿은 몹시 불안해졌다."]], [["“Have you told Frederick?”", "“오빠에게 말했어요?”"], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“No,” said Dixon.", "“아니요.”"], ["“I were uneasy in my mind at knowing that bad Leonards was in town; but there was so much else to think about that I did not dwell on it all.", "딕슨이 말했다. “그 못된 레너즈가 이 도시에 있다는 걸 알고 마음이 편치 않았지만, 달리 생각할 일이 하도 많아서 거기까지 마음을 쓰지 못했어요."], ["But when I saw master sitting so stiff, and with his eyes so glazed and sad, I thought it might rouse him to have to think of Master Frederick in hiding, he would have to go, poor fellow, before Mr.", "그런데 주인어른이 그렇게 굳은 채로 앉아 계시고 눈은 그렇게 흐릿하고 서글픈 것을 보니, 프레더릭 도련님이 숨어 지내는 일을 생각하시게 하면 정신이 드실까 싶었지요. 도련님은 가셔야 해요, 딱하시게도."], ["Bell came.”", "벨 씨가 오시기 전에요.”"]], [["“Oh, I’m not afraid of Mr.", "“아, 벨 씨는 두렵지 않아요."], ["Bell; but I am afraid of this Leonards.", "그렇지만 그 레너즈라는 사람은 두려워요."], ["I must tell Frederick.", "오빠에게 말해야겠어요."], ["What did Leonards look like?”", "레너즈는 어떻게 생겼던가요?”"]], [["“A bad-looking fellow, I can assure you, miss.", "“못되게 생긴 놈이지요, 아가씨. 정말이랍니다."], ["Whiskers such as I should be ashamed to wear—they are so red.", "구레나룻은 어찌나 빨간지 제가 다 부끄러울 지경이고요."], ["And for all he said he’d got a confidential situation, he was dressed in fustian just like a working-man.”", "남이 믿고 맡기는 자리에 있다고 큰소리는 쳤어도, 옷차림은 영락없는 노동자처럼 퍼스티언 천이더군요.”"]], [["It was evident that Frederick must go.", "프레더릭이 떠나야 한다는 것은 분명했다."], ["Go, too, when he had so completely vaulted into his place in the family, and promised to be such a stay and staff to his father and sister.", "그것도 그가 그토록 온전히 제자리로 뛰어들어 아버지와 누이에게 든든한 기둥이자 지팡이가 되어 주겠다고 약속한 마당에."], ["Go, when his cares for the living mother, and sorrow for the dead, seemed to make him one of those peculiar people who are bound to us by a fellow-love for them that are taken away.", "살아 계실 적 어머니를 향한 보살핌과 돌아가신 어머니를 향한 슬픔이 그를, 우리에게서 떠나간 이를 함께 사랑함으로써 우리와 맺어지는 그 각별한 사람들 가운데 하나로 만들어 준 마당에."], ["Just as Margaret was thinking all this, sitting over the drawing-room fire—her father restless and uneasy under the pressure of this newly-aroused fear, of which he had not as yet spoken—Frederick came in, his brightness dimmed, but the extreme violence of his grief passed away.", "마거릿이 응접실 난롯가에 앉아 이 모든 것을 생각하고 있을 때—아버지는 아직 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 새로 일깨워진 이 두려움에 눌려 안절부절못하고 있었다—프레더릭이 들어왔다. 밝은 빛은 흐려졌지만 슬픔의 사나운 물결은 지나간 뒤였다."], ["He came up to Margaret, and kissed her forehead.", "그는 마거릿에게 다가와 이마에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“How wan you look, Margaret!”", "“얼굴이 어쩌면 그리 핼쑥하니, 마거릿!”"], ["said he in a low voice.", "그가 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You have been thinking of everybody, and no one has thought of you.", "“너는 모두를 생각해 주었는데 아무도 너를 생각해 주지 않았구나."], ["Lie on this sofa—there is nothing for you to do.”", "이 소파에 누우렴. 네가 할 일은 아무것도 없다.”"]], [["“That is the worst,”", "“그게 제일 견디기 힘든 일이에요.”"], ["said Margaret, in a sad whisper.", "마거릿이 서글픈 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["But she went and lay down, and her brother covered her feet with a shawl and then sate on the ground by her side; and the two began to talk together in a subdued tone.", "그러면서도 그녀는 가서 누웠고, 오빠는 숄로 그녀의 발을 덮어 준 뒤 곁의 바닥에 앉았다. 그리고 두 사람은 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["Margaret told him all that Dixon had related of her interview with young Leonards.", "마거릿은 딕슨이 젊은 레너즈와 마주친 이야기를 들려준 그대로 오빠에게 옮겼다."], ["Frederick’s lips closed with a long whew of dismay.", "프레더릭의 입술이 길게 휘파람 같은 낭패의 소리를 내며 다물렸다."]], [["“I should just like to have it out with that young fellow.", "“그 젊은 놈과 한번 담판을 지어 보고 싶구나."], ["A worse sailor was never on board ship—nor a much worse man either.", "그놈보다 못한 뱃사람은 배에 타 본 적이 없고, 사람됨도 그만큼이나 못했지."], ["I declare, Margaret—you know the circumstances of the whole affair?”", "정말이지, 마거릿, 너 그 일이 어떻게 된 건지 전부 알고 있지?”"]], [["“Yes, mamma told me.”", "“네, 어머니가 말씀해 주셨어요.”"]], [["“Well, when all the sailors who were good for anything were indignant with our captain, this fellow to curry favour—pah!", "“그래, 쓸 만한 뱃사람이라면 다들 우리 함장에게 분개하고 있을 때 그놈은 비위를 맞추겠다고, 쳇!"], ["And to think of his being here!", "그런 놈이 여기 와 있다니!"], ["Oh, if he’d a notion I was within twenty miles of him, he’d ferret me out to pay all old grudges.", "아, 내가 20마일 안에 있다는 낌새라도 챘다간 묵은 원한을 죄다 갚으려고 나를 기어이 캐낼 거다."], ["I’d rather anybody had the hundred pounds they think I am worth than that rascal.", "저 악당이 가져가느니 차라리 다른 누구라도 사람들이 내 목에 걸었다는 100파운드를 챙기는 게 낫겠어."], ["What a pity poor old Dixon could not be persuaded to give me up, and make a provision for her old age!”", "가엾은 늙은 딕슨을 구슬려 나를 넘기게 하고 노후 밑천이나 마련해 주지 못하는 게 안타깝군!”"]], [["“Oh, Frederick, hush!", "“아, 오빠, 그만해요!"], ["Don’t talk so.”", "그런 말 하지 마세요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale came towards them, eager and trembling.", "헤일 씨가 다급하게 몸을 떨며 그들에게 다가왔다."], ["He had overheard what they were saying.", "그는 두 사람이 하는 말을 엿들었던 것이다."], ["He took Frederick’s hand in both of his:", "그는 두 손으로 프레더릭의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“My boy, you must go.", "“얘야, 너는 가야 한다."], ["It is very bad—but I see you must.", "참으로 괴로운 일이다만, 아무래도 가야겠구나."], ["You have done all you could—you have been a comfort to her.”", "너는 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다. 네가 그 사람에게 위안이 되어 주었어.”"]], [["“Oh, papa, must he go?”", "“아, 아버지, 오빠가 꼭 가야 하나요?”"], ["said Margaret, pleading against her own conviction of necessity.", "마거릿이 그럴 수밖에 없다는 자신의 확신을 거스르며 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["“I declare, I’ve a good mind to face it out, and stand my trial.", "“정말이지 차라리 맞서서 재판을 받아 보고 싶은 심정입니다."], ["If I could only pick up my evidence!", "증거만 그러모을 수 있다면!"], ["I cannot endure the thought of being in the power of such a blackguard as Leonards.", "레너즈 같은 악당의 손아귀에 놓인다는 생각은 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I could almost have enjoyed—in other circumstances—this stolen visit: it has had all the charm which the Frenchwoman attributed to forbidden pleasures.”", "다른 사정만 아니었다면 이 몰래 온 방문이 즐거울 뻔했는데요. 어느 프랑스 부인이 금지된 즐거움에 있다고 한 그 온갖 매력이 이 방문에 다 있었으니까요.”"]], [["“One of the earliest things I can remember,”", "“제가 기억하는 가장 오래된 일 가운데 하나가,” 마거릿이 말했다."], ["said Margaret, “was your being in some great disgrace, Fred, for stealing apples.", "“오빠가 사과를 훔쳐서 크게 야단맞던 일이에요, 프레드 오빠. 우리 집에도 사과가 잔뜩 있었잖아요."], ["We had plenty of our own—trees loaded with them; but some one had told you that stolen fruit tasted sweetest, which you took au pied de la lettre, and off you went a-robbing.", "나무마다 주렁주렁 달렸는데. 그런데 누가 훔친 과일이 제일 달다고 오빠에게 일러 주었고, 오빠는 그 말을 글자 그대로 받아들여 훔치러 나갔지요."], ["You have not changed your feelings much since then.”", "오빠는 그때 이후로 마음 씀씀이가 별로 달라지지 않았네요.”"]], [["“Yes—you must go,” repeated Mr.", "“그래, 너는 가야 한다.”"], ["Hale, answering Margaret’s question, which she had asked some time ago.", "헤일 씨가 마거릿이 한참 전에 던진 물음에 답하며 되풀이했다."], ["His thoughts were fixed on one subject, and it was an effort to him to follow the zigzag remarks of his children—an effort which he did not make.", "그의 생각은 한 가지에만 붙박여 있었고, 자식들의 종잡을 수 없는 이야기를 좇는 것은 그에게 애를 써야 하는 일이었다. 그리고 그는 그런 애를 쓰지 않았다."]], [["Margaret and Frederick looked at each other.", "마거릿과 프레더릭은 서로를 바라보았다."], ["That quick momentary sympathy would be theirs no longer if he went away.", "오빠가 떠나고 나면 그 순간적으로 통하는 마음은 더 이상 둘의 것이 아닐 터였다."], ["So much was understood through eyes that could not be put into words.", "말로 옮길 수 없는 그 많은 것이 눈길을 통해 오갔다."], ["Both coursed the same thought till it was lost in sadness.", "두 사람은 같은 생각을 좇다가 슬픔 속에서 그것을 놓쳤다."], ["Frederick shook it off first:", "프레더릭이 먼저 그것을 떨쳐 냈다."]], [["“Do you know, Margaret, I was very nearly giving both Dixon and myself a good fright this afternoon.", "“있잖니, 마거릿, 오늘 오후에 딕슨과 나를 둘 다 크게 놀라게 할 뻔했다."], ["I was in my bedroom; I had heard a ring at the front door, but I thought the ringer must have done his business and gone away long ago; so I was on the point of making my appearance in the passage, when, as I opened my room door, I saw Dixon coming downstairs; and she frowned and kicked me into hiding again.", "나는 내 방에 있었는데, 현관에서 초인종 소리가 난 것은 들었지만 벨을 누른 사람이 볼일을 마치고 진작에 갔으려니 했지. 그래서 막 복도로 나서려던 참이었는데, 방문을 여니 딕슨이 계단을 내려오고 있더구나."], ["I kept the door open, and heard a message given to some man that was in my father’s study, and that then went away.", "딕슨은 얼굴을 찌푸리며 나를 도로 숨으라고 밀어 넣었지. 나는 문을 열어 둔 채로 있었는데, 아버지 서재에 있던 어떤 사람에게 전갈을 전하는 소리가 들리더니 그 사람이 가더라."], ["What could it have been?", "대체 무슨 일이었을까?"], ["Some of the shopmen?”", "가게 사람인가?”"]], [["“Very likely,”", "“그럴 거예요.”"], ["said Margaret indifferently.", "마거릿이 무심하게 말했다."], ["“There was a little quiet man who came up for orders about two o’clock.”", "“2시쯤에 주문을 받으러 온 자그맣고 조용한 사람이 있었어요.”"]], [["“But this was not a little man—a great powerful fellow; and it was past four when he was here.”", "“그런데 이건 자그마한 사람이 아니었어. 덩치가 크고 힘깨나 쓰게 생긴 사내였고, 여기 왔을 때는 4시가 넘어서였다.”"]], [["“It was Mr.", "“손턴 씨였다.”"], ["Thornton,” said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Hale. They were glad to have drawn him into the conversation.", "두 사람은 아버지를 대화 속으로 끌어들이게 되어 반가웠다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton!”", "“손턴 씨요!”"], ["said Margaret, a little surprised.", "마거릿이 조금 놀라 말했다."], ["“I thought——”", "“저는 또…….”"]], [["“Well, little one, what did you think?”", "“그래, 우리 꼬마 아가씨, 뭐라고 생각했는데?”"], ["asked Frederick, as she did not finish her sentence.", "그녀가 말을 맺지 않자 프레더릭이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, only,”", "“아, 그냥,”"], ["said she, reddening and looking straight at him, “I fancied you meant some one of a different class, not a gentleman; somebody come on an errand.”", "그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 똑바로 바라보고 말했다. “오빠가 다른 계층의 사람, 신사가 아닌 사람, 무슨 심부름으로 온 사람을 말하는 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“He looked like someone of that kind,”", "“그런 부류로 보이던걸.”"], ["said Frederick, carelessly.", "프레더릭이 대수롭지 않게 말했다."], ["“I took him for a shopman, and he turns out a manufacturer.”", "“나는 가게 사람인 줄 알았는데 공장주라니.”"]], [["Margaret was silent.", "마거릿은 잠자코 있었다."], ["She remembered how at first, before she knew his character, she had spoken and thought of him just as Frederick was doing.", "그녀는 처음에, 그의 사람됨을 알기 전에 자기도 꼭 프레더릭처럼 그를 말하고 그렇게 생각했던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["It was but a natural impression that was made upon him, and yet she was a little annoyed by it.", "오빠가 받은 것은 자연스러운 인상일 뿐이었으나, 그래도 그녀는 그것이 조금 언짢았다."], ["She was unwilling to speak; she wanted to make Frederick understand what kind of person Mr.", "그녀는 말하고 싶지 않았다. 손턴 씨가 어떤 사람인지 프레더릭에게 알려 주고 싶었다."], ["Thornton was—but she was tongue-tied.", "그러나 혀가 굳은 듯 말이 나오지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Hale went on.", "헤일 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["“He came to offer any assistance in his power, I believe.", "“자기가 할 수 있는 도움이라면 무엇이든 주겠다고 온 모양이더라."], ["But I could not see him.", "그렇지만 나는 그를 만날 수가 없었다."], ["I told Dixon to ask him if he would like to see you—I think I asked her to find you, and you would go to him.", "딕슨더러 그에게 너를 보고 싶은지 물어보라고 했지. 너를 찾아보라고, 그러면 네가 그에게 가겠다고 한 것 같기도 하고."], ["I don’t know what I said.”", "내가 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠구나.”"]], [["“He has been a very agreeable acquaintance, has he not?”", "“아주 마음에 맞는 지인이었나 보군요?”"], ["asked Frederick, throwing the question like a ball for any one to catch who chose.", "프레더릭이 누구든 잡고 싶은 사람이 잡으라는 듯 공을 던지듯 물었다."]], [["“A very kind friend,”", "“아주 친절한 벗이지요.”"], ["said Margaret, when her father did not answer.", "아버지가 대답하지 않자 마거릿이 말했다."]], [["Frederick was silent for a time.", "프레더릭은 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["At last he spoke:", "마침내 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Margaret, it is painful to think I can never thank those who have shown you kindness.", "“마거릿, 너에게 친절을 베푼 사람들에게 내가 영영 고맙다는 말을 할 수 없다니 마음이 아프구나."], ["Your acquaintances and mine must be separate.", "네 지인과 내 지인은 따로일 수밖에 없겠지."], ["Unless, indeed, I run the chances of a court-martial, or unless you and my father would come to Spain.”", "내가 군법회의를 무릅쓰지 않는 한, 아니면 너와 아버지가 스페인으로 오시지 않는 한.” 그는 이 마지막 말을 슬쩍 떠보듯 던져 놓고는 불쑥 뛰어들었다."], ["He threw out this last suggestion as a kind of feeler; and then suddenly made the plunge.", "“두 분이 와 주시기를 제가 얼마나 바라는지 모르실 겁니다. 저는 자리도 괜찮고 더 나아질 가망도 있어요.”"], ["“You don’t know how I wish you would.", "그는 처녀처럼 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 이었다."], ["I have a good position—the chance of a better,”", "“마거릿, 내가 말한 그 돌로레스 바버 말이다."], ["continued he, reddening like a girl.", "네가 그 사람을 알기만 하면 좋겠구나. 너도 분명 좋아할 거야."], ["“That Dolores Barbour that I was telling you of, Margaret—I only wish you knew her; I am sure you would like—no, love is the right word, like is so poor—you would love her, father, if you knew her.", "아니, 사랑이라는 말이 맞겠다, 좋아한다는 말은 너무 약하니까. 아버지도 그 사람을 아시면 사랑하실 겁니다."], ["She is not eighteen; but if she is in the same mind another year, she is to be my wife.", "아직 18살도 되지 않았어요. 그렇지만 한 해가 더 지나도 마음이 그대로면 제 아내가 될 거예요."], ["Mr. Barbour won’t let us call it an engagement.", "바버 씨가 아직 약혼이라고는 부르지 못하게 하지만요."], ["But if you would come you would find friends everywhere, besides Dolores.", "그래도 오시기만 하면 돌로레스 말고도 곳곳에서 벗을 만나실 겁니다."], ["Think of it, father.", "생각해 보세요, 아버지."], ["Margaret be on my side.”", "마거릿, 너도 내 편을 들어라.”"]], [["“No—no more removals for me,”", "“아니다. 나는 더는 옮겨 다니지 않겠다.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“One has cost me my wife.", "“한 번 옮긴 것이 내게서 아내를 앗아 갔다."], ["No more removals in this life.", "이 생에서 더 이상의 이사는 없다."], ["She will be here; and here will I stay out my appointed time.”", "그 사람이 여기 있을 테니, 나도 여기서 내게 정해진 때를 다 채우겠다.”"]], [["“Oh, Frederick,”", "“아, 오빠,”"], ["said Margaret, “tell us more about her.", "마거릿이 말했다. “그분 이야기를 더 해 주세요."], ["I never thought of this; but I am so glad.", "이런 일은 생각도 못 했는데, 정말 기뻐요."], ["You will have some one to love and care for you out there.", "저쪽에서 오빠를 사랑하고 보살펴 줄 사람이 생기는 거잖아요."], ["Tell us all about it.”", "다 이야기해 주세요.”"]], [["“In the first place, she is a Roman Catholic.", "“우선, 그 사람은 로마 가톨릭이란다."], ["That’s the only objection I anticipated.", "그게 내가 예상한 유일한 걸림돌이었지."], ["But my father’s change of opinion—nay, Margaret, don’t sigh.”", "하지만 아버지의 생각이 바뀌셨으니……. 아니다, 마거릿, 한숨 쉬지 마라.”"]], [["Margaret had reason to sigh a little more before the conversation ended.", "마거릿은 대화가 끝나기 전에 한숨을 조금 더 쉴 만한 까닭을 얻었다."], ["Frederick himself was Roman Catholic in fact, though not in profession as yet.", "프레더릭 자신이 아직 공표하지는 않았을 뿐 사실상 로마 가톨릭이었던 것이다."], ["This was, then, the reason why his sympathy in her extreme distress at her father’s leaving the church had been so faintly expressed in his letters.", "아버지가 국교회를 떠난 일로 그녀가 극심한 괴로움을 겪을 때 그가 편지에서 그토록 희미하게만 동정을 표했던 까닭이 바로 이것이었다."], ["She had thought it was the carelessness of a sailor; but the truth was, that even then he was himself inclined to give up the form of religion into which had been baptized, only that his opinions were tending in exactly the opposite direction to those of his father.", "그녀는 그것을 뱃사람다운 무심함이라고 여겼었다. 그러나 진실은, 그때 이미 그 자신도 세례를 받은 종교의 형식을 버릴 쪽으로 마음이 기울어 있었다는 것, 다만 그의 견해가 아버지의 그것과는 정반대 방향으로 향하고 있었다는 것이었다."], ["How much love had to do with this change not even Frederick himself could have told.", "이 변화에 사랑이 얼마나 관여했는지는 프레더릭 자신도 말할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Margaret gave up talking about this branch of the subject at last; and, returning to the fact of the engagement, she began to consider it in some fresh light.", "마거릿은 마침내 이 갈래의 이야기는 그만두었다. 그리고 약혼이라는 사실로 돌아와 그것을 새로운 빛 아래에서 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["“But for her sake, Fred, you surely will try and clear yourself of the exaggerated charges brought against you, even if the charge of mutiny itself be true.", "“그렇지만 그분을 위해서라도, 프레드 오빠, 오빠에게 씌워진 부풀려진 혐의만큼은 벗으려고 애써야 하지 않겠어요? 반란이라는 혐의 자체는 사실이라 하더라도요."], ["If there were to be a court-martial, and you could find your witnesses, you might, at any rate, show your disobedience to authority was because that authority was unworthily exercised.”", "군법회의가 열리고 증인을 찾을 수만 있다면, 적어도 오빠가 상관의 명령을 거스른 것은 그 권한이 부당하게 행사되었기 때문이라는 것쯤은 밝힐 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale roused himself up to listen to his son’s answer.", "헤일 씨가 아들의 대답을 들으려고 몸을 일으켰다."]], [["“In the first place, Margaret, who is to hunt up my witnesses?", "“우선, 마거릿, 내 증인들을 누가 찾아다니겠니? 그들은 죄다 뱃사람이고 다른 배로 흩어졌다."], ["All of them are sailors, drafted off to other ships, except those whose evidence would go for very little, as they took part or sympathised in the affair.", "남은 건 그 일에 가담했거나 동조한 자들뿐인데, 그들의 증언은 거의 값이 없지. 다음으로, 이런 말을 해도 될지 모르겠다만, 너는 군법회의라는 것이 어떤 자리인지 모른다."], ["In the next place allow me to tell you, you don’t know what a court-martial is, and consider it as an assembly where justice is administered, instead of what it really is—a court where authority weighs nine-tenths in the balance, and evidence forms only the other tenth.", "정의가 집행되는 자리라고 여기는 모양인데 실은 그렇지 않아. 저울에서 권위가 10분의 9를 차지하고 증거는 나머지 10분의 1밖에 되지 않는 법정이란다."], ["In such cases, evidence itself can hardly escape being influenced by the prestige of authority.”", "그런 자리에서는 증거 자체도 권위의 위세에 휘둘리는 것을 피하기 어렵지.”"]], [["“But is it not worth trying, to see how much evidence might be discovered and arrayed on your behalf?", "“그래도 오빠를 위해 얼마나 많은 증거를 찾아내어 늘어놓을 수 있을지 해 볼 만한 일 아닌가요?"], ["At present, all those who knew you formerly, believe you guilty without any shadow of excuse.", "지금은 예전에 오빠를 알던 사람들 모두가 오빠를 변명의 여지도 없는 죄인으로 여기고 있어요."], ["You have never tried to justify yourself, and we have never known where to seek for proofs of your justification.", "오빠는 한 번도 스스로를 변호하려 하지 않았고, 우리도 오빠의 결백을 밝힐 증거를 어디서 찾아야 할지 알지 못했고요. 이제 바버 양을 위해서라도 오빠의 행동을 세상의 눈앞에 되도록 분명하게 밝히세요."], ["Now, for Miss Barbour’s sake, make your conduct as clear as you can in the eye of the world.", "그분은 그런 것을 개의치 않을지도 몰라요. 우리 모두가 그러하듯 그분도 오빠를 믿고 있을 테니까요."], ["She may not care for it; she has, I am sure, that trust in you that we all have; but you ought not to let her ally herself to one under such a serious charge, without showing the world exactly how it is you stand.", "그렇지만 이렇게 무거운 혐의를 쓴 사람과 인연을 맺게 하면서, 오빠의 처지가 정확히 어떤 것인지 세상에 보이지 않는 것은 도리가 아니에요. 오빠는 상관의 명령을 거슬렀어요."], ["You disobeyed authority—that was bad; but to have stood by, without word or act, while the authority was brutally used, would have been infinitely worse.", "그건 나쁜 일이지요. 그렇지만 그 권한이 잔인하게 휘둘리는 동안 말 한마디 몸짓 하나 없이 지켜보고만 있었다면 그건 비할 데 없이 더 나빴을 거예요."], ["People know what you did; but not the motives that elevate it out of a crime into an heroic protection of the weak.", "사람들은 오빠가 무슨 일을 했는지는 알지만, 그것을 죄가 아니라 약한 이를 지켜 낸 영웅적인 행동으로 끌어올려 주는 그 동기는 모르잖아요."], ["For Dolores’ sake, they ought to know.”", "돌로레스를 위해서라도 그들이 알아야 해요.”"]], [["“But how must I make them know?", "“그렇지만 그들에게 어떻게 알린단 말이냐?"], ["I am not sufficiently sure of the purity and justice of those who would be my judges, to give myself up to a court-martial, even if I could bring a whole array of truth-speaking witnesses.", "나는 나를 재판할 사람들의 결백과 공정을 그다지 믿지 못하니, 진실을 말해 줄 증인을 한 줄로 세울 수 있다 해도 군법회의에 나를 내맡길 수는 없다."], ["I can’t send a bell-man about, to cry aloud and proclaim in the streets what you are pleased to call my heroism.", "네가 좋게도 영웅적인 행동이라고 부르는 것을 거리에서 크게 외치고 다니라고 포고꾼을 보낼 수도 없는 노릇이고."], ["No one would read a pamphlet of self-justification so long after the deed, even if I put one out.”", "일이 있고 나서 이렇게 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤에 자기변호의 소책자를 낸다 한들 읽어 줄 사람도 없을 거다.”"]], [["“Will you consult a lawyer as to your chances of exculpation?”", "“죄를 벗을 가망이 얼마나 되는지 변호사와 상의해 보시겠어요?”"], ["asked Margaret, looking up, and turning very red.", "마거릿이 고개를 들며 얼굴이 몹시 붉어져 물었다."]], [["“I must first catch my lawyer, and have a look at him, and see how I like him, before I make him into my confidant.", "“먼저 그 변호사를 붙잡아 얼굴을 보고 마음에 드는지 살펴본 다음에야 속을 털어놓겠지."], ["Many a briefless barrister might twist his conscience into thinking, that he could earn a hundred pounds very easily by doing a good action—in giving me, a criminal, up to justice.”", "사건 하나 못 맡은 법정 변호사라면 제 양심을 이리저리 비틀어, 나 같은 죄인을 법에 넘기는 착한 일로 100파운드를 손쉽게 벌 수 있다고 여길지도 모르니까.”"]], [["“Nonsense, Frederick!—because I know a lawyer on whose honour I can rely; of whose cleverness in his profession people speak very highly; and who would, I think, take a good deal of trouble for any of—Aunt Shaw’s relations.", "“말도 안 돼요, 오빠!"], ["Mr.", "저는 명예를 믿고 맡길 수 있는 변호사를 알고 있어요."], ["Henry Lennox, papa.”", "사람들이 그 일에 아주 밝다고 입을 모으고, 쇼 이모의 친척이라면 누구를 위해서든 수고를 아끼지 않을 분이에요. 헨리 레녹스 씨예요, 아버지.”"]], [["“I think it is a good idea,”", "“좋은 생각인 것 같구나.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“But don’t propose anything which will detain Frederick in England.", "“하지만 프레더릭을 영국에 붙들어 두는 일은 제안하지 마라."], ["Don’t, for your mother’s sake.”", "네 어머니를 생각해서라도 그러지 마라.”"]], [["“You could go to London by a night-train,”", "“밤 기차로 런던에 가시면 돼요.”"], ["continued Margaret, warming up into her plan.", "마거릿이 자기 계획에 열이 올라 말을 이었다."], ["“He must go to-morrow, I’m afraid, papa,”", "“아무래도 오빠는 내일 떠나야 해요, 아버지.”"], ["said she, tenderly; “we fixed that, because of Mr.", "그녀가 다정하게 말했다."], ["Bell, and Dixon’s disagreeable acquaintance.”", "“벨 씨 일도 있고 딕슨이 만난 그 고약한 사람 일도 있어서 그렇게 정했어요.”"]], [["“Yes; I must go to-morrow,”", "“그래, 나는 내일 가야 한다.”"], ["said Frederick, decidedly.", "프레더릭이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["Mr. Hale groaned.", "헤일 씨가 신음했다."], ["“I can’t bear to part with you, and yet I am miserable with anxiety as long as you stop here.”", "“너를 보내는 것도 견딜 수 없는데, 네가 여기 머무는 동안은 불안해서 비참하구나.”"]], [["“Well then,”", "“그럼 이렇게 해요.”"], ["said Margaret, “listen to my plan.", "마거릿이 말했다. “제 계획을 들어 보세요."], ["He gets to London on Friday morning.", "오빠는 금요일 아침에 런던에 도착해요."], ["I will—you might—no!", "제가, 아니 오빠가, 아니에요!"], ["it would be better to give him a note to Mr. Lennox.", "레녹스 씨에게 보낼 쪽지를 제가 써 드리는 편이 낫겠어요."], ["You will find him at his chambers in the Temple.”", "템플에 있는 그분 사무실로 가시면 만날 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“I will write down a list of all the names I can remember on board the Orion.", "“오라이언호에 탔던 사람들 가운데 기억나는 이름을 다 적어 두마."], ["I could leave it with him to ferret them out.", "그걸 그 사람에게 맡겨 찾아내게 하면 되겠지."], ["He is Edith’s husband’s brother, isn’t he?", "그 사람이 이디스 남편의 동생이지?"], ["I remember your naming him in your letters.", "네 편지에서 이름을 본 기억이 나는구나."], ["I have money in Barbour’s hands. I can pay a pretty long bill, if there’s any chance of success.", "바버 씨에게 맡겨 둔 돈이 있으니 성공할 가망만 있다면 꽤 긴 계산서라도 치를 수 있다."], ["Money, dear father, that I had meant for a different purpose; so I shall only consider it as borrowed from you and Margaret.”", "아버지, 다른 데 쓰려던 돈이니 아버지와 마거릿에게서 빌린 셈만 치겠습니다.”"]], [["“Don’t do that,”", "“그러지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You won’t risk it if you do.", "“그러면 오빠는 위험을 무릅쓰지 않을 테니까요."], ["And it will be as risk; only it is worth trying.", "그리고 이건 위험을 무릅쓰는 일이 될 거예요. 다만 해 볼 만한 일이고요."], ["You can sail from London as well as from Liverpool?”", "리버풀에서만이 아니라 런던에서도 배를 탈 수 있지요?”"]], [["“To be sure, little goose.", "“물론이지, 우리 바보 아가씨."], ["Wherever I feel water heaving under a plank, there I feel at home.", "널빤지 밑에서 물이 출렁이는 것이 느껴지는 곳이면 어디든 내 집 같으니까."], ["I’ll pick up some craft or other to take me off, never fear.", "나를 태워 갈 배야 어떻게든 구할 테니 걱정 마라."], ["I won’t stay twenty-four hours in London, away from you on the one hand, and from somebody else on the other.”", "런던에는 24시간도 머물지 않을 거다. 한쪽으로는 너와 떨어져, 다른 한쪽으로는 또 다른 누군가와 떨어져서 말이야.”"]], [["It was rather a comfort to Margaret that Frederick took it into his head to look over her shoulder as she wrote to Mr. Lennox.", "마거릿이 레녹스 씨에게 편지를 쓰는 동안 프레더릭이 어깨너머로 들여다볼 생각을 한 것은 그녀에게 오히려 다행이었다."], ["If she had not been thus compelled to write steadily and concisely on, she might have hesitated over many a word, and been puzzled to choose between many an expression, in the awkwardness of being the first to resume the intercourse of which the concluding event had been so unpleasant to both sides.", "그렇게 흔들림 없이 간결하게 계속 써 내려가지 않을 수 없었기에 망정이지, 아니었다면 그녀는 숱한 낱말 앞에서 머뭇거렸을 테고 숱한 표현 가운데 무엇을 고를지 몰라 애를 먹었을 것이다. 마지막 일이 양쪽 모두에게 그토록 언짢았던 그 왕래를 먼저 다시 트는 어색함 속에서 말이다."], ["However, the note was taken from her before she had even had time to look it over, and treasured up in a pocket-book, out of which fell a long lock of black hair, the sight of which caused Frederick’s eyes to glow with pleasure.", "그러나 쪽지는 그녀가 다시 훑어볼 겨를도 없이 손에서 건네졌고, 수첩 안에 소중히 간직되었다. 그 수첩에서 검은 머리카락 한 타래가 길게 떨어졌고, 그것을 본 프레더릭의 눈이 기쁨으로 반짝였다."]], [["“Now you would like to see that, wouldn’t you?”", "“자, 이걸 보고 싶겠지?” 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “No!", "“안 된다!"], ["you must wait till you see her herself.", "그 사람을 직접 볼 때까지 기다려야 해."], ["She is too perfect to be known by fragments.", "그 사람은 조각으로 알기에는 너무나 완전하니까."], ["No mean brick shall be a specimen of the building of my palace.”", "내 궁전을 짓는 데는 어떤 볼품없는 벽돌 하나도 본보기가 될 수 없어.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII", "# 제32장"]], [["“What!", "「무엇이라!"], ["remain to be Denounced—dragged, it may be, in chains.”", "남아서 고발당하고, 어쩌면 쇠사슬에 묶인 채 끌려가라는 말인가.」"], ["WERNER.", "— 베르너."]], [["All the next day they sate together—they three. Mr.", "이튿날 온종일 그들은 함께 앉아 있었다 — 세 사람이."], ["Hale hardly ever spoke but when his children asked him questions, and forced him, as it were, into the present.", "헤일 씨는 자식들이 물음을 던져 그를 억지로 현재로 끌어낼 때 말고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않았다. 프레더릭의 슬픔은 이제 보이지도 들리지도 않았다."], ["Frederick’s grief was no more to be seen or heard; the first paroxysm had passed over, and now he was ashamed of having been so battered down by emotion; and though his sorrow for the loss of his mother was a deep real feeling, and would last out his life, it was never to be spoken of again.", "첫 발작은 지나갔고, 이제 그는 감정에 그토록 무너져 내렸던 것이 부끄러웠다. 어머니를 잃은 슬픔은 깊고 참된 것이어서 평생을 갈 것이었지만, 다시는 입에 올리지 않을 것이었다."], ["Margaret, not so passionate at first, was more suffering now.", "마거릿은 처음에는 그만큼 격하지 않았으나 지금은 더 괴로웠다."], ["At times she cried a good deal; and her manner, even when speaking on different things, had a mournful tenderness about it, which was deepened whenever her looks fell upon Frederick, and she thought of his rapidly approaching departure.", "이따금 그녀는 한참을 울었다. 다른 이야기를 할 때조차 그녀의 태도에는 애처로운 다정함이 배어 있었고, 눈길이 프레더릭에게 닿아 머지않아 다가올 그의 떠남을 떠올릴 때면 그것이 한층 짙어졌다."], ["She was glad he was going, on her father’s account, however much she might grieve over it on her own.", "아버지를 생각하면 그가 떠나는 것이 다행이었다. 그녀 자신으로서는 아무리 그것이 서럽더라도 그랬다."], ["The anxious terror in which Mr. Hale lived lest his son should be detected and captured, far outweighed the pleasure he derived from his presence.", "아들이 발각되어 붙잡히지나 않을까 하는 두려움 속에서 헤일 씨가 살고 있던 그 조바심은 아들이 곁에 있는 데서 얻는 기쁨을 훨씬 웃돌았다."], ["The nervousness had increased since Mrs. Hale’s death, probably because he dwelt upon it more exclusively.", "그 초조함은 헤일 부인이 세상을 떠난 뒤로 더해졌는데, 아마도 그가 그 생각만을 골똘히 붙들고 있었기 때문이리라."], ["He started at every unusual sound, and was never comfortable unless Frederick sate out of the immediate view of any one entering the room.", "그는 낯선 소리만 나도 흠칫했고, 프레더릭이 방에 들어오는 사람의 눈에 곧바로 띄지 않는 자리에 앉아 있지 않으면 도무지 마음을 놓지 못했다."], ["Towards evening he said:", "저녁 무렵 그가 말했다."]], [["“You will go with Frederick to the station, Margaret?", "“마거릿, 네가 프레더릭과 함께 역에 가 주겠니?"], ["I shall want to know he is safely off.", "저 애가 무사히 떠나는 것을 알아야겠구나."], ["You will bring me word that he is clear of Milton, at any rate?”", "적어도 저 애가 밀턴을 벗어났다는 소식은 네가 전해 주겠지?”"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론이지요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“I shall like it, if you won’t be lonely without me, papa.”", "“저도 그러고 싶어요. 제가 없어도 아버지가 외롭지 않으시다면요.”"]], [["“No, no!", "“아니다, 아니야!"], ["I should always be fancying some one had known him, and that he had been stopped, unless you could tell me you had seen him off.", "네가 배웅했다고 말해 주지 않으면, 나는 누군가 저 애를 알아보고 붙잡아 세운 것은 아닐까 하는 상상만 하고 있을 게다."], ["And go to the Outwood station.", "그리고 아웃우드 역으로 가거라."], ["It is quite as near, and not so many people about.", "거리도 비슷하고 사람도 그리 많지 않으니."], ["Take a cab there.", "거기까지는 마차를 타고 가려무나."], ["There is less risk of his being seen.", "눈에 띌 위험이 덜하겠지."], ["What time is your train, Fred?”", "기차가 몇 시냐, 프레드?”"]], [["“Ten minutes past six; very nearly dark.", "“6시 10분이에요. 거의 캄캄해질 무렵이지요."], ["So what will you do, Margaret?”", "그러면 마거릿, 너는 어떻게 할 거니?”"]], [["“Oh, I can manage.", "“아, 저는 어떻게든 할 수 있어요."], ["I am getting very brave and very hard.", "요즘은 아주 용감하고 아주 단단해졌는걸요."], ["It is a well-lighted road all the way home, if it should be dark.", "어두워진다 해도 집까지 내내 등불이 환한 길이에요."], ["But I was out last week much later.”", "게다가 지난주에는 이보다 훨씬 늦게까지 밖에 있었는걸요.”"]], [["Margaret was thankful when the parting was over—the parting from the dead mother and the living father.", "마거릿은 작별이 끝났을 때 고마운 마음이 들었다 — 죽은 어머니와의 작별, 그리고 살아 있는 아버지와의 작별이."], ["She hurried Frederick into the cab, in order to shorten a scene which she saw was so bitterly painful to her father, who would accompany his son as he took his last look at his mother.", "그녀는 프레더릭을 서둘러 마차에 태웠다. 아버지에게 그토록 쓰라리게 괴로워 보이는 장면을 조금이라도 줄이기 위해서였다."], ["Partly in consequence of this, and partly owing to one of the very common mistakes in the “Railway Guide”", "아버지는 아들이 어머니를 마지막으로 보러 갈 때 굳이 따라 들어갔던 것이다."], ["as to the times when trains arrive at the smaller stations, they found, on reaching Outwood, that they had nearly twenty minutes to spare.", "얼마쯤은 그 때문에, 또 얼마쯤은 작은 역에 기차가 닿는 시각에 대해 “철도 안내서”에 흔히 있는 오류 가운데 하나 때문에, 두 사람은 아웃우드에 이르러 20분 가까이 시간이 남는다는 것을 알았다."], ["The booking-office was not open, so they could not even take the ticket.", "매표소는 아직 열려 있지 않아 표조차 끊을 수 없었다."], ["They accordingly went down the flight of steps that led to the level of the ground below the railway.", "그래서 그들은 철길 아래 지면으로 이어지는 계단을 따라 내려갔다."], ["There was a broad cinder-path diagonally crossing a field which lay alongside of the carriage-road, and they went there to walk backwards and forwards for the few minutes they had to spare.", "마찻길과 나란히 놓인 들판을 비스듬히 가로지르는 널찍한 잿길이 있었고, 두 사람은 남은 몇 분 동안 그곳을 오락가락 거닐려고 그리로 갔다."]], [["Margaret’s hand lay in Frederick’s arm.", "마거릿의 손이 프레더릭의 팔에 놓여 있었다."], ["He took hold of it affectionately.", "그가 다정하게 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Margaret! I am going to consult Mr.", "“마거릿!"], ["Lennox as to the chance of exculpating myself so that I may return to England whenever I choose, more for your sake than for the sake of anyone else.", "나는 레녹스 씨를 찾아가서 내 혐의를 벗을 가망이 있는지 의논해 볼 참이야. 그래야 내가 원할 때 언제든 영국으로 돌아올 수 있을 테니까."], ["I can’t bear to think of your lonely position if any thing should happen to my father.", "다른 누구보다도 너를 위해서야. 아버지께 무슨 일이라도 생기면 네가 얼마나 외로운 처지가 될지, 생각만 해도 견딜 수가 없구나."], ["He looks sadly changed—terribly shaken.", "아버지는 몰라보게 변하셨어. 몹시 흔들리고 계셔."], ["I wish you could get him to think of the Cadiz plan, for many reasons.", "여러 가지로 보아 아버지가 카디스 계획을 생각해 보시도록 네가 해 드리면 좋겠는데."], ["What could you do if he were taken away?", "아버지가 떠나시고 나면 너는 어떻게 하겠니?"], ["You have no friend near.", "가까이 벗도 없잖아."], ["We are curiously bare of relations.”", "우리 집안은 이상하리만치 친척이 없으니 말이야.”"]], [["Margaret could hardly keep from crying at the tender anxiety with which Frederick was bringing before her an event which she herself felt was not very improbable, so severely had the cares of the last few months told upon Mr.", "프레더릭이 다정한 근심으로 그녀 앞에 그려 보이는 그 일을 마거릿 자신도 그리 있음직하지 않다고 여기지는 않던 터라, 그녀는 눈물을 참기가 어려웠다. 지난 몇 달의 근심이 헤일 씨를 그토록 심하게 갉아먹었던 것이다."], ["Hale. But she tried to rally as she said:", "그러나 그녀는 애써 기운을 차리며 말했다."]], [["“There have been such strange unexpected changes in my life during these last two years, that I feel more than ever that it is not worth while to calculate too closely what I should do if any future event took place.", "“지난 두 해 동안 제 삶에는 참으로 이상하고 뜻밖인 변화들이 있었어요. 그래서 저는 어느 때보다도 이렇게 느껴요."], ["I try to think only upon the present.”", "앞으로 어떤 일이 생기면 어떻게 할까를 너무 꼼꼼히 따져 보는 건 부질없다고요. 저는 오직 지금만 생각하려고 해요.”"], ["She paused; they were standing still for a moment, close on the field side of the stile leading into the road; the setting sun fell on their faces.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 두 사람은 길로 이어지는 층계 담의 들판 쪽 바로 곁에 잠시 가만히 서 있었다."], ["Frederick held her hand in his, and looked with wistful anxiety into her face, reading there more care and trouble than she would betray by words.", "지는 해가 그들의 얼굴에 내렸다. 프레더릭은 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채 애틋한 근심을 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 거기서 그녀가 말로는 드러내지 않을 근심과 시름을 읽어 냈다."], ["She went on:", "그녀가 말을 이었다."]], [["“We shall write often to one another, and I will promise—for I see it will set your mind at ease—to tell you every worry I have. Papa is”—she started a little, a hardly visible start—but Frederick felt the sudden motion of the hand he held, and turned his full face to the road, along which a horseman was slowly riding, just passing the very stile where they stood. Margaret bowed; her bow was stiffly returned.", "“우리 자주 편지해요. 그리고 약속할게요 — 그래야 오빠 마음이 놓일 테니까 — 제 걱정거리를 하나도 빼놓지 않고 다 알려 드릴게요. 아버지는…” 그녀는 흠칫했다. 거의 눈에 띄지 않을 만큼 작은 움찔거림이었지만, 프레더릭은 자기가 쥐고 있던 손의 갑작스러운 움직임을 느끼고 길 쪽으로 얼굴을 온전히 돌렸다. 그 길을 따라 말 탄 사람이 천천히 지나가고 있었다. 마침 두 사람이 서 있는 바로 그 층계 담을 스쳐 지나는 참이었다. 마거릿이 목례했다. 그 인사는 뻣뻣하게 돌아왔다."]], [["“Who is that?”", "“저 사람은 누구야?”"], ["said Frederick, almost before he was out of hearing.", "그가 미처 소리 닿지 않는 곳으로 가기도 전에 프레더릭이 말했다."]], [["Margaret was a little drooping, a little flushed, as she replied: “Mr.", "마거릿은 조금 고개를 떨구고 조금 낯을 붉힌 채 대답했다. “손턴 씨예요."], ["Thornton; you saw him before, you know.”", "오빠도 전에 뵌 적 있잖아요.”"]], [["“Only his back.", "“뒷모습만 봤지."], ["He is an unprepossessing-looking fellow.", "볼품없이 생긴 사람이더구나."], ["What a scowl he has!”", "저 찌푸린 얼굴 좀 봐!”"]], [["“Something has happened to vex him,”", "“무슨 언짢은 일이 있었나 봐요.”"], ["said Margaret apologetically.", "마거릿이 감싸듯 말했다."], ["“You would not have thought him unprepossessing if you had seen him with mamma.”", "“어머니 곁에 있을 때의 그분을 보셨다면 볼품없다고 여기지 않으셨을 거예요.”"]], [["“I fancy it must be time to go and take my ticket.", "“이제 가서 표를 끊어야 할 때가 된 모양이구나."], ["If I had known how dark it would be, we wouldn’t have sent back the cab, Margaret.”", "이렇게 어두워질 줄 알았더라면 마차를 돌려보내지 않았을 텐데, 마거릿.”"]], [["“Oh, don’t fidget about that.", "“아, 그런 건 마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["I can take a cab here, if I like; or go back by the rail-road, when I should have shops and people and lamps all the way from the Milton station-house.", "원하면 여기서 마차를 잡을 수도 있고, 아니면 기차로 돌아가도 돼요. 밀턴 역사에서부터는 내내 가게며 사람이며 등불이 있을 테니까요."], ["Don’t think of me; take care of yourself.", "제 걱정은 마시고 오빠나 조심하세요."], ["I am sick with the thought that Leonards may be in the same train with you.", "레너즈가 오빠와 같은 기차에 타고 있을지도 모른다는 생각만 해도 속이 메스꺼워요."], ["Look well into the carriage before you get in.”", "타기 전에 객실을 잘 들여다보세요.”"]], [["They went back to the station.", "두 사람은 역으로 돌아갔다."], ["Margaret insisted on going into the full light of the flaring gas inside to take the ticket.", "마거릿은 표를 끊으러 가스등이 활활 타오르는 환한 안쪽으로 자기가 들어가겠다고 우겼다."], ["Some idle-looking young men were lounging about with the station-master.", "빈둥거리는 듯한 젊은 사내 몇이 역장 곁에서 어슬렁대고 있었다."], ["Margaret thought she had seen the face of one of them before, and returned him a proud look of offended dignity for his somewhat impertinent stare of undisguised admiration.", "마거릿은 그중 한 사람의 얼굴을 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같았고, 감탄을 감추지도 않은 채 다소 무례하게 빤히 쳐다보는 그에게 모욕당한 이의 도도한 눈길을 돌려주었다."], ["She went hastily to her brother, who was standing outside, and took hold of his arm.", "그녀는 바깥에 서 있던 오빠에게 서둘러 다가가 그의 팔을 붙들었다."], ["“Have you got your bag?", "“가방은 챙기셨어요?"], ["Let us walk about here on the platform,”", "여기 승강장을 좀 걸어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, a little flurried at the idea of so soon being left alone, and her bravery oozing out rather faster than she liked to acknowledge even to herself.", "머지않아 혼자 남게 된다는 생각에 조금 당황했고, 스스로에게조차 인정하고 싶지 않을 만큼 빠르게 용기가 새어 나가고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 포석을 따라 자기들 뒤를 밟는 발소리를 들었다."], ["She heard a step following them along the flags; it stopped when they stopped, looking out along the line and hearing the whizz of the coming train.", "두 사람이 멈추자 그 발소리도 멈추었다. 그들은 선로를 따라 멀리 내다보며 다가오는 기차의 쇳소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["They did not speak; their hearts were too full.", "두 사람은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 가슴이 너무 벅찼던 것이다."], ["Another moment, and the train would be here; a minute more, and he would be gone.", "잠깐이면 기차가 이곳에 닿을 것이고, 다시 1분이면 그는 떠나고 없을 터였다."], ["Margaret almost repented the urgency with which she had entreated him to go to London; it was throwing more chances of detection in his way.", "마거릿은 그에게 런던으로 가라고 그토록 다급히 졸랐던 것을 거의 뉘우쳤다. 그것은 발각될 기회를 그의 앞길에 더 많이 던져 놓는 일이었다."], ["If he had sailed for Spain by Liverpool, he might have been off in two or three hours.", "리버풀에서 스페인으로 가는 배를 탔더라면 두세 시간 안에 떠날 수 있었을 텐데."]], [["Frederick turned round, right facing the lamp, where the gas darted up in vivid anticipation of the train.", "프레더릭이 몸을 돌려 등불을 정면으로 마주 보았다. 기차가 온다는 기별에 가스 불꽃이 생생하게 솟구치던 참이었다."], ["A man in the dress of a railway porter started forward; a bad-looking man, who seemed to have drunk himself into a state of brutality, although his senses were in perfect order.", "철도 짐꾼 차림의 사내 하나가 앞으로 나섰다. 험상궂은 사내였는데, 술을 마셔 사나운 지경에 이른 듯하면서도 정신만은 멀쩡해 보였다."]], [["“By your leave, miss!”", "“실례합니다, 아가씨!”"], ["said he, pushing Margaret rudely on one side, and seizing Frederick by the collar.", "그가 이렇게 말하며 마거릿을 한쪽으로 거칠게 밀치고 프레더릭의 멱살을 붙잡았다."]], [["“Your name is Hale, I believe?”", "“당신 이름이 헤일이지, 아마?”"]], [["In an instant, how, Margaret did not see, for everything danced before her eyes—but by some sleight of wrestling, Frederick had tripped him up, and he fell from the height of three or four feet, which the platform was elevated above the space of soft ground, by the side of the railroad.", "눈앞의 모든 것이 어지럽게 흔들려 마거릿은 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 보지 못했다. 그러나 순식간에, 어떤 씨름 솜씨로 프레더릭이 그의 발을 걸어 넘어뜨렸고, 승강장은 철길 옆 물렁한 땅바닥보다 3, 4피트쯤 높이 솟아 있었는데 그는 그 높이에서 떨어졌다."], ["There he lay.", "그는 그대로 거기 누워 있었다."]], [["“Run, run!” gasped Margaret.", "“어서 뛰어요, 어서요!”"], ["“The train is here.", "마거릿이 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["It was Leonards, was it?", "“기차가 왔어요. 레너즈였죠, 그렇죠?"], ["oh, run!", "아, 어서요!"], ["I will carry your bag.”", "가방은 제가 들게요.”"], ["And she took him by the arm to push him along with all her feeble force.", "그러고는 그의 팔을 붙잡아 가녀린 힘을 다해 그를 밀어 보냈다. 객실 문 하나가 열렸다."], ["A door was opened in a carriage—he jumped in; and as he leant out to say, “God bless you, Margaret!”", "그가 뛰어올랐다. 그리고 그가 몸을 내밀어 “신의 가호가 있기를, 마거릿!”"], ["the train rushed past her; and she was left standing alone.", "하고 말하는 순간 기차가 그녀 옆을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그녀는 홀로 남겨졌다."], ["She was so terribly sick and faint that she was thankful to be able to turn into the ladies’ waiting-room, and sit down for an instant.", "그녀는 지독히 메스껍고 어지러워서 부인 대합실로 들어가 잠깐 앉을 수 있는 것이 고마울 지경이었다. 처음에는 숨을 몰아쉬는 것 말고 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["At first she could do nothing but gasp for breath.", "너무나 황급했고, 너무나 속을 뒤집는 놀라움이었으며, 너무나 아슬아슬했다."], ["It was such a hurry; such a sickening alarm; such a near chance.", "그 순간에 기차가 와 있지 않았더라면 그 사내는 다시 일어나 그를 붙잡으라고 사람들을 불렀을 것이다."], ["If the train had not been there at the moment, the man would have jumped up again and called for assistance to arrest him.", "그녀는 그 사내가 일어났을까 궁금했다. 그가 움직이는 것을 보았는지 떠올려 보려 애썼다."], ["She wondered if the man had got up: she tried to remember if she had seen him move: she wondered if he could have been seriously hurt.", "그가 크게 다쳤을 수도 있을까 궁금했다. 그녀는 큰맘 먹고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["She ventured out; the platform was all alight, but still quite deserted; she went to the end, and looked over, somewhat fearfully.", "승강장은 온통 환했지만 여전히 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 끝까지 가서 다소 겁먹은 채 아래를 내려다보았다."], ["No one was there; and then she was glad she had made herself go, and inspect, for otherwise terrible thoughts would have haunted her dreams.", "아무도 없었다. 그러자 그녀는 스스로를 다그쳐 가서 살펴보기를 잘했다고 여겼다."], ["And even as it was, she was so trembling and affrighted that she felt she could not walk home along the road, which did indeed seem lonely and dark, as she gazed down upon it from the blaze of the station.", "그러지 않았더라면 끔찍한 생각들이 그녀의 꿈자리를 따라다녔을 테니까. 그런데도 그녀는 어찌나 떨리고 겁이 나는지, 길을 따라 집까지 걸어갈 수는 없겠다고 느꼈다."], ["She would wait till the down train passed and take her seat in it.", "역의 불빛 속에서 내려다보는 그 길은 정말이지 외지고 캄캄해 보였다."], ["But what if Leonards recognized her as Frederick’s companion!", "그녀는 하행 열차가 올 때까지 기다렸다가 거기 자리를 잡기로 했다."], ["She peered about, before venturing into the booking-office to take her ticket.", "그러나 레너즈가 그녀를 프레더릭의 일행으로 알아본다면 어쩐단 말인가!"], ["There were only some railway officials standing about; and talking loud to one another.", "그녀는 표를 끊으러 매표소에 들어가기 전에 사방을 살폈다. 철도 직원 몇 사람만이 서서 서로 큰 소리로 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["“So Leonards has been drinking again!”", "“레너즈가 또 술을 마셨다지!”"], ["said one, seemingly in authority.", "윗사람인 듯한 하나가 말했다."], ["“He’ll need all his boasted influence to keep his place this time.”", "“이번에는 자리를 지키려면 그 잘난 뒷배를 죄다 끌어대야 할걸.”"]], [["“Where is he?”", "“그자는 어디 있나?”"], ["asked another, while Margaret, her back towards them, was counting her change with trembling fingers, not daring to turn round until she heard the answer to this question.", "다른 하나가 물었다. 그동안 마거릿은 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 떨리는 손가락으로 거스름돈을 세고 있었고, 이 물음에 대한 답을 들을 때까지는 감히 돌아설 엄두를 내지 못했다."]], [["“I don’t know.", "“몰라. 5분도 채 안 돼서 들어왔지."], ["He came in not five minutes ago, with some long story or other about a fall he’d had, swearing awfully: and wanted to borrow some money from me to go to London by the next up-train.", "넘어졌다나 어쨌다나 하는 이야기를 길게 늘어놓으면서 험하게 욕을 퍼붓더군. 그러더니 다음 상행 열차로 런던에 간다면서 내게 돈을 좀 꾸어 달라지 뭔가."], ["He made all sorts of tipsy promises, but I’d something else to do than listen to him; I told him to go about his business; and he went off at the front door.”", "술김에 온갖 약속을 다 하는데, 나야 그런 소리나 듣고 있을 만큼 한가하지 않아서 네 볼일이나 보라고 했지. 그랬더니 정문으로 나가 버리더군.”"]], [["“He’s at the nearest vaults, I’ll be bound,”", "“가까운 술집에 가 있을 게 뻔하지.”"], ["said the first speaker.", "처음에 말한 이가 말했다."], ["“Your money would have gone there too, if you’d been such a fool as to lend it.”", "“자네가 어리석게 빌려주었더라면 자네 돈도 거기로 갔을 거야.”"]], [["“Catch me!", "“내가 그럴까 봐!"], ["I knew better what his London meant.", "그자가 말하는 런던이 무슨 뜻인지 나는 잘 알지."], ["Why, he has never paid me off that five shillings”—and so they went on.", "아니 글쎄, 그 5실링도 아직 갚지 않았다니까.” 그들의 이야기는 그렇게 이어졌다."]], [["And now all Margaret’s anxiety was for the train to come.", "이제 마거릿의 온 마음은 기차가 오기만을 바라고 있었다."], ["She hid herself once more in the ladies’ waiting-room, and fancied every noise was Leonards’ step—every loud and boisterous voice was his.", "그녀는 다시 한번 부인 대합실에 몸을 숨긴 채, 무슨 소리든 다 레너즈의 발소리로, 크고 왁자한 목소리는 다 그의 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["But no one came near her until the train drew up; when she was civilly helped into a carriage by a porter, into whose face she durst not look till they were in motion, and then she saw that it was not Leonards’.", "그러나 기차가 들어설 때까지 아무도 그녀 곁에 오지 않았다. 그때 짐꾼 하나가 정중하게 그녀를 객실에 태워 주었는데, 그녀는 기차가 움직일 때까지 감히 그의 얼굴을 쳐다보지 못하다가 그제야 그것이 레너즈의 얼굴이 아님을 알았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII", "# 제33장"]], [["“Sleep on, my love, in thy cold bed, Never to be disquieted!", "“잠들어 있으라, 내 사랑이여, 그대의 차가운 잠자리에서, 다시는 어지럽혀지지 않기를!"], ["My last Good Night—thou wilt not wake Till I thy fate shall overtake.”", "나의 마지막 밤 인사 — 그대는 내가 그대의 운명을 뒤따를 때까지 깨어나지 않으리라.”"], ["DR. KING.", "— 킹 박사."]], [["Home seemed unnaturally quiet after all this terror and noisy commotion.", "그 모든 공포와 요란한 소동이 지나간 뒤라 집 안은 부자연스러울 만큼 조용해 보였다."], ["Her father had seen all due preparation made for her refreshment on her return; and then sate down again in his accustomed chair, to fall into one of his sad waking dreams.", "아버지는 돌아온 딸이 요기할 수 있도록 준비를 빠짐없이 시켜 두었고, 그러고는 늘 앉던 의자에 다시 앉아 서글픈 백일몽 가운데 하나에 잠겨 들었다. 딕슨은 부엌에서 메리 히긴스를 붙들고 나무라고 이것저것 시키는 중이었다."], ["Dixon had got Mary Higgins to scold and direct in the kitchen; and her scolding was not the less energetic because it was delivered in an angry whisper; for, speaking above her breath she would have thought irreverent, as long as there was any one lying dead in the house.", "그 잔소리는 성난 속삭임으로 전해졌지만 그렇다고 기세가 덜하지도 않았다. 집 안에 죽은 이가 누워 있는 동안 목소리를 숨결 위로 올리는 것은 불경하다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Margaret had resolved not to mention the crowning and closing affright to her father.", "마거릿은 마지막에 겪은 그 절정의 놀라움을 아버지에게 말하지 않기로 마음먹었다. 이야기해 봐야 소용이 없었다."], ["There was no use in speaking about it; it had ended well; the only thing to be feared was lest Leonards should in some way borrow money enough to effect his purpose of following Frederick to London, and hunting him out there.", "잘 끝난 일이었다. 다만 두려운 것은 레너즈가 어떻게든 돈을 마련해 프레더릭을 뒤따라 런던으로 가서 그를 찾아내려는 뜻을 이루지나 않을까 하는 것뿐이었다."], ["But there were immense chances against the success of any such plan; and Margaret determined not to torment herself by thinking of what she could do nothing to prevent.", "그러나 그런 계획이 성공하지 못할 가능성이 압도적으로 컸다. 마거릿은 자기가 막을 수 없는 일을 생각하며 스스로를 괴롭히지 않기로 결심했다."], ["Frederick would be as much on his guard as she could put him; and in a day or two at most he would be safely out of England.", "프레더릭은 그녀가 일러 줄 수 있는 만큼 경계하고 있을 터였고, 길어야 하루 이틀이면 무사히 잉글랜드를 벗어날 것이었다."]], [["“I suppose we shall hear from Mr. Bell to-morrow,”", "“내일이면 벨 씨한테서 소식이 오겠지요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["replied her father.", "아버지가 대답했다."], ["“I suppose so.”", "“그렇겠지.”"]], [["“If he can come, he will be here to-morrow evening, I should think.”", "“오실 수 있다면 내일 저녁에는 여기 닿으시겠네요.”"]], [["“If he cannot come, I shall ask Mr. Thornton to go with me to the funeral.", "“오시지 못하면 손턴 씨한테 장례에 함께 가 달라고 부탁해야겠구나."], ["I cannot go alone.", "나 혼자서는 못 간다."], ["I should break down utterly.”", "아주 무너져 버릴 게다.”"]], [["“Don’t ask Mr. Thornton, papa.", "“손턴 씨한테 부탁하지 마세요, 아버지."], ["Let me go with you,”", "제가 함께 갈게요.”"], ["said Margaret, impetuously.", "마거릿이 다급하게 말했다."]], [["“You!", "“네가!"], ["My dear, women do not generally go.”", "얘야, 여자들은 보통 가지 않는단다.”"]], [["“No; because they can’t control themselves.", "“가지 않는 건 스스로를 다스리지 못해서예요."], ["Women of our class don’t go, because they have no power over their emotions, and yet are ashamed of showing them.", "우리 같은 계층의 여자들이 가지 않는 건 감정을 억누를 힘이 없으면서도 그것을 드러내는 걸 부끄러워하기 때문이고요."], ["Poor women go, and don’t care if they are seen overwhelmed with grief.", "가난한 여자들은 가요. 슬픔에 무너진 모습을 남이 보아도 개의치 않지요."], ["But I promise you, papa, that if you will let me go, I will be no trouble.", "하지만 약속드릴게요, 아버지. 저를 가게 해 주시면 조금도 폐가 되지 않겠어요."], ["Don’t have a stranger, and leave me out.", "남을 데려가고 저를 빼놓지 마세요."], ["Dear papa!", "아버지!"], ["if Mr. Bell cannot come, I shall go.", "벨 씨가 오시지 못하면 제가 가겠어요."], ["I won’t urge my wish against your will, if he does.”", "오신다면 아버지 뜻을 거슬러 제 바람을 내세우지 않을게요.”"]], [["Mr. Bell could not come.", "벨 씨는 올 수 없었다."], ["He had the gout.", "통풍이 도졌던 것이다."], ["It was a most affectionate letter, and expressed great and true regret for his inability to attend.", "더없이 애정 어린 편지였고, 참석하지 못하는 데 대한 크고 진심 어린 아쉬움을 담고 있었다. 그는 받아 준다면 곧 찾아가 머물다 오고 싶다고 했다."], ["He hoped to come and pay them a visit soon, if they would have him; his Milton property required some looking after, and his agent had written to him to say that his presence was absolutely necessary; or else he had avoided coming near Milton as long as he could, and now the only thing that would reconcile him to this necessary visit was the idea that he should see, and might possibly be able to comfort his old friend.", "밀턴의 소유지를 좀 돌보아야 하는데 대리인이 편지를 보내 그가 반드시 와야 한다고 알려 왔다는 것이었다. 그렇지 않았다면 그는 될 수 있는 한 오래 밀턴 근처에 가지 않았을 것이고, 이제 이 피할 수 없는 걸음을 그나마 받아들일 만하게 해 주는 것은 오랜 벗을 만나 어쩌면 위로할 수 있을지 모른다는 생각뿐이라고 했다."]], [["Margaret had all the difficulty in the world to persuade her father not to invite Mr.", "마거릿은 손턴 씨를 부르지 말자고 아버지를 설득하느라 세상에 없을 애를 먹었다."], ["Thornton. She had an indescribable repugnance to this step being taken.", "그녀는 그런 조치를 취한다는 데 말로 다 할 수 없는 거부감을 느꼈다."], ["The night before the funeral, came a stately note from Mrs.", "장례 전날 밤, 손턴 부인에게서 헤일 양 앞으로 격식 차린 쪽지가 왔다."], ["Thornton to Miss Hale, saying that, at her son’s desire, their carriage should attend the funeral, if it would not be disagreeable to the family.", "아들의 뜻에 따라, 유족에게 불쾌하지 않다면 자기네 마차를 장례에 보내겠다는 내용이었다."], ["Margaret tossed the note to her father.", "마거릿은 그 쪽지를 아버지에게 던지듯 건넸다."]], [["“Oh, don’t let us have these forms,”", "“아, 이런 형식은 그만두어요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Let us go alone—you and me, papa.", "“우리끼리 가요, 아버지와 저 둘이서요."], ["They don’t care for us, or else he would have offered to go himself, and not have proposed this sending an empty carriage.”", "저 사람들은 우리를 마음에 두지 않아요. 그랬다면 손턴 씨가 직접 가겠다고 했을 테고, 빈 마차를 보내겠다는 이런 말을 꺼내지도 않았을 거예요.”"]], [["“I thought you were so extremely averse to his going, Margaret,”", "“나는 네가 그분이 오시는 걸 그토록 몹시 꺼리는 줄 알았는데, 마거릿.”"], ["said Mr. Hale in some surprise.", "헤일 씨가 다소 놀라서 말했다."]], [["“And so I am.", "“꺼려요."], ["I don’t want him to come at all; and I should especially dislike the idea of our asking him.", "그분이 오시는 건 조금도 바라지 않고, 우리가 부탁한다는 생각은 특히나 싫어요. 하지만 이건 애도를 이렇게 우롱하는 것 같아서, 그분에게서 이런 걸 기대하지는 않았어요.”"], ["But this seems such a mockery of mourning that I did not expect it from him.”", "그러고는 갑자기 울음을 터뜨려 아버지를 놀라게 했다. 그녀는 슬픔 속에서도 그토록 자신을 눌러 왔고, 남을 그토록 헤아렸으며, 모든 일에 그토록 온순하고 참을성이 있었기에, 아버지는 그날 밤 그녀의 조급한 태도를 이해할 수 없었다."], ["She startled her father by bursting into tears.", "그녀는 동요하고 안절부절못하는 듯 보였다."], ["She had been so subdued in her grief, so thoughtful for others, so gentle and patient in all things, that he could not understand her impatient ways to-night; she seemed agitated and restless; and at all the tenderness which her father in his turn now lavished upon her, she only cried the more.", "이번에는 아버지가 그녀에게 온갖 다정함을 쏟아부었지만, 그럴수록 그녀는 더 울기만 했다."]], [["She passed so bad a night that she was ill prepared for the additional anxiety caused by a letter received from Frederick. Mr.", "밤을 어찌나 나쁘게 보냈던지, 그녀는 프레더릭에게서 온 편지가 안겨 준 새로운 근심을 감당할 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["Lennox was out of town; his clerk said that he would return by the following Tuesday at the latest; that he might possibly be home on Monday.", "레녹스 씨는 런던을 떠나 있었다. 사무원 말로는 늦어도 다음 화요일까지는 돌아올 것이고, 어쩌면 월요일에 올 수도 있다고 했다."], ["Consequently, after some consideration, Frederick had determined upon remaining in London a day or two longer.", "그래서 프레더릭은 얼마간 궁리한 끝에 하루 이틀 더 런던에 머물기로 정했다는 것이었다."], ["He had thought of coming down to Milton again; the temptation had been very strong; but the idea of Mr. Bell domesticated in his father’s house, and the alarm he had received at the last moment at the railway station, had made him resolve to stay in London.", "다시 밀턴으로 내려갈까 생각도 했고 그 유혹이 무척 강했지만, 벨 씨가 아버지 집에 묵고 있으리라는 생각과 마지막 순간 기차역에서 겪은 놀라움 때문에 런던에 남기로 마음먹었다고 했다."], ["Margaret might be assured he would take every precaution against being tracked by Leonards.", "레너즈에게 뒤를 밟히지 않도록 온갖 조심을 다할 테니 마거릿은 안심해도 좋다고 했다."], ["Margaret was thankful that she received this letter while her father was absent in her mother’s room.", "마거릿은 아버지가 어머니 방에 가 있는 동안 이 편지를 받은 것이 고마웠다."], ["If he had been present, he would have expected her to read it aloud to him, and it would have raised in him a state of nervous alarm which she would have found it impossible to soothe away.", "아버지가 옆에 있었다면 소리 내어 읽어 주기를 바랐을 테고, 그러면 그녀로서는 도저히 달랠 수 없는 신경질적인 불안에 빠졌을 것이다. 그녀를 몹시 뒤흔든 프레더릭의 런던 체류라는 사실만이 아니었다."], ["There was not merely the fact, which disturbed her excessively, of Frederick’s detention in London, but there were allusions to the recognition at the last moment at Milton, and the possibility of a pursuit, which made her blood run cold; and how then would it have affected her father?", "밀턴에서 마지막 순간에 알아보인 일과 추적 가능성에 대한 언급들이 있었고, 그것은 그녀의 피를 얼어붙게 했다. 하물며 아버지에게는 어떤 영향을 미쳤겠는가?"], ["Many a time did Margaret repent of having suggested and urged on the plan of consulting Mr. Lennox.", "마거릿은 레녹스 씨와 상의하자는 계획을 자기가 꺼내고 밀어붙인 것을 몇 번이고 뉘우쳤다."], ["At the moment, it had seemed as if it would occasion so little delay—add so little to the apparently small chances of detection; and yet everything that had since occurred had tended to make it so undesirable.", "그때는 지체될 일도 거의 없고, 얼마 되지 않아 보이던 발각의 위험을 더할 것도 거의 없어 보였다. 그런데 그 뒤로 벌어진 모든 일이 그 계획을 그토록 바람직하지 않은 것으로 만들어 놓았다."], ["Margaret battled hard against this regret of hers for what could not now be helped; this self-reproach for having said what at the time appeared to be wise, but which after events were proving to have been so foolish.", "마거릿은 이제 와서 어쩔 수 없는 일에 대한 이 후회와, 그때는 지혜로워 보였으나 나중 일이 어리석었음을 증명해 가고 있는 그 말을 했다는 자책과 힘껏 싸웠다."], ["But her father was in too depressed a state of mind and body to struggle healthily; he would succumb to all these causes for morbid regret over what could not be recalled.", "그러나 아버지는 정신도 몸도 너무 가라앉아 건강하게 싸울 수 없었다. 그는 돌이킬 수 없는 일에 대한 병적인 후회의 온갖 빌미에 굴복하고 말 터였다."], ["Margaret summoned up all her forces to her aid.", "마거릿은 있는 힘을 모두 그러모아 스스로를 도왔다."], ["Her father seemed to have forgotten that they had any reason to expect a letter from Frederick that morning.", "아버지는 그날 아침 프레더릭에게서 편지가 올 만한 이유가 있었다는 것조차 잊은 듯했다."], ["He was absorbed in one idea—that the last visible token of the presence of his wife was to be carried away from him, and hidden from his sight.", "그는 한 가지 생각에 사로잡혀 있었다. 아내가 곁에 있었다는 마지막 눈에 보이는 표적이 자기에게서 실려 나가 눈앞에서 감추어지리라는 생각이었다."], ["He trembled pitifully as the undertaker’s man was arranging his crape draperies around him.", "장의사 일꾼이 그의 몸에 검은 상장(喪章) 천을 둘러 주는 동안 그는 가엾도록 떨었다."], ["He looked wistfully at Margaret; and, when released, he tottered towards her, murmuring, “Pray for me, Margaret.", "그는 애타는 눈으로 마거릿을 바라보았고, 몸이 풀려나자 비틀거리며 그녀에게 다가와 중얼거렸다. “나를 위해 기도해 다오, 마거릿."], ["I have no strength left in me.", "내게는 힘이 남아 있지 않구나."], ["I cannot pray.", "기도를 할 수가 없다."], ["I give her up because I must.", "어쩔 수 없으니 그이를 내어 놓는다."], ["I try to bear it: indeed I do.", "견디려 애쓰고 있어. 정말 그러고 있단다."], ["I know it is God’s will.", "하느님의 뜻인 줄도 안다."], ["But I cannot see why she died.", "그러나 왜 그이가 죽어야 했는지는 알 수가 없구나."], ["Pray for me, Margaret, that I may have faith to pray.", "나를 위해 기도해 다오, 마거릿. 기도할 믿음을 갖게 되도록."], ["It is a great strait, my child.”", "참으로 험한 고비로구나, 얘야.”"]], [["Margaret sat by him in the coach, almost supporting him in her arms; and repeating all the noble verses of holy comfort, or texts expressive of faithful resignation, that she could remember.", "마거릿은 마차 안에서 아버지 곁에 앉아 거의 두 팔로 그를 떠받치다시피 하며, 기억나는 대로 거룩한 위로가 담긴 고귀한 구절들과 믿음으로 순종함을 나타내는 성구들을 하나하나 읊었다."], ["Her voice never faltered; and she herself gained strength by doing this.", "그녀의 목소리는 한 번도 흔들리지 않았고, 그렇게 하는 동안 그녀 자신도 힘을 얻었다."], ["Her father’s lips moved after her, repeating the well-known texts as her words suggested them; it was terrible to see the patient struggling effort to obtain the resignation which he had not strength to take into his heart as part of himself.", "아버지의 입술은 그녀를 따라 움직이며 그녀의 말이 일깨워 주는 대로 낯익은 성구들을 되뇌었다. 스스로의 일부로 마음에 받아들일 힘이 없는 그 순종을 얻어 보려고 참을성 있게 애쓰는 그 몸부림을 지켜보는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다."]], [["Margaret’s fortitude nearly gave way as Dixon, with a slight motion of her hand, directed her notice to Nicholas Higgins and his daughter, standing a little aloof, but deeply attentive to the ceremonial.", "딕슨이 손을 살짝 움직여 니컬러스 히긴스와 그의 딸을 눈짓해 보였을 때, 마거릿의 굳센 마음은 하마터면 무너질 뻔했다. 두 사람은 조금 떨어져 서 있었지만 예식을 깊이 눈여겨보고 있었다."], ["Nicholas wore his usual fustian clothes, but had a bit of black stuff sown round his hat—a mark of mourning which he had never shown to his daughter Bessy’s memory.", "니컬러스는 늘 입는 퍼스티언 옷차림이었으나 모자 둘레에 검은 천 조각을 꿰매어 달고 있었다. 제 딸 베시를 기릴 때도 한 번 보인 적이 없는 애도의 표시였다."], ["But Mr.", "그러나 헤일 씨는 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["Hale saw nothing.", "그는 집전하는 목사가 읽어 나가는 장례 예식을 마치 기계처럼 혼자 따라 되뇌기만 했다."], ["He went on repeating to himself, mechanically as it were, all the funeral service as it was read by the officiating clergyman; he sighed twice or thrice when all was ended; and then, putting his hand on Margaret’s arm, he mutely entreated to be led away, as if he were blind, and she his faithful guide.", "모든 것이 끝나자 그는 두세 번 한숨을 쉬었고, 그러고는 마거릿의 팔에 손을 얹고 자기를 데려가 달라고 말없이 청했다. 마치 자기는 눈먼 사람이고 그녀는 그의 충실한 길잡이라는 듯이."]], [["Dixon sobbed aloud; she covered her face with her handkerchief, and was so absorbed in her own grief that she did not perceive that the crowd, attracted on such occasions, was dispersing, till she was spoken to by some one close at hand.", "딕슨은 소리 내어 흐느꼈다. 그녀는 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 자기 슬픔에 너무 빠져 있어서, 그런 자리면 으레 몰려드는 구경꾼들이 흩어지고 있는 것도 알아차리지 못했다."], ["It was Mr.", "바로 곁에서 누군가 말을 걸고 나서야 알았다."], ["Thornton.", "손턴 씨였다."], ["He had been present all the time, standing, with bent head, behind a group of people, so that, in fact, no one had recognised him.", "그는 내내 그 자리에 있었다. 사람들 무리 뒤에 고개를 숙이고 서 있었던 터라 사실 아무도 그를 알아보지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,—but, can you tell me how Mr. Hale is?", "“실례합니다만, 헤일 씨는 어떠신지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?"], ["And Miss Hale, too?", "헤일 양도요."], ["I should like to know how they both are.”", "두 분 다 어떠신지 알고 싶습니다.”"]], [["“Of course, sir.", "“그럼요, 나리."], ["They are much as is to be expected.", "짐작하시는 그대로지요."], ["Master is terribly broke down.", "주인어른은 아주 무너지셨습니다요."], ["Miss Hale bears up better than likely.”", "헤일 아가씨는 뜻밖에도 잘 견디시고요.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton would rather have heard that she was suffering the natural sorrow.", "손턴 씨는 그녀가 마땅한 슬픔에 젖어 있다는 말을 듣는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["In the first place, there was selfishness enough in him to have taken pleasure in the idea that his great love might come in to comfort and console her; much the same kind of passionate pleasure which comes stinging through a mother’s heart, when her drooping infant nestles close to her, and is dependent upon her for everything.", "우선 그의 안에는 자기의 크나큰 사랑이 그녀를 달래고 위로해 줄 수 있으리라는 생각에서 기쁨을 얻을 만큼의 이기심이 있었다. 시들시들한 젖먹이가 제 어미에게 바싹 파고들어 모든 것을 어미에게 기댈 때, 어미의 가슴을 찌르며 밀려드는 그 격렬한 기쁨과 거의 같은 종류의 것이었다."], ["But this delicious vision of what might have been—in which, in spite of all Margaret’s repulse, he would have indulged only a few days ago—was miserably disturbed by the recollection of what he had seen near the Outwood station.", "그러나 있었을 수도 있었을 일에 대한 이 달콤한 환상은 — 마거릿에게 그토록 거절당하고서도 며칠 전만 해도 그가 기꺼이 빠져들었을 환상은 — 아웃우드 역 근처에서 본 것에 대한 기억으로 비참하게 흐트러졌다."], ["“Miserably disturbed!” that is not strong enough.", "“비참하게 흐트러졌다”는 말로는 부족하다."], ["He was haunted by the remembrance of the handsome young man, with whom she stood in an attitude of such familiar confidence; and the remembrance shot through him like an agony, till it made him clench his hands tight in order to subdue the pain.", "그토록 스스럼없는 신뢰의 몸짓으로 그녀와 나란히 서 있던 그 잘생긴 젊은이의 기억이 그를 따라다녔고, 그 기억은 고통처럼 그를 꿰뚫어, 그는 아픔을 억누르려고 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐어야 했다."], ["At that late hour, so far from home!", "그 늦은 시각에, 집에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서!"], ["It took a great moral effort to galvanise his trust—erewhile so perfect—in Margaret’s pure and exquisite maidenliness into life; as soon as the effort ceased, his trust dropped down dead and powerless: and all sorts of wild fancies chased each other like dreams, through his mind.", "마거릿의 순결하고 빼어난 처녀다움에 대해 얼마 전까지 그토록 완전했던 자신의 믿음을 되살려 내려면 엄청난 도덕적 노력이 필요했다. 그 노력을 멈추는 순간 그의 믿음은 죽은 듯 무력하게 주저앉았다."], ["Here was a little bit of miserable, gnawing confirmation.", "그리고 온갖 걷잡을 수 없는 상상이 꿈처럼 그의 머릿속을 앞서거니 뒤서거니 스쳐 갔다. 여기 비참하게 속을 갉아먹는 자그마한 확증이 있었다."], ["“She bore up better than likely”", "그녀가 이 슬픔 속에서 “뜻밖에도 잘 견디고”"], ["under this grief.", "있다는 것."], ["She had then some hope to look to, so bright that even in her affectionate nature it could come in to lighten the dark hours of a daughter newly made motherless.", "그렇다면 그녀에게는 바라볼 무슨 희망이 있는 것이었다. 어찌나 밝은 희망이었으면, 정 많은 그녀의 천성에서조차 갓 어머니를 잃은 딸의 어두운 시간을 밝혀 줄 만하겠는가."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["he knew how she would love.", "그는 그녀가 어떻게 사랑할지 알고 있었다."], ["He had not loved her without gaining that instinctive knowledge of what capabilities were in her.", "그녀 안에 어떤 힘이 깃들어 있는지 본능적으로 알아채지 못한 채 그녀를 사랑했던 것이 아니었다."], ["Her soul would walk in glorious sunlight if any man was worthy, by his power of loving, to win back her love.", "사랑하는 힘으로 그녀의 사랑을 얻어 낼 만한 자격을 갖춘 사내가 있다면, 그녀의 영혼은 눈부신 햇빛 속을 거닐 것이었다."], ["Even in her mourning she would rest with a peaceful faith upon his sympathy.", "애도 중에도 그녀는 그의 공감에 평온한 믿음을 얹고 쉴 것이었다."], ["His sympathy!", "그의 공감이라니!"], ["Whose?", "누구의?"], ["That other man’s.", "그 다른 사내의 것이지."], ["And that it was another was enough to make Mr. Thornton’s pale grave face grow doubly wan and stern at Dixon’s answer.", "그리고 그것이 다른 사내의 것이라는 사실만으로도, 딕슨의 대답을 들은 손턴 씨의 창백하고 굳은 얼굴은 갑절로 핏기를 잃고 험악해지기에 충분했다."]], [["“I suppose I may call,”", "“찾아뵈어도 되겠지요.”"], ["said he coldly.", "그가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“On Mr. Hale, I mean.", "“헤일 씨를 말입니다."], ["He will perhaps admit me after to-morrow or so.”", "내일쯤 지나면 아마 저를 들여 주시겠지요.”"]], [["He spoke as if the answer were a matter of indifference to him.", "그는 마치 그 대답이 자기에게는 아무래도 상관없다는 투로 말했다."], ["But it was not so.", "그러나 그렇지 않았다."], ["For all his pain, he longed to see the author of it.", "그 모든 고통에도 불구하고 그는 그 고통을 지어낸 사람을 보고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Although he hated Margaret at times, when he thought of that gentle, familiar attitude and all the attendant circumstances, he had a restless desire to renew her picture in his mind—a longing for the very atmosphere she breathed.", "그 다정하고 스스럼없던 몸짓과 거기에 딸린 온갖 정황을 떠올릴 때면 이따금 마거릿을 미워하기도 했지만, 그는 그녀의 모습을 마음속에 새로 그려 보고 싶은 안절부절못하는 갈망을, 그녀가 숨 쉬는 바로 그 공기에 대한 그리움을 품고 있었다."], ["He was in the Charybdis of passion, and must perforce circle and circle ever nearer round the fatal centre.", "그는 정념의 카리브디스에 빠져 있었고, 어쩔 수 없이 그 치명적인 중심 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 자꾸만 가까이 다가갈 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“I dare say, sir, master will see you.", "“아무렴요, 나리. 주인어른께서 만나 주실 겁니다요."], ["He was very sorry to have to deny you the other day; but circumstances was not agreeable just then.”", "지난번에 나리를 물리치실 수밖에 없었던 걸 아주 미안해하셨어요. 다만 그때는 사정이 좋질 못했지요.”"]], [["For some reason or other, Dixon never named this interview that she had had with Mr. Thornton to Margaret.", "무슨 까닭에서인지 딕슨은 손턴 씨와 나눈 이 대화를 마거릿에게 한 번도 이야기하지 않았다."], ["It might have been mere chance, but so it was that Margaret never heard that he had attended her poor mother’s funeral.", "그저 우연이었을 수도 있으나, 어쨌든 마거릿은 그가 가엾은 어머니의 장례에 참석했다는 것을 끝내 듣지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV", "# 제34장"]], [["“Truth will fail thee never, never!", "“진리는 결코, 결코 그대를 저버리지 않으리!"], ["Though thy bark be tempest-driven, Though each plank be rent and riven, Truth will bear thee on for ever!”", "그대의 배가 폭풍에 떠밀려 가도, 널빤지마다 찢기고 갈라져도, 진리는 그대를 영원히 실어 가리라!”"], ["ANON.", "— 작자 미상."]], [["The “bearing up better than likely” was a terrible strain upon Margaret.", "“생각보다 잘 견디고 있다”는 그 말은 마거릿에게 끔찍한 부담이었다."], ["Sometimes she thought she must give way, and cry out with pain, as the sudden sharp thought came across her, even during her apparently cheerful conversations with her father, that she had no longer a mother.", "때때로 그녀는 그만 무너져 고통에 소리치고 말 것 같았다. 아버지와 겉으로는 명랑하게 이야기를 나누는 중에도, 이제 자기에게 어머니가 없다는 생각이 문득 날카롭게 스치고 지나갔던 것이다."], ["About Frederick, too, there was great uneasiness.", "프레더릭에 대해서도 큰 불안이 있었다."], ["The Sunday post intervened, and interfered with their London letters; and on Tuesday Margaret was surprised and disheartened to find that there was still no letter.", "일요일 우편이 끼어드는 바람에 런던에서 오는 편지가 늦어졌고, 화요일이 되어서도 여전히 편지가 없다는 것을 알고 마거릿은 놀랍고도 낙담했다."], ["She was quite in the dark as to his plans, and her father was miserable at all this uncertainty.", "그녀는 오빠의 계획에 대해 아무것도 알지 못했고, 아버지는 이 모든 불확실함에 비참해했다."], ["It broke in upon his lately acquired habit of sitting still in one easy chair for half a day together.", "그것은 한나절 내내 안락의자 하나에 가만히 앉아 있는, 근래에 몸에 밴 그의 습관마저 깨뜨려 놓았다."], ["He kept pacing up and down the room; then out of it; and she heard him upon the landing opening and shutting the bed-room doors, without any apparent object.", "그는 방 안을 이리저리 서성이다가 밖으로 나갔고, 계단참에서 아무 뚜렷한 목적도 없이 침실 문들을 여닫는 소리가 그녀의 귀에 들려왔다."], ["She tried to tranquillise him by reading aloud; but it was evident he could not listen for long together.", "그녀는 소리 내어 책을 읽어 아버지를 가라앉히려 했지만, 그가 오래 귀를 기울이지 못한다는 것이 분명했다."], ["How thankful she was then, that she had kept to herself the additional cause for anxiety", "그러니 걱정거리 하나를 더 자기 혼자만 간직해 둔 것을 그때 그녀가 얼마나 다행으로 여겼던가."]], [["Thornton announced.", "그때 손턴 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들려왔다."], ["His visit would force her father’s thoughts into another channel.", "그의 방문은 아버지의 생각을 다른 물길로 돌려놓을 터였다."]], [["He came up straight to her father, whose hands he took and wrung without a word—holding them in his for a minute or two, during which time his face, his eyes, his look, told of more sympathy than could be put into words.", "그는 곧장 그녀의 아버지에게 다가가 말없이 두 손을 잡고 꼭 쥐었다. 한두 분 동안 그 손을 자기 손 안에 쥐고 있는 사이, 그의 얼굴과 눈과 그 눈길이 말로는 담을 수 없는 깊은 위로를 전했다."], ["Then he turned to Margaret.", "그러고서 그는 마거릿에게로 돌아섰다."], ["Not “better than likely”", "그녀는 “생각보다 잘”"], ["did she look.", "견디고 있는 얼굴이 아니었다."], ["Her stately beauty was dimmed with much watching and with many tears.", "그 당당한 아름다움은 오랜 간병과 수많은 눈물로 빛이 바래 있었다."], ["The expression on her countenance was of gentle patient sadness—nay of positive present suffering.", "얼굴에 어린 표정은 온순하게 견디는 슬픔이었다. 아니, 지금 이 순간에도 또렷한 괴로움이었다."], ["He had not meant to greet her otherwise than with his late studied coldness of demeanour; but he could not help going up to her, as she stood a little aside, rendered timid by the uncertainty of his manner of late, and saying the few necessary common-place words in so tender a voice, that her eyes filled with tears, and she turned away to hide her emotion.", "그는 요즈음 애써 지켜 온 차가운 태도 말고 달리 그녀를 맞을 생각이 없었다. 그러나 근래 그의 태도를 종잡을 수 없어 주눅이 든 채 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서 있는 그녀에게 다가가지 않을 수 없었고, 꼭 필요한 몇 마디 상투적인 인사말을 어찌나 부드러운 목소리로 건넸던지 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 차올랐고 그녀는 감정을 감추려 몸을 돌렸다."], ["She took her work and sate down very quiet and silent. Mr.", "그녀는 바느질감을 집어 들고 아주 조용히, 말없이 앉았다."], ["Thornton’s heart beat quick and strong, and for the time he utterly forgot the Outwood lane. He tried to talk to Mr.", "손턴 씨의 심장은 빠르고 세차게 뛰었고, 그 순간만큼은 그는 아웃우드 오솔길을 까맣게 잊었다."], ["Hale: and—his presence always a certain kind of pleasure to Mr.", "그는 헤일 씨와 이야기를 나누려 애썼다."], ["Hale, as his power and decision made him, and his opinions, a safe, sure port—was unusually agreeable to her father, as Margaret saw.", "그의 존재는 늘 헤일 씨에게 어떤 즐거움이었는데, 그의 힘과 결단력이 그와 그의 견해를 안전하고 확실한 항구로 만들어 주었기 때문이다. 마거릿이 보기에 그날 그는 여느 때보다도 아버지의 마음에 드는 듯했다."]], [["Presently Dixon came to the door and said, “Miss Hale, you are wanted.”", "이윽고 딕슨이 문간으로 와서 말했다. “헤일 아가씨, 좀 나와 보셔야겠습니다.”"]], [["Dixon’s manner was so flurried that Margaret turned sick at heart.", "딕슨의 태도가 어찌나 허둥대던지 마거릿은 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다."], ["Something had happened to Fred. She had no doubt of that.", "프레드에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것이다."], ["It was well that her father and Mr.", "그 점에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["Thornton were so much occupied by their conversation.", "아버지와 손턴 씨가 대화에 그토록 몰두해 있는 것이 다행이었다."]], [["“What is it, Dixon?”", "“무슨 일이에요, 딕슨?”"], ["asked Margaret, the moment she had shut the drawing-room door.", "응접실 문을 닫자마자 마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Come this way, miss,”", "“이쪽으로 오세요, 아가씨.”"], ["said Dixon, opening the door of what had been Mrs.", "딕슨은 헤일 부인의 침실이었다가 이제는 마거릿의 방이 된 곳의 문을 열며 말했다."], ["Hale’s bed-chamber, now Margaret’s, for her father refused to sleep there again after his wife’s death.", "헤일 씨가 아내를 잃은 뒤로 다시는 그 방에서 자기를 마다했기 때문이었다. “아무 일도 아닙니다요, 아가씨.”"], ["“It’s nothing, miss,”", "딕슨이 살짝 목이 메어 말했다."], ["said Dixon, choking a little. “Only a police-inspector.", "“그저 경찰 조사관이 하나 왔을 뿐이에요."], ["He wants to see you, miss.", "아가씨를 뵙자고 합니다."], ["But I dare say, it’s about nothing at all.”", "하지만 아마 아무것도 아닐 거예요.”"]], [["“Did he name—”", "“그 사람이 이름을 대던가요……”"], ["asked Margaret, almost inaudibly.", "마거릿이 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 소리로 물었다."]], [["“No, miss; he named nothing.", "“아니요, 아가씨. 아무 이름도 대지 않았어요."], ["He only asked if you lived here, and if he could speak to you.", "그저 아가씨가 여기 사시는지, 말씀을 나눌 수 있는지만 묻더군요."], ["Martha went to the door, and let him in; she has shown him into master’s study.", "마사가 문으로 나가서 들여보냈고, 주인어른 서재로 안내했습니다."], ["I went to him myself, to try if that would do; but no—it’s you, miss, he wants.”", "제가 직접 가서 저로 되겠느냐고 해 보았지만 안 된다더군요. 아가씨라야 한답니다.”"]], [["Margaret did not speak again till her hand was on the lock of the study door.", "마거릿은 서재 문의 손잡이에 손을 얹을 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다."], ["Here she turned round and said, “Take care papa does not come down.", "여기서 그녀는 돌아서서 말했다. “아버지가 내려오시지 않게 잘 봐요."], ["Mr. Thornton is with him now.”", "지금 손턴 씨가 함께 계시니까.”"]], [["The inspector was almost daunted by the haughtiness of her manner as she entered.", "그녀가 들어설 때의 그 오만한 태도에 조사관은 거의 기가 죽었다."], ["There was something of indignation expressed in her countenance, but so kept down and controlled, that it gave her a superb air of disdain.", "얼굴에는 분노 같은 것이 드러나 있었으나 어찌나 눌리고 다스려져 있던지, 그것이 그녀에게 도도한 경멸의 기품을 주었다."], ["There was no surprise, no curiosity.", "놀라움도 호기심도 없었다."], ["She stood awaiting the opening of his business there.", "그녀는 그가 용건을 꺼내기를 기다리며 서 있었다."], ["Not a question did she ask.", "한마디도 묻지 않았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, ma’am, but my duty obliges me to ask you a few plain questions.", "“실례합니다, 부인. 하오나 직무상 몇 가지 간단한 질문을 드려야겠습니다."], ["A man has died at the Infirmary, in consequence of a fall, received at Outwood station, between the hours of five and six on Thursday evening, the twenty-sixth instant.", "이달 26일 목요일 저녁 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이에 아웃우드 역에서 넘어진 사람이 그 일로 병원에서 죽었습니다."], ["At the time, this fall did not seem of much consequence; but it was rendered fatal, the doctors say, by the presence of some internal complaint, and the man’s own habit of drinking.”", "당시에는 그 낙상이 그리 대수롭지 않아 보였습니다만, 의사들 말로는 어떤 내장의 병과 그자 자신의 술버릇 탓에 목숨을 잃게 되었다고 합니다.”"]], [["The large dark eyes, gazing straight into the inspector’s face, dilated a little.", "조사관의 얼굴을 똑바로 응시하던 크고 검은 눈이 조금 커졌다."], ["Otherwise there was no motion perceptible to his experienced observation.", "그 밖에는 노련한 그의 관찰로도 알아챌 만한 움직임이 없었다."], ["Her lips swelled out into a richer curve than ordinary, owing to the enforced tension of the muscles, but he did not know what was their usual appearance, so as to recognise the unwonted sullen defiance of the firm sweeping lines.", "근육이 억지로 팽팽해진 탓에 그녀의 입술은 여느 때보다 도톰한 곡선으로 부풀었으나, 그는 그 입술의 평소 모습을 알지 못했으므로 그 굳고 시원스러운 선에 어린 뜻밖의 뿌루퉁한 반항을 알아보지 못했다."], ["She never blenched or trembled.", "그녀는 움츠리지도 떨지도 않았다."], ["She fixed him with her eye.", "그녀는 눈으로 그를 붙들었다."], ["Now—as he paused before going on, she said, almost as if she would encourage him in telling his tale—“Well—go on!”", "이제 그가 말을 잇기 전에 뜸을 들이자, 그녀는 마치 그가 이야기를 계속하도록 부추기기라도 하듯 말했다. “그래서요, 계속하세요!”"]], [["“It is supposed that an inquest will have to be held; there is some slight evidence to prove that the blow, or push, or scuffle that caused the fall, was provoked by this poor fellow’s half-tipsy impertinence to a young lady, walking with the man who pushed the deceased over the edge of the platform.", "“검시가 열려야 하리라 봅니다. 그 낙상을 부른 타격이나 떠밀기, 혹은 몸싸움이 이 가엾은 자가 반쯤 취해 어떤 젊은 아가씨에게 무례를 범한 데서 비롯되었다는 정황 증거가 얼마간 있습니다."], ["This much was observed by some one on the platform, who, however, thought no more about the matter, as the blow seemed of slight consequence.", "그 아가씨는 죽은 자를 승강장 가장자리 너머로 떠민 남자와 함께 걷고 있었지요. 승강장에 있던 누군가가 이만큼을 보았습니다만, 그 타격이 대수롭지 않아 보였으므로 그는 그 일에 더는 마음을 두지 않았습니다."], ["There is also some reason to identify the lady with yourself; in which case—”", "또한 그 아가씨를 당신으로 볼 만한 근거도 얼마간 있습니다. 그렇다면……”"]], [["“I was not there,”", "“저는 그곳에 없었습니다.”"], ["said Margaret, still keeping her expressionless eyes fixed on his face, with the unconscious look of a sleep-walker.", "마거릿이 말했다. 몽유병자처럼 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 눈빛으로, 여전히 아무 표정 없는 눈을 그의 얼굴에 붙박은 채였다."]], [["The inspector bowed but did not speak.", "조사관은 고개를 숙였을 뿐 말이 없었다."], ["The lady standing before him showed no emotion, no fluttering fear, no anxiety, no desire to end the interview.", "앞에 선 아가씨는 아무런 감정도, 떨리는 두려움도, 불안도, 이 면담을 끝내려는 기색도 보이지 않았다. 그가 얻은 정보는 매우 모호했다."], ["The information he had received was very vague; one of the porters, rushing out to be in readiness for the train, had seen a scuffle, at the other end of the platform, between Leonards and a gentleman accompanied by a lady, but heard no noise; and before the train had got to its full speed after starting, he had been almost knocked down by the headlong run of the enraged half-intoxicated Leonards, swearing and cursing awfully.", "짐꾼 하나가 기차를 맞을 채비를 하려고 뛰어나갔다가 승강장 저쪽 끝에서 레너즈와 어떤 아가씨를 동반한 신사 사이에 몸싸움이 벌어지는 것을 보았으나 소리는 듣지 못했다. 그리고 기차가 출발해 속도를 다 내기 전에, 성이 나 반쯤 취한 레너즈가 무섭게 욕설과 저주를 퍼부으며 곤두박질치듯 달려오는 바람에 그는 하마터면 떠밀려 넘어질 뻔했다."], ["He had not thought any more about it, till his evidence was routed out by the inspector, who, on making some farther inquiry at the railroad station, had heard from the station-master that a young lady and gentleman had been there about that hour—the lady remarkably handsome—and said, by some grocer’s assistant present at the time, to be a Miss Hale, living at Crampton, whose family dealt at his shop.", "그는 조사관이 그 증언을 캐낼 때까지 그 일에 더는 마음을 두지 않았다. 조사관이 철도역에서 더 캐물었더니, 역장에게서 그 시각쯤 젊은 아가씨와 신사가 거기 있었다는 말을, 그 아가씨가 유난히 아름다웠다는 말을 들었다."], ["There was no certainty that the one lady and gentleman were identical with the other pair, but there was great probability.", "그리고 그때 그 자리에 있던 어느 식료품점 점원이 그 아가씨는 크램프턴에 사는 헤일 양이며 그 집안이 자기 가게에서 물건을 산다고 말했다는 것이었다. 그 아가씨와 신사가 앞서의 두 사람과 같은 이들이라는 확증은 없었으나, 그럴 개연성은 컸다."], ["Leonards himself had gone, half-mad with rage and pain, to the nearest gin-palace for comfort; and his tipsy words had not been attended to by the busy waiters there; they, however, remembered his starting up and cursing himself for not having sooner thought of the electric telegraph, for some purpose unknown; and they believed that he left with the idea of going there.", "레너즈 자신은 분노와 통증으로 반쯤 미쳐서 위안을 얻으려 가장 가까운 술집으로 갔다. 그가 취해서 지껄인 말에 그곳의 바쁜 종업원들은 귀를 기울이지 않았다."], ["On his way, overcome by pain or drink, he had lain down in the road, where the police had found him and taken him to the Infirmary: there he had never recovered sufficient consciousness to give any distinct account of his fall, although once or twice he had had glimmerings of sense sufficient to make the authorities send for the nearest magistrate, in hopes that he might be able to take down the dying man’s deposition of the cause of his death.", "다만 그가 벌떡 일어나 전기 전신을 진작에 떠올리지 못했다며 스스로를 저주하던 것은 기억했는데, 무슨 속셈이었는지는 알 수 없었다. 그리고 그가 거기로 갈 작정으로 나갔으리라고들 여겼다."], ["But when the magistrate had come, he was rambling about being at sea, and mixing up names of captains and lieutenants in an indistinct manner with those of his fellow porters at the railway; and his last words were a curse on the “Cornish trick”", "가는 길에 통증인지 술인지에 못 이겨 그는 길바닥에 드러누웠고, 경찰이 그를 발견해 병원으로 데려갔다. 그곳에서 그는 자신이 어떻게 넘어졌는지 또렷이 이야기할 만큼 의식을 되찾지 못했다."], ["which had, he said, made him a hundred pounds poorer than he ought to have been.", "다만 한두 번 정신이 어렴풋이 돌아온 적이 있어, 당국은 죽어 가는 자에게서 사인에 관한 진술을 받아 둘 수 있을까 하여 가장 가까운 치안판사를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그러나 치안판사가 왔을 때 그는 바다에 있다며 횡설수설했고, 함장과 부관들의 이름을 철도의 동료 짐꾼들의 이름과 알아들을 수 없게 뒤섞고 있었다."], ["The inspector ran all this over in his mind—the vagueness of the evidence to prove that Margaret had been at the station—the unflinching, calm denial which she gave to such a supposition.", "그리고 그의 마지막 말은 “콘월식 속임수”에 대한 저주였는데, 그 때문에 자기가 마땅히 가졌어야 할 것보다 100파운드나 가난해졌다는 것이었다. 조사관은 이 모든 것을 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았다."], ["She stood awaiting his next word with a composure that appeared supreme.", "마거릿이 그 역에 있었음을 증명하기에는 증거가 모호하다는 것, 그리고 그런 추정에 대해 그녀가 내놓은 흔들림 없이 차분한 부인. 그녀는 더할 나위 없어 보이는 침착함으로 그의 다음 말을 기다리며 서 있었다."]], [["“Then, madam, I have your denial that you were the lady accompanying the gentleman who struck the blow, or gave the push, which caused the death of this poor man?”", "“그러면 부인, 이 가엾은 사람을 죽음에 이르게 한 타격을 가했거나 떠민 그 신사와 함께 있던 아가씨가 자신이 아니라고 부인하시는 것으로 알겠습니다?”"]], [["A quick, sharp pain went through Margaret’s brain.", "빠르고 날카로운 통증이 마거릿의 머릿속을 관통했다."], ["“Oh God! that I knew Frederick were safe!”", "“아, 하느님, 프레더릭 오빠가 무사하다는 것만 알 수 있다면!”"], ["A deep observer of human countenances might have seen the momentary agony shoot out of her great gloomy eyes, like the torture of some creature brought to bay.", "사람의 얼굴을 깊이 읽을 줄 아는 이라면 그 크고 어두운 눈에서 한순간 솟구친 고뇌를, 궁지에 몰린 짐승의 고통 같은 그것을 보았을지도 모른다."], ["But the inspector though a very keen, was not a very deep observer.", "그러나 조사관은 매우 예리하기는 해도 그리 깊이 보는 사람은 아니었다."], ["He was a little struck, notwithstanding, by the form of the answer, which sounded like a mechanical repetition of her first reply—not changed and modified in shape so as to meet his last question.", "그래도 그는 그 대답의 형태에 조금 마음이 걸렸다. 그것은 첫 대답을 기계적으로 되풀이한 것처럼 들렸고, 그의 마지막 질문에 맞추어 모양을 바꾸거나 다듬은 데가 없었다."]], [["“I was not there,”", "“저는 그곳에 없었습니다.”"], ["said she, slowly and heavily.", "그녀가 느리고 무겁게 말했다."], ["And all this time she never closed her eyes, or ceased from that glassy, dream-like stare.", "그러는 내내 그녀는 눈을 감지도, 그 유리알 같은 꿈결의 응시를 거두지도 않았다."], ["His quick suspicions were aroused by this dull echo of her former denial.", "앞선 부인의 이 둔탁한 메아리에 그의 재빠른 의심이 일었다."], ["It was as if she had forced herself to one untruth, and had been stunned out of all power of varying it.", "마치 그녀가 스스로에게 거짓 하나를 억지로 밀어붙여 놓고는, 그것을 조금이라도 바꿀 힘마저 잃도록 얼이 빠져 버린 것 같았다."]], [["He put up his book of notes in a very deliberate manner.", "그는 아주 신중한 태도로 수첩을 집어넣었다."], ["Then he looked up; she had not moved any more than if she had been some great Egyptian statue.", "그러고서 고개를 들었다. 그녀는 무슨 거대한 이집트 조각상이기라도 한 듯 조금도 움직이지 않고 있었다."]], [["“I hope you will not think me impertinent when I say, that I may have to call on you again.", "“무례하다고 여기지 않으셨으면 합니다만, 제가 다시 찾아뵈어야 할지도 모르겠습니다."], ["I may have to summon you to appear on the inquest, and prove an alibi, if my witnesses” (it was but one who had recognised her) “persist in deposing to your presence at the unfortunate event.”", "제 증인들이”(그녀를 알아본 사람은 하나뿐이었다) “그 불행한 사건 현장에 당신이 있었다고 계속 진술한다면, 검시에 출두하셔서 그곳에 없었음을 증명해 주십사 소환해야 할지도 모릅니다.”"], ["He looked at her sharply.", "그는 그녀를 날카롭게 바라보았다."], ["She was still perfectly quiet—no change of colour, or darker shadow of guilt, on her proud face.", "그녀는 여전히 완벽하게 고요했다. 그 도도한 얼굴에는 낯빛의 변화도, 죄를 짐작케 하는 어두운 그늘도 없었다."], ["He thought to have seen her wince: he did not know Margaret Hale.", "그는 그녀가 움찔하는 것을 보게 되리라 여겼으나, 마거릿 헤일을 몰랐던 것이다."], ["He was a little abashed by her real composure.", "그는 그녀의 진짜 침착함에 조금 무안해졌다."], ["It must have been a mistake of identity.", "사람을 잘못 본 것이 틀림없었다."], ["He went on:", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“It is very unlikely, ma’am, that I shall have to do anything of the kind.", "“부인, 제가 그런 일을 해야 할 가능성은 아주 낮습니다."], ["I hope you will excuse me for doing what is only my duty, although it may appear impertinent.”", "무례해 보일지 모르나 그저 제 직무를 다하는 것이니 너그러이 보아 주시기 바랍니다.”"]], [["Margaret bowed her head as he went towards the door.", "그가 문 쪽으로 가는 동안 마거릿은 고개를 숙였다."], ["Her lips were stiff and dry.", "그녀의 입술은 뻣뻣하게 말라 있었다."], ["She could not speak even the common words of farewell.", "그녀는 흔한 작별 인사조차 입에 올릴 수 없었다."], ["But suddenly she walked forwards, and opened the study door, and preceded him to the door of the house, which she threw wide open for his exit.", "그러나 문득 그녀는 앞으로 걸어 나가 서재 문을 열었고, 그를 앞질러 현관문까지 가서 그가 나가도록 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["She kept her eyes upon him in the same dull, fixed manner, until he was fairly out of the house.", "그녀는 그가 완전히 집 밖으로 나갈 때까지 여전히 그 둔하고 붙박인 눈길로 그를 지켜보았다."], ["She shut the door, and went half-way into the study; then turned back, as if moved by some passionate impulse, and locked the door inside.", "그녀는 문을 닫고 서재로 반쯤 들어갔다. 그러다 무슨 격정에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 되돌아가 안에서 문을 잠갔다."]], [["Then she went into the study, paused—tottered forward—paused again—swayed for an instant where she stood, and fell prone on the floor in a dead swoon.", "그러고서 그녀는 서재로 들어가 멈춰 섰다. 비틀거리며 앞으로 나아가다 다시 멈춰 섰고, 선 자리에서 한순간 휘청이더니 바닥에 엎어져 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV", "# 제35장"]], [["“There’s nought so finely spun But it cometh to the sun.”", "「아무리 곱게 자아낸 실이라 해도 끝내는 햇빛 아래로 드러나 오기 마련이라네.」"]], [["Mr. Thornton sate on and on.", "손턴 씨는 자리를 뜨지 못하고 하염없이 앉아 있었다."], ["He felt that his company gave pleasure to Mr. Hale; and was touched by the half-spoken wishful entreaty that he would remain a little longer—the plaintive “Don’t go yet,”", "자기가 곁에 있는 것이 헤일 씨에게 위안이 된다는 것을 느꼈고, 조금만 더 있어 달라는 반쯤 삼킨 간절한 청에 마음이 움직였다."], ["which his poor friend put forth from time to time.", "가엾은 벗이 이따금 내뱉는 “아직 가지 마시게”"], ["He wondered Margaret did not return; but it was with no view of seeing her that he lingered.", "하는 그 애처로운 말에. 그는 마거릿이 돌아오지 않는 것을 이상히 여겼지만, 그녀를 보려고 머무는 것은 아니었다."], ["For the hour—and in the presence of one who was so thoroughly feeling the nothingness of earth—he was reasonable and self-controlled.", "그 시간만큼은—그리고 이 세상의 덧없음을 뼛속까지 느끼고 있는 사람 앞에서는—그도 이성적이고 절제되어 있었다."], ["He was deeply interested in all her father said", "그는 그녀의 아버지가 하는 모든 말에 깊이 마음을 쏟고 있었다."]], [["“Of death, and of the heavy lull, And of the brain that has grown dull.”", "「죽음에 대하여, 그 무거운 정적에 대하여, 그리고 무디어져 버린 머릿골에 대하여.」"]], [["It was curious how the presence of Mr. Thornton had power over Mr.", "손턴 씨가 곁에 있으면 헤일 씨가 마거릿에게조차 닫아 두었던 은밀한 생각들을 풀어놓게 되는 것은 이상한 일이었다."], ["Hale to make him unlock the secret thoughts which he kept shut up even from Margaret. Whether it was that her sympathy would be so keen, and show itself in so lively a manner, that he was afraid of the reaction upon himself, or whether it was that to his speculative mind all kinds of doubts presented themselves at such a time, pleading and crying aloud to be resolved into certainties, and that he knew she would have shrunk from the expression of any such doubts—nay, from him himself as capable of conceiving them—whatever was the reason, he could unburden himself better to Mr.", "딸의 공감이 너무나 예민하고 또 너무나 생생하게 드러나는 터라 그 반동이 자기에게 되돌아올까 두려웠기 때문인지, 아니면 사변적인 그의 머릿속에는 이런 때면 온갖 의심이 떠올라 확신으로 풀어 달라고 아우성치며 매달리는데 딸이라면 그런 의심을 입에 담는 것 자체를—아니, 그런 의심을 품을 수 있는 아버지라는 사람 자체를—움츠러들며 피하리라는 것을 알았기 때문인지, 이유가 무엇이었든 그는 지금껏 머릿속에 얼어붙어 있던 온갖 생각과 공상과 두려움을 딸보다 손턴 씨에게 훨씬 잘 털어놓을 수 있었다."], ["Thornton than to her of all the thoughts and fancies and fears that had been frost-bound in his brain till now. Mr.", "손턴 씨는 말을 거의 하지 않았다."], ["Thornton said very little; but every sentence he uttered added to Mr. Hale’s reliance and regard for him.", "그러나 그가 내뱉는 한마디 한마디가 헤일 씨의 신뢰와 존경을 더해 갔다."], ["Was it that he paused in the expression of some remembered agony, Mr. Thornton’s two or three words would complete the sentence, and show how deeply its meaning was entered into.", "기억 속의 어떤 고통을 이야기하다 그가 말을 멈추면, 손턴 씨가 던지는 두어 마디가 그 문장을 마저 완성해 주었고, 그 뜻을 얼마나 깊이 헤아리고 있는지를 보여 주었다."], ["Was it a doubt—a fear—a wandering uncertainty seeking rest, but finding none—so tear-blinded were its eyes—Mr. Thornton, instead of being shocked, seemed to have passed through that very stage of thought himself, and could suggest where the exact ray of light was to be found, which should make the dark places plain.", "의심이든 두려움이든, 안식을 찾아 헤매지만 눈이 눈물에 가려 끝내 찾지 못하는 갈피 없는 불안이든, 손턴 씨는 충격을 받기는커녕 스스로도 바로 그 단계를 지나온 사람처럼, 어두운 곳을 환히 밝혀 줄 빛줄기가 정확히 어디에 있는지 일러 줄 수 있었다."], ["Man of action as he was, busy in the world’s great battle, there was a deeper religion binding him to God in his heart, in spite of his strong wilfulness, through all his mistakes, than Mr. Hale had ever dreamed.", "세상의 큰 싸움터에서 분주한 행동가였으되, 그의 마음속에는 그 억센 고집에도 불구하고, 그가 저지른 온갖 잘못을 지나서도, 그를 하느님께 붙들어 매는 더 깊은 신앙이 헤일 씨가 꿈에도 짐작하지 못한 만큼 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["They never spoke of such things again, as it happened; but this one conversation made them peculiar people to each other; knit them together, in a way which no loose indiscriminate talking about sacred things can ever accomplish.", "공교롭게도 두 사람은 다시는 그런 이야기를 나누지 않았다. 그러나 이 한 번의 대화가 서로를 각별한 사람으로 만들었고, 거룩한 것에 대해 아무렇게나 늘어놓는 헐거운 말로는 결코 이루지 못할 방식으로 두 사람을 하나로 묶었다."], ["When all are admitted, how can there be a Holy of Holies?", "누구나 다 들어올 수 있다면 어찌 지성소가 있을 수 있겠는가?"]], [["And all this while, Margaret lay as still and white as death on the study floor!", "그러는 내내 마거릿은 서재 바닥에 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게, 미동도 없이 누워 있었다!"], ["She had sunk under her burden.", "그녀는 짐에 짓눌려 무너진 것이었다."], ["It had been heavy in weight and long carried; and she had been very meek and patient, till all at once her faith had given way, and she had groped in vain for help!", "그 짐은 무거웠고 오래 지고 온 것이었다. 그녀는 참으로 온순하고 참을성 있게 견뎌 왔으나, 마침내 한순간에 믿음이 무너졌고, 도움을 찾아 더듬었으나 헛일이었다!"], ["There was a pitiful contraction of suffering upon her beautiful brows, although there was no other sign of consciousness remaining.", "의식이 남아 있다는 다른 기색은 없었건만, 아름다운 이마에는 고통스러운 주름이 애처롭게 잡혀 있었다."], ["The mouth—a little while ago, so sullenly projected in defiance—was relaxed and livid.", "조금 전까지 반항하듯 뾰로통하게 내밀고 있던 입은 힘없이 풀린 채 파리했다."]], [["“E par che de la sua labbia si mova Uno spirto soave e pien d’amore, Chi va dicendo a l’anima: sospira!”", "「그리고 그 얼굴에서 부드럽고 사랑으로 가득한 정령이 하나 움직여 나와, 영혼을 향해 이렇게 말하는 듯하구나. 한숨지어라!」"]], [["The first symptom of returning life was a quivering about the lips—a little mute soundless attempt at speech; but the eyes were still closed; and the quivering sank into stillness.", "되살아나는 생명의 첫 징후는 입술의 떨림이었다. 소리 없는 작은 말의 시늉이었다."], ["Then, feebly leaning on her arms for an instant to steady herself, Margaret gathered herself up, and rose.", "그러나 눈은 여전히 감긴 채였고, 떨림도 이내 잦아들어 고요해졌다. 이윽고 마거릿은 잠시 두 팔로 몸을 짚어 가누고는 몸을 추슬러 일어났다."], ["Her comb had fallen out of her hair; and with an intuitive desire to efface the traces of weakness, and bring herself into order again, she sought for it, although from time to time, in the course of the search, she had to sit down and recover strength.", "머리에서 빗이 빠져 떨어져 있었다. 나약함의 흔적을 지우고 다시 몸가짐을 가다듬으려는 본능적인 마음에 그녀는 빗을 찾았다."], ["Her head drooped forwards—her hands meekly laid one upon the other—she tried to recall the force of her temptation, by endeavouring to remember the details which had thrown her into such deadly fright; but she could not.", "다만 찾는 동안에도 이따금 주저앉아 기운을 되찾아야 했다. 고개를 앞으로 떨구고 두 손을 얌전히 포갠 채, 그녀는 자기를 그토록 무서운 공포로 몰아넣었던 세세한 일들을 떠올려 봄으로써 자기가 받은 유혹의 힘이 무엇이었는지 되새겨 보려 했다."], ["She only understood two facts—that Frederick had been in danger of being pursued and detected in London, as not only guilty of manslaughter, but as the more unpardonable leader of the mutiny, and that she had lied to save him.", "그러나 되지 않았다. 그녀가 알아들은 것은 두 가지 사실뿐이었다."], ["There was one comfort; her lie had saved him, if only by gaining some additional time.", "프레더릭이 런던에서 쫓기고 발각될 위험에 처했었다는 것—사람을 죽인 죄만이 아니라 더 용서받지 못할 반란의 주모자로서—그리고 자기가 오빠를 구하려고 거짓말을 했다는 것. 위안이 하나 있었다."], ["If the inspector came again to-morrow, after she had received the letter she longed for to assure her of her brother’s safety, she would brave shame, and stand in her bitter penance—she the lofty Margaret—acknowledging before a crowded justice-room, if need were, that she had been as “a dog, and done this thing.”", "비록 시간을 조금 더 벌어 준 것뿐일지라도 그 거짓말이 오빠를 구했다는 것. 오빠가 무사하다는 것을 알려 줄, 그토록 애타게 기다리는 편지를 받은 뒤에 경위가 내일 다시 온다면, 그때는 수치를 무릅쓰고 쓰디쓴 참회의 자리에 서리라."], ["But if he came before she heard from Frederick; if he returned, as he had half threatened, in a few hours, why!", "그 도도한 마거릿이, 필요하다면 사람들로 가득 찬 재판정 앞에서 자기가 “개처럼 이런 짓을 저질렀노라”고 시인하리라."], ["she would tell that lie again; though how the words would come out, after all this terrible pause for reflection and self-reproach, without betraying her falsehood, she did not know, she could not tell.", "그러나 프레더릭에게서 소식을 듣기 전에 그가 온다면, 반쯤 으름장을 놓았듯 몇 시간 안에 그가 돌아온다면, 그때는—그때는 그 거짓말을 또 하리라. 다만 이 무서운 반성과 자책의 시간을 다 겪고 난 뒤에 어떻게 그 말이 거짓임을 드러내지 않고 입 밖으로 나올 수 있을지, 그녀는 알지 못했고 짐작할 수도 없었다."], ["But her repetition of it would gain time—time for Frederick.", "그러나 거듭 거짓말을 하면 시간을 벌 수 있을 터였다. 프레더릭을 위한 시간을."]], [["She was roused by Dixon’s entrance into the room; she had just been letting out Mr.", "딕슨이 방에 들어오는 바람에 그녀는 정신이 들었다."], ["Thornton.", "딕슨은 막 손턴 씨를 배웅하고 온 참이었다."]], [["He had hardly gone ten steps in the street, before a passing omnibus stopped close by him, and a man got down, and came up to him, touching his hat as he did so.", "그가 거리로 나서 열 걸음도 채 못 갔을 때, 지나가던 승합마차가 그의 곁에 멎더니 한 사내가 내려 다가와 모자에 손을 얹어 인사했다."], ["It was the police-inspector.", "경찰 경위였다."]], [["Mr. Thornton had obtained for him his first situation in the police, and had heard from time to time of the progress of his protégé, but they had not often met, and at first Mr.", "손턴 씨는 그에게 경찰의 첫 자리를 얻어 주었고, 이따금 그 피후견인이 어떻게 되어 가는지 소식을 들어 왔다."], ["Thornton did not remember him.", "그러나 자주 만나지는 못한 터라 처음에는 그를 알아보지 못했다."]], [["“My name is Watson—George Watson, sir, that you got——”", "“저는 왓슨입니다. 조지 왓슨이라고, 나리께서 자리를 마련해 주신——”"]], [["“Ah, yes!", "“아, 그렇지!"], ["Why you are getting on famously, I hear.”", "아주 잘해 나가고 있다고 들었네.”"]], [["“Yes, sir.", "“예, 나리."], ["I ought to thank you, sir.", "감사드려야 마땅합니다."], ["But it is on a little matter of business I made so bold as to speak to you now.", "하오나 지금 이렇게 감히 말씀을 올리는 것은 조그마한 일 때문입니다."], ["I believe you were the magistrate who attended to take down the deposition of a poor man who died in the Infirmary last night.”", "어젯밤 시립병원에서 죽은 가엾은 사내의 진술을 받으러 나오신 치안판사가 나리시라고 알고 있습니다만.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇네.”"], ["replied Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I went and heard some kind of a rambling statement, which the clerk said was of no great use.", "“가서 두서없는 진술 비슷한 것을 듣기는 했네. 서기는 별 쓸모가 없다고 하더군."], ["I’m afraid he was but a drunken fellow, though there is no doubt he came to his death by violence at last.", "아무래도 술에 절어 있던 자 같았네. 하기야 끝내 폭력으로 죽음에 이른 것만은 틀림없지만."], ["One of my mother’s servants was engaged to him, I believe, and she is in great distress to-day.", "우리 어머니 댁 하녀 하나가 그자와 약혼한 사이였다는데, 오늘 몹시 상심해 있네."], ["What about him?”", "그자가 어쨌다는 건가?”"]], [["“Why, sir, his death is oddly mixed up with somebody in the house I saw you coming out of just now; it was a Mr.", "“실은 나리, 그자의 죽음이 방금 나리께서 나오시는 것을 제가 뵌 그 댁 사람과 묘하게 얽혀 있습니다."], ["Hales’s I believe.”", "헤일 씨 댁이지요, 아마.”"]], [["“Yes!” said Mr.", "“그렇네!”"], ["Thornton, turning sharp round and looking into the inspector’s face with sudden interest.", "손턴 씨가 홱 돌아서서 갑작스러운 관심으로 경위의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“What about it?”", "“그게 어쨌다는 건가?”"]], [["“Why, sir, it seems to me that I have got a pretty distinct chain of evidence, inculpating a gentleman who was walking with Miss Hale that night at the Outwood station, as the man who struck or pushed Leonards off the platform and so caused his death.", "“실은 나리, 제가 보기에는 증거의 사슬이 꽤 또렷하게 잡힙니다. 그날 밤 아웃우드 역에서 헤일 양과 함께 걷고 있던 신사가 레너즈를 승강장에서 치거나 밀어 떨어뜨려 죽음에 이르게 한 자라는 것이지요."], ["But the young lady denies that she was there at the time.”", "그런데 그 아가씨는 그 시각에 자기가 거기 없었다고 부인합니다.”"]], [["“Miss Hale denies she was there!”", "“헤일 양이 거기 없었다고 부인한다고!”"], ["repeated Mr. Thornton in an altered voice.", "손턴 씨가 달라진 목소리로 되뇌었다."], ["“Tell me, what evening was it?", "“말해 보게, 그게 어느 날 저녁이었나?"], ["What time?”", "몇 시였고?”"]], [["“About six o’clock, on the evening of Thursday, the twenty-sixth.”", "“26일 목요일 저녁, 여섯 시쯤입니다.”"]], [["They walked on, side by side, in silence for a minute or two.", "두 사람은 나란히 걸으며 일이 분 동안 말이 없었다."], ["The inspector was the first to speak.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 경위였다."]], [["“You see, sir, there is like to be a coroner’s inquest; and I’ve got a young man who is pretty positive,—at least he was at first;—since he has heard of the young lady’s denial, he says he should not like to swear; but still he’s pretty positive that he saw Miss Hale at the station, walking about with a gentleman, not five minutes before the time, when one of the porters saw a scuffle, which he set down to some of Leonards’ impudence—but which led to the fall which caused his death. And seeing you come out of the very house, sir, I thought I might make bold to ask if—you see, it’s always awkward having to do with cases of disputed identity, and one doesn’t like to doubt the word of a respectable young woman unless one has strong proof to the contrary.”", "“보십시오, 나리, 검시가 열릴 듯싶습니다. 그런데 꽤 확신하는 젊은이가 하나 있습니다. 적어도 처음에는 그랬지요. 그 아가씨가 부인했다는 말을 듣고는 선서까지는 하고 싶지 않다고 합니다만, 그래도 헤일 양이 역에서 어떤 신사와 함께 거니는 것을 보았다고 꽤 확신합니다. 짐꾼 하나가 몸싸움을 본 시각으로부터 채 오 분도 안 되기 전에 말입니다. 짐꾼은 그 몸싸움을 레너즈가 늘 부리던 건방진 수작쯤으로 여겼습니다만, 그 일로 그자가 떨어져 죽게 된 것이지요. 그런데 나리께서 바로 그 댁에서 나오시는 것을 뵈니 감히 여쭈어도 될까 싶었습니다. 아시다시피 사람이 누구인지를 두고 다투는 사건은 늘 거북하고, 어지간한 반증이 없이는 점잖은 젊은 아가씨의 말을 의심하고 싶지도 않으니까요.”"]], [["“And she denied having been at the station that evening!”", "“그런데 그 저녁에 역에 있지 않았다고 부인했다고!”"], ["repeated Mr. Thornton, in a low, brooding tone.", "손턴 씨가 낮게 잠긴 목소리로 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, sir, twice over, as distinct as could be.", "“예, 나리. 두 번이나, 더없이 또렷하게요."], ["I told her I should call again, but seeing you just as I was on my way back from questioning the young man who said it was her, I thought I would ask your advice, both as the magistrate who saw Leonards on his death-bed, and as the gentleman who got me my berth in the force.”", "제가 다시 찾아뵙겠다고 말해 두었습니다만, 그 아가씨가 맞다고 한 젊은이를 캐묻고 돌아오는 길에 마침 나리를 뵙게 되니, 레너즈의 임종 자리를 지켜보신 치안판사로서도 그렇고 제게 경찰 자리를 마련해 주신 어른으로서도 그렇고, 조언을 여쭙고 싶었습니다.”"]], [["“You were quite right,”", "“잘했네.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 말했다."], ["“Don’t take any steps till you have seen me again.”", "“나를 다시 만나기 전에는 아무 조치도 취하지 말게.”"]], [["“The young lady will expect me to call, from what I said.”", "“제가 그리 말씀드렸으니 그 아가씨는 제가 찾아올 줄 알고 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“I only want to delay you an hour.", "“한 시간만 늦추라는 것일세."], ["It’s now three.", "지금이 세 시로군."], ["Come to my warehouse at four.”", "네 시에 내 창고로 오게.”"]], [["“Very well, sir!”", "“알겠습니다, 나리!”"]], [["And they parted company. Mr.", "그러고서 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["Thornton hurried to his warehouse, and, sternly forbidding his clerks to allow any one to interrupt him, he went his way to his own private room, and locked the door.", "손턴 씨는 서둘러 창고로 가서 서기들에게 누구도 자기를 방해하지 못하게 하라고 엄히 이르고는, 자기 집무실로 들어가 문을 잠갔다."], ["Then he indulged himself in the torture of thinking it all over, and realising every detail.", "그러고는 모든 일을 하나하나 되짚으며 세세한 대목까지 실감하는 고문을 스스로에게 실컷 안겼다."], ["How could he have lulled himself into the unsuspicious calm in which her tearful image had mirrored itself not two hours before, till he had weakly pitied her and yearned towards her, and forgotten the savage, distrustful jealousy with which the sight of her—and that unknown to him—at such an hour—in such a place—had inspired him!", "어떻게 자기가 두 시간도 채 되기 전에 눈물 젖은 그녀의 모습이 마음에 비쳐 들던 그 아무 의심 없는 평온에 젖어 들었단 말인가. 그리하여 나약하게도 그녀를 가엾이 여기고 그녀에게 마음이 끌렸으며, 그런 시각에, 그런 곳에서, 그것도 자기가 모르는 사이에 그녀를 본 것이 불러일으켰던 그 사납고 불신에 찬 질투를 잊고 있었다니!"], ["How could one so pure have stooped from her decorous and noble manner of bearing!", "그토록 순결한 사람이 어떻게 그 단정하고 고결한 몸가짐에서 그렇게 내려앉을 수 있단 말인가!"], ["But was it decorous—was it?", "그런데 그것은 단정한 것이었던가—과연 그러했던가?"], ["He hated himself for the idea that forced itself upon him, just for an instant—no more—and yet, while it was present, thrilled him with its old potency of attraction towards her image.", "한순간, 그 이상은 아니었으나, 억지로 밀고 들어오는 그 생각 때문에 그는 자기 자신이 미웠다. 그러면서도 그 생각이 머무는 동안 그녀의 모습을 향한 옛 이끌림이 그 힘을 되찾아 그를 떨리게 했다."], ["And then this falsehood—how terrible must be some dread of shame to be revealed—for, after all, the provocation given by such a man as Leonards was, when excited by drinking, might, in all probability, be more than enough to justify any one who came forward to state the circumstances openly and without reserve!", "게다가 이 거짓말—드러나는 것이 그토록 두려운 수치라면 대체 얼마나 무서운 것이란 말인가. 따지고 보면 레너즈 같은 자가, 그것도 술에 취해 달아올랐을 때 걸어온 시비라면, 앞에 나서서 사정을 숨김없이 밝히는 사람 누구에게든 넉넉히 명분이 될 법도 하지 않은가!"], ["How creeping and deadly that fear which could bow down the truthful Margaret to falsehood!", "정직한 마거릿을 거짓말 앞에 무릎 꿇게 만든 그 두려움은 얼마나 은밀하고 치명적인가!"], ["He could almost pity her.", "그는 그녀가 가엾다는 마음마저 들었다."], ["What would be the end of it?", "이 일의 끝은 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["She could not have considered all she was entering upon; if there was an inquest and the young man came forward.", "검시가 열리고 그 젊은이가 나선다면, 자기가 무슨 일에 발을 들이는지 그녀는 미처 헤아리지 못했을 터였다. 문득 그가 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["Suddenly he started up.", "검시는 열려서는 안 된다."], ["There should be no inquest.", "자기가 마거릿을 구하리라. 검시를 막는 책임을 자기가 지리라."], ["He would save Margaret.", "어차피 그 결말도 의학적 소견이 불확실하니 미덥지 못할 것이었다."], ["He would take the responsibility of preventing the inquest, the issue of which, from the uncertainty of the medical testimony (which he had vaguely heard the night before, from the surgeon in attendance), could be but doubtful; the doctors had discovered an internal disease far advanced, and sure to prove fatal; they had stated that death might have been accelerated by the fall, or by the subsequent drinking and exposure to cold.", "전날 밤 진료를 맡은 외과의에게서 어렴풋이 들은 바로는, 의사들은 이미 깊이 진행되어 틀림없이 목숨을 앗아 갈 내부 질환을 발견했고, 추락 때문에, 혹은 그 뒤의 음주와 추위에 노출된 탓으로 죽음이 앞당겨졌을 수도 있다고 밝혔다는 것이었다. 마거릿이 이 일에 어떻게 얽혀 들지 알기만 했더라면, 그녀가 거짓말로 자기 결백을 더럽히게 되리라 내다보기만 했더라면, 그는 한마디로 그녀를 구할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["If he had but known how Margaret would have become involved in the affair—if he had but foreseen that he would have stained her whiteness by a falsehood, he could have saved her by a word; for the question, of inquest or no inquest, had hung trembling in the balance only the night before.", "검시를 할 것인가 말 것인가는 바로 전날 밤만 해도 저울 위에서 떨고 있었으니 말이다. 헤일 양이 다른 사람을 사랑할지도 모른다."], ["Miss Hale might love another—was indifferent and contemptuous to him—but he would yet do her faithful acts of service of which she should never know.", "자기에게는 무심하고 경멸하는지도 모른다. 그래도 그는 그녀가 결코 알지 못할 충직한 도움을 베풀 것이었다."], ["He might despise her, but the woman whom he had once loved should be kept from shame; and shame it would be to pledge herself to a lie in a public court, or otherwise to stand and acknowledge her reason for desiring darkness rather than light.", "그녀를 경멸할지언정, 한때 사랑했던 여인이 수치를 당하게 두지는 않으리라. 공개된 법정에서 거짓말에 자기를 걸어야 하는 것도, 그러지 않으려면 어찌하여 빛보다 어둠을 바라는지 그 까닭을 서서 시인해야 하는 것도, 수치임에 틀림없었다."]], [["Very gray and stern did Mr. Thornton look, as he passed out through his wondering clerks.", "어리둥절해하는 서기들 사이를 지나 나가는 손턴 씨의 얼굴은 몹시 잿빛이고 굳어 있었다."], ["He was away about half an hour; and scarcely less stern did he look when he returned, although his errand had been successful.", "그는 반 시간쯤 자리를 비웠다. 돌아왔을 때도, 볼일은 뜻대로 되었건만, 얼굴은 조금도 덜 굳어 보이지 않았다."]], [["He wrote two lines on a slip of paper, put it in an envelope, and sealed it up.", "그는 종잇조각에 두 줄을 적어 봉투에 넣고 봉했다."], ["This he gave to one of the clerks, saying:—", "그리고 그것을 서기 하나에게 건네며 말했다."]], [["“I appointed Watson—he who was a packer in the warehouse, and who went into the police—to call on me at four o’clock.", "“창고에서 짐 싸는 일을 하다가 경찰로 간 왓슨이라는 사람에게 네 시에 나를 찾아오라고 일러 두었네."], ["I have just met a gentleman from Liverpool who wishes to see me before he leaves town.", "그런데 방금 리버풀에서 온 신사를 만났는데, 이 도시를 떠나기 전에 나를 만나고 싶어 하는군."], ["Take care to give this note to Watson when he calls.”", "왓슨이 오거든 이 쪽지를 꼭 전해 주게.”"]], [["The note contained these words:", "쪽지에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“There will be no inquest.", "“검시는 없을 것이오."], ["Medical evidence not sufficient to justify it.", "의학적 증거가 검시를 할 만큼 충분치 않소."], ["Take no further steps.", "더는 아무 조치도 취하지 마시오."], ["I have not seen the coroner; but I will take the responsibility.”", "검시관을 만나지는 않았으나 책임은 내가 지겠소.”"]], [["“Well,”", "“거참.”"], ["thought Watson, “it relieves me from an awkward job.", "왓슨은 생각했다. “거북한 일을 덜었군."], ["None of my witnesses seemed certain of anything except the young woman.", "그 젊은 아가씨 말고는 내 증인 가운데 무엇 하나 확실한 자가 없었지."], ["She was clear and distinct enough; the porter at the railroad had seen a scuffle!", "아가씨는 더없이 분명하고 또렷했고."], ["or when he found it was likely to bring him in as a witness, then it might not have been a scuffle, only a little larking, and Leonards might have jumped off the platform himself;—he would not stick firm to anything.", "철도 짐꾼은 몸싸움을 보았다고 했다가, 그 일로 자기가 증인으로 불려 나가게 생겼다 싶으니 몸싸움이 아니라 그저 장난질이었을 수도 있다, 레너즈가 제 발로 승강장에서 뛰어내렸을 수도 있다 하더군. 무엇 하나 딱 붙들고 말하는 게 없었어."], ["And Jennings, the grocer’s shopman,—well, he was not quite so bad, but I doubt if I could have got him up to an oath after he heard that Miss Hale flatly denied it.", "식료품 가게 점원 제닝스는, 글쎄, 그만큼 나쁘지는 않았지만, 헤일 양이 딱 잘라 부인했다는 말을 들은 뒤로 선서까지 시킬 수 있었을지는 모르겠군."], ["It would have been a troublesome job and no satisfaction.", "골치만 아프고 소득은 없는 일이었을 거야."], ["And now I must go and tell them they won’t be wanted.”", "이제 가서 이 사람들한테 부를 일 없게 됐다고 알려야겠군.”"]], [["He accordingly presented himself again at Mr. Hale’s that evening.", "그리하여 그는 그날 저녁 다시 헤일 씨 댁을 찾았다."], ["Her father and Dixon would fain have persuaded Margaret to go to bed; but they, neither of them, knew the reason for her low continued refusals to do so.", "마거릿의 아버지와 딕슨은 어떻게든 그녀를 잠자리에 들게 하려 했으나, 두 사람 다 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 한사코 마다하는 까닭을 알지 못했다."], ["Dixon had learnt part of the truth—but only part.", "딕슨은 진실의 일부를 알고 있었다. 다만 일부뿐이었다."], ["Margaret would not tell any human being of what she had said, and she did not reveal the fatal termination to Leonards’ fall from the platform.", "마거릿은 자기가 무슨 말을 했는지 어떤 사람에게도 말하지 않으려 했고, 레너즈가 승강장에서 떨어진 일이 죽음으로 끝났다는 것도 밝히지 않았다."], ["So Dixon’s curiosity combined with her allegiance to urge Margaret to go to rest, which her appearance, as she lay on the sofa, showed but too clearly that she required.", "그래서 딕슨은 호기심에 충직함까지 더해 마거릿에게 쉬라고 다그쳤다. 소파에 누운 그녀의 모습은 쉬어야 한다는 것을 너무나 분명히 보여 주고 있었다."], ["She did not speak except when spoken to; she tried to smile back in reply to her father’s anxious looks and words of tender inquiry; but, instead of a smile, the wan lips resolved themselves into a sigh.", "그녀는 누가 말을 걸 때가 아니면 입을 열지 않았다. 아버지가 걱정스레 바라보며 다정히 묻는 말에 미소로 답하려 애썼으나, 미소 대신 핏기 없는 입술은 한숨이 되어 버렸다."], ["He was so miserably uneasy that, at last, she consented to go into her own room, and prepare for going to bed.", "아버지가 하도 안절부절못하는 통에 그녀는 마침내 제 방으로 들어가 잘 채비를 하겠다고 했다."], ["She was indeed inclined to give up the idea that the inspector would call again that night, as it was already past nine o’clock.", "하기야 이미 아홉 시가 넘었으니 그날 밤 경위가 다시 오지는 않으리라는 쪽으로 그녀의 마음도 기울고 있었다."]], [["She stood by her father, holding on to the back of his chair.", "그녀는 아버지 곁에 서서 의자 등받이를 붙들고 있었다."]], [["“You will go to bed soon, papa, won’t you?", "“아버지도 곧 주무실 거죠?"], ["Don’t sit up alone!”", "혼자 앉아 계시지 마세요!”"]], [["What his answer was she did not hear; the words were lost in the far smaller sound that magnified itself to her fears, and filled her brain.", "아버지의 대답이 무엇이었는지 그녀는 듣지 못했다. 그 말은 훨씬 작은 소리에 묻혀 버렸다."], ["There was a low ring at the door-bell.", "그 소리는 그녀의 두려움 속에서 크게 부풀어 올라 머릿속을 가득 채웠다. 현관 초인종이 나직하게 울린 것이었다."]], [["She kissed her father and glided down stairs, with a rapidity of motion of which no one would have thought her capable, who had seen her the minute before.", "그녀는 아버지에게 입을 맞추고 아래층으로 미끄러지듯 내려갔다. 조금 전의 그녀를 본 사람이라면 그런 몸놀림이 가능하리라고는 생각지 못했을 만큼 빠른 걸음이었다."], ["She put aside Dixon.", "그녀는 딕슨을 옆으로 물렸다."]], [["“Don’t come; I will open the door. I know it is him—I can—I must manage it all myself.”", "“오지 마요. 내가 문을 열 테니. 그 사람인 줄 알아요. 내가 할 수 있어요. 내가 다 알아서 해야 해요.”"]], [["“As you please, miss!”", "“좋으실 대로 하세요, 아가씨!” 딕슨이 퉁명스레 말했다."], ["said Dixon, testily; but in a moment afterwards, she added, “But you’re not fit for it.", "그러나 곧이어 덧붙였다. “하지만 아가씨는 그럴 몸이 아니에요."], ["You are more dead than alive.”", "산 사람이라기보다 죽은 사람 꼴이라고요.”"]], [["“Am I?”", "“내가요?” 마거릿이 돌아서며 말했다."], ["said Margaret, turning round and showing her eyes all aglow with strange fire, her cheeks flushed, though her lips were baked and livid still.", "그 눈은 야릇한 불길로 온통 이글거렸고 두 뺨은 달아올라 있었다. 다만 입술만은 여전히 마르고 파리했다."]], [["She opened the door to the Inspector, and preceded him into the study.", "그녀는 경위에게 문을 열어 주고 앞장서 서재로 들어갔다."], ["She placed the candle on the table, and snuffed it carefully, before she turned round and faced him.", "촛불을 탁자 위에 놓고 심지를 조심스레 자른 다음에야 몸을 돌려 그와 마주 섰다."]], [["“You are late!”", "“늦으셨군요!”"], ["said she. “Well?”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["She held her breath for the answer.", "“그래서요?” 대답을 기다리며 그녀는 숨을 죽였다."]], [["“I am sorry to have given any unnecessary trouble, ma’am; for, after all, they’ve given up all thoughts of holding an inquest.", "“공연한 수고를 끼쳐 드려 죄송합니다, 아가씨. 결국 검시를 열 생각은 다들 접었습니다."], ["I have had other work to do and other people to see, or I should have been here before now.”", "다른 일도 보고 만날 사람도 있어서 늦었지, 아니었으면 진작에 왔을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Then it is ended,”", "“그럼 끝난 거군요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“There is to be no further enquiry.”", "“더 이상 조사는 없는 거고요.”"]], [["“I believe I’ve got Mr. Thornton’s note about me,”", "“손턴 씨가 제게 보낸 쪽지를 갖고 있을 겁니다.”"], ["said the Inspector, fumbling in his pocket-book.", "경위가 수첩을 뒤적이며 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton’s!”", "“손턴 씨가요!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Yes!", "“예!"], ["he’s a magistrate—ah!", "그분이 치안판사시니까요."], ["here it is.”", "아, 여기 있군요.”"]], [["She could not see to read it—no, not although she was close to the candle.", "그녀는 눈으로 그것을 읽을 수가 없었다. 촛불 바로 곁에 서 있었는데도 그랬다."], ["The words swam before her.", "글자가 눈앞에서 헤엄쳤다."], ["But she held it in her hand, and looked at it as if she were intently studying it.", "그래도 그녀는 그것을 손에 들고 열심히 들여다보는 척했다."]], [["“I’m sure, ma’am, it’s a great weight off my mind; for the evidence was so uncertain, you see, that the man had received any blow at all, and if any question of identity came in, it so complicated the case, as I told Mr.", "“정말이지 아가씨, 저로서는 큰 짐을 던 셈입니다. 아시다시피 그 사람이 맞기는 맞았는지조차 증거가 하도 불확실해서, 거기에 신원을 두고 다투는 문제까지 끼어들면 사건이 여간 복잡해지는 게 아니거든요."], ["Thornton——”", "그래서 손턴 씨께도 말씀을 드렸——”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton!”", "“손턴 씨께요!”"], ["said Margaret again.", "마거릿이 다시 말했다."]], [["“I met him this morning, just as he was coming out of this house, and, as he’s an old friend of mine, besides being the magistrate who saw Leonards last night, I made bold to tell him of my difficulty.”", "“오늘 아침에 그분이 바로 이 댁에서 나오시는 참에 마주쳤습니다. 제 오랜 은인이신 데다 어젯밤 레너즈를 보신 치안판사이기도 하시니, 감히 제 곤란한 사정을 말씀드렸지요.”"]], [["Margaret sighed deeply.", "마거릿은 깊이 한숨지었다."], ["She did not want to hear any more; she was afraid alike of what she had heard, and of what she might hear.", "그녀는 더 듣고 싶지 않았다. 이미 들은 것도, 앞으로 들을지 모르는 것도 다 같이 두려웠다."], ["She wished that the man would go.", "이 사람이 어서 가 주기를 바랐다."], ["She forced herself to speak.", "그녀는 억지로 입을 열었다."]], [["“Thank you for calling.", "“찾아와 주셔서 고맙습니다."], ["It is very late.", "아주 늦었네요."], ["I dare say it is past ten o’clock.", "열 시가 넘었을 거예요."], ["Oh! here is the note!”", "아, 여기 쪽지요!”"], ["she continued, suddenly interpreting the meaning of the hand held out to receive it.", "그녀는 그것을 받으려고 내민 손의 뜻을 문득 알아차리고 말을 이었다."], ["He was putting it up, when she said, “I think it is a cramped, dazzling sort of writing.", "그가 그것을 도로 넣으려 하자 그녀가 말했다. “글씨가 빽빽하고 어지러워서 읽을 수가 없었어요."], ["I could not read it; will you just read it to me?”", "제게 읽어 주시겠어요?”"]], [["He read it aloud to her.", "그는 그것을 소리 내어 읽어 주었다."]], [["“Thank you.", "“고맙습니다."], ["You told Mr. Thornton that I was not there?”", "제가 거기 없었다고 손턴 씨께 말씀드리셨나요?”"]], [["“Oh, of course, ma’am.", "“아, 물론입니다, 아가씨."], ["I’m sorry now that I acted upon information, which seems to have been so erroneous.", "그렇게나 잘못된 정보를 믿고 움직인 것이 이제 와 죄송할 따름입니다."], ["At first the young man was so positive; and now he says that he doubted all along, and hopes that his mistake won’t have occasioned you such annoyance as to lose their shop your custom.", "처음에는 그 젊은이가 하도 확신하기에 그랬지요. 그런데 이제 와서는 처음부터 미심쩍었다고 하면서, 자기 잘못 때문에 아가씨가 언짢으셔서 그 가게 단골을 끊으시는 일은 없기를 바란다더군요."], ["Good night, ma’am.”", "안녕히 주무십시오, 아가씨.”"]], [["“Good night.”", "“안녕히 가세요.”"], ["She rang the bell for Dixon to show him out.", "그녀는 딕슨에게 그를 배웅하라고 종을 울렸다."], ["As Dixon returned up the passage Margaret passed her swiftly.", "딕슨이 복도를 되돌아오는데 마거릿이 재빨리 그 곁을 지나쳤다."]], [["“It is all right!”", "“다 잘됐어요!”"], ["said she, without looking at Dixon; and before the woman could follow her with further questions she had sped upstairs, and entered her bed-chamber, and bolted her door.", "그녀는 딕슨을 쳐다보지도 않고 말했다. 그리고 딕슨이 더 캐물으며 따라오기도 전에 그녀는 위층으로 달아나 침실에 들어가서는 문에 빗장을 질렀다."]], [["She threw herself, dressed as she was, upon her bed.", "그녀는 옷도 갈아입지 않은 채 침대에 몸을 던졌다."], ["She was too much exhausted to think.", "생각하기에는 너무나 지쳐 있었다."], ["Half-an-hour or more elapsed before the cramped nature of her position, and the chilliness, supervening upon great fatigue, had the power to rouse her numbed faculties.", "반 시간 남짓 지나서야 옹송그린 자세와, 심한 피로 끝에 밀려든 한기가 마비된 그녀의 정신을 흔들어 깨울 힘을 얻었다."], ["Then she began to recall, to combine, to wonder.", "그제야 그녀는 되짚어 보고, 이어 맞추어 보고, 의아해하기 시작했다."], ["The first idea that presented itself to her was, that all this sickening alarm on Frederick’s behalf was over; that the strain was past.", "가장 먼저 떠오른 생각은 프레더릭 때문에 애간장을 태우던 이 모든 두려움이 끝났다는 것, 그 긴장이 지나갔다는 것이었다."], ["The next was a wish to remember every word of the Inspector’s which related to Mr.", "다음에 든 것은 손턴 씨와 관계된 경위의 말을 한마디도 빠짐없이 떠올리고 싶다는 마음이었다."], ["Thornton. When had he seen him?", "그가 언제 손턴 씨를 만났다고 했던가?"], ["What had he said?", "무슨 말을 했던가?"], ["What had Mr. Thornton done?", "손턴 씨는 무엇을 했던가?"], ["What were the exact words of his note?", "그 쪽지의 말은 정확히 무엇이었던가?"], ["And until she could recollect, even to the placing or omitting an article, the very expressions which he had used in the note, her mind refused to go on with its progress.", "그리고 관사 하나를 넣고 뺀 것까지, 그가 쪽지에 쓴 바로 그 표현을 떠올려 낼 때까지 그녀의 마음은 앞으로 나아가기를 거부했다."], ["But the next conviction she came to was clear enough;—Mr.", "그러나 이어서 다다른 확신만은 더없이 또렷했다."], ["Thornton had seen her close to Outwood station on the fatal Thursday night, and had been told of her denial that she was there.", "손턴 씨는 그 운명의 목요일 밤 아웃우드 역 가까이에서 자기를 보았고, 자기가 거기 없었다고 부인했다는 말을 들은 것이다."], ["She stood a liar in his eyes.", "그의 눈에 자기는 거짓말쟁이로 서 있었다."], ["She was a liar.", "자기는 거짓말쟁이였다."], ["But she had no thought of penitence before God; nothing but chaos and night surrounded the one lurid fact that, in Mr.", "그러나 그녀는 하느님 앞에서 뉘우칠 생각은 하지 못했다. 손턴 씨의 눈에 자기가 비천해졌다는 그 한 가지 섬뜩한 사실 둘레에는 오직 혼돈과 어둠뿐이었다."], ["Thornton’s eyes, she was degraded. She cared not to think, even to herself, of how much of excuse she might plead.", "자기가 얼마나 변명할 거리를 댈 수 있는지는 스스로에게조차 생각하고 싶지 않았다."], ["That had nothing to do with Mr.", "그것은 손턴 씨와 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["Thornton; she never dreamed that he, or any one else, could find cause for suspicion in what was so natural as her accompanying her brother; but what was really false and wrong was known to him, and he had a right to judge her.", "오빠와 함께 걷는 것처럼 자연스러운 일에서 그가, 혹은 누구라도 의심할 거리를 찾아내리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 않았다. 그러나 정말로 거짓되고 그릇된 그 일을 그는 알고 있었고, 그에게는 자기를 판단할 자격이 있었다."], ["“Oh, Frederick!", "“아, 프레더릭!"], ["Frederick!”", "프레더릭!”"], ["she cried, “what have I not sacrificed for you!”", "그녀가 부르짖었다. “오빠를 위해 내가 무엇을 희생하지 않았던가!”"], ["Even when she fell asleep her thoughts were compelled to travel the same circle, only with exaggerated and monstrous circumstances of pain.", "잠이 들어서도 그녀의 생각은 같은 자리를 맴돌아야 했다. 다만 고통의 정황이 부풀려지고 흉측해졌을 뿐이었다."]], [["When she awoke a new idea flashed upon her with all the brightness of the morning. Mr.", "잠에서 깨었을 때 아침 햇살처럼 환한 새로운 생각이 그녀에게 번쩍 떠올랐다."], ["Thornton had learnt her falsehood before he went to the coroner; that suggested the thought, that he had possibly been influenced so to do with a view of sparing her the repetition of her denial.", "손턴 씨는 검시관에게 가기 전에 그녀의 거짓말을 알았다. 그렇다면 그가 그렇게 한 것은 그녀가 그 부인을 되풀이하지 않아도 되도록 감싸 주려는 뜻에서였을지도 모른다는 생각이었다."], ["But she pushed this notion on one side with the sick wilfulness of a child.", "그러나 그녀는 병든 아이 같은 고집으로 이 생각을 밀쳐 냈다."], ["If it were so, she felt no gratitude to him, as it only showed her how keenly he must have seen that she was disgraced already, before he took such unwonted pains to spare her any further trial of truthfulness, which had already failed so signally.", "정말 그렇다면 그것은 고마울 일이 아니었다. 이미 그렇게도 참담하게 무너져 버린 정직함을 더 시험당하지 않도록 그가 그토록 유별난 수고를 하기에 앞서, 자기가 이미 욕된 처지에 놓였음을 그가 얼마나 뼈저리게 알아보았는지를 드러낼 뿐이었으니까."], ["She would have gone through the whole—she would have perjured herself to save Frederick, rather—far rather—than Mr.", "차라리 그 모든 것을 다 겪는 편이 나았을 것이다. 프레더릭을 구하려고 위증까지 했을 것이다."], ["Thornton should have had the knowledge that prompted him to interfere to save her.", "손턴 씨가 자기를 구하려고 나서게 만든 그 앎을 그가 가지느니, 차라리, 훨씬 더 차라리 그편이 나았을 것이다."], ["What ill-fate brought him in contact with the Inspector?", "무슨 몹쓸 운수가 그를 경위와 마주치게 했단 말인가?"], ["What made him be the very magistrate sent for to receive Leonards’ deposition?", "무엇 때문에 하필 그가 레너즈의 진술을 받으러 불려 나온 치안판사였단 말인가?"], ["What had Leonards said?", "레너즈는 무슨 말을 했을까?"], ["How much of it was intelligible to Mr.", "그중 얼마나가 손턴 씨에게 알아들을 만한 것이었을까?"], ["Thornton, who might already, for aught she knew, be aware of the old accusation against Frederick, through their mutual friend, Mr. Bell?", "그는 두 사람의 공통된 벗인 벨 씨를 통해 프레더릭에게 걸린 옛 혐의를 이미 알고 있을지도 모르지 않는가?"], ["If so, he had striven to save the son, who came in defiance of the law to attend his mother’s death-bed.", "그렇다면 그는 어머니의 임종을 지키려고 법을 어기고 돌아온 아들을 구해 주려 애쓴 셈이었다. 그런 생각이라면 그녀도 고마움을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["And under this idea she could feel grateful—not yet, if ever she should, if his interference had been prompted by contempt. Oh!", "그러나 그의 개입이 경멸에서 나온 것이라면, 언젠가는 몰라도 아직은 아니었다."], ["had any one just cause to feel contempt for her? Mr.", "아, 자기를 경멸할 만한 정당한 까닭이 누구엔들 있었단 말인가?"], ["Thornton, above all people, on whom she had looked down from her imaginary heights till now!", "하필 손턴 씨에게라니. 지금까지 자기가 상상 속의 높은 자리에서 내려다보던 바로 그 사람에게!"], ["She suddenly found herself at his feet, and was strangely distressed at her fall.", "문득 그녀는 자기가 그의 발밑에 서 있음을 깨달았고, 자기가 떨어진 그 자리에 야릇하게도 마음이 아팠다."], ["She shrank from following out the premises to their conclusion, and so acknowledging to herself how much she valued his respect and good opinion.", "그녀는 이 전제를 끝까지 밀고 가서 그의 존중과 좋은 평판을 자기가 얼마나 소중히 여기는지 스스로 인정하는 데까지 나아가기를 움츠러들며 피했다."], ["Whenever this idea presented itself to her at the end of a long avenue of thoughts, she turned away from following that path—she would not believe in it.", "긴 생각의 오솔길 끝에서 이 생각이 떠오를 때마다 그녀는 그 길을 따라가기를 그만두고 돌아섰다. 그녀는 그것을 믿으려 하지 않았다."]], [["It was later than she fancied, for in the agitation of the previous night, she had forgotten to wind up her watch; and Mr.", "그녀가 짐작한 것보다 시간이 늦어 있었다."], ["Hale had given especial orders that she was not to be disturbed by the usual awakening.", "전날 밤의 소란 통에 시계 태엽 감는 것을 잊었기 때문이고, 헤일 씨가 여느 때처럼 깨우지 말라고 각별히 일러 두었기 때문이다."], ["By and by the door opened cautiously, and Dixon put her head in.", "이윽고 문이 조심스레 열리며 딕슨이 고개를 들이밀었다."], ["Perceiving that Margaret was awake, she came forwards with a letter.", "마거릿이 깨어 있는 것을 보고 그녀는 편지 한 통을 들고 앞으로 나왔다."]], [["“Here’s something to do you good, miss.", "“아가씨한테 약이 될 게 왔어요."], ["A letter from Master Frederick.”", "프레더릭 도련님한테서 온 편지랍니다.”"]], [["“Thank you, Dixon.", "“고마워요, 딕슨."], ["How late it is!”", "시간이 이렇게나 됐네!”"]], [["She spoke very languidly, and suffered Dixon to lay it on the counterpane before her, without putting out a hand to take it.", "그녀는 몹시 힘없이 말했고, 편지를 받으려 손을 내밀지도 않은 채 딕슨이 그것을 침대보 위에 놓아두게 두었다."]], [["“You want your breakfast, I’m sure.", "“아침을 드셔야죠, 암요."], ["I will bring it you in a minute.", "곧 가져다 드릴게요."], ["Master has got the tray all ready, I know.”", "주인어른께서 쟁반을 다 차려 놓으신 걸 제가 압니다요.”"]], [["Margaret did not reply; she let her go; she felt that she must be alone before she could open that letter.", "마거릿은 대답하지 않았다."], ["She opened it at last.", "그녀는 딕슨을 그대로 내보냈다."], ["The first thing that caught her eye was the date two days earlier than she received it.", "저 편지를 열어 보려면 혼자여야 한다는 느낌이 들었다. 마침내 그녀는 편지를 열었다."], ["He had then written when he had promised, and their alarm might have been spared.", "맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 받은 날보다 이틀이나 앞선 날짜였다. 그렇다면 오빠는 약속한 대로 편지를 썼던 것이고, 그 놀람과 두려움은 겪지 않아도 되었을 것이었다."], ["But she would read the letter and see.", "그러나 어쨌든 읽어 보아야 알 일이었다."], ["It was hasty enough, but perfectly satisfactory.", "편지는 급히 쓴 티가 났지만 더없이 안심되는 내용이었다. 오빠는 헨리 레녹스를 만났다."], ["He had seen Henry Lennox, who knew enough of the case to shake his head over it, in the first instance, and tell him he had done a very daring thing in returning to England, with such an accusation, backed by such powerful influence, hanging over him. But when they had come to talk it over, Mr.", "레녹스는 이 사건을 꽤 알고 있어서 처음에는 고개를 저으며, 그런 혐의가, 그것도 그토록 막강한 세력의 뒷받침을 받는 혐의가 걸려 있는 몸으로 영국에 돌아온 것은 참으로 무모한 짓이라고 말했다."], ["Lennox had acknowledged that there might be some chance of his acquittal, if he could but prove his statements by credible witnesses—that in such case it might be worth while to stand his trial, otherwise it would be a great risk.", "그러나 이야기를 나누다 보니 레녹스 씨도, 믿을 만한 증인들로 진술을 입증할 수만 있다면 무죄로 풀려날 가망이 얼마쯤은 있으리라고 인정했다. 그런 경우라면 재판을 받아 볼 만하겠지만, 그러지 못한다면 큰 모험이 되리라는 것이었다."], ["He would examine—he would take every pains.", "그는 알아보겠다고, 온갖 수고를 아끼지 않겠다고 했다."], ["“It struck me,”", "“내 짐작에는,”"], ["said Frederick, “that your introduction, little sister of mine, went a long way.", "프레더릭은 이렇게 썼다. “귀여운 내 동생아, 네 소개가 큰 몫을 한 것 같더구나."], ["Is it so?", "그런 게냐?"], ["He made so many inquiries, I can assure you.", "정말이지 그 사람이 어찌나 이것저것 묻던지."], ["He seemed a sharp, intelligent fellow, and in good practice too, to judge from the signs of business and the number of clerks about him.", "날카롭고 영리한 사람 같았고, 사무실의 분주한 기색이며 서기들 수로 보아 일도 잘 풀리는 모양이더라."], ["But these may be only lawyer’s dodges.", "하기야 이런 건 변호사의 술수일 뿐인지도 모르지."], ["I have just caught a packet on the point of sailing—I am off in five minutes.", "마침 막 떠나려는 배편을 잡았다. 오 분 뒤면 떠난다."], ["I may have to come back to England again on this business, so keep my visit secret.", "이 일 때문에 다시 영국에 와야 할지도 모르니 내가 다녀갔다는 것은 비밀로 해 다오."], ["I shall send my father some rare old sherry, such as you cannot buy in England (such stuff as I’ve got in the bottle before me)!", "아버지께는 영국에서는 살 수 없는 진귀한 옛 셰리주를 보내 드리마(지금 내 앞에 놓인 병에 든 그런 것 말이다)! 아버지께는 그런 게 필요하시다."], ["He needs something of the kind—my dear love to him—God bless him.", "사랑을 담아 안부 전해 다오. 하느님의 축복이 함께하시기를."], ["I’m sure—here’s my cab.", "정말이지—아, 마차가 왔구나."], ["P.S.—What an escape that was!", "추신. 참으로 아슬아슬했지!"], ["Take care you don’t breathe of my having been—not even to the Shaws.”", "내가 다녀갔다는 말은 절대 입 밖에 내지 마라. 쇼 이모네에도 말이다.”"]], [["Margaret turned to the envelope: it was marked “Too late.”", "마거릿은 봉투를 살펴보았다. 봉투에는 “발송 지연”이라는 표시가 찍혀 있었다."], ["The letter had probably been trusted to some careless waiter, who had forgotten to post it.", "아마 어느 덤벙대는 급사에게 맡겼는데 그 사람이 부치는 것을 잊은 모양이었다."], ["Oh! what slight cobwebs of chances stand between us and Temptation!", "아, 우리와 유혹 사이를 가로막고 선 우연이란 얼마나 가느다란 거미줄인가!"], ["Frederick had been safe, and out of England twenty, nay, thirty hours ago; and it was only about seventeen hours since she had told a falsehood to baffle pursuit, which even then would have been vain.", "프레더릭은 스무 시간, 아니 서른 시간 전에 이미 무사히 영국을 떠나 있었다. 그런데 그녀가 추격을 따돌리려고 거짓말을 한 것은 겨우 열일곱 시간쯤 전이었으니, 그때도 이미 헛된 짓이었던 것이다."], ["How faithless she had been!", "그녀는 얼마나 믿음이 없었던가!"], ["Where now was her proud motto, “Fais ce que dois, advienne que pourra?”", "이제 “마땅히 할 바를 하라, 그 뒤는 될 대로 되게 하라”던 그 자랑스러운 좌우명은 어디로 갔는가?"], ["If she had but dared to bravely tell the truth as regarded herself, defying them to find out what she refused to tell concerning another, how light of heart she would now have felt!", "자기 일에 관한 한 용감하게 사실대로 말하고, 남에 관해 말하기를 거부하는 것은 알아낼 테면 알아내 보라고 맞섰더라면, 지금 그녀의 마음은 얼마나 가벼웠을 것인가!"], ["Not humbled before God, as having failed in trust towards Him; not degraded and abased in Mr. Thornton’s sight.", "하느님을 향한 믿음에 실패한 자로서 그분 앞에 낮추어질 일도 없었을 것이고, 손턴 씨의 눈에 비천해지고 욕될 일도 없었을 것이다."], ["She caught herself up at this with a miserable tremor; here was she classing his low opinion of her alongside with the displeasure of God.", "여기까지 생각하다 그녀는 비참한 떨림과 함께 스스로를 붙들었다. 자기가 그의 낮아진 평가를 하느님의 노여움과 나란히 놓고 있었던 것이다."], ["How was it that he haunted her imagination so persistently?", "어찌하여 그가 이토록 끈질기게 그녀의 마음을 떠나지 않는단 말인가?"], ["What could it be?", "대체 무엇 때문인가?"], ["Why did she care for what he thought, in spite of all her pride; in spite of herself?", "그 온갖 자존심에도 불구하고, 자기 자신에게 맞서면서까지, 어찌하여 그가 어떻게 생각하는지를 마음에 두는 것인가?"], ["She believed that she could have borne the sense of Almighty displeasure, because He knew all, and could read her penitence, and hear her cries for help in time to come.", "전능하신 분의 노여움이라면 견딜 수 있으리라고 그녀는 믿었다. 그분은 모든 것을 아시고, 그녀의 뉘우침을 읽으실 수 있으며, 앞으로 도움을 구하는 그녀의 부르짖음을 들으실 수 있으니까."], ["But Mr. Thornton—why did she tremble, and hide her face in the pillow?", "그러나 손턴 씨는—어찌하여 그녀는 떨면서 베개에 얼굴을 묻는 것인가?"], ["What strong feeling had overtaken her at last?", "마침내 어떤 거센 감정이 그녀를 사로잡은 것인가?"]], [["She sprang out of bed and prayed long and earnestly.", "그녀는 침대에서 뛰어내려 오래도록 간절히 기도했다."], ["It soothed and comforted her so to open her heart.", "그렇게 마음을 열고 나니 위로가 되고 마음이 가라앉았다."], ["But as soon as she reviewed her position she found the sting was still there; that she was not good enough, nor pure enough to be indifferent to the lowered opinion of a fellow creature: that the thought of how he must be looking upon her with contempt, stood between her and her sense of wrong-doing.", "그러나 자기 처지를 다시 돌아보자마자 가시가 여전히 거기 박혀 있음을 알았다. 자기는 한낱 사람의 낮아진 평가에 아랑곳하지 않을 만큼 착하지도, 순결하지도 못하다는 것."], ["She took her letter in to her father as soon as she was drest.", "그가 자기를 경멸하는 눈으로 보고 있으리라는 생각이 자기와 죄를 뉘우치는 마음 사이를 가로막고 서 있다는 것."], ["There was so slight an allusion to their alarm at the railroad station, that Mr.", "그녀는 옷을 갈아입자마자 편지를 들고 아버지에게 갔다."], ["Hale passed over it without paying any attention to it.", "역에서 겪은 놀람에 대한 언급이 하도 스치듯 짧아서 헤일 씨는 그 대목을 눈여겨보지 않고 지나쳤다."], ["Indeed, beyond the mere fact of Frederick having sailed undiscovered and unsuspected, he did not gather much from the letter at the time, he was so uneasy about Margaret’s pallid looks.", "사실 그때 그는 프레더릭이 들키지도 의심받지도 않고 배를 탔다는 사실 말고는 편지에서 별로 알아낸 것이 없었다. 마거릿의 핏기 없는 얼굴이 그토록 마음에 걸렸기 때문이다."], ["She seemed continually on the point of weeping.", "그녀는 자꾸만 금세라도 울음을 터뜨릴 것만 같아 보였다."]], [["“You are sadly overdone, Margaret.", "“너무 무리했구나, 마거릿."], ["It is no wonder.", "그럴 만도 하지."], ["But you must let me nurse you now.”", "하지만 이제는 내가 너를 돌보게 해 다오.”"]], [["He made her lie down on the sofa, and went for a shawl to cover her with.", "그는 딸을 소파에 눕히고 덮어 줄 숄을 가지러 갔다."], ["His tenderness released her tears; and she cried bitterly.", "아버지의 다정함에 눈물이 터져 나왔고, 그녀는 서럽게 울었다."]], [["“Poor child!—poor child!”", "“가엾은 것! 가엾은 것!”"], ["said he, looking fondly at her, as she lay with her face to the wall, shaking with her sobs.", "딸이 벽 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린 채 흐느낌으로 몸을 떠는 것을 애틋하게 바라보며 그가 말했다."], ["After a while they ceased, and she began to wonder whether she durst give herself the relief of telling her father of all her trouble.", "이윽고 울음이 그쳤고, 그녀는 자기 괴로움을 아버지에게 다 털어놓는 위안을 감히 누려도 될지 생각해 보기 시작했다. 그러나 그러지 말아야 할 이유가 그래야 할 이유보다 많았다."], ["But there were more reasons against it than for it.", "그래야 할 이유는 자기 마음이 가벼워진다는 것 하나뿐이었다."], ["The only one for it was the relief to herself; and against it was the thought that it would add materially to her father’s nervousness, if it were indeed necessary for Frederick to come to England again; that he would dwell on the circumstance of his son’s having caused the death of a man, however unwittingly and unwillingly; that this knowledge would perpetually recur to trouble him, in various shapes of exaggeration and distortion from the simple truth.", "그러지 말아야 할 이유로는, 만일 정말로 프레더릭이 다시 영국에 와야 한다면 아버지의 불안이 크게 더해지리라는 생각이 있었다. 아무리 모르고 한 일, 바라지 않은 일이라 해도 아들이 사람을 죽게 만들었다는 정황을 아버지는 곱씹을 것이었다."], ["And about her own great fault—he would be distressed beyond measure at her want of courage and faith, yet perpetually troubled to make excuses for her.", "그 앎이 단순한 사실에서 부풀려지고 뒤틀린 온갖 모습으로 끊임없이 되살아나 아버지를 괴롭힐 것이었다. 그리고 자기가 저지른 큰 잘못에 대해서는—아버지는 딸의 용기 없음과 믿음 없음에 한없이 마음 아파하면서도, 딸을 위해 변명거리를 찾느라 내내 애를 태울 것이었다."], ["Formerly Margaret would have come to him as priest as well as father, to tell him of her temptation and her sin; but latterly they had not spoken much on such subjects; and she knew not how, in his change of opinions, he would reply if the depth of her soul called unto his.", "예전 같았으면 마거릿은 아버지이자 사제인 그에게 가서 자기가 받은 유혹과 자기 죄를 말했을 것이다. 그러나 근래에는 그런 문제로 이야기를 나눈 적이 별로 없었고, 생각이 바뀐 그가 자기 영혼의 깊은 곳이 그의 영혼을 부를 때 무어라 답할지 그녀는 알 수 없었다."], ["No; she would keep her secret, and bear the burden alone.", "아니, 그녀는 자기 비밀을 지키고 그 짐을 홀로 지리라."], ["Alone she would go before God, and cry for His absolution.", "홀로 하느님 앞에 나아가 용서를 구하리라."], ["Alone she would endure her disgraced position in the opinion of Mr.", "손턴 씨의 눈에 욕된 처지가 된 것도 홀로 견디리라."], ["Thornton. She was unspeakably touched by the tender efforts of her father to think of cheerful subjects on which to talk, and so to take her thoughts away from dwelling on all that had happened of late.", "그녀는 아버지가 즐거운 이야깃거리를 찾아내어 요즘 일어난 일들에서 딸의 생각을 돌려놓으려고 다정히 애쓰는 것에 말할 수 없이 마음이 움직였다."], ["It was some months since he had been so talkative as he was this day.", "아버지가 이날처럼 말이 많았던 것은 몇 달 만이었다."], ["He would not let her sit up, and offended Dixon desperately by insisting on waiting upon her himself.", "그는 딸을 일어나 앉게 두지 않았고, 손수 시중을 들겠다고 고집하여 딕슨의 비위를 몹시 상하게 했다."]], [["At last she smiled; a poor, weak little smile; but it gave him the truest pleasure.", "마침내 그녀가 미소를 지었다. 힘없고 가녀린 미소였으나 아버지에게는 더없는 기쁨이었다."]], [["“It seems strange to think, that what gives us most hope for the future should be called Dolores,”", "“우리에게 앞날의 희망을 가장 크게 주는 사람의 이름이 돌로레스라니, 생각하면 이상해요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["The remark was more in character with her father than with her usual self; but to-day they seemed to have changed natures.", "그것은 평소의 그녀보다 아버지에게 더 어울리는 말이었다. 그러나 그날은 두 사람의 성정이 뒤바뀐 듯했다."]], [["“Her mother was a Spaniard, I believe: that accounts for her religion.", "“그 애 어머니가 스페인 사람이었다더구나. 그래서 종교도 그런 게지."], ["Her father was a stiff Presbyterian when I knew him.", "아버지는 내가 알던 무렵에는 꼿꼿한 장로교도였다."], ["But it is a very soft and pretty name.”", "어쨌든 아주 부드럽고 예쁜 이름이야.”"]], [["“How young she is!—younger by fourteen months than I am.", "“참 어리네요! 저보다 열네 달이나 아래잖아요."], ["Just the age that Edith was when she was engaged to Captain Lennox.", "이디스가 레녹스 대위와 약혼했을 때가 딱 그 나이였어요."], ["Papa, we will go and see them in Spain.”", "아버지, 우리 스페인에 가서 그 사람들을 만나요.”"]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."], ["But he said, “If you wish it Margaret.", "그러나 이렇게 말했다. “네가 바란다면야, 마거릿."], ["Only let us come back here.", "다만 여기로 돌아오자꾸나."], ["It would seem unfair—unkind to your mother, who always, I’m afraid, disliked Milton so much, if we left it now she is lying here, and cannot go with us.", "네 어머니에게 온당치 못한 일, 매정한 일로 여겨질 게다. 아무래도 어머니는 늘 밀턴을 몹시 싫어했던 것 같지만, 그래도 어머니가 여기 누워 있고 우리와 함께 갈 수 없는데 지금 이곳을 떠난다면 말이다."], ["No, dear; you shall go and see them, and bring me back a report of my Spanish daughter.”", "아니다, 얘야. 너 혼자 가서 그들을 만나고 내 스페인 딸 이야기를 전해 다오.”"]], [["“No, papa, I won’t go without you.", "“아니에요, 아버지. 아버지 없이는 안 가요."], ["Who is to take care of you when I am gone?”", "제가 가면 누가 아버지를 돌보나요?”"]], [["“I should like to know which of us is taking care of the other.", "“우리 둘 중에 누가 누구를 돌보고 있는지 나도 알고 싶구나."], ["But if you went, I should persuade Mr. Thornton to let me give him double lessons.", "하지만 네가 간다면 손턴 씨를 졸라 수업을 갑절로 해 달라고 하마."], ["We would work up the classics famously.", "우리 둘이서 고전을 신나게 파고들 게다."], ["That would be a perpetual interest.", "그거야 언제까지고 재미있는 일이지."], ["You might go on, and see Edith at Corfu, if you liked.”", "원한다면 코르푸까지 가서 이디스도 만나고 오려무나.”"]], [["Margaret did not speak all at once.", "마거릿은 곧바로 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["Then she said rather gravely: “Thank you, papa.", "그러다 자못 진지하게 말했다. “고마워요, 아버지."], ["But I don’t want to go.", "하지만 저는 가고 싶지 않아요."], ["We will hope that Mr. Lennox will manage so well, that Frederick may bring Dolores to see us when they are married.", "레녹스 씨가 일을 잘 처리해서 오빠가 혼인한 뒤에 돌로레스를 데리고 우리를 보러 올 수 있게 되기를 바라요."], ["And as for Edith, the regiment won’t remain much longer in Corfu.", "이디스로 말하면, 연대가 코르푸에 그리 오래 머물지는 않을 거예요."], ["Perhaps we shall see both of them here before another year is out.”", "어쩌면 한 해가 가기 전에 두 사람 다 여기서 보게 될지도 모르죠.”"]], [["Mr. Hale’s cheerful subjects had come to an end.", "헤일 씨의 즐거운 이야깃거리는 바닥이 났다."], ["Some painful recollections had stolen across his mind, and driven him into silence.", "괴로운 기억들이 슬며시 마음을 스치고 지나가 그를 침묵으로 몰아넣었다."], ["By and by Margaret said:", "이윽고 마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“Papa—did you see Nicholas Higgins at the funeral?", "“아버지, 장례식에서 니컬러스 히긴스를 보셨어요?"], ["He was there, and Mary too.", "그 사람도 왔었고 메리도 왔었어요."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["it was his way of showing sympathy.", "그게 그 사람 나름의 조의 표시였던 거예요."], ["He has a good warm heart under his bluff abrupt ways.”", "무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 겉모습 아래 따뜻하고 좋은 마음을 지닌 사람이에요.”"]], [["“I am sure of it,”", "“나도 그렇게 믿는다.”"], ["replied Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I saw it all along, even while you tried to persuade me that he was all sorts of bad things.", "“네가 그 사람을 온갖 못된 사람으로 몰아붙이려 하던 때에도 나는 줄곧 그걸 알고 있었지."], ["We will go and see them to-morrow, if you are strong enough to walk so far.”", "네 몸이 그만큼 걸을 만큼 튼튼하다면 내일 가서 만나 보자꾸나.”"]], [["“Oh yes.", "“네, 좋아요."], ["I want to see them.", "저도 만나고 싶어요."], ["We did not pay Mary—or rather she refused to take it, Dixon says.", "메리에게 삯을 못 치렀어요. 아니, 딕슨 말로는 메리가 받지 않겠다고 했다더군요."], ["We will go so as to catch him just after his dinner, and before he goes to his work.”", "그 사람이 끼니를 든 뒤, 일 나가기 전에 딱 맞춰 가도록 해요.”"]], [["Towards evening Mr. Hale said:", "저녁 무렵 헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I half expected Mr. Thornton would have called.", "“손턴 씨가 들르지 않을까 반쯤 기대했다만."], ["He spoke of a book yesterday which he had, and which I wanted to see. He said he would try and bring it to-day.”", "어제 자기가 가진 책 이야기를 하기에 내가 보고 싶다고 했더니, 오늘 가져와 보겠다고 했거든.”"]], [["Margaret sighed.", "마거릿은 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["She knew he would not come.", "그녀는 그가 오지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He would be too delicate to run the chance of meeting her, while her shame must be so fresh in his memory.", "자기가 당한 수치가 그의 기억에 아직 그토록 생생할 텐데, 그는 너무나 조심스러운 사람이라 자기와 마주칠 위험을 무릅쓰지 않을 터였다."], ["The very mention of his name renewed her trouble, and produced a relapse into the feeling of depressed, pre-occupied exhaustion.", "그의 이름이 나오는 것만으로도 괴로움이 되살아났고, 그녀는 다시 짓눌린 채 무언가에 사로잡힌 듯한 탈진 상태로 빠져들었다."], ["She gave way to listless languor.", "그녀는 맥없는 나른함에 자기를 내맡겼다."], ["Suddenly it struck her that this was a strange manner to show her patience, or to reward her father for his watchful care of her all through the day.", "그러다 문득, 이것이야말로 인내를 보이는 데도, 하루 종일 자기를 살뜰히 보살펴 준 아버지에게 보답하는 데도 어울리지 않는 태도라는 생각이 들었다."], ["She sate up, and offered to read aloud.", "그녀는 몸을 일으켜 앉아 소리 내어 책을 읽어 드리겠다고 했다."], ["His eyes were failing, and he gladly accepted her proposal.", "아버지는 눈이 침침해진 터라 딸의 제안을 반갑게 받아들였다. 그녀는 잘 읽었다."], ["She read well: she gave the due emphasis; but had any one asked her, when she had ended, the meaning of what she had been reading, she could not have told.", "강약을 알맞게 주었다. 그러나 다 읽고 났을 때 누가 방금 읽은 것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었더라면 그녀는 대답하지 못했을 것이다."], ["She was smitten with a feeling of ingratitude to Mr.", "그녀는 손턴 씨에게 은혜를 모르는 사람이 되었다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다."], ["Thornton, inasmuch as, in the morning, she had refused to accept the kindness he had shown her in making further inquiry from the medical men, so as to obviate any inquest being held.", "아침에 그녀는, 검시가 열리지 않도록 의사들에게 다시 알아봐 준 그의 친절을 받아들이기를 마다했던 것이다."], ["Oh! she was grateful!", "아, 그녀는 고마웠다!"], ["She had been cowardly and false, and had shown her cowardliness and falsehood in action that could not be recalled; but she was not ungrateful.", "자기는 비겁하고 거짓되었으며, 그 비겁함과 거짓됨을 돌이킬 수 없는 행동으로 드러내고 말았다. 그러나 은혜를 모르는 사람은 아니었다."], ["It sent a glow to her heart, to know she could feel towards one who had reason to despise her.", "자기를 경멸할 만한 까닭이 있는 사람에게 이런 마음을 품을 수 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 그녀의 가슴은 뜨거워졌다."], ["His cause for contempt was so just, that she should have respected him less if she had thought he did not feel contempt.", "그가 경멸할 까닭은 너무나 정당해서, 만일 그가 경멸을 느끼지 않는다고 생각했다면 그녀는 그를 덜 존경했을 것이다."], ["It was a pleasure to feel how thoroughly she respected him.", "자기가 그를 얼마나 온전히 존경하는지를 느끼는 것은 기쁨이었다."], ["He could not prevent her doing that; it was the one comfort in all this misery.", "그것만은 그가 막을 수 없었다. 그것이 이 모든 비참함 속에서 단 하나의 위안이었다."]], [["Late in the evening, the expected book arrived, “with Mr.", "저녁 늦게 기다리던 책이 도착했다."], ["Thornton’s kind regards, and wishes to know how Mr.", "“손턴 씨가 안부를 전하며 헤일 씨께서 어떠하신지 여쭙습니다”"], ["Hale is.”", "하는 말과 함께였다."]], [["“Say that I am much better, Dixon, but that Miss Hale—”", "“훨씬 나아졌다고 전해 주게, 딕슨. 다만 헤일 양이——”"]], [["“No, papa,”", "“아니에요, 아버지.”"], ["said Margaret, eagerly—“don’t say anything about me.", "마거릿이 다급하게 말했다. “제 이야기는 하지 마세요."], ["He does not ask.”", "저를 묻지도 않았잖아요.”"]], [["“My dear child, how you are shivering!”", "“얘야, 이렇게 떨고 있구나!”"], ["said her father, a few minutes afterwards.", "몇 분 뒤 아버지가 말했다."], ["“You must go to bed directly.", "“당장 자리에 들어야겠다."], ["You have turned quite pale!”", "얼굴이 아주 새하얘졌어!”"]], [["Margaret did not refuse to go, though she was loth to leave her father alone.", "마거릿은 아버지를 혼자 두기가 마음에 걸리기는 했으나 가기를 마다하지 않았다."], ["She needed the relief of solitude after a day of busy thinking, and busier repenting.", "하루 종일 분주히 생각하고 더 분주히 뉘우친 끝에 홀로 있는 위안이 필요했다."]], [["But she seemed much as usual the next day; the lingering gravity and sadness, and the occasional absence of mind, were not unnatural symptoms in the early days of grief.", "그러나 이튿날 그녀는 여느 때와 별로 다르지 않아 보였다. 가시지 않은 무거움과 슬픔, 이따금씩의 넋 나감은 상을 당한 지 얼마 안 된 사람에게 이상할 것 없는 기색이었다."], ["And almost in proportion to her re-establishment in health, was her father’s relapse into his abstracted musings upon the wife he had lost, and the past era in his life that was closed to him for ever.", "그리고 그녀가 건강을 되찾는 것과 거의 나란히, 아버지는 잃어버린 아내와 그에게 영영 닫혀 버린 인생의 지난 시절에 대한 넋 나간 상념 속으로 도로 빠져들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI", "# 제36장"]], [["“The steps of the bearers, heavy and slow, The sobs of the mourners, deep and low.”", "“관을 멘 이들의 걸음은 무겁고 더디며, 애도하는 이들의 흐느낌은 깊고 나직하다.”"], ["SHELLEY.", "— 셸리."]], [["At the time arranged the previous day, they set out on their walk to see Nicholas Higgins and his daughter.", "전날 정해 둔 시각에 두 사람은 니컬러스 히긴스와 그의 딸을 만나러 걸어서 길을 나섰다."], ["They both were reminded of their recent loss, by a strange kind of shyness in their new habiliments, and in the fact that it was the first time, for many weeks, that they had deliberately gone out together.", "새로 지어 입은 상복이 어쩐지 낯설고 서먹한 데다, 여러 주 만에 처음으로 마음먹고 함께 밖에 나선 것이어서, 둘은 얼마 전의 상실을 새삼 떠올렸다."], ["They drew very close to each other in unspoken sympathy.", "그들은 말 없는 연민 속에서 서로에게 바싹 붙어 걸었다."]], [["Nicholas was sitting by the fire-side in his accustomed corner; but he had not his accustomed pipe.", "니컬러스는 늘 앉던 구석 자리 난롯가에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 늘 물던 파이프는 물고 있지 않았다."], ["He was leaning his head upon his hand, his arm resting on his knee.", "그는 한 손에 머리를 괸 채 팔을 무릎에 얹고 있었다."], ["He did not get up when he saw them, though Margaret could read the welcome in his eye.", "두 사람을 보고도 일어서지 않았지만, 마거릿은 그의 눈에 어린 반가움을 읽을 수 있었다."]], [["“Sit ye down, sit ye down.", "“앉으슈, 앉으슈."], ["Fire’s welly out,”", "불이 다 죽었구먼.”"], ["said he, giving it a vigorous poke, as if to turn attention away from himself.", "그가 이렇게 말하며 불을 힘차게 쑤셨다. 마치 자기에게서 눈길을 돌려놓으려는 듯이."], ["He was rather disorderly, to be sure, with a black unshaven beard of several days’ growth, making his pale face look yet paler, and a jacket which would have been all the better for patching.", "과연 그의 몰골은 어수선했다. 며칠씩 깎지 않은 시커먼 수염이 창백한 얼굴을 한층 더 창백해 보이게 했고, 웃옷은 기워 입는 편이 훨씬 나을 지경이었다."]], [["“We thought we should have a good chance of finding you, just after dinner-time,”", "“식사 때가 막 지난 참이면 계실 것 같아서 왔어요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["“We have had our sorrows too, since we saw you,”", "“우리도 자네를 본 뒤로 슬픈 일을 겪었네.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay.", "“예, 예."], ["Sorrows is more plentiful than dinners just now; I reckon, my dinner hour stretches all o’er the day; yo’re pretty sure of finding me.”", "요즘은 슬픔이 끼니보다 흔하지요. 내 끼니때는 하루 온종일 늘어져 있으니, 언제 오셔도 나를 만나기는 어렵잖을 거요.”"]], [["“Are you out of work?”", "“일자리를 잃으셨어요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Ay,”", "“그렇소.”"], ["he replied shortly.", "그가 짧게 대답했다."], ["Then, after a moment’s silence, he added, looking up for the first time: “I’m not wanting brass.", "그러고는 잠시 잠잠하다가 처음으로 고개를 들며 덧붙였다. “돈이 궁한 건 아니오."], ["Dunno yo’ think it.", "그리 생각지 마슈."], ["Bess, poor lass, had a little stock under her pillow, ready to slip into my hand, last moment, and Mary is fustian-cutting.", "베스가, 가엾은 것이, 마지막 순간에 내 손에 쥐여 주려고 베개 밑에 조금 모아 두었더랬고, 메리는 퍼스티언 천을 자르고 있으니까."], ["But I’m out of work a’ the same.”", "그래도 일자리가 없는 건 매한가지지만.”"]], [["“We owe Mary some money,” said Mr.", "“우리가 메리에게 갚을 돈이 좀 있네.”"], ["Hale, before Margaret’s sharp pressure on his arm could arrest the words.", "마거릿이 팔을 세게 눌러 그 말을 막기도 전에 헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“If hoo takes it, I’ll turn her out o’ doors.", "“그 애가 그걸 받는 날엔 문밖으로 내쫓아 버리겠소."], ["I’ll bide inside these four walls, and she’ll bide out.", "나는 이 네 벽 안에 있고 그 애는 밖에 있게 될 거요."], ["That’s a’.”", "그뿐이오.”"]], [["“But we owe her many thanks for her kind service,”", "“하지만 그 애의 고마운 수고에 우리는 큰 신세를 졌는데.”"], ["began Mr. Hale again.", "헤일 씨가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“I ne’er thanken your daughter theer for her deeds o’ love to my poor wench.", "“나는 저기 댁의 따님이 내 가엾은 딸년한테 베푼 사랑에 고맙단 말 한마디 한 적이 없소."], ["I ne’er could find th’ words.", "도무지 말이 떠오르질 않더구먼."], ["I’se have to begin to try now, if yo’ start making an ado about what little Mary could sarve yo’.”", "메리가 댁들한테 해 드린 그 하찮은 일로 이러쿵저러쿵 소란을 피우실 양이면, 나도 이제부터 그 말을 찾아보기 시작해야겠소.”"]], [["“Is it because of the strike you’re out of work?”", "“파업 때문에 일자리를 잃으신 건가요?”"], ["asked Margaret gently.", "마거릿이 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["“Strike’s ended.", "“파업은 끝났소."], ["It’s o’er for this time.", "이번 판은 다 끝난 거요."], ["I’m out o’ work because I ne’er asked for it.", "내가 일자리가 없는 건 달라고 하질 않았기 때문이오."], ["And I ne’er asked for it, because good words is scarce, and bad words is plentiful.”", "그리고 달라고 하지 않은 건, 좋은 말은 귀하고 나쁜 말은 흔해 빠졌기 때문이고.”"]], [["He was in a mood to take a surly pleasure in giving answers that were like riddles.", "그는 수수께끼 같은 대답을 내놓으며 뿌루퉁한 즐거움을 맛보는 기분이었다."], ["But Margaret saw that he would like to be asked for the explanation.", "그러나 마거릿은 그가 그 뜻을 물어봐 주기를 바란다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“And good words are—?”", "“좋은 말이란 건……?”"]], [["“Asking for work.", "“일 좀 달라고 하는 거요."], ["I reckon them’s almost the best words that men can say.", "사람이 입에 담을 수 있는 말 중에 그만한 게 또 없지 싶소."], ["‘Gi’ me work’ means ‘and I’ll do it like a man.’ Them’s good words.”", "‘일을 주슈’ 하는 건 ‘사내답게 해내겠소’ 하는 뜻이니까. 그게 좋은 말이오.”"]], [["“And bad words are refusing you work when you ask for it.”", "“그러면 나쁜 말이란, 그렇게 청하는데 일을 주지 않는 것이겠네요.”"]], [["“Ay.", "“그렇소."], ["Bad words is saying ‘Aha, my fine chap!", "나쁜 말이란 이런 거요. ‘아하, 이 잘난 친구!"], ["Yo’ve been true to yo’r order, and I’ll be true to mine.", "자네는 자네 패거리한테 의리를 지켰으니, 나는 내 패거리한테 의리를 지키겠네."], ["Yo’ did the best yo’ could for them as wanted help; that’s yo’re way of being true to yo’re kind: and I’ll be true to mine.", "자네는 도움이 필요한 자들을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 다 했지. 그게 자네가 자네 부류한테 의리를 지키는 방식이렷다."], ["Yo’ve been a poor fool, as knowed no better nor be a true faithful fool.", "그러니 나도 내 부류한테 의리를 지키겠네. 자네는 더 나은 걸 몰라 우직하게 충실한 바보 노릇이나 한 딱한 바보야."], ["So go and be d—d to yo’.", "그러니 썩 꺼져 버리게."], ["There’s no work for yo’ here.’ Them’s bad words.", "여기 자네한테 줄 일은 없네.’ 그게 나쁜 말이오."], ["I’m not a fool; and if I was, folk ought to ha’ taught me how to be wise after their fashion.", "나는 바보가 아니외다. 설령 바보였다 한들, 사람들이 저희 방식대로 똑똑해지는 법을 나한테 가르쳤어야지."], ["I could m’appen ha’ learnt, if any one had tried to teach me.”", "누구든 가르쳐 보려고나 했으면 나도 배웠을지 모를 일 아니오.”"]], [["“Would it not be worth while,” said Mr.", "“옛 주인에게 다시 받아 달라고 청해 보는 것이 그래도 해 볼 만한 일 아니겠나?”"], ["Hale, “to ask your old master if he would take you back again?", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["It might be a poor chance, but it would be a chance.”", "“가망이야 적겠지만, 그래도 가망은 가망이니.”"]], [["He looked up again, with a sharp glance at the questioner; and then tittered a low and bitter laugh.", "그는 다시 고개를 들어 묻는 이를 날카롭게 흘긋 보더니, 나직하고 씁쓸한 웃음을 흘렸다."]], [["“Measter!", "“선생."], ["if it’s no offence, I’ll ask yo’ a question or two in my turn.”", "언짢게 여기지 않으신다면, 이번엔 내가 한두 가지 여쭤 보겠소.”"]], [["“You’re quite welcome,”", "“얼마든지 물어보게.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I reckon yo’n some way of earning your bread.", "“선생도 밥벌이할 방도가 있으실 것 아니오."], ["Folk seldom lives i’ Milton just for pleasure, if they can live anywhere else.”", "다른 데서 살 수 있다면야, 그저 좋아서 밀턴에 사는 사람은 드무니까.”"]], [["“You are quite right.", "“자네 말이 옳네."], ["I have some independent property, but my intention in settling in Milton was to become a private tutor.”", "따로 물려받은 재산이 좀 있기는 하네만, 내가 밀턴에 자리를 잡은 것은 개인 교사가 되기 위해서였네.”"]], [["“To teach folk.", "“사람을 가르친다."], ["Well!", "흠!"], ["I reckon they pay yo’ for teaching them, dunnot they?”", "그럼 가르쳐 준 값으로 돈을 받으시겠구려, 안 그렇소?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇지.”"], ["replied Mr. Hale, smiling.", "헤일 씨가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["“I teach in order to get paid.”", "“돈을 받으려고 가르치는 걸세.”"]], [["“And them that pays yo’, dun they tell yo’ whatten to do, or whatten not to do wi’ the money they gives you in just payment for your pains—in fair exchange like?”", "“그럼 그 돈을 주는 자들이, 수고한 값으로 정당하게, 말하자면 공평한 맞바꿈으로 준 그 돈을 가지고 뭘 해라 뭘 하지 마라 이래라저래라 합디까?”"]], [["“No; to be sure not!”", "“아니, 그럴 리가 있나!”"]], [["“They dunnot say, ‘Yo’ may have a brother, or a friend as dear as a brother, who wants this here brass for a purpose both yo’ and he think right; but yo’ mun promise not give it to him.", "“‘당신한테 형제가, 아니면 형제만큼이나 소중한 벗이 있어서 그 사람이 이 돈을 어떤 일에 쓰려 하고 당신도 그 사람도 그게 옳다고 여긴다 칩시다. 하지만 그 사람한테는 주지 않겠다고 약조하시오."], ["Yo’ may see a good use, as yo’ think, to put yo’r money to; but we don’t think it good, and so if yo’ spend it a-thatens we’ll just leave off dealing with yo’.’ They dunnot say that, dun they?”", "당신 눈엔 그 돈을 쓸 좋은 데가 보일지 몰라도, 우리 눈엔 그게 좋아 보이질 않소. 그러니 그런 식으로 쓴다면 우리는 당신과 거래를 끊겠소.’ 이렇게 말하진 않는단 말이지요, 그렇지 않소?”"]], [["“No: to be sure not!”", "“물론 그럴 리가 없지!”"]], [["“Would yo’ stand it if they did?”", "“만약 그렇게 한다면 참고 견디시겠소?”"]], [["“It would be some very hard pressure that would make me even think of submitting to such dictation.”", "“그런 지시에 따를 생각이라도 하게 되려면 어지간히 심한 압박이 있어야 할 걸세.”"]], [["“There’s not the pressure on all the broad earth that would make me,”", "“이 넓은 땅덩이 어디에도 나를 그렇게 만들 압박은 없소.”"], ["said Nicholas Higgins.", "니컬러스 히긴스가 말했다."], ["“Now yo’ve got it.", "“자, 이제 아셨구려."], ["Yo’ve hit the bull’s eye.", "정통으로 맞히셨소."], ["Hampers—that’s where I worked—makes their men pledge ’emselves they’ll not give a penny to help th’ Union or keep turn-outs fro’ clemming.", "햄퍼네가—내가 일하던 데요—직공들한테 조합을 돕거나 파업한 사람들이 굶주리지 않게 하는 데 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고 서약을 시킨단 말이오."], ["They may pledge and make pledge,”", "서약이야 시키든 말든.”"], ["continued he, scornfully; “they nobbut make liars and hypocrites.", "그가 비웃듯 말을 이었다. “거짓말쟁이하고 위선자를 만들어 낼 뿐이지."], ["And that’s a less sin, to my mind, to make men’s hearts so hard that they’ll not do a kindness to them as needs it, or help on the right and just cause, though it goes again the strong hand.", "그리고 그건 내 생각엔 그래도 작은 죄요. 사람 마음을 모질게 굳혀서 도움이 필요한 이한테 인정 한번 못 베풀게 하고, 힘센 손에 거스른다는 이유로 옳고 바른 일에 손을 못 보태게 하는 것에 견주면 말이오."], ["But I’ll ne’er forswear mysel’ for a’ the work the king could gi’e me.", "하지만 나는 임금님이 주실 수 있는 일자리를 죄다 준다 해도 내 맹세를 저버리진 않겠소."], ["I’m a member o’ the Union; and I think it’s the only thing to do the workmen any good.", "나는 조합원이오. 그리고 노동자한테 이로운 건 그것뿐이라고 생각하오."], ["And I’ve been a turn-out, and known what it was to clem; so if I get a shilling, sixpence shall go to them if they axe it from me.", "나도 파업을 해 봤고 굶주림이 어떤 건지도 겪어 봤소. 그러니 1실링이 생기면 그중 6펜스는 달라는 사람한테 갈 거요."], ["Consequence is, I dunnot see where I’m to get a shilling.”", "그러다 보니 1실링을 어디서 벌지 알 수가 없게 된 거지.”"]], [["“Is that rule about not contributing to the Union in force at all the mills?”", "“조합에 돈을 내지 못하게 하는 그 규칙이 모든 공장에서 시행되고 있나요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“I cannot say.", "“그건 모르겠소."], ["It’s a new regulation at ours; and I reckon they’ll find that they cannot stick to it.", "우리 공장에선 새로 생긴 규정이오. 그리고 내가 보기엔 그자들도 그걸 끝까지 밀고 가진 못할 거요."], ["But it’s in force now.", "하지만 지금은 시행 중이지."], ["By-and-by they’ll find out, tyrants makes liars.”", "머잖아 알게 되겠지, 폭군이 거짓말쟁이를 만든다는 걸.”"]], [["There was a little pause.", "잠깐 말이 끊겼다."], ["Margaret was hesitating whether she should say what was in her mind; she was unwilling to irritate one who was always gloomy and despondent enough.", "마거릿은 마음에 있는 말을 해야 할지 망설였다. 언제나 충분히 우울하고 낙담해 있는 사람의 심기를 건드리고 싶지 않았던 것이다."], ["At last out it came.", "마침내 말이 나오고 말았다."], ["But in her soft tones, and with her reluctant manner, shewing that she was unwilling to say anything unpleasant, it did not seem to annoy Higgins, only to perplex him.", "그러나 그 부드러운 어조와 머뭇거리는 태도가 언짢은 말은 하고 싶지 않다는 마음을 드러내 보였으므로, 히긴스는 화가 나기보다 그저 어리둥절해하는 듯했다."]], [["“Do you remember poor Boucher saying that the Union was a tyrant?", "“가엾은 바우처가 조합이 폭군이라고 하던 말 기억하세요?"], ["I think he said it was the worse tyrant of all.", "그중에서도 가장 나쁜 폭군이라고 했던 것 같아요."], ["And I remember, at the time I agreed with him.”", "그리고 그때 저도 그 말에 동의했던 게 기억나요.”"]], [["It was a long while before he spoke.", "그가 입을 열기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["He was resting his head on his two hands, and looking down into the fire, so she could not read the expression on his face.", "그는 두 손에 머리를 괸 채 불 속을 내려다보고 있어서, 그녀는 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 읽을 수 없었다."]], [["“I’ll not deny but what th’ Union finds it necessary to force a man into his own good.", "“조합이 사람을 억지로 제 이익 쪽으로 몰아붙이는 걸 필요하다고 여긴다는 건 나도 부인하지 않겠소."], ["I’ll speak truth.", "곧이곧대로 말하리다."], ["A man leads a dree life who’s not i’ th’ Union.", "조합에 들지 않은 사람은 고달픈 삶을 살지요."], ["But once i’ th’ Union, his interests are taken care on better nor he could do it for himsel’, or by himsel’, for that matter.", "하지만 일단 조합에 들면, 저 혼자 제 앞가림을 하는 것보다, 아니 제 힘만으로 하는 것보다 훨씬 낫게 제 몫을 챙기게 되오."], ["It’s the only way working men can get their rights, by all joining together.", "노동자가 제 권리를 얻는 길은 그것뿐이오. 다 함께 뭉치는 것 말이오."], ["More the members, more chance for each one separate man having justice done him.", "조합원이 많을수록 사람 하나하나가 정당한 대접을 받을 가망도 커지지."], ["Government takes care o’ fools and madmen; and if any man is inclined to do himsel’ or his neighbour a hurt, it puts a bit of a check on him, whether he likes it or no.", "나라에서도 바보와 미친 사람을 돌보지 않소. 그리고 누가 저 자신이나 이웃을 해치려 들면, 좋든 싫든 고삐를 채우지 않소."], ["That’s all we do i’ th’ Union.", "조합에서 하는 일도 그게 다요."], ["We can’t clap folk into prison; but we can make a man’s life so heavy to be borne, that he’s obliged to come in, and be wise and helpful in spite of himsel’.", "우리는 사람을 옥에 처넣을 수야 없지. 하지만 한 사람의 삶을 견디기 어려울 만큼 무겁게 만들어서, 제 뜻과 상관없이 들어와 슬기롭고 쓸모 있는 사람이 되게 할 수는 있소."], ["Boucher were a fool all along, and ne’er a worse fool than at th’ last.”", "바우처는 처음부터 끝까지 바보였고, 마지막이 제일 지독한 바보짓이었소.”"]], [["“He did you harm?”", "“그가 여러분에게 해를 끼쳤나요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Aye, that did he.", "“그렇소, 끼쳤소."], ["We had public opinion on our side, till he and his sort began rioting and breaking laws.", "그자와 그 패거리가 소동을 벌이고 법을 어기기 전까지는 여론이 우리 편이었소."], ["It were all o’er wi’ the strike then.”", "그때 파업은 다 끝장이 난 거요.”"]], [["“Then would it not have been far better to have left him alone, and not forced him to join the Union?", "“그렇다면 그를 그냥 내버려 두고 억지로 조합에 들이지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫지 않았을까요?"], ["He did you no good; and you drove him mad.”", "그는 여러분에게 아무 보탬도 되지 않았고, 여러분은 그를 미치게 만들었잖아요.”"]], [["“Margaret,”", "“마거릿.”"], ["said her father, in a low and warning tone, for he saw the cloud gathering on Higgins’s face.", "아버지가 낮고 조심스러운 목소리로 불렀다. 히긴스의 얼굴에 먹구름이 몰려드는 것을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["“I like her,” said Higgins, suddenly.", "“나는 이 아가씨가 마음에 드오.”"], ["“Hoo speaks plain out what’s in her mind.", "히긴스가 불쑥 말했다. “마음에 있는 걸 툭 터놓고 말하니 말이오."], ["Hoo doesn’t comprehend th’ Union for all that.", "그래도 조합이 뭔지는 모르지만. 그건 대단한 힘이오."], ["It’s a great power: it’s our only power.", "우리한테 있는 유일한 힘이지."], ["I ha’ read a bit o’ poetry about a plough going o’er a daisy, as made tears come into my eyes, afore I’d other cause for crying.", "쟁기가 데이지 꽃 위로 지나가는 이야기를 읊은 시를 조금 읽은 적이 있는데, 내가 울 다른 까닭이 생기기도 전에 눈물이 다 나옵디다."], ["But the chap ne’er stopped driving the plough, I’se warrant, for all he was pitiful about the daisy.", "그렇지만 그 사람도 데이지를 가엾어했을 뿐, 쟁기질을 멈추진 않았을 거요, 내 장담하리다."], ["He’d too much mother-wit for that.", "그럴 만큼 어리석진 않았을 테니."], ["Th’ Union’s the plough, making ready the land for harvest-time.", "조합이 그 쟁기요. 수확 때를 위해 땅을 갈아 놓는 거지."], ["Such as Boucher—’twould be settin’ him up too much to liken him to a daisy; he’s liker a weed lounging over the ground—mun just made up their mind to be put out o’ the way.", "바우처 같은 자는—데이지에 견주는 것도 너무 치켜세우는 거요. 그자는 땅바닥에 널브러진 잡초에 가깝지—그저 치워질 각오를 해야 하는 거요."], ["I’m sore vexed wi’ him just now.", "지금은 그자한테 심사가 아주 뒤틀려 있소."], ["So m’appen, I dunnot speak him fair.", "그러니 그자를 공평하게 말하고 있지 않은지도 모르지."], ["I could go o’er him wi’ a plough mysel’, wi’ a’ the pleasure in life.”", "나 같으면 신이 나서 그자 위로 쟁기를 몰고 지나갈 것 같소.”"]], [["“Why?", "“왜요?"], ["What has he been doing?", "그가 무슨 일을 했나요?"], ["Anything fresh?”", "또 무슨 일이 있었어요?”"]], [["“Ay, to be sure.", "“있고말고요."], ["He’s ne’er out o’ mischief, that man.", "그 인간은 사고를 안 치고는 못 배기지."], ["First of a’ he must go raging like a mad fool, and kick up yon riot.", "우선은 미친 바보처럼 날뛰며 그 소동을 일으켰소."], ["Then he’d to go into hiding, where he’d a been yet, if Thornton had followed him out as I’d hoped he would ha’ done.", "그러곤 숨어 지내야 했지. 손턴이 내 바람대로 끝까지 쫓았더라면 아직도 숨어 있었을 거요."], ["But Thornton, having got his own purpose, didn’t care to go on wi’ the prosecution for the riot.", "하지만 손턴은 제 목적을 이뤘으니 폭동 건으로 계속 기소할 마음이 없었던 게지."], ["So Boucher slunk back again to his house.", "그래서 바우처는 슬금슬금 제 집으로 기어 들어왔소."], ["He ne’er showed himsel’ abroad for a day or two.", "하루 이틀은 밖에 얼굴을 안 내밀더군."], ["He had that grace.", "그만한 염치는 있었던 거요."], ["And then, where think ye that he went?", "그러고는, 그자가 어디로 갔을 것 같소?"], ["Why, to Hampers’.", "아, 햄퍼네로 갔지 뭐요."], ["Damn him!", "빌어먹을 놈!"], ["He went wi’ his mealy-mouthed face, that turns me sick to look at, a-asking for work, though he knowed well enough the new rule, o’ pledging themselves to give nought to th’ Unions; nought to help the starving turn-out!", "보고만 있어도 속이 뒤집히는 그 굽실거리는 낯짝을 하고 가서 일을 달라고 했단 말이오. 조합에 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고, 굶주리는 파업자를 돕지 않겠다고 서약하는 새 규칙을 뻔히 알면서도!"], ["Why he’d a clemmed to death, if th’ Union had na helped him in his pinch.", "아니, 궁할 때 조합이 돕지 않았으면 그자는 굶어 죽었을 텐데! 그런 주제에 가서는 무슨 약조든 하겠다, 무슨 서약이든 하겠다, 우리가 하는 일을 죄다 일러바치겠다고 굽실거렸다지 뭐요."], ["There he went, ossing to promise aught, and pledge himsel’ to aught—to tell a’ he know’d on our proceedings, the good-for-nothing Judas!", "그 쓸모없는 유다 같은 놈! 하지만 햄퍼에 대해선 이 말만은 해 주겠고, 죽는 날까지 그 일로 고마워하겠소."], ["But I’ll say this for Hamper, and thank him for it at my dying day, he drove Boucher away, and would na listen to him—ne’er a word—though folk standing by, says the traitor cried like a babby!”", "그자가 바우처를 쫓아 버리고 한마디도 들어주지 않았다는 거요. 곁에 있던 사람들 말로는 그 배신자가 어린애처럼 울었다는데도 말이오!”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["how shocking!", "어쩌면 그렇게 끔찍한!"], ["how pitiful!”", "어쩌면 그렇게 딱한!”"], ["exclaimed Margaret.", "마거릿이 외쳤다."], ["“Higgins, I don’t know you to-day.", "“히긴스, 오늘은 당신을 모르겠어요."], ["Don’t you see how you’ve made Boucher what he is, by driving him into the Union against his will—without his heart going with it.", "바우처를 지금의 저 사람으로 만든 게 바로 당신들이라는 걸 모르시겠어요? 마음이 따라가지도 않는데 억지로 조합에 몰아넣어서 말이에요."], ["You have made him what he is!”", "당신들이 그를 지금의 저 사람으로 만든 거예요!”"]], [["Made him what he is!", "그를 지금의 저 사람으로 만들었다니!"], ["What was he?", "그는 지금 무엇이 되어 있었던가?"]], [["Gathering, gathering along the narrow street, came a hollow, measured sound; now forcing itself on their attention.", "좁은 거리를 따라 모여들고 또 모여들며, 공허하고 일정한 소리가 다가왔다. 이제는 그 소리가 억지로 그들의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Many voices were hushed and low: many steps were heard, not moving onwards, at least not with any rapidity or steadiness of motion, but as if circling round one spot.", "숱한 목소리가 낮게 잦아들어 있었고, 숱한 발소리가 들렸다. 앞으로 나아가는 것이 아니라, 적어도 빠르거나 고른 걸음으로 나아가는 것이 아니라, 한 지점을 빙 둘러싸고 도는 듯한 발소리였다."], ["Yes, there was one distinct, slow tramp of feet, which made itself a clear path through the air, and reached their ears; the measured laboured walk of men carrying a heavy burden.", "그렇다, 그중에는 또렷하고 느린 발소리가 하나 있어, 허공에 제 길을 훤히 내며 그들의 귀에 닿았다. 무거운 짐을 진 사람들의 일정하고 고된 걸음이었다."], ["They were all drawn towards the house-door by some irresistible impulse; impelled thither—not by a poor curiosity, but as if by some solemn blast.", "두 사람은 거역할 수 없는 어떤 충동에 이끌려 집 문간으로 나갔다. 하찮은 호기심 때문이 아니라, 무슨 엄숙한 나팔 소리에 떠밀리듯 그리로 나선 것이었다."]], [["Six men walked in the middle of the road, three of them being policemen.", "6명의 남자가 길 한가운데를 걸어오고 있었고, 그중 3명은 경관이었다."], ["They carried a door, taken off its hinges, upon their shoulders, on which lay some dead human creature; and from each side of the door there were constant droppings.", "그들은 돌쩌귀에서 떼어 낸 문짝 하나를 어깨에 메고 있었고, 그 위에는 어떤 주검이 놓여 있었다. 문짝 양옆에서는 끊임없이 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌다."], ["All the street turned out to see, and, seeing, to accompany the procession, each one questioning the bearers, who answered almost reluctantly at last, so often had they told the tale.", "거리의 사람들이 모두 쏟아져 나와 구경하고, 구경하다가는 그 행렬을 따라나섰다. 저마다 멘 이들에게 물었고, 그들은 하도 여러 번 이야기를 되풀이한 터라 나중에는 마지못한 듯 대답했다."]], [["“We found him i’ th’ brook in the field beyond there.”", "“저 너머 들판 개울에서 찾았소.”"]], [["“Th’ brook!—why there’s not water enough to drown him!”", "“개울이라니! 아니, 거기 사람이 빠져 죽을 만큼 물이 있나?”"]], [["“He was a determined chap.", "“독한 사람이었지."], ["He lay with his face downwards.", "얼굴을 아래로 하고 엎드려 있었소."], ["He was sick enough o’ living, choose what cause he had for it.”", "무슨 까닭이 있었든, 사는 게 어지간히 지겨웠던 게요.”"]], [["Higgins crept up to Margaret’s side, and said in a weak piping kind of voice: “It’s not John Boucher?", "히긴스가 마거릿 곁으로 슬금슬금 다가와 가늘게 새는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. “존 바우처는 아니겠지요?"], ["He had na spunk enough.", "그자는 그럴 배짱도 없었소."], ["Sure!", "아무렴!"], ["It’s not John Boucher!", "존 바우처일 리 없소!"], ["Why, they are a’ looking this way!", "아니, 다들 왜 이쪽을 보는 거요!"], ["Listen!", "들어 보슈!"], ["I’ve a singing in my head, and I cannot hear.”", "머릿속에서 웅웅 소리가 나서 들리질 않소.”"]], [["They put the door down carefully upon the stones, and all might see the poor drowned wretch—his glassy eyes, one half-open, staring right upwards to the sky.", "사람들이 문짝을 돌바닥에 조심스레 내려놓자, 물에 빠져 죽은 그 가엾은 사내가 모두의 눈에 들어왔다. 유리알 같은 두 눈은 한쪽이 반쯤 열린 채 곧장 하늘을 올려다보고 있었다."], ["Owing to the position in which he had been found lying, his face was swollen and discoloured; besides, his skin was stained by the water in the brook, which had been used for dyeing purposes.", "엎드린 자세로 발견된 탓에 얼굴은 부어오르고 빛이 변해 있었다. 게다가 염색에 쓰이던 개울물에 살갗이 물들어 있었다."], ["The fore part of his head was bald; but the hair grew thin and long behind, and every separate lock was a conduit for water.", "앞머리는 벗어졌고 뒷머리는 성글고 길게 자라 있어, 갈래갈래 늘어진 머리카락마다 물이 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["Through all these disfigurements, Margaret recognised John Boucher.", "그렇게 일그러진 모습 속에서도 마거릿은 존 바우처를 알아보았다."], ["It seemed to her so sacrilegious to be peering into that poor distorted, agonised face, that, by a flash of instinct, she went forwards and softly covered the dead man’s countenance with her handkerchief.", "그 가엾게 뒤틀리고 고통에 찬 얼굴을 들여다보는 것이 신성모독처럼 느껴져, 그녀는 문득 이는 마음결에 이끌려 앞으로 나아가 손수건으로 죽은 이의 얼굴을 가만히 덮어 주었다."], ["The eyes that saw her do this followed her, as she turned away from her pious office, and were thus led to the place where Nicholas Higgins stood, like one rooted to the spot.", "그녀가 그렇게 하는 것을 본 눈길들이, 그 경건한 소임을 마치고 돌아서는 그녀를 따라갔고, 그리하여 니컬러스 히긴스가 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 사람처럼 서 있는 곳에 이르렀다."], ["The men spoke together, and then one of them came up to Higgins, who would have fain shrunk back into his house.", "남자들이 저희끼리 이야기를 나누더니, 그중 하나가 히긴스에게 다가왔다. 히긴스는 어떻게든 제 집 안으로 물러나고만 싶었다."]], [["“Higgins, thou knowed him!", "“히긴스, 자네가 저 사람을 알잖나!"], ["Thou mun go tell the wife.", "자네가 가서 부인한테 알려야 하네. 부드럽게 말하게, 이 사람아."], ["Do it gently, man, but do it quick, for we canna leave him here long.”", "그렇지만 서둘러야 해. 여기 오래 눕혀 둘 수는 없으니까.”"]], [["“I canna go,”", "“나는 못 가오.”"], ["said Higgins.", "히긴스가 말했다."], ["“Dunnot ask me.", "“나한테 시키지 마슈."], ["I canna face her.”", "그 여자 얼굴을 볼 낯이 없소.”"]], [["“Thou knows her best,”", "“자네가 그 여자를 제일 잘 알잖나.”"], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“We’n done a deal in bringing him here—thou take thy share.”", "“우린 저 사람을 여기까지 옮겨 오느라 할 만큼 했네. 자네도 자네 몫을 하게.”"]], [["“I canna do it,”", "“나는 못 하오.”"], ["said Higgins.", "히긴스가 말했다."], ["“I’m welly felled wi’ seeing him.", "“저 사람을 보고 나니 다리에 힘이 다 빠졌소."], ["We wasn’t friends; and now he’s dead.”", "우린 사이가 좋지 않았는데, 이제 저렇게 죽어 버렸구먼.”"]], [["“Well, if thou wunnot thou wunnot.", "“뭐, 안 하겠다면 안 하는 거지."], ["Some one mun though.", "그래도 누군가는 해야 해."], ["It’s a dree task; but it’s a chance, every minute, as she doesn’t hear on it in some rougher way nor a person going to make her let on by degrees, as it were.”", "고달픈 일이긴 하네만, 이렇게 차차 알아차리게 해 줄 사람이 가지 않으면 저 여자가 더 험한 식으로 소식을 듣게 될 판이란 말일세. 지금 이 순간에도 말이야.”"]], [["“Papa, do you go,”", "“아버지, 아버지가 가 주세요.”"], ["said Margaret, in a low voice.", "마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“If I could—if I had time to think of what I had better say; but all at once——”", "“내가 할 수 있다면—무슨 말을 하는 게 나을지 생각할 틈이라도 있다면 모르겠다만, 이렇게 갑자기는——”"], ["Margaret saw that her father was indeed unable.", "마거릿은 아버지가 정말로 할 수 없는 상태임을 알아보았다."], ["He was trembling from head to foot.", "그는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었다."]], [["“I will go,”", "“제가 갈게요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Bless yo’ miss, it will be a kind act; for she’s been but a sickly sort o’ body, I hear, and few hereabouts know much on her.”", "“고맙수, 아가씨. 인정 많은 일이오. 듣자니 그 여자가 워낙 병약한 사람인 데다, 이 근방에 그 여자를 잘 아는 이도 몇 없으니 말이오.”"]], [["Margaret knocked at the closed door; but there was such a noise, as of many little ill-ordered children, that she could hear no reply; indeed, she doubted if she was heard, and as every moment of delay made her recoil from her task more and more, she opened the door and went in, shutting it after her, and even, unseen by the woman, fastening the bolt.", "마거릿은 닫힌 문을 두드렸다. 그러나 안에서는 제멋대로 구는 어린것들이 여럿 있는 듯한 소란이 어찌나 시끄럽던지 대답이 들리지 않았다. 아니, 자기 소리가 들리기나 했을지도 의심스러웠다. 그리고 지체하는 순간순간마다 이 일에서 더욱더 뒷걸음질 치게 되었으므로, 그녀는 문을 열고 들어가 뒤로 닫았다. 게다가 그 여인이 보지 못하는 사이에 빗장까지 걸어 두었다."]], [["Mrs.", "바우처 부인은 제대로 치우지 않은 벽난로 건너편에서 흔들의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["Boucher was sitting in a rocking-chair, on the other side of the ill-redd-up fireplace; it looked as if the house had been untouched for days by any effort at cleanliness.", "며칠 동안 집 안에 청소라고는 손 한 번 대지 않은 듯한 꼴이었다."]], [["Margaret said something, she hardly knew what, her throat and mouth were so dry, and the children’s noise completely prevented her from being heard.", "마거릿은 무슨 말인가를 했지만 스스로도 무슨 말인지 알지 못했다. 목과 입이 바싹 말라 있었고, 아이들의 소란 때문에 그녀의 말은 도무지 들리지 않았다."], ["She tried again.", "그녀는 다시 말해 보았다."]], [["“How are you, Mrs. Boucher?", "“좀 어떠세요, 바우처 부인?"], ["But very poorly, I’m afraid.”", "많이 편찮으신가 봐요.”"]], [["“I’ve chance o’ being well,”", "“내가 성할 턱이 있겠수.”"], ["said she querulously.", "그녀가 푸념하듯 말했다."], ["“I’m left alone to manage these childer, and nought for to give ’em for to keep ’em quiet.", "“이 어린것들을 나 혼자 건사하는데, 조용히 시키려 해도 쥐여 줄 게 있나."], ["John should na ha’ left me, and me so poorly.”", "존은 날 두고 가지 말았어야 했수. 이렇게 몸도 성치 않은데.”"]], [["“How long is it since he went away?”", "“나가신 지 얼마나 되셨나요?”"]], [["“Four days sin’.", "“4일 됐수."], ["No one would give him work here, and he’d to go on tramp toward Greenfield.", "여기선 아무도 그이한테 일을 안 주니 그린필드 쪽으로 떠돌러 가야 했지."], ["But he might ha’ been back afore this, or sent me some word if he’d getten work.", "그래도 벌써 돌아왔거나, 일자리를 잡았으면 기별이라도 보냈어야 하는 거 아뉴."], ["He might——”", "그이는——”"]], [["“Oh, don’t blame him,”", "“아, 그분을 탓하지 마세요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“He felt it deeply, I’m sure——”", "“그분도 뼈저리게 느끼셨을 거예요, 분명히——”"]], [["“Willto’ hold thy din, and let me hear the lady speak!”", "“시끄러운 소리 좀 그치고 이 아가씨 말씀 좀 듣게 해 주련!”"], ["addressing herself, in no very gentle voice, to a little urchin of about a year old.", "한 살쯤 된 조무래기에게 그리 곱지 않은 목소리로 하는 말이었다."], ["She apologetically continued to Margaret, “He’s always mithering me for ‘daddy’ and ‘butty’; and I ha’ no butties to give him, and daddy’s away, and forgotten us a’, I think.", "그러고는 미안한 듯 마거릿에게 말을 이었다. “이 녀석이 노상 ‘아빠’ ‘빵’ 하고 졸라 대서 성가시게 구는데, 줄 빵도 없고 아빠는 나가서는 우릴 죄다 잊어버린 모양이지 뭐유."], ["He’s his father’s darling, he is,”", "얘가 제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라우.”"], ["said she, with a sudden turn of mood, and, dragging the child up to her knee, she began kissing it fondly.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 갑자기 기분이 바뀌더니, 아이를 제 무릎께로 끌어당겨 애틋하게 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["Margaret laid her hand on the woman’s arm to arrest her attention.", "마거릿은 여인의 팔에 손을 얹어 주의를 끌었다."], ["Their eyes met.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."]], [["“Poor little fellow!”", "“가엾은 것.”"], ["said Margaret, slowly; “he was his father’s darling.”", "마거릿이 천천히 말했다. “제 아버지가 끔찍이 아끼던 아이였군요.”"]], [["“He is his father’s darling,”", "“제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라니까요.”"], ["said the woman, rising hastily, and standing face to face with Margaret.", "여인이 황급히 일어서서 마거릿과 얼굴을 맞대고 서며 말했다."], ["Neither of them spoke for a moment or two. Then Mrs.", "한동안 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Boucher began in a low growling tone, gathering in wildness as she went on: “He is his father’s darling, I say.", "이윽고 바우처 부인이 낮게 으르렁대는 어조로 입을 열더니, 말을 이어 갈수록 사나워졌다. “제 아비가 끔찍이 아끼는 아이라고 하잖수."], ["Poor folk can love their childer as well as rich.", "가난한 사람도 제 자식을 부자만큼 사랑할 줄 안다우."], ["Why dunno yo’ speak?", "왜 말이 없수?"], ["Why dun yo’ stare at me wi’ your great pitiful eyes?", "왜 그 큰 눈에 가엾다는 빛을 담고 날 빤히 쳐다보는 거유?"], ["Where’s John?”", "존은 어디 있수?”"], ["Weak as she was, she shook Margaret to force out an answer.", "몸이 약한데도 그녀는 대답을 끌어내려고 마거릿을 흔들었다."], ["“Oh, my God!”", "“아이고, 하느님!”"], ["said she, understanding the meaning of that tearful look.", "눈물 어린 그 눈빛의 뜻을 알아차리고 그녀가 말했다."], ["She sank back into the chair.", "그러고는 의자에 도로 주저앉았다."], ["Margaret took up the child and put him into her arms.", "마거릿은 아이를 들어 올려 그녀의 품에 안겨 주었다."]], [["“He loved him,”", "“그분은 이 아이를 사랑하셨어요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Ay,”", "“그래요.”"], ["said the woman, shaking her head, “he loved us a’.", "여인이 고개를 흔들며 말했다. “그이는 우릴 다 사랑했다우."], ["We had some one to love us once.", "우리한테도 한때는 우릴 사랑해 주는 사람이 있었지."], ["It’s a long time ago; but when he were in life and with us he did love us, he did.", "오래전 일이지만, 살아서 우리 곁에 있을 적엔 정말이지 우릴 사랑했다우, 정말로. 우리 중에 이 갓난것을 제일 사랑했는지도 모르지."], ["He loved this babby m’appen the best on us; but he loved me and I loved him, though I was calling him five minutes agone.", "그래도 그이는 날 사랑했고 나도 그일 사랑했수. 5분 전에는 욕을 하고 있었지만서두."], ["Are yo’ sure he’s dead?”", "그이가 정말 죽었수?”"], ["said she, trying to get up.", "그녀가 일어서려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“If it’s only that he’s ill and like to die, they may bring him round yet.", "“그저 앓아누워 죽게 됐을 뿐이라면 아직 살려 낼 수 있을지도 모르잖수."], ["I’m but an ailing creature mysel’—I’ve been ailing this long time.”", "나도 앓는 몸이라우—오래도록 앓아 왔다우.”"]], [["“But he is dead—he is drowned!”", "“하지만 돌아가셨어요—물에 빠지셨어요!”"]], [["“Folk are brought round after they’re dead-drowned.", "“물에 빠져 죽었다가도 되살아나는 사람이 있잖수."], ["Whatten was I thinking of, to sit still when I should be stirring mysel’?", "내가 무슨 정신으로 가만히 앉아 있었담, 서둘러 나서야 할 판에?"], ["Here, whisth thee, child—whisth thee!", "자, 뚝 그쳐라, 아가—뚝 그쳐!"], ["tak’ this, tak’ aught to play wi’, but dunnot cry while my heart’s breaking!", "이걸 받아라, 아무거나 가지고 놀아라. 그렇지만 내 가슴이 미어지는데 울지는 말아 다오!"], ["Oh, where is my strength gone to?", "아아, 내 기운이 다 어디로 갔누?"], ["Oh John—husband!”", "아아, 존—여보!”"]], [["Margaret saved her from falling by catching her in her arms.", "마거릿은 그녀를 팔로 붙들어 쓰러지지 않게 했다."], ["She sate down in the rocking chair, and held the woman upon her knees, her head lying on Margaret’s shoulder.", "그녀는 흔들의자에 앉아 여인을 무릎에 눕히고, 그 머리를 제 어깨에 기대게 했다."], ["The other children, clustered together in affright, began to understand the mystery of the scene; but the ideas came slowly, for their brains were dull and languid of perception.", "겁에 질려 옹기종기 모여 있던 다른 아이들이 이 광경의 수수께끼를 알아차리기 시작했다. 그러나 머리가 둔하고 알아차림이 굼떠서 생각은 더디게 왔다."], ["They set up such a cry of despair as they guessed the truth, that Margaret knew not how to bear it.", "사실을 짐작한 아이들이 어찌나 절망에 찬 울음을 터뜨렸던지 마거릿은 견딜 재간이 없었다."], ["Johnny’s cry was loudest of them all, though he knew not why he cried, poor little fellow.", "조니의 울음소리가 그중 제일 컸는데, 정작 그 가엾은 것은 제가 왜 우는지도 몰랐다."]], [["The mother quivered as she lay in Margaret’s arms.", "어머니는 마거릿의 품에 안긴 채 부들부들 떨었다."], ["Margaret heard a noise at the door.", "마거릿은 문간에서 나는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Open it.", "“저걸 열어라."], ["Open it quick,”", "얼른 열어.”"], ["said she to the eldest child.", "그녀가 맏이에게 말했다."], ["“It’s bolted; make no noise—be very still.", "“빗장이 걸려 있단다. 소리는 내지 말고, 아주 가만가만히."], ["Oh, papa, let them go upstairs very softly and carefully, and perhaps she will not hear them.", "아, 아버지, 저분들을 아주 조용하고 조심스럽게 위층으로 올려 보내세요. 그러면 어쩌면 이 사람이 못 들을지도 몰라요."], ["She has fainted—that’s all.”", "기절했을 뿐이니까요.”"]], [["“It’s as well for her, poor creature,”", "“차라리 잘된 거요, 가엾은 사람.”"], ["said a woman following in the wake of the bearers of the dead.", "주검을 멘 이들의 뒤를 따라 들어온 여인이 말했다."], ["“But yo’re not fit to hold her.", "“그렇지만 아가씨가 붙들고 있기엔 무리요."], ["Stay, I’ll run fetch a pillow, and we’ll let her down easy on the floor.”", "가만있어 보슈, 내가 뛰어가서 베개를 가져올 테니 바닥에 살며시 내려놓읍시다.”"]], [["This helpful neighbour was a great relief to Margaret; she was evidently a stranger to the house, a new-comer in the district, indeed; but she was so kind and thoughtful that Margaret felt she was no longer needed; and that it would be better, perhaps, to set an example of clearing the house, which was filled with idle, if sympathising gazers.", "이 고마운 이웃 여인은 마거릿에게 큰 도움이 되었다. 그 여인은 이 집에 낯선 사람인 것이 분명했고, 실은 이 동네에 새로 온 사람이었다. 그러나 어찌나 친절하고 사려 깊던지 마거릿은 이제 자기가 없어도 되겠다고 느꼈다. 그리고 딱하게 여기기는 하나 할 일 없이 구경만 하는 사람들로 가득 찬 집을 비우는 본을 보이는 편이 나으리라 생각했다."]], [["She looked round for Nicholas Higgins.", "그녀는 니컬러스 히긴스를 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["He was not there.", "그는 거기에 없었다."], ["So she spoke to the woman who had taken the lead in placing Mrs. Boucher on the floor.", "그래서 바우처 부인을 바닥에 눕히는 일을 앞장서서 한 여인에게 말했다."]], [["“Can you give all these people a hint that they had better leave in quietness?", "“이 사람들에게 조용히 돌아가는 게 좋겠다고 넌지시 일러 주시겠어요?"], ["So that when she comes round, she could only find one or two that she knows about her.", "이분이 깨어났을 때 곁에 아는 사람 한둘만 보이도록요."], ["Papa, will you speak to the men, and get them to go away.", "아버지, 저 남자분들께 말씀하셔서 돌아가시게 해 주세요."], ["She cannot breathe, poor thing, with this crowd about her.”", "이렇게 사람들이 둘러싸고 있으면 이분이 숨을 못 쉬어요, 가엾게도.”"]], [["Margaret was kneeling down by Mrs. Boucher and bathing her face with vinegar; but in a few minutes she was surprised at the gush of fresh air.", "마거릿은 바우처 부인 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 식초로 그 얼굴을 씻어 주고 있었는데, 몇 분도 지나지 않아 신선한 바람이 확 밀려드는 데 놀랐다."], ["She looked round, and saw a smile pass between her father and the woman.", "둘러보니 아버지와 그 여인 사이에 미소가 오가는 것이 보였다."]], [["“What is it?”", "“무슨 일이에요?”"], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Only our good friend here,”", "“다름이 아니라 여기 이 고마운 분이,”"], ["replied her father, “hit on a capital expedient for clearing the place.”", "아버지가 대답했다. “집을 비우게 할 아주 훌륭한 방책을 생각해 내셨구나.”"]], [["“I bid ’em begone, and each take a child with ’em, and to mind that they were orphans, and their mother a widow.", "“다들 돌아가라고 했지요. 가는 김에 아이를 하나씩 데려가라고, 이 아이들이 아비 없는 자식이고 저 어미는 과부라는 걸 잊지 말라고 했다우."], ["It was who could do most, and the childer are sure of a bellyful to-day, and of kindness too.", "서로 더 하겠다고 나서더군요. 그러니 오늘은 아이들이 배불리 먹고 인정도 받을 게 틀림없수."], ["Does hoo know how he died?”", "저 사람은 남편이 어떻게 죽었는지 아우?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니요.”"], ["said Margaret; “I could not tell her all at once.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “한꺼번에 다 말씀드릴 수는 없었어요.”"]], [["“Hoo mun be told because of th’ Inquest.", "“검시가 있을 테니 알려 줘야 하우."], ["See!", "보슈!"], ["Hoo’s coming round; shall you or I do it?", "정신이 돌아오고 있구려. 아가씨가 하겠수, 내가 하리까?"], ["or m’appen your father would be best?”", "아니면 아가씨 아버님이 제일 나을까?”"]], [["“No; you, you,”", "“아니요, 아주머니가, 아주머니가 해 주세요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["They awaited her perfect recovery in silence.", "두 사람은 그녀가 완전히 깨어나기를 잠자코 기다렸다."], ["Then the neighbour woman sat down on the floor, and took Mrs. Boucher’s head and shoulders on her lap.", "이윽고 이웃 여인이 바닥에 앉아 바우처 부인의 머리와 어깨를 제 무릎에 받쳐 안았다."]], [["“Neighbour,”", "“이보우.”"], ["said she, “your man is dead.", "그녀가 말했다. “댁의 남편이 죽었수."], ["Guess yo’ how he died?”", "어떻게 죽었는지 짐작이 가우?”"]], [["“He were drowned,”", "“물에 빠졌지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Boucher, feebly, beginning to cry for the first time, at this rough probing of her sorrows.", "바우처 부인이 힘없이 말하며, 이렇게 거칠게 슬픔을 헤집는 말에 비로소 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“He were found drowned.", "“물에 빠져 죽은 채로 발견됐수."], ["He were coming home very hopeless o’ aught on earth.", "이 세상 아무것에도 희망을 잃은 채 집으로 오던 길이었지."], ["He thought God could na be harder than men; m’appen not so hard; m’appen as tender as a mother; m’appen tenderer.", "하느님이 사람보다야 모질지 않으리라 여겼겠지. 어쩌면 그만큼 모질지도 않고, 어쩌면 어미만큼 자애로우리라고, 어쩌면 그보다 더하리라고 여겼겠지."], ["I’m not saying he did right, and I’m not saying he did wrong.", "나는 그이가 옳게 했다고도, 그르게 했다고도 말하지 않겠수. 내가 하는 말은 이것뿐이우."], ["All I say is, may neither me nor mine ever have his sore heart, or we may do like things.”", "나도 내 식구도 부디 그이 같은 쓰라린 마음일랑 겪지 않기를. 안 그러면 우리도 똑같은 짓을 할지 모르니까.”"]], [["“He has left me alone wi’ a’ these children!”", "“그이가 이 아이들을 다 남겨 놓고 나만 혼자 두고 갔구려!” 과부가 신음하듯 말했다."], ["moaned the widow, less distressed at the manner of the death than Margaret expected; but it was of a piece with her helpless character to feel his loss as principally affecting herself and her children.", "죽은 방식에 대해서는 마거릿이 예상한 것만큼 괴로워하지 않았다. 그러나 남편을 잃은 일을 무엇보다 저와 제 아이들에게 미치는 일로 느끼는 것은 그 무력한 성정에 걸맞은 일이었다."]], [["“Not alone,” said Mr.", "“혼자가 아닙니다.”"], ["Hale, solemnly. “Who is with you?", "헤일 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["Who will take up your cause?”", "“누가 부인과 함께 계십니까?"], ["The widow opened her eyes wide, and looked at the new speaker, of whose presence she had not been aware till then.", "누가 부인의 사정을 떠맡아 주시겠습니까?” 과부는 눈을 크게 뜨고, 그제야 있는 줄 알게 된 새 목소리의 주인을 바라보았다."]], [["“Who has promised to be a father to the fatherless?”", "“아비 없는 자에게 아버지가 되어 주겠다고 약속하신 분이 누구입니까?”"], ["continued he.", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“But I’ve getten six children, sir, and the eldest not eight years of age.", "“하지만 나리, 저는 아이가 6명이나 되고 맏이가 8살도 안 되었답니다. 그분의 능력을 의심하려는 게 아니에요, 나리."], ["I’m not meaning for to doubt His power, sir,—only it needs a deal o’ trust”; and she began to cry afresh.", "다만 그러려면 믿음이 아주 많이 있어야 하는걸요.” 그러고는 다시 울기 시작했다."]], [["“Hoo’ll be better able to talk to-morrow, sir,”", "“내일이면 말을 더 잘할 수 있을 거예요, 나리.”"], ["said the neighbour.", "이웃 여인이 말했다."], ["“Best comfort now would be the feel of a child at her heart.", "“지금 제일 위로가 될 건 가슴에 아이를 안는 느낌일 텐데."], ["I’m sorry they took the babby.”", "갓난것을 데려가 버려서 안됐구먼.”"]], [["“I’ll go for it,”", "“제가 데려올게요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["And in a few minutes she returned, carrying Johnnie, his face all smeared with eating, and his hands loaded with treasures in the shape of shells, and bits of crystal, and the head of a plaster figure.", "그리고 몇 분 뒤 조니를 안고 돌아왔다. 아이의 얼굴은 먹은 것으로 온통 범벅이었고, 두 손에는 조개껍데기며 수정 조각이며 석고 인형 머리 같은 보물이 가득했다."], ["She placed him on his mother’s arms.", "그녀는 아이를 어머니의 품에 안겨 주었다."]], [["“There!”", "“자!”"], ["said the woman, “now you go.", "여인이 말했다. “이제 가 보슈."], ["They’ll cry together, and comfort together, better nor any one but a child can do.", "둘이서 함께 울고 함께 위로할 거요. 아이 말고는 아무도 못 해 줄 위로를 말이오."], ["I’ll stop with her as long as I’m needed, and if you come to-morrow, you’ can have a deal o’ wise talk with her, that she’s not up to to-day.”", "나는 필요할 때까지 곁에 있겠수. 그리고 내일 오시면 오늘은 감당하지 못할 지혜로운 말씀을 실컷 나눌 수 있을 거유.”"]], [["As Margaret and her father went slowly up the street, she paused at Higgins’s closed door.", "마거릿과 아버지가 천천히 거리를 올라가는데, 그녀는 히긴스의 닫힌 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Shall we go in?”", "“들어가 볼까?”"], ["asked her father.", "아버지가 물었다."], ["“I was thinking of him too.”", "“나도 그 사람 생각을 하고 있었단다.”"]], [["They knocked.", "두 사람은 문을 두드렸다."], ["There was no answer, so they tried the door.", "대답이 없어 문을 밀어 보았다."], ["It was bolted, but they thought they heard him moving within.", "빗장이 걸려 있었지만, 안에서 그가 움직이는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."]], [["“Nicholas!”", "“니컬러스!”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 불렀다."], ["There was no answer, and they might have gone away, believing the house to be empty, if there had not been some accidental fall, as of a book, within.", "대답이 없었다. 안에서 책 같은 것이 우연히 떨어지는 소리가 나지 않았더라면, 두 사람은 집이 비었다고 여기고 돌아갔을 것이다."]], [["“Nicholas!”", "“니컬러스!”"], ["said Margaret again.", "마거릿이 다시 불렀다."], ["“It is only us.", "“저희예요."], ["Won’t you let us come in?”", "좀 들어가게 해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“No,” said he.", "“안 되오.”"], ["“I spoke as plain as I could, ’bout using words, when I bolted th’ door.", "그가 말했다. “문에 빗장을 걸었을 때, 말을 쓰지 않고도 할 수 있는 한 분명히 말한 셈이오."], ["Let me be, this day.”", "오늘은 나를 좀 내버려 두슈.”"]], [["Mr. Hale would have urged their desire, but Margaret placed her finger on his lips.", "헤일 씨는 들어가고 싶다는 뜻을 더 밀어붙이려 했지만, 마거릿이 그의 입술에 손가락을 얹었다."]], [["“I don’t wonder at it,”", "“그럴 만도 해요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I myself long to be alone.", "“저도 혼자 있고 싶은걸요."], ["It seems the only thing to do one good after a day like this.”", "이런 하루를 보낸 뒤엔 마음에 도움이 될 일이 그것밖에 없는 것 같아요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII", "# 제37장"]], [["“A spade!", "“삽!"], ["a rake!", "갈퀴!"], ["a hoe!", "괭이!"], ["A pickaxe or a bill!", "곡괭이나 낫자루!"], ["A hook to reap, or a scythe to mow, A flail or what ye will— And here’s a ready hand To ply the needful tool, And skill’d enough, by lessons rough, In Labour’s rugged school.”", "거두는 낫이든 베어 넘기는 큰낫이든, 도리깨든 무엇이든 좋다— 여기 그 필요한 연장을 부릴 채비된 손이 있으니, 노동의 거친 학교에서 모진 수업으로 익힐 만큼 익힌 손이다.”"], ["HOOD.", "— 후드."]], [["Higgins’s door was locked the next day, when they went to pay their call on the widow Boucher: but they learnt this time from an officious neighbour, that he was really from home.", "이튿날 그들이 과부가 된 바우처 부인을 찾아갔을 때 히긴스의 문은 잠겨 있었다. 그러나 이번에는 참견하기 좋아하는 이웃에게서 그가 정말로 집을 비웠다는 말을 들었다."], ["He had, however, been in to see Mrs. Boucher, before starting on his day’s business, whatever that was.", "그래도 그는 그날의 볼일이 무엇이든 그것에 나서기 전에 바우처 부인을 들여다보고 갔다고 했다."], ["It was but an unsatisfactory visit to Mrs.", "바우처 부인을 찾아간 것은 그리 흡족한 방문이 못 되었다."], ["Boucher; she considered herself an ill-used woman by her poor husband’s suicide; and there was quite germ of truth enough in this idea to make it a very difficult one to refute.", "그녀는 가엾은 남편의 자살로 자기가 몹쓸 꼴을 당했다고 여기고 있었는데, 그 생각에는 반박하기가 몹시 어려울 만큼의 진실의 씨앗이 들어 있었다."], ["Still, it was unsatisfactory to see how completely her thoughts were turned upon herself and her own position, and this selfishness extended even to her relations with her children, whom she considered as incumbrances, even in the very midst of her somewhat animal affection for them.", "그렇더라도 그녀의 생각이 온통 자기 자신과 자기 처지에만 쏠려 있는 것을 보는 일은 마음 편치 않았다. 그 이기심은 아이들과의 관계에까지 뻗어 있어서, 짐승 같은 애정을 한창 쏟는 와중에도 그녀는 아이들을 짐스러운 것으로 여겼다."], ["Margaret tried to make acquaintances with one or two of them, while her father strove to raise the widow’s thoughts into some higher channel than that of mere helpless querulousness.", "마거릿은 아이들 한둘과 낯을 익히려 애썼고, 그동안 그녀의 아버지는 그저 속수무책의 푸념에 지나지 않는 과부의 생각을 좀 더 높은 물길로 끌어올리려 애썼다."], ["She found that the children were truer and simpler mourners than the widow.", "마거릿이 보기에 아이들은 과부보다 더 진실하고 소박한 상주였다."], ["Daddy had been a kind daddy to them; each could tell, in their eager stammering way, of some tenderness shown, some indulgence granted by the lost father.", "아빠는 그들에게 다정한 아빠였다. 아이들은 저마다 조급하게 더듬거리는 말투로, 잃어버린 아버지가 베풀어 준 어떤 다정함이며 어떤 너그러움을 이야기할 수 있었다."]], [["“Is yon thing upstairs really him?", "“위층에 있는 저게 정말 아빠유?"], ["it doesna look like him.", "아빠처럼 보이질 않는데."], ["I’m feared on it, and I never was feared o’ daddy.”", "난 저게 무섭구먼. 아빠가 무서웠던 적은 한 번도 없었는데.”"]], [["Margaret’s heart bled to hear that the mother, in her selfish requirement of sympathy, had taken her children upstairs to see their dead disfigured father.", "어머니가 이기적으로 동정을 갈구한 나머지 아이들을 위층으로 데려가 죽어 일그러진 아버지를 보게 했다는 것을 듣고 마거릿은 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["It was intermingling the coarseness of horror with the profoundness of natural grief.", "그것은 자연스러운 슬픔의 깊이에 공포의 조야함을 뒤섞는 짓이었다."], ["She tried to turn their thoughts in some other direction; on what they could do for mother; on what—for this was a more efficacious way of putting it—what father would have wished them to do.", "그녀는 아이들의 생각을 다른 쪽으로 돌리려 애썼다. 어머니를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지로, 그리고—이 편이 더 효험 있는 말투였는데—아버지라면 무엇을 하기를 바랐을지로."], ["Margaret was more successful than Mr. Hale in her efforts.", "마거릿은 그런 노력에서 헤일 씨보다 성공적이었다."], ["The children seeing their little duties lie in action close around them, began to try each one to do something that she suggested towards redding up the slatternly room.", "아이들은 자기가 할 자그마한 도리가 바로 곁에 놓여 있음을 보고, 저마다 그녀가 일러 준 대로 어질러진 방을 치우는 일에 무엇이든 해 보려 들기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그 게으른 병자 앞에 너무 높은 기준과 너무 추상적인 견해를 내놓았다."], ["But her father set too high a standard, and too abstract a view, before the indolent invalid.", "그녀는 자신의 무딘 마음을 일깨워 남편이 그 마지막 무서운 걸음을 내딛기 전에 어떤 비참함을 겪었을지 생생히 그려 보지 못했다. 그녀는 오직 그 일이 자기에게 미친 영향으로만 그것을 바라볼 수 있었다."], ["She could not rouse her torpid mind into any vivid imagination of what her husband’s misery might have been, before he had resorted to the last terrible step; she could only look upon it as it affected herself; she could not enter into the enduring mercy of the God who had not specially interposed to prevent the water from drowning her prostrate husband; and although she was secretly blaming her husband for having fallen into such drear despair, and denying that he had any excuse for his last rash act, she was inveterate in her abuse of all who could by any possibility be supposed to have driven him to such desperation.", "엎어진 남편을 물이 덮어 삼키는 것을 특별히 막아 주지 않으신 하느님의 오래 참으시는 자비를 그녀는 헤아리지 못했다. 그리고 남편이 그런 암담한 절망에 빠진 것을 속으로는 나무라고 그 마지막 무모한 짓에 아무 변명거리도 없다고 하면서도, 어떻게든 그를 그런 절망으로 몰아넣었을 법한 이들 모두를 헐뜯는 데는 지독히도 끈질겼다."], ["The masters—Mr. Thornton in particular, whose mill had been attacked by Boucher, and who, after the warrant had been issued for his apprehension on the charge of rioting, had caused it to be withdrawn,—the Union, of which Higgins was a representative to the poor woman,—the children so numerous, so hungry, and so noisy—all made up one great army of personal enemies, whose fault it was that she was now a helpless widow.", "공장주들—그중에서도 특히, 바우처의 습격을 받은 공장의 주인이면서 폭동 혐의로 그를 잡아들일 영장이 발부된 뒤에 그것을 거두게 한 손턴 씨—그리고 그 가엾은 여인에게는 히긴스가 곧 대표자였던 노동조합, 그리고 그토록 수도 많고 배고프고 시끄러운 아이들, 이 모두가 하나의 커다란 사적인 원수의 군대를 이루었고, 그녀가 지금 속수무책의 과부가 된 것은 다 그들의 잘못이었다."]], [["Margaret heard enough of this unreasonableness to dishearten her; and when they came away she found it impossible to cheer her father.", "마거릿은 이 터무니없는 소리를 낙심할 만큼 실컷 들었다. 그리고 그 집을 나섰을 때 아버지를 도무지 위로할 수가 없다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“It is the town life,”", "“도시 생활 탓이에요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Their nerves are quickened by the haste and bustle and speed of everything around them, to say nothing of the confinement in these pent-up houses, which of itself is enough to induce depression and worry of spirits.", "“주위의 모든 것이 서두르고 북적이고 빨라서 신경이 곤두서는 거예요. 이 답답한 집에 갇혀 지내는 것만 해도 우울과 근심을 불러오기에 충분하고요."], ["Now in the country, people live so much more out of doors, even children, and even in the winter.”", "시골에서는 사람들이 훨씬 더 바깥에서 지내잖아요. 아이들도 그렇고, 겨울에도 그렇고요.”"]], [["“But people must live in towns.", "“하지만 사람들은 도시에 살아야 하지 않느냐."], ["And in the country some get such stagnant habits of mind that they are almost fatalists.”", "그리고 시골에서는 마음이 어찌나 고여 버리는지 거의 숙명론자가 되어 버리는 이들도 있단다.”"]], [["“Yes; I acknowledge that.", "“네, 그건 인정해요."], ["I suppose each mode of life produces its own trials and its own temptations.", "저마다의 삶의 방식이 저마다의 시련과 저마다의 유혹을 낳는 거겠지요."], ["The dweller in towns must find it as difficult to be patient, as the country-bred man must find it to be active, and equal to unwonted emergencies.", "도시에 사는 사람은 참는 것이 어려울 테고, 시골에서 자란 사람은 부지런히 움직이며 익숙지 않은 위급한 일에 맞서는 것이 어려울 테고요."], ["Both must find it hard to realise a future of any kind; the one because the present is so living and hurrying and close around him; the other because his life tempts him to revel in the mere sense of animal existence, not knowing of, and consequently not caring for any pungency of pleasure, for the attainment of which he can plan, and deny himself and look forward.”", "둘 다 어떤 종류의 미래든 실감하기가 어려울 거예요. 한쪽은 현재가 너무 생생하고 급하고 바싹 다가와 있기 때문이고, 다른 쪽은 그 삶이 그저 짐승처럼 살아 있다는 느낌에 젖어 들도록 부추기는 탓에 계획하고 자신을 억누르고 앞을 내다보아 얻어 낼 수 있는 어떤 짜릿한 기쁨도 알지 못하고, 따라서 마음에 두지도 않기 때문이지요.”"]], [["“And thus both the necessity for engrossment, and the stupid content in the present, produce the same effects.", "“그러니까 몰두하지 않을 수 없는 처지나 현재에 대한 우둔한 만족이나 같은 결과를 낳는 셈이구나."], ["But this poor Mrs. Boucher!", "하지만 저 가엾은 바우처 부인!"], ["how little we can do for her.”", "우리가 저 사람을 위해 해 줄 수 있는 것이 어찌 이리 적은지.”"]], [["“And yet we dare not leave her without our efforts, although they may seem so useless.", "“그래도 소용없어 보인다고 해서 애쓰기를 그만두고 저분을 내버려 둘 수는 없어요."], ["Oh papa!", "아, 아버지!"], ["it’s a hard world to live in!”", "살아가기에 참 모진 세상이에요!”"]], [["“So it is, my child.", "“그렇구나, 얘야."], ["We feel it so just now, at any rate; but we have been very happy, even in the midst of our sorrow.", "적어도 지금 우리는 그렇게 느끼지. 하지만 우리는 슬픔 한가운데서도 아주 행복했단다."], ["What a pleasure Frederick’s visit was!”", "프레더릭이 다녀간 것이 얼마나 큰 기쁨이었느냐!”"]], [["“Yes, that it was,”", "“네, 정말 그랬어요.”"], ["said Margaret, brightly.", "마거릿이 환하게 말했다."], ["“It was such a charming, snatched, forbidden thing.”", "“그렇게 매혹적이고, 몰래 훔쳐 낸, 금지된 일이었어요.”"], ["But she suddenly stopped speaking.", "그러나 그녀는 문득 말을 멈추었다."], ["She had spoiled the remembrance of Frederick’s visit to herself by her own cowardice.", "그녀는 자신의 비겁함으로 프레더릭이 다녀간 기억을 스스로 망쳐 버렸다. 남에게서 그녀가 가장 경멸하는 결점이 바로 용기의 부족이었다."], ["Of all faults the one she most despised in others was the want of bravery; the meanness of heart which leads to untruth.", "거짓으로 이끄는 마음의 비루함이었다. 그런데 그녀 자신이 그 죄를 저지른 것이다."], ["And here had she been guilty of it! Then came the thought of Mr.", "그러자 손턴 씨가 그녀의 거짓을 알고 있다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Thornton’s cognisance of her falsehood.", "다른 누구에게 들켰더라도 이만큼이나 마음이 쓰였을까 싶었다."], ["She wondered if she should have minded detection half so much from any one else.", "그녀는 상상 속에서 스스로를 시험해 보았다. 쇼 이모와 이디스에게, 아버지에게, 레녹스 대위와 레녹스 씨에게, 프레더릭에게."], ["She tried herself in imagination with her Aunt Shaw and Edith; with her father; with Captain and Mr. Lennox; with Frederick.", "마지막 사람이, 그것도 자기를 위해 한 일인데도, 그녀가 한 짓을 안다고 생각하면 가장 괴로웠다."], ["The thought of the last knowing what she had done, even in his own behalf, was the most painful, for the brother and sister were in the first flush of their mutual regard and love; but even any fall in Frederick’s opinion was as nothing to the shame, the shrinking shame she felt at the thought of meeting Mr.", "오누이는 서로에 대한 정과 사랑이 처음으로 활짝 피어난 참이었으니까. 그러나 프레더릭의 평가가 떨어지는 것조차, 손턴 씨를 다시 만난다고 생각할 때 느끼는 부끄러움, 몸이 움츠러드는 그 부끄러움에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Thornton again.", "그러면서도 그녀는 그를 보고 싶었다."], ["And yet she longed to see him, to get it over; to understand where she stood in his opinion.", "얼른 겪어 치우고 싶었다. 그의 눈에 자기가 어디쯤 서 있는지 알고 싶었다."], ["Her cheeks burnt as she recollected how proudly she had implied an objection to trade (in the early days of their acquaintance), because it too often led to the deceit of passing off inferior for superior goods, in the one branch; of assuming credit for wealth and resources not possessed, in the other.", "두 사람이 알게 된 초기에 자기가 얼마나 도도하게 장사에 대한 반감을 내비쳤던가를 떠올리자 뺨이 화끈거렸다. 장사란 한쪽에서는 못한 물건을 좋은 물건으로 속여 넘기는 기만으로, 다른 쪽에서는 가지지도 않은 재산과 자력을 가진 척하는 기만으로 너무 자주 이어진다는 이유에서였다."], ["She remembered Mr.", "그녀는 손턴 씨의 차분한 경멸의 표정을 기억했다."], ["Thornton’s look of calm disdain, as in few words he gave her to understand that, in the great scheme of commerce, all dishonourable ways of acting were sure to prove injurious in the long run, and that, testing such actions simply according to the poor standard of success, there was folly and not wisdom in all such, and every kind of deceit in trade, as well as in other things.", "그는 몇 마디 말로, 상업이라는 커다란 얼개 안에서 명예롭지 못한 온갖 행위는 길게 보면 반드시 해가 되고 만다는 것을, 그리고 그런 행위를 그저 성공이라는 초라한 잣대로만 재어 보아도 장사에서의 온갖 기만은 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로 지혜가 아니라 어리석음이라는 것을 그녀에게 알려 주었다. 그녀는 기억했다."], ["She remembered—she, then strong in her own untempted truth—asking him, if he did not think that buying in the cheapest and selling in the dearest market proved some want of the transparent justice which is so intimately connected with the idea of truth: and she had used the word chivalric—and her father had corrected her with the higher word, Christian; and so drawn the argument upon himself, while she sate silent by with a slight feeling of contempt.", "그때 아직 시험받지 않은 자신의 진실함에 힘입어 당당하던 그녀가, 가장 싼 데서 사서 가장 비싼 데 파는 것은 진실이라는 관념과 그토록 긴밀히 이어진 투명한 정의가 얼마쯤 모자란다는 뜻이 아니겠느냐고 그에게 물었던 것을. 그리고 그녀는 기사도적이라는 말을 썼고, 아버지가 그것을 더 높은 말인 그리스도교적이라는 말로 바로잡아 주었으며, 그리하여 그 논쟁을 자기 쪽으로 끌어갔고, 그동안 그녀는 얼마쯤 경멸을 느끼며 곁에 잠자코 앉아 있었다는 것을."]], [["No more contempt for her!—no more talk about the chivalric!", "이제 그녀에게 경멸 따위는 없었다! 기사도 운운하는 말도 없었다!"], ["Henceforward she must feel humiliated and disgraced in his sight.", "이제부터 그녀는 그의 눈앞에서 굴욕스럽고 부끄러운 사람으로 있어야 했다."], ["But when should she see him?", "그런데 언제 그를 보게 될까?"], ["Her heart leaped up in apprehension at every ring of the door-bell; and yet, when it fell down to calmness, she felt strangely saddened and sick at heart at each disappointment.", "현관 종이 울릴 때마다 그녀의 가슴은 겁먹은 듯 튀어 올랐다. 그러다 도로 잠잠해지면 그 실망마다 이상하게 서글프고 속이 메스꺼웠다."], ["It was very evident that her father expected to see him, and was surprised that he did not come.", "아버지가 그를 보기를 기다리고 있으며 그가 오지 않는 것을 이상히 여기고 있는 것은 아주 분명했다."], ["The truth was, that there were points in their conversation the other night on which they had no time then to enlarge; but it had been understood that if possible on the succeeding evening—if not then, at least the very first evening that Mr.", "사실인즉 지난밤 두 사람의 대화에는 그때 미처 넓혀 나갈 겨를이 없었던 대목들이 있었다. 그래서 될 수 있으면 그다음 날 저녁에, 그것이 안 되면 적어도 손턴 씨가 시간을 낼 수 있는 가장 이른 저녁에 만나 더 이야기하기로 되어 있었다."], ["Thornton could command,—they should meet for further discussion.", "헤일 씨는 헤어진 뒤로 줄곧 이 만남을 고대해 왔다."], ["Mr. Hale had looked forward to this meeting ever since they had parted.", "그는 아내의 병이 더 위중해지기 시작하면서 그만두었던 제자들 수업을 아직 다시 시작하지 않았으므로 여느 때보다 할 일이 적었다."], ["He had not yet resumed the instruction to his pupils, which he had relinquished at the commencement of his wife’s more serious illness, so he had fewer occupations than usual; and the great interest of the last day or so (Boucher’s suicide) had driven him back with more eagerness than ever upon his speculations.", "게다가 요 하루 이틀 사이의 큰 사건, 곧 바우처의 자살이 그를 어느 때보다 더 간절히 자기 사색으로 밀어 넣었다. 그는 저녁 내내 안절부절못했다."], ["He was restless all the evening.", "그는 자꾸만 말했다."], ["He kept saying, “I quite expected to have seen Mr. Thornton.", "“나는 손턴 씨를 보게 되리라 아주 믿고 있었는데."], ["I think the messenger who brought the book last night must have had some note, and forgot to deliver it.", "어젯밤 책을 가져온 심부름꾼이 쪽지도 함께 받아 놓고는 전하는 것을 잊어버린 게 아닌가 싶구나."], ["Do you think there has been any message left to-day?”", "오늘 무슨 전갈이 와 있지 않겠니?”"]], [["“I will go and inquire, papa,”", "“가서 여쭤 보고 올게요, 아버지.”"], ["said Margaret, after the changes on these sentences had been rung once or twice, “Stay, there’s a ring!”", "이 말들을 한두 번 이리저리 바꿔 되풀이한 끝에 마거릿이 말했다. “잠깐, 종이 울려요!”"], ["She sat down instantly, and bent her head attentively over her work.", "그녀는 곧바로 앉아 하던 바느질 위로 열심히 고개를 숙였다."], ["She heard a step on the stairs, but it was only one, and she knew it was Dixon’s.", "계단에서 발소리가 들렸으나 하나뿐이었고, 그녀는 그것이 딕슨의 발소리임을 알았다."], ["She lifted up her head and sighed, and believed she felt glad.", "그녀는 고개를 들고 한숨을 쉬었고, 자기가 기쁘다고 믿었다."]], [["“It’s that Higgins, sir.", "“그 히긴스라는 사람이에요, 나리."], ["He wants to see you, or else Miss Hale.", "나리를 뵙자는데, 아니면 헤일 아가씨를 뵙자는 건지."], ["Or it might be Miss Hale first, and then you, sir; for he’s in a strange kind of way.”", "아니면 헤일 아가씨를 먼저 뵙고 그다음에 나리를 뵙자는 건지도 모르겠어요. 사람이 어째 이상하게 굴어서요.”"]], [["“He had better come up here, Dixon; and then he can see us both, and choose which he likes for his listener.”", "“이리로 올라오시라고 하게, 딕슨. 그러면 우리 둘을 다 볼 수 있으니 마음에 드는 쪽을 골라 이야기하면 되지 않겠나.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["very well, sir.", "그러시죠, 나리."], ["I’ve no wish to hear what he’s got to say, I’m sure; only, if you could see his shoes, I’m sure you’d say the kitchen was the fitter place.”", "저야 그 사람이 무슨 말을 하든 듣고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없습니다요. 다만 그 신발을 보셨더라면 부엌이 더 어울리는 자리라고 하셨을 겁니다요.”"]], [["“He can wipe them, I suppose,”", "“닦으면 되겠지.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["So Dixon flung off, to bid him walk up-stairs.", "그래서 딕슨은 그에게 위층으로 올라가라고 이르러 홱 나가 버렸다."], ["She was a little mollified, however, when he looked at his feet with a hesitating air; and then, sitting down on the bottom stair, he took off the offending shoes, and without a word walked up-stairs.", "그러나 그가 머뭇거리는 낯빛으로 자기 발을 내려다보더니 맨 아래 계단에 앉아 그 말썽 많은 신발을 벗고는 말없이 위층으로 올라가는 것을 보고 그녀의 마음도 조금은 풀렸다."]], [["“Sarvant, sir!”", "“안녕하십쇼, 나리!” 방으로 들어서면서 그가 머리를 매만져 내리며 말했다."], ["said he, slicking his hair down when he came into the room: “If hoo’l excuse me (looking at Margaret) for being i’ my stockings; I’se been tramping a’ day, and streets is none o’ th’ cleanest.”", "“아가씨가 (마거릿을 보며) 양해해 주신다면 양말 바람으로 있겠소. 하루 종일 돌아다녔는데 거리가 도무지 깨끗하질 않아서요.”"]], [["Margaret thought that fatigue might account for the change in his manner, for he was unusually quiet and subdued; and he had evidently some difficulty in saying what he came to say.", "마거릿은 그의 태도가 달라진 것이 고단함 탓인가 보다 생각했다. 그는 여느 때와 달리 조용하고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그리고 하려고 온 말을 꺼내기가 분명 어려운 눈치였다."]], [["Mr. Hale’s ever-ready sympathy with anything of shyness or hesitation, or want of self-possession, made him come to his aid.", "수줍음이나 머뭇거림, 침착함을 잃은 기색이라면 무엇에나 늘 곧바로 마음을 써 주는 헤일 씨가 그를 거들고 나섰다."]], [["“We shall have tea up directly, and then you’ll take a cup with us, Mr.", "“곧 차가 올라올 걸세. 그러면 우리와 한 잔 하게, 히긴스 씨."], ["Higgins. I am sure you are tired, if you’ve been out much this wet relaxing day.", "이렇게 축축하고 나른한 날에 밖에 오래 있었다면 분명 고단할 테지."], ["Margaret, my dear, can’t you hasten tea?”", "마거릿, 얘야, 차를 좀 서둘러 줄 수 있겠니?”"]], [["Margaret could only hasten tea by taking the preparation of it into her own hands, and so offending Dixon, who was emerging out of her sorrow for her late mistress into a very touchy, irritable state.", "마거릿이 차를 서두를 수 있는 길은 준비를 직접 떠맡는 것뿐이었고, 그러면 딕슨의 심기를 건드리게 되었다. 딕슨은 돌아가신 안주인에 대한 슬픔에서 빠져나오면서 무척 예민하고 짜증스러운 상태가 되어 있었다."], ["But Martha, like all who came in contact with Margaret—even Dixon herself, in the long run—felt it a pleasure and an honour to forward any of her wishes; and her readiness, and Margaret’s sweet forbearance, soon made Dixon ashamed of herself.", "그러나 마사도 마거릿과 마주치는 모든 이가 그러하듯—길게 보면 딕슨 자신까지도—그녀의 바람이라면 무엇이든 앞장서 이루어 주는 것을 기쁨이자 영광으로 여겼다. 그리고 마사의 선선함과 마거릿의 상냥한 참을성은 이내 딕슨이 스스로를 부끄러워하게 만들었다."]], [["“Why master and you must always be asking the lower classes up-stairs, since we came to Milton, I cannot understand.", "“밀턴에 온 뒤로 어째서 나리와 아가씨가 늘 아랫것들을 위층으로 불러올리셔야 하는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다요."], ["Folk at Helstone were never brought higher than the kitchen; and I’ve let one or two of them know before now that they might think it an honour to be even there.”", "헬스톤에서는 그런 사람들을 부엌보다 높은 데로 들인 적이 없었어요. 게다가 전에는 한둘한테 부엌에 들어온 것만도 영광인 줄 알라고 일러 준 적도 있는걸요.”"]], [["Higgins found it easier to unburden himself to one than to two.", "히긴스는 둘보다 하나에게 속을 털어놓는 편이 더 쉽다는 것을 알았다."], ["After Margaret left the room, he went to the door and assured himself that it was shut.", "마거릿이 방을 나가자 그는 문께로 가서 문이 닫혔는지 확인했다."], ["Then he came and stood close to Mr. Hale.", "그러고는 돌아와 헤일 씨 가까이에 섰다."]], [["“Master,”", "“나리.”"], ["said he, “yo’d not guess easy what I’ve been tramping after to-day.", "그가 말했다. “오늘 내가 무얼 찾아 쏘다녔는지는 쉽게 짐작 못 하실 거요."], ["Special if yo’d remember my manner o’ talk yesterday.", "어제 내 말투를 기억하신다면 더더욱 그렇겠지."], ["I’ve been a seeking work.", "나는 일자리를 찾아다녔소."], ["I have,”", "그랬소.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I said to mysel’, I’d keep a civil tongue in my head, let who would say what ’em would.", "“나는 속으로 다짐했소. 누가 무슨 소리를 하든 입은 공손히 놀리자고."], ["I’d set my teeth into my tongue sooner nor speak i’ haste.", "급하게 말을 뱉느니 차라리 혓바닥을 이로 물어 버리자고."], ["For that man’s sake—yo’ understand,”", "그 사람을 봐서 말이오. 무슨 말인지 아시겠소.”"], ["jerking his thumb back in some unknown direction.", "그러면서 알 수 없는 방향으로 엄지를 뒤로 홱 젖혔다."]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "“아니, 모르겠네.”"], ["said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Hale, seeing he waited for some kind of assent, and completely bewildered as to who “that man” could be.", "상대가 무언가 수긍하는 대답을 기다리는 눈치였으나, “그 사람”이 누구인지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없었던 것이다."]], [["“That chap as lies theer,”", "“저기 누워 있는 그 친구 말이오.”"], ["said he, with another jerk.", "그가 또 한 번 엄지를 젖히며 말했다."], ["“Him as went and drownded himself, poor chap!", "“가서 제 몸을 물에 빠뜨린 사람 말이오, 가엾은 친구!"], ["I did na think he’d got it in him to lie still and let the water creep o’er him till he died.", "나는 그 사람한테 가만히 누워 물이 제 몸을 덮어 죽을 때까지 견딜 배짱이 있을 줄은 몰랐소."], ["Boucher, yo’ know.”", "바우처 말이오, 아시잖소.”"]], [["“Yes, I know now,”", "“그래, 이제 알겠네.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“Go back to what you were saying: you’d not speak in haste——”", "“하던 말로 돌아가세. 급하게 말하지 않겠다고 했지—”"]], [["“For his sake.", "“그 사람을 봐서 말이오. 아니, 그 사람을 봐서는 아니지."], ["Yet not for his sake; for where’er he is, and whate’er, he’ll ne’er know other clemming or cold again; but for the wife’s sake, and the bits o’ childer.”", "그가 어디에 있든 무엇이 되었든 이제 다시는 굶주림도 추위도 모를 테니까. 그 안사람과 그 어린것들을 봐서요.”"]], [["“God bless you!” said Mr.", "“하느님이 자네에게 복 주시기를!”"], ["Hale, starting up; then, calming down, he said, breathlessly, “What do you mean?", "헤일 씨가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. 그러고는 마음을 가라앉히고 숨 가쁘게 말했다."], ["Tell me out.”", "“무슨 뜻인가? 다 털어놓게.”"]], [["“I have telled yo’,”", "“벌써 말씀드렸잖소.”"], ["said Higgins, a little surprised at Mr. Hale’s agitation.", "헤일 씨가 흥분하는 것에 조금 놀라며 히긴스가 말했다."], ["“I would na ask for work for mysel’; but them’s left as a charge on me.", "“내 앞가림하자고 일자리를 구걸하진 않소. 하지만 그 사람들이 내게 맡겨진 짐으로 남았으니 말이오."], ["I reckon, I would ha guided Boucher to a better end; but I set him off o’ th’ road, and so I mun answer for him.”", "나라면 바우처를 더 나은 끝으로 이끌 수도 있었을 거요. 그런데 내가 그를 길에서 벗어나게 했으니, 그러니 내가 그를 책임져야 하는 거요.”"]], [["Mr. Hale got hold of Higgins’s hand and shook it heartily, without speaking.", "헤일 씨는 히긴스의 손을 붙잡고 아무 말 없이 힘껏 흔들었다."], ["Higgins looked awkward and ashamed.", "히긴스는 어색하고 겸연쩍은 낯빛이었다."]], [["“Theer, theer, master! Theer’s ne’er a man, to call a man, amongst us, but what would do th’ same; ay, and better too; for, belie’ me, Is’e ne’er got a stroke o’ work, nor yet a sight of any. For all I telled Hamper that, let alone his pledge—which I would not sign—no, I could na, not e’en for this—he’d ne’er ha’ such a worker on his mill as I would be—he’d ha’ none o’ me—no more would none o’ th’ others. I’m a poor black feckless sheep—childer may clem for ought I can do, unless, parson, yo’d help me?”", "“아이고, 아이고, 나리! 우리네 가운데 사람이라고 부를 만한 사람이라면 누구든 똑같이 했을 거요. 아니, 더 잘했을 거요. 정말이지 나는 일감을 한 손도 못 얻었고, 일감 구경조차 못 했으니 말이오. 햄퍼한테 그렇게 말했는데도 그렇소. 그 서약이야 접어 두더라도—나는 그건 서명 못 하오, 아니, 못 하겠소, 이 일을 위해서라도—내가 될 만한 일꾼만 한 일꾼은 그 공장에 다시 없을 거라고 했는데도, 그는 나를 안 쓰겠다는 거요. 다른 사람들도 다 마찬가지고. 나는 가엾고 시커먼 못난 양이오. 내가 무슨 수를 써도 어린것들은 굶어야 할 판이오. 목사님, 나를 좀 도와주시지 않겠소?”"]], [["“Help you! How?", "“자네를 돕는다고!"], ["I would do anything,—but what can I do?”", "어떻게? 무엇이든 하겠네만—내가 무얼 할 수 있겠나?”"]], [["“Miss there”—for Margaret had re-entered the room, and stood silent, listening—“has often talked grand o’ the South, and the ways down there.", "“저기 아가씨가”—마거릿이 방에 다시 들어와 말없이 서서 듣고 있었다—“남쪽 이야기며 거기 사는 방식을 곧잘 대단하게 말했잖소. 그게 얼마나 먼지는 모르겠소만, 저것들을 거기로 데려갈 수만 있다면 하고 생각해 봤소."], ["Now I dunnot know how far off it is, but I been thinking if I could get ’em down theer, where food is cheap and wages good, and all the folk, rich and poor, master and man, friendly like; yo’ could, may be, help me to work.", "먹을 것이 싸고 품삯이 좋고, 부자든 가난뱅이든 공장주든 일꾼이든 다들 다정하다는 그곳으로 말이오. 나리가 일자리를 얻게 도와주실 수 있을지도 모르잖소."], ["I’m not forty-five, and I’ve a deal o’ strength in me, measter.”", "나는 아직 45살이 안 됐고 힘도 넉넉히 남았소, 나리.”"]], [["“But what kind of work could you do, my man?”", "“하지만 자네가 할 수 있는 일이 어떤 것이겠나?”"]], [["“Well, I reckon I could spade a bit——”", "“글쎄, 삽질쯤은 할 수 있을 것 같소만——”"]], [["“And for that,”", "“그런데 그것으로는,” 마거릿이 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["said Margaret, stepping forwards, “for anything you could do, Higgins, with the best will in the world, you would, may be, get nine shillings a week; may be ten, at the outside.", "“히긴스, 세상에서 가장 좋은 마음으로 무슨 일을 하시든, 아마 주급으로 9실링쯤 받을 거예요. 잘해야 10실링이겠지요."], ["Food is much the same as here, except that you might have a little garden——”", "먹을 것은 여기와 별로 다르지 않고요. 텃밭을 조금 가질 수 있다는 것 말고는——”"]], [["“The childer could work at that,”", "“그건 어린것들이 할 수 있소.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’m sick o’ Milton anyways, and Milton is sick o’ me.”", "“나는 어차피 밀턴이 지긋지긋하고, 밀턴도 내가 지긋지긋하고.”"]], [["“You must not go the South,”", "“그래도 남쪽으로 가시면 안 돼요.”"], ["said Margaret, “for all that.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["You could not stand it.", "“견디지 못하실 거예요."], ["You would have to be out all weathers.", "어떤 날씨에도 바깥에 나가 있어야 해요."], ["It would kill you with rheumatism.", "류머티즘으로 죽고 말 거예요."], ["The mere bodily work at your time of life would break you down.", "그 나이에 하는 몸일만으로도 몸이 망가질 거고요."], ["The fare is far different to what you have been accustomed to.”", "먹는 것도 익숙해진 것과는 아주 달라요.”"]], [["“I’se nought particular about my meat,”", "“나는 먹는 것 가지고 까다롭게 굴지 않소.”"], ["said he, as if offended.", "그가 기분이 상한 듯 말했다."]], [["“But you’ve reckoned on having butcher’s meat once a day, if you’re in work; pay for that out of your ten shillings, and keep those poor children if you can.", "“하지만 일을 하고 있으면 하루 한 번은 푸줏간 고기를 먹으리라 셈하셨잖아요. 그 10실링에서 그 값을 치르고, 그러고도 저 가엾은 아이들을 건사해 보세요."], ["I owe it to you—since it’s my way of talking that has set you off on this idea—to put it all clear before you.", "저는 그것을 다 낱낱이 말씀드릴 빚이 있어요. 이런 생각을 하게 만든 것이 바로 제가 말하던 방식이니까요."], ["You would not bear the dulness of the life; you don’t know what it is; it would eat you away like rust.", "그 삶의 따분함을 견디지 못하실 거예요. 그게 어떤 것인지 모르시잖아요."], ["Those that have lived there all their lives, are used to soaking in the stagnant waters.", "그것은 녹처럼 사람을 갉아먹어요. 거기서 평생 살아온 이들은 고인 물에 젖어 지내는 데 익숙해요."], ["They labour on from day to day, in the great solitude of steaming fields—never speaking or lifting up their poor, bent, downcast heads.", "그들은 김이 오르는 들판의 그 커다란 적막 속에서 날마다 일할 뿐이에요. 말 한마디 나누는 법도, 가엾게 구부러져 숙인 고개를 드는 법도 없이요."], ["The hard spadework robs their brain of life; the sameness of their toil deadens their imagination; they don’t care to meet to talk over thoughts and speculations, even of the weakest, wildest kind, after their work is done; they go home brutishly tired, poor creatures!", "고된 삽질은 그들의 머리에서 생기를 앗아 가고, 늘 똑같은 노역은 상상력을 죽여 버려요. 일이 끝난 뒤에 아무리 시시하고 터무니없는 것이라도 생각이며 궁리를 나누자고 모일 마음조차 없어요."], ["caring for nothing but food and rest.", "그들은 짐승처럼 지쳐서 집으로 돌아가요, 가엾은 사람들! 먹을 것과 쉴 것 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳하지 않고요."], ["You could not stir them up into any companionship, which you get in a town as plentiful as the air you breathe, whether it be good or bad—and that I don’t know; but I do know, that you of all men are not one to bear a life among such labourers.", "도시에서라면 숨 쉬는 공기만큼이나 넉넉히 얻는 그 어울림으로 그들을 일으켜 세우지는 못하실 거예요. 그것이 좋은 것인지 나쁜 것인지는 저도 모르겠어요."], ["What would be peace to them, would be eternal fretting to you.", "하지만 이것만은 알아요. 다른 누구도 아닌 당신 같은 분이야말로 그런 일꾼들 틈에서 살아갈 수 있는 사람이 아니라는 것을요."], ["Think no more of it, Nicholas, I beg.", "그들에게 평온인 것이 당신에게는 끝없는 속앓이가 될 거예요. 그러니 부디 더는 생각하지 마세요, 니컬러스."], ["Besides, you could never pay to get mother and children all there—that’s one good thing.”", "게다가 어머니와 아이들을 모두 그리로 데려갈 여비도 결코 못 대실 테고요. 그건 그나마 다행한 일이네요.”"]], [["“I’ve reckoned for that.", "“그것도 셈해 봤소."], ["One house mun do for us a’, and the furniture o’ t’other would go a good way.", "집 한 채면 우리 모두 지낼 수 있고, 다른 집 세간을 팔면 꽤 보탬이 될 거요."], ["And men theer mun have their families to keep—m’appen six or seven childer. God help ’em!”", "그리고 거기 사람들도 먹여 살릴 식구가 있을 테지, 어쩌면 어린것이 여섯 일곱은 될 테고."], ["said he, more convinced by his own presentation of the facts than by all Margaret had said, and suddenly renouncing the idea, which had but recently formed itself in a brain worn out by the day’s fatigue and anxiety.", "하느님이 그들을 도우시기를!” 그는 마거릿이 한 온갖 말보다 자기가 스스로 늘어놓은 사실들에 더 설득되어 말했고, 하루의 고단함과 근심으로 지친 머릿속에 이제 막 생겨났던 그 생각을 문득 접어 버렸다."], ["“God help ’em!", "“하느님이 그들을 도우시기를!"], ["North an’ South have each getten their own troubles.", "북쪽이나 남쪽이나 저마다 제 고생을 안고 있구먼."], ["If work’s sure and steady theer, labour’s paid at starvation wages; while here we’n rucks o’ money coming in one quarter, and ne’er a farthing th’ next.", "거기서는 일감이 확실하고 꾸준하다면 품삯이 굶어 죽을 만큼밖에 안 되고, 여기서는 한 철에 돈이 무더기로 들어오다가 다음 철에는 한 푼도 안 들어오고."], ["For sure, th’ world is in a confusion that passes me or any other man to understand; it needs fettling, and who’s to fettle it if it’s as yon folks say, and there’s nought but what we see?”", "정말이지 세상은 나나 다른 어떤 사람이나 알아먹을 수 없을 만큼 뒤죽박죽이오. 손을 봐야 하는데, 저 사람들 말대로 우리 눈에 보이는 것 말고는 아무것도 없다면 대체 누가 손을 본단 말이오?”"]], [["Mr.", "헤일 씨는 빵에 버터를 바르느라 바빴다. 마거릿은 그것이 다행이었다."], ["Hale was busy cutting bread and butter; Margaret was glad of this, for she saw that Higgins was better left to himself: that if her father began to speak ever so mildly on the subject of Higgins’s thoughts, the latter would consider himself challenged to an argument, and would feel himself bound to maintain his own ground.", "히긴스는 혼자 내버려 두는 편이 낫다는 것을, 아버지가 히긴스의 생각에 대해 아무리 부드럽게 말을 꺼내더라도 히긴스는 자기에게 논쟁이 걸려 왔다고 여기고 제 자리를 지켜야 한다고 생각하리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그녀와 아버지는 히긴스가 먹는지 마는지도 거의 모르는 채 아주 든든하게 한 끼를 다 먹을 때까지 심상한 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["She and her father kept up an indifferent conversation until Higgins, scarcely aware whether he ate or not, had made a very substantial meal.", "그러고 나서 그는 의자를 식탁에서 밀어내고 두 사람이 하는 이야기에 관심을 가져 보려 애썼다. 그러나 소용없었다."], ["Then he pushed his chair away from the table, and tried to take an interest in what they were saying; but it was of no use; and he fell back into dreamy gloom.", "그는 다시 몽롱한 우울 속으로 가라앉았다. 문득 마거릿이 말했다."], ["Suddenly, Margaret said (she had been thinking of it for some time, but the words had stuck in her throat), “Higgins have you been to Marlborough Mills to seek for work?”", "한동안 생각하고 있었으나 말이 목에 걸려 나오지 않던 것이었다. “히긴스, 일자리를 구하러 말버러 공장에 가 보셨어요?”"]], [["“Thornton’s?”", "“손턴네 말이오?”"], ["asked he.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Ay, I’ve been at Thornton’s”", "“그럼, 손턴네도 가 봤소.”"]], [["“And what did he say?”", "“그래서 그분이 뭐라고 하셨어요?”"]], [["“Such a chap as me is not like to see the measter.", "“나 같은 놈이 공장주 얼굴을 볼 성싶소."], ["Th’ o’erlooker bid me go and be d——d.”", "감독이 나더러 꺼져 버리라며 욕지거리를 퍼붓더군.”"]], [["“I wish you had seen Mr. Thornton,”", "“자네가 손턴 씨를 만났더라면 좋았을걸.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“He might not have given you work, but he would not have used such language.”", "“그가 일자리를 주지는 않았을지 몰라도 그런 말을 쓰지는 않았을 걸세.”"]], [["“As to th’ language, I’m welly used to it; it dunnot matter to me.", "“그런 말이야 이골이 났소. 나한테는 아무렇지도 않소."], ["I’m not nesh mysel’ when I’m put out.", "나도 성이 나면 무른 사람은 아니고."], ["It were th’ fact that I were na wanted theer, no more nor ony other place, as I minded.”", "내가 마음에 걸린 건 거기서도 나를 원치 않는다는 사실, 다른 어느 데서도 마찬가지라는 사실이었소.”"]], [["“But I wish you had seen Mr. Thornton,”", "“하지만 저도 손턴 씨를 만나 보셨으면 좋았겠어요.”"], ["repeated Margaret.", "마거릿이 되풀이했다."], ["“Would you go again—it’s a good deal to ask, I know—but would you go to-morrow and try him?", "“한 번 더 가 주시겠어요? 큰 부탁이라는 건 알아요."], ["I should be so glad if you would.”", "그래도 내일 가서 그분에게 청해 보시겠어요? 그래 주시면 정말 기쁘겠어요.”"]], [["“I’m afraid it would be of no use,”", "“소용없을 것 같구나.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, in a low voice.", "헤일 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It would be better to let me speak to him.”", "“내가 그에게 말하는 편이 낫겠다.”"], ["Margaret still looked at Higgins for his answer.", "마거릿은 여전히 히긴스의 대답을 기다리며 그를 바라보았다."], ["Those grave soft eyes of hers were difficult to resist.", "진지하고 부드러운 그 눈길은 뿌리치기 어려웠다."], ["He gave a great sigh.", "그가 크게 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“It would tax my pride above a bit; if it were for mysel’, I could stand a deal o’ clemming first; I’d sooner knock him down than ask a favour from him.", "“그건 내 자존심에 여간 힘든 일이 아니오. 나 자신을 위해서라면 굶는 걸 한참 더 견디고 말지."], ["I’d a deal sooner be flogged mysel’; but yo’re not a common wench, axing yo’r pardon, nor yet have yo’ common ways about yo’.", "그 사람한테 은혜를 청하느니 차라리 한 대 갈기겠소. 차라리 내가 매를 맞고 말겠소."], ["I’ll e’en make a wry face, and go at it to-morrow. Dunna yo’ think that he’ll do it.", "하지만 실례되는 말이지만 아가씨는 흔한 계집아이가 아니고, 아가씨가 하는 짓도 흔한 짓이 아니오."], ["That man has it in him to be burnt at the stake afore he’ll give in.", "나는 얼굴을 찡그리면서라도 내일 그 일에 나서 보겠소. 그 사람이 들어줄 거라고는 생각지 마시오."], ["I’ll do it for yo’r sake, Miss Hale, and it’s first time in my life as e’er I give way to a woman.", "그 사람은 굽히느니 화형대에서 타 죽을 위인이오. 아가씨를 봐서 하는 거요, 헤일 아가씨."], ["Neither my wife nor Bess could e’er say that much again me.”", "그리고 내 평생 여자한테 진 건 이번이 처음이오. 내 안사람도 베스도 그런 소리는 못 했지.”"]], [["“All the more do I thank you,”", "“그러니 더욱 고마워요.”"], ["said Margaret, smiling.", "마거릿이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“Though I don’t believe you: I believe you have just given way to wife and daughter as much as most men.”", "“그래도 저는 그 말을 믿지 않아요. 히긴스도 여느 남자들만큼은 아내와 딸에게 져 주셨을 거예요.”"]], [["“And as to Mr. Thornton,”", "“그리고 손턴 씨 일이라면,”"], ["said Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Hale, “I’ll give you a note to him, which, I think I may venture say, will ensure you a hearing.”", "“내가 그에게 보내는 쪽지를 하나 써 주겠네. 감히 말하건대 그것이면 적어도 자네 말을 들어 주기는 할 걸세.”"]], [["“I thank yo’ kindly, sir, but I’d as lief stand on my own bottom.", "“고맙습니다, 나리. 하지만 나는 내 발로 서는 편이 낫겠소."], ["I dunnot stomach the notion of having favour curried for me, by one as doesn’t know the ins and outs of the quarrel.", "싸움의 속사정을 모르는 사람을 통해 남의 비위를 맞춰 얻어 내는 건 내 속이 받질 않소."], ["Meddling ’twixt master and man is liker meddling ’twixt husband and wife than aught else: it takes a deal of wisdom to do ony good.", "공장주와 일꾼 사이에 끼어드는 건 남편과 아내 사이에 끼어드는 것보다 더하면 더했지 덜하지 않소. 조금이라도 좋게 하려면 여간한 지혜가 드는 게 아니오."], ["I’ll stand guard at the lodge door.", "나는 정문 수위실 앞에 지키고 서 있겠소."], ["I’ll stand there fro’ six in the morning till I get speech on him.", "아침 6시부터 그와 말을 나눌 때까지 거기 서 있겠소."], ["But I’d liefer sweep th’ streets, if paupers had na’ got hold on that work.", "하지만 거리 청소 일이 거지들 차지만 아니었어도 차라리 그 일을 하고 말았을 거요."], ["Dunna yo’ hope, miss.", "아가씨, 기대는 마시오."], ["There’ll be more chance o’ getting milk out of a flint.", "부싯돌에서 젖을 짜내는 편이 더 가망 있을 거요."], ["I wish yo’ a very good night, and many thanks to yo’.”", "아주 편안한 밤 되시고, 여러모로 고맙소.”"]], [["“You’ll find your shoes by the kitchen fire; I took them there to dry,”", "“신발은 부엌 난롯가에 있어요. 제가 말리려고 거기 가져다 놓았거든요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["He turned round and looked at her steadily, and then he brushed his lean hand across his eyes and went his way.", "그는 돌아서서 그녀를 물끄러미 바라보았다. 그러고는 여윈 손으로 눈가를 훔치고 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["“How proud that man is!”", "“저 사람은 어찌 저리 자존심이 센지!”"], ["said her father, who was a little annoyed at the manner in which Higgins had declined his intercession with Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨에게 나서 주겠다는 자기 제안을 히긴스가 물리친 태도에 조금 언짢아진 아버지가 말했다."]], [["“He is,”", "“그러네요.”"], ["said Margaret; “but what grand makings of a man there are in him, pride and all.”", "마거릿이 말했다. “하지만 자존심까지 다 합쳐서, 저 사람 안에는 얼마나 훌륭한 사람이 될 재목이 들어 있는지요.”"]], [["“It’s amusing to see how he evidently respects the part in Mr. Thornton’s character which is like his own.”", "“손턴 씨의 성품 가운데 자기와 닮은 부분을 저 사람이 저렇게 대놓고 존경하는 걸 보면 재미있구나.”"]], [["“There’s granite in all these northern people, papa, is there not?”", "“이 북쪽 사람들에게는 다들 화강암 같은 데가 있어요, 아버지. 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“There was none in poor Boucher, I am afraid; none in his wife either.”", "“가엾은 바우처에게는 없었던 것 같구나. 그 아내에게도 없고.”"]], [["“I should guess by their tones that they had Irish blood in them. I wonder what success he’ll have to-morrow.", "“말투로 미루어 보면 아일랜드 피가 섞였을 것 같아요."], ["If he and Mr. Thornton would speak out together as man to man—if Higgins would forget that Mr.", "저분이 내일 얼마나 잘될지 궁금해요."], ["Thornton was a master, and speak to him as he does to us—and if Mr. Thornton would be patient enough to listen to him with his human heart, not with his master’s ears—”", "저분과 손턴 씨가 사람 대 사람으로 툭 터놓고 이야기한다면, 히긴스가 손턴 씨는 공장주라는 것을 잊고 우리에게 말하듯 그분에게 말한다면, 그리고 손턴 씨가 공장주의 귀가 아니라 사람의 마음으로 저분의 말을 들어 줄 만큼 참을성이 있다면—”"]], [["“You are getting to do Mr. Thornton justice at last, Margaret,”", "“드디어 네가 손턴 씨를 공정하게 보게 되었구나, 마거릿.”"], ["said her father, pinching her ear.", "아버지가 그녀의 귀를 꼬집으며 말했다."]], [["Margaret had a strange choking at her heart, which made her unable to answer, “Oh!”", "마거릿은 가슴이 이상하게 메어 대답할 수가 없었다. “아,”"], ["thought she, “I wish I were a man, that I could go and force him to express his disapprobation, and tell him honestly that I knew I deserved it.", "그녀는 생각했다. “내가 남자였더라면."], ["It seems hard to lose him as a friend just when I had begun to feel his value.", "그러면 그분에게 찾아가서 못마땅하다는 말을 하게 만들고, 내가 그 말을 들어 마땅한 줄 안다고 정직하게 말할 수 있을 텐데. 그분의 진가를 이제 막 느끼기 시작한 참에 벗으로서 그분을 잃는다는 건 참 모진 일이야."], ["How tender he was with dear mamma!", "그분은 사랑하는 어머니에게 얼마나 다정했던가!"], ["If it were only for her sake, I wish he would come, and then at least I should know how much I was abased in his eyes.”", "어머니를 봐서라도 그분이 와 주었으면. 그러면 적어도 내가 그분의 눈에 얼마나 낮아졌는지는 알 수 있을 텐데.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII", "# 제38장"]], [["“Then proudly, proudly up she rose, Tho’ the tear was in her e’e, Whate’er ye say, think what ye may, Ye’s get na word frae me!”", "“그러자 그 처녀는 도도하게, 도도하게 몸을 일으켰네, 두 눈에는 눈물이 어렸어도. 그대들이 무슨 말을 하든, 무슨 생각을 하든, 내게서는 한마디도 듣지 못하리라!”"], ["SCOTCH BALLAD.", "— 스코틀랜드 발라드."]], [["It was not merely that Margaret was known to Mr.", "마거릿이 거짓말을 했다는 것을 손턴 씨가 알고 있다는 사실만이 문제는 아니었다."], ["Thornton to have spoken falsely,—though she imagined that for this reason only was she so turned in his opinion,—but that this falsehood of hers bore a distinct reference in his mind to some other lover.", "그녀는 오직 그 이유 하나로 자신이 그의 눈 밖에 났다고 여겼지만, 정작 문제는 그 거짓말이 그의 마음속에서 어떤 다른 연인과 또렷이 결부되어 있다는 데 있었다. 그는 그녀와 어떤 낯선 사내 사이에 오간 그 애틋하고 간절한 눈길을 잊을 수가 없었다."], ["He could not forget the fond and earnest look that had passed between her and some other man—the attitude of familiar confidence, if not of positive endearment.", "노골적인 애무까지는 아니라 해도 허물없이 기대는 그 몸짓을. 그 생각이 끊임없이 그를 찔러 댔다."], ["The thought of this perpetually stung him; it was a picture before his eyes, wherever he went and whatever he was doing.", "어디를 가든 무엇을 하든 그것은 눈앞에 걸린 한 폭의 그림이었다. 게다가 (그는 그것을 떠올리며 이를 갈았다) 시각은 땅거미 지는 황혼이었고, 장소는 집에서 그토록 멀고 인적도 드문 곳이었다."], ["In addition to this (and he ground his teeth as he remembered it), was the hour, dusky twilight; the place, so far away from home, and comparatively unfrequented.", "그의 더 고결한 자아는 처음에 이 모든 정황이 우연일 수도, 결백할 수도, 해명될 수도 있다고 말했다. 그러나 일단 그녀에게 사랑하고 사랑받을 권리가 있음을 인정하고 나면 — 그리고 그가 그 권리를 부정할 무슨 근거가 있단 말인가?"], ["His nobler self had said at first, that all this last might be accidental, innocent, justifiable; but once allow her right to love and be beloved (and had he any reason to deny her right?—had not her words been severely explicit when she cast his love away from her?), she might easily have been beguiled into a longer walk, on to a later hour than she had anticipated.", "그녀가 그의 사랑을 내던질 때 그 말은 냉혹하리만치 분명하지 않았던가? — 그녀가 예상보다 긴 산책에, 예상보다 늦은 시각까지 이끌려 갔다는 것쯤은 얼마든지 있을 수 있는 일이었다."], ["But that falsehood!", "그러나 그 거짓말! 무언가 잘못된 것이, 감추어야 할 것이 있음을 치명적으로 의식하고 있음을 드러낸, 그녀답지 않은 그 거짓말."], ["which showed a fatal consciousness of something wrong, and to be concealed, which was unlike her.", "그는 그녀에게 그만한 공정함은 베풀었다. 물론 내내 그녀가 자기 존경을 받을 자격이 조금도 없는 여자라고 믿을 수 있었다면 차라리 마음이 놓였겠지만."], ["He did her that justice, though all the time it would have been a relief to believe her utterly unworthy of his esteem.", "바로 이것이 그의 비참함을 이루었다. 그는 그녀를 뜨겁게 사랑했고, 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 그녀가 다른 어떤 여자보다 사랑스럽고 뛰어나다고 여겼다."], ["It was this that made the misery—that he passionately loved her, and thought her, even with all her faults, more lovely and more excellent than any other woman; yet he deemed her so attached to some other man, so led away by her affection for him, as to violate her truthful nature.", "그러면서도 그녀가 어떤 다른 사내에게 그토록 매여 있고 그에 대한 애정에 그토록 끌려간 나머지 진실한 천성마저 저버렸다고 생각했다. 그녀를 더럽힌 그 거짓말이야말로 그녀가 다른 사내를 얼마나 눈멀게 사랑하는지에 대한 증거였다."], ["The very falsehood that stained her, was a proof how blindly she loved another—this dark, slight, elegant, handsome man—while he himself was rough, and stern, and strongly made.", "그 검은 머리에 호리호리하고 우아하고 잘생긴 사내를. 그에 비해 자신은 거칠고 굳세고 우락부락하게 생긴 사내였다."], ["He lashed himself into an agony of fierce jealousy.", "그는 스스로를 채찍질해 사나운 질투의 고통 속으로 몰아넣었다. 그 눈길, 그 몸짓을 떠올렸다!"], ["He thought of that look, that attitude!—how he would have laid his life at her feet for such tender glances, such fond detention!", "그런 다정한 눈길, 그런 애틋한 붙듦을 받을 수만 있다면 그는 목숨을 그녀의 발치에 내려놓았으리라! 그는 폭도의 광기로부터 그녀가 기계적으로 자기를 감싸 준 일을 소중히 여겼던 자신을 비웃었다."], ["He mocked at himself, for having valued the mechanical way in which she had protected him from the fury of the mob; now he had seen how soft and bewitching she looked when with a man she really loved.", "이제 그는 그녀가 진정으로 사랑하는 사내와 함께 있을 때 얼마나 부드럽고 홀리는 얼굴이 되는지를 보아 버린 것이다. 그는 그녀의 모진 말들을 한마디 한마디 되새겼다."], ["He remembered, point by point, the sharpness of her words—“There was not a man in all that crowd for whom she would not have done as much, far more readily than for him.”", "“그 군중 속 어느 누구를 위해서라도 그만큼은, 아니 훨씬 더 기꺼이 했을 것”이라던 말을. 그는 그들에게서 유혈을 막고자 하는 그녀의 바람을 폭도와 나누어 가졌을 뿐이었다."], ["He shared with the mob, in her desire of averting bloodshed from them; but this man, this hidden lover, shared with nobody; he had looks, words, hand-cleavings, lies, concealment, all to himself.", "그러나 이 사내, 이 숨겨진 연인은 누구와도 나누지 않았다. 눈길도, 말도, 맞잡은 손도, 거짓말도, 은폐도 모두 그 사내 혼자만의 것이었다."]], [["Mr.", "손턴 씨는 평생 지금처럼 신경이 곤두선 적이 없었음을 스스로 알고 있었다. 무엇이든 묻는 사람마다 말이라기보다 짖는 소리에 가까운 짧고 퉁명스러운 대답을 던지고 싶어졌다."], ["Thornton was conscious that he had never been so irritable as he was now, in all his life long; he felt inclined to give a short abrupt answer, more like a bark than a speech, to every one that asked him a question; and this consciousness hurt his pride: he had always piqued himself on his self-control, and control himself he would.", "그리고 그런 자각이 그의 자존심을 상하게 했다. 그는 늘 자제력을 자랑으로 삼아 왔고, 기어이 자신을 다스릴 작정이었다."], ["So the manner was subdued to a quiet deliberation, but the matter was even harder and sterner than common.", "그리하여 태도는 차분한 신중함으로 눌러 두었으나, 내용은 여느 때보다 오히려 더 딱딱하고 매서웠다. 집에서 그는 평소보다 더 말이 없었다."], ["He was more than usually silent at home; employing his evenings in a continual pace backwards and forwards, which would have annoyed his mother exceedingly if it had been practised by any one else; and did not tend to promote any forbearance on her part even to this beloved son.", "저녁이면 내내 방 안을 오락가락 서성이며 보냈는데, 다른 사람이 그랬다면 어머니를 지독히 짜증 나게 했을 짓이었다. 그리고 그것은 그 사랑하는 아들에게조차 어머니의 인내심을 북돋우는 데 도움이 되지 않았다."]], [["“Can you stop—can you sit down for a moment?", "“좀 멈출 수 없겠니 — 잠깐 앉을 수 없겠어?"], ["I have something to say to you, if you would give up that everlasting walk, walk, walk.”", "그 끝없는 걷기, 걷기, 걷기를 그만둔다면 네게 할 말이 있다.”"]], [["He sat down instantly, on a chair against the wall.", "그는 즉시 벽에 붙은 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“I want to speak to you about Betsy.", "“벳시 이야기를 하고 싶구나."], ["She says she must leave us; that her lover’s death has so affected her spirits she can’t give her heart to her work.”", "그 애가 우리를 떠나야겠다는구나. 연인이 죽은 뒤로 마음이 너무 상해서 일에 마음을 붙일 수가 없다고 말이야.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“그러시지요."], ["I suppose other cooks are to be met with.”", "요리사야 다른 사람도 구할 수 있을 테니까요.”"]], [["“That’s so like a man.", "“꼭 남자다운 소리로구나."], ["It’s not merely the cooking, it is that she knows all the ways of the house.", "요리만이 문제가 아니라, 그 애가 이 집안 사정을 속속들이 안다는 게 문제야."], ["Besides, she tells me something about your friend Miss Hale.”", "게다가 그 애가 네 친구 헤일 양에 대해 뭔가 이야기를 하더구나.”"]], [["“Miss Hale is no friend of mine.", "“헤일 양은 제 친구가 아닙니다."], ["Mr. Hale is my friend.”", "헤일 씨가 제 친구지요.”"]], [["“I am glad to hear you say so, for if she had been your friend, what Betsy says would have annoyed you.”", "“그런 말을 들으니 다행이다. 그 애가 네 친구였다면 벳시의 말이 널 언짢게 했을 테니 말이야.”"]], [["“Let me hear it,”", "“들어 보지요.”"], ["said he, with the extreme quietness of manner he had been assuming for the last few days.", "그가 요 며칠 몸에 익혀 온 그 극도로 차분한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Betsy says, that the night on which her lover—I forget his name—for she always calls him ‘he’——”", "“벳시 말로는, 그 애 연인이 — 이름은 잊었구나, 그 애는 늘 ‘그이’라고만 부르니 —”"]], [["“Leonards.”", "“레너즈요.”"]], [["“The night on which Leonards was last seen at the station—when he was last seen on duty, in fact—Miss Hale was there, walking about with a young man who, Bessy believes, killed Leonards by some blow or push.”", "“레너즈가 역에서 마지막으로 목격된 그날 밤 — 사실은 근무 중에 마지막으로 목격된 그날 밤에 — 헤일 양이 거기 있었고, 어떤 젊은 남자와 함께 거닐고 있었다는구나. 벳시는 그 남자가 무언가로 치거나 밀어서 레너즈를 죽였다고 믿고 있어.”"]], [["“Leonards was not killed by any blow or push.”", "“레너즈는 어떤 타격이나 밀침으로 죽은 것이 아닙니다.”"]], [["“How do you know?”", "“그걸 네가 어떻게 아느냐?”"]], [["“Because I distinctly put the question to the surgeon of the Infirmary.", "“제가 병원 외과의에게 똑똑히 물어보았으니까요."], ["He told me there was an internal disease of long standing, caused by Leonards’ habit of drinking to excess; that the fact of his becoming rapidly worse while in a state of intoxication, settled the question as to whether the last fatal attack was caused by excess of drinking, or the fall.”", "그가 말하기를, 레너즈에게는 지나치게 술을 마시는 버릇 때문에 생긴 오래된 내장 질환이 있었다고 했습니다. 그리고 그가 취한 상태에서 급격히 나빠졌다는 사실이, 마지막 치명적인 발작이 과음 때문인지 낙상 때문인지 하는 문제를 결론지어 준다고 했습니다.”"]], [["“The fall!", "“낙상이라니!"], ["What fall?”", "무슨 낙상 말이냐?”"]], [["“Caused by the blow or push of which Betsy speaks.”", "“벳시가 말한 그 타격이나 밀침으로 생긴 낙상 말입니다.”"]], [["“Then there was a blow or push?”", "“그러면 타격이나 밀침이 있기는 있었단 말이구나?”"]], [["“I believe so.”", "“그랬다고 봅니다.”"]], [["“And who did it?”", "“그럼 그건 누가 한 짓이냐?”"]], [["“As there was no inquest, in consequence of the doctor’s opinion, I cannot tell you.”", "“의사의 소견 때문에 검시가 없었으니, 저도 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.”"]], [["“But Miss Hale was there?”", "“하지만 헤일 양이 그 자리에 있었지?”"]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."]], [["“And with a young man?”", "“게다가 젊은 남자와 함께?”"]], [["Still no answer.", "여전히 대답이 없었다."], ["At last he said: “I tell you, mother, that there was no inquest—no inquiry.", "마침내 그가 말했다. “어머니, 검시는 없었다고 말씀드리지 않습니까."], ["No judicial inquiry, I mean.”", "아무 조사도 없었습니다. 사법적인 조사 말입니다.”"]], [["“Betsy says that Woolmer (some man she knows, who is in a grocer’s shop out at Crampton) can swear that Miss Hale was at the station at that hour, walking backwards and forwards with a young man.”", "“벳시 말로는 울머라는 사람이 — 그 애가 아는 사람인데, 크램프턴 저쪽 식료품 가게에 있다더구나 — 그 시각에 헤일 양이 역에서 젊은 남자와 함께 이리저리 거닐고 있었다고 맹세할 수 있다는구나.”"]], [["“I don’t see what we have to do with that.", "“그것이 저희와 무슨 상관인지 모르겠습니다."], ["Miss Hale is at liberty to please herself.”", "헤일 양은 자기 뜻대로 할 자유가 있으니까요.”"]], [["“I’m glad to hear you say so,”", "“그런 말을 들으니 다행이구나.”"], ["says Mrs. Thornton, eagerly.", "손턴 부인이 열을 올리며 말했다."], ["“It certainly signifies very little to us—not at all to you, after what has passed!", "“그야 우리에게는 거의 아무 의미도 없는 일이지. 그런 일이 있었으니 네게는 더더욱 아무것도 아니고!"], ["but I—I made a promise to Mrs.", "하지만 나는 — 나는 헤일 부인에게 약속을 했다."], ["Hale, that I would not allow her daughter to go wrong without advising and remonstrating with her.", "그 딸이 그릇된 길로 가는 것을 충고와 나무람 없이 그냥 두지는 않겠다고 말이야."], ["I shall certainly let her know my opinion of such conduct.”", "나는 반드시 그 아이에게 그런 행실에 대한 내 생각을 알려 줄 참이다.”"]], [["“I do not see any harm in what she did that evening,” said Mr.", "“그날 저녁 그분이 한 일에서 저는 아무 해로움도 보지 못하겠습니다.”"], ["Thornton, getting up, and coming near to his mother; he stood by the chimney-piece with his face turned away from the room.", "손턴 씨가 일어나 어머니 곁으로 다가서며 말했다. 그는 벽난로 선반 옆에 서서 방 쪽으로부터 얼굴을 돌리고 있었다."]], [["“You would not have approved of Fanny’s being seen out, after dark, in rather a lonely place, walking about with a young man.", "“패니가 어두워진 뒤에 꽤나 외진 곳에서 젊은 남자와 거니는 모습을 남에게 보였다면 너는 좋게 여기지 않았을 게다."], ["I say nothing of the taste which could choose the time, when her mother lay unburied, for such a promenade.", "제 어미가 아직 땅에 묻히지도 않은 때를 그런 산책에 골라잡을 수 있는 그 취향에 대해서는 아무 말도 않으마."], ["Should you have liked your sister to have been noticed by a grocer’s assistant for doing so?”", "네 누이가 그런 짓을 하다가 식료품점 점원의 눈에 띄었다면 너는 좋았겠느냐?”"]], [["“In the first place, as it is not many years since I myself was a draper’s assistant, the mere circumstance of a grocer’s assistant noticing any act does not alter the character of the act to me.", "“우선, 저 자신이 포목상 점원 노릇을 한 지 그리 여러 해 되지 않았으니, 식료품점 점원이 어떤 행동을 눈여겨보았다고 해서 그 행동의 성격이 제게 달라지지는 않습니다."], ["And in the next place, I see a great deal of difference between Miss Hale and Fanny.", "그리고 다음으로, 저는 헤일 양과 패니 사이에 아주 큰 차이가 있다고 봅니다."], ["I can imagine that the one may have weighty reasons, which may and ought to make her overlook any seeming impropriety in her conduct.", "한쪽은 자기 행실에 겉으로 드러나는 부적절함이 있더라도 그것을 넘어서게 만들 만한, 또 마땅히 그래야 할 무거운 이유를 지녔을 수 있다고 상상할 수 있습니다."], ["I never knew Fanny have weighty reasons for anything.", "패니가 무슨 일에든 무거운 이유를 지녔던 적은 없지요."], ["Other people must guard her.", "그 애는 남들이 지켜 주어야 합니다."], ["I believe Miss Hale is a guardian to herself.”", "헤일 양은 스스로 자신을 지키는 사람이라고 저는 믿습니다.”"]], [["“A pretty character of your sister, indeed!", "“참으로 네 누이에 대한 훌륭한 평이로구나!"], ["Really, John, one would have thought Miss Hale had done enough to make you clear-sighted.", "정말이지 존, 헤일 양이 네 눈을 뜨게 하기에 충분한 짓을 했으리라고 여겼을 텐데 말이다."], ["She drew you on to an offer, by a bold display of pretended regard for you,—to play you off against this very young man, I’ve no doubt.", "그 아이는 널 좋아하는 척 대담하게 내보여 청혼을 이끌어 낸 게야. 틀림없이 바로 그 젊은 남자를 상대로 널 이용해 먹으려고 말이지."], ["Her whole conduct is clear to me now.", "이제 그 아이의 행실이 내게는 훤히 보인다."], ["You believe he is her lover, I suppose—you agree to that.”", "너도 그 남자가 그 아이의 연인이라고 믿는 게지. 그것만은 인정하겠지.”"]], [["He turned round to his mother; his face was very gray and grim.", "그가 어머니 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 얼굴은 몹시 잿빛이고 험했다."], ["“Yes, mother.", "“예, 어머니."], ["I do believe he is her lover.”", "저도 그 사람이 그분의 연인이라고 믿습니다.”"], ["When he had spoken, he turned round again; he writhed himself about, like one in bodily pain.", "그렇게 말하고 나서 그는 다시 몸을 돌렸다. 몸이 아픈 사람처럼 몸부림쳤다."], ["He leant his face against his hand.", "얼굴을 손에 기댔다."], ["Then before she could speak, he turned sharp again:", "그러고는 어머니가 입을 열기도 전에 홱 돌아섰다."]], [["“Mother.", "“어머니."], ["He is her lover, whoever he is; but she may need help and womanly counsel;—there may be difficulties or temptations which I don’t know.", "그 사람이 누구든 그분의 연인인 것은 맞습니다. 그러나 그분에게는 도움과 여인의 조언이 필요할지도 모릅니다."], ["I fear there are.", "제가 모르는 어려움이나 유혹이 있을지도 모르지요."], ["I don’t want to know what they are; but as you have ever been a good—ay!", "저는 있다고 봅니다. 그것이 무엇인지는 알고 싶지 않습니다."], ["and a tender mother to me, go to her, and gain her confidence, and tell her what is best to be done.", "다만 어머니께서 늘 제게 좋은 — 예! 다정한 어머니셨으니, 그분에게 가셔서 그분의 신뢰를 얻고 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 일러 주십시오."], ["I know that something is wrong; some dread, which must be a terrible torture to her.”", "무언가 잘못되어 있다는 것을 저는 압니다. 그분에게는 끔찍한 고문일 것이 분명한 어떤 두려움이 있습니다.”"]], [["“For God’s sake, John!”", "“맙소사, 존!”"], ["said his mother, now really shocked, “what do you mean?", "이제야 정말로 충격을 받은 어머니가 말했다. “그게 무슨 소리냐?"], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 소리야?"], ["What do you know?”", "넌 무엇을 알고 있는 게냐?”"]], [["He did not reply to her.", "그는 어머니에게 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“John!", "“존!"], ["I don’t know what I shan’t think unless you speak.", "네가 말하지 않으면 내가 무슨 생각인들 안 하겠느냐."], ["You have no right to say what you have done against her.”", "방금 그 아이에게 불리한 말을 해 놓고 그대로 둘 권리는 네게 없다.”"]], [["“Not against her, mother!", "“그분에게 불리한 말이 아닙니다, 어머니!"], ["I could not speak against her.”", "저는 그분에게 불리한 말은 할 수가 없습니다.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그렇다면!"], ["you have no right to say what you have done, unless you say more.", "더 말하지 않을 거면 방금 한 말도 할 권리가 없는 게다."], ["These half-expressions are what ruin a woman’s character.”", "이런 어중간한 말들이 여자의 평판을 망치는 법이야.”"]], [["“Her character!", "“그분의 평판이라니요!"], ["Mother, you do not dare—”", "어머니, 감히 —” 그가 홱 돌아서서 이글거리는 눈으로 어머니의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["he faced about, and looked into her face with his flaming eyes.", "그러고는 단호한 침착과 위엄으로 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “이보다 더는 말씀드리지 않겠습니다."], ["Then, drawing himself up into determined composure and dignity, he said, “I will not say any more than this, which is neither more nor less than the simple truth, and I am sure you believe me,—I have good reason to believe, that Miss Hale is in some strait and difficulty connected with an attachment which, of itself, from my knowledge of Miss Hale’s character, is perfectly innocent and right.", "이것은 그저 있는 그대로의 진실일 뿐이고, 어머니께서도 저를 믿으시리라 확신합니다. 저는 헤일 양이 어떤 애착과 얽힌 곤경과 어려움에 처해 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있습니다."], ["What my reason is, I refuse to tell.", "그리고 그 애착 자체는, 제가 아는 헤일 양의 성품으로 보아 조금도 흠 없고 옳은 것입니다."], ["But never let me hear any one say a word against her, implying any more serious imputation than that she now needs the counsel of some kind and gentle woman.", "그 근거가 무엇인지는 말씀드리기를 거부합니다. 다만 누구든 그분에 대해, 지금 어질고 온화한 여인의 조언이 필요하다는 것 이상의 무거운 혐의를 넌지시 비추는 말을 하는 것은 결코 듣지 않겠습니다."], ["You promised Mrs. Hale to be that woman!”", "어머니께서는 헤일 부인에게 그런 여인이 되어 주겠다고 약속하셨습니다!”"]], [["“No!” said Mrs.", "“아니다!”"], ["Thornton.", "손턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“I am happy to say, I did not promise kindness and gentleness, for I felt at the time that it might be out of my power to render these to one of Miss Hale’s character and disposition.", "“다행히도 나는 어짊과 온화함을 약속하지는 않았다. 그때도 헤일 양 같은 성품과 기질을 지닌 사람에게 그런 것을 베푸는 일은 내 힘 밖일지 모른다고 느꼈으니까."], ["I promised counsel and advice, such as I would give to my own daughter; I shall speak to her as I would do to Fanny, if she had gone gallivanting with a young man in the dusk.", "내가 약속한 것은 내 딸에게 해 주었을 법한 충고와 조언이었다. 패니가 어스름에 젊은 남자와 쏘다녔다면 내가 그 애에게 했을 말을 그 아이에게도 하겠다."], ["I shall speak with relation to the circumstances I know, without being influenced either one way or another by the ‘strong reasons’ which you will not confide to me.", "나는 내가 아는 정황에 관해서만 말할 것이고, 네가 내게 털어놓지 않으려는 그 ‘충분한 근거’에 이리로도 저리로도 휘둘리지 않겠다."], ["Then I shall have fulfilled my promise, and done my duty.", "그러면 나는 내 약속을 다한 것이고 내 의무를 한 것이다.”"]], [["“She will never bear it,”", "“그분은 결코 견디지 못할 겁니다.”"], ["said he passionately.", "그가 격하게 말했다."]], [["“She will have to bear it, if I speak in her dead mother’s name.”", "“내가 죽은 제 어미의 이름으로 말한다면 견뎌야만 하겠지.”"]], [["“Well!”", "“그만!”"], ["said he, breaking away, “don’t tell me any more about it.", "그가 몸을 빼며 말했다. “더는 그 이야기를 제게 하지 마십시오."], ["I cannot endure to think of it.", "생각하는 것조차 견딜 수가 없습니다."], ["It will be better that you should speak to her any way, than that she should not be spoken to at all.—Oh!", "아예 아무 말도 듣지 못하느니 어떤 식으로든 어머니께서 말씀해 주시는 편이 낫겠지요."], ["that look of love!”", "— 아, 그 사랑에 찬 눈길!”"], ["continued he, between his teeth, as he bolted himself into his own private room.", "그는 제 방에 들어가 빗장을 지르며 이를 악물고 말을 이었다."], ["“And that cursed lie; which showed some terrible shame in the background, to be kept from the light in which I thought she lived perpetually!", "“그리고 그 저주스러운 거짓말! 내가 늘 그 사람이 살고 있다고 믿었던 그 빛으로부터 감추어야 할 어떤 무서운 수치가 뒤에 있음을 드러낸 그 거짓말!"], ["Oh, Margaret, Margaret!", "아, 마거릿, 마거릿!"], ["Mother, how you have tortured me! Oh!", "어머니, 어머니가 나를 얼마나 괴롭히셨는지!"], ["Margaret, could you not have loved me?", "아, 마거릿, 당신은 나를 사랑할 수 없었단 말입니까?"], ["I am but uncouth and hard, but I would never have led you into any falsehood for me.”", "나는 그저 투박하고 무뚝뚝한 사내일 뿐이지만, 나를 위해 당신을 어떤 거짓말로도 이끌지는 않았을 텐데.”"]], [["The more Mrs. Thornton thought over what her son had said, in pleading for a merciful judgment for Margaret’s indiscretion, the more bitterly she felt inclined towards her.", "손턴 부인은 아들이 마거릿의 경솔함에 대해 너그러운 판단을 청하며 한 말을 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록 그 아가씨에게 더 모질어졌다."], ["She took a savage pleasure in the idea of “speaking her mind”", "그녀는 의무의 이행이라는 탈을 쓰고 “속에 있는 말을 다 해 주는”"], ["to her, in the guise of fulfilment of a duty.", "생각에 사나운 쾌감을 느꼈다."], ["She enjoyed the thought of showing herself untouched by the “glamour,”", "마거릿이 많은 사람들에게 뒤집어씌우는 힘을 지녔음을 익히 아는 그 “홀림”에 자신은 조금도 물들지 않았음을 보여 준다는 생각이 즐거웠다."], ["which she was well aware Margaret had the power of throwing over many people.", "그녀는 제 희생자의 아름다움을 그리며 경멸스럽게 코웃음을 쳤다."], ["She snorted scornfully over the picture of the beauty of her victim; her jet black hair, her clear smooth skin, her lucid eyes would not help to save her one word of the just and stern reproach which Mrs. Thornton spent half the night in preparing to her mind.", "그 새까만 머리도, 맑고 매끄러운 살결도, 투명한 눈도, 손턴 부인이 밤을 반이나 새워 마음속으로 다듬어 놓은 정당하고 준엄한 질책의 한마디도 덜어 주지는 못하리라."]], [["“Is Miss Hale within?”", "“헤일 양은 안에 계신가?” 그녀는 그녀가 안에 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["She knew she was, for she had seen her at the window, and she had her feet inside the little hall before Martha had half answered the question.", "창가에서 그 모습을 보았기 때문이다. 그리고 마사가 그 물음에 절반쯤 대답했을 때 이미 그녀의 발은 작은 현관 안에 들어와 있었다."]], [["Margaret was sitting alone, writing to Edith, and giving her many particulars of her mother’s last days.", "마거릿은 혼자 앉아 이디스에게 편지를 쓰며 어머니의 마지막 날들에 대해 이런저런 자세한 이야기를 들려주던 참이었다."], ["It was a softening employment, and she had to brush away the unbidden tears as Mrs. Thornton was announced.", "마음을 누그러뜨리는 일이었으므로, 손턴 부인이 오셨다는 전갈을 받았을 때 그녀는 절로 흐른 눈물을 훔쳐 내야 했다."]], [["She was so gentle and ladylike in her mode of reception that her visitor was somewhat daunted; and it became impossible to utter the speech, so easy of arrangement with no one to address it to.", "그녀의 맞이하는 태도가 어찌나 부드럽고 기품 있었던지 손님은 다소 기가 꺾였다. 그리고 들려줄 사람이 없을 때는 그토록 손쉽게 꾸며 놓았던 그 연설을 입 밖에 내기가 불가능해졌다."], ["Margaret’s low rich voice was softer than usual; her manner more gracious, because in her heart she was feeling very grateful to Mrs. Thornton for the courteous attention of her call.", "마거릿의 낮고 풍부한 목소리는 여느 때보다 부드러웠고, 태도는 한결 상냥했다."], ["She exerted herself to find subjects of interest for conversation; praised Martha, the servant whom Mrs.", "마음속으로 손턴 부인이 예의를 갖추어 찾아와 준 것을 무척 고맙게 여기고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 애를 써서 이야깃거리를 찾아냈다."], ["Thornton had found for them: had asked Edith for a little Greek air, about which she had spoken to Miss Thornton. Mrs.", "손턴 부인이 구해 준 하녀 마사를 칭찬했고, 손턴 양에게 이야기했던 그리스 소곡을 이디스에게 부탁해 두었다고 말했다."], ["Thornton was fairly discomfited.", "손턴 부인은 완전히 당황했다."], ["Her sharp Damascus blade seemed out of place, and useless among rose-leaves.", "그 날카로운 다마스쿠스 칼날은 장미 꽃잎들 사이에서 자리에 맞지 않고 쓸모도 없어 보였다."], ["She was silent because she was trying to task herself up to her duty.", "그녀는 스스로를 채근해 제 의무에 이르게 하려고 애쓰느라 잠자코 있었다."], ["At last she stung herself into its performance by a suspicion which, in spite of all probability, she allowed to cross her mind, that all this sweetness was put on with a view of propitiating Mr.", "마침내 그녀는 온갖 정황에 어긋나는데도 마음에 스치도록 내버려 둔 어떤 의심으로 스스로를 찔러 그 일을 해내게 만들었다."], ["Thornton; that, somehow, the other attachment had fallen through, and that it suited Miss Hale’s purpose to recall her rejected lover.", "이 모든 상냥함이 손턴 씨의 마음을 달래려는 속셈으로 꾸민 것이며, 어찌 된 셈인지 그 다른 애착이 깨어지는 바람에 헤일 양이 물리쳤던 구혼자를 도로 불러들이는 편이 제 속셈에 맞게 되었으리라는 의심이었다."], ["Poor Margaret!", "가엾은 마거릿!"], ["there was perhaps so much truth in the suspicion as this; that Mrs.", "그 의심에는 어쩌면 이만한 진실은 담겨 있었는지도 모른다."], ["Thornton was the mother of one whose regard she valued, and feared to have lost; and this thought unconsciously added to her natural desire of pleasing one who was showing her kindness by her visit.", "손턴 부인은 마거릿이 소중히 여기던, 그리고 잃었을까 두려워하던 존경의 주인공의 어머니라는 것. 그리고 그 생각이 자기도 모르게, 찾아옴으로써 친절을 베풀어 준 사람을 기쁘게 해 주고 싶다는 자연스러운 마음에 보태졌다는 것."], ["Mrs. Thornton stood up to go, but yet she seemed to have something more to say.", "손턴 부인은 가려고 일어섰으나 아직 무언가 더 할 말이 있는 듯했다."], ["She cleared her throat and began:", "그녀는 목을 가다듬고 입을 열었다."]], [["“Miss Hale, I have a duty to perform.", "“헤일 양, 나는 이행해야 할 의무가 있어요."], ["I promised your poor mother that, as far as my poor judgment went, I would not allow you to act in any way wrongly, or (she softened her speech down a little here) inadvertently, without remonstrating; at least without offering advice, whether you took it or not.”", "나는 가엾은 그대 어머니에게, 내 얕은 판단이 미치는 한, 그대가 어떤 식으로든 그릇되게, 혹은 (그녀는 여기서 말을 조금 눅였다) 무심코 행동하는 것을 나무람 없이 그냥 두지는 않겠다고 약속했습니다. 적어도 그대가 받아들이든 아니든 조언은 하겠다고 말이오.”"]], [["Margaret stood before her, blushing like any culprit, with her eyes dilating as she gazed at Mrs. Thornton.", "마거릿은 죄인처럼 얼굴을 붉힌 채 그녀 앞에 서서 손턴 부인을 바라보며 눈을 크게 떴다."], ["She thought she had come to speak to her about the falsehood she had told—that Mr.", "그녀는 부인이 자신이 한 거짓말에 대해 이야기하러 온 줄로 알았다."], ["Thornton had employed her to explain the danger she had exposed herself to, of being confuted in full court!", "손턴 씨가 부인을 시켜, 법정에서 만인 앞에 그 말이 뒤집힐 위험에 스스로를 내던졌음을 일깨우려는 것이라고 생각한 것이다!"], ["and although her heart sank to think he had not rather chosen to come himself, and upbraid her, and receive her penitence, and restore her again to his good opinion, yet she was too much humbled not to bear any blame on this subject patiently and meekly.", "그리고 그가 차라리 몸소 찾아와 자신을 꾸짖고 뉘우침을 받아들여 다시 자기를 좋게 보아 주기를 택하지 않았다는 생각에 가슴이 내려앉기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀는 이 문제에 관해서라면 어떤 책망도 참을성 있게 온순히 받아들이지 않을 수 없을 만큼 겸허해져 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton went on:", "손턴 부인이 말을 이었다."]], [["“At first, when I heard from one of my servants, that you had been seen walking about with a gentleman, so far from home as the Outwood station, at such a time of the evening, I could hardly believe it.", "“처음에 내 하인 중 하나에게서, 그대가 아웃우드 역이라는 집에서 그토록 먼 곳에서 그런 저녁 시각에 어떤 신사와 함께 거니는 모습을 보였다는 말을 들었을 때, 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요."], ["But my son, I am sorry to say, confirmed her story.", "그런데 안타깝게도 내 아들이 그 말을 확인해 주더군요."], ["It was indiscreet, to say the least; many a young woman has lost her character before now——”", "아무리 좋게 말해도 그것은 경솔한 짓이었소. 여태껏 많은 젊은 여자들이 그렇게 해서 평판을 잃었지요 —”"]], [["Margaret’s eyes flashed fire.", "마거릿의 눈에서 불꽃이 튀었다."], ["This was a new idea—this was too insulting. If Mrs.", "이것은 생각지도 못한 이야기였다."], ["Thornton had spoken to her about the lie she had told, well and good—she would have owned it, and humiliated herself.", "이것은 너무나 모욕적이었다. 손턴 부인이 자신이 한 거짓말에 대해 말했다면 좋다, 그녀는 그것을 인정하고 스스로를 낮추었을 것이다."], ["But to interfere with her conduct—to speak of her character! she—Mrs.", "그러나 자신의 행실에 참견하다니, 자신의 평판을 입에 올리다니!"], ["Thornton, a mere stranger—it was too impertinent!", "그것도 손턴 부인이, 한낱 남남인 사람이! 너무나 무례했다!"], ["She would not answer her—not one word.", "그녀는 대답하지 않으리라, 단 한마디도."], ["Mrs. Thornton saw the battle-spirit in Margaret’s eyes, and it called up her combativeness also.", "손턴 부인은 마거릿의 눈에 서린 싸움의 기운을 보았고, 그것이 그녀의 호전성 또한 불러일으켰다."]], [["“For your mother’s sake, I have thought it right to warn you against such improprieties; they must degrade you in the long run in the estimation of the world, even if in fact they do not lead you to positive harm.”", "“그대 어머니를 생각해서, 나는 그런 무분별한 짓에 대해 그대에게 경고하는 것이 옳다고 여겼소. 설령 실제로 그것이 그대를 확실한 해악으로 이끌지는 않는다 해도, 길게 보면 세상의 평가 속에서 그대를 깎아내리고 말 테니까요.”"]], [["“For my mother’s sake,”", "“제 어머니를 생각해서라면,”"], ["said Margaret, in a tearful voice, “I will bear much; but I cannot bear everything.", "마거릿이 눈물 젖은 목소리로 말했다, “저는 많은 것을 견디겠습니다. 그러나 모든 것을 견딜 수는 없어요."], ["She never meant me to be exposed to insult, I am sure.”", "어머니는 결코 제가 모욕당하기를 바라지 않으셨을 겁니다, 분명히요.”"]], [["“Insult, Miss Hale!”", "“모욕이라니요, 헤일 양!”"]], [["“Yes, madam,”", "“그렇습니다, 부인.”"], ["said Margaret more steadily, “it is insult.", "마거릿이 한결 단단해진 목소리로 말했다."], ["What do you know of me that should lead you to suspect—Oh!”", "“모욕입니다. 저에 대해 무엇을 아시기에 그런 의심을 — 아!”"], ["said she, breaking down, and covering her face with her hands—“I know now, Mr.", "그녀는 무너져 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리며 말했다."], ["Thornton has told you——”", "“이제 알겠어요, 손턴 씨가 말씀하셨군요 —”"]], [["“No, Miss Hale,” said Mrs.", "“아닙니다, 헤일 양.”"], ["Thornton, her truthfulness causing her to arrest the confession Margaret was on the point of making, though her curiosity was itching to hear it.", "손턴 부인이 말했다. 호기심은 그 말을 듣고 싶어 근질거렸으나, 정직함이 그녀로 하여금 마거릿이 막 하려던 고백을 가로막게 했다."], ["“Stop. Mr.", "“멈추시오."], ["Thornton has told me nothing.", "손턴 씨는 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았소."], ["You do not know my son.", "그대는 내 아들을 모르오."], ["You are not worthy to know him.", "그대는 그를 알 자격이 없어요."], ["He said this.", "그 애는 이렇게 말했소."], ["Listen, young lady, that you may understand, if you can, what sort of a man you rejected.", "젊은 아가씨, 들어 보시오. 그대가 물리친 사람이 어떤 사내인지 알 수 있다면 알라고 말이오."], ["This Milton manufacturer, his great tender heart scorned as it was scorned, said to me only last night, ‘Go to her.", "이 밀턴의 공장주는, 그 크고 다정한 마음이 그토록 멸시당하고도, 바로 어젯밤 내게 이렇게 말했소. ‘어머니, 그분에게 가 보십시오."], ["I have good reason to know that she is in some strait arising out of some attachment; and she needs womanly counsel.’ I believe those were his very words.", "그분이 어떤 애착에서 비롯된 곤경에 처해 있다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 제게 있습니다. 그분에게는 여인의 조언이 필요합니다.’ 그것이 그 애가 한 바로 그 말이었다고 나는 믿소."], ["Farther than that—beyond admitting the fact of your being at the Outwood station with a gentleman, on the evening of the twenty-sixth—he has said nothing—not one word against you.", "그 이상은 — 26일 저녁에 그대가 어떤 신사와 함께 아웃우드 역에 있었다는 사실을 인정한 것 말고는 — 그 애는 아무 말도 하지 않았소. 그대에게 불리한 말은 한마디도."], ["If he has knowledge of anything which should make you sob so, he keeps it to himself.”", "그대를 그토록 흐느끼게 만들 무언가를 그 애가 알고 있다 해도, 그 애는 그것을 혼자 간직하고 있어요.”"]], [["Margaret’s face was still hidden in her hands, the fingers of which were wet with tears. Mrs.", "마거릿의 얼굴은 여전히 두 손에 가려져 있었고, 그 손가락들은 눈물에 젖어 있었다."], ["Thornton was a little mollified.", "손턴 부인은 조금 누그러졌다."]], [["“Come, Miss Hale.", "“자, 헤일 양."], ["There may be circumstances, I’ll allow, that, if explained, may take off from the seeming impropriety.”", "설명만 된다면 겉보기의 부적절함을 덜어 줄 만한 사정이 있을 수도 있겠지요, 그건 인정하리다.”"]], [["Still no answer.", "여전히 대답이 없었다."], ["Margaret was considering what to say; she wished to stand well with Mrs.", "마거릿은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 궁리하고 있었다. 그녀는 손턴 부인과 좋게 지내고 싶었다."], ["Thornton; and yet she could not, might not, give any explanation.", "그러면서도 어떤 설명도 할 수 없었고, 해서도 안 되었다."], ["Mrs. Thornton grew impatient.", "손턴 부인은 참을성을 잃어 갔다."]], [["“I shall be sorry to break off an acquaintance; but for Fanny’s sake—as I told my son, if Fanny had done so we should consider it a great disgrace—and Fanny might be led away——”", "“알고 지내던 사이를 끊게 되는 것은 유감이오. 하지만 패니를 위해서는 — 내가 아들에게도 말했지만, 패니가 그런 짓을 했다면 우리는 그것을 큰 수치로 여겼을 거요 — 그리고 패니가 그릇된 길로 이끌릴 수도 있으니 —”"]], [["“I can give you no explanation,”", "“저는 아무 설명도 드릴 수 없습니다.” 마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["said Margaret, in a low voice.", "“저는 잘못을 저질렀습니다. 그러나 부인께서 생각하시거나 알고 계신 그런 식으로는 아닙니다."], ["“I have done wrong, but not in the way you think or know about. I think Mr.", "손턴 씨가 저를 부인보다 더 자비롭게 판단하시는 것 같군요.”"], ["Thornton judges me more mercifully than you;”—she had hard work to keep herself from choking with her tears—“but, I believe, madam, you mean to do rightly.”", "그녀는 눈물에 목이 메지 않도록 애를 써야 했다. “하지만 부인, 저는 부인께서 옳게 하려 하신다는 것은 믿습니다.”"]], [["“Thank you,” said Mrs.", "“고맙군요.”"], ["Thornton, drawing herself up; “I was not aware that my meaning was doubted.", "손턴 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “내 뜻이 의심받고 있는 줄은 몰랐소."], ["It is the last time I shall interfere.", "내가 참견하는 것은 이번이 마지막이오."], ["I was unwilling to consent to do it, when your mother asked me.", "그대 어머니가 부탁했을 때도 나는 그러겠다고 하기가 내키지 않았어요."], ["I had not approved of my son’s attachment to you, while I only suspected it.", "나는 내 아들이 그대에게 마음을 두었다는 것을 짐작만 하고 있던 동안에도 그것을 못마땅해했소."], ["You did not appear to me worthy of him.", "그대는 내게 그 애에게 어울리는 사람으로 보이지 않았으니까."], ["But when you compromised yourself as you did at the time of the riot, and exposed yourself to the comments of servants and workpeople, I felt it was no longer right to set myself against my son’s wish of proposing to you—a wish, by the way, which he had always denied entertaining until the day of the riot.”", "그러나 그대가 폭동 때 그렇게 스스로를 위태롭게 하고 하인들과 직공들의 입방아에 오르게 되었을 때, 나는 그대에게 청혼하겠다는 아들의 뜻을 더는 거스르는 것이 옳지 않다고 느꼈소. 덧붙이자면, 그 애는 폭동이 있던 날까지 그런 뜻을 품은 적이 없다고 늘 부인해 왔지요.”"], ["Margaret winced, and drew in her breath with a long, hissing sound; of which, however, Mrs.", "마거릿은 움찔하며 길게 쉭 하는 소리를 내며 숨을 들이켰다. 그러나 손턴 부인은 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Thornton took no notice.", "“그 애가 갔지요."], ["“He came; you had apparently changed your mind.", "그런데 그대는 마음이 바뀐 듯 보였소."], ["I told my son yesterday, that I thought it possible, short as was the interval, you might have heard or learnt something of this other lover——”", "나는 어제 아들에게, 그 짧은 사이에도 그대가 이 다른 연인에 대해 무언가 듣거나 알게 되었을 수 있겠다고 말했소 —”"]], [["“What must you think of me, madam?”", "“저를 어떻게 보시는 겁니까, 부인?”"], ["asked Margaret, throwing her head back with proud disdain, till her throat curved outwards like a swan’s.", "마거릿이 도도한 경멸로 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 물었다. 목이 백조의 목처럼 바깥으로 휘었다."], ["“You can say nothing more, Mrs. Thornton.", "“더는 하실 말씀이 없으실 겁니다, 손턴 부인."], ["I decline every attempt to justify myself for anything.", "저는 무엇에 대해서든 저를 변호하려는 시도를 일절 사양하겠습니다."], ["You must allow me to leave the room.”", "이 방에서 나가도록 허락해 주십시오.”"]], [["And she swept out of it with the noiseless grace of an offended princess.", "그러고는 모욕당한 공주처럼 소리 없이 우아하게 방을 빠져나갔다."], ["Mrs. Thornton had quite enough of natural humour to make her feel the ludricrousness of the position in which she was left.", "손턴 부인은 자기가 남겨진 처지의 우스꽝스러움을 느낄 만큼은 타고난 해학을 갖추고 있었다."], ["There was nothing for it but to show herself out.", "스스로 걸어 나가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["She was not particularly annoyed at Margaret’s way of behaving.", "그녀는 마거릿의 태도에 딱히 화가 나지도 않았다."], ["She did not care enough for her for that. She had taken Mrs.", "그만큼 그 아가씨를 마음에 두고 있지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Thornton’s remonstrance to the full as keenly to heart as that lady expected; and Margaret’s passion at once mollified her visitor, far more than any silence or reserve could have done.", "그 아가씨는 손턴 부인의 나무람을 부인이 기대한 만큼 뼈아프게 가슴에 새겼다. 그리고 마거릿의 격정은 어떤 침묵이나 삼감보다 훨씬 더 손님의 마음을 곧바로 누그러뜨렸다."], ["It showed the effect of her words. “My young lady,”", "그것은 제 말이 먹혔음을 보여 주었으니까."], ["thought Mrs.", "“아가씨,”"], ["Thornton to herself; “you’ve a pretty good temper of your own.", "손턴 부인은 속으로 생각했다. “성깔이 꽤나 대단하시군."], ["If John and you had come together, he would have had to keep a tight hand over you, to make you know your place.", "만일 존과 그대가 맺어졌더라면 그 애는 그대를 단단히 틀어쥐고 제 분수를 알게 만들어야 했겠지."], ["But I don’t think you will go a-walking again with your beau, at such an hour of the day, in a hurry.", "하지만 그대가 하루 중 그런 시각에 그 사내와 다시 거니는 일은 서둘러 하지 않을 성싶군."], ["You’ve too much pride and spirit in you for that.", "그러기에는 자존심과 기개가 너무 세니까."], ["I like to see a girl fly out at the notion of being talked about.", "나는 남의 입에 오른다는 생각에 발끈하는 처녀가 보기 좋더라."], ["It shows they’re neither giddy, nor bold by nature.", "그건 천성이 들뜨지도 뻔뻔하지도 않다는 뜻이니까."], ["As for that girl, she might be bold, but she’d never be giddy.", "저 처녀로 말하자면 뻔뻔할지는 몰라도 들뜨지는 않을 게야."], ["I’ll do her that justice.", "그만한 공정함은 베풀어 주마."], ["Now as to Fanny, she’d be giddy, and not bold.", "그런데 패니로 말하자면, 그 애는 들뜨기는 해도 뻔뻔하지는 않겠지."], ["She’s no courage in her, poor thing!”", "가엾은 것, 그 애에게는 배짱이라고는 없으니!”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was not spending the morning so satisfactorily as his mother.", "손턴 씨는 어머니만큼 흡족하게 아침을 보내고 있지 못했다."], ["She, at any rate, was fulfilling her determined purpose.", "어머니는 어쨌든 작정한 목적을 이루어 가는 중이었다."], ["He was trying to understand where he stood; what damage the strike had done him.", "그는 자기가 어디에 서 있는지, 파업이 자기에게 얼마만 한 손해를 입혔는지 헤아려 보려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["A good deal of his capital was locked up in new and expensive machinery; and he had also bought cotton largely, with a view to some great orders which he had in hand.", "그의 자본 가운데 상당한 몫이 새로 들여온 값비싼 기계에 묶여 있었다. 게다가 그는 손에 쥔 몇몇 큰 주문을 내다보고 면화도 대량으로 사들여 놓은 참이었다."], ["The strike had thrown him terribly behindhand, as to the completion of these orders.", "파업은 그 주문들을 마무리하는 데에 그를 끔찍하게 뒤처지게 만들었다."], ["Even with his own accustomed and skilled workpeople, he would have had some difficulty in fulfilling his engagements; as it was, the incompetence of the Irish hands, who had to be trained to their work, at a time requiring unusual activity, was a daily annoyance.", "손에 익고 숙련된 제 직공들을 데리고서도 그는 계약을 지키는 데 얼마간 애를 먹었을 터였다. 그런 마당에, 유난한 활기가 필요한 때에 일부터 가르쳐야 하는 아일랜드 직공들의 서투름은 날마다의 골칫거리였다."]], [["It was not a favourable hour for Higgins to make his request.", "히긴스가 청을 넣기에 좋은 때는 아니었다."], ["But he had promised Margaret to do it at any cost.", "그러나 그는 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 그렇게 하겠다고 마거릿에게 약속한 터였다."], ["So, though every moment added to his repugnance, his pride, and his sullenness of temper, he stood leaning against the dead wall, hour after hour, first on one leg, then on the other.", "그래서 그는 매 순간 반감과 자존심과 뾰로통한 심사가 더해 가는데도 막다른 담벼락에 기대선 채 몇 시간이고 이 다리로 섰다 저 다리로 섰다 했다."], ["At last the latch was sharply lifted, and out came Mr. Thornton.", "마침내 걸쇠가 날카롭게 들리더니 손턴 씨가 나왔다."]], [["“I want for to speak to yo’, sir.”", "“나리께 드릴 말씀이 있소.”"]], [["“Can’t stay now, my man.", "“지금은 시간이 없네."], ["I’m too late as it is.”", "이미 늦었어.”"]], [["“Well, sir, I reckon I can wait till yo’ come back.”", "“그러면 나리께서 돌아오실 때까지 기다리면 되겠구먼요.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was half way down the street.", "손턴 씨는 이미 길 저만치 가 있었다."], ["Higgins sighed. But it was no use.", "히긴스는 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["To catch him in the street, was his only chance of seeing “the measter!”", "그러나 별수 없었다. 길에서 붙드는 것이 “공장주 나리”를 만날 유일한 기회였다."], ["if he had rung the lodge bell or even gone up to the house to ask for him, he would have been referred to the overlooker.", "수위실 종을 울리거나 집으로 찾아가 그를 찾았더라면 감독에게 넘겨졌을 것이다. 그래서 그는 다시 가만히 섰다."], ["So he stood still again, vouchsafing no answer, but a short nod of recognition to the few men who knew and spoke to him, as the crowd drove out the millyard at dinner-time, and scowling with all his might at the Irish “knobsticks” who had just been imported.", "점심때가 되어 사람들이 공장 마당에서 우르르 몰려나올 때 그를 알아보고 말을 거는 몇몇에게는 대답 없이 짧게 고개만 끄덕여 보이고, 갓 들여온 아일랜드 “파업 파괴자들”에게는 있는 힘껏 험한 눈길을 던지면서."], ["At last Mr. Thornton returned.", "마침내 손턴 씨가 돌아왔다."]], [["“What!", "“아니!"], ["you there still!”", "자네 아직도 거기 있나!”"]], [["“Ay, sir, I mun speak to yo’.”", "“예, 나리. 꼭 드릴 말씀이 있소.”"]], [["“Come in here, then.", "“그럼 이리 들어오게."], ["Stay, we’ll go across the yard; the men are not come back, and we shall have it to ourselves.", "아니, 마당을 가로질러 가지. 사람들이 아직 돌아오지 않았으니 우리끼리 조용히 이야기할 수 있겠군."], ["These good people, I see, are at dinner,”", "보아하니 이 착한 사람들이 밥을 먹고 있구먼.”"], ["said he, closing the door of the porter’s lodge.", "그가 수위실 문을 닫으며 말했다."]], [["He stopped to speak to the overlooker.", "그는 걸음을 멈추고 감독에게 말을 건넸다."], ["The latter said in a low tone:", "감독이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I suppose you know, sir, that that man is Higgins, one of the leaders of the Union; he that made that speech in Hurstfield.”", "“나리, 저 사람이 히긴스라는 거 아시지요? 조합의 우두머리 중 하나입니다. 허스트필드에서 그 연설을 한 자예요.”"]], [["“No, I didn’t,”", "“몰랐네.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, looking round sharply at his follower.", "손턴 씨가 뒤따라오는 사내를 홱 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Higgins was known to him by name as a turbulent spirit.", "히긴스는 소란스러운 자로 그에게 이름만은 알려져 있었다."]], [["“Come along,”", "“따라오게.”"], ["said he, and his tone was rougher than before.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It is men such as this,”", "말투는 아까보다 더 거칠어져 있었다."], ["thought he, “who interrupt commerce and injure the very town they live in: mere demagogues, lovers of power at whatever cost to others.”", "‘상업을 가로막고 제가 사는 바로 그 도시를 해치는 것이 이런 자들이지.’ 그는 생각했다. ‘한낱 선동가들, 남이 어떤 대가를 치르든 권력만 좋아하는 자들.’"]], [["“Well, sir!", "“자, 이 사람아!"], ["what do you want with me?”", "내게 무슨 볼일인가?”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, facing round at him, as soon as they were in the counting-house of the mill.", "공장 사무실에 들어서자마자 손턴 씨가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“My name is Higgins”——", "“제 이름은 히긴스라 하는데—”"]], [["“I know that,”", "“그건 알아.”"], ["broke in Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 말을 잘랐다."], ["“What do you want, Mr. Higgins?", "“무엇을 원하나, 히긴스 씨?"], ["That’s the question.”", "그게 문제지.”"]], [["“I want work.”", "“일자리를 원하오.”"]], [["“Work!", "“일자리라!"], ["You’re a pretty chap to come asking me for work.", "내게 일자리를 청하러 오다니 참 대단한 사람이군."], ["You don’t want impudence, that’s very clear.”", "뻔뻔함만은 모자라지 않는 게 아주 분명해.”"]], [["“I’ve getten enemies and backbiters, like my betters; but I ne’er heerd o’ ony of them calling me o’er-modest,”", "“나도 윗분들처럼 원수도 있고 험담꾼도 있소만, 그중 누구도 나더러 너무 겸손하다고 하는 소리는 못 들어 봤소.”"], ["said Higgins.", "히긴스가 말했다."], ["His blood was a little roused by Mr. Thornton’s manner, more than by his words.", "손턴 씨의 말보다도 그 태도에 그의 피가 조금 끓어올랐다."]], [["Mr. Thornton saw a letter addressed to himself on the table.", "손턴 씨는 탁자 위에 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 놓인 것을 보았다."], ["He took it up and read it through.", "그는 그것을 집어 끝까지 읽었다."], ["At the end, he looked up and said, “What are you waiting for?”", "다 읽고 나서 고개를 들고 말했다. “뭘 기다리고 있나?”"]], [["“An answer to th’ question I axed.”", "“내가 여쭌 물음에 대한 답을 기다리오.”"]], [["“I gave it you before.", "“아까 주지 않았나."], ["Don’t waste any more of your time.”", "더는 자네 시간 낭비하지 말게.”"]], [["“Yo’ made a remark, sir, on my impudence: but I were taught that it was manners to say either ‘yes’ or ‘no,’ when I were axed a civil question.", "“나리께서 제 뻔뻔함에 대해 한마디 하셨지만, 나는 예의를 갖춘 물음을 받으면 ‘예’든 ‘아니오’든 대답하는 것이 예절이라고 배웠소."], ["I should be thankfu’ to yo’ if yo’d give me work.", "일자리를 주신다면 고맙겠구먼요."], ["Hamper will speak to my being a good hand.”", "내가 좋은 직공이라는 건 햄퍼 씨가 말해 줄 거요.”"]], [["“I’ve a notion you’d better not send me to Hamper to ask for a character, my man.", "“내 생각에는 자네 인물평을 물으라고 나를 햄퍼에게 보내지 않는 편이 좋겠는데."], ["I might hear more than you’d like.”", "자네가 좋아할 것 이상을 듣게 될지도 모르니.”"]], [["“I’d take th’ risk.", "“그 위험은 감수하겠소."], ["Worst they could say of me is, that I did what I thought best, even to my own wrong.”", "그 사람들이 나에 대해 할 수 있는 최악의 말이라야, 내 손해를 무릅쓰고서라도 옳다고 여긴 대로 했다는 것뿐일 거요.”"]], [["“You’d better go and try them, then, and see whether they’ll give you work.", "“그럼 가서 그들에게 청해 보고 일자리를 주는지 보게."], ["I’ve turned off upwards of a hundred of my best hands, for no other fault than following you and such as you; and d’ye think I’ll take you on?", "나는 자네와 자네 같은 자들을 따랐다는 것 말고 아무 잘못도 없는 가장 좋은 직공을 백 명 넘게 내보냈네. 그런데 내가 자네를 들일 성싶은가?"], ["I might as well put a fire-brand into the midst of the cotton-waste.”", "그럴 바에야 솜 부스러기 한가운데에 불붙은 장작을 던져 넣는 편이 낫지.”"]], [["Higgins turned away: then the recollection of Boucher came over him, and he faced round with the greatest concession he could persuade himself to make.", "히긴스는 돌아섰다. 그러다 바우처의 기억이 밀려들었고, 그는 스스로를 설득해 할 수 있는 최대의 양보를 안고 다시 돌아섰다."]], [["“I’d promise yo’, measter, I’d not speak a word as could do harm, if so be yo’ did right by us; and I’d promise more: I’d promise that when I seed yo’ going wrong, and acting unfair, I’d speak to yo’ in private first; and that would be a fair warning. If yo’ and I did na agree in our opinion o’ your conduct, yo’ might turn me off at an hour’s notice.”", "“나리, 약속드리리다. 나리가 우리에게 옳게 대해 주시는 한 해가 될 말은 한마디도 하지 않겠소. 그리고 더 약속하리다. 나리가 그릇된 길로 가시거나 불공정하게 하시는 걸 보면 먼저 사사로이 나리께 말씀드리겠소. 그러면 공정한 경고가 되겠지요. 나리의 처사를 두고 나리와 내 생각이 맞지 않는다면 한 시간 전에 통보하고 나를 내보내셔도 좋소.”"]], [["“Upon my word, you don’t think small beer of yourself!", "“이런, 자네는 스스로를 대단히 높이 치는군!"], ["Hamper has had a loss of you.", "햄퍼가 자네를 잃어 손해가 컸겠어."], ["How came he to let you and your wisdom go?”", "어쩌다 자네와 자네의 그 지혜를 놓아 보냈다던가?”"]], [["“Well, we parted wi’ mutual dissatisfaction. I wouldn’t gi’e the pledge they were asking; and they wouldn’t have me at no rate. So I’m free to make another engagement; and as I said before, though I should na’ say it, I’m a good hand, measter, and a steady man—specially when I can keep fro’ drink; and that I shall do now, if I ne’er did afore.”", "“글쎄, 우리는 서로 못마땅해하며 갈라섰소. 나는 그들이 요구하는 서약을 해 줄 수 없었고, 그들은 어떤 조건으로도 나를 두려 하지 않았지요. 그러니 나는 다른 데 자유로이 매일 수 있는 몸이오. 아까도 말했듯이, 내 입으로 할 소리는 아니오만 나는 좋은 직공이오, 나리. 그리고 착실한 사람이오. 특히 술만 멀리할 수 있으면 말이오. 그리고 전에는 한 번도 못 했더라도 이제는 그렇게 하겠소.”"]], [["“That you may have more money laid up for another strike, I suppose?”", "“다음 파업에 대비해 돈을 더 모아 두려고 그러겠지?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니오! 그럴 여유가 있다면 고맙기나 하겠소."], ["I’d be thankful if I was free to do that; it’s for to keep th’ widow and childer of a man who was drove mad by them knobsticks o’ yourn; put out of his place by a Paddy that did na know weft fro’ warp.”", "나리네 그 파업 파괴자들 때문에 미쳐 버린 사람의 과부와 아이들을 먹여 살리려는 거요. 날실 씨실도 구별 못 하는 아일랜드 놈한테 제자리를 빼앗긴 사람 말이오.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그래!"], ["you’d better turn to something else, if you’ve any such good intention in your head.", "그런 갸륵한 뜻을 품고 있다면 다른 일로 돌아서는 게 낫겠군."], ["I shouldn’t advise you to stay in Milton: you’re too well known here.”", "밀턴에 머물라고 권하고 싶지는 않네. 여기서는 자네가 너무 잘 알려져 있어.”"]], [["“If it were summer,”", "“여름이라면,”"], ["said Higgins, “I’d take to Paddy’s work, and go as a navvy, or haymaking, or summat, and ne’er see Milton again.", "히긴스가 말했다, “나도 아일랜드 사람들 하는 일을 붙잡아 토목 인부로든 건초 일로든 뭐로든 나가서 밀턴은 다시 쳐다보지도 않겠소."], ["But it’s winter, and th’ childer will clem.”", "하지만 지금은 겨울이고, 아이들이 굶주릴 거요.”"]], [["“A pretty navvy you’d make!", "“자네가 무슨 토목 인부가 되겠나!"], ["why you couldn’t do half a day’s work at digging against an Irishman.”", "땅 파는 일로는 아일랜드 사람 상대로 반나절 몫도 못 해낼 텐데.”"]], [["“I’d only charge half-a-day for th’ twelve hours, if I could only do half-a-day’s work in th’ time.", "“열두 시간에 반나절 몫밖에 못 한다면 반나절 삯만 받으리다."], ["Yo’re not knowing of any place, where they could gi’ me a trial, away fro’ the mills, if I’m such a firebrand?", "내가 그렇게 위험한 불씨라면, 공장 말고 어디 시켜나 볼 만한 자리를 아시는 데는 없소?"], ["I’d take any wage they thought I was worth, for the sake of those childer.”", "그 아이들을 위해서라면 그들이 매기는 대로 어떤 삯이라도 받겠소.”"]], [["“Don’t you see what you would be? You’d be a knobstick.", "“자네가 무엇이 될지 모르겠나?"], ["You’d be taking less wages than the other labourers—all for the sake of another man’s children.", "자네가 바로 파업 파괴자가 되는 걸세. 남의 아이들을 위한답시고 다른 일꾼들보다 적은 삯을 받게 되는 거야."], ["Think how you’d abuse any poor fellow who was willing to take what he could get to keep his own children.", "제 자식을 먹이려고 받을 수 있는 만큼만 받겠다는 딱한 사람을 자네가 어떻게 욕할지 생각해 보게."], ["You and your Union would soon be down upon him.", "자네와 자네 조합이 곧바로 그 사람에게 달려들 테지."], ["No! no!", "안 돼!"], ["if it’s only for the recollection of the way in which you’ve used the poor knobsticks before now, I say No!", "안 돼! 자네가 지금껏 그 딱한 파업 파괴자들을 어떻게 다루어 왔는지 그것만 떠올려도, 나는 자네 물음에 아니오라고 말하겠네."], ["to your question. I’ll not give you work.", "자네에게 일자리를 주지 않겠어."], ["I won’t say, I don’t believe your pretext for coming and asking for work; I know nothing about it.", "자네가 일자리를 청하러 온 구실을 믿지 않는다는 말은 하지 않겠네. 나는 그에 대해 아무것도 모르니까."], ["It may be true, or it may not.", "참말일 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지."], ["It’s a very unlikely story, at any rate.", "어쨌든 있음 직하지 않은 이야기야."], ["Let me pass.", "비켜 주게."], ["I’ll not give you work.", "자네에게 일자리를 주지 않겠네."], ["There’s your answer.”", "그게 자네가 받을 답일세.”"]], [["“I hear, sir.", "“알아들었소, 나리."], ["I would na ha’ troubled you’, but that I were bid to come, by one as seemed to think yo’d getten some place in your heart.", "나리 마음속에 무언가 자리가 있으리라 여기는 듯한 사람이 가 보라고 시키지만 않았어도 나리를 성가시게 하지 않았을 거요."], ["Hoo were mistook, and I were misled.", "그 사람이 잘못 안 거고, 나는 잘못 이끌린 거지요."], ["But I’m not the first man as is misled by a woman.”", "하지만 여자한테 잘못 이끌린 사내가 내가 처음도 아니잖소.”"]], [["“Tell her to mind her own business the next time, instead of taking up your time and mine too.", "“다음번에는 자네 시간 내 시간 다 잡아먹지 말고 제 앞가림이나 하라고 그 여자에게 이르게."], ["I believe women are at the bottom of every plague in this world.", "나는 이 세상 모든 재앙의 밑바닥에는 여자가 있다고 믿네."], ["Be off with you.”", "물러가게.”"]], [["“I’m obleeged to you for a’ yo’r kindness, measter, and most of a’ for yo’r civil way o’ saying good-bye.”", "“나리의 온갖 친절에, 그중에서도 그 정중한 작별 인사에 참으로 고맙구먼요, 나리.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton did not deign a reply.", "손턴 씨는 대꾸할 가치조차 두지 않았다."], ["But, looking out of the window a minute later, he was struck with the lean, bent figure going out of the yard; the heavy walk was in strange contrast with the resolute, clear determination of the man to speak to him.", "그러나 잠시 뒤 창밖을 내다보다가 마당을 나서는 그 여위고 굽은 모습에 마음이 찔렸다. 그 무거운 걸음걸이는 자기에게 말을 걸겠다던 그 사내의 단호하고 또렷한 결의와 묘하게 어긋났다."], ["He crossed to the porter’s lodge:", "그는 수위실로 건너갔다."]], [["“How long has that man Higgins been waiting to speak to me?”", "“저 히긴스라는 사람이 나와 이야기하려고 얼마나 기다렸나?”"]], [["“He was outside the gate before eight o’clock, sir.", "“여덟 시도 되기 전부터 문밖에 있었습니다, 나리."], ["I think he’s been there ever since.”", "그때부터 내내 거기 있었던 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“And it is now—?”", "“그런데 지금이 —?”"]], [["“Just one, sir.”", "“막 한 시입니다, 나리.”"]], [["“Five hours,” thought Mr.", "‘다섯 시간이라.’ 손턴 씨는 생각했다."], ["Thornton; “it’s a long time for a man to wait, doing nothing but first hoping and then fearing.”", "‘사람이 아무것도 하지 않고 그저 바랐다 두려워했다 하며 기다리기에는 긴 시간이지.’"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX", "# 제39장"]], [["“Nay, I have done; you get no more of me; And I am glad, yea glad with all my heart, That thus so clearly I myself am free.”", "“아니, 나는 다 끝냈다. 그대는 더는 나를 얻지 못하리라."], ["DRAYTON.", "그리고 나는 기쁘다, 그렇다, 온 마음으로 기쁘다, 이토록 또렷이 내가 나 자신으로서 자유로우니.” — 드레이턴."]], [["Margaret shut herself up in her own room, after she had quitted Mrs. Thornton.", "마거릿은 손턴 부인 앞에서 물러나자마자 자기 방에 틀어박혔다."], ["She began to walk backwards and forwards, in her old habitual way of showing agitation; but, then, remembering that in that slightly-built house every step was heard from one room to another, she sate down until she heard Mrs.", "그녀는 마음이 흔들릴 때면 늘 그러하듯 방 안을 이리저리 오가기 시작했다. 그러나 이 허술하게 지은 집에서는 한 방의 발소리가 다른 방까지 죄다 들린다는 것이 떠올라, 손턴 부인이 무사히 집을 나서는 소리가 들릴 때까지 앉아 있었다."], ["Thornton go safely out of the house. She forced herself to recollect all the conversation that had passed between them; speech by speech, she compelled her memory to go through with it.", "그녀는 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화를 하나도 빠짐없이 떠올리도록 스스로를 몰아붙였다."], ["At the end, she rose up, and said to herself, in a melancholy tone:", "한마디 한마디, 그녀는 기억을 다그쳐 그것을 끝까지 되짚게 했다. 마침내 그녀는 몸을 일으키며 침울한 어조로 혼잣말했다."]], [["“At any rate, her words do not touch me; they fall off from me; for I am innocent of all the motives she attributes to me.", "“어쨌든 그분의 말은 나를 건드리지 못해. 그 말들은 나에게서 그대로 미끄러져 떨어질 뿐이야."], ["But still, it is hard to think that any one—any woman—can believe all this of another so easily.", "나는 그분이 내게 갖다 붙인 그 온갖 속셈과 아무 상관이 없으니까. 그래도 누군가가, 그것도 여자가 다른 여자에 대해 이 모든 것을 그토록 쉽게 믿어 버릴 수 있다는 걸 생각하면 견디기 힘들어."], ["It is hard and sad.", "힘들고 서글퍼."], ["Where I have done wrong, she does not accuse me—she does not know.", "내가 정말로 잘못한 일에 대해서는 그분이 나를 나무라지 않아. 모르시는 거야."], ["He never told her: I might have known he would not!”", "그가 어머니께 말하지 않은 거야. 그러지 않으리라는 걸 나도 알 수 있었을 텐데!”"]], [["She lifted up her head, as if she took pride in any delicacy of feeling which Mr. Thornton had shown.", "그녀는 손턴 씨가 보여 준 그 섬세한 마음씨를 자랑스러워하기라도 하듯 고개를 들었다."], ["Then, as a new thought came across her, she pressed her hands tightly together:", "그러다 새로운 생각이 스치고 지나가자 두 손을 꽉 맞잡았다."]], [["“He, too, must take poor Frederick for some lover.”", "“그분도 가엾은 오빠를 무슨 연인으로 여기고 있는 게 틀림없어.”"], ["(She blushed as the word passed through her mind.) “I see it now.", "(그 말이 머릿속을 지나가자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔다.) “이제야 알겠어."], ["It is not merely that he knows of my falsehood, but he believes that some one else cares for me; and that I—— Oh dear!—oh dear!", "그분은 내가 거짓말을 했다는 걸 아는 것만이 아니라, 다른 누군가가 나를 마음에 두고 있다고, 그리고 내가…… 아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["What shall I do?", "아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["What do I mean?", "나는 어떻게 해야 하지?"], ["Why do I care what he thinks, beyond the mere loss of his good opinion as regards my telling the truth or not?", "내가 무슨 소리를 하고 있는 거지? 내가 진실을 말했는지 아닌지에 관해 그분의 좋은 평가를 잃는 것 말고, 어째서 나는 그분이 나를 어떻게 생각하는지 마음을 쓰는 걸까?"], ["I cannot tell.", "알 수 없어."], ["But I am very miserable!", "하지만 나는 너무나 비참해!"], ["Oh, how unhappy this last year has been!", "아, 지난 한 해가 얼마나 불행했던지!"], ["I have passed out of childhood into old age.", "나는 어린 시절에서 곧장 늙은 나이로 건너와 버렸어."], ["I have had no youth—no womanhood; the hopes of womanhood have closed for me—for I shall never marry; and I anticipate cares and sorrows just as if I were an old woman, and with the same tearful spirit.", "젊음도 없었고, 여자로서의 시절도 없었어. 여자로서 품을 희망은 내게서 닫혀 버렸어."], ["I am weary of this continual call upon me for strength.", "나는 결코 혼인하지 않을 테니까. 그리고 나는 마치 늙은 여인이라도 된 듯이, 그와 똑같이 눈물겨운 마음으로 근심과 슬픔을 미리 맞이하고 있어."], ["I could bear up for papa; because that is a natural, pious duty.", "힘을 내라는 이 끝없는 요구에 나는 지쳤어. 아버지를 위해서라면 버틸 수 있어."], ["And I think I could bear up against—at any rate, I could have the energy to resent, Mrs.", "그건 자연스럽고 경건한 도리니까. 그리고 손턴 부인의 부당하고 무례한 의심에도 버틸 수 있을 것 같아."], ["Thornton’s unjust, impertinent suspicions.", "적어도 그것에 분개할 기운은 낼 수 있어."], ["But it is hard to feel how completely he must misunderstand me.", "하지만 그분이 나를 얼마나 완전히 오해하고 있을지 느끼는 것은 견디기 힘들어."], ["What has happened to make me so morbid to-day?", "오늘 나에게 무슨 일이 있었기에 이렇게 병적이 되었을까?"], ["I do not know.", "모르겠어."], ["I only know I cannot help it.", "다만 어쩔 수 없다는 것만 알아."], ["I must give way sometimes.", "나도 이따금은 무너져야 해."], ["No, I will not though,”", "아니, 그래도 그러지 않겠어.”"], ["said she, springing to her feet.", "그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["“I will not—I will not think of myself and my own position.", "“그러지 않을 거야. 나 자신과 내 처지를 생각하지 않겠어."], ["I won’t examine into my own feelings.", "내 감정을 들여다보지 않겠어."], ["It would be of no use now.", "이제 와서 아무 소용도 없어."], ["Some time, if I live to be an old woman, I may sit over the fire, and looking into the embers, see the life that might have been.”", "언젠가 늙은 여인이 될 때까지 살아 있다면, 그때는 난롯가에 앉아 잉걸불을 들여다보며 있었을지도 모를 삶을 볼 수 있겠지.”"]], [["All this time she was hastily putting on her things to go out, only stopping from time to time to wipe her eyes, with an impatience of gesture at the tears that would come again, in spite of all her bravery.", "그러는 내내 그녀는 밖에 나갈 채비를 서둘러 갖추고 있었다. 다만 이따금 손을 멈추고 눈물을 훔쳤다. 온갖 용기를 다 내어도 자꾸만 다시 차오르는 눈물에 조바심 난 몸짓이었다."]], [["“I dare say there’s many a woman makes as sad a mistake as I have done, and only finds it out too late.", "“나만큼이나 서글픈 잘못을 저지르고도 너무 늦어서야 그것을 깨닫는 여자가 많겠지."], ["And how proudly and impertinently I spoke to him that day!", "그날 나는 그분에게 얼마나 오만하고 무례하게 말했던가!"], ["But I did not know then.", "하지만 그때는 몰랐어."], ["It has come upon me little by little, and I don’t know where it began.", "이 마음은 조금씩 조금씩 내게로 다가왔고, 어디서 시작되었는지도 모르겠어."], ["Now I won’t give way.", "이제 나는 무너지지 않겠어."], ["I shall find it difficult to behave in the same way to him, with this miserable consciousness upon me; but I will be very calm and very quiet, and say very little.", "이 비참한 자각을 안은 채 그분을 예전과 똑같이 대하기는 어렵겠지. 그래도 나는 아주 침착하고 아주 조용하게 있으면서 말을 아주 적게 하겠어."], ["But, to be sure, I may not see him; he keeps out of our way evidently.", "하기야 그분을 못 볼지도 모르지. 그분은 분명히 우리를 피하고 있으니까."], ["That would be worse than all.", "그건 무엇보다도 더 견디기 힘들 거야."], ["And yet no wonder that he avoids me, believing what he must about me.”", "그렇지만 나에 대해 그렇게 믿고 있으니 나를 피하는 것도 이상할 게 없지.”"]], [["She went out, going rapidly towards the country, and trying to drown reflection by swiftness of motion.", "그녀는 밖으로 나가 들판 쪽으로 빠르게 걸으며, 걸음의 빠르기로 생각을 잠재우려 했다."]], [["As she stood on the door-step, at her return, her father came up:", "돌아와 현관 계단에 서 있는데 아버지가 다가왔다."]], [["“Good girl!”", "“착하구나.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You’ve been to Mrs. Boucher’s.", "“바우처 부인 댁에 다녀왔구나."], ["I was just meaning to go there, if I had time, before dinner.”", "나도 시간이 나면 식사 전에 가 볼 참이었단다.”"]], [["“No, papa; I have not,”", "“아니에요, 아버지. 안 갔어요.”"], ["said Margaret, reddening.", "마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I never thought about her.", "“그분 생각을 미처 못 했어요."], ["But I will go directly after dinner; I will go while you are taking your nap.”", "그렇지만 식사 마치고 바로 갈게요. 아버지가 낮잠 주무시는 동안에 다녀올게요.”"]], [["Accordingly Margaret went. Mrs.", "그리하여 마거릿은 다녀왔다."], ["Boucher was very ill; really ill—not merely ailing.", "바우처 부인은 몹시 아팠다. 정말로 앓고 있었다."], ["The kind and sensible neighbour, who had come in the other day, seemed to have taken charge of everything.", "그저 몸이 편찮은 정도가 아니었다. 며칠 전에 들렀던 그 친절하고 사려 깊은 이웃 여자가 모든 일을 도맡은 듯했다."], ["Some of the children were gone to the neighbours.", "아이들 몇은 이웃집에 가 있었다."], ["Mary Higgins had come for the three youngest at dinner-time; and since then Nicholas had gone for the doctor.", "메리 히긴스가 낮 식사 때 어린 세 아이를 데리러 왔고, 그 뒤로 니컬러스가 의사를 부르러 갔다."], ["He had not come as yet; Mrs.", "의사는 아직 오지 않았다."], ["Boucher was dying; and there was nothing to do but wait.", "바우처 부인은 죽어 가고 있었고, 기다리는 것 말고는 할 수 있는 일이 없었다."], ["Margaret thought that she should like to hear his opinion, and that she could not do better than go and see the Higginses in the meantime.", "마거릿은 의사의 소견을 듣고 싶었고, 그동안에는 히긴스네에 가 보는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["She might then possibly hear whether Nicholas had been able to make his application to Mr. Thornton.", "그러면 니컬러스가 손턴 씨에게 부탁을 넣어 볼 수 있었는지도 어쩌면 들을 수 있을 터였다."]], [["She found Nicholas busily engaged in making a penny spin on the dresser, for the amusement of three little children, who were clinging to him in a fearless manner.", "가 보니 니컬러스는 찬장 위에서 동전 하나를 팽이처럼 돌리느라 여념이 없었다. 겁도 없이 그에게 매달려 있는 어린아이 셋을 즐겁게 해 주기 위해서였다."], ["He, as well as they, was smiling at a good long spin; and Margaret thought, that the happy look of interest in his occupation was a good sign.", "동전이 길게 잘 돌아가자 아이들과 마찬가지로 그도 웃고 있었다. 마거릿은 하는 일에 그렇게 재미를 붙인 그 행복한 표정이 좋은 조짐이라고 생각했다."], ["When the penny stopped spinning, “lile Johnnie” began to cry.", "동전이 돌기를 멈추자 “꼬맹이 조니”가 울기 시작했다."]], [["“Come to me,”", "“이리 오렴.” 마거릿이 아이를 찬장에서 내려 품에 안으며 말했다."], ["said Margaret, taking him off the dresser, and holding him in her arms; she held her watch to his ear, while she asked Nicholas if he had seen Mr. Thornton.", "그녀는 시계를 아이의 귀에 대 주면서 니컬러스에게 손턴 씨를 만났느냐고 물었다."]], [["The look on his face changed instantly.", "그의 얼굴빛이 대번에 달라졌다."]], [["“Ay!”", "“봤수!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’ve seen and heerd too much on him.”", "“보기도 하고 듣기도 너무 많이 들었지.”"]], [["“He refused you, then?”", "“그러면 거절당하셨군요.”"], ["said Margaret, sorrowfully.", "마거릿이 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“To be sure.", "“그럼요."], ["I knew he’d do it all along.", "처음부터 그럴 줄 알았수."], ["It’s no good expecting marcy at the hands o’ them measters.", "그런 공장주들 손에서 자비를 바라 봐야 헛일이오."], ["Yo’r a stranger and a foreigner, and aren’t likely to know their ways; but I knowed it.”", "댁은 낯선 사람이고 외지 사람이니 저들 하는 짓을 알 리가 없겠지만, 나는 알고 있었소.”"]], [["“I am sorry I asked you.", "“여쭤 보시라고 한 게 죄송해요."], ["Was he angry?", "그분이 화를 내던가요?"], ["He did not speak to you as Hamper did, did he?”", "햄퍼 씨가 그랬던 것처럼 말하지는 않았지요?”"]], [["“He weren’t o’er-civil!”", "“딱히 예의 바르지도 않습디다!”"], ["said Nicholas, spinning the penny again, as much for his own amusement as for that of the children.", "니컬러스가 아이들을 위해서만큼이나 제 재미로도 다시 동전을 돌리며 말했다. “아가씨는 신경 쓰지 마시우."], ["“Never yo’ fret, I’m only where I was.", "나야 있던 자리에 그대로 있을 뿐이오."], ["I’ll go on tramp to-morrow.", "내일은 일자리 찾아 떠돌아다닐 거요."], ["I gave him as good as I got.", "나도 받은 만큼 되돌려 줬소."], ["I telled him, I’d not that good opinion on him that I’d ha’ come a second time of mysel’; but yo’d advised me for to come, and I were beholden to yo’.”", "내 발로 두 번씩이나 찾아올 만큼 그를 대단하게 여기지는 않는다고, 다만 아가씨가 가 보라 일렀고 나는 아가씨한테 신세를 진 게 있어서 왔다고 말해 줬지.”"]], [["“You told him I sent you?”", "“제가 보냈다고 말씀하셨어요?”"]], [["“I dunno, if I ca’d yo’ by your name.", "“아가씨 이름까지 댔는지는 모르겠소."], ["I dunnot think I did.", "대지 않은 것 같은데."], ["I said a woman who knew no better had advised me for to come and see if there was a soft place in his heart.”", "그저 뭘 잘 모르는 여자 하나가 그 사람 마음에 무른 데가 있는지 가서 보라고 권하더라고 했소.”"]], [["“And he—?”", "“그랬더니 그분은요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Said I were to tell yo’ to mind yo’r own business.—That’s the longest spin yet, my lads.—And them’s civil words to what he used to me.", "“아가씨더러 남의 일에 참견 말라고 전하라더군.—이번 게 제일 오래 도네, 얘들아.—그것도 나한테 쓴 말에 대면 점잖은 축이오."], ["But ne’er mind.", "하지만 아무래도 좋소."], ["We’re but where we was; and I’ll break stones on the road afore I let these little uns clem.”", "우리야 있던 자리 그대로고, 나는 이 어린것들 굶기느니 길바닥에서 돌을 깨겠소.”"]], [["Margaret put the struggling Johnnie out of her arms, back into his former place on the dresser.", "마거릿은 버둥거리는 조니를 품에서 내려 찬장 위 제자리에 도로 놓았다."]], [["“I am sorry I asked you to go to Mr. Thornton’s.", "“손턴 씨에게 가 보시라고 한 게 죄송해요."], ["I am disappointed in him.”", "그분에게 실망했어요.”"]], [["There was a slight noise behind her.", "그녀의 등 뒤에서 자그마한 기척이 났다."], ["Both she and Nicholas turned round at the same moment, and there stood Mr.", "그녀와 니컬러스가 동시에 돌아보니, 언짢은 놀라움이 어린 얼굴로 손턴 씨가 서 있었다."], ["Thornton, with a look of displeased surprise upon his face.", "마거릿은 순간의 충동에 따라 한마디도 없이 그의 앞을 지나 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Obeying her swift impulse, Margaret passed out before him, saying not a word, only bowing low to hide the sudden paleness that she felt had come over her face.", "얼굴에 갑자기 번진 것이 느껴지는 창백함을 감추려고 깊이 고개를 숙였을 뿐이었다. 그도 똑같이 깊이 고개를 숙여 답하고는 그녀의 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["He bent equally low in return, and then closed the door after her. As she hurried to Mrs.", "바우처 부인 댁으로 서둘러 가는 길에 그 문 닫히는 소리가 들려왔고, 그것이 그녀의 굴욕을 가득 채우는 듯했다."], ["Boucher’s, she heard the clang, and it seemed to fill up the measure of her mortification.", "그 또한 그녀가 거기 있는 것을 보고 언짢았다. 그의 가슴에는 다정함이 있었다."], ["He too was annoyed to find her there.", "니컬러스 히긴스가 말한 그 “무른 데”가."], ["He had tenderness in his heart—“a soft place,”", "그러나 그는 얼마간의 자존심으로 그것을 감추었다."], ["as Nicholas Higgins called it; but he had some pride in concealing it; he kept it very sacred and safe, and was jealous of every circumstance that tried to gain admission.", "그는 그것을 아주 신성하고 안전하게 간직했고, 그 안으로 비집고 들어오려는 온갖 사정에 날을 세웠다. 그러나 자기 다정함이 드러나는 것을 두려워한 만큼이나, 그는 자기가 공정하다는 것을 모든 사람이 알아주기를 바랐다."], ["But if he dreaded exposure of his tenderness, he was equally desirous that all men should recognise his justice; and he felt that he had been unjust in giving so scornful a hearing to any one who had waited, with humble patience, for five hours, to speak to him.", "그런데 자기와 말 한마디 나누려고 겸손하게 참으며 다섯 시간을 기다린 사람에게 그토록 업신여기는 태도로 귀를 내주었으니 자기가 공정하지 못했다고 그는 느꼈다. 그 사람이 기회를 잡았을 때 자기에게 건방지게 굴었다는 것은 손턴 씨에게 아무것도 아니었다."], ["That the man had spoken saucily to him when he had the opportunity, was nothing to Mr. Thornton.", "오히려 그 때문에 그가 마음에 들었다."], ["He rather liked him for it; and he was conscious of his own irritability of temper at the time, which probably made them both quits.", "게다가 그는 그때 자기 성미가 곤두서 있었다는 것도 알고 있었으니, 그것으로 아마 서로 비긴 셈이었다. 손턴 씨의 마음을 친 것은 그 다섯 시간의 기다림이었다."], ["It was the five hours of waiting that struck Mr. Thornton.", "자기에게는 다섯 시간의 여유가 없었다."], ["He had not five hours to spare, himself; but one hour—two hours, of his hard penetrating intellectual, as well as bodily labour, did he give up to going about collecting evidence as to the truth of Higgins’s story, the nature of his character, the tenor of his life.", "그러나 그는 몸과 머리를 모두 갈아 넣는 그 고된 노동 가운데 한 시간, 두 시간을 떼어, 히긴스가 한 이야기가 사실인지, 그 사람됨이 어떠한지, 그가 살아온 내력이 어떠한지 증거를 모으러 다녔다. 그는 그러지 않으려 애썼으나 히긴스가 한 말이 모두 사실이라고 확신하게 되었다."], ["He tried not to be, but was convinced that all that Higgins had said was true.", "그러자 그 확신이 무슨 주문에라도 걸린 듯 안으로 스며들어 그의 마음속에 잠자던 다정함을 건드렸다. 그 사람의 참을성과 그 동기의 꾸밈없는 너그러움은—그는 바우처와 히긴스 사이의 다툼에 대해서도 알아냈던 것이다—공정함을 따지는 이치 따위를 그가 아주 잊어버리게 만들었고, 더 신성한 본능으로 그것을 훌쩍 뛰어넘게 했다."], ["And then the conviction went in, as if by some spell, and touched the latent tenderness of his heart; the patience of the man, the simple generosity of the motive (for he had learnt about the quarrel between Boucher and Higgins), made him forget entirely the mere reasonings of justice, and overleap them by a diviner instinct.", "그는 히긴스에게 일자리를 주겠다고 말하러 온 것이었다. 그런데 그녀의 마지막 말을 들은 것보다도 마거릿이 거기 있다는 사실이 그를 더 언짢게 했다."], ["He came to tell Higgins he would give him work; and he was more annoyed to find Margaret there than by hearing her last words; for then he understood that she was the woman who had urged Higgins to come to him; and he dreaded the admission of any thought of her, as a motive to what he was doing solely because it was right.", "그 말로 히긴스에게 자기를 찾아가라고 권한 여자가 바로 그녀임을 알게 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 오로지 옳기 때문에 하고 있는 이 일에 그녀에 대한 어떤 생각이 동기로 끼어드는 것이 두려웠다."]], [["“So that was the lady you spoke of as a woman?”", "“그러면 아까 여자라고 말한 그 사람이 저 아가씨였다는 건가?”"], ["said he indignantly to Higgins.", "그가 히긴스에게 발끈하여 말했다."], ["“You might have told me who she was.”", "“누구인지 말해 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.”"]], [["“And then, maybe, yo’d have spoken of her more civil than yo’ did; yo’d getten a mother who might ha’ kept yo’r tongue in check when yo’ were talking o’ women being at the root of all the plagues.”", "“그랬으면 아마 댁도 그이를 두고 좀 더 점잖게 말했겠구먼. 댁한테도 어머니가 계실 테니, 온갖 재앙의 뿌리에 여자가 있다느니 떠들 때 그 혀를 붙들어 줬을지도 모르지.”"]], [["“Of course you told that to Miss Hale?”", "“물론 그 말을 헤일 양에게 전했겠지?”"]], [["“In coorse I did.", "“그럼 전했지."], ["Leastways, I reckon I did.", "아무튼 전한 것 같소."], ["I telled her she weren’t to meddle again in aught that concerned yo’.”", "댁에 관한 일에는 다시는 참견하지 말라고 일러 뒀소.”"]], [["“Whose children are those—yours?”", "“저 아이들은 누구 아이들인가?"], ["Mr.", "자네 아이들인가?”"], ["Thornton had a pretty good notion whose they were, from what he had heard; but he felt awkward in turning the conversation round from this unpromising beginning.", "손턴 씨는 들은 이야기로 미루어 그 아이들이 누구인지 어지간히 짐작하고 있었다. 그러나 이 답답한 첫머리에서 이야기를 돌려놓기가 어색했다."]], [["“They’re not mine, and they are mine.”", "“내 아이들이 아니면서 내 아이들이오.”"]], [["“They are the children you spoke of to me this morning?”", "“오늘 아침에 내게 말한 그 아이들인가?”"]], [["“When yo’ said,”", "“댁이 말했지.”"], ["replied Higgins, turning round, with ill-smothered fierceness, “that my story might be true or might not, but it were a very unlikely one.", "히긴스가 잘 감추지 못한 사나움으로 돌아서며 대답했다. “내 얘기가 사실일 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지만 아무래도 있을 법하지 않다고."], ["Measter, I’ve not forgotten.”", "나리, 나는 안 잊었소.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was silent for a moment; then he said: “No more have I.", "손턴 씨는 잠시 잠자코 있다가 말했다."], ["I remember what I said.", "“나도 잊지 않았네."], ["I spoke to you about those children in a way I had no business to.", "내가 무슨 말을 했는지 기억하고 있어. 나는 그 아이들에 대해 그렇게 말할 자격이 없었네."], ["I did not believe you.", "나는 자네 말을 믿지 않았어."], ["I could not have taken care of another man’s children myself, if he had acted towards me as I hear Boucher did towards you.", "내가 듣기로 바우처가 자네에게 한 것처럼 누가 내게 그리했다면, 나라면 남의 아이들을 떠맡지 못했을 걸세."], ["But I know now you spoke truth.", "하지만 이제는 자네가 진실을 말했다는 걸 아네."], ["I beg your pardon.”", "미안하네.”"]], [["Higgins did not turn round, or immediately respond to this.", "히긴스는 돌아서지도, 곧바로 대꾸하지도 않았다."], ["But when he did speak, it was in a softened tone, although the words were gruff enough.", "그러나 마침내 입을 열었을 때 그 어조는 누그러져 있었다. 말은 여전히 퉁명스러웠지만."]], [["“Yo’ve no business to go prying into what happened between Boucher and me.", "“바우처와 나 사이에 있었던 일을 캐고 다닐 자격은 댁한테도 없수."], ["He’s dead, and I’m sorry.", "그는 죽었고, 나는 마음이 아프오."], ["That’s enough.”", "그걸로 됐소.”"]], [["“So it is.", "“그렇지."], ["Will you take work with me?", "나와 함께 일하겠나?"], ["That’s what I came to ask.”", "그걸 물으러 왔네.”"]], [["Higgins’s obstinacy wavered, recovered strength, and stood firm.", "히긴스의 고집이 흔들렸다가 다시 기운을 차리고 버텨 섰다."], ["He would not speak. Mr.", "그는 말하려 하지 않았다."], ["Thornton would not ask again.", "손턴 씨도 두 번 묻지 않았다."], ["Higgins’s eye fell on the children.", "히긴스의 눈길이 아이들에게 떨어졌다."]], [["“Yo’ve called me impudent, and a liar, and a mischief-maker, and yo’ might ha’ said wi’ some truth, as I were now and then given to drink.", "“댁은 나를 뻔뻔하다고, 거짓말쟁이라고, 말썽꾼이라고 불렀고, 이따금 술에 빠진다고 했더라도 그건 얼마쯤 사실이오."], ["An’, I ha’ called you a tyrant, an’ an oud bull dog, and a hard, cruel master; that’s where it stands.", "그리고 나는 댁을 폭군이라고, 늙은 불도그라고, 모질고 잔인한 공장주라고 불렀지. 사정이 그렇소."], ["But for th’ childer, Measter, do yo’ think we can e’er get on together?”", "하지만 이 아이들을 봐서, 나리, 우리가 언제고 함께 지낼 수 있으리라 보시오?”"]], [["“Well!” said Mr.", "“허!”"], ["Thornton, half-laughing, “it was not my proposal that we should go together.", "손턴 씨가 반쯤 웃으며 말했다. “함께 지내자고 한 건 내 제안이 아니었네."], ["But there’s one comfort on your own showing.", "그렇지만 자네 말대로라면 한 가지 위안은 있군."], ["We neither of us can think much worse of the other than we do now.”", "우리 둘 다 상대를 지금보다 더 나쁘게 여기기는 어려울 테니까.”"]], [["“That’s true,”", "“그건 그렇소.”"], ["said Higgins, reflectively.", "히긴스가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["“I’ve been thinking ever sin’ I saw you, what a marcy it were yo’ did na take me on, for that I ne’er saw a man whom I could less abide.", "“댁을 본 뒤로 줄곧 생각했지. 나를 안 받아 준 게 얼마나 다행이냐고."], ["But that’s maybe been a hasty judgment; and work’s work to such as me.", "그토록 견디기 힘든 사람은 처음 봤으니까. 하지만 그건 아마 성급한 판단이었겠지."], ["So, measter, I’ll come; and what’s more, I thank yo’; and that’s a deal fro’ me,”", "게다가 나 같은 놈한테는 일은 일이오. 그러니 나리, 가겠소."], ["said he, more frankly, suddenly turning round and facing Mr.", "그뿐 아니라 고맙다는 말도 하겠소. 나한테는 그게 대단한 말이오.”"], ["Thornton fully for the first time.", "그가 한결 스스럼없이 말하며, 갑자기 돌아서서 처음으로 손턴 씨를 똑바로 마주 보았다."]], [["“And this is a deal from me,” said Mr.", "“나에게도 이건 대단한 일이네.”"], ["Thornton, giving Higgins’s hand a good grip.", "손턴 씨가 히긴스의 손을 힘주어 잡으며 말했다."], ["“Now mind you come sharp to your time,”", "“자, 시간은 꼭 맞춰 나오게.”"], ["continued he, resuming the master.", "그가 다시 공장주로 돌아가 말을 이었다."], ["“I’ll have no laggards at my mill.", "“내 공장에 게으름뱅이는 두지 않네."], ["What fines we have, we keep pretty sharply.", "벌금을 물릴 일이 있으면 우리는 꽤 매섭게 물리지."], ["And the first time I catch you making mischief, off you go.", "그리고 자네가 말썽을 부리는 걸 처음으로 잡아내는 날 자네는 나가는 거네."], ["So now you know where you are.”", "자, 이제 자네 처지를 알았겠지.”"]], [["“Yo’ spoke of my wisdom this morning.", "“오늘 아침에 내 지혜 이야기를 하셨지."], ["I reckon I may bring it wi’ me; or would yo’ rayther have me ’bout my brains?”", "그것도 데려가도 되겠소? 아니면 머리는 두고 오는 편이 낫겠소?”"]], [["“’Bout your brains if you use them for meddling with my business; with your brains if you can keep to your own.”", "“내 일에 참견하는 데 쓸 거라면 머리는 두고 오고, 자네 일만 지킬 수 있다면 머리도 데려오게.”"]], [["“I shall need a deal o’ brains to settle where my business ends and yo’rs begins.”", "“내 일이 어디서 끝나고 나리 일이 어디서 시작되는지 가리려면 머리가 꽤나 많이 필요하겠수.”"]], [["“Your business has not begun yet, and mine stands still for me.", "“자네 일은 아직 시작도 안 했고, 내 일은 나를 기다리며 멈춰 서 있네."], ["So good afternoon.”", "그러면 잘 가게.”"]], [["Just before Mr. Thornton came up to Mrs.", "손턴 씨가 바우처 부인 댁 문 앞에 이르기 바로 전에 마거릿이 그 문에서 나왔다."], ["Boucher’s door, Margaret came out of it.", "그녀는 그를 보지 못했다."], ["She did not see him; and he followed her for several yards, admiring her light and easy walk, and her tall and graceful figure.", "그는 몇 야드쯤 그녀를 뒤따르며 가볍고 편안한 걸음걸이와 훤칠하고 우아한 자태를 감탄하며 바라보았다."], ["But, suddenly, this simple emotion of pleasure was tainted, poisoned by jealousy.", "그러나 이 소박한 기쁨의 감정은 문득 질투에 물들고 병들어 버렸다."], ["He wished to overtake her, and speak to her, to see how she would receive him, now she must know he was aware of some other attachment.", "그는 그녀를 따라잡아 말을 걸고 싶었다. 다른 애착이 있다는 것을 자기가 알고 있음을 그녀도 이제 알 터인데, 그녀가 자기를 어떻게 맞이하는지 보고 싶었던 것이다."], ["He wished too, but of this wish he was rather ashamed, that she should know that he had justified her wisdom in sending Higgins to him to ask for work, and had repented him of his morning’s decision.", "그는 또한, 이 바람에 대해서는 다소 부끄러웠지만, 히긴스를 자기에게 보내 일자리를 청하게 한 그녀의 판단이 옳았음을 자기가 인정했다는 것을, 그리고 아침의 결정을 뉘우쳤다는 것을 그녀가 알아주기를 바랐다."], ["He came up to her.", "그가 그녀에게 다가갔다."], ["She started.", "그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“Allow me to say, Miss Hale, that you were rather premature in expressing your disappointment.", "“한 말씀 드리자면, 헤일 양, 실망하시기에는 좀 일렀습니다."], ["I have taken Higgins on.”", "히긴스를 받아들였습니다.”"]], [["“I am glad of it,”", "“다행이네요.”"], ["said she coldly.", "그녀가 쌀쌀하게 말했다."]], [["“He tells me, he repeated to you, what I said this morning about—,”", "“오늘 아침에 제가 한 말을 그가 당신에게 옮겼다고 하더군요. 그러니까……”"], ["Mr. Thornton hesitated.", "손턴 씨가 말을 머뭇거렸다."], ["Margaret took it up:", "마거릿이 받았다."]], [["“About women not meddling.", "“여자는 참견하지 말라는 말씀이지요."], ["You had a perfect right to express your opinion, which was a very correct one, I have no doubt.", "선생님께는 의견을 말씀하실 온전한 권리가 있고, 그 의견은 아주 옳았을 거예요. 저도 의심하지 않아요."], ["But,”", "하지만,”"], ["she went on a little more eagerly, “Higgins did not quite tell you the exact truth.”", "그녀가 조금 더 열을 내어 말을 이었다. “히긴스 씨가 정확한 진실을 다 말씀드리지는 않았어요.”"], ["The word “truth,” reminded her of her own untruth, and she stopped short, feeling exceedingly uncomfortable.", "“진실”이라는 낱말이 자기가 한 거짓말을 떠올리게 했고, 그녀는 몹시 거북해져 말을 뚝 그쳤다."]], [["Mr. Thornton at first was puzzled to account for her silence; and then he remembered the lie she had told, and all that was foregone.", "손턴 씨는 처음에는 그녀의 침묵을 어떻게 새겨야 할지 몰라 어리둥절했다."], ["“The exact truth!” said he.", "그러다 그녀가 한 거짓말과 그전에 있었던 모든 일이 떠올랐다."], ["“Very few people do speak the exact truth.", "“정확한 진실이라!” 그가 말했다."], ["I have given up hoping for it.", "“정확한 진실을 말하는 사람은 아주 드물지요."], ["Miss Hale, have you no explanation to give me?", "저는 그런 것을 바라기를 그만두었습니다. 헤일 양, 제게 해 주실 설명은 없습니까?"], ["You must perceive what I cannot but think.”", "제가 어떻게 생각할 수밖에 없는지 아실 텐데요.”"]], [["Margaret was silent.", "마거릿은 잠자코 있었다."], ["She was wondering whether an explanation of any kind would be consistent with her loyalty to Frederick.", "그녀는 어떤 종류의 설명이든 그것이 오빠에 대한 의리와 어긋나지 않을지 헤아리고 있었다."]], [["“Nay,”", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said he, “I will ask no farther.", "그가 말했다. “더는 묻지 않겠습니다."], ["I may be putting temptation in your way.", "제가 당신을 시험에 들게 하고 있는지도 모르지요."], ["At present, believe me, your secret is safe with me.", "지금으로서는, 믿어 주십시오, 당신의 비밀은 제게 안전합니다."], ["But you run great risks, allow me to say, in being so indiscreet.", "그러나 한 말씀 드리자면, 그렇게 조심성 없이 구시면 큰 위험을 무릅쓰게 됩니다."], ["I am only speaking as a friend of your father’s: if I had any other thought or hope, of course that is at an end.", "저는 그저 당신 아버님의 친구로서 말씀드리는 것입니다. 제게 다른 어떤 생각이나 희망이 있었다 해도 그것은 물론 끝났습니다."], ["I am quite disinterested.”", "저는 아무런 사심이 없습니다.”"]], [["“I am aware of that,”", "“알고 있어요.”"], ["said Margaret, forcing herself to speak in an indifferent, careless way.", "마거릿이 아무렇지도 않고 무심한 투로 말하려고 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“I am aware of what I must appear to you, but the secret is another person’s, and I cannot explain it without doing him harm.”", "“제가 선생님께 어떻게 보일지도 알고 있어요. 하지만 그 비밀은 다른 사람의 것이고, 그 사람을 해치지 않고서는 설명할 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“I have not the slightest wish to pry into the gentleman’s secrets,”", "“그분의 비밀을 캐고 싶은 마음은 조금도 없습니다.”"], ["he said, with growing anger.", "그가 점점 화가 치미는 채로 말했다."], ["“My own interest in you is—simply that of a friend.", "“당신에 대한 제 관심은 그저 친구로서의 것입니다."], ["You may not believe me, Miss Hale, but it is—in spite of the persecution I’m afraid I threatened you with at one time—but that is all given up; all passed away.", "믿지 않으실지 모르지만, 헤일 양, 정말 그렇습니다. 한때 제가 당신을 괴롭히겠다고 으름장을 놓은 것은 사실이지만, 그것은 다 접었습니다."], ["You believe me, Miss Hale?”", "다 지나갔습니다. 믿으시겠습니까, 헤일 양?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said Margaret, quietly and sadly.", "마거릿이 조용하고 서글프게 말했다."]], [["“Then, really, I don’t see any reason for us to go on walking together.", "“그러면 정말이지 우리가 계속 함께 걸을 이유가 없군요."], ["I thought, perhaps, you might have had something to say, but I see we are nothing to each other.", "어쩌면 하실 말씀이 있으실까 했습니다만, 우리는 서로에게 아무것도 아니라는 걸 알겠습니다."], ["If you’re quite convinced, that any foolish passion on my part is entirely over, I wish you good afternoon.”", "제 쪽의 어리석은 연정이 완전히 끝났다는 것을 확실히 아셨다면, 이만 실례하겠습니다.”"], ["He walked off very hastily.", "그는 몹시 서둘러 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“What can he mean?”", "“저분이 무슨 뜻으로 그러시는 걸까?” 마거릿은 생각했다."], ["thought Margaret,—“what could he mean by speaking so, as if I were always thinking that he cared for me, when I know he does not; he cannot.", "“내가 늘 그분이 나를 마음에 두고 있다고 여기기라도 하는 듯이 그렇게 말씀하시다니, 대체 무슨 뜻일까? 나는 그렇지 않다는 걸 아는데."], ["His mother will have said all those cruel things about me to him.", "그럴 수가 없는데. 어머니가 나에 대해 그 모진 말들을 다 그분에게 했겠지."], ["But I won’t care for him.", "하지만 나는 그분을 마음에 두지 않겠어."], ["I surely am mistress enough of myself to control this wild, strange, miserable feeling, which tempted me even to betray my own dear Frederick, so that I might but regain his good opinion—the good opinion of a man who takes such pains to tell me that I am nothing to him.", "나는 분명 나 자신을 다스릴 만큼은 되니까, 이 사납고 낯설고 비참한 감정도 억누를 수 있어. 그분의 좋은 평가를 되찾아 보겠다고 사랑하는 오빠를 저버리라고까지 나를 꾀던 이 감정을."], ["Come! poor little heart!", "그것도 나는 자기에게 아무것도 아니라고 그토록 애써 일러 주는 사람의 평가를."], ["be cheery and brave.", "자! 가엾은 작은 마음아, 기운을 내고 씩씩해지렴."], ["We’ll be a great deal to one another, if we are thrown off and left desolate.”", "우리가 내쳐져 홀로 남겨진다면, 우리 둘이 서로에게 아주 큰 존재가 되어 주자.”"]], [["Her father was almost startled by her merriment this morning.", "아버지는 그날 아침 그녀의 명랑함에 거의 놀랄 지경이었다."], ["She talked incessantly, and forced her natural humour to an unusual pitch; and if there was a tinge of bitterness in much of what she said; if her accounts of the old Harley Street set were a little sarcastic, her father could not bear to check her, as he would have done at another time—for he was glad to see her shake off her cares.", "그녀는 쉴 새 없이 이야기했고, 타고난 유머를 여느 때보다 높은 가락으로 끌어올렸다. 그녀가 하는 말 가운데 많은 것에 씁쓸한 기색이 배어 있었다 해도, 옛 할리 가 사람들에 대한 이야기가 다소 비꼬는 투였다 해도, 아버지는 다른 때 같으면 그랬을 것처럼 딸을 나무랄 마음이 나지 않았다."], ["In the middle of the evening, she was called down to speak to Mary Higgins; and when she came back, Mr.", "딸이 근심을 떨쳐 내는 것이 반가웠기 때문이다."], ["Hale imagined that he saw traces of tears on her cheeks.", "저녁이 한창일 때 그녀는 메리 히긴스와 이야기하러 아래층으로 불려 내려갔다."], ["But that could not be, for she brought good news—that Higgins had got work at Mr. Thornton’s mill.", "그리고 돌아왔을 때 헤일 씨는 딸의 뺨에 눈물 자국이 있는 것을 본 듯했다."], ["Her spirits were damped, at any rate, and she found it very difficult to go on talking at all, much more in the wild way that she had done.", "그러나 그럴 리가 없었다. 딸이 좋은 소식을 가져왔으니 말이다."], ["For some days her spirits varied strangely; and her father was beginning to be anxious about her, when news arrived from one or two quarters that promised some change and variety for her.", "히긴스가 손턴 씨의 공장에 일자리를 얻었다는 소식이었다. 아무튼 그녀의 기운은 가라앉았고, 그녀는 아예 말을 이어 가기가 몹시 어려워졌다."], ["Mr. Hale received a letter from Mr.", "아까처럼 들뜬 투로 말하기는 더더욱 어려웠다."], ["Bell, in which that gentleman volunteered a visit to them; and Mr.", "며칠 동안 그녀의 기분은 이상하리만큼 오르내렸다."], ["Hale imagined that the promised society of his old Oxford friend would give as agreeable a turn to Margaret’s ideas as it did to his own.", "아버지가 딸을 걱정하기 시작할 무렵, 그녀에게 얼마간의 변화와 새로움을 약속하는 소식이 한두 군데에서 날아들었다. 헤일 씨는 벨 씨에게서 편지를 받았는데, 그 노신사는 자청해서 그들을 찾아오겠다고 했다."], ["Margaret tried to take an interest in what pleased her father; but she was too languid to care about any Mr.", "헤일 씨는 옛 옥스퍼드 친구와 함께 지내는 것이 자기에게 그러하듯 마거릿의 생각에도 유쾌한 전환을 가져다주리라 여겼다."], ["Bell, even though he were twenty times her godfather.", "마거릿은 아버지를 기쁘게 하는 일에 관심을 가져 보려 애썼다."], ["She was more roused by a letter from Edith, full of sympathy about her aunt’s death; full of details about herself, her husband, and child; and at the end saying, that as the climate did not suit the baby, and as Mrs.", "그러나 그녀는 너무 기운이 없어서, 벨 씨가 스무 번쯤 그녀의 대부라 한들 그가 어떤 사람이든 마음에 두지 못했다. 그녀의 마음을 더 흔든 것은 이디스에게서 온 편지였다."], ["Shaw was talking of returning to England, she thought it probable that Captain Lennox might sell out, and that they might all go and live again in the old Harley Street house; which, however, would seem very incomplete without Margaret.", "이모의 죽음에 대한 위로가 가득했고, 자기와 남편과 아이에 대한 이야기가 잔뜩 적혀 있었으며, 끝에 가서는 그곳 기후가 아기에게 맞지 않고 쇼 부인도 잉글랜드로 돌아가자는 이야기를 하고 있으니 레녹스 대위가 군직을 팔고 나와 모두가 옛 할리 가 집에서 다시 함께 살게 될지도 모르겠다고 적혀 있었다. 다만 마거릿이 없으면 그 집은 몹시 허전할 것이라고 했다."], ["Margaret yearned after that old house, and the placid tranquility of that well-ordered, monotonous life.", "마거릿은 그 옛집이, 그리고 잘 짜인 단조로운 삶의 잔잔한 고요가 사무치게 그리웠다. 그 삶을 살고 있을 때는 이따금 지겨웠다."], ["She had found it occasionally tiresome while it lasted; but since then she had been buffeted about, and felt so exhausted by this recent struggle with herself, that she thought that even stagnation would be a rest and a refreshment.", "그러나 그 뒤로 그녀는 이리저리 시달렸고, 최근 자기 자신과 벌인 이 싸움으로 어찌나 지쳤던지, 고여 있는 것조차 쉼이고 새 기운이 되리라 여겨졌다. 그리하여 그녀는 레녹스 부부가 잉글랜드로 돌아오면 그들 집에 오래 머무는 일을 하나의 지점으로 바라보기 시작했다."], ["So she began to look towards a long visit to the Lennoxes, on their return to England, as to a point—no, not of hope—but of leisure, in which she could regain her power and command over herself.", "희망의 지점이 아니라 여유의 지점으로, 그 안에서 자기 자신에 대한 힘과 다스림을 되찾을 수 있을 그런 지점으로. 지금으로서는 모든 화제가 손턴 씨에게로 흘러가는 것만 같았다."], ["At present it seemed to her as if all subjects tended towards Mr.", "아무리 애를 써도 그를 잊을 수 없는 것만 같았다."], ["Thornton; as if she could not forget him with all her endeavours.", "히긴스네에 가면 거기서 그의 이야기를 들었다."], ["If she went to see the Higginses, she heard of him there; her father had resumed their readings together, and quoted his opinions perpetually; even Mr.", "아버지는 그와의 독서를 다시 시작해 그의 견해를 끊임없이 인용했다. 벨 씨의 방문마저도 그 세입자의 이름을 화제에 올려놓았다."], ["Bell’s visit brought his tenant’s name upon the tapis; for he wrote word, that he believed he must be occupied some great part of his time with Mr. Thornton, as a new lease was in preparation, and the terms of it must be agreed upon.", "새 임대차 계약이 준비 중이고 그 조건을 합의해야 하니 자기 시간의 상당 부분을 손턴 씨와 보내야 할 것 같다고 편지에 적어 보냈기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL", "# 제40장"]], [["“I have no wrong, where I can claim no right, Naught ta’en me fro, where I have nothing had, Yet of my woe I cannot so be quite; Namely, since that another may be glad With that, that thus in sorrow makes me sad.”", "“권리를 주장할 수 없는 곳에서는 억울함도 없고, 가져 본 적 없는 것을 빼앗겼을 리도 없건만, 그래도 나는 내 슬픔에서 놓여나지 못하네. 하물며 나를 이토록 슬픔에 잠기게 하는 바로 그것으로 다른 이가 기뻐할 수 있음에랴.”"], ["WYATT.", "— 와이엇."]], [["Margaret had not expected much pleasure to herself from Mr.", "마거릿은 벨 씨의 방문에서 자신이 큰 즐거움을 얻으리라고는 기대하지 않았다. 오직 아버지를 생각해서 그날을 기다렸을 뿐이다."], ["Bell’s visit—she had only looked forward to it on her father’s account, but when her godfather came, she at once fell into the most natural position of friendship in the world.", "그러나 막상 대부가 오자 그녀는 곧바로 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 우정의 자리로 들어섰다. 벨 씨는 그녀가 그토록 제 마음에 꼭 맞는 처녀인 데에는 그녀 자신의 공이 없다고 말했다."], ["He said she had no merit in being what she was, a girl so entirely after his own heart: it was an hereditary power which she had, to walk in and take possession of his regard; while she, in reply, gave him much credit for being so fresh and young under his Fellow’s cap and gown.", "성큼 걸어 들어와 그의 호의를 통째로 차지해 버리는 것은 그녀가 물려받은 힘이라는 것이었다. 그러자 그녀는 답례로, 펠로의 사각모와 가운을 걸치고도 그토록 싱싱하고 젊음을 잃지 않은 것은 대단한 일이라며 그를 추어올렸다."]], [["“Fresh and young in warmth and kindness, I mean.", "“따뜻함과 다정함이 싱싱하고 젊다는 말이에요."], ["I’m afraid I must own, that I think your opinions are the oldest and mustiest I have met with this long time.”", "하지만 솔직히 털어놓아야겠는데, 대부님의 의견들은 제가 오랫동안 만나 본 것 가운데 가장 낡고 곰팡내 나는 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Hear this daughter of yours, Hale!", "“자네 딸이 하는 말 좀 들어 보게, 헤일!"], ["Her residence in Milton has quite corrupted her.", "밀턴에 살더니 아주 물이 들었구먼."], ["She’s a democrat, a red republican, a member of the Peace Society, a socialist—”", "민주주의자에, 붉은 공화주의자에, 평화협회 회원에, 사회주의자에—”"]], [["“Papa, it’s all because I’m standing up for the progress of commerce.", "“아버지, 다 제가 상업의 진보를 편들었기 때문이에요."], ["Mr. Bell would have had it keep still at exchanging wild-beast skins for acorns.”", "벨 씨는 상업이 짐승 가죽과 도토리를 맞바꾸던 시절에 그대로 멈춰 서 있기를 바라시거든요.”"]], [["“No, no.", "“아니, 아니지."], ["I’d dig the ground and grow potatoes.", "나라면 땅을 파서 감자를 심겠네."], ["And I’d shave the wild-beast skin and make the wool into broadcloth.", "그리고 그 짐승 가죽의 털을 깎아서 나사를 짜겠지. 과장은 말게, 아가씨."], ["Don’t exaggerate, missy. But I’m tired of this bustle.", "다만 나는 이 부산스러움에 진력이 났을 뿐이야."], ["Everybody rushing over everybody, in their hurry to get rich.”", "다들 부자가 되겠다고 서두르느라 서로를 짓밟고 달려들지 않나.”"]], [["“It is not everyone who can sit comfortably in a set of college rooms and let his riches grow without any exertion of his own.", "“누구나 대학 숙사에 편히 앉아 제 손 하나 까딱하지 않고 재산이 불어나도록 내버려 둘 수 있는 것은 아니라네.”"], ["No doubt there is many a man here who would be thankful if his property would increase as yours has done, without his taking any trouble about it,”", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Hale.", "“이곳에도 아무 수고 없이 자네 재산처럼 불어나 준다면 고맙게 여길 사람이 수두룩할 걸세.”"]], [["“I don’t believe they would.", "“그럴 리 없어."], ["It’s the bustle and the struggle they like.", "그자들이 좋아하는 건 그 부산함과 아등바등하는 다툼이니까."], ["As for sitting still, and learning from the past, or shaping out the future by faithful work done in a prophetic spirit—Why!", "가만히 앉아서 지난 시대에서 배운다거나, 예언자의 정신으로 성실히 일하며 앞날의 모양을 잡아 나간다거나 하는 것으로 말하자면—원!"], ["Pooh!", "흥!"], ["I don’t believe there’s a man in Milton who knows how to sit still; and it is a great art.”", "밀턴에 가만히 앉아 있을 줄 아는 사내가 하나라도 있을 성싶지 않네. 그런데 그건 대단한 기술이란 말이야.”"]], [["“Milton people, I suspect, think Oxford men don’t know how to move.", "“밀턴 사람들은 아마 옥스퍼드 사람들이 움직일 줄을 모른다고 여길 걸세."], ["It would be a very good thing if they mixed a little more.”", "서로 조금 더 어울린다면 아주 좋은 일이 되겠지.”"]], [["“It might be good for the Miltoners.", "“밀턴 사람들에게야 좋을지 모르지."], ["Many things might be good for them which would be very disagreeable for other people.”", "그자들에게 좋을 일 가운데는 다른 사람들에게는 아주 못마땅한 일이 많으니까.”"]], [["“Are you not a Milton man yourself?”", "“대부님도 밀턴 사람이 아니신가요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."], ["“I should have thought you would have been proud of your town.”", "“저는 대부님이 고향 도시를 자랑스러워하실 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“I confess, I don’t see what there is to be proud of.", "“고백하건대, 나는 자랑할 만한 것이 무엇인지 모르겠구나."], ["If you’ll only come to Oxford, Margaret, I will show you a place to glory in.”", "마거릿, 옥스퍼드에 한 번만 와 보렴. 자랑스러워할 만한 곳이 어떤 것인지 보여 주마.”"]], [["“Well!” said Mr.", "“그런데 말일세,”"], ["Hale, “Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Thornton is coming to drink tea with us to-night, and he is as proud of Milton as you of Oxford.", "“오늘 저녁에 손턴 씨가 우리와 차를 마시러 오기로 했네. 자네가 옥스퍼드를 자랑스러워하는 만큼 그는 밀턴을 자랑스러워하지."], ["You two must try and make each other a little more liberal-minded.”", "두 사람이 서로를 조금 더 너그러운 사람으로 만들어 보게나.”"]], [["“I don’t want to be more liberal-minded, thank you,”", "“나는 더 너그러워지고 싶은 생각이 없네, 고맙지만.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Is Mr. Thornton coming to tea, papa!”", "“손턴 씨가 차 마시러 오시나요, 아버지?”"], ["asked Margaret in a low voice.", "마거릿이 나직한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Either to tea or soon after.", "“차 시간에 오거나 곧 뒤에 오거나 할 게다."], ["He could not tell.", "자기도 잘 모르겠다더구나."], ["He told us not to wait.”", "기다리지 말라고 했다.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton had determined that he would make no inquiry of his mother as to how far she had put her project into execution of speaking to Margaret about the impropriety of her conduct.", "손턴 씨는 어머니가 마거릿에게 그 행실의 부적절함을 이야기하겠다던 계획을 얼마나 실행에 옮겼는지 어머니에게 묻지 않기로 마음먹고 있었다."], ["He felt pretty sure that, if this interview took place, his mother’s account of what passed at it would only annoy and chagrin him, though he would all the time be aware of the colouring which it received by passing through her mind.", "만일 그 면담이 실제로 있었다면 거기서 오간 말에 대한 어머니의 전언은 자신을 성가시고 언짢게 만들 뿐이리라고 그는 거의 확신했다. 그러면서도 그 이야기가 어머니의 마음을 거치며 어떤 빛깔로 물들었을지 내내 의식하게 되리라는 것도 알았다."], ["He shrank from hearing Margaret’s very name mentioned; he, while he blamed her—while he was jealous of her—while he renounced her—he loved her sorely, in spite of himself.", "그는 마거릿의 이름이 입에 오르는 것조차 듣기 싫었다. 그녀를 탓하면서도, 그녀를 질투하면서도, 그녀를 단념하면서도, 그는 스스로도 어쩌지 못한 채 그녀를 사무치게 사랑했다."], ["He dreamt of her; he dreamt she came dancing towards him with outspread arms, and with a lightness and gaiety which made him loathe her, even while it allured him.", "그는 그녀의 꿈을 꾸었다. 꿈속에서 그녀는 두 팔을 벌린 채 춤추듯 그에게 다가왔는데, 그 가벼움과 들뜸은 그를 홀리면서도 동시에 그녀를 역겹게 여기게 만들었다."], ["But the impression of this figure of Margaret—with all Margaret’s character taken out of it, as completely as if some evil spirit had got possession of her form—was so deeply stamped upon his imagination, that when he wakened he felt hardly able to separate the Una from the Duessa; and the dislike he had to the latter seemed to envelop and disfigure the former.", "그러나 마거릿의 성품이라고는 깡그리 빠져나간—마치 어떤 악령이 그 모습만 차지해 버린 듯한—그 마거릿의 형상이 그의 상상에 어찌나 깊이 찍혔던지, 잠에서 깨어난 뒤에도 그는 우나와 두에사를 좀처럼 갈라놓지 못할 지경이었다. 그리고 뒤의 것에 대한 혐오가 앞의 것까지 휘감아 일그러뜨리는 듯했다."], ["Yet he was too proud to acknowledge his weakness by avoiding the sight of her.", "그러면서도 그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 피함으로써 제 나약함을 인정하기에는 자존심이 너무 셌다."], ["He would neither seek an opportunity of being in her company nor avoid it.", "그녀와 한자리에 있을 기회를 일부러 찾지도, 일부러 피하지도 않겠다는 것이었다."], ["To convince himself of his power of self-control, he lingered over every piece of business this afternoon; he forced every movement into unnatural slowness and deliberation; and it was consequently past eight o’clock before he reached Mr.", "자기에게 자제력이 있음을 스스로 확인하려고 그는 그날 오후 모든 일을 하나하나 질질 끌었다. 몸짓 하나하나를 부자연스러울 만큼 느리고 신중하게 만들었다."], ["Hale’s. Then there were business arrangements to be transacted in the study with Mr.", "그리하여 그가 헤일 씨 댁에 닿았을 때는 이미 여덟 시가 넘어 있었다."], ["Bell; and the latter kept on, sitting over the fire, and talking wearily, long after all business was transacted, and when they might just as well have gone upstairs.", "그다음에는 서재에서 벨 씨와 처리할 사업상의 일이 있었다. 그런데 벨 씨는 볼일이 다 끝나고 위층으로 올라가도 좋았을 때가 한참 지나도록 난롯가에 앉아 지루하게 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["But Mr. Thornton would not say a word about moving their quarters; he chafed and chafed, and thought Mr.", "그러나 손턴 씨는 자리를 옮기자는 말을 한마디도 꺼내려 하지 않았다."], ["Bell a most prosy companion; while Mr. Bell returned the compliment in secret, by considering Mr.", "그는 속으로 애가 타고 또 탔고, 벨 씨야말로 더없이 따분한 말상대라고 생각했다."], ["Thornton about as brusque and curt a fellow as he had ever met with, and terribly gone off both in intelligence and manner. At last some slight noise in the room above suggested the desirableness of moving there.", "한편 벨 씨는 벨 씨대로 속으로 그 인사를 고스란히 되돌려 주어, 손턴 씨야말로 여태 만나 본 가운데 가장 무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 사내이며 머리도 태도도 형편없이 나빠졌다고 여겼다."], ["They found Margaret with a letter open before her, eagerly discussing its contents with her father.", "마침내 위층 방에서 무슨 기척이 들려 그리로 올라가는 편이 좋겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["On the entrance of the gentlemen, it was immediately put aside; but Mr.", "두 사람은 마거릿이 편지를 펼쳐 놓고 그 내용을 아버지와 열심히 이야기하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Thornton’s eager senses caught some few words of Mr.", "신사들이 들어서자 편지는 곧 치워졌다."], ["Hale’s to Mr. Bell.", "그러나 곤두서 있던 손턴 씨의 감각은 헤일 씨가 벨 씨에게 건네는 몇 마디를 붙잡고 말았다."]], [["“A letter from Henry Lennox.", "“헨리 레녹스에게서 온 편지라네."], ["It makes Margaret very hopeful.”", "마거릿이 아주 희망에 부풀었지.”"]], [["Mr. Bell nodded.", "벨 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["Margaret was red as a rose when Mr. Thornton looked at her.", "손턴 씨가 바라보았을 때 마거릿의 얼굴은 장미처럼 붉었다."], ["He had the greatest mind in the world to get up and go out of the room that very instant, and never set foot in the house again.", "그는 그 자리에서 당장 일어나 방을 나가서 다시는 이 집에 발을 들이지 않고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았다."]], [["“We were thinking,” said Mr.", "“우리는 이렇게들 생각했다네.”"], ["Hale, “that you and Mr.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["Thornton had taken Margaret’s advice, and were each trying to convert the other, you were so long in the study.”", "“자네와 손턴 씨가 마거릿의 충고를 받아들여 서로를 개종시키려 애쓰고 있는 모양이라고 말이야. 서재에 그렇게 오래 있었으니.”"]], [["“And you thought there would be nothing left of us but an opinion, like the Kilkenny cat’s tail.", "“그래서 킬케니 고양이 꼬리처럼 우리에게서도 의견 하나밖에 남지 않으리라 생각했다는 건가."], ["Pray whose opinion did you think would have the most obstinate vitality?”", "그래, 누구 의견이 가장 질기게 살아남을 것 같던가?”"]], [["Mr. Thornton had not a notion what they were talking about, and disdained to inquire.", "손턴 씨는 두 사람이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 도무지 알 수 없었으나 물어보는 것은 자존심이 상해서 하지 않았다."], ["Mr. Hale politely enlightened him.", "헤일 씨가 정중하게 사정을 일러 주었다."]], [["“Mr. Thornton, we were accusing Mr.", "“손턴 씨, 우리는 오늘 아침에 벨 씨가 고향 도시에 대해 일종의 옥스퍼드식 중세적 편견을 품고 있다고 나무라던 참이었습니다."], ["Bell this morning of a kind of Oxonian mediæval bigotry against his native town; and we—Margaret, I believe—suggested that it would do him good to associate a little with Milton manufacturers.”", "그러면서 우리가—아마 마거릿이었지 싶은데—밀턴의 공장주들과 조금 어울려 보는 것이 벨 씨에게 이로우리라고 말했지요.”"]], [["“I beg your pardon.", "“실례지만 그게 아닐세."], ["Margaret thought it would do the Milton manufacturers good to associate a little more with Oxford men.", "마거릿은 밀턴의 공장주들이 옥스퍼드 사람들과 조금 더 어울리는 것이 그들에게 이로우리라고 했네."], ["Now wasn’t it so, Margaret?”", "안 그러냐, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I believe I thought it would do both good to see a little more of the other,—I did not know it was my idea any more than papa’s.”", "“제 생각에는 서로가 서로를 조금 더 겪어 보는 것이 양쪽 모두에게 좋으리라고 말한 것 같아요. 그게 아버지 생각이 아니라 제 생각이었는지도 몰랐고요.”"]], [["“And so you see, Mr.", "“그러니 보십시오, 손턴 씨."], ["Thornton, we ought to have been improving each other down-stairs, instead of talking over vanished families of Smith’s and Harrison’s.", "우리는 아래층에서 사라져 버린 스미스 집안이니 해리슨 집안이니 하는 이야기를 늘어놓을 게 아니라 서로를 갈고닦았어야 했던 겁니다."], ["However, I am willing to do my part now.", "하지만 이제라도 내 몫은 하지요."], ["I wonder when you Milton men intend to live.", "대체 밀턴 사람들은 언제쯤 살아 볼 작정인지 궁금하군요."], ["All your lives seem to be spent in gathering together the materials for life.”", "당신들 삶은 통째로 삶의 재료를 그러모으는 데만 쓰이는 것 같으니 말입니다.”"]], [["“By living, I suppose you mean enjoyment.”", "“산다는 것으로 즐김을 말씀하시는 모양이군요.”"]], [["“Yes, enjoyment,—I don’t specify of what, because I trust we should both consider mere pleasure as very poor enjoyment.”", "“그렇지요, 즐김. 무엇을 즐기느냐까지는 따지지 않겠습니다. 당신이나 나나 한낱 쾌락 따위는 아주 보잘것없는 즐김으로 여기리라 믿으니까요.”"]], [["“I would rather have the nature of the enjoyment defined.”", "“저로서는 그 즐김이라는 것의 성격을 좀 더 분명히 해 주셨으면 합니다.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그럼 이렇게 말하지요."], ["enjoyment or leisure—enjoyment of the power and influence which money gives.", "즐김 또는 여유—돈이 가져다주는 힘과 영향력을 누리는 일 말입니다."], ["You are all striving for money.", "당신들은 모두 돈을 얻으려고 애쓰지요."], ["What do you want it for?”", "대체 무엇에 쓰려는 겁니까?”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was silent.", "손턴 씨는 잠자코 있었다."], ["Then he said, “I really don’t know.", "그러다 입을 열었다. “정말이지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다."], ["But money is not what I strive for.”", "다만 제가 얻으려고 애쓰는 것은 돈이 아닙니다.”"]], [["“What then?”", "“그럼 무엇입니까?”"]], [["“It is a home question.", "“그건 속을 파고드는 물음입니다."], ["I shall have to lay myself open to such a catechist, and I am not sure that I am prepared to do it.”", "그런 심문관 앞에서는 제 속을 다 드러내야 할 텐데, 제가 그럴 준비가 되어 있는지는 잘 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“No!” said Mr.", "“안 되지요!”"], ["Hale; “don’t let us be personal in our catechism.", "헤일 씨가 말했다. “우리 문답에서 개인적인 이야기는 빼기로 합시다."], ["You are neither of you representative men; you are each of you too individual for that.”", "두 분 다 대표자가 될 만한 사람이 아니에요. 각자가 너무 개성이 강해서 말이지요.”"]], [["“I am not sure whether to consider that as a compliment or not.", "“그걸 칭찬으로 여겨야 할지 어떨지 모르겠구먼."], ["I should like to be the representative of Oxford, with its beauty and its learning, and its proud old history.", "나는 아름다움과 학문과 자랑스러운 옛 역사를 지닌 옥스퍼드를 대표하고 싶은데."], ["What do you say, Margaret; ought I to be flattered?”", "어떻게 생각하느냐, 마거릿. 내가 우쭐해해도 되겠니?”"]], [["“I don’t know Oxford.", "“저는 옥스퍼드를 몰라요."], ["But there is a difference between being the representative of a city and the representative man of its inhabitants.”", "하지만 어떤 도시를 대표하는 것과 그 주민들을 대표하는 사람이 되는 것은 다르지요.”"]], [["“Very true, Miss Margaret.", "“지당한 말이다, 마거릿 아가씨."], ["Now I remember, you were against me this morning, and were quite Miltonian and manufacturing in your preferences.”", "그러고 보니 오늘 아침에도 너는 내 편이 아니었지. 취향이 아주 밀턴 사람 같고 공장 사람 같더구나.”"]], [["Margaret saw the quick glance of surprise that Mr. Thornton gave her, and she was annoyed at the construction which he might put on this speech of Mr. Bell’s. Mr. Bell went on—", "마거릿은 손턴 씨가 자기에게 재빨리 던지는 놀란 눈길을 알아차렸고, 벨 씨의 이 말을 그가 어떻게 새길지 생각하니 마음이 언짢았다. 벨 씨가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I wish I could show you our High Street—our Radcliffe Square.", "우리 하이 스트리트와 래드클리프 광장을 보여 드릴 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["I am leaving out our colleges, just as I give Mr.", "대학 건물들은 빼고 말하는 겁니다."], ["Thornton leave to omit his factories in speaking of the charms of Milton.", "손턴 씨가 밀턴의 매력을 말할 때 공장들을 빼도 좋다고 허락하는 것처럼요."], ["I have a right to abuse my birth-place.", "나는 내 고향을 헐뜯을 권리가 있지요."], ["Remember I am a Milton man.”", "나도 밀턴 사람이라는 걸 잊지 마십시오.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton was annoyed more than he ought to have been at all that Mr.", "손턴 씨는 벨 씨의 말 하나하나에 필요 이상으로 언짢아졌다."], ["Bell was saying.", "농담을 받아 줄 기분이 아니었던 것이다."], ["He was not in a mood for joking. At another time, he could have enjoyed Mr.", "다른 때였다면 자기가 몸에 익힌 온갖 습관과 그토록 어긋나는 삶이 이루어지는 도시를 두고 벨 씨가 짐짓 심술궂게 나무라는 것을 재미있게 들었을 것이다."], ["Bell’s half testy condemnation of a town where the life was so at variance with every habit he had formed; but now, he was galled enough to attempt to defend what was never meant to be seriously attacked.", "그러나 지금은 속이 하도 쓰라려서 애초에 진지한 공격이 아니었던 것을 굳이 막아서려 들었다."]], [["“I don’t set up Milton as a model of a town.”", "“저는 밀턴을 도시의 본보기라고 내세우지 않습니다.”"]], [["“Not in architecture?”", "“건축에서도 아니고?”"], ["slyly asked Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 짓궂게 물었다."]], [["“No!", "“그럼요!"], ["We’ve been too busy to attend to mere outward appearances.”", "우리는 너무 바빠서 겉모습 따위에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없었습니다.”"]], [["“Don’t say mere outward appearances,”", "“겉모습 ‘따위’라고는 하지 마십시오.”"], ["said Mr. Hale, gently.", "헤일 씨가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“They impress us all, from childhood upward—every day of our life.”", "“그것은 어릴 적부터 우리 모두에게, 살아가는 하루하루에 자국을 남기니까요.”"]], [["“Wait a little while,”", "“조금만 기다려 보십시오.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 말했다."], ["“Remember we are of a different race from the Greeks, to whom beauty was everything, and to whom Mr.", "“우리는 그리스인들과는 다른 핏줄임을 잊지 마십시오. 그들에게는 아름다움이 전부였고, 그들에게라면 벨 씨께서 여유와 고요한 즐김의 삶을 말씀하실 만도 하지요."], ["Bell might speak of a life of leisure and serene enjoyment, much of which entered in through their outward senses.", "그 즐김의 상당 부분은 바깥 감각을 통해 들어오는 것이었으니까요. 저는 그들을 흉내 낼 생각이 없는 만큼이나 그들을 업신여길 생각도 없습니다."], ["I don’t mean to despise them, any more than I would ape them.", "다만 저는 게르만의 피를 이었습니다. 잉글랜드의 다른 지방에 비하면 이 지방에서는 그 피가 덜 섞였지요."], ["But I belong to Teutonic blood; it is little mingled in this part of England to what it is in others; we retain much of their language; we retain more of their spirit; we do not look upon life as a time for enjoyment, but as a time for action and exertion.", "우리는 그들의 말을 많이 간직하고 있고, 그들의 정신은 더 많이 간직하고 있습니다. 우리는 삶을 즐길 시간이 아니라 행하고 힘쓸 시간으로 봅니다."], ["Our glory and our beauty arise out of our outward strength, which makes us victorious over material resistance, and over greater difficulties still.", "우리의 영광과 아름다움은 우리의 바깥 힘에서 솟아납니다. 그 힘이 우리를 물질의 저항 앞에서, 나아가 더 큰 어려움 앞에서 이기게 하지요."], ["We are Teutonic up here in Darkshire in another way.", "우리 다크셔 사람들은 또 다른 뜻에서도 게르만 사람입니다."], ["We hate to have laws made for us at a distance.", "우리는 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 우리를 위한 법이 만들어지는 것을 몹시 싫어합니다."], ["We wish people would allow us to right ourselves, instead of continually meddling, with their imperfect legislation.", "어설픈 입법으로 끊임없이 참견할 게 아니라, 우리 스스로 우리 일을 바로잡게 내버려 두었으면 합니다."], ["We stand up for self-government, and oppose centralisation.”", "우리는 자치를 내세우고 중앙집권에 반대합니다.”"]], [["“In short, you would like the Heptarchy back again.", "“요컨대 칠왕국 시절로 돌아가고 싶다는 말씀이군요."], ["Well, at any rate, I revoke what I said this morning—that you Milton people did not reverence the past.", "아무튼 오늘 아침에 내가 한 말은 거두어들이겠습니다. 밀턴 사람들이 과거를 존중하지 않는다고 했던 말 말입니다."], ["You are regular worshippers of Thor.”", "당신들은 어엿한 토르 숭배자로군요.”"]], [["“If we do not reverence the past as you do in Oxford, it is because we want something which can apply to the present more directly.", "“우리가 옥스퍼드 사람들처럼 과거를 받들지 않는다면, 그것은 현재에 좀 더 곧바로 쓰일 무언가를 원하기 때문입니다."], ["It is fine when the study of the past leads to a prophecy of the future.", "과거를 연구해 미래를 내다보는 예언에 이른다면 훌륭한 일이지요."], ["But to men groping in new circumstances, it would be finer if the words of experience could direct us how to act in what concerns us most intimately and immediately; which is full of difficulties to be encountered; and upon the mode in which they are met and conquered—not merely pushed aside for the time—depends our future.", "그러나 새로운 형편 속을 더듬어 나가는 사람들에게는, 경험에서 나온 말이 우리와 가장 가깝고 가장 급한 일에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지를 일러 준다면 그편이 더 훌륭할 겁니다. 그 일에는 부딪쳐야 할 어려움이 가득하고, 그것을 어떤 식으로 맞아 이겨 내느냐에—당장만 모면하려고 옆으로 밀어 두는 것이 아니라—우리의 앞날이 달려 있으니까요."], ["But no!", "그런데 그렇지가 않아요!"], ["People can speak of Utopia much more easily than of the next day’s duty; and yet when that duty is all done by others, who so ready to cry, ‘Fie, for shame!’”", "사람들은 내일 해야 할 일보다 유토피아를 이야기하기가 훨씬 쉽지요. 그러면서도 그 일을 남들이 다 해 놓고 나면 ‘아이고, 부끄러운 줄 알아라!’ 하고 외칠 준비는 누구보다 잘 되어 있습니다.”"]], [["“And all this time I don’t see what you are talking about.", "“그런데도 나는 여태 당신이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 모르겠군요."], ["Would you Milton men condescend to send up your to-day’s difficulty to Oxford?", "밀턴 사람들께서는 오늘의 어려움을 옥스퍼드로 올려 보내 주실 생각은 없으신지요?"], ["You have not tried us yet.”", "아직 우리를 시험해 보지도 않으셨잖습니까.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton laughed outright at this.", "이 말에 손턴 씨는 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["“I believe I was talking with reference to a good deal that has been troubling us of late; I was thinking of the strikes we have gone through, which are troublesome and injurious things enough, as I am finding to my cost.", "“요즘 우리를 꽤나 괴롭혀 온 일들을 떠올리며 말한 듯합니다. 우리가 겪어 온 파업들을 생각하고 있었지요."], ["And yet this last strike, under which I am smarting, has been respectable.”", "제가 손해를 보며 깨닫고 있듯이 성가시고 해로운 일입니다. 그런데도 제가 지금 쓰라림을 겪고 있는 이 마지막 파업은 훌륭했습니다.”"]], [["“A respectable strike!”", "“훌륭한 파업이라!”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“That sounds as if you were far gone in the worship of Thor.”", "“토르 숭배가 꽤나 깊어졌다는 소리로 들리는군요.”"]], [["Margaret felt, rather than saw, that Mr. Thornton was chagrined by the repeated turning into jest of what he was feeling as very serious.", "마거릿은 자기로서는 아주 진지하게 느끼는 일이 자꾸만 농담거리로 바뀌는 데 손턴 씨가 속상해하고 있음을 눈으로 보기보다 마음으로 느꼈다."], ["She tried to change the conversation from a subject about which one party cared little, while, to the other, it was deeply, because personally, interesting.", "그녀는 한쪽은 대수롭지 않게 여기지만 다른 한쪽에게는 자기 일이기에 깊이 사무치는 이 화제에서 이야기를 돌려 보려 애썼다."], ["She forced herself to say something.", "그녀는 억지로 무슨 말이든 꺼냈다."]], [["“Edith says she finds the printed calicoes in Corfu better and cheaper than in London.”", "“이디스가 그러는데 코르푸에서는 무늬 놓은 옥양목이 런던보다 좋고 값도 싸대요.”"]], [["“Does she?”", "“그러더냐?”"], ["said her father, “I think that must be one of Edith’s exaggerations.", "아버지가 말했다. “그건 이디스가 늘 하는 과장 가운데 하나일 게다."], ["Are you sure of it, Margaret?”", "확실하냐, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I am sure she says so, papa.”", "“그 애가 그렇게 말한 건 확실해요, 아버지.”"]], [["“Then I am sure of the fact,”", "“그럼 그건 사실일세.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“Margaret, I go so far in my idea of your truthfulness, that it shall cover your cousin’s character.", "“마거릿, 나는 네 정직함을 어찌나 믿는지 그 믿음이 네 사촌의 인품까지 덮어 주는구나."], ["I don’t believe a cousin of yours could exaggerate.”", "네 사촌이라면 과장을 할 리 없지.”"]], [["“Is Miss Hale so remarkable for truth?”", "“헤일 양이 그렇게 정직하기로 이름났습니까?”"], ["said Mr. Thornton, bitterly.", "손턴 씨가 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["The moment he had done so, he could have bitten his tongue out.", "그 말을 내뱉은 순간 그는 제 혀를 물어뜯고 싶었다."], ["What was he?", "자기가 무엇이라고?"], ["And why should he stab her with her shame in this way?", "무엇 때문에 그녀의 수치를 들추어 이런 식으로 그녀를 찌른단 말인가? 오늘 밤 그는 어찌 이리 못돼 먹었는가."], ["How evil he was to-night: possessed by ill-humour at being detained so long from her; irritated by the mention of some name, because he thought it belonged to a more successful lover; now ill-tempered because he had been unable to cope, with a light heart, against one who was trying, by gay and careless speeches, to make the evening pass pleasantly away,—the kind old friend to all parties, whose manner by this time might be well known to Mr.", "그토록 오래 그녀에게서 붙들려 있었다는 심술에 사로잡히고, 어떤 이름이 나왔다고 해서—그것이 자기보다 운 좋은 연적의 이름이라 여겨져서—짜증이 나고, 흥겹고 스스럼없는 말로 저녁 한때를 즐겁게 흘려보내려 애쓰는 사람을 가벼운 마음으로 상대해 주지 못한 탓에 심사가 뒤틀렸다. 상대는 모두에게 다정한 옛 벗이고, 여러 해 알고 지낸 손턴 씨라면 그 사람됨을 이미 잘 알 만도 했는데 말이다."], ["Thornton, who had been acquainted with him for many years.", "그러고서 마거릿에게 그런 말을 하다니! 그녀는 예전처럼 자리에서 일어나 방을 나가지는 않았다."], ["And then to speak to Margaret as he had done!", "그의 퉁명스러움이나 성미가 그녀를 언짢게 할 때면 늘 그러던 것과 달리."], ["She did not get up and leave the room, as she had done in former days, when his abruptness or his temper had annoyed her.", "그녀는 뜻밖의 매몰찬 대접을 받은 아이처럼 서글피 놀란 눈길을 한순간 던졌을 뿐, 그대로 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그 눈은 천천히 커지며 구슬프고 원망 어린 슬픔으로 차올랐다."], ["She sat quite still, after the first momentary glance of grieved surprise, that made her eyes look like some child’s who has met with an unexpected rebuff; they slowly dilated into mournful, reproachful sadness; and then they fell, and she bent over her work, and did not speak again.", "그러다 눈길이 떨어졌고, 그녀는 바느질감 위로 몸을 숙인 채 다시는 입을 열지 않았다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바라보지 않을 수 없었고, 낯선 한기에 떨기라도 하듯 그녀의 몸을 훑고 지나가는 한숨을 보았다."], ["But he could not help looking at her, and he saw a sigh tremble over her body, as if she quivered in some unwonted chill.", "그는 아기를 “흔들어 어르고 나무라던” 어머니가, 어머니의 사랑을 온전히 믿는다는 뜻으로 아기가 천천히 지어 보이는 그 미소로 사랑이 되살아났음을 확인하기도 전에 불려 나가야 했을 때의 심정이었다."], ["He felt as the mother would have done, in the midst of “her rocking it, and rating it,”", "그는 짤막하고 날 선 대답만 했다. 마음이 편치 않고 뾰족해져서 농담과 진담을 가려 듣지도 못했다."], ["had she been called away before her slow confiding smile, implying perfect trust in mother’s love, had proved the renewing of its love.", "오직 그녀의 눈길 한 번, 말 한마디를 바랐다. 그 앞에 뉘우침으로 겸허히 엎드릴 수 있도록."], ["He gave short sharp answers; he was uneasy and cross, unable to discern between jest and earnest; anxious only for a look, a word of hers, before which to prostrate himself in penitent humility.", "그러나 그녀는 바라보지도 말하지도 않았다. 그녀의 동그랗고 갸름한 손가락이 마치 그것이 평생의 일이기라도 한 듯 한결같고 빠르게 바느질감을 들락거렸다."], ["But she neither looked nor spoke.", "그녀는 자기를 마음에 둘 리 없다고 그는 생각했다."], ["Her round taper fingers flew in and out of her sewing, as steadily and swiftly as if that were the business of her life.", "그렇지 않다면 자기 바람이 이토록 뜨거우니 그녀도 단 한순간이나마 그 눈을 들어 자기 눈 속의 뒤늦은 뉘우침을 읽지 않을 수 없었으리라고."], ["She could not care for him, he thought, or else the passionate fervour of his wish would have forced her to raise those eyes, if but for an instant, to read the late repentance in his.", "그는 떠나기 전에 그녀를 때리기라도 하고 싶었다. 그런 터무니없이 무례한 짓이라도 저질러야 제 가슴을 갉아먹는 회한을 그녀에게 털어놓을 자격을 얻을 것 같아서였다."], ["He could have struck her before he left, in order that by some strange overt act of rudeness, he might earn the privilege of telling her the remorse that gnawed at his heart.", "그날 저녁의 마무리가 바깥바람을 맞으며 오래 걷는 일이었던 것은 다행이었다. 그 걸음이 그를 차분하게 가라앉혀, 앞으로는 되도록 그녀를 보지 않겠다는 무거운 결심으로 되돌려 놓았다."], ["It was well that the long walk in the open air wound up this evening for him.", "그 얼굴과 자태를 보는 것만으로도, 그 목소리를 듣는 것만으로도—맑은 가락처럼 부드러운 바람 같은 그 목소리—그의 중심이 이토록 흔들리니 말이다. 그렇다!"], ["It sobered him back into grave resolution that henceforth he would see as little of her as possible,—since the very sight of that face and form, the very sounds of that voice (like the soft winds of pure melody) had such power to move him from his balance. Well!", "그는 사랑이 무엇인지 알게 되었다."], ["He had known what love was—a sharp pang, a fierce experience, in the midst of whose flames he was struggling!", "날카로운 아픔, 사나운 겪음이었다. 그는 그 불길 한가운데서 몸부림치고 있었다."], ["but, through the furnace he would fight his way out into the serenity of middle age,—all the richer and more human for having known this great passion.", "그러나 그 용광로를 지나 중년의 고요함으로 싸워 나갈 참이었다. 이 큰 열정을 겪었기에 한결 더 풍요롭고 한결 더 사람다워진 채로."]], [["When he had somewhat abruptly left the room, Margaret rose from her seat, and began silently to fold up her work.", "그가 다소 갑작스레 방을 나가자 마거릿은 자리에서 일어나 말없이 바느질감을 개기 시작했다."], ["The long seams were heavy, and had an unusual weight for her languid arms.", "길게 이어진 시접이 무겁게 느껴졌고, 힘 빠진 그녀의 팔에는 여느 때와 달리 묵직했다."], ["The round lines in her face took a lengthened, straighter form, and her whole appearance was that of one who had gone through a day of great fatigue. As the three prepared for bed, Mr.", "그녀의 동그스름하던 얼굴선이 길고 곧게 뻗은 모양으로 바뀌었고, 온몸이 하루 종일 몹시 고단한 일을 겪어 낸 사람 같았다."], ["Bell muttered a little condemnation of Mr. Thornton.", "세 사람이 잠자리에 들 채비를 하는 동안 벨 씨는 손턴 씨를 조금 나무라는 말을 웅얼거렸다."]], [["“I never saw a fellow so spoiled by success.", "“성공에 저렇게 망가진 사내는 처음 보네."], ["He can’t bear a word; a jest of any kind.", "말 한마디를 못 견뎌. 무슨 농담이든 못 견디고."], ["Everything seems to touch on the soreness of his high dignity.", "무엇이든 그 잘난 위엄의 아픈 데를 건드리는 모양이야."], ["Formerly, he was as simple and noble as the open day; you could not offend him, because he had no vanity.”", "예전에는 훤한 대낮처럼 소탈하고 늠름한 사람이었는데. 허영이라고는 없어서 기분을 상하게 할 수도 없었지.”"]], [["“He is not vain now,”", "“지금도 허영은 없어요.”"], ["said Margaret, turning round from the table, and speaking with quiet distinctness.", "마거릿이 탁자에서 몸을 돌리며 조용하고 또렷하게 말했다."], ["“To-night he has not been like himself.", "“오늘 밤에는 평소의 그분답지 않았을 뿐이에요."], ["Something must have annoyed him before he came here.”", "여기 오시기 전에 무슨 언짢은 일이 있었던 게 분명해요.”"]], [["Mr. Bell gave her one of his sharp glances from above his spectacles.", "벨 씨는 안경 너머로 그 날카로운 눈길 가운데 하나를 그녀에게 던졌다."], ["She stood it quite calmly; but, after she had left the room, he suddenly asked,—", "그녀는 아주 침착하게 그 눈길을 받아 냈다. 그러나 그녀가 방을 나간 뒤 그가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Hale!", "“헤일!"], ["did it ever strike you that Thornton and your daughter have what the French call a tendresse for each other?”", "손턴과 자네 딸 사이에 프랑스 사람들이 말하는 ‘탕드레스’가 있다는 생각이 든 적은 없나?”"]], [["“Never!” said Mr.", "“한 번도 없네!”"], ["Hale, first startled and then flurried by the new idea.", "헤일 씨가 말했다. 처음에는 놀라더니 이내 그 새로운 생각에 허둥댔다."], ["“No, I am sure you are wrong, I am almost certain you are mistaken.", "“아니야, 자네가 틀렸다고 확신하네. 거의 틀림없이 잘못 본 걸세."], ["If there is anything, it is all on Mr. Thornton’s side.", "무언가 있다면 그건 다 손턴 씨 쪽 이야기지."], ["Poor fellow!", "딱한 사람!"], ["I hope and trust he is not thinking of her, for I am sure she would not have him.”", "그가 그 애를 마음에 두고 있지 않기를 바라고 또 바라네. 그 애가 그를 받아들일 리 없다는 걸 잘 아니까.”"]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄!"], ["I’m a bachelor, and have steered clear of love affairs all my life; so perhaps my opinion is not worth having.", "나야 독신이고 평생 연애라고는 비껴서 살았으니 내 견해는 값어치가 없을지도 모르지."], ["Or else I should say there were very pretty symptoms about her!”", "그렇지만 않다면, 그 애 쪽에도 아주 그럴듯한 낌새가 있더라고 말하겠네만!”"]], [["“Then I am sure you are wrong,”", "“그렇다면 자네가 틀린 것이 분명하네.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“He may care for her, though she really has been almost rude to him at times.", "“그가 그 애를 마음에 둘 수는 있겠지. 사실 그 애가 때로는 그에게 거의 무례하다시피 했는데도 말이야."], ["But she!—why, Margaret would never think of him, I’m sure.", "하지만 그 애가! 아니, 마거릿이 그를 두고 그런 생각을 할 리 없네."], ["Such a thing has never entered her head.”", "그런 일은 그 애 머릿속에 들어온 적도 없을 걸세.”"]], [["“Entering her heart would do.", "“머리 말고 가슴에 들어왔으면 되는 거지."], ["But I merely threw out a suggestion of what might be.", "하지만 나야 그럴 수도 있겠다는 짐작을 던져 보았을 뿐이네."], ["I dare say I was wrong.", "아마 내가 틀렸겠지."], ["And whether I was wrong or right, I’m very sleepy; so, having disturbed your night’s rest (as I can see) with my untimely fancies, I’ll betake myself with an easy mind to my own.”", "틀렸든 맞았든 나는 몹시 졸리네. 그러니 철없는 공상으로 자네 밤잠을 흩어 놓았으니(그런 눈치가 보이는군) 나는 홀가분한 마음으로 내 잠자리에나 가겠네.”"]], [["But Mr.", "그러나 헤일 씨는 그런 실없는 생각에 흔들리지 않겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["Hale resolved that he would not be disturbed by any such nonsensical idea; so he lay awake, determining not to think about it.", "그래서 그 생각을 하지 않기로 작정한 채 잠 못 이루고 누워 있었다."]], [["Mr. Bell took his leave the next day, bidding Margaret look to him as one who had a right to help and protect her in all her troubles, of whatever nature they might be.", "이튿날 벨 씨는 작별을 고하면서, 어떤 종류의 어려움이든 그 속에서 자기를 도와주고 지켜 줄 권리가 있는 사람으로 여겨 달라고 마거릿에게 당부했다."], ["To Mr. Hale he said,—", "헤일 씨에게는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“That Margaret of yours has gone deep into my heart.", "“자네의 그 마거릿이 내 가슴 깊이 들어와 버렸네. 잘 보살피게."], ["Take care of her, for she is a very precious creature,—a great deal too good for Milton—only fit for Oxford, in fact.", "아주 귀한 아이야. 밀턴에는 너무나 과분하고, 사실 옥스퍼드에나 어울리지."], ["The town, I mean; not the men.", "도시 말일세, 사람들 말고."], ["I can’t match her yet.", "아직은 짝을 못 찾겠네."], ["When I can, I shall bring my young man to stand side by side with your young woman, just as the genii in the Arabian Nights brought Prince Caralmazan to match with the fairy’s Princess Badoura.”", "찾게 되면 내 젊은이를 데려와 자네 젊은 처자와 나란히 세워 보겠네. 아라비안나이트의 정령들이 카랄마잔 왕자를 데려와 요정의 바두라 공주와 견주게 했듯이 말이야.”"]], [["“I beg you’ll do no such thing.", "“제발 그런 짓은 하지 말게."], ["Remember the misfortunes that ensued; and besides, I can’t spare Margaret.”", "그다음에 닥친 불행들을 떠올려 보게나. 게다가 나는 마거릿을 내줄 수 없네.”"]], [["“No; on second thoughts, we’ll have her to nurse us ten years hence, when we shall be two cross old invalids.", "“아니지. 다시 생각해 보니 10년 뒤에 우리 둘이 심술궂은 늙은 병자가 되었을 때 그 애더러 우리를 돌보게 하세."], ["Seriously, Hale!", "진담일세, 헤일! 자네가 밀턴을 떠났으면 좋겠네."], ["I wish you’d leave Milton; which is a most unsuitable place for you, though it was my recommendation in the first instance.", "애초에 내가 권하기는 했다만 자네에게는 더없이 맞지 않는 곳이야. 그래 준다면 나는 내 미심쩍은 그늘들을 삼켜 버리고 대학에서 주는 성직록을 받겠네."], ["If you would, I’d swallow my shadows of doubts, and take a college living; and you and Margaret should come and live at the parsonage—you to be a sort of lay curate, and take the unwashed off my hands; and she to be our housekeeper—the village Lady Bountiful—by day; and read us to sleep in the evenings.", "그러면 자네와 마거릿이 목사관에 와서 사는 거지. 자네는 평신도 부목사쯤 되어 씻지도 않은 무리를 내 손에서 덜어 가고, 그 애는 낮에는 우리 살림꾼—마을의 자선 부인—노릇을 하고, 저녁에는 우리에게 책을 읽어 주어 잠들게 하고."], ["I could be very happy in such a life.", "그런 삶이라면 나는 아주 행복하겠네."], ["What do you think of it?”", "어떤가?”"]], [["“Never,” said Mr.", "“절대로 안 되네.”"], ["Hale, decidedly.", "헤일 씨가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“My one great change has been made and my price of suffering paid.", "“내 인생의 큰 변화는 이미 한 번 치렀고, 그 값으로 치를 고통도 다 치렀네."], ["Here I stay out my life; and here will I be buried, and lost in the crowd.”", "나는 여기서 남은 생을 살고, 여기 묻혀 사람들 틈에 묻혀 사라질 걸세.”"]], [["“I don’t give up my plan yet.", "“나는 아직 내 계획을 접지 않았네."], ["Only I won’t bait you with it any more just now.", "다만 지금은 더 이상 그것으로 자네를 들쑤시지 않겠네."], ["Where’s the Pearl.", "우리 진주는 어디 있나."], ["Come, Margaret, give me a farewell kiss; and remember, my dear, where you may find a true friend, as far as his capability goes.", "이리 오너라, 마거릿, 작별의 입맞춤을 다오. 그리고 얘야, 힘이 닿는 데까지 참된 벗을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 잊지 마라."], ["You are my child, Margaret.", "너는 내 아이다, 마거릿."], ["Remember that, and God bless you!”", "그것을 기억하렴. 그리고 하느님의 축복이 있기를!”"]], [["So they fell back into the monotony of the quiet life they would henceforth lead.", "그렇게 하여 그들은 앞으로 이어 갈 조용한 삶의 단조로움 속으로 되돌아갔다."], ["There was no invalid to hope and fear about; even the Higginses—so long a vivid interest—seemed to have receded from any need of immediate thought.", "이제 마음 졸이며 바라고 두려워할 병자도 없었다. 그토록 오래 생생한 관심거리였던 히긴스 집안 사람들조차 당장 마음 쓸 일에서는 멀어진 듯했다."], ["The Boucher children, left motherless orphans, claimed what of Margaret’s care she could bestow; and she went pretty often to see Mary Higgins, who had charge of them.", "어미를 잃고 고아가 된 바우처네 아이들이 마거릿이 베풀 수 있는 만큼의 보살핌을 요구했고, 그녀는 아이들을 맡고 있는 메리 히긴스를 자주 보러 갔다."], ["The two families were living in one house: the elder children were at humble schools, the younger ones were tended, in Mary’s absence at her work, by the kind neighbour whose good sense had struck Margaret at the time of Boucher’s death.", "두 집안은 한집에서 살고 있었다. 큰 아이들은 변변찮은 학교에 다녔고, 메리가 일하러 나가 자리를 비우면 작은 아이들은 바우처가 죽었을 때 그 분별력이 마거릿의 마음에 남았던 그 친절한 이웃 여자가 돌보았다."], ["Of course she was paid for her trouble; and, indeed, in all his little plans and arrangements for these orphan children, Nicholas showed a sober judgment, and regulated method of thinking, which were at variance with his former more eccentric jerks of action.", "물론 그 수고에는 삯이 치러졌다. 실제로 니컬러스는 이 고아들을 위한 자잘한 계획과 처리에서 하나같이 차분한 판단력과 정연한 사고방식을 보였는데, 이는 예전의 별스럽고 들쭉날쭉하던 행동거지와는 사뭇 달랐다."], ["He was so steady at his work, that Margaret did not often see him during these winter months; but when she did, she saw that he winced away from any reference to the father of those children, whom he had so fully and heartily taken under his care.", "그는 어찌나 꾸준히 일했던지 마거릿은 그 겨울 몇 달 동안 그를 자주 보지 못했다. 그러나 만날 때면, 그토록 온전히 진심으로 떠맡은 그 아이들의 아버지 이야기가 나오면 그가 움찔하며 물러선다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He did not speak easily of Mr. Thornton.", "그는 손턴 씨 이야기도 쉽사리 하지 못했다."]], [["“To tell the truth,”", "“솔직히 말해서요,”"], ["said he, “he fairly bamboozles me.", "그가 말했다. “그 양반은 나를 아주 헷갈리게 만들어 놓습니다."], ["He’s two chaps.", "사람이 둘이란 말이오."], ["One chap I knowed of old as were measter all o’er.", "하나는 내가 옛날부터 알던, 뼛속까지 공장주인 놈이지요."], ["T’other chap hasn’t an ounce of measter’s flesh about him.", "다른 하나는 공장주의 살점이라고는 한 점도 없는 사람이고."], ["How them two chaps is bound up in one body, is a craddy for me to find out.", "그 두 사람이 어떻게 한 몸에 묶여 있는지가 내가 풀어야 할 수수께끼요."], ["I’ll not be beat by it, though.", "그래도 나는 거기 지지 않을 참이오."], ["Meanwhile he comes here pretty often; that’s how I know the chap that’s a man, not a measter.", "그 사이에도 그 양반이 여기 꽤 자주 오는데, 그래서 공장주가 아니라 사람인 쪽을 내가 아는 거요."], ["And I reckon he’s taken aback by me pretty much as I am by him; for he sits and listens and stares, as if I were some strange beast newly caught in some of the zones.", "그리고 내가 그 양반한테 어리둥절한 만큼 그 양반도 나한테 어리둥절한 모양이오. 앉아서 듣고 빤히 쳐다보는 게, 꼭 내가 무슨 낯선 지방에서 갓 잡혀 온 짐승이라도 되는 것 같단 말이오."], ["But I’m none daunted.", "하지만 나는 조금도 주눅 들지 않소."], ["It would take a deal to daunt me in my own house, as he sees.", "내 집에서 나를 주눅 들게 하려면 어지간해서는 안 되지요, 그 양반도 보다시피."], ["And I tell him some of my mind that I reckon he’d ha’ been the better of hearing when he were a younger man.”", "그러고는 그 양반이 젊었을 때 들어 두었으면 좋았을 만한 내 생각을 좀 들려주지요.”"]], [["“And does he not answer you?”", "“그러면 그 사람이 대꾸를 하지 않던가?”"], ["asked Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄!"], ["I’ll not say th’ advantage is all on his side, for all I take credit for improving him above a bit.", "내가 그 양반을 제법 낫게 만들었다고 자부하기는 해도, 득이 온통 그 양반 쪽에만 있다고는 못 하겠소."], ["Sometimes he says a rough thing or two, which is not agreeable to look at at first, but has a queer smack o’ truth in it when yo’ come to chew it.", "이따금 거친 소리를 한두 마디 하는데, 처음 들으면 보기 좋지 않아도 곱씹어 보면 묘하게 진실의 맛이 나거든요."], ["He’ll be coming to-night, I reckon, about them childer’s schooling.", "오늘 밤에도 올 거요, 아마. 그 아이들 학교 문제로요."], ["He’s not satisfied wi’ the make of it, and wants for t’ examine ’em.”", "학교 돌아가는 모양이 마음에 안 든다고 아이들을 좀 살펴보겠다는구먼.”"]], [["“What are they”—began Mr.", "“그 아이들이 무엇을—”"], ["Hale; but Margaret, touching his arm, showed him her watch.", "하고 헤일 씨가 말을 꺼내는데, 마거릿이 그의 팔을 건드리며 자기 시계를 보여 주었다."]], [["“It is nearly seven,” she said.", "“일곱 시가 다 됐어요.”"], ["“The evenings are getting longer now.", "그녀가 말했다. “이제 저녁이 길어지고 있어요."], ["Come, papa.”", "가요, 아버지.”"], ["She did not breathe freely till they were some distance from the house.", "그녀는 그 집에서 어느 정도 멀어질 때까지 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬지 못했다."], ["Then, as she became more calm, she wished that she had not been in so great a hurry; for, somehow, they saw Mr.", "그러다 마음이 가라앉자 그렇게까지 서두르지 않았더라면 좋았으리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["Thornton but very seldom now; and he might have come to see Higgins, and for the old friendship’s sake she should like to have seen him to-night.", "어쩐 일인지 요즘은 손턴 씨를 아주 드물게밖에 보지 못했고, 그가 히긴스를 보러 왔을지도 모르는데, 오랜 우정을 생각하면 오늘 밤 그를 보았더라면 좋았을 것이었다."]], [["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["he came very seldom, even for the dull cold purpose of lessons.", "그는 아주 드물게 왔다. 수업이라는 무미건조하고 서늘한 볼일을 위해서조차 그랬다."], ["Mr. Hale was disappointed in his pupil’s lukewarmness about Greek literature, which had but a short time ago so great an interest for him.", "헤일 씨는 얼마 전만 해도 그토록 큰 관심을 보이던 그리스 문학에 대해 제자가 미지근해진 것에 실망했다."], ["And now it often happened that a hurried note from Mr. Thornton would arrive, just at the last moment, saying that he was so much engaged that he could not come to read with Mr.", "그리고 요즘은 마지막 순간에야 손턴 씨의 급한 쪽지가 도착해 일이 너무 많아 오늘 저녁에는 함께 읽을 수 없겠다고 알려 오는 일이 잦았다."], ["Hale that evening. And though other pupils had taken more than his place as to time, no one was like his first scholar in Mr.", "시간으로 따지자면 다른 제자들이 그의 자리를 넘치도록 채웠지만, 헤일 씨의 마음속에서 첫 제자만 한 사람은 없었다."], ["Hale’s heart. He was depressed and sad at this partial cessation of an intercourse which had become dear to him; and he used to sit pondering over the reason that could have occasioned this change.", "그는 어느새 소중해진 그 교류가 반쯤 끊긴 것에 풀이 죽고 서글퍼졌고, 이런 변화를 불러왔을 까닭을 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있곤 했다."]], [["He startled Margaret, one evening as she sate at her work, by suddenly asking:", "어느 저녁 마거릿이 바느질감을 붙들고 앉아 있는데 그가 불쑥 이렇게 물어 그녀를 놀라게 했다."]], [["“Margaret!", "“마거릿!"], ["had you ever any reason for thinking that Mr. Thornton cared for you?”", "손턴 씨가 너를 마음에 두고 있다고 여길 만한 까닭이 혹시 있었느냐?”"]], [["He almost blushed as he put this question; but Mr.", "이 물음을 던지면서 그는 거의 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Bell’s scouted idea recurred to him, and the words were out of his mouth before he well knew what he was about.", "그러나 물리쳐 두었던 벨 씨의 생각이 다시 떠올랐고, 그는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 채 깨닫기도 전에 말을 뱉고 말았다."]], [["Margaret did not answer immediately; but by the bent drooping of her head, he guessed what her reply would be.", "마거릿은 곧바로 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 고개를 수그린 모양새로 그는 그 대답이 무엇일지 짐작했다."]], [["“Yes; I believe—oh, papa, I should have told you.”", "“네, 그런 것 같아요—아, 아버지, 진작 말씀드렸어야 했어요.”"], ["And she dropped her work, and hid her face in her hands.", "그러고는 바느질감을 떨어뜨리고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“No, dear; don’t think that I am impertinently curious.", "“아니다, 얘야. 내가 주제넘게 캐묻는 것이라고는 생각지 마라."], ["I am sure you would have told me if you had felt that you could return his regard.", "네가 그의 마음에 응할 수 있다고 느꼈다면 나에게 말했으리라고 믿는다."], ["Did he speak to you about it?”", "그가 너에게 그 이야기를 하더냐?”"]], [["No answer at first; but by-and-by a little gentle reluctant “Yes.”", "처음에는 대답이 없었다. 그러나 이윽고 나직하고 마지못한 작은 “네.”가 들려왔다."]], [["“And you refused him?”", "“그래서 거절했고?”"]], [["A long sigh: a more helpless, nerveless attitude, and another “Yes.” But before her father could speak, Margaret lifted up her face, rosy with some beautiful shame, and, fixing her eyes upon him, said:", "긴 한숨. 한층 더 힘없이 늘어진 자세. 그리고 또 한 번의 “네.” 그러나 아버지가 미처 입을 열기 전에 마거릿이 얼굴을 들었다. 어여쁜 부끄러움으로 발그레해진 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 아버지에게 눈길을 붙박고 말했다."]], [["“Now, papa, I have told you all this, and I cannot tell you more; and then the whole thing is so painful to me; every word and action connected with it is so unspeakably bitter, that I cannot bear to think of it.", "“아버지, 이제 이 이야기는 다 말씀드렸고 그 이상은 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 게다가 이 모든 일이 저에게는 너무 아파요."], ["Oh, papa, I am sorry to have lost you this friend, but I could not help it—but oh!", "거기 얽힌 말 한마디 몸짓 하나가 이루 말할 수 없이 쓰라려서 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없어요. 아, 아버지, 아버지께 이 친구분을 잃어 드려서 죄송해요."], ["I am very sorry.”", "하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요."], ["She sate down on the ground, and laid her head on his knees.", "그래도 아, 정말 죄송해요.” 그녀는 바닥에 앉아 아버지의 무릎에 머리를 얹었다."]], [["“I, too, am sorry, my dear. Mr.", "“나도 안타깝구나, 얘야."], ["Bell quite startled me when he said, some idea of the kind—”", "벨 씨가 그런 짐작을 말했을 때 나는 무척 놀랐단다—”"]], [["“Mr. Bell!", "“벨 씨가요!"], ["Oh did Mr. Bell see it?”", "아, 벨 씨가 눈치채셨어요?”"]], [["“A little; but he took it into his head that you—how shall I say it?—that you were not ungraciously disposed towards Mr.", "“조금은. 하지만 그는 네가—뭐라고 해야 하나?—네가 손턴 씨에게 싫지 않은 마음을 품고 있다고 짐작했더구나."], ["Thornton. I knew that could never be.", "나는 그럴 리 없다는 것을 알고 있었지."], ["I hoped the whole thing was but an imagination; but I knew too well what your real feelings were to suppose that you could ever like Mr. Thornton in that way.", "나는 이 모든 것이 그저 그의 공상이기를 바랐다만, 네 진짜 마음을 너무 잘 알고 있었으니 네가 손턴 씨를 그런 식으로 좋아할 수 있으리라고는 생각할 수 없었지."], ["But I am very sorry.”", "그래도 참으로 안타깝구나.”"]], [["They were very quiet and still for some minutes.", "두 사람은 몇 분 동안 아주 조용히 가만히 있었다."], ["But, on stroking her cheek in a caressing way soon after, he was almost shocked to find her face wet with tears.", "그러나 얼마 뒤 아버지가 어루만지듯 그녀의 뺨을 쓸어내리다가 그 얼굴이 눈물로 젖어 있는 것을 알고는 거의 충격을 받았다."], ["As he touched her, she sprang up, and smiling with forced brightness, began to talk of the Lennoxes with such a vehement desire to turn the conversation, that Mr.", "그가 손을 대자 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 억지로 환하게 웃으며 레녹스 집안 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["Hale was too tender-hearted to try to force it back into the old channel.", "화제를 돌리려는 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 마음 여린 헤일 씨는 이야기를 도로 본래의 물길로 돌리려 하지 못했다."]], [["“To-morrow—yes, to-morrow they will be back in Harley Street.", "“내일—그래요, 내일이면 그 사람들이 할리 가로 돌아오겠네요."], ["Oh, how strange it will be!", "아, 얼마나 낯설까!"], ["I wonder what room they will make into the nursery?", "어느 방을 아기방으로 만들까요?"], ["Aunt Shaw will be happy with the baby.", "쇼 이모는 아기와 함께 행복하시겠죠."], ["Fancy Edith a mamma!", "이디스가 엄마가 되다니!"], ["And Captain Lennox—I wonder what he will do with himself now he has sold out!”", "그리고 레녹스 대위는—군직을 팔았으니 이제 뭘 하며 지낼지 궁금해요!”"]], [["“I’ll tell you what,”", "“이렇게 하자꾸나.”"], ["said her father, anxious to indulge her in this fresh subject of interest, “I think I must spare you for a fortnight just to run up to town and see the travellers.", "아버지가 그녀의 새로운 관심거리를 북돋아 주고 싶은 마음에 말했다. “두 주쯤 너를 보내 주어야겠다."], ["You could learn more by half an hour’s conversation with Mr.", "런던에 올라가 여행에서 돌아온 사람들을 보고 오려무나."], ["Henry Lennox about Frederick’s chances, than in a dozen of these letters of his; so it would, in fact, be uniting business with pleasure.”", "프레더릭의 앞날에 대해서는 헨리 레녹스 씨와 반 시간만 이야기해도 그가 보낸 편지 열두 통보다 더 많이 알게 될 게다. 그러니 사실 볼일과 즐거움을 겸하는 셈이지.”"]], [["“No, papa, you cannot spare me, and what’s more, I won’t be spared.”", "“아니에요, 아버지. 아버지는 저를 보내실 수 없고, 게다가 저도 보내지지 않을 거예요.”"], ["Then after a pause, she added: “I am losing hope sadly about Frederick; he is letting us down gently, but I can see that Mr.", "그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 덧붙였다. “저는 프레더릭 오빠 일에 대해서는 슬프게도 희망을 잃어 가고 있어요."], ["Lennox himself has no hope of hunting up the witnesses under years and years of time.", "오빠는 우리에게 부드럽게 알려 주고 있지만, 레녹스 씨 자신도 여러 해가 지나기 전에는 증인들을 찾아낼 가망이 없다고 여긴다는 걸 알 수 있어요. 아니요.”"], ["No,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “that bubble was very pretty, and very dear to our hearts; but it has burst like many another; and we must console ourselves with being glad that Frederick is so happy, and with being a great deal to each other.", "“그 물거품은 참 예뻤고 우리 마음에 참 소중했지만, 다른 숱한 물거품처럼 터져 버렸어요. 우리는 프레더릭 오빠가 그토록 행복하다는 데서 위안을 얻고, 서로에게 큰 사람이 되어 주는 데서 위안을 얻어야 해요."], ["So don’t offend me by talking of being able to spare me, papa, for I assure you you can’t.”", "그러니 저를 보내 줄 수 있다는 말씀으로 저를 서운하게 하지 마세요, 아버지. 정말이지 그러실 수 없으니까요.”"]], [["But the idea of a change took root and germinated in Margaret’s heart, although not in the way in which her father proposed it at first.", "그러나 변화라는 생각은 마거릿의 마음에 뿌리를 내리고 싹을 틔웠다. 처음에 아버지가 내놓았던 방식으로는 아니었지만 말이다."], ["She began to consider how desirable something of the kind would be to her father, whose spirits, always feeble, now became too frequently depressed, and whose health, though he never complained, had been seriously affected by his wife’s illness and death.", "그녀는 그런 종류의 무언가가 아버지에게 얼마나 필요할지 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 아버지의 기운은 늘 약했지만 이제는 너무 자주 가라앉았고, 건강도 결코 불평하는 법은 없었으나 아내의 병과 죽음으로 심하게 상해 있었다."], ["There were the regular hours of reading with his pupils, but that all giving and no receiving could no longer be called companionship, as in the old days when Mr.", "제자들과 정해진 시간에 책을 읽기는 했다. 그러나 주기만 하고 받는 것이 없는 그 일은, 손턴 씨가 그에게 배우러 오던 옛날처럼 벗함이라고 부를 수 없었다."], ["Thornton came to study under him.", "마거릿은 아버지 자신은 알지 못하는 결핍을 겪고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Margaret was conscious of the want under which he was suffering, unknown to himself; the want of a man’s intercourse with men.", "남자가 남자들과 나누는 교류의 결핍이었다. 헬스톤에서는 이웃 목사들과 오가며 왕래할 일이 끊이지 않았고, 들에서 일하는 가난한 일꾼들이나 저녁에 느릿느릿 집으로 걸어가는 이들이나 숲에서 소를 돌보는 이들에게는 언제든 말을 걸고 말을 받을 여유가 있었다."], ["At Helstone there had been perpetual occasion for an interchange of visits with neighbouring clergymen; and the poor labourers in the fields, or leisurely tramping home at eve, or tending their cattle in the forest, were always at liberty to speak or be spoken to.", "그러나 밀턴에서는 누구나 너무 바빠서 조용한 이야기도, 무르익은 생각의 오고 감도 없었다. 그들이 하는 말은 장사에 관한 것이었고 아주 눈앞의 실제적인 것이었다."], ["But in Milton every one was too busy for quiet speech, or any ripened intercourse of thought; what they said was about business, very present and actual; and when the tension of mind relating to their daily affairs was over, they sunk into fallow rest until next morning.", "그리고 하루하루의 일에 매인 긴장이 풀리고 나면 그들은 다음 날 아침까지 묵정밭 같은 휴식 속으로 가라앉았다. 하루 일이 끝난 뒤에는 노동자를 찾을 수 없었다."], ["The workman was not to be found after the day’s work was done; he had gone away to some lecture, or some club, or some beershop, according to his degree of character.", "그는 사람됨의 정도에 따라 어떤 강연으로, 어떤 모임으로, 어떤 선술집으로 가 버렸다. 헤일 씨는 몇몇 학회에서 연속 강연을 해 볼까 생각했다."], ["Mr. Hale thought of trying to deliver a course of lectures at some of the institutions, but he contemplated doing this so much as an effort of duty, and with so little of the genial impulse of love towards his work and its end, that Margaret was sure that it would not be well done until he could look upon it with some kind of zest.", "그러나 그것을 하려는 마음이 너무나 의무를 다하려는 노력에 가깝고, 그 일과 그 목적을 향한 다정한 사랑의 충동은 너무나 적었으므로, 마거릿은 아버지가 그 일을 어떤 흥으로 바라볼 수 있게 되기 전에는 잘해 내지 못하리라고 확신했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLI", "# 제41장"]], [["I see my way as birds their trackless way— I shall arrive!", "나는 새들이 길 없는 제 길을 알듯 내 길을 안다— 나는 다다르리라! 언제일지, 어느 굽이를 먼저 돌지, 나는 묻지 않는다."], ["what time, what circuit first, I ask not: but unless God sends his hail Or blinding fire-balls, sleet, or stifling snow, In some time—his good time—I shall arrive; He guides me and the bird.", "다만 하느님이 우박을, 눈멀게 하는 불덩이를, 진눈깨비를, 숨 막히는 눈을 내려보내지 않으신다면, 언젠가는—그분의 좋은 때에—나는 다다르리라. 그분이 나를 인도하시고 새를 인도하신다."], ["In His good time!", "그분의 좋은 때에!"], ["BROWNING’S PARACELSUS.", "— 브라우닝, 파라켈수스."]], [["So the winter was getting on, and the days were beginning to lengthen, without bringing with them any of the brightness of hope which usually accompanies the rays of a February sun. Mrs.", "그리하여 겨울은 깊어 가고 낮은 길어지기 시작했으나, 2월의 햇살에 으레 따라오는 희망의 밝음은 조금도 함께 오지 않았다."], ["Thornton had of course entirely ceased to come to the house.", "손턴 부인은 물론 이 집에 오는 일을 아주 그만두었다."], ["Mr. Thornton came occasionally, but his visits were addressed to her father, and were confined to the study.", "손턴 씨는 이따금 왔지만 그의 방문은 그녀의 아버지를 향한 것이었고 서재를 벗어나지 않았다."], ["Mr. Hale spoke of him as always the same; indeed, the very rarity of their intercourse seemed to make Mr.", "헤일 씨는 그가 늘 한결같다고 말했다."], ["Hale set only the higher value on it. And from what Margaret could gather of what Mr.", "실은 두 사람의 왕래가 드물어졌다는 바로 그 사실이 헤일 씨로 하여금 그 왕래를 한층 더 귀하게 여기게 만드는 듯했다."], ["Thornton had said, there was nothing in the cessation of his visits which could arise from any umbrage or vexation.", "그리고 손턴 씨가 한 말이라며 아버지에게서 전해 들을 수 있는 것으로 미루어, 그의 발길이 뜸해진 데에는 어떤 노여움이나 불쾌함에서 비롯된 구석이 조금도 없었다."], ["His business affairs had become complicated during the strike, and required closer attention than he had given to them last winter.", "파업 동안 그의 사업이 복잡하게 얽혀, 지난겨울에 기울이던 것보다 더 세심한 주의를 요하게 된 것이었다."], ["Nay, Margaret could even discover that he spoke from time to time of her, and always, as far as she could learn, in the same calm friendly way, never avoiding and never seeking any mention of her name.", "아니, 마거릿은 그가 이따금 자기 이야기를 한다는 것까지도 알아낼 수 있었다. 그리고 그녀가 알 수 있는 한 언제나 똑같이 차분하고 다정한 말투로, 그녀의 이름을 입에 올리기를 피하지도 않고 굳이 찾지도 않으면서였다."]], [["She was not in spirits to raise her father’s tone of mind.", "그녀는 아버지의 마음을 북돋울 만한 기운이 없었다."], ["The dreary peacefulness of the present time had been preceded by so long a period of anxiety and care—even intermixed with storms—that her mind had lost its elasticity.", "지금의 이 음울한 평온에 앞서, 때때로 폭풍까지 섞인 불안과 근심의 시기가 너무나 길게 이어졌던 터라 그녀의 마음은 탄력을 잃어버렸다. 그녀는 어린 바우처 아이 둘을 가르치는 일에서 소일거리를 찾으려 했고, 선하게 살고자 힘껏 애썼다."], ["She tried to find herself occupation in teaching the two younger Boucher children, and worked hard at goodness; hard, I say most truly, for her heart seemed dead to the end of all her efforts; and though she made them punctually and painfully, yet she stood as far off as ever from any cheerfulness; her life seemed still bleak and dreary.", "힘껏이라는 말을 나는 참으로 진실하게 쓴다. 그 모든 노력의 끝에서 그녀의 마음은 죽은 듯 무감각했기 때문이다."], ["The only thing she did well, was what she did out of unconscious piety, the silent comforting and consoling of her father.", "그리고 그녀가 그 노력을 어김없이, 또 고통스럽게 기울였음에도 그녀는 여전히 어떤 명랑함에서도 예전만큼이나 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그녀의 삶은 여전히 황량하고 음울해 보였다."], ["Not a mood of his but what found a ready sympathiser in Margaret; not a wish of his that she did not strive to forecast, and to fulfil.", "그녀가 잘해낸 단 한 가지는 스스로 의식하지 못한 경건함에서 나온 것, 곧 말없이 아버지를 위로하고 달래는 일이었다. 아버지의 어떤 기분도 마거릿에게서 선뜻 공감을 얻지 못하는 법이 없었고, 아버지의 어떤 바람도 그녀가 미리 헤아려 이루어 드리려 애쓰지 않는 법이 없었다."], ["They were quiet wishes to be sure, and hardly named without hesitation and apology.", "물론 그것은 조용한 바람들이었고, 망설임과 미안함 없이는 입에 담기지도 않는 것들이었다."], ["All the more complete and beautiful was her meek spirit of obedience.", "그럴수록 그녀의 온순한 순종의 마음은 더욱 온전하고 아름다웠다."], ["March brought the news of Frederick’s marriage.", "3월에 프레더릭의 결혼 소식이 왔다."], ["He and Dolores wrote; she in Spanish-English, as was but natural, and he with little turns and inversions of words which proved how far the idioms of his bride’s country were infecting him.", "그와 돌로레스가 나란히 편지를 썼다. 그녀는 당연하게도 스페인식 영어로 썼고, 그는 신부의 나라 말투가 그에게 얼마나 깊이 스며들고 있는지를 보여 주는 자잘한 어순과 표현의 뒤틀림을 섞어 썼다."]], [["On the receipt of Henry Lennox’s letter, announcing how little hope there was of his ever clearing himself at a court-martial, in the absence of the missing witnesses, Frederick had written to Margaret a pretty vehement letter, containing his renunciation of England as his country; he wished he could unnative himself, and declared that he would not take his pardon if it were offered him, nor live in the country if he had permission to do so.", "사라진 증인들이 없는 마당에 군법회의에서 스스로를 밝힐 가망이 얼마나 희박한지를 알리는 헨리 레녹스의 편지를 받고서, 프레더릭은 마거릿에게 꽤나 격렬한 편지를 써 보냈다. 영국을 제 조국으로 여기기를 그만두겠다는 선언이 담긴 편지였다."], ["All of which made Margaret cry sorely, so unnatural did it seem to her at the first opening; but on consideration, she saw rather in such expression the poignancy of the disappointment which had thus crushed his hopes; and she felt that there was nothing for it but patience.", "그는 자기가 그 나라 태생이 아니게 될 수 있기를 바랐고, 사면을 준다 해도 받지 않겠으며 허락을 받는다 해도 그 나라에서는 살지 않겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그 모든 것이 마거릿을 몹시 울게 했으니, 처음 펼쳐 보았을 때에는 그것이 그녀에게 너무나 인정에 어긋나 보였던 것이다."], ["In the next letter Frederick spoke so joyfully of the future that he had no thought for the past; and Margaret found a use in herself for the patience she had been craving for him.", "그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 표현에서 오히려 그의 희망을 이렇게 짓뭉개 버린 실망의 사무침이 보였다. 그리하여 그녀는 참는 수밖에 다른 길이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["She would have to be patient.", "다음 편지에서 프레더릭은 앞날에 대해 어찌나 기쁘게 이야기했던지 지난 일은 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["But the pretty, timid, girlish letters of Dolores were beginning to have a charm for both Margaret and her father.", "그리고 마거릿은 오빠를 위해 바라던 그 인내를 정작 자기 자신에게 쓸 곳이 있음을 알았다. 그녀야말로 참아야 할 터였다."], ["The young Spaniard was so evidently anxious to make a favourable impression upon her lover’s English relations, that her feminine care peeped out at every erasure; and the letters announcing the marriage, were accompanied by a splendid black lace mantilla, chosen by Dolores herself for her unseen sister-in-law, whom Frederick had represented as a paragon of beauty, wisdom and virtue.", "그러나 돌로레스의 어여쁘고 수줍은 소녀다운 편지들은 마거릿과 아버지 두 사람 모두에게 매력을 지니기 시작했다. 그 젊은 스페인 아가씨는 연인의 영국 친척들에게 좋은 인상을 주고 싶어 하는 마음이 너무나 뚜렷해서, 지워 고쳐 쓴 자리마다 여자다운 조심스러움이 내비쳤다."], ["Frederick’s worldly position was raised by this marriage on to as high a level as they could desire. Barbour and Co.", "그리고 결혼을 알리는 편지에는 한 번도 본 적 없는 시누이를 위해 돌로레스가 손수 고른 훌륭한 검은 레이스 만틸라가 함께 왔다."], ["was one of the most extensive Spanish houses, and into it he was received as a junior partner.", "프레더릭이 그 시누이를 아름다움과 지혜와 덕의 본보기라고 일러 준 터였다. 이 결혼으로 프레더릭의 세속적인 처지는 그들이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 높은 자리로 올라섰다."], ["Margaret smiled a little and then sighed as she remembered afresh her old tirades against trade.", "바버 상회는 스페인에서 가장 큰 상사 가운데 하나였고, 그는 그곳에 젊은 동업자로 받아들여졌다."], ["Here was her preux chevalier of a brother turned merchant, trader!", "마거릿은 장사를 두고 예전에 늘어놓던 열변이 새삼 떠올라 조금 웃었다가 이내 한숨을 지었다."], ["But then she rebelled against herself, and protested silently against the confusion implied between a Spanish merchant and a Milton millowner.", "그녀의 늠름한 기사였던 오빠가 상인이, 장사꾼이 되다니! 그러나 곧 그녀는 자기 자신에게 반기를 들고, 스페인 상인과 밀턴 공장주를 한데 뒤섞는 그 생각에 소리 없이 항의했다."], ["Well, trade or no trade, Frederick was very, very happy.", "그래, 장사면 어떻고 아니면 어떤가, 프레더릭은 아주아주 행복했다."], ["Dolores must be charming, and the mantilla was exquisite!", "돌로레스는 분명 사랑스러운 사람일 테고, 만틸라는 더없이 아름다웠다!"], ["And then she returned to the present life.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 지금의 삶으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Her father had occasionally experienced a difficulty in breathing this spring, which had for the time distressed him exceedingly.", "그해 봄 아버지는 이따금 숨쉬기가 힘든 증세를 겪었고, 그럴 때마다 몹시 괴로워했다. 그 증세가 사이사이 말끔히 가시곤 했으므로 마거릿은 덜 놀랐다."], ["Margaret was less alarmed, as this difficulty went off completely in the intervals; but she still was so desirous of his shaking off the liability altogether, as to make her very urgent that he should accept Mr. Bell’s invitation to visit him at Oxford this April.", "그러나 아버지가 그 병기를 아예 떨쳐 버리기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 그녀는 이번 4월에 옥스퍼드로 와 달라는 벨 씨의 초대를 받아들이시라고 몹시 채근했다."], ["Mr. Bell’s invitation included Margaret.", "벨 씨의 초대에는 마거릿도 들어 있었다."], ["Nay more, he wrote a special letter commanding her to come; but she felt as if it would be a greater relief to her to remain quietly at home, entirely free from any responsibility whatever, and so to rest her mind and heart in a manner which she had not been able to do for more than two years past.", "아니, 그뿐 아니라 그는 그녀에게 꼭 오라고 명하는 특별한 편지까지 따로 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 아무 책임도 지지 않은 채 조용히 집에 남아, 지난 2년 남짓 도무지 할 수 없었던 방식으로 마음과 가슴을 쉬게 하는 편이 자기에게는 더 큰 안식이 되리라고 느꼈다."]], [["When her father had driven off on his way to the railroad, Margaret felt how great and long had been the pressure on her time and her spirits.", "아버지가 철도역으로 가는 마차를 타고 떠나자, 마거릿은 자기의 시간과 정신에 얼마나 크고 오랜 압박이 있었는지를 실감했다. 스스로가 그토록 자유롭다고 느끼는 것은 놀랍다 못해 얼떨떨할 지경이었다."], ["It was astonishing, almost stunning, to feel herself so much at liberty; no one depending on her for cheering care, if not for positive happiness; no invalid to plan and think for; she might be idle, and silent and forgetful,—and what seemed worth more than all the other privileges—she might be unhappy if she liked.", "뚜렷한 행복까지는 아니더라도 기운을 북돋우는 보살핌을 그녀에게 기대는 사람이 아무도 없었다. 계획을 세우고 생각을 기울여야 할 환자도 없었다."], ["For months past, all her own personal cares and troubles had had to be stuffed away into a dark cupboard; but now she had leisure to take them out, and mourn over them, and study their nature, and seek the true method of subduing them into the elements of peace.", "그녀는 게을러도 되고 말이 없어도 되고 잊어버려도 되었다. 그리고 다른 모든 특권보다 더 값져 보이는 것, 곧 마음 내키면 불행해해도 되었다."], ["All these weeks she had been conscious of their existence in a dull kind of way, though they were hidden out of sight.", "지난 몇 달 동안 그녀 자신의 온갖 근심과 괴로움은 어두운 벽장 속에 밀어 넣어 두어야 했다. 그러나 이제 그녀에게는 그것들을 꺼내 놓고 슬퍼하고, 그 본성을 살피고, 그것들을 평화의 요소로 다스릴 참된 방법을 찾을 여유가 있었다."], ["Now, once for all she would consider them and appoint to each of them its right work in her life.", "그 몇 주 내내 그녀는 그것들이 눈에 보이지 않게 감춰져 있었음에도 그 존재를 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었다."], ["So she sat almost motionless for hours in the drawing-room, going over the bitterness of every remembrance with an unwincing resolution.", "이제 단번에 아주, 그녀는 그것들을 하나하나 살펴 자기 삶에서 저마다 마땅한 자리를 정해 줄 참이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 응접실에서 몇 시간이고 거의 꼼짝도 하지 않고 앉아, 움츠러들지 않는 결의로 기억마다의 쓰라림을 하나하나 되짚어 나갔다."], ["Only once she cried aloud, at the stinging thought of the faithlessness which gave birth to that abasing falsehood.", "다만 한 번, 그 굴욕적인 거짓말을 낳은 믿음 없음을 떠올리며 찌르는 듯한 아픔에 소리 내어 울었다."]], [["She now would not even acknowledge the force of the temptation; her plans for Frederick had all failed, and the temptation lay there a dead mockery,—a mockery which had never had life in it; the lie had been so despicably foolish, seen by the light of the ensuing events, and faith in the power of truth so infinitely the greater wisdom!", "그녀는 이제 그 유혹의 힘조차 인정하려 들지 않았다. 프레더릭을 위한 계획은 모두 어그러졌고, 유혹은 죽은 조롱으로, 애초에 생명이라고는 없었던 조롱으로 거기 놓여 있었다. 뒤이어 벌어진 일들에 비추어 보면 그 거짓말은 그토록 비열하고 어리석었으며, 진실의 힘에 대한 믿음이야말로 비할 데 없이 더 큰 지혜였던 것이다!"]], [["In her nervous agitation, she unconsciously opened a book of her father’s that lay upon the table,—the words that caught her eye in it, seemed almost made for her present state of acute self-abasement:—", "신경이 곤두선 채로 그녀는 무심코 탁자 위에 놓인 아버지의 책 한 권을 펼쳤다. 그 안에서 눈에 들어온 구절은 사무치게 스스로를 낮추고 있는 지금의 그녀를 위해 마련된 것만 같았다."]], [["“Je ne voudrois pas reprendre mon cœur en ceste sorte: meurs de honte, aveugle, impudent, traistre et desloyal à ton Dieu, et sembables choses; mais je voudrois le corriger par voye de compassion.", "“나는 내 마음을 이런 식으로 나무라고 싶지는 않다. 부끄러워 죽어라, 눈먼 것아, 뻔뻔한 것아, 네 하느님을 배반하고 저버린 것아, 하는 식으로는."], ["Or sus, mon pauvre cœur, nous voilà tombe dans la fosse, laquelle nous avions tant resolu d’eschapper.", "나는 오히려 연민의 길로 그것을 고치고 싶다. 자, 나의 가엾은 마음아, 우리가 그토록 벗어나기로 굳게 마음먹었던 그 구덩이에 우리가 이렇게 빠지고 말았구나."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["revelons-nous, et quittons-la pour jamais, reclamons la misericorde de Dieu, et esperons en elle qu’elle nous assistera pour desormais estra plus fermes; et remettons-nous au chemin de l’humilitè.", "다시 일어나자, 그리고 그곳을 영영 떠나자. 하느님의 자비를 구하고, 그 자비가 이제부터 우리를 도와 더 굳세게 서게 하시리라 바라자."], ["Courage, soyons meshuy sur nos gardes, Dieu nous aydera.”", "그리고 겸손의 길로 다시 돌아서자. 용기를 내자, 이제부터 우리 자신을 살피자, 하느님께서 우리를 도우시리라.”"]], [["“The way of humility.", "“겸손의 길."], ["Ah,”", "아,”"], ["thought Margaret, “that is what I have missed!", "마거릿은 생각했다. “그것이 바로 내가 놓쳐 버린 것이구나!"], ["But courage, little heart.", "그러나 용기를 내렴, 작은 마음아."], ["We will turn back, and by God’s help we may find the lost path.”", "우리는 되돌아설 것이고, 하느님의 도우심으로 잃어버린 길을 찾을 수 있으리라.”"]], [["So she rose up, and determined at once to set to on some work which should take her out of herself.", "그리하여 그녀는 일어나, 자기 자신에게서 빠져나오게 해 줄 어떤 일에 당장 착수하기로 마음먹었다. 우선 그녀는 마사가 위층으로 올라가느라 응접실 문 앞을 지나갈 때 그녀를 불러 세워, 거의 기계적인 순종으로 제 개성을 딱딱하게 덮어 싸고 있는 그 진지하고 공손한 하인다운 태도 아래에 무엇이 있는지 알아보려 했다."], ["To begin with, she called in Martha, as she passed the drawing-room door in going up-stairs, and tried to find out what was below the grave, respectful, servant-like manner, which crusted over her individual character with an obedience which was almost mechanical.", "마사에게 자기 자신에 관한 이야기를 하게 만들기는 어려웠다. 그러나 마침내 손턴 부인의 이름을 대자 옳은 줄이 울렸다."], ["She found it difficult to induce Martha to speak of any of her personal interests; but at last she touched the right chord, in naming Mrs. Thornton.", "마사의 얼굴이 온통 환해졌고, 조금 부추기자 긴 이야기가 흘러나왔다."], ["Martha’s whole face brightened, and, on a little encouragement, out came a long story, of how her father had been in early life connected with Mrs.", "자기 아버지가 젊은 시절 손턴 부인의 남편과 인연이 있었고, 아니 그에게 어떤 친절을 베풀 만한 처지이기까지 했다는 것이었다. 무슨 친절이었는지는 마사도 잘 알지 못했다."], ["Thornton’s husband—nay, had even been in a position to show him some kindness; what, Martha hardly knew, for it had happened when she was quite a little child; and circumstances had intervened to separate the two families until Martha was nearly grown up, when, her father having sunk lower and lower from his original occupation as clerk in a warehouse, and her mother being dead, she and her sister, to use Martha’s own expression, would have been “lost”", "자기가 아주 어린아이였을 때 있었던 일이기 때문이었다. 그리고 이런저런 사정이 끼어들어 두 집안은 마사가 거의 다 자랄 때까지 서로 떨어져 지냈는데, 그 무렵 창고 서기라는 본래의 일자리에서 아버지가 점점 더 아래로 가라앉고 어머니마저 세상을 떠나, 마사 자신의 표현을 빌리자면 그녀와 언니는 손턴 부인이 아니었더라면 “못쓰게 되었을”"], ["but for Mrs.", "것이라고 했다."], ["Thornton; who sought them out, and thought for them, and cared for them.", "손턴 부인이 그들을 찾아내어 그들을 위해 생각해 주고 그들을 돌보아 주었던 것이다."]], [["“I had had the fever, and was but delicate; and Mrs.", "“저는 열병을 앓고 나서 몸이 약했습니다."], ["Thornton and Mr. Thornton too, they never rested till they had nursed me up in their own house, and sent me to the sea and all.", "그런데 손턴 부인이며 손턴 씨며, 저를 당신들 집에서 간호해 몸을 추스르게 하고 바닷가까지 보내 주시고 하기 전에는 도무지 쉬지를 않으셨어요."], ["The doctor said the fever was catching, but they cared none for that—only Miss Fanny, and she went a-visiting these folk that she is going to marry into.", "의사는 그 열병이 옮는다고 했지만 그분들은 그런 건 아랑곳도 않으셨지요. 다만 패니 아가씨만은 달라서, 이번에 시집가실 그 댁에 가 계셨답니다."], ["So though she was afraid at the time, it has all ended well.”", "그러니 그때는 겁을 내셨어도 결국은 다 잘된 셈이에요.”"]], [["“Miss Fanny going to be married!”", "“패니 아가씨가 결혼을 하신다고요!”"], ["exclaimed Margaret.", "마거릿이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes; and to a rich gentleman, too, only he’s a deal older than she is.", "“네, 그것도 부자 신사분한테요. 다만 아가씨보다 나이가 한참 위지요."], ["His name is Watson; and his mills are somewhere out beyond Hayleigh; it’s a very good marriage, for all he’s got such gray hair.”", "왓슨 씨라고 하는데, 공장은 헤일리 너머 어디쯤에 있답니다. 머리가 그렇게 희끗희끗해도 아주 좋은 혼처예요.”"]], [["At this piece of information, Margaret was silent long enough for Martha to recover her propriety, and, with it, her habitual shortness of answer.", "이 소식에 마거릿은 한참 말이 없었고, 그동안 마사는 다시 제 몸가짐을 되찾았으며 그와 함께 늘 하던 대로 짧게 대답하는 버릇도 되찾았다."], ["She swept up the hearth, asked at what time she should prepare tea, and quitted the room with the same wooden face with which she had entered it.", "마사는 난롯가를 쓸고, 차를 몇 시에 준비할지 물은 뒤, 들어올 때와 똑같이 나무토막 같은 얼굴로 방을 나갔다."], ["Margaret had to pull herself up from indulging a bad trick, which she had lately fallen into, of trying to imagine how every event that she heard of in relation to Mr. Thornton would affect him: whether he would like it or dislike it.", "마거릿은 요즘 자기가 빠져든 나쁜 버릇, 곧 손턴 씨와 관련해 듣게 되는 일마다 그것이 그에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지, 그가 그것을 좋아할지 싫어할지 그려 보려는 버릇에 젖어 들지 않으려고 스스로를 다잡아야 했다."]], [["The next day she had the little Boucher children for their lessons, and took a long walk, and ended by a visit to Mary Higgins.", "이튿날 그녀는 어린 바우처 아이들을 데리고 공부를 시켰고, 오래 걸었으며, 마지막으로 메리 히긴스를 찾아갔다."], ["Somewhat to Margaret’s surprise, she found Nicholas already come home from his work; the lengthening light had deceived her as to the lateness of the evening.", "마거릿은 니컬러스가 벌써 일을 마치고 집에 와 있는 것을 보고 조금 놀랐다. 길어진 해가 저녁이 얼마나 늦었는지를 그녀에게 잘못 알려 준 것이었다."], ["He too seemed, by his manners, to have entered a little more on the way of humility; he was quieter, and less self-asserting.", "그 역시 태도로 보아 겸손의 길에 조금은 들어선 듯했다. 그는 전보다 조용했고 제 주장을 덜 내세웠다."]], [["“So th’ oud gentleman’s away on his travels, is he?”", "“그래, 그 노신사 어른이 여행을 떠나셨다지요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Little ’uns telled me so.", "“꼬맹이들이 나한테 그럽디다."], ["Eh!", "어유!"], ["but the’re sharp ’uns they are; I a’most think they beat my own wenches for sharpness, though m’appen it’s wrong to say so, and one on ’em in her grave.", "그것들 어찌나 야무진지, 우리 집 계집애들보다 더 똑똑한 것 같다는 생각까지 든다니까. 그런 말 하면 못쓰는 건지도 모르고, 게다가 하나는 무덤에 누워 있으니 더 그렇소만."], ["There’s summut in th’ weather, I reckon, as sets folk a-wandering.", "날씨에 뭔가가 있나 보오, 사람을 자꾸 떠돌게 만드는 뭔가가."], ["My measter, him at th’ shop yonder, is spinning about th’ world somewhere.”", "우리 공장주 양반도, 저기 공장에 있는 그 사람 말이오, 지금 세상 어디께를 빙빙 돌아다니고 있다지 뭐요.”"]], [["“Is that the reason you’re so soon at home to-night?”", "“그래서 오늘 밤 이렇게 일찍 집에 계신 거예요?”"], ["asked Margaret innocently.", "마거릿이 아무 뜻 없이 물었다."]], [["“Thou know’st nought about it, that’s all,”", "“댁은 그 일을 하나도 모르니 하는 소리요.”"], ["said he, contemptuously.", "그가 업신여기듯 말했다."], ["“I’m not one wi’ two faces—one for my measter, and t’other for his back.", "“나는 얼굴이 둘인 사람이 아니외다. 공장주 앞에서 쓰는 얼굴 하나, 그 등 뒤에서 쓰는 얼굴 하나, 그런 사람 말이오."], ["I counted a’ th’ clocks in the town striking afore I’d leave my work.", "나는 일을 놓기 전에 온 동네 시계가 치는 소리를 다 세었소. 아니지!"], ["No! yon Thornton’s good enough for to fight wi’, but too good for to be cheated.", "저 손턴이라는 사람은 맞붙어 싸울 만은 해도 속여먹기에는 너무 아까운 사람이오."], ["It were you as getten me the place, and I thank yo’ for it.", "그 자리를 나한테 얻어 준 게 댁이었으니, 그건 고맙게 여기고 있소."], ["Thornton’s is not a bad mill, as times go.", "손턴네 공장은 요즘 세상에 나쁜 공장은 아니지."], ["Stand down, lad, and say yo’r pretty hymn to Miss Marget.", "내려서라, 얘야, 그리고 마거릿 아가씨한테 그 고운 찬송을 읊어 드려라."], ["That’s right; steady on thy legs, and right arm out as straight as a skewer.", "옳지. 다리에 힘 딱 주고, 오른팔은 꼬챙이처럼 쭉 뻗고."], ["One to stop, two to stay, three mak’ ready, and four away!”", "하나 서고, 둘 멈추고, 셋 준비, 넷 출발!”"]], [["The little fellow repeated a Methodist hymn, far above his comprehension in point of language, but of which the swinging rhythm had caught his ear, and which he repeated with all the developed cadence of a member of parliament.", "그 어린 녀석은 말뜻으로 보자면 제 이해를 훌쩍 넘어서는 감리교 찬송가 한 편을 읊었다. 그러나 그 흔들리는 가락이 아이의 귀에 붙어 있었고, 아이는 국회의원이나 됨 직한 완연한 억양으로 그것을 읊어 냈다."], ["When Margaret had duly applauded, Nicholas called for another, and yet another, much to her surprise, as she found him thus oddly and unconsciously led to take an interest in the sacred things which he had formerly scouted.", "마거릿이 마땅히 손뼉을 쳐 주자 니컬러스는 또 하나를, 다시 또 하나를 청했다. 그가 예전에는 비웃던 거룩한 것들에 이렇게 묘하게, 저도 모르게 마음을 두게 된 것을 알고 그녀는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["It was past the usual tea-time when she reached home; but she had the comfort of feeling that no one had been kept waiting for her; and of thinking her own thoughts while she rested, instead of anxiously watching another person to learn whether to be grave or gay.", "그녀가 집에 닿았을 때는 여느 때의 차 시간이 지나 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게는 아무도 자기를 기다리느라 붙들려 있지 않았다는 위안이 있었고, 다른 사람을 애태우며 살펴서 심각해야 할지 명랑해야 할지를 알아내는 대신 쉬면서 자기 생각을 할 수 있다는 위안이 있었다."], ["After tea she resolved to examine a large packet of letters, and pick out those that were to be destroyed.", "차를 마시고 나서 그녀는 큼직한 편지 꾸러미를 살펴 없애 버릴 것들을 골라내기로 했다."]], [["Among them she came to four or five of Mr.", "그 가운데서 그녀는 프레더릭의 일에 관한 헨리 레녹스 씨의 편지 네댓 통을 찾아냈다."], ["Henry Lennox’s, relating to Frederick’s affairs; and she carefully read them over again, with the sole intention, when she began, to ascertain exactly on how fine a chance the justification of her brother hung.", "그리고 처음 시작할 때에는 오로지 오빠의 무죄를 밝힐 가망이 얼마나 가느다란 실낱에 걸려 있는지를 정확히 알아보려는 뜻으로 그것들을 꼼꼼히 다시 읽었다. 그러나 마지막 장을 다 읽고 이로운 점과 불리한 점을 저울질하고 나자, 그 편지들에 담긴 자그마한 인품의 드러남이 저절로 눈에 들어왔다."], ["But when she had finished the last, and weighed the pros and cons, the little personal revelation of character contained in them forced itself on her notice.", "문면의 뻣뻣함으로 보아, 레녹스 씨가 그 편지들의 사안에 어떤 관심을 느끼든 자기와 그녀 사이의 관계를 한 번도 잊은 적이 없다는 것이 충분히 뚜렷했다."], ["It was evident enough, from the stiffness of the wording, that Mr.", "영리한 편지들이었다. 마거릿은 그것을 대번에 알아보았다."], ["Lennox had never forgotten his relation to her in any interest he might feel in the subject of the correspondence.", "그러나 그 편지들에는 진심 어린 따뜻한 기운이 없었다. 그래도 값진 것이니 간직해야 했다."], ["They were clever letters; Margaret saw that in a twinkling; but she missed out of them all hearty and genial atmosphere.", "그래서 그녀는 그것들을 조심스레 한쪽에 놓아두었다. 이 자잘한 일이 끝나자 그녀는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["They were to be preserved, however, as valuable; so she laid them carefully on one side.", "그리고 그날 밤 마거릿의 머릿속에는 멀리 있는 아버지 생각이 이상하리만치 맴돌았다."], ["When this little piece of business was ended, she fell into a reverie; and the thought of her absent father ran strangely in Margaret’s head that night.", "그녀는 자기의 혼자 있음을, 따라서 아버지의 부재를 안식으로 느꼈던 것을 스스로 나무랄 뻔했다."], ["She almost blamed herself for having felt her solitude (and consequently his absence) as a relief; but these two days had set her up afresh, with new strength and brighter hope.", "그러나 이 이틀이 그녀를 새로운 힘과 더 밝은 희망으로 다시 일으켜 세웠다."], ["Plans which had lately appeared to her in the guise of tasks, now appeared like pleasures.", "얼마 전까지 짐처럼 보이던 계획들이 이제는 즐거움처럼 보였다."], ["The morbid scales had fallen from her eyes, and she saw her position and her work more truly. If only Mr.", "병든 비늘이 그녀의 눈에서 떨어져 나가, 그녀는 제 처지와 제 할 일을 더 참되게 보았다."], ["Thornton would restore her the lost friendship,—nay, if he would only come from time to time to cheer her father as in former days,—though she should never see him, she felt as if the course of her future life, though not brilliant in prospect, might lie clear and even before her.", "손턴 씨가 잃어버린 우정을 그녀에게 되돌려 주기만 한다면, 아니 그가 예전처럼 이따금 찾아와 아버지의 마음을 밝혀 주기만 한다면, 설령 그녀가 그를 다시는 보지 못한다 해도 앞날의 삶은 눈부시지는 않을지언정 그녀 앞에 맑고 고르게 놓일 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["She sighed as she rose up to go to bed.", "그녀는 잠자리에 들려고 일어서며 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["In spite of the “One step’s enough for me,”—in spite of the one plain duty of devotion to her father,—there lay at her heart an anxiety and a pang of sorrow.", "“한 걸음이면 내게는 충분하다”는 말에도 불구하고, 아버지에게 헌신한다는 그 하나의 분명한 본분에도 불구하고, 그녀의 가슴속에는 불안과 슬픔의 아픔이 놓여 있었다."]], [["And Mr. Hale thought of Margaret, that April evening, just as strangely and as persistently as she was thinking of him.", "그리고 그 4월 저녁, 헤일 씨도 마거릿이 자기를 생각하는 것만큼이나 이상하고 끈질기게 마거릿을 생각하고 있었다."], ["He had been fatigued by going about among his old friends and old familiar places.", "그는 옛 친구들과 옛 낯익은 자리들을 두루 찾아다니느라 지쳐 있었다. 그는 자기 견해가 달라진 것이 친구들이 자기를 맞이하는 태도에 어떤 변화를 가져올지를 지나치게 부풀려 생각해 왔다."], ["He had had exaggerated ideas of the change which his altered opinions might make in his friends’ reception of him; but although some of them might have felt shocked or grieved, or indignant at his falling off in the abstract, as soon as they saw the face of the man whom they had once loved, they forgot his opinions in himself; or only remembered them enough to give an additional tender gravity to their manner.", "그러나 그중 몇몇은 그가 신앙에서 떨어져 나갔다는 사실 자체에 충격을 받거나 마음 아파하거나 분개했을지 몰라도, 한때 사랑했던 사람의 얼굴을 보는 순간 그의 견해를 잊고 그 사람만을 보았다. 아니면 그 견해를 기억하더라도 다만 태도에 한층 더 부드러운 무게를 더할 만큼만 기억했다."], ["For Mr.", "헤일 씨를 아는 사람은 많지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Hale had not been known to many; he had belonged to one of the smaller colleges, and had always been shy and reserved; but those who in youth had cared to penetrate to the delicacy of thought and feeling that lay below his silence and indecision, took him to their hearts, with something of the protecting kindness which they would have shown to a woman.", "그는 작은 칼리지 가운데 하나에 속해 있었고 늘 수줍고 말이 없었다. 그러나 젊은 시절 그의 침묵과 우유부단함 아래에 놓인 섬세한 생각과 감정에까지 파고들 마음을 냈던 이들은 여자에게나 보였을 법한 감싸는 다정함으로 그를 가슴에 품었다."], ["And the renewal of this kindliness, after the lapse of years, and an interval of so much change, overpowered him more than any roughness or expression of disapproval could have done.", "그리고 여러 해가 흐르고 그토록 많은 변화의 세월이 사이에 놓인 뒤 이 다정함이 되살아난 것은, 어떤 거친 말이나 못마땅한 표현보다도 그를 더욱 무너뜨렸다."]], [["“I’m afraid we’ve done too much,”", "“우리가 너무 무리를 한 모양이네.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“You’re suffering now from having lived so long in that Milton air.”", "“자네가 그 밀턴 공기 속에서 그리 오래 산 탓에 지금 고생을 하는 걸세.”"]], [["“I am tired,”", "“피곤하긴 하네.”"], ["said Mr. Hale.", "헤일 씨가 말했다."], ["“But it is not Milton air.", "“하지만 밀턴 공기 탓은 아닐세."], ["I’m fifty-five years of age, and that little fact of itself accounts for any loss of strength.”", "나는 55세야. 그 사소한 사실 하나만으로도 기운이 줄어든 것쯤은 다 설명이 되지.”"]], [["“Nonsense!", "“말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["I’m upwards of sixty, and feel no loss of strength, either bodily or mental.", "나는 60이 넘었어도 몸이든 마음이든 기운이 줄어든 것을 느끼지 못하네."], ["Don’t let me hear you talking so.", "자네 입에서 그런 소리가 나오게 하지 말게."], ["Fifty-five!", "55라니!"], ["why, you’re quite a young man.”", "아니, 자네는 아주 젊은 사람일세.”"]], [["Mr. Hale shook his head.", "헤일 씨는 고개를 저었다."], ["“These last few years!”", "“지난 몇 해가 말일세!”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["But after a minute’s pause, he raised himself from his half recumbent position, in one of Mr. Bell’s luxurious easy-chairs, and said with a kind of trembling earnestness:", "그러나 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그는 벨 씨의 더없이 안락한 안락의자 하나에 반쯤 누워 있던 몸을 일으켜 세우고 어딘가 떨리는 간절함을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Bell!", "“벨!"], ["you’re not to think, that if I could have foreseen all that would come of my change of opinion, and my resignation of my living—no!", "자네는 이렇게 생각해서는 안 되네."], ["not even if I could have known how she would have suffered,—that I would undo it—the act of open acknowledgment that I no longer held the same faith as the church in which I was a priest.", "내 견해가 바뀌고 성직록을 내놓은 데서 비롯될 모든 일을 내가 미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면, 아니, 그 사람이 얼마나 고통을 겪게 될지 알 수 있었더라 해도, 내가 그 일을, 곧 내가 사제로 있던 교회와 더는 같은 믿음을 지니지 않았음을 드러내어 밝힌 그 행위를 되돌리겠다고는 생각지 말게."], ["As I think now, even if I could have foreseen that cruellest martyrdom of suffering, through the sufferings of one whom I loved, I would have done just the same as far as that step of openly leaving the church went.", "지금 생각해도, 내가 사랑하는 사람의 고통을 통해 겪게 될 그 더없이 잔인한 순교의 아픔을 미리 내다볼 수 있었다 해도, 교회를 드러내 놓고 떠나는 그 한 걸음에 관한 한 나는 똑같이 했을 걸세."], ["I might have done differently, and acted more wisely, in all that I subsequently did for my family.", "그 뒤로 가족을 위해 한 모든 일에서는 다르게, 더 지혜롭게 할 수도 있었겠지."], ["But I don’t think God endued me with over-much wisdom or strength,”", "하지만 하느님이 내게 넘칠 만큼의 지혜나 힘을 주셨다고는 생각지 않네.”"], ["he added, falling back into his old position.", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이며 아까의 자세로 도로 몸을 뉘었다."]], [["Mr. Bell blew his nose ostentatiously before answering.", "벨 씨는 대답하기 전에 요란하게 코를 풀었다."], ["Then he said:", "그러고서 말했다."]], [["“He gave you strength to do what your conscience told you was right; and I don’t see that we need any higher or holier strength than that; or wisdom either.", "“하느님은 자네 양심이 옳다고 이르는 바를 행할 힘을 자네에게 주셨네. 그리고 나는 우리에게 그보다 더 높거나 더 거룩한 힘이 필요하다고는 보지 않아."], ["I know I have not that much; and yet men set me down in their fool’s books as a wise man; an independent character; strong-minded, and all that cant.", "지혜도 마찬가지고. 나에게 그만한 것이 없다는 건 내가 알지."], ["The veriest idiot who obeys his own simple law of right, if it be but in wiping his shoes on a door-mat, is wiser and stronger than I.", "그런데도 사람들은 저희 바보 장부에 나를 지혜로운 사람이라고, 줏대 있는 인물이라고, 심지가 굳다느니 하는 그 온갖 허튼소리로 적어 놓는단 말일세. 제 앞의 깔개에 신발을 문질러 닦는 일에 지나지 않을망정 제 나름의 단순한 옳음의 법을 지키는 얼간이가 나보다 더 지혜롭고 더 강하네."], ["But what gulls men are!”", "사람이란 어찌 이리도 잘 속아 넘어가는지!”"]], [["There was a pause. Mr.", "잠시 말이 끊겼다."], ["Hale spoke first, in continuation of his thought:", "헤일 씨가 제 생각을 이어 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“About Margaret.”", "“마거릿 말인데.”"]], [["“Well!", "“그래!"], ["about Margaret.", "마거릿 말이지."], ["What then?”", "그래서?”"]], [["“If I die——”", "“내가 죽으면……”"]], [["“Nonsense!”", "“말도 안 되는 소리!”"]], [["“What will become of her—I often think?", "“그 애가 어떻게 될까, 나는 자주 생각한다네."], ["I suppose the Lennoxes will ask her to live with them.", "레녹스네가 함께 살자고 청하겠지."], ["I try to think they will.", "그러리라고 생각하려 애쓰네."], ["Her Aunt Shaw loved her very well in her own quiet way; but she forgets to love the absent.”", "쇼 처형은 그 나름의 조용한 방식으로 그 애를 무척 사랑했지. 하지만 눈앞에 없는 사람을 사랑하는 일은 잊어버리는 사람이야.”"]], [["“A very common fault.", "“아주 흔한 허물이지."], ["What sort of people are the Lennoxes?”", "레녹스네는 어떤 사람들인가?”"]], [["“He, handsome, fluent, and agreeable.", "“그 사람은 잘생기고 말주변 좋고 붙임성이 있네."], ["Edith, a sweet spoiled beauty.", "이디스는 곱게 자란 어여쁜 미인이고."], ["Margaret loves her with all her heart, and Edith with as much of her heart as she can spare.”", "마거릿은 온 마음을 다해 이디스를 사랑하고, 이디스는 남는 만큼의 마음으로 마거릿을 사랑하지.”"]], [["“Now, Hale; you know that girl of yours has got pretty nearly all my heart.", "“여보게, 헤일. 자네도 알다시피 자네 그 딸아이가 내 마음을 거의 다 가져갔네."], ["I told you that before.", "전에도 자네한테 말했지."], ["Of course, as your daughter, as my god-daughter, I took great interest in her before I saw her the last time.", "물론 자네 딸이자 내 대녀이니, 지난번에 만나기 전부터도 나는 그 애에게 큰 관심이 있었네."], ["But this visit that I paid to you at Milton made me her slave.", "하지만 밀턴으로 자네를 찾아갔던 그 방문이 나를 그 애의 종으로 만들어 버렸어."], ["I went, a willing old victim, following the car of the conqueror.", "나는 기꺼운 늙은 제물이 되어 정복자의 수레를 따라갔지."], ["For, indeed, she looks as grand and serene as one who has struggled, and may be struggling, and yet has the victory secure in sight.", "정말이지 그 애는 싸워 왔고 아마 지금도 싸우고 있으면서도 승리를 눈앞에 확실히 두고 있는 사람처럼 당당하고 고요해 보이거든."], ["Yes, in spite of all her present anxieties, that was the look on her face.", "그래, 지금 그 애가 안고 있는 온갖 근심에도 불구하고 얼굴에 어린 것은 바로 그런 표정이었네."], ["And so, all I have is at her service, if she needs it; and will be hers, whether she will or no, when I die.", "그러니 내가 가진 모든 것은 그 애가 필요로 한다면 그 애를 위한 것이고, 그 애가 원하든 원치 않든 내가 죽으면 그 애 것이 될 걸세."], ["Moreover, I myself, will be her preux chevalier, sixty and gouty though I be.", "게다가 나 자신이 그 애의 늠름한 기사가 되겠네, 60에 통풍까지 앓는 몸일망정."], ["Seriously, old friend, your daughter shall be my principal charge in life, and all the help that either my wit or my wisdom or my willing heart can give, shall be hers.", "진심일세, 오랜 벗이여, 자네 딸은 내 남은 생의 으뜸가는 소임이 될 것이고, 내 재치든 내 지혜든 내 기꺼운 마음이든 줄 수 있는 모든 도움은 그 애의 것이 될 걸세."], ["I don’t choose her out as a subject for fretting.", "나는 그 애를 자네가 애태울 거리로 골라 주려는 게 아니야."], ["Something, I know of old, you must have to worry yourself about, or you wouldn’t be happy.", "자네에게는 무언가 걱정거리가 있어야만 한다는 걸 내가 예전부터 알지. 그렇지 않으면 행복하지가 않으니까."], ["But you’re going to outlive me by many a long year.", "하지만 자네는 나보다 여러 해를 더 살 걸세."], ["You, spare, thin men are always tempting and always cheating Death!", "자네처럼 마르고 여윈 사람들은 늘 죽음을 꾀어 놓고는 늘 속여 넘기지!"], ["It’s the stout, florid fellows like me, that always go off first.”", "먼저 가는 건 언제나 나같이 살집 좋고 혈색 좋은 친구들이야.”"]], [["If Mr. Bell had had a prophetic eye he might have seen the torch all but inverted, and the angel with the grave and composed face standing very nigh, beckoning to his friend.", "벨 씨에게 앞을 내다보는 눈이 있었더라면, 그는 횃불이 이미 거의 거꾸로 뒤집힌 것을, 그리고 진지하고 고요한 얼굴의 천사가 아주 가까이 서서 제 벗을 손짓해 부르는 것을 보았을 것이다."], ["That night Mr. Hale laid his head down upon the pillow on which it never more should stir with life.", "그날 밤 헤일 씨는 다시는 살아서 들지 못할 베개 위에 머리를 뉘었다."], ["The servant who entered his room in the morning, received no answer to his speech; drew near the bed, and saw the calm, beautiful face lying white and cold under the ineffaceable seal of death.", "아침에 그의 방으로 들어간 하인은 제 인사에 아무 대답도 듣지 못하자 침대 가까이 다가갔고, 지울 수 없는 죽음의 봉인 아래 희고 차갑게 누워 있는 고요하고 아름다운 얼굴을 보았다."], ["The attitude was exquisitely easy; there had been no pain—no struggle.", "자세는 더없이 편안했고, 아픔도 몸부림도 없었다."], ["The action of the heart must have ceased as he lay down.", "심장은 그가 몸을 눕히던 그 순간에 멎었음이 틀림없었다."]], [["Mr. Bell was stunned by the shock; and only recovered when the time came for being angry at every suggestion of his man’s.", "벨 씨는 충격에 얼이 빠져 있다가, 하인이 무슨 말을 꺼낼 때마다 화를 낼 때가 되어서야 겨우 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“A coroner’s inquest?", "“검시라고?"], ["Pooh.", "쳇."], ["You don’t think I poisoned him! Dr.", "자네는 내가 그를 독살했다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지!"], ["Forbes says it is just the natural end of a heart complaint.", "포브스 의사는 그저 심장병의 자연스러운 끝이라고 하네."], ["Poor old Hale!", "가엾은 헤일!"], ["You wore out that tender heart of yours before its time.", "자네는 그 여린 심장을 제때가 오기도 전에 다 닳게 만들었구먼."], ["Poor old friend!", "가엾은 옛 벗!"], ["how he talked of his—— Wallis, pack up a carpet-bag for me in five minutes.", "그가 어젯밤에 제 딸 이야기를 얼마나…… 월리스, 5분 안에 내 여행 가방을 꾸리게."], ["Here have I been talking. Pack it up, I say.", "나는 여기서 이렇게 떠들고만 있었군."], ["I must go to Milton by the next train.”", "꾸리라니까. 다음 기차로 밀턴에 가야 하네.”"]], [["The bag was packed, the cab ordered, the railway reached, in twenty minutes from the time of this decision.", "가방이 꾸려지고 마차가 불려지고 철도역에 닿기까지, 이 결심을 한 때로부터 20분이 걸렸다."], ["The London train whizzed by, drew back some yards, and in Mr. Bell was hurried by the impatient guard.", "런던행 기차가 휙 지나갔다가 몇 야드 뒤로 물러섰고, 성마른 차장이 벨 씨를 떠밀듯 태웠다."], ["He threw himself back in his seat, to try, with closed eyes, to understand how one in life yesterday could be dead to-day; and shortly tears stole out between his grizzled eye-lashes, at the feeling of which he opened his keen eyes, and looked as severely cheerful as his set determination could make him.", "그는 좌석에 몸을 던지고 눈을 감은 채, 어제까지 살아 있던 사람이 어떻게 오늘 죽어 있을 수 있는지를 이해해 보려 했다. 이윽고 희끗한 속눈썹 사이로 눈물이 새어 나왔고, 그것이 느껴지자 그는 날카로운 눈을 뜨고 굳게 다잡은 결심이 낼 수 있는 한도까지 짐짓 엄격하고 명랑한 얼굴을 지었다."], ["He was not going to blubber before a set of strangers.", "낯모르는 사람들 앞에서 훌쩍거릴 생각은 없었다."], ["Not he!", "그럴 그가 아니었다!"]], [["There was no set of strangers, only one sitting far from him on the same side.", "낯모르는 사람들이라고 할 것도 없었다. 같은 쪽 저 멀리에 한 사람이 앉아 있을 뿐이었다."], ["By-and-by Mr. Bell peered at him, to discover what manner of man it was that might have been observing his emotion; and behind the great sheet of the outspread “Times,”", "이윽고 벨 씨는 제 감정을 지켜보았을지 모를 사람이 어떤 사람인지 알아보려고 그쪽을 유심히 보았다."], ["he recognized Mr. Thornton.", "그리고 활짝 펼쳐진 큼직한 타임스 신문 뒤에서 손턴 씨를 알아보았다."]], [["“Why, Thornton!", "“아니, 손턴!"], ["is that you?”", "자넨가?”"], ["said he, removing hastily to a closer proximity. He shook Mr.", "그가 서둘러 가까운 자리로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다."], ["Thornton vehemently by the hand, until the gripe ended in a sudden relaxation, for the hand was wanted to wipe away tears.", "그는 손턴 씨의 손을 세차게 흔들었고, 이윽고 그 손아귀가 문득 풀렸다. 그 손으로 눈물을 닦아야 했기 때문이다."], ["He had last seen Mr. Thornton in his friend Hale’s company.", "그가 손턴 씨를 마지막으로 본 것은 벗 헤일과 함께 있는 자리에서였다."]], [["“I’m going to Milton, bound on a melancholy errand.", "“나는 밀턴으로 가는 길일세, 서글픈 볼일을 안고서."], ["Going to break to Hale’s daughter the news of his sudden death!”", "헤일의 딸에게 아버지가 갑작스레 세상을 떠났다는 소식을 전하러 가네!”"]], [["“Death!", "“죽었다니요!"], ["Mr. Hale dead!”", "헤일 씨가 돌아가셨다고요!”"]], [["“Ay; I keep saying it to myself, ‘Hale is dead!’ but it doesn’t make it any the more real.", "“그렇다네. 나는 자꾸만 속으로 되뇐다네, ‘헤일이 죽었다!’ 하고."], ["Hale is dead for all that.", "하지만 그런다고 조금도 더 실감이 나지는 않아."], ["He went to bed well, to all appearance, last night, and was quite cold this morning when my servant went to call him.”", "그래도 헤일은 죽었네. 어젯밤에는 보기에 멀쩡하게 잠자리에 들었는데, 오늘 아침 내 하인이 깨우러 갔을 때는 이미 싸늘하게 식어 있었어.”"]], [["“Where?", "“어디서요?"], ["I don’t understand!”", "도무지 모르겠습니다!”"]], [["“At Oxford.", "“옥스퍼드에서."], ["He came to stay with me: hadn’t been in Oxford this seventeen years—and this is the end of it.”", "나한테 머물러 있으려고 왔었네. 17년 만에 처음 옥스퍼드에 온 것이었는데, 그 끝이 이 모양일세.”"]], [["Not one word was spoken for above a quarter of an hour.", "15분이 넘도록 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Then Mr. Thornton said:", "그러다 손턴 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And she!”", "“그러면 그분은!”"], ["and stopped full short.", "그러고는 뚝 멈추었다."]], [["“Margaret you mean.", "“마거릿 말이지."], ["Yes!", "그래!"], ["I am going to tell her.", "내가 가서 알릴 걸세."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["how full his thoughts were of her all last night!", "어젯밤 그의 생각은 온통 그 애로 가득했는데!"], ["Good God!", "맙소사!"], ["Last night only.", "겨우 어젯밤이었어."], ["And how immeasurably distant he is now!", "그런데 지금 그는 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 멀리 있는지!"], ["But I take Margaret as my child for his sake.", "하지만 나는 그를 위해서 마거릿을 내 자식으로 삼겠네."], ["I said last night I would take her for her own sake.", "어젯밤에 나는 그 애 자신을 위해 그 애를 맡겠다고 말했지."], ["Well, I take her for both.”", "그러니 이제 나는 두 사람 모두를 위해 그 애를 맡는 걸세.”"]], [["Mr. Thornton made one or two fruitless attempts to speak, before he could get out the words:", "손턴 씨는 이 말을 겨우 입 밖에 내기까지 한두 번 헛되이 말을 하려 애썼다."]], [["“What will become of her?”", "“그분은 어떻게 되는 겁니까?”"]], [["“I rather fancy there will be two people waiting for her: myself for one.", "“내 짐작에는 그 애를 기다리는 사람이 둘 있을 것 같네."], ["I would take a live dragon into my house to live, if by hiring such a chaperon, and setting up an establishment of my own, I could make my old age happy with having Margaret for a daughter.", "하나는 나고. 그런 보호자를 하나 들이고 내 살림을 차려서 마거릿을 딸로 두고 늘그막을 행복하게 보낼 수만 있다면, 나는 살아 있는 용이라도 집에 들여 함께 살겠네."], ["But there are those Lennoxes!”", "그런데 그 레녹스네가 있단 말일세!”"]], [["“Who are they?” asked Mr.", "“그들은 누구입니까?”"], ["Thornton with trembling interest.", "손턴 씨가 떨리는 관심으로 물었다."]], [["“Oh, smart London people, who very likely will think that they’ve the best right to her.", "“아, 런던의 세련된 사람들이지. 십중팔구 자기들에게 그 애에 대한 가장 큰 권리가 있다고 여길 사람들이야."], ["Captain Lennox married her cousin—the girl she was brought up with.", "레녹스 대위가 그 애의 사촌과 결혼했네. 그 애와 함께 자란 아가씨지."], ["Good enough people, I dare say.", "어지간히 괜찮은 사람들이겠지."], ["And there’s her Aunt, Mrs. Shaw.", "그리고 그 애의 이모인 쇼 부인이 있고."], ["There might be a way open, perhaps, by my offering to marry that worthy lady!", "어쩌면 내가 그 훌륭한 부인에게 청혼을 하면 길이 열릴지도 모르지!"], ["but that would be quite a pis aller.", "하지만 그건 참으로 궁여지책일 걸세."], ["And then there’s that brother!”", "그리고 또 그 동생이 있단 말이야!”"]], [["“What brother?", "“무슨 동생 말입니까?"], ["A brother of her aunt’s?”", "그 이모의 동생인가요?”"]], [["“No, no; a clever Lennox (the captain’s a fool, you must understand); a young barrister, who will be setting his cap at Margaret. I know he has had her in his mind these five years or more; one of his chums told me as much; and he was only kept back by want of fortune. Now that will be done away with.”", "“아니, 아니. 영리한 쪽 레녹스일세. 대위는 얼간이라는 걸 알아 두게나. 마거릿에게 마음을 두려 드는 젊은 법정 변호사지. 그가 5년도 넘게 그 애를 마음에 품어 왔다는 걸 나는 알고 있네. 그의 친구 하나가 그만큼을 내게 일러 주었지. 다만 재산이 없어서 물러서 있었을 뿐이야. 이제 그 걸림돌이 없어질 걸세.”"]], [["“How?” asked Mr.", "“어떻게요?”"], ["Thornton, too earnestly curious to be aware of the impertinence of his question.", "손턴 씨가 물었다. 너무나 간절히 궁금한 나머지 그는 제 물음이 무례하다는 것도 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“Why, she’ll have my money at my death.", "“내가 죽으면 그 애가 내 재산을 갖게 되니까."], ["And if this Henry Lennox is half good enough for her, and she likes him—well!", "그리고 이 헨리 레녹스라는 사람이 그 애에게 절반이라도 걸맞은 사람이고 그 애도 그를 좋아한다면, 뭐!"], ["I might find another way of getting a home through a marriage.", "그러면 나는 혼인을 통해 집을 얻을 또 다른 길을 찾아야 할지도 모르지."], ["I’m dreadfully afraid of being tempted, at an unguarded moment, by her aunt.”", "방심한 틈에 그 애의 이모에게 넘어가고 말까 봐 나는 무섭기 짝이 없다네.”"]], [["Neither Mr. Bell nor Mr.", "벨 씨도 손턴 씨도 웃을 기분이 아니었다."], ["Thornton was in a laughing humour; so the oddity of any of the speeches which the former made was unnoticed by them.", "그래서 앞사람이 한 말들 가운데 어떤 것이 얼마나 별났는지는 두 사람 다 알아채지 못했다."], ["Mr. Bell whistled, without emitting any sound beyond a long hissing breath; changed his seat, without finding comfort or rest; while Mr.", "벨 씨는 길게 새는 숨소리 말고는 아무 소리도 나지 않는 휘파람을 불었고, 자리를 옮겨 보았으나 편안함도 쉼도 찾지 못했다."], ["Thornton sat immovably still, his eyes fixed on one spot in the newspaper, which he had taken up in order to give himself leisure to think.", "그동안 손턴 씨는 꼼짝도 하지 않고 앉아, 생각할 겨를을 얻으려고 집어 들었던 신문의 한 지점에 눈을 못 박고 있었다."]], [["“Where have you been?”", "“어디에 다녀오는 길인가?”"], ["asked Mr. Bell, at length.", "마침내 벨 씨가 물었다."]], [["“To Havre.", "“아브르에요."], ["Trying to detect the secret of the great rise in the price of cotton.”", "면화 값이 크게 오른 까닭을 알아내 보려고요.”"]], [["“Ugh!", "“어휴!"], ["Cotton, and speculations, and smoke, well-cleansed and well-cared-for machinery, and unwashed and neglected hands.", "면화니 투기니 연기니, 잘 닦이고 잘 보살펴진 기계, 그리고 씻지도 못하고 돌봄도 받지 못하는 직공들이라니."], ["Poor old Hale!", "가엾은 헤일!"], ["Poor old Hale!", "가엾은 헤일!"], ["If you could have known the change which it was to him from Helstone.", "헬스톤에서 그리로 옮겨 온 것이 그에게 얼마나 큰 변화였는지 자네가 알 수 있었더라면."], ["Do you know the New Forest at all?”", "자네 뉴 포리스트를 좀 아는가?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“압니다.”"], ["(Very shortly.)", "(아주 짧게.)"]], [["“Then you can fancy the difference between it and Milton.", "“그럼 그곳과 밀턴의 차이를 짐작할 수 있겠군."], ["What part were you in?", "어느 쪽에 있었나?"], ["Were you ever at Helstone?", "헬스톤에 가 본 적이 있는가?"], ["a little picturesque village, like some in the Odenwald?", "오덴발트의 어느 마을 같은 그림 같은 작은 마을 말일세."], ["You know Helstone?”", "헬스톤을 아는가?”"]], [["“I have seen it.", "“본 적이 있습니다."], ["It was a great change to leave it and come to Milton.”", "그곳을 떠나 밀턴으로 오는 것은 큰 변화였겠지요.”"]], [["He took up his newspaper with a determined air, as if resolved to avoid further conversation; and Mr.", "그는 더 이상의 대화를 피하기로 작정한 듯 단호한 태도로 신문을 집어 들었다."], ["Bell was fain to resort to his former occupation of trying to find out how he could best break the news to Margaret.", "그리하여 벨 씨는 마거릿에게 이 소식을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 전할 수 있을까 궁리하던 아까의 일로 어쩔 수 없이 되돌아갔다."]], [["She was at an upstairs window; she saw him alight; she guessed the truth with an instinctive flash. She stood in the middle of the drawing-room, as if arrested in her first impulse to rush downstairs, and as if by the same restraining thought she had been turned to stone; so white and immovable was she.", "그녀는 위층 창가에 있었다. 그녀는 그가 마차에서 내리는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 본능이 번쩍이듯 진실을 알아차렸다. 그녀는 아래층으로 뛰어 내려가려던 첫 충동에 붙들린 듯, 그리고 바로 그 붙드는 생각에 돌로 변해 버린 듯 응접실 한가운데에 서 있었다. 그만큼 그녀는 희고 미동도 없었다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["don’t tell me!", "말씀하지 마세요!"], ["I know it from your face!", "얼굴만 봐도 알겠어요!"], ["You would have sent—you would not have left him—if he were alive!", "살아 계셨다면 사람을 보내셨겠지요. 살아 계셨다면 곁을 떠나오지 않으셨겠지요!"], ["Oh, papa, papa!”", "아, 아버지, 아버지!”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLII", "# 제42장"]], [["“When some beloved voice that was to you Both sound and sweetness, faileth suddenly, And silence, against which you dare not cry, Aches round you like a strong disease and new,— What hope?", "“그대에게 소리이자 감미로움이던 어느 사랑하는 목소리가 문득 끊기고, 감히 맞서 부르짖을 수도 없는 침묵이 새로 얻은 모진 병처럼 그대를 에워싸고 아려 올 때—무슨 희망이 있으랴? 무슨 도움이 있으랴?"], ["what help? what music will undo That silence to your sense?”", "어떤 음악이 그대의 감각에서 그 침묵을 풀어 주랴?”"], ["MRS. BROWNING.", "— 브라우닝 부인."]], [["The shock had been great.", "충격은 컸다."], ["Margaret fell into a state of prostration, which did not show itself in sobs and tears, or even find the relief of words.", "마거릿은 극도의 탈진 상태에 빠졌는데, 그것은 흐느낌이나 눈물로 드러나지도 않고 말로써 위안을 얻지도 못하는 종류의 것이었다."], ["She lay on the sofa with her eyes shut, never speaking but when spoken to, and then replying in whispers. Mr.", "그녀는 눈을 감은 채 소파에 누워, 누가 말을 걸 때가 아니면 입을 열지 않았고, 그때에도 속삭임으로만 대답했다."], ["Bell was perplexed.", "벨 씨는 난감했다."], ["He dared not leave her; he dared not ask her to accompany him back to Oxford, which had been one of the plans he had formed on the journey to Milton, her physical exhaustion was evidently too complete for her to undertake any such fatigue—putting the sight that she would have to encounter out of the question.", "그녀를 두고 떠날 수도 없었고, 밀턴으로 오는 길에 세워 두었던 계획 가운데 하나였던 대로 자기와 함께 옥스퍼드로 가자고 청할 수도 없었다. 그녀가 마주해야 할 광경은 논외로 하더라도, 그런 고생을 감당하기에는 몸의 기진함이 너무나 완연했다."], ["Mr. Bell sate over the fire, considering what he had better do.", "벨 씨는 난롯가에 앉아 어떻게 하는 것이 좋을지 궁리했다."], ["Margaret lay motionless, and almost breathless by him.", "마거릿은 그의 곁에서 꼼짝도 않고, 숨소리조차 들리지 않는 채로 누워 있었다."], ["He would not leave her, even for the dinner which Dixon had prepared for him downstairs, and, with sobbing hospitality, would fain have tempted him to eat.", "딕슨이 아래층에 차려 놓고 흐느끼는 환대로 기어이 권하려 드는 저녁 식사에조차 그는 그녀를 두고 내려가려 하지 않았다."], ["He had a plateful of something brought up to him.", "그는 접시에 담은 음식을 위층으로 가져오게 했다."], ["In general, he was particular and dainty enough, and knew well each shade of flavour in his food, but now the devilled chicken tasted like sawdust.", "평소의 그는 입이 꽤 까다롭고 세심하여 음식의 맛 하나하나를 잘 가려냈지만, 지금은 양념한 닭고기가 톱밥 같은 맛이었다."], ["He minced up some of the fowl for Margaret, and peppered and salted it well; but when Dixon, following his directions, tried to feed her, the languid shake of head, proved that in such a state as Margaret was in, food would only choke, not nourish her.", "그는 마거릿을 위해 닭고기를 잘게 다지고 후추와 소금을 잘 쳤다. 그러나 딕슨이 그의 지시대로 그녀에게 먹이려 하자, 힘없이 젓는 고갯짓이 지금 마거릿 같은 상태에서는 음식이 목을 메게 할 뿐 몸을 살리지는 못하리라는 것을 말해 주었다."]], [["Mr. Bell gave a great sigh; lifted up his stout old limbs (stiff with travelling) from their easy position, and followed Dixon out of the room.", "벨 씨는 크게 한숨을 내쉬고는, 여행으로 뻣뻣해진 튼실한 늙은 팔다리를 편안한 자세에서 일으켜 딕슨을 따라 방을 나섰다."]], [["“I can’t leave her.", "“그 애를 두고 갈 수가 없네."], ["I must write to them at Oxford, to see that the preparations are made: they can be getting on with these till I arrive.", "옥스퍼드로 편지를 써서 준비가 되도록 해야겠어. 내가 갈 때까지 그쪽에서 일을 진행해 놓을 수 있겠지."], ["Can’t Mrs. Lennox come to her?", "레녹스 부인이 저 애에게 와 줄 수는 없나?"], ["I’ll write and tell her she must.", "내가 편지를 써서 꼭 와야 한다고 이르겠네."], ["The girl must have some woman-friend about her, if only to talk her into a good fit of crying.”", "저 아가씨 곁에는 여자 친구가 하나 있어야 해. 실컷 한바탕 울게 만들어 주기 위해서라도 말이야.”"]], [["Dixon was crying—enough for two; but, after wiping her eyes and steadying her voice, she managed to tell Mr.", "딕슨은 울고 있었다—두 사람 몫으로도 넉넉할 만큼."], ["Bell, that Mrs. Lennox was too near her confinement to be able to undertake any journey at present.", "그러나 눈물을 훔치고 목소리를 가다듬은 뒤, 그녀는 레녹스 부인이 해산이 너무 가까워 지금은 어떤 여행도 할 수 없다는 것을 벨 씨에게 겨우 전했다."]], [["“Well!", "“그래!"], ["I suppose we must have Mrs.", "그렇다면 쇼 부인을 모셔 와야겠구먼."], ["Shaw; she’s come back to England, isn’t she?”", "그분은 영국으로 돌아오셨지, 안 그런가?”"]], [["“Yes, sir, she’s come back; but I don’t think she will like to leave Mrs.", "“네, 나리, 돌아오셨습니다. 하지만 이런 중요한 시기에 레녹스 부인 곁을 떠나고 싶어 하실 것 같지는 않은데요.”"], ["Lennox at such an interesting time,”", "딕슨이 말했다."], ["said Dixon, who did not much approve of a stranger entering the household, to share with her in her ruling care of Margaret.", "낯선 사람이 이 집에 들어와, 마거릿을 돌보는 자신의 지배권을 나누어 갖는 것이 그리 달갑지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“Interesting time be——.” Mr.", "“중요한 시기는 무슨 빌어먹을—.”"], ["Bell restricted himself by coughing over the end of his sentence.", "벨 씨는 문장 끝을 기침으로 덮으며 스스로를 억눌렀다."], ["“She could be content to be at Venice or Naples, or some of those Popish places, at the last ‘interesting time,’ which took place in Corfu, I think.", "“지난번 그 ‘중요한 시기’ 때는—내 기억에 코르푸에서였지—베네치아나 나폴리, 아니면 그 교황쟁이들 사는 곳 어디쯤에 태평하게 머물러 계셨으면서."], ["And what does that little prosperous woman’s ‘interesting time’ signify, in comparison with that poor creature there,—that helpless, homeless, friendless, Margaret—lying as still on that sofa as if it were an altar-tomb, and she the stone statue on it.", "게다가 그 팔자 좋은 부인네의 ‘중요한 시기’가 저기 저 가엾은 것에 비하면 대체 무슨 대수란 말인가—저 무력하고, 집도 없고, 의지할 이도 없는 마거릿 말일세. 소파에 저렇게 꼼짝없이 누워 있는 꼴이 꼭 석관 위에 얹힌 돌 조각상 같지 않은가."], ["I tell you, Mrs. Shaw shall come.", "내 말하는데, 쇼 부인은 반드시 오셔야 하네."], ["See that a room, or whatever she wants, is got ready for her by to-morrow night.", "내일 밤까지 방이든 뭐든 그분께 필요한 것을 다 갖춰 놓게."], ["I’ll take care she comes.”", "오시게 하는 건 내가 책임지지.”"]], [["Accordingly Mr. Bell wrote a letter, which Mrs.", "그리하여 벨 씨는 편지를 한 통 썼는데, 쇼 부인은 눈물을 쏟으며 그 편지가 돌아가신 장군이 통풍 발작을 일으키려 할 때 쓰던 것과 어찌나 똑같은지 평생 소중히 간직하겠노라고 말했다."], ["Shaw declared, with many tears, to be so like one of the dear general’s when he was going to have a fit of the gout, that she should always value and preserve it. If he had given her the option, by requesting or urging her, as if a refusal were possible, she might not have come—true and sincere as was her sympathy with Margaret.", "만일 그가 거절이 가능하기라도 한 듯 청하거나 권하는 식으로 선택의 여지를 주었더라면, 마거릿을 향한 그녀의 동정이 아무리 진실하고 참되었다 해도 그녀는 오지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["It needed the sharp uncourteous command to make her conquer her vis inertiæ and allow herself to be packed by her maid, after the latter had completed the boxes.", "그 무뚝뚝하고 무례한 명령이 있고서야 그녀는 타고난 게으름의 관성을 이겨 내고, 하녀가 짐 상자를 다 꾸린 뒤 자신마저 꾸려 실려 가도록 몸을 맡길 수 있었다."], ["Edith, all cap, shawl, and tears, came out to the top of the stairs, as Captain Lennox was taking her mother down to the carriage:", "레녹스 대위가 장모를 마차까지 데려다주려 할 때, 모자에 숄에 눈물로 뒤범벅이 된 이디스가 층계참 꼭대기까지 나왔다."]], [["“Don’t forget, mamma; Margaret must come and live with us.", "“잊지 마세요, 어머니. 마거릿은 꼭 우리와 함께 살러 와야 해요."], ["Sholto will go to Oxford on Wednesday, and you must send word by Mr. Bell to him when we’re to expect you.", "숄토가 수요일에 옥스퍼드에 갈 테니까, 언제쯤 오실지 벨 씨 편에 말씀을 전해 주세요."], ["And if you want Sholto, he can go on from Oxford to Milton.", "그리고 숄토가 필요하시면 옥스퍼드에서 밀턴으로 곧장 갈 수 있어요."], ["Don’t forget, mamma; you are to bring back Margaret.”", "잊지 마세요, 어머니. 마거릿을 데리고 돌아오셔야 해요.”"]], [["Edith re-entered the drawing-room.", "이디스는 응접실로 다시 들어갔다."], ["Mr. Henry Lennox was there, cutting open the pages of a new Review.", "그곳에는 헨리 레녹스 씨가 새로 나온 평론지의 책장을 자르고 있었다."], ["Without lifting his head, he said, “If you don’t like Sholto to be so long absent from you, Edith, I hope you will let me go down to Milton, and give what assistance I can.”", "그는 고개도 들지 않은 채 말했다. “숄토가 그렇게 오래 곁을 비우는 게 싫으시다면, 이디스, 제가 밀턴에 내려가서 할 수 있는 도움을 드리게 해 주시죠.”"]], [["“Oh, thank you,”", "“아, 고마워요.”"], ["said Edith, “I dare say old Mr.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["Bell will do everything he can, and more help may not be needed.", "“늙은 벨 씨가 하실 수 있는 건 다 해 주실 테고, 더 손이 필요하지는 않을 거예요."], ["Only one does not look for much savoir-faire from a resident Fellow.", "다만 대학에 눌러앉은 펠로한테 무슨 세상 물정을 기대하기는 어렵겠지만요."], ["Dear, darling Margaret; won’t it be nice to have her here, again?", "사랑하는 우리 마거릿. 그 애가 다시 여기 와 있으면 얼마나 좋을까요?"], ["You were both great allies, years ago.”", "두 분은 몇 해 전에 아주 각별한 사이였잖아요.”"]], [["“Were we?”", "“그랬던가요?”"], ["asked he indifferently, with an appearance of being interested in a passage in the Review.", "그는 평론지의 어느 대목에 마음이 가 있는 듯한 기색으로 무심하게 물었다."]], [["“Well, perhaps not—I forget.", "“글쎄요, 아니었을지도 모르죠—잘 기억이 안 나요."], ["I was so full of Sholto.", "그땐 온통 숄토 생각뿐이었으니까."], ["But doesn’t it fall out well, that if my uncle was to die, it should be just now, when we are come home, and settled in the old house, and quite ready to receive Margaret?", "그런데 이모부가 돌아가실 거였다면 하필 지금이라는 게 오히려 잘된 일 아닌가요? 우리가 귀국해서 옛집에 자리를 잡고, 마거릿을 맞을 준비가 다 되어 있는 바로 이때 말이에요."], ["Poor thing!", "가엾어라!"], ["what a change it will be to her from Milton!", "밀턴에 있다가 오면 얼마나 큰 변화일까요!"], ["I’ll have new chintz for her bed-room, and make it look new and bright, and cheer her up a little.”", "그 애 침실에는 새 사라사 천을 들여서 산뜻하고 화사하게 꾸며 주고, 기운도 좀 북돋아 줘야겠어요.”"]], [["In the same spirit of kindness, Mrs.", "쇼 부인도 그와 똑같은 종류의 친절한 마음으로 밀턴을 향해 길을 떠났다."], ["Shaw journeyed to Milton, occasionally dreading the first meeting, and wondering how it would be got over; but more frequently planning how soon she could get Margaret away from “that horrid place,” and back in the pleasant comforts of Harley Street.", "이따금 첫 대면이 두려워지고 그 순간을 어떻게 넘길까 걱정도 되었지만, 그보다는 마거릿을 얼마나 빨리 “그 끔찍한 곳”에서 데리고 나와 할리 가의 안락한 살림 속으로 되돌려 놓을 수 있을까 하는 궁리를 더 자주 했다."]], [["“Oh dear!”", "“어머나!”"], ["she said to her maid; “look at those chimneys!", "그녀가 하녀에게 말했다. “저 굴뚝들 좀 봐라!"], ["My poor sister Hale!", "가엾은 내 동생 헤일!"], ["I don’t think I could have rested at Naples, if I had known what it was!", "여기가 이런 곳인 줄 알았더라면 나폴리에서 마음 편히 지내지 못했을 게다."], ["I must have come and fetched her and Margaret away.”", "진작 와서 동생과 마거릿을 데려갔어야 했어.”"], ["And to herself she acknowledged, that she had always thought her brother-in-law rather a weak man, but never so weak as now, when she saw for what a place he had exchanged the lovely Helstone home.", "그리고 속으로는 제부가 늘 좀 무른 사람이라고 여겨 왔지만, 그 아름다운 헬스톤의 집을 무엇과 맞바꾸었는지 눈으로 확인한 지금만큼 무르게 여겨진 적은 없다고 인정했다."]], [["Margaret had remained in the same state; white, motionless, speechless, tearless.", "마거릿은 여전히 같은 상태였다. 창백하고, 미동도 없고, 말이 없고, 눈물도 없었다."], ["They had told her that her aunt Shaw was coming; but she had not expressed either surprise or pleasure, or dislike to the idea. Mr.", "쇼 이모가 온다는 이야기를 들었지만, 그녀는 놀라움도 기쁨도, 싫은 기색도 내비치지 않았다."], ["Bell, whose appetite had returned, and who appreciated Dixon’s endeavours to gratify it, in vain urged upon her to taste some sweetbreads stewed with oysters; she shook her head with the same quiet obstinacy as on the previous day; and he was obliged to console himself for her rejection, by eating them all himself.", "입맛이 돌아온 벨 씨는 그것을 채워 주려는 딕슨의 노력을 고맙게 여기면서도, 굴과 함께 조린 흉선 요리를 좀 들어 보라고 그녀에게 아무리 권해 보아도 소용이 없었다. 그녀는 전날과 똑같이 조용한 고집으로 고개를 저었다."], ["But Margaret was the first to hear the stopping of the cab that brought her aunt from the railway station.", "그는 그 거절을 스스로 위로하기 위해 그것을 혼자 다 먹는 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 이모를 기차역에서 태워 온 마차가 멎는 소리를 맨 먼저 들은 사람은 마거릿이었다."], ["Her eyelids quivered, her lips coloured and trembled.", "그녀의 눈꺼풀이 떨렸고, 입술에 핏기가 돌며 떨렸다."], ["Mr. Bell went down to meet Mrs.", "벨 씨가 쇼 부인을 맞으러 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Shaw; and when they came up, Margaret was standing, trying to steady her dizzy self; and when she saw her aunt, she went forward to the arms open to receive her, and first found the passionate relief of tears on her aunt’s shoulder.", "두 사람이 올라왔을 때 마거릿은 어지러운 몸을 가누려 애쓰며 서 있었다. 그리고 이모를 보자 자신을 안으려 벌린 두 팔 속으로 걸어 들어가, 이모의 어깨에 기대어 비로소 눈물이라는 격렬한 위안을 찾았다."], ["All thoughts of quiet habitual love, of tenderness for years, of relationship to the dead, all that inexplicable likeness in look, tone, and gesture, that seem to belong to one family, and which reminded Margaret so forcibly at this moment of her mother, came in to melt and soften her numbed heart into the overflow of warm tears.", "조용하고 몸에 밴 사랑, 오랜 세월의 다정함, 죽은 이와 이어진 핏줄, 그리고 한 집안 사람에게만 있는 듯한 그 설명할 길 없는 눈빛과 목소리와 몸짓의 닮음—바로 그 순간 마거릿에게 어머니를 그토록 사무치게 떠올리게 한 그 모든 것이 밀려들어, 얼어붙었던 그녀의 마음을 녹이고 풀어 따뜻한 눈물이 넘쳐흐르게 했다."]], [["Mr. Bell stole out of the room, and went down into the study, where he ordered a fire, and tried to divert his thoughts by taking down and examining the different books.", "벨 씨는 방에서 살그머니 빠져나와 아래층 서재로 내려갔다."], ["Each volume brought a remembrance or a suggestion of his dead friend.", "그는 그곳에 불을 지피게 하고, 이런저런 책을 꺼내 들여다보며 생각을 딴 데로 돌리려 애썼다."], ["It might be a change of employment from his two days’ work of watching Margaret, but it was no change of thought.", "책 한 권 한 권이 죽은 벗의 기억이나 그를 떠올리게 하는 실마리를 가져왔다."], ["He was glad to catch the sound of Mr. Thornton’s voice, making enquiry at the door.", "이틀 동안 마거릿을 지켜보던 일에서 하는 일이야 바뀌었을지 몰라도, 생각이 바뀐 것은 아니었다."], ["Dixon was rather cavalierly dismissing him; for with the appearance of Mrs.", "현관에서 안부를 묻는 손턴 씨의 목소리가 들려오자 그는 반가웠다."], ["Shaw’s maid, came visions of former grandeur, of the Beresford blood, of the “station”", "딕슨은 그를 다소 매몰차게 돌려보내려 하고 있었다."], ["(so she was pleased to term it) from which her young lady had been ousted, and to which she was now, please God, to be restored. These visions, which she had been dwelling on with complacency in her conversation with Mrs.", "쇼 부인의 하녀가 나타나면서 지난날의 위세, 베러스퍼드 가의 핏줄, 그리고 자기 아가씨가 밀려났다가 이제 하느님 뜻이라면 되돌아가게 될 그 “지위”(그녀는 그렇게 부르기를 좋아했다)의 환영이 함께 떠올랐기 때문이다."], ["Shaw’s maid (skilfully eliciting meanwhile all the circumstances of state and consequence connected with the Harley Street establishment, for the edification of the listening Martha), made Dixon rather inclined to be supercilious in her treatment of any inhabitant of Milton; so, though she always stood rather in awe of Mr.", "그녀는 쇼 부인의 하녀와 이야기를 나누며—그러는 사이 할리 가 살림살이의 격식과 위세에 관한 사정을 솜씨 좋게 죄다 캐내어, 옆에서 듣고 있던 마사를 교화하기도 하면서—이런 환영들을 흡족하게 곱씹던 참이라, 밀턴 사람이라면 누구에게든 거만하게 굴고 싶어졌다."], ["Thornton, she was as curt as she durst be in telling him that he could see none of the inmates of the house that night.", "그래서 늘 손턴 씨 앞에서는 다소 주눅이 들던 그녀도, 오늘 밤에는 이 집 사람 누구도 만날 수 없다고 감히 낼 수 있는 최대한 퉁명스러운 말투로 일러 주었다."], ["It was rather uncomfortable to be contradicted in her statement by Mr. Bell’s opening the study-door, and calling out:", "그런데 벨 씨가 서재 문을 열고 이렇게 외치는 바람에 자기 말이 뒤집히고 만 것은 여간 민망한 일이 아니었다."]], [["“Thornton!", "“손턴인가?"], ["is that you?", "자넨가?"], ["Come in for a minute or two; I want to speak to you.”", "잠깐만 들어오게. 자네한테 할 말이 있네.”"], ["So Mr. Thornton went into the study, and Dixon had to retreat into the kitchen, and reinstate herself in her own esteem by a prodigious story of Sir John Beresford’s coach and six, when he was high sheriff.", "그리하여 손턴 씨는 서재로 들어갔고, 딕슨은 부엌으로 물러나 존 베러스퍼드 경이 주 장관이던 시절 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차 이야기를 거창하게 늘어놓음으로써 스스로에 대한 자존심을 되찾아야 했다."]], [["“I don’t know what I wanted to say to you after all.", "“막상 자네한테 무슨 말을 하려 했는지 모르겠구먼."], ["Only it’s dull enough to sit in a room where everything speaks to you of a dead friend.", "다만 온통 죽은 벗을 이야기하는 물건들뿐인 방에 앉아 있자니 여간 적적해야 말이지."], ["Yet Margaret and her aunt must have the drawing-room to themselves!”", "그렇다고 마거릿과 그 이모에게 응접실을 내주지 않을 수도 없고!”"]], [["“Is Mrs.—is her aunt come?”", "“그—이모님이 오셨습니까?”"], ["asked Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Come? Yes!", "“왔지!"], ["maid and all.", "하녀까지 데리고."], ["One would have thought she might have come by herself at such a time!", "이런 때에는 혼자 올 법도 하건만!"], ["And now I shall have to turn out and find my way to the Clarendon.”", "그래서 이제 내가 나가서 클래런던 여관을 찾아가야 할 판이라네.”"]], [["“You must not go to the Clarendon.", "“클래런던에 가시면 안 됩니다."], ["We have five or six empty bed-rooms at home.”", "저희 집에 빈 침실이 대여섯은 있습니다.”"]], [["“Well aired?”", "“바람은 잘 통하게 해 두었고?”"]], [["“I think you may trust my mother for that.”", "“그건 저희 어머니를 믿으셔도 됩니다.”"]], [["“Then I’ll only run upstairs and wish that wan girl good-night, and make my bow to her aunt, and go off with you straight.”", "“그럼 위층에 잠깐 올라가서 그 핏기 없는 아가씨에게 잘 자라고 인사하고, 이모에게 인사도 드린 다음 자네와 곧장 나서겠네.”"]], [["Mr.", "벨 씨는 위층에서 한참을 머물렀다."], ["Bell was some time upstairs. Mr.", "손턴 씨는 시간이 길게 느껴지기 시작했다."], ["Thornton began to think it long, for he was full of business, and had hardly been able to spare the time for running up to Crampton, and enquiring how Miss Hale was.", "그는 일이 산더미 같아서 크램프턴까지 올라와 헤일 양의 안부를 묻는 데 쓸 시간조차 겨우 냈던 것이다."]], [["When they had set out upon their walk, Mr. Bell said:", "두 사람이 걷기 시작했을 때 벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I was kept by those women in the drawing-room.", "“응접실에서 여자들한테 붙들려 있었네."], ["Mrs. Shaw is anxious to get home—on account of her daughter, she says—and wants Margaret to go off with her at once.", "쇼 부인은 딸 때문이라면서 어서 집에 돌아가고 싶어 하고, 마거릿을 당장 데려가려 하네."], ["Now she is no more fit for travelling than I am for flying.", "그런데 저 애가 여행을 할 만한 몸이라면 나는 하늘도 날 수 있을 걸세."], ["Besides, she says, and very justly, that she has friends she must see—that she must wish good-bye to several people; and then her aunt worried her about old claims, and was she forgetful of old friends?", "게다가 저 애 말도 지당한 것이, 만나야 할 벗들이 있고 몇 사람에게는 작별 인사를 해야 한다는 게야. 그러자 이모가 오래된 인연을 들먹이며 옛 친구들은 잊었느냐고 저 애를 들볶더군."], ["And she said, with a great burst of crying, she should be glad enough to go from a place where she had suffered so much.", "그러니까 저 애가 한바탕 울음을 터뜨리면서, 그토록 많은 고통을 겪은 곳을 떠날 수 있다면 오히려 기쁘겠다고 하지 않겠나."], ["Now I must return to Oxford to-morrow, and I don’t know on which side of the scale to throw in my voice.”", "나는 내일 옥스퍼드로 돌아가야 하는데, 저울 어느 쪽에 내 목소리를 얹어야 할지 모르겠네.”"]], [["He paused, as if asking a question; but he received no answer from his companion, the echo of whose thoughts kept repeating—", "그는 마치 묻기라도 하듯 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 동행에게서는 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았다. 그의 머릿속에서는 그 말의 메아리가 되풀이되고 있었다—"]], [["“Where she had suffered so much.”", "“그토록 많은 고통을 겪은 곳.”"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["and that was the way in which this eighteen months in Milton—to him so unspeakably precious, down to its very bitterness, which was worth all the rest of life’s sweetness—would be remembered. Neither loss of father, nor loss of mother, dear as she was to Mr.", "그에게는 그 쓰디쓴 밑바닥까지도—그리고 그 쓴맛이야말로 인생의 다른 모든 단맛을 합친 것보다 값졌는데—말할 수 없이 소중했던 밀턴에서의 그 열여덟 달이, 그녀에게는 그렇게 기억되는구나."], ["Thornton, could have poisoned the remembrance of the weeks, the days, the hours, when a walk of two miles, every step of which was pleasant, as it brought him nearer and nearer to her, took him to her sweet presence—every step of which was rich, as each recurring moment that bore him away from her made him recall some fresh grace in her demeanour, or pleasant pungency in her character.", "아버지를 잃는 일도, 손턴 씨에게 그토록 소중한 어머니를 잃는 일도, 그 몇 주, 그 며칠, 그 몇 시간의 기억만은 더럽히지 못했으리라. 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그녀에게 가까워지기에 즐거웠던 2마일의 길이 그를 그녀의 다정한 앞으로 데려다주던 시간, 그리고 그녀에게서 멀어지는 순간순간이 그녀의 몸가짐에서 새로운 우아함을, 성품에서 유쾌하게 톡 쏘는 맛을 자꾸만 떠올리게 해 주어 돌아오는 걸음마다 넉넉하던 시간을."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["whatever had happened to him, external to his relation to her, he could never have spoken of that time, when he could have seen her every day—when he had her within his grasp, as it were—as a time of suffering.", "그녀와의 관계 바깥에서 그에게 무슨 일이 일어났든, 그녀를 날마다 볼 수 있었던 그때—말하자면 그녀가 손 닿는 곳에 있었던 그때—를 그는 결코 고통의 시간이라 부를 수 없었다."], ["It had been a royal time of luxury to him, with all its stings and contumelies, compared to the poverty that crept round and clipped the anticipation of the future down to sordid fact, and life without an atmosphere of either hope or fear.", "그것은 온갖 가시와 모욕을 안고서도 그에게는 호사스러운 왕의 시절이었다. 슬금슬금 다가와 앞날에 대한 기대를 초라한 사실로 깎아내리고, 희망도 두려움도 없는 공기 속에 삶을 남겨 둔 지금의 빈곤에 비하면 말이다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton and Fanny were in the dining-room; the latter in a flutter of small exultation, as the maid held up one glossy material after another, to try the effect of the wedding-dresses by candle-light.", "손턴 부인과 패니는 식당에 있었다."], ["Her mother really tried to sympathise with her, but could not.", "패니는 하녀가 번쩍이는 옷감을 하나씩 들어 올려 촛불 아래서 혼례복이 어떻게 보이는지 살펴 주는 동안 자잘한 우쭐함으로 들떠 있었다."], ["Neither taste nor dress were in her line of subjects, and she heartily wished that Fanny had accepted her brother’s offer of having the wedding clothes provided by some first-rate London dressmaker, without the endless troublesome discussions, and unsettled waverings, that arose out of Fanny’s desire to choose and superintend everything herself.", "어머니는 정말로 딸의 마음에 맞춰 주려 애썼지만 그럴 수가 없었다. 취향이니 옷차림이니 하는 것은 그녀의 관심사가 아니었고, 그녀는 패니가 모든 것을 제 손으로 고르고 감독하겠다고 고집하는 바람에 생기는 끝없이 성가신 상의와 갈팡질팡하는 마음 바꾸기 없이, 런던의 일류 재봉사에게 혼수옷 일체를 맡기라는 오라비의 제안을 딸이 받아들였더라면 하고 진심으로 바랐다."], ["Mr. Thornton was only too glad to mark his grateful approbation of any sensible man, who could be captivated by Fanny’s second-rate airs and graces, by giving her ample means for providing herself with the finery, which certainly rivalled, if it did not exceed, the lover in her estimation.", "손턴 씨로 말하자면, 패니의 이류짜리 애교와 우아 떠는 태도에 홀릴 만큼 분별 있는 남자에게 고마운 마음을 표시할 기회를 얻은 것이 그저 반가웠기에, 누이가 마음껏 치장거리를 장만할 수 있도록 돈을 넉넉히 대 주었다."], ["When her brother and Mr.", "패니의 마음속에서 그 치장거리들은 신랑감을 넘어서지는 못할지언정 적어도 어깨를 겨루기는 하는 것이 분명했다."], ["Bell came in, Fanny blushed and simpered, and fluttered over the signs of her employment, in a way which could not have failed to draw attention from any one else but Mr. Bell.", "오라비와 벨 씨가 들어서자 패니는 얼굴을 붉히며 새침하게 웃고는, 자기가 하던 일의 흔적들 위로 부산을 떨었다."], ["If he thought about her and her silks and satins at all, it was to compare her and them with the pale sorrow he had left behind him, sitting motionless, with bent head and folded hands, in a room where the stillness was so great that you might almost fancy the rush in your straining ears was occasioned by the spirits of the dead, yet hovering round their beloved.", "벨 씨가 아닌 다른 사람이었다면 누구라도 눈길을 주지 않을 수 없었을 만한 태도였다. 그가 패니와 그 비단과 공단을 조금이라도 생각했다면, 그것은 그것들을 자기가 두고 온 창백한 슬픔과 견주어 보기 위해서였을 뿐이다."], ["For when Mr. Bell had first gone upstairs, Mrs.", "고개를 숙이고 두 손을 포갠 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있던 그 슬픔, 그리고 어찌나 고요한지 곤두선 귓속에서 울리는 소리마저 아직 사랑하는 이의 곁을 맴도는 죽은 자들의 영혼이 내는 것이 아닌가 싶을 지경이던 그 방을."], ["Shaw lay asleep on the sofa; and no sound broke the silence.", "벨 씨가 처음 위층에 올라갔을 때 쇼 부인은 소파에 누워 잠들어 있었고, 어떤 소리도 그 고요를 깨뜨리지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Thornton gave Mr.", "손턴 부인은 벨 씨에게 격식 있는 환대의 인사를 건넸다."], ["Bell her formal, hospitable welcome.", "그녀는 아들의 집에서 아들의 벗을 맞이할 때만큼 상냥한 적이 없었다."], ["She was never so gracious as when receiving her son’s friends in her son’s house; and the more unexpected they were, the more honour to her admirable housekeeping preparations for comfort.", "그리고 손님이 뜻밖일수록, 편안함을 위해 빈틈없이 갖춰 둔 그녀의 살림 솜씨에 돌아오는 영예도 커지는 법이었다."]], [["“How is Miss Hale?”", "“헤일 양은 어떻습니까?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“About as broken down by this last stroke as she can be.”", "“이번 일격에 무너질 수 있는 데까지 무너졌지요.”"]], [["“I am sure it is very well for her that she has such a friend as you.”", "“그 아가씨에게 선생 같은 벗이 있다는 건 참으로 다행한 일입니다.”"]], [["“I wish I were her only friend, madam.", "“내가 그 애의 유일한 벗이었으면 좋겠소이다, 부인."], ["I daresay it sounds very brutal; but here have I been displaced, and turned out of my post of comforter and adviser by a fine lady aunt; and there are cousins and what not claiming her in London, as if she were a lap-dog belonging to them.", "아주 매정하게 들리겠지만, 나로 말하자면 어디서 굴러온 귀부인 이모한테 위로자며 조언자 자리에서 밀려나 쫓겨난 신세라오. 게다가 런던에는 사촌이니 뭐니 하는 것들이 그 애를 제 집 애완견이라도 되는 양 제 것이라 주장하고 있고 말이오."], ["And she is too weak and miserable to have a will of her own.”", "그런데 그 애는 너무 약해지고 비참해져서 제 뜻이라는 걸 세울 힘도 없구려.”"]], [["“She must indeed be weak,” said Mrs.", "“정말이지 약해졌겠군요.”"], ["Thornton, with an implied meaning which her son understood well.", "손턴 부인이 말했는데, 그 속뜻을 아들은 잘 알아들었다."], ["“But where,” continued Mrs.", "“그런데 말이지요,”"], ["Thornton, “have these relations been all this time that Miss Hale has appeared almost friendless, and has certainly had a good deal of anxiety to bear?”", "손턴 부인이 말을 이었다. “헤일 양이 벗 하나 없는 사람처럼 지내고, 또 분명 적잖은 걱정을 견뎌 내는 동안 그 친척이라는 사람들은 다들 어디 있었답니까?”"], ["But she did not feel interest enough in the answer to her question to wait for it.", "그러나 그녀는 자기가 던진 물음의 답을 기다릴 만큼 관심이 있지도 않았다."], ["She left the room to make her household arrangements.", "그녀는 집안일을 챙기러 방을 나갔다."]], [["“They have been living abroad.", "“외국에 나가 살았지요."], ["They have some kind of claim upon her.", "그래도 그 애에 대해 어느 정도 연고를 주장할 만은 하오."], ["I will do them that justice.", "그 점은 인정해 주리다."], ["The aunt brought her up, and she and the cousin have been like sisters.", "이모가 그 애를 길렀고, 그 애와 사촌은 자매나 다름없이 지냈으니까."], ["The thing vexing me, you see, is that I wanted to take her for a child of my own; and I am jealous of these people, who don’t seem to value the privilege of their right.", "다만 나를 언짢게 하는 건 말이오, 나는 그 애를 내 자식으로 삼고 싶었는데, 제 권리의 고마움도 모르는 듯한 저 사람들에게 질투가 난다는 거요."], ["Now it would be different if Frederick claimed her.”", "프레더릭이 그 애를 제 것이라 한다면 이야기가 다르겠지만.”"]], [["“Frederick!” exclaimed Mr.", "“프레더릭이라니!”"], ["Thornton. “Who is he?", "손턴 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["What right——?”", "“그게 누굽니까?"], ["He stopped short in his vehement question.", "무슨 권리로——” 그는 격해진 물음을 뚝 끊었다."]], [["“Frederick,” said Mr.", "“프레더릭 말일세.”"], ["Bell in surprise.", "벨 씨가 놀라며 말했다."], ["“Why, don’t you know?", "“아니, 자네 모르나?"], ["He’s her brother.", "그 애 오라비일세."], ["Have you not heard——”", "못 들었단 말인가——”"]], [["“I never heard his name before.", "“그런 이름은 처음 듣습니다."], ["Where is he?", "어디 있습니까?"], ["Who is he?”", "어떤 사람입니까?”"]], [["“Surely I told you about him, when the family first came to Milton—the son who was concerned in that mutiny.”", "“그 집안이 처음 밀턴에 왔을 때 내가 분명 그 아이 이야기를 했을 텐데—그 반란에 연루된 아들 말이야.”"]], [["“I never heard of him till this moment.", "“이 순간까지 한 번도 들은 적이 없습니다."], ["Where does he live?”", "어디 삽니까?”"]], [["“In Spain.", "“스페인일세."], ["He’s liable to be arrested the moment he sets foot on English ground.", "영국 땅에 발을 딛는 순간 체포당할 몸이지."], ["Poor fellow!", "딱한 녀석!"], ["he will grieve at not being able to attend his father’s funeral.", "제 아비 장례에 참석할 수 없으니 얼마나 애가 타겠나."], ["We must be content with Captain Lennox; for I don’t know of any other relation.”", "우리로서는 레녹스 대위로 만족하는 수밖에. 달리 친척이라고는 아는 이가 없으니.”"]], [["“I hope I may be allowed to go?”", "“저도 참석해도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly; thankfully.", "“물론이지, 고마운 일일세."], ["You’re a good fellow, after all, Thornton.", "자네도 알고 보면 좋은 사람이야, 손턴."], ["Hale liked you.", "헤일이 자네를 좋아했네."], ["He spoke to me, only the other day, at Oxford.", "요전 날에도 옥스퍼드에서 내게 자네 이야기를 하더군."], ["He regretted he had seen so little of you lately.", "요즘 자네를 너무 못 봐서 아쉽다고 말이야."], ["I am obliged to you for wishing to show him respect.”", "그를 예우하겠다니 나로서는 고맙네.”"]], [["“But about Frederick.", "“그런데 프레더릭 말입니다."], ["Does he never come to England?”", "그는 영국에 한 번도 오지 않았습니까?”"]], [["“Never.”", "“한 번도.”"]], [["“He was not over here about the time of Mrs. Hale’s death?”", "“헤일 부인이 돌아가실 무렵에도 이곳에 와 있지 않았습니까?”"]], [["“No.", "“아닐세."], ["Why, I was here then.", "아니, 그때는 내가 여기 있었네."], ["I hadn’t seen Hale for years and years: and, if you remember, I came—No, it was some time after that that I came.", "몇 해, 아니 여러 해 동안 헤일을 못 보다가, 자네도 기억하겠지만 내가 온 게—아니, 내가 온 건 그로부터 한참 뒤였지."], ["But poor Frederick Hale was not here then.", "아무튼 가엾은 프레더릭 헤일은 그때 여기 없었네."], ["What made you think he was?”", "왜 그가 있었다고 생각했나?”"]], [["“I saw a young man walking with Miss Hale one day,”", "“어느 날 한 젊은이가 헤일 양과 걷고 있는 것을 보았습니다.”"], ["replied Mr.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["Thornton, “and I think it was about that time.”", "“그리고 그게 그 무렵이었던 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“Oh, that would be this young Lennox, the Captain’s brother.", "“아, 그건 그 젊은 레녹스일 걸세. 대위의 동생 말이야."], ["He’s a lawyer, and they were in pretty constant correspondence with him; and I remember Mr.", "변호사인데, 그 집안과 꽤 꾸준히 편지를 주고받았지."], ["Hale told me he thought he would come down.", "헤일 씨가 그 사람이 내려올 것 같다고 하던 게 기억나는군."], ["Do you know,” said Mr.", "그런데 말일세,”"], ["Bell, wheeling round, and shutting one eye, the better to bring the forces of the other to bear with keen scrutiny on Mr.", "벨 씨는 몸을 홱 돌리고 한쪽 눈을 감았다. 다른 쪽 눈의 힘을 온통 모아 손턴 씨의 얼굴을 날카롭게 살피기 위해서였다."], ["Thornton’s face, “that I once fancied you had a little tenderness for Margaret?”", "“한때 나는 자네가 마거릿에게 조금은 마음이 있는 줄로 여겼다네.”"]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["No change of countenance.", "낯빛에도 아무 변화가 없었다."]], [["“And so did poor Hale.", "“가엾은 헤일도 그렇게 여겼지."], ["Not at first, and not till I had put it into his head.”", "처음부터는 아니고, 내가 그 생각을 심어 준 뒤에야 그랬지만.”"]], [["“I admired Miss Hale. Every one must do so.", "“저는 헤일 양을 흠모했습니다."], ["She is a beautiful creature,” said Mr.", "누구라도 그럴 수밖에 없지요."], ["Thornton, driven to bay by Mr.", "아름다운 사람이니까요.”"], ["Bell’s pertinacious questioning.", "벨 씨의 집요한 물음에 몰린 손턴 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Is that all!", "“그게 다인가!"], ["You can speak of her in that measured way, as simply a ‘beautiful creature’—only something to catch the eye.", "자네는 그 애를 그런 잰 듯한 말투로, 그저 ‘아름다운 사람’이라고—눈길이나 끄는 무엇이라고 말할 수 있단 말이지."], ["I did hope you had had nobleness enough in you to make you pay her the homage of the heart.", "나는 자네에게 마음의 경의를 바칠 만한 고귀함이 있기를 정말로 바랐네."], ["Though I believe—in fact I know, she would have rejected you, still to have loved her without return would have lifted you higher than all those, be they who they may, that have ever known her to love.", "하기야 나는 믿네—아니, 사실 알고 있네, 그 애가 자네를 거절했으리라는 걸. 그렇더라도 보답 없이 그 애를 사랑했다면, 여태 그 애를 사랑해 본 이가 누구든 간에 그 모든 이들보다 자네가 더 높은 곳에 올랐을 걸세."], ["‘Beautiful creature’ indeed!", "‘아름다운 사람’이라니, 원!"], ["Do you speak of her as you would of a horse or a dog?”", "자네는 말이나 개를 두고 말하듯 그 애를 말하는 겐가?”"]], [["Mr. Thornton’s eyes glowed like red embers.", "손턴 씨의 두 눈이 벌겋게 달아오른 잉걸불처럼 이글거렸다."]], [["“Mr. Bell,”", "“벨 씨,”"], ["said he, “before you speak so, you should remember that all men are not as free to express what they feel as you are.", "그가 말했다. “그렇게 말씀하시기 전에, 세상 모든 사람이 어르신처럼 제 감정을 자유로이 드러낼 수 있는 것은 아니라는 점을 기억해 주셔야 합니다."], ["Let us talk of something else.”", "다른 이야기를 하시지요.”"], ["For though his heart leaped up, as at a trumpet-call, to every word that Mr. Bell had said, and though he knew that what he had said would henceforward bind the thought of the old Oxford Fellow closely up with the most precious things of his heart, yet he would not be forced into any expression of what he felt towards Margaret.", "벨 씨가 한 말 한마디 한마디에 나팔 소리를 들은 듯 그의 가슴이 뛰어올랐고, 방금 들은 그 말들이 앞으로 저 늙은 옥스퍼드 펠로에 대한 생각을 제 마음속 가장 귀한 것들과 단단히 묶어 놓으리라는 것을 알았지만, 그럼에도 그는 마거릿을 향한 제 감정을 어떤 말로도 억지로 드러내려 하지 않았다."], ["He was no mocking-bird of praise to try, because another extolled what he reverenced and passionately loved, to outdo him in laudation.", "그는 남이 제가 우러르고 애타게 사랑하는 것을 치켜세운다고 해서 그보다 더 크게 칭송하려 드는, 남의 찬사나 흉내 내는 앵무새가 아니었다."], ["So he turned to some of the dry matters of business that lay between Mr. Bell and him, as landlord and tenant.", "그리하여 그는 지주와 세입자로서 벨 씨와 자기 사이에 놓인 무미건조한 사업 이야기로 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“What is that heap of brick and mortar we came against in the yard?", "“마당에서 부딪칠 뻔한 저 벽돌하고 회 반죽 더미는 뭔가?"], ["Any repairs wanted?”", "수리할 데라도 있나?”"]], [["“No, none, thank you.”", "“아닙니다, 없습니다. 고맙습니다.”"]], [["“Are you building on your own account?", "“자네 돈으로 무얼 짓고 있는 건가?"], ["If you are, I’m very much obliged to you.”", "그렇다면 나야 아주 고마운 일이지.”"]], [["“I’m building a dining-room—for the men I mean—the hands.”", "“식당을 짓고 있습니다—사람들을 위해서요, 그러니까 직공들 말입니다.”"]], [["“I thought you were hard to please, if this room wasn’t good enough to satisfy you, a bachelor.”", "“독신 사내인 자네한테 이 방으로도 성에 차지 않는다면 자네 입맛이 여간 까다로운 게 아니라고 생각하던 참이었네.”"]], [["“I’ve got acquainted with a strange kind of chap, and I put one or two children in whom he is interested to school.", "“제가 좀 별난 친구 하나를 알게 되었는데, 그 사람이 마음을 쓰는 아이 한둘을 학교에 넣어 주었습니다. 그래서 어느 날 그 집 근처를 지나는 길에 얼마 안 되는 돈을 치를 일이 있어 들렀지요."], ["So, as I happened to be passing near his house one day, I just went there about some trifling payment to be made; and I saw such a miserable black frizzle of a dinner—a greasy cinder of meat, as first set me a-thinking.", "그런데 시커멓게 타 버린 초라한 저녁 식사를—기름 덩어리처럼 눌어붙은 고기 한 점을 보고서 처음으로 생각이란 걸 하게 되었습니다. 그러나 이번 겨울에 식료품값이 그토록 뛰고 나서야, 물건을 도매로 사들이고 많은 양을 한꺼번에 조리하면 돈도 많이 아끼고 편의도 크게 얻을 수 있겠다는 데에 생각이 미쳤습니다."], ["But it was not till provisions grew so high this winter that I bethought me how, by buying things wholesale, and cooking a good quantity of provisions together, much money might be saved, and much comfort gained.", "그래서 제 친구—아니 제 적이라고 해야 하나—아까 말씀드린 그 사람에게 이야기를 꺼냈더니, 제 계획의 세세한 대목마다 트집을 잡더군요. 그래서 저는 그 계획을 접어 두었습니다."], ["So I spoke to my friend—or my enemy—the man I told you of—and he found fault with every detail of my plan; and in consequence I laid it aside, both as impracticable, and also because if I forced it into operation I should be interfering with the independence of my men; when, suddenly, this Higgins came to me and graciously signified his approval of a scheme so nearly the same as mine, that I might fairly have claimed it; and, moreover, the approval of several of his fellow-workmen, to whom he had spoken.", "실행이 어렵기도 하거니와, 억지로 밀어붙인다면 제 사람들의 독립성을 침해하는 셈이 될 테니까요. 그런데 느닷없이 이 히긴스라는 사람이 저를 찾아와서는, 제 것이라고 주장해도 좋을 만큼 제 것과 거의 똑같은 계획을 황송하게도 승인해 주더군요."], ["I was a little ‘riled,’ I confess, by his manner, and thought of throwing the whole thing overboard to sink or swim.", "게다가 자기가 이야기해 둔 동료 직공 여럿의 승인까지 말입니다. 솔직히 그 태도에 좀 ‘부아가’ 나서, 죄다 물속에 내던져 뜨든 가라앉든 내버려 둘까 하는 생각도 했습니다."], ["But it seemed childish to relinquish a plan which I had once thought wise and well-laid, just because I myself did not receive all the honour and consequence due to the originator.", "하지만 한때 현명하고 잘 짜였다고 여겼던 계획을, 단지 그것을 처음 낸 사람으로서 마땅한 영예와 대접을 제가 다 받지 못한다는 이유로 내던지는 건 어린애 같은 짓 같았습니다."], ["So I coolly took the part assigned to me, which is something like that of a steward to a club.", "그래서 저는 제게 맡겨진 몫을 담담히 떠맡았지요. 클럽의 관리인쯤 되는 자리랄까요."], ["I buy in the provisions wholesale, and provide a fitting matron or cook.”", "저는 식료품을 도매로 사들이고, 알맞은 관리인이나 요리사를 구해 주는 겁니다.”"]], [["“I hope you give satisfaction in your new capacity.", "“새 직분에서도 만족스러운 평판을 얻고 있기를 바라네."], ["Are you a good judge of potatoes and onions?", "자네가 감자와 양파를 볼 줄은 아는가?"], ["But I suppose Mrs. Thornton assists you in your marketing.”", "하기야 장 보는 일이야 손턴 부인이 거들어 주시겠지.”"]], [["“Not a bit,”", "“전혀 아닙니다.”"], ["replied Mr. Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 대답했다."], ["“She disapproves of the whole plan, and now we never mention it to each other.", "“어머니는 그 계획 전체를 못마땅해하시고, 이제 우리는 서로 그 이야기를 입에 올리지도 않습니다."], ["But I manage pretty well, getting in great stocks from Liverpool, and being served in butcher’s meat by our own family butcher.", "하지만 저는 꽤 잘해 나가고 있어요. 리버풀에서 물건을 대량으로 들여오고, 정육은 우리 집에 대는 푸줏간에서 받고 있습니다."], ["I can assure you, the hot dinners the matron turns out are not to be despised.”", "장담하는데, 그 관리인이 내놓는 따뜻한 밥상은 얕볼 만한 것이 아닙니다.”"]], [["“Do you taste each dish as it goes in, in virtue of your office?", "“직분에 걸맞게 들어가는 요리마다 맛을 보나?"], ["I hope you have a white wand.”", "흰 지팡이라도 하나 짚었으면 좋겠구먼.”"]], [["“I was very scrupulous, at first, in confining myself to the mere purchasing part, and even in that I rather obeyed the men’s orders, conveyed through the housekeeper, than went by my own judgment.", "“처음에는 아주 조심스럽게 물건 사는 일에만 저를 한정했고, 그것마저도 제 판단대로 하기보다는 가정부를 통해 전해지는 사람들의 주문에 따랐습니다. 어떤 때는 쇠고기가 너무 크다고 하고, 또 어떤 때는 양고기에 기름기가 모자란다고 하더군요."], ["At one time, the beef was too large, at another the mutton was not fat enough.", "제가 그들을 자유롭게 두려고, 제 생각을 그들에게 들이밀지 않으려고 얼마나 조심하는지 그들도 알아보았던 모양입니다."], ["I think they saw how careful I was to leave them free, and not to intrude my own ideas upon them; so, one day, two or three of the men—my friend Higgins among them—asked me if I would not come in and take a snack.", "그래서 어느 날 두세 사람이—그중에는 제 친구 히긴스도 있었지요—저에게 들어와서 한술 뜨지 않겠느냐고 묻더군요. 무척 바쁜 날이었지만, 그들이 먼저 다가온 마당에 제가 절반쯤이라도 마주 나아가지 않으면 마음이 상하겠구나 싶어서 들어갔습니다."], ["It was a very busy day, but I saw that the men would be hurt if, after making the advance, I didn’t meet them half-way, so I went in, and I never made a better dinner in my life.", "그리고 평생 그보다 좋은 식사를 해 본 적이 없습니다. 저는 그들에게—그러니까 제 양옆에 앉은 사람들에게 말이지요, 저는 연설 같은 건 못하니까요—얼마나 맛있게 먹었는지 이야기했습니다."], ["I told them (my next neighbours I mean, for I’m no speech-maker) how much I enjoyed it; and for some time, whenever that especial dinner recurred in their dietary, I was sure to be met by these men, with a ‘Master, there’s hot-pot for dinner to-day, win yo’ come in?’ If they had not asked me, I would no more have intruded on them than I’d have gone to the mess at the barracks without invitation.”", "그 뒤로 한동안은 그 특별한 요리가 식단에 오르는 날이면 어김없이 그 사람들이 저를 붙들고 이렇게 말하곤 했지요. ‘공장주님, 오늘 저녁은 핫포트인데 들어오시려우?’ 그들이 청하지 않았다면, 저는 초대도 없이 병영 식당에 끼어드는 것만큼이나 그들 자리에 끼어들 생각이 없었습니다.”"]], [["“I should think you were rather a restraint on your hosts’ conversation.", "“자네는 자네를 부른 사람들의 이야기판에 꽤나 걸림돌이 되겠구먼."], ["They can’t abuse the masters while you’re there.", "자네가 있으면 공장주들 흉을 볼 수가 없으니 말이야."], ["I expect they take it out on non-hot-pot days.”", "아마 핫포트 없는 날에 그 몫까지 실컷 풀어놓을 걸세.”"]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄요!"], ["hitherto we’ve steered clear of all vexed questions.", "지금까지는 말썽 나는 문제들은 다 비껴갔습니다."], ["But if any of the old disputes came up again, I would certainly speak out my mind next hot-pot day.", "하지만 옛날의 그 다툼거리가 다시 나온다면, 다음 핫포트 날에는 저도 분명 제 생각을 털어놓을 겁니다."], ["But you are hardly acquainted with our Darkshire fellows, for all you’re a Darkshire man yourself.", "그런데 어르신은 다크셔 사람이면서도 우리 다크셔 사내들을 잘 모르시는군요."], ["They have such a sense of humour, and such a racy mode of expression!", "그들에게는 대단한 해학이 있고, 얼마나 구수한 말솜씨가 있는지 모릅니다!"], ["I am getting really to know some of them now, and they talk pretty freely before me.”", "이제야 저도 몇 사람을 정말로 알아 가고 있는데, 그들도 제 앞에서 꽤 거리낌 없이 이야기합니다.”"]], [["“Nothing like the act of eating for equalising men.", "“사람을 고르게 만드는 데는 먹는 일만 한 것이 없지."], ["Dying is nothing to it.", "죽는 일도 거기에는 못 미치네."], ["The philosopher dies sententiously—the pharisee ostentatiously—the simple-hearted humbly—the poor idiot blindly, as the sparrow falls to the ground; the philosopher and idiot, publican and pharisee, all eat after the same fashion—given an equally good digestion.", "철학자는 잠언을 읊으며 죽고, 바리새인은 남의 눈을 의식하며 죽고, 마음이 단순한 자는 겸손하게 죽고, 가엾은 바보는 참새가 땅에 떨어지듯 아무것도 모른 채 죽지. 그러나 철학자든 바보든, 세리든 바리새인이든 소화력만 똑같이 좋다면 먹는 모양새는 다 한가지라네."], ["There’s theory for theory for you!”", "자네 이론에 맞서는 내 이론일세!”"]], [["“Indeed I have no theory; I hate theories.”", "“저는 정말이지 이론이라고는 없습니다. 저는 이론이라면 질색입니다.”"]], [["“I beg your pardon.", "“미안하네."], ["To show my penitence, will you accept a ten pound note towards your marketing, and give the poor fellows a feast?”", "뉘우치는 뜻으로, 장 보는 데 보태라고 10파운드 지폐 한 장을 받아 주겠나? 그 딱한 사람들에게 잔치라도 한번 베풀어 주게.”"]], [["“Thank you; but I’d rather not.", "“고맙습니다만, 사양하겠습니다."], ["They pay me rent for the oven and cooking-places at the back of the mill: and will have to pay more for the new dining-room.", "그들은 공장 뒤편의 화덕과 조리 시설에 대해 제게 세를 내고 있고, 새 식당에 대해서는 더 많이 내야 할 겁니다."], ["I don’t want it to fall into a charity.", "저는 이 일이 자선으로 굴러떨어지는 걸 원하지 않습니다."], ["I don’t want donations.", "기부도 원하지 않습니다."], ["Once let in the principle, and I should have people going and talking, and spoiling the simplicity of the whole thing.”", "한번 그 원칙을 들이면, 사람들이 드나들며 이러쿵저러쿵 말을 만들어 이 일의 단순함을 망쳐 놓을 테니까요.”"]], [["“People will talk about any new plan.", "“새로운 일에는 무슨 일이든 사람들이 말을 하는 법일세."], ["You can’t help that.”", "그건 어쩔 수 없어.”"]], [["“My enemies, if I have any, may make a philanthropic fuss about this dinner-scheme; but you are a friend, and I expect that you will now pay my experiment the respect of silence.", "“제게 적이 있다면, 그들이야 이 식사 계획을 두고 박애니 뭐니 하며 소란을 피울 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 어르신은 벗이시니, 이제부터는 제 시도에 침묵이라는 예우를 베풀어 주시리라 기대합니다."], ["It is but a new broom at present, and sweeps clean enough.", "아직은 새 빗자루라 제법 잘 쓸릴 뿐입니다."], ["But by-an-bye we shall meet with plenty of stumbling-blocks, no doubt.”", "하지만 머지않아 걸림돌을 숱하게 만나게 되겠지요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII", "# 제43장"]], [["“The meanest thing to which we bid adieu, Loses its meanness in the parting hour.”", "“우리가 작별을 고하는 가장 하찮은 것도 헤어지는 그 시각에는 그 하찮음을 잃는다.”"], ["ELLIOTT.", "— 엘리엇."]], [["Mrs. Shaw took as vehement a dislike as it was possible for one of her gentle nature to do, against Milton.", "쇼 부인은 그 온순한 천성으로 지을 수 있는 한 가장 격렬한 반감을 밀턴에 품었다."], ["It was noisy, and smoky, and the poor people whom she saw in the streets were dirty, and the rich ladies over-dressed, and not a man that she saw, high or low, had his clothes made to fit him.", "밀턴은 시끄럽고 매캐했으며, 거리에서 보이는 가난한 사람들은 꾀죄죄했고 부유한 부인들은 지나치게 차려입었으며, 위아래를 막론하고 그녀가 본 남자들 가운데 제 몸에 맞게 지은 옷을 걸친 이가 하나도 없었다. 마거릿이 밀턴에 머무는 한 잃어버린 기력을 결코 되찾지 못하리라고 그녀는 확신했다."], ["She was sure Margaret would never regain her lost strength while she stayed in Milton; and she herself was afraid of one of her old attacks of the nerves.", "게다가 자기도 예전의 신경 발작이 도질까 겁이 났다. 마거릿은 자기와 함께 돌아가야 한다, 그것도 서둘러."], ["Margaret must return with her, and that quickly.", "그녀가 쓴 말이 정확히 그러하지는 않았더라도 마거릿에게 채근한 바의 정신은 적어도 그러했다."], ["This, if not the exact force of her words, was at any rate the spirit of what she urged on Margaret, till the latter, weak, weary, and broken-spirited, yielded a reluctant promise that, as soon as Wednesday was over, she would prepare to accompany her aunt back to town, leaving Dixon in charge of all the arrangements for paying bills, disposing of furniture, and shutting up the house.", "그리하여 마거릿은 쇠약하고 지치고 기가 꺾인 채, 수요일만 지나면 이모를 따라 런던으로 갈 채비를 하겠노라고 마지못한 약속을 내주었다. 청구서를 치르고 가구를 처분하고 집을 닫아거는 온갖 뒤처리는 딕슨에게 맡기기로 하고."], ["Before that Wednesday—that mournful Wednesday, when Mr.", "그 수요일이 오기 전에—헤일 씨가 생전에 알던 두 집 어느 쪽에서도 멀리 떨어진 곳에, 낯선 이들 틈에 홀로 누운 아내에게서도 멀리 떨어진 곳에 묻히게 될 그 서글픈 수요일이 오기 전에(그리고 이 마지막 대목이 마거릿의 큰 괴로움이었다."], ["Hale was to be interred, far away from either of the homes he had known in life, and far away from the wife who lay lonely among strangers (and this last was Margaret’s great trouble, for she thought that if she had not given way to that overwhelming stupor during the first sad days, she could have arranged things otherwise)—before that Wednesday, Margaret received a letter from Mr. Bell.", "처음 며칠의 그 압도적인 망연자실에 몸을 내맡기지만 않았더라면 일을 달리 처리할 수도 있었으리라 여겼기 때문이다)—마거릿은 벨 씨에게서 편지 한 통을 받았다."]], [["“MY DEAR MARGARET:—I did mean to have returned to Milton on Thursday, but unluckily it turns out to be one of the rare occasions when we, Plymouth Fellows, are called upon to perform any kind of duty, and I must not be absent from my post.", "“친애하는 마거릿에게—나는 목요일에 밀턴으로 돌아갈 작정이었네만, 공교롭게도 우리 플리머스 펠로들이 무슨 직무라도 수행하라고 불려 나가는 드문 경우 가운데 하나가 하필 그때라, 자리를 비울 수가 없게 되었네."], ["Captain Lennox and Mr. Thornton are here.", "레녹스 대위와 손턴 씨가 여기 와 있네."], ["The former seems a smart, well-meaning man; and has proposed to go over to Milton, and assist you in any search for the will; of course there is none, or you would have found it by this time, if you followed my directions.", "앞사람은 영리하고 선의를 지닌 사람으로 보이는데, 밀턴으로 건너가 유언장을 찾는 일이라면 무엇이든 도와주겠다고 나섰네. 물론 유언장은 없네."], ["Then the Captain declares he must take you and his mother-in-law home; and, in his wife’s present state, I don’t see how you can expect him to remain away longer than Friday.", "있었다면 내가 일러 준 대로 했을 테니 지금쯤 벌써 찾아냈을 걸세. 그러고는 대위가 자네와 장모를 집으로 데려가야 한다고 우기는데, 아내가 지금 저런 형편이니 그가 금요일보다 더 오래 떠나 있으리라고는 기대하기 어렵겠지."], ["However, that Dixon of yours is trusty; and can hold her, or your own, till I come.", "하지만 자네의 그 딕슨은 믿을 만하니, 내가 갈 때까지 제 자리든 자네 자리든 지켜 줄 수 있을 걸세."], ["I will put matters into the hands of my Milton attorney if there is no will; for I doubt this smart Captain is no great man of business.", "유언장이 없다면 밀턴의 내 변호사에게 일을 넘기겠네. 이 영리한 대위가 사무에 밝은 사람 같지는 않으니 말일세."], ["Nevertheless, his moustachios are splendid.", "그래도 콧수염 하나는 훌륭하더군."], ["There will have to be a sale, so select what things you wish to be reserved.", "경매를 붙여야 할 테니 남겨 두고 싶은 물건을 골라 두게."], ["Or you can send a list afterwards.", "아니면 나중에 목록을 보내도 되네."], ["Now two things more, and I have done.", "이제 두 가지만 더 말하면 내 할 말은 끝이네."], ["You know, or if you don’t, your poor father did, that you are to have my money and goods when I die.", "자네도 알 테고, 모른다 해도 자네의 가엾은 아버지는 알고 있었네만, 내가 죽으면 내 돈과 재물은 자네 것이 되네."], ["Not that I mean to die yet; but I name this just to explain what is coming.", "아직 죽을 작정이라는 말은 아닐세. 다만 앞으로 할 말을 설명하려고 짚어 두는 걸세."], ["These Lennoxes seem very fond of you now; and perhaps may continue to be; perhaps not.", "저 레녹스 사람들은 지금 자네를 무척 아끼는 듯하고, 앞으로도 그럴지 모르지. 아닐 수도 있고."], ["So it is best to start with a formal agreement; namely, that you are to pay to them two hundred and fifty pounds a year, as long as you and they find it pleasant to live together.", "그러니 처음부터 격식을 갖춘 약조로 시작하는 편이 낫네. 곧, 자네와 그들이 함께 사는 것을 서로 좋게 여기는 동안 자네가 그들에게 해마다 250파운드를 치르는 걸세."], ["(This, of course, includes Dixon; mind you don’t be cajoled into paying more for her.) Then you won’t be thrown adrift, if some day the captain wishes to have his house to himself, but you can carry yourself and your two hundred and fifty pounds off somewhere else; if, indeed, I have not claimed you to come and keep house for me first.", "(물론 여기에는 딕슨 몫도 들었네. 그 사람 몫으로 더 내라는 말에 넘어가지 않도록 조심하게.) 그러면 언젠가 대위가 제 집을 저희끼리만 쓰고 싶어 하더라도 자네가 내쫓기는 신세가 되지 않고, 자네 몸과 자네의 250파운드를 챙겨 다른 데로 옮겨 갈 수 있네."], ["Then as to dress, and Dixon, and personal expenses, and confectionery (all young ladies eat confectionery till wisdom comes by age), I shall consult some lady of my acquaintance, and see how much you will have from your father before fixing this.", "하기야 그 전에 내가 먼저 와서 살림을 맡아 달라고 자네를 데려가지 않는다면 말일세. 그다음 옷이며 딕슨이며 개인 비용이며 과자 값은(젊은 아가씨는 나이 들어 철이 들 때까지 다들 과자를 먹는 법이지) 내가 아는 부인 한 분께 여쭈어 보고, 자네가 아버지에게서 얼마나 받게 될지 알아본 뒤에 정하겠네."], ["Now, Margaret, have you flown out before you have read this far, and wondered what right the old man has to settle your affairs for you so cavalierly?", "자, 마거릿, 여기까지 읽기도 전에 발끈해서 저 늙은이가 무슨 권리로 이렇게 함부로 남의 일을 정해 주느냐고 따지지는 않았는가?"], ["I make no doubt you have.", "그랬으리라고 나는 믿어 의심치 않네."], ["Yet the old man has a right.", "그러나 늙은이에게는 권리가 있네."], ["He has loved your father for five and thirty years; he stood beside him on his wedding-day; he closed his eyes in death.", "그는 자네 아버지를 35년 동안 사랑했고, 혼례날 그의 곁에 섰으며, 죽음의 자리에서 그의 눈을 감겨 주었네."], ["Moreover, he is your godfather; and as he cannot do you much good spiritually, having a hidden consciousness of your superiority in such things, he would fain do you the poor good of endowing you materially.", "게다가 그는 자네의 대부일세. 그리고 그런 일에서는 자네가 자기보다 낫다는 것을 속으로 알고 있어 영적으로는 자네에게 크게 보탬이 될 수 없으니, 물질로나마 자네를 넉넉하게 해 주는 변변찮은 선행이라도 기꺼이 하고 싶은 걸세."], ["And the old man has not a known relation on earth; ‘who is there to mourn for Adam Bell?’ and his whole heart is set and bent upon this one thing, and Margaret Hale is not the girl to say him nay.", "게다가 늙은이에게는 이 세상에 알려진 친척이 하나도 없네. ‘애덤 벨을 위해 슬퍼해 줄 이가 어디 있는가?’ 그의 온 마음이 이 한 가지에 붙박여 기울어 있으니, 마거릿 헤일은 그에게 아니라고 말할 아가씨가 아닐세."], ["Write by return if only two lines, to tell me your answer.", "답장은 곧바로 부치게. 단 두 줄이라도 좋으니 자네 답을 알려 주게."], ["But no thanks.”", "다만 고맙다는 말은 말고.”"]], [["Margaret took up a pen and scrawled with trembling hand, “Margaret Hale is not the girl to say him nay.”", "마거릿은 펜을 들어 떨리는 손으로 “마거릿 헤일은 그에게 아니라고 말할 아가씨가 아닙니다.”라고 갈겨썼다."], ["In her weak state she could not think of any other words, and yet, she was vexed to use these.", "쇠약한 상태라 다른 말을 떠올릴 수가 없었지만, 그래도 이 말을 쓰는 것이 언짢았다."], ["But she was so much fatigued even by this slight exertion, that if she could have thought of another form of acceptance, she could not have sate up to write a syllable of it.", "그러나 이 사소한 수고만으로도 어찌나 지쳤던지, 설령 다른 형태의 승낙을 생각해 낼 수 있었더라도 그것을 한 자라도 쓰려고 몸을 일으켜 앉아 있지는 못했을 것이다."], ["She was obliged to lie down again and try not to think.", "그녀는 다시 자리에 누워 아무 생각도 하지 않으려 애쓸 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“My dearest child!", "“내 사랑하는 아가!"], ["Has that letter vexed or troubled you?”", "그 편지가 널 언짢게 하거나 속상하게 하더냐?”"]], [["“No!” said Margaret feebly.", "“아니에요!”"], ["“I shall be better when to-morrow is over.”", "마거릿이 힘없이 말했다. “내일만 지나면 나아질 거예요.”"]], [["“I feel sure, darling, you won’t be better till I get you out of this horrid air.", "“아무래도 이 끔찍한 공기에서 널 데리고 나가기 전에는 나아지지 않을 게 틀림없다, 얘야."], ["How you can have borne it these two years I can’t imagine.”", "이 년 동안 어떻게 견뎠는지 나는 짐작도 안 간다.”"]], [["“Where could I go to?", "“제가 어디로 갈 수 있었겠어요?"], ["I could not leave papa and mamma.”", "아버지 어머니를 두고 떠날 수는 없었잖아요.”"]], [["“Well, don’t distress yourself, my dear.", "“그래, 속상해하지 마라, 얘야."], ["I dare say it was all for the best, only I had no conception of how you were living.", "다 그게 최선이었겠지. 다만 네가 어떻게 살고 있는지 나는 짐작도 못 했구나."], ["Our butler’s wife lives in a better house than this.”", "우리 집사 아내도 이보다는 나은 집에 산단다.”"]], [["“It is sometimes very pretty—in summer; you can’t judge by what it is now.", "“여기도 가끔은 아주 예뻐요—여름이면요. 지금 모습만 보고 판단하실 수는 없어요."], ["I have been very happy here,”", "저는 여기서 아주 행복했어요.”"], ["and Margaret closed her eyes by way of stopping the conversation.", "그러고서 마거릿은 이야기를 그만두려는 뜻으로 눈을 감았다."]], [["The house teemed with comfort now, compared to what it had been.", "집은 예전에 견주면 이제 안락함으로 넘쳐 났다."], ["The evenings were chilly, and by Mrs. Shaw’s directions fires were lighted in every bedroom.", "저녁이면 쌀쌀했으므로 쇼 부인의 지시로 침실마다 불이 지펴졌다."], ["She petted Margaret in every possible way, and bought every delicacy, or soft luxury in which she herself would have burrowed and sought comfort.", "그녀는 온갖 방법으로 마거릿을 어루만졌고, 자기 같으면 파묻혀 위안을 찾았을 진미며 보드라운 사치품을 죄다 사들였다."], ["But Margaret was indifferent to all these things; or, if they forced themselves upon her attention, it was simply as causes for gratitude to her aunt, who was putting herself so much out of the way to think of her.", "그러나 마거릿은 그 모든 것에 무심했다. 혹 그것들이 억지로 그녀의 눈에 들어오더라도, 그것은 오직 자기를 생각하느라 그토록 애를 쓰는 이모에게 고마움을 느낄 까닭이 될 뿐이었다."], ["She was restless, though so weak.", "그토록 쇠약한데도 그녀는 가만히 있지 못했다."], ["All the day long, she kept herself from thinking of the ceremony which was going on at Oxford, by wandering from room to room, and languidly setting aside such articles as she wished to retain.", "하루 종일 그녀는 옥스퍼드에서 치러지고 있을 예식을 생각하지 않으려고 이 방 저 방을 거닐며 간직하고 싶은 물건들을 힘없이 따로 골라 놓았다. 딕슨은 쇼 부인의 뜻에 따라 그녀를 좇아다녔다."], ["Dixon followed her by Mrs. Shaw’s desire, ostensibly to receive instructions, but with a private injunction to soothe her into repose as soon as might be.", "겉으로는 지시를 받으려는 것이었으나, 되도록 빨리 그녀를 달래어 쉬게 하라는 은밀한 분부를 받은 터였다."]], [["“These books, Dixon, I will keep.", "“이 책들은, 딕슨, 내가 간직할게요."], ["All the rest will you send to Mr. Bell?", "나머지는 전부 벨 씨께 보내 주겠어요?"], ["They are of a kind that he will value for themselves, as well as for papa’s sake.", "그분이 책 자체로도, 또 아버지를 생각해서도 소중히 여기실 만한 것들이에요."], ["This—— I should like you to take this to Mr. Thornton, after I am gone.", "이건—— 내가 떠난 뒤에 이걸 손턴 씨께 가져다 드렸으면 해요."], ["Stay; I will write a note with it.”", "잠깐만요. 쪽지를 함께 써 둘게요.”"], ["And she sate down hastily, as if afraid of thinking, and wrote:", "그러고서 그녀는 생각하기가 두려운 사람처럼 서둘러 앉아 이렇게 썼다."]], [["“DEAR SIR,—The accompanying book I am sure will be valued by you for the sake of my father, to whom it belonged.", "“선생님께—여기 함께 보내는 책은 제 아버지의 것이었으니, 아버지를 생각하셔서 선생님께서 소중히 여겨 주시리라 믿습니다."]], [["“Yours sincerely,", "“그럼 이만 줄입니다."]], [["She set out again upon her travels through the house, turning over articles, known to her from her childhood, with a sort of caressing reluctance to leave them—old-fashioned, worn and shabby, as they might be.", "그녀는 다시 집 안을 돌아다니기 시작해, 어린 시절부터 낯익은 물건들을 하나하나 뒤집어 보았다. 낡고 닳고 초라할지언정 그것들을 두고 떠나기가 아쉬워 쓰다듬듯 매만지면서."], ["But she hardly spoke again; and Dixon’s report to Mrs.", "그러나 그녀는 좀처럼 다시 입을 열지 않았다."], ["Shaw was, that “she doubted whether Miss Hale heard a word of what she said, though she talked the whole time, in order to divert her intention.”", "쇼 부인에게 올린 딕슨의 보고는 이러했다. 마음을 딴 데로 돌려 드리려고 내내 말을 걸었습니다만, 헤일 아가씨가 제 말을 한 마디라도 들으셨는지 모르겠습니다요, 하고."], ["The consequence of her being on her feet all day was excessive bodily weariness in the evening, and a better night’s rest than she had had since she heard of Mr. Hale’s death.", "하루 종일 서서 지낸 결과 저녁에는 몸이 지독히도 고단해졌고, 아버지의 부고를 들은 이래 가장 나은 밤잠을 잤다."]], [["At breakfast time the next day, she expressed her wish to go and bid one or two friends good-bye.", "이튿날 아침 식사 자리에서 그녀는 친구 한둘에게 작별 인사를 하러 가고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Mrs. Shaw objected:", "쇼 부인은 반대했다."]], [["“I am sure, my dear, you can have no friends here with whom you are sufficiently intimate to justify you in calling upon them so soon; before you have been at church.”", "“얘야, 이렇게 서둘러—교회에 한 번 나가 보기도 전에—찾아갈 만큼 가까운 벗이 여기에 네게 있을 리 없다고 나는 생각한다.”"]], [["“But to-day is my only day; if Captain Lennox comes this afternoon, and if we must—if I must really go to-morrow——”", "“하지만 오늘밖에 없는걸요. 레녹스 대위가 오늘 오후에 오고, 우리가 정말로—제가 정말로 내일 떠나야 한다면——”"]], [["“Oh, yes, we shall go to-morrow.", "“오, 그럼, 내일 떠나야지."], ["I am more and more convinced that this air is bad for you, and makes you look so pale and ill; besides, Edith expects us; and she may be waiting for me; and you cannot be left alone, my dear, at your age.", "이 공기가 네게 나쁘고 널 그렇게 창백하고 아파 보이게 만든다는 확신이 점점 굳어지는구나. 게다가 이디스가 우릴 기다리고, 나를 기다리고 있을 게다."], ["No; if you must make these calls, I will go with you.", "그리고 얘야, 네 나이에 널 혼자 둘 수는 없지. 아니다, 꼭 그 인사를 다녀야겠다면 내가 함께 가마."], ["Dixon can get us a coach, I suppose?”", "딕슨이 마차를 불러 줄 수 있겠지?”"]], [["So Mrs. Shaw went to take care of Margaret and took her maid with her to take care of the shawls and air-cushions.", "그리하여 쇼 부인은 마거릿을 돌보러 나섰고, 숄과 공기 방석을 챙길 하녀까지 데리고 갔다."], ["Margaret’s face was too sad to lighten up into a smile at all this preparation for paying two visits, that she had often made by herself at all hours of the day.", "마거릿이 하루 중 아무 때고 혼자서 곧잘 다녀오곤 하던 두 곳을 방문하는 데 이런 채비가 벌어지는데도, 그녀의 얼굴은 너무 슬퍼서 미소로도 밝아지지 않았다."], ["She was half afraid of owning that one place to which she was going was Nicholas Higgins’; all she could do was to hope her aunt would be indisposed to get out of the coach and walk up the court, and at every breath of wind have her face slapped by wet clothes, hanging out to dry on ropes stretched from house to house.", "가려는 곳 하나가 니컬러스 히긴스의 집이라는 것을 밝히기가 그녀는 반쯤 두려웠다. 이모가 마차에서 내려 그 골목을 걸어 올라가고, 바람이 불 때마다 집과 집 사이에 걸린 줄에 널린 젖은 빨래에 얼굴을 얻어맞는 일을 내키지 않아 하기를 바라는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["There was a little battle in Mrs.", "쇼 부인의 마음속에서는 편안함과 부인다운 체면 사이에 자그마한 싸움이 벌어졌으나 앞의 것이 이겼다."], ["Shaw’s mind between ease and a sense of matronly propriety; but the former gained the day; and with many an injunction to Margaret to be careful of herself, and not to catch any fever, such as was always lurking in such places, her aunt permitted her to go where she had often been before without taking any precaution or requiring any permission.", "그리하여 몸조심하라고, 그런 데는 늘 열병이 도사리고 있으니 옮지 않도록 하라고 마거릿에게 몇 번이고 당부한 끝에, 이모는 그녀가 아무런 조심도 하지 않고 아무 허락도 구하지 않은 채 곧잘 드나들던 곳에 가도록 허락해 주었다."]], [["Nicholas was out; only Mary and one or two of the Boucher children at home.", "니컬러스는 나가고 없었다. 집에는 메리와 바우처네 아이 한둘뿐이었다."], ["Margaret was vexed with herself for not having timed her visit better.", "마거릿은 방문 시간을 더 잘 맞추지 못한 것이 스스로 언짢았다."], ["Mary had a very blunt intellect, although her feelings were warm and kind; and the instant she understood what Margaret’s purpose was in coming to see them, she began to cry and sob with so little restraint that Margaret found it useless to say any of the thousand little things which had suggested themselves to her as she was coming along in the coach.", "메리는 마음이 따뜻하고 착하기는 해도 머리가 몹시 둔했다. 그리고 마거릿이 자기들을 보러 온 뜻이 무엇인지 알아차린 순간, 그녀는 어찌나 걷잡을 수 없이 울며 흐느끼기 시작했는지, 마거릿은 마차를 타고 오면서 떠올려 둔 자잘한 말 천 가지 가운데 어느 하나도 소용없다는 것을 알았다."], ["She could only try to comfort her a little by suggesting the vague chance of their meeting again, at some possible time, in some possible place, and bid her tell her father how much she wished, if he could manage it, that he should come to see her when he had done his work in the evening.", "그녀는 그저 언젠가 어느 곳에선가 다시 만날지도 모른다는 막연한 기대를 내비치며 조금이나마 위로하려 애쓸 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 아버지가 어떻게든 시간을 낼 수 있다면 저녁에 일을 마치고 자기를 보러 와 주기를 얼마나 바라는지 전해 달라고 일렀다."]], [["As she was leaving the place, she stopped and looked around; then hesitated a little before she said:", "그곳을 나서면서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 조금 망설이다가 말했다."]], [["“I should like to have some little thing to remind me of Bessy.”", "“베시를 기억할 만한 자그마한 물건 하나를 가졌으면 해요.”"]], [["Instantly Mary’s generosity was keenly alive.", "그 순간 메리의 인심이 대뜸 되살아났다."], ["What could they give?", "무엇을 드릴 수 있을까?"], ["And on Margaret’s singling out a little common drinking-cup, which she remembered as the one always standing by Bessy’s side with a drink for her feverish lips, Mary said:", "그리고 마거릿이 흔해빠진 자그마한 물잔 하나를 골라내자—열에 들뜬 베시의 입술을 적실 물을 담고 늘 그 곁에 놓여 있던 것으로 그녀가 기억하는 잔이었다—메리가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, take summat better; that only cost fourpence!”", "“아이고, 더 좋은 걸 가져가시우. 그건 4펜스밖에 안 하는 거요!”"]], [["“That will do, thank you,”", "“이거면 돼요, 고마워요.”"], ["said Margaret; and she went quickly away, while the light caused by the pleasure of having something to give yet lingered on Mary’s face.", "마거릿이 말했다. 그러고는 무언가 줄 것이 있다는 기쁨이 밝혀 놓은 빛이 아직 메리의 얼굴에 어른거리는 사이 서둘러 그곳을 떠났다."]], [["“Now to Mrs. Thornton’s,”", "“이제 손턴 부인 댁으로.”"], ["thought she to herself.", "그녀는 속으로 생각했다."], ["“It must be done.”", "“해야 할 일이야.”"], ["But she looked rather rigid and pale at the thoughts of it, and had hard work to find the exact words in which to explain to her aunt who Mrs. Thornton was, and why she should go to bid her farewell.", "그러나 그 생각에 그녀의 낯빛은 자못 굳고 창백해졌으며, 손턴 부인이 어떤 사람이고 왜 작별 인사를 하러 가야 하는지 이모에게 설명할 정확한 말을 찾느라 애를 먹었다."]], [["They (for Mrs. Shaw alighted here) were shown into the drawing-room, in which a fire had only just been kindled.", "두 사람은(쇼 부인도 여기서는 마차에서 내렸다) 응접실로 안내되었는데, 그곳에는 불이 이제 막 지펴진 참이었다."], ["Mrs. Shaw huddled herself up in her shawl and shivered.", "쇼 부인은 숄을 껴안고 몸을 웅크린 채 떨었다."]], [["“What an icy room!”", "“어쩌면 이렇게 얼음장 같은 방이 다 있담!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["They had to wait for some time before Mrs. Thornton entered.", "손턴 부인이 들어오기까지 두 사람은 한참을 기다려야 했다."], ["There was some softening in her heart towards Margaret, now that she was going away out of her sight.", "마거릿이 이제 자기 눈앞에서 떠나 버린다고 하니 그녀의 마음에도 다소 누그러진 데가 있었다."], ["She remembered her spirit, as shown at various times and places, even more than the patience with which she had endured long and wearing cares.", "그녀는 마거릿이 길고 고단한 근심을 견뎌 낸 인내보다도 이런저런 때와 자리에서 보여 준 그 기개를 더 또렷이 기억했다."], ["Her countenance was blander than usual, as she greeted her; there was even a shade of tenderness in her manner, as she noticed the white, tear-swollen face, and the quiver in the voice which Margaret tried to make so steady.", "그녀를 맞이하는 부인의 낯빛은 여느 때보다 부드러웠다. 눈물에 부어오른 창백한 얼굴과, 마거릿이 그토록 가다듬으려 애쓰는 목소리의 떨림을 알아차렸을 때는 그 태도에 다정함의 그림자마저 어렸다."]], [["“Allow me to introduce my aunt, Mrs. Shaw.", "“제 이모 쇼 부인을 소개해 드리겠습니다."], ["I am going away from Milton to-morrow; I do not know if you are aware of it, but I wanted to see you once again, Mrs.", "저는 내일 밀턴을 떠납니다. 알고 계신지 모르겠습니다만, 손턴 부인, 떠나기 전에 한 번 더 뵙고 싶었어요."], ["Thornton, to—to apologize for my manner the last time I saw you; and to say that I am sure you meant kindly—however much we may have misunderstood each other.”", "지난번에 뵈었을 때의 제 태도를 사과드리려고요. 그리고 우리가 아무리 서로를 오해했다 하더라도 부인께서 좋은 뜻으로 하신 일이라는 것을 제가 잘 안다고 말씀드리고 싶었습니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Shaw looked extremely perplexed by what Margaret had said.", "쇼 부인은 마거릿의 말에 몹시 어리둥절한 낯빛이었다."], ["Thanks for kindness!", "친절에 대한 감사라니!"], ["and apologies for failure in good manners!", "예의를 갖추지 못한 데 대한 사과라니!"], ["But Mrs. Thornton replied:", "그러나 손턴 부인은 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Miss Hale, I am glad you do me justice.", "“헤일 양, 나를 공정하게 보아 주니 기쁘군요."], ["I did no more than I believed to be my duty in remonstrating with you as I did.", "나는 그때 그렇게 충고한 것이 내 도리라고 믿었기에 그리했을 뿐, 그 이상은 하지 않았습니다."], ["I have always desired to act the part of a friend to you.", "나는 늘 아가씨에게 벗의 도리를 다하고자 했어요."], ["I am glad you do me justice.”", "나를 공정하게 보아 주니 기쁩니다.”"]], [["“And,”", "“그러면,” 마거릿이 말하면서 몹시 붉어졌다."], ["said Margaret, blushing excessively as she spoke, “will you do me justice, and believe that though I cannot—I do not choose—to give explanations of my conduct, I have not acted in the unbecoming way you apprehended?”", "“부인께서도 저를 공정하게 보아 주시겠어요? 제가 제 행동을 설명할 수 없다 해도—설명하지 않기로 한다 해도—부인께서 염려하신 그런 온당치 못한 처신을 하지는 않았다는 것을 믿어 주시겠어요?”"]], [["Margaret’s voice was so soft, and her eyes so pleading, that Mrs. Thornton was for once affected by the charm of manner to which she had hitherto proved herself invulnerable.", "마거릿의 목소리는 어찌나 부드럽고 그 눈은 어찌나 간절했던지, 여태껏 스스로 끄떡없음을 증명해 보이던 그 태도의 매력에 손턴 부인도 이번만은 마음이 움직였다."]], [["“Yes, I do believe you.", "“그래요, 믿습니다."], ["Let us say no more about it.", "그 일은 더 이야기하지 맙시다."], ["Where are you going to reside, Miss Hale?", "어디에 가서 살 겁니까, 헤일 양?"], ["I understood from Mr. Bell that you were going to leave Milton.", "벨 씨에게서 아가씨가 밀턴을 떠난다는 말은 들었어요."], ["You never liked Milton, you know,” said Mrs.", "아가씨는 밀턴을 좋아한 적이 없지요.”"], ["Thornton, with a sort of grim smile; “but for all that, you must not expect me to congratulate you on quitting it.", "손턴 부인이 어딘가 씁쓸한 미소를 띠며 말했다. “그렇더라도 밀턴을 떠나는 것을 내가 축하해 주리라고는 기대하지 마세요."], ["Where shall you live?”", "어디서 살게 됩니까?”"]], [["“With my aunt,”", "“이모와 함께요.”"], ["replied Margaret, turning towards Mrs. Shaw.", "마거릿이 쇼 부인 쪽으로 돌아서며 대답했다."]], [["“My niece will reside with me in Harley Street.", "“제 조카는 저와 함께 할리 가에서 살 거예요."], ["She is almost like a daughter to me,” said Mrs.", "저에게는 거의 딸이나 다름없답니다.”"], ["Shaw, looking fondly at Margaret; “and I am glad to acknowledge my own obligation for any kindness that has been shown to her.", "쇼 부인이 마거릿을 애틋하게 바라보며 말했다. “그리고 이 아이에게 베풀어 주신 어떤 친절에 대해서든 제가 신세를 졌다고 인정하게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["If you and your husband ever come to the town, my son and daughter, Captain and Mrs. Lennox, will, I am sure, join with me in wishing to do anything in our power to show you attention.”", "부인과 바깥어른께서 언제라도 런던에 오시게 되면, 제 아들딸인 레녹스 대위 내외도 저와 뜻을 같이해 두 분께 저희가 할 수 있는 예를 다하고자 할 것입니다.”"]], [["Mrs.", "손턴 부인은 속으로 생각했다."], ["Thornton thought in her own mind, that Margaret had not taken much care to enlighten her aunt as to the relationship between the Mr. and Mrs.", "저 귀부인 티 나는 이모가 부드러운 후의를 베풀고 있는 손턴 씨와 손턴 부인이 서로 어떤 사이인지, 마거릿이 이모에게 굳이 밝혀 두지는 않았구나, 하고."], ["Thornton, towards whom the fine-lady aunt was extending her soft patronage; so she answered shortly,", "그래서 그녀는 짧게 대답했다."]], [["“My husband is dead.", "“제 남편은 죽었습니다."], ["Mr. Thornton is my son.", "손턴 씨는 제 아들이고요."], ["I never go to London; so I am not likely to be able to avail myself of your polite offers.”", "저는 런던에 가는 일이 없으니 부인의 정중한 제안을 이용할 일도 없을 듯합니다.”"]], [["At this instant Mr.", "바로 그 순간 손턴 씨가 방에 들어왔다."], ["Thornton entered the room; he had only just returned from Oxford.", "옥스퍼드에서 이제 막 돌아온 참이었다."], ["His mourning suit spoke of the reason that had called him there.", "그의 상복이 그를 그곳으로 부른 까닭을 말해 주고 있었다."]], [["“John,”", "“존,”"], ["said his mother, “this lady is Mrs.", "어머니가 말했다. “이분은 헤일 양의 이모 되시는 쇼 부인이시다."], ["Shaw, Miss Hale’s aunt. I am sorry to say, that Miss Hale’s call is to wish us good-bye.”", "안됐지만, 헤일 양이 찾아온 것은 우리에게 작별 인사를 하기 위해서라는구나.”"]], [["“You are going then!”", "“그럼 떠나시는군요!”"], ["said he in a low voice.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“We leave to-morrow.”", "“내일 떠납니다.”"]], [["“My son-in-law comes this evening to escort us,”", "“제 사위가 오늘 저녁에 저희를 데리러 옵니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Shaw.", "쇼 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Thornton turned away.", "손턴 씨는 돌아섰다."], ["He had not sat down, and now he seemed to be examining something on the table, almost as if he had discovered an unopened letter, which had made him forget the present company.", "그는 앉지도 않은 채 이제 탁자 위의 무언가를 살펴보는 듯했다. 마치 뜯지 않은 편지라도 발견해 지금 자리에 있는 사람들을 잊어버린 사람처럼."], ["He did not even seem to be aware when they got up to take leave.", "그는 두 사람이 작별하려고 일어섰을 때조차 알아차리지 못하는 것 같았다."], ["He started forwards, however, to hand Mrs. Shaw down to the carriage.", "그러나 그는 앞으로 나서서 쇼 부인을 마차까지 모셔다 주었다."], ["As it drove up, he and Margaret stood close together on the door-step, and it was impossible but that the recollection of the day of the riot should force itself into both their minds.", "마차가 다가오는 동안 그와 마거릿은 문간 계단에 바짝 붙어 서 있었고, 폭동이 있던 날의 기억이 두 사람의 마음속에 떠오르지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Into his it came associated with the speeches of the following day; her passionate declaration that there was not a man in all that violent and a desperate crowd, for whom she did not care as much as for him.", "그의 마음속에서 그 기억은 이튿날의 말들과 얽혀 있었다. 그 사납고 절박한 무리 가운데 자기가 그를 아끼는 만큼 아끼지 않을 사람은 하나도 없다던 그녀의 격정 어린 선언과 함께."], ["And at the remembrance of her taunting words, his brow grew stern, though his heart beat thick with longing love.", "그리고 자기를 비웃던 그 말이 떠오르자 그의 이마는 굳어졌다. 가슴은 그리움 어린 사랑으로 세차게 뛰고 있었으나."], ["“No!”", "“아니다.”"], ["said he, “I put it to the touch once, and I lost it all.", "그는 생각했다. “나는 한 번 운을 걸었고 모두 잃었다."], ["Let her go,—with her stony heart, and her beauty;—how set and terrible her look is now, for all her loveliness of feature!", "보내 주자—저 돌 같은 마음과 저 아름다움을 지닌 채로. 이목구비가 그토록 사랑스러운데도 지금 저 표정은 어찌 저리 굳고 무섭단 말인가!"], ["She is afraid I shall speak what will require some stern repression.", "저 사람은 내가 무슨 말을 해서 모질게 눌러야 할 일이 생길까 두려워하는구나."], ["Let her go.", "보내 주자."], ["Beauty and heiress as she may be, she will find it hard to meet with a truer heart than mine.", "미인이든 상속녀든 간에, 내 마음보다 더 참된 마음을 만나기는 어려울 것이다."], ["Let her go!”", "보내 주자!”"]], [["And there was no tone of regret, or emotion of any kind in the voice with which he said good-bye; and the offered hand was taken with a resolute calmness, and dropped as carelessly as if it had been a dead and withered flower.", "그리고 그가 작별 인사를 하는 목소리에는 아쉬움의 기색도, 어떤 종류의 감정도 없었다. 내밀어진 손은 단호한 침착함으로 잡혔다가, 시들어 죽은 꽃이기라도 한 듯 무심하게 놓였다."], ["But none in his household saw Mr. Thornton again that day.", "그러나 그날 그의 집안에서 손턴 씨를 다시 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["He was busily engaged; or so he said.", "그는 바쁜 일이 있었다. 적어도 그는 그렇게 말했다."]], [["Margaret’s strength was so utterly exhausted by these visits, that she had to submit to much watching and petting, and sighing “I-told-you-so’s,” from her aunt.", "이 방문들로 마거릿의 기력은 완전히 바닥나서, 그녀는 이모의 극진한 간호와 어루만짐과 “그러게 내가 뭐랬니”"], ["Dixon said she was quite as bad as she had been on the first day she heard of her father’s death; and she and Mrs.", "하는 한숨을 고스란히 견뎌야 했다. 딕슨은 아가씨가 아버지의 부고를 들은 첫날만큼이나 상태가 나쁘다고 했다."], ["Shaw consulted as to the desirableness of delaying the morrow’s journey.", "그리고 딕슨과 쇼 부인은 이튿날의 여정을 미루는 편이 좋겠는지 의논했다."], ["But when her aunt reluctantly proposed a few days’ delay to Margaret, the latter writhed her body as if in acute suffering, and said:", "그러나 이모가 마지못해 며칠 미루자고 말을 꺼내자, 마거릿은 심한 고통이라도 겪는 사람처럼 몸을 비틀며 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["let us go.", "떠나요."], ["I cannot be patient here.", "저는 여기서는 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I shall not get well here.", "여기서는 낫지 않을 거예요."], ["I want to forget.”", "잊고 싶어요.”"]], [["So the arrangements went on: and Captain Lennox came, and with him news of Edith and the little boy; and Margaret found that the indifferent, careless conversation of one who, however kind, was not too warm and anxious a sympathiser, did her good. She roused up; and by the time that she knew she might expect Higgins, she was able to leave the room quietly, and await in her own chamber the expected summons.", "그리하여 채비는 계속되었다. 레녹스 대위가 왔고, 그와 함께 이디스와 어린 아들의 소식도 왔다. 그리고 마거릿은 아무리 친절하다 해도 지나치게 따뜻하고 안달하는 동정자는 아닌 사람의 심상하고 무심한 대화가 자기에게 도움이 된다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 기운을 차렸다. 그리고 히긴스가 올 만한 때가 되었다 싶자 조용히 방을 나와 자기 방에서 기다리던 기별을 기다릴 수 있었다."]], [["“Eh!”", "“이런!”"], ["said he, as she came in, “to think of th’ oud gentleman dropping off as he did!", "그녀가 들어오자 그가 말했다. “그 어르신이 그렇게 훌쩍 가 버리시다니!"], ["Yo’ might ha’ knocked me down wi’ a straw when they telled me.", "사람들이 그 말을 전했을 때 지푸라기로 밀어도 넘어질 판이었소. ‘헤일 씨라고?’ 내가 그랬지."], ["‘Mr. Hale?’ said I; ‘him as was th’ parson?’ ‘Ay,’ said they.", "‘그 목사 하시던 분?’ ‘그렇다네.’ 사람들이 그러더군."], ["‘Then,’ said I, ‘there’s as good a man gone as ever lived on this earth, let who will be t’other!’ And I came to see yo’, and tell yo’ how grieved I were, but them women in th’ kitchen wouldn’t tell yo’ I were there.", "‘그럼,’ 내가 그랬소. ‘이 땅에 살았던 누구 못지않게 좋은 사람이 갔구먼."], ["They said yo’ were ill,—and butter me, but yo’ dunnot look like the same wench.", "다른 누가 어떻든 간에!’ 그러고서 아가씨를 보러 와서 얼마나 슬픈지 말하려 했는데, 부엌에 있는 그 여편네들이 내가 왔다는 걸 아가씨한테 전해 주질 않더군. 아가씨가 아프다길래—그런데 이런, 참말로 그전 그 처녀 같아 보이질 않는구먼."], ["And yo’re going to be a grand lady up i’ Lunnon, aren’t yo’?”", "그리고 이제 런던에 가서 훌륭한 마님이 되신다지, 안 그렇소?”"]], [["“Not a grand lady,”", "“훌륭한 마님은 아니에요.”"], ["said Margaret, half smiling.", "마거릿이 반쯤 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Well!", "“원!"], ["Thornton said—says he, a day or two ago, ‘Higgins, have yo’ seen Miss Hale?’ ‘No,’ says I; ‘there’s a pack o’ women who won’t let me at her.", "손턴이 그러더군—하루 이틀 전에 이러는 거요. ‘히긴스, 헤일 양을 만나 봤나?’ ‘아니오.’ 내가 그랬지."], ["But I can bide my time, if she’s ill.", "‘그 아가씨한테 나를 못 가게 막는 여편네들이 한 무더기요."], ["She and I knows each other pretty well; and hoo’l not go doubting that I’m main sorry for th’ oud gentleman’s death, just because I can’t get at her and tell her so.’ And says he, ‘Yo’ll not have much time for to try and see her, my fine chap.", "하지만 아프다니 내가 때를 기다릴 수 있소. 그 아가씨하고 나는 서로 꽤 잘 아는 사이니, 내가 곁에 못 가서 말을 못 했다고 해서 그 어르신 돌아가신 걸 내가 몹시 슬퍼한다는 걸 의심할 사람은 아니오.’ 그랬더니 이러는 거요."], ["She’s not for staying with us a day longer nor she can help.", "‘자네가 그 사람을 만나려 애써 볼 시간은 많지 않을 걸세, 이 사람아."], ["She’s got grand relations, and they’re carrying her off; and we shan’t see her no more.’ ‘Measter,’ said I, ‘if I dunnot see her afore hoo goes, I’ll strive to get up to Lunnon next Whitsuntide, that I will.", "그 사람은 어쩔 수 없는 하루라도 우리와 더 머물 사람이 아닐세. 훌륭한 친척들이 있어서 그이들이 데려가 버릴 테고, 우리는 다시는 못 볼 걸세.’ ‘나리,’ 내가 그랬소."], ["I’ll not be baulked of saying her good-bye by any relations whatsomdever.", "‘그 아가씨가 가기 전에 내가 못 만난다면, 다음 성령강림절에 런던까지 올라가고 말겠소."], ["But bless yo’, I knowed yo’d come.", "정말 그러겠소."], ["It were only for to humour the measter, I let on as if I thought yo’d m’appen leave Milton without seeing me.”", "어떤 친척이 됐든 내가 그 아가씨한테 작별 인사 하는 걸 막지는 못하오.’ 하지만 이거 참, 나는 아가씨가 부르시리라는 걸 알고 있었소. 그저 나리 비위를 맞추느라, 아가씨가 나를 안 보고 밀턴을 떠날지도 모른다고 여기는 척한 것뿐이지.”"]], [["“You’re quite right,”", "“옳으신 말씀이에요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You only do me justice.", "“저를 제대로 보아 주셨어요."], ["And you’ll not forget me, I’m sure.", "그리고 저를 잊지 않으시리라 믿어요."], ["If no one else in Milton remembers me, I’m certain you will; and papa too.", "밀턴에서 아무도 저를 기억하지 않는다 해도 히긴스는 분명 기억해 주실 거예요. 아버지도요."], ["You know how good and tender he was.", "아버지가 얼마나 선하고 다정한 분이었는지 아시잖아요."], ["Look, Higgins!", "보세요, 히긴스!"], ["here is his Bible.", "여기 아버지의 성경이에요."], ["I have kept it for you.", "히긴스를 드리려고 간직해 두었어요."], ["I can ill spare it; but I know he would have liked you to have it.", "저도 내주기가 쉽지는 않지만, 아버지라면 히긴스가 갖기를 바라셨을 거예요."], ["I’m sure you’ll care for it, and study what is in it, for his sake.”", "히긴스가 이걸 아끼고 그 안에 담긴 것을 아버지를 생각해서 공부하시리라 믿어요.”"]], [["“Yo’ may say that.", "“그렇게 말해도 좋소."], ["If it were the deuce’s own scribble, and yo’ axed me to read in it for yo’r sake and the oud gentleman’s, I’d do it.", "그게 마귀가 갈겨쓴 것이라 해도, 아가씨와 그 어르신을 생각해서 읽어 달라고 하면 나는 읽겠소."], ["Whatten’s this, wench!", "이건 뭐요, 아가씨!"], ["I’m not going for to take yo’r brass, so dunnot think it.", "나는 아가씨 돈은 안 받을 참이니 그런 생각일랑 마시오."], ["We’ve been great friends, ’bout the sound o’ money passing between us.”", "우리는 사이에 돈 소리 나는 일 없이도 좋은 벗으로 지내 왔잖소.”"]], [["“For the children—for Boucher’s children,”", "“아이들을 위해서예요—바우처네 아이들을 위해서요.”"], ["said Margaret, hurriedly.", "마거릿이 서둘러 말했다."], ["“They may need it.", "“그 애들에게는 필요할지도 몰라요."], ["You’ve no right to refuse it for them.", "그 애들 몫을 거절할 권리는 없으세요."], ["I would not give you one penny,”", "히긴스한테라면 한 푼도 드리지 않을 거예요.”"], ["she said, smiling; “don’t think there’s any of it for you.”", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. “이 가운데 조금이라도 히긴스 몫이라고는 생각하지 마세요.”"]], [["“Well, wench!", "“허, 아가씨!"], ["I can nobbut say, Bless yo’!", "나는 그저 이 말밖에 못 하겠소. 복 받으시오!"], ["and bless yo’!—and amen.”", "또 복 받으시오!—그리고 아멘.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV", "# 제44장"]], [["“A dull rotation, never at a stay, Yesterday’s face twin image of to-day.”", "“한자리에 머무는 법 없는 따분한 되풀이, 어제의 얼굴은 오늘의 얼굴과 쌍둥이일 뿐.”"], ["COWPER.", "— 카우퍼."]], [["“Of what each one should be, he sees the form and rule, And till he reach to that, his joy can ne’er be full.”", "“저마다 마땅히 되어야 할 바의 모습과 법도를 그는 보나니, 거기에 이르기까지 그의 기쁨은 결코 온전할 수 없으리.”"], ["RÜCKERT.", "— 뤼케르트."]], [["It was very well for Margaret that the extreme quiet of the Harley Street house, during Edith’s recovery from her confinement, gave her the natural rest which she needed.", "이디스가 해산 뒤 몸을 추스르는 동안 할리 가 저택이 더없이 고요했던 것은 마거릿에게 참으로 다행한 일이었다. 그 덕분에 그녀는 필요했던 자연스러운 휴식을 얻었다."], ["It gave her time to comprehend the sudden change which had taken place in her circumstances within the last two months.", "지난 두 달 사이에 자신의 처지에 닥친 급작스러운 변화를 헤아려 볼 시간도 얻었다."], ["She found herself at once an inmate of a luxurious house, where the bare knowledge of the existence of every trouble or care seemed scarcely to have penetrated.", "그녀는 어느새 호화로운 저택의 식구가 되어 있었다. 온갖 근심과 걱정이라는 것이 이 세상에 있다는 사실조차 좀처럼 스며들어 본 적 없는 듯한 집이었다."], ["The wheels of the machinery of daily life were well oiled, and went along with delicious smoothness.", "일상이라는 기계의 바퀴에는 기름이 잘 쳐져 있어 감미로울 만큼 매끄럽게 굴러갔다."], ["Mrs. Shaw and Edith could hardly make enough of Margaret, on her return to what they persisted in calling her home.", "쇼 부인과 이디스는 마거릿이 그들이 한사코 그녀의 집이라 부르는 곳으로 돌아오자 아무리 잘해 주어도 성에 차지 않는 듯했다."], ["And she felt that it was almost ungrateful in her to have a secret feeling that the Helstone vicarage—nay, even the poor little house at Milton, with her anxious father and her invalid mother, and all the small household cares of comparative poverty, composed her idea of home.", "그런 만큼 헬스톤 목사관—아니, 근심에 찬 아버지와 병든 어머니와 가난에 가까운 살림의 자잘한 걱정거리들이 있던 밀턴의 그 초라하고 작은 집마저—이야말로 자기 마음속 집의 모습이라는 남모를 감정을 품고 있다는 것이 그녀 자신에게도 배은망덕하게 느껴졌다."], ["Edith was impatient to get well, in order to fill Margaret’s bed-room with all the soft comforts, and pretty knick-knacks, with which her own abounded.", "이디스는 어서 나아서 자기 방에 넘쳐 나는 온갖 포근한 살림살이와 예쁜 자잘한 장식품으로 마거릿의 침실을 채워 주고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["Mrs. Shaw and her maid found plenty of occupation in restoring Margaret’s wardrobe to a state of elegant variety.", "쇼 부인과 그녀의 하녀는 마거릿의 옷장을 우아하고 다채로운 상태로 되돌리는 일로 넉넉히 소일거리를 찾아냈다."], ["Captain Lennox was easy, kind, and gentlemanly; sate with his wife in her dressing-room an hour or two every day; played with his little boy for another hour, and lounged away the rest of his time at his club, when he was not engaged out to dinner.", "레녹스 대위는 느긋하고 다정하며 신사다운 사람이었다. 날마다 한두 시간은 아내의 화장실에서 함께 앉아 있었고, 또 한 시간은 어린 아들과 놀아 주었으며, 나머지 시간은 저녁 초대가 없는 날이면 클럽에서 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다."], ["Just before Margaret had recovered from her necessity for quiet and repose—before she had begun to feel her life wanting and dull—Edith came downstairs and resumed her usual part in the household; and Margaret fell into the old habit of watching, and admiring, and ministering to her cousin.", "마거릿이 고요와 안식을 필요로 하던 상태에서 막 벗어날 무렵—자기 삶이 허전하고 따분하다고 느끼기 시작하기 직전에—이디스가 아래층으로 내려와 집안에서 늘 맡던 몫을 다시 떠맡았다. 그러자 마거릿도 사촌을 지켜보고 감탄하고 시중드는 옛 습관으로 되돌아갔다."], ["She gladly took all charge of the semblances of duties off Edith’s hands; answered notes, reminded her of engagements, tended her when no gaiety was in prospect, and she was consequently rather inclined to fancy herself ill.", "그녀는 이디스의 손에서 의무라는 이름의 겉치레들을 기꺼이 모두 떠맡았다. 편지에 답장을 쓰고, 약속을 일깨워 주고, 즐거운 일이 앞에 없어 이디스가 곧잘 자기가 아프다고 여기고 싶어 할 때면 곁에서 보살폈다."], ["But all the rest of the family were in the full business of the London season, and Margaret was often left alone.", "그러나 나머지 식구들은 런던 사교철의 한복판에 있었고, 마거릿은 자주 홀로 남겨졌다."], ["Then her thoughts went back to Milton, with a strange sense of the contrast between the life there, and here.", "그럴 때면 그녀의 생각은 밀턴으로 돌아갔고, 그곳의 삶과 이곳의 삶이 얼마나 다른가 하는 야릇한 느낌이 밀려왔다."], ["She was getting surfeited of the eventless ease in which no struggle or endeavour was required.", "아무런 싸움도 애씀도 요구되지 않는, 사건 없는 안락에 그녀는 물려 가고 있었다."], ["She was afraid lest she should even become sleepily deadened into forgetfulness of anything beyond the life which was lapping her round with luxury.", "자기를 호사로 감싸고 도는 이 삶 너머의 무엇도 다 잊어버릴 만큼 나른하게 무뎌지지나 않을까 두려웠다. 런던에도 뼈 빠지게 일하고 애쓰는 사람들이 있을 터였지만 그녀는 그들을 본 적이 없었다."], ["There might be toilers and moilers there in London, but she never saw them; the very servants lived in an underground world of their own, of which she knew neither the hopes nor the fears; they only seemed to start into existence when some want or whim of their master and mistress needed them.", "하인들조차 자기들만의 지하 세계에서 살았고, 그녀는 그들의 희망도 두려움도 알지 못했다. 그들은 주인 내외의 어떤 필요나 변덕이 부를 때에만 불쑥 존재하기 시작하는 것 같았다."], ["There was a strange unsatisfied vacuum in Margaret’s heart and mode of life; and, once when she had dimly hinted this to Edith, the latter, wearied with dancing the night before, languidly stroked Margaret’s cheek as she sat by her in the old attitude,—she on a footstool by the sofa where Edith lay.", "마거릿의 마음에도 살아가는 방식에도 채워지지 않는 야릇한 공백이 있었다. 언젠가 그것을 이디스에게 어렴풋이 내비쳤을 때, 간밤에 춤을 추느라 지쳐 있던 이디스는 예전과 같은 자세로—이디스가 누운 소파 옆 발판에 마거릿이 앉아 있는 그 자세로—곁에 앉은 마거릿의 뺨을 나른하게 쓰다듬었다."]], [["“Poor child!”", "“가엾은 것!”"], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["“It is a little sad for you to be left, night after night, just at this time when all the world is so gay.", "“온 세상이 이렇게 흥청거리는 이 철에 밤마다 혼자 남겨지니 조금 서글프기도 하겠지. 그래도 곧 만찬회를 열 거야."], ["But we shall be having our dinner-parties soon—as soon as Henry comes back from circuit—and then there will be a little pleasant variety for you.", "헨리가 순회재판에서 돌아오는 대로. 그러면 너한테도 자그마한 즐거운 변화가 생기겠지."], ["No wonder it is moped, poor darling!”", "시무룩해질 만도 하지, 가엾은 것!”"]], [["Margaret did not feel as if the dinner-parties would be a panacea.", "마거릿은 만찬회가 만병통치약이 되리라고는 느껴지지 않았다."], ["But Edith piqued herself on her dinner-parties; “so different,”", "그러나 이디스는 자기 만찬회를 자랑스러워했다."], ["as she said, “from the old dowager dinners under mamma’s régime”; and Mrs.", "스스로 말하기를 “어머니 시절의 그 케케묵은 노부인 만찬과는 아주 다르다”는 것이었다."], ["Shaw herself seemed to take exactly the same kind of pleasure in the very different arrangements and circle of acquaintances which were to Captain and Mrs. Lennox’s taste, as she did in the more formal and ponderous entertainments which she herself used to give.", "그리고 쇼 부인 자신도 레녹스 대위 부부의 취향에 맞춘 사뭇 다른 차림새와 손님 무리에서, 예전에 자기가 베풀던 더 격식 차리고 묵직한 연회에서 느끼던 것과 똑같은 종류의 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Captain Lennox was always extremely kind and brotherly to Margaret.", "레녹스 대위는 언제나 마거릿에게 더없이 다정하고 오라비처럼 굴었다."], ["She was really very fond of him, excepting when he was anxiously attentive to Edith’s dress and appearance, with a view to her beauty making a sufficient impression on the world.", "그녀도 그를 정말 좋아했다. 다만 그가 이디스의 아름다움이 세상에 충분한 인상을 남기도록 하려고 아내의 옷차림과 겉모습에 안달하며 신경 쓸 때만은 예외였다."], ["Then all the latent Vashti in Margaret was roused, and she could hardly keep herself from expressing her feelings.", "그럴 때면 마거릿 안에 잠자던 와스디가 온통 깨어나, 그녀는 자기 감정을 드러내지 않으려고 애를 써야 했다."]], [["The course of Margaret’s day was this: a quiet hour or two before a late breakfast; an unpunctual meal, lazily eaten by weary and half-awake people, but yet at which, in all its dragged out length, she was expected to be present, because, directly afterwards, came a discussion of plans, at which, although they none of them concerned her, she was expected to give her sympathy, if she could not assist with her advice; an endless number of notes to write, which Edith invariably left to her, with many caressing compliments as to her eloquence du billet; a little play with Sholto as he returned from his morning’s walk; besides the care of the children during the servants’ dinner; a drive or callers; and some dinner or morning engagement for her aunt and cousins, which left Margaret free, it is true, but rather wearied with the inactivity of the day, coming upon depressed spirits and delicate health.", "마거릿의 하루는 이렇게 흘러갔다. 늦은 아침 식사 전의 조용한 한두 시간. 그다음은 시간을 지키지 않는 식사였는데, 지치고 반쯤 잠에서 덜 깬 사람들이 게으르게 먹는 자리였지만 그 지루하게 늘어지는 내내 그녀는 자리를 지켜야 했다. 곧바로 이어서 계획을 논하는 자리가 있었기 때문이다. 하나같이 그녀와는 무관한 일들이었으나, 조언으로 거들지 못한다면 공감이라도 보태야 했다. 그다음은 끝도 없이 써야 하는 편지였다. 이디스는 그녀의 “서한의 능변”을 어루만지듯 한껏 추어올리며 그 일을 어김없이 그녀에게 떠넘겼다. 그리고 아침 산책에서 돌아온 숄토와 잠깐 놀아 주기, 그 밖에 하인들이 식사하는 동안 아이들 돌보기, 마차 산책이나 손님맞이. 그러고 나면 이모와 사촌 내외는 만찬 약속이나 오전 약속으로 나가 버려 마거릿은 자유로워졌다. 그것은 사실이었으나, 가라앉은 마음과 약해진 몸 위로 겹쳐 오는 그 하루의 무위(無爲)에 그녀는 오히려 지쳐 있었다."]], [["She looked forward with longing, though unspoken interest to the homely object of Dixon’s return from Milton; where, until now, the old servant had been busily engaged in winding up all the affairs of the Hale family.", "그녀는 딕슨이 밀턴에서 돌아오는 그 소박한 일을 말은 못 해도 애타는 마음으로 기다렸다. 지금까지 그 늙은 하녀는 헤일 집안의 온갖 뒷일을 정리하느라 바삐 매여 있었다."], ["It had appeared a sudden famine to her heart, this entire cessation of any news respecting the people among whom she had lived so long.", "오래도록 함께 살아온 사람들에 관한 소식이 이렇게 뚝 끊긴 것은 그녀의 마음에 별안간 닥친 굶주림 같았다. 물론 딕슨은 사무적인 편지 속에서 이따금 손턴 씨의 의견을 옮기기는 했다."], ["It was true, that Dixon, in her business-like letters, quoted, every now and then, an opinion of Mr.", "가구를 어떻게 하는 것이 좋을지, 크램프턴 테라스 집의 집주인에게는 어떻게 대응할지 하는 것들이었다. 그러나 그 이름이, 아니 밀턴의 어떤 이름이라도 나오는 것은 여기저기 띄엄띄엄일 뿐이었다."], ["Thornton’s as to what she had better do about the furniture, or how act in regard to the landlord of the Crampton Terrace house.", "어느 저녁 마거릿은 레녹스 집안 응접실에 혼자 앉아 있었다. 딕슨의 편지를 손에 쥐고 있었으나 읽지는 않고 그저 그 내용을 곱씹으며, 지나간 날들을 떠올리고, 자기 삶이 뽑혀 나왔는데도 아무도 그 빈자리를 아쉬워하지 않은 그 분주한 삶을 그려 보고 있었다."], ["But it was only here and there that the name came in, or any Milton name, indeed; and Margaret was sitting one evening, all alone in the Lennox’s drawing-room, not reading Dixon’s letters, which yet she held in her hand, but thinking over them, and recalling the days which had been, and picturing the busy life out of which her own had been taken and never missed; wondering if all went on in that whirl just as if she and her father had never been; questioning within herself, if no one in all the crowd missed her (not Higgins, she was not thinking of him), when, suddenly Mr.", "자기와 아버지가 애초에 없었던 것처럼 그 소용돌이가 그대로 돌아가고 있을까 궁금해하고, 그 많은 사람 가운데 아무도 자기를 그리워하지 않는지 마음속으로 물어보고 있었다(히긴스는 아니었다, 그녀가 생각하고 있던 사람은). 그때 갑자기 벨 씨가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔다."], ["Bell was announced; and Margaret hurried the letters into her work-basket, and started up, blushing as if she had been doing some guilty thing.", "마거릿은 편지를 반짇고리에 급히 밀어 넣고 벌떡 일어섰다. 무슨 죄라도 지은 것처럼 얼굴을 붉히면서."]], [["“Oh, Mr. Bell!", "“어머, 벨 씨!"], ["I never thought of seeing you!”", "뵈리라고는 생각도 못 했어요!”"]], [["“But you give me a welcome, I hope, as well as that very pretty start of surprise.”", "“그래도 그 아주 예쁜 놀란 몸짓뿐 아니라 반갑다는 인사도 해 주는 거겠지.”"]], [["“Have you dined?", "“저녁은 드셨어요?"], ["How did you come?", "어떻게 오셨어요?"], ["Let me order you some dinner.”", "저녁을 좀 차리라고 할게요.”"]], [["“If you’re going to have any.", "“자네도 뭘 먹을 참이라면 그러게."], ["Otherwise, you know, there is no one who cares less for eating than I do.", "안 그러면, 알다시피 나만큼 먹는 데 관심 없는 사람도 없으니까."], ["But where are the others?", "그런데 다른 사람들은 어디 있나?"], ["Gone out to dinner?", "저녁 초대를 받아 나갔나?"], ["Left you alone?”", "자네만 혼자 두고?”"]], [["“Oh yes!", "“네, 그래요."], ["and it is such a rest.", "그게 어찌나 편한지요."], ["I was just thinking—But will you run the risk of dinner?", "마침 생각하고 있던 참인데—그런데 저녁을 한번 걸어 보시겠어요?"], ["I don’t know if there is anything in the house.”", "집에 뭐가 있는지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Why, to tell you the truth, I dined at my club.", "“실은 말이지, 클럽에서 저녁을 먹었네."], ["Only they don’t cook as well as they did, so I thought, if you were going to dine, I might try and make out my dinner.", "다만 예전만큼 요리를 잘하지 못하기에, 자네가 저녁을 먹을 참이면 나도 한번 저녁을 채워 볼까 했던 게지."], ["But never mind, never mind!", "하지만 됐네, 됐어!"], ["There aren’t ten cooks in England to be trusted at impromptu dinners.", "잉글랜드를 통틀어도 갑작스러운 저녁상을 믿고 맡길 요리사는 열 명도 못 되네."], ["If their skill and their fires will stand it, their tempers won’t.", "솜씨와 불이 견뎌 준다 해도 성깔이 못 견디지."], ["You shall make me some tea, Margaret.", "차나 좀 끓여 주게, 마거릿."], ["And now, what were you thinking of?", "그래, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었나?"], ["you were going to tell me.", "말해 주려던 참이었잖은가."], ["Whose letters were those, god-daughter, that you hid away so speedily?”", "그건 누구 편지인가, 대녀야? 그렇게 잽싸게 감춘 것 말이야.”"]], [["“Only Dixon’s,”", "“그냥 딕슨의 편지예요.”"], ["replied Margaret, growing very red.", "마거릿이 얼굴이 새빨개져서 대답했다."]], [["“Whew!", "“허!"], ["is that all?", "그게 다인가?"], ["Who do you think came up in the train with me?”", "나랑 같이 기차를 타고 올라온 사람이 누구였을 것 같나?”"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“모르겠어요.”"], ["said Margaret, resolved against making a guess.", "마거릿은 짐작 따위는 하지 않겠다고 마음먹고 말했다."]], [["“Your what d’ye call him?", "“그 뭐라고 하더라?"], ["What’s the right name for a cousin-in-law’s brother?”", "사촌의 남편의 동생은 뭐라고 부르는 게 옳은가?”"]], [["“Mr. Henry Lennox?”", "“헨리 레녹스 씨요?”"], ["asked Margaret.", "마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["replied Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 대답했다."], ["“You knew him formerly, didn’t you?", "“예전부터 알던 사이였지, 안 그런가?"], ["What sort of a person is he, Margaret?”", "어떤 사람인가, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I liked him long ago,”", "“오래전에는 좋아했어요.”"], ["said Margaret, glancing down for a moment.", "마거릿은 잠깐 눈을 내리깔며 말했다."], ["And then she looked straight up and went on in her natural manner.", "그러고는 곧장 고개를 들고 여느 때의 태도로 말을 이었다."], ["“You know we have been corresponding about Frederick since; but I have not seen him for nearly three years, and he may be changed.", "“그 뒤로 프레더릭 일로 편지를 주고받아 온 건 아시잖아요. 하지만 삼 년 가까이 만나지 못했으니 달라졌을지도 모르죠."], ["What did you think of him?”", "어떻게 보셨어요?”"]], [["“I don’t know.", "“모르겠네."], ["He was so busy trying to find out who I was, in the first instance, and what I was in the second, that he never let out what he was; unless indeed that veiled curiosity of his as to what manner of man he had to talk to was not a good piece, and a fair indication of his character.", "처음에는 내가 누구인지 알아내려고, 다음에는 내가 무엇 하는 사람인지 알아내려고 어찌나 바쁘던지, 정작 자기가 어떤 사람인지는 끝내 내비치지 않더군. 하기야 무슨 사람을 상대로 이야기하는 것인지 알아보려는 그 감춰진 호기심 자체가 제법 볼만한 것이었고, 그 성품을 꽤 잘 보여 준 셈이라면 몰라도."], ["Do you call him good-looking, Margaret?”", "자네는 그 사람이 잘생겼다고 보나, 마거릿?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니요!"], ["certainly not.", "전혀요."], ["Do you?”", "그렇게 보세요?”"]], [["“Not I.", "“나야 아니지."], ["But I thought perhaps you might.", "다만 자네는 혹시 그럴까 싶었네."], ["Is he a great deal here?”", "여기 자주 오나?”"]], [["“I fancy he is when he is in town.", "“런던에 있을 때는 그런 것 같아요."], ["He has been on circuit now since I came.", "제가 온 뒤로는 순회재판에 나가 있었고요."], ["But—Mr. Bell—have you come from Oxford or from Milton?”", "그런데—벨 씨—옥스퍼드에서 오셨어요, 밀턴에서 오셨어요?”"]], [["“From Milton.", "“밀턴에서지."], ["Don’t you see I’m smoke-dried?”", "내가 연기에 절어 있는 게 안 보이나?”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“그러네요."], ["But I thought that it might be the effect of the antiquities of Oxford.”", "하지만 저는 옥스퍼드의 그 고색창연한 것들 탓인 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“Come now, be a sensible woman!", "“이보게, 철든 여인답게 굴어야지!"], ["In Oxford, I could have managed all the landlords in the place, and had my own way, with half the trouble your Milton landlord has given me, and defeated me after all.", "옥스퍼드에서라면 나는 그 동네 집주인들쯤은 다 주무르고 내 뜻대로 했을 걸세. 자네네 밀턴 집주인이 나한테 안긴 수고의 절반만 들이고도 말이야."], ["He won’t take the house off our hands till next June twelve-month.", "그런데 그 사람은 결국 나를 이겨 먹었네. 내년 유월이 되어야 그 집을 우리 손에서 거두어 가겠다는 거야."], ["Luckily, Mr. Thornton found a tenant for it.", "다행히 손턴 씨가 세를 들 사람을 찾아 주었지."], ["Why don’t you ask after Mr. Thornton, Margaret?", "자네는 왜 손턴 씨 안부는 묻지 않나, 마거릿?"], ["He has proved himself a very active friend of yours, I can tell you.", "그 사람이 자네한테 아주 발 벗고 나선 친구 노릇을 했다는 걸 내가 말해 주지."], ["Taken more than half the trouble off my hands.”", "내 수고의 절반도 넘게 덜어 주었네.”"]], [["“And how is he?", "“그분은 어떠세요?"], ["How is Mrs. Thornton?”", "손턴 부인도 안녕하시고요?”"], ["asked Margaret hurriedly and below her breath, though she tried to speak out.", "마거릿은 소리 내어 말하려 애썼으나 서둘러 숨죽인 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“I suppose they’re well.", "“다들 잘 있는 것 같더군."], ["I’ve been staying at their house till I was driven out of it by the perpetual clack about that Thornton girl’s marriage.", "그 집에 묵고 있다가 손턴네 딸 혼사 이야기를 쉴 새 없이 재잘거리는 통에 쫓겨나다시피 나왔네."], ["It was too much for Thornton himself, though she was his sister.", "누이인데도 손턴 자신조차 견디기 어려워하더구먼."], ["He used to go and sit in his own room perpetually.", "늘 제 방에 들어가 앉아 있곤 했지."], ["He’s getting past the age for caring for such things, either as principal or accessory.", "그 사람도 이제 그런 일에 당사자로든 곁다리로든 마음 쓸 나이는 지났으니까."], ["I was surprised to find the old lady falling into the current, and carried away by her daughter’s enthusiasm for orange-blossoms and lace.", "나는 그 노부인이 그 물살에 휩쓸려 딸의 오렌지 꽃과 레이스 타령에 함께 들떠 있는 걸 보고 놀랐네."], ["I thought Mrs. Thornton had been made of sterner stuff.”", "손턴 부인은 더 단단한 재료로 빚어진 사람인 줄 알았거든.”"]], [["“She would put on any assumption of feeling to veil her daughter’s weakness,”", "“딸의 약한 구석을 가리기 위해서라면 어떤 감정이든 꾸며 내실 분이에요.”"], ["said Margaret in a low voice.", "마거릿이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps so.", "“그럴지도 모르지."], ["You’ve studied her, have you?", "자네가 그 부인을 연구해 본 모양이군?"], ["She doesn’t seem over fond of you, Margaret.”", "그 부인은 자네를 그리 좋아하지 않는 것 같더구먼, 마거릿.”"]], [["“I know it,”", "“알아요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“Oh, here is tea, at last!”", "“아, 드디어 차가 왔네요!”"], ["exclaimed she, as if relieved. And with tea came Mr.", "그녀는 마음이 놓인 듯 외쳤다."], ["Henry Lennox, who had walked up to Harley Street after a late dinner, and had evidently expected to find his brother and sister-in-law at home.", "그리고 차와 함께 헨리 레녹스 씨가 들어왔다. 늦은 저녁 식사를 마치고 할리 가까지 걸어 올라온 그는 형과 형수가 집에 있으리라고 여긴 것이 분명했다."], ["Margaret suspected him of being as thankful as she was at the presence of a third party, on this their first meeting since the memorable day of his offer, and her refusal at Helstone.", "헬스톤에서 그가 청혼하고 그녀가 거절했던 그 잊지 못할 날 이후 처음 마주하는 자리인 만큼, 제삼자가 있다는 것을 그도 자기만큼이나 고맙게 여기리라고 마거릿은 짐작했다."], ["She could hardly tell what to say at first, and was thankful for all the tea-table occupations, which gave her an excuse for keeping silence, and him an opportunity for recovering himself.", "그녀는 처음에 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 침묵을 지킬 구실을 주고 그에게는 마음을 추스를 여유를 주는 찻상의 온갖 일거리가 고마웠다."], ["For, to tell the truth, he had rather forced himself up to Harley Street this evening, with a view of getting over an awkward meeting, awkward even in the presence of Captain Lennox and Edith, and doubly awkward now that he found her the only lady there, and the person to whom he must naturally and perforce address a great part of his conversation.", "사실을 말하자면 그는 이날 저녁 어색한 만남을 넘겨 버릴 셈으로 억지로 몸을 일으켜 할리 가로 온 것이었다. 레녹스 대위와 이디스가 있어도 어색할 자리인데, 그녀가 그곳의 유일한 부인이고 자연히 또 어쩔 수 없이 대화의 많은 부분을 그녀에게 건네야 하는 처지가 되고 보니 어색함이 갑절이 되었다."], ["She was the first to recover her self-possession.", "먼저 평정을 되찾은 쪽은 그녀였다."], ["She began to talk on the subject which came uppermost in her mind, after the first flush of awkward shyness.", "어색한 수줍음의 첫 물결이 지나가자 그녀는 마음에 가장 먼저 떠오른 화제를 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["“Mr. Lennox, I have been so much obliged to you for all you have done about Frederick.”", "“레녹스 씨, 프레더릭 일로 애써 주신 모든 것에 정말 감사드립니다.”"]], [["“I am only sorry it has been so unsuccessful,”", "“그렇게 성과가 없었던 것이 안타까울 뿐입니다.”"], ["replied he, with a quick glance towards Mr. Bell, as if reconnoitring how much he might say before him.", "그가 대답하면서 벨 씨 앞에서 어디까지 말해도 좋을지 살피듯 그쪽으로 재빨리 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Margaret, as if she read his thought, addressed herself to Mr.", "마거릿은 그의 속내를 읽은 듯 벨 씨를 향해 말을 건넸다."], ["Bell, both including him in the conversation, and implying that he was perfectly aware of the endeavours that had been made to clear Frederick.", "그를 대화 안으로 끌어들이는 동시에, 프레더릭의 누명을 벗기려 해 온 노력들을 그가 이미 훤히 알고 있다는 뜻을 내비친 것이었다."]], [["“That Horrocks—that very last witness of all, has proved as unavailing as all the others. Mr.", "“그 호럭스라는 사람—맨 마지막 증인이었던 그 사람도 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 아무 소용이 없는 것으로 드러났어요."], ["Lennox has discovered that he sailed for Australia only last August; only two months before Frederick was in England, and gave us the names of——”", "레녹스 씨가 알아내신 바로는 그 사람은 바로 지난 팔월에 오스트레일리아로 떠났대요. 프레더릭이 잉글랜드에 와서 우리에게 그 이름들을 알려 주기 겨우 두 달 전에요——”"]], [["“Frederick in England!", "“프레더릭이 잉글랜드에 왔었다고!"], ["you never told me that!” exclaimed Mr.", "그런 말은 한 번도 안 했잖은가!”"], ["Bell in surprise.", "벨 씨가 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["“I thought you knew.", "“아시는 줄 알았어요."], ["I never doubted you had been told.", "들으셨으리라고 믿어 의심치 않았어요."], ["Of course, it was a great secret, and perhaps I should not have named it now,”", "물론 큰 비밀이었으니 지금 입에 올리지 말았어야 했는지도 모르겠네요.”"], ["said Margaret, a little dismayed.", "마거릿이 조금 당황하여 말했다."]], [["“I have never named it to either my brother or your cousin,” said Mr.", "“저는 형에게도 형수에게도 그 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 없습니다.”"], ["Lennox, with a little professional dryness of implied reproach.", "레녹스 씨가 나무라는 뜻을 담은 직업적인 무미건조함으로 말했다."]], [["“Never mind, Margaret.", "“괜찮네, 마거릿."], ["I am not living in a talking, babbling world, nor yet among people who are trying to worm facts out of me; you needn’t look so frightened because you have let the cat out of the bag to a faithful old hermit like me.", "나는 떠들썩하게 수다 떠는 세상에 사는 것도 아니고, 나한테서 사실을 캐내려 드는 사람들 틈에 있는 것도 아니야. 나 같은 미덥고 늙은 은둔자한테 비밀을 흘렸다고 그렇게 겁먹은 얼굴을 할 것 없네."], ["I shall never name his having been in England; I shall be out of temptation, for no one will ask me.", "나는 그 사람이 잉글랜드에 왔었다는 말은 결코 입에 담지 않을 걸세. 아무도 나한테 묻지 않을 테니 유혹에 빠질 일도 없지."], ["Stay!”", "가만!”"], ["(interrupting himself rather abruptly). “Was it at your mother’s funeral?”", "(그가 다소 불쑥 자기 말을 끊었다.) “자네 어머니 장례 때였나?”"]], [["“He was with mamma when she died,”", "“오빠는 어머니가 돌아가실 때 곁에 있었어요.”"], ["said Margaret, softly.", "마거릿이 나직이 말했다."]], [["“To be sure!", "“그렇지!"], ["To be sure!", "그렇고말고!"], ["Why, some one asked me if he had not been over then, and I denied it stoutly—not many weeks ago—who could it have been?", "아, 누군가 나한테 그 사람이 그때 건너왔던 게 아니냐고 묻기에 내가 딱 잘라 아니라고 했는데—몇 주 되지도 않았어—그게 누구였더라?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["I recollect!”", "이제 생각나는군!”"]], [["But he did not say the name; and although Margaret would have given much to know if her suspicions were right, and it had been Mr.", "그러나 그는 그 이름을 말하지 않았다."], ["Thornton who had made the enquiry, she could not ask the question of Mr. Bell, much as she longed to do so.", "마거릿은 자기 짐작이 맞는지, 그 질문을 한 사람이 손턴 씨였는지 알기 위해서라면 많은 것을 내놓을 수도 있었지만, 그토록 묻고 싶었으면서도 벨 씨에게 그 물음을 던질 수 없었다."]], [["There was a pause for a moment or two.", "잠깐 말이 끊겼다."], ["Then Mr. Lennox said, addressing himself to Margaret, “I suppose as Mr.", "그러다가 레녹스 씨가 마거릿을 향해 말했다."], ["Bell is now acquainted with all the circumstances attending your brother’s unfortunate dilemma, I cannot do better than inform him exactly how the research into the evidence we once hoped to produce in his favour stands at present.", "“벨 선생님께서 이제 오라버님의 그 딱한 처지에 얽힌 사정을 다 알고 계시다면, 한때 그분께 유리하게 내놓을 수 있으리라 바랐던 증거를 찾는 일이 지금 어디까지 와 있는지 정확히 알려 드리는 것이 가장 좋겠군요."], ["So, if he will do me the honour to breakfast with me to-morrow, we will go over the names of these missing gentry.”", "그러니 내일 아침 저와 함께 식사해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 그 행방이 묘연한 사람들의 이름을 하나씩 짚어 보겠습니다.”"]], [["“I should like to hear all the particulars, if I may.", "“괜찮다면 저도 그 자세한 사정을 다 듣고 싶어요."], ["Cannot you come here?", "이리로 오실 수는 없나요?"], ["I dare not ask you both to breakfast, though I am sure you would be welcome.", "두 분을 아침 식사에 부르자고 감히 말씀드리지는 못하겠어요. 물론 두 분 다 환영받으시겠지만요."], ["But let me know all you can about Frederick, even though there may be no hope at present.”", "그래도 프레더릭에 대해 아시는 것은 다 알려 주세요. 지금은 아무 희망이 없다 해도요.”"]], [["“I have an engagement at half-past eleven.", "“열한 시 반에 약속이 있습니다."], ["But I will certainly come if you wish it,” replied Mr.", "하지만 원하신다면 반드시 오지요.”"], ["Lennox with a little after-thought of extreme willingness, which made Margaret shrink into herself, and almost wish that she had not proposed her natural request. Mr.", "레녹스 씨가 뒤늦게 지나칠 만큼 기꺼운 기색을 덧붙여 대답하는 바람에, 마거릿은 몸이 움츠러들며 그 자연스러운 부탁을 꺼내지 말걸 그랬다고 거의 후회할 뻔했다."], ["Bell got up and looked around him for his hat, which had been removed to make room for tea.", "벨 씨가 일어나서 자기 모자를 찾아 둘러보았다. 차를 놓을 자리를 내느라 치워 둔 모자였다."]], [["“Well!”", "“자!”"], ["said he, “I don’t know what Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Lennox is inclined to do, but I’m disposed to be moving off homewards.", "“레녹스 씨는 어쩔 생각인지 모르겠네만, 나는 슬슬 집으로 갈까 하네."], ["I’ve been a journey to-day, and journeys begin to tell upon my sixty and odd years.”", "오늘 여행을 했더니, 예순 몇 해나 먹은 나이에는 여행이 몸에 배어 나오기 시작하는군.”"]], [["“I believe I shall stay and see my brother and sister,”", "“저는 남아서 형과 형수를 보고 갈까 합니다.”"], ["said Mr. Lennox, making no movement of departure.", "레녹스 씨가 떠날 기색을 조금도 보이지 않고 말했다."], ["Margaret was seized with a shy awkward dread of being left alone with him.", "마거릿은 그와 단둘이 남겨진다는 데 수줍고 어색한 두려움에 사로잡혔다."], ["The scene on the little terrace in the Helstone garden was so present to her, that she could hardly help believing it was so with him.", "헬스톤 정원의 그 작은 테라스에서 있었던 장면이 그녀에게 너무도 생생해서, 그에게도 그러리라고 믿지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Don’t go yet, please Mr. Bell,”", "“아직 가지 마세요, 벨 씨.”"], ["said she, hastily.", "그녀가 서둘러 말했다."], ["“I want you to see Edith; and I want Edith to know you.", "“이디스를 보셨으면 해요. 이디스도 벨 씨를 알았으면 하고요."], ["Please!”", "제발요!”"], ["said she, laying a light but determined hand on his arm.", "그러면서 그녀는 가볍지만 단호한 손을 그의 팔에 얹었다."], ["He looked at her, and saw the confusion stirring in her countenance; he sate down again, as if her little touch had been possessed of resistless strength.", "그가 그녀를 바라보고는 그 얼굴에 일렁이는 당혹을 알아차렸다. 그는 그 작은 손길에 거스를 수 없는 힘이라도 실린 듯 도로 앉았다."]], [["“You see how she overpowers me, Mr. Lennox,”", "“이 아이가 나를 이렇게 휘어잡는군요, 레녹스 씨.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“And I hope you noticed the happy choice of her expressions; she wants me to ‘see’ this cousin Edith, who, I am told, is a great beauty; but she has the honesty to change her word when she comes to me—Mrs.", "“그리고 이 아이가 고른 말이 얼마나 절묘한지도 알아채셨기 바랍니다. 대단한 미인이라고 하는 이 사촌 이디스는 나더러 ‘보라’고 하고서는, 나한테 이르러서는 솔직하게 말을 바꾸었으니 말이오."], ["Lennox is to ‘know’ me.", "레녹스 부인은 나를 ‘알아야’ 한다지요."], ["I suppose I am not much to ‘see,’ eh, Margaret?”", "나는 별로 ‘볼’ 것이 못 되는 모양이지, 응, 마거릿?”"]], [["He joked, to give her time to recover from the slight flutter which he had detected in her manner on his proposal to leave; and she caught the tone, and threw the ball back. Mr.", "그는 자기가 가겠다고 하자 그녀의 태도에서 감지한 자그마한 동요를 가라앉힐 틈을 주려고 농을 던졌고, 그녀도 그 어조를 받아 공을 되던졌다."], ["Lennox wondered how his brother, the Captain, could have reported her as having lost all her good looks.", "레녹스 씨는 형인 대위가 어떻게 그녀가 고운 모습을 다 잃었다고 전할 수 있었는지 의아했다."], ["To be sure, in her quiet black dress, she was a contrast to Edith, dancing in her white crape mourning, and long floating golden hair, all softness and glitter.", "물론 조용한 검은 드레스 차림의 그녀는 하늘하늘한 흰 크레이프 상복을 입고 길게 늘어뜨린 금빛 머리칼을 나부끼며 온통 부드러움과 광채로 춤추듯 다니는 이디스와는 대조를 이루었다."], ["She dimpled and blushed most becomingly when introduced to Mr.", "이디스는 벨 씨에게 소개받자 더없이 어울리게 보조개를 지으며 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Bell, conscious that she had her reputation as a beauty to keep up, and that it would not do to have a Mordecai refusing to worship and admire, even in the shape of an old Fellow of a College, which nobody had ever heard of.", "미인이라는 평판을 지켜야 한다는 것을, 그리고 아무도 들어 본 적 없는 어느 대학의 늙은 펠로의 모습을 하고 있을망정 절하고 우러르기를 거부하는 모르드개가 있어서는 안 된다는 것을 그녀는 의식하고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Shaw and Captain Lennox, each in their separate way, gave Mr.", "쇼 부인과 레녹스 대위도 저마다의 방식으로 벨 씨를 다정하고 진심 어린 태도로 맞아, 그가 자기도 모르게 그들을 좋아하게 만들었다."], ["Bell a kind and sincere welcome, winning him over to like them almost in spite of himself, especially when he saw how naturally Margaret took her place as sister and daughter of the house.", "특히 마거릿이 이 집의 딸이자 누이로서 얼마나 자연스럽게 제자리를 잡고 있는지를 보고 나서는 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“What a shame that we were not at home to receive you,”", "“집에서 맞아 드리지 못했다니 정말 죄송해요.”"], ["said Edith.", "이디스가 말했다."], ["“You, too, Henry!", "“헨리, 도련님도요!"], ["though I don’t know that we should have stayed at home for you.", "하기야 도련님을 위해서라면 집에 남아 있었을 것 같지는 않지만요."], ["And for Mr. Bell!", "하지만 벨 씨를 위해서라면!"], ["for Margaret’s Mr. Bell——”", "마거릿의 벨 씨를 위해서라면——”"]], [["“There is no knowing what sacrifices you would not have made,”", "“형수님이 어떤 희생인들 못 치렀겠습니까.”"], ["said her brother-in-law.", "시동생이 말했다."], ["“Even a dinner-party!", "“만찬회까지도요!"], ["and the delight of wearing this very becoming dress.”", "그리고 이 아주 잘 어울리는 드레스를 입는 즐거움까지도요.”"]], [["Edith did not know whether to frown or to smile, but it did not suit Mr.", "이디스는 이맛살을 찌푸려야 할지 웃어야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Lennox to drive her to the first of these alternatives; so he went on.", "그러나 레녹스 씨는 그녀를 앞엣것 쪽으로 몰고 갈 생각이 없었으므로 말을 이었다."]], [["“Will you show your readiness to make sacrifices to-morrow morning, first by asking me to breakfast, to meet Mr.", "“내일 아침에 희생할 각오가 되어 있다는 것을 보여 주시겠습니까?"], ["Bell, and secondly, by being so kind as to order it at half-past nine, instead of at ten o’clock?", "우선 벨 선생님을 만나뵐 수 있게 저를 아침 식사에 불러 주시고, 다음으로는 열 시가 아니라 아홉 시 반에 상을 차리도록 일러 주시는 친절을 베풀어서요."], ["I have some letters and papers that I want to show to Miss Hale and Mr. Bell.”", "헤일 양과 벨 선생님께 보여 드리고 싶은 편지와 서류가 있습니다.”"]], [["“I hope Mr. Bell will make our house his own during his stay in London,”", "“벨 선생님께서 런던에 머무시는 동안 저희 집을 제 집처럼 여기셨으면 합니다.”"], ["said Captain Lennox.", "레녹스 대위가 말했다."], ["“I am only so sorry we cannot offer him a bedroom.”", "“침실을 내어 드릴 수 없는 것이 그저 죄송할 따름입니다.”"]], [["“Thank you.", "“고맙습니다."], ["I am much obliged to you.", "참으로 감사한 말씀이오."], ["You would only think me a churl if you had, for I should decline it, I believe, in spite of all the temptations of such agreeable company,”", "하지만 내어 주셨다면 나를 무뚝뚝한 사람으로만 여기셨을 겁니다. 이토록 유쾌한 벗들이라는 온갖 유혹에도 불구하고 사양했을 테니까요.”"], ["said Mr. Bell, bowing all round, and secretly congratulating himself on the neat turn he had given to his sentence, which, if put into plain language, would have been more to this effect: “I couldn’t stand the restraints of such a proper-behaved and civil-spoken people as these are: it would be like meat without salt.", "벨 씨는 좌중을 향해 두루 고개를 숙이며 이렇게 말하고는, 자기 문장을 이토록 말끔하게 마무리한 것을 남몰래 대견해했다."], ["I’m thankful they haven’t a bed.", "그 말을 꾸밈없는 말로 옮겼더라면 이런 뜻에 더 가까웠을 것이다. “이렇게 예의 바르고 점잖게 말하는 사람들 틈의 갑갑함은 나는 못 견디지."], ["And how well I rounded my sentence!", "소금 없는 고기 같을 게야. 침대가 없다니 고마운 일이군."], ["I’m absolutely catching the trick of good manners.”", "그런데 문장을 참 잘도 둥글렸어! 내가 아주 좋은 예절의 요령을 익히고 있구먼.”"]], [["His self-satisfaction lasted him till he was fairly out in the streets, walking side by side with Henry Lennox.", "그 자기만족은 그가 거리로 나와 헨리 레녹스와 나란히 걸을 때까지 이어졌다."], ["Here he suddenly remembered Margaret’s little look of entreaty as she urged him to stay longer, and he also recollected a few hints given him long ago by an acquaintance of Mr. Lennox’s, as to his admiration of Margaret.", "여기서 그는 문득 더 머물러 달라고 조르던 마거릿의 애원 어린 눈빛을 떠올렸고, 오래전 레녹스 씨의 어느 지인에게서 들었던, 그가 마거릿을 흠모한다는 몇 마디 귀띔도 생각났다."], ["It gave a new direction to his thoughts.", "그것이 그의 생각에 새로운 방향을 주었다."], ["“You have known Miss Hale for a long time, I believe.", "“헤일 양을 오래 알고 지내셨겠지요."], ["How do you think her looking?", "그 아이 안색이 어떻습니까?"], ["She strikes me as pale and ill.”", "나한테는 창백하고 아파 보이는군요.”"]], [["“I thought her looking remarkably well.", "“제 눈에는 대단히 좋아 보이던데요."], ["Perhaps not when I first came—now I think of.", "처음 들어갔을 때는 아니었는지도 모르겠습니다."], ["But certainly, when she grew animated, she looked as well as ever I saw her do.”", "지금 생각해 보니 말입니다. 하지만 생기가 돌기 시작하니 제가 본 어느 때 못지않게 좋아 보였습니다.”"]], [["“She has had a great deal to go through,”", "“겪어 낸 일이 참 많았지요.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그렇습니다! 그분이 견뎌야 했던 온갖 일을 전해 듣고 마음이 아팠습니다."], ["I have been sorry to hear of all she has had to bear; not merely the common and universal sorrow arising from death, but all the annoyance which her father’s conduct must have caused her, and then——”", "죽음에서 오는 그 흔하고 누구에게나 닥치는 슬픔만이 아니라, 아버님의 처신이 그분께 안겼을 온갖 괴로움까지도요. 게다가——”"]], [["“Her father’s conduct!” said Mr.", "“아버지의 처신이라니!”"], ["Bell, in an accent of surprise.", "벨 씨가 놀란 어조로 말했다."], ["“You must have heard some wrong statement.", "“뭔가 잘못된 이야기를 들으신 게 분명합니다."], ["He behaved in the most conscientious manner.", "그분은 더없이 양심에 따라 행동했습니다."], ["He showed more resolute strength than I should ever have given him credit for formerly.”", "예전의 그분을 두고는 내가 도저히 인정해 주지 않았을 만큼 굳센 결단력을 보여 주셨지요.”"]], [["“Perhaps I have been wrongly informed.", "“제가 잘못 전해 들었는지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 그분의 성직록을 이어받은 분—영리하고 사리 밝은 데다 아주 부지런한 성직자였습니다—에게서 들은 바로는, 헤일 씨가 그렇게까지 하실 까닭은 없었다는 겁니다."], ["But I have been told, by his successor in the living—a clever sensible man, and a thoroughly active clergyman—that there was no call upon Mr.", "성직록을 내놓고 자신과 가족을 공업 도시에서 개인 교습이나 하는 팔자에 내던지실 까닭 말입니다. 주교가 다른 성직록을 제의한 것은 사실이지만, 어떤 의심을 품게 되었다 하더라도 그 자리에 그대로 머무를 수 있었으니 사임할 이유가 없었다는 것이지요."], ["Hale to do what he did, relinquish the living, and throw himself and his family on the tender mercies of private teaching in a manufacturing town; the bishop had offered him another living, it is true, but if he had come to entertain certain doubts, he could have remained where he was, and so had no occasion to resign.", "사실인즉 이 시골 성직자들은 워낙 고립된 삶을 살지 않습니까. 고립이란 자기와 같은 수준의 교양을 갖춘 사람들과의 왕래가 없다는 뜻입니다."], ["But the truth is, these country clergymen live such isolated lives—isolated, I mean, from all intercourse with men of equal cultivation with themselves, by whose minds they might regulate their own, and discover when they were going either too fast or too slow—that they are very apt to disturb themselves with imaginary doubts as to the articles of faith, and throw up certain opportunities of doing good for very uncertain fancies of their own.”", "그런 이들의 생각에 견주어 제 생각을 다스리기도 하고, 자기가 너무 앞서가는지 너무 뒤처지는지 알아차리기도 할 텐데 말입니다. 그러다 보니 신앙의 조항을 두고 있지도 않은 의심으로 스스로를 어지럽히고, 선을 행할 수 있는 확실한 기회를 아주 불확실한 제 공상 때문에 내던지기가 아주 쉬운 것이지요.”"]], [["“I differ from you.", "“나는 생각이 다릅니다."], ["I do not think they are very apt to do as my poor friend Hale did.”", "그들이 내 가엾은 친구 헤일처럼 하기가 아주 쉽다고는 보지 않아요.”"], ["Mr. Bell was inwardly chafing.", "벨 씨는 속으로 부아가 치밀고 있었다."]], [["“Perhaps I used too general an expression in saying ‘very apt.’ But certainly, their lives are such as very often to produce either inordinate self-sufficiency, or a morbid state of conscience,”", "“‘아주 쉽다’고 한 것은 제가 너무 두루뭉술한 표현을 쓴 것인지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 그들의 삶이란 것이 지나친 자만이나 병적인 양심 상태 가운데 하나를 아주 자주 낳는 것만은 분명합니다.”"], ["replied Mr. Lennox with perfect coolness.", "레녹스 씨가 더없이 태연하게 대답했다."]], [["“You don’t meet with any self-sufficiency among the lawyers, for instance?”", "“이를테면 법률가들 사이에서는 자만이라는 것을 만나 보신 적이 없습니까?”"], ["asked Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 물었다."], ["“And seldom, I imagine, any cases of morbid conscience.”", "“그리고 병적인 양심의 사례도 좀처럼 없겠지요.”"], ["He was becoming more and more vexed, and forgetting his lately-caught trick of good manners. Mr.", "그는 점점 더 부아가 나서, 얼마 전에 익혀 둔 좋은 예절의 요령도 잊어 가고 있었다."], ["Lennox saw now that he had annoyed his companion; and as he had talked pretty much for the sake of saying something, and so passing the time away while their road lay together, he was very indifferent as to the exact side he took upon the question, and quietly came round by saying: “To be sure, there is something fine in a man of Mr.", "레녹스 씨는 이제 자기가 동행을 언짢게 했음을 알아차렸다. 그리고 그는 무슨 말이든 하려고, 그래서 길이 같이 이어지는 동안 시간이나 보내려고 이야기했을 뿐이므로 이 문제에서 어느 편에 서든 아무래도 좋았고, 그래서 조용히 이렇게 말하며 태도를 돌렸다."], ["Hale’s age leaving his home of twenty years, and giving up all settled habits, for an idea which was probably erroneous—but that does not matter—an untangible thought.", "“물론 헤일 씨 같은 연세의 분이 이십 년 살던 집을 떠나고 자리 잡힌 습관을 다 버리는 데에는, 그것도 아마 그릇된 생각—아니, 그건 상관없지요—손에 잡히지 않는 어떤 관념 때문에 그러는 데에는 훌륭한 데가 있습니다."], ["One cannot help admiring him, with a mixture of pity in one’s admiration, something like what one feels for Don Quixote.", "감탄하지 않을 수 없지요. 다만 그 감탄에는 연민이 섞이는데, 돈키호테에게 느끼는 것과 비슷한 감정입니다."], ["Such a gentleman as he was too!", "게다가 얼마나 신사다운 분이었는지요!"], ["I shall never forget the refined and simple hospitality he showed to me that last day at Helstone.”", "헬스톤에서 보낸 그 마지막 날에 저에게 베풀어 주신 고상하고 소박한 환대를 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["Only half-mollified, and yet anxious, in order to lull certain qualms of his own conscience, to believe that Mr.", "절반쯤밖에 누그러지지 않았으면서도, 자기 양심의 어떤 가책을 달래기 위해서라도 헤일 씨의 처신에 돈키호테 같은 데가 있었다고 믿고 싶었던 벨 씨는 이렇게 으르렁거리듯 내뱉었다. “그렇지요!"], ["Hale’s conduct had a tinge of Quixotism in it, Mr.", "그리고 선생은 밀턴을 모릅니다. 헬스톤에서 그리로 옮겨 갔으니 얼마나 큰 변화였겠소!"], ["Bell growled out—“Aye! And you don’t know Milton.", "내가 헬스톤에 가 본 지도 여러 해 되었소만, 장담하건대 그곳은 아직도 그대로 서 있을 겁니다."], ["Such a change from Helstone!", "나뭇가지 하나 돌멩이 하나까지 지난 백 년 동안 그래 왔듯이 말이오."], ["It is years since I have been at Helstone—but I’ll answer for it, it is standing there yet—every stick and every stone as it has done for the last century, while Milton!", "그런데 밀턴은! 나는 사오 년마다 그리로 가고, 게다가 거기서 태어났소."], ["I go there every four or five years—and I was born there—yet I do assure you, I often lose my way—aye, among the very piles of warehouses that are built upon my father’s orchard.", "그런데도 분명히 말하는데 나는 곧잘 길을 잃는다오. 그렇소, 우리 아버지 과수원 위에 세워진 바로 그 창고 더미들 틈에서 말이오."], ["Do we part here?", "여기서 헤어지는 겁니까?"], ["Well, good-night, sir; I suppose we shall meet in Harley Street to-morrow morning.”", "그럼 안녕히 가시오, 선생. 내일 아침 할리 가에서 뵙겠지요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLV", "# 제45장"]], [["“Where are the sounds that swam along The buoyant air when I was young; The last vibration now is o’er, And they who listened are no more; Ah!", "“내가 젊던 날 하늘거리는 대기를 타고 흐르던 그 소리들은 어디에 있는가. 마지막 떨림마저 이제는 스러졌고, 귀 기울이던 이들도 이제는 없구나."], ["let me close my eyes and dream.”", "아! 눈을 감고 꿈이나 꾸게 해 다오.”"], ["W. S.", "— W."], ["LANDOR.", "S. 랜더."]], [["The idea of Helstone had been suggested to Mr. Bell’s waking mind by his conversation with Mr.", "헬스톤 생각은 레녹스 씨와 나눈 대화를 통해 깨어 있는 벨 씨의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 밤새도록 그 생각은 그의 꿈속을 마구 헤집고 다녔다."], ["Lennox, and all night long it ran riot through his dreams.", "그는 다시 대학의 지도 교사였다. 지금은 펠로의 자리에 오른 바로 그 대학에서."], ["He was again the tutor in the college where he now held the rank of Fellow; it was again a long vacation, and he was staying with his newly-married friend, the proud husband, and happy Vicar of Helstone.", "다시 긴 방학이었고, 그는 갓 혼인한 벗의 집에 머물고 있었다. 자랑스러운 남편이자 행복한 헬스톤 교구 목사의 집에."], ["Over babbling brooks they took impossible leaps, which seemed to keep them whole days suspended in the air.", "재잘거리는 시내 위로 두 사람은 있을 수 없는 도약을 했고, 그 도약은 며칠씩이나 그들을 공중에 매달아 두는 듯했다."], ["Time and space were not, though all other things seemed real.", "시간도 공간도 없었으나 다른 모든 것은 실재하는 것만 같았다."], ["Every event was measured by the emotions of the mind, not by its actual existence, for existence it had none.", "모든 일은 실제로 있고 없음이 아니라 마음의 감정으로 재어졌으니, 실제로 있는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었기 때문이다."], ["But the trees were gorgeous in their autumnal leafiness—the warm odours of flower and herb came sweet upon the sense—the young wife moved about the house with just that mixture of annoyance at her position, as regarded wealth, with pride in her handsome and devoted husband, which Mr.", "그러나 나무들은 가을빛 잎사귀로 화려했고, 꽃과 풀의 따스한 향내가 감각에 달콤하게 스몄으며, 젊은 아내는 재산에 관한 한 제 처지가 못마땅하다는 기색과 잘생기고 헌신적인 남편에 대한 자부심이 꼭 그만큼씩 뒤섞인 몸짓으로 집 안을 오갔다."], ["Bell had noticed in real life a quarter of a century ago.", "25년 전 실제 삶에서 벨 씨가 눈여겨보았던 바로 그 모습이었다."], ["The dream was so like life that, when he awoke, his present life seemed a dream.", "꿈이 어찌나 삶을 닮았던지, 깨어나 보니 도리어 지금의 삶이 꿈처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Where was he?", "그는 어디에 있는가?"], ["In the close, handsomely furnished room of a London hotel!", "런던 호텔의 답답하고 번듯하게 꾸며진 방 안에!"], ["Where were those who spoke to him, moved around him, touched him, not an instant ago? Dead!", "조금 전까지 그에게 말을 걸고 그의 곁을 오가고 그를 만지던 이들은 어디에 있는가?"], ["buried!", "죽었다!"], ["lost for evermore, as far as earth’s for evermore would extend.", "묻혔다! 이 땅의 영원이 미치는 데까지 영영 사라져 버렸다."], ["He was an old man, so lately exultant in the full strength of manhood.", "그는 노인이었다. 얼마 전만 해도 장년의 온전한 힘으로 의기양양하던 그가."], ["The utter loneliness of his life was insupportable to think about.", "제 삶의 그 지독한 외로움은 떠올리는 것만으로도 견딜 수 없었다."], ["He got up hastily, and tried to forget what never more might be, in a hurried dressing for the breakfast in Harley Street.", "그는 서둘러 자리에서 일어나, 다시는 있을 수 없게 된 것을 잊어 보려고 할리 가의 아침 식사에 갈 채비를 부산히 했다."]], [["He could not attend to all the lawyer’s details, which, as he saw, made Margaret’s eyes dilate, and her lips grow pale, as one by one fate decreed, or so it seemed, every morsel of evidence which would exonerate Frederick, should fall from beneath her feet and disappear.", "그는 변호사가 늘어놓는 세세한 대목을 다 좇을 수 없었다. 그가 보기에 그 대목들은 마거릿의 눈을 커지게 하고 입술을 창백하게 만들었다."], ["Even Mr. Lennox’s well-regulated professional voice took a softer, tenderer tone, as he drew near to the extinction of the last hope.", "프레더릭의 누명을 벗겨 줄 증거라는 증거가 하나씩 차례로, 운명이 그렇게 정해 놓기라도 한 듯, 그녀의 발밑에서 무너져 사라져 갔기 때문이다."], ["It was not that Margaret had not been perfectly aware of the result before.", "마지막 희망이 꺼져 가는 대목에 이르자 레녹스 씨의 절제된 직업적 목소리마저 한결 부드럽고 다정한 어조를 띠었다."], ["It was only that the details of each successive disappointment came with such relentless minuteness to quench all hope, that she at last fairly gave way to tears.", "마거릿이 그 결말을 미리 온전히 알지 못했던 것은 아니다. 다만 잇따른 낙담의 세세한 대목들이 어찌나 가차 없이 촘촘하게 다가와 온갖 희망을 꺼뜨렸던지, 그녀는 마침내 눈물을 쏟고야 말았다."], ["Mr. Lennox stopped reading.", "레녹스 씨가 읽기를 멈추었다."]], [["“I had better not go on,”", "“더 읽지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.”"], ["said he, in a concerned voice.", "그가 걱정스러운 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It was a foolish proposal of mine.", "“제 어리석은 제안이었습니다."], ["Lieutenant Hale,”", "헤일 중위는,”"], ["and even this giving him the title of the service from which he had so harshly been expelled, was soothing to Margaret.", "그토록 모질게 내쫓긴 그 군대의 계급으로 오빠를 불러 주는 것만으로도 마거릿에게는 위로가 되었다."], ["“Lieutenant Hale is happy now; more secure in fortune and future prospects than he could ever have been in the navy; and has, doubtless, adopted his wife’s country as his own.”", "“헤일 중위는 지금 행복합니다. 해군에 남아 있었더라면 결코 누리지 못했을 만큼 재산도 앞날도 든든하고, 틀림없이 아내의 나라를 제 나라로 삼았을 겁니다.”"]], [["“That is it,”", "“그게 바로 그거예요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“It seems so selfish in me to regret it,”", "“그걸 아쉬워하다니 제가 너무 이기적인 것 같아요.” 그녀는 웃어 보이려 애썼다."], ["trying to smile, “and yet he is lost to me, and I am so lonely.” Mr.", "“그래도 오빠는 제게서 사라졌고, 저는 이렇게 외로워요.”"], ["Lennox turned over his papers, and wished that he were as rich and prosperous as he believed he should be some day. Mr.", "레녹스 씨는 서류를 뒤적이며, 언젠가는 그리되리라 믿는 만큼 지금 제가 부유하고 번듯했으면 하고 바랐다."], ["Bell blew his nose, but, otherwise, he also kept silence; and Margaret, in a minute or two, had apparently recovered her usual composure.", "벨 씨는 코를 풀었으나, 그 밖에는 그 역시 잠자코 있었다. 그리고 마거릿은 일이 분 만에 겉으로는 여느 때의 침착함을 되찾은 듯했다."], ["She thanked Mr. Lennox very courteously for his trouble; all the more courteously and graciously because she was conscious that, by her behaviour, he might probably be led to imagine that he had given her needless pain.", "그녀는 수고해 준 레녹스 씨에게 무척 정중하게 고마움을 표했다."], ["Yet it was pain she would not have been without.", "제 태도 때문에 그가 공연히 그녀에게 아픔을 주었다고 여길지도 모른다는 것을 의식했기에 더욱 정중하고 상냥하게. 그러나 그것은 겪지 않았더라면 좋았을 아픔은 아니었다."]], [["Mr. Bell came up to wish her good-bye.", "벨 씨가 작별 인사를 하려고 다가왔다."]], [["“Margaret!”", "“마거릿!”"], ["said he, as he fumbled with his gloves, “I am going down to Helstone to-morrow, to look at the old place.", "그가 장갑을 만지작거리며 말했다. “나는 내일 헬스톤에 내려가 옛 고장을 좀 볼 참이다."], ["Would you like to come with me?", "나와 함께 가겠니?"], ["Or would it give you too much pain?", "아니면 너한테 너무 괴로운 일이겠느냐?"], ["Speak out, don’t be afraid.”", "솔직히 말해 보렴, 겁내지 말고.”"]], [["“Oh, Mr. Bell,”", "“오, 벨 씨.”"], ["said she—and could say no more.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["But she took his old gouty hand, and kissed it.", "그러나 통풍으로 마디가 굵어진 그의 늙은 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Come, come; that’s enough,”", "“자, 자, 그만하면 됐다.”"], ["said he, reddening with awkwardness.", "그가 겸연쩍어 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I suppose your aunt Shaw will trust you with me.", "“네 쇼 이모도 너를 내게 맡겨 주겠지."], ["We’ll go to-morrow morning, and we shall get there about two o’clock, I fancy.", "내일 아침에 떠나면 두 시쯤 닿을 게다. 요기를 좀 하고 작은 여관에 저녁을 시켜 두자꾸나."], ["We’ll take a snack, and order dinner at the little inn—the Lennard Arms, it used to be—and go and get an appetite in the forest.", "예전에는 레너드 암스라고 했지. 그러고는 숲으로 나가 입맛을 돋우는 거야."], ["Can you stand it, Margaret?", "견딜 수 있겠느냐, 마거릿?"], ["It will be a trial, I know, to both of us, but it will be a pleasure to me, at least.", "우리 둘 다에게 시련이 되리라는 건 나도 안다만, 적어도 내게는 기쁨이 될 게다."], ["And there we’ll dine—it will be but doe-venison, if we can get it at all—and then I’ll take my nap, while you go out and see old friends.", "거기서 저녁을 먹고, 구할 수만 있다면 암사슴 고기밖에 없겠지만, 그런 다음 나는 낮잠을 잘 테니 너는 옛 벗들을 만나러 나가거라. 기차 사고만 없다면 성한 몸으로 무사히 돌려보내 주마."], ["I’ll give you back safe and sound, barring railway accidents, and I’ll insure your life for a thousand pounds before starting, which may be some comfort to your relations; but otherwise, I’ll bring you back to Mrs.", "떠나기 전에 네 목숨에 1,000파운드 보험을 들어 두마. 그러면 네 친척들에게도 얼마쯤 위안이 되겠지."], ["Shaw by lunch time on Friday.", "아무 일 없으면 금요일 점심때까지 쇼 부인에게 데려다주마."], ["So, if you say yes, I’ll just go upstairs and propose it.”", "그러니 네가 좋다고 하면 나는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 그 얘기를 꺼내겠다.”"]], [["“It’s no use my trying to say how much I shall like it,”", "“얼마나 좋을지 말로는 도무지 표현할 수가 없어요.”"], ["said Margaret, through her tears.", "마거릿이 눈물 사이로 말했다."]], [["“Well, then, prove your gratitude by keeping those fountains of yours dry for the next two days.", "“그렇다면 앞으로 이틀 동안 그 눈물샘을 마른 채로 두어서 고마움을 증명해 보렴."], ["If you don’t, I shall feel queer myself about the lachrymal ducts, and I don’t like that.”", "그러지 않으면 나도 눈물샘이 이상해질 것 같은데, 나는 그런 게 싫으니 말이다.”"]], [["“I won’t cry a drop,”", "“한 방울도 울지 않을게요.”"], ["said Margaret, winking her eyes to shake the tears off her eyelashes, and forcing a smile.", "마거릿이 속눈썹에 맺힌 눈물을 떨구려고 눈을 깜빡이며 억지로 미소를 지은 채 말했다."]], [["“There’s my good girl.", "“그래야 착한 아이지."], ["Then we’ll go upstairs and settle it all.”", "그럼 위층으로 올라가 다 정해 놓자꾸나.”"], ["Margaret was in a state of almost trembling eagerness, while Mr.", "벨 씨가 쇼 이모와 그 계획을 의논하는 동안 마거릿은 거의 떨릴 만큼 들뜬 상태였다."], ["Bell discussed his plan with her aunt Shaw, who was first startled, then doubtful and perplexed, and in the end, yielding rather to the rough force of Mr.", "쇼 이모는 처음에는 깜짝 놀랐고, 이어 미심쩍어하며 난감해했으며, 끝내는 제 확신보다는 벨 씨가 내뱉는 말의 거친 힘에 못 이겨 물러서고 말았다."], ["Bell’s words than to her own conviction; for to the last, whether it was right or wrong, proper or improper, she could not settle to her own satisfaction, till Margaret’s safe return, the happy fulfilment of the project, gave her decision enough to say, “she was sure it had been a very kind thought of Mr.", "그것이 옳은지 그른지, 온당한지 온당치 않은지 그녀는 끝까지 스스로 흡족할 만큼 정하지 못했으니, 마거릿이 무사히 돌아와 그 계획이 흐뭇하게 이루어지고 나서야 비로소 이렇게 말할 만한 결단이 섰던 것이다."], ["Bell’s, and just what she herself had been wishing for Margaret, as giving her the very change which she required, after all the anxious time she had had.”", "그것은 참으로 벨 씨다운 고마운 생각이었고, 그동안 그 애가 겪은 온갖 마음 졸이는 시간 끝에 꼭 필요로 하던 바로 그 변화를 준다는 점에서 자기 역시 마거릿에게 바라 마지않던 바로 그것이었다고 확신한다고."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI", "# 제46장"]], [["“So on those happy days of yore Oft as I dare to dwell once more, Still must I miss the friends so tried, Whom Death has severed from my side.", "“그리하여 지난날의 그 행복하던 나날에 다시 한 번 마음을 머무르려 할 때마다, 나는 여전히 그리워해야 하네. 죽음이 내 곁에서 갈라놓은, 그토록 미덥던 벗들을.”"]], [["But ever when true friendship binds, Spirit it is that spirit finds; In spirit then our bliss we found, In spirit yet to them I’m bound.”", "“그러나 참된 우정이 맺어질 때는 언제나 영혼이 영혼을 찾아내는 법. 그때 우리는 영혼으로 기쁨을 얻었으니, 지금도 나는 영혼으로 그들에게 매여 있네.”"], ["UHLAND.", "— 울란트."]], [["Margaret was ready long before the appointed time, and had leisure enough to cry a little, quietly, when unobserved, and to smile brightly when any one looked at her.", "마거릿은 정해진 시각보다 한참 전에 채비를 마쳤고, 아무도 보지 않을 때 조용히 조금 울고 누군가 쳐다보면 환하게 웃을 만한 여유까지 있었다."], ["Her last alarm was lest they should be too late and miss the train; but no!", "마지막 걱정은 늦어서 기차를 놓치지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그러나 아니었다."], ["they were all in time; and she breathed freely and happily at length, seated in the carriage opposite to Mr.", "모두 제시간에 닿았다. 그리하여 그녀는 마침내 편안하고 행복하게 숨을 쉬었다."], ["Bell, and whirling away past the well-known stations; seeing the old south country-towns and hamlets sleeping in the warm light of the pure sun, which gave a yet ruddier colour to their tiled roofs, so different to the cold slates of the north.", "벨 씨와 마주 보고 객차에 앉아 낯익은 역들을 지나 쏜살같이 달려가면서, 남부의 오래된 소읍과 마을들이 맑은 햇살의 따스한 빛 속에 잠들어 있는 것을 바라보면서. 그 햇빛은 기와지붕에 한층 더 붉은 빛을 더해 주었으니, 북부의 차가운 슬레이트와는 어찌나 다른지."], ["Broods of pigeons hovered around these peaked quaint gables, slowly settling here and there, and ruffling their soft, shiny feathers, as if exposing every fibre to the delicious warmth.", "비둘기 떼가 뾰족하고 예스러운 박공 언저리를 맴돌다 여기저기 천천히 내려앉아 부드럽고 반들거리는 깃털을 부풀렸다. 마치 깃털 한 올 한 올을 그 감미로운 온기에 내맡기려는 듯이."], ["There were few people about at the stations, it almost seemed as if they were too lazily content to wish to travel; none of the bustle and stir that Margaret had noticed in her two journeys on the London and North-Western line.", "역마다 사람은 별로 없었다. 어쩐지 너무 나른하게 만족하여 여행할 마음조차 없는 듯이 보였다."], ["Later on in the year, this line of railway should be stirring and alive with rich pleasure-seekers; but as to the constant going to and fro of busy tradespeople it would always be widely different from the northern lines.", "마거릿이 런던·노스웨스턴 선을 두 차례 오가며 보았던 그 부산함과 술렁임은 하나도 없었다. 해가 더 깊어지면 이 노선도 넉넉한 유람객들로 북적이며 활기를 띠겠지만, 바쁜 장사치들이 끊임없이 오가는 것으로 치자면 북부 노선과는 언제까지나 크게 다를 터였다."], ["Here a spectator or two stood lounging at nearly every station, with his hands in his pockets, so absorbed in the simple act of watching, that it made the travellers wonder what he could find to do when the train whirled away, and only the blank of a railway, some sheds, and a distant field or two were left for him to gaze upon.", "이곳에서는 거의 모든 역마다 구경꾼 한둘이 주머니에 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁거리며 서 있었는데, 그저 바라보는 그 단순한 행위에 어찌나 몰두해 있는지, 기차가 휙 떠나 버리고 텅 빈 철길과 헛간 몇 채와 저 멀리 밭 한두 뙈기만 바라볼 거리로 남으면 저 사람은 대체 무엇을 하며 지낼까 하고 나그네들이 궁금해할 지경이었다."], ["The hot air danced over the golden stillness of the land, farm after farm was left behind, each reminding Margaret of German Idylls—of Herman and Dorothea—of Evangeline.", "더운 공기가 황금빛으로 고요한 대지 위에서 아른거렸고 농가가 하나둘 뒤로 흘러갔는데, 그 하나하나가 마거릿에게 독일의 목가를, 「헤르만과 도로테아」를, 「에반젤린」을 떠올리게 했다."], ["From this waking dream she was roused.", "그 깨어 있는 꿈에서 그녀는 깨어났다."], ["It was the place to leave the train and take the fly to Helstone.", "기차에서 내려 헬스톤행 삯마차를 탈 곳이었다."], ["And now sharper feelings came shooting through her heart, whether pain or pleasure she could hardly tell.", "이제 한결 날카로운 감정이 그녀의 가슴을 꿰뚫었는데, 그것이 아픔인지 기쁨인지 그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다. 1마일 1마일마다 추억이 배어 있었다."], ["Every mile was redolent of associations, which she would not have missed for the world, but each of which made her cry upon “the days that are no more,” with ineffable longing.", "세상 무엇과도 바꾸고 싶지 않은 추억이었으나, 그 하나하나가 이루 말할 수 없는 그리움으로 “이제는 없는 날들”을 부르짖게 만들었다."], ["The last time she had passed along this road was when she had left it with her father and mother—the day, the season, had been gloomy, and she herself hopeless, but they were there with her.", "그녀가 이 길을 마지막으로 지난 것은 아버지 어머니와 함께 이곳을 떠나던 때였다. 그날도 그 계절도 음울했고 그녀 자신도 앞이 캄캄했지만, 두 분은 그녀 곁에 있었다."], ["Now she was alone, an orphan, and they, strangely, had gone away from her, and vanished from the face of the earth.", "이제 그녀는 혼자였다. 고아였다."], ["It hurt her to see the Helstone road so flooded in the sunlight, and every turn and every familiar tree so precisely the same in its summer glory as it had been in former years.", "두 분은 이상하게도 그녀 곁을 떠나 이 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸다. 헬스톤 가는 길이 그토록 햇빛에 잠겨 있는 것이, 굽이굽이마다 낯익은 나무 하나하나가 지난 세월과 조금도 다름없이 여름의 영광 속에 그대로인 것이 그녀에게는 아프게 다가왔다."], ["Nature felt no change and was ever young.", "자연은 아무 변화도 느끼지 않았고 언제까지나 젊었다."]], [["Mr. Bell knew something of what would be passing through her mind, and wisely and kindly held his tongue.", "벨 씨는 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 지나가고 있을지 어느 정도 알았기에 슬기롭고도 자상하게 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["They drove up to the Lennard Arms; half farm-house, half-inn, standing a little apart from the road, as much as to say, that the host did not so depend on the custom of travellers, as to have to court it by any obtrusiveness; they, rather, must seek him out.", "두 사람은 레너드 암스로 마차를 몰아 올라갔다. 반은 농가요 반은 여관인 그곳은 길에서 조금 물러나 서 있었는데, 마치 주인이 나그네들의 발길에 그리 매여 있지 않아 나서서 붙잡을 것도 없으니 오히려 나그네 쪽에서 찾아와야 한다고 말하는 듯했다."], ["The house fronted the village green; and right before it stood an immemorial lime-tree benched all round, in some hidden recesses of whose leafy wealth hung the grim escutcheon of the Lennards.", "집은 마을 공터를 마주 보고 있었고, 바로 앞에는 아득한 옛날부터 서 있던 보리수 한 그루가 둘레에 온통 걸상을 두른 채 서 있었으며, 그 무성한 잎사귀의 어느 은밀한 구석에는 레너드 집안의 음침한 문장이 걸려 있었다."], ["The door of the inn stood wide open, but there was no hospitable hurry to receive the travellers.", "여관 문은 활짝 열려 있었으나 나그네를 맞으려는 살가운 서두름은 없었다."], ["When the landlady did appear—and they might have abstracted many an article first—she gave them a kind welcome, almost as if they had been invited guests, and apologised for her coming having been so delayed, by saying, that it was hay-time, and the provisions for the men had to be sent a-field, and she had been too busy packing up the baskets to hear the noise of wheels over the road, which, since they left the highway, ran over soft short turf.", "마침내 안주인이 나타났을 때에는 — 그 사이에 물건 여러 개쯤은 슬쩍해 갈 수도 있었으리라 — 마치 초대받은 손님이라도 되는 양 따뜻이 맞아 주었고, 이렇게 늦은 것을 사과하며 지금이 건초 때라 일꾼들 먹을 것을 들로 내보내야 해서 바구니를 꾸리느라 너무 바빠 길 위로 굴러오는 바퀴 소리를 못 들었다고 했다. 큰길을 벗어난 뒤로는 바퀴가 부드럽고 짧은 잔디 위를 달렸던 것이다."]], [["“Why, bless me!”", "“아이고, 세상에!”"], ["exclaimed she, as at the end of her apology a glint of sunshine showed her Margaret’s face, hitherto unobserved in that shady parlour.", "사과가 끝날 무렵 한 줄기 햇살이 그늘진 응접실에서 여태 눈에 띄지 않던 마거릿의 얼굴을 비추자 안주인이 소리쳤다."], ["“It’s Miss Hale, Jenny,”", "“헤일 아가씨야, 제니!”"], ["said she, running to the door, and calling to her daughter.", "그녀는 문으로 달려가며 딸을 불렀다."], ["“Come here, come directly, it’s Miss Hale!”", "“이리 온, 어서 와 봐, 헤일 아가씨셔!”"], ["And then she went up to Margaret, and shook hands with motherly fondness.", "그러고는 마거릿에게 다가와 어머니 같은 다정함으로 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“And how are you all?", "“다들 어떻게 지내세요?"], ["How’s the Vicar and Miss Dixon?", "목사님하고 딕슨 아주머니는요?"], ["The Vicar above all!", "무엇보다 목사님은요!"], ["God bless him!", "하느님의 복이 있으시길!"], ["We’ve never ceased to be sorry that he left.”", "그분이 떠나신 게 우리는 내내 서운했다니까요.”"]], [["Margaret tried to speak and tell her of her father’s death; of her mother’s it was evident that Mrs.", "마거릿은 아버지의 죽음을 말하려 애썼다. 어머니의 죽음은 퍼키스 부인이 그 이름을 빼놓은 것으로 보아 이미 알고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["Purkis was aware, from her omission of her name. But she choked in the effort, and could only touch her deep mourning, and say the one word, “Papa.”", "그러나 그녀는 말을 꺼내려다 목이 메었고, 그저 자기의 짙은 상복을 만져 보이며 “아버지”라는 한마디밖에 하지 못했다."]], [["“Surely, sir, it’s never so!” said Mrs.", "“설마, 나리, 그럴 리가요!”"], ["Purkis, turning to Mr. Bell for confirmation of the sad suspicion that now entered her mind.", "퍼키스 부인은 방금 머릿속에 떠오른 그 슬픈 짐작을 확인받으려고 벨 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“There was a gentleman here in the spring—might have been as long ago as last winter—who told us a deal of Mr.", "“봄에 어떤 신사분이 여기 오셨었는데, 하기야 지난겨울만큼 오래전이었을지도 모르지요, 헤일 씨하고 마거릿 아가씨 이야기를 잔뜩 해 주셨어요."], ["Hale and Miss Margaret; and he said Mrs.", "헤일 부인께서 돌아가셨다고, 가엾은 마님이라고요."], ["Hale was gone, poor lady. But never a word of the Vicar’s being ailing!”", "그렇지만 목사님이 편찮으시다는 말씀은 한마디도 없었는데요!”"]], [["“It is so, however,”", "“그렇지만 사실이오.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“He died quite suddenly, when on a visit to me at Oxford.", "“옥스퍼드로 나를 찾아오셨다가 아주 갑작스레 세상을 떠나셨소."], ["He was a good man, Mrs.", "좋은 분이었지요, 퍼키스 부인."], ["Purkis, and there’s many of us that might be thankful to have as calm an end as his.", "우리 가운데 그분처럼 평온한 마지막을 맞을 수 있다면 고마워할 사람이 많을 게요."], ["Come, Margaret, my dear!", "자, 마거릿, 얘야!"], ["Her father was my oldest friend, and she’s my god-daughter, so I thought we would just come down together and see the old place; and I know of old you can give us comfortable rooms and a capital dinner.", "이 아이 아버지가 내 가장 오랜 벗이고 이 아이는 내 대녀라, 우리 둘이 함께 내려와 옛 고장을 좀 보자 싶었소. 여기서 편안한 방과 훌륭한 저녁상을 내준다는 건 내가 예전부터 알고 있고."], ["You don’t remember me I see, but my name is Bell, and once or twice when the parsonage has been full, I’ve slept here, and tasted your good ale.”", "나를 기억하지 못하시는 모양인데, 내 이름은 벨이오. 목사관이 꽉 찼을 때 한두 번 여기서 자고 댁의 맛좋은 에일도 맛본 적이 있소.”"]], [["“To be sure; I ask your pardon; but you see I was taken up with Miss Hale.", "“아이고, 그럼요. 용서하세요."], ["Let me show you to a room, Miss Margaret, where you can take off your bonnet, and wash your face.", "그런데 보시다시피 제가 헤일 아가씨한테 정신이 팔려서요. 마거릿 아가씨, 방으로 안내해 드릴게요."], ["It’s only this very morning I plunged some fresh-gathered roses head downward in the water-jug, for thought I, perhaps some one will be coming, and there’s nothing so sweet as spring-water scented by a musk rose or two.", "거기서 보닛도 벗으시고 세수도 하시고요. 바로 오늘 아침에 갓 딴 장미를 물병에 거꾸로 꽂아 두었답니다."], ["To think of the Vicar being dead!", "혹시 누가 오실지도 모른다 싶어서요. 사향장미 한두 송이 향이 밴 샘물만큼 향긋한 게 없거든요."], ["Well, to be sure, we must all die; only that gentleman said, he was quite picking up after his trouble about Mrs.", "목사님이 돌아가시다니! 하기야 우리 모두 죽기야 하지요."], ["Hale’s death.”", "그런데 그 신사분 말씀으로는 헤일 부인 상을 당하신 뒤로 많이 추스르셨다던데요.”"]], [["“Come down to me, Mrs. Purkis, after you have attended to Miss Hale.", "“퍼키스 부인, 헤일 양을 살펴 드린 다음에 내게로 내려오시오."], ["I want to have a consultation with you about dinner.”", "저녁상 일로 상의 좀 합시다.”"]], [["The little casement window in Margaret’s bed-chamber was almost filled up with rose and vine branches; but, pushing them aside, and stretching a little out, she could see the tops of the parsonage chimneys above the trees, and distinguish many a well-known line through the leaves.", "마거릿의 침실에 난 자그마한 여닫이창은 장미와 포도 덩굴 가지로 거의 다 막혀 있었다. 그러나 그것을 옆으로 밀치고 몸을 조금 내밀자 나무들 위로 목사관 굴뚝 꼭대기가 보였고, 잎사귀 사이로 낯익은 윤곽이 여럿 분간되었다."]], [["“Aye!” said Mrs. Purkis, smoothing down the bed, and despatching Jenny for an armful of lavender-scented towels, “times is changed, miss; our new Vicar has seven children, and is building a nursery ready for more, just where the arbour and tool-house used to be in old times. And he has had new grates put in, and a plate-glass window in the drawing-room. He and his wife are stirring people, and have done a deal of good; at least they say it’s doing good; if it were not, I should call it turning things upside down for very little purpose. The new Vicar is a teetotaller, miss, and a magistrate, and his wife has a deal of receipts for economical cooking, and is for making bread without yeast; and they both talk so much, and both at a time, that they knock one down as it were, and it’s not till they’re gone, and one’s a little at peace, that one can think that there were things one might have said on one’s own side of the question. He’ll be after the men’s cans in the hay-field, and peeping in; and then there’ll be an ado because it’s not ginger-beer, but I can’t help it. My mother and my grandmother before me sent good malt liquor to hay-makers, and took salts and senna when anything ailed them; and I must e’en go on in their ways, though Mrs. Hepworth does want to give me comfits instead of medicine, which, as she says, is a deal pleasanter, only I’ve no faith in it. But I must go, miss, though I’m wanting to hear many a thing; I’ll come back to you before long.”", "“아유!” 퍼키스 부인은 침대를 매만지고 제니를 시켜 라벤더 향이 밴 수건을 한 아름 가져오게 하며 말했다. “세상이 변했어요, 아가씨. 새로 오신 목사님은 아이가 일곱이나 되는데 더 낳으려고 아이방을 짓고 계세요. 옛날에 정자하고 연장 광이 있던 바로 그 자리에다요. 화덕도 새것으로 갈아 넣으시고 응접실에는 판유리 창을 다셨고요. 그분하고 사모님은 부지런한 분들이라 좋은 일도 많이 하셨어요. 하기야 좋은 일이라고들 하니까요. 그게 아니라면 저는 별것도 아닌 일로 죄다 뒤집어엎었다고 하겠지만요. 새 목사님은 금주가시고요, 아가씨, 치안판사이기도 하시고요. 사모님은 알뜰한 요리법을 잔뜩 아셔서 효모 없이 빵을 만들자고 하세요. 두 분 다 어찌나 말씀이 많은지, 그것도 한꺼번에 하시니 사람을 아주 쓰러뜨려 놓는다니까요. 그러다 두 분이 가시고 좀 조용해지고 나서야 내 쪽에서도 할 말이 있었구나 하는 생각이 든다니까요. 그분은 건초밭 일꾼들 술통을 쫓아다니며 들여다보실 거예요. 그러면 그게 생강 맥주가 아니라고 한바탕 소동이 나겠지만, 저야 어쩌겠어요. 우리 어머니도 그 앞의 우리 할머니도 건초 일꾼들한테 좋은 맥주를 내보냈고, 어디가 아프면 소금하고 센나를 먹었지요. 저도 그 길을 그대로 갈 수밖에요. 헵워스 사모님은 약 대신 사탕을 주고 싶어 하시고 그게 훨씬 기분 좋다고 하시지만, 저는 그런 건 못 믿겠어요. 그런데 가 봐야겠네요, 아가씨. 여쭙고 싶은 게 많은데도요. 이따 곧 다시 올게요.”"]], [["Mr.", "마거릿이 아래층에 내려오니 벨 씨는 그녀를 위해 딸기와 크림, 통밀빵 한 덩이, 우유 한 병을 준비해 두고 있었다(자기 혼자 즐길 것으로는 스틸턴 치즈와 포트와인 한 병도 곁들여)."], ["Bell had strawberries and cream, a loaf of brown bread, and a jug of milk (together with a Stilton cheese and a bottle of port for his own private refreshment), ready for Margaret on her coming down stairs; and after this rustic luncheon they set out for a walk, hardly knowing in what direction to turn, so many old familiar inducements were there in each.", "이 소박한 점심을 마치고 두 사람은 산책을 나섰는데, 어느 쪽으로 발길을 돌려야 할지 좀처럼 정하지 못했다. 어느 길이든 정겹고 낯익은 것들이 그리로 오라 손짓했기 때문이다."]], [["“Shall we go past the vicarage?”", "“목사관 쪽으로 지나가 볼까?”"], ["asked Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 물었다."]], [["“No, not yet.", "“아뇨, 아직요."], ["We will go this way, and make a round so as to come back by it,”", "이쪽으로 가서 한 바퀴 돌아 그리로 돌아오는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."]], [["Here and there old trees had been felled the autumn before; or a squatter’s cottage roughly-built and decaying cottage had disappeared.", "여기저기 지난가을에 베어 낸 늙은 나무들이 있었고, 무단 정착민이 엉성하게 지어 놓았던 다 쓰러져 가는 오두막도 사라지고 없었다."], ["Margaret missed them each and all, and grieved over them like old friends.", "마거릿은 그 하나하나가 아쉬웠고 오랜 벗을 잃은 듯 서러웠다."], ["They came past the spot where she and Mr. Lennox had sketched.", "두 사람은 그녀와 레녹스 씨가 그림을 그리던 자리를 지났다."], ["The white, lightning-scarred trunk of the venerable beech, among whose roots they had sate down was there no more; the old man, the inhabitant of the ruinous cottage, was dead; the cottage had been pulled down, and a new one, tidy and respectable, had been built in its stead.", "그 뿌리 사이에 앉았던, 벼락 맞은 자국이 허옇게 남은 늙은 너도밤나무 둥치는 이제 없었다. 다 허물어져 가던 오두막에 살던 노인은 죽었고, 오두막은 헐렸으며, 그 자리에 말끔하고 번듯한 새 오두막이 들어서 있었다."], ["There was a small garden on the place where the beech-tree had been.", "너도밤나무가 서 있던 자리에는 자그마한 텃밭이 생겨 있었다."]], [["“I did not think I had been so old,”", "“제가 이렇게 나이를 먹은 줄은 몰랐어요.”"], ["said Margaret after a pause of silence; and she turned away sighing.", "한동안 말이 없다가 마거릿이 말했다. 그러고는 한숨을 쉬며 돌아섰다."]], [["“Yes!”", "“그렇지!”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“It is the first changes among familiar things that make such a mystery of time to the young, afterwards we lose the sense of the mysterious.", "“젊은이에게 시간을 그토록 알 수 없는 것으로 만드는 건 낯익은 것들 사이에 일어나는 첫 변화라네. 그 뒤로는 그 알 수 없다는 느낌마저 잃어버리지."], ["I take changes in all I see as a matter of course.", "나는 눈에 보이는 모든 변화를 당연한 일로 받아들이네."], ["The instability of all human things is familiar to me, to you it is new and oppressive.”", "인간사 모두가 덧없다는 건 내게는 익숙한 일이지만, 자네에게는 새롭고 짓누르는 일이지.”"]], [["“Let us go on to see little Susan,”", "“꼬마 수전을 보러 가요.”"], ["said Margaret, drawing her companion up a grassy road-way, leading under the shadow of a forest glade.", "마거릿이 숲 속 빈터의 그늘 아래로 이어지는 풀 덮인 길로 동행을 이끌며 말했다."]], [["“With all my heart, though I have not an idea who little Susan may be.", "“기꺼이. 꼬마 수전이 누구인지는 전혀 짐작도 안 가지만 말일세."], ["But I have a kindness for all Susans, for simple Susan’s sake.”", "그래도 나는 「소박한 수전」을 봐서라도 수전이라는 이름은 다 좋아하네.”"]], [["“My little Susan was disappointed when I left without wishing her good-bye; and it has been on my conscience ever since, that I gave her pain which a little more exertion on my part might have prevented.", "“제 꼬마 수전은 제가 작별 인사도 없이 떠나서 서운해했어요. 조금만 더 애썼으면 막을 수 있었을 아픔을 그 애에게 준 것이 그 뒤로 내내 마음에 걸렸고요."], ["But it is a long way.", "그런데 길이 멀어요."], ["Are you sure you will not be tired?”", "정말 힘들지 않으시겠어요?”"]], [["“Quite sure.", "“아무렴."], ["That is, if you don’t walk so fast.", "자네가 그렇게 빨리 걷지만 않는다면 말이지."], ["You see, here there are no views that can give one an excuse for stopping to take breath.", "보다시피 여기엔 숨 돌리려고 멈춰 설 핑계가 될 만한 경치가 없단 말이야."], ["You would think it romantic to be walking with a person ‘fat and scant o’ breath’ if I were Hamlet, Prince of Denmark.", "내가 덴마크 왕자 햄릿이라면 ‘살찌고 숨 가쁜’ 사람과 함께 걷는 것이 낭만적이라고 여기겠지."], ["Have compassion on my infirmities for his sake.”", "그를 봐서라도 이 늙은이의 병약함을 좀 가엾이 여겨 주게.”"]], [["“I will walk slower for your own sake.", "“벨 씨를 위해서 천천히 걸을게요."], ["I like you twenty times better than Hamlet.”", "저는 햄릿보다 벨 씨가 스무 배는 더 좋아요.”"]], [["“On the principle that a living ass is better than a dead lion?”", "“산 나귀가 죽은 사자보다 낫다는 이치로?”"]], [["“Perhaps so.", "“아마 그렇겠죠."], ["I don’t analyse my feelings.”", "저는 제 마음을 따져 보지 않아요.”"]], [["“I am content to take your liking me, without examining too curiously into the materials it is made of.", "“나는 자네가 나를 좋아한다는 것만으로 만족하네. 그것이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 지나치게 캐묻지 않고 말이야."], ["Only we need not walk at a snail’s pace.”", "다만 달팽이 걸음으로 걸을 것까지는 없겠지.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋아요."], ["Walk at your own pace, and I will follow.", "벨 씨 걸음대로 걸으세요, 제가 따라갈게요."], ["Or stop still and meditate, like the Hamlet you compare yourself to, if I go too fast.”", "아니면 제가 너무 빨리 가거든 스스로 견주어 보신 그 햄릿처럼 가만히 멈춰 서서 사색에 잠기시든가요.”"]], [["“Thank you.", "“고맙네."], ["But as my mother has not murdered my father, and afterwards married my uncle, I shouldn’t know what to think about, unless it were balancing the chances of our having a well-cooked dinner or not.", "그렇지만 내 어머니가 내 아버지를 죽이고 나서 내 숙부와 혼인한 것도 아니니, 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 알 수가 있어야지. 저녁상이 잘 차려질 가망이 얼마나 되는지 저울질하는 것 말고는 말이야."], ["What do you think?”", "자네 생각은 어떤가?”"]], [["“I am in good hopes.", "“저는 기대가 커요."], ["She used to be considered a famous cook as far as Helstone opinion went.”", "헬스톤 사람들이 보기에 그분은 이름난 요리 솜씨였거든요.”"]], [["“But have you considered the distraction of mind produced by all this haymaking?”", "“하지만 이 건초 만들기 소동 때문에 마음이 산란해진 것은 생각해 봤나?”"]], [["Margaret felt all Mr. Bell’s kindness in trying to make cheerful talk about nothing, to endeavour to prevent her from thinking too curiously about the past.", "마거릿은 아무것도 아닌 일로 유쾌한 이야기를 꾸며 내며 자기가 지난날을 너무 골똘히 곱씹지 않게 하려는 벨 씨의 배려를 모두 느끼고 있었다."], ["But she would rather have gone over these dear-loved walks in silence, if indeed she were not ungrateful enough to wish that she might have been alone.", "그러나 그녀로서는 이 정겨운 길들을 차라리 말없이 걷고 싶었다. 혼자였으면 좋겠다고 바랄 만큼 은혜를 모르는 것은 아니라 하더라도."]], [["They reached the cottage where Susan’s widowed mother lived.", "두 사람은 수전의 홀어머니가 사는 오두막에 이르렀다."], ["Susan was not there.", "수전은 없었다."], ["She was gone to the parochial school.", "교구 학교에 가고 없었다."], ["Margaret was disappointed, and the poor woman saw it, and began to make a kind of apology.", "마거릿은 실망했고, 그 가엾은 여인은 그것을 알아채고는 변명 비슷한 것을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh! it is quite right,”", "“아니에요, 잘된 일이죠.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“I am very glad to hear it.", "“정말 반가운 소식이에요."], ["I might have thought of it.", "제가 미처 생각했어야 했는데."], ["Only she used to stop at home with you.”", "다만 예전에는 아주머니 곁에 붙어 있었잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes, she did; and I miss her sadly.", "“네, 그랬죠. 그래서 어찌나 허전한지 몰라요."], ["I used to teach her what little I knew at nights.", "밤이면 제가 아는 얼마 안 되는 걸 가르치곤 했지요."], ["It were not much to be sure.", "대단할 건 없었지만요."], ["But she were getting such a handy girl, that I miss her sore.", "그래도 그 애가 어찌나 야무진 아이가 되어 가던지, 없으니 사무치게 아쉽구먼요."], ["But she’s a deal above me in learning now.”", "그래도 이젠 배운 게 저보다 한참 위지요.”"], ["And the mother sighed.", "그러고는 어머니가 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“I’m all wrong,” growled Mr.", "“내가 다 틀렸구먼.”"], ["Bell. “Don’t mind what I say.", "벨 씨가 투덜거렸다."], ["I’m a hundred years behind the world.", "“내 말은 신경 쓰지 마시오."], ["But I should say, that the child was getting a better and simpler, and more natural education stopping at home, and helping her mother, and learning to read a chapter in the New Testament every night by her side, than from all the schooling under the sun.”", "나는 세상보다 100년은 뒤처진 사람이니. 그래도 내 말은, 그 아이가 집에 남아 어머니를 도우며 밤마다 곁에서 신약 한 장씩 읽는 법을 배우는 편이 세상의 온갖 학교 교육을 다 받는 것보다 더 낫고 더 소박하고 더 자연스러운 가르침이었으리라는 게요.”"]], [["Margaret did not want to encourage him to go on by replying to him, and so prolonging the discussion before the mother.", "마거릿은 대꾸를 해서 그가 말을 이어 가도록 부추기고 싶지 않았고, 어머니 앞에서 논쟁을 길게 끌고 싶지도 않았다."], ["So she turned to her and asked,", "그래서 여인 쪽으로 돌아서서 물었다."]], [["“How is old Betty Barnes?”", "“베티 반스 할머니는 어떻게 지내세요?”"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“모르겠구먼요.”"], ["said the woman rather shortly.", "여인이 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["“We’se not friends.”", "“우린 사이가 틀어졌어요.”"]], [["“Why not?”", "“왜요?”"], ["asked Margaret, who had formerly been the peace-maker of the village.", "예전에 마을의 화해꾼 노릇을 하던 마거릿이 물었다."]], [["“She stole my cat.”", "“내 고양이를 훔쳐 갔거든요.”"]], [["“Did she know it was yours?”", "“그게 아주머니 것인 줄 알았대요?”"]], [["“I don’t know.", "“모르죠."], ["I reckon not.”", "몰랐지 싶어요.”"]], [["“Well! could not you get it back again when you told her it was yours?”", "“그럼 아주머니 것이라고 말하고 도로 찾아올 수는 없었어요?”"]], [["“No!", "“없죠!"], ["for she’d burnt it.”", "태워 버렸으니까요.”"]], [["“Burnt it!”", "“태워요!”"], ["exclaimed both Margaret and Mr. Bell.", "마거릿과 벨 씨가 동시에 소리쳤다."]], [["“Roasted it!”", "“구웠다고요!”"], ["explained the woman.", "여인이 설명했다."]], [["It was no explanation. By dint of questioning, Margaret extracted from her the horrible fact that Betty Barnes, having been induced by a gipsy fortune-teller to lend the latter her husband’s Sunday clothes, on promise of having them faithfully returned on the Saturday night before Goodman Barnes should have missed them, became alarmed by their non-appearance, and her consequent dread of her husband’s anger, and as, according to one of the savage country superstitions, the cries of a cat, in the agonies of being boiled or roasted alive; compelled (as it were) the powers of darkness to fulfil the wishes of the executioner, resort had been had to the charm. The poor woman evidently believed in its efficacy; her only feeling was indignation that her cat had been chosen out from all others for a sacrifice. Margaret listened in horror; and endeavoured in vain to enlighten the woman’s mind; but she was obliged to give it up in despair. Step by step she got the woman to admit certain facts, of which the logical connexion and sequence was perfectly clear to Margaret; but at the end, the bewildered woman simply repeated her first assertion, namely, that “it were very cruel for sure, and she should not like to do it; but that there was nothing like it for giving a person what they wished for; she had heard it all her life; but it were very cruel for all that.” Margaret gave it up in despair, and walked away sick at heart.", "그것은 설명이 되지 못했다. 마거릿은 거듭 캐물은 끝에 그 끔찍한 사실을 알아냈다. 베티 반스가 어느 집시 점쟁이의 꾐에 넘어가 남편의 나들이옷을 빌려준 일이 있었는데, 반스 영감이 옷이 없어진 것을 알아채기 전인 토요일 밤에 틀림없이 돌려주겠다는 약속이었다. 그런데 옷이 나타나지 않자 겁이 났고 남편의 노여움이 두려워진 나머지 그 주술에 기댔던 것이다. 시골의 모진 미신 가운데 하나에 따르면, 산 채로 삶기거나 구워지는 고통 속에서 지르는 고양이의 울음소리가 어둠의 힘으로 하여금 그 짓을 저지른 자의 소원을 들어주지 않을 수 없게 만든다는 것이었다. 그 가엾은 여인은 그 효험을 정말로 믿고 있는 것이 분명했다. 그녀의 유일한 감정은 하필 자기 고양이가 다른 모든 고양이를 제쳐 두고 제물로 골라졌다는 데 대한 분함뿐이었다. 마거릿은 소름이 끼친 채 듣고 있었고 그 여인의 생각을 깨우쳐 보려 애썼으나 헛일이었다. 결국 절망하여 단념할 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 한 걸음 한 걸음 여인에게서 몇 가지 사실을 인정받아 냈고, 그 논리적인 연결과 순서는 마거릿에게 더없이 분명했다. 그러나 끝에 가서 그 어리둥절해진 여인은 그저 처음에 한 말을 되풀이할 뿐이었다. 즉 “그야 참말 모진 짓이고 나 같으면 하고 싶지 않지만, 사람 소원 들어주는 데는 그만한 게 없다고, 평생을 두고 그리 들어 왔다고, 그래도 참 모진 짓이기는 하다고.” 마거릿은 절망 속에 단념하고 가슴이 아려 오는 채로 걸어 나왔다."]], [["“You are a good girl not to triumph over me,”", "“나를 두고 우쭐거리지 않다니 착한 아가씨로군.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“How?", "“네?"], ["What do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 말씀이세요?”"]], [["“I own I am wrong about schooling.", "“학교 교육에 대해 내가 틀렸다는 것을 인정하네."], ["Anything rather than have that child brought up in such practical paganism.”", "저런 실속 있는 이교 속에서 저 아이를 키우느니 무엇이든 낫지.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I remember.", "생각났어요."], ["Poor little Susan!", "가엾은 꼬마 수전!"], ["I must go and see her; would you mind calling at the school?”", "그 애를 보러 가야겠어요. 학교에 들르는 것 괜찮으시겠어요?”"]], [["“Not a bit.", "“조금도 싫지 않네."], ["I am curious to see something of the teaching she is to receive.”", "나도 그 아이가 받는다는 가르침이 어떤 것인지 좀 보고 싶으니.”"]], [["They did not speak much more, but thridded their way through many a bosky dell, whose soft green influence could not charm away the shock and the pain in Margaret’s heart, caused by the recital of such cruelty; a recital too, the manner of which betrayed such utter want of imagination, and therefore of any sympathy with the suffering animal.", "두 사람은 더 말을 나누지 않고 나무 우거진 골짜기를 여럿 헤치며 나아갔다. 그 부드러운 초록빛도 그런 잔혹한 이야기를 들은 마거릿의 가슴에 남은 충격과 아픔을 씻어 주지는 못했다. 게다가 그 이야기를 전하는 태도에는 상상력이라고는 도무지 없다는 것이, 따라서 고통받는 짐승에 대한 어떤 연민도 없다는 것이 그대로 드러나 있었다."]], [["The buzz of voices, like the murmur of a hive of busy human bees, made itself heard as soon as they emerged from the forest on the more open village-green on which the school was situated.", "바쁜 인간 벌들의 벌통이 웅웅거리는 듯한 말소리가, 숲을 벗어나 학교가 자리한 좀 더 트인 마을 공터로 나서자마자 들려왔다. 문은 활짝 열려 있었고 두 사람은 안으로 들어섰다."], ["The door was wide open, and they entered.", "여기저기 사방을 누비던 검은 옷차림의 활달한 부인이 두 사람을 알아보고 어딘가 안주인 같은 태도로 맞아들였다."], ["A brisk lady in black, here, there, and everywhere, perceived them, and bade them welcome with somewhat of the hostess-air which, Margaret remembered, her mother was wont to assume, only in a more soft and languid manner, when any rare visitors strayed in to inspect the school.", "마거릿은 어쩌다 학교를 둘러보러 오는 손님이 들르면 어머니도 그런 태도를 지었던 것을 기억했다. 다만 어머니 쪽이 훨씬 부드럽고 나른했을 뿐이었다."], ["She knew at once it was the present Vicar’s wife, her mother’s successor; and she would have drawn back from the interview had it been possible; but in an instant she had conquered this feeling, and modestly advanced, meeting many a bright glance of recognition, and hearing many a half-suppressed murmur of “It’s Miss Hale.”", "그녀는 그 부인이 지금의 목사 부인, 즉 어머니의 후임이라는 것을 대뜸 알아차렸다. 그리고 될 수만 있었다면 그 만남에서 물러섰을 것이다."], ["The Vicar’s lady heard the name, and her manner at once became more kindly.", "그러나 곧 그 마음을 이겨 내고 얌전히 앞으로 나아갔고, 알아보는 반가운 눈길을 여럿 마주쳤으며 반쯤 죽인 “헤일 아가씨야” 하는 수군거림도 여럿 들었다."], ["Margaret wished she could have helped feeling that it also became more patronizing.", "목사 부인은 그 이름을 듣고 대뜸 한결 다정한 태도가 되었다. 마거릿은 그 태도가 동시에 한결 생색내는 투가 되었다고 느끼지 않을 수 있었으면 싶었다."], ["The lady held out a hand to Mr. Bell, with,—", "부인은 벨 씨에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Your father, I presume, Miss Hale.", "“아버님이시군요, 헤일 양."], ["I see it by the likeness.", "닮은 데를 보니 알겠네요."], ["I am sure I am very glad to see you, sir, and so will the Vicar be.”", "뵙게 되어 정말 반갑습니다, 선생님. 목사님도 그러실 거예요.”"]], [["Margaret explained that it was not her father, and stammered out the fact of his death; wondering all the time how Mr.", "마거릿은 아버지가 아니라고 설명하고는 아버지가 세상을 떠났다는 사실을 더듬더듬 말했다."], ["Hale could have borne coming to revisit Helstone, if it had been as the Vicar’s lady supposed. She did not hear what Mrs.", "그러면서 내내, 만일 목사 부인이 짐작한 대로였다면 아버지가 헬스톤을 다시 찾는 일을 어떻게 견뎠을까 생각했다."], ["Hepworth was saying, and left it to Mr. Bell to reply, looking round, meanwhile, for her old acquaintances.", "그녀는 헵워스 부인이 하는 말을 듣지 못했고, 대꾸는 벨 씨에게 맡긴 채 옛 낯익은 얼굴들을 찾아 둘러보았다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I see you would like to take a class, Miss Hale.", "수업을 하나 맡고 싶으신 게로군요, 헤일 양."], ["I know it by myself.", "제가 그 마음을 잘 알지요."], ["First class stand up for a parsing lesson with Miss Hale.”", "1반, 일어서서 헤일 양과 문장 분석 수업을 하세요.”"]], [["Poor Margaret, whose visit was sentimental, not in any degree inspective, felt herself taken in; but as in some way bringing her in contact with little eager faces, once well-known, and who had received the solemn rite of baptism from her father, she sate down, half losing herself in tracing out the changing features of the girls, and holding Susan’s hand for a minute or two, unobserved by all, while the first class sought for their books, and the Vicar’s lady went as near as a lady could towards holding Mr.", "가엾은 마거릿은 이번 방문이 감상에서 온 것이지 조금도 시찰하러 온 것이 아니었으므로 꼼짝없이 걸려들었다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그것이 어떤 식으로든 그녀를 한때 잘 알던, 아버지에게서 엄숙한 세례를 받았던 그 조그맣고 초롱초롱한 얼굴들과 맞닿게 해 주었으므로 그녀는 자리에 앉았고, 아이들의 달라진 이목구비를 더듬어 보느라 반쯤 넋을 잃은 채 아무도 보지 못하게 수전의 손을 잠깐 잡고 있었다."], ["Bell by the button, while she explained the Phonetic system to him, and gave him a conversation she had had with the Inspector about it.", "그동안 1반 아이들은 책을 찾았고, 목사 부인은 숙녀로서 할 수 있는 한 벨 씨의 단추라도 붙들 듯이 바싹 다가서서 그에게 발음 교수법을 설명하고 그 문제로 장학관과 나눈 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["Margaret bent over her book, and seeing nothing but that—hearing the buzz of children’s voices, old times rose up, and she thought of them, and her eyes filled with tears, till all at once there was a pause—one of the girls was stumbling over the apparently simple word “a,”", "마거릿은 책 위로 몸을 숙인 채 그것 말고는 아무것도 보지 않았다. 그리고 아이들 목소리의 웅성거림을 들으니 옛 시절이 떠올라 그것을 생각하는 사이 눈에 눈물이 고였다."], ["uncertain what to call it.", "그러다 별안간 수업이 멈추었다. 여자아이 하나가 보기에 단순하기 짝이 없는 “a”라는 낱말에서 걸려, 그것을 무어라 불러야 할지 몰라 하고 있었다."]], [["“A, an indefinite article,”", "“a는 부정관사예요.”"], ["said Margaret, mildly.", "마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "“죄송하지만요.”"], ["said the Vicar’s wife, all eyes and ears; “but we are taught by Mr.", "눈과 귀를 온통 곤두세우고 있던 목사 부인이 말했다."], ["Milsome to call ‘a’ an—who can remember?”", "“저희는 밀섬 선생님께 ‘a’를 무엇이라고 부르라고 배웠지요, 누가 기억하고 있나요?”"]], [["“An adjective absolute,”", "“절대 형용사요.”"], ["said half-a-dozen voices at once.", "대여섯 목소리가 한꺼번에 말했다."], ["And Margaret sate abashed.", "그러자 마거릿은 무안해져서 앉아 있었다."], ["The children knew more than she did. Mr.", "아이들이 그녀보다 더 많이 알고 있었다."], ["Bell turned away, and smiled.", "벨 씨는 고개를 돌리고 웃었다."]], [["Margaret spoke no more during the lesson.", "마거릿은 수업 내내 더는 말하지 않았다."], ["But after it was over, she went quietly round to one or two old favourites, and talked to them a little.", "그러나 수업이 끝난 뒤 조용히 옛날에 유난히 예뻐하던 아이 한둘에게 돌아가 잠시 이야기를 나누었다."], ["They were growing out of children into great girls; passing out of their recollection in their rapid development, as she, by her three year’s absence, was vanishing from theirs.", "아이들은 어린것에서 어엿한 처녀로 자라 가고 있었다. 그 빠른 성장 속에 아이들이 그녀의 기억에서 지워져 가듯, 그녀 또한 3년의 부재로 아이들의 기억에서 사라져 가고 있었다."], ["Still she was glad to have seen them all again, though a tinge of sadness mixed itself with her pleasure.", "그래도 그녀는 아이들을 모두 다시 본 것이 기뻤다. 그 기쁨에 슬픔의 빛이 섞이기는 했지만."], ["When school was over for the day, it was yet early in the summer afternoon; and Mrs.", "그날 수업이 끝났을 때는 아직 여름 오후의 이른 시간이었다."], ["Hepworth proposed to Margaret that she and Mr. Bell should accompany her to the parsonage, and see—the word “improvements”", "헵워스 부인은 마거릿에게 벨 씨와 함께 목사관으로 가서 지금 목사가 하고 있는 “개량”을 보고 가라고 청했다."], ["had half slipped out of her mouth, but she substituted the more cautious term “alterations”", "그 말이 입에서 반쯤 흘러나오다가 그녀는 좀 더 조심스러운 “변경”이라는 말로 바꾸었다. 마거릿은 그 변경 따위를 보는 데는 털끝만큼도 관심이 없었다."], ["which the present Vicar was making.", "그것은 제 집이 어떠했는가에 대한 애틋한 기억에 거슬리기만 했다."], ["Margaret did not care a straw about seeing the alterations, which jarred upon her fond recollection of what her home had been; but she longed to see the old place once more, even though she shivered away from the pain which she knew she should feel.", "그러나 옛 그 자리를 한 번 더 보고 싶었다. 느끼게 되리라는 것을 알고 있는 그 아픔에 몸이 움츠러들면서도."]], [["The parsonage was so altered, both inside and out, that the real pain was less than she anticipated.", "목사관은 안팎으로 어찌나 달라졌던지 실제로 느낀 아픔은 예상보다 덜했다. 같은 곳 같지가 않았다."], ["It was not like the same place.", "예전에는 장미 잎사귀 하나만 떨어져 있어도 그 흠잡을 데 없는 정돈과 단정함에 얼룩처럼 보일 만큼 곱게 손질되어 있던 뜰과 잔디밭은 아이들의 물건으로 어질러져 있었다."], ["The garden, the grass-plat, formerly so daintily trim that even a stray rose-leaf seemed like a fleck on the exquisite arrangement and propriety, was strewed with children’s things; a bag of marbles here, a hoop there; a straw-hat forced down upon a rose-tree as on a peg, to the destruction of a long beautiful tender branch laden with flowers, which in former days would have been trained up tenderly, as if beloved.", "여기엔 구슬 주머니, 저기엔 굴렁쇠. 밀짚모자 하나가 장미나무에 걸이라도 되는 양 눌러 씌워져 있어, 꽃이 잔뜩 달린 길고 아름다운 여린 가지 하나가 상해 있었다."], ["The little square matted hall was equally filled with signs of merry healthy rough childhood.", "지난날 같았으면 사랑스러운 것인 양 조심스레 매어 길렀을 가지였다. 돗자리 깔린 자그마한 네모난 현관도 명랑하고 튼튼하고 거친 아이들의 흔적으로 가득했다."]], [["“Ah!” said Mrs.", "“아유!”"], ["Hepworth, “you must excuse this untidiness, Miss Hale.", "헵워스 부인이 말했다. “이 어수선한 꼴을 용서하세요, 헤일 양."], ["When the nursery is finished, I shall insist upon a little order.", "아이방이 다 되면 좀 정돈을 하자고 우길 참이에요."], ["We are building a nursery out of your room, I believe.", "아이방을 아가씨가 쓰시던 방을 헐어 짓고 있는 것 같은데요."], ["How did you manage, Miss Hale, without a nursery?”", "헤일 양, 아이방도 없이 어떻게 지내셨어요?”"]], [["“We were but two,”", "“저희는 둘뿐이었으니까요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["“You have many children, I presume?”", "“아이들이 많으신 모양이지요?”"]], [["“Seven.", "“일곱이에요."], ["Look here!", "여기 좀 보세요!"], ["we are throwing out a window to the road on this side. Mr.", "이쪽으로 길 쪽에 창을 내고 있답니다."], ["Hepworth is spending an immense deal of money on this house; but really it was scarcely habitable when we came—for so large a family as ours I mean, of course.”", "헵워스 씨가 이 집에 어마어마한 돈을 쓰고 있어요. 그렇지만 정말이지 저희가 왔을 때는 사람이 살 만한 집이 못 되었거든요."], ["Every room in the house was changed, besides the one of which Mrs.", "저희처럼 식구가 많은 집에는요, 물론 그런 뜻이지요.” 헵워스 부인이 말한 그 방 말고도 집 안의 방이란 방은 모두 달라져 있었다."], ["Hepworth spoke, which had been Mr.", "그 방은 예전에 헤일 씨의 서재였다."], ["Hale’s study formerly; and where the green gloom and delicious quiet of the place had conduced, as he had said, to a habit of meditation, but, perhaps, in some degree to the formation of a character more fitted for thought than action.", "그곳의 초록빛 어스름과 감미로운 고요는 그 자신이 말한 대로 사색하는 버릇을 길러 주었으나, 어쩌면 어느 정도는 행동보다 생각에 더 알맞은 성품을 빚어 놓기도 했을 것이다. 새로 낸 창은 길이 내다보였고, 헵워스 부인이 짚어 준 대로 이로운 점이 많았다."], ["The new window gave a view of the road, and had many advantages, as Mrs. Hepworth pointed out.", "그 창으로는 남편의 양 떼 가운데 길 잃은 것들이 저희 딴에는 남의 눈에 안 띄기를 바라며 유혹적인 술집으로 슬금슬금 새어 나가는 모습이 보였다."], ["From it the wandering sheep of her husband’s flock might be seen, who straggled to the tempting beer-house, unobserved as they might hope, but not unobserved in reality; for the active Vicar kept his eye on the road, even during the composition of his most orthodox sermons, and had a hat and stick hanging ready at hand to seize, before sallying out after his parishioners, who had need of quick legs if they could take refuge in the “Jolly Forester”", "실은 남의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 부지런한 목사는 가장 정통적인 설교문을 지으면서도 길에서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 모자와 지팡이를 손 닿는 곳에 걸어 두었다가 낚아채고는 교구민들을 뒤쫓아 나섰기 때문이다."], ["before the teetotal Vicar had arrested them. The whole family were quick, brisk, loud-talking, kind-hearted, and not troubled with much delicacy of perception.", "그러니 금주가 목사에게 붙들리기 전에 “유쾌한 삼림지기”로 피신하려면 다리가 재빨라야 했다."], ["Margaret feared that Mrs. Hepworth would find out that Mr.", "온 식구가 재바르고 활달하고 목소리가 크고 마음씨가 고왔으되, 섬세한 눈치 따위로 괴로워하는 법은 없었다."], ["Bell was playing upon her, in the admiration he thought fit to express for everything that especially grated on his taste. But no!", "마거릿은 벨 씨가 자기 취향에 특히 거슬리는 것마다 짐짓 감탄해 보이는 것으로 헵워스 부인을 놀리고 있다는 것을 부인이 알아채지나 않을까 걱정했다."], ["she took it all literally, and with such good faith, that Margaret could not help remonstrating with him as they walked slowly away from the parsonage back to their inn.", "그러나 아니었다! 부인은 그것을 죄다 곧이곧대로, 그토록 순진하게 받아들였고, 그래서 목사관에서 여관 쪽으로 천천히 걸어 나오면서 마거릿은 그에게 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Don’t scold, Margaret.", "“꾸짖지 말게, 마거릿."], ["It was all because of you.", "다 자네 때문이었네."], ["If she had not shown you every change with such evident exultation in their superior sense, in perceiving what an improvement this and that would be, I could have behaved well.", "그 부인이 이러이러하게 고치면 얼마나 나아지는지 안다는 우월한 안목에 그토록 대놓고 우쭐거리며 자네에게 변화란 변화를 죄다 보여 주지만 않았어도 나는 얌전히 굴 수 있었을 걸세."], ["But if you must go on preaching, keep it till after dinner, when it will send me to sleep, and help my digestion.”", "그래도 굳이 설교를 늘어놓아야겠거든 저녁 먹은 뒤까지 아껴 두게. 그때라면 나를 잠들게 해 주고 소화도 도와줄 테니.”"]], [["They were both of them tired, and Margaret herself so much so, that she was unwilling to go out as she had proposed to do, and have another ramble among the woods and fields so close to the home of her childhood.", "두 사람 다 지쳐 있었고 마거릿은 특히 그러해서, 어릴 적 집에서 그토록 가까운 숲과 들판을 한 번 더 거닐어 보겠다던 애초의 생각을 접고 나가고 싶어 하지 않았다. 그리고 어쩐지 이번 헬스톤 방문은 전부가, 그녀가 기대했던 것과 꼭 그대로는 아니었다."], ["And, somehow, this visit to Helstone had not been all—had not been exactly what she had expected.", "어디에나 변화가 있었다. 사소하되 모든 것에 스며 있는 변화였다."], ["There was change everywhere; slight, yet pervading all.", "집집마다 누가 떠나거나 죽거나 혼인하거나, 아니면 나날과 달과 해가 가져오는 자연스러운 변천으로 달라져 있었다."], ["Households were changed by absence, or death, or marriage, or the natural mutations brought by days and months and years, which carry us on imperceptibly from childhood to youth, and thence through manhood to age, whence we drop like fruit, fully ripe, into the quiet mother earth.", "그 변천은 우리를 어린 시절에서 젊음으로, 다시 장년을 거쳐 노년으로 알아채지 못하게 실어 가고, 마침내 우리는 무르익은 열매처럼 고요한 어머니 대지로 떨어진다. 장소도 달라져 있었다."], ["Places were changed—a tree gone here, a bough there, bringing in a long ray of light where no light was before—a road was trimmed and narrowed, and the green straggling pathway by its side enclosed and cultivated.", "여기서 나무 한 그루가 없어지고 저기서 가지 하나가 사라져 전에는 빛이 들지 않던 곳에 긴 빛줄기가 들어왔다. 길은 반듯이 다듬어져 좁아졌고, 그 곁에 제멋대로 뻗어 있던 푸른 오솔길은 울타리가 쳐지고 밭이 되어 있었다."], ["A great improvement it was called; but Margaret sighed over the old picturesqueness, the old gloom, and the grassy way-side of former days.", "사람들은 그것을 큰 개량이라 불렀다. 그러나 마거릿은 옛날의 그 그림 같은 정취를, 옛날의 그 어스름을, 지난날 길가의 풀밭을 두고 한숨지었다."], ["She sate by the window on the little settle, sadly gazing out upon the gathering shades of night, which harmonized well with her pensive thought.", "그녀는 창가의 작은 걸상에 앉아 몰려드는 밤의 그림자를 서글피 내다보았다. 그 그림자는 그녀의 시름겨운 생각과 잘 어울렸다."], ["Mr. Bell slept soundly, after his unusual exercise through the day.", "벨 씨는 그날 하루 평소 없던 운동을 한 탓에 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["At last he was roused by the entrance of the tea-tray, brought in by a flushed-looking country-girl, who had evidently been finding some variety from her usual occupation of waiter, in assisting this day in the hay-field.", "마침내 그는 차 쟁반이 들어오는 바람에 깨어났다. 상기된 얼굴의 시골 처녀가 그것을 들고 왔는데, 이날 건초밭 일을 거들면서 여느 때의 시중드는 일에서 벗어나 색다른 재미를 맛본 것이 분명했다."]], [["“Hallo!", "“어!"], ["Who’s there!", "거기 누구요!"], ["Where are we?", "여기가 어디지?"], ["Who’s that,—Margaret?", "저건 누구야, 마거릿인가?"], ["Oh, now I remember all.", "아, 이제 다 생각났네."], ["I could not imagine what woman was sitting there in such a doleful attitude, with her hands clasped straight out upon her knees, and her face looking so steadfastly before her.", "대체 어떤 여자가 저렇게 처량한 자세로, 두 손을 무릎 위에 곧게 맞잡고 얼굴을 저리 꼼짝 않고 앞으로 향한 채 앉아 있나 했지."], ["What are you looking at?” asked Mr.", "무얼 보고 있는 겐가?”"], ["Bell, coming to the window, and standing behind Margaret.", "벨 씨가 창가로 다가와 마거릿 뒤에 서며 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "“아무것도 아니에요.”"], ["said she, rising up quickly, and speaking as cheerfully as she could at a moment’s notice.", "그녀가 얼른 일어서며, 갑작스러운 순간에 낼 수 있는 한 가장 밝은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Nothing indeed!", "“아무것도 아니라니!"], ["A bleak back-ground of trees, some white linen hung out on the sweet-briar hedge, and a great waft of damp air.", "을씨년스러운 나무들이 배경이고, 들장미 울타리에 흰 빨래가 널려 있고, 눅눅한 바람이 한 자락 불어오는군."], ["Shut the window, and come in and make tea.”", "창을 닫고 이리 와서 차를 따르게.”"]], [["Margaret was silent for some time.", "마거릿은 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["She played with her teaspoon, and did not attend particularly to what Mr. Bell said.", "찻숟가락을 만지작거리며 벨 씨가 하는 말에 별로 귀 기울이지 않았다."], ["He contradicted her, and she took the same sort of smiling notice of his opinion as if he had agreed with her.", "그가 그녀의 말을 반박해도 그녀는 그가 동의하기라도 한 양 웃음 띤 얼굴로 그의 의견을 받아 넘겼다."], ["Then she sighed, and putting down her spoon, she began, apropos of nothing at all, and in the high-pitched voice which usually shows that the speaker has been thinking for some time on the subject that they wish to introduce—“Mr.", "이윽고 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 숟가락을 내려놓더니, 아무 맥락도 없이, 그리고 말하는 이가 꺼내고 싶은 화제를 얼마 동안 마음에 두고 있었음을 흔히 드러내는 그 높다란 목소리로 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Bell, you remember what we were saying about Frederick last night, don’t you?”", "“벨 씨, 어젯밤에 우리가 프레더릭 오빠 이야기를 한 것 기억하시죠?”"]], [["“Last night.", "“어젯밤이라."], ["Where was I?", "내가 어디 있었더라?"], ["Oh, I remember!", "아, 생각났네!"], ["Why, it seems a week ago.", "아니, 일주일은 지난 것 같군."], ["Yes, to be sure, I recollect we talked about him, poor fellow.”", "그래, 그럼, 그 딱한 사람 이야기를 한 것이 기억나네.”"]], [["“Yes—and do you remember that Mr.", "“네."], ["Lennox spoke about his having been in England about the time of dear mamma’s death?”", "그리고 레녹스 씨가 어머니께서 돌아가실 무렵에 오빠가 영국에 와 있었다고 말한 것도 기억하세요?”"], ["asked Margaret, her voice now lower than usual.", "마거릿의 목소리가 이제 여느 때보다 낮았다."]], [["“I recollect.", "“기억하네."], ["I hadn’t heard of it before.”", "그전에는 들은 적이 없었지.”"]], [["“And I thought—I always thought that papa had told you about it.”", "“저는 아버지께서 그 이야기를 하신 줄로만, 늘 그런 줄로만 알았어요.”"]], [["“No!", "“아니야!"], ["he never did.", "한 번도 안 하셨네."], ["But what about it, Margaret?”", "그런데 그게 어쨌다는 건가, 마거릿?”"]], [["“I want to tell you of something I did that was very wrong, about that time,”", "“그 무렵에 제가 아주 잘못한 일이 있는데, 그 이야기를 하고 싶어요.”"], ["said Margaret, suddenly looking up at him with her clear honest eyes.", "마거릿이 맑고 정직한 눈으로 갑자기 그를 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“I told a lie!”", "“저는 거짓말을 했어요!”"], ["and her face became scarlet.", "그러고는 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."]], [["“True, that was bad I own; not but what I have told a pretty round number in my life, not all in downright words, as I suppose you did, but in actions, or in some shabby circumlocutory way, leading people either to disbelieve the truth, or believe a falsehood.", "“그렇군, 그건 나쁜 일이었지, 나도 인정하네. 하기야 나도 평생 꽤 많은 거짓말을 했지만 말이야."], ["You know who is the father of lies, Margaret?", "자네처럼 대놓고 말로 한 것은 아니어도 행동으로, 또는 어딘가 옹색하게 에두르는 방식으로 남들이 참을 믿지 않게 하거나 거짓을 믿게 만들었지. 거짓의 아비가 누구인지 알지, 마거릿?"], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["a great number of folk, thinking themselves very good, have odd sorts of connexion with lies, left-hand marriages, and second-cousins-once-removed.", "스스로 무척 착하다고 여기는 사람들이 참 많은데, 그들도 거짓과 묘한 인연을 맺고 있다네. 떳떳지 못한 혼인이며 육촌쯤 되는 인연 말이야."], ["The tainting blood of falsehood runs through us all.", "거짓이라는 더러운 피는 우리 모두에게 흐르고 있어."], ["I should have guessed you as far from it as most people.", "자네만은 여느 사람보다 그것에서 멀리 있으리라 여겼네만."], ["What! crying, child?", "아니, 우는 겐가, 얘야?"], ["Nay, now we’ll not talk of it, if it ends in this way.", "이런 식으로 끝날 이야기라면 그만두세. 자네가 그 일을 뉘우쳤고 다시는 그러지 않으리라는 것은 짐작이 가네."], ["I dare say you have been sorry for it, and that you won’t do it again, and it’s long ago now, and in short I want you to be very cheerful, and not very sad, this evening.”", "게다가 이젠 오래전 일이고. 요컨대 나는 오늘 저녁 자네가 아주 유쾌하고 조금도 슬프지 않기를 바라네.”"]], [["Margaret wiped her eyes, and tried to talk about something else, but suddenly she burst out afresh.", "마거릿은 눈을 닦고 다른 이야기를 해 보려 애썼으나 갑자기 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Please, Mr.", "“제발, 벨 씨, 그 이야기를 하게 해 주세요."], ["Bell, let me tell you about it—you could perhaps help me a little; no, not help me, but if you knew the truth, perhaps you could put me to rights—that is not it, after all,”", "어쩌면 저를 조금 도와주실 수 있을지도 몰라요. 아니, 도와주시는 게 아니라, 진실을 아시면 어쩌면 저를 바로잡아 주실 수도, 결국 그것도 아니네요.”"], ["said she, in despair at not being able to express herself more exactly as she wished.", "그녀는 뜻하는 대로 좀 더 정확하게 표현하지 못하는 것이 안타까워 이렇게 말했다."]], [["Mr. Bell’s whole manner changed.", "벨 씨의 태도가 온통 달라졌다."], ["“Tell me all about it, child,”", "“다 이야기해 보게, 얘야.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It’s a long story; but when Fred came, mamma was very ill, and I was undone with anxiety, and afraid, too, that I might have drawn him into danger; and we had an alarm just after her death, for Dixon met some one in Milton—a man called Leonards—who had known Fred, and who seemed to owe him a grudge, or at any rate to be tempted by the recollection of the reward offered for his apprehension; and with this new fright, I thought I had better hurry off Fred to London, where, as you would understand from what we said the other night, he was to go to consult Mr. Lennox as to his chances if he stood trial. So we—that is, he and I,—went to the railway station; it was one evening, and it was just getting rather dusk, but still light enough to recognize and be recognized, and we were too early, and went out to walk in a field just close by; I was always in a panic about this Leonards, who was, I knew, somewhere in the neighbourhood; and then, when we were in the field, the low red sunlight just in my face, some one came by on horseback in the road just below the field-style on which we stood.—I saw him look at me, but I did not know who it was at first, the sun was so in my eyes, but in an instant the dazzle went off, and I saw it was Mr. Thornton, and we bowed”——", "“긴 이야기예요. 하지만 프레드 오빠가 왔을 때 어머니는 몹시 편찮으셨고 저는 걱정으로 넋이 나가 있었어요. 게다가 제가 오빠를 위험으로 끌어들인 게 아닐까 두렵기도 했고요. 그리고 어머니께서 돌아가신 직후에 놀랄 일이 있었어요. 딕슨이 밀턴에서 어떤 사람을 만났거든요. 레너즈라는 남자였는데, 오빠를 알던 사람이었고 오빠에게 무슨 원한이 있는 듯했어요. 아니면 적어도 오빠를 잡으면 준다는 현상금 생각에 마음이 동한 듯했고요. 이 새로운 겁 때문에 저는 오빠를 서둘러 런던으로 보내는 게 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 며칠 전 밤에 우리가 한 이야기로 아시겠지만, 오빠는 거기서 재판을 받을 경우 가망이 어떻겠는지 레녹스 씨와 상의하기로 되어 있었으니까요. 그래서 우리는, 그러니까 오빠와 저는 기차역으로 갔어요. 어느 날 저녁이었고 막 어스름이 내리려던 참이었지만, 그래도 서로 알아보고 알아보일 만큼은 밝았어요. 그리고 우리는 너무 일찍 갔던 터라 바로 옆에 있는 들판으로 걸어 나갔어요. 저는 그 레너즈라는 사람 때문에 늘 조마조마했어요. 그가 이 근처 어딘가에 있다는 걸 알고 있었으니까요. 그런데 우리가 들판에 있을 때, 나직한 붉은 햇빛이 바로 제 얼굴에 비치고 있는데, 우리가 서 있던 들판 층계 바로 아래 길로 누군가 말을 타고 지나갔어요. 그가 저를 보는 것이 보였지만 저는 처음엔 그가 누구인지 몰랐어요. 해가 눈에 너무 비쳐서요. 그런데 순간 눈부심이 걷히고 저는 그가 손턴 씨라는 걸 알았어요. 그리고 우리는 인사를 나누었죠——”"]], [["“And he saw Frederick of course,” said Mr.", "“그리고 그가 물론 프레더릭을 보았겠지.”"], ["Bell, helping her on with her story, as he thought.", "벨 씨가 이야기를 거들어 준다고 생각하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; and then at the station a man came up—tipsy and reeling—and he tried to collar Fred, and over-balanced himself as Fred wrenched himself away, and fell over the hedge of the platform; not far, not deep; not above three feet; but oh! Mr. Bell, somehow that fall killed him!”", "“네. 그리고 역에서 한 남자가 다가왔어요. 술에 취해 비틀거리면서요. 그가 프레드 오빠의 멱살을 잡으려 했는데, 오빠가 몸을 홱 뿌리치는 바람에 중심을 잃고 승강장 울타리 너머로 떨어졌어요. 멀지도 깊지도 않았어요. 3피트가 채 안 됐고요. 그런데, 아, 벨 씨! 어쩐 일인지 그 추락이 그를 죽게 했어요!”"]], [["“How awkward.", "“저런 딱한 일이."], ["It was this Leonards, I suppose.", "그게 그 레너즈였겠군."], ["And how did Fred get off?”", "그런데 프레드는 어떻게 빠져나갔나?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아! 오빠는 그 사람이 떨어진 직후에 바로 떠났어요."], ["he went off immediately after the fall, which we never thought could have done the poor fellow any harm, it seemed so slight an injury.”", "우리는 그 일로 그 딱한 사람이 다치리라고는 꿈에도 생각하지 못했고요. 아주 가벼운 상처로 보였거든요.”"]], [["“Then he did not die directly?”", "“그럼 그가 그 자리에서 죽은 것은 아니고?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니에요!"], ["not for two or three days.", "이삼일 뒤였어요. 그리고 그다음이, 아, 벨 씨!"], ["And then—oh, Mr. Bell!", "이제 나쁜 대목이 나와요.”"], ["now comes the bad part,”", "그녀는 손가락을 초조하게 맞비틀며 말했다."], ["said she, nervously twining her fingers together.", "“경찰 경위가 와서, 레너즈를 죽음에 이르게 한 그 젊은 남자와 제가 동행이었다고 저를 몰아세웠어요."], ["“A police inspector came and taxed me with having been a companion of the young man, whose push or blow had occasioned Leonards’ death; that was a false accusation, you know, but we had not heard that Fred had sailed, he might still be in London and liable to be arrested on this false charge, and his identity with the Lieutenant Hale, accused of causing the mutiny, discovered, he might be shot; all this flashed through my mind, and I said it was not me.", "그건 거짓 혐의였지만, 아시다시피 우리는 프레드 오빠가 배를 탔다는 소식을 듣지 못한 상태였어요. 오빠가 아직 런던에 있을 수도 있고 이 거짓 혐의로 붙잡힐 수도 있고, 그러다 반란을 일으켰다고 고발당한 헤일 중위와 같은 사람이라는 게 드러나면 총살당할 수도 있었어요."], ["I was not at the railway station that night.", "이 모든 것이 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔고, 저는 제가 아니라고 말했어요."], ["I knew nothing about it.", "그날 밤 저는 기차역에 있지 않았다고요."], ["I had no conscience or thought but to save Frederick.”", "그 일에 대해 아무것도 모른다고요. 저에게는 프레더릭 오빠를 구하는 것 말고는 아무 양심도 생각도 없었어요.”"]], [["“I say it was right.", "“나는 그게 옳았다고 말하겠네."], ["I should have done the same.", "나라도 똑같이 했을 걸세."], ["You forgot yourself in thought for another.", "자네는 남을 생각하느라 자기를 잊은 것이야."], ["I hope I should have done the same.”", "나도 그렇게 했기를 바라네.”"]], [["“No, you would not.", "“아니에요, 그러지 않으셨을 거예요."], ["It was wrong, disobedient, faithless.", "그건 그릇된 일이었고 불순종이었고 믿음 없는 짓이었어요."], ["At that very time Fred was safely out of England, and in my blindness I forgot that there was another witness who could testify to my being there.”", "바로 그때 프레드 오빠는 무사히 영국을 떠나 있었고, 저는 눈이 멀어서 제가 그곳에 있었다는 것을 증언할 수 있는 또 다른 증인이 있다는 걸 잊고 있었어요.”"]], [["“Who?”", "“누구?”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton.", "“손턴 씨요."], ["You know he had seen me close to the station; we had bowed to each other.”", "아시다시피 그분은 역 가까이에서 저를 보았어요. 우리는 서로 인사를 나누었고요.”"]], [["“Well!", "“허!"], ["he would know nothing of this riot, about the drunken fellow’s death.", "그 사람이 이 소동에 대해서, 그 취한 자의 죽음에 대해서 알 리가 없지 않은가."], ["I suppose the injury never came to anything.”", "그 상처가 아무 일도 아니었던 모양이지.”"]], [["“No!", "“아니에요!"], ["the proceedings they had begun to talk about on the inquest were stopped.", "검시에서 이야기가 나오기 시작하던 절차는 중단되었어요."], ["Mr. Thornton did know all about it.", "손턴 씨는 그 일을 다 알고 계셨어요."], ["He was a magistrate, and he found out that it was not the fall that had caused the death.", "그분은 치안판사였고, 죽음의 원인이 그 추락이 아니라는 것을 밝혀내셨어요."], ["But not before he knew what I had said.", "하지만 그건 제가 한 말을 알고 난 뒤였어요."], ["Oh, Mr. Bell!”", "아, 벨 씨!”"], ["She suddenly covered her face with her hands, as if wishing to hide herself from the presence of the recollection.", "그녀는 그 기억이 있는 자리에서 자신을 숨기고 싶기라도 한 듯 갑자기 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“Did you have any explanation with him?", "“그와 무슨 해명이라도 했나?"], ["Did you ever tell him the strong, instinctive motive?”", "그 강한 본능적인 동기를 그에게 이야기한 적이 있나?”"]], [["“The instinctive want of faith, and clutching at a sin to keep myself from sinking,”", "“본능적으로 믿음이 없어서, 가라앉지 않으려고 죄를 붙잡은 거죠.”"], ["said she bitterly. “No!", "그녀가 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["How could I?", "“아니요!"], ["He knew nothing of Frederick.", "어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠어요?"], ["To put myself to rights in his good opinion, was I to tell him the secrets of our family, involving, as they seemed to do, the chances of poor Frederick’s entire exculpation?", "그분은 프레더릭 오빠에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어요. 그분이 저를 좋게 보도록 제 자리를 바로잡자고, 가엾은 프레더릭 오빠가 완전히 혐의를 벗을 가망까지 걸려 있는 듯한 우리 집안의 비밀을 그분에게 이야기해야 했겠어요?"], ["Fred’s last words had been to enjoin me to keep his visit a secret from all.", "프레드 오빠의 마지막 말은 자기가 왔다 간 것을 누구에게도 비밀로 하라는 당부였어요. 보세요, 아버지도 벨 씨에게조차 말씀하지 않으셨잖아요."], ["You see, papa never told, even you.", "아니에요! 저는 그 부끄러움을 견딜 수 있었어요."], ["No! I could bear the shame—I thought I could at least.", "적어도 견딜 수 있으리라 생각했어요."], ["I did bear it.", "그리고 견뎠고요."], ["Mr. Thornton has never respected me since.”", "손턴 씨는 그 뒤로 저를 존중한 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“He respects you, I am sure,”", "“그는 자네를 존중하네, 틀림없이.”"], ["said Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 말했다."], ["“To be sure it accounts a little for——.", "“하기야 그것으로 얼마간 설명이 되는군——."], ["But he always speaks of you with regard and esteem, though now I understand certain reservations in his manner.”", "그래도 그는 늘 자네를 존경과 흠모로 이야기한다네. 이제야 그의 태도에 어딘가 유보하는 데가 있었던 까닭을 알겠네만.”"]], [["Margaret did not speak; did not attend to what Mr.", "마거릿은 말이 없었다."], ["Bell had to say; lost all sense of it.", "벨 씨가 하는 말에 귀 기울이지도, 그것을 알아듣지도 못했다."], ["By-and-by she said:", "이윽고 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Will you tell me what you refer to about ‘reservations’ in his manner of speaking to me?”", "“그분이 저에 대해 말할 때 태도에 ‘유보하는 데’가 있었다는 게 무슨 말씀이신지 말씀해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["simply he has annoyed me by not joining in my praises of you.", "그저 내가 자네를 칭찬하는데 그가 맞장구를 치지 않아서 언짢았다는 게지."], ["Like an old fool, I thought that every one would have the same opinion as I had; and he evidently could not agree with me.", "늙은 바보처럼 나는 누구나 나와 같은 생각일 줄 알았는데, 그는 분명히 내 말에 동의할 수 없었던 게야."], ["I was puzzled at the time.", "그때는 어리둥절했지."], ["But he must be perplexed, if the affair has never been in the least explained.", "그렇지만 그 일이 조금도 해명되지 않았다면 그도 어리둥절할밖에."], ["There was first your walking out with a young man in the dark——”", "우선 자네가 어둠 속에서 젊은 남자와 함께 걸어 나갔다는 것부터가——”"]], [["“But it was my brother!”", "“하지만 그건 제 오빠였어요!”"], ["said Margaret, surprised.", "마거릿이 놀라서 말했다."]], [["“True.", "“그렇지."], ["But how was he to know that?”", "그렇지만 그가 그것을 어떻게 알겠나?”"]], [["“I don’t know.", "“모르겠어요."], ["I never thought anything of that kind,”", "저는 그런 것은 조금도 생각해 보지 않았어요.”"], ["said Margaret, reddening, and looking hurt and offended.", "마거릿이 얼굴을 붉히며 상하고 언짢은 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“And perhaps he never would, but for the lie,—which, under the circumstances, I maintain, was necessary.”", "“아마 그도 끝내 그런 생각은 하지 않았을 게야. 그 거짓말만 아니었더라면 말이지. 그 거짓말은 그 처지에서는 어쩔 수 없었다고 나는 여전히 주장하네만.”"]], [["“It was not.", "“어쩔 수 없지 않았어요."], ["I know it now.", "이제는 알아요."], ["I bitterly repent it.”", "저는 그것을 뼈저리게 뉘우쳐요.”"]], [["There was a long pause of silence.", "오랫동안 말이 없었다."], ["Margaret was the first to speak.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 마거릿이었다."]], [["“I am not likely ever to see Mr.", "“저는 아마 손턴 씨를 다시 만날 일이 없을 거예요.”"], ["Thornton again,”—and there she stopped.", "그러고는 거기서 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“There are many things more unlikely, I should say,”", "“그보다 더 있을 법하지 않은 일도 많다고 나는 말하겠네.”"], ["replied Mr. Bell.", "벨 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“But I believe I never shall.", "“그래도 저는 다시는 뵐 일이 없으리라 믿어요."], ["Still, somehow one does not like to have sunk so low in—in a friend’s opinion as I have done in his.”", "그래도 어쩐지 사람이란, 어떤 벗의 눈에 제가 그분의 눈에 그리된 것처럼 그토록 낮게 떨어지는 것은 좋아하지 않잖아요.”"], ["Her eyes were full of tears, but her voice was steady, and Mr. Bell was not looking at her.", "그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했으나 목소리는 흔들리지 않았고, 벨 씨는 그녀를 보고 있지 않았다."], ["“And now that Frederick has given up all hope, and almost all wish of ever clearing himself, and returning to England, it would be only doing myself justice to have all this explained.", "“그리고 이제 프레더릭 오빠가 자기 혐의를 벗고 영국으로 돌아올 모든 희망을, 거의 모든 바람마저 버린 마당이니, 이 모든 것을 해명하는 것이 저 자신에게 공정한 일일 뿐이겠지요. 괜찮으시다면, 그리고 하실 수 있다면, 좋은 기회가 생기거든, 억지로 해명을 들이대지는 마세요, 부디, 하지만 하실 수 있다면 그분에게 사정을 다 말씀해 주시겠어요?"], ["If you please, and if you can, if there is a good opportunity, (don’t force an explanation upon him, pray,) but if you can, will you tell him the whole circumstances, and tell him also that I gave you leave to do so, because I felt that for poor papa’s sake I should not like to lose his respect, though we may never be likely to meet again?”", "그리고 제가 그렇게 해도 좋다고 허락했다고도 말씀해 주세요. 다시 만날 일이 없을지라도 가엾은 아버지를 봐서라도 그분의 존중을 잃고 싶지는 않다고 느꼈으니까요.”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“물론이지."], ["I think he ought to know.", "그는 알아야 마땅하다고 생각하네."], ["I do not like you to rest even under a shadow of an impropriety; he would not know what to think of seeing you alone with a young man.”", "나는 자네가 부적절하다는 그림자 밑에 조금이라도 놓여 있는 게 싫으이. 그도 자네가 젊은 남자와 단둘이 있는 것을 보고 무슨 생각을 해야 할지 몰랐을 게야.”"]], [["“As for that,”", "“그 점에 대해서라면,” 마거릿이 자못 도도하게 말했다."], ["said Margaret, rather haughtily, “I hold it is ‘Honi soit qui mal y pense,’ Yet still I should choose to have it explained, if any natural opportunity for easy explanation occurs.", "“저는 ‘악을 생각하는 자에게 화 있을진저’라고 봐요. 그래도 손쉽게 해명할 자연스러운 기회가 생긴다면 해명이 되기를 바라요."], ["But it is not to clear myself of any suspicion of improper conduct that I wish to have him told—if I thought that he had suspected me, I should not care for his good opinion—no!", "하지만 제가 그분에게 이야기가 전해지기를 바라는 것은 부적절한 처신을 했다는 의심을 벗기 위해서가 아니에요. 그분이 저를 의심했다고 생각했다면 저는 그분이 저를 좋게 보든 말든 개의치 않았을 거예요."], ["it is that he may learn how I was tempted, and how I fell into the snare; why I told that falsehood, in short.”", "아니에요! 제가 어떻게 유혹을 받았고 어떻게 그 덫에 빠졌는지, 요컨대 제가 왜 그 거짓말을 했는지 그분이 알게 되기를 바라는 거예요.”"]], [["“Which I don’t blame you for.", "“나는 그 일로 자네를 탓하지 않네."], ["It is no partiality of mine, I assure you.”", "내가 자네 편을 들어서 하는 말이 아닐세, 정말이야.”"]], [["“What other people may think of the rightness or wrongness is nothing in comparison to my own deep knowledge, my innate conviction that it was wrong.", "“다른 사람들이 그것이 옳으냐 그르냐를 어떻게 생각하는지는, 그것이 그르다는 저 자신의 깊은 앎, 타고난 확신에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요."], ["But we will not talk of that any more, if you please.", "하지만 괜찮으시다면 그 이야기는 더 하지 않겠어요. 이미 지난 일이에요."], ["It is done—my sin is sinned.", "제 죄는 이미 저질러졌어요."], ["I have now to put it behind me, and be truthful for evermore, if I can.”", "이제는 그것을 뒤로 하고, 할 수만 있다면 앞으로는 언제까지나 진실하게 살아야지요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋아."], ["If you like to be uncomfortable and morbid, be so.", "자네가 불편하고 병적으로 지내고 싶다면 그러게."], ["I always keep my conscience as tight shut up as a jack-in-a-box, for when it jumps into existence it surprises me by its size.", "나는 내 양심을 늘 도깨비 상자처럼 꽉 닫아 두네. 그게 튀어나오면 그 덩치에 내가 놀라거든."], ["So I coax it down again, as the fisherman coaxed the genii.", "그래서 어부가 정령을 구슬리듯 나도 그것을 구슬려 도로 집어넣지."], ["‘Wonderful,’ says I, ‘to think that you have been concealed so long, and in so small a compass, that I really did not know of your existence.", "‘놀랍구나’ 하고 나는 말하네. ‘그토록 오래, 그토록 작은 틈에 숨어 있었다니, 나는 정말 네가 있는 줄도 몰랐구나."], ["Pray, sir, instead of growing larger and larger every instant, and bewildering me with your misty outlines, would you once more compress yourself into your former dimensions?’ And when I’ve got him down, don’t I clap the seal on the vase, and take good care how I open it again, and how I go against Solomon, wisest of men, who confined him there.”", "부디, 나리, 순간마다 점점 더 커져서 그 자욱한 윤곽으로 나를 어리둥절하게 하지 말고, 한 번만 더 예전 크기로 몸을 줄여 주지 않겠소?’ 그렇게 해서 그놈을 들여보내고 나면 내가 항아리에 봉인을 찍고, 다시 열 때는 얼마나 조심하는지, 그리고 그놈을 거기 가둔, 사람 가운데 가장 지혜로운 솔로몬을 거스르지 않으려고 얼마나 애쓰는지 아나.”"]], [["But it was no smiling matter to Margaret.", "그러나 마거릿에게는 웃을 일이 아니었다."], ["She hardly attended to what Mr. Bell was saying.", "그녀는 벨 씨가 하는 말에 거의 귀를 기울이지 않았다."], ["Her thoughts ran upon the idea, before entertained, but which now had assumed the strength of a conviction, that Mr.", "그녀의 생각은 전부터 품고 있었으나 이제는 확신의 힘을 얻은 그 생각에 머물러 있었다. 손턴 씨가 더는 예전처럼 자기를 좋게 여기지 않는다는 것, 그가 자기에게 실망했다는 것이었다."], ["Thornton no longer held his former good opinion of her—that he was disappointed in her.", "그녀는 어떤 해명으로도 자신이 제자리를 되찾을 수 있으리라고 느껴지지 않았다. 그의 사랑 안에서가 아니었다."], ["She did not feel as if any explanation could ever reinstate her—not in his love, for that and any return on her part she had resolved never to dwell upon, and she kept rigidly to her resolution—but in the respect and high regard which she had hoped would have ever made him willing, in the spirit of Gerald Griffin’s beautiful lines,", "그것도, 그에 대한 자기 쪽의 어떤 응답도 결코 마음에 두지 않겠다고 그녀는 다짐했고 그 다짐을 엄격히 지켰다. 다만 제럴드 그리핀의 아름다운 시구에 담긴 그 마음으로 그가 언제까지나 기꺼이 이렇게 해 주기를 바랐던, 그 존경과 높은 평가 안에서였다."]], [["“To turn and look back when thou hearest The sound of my name.”", "“그대 내 이름 소리를 들을 때 돌아서서 뒤를 돌아보아 주기를.”"]], [["She kept choking and swallowing all the time that she thought about it.", "그것을 생각하는 내내 그녀는 자꾸만 목이 메어 삼켰다."], ["She tried to comfort herself with the idea, that what he imagined her to be, did not alter the fact of what she was.", "그가 자기를 어떤 사람으로 상상하든 그것이 자기가 어떤 사람인가 하는 사실을 바꾸지는 못한다는 생각으로 스스로를 달래 보려 했다."], ["But it was a truism, a phantom, and broke down under the weight of her regret.", "그러나 그것은 뻔한 말이었고 허깨비였으며, 그녀의 회한의 무게 아래 무너져 내렸다."], ["She had twenty questions on the tip of her tongue to ask Mr.", "그녀는 벨 씨에게 묻고 싶은 물음을 스무 가지나 혀끝에 달고 있었으나 하나도 입 밖에 내지 못했다."], ["Bell, but not one of them did she utter. Mr.", "벨 씨는 그녀가 피곤한 줄 알고 일찍 방으로 올려 보냈다."], ["Bell thought that she was tired, and sent her early to her room, where she sate long hours by the open window, gazing out on the purple dome above, where the stars arose, and twinkled and disappeared behind the great umbrageous trees before she went to bed.", "그녀는 그곳에서 오랜 시간 열린 창가에 앉아, 별들이 떠올랐다가 반짝이다가 그늘 짙은 큰 나무들 뒤로 사라질 때까지 머리 위의 자줏빛 하늘을 내다보다가 잠자리에 들었다."], ["All night long too, there burnt a little light on earth; a candle in her old bedroom, which was the nursery with the present inhabitants of the parsonage, until the new one was built.", "밤새도록 땅 위에도 작은 불빛 하나가 타고 있었다. 그녀의 옛 침실에 켜진 촛불이었는데, 그곳은 새 아이방이 지어질 때까지 지금 목사관 식구들의 아이방으로 쓰이고 있었다."], ["A sense of change, of individual nothingness, of perplexity and disappointment, overpowered Margaret.", "변화의 느낌, 개인의 하찮음, 당혹과 실망이 마거릿을 짓눌렀다. 어느 것도 예전과 같지 않았다."], ["Nothing had been the same; and this slight, all-pervading instability, had given her greater pain than if all had been too entirely changed for her to recognise it.", "그리고 이 사소하고 온 데 스며든 불안정함은 모든 것이 아주 달라져 알아볼 수 없게 되었을 경우보다 그녀에게 더 큰 아픔을 주었다."]], [["“I begin to understand now what heaven must be—and, oh!", "“이제야 천국이 어떤 것인지 알 것 같아."], ["the grandeur and repose of the words—‘The same yesterday, to-day, and for ever.’ Everlasting!", "아, 그 말씀의 장엄함과 안식이라니. ‘어제나 오늘이나 영원토록 동일하시니라.’ 영원하다!"], ["‘From everlasting to everlasting, Thou art God.’ That beautiful sky above me looks as though it could not change, and yet it will.", "‘영원부터 영원까지 주는 하나님이시니이다.’ 내 머리 위의 저 아름다운 하늘은 결코 변하지 않을 듯이 보이지만 그래도 변하겠지. 나는 너무 지쳤어."], ["I am so tired—so tired of being whirled on through all these phases of my life, in which nothing abides by me, no creature, no place; it is like the circle in which the victims of earthly passion eddy continually.", "아무것도 내 곁에 머물지 않는 이 온갖 삶의 국면을 지나 휘둘려 가는 것이 너무 지쳐. 어떤 사람도, 어떤 장소도."], ["I am in the mood in which women of another religion take the veil.", "그것은 지상의 정욕에 사로잡힌 자들이 끝없이 맴도는 그 굴레와 같아."], ["I seek heavenly steadfastness in earthly monotony.", "나는 다른 종교의 여자들이 수도복을 입는 그런 심정이야."], ["If I were a Roman Catholic and could deaden my heart, stun it with some great blow, I might become a nun.", "지상의 단조로움 속에서 천상의 굳건함을 찾는 거지. 내가 로마 가톨릭 신자여서 큰 충격 하나로 마음을 마비시키고 감각을 잃게 할 수 있다면 수녀가 될 수도 있을 텐데."], ["But I should pine after my kind; no, not my kind, for love for my species could never fill my heart to the utter exclusion of love for individuals.", "하지만 나는 같은 사람들이 그리워 애가 탈 거야. 아니, 같은 사람들이라기보다는, 인류에 대한 사랑이 개개인에 대한 사랑을 아주 몰아낼 만큼 내 마음을 가득 채우지는 결코 못할 테니까."], ["Perhaps it ought to be so, perhaps not; I cannot decide to-night.”", "어쩌면 그래야 하는지도, 어쩌면 그러지 않아야 하는지도 모르지. 오늘 밤에는 결정할 수가 없어.”"]], [["Wearily she went to bed, wearily she arose in four or five hours’ time.", "지친 몸으로 그녀는 잠자리에 들었고, 지친 몸으로 네다섯 시간 뒤에 일어났다."], ["But with the morning came hope, and a brighter view of things.", "그러나 아침과 더불어 희망이 찾아왔고 사물이 한결 밝게 보였다."]], [["“After all it is right,”", "“그래도 이것이 옳은 거야.”"], ["said she, hearing the voices of children at play while she was dressing.", "옷을 입으면서 노는 아이들의 목소리를 들으며 그녀가 말했다."], ["“If the world stood still, it would retrograde and become corrupt, if that is not Irish.", "“세상이 멈춰 선다면 뒷걸음질 쳐서 썩어 버리겠지. 그것이 앞뒤가 안 맞는 말이 아니라면 말이야."], ["Looking out of myself and my own painful sense of change, the progress of all around me is right and necessary.", "나 자신과 변화에 대한 나 자신의 아픈 느낌 밖으로 나가서 보면, 내 둘레의 모든 것이 나아가는 것은 옳고 필요한 일이야."], ["I must not think so much of how circumstances affect me myself, but how they affect others, if I wish to have a right judgment, or a hopeful trustful heart.”", "바른 판단을, 또는 희망차고 믿음 있는 마음을 갖고 싶다면, 상황이 나 자신에게 어떤 영향을 주는가보다 다른 사람들에게 어떤 영향을 주는가를 더 생각해야 해.”"], ["And with a smile ready in her eyes to quiver down to her lips, she went into the parlour and greeted Mr. Bell.", "그러고는 곧 입가로 번져 내릴 듯한 웃음을 눈가에 머금은 채 그녀는 응접실로 들어가 벨 씨에게 인사했다."]], [["“Ah, Missy!", "“아, 아가씨!"], ["you were up late last night, and so you’re late this morning.", "어젯밤에 늦게까지 안 잤으니 아침에도 늦었구먼."], ["Now I’ve got a little piece of news for you.", "자, 자네에게 전할 소식이 하나 있네."], ["What do you think of an invitation to dinner?", "저녁 초대를 받았다면 어떻겠나?"], ["a morning call, literally in the dewy morning.", "아침 방문을 받았지, 그야말로 이슬 맺힌 아침에 말이야."], ["Why, I’ve had the Vicar here already, on his way to the school.", "아, 내가 아까 목사님을 여기서 만났다네. 학교로 가는 길이었지."], ["How much the desire of giving our hostess a teetotal lecture for the benefit of the hay-makers, had to do with his earliness, I don’t know; but here he was, when I came down just before nine; and we are asked to dine there to-day.”", "건초 일꾼들을 위해 우리 안주인한테 금주 강연을 하고 싶은 마음이 그가 그토록 일찍 온 것과 얼마나 관계가 있는지는 모르겠네만, 아무튼 아홉 시가 채 안 되어 내가 내려왔을 때 여기 와 있었네. 그래서 우리가 오늘 거기서 저녁을 먹자고 청함을 받았지.”"]], [["“But Edith expects me back—I cannot go,”", "“하지만 이디스가 저를 기다리고 있어서 갈 수 없어요.”"], ["said Margaret, thankful to have so good an excuse.", "마거릿이 그렇게 좋은 핑계가 있는 것을 고맙게 여기며 말했다."]], [["“Yes!", "“그래!"], ["I know; so I told him.", "나도 아네. 그래서 그렇게 말했지."], ["I thought you would not want to go.", "자네가 가고 싶어 하지 않으리라 생각했네."], ["Still it is open, if you would like it.”", "그래도 자네가 원한다면 아직 열려 있는 이야기야.”"]], [["“Oh, no!” said Margaret.", "“아뇨, 괜찮아요!”"], ["“Let us keep to our plan.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["Let us start at twelve.", "“우리 계획대로 해요."], ["It is very good and kind of them; but indeed I could not go.”", "열두 시에 떠나요. 참 좋고 고마운 분들이지만, 정말이지 저는 갈 수 없어요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋아."], ["Don’t fidget yourself, and I’ll arrange it all.”", "안달하지 말게, 내가 다 알아서 하지.”"]], [["Before they left, Margaret stole round to the back of the Vicarage garden, and gathered a little straggling piece of honeysuckle.", "떠나기 전에 마거릿은 목사관 뜰 뒤쪽으로 몰래 돌아가서 제멋대로 뻗은 인동덩굴 한 가닥을 꺾었다."], ["She would not take a flower the day before, for fear of being observed, and her motives and feelings commented upon.", "전날에는 남의 눈에 띄어 제 마음과 심정이 이러쿵저러쿵 이야기되는 것이 두려워 꽃 한 송이 꺾지 않았던 것이다."], ["But as she returned across the common, the place was reinvested with the old enchanting atmosphere.", "그러나 공터를 가로질러 돌아오는 길에 그곳은 다시금 옛날의 그 홀리는 듯한 정취를 되찾고 있었다."], ["The common sounds of life were more musical there than anywhere else in the whole world, the light more golden, the life more tranquil and full of dreamy delight.", "삶의 흔한 소리들이 세상 어느 곳에서보다 그곳에서 더 음악 같았고, 빛은 더 금빛이었으며, 삶은 더 고요하고 꿈결 같은 기쁨으로 가득했다."], ["As Margaret remembered her feelings yesterday, she said to herself:", "마거릿은 어제의 자기 심정을 떠올리며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["“And I too change perpetually—now this, now that—now disappointment and peevish because all is not exactly as I had pictured it, and now suddenly discovering that the reality is far more beautiful than I had imagined it.", "“나도 끊임없이 변하는구나. 이랬다 저랬다."], ["Oh, Helstone!", "모든 것이 내가 그려 본 그대로가 아니라고 실망하고 짜증을 내다가, 이제는 문득 현실이 내가 상상했던 것보다 훨씬 아름답다는 것을 깨닫고."], ["I shall never love any place like you.”", "아, 헬스톤! 나는 결코 어떤 곳도 너처럼 사랑하지 못할 거야.”"]], [["A few days afterwards, she had found her level, and decided that she was very glad to have been there, and that she had seen it again, and that to her it would always be the prettiest spot in the world, but that it was so full of associations with former days, and especially with her father and mother, that if it were all to come over again, she should shrink back from such another visit as that which she had paid with Mr.", "며칠 뒤 그녀는 마음의 균형을 되찾고 이렇게 결론지었다."], ["Bell.", "그곳에 다녀온 것이, 그곳을 다시 본 것이 무척 기쁘다고, 자기에게 그곳은 언제까지나 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 곳이리라고, 그러나 그곳은 지난날의, 특히 아버지 어머니와 얽힌 추억으로 어찌나 가득한지, 만일 모든 것을 다시 겪어야 한다면 벨 씨와 함께 다녀온 것과 같은 그런 방문은 뒷걸음질 치며 피하리라고."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII", "# 제47장"]], [["“Experience, like a pale musician, holds A dulcimer of patience in his hand; Whence harmonies we cannot understand, Of God’s will in His worlds, the strain unfolds In sad, perplexed minors.”", "“경험은 창백한 악사처럼 제 손에 인내의 덜시머를 들고 있으니, 거기에서 우리가 알아들을 수 없는 화음이, 그분의 세계 속에 담긴 하느님 뜻의 가락이 슬프고 어지러운 단조로 풀려 나온다.”"], ["MRS. BROWNING.", "— 브라우닝 부인."]], [["About this time Dixon returned from Milton, and assumed her post as Margaret’s maid.", "이 무렵 딕슨이 밀턴에서 돌아와 마거릿의 몸종 자리를 다시 맡았다. 그녀는 밀턴의 소문을 끝도 없이 가져왔다."], ["She brought endless pieces of Milton gossip: How Martha had gone to live with Miss Thornton, on the latter’s marriage; with an account of the bridesmaids, dresses and breakfasts, at that interesting ceremony; how people thought that Mr. Thornton had made too grand a wedding of it, considering he had lost a deal by the strike, and had had to pay so much for the failure of his contracts; how little money articles of furniture—long cherished by Dixon—had fetched at the sale, which was a shame considering how rich folks were at Milton; how Mrs.", "마사가 손턴 양이 혼인하면서 그 댁으로 들어가 살게 되었다는 이야기며, 그 흥미로운 예식에서 신부 들러리와 드레스와 피로연이 어떠했는지 하는 이야기며, 파업으로 큰 손해를 보고 계약이 어그러진 값까지 그토록 많이 물어야 했던 처지에 손턴 씨가 혼례를 너무 성대하게 치렀다고들 하더라는 이야기며, 딕슨이 오래도록 아껴 온 세간붙이들이 경매에서 얼마나 헐값에 넘어갔는지, 밀턴 사람들이 그토록 부유한 것을 생각하면 부끄러운 일이라는 이야기며, 어느 날은 손턴 부인이 와서 두세 가지를 아주 싸게 사 갔는데 이튿날은 손턴 씨가 와서 한두 가지를 꼭 손에 넣고 싶은 마음에 제가 부른 값에 제가 또 값을 얹어 불러 구경꾼들을 크게 즐겁게 했다는 이야기까지."], ["Thornton had come one day and got two or three good bargains, and Mr.", "그러니 딕슨이 보기에는 그것으로 셈이 맞은 셈이었다."], ["Thornton had come the next, and in his desire to obtain one or two things, had bid against himself, much to the enjoyment of the bystanders, so as Dixon observed, that made things even; if Mrs.", "손턴 부인이 너무 적게 냈다면 손턴 씨가 너무 많이 냈으니까."], ["Thornton paid too little, Mr. Thornton paid too much.", "벨 씨는 책에 관해 온갖 지시를 보내왔는데, 어찌나 까다로운지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다."], ["Mr. Bell had sent all sorts of orders about the books; there was no understanding him, he was so particular; if he had come himself it would have been all right, but letters always were and always will be more puzzling than they are worth.", "직접 왔더라면 다 잘되었을 것을, 편지란 언제나 그 값어치보다 사람을 더 헷갈리게 만들었고 앞으로도 그럴 것이었다."], ["Dixon had not much to tell about the Higginses.", "히긴스네에 대해서는 딕슨이 들려줄 이야기가 별로 없었다."], ["Her memory had an aristocratic bias, and was very treacherous whenever she tried to recall any circumstance connected with those below her in life.", "그녀의 기억력은 귀족 취향으로 기울어 있어서, 제 아래 신분인 사람들에 얽힌 일을 떠올리려 들 때면 여간 미덥지 않았다."], ["Nicholas was very well she believed.", "니컬러스는 아주 잘 지내는 것 같다고 했다."], ["He had been several times at the house asking for news of Miss Margaret—the only person who ever did ask, except once Mr.", "그는 마거릿 아가씨의 소식을 물으러 몇 번이나 집에 들렀다. 손턴 씨가 한 번 물은 것 말고는 그런 것을 물어 온 사람이라고는 그뿐이었다."], ["Thornton. And Mary?", "그러면 메리는?"], ["oh! of course she was very well, a great, stout, slatternly thing!", "아, 물론 아주 잘 지내지요, 그 덩치 크고 뚱뚱하고 칠칠맞은 것이!"], ["She did hear, or perhaps it was only a dream of hers, though it would be strange if she had dreamt of such people as the Higginses, that Mary had gone to work at Mr.", "그녀는 들은 것 같기도 했다. 아니면 그저 제가 꾼 꿈이었는지도 모르지만 — 하기야 히긴스네 같은 사람들 꿈을 꾸었다면 그것도 이상한 일이었다 — 아버지가 요리를 배우게 하고 싶어 해서 메리가 손턴 씨 공장에 일하러 갔다던가."], ["Thornton’s mill, because her father wished her to know how to cook; but what nonsense that could mean she didn’t know.", "그렇지만 그게 무슨 말도 안 되는 소린지는 자기도 모르겠다고 했다."], ["Margaret rather agreed with her that the story was incoherent enough to be like a dream.", "마거릿도 그 이야기가 꿈이라 해도 좋을 만큼 앞뒤가 안 맞는다는 데 동의하는 편이었다."], ["Still it was pleasant to have some one now with whom she could talk of Milton, and Milton people.", "그래도 이제 밀턴과 밀턴 사람들 이야기를 나눌 상대가 생겼다는 것은 즐거운 일이었다."], ["Dixon was not over-fond of the subject, rather wishing to leave that part of her life in shadow.", "딕슨은 그 화제를 그리 좋아하지 않았다. 제 삶의 그 대목은 그늘 속에 두고 싶어 하는 쪽이었다."], ["She liked much more to dwell upon speeches of Mr. Bell’s, which had suggested an idea to her of what was really his intention—making Margaret his heiress.", "그녀는 벨 씨가 한 말들을 되뇌는 쪽을 훨씬 좋아했으니, 그 말들이 그의 진짜 속뜻이 무엇인지에 대한 생각 하나를 그녀에게 심어 주었기 때문이다."], ["But her young lady gave her no encouragement, nor in any way gratified her insinuating enquiries, however disguised in the form of suspicions or assertions.", "마거릿을 제 상속인으로 삼으려 한다는 것. 그러나 그녀의 아가씨는 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았고, 의심이나 단정의 형태로 아무리 그럴듯하게 감춘들 그 넌지시 캐묻는 말에 조금도 응해 주지 않았다."]], [["All this time Margaret had a strange undefined longing to hear that Mr.", "이 내내 마거릿은 벨 씨가 사업차 밀턴을 찾아갔다는 소식을 듣고 싶다는, 이상하게 뭐라 이름 붙일 수 없는 갈망을 품고 있었다."], ["Bell had gone to pay one of his business visits to Milton; for it had been well understood between them, at the time of their conversation at Helstone, that the explanation she had desired should only be given to Mr. Thornton by word of mouth, and even in that manner should be in nowise forced upon him.", "헬스톤에서 이야기를 나눌 때 두 사람 사이에는, 그녀가 바라는 그 해명은 오직 말로만 손턴 씨에게 전해져야 하고 그렇게 하더라도 결코 억지로 떠맡겨서는 안 된다는 것이 잘 이해되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Bell was no great correspondent, but he wrote from time to time long or short letters, as the humour took him, and although Margaret was not conscious of any definite hope, on receiving them, yet she always put away his notes with a little feeling of disappointment.", "벨 씨는 부지런한 편지 상대가 못 되었으나 기분 내키는 대로 이따금 길거나 짧은 편지를 보내왔고, 마거릿은 그것을 받으며 어떤 뚜렷한 기대를 품고 있다고 스스로 의식하지는 않으면서도 늘 조금은 실망스러운 마음으로 그 쪽지를 치워 두곤 했다."], ["He was not going to Milton; he said nothing about it at any rate.", "그는 밀턴에 갈 생각이 없었다. 적어도 그에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["she must be patient.", "참아야지."], ["Sooner or later the mists would be cleared away. Mr.", "이르든 늦든 안개는 걷힐 것이다."], ["Bell’s letters were hardly like his usual self; they were short, and complaining, with every now and then a little touch of bitterness that was unusual.", "벨 씨의 편지는 여느 때의 그답지 않았다. 짧고 투정이 섞였으며, 이따금 그답지 않은 씁쓸함이 살짝 배어 있었다."], ["He did not look forward to the future; he rather seemed to regret the past, and be weary of the present.", "그는 앞날을 내다보지 않았다. 오히려 지난날을 아쉬워하고 지금을 지겨워하는 듯했다."], ["Margaret fancied that he could not be well; but in answer to some enquiry of hers as to his health, he sent her a short note, saying there was an old-fashioned complaint called the spleen; that he was suffering from that, and it was for her to decide if it was more mental or physical; but that he should like to indulge himself in grumbling, without being obliged to send a bulletin every time.", "마거릿은 그가 편치 않은 것이 아닐까 싶었다. 그러나 건강이 어떠냐는 그녀의 물음에 그는 짤막한 쪽지를 보내, 비장증이라 부르는 옛날식 병이 있는데 자기가 그것을 앓고 있으며 그것이 마음의 것인지 몸의 것인지는 그녀가 정할 일이라고, 다만 자기는 투덜거릴 때마다 병세 보고서를 보내야 하는 의무 없이 마음껏 투덜거리고 싶다고 했다."]], [["In consequence of this note, Margaret made no more enquiries about his health.", "이 쪽지 때문에 마거릿은 그의 건강에 대해 더는 묻지 않았다."], ["One day Edith let out accidentally a fragment of a conversation which she had had with Mr.", "어느 날 이디스가 벨 씨와 나눈 대화의 한 토막을 무심코 흘렸다."], ["Bell, when he was last in London, which possessed Margaret with the idea that he had some notion of taking her to pay a visit to her brother and new sister-in-law, at Cadiz, in the autumn.", "그가 마지막으로 런던에 왔을 때의 일이었는데, 그 이야기가 마거릿의 마음을 사로잡아, 그가 가을에 그녀를 카디스로 데려가 오빠와 새 올케를 찾아보게 할 생각을 품고 있다는 생각에 빠지게 했다."], ["She questioned and cross-questioned Edith, till the latter was weary, and declared that there was nothing more to remember; all he had said was that he half-thought he should go, and hear for himself what Frederick had to say about the mutiny; and that it would be a good opportunity for Margaret to become acquainted with her new sister-in-law; that he always went somewhere during the long vacation, and did not see why he should not go to Spain as well as anywhere else.", "그녀는 이디스가 지쳐서 더 기억날 것이 없다고 잘라 말할 때까지 묻고 또 캐물었다. 그가 한 말이라고는 자기가 갈까 반쯤 생각 중이고 프레더릭이 반란에 대해 무어라 하는지 직접 들어 보고 싶다는 것, 마거릿이 새 올케와 알고 지내기에도 좋은 기회가 되리라는 것, 자기는 긴 방학이면 늘 어디든 가는데 다른 어디나 마찬가지로 스페인이라고 못 갈 까닭이 없지 않으냐는 것뿐이었다."], ["That was all.", "그것이 전부였다."], ["Edith hoped Margaret did not want to leave them, that she was so anxious about all this.", "이디스는 마거릿이 이 일에 그토록 애를 태우는 것이 자기들을 떠나고 싶어서는 아니기를 바랐다."], ["And then, having nothing else particular to do, she cried, and said that she knew she cared much more for Margaret than Margaret did for her.", "그러고는 달리 딱히 할 일도 없던 참이라 울음을 터뜨리며, 마거릿이 자기를 생각하는 것보다 자기가 마거릿을 훨씬 더 아낀다는 것을 안다고 말했다."], ["Margaret comforted her as well as she could, but she could hardly explain to her how this idea of Spain, mere Château en Espagne as it might be, charmed and delighted her.", "마거릿은 힘닿는 대로 그녀를 달랬으나, 이 스페인 이야기가 그저 스페인의 성일 뿐일지라도 얼마나 제 마음을 사로잡고 들뜨게 하는지는 도무지 설명할 수가 없었다."], ["Edith was in the mood to think that any pleasure enjoyed away from her was a tacit affront, or at best a proof of indifference.", "이디스는 제 곁을 떠나 누리는 즐거움이라면 무엇이든 말 없는 모욕이거나 잘 봐줘야 무관심의 증거라고 여길 기분이었다."], ["So Margaret had to keep her pleasure to herself, and could only let it escape by the safety-valve of asking Dixon, when she dressed for dinner, if she would not like to see Master Frederick and his new wife very much indeed?", "그래서 마거릿은 그 기쁨을 혼자 간직할 수밖에 없었고, 저녁 식사 채비로 옷을 갈아입을 때 딕슨에게 프레더릭 도련님과 그 새색시를 정말이지 몹시 보고 싶지 않으냐고 묻는 안전밸브로나 겨우 그것을 흘려보낼 수 있었다."]], [["“She’s a Papist, Miss, isn’t she?”", "“그 아씨는 교황파 아닙니까요, 아가씨?”"]], [["“I believe—oh yes, certainly!”", "“그럴 거예요. 아, 그래요, 틀림없이요!”"], ["said Margaret, a little damped for an instant at this recollection.", "마거릿이 그 생각이 떠올라 잠깐 김이 빠진 채 말했다."]], [["“And they live in a Popish country?”", "“그리고 두 분은 교황파 나라에서 사시고요?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그래요.”"]], [["“Then I’m afraid I must say, that my soul is dearer to me than even Master Frederick, his own dear self.", "“그렇다면 죄송하지만 이렇게 말씀드려야겠습니다요. 제 영혼은 제게 프레더릭 도련님보다도, 그 귀하신 몸소보다도 더 소중하니까요."], ["I should be in a perpetual terror, Miss, lest I should be converted.”", "저는 노상 벌벌 떨며 지낼 겁니다요, 아가씨. 혹시라도 제가 개종당할까 봐서요.”"]], [["“Oh,”", "“어머,” 마거릿이 말했다."], ["said Margaret, “I do not know that I am going; and if I go, I am not such a fine lady as to be unable to travel without you.", "“내가 간다고 정해진 것도 아니에요. 그리고 간다 해도 나는 딕슨 없이는 여행도 못 할 만큼 대단한 귀부인은 아니고요."], ["No!", "아니에요!"], ["dear old Dixon, you shall have a long holiday, if we go.", "정다운 딕슨 할멈, 우리가 가게 되면 딕슨은 긴 휴가를 얻는 거예요."], ["But I’m afraid it is a long ‘if.’”", "하지만 아무래도 그건 아주 긴 ‘만약’인 것 같네요.”"]], [["Now Dixon did not like this speech.", "그런데 딕슨은 이 말이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["In the first place, she did not like Margaret’s trick of calling her ‘dear old Dixon’ whenever she was particularly demonstrative.", "우선 그녀는 마거릿이 유난히 다정해질 때면 자기를 ‘정다운 딕슨 할멈’이라고 부르는 버릇이 싫었다."], ["She knew that Miss Hale was apt to call all people that she liked “old,”", "헤일 양이 좋아하는 사람이면 누구에게나 애정의 표시 삼아 ‘할멈’이니 ‘영감’이니 하는 말을 붙이곤 한다는 것은 그녀도 알고 있었다."], ["as a sort of term of endearment; but Dixon always winced away from the application of the word to herself, who, being not much past fifty, was, she thought, in the very prime of life.", "그러나 딕슨은 그 말이 제게 붙는 데는 늘 움찔하며 물러났으니, 쉰을 얼마 넘기지 않은 자기야말로 인생의 한창때에 있다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Secondly, she did not like being so easily taken at her word; she had, with all her terror, a lurking curiosity about Spain, the Inquisition, and Popish mysteries.", "둘째로 그녀는 제 말이 그렇게 선뜻 곧이곧대로 받아들여지는 것도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그 온갖 겁에도 불구하고 그녀에게는 스페인과 종교재판과 교황파의 신비에 대한 은근한 호기심이 도사리고 있었던 것이다."], ["So, after clearing her throat, as if to show her willingness to do away with difficulties, she asked Miss Hale whether she thought, if she took care never to see a priest, or enter into one of their churches, there would be so very much danger of her being converted?", "그리하여 그녀는 어려움을 없애 볼 뜻이 있음을 내보이기라도 하듯 헛기침을 하고 나서, 사제를 절대로 보지 않고 그들의 교회에도 들어가지 않도록 조심만 한다면 그래도 제가 개종당할 위험이 그렇게까지 크겠느냐고 헤일 양에게 물었다."], ["Master Frederick, to be sure, had gone over unaccountably.", "하기야 프레더릭 도련님이야 알 수 없게도 넘어가 버리셨지만."]], [["“I fancy it was love that first predisposed him to conversion,”", "“오빠를 먼저 개종 쪽으로 기울게 한 건 사랑이었다고 생각해요.”"], ["said Margaret, sighing.", "마거릿이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, Miss!”", "“그렇습니까요, 아가씨!”"], ["said Dixon; “well!", "딕슨이 말했다. “그렇다면!"], ["I can preserve myself from priests, and from churches; but love steals in unawares!", "저야 사제한테서도 교회한테서도 저를 지킬 수 있습니다만, 사랑이란 건 저도 모르는 새에 스며드는 것이잖습니까요!"], ["I think it’s as well I should not go.”", "아무래도 저는 가지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다요.”"]], [["Margaret was afraid of letting her mind run too much upon this Spanish plan.", "마거릿은 이 스페인 계획에 마음을 너무 많이 내달리게 두는 것이 겁났다."], ["But it took off her thoughts from too impatiently dwelling upon her desire to have all explained to Mr. Thornton.", "그러나 그 덕분에 손턴 씨에게 모든 것을 밝히고 싶다는 소망을 지나치게 조바심 내며 곱씹지 않을 수 있었다."], ["Mr. Bell appeared for the present to be stationary at Oxford, and to have no immediate purpose of going to Milton, and some secret restraint seemed to hang over Margaret, and prevent her from even asking, or alluding again to any probability of such a visit on his part.", "벨 씨는 당분간 옥스퍼드에 붙박여 있는 듯했고 밀턴에 갈 당장의 뜻은 없어 보였는데, 어떤 은밀한 억제 같은 것이 마거릿을 짓눌러 그가 그런 걸음을 할 가망이 있는지 묻는 것조차, 다시 그 이야기를 넌지시 꺼내는 것조차 하지 못하게 막았다."], ["Nor did she feel at liberty to name what Edith had told her of the idea he had entertained,—it might be but for five minutes,—of going to Spain.", "이디스가 전해 준 이야기, 그가 스페인에 갈 생각을 — 어쩌면 5분 동안만 품었을 그 생각을 — 했다는 것을 입에 올릴 자유도 느끼지 못했다. 그는 헬스톤에서, 그 볕 좋고 한가롭던 하루 내내 그런 말을 한 번도 하지 않았다."], ["He had never named it at Helstone, during all that sunny day of leisure; it was very probably but the fancy of a moment,—but if it were true, what a bright outlet it would be from the monotony of her present life, which was beginning to fall upon her.", "아마도 한순간의 공상에 지나지 않았으리라. 그러나 만약 그것이 참이라면, 이제 그녀를 짓누르기 시작한 지금 삶의 단조로움에서 얼마나 환한 출구가 되어 줄 것인가."]], [["One of the great pleasures of Margaret’s life at this time was in Edith’s boy.", "이 무렵 마거릿의 삶에서 큰 즐거움 가운데 하나는 이디스의 아들이었다."], ["He was the pride and plaything of both father and mother, as long as he was good; but he had a strong will of his own, and as soon as he burst out into one of his stormy passions, Edith would throw herself back in despair and fatigue, and sigh out, “Oh dear, what shall I do with him!", "아이는 얌전할 때는 아버지와 어머니 양쪽의 자랑이자 노리개였다. 그러나 아이에게는 제 나름의 억센 고집이 있었고, 그 사나운 성깔을 한바탕 터뜨릴 때면 이디스는 절망과 피로에 젖어 몸을 뒤로 던지고는 한숨을 쉬며 말하곤 했다."], ["Do, Margaret, please ring the bell for Hanley.”", "“어머나, 얘를 어떡하면 좋아! 마거릿, 제발 종을 울려서 핸리 좀 불러 줘.”"]], [["But Margaret almost liked him better in these manifestations of character than in his good blue-sashed moods.", "그러나 마거릿은 파란 띠를 두른 얌전한 기분일 때보다 이렇게 성깔을 드러낼 때의 아이를 오히려 더 좋아하는 편이었다. 그녀는 아이를 안고 어느 방으로 들어가 단둘이서 그것을 겨루어 냈다."], ["She would carry him off into a room, where they two alone battled it out; she with a firm power which subdued him into peace, while every sudden charm and wile she possessed was exerted on the side of right, until he would rub his little hot and tear-smeared face all over hers, kissing and caressing till he often fell asleep in her arms or on her shoulder.", "그녀는 아이를 평온으로 다스려 눌러 앉히는 흔들림 없는 힘을 지녔고, 제가 지닌 온갖 불시의 매력과 재주를 옳은 편에 실어 다 쏟아부었다. 그러면 아이는 마침내 뜨겁고 눈물로 얼룩진 작은 얼굴을 그녀의 얼굴에 온통 비벼 대며 입 맞추고 어루만지다가, 그녀의 품이나 어깨에서 잠들어 버리기 일쑤였다."], ["Those were Margaret’s sweetest moments.", "그것이 마거릿에게는 가장 달콤한 순간이었다."], ["They gave her a taste of the feeling that she believed would be denied to her for ever.", "그 순간은 그녀가 자기에게는 영영 허락되지 않으리라 믿고 있던 감정을 조금이나마 맛보게 해 주었다."]], [["Mr. Henry Lennox added a new and not disagreeable element to the course of the household life by his frequent presence.", "헨리 레녹스 씨는 자주 드나들며 이 집안의 살아가는 흐름에 새롭고 싫지 않은 요소를 더해 주었다."], ["Margaret thought him colder, if more brilliant than formerly; but there were strong intellectual tastes, and much and varied knowledge, which gave flavour to the otherwise rather insipid conversation.", "마거릿이 보기에 그는 예전보다 한결 빛나기는 해도 더 차가워진 듯했다. 그러나 그에게는 강한 지적 취향과 넓고 다양한 지식이 있어서, 그것이 없었더라면 다소 밍밍했을 대화에 맛을 내 주었다."], ["Margaret saw glimpses in him of a slight contempt for his brother and sister-in-law, and for their mode of life, which he seemed to consider as frivolous and purposeless.", "마거릿은 그가 제 형과 형수를, 그리고 그들의 살아가는 방식을 살짝 경멸하는 기색을 언뜻언뜻 엿보았다. 그는 그 삶을 경박하고 목적 없는 것으로 여기는 눈치였다."], ["He once or twice spoke to his brother, in Margaret’s presence, in a pretty sharp tone of enquiry, as to whether he meant entirely to relinquish his profession; and on Captain Lennox’s reply, that he had quite enough to live upon, she had seen Mr.", "그는 마거릿이 있는 자리에서 한두 번 제 형에게 자못 날카로운 물음의 어조로, 정말로 직업을 아주 놓아 버릴 작정이냐고 물은 적이 있었다. 그리고 먹고살 만큼은 넉넉하다는 레녹스 대위의 대답에 레녹스 씨가 입술을 비틀며 이렇게 말하는 것을 그녀는 보았다."], ["Lennox’s curl of the lip as he said, “And is that all you live for?”", "“그러면 형님이 사는 목적이 그게 전부입니까?”"]], [["But the brothers were much attached to each other, in the way that any two persons are, when the one is cleverer and always leads the other, and this last is patiently content to be led.", "그러나 두 형제는 서로에게 무척 정이 깊었다. 한쪽이 더 영리해 늘 앞장서 이끌고 다른 한쪽은 이끌리는 데 참을성 있게 만족하는 두 사람 사이에 흔히 그러하듯이."], ["Mr. Lennox was pushing on in his profession; cultivating, with profound calculation, all those connections that might eventually be of service to him; keen-sighted, far-seeing, intelligent, sarcastic, and proud.", "레녹스 씨는 제 직업에서 앞으로 밀고 나가는 중이었다."], ["Since the one long conversation relating to Frederick’s affairs, which she had with him the first evening in Mr.", "언젠가 자기에게 도움이 될 만한 연줄이라면 무엇이든 깊은 계산 아래 가꾸었고, 눈이 밝고 멀리 내다보며 총명하고 빈정거리기 좋아하고 자존심이 강했다."], ["Bell’s presence, she had had no great intercourse with him, further than that which arose out of their close relations with the same household.", "벨 씨가 있는 자리에서 첫날 저녁에 그와 나누었던, 프레더릭의 일에 관한 그 한 차례 긴 대화 이후로 그녀는 같은 집안과 가까이 얽힌 데서 생기는 왕래 말고는 그와 별다른 교류가 없었다."], ["But this was enough to wear off the shyness on her side, and any symptoms of mortified pride and vanity on his.", "그러나 그것만으로도 그녀 쪽의 수줍음과 그의 쪽에 남아 있던 상한 자존심과 허영의 기색은 다 씻겨 나갔다."], ["They met, continually, of course, but she thought that he rather avoided being alone with her; she fancied that he, as well as she, perceived that they had drifted strangely apart from their former anchorage, side by side, in many of their opinions, and all their tastes.", "물론 두 사람은 끊임없이 마주쳤으나, 그녀는 그가 자기와 단둘이 있는 것을 오히려 피한다고 여겼다. 그도 자기와 마찬가지로, 두 사람이 나란히 매여 있던 예전의 정박지에서 이상하리만치 멀리 떠내려왔음을, 많은 의견에서 그리고 온갖 취향에서 그러함을 알아차리고 있는 것이라고 그녀는 짐작했다."]], [["And yet, when he had spoken unusually well, or with remarkable epigrammatic point, she felt that his eye sought the expression of her countenance first of all, if but for an instant; and that, in the family intercourse which constantly threw them together, her opinion was the one to which he listened with a deference,—the more complete, because it was reluctantly paid, and concealed as much as possible.", "그러면서도 그가 유난히 말을 잘했을 때나 놀랄 만큼 날 선 경구를 던졌을 때면, 그의 눈이 무엇보다 먼저 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정을 찾는다는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다. 한순간에 지나지 않더라도 그러했다. 그리고 두 사람을 끊임없이 한자리에 몰아넣는 그 집안의 왕래 속에서, 그가 공손함을 담아 귀 기울이는 의견은 바로 그녀의 것이었다. 그 공손함은 마지못해 바쳐지고 될 수 있는 대로 감추어졌기에 더욱 온전한 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII", "# 제48장"]], [["“NE’ER TO BE FOUND AGAIN.”", "「다시는 찾을 수 없는 것」"]], [["“My own, my father’s friend!", "“내 벗이여, 내 아버지의 벗이여!"], ["I cannot part with thee!", "나는 그대와 헤어질 수 없어라!"], ["I ne’er have shown, thou ne’er hast known, How dear thou art to me.”", "나는 한 번도 내보인 적 없고, 그대는 한 번도 알지 못했으니, 그대가 내게 얼마나 소중한지를.”"], ["ANON.", "— 작자 미상."]], [["The elements of the dinner-parties which Mrs.", "레녹스 부인이 여는 만찬을 이루는 요소는 이러했다."], ["Lennox gave, were these; her friends contributed the beauty, Captain Lennox the easy knowledge of the subjects of the day; and Mr.", "그녀의 친구들은 아름다움을 보탰고, 레녹스 대위는 그날그날의 화제에 대한 여유로운 식견을 보탰다."], ["Henry Lennox, and the sprinkling of rising men who were received as his friends, brought the wit, the cleverness, the keen and extensive knowledge of which they knew well enough how to avail themselves without seeming pedantic, or burdening the rapid flow of conversation.", "그리고 헨리 레녹스 씨와, 그의 벗으로 받아들여진 몇몇 떠오르는 인사들은 재치와 영민함과 예리하고 폭넓은 지식을 가져왔는데, 그들은 현학적으로 보이지도 않고 빠르게 흘러가는 대화에 부담을 주지도 않으면서 그것을 활용하는 법을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["These dinners were delightful; but even here Margaret’s dissatisfaction found her out.", "이 만찬들은 즐거웠다. 그러나 여기서도 마거릿의 불만은 기어이 그녀를 찾아냈다."], ["Every talent, every feeling, every acquirement; nay, even every tendency towards virtue, was used up as materials for fireworks; the hidden, sacred fire, exhausted itself in sparkle and crackle.", "온갖 재능, 온갖 감정, 온갖 소양, 아니 미덕을 향한 온갖 성향까지도 불꽃놀이의 재료로 소모되었다. 감추어져 있어야 할 신성한 불이 반짝임과 탁탁거림 속에 저를 다 태워 없앴다."], ["They talked about art in a merely sensuous way, dwelling on outside effects, instead of allowing themselves to learn what it has to teach.", "그들은 예술을 두고 오로지 감각적인 방식으로만 이야기했고, 예술이 가르쳐 주는 바를 배우려 하는 대신 겉으로 드러난 효과에만 머물렀다."], ["They lashed themselves up into an enthusiasm about high subjects in company, and never thought about them when they were alone; they squandered their capabilities of appreciation into a mere flow of appropriate words.", "그들은 사람들 앞에서는 고상한 주제를 두고 스스로를 채찍질하듯 열광에 몰아넣었으나, 혼자 있을 때에는 그런 것을 한 번도 생각하지 않았다."], ["One day, after the gentlemen had come up into the drawing-room, Mr.", "그들은 감상할 줄 아는 능력을 그저 알맞은 말이 흘러나오게 하는 데 탕진해 버렸다."], ["Lennox drew near to Margaret, and addressed her in almost the first voluntary words he had spoken to her since she had returned to live in Harley Street.", "어느 날 신사들이 응접실로 올라온 뒤, 레녹스 씨가 마거릿에게 다가와 말을 건넸다. 그녀가 할리 가로 돌아와 살게 된 뒤로 그가 자진해서 그녀에게 건넨 거의 첫마디였다."]], [["“You did not look pleased at what Shirley was saying at dinner.”", "“저녁 식사 때 셜리가 하는 말이 마음에 들지 않으시는 눈치더군요.”"]], [["“Didn’t I?", "“그랬나요?"], ["My face must be very expressive,”", "제 얼굴이 어지간히 속을 다 드러내나 봐요.”"], ["replied Margaret.", "마거릿이 대답했다."]], [["“It always was.", "“늘 그랬지요."], ["It has not lost the trick of being eloquent.”", "아직도 웅변하는 재주를 잃지 않았군요.”"]], [["“I did not like,”", "“저는 마음에 들지 않았어요.”"], ["said Margaret, hastily, “his way of advocating what he knew to be wrong—so glaringly wrong—even in jest.”", "마거릿이 서둘러 말했다. “틀린 줄 뻔히 알면서도, 그토록 빤히 틀린 것을 농담으로라도 두둔하는 그 방식이요.”"]], [["“But it was very clever.", "“하지만 아주 영리했지요."], ["How every word told!", "한마디 한마디가 얼마나 잘 들어맞던지!"], ["Did you remember the happy epithets?”", "그 절묘한 표현들을 기억하시나요?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“네.”"]], [["“And despise them, you would like to add.", "“그리고 그것들을 경멸한다고 덧붙이고 싶으시겠지요."], ["Pray don’t scruple, though he is my friend.”", "그가 제 벗이라 해도 개의치 마십시오.”"]], [["“There!", "“바로 그거예요!"], ["that is the exact tone in you, that”—she stopped short.", "당신의 그 말투가 바로—” 그녀는 말을 뚝 끊었다."]], [["He listened for a moment to see if she would finish her sentence; but she only reddened, and turned away; before she did so, however, she heard him say, in a very low, clear voice,—", "그는 그녀가 문장을 끝맺을지 잠시 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그녀는 얼굴만 붉힌 채 돌아서 버렸다. 다만 돌아서기 전에 그가 아주 낮고 또렷한 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“If my tones, or modes of thought, are what you dislike, will you do me the justice to tell me so, and so give me the chance of learning to please you.”", "“제 말투나 사고방식이 당신께서 싫어하시는 것이라면, 저에게 그렇다고 말해 주는 공정함을 베풀어 주시겠습니까? 그래서 당신 마음에 들도록 배울 기회를 저에게 주시겠습니까.”"]], [["All these weeks there was no intelligence of Mr. Bell’s going to Milton.", "그 몇 주 내내 벨 씨가 밀턴에 간다는 소식은 없었다."], ["He had spoken of it at Helstone as of a journey which he might have to take in a very short time from then; but he must have transacted his business by writing.", "그는 헬스톤에서 그것을 머지않아 떠나야 할지도 모를 여행처럼 말했었다. 그러나 이제 와 생각해 보면 그는 편지로 그 일을 처리해 버렸을 것이라고 마거릿은 여겼다."], ["Margaret thought, ere now, and she knew that if he could, he would avoid going to a place which he disliked, and moreover would little understand the secret importance which she affixed to the explanation that could only be given by word of mouth.", "그리고 그가 할 수만 있다면 싫어하는 곳에 가는 일을 피하리라는 것을, 게다가 오직 직접 말로만 해 줄 수 있는 그 해명에 자신이 남몰래 부여한 중대함을 그가 거의 이해하지 못하리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She knew that he would feel that it was necessary that it should be done; but whether in summer, autumn, or winter, it would signify very little.", "그가 그 일을 꼭 해야 한다고 느끼기는 하겠지만, 그것이 여름이든 가을이든 겨울이든 그에게는 별로 대수롭지 않으리라는 것도 그녀는 알았다."], ["It was now August, and there had been no mention of the Spanish journey to which he had alluded to Edith, and Margaret tried to reconcile herself to the fading away of this illusion.", "이제 8월이었고, 그가 이디스에게 넌지시 비쳤던 스페인 여행 이야기도 전혀 없었다. 마거릿은 이 헛된 꿈이 스러져 가는 것에 스스로를 체념시키려 애썼다."]], [["But one morning she received a letter, saying that next week he meant to come up to town; he wanted to see her about a plan which he had in his head; and, moreover, he intended to treat himself to a little doctoring, as he had begun to come round to her opinion, that it would be pleasanter to think that his health was more in fault than he, when he found himself irritable and cross. There was altogether a tone of forced cheerfulness in the letter, as Margaret noticed afterwards; but at the time her attention was taken up by Edith’s exclamations.", "그러나 어느 날 아침 그녀는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 다음 주에 런던으로 올라올 작정이라는 것이었다. 머릿속에 품고 있는 계획 하나를 두고 그녀를 만나고 싶다고 했다. 게다가 자신이 짜증스럽고 심통이 날 때면 자기 탓이라기보다 건강 탓이라고 여기는 편이 마음 편하리라는 그녀의 의견에 이제 동의하게 되었으므로, 진료를 좀 받아 보는 호사를 누릴 참이라고도 했다. 나중에 마거릿이 알아차린 대로 그 편지 전체에는 억지로 꾸며 낸 쾌활함의 어조가 배어 있었다. 그러나 그때는 이디스의 탄성에 정신이 팔려 있었다."]], [["“Coming up to town!", "“런던으로 올라오신다고!"], ["Oh dear!", "어머나!"], ["and I am so worn out by the heat that I don’t believe I have strength enough in me for another dinner.", "난 더위에 어찌나 지쳤는지 만찬을 한 번 더 치를 기운이 남아 있을 것 같지도 않은데."], ["Besides, everybody has left but our dear stupid selves, who can’t settle where to go to.", "게다가 어디로 갈지 정하지도 못한 우리 어리석은 식구들만 빼고는 다들 떠나 버렸잖아."], ["There would be nobody to meet him.”", "그분을 맞아 드릴 사람이 아무도 없겠는걸.”"]], [["“I’m sure he would much rather come and dine with us quite alone than with the most agreeable strangers you could pick up.", "“네가 그러모을 수 있는 가장 유쾌한 낯선 분들과 함께하시느니 우리끼리 조용히 저녁을 드시는 편을 훨씬 더 좋아하실 게 틀림없어."], ["Besides, if he is not well he won’t wish for invitations.", "게다가 편찮으시다면 초대받고 싶어 하지도 않으시겠지."], ["I am glad he has owned it at last.", "마침내 털어놓으셔서 다행이야."], ["I was sure he was ill from the whole tone of his letters, and yet he would not answer me when I asked him, and I had no third person to whom I could apply for news.”", "편지의 온 어조로 보아 편찮으신 게 틀림없다고 여겼는데, 내가 여쭈어도 대답을 안 하셨고, 소식을 물어볼 만한 제삼자도 없었으니까.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["he is not very ill, or he would not think of Spain.”", "아주 편찮으신 건 아닐 거야. 그렇다면 스페인 생각을 하실 리가 없지.”"]], [["“He never mentions Spain.”", "“스페인 말씀은 한 번도 없으셨어.”"]], [["“No!", "“아니야!"], ["but his plan that is to be proposed evidently relates to that.", "하지만 제안하시겠다는 그 계획이란 게 분명 그 일과 관계있는 거지."], ["But would you really go in such weather as this?”", "그런데 이런 날씨에 정말로 갈 거야?”"]], [["“Oh, it will get cooler every day.", "“아, 날마다 시원해질 거야."], ["Yes!", "그럼!"], ["Think of it.", "생각해 봐. 다만 내가 너무 많이 생각하고 너무 많이 바란 게 아닌가 두려울 뿐이야."], ["I am only afraid I have thought and wished too much—in that absorbing wilful way which is sure to be disappointed—or else gratified, to the letter, while in the spirit it gives no pleasure.”", "꼭 실망하고 마는 그 사로잡힌 듯한 고집스러운 방식으로 말이야. 아니면 글자 그대로는 이루어지되 정작 마음에는 아무 기쁨도 주지 않는 식이 되든지.”"]], [["“But that’s superstitious, I’m sure, Margaret.”", "“하지만 그건 미신이야, 마거릿, 틀림없이.”"]], [["“No, I don’t think it is.", "“아니야, 그렇게 생각하지 않아."], ["Only it ought to warn me, and check me from giving way to such passionate wishes.", "다만 그것이 나를 일깨워서, 그런 격렬한 소망에 나를 내맡기지 않도록 막아 주어야 해."], ["It is a sort of ‘Give me children, or else I die.’ I’m afraid my cry is, ‘Let me go to Cadiz, or else I die.’”", "‘내게 자식을 주소서, 그렇지 않으면 내가 죽겠나이다’ 하는 것과 같은 거지. 내 외침은 ‘카디스로 가게 해 주소서, 그렇지 않으면 내가 죽겠나이다’인 것 같아 두려워.”"]], [["“My dear Margaret!", "“마거릿, 얘!"], ["You’ll be persuaded to stay there; and then what shall I do?", "넌 거기 눌러앉으라고 설득당하고 말 거야. 그럼 난 어쩌라고?"], ["Oh! I wish I could find somebody for you to marry here, that I could be sure of you!”", "아, 여기서 네가 결혼할 사람을 하나 찾아낼 수만 있다면, 그래서 너를 붙잡아 둘 수만 있다면 좋겠어!”"]], [["“I shall never marry.”", "“나는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거야.”"]], [["“Nonsense, and double nonsense!", "“말도 안 돼, 두 번 말도 안 돼!"], ["Why, as Sholto says, you’re such an attraction to the house, that he knows ever so many men who will be glad to visit here next year for your sake.”", "아니, 숄토 말로는 네가 이 집에 어찌나 큰 매력이 되는지, 내년에 너 때문에 기꺼이 찾아올 남자들을 여럿 알고 있다던걸.”"]], [["Margaret drew herself up haughtily.", "마거릿이 도도하게 몸을 꼿꼿이 세웠다."], ["“Do you know, Edith, I sometimes think your Corfu life has taught you——”", "“있잖아, 이디스, 나는 가끔 네 코르푸 생활이 너에게 가르쳐 준 게——”"]], [["“Well!”", "“뭔데!”"]], [["“Just a shade or two of coarseness.”", "“한두 겹쯤의 상스러움이라는 생각이 들어.”"]], [["Edith began to sob so bitterly, and to declare so vehemently that Margaret had lost all love for her, and no longer looked upon her as a friend, that Margaret came to think that she had expressed too harsh an opinion for the relief of her own wounded pride, and ended by being Edith’s slave for the rest of the day; while that little lady, overcome by wounded feeling, lay like a victim on the sofa, heaving occasionally a profound sigh, till at last she fell asleep.", "이디스가 어찌나 서럽게 흐느끼기 시작하고, 마거릿이 자기에 대한 사랑을 다 잃었으며 더는 자기를 벗으로 여기지 않는다고 어찌나 격하게 우겨 대던지, 마거릿은 상처받은 제 자존심을 달래려고 너무 모진 말을 했다고 여기게 되었고, 결국 그날 남은 내내 이디스의 종노릇을 하고 말았다. 그러는 동안 그 자그마한 부인은 상한 감정에 짓눌려 제물처럼 소파에 드러누운 채 이따금 깊은 한숨을 내쉬다가 마침내 잠이 들었다."]], [["Mr.", "벨 씨는 두 번째로 방문을 미룬 그 날짜에도 모습을 나타내지 않았다."], ["Bell did not make his appearance even on the day to which he had for a second time deferred his visit.", "이튿날 아침 그의 하인 월리스에게서 편지가 왔다."], ["The next morning there came a letter from Wallis, his servant, stating that his master had not been feeling well for some time, which had been the true reason of his putting off his journey; and that at the very time when he should have set out for London, he had been seized with an apoplectic fit; it was, indeed, Wallis added, the opinion of the medical men—that he could not survive the night; and more than probable, that by the time Miss Hale received this letter his poor master would be no more.", "주인어른이 얼마 전부터 몸이 편치 않으셨고 그것이 여행을 미룬 진짜 까닭이었다는 것, 그리고 런던으로 떠나야 할 바로 그때에 졸중 발작을 일으키셨다는 것이었다. 월리스는 덧붙이기를, 의사들의 소견으로는 그분이 이 밤을 넘기지 못하시리라 하며, 헤일 양이 이 편지를 받을 무렵이면 가엾은 주인어른은 이미 이 세상 사람이 아닐 공산이 크다고 했다."]], [["Margaret received this letter at breakfast-time, and turned very pale as she read it; then, silently putting into Edith’s hands, she left the room.", "마거릿은 아침 식사 시간에 이 편지를 받았고, 읽으면서 얼굴이 몹시 창백해졌다. 그러고는 말없이 이디스의 손에 편지를 쥐여 주고 방을 나갔다."]], [["Edith was terribly shocked as she read it, and cried in a sobbing, frightened, childish way, much to her husband’s distress.", "이디스는 편지를 읽고 몹시 충격을 받아 흐느끼며 겁먹은 아이처럼 울었고, 남편은 크게 난처해졌다."], ["Mrs. Shaw was breakfasting in her own room, and upon him devolved the task of reconciling his wife to the near contact into which she seemed to be brought with death, for the first time that she could remember in her life.", "쇼 부인은 자기 방에서 아침을 들고 있었으므로, 아내가 평생 기억하기로는 처음으로 죽음과 이토록 가까이 맞닥뜨리게 된 것을 받아들이도록 다독이는 일이 그의 몫으로 떨어졌다."], ["Here was a man who was to have dined with them to-day lying dead or dying instead!", "오늘 자기들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 했던 사람이 그 대신 죽어 가거나 이미 죽어 누워 있다니!"], ["It was some time before she could think of Margaret.", "그녀가 마거릿을 떠올리기까지는 얼마쯤 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Then she started up, and followed her upstairs into her room.", "이윽고 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 위층 마거릿의 방으로 뒤따라 올라갔다."], ["Dixon was packing up a few toilette articles, and Margaret was hastily putting on her bonnet, shedding tears all the time, and her hands trembling so that she could hardly tie the strings.", "딕슨은 세면도구 몇 가지를 챙기고 있었고, 마거릿은 줄곧 눈물을 흘리며 서둘러 보닛을 쓰고 있었는데, 손이 어찌나 떨리는지 끈을 제대로 매지도 못했다."]], [["“Oh, dear Margaret!", "“아, 마거릿!"], ["how shocking! What are you doing?", "이 무슨 끔찍한 일이니!"], ["Are you going out?", "뭘 하는 거야? 나가려고?"], ["Sholto would telegraph or do anything you like.”", "숄토가 전보를 치든 뭐든 네가 원하는 대로 해 줄 텐데.”"]], [["“I am going to Oxford.", "“옥스퍼드에 갈 거야."], ["There is a train in half-an-hour.", "30분 뒤에 기차가 있어."], ["Dixon has offered to go with me, but I could have gone by myself.", "딕슨이 같이 가 주겠다고 했지만 나 혼자서도 갈 수 있었어."], ["I must see him again.", "그분을 다시 뵈어야 해."], ["Besides, he may be better, and want some care.", "게다가 차도가 있으셔서 누가 돌봐 드려야 할지도 모르잖아."], ["He has been like a father to me.", "그분은 내게 아버지 같은 분이셨어."], ["Don’t stop me, Edith.”", "붙잡지 마, 이디스.”"]], [["“But I must.", "“하지만 붙잡아야겠어."], ["Mamma won’t like it at all.", "어머니가 도무지 좋아하지 않으실 거야."], ["Come and ask her about it, Margaret.", "가서 여쭈어보자, 마거릿."], ["You don’t know where you’re going.", "넌 네가 어디로 가는지도 모르잖아."], ["I should not mind if he had a house of his own; but in his Fellow’s rooms!", "그분이 당신 집을 갖고 계시다면 나도 개의치 않겠지만, 펠로 숙소라니!"], ["Come to mamma, and do ask her before you go.", "어머니께 가서 떠나기 전에 꼭 여쭈어봐."], ["It will not take a minute.”", "잠깐이면 될 거야.”"]], [["Margaret yielded, and lost her train.", "마거릿은 물러섰고, 기차를 놓쳤다."], ["In the suddenness of the event, Mrs. Shaw became bewildered and hysterical, and so the precious time slipped by.", "갑작스러운 일에 쇼 부인은 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 신경질을 부렸고, 그렇게 귀한 시간이 흘러가 버렸다."], ["But there was another train in a couple of hours; and after various discussions on propriety and impropriety, it was decided that Captain Lennox should accompany Margaret, as the one thing to which she was constant was her resolution to go, alone or otherwise, by the next train, whatever might be said of the propriety or impropriety of the step.", "그러나 두 시간 뒤에 또 기차가 있었다. 그리고 온당하다느니 온당하지 않다느니 하는 갖가지 논의 끝에, 레녹스 대위가 마거릿과 동행하기로 결정되었다."], ["Her father’s friend, her own friend, was lying at the point of death; and the thought of this came upon her with such vividness, that she was surprised herself at the firmness with which she asserted something of her right to independence of action; and five minutes before the time of starting, she found herself sitting in a railway carriage opposite to Captain Lennox.", "그녀가 한결같이 붙들고 있는 단 하나는 그 처신이 온당하다는 말을 듣든 온당하지 않다는 말을 듣든 간에 혼자서든 누구와 함께든 다음 기차로 가겠다는 결심이었기 때문이다. 아버지의 벗이자 그녀 자신의 벗이 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있었다."], ["It was always a comfort to her to think that she had gone, though it was only to hear that he had died in the night.", "그 생각이 어찌나 생생하게 밀려들었던지, 그녀는 행동의 독립에 대한 제 권리를 얼마간 주장하는 자신의 단호함에 스스로도 놀랐다. 그리고 출발 5분 전, 그녀는 기차간에서 레녹스 대위와 마주 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["She saw the rooms that he had occupied, and associated them ever after most fondly in her memory with the idea of her father, and his one cherished and faithful friend.", "비록 그분이 간밤에 돌아가셨다는 말을 듣기 위해 간 것에 지나지 않았지만, 자기가 갔었다는 사실은 언제나 그녀에게 위안이 되었다. 그녀는 그가 지내던 방을 보았고, 그 뒤로 언제까지나 그 방을 아버지에 대한 생각과, 아버지의 하나뿐이던 소중하고 신실한 벗에 대한 생각과 더불어 더없이 애틋하게 기억 속에 간직했다."]], [["They had promised Edith before starting, that if all had ended as they feared, they would return to dinner; so that long, lingering look around the room in which her father had died, had to be interrupted, and a quiet farewell taken of the kind old face that had so often come out with pleasant words, and merry quips and cranks.", "두 사람은 떠나기 전에 이디스에게 약속했었다. 만일 모든 일이 두려워하던 대로 끝나 있다면 저녁 식사 시간에 맞추어 돌아오겠다고. 그래서 아버지가 숨을 거둔 그 방을 길게 미련 두어 둘러보던 눈길도 중간에 거두어야 했고, 그토록 자주 다정한 말과 유쾌한 농지거리와 익살을 건네던 그 정겨운 노인의 얼굴에 조용히 작별을 고해야 했다."]], [["Captain Lennox fell asleep on their journey home; and Margaret could cry at leisure, and bethink her of this fatal year, and all the woes it had brought to her.", "레녹스 대위는 돌아오는 길에 잠이 들었다. 그래서 마거릿은 마음껏 울 수 있었고, 이 모진 한 해와 그것이 자기에게 안겨 준 온갖 슬픔을 곱씹을 수 있었다."], ["No sooner was she fully aware of one loss than another came—not to supersede her grief for the one before, but to re-open wounds and feelings scarcely healed.", "한 가지 상실을 온전히 깨닫기가 무섭게 또 다른 상실이 찾아왔다. 앞선 슬픔을 밀어내기 위해서가 아니라, 아물지도 못한 상처와 감정을 다시 헤집기 위해서였다."], ["But at the sound of the tender voices of her aunt and Edith, of merry little Sholto’s glee at her arrival, and at the sight of the well-lighted rooms, with their mistress, pretty in her paleness and her eager sorrowful interest, Margaret roused herself from her heavy trance of almost superstitious hopelessness, and began to feel that even around her joy and gladness might gather.", "그러나 이모와 이디스의 다정한 목소리를 듣고, 그녀가 온 것을 반기는 어린 숄토의 환호를 듣고, 환하게 불 밝힌 방과 창백한 낯빛으로 오히려 어여쁜, 애틋하고 슬픈 관심을 담은 그 안주인을 보자, 마거릿은 미신에 가까운 절망의 무거운 혼미에서 몸을 일으켰고, 자기 주위에도 기쁨과 즐거움이 모여들 수 있으리라는 것을 느끼기 시작했다. 그녀는 소파의 이디스 자리를 차지했다."], ["She had Edith’s place on the sofa; Sholto was taught to carry Aunt Margaret’s cup of tea very carefully to her; and by the time she went up to dress, she could thank God for having spared her dear old friend a long or a painful illness.", "숄토는 마거릿 이모의 찻잔을 아주 조심스럽게 날라다 드리도록 배웠다. 그리고 옷을 갈아입으러 올라갈 무렵에는, 소중한 옛 벗에게 길거나 고통스러운 병을 면하게 해 주신 데 대해 하느님께 감사드릴 수 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX", "# 제49장"]], [["“And down the sunny beach she paces slowly, With many doubtful pauses by the way; Grief hath an influence so hushed and holy.”", "“그리고 볕 드는 바닷가를 그녀는 천천히 거닌다, 길 위에서 여러 번 머뭇거려 멈추면서. 슬픔에는 그토록 고요하고 거룩한 힘이 있으니.”"], ["HOOD.", "— 후드."]], [["“Is not Margaret the heiress?”", "“마거릿이 상속녀 아니에요?”"], ["whispered Edith to her husband, as they were in their room alone at night after the sad journey to Oxford.", "옥스퍼드로 다녀온 서글픈 여행이 끝난 그날 밤, 방에 단둘이 남게 되자 이디스가 남편에게 속삭였다. 그녀는 그의 훤칠한 머리를 끌어내리고 발끝으로 선 채, 감히 이 물음을 꺼내기 전에 부디 놀라지 말아 달라고 애원했다."], ["She had pulled his tall head down, and stood upon tiptoe, and implored him not to be shocked, before she ventured to ask this question.", "그러나 레녹스 대위는 도무지 아는 바가 없었다. 들은 적이 있었다 해도 잊어버린 터였다."], ["Captain Lennox was, however, quite in the dark; if he had ever heard, he had forgotten; it could not be much that a Fellow of a small College had to leave; but he had never wanted her to pay for her board; and two hundred and fifty pounds a year was something ridiculous, considering that she did not take wine.", "작은 칼리지의 펠로가 남길 것이 그리 많을 리 없었다. 그러나 그는 마거릿에게 하숙비를 내라고 한 적이 한 번도 없었고, 그녀가 포도주도 마시지 않는 것을 생각하면 1년에 250파운드라는 것은 터무니없는 액수였다."], ["Edith came down upon her feet a little bit sadder; with a romance blown to pieces.", "이디스는 낭만이 산산조각 난 채 조금 더 서글퍼져서 발뒤꿈치를 내렸다."]], [["A week afterwards, she came prancing towards her husband, and made him a low curtsey:", "일주일 뒤 그녀는 깡충거리며 남편에게 다가와 무릎을 깊숙이 굽혀 인사했다."]], [["“I am right, and you are wrong, most noble Captain.", "“제 말이 맞고 당신이 틀렸어요, 고귀하신 대위님."], ["Margaret has had a lawyer’s letter, and she is residuary legatee—the legacies being about two thousand pounds, and the remainder about forty thousand, at the present value of property in Milton.”", "마거릿한테 변호사 편지가 왔는데, 그 애가 잔여재산 수유자래요. 따로 정해진 유증이 2,000파운드쯤 되고, 나머지가 밀턴의 지금 재산 시세로 40,000파운드쯤 된다지 뭐예요.”"]], [["“Indeed!", "“그것참!"], ["and how does she take her good fortune?”", "그래, 마거릿은 그 행운을 어떻게 받아들이던가?”"]], [["“Oh, it seems she knew she was to have it all along; only she had no idea it was so much.", "“아, 진작부터 다 자기 몫이 되리라는 걸 알고 있었나 봐요. 다만 그렇게 많을 줄은 몰랐대요."], ["She looks very white and pale, and says she’s afraid of it; but that’s nonsense, you know, and will soon go off.", "얼굴이 아주 하얗게 질려서는 그게 겁이 난다고 하지 뭐예요. 하지만 그건 말도 안 되는 소리고, 곧 사라질 거예요."], ["I left mamma pouring congratulations down her throat, and stole away to tell you.”", "어머니가 그 애 목구멍에 축하를 퍼붓고 계시길래 저는 살짝 빠져나와 당신한테 알려 주러 왔어요.”"]], [["It seemed to be supposed, by general consent, that the most natural thing was to consider Mr. Lennox henceforward as Margaret’s legal adviser.", "이제부터 레녹스 씨를 마거릿의 법률 고문으로 여기는 것이 가장 자연스러운 일이라고 다들 암묵적으로 동의한 듯했다."], ["She was so entirely ignorant of all forms of business that in nearly everything she had to refer to him.", "그녀는 사무라는 것의 온갖 절차에 워낙 어두워서 거의 모든 일을 그에게 물어보아야 했다."], ["He chose out her attorney; he came to her with papers to be signed.", "그가 그녀의 변호사를 골라 주었고, 서명할 서류를 들고 그녀를 찾아왔다."], ["He was never so happy as when teaching her of what all these mysteries of the law were the signs and types.", "이 온갖 법률의 신비가 무엇을 가리키고 무엇을 뜻하는지 그녀에게 가르쳐 줄 때만큼 그가 행복한 때는 없었다."]], [["“Henry,”", "“헨리 도련님.”"], ["said Edith, one day, archly; “do you know what I hope and expect all these long conversations with Margaret will end in?”", "어느 날 이디스가 짓궂게 말했다. “마거릿과 나누는 그 긴 대화들이 결국 어떻게 끝나기를 제가 바라고 또 기대하는지 아세요?”"]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "“아니요, 모르겠습니다.”"], ["said he, reddening.", "그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“And I desire you not to tell me.”", "“그리고 말씀하지 말아 주시기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["“Oh, very well; then I need not tell Sholto not to ask Mr.", "“아, 그러세요."], ["Montagu so often to the house.”", "그럼 숄토한테 몬터규 씨를 그렇게 자주 집에 부르지 말라고 이를 필요도 없겠네요.”"]], [["“Just as you choose,”", "“좋으실 대로 하십시오.”"], ["said he with forced coolness.", "그가 억지로 냉정한 척하며 말했다."], ["“What you are thinking of may or may not happen; but this time, before I commit myself, I will see my ground clear.", "“형수님이 생각하시는 일은 일어날 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지요. 하지만 이번에는 몸을 내던지기 전에 발밑을 분명히 살펴볼 참입니다."], ["Ask whom you choose.", "누구든 부르고 싶은 대로 부르십시오."], ["It may not be very civil, Edith, but if you meddle in it you will mar it.", "이런 말씀이 그리 예의 바르지는 않겠습니다만, 이디스, 끼어드시면 오히려 망칠 겁니다."], ["She has been very farouche with me for a long time; and is only just beginning to thaw a little from her Zenobia ways.", "그분은 오랫동안 저를 몹시 사납게 대했고, 이제야 겨우 그 제노비아 같은 태도를 조금 누그러뜨리기 시작했으니까요."], ["She has the making of a Cleopatra in her, if only she were a little more pagan.”", "조금만 더 이교도다웠다면 클레오파트라가 되고도 남을 자질이 그분 안에 있습니다.”"]], [["“For my part,”", "“저로 말하자면,”"], ["said Edith, a little maliciously, “I am very glad she is a Christian.", "이디스가 조금 짓궂게 말했다. “그 애가 기독교도라서 정말 다행이에요."], ["I know so very few!”", "제가 아는 기독교도가 워낙 적어서요!”"]], [["There was no Spain for Margaret that autumn; although to the last she hoped that some fortunate occasion would call Frederick to Paris, whither she could easily have met with a convoy.", "그해 가을 마거릿에게 스페인은 없었다. 마지막 순간까지 그녀는 무슨 다행스러운 일이 생겨 프레더릭이 파리로 불려 가기를 바랐다."], ["Instead of Cadiz, she had to content herself with Cromer.", "파리라면 함께 갈 일행을 어렵지 않게 구해 그를 만나러 갈 수 있었을 것이다."], ["To that place her aunt Shaw and the Lennoxes were bound.", "카디스 대신 그녀는 크로머로 만족해야 했다."], ["They had all along wished her to accompany them, and, consequently, with their characters, they made but lazy efforts to forward her own separate wish.", "쇼 이모와 레녹스 부부가 그리로 갈 참이었다. 그들은 처음부터 그녀가 함께 가기를 바랐고, 그런 만큼 그들의 성정대로 그녀 자신의 소망을 이루어 주는 일에는 게으른 노력밖에 기울이지 않았다."], ["Perhaps Cromer was, in one sense of the expression, the best for her.", "어쩌면 어떤 뜻에서는 크로머가 그녀에게 가장 좋았을지도 모른다."], ["She needed bodily strengthening and bracing as well as rest.", "그녀에게는 쉼만이 아니라 몸을 튼튼히 하고 다잡는 일도 필요했으니까."]], [["Among other hopes that had vanished, was the hope, the trust she had had, that Mr. Bell would have given Mr.", "사라져 버린 여러 희망 가운데는 벨 씨가 손턴 씨에게 레너즈의 죽음으로 이어진 그 불행한 사고에 앞서 있었던 집안 사정을 있는 그대로 이야기해 주었으리라는 희망이, 그런 믿음이 있었다."], ["Thornton the simple facts of the family circumstances which had preceded the unfortunate accident that led to Leonards’ death. Whatever opinion—however changed it might be from what Mr.", "손턴 씨가 한때 품었던 것과 아무리 달라졌다 한들 그가 어떤 판단을 내리든, 그녀는 그 판단이 자기가 무엇을 했고 왜 그렇게 했는지에 대한 참된 이해 위에 서기를 바랐다."], ["Thornton had once entertained, she had wished it to be based upon a true understanding of what she had done, and why she had done it.", "그것은 그녀에게 기쁨이 되었을 것이다. 이제 평생 마음이 놓이지 않을 그 한 가지 점에서 그녀에게 쉼을 주었을 것이다."], ["It would have been a pleasure to her: would have given her rest on a point on which she should now all her life be restless, unless she could resolve not to think upon it.", "아예 그 일을 생각하지 않기로 마음먹을 수 있다면 모르지만. 그 일들이 있은 지 워낙 오래되어, 이제는 벨 씨의 죽음으로 잃어버린 그 한 가지 길 말고는 그것을 설명할 방도가 없었다."], ["It was now so long after the time of these occurrences, that there was no possible way of explaining them save the one which she had lost by Mr.", "그녀는 다른 숱한 사람들처럼 그저 오해받는 것을 감수할 수도 있었다."], ["Bell’s death. She might just submit, like many another, to be misunderstood; but, though reasoning herself into the belief that in this hers was no uncommon lot, her heart did not ache the less with longing that some time—years and years hence—before he died at any rate, he might know how much she had been tempted.", "그러나 이런 것이 자기에게만 있는 드문 팔자가 아니라고 스스로를 타이르면서도, 언젠가—몇 해가, 아주 여러 해가 흐른 뒤라도—적어도 그가 죽기 전에는 자기가 얼마나 큰 유혹을 받았는지 그가 알아주었으면 하는 그리움에 그녀의 가슴은 조금도 덜 아프지 않았다."], ["She thought that she did not want to hear that all was explained to him, if only she could be sure that he would know.", "모든 것이 그에게 설명되었다는 말을 듣고 싶은 것은 아니라고, 그가 알게 되기만 한다면 그것으로 되었다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["But this wish was vain, like so many others; and when she had schooled herself into this conviction, she turned with all her heart and strength to the life that lay immediately before her, and resolved to strive and make the best of that.", "그러나 이 바람도 다른 수많은 바람들처럼 헛되었다. 그리하여 그런 확신을 스스로에게 가르쳐 넣고 나자, 그녀는 온 마음과 온 힘을 다해 바로 눈앞에 놓인 삶으로 돌아서서 그 삶을 힘껏 살아 내기로 마음먹었다."]], [["She used to sit long hours upon the beach, gazing intently on the waves as they chafed with perpetual motion against the pebbly shore,—or she looked out upon the more distant heave, and sparkle against the sky, and heard, without being conscious of hearing, the eternal psalm, which went up continually.", "그녀는 바닷가에 오래도록 앉아, 파도가 자갈 깔린 물가에 끊임없이 밀려와 부딪히는 모습을 골똘히 바라보곤 했다. 아니면 더 먼 곳의 너울과 하늘을 배경으로 반짝이는 빛을 내다보며, 쉼 없이 올라가는 그 영원한 시편 소리를 듣는 줄도 모른 채 들었다."], ["She was soothed without knowing how or why.", "어떻게 왜 그런지도 모르는 채 그녀의 마음은 달래졌다."], ["Listlessly she sat there, on the ground, her hands clasped round her knees, while her aunt Shaw did small shoppings, and Edith and Captain Lennox rode far and wide on shore and inland.", "그녀는 두 손으로 무릎을 감싸 안고 땅바닥에 넋 놓고 앉아 있었고, 그동안 쇼 이모는 자잘한 물건을 사러 다녔으며 이디스와 레녹스 대위는 바닷가로 내륙으로 멀리까지 말을 달렸다."], ["The nurses, sauntering on with their charges, would pass and repass her, and wonder in whispers what she could find to look at so long, day after day.", "아이들을 데리고 어슬렁거리던 유모들이 그녀 앞을 오가면서, 날마다 저렇게 오래도록 대체 무엇을 보고 있는 것일까 하고 소곤소곤 궁금해했다."], ["And when the family gathered at dinner-time, Margaret was so silent and absorbed that Edith voted her moped, and hailed a proposal of her husband’s with great satisfaction, that Mr. Henry Lennox should be asked to take Cromer for a week, on his return from Scotland in October.", "그리고 저녁 식사 때 식구들이 모이면 마거릿이 어찌나 말이 없고 골똘한지, 이디스는 그녀가 우울증에 빠졌다고 단정해 버리고는 10월에 스코틀랜드에서 돌아오는 헨리 레녹스 씨에게 크로머에서 일주일을 지내자고 청하자는 남편의 제안을 크게 반겼다."]], [["But all this time for thought enabled Margaret to put events in their right places, as to origin and significance, both as regarded her past life and her future.", "그러나 이 모든 생각의 시간 덕분에 마거릿은 지난 삶에 대해서도 앞날에 대해서도 여러 일들을 그 근원과 뜻에 따라 제자리에 놓을 수 있었다."], ["Those hours by the seaside were not lost, as any one might have seen who had had the perception to read, or the care to understand, the look that Margaret’s face was gradually acquiring.", "바닷가에서의 그 시간들은 헛되지 않았다. 마거릿의 얼굴이 조금씩 얻어 가고 있던 그 표정을 읽어 낼 눈이 있거나 그것을 이해하려 마음 쓰는 사람이라면 누구나 알아보았을 것이다."], ["Mr. Henry Lennox was excessively struck by the change.", "헨리 레녹스 씨는 그 변화에 몹시 놀랐다."]], [["“The sea has done Miss Hale an immense deal of good, I should fancy,”", "“바다가 헤일 양에게 이루 말할 수 없이 좋았던 모양입니다.”"], ["said he, when she first left the room after his arrival in their family circle.", "그가 이 집안 자리에 온 뒤 그녀가 처음으로 방을 나서자 말했다."], ["“She looks ten years younger than she did in Harley Street.”", "“할리 가에 계실 때보다 10년은 젊어 보이시는군요.”"]], [["“That’s the bonnet I got her!”", "“그건 제가 사 준 보닛이에요!”"], ["said Edith, triumphantly.", "이디스가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["“I knew it would suit her the moment I saw it.”", "“보는 순간 그 애한테 어울릴 줄 알았다니까요.”"]], [["“I beg your pardon,” said Mr.", "“죄송합니다만,”"], ["Lennox, in the half-contemptuous, half-indulgent tone he generally used to Edith.", "레녹스 씨가 이디스에게 늘 쓰는 반은 깔보는 듯 반은 너그러운 어조로 말했다. “저도 옷의 매력과 여자의 매력의 차이쯤은 안다고 생각합니다."], ["“But I believe I know the difference between the charms of a dress and the charms of a woman.", "한낱 보닛 따위가 헤일 양의 눈을 저토록 빛나면서도 부드럽게, 입술을 저토록 무르익어 붉게, 얼굴 전체를 저토록 평화와 빛으로 가득하게 만들 수는 없지요. 저분은 닮았습니다, 아니 그 이상입니다.”"], ["No mere bonnet would have made Miss Hale’s eyes so lustrous and yet so soft, or her lips so ripe and red—and her face altogether so full of peace and light.—She is like, and yet more,”—he dropped his voice,—“like the Margaret Hale of Helstone.”", "그가 목소리를 낮추었다. “헬스톤의 마거릿 헤일을 닮았어요.”"]], [["From this time the clever and ambitious man bent all his powers to gaining Margaret.", "이때부터 그 영리하고 야심 있는 사내는 마거릿을 얻는 데 온 힘을 기울였다."], ["He loved her sweet beauty.", "그는 그녀의 다정한 아름다움을 사랑했다."], ["He saw the latent sweep of her mind, which could easily (he thought) be led to embrace all the objects on which he had set his heart.", "그녀의 마음에 잠재된 넓은 폭을 보았고, 자기가 마음에 품은 온갖 목표를 끌어안도록 그것을 쉽게 이끌 수 있으리라고 (그는 그렇게 생각했다) 여겼다."], ["He looked upon her fortune only as a part of the complete and superb character of herself and her position; yet he was fully aware of the rise which it would immediately enable him, the poor barrister, to take.", "그는 그녀의 재산을 그녀 자신과 그 처지가 이루는 완전하고 훌륭한 됨됨이의 한 부분으로만 보았다. 그러면서도 가난한 법정 변호사인 자신을 그 재산이 당장 얼마나 끌어올려 줄지 똑똑히 알고 있었다."], ["Eventually he would earn such success, and such honours, as would enable him to pay her back, with interest, that first advance in wealth which he should owe to her.", "언젠가는 그도 성공과 영예를 거두어, 그녀에게 빚지게 될 그 첫 재산의 도움을 이자까지 붙여 갚을 수 있으리라."], ["He had been to Milton on business connected with her property, on his return from Scotland; and with the quick eye of a skilled lawyer, ready ever to take in and weigh contingencies, he had seen that much additional value was yearly accruing to the lands and tenements which she owned in that prosperous and increasing town.", "그는 스코틀랜드에서 돌아오는 길에 그녀의 재산에 관한 일로 밀턴에 다녀왔다. 그리고 늘 만일의 사정을 헤아리고 가늠할 채비가 되어 있는 숙련된 법률가의 빠른 눈으로, 번창하며 커 가는 그 도시에 그녀가 소유한 토지와 건물의 값이 해마다 크게 오르고 있음을 보았다."], ["He was glad to find that the present relationship between Margaret and himself, of client and legal adviser, was gradually superseding the recollection of that unlucky, mismanaged day at Helstone.", "그는 마거릿과 자신 사이의 지금 이 의뢰인과 법률 고문이라는 관계가 헬스톤에서의 그 운 없고 서투르게 그르친 하루의 기억을 조금씩 밀어내고 있음을 알고 기뻤다."], ["He had thus unusual opportunities of intimate intercourse with her, besides those that arose from the connection between the families.", "그리하여 그는 두 집안의 연고에서 생기는 것 말고도 그녀와 가깝게 오갈 흔치 않은 기회를 얻었다."]], [["Margaret was only too willing to listen as long as he talked of Milton, though he had seen none of the people whom she more especially knew.", "마거릿은 그가 밀턴 이야기를 하는 한 얼마든지 기꺼이 귀를 기울였다. 그가 그녀와 유난히 가까웠던 사람들은 하나도 만나지 않았는데도 그랬다."], ["It had been the tone with her aunt and cousin to speak of Milton with dislike and contempt; just such feelings as Margaret was ashamed to remember she had expressed and felt on first going to live there.", "이모와 사촌은 밀턴을 싫어하고 업신여기며 말하는 것이 버릇이었다. 마거릿이 그곳에 처음 살러 갔을 때 스스로 드러내고 또 느꼈던, 이제는 떠올리기조차 부끄러운 바로 그런 감정이었다."], ["But Mr. Lennox almost exceeded Margaret in his appreciation of the character of Milton and its inhabitants.", "그러나 레녹스 씨는 밀턴과 그곳 사람들의 기질을 높이 사는 데서 마거릿을 거의 앞질렀다."], ["Their energy, their power, their indomitable courage in struggling and fighting; their lurid vividness of existence, captivated and arrested his attention.", "그들의 활력, 그들의 힘, 싸우고 겨루는 데서 보이는 굽힐 줄 모르는 용기, 그 이글거리듯 생생한 삶이 그의 관심을 사로잡고 붙들었다. 그는 그들 이야기를 하는 데 지칠 줄 몰랐다."], ["He was never tired of talking about them; and had never perceived how selfish and material were too many of the ends they proposed to themselves as the result of all their mighty, untiring endeavour, till Margaret, even in the midst of her gratification, had the candour to point this out, as the tainting sin in so much that was noble and to be admired.", "그리고 그 모든 굳세고 지칠 줄 모르는 애씀의 결과로 그들이 스스로 내건 목표 가운데 너무나 많은 것이 얼마나 이기적이고 물질적인지 미처 알아차리지 못했다. 마거릿이 그 이야기에 흐뭇해하는 와중에도 솔직하게, 그토록 고귀하고 우러를 만한 것들을 더럽히는 죄가 바로 그것이라고 짚어 주기 전까지는."], ["Still, when other subjects palled upon her, and she gave but short answers to many questions, Henry Lennox found out that an enquiry as to some Darkshire peculiarity of character called back the light into her eye, the glow into her cheek.", "그래도 다른 이야깃거리가 시들해져 그녀가 여러 물음에 짧은 대답만 내놓을 때면, 다크셔 특유의 기질에 대해 물어보기만 하면 그녀의 눈에 다시 빛이, 뺨에 다시 홍조가 돌아온다는 것을 헨리 레녹스는 알아냈다."]], [["When they returned to town, Margaret fulfilled one of her sea-side resolves, and took her life into her own hands.", "런던으로 돌아왔을 때 마거릿은 바닷가에서 한 결심 가운데 하나를 지켜 자기 삶을 자기 손에 쥐었다."], ["Before they went to Cromer, she had been as docile to her aunt’s laws as if she were still the scared little stranger who cried herself to sleep that first night in the Harley Street nursery.", "크로머에 가기 전까지 그녀는, 할리 가 아이방에서 첫날 밤 울다 잠들었던 그 겁먹은 어린 이방인이 아직도 그대로인 양 이모의 규율에 고분고분했다. 그러나 그 엄숙한 사색의 시간 동안 그녀는 언젠가 자기 자신의 삶에 대해, 그 삶으로 무엇을 했는지에 대해 스스로 답해야 한다는 것을 배웠다."], ["But she had learnt, in those solemn hours of thought, that she herself must one day answer for her own life, and what she had done with it; and she tried to settle that most difficult problem for woman, how much was to be utterly merged in obedience to authority, and how much might be set apart for freedom in working.", "그리하여 여자에게 가장 어려운 저 문제를, 곧 얼마만큼을 권위에 대한 순종에 온전히 맡기고 얼마만큼을 일하는 자유로 떼어 둘 것인가를 정리해 보려 애썼다. 쇼 부인은 더할 나위 없이 성품이 좋았고, 이디스도 이 사랑스러운 집안의 미덕을 물려받았다."], ["Mrs.", "마거릿 자신은 아마 셋 가운데 성미가 가장 나빴을 것이다."], ["Shaw was as good-tempered as could be; and Edith had inherited this charming domestic quality; Margaret herself had probably the worst temper of the three, for her quick perceptions and over-lively imagination made her hasty, and her early isolation from sympathy had made her proud: but she had an indescribable childlike sweetness of heart, which made her manners, even in her rarely wilful moods, irresistible of old; and now, chastened even by what the world called her good fortune, she charmed her reluctant aunt into acquiescence with her will.", "재빠른 지각과 지나치게 활발한 상상력이 그녀를 성급하게 만들었고, 어릴 적부터 공감에서 떨어져 지낸 탓에 자존심이 세졌으니까. 그러나 그녀에게는 뭐라 말할 수 없는 어린아이 같은 마음의 다정함이 있어서, 드물게 고집을 부릴 때조차 예부터 그 태도에는 아무도 거스를 수가 없었다."], ["So Margaret gained the acknowledgment of her right to follow her own ideas of duty.", "그리고 이제 세상이 행운이라 부르는 것으로 오히려 다듬어진 그녀는 내키지 않아 하는 이모를 사로잡아 자기 뜻을 따르게 만들었다. 그리하여 마거릿은 자기가 생각하는 의무를 따를 권리를 인정받았다."]], [["“Only don’t be strong-minded,”", "“그저 드센 여자만은 되지 마.”"], ["pleaded Edith.", "이디스가 사정했다."], ["“Mamma wants you to have a footman of your own; and I’m sure you’re very welcome, for they’re great plagues.", "“어머니는 네가 네 하인을 하나 두기를 바라셔. 나야 얼마든지 좋아."], ["Only to please me, darling, don’t go and have a strong mind; it’s the only thing I ask.", "그것들은 어찌나 골칫거리인지 몰라. 그저 나를 봐서라도, 얘, 드센 마음일랑 갖지 마."], ["Footman or no footman, don’t be strong-minded.”", "내가 부탁하는 건 그것뿐이야. 하인이 있든 없든, 드센 여자는 되지 마.”"]], [["“Don’t be afraid, Edith.", "“걱정 마, 이디스."], ["I’ll faint on your hands at the servants’ dinner-time, the very first opportunity; and then, what with Sholto playing with the fire, and the baby crying, you’ll begin to wish for a strong-minded woman, equal to any emergency.”", "첫 기회가 오는 대로 하인들 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 네 팔에 안겨 쓰러져 줄게. 그러면 숄토는 불장난을 하고 아기는 울어 대는 통에, 너도 어떤 급한 일에도 끄떡없는 드센 여자가 하나 있었으면 하고 바라기 시작할걸.”"]], [["“And you’ll not grow too good to joke and be merry?”", "“그리고 너무 착해져서 농담도 안 하고 즐거워하지도 않게 되진 않을 거지?”"]], [["“Not I.", "“천만에."], ["I shall be merrier than I have ever been, now I have got my own way.”", "이제 내 뜻대로 하게 되었으니 그 어느 때보다 더 즐거워질 거야.”"]], [["“And you’ll not go a figure, but let me buy your dresses for you?”", "“그리고 꼴사납게 하고 다니지 말고, 내가 네 옷을 사 주게 해 줄 거지?”"]], [["“Indeed I mean to buy them for myself.", "“정말이지 내 옷은 내가 사려고 해."], ["You shall come with me if you like; but no one can please me but myself.”", "원하면 같이 가도 좋아. 하지만 나를 만족시킬 수 있는 사람은 나뿐이야.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“어머!"], ["I was afraid you’d dress in brown and dust-colour, not to show the dirt you’ll pick up in all those places.", "나는 네가 온통 갈색이나 흙빛 옷만 입을까 봐 걱정했어. 그런 데를 돌아다니며 묻혀 오는 때가 안 보이도록 말이야."], ["I’m glad you’re going to keep one or two vanities, just by way of specimens of the old Adam.”", "옛 아담의 표본 삼아 허영을 한두 가지 남겨 두겠다니 다행이다.”"]], [["“I am going to be just the same, Edith, if you and my aunt could but fancy so.", "“나는 예전과 똑같이 지낼 거야, 이디스. 너와 이모가 그렇게 생각해 주기만 한다면."], ["Only as I have neither husband nor child to give me natural duties, I must make myself some, in addition to ordering my gowns.”", "다만 나는 자연히 주어지는 의무를 안겨 줄 남편도 자식도 없으니, 옷을 맞추는 일 말고도 무언가 할 일을 스스로 만들어야 해.”"]], [["In the family conclave, which was made up of Edith, her mother, and her husband, it was decided that perhaps all these plans of hers would only secure her the more for Henry Lennox.", "이디스와 그 어머니와 남편으로 이루어진 집안 회의에서는, 어쩌면 그녀의 이 모든 계획들이 오히려 그녀를 헨리 레녹스에게 더욱 단단히 붙들어 매어 줄 뿐이라는 결론이 났다."], ["They kept her out of the way of other friends who might have been eligible sons or brothers; and it was also agreed that she never seemed to take much pleasure in the society of any one but Henry, out of their own family.", "그 계획들은 신랑감이 될 만한 아들이나 형제인 다른 벗들에게서 그녀를 떼어 놓았다. 또한 그녀가 자기네 집안사람 가운데 헨리 말고는 그 누구와 어울리는 데서도 별로 즐거움을 얻는 것 같지 않다는 데도 뜻이 모였다."], ["The other admirers, attracted by her appearance or the reputation of her fortune, were swept away, by her unconscious smiling disdain, into the paths frequented by other beauties less fastidious, or other heiresses with a larger amount of gold.", "그녀의 외모나 재산에 대한 소문에 끌린 다른 구애자들은 그녀의 무심하고 웃는 듯한 경멸에 밀려, 덜 까다로운 다른 미인들이나 금붙이를 더 많이 지닌 다른 상속녀들이 다니는 길로 쓸려 갔다."], ["Henry and she grew slowly into closer intimacy; but neither he nor she were people to brook the slightest notice of their proceedings.", "헨리와 그녀는 천천히 더 가까운 사이로 자라 갔다. 그러나 그도 그녀도 자기들의 일에 남이 조금이라도 참견하는 것을 참아 낼 사람은 아니었다."]], [["# CHAPTER L", "# 제50장"]], [["“Here we go up, up, up; And here we go down, down, downee!”", "“여기서 우리는 올라가네, 올라, 올라가네. 그리고 여기서 우리는 내려가네, 내려, 아래로 내려가네!”"], ["NURSERY SONG.", "— 동요."]], [["Meanwhile at Milton the chimneys smoked, the ceaseless roar and mighty beat and dazzling whirr of machinery struggled and strove perpetually. Senseless and purposeless were wood and iron and steam in their endless labours; but the persistence of their monotonous work was rivalled in tireless endurance by the strong crowds, who, with sense and with purpose, were busy and restless in seeking after—What? In the streets there were few loiterers,—none walking for mere pleasure; every man’s face was set in lines of eagerness or anxiety; news was sought for with fierce avidity; and men jostled each other aside in the Mart and in the Exchange, as they did in life, in the deep selfishness of competition. There was gloom over the town. Few came to buy, and those who did were looked at suspiciously by the sellers; for credit was insecure, and the most stable might have their fortunes affected by the sweep in the great neighbouring port among the shipping houses. Hitherto there had been no failures in Milton; but from the immense speculations that had come to light in making a bad end in America, and yet nearer home, it was known that some Milton houses of business must suffer so severely that every day men’s faces asked, if their tongues did not, “What news? Who is gone? How will it affect me?” And if two or three spoke together, they dwelt rather on the names of those who were safe than dared to hint at those likely, in their opinion, to go: for idle breath may, at such times, cause the downfall of some who might otherwise weather the storm; and one going down drags many after. “Thornton is safe,” say they. “His business is large—extending every year; but such a head as he has, and so prudent with all his daring!” Then one man draws another aside, and walks a little apart, and with head inclined into his neighbour’s ear, he says, “Thornton’s business is large; but he has spent his profits in extending it; he has no capital laid by; his machinery is new within these two years, and has cost him—we won’t say what!—a word to the wise!” But that Mr. Harrison was a croaker,—a man who had succeeded to his father’s trade-made fortune, which he had feared to lose by altering his mode of business to any having a larger scope; yet he grudged every penny made by others more daring and far-sighted.", "그러는 동안 밀턴에서는 굴뚝이 연기를 뿜었고, 기계의 그침 없는 굉음과 육중한 박동과 눈부신 회전이 쉼 없이 다투고 겨루었다. 나무와 쇠와 증기는 그 끝없는 노역 속에서 아무 감각도 목적도 없었다. 그러나 그 단조로운 일이 지닌 끈질김에 못지않게 지칠 줄 모르는 인내를 보인 것은, 감각과 목적을 지닌 채 무엇인가를 좇아 바쁘고 쉴 새 없이 움직이던 억센 무리였다. 무엇을 좇아서였을까? 거리에는 어슬렁거리는 이가 드물었고, 그저 즐거움을 위해 걷는 이는 하나도 없었다. 사람마다 얼굴에 열의나 근심의 선이 굳어 있었다. 소식은 사나운 탐욕으로 구해졌고, 사람들은 삶에서 그러하듯 경쟁의 깊은 이기심 속에서 시장에서도 거래소에서도 서로를 밀쳐 냈다. 도시 위에 침울함이 드리워 있었다. 사러 오는 이가 드물었고, 오는 이들조차 파는 이들에게 의심스러운 눈길을 받았다. 신용이 불안했고, 가장 탄탄한 이라도 이웃한 큰 항구의 선박 상회들을 휩쓴 몰락에 재산이 흔들릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 지금까지 밀턴에는 파산이 없었다. 그러나 미국에서, 또 그보다 더 가까운 곳에서 나쁘게 끝나며 드러난 엄청난 투기들 탓에 밀턴의 몇몇 상회가 몹시 심하게 타격을 입으리라는 것이 알려져 있었다. 그리하여 날마다 사람들의 얼굴은, 혀가 묻지 않더라도 이렇게 묻고 있었다. “무슨 소식인가? 누가 무너졌나? 그것이 나에게 어떤 영향을 미칠까?” 그리고 두셋이 모여 이야기할 때면, 그들은 자기들 생각에 무너질 성싶은 이들의 이름을 감히 입에 올리기보다 무사한 이들의 이름을 되뇌었다. 그런 때에는 부질없는 말 한마디가, 그러지만 않았다면 폭풍을 견뎌 냈을 이들을 무너뜨릴 수도 있고, 하나가 무너지면 여럿이 뒤따라 끌려 들어가기 때문이다. “손턴은 안전하지.” 그들은 말했다. “그 사람 사업은 크고 해마다 커지고 있어. 하지만 그만한 머리를 지녔고, 그 대담함에도 그토록 신중하지 않은가!” 그러면 한 사람이 다른 사람을 한쪽으로 데려가 조금 떨어져 걸으며, 이웃의 귀에 고개를 기울이고는 이렇게 말한다. “손턴의 사업은 크지. 하지만 그는 이익을 사업을 키우는 데 다 써 버렸어. 쟁여 둔 자본이 없다고. 기계는 이 2년 안에 새로 들인 것인데, 그 값이 얼마였는지는 말하지 않겠네! 아는 사람에게는 한마디면 족하지!” 그러나 그렇게 말한 해리슨 씨는 투덜이였다. 아버지가 장사로 이룬 재산을 물려받고서, 사업 방식을 조금이라도 넓은 쪽으로 바꾸었다가 그것을 잃을까 두려워한 사람이었다. 그러면서도 더 대담하고 멀리 보는 이들이 벌어들이는 한 푼 한 푼을 아까워했다."]], [["But the truth was, Mr.", "그러나 사실인즉 손턴 씨는 몹시 쪼들리고 있었다."], ["Thornton was hard pressed.", "그는 그것을 자신의 약한 곳에서 뼈아프게 느꼈다."], ["He felt it acutely in his vulnerable point—his pride in the commercial character which he had established for himself.", "스스로 세워 놓은 상인으로서의 평판에 대한 자부심이 바로 그것이었다. 제 운명의 설계자였던 그는 그것을 자신의 특별한 공로나 자질 덕분으로 돌리지 않았다."], ["Architect of his own fortunes, he attributed this to no special merit or qualities of his own, but to the power, which he believed that commerce gave to every brave, honest, and persevering man, to raise himself to a level from which he might see and read the great game of worldly success, and honestly, by such far-sightedness, command more power and influence than in any other mode of life.", "그는 상업이 용감하고 정직하고 끈기 있는 모든 사람에게 힘을 준다고 믿었다. 세속적 성공이라는 큰 판을 내려다보고 읽어 낼 수 있는 자리까지 스스로를 끌어올리고, 그렇게 멀리 봄으로써 정직하게, 다른 어떤 삶의 방식에서보다 더 큰 힘과 영향력을 부릴 수 있게 해 주는 힘이었다."], ["Far away, in the East and in the West, where his person would never be known, his name was to be regarded, and his wishes to be fulfilled, and his word pass like gold.", "멀리 동방에서도 서방에서도, 제 사람됨은 결코 알려지지 않을 곳에서 제 이름이 존중받고, 제 뜻이 이루어지고, 제 말이 금처럼 통하게 되는 것. 그것이 손턴 씨가 출발점으로 삼았던 상인의 삶이라는 관념이었다."], ["That was the idea of merchant-life with which Mr.", "“그 상인들은 왕후와 같으니.” 어머니가 그 구절을 소리 내어 읽으며 말하곤 했다."], ["Thornton had started. “Her merchants be like princes,”", "마치 그것이 제 아들을 싸움터로 부르는 나팔 소리이기라도 한 듯이."], ["said his mother, reading the text aloud, as if it were a trumpet-call to invite her boy to the struggle.", "그도 다른 많은 이들과 다르지 않았다. 남자든 여자든 아이든, 먼 것에는 깨어 있고 가까운 것에는 죽어 있는 사람들과 다르지 않았다."], ["He was but like many others—men, women, and children—alive to distant, and dead to near things.", "그는 낯선 나라와 아득한 바다에서 통하는 이름의 영향력을 지니기를, 대대로 알려질 상회의 우두머리가 되기를 구했다. 그리고 지금, 오늘, 여기 제 고장에서, 제 공장에서, 제 사람들 사이에서 자신이 어떤 존재가 될 수 있는지를 어렴풋이나마 알아차리기까지 그에게는 오랜 침묵의 세월이 걸렸다."], ["He sought to possess the influence of a name in foreign countries and far-away seas,—to become the head of a firm that should be known for generations; and it had taken him long silent years to come even to a glimmering of what he might be now, to-day, here in his own town, his own factory, among his own people.", "그와 그들은 나란히 뻗은 삶을 살아왔다. 아주 가까웠으나 결코 맞닿지 않는 삶이었다."], ["He and they had led parallel lives—very close, but never touching—till the accident (or so it seemed) of his acquaintance with Higgins.", "히긴스와 알게 된 그 우연한 일이 있기까지는, 적어도 우연으로 보였던 그 일이 있기까지는 그러했다. 한 번 얼굴을 마주하고 사람 대 사람으로 제 둘레의 대중 가운데 한 개인과 마주 선 뒤로, 그리고 눈여겨보라, 처음부터 공장주와 직공이라는 자격을 벗어나 마주 선 뒤로, 두 사람은 저마다 “우리 모두에게는 하나의 사람 마음이 있다”는 것을 알아보기 시작했다."], ["Once brought face to face, man to man, with an individual of the masses around him, and (take notice) out of the character of master and workman, in the first instance, they had each begun to recognise that “we have all of us one human heart.”", "그것이 쐐기의 뾰족한 끝이었다. 그리고 지금에 이르러서야 그는 비로소 깨달았다."], ["It was the fine point of the wedge; and until now, when the apprehension of losing his connection with two or three of the workmen whom he had so lately begun to know as men,—of having a plan or two, which were experiments lying very close to his heart, roughly nipped off without trial,—gave a new poignancy to the subtle fear that came over him from time to time; until now, he had never recognised how much and how deep was the interest he had grown of late to feel in his position of manufacturer, simply because it led him into such close contact, and gave him the opportunity of so much power, among a race of people strange, shrewd, ignorant; but, above all, full of character and strong human feeling.", "사람으로서 이제 막 알게 된 두어 명의 직공과 맺은 인연을 잃을지도 모른다는 걱정, 그의 마음에 아주 가까이 놓인 실험과도 같은 계획 한두 가지가 시험도 해 보지 못한 채 거칠게 잘려 나가리라는 걱정이, 이따금 그를 덮치던 그 미묘한 두려움에 새로운 아픔을 더했을 때에야 비로소, 자신이 공장주라는 자리에 대해 요사이 얼마나 크고 깊은 관심을 품게 되었는지를 깨달았던 것이다. 그저 그 자리가 자신을 그토록 가까운 접촉으로 이끌어 주고, 낯설고 약삭빠르고 무지하지만 무엇보다 개성과 강한 인간적 감정으로 가득한 한 무리의 사람들 사이에서 그토록 큰 힘을 쓸 기회를 주기 때문이었다."]], [["He reviewed his position as a Milton manufacturer.", "그는 밀턴 공장주로서 자신의 처지를 되짚어 보았다. 1년 반 전의 그 파업은, 아니 그보다 더 오래되었다."], ["The strike a year and a half ago,—or more, for it was now untimely wintry weather, in a late spring,—that strike, when he was young, and he now was old—had prevented his completing some of the large orders he had then on hand.", "지금은 늦봄인데도 때 아닌 겨울 날씨였으니 말이다. 그 파업이 있을 때 그는 젊었고, 지금 그는 늙어 있었다."], ["He had locked up a good deal of his capital in new and expensive machinery, and he had also bought cotton largely, for the fulfilment of these orders, taken under contract.", "그 파업 탓에 그는 그때 손에 쥐고 있던 큰 주문 몇 건을 끝내지 못했다. 그는 새롭고 값비싼 기계에 자본을 꽤 많이 묶어 두었고, 계약으로 받아 둔 그 주문들을 채우려고 면화도 많이 사들였던 터였다."], ["That he had not been able to complete them, was owing in some degree to the utter want of skill on the part of the Irish hands whom he had imported; much of their work was damaged and unfit to be sent forth by a house which prided itself on turning out nothing but first-rate articles.", "그것들을 끝내지 못한 데에는 그가 들여온 아일랜드 직공들의 솜씨가 형편없이 모자랐던 탓도 얼마쯤 있었다. 그들이 한 일은 상당 부분 망가져 있어서, 일류 물건만 내놓는다고 자부하는 상회가 내보낼 만한 것이 못 되었다."], ["For many months, the embarrassment caused by the strike had been an obstacle in Mr.", "여러 달 동안 파업이 불러온 곤경은 손턴 씨의 앞길을 가로막는 장애였다."], ["Thornton’s way; and often, when his eye fell on Higgins, he could have spoken angrily to him without any present cause, just from feeling how serious was the injury that had arisen from this affair in which he was implicated.", "그리고 히긴스에게 눈길이 가 닿을 때면 그는 당장은 아무 까닭이 없는데도 그에게 성난 말을 던질 뻔한 적이 여러 번이었다. 히긴스가 얽혀 있던 그 일에서 비롯된 손해가 얼마나 심각한지를 절감했기 때문이다."], ["But when he became conscious of this sudden, quick resentment, he resolved to curb it.", "그러나 이 갑작스럽고 재빠른 분함을 스스로 알아차렸을 때 그는 그것을 억누르기로 마음먹었다."], ["It would not satisfy him to avoid Higgins; he must convince himself that he was master over his own anger, by being particularly careful to allow Higgins access to him, whenever the strict rules of business, or Mr.", "히긴스를 피하는 것으로는 만족할 수 없었다. 그는 사업의 엄격한 규칙이나 자신의 여가가 허락하는 한 언제든 히긴스가 자신에게 다가올 수 있도록 각별히 마음을 씀으로써, 자신이 제 분노의 주인임을 스스로에게 납득시켜야 했다."], ["Thornton’s leisure permitted.", "그러다 이윽고 그는 분함을 다 잊고 놀라움에 빠져들었다."], ["And by-and-bye, he lost all sense of resentment in wonder how it was, or could be, that two men like himself and Higgins, living by the same trade, working in their different ways at the same object, could look upon each other’s position and duties in so strangely different a way.", "자기와 히긴스처럼 같은 업으로 먹고살고 저마다 다른 방식으로 같은 목적을 향해 일하는 두 사람이 어떻게 서로의 처지와 의무를 그토록 이상하리만치 다르게 볼 수 있는가 하는 놀라움이었다. 그리하여 그 왕래가 생겨났다."], ["And thence arose that intercourse, which, though it might not have the effect of preventing all future clash of opinion and action, when the occasion arose, would, at any rate, enable both master and man to look upon each other with far more charity and sympathy, and bear with each other more patiently and kindly. Besides this improvement of feeling, both Mr.", "그 왕래가 훗날 일이 생겼을 때 의견과 행동의 충돌을 모조리 막아 주지는 못하더라도, 적어도 공장주와 직공이 서로를 훨씬 더 너그럽고 따뜻한 눈으로 바라보고, 서로를 더 참을성 있고 친절하게 견뎌 낼 수 있게 해 주기는 할 터였다."], ["Thornton and his workmen found out their ignorance as to positive matters of fact, known heretofore to one side, but not to the other.", "이렇게 감정이 나아진 것 말고도, 손턴 씨와 그의 직공들은 사실에 관한 문제에서 자신들이 얼마나 무지한지를 알아차리게 되었다. 지금까지 한쪽은 알고 다른 쪽은 모르던 사실들이었다."]], [["But now had come one of those periods of bad trade, when the market falling brought down the value of all large stocks; Mr.", "그러나 이제 불황의 시기 가운데 하나가 닥쳐, 시세가 떨어지면서 쌓여 있는 모든 재고의 값이 함께 내려앉았다. 손턴 씨의 것은 거의 절반으로 떨어졌다."], ["Thornton’s fell to nearly half.", "주문은 들어오지 않았다."], ["No orders were coming in; so he lost the interest of the capital he had locked up in machinery; indeed, it was difficult to get payment for the orders completed; yet there was the constant drain of expenses for working the business.", "그래서 그는 기계에 묶어 둔 자본의 이자를 잃었다. 아니, 이미 마친 주문의 대금을 받아 내는 일조차 어려웠다."], ["Then the bills became due for the cotton he had purchased; and money being scarce, he could only borrow at exorbitant interest, and yet he could not realise any of his property.", "그런데도 사업을 돌리는 비용은 끊임없이 새어 나갔다. 그러다 사들인 면화의 어음 만기가 돌아왔다."], ["But he did not despair; he exerted himself day and night to foresee and to provide for all emergencies; he was as calm and gentle to the women in his home as ever; to the workmen in his mill he spoke not many words, but they knew him by this time; and many a curt, decided answer was received by them rather with sympathy for the care they saw pressing upon him, than with the suppressed antagonism which had formerly been smouldering, and ready for hard words and hard judgments on all occasions.", "돈이 귀한 때라 그는 터무니없는 이자로밖에 빌릴 수 없었는데, 그러면서도 제 재산은 무엇 하나 현금으로 바꾸지 못했다. 그러나 그는 절망하지 않았다."], ["“Th’ measter’s a deal to potter him,”", "그는 낮이고 밤이고 애를 써서 온갖 급한 일을 미리 내다보고 대비했다."], ["said Higgins, one day, as he heard Mr.", "집안의 여자들에게는 여느 때와 다름없이 차분하고 부드러웠다."], ["Thornton’s short, sharp inquiry, why such a command had not been obeyed; and caught the sound of the suppressed sigh which he heaved in going past the room where some of the men were working.", "공장의 직공들에게 그는 많은 말을 하지 않았으나, 그들도 이제는 그를 알고 있었다. 그가 내놓는 짧고 단호한 대답을 그들은 예전처럼 속으로 부글거리며 걸핏하면 모진 말과 모진 판단을 쏟아 낼 태세로 받아들이기보다, 그를 짓누르는 근심을 알아보고 안쓰러워하며 받아들이는 일이 많았다."], ["Higgins and another man stopped over-hours that night, unknown to any one, to get the neglected piece of work done; and Mr.", "“나리는 속 썩일 일이 많구먼.” 어느 날 히긴스는 어째서 그런 지시를 따르지 않았느냐고 묻는 손턴 씨의 짧고 날카로운 물음을 듣고, 또 직공 몇이 일하고 있는 방을 지나가며 그가 삼키던 한숨 소리를 듣고는 그렇게 말했다."], ["Thornton never knew but that the overlooker, to whom he had given the command in the first instance, had done it himself.", "그날 밤 히긴스와 또 한 사람은 아무도 모르게 시간을 넘겨 남아, 미뤄져 있던 그 일을 끝내 놓았다. 그리고 손턴 씨는 처음에 그 지시를 내렸던 감독이 손수 한 것이려니 여겼을 뿐 다른 것은 알지 못했다."]], [["“Eh!", "“에!"], ["I reckon I know who’d ha’ been sorry for to see our measter sitting so like a piece o’ grey calico!", "우리 나리가 잿빛 옥양목 쪼가리처럼 저리 앉아 있는 꼴을 봤으면 누가 안쓰러워했을지 나는 알지!"], ["Th’ oud parson would ha’ fretted his woman’s heart out, if he’d seen the woeful looks I have seen on our measter’s face,”", "우리 나리 얼굴에 서린 그 서글픈 낯빛을 그 늙은 목사 양반이 봤더라면 여자 같은 그 속을 다 태웠을 거여.”"], ["thought Higgins, one day, as he was approaching Mr. Thornton in Marlborough Street.", "어느 날 말버러 가에서 손턴 씨에게 다가가며 히긴스는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["“Measter,”", "“나리.”"], ["said he, stopping his employer in his quick resolved walk, and causing that gentleman to look up with a sudden annoyed start, as if his thoughts had been far away.", "그가 말하며, 빠르고 단호한 걸음으로 걷던 고용주를 멈춰 세웠다. 그 신사는 생각이 멀리 가 있기라도 했던 듯 갑자기 언짢게 흠칫하며 고개를 들었다."]], [["“Have yo’ heerd aught of Miss Marget lately?”", "“요새 마거릿 아가씨 소식 들으신 게 있소?”"]], [["“Miss—who?” replied Mr.", "“아가씨라니, 누구 말인가?”"], ["Thornton.", "손턴 씨가 되물었다."]], [["“Miss Marget—Miss Hale—th’ oud parson’s daughter—yo’ known who I mean well enough—if yo’ll only think a bit—”", "“마거릿 아가씨요, 헤일 양 말이오. 그 늙은 목사님 따님 말이오."], ["(there was nothing disrespectful in the tone in which this was said).", "조금만 생각해 보시면 내가 누굴 말하는지 잘 아실 텐데……” (이렇게 말하는 그의 말투에는 무례한 구석이 조금도 없었다.)"]], [["“Oh yes!”", "“아, 그렇지!”"], ["and suddenly the wintry frost-bound look of care had left Mr.", "그러자 대뜸 겨울 서리에 얼어붙은 듯하던 근심의 빛이 손턴 씨의 얼굴에서 사라졌다."], ["Thornton’s face, as if some soft summer gale had blown all anxiety away from his mind; and though his mouth was as much compressed as before, his eyes smiled out benignly on his questioner.", "마치 부드러운 여름 바람이 그의 마음에서 온갖 걱정을 다 날려 보내기라도 한 듯했다. 입은 여전히 아까처럼 굳게 다물려 있었으나, 그의 눈은 묻는 이를 향해 인자하게 웃고 있었다."]], [["“She’s my landlord now, you know, Higgins.", "“그분은 이제 내 집주인일세, 히긴스."], ["I hear of her through her agent here, every now and then.", "이곳에 있는 그분 대리인을 통해 이따금 소식을 듣지."], ["She’s well and among friends—thank you, Higgins.”", "잘 지내고 계시고, 좋은 이들 곁에 계시다네."], ["That “thank you”", "고맙네, 히긴스.”"], ["lingered after the other words, and yet came with so much warmth of feeling, let in a new light to the acute Higgins.", "그 “고맙네”는 다른 말들보다 뒤에 남아 맴돌았고, 그러면서도 어찌나 따뜻한 마음이 담겨 나왔던지, 눈치 빠른 히긴스에게 새로운 빛을 던져 주었다."], ["It might be but a will-o’-th’-wisp, but he thought he would follow it and ascertain whither it would lead him.", "도깨비불에 지나지 않을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그는 그것을 따라가 어디로 데려가는지 알아보리라 마음먹었다."]], [["“And she’s not getten married, measter?”", "“그러면 그 아가씨는 아직 시집을 안 갔소, 나리?”"]], [["“Not yet.”", "“아직은.”"], ["The face was cloudy once more.", "얼굴이 다시 흐려졌다."], ["“There is some talk of it, as I understand, with a connection of the family.”", "“내가 듣기로는 집안과 인연이 닿는 사람과 그런 이야기가 오간다더군.”"]], [["“Then she’ll not be for coming to Milton again, I reckon.”", "“그러면 다시는 밀턴에 오실 일이 없겠구먼요.”"]], [["“No!”", "“없겠지!”"]], [["“Stop a minute, measter.”", "“잠깐만요, 나리.”"], ["Then going up confidentially close, he said, “Is th’ young gentleman cleared?”", "그러고는 은밀하게 바짝 다가서며 그가 말했다. “그 젊은 나리는 혐의를 벗었소?”"], ["He enforced the depth of his intelligence by a wink of the eye, which only made things more mysterious to Mr. Thornton.", "그는 한쪽 눈을 찡긋해 보이며 자기가 아는 바가 얼마나 깊은지를 힘주어 알렸는데, 그 바람에 손턴 씨에게는 일이 더 알쏭달쏭해질 뿐이었다."]], [["“Th’ young gentleman, I mean—Master Frederick, they ca’ed him—her brother as was over here, yo’ known.”", "“그 젊은 나리 말이오. 다들 프레더릭 도련님이라 부르던, 여기 와 있었던 아가씨 오라버니 말이오. 아시잖소.”"]], [["“Over here.”", "“여기 와 있었다고.”"]], [["“Ay, to be sure, at th’ missus’s death. Yo’ need na be feared of my telling; for Mary and me, we knowed it all along, only we held our peace, for we got it through Mary working in th’ house.”", "“그럼요, 그렇고말고요. 마님 돌아가셨을 때 말이오. 내가 떠벌릴까 봐 걱정하실 것 없소. 메리하고 나는 처음부터 다 알고 있었지만 입을 다물었으니까. 메리가 그 댁에서 일한 덕에 알게 된 일이라서요.”"]], [["“And he was over.", "“정말로 와 있었군."], ["It was her brother.”", "그 사람이 그분의 오라버니였고.”"]], [["“Sure enough, and I reckoned yo’ knowed it, or I’d never ha’ let on.", "“암, 그렇지요. 나는 나리도 아시는 줄 알았소."], ["Yo’ knowed she had a brother?”", "안 그랬으면 입 밖에 내지도 않았을 거요. 아가씨한테 오라버니가 있는 건 아셨지요?”"]], [["“Yes, I know all about him.", "“그래, 그 사람에 대해서는 다 알고 있네."], ["And he was over at Mrs. Hale’s death?”", "그런데 그 사람이 헤일 부인이 돌아가실 때 여기 와 있었단 말인가?”"]], [["“Nay!", "“아니오!"], ["I’m not going for to tell more.", "더는 말하지 않으려오."], ["I’ve maybe getten them into mischief already, for they kept it very close.", "그들이 아주 꽁꽁 숨겨 온 일인데 내가 벌써 곤란하게 만들었을지도 모르니까."], ["I nobbut wanted to know if they’d getten him cleared?”", "나는 그저 그 사람 혐의를 벗겨 냈는지 그것만 알고 싶었소.”"]], [["“Not that I know of.", "“내가 아는 바로는 아닐세."], ["I know nothing.", "나는 아무것도 모른다네."], ["I only hear of Miss Hale, now, as my landlord, and through her lawyer.”", "이제는 헤일 양 소식도 내 집주인으로서, 또 그분 변호사를 통해서 들을 뿐이야.”"]], [["He broke off from Higgins, to follow the business on which he had been bent when the latter first accosted him; leaving Higgins baffled in his endeavour.", "그는 히긴스에게서 말을 끊고, 히긴스가 처음 그를 붙들었을 때 향하고 있던 볼일을 보러 갔다. 히긴스는 애쓴 보람도 없이 어리둥절한 채로 남겨졌다."]], [["“It was her brother,”", "“그 사람은 그분의 오라버니였구나.” 손턴 씨가 속으로 말했다."], ["said Mr.", "“다행이다."], ["Thornton to himself. “I am glad.", "나는 그분을 다시는 못 볼지도 모른다."], ["I may never see her again; but it is a comfort—a relief—to know that much.", "그러나 그것만이라도 알게 되어 위안이 된다. 아니, 마음이 놓인다."], ["I knew she could not be unmaidenly; and yet I yearned for conviction.", "그분이 처녀답지 못한 짓을 할 리 없다는 것은 알고 있었다. 그런데도 확신이 그토록 간절했다."], ["Now I am glad!”", "이제 다행이다!”"]], [["It was a little golden thread running through the dark web of his present fortunes; which were growing ever gloomier and more gloomy.", "그것은 갈수록 더 어두워져만 가는 그의 현재 운수라는 검은 거미줄을 가로지르는 조그만 황금 실 한 오라기였다."], ["His agent had largely trusted a house in the American trade, which went down, along with several others, just at this time, like a pack of cards, the fall of one compelling other failures.", "그의 대리인은 미국 무역을 하는 어느 상회에 큰돈을 믿고 맡겨 두었는데, 바로 이 무렵 그 상회가 다른 여러 곳과 함께 카드로 쌓은 집처럼 무너졌다. 하나가 무너지면서 다른 것들의 파산을 불러온 것이었다."], ["What were Mr. Thornton’s engagements?", "손턴 씨가 짊어진 채무는 얼마인가?"], ["Could he stand?", "그는 버텨 낼 수 있을까?"]], [["Night after night he took books and papers into his own private room, and sate up there long after the family were gone to bed.", "밤이면 밤마다 그는 장부와 서류를 제 서재로 가져가 식구들이 다 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 오래도록 앉아 있었다."], ["He thought no one knew of this occupation of the hours he should have spent in sleep.", "잠으로 보내야 할 시간을 그렇게 쓰고 있다는 것을 아무도 모르리라고 그는 생각했다."], ["One morning, when daylight was stealing in through the crevices of his shutters, and he had never been in bed, and, in hopeless indifference of mind, was thinking that he could do without the hour or two of rest, which was all that he should be able to take before the stir of daily labour began again, the door of his room opened, and his mother stood there, dressed as she had been the day before.", "어느 날 아침, 덧문 틈으로 햇빛이 스며들고, 그는 잠자리에 든 적조차 없이 절망적인 무심함에 잠겨, 날마다의 노동이 다시 시작되기 전에 겨우 얻을 수 있는 한두 시간의 휴식쯤은 없어도 되겠다고 생각하고 있을 때였다. 방문이 열리더니 어머니가 어제와 똑같은 옷차림으로 거기 서 있었다."], ["She had never laid herself down to slumber any more than he.", "그녀 역시 그와 마찬가지로 잠들려고 몸을 누인 적이 없었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."], ["Their eyes met. Their faces were cold and rigid, and wan, from long watching.", "오래 깨어 있었던 탓에 두 얼굴은 차갑고 굳어 있었으며 핏기가 없었다."]], [["“Mother!", "“어머니!"], ["why are you not in bed?”", "어째서 주무시지 않으셨습니까?”"]], [["“Son John,”", "“존아.”"], ["said she, “do you think I can sleep with an easy mind, while you keep awake full of care?", "그녀가 말했다. “네가 근심에 싸여 뜬눈으로 지새우는데 내가 마음 편히 잘 수 있겠느냐?"], ["You have not told me what your trouble is; but sore trouble you have had these many days past.”", "무엇이 걱정인지 네가 내게 말해 주지는 않았다만, 요 며칠 네가 지독한 걱정거리를 안고 있다는 것쯤은 안다.”"]], [["“Trade is bad.”", "“장사가 나쁩니다.”"]], [["“And you dread——”", "“그래서 네가 두려워하는 것은……”"]], [["“I dread nothing,”", "“두려워하는 것은 없습니다.”"], ["replied he, drawing up his head, and holding it erect.", "그가 고개를 들어 꼿꼿이 세우며 대답했다."], ["“I know that no man will suffer by me.", "“저 때문에 손해 볼 사람은 아무도 없다는 것을 압니다."], ["That was my anxiety.”", "제 걱정은 그것뿐이었습니다.”"]], [["“But how do you stand?", "“그런데 형편이 어떠냐?"], ["Shall you—will it be a failure?”", "설마…… 파산하게 되는 것이냐?”"], ["her steady voice trembling in an unwonted manner.", "늘 흔들림 없던 그녀의 목소리가 여느 때와 달리 떨렸다."]], [["“Not a failure.", "“파산은 아닙니다."], ["I must give up business, but I pay all men.", "사업은 접어야겠지만 사람들에게는 다 갚아 줍니다."], ["I might redeem myself—I am sorely tempted—”", "저를 다시 일으킬 수도 있습니다. 몹시 마음이 흔들립니다만……”"]], [["“How?", "“어떻게?"], ["Oh, John!", "오, 존!"], ["keep up your name—try all risks for that.", "네 이름은 지켜야 한다. 그것을 위해서라면 어떤 위험이든 무릅써라."], ["How redeem it?”", "어떻게 다시 일으킨다는 말이냐?”"]], [["“By a speculation offered to me, but full of risk; but, if successful, placing me high above water-mark, so that no one need ever know the strait I am in.", "“제게 들어온 투기 이야기가 있습니다. 위험천만하지요."], ["Still, if it fails—”", "그러나 성공하면 저를 물 위 높은 곳에 올려놓아, 제가 처한 궁지를 아무도 알 필요가 없게 될 겁니다. 그래도 실패하면……”"]], [["“And if it fails,”", "“실패하면?”"], ["said she, advancing, and laying her hand on his arm, her eyes full of eager light.", "그녀가 앞으로 나서며 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다. 두 눈에 간절한 빛이 가득했다."], ["She held her breath to hear the end of his speech.", "그녀는 그의 말끝을 들으려고 숨을 죽였다."]], [["“Honest men are ruined by a rogue,”", "“정직한 사람들이 한 사기꾼 때문에 망하는 것이지요.”"], ["said he gloomily.", "그가 침울하게 말했다."], ["“As I stand now, my creditors’ money is safe—every farthing of it; but I don’t know where to find my own—it may be all gone, and I penniless at this moment.", "“지금 제 형편으로는 채권자들의 돈은 한 푼도 빠짐없이 안전합니다. 그러나 제 몫이 어디에 있는지는 모르겠습니다."], ["Therefore, it is my creditors’ money that I should risk.”", "다 사라졌을지도 모르고, 저는 이 순간 무일푼일지도 모릅니다. 그러니 제가 걸어야 할 것은 채권자들의 돈입니다.”"]], [["“But if it succeeded, they need never know.", "“하지만 성공하면 그들은 알 필요도 없지 않느냐."], ["Is it so desperate a speculation?", "그렇게까지 무모한 투기냐?"], ["I am sure it is not, or you would never have thought of it.", "그럴 리 없다. 그랬다면 너는 애초에 생각도 하지 않았을 테지."], ["If it succeeded—”", "성공한다면……”"]], [["“I should be a rich man, and my peace of conscience would be gone!”", "“저는 부자가 되겠지요. 그리고 제 양심의 평화는 사라질 겁니다!”"]], [["“Why!", "“어째서!"], ["You would have injured no one.”", "네가 누구를 해친 것도 아닌데.”"]], [["“No; but I should have run the risk of ruining many for my own paltry aggrandisement.", "“해친 것은 아니지요. 그러나 제 하찮은 영달을 위해 여러 사람을 망하게 할 위험을 무릅쓴 것이 됩니다."], ["Mother, I have decided!", "어머니, 저는 마음을 정했습니다!"], ["You won’t much grieve over our leaving this house, shall you, dear mother?”", "이 집을 떠나는 일로 그리 슬퍼하지는 않으시겠지요, 어머니?”"]], [["“No!", "“그렇다!"], ["but to have you other than what you are will break my heart.", "하지만 네가 지금의 너 아닌 다른 무엇이 되는 것은 내 가슴을 찢어 놓을 게다."], ["What can you do?”", "이제 어찌하려느냐?”"]], [["“Be always the same John Thornton in whatever circumstances; endeavouring to do right, and making great blunders; and then trying to be brave in setting to afresh.", "“어떤 형편에 놓이든 늘 같은 존 손턴으로 있으렵니다. 옳은 일을 하려 애쓰고, 크게 헛디디고, 그러고 나서 다시 시작하는 데 용감해지려 애쓰면서요."], ["But it is hard, mother.", "그러나 힘듭니다, 어머니."], ["I have worked and planned.", "저는 일했고 계획했습니다."], ["I have discovered new powers in my situation too late—and now all is over.", "제 자리에서 새로운 힘을 발견했는데 너무 늦었고, 이제 모든 것이 끝났습니다."], ["I am too old to begin again with the same heart.", "같은 마음으로 다시 시작하기에 저는 너무 늙었습니다."], ["It is hard, mother.”", "힘듭니다, 어머니.”"]], [["He turned away from her, and covered his face with his hands.", "그는 어머니에게서 몸을 돌리고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“I can’t think,”", "“나는 알 수가 없구나.”"], ["said she, with gloomy defiance in her tone, “how it comes about.", "그녀가 침울한 반항이 서린 말투로 말했다. “어쩌다 이렇게 되었는지."], ["Here is my boy—good son, just man, tender heart—and he fails in all he sets his mind upon: he finds a woman to love, and she cares no more for his affection than if he had been a common man; he labours, and his labour comes to nought.", "여기 내 아들이 있다. 착한 아들이요 올바른 사람이요 따뜻한 마음을 지녔건만, 마음먹은 일마다 실패하는구나."], ["Other people grow rich, and hold their paltry names high and dry above shame.”", "사랑할 여자를 만나면 그 여자는 흔해 빠진 사내의 마음이나 다름없이 그 애정을 대수롭지 않게 여기고, 힘써 일하면 그 수고는 헛것이 되고 말지. 다른 사람들은 부자가 되어 그 하찮은 이름을 부끄러움이 닿지 않는 높고 마른 자리에 올려놓는데.”"]], [["“Shame never touched me,”", "“부끄러움은 저를 건드린 적이 없습니다.”"], ["said he, in a low tone: but she went on.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“I sometimes have wondered where justice was gone to, and now I don’t believe there is such a thing in the world—now you are come to this; you, my own John Thornton, though you and I may be beggars together—my own dear son!”", "“나는 이따금 정의라는 것이 대체 어디로 갔는가 싶었다만, 이제는 그런 것이 이 세상에 있다고 믿지 않는다. 네가 이 지경이 되고 보니 말이다. 다른 누구도 아닌 내 아들 존 손턴이, 너와 내가 함께 거지가 될지언정, 내 사랑하는 아들이!”"]], [["She fell upon his neck, and kissed him through her tears.", "그녀는 아들의 목을 끌어안고 눈물 사이로 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Mother!”", "“어머니!”"], ["said he, holding her gently in his arms, “who has sent me my lot in life, both of good and evil?”", "그가 어머니를 두 팔로 부드럽게 안으며 말했다. “좋은 것이든 나쁜 것이든 제 몫의 운명을 저에게 보내신 분이 누구입니까?”"]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 저었다."], ["She would have nothing to do with religion just then.", "그때만큼은 종교와 아무 상관도 맺고 싶지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“Mother,”", "“어머니.”"], ["he went on, seeing that she would not speak.", "어머니가 말하지 않으려는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["“I, too, have been rebellious; but I’m striving to be so no longer.", "“저도 거역해 왔습니다. 그러나 이제 더는 그러지 않으려고 애쓰고 있습니다."], ["Help me, as you helped me when I was a child.", "제가 어릴 적에 도와주셨듯이 저를 도와주십시오."], ["Then you said many good words—when my father died, and we were sometimes sorely short of comforts—which we shall never be now; you said brave, noble, trustful words then, mother, which I have never forgotten, though they may have lain dormant.", "그때 어머니는 좋은 말씀을 많이 해 주셨습니다. 아버지가 돌아가시고 우리에게 필요한 것들이 몹시 모자라던 때에요."], ["Speak to me again in the old way, mother.", "이제 다시는 그렇게 모자라지 않을 테지만요."], ["Do not let us have to think that the world has too much hardened our hearts.", "그때 어머니는 용감하고 고결하고 믿음이 담긴 말씀을 해 주셨고, 저는 그 말씀을 한 번도 잊은 적이 없습니다. 그동안 잠들어 있었을지는 몰라도요."], ["If you would say the old good words, it would make me feel something of the pious simplicity of my childhood.", "다시 옛날처럼 제게 말씀해 주십시오, 어머니. 세상이 우리 마음을 너무 굳게 만들었다는 생각을 하게 두지 마십시오."], ["I say them to myself, but they would come differently from you, remembering all the cares and trials you have had to bear.”", "그 옛날의 좋은 말씀을 해 주신다면 제 어린 시절의 경건한 소박함을 조금이나마 느낄 수 있을 것 같습니다. 저 혼자 되뇌어 보기도 합니다만, 어머니가 견뎌 오신 온갖 근심과 시련을 생각하면 어머니 입에서 나오는 말은 다르게 들릴 것입니다.”"]], [["“I have had a many,”", "“근심이야 많았지.”"], ["said she, sobbing, “but none so sore as this.", "그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다. “하지만 이만큼 쓰라린 것은 없었다."], ["To see you cast down from your rightful place!", "네가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리에서 내던져지는 것을 보다니!"], ["I could say it for myself, John, but not for you.", "나 자신을 두고라면 그 말을 할 수 있겠다만, 너를 두고는 못 하겠구나."], ["Not for you!", "너를 두고는!"], ["God has seen fit to be very hard on you, very.”", "하느님이 너에게는 아주 모질기로 작정하신 게야, 아주.”"]], [["She shook with the sobs that come so convulsively when an old person weeps.", "그녀는 늙은 사람이 울 때면 그렇게도 발작처럼 터져 나오는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었다."], ["The silence around her struck her at last; and she quieted herself to listen.", "마침내 둘레의 고요가 그녀의 마음을 쳤다. 그녀는 귀를 기울이려고 스스로를 가라앉혔다."], ["No sound. She looked.", "아무 소리도 없었다."], ["Her son sate by the table, his arms thrown half across it, his head bent face downwards.", "그녀가 바라보았다. 아들은 탁자 곁에 앉아 두 팔을 탁자 위에 반쯤 걸치고 고개를 숙여 얼굴을 묻고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, John!”", "“오, 존!”"], ["she said, and she lifted his face up.", "그녀가 말하며 아들의 얼굴을 들어 올렸다."], ["Such a strange, pallid look of gloom was on it, that for a moment it struck her that this look was the forerunner of death; but as the rigidity melted out of the countenance and the natural colour returned, and she saw that he was himself once again, all worldly mortification sank to nothing before the consciousness of the great blessing that he himself, by his simple existence was to her.", "그 얼굴에 어찌나 낯설고 창백한 침울함이 서려 있던지, 한순간 그녀는 이 낯빛이 죽음의 전조가 아닌가 하는 생각에 가슴이 철렁했다. 그러나 얼굴에서 굳은 기운이 녹아내리고 본디의 핏기가 돌아와 아들이 다시 제 모습을 되찾은 것을 보자, 세상의 온갖 굴욕은 그가 그저 살아 있다는 것만으로 자신에게 얼마나 큰 축복인가 하는 깨달음 앞에서 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었다."], ["She thanked God for this, and this alone, with a fervour that swept away all rebellious feelings from her mind.", "그녀는 오직 이 한 가지를 두고 하느님께 감사드렸고, 그 뜨거움이 그녀의 마음에서 온갖 거역하는 감정을 쓸어 갔다."]], [["He did not speak readily; but he went and opened the shutters, and let the ruddy light of dawn flood the room.", "그는 선뜻 입을 열지 않았다. 다만 가서 덧문을 열어 붉은 새벽빛이 방 안에 넘쳐흐르게 했다."], ["But the wind was in the east; the weather was piercing cold, as it had been for weeks; there would be no demand for light summer goods this year.", "그러나 바람은 동쪽에서 불어왔다. 몇 주째 그러했듯 날씨는 살을 에듯 추웠다."], ["That hope for the revival of trade must utterly be given up.", "올해에는 가벼운 여름 옷감을 찾는 수요가 없을 터였다. 장사가 되살아나리라는 그 희망은 아주 접어야 했다."]], [["It was a great comfort to have had this conversation with his mother; and to feel sure that, however they might henceforward keep silence on all these anxieties, they yet understood each other’s feelings, and were, if not in harmony, at least in discord with each other, in their way of viewing them.", "어머니와 이런 대화를 나눈 것은 큰 위안이었다. 앞으로 이 모든 근심에 대해 둘이서 아무리 입을 다물게 되더라도 서로의 마음만은 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 그것을 바라보는 방식에서 화음까지는 아닐지언정 적어도 서로 어우러지는 불협화음을 이루고 있다는 것을 확신할 수 있어 큰 위안이었다."], ["Fanny’s husband was vexed at Thornton’s refusal to take any share in the speculation which he had offered to him, and withdrew from any possibility of being supposed able to assist him with the ready money, which indeed the speculator needed for his own venture.", "패니의 남편은 자기가 내놓은 투기에 손턴이 한몫 끼기를 거절한 것에 부아가 났고, 현금으로 그를 도울 수 있으리라고 여겨질 만한 여지에서 아예 물러나 버렸다. 사실 그 투기꾼에게는 제 사업에 쓸 현금이 필요하기도 했다."]], [["There was nothing for it at last, but that which Mr.", "마침내 손턴 씨가 여러 주 동안 두려워해 온 그 일 말고는 달리 길이 없게 되었다."], ["Thornton had dreaded for many weeks; he had to give up the business in which he had been so long engaged with so much honour and success; and look out for a subordinate situation.", "그는 그토록 오랫동안 그토록 큰 명예와 성공을 누리며 해 온 사업을 접고 남의 밑에 들어갈 자리를 알아보아야 했다. 말버러 공장과 거기 딸린 집은 장기 임차로 얻어 둔 것이었으니, 되도록 다시 세를 놓아야 했다."], ["Marlborough Mills and the adjacent dwelling were held under a long lease; they must if possible be relet.", "손턴 씨에게는 곧바로 여러 자리가 제안되어 고를 수 있었다."], ["There was an immediate choice of situations offered to Mr. Thornton.", "햄퍼 씨는 이웃 도시에서 큰 자본으로 개업시키려는 자기 아들에게 든든하고 노련한 동업자로 그를 붙들 수만 있다면 더없이 반가워했을 것이다."], ["Mr. Hamper would have been only too glad to have secured him as a steady and experienced partner for his son, whom he was setting up with a large capital in a neighbouring town; but the young man was half-educated as regarded information, and wholly uneducated as regarded any other responsibility than that of getting money, and brutalised both as to his pleasures and his pains.", "그러나 그 젊은이는 지식이라는 면에서 어중간하게 배웠고, 돈 버는 일 말고 다른 어떤 책임이라는 면에서는 아예 배우지 못했으며, 즐거움에서나 괴로움에서나 거칠고 무디었다."], ["Mr. Thornton declined having any share in a partnership, which would frustrate what few plans he had that survived the wreck of his fortunes.", "손턴 씨는 그 동업에 끼기를 거절했다."], ["He would sooner consent to be only a manager, where he could have a certain degree of power beyond the mere money-getting part, than have to fall in with the tyrannical humours of a moneyed partner with whom he felt sure that he should quarrel in a few months.", "그것은 제 재산의 난파에서 살아남은 몇 안 되는 계획마저 헛되이 만들 터였다. 몇 달 안에 반드시 다투게 되리라 확신하는 돈 가진 동업자의 횡포한 변덕에 맞춰 사는 것보다는, 차라리 그저 지배인에 지나지 않더라도 돈 버는 일 너머로 어느 정도 힘을 쓸 수 있는 자리에 앉는 편이 나았다."]], [["So he waited, and stood on one side with profound humility, as the news swept through the Exchange of the enormous fortune which his brother-in-law had made by his daring speculation.", "그리하여 그는 기다렸고, 처남이 대담한 투기로 어마어마한 재산을 벌었다는 소식이 거래소를 휩쓸고 지나가는 동안 깊은 겸손으로 한쪽에 비켜서 있었다."], ["It was a nine days’ wonder.", "그것은 아흐레짜리 소문거리였다."], ["Success brought with it its worldly consequence of extreme admiration.", "성공에는 극진한 찬탄이라는 세속의 결과가 따라왔다."], ["No one was considered so wise and far-seeing as Mr. Watson.", "왓슨 씨만큼 지혜롭고 멀리 내다보는 사람은 없다고들 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER LI", "# 제51장"]], [["“Bear up, brave heart!", "“견뎌 다오, 용감한 마음이여!"], ["we will be calm and strong; Sure, we can master eyes, or cheek, or tongue, Nor let the smallest tell-tale sign appear She ever was, and is, and will be dear.”", "우리는 침착하고 굳세리라. 정녕 우리는 눈도, 뺨도, 혀도 다스릴 수 있으리니, 그이가 예전에도 소중했고 지금도 소중하며 앞으로도 소중하리라는 것을 일러바치는 아주 작은 기색조차 내비치지 않으리라.”"], ["RHYMING PLAY.", "— 운문극."]], [["It was a hot summer’s evening.", "무더운 여름 저녁이었다. 이디스가 마거릿의 침실로 들어왔다."], ["Edith came into Margaret’s bed-room, the first time in her habit, the second ready dressed for dinner.", "처음에는 승마복 차림이었고, 두 번째에는 만찬 채비를 다 갖춘 차림이었다. 처음에는 아무도 없었다."], ["No one was there at first; the next time Edith found Dixon laying out Margaret’s dress on the bed; but no Margaret.", "두 번째로 왔을 때 이디스는 딕슨이 침대 위에 마거릿의 드레스를 펼쳐 놓고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 마거릿은 없었다."], ["Edith remained to fidget about.", "이디스는 남아서 안절부절못하며 서성거렸다."]], [["“Oh, Dixon!", "“어머, 딕슨!"], ["not those horrid blue flowers to that dead gold-coloured gown. What taste!", "그 칙칙한 금빛 드레스에 저 흉한 파란 꽃은 안 돼요."], ["Wait a minute, and I will bring you some pomegranate blossoms.”", "무슨 취향이 그래요! 잠깐만 기다려요, 내가 석류꽃을 좀 가져올 테니.”"]], [["“It’s not a dead gold-colour ma’am.", "“칙칙한 금빛이 아닙니다요, 마님."], ["It’s a straw-colour.", "밀짚색이지요."], ["And blue always goes with straw-colour.”", "그리고 파랑은 밀짚색과 언제나 어울립니다요.”"], ["But Edith had brought the brilliant scarlet flowers before Dixon had got half through her remonstrance.", "그러나 딕슨이 항변을 절반도 마치기 전에 이디스는 이미 눈부신 진홍빛 꽃을 가져와 있었다."]], [["“Where is Miss Hale?”", "“헤일 양은 어디 있어요?”"], ["asked Edith, as soon as she had tried the effect of the garniture.", "장식이 어떤지 대 보자마자 이디스가 물었다. “도무지 알 수가 없다니까요.”"], ["“I can’t think,”", "그녀는 짜증스레 말을 이었다."], ["she went on, pettishly, “how my aunt allowed her to get into such rambling habits in Milton!", "“이모는 어쩌자고 밀턴에서 그 애가 그렇게 쏘다니는 버릇을 들이도록 내버려 두셨을까!"], ["I’m sure I’m always expecting to hear of her having met with something horrible among all those wretched places she pokes herself into.", "그 애가 기어드는 그 형편없는 곳들에서 무슨 끔찍한 일이라도 당했다는 소식을 듣게 될 것만 같아 늘 조마조마해요."], ["I should never dare to go down some of those streets without a servant.", "나라면 하인도 없이 그런 거리를 지나갈 엄두조차 못 낼 거예요."], ["They’re not fit for ladies.”", "숙녀가 다닐 만한 데가 아니라고요.”"]], [["Dixon was still huffed about her despised taste; so she replied rather shortly:", "딕슨은 무시당한 자기 안목 때문에 아직 심통이 나 있었다. 그래서 다소 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다."]], [["“It’s no wonder to my mind, when I hear ladies talk such a deal about ladies—and when they’re such fearful, delicate, dainty ladies too—I say it’s no wonder to me that there are no longer any saints on earth——”", "“제 생각에는 조금도 이상할 게 없습니다요. 숙녀분들이 숙녀에 대해 그렇게나 말이 많은 걸 듣고 있으면요. 게다가 그렇게나 겁 많고 가녀리고 까다로운 숙녀분들일 때는 더요. 이 땅에 성인이 더는 남아 있지 않은 것도 저한테는 조금도 이상할 게 없다, 이 말씀입니다요——”"]], [["“Oh, Margaret!", "“아, 마거릿!"], ["here you are!", "여기 있었구나!"], ["I have been so wanting you.", "너를 얼마나 찾았다고."], ["But how your cheeks are flushed with the heat, poor child!", "그런데 더위에 뺨이 온통 달아올랐네, 가엾은 것!"], ["But only think what that tiresome Henry has done; really, he exceeds brother-in-law’s limits.", "그건 그렇고 그 성가신 헨리가 무슨 일을 벌였는지 좀 들어 봐. 정말이지 시동생의 도리를 넘어섰다니까."], ["Just when my party was made up so beautifully—fitted in so precisely for Mr. Colthurst—there has Henry come, with an apology it is true, and making use of your name for an excuse, and asked me if he may bring that Mr.", "내 자리가 그렇게나 아름답게 짜였는데, 콜서스트 씨에게 꼭 맞게 딱 들어맞았는데, 헨리가 나타나서는, 사과를 하기는 했지만, 게다가 네 이름까지 핑계 삼아 들먹이면서, 밀턴의 그 손턴 씨라는 사람을 데려와도 되겠냐고 묻지 뭐야."], ["Thornton of Milton—your tenant, you know—who is in London about some law business.", "네 세입자 말이야, 알지. 무슨 법률 일로 런던에 와 있다는구나."], ["It will spoil my number, quite.”", "인원수가 아주 엉망이 되겠어.”"]], [["“I don’t mind dinner.", "“저는 만찬은 괜찮아요."], ["I don’t want any,”", "먹고 싶지도 않고요.”"], ["said Margaret, in a low voice.", "마거릿이 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Dixon can get me a cup of tea here, and I will be in the drawing-room by the time you come up.", "“여기서 딕슨한테 차나 한 잔 얻어 마실게요. 언니가 올라올 때쯤이면 응접실에 나가 있을게요."], ["I shall really be glad to lie down.”", "정말이지 좀 누워 있었으면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“No, no!", "“안 돼, 안 돼!"], ["that will never do.", "그건 절대 안 되지."], ["You do look wretchedly white, to be sure; but that is just the heat, and we can’t do without you possibly.", "하기야 얼굴이 지독하게 창백해 보이기는 해. 하지만 그건 그저 더위 탓이고, 우리는 도저히 너 없이는 안 돼."], ["(Those flowers a little lower, Dixon.", "(그 꽃은 조금 더 아래로, 딕슨."], ["They look glorious flames, Margaret, in your black hair.) You know we planned you to talk about Milton to Mr. Colthurst.", "그 꽃이 네 검은 머리에서 눈부신 불꽃처럼 보이는구나, 마거릿.) 네가 콜서스트 씨에게 밀턴 이야기를 해 주기로 우리가 계획해 둔 거 알잖니."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["to be sure!", "그러고 보니!"], ["and this man comes from Milton.", "이 사람도 밀턴에서 왔네."], ["I believe it will be capital, after all. Mr.", "결국 아주 잘된 일일 것 같아."], ["Colthurst can pump him well on all the subjects in which he is interested, and it will be great fun to trace out your experiences, and this Mr. Thornton’s wisdom, in Mr.", "콜서스트 씨가 관심 있는 온갖 주제에 대해 그 사람한테서 실컷 캐낼 수 있을 테고, 콜서스트 씨가 다음번에 의회에서 하는 연설 속에서 네 경험과 이 손턴 씨의 식견을 짚어 내는 것도 아주 재미있겠지."], ["Colthurst’s next speech in the House.", "정말이지 헨리의 묘수였다는 생각이 들어."], ["Really, I think it is a happy hit of Henry’s.", "부끄러워할 만한 사람이냐고 내가 물었더니 이렇게 대답하더구나."], ["I asked him if he was a man one would be ashamed of; and he replied, ‘Not if you’ve any sense in you, my little sister.’ So I suppose he is able to sound his h’s, which is not a common Darkshire accomplishment—eh, Margaret?”", "‘누나한테 분별이란 게 조금이라도 있다면 그럴 리 없죠.’ 그러니 h 발음쯤은 제대로 낼 줄 아는 사람인가 봐. 다크셔에서는 흔한 재주가 아니라던데, 그렇지, 마거릿?”"]], [["“Mr. Lennox did not say why Mr.", "“레녹스 씨가 손턴 씨는 무슨 일로 상경했는지는 말하지 않던가요?"], ["Thornton was come up to town? Was it law business connected with the property?”", "그 부동산과 관련된 법률 문제였나요?”"], ["asked Margaret, in a constrained voice.", "마거릿이 억눌린 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["he’s failed, or something of the kind, that Henry told you of that day you had such a headache—what was it?", "그 사람 파산했다든가 뭐 그런 거래. 네가 그렇게 머리가 아팠던 날 헨리가 너한테 말했다던 그거 말이야."], ["(There, that’s capital, Dixon.", "뭐였더라? (그래, 아주 훌륭해, 딕슨."], ["Miss Hale does us credit, does she not?) I wish I was as tall as a queen, and as brown as a gipsy, Margaret.”", "헤일 양이 우리 체면을 세워 주겠지, 안 그래?) 나도 너처럼 여왕같이 훤칠하고 집시처럼 볕에 그을렸으면 좋겠구나, 마거릿.”"]], [["“But about Mr. Thornton?”", "“그런데 손턴 씨 이야기는요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I really have such a terrible head for law business.", "나는 정말이지 법률 일에는 머리가 형편없이 안 돌아가서."], ["Henry will like nothing better than to tell you all about it. I know the impression he made upon me was, that Mr.", "헨리가 너한테 전부 이야기해 주는 걸 무엇보다 좋아할걸."], ["Thornton is very badly off, and a very respectable man, and that I’m to be very civil to him: and as I did not know how, I came to you to ask you to help me.", "내가 받은 인상이라면, 손턴 씨가 형편이 아주 나빠졌고, 아주 존경할 만한 사람이며, 내가 그분께 아주 깍듯이 대해야 한다는 것 정도야. 그런데 어떻게 해야 할지 몰라서 너한테 도와 달라고 부탁하러 온 거고."], ["And now come down with me, and rest on the sofa for a quarter of an hour.”", "자, 이제 나랑 같이 내려가서 15분쯤 소파에 누워 쉬자.”"]], [["The privileged brother-in-law came early; and Margaret, reddening as she spoke, began to ask him the questions she wanted to hear answered about Mr.", "특권을 누리는 그 시동생은 일찍 왔다."], ["Thornton.", "마거릿은 말을 꺼내면서 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 손턴 씨에 대해 듣고 싶었던 것들을 그에게 묻기 시작했다."]], [["“He came up about this sub-letting the property—Marlborough Mills, and the house and premises adjoining, I mean.", "“그분은 그 부동산을 전대하는 문제로 올라왔습니다. 말버러 공장과 거기 딸린 집과 부지 말입니다."], ["He is unable to keep it on; and there are deeds and leases to be looked over and agreements to be drawn up.", "계속 유지할 수가 없게 된 거지요. 그래서 살펴봐야 할 증서와 임차 계약이 있고, 작성해야 할 합의서도 있습니다."], ["I hope Edith will receive him properly; but she was rather put out, as I could see, by the liberty I had taken in begging for an invitation for him.", "이디스가 그분을 제대로 맞아 주면 좋겠는데, 제가 주제넘게 초대를 청한 것 때문에 형수가 다소 언짢아하는 눈치더군요."], ["But I thought you would like to have some attention shown him: and one would be particularly scrupulous in paying every respect to a man who is going down in the world.”", "하지만 당신이 그분에게 어느 정도 대접을 해 드리고 싶어 하리라 생각했습니다. 게다가 세상에서 몰락해 가는 사람에게는 유난히 조심스럽게 온갖 예를 다하고 싶어지는 법이지요.”"], ["He had dropped his voice to speak to Margaret, by whom he was sitting; but as he ended he sprang up, and introduced Mr.", "그는 옆자리에 앉은 마거릿에게 말하느라 목소리를 낮추고 있었다."], ["Thornton, who had that moment entered, to Edith and Captain Lennox.", "그러나 말을 마치자마자 벌떡 일어나, 그 순간 막 들어선 손턴 씨를 이디스와 레녹스 대위에게 소개했다."]], [["Margaret looked with an anxious eye at Mr. Thornton while he was thus occupied.", "마거릿은 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 근심 어린 눈으로 손턴 씨를 바라보았다."], ["It was considerably more than a year since she had seen him; and events had occurred to change him much in that time.", "그를 마지막으로 본 지 일 년이 훌쩍 넘었고, 그사이 그를 크게 바꾸어 놓을 만한 일들이 일어났다."], ["His fine figure yet bore him above the common height of men; and gave him a distinguished appearance, from the ease of motion which arose out of it, and was natural to him; but his face looked older and careworn; yet a noble composure sate upon it, which impressed those who had just been hearing of his changed position, with a sense of inherent dignity and manly strength.", "훤칠한 체구는 여전히 그를 보통 남자들보다 크게 보이게 했고, 거기서 우러나 그에게 자연스레 배어 있는 여유로운 몸놀림이 그에게 남다른 풍모를 주었다. 그러나 얼굴은 더 늙고 근심에 시달린 듯 보였다."], ["He was aware, from the first glance he had given round the room, that Margaret was there; he had seen her intent look of occupation as she listened to Mr.", "그런데도 그 위에는 고결한 평정이 어려 있어서, 그가 처지가 달라졌다는 이야기를 방금 들은 이들에게 타고난 위엄과 사내다운 굳셈을 느끼게 했다. 그는 방 안을 처음 한 번 둘러보았을 때부터 마거릿이 거기 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Henry Lennox; and he came up to her with the perfectly regulated manner of an old friend.", "그녀가 헨리 레녹스 씨의 말에 귀 기울이며 골똘히 몰두한 표정을 짓고 있는 것도 보았다."], ["With his first calm words a vivid colour flashed into her cheeks, which never left them again during the evening.", "그리고 그는 오랜 벗의 완벽하게 절제된 태도로 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그의 첫 담담한 말과 함께 선연한 붉은빛이 그녀의 뺨으로 확 번졌고, 그 빛은 그날 저녁 내내 다시 가시지 않았다."], ["She did not seem to have much to say to him.", "그녀는 그에게 딱히 할 말이 많지 않은 듯했다."], ["She disappointed him by the quiet way in which she asked what seemed to him to be the merely necessary questions respecting her old acquaintances, in Milton; but others came in—more intimate in the house than he—and he fell into the background, where he and Mr.", "그녀는 밀턴의 옛 지인들에 대해 그가 보기에는 그저 형식적으로 필요한 정도의 질문들을 조용히 던지는 것으로 그를 실망시켰다. 그러나 다른 사람들이 들어왔다."], ["Lennox talked together from time to time.", "그보다 이 집안과 가까운 이들이었다. 그리하여 그는 뒤로 물러나게 되었고, 거기서 이따금 레녹스 씨와 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["“You think Miss Hale looking well,”", "“헤일 양의 안색이 좋아 보이지요.” 레녹스 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Lennox, “don’t you?", "“그렇지 않습니까?"], ["Milton didn’t agree with her, I imagine; for when she first came to London, I thought I had never seen any one so much changed.", "밀턴은 저 사람과 맞지 않았던 모양입니다. 처음 런던에 왔을 때는 사람이 그토록 변한 것을 본 적이 없다 싶었으니까요."], ["To-night she is looking radiant.", "오늘 밤은 환히 빛나는군요."], ["But she is much stronger.", "게다가 몸도 훨씬 튼튼해졌습니다."], ["Last autumn she was fatigued with a walk of a couple of miles. On Friday evening we walked up to Hampstead and back.", "지난가을만 해도 3킬로미터쯤 걷고는 지쳤는데, 금요일 저녁에는 우리가 햄프스테드까지 걸어갔다가 돌아왔지요."], ["Yet on Saturday she looked as well as she does now.”", "그런데도 토요일에 지금처럼 좋아 보이더군요.”"]], [["“We!” Who?", "“우리라니!”"], ["They two alone?", "누구와? 두 사람만이?"]], [["Mr. Colthurst was a very clever man, and a rising member of Parliament.", "콜서스트 씨는 매우 영민한 사람이었고, 떠오르는 하원 의원이었다."], ["He had a quick eye at discerning character, and was struck by a remark which Mr. Thornton made at dinner-time.", "그는 사람됨을 알아보는 눈이 빨랐는데, 만찬 자리에서 손턴 씨가 한 말 한마디에 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["He enquired from Edith who that gentleman was; and, rather to her surprise, she found, from the tone of his “Indeed!”", "그는 저 신사가 누구냐고 이디스에게 물었다. 그리고 이디스는 다소 놀랍게도 그의 “아, 그렇습니까!”"], ["that Mr. Thornton of Milton was not such an unknown name to him as she had imagined it would be.", "하는 어조에서 밀턴의 손턴 씨가 자기가 짐작했던 것처럼 그에게 낯선 이름이 아니라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Her dinner was going off well.", "그녀의 만찬은 잘 굴러가고 있었다."], ["Henry was in good humour, and brought out his dry caustic wit admirably. Mr.", "헨리는 기분이 좋았고, 그 특유의 메마르고 신랄한 재치를 훌륭하게 펼쳐 보였다."], ["Thornton and Mr. Colthurst found one or two mutual subjects of interest, which they could only touch upon then, reserving them for more private after-dinner talk.", "손턴 씨와 콜서스트 씨는 서로 관심이 겹치는 주제를 한두 가지 찾아냈는데, 그 자리에서는 잠깐 건드리기만 하고 만찬 뒤의 좀 더 사사로운 이야기 시간으로 미루어 두었다."], ["Margaret looked beautiful in the pomegranate flowers; and if she did lean back in her chair and speak but little, Edith was not annoyed, for the conversation flowed on smoothly without her.", "마거릿은 석류꽃을 꽂은 모습이 아름다웠다. 그리고 그녀가 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 말을 거의 하지 않아도 이디스는 언짢아하지 않았으니, 그녀 없이도 대화가 매끄럽게 흘러갔기 때문이다."], ["Margaret was watching Mr. Thornton’s face.", "마거릿은 손턴 씨의 얼굴을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["He never looked at her: so she might study him unobserved, and note the changes which even this short time had wrought in him.", "그는 한 번도 그녀를 보지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 그를 살필 수 있었고, 이 짧은 시간이 그에게 새겨 놓은 변화를 헤아릴 수 있었다."], ["Only at some unexpected mot of Mr.", "다만 레녹스 씨의 예기치 못한 한마디가 나올 때면, 그의 얼굴에 예전의 그 짙은 즐거움이 확 번졌다."], ["Lennox’s, his face flashed out into the old look of intense enjoyment: the merry brightness returned to his eyes, the lips just parted to suggest the brilliant smile of former days; and for an instant, his glance instinctively sought hers, as if he wanted her sympathy.", "유쾌한 광채가 눈으로 돌아왔고, 입술이 살짝 벌어지며 지난날의 눈부신 미소를 예고했다. 그리고 한순간 그의 시선은 마치 그녀의 공감을 구하기라도 하듯 본능적으로 그녀의 시선을 찾았다."], ["But when their eyes met, his whole countenance changed; he was grave and anxious once more; and he resolutely avoided even looking near her again during dinner.", "그러나 두 사람의 눈이 마주치자 그의 온 낯빛이 달라졌다. 그는 다시금 무겁고 근심스러워졌고, 만찬이 끝날 때까지 그녀 쪽으로 눈길을 주는 것조차 단호히 피했다."]], [["There were only two ladies beside their own party, and as these were occupied in conversation by her aunt and Edith, when they went up into the drawing-room, Margaret languidly employed herself about some work.", "그들 일행 말고는 부인이 둘밖에 없었는데, 응접실로 올라갔을 때 그 두 사람은 쇼 이모와 이디스가 붙들고 이야기를 나누는 통에, 마거릿은 나른하게 바느질감을 붙들고 앉았다."], ["Presently the gentlemen came up, Mr.", "이윽고 신사들이 올라왔다."], ["Colthurst and Mr. Thornton in close conversation.", "콜서스트 씨와 손턴 씨는 열띤 대화를 나누는 중이었다."], ["Mr. Lennox drew near to Margaret, and said in a low voice:", "레녹스 씨가 마거릿 가까이 다가와 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I really think Edith owes me thanks for my contribution to her party.", "“이디스가 자기 만찬에 내가 보탠 몫에 대해 정말 고마워해야 할 것 같군요."], ["You’ve no idea what an agreeable, sensible fellow this tenant of yours is.", "당신의 저 세입자가 얼마나 유쾌하고 분별 있는 사람인지 당신은 모르실 겁니다."], ["He has been the very man to give Colthurst all the facts he wanted coaching in.", "콜서스트가 배워 두고 싶어 하던 사실들을 전부 알려 주기에 더없이 알맞은 사람이었어요."], ["I can’t conceive how he contrived to mismanage his affairs.”", "그런 사람이 어떻게 제 일을 그르쳤는지 나로서는 짐작이 안 갑니다.”"]], [["“With his powers and opportunities you would have succeeded,”", "“그분이 지녔던 역량과 기회가 있었다면 당신은 성공했겠지요.”"], ["said Margaret.", "마거릿이 말했다."], ["He did not quite relish the tone in which she spoke, although the words but expressed a thought which had passed through his own mind. As he was silent, they caught a swell in the sound of conversation going on near the fire-place between Mr.", "그 말이 그 자신의 머릿속을 스쳐 간 생각을 그대로 옮긴 것이었는데도, 그는 그녀가 말한 어조가 썩 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["Colthurst and Mr. Thornton.", "그가 잠자코 있는 사이, 두 사람은 벽난로 옆에서 콜서스트 씨와 손턴 씨가 나누는 대화가 한결 높아지는 것을 들었다."]], [["“I assure you, I heard it spoken of with great interest—curiosity as to its result, perhaps I should rather say.", "“정말이지, 사람들이 그 일을 대단한 관심을 가지고 이야기하는 것을 들었습니다. 어쩌면 결과가 어찌 될지 궁금해한다고 말하는 편이 낫겠군요."], ["I heard your name frequently mentioned during my short stay in the neighbourhood.”", "그 근방에 잠깐 머무는 동안 선생 성함이 자주 오르내리는 것을 들었습니다.” 그러고는 몇 마디를 놓쳤다."], ["Then they lost some words; and when next they could hear Mr. Thornton was speaking.", "다시 들리기 시작했을 때는 손턴 씨가 말하고 있었다."]], [["“I have not the elements for popularity—if they spoke of me in that way, they were mistaken.", "“저에게는 인기를 끌 만한 자질이 없습니다. 사람들이 저를 그런 식으로 말했다면 잘못 본 것이지요."], ["I fall slowly into new projects; and I find it difficult to let myself be known, even by those whom I desire to know, and with whom I would fain have no reserve.", "저는 새 계획에 더디게 들어서고, 저 자신을 남에게 알리는 일을 어려워합니다. 제가 알고 지내고 싶은 사람들, 아무런 거리낌도 두고 싶지 않은 사람들에게조차 그렇습니다."], ["Yet, even with all these drawbacks, I felt that I was on the right path, and that, starting from a kind of friendship with one, I was becoming acquainted with many.", "그런데도 이런 온갖 결점을 안고서도 제가 옳은 길 위에 있다는 것을 느꼈고, 한 사람과의 우정 비슷한 것에서 출발해 여러 사람과 아는 사이가 되어 가고 있다는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["The advantages were mutual: we were both unconsciously and consciously teaching each other.”", "이로움은 양쪽 모두에게 돌아갔지요. 우리는 저도 모르게, 또 알면서도 서로를 가르치고 있었습니다.”"]], [["“You say ‘were.’ I trust you are intending to pursue the same course?”", "“‘있었다’고 말씀하시는군요. 앞으로도 같은 길을 이어 가실 생각이겠지요?”"]], [["“I must stop Colthurst,” said Henry Lennox, hastily.", "“콜서스트를 막아야겠습니다.”"], ["And by an abrupt, yet apropos question, he turned the current of the conversation, so as not to give Mr.", "헨리 레녹스가 급히 말했다."], ["Thornton the mortification of acknowledging his want of success and consequent change of position. But as soon as the newly-started subject had come to a close, Mr.", "그러고는 불쑥 던지면서도 때에 맞는 물음으로 대화의 물줄기를 돌려놓아, 손턴 씨가 자신의 실패와 그로 인한 처지의 변화를 인정하는 굴욕을 겪지 않도록 했다."], ["Thornton resumed the conversation just where it had been interrupted, and gave Mr. Colthurst the reply to his inquiry.", "그러나 새로 꺼낸 화제가 마무리되자마자 손턴 씨는 끊겼던 바로 그 자리에서 대화를 다시 이어, 콜서스트 씨의 물음에 답을 주었다."]], [["“I have been unsuccessful in business, and have had to give up my position as a master.", "“저는 사업에 실패했고, 공장주라는 자리를 내놓아야 했습니다."], ["I am on the look-out for a situation in Milton, where I may meet with employment under some one who will be willing to let me go along my own way in such matters as these.", "지금은 밀턴에서 일자리를 찾고 있습니다. 이런 일들에 관해서만은 제 방식대로 하도록 놓아둘 뜻이 있는 누군가 밑에서 일할 수 있는 자리 말입니다."], ["I can depend upon myself for having no go-ahead theories that I would rashly bring into practice.", "함부로 실행에 옮길 만한 앞서 나간 이론 따위는 제가 품지 않으리라는 것은 저 자신이 보증할 수 있습니다."], ["My only wish is to have the opportunity of cultivating some intercourse with the hands beyond the mere ‘cash nexus.’ But it might be the point Archimedes sought from which to move the earth, to judge by the importance attached to it by some of our manufacturers, who shake their heads and look grave as soon as I name the one or two experiments that I should like to try.”", "제 유일한 바람은 단순한 ‘현금 관계’를 넘어 직공들과 얼마간 교류를 가꿀 기회를 얻는 것뿐입니다. 그런데 제가 시도해 보고 싶은 한두 가지 실험을 입에 올리기가 무섭게 고개를 젓고 심각한 낯빛을 짓는 우리 공장주들이 거기에 부여하는 무게로 보자면, 그것은 아르키메데스가 지구를 움직이려고 찾던 그 받침점쯤 되는 모양입니다.”"]], [["“You call them ‘experiments’ I notice,”", "“‘실험’이라고 부르시는군요.”"], ["said Mr. Colthurst, with a delicate increase of respect in his manner.", "콜서스트 씨가 태도에 은근히 존경을 더하며 말했다."]], [["“Because I believe them to be such.", "“그것이 실험이라고 믿기 때문입니다."], ["I am not sure of the consequences that may result from them.", "저는 거기서 어떤 결과가 나올지 확신하지 못합니다. 그러나 시도해 보아야 마땅하다는 것만은 확신합니다."], ["But I am sure they ought to be tried.", "저는 이런 확신에 이르렀습니다."], ["I have arrived at the conviction that no mere institutions, however wise, and however much thought may have been required to organise and arrange them, can attach class to class as they should be attached, unless the working out of such institutions bring the individuals of the different classes into actual personal contact.", "아무리 지혜롭고, 그것을 조직하고 정비하는 데 아무리 많은 궁리가 들어갔다 하더라도, 제도 그 자체만으로는 계급과 계급을 마땅히 이어져야 할 만큼 이어 놓을 수 없다는 것입니다. 그 제도를 실제로 굴려 나가는 과정이 서로 다른 계급의 사람들을 참으로 사람 대 사람으로 맞닿게 하지 않는 한 말이지요."], ["Such intercourse is the very breath of life.", "그런 교류야말로 삶의 숨결 그 자체입니다."], ["A working man can hardly be made to feel and know how much his employer may have laboured in his study at plans for the benefit of his workpeople.", "노동자에게, 자기 고용주가 일꾼들의 이익을 위한 계획을 세우느라 서재에서 얼마나 애를 썼는지를 느끼고 알게 만들기란 좀처럼 어려운 일입니다. 완성된 계획은 마치 기계 한 대처럼 튀어나옵니다."], ["A complete plan emerges like a piece of machinery, apparently fitted for every emergency.", "겉보기에는 어떤 상황에도 들어맞도록 짜여 있지요. 그러나 직공들은 그것을 기계를 받아들이듯 받아들일 뿐, 그것을 그만한 완성도로 끌어올리기까지 필요했던 극심한 정신노동과 미리 헤아린 수고는 알지 못합니다."], ["But the hands accept it as they do machinery, without understanding the intense mental labour and forethought required to bring it to such perfection.", "하지만 저라면 실행하자면 반드시 사람 대 사람의 교류가 있어야만 하는 그런 착상을 택하겠습니다. 처음에는 잘 굴러가지 않을지도 모릅니다."], ["But I would take an idea, the working out of which would necessitate personal intercourse; it might not go well at first, but at every hitch interest would be felt by an increasing number of men, and at last its success in working come to be desired by all, as all had borne a part in the formation of the plan; and even then I am sure that it would lose its vitality, cease to be living, as soon as it was no longer carried on by that sort of common interest which invariably makes people find means and ways of seeing each other, and becoming acquainted with each others’ characters and persons, and even tricks of temper and modes of speech.", "그러나 걸림돌이 생길 때마다 점점 더 많은 사람이 관심을 갖게 될 것이고, 마침내는 모두가 그 계획을 만드는 데 한몫을 했으니 그것이 잘 굴러가기를 모두가 바라게 될 것입니다. 그리고 그때조차도 저는 확신합니다."], ["We should understand each other better, and I’ll venture to say we should like each other more.”", "서로 얼굴을 볼 방도와 길을 어김없이 찾아내게 하고, 서로의 성품과 사람됨을, 나아가 성미의 버릇과 말투까지도 알게 만드는 그런 공통의 관심이 더는 그것을 떠받치지 않게 되는 순간, 그 계획은 생기를 잃고 살아 있기를 그치리라는 것을요. 우리는 서로를 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이고, 감히 말하건대 서로를 더 좋아하게 될 것입니다.”"]], [["“And you think they may prevent recurrence of strikes?”", "“그러면 그것이 파업의 재발을 막아 주리라고 보십니까?”"]], [["“Not at all.", "“전혀 아닙니다."], ["My utmost expectation only goes as far as this—that they may render strikes not the bitter, venomous sources of hatred they have hitherto been.", "제가 최대한으로 기대하는 것은 여기까지입니다. 파업이 지금까지 그래 왔던 것처럼 쓰라리고 독한 증오의 원천이 되지는 않게 하리라는 것 말입니다."], ["A more hopeful man might imagine that a closer and more genial intercourse between classes might do away with strikes.", "저보다 희망을 품은 사람이라면 계급 사이의 더 가깝고 더 다정한 교류가 파업을 아예 없애 주리라고 상상할지도 모르지요."], ["But I am not a hopeful man.”", "그러나 저는 희망을 품은 사람이 아닙니다.”"]], [["Suddenly, as if a new idea had struck him, he crossed over to where Margaret was sitting, and began, without preface, as if he knew she had been listening to all that had passed:", "문득 새로운 생각이 떠오르기라도 한 듯 그는 마거릿이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 건너가더니, 그녀가 오간 이야기를 전부 듣고 있었음을 안다는 듯 아무런 서두도 없이 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Miss Hale, I had a round-robin from some of my men—I suspect in Higgins’ handwriting—stating their wish to work for me, if ever I was in a position to employ men again on my own behalf.", "“헤일 양, 제 일꾼들 몇몇이 보낸 연명 편지를 받았습니다. 히긴스의 필체인 것 같더군요."], ["That was good, wasn’t it?”", "제가 다시 제 이름으로 사람을 부릴 처지가 되기만 하면 저를 위해 일하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝힌 것이었습니다. 좋은 일이지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["Just right.", "참으로 마땅한 일이에요."], ["I am glad of it,”", "저도 기뻐요.”"], ["said Margaret, looking up straight into his face with her speaking eyes, and then dropping them under his eloquent glance.", "마거릿이 말하는 듯한 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 올려다보며 말했다. 그러고는 무언가를 말하는 듯한 그의 눈길 아래 시선을 떨구었다."], ["He gazed back at her for a minute, as if he did not know exactly what he was about.", "그는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 정확히 알지 못하는 사람처럼 잠시 그녀를 마주 바라보았다."], ["Then sighed; and saying, “I knew you would like it,”", "그러고는 한숨을 쉬었다. 그리고 “좋아하시리라 알고 있었습니다.”"], ["he turned away, and never spoke to her again until he bid her a formal “good night.”", "하고 말하고는 돌아섰고, 격식을 차린 “안녕히 계십시오.”라는 인사를 건넬 때까지 그녀에게 다시는 말을 걸지 않았다."]], [["As Mr. Lennox took his departure, Margaret said, with a blush that she could not repress, and with some hesitation,", "레녹스 씨가 돌아가려 할 때, 마거릿은 억누를 수 없는 홍조를 띤 채 얼마간 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Can I speak to you to-morrow?", "“내일 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있을까요?"], ["I want your help about—something.”", "어떤 일에 대해 도움이 필요해서요.”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“물론입니다."], ["I will come at whatever time you name.", "시간을 정해 주시면 언제든 오지요."], ["You cannot give me a greater pleasure than by making me of any use.", "저를 어디엔가 쓸모 있게 해 주시는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨을 제게 주실 수는 없습니다."], ["At eleven?", "11시에요?"], ["Very well.”", "좋습니다.”"]], [["His eye brightened with exultation.", "그의 눈이 의기양양하게 빛났다."], ["How she was learning to depend upon him!", "그녀가 자신에게 기대는 법을 배워 가고 있지 않은가!"], ["It seemed as if any day now might give him the certainty, without having which he had determined never to offer to her again.", "이제는 어느 날엔가 그 확신이 주어질 것만 같았다. 그 확신 없이는 그녀에게 다시는 청혼하지 않겠다고 그는 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LII", "# 제52장"]], [["“PACK CLOUDS AWAY.”", "「구름아 걷혀라」"]], [["“For joy or grief, for hope or fear, For all hereafter, as for here, In peace or strife, in storm or shine.”", "“기쁨에도 슬픔에도, 희망에도 두려움에도, 여기서와 마찬가지로 앞으로 올 모든 날에도, 평화에도 다툼에도, 폭풍에도 햇살에도.”"], ["ANON.", "— 작자 미상."]], [["Edith went about on tip-toe, and checked Sholto in all loud speaking that next morning, as if any sudden noise would interrupt the conference that was taking place in the drawing-room.", "이디스는 이튿날 아침 내내 발끝으로 걸어 다녔고, 숄토가 큰 소리로 말할 때마다 제지했다. 마치 갑작스러운 소리라도 나면 응접실에서 벌어지고 있는 담판이 끊기기라도 할 것처럼."], ["Two o’clock came; and they still sate there with closed doors.", "두 시가 되었다. 그런데도 두 사람은 여전히 문을 닫아건 채 그 안에 앉아 있었다."], ["Then there was a man’s footstep running down stairs; and Edith peeped out of the drawing-room.", "이윽고 층계를 뛰어 내려오는 남자의 발소리가 났고, 이디스가 응접실 밖으로 고개를 내밀었다."]], [["“Well, Henry?”", "“그래서, 헨리?”"], ["said she, with a look of interrogation.", "그녀가 묻는 듯한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Well!”", "“그래서라니요!”"], ["said he, rather shortly.", "그가 다소 퉁명스럽게 대답했다."]], [["“Come in to lunch!”", "“들어와서 점심 들어요!”"]], [["“No, thank you, I can’t.", "“아뇨, 고맙지만 그럴 수 없습니다."], ["I’ve lost too much time here already.”", "여기서 이미 너무 많은 시간을 허비했어요.”"]], [["“Then it’s not all settled?”", "“그럼 아직 다 정해진 게 아니군요?”"], ["said Edith, despondingly.", "이디스가 낙담해서 말했다."]], [["“No!", "“아뇨!"], ["not at all.", "전혀 아닙니다."], ["It never will be settled, if the ‘it’ is what I conjecture you mean.", "그 ‘다’라는 것이 제 짐작대로 형수님이 뜻하는 그것이라면, 그건 영영 정해지지 않을 겁니다."], ["That will never be, Edith, so give up thinking about it.”", "절대 그렇게 되지 않을 테니, 이디스, 그 생각은 그만두세요.”"]], [["“But it would be so nice for us all,” pleaded Edith.", "“하지만 우리 모두에게 얼마나 좋겠어요.”"], ["“I should always feel comfortable about the children, if I had Margaret settled down near to me.", "이디스가 매달리듯 말했다. “마거릿이 내 가까이에 자리를 잡아 준다면 아이들 걱정은 늘 놓을 수 있을 텐데."], ["As it is, I am always afraid of her going off to Cadiz.”", "지금 같아서는 그 애가 카디스로 훌쩍 가 버릴까 봐 늘 조마조마해요.”"]], [["“I will try, when I marry, to look out for a young lady who has a knowledge of the management of children.", "“저는 결혼할 때가 되면 아이 다루는 법을 아는 아가씨를 찾아보도록 하지요."], ["That is all I can do.", "제가 할 수 있는 건 그것뿐입니다."], ["Miss Hale will not have me. And I shall not ask her.”", "헤일 양은 저를 받아 주지 않을 테고, 저도 청하지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Then what have you been talking about?”", "“그럼 무슨 이야기를 그렇게 한 거예요?”"]], [["“A thousand things you would not understand: Investments, and leases, and value of land.”", "“형수님은 알아듣지 못할 천 가지 것들이지요. 투자며 임대차며 땅값 같은 것들.”"]], [["“Oh, go away if that’s all.", "“아, 그게 다라면 어서 가 봐요."], ["You and she will be unbearably stupid, if you’ve been talking all this time about such weary things.”", "그렇게 오래도록 그런 따분한 이야기만 했다면, 도련님이나 그 애나 못 견디게 재미없는 사람들이네요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋습니다."], ["I’m coming again to-morrow, and bringing Mr.", "내일 다시 오지요."], ["Thornton with me, to have some more talk with Miss Hale.”", "손턴 씨를 모시고 와서 헤일 양과 이야기를 좀 더 나눌 겁니다.”"]], [["“Mr. Thornton!", "“손턴 씨요!"], ["What has he to do with it?”", "그분이 그 일과 무슨 상관이에요?”"]], [["“He is Miss Hale’s tenant,”", "“그분은 헤일 양의 세입자니까요.”"], ["said Mr. Lennox, turning away.", "레녹스 씨가 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“And he wishes to give up his lease.”", "“그리고 임차를 그만두고 싶어 합니다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["very well.", "알겠어요."], ["I can’t understand details, so don’t give them me.”", "자세한 건 알아듣지도 못하니 나한테 늘어놓지 말아요.”"]], [["“The only detail I want you to understand is, to let us have the back drawing-room undisturbed, as it was to-day.", "“제가 알아주셨으면 하는 자세한 사정은 딱 하나입니다. 오늘처럼 안쪽 응접실을 방해받지 않게 내어 주십사 하는 것이지요."], ["In general, the children and servants are so in and out, that I can never get any business satisfactorily explained; and the arrangements we have to make to-morrow are of importance.”", "평소에는 아이들과 하인들이 어찌나 들락날락하는지 무슨 일이든 제대로 설명해 볼 수가 없습니다. 내일 우리가 매듭지어야 할 일들은 중요한 것들이에요.”"]], [["No one ever knew why Mr.", "이튿날 레녹스 씨가 왜 그 약속을 지키지 않았는지는 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["Lennox did not keep to his appointment on the following day.", "손턴 씨는 시간에 맞춰 왔다."], ["Mr. Thornton came true to his time; and after keeping him waiting for nearly an hour, Margaret came in looking very white and anxious.", "그리고 한 시간 가까이 그를 기다리게 한 끝에, 마거릿이 몹시 창백하고 불안한 얼굴로 들어왔다."]], [["She began hurriedly:", "그녀는 서둘러 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I am so sorry Mr.", "“레녹스 씨가 안 계셔서 정말 죄송해요."], ["Lennox is not here,—he could have done it so much better than I can.", "저보다 훨씬 잘 해내셨을 텐데."], ["He is my adviser in this”——", "이 일에서는 그분이 제 조언자시거든요……”"]], [["“I am sorry that I came, if it troubles you. Shall I go to Mr.", "“제가 와서 번거롭게 해 드렸다면 죄송합니다."], ["Lennox’s chambers and try and find him?”", "레녹스 씨 사무실로 가서 찾아볼까요?”"]], [["“No, thank you.", "“아뇨, 괜찮습니다."], ["I wanted to tell you, how grieved I was to find that I am to lose you as a tenant.", "제가 드리고 싶었던 말씀은, 세입자로 모시던 손턴 씨를 잃게 되었다는 걸 알고 얼마나 마음이 아팠는지 하는 거예요."], ["But, Mr. Lennox says, things are sure to brighten”——", "하지만 레녹스 씨 말씀으로는 사정이 틀림없이 나아질 거라고……”"]], [["“Mr. Lennox knows little about it,”", "“레녹스 씨는 그 일을 잘 모릅니다.”"], ["said Mr. Thornton quietly.", "손턴 씨가 조용히 말했다."], ["“Happy and fortunate in all a man cares for, he does not understand what it is to find oneself no longer young—yet thrown back to the starting-point which requires the hopeful energy of youth—to feel one half of life gone, and nothing done—nothing remaining of wasted opportunity, but the bitter recollection that it has been.", "“사람이 바랄 만한 모든 것에서 행복하고 운이 좋은 이는, 자신이 더 이상 젊지 않다는 것을 깨닫는 일이 어떤 것인지 모릅니다. 그러면서도 젊음의 희망찬 기운을 요구하는 출발점으로 도로 내던져지는 것이, 인생의 절반이 가 버렸는데 이룬 것은 하나도 없다고 느끼는 것이, 허비한 기회에서 남은 것이라고는 그런 일이 있었다는 쓰디쓴 기억뿐임을 아는 것이 어떤 것인지를 모른단 말입니다."], ["Miss Hale, I would rather not hear Mr. Lennox’s opinion of my affairs.", "헤일 양, 제 일에 대한 레녹스 씨의 의견은 차라리 듣고 싶지 않습니다."], ["Those who are happy and successful themselves are too apt to make light of the misfortunes of others.”", "스스로 행복하고 성공한 사람들은 남의 불운을 너무나 쉽게 가벼이 여기니까요.”"]], [["“You are unjust,”", "“그건 부당하세요.”"], ["said Margaret, gently. “Mr.", "마거릿이 부드럽게 말했다."], ["Lennox has only spoken of the great probability which he believes there to be of your redeeming—your more than redeeming what you have lost—don’t speak till I have ended—pray don’t.”", "“레녹스 씨는 다만 손턴 씨가 잃으신 것을 되찾을, 아니 되찾고도 남을 가능성이 크다고 믿기에 그 말씀을 하셨을 뿐이에요. 제 말이 끝날 때까지는 아무 말씀 마세요."], ["And collecting herself once more, she went on rapidly turning over some law papers, and statement of accounts in a trembling hurried manner.", "부디요.” 그러고는 다시 마음을 가다듬고, 떨리는 손으로 조급하게 법률 서류와 계산서를 뒤적이며 빠르게 말을 이었다."], ["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["here it is!", "여기 있네요. 그리고 그분이 제게 제안서를 하나 써 주셨어요."], ["and—he drew me out a proposal—I wish he was here to explain it—showing that if you would take some money of mine, eighteen hundred and fifty-seven pounds, lying just at this moment unused in the bank, and bringing me in only two and a half per cent.—you could pay me much better interest, and might go on working Marlborough Mills.”", "설명해 주실 수 있게 그분이 여기 계셨으면 좋았을 텐데. 지금 이 순간 은행에 쓰이지 않은 채 놓여 있으면서 저에게 겨우 2.5퍼센트밖에 가져다주지 않는 제 돈 1,857파운드를 손턴 씨가 받아 주신다면, 저에게 훨씬 나은 이자를 주실 수 있고 말버러 공장도 계속 돌리실 수 있다는 것이었어요.”"], ["Her voice had cleared itself and become more steady. Mr.", "그녀의 목소리는 어느새 맑아지고 한결 차분해져 있었다."], ["Thornton did not speak, and she went on looking for some paper on which were written down the proposals for security; for she was most anxious to have it all looked upon in the light of a mere business arrangement, in which the principal advantage would be on her side.", "손턴 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 그녀는 담보 조건이 적힌 종이를 계속 찾았다. 이 모든 것이 순전한 사업상의 약정으로만, 그것도 이득의 대부분이 자기 쪽에 있는 거래로만 보이기를 그녀는 무엇보다 간절히 바랐던 것이다."], ["While she sought for this paper, her very heart-pulse was arrested by the tone in which Mr. Thornton spoke.", "그 종이를 찾고 있는 사이, 손턴 씨가 입을 여는 그 어조에 그녀의 심장 박동이 그대로 멎어 버렸다."], ["His voice was hoarse, and trembling with tender passion, as he said:—", "그의 목소리는 쉬어 있었고, 애틋한 정열로 떨리고 있었다."]], [["“Margaret!”", "“마거릿!”"]], [["For an instant she looked up; and then sought to veil her luminous eyes by dropping her forehead on her hands.", "한순간 그녀는 눈을 들었다. 그러고는 두 손에 이마를 묻어 빛나는 눈을 가리려 했다."], ["Again, stepping nearer, he besought her with another tremulous eager call upon her name.", "그가 한 걸음 더 다가서서, 떨리면서도 간절한 부름으로 그녀의 이름을 또 한 번 불렀다."]], [["“Margaret!”", "“마거릿!”"]], [["Still lower went the head; more closely hidden was the face, almost resting on the table before her.", "고개는 더욱 낮게 떨구어졌고, 얼굴은 더욱 깊이 감추어져 앞의 탁자에 거의 닿을 듯했다."], ["He came close to her.", "그가 그녀에게 바싹 다가섰다."], ["He knelt by her side, to bring his face to a level with her ear; and whispered—panted out the words:—", "그는 그녀의 곁에 무릎을 꿇어 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 귀 높이에 맞추고는, 속삭이듯 헐떡이듯 말을 뱉었다."]], [["“Take care.—If you do not speak—I shall claim you as my own in some strange presumptuous way.—Send me away at once, if I must go;—Margaret!—”", "“조심하세요. 당신이 아무 말도 하지 않으면, 저는 무언가 이상하고 주제넘은 방식으로 당신을 제 사람이라 주장하고 말 겁니다. 제가 가야 한다면 지금 당장 저를 내치세요. 마거릿!”"]], [["At that third call she turned her face, still covered with her small white hands, towards him, and laid it on his shoulder, even hiding it there; and it was too delicious to feel her soft cheek against his, for him to wish to see either deep blushes or loving eyes.", "그 세 번째 부름에 그녀는 여전히 작고 흰 두 손으로 가린 얼굴을 그에게로 돌려 그의 어깨에 기댔고, 아예 그 안에 묻어 버렸다. 그녀의 보드라운 뺨이 자기 뺨에 닿는 느낌이 너무나 감미로워, 그는 짙게 물든 홍조도 사랑에 젖은 눈도 굳이 보고 싶지 않았다."], ["He clasped her close.", "그는 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["But they both kept silence.", "그러나 두 사람 다 말이 없었다."], ["At length she murmured in a broken voice:", "이윽고 그녀가 갈라진 목소리로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Oh, Mr. Thornton, I am not good enough!”", "“아, 손턴 씨, 저는 그럴 만한 사람이 못 돼요!”"]], [["“Not good enough!", "“그럴 만하지 않다니요!"], ["Don’t mock my own deep feeling of unworthiness.”", "스스로 자격이 없다고 느끼는 제 깊은 마음을 놀리지 마세요.”"]], [["After a minute or two he gently disengaged her hands from her face, and laid her arms as they had once before been placed to protect him from the rioters.", "1, 2분쯤 지난 뒤, 그는 그녀의 두 손을 얼굴에서 부드럽게 떼어 내고, 언젠가 폭도들로부터 그를 지키려 놓였던 그대로 그녀의 두 팔을 놓아 주었다."]], [["“Do you remember, love?”", "“기억하시나요, 내 사랑?”"], ["he murmured.", "그가 나직이 말했다."], ["“And how I requited you with my insolence the next day?”", "“그리고 제가 그 이튿날 어떤 무례로 그 은혜를 갚았는지도요?”"]], [["“I remember how wrongly I spoke to you,—that is all.”", "“제가 얼마나 잘못된 말을 했는지가 기억날 뿐이에요.”"]], [["“Look here!", "“여기 보세요!"], ["Lift up your head.", "고개를 드세요."], ["I have something to show you!”", "보여 드릴 것이 있습니다!”"], ["She slowly faced him, glowing with beautiful shame.", "그녀는 아름다운 부끄러움으로 발갛게 달아오른 채 천천히 그를 마주 보았다."]], [["“Do you know these roses?”", "“이 장미들을 아시겠습니까?”"], ["he said, drawing out his pocket-book, in which were treasured up some dead flowers.", "그가 말하며 수첩을 꺼냈다. 그 안에는 시든 꽃 몇 송이가 소중히 간직되어 있었다."]], [["“No!”", "“아뇨!”"], ["she replied, with innocent curiosity.", "그녀가 천진한 호기심으로 대답했다."], ["“Did I give them to you?”", "“제가 드린 건가요?”"]], [["“No!", "“아닙니다."], ["Vanity; you did not.", "우쭐하셨군요. 당신이 주신 게 아닙니다."], ["You may have worn sister roses very probably.”", "아마 그 자매쯤 되는 장미를 몸에 다신 적은 있겠지요.”"]], [["She looked at them, wondering for a minute, then she smiled a little as she said—", "그녀는 잠시 들여다보며 궁금해하다가, 이윽고 살며시 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“They are from Helstone, are they not?", "“헬스톤 장미로군요, 그렇죠?"], ["I know the deep indentations round the leaves.", "잎 가장자리의 깊은 톱니를 알아보겠어요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["have you been there?", "거기에 가셨었나요?"], ["When were you there?”", "언제 가셨어요?”"]], [["“I wanted to see the place where Margaret grew to what she is, even at the worst time of all, when I had no hope of ever calling her mine.", "“마거릿이 지금의 마거릿으로 자라난 곳을 보고 싶었습니다. 그녀를 제 사람이라 부를 희망이라고는 조금도 없던, 가장 힘든 그때조차도 말입니다."], ["I went there on my return from Havre.”", "아브르에서 돌아오는 길에 그곳에 갔습니다.”"]], [["“You must give them to me,”", "“저에게 주셔야 해요.”"], ["she said, trying to take them out of his hand with gentle violence.", "그녀가 말하며 부드러운 힘으로 그의 손에서 그것을 빼앗으려 했다."]], [["“Very well.", "“좋습니다."], ["Only you must pay me for them!”", "다만 값을 치르셔야 합니다!”"]], [["“How shall I ever tell Aunt Shaw?”", "“쇼 이모에게는 어떻게 말씀드리죠?”"], ["she whispered, after some time of delicious silence.", "감미로운 침묵이 얼마쯤 흐른 뒤 그녀가 속삭였다."]], [["“Let me speak to her.”", "“제가 말씀드리게 해 주세요.”"]], [["“Oh, no!", "“아, 안 돼요!"], ["I owe to her,—but what will she say?”", "제가 직접 말씀드리는 것이 도리인걸요. 그런데 뭐라고 하실까요?”"]], [["“I can guess.", "“짐작이 갑니다."], ["Her first exclamation will be, ‘That man!’”", "첫마디는 이러시겠지요. ‘그 남자가!’”"]], [["“Hush!”", "“쉿!”"], ["said Margaret, “or I shall try and show you your mother’s indignant tones as she says, ‘That woman!’”", "마거릿이 말했다. “안 그러면 어머님께서 ‘그 여자가!’ 하실 때의 그 노기 띤 어조를 흉내 내 보일 거예요.”"]]];
